{"format":"care-public-library/1","exported":"2026-10-05","works":[{"id":"WORK_KANT_PURE_REASON","slug":"kant","title":"The Critique of Pure Reason","author":"Immanuel Kant","edition":"J. M. D. Meiklejohn translation · Gutenberg eText #4280","year":"Undated","original_year":null,"publication_year":1781,"description":"What can we know—and what gives a claim the right to call itself knowledge?","cover_url":"./assets/covers/beta-73b8664b61f6f07f-e4b1ac743ae9ce7e.png","source_url":"","source_notices":{},"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","sources":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP","title":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge","order":1,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_IDBP","text":"## Introduction\n\n### I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge\n\nThat all our knowledge begins with experience there can be no doubt. For how is it possible that the faculty of cognition should be awakened into exercise otherwise than by means of objects which affect our senses, and partly of themselves produce representations, partly rouse our powers of understanding into activity, to compare to connect, or to separate these, and so to convert the raw material of our sensuous impressions into a knowledge of objects, which is called experience? In respect of time, therefore, no knowledge of ours is antecedent to experience, but begins with it.\n\nBut, though all our knowledge begins with experience, it by no means follows that all arises out of experience. For, on the contrary, it is quite possible that our empirical knowledge is a compound of that which we receive through impressions, and that which the faculty of cognition supplies from itself (sensuous impressions giving merely the occasion), an addition which we cannot distinguish from the original element given by sense, till long practice has made us attentive to, and skilful in separating it. It is, therefore, a question which requires close investigation, and not to be answered at first sight, whether there exists a knowledge altogether independent of experience, and even of all sensuous impressions? Knowledge of this kind is called à priori, in contradistinction to empirical knowledge, which has its sources à posteriori, that is, in experience.\n\nBut the expression, “à priori,” is not as yet definite enough adequately to indicate the whole meaning of the question above started. For, in speaking of knowledge which has its sources in experience, we are wont to say, that this or that may be known à priori, because we do not derive this knowledge immediately from experience, but from a general rule, which, however, we have itself borrowed from experience. Thus, if a man undermined his house, we say, “he might know à priori that it would have fallen;” that is, he needed not to have waited for the experience that it did actually fall. But still, à priori, he could not know even this much. For, that bodies are heavy, and, consequently, that they fall when their supports are taken away, must have been known to him previously, by means of experience.\n\nBy the term “knowledge à priori,” therefore, we shall in the sequel understand, not such as is independent of this or that kind of experience, but such as is absolutely so of all experience. Opposed to this is empirical knowledge, or that which is possible only à posteriori, that is, through experience. Knowledge à priori is either pure or impure. Pure knowledge à priori is that with which no empirical element is mixed up. For example, the proposition, “Every change has a cause,” is a proposition à priori, but impure, because change is a conception which can only be derived from experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","text":"## Introduction\n\n### I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge\n\nThat all our knowledge begins with experience there can be no doubt. For how is it possible that the faculty of cognition should be awakened into exercise otherwise than by means of objects which affect our senses, and partly of themselves produce representations, partly rouse our powers of understanding into activity, to compare to connect, or to separate these, and so to convert the raw material of our sensuous impressions into a knowledge of objects, which is called experience? In respect of time, therefore, no knowledge of ours is antecedent to experience, but begins with it.\n\nBut, though all our knowledge begins with experience, it by no means follows that all arises out of experience. For, on the contrary, it is quite possible that our empirical knowledge is a compound of that which we receive through impressions, and that which the faculty of cognition supplies from itself (sensuous impressions giving merely the occasion), an addition which we cannot distinguish from the original element given by sense, till long practice has made us attentive to, and skilful in separating it. It is, therefore, a question which requires close investigation, and not to be answered at first sight, whether there exists a knowledge altogether independent of experience, and even of all sensuous impressions? Knowledge of this kind is called à priori, in contradistinction to empirical knowledge, which has its sources à posteriori, that is, in experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"1.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","text":"But the expression, “à priori,” is not as yet definite enough adequately to indicate the whole meaning of the question above started. For, in speaking of knowledge which has its sources in experience, we are wont to say, that this or that may be known à priori, because we do not derive this knowledge immediately from experience, but from a general rule, which, however, we have itself borrowed from experience. Thus, if a man undermined his house, we say, “he might know à priori that it would have fallen;” that is, he needed not to have waited for the experience that it did actually fall. But still, à priori, he could not know even this much. For, that bodies are heavy, and, consequently, that they fall when their supports are taken away, must have been known to him previously, by means of experience.\n\nBy the term “knowledge à priori,” therefore, we shall in the sequel understand, not such as is independent of this or that kind of experience, but such as is absolutely so of all experience. Opposed to this is empirical knowledge, or that which is possible only à posteriori, that is, through experience. Knowledge à priori is either pure or impure. Pure knowledge à priori is that with which no empirical element is mixed up. For example, the proposition, “Every change has a cause,” is a proposition à priori, but impure, because change is a conception which can only be derived from experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"1.2"}],"sha256":"fd5669aba309e950413a9d61528ae8a0082bfa460a74b2f917dd8c7ef3be9d57","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE","title":"II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.","order":2,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_IHIE","text":"### II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.\n\nThe question now is as to a criterion, by which we may securely distinguish a pure from an empirical cognition. Experience no doubt teaches us that this or that object is constituted in such and such a manner, but not that it could not possibly exist otherwise. Now, in the first place, if we have a proposition which contains the idea of necessity in its very conception, it is priori. If, moreover, it is not derived from any other proposition, unless from one equally involving the idea of necessity, it is absolutely priori. Secondly, an empirical judgement never exhibits strict and absolute, but only assumed and comparative universality (by induction); therefore, the most we can say is—so far as we have hitherto observed, there is no exception to this or that rule. If, on the other hand, a judgement carries with it strict and absolute universality, that is, admits of no possible exception, it is not derived from experience, but is valid absolutely à priori.\n\nEmpirical universality is, therefore, only an arbitrary extension of validity, from that which may be predicated of a proposition valid in most cases, to that which is asserted of a proposition which holds good in all; as, for example, in the affirmation, “All bodies are heavy.” When, on the contrary, strict universality characterizes a judgement, it necessarily indicates another peculiar source of knowledge, namely, a faculty of cognition à priori. Necessity and strict universality, therefore, are infallible tests for distinguishing pure from empirical knowledge, and are inseparably connected with each other. But as in the use of these criteria the empirical limitation is sometimes more easily detected than the contingency of the judgement, or the unlimited universality which we attach to a judgement is often a more convincing proof than its necessity, it may be advisable to use the criteria separately, each being by itself infallible.\n\nNow, that in the sphere of human cognition we have judgements which are necessary, and in the strictest sense universal, consequently pure à priori, it will be an easy matter to show. If we desire an example from the sciences, we need only take any proposition in mathematics. If we cast our eyes upon the commonest operations of the understanding, the proposition, “Every change must have a cause,” will amply serve our purpose. In the latter case, indeed, the conception of a cause so plainly involves the conception of a necessity of connection with an effect, and of a strict universality of the law, that the very notion of a cause would entirely disappear, were we to derive it, like Hume, from a frequent association of what happens with that which precedes; and the habit thence originating of connecting representations—the necessity inherent in the judgement being therefore merely subjective. Besides, without seeking for such examples of principles existing à priori in cognition, we might easily show that such principles are the indispensable basis of the possibility of experience itself, and consequently prove their existence à priori. For whence could our experience itself acquire certainty, if all the rules on which it depends were themselves empirical, and consequently fortuitous? No one, therefore, can admit the validity of the use of such rules as first principles. But, for the present, we may content ourselves with having established the fact, that we do possess and exercise a faculty of pure à priori cognition; and, secondly, with having pointed out the proper tests of such cognition, namely, universality and necessity.\n\nNot only in judgements, however, but even in conceptions, is an à priori origin manifest. For example, if we take away by degrees from our conceptions of a body all that can be referred to mere sensuous experience—colour, hardness or softness, weight, even impenetrability—the body will then vanish; but the space which it occupied still remains, and this it is utterly impossible to annihilate in thought. Again, if we take away, in like manner, from our empirical conception of any object, corporeal or incorporeal, all properties which mere experience has taught us to connect with it, still we cannot think away those through which we cogitate it as substance, or adhering to substance, although our conception of substance is more determined than that of an object. Compelled, therefore, by that necessity with which the conception of substance forces itself upon us, we must confess that it has its seat in our faculty of cognition à priori.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","text":"### II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.\n\nThe question now is as to a criterion, by which we may securely distinguish a pure from an empirical cognition. Experience no doubt teaches us that this or that object is constituted in such and such a manner, but not that it could not possibly exist otherwise. Now, in the first place, if we have a proposition which contains the idea of necessity in its very conception, it is priori. If, moreover, it is not derived from any other proposition, unless from one equally involving the idea of necessity, it is absolutely priori. Secondly, an empirical judgement never exhibits strict and absolute, but only assumed and comparative universality (by induction); therefore, the most we can say is—so far as we have hitherto observed, there is no exception to this or that rule. If, on the other hand, a judgement carries with it strict and absolute universality, that is, admits of no possible exception, it is not derived from experience, but is valid absolutely à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"2.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","text":"Empirical universality is, therefore, only an arbitrary extension of validity, from that which may be predicated of a proposition valid in most cases, to that which is asserted of a proposition which holds good in all; as, for example, in the affirmation, “All bodies are heavy.” When, on the contrary, strict universality characterizes a judgement, it necessarily indicates another peculiar source of knowledge, namely, a faculty of cognition à priori. Necessity and strict universality, therefore, are infallible tests for distinguishing pure from empirical knowledge, and are inseparably connected with each other. But as in the use of these criteria the empirical limitation is sometimes more easily detected than the contingency of the judgement, or the unlimited universality which we attach to a judgement is often a more convincing proof than its necessity, it may be advisable to use the criteria separately, each being by itself infallible.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"2.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","text":"Now, that in the sphere of human cognition we have judgements which are necessary, and in the strictest sense universal, consequently pure à priori, it will be an easy matter to show. If we desire an example from the sciences, we need only take any proposition in mathematics. If we cast our eyes upon the commonest operations of the understanding, the proposition, “Every change must have a cause,” will amply serve our purpose. In the latter case, indeed, the conception of a cause so plainly involves the conception of a necessity of connection with an effect, and of a strict universality of the law, that the very notion of a cause would entirely disappear, were we to derive it, like Hume, from a frequent association of what happens with that which precedes; and the habit thence originating of connecting representations—the necessity inherent in the judgement being therefore merely subjective. Besides, without seeking for such examples of principles existing à priori in cognition, we might easily show that such principles are the indispensable basis of the possibility of experience itself, and consequently prove their existence à priori. For whence could our experience itself acquire certainty, if all the rules on which it depends were themselves empirical, and consequently fortuitous? No one, therefore, can admit the validity of the use of such rules as first principles. But, for the present, we may content ourselves with having established the fact, that we do possess and exercise a faculty of pure à priori cognition; and, secondly, with having pointed out the proper tests of such cognition, namely, universality and necessity.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"2.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","text":"Not only in judgements, however, but even in conceptions, is an à priori origin manifest. For example, if we take away by degrees from our conceptions of a body all that can be referred to mere sensuous experience—colour, hardness or softness, weight, even impenetrability—the body will then vanish; but the space which it occupied still remains, and this it is utterly impossible to annihilate in thought. Again, if we take away, in like manner, from our empirical conception of any object, corporeal or incorporeal, all properties which mere experience has taught us to connect with it, still we cannot think away those through which we cogitate it as substance, or adhering to substance, although our conception of substance is more determined than that of an object. Compelled, therefore, by that necessity with which the conception of substance forces itself upon us, we must confess that it has its seat in our faculty of cognition à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"2.4"}],"sha256":"246eab62a08dcabf4d800525956e68dfbbb835d624e64dcb0ee0a6b36941a3c2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN","title":"III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”","order":3,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_IPSN","text":"### III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”\n\nOf far more importance than all that has been above said, is the consideration that certain of our cognitions rise completely above the sphere of all possible experience, and by means of conceptions, to which there exists in the whole extent of experience no corresponding object, seem to extend the range of our judgements beyond its bounds. And just in this transcendental or supersensible sphere, where experience affords us neither instruction nor guidance, lie the investigations of reason, which, on account of their importance, we consider far preferable to, and as having a far more elevated aim than, all that the understanding can achieve within the sphere of sensuous phenomena. So high a value do we set upon these investigations, that even at the risk of error, we persist in following them out, and permit neither doubt nor disregard nor indifference to restrain us from the pursuit. These unavoidable problems of mere pure reason are God, freedom (of will), and immortality. The science which, with all its preliminaries, has for its especial object the solution of these problems is named metaphysics—a science which is at the very outset dogmatical, that is, it confidently takes upon itself the execution of this task without any previous investigation of the ability or inability of reason for such an undertaking.\n\nNow the safe ground of experience being thus abandoned, it seems nevertheless natural that we should hesitate to erect a building with the cognitions we possess, without knowing whence they come, and on the strength of principles, the origin of which is undiscovered. Instead of thus trying to build without a foundation, it is rather to be expected that we should long ago have put the question, how the understanding can arrive at these à priori cognitions, and what is the extent, validity, and worth which they may possess? We say, “This is natural enough,” meaning by the word natural, that which is consistent with a just and reasonable way of thinking; but if we understand by the term, that which usually happens, nothing indeed could be more natural and more comprehensible than that this investigation should be left long unattempted. For one part of our pure knowledge, the science of mathematics, has been long firmly established, and thus leads us to form flattering expectations with regard to others, though these may be of quite a different nature. Besides, when we get beyond the bounds of experience, we are of course safe from opposition in that quarter; and the charm of widening the range of our knowledge is so great that, unless we are brought to a standstill by some evident contradiction, we hurry on undoubtingly in our course. This, however, may be avoided, if we are sufficiently cautious in the construction of our fictions, which are not the less fictions on that account.\n\nMathematical science affords us a brilliant example, how far, independently of all experience, we may carry our à priori knowledge. It is true that the mathematician occupies himself with objects and cognitions only in so far as they can be represented by means of intuition. But this circumstance is easily overlooked, because the said intuition can itself be given à priori, and therefore is hardly to be distinguished from a mere pure conception. Deceived by such a proof of the power of reason, we can perceive no limits to the extension of our knowledge. The light dove cleaving in free flight the thin air, whose resistance it feels, might imagine that her movements would be far more free and rapid in airless space. Just in the same way did Plato, abandoning the world of sense because of the narrow limits it sets to the understanding, venture upon the wings of ideas beyond it, into the void space of pure intellect. He did not reflect that he made no real progress by all his efforts; for he met with no resistance which might serve him for a support, as it were, whereon to rest, and on which he might apply his powers, in order to let the intellect acquire momentum for its progress. It is, indeed, the common fate of human reason in speculation, to finish the imposing edifice of thought as rapidly as possible, and then for the first time to begin to examine whether the foundation is a solid one or no. Arrived at this point, all sorts of excuses are sought after, in order to console us for its want of stability, or rather, indeed, to enable Us to dispense altogether with so late and dangerous an investigation. But what frees us during the process of building from all apprehension or suspicion, and flatters us into the belief of its solidity, is this. A great part, perhaps the greatest part, of the business of our reason consists in the analysation of the conceptions which we already possess of objects. By this means we gain a multitude of cognitions, which although really nothing more than elucidations or explanations of that which (though in a confused manner) was already thought in our conceptions, are, at least in respect of their form, prized as new introspections; whilst, so far as regards their matter or content, we have really made no addition to our conceptions, but only disinvolved them. But as this process does furnish a real priori knowledge, which has a sure progress and useful results, reason, deceived by this, slips in, without being itself aware of it, assertions of a quite different kind; in which, to given conceptions it adds others, à priori indeed, but entirely foreign to them, without our knowing how it arrives at these, and, indeed, without such a question ever suggesting itself. I shall therefore at once proceed to examine the difference between these two modes of knowledge.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","text":"### III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”\n\nOf far more importance than all that has been above said, is the consideration that certain of our cognitions rise completely above the sphere of all possible experience, and by means of conceptions, to which there exists in the whole extent of experience no corresponding object, seem to extend the range of our judgements beyond its bounds. And just in this transcendental or supersensible sphere, where experience affords us neither instruction nor guidance, lie the investigations of reason, which, on account of their importance, we consider far preferable to, and as having a far more elevated aim than, all that the understanding can achieve within the sphere of sensuous phenomena. So high a value do we set upon these investigations, that even at the risk of error, we persist in following them out, and permit neither doubt nor disregard nor indifference to restrain us from the pursuit. These unavoidable problems of mere pure reason are God, freedom (of will), and immortality. The science which, with all its preliminaries, has for its especial object the solution of these problems is named metaphysics—a science which is at the very outset dogmatical, that is, it confidently takes upon itself the execution of this task without any previous investigation of the ability or inability of reason for such an undertaking.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"3.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","text":"Now the safe ground of experience being thus abandoned, it seems nevertheless natural that we should hesitate to erect a building with the cognitions we possess, without knowing whence they come, and on the strength of principles, the origin of which is undiscovered. Instead of thus trying to build without a foundation, it is rather to be expected that we should long ago have put the question, how the understanding can arrive at these à priori cognitions, and what is the extent, validity, and worth which they may possess? We say, “This is natural enough,” meaning by the word natural, that which is consistent with a just and reasonable way of thinking; but if we understand by the term, that which usually happens, nothing indeed could be more natural and more comprehensible than that this investigation should be left long unattempted. For one part of our pure knowledge, the science of mathematics, has been long firmly established, and thus leads us to form flattering expectations with regard to others, though these may be of quite a different nature. Besides, when we get beyond the bounds of experience, we are of course safe from opposition in that quarter; and the charm of widening the range of our knowledge is so great that, unless we are brought to a standstill by some evident contradiction, we hurry on undoubtingly in our course. This, however, may be avoided, if we are sufficiently cautious in the construction of our fictions, which are not the less fictions on that account.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"3.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","text":"Mathematical science affords us a brilliant example, how far, independently of all experience, we may carry our à priori knowledge. It is true that the mathematician occupies himself with objects and cognitions only in so far as they can be represented by means of intuition. But this circumstance is easily overlooked, because the said intuition can itself be given à priori, and therefore is hardly to be distinguished from a mere pure conception. Deceived by such a proof of the power of reason, we can perceive no limits to the extension of our knowledge. The light dove cleaving in free flight the thin air, whose resistance it feels, might imagine that her movements would be far more free and rapid in airless space. Just in the same way did Plato, abandoning the world of sense because of the narrow limits it sets to the understanding, venture upon the wings of ideas beyond it, into the void space of pure intellect. He did not reflect that he made no real progress by all his efforts; for he met with no resistance which might serve him for a support, as it were, whereon to rest, and on which he might apply his powers, in order to let the intellect acquire momentum for its progress. It is, indeed, the common fate of human reason in speculation, to finish the imposing edifice of thought as rapidly as possible, and then for the first time to begin to examine whether the foundation is a solid one or no. Arrived at this point, all sorts of excuses are sought after, in order to console us for its want of stability, or rather, indeed, to enable Us to dispense altogether with so late and dangerous an investigation. But what frees us during the process of building from all apprehension or suspicion, and flatters us into the belief of its solidity, is this. A great part, perhaps the greatest part, of the business of our reason consists in the analysation of the conceptions which we already possess of objects. By this means we gain a multitude of cognitions, which although really nothing more than elucidations or explanations of that which (though in a confused manner) was already thought in our conceptions, are, at least in respect of their form, prized as new introspections; whilst, so far as regards their matter or content, we have really made no addition to our conceptions, but only disinvolved them. But as this process does furnish a real priori knowledge, which has a sure progress and useful results, reason, deceived by this, slips in, without being itself aware of it, assertions of a quite different kind; in which, to given conceptions it adds others, à priori indeed, but entirely foreign to them, without our knowing how it arrives at these, and, indeed, without such a question ever suggesting itself. I shall therefore at once proceed to examine the difference between these two modes of knowledge.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"3.3"}],"sha256":"a3a558a7b642c2ca425c9f1d7b07f6ef5f3690124102bb9b5997e8a6df9f620e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA","title":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.","order":4,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_IDBA","text":"### IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.\n\nIn all judgements wherein the relation of a subject to the predicate is cogitated (I mention affirmative judgements only here; the application to negative will be very easy), this relation is possible in two different ways. Either the predicate B belongs to the subject A, as somewhat which is contained (though covertly) in the conception A; or the predicate B lies completely out of the conception A, although it stands in connection with it. In the first instance, I term the judgement analytical, in the second, synthetical. Analytical judgements (affirmative) are therefore those in which the connection of the predicate with the subject is cogitated through identity; those in which this connection is cogitated without identity, are called synthetical judgements. The former may be called explicative, the latter augmentative judgements; because the former add in the predicate nothing to the conception of the subject, but only analyse it into its constituent conceptions, which were thought already in the subject, although in a confused manner; the latter add to our conceptions of the subject a predicate which was not contained in it, and which no analysis could ever have discovered therein. For example, when I say, “All bodies are extended,” this is an analytical judgement. For I need not go beyond the conception of body in order to find extension connected with it, but merely analyse the conception, that is, become conscious of the manifold properties which I think in that conception, in order to discover this predicate in it: it is therefore an analytical judgement. On the other hand, when I say, “All bodies are heavy,” the predicate is something totally different from that which I think in the mere conception of a body. By the addition of such a predicate, therefore, it becomes a synthetical judgement.\n\nJudgements of experience, as such, are always synthetical. For it would be absurd to think of grounding an analytical judgement on experience, because in forming such a judgement I need not go out of the sphere of my conceptions, and therefore recourse to the testimony of experience is quite unnecessary. That “bodies are extended” is not an empirical judgement, but a proposition which stands firm à priori. For before addressing myself to experience, I already have in my conception all the requisite conditions for the judgement, and I have only to extract the predicate from the conception, according to the principle of contradiction, and thereby at the same time become conscious of the necessity of the judgement, a necessity which I could never learn from experience. On the other hand, though at first I do not at all include the predicate of weight in my conception of body in general, that conception still indicates an object of experience, a part of the totality of experience, to which I can still add other parts; and this I do when I recognize by observation that bodies are heavy. I can cognize beforehand by analysis the conception of body through the characteristics of extension, impenetrability, shape, etc., all which are cogitated in this conception. But now I extend my knowledge, and looking back on experience from which I had derived this conception of body, I find weight at all times connected with the above characteristics, and therefore I synthetically add to my conceptions this as a predicate, and say, “All bodies are heavy.” Thus it is experience upon which rests the possibility of the synthesis of the predicate of weight with the conception of body, because both conceptions, although the one is not contained in the other, still belong to one another (only contingently, however), as parts of a whole, namely, of experience, which is itself a synthesis of intuitions.\n\nBut to synthetical judgements à priori, such aid is entirely wanting. If I go out of and beyond the conception A, in order to recognize another B as connected with it, what foundation have I to rest on, whereby to render the synthesis possible? I have here no longer the advantage of looking out in the sphere of experience for what I want. Let us take, for example, the proposition, “Everything that happens has a cause.” In the conception of “something that happens,” I indeed think an existence which a certain time antecedes, and from this I can derive analytical judgements. But the conception of a cause lies quite out of the above conception, and indicates something entirely different from “that which happens,” and is consequently not contained in that conception. How then am I able to assert concerning the general conception—“that which happens”—something entirely different from that conception, and to recognize the conception of cause although not contained in it, yet as belonging to it, and even necessarily? what is here the unknown = X, upon which the understanding rests when it believes it has found, out of the conception A a foreign predicate B, which it nevertheless considers to be connected with it? It cannot be experience, because the principle adduced annexes the two representations, cause and effect, to the representation existence, not only with universality, which experience cannot give, but also with the expression of necessity, therefore completely à priori and from pure conceptions. Upon such synthetical, that is augmentative propositions, depends the whole aim of our speculative knowledge à priori; for although analytical judgements are indeed highly important and necessary, they are so, only to arrive at that clearness of conceptions which is requisite for a sure and extended synthesis, and this alone is a real acquisition.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","text":"### IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.\n\nIn all judgements wherein the relation of a subject to the predicate is cogitated (I mention affirmative judgements only here; the application to negative will be very easy), this relation is possible in two different ways. Either the predicate B belongs to the subject A, as somewhat which is contained (though covertly) in the conception A; or the predicate B lies completely out of the conception A, although it stands in connection with it. In the first instance, I term the judgement analytical, in the second, synthetical. Analytical judgements (affirmative) are therefore those in which the connection of the predicate with the subject is cogitated through identity; those in which this connection is cogitated without identity, are called synthetical judgements. The former may be called explicative, the latter augmentative judgements; because the former add in the predicate nothing to the conception of the subject, but only analyse it into its constituent conceptions, which were thought already in the subject, although in a confused manner; the latter add to our conceptions of the subject a predicate which was not contained in it, and which no analysis could ever have discovered therein. For example, when I say, “All bodies are extended,” this is an analytical judgement. For I need not go beyond the conception of body in order to find extension connected with it, but merely analyse the conception, that is, become conscious of the manifold properties which I think in that conception, in order to discover this predicate in it: it is therefore an analytical judgement. On the other hand, when I say, “All bodies are heavy,” the predicate is something totally different from that which I think in the mere conception of a body. By the addition of such a predicate, therefore, it becomes a synthetical judgement.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"4.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","text":"Judgements of experience, as such, are always synthetical. For it would be absurd to think of grounding an analytical judgement on experience, because in forming such a judgement I need not go out of the sphere of my conceptions, and therefore recourse to the testimony of experience is quite unnecessary. That “bodies are extended” is not an empirical judgement, but a proposition which stands firm à priori. For before addressing myself to experience, I already have in my conception all the requisite conditions for the judgement, and I have only to extract the predicate from the conception, according to the principle of contradiction, and thereby at the same time become conscious of the necessity of the judgement, a necessity which I could never learn from experience. On the other hand, though at first I do not at all include the predicate of weight in my conception of body in general, that conception still indicates an object of experience, a part of the totality of experience, to which I can still add other parts; and this I do when I recognize by observation that bodies are heavy. I can cognize beforehand by analysis the conception of body through the characteristics of extension, impenetrability, shape, etc., all which are cogitated in this conception. But now I extend my knowledge, and looking back on experience from which I had derived this conception of body, I find weight at all times connected with the above characteristics, and therefore I synthetically add to my conceptions this as a predicate, and say, “All bodies are heavy.” Thus it is experience upon which rests the possibility of the synthesis of the predicate of weight with the conception of body, because both conceptions, although the one is not contained in the other, still belong to one another (only contingently, however), as parts of a whole, namely, of experience, which is itself a synthesis of intuitions.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"4.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","text":"But to synthetical judgements à priori, such aid is entirely wanting. If I go out of and beyond the conception A, in order to recognize another B as connected with it, what foundation have I to rest on, whereby to render the synthesis possible? I have here no longer the advantage of looking out in the sphere of experience for what I want. Let us take, for example, the proposition, “Everything that happens has a cause.” In the conception of “something that happens,” I indeed think an existence which a certain time antecedes, and from this I can derive analytical judgements. But the conception of a cause lies quite out of the above conception, and indicates something entirely different from “that which happens,” and is consequently not contained in that conception. How then am I able to assert concerning the general conception—“that which happens”—something entirely different from that conception, and to recognize the conception of cause although not contained in it, yet as belonging to it, and even necessarily? what is here the unknown = X, upon which the understanding rests when it believes it has found, out of the conception A a foreign predicate B, which it nevertheless considers to be connected with it? It cannot be experience, because the principle adduced annexes the two representations, cause and effect, to the representation existence, not only with universality, which experience cannot give, but also with the expression of necessity, therefore completely à priori and from pure conceptions. Upon such synthetical, that is augmentative propositions, depends the whole aim of our speculative knowledge à priori; for although analytical judgements are indeed highly important and necessary, they are so, only to arrive at that clearness of conceptions which is requisite for a sure and extended synthesis, and this alone is a real acquisition.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"4.3"}],"sha256":"ed338e8ab27dd14dd60d70fdf71c9576eb54ac70aaaf38949db36cd6c330ce59","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS","title":"V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.","order":5,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_VATS","text":"### V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.\n\n1. Mathematical judgements are always synthetical. Hitherto this fact, though incontestably true and very important in its consequences, seems to have escaped the analysts of the human mind, nay, to be in complete opposition to all their conjectures. For as it was found that mathematical conclusions all proceed according to the principle of contradiction (which the nature of every apodeictic certainty requires), people became persuaded that the fundamental principles of the science also were recognized and admitted in the same way. But the notion is fallacious; for although a synthetical proposition can certainly be discerned by means of the principle of contradiction, this is possible only when another synthetical proposition precedes, from which the latter is deduced, but never of itself.\n\nBefore all, be it observed, that proper mathematical propositions are always judgements à priori, and not empirical, because they carry along with them the conception of necessity, which cannot be given by experience. If this be demurred to, it matters not; I will then limit my assertion to pure mathematics, the very conception of which implies that it consists of knowledge altogether non-empirical and à priori.\n\nWe might, indeed at first suppose that the proposition 7 + 5 = 12 is a merely analytical proposition, following (according to the principle of contradiction) from the conception of a sum of seven and five. But if we regard it more narrowly, we find that our conception of the sum of seven and five contains nothing more than the uniting of both sums into one, whereby it cannot at all be cogitated what this single number is which embraces both. The conception of twelve is by no means obtained by merely cogitating the union of seven and five; and we may analyse our conception of such a possible sum as long as we will, still we shall never discover in it the notion of twelve. We must go beyond these conceptions, and have recourse to an intuition which corresponds to one of the two—our five fingers, for example, or like Segner in his Arithmetic five points, and so by degrees, add the units contained in the five given in the intuition, to the conception of seven. For I first take the number 7, and, for the conception of 5 calling in the aid of the fingers of my hand as objects of intuition, I add the units, which I before took together to make up the number 5, gradually now by means of the material image my hand, to the number 7, and by this process, I at length see the number 12 arise. That 7 should be added to 5, I have certainly cogitated in my conception of a sum = 7 + 5, but not that this sum was equal to 12. Arithmetical propositions are therefore always synthetical, of which we may become more clearly convinced by trying large numbers. For it will thus become quite evident that, turn and twist our conceptions as we may, it is impossible, without having recourse to intuition, to arrive at the sum total or product by means of the mere analysis of our conceptions. Just as little is any principle of pure geometry analytical. “A straight line between two points is the shortest,” is a synthetical proposition. For my conception of straight contains no notion of quantity, but is merely qualitative. The conception of the shortest is therefore fore wholly an addition, and by no analysis can it be extracted from our conception of a straight line. Intuition must therefore here lend its aid, by means of which, and thus only, our synthesis is possible.\n\nSome few principles preposited by geometricians are, indeed, really analytical, and depend on the principle of contradiction. They serve, however, like identical propositions, as links in the chain of method, not as principles—for example, a = a, the whole is equal to itself, or (a+b) —> a, the whole is greater than its part. And yet even these principles themselves, though they derive their validity from pure conceptions, are only admitted in mathematics because they can be presented in intuition. What causes us here commonly to believe that the predicate of such apodeictic judgements is already contained in our conception, and that the judgement is therefore analytical, is merely the equivocal nature of the expression. We must join in thought a certain predicate to a given conception, and this necessity cleaves already to the conception. But the question is, not what we must join in thought to the given conception, but what we really think therein, though only obscurely, and then it becomes manifest that the predicate pertains to these conceptions, necessarily indeed, yet not as thought in the conception itself, but by virtue of an intuition, which must be added to the conception.\n\n2. The science of natural philosophy (physics) contains in itself synthetical judgements à priori, as principles. I shall adduce two propositions. For instance, the proposition, “In all changes of the material world, the quantity of matter remains unchanged”; or, that, “In all communication of motion, action and reaction must always be equal.” In both of these, not only is the necessity, and therefore their origin à priori clear, but also that they are synthetical propositions. For in the conception of matter, I do not cogitate its permanency, but merely its presence in space, which it fills. I therefore really go out of and beyond the conception of matter, in order to think on to it something à priori, which I did not think in it. The proposition is therefore not analytical, but synthetical, and nevertheless conceived à priori; and so it is with regard to the other propositions of the pure part of natural philosophy.\n\n3. As to metaphysics, even if we look upon it merely as an attempted science, yet, from the nature of human reason, an indispensable one, we find that it must contain synthetical propositions à priori. It is not merely the duty of metaphysics to dissect, and thereby analytically to illustrate the conceptions which we form à priori of things; but we seek to widen the range of our à priori knowledge. For this purpose, we must avail ourselves of such principles as add something to the original conception—something not identical with, nor contained in it, and by means of synthetical judgements à priori, leave far behind us the limits of experience; for example, in the proposition, “the world must have a beginning,” and such like. Thus metaphysics, according to the proper aim of the science, consists merely of synthetical propositions à priori.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","text":"### V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.\n\n1. Mathematical judgements are always synthetical. Hitherto this fact, though incontestably true and very important in its consequences, seems to have escaped the analysts of the human mind, nay, to be in complete opposition to all their conjectures. For as it was found that mathematical conclusions all proceed according to the principle of contradiction (which the nature of every apodeictic certainty requires), people became persuaded that the fundamental principles of the science also were recognized and admitted in the same way. But the notion is fallacious; for although a synthetical proposition can certainly be discerned by means of the principle of contradiction, this is possible only when another synthetical proposition precedes, from which the latter is deduced, but never of itself.\n\nBefore all, be it observed, that proper mathematical propositions are always judgements à priori, and not empirical, because they carry along with them the conception of necessity, which cannot be given by experience. If this be demurred to, it matters not; I will then limit my assertion to pure mathematics, the very conception of which implies that it consists of knowledge altogether non-empirical and à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"5.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","text":"We might, indeed at first suppose that the proposition 7 + 5 = 12 is a merely analytical proposition, following (according to the principle of contradiction) from the conception of a sum of seven and five. But if we regard it more narrowly, we find that our conception of the sum of seven and five contains nothing more than the uniting of both sums into one, whereby it cannot at all be cogitated what this single number is which embraces both. The conception of twelve is by no means obtained by merely cogitating the union of seven and five; and we may analyse our conception of such a possible sum as long as we will, still we shall never discover in it the notion of twelve. We must go beyond these conceptions, and have recourse to an intuition which corresponds to one of the two—our five fingers, for example, or like Segner in his Arithmetic five points, and so by degrees, add the units contained in the five given in the intuition, to the conception of seven. For I first take the number 7, and, for the conception of 5 calling in the aid of the fingers of my hand as objects of intuition, I add the units, which I before took together to make up the number 5, gradually now by means of the material image my hand, to the number 7, and by this process, I at length see the number 12 arise. That 7 should be added to 5, I have certainly cogitated in my conception of a sum = 7 + 5, but not that this sum was equal to 12. Arithmetical propositions are therefore always synthetical, of which we may become more clearly convinced by trying large numbers. For it will thus become quite evident that, turn and twist our conceptions as we may, it is impossible, without having recourse to intuition, to arrive at the sum total or product by means of the mere analysis of our conceptions. Just as little is any principle of pure geometry analytical. “A straight line between two points is the shortest,” is a synthetical proposition. For my conception of straight contains no notion of quantity, but is merely qualitative. The conception of the shortest is therefore fore wholly an addition, and by no analysis can it be extracted from our conception of a straight line. Intuition must therefore here lend its aid, by means of which, and thus only, our synthesis is possible.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"5.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","text":"Some few principles preposited by geometricians are, indeed, really analytical, and depend on the principle of contradiction. They serve, however, like identical propositions, as links in the chain of method, not as principles—for example, a = a, the whole is equal to itself, or (a+b) —> a, the whole is greater than its part. And yet even these principles themselves, though they derive their validity from pure conceptions, are only admitted in mathematics because they can be presented in intuition. What causes us here commonly to believe that the predicate of such apodeictic judgements is already contained in our conception, and that the judgement is therefore analytical, is merely the equivocal nature of the expression. We must join in thought a certain predicate to a given conception, and this necessity cleaves already to the conception. But the question is, not what we must join in thought to the given conception, but what we really think therein, though only obscurely, and then it becomes manifest that the predicate pertains to these conceptions, necessarily indeed, yet not as thought in the conception itself, but by virtue of an intuition, which must be added to the conception.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"5.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","text":"2. The science of natural philosophy (physics) contains in itself synthetical judgements à priori, as principles. I shall adduce two propositions. For instance, the proposition, “In all changes of the material world, the quantity of matter remains unchanged”; or, that, “In all communication of motion, action and reaction must always be equal.” In both of these, not only is the necessity, and therefore their origin à priori clear, but also that they are synthetical propositions. For in the conception of matter, I do not cogitate its permanency, but merely its presence in space, which it fills. I therefore really go out of and beyond the conception of matter, in order to think on to it something à priori, which I did not think in it. The proposition is therefore not analytical, but synthetical, and nevertheless conceived à priori; and so it is with regard to the other propositions of the pure part of natural philosophy.\n\n3. As to metaphysics, even if we look upon it merely as an attempted science, yet, from the nature of human reason, an indispensable one, we find that it must contain synthetical propositions à priori. It is not merely the duty of metaphysics to dissect, and thereby analytically to illustrate the conceptions which we form à priori of things; but we seek to widen the range of our à priori knowledge. For this purpose, we must avail ourselves of such principles as add something to the original conception—something not identical with, nor contained in it, and by means of synthetical judgements à priori, leave far behind us the limits of experience; for example, in the proposition, “the world must have a beginning,” and such like. Thus metaphysics, according to the proper aim of the science, consists merely of synthetical propositions à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"5.4"}],"sha256":"248a2b7aa1eb6c0f0c47d90226b98c736451a976ee57bcca7322add9ac66cb5e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP","title":"VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.","order":6,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_VUPP","text":"### VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.\n\nIt is extremely advantageous to be able to bring a number of investigations under the formula of a single problem. For in this manner, we not only facilitate our own labour, inasmuch as we define it clearly to ourselves, but also render it more easy for others to decide whether we have done justice to our undertaking. The proper problem of pure reason, then, is contained in the question: “How are synthetical judgements à priori possible?”\n\nThat metaphysical science has hitherto remained in so vacillating a state of uncertainty and contradiction, is only to be attributed to the fact that this great problem, and perhaps even the difference between analytical and synthetical judgements, did not sooner suggest itself to philosophers. Upon the solution of this problem, or upon sufficient proof of the impossibility of synthetical knowledge à priori, depends the existence or downfall of the science of metaphysics. Among philosophers, David Hume came the nearest of all to this problem; yet it never acquired in his mind sufficient precision, nor did he regard the question in its universality. On the contrary, he stopped short at the synthetical proposition of the connection of an effect with its cause (principium causalitatis), insisting that such proposition à priori was impossible. According to his conclusions, then, all that we term metaphysical science is a mere delusion, arising from the fancied insight of reason into that which is in truth borrowed from experience, and to which habit has given the appearance of necessity. Against this assertion, destructive to all pure philosophy, he would have been guarded, had he had our problem before his eyes in its universality. For he would then have perceived that, according to his own argument, there likewise could not be any pure mathematical science, which assuredly cannot exist without synthetical propositions à priori—an absurdity from which his good understanding must have saved him.\n\nIn the solution of the above problem is at the same time comprehended the possibility of the use of pure reason in the foundation and construction of all sciences which contain theoretical knowledge à priori of objects, that is to say, the answer to the following questions:\n\nHow is pure mathematical science possible?\n\nHow is pure natural science possible?\n\nRespecting these sciences, as they do certainly exist, it may with propriety be asked, how they are possible?—for that they must be possible is shown by the fact of their really existing.[[9]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-9) But as to metaphysics, the miserable progress it has hitherto made, and the fact that of no one system yet brought forward, far as regards its true aim, can it be said that this science really exists, leaves any one at liberty to doubt with reason the very possibility of its existence.\n\n[[9]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-9) As to the existence of pure natural science, or physics, perhaps many may still express doubts. But we have only to look at the different propositions which are commonly treated of at the commencement of proper (empirical) physical science—those, for example, relating to the permanence of the same quantity of matter, the vis inertiae, the equality of action and reaction, etc.—to be soon convinced that they form a science of pure physics (physica pura, or rationalis), which well deserves to be separately exposed as a special science, in its whole extent, whether that be great or confined.\n\nYet, in a certain sense, this kind of knowledge must unquestionably be looked upon as given; in other words, metaphysics must be considered as really existing, if not as a science, nevertheless as a natural disposition of the human mind (metaphysica naturalis). For human reason, without any instigations imputable to the mere vanity of great knowledge, unceasingly progresses, urged on by its own feeling of need, towards such questions as cannot be answered by any empirical application of reason, or principles derived therefrom; and so there has ever really existed in every man some system of metaphysics. It will always exist, so soon as reason awakes to the exercise of its power of speculation. And now the question arises: “How is metaphysics, as a natural disposition, possible?” In other words, how, from the nature of universal human reason, do those questions arise which pure reason proposes to itself, and which it is impelled by its own feeling of need to answer as well as it can?\n\nBut as in all the attempts hitherto made to answer the questions which reason is prompted by its very nature to propose to itself, for example, whether the world had a beginning, or has existed from eternity, it has always met with unavoidable contradictions, we must not rest satisfied with the mere natural disposition of the mind to metaphysics, that is, with the existence of the faculty of pure reason, whence, indeed, some sort of metaphysical system always arises; but it must be possible to arrive at certainty in regard to the question whether we know or do not know the things of which metaphysics treats. We must be able to arrive at a decision on the subjects of its questions, or on the ability or inability of reason to form any judgement respecting them; and therefore either to extend with confidence the bounds of our pure reason, or to set strictly defined and safe limits to its action. This last question, which arises out of the above universal problem, would properly run thus: “How is metaphysics possible as a science?”\n\nThus, the critique of reason leads at last, naturally and necessarily, to science; and, on the other hand, the dogmatical use of reason without criticism leads to groundless assertions, against which others equally specious can always be set, thus ending unavoidably in scepticism.\n\nBesides, this science cannot be of great and formidable prolixity, because it has not to do with objects of reason, the variety of which is inexhaustible, but merely with Reason herself and her problems; problems which arise out of her own bosom, and are not proposed to her by the nature of outward things, but by her own nature. And when once Reason has previously become able completely to understand her own power in regard to objects which she meets with in experience, it will be easy to determine securely the extent and limits of her attempted application to objects beyond the confines of experience.\n\nWe may and must, therefore, regard the attempts hitherto made to establish metaphysical science dogmatically as non-existent. For what of analysis, that is, mere dissection of conceptions, is contained in one or other, is not the aim of, but only a preparation for metaphysics proper, which has for its object the extension, by means of synthesis, of our à priori knowledge. And for this purpose, mere analysis is of course useless, because it only shows what is contained in these conceptions, but not how we arrive, à priori, at them; and this it is her duty to show, in order to be able afterwards to determine their valid use in regard to all objects of experience, to all knowledge in general. But little self-denial, indeed, is needed to give up these pretensions, seeing the undeniable, and in the dogmatic mode of procedure, inevitable contradictions of Reason with herself, have long since ruined the reputation of every system of metaphysics that has appeared up to this time. It will require more firmness to remain undeterred by difficulty from within, and opposition from without, from endeavouring, by a method quite opposed to all those hitherto followed, to further the growth and fruitfulness of a science indispensable to human reason—a science from which every branch it has borne may be cut away, but whose roots remain indestructible.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","text":"### VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.\n\nIt is extremely advantageous to be able to bring a number of investigations under the formula of a single problem. For in this manner, we not only facilitate our own labour, inasmuch as we define it clearly to ourselves, but also render it more easy for others to decide whether we have done justice to our undertaking. The proper problem of pure reason, then, is contained in the question: “How are synthetical judgements à priori possible?”\n\nThat metaphysical science has hitherto remained in so vacillating a state of uncertainty and contradiction, is only to be attributed to the fact that this great problem, and perhaps even the difference between analytical and synthetical judgements, did not sooner suggest itself to philosophers. Upon the solution of this problem, or upon sufficient proof of the impossibility of synthetical knowledge à priori, depends the existence or downfall of the science of metaphysics. Among philosophers, David Hume came the nearest of all to this problem; yet it never acquired in his mind sufficient precision, nor did he regard the question in its universality. On the contrary, he stopped short at the synthetical proposition of the connection of an effect with its cause (principium causalitatis), insisting that such proposition à priori was impossible. According to his conclusions, then, all that we term metaphysical science is a mere delusion, arising from the fancied insight of reason into that which is in truth borrowed from experience, and to which habit has given the appearance of necessity. Against this assertion, destructive to all pure philosophy, he would have been guarded, had he had our problem before his eyes in its universality. For he would then have perceived that, according to his own argument, there likewise could not be any pure mathematical science, which assuredly cannot exist without synthetical propositions à priori—an absurdity from which his good understanding must have saved him.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"6.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","text":"In the solution of the above problem is at the same time comprehended the possibility of the use of pure reason in the foundation and construction of all sciences which contain theoretical knowledge à priori of objects, that is to say, the answer to the following questions:\n\nHow is pure mathematical science possible?\n\nHow is pure natural science possible?\n\nRespecting these sciences, as they do certainly exist, it may with propriety be asked, how they are possible?—for that they must be possible is shown by the fact of their really existing.[[9]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-9) But as to metaphysics, the miserable progress it has hitherto made, and the fact that of no one system yet brought forward, far as regards its true aim, can it be said that this science really exists, leaves any one at liberty to doubt with reason the very possibility of its existence.\n\n[[9]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-9) As to the existence of pure natural science, or physics, perhaps many may still express doubts. But we have only to look at the different propositions which are commonly treated of at the commencement of proper (empirical) physical science—those, for example, relating to the permanence of the same quantity of matter, the vis inertiae, the equality of action and reaction, etc.—to be soon convinced that they form a science of pure physics (physica pura, or rationalis), which well deserves to be separately exposed as a special science, in its whole extent, whether that be great or confined.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"6.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","text":"Yet, in a certain sense, this kind of knowledge must unquestionably be looked upon as given; in other words, metaphysics must be considered as really existing, if not as a science, nevertheless as a natural disposition of the human mind (metaphysica naturalis). For human reason, without any instigations imputable to the mere vanity of great knowledge, unceasingly progresses, urged on by its own feeling of need, towards such questions as cannot be answered by any empirical application of reason, or principles derived therefrom; and so there has ever really existed in every man some system of metaphysics. It will always exist, so soon as reason awakes to the exercise of its power of speculation. And now the question arises: “How is metaphysics, as a natural disposition, possible?” In other words, how, from the nature of universal human reason, do those questions arise which pure reason proposes to itself, and which it is impelled by its own feeling of need to answer as well as it can?","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"6.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","text":"But as in all the attempts hitherto made to answer the questions which reason is prompted by its very nature to propose to itself, for example, whether the world had a beginning, or has existed from eternity, it has always met with unavoidable contradictions, we must not rest satisfied with the mere natural disposition of the mind to metaphysics, that is, with the existence of the faculty of pure reason, whence, indeed, some sort of metaphysical system always arises; but it must be possible to arrive at certainty in regard to the question whether we know or do not know the things of which metaphysics treats. We must be able to arrive at a decision on the subjects of its questions, or on the ability or inability of reason to form any judgement respecting them; and therefore either to extend with confidence the bounds of our pure reason, or to set strictly defined and safe limits to its action. This last question, which arises out of the above universal problem, would properly run thus: “How is metaphysics possible as a science?”\n\nThus, the critique of reason leads at last, naturally and necessarily, to science; and, on the other hand, the dogmatical use of reason without criticism leads to groundless assertions, against which others equally specious can always be set, thus ending unavoidably in scepticism.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"6.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","text":"Besides, this science cannot be of great and formidable prolixity, because it has not to do with objects of reason, the variety of which is inexhaustible, but merely with Reason herself and her problems; problems which arise out of her own bosom, and are not proposed to her by the nature of outward things, but by her own nature. And when once Reason has previously become able completely to understand her own power in regard to objects which she meets with in experience, it will be easy to determine securely the extent and limits of her attempted application to objects beyond the confines of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"6.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","text":"We may and must, therefore, regard the attempts hitherto made to establish metaphysical science dogmatically as non-existent. For what of analysis, that is, mere dissection of conceptions, is contained in one or other, is not the aim of, but only a preparation for metaphysics proper, which has for its object the extension, by means of synthesis, of our à priori knowledge. And for this purpose, mere analysis is of course useless, because it only shows what is contained in these conceptions, but not how we arrive, à priori, at them; and this it is her duty to show, in order to be able afterwards to determine their valid use in regard to all objects of experience, to all knowledge in general. But little self-denial, indeed, is needed to give up these pretensions, seeing the undeniable, and in the dogmatic mode of procedure, inevitable contradictions of Reason with herself, have long since ruined the reputation of every system of metaphysics that has appeared up to this time. It will require more firmness to remain undeterred by difficulty from within, and opposition from without, from endeavouring, by a method quite opposed to all those hitherto followed, to further the growth and fruitfulness of a science indispensable to human reason—a science from which every branch it has borne may be cut away, but whose roots remain indestructible.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"6.6"}],"sha256":"19219f9a535e1eb43e946ed168b00daa983e59dc42b83721c5cef681df341863","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP","title":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.","order":7,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_VIDP","text":"### VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.\n\nFrom all that has been said, there results the idea of a particular science, which may be called the Critique of Pure Reason. For reason is the faculty which furnishes us with the principles of knowledge à priori. Hence, pure reason is the faculty which contains the principles of cognizing anything absolutely à priori. An organon of pure reason would be a compendium of those principles according to which alone all pure cognitions à priori can be obtained. The completely extended application of such an organon would afford us a system of pure reason. As this, however, is demanding a great deal, and it is yet doubtful whether any extension of our knowledge be here possible, or, if so, in what cases; we can regard a science of the mere criticism of pure reason, its sources and limits, as the propædeutic to a system of pure reason. Such a science must not be called a doctrine, but only a critique of pure reason; and its use, in regard to speculation, would be only negative, not to enlarge the bounds of, but to purify, our reason, and to shield it against error—which alone is no little gain. I apply the term transcendental to all knowledge which is not so much occupied with objects as with the mode of our cognition of these objects, so far as this mode of cognition is possible à priori. A system of such conceptions would be called transcendental philosophy. But this, again, is still beyond the bounds of our present essay. For as such a science must contain a complete exposition not only of our synthetical à priori, but of our analytical à priori knowledge, it is of too wide a range for our present purpose, because we do not require to carry our analysis any farther than is necessary to understand, in their full extent, the principles of synthesis à priori, with which alone we have to do. This investigation, which we cannot properly call a doctrine, but only a transcendental critique, because it aims not at the enlargement, but at the correction and guidance, of our knowledge, and is to serve as a touchstone of the worth or worthlessness of all knowledge à priori, is the sole object of our present essay. Such a critique is consequently, as far as possible, a preparation for an organon; and if this new organon should be found to fail, at least for a canon of pure reason, according to which the complete system of the philosophy of pure reason, whether it extend or limit the bounds of that reason, might one day be set forth both analytically and synthetically. For that this is possible, nay, that such a system is not of so great extent as to preclude the hope of its ever being completed, is evident. For we have not here to do with the nature of outward objects, which is infinite, but solely with the mind, which judges of the nature of objects, and, again, with the mind only in respect of its cognition à priori. And the object of our investigations, as it is not to be sought without, but, altogether within, ourselves, cannot remain concealed, and in all probability is limited enough to be completely surveyed and fairly estimated, according to its worth or worthlessness. Still less let the reader here expect a critique of books and systems of pure reason; our present object is exclusively a critique of the faculty of pure reason itself. Only when we make this critique our foundation, do we possess a pure touchstone for estimating the philosophical value of ancient and modern writings on this subject; and without this criterion, the incompetent historian or judge decides upon and corrects the groundless assertions of others with his own, which have themselves just as little foundation.\n\nTranscendental philosophy is the idea of a science, for which the Critique of Pure Reason must sketch the whole plan architectonically, that is, from principles, with a full guarantee for the validity and stability of all the parts which enter into the building. It is the system of all the principles of pure reason. If this Critique itself does not assume the title of transcendental philosophy, it is only because, to be a complete system, it ought to contain a full analysis of all human knowledge à priori. Our critique must, indeed, lay before us a complete enumeration of all the radical conceptions which constitute the said pure knowledge. But from the complete analysis of these conceptions themselves, as also from a complete investigation of those derived from them, it abstains with reason; partly because it would be deviating from the end in view to occupy itself with this analysis, since this process is not attended with the difficulty and insecurity to be found in the synthesis, to which our critique is entirely devoted, and partly because it would be inconsistent with the unity of our plan to burden this essay with the vindication of the completeness of such an analysis and deduction, with which, after all, we have at present nothing to do. This completeness of the analysis of these radical conceptions, as well as of the deduction from the conceptions à priori which may be given by the analysis, we can, however, easily attain, provided only that we are in possession of all these radical conceptions, which are to serve as principles of the synthesis, and that in respect of this main purpose nothing is wanting.\n\nTo the Critique of Pure Reason, therefore, belongs all that constitutes transcendental philosophy; and it is the complete idea of transcendental philosophy, but still not the science itself; because it only proceeds so far with the analysis as is necessary to the power of judging completely of our synthetical knowledge à priori.\n\nThe principal thing we must attend to, in the division of the parts of a science like this, is that no conceptions must enter it which contain aught empirical; in other words, that the knowledge à priori must be completely pure. Hence, although the highest principles and fundamental conceptions of morality are certainly cognitions à priori, yet they do not belong to transcendental philosophy; because, though they certainly do not lay the conceptions of pain, pleasure, desires, inclinations, etc. (which are all of empirical origin), at the foundation of its precepts, yet still into the conception of duty—as an obstacle to be overcome, or as an incitement which should not be made into a motive—these empirical conceptions must necessarily enter, in the construction of a system of pure morality. Transcendental philosophy is consequently a philosophy of the pure and merely speculative reason. For all that is practical, so far as it contains motives, relates to feelings, and these belong to empirical sources of cognition.\n\nIf we wish to divide this science from the universal point of view of a science in general, it ought to comprehend, first, a Doctrine of the Elements, and, secondly, a Doctrine of the Method of pure reason. Each of these main divisions will have its subdivisions, the separate reasons for which we cannot here particularize. Only so much seems necessary, by way of introduction of premonition, that there are two sources of human knowledge (which probably spring from a common, but to us unknown root), namely, sense and understanding. By the former, objects are given to us; by the latter, thought. So far as the faculty of sense may contain representations à priori, which form the conditions under which objects are given, in so far it belongs to transcendental philosophy. The transcendental doctrine of sense must form the first part of our science of elements, because the conditions under which alone the objects of human knowledge are given must precede those under which they are thought.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","text":"### VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.\n\nFrom all that has been said, there results the idea of a particular science, which may be called the Critique of Pure Reason. For reason is the faculty which furnishes us with the principles of knowledge à priori. Hence, pure reason is the faculty which contains the principles of cognizing anything absolutely à priori. An organon of pure reason would be a compendium of those principles according to which alone all pure cognitions à priori can be obtained. The completely extended application of such an organon would afford us a system of pure reason. As this, however, is demanding a great deal, and it is yet doubtful whether any extension of our knowledge be here possible, or, if so, in what cases; we can regard a science of the mere criticism of pure reason, its sources and limits, as the propædeutic to a system of pure reason. Such a science must not be called a doctrine, but only a critique of pure reason; and its use, in regard to speculation, would be only negative, not to enlarge the bounds of, but to purify, our reason, and to shield it against error—which alone is no little gain. I apply the term transcendental to all knowledge which is not so much occupied with objects as with the mode of our cognition of these objects, so far as this mode of cognition is possible à priori. A system of such conceptions would be called transcendental philosophy. But this, again, is still beyond the bounds of our present essay.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"7.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","text":"For as such a science must contain a complete exposition not only of our synthetical à priori, but of our analytical à priori knowledge, it is of too wide a range for our present purpose, because we do not require to carry our analysis any farther than is necessary to understand, in their full extent, the principles of synthesis à priori, with which alone we have to do. This investigation, which we cannot properly call a doctrine, but only a transcendental critique, because it aims not at the enlargement, but at the correction and guidance, of our knowledge, and is to serve as a touchstone of the worth or worthlessness of all knowledge à priori, is the sole object of our present essay. Such a critique is consequently, as far as possible, a preparation for an organon; and if this new organon should be found to fail, at least for a canon of pure reason, according to which the complete system of the philosophy of pure reason, whether it extend or limit the bounds of that reason, might one day be set forth both analytically and synthetically. For that this is possible, nay, that such a system is not of so great extent as to preclude the hope of its ever being completed, is evident. For we have not here to do with the nature of outward objects, which is infinite, but solely with the mind, which judges of the nature of objects, and, again, with the mind only in respect of its cognition à priori. And the object of our investigations, as it is not to be sought without, but, altogether within, ourselves, cannot remain concealed, and in all probability is limited enough to be completely surveyed and fairly estimated, according to its worth or worthlessness.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"7.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0003","text":"Still less let the reader here expect a critique of books and systems of pure reason; our present object is exclusively a critique of the faculty of pure reason itself. Only when we make this critique our foundation, do we possess a pure touchstone for estimating the philosophical value of ancient and modern writings on this subject; and without this criterion, the incompetent historian or judge decides upon and corrects the groundless assertions of others with his own, which have themselves just as little foundation.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"7.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","text":"Transcendental philosophy is the idea of a science, for which the Critique of Pure Reason must sketch the whole plan architectonically, that is, from principles, with a full guarantee for the validity and stability of all the parts which enter into the building. It is the system of all the principles of pure reason. If this Critique itself does not assume the title of transcendental philosophy, it is only because, to be a complete system, it ought to contain a full analysis of all human knowledge à priori. Our critique must, indeed, lay before us a complete enumeration of all the radical conceptions which constitute the said pure knowledge. But from the complete analysis of these conceptions themselves, as also from a complete investigation of those derived from them, it abstains with reason; partly because it would be deviating from the end in view to occupy itself with this analysis, since this process is not attended with the difficulty and insecurity to be found in the synthesis, to which our critique is entirely devoted, and partly because it would be inconsistent with the unity of our plan to burden this essay with the vindication of the completeness of such an analysis and deduction, with which, after all, we have at present nothing to do. This completeness of the analysis of these radical conceptions, as well as of the deduction from the conceptions à priori which may be given by the analysis, we can, however, easily attain, provided only that we are in possession of all these radical conceptions, which are to serve as principles of the synthesis, and that in respect of this main purpose nothing is wanting.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"7.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","text":"To the Critique of Pure Reason, therefore, belongs all that constitutes transcendental philosophy; and it is the complete idea of transcendental philosophy, but still not the science itself; because it only proceeds so far with the analysis as is necessary to the power of judging completely of our synthetical knowledge à priori.\n\nThe principal thing we must attend to, in the division of the parts of a science like this, is that no conceptions must enter it which contain aught empirical; in other words, that the knowledge à priori must be completely pure. Hence, although the highest principles and fundamental conceptions of morality are certainly cognitions à priori, yet they do not belong to transcendental philosophy; because, though they certainly do not lay the conceptions of pain, pleasure, desires, inclinations, etc. (which are all of empirical origin), at the foundation of its precepts, yet still into the conception of duty—as an obstacle to be overcome, or as an incitement which should not be made into a motive—these empirical conceptions must necessarily enter, in the construction of a system of pure morality. Transcendental philosophy is consequently a philosophy of the pure and merely speculative reason. For all that is practical, so far as it contains motives, relates to feelings, and these belong to empirical sources of cognition.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"7.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","text":"If we wish to divide this science from the universal point of view of a science in general, it ought to comprehend, first, a Doctrine of the Elements, and, secondly, a Doctrine of the Method of pure reason. Each of these main divisions will have its subdivisions, the separate reasons for which we cannot here particularize. Only so much seems necessary, by way of introduction of premonition, that there are two sources of human knowledge (which probably spring from a common, but to us unknown root), namely, sense and understanding. By the former, objects are given to us; by the latter, thought. So far as the faculty of sense may contain representations à priori, which form the conditions under which objects are given, in so far it belongs to transcendental philosophy. The transcendental doctrine of sense must form the first part of our science of elements, because the conditions under which alone the objects of human knowledge are given must precede those under which they are thought.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"7.6"}],"sha256":"78be5cf4ffc44a178346df21ce30ef78bd52610218e2b112459e8e3986ca4304","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT","title":"§ I. Introductory.","order":8,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_IINT","text":"## I. TRANSCENDENTAL DOCTRINE OF ELEMENTS.\n\n### FIRST PART. TRANSCENDENTAL ÆSTHETIC.\n\n### § I. Introductory.\n\nIn whatsoever mode, or by whatsoever means, our knowledge may relate to objects, it is at least quite clear that the only manner in which it immediately relates to them is by means of an intuition. To this as the indispensable groundwork, all thought points. But an intuition can take place only in so far as the object is given to us. This, again, is only possible, to man at least, on condition that the object affect the mind in a certain manner. The capacity for receiving representations (receptivity) through the mode in which we are affected by objects, objects, is called sensibility. By means of sensibility, therefore, objects are given to us, and it alone furnishes us with intuitions; by the understanding they are thought, and from it arise conceptions. But an thought must directly, or indirectly, by means of certain signs, relate ultimately to intuitions; consequently, with us, to sensibility, because in no other way can an object be given to us.\n\nThe effect of an object upon the faculty of representation, so far as we are affected by the said object, is sensation. That sort of intuition which relates to an object by means of sensation is called an empirical intuition. The undetermined object of an empirical intuition is called phenomenon. That which in the phenomenon corresponds to the sensation, I term its matter; but that which effects that the content of the phenomenon can be arranged under certain relations, I call its form. But that in which our sensations are merely arranged, and by which they are susceptible of assuming a certain form, cannot be itself sensation. It is, then, the matter of all phenomena that is given to us à posteriori; the form must lie ready à priori for them in the mind, and consequently can be regarded separately from all sensation.\n\nI call all representations pure, in the transcendental meaning of the word, wherein nothing is met with that belongs to sensation. And accordingly we find existing in the mind à priori, the pure form of sensuous intuitions in general, in which all the manifold content of the phenomenal world is arranged and viewed under certain relations. This pure form of sensibility I shall call pure intuition. Thus, if I take away from our representation of a body all that the understanding thinks as belonging to it, as substance, force, divisibility, etc., and also whatever belongs to sensation, as impenetrability, hardness, colour, etc.; yet there is still something left us from this empirical intuition, namely, extension and shape. These belong to pure intuition, which exists à priori in the mind, as a mere form of sensibility, and without any real object of the senses or any sensation.\n\nThe science of all the principles of sensibility à priori, I call transcendental æsthetic.[[10]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-10) There must, then, be such a science forming the first part of the transcendental doctrine of elements, in contradistinction to that part which contains the principles of pure thought, and which is called transcendental logic.\n\n[[10]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-10) The Germans are the only people who at present use this word to indicate what others call the critique of taste. At the foundation of this term lies the disappointed hope, which the eminent analyst, Baumgarten, conceived, of subjecting the criticism of the beautiful to principles of reason, and so of elevating its rules into a science. But his endeavours were vain. For the said rules or criteria are, in respect to their chief sources, merely empirical, consequently never can serve as determinate laws à priori, by which our judgement in matters of taste is to be directed. It is rather our judgement which forms the proper test as to the correctness of the principles. On this account it is advisable to give up the use of the term as designating the critique of taste, and to apply it solely to that doctrine, which is true science—the science of the laws of sensibility—and thus come nearer to the language and the sense of the ancients in their well-known division of the objects of cognition into aiotheta kai noeta, or to share it with speculative philosophy, and employ it partly in a transcendental, partly in a psychological signification.\n\nIn the science of transcendental æsthetic accordingly, we shall first isolate sensibility or the sensuous faculty, by separating from it all that is annexed to its perceptions by the conceptions of understanding, so that nothing be left but empirical intuition. In the next place we shall take away from this intuition all that belongs to sensation, so that nothing may remain but pure intuition, and the mere form of phenomena, which is all that the sensibility can afford à priori. From this investigation it will be found that there are two pure forms of sensuous intuition, as principles of knowledge à priori, namely, space and time. To the consideration of these we shall now proceed.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","text":"## I. TRANSCENDENTAL DOCTRINE OF ELEMENTS.\n\n### FIRST PART. TRANSCENDENTAL ÆSTHETIC.\n\n### § I. Introductory.\n\nIn whatsoever mode, or by whatsoever means, our knowledge may relate to objects, it is at least quite clear that the only manner in which it immediately relates to them is by means of an intuition. To this as the indispensable groundwork, all thought points. But an intuition can take place only in so far as the object is given to us. This, again, is only possible, to man at least, on condition that the object affect the mind in a certain manner. The capacity for receiving representations (receptivity) through the mode in which we are affected by objects, objects, is called sensibility. By means of sensibility, therefore, objects are given to us, and it alone furnishes us with intuitions; by the understanding they are thought, and from it arise conceptions. But an thought must directly, or indirectly, by means of certain signs, relate ultimately to intuitions; consequently, with us, to sensibility, because in no other way can an object be given to us.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"8.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","text":"The effect of an object upon the faculty of representation, so far as we are affected by the said object, is sensation. That sort of intuition which relates to an object by means of sensation is called an empirical intuition. The undetermined object of an empirical intuition is called phenomenon. That which in the phenomenon corresponds to the sensation, I term its matter; but that which effects that the content of the phenomenon can be arranged under certain relations, I call its form. But that in which our sensations are merely arranged, and by which they are susceptible of assuming a certain form, cannot be itself sensation. It is, then, the matter of all phenomena that is given to us à posteriori; the form must lie ready à priori for them in the mind, and consequently can be regarded separately from all sensation.\n\nI call all representations pure, in the transcendental meaning of the word, wherein nothing is met with that belongs to sensation. And accordingly we find existing in the mind à priori, the pure form of sensuous intuitions in general, in which all the manifold content of the phenomenal world is arranged and viewed under certain relations. This pure form of sensibility I shall call pure intuition. Thus, if I take away from our representation of a body all that the understanding thinks as belonging to it, as substance, force, divisibility, etc., and also whatever belongs to sensation, as impenetrability, hardness, colour, etc.; yet there is still something left us from this empirical intuition, namely, extension and shape. These belong to pure intuition, which exists à priori in the mind, as a mere form of sensibility, and without any real object of the senses or any sensation.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"8.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","text":"The science of all the principles of sensibility à priori, I call transcendental æsthetic.[[10]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-10) There must, then, be such a science forming the first part of the transcendental doctrine of elements, in contradistinction to that part which contains the principles of pure thought, and which is called transcendental logic.\n\n[[10]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-10) The Germans are the only people who at present use this word to indicate what others call the critique of taste. At the foundation of this term lies the disappointed hope, which the eminent analyst, Baumgarten, conceived, of subjecting the criticism of the beautiful to principles of reason, and so of elevating its rules into a science. But his endeavours were vain. For the said rules or criteria are, in respect to their chief sources, merely empirical, consequently never can serve as determinate laws à priori, by which our judgement in matters of taste is to be directed. It is rather our judgement which forms the proper test as to the correctness of the principles. On this account it is advisable to give up the use of the term as designating the critique of taste, and to apply it solely to that doctrine, which is true science—the science of the laws of sensibility—and thus come nearer to the language and the sense of the ancients in their well-known division of the objects of cognition into aiotheta kai noeta, or to share it with speculative philosophy, and employ it partly in a transcendental, partly in a psychological signification.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"8.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","text":"In the science of transcendental æsthetic accordingly, we shall first isolate sensibility or the sensuous faculty, by separating from it all that is annexed to its perceptions by the conceptions of understanding, so that nothing be left but empirical intuition. In the next place we shall take away from this intuition all that belongs to sensation, so that nothing may remain but pure intuition, and the mere form of phenomena, which is all that the sensibility can afford à priori. From this investigation it will be found that there are two pure forms of sensuous intuition, as principles of knowledge à priori, namely, space and time. To the consideration of these we shall now proceed.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"8.4"}],"sha256":"3a8ddc8d60ea99e3c80f6aa2a979988b50bff6cd8bf0e9f783bd4a4f989fa05b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET","title":"§ 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","order":9,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_2MET","text":"### SECTION I. Of Space.\n\n### § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.\n\nBy means of the external sense (a property of the mind), we represent to ourselves objects as without us, and these all in space. Herein alone are their shape, dimensions, and relations to each other determined or determinable. The internal sense, by means of which the mind contemplates itself or its internal state, gives, indeed, no intuition of the soul as an object; yet there is nevertheless a determinate form, under which alone the contemplation of our internal state is possible, so that all which relates to the inward determinations of the mind is represented in relations of time. Of time we cannot have any external intuition, any more than we can have an internal intuition of space. What then are time and space? Are they real existences? Or, are they merely relations or determinations of things, such, however, as would equally belong to these things in themselves, though they should never become objects of intuition; or, are they such as belong only to the form of intuition, and consequently to the subjective constitution of the mind, without which these predicates of time and space could not be attached to any object? In order to become informed on these points, we shall first give an exposition of the conception of space. By exposition, I mean the clear, though not detailed, representation of that which belongs to a conception; and an exposition is metaphysical when it contains that which represents the conception as given à priori.\n\n1. Space is not a conception which has been derived from outward experiences. For, in order that certain sensations may relate to something without me (that is, to something which occupies a different part of space from that in which I am); in like manner, in order that I may represent them not merely as without, of, and near to each other, but also in separate places, the representation of space must already exist as a foundation. Consequently, the representation of space cannot be borrowed from the relations of external phenomena through experience; but, on the contrary, this external experience is itself only possible through the said antecedent representation.\n\n2. Space then is a necessary representation à priori, which serves for the foundation of all external intuitions. We never can imagine or make a representation to ourselves of the non-existence of space, though we may easily enough think that no objects are found in it. It must, therefore, be considered as the condition of the possibility of phenomena, and by no means as a determination dependent on them, and is a representation à priori, which necessarily supplies the basis for external phenomena.\n\n3. Space is no discursive, or as we say, general conception of the relations of things, but a pure intuition. For, in the first place, we can only represent to ourselves one space, and, when we talk of divers spaces, we mean only parts of one and the same space. Moreover, these parts cannot antecede this one all-embracing space, as the component parts from which the aggregate can be made up, but can be cogitated only as existing in it. Space is essentially one, and multiplicity in it, consequently the general notion of spaces, of this or that space, depends solely upon limitations. Hence it follows that an à priori intuition (which is not empirical) lies at the root of all our conceptions of space. Thus, moreover, the principles of geometry—for example, that “in a triangle, two sides together are greater than the third,” are never deduced from general conceptions of line and triangle, but from intuition, and this à priori, with apodeictic certainty.\n\n4. Space is represented as an infinite given quantity. Now every conception must indeed be considered as a representation which is contained in an infinite multitude of different possible representations, which, therefore, comprises these under itself; but no conception, as such, can be so conceived, as if it contained within itself an infinite multitude of representations. Nevertheless, space is so conceived of, for all parts of space are equally capable of being produced to infinity. Consequently, the original representation of space is an intuition à priori, and not a conception.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","text":"### SECTION I. Of Space.\n\n### § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.\n\nBy means of the external sense (a property of the mind), we represent to ourselves objects as without us, and these all in space. Herein alone are their shape, dimensions, and relations to each other determined or determinable. The internal sense, by means of which the mind contemplates itself or its internal state, gives, indeed, no intuition of the soul as an object; yet there is nevertheless a determinate form, under which alone the contemplation of our internal state is possible, so that all which relates to the inward determinations of the mind is represented in relations of time. Of time we cannot have any external intuition, any more than we can have an internal intuition of space. What then are time and space? Are they real existences? Or, are they merely relations or determinations of things, such, however, as would equally belong to these things in themselves, though they should never become objects of intuition; or, are they such as belong only to the form of intuition, and consequently to the subjective constitution of the mind, without which these predicates of time and space could not be attached to any object? In order to become informed on these points, we shall first give an exposition of the conception of space. By exposition, I mean the clear, though not detailed, representation of that which belongs to a conception; and an exposition is metaphysical when it contains that which represents the conception as given à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"9.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","text":"1. Space is not a conception which has been derived from outward experiences. For, in order that certain sensations may relate to something without me (that is, to something which occupies a different part of space from that in which I am); in like manner, in order that I may represent them not merely as without, of, and near to each other, but also in separate places, the representation of space must already exist as a foundation. Consequently, the representation of space cannot be borrowed from the relations of external phenomena through experience; but, on the contrary, this external experience is itself only possible through the said antecedent representation.\n\n2. Space then is a necessary representation à priori, which serves for the foundation of all external intuitions. We never can imagine or make a representation to ourselves of the non-existence of space, though we may easily enough think that no objects are found in it. It must, therefore, be considered as the condition of the possibility of phenomena, and by no means as a determination dependent on them, and is a representation à priori, which necessarily supplies the basis for external phenomena.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"9.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","text":"3. Space is no discursive, or as we say, general conception of the relations of things, but a pure intuition. For, in the first place, we can only represent to ourselves one space, and, when we talk of divers spaces, we mean only parts of one and the same space. Moreover, these parts cannot antecede this one all-embracing space, as the component parts from which the aggregate can be made up, but can be cogitated only as existing in it. Space is essentially one, and multiplicity in it, consequently the general notion of spaces, of this or that space, depends solely upon limitations. Hence it follows that an à priori intuition (which is not empirical) lies at the root of all our conceptions of space. Thus, moreover, the principles of geometry—for example, that “in a triangle, two sides together are greater than the third,” are never deduced from general conceptions of line and triangle, but from intuition, and this à priori, with apodeictic certainty.\n\n4. Space is represented as an infinite given quantity. Now every conception must indeed be considered as a representation which is contained in an infinite multitude of different possible representations, which, therefore, comprises these under itself; but no conception, as such, can be so conceived, as if it contained within itself an infinite multitude of representations. Nevertheless, space is so conceived of, for all parts of space are equally capable of being produced to infinity. Consequently, the original representation of space is an intuition à priori, and not a conception.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"9.3"}],"sha256":"813671d135a25f602bb5418657e2fbad52c65644fa5cfcd8088d787f1bfb7f63","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC","title":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.","order":10,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_3TEC","text":"### § 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.\n\nBy a transcendental exposition, I mean the explanation of a conception, as a principle, whence can be discerned the possibility of other synthetical à priori cognitions. For this purpose, it is requisite, firstly, that such cognitions do really flow from the given conception; and, secondly, that the said cognitions are only possible under the presupposition of a given mode of explaining this conception.\n\nGeometry is a science which determines the properties of space synthetically, and yet à priori. What, then, must be our representation of space, in order that such a cognition of it may be possible? It must be originally intuition, for from a mere conception, no propositions can be deduced which go out beyond the conception, and yet this happens in geometry. (Introd. V.) But this intuition must be found in the mind à priori, that is, before any perception of objects, consequently must be pure, not empirical, intuition. For geometrical principles are always apodeictic, that is, united with the consciousness of their necessity, as: “Space has only three dimensions.” But propositions of this kind cannot be empirical judgements, nor conclusions from them. (Introd. II.) Now, how can an external intuition anterior to objects themselves, and in which our conception of objects can be determined à priori, exist in the human mind? Obviously not otherwise than in so far as it has its seat in the subject only, as the formal capacity of the subject’s being affected by objects, and thereby of obtaining immediate representation, that is, intuition; consequently, only as the form of the external sense in general.\n\nThus it is only by means of our explanation that the possibility of geometry, as a synthetical science à priori, becomes comprehensible. Every mode of explanation which does not show us this possibility, although in appearance it may be similar to ours, can with the utmost certainty be distinguished from it by these marks.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","text":"### § 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.\n\nBy a transcendental exposition, I mean the explanation of a conception, as a principle, whence can be discerned the possibility of other synthetical à priori cognitions. For this purpose, it is requisite, firstly, that such cognitions do really flow from the given conception; and, secondly, that the said cognitions are only possible under the presupposition of a given mode of explaining this conception.\n\nGeometry is a science which determines the properties of space synthetically, and yet à priori. What, then, must be our representation of space, in order that such a cognition of it may be possible? It must be originally intuition, for from a mere conception, no propositions can be deduced which go out beyond the conception, and yet this happens in geometry. (Introd. V.) But this intuition must be found in the mind à priori, that is, before any perception of objects, consequently must be pure, not empirical, intuition. For geometrical principles are always apodeictic, that is, united with the consciousness of their necessity, as: “Space has only three dimensions.” But propositions of this kind cannot be empirical judgements, nor conclusions from them. (Introd. II.) Now, how can an external intuition anterior to objects themselves, and in which our conception of objects can be determined à priori, exist in the human mind? Obviously not otherwise than in so far as it has its seat in the subject only, as the formal capacity of the subject’s being affected by objects, and thereby of obtaining immediate representation, that is, intuition; consequently, only as the form of the external sense in general.\n\nThus it is only by means of our explanation that the possibility of geometry, as a synthetical science à priori, becomes comprehensible. Every mode of explanation which does not show us this possibility, although in appearance it may be similar to ours, can with the utmost certainty be distinguished from it by these marks.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"10.1"}],"sha256":"35e4f00a8947d50bddb9a3fdaf526208b6848b273c440d522f345b781a7a7bec","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC","title":"§ 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.","order":11,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_4CFC","text":"### § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.\n\n(a) Space does not represent any property of objects as things in themselves, nor does it represent them in their relations to each other; in other words, space does not represent to us any determination of objects such as attaches to the objects themselves, and would remain, even though all subjective conditions of the intuition were abstracted. For neither absolute nor relative determinations of objects can be intuited prior to the existence of the things to which they belong, and therefore not à priori.\n\n(b) Space is nothing else than the form of all phenomena of the external sense, that is, the subjective condition of the sensibility, under which alone external intuition is possible. Now, because the receptivity or capacity of the subject to be affected by objects necessarily antecedes all intuitions of these objects, it is easily understood how the form of all phenomena can be given in the mind previous to all actual perceptions, therefore à priori, and how it, as a pure intuition, in which all objects must be determined, can contain principles of the relations of these objects prior to all experience.\n\nIt is therefore from the human point of view only that we can speak of space, extended objects, etc. If we depart from the subjective condition, under which alone we can obtain external intuition, or, in other words, by means of which we are affected by objects, the representation of space has no meaning whatsoever. This predicate is only applicable to things in so far as they appear to us, that is, are objects of sensibility. The constant form of this receptivity, which we call sensibility, is a necessary condition of all relations in which objects can be intuited as existing without us, and when abstraction of these objects is made, is a pure intuition, to which we give the name of space. It is clear that we cannot make the special conditions of sensibility into conditions of the possibility of things, but only of the possibility of their existence as far as they are phenomena. And so we may correctly say that space contains all which can appear to us externally, but not all things considered as things in themselves, be they intuited or not, or by whatsoever subject one will. As to the intuitions of other thinking beings, we cannot judge whether they are or are not bound by the same conditions which limit our own intuition, and which for us are universally valid. If we join the limitation of a judgement to the conception of the subject, then the judgement will possess unconditioned validity. For example, the proposition, “All objects are beside each other in space,” is valid only under the limitation that these things are taken as objects of our sensuous intuition. But if I join the condition to the conception and say, “All things, as external phenomena, are beside each other in space,” then the rule is valid universally, and without any limitation. Our expositions, consequently, teach the reality (i.e., the objective validity) of space in regard of all which can be presented to us externally as object, and at the same time also the ideality of space in regard to objects when they are considered by means of reason as things in themselves, that is, without reference to the constitution of our sensibility. We maintain, therefore, the empirical reality of space in regard to all possible external experience, although we must admit its transcendental ideality; in other words, that it is nothing, so soon as we withdraw the condition upon which the possibility of all experience depends and look upon space as something that belongs to things in themselves.\n\nBut, with the exception of space, there is no representation, subjective and referring to something external to us, which could be called objective à priori. For there are no other subjective representations from which we can deduce synthetical propositions à priori, as we can from the intuition of space. (See § 3.) Therefore, to speak accurately, no ideality whatever belongs to these, although they agree in this respect with the representation of space, that they belong merely to the subjective nature of the mode of sensuous perception; such a mode, for example, as that of sight, of hearing, and of feeling, by means of the sensations of colour, sound, and heat, but which, because they are only sensations and not intuitions, do not of themselves give us the cognition of any object, least of all, an à priori cognition. My purpose, in the above remark, is merely this: to guard any one against illustrating the asserted ideality of space by examples quite insufficient, for example, by colour, taste, etc.; for these must be contemplated not as properties of things, but only as changes in the subject, changes which may be different in different men. For, in such a case, that which is originally a mere phenomenon, a rose, for example, is taken by the empirical understanding for a thing in itself, though to every different eye, in respect of its colour, it may appear different. On the contrary, the transcendental conception of phenomena in space is a critical admonition, that, in general, nothing which is intuited in space is a thing in itself, and that space is not a form which belongs as a property to things; but that objects are quite unknown to us in themselves, and what we call outward objects, are nothing else but mere representations of our sensibility, whose form is space, but whose real correlate, the thing in itself, is not known by means of these representations, nor ever can be, but respecting which, in experience, no inquiry is ever made.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","text":"### § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.\n\n(a) Space does not represent any property of objects as things in themselves, nor does it represent them in their relations to each other; in other words, space does not represent to us any determination of objects such as attaches to the objects themselves, and would remain, even though all subjective conditions of the intuition were abstracted. For neither absolute nor relative determinations of objects can be intuited prior to the existence of the things to which they belong, and therefore not à priori.\n\n(b) Space is nothing else than the form of all phenomena of the external sense, that is, the subjective condition of the sensibility, under which alone external intuition is possible. Now, because the receptivity or capacity of the subject to be affected by objects necessarily antecedes all intuitions of these objects, it is easily understood how the form of all phenomena can be given in the mind previous to all actual perceptions, therefore à priori, and how it, as a pure intuition, in which all objects must be determined, can contain principles of the relations of these objects prior to all experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"11.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","text":"It is therefore from the human point of view only that we can speak of space, extended objects, etc. If we depart from the subjective condition, under which alone we can obtain external intuition, or, in other words, by means of which we are affected by objects, the representation of space has no meaning whatsoever. This predicate is only applicable to things in so far as they appear to us, that is, are objects of sensibility. The constant form of this receptivity, which we call sensibility, is a necessary condition of all relations in which objects can be intuited as existing without us, and when abstraction of these objects is made, is a pure intuition, to which we give the name of space. It is clear that we cannot make the special conditions of sensibility into conditions of the possibility of things, but only of the possibility of their existence as far as they are phenomena. And so we may correctly say that space contains all which can appear to us externally, but not all things considered as things in themselves, be they intuited or not, or by whatsoever subject one will. As to the intuitions of other thinking beings, we cannot judge whether they are or are not bound by the same conditions which limit our own intuition, and which for us are universally valid. If we join the limitation of a judgement to the conception of the subject, then the judgement will possess unconditioned validity. For example, the proposition, “All objects are beside each other in space,” is valid only under the limitation that these things are taken as objects of our sensuous intuition. But if I join the condition to the conception and say, “All things, as external phenomena, are beside each other in space,” then the rule is valid universally, and without any limitation. Our expositions, consequently, teach the reality (i.e., the objective validity) of space in regard of all which can be presented to us externally as object, and at the same time also the ideality of space in regard to objects when they are considered by means of reason as things in themselves, that is, without reference to the constitution of our sensibility. We maintain, therefore, the empirical reality of space in regard to all possible external experience, although we must admit its transcendental ideality; in other words, that it is nothing, so soon as we withdraw the condition upon which the possibility of all experience depends and look upon space as something that belongs to things in themselves.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"11.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","text":"But, with the exception of space, there is no representation, subjective and referring to something external to us, which could be called objective à priori. For there are no other subjective representations from which we can deduce synthetical propositions à priori, as we can from the intuition of space. (See § 3.) Therefore, to speak accurately, no ideality whatever belongs to these, although they agree in this respect with the representation of space, that they belong merely to the subjective nature of the mode of sensuous perception; such a mode, for example, as that of sight, of hearing, and of feeling, by means of the sensations of colour, sound, and heat, but which, because they are only sensations and not intuitions, do not of themselves give us the cognition of any object, least of all, an à priori cognition. My purpose, in the above remark, is merely this: to guard any one against illustrating the asserted ideality of space by examples quite insufficient, for example, by colour, taste, etc.; for these must be contemplated not as properties of things, but only as changes in the subject, changes which may be different in different men. For, in such a case, that which is originally a mere phenomenon, a rose, for example, is taken by the empirical understanding for a thing in itself, though to every different eye, in respect of its colour, it may appear different. On the contrary, the transcendental conception of phenomena in space is a critical admonition, that, in general, nothing which is intuited in space is a thing in itself, and that space is not a form which belongs as a property to things; but that objects are quite unknown to us in themselves, and what we call outward objects, are nothing else but mere representations of our sensibility, whose form is space, but whose real correlate, the thing in itself, is not known by means of these representations, nor ever can be, but respecting which, in experience, no inquiry is ever made.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"11.3"}],"sha256":"4cb64eb032ebc7ba01a66504f8755892d1a140baee5ad898cf6d0422ef0d460f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET","title":"§ 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","order":12,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_5MET","text":"### SECTION II. Of Time.\n\n### § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.\n\n1. Time is not an empirical conception. For neither coexistence nor succession would be perceived by us, if the representation of time did not exist as a foundation à priori. Without this presupposition we could not represent to ourselves that things exist together at one and the same time, or at different times, that is, contemporaneously, or in succession.\n\n2. Time is a necessary representation, lying at the foundation of all our intuitions. With regard to phenomena in general, we cannot think away time from them, and represent them to ourselves as out of and unconnected with time, but we can quite well represent to ourselves time void of phenomena. Time is therefore given à priori. In it alone is all reality of phenomena possible. These may all be annihilated in thought, but time itself, as the universal condition of their possibility, cannot be so annulled.\n\n3. On this necessity à priori is also founded the possibility of apodeictic principles of the relations of time, or axioms of time in general, such as: “Time has only one dimension,” “Different times are not coexistent but successive” (as different spaces are not successive but coexistent). These principles cannot be derived from experience, for it would give neither strict universality, nor apodeictic certainty. We should only be able to say, “so common experience teaches us,” but not “it must be so.” They are valid as rules, through which, in general, experience is possible; and they instruct us respecting experience, and not by means of it.\n\n4. Time is not a discursive, or as it is called, general conception, but a pure form of the sensuous intuition. Different times are merely parts of one and the same time. But the representation which can only be given by a single object is an intuition. Besides, the proposition that different times cannot be coexistent could not be derived from a general conception. For this proposition is synthetical, and therefore cannot spring out of conceptions alone. It is therefore contained immediately in the intuition and representation of time.\n\n5. The infinity of time signifies nothing more than that every determined quantity of time is possible only through limitations of one time lying at the foundation. Consequently, the original representation, time, must be given as unlimited. But as the determinate representation of the parts of time and of every quantity of an object can only be obtained by limitation, the complete representation of time must not be furnished by means of conceptions, for these contain only partial representations. Conceptions, on the contrary, must have immediate intuition for their basis.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","text":"### SECTION II. Of Time.\n\n### § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.\n\n1. Time is not an empirical conception. For neither coexistence nor succession would be perceived by us, if the representation of time did not exist as a foundation à priori. Without this presupposition we could not represent to ourselves that things exist together at one and the same time, or at different times, that is, contemporaneously, or in succession.\n\n2. Time is a necessary representation, lying at the foundation of all our intuitions. With regard to phenomena in general, we cannot think away time from them, and represent them to ourselves as out of and unconnected with time, but we can quite well represent to ourselves time void of phenomena. Time is therefore given à priori. In it alone is all reality of phenomena possible. These may all be annihilated in thought, but time itself, as the universal condition of their possibility, cannot be so annulled.\n\n3. On this necessity à priori is also founded the possibility of apodeictic principles of the relations of time, or axioms of time in general, such as: “Time has only one dimension,” “Different times are not coexistent but successive” (as different spaces are not successive but coexistent). These principles cannot be derived from experience, for it would give neither strict universality, nor apodeictic certainty. We should only be able to say, “so common experience teaches us,” but not “it must be so.” They are valid as rules, through which, in general, experience is possible; and they instruct us respecting experience, and not by means of it.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"12.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","text":"4. Time is not a discursive, or as it is called, general conception, but a pure form of the sensuous intuition. Different times are merely parts of one and the same time. But the representation which can only be given by a single object is an intuition. Besides, the proposition that different times cannot be coexistent could not be derived from a general conception. For this proposition is synthetical, and therefore cannot spring out of conceptions alone. It is therefore contained immediately in the intuition and representation of time.\n\n5. The infinity of time signifies nothing more than that every determined quantity of time is possible only through limitations of one time lying at the foundation. Consequently, the original representation, time, must be given as unlimited. But as the determinate representation of the parts of time and of every quantity of an object can only be obtained by limitation, the complete representation of time must not be furnished by means of conceptions, for these contain only partial representations. Conceptions, on the contrary, must have immediate intuition for their basis.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"12.2"}],"sha256":"0ebc0f16c945ebf62b6b672c3beb24c274142839c1b69c395dbe39f5be78543a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC","title":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.","order":13,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_6TEC","text":"### § 6 Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.\n\nI may here refer to what is said above (§ 5, 3), where, for or sake of brevity, I have placed under the head of metaphysical exposition, that which is properly transcendental. Here I shall add that the conception of change, and with it the conception of motion, as change of place, is possible only through and in the representation of time; that if this representation were not an intuition (internal) à priori, no conception, of whatever kind, could render comprehensible the possibility of change, in other words, of a conjunction of contradictorily opposed predicates in one and the same object, for example, the presence of a thing in a place and the non-presence of the same thing in the same place. It is only in time that it is possible to meet with two contradictorily opposed determinations in one thing, that is, after each other. Thus our conception of time explains the possibility of so much synthetical knowledge à priori, as is exhibited in the general doctrine of motion, which is not a little fruitful.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","text":"### § 6 Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.\n\nI may here refer to what is said above (§ 5, 3), where, for or sake of brevity, I have placed under the head of metaphysical exposition, that which is properly transcendental. Here I shall add that the conception of change, and with it the conception of motion, as change of place, is possible only through and in the representation of time; that if this representation were not an intuition (internal) à priori, no conception, of whatever kind, could render comprehensible the possibility of change, in other words, of a conjunction of contradictorily opposed predicates in one and the same object, for example, the presence of a thing in a place and the non-presence of the same thing in the same place. It is only in time that it is possible to meet with two contradictorily opposed determinations in one thing, that is, after each other. Thus our conception of time explains the possibility of so much synthetical knowledge à priori, as is exhibited in the general doctrine of motion, which is not a little fruitful.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"13.1"}],"sha256":"61d70a97c531b00e9b4867491fddaba0ebbc06b74e65a218e069b63fc2759542","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC","title":"§ 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.","order":14,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_7CAC","text":"### § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.\n\n(a) Time is not something which subsists of itself, or which inheres in things as an objective determination, and therefore remains, when abstraction is made of the subjective conditions of the intuition of things. For in the former case, it would be something real, yet without presenting to any power of perception any real object. In the latter case, as an order or determination inherent in things themselves, it could not be antecedent to things, as their condition, nor discerned or intuited by means of synthetical propositions à priori. But all this is quite possible when we regard time as merely the subjective condition under which all our intuitions take place. For in that case, this form of the inward intuition can be represented prior to the objects, and consequently à priori.\n\n(b) Time is nothing else than the form of the internal sense, that is, of the intuitions of self and of our internal state. For time cannot be any determination of outward phenomena. It has to do neither with shape nor position; on the contrary, it determines the relation of representations in our internal state. And precisely because this internal intuition presents to us no shape or form, we endeavour to supply this want by analogies, and represent the course of time by a line progressing to infinity, the content of which constitutes a series which is only of one dimension; and we conclude from the properties of this line as to all the properties of time, with this single exception, that the parts of the line are coexistent, whilst those of time are successive. From this it is clear also that the representation of time is itself an intuition, because all its relations can be expressed in an external intuition.\n\n(c) Time is the formal condition à priori of all phenomena whatsoever. Space, as the pure form of external intuition, is limited as a condition à priori to external phenomena alone. On the other hand, because all representations, whether they have or have not external things for their objects, still in themselves, as determinations of the mind, belong to our internal state; and because this internal state is subject to the formal condition of the internal intuition, that is, to time—time is a condition à priori of all phenomena whatsoever—the immediate condition of all internal, and thereby the mediate condition of all external phenomena. If I can say à priori, “All outward phenomena are in space, and determined à priori according to the relations of space,” I can also, from the principle of the internal sense, affirm universally, “All phenomena in general, that is, all objects of the senses, are in time and stand necessarily in relations of time.”\n\nIf we abstract our internal intuition of ourselves and all external intuitions, possible only by virtue of this internal intuition and presented to us by our faculty of representation, and consequently take objects as they are in themselves, then time is nothing. It is only of objective validity in regard to phenomena, because these are things which we regard as objects of our senses. It no longer objective we, make abstraction of the sensuousness of our intuition, in other words, of that mode of representation which is peculiar to us, and speak of things in general. Time is therefore merely a subjective condition of our (human) intuition (which is always sensuous, that is, so far as we are affected by objects), and in itself, independently of the mind or subject, is nothing. Nevertheless, in respect of all phenomena, consequently of all things which come within the sphere of our experience, it is necessarily objective. We cannot say, “All things are in time,” because in this conception of things in general, we abstract and make no mention of any sort of intuition of things. But this is the proper condition under which time belongs to our representation of objects. If we add the condition to the conception, and say, “All things, as phenomena, that is, objects of sensuous intuition, are in time,” then the proposition has its sound objective validity and universality à priori.\n\nWhat we have now set forth teaches, therefore, the empirical reality of time; that is, its objective validity in reference to all objects which can ever be presented to our senses. And as our intuition is always sensuous, no object ever can be presented to us in experience, which does not come under the conditions of time. On the other hand, we deny to time all claim to absolute reality; that is, we deny that it, without having regard to the form of our sensuous intuition, absolutely inheres in things as a condition or property. Such properties as belong to objects as things in themselves never can be presented to us through the medium of the senses. Herein consists, therefore, the transcendental ideality of time, according to which, if we abstract the subjective conditions of sensuous intuition, it is nothing, and cannot be reckoned as subsisting or inhering in objects as things in themselves, independently of its relation to our intuition. This ideality, like that of space, is not to be proved or illustrated by fallacious analogies with sensations, for this reason—that in such arguments or illustrations, we make the presupposition that the phenomenon, in which such and such predicates inhere, has objective reality, while in this case we can only find such an objective reality as is itself empirical, that is, regards the object as a mere phenomenon. In reference to this subject, see the remark in Section I (§ 4)","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","text":"### § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.\n\n(a) Time is not something which subsists of itself, or which inheres in things as an objective determination, and therefore remains, when abstraction is made of the subjective conditions of the intuition of things. For in the former case, it would be something real, yet without presenting to any power of perception any real object. In the latter case, as an order or determination inherent in things themselves, it could not be antecedent to things, as their condition, nor discerned or intuited by means of synthetical propositions à priori. But all this is quite possible when we regard time as merely the subjective condition under which all our intuitions take place. For in that case, this form of the inward intuition can be represented prior to the objects, and consequently à priori.\n\n(b) Time is nothing else than the form of the internal sense, that is, of the intuitions of self and of our internal state. For time cannot be any determination of outward phenomena. It has to do neither with shape nor position; on the contrary, it determines the relation of representations in our internal state. And precisely because this internal intuition presents to us no shape or form, we endeavour to supply this want by analogies, and represent the course of time by a line progressing to infinity, the content of which constitutes a series which is only of one dimension; and we conclude from the properties of this line as to all the properties of time, with this single exception, that the parts of the line are coexistent, whilst those of time are successive. From this it is clear also that the representation of time is itself an intuition, because all its relations can be expressed in an external intuition.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"14.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","text":"(c) Time is the formal condition à priori of all phenomena whatsoever. Space, as the pure form of external intuition, is limited as a condition à priori to external phenomena alone. On the other hand, because all representations, whether they have or have not external things for their objects, still in themselves, as determinations of the mind, belong to our internal state; and because this internal state is subject to the formal condition of the internal intuition, that is, to time—time is a condition à priori of all phenomena whatsoever—the immediate condition of all internal, and thereby the mediate condition of all external phenomena. If I can say à priori, “All outward phenomena are in space, and determined à priori according to the relations of space,” I can also, from the principle of the internal sense, affirm universally, “All phenomena in general, that is, all objects of the senses, are in time and stand necessarily in relations of time.”","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"14.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","text":"If we abstract our internal intuition of ourselves and all external intuitions, possible only by virtue of this internal intuition and presented to us by our faculty of representation, and consequently take objects as they are in themselves, then time is nothing. It is only of objective validity in regard to phenomena, because these are things which we regard as objects of our senses. It no longer objective we, make abstraction of the sensuousness of our intuition, in other words, of that mode of representation which is peculiar to us, and speak of things in general. Time is therefore merely a subjective condition of our (human) intuition (which is always sensuous, that is, so far as we are affected by objects), and in itself, independently of the mind or subject, is nothing. Nevertheless, in respect of all phenomena, consequently of all things which come within the sphere of our experience, it is necessarily objective. We cannot say, “All things are in time,” because in this conception of things in general, we abstract and make no mention of any sort of intuition of things. But this is the proper condition under which time belongs to our representation of objects. If we add the condition to the conception, and say, “All things, as phenomena, that is, objects of sensuous intuition, are in time,” then the proposition has its sound objective validity and universality à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"14.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","text":"What we have now set forth teaches, therefore, the empirical reality of time; that is, its objective validity in reference to all objects which can ever be presented to our senses. And as our intuition is always sensuous, no object ever can be presented to us in experience, which does not come under the conditions of time. On the other hand, we deny to time all claim to absolute reality; that is, we deny that it, without having regard to the form of our sensuous intuition, absolutely inheres in things as a condition or property. Such properties as belong to objects as things in themselves never can be presented to us through the medium of the senses. Herein consists, therefore, the transcendental ideality of time, according to which, if we abstract the subjective conditions of sensuous intuition, it is nothing, and cannot be reckoned as subsisting or inhering in objects as things in themselves, independently of its relation to our intuition. This ideality, like that of space, is not to be proved or illustrated by fallacious analogies with sensations, for this reason—that in such arguments or illustrations, we make the presupposition that the phenomenon, in which such and such predicates inhere, has objective reality, while in this case we can only find such an objective reality as is itself empirical, that is, regards the object as a mere phenomenon. In reference to this subject, see the remark in Section I (§ 4)","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"14.4"}],"sha256":"f22890f5e4a3edf8b5c520d7c1b91e99859483055e7159e490bf9ebda36ea775","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU","title":"§ 8. Elucidation.","order":15,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_8ELU","text":"### § 8. Elucidation.\n\nAgainst this theory, which grants empirical reality to time, but denies to it absolute and transcendental reality, I have heard from intelligent men an objection so unanimously urged that I conclude that it must naturally present itself to every reader to whom these considerations are novel. It runs thus: “Changes are real” (this the continual change in our own representations demonstrates, even though the existence of all external phenomena, together with their changes, is denied). Now, changes are only possible in time, and therefore time must be something real. But there is no difficulty in answering this. I grant the whole argument. Time, no doubt, is something real, that is, it is the real form of our internal intuition. It therefore has subjective reality, in reference to our internal experience, that is, I have really the representation of time and of my determinations therein. Time, therefore, is not to be regarded as an object, but as the mode of representation of myself as an object. But if I could intuite myself, or be intuited by another being, without this condition of sensibility, then those very determinations which we now represent to ourselves as changes, would present to us a knowledge in which the representation of time, and consequently of change, would not appear. The empirical reality of time, therefore, remains, as the condition of all our experience. But absolute reality, according to what has been said above, cannot be granted it. Time is nothing but the form of our internal intuition.[[11]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-11) If we take away from it the special condition of our sensibility, the conception of time also vanishes; and it inheres not in the objects themselves, but solely in the subject (or mind) which intuites them.\n\n[[11]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-11) I can indeed say “my representations follow one another, or are successive”; but this means only that we are conscious of them as in a succession, that is, according to the form of the internal sense. Time, therefore, is not a thing in itself, nor is it any objective determination pertaining to, or inherent in things.\n\nBut the reason why this objection is so unanimously brought against our doctrine of time, and that too by disputants who cannot start any intelligible arguments against the doctrine of the ideality of space, is this—they have no hope of demonstrating apodeictically the absolute reality of space, because the doctrine of idealism is against them, according to which the reality of external objects is not capable of any strict proof. On the other hand, the reality of the object of our internal sense (that is, myself and my internal state) is clear immediately through consciousness. The former—external objects in space—might be a mere delusion, but the latter—the object of my internal perception—is undeniably real. They do not, however, reflect that both, without question of their reality as representations, belong only to the genus phenomenon, which has always two aspects, the one, the object considered as a thing in itself, without regard to the mode of intuiting it, and the nature of which remains for this very reason problematical, the other, the form of our intuition of the object, which must be sought not in the object as a thing in itself, but in the subject to which it appears—which form of intuition nevertheless belongs really and necessarily to the phenomenal object.\n\nTime and space are, therefore, two sources of knowledge, from which, à priori, various synthetical cognitions can be drawn. Of this we find a striking example in the cognitions of space and its relations, which form the foundation of pure mathematics. They are the two pure forms of all intuitions, and thereby make synthetical propositions à priori possible. But these sources of knowledge being merely conditions of our sensibility, do therefore, and as such, strictly determine their own range and purpose, in that they do not and cannot present objects as things in themselves, but are applicable to them solely in so far as they are considered as sensuous phenomena. The sphere of phenomena is the only sphere of their validity, and if we venture out of this, no further objective use can be made of them. For the rest, this formal reality of time and space leaves the validity of our empirical knowledge unshaken; for our certainty in that respect is equally firm, whether these forms necessarily inhere in the things themselves, or only in our intuitions of them. On the other hand, those who maintain the absolute reality of time and space, whether as essentially subsisting, or only inhering, as modifications, in things, must find themselves at utter variance with the principles of experience itself. For, if they decide for the first view, and make space and time into substances, this being the side taken by mathematical natural philosophers, they must admit two self-subsisting nonentities, infinite and eternal, which exist (yet without there being anything real) for the purpose of containing in themselves everything that is real. If they adopt the second view of inherence, which is preferred by some metaphysical natural philosophers, and regard space and time as relations (contiguity in space or succession in time), abstracted from experience, though represented confusedly in this state of separation, they find themselves in that case necessitated to deny the validity of mathematical doctrines à priori in reference to real things (for example, in space)—at all events their apodeictic certainty. For such certainty cannot be found in an à posteriori proposition; and the conceptions à priori of space and time are, according to this opinion, mere creations of the imagination, having their source really in experience, inasmuch as, out of relations abstracted from experience, imagination has made up something which contains, indeed, general statements of these relations, yet of which no application can be made without the restrictions attached thereto by nature. The former of these parties gains this advantage, that they keep the sphere of phenomena free for mathematical science. On the other hand, these very conditions (space and time) embarrass them greatly, when the understanding endeavours to pass the limits of that sphere. The latter has, indeed, this advantage, that the representations of space and time do not come in their way when they wish to judge of objects, not as phenomena, but merely in their relation to the understanding. Devoid, however, of a true and objectively valid à priori intuition, they can neither furnish any basis for the possibility of mathematical cognitions à priori, nor bring the propositions of experience into necessary accordance with those of mathematics. In our theory of the true nature of these two original forms of the sensibility, both difficulties are surmounted.\n\nIn conclusion, that transcendental æsthetic cannot contain any more than these two elements—space and time, is sufficiently obvious from the fact that all other conceptions appertaining to sensibility, even that of motion, which unites in itself both elements, presuppose something empirical. Motion, for example, presupposes the perception of something movable. But space considered in itself contains nothing movable, consequently motion must be something which is found in space only through experience—in other words, an empirical datum. In like manner, transcendental æsthetic cannot number the conception of change among its data à priori; for time itself does not change, but only something which is in time. To acquire the conception of change, therefore, the perception of some existing object and of the succession of its determinations, in one word, experience, is necessary.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","text":"### § 8. Elucidation.\n\nAgainst this theory, which grants empirical reality to time, but denies to it absolute and transcendental reality, I have heard from intelligent men an objection so unanimously urged that I conclude that it must naturally present itself to every reader to whom these considerations are novel. It runs thus: “Changes are real” (this the continual change in our own representations demonstrates, even though the existence of all external phenomena, together with their changes, is denied). Now, changes are only possible in time, and therefore time must be something real. But there is no difficulty in answering this. I grant the whole argument. Time, no doubt, is something real, that is, it is the real form of our internal intuition. It therefore has subjective reality, in reference to our internal experience, that is, I have really the representation of time and of my determinations therein. Time, therefore, is not to be regarded as an object, but as the mode of representation of myself as an object. But if I could intuite myself, or be intuited by another being, without this condition of sensibility, then those very determinations which we now represent to ourselves as changes, would present to us a knowledge in which the representation of time, and consequently of change, would not appear. The empirical reality of time, therefore, remains, as the condition of all our experience. But absolute reality, according to what has been said above, cannot be granted it. Time is nothing but the form of our internal intuition.[[11]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-11) If we take away from it the special condition of our sensibility, the conception of time also vanishes; and it inheres not in the objects themselves, but solely in the subject (or mind) which intuites them.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"15.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","text":"[[11]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-11) I can indeed say “my representations follow one another, or are successive”; but this means only that we are conscious of them as in a succession, that is, according to the form of the internal sense. Time, therefore, is not a thing in itself, nor is it any objective determination pertaining to, or inherent in things.\n\nBut the reason why this objection is so unanimously brought against our doctrine of time, and that too by disputants who cannot start any intelligible arguments against the doctrine of the ideality of space, is this—they have no hope of demonstrating apodeictically the absolute reality of space, because the doctrine of idealism is against them, according to which the reality of external objects is not capable of any strict proof. On the other hand, the reality of the object of our internal sense (that is, myself and my internal state) is clear immediately through consciousness. The former—external objects in space—might be a mere delusion, but the latter—the object of my internal perception—is undeniably real. They do not, however, reflect that both, without question of their reality as representations, belong only to the genus phenomenon, which has always two aspects, the one, the object considered as a thing in itself, without regard to the mode of intuiting it, and the nature of which remains for this very reason problematical, the other, the form of our intuition of the object, which must be sought not in the object as a thing in itself, but in the subject to which it appears—which form of intuition nevertheless belongs really and necessarily to the phenomenal object.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"15.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","text":"Time and space are, therefore, two sources of knowledge, from which, à priori, various synthetical cognitions can be drawn. Of this we find a striking example in the cognitions of space and its relations, which form the foundation of pure mathematics. They are the two pure forms of all intuitions, and thereby make synthetical propositions à priori possible. But these sources of knowledge being merely conditions of our sensibility, do therefore, and as such, strictly determine their own range and purpose, in that they do not and cannot present objects as things in themselves, but are applicable to them solely in so far as they are considered as sensuous phenomena. The sphere of phenomena is the only sphere of their validity, and if we venture out of this, no further objective use can be made of them. For the rest, this formal reality of time and space leaves the validity of our empirical knowledge unshaken; for our certainty in that respect is equally firm, whether these forms necessarily inhere in the things themselves, or only in our intuitions of them. On the other hand, those who maintain the absolute reality of time and space, whether as essentially subsisting, or only inhering, as modifications, in things, must find themselves at utter variance with the principles of experience itself. For, if they decide for the first view, and make space and time into substances, this being the side taken by mathematical natural philosophers, they must admit two self-subsisting nonentities, infinite and eternal, which exist (yet without there being anything real) for the purpose of containing in themselves everything that is real. If they adopt the second view of inherence, which is preferred by some metaphysical natural philosophers, and regard space and time as relations (contiguity in space or succession in time), abstracted from experience, though represented confusedly in this state of separation, they find themselves in that case necessitated to deny the validity of mathematical doctrines à priori in reference to real things (for example, in space)—at all events their apodeictic certainty. For such certainty cannot be found in an à posteriori proposition; and the conceptions à priori of space and time are, according to this opinion, mere creations of the imagination, having their source really in experience, inasmuch as, out of relations abstracted from experience, imagination has made up something which contains, indeed, general statements of these relations, yet of which no application can be made without the restrictions attached thereto by nature. The former of these parties gains this advantage, that they keep the sphere of phenomena free for mathematical science. On the other hand, these very conditions (space and time) embarrass them greatly, when the understanding endeavours to pass the limits of that sphere. The latter has, indeed, this advantage, that the representations of space and time do not come in their way when they wish to judge of objects, not as phenomena, but merely in their relation to the understanding. Devoid, however, of a true and objectively valid à priori intuition, they can neither furnish any basis for the possibility of mathematical cognitions à priori, nor bring the propositions of experience into necessary accordance with those of mathematics. In our theory of the true nature of these two original forms of the sensibility, both difficulties are surmounted.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"15.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0004","text":"In conclusion, that transcendental æsthetic cannot contain any more than these two elements—space and time, is sufficiently obvious from the fact that all other conceptions appertaining to sensibility, even that of motion, which unites in itself both elements, presuppose something empirical. Motion, for example, presupposes the perception of something movable. But space considered in itself contains nothing movable, consequently motion must be something which is found in space only through experience—in other words, an empirical datum. In like manner, transcendental æsthetic cannot number the conception of change among its data à priori; for time itself does not change, but only something which is in time. To acquire the conception of change, therefore, the perception of some existing object and of the succession of its determinations, in one word, experience, is necessary.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"15.4"}],"sha256":"89abecbac410cc879bf7f6a57dbe17e00e7641d8487e0a6544320d89bc6a3c1d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT","title":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.","order":16,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_9GRT","text":"### § 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.\n\nI. In order to prevent any misunderstanding, it will be requisite, in the first place, to recapitulate, as clearly as possible, what our opinion is with respect to the fundamental nature of our sensuous cognition in general. We have intended, then, to say that all our intuition is nothing but the representation of phenomena; that the things which we intuite, are not in themselves the same as our representations of them in intuition, nor are their relations in themselves so constituted as they appear to us; and that if we take away the subject, or even only the subjective constitution of our senses in general, then not only the nature and relations of objects in space and time, but even space and time themselves disappear; and that these, as phenomena, cannot exist in themselves, but only in us. What may be the nature of objects considered as things in themselves and without reference to the receptivity of our sensibility is quite unknown to us. We know nothing more than our mode of perceiving them, which is peculiar to us, and which, though not of necessity pertaining to every animated being, is so to the whole human race. With this alone we have to do. Space and time are the pure forms thereof; sensation the matter. The former alone can we cognize à priori, that is, antecedent to all actual perception; and for this reason such cognition is called pure intuition. The latter is that in our cognition which is called cognition à posteriori, that is, empirical intuition. The former appertain absolutely and necessarily to our sensibility, of whatsoever kind our sensations may be; the latter may be of very diversified character. Supposing that we should carry our empirical intuition even to the very highest degree of clearness, we should not thereby advance one step nearer to a knowledge of the constitution of objects as things in themselves. For we could only, at best, arrive at a complete cognition of our own mode of intuition, that is of our sensibility, and this always under the conditions originally attaching to the subject, namely, the conditions of space and time; while the question: “What are objects considered as things in themselves?” remains unanswerable even after the most thorough examination of the phenomenal world.\n\nTo say, then, that all our sensibility is nothing but the confused representation of things containing exclusively that which belongs to them as things in themselves, and this under an accumulation of characteristic marks and partial representations which we cannot distinguish in consciousness, is a falsification of the conception of sensibility and phenomenization, which renders our whole doctrine thereof empty and useless. The difference between a confused and a clear representation is merely logical and has nothing to do with content. No doubt the conception of right, as employed by a sound understanding, contains all that the most subtle investigation could unfold from it, although, in the ordinary practical use of the word, we are not conscious of the manifold representations comprised in the conception. But we cannot for this reason assert that the ordinary conception is a sensuous one, containing a mere phenomenon, for right cannot appear as a phenomenon; but the conception of it lies in the understanding, and represents a property (the moral property) of actions, which belongs to them in themselves. On the other hand, the representation in intuition of a body contains nothing which could belong to an object considered as a thing in itself, but merely the phenomenon or appearance of something, and the mode in which we are affected by that appearance; and this receptivity of our faculty of cognition is called sensibility, and remains toto caelo different from the cognition of an object in itself, even though we should examine the content of the phenomenon to the very bottom.\n\nIt must be admitted that the Leibnitz-Wolfian philosophy has assigned an entirely erroneous point of view to all investigations into the nature and origin of our cognitions, inasmuch as it regards the distinction between the sensuous and the intellectual as merely logical, whereas it is plainly transcendental, and concerns not merely the clearness or obscurity, but the content and origin of both. For the faculty of sensibility not only does not present us with an indistinct and confused cognition of objects as things in themselves, but, in fact, gives us no knowledge of these at all. On the contrary, so soon as we abstract in thought our own subjective nature, the object represented, with the properties ascribed to it by sensuous intuition, entirely disappears, because it was only this subjective nature that determined the form of the object as a phenomenon.\n\nIn phenomena, we commonly, indeed, distinguish that which essentially belongs to the intuition of them, and is valid for the sensuous faculty of every human being, from that which belongs to the same intuition accidentally, as valid not for the sensuous faculty in general, but for a particular state or organization of this or that sense. Accordingly, we are accustomed to say that the former is a cognition which represents the object itself, whilst the latter presents only a particular appearance or phenomenon thereof. This distinction, however, is only empirical. If we stop here (as is usual), and do not regard the empirical intuition as itself a mere phenomenon (as we ought to do), in which nothing that can appertain to a thing in itself is to be found, our transcendental distinction is lost, and we believe that we cognize objects as things in themselves, although in the whole range of the sensuous world, investigate the nature of its objects as profoundly as we may, we have to do with nothing but phenomena. Thus, we call the rainbow a mere appearance of phenomenon in a sunny shower, and the rain, the reality or thing in itself; and this is right enough, if we understand the latter conception in a merely physical sense, that is, as that which in universal experience, and under whatever conditions of sensuous perception, is known in intuition to be so and so determined, and not otherwise. But if we consider this empirical datum generally, and inquire, without reference to its accordance with all our senses, whether there can be discovered in it aught which represents an object as a thing in itself (the raindrops of course are not such, for they are, as phenomena, empirical objects), the question of the relation of the representation to the object is transcendental; and not only are the raindrops mere phenomena, but even their circular form, nay, the space itself through which they fall, is nothing in itself, but both are mere modifications or fundamental dispositions of our sensuous intuition, whilst the transcendental object remains for us utterly unknown.\n\nThe second important concern of our æsthetic is that it does not obtain favour merely as a plausible hypothesis, but possess as undoubted a character of certainty as can be demanded of any theory which is to serve for an organon. In order fully to convince the reader of this certainty, we shall select a case which will serve to make its validity apparent, and also to illustrate what has been said in § 3.\n\nSuppose, then, that space and time are in themselves objective, and conditions of the—possibility of objects as things in themselves. In the first place, it is evident that both present us, with very many apodeictic and synthetic propositions à priori, but especially space—and for this reason we shall prefer it for investigation at present. As the propositions of geometry are cognized synthetically à priori, and with apodeictic certainty, I inquire: Whence do you obtain propositions of this kind, and on what basis does the understanding rest, in order to arrive at such absolutely necessary and universally valid truths?\n\nThere is no other way than through intuitions or conceptions, as such; and these are given either à priori or à posteriori. The latter, namely, empirical conceptions, together with the empirical intuition on which they are founded, cannot afford any synthetical proposition, except such as is itself also empirical, that is, a proposition of experience. But an empirical proposition cannot possess the qualities of necessity and absolute universality, which, nevertheless, are the characteristics of all geometrical propositions. As to the first and only means to arrive at such cognitions, namely, through mere conceptions or intuitions à priori, it is quite clear that from mere conceptions no synthetical cognitions, but only analytical ones, can be obtained. Take, for example, the proposition: “Two straight lines cannot enclose a space, and with these alone no figure is possible,” and try to deduce it from the conception of a straight line and the number two; or take the proposition: “It is possible to construct a figure with three straight lines,” and endeavour, in like manner, to deduce it from the mere conception of a straight line and the number three. All your endeavours are in vain, and you find yourself forced to have recourse to intuition, as, in fact, geometry always does. You therefore give yourself an object in intuition. But of what kind is this intuition? Is it a pure à priori, or is it an empirical intuition? If the latter, then neither an universally valid, much less an apodeictic proposition can arise from it, for experience never can give us any such proposition. You must, therefore, give yourself an object à priori in intuition, and upon that ground your synthetical proposition. Now if there did not exist within you a faculty of intuition à priori; if this subjective condition were not in respect to its form also the universal condition à priori under which alone the object of this external intuition is itself possible; if the object (that is, the triangle) were something in itself, without relation to you the subject; how could you affirm that that which lies necessarily in your subjective conditions in order to construct a triangle, must also necessarily belong to the triangle in itself? For to your conceptions of three lines, you could not add anything new (that is, the figure); which, therefore, must necessarily be found in the object, because the object is given before your cognition, and not by means of it. If, therefore, space (and time also) were not a mere form of your intuition, which contains conditions à priori, under which alone things can become external objects for you, and without which subjective conditions the objects are in themselves nothing, you could not construct any synthetical proposition whatsoever regarding external objects. It is therefore not merely possible or probable, but indubitably certain, that space and time, as the necessary conditions of all our external and internal experience, are merely subjective conditions of all our intuitions, in relation to which all objects are therefore mere phenomena, and not things in themselves, presented to us in this particular manner. And for this reason, in respect to the form of phenomena, much may be said à priori, whilst of the thing in itself, which may lie at the foundation of these phenomena, it is impossible to say anything.\n\nII. In confirmation of this theory of the ideality of the external as well as internal sense, consequently of all objects of sense, as mere phenomena, we may especially remark that all in our cognition that belongs to intuition contains nothing more than mere relations. (The feelings of pain and pleasure, and the will, which are not cognitions, are excepted.) The relations, to wit, of place in an intuition (extension), change of place (motion), and laws according to which this change is determined (moving forces). That, however, which is present in this or that place, or any operation going on, or result taking place in the things themselves, with the exception of change of place, is not given to us by intuition. Now by means of mere relations, a thing cannot be known in itself; and it may therefore be fairly concluded, that, as through the external sense nothing but mere representations of relations are given us, the said external sense in its representation can contain only the relation of the object to the subject, but not the essential nature of the object as a thing in itself.\n\nThe same is the case with the internal intuition, not only because, in the internal intuition, the representation of the external senses constitutes the material with which the mind is occupied; but because time, in which we place, and which itself antecedes the consciousness of, these representations in experience, and which, as the formal condition of the mode according to which objects are placed in the mind, lies at the foundation of them, contains relations of the successive, the coexistent, and of that which always must be coexistent with succession, the permanent. Now that which, as representation, can antecede every exercise of thought (of an object), is intuition; and when it contains nothing but relations, it is the form of the intuition, which, as it presents us with no representation, except in so far as something is placed in the mind, can be nothing else than the mode in which the mind is affected by its own activity, to wit—its presenting to itself representations, consequently the mode in which the mind is affected by itself; that is, it can be nothing but an internal sense in respect to its form. Everything that is represented through the medium of sense is so far phenomenal; consequently, we must either refuse altogether to admit an internal sense, or the subject, which is the object of that sense, could only be represented by it as phenomenon, and not as it would judge of itself, if its intuition were pure spontaneous activity, that is, were intellectual. The difficulty here lies wholly in the question: How can the subject have an internal intuition of itself? But this difficulty is common to every theory. The consciousness of self (apperception) is the simple representation of the “ego”; and if by means of that representation alone, all the manifold representations in the subject were spontaneously given, then our internal intuition would be intellectual. This consciousness in man requires an internal perception of the manifold representations which are previously given in the subject; and the manner in which these representations are given in the mind without spontaneity, must, on account of this difference (the want of spontaneity), be called sensibility. If the faculty of self-consciousness is to apprehend what lies in the mind, it must all act that and can in this way alone produce an intuition of self. But the form of this intuition, which lies in the original constitution of the mind, determines, in the representation of time, the manner in which the manifold representations are to combine themselves in the mind; since the subject intuites itself, not as it would represent itself immediately and spontaneously, but according to the manner in which the mind is internally affected, consequently, as it appears, and not as it is.\n\nIII. When we say that the intuition of external objects, and also the self-intuition of the subject, represent both, objects and subject, in space and time, as they affect our senses, that is, as they appear—this is by no means equivalent to asserting that these objects are mere illusory appearances. For when we speak of things as phenomena, the objects, nay, even the properties which we ascribe to them, are looked upon as really given; only that, in so far as this or that property depends upon the mode of intuition of the subject, in the relation of the given object to the subject, the object as phenomenon is to be distinguished from the object as a thing in itself. Thus I do not say that bodies seem or appear to be external to me, or that my soul seems merely to be given in my self-consciousness, although I maintain that the properties of space and time, in conformity to which I set both, as the condition of their existence, abide in my mode of intuition, and not in the objects in themselves. It would be my own fault, if out of that which I should reckon as phenomenon, I made mere illusory appearance.[[12]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-12) But this will not happen, because of our principle of the ideality of all sensuous intuitions. On the contrary, if we ascribe objective reality to these forms of representation, it becomes impossible to avoid changing everything into mere appearance. For if we regard space and time as properties, which must be found in objects as things in themselves, as sine quibus non of the possibility of their existence, and reflect on the absurdities in which we then find ourselves involved, inasmuch as we are compelled to admit the existence of two infinite things, which are nevertheless not substances, nor anything really inhering in substances, nay, to admit that they are the necessary conditions of the existence of all things, and moreover, that they must continue to exist, although all existing things were annihilated—we cannot blame the good Berkeley for degrading bodies to mere illusory appearances. Nay, even our own existence, which would in this case depend upon the self-existent reality of such a mere nonentity as time, would necessarily be changed with it into mere appearance—an absurdity which no one has as yet been guilty of.\n\n[[12]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-12) The predicates of the phenomenon can be affixed to the object itself in relation to our sensuous faculty; for example, the red colour or the perfume to the rose. But (illusory) appearance never can be attributed as a predicate to an object, for this very reason, that it attributes to this object in itself that which belongs to it only in relation to our sensuous faculty, or to the subject in general, e.g., the two handles which were formerly ascribed to Saturn. That which is never to be found in the object itself, but always in the relation of the object to the subject, and which moreover is inseparable from our representation of the object, we denominate phenomenon. Thus the predicates of space and time are rightly attributed to objects of the senses as such, and in this there is no illusion. On the contrary, if I ascribe redness of the rose as a thing in itself, or to Saturn his handles, or extension to all external objects, considered as things in themselves, without regarding the determinate relation of these objects to the subject, and without limiting my judgement to that relation—then, and then only, arises illusion.\n\nIV. In natural theology, where we think of an object—God—which never can be an object of intuition to us, and even to himself can never be an object of sensuous intuition, we carefully avoid attributing to his intuition the conditions of space and time—and intuition all his cognition must be, and not thought, which always includes limitation. But with what right can we do this if we make them forms of objects as things in themselves, and such, moreover, as would continue to exist as à priori conditions of the existence of things, even though the things themselves were annihilated? For as conditions of all existence in general, space and time must be conditions of the existence of the Supreme Being also. But if we do not thus make them objective forms of all things, there is no other way left than to make them subjective forms of our mode of intuition—external and internal; which is called sensuous, because it is not primitive, that is, is not such as gives in itself the existence of the object of the intuition (a mode of intuition which, so far as we can judge, can belong only to the Creator), but is dependent on the existence of the object, is possible, therefore, only on condition that the representative faculty of the subject is affected by the object.\n\nIt is, moreover, not necessary that we should limit the mode of intuition in space and time to the sensuous faculty of man. It may well be that all finite thinking beings must necessarily in this respect agree with man (though as to this we cannot decide), but sensibility does not on account of this universality cease to be sensibility, for this very reason, that it is a deduced (intuitus derivativus), and not an original (intuitus originarius), consequently not an intellectual intuition, and this intuition, as such, for reasons above mentioned, seems to belong solely to the Supreme Being, but never to a being dependent, quoad its existence, as well as its intuition (which its existence determines and limits relatively to given objects). This latter remark, however, must be taken only as an illustration, and not as any proof of the truth of our æsthetical theory.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","text":"### § 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.\n\nI. In order to prevent any misunderstanding, it will be requisite, in the first place, to recapitulate, as clearly as possible, what our opinion is with respect to the fundamental nature of our sensuous cognition in general. We have intended, then, to say that all our intuition is nothing but the representation of phenomena; that the things which we intuite, are not in themselves the same as our representations of them in intuition, nor are their relations in themselves so constituted as they appear to us; and that if we take away the subject, or even only the subjective constitution of our senses in general, then not only the nature and relations of objects in space and time, but even space and time themselves disappear; and that these, as phenomena, cannot exist in themselves, but only in us. What may be the nature of objects considered as things in themselves and without reference to the receptivity of our sensibility is quite unknown to us. We know nothing more than our mode of perceiving them, which is peculiar to us, and which, though not of necessity pertaining to every animated being, is so to the whole human race. With this alone we have to do. Space and time are the pure forms thereof; sensation the matter. The former alone can we cognize à priori, that is, antecedent to all actual perception; and for this reason such cognition is called pure intuition. The latter is that in our cognition which is called cognition à posteriori, that is, empirical intuition. The former appertain absolutely and necessarily to our sensibility, of whatsoever kind our sensations may be; the latter may be of very diversified character. Supposing that we should carry our empirical intuition even to the very highest degree of clearness, we should not thereby advance one step nearer to a knowledge of the constitution of objects as things in themselves. For we could only, at best, arrive at a complete cognition of our own mode of intuition, that is of our sensibility, and this always under the conditions originally attaching to the subject, namely, the conditions of space and time; while the question: “What are objects considered as things in themselves?” remains unanswerable even after the most thorough examination of the phenomenal world.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"16.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0002","text":"To say, then, that all our sensibility is nothing but the confused representation of things containing exclusively that which belongs to them as things in themselves, and this under an accumulation of characteristic marks and partial representations which we cannot distinguish in consciousness, is a falsification of the conception of sensibility and phenomenization, which renders our whole doctrine thereof empty and useless. The difference between a confused and a clear representation is merely logical and has nothing to do with content. No doubt the conception of right, as employed by a sound understanding, contains all that the most subtle investigation could unfold from it, although, in the ordinary practical use of the word, we are not conscious of the manifold representations comprised in the conception. But we cannot for this reason assert that the ordinary conception is a sensuous one, containing a mere phenomenon, for right cannot appear as a phenomenon; but the conception of it lies in the understanding, and represents a property (the moral property) of actions, which belongs to them in themselves. On the other hand, the representation in intuition of a body contains nothing which could belong to an object considered as a thing in itself, but merely the phenomenon or appearance of something, and the mode in which we are affected by that appearance; and this receptivity of our faculty of cognition is called sensibility, and remains toto caelo different from the cognition of an object in itself, even though we should examine the content of the phenomenon to the very bottom.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"16.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0003","text":"It must be admitted that the Leibnitz-Wolfian philosophy has assigned an entirely erroneous point of view to all investigations into the nature and origin of our cognitions, inasmuch as it regards the distinction between the sensuous and the intellectual as merely logical, whereas it is plainly transcendental, and concerns not merely the clearness or obscurity, but the content and origin of both. For the faculty of sensibility not only does not present us with an indistinct and confused cognition of objects as things in themselves, but, in fact, gives us no knowledge of these at all. On the contrary, so soon as we abstract in thought our own subjective nature, the object represented, with the properties ascribed to it by sensuous intuition, entirely disappears, because it was only this subjective nature that determined the form of the object as a phenomenon.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"16.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0004","text":"In phenomena, we commonly, indeed, distinguish that which essentially belongs to the intuition of them, and is valid for the sensuous faculty of every human being, from that which belongs to the same intuition accidentally, as valid not for the sensuous faculty in general, but for a particular state or organization of this or that sense. Accordingly, we are accustomed to say that the former is a cognition which represents the object itself, whilst the latter presents only a particular appearance or phenomenon thereof. This distinction, however, is only empirical. If we stop here (as is usual), and do not regard the empirical intuition as itself a mere phenomenon (as we ought to do), in which nothing that can appertain to a thing in itself is to be found, our transcendental distinction is lost, and we believe that we cognize objects as things in themselves, although in the whole range of the sensuous world, investigate the nature of its objects as profoundly as we may, we have to do with nothing but phenomena. Thus, we call the rainbow a mere appearance of phenomenon in a sunny shower, and the rain, the reality or thing in itself; and this is right enough, if we understand the latter conception in a merely physical sense, that is, as that which in universal experience, and under whatever conditions of sensuous perception, is known in intuition to be so and so determined, and not otherwise. But if we consider this empirical datum generally, and inquire, without reference to its accordance with all our senses, whether there can be discovered in it aught which represents an object as a thing in itself (the raindrops of course are not such, for they are, as phenomena, empirical objects), the question of the relation of the representation to the object is transcendental; and not only are the raindrops mere phenomena, but even their circular form, nay, the space itself through which they fall, is nothing in itself, but both are mere modifications or fundamental dispositions of our sensuous intuition, whilst the transcendental object remains for us utterly unknown.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"16.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0005","text":"The second important concern of our æsthetic is that it does not obtain favour merely as a plausible hypothesis, but possess as undoubted a character of certainty as can be demanded of any theory which is to serve for an organon. In order fully to convince the reader of this certainty, we shall select a case which will serve to make its validity apparent, and also to illustrate what has been said in § 3.\n\nSuppose, then, that space and time are in themselves objective, and conditions of the—possibility of objects as things in themselves. In the first place, it is evident that both present us, with very many apodeictic and synthetic propositions à priori, but especially space—and for this reason we shall prefer it for investigation at present. As the propositions of geometry are cognized synthetically à priori, and with apodeictic certainty, I inquire: Whence do you obtain propositions of this kind, and on what basis does the understanding rest, in order to arrive at such absolutely necessary and universally valid truths?","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"16.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","text":"There is no other way than through intuitions or conceptions, as such; and these are given either à priori or à posteriori. The latter, namely, empirical conceptions, together with the empirical intuition on which they are founded, cannot afford any synthetical proposition, except such as is itself also empirical, that is, a proposition of experience. But an empirical proposition cannot possess the qualities of necessity and absolute universality, which, nevertheless, are the characteristics of all geometrical propositions. As to the first and only means to arrive at such cognitions, namely, through mere conceptions or intuitions à priori, it is quite clear that from mere conceptions no synthetical cognitions, but only analytical ones, can be obtained. Take, for example, the proposition: “Two straight lines cannot enclose a space, and with these alone no figure is possible,” and try to deduce it from the conception of a straight line and the number two; or take the proposition: “It is possible to construct a figure with three straight lines,” and endeavour, in like manner, to deduce it from the mere conception of a straight line and the number three. All your endeavours are in vain, and you find yourself forced to have recourse to intuition, as, in fact, geometry always does. You therefore give yourself an object in intuition. But of what kind is this intuition? Is it a pure à priori, or is it an empirical intuition? If the latter, then neither an universally valid, much less an apodeictic proposition can arise from it, for experience never can give us any such proposition. You must, therefore, give yourself an object à priori in intuition, and upon that ground your synthetical proposition. Now if there did not exist within you a faculty of intuition à priori; if this subjective condition were not in respect to its form also the universal condition à priori under which alone the object of this external intuition is itself possible; if the object (that is, the triangle) were something in itself, without relation to you the subject; how could you affirm that that which lies necessarily in your subjective conditions in order to construct a triangle, must also necessarily belong to the triangle in itself? For to your conceptions of three lines, you could not add anything new (that is, the figure); which, therefore, must necessarily be found in the object, because the object is given before your cognition, and not by means of it. If, therefore, space (and time also) were not a mere form of your intuition, which contains conditions à priori, under which alone things can become external objects for you, and without which subjective conditions the objects are in themselves nothing, you could not construct any synthetical proposition whatsoever regarding external objects. It is therefore not merely possible or probable, but indubitably certain, that space and time, as the necessary conditions of all our external and internal experience, are merely subjective conditions of all our intuitions, in relation to which all objects are therefore mere phenomena, and not things in themselves, presented to us in this particular manner. And for this reason, in respect to the form of phenomena, much may be said à priori, whilst of the thing in itself, which may lie at the foundation of these phenomena, it is impossible to say anything.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"16.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0007","text":"II. In confirmation of this theory of the ideality of the external as well as internal sense, consequently of all objects of sense, as mere phenomena, we may especially remark that all in our cognition that belongs to intuition contains nothing more than mere relations. (The feelings of pain and pleasure, and the will, which are not cognitions, are excepted.) The relations, to wit, of place in an intuition (extension), change of place (motion), and laws according to which this change is determined (moving forces). That, however, which is present in this or that place, or any operation going on, or result taking place in the things themselves, with the exception of change of place, is not given to us by intuition. Now by means of mere relations, a thing cannot be known in itself; and it may therefore be fairly concluded, that, as through the external sense nothing but mere representations of relations are given us, the said external sense in its representation can contain only the relation of the object to the subject, but not the essential nature of the object as a thing in itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"16.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","text":"The same is the case with the internal intuition, not only because, in the internal intuition, the representation of the external senses constitutes the material with which the mind is occupied; but because time, in which we place, and which itself antecedes the consciousness of, these representations in experience, and which, as the formal condition of the mode according to which objects are placed in the mind, lies at the foundation of them, contains relations of the successive, the coexistent, and of that which always must be coexistent with succession, the permanent. Now that which, as representation, can antecede every exercise of thought (of an object), is intuition; and when it contains nothing but relations, it is the form of the intuition, which, as it presents us with no representation, except in so far as something is placed in the mind, can be nothing else than the mode in which the mind is affected by its own activity, to wit—its presenting to itself representations, consequently the mode in which the mind is affected by itself; that is, it can be nothing but an internal sense in respect to its form. Everything that is represented through the medium of sense is so far phenomenal; consequently, we must either refuse altogether to admit an internal sense, or the subject, which is the object of that sense, could only be represented by it as phenomenon, and not as it would judge of itself, if its intuition were pure spontaneous activity, that is, were intellectual. The difficulty here lies wholly in the question: How can the subject have an internal intuition of itself? But this difficulty is common to every theory. The consciousness of self (apperception) is the simple representation of the “ego”; and if by means of that representation alone, all the manifold representations in the subject were spontaneously given, then our internal intuition would be intellectual. This consciousness in man requires an internal perception of the manifold representations which are previously given in the subject; and the manner in which these representations are given in the mind without spontaneity, must, on account of this difference (the want of spontaneity), be called sensibility. If the faculty of self-consciousness is to apprehend what lies in the mind, it must all act that and can in this way alone produce an intuition of self. But the form of this intuition, which lies in the original constitution of the mind, determines, in the representation of time, the manner in which the manifold representations are to combine themselves in the mind; since the subject intuites itself, not as it would represent itself immediately and spontaneously, but according to the manner in which the mind is internally affected, consequently, as it appears, and not as it is.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"16.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","text":"III. When we say that the intuition of external objects, and also the self-intuition of the subject, represent both, objects and subject, in space and time, as they affect our senses, that is, as they appear—this is by no means equivalent to asserting that these objects are mere illusory appearances. For when we speak of things as phenomena, the objects, nay, even the properties which we ascribe to them, are looked upon as really given; only that, in so far as this or that property depends upon the mode of intuition of the subject, in the relation of the given object to the subject, the object as phenomenon is to be distinguished from the object as a thing in itself. Thus I do not say that bodies seem or appear to be external to me, or that my soul seems merely to be given in my self-consciousness, although I maintain that the properties of space and time, in conformity to which I set both, as the condition of their existence, abide in my mode of intuition, and not in the objects in themselves. It would be my own fault, if out of that which I should reckon as phenomenon, I made mere illusory appearance.[[12]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-12) But this will not happen, because of our principle of the ideality of all sensuous intuitions. On the contrary, if we ascribe objective reality to these forms of representation, it becomes impossible to avoid changing everything into mere appearance. For if we regard space and time as properties, which must be found in objects as things in themselves, as sine quibus non of the possibility of their existence, and reflect on the absurdities in which we then find ourselves involved, inasmuch as we are compelled to admit the existence of two infinite things, which are nevertheless not substances, nor anything really inhering in substances, nay, to admit that they are the necessary conditions of the existence of all things, and moreover, that they must continue to exist, although all existing things were annihilated—we cannot blame the good Berkeley for degrading bodies to mere illusory appearances. Nay, even our own existence, which would in this case depend upon the self-existent reality of such a mere nonentity as time, would necessarily be changed with it into mere appearance—an absurdity which no one has as yet been guilty of.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"16.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0010","text":"[[12]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-12) The predicates of the phenomenon can be affixed to the object itself in relation to our sensuous faculty; for example, the red colour or the perfume to the rose. But (illusory) appearance never can be attributed as a predicate to an object, for this very reason, that it attributes to this object in itself that which belongs to it only in relation to our sensuous faculty, or to the subject in general, e.g., the two handles which were formerly ascribed to Saturn. That which is never to be found in the object itself, but always in the relation of the object to the subject, and which moreover is inseparable from our representation of the object, we denominate phenomenon. Thus the predicates of space and time are rightly attributed to objects of the senses as such, and in this there is no illusion. On the contrary, if I ascribe redness of the rose as a thing in itself, or to Saturn his handles, or extension to all external objects, considered as things in themselves, without regarding the determinate relation of these objects to the subject, and without limiting my judgement to that relation—then, and then only, arises illusion.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"16.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","text":"IV. In natural theology, where we think of an object—God—which never can be an object of intuition to us, and even to himself can never be an object of sensuous intuition, we carefully avoid attributing to his intuition the conditions of space and time—and intuition all his cognition must be, and not thought, which always includes limitation. But with what right can we do this if we make them forms of objects as things in themselves, and such, moreover, as would continue to exist as à priori conditions of the existence of things, even though the things themselves were annihilated? For as conditions of all existence in general, space and time must be conditions of the existence of the Supreme Being also. But if we do not thus make them objective forms of all things, there is no other way left than to make them subjective forms of our mode of intuition—external and internal; which is called sensuous, because it is not primitive, that is, is not such as gives in itself the existence of the object of the intuition (a mode of intuition which, so far as we can judge, can belong only to the Creator), but is dependent on the existence of the object, is possible, therefore, only on condition that the representative faculty of the subject is affected by the object.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"16.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0012","text":"It is, moreover, not necessary that we should limit the mode of intuition in space and time to the sensuous faculty of man. It may well be that all finite thinking beings must necessarily in this respect agree with man (though as to this we cannot decide), but sensibility does not on account of this universality cease to be sensibility, for this very reason, that it is a deduced (intuitus derivativus), and not an original (intuitus originarius), consequently not an intellectual intuition, and this intuition, as such, for reasons above mentioned, seems to belong solely to the Supreme Being, but never to a being dependent, quoad its existence, as well as its intuition (which its existence determines and limits relatively to given objects). This latter remark, however, must be taken only as an illustration, and not as any proof of the truth of our æsthetical theory.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"16.12"}],"sha256":"ce7ead8ccac3badf7d6a2a33ad42fc5779c1ed4709c2797c57f3fca1b62c5495","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS","title":"§ 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.","order":17,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_1CTS","text":"### § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.\n\nWe have now completely before us one part of the solution of the grand general problem of transcendental philosophy, namely, the question: “How are synthetical propositions à priori possible?” That is to say, we have shown that we are in possession of pure à priori intuitions, namely, space and time, in which we find, when in a judgement à priori we pass out beyond the given conception, something which is not discoverable in that conception, but is certainly found à priori in the intuition which corresponds to the conception, and can be united synthetically with it. But the judgements which these pure intuitions enable us to make, never reach farther than to objects of the senses, and are valid only for objects of possible experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","text":"### § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.\n\nWe have now completely before us one part of the solution of the grand general problem of transcendental philosophy, namely, the question: “How are synthetical propositions à priori possible?” That is to say, we have shown that we are in possession of pure à priori intuitions, namely, space and time, in which we find, when in a judgement à priori we pass out beyond the given conception, something which is not discoverable in that conception, but is certainly found à priori in the intuition which corresponds to the conception, and can be united synthetically with it. But the judgements which these pure intuitions enable us to make, never reach farther than to objects of the senses, and are valid only for objects of possible experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"17.1"}],"sha256":"981feb5395a71ac211d1a07298ffd4d3a84d5abad674eb37aaad79ee5b4ee598","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0","title":"I. Of Logic in General.","order":18,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_ILG0","text":"# Transcendental Logic\n\n### Second Part—TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC\n\n### INTRODUCTION. Idea of a Transcendental Logic.\n\n### I. Of Logic in General.\n\nOur knowledge springs from two main sources in the mind, first of which is the faculty or power of receiving representations (receptivity for impressions); the second is the power of cognizing by means of these representations (spontaneity in the production of conceptions). Through the first an object is given to us; through the second, it is, in relation to the representation (which is a mere determination of the mind), thought. Intuition and conceptions constitute, therefore, the elements of all our knowledge, so that neither conceptions without an intuition in some way corresponding to them, nor intuition without conceptions, can afford us a cognition. Both are either pure or empirical. They are empirical, when sensation (which presupposes the actual presence of the object) is contained in them; and pure, when no sensation is mixed with the representation. Sensations we may call the matter of sensuous cognition. Pure intuition consequently contains merely the form under which something is intuited, and pure conception only the form of the thought of an object. Only pure intuitions and pure conceptions are possible à priori; the empirical only à posteriori.\n\nWe apply the term sensibility to the receptivity of the mind for impressions, in so far as it is in some way affected; and, on the other hand, we call the faculty of spontaneously producing representations, or the spontaneity of cognition, understanding. Our nature is so constituted that intuition with us never can be other than sensuous, that is, it contains only the mode in which we are affected by objects. On the other hand, the faculty of thinking the object of sensuous intuition is the understanding. Neither of these faculties has a preference over the other. Without the sensuous faculty no object would be given to us, and without the understanding no object would be thought. Thoughts without content are void; intuitions without conceptions, blind. Hence it is as necessary for the mind to make its conceptions sensuous (that is, to join to them the object in intuition), as to make its intuitions intelligible (that is, to bring them under conceptions). Neither of these faculties can exchange its proper function. Understanding cannot intuite, and the sensuous faculty cannot think. In no other way than from the united operation of both, can knowledge arise. But no one ought, on this account, to overlook the difference of the elements contributed by each; we have rather great reason carefully to separate and distinguish them. We therefore distinguish the science of the laws of sensibility, that is, æsthetic, from the science of the laws of the understanding, that is, logic.\n\nNow, logic in its turn may be considered as twofold—namely, as logic of the general, or of the particular use of the understanding. The first contains the absolutely necessary laws of thought, without which no use whatsoever of the understanding is possible, and gives laws therefore to the understanding, without regard to the difference of objects on which it may be employed. The logic of the particular use of the understanding contains the laws of correct thinking upon a particular class of objects. The former may be called elemental logic—the latter, the organon of this or that particular science. The latter is for the most part employed in the schools, as a propædeutic to the sciences, although, indeed, according to the course of human reason, it is the last thing we arrive at, when the science has been already matured, and needs only the finishing touches towards its correction and completion; for our knowledge of the objects of our attempted science must be tolerably extensive and complete before we can indicate the laws by which a science of these objects can be established.\n\nGeneral logic is again either pure or applied. In the former, we abstract all the empirical conditions under which the understanding is exercised; for example, the influence of the senses, the play of the fantasy or imagination, the laws of the memory, the force of habit, of inclination, etc., consequently also, the sources of prejudice—in a word, we abstract all causes from which particular cognitions arise, because these causes regard the understanding under certain circumstances of its application, and, to the knowledge of them experience is required. Pure general logic has to do, therefore, merely with pure à priori principles, and is a canon of understanding and reason, but only in respect of the formal part of their use, be the content what it may, empirical or transcendental. General logic is called applied, when it is directed to the laws of the use of the understanding, under the subjective empirical conditions which psychology teaches us. It has therefore empirical principles, although, at the same time, it is in so far general, that it applies to the exercise of the understanding, without regard to the difference of objects. On this account, moreover, it is neither a canon of the understanding in general, nor an organon of a particular science, but merely a cathartic of the human understanding.\n\nIn general logic, therefore, that part which constitutes pure logic must be carefully distinguished from that which constitutes applied (though still general) logic. The former alone is properly science, although short and dry, as the methodical exposition of an elemental doctrine of the understanding ought to be. In this, therefore, logicians must always bear in mind two rules:\n\n1. As general logic, it makes abstraction of all content of the cognition of the understanding, and of the difference of objects, and has to do with nothing but the mere form of thought.\n\n2. As pure logic, it has no empirical principles, and consequently draws nothing (contrary to the common persuasion) from psychology, which therefore has no influence on the canon of the understanding. It is a demonstrated doctrine, and everything in it must be certain completely à priori.\n\nWhat I called applied logic (contrary to the common acceptation of this term, according to which it should contain certain exercises for the scholar, for which pure logic gives the rules), is a representation of the understanding, and of the rules of its necessary employment in concreto, that is to say, under the accidental conditions of the subject, which may either hinder or promote this employment, and which are all given only empirically. Thus applied logic treats of attention, its impediments and consequences, of the origin of error, of the state of doubt, hesitation, conviction, etc., and to it is related pure general logic in the same way that pure morality, which contains only the necessary moral laws of a free will, is related to practical ethics, which considers these laws under all the impediments of feelings, inclinations, and passions to which men are more or less subjected, and which never can furnish us with a true and demonstrated science, because it, as well as applied logic, requires empirical and psychological principles.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","text":"# Transcendental Logic\n\n### Second Part—TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC\n\n### INTRODUCTION. Idea of a Transcendental Logic.\n\n### I. Of Logic in General.\n\nOur knowledge springs from two main sources in the mind, first of which is the faculty or power of receiving representations (receptivity for impressions); the second is the power of cognizing by means of these representations (spontaneity in the production of conceptions). Through the first an object is given to us; through the second, it is, in relation to the representation (which is a mere determination of the mind), thought. Intuition and conceptions constitute, therefore, the elements of all our knowledge, so that neither conceptions without an intuition in some way corresponding to them, nor intuition without conceptions, can afford us a cognition. Both are either pure or empirical. They are empirical, when sensation (which presupposes the actual presence of the object) is contained in them; and pure, when no sensation is mixed with the representation. Sensations we may call the matter of sensuous cognition. Pure intuition consequently contains merely the form under which something is intuited, and pure conception only the form of the thought of an object. Only pure intuitions and pure conceptions are possible à priori; the empirical only à posteriori.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"18.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","text":"We apply the term sensibility to the receptivity of the mind for impressions, in so far as it is in some way affected; and, on the other hand, we call the faculty of spontaneously producing representations, or the spontaneity of cognition, understanding. Our nature is so constituted that intuition with us never can be other than sensuous, that is, it contains only the mode in which we are affected by objects. On the other hand, the faculty of thinking the object of sensuous intuition is the understanding. Neither of these faculties has a preference over the other. Without the sensuous faculty no object would be given to us, and without the understanding no object would be thought. Thoughts without content are void; intuitions without conceptions, blind. Hence it is as necessary for the mind to make its conceptions sensuous (that is, to join to them the object in intuition), as to make its intuitions intelligible (that is, to bring them under conceptions). Neither of these faculties can exchange its proper function. Understanding cannot intuite, and the sensuous faculty cannot think. In no other way than from the united operation of both, can knowledge arise. But no one ought, on this account, to overlook the difference of the elements contributed by each; we have rather great reason carefully to separate and distinguish them. We therefore distinguish the science of the laws of sensibility, that is, æsthetic, from the science of the laws of the understanding, that is, logic.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"18.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","text":"Now, logic in its turn may be considered as twofold—namely, as logic of the general, or of the particular use of the understanding. The first contains the absolutely necessary laws of thought, without which no use whatsoever of the understanding is possible, and gives laws therefore to the understanding, without regard to the difference of objects on which it may be employed. The logic of the particular use of the understanding contains the laws of correct thinking upon a particular class of objects. The former may be called elemental logic—the latter, the organon of this or that particular science. The latter is for the most part employed in the schools, as a propædeutic to the sciences, although, indeed, according to the course of human reason, it is the last thing we arrive at, when the science has been already matured, and needs only the finishing touches towards its correction and completion; for our knowledge of the objects of our attempted science must be tolerably extensive and complete before we can indicate the laws by which a science of these objects can be established.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"18.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","text":"General logic is again either pure or applied. In the former, we abstract all the empirical conditions under which the understanding is exercised; for example, the influence of the senses, the play of the fantasy or imagination, the laws of the memory, the force of habit, of inclination, etc., consequently also, the sources of prejudice—in a word, we abstract all causes from which particular cognitions arise, because these causes regard the understanding under certain circumstances of its application, and, to the knowledge of them experience is required. Pure general logic has to do, therefore, merely with pure à priori principles, and is a canon of understanding and reason, but only in respect of the formal part of their use, be the content what it may, empirical or transcendental. General logic is called applied, when it is directed to the laws of the use of the understanding, under the subjective empirical conditions which psychology teaches us. It has therefore empirical principles, although, at the same time, it is in so far general, that it applies to the exercise of the understanding, without regard to the difference of objects. On this account, moreover, it is neither a canon of the understanding in general, nor an organon of a particular science, but merely a cathartic of the human understanding.\n\nIn general logic, therefore, that part which constitutes pure logic must be carefully distinguished from that which constitutes applied (though still general) logic. The former alone is properly science, although short and dry, as the methodical exposition of an elemental doctrine of the understanding ought to be. In this, therefore, logicians must always bear in mind two rules:","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"18.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","text":"1. As general logic, it makes abstraction of all content of the cognition of the understanding, and of the difference of objects, and has to do with nothing but the mere form of thought.\n\n2. As pure logic, it has no empirical principles, and consequently draws nothing (contrary to the common persuasion) from psychology, which therefore has no influence on the canon of the understanding. It is a demonstrated doctrine, and everything in it must be certain completely à priori.\n\nWhat I called applied logic (contrary to the common acceptation of this term, according to which it should contain certain exercises for the scholar, for which pure logic gives the rules), is a representation of the understanding, and of the rules of its necessary employment in concreto, that is to say, under the accidental conditions of the subject, which may either hinder or promote this employment, and which are all given only empirically. Thus applied logic treats of attention, its impediments and consequences, of the origin of error, of the state of doubt, hesitation, conviction, etc., and to it is related pure general logic in the same way that pure morality, which contains only the necessary moral laws of a free will, is related to practical ethics, which considers these laws under all the impediments of feelings, inclinations, and passions to which men are more or less subjected, and which never can furnish us with a true and demonstrated science, because it, as well as applied logic, requires empirical and psychological principles.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"18.5"}],"sha256":"007f27531f5e13ae9b5618996295c657486539cfc87e28a65bdae0245178b0ff","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0","title":"II. Of Transcendental Logic.","order":19,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_ITL0","text":"### II. Of Transcendental Logic.\n\nGeneral logic, as we have seen, makes abstraction of all content of cognition, that is, of all relation of cognition to its object, and regards only the logical form in the relation of cognitions to each other, that is, the form of thought in general. But as we have both pure and empirical intuitions (as transcendental æsthetic proves), in like manner a distinction might be drawn between pure and empirical thought (of objects). In this case, there would exist a kind of logic, in which we should not make abstraction of all content of cognition; for or logic which should comprise merely the laws of pure thought (of an object), would of course exclude all those cognitions which were of empirical content. This kind of logic would also examine the origin of our cognitions of objects, so far as that origin cannot be ascribed to the objects themselves; while, on the contrary, general logic has nothing to do with the origin of our cognitions, but contemplates our representations, be they given primitively à priori in ourselves, or be they only of empirical origin, solely according to the laws which the understanding observes in employing them in the process of thought, in relation to each other. Consequently, general logic treats of the form of the understanding only, which can be applied to representations, from whatever source they may have arisen.\n\nAnd here I shall make a remark, which the reader must bear well in mind in the course of the following considerations, to wit, that not every cognition à priori, but only those through which we cognize that and how certain representations (intuitions or conceptions) are applied or are possible only à priori; that is to say, the à priori possibility of cognition and the à priori use of it are transcendental. Therefore neither is space, nor any à priori geometrical determination of space, a transcendental Representation, but only the knowledge that such a representation is not of empirical origin, and the possibility of its relating to objects of experience, although itself à priori, can be called transcendental. So also, the application of space to objects in general would be transcendental; but if it be limited to objects of sense it is empirical. Thus, the distinction of the transcendental and empirical belongs only to the critique of cognitions, and does not concern the relation of these to their object.\n\nAccordingly, in the expectation that there may perhaps be conceptions which relate à priori to objects, not as pure or sensuous intuitions, but merely as acts of pure thought (which are therefore conceptions, but neither of empirical nor æsthetical origin)—in this expectation, I say, we form to ourselves, by anticipation, the idea of a science of pure understanding and rational cognition, by means of which we may cogitate objects entirely à priori. A science of this kind, which should determine the origin, the extent, and the objective validity of such cognitions, must be called transcendental logic, because it has not, like general logic, to do with the laws of understanding and reason in relation to empirical as well as pure rational cognitions without distinction, but concerns itself with these only in an à priori relation to objects.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","text":"### II. Of Transcendental Logic.\n\nGeneral logic, as we have seen, makes abstraction of all content of cognition, that is, of all relation of cognition to its object, and regards only the logical form in the relation of cognitions to each other, that is, the form of thought in general. But as we have both pure and empirical intuitions (as transcendental æsthetic proves), in like manner a distinction might be drawn between pure and empirical thought (of objects). In this case, there would exist a kind of logic, in which we should not make abstraction of all content of cognition; for or logic which should comprise merely the laws of pure thought (of an object), would of course exclude all those cognitions which were of empirical content. This kind of logic would also examine the origin of our cognitions of objects, so far as that origin cannot be ascribed to the objects themselves; while, on the contrary, general logic has nothing to do with the origin of our cognitions, but contemplates our representations, be they given primitively à priori in ourselves, or be they only of empirical origin, solely according to the laws which the understanding observes in employing them in the process of thought, in relation to each other. Consequently, general logic treats of the form of the understanding only, which can be applied to representations, from whatever source they may have arisen.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"19.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","text":"And here I shall make a remark, which the reader must bear well in mind in the course of the following considerations, to wit, that not every cognition à priori, but only those through which we cognize that and how certain representations (intuitions or conceptions) are applied or are possible only à priori; that is to say, the à priori possibility of cognition and the à priori use of it are transcendental. Therefore neither is space, nor any à priori geometrical determination of space, a transcendental Representation, but only the knowledge that such a representation is not of empirical origin, and the possibility of its relating to objects of experience, although itself à priori, can be called transcendental. So also, the application of space to objects in general would be transcendental; but if it be limited to objects of sense it is empirical. Thus, the distinction of the transcendental and empirical belongs only to the critique of cognitions, and does not concern the relation of these to their object.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"19.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","text":"Accordingly, in the expectation that there may perhaps be conceptions which relate à priori to objects, not as pure or sensuous intuitions, but merely as acts of pure thought (which are therefore conceptions, but neither of empirical nor æsthetical origin)—in this expectation, I say, we form to ourselves, by anticipation, the idea of a science of pure understanding and rational cognition, by means of which we may cogitate objects entirely à priori. A science of this kind, which should determine the origin, the extent, and the objective validity of such cognitions, must be called transcendental logic, because it has not, like general logic, to do with the laws of understanding and reason in relation to empirical as well as pure rational cognitions without distinction, but concerns itself with these only in an à priori relation to objects.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"19.3"}],"sha256":"ae5dd74dd038bd5e71c0a06d24d48fbe1310809d1fcecc84758da603c2ba709f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL","title":"III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.","order":20,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_IDGL","text":"### III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.\n\nThe old question with which people sought to push logicians into a corner, so that they must either have recourse to pitiful sophisms or confess their ignorance, and consequently the vanity of their whole art, is this: “What is truth?” The definition of the word truth, to wit, “the accordance of the cognition with its object,” is presupposed in the question; but we desire to be told, in the answer to it, what is the universal and secure criterion of the truth of every cognition.\n\nTo know what questions we may reasonably propose is in itself a strong evidence of sagacity and intelligence. For if a question be in itself absurd and unsusceptible of a rational answer, it is attended with the danger—not to mention the shame that falls upon the person who proposes it—of seducing the unguarded listener into making absurd answers, and we are presented with the ridiculous spectacle of one (as the ancients said) “milking the he-goat, and the other holding a sieve.”\n\nIf truth consists in the accordance of a cognition with its object, this object must be, ipso facto, distinguished from all others; for a cognition is false if it does not accord with the object to which it relates, although it contains something which may be affirmed of other objects. Now an universal criterion of truth would be that which is valid for all cognitions, without distinction of their objects. But it is evident that since, in the case of such a criterion, we make abstraction of all the content of a cognition (that is, of all relation to its object), and truth relates precisely to this content, it must be utterly absurd to ask for a mark of the truth of this content of cognition; and that, accordingly, a sufficient, and at the same time universal, test of truth cannot possibly be found. As we have already termed the content of a cognition its matter, we shall say: “Of the truth of our cognitions in respect of their matter, no universal test can be demanded, because such a demand is self-contradictory.”\n\nOn the other hand, with regard to our cognition in respect of its mere form (excluding all content), it is equally manifest that logic, in so far as it exhibits the universal and necessary laws of the understanding, must in these very laws present us with criteria of truth. Whatever contradicts these rules is false, because thereby the understanding is made to contradict its own universal laws of thought; that is, to contradict itself. These criteria, however, apply solely to the form of truth, that is, of thought in general, and in so far they are perfectly accurate, yet not sufficient. For although a cognition may be perfectly accurate as to logical form, that is, not self-contradictory, it is notwithstanding quite possible that it may not stand in agreement with its object. Consequently, the merely logical criterion of truth, namely, the accordance of a cognition with the universal and formal laws of understanding and reason, is nothing more than the conditio sine qua non, or negative condition of all truth. Farther than this logic cannot go, and the error which depends not on the form, but on the content of the cognition, it has no test to discover.\n\nGeneral logic, then, resolves the whole formal business of understanding and reason into its elements, and exhibits them as principles of all logical judging of our cognitions. This part of logic may, therefore, be called analytic, and is at least the negative test of truth, because all cognitions must first of an be estimated and tried according to these laws before we proceed to investigate them in respect of their content, in order to discover whether they contain positive truth in regard to their object. Because, however, the mere form of a cognition, accurately as it may accord with logical laws, is insufficient to supply us with material (objective) truth, no one, by means of logic alone, can venture to predicate anything of or decide concerning objects, unless he has obtained, independently of logic, well-grounded information about them, in order afterwards to examine, according to logical laws, into the use and connection, in a cohering whole, of that information, or, what is still better, merely to test it by them. Notwithstanding, there lies so seductive a charm in the possession of a specious art like this—an art which gives to all our cognitions the form of the understanding, although with respect to the content thereof we may be sadly deficient—that general logic, which is merely a canon of judgement, has been employed as an organon for the actual production, or rather for the semblance of production, of objective assertions, and has thus been grossly misapplied. Now general logic, in its assumed character of organon, is called dialectic.\n\nDifferent as are the significations in which the ancients used this term for a science or an art, we may safely infer, from their actual employment of it, that with them it was nothing else than a logic of illusion—a sophistical art for giving ignorance, nay, even intentional sophistries, the colouring of truth, in which the thoroughness of procedure which logic requires was imitated, and their topic employed to cloak the empty pretensions. Now it may be taken as a safe and useful warning, that general logic, considered as an organon, must always be a logic of illusion, that is, be dialectical, for, as it teaches us nothing whatever respecting the content of our cognitions, but merely the formal conditions of their accordance with the understanding, which do not relate to and are quite indifferent in respect of objects, any attempt to employ it as an instrument (organon) in order to extend and enlarge the range of our knowledge must end in mere prating; any one being able to maintain or oppose, with some appearance of truth, any single assertion whatever.\n\nSuch instruction is quite unbecoming the dignity of philosophy. For these reasons we have chosen to denominate this part of logic dialectic, in the sense of a critique of dialectical illusion, and we wish the term to be so understood in this place.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0001","text":"### III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.\n\nThe old question with which people sought to push logicians into a corner, so that they must either have recourse to pitiful sophisms or confess their ignorance, and consequently the vanity of their whole art, is this: “What is truth?” The definition of the word truth, to wit, “the accordance of the cognition with its object,” is presupposed in the question; but we desire to be told, in the answer to it, what is the universal and secure criterion of the truth of every cognition.\n\nTo know what questions we may reasonably propose is in itself a strong evidence of sagacity and intelligence. For if a question be in itself absurd and unsusceptible of a rational answer, it is attended with the danger—not to mention the shame that falls upon the person who proposes it—of seducing the unguarded listener into making absurd answers, and we are presented with the ridiculous spectacle of one (as the ancients said) “milking the he-goat, and the other holding a sieve.”","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"20.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","text":"If truth consists in the accordance of a cognition with its object, this object must be, ipso facto, distinguished from all others; for a cognition is false if it does not accord with the object to which it relates, although it contains something which may be affirmed of other objects. Now an universal criterion of truth would be that which is valid for all cognitions, without distinction of their objects. But it is evident that since, in the case of such a criterion, we make abstraction of all the content of a cognition (that is, of all relation to its object), and truth relates precisely to this content, it must be utterly absurd to ask for a mark of the truth of this content of cognition; and that, accordingly, a sufficient, and at the same time universal, test of truth cannot possibly be found. As we have already termed the content of a cognition its matter, we shall say: “Of the truth of our cognitions in respect of their matter, no universal test can be demanded, because such a demand is self-contradictory.”","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"20.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","text":"On the other hand, with regard to our cognition in respect of its mere form (excluding all content), it is equally manifest that logic, in so far as it exhibits the universal and necessary laws of the understanding, must in these very laws present us with criteria of truth. Whatever contradicts these rules is false, because thereby the understanding is made to contradict its own universal laws of thought; that is, to contradict itself. These criteria, however, apply solely to the form of truth, that is, of thought in general, and in so far they are perfectly accurate, yet not sufficient. For although a cognition may be perfectly accurate as to logical form, that is, not self-contradictory, it is notwithstanding quite possible that it may not stand in agreement with its object. Consequently, the merely logical criterion of truth, namely, the accordance of a cognition with the universal and formal laws of understanding and reason, is nothing more than the conditio sine qua non, or negative condition of all truth. Farther than this logic cannot go, and the error which depends not on the form, but on the content of the cognition, it has no test to discover.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"20.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","text":"General logic, then, resolves the whole formal business of understanding and reason into its elements, and exhibits them as principles of all logical judging of our cognitions. This part of logic may, therefore, be called analytic, and is at least the negative test of truth, because all cognitions must first of an be estimated and tried according to these laws before we proceed to investigate them in respect of their content, in order to discover whether they contain positive truth in regard to their object. Because, however, the mere form of a cognition, accurately as it may accord with logical laws, is insufficient to supply us with material (objective) truth, no one, by means of logic alone, can venture to predicate anything of or decide concerning objects, unless he has obtained, independently of logic, well-grounded information about them, in order afterwards to examine, according to logical laws, into the use and connection, in a cohering whole, of that information, or, what is still better, merely to test it by them. Notwithstanding, there lies so seductive a charm in the possession of a specious art like this—an art which gives to all our cognitions the form of the understanding, although with respect to the content thereof we may be sadly deficient—that general logic, which is merely a canon of judgement, has been employed as an organon for the actual production, or rather for the semblance of production, of objective assertions, and has thus been grossly misapplied. Now general logic, in its assumed character of organon, is called dialectic.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"20.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","text":"Different as are the significations in which the ancients used this term for a science or an art, we may safely infer, from their actual employment of it, that with them it was nothing else than a logic of illusion—a sophistical art for giving ignorance, nay, even intentional sophistries, the colouring of truth, in which the thoroughness of procedure which logic requires was imitated, and their topic employed to cloak the empty pretensions. Now it may be taken as a safe and useful warning, that general logic, considered as an organon, must always be a logic of illusion, that is, be dialectical, for, as it teaches us nothing whatever respecting the content of our cognitions, but merely the formal conditions of their accordance with the understanding, which do not relate to and are quite indifferent in respect of objects, any attempt to employ it as an instrument (organon) in order to extend and enlarge the range of our knowledge must end in mere prating; any one being able to maintain or oppose, with some appearance of truth, any single assertion whatever.\n\nSuch instruction is quite unbecoming the dignity of philosophy. For these reasons we have chosen to denominate this part of logic dialectic, in the sense of a critique of dialectical illusion, and we wish the term to be so understood in this place.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"20.5"}],"sha256":"53d51769011894c47d9a85c0a0726c450b3b079a048448890254968ce74d0706","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL","title":"IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.","order":21,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_IDTL","text":"### IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.\n\nIn transcendental logic we isolate the understanding (as in transcendental æsthetic the sensibility) and select from our cognition merely that part of thought which has its origin in the understanding alone. The exercise of this pure cognition, however, depends upon this as its condition, that objects to which it may be applied be given to us in intuition, for without intuition the whole of our cognition is without objects, and is therefore quite void. That part of transcendental logic, then, which treats of the elements of pure cognition of the understanding, and of the principles without which no object at all can be thought, is transcendental analytic, and at the same time a logic of truth. For no cognition can contradict it, without losing at the same time all content, that is, losing all reference to an object, and therefore all truth. But because we are very easily seduced into employing these pure cognitions and principles of the understanding by themselves, and that even beyond the boundaries of experience, which yet is the only source whence we can obtain matter (objects) on which those pure conceptions may be employed—understanding runs the risk of making, by means of empty sophisms, a material and objective use of the mere formal principles of the pure understanding, and of passing judgements on objects without distinction—objects which are not given to us, nay, perhaps cannot be given to us in any way. Now, as it ought properly to be only a canon for judging of the empirical use of the understanding, this kind of logic is misused when we seek to employ it as an organon of the universal and unlimited exercise of the understanding, and attempt with the pure understanding alone to judge synthetically, affirm, and determine respecting objects in general. In this case the exercise of the pure understanding becomes dialectical. The second part of our transcendental logic must therefore be a critique of dialectical illusion, and this critique we shall term transcendental dialectic—not meaning it as an art of producing dogmatically such illusion (an art which is unfortunately too current among the practitioners of metaphysical juggling), but as a critique of understanding and reason in regard to their hyperphysical use. This critique will expose the groundless nature of the pretensions of these two faculties, and invalidate their claims to the discovery and enlargement of our cognitions merely by means of transcendental principles, and show that the proper employment of these faculties is to test the judgements made by the pure understanding, and to guard it from sophistical delusion.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","text":"### IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.\n\nIn transcendental logic we isolate the understanding (as in transcendental æsthetic the sensibility) and select from our cognition merely that part of thought which has its origin in the understanding alone. The exercise of this pure cognition, however, depends upon this as its condition, that objects to which it may be applied be given to us in intuition, for without intuition the whole of our cognition is without objects, and is therefore quite void. That part of transcendental logic, then, which treats of the elements of pure cognition of the understanding, and of the principles without which no object at all can be thought, is transcendental analytic, and at the same time a logic of truth. For no cognition can contradict it, without losing at the same time all content, that is, losing all reference to an object, and therefore all truth. But because we are very easily seduced into employing these pure cognitions and principles of the understanding by themselves, and that even beyond the boundaries of experience, which yet is the only source whence we can obtain matter (objects) on which those pure conceptions may be employed—understanding runs the risk of making, by means of empty sophisms, a material and objective use of the mere formal principles of the pure understanding, and of passing judgements on objects without distinction—objects which are not given to us, nay, perhaps cannot be given to us in any way. Now, as it ought properly to be only a canon for judging of the empirical use of the understanding, this kind of logic is misused when we seek to employ it as an organon of the universal and unlimited exercise of the understanding, and attempt with the pure understanding alone to judge synthetically, affirm, and determine respecting objects in general. In this case the exercise of the pure understanding becomes dialectical. The second part of our transcendental logic must therefore be a critique of dialectical illusion, and this critique we shall term transcendental dialectic—not meaning it as an art of producing dogmatically such illusion (an art which is unfortunately too current among the practitioners of metaphysical juggling), but as a critique of understanding and reason in regard to their hyperphysical use. This critique will expose the groundless nature of the pretensions of these two faculties, and invalidate their claims to the discovery and enlargement of our cognitions merely by means of transcendental principles, and show that the proper employment of these faculties is to test the judgements made by the pure understanding, and to guard it from sophistical delusion.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"21.1"}],"sha256":"685d70282aa91801762cfcfb45078e283cd7a9a633d2e48f81f07a7f9bda3587","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL","title":"FIRST DIVISION. TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC. TRANSCENDENTAL ANALYTIC. § 1","order":22,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_FDTL","text":"### FIRST DIVISION. TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC. TRANSCENDENTAL ANALYTIC. § 1\n\nTranscendental analytic is the dissection of the whole of our à priori knowledge into the elements of the pure cognition of the understanding. In order to effect our purpose, it is necessary: (1) That the conceptions be pure and not empirical; (2) That they belong not to intuition and sensibility, but to thought and understanding; (3) That they be elementary conceptions, and as such, quite different from deduced or compound conceptions; (4) That our table of these elementary conceptions be complete, and fill up the whole sphere of the pure understanding. Now this completeness of a science cannot be accepted with confidence on the guarantee of a mere estimate of its existence in an aggregate formed only by means of repeated experiments and attempts. The completeness which we require is possible only by means of an idea of the totality of the à priori cognition of the understanding, and through the thereby determined division of the conceptions which form the said whole; consequently, only by means of their connection in a system. Pure understanding distinguishes itself not merely from everything empirical, but also completely from all sensibility. It is a unity self-subsistent, self-sufficient, and not to be enlarged by any additions from without. Hence the sum of its cognition constitutes a system to be determined by and comprised under an idea; and the completeness and articulation of this system can at the same time serve as a test of the correctness and genuineness of all the parts of cognition that belong to it. The whole of this part of transcendental logic consists of two books, of which the one contains the conceptions, and the other the principles of pure understanding.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","text":"### FIRST DIVISION. TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC. TRANSCENDENTAL ANALYTIC. § 1\n\nTranscendental analytic is the dissection of the whole of our à priori knowledge into the elements of the pure cognition of the understanding. In order to effect our purpose, it is necessary: (1) That the conceptions be pure and not empirical; (2) That they belong not to intuition and sensibility, but to thought and understanding; (3) That they be elementary conceptions, and as such, quite different from deduced or compound conceptions; (4) That our table of these elementary conceptions be complete, and fill up the whole sphere of the pure understanding. Now this completeness of a science cannot be accepted with confidence on the guarantee of a mere estimate of its existence in an aggregate formed only by means of repeated experiments and attempts. The completeness which we require is possible only by means of an idea of the totality of the à priori cognition of the understanding, and through the thereby determined division of the conceptions which form the said whole; consequently, only by means of their connection in a system. Pure understanding distinguishes itself not merely from everything empirical, but also completely from all sensibility. It is a unity self-subsistent, self-sufficient, and not to be enlarged by any additions from without. Hence the sum of its cognition constitutes a system to be determined by and comprised under an idea; and the completeness and articulation of this system can at the same time serve as a test of the correctness and genuineness of all the parts of cognition that belong to it. The whole of this part of transcendental logic consists of two books, of which the one contains the conceptions, and the other the principles of pure understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"22.1"}],"sha256":"52f8d30f77c35337fb54ead4d2a5802e519888d55e99530b8ba2e61ead568d3e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC","title":"BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2","order":23,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_BIAC","text":"### BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2\n\nBy the term Analytic of Conceptions, I do not understand the analysis of these, or the usual process in philosophical investigations of dissecting the conceptions which present themselves, according to their content, and so making them clear; but I mean the hitherto little attempted dissection of the faculty of understanding itself, in order to investigate the possibility of conceptions à priori, by looking for them in the understanding alone, as their birthplace, and analysing the pure use of this faculty. For this is the proper duty of a transcendental philosophy; what remains is the logical treatment of the conceptions in philosophy in general. We shall therefore follow up the pure conceptions even to their germs and beginnings in the human understanding, in which they lie, until they are developed on occasions presented by experience, and, freed by the same understanding from the empirical conditions attaching to them, are set forth in their unalloyed purity.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","text":"### BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2\n\nBy the term Analytic of Conceptions, I do not understand the analysis of these, or the usual process in philosophical investigations of dissecting the conceptions which present themselves, according to their content, and so making them clear; but I mean the hitherto little attempted dissection of the faculty of understanding itself, in order to investigate the possibility of conceptions à priori, by looking for them in the understanding alone, as their birthplace, and analysing the pure use of this faculty. For this is the proper duty of a transcendental philosophy; what remains is the logical treatment of the conceptions in philosophy in general. We shall therefore follow up the pure conceptions even to their germs and beginnings in the human understanding, in which they lie, until they are developed on occasions presented by experience, and, freed by the same understanding from the empirical conditions attaching to them, are set forth in their unalloyed purity.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"23.1"}],"sha256":"616d0d5522dfb8ff4f4e62eccf1a677c09bd432b9bcb9841a9af6d4bca06dde3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3","title":"Introductory § 3","order":24,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_INT3","text":"### Chapter I. Of the Transcendental Clue to the Discovery of all Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Introductory § 3\n\nWhen we call into play a faculty of cognition, different conceptions manifest themselves according to the different circumstances, and make known this faculty, and assemble themselves into a more or less extensive collection, according to the time or penetration that has been applied to the consideration of them. Where this process, conducted as it is mechanically, so to speak, will end, cannot be determined with certainty. Besides, the conceptions which we discover in this haphazard manner present themselves by no means in order and systematic unity, but are at last coupled together only according to resemblances to each other, and arranged in series, according to the quantity of their content, from the simpler to the more complex—series which are anything but systematic, though not altogether without a certain kind of method in their construction.\n\nTranscendental philosophy has the advantage, and moreover the duty, of searching for its conceptions according to a principle; because these conceptions spring pure and unmixed out of the understanding as an absolute unity, and therefore must be connected with each other according to one conception or idea. A connection of this kind, however, furnishes us with a ready prepared rule, by which its proper place may be assigned to every pure conception of the understanding, and the completeness of the system of all be determined à priori—both which would otherwise have been dependent on mere choice or chance.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","text":"### Chapter I. Of the Transcendental Clue to the Discovery of all Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Introductory § 3\n\nWhen we call into play a faculty of cognition, different conceptions manifest themselves according to the different circumstances, and make known this faculty, and assemble themselves into a more or less extensive collection, according to the time or penetration that has been applied to the consideration of them. Where this process, conducted as it is mechanically, so to speak, will end, cannot be determined with certainty. Besides, the conceptions which we discover in this haphazard manner present themselves by no means in order and systematic unity, but are at last coupled together only according to resemblances to each other, and arranged in series, according to the quantity of their content, from the simpler to the more complex—series which are anything but systematic, though not altogether without a certain kind of method in their construction.\n\nTranscendental philosophy has the advantage, and moreover the duty, of searching for its conceptions according to a principle; because these conceptions spring pure and unmixed out of the understanding as an absolute unity, and therefore must be connected with each other according to one conception or idea. A connection of this kind, however, furnishes us with a ready prepared rule, by which its proper place may be assigned to every pure conception of the understanding, and the completeness of the system of all be determined à priori—both which would otherwise have been dependent on mere choice or chance.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"24.1"}],"sha256":"e4c1bf9d0ba2abc5e0f19eb301014dace30f48efd2935725f4bf8fd73d8095c2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU","title":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4","order":25,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SILU","text":"### Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4\n\nThe understanding was defined above only negatively, as a non-sensuous faculty of cognition. Now, independently of sensibility, we cannot possibly have any intuition; consequently, the understanding is no faculty of intuition. But besides intuition there is no other mode of cognition, except through conceptions; consequently, the cognition of every, at least of every human, understanding is a cognition through conceptions—not intuitive, but discursive. All intuitions, as sensuous, depend on affections; conceptions, therefore, upon functions. By the word function I understand the unity of the act of arranging diverse representations under one common representation. Conceptions, then, are based on the spontaneity of thought, as sensuous intuitions are on the receptivity of impressions. Now, the understanding cannot make any other use of these conceptions than to judge by means of them. As no representation, except an intuition, relates immediately to its object, a conception never relates immediately to an object, but only to some other representation thereof, be that an intuition or itself a conception. A judgement, therefore, is the mediate cognition of an object, consequently the representation of a representation of it. In every judgement there is a conception which applies to, and is valid for many other conceptions, and which among these comprehends also a given representation, this last being immediately connected with an object. For example, in the judgement—“All bodies are divisible,” our conception of divisible applies to various other conceptions; among these, however, it is here particularly applied to the conception of body, and this conception of body relates to certain phenomena which occur to us. These objects, therefore, are mediately represented by the conception of divisibility. All judgements, accordingly, are functions of unity in our representations, inasmuch as, instead of an immediate, a higher representation, which comprises this and various others, is used for our cognition of the object, and thereby many possible cognitions are collected into one. But we can reduce all acts of the understanding to judgements, so that understanding may be represented as the faculty of judging. For it is, according to what has been said above, a faculty of thought. Now thought is cognition by means of conceptions. But conceptions, as predicates of possible judgements, relate to some representation of a yet undetermined object. Thus the conception of body indicates something—for example, metal—which can be cognized by means of that conception. It is therefore a conception, for the reason alone that other representations are contained under it, by means of which it can relate to objects. It is therefore the predicate to a possible judgement; for example: “Every metal is a body.” All the functions of the understanding therefore can be discovered, when we can completely exhibit the functions of unity in judgements. And that this may be effected very easily, the following section will show.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","text":"### Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4\n\nThe understanding was defined above only negatively, as a non-sensuous faculty of cognition. Now, independently of sensibility, we cannot possibly have any intuition; consequently, the understanding is no faculty of intuition. But besides intuition there is no other mode of cognition, except through conceptions; consequently, the cognition of every, at least of every human, understanding is a cognition through conceptions—not intuitive, but discursive. All intuitions, as sensuous, depend on affections; conceptions, therefore, upon functions. By the word function I understand the unity of the act of arranging diverse representations under one common representation. Conceptions, then, are based on the spontaneity of thought, as sensuous intuitions are on the receptivity of impressions. Now, the understanding cannot make any other use of these conceptions than to judge by means of them. As no representation, except an intuition, relates immediately to its object, a conception never relates immediately to an object, but only to some other representation thereof, be that an intuition or itself a conception. A judgement, therefore, is the mediate cognition of an object, consequently the representation of a representation of it. In every judgement there is a conception which applies to, and is valid for many other conceptions, and which among these comprehends also a given representation, this last being immediately connected with an object.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"25.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","text":"For example, in the judgement—“All bodies are divisible,” our conception of divisible applies to various other conceptions; among these, however, it is here particularly applied to the conception of body, and this conception of body relates to certain phenomena which occur to us. These objects, therefore, are mediately represented by the conception of divisibility. All judgements, accordingly, are functions of unity in our representations, inasmuch as, instead of an immediate, a higher representation, which comprises this and various others, is used for our cognition of the object, and thereby many possible cognitions are collected into one. But we can reduce all acts of the understanding to judgements, so that understanding may be represented as the faculty of judging. For it is, according to what has been said above, a faculty of thought. Now thought is cognition by means of conceptions. But conceptions, as predicates of possible judgements, relate to some representation of a yet undetermined object. Thus the conception of body indicates something—for example, metal—which can be cognized by means of that conception. It is therefore a conception, for the reason alone that other representations are contained under it, by means of which it can relate to objects. It is therefore the predicate to a possible judgement; for example: “Every metal is a body.” All the functions of the understanding therefore can be discovered, when we can completely exhibit the functions of unity in judgements. And that this may be effected very easily, the following section will show.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"25.2"}],"sha256":"4ef89e750a822653bac22f9d5c6aa85df1d361618117bfc88a3cb446ae65ecfa","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF","title":"Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5","order":26,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SILF","text":"### Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5\n\nIf we abstract all the content of a judgement, and consider only the intellectual form thereof, we find that the function of thought in a judgement can be brought under four heads, of which each contains three momenta. These may be conveniently represented in the following table:\n\n                                    1\n                         _Quantity of judgements_\n                                Universal\n                                Particular\n                                Singular\n\n                      2                           3\n                    _Quality                   Relation_\n                  Affirmative                Categorical\n                  Negative                   Hypothetical\n                  Infinite                   Disjunctive\n\n                                    4\n                                 _Modality_\n                               Problematical\n                               Assertorical\n                               Apodeictical\n\nAs this division appears to differ in some, though not essential points, from the usual technique of logicians, the following observations, for the prevention of otherwise possible misunderstanding, will not be without their use.\n\n1. Logicians say, with justice, that in the use of judgements in syllogisms, singular judgements may be treated like universal ones. For, precisely because a singular judgement has no extent at all, its predicate cannot refer to a part of that which is contained in the conception of the subject and be excluded from the rest. The predicate is valid for the whole conception just as if it were a general conception, and had extent, to the whole of which the predicate applied. On the other hand, let us compare a singular with a general judgement, merely as a cognition, in regard to quantity. The singular judgement relates to the general one, as unity to infinity, and is therefore in itself essentially different. Thus, if we estimate a singular judgement (_judicium singulare_) not merely according to its intrinsic validity as a judgement, but also as a cognition generally, according to its quantity in comparison with that of other cognitions, it is then entirely different from a general judgement (_judicium commune_), and in a complete table of the momenta of thought deserves a separate place—though, indeed, this would not be necessary in a logic limited merely to the consideration of the use of judgements in reference to each other.\n\n2. In like manner, in transcendental logic, infinite must be distinguished from affirmative judgements, although in general logic they are rightly enough classed under affirmative. General logic abstracts all content of the predicate (though it be negative), and only considers whether the said predicate be affirmed or denied of the subject. But transcendental logic considers also the worth or content of this logical affirmation—an affirmation by means of a merely negative predicate, and inquires how much the sum total of our cognition gains by this affirmation. For example, if I say of the soul, “It is not mortal”—by this negative judgement I should at least ward off error. Now, by the proposition, “The soul is not mortal,” I have, in respect of the logical form, really affirmed, inasmuch as I thereby place the soul in the unlimited sphere of immortal beings. Now, because of the whole sphere of possible existences, the mortal occupies one part, and the immortal the other, neither more nor less is affirmed by the proposition than that the soul is one among the infinite multitude of things which remain over, when I take away the whole mortal part. But by this proceeding we accomplish only this much, that the infinite sphere of all possible existences is in so far limited that the mortal is excluded from it, and the soul is placed in the remaining part of the extent of this sphere. But this part remains, notwithstanding this exception, infinite, and more and more parts may be taken away from the whole sphere, without in the slightest degree thereby augmenting or affirmatively determining our conception of the soul. These judgements, therefore, infinite in respect of their logical extent, are, in respect of the content of their cognition, merely limitative; and are consequently entitled to a place in our transcendental table of all the momenta of thought in judgements, because the function of the understanding exercised by them may perhaps be of importance in the field of its pure à priori cognition.\n\n3. All relations of thought in judgements are those (a) of the predicate to the subject; (b) of the principle to its consequence; (c) of the divided cognition and all the members of the division to each other. In the first of these three classes, we consider only two conceptions; in the second, two judgements; in the third, several judgements in relation to each other. The hypothetical proposition, “If perfect justice exists, the obstinately wicked are punished,” contains properly the relation to each other of two propositions, namely, “Perfect justice exists,” and “The obstinately wicked are punished.” Whether these propositions are in themselves true is a question not here decided. Nothing is cogitated by means of this judgement except a certain consequence. Finally, the disjunctive judgement contains a relation of two or more propositions to each other—a relation not of consequence, but of logical opposition, in so far as the sphere of the one proposition excludes that of the other. But it contains at the same time a relation of community, in so far as all the propositions taken together fill up the sphere of the cognition. The disjunctive judgement contains, therefore, the relation of the parts of the whole sphere of a cognition, since the sphere of each part is a complemental part of the sphere of the other, each contributing to form the sum total of the divided cognition. Take, for example, the proposition, “The world exists either through blind chance, or through internal necessity, or through an external cause.” Each of these propositions embraces a part of the sphere of our possible cognition as to the existence of a world; all of them taken together, the whole sphere. To take the cognition out of one of these spheres, is equivalent to placing it in one of the others; and, on the other hand, to place it in one sphere is equivalent to taking it out of the rest. There is, therefore, in a disjunctive judgement a certain community of cognitions, which consists in this, that they mutually exclude each other, yet thereby determine, as a whole, the true cognition, inasmuch as, taken together, they make up the complete content of a particular given cognition. And this is all that I find necessary, for the sake of what follows, to remark in this place.\n\n4. The modality of judgements is a quite peculiar function, with this distinguishing characteristic, that it contributes nothing to the content of a judgement (for besides quantity, quality, and relation, there is nothing more that constitutes the content of a judgement), but concerns itself only with the value of the copula in relation to thought in general. Problematical judgements are those in which the affirmation or negation is accepted as merely possible (ad libitum). In the assertorical, we regard the proposition as real (true); in the apodeictical, we look on it as necessary.[[13]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-13) Thus the two judgements (antecedens et consequens), the relation of which constitutes a hypothetical judgement, likewise those (the members of the division) in whose reciprocity the disjunctive consists, are only problematical. In the example above given the proposition, “There exists perfect justice,” is not stated assertorically, but as an ad libitum judgement, which someone may choose to adopt, and the consequence alone is assertorical. Hence such judgements may be obviously false, and yet, taken problematically, be conditions of our cognition of the truth. Thus the proposition, “The world exists only by blind chance,” is in the disjunctive judgement of problematical import only: that is to say, one may accept it for the moment, and it helps us (like the indication of the wrong road among all the roads that one can take) to find out the true proposition. The problematical proposition is, therefore, that which expresses only logical possibility (which is not objective); that is, it expresses a free choice to admit the validity of such a proposition—a merely arbitrary reception of it into the understanding. The assertorical speaks of logical reality or truth; as, for example, in a hypothetical syllogism, the antecedens presents itself in a problematical form in the major, in an assertorical form in the minor, and it shows that the proposition is in harmony with the laws of the understanding. The apodeictical proposition cogitates the assertorical as determined by these very laws of the understanding, consequently as affirming à priori, and in this manner it expresses logical necessity. Now because all is here gradually incorporated with the understanding—inasmuch as in the first place we judge problematically; then accept assertorically our judgement as true; lastly, affirm it as inseparably united with the understanding, that is, as necessary and apodeictical—we may safely reckon these three functions of modality as so many momenta of thought.\n\n[[13]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-13) Just as if thought were in the first instance a function of the understanding; in the second, of judgement; in the third, of reason. A remark which will be explained in the sequel.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","text":"### Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5\n\nIf we abstract all the content of a judgement, and consider only the intellectual form thereof, we find that the function of thought in a judgement can be brought under four heads, of which each contains three momenta. These may be conveniently represented in the following table:\n\n                                    1\n                         _Quantity of judgements_\n                                Universal\n                                Particular\n                                Singular\n\n                      2                           3\n                    _Quality                   Relation_\n                  Affirmative                Categorical\n                  Negative                   Hypothetical\n                  Infinite                   Disjunctive\n\n                                    4\n                                 _Modality_\n                               Problematical\n                               Assertorical\n                               Apodeictical\n\nAs this division appears to differ in some, though not essential points, from the usual technique of logicians, the following observations, for the prevention of otherwise possible misunderstanding, will not be without their use.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"26.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","text":"1. Logicians say, with justice, that in the use of judgements in syllogisms, singular judgements may be treated like universal ones. For, precisely because a singular judgement has no extent at all, its predicate cannot refer to a part of that which is contained in the conception of the subject and be excluded from the rest. The predicate is valid for the whole conception just as if it were a general conception, and had extent, to the whole of which the predicate applied. On the other hand, let us compare a singular with a general judgement, merely as a cognition, in regard to quantity. The singular judgement relates to the general one, as unity to infinity, and is therefore in itself essentially different. Thus, if we estimate a singular judgement (_judicium singulare_) not merely according to its intrinsic validity as a judgement, but also as a cognition generally, according to its quantity in comparison with that of other cognitions, it is then entirely different from a general judgement (_judicium commune_), and in a complete table of the momenta of thought deserves a separate place—though, indeed, this would not be necessary in a logic limited merely to the consideration of the use of judgements in reference to each other.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"26.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","text":"2. In like manner, in transcendental logic, infinite must be distinguished from affirmative judgements, although in general logic they are rightly enough classed under affirmative. General logic abstracts all content of the predicate (though it be negative), and only considers whether the said predicate be affirmed or denied of the subject. But transcendental logic considers also the worth or content of this logical affirmation—an affirmation by means of a merely negative predicate, and inquires how much the sum total of our cognition gains by this affirmation. For example, if I say of the soul, “It is not mortal”—by this negative judgement I should at least ward off error. Now, by the proposition, “The soul is not mortal,” I have, in respect of the logical form, really affirmed, inasmuch as I thereby place the soul in the unlimited sphere of immortal beings. Now, because of the whole sphere of possible existences, the mortal occupies one part, and the immortal the other, neither more nor less is affirmed by the proposition than that the soul is one among the infinite multitude of things which remain over, when I take away the whole mortal part. But by this proceeding we accomplish only this much, that the infinite sphere of all possible existences is in so far limited that the mortal is excluded from it, and the soul is placed in the remaining part of the extent of this sphere. But this part remains, notwithstanding this exception, infinite, and more and more parts may be taken away from the whole sphere, without in the slightest degree thereby augmenting or affirmatively determining our conception of the soul. These judgements, therefore, infinite in respect of their logical extent, are, in respect of the content of their cognition, merely limitative; and are consequently entitled to a place in our transcendental table of all the momenta of thought in judgements, because the function of the understanding exercised by them may perhaps be of importance in the field of its pure à priori cognition.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"26.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","text":"3. All relations of thought in judgements are those (a) of the predicate to the subject; (b) of the principle to its consequence; (c) of the divided cognition and all the members of the division to each other. In the first of these three classes, we consider only two conceptions; in the second, two judgements; in the third, several judgements in relation to each other. The hypothetical proposition, “If perfect justice exists, the obstinately wicked are punished,” contains properly the relation to each other of two propositions, namely, “Perfect justice exists,” and “The obstinately wicked are punished.” Whether these propositions are in themselves true is a question not here decided. Nothing is cogitated by means of this judgement except a certain consequence. Finally, the disjunctive judgement contains a relation of two or more propositions to each other—a relation not of consequence, but of logical opposition, in so far as the sphere of the one proposition excludes that of the other. But it contains at the same time a relation of community, in so far as all the propositions taken together fill up the sphere of the cognition. The disjunctive judgement contains, therefore, the relation of the parts of the whole sphere of a cognition, since the sphere of each part is a complemental part of the sphere of the other, each contributing to form the sum total of the divided cognition. Take, for example, the proposition, “The world exists either through blind chance, or through internal necessity, or through an external cause.” Each of these propositions embraces a part of the sphere of our possible cognition as to the existence of a world; all of them taken together, the whole sphere. To take the cognition out of one of these spheres, is equivalent to placing it in one of the others; and, on the other hand, to place it in one sphere is equivalent to taking it out of the rest. There is, therefore, in a disjunctive judgement a certain community of cognitions, which consists in this, that they mutually exclude each other, yet thereby determine, as a whole, the true cognition, inasmuch as, taken together, they make up the complete content of a particular given cognition. And this is all that I find necessary, for the sake of what follows, to remark in this place.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"26.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","text":"4. The modality of judgements is a quite peculiar function, with this distinguishing characteristic, that it contributes nothing to the content of a judgement (for besides quantity, quality, and relation, there is nothing more that constitutes the content of a judgement), but concerns itself only with the value of the copula in relation to thought in general. Problematical judgements are those in which the affirmation or negation is accepted as merely possible (ad libitum). In the assertorical, we regard the proposition as real (true); in the apodeictical, we look on it as necessary.[[13]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-13) Thus the two judgements (antecedens et consequens), the relation of which constitutes a hypothetical judgement, likewise those (the members of the division) in whose reciprocity the disjunctive consists, are only problematical. In the example above given the proposition, “There exists perfect justice,” is not stated assertorically, but as an ad libitum judgement, which someone may choose to adopt, and the consequence alone is assertorical. Hence such judgements may be obviously false, and yet, taken problematically, be conditions of our cognition of the truth. Thus the proposition, “The world exists only by blind chance,” is in the disjunctive judgement of problematical import only: that is to say, one may accept it for the moment, and it helps us (like the indication of the wrong road among all the roads that one can take) to find out the true proposition. The problematical proposition is, therefore, that which expresses only logical possibility (which is not objective); that is, it expresses a free choice to admit the validity of such a proposition—a merely arbitrary reception of it into the understanding. The assertorical speaks of logical reality or truth; as, for example, in a hypothetical syllogism, the antecedens presents itself in a problematical form in the major, in an assertorical form in the minor, and it shows that the proposition is in harmony with the laws of the understanding. The apodeictical proposition cogitates the assertorical as determined by these very laws of the understanding, consequently as affirming à priori, and in this manner it expresses logical necessity. Now because all is here gradually incorporated with the understanding—inasmuch as in the first place we judge problematically; then accept assertorically our judgement as true; lastly, affirm it as inseparably united with the understanding, that is, as necessary and apodeictical—we may safely reckon these three functions of modality as so many momenta of thought.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"26.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0006","text":"[[13]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-13) Just as if thought were in the first instance a function of the understanding; in the second, of judgement; in the third, of reason. A remark which will be explained in the sequel.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"26.6"}],"sha256":"7b06cf49eb113b4ef9bfb4b94e030f38c430ff78dc31801c59670fb28e1162f3","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC","title":"Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6","order":27,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIPC","text":"### Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6\n\nGeneral logic, as has been repeatedly said, makes abstraction of all content of cognition, and expects to receive representations from some other quarter, in order, by means of analysis, to convert them into conceptions. On the contrary, transcendental logic has lying before it the manifold content of à priori sensibility, which transcendental æsthetic presents to it in order to give matter to the pure conceptions of the understanding, without which transcendental logic would have no content, and be therefore utterly void. Now space and time contain an infinite diversity of determinations of pure à priori intuition, but are nevertheless the condition of the mind’s receptivity, under which alone it can obtain representations of objects, and which, consequently, must always affect the conception of these objects. But the spontaneity of thought requires that this diversity be examined after a certain manner, received into the mind, and connected, in order afterwards to form a cognition out of it. This Process I call synthesis.\n\nBy the word synthesis, in its most general signification, I understand the process of joining different representations to each other and of comprehending their diversity in one cognition. This synthesis is pure when the diversity is not given empirically but à priori (as that in space and time). Our representations must be given previously to any analysis of them; and no conceptions can arise, quoad their content, analytically. But the synthesis of a diversity (be it given à priori or empirically) is the first requisite for the production of a cognition, which in its beginning, indeed, may be crude and confused, and therefore in need of analysis—still, synthesis is that by which alone the elements of our cognitions are collected and united into a certain content, consequently it is the first thing on which we must fix our attention, if we wish to investigate the origin of our knowledge.\n\nSynthesis, generally speaking, is, as we shall afterwards see, the mere operation of the imagination—a blind but indispensable function of the soul, without which we should have no cognition whatever, but of the working of which we are seldom even conscious. But to reduce this synthesis to conceptions is a function of the understanding, by means of which we attain to cognition, in the proper meaning of the term.\n\nPure synthesis, represented generally, gives us the pure conception of the understanding. But by this pure synthesis, I mean that which rests upon a basis of à priori synthetical unity. Thus, our numeration (and this is more observable in large numbers) is a synthesis according to conceptions, because it takes place according to a common basis of unity (for example, the decade). By means of this conception, therefore, the unity in the synthesis of the manifold becomes necessary.\n\nBy means of analysis different representations are brought under one conception—an operation of which general logic treats. On the other hand, the duty of transcendental logic is to reduce to conceptions, not representations, but the pure synthesis of representations. The first thing which must be given to us for the sake of the à priori cognition of all objects, is the diversity of the pure intuition; the synthesis of this diversity by means of the imagination is the second; but this gives, as yet, no cognition. The conceptions which give unity to this pure synthesis, and which consist solely in the representation of this necessary synthetical unity, furnish the third requisite for the cognition of an object, and these conceptions are given by the understanding.\n\nThe same function which gives unity to the different representation in a judgement, gives also unity to the mere synthesis of different representations in an intuition; and this unity we call the pure conception of the understanding. Thus, the same understanding, and by the same operations, whereby in conceptions, by means of analytical unity, it produced the logical form of a judgement, introduces, by means of the synthetical unity of the manifold in intuition, a transcendental content into its representations, on which account they are called pure conceptions of the understanding, and they apply à priori to objects, a result not within the power of general logic.\n\nIn this manner, there arise exactly so many pure conceptions of the understanding, applying à priori to objects of intuition in general, as there are logical functions in all possible judgements. For there is no other function or faculty existing in the understanding besides those enumerated in that table. These conceptions we shall, with Aristotle, call categories, our purpose being originally identical with his, notwithstanding the great difference in the execution.\n\n                     TABLE OF THE CATEGORIES\n\n                    1                         2\n\n              _Of Quantity                Of Quality_\n              Unity                      Reality\n              Plurality                  Negation\n              Totality                   Limitation\n\n                           3\n                      _Of Relation_\n   Of Inherence and Subsistence (substantia et accidens)\n   Of Causality and Dependence (cause and effect)\n   Of Community (reciprocity between the agent and patient)\n\n                           4\n                     _Of Modality_\n              Possibility—Impossibility\n              Existence—Non-existence\n              Necessity—Contingence\n\nThis, then, is a catalogue of all the originally pure conceptions of the synthesis which the understanding contains à priori, and these conceptions alone entitle it to be called a pure understanding; inasmuch as only by them it can render the manifold of intuition conceivable, in other words, think an object of intuition. This division is made systematically from a common principle, namely the faculty of judgement (which is just the same as the power of thought), and has not arisen rhapsodically from a search at haphazard after pure conceptions, respecting the full number of which we never could be certain, inasmuch as we employ induction alone in our search, without considering that in this way we can never understand wherefore precisely these conceptions, and none others, abide in the pure understanding. It was a design worthy of an acute thinker like Aristotle, to search for these fundamental conceptions. Destitute, however, of any guiding principle, he picked them up just as they occurred to him, and at first hunted out ten, which he called categories (predicaments). Afterwards be believed that he had discovered five others, which were added under the name of post predicaments. But his catalogue still remained defective. Besides, there are to be found among them some of the modes of pure sensibility (quando, ubi, situs, also prius, simul), and likewise an empirical conception (motus)—which can by no means belong to this genealogical register of the pure understanding. Moreover, there are deduced conceptions (actio, passio) enumerated among the original conceptions, and, of the latter, some are entirely wanting.\n\nWith regard to these, it is to be remarked, that the categories, as the true primitive conceptions of the pure understanding, have also their pure deduced conceptions, which, in a complete system of transcendental philosophy, must by no means be passed over; though in a merely critical essay we must be contented with the simple mention of the fact.\n\nLet it be allowed me to call these pure, but deduced conceptions of the understanding, the predicables of the pure understanding, in contradistinction to predicaments. If we are in possession of the original and primitive, the deduced and subsidiary conceptions can easily be added, and the genealogical tree of the understanding completely delineated. As my present aim is not to set forth a complete system, but merely the principles of one, I reserve this task for another time. It may be easily executed by any one who will refer to the ontological manuals, and subordinate to the category of causality, for example, the predicables of force, action, passion; to that of community, those of presence and resistance; to the categories of modality, those of origination, extinction, change; and so with the rest. The categories combined with the modes of pure sensibility, or with one another, afford a great number of deduced à priori conceptions; a complete enumeration of which would be a useful and not unpleasant, but in this place a perfectly dispensable, occupation.\n\nI purposely omit the definitions of the categories in this treatise. I shall analyse these conceptions only so far as is necessary for the doctrine of method, which is to form a part of this critique. In a system of pure reason, definitions of them would be with justice demanded of me, but to give them here would only bide from our view the main aim of our investigation, at the same time raising doubts and objections, the consideration of which, without injustice to our main purpose, may be very well postponed till another opportunity. Meanwhile, it ought to be sufficiently clear, from the little we have already said on this subject, that the formation of a complete vocabulary of pure conceptions, accompanied by all the requisite explanations, is not only a possible, but an easy undertaking. The compartments already exist; it is only necessary to fill them up; and a systematic topic like the present, indicates with perfect precision the proper place to which each conception belongs, while it readily points out any that have not yet been filled up.\n\n§ 7\n\nOur table of the categories suggests considerations of some importance, which may perhaps have significant results in regard to the scientific form of all rational cognitions. For, that this table is useful in the theoretical part of philosophy, nay, indispensable for the sketching of the complete plan of a science, so far as that science rests upon conceptions à priori, and for dividing it mathematically, according to fixed principles, is most manifest from the fact that it contains all the elementary conceptions of the understanding, nay, even the form of a system of these in the understanding itself, and consequently indicates all the momenta, and also the internal arrangement of a projected speculative science, as I have elsewhere shown.[[14]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-14) Here follow some of these observations.\n\n[[14]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-14) In the “Metaphysical Principles of Natural Science.”\n\nI. This table, which contains four classes of conceptions of the understanding, may, in the first instance, be divided into two classes, the first of which relates to objects of intuition—pure as well as empirical; the second, to the existence of these objects, either in relation to one another, or to the understanding.\n\nThe former of these classes of categories I would entitle the mathematical, and the latter the dynamical categories. The former, as we see, has no correlates; these are only to be found in the second class. This difference must have a ground in the nature of the human understanding.\n\nII. The number of the categories in each class is always the same, namely, three—a fact which also demands some consideration, because in all other cases division à priori through conceptions is necessarily dichotomy. It is to be added, that the third category in each triad always arises from the combination of the second with the first.\n\nThus totality is nothing else but plurality contemplated as unity; limitation is merely reality conjoined with negation; community is the causality of a substance, reciprocally determining, and determined by other substances; and finally, necessity is nothing but existence, which is given through the possibility itself. Let it not be supposed, however, that the third category is merely a deduced, and not a primitive conception of the pure understanding. For the conjunction of the first and second, in order to produce the third conception, requires a particular function of the understanding, which is by no means identical with those which are exercised in the first and second. Thus, the conception of a number (which belongs to the category of totality) is not always possible, where the conceptions of multitude and unity exist (for example, in the representation of the infinite). Or, if I conjoin the conception of a cause with that of a substance, it does not follow that the conception of influence, that is, how one substance can be the cause of something in another substance, will be understood from that. Thus it is evident that a particular act of the understanding is here necessary; and so in the other instances.\n\nIII. With respect to one category, namely, that of community, which is found in the third class, it is not so easy as with the others to detect its accordance with the form of the disjunctive judgement which corresponds to it in the table of the logical functions.\n\nIn order to assure ourselves of this accordance, we must observe that in every disjunctive judgement, the sphere of the judgement (that is, the complex of all that is contained in it) is represented as a whole divided into parts; and, since one part cannot be contained in the other, they are cogitated as co-ordinated with, not subordinated to each other, so that they do not determine each other unilaterally, as in a linear series, but reciprocally, as in an aggregate—(if one member of the division is posited, all the rest are excluded; and conversely).\n\nNow a like connection is cogitated in a whole of things; for one thing is not subordinated, as effect, to another as cause of its existence, but, on the contrary, is co-ordinated contemporaneously and reciprocally, as a cause in relation to the determination of the others (for example, in a body—the parts of which mutually attract and repel each other). And this is an entirely different kind of connection from that which we find in the mere relation of the cause to the effect (the principle to the consequence), for in such a connection the consequence does not in its turn determine the principle, and therefore does not constitute, with the latter, a whole—just as the Creator does not with the world make up a whole. The process of understanding by which it represents to itself the sphere of a divided conception, is employed also when we think of a thing as divisible; and in the same manner as the members of the division in the former exclude one another, and yet are connected in one sphere, so the understanding represents to itself the parts of the latter, as having—each of them—an existence (as substances), independently of the others, and yet as united in one whole.\n\n§ 8\n\nIn the transcendental philosophy of the ancients there exists one more leading division, which contains pure conceptions of the understanding, and which, although not numbered among the categories, ought, according to them, as conceptions à priori, to be valid of objects. But in this case they would augment the number of the categories; which cannot be. These are set forth in the proposition, so renowned among the schoolmen—‘_Quodlibet ens est UNUM, VERUM, BONUM_.’ Now, though the inferences from this principle were mere tautological propositions, and though it is allowed only by courtesy to retain a place in modern metaphysics, yet a thought which maintained itself for such a length of time, however empty it seems to be, deserves an investigation of its origin, and justifies the conjecture that it must be grounded in some law of the understanding, which, as is often the case, has only been erroneously interpreted. These pretended transcendental predicates are, in fact, nothing but logical requisites and criteria of all cognition of objects, and they employ, as the basis for this cognition, the categories of quantity, namely, unity, plurality, and totality. But these, which must be taken as material conditions, that is, as belonging to the possibility of things themselves, they employed merely in a formal signification, as belonging to the logical requisites of all cognition, and yet most unguardedly changed these criteria of thought into properties of objects, as things in themselves. Now, in every cognition of an object, there is unity of conception, which may be called qualitative unity, so far as by this term we understand only the unity in our connection of the manifold; for example, unity of the theme in a play, an oration, or a story. Secondly, there is truth in respect of the deductions from it. The more true deductions we have from a given conception, the more criteria of its objective reality. This we might call the qualitative plurality of characteristic marks, which belong to a conception as to a common foundation, but are not cogitated as a quantity in it. Thirdly, there is perfection—which consists in this, that the plurality falls back upon the unity of the conception, and accords completely with that conception and with no other. This we may denominate qualitative completeness. Hence it is evident that these logical criteria of the possibility of cognition are merely the three categories of quantity modified and transformed to suit an unauthorized manner of applying them. That is to say, the three categories, in which the unity in the production of the quantum must be homogeneous throughout, are transformed solely with a view to the connection of heterogeneous parts of cognition in one act of consciousness, by means of the quality of the cognition, which is the principle of that connection. Thus the criterion of the possibility of a conception (not of its object) is the definition of it, in which the unity of the conception, the truth of all that may be immediately deduced from it, and finally, the completeness of what has been thus deduced, constitute the requisites for the reproduction of the whole conception. Thus also, the criterion or test of an hypothesis is the intelligibility of the received principle of explanation, or its unity (without help from any subsidiary hypothesis)—the truth of our deductions from it (consistency with each other and with experience)—and lastly, the completeness of the principle of the explanation of these deductions, which refer to neither more nor less than what was admitted in the hypothesis, restoring analytically and à posteriori, what was cogitated synthetically and à priori. By the conceptions, therefore, of unity, truth, and perfection, we have made no addition to the transcendental table of the categories, which is complete without them. We have, on the contrary, merely employed the three categories of quantity, setting aside their application to objects of experience, as general logical laws of the consistency of cognition with itself.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","text":"### Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6\n\nGeneral logic, as has been repeatedly said, makes abstraction of all content of cognition, and expects to receive representations from some other quarter, in order, by means of analysis, to convert them into conceptions. On the contrary, transcendental logic has lying before it the manifold content of à priori sensibility, which transcendental æsthetic presents to it in order to give matter to the pure conceptions of the understanding, without which transcendental logic would have no content, and be therefore utterly void. Now space and time contain an infinite diversity of determinations of pure à priori intuition, but are nevertheless the condition of the mind’s receptivity, under which alone it can obtain representations of objects, and which, consequently, must always affect the conception of these objects. But the spontaneity of thought requires that this diversity be examined after a certain manner, received into the mind, and connected, in order afterwards to form a cognition out of it. This Process I call synthesis.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"27.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","text":"By the word synthesis, in its most general signification, I understand the process of joining different representations to each other and of comprehending their diversity in one cognition. This synthesis is pure when the diversity is not given empirically but à priori (as that in space and time). Our representations must be given previously to any analysis of them; and no conceptions can arise, quoad their content, analytically. But the synthesis of a diversity (be it given à priori or empirically) is the first requisite for the production of a cognition, which in its beginning, indeed, may be crude and confused, and therefore in need of analysis—still, synthesis is that by which alone the elements of our cognitions are collected and united into a certain content, consequently it is the first thing on which we must fix our attention, if we wish to investigate the origin of our knowledge.\n\nSynthesis, generally speaking, is, as we shall afterwards see, the mere operation of the imagination—a blind but indispensable function of the soul, without which we should have no cognition whatever, but of the working of which we are seldom even conscious. But to reduce this synthesis to conceptions is a function of the understanding, by means of which we attain to cognition, in the proper meaning of the term.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"27.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","text":"Pure synthesis, represented generally, gives us the pure conception of the understanding. But by this pure synthesis, I mean that which rests upon a basis of à priori synthetical unity. Thus, our numeration (and this is more observable in large numbers) is a synthesis according to conceptions, because it takes place according to a common basis of unity (for example, the decade). By means of this conception, therefore, the unity in the synthesis of the manifold becomes necessary.\n\nBy means of analysis different representations are brought under one conception—an operation of which general logic treats. On the other hand, the duty of transcendental logic is to reduce to conceptions, not representations, but the pure synthesis of representations. The first thing which must be given to us for the sake of the à priori cognition of all objects, is the diversity of the pure intuition; the synthesis of this diversity by means of the imagination is the second; but this gives, as yet, no cognition. The conceptions which give unity to this pure synthesis, and which consist solely in the representation of this necessary synthetical unity, furnish the third requisite for the cognition of an object, and these conceptions are given by the understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"27.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","text":"The same function which gives unity to the different representation in a judgement, gives also unity to the mere synthesis of different representations in an intuition; and this unity we call the pure conception of the understanding. Thus, the same understanding, and by the same operations, whereby in conceptions, by means of analytical unity, it produced the logical form of a judgement, introduces, by means of the synthetical unity of the manifold in intuition, a transcendental content into its representations, on which account they are called pure conceptions of the understanding, and they apply à priori to objects, a result not within the power of general logic.\n\nIn this manner, there arise exactly so many pure conceptions of the understanding, applying à priori to objects of intuition in general, as there are logical functions in all possible judgements. For there is no other function or faculty existing in the understanding besides those enumerated in that table. These conceptions we shall, with Aristotle, call categories, our purpose being originally identical with his, notwithstanding the great difference in the execution.\n\n                     TABLE OF THE CATEGORIES\n\n                    1                         2\n\n              _Of Quantity                Of Quality_\n              Unity                      Reality\n              Plurality                  Negation\n              Totality                   Limitation\n\n                           3\n                      _Of Relation_\n   Of Inherence and Subsistence (substantia et accidens)\n   Of Causality and Dependence (cause and effect)\n   Of Community (reciprocity between the agent and patient)\n\n                           4\n                     _Of Modality_\n              Possibility—Impossibility\n              Existence—Non-existence\n              Necessity—Contingence","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"27.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","text":"This, then, is a catalogue of all the originally pure conceptions of the synthesis which the understanding contains à priori, and these conceptions alone entitle it to be called a pure understanding; inasmuch as only by them it can render the manifold of intuition conceivable, in other words, think an object of intuition. This division is made systematically from a common principle, namely the faculty of judgement (which is just the same as the power of thought), and has not arisen rhapsodically from a search at haphazard after pure conceptions, respecting the full number of which we never could be certain, inasmuch as we employ induction alone in our search, without considering that in this way we can never understand wherefore precisely these conceptions, and none others, abide in the pure understanding. It was a design worthy of an acute thinker like Aristotle, to search for these fundamental conceptions. Destitute, however, of any guiding principle, he picked them up just as they occurred to him, and at first hunted out ten, which he called categories (predicaments). Afterwards be believed that he had discovered five others, which were added under the name of post predicaments. But his catalogue still remained defective. Besides, there are to be found among them some of the modes of pure sensibility (quando, ubi, situs, also prius, simul), and likewise an empirical conception (motus)—which can by no means belong to this genealogical register of the pure understanding. Moreover, there are deduced conceptions (actio, passio) enumerated among the original conceptions, and, of the latter, some are entirely wanting.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"27.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","text":"With regard to these, it is to be remarked, that the categories, as the true primitive conceptions of the pure understanding, have also their pure deduced conceptions, which, in a complete system of transcendental philosophy, must by no means be passed over; though in a merely critical essay we must be contented with the simple mention of the fact.\n\nLet it be allowed me to call these pure, but deduced conceptions of the understanding, the predicables of the pure understanding, in contradistinction to predicaments. If we are in possession of the original and primitive, the deduced and subsidiary conceptions can easily be added, and the genealogical tree of the understanding completely delineated. As my present aim is not to set forth a complete system, but merely the principles of one, I reserve this task for another time. It may be easily executed by any one who will refer to the ontological manuals, and subordinate to the category of causality, for example, the predicables of force, action, passion; to that of community, those of presence and resistance; to the categories of modality, those of origination, extinction, change; and so with the rest. The categories combined with the modes of pure sensibility, or with one another, afford a great number of deduced à priori conceptions; a complete enumeration of which would be a useful and not unpleasant, but in this place a perfectly dispensable, occupation.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"27.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0007","text":"I purposely omit the definitions of the categories in this treatise. I shall analyse these conceptions only so far as is necessary for the doctrine of method, which is to form a part of this critique. In a system of pure reason, definitions of them would be with justice demanded of me, but to give them here would only bide from our view the main aim of our investigation, at the same time raising doubts and objections, the consideration of which, without injustice to our main purpose, may be very well postponed till another opportunity. Meanwhile, it ought to be sufficiently clear, from the little we have already said on this subject, that the formation of a complete vocabulary of pure conceptions, accompanied by all the requisite explanations, is not only a possible, but an easy undertaking. The compartments already exist; it is only necessary to fill them up; and a systematic topic like the present, indicates with perfect precision the proper place to which each conception belongs, while it readily points out any that have not yet been filled up.\n\n§ 7","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"27.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0008","text":"Our table of the categories suggests considerations of some importance, which may perhaps have significant results in regard to the scientific form of all rational cognitions. For, that this table is useful in the theoretical part of philosophy, nay, indispensable for the sketching of the complete plan of a science, so far as that science rests upon conceptions à priori, and for dividing it mathematically, according to fixed principles, is most manifest from the fact that it contains all the elementary conceptions of the understanding, nay, even the form of a system of these in the understanding itself, and consequently indicates all the momenta, and also the internal arrangement of a projected speculative science, as I have elsewhere shown.[[14]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-14) Here follow some of these observations.\n\n[[14]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-14) In the “Metaphysical Principles of Natural Science.”\n\nI. This table, which contains four classes of conceptions of the understanding, may, in the first instance, be divided into two classes, the first of which relates to objects of intuition—pure as well as empirical; the second, to the existence of these objects, either in relation to one another, or to the understanding.\n\nThe former of these classes of categories I would entitle the mathematical, and the latter the dynamical categories. The former, as we see, has no correlates; these are only to be found in the second class. This difference must have a ground in the nature of the human understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"27.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0009","text":"II. The number of the categories in each class is always the same, namely, three—a fact which also demands some consideration, because in all other cases division à priori through conceptions is necessarily dichotomy. It is to be added, that the third category in each triad always arises from the combination of the second with the first.\n\nThus totality is nothing else but plurality contemplated as unity; limitation is merely reality conjoined with negation; community is the causality of a substance, reciprocally determining, and determined by other substances; and finally, necessity is nothing but existence, which is given through the possibility itself. Let it not be supposed, however, that the third category is merely a deduced, and not a primitive conception of the pure understanding. For the conjunction of the first and second, in order to produce the third conception, requires a particular function of the understanding, which is by no means identical with those which are exercised in the first and second. Thus, the conception of a number (which belongs to the category of totality) is not always possible, where the conceptions of multitude and unity exist (for example, in the representation of the infinite). Or, if I conjoin the conception of a cause with that of a substance, it does not follow that the conception of influence, that is, how one substance can be the cause of something in another substance, will be understood from that. Thus it is evident that a particular act of the understanding is here necessary; and so in the other instances.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"27.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0010","text":"III. With respect to one category, namely, that of community, which is found in the third class, it is not so easy as with the others to detect its accordance with the form of the disjunctive judgement which corresponds to it in the table of the logical functions.\n\nIn order to assure ourselves of this accordance, we must observe that in every disjunctive judgement, the sphere of the judgement (that is, the complex of all that is contained in it) is represented as a whole divided into parts; and, since one part cannot be contained in the other, they are cogitated as co-ordinated with, not subordinated to each other, so that they do not determine each other unilaterally, as in a linear series, but reciprocally, as in an aggregate—(if one member of the division is posited, all the rest are excluded; and conversely).","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"27.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0011","text":"Now a like connection is cogitated in a whole of things; for one thing is not subordinated, as effect, to another as cause of its existence, but, on the contrary, is co-ordinated contemporaneously and reciprocally, as a cause in relation to the determination of the others (for example, in a body—the parts of which mutually attract and repel each other). And this is an entirely different kind of connection from that which we find in the mere relation of the cause to the effect (the principle to the consequence), for in such a connection the consequence does not in its turn determine the principle, and therefore does not constitute, with the latter, a whole—just as the Creator does not with the world make up a whole. The process of understanding by which it represents to itself the sphere of a divided conception, is employed also when we think of a thing as divisible; and in the same manner as the members of the division in the former exclude one another, and yet are connected in one sphere, so the understanding represents to itself the parts of the latter, as having—each of them—an existence (as substances), independently of the others, and yet as united in one whole.\n\n§ 8","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"27.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","text":"In the transcendental philosophy of the ancients there exists one more leading division, which contains pure conceptions of the understanding, and which, although not numbered among the categories, ought, according to them, as conceptions à priori, to be valid of objects. But in this case they would augment the number of the categories; which cannot be. These are set forth in the proposition, so renowned among the schoolmen—‘_Quodlibet ens est UNUM, VERUM, BONUM_.’ Now, though the inferences from this principle were mere tautological propositions, and though it is allowed only by courtesy to retain a place in modern metaphysics, yet a thought which maintained itself for such a length of time, however empty it seems to be, deserves an investigation of its origin, and justifies the conjecture that it must be grounded in some law of the understanding, which, as is often the case, has only been erroneously interpreted. These pretended transcendental predicates are, in fact, nothing but logical requisites and criteria of all cognition of objects, and they employ, as the basis for this cognition, the categories of quantity, namely, unity, plurality, and totality. But these, which must be taken as material conditions, that is, as belonging to the possibility of things themselves, they employed merely in a formal signification, as belonging to the logical requisites of all cognition, and yet most unguardedly changed these criteria of thought into properties of objects, as things in themselves. Now, in every cognition of an object, there is unity of conception, which may be called qualitative unity, so far as by this term we understand only the unity in our connection of the manifold; for example, unity of the theme in a play, an oration, or a story. Secondly, there is truth in respect of the deductions from it. The more true deductions we have from a given conception, the more criteria of its objective reality. This we might call the qualitative plurality of characteristic marks, which belong to a conception as to a common foundation, but are not cogitated as a quantity in it. Thirdly, there is perfection—which consists in this, that the plurality falls back upon the unity of the conception, and accords completely with that conception and with no other. This we may denominate qualitative completeness. Hence it is evident that these logical criteria of the possibility of cognition are merely the three categories of quantity modified and transformed to suit an unauthorized manner of applying them. That is to say, the three categories, in which the unity in the production of the quantum must be homogeneous throughout, are transformed solely with a view to the connection of heterogeneous parts of cognition in one act of consciousness, by means of the quality of the cognition, which is the principle of that connection. Thus the criterion of the possibility of a conception (not of its object) is the definition of it, in which the unity of the conception, the truth of all that may be immediately deduced from it, and finally, the completeness of what has been thus deduced, constitute the requisites for the reproduction of the whole conception. Thus also, the criterion or test of an hypothesis is the intelligibility of the received principle of explanation, or its unity (without help from any subsidiary hypothesis)—the truth of our deductions from it (consistency with each other and with experience)—and lastly, the completeness of the principle of the explanation of these deductions, which refer to neither more nor less than what was admitted in the hypothesis, restoring analytically and à posteriori, what was cogitated synthetically and à priori. By the conceptions, therefore, of unity, truth, and perfection, we have made no addition to the transcendental table of the categories, which is complete without them. We have, on the contrary, merely employed the three categories of quantity, setting aside their application to objects of experience, as general logical laws of the consistency of cognition with itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"27.12"}],"sha256":"3a3db951869b780f7d54aba28a63deb799e1f5f7e57169cc94dc15a711cf742d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT","title":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9","order":28,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIPT","text":"### Chapter II. Of the Deduction of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9\n\nTeachers of jurisprudence, when speaking of rights and claims, distinguish in a cause the question of right (quid juris) from the question of fact (quid facti), and while they demand proof of both, they give to the proof of the former, which goes to establish right or claim in law, the name of deduction. Now we make use of a great number of empirical conceptions, without opposition from any one; and consider ourselves, even without any attempt at deduction, justified in attaching to them a sense, and a supposititious signification, because we have always experience at hand to demonstrate their objective reality. There exist also, however, usurped conceptions, such as fortune, fate, which circulate with almost universal indulgence, and yet are occasionally challenged by the question, “quid juris?” In such cases, we have great difficulty in discovering any deduction for these terms, inasmuch as we cannot produce any manifest ground of right, either from experience or from reason, on which the claim to employ them can be founded.\n\nAmong the many conceptions, which make up the very variegated web of human cognition, some are destined for pure use à priori, independent of all experience; and their title to be so employed always requires a deduction, inasmuch as, to justify such use of them, proofs from experience are not sufficient; but it is necessary to know how these conceptions can apply to objects without being derived from experience. I term, therefore, an examination of the manner in which conceptions can apply à priori to objects, the transcendental deduction of conceptions, and I distinguish it from the empirical deduction, which indicates the mode in which conception is obtained through experience and reflection thereon; consequently, does not concern itself with the right, but only with the fact of our obtaining conceptions in such and such a manner. We have already seen that we are in possession of two perfectly different kinds of conceptions, which nevertheless agree with each other in this, that they both apply to objects completely à priori. These are the conceptions of space and time as forms of sensibility, and the categories as pure conceptions of the understanding. To attempt an empirical deduction of either of these classes would be labour in vain, because the distinguishing characteristic of their nature consists in this, that they apply to their objects, without having borrowed anything from experience towards the representation of them. Consequently, if a deduction of these conceptions is necessary, it must always be transcendental.\n\nMeanwhile, with respect to these conceptions, as with respect to all our cognition, we certainly may discover in experience, if not the principle of their possibility, yet the occasioning causes of their production. It will be found that the impressions of sense give the first occasion for bringing into action the whole faculty of cognition, and for the production of experience, which contains two very dissimilar elements, namely, a matter for cognition, given by the senses, and a certain form for the arrangement of this matter, arising out of the inner fountain of pure intuition and thought; and these, on occasion given by sensuous impressions, are called into exercise and produce conceptions. Such an investigation into the first efforts of our faculty of cognition to mount from particular perceptions to general conceptions is undoubtedly of great utility; and we have to thank the celebrated Locke for having first opened the way for this inquiry. But a deduction of the pure à priori conceptions of course never can be made in this way, seeing that, in regard to their future employment, which must be entirely independent of experience, they must have a far different certificate of birth to show from that of a descent from experience. This attempted physiological derivation, which cannot properly be called deduction, because it relates merely to a quaestio facti, I shall entitle an explanation of the possession of a pure cognition. It is therefore manifest that there can only be a transcendental deduction of these conceptions and by no means an empirical one; also, that all attempts at an empirical deduction, in regard to pure à priori conceptions, are vain, and can only be made by one who does not understand the altogether peculiar nature of these cognitions.\n\nBut although it is admitted that the only possible deduction of pure à priori cognition is a transcendental deduction, it is not, for that reason, perfectly manifest that such a deduction is absolutely necessary. We have already traced to their sources the conceptions of space and time, by means of a transcendental deduction, and we have explained and determined their objective validity à priori. Geometry, nevertheless, advances steadily and securely in the province of pure à priori cognitions, without needing to ask from philosophy any certificate as to the pure and legitimate origin of its fundamental conception of space. But the use of the conception in this science extends only to the external world of sense, the pure form of the intuition of which is space; and in this world, therefore, all geometrical cognition, because it is founded upon à priori intuition, possesses immediate evidence, and the objects of this cognition are given à priori (as regards their form) in intuition by and through the cognition itself. With the pure conceptions of understanding, on the contrary, commences the absolute necessity of seeking a transcendental deduction, not only of these conceptions themselves, but likewise of space, because, inasmuch as they make affirmations concerning objects not by means of the predicates of intuition and sensibility, but of pure thought à priori, they apply to objects without any of the conditions of sensibility. Besides, not being founded on experience, they are not presented with any object in à priori intuition upon which, antecedently to experience, they might base their synthesis. Hence results, not only doubt as to the objective validity and proper limits of their use, but that even our conception of space is rendered equivocal; inasmuch as we are very ready with the aid of the categories, to carry the use of this conception beyond the conditions of sensuous intuition—and, for this reason, we have already found a transcendental deduction of it needful. The reader, then, must be quite convinced of the absolute necessity of a transcendental deduction, before taking a single step in the field of pure reason; because otherwise he goes to work blindly, and after he has wondered about in all directions, returns to the state of utter ignorance from which he started. He ought, moreover, clearly to recognize beforehand the unavoidable difficulties in his undertaking, so that he may not afterwards complain of the obscurity in which the subject itself is deeply involved, or become too soon impatient of the obstacles in his path; because we have a choice of only two things—either at once to give up all pretensions to knowledge beyond the limits of possible experience, or to bring this critical investigation to completion.\n\nWe have been able, with very little trouble, to make it comprehensible how the conceptions of space and time, although à priori cognitions, must necessarily apply to external objects, and render a synthetical cognition of these possible, independently of all experience. For inasmuch as only by means of such pure form of sensibility an object can appear to us, that is, be an object of empirical intuition, space and time are pure intuitions, which contain à priori the condition of the possibility of objects as phenomena, and an à priori synthesis in these intuitions possesses objective validity.\n\nOn the other hand, the categories of the understanding do not represent the conditions under which objects are given to us in intuition; objects can consequently appear to us without necessarily connecting themselves with these, and consequently without any necessity binding on the understanding to contain à priori the conditions of these objects. Thus we find ourselves involved in a difficulty which did not present itself in the sphere of sensibility, that is to say, we cannot discover how the subjective conditions of thought can have objective validity, in other words, can become conditions of the possibility of all cognition of objects; for phenomena may certainly be given to us in intuition without any help from the functions of the understanding. Let us take, for example, the conception of cause, which indicates a peculiar kind of synthesis, namely, that with something, A, something entirely different, B, is connected according to a law. It is not à priori manifest why phenomena should contain anything of this kind (we are of course debarred from appealing for proof to experience, for the objective validity of this conception must be demonstrated à priori), and it hence remains doubtful à priori, whether such a conception be not quite void and without any corresponding object among phenomena. For that objects of sensuous intuition must correspond to the formal conditions of sensibility existing à priori in the mind is quite evident, from the fact that without these they could not be objects for us; but that they must also correspond to the conditions which understanding requires for the synthetical unity of thought is an assertion, the grounds for which are not so easily to be discovered. For phenomena might be so constituted as not to correspond to the conditions of the unity of thought; and all things might lie in such confusion that, for example, nothing could be met with in the sphere of phenomena to suggest a law of synthesis, and so correspond to the conception of cause and effect; so that this conception would be quite void, null, and without significance. Phenomena would nevertheless continue to present objects to our intuition; for mere intuition does not in any respect stand in need of the functions of thought.\n\nIf we thought to free ourselves from the labour of these investigations by saying: “Experience is constantly offering us examples of the relation of cause and effect in phenomena, and presents us with abundant opportunity of abstracting the conception of cause, and so at the same time of corroborating the objective validity of this conception”; we should in this case be overlooking the fact, that the conception of cause cannot arise in this way at all; that, on the contrary, it must either have an à priori basis in the understanding, or be rejected as a mere chimera. For this conception demands that something, A, should be of such a nature that something else, B, should follow from it necessarily, and according to an absolutely universal law. We may certainly collect from phenomena a law, according to which this or that usually happens, but the element of necessity is not to be found in it. Hence it is evident that to the synthesis of cause and effect belongs a dignity, which is utterly wanting in any empirical synthesis; for it is no mere mechanical synthesis, by means of addition, but a dynamical one; that is to say, the effect is not to be cogitated as merely annexed to the cause, but as posited by and through the cause, and resulting from it. The strict universality of this law never can be a characteristic of empirical laws, which obtain through induction only a comparative universality, that is, an extended range of practical application. But the pure conceptions of the understanding would entirely lose all their peculiar character, if we treated them merely as the productions of experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","text":"### Chapter II. Of the Deduction of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9\n\nTeachers of jurisprudence, when speaking of rights and claims, distinguish in a cause the question of right (quid juris) from the question of fact (quid facti), and while they demand proof of both, they give to the proof of the former, which goes to establish right or claim in law, the name of deduction. Now we make use of a great number of empirical conceptions, without opposition from any one; and consider ourselves, even without any attempt at deduction, justified in attaching to them a sense, and a supposititious signification, because we have always experience at hand to demonstrate their objective reality. There exist also, however, usurped conceptions, such as fortune, fate, which circulate with almost universal indulgence, and yet are occasionally challenged by the question, “quid juris?” In such cases, we have great difficulty in discovering any deduction for these terms, inasmuch as we cannot produce any manifest ground of right, either from experience or from reason, on which the claim to employ them can be founded.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"28.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","text":"Among the many conceptions, which make up the very variegated web of human cognition, some are destined for pure use à priori, independent of all experience; and their title to be so employed always requires a deduction, inasmuch as, to justify such use of them, proofs from experience are not sufficient; but it is necessary to know how these conceptions can apply to objects without being derived from experience. I term, therefore, an examination of the manner in which conceptions can apply à priori to objects, the transcendental deduction of conceptions, and I distinguish it from the empirical deduction, which indicates the mode in which conception is obtained through experience and reflection thereon; consequently, does not concern itself with the right, but only with the fact of our obtaining conceptions in such and such a manner. We have already seen that we are in possession of two perfectly different kinds of conceptions, which nevertheless agree with each other in this, that they both apply to objects completely à priori. These are the conceptions of space and time as forms of sensibility, and the categories as pure conceptions of the understanding. To attempt an empirical deduction of either of these classes would be labour in vain, because the distinguishing characteristic of their nature consists in this, that they apply to their objects, without having borrowed anything from experience towards the representation of them. Consequently, if a deduction of these conceptions is necessary, it must always be transcendental.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"28.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","text":"Meanwhile, with respect to these conceptions, as with respect to all our cognition, we certainly may discover in experience, if not the principle of their possibility, yet the occasioning causes of their production. It will be found that the impressions of sense give the first occasion for bringing into action the whole faculty of cognition, and for the production of experience, which contains two very dissimilar elements, namely, a matter for cognition, given by the senses, and a certain form for the arrangement of this matter, arising out of the inner fountain of pure intuition and thought; and these, on occasion given by sensuous impressions, are called into exercise and produce conceptions. Such an investigation into the first efforts of our faculty of cognition to mount from particular perceptions to general conceptions is undoubtedly of great utility; and we have to thank the celebrated Locke for having first opened the way for this inquiry. But a deduction of the pure à priori conceptions of course never can be made in this way, seeing that, in regard to their future employment, which must be entirely independent of experience, they must have a far different certificate of birth to show from that of a descent from experience. This attempted physiological derivation, which cannot properly be called deduction, because it relates merely to a quaestio facti, I shall entitle an explanation of the possession of a pure cognition. It is therefore manifest that there can only be a transcendental deduction of these conceptions and by no means an empirical one; also, that all attempts at an empirical deduction, in regard to pure à priori conceptions, are vain, and can only be made by one who does not understand the altogether peculiar nature of these cognitions.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"28.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","text":"But although it is admitted that the only possible deduction of pure à priori cognition is a transcendental deduction, it is not, for that reason, perfectly manifest that such a deduction is absolutely necessary. We have already traced to their sources the conceptions of space and time, by means of a transcendental deduction, and we have explained and determined their objective validity à priori. Geometry, nevertheless, advances steadily and securely in the province of pure à priori cognitions, without needing to ask from philosophy any certificate as to the pure and legitimate origin of its fundamental conception of space. But the use of the conception in this science extends only to the external world of sense, the pure form of the intuition of which is space; and in this world, therefore, all geometrical cognition, because it is founded upon à priori intuition, possesses immediate evidence, and the objects of this cognition are given à priori (as regards their form) in intuition by and through the cognition itself. With the pure conceptions of understanding, on the contrary, commences the absolute necessity of seeking a transcendental deduction, not only of these conceptions themselves, but likewise of space, because, inasmuch as they make affirmations concerning objects not by means of the predicates of intuition and sensibility, but of pure thought à priori, they apply to objects without any of the conditions of sensibility. Besides, not being founded on experience, they are not presented with any object in à priori intuition upon which, antecedently to experience, they might base their synthesis. Hence results, not only doubt as to the objective validity and proper limits of their use, but that even our conception of space is rendered equivocal; inasmuch as we are very ready with the aid of the categories, to carry the use of this conception beyond the conditions of sensuous intuition—and, for this reason, we have already found a transcendental deduction of it needful. The reader, then, must be quite convinced of the absolute necessity of a transcendental deduction, before taking a single step in the field of pure reason; because otherwise he goes to work blindly, and after he has wondered about in all directions, returns to the state of utter ignorance from which he started. He ought, moreover, clearly to recognize beforehand the unavoidable difficulties in his undertaking, so that he may not afterwards complain of the obscurity in which the subject itself is deeply involved, or become too soon impatient of the obstacles in his path; because we have a choice of only two things—either at once to give up all pretensions to knowledge beyond the limits of possible experience, or to bring this critical investigation to completion.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"28.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","text":"We have been able, with very little trouble, to make it comprehensible how the conceptions of space and time, although à priori cognitions, must necessarily apply to external objects, and render a synthetical cognition of these possible, independently of all experience. For inasmuch as only by means of such pure form of sensibility an object can appear to us, that is, be an object of empirical intuition, space and time are pure intuitions, which contain à priori the condition of the possibility of objects as phenomena, and an à priori synthesis in these intuitions possesses objective validity.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"28.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","text":"On the other hand, the categories of the understanding do not represent the conditions under which objects are given to us in intuition; objects can consequently appear to us without necessarily connecting themselves with these, and consequently without any necessity binding on the understanding to contain à priori the conditions of these objects. Thus we find ourselves involved in a difficulty which did not present itself in the sphere of sensibility, that is to say, we cannot discover how the subjective conditions of thought can have objective validity, in other words, can become conditions of the possibility of all cognition of objects; for phenomena may certainly be given to us in intuition without any help from the functions of the understanding. Let us take, for example, the conception of cause, which indicates a peculiar kind of synthesis, namely, that with something, A, something entirely different, B, is connected according to a law. It is not à priori manifest why phenomena should contain anything of this kind (we are of course debarred from appealing for proof to experience, for the objective validity of this conception must be demonstrated à priori), and it hence remains doubtful à priori, whether such a conception be not quite void and without any corresponding object among phenomena. For that objects of sensuous intuition must correspond to the formal conditions of sensibility existing à priori in the mind is quite evident, from the fact that without these they could not be objects for us; but that they must also correspond to the conditions which understanding requires for the synthetical unity of thought is an assertion, the grounds for which are not so easily to be discovered. For phenomena might be so constituted as not to correspond to the conditions of the unity of thought; and all things might lie in such confusion that, for example, nothing could be met with in the sphere of phenomena to suggest a law of synthesis, and so correspond to the conception of cause and effect; so that this conception would be quite void, null, and without significance. Phenomena would nevertheless continue to present objects to our intuition; for mere intuition does not in any respect stand in need of the functions of thought.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"28.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","text":"If we thought to free ourselves from the labour of these investigations by saying: “Experience is constantly offering us examples of the relation of cause and effect in phenomena, and presents us with abundant opportunity of abstracting the conception of cause, and so at the same time of corroborating the objective validity of this conception”; we should in this case be overlooking the fact, that the conception of cause cannot arise in this way at all; that, on the contrary, it must either have an à priori basis in the understanding, or be rejected as a mere chimera. For this conception demands that something, A, should be of such a nature that something else, B, should follow from it necessarily, and according to an absolutely universal law. We may certainly collect from phenomena a law, according to which this or that usually happens, but the element of necessity is not to be found in it. Hence it is evident that to the synthesis of cause and effect belongs a dignity, which is utterly wanting in any empirical synthesis; for it is no mere mechanical synthesis, by means of addition, but a dynamical one; that is to say, the effect is not to be cogitated as merely annexed to the cause, but as posited by and through the cause, and resulting from it. The strict universality of this law never can be a characteristic of empirical laws, which obtain through induction only a comparative universality, that is, an extended range of practical application. But the pure conceptions of the understanding would entirely lose all their peculiar character, if we treated them merely as the productions of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"28.7"}],"sha256":"8b3dd61c230fc247d1e4d91c1082953150d65e222c41714f53fa9680cf07eefa","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC","title":"Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10","order":29,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_TTDC","text":"# Catagories\n\n### Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10\n\nThere are only two possible ways in which synthetical representation and its objects can coincide with and relate necessarily to each other, and, as it were, meet together. Either the object alone makes the representation possible, or the representation alone makes the object possible. In the former case, the relation between them is only empirical, and an à priori representation is impossible. And this is the case with phenomena, as regards that in them which is referable to mere sensation. In the latter case—although representation alone (for of its causality, by means of the will, we do not here speak) does not produce the object as to its existence, it must nevertheless be à priori determinative in regard to the object, if it is only by means of the representation that we can cognize anything as an object. Now there are only two conditions of the possibility of a cognition of objects; firstly, intuition, by means of which the object, though only as phenomenon, is given; secondly, conception, by means of which the object which corresponds to this intuition is thought. But it is evident from what has been said on æsthetic that the first condition, under which alone objects can be intuited, must in fact exist, as a formal basis for them, à priori in the mind. With this formal condition of sensibility, therefore, all phenomena necessarily correspond, because it is only through it that they can be phenomena at all; that is, can be empirically intuited and given. Now the question is whether there do not exist, à priori in the mind, conceptions of understanding also, as conditions under which alone something, if not intuited, is yet thought as object. If this question be answered in the affirmative, it follows that all empirical cognition of objects is necessarily conformable to such conceptions, since, if they are not presupposed, it is impossible that anything can be an object of experience. Now all experience contains, besides the intuition of the senses through which an object is given, a conception also of an object that is given in intuition. Accordingly, conceptions of objects in general must lie as à priori conditions at the foundation of all empirical cognition; and consequently, the objective validity of the categories, as à priori conceptions, will rest upon this, that experience (as far as regards the form of thought) is possible only by their means. For in that case they apply necessarily and à priori to objects of experience, because only through them can an object of experience be thought.\n\nThe whole aim of the transcendental deduction of all à priori conceptions is to show that these conceptions are à priori conditions of the possibility of all experience. Conceptions which afford us the objective foundation of the possibility of experience are for that very reason necessary. But the analysis of the experiences in which they are met with is not deduction, but only an illustration of them, because from experience they could never derive the attribute of necessity. Without their original applicability and relation to all possible experience, in which all objects of cognition present themselves, the relation of the categories to objects, of whatever nature, would be quite incomprehensible.\n\nThe celebrated Locke, for want of due reflection on these points, and because he met with pure conceptions of the understanding in experience, sought also to deduce them from experience, and yet proceeded so inconsequently as to attempt, with their aid, to arrive it cognitions which lie far beyond the limits of all experience. David Hume perceived that, to render this possible, it was necessary that the conceptions should have an à priori origin. But as he could not explain how it was possible that conceptions which are not connected with each other in the understanding must nevertheless be thought as necessarily connected in the object—and it never occurred to him that the understanding itself might, perhaps, by means of these conceptions, be the author of the experience in which its objects were presented to it—he was forced to drive these conceptions from experience, that is, from a subjective necessity arising from repeated association of experiences erroneously considered to be objective—in one word, from habit. But he proceeded with perfect consequence and declared it to be impossible, with such conceptions and the principles arising from them, to overstep the limits of experience. The empirical derivation, however, which both of these philosophers attributed to these conceptions, cannot possibly be reconciled with the fact that we do possess scientific à priori cognitions, namely, those of pure mathematics and general physics.\n\nThe former of these two celebrated men opened a wide door to extravagance—(for if reason has once undoubted right on its side, it will not allow itself to be confined to set limits, by vague recommendations of moderation); the latter gave himself up entirely to scepticism—a natural consequence, after having discovered, as he thought, that the faculty of cognition was not trustworthy. We now intend to make a trial whether it be not possible safely to conduct reason between these two rocks, to assign her determinate limits, and yet leave open for her the entire sphere of her legitimate activity.\n\nI shall merely premise an explanation of what the categories are. They are conceptions of an object in general, by means of which its intuition is contemplated as determined in relation to one of the logical functions of judgement. The following will make this plain. The function of the categorical judgement is that of the relation of subject to predicate; for example, in the proposition: “All bodies are divisible.” But in regard to the merely logical use of the understanding, it still remains undetermined to which Of these two conceptions belongs the function Of subject and to which that of predicate. For we could also say: “Some divisible is a body.” But the category of substance, when the conception of a body is brought under it, determines that; and its empirical intuition in experience must be contemplated always as subject and never as mere predicate. And so with all the other categories.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","text":"# Catagories\n\n### Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10\n\nThere are only two possible ways in which synthetical representation and its objects can coincide with and relate necessarily to each other, and, as it were, meet together. Either the object alone makes the representation possible, or the representation alone makes the object possible. In the former case, the relation between them is only empirical, and an à priori representation is impossible. And this is the case with phenomena, as regards that in them which is referable to mere sensation. In the latter case—although representation alone (for of its causality, by means of the will, we do not here speak) does not produce the object as to its existence, it must nevertheless be à priori determinative in regard to the object, if it is only by means of the representation that we can cognize anything as an object. Now there are only two conditions of the possibility of a cognition of objects; firstly, intuition, by means of which the object, though only as phenomenon, is given; secondly, conception, by means of which the object which corresponds to this intuition is thought. But it is evident from what has been said on æsthetic that the first condition, under which alone objects can be intuited, must in fact exist, as a formal basis for them, à priori in the mind. With this formal condition of sensibility, therefore, all phenomena necessarily correspond, because it is only through it that they can be phenomena at all; that is, can be empirically intuited and given. Now the question is whether there do not exist, à priori in the mind, conceptions of understanding also, as conditions under which alone something, if not intuited, is yet thought as object. If this question be answered in the affirmative, it follows that all empirical cognition of objects is necessarily conformable to such conceptions, since, if they are not presupposed, it is impossible that anything can be an object of experience. Now all experience contains, besides the intuition of the senses through which an object is given, a conception also of an object that is given in intuition. Accordingly, conceptions of objects in general must lie as à priori conditions at the foundation of all empirical cognition; and consequently, the objective validity of the categories, as à priori conceptions, will rest upon this, that experience (as far as regards the form of thought) is possible only by their means. For in that case they apply necessarily and à priori to objects of experience, because only through them can an object of experience be thought.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"29.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","text":"The whole aim of the transcendental deduction of all à priori conceptions is to show that these conceptions are à priori conditions of the possibility of all experience. Conceptions which afford us the objective foundation of the possibility of experience are for that very reason necessary. But the analysis of the experiences in which they are met with is not deduction, but only an illustration of them, because from experience they could never derive the attribute of necessity. Without their original applicability and relation to all possible experience, in which all objects of cognition present themselves, the relation of the categories to objects, of whatever nature, would be quite incomprehensible.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"29.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","text":"The celebrated Locke, for want of due reflection on these points, and because he met with pure conceptions of the understanding in experience, sought also to deduce them from experience, and yet proceeded so inconsequently as to attempt, with their aid, to arrive it cognitions which lie far beyond the limits of all experience. David Hume perceived that, to render this possible, it was necessary that the conceptions should have an à priori origin. But as he could not explain how it was possible that conceptions which are not connected with each other in the understanding must nevertheless be thought as necessarily connected in the object—and it never occurred to him that the understanding itself might, perhaps, by means of these conceptions, be the author of the experience in which its objects were presented to it—he was forced to drive these conceptions from experience, that is, from a subjective necessity arising from repeated association of experiences erroneously considered to be objective—in one word, from habit. But he proceeded with perfect consequence and declared it to be impossible, with such conceptions and the principles arising from them, to overstep the limits of experience. The empirical derivation, however, which both of these philosophers attributed to these conceptions, cannot possibly be reconciled with the fact that we do possess scientific à priori cognitions, namely, those of pure mathematics and general physics.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"29.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","text":"The former of these two celebrated men opened a wide door to extravagance—(for if reason has once undoubted right on its side, it will not allow itself to be confined to set limits, by vague recommendations of moderation); the latter gave himself up entirely to scepticism—a natural consequence, after having discovered, as he thought, that the faculty of cognition was not trustworthy. We now intend to make a trial whether it be not possible safely to conduct reason between these two rocks, to assign her determinate limits, and yet leave open for her the entire sphere of her legitimate activity.\n\nI shall merely premise an explanation of what the categories are. They are conceptions of an object in general, by means of which its intuition is contemplated as determined in relation to one of the logical functions of judgement. The following will make this plain. The function of the categorical judgement is that of the relation of subject to predicate; for example, in the proposition: “All bodies are divisible.” But in regard to the merely logical use of the understanding, it still remains undetermined to which Of these two conceptions belongs the function Of subject and to which that of predicate. For we could also say: “Some divisible is a body.” But the category of substance, when the conception of a body is brought under it, determines that; and its empirical intuition in experience must be contemplated always as subject and never as mere predicate. And so with all the other categories.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"29.4"}],"sha256":"3368d7abd6733d9ef2f66f414a5d132c77143600a70b65cee2ad3c3dad35a108","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR","title":"Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.","order":30,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_PCMR","text":"### Section II Transcendental Deduction of the pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.\n\nThe manifold content in our representations can be given in an intuition which is merely sensuous—in other words, is nothing but susceptibility; and the form of this intuition can exist à priori in our faculty of representation, without being anything else but the mode in which the subject is affected. But the conjunction (conjunctio) of a manifold in intuition never can be given us by the senses; it cannot therefore be contained in the pure form of sensuous intuition, for it is a spontaneous act of the faculty of representation. And as we must, to distinguish it from sensibility, entitle this faculty understanding; so all conjunction whether conscious or unconscious, be it of the manifold in intuition, sensuous or non-sensuous, or of several conceptions—is an act of the understanding. To this act we shall give the general appellation of synthesis, thereby to indicate, at the same time, that we cannot represent anything as conjoined in the object without having previously conjoined it ourselves. Of all mental notions, that of conjunction is the only one which cannot be given through objects, but can be originated only by the subject itself, because it is an act of its purely spontaneous activity. The reader will easily enough perceive that the possibility of conjunction must be grounded in the very nature of this act, and that it must be equally valid for all conjunction, and that analysis, which appears to be its contrary, must, nevertheless, always presuppose it; for where the understanding has not previously conjoined, it cannot dissect or analyse, because only as conjoined by it, must that which is to be analysed have been given to our faculty of representation.\n\nBut the conception of conjunction includes, besides the conception of the manifold and of the synthesis of it, that of the unity of it also. Conjunction is the representation of the synthetical unity of the manifold.[[15]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-15) This idea of unity, therefore, cannot arise out of that of conjunction; much rather does that idea, by combining itself with the representation of the manifold, render the conception of conjunction possible. This unity, which à priori precedes all conceptions of conjunction, is not the category of unity (§ 6); for all the categories are based upon logical functions of judgement, and in these functions we already have conjunction, and consequently unity of given conceptions. It is therefore evident that the category of unity presupposes conjunction. We must therefore look still higher for this unity (as qualitative, § 8), in that, namely, which contains the ground of the unity of diverse conceptions in judgements, the ground, consequently, of the possibility of the existence of the understanding, even in regard to its logical use.\n\n[[15]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-15) Whether the representations are in themselves identical, and consequently whether one can be thought analytically by means of and through the other, is a question which we need not at present consider. Our Consciousness of the one, when we speak of the manifold, is always distinguishable from our consciousness of the other; and it is only respecting the synthesis of this (possible) consciousness that we here treat.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","text":"### Section II Transcendental Deduction of the pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\n### Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.\n\nThe manifold content in our representations can be given in an intuition which is merely sensuous—in other words, is nothing but susceptibility; and the form of this intuition can exist à priori in our faculty of representation, without being anything else but the mode in which the subject is affected. But the conjunction (conjunctio) of a manifold in intuition never can be given us by the senses; it cannot therefore be contained in the pure form of sensuous intuition, for it is a spontaneous act of the faculty of representation. And as we must, to distinguish it from sensibility, entitle this faculty understanding; so all conjunction whether conscious or unconscious, be it of the manifold in intuition, sensuous or non-sensuous, or of several conceptions—is an act of the understanding. To this act we shall give the general appellation of synthesis, thereby to indicate, at the same time, that we cannot represent anything as conjoined in the object without having previously conjoined it ourselves. Of all mental notions, that of conjunction is the only one which cannot be given through objects, but can be originated only by the subject itself, because it is an act of its purely spontaneous activity. The reader will easily enough perceive that the possibility of conjunction must be grounded in the very nature of this act, and that it must be equally valid for all conjunction, and that analysis, which appears to be its contrary, must, nevertheless, always presuppose it; for where the understanding has not previously conjoined, it cannot dissect or analyse, because only as conjoined by it, must that which is to be analysed have been given to our faculty of representation.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"30.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","text":"But the conception of conjunction includes, besides the conception of the manifold and of the synthesis of it, that of the unity of it also. Conjunction is the representation of the synthetical unity of the manifold.[[15]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-15) This idea of unity, therefore, cannot arise out of that of conjunction; much rather does that idea, by combining itself with the representation of the manifold, render the conception of conjunction possible. This unity, which à priori precedes all conceptions of conjunction, is not the category of unity (§ 6); for all the categories are based upon logical functions of judgement, and in these functions we already have conjunction, and consequently unity of given conceptions. It is therefore evident that the category of unity presupposes conjunction. We must therefore look still higher for this unity (as qualitative, § 8), in that, namely, which contains the ground of the unity of diverse conceptions in judgements, the ground, consequently, of the possibility of the existence of the understanding, even in regard to its logical use.\n\n[[15]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-15) Whether the representations are in themselves identical, and consequently whether one can be thought analytically by means of and through the other, is a question which we need not at present consider. Our Consciousness of the one, when we speak of the manifold, is always distinguishable from our consciousness of the other; and it is only respecting the synthesis of this (possible) consciousness that we here treat.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"30.2"}],"sha256":"c40f4fbe2b05f885285490eb11d0345c8fea0959f4c73f7dc0abbb14d4506089","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA","title":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12","order":31,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_OSUA","text":"### Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12\n\nThe “I think” must accompany all my representations, for otherwise something would be represented in me which could not be thought; in other words, the representation would either be impossible, or at least be, in relation to me, nothing. That representation which can be given previously to all thought is called intuition. All the diversity or manifold content of intuition, has, therefore, a necessary relation to the “I think,” in the subject in which this diversity is found. But this representation, “I think,” is an act of spontaneity; that is to say, it cannot be regarded as belonging to mere sensibility. I call it pure apperception, in order to distinguish it from empirical; or primitive apperception, because it is self-consciousness which, whilst it gives birth to the representation “I think,” must necessarily be capable of accompanying all our representations. It is in all acts of consciousness one and the same, and unaccompanied by it, no representation can exist for me. The unity of this apperception I call the transcendental unity of self-consciousness, in order to indicate the possibility of à priori cognition arising from it. For the manifold representations which are given in an intuition would not all of them be my representations, if they did not all belong to one self-consciousness, that is, as my representations (even although I am not conscious of them as such), they must conform to the condition under which alone they can exist together in a common self-consciousness, because otherwise they would not all without exception belong to me. From this primitive conjunction follow many important results.\n\nFor example, this universal identity of the apperception of the manifold given in intuition contains a synthesis of representations and is possible only by means of the consciousness of this synthesis. For the empirical consciousness which accompanies different representations is in itself fragmentary and disunited, and without relation to the identity of the subject. This relation, then, does not exist because I accompany every representation with consciousness, but because I join one representation to another, and am conscious of the synthesis of them. Consequently, only because I can connect a variety of given representations in one consciousness, is it possible that I can represent to myself the identity of consciousness in these representations; in other words, the analytical unity of apperception is possible only under the presupposition of a synthetical unity.[[16]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-16) The thought, “These representations given in intuition belong all of them to me,” is accordingly just the same as, “I unite them in one self-consciousness, or can at least so unite them”; and although this thought is not itself the consciousness of the synthesis of representations, it presupposes the possibility of it; that is to say, for the reason alone that I can comprehend the variety of my representations in one consciousness, do I call them my representations, for otherwise I must have as many-coloured and various a self as are the representations of which I am conscious. Synthetical unity of the manifold in intuitions, as given à priori, is therefore the foundation of the identity of apperception itself, which antecedes à priori all determinate thought. But the conjunction of representations into a conception is not to be found in objects themselves, nor can it be, as it were, borrowed from them and taken up into the understanding by perception, but it is on the contrary an operation of the understanding itself, which is nothing more than the faculty of conjoining à priori and of bringing the variety of given representations under the unity of apperception. This principle is the highest in all human cognition.\n\n[[16]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-16) All general conceptions—as such—depend, for their existence, on the analytical unity of consciousness. For example, when I think of red in general, I thereby think to myself a property which (as a characteristic mark) can be discovered somewhere, or can be united with other representations; consequently, it is only by means of a forethought possible synthetical unity that I can think to myself the analytical. A representation which is cogitated as common to different representations, is regarded as belonging to such as, besides this common representation, contain something different; consequently it must be previously thought in synthetical unity with other although only possible representations, before I can think in it the analytical unity of consciousness which makes it a conceptas communis. And thus the synthetical unity of apperception is the highest point with which we must connect every operation of the understanding, even the whole of logic, and after it our transcendental philosophy; indeed, this faculty is the understanding itself.\n\nThis fundamental principle of the necessary unity of apperception is indeed an identical, and therefore analytical, proposition; but it nevertheless explains the necessity for a synthesis of the manifold given in an intuition, without which the identity of self-consciousness would be incogitable. For the ego, as a simple representation, presents us with no manifold content; only in intuition, which is quite different from the representation ego, can it be given us, and by means of conjunction it is cogitated in one self-consciousness. An understanding, in which all the manifold should be given by means of consciousness itself, would be intuitive; our understanding can only think and must look for its intuition to sense. I am, therefore, conscious of my identical self, in relation to all the variety of representations given to me in an intuition, because I call all of them my representations. In other words, I am conscious myself of a necessary à priori synthesis of my representations, which is called the original synthetical unity of apperception, under which rank all the representations presented to me, but that only by means of a synthesis.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","text":"### Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12\n\nThe “I think” must accompany all my representations, for otherwise something would be represented in me which could not be thought; in other words, the representation would either be impossible, or at least be, in relation to me, nothing. That representation which can be given previously to all thought is called intuition. All the diversity or manifold content of intuition, has, therefore, a necessary relation to the “I think,” in the subject in which this diversity is found. But this representation, “I think,” is an act of spontaneity; that is to say, it cannot be regarded as belonging to mere sensibility. I call it pure apperception, in order to distinguish it from empirical; or primitive apperception, because it is self-consciousness which, whilst it gives birth to the representation “I think,” must necessarily be capable of accompanying all our representations. It is in all acts of consciousness one and the same, and unaccompanied by it, no representation can exist for me. The unity of this apperception I call the transcendental unity of self-consciousness, in order to indicate the possibility of à priori cognition arising from it. For the manifold representations which are given in an intuition would not all of them be my representations, if they did not all belong to one self-consciousness, that is, as my representations (even although I am not conscious of them as such), they must conform to the condition under which alone they can exist together in a common self-consciousness, because otherwise they would not all without exception belong to me. From this primitive conjunction follow many important results.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"31.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","text":"For example, this universal identity of the apperception of the manifold given in intuition contains a synthesis of representations and is possible only by means of the consciousness of this synthesis. For the empirical consciousness which accompanies different representations is in itself fragmentary and disunited, and without relation to the identity of the subject. This relation, then, does not exist because I accompany every representation with consciousness, but because I join one representation to another, and am conscious of the synthesis of them. Consequently, only because I can connect a variety of given representations in one consciousness, is it possible that I can represent to myself the identity of consciousness in these representations; in other words, the analytical unity of apperception is possible only under the presupposition of a synthetical unity.[[16]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-16) The thought, “These representations given in intuition belong all of them to me,” is accordingly just the same as, “I unite them in one self-consciousness, or can at least so unite them”; and although this thought is not itself the consciousness of the synthesis of representations, it presupposes the possibility of it; that is to say, for the reason alone that I can comprehend the variety of my representations in one consciousness, do I call them my representations, for otherwise I must have as many-coloured and various a self as are the representations of which I am conscious. Synthetical unity of the manifold in intuitions, as given à priori, is therefore the foundation of the identity of apperception itself, which antecedes à priori all determinate thought. But the conjunction of representations into a conception is not to be found in objects themselves, nor can it be, as it were, borrowed from them and taken up into the understanding by perception, but it is on the contrary an operation of the understanding itself, which is nothing more than the faculty of conjoining à priori and of bringing the variety of given representations under the unity of apperception. This principle is the highest in all human cognition.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"31.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0003","text":"[[16]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-16) All general conceptions—as such—depend, for their existence, on the analytical unity of consciousness. For example, when I think of red in general, I thereby think to myself a property which (as a characteristic mark) can be discovered somewhere, or can be united with other representations; consequently, it is only by means of a forethought possible synthetical unity that I can think to myself the analytical. A representation which is cogitated as common to different representations, is regarded as belonging to such as, besides this common representation, contain something different; consequently it must be previously thought in synthetical unity with other although only possible representations, before I can think in it the analytical unity of consciousness which makes it a conceptas communis. And thus the synthetical unity of apperception is the highest point with which we must connect every operation of the understanding, even the whole of logic, and after it our transcendental philosophy; indeed, this faculty is the understanding itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"31.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","text":"This fundamental principle of the necessary unity of apperception is indeed an identical, and therefore analytical, proposition; but it nevertheless explains the necessity for a synthesis of the manifold given in an intuition, without which the identity of self-consciousness would be incogitable. For the ego, as a simple representation, presents us with no manifold content; only in intuition, which is quite different from the representation ego, can it be given us, and by means of conjunction it is cogitated in one self-consciousness. An understanding, in which all the manifold should be given by means of consciousness itself, would be intuitive; our understanding can only think and must look for its intuition to sense. I am, therefore, conscious of my identical self, in relation to all the variety of representations given to me in an intuition, because I call all of them my representations. In other words, I am conscious myself of a necessary à priori synthesis of my representations, which is called the original synthetical unity of apperception, under which rank all the representations presented to me, but that only by means of a synthesis.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"31.4"}],"sha256":"88b1dbeae2227a33b096458fa905d3f8f64e14c733a79fc73b7c1d5b8a4140f2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA","title":"The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13","order":32,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_PSUA","text":"### The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13\n\nThe supreme principle of the possibility of all intuition in relation to sensibility was, according to our transcendental æsthetic, that all the manifold in intuition be subject to the formal conditions of space and time. The supreme principle of the possibility of it in relation to the understanding is that all the manifold in it be subject to conditions of the originally synthetical unity or apperception.[[17]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-17) To the former of these two principles are subject all the various representations of intuition, in so far as they are given to us; to the latter, in so far as they must be capable of conjunction in one consciousness; for without this nothing can be thought or cognized, because the given representations would not have in common the act Of the apperception “I think” and therefore could not be connected in one self-consciousness.\n\n[[17]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-17) Space and time, and all portions thereof, are intuitions; consequently are, with a manifold for their content, single representations. (See the Transcendental Æsthetic.) Consequently, they are not pure conceptions, by means of which the same consciousness is found in a great number of representations; but, on the contrary, they are many representations contained in one, the consciousness of which is, so to speak, compounded. The unity of consciousness is nevertheless synthetical and, therefore, primitive. From this peculiar character of consciousness follow many important consequences. (See § 21.)\n\nUnderstanding is, to speak generally, the faculty Of cognitions. These consist in the determined relation of given representation to an object. But an object is that, in the conception of which the manifold in a given intuition is united. Now all union of representations requires unity of consciousness in the synthesis of them. Consequently, it is the unity of consciousness alone that constitutes the possibility of representations relating to an object, and therefore of their objective validity, and of their becoming cognitions, and consequently, the possibility of the existence of the understanding itself.\n\nThe first pure cognition of understanding, then, upon which is founded all its other exercise, and which is at the same time perfectly independent of all conditions of mere sensuous intuition, is the principle of the original synthetical unity of apperception. Thus the mere form of external sensuous intuition, namely, space, affords us, per se, no cognition; it merely contributes the manifold in à priori intuition to a possible cognition. But, in order to cognize something in space (for example, a line), I must draw it, and thus produce synthetically a determined conjunction of the given manifold, so that the unity of this act is at the same time the unity of consciousness (in the conception of a line), and by this means alone is an object (a determinate space) cognized. The synthetical unity of consciousness is, therefore, an objective condition of all cognition, which I do not merely require in order to cognize an object, but to which every intuition must necessarily be subject, in order to become an object for me; because in any other way, and without this synthesis, the manifold in intuition could not be united in one consciousness.\n\nThis proposition is, as already said, itself analytical, although it constitutes the synthetical unity, the condition of all thought; for it states nothing more than that all my representations in any given intuition must be subject to the condition which alone enables me to connect them, as my representation with the identical self, and so to unite them synthetically in one apperception, by means of the general expression, “I think.”\n\nBut this principle is not to be regarded as a principle for every possible understanding, but only for the understanding by means of whose pure apperception in the thought I am, no manifold content is given. The understanding or mind which contained the manifold in intuition, in and through the act itself of its own self-consciousness, in other words, an understanding by and in the representation of which the objects of the representation should at the same time exist, would not require a special act of synthesis of the manifold as the condition of the unity of its consciousness, an act of which the human understanding, which thinks only and cannot intuite, has absolute need. But this principle is the first principle of all the operations of our understanding, so that we cannot form the least conception of any other possible understanding, either of one such as should be itself intuition, or possess a sensuous intuition, but with forms different from those of space and time.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","text":"### The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13\n\nThe supreme principle of the possibility of all intuition in relation to sensibility was, according to our transcendental æsthetic, that all the manifold in intuition be subject to the formal conditions of space and time. The supreme principle of the possibility of it in relation to the understanding is that all the manifold in it be subject to conditions of the originally synthetical unity or apperception.[[17]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-17) To the former of these two principles are subject all the various representations of intuition, in so far as they are given to us; to the latter, in so far as they must be capable of conjunction in one consciousness; for without this nothing can be thought or cognized, because the given representations would not have in common the act Of the apperception “I think” and therefore could not be connected in one self-consciousness.\n\n[[17]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-17) Space and time, and all portions thereof, are intuitions; consequently are, with a manifold for their content, single representations. (See the Transcendental Æsthetic.) Consequently, they are not pure conceptions, by means of which the same consciousness is found in a great number of representations; but, on the contrary, they are many representations contained in one, the consciousness of which is, so to speak, compounded. The unity of consciousness is nevertheless synthetical and, therefore, primitive. From this peculiar character of consciousness follow many important consequences. (See § 21.)","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"32.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","text":"Understanding is, to speak generally, the faculty Of cognitions. These consist in the determined relation of given representation to an object. But an object is that, in the conception of which the manifold in a given intuition is united. Now all union of representations requires unity of consciousness in the synthesis of them. Consequently, it is the unity of consciousness alone that constitutes the possibility of representations relating to an object, and therefore of their objective validity, and of their becoming cognitions, and consequently, the possibility of the existence of the understanding itself.\n\nThe first pure cognition of understanding, then, upon which is founded all its other exercise, and which is at the same time perfectly independent of all conditions of mere sensuous intuition, is the principle of the original synthetical unity of apperception. Thus the mere form of external sensuous intuition, namely, space, affords us, per se, no cognition; it merely contributes the manifold in à priori intuition to a possible cognition. But, in order to cognize something in space (for example, a line), I must draw it, and thus produce synthetically a determined conjunction of the given manifold, so that the unity of this act is at the same time the unity of consciousness (in the conception of a line), and by this means alone is an object (a determinate space) cognized. The synthetical unity of consciousness is, therefore, an objective condition of all cognition, which I do not merely require in order to cognize an object, but to which every intuition must necessarily be subject, in order to become an object for me; because in any other way, and without this synthesis, the manifold in intuition could not be united in one consciousness.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"32.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","text":"This proposition is, as already said, itself analytical, although it constitutes the synthetical unity, the condition of all thought; for it states nothing more than that all my representations in any given intuition must be subject to the condition which alone enables me to connect them, as my representation with the identical self, and so to unite them synthetically in one apperception, by means of the general expression, “I think.”\n\nBut this principle is not to be regarded as a principle for every possible understanding, but only for the understanding by means of whose pure apperception in the thought I am, no manifold content is given. The understanding or mind which contained the manifold in intuition, in and through the act itself of its own self-consciousness, in other words, an understanding by and in the representation of which the objects of the representation should at the same time exist, would not require a special act of synthesis of the manifold as the condition of the unity of its consciousness, an act of which the human understanding, which thinks only and cannot intuite, has absolute need. But this principle is the first principle of all the operations of our understanding, so that we cannot form the least conception of any other possible understanding, either of one such as should be itself intuition, or possess a sensuous intuition, but with forms different from those of space and time.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"32.3"}],"sha256":"be4f5020d4ff547eab4229ba69c69182a7cfdd9df4960e7cd0a027f927d182c0","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS","title":"What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14","order":33,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_WOUS","text":"### What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14\n\nIt is by means of the transcendental unity of apperception that all the manifold, given in an intuition is united into a conception of the object. On this account it is called objective, and must be distinguished from the subjective unity of consciousness, which is a determination of the internal sense, by means of which the said manifold in intuition is given empirically to be so united. Whether I can be empirically conscious of the manifold as coexistent or as successive, depends upon circumstances, or empirical conditions. Hence the empirical unity of consciousness by means of association of representations, itself relates to a phenomenal world and is wholly contingent. On the contrary, the pure form of intuition in time, merely as an intuition, which contains a given manifold, is subject to the original unity of consciousness, and that solely by means of the necessary relation of the manifold in intuition to the “I think,” consequently by means of the pure synthesis of the understanding, which lies à priori at the foundation of all empirical synthesis. The transcendental unity of apperception is alone objectively valid; the empirical which we do not consider in this essay, and which is merely a unity deduced from the former under given conditions in concreto, possesses only subjective validity. One person connects the notion conveyed in a word with one thing, another with another thing; and the unity of consciousness in that which is empirical, is, in relation to that which is given by experience, not necessarily and universally valid.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","text":"### What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14\n\nIt is by means of the transcendental unity of apperception that all the manifold, given in an intuition is united into a conception of the object. On this account it is called objective, and must be distinguished from the subjective unity of consciousness, which is a determination of the internal sense, by means of which the said manifold in intuition is given empirically to be so united. Whether I can be empirically conscious of the manifold as coexistent or as successive, depends upon circumstances, or empirical conditions. Hence the empirical unity of consciousness by means of association of representations, itself relates to a phenomenal world and is wholly contingent. On the contrary, the pure form of intuition in time, merely as an intuition, which contains a given manifold, is subject to the original unity of consciousness, and that solely by means of the necessary relation of the manifold in intuition to the “I think,” consequently by means of the pure synthesis of the understanding, which lies à priori at the foundation of all empirical synthesis. The transcendental unity of apperception is alone objectively valid; the empirical which we do not consider in this essay, and which is merely a unity deduced from the former under given conditions in concreto, possesses only subjective validity. One person connects the notion conveyed in a word with one thing, another with another thing; and the unity of consciousness in that which is empirical, is, in relation to that which is given by experience, not necessarily and universally valid.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"33.1"}],"sha256":"fe51095b02ab6ba96b1878c8d409127270329c250366485c6204c3f117c87048","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ","title":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15","order":34,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_LFAJ","text":"### The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15\n\nI could never satisfy myself with the definition which logicians give of a judgement. It is, according to them, the representation of a relation between two conceptions. I shall not dwell here on the faultiness of this definition, in that it suits only for categorical and not for hypothetical or disjunctive judgements, these latter containing a relation not of conceptions but of judgements themselves—a blunder from which many evil results have followed.[[18]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-18) It is more important for our present purpose to observe, that this definition does not determine in what the said relation consists.\n\n[[18]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-18) The tedious doctrine of the four syllogistic figures concerns only categorical syllogisms; and although it is nothing more than an artifice by surreptitiously introducing immediate conclusions (consequentiae immediatae) among the premises of a pure syllogism, to give rise to an appearance of more modes of drawing a conclusion than that in the first figure, the artifice would not have had much success, had not its authors succeeded in bringing categorical judgements into exclusive respect, as those to which all others must be referred—a doctrine, however, which, according to § 5, is utterly false.\n\nBut if I investigate more closely the relation of given cognitions in every judgement, and distinguish it, as belonging to the understanding, from the relation which is produced according to laws of the reproductive imagination (which has only subjective validity), I find that judgement is nothing but the mode of bringing given cognitions under the objective unit of apperception. This is plain from our use of the term of relation is in judgements, in order to distinguish the objective unity of given representations from the subjective unity. For this term indicates the relation of these representations to the original apperception, and also their necessary unity, even although the judgement is empirical, therefore contingent, as in the judgement: “All bodies are heavy.” I do not mean by this, that these representations do necessarily belong to each other in empirical intuition, but that by means of the necessary unity of appreciation they belong to each other in the synthesis of intuitions, that is to say, they belong to each other according to principles of the objective determination of all our representations, in so far as cognition can arise from them, these principles being all deduced from the main principle of the transcendental unity of apperception. In this way alone can there arise from this relation a judgement, that is, a relation which has objective validity, and is perfectly distinct from that relation of the very same representations which has only subjective validity—a relation, to wit, which is produced according to laws of association. According to these laws, I could only say: “When I hold in my hand or carry a body, I feel an impression of weight”; but I could not say: “It, the body, is heavy”; for this is tantamount to saying both these representations are conjoined in the object, that is, without distinction as to the condition of the subject, and do not merely stand together in my perception, however frequently the perceptive act may be repeated.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","text":"### The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15\n\nI could never satisfy myself with the definition which logicians give of a judgement. It is, according to them, the representation of a relation between two conceptions. I shall not dwell here on the faultiness of this definition, in that it suits only for categorical and not for hypothetical or disjunctive judgements, these latter containing a relation not of conceptions but of judgements themselves—a blunder from which many evil results have followed.[[18]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-18) It is more important for our present purpose to observe, that this definition does not determine in what the said relation consists.\n\n[[18]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-18) The tedious doctrine of the four syllogistic figures concerns only categorical syllogisms; and although it is nothing more than an artifice by surreptitiously introducing immediate conclusions (consequentiae immediatae) among the premises of a pure syllogism, to give rise to an appearance of more modes of drawing a conclusion than that in the first figure, the artifice would not have had much success, had not its authors succeeded in bringing categorical judgements into exclusive respect, as those to which all others must be referred—a doctrine, however, which, according to § 5, is utterly false.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"34.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","text":"But if I investigate more closely the relation of given cognitions in every judgement, and distinguish it, as belonging to the understanding, from the relation which is produced according to laws of the reproductive imagination (which has only subjective validity), I find that judgement is nothing but the mode of bringing given cognitions under the objective unit of apperception. This is plain from our use of the term of relation is in judgements, in order to distinguish the objective unity of given representations from the subjective unity. For this term indicates the relation of these representations to the original apperception, and also their necessary unity, even although the judgement is empirical, therefore contingent, as in the judgement: “All bodies are heavy.” I do not mean by this, that these representations do necessarily belong to each other in empirical intuition, but that by means of the necessary unity of appreciation they belong to each other in the synthesis of intuitions, that is to say, they belong to each other according to principles of the objective determination of all our representations, in so far as cognition can arise from them, these principles being all deduced from the main principle of the transcendental unity of apperception. In this way alone can there arise from this relation a judgement, that is, a relation which has objective validity, and is perfectly distinct from that relation of the very same representations which has only subjective validity—a relation, to wit, which is produced according to laws of association. According to these laws, I could only say: “When I hold in my hand or carry a body, I feel an impression of weight”; but I could not say: “It, the body, is heavy”; for this is tantamount to saying both these representations are conjoined in the object, that is, without distinction as to the condition of the subject, and do not merely stand together in my perception, however frequently the perceptive act may be repeated.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"34.2"}],"sha256":"6320996b0ef2651f303bb97c4f3d41c9f4eb0800c2c3f1e01971a3f93f214f03","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA","title":"All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16","order":35,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_ASIA","text":"### All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16\n\nThe manifold content given in a sensuous intuition comes necessarily under the original synthetical unity of apperception, because thereby alone is the unity of intuition possible (§ 13). But that act of the understanding, by which the manifold content of given representations (whether intuitions or conceptions) is brought under one apperception, is the logical function of judgements (§ 15). All the manifold, therefore, in so far as it is given in one empirical intuition, is determined in relation to one of the logical functions of judgement, by means of which it is brought into union in one consciousness. Now the categories are nothing else than these functions of judgement so far as the manifold in a given intuition is determined in relation to them (§ 9). Consequently, the manifold in a given intuition is necessarily subject to the categories of the understanding.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","text":"### All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16\n\nThe manifold content given in a sensuous intuition comes necessarily under the original synthetical unity of apperception, because thereby alone is the unity of intuition possible (§ 13). But that act of the understanding, by which the manifold content of given representations (whether intuitions or conceptions) is brought under one apperception, is the logical function of judgements (§ 15). All the manifold, therefore, in so far as it is given in one empirical intuition, is determined in relation to one of the logical functions of judgement, by means of which it is brought into union in one consciousness. Now the categories are nothing else than these functions of judgement so far as the manifold in a given intuition is determined in relation to them (§ 9). Consequently, the manifold in a given intuition is necessarily subject to the categories of the understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"35.1"}],"sha256":"7b3582aaad179eed331a3f033cba3ca94fa2820b534b022a95a0dfb35e8ab467","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1","title":"Observation § 17","order":36,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_OBS1","text":"### Observation § 17\n\nThe manifold in an intuition, which I call mine, is represented by means of the synthesis of the understanding, as belonging to the necessary unity of self-consciousness, and this takes place by means of the category.[[19]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-19) The category indicates accordingly that the empirical consciousness of a given manifold in an intuition is subject to a pure self-consciousness à priori, in the same manner as an empirical intuition is subject to a pure sensuous intuition, which is also à priori. In the above proposition, then, lies the beginning of a deduction of the pure conceptions of the understanding. Now, as the categories have their origin in the understanding alone, independently of sensibility, I must in my deduction make abstraction of the mode in which the manifold of an empirical intuition is given, in order to fix my attention exclusively on the unity which is brought by the understanding into the intuition by means of the category. In what follows (§ 22), it will be shown, from the mode in which the empirical intuition is given in the faculty of sensibility, that the unity which belongs to it is no other than that which the category (according to § 16) imposes on the manifold in a given intuition, and thus, its à priori validity in regard to all objects of sense being established, the purpose of our deduction will be fully attained.\n\n[[19]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-19) The proof of this rests on the represented unity of intuition, by means of which an object is given, and which always includes in itself a synthesis of the manifold to be intuited, and also the relation of this latter to unity of apperception.\n\nBut there is one thing in the above demonstration of which I could not make abstraction, namely, that the manifold to be intuited must be given previously to the synthesis of the understanding, and independently of it. How this takes place remains here undetermined. For if I cogitate an understanding which was itself intuitive (as, for example, a divine understanding which should not represent given objects, but by whose representation the objects themselves should be given or produced), the categories would possess no significance in relation to such a faculty of cognition. They are merely rules for an understanding, whose whole power consists in thought, that is, in the act of submitting the synthesis of the manifold which is presented to it in intuition from a very different quarter, to the unity of apperception; a faculty, therefore, which cognizes nothing per se, but only connects and arranges the material of cognition, the intuition, namely, which must be presented to it by means of the object. But to show reasons for this peculiar character of our understandings, that it produces unity of apperception à priori only by means of categories, and a certain kind and number thereof, is as impossible as to explain why we are endowed with precisely so many functions of judgement and no more, or why time and space are the only forms of our intuition.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","text":"### Observation § 17\n\nThe manifold in an intuition, which I call mine, is represented by means of the synthesis of the understanding, as belonging to the necessary unity of self-consciousness, and this takes place by means of the category.[[19]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-19) The category indicates accordingly that the empirical consciousness of a given manifold in an intuition is subject to a pure self-consciousness à priori, in the same manner as an empirical intuition is subject to a pure sensuous intuition, which is also à priori. In the above proposition, then, lies the beginning of a deduction of the pure conceptions of the understanding. Now, as the categories have their origin in the understanding alone, independently of sensibility, I must in my deduction make abstraction of the mode in which the manifold of an empirical intuition is given, in order to fix my attention exclusively on the unity which is brought by the understanding into the intuition by means of the category. In what follows (§ 22), it will be shown, from the mode in which the empirical intuition is given in the faculty of sensibility, that the unity which belongs to it is no other than that which the category (according to § 16) imposes on the manifold in a given intuition, and thus, its à priori validity in regard to all objects of sense being established, the purpose of our deduction will be fully attained.\n\n[[19]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-19) The proof of this rests on the represented unity of intuition, by means of which an object is given, and which always includes in itself a synthesis of the manifold to be intuited, and also the relation of this latter to unity of apperception.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"36.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","text":"But there is one thing in the above demonstration of which I could not make abstraction, namely, that the manifold to be intuited must be given previously to the synthesis of the understanding, and independently of it. How this takes place remains here undetermined. For if I cogitate an understanding which was itself intuitive (as, for example, a divine understanding which should not represent given objects, but by whose representation the objects themselves should be given or produced), the categories would possess no significance in relation to such a faculty of cognition. They are merely rules for an understanding, whose whole power consists in thought, that is, in the act of submitting the synthesis of the manifold which is presented to it in intuition from a very different quarter, to the unity of apperception; a faculty, therefore, which cognizes nothing per se, but only connects and arranges the material of cognition, the intuition, namely, which must be presented to it by means of the object. But to show reasons for this peculiar character of our understandings, that it produces unity of apperception à priori only by means of categories, and a certain kind and number thereof, is as impossible as to explain why we are endowed with precisely so many functions of judgement and no more, or why time and space are the only forms of our intuition.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"36.2"}],"sha256":"2a21547e866cf9715c5cc634bf824fde93b0831545c65b0d0531fc5c6c85b953","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE","title":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18","order":37,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_CAOE","text":"### In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18\n\nTo think an object and to cognize an object are by no means the same thing. In cognition there are two elements: firstly, the conception, whereby an object is cogitated (the category); and, secondly, the intuition, whereby the object is given. For supposing that to the conception a corresponding intuition could not be given, it would still be a thought as regards its form, but without any object, and no cognition of anything would be possible by means of it, inasmuch as, so far as I knew, there existed and could exist nothing to which my thought could be applied. Now all intuition possible to us is sensuous; consequently, our thought of an object by means of a pure conception of the understanding, can become cognition for us only in so far as this conception is applied to objects of the senses. Sensuous intuition is either pure intuition (space and time) or empirical intuition—of that which is immediately represented in space and time by means of sensation as real. Through the determination of pure intuition we obtain à priori cognitions of objects, as in mathematics, but only as regards their form as phenomena; whether there can exist things which must be intuited in this form is not thereby established. All mathematical conceptions, therefore, are not per se cognition, except in so far as we presuppose that there exist things which can only be represented conformably to the form of our pure sensuous intuition. But things in space and time are given only in so far as they are perceptions (representations accompanied with sensation), therefore only by empirical representation. Consequently the pure conceptions of the understanding, even when they are applied to intuitions à priori (as in mathematics), produce cognition only in so far as these (and therefore the conceptions of the understanding by means of them) can be applied to empirical intuitions. Consequently the categories do not, even by means of pure intuition afford us any cognition of things; they can only do so in so far as they can be applied to empirical intuition. That is to say, the categories serve only to render empirical cognition possible. But this is what we call experience. Consequently, in cognition, their application to objects of experience is the only legitimate use of the categories.\n\n§ 19\n\nThe foregoing proposition is of the utmost importance, for it determines the limits of the exercise of the pure conceptions of the understanding in regard to objects, just as transcendental æsthetic determined the limits of the exercise of the pure form of our sensuous intuition. Space and time, as conditions of the possibility of the presentation of objects to us, are valid no further than for objects of sense, consequently, only for experience. Beyond these limits they represent to us nothing, for they belong only to sense, and have no reality apart from it. The pure conceptions of the understanding are free from this limitation, and extend to objects of intuition in general, be the intuition like or unlike to ours, provided only it be sensuous, and not intellectual. But this extension of conceptions beyond the range of our intuition is of no advantage; for they are then mere empty conceptions of objects, as to the possibility or impossibility of the existence of which they furnish us with no means of discovery. They are mere forms of thought, without objective reality, because we have no intuition to which the synthetical unity of apperception, which alone the categories contain, could be applied, for the purpose of determining an object. Our sensuous and empirical intuition can alone give them significance and meaning.\n\nIf, then, we suppose an object of a non-sensuous intuition to be given we can in that case represent it by all those predicates which are implied in the presupposition that nothing appertaining to sensuous intuition belongs to it; for example, that it is not extended, or in space; that its duration is not time; that in it no change (the effect of the determinations in time) is to be met with, and so on. But it is no proper knowledge if I merely indicate what the intuition of the object is not, without being able to say what is contained in it, for I have not shown the possibility of an object to which my pure conception of understanding could be applicable, because I have not been able to furnish any intuition corresponding to it, but am only able to say that our intuition is not valid for it. But the most important point is this, that to a something of this kind not one category can be found applicable. Take, for example, the conception of substance, that is, something that can exist as subject, but never as mere predicate; in regard to this conception I am quite ignorant whether there can really be anything to correspond to such a determination of thought, if empirical intuition did not afford me the occasion for its application. But of this more in the sequel.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","text":"### In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18\n\nTo think an object and to cognize an object are by no means the same thing. In cognition there are two elements: firstly, the conception, whereby an object is cogitated (the category); and, secondly, the intuition, whereby the object is given. For supposing that to the conception a corresponding intuition could not be given, it would still be a thought as regards its form, but without any object, and no cognition of anything would be possible by means of it, inasmuch as, so far as I knew, there existed and could exist nothing to which my thought could be applied. Now all intuition possible to us is sensuous; consequently, our thought of an object by means of a pure conception of the understanding, can become cognition for us only in so far as this conception is applied to objects of the senses. Sensuous intuition is either pure intuition (space and time) or empirical intuition—of that which is immediately represented in space and time by means of sensation as real. Through the determination of pure intuition we obtain à priori cognitions of objects, as in mathematics, but only as regards their form as phenomena; whether there can exist things which must be intuited in this form is not thereby established. All mathematical conceptions, therefore, are not per se cognition, except in so far as we presuppose that there exist things which can only be represented conformably to the form of our pure sensuous intuition. But things in space and time are given only in so far as they are perceptions (representations accompanied with sensation), therefore only by empirical representation. Consequently the pure conceptions of the understanding, even when they are applied to intuitions à priori (as in mathematics), produce cognition only in so far as these (and therefore the conceptions of the understanding by means of them) can be applied to empirical intuitions. Consequently the categories do not, even by means of pure intuition afford us any cognition of things; they can only do so in so far as they can be applied to empirical intuition. That is to say, the categories serve only to render empirical cognition possible. But this is what we call experience. Consequently, in cognition, their application to objects of experience is the only legitimate use of the categories.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"37.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","text":"§ 19\n\nThe foregoing proposition is of the utmost importance, for it determines the limits of the exercise of the pure conceptions of the understanding in regard to objects, just as transcendental æsthetic determined the limits of the exercise of the pure form of our sensuous intuition. Space and time, as conditions of the possibility of the presentation of objects to us, are valid no further than for objects of sense, consequently, only for experience. Beyond these limits they represent to us nothing, for they belong only to sense, and have no reality apart from it. The pure conceptions of the understanding are free from this limitation, and extend to objects of intuition in general, be the intuition like or unlike to ours, provided only it be sensuous, and not intellectual. But this extension of conceptions beyond the range of our intuition is of no advantage; for they are then mere empty conceptions of objects, as to the possibility or impossibility of the existence of which they furnish us with no means of discovery. They are mere forms of thought, without objective reality, because we have no intuition to which the synthetical unity of apperception, which alone the categories contain, could be applied, for the purpose of determining an object. Our sensuous and empirical intuition can alone give them significance and meaning.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"37.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","text":"If, then, we suppose an object of a non-sensuous intuition to be given we can in that case represent it by all those predicates which are implied in the presupposition that nothing appertaining to sensuous intuition belongs to it; for example, that it is not extended, or in space; that its duration is not time; that in it no change (the effect of the determinations in time) is to be met with, and so on. But it is no proper knowledge if I merely indicate what the intuition of the object is not, without being able to say what is contained in it, for I have not shown the possibility of an object to which my pure conception of understanding could be applicable, because I have not been able to furnish any intuition corresponding to it, but am only able to say that our intuition is not valid for it. But the most important point is this, that to a something of this kind not one category can be found applicable. Take, for example, the conception of substance, that is, something that can exist as subject, but never as mere predicate; in regard to this conception I am quite ignorant whether there can really be anything to correspond to such a determination of thought, if empirical intuition did not afford me the occasion for its application. But of this more in the sequel.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"37.3"}],"sha256":"2a8bbe82a3d4268fb1e1e5352b7beaae2bb45bf231ddcb6dbdf45e6d12cc2550","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS","title":"Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20","order":38,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_ACOS","text":"### Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20\n\nThe pure conceptions of the understanding apply to objects of intuition in general, through the understanding alone, whether the intuition be our own or some other, provided only it be sensuous, but are, for this very reason, mere forms of thought, by means of which alone no determined object can be cognized. The synthesis or conjunction of the manifold in these conceptions relates, we have said, only to the unity of apperception, and is for this reason the ground of the possibility of à priori cognition, in so far as this cognition is dependent on the understanding. This synthesis is, therefore, not merely transcendental, but also purely intellectual. But because a certain form of sensuous intuition exists in the mind à priori which rests on the receptivity of the representative faculty (sensibility), the understanding, as a spontaneity, is able to determine the internal sense by means of the diversity of given representations, conformably to the synthetical unity of apperception, and thus to cogitate the synthetical unity of the apperception of the manifold of sensuous intuition à priori, as the condition to which must necessarily be submitted all objects of human intuition. And in this manner the categories as mere forms of thought receive objective reality, that is, application to objects which are given to us in intuition, but that only as phenomena, for it is only of phenomena that we are capable of à priori intuition.\n\nThis synthesis of the manifold of sensuous intuition, which is possible and necessary à priori, may be called figurative (synthesis speciosa), in contradistinction to that which is cogitated in the mere category in regard to the manifold of an intuition in general, and is called connection or conjunction of the understanding (synthesis intellectualis). Both are transcendental, not merely because they themselves precede à priori all experience, but also because they form the basis for the possibility of other cognition à priori.\n\nBut the figurative synthesis, when it has relation only to the originally synthetical unity of apperception, that is to the transcendental unity cogitated in the categories, must, to be distinguished from the purely intellectual conjunction, be entitled the transcendental synthesis of imagination. Imagination is the faculty of representing an object even without its presence in intuition. Now, as all our intuition is sensuous, imagination, by reason of the subjective condition under which alone it can give a corresponding intuition to the conceptions of the understanding, belongs to sensibility. But in so far as the synthesis of the imagination is an act of spontaneity, which is determinative, and not, like sense, merely determinable, and which is consequently able to determine sense à priori, according to its form, conformably to the unity of apperception, in so far is the imagination a faculty of determining sensibility à priori, and its synthesis of intuitions according to the categories must be the transcendental synthesis of the imagination. It is an operation of the understanding on sensibility, and the first application of the understanding to objects of possible intuition, and at the same time the basis for the exercise of the other functions of that faculty. As figurative, it is distinguished from the merely intellectual synthesis, which is produced by the understanding alone, without the aid of imagination. Now, in so far as imagination is spontaneity, I sometimes call it also the productive imagination, and distinguish it from the reproductive, the synthesis of which is subject entirely to empirical laws, those of association, namely, and which, therefore, contributes nothing to the explanation of the possibility of à priori cognition, and for this reason belongs not to transcendental philosophy, but to psychology.\n\nWe have now arrived at the proper place for explaining the paradox which must have struck every one in our exposition of the internal sense (§ 6), namely—how this sense represents us to our own consciousness, only as we appear to ourselves, not as we are in ourselves, because, to wit, we intuite ourselves only as we are inwardly affected. Now this appears to be contradictory, inasmuch as we thus stand in a passive relation to ourselves; and therefore in the systems of psychology, the internal sense is commonly held to be one with the faculty of apperception, while we, on the contrary, carefully distinguish them.\n\nThat which determines the internal sense is the understanding, and its original power of conjoining the manifold of intuition, that is, of bringing this under an apperception (upon which rests the possibility of the understanding itself). Now, as the human understanding is not in itself a faculty of intuition, and is unable to exercise such a power, in order to conjoin, as it were, the manifold of its own intuition, the synthesis of understanding is, considered per se, nothing but the unity of action, of which, as such, it is self-conscious, even apart from sensibility, by which, moreover, it is able to determine our internal sense in respect of the manifold which may be presented to it according to the form of sensuous intuition. Thus, under the name of a transcendental synthesis of imagination, the understanding exercises an activity upon the passive subject, whose faculty it is; and so we are right in saying that the internal sense is affected thereby. Apperception and its synthetical unity are by no means one and the same with the internal sense. The former, as the source of all our synthetical conjunction, applies, under the name of the categories, to the manifold of intuition in general, prior to all sensuous intuition of objects. The internal sense, on the contrary, contains merely the form of intuition, but without any synthetical conjunction of the manifold therein, and consequently does not contain any determined intuition, which is possible only through consciousness of the determination of the manifold by the transcendental act of the imagination (synthetical influence of the understanding on the internal sense), which I have named figurative synthesis.\n\nThis we can indeed always perceive in ourselves. We cannot cogitate a geometrical line without drawing it in thought, nor a circle without describing it, nor represent the three dimensions of space without drawing three lines from the same point perpendicular to one another. We cannot even cogitate time, unless, in drawing a straight line (which is to serve as the external figurative representation of time), we fix our attention on the act of the synthesis of the manifold, whereby we determine successively the internal sense, and thus attend also to the succession of this determination. Motion as an act of the subject (not as a determination of an object),[[20]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-20) consequently the synthesis of the manifold in space, if we make abstraction of space and attend merely to the act by which we determine the internal sense according to its form, is that which produces the conception of succession. The understanding, therefore, does by no means find in the internal sense any such synthesis of the manifold, but produces it, in that it affects this sense. At the same time, how “I who think” is distinct from the “i” which intuites itself (other modes of intuition being cogitable as at least possible), and yet one and the same with this latter as the same subject; how, therefore, I am able to say: “I, as an intelligence and thinking subject, cognize myself as an object thought, so far as I am, moreover, given to myself in intuition—only, like other phenomena, not as I am in myself, and as considered by the understanding, but merely as I appear”—is a question that has in it neither more nor less difficulty than the question—“How can I be an object to myself?” or this—“How I can be an object of my own intuition and internal perceptions?” But that such must be the fact, if we admit that space is merely a pure form of the phenomena of external sense, can be clearly proved by the consideration that we cannot represent time, which is not an object of external intuition, in any other way than under the image of a line, which we draw in thought, a mode of representation without which we could not cognize the unity of its dimension, and also that we are necessitated to take our determination of periods of time, or of points of time, for all our internal perceptions from the changes which we perceive in outward things. It follows that we must arrange the determinations of the internal sense, as phenomena in time, exactly in the same manner as we arrange those of the external senses in space. And consequently, if we grant, respecting this latter, that by means of them we know objects only in so far as we are affected externally, we must also confess, with regard to the internal sense, that by means of it we intuite ourselves only as we are internally affected by ourselves; in other words, as regards internal intuition, we cognize our own subject only as phenomenon, and not as it is in itself.[[21]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-21)\n\n[[20]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-20) Motion of an object in space does not belong to a pure science, consequently not to geometry; because, that a thing is movable cannot be known à priori, but only from experience. But motion, considered as the description of a space, is a pure act of the successive synthesis of the manifold in external intuition by means of productive imagination, and belongs not only to geometry, but even to transcendental philosophy.\n\n[[21]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-21) I do not see why so much difficulty should be found in admitting that our internal sense is affected by ourselves. Every act of attention exemplifies it. In such an act the understanding determines the internal sense by the synthetical conjunction which it cogitates, conformably to the internal intuition which corresponds to the manifold in the synthesis of the understanding. How much the mind is usually affected thereby every one will be able to perceive in himself.\n\n§ 21\n\nOn the other hand, in the transcendental synthesis of the manifold content of representations, consequently in the synthetical unity of apperception, I am conscious of myself, not as I appear to myself, nor as I am in myself, but only that “I am.” This representation is a thought, not an intuition. Now, as in order to cognize ourselves, in addition to the act of thinking, which subjects the manifold of every possible intuition to the unity of apperception, there is necessary a determinate mode of intuition, whereby this manifold is given; although my own existence is certainly not mere phenomenon (much less mere illusion), the determination of my existence[[22]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-22) Can only take place conformably to the form of the internal sense, according to the particular mode in which the manifold which I conjoin is given in internal intuition, and I have therefore no knowledge of myself as I am, but merely as I appear to myself. The consciousness of self is thus very far from a knowledge of self, in which I do not use the categories, whereby I cogitate an object, by means of the conjunction of the manifold in one apperception. In the same way as I require, for the sake of the cognition of an object distinct from myself, not only the thought of an object in general (in the category), but also an intuition by which to determine that general conception, in the same way do I require, in order to the cognition of myself, not only the consciousness of myself or the thought that I think myself, but in addition an intuition of the manifold in myself, by which to determine this thought. It is true that I exist as an intelligence which is conscious only of its faculty of conjunction or synthesis, but subjected in relation to the manifold which this intelligence has to conjoin to a limitative conjunction called the internal sense. My intelligence (that is, I) can render that conjunction or synthesis perceptible only according to the relations of time, which are quite beyond the proper sphere of the conceptions of the understanding and consequently cognize itself in respect to an intuition (which cannot possibly be intellectual, nor given by the understanding), only as it appears to itself, and not as it would cognize itself, if its intuition were intellectual.\n\n[[22]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-22) The “I think” expresses the act of determining my own existence. My existence is thus already given by the act of consciousness; but the mode in which I must determine my existence, that is, the mode in which I must place the manifold belonging to my existence, is not thereby given. For this purpose intuition of self is required, and this intuition possesses a form given à priori, namely, time, which is sensuous, and belongs to our receptivity of the determinable. Now, as I do not possess another intuition of self which gives the determining in me (of the spontaneity of which I am conscious), prior to the act of determination, in the same manner as time gives the determinable, it is clear that I am unable to determine my own existence as that of a spontaneous being, but I am only able to represent to myself the spontaneity of my thought, that is, of my determination, and my existence remains ever determinable in a purely sensuous manner, that is to say, like the existence of a phenomenon. But it is because of this spontaneity that I call myself an intelligence.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","text":"### Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20\n\nThe pure conceptions of the understanding apply to objects of intuition in general, through the understanding alone, whether the intuition be our own or some other, provided only it be sensuous, but are, for this very reason, mere forms of thought, by means of which alone no determined object can be cognized. The synthesis or conjunction of the manifold in these conceptions relates, we have said, only to the unity of apperception, and is for this reason the ground of the possibility of à priori cognition, in so far as this cognition is dependent on the understanding. This synthesis is, therefore, not merely transcendental, but also purely intellectual. But because a certain form of sensuous intuition exists in the mind à priori which rests on the receptivity of the representative faculty (sensibility), the understanding, as a spontaneity, is able to determine the internal sense by means of the diversity of given representations, conformably to the synthetical unity of apperception, and thus to cogitate the synthetical unity of the apperception of the manifold of sensuous intuition à priori, as the condition to which must necessarily be submitted all objects of human intuition. And in this manner the categories as mere forms of thought receive objective reality, that is, application to objects which are given to us in intuition, but that only as phenomena, for it is only of phenomena that we are capable of à priori intuition.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"38.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0002","text":"This synthesis of the manifold of sensuous intuition, which is possible and necessary à priori, may be called figurative (synthesis speciosa), in contradistinction to that which is cogitated in the mere category in regard to the manifold of an intuition in general, and is called connection or conjunction of the understanding (synthesis intellectualis). Both are transcendental, not merely because they themselves precede à priori all experience, but also because they form the basis for the possibility of other cognition à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"38.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","text":"But the figurative synthesis, when it has relation only to the originally synthetical unity of apperception, that is to the transcendental unity cogitated in the categories, must, to be distinguished from the purely intellectual conjunction, be entitled the transcendental synthesis of imagination. Imagination is the faculty of representing an object even without its presence in intuition. Now, as all our intuition is sensuous, imagination, by reason of the subjective condition under which alone it can give a corresponding intuition to the conceptions of the understanding, belongs to sensibility. But in so far as the synthesis of the imagination is an act of spontaneity, which is determinative, and not, like sense, merely determinable, and which is consequently able to determine sense à priori, according to its form, conformably to the unity of apperception, in so far is the imagination a faculty of determining sensibility à priori, and its synthesis of intuitions according to the categories must be the transcendental synthesis of the imagination. It is an operation of the understanding on sensibility, and the first application of the understanding to objects of possible intuition, and at the same time the basis for the exercise of the other functions of that faculty. As figurative, it is distinguished from the merely intellectual synthesis, which is produced by the understanding alone, without the aid of imagination. Now, in so far as imagination is spontaneity, I sometimes call it also the productive imagination, and distinguish it from the reproductive, the synthesis of which is subject entirely to empirical laws, those of association, namely, and which, therefore, contributes nothing to the explanation of the possibility of à priori cognition, and for this reason belongs not to transcendental philosophy, but to psychology.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"38.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0004","text":"We have now arrived at the proper place for explaining the paradox which must have struck every one in our exposition of the internal sense (§ 6), namely—how this sense represents us to our own consciousness, only as we appear to ourselves, not as we are in ourselves, because, to wit, we intuite ourselves only as we are inwardly affected. Now this appears to be contradictory, inasmuch as we thus stand in a passive relation to ourselves; and therefore in the systems of psychology, the internal sense is commonly held to be one with the faculty of apperception, while we, on the contrary, carefully distinguish them.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"38.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","text":"That which determines the internal sense is the understanding, and its original power of conjoining the manifold of intuition, that is, of bringing this under an apperception (upon which rests the possibility of the understanding itself). Now, as the human understanding is not in itself a faculty of intuition, and is unable to exercise such a power, in order to conjoin, as it were, the manifold of its own intuition, the synthesis of understanding is, considered per se, nothing but the unity of action, of which, as such, it is self-conscious, even apart from sensibility, by which, moreover, it is able to determine our internal sense in respect of the manifold which may be presented to it according to the form of sensuous intuition. Thus, under the name of a transcendental synthesis of imagination, the understanding exercises an activity upon the passive subject, whose faculty it is; and so we are right in saying that the internal sense is affected thereby. Apperception and its synthetical unity are by no means one and the same with the internal sense. The former, as the source of all our synthetical conjunction, applies, under the name of the categories, to the manifold of intuition in general, prior to all sensuous intuition of objects. The internal sense, on the contrary, contains merely the form of intuition, but without any synthetical conjunction of the manifold therein, and consequently does not contain any determined intuition, which is possible only through consciousness of the determination of the manifold by the transcendental act of the imagination (synthetical influence of the understanding on the internal sense), which I have named figurative synthesis.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"38.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","text":"This we can indeed always perceive in ourselves. We cannot cogitate a geometrical line without drawing it in thought, nor a circle without describing it, nor represent the three dimensions of space without drawing three lines from the same point perpendicular to one another. We cannot even cogitate time, unless, in drawing a straight line (which is to serve as the external figurative representation of time), we fix our attention on the act of the synthesis of the manifold, whereby we determine successively the internal sense, and thus attend also to the succession of this determination. Motion as an act of the subject (not as a determination of an object),[[20]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-20) consequently the synthesis of the manifold in space, if we make abstraction of space and attend merely to the act by which we determine the internal sense according to its form, is that which produces the conception of succession. The understanding, therefore, does by no means find in the internal sense any such synthesis of the manifold, but produces it, in that it affects this sense. At the same time, how “I who think” is distinct from the “i” which intuites itself (other modes of intuition being cogitable as at least possible), and yet one and the same with this latter as the same subject; how, therefore, I am able to say: “I, as an intelligence and thinking subject, cognize myself as an object thought, so far as I am, moreover, given to myself in intuition—only, like other phenomena, not as I am in myself, and as considered by the understanding, but merely as I appear”—is a question that has in it neither more nor less difficulty than the question—“How can I be an object to myself?” or this—“How I can be an object of my own intuition and internal perceptions?” But that such must be the fact, if we admit that space is merely a pure form of the phenomena of external sense, can be clearly proved by the consideration that we cannot represent time, which is not an object of external intuition, in any other way than under the image of a line, which we draw in thought, a mode of representation without which we could not cognize the unity of its dimension, and also that we are necessitated to take our determination of periods of time, or of points of time, for all our internal perceptions from the changes which we perceive in outward things. It follows that we must arrange the determinations of the internal sense, as phenomena in time, exactly in the same manner as we arrange those of the external senses in space. And consequently, if we grant, respecting this latter, that by means of them we know objects only in so far as we are affected externally, we must also confess, with regard to the internal sense, that by means of it we intuite ourselves only as we are internally affected by ourselves; in other words, as regards internal intuition, we cognize our own subject only as phenomenon, and not as it is in itself.[[21]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-21)","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"38.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0007","text":"[[20]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-20) Motion of an object in space does not belong to a pure science, consequently not to geometry; because, that a thing is movable cannot be known à priori, but only from experience. But motion, considered as the description of a space, is a pure act of the successive synthesis of the manifold in external intuition by means of productive imagination, and belongs not only to geometry, but even to transcendental philosophy.\n\n[[21]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-21) I do not see why so much difficulty should be found in admitting that our internal sense is affected by ourselves. Every act of attention exemplifies it. In such an act the understanding determines the internal sense by the synthetical conjunction which it cogitates, conformably to the internal intuition which corresponds to the manifold in the synthesis of the understanding. How much the mind is usually affected thereby every one will be able to perceive in himself.\n\n§ 21","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"38.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","text":"On the other hand, in the transcendental synthesis of the manifold content of representations, consequently in the synthetical unity of apperception, I am conscious of myself, not as I appear to myself, nor as I am in myself, but only that “I am.” This representation is a thought, not an intuition. Now, as in order to cognize ourselves, in addition to the act of thinking, which subjects the manifold of every possible intuition to the unity of apperception, there is necessary a determinate mode of intuition, whereby this manifold is given; although my own existence is certainly not mere phenomenon (much less mere illusion), the determination of my existence[[22]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-22) Can only take place conformably to the form of the internal sense, according to the particular mode in which the manifold which I conjoin is given in internal intuition, and I have therefore no knowledge of myself as I am, but merely as I appear to myself. The consciousness of self is thus very far from a knowledge of self, in which I do not use the categories, whereby I cogitate an object, by means of the conjunction of the manifold in one apperception. In the same way as I require, for the sake of the cognition of an object distinct from myself, not only the thought of an object in general (in the category), but also an intuition by which to determine that general conception, in the same way do I require, in order to the cognition of myself, not only the consciousness of myself or the thought that I think myself, but in addition an intuition of the manifold in myself, by which to determine this thought. It is true that I exist as an intelligence which is conscious only of its faculty of conjunction or synthesis, but subjected in relation to the manifold which this intelligence has to conjoin to a limitative conjunction called the internal sense. My intelligence (that is, I) can render that conjunction or synthesis perceptible only according to the relations of time, which are quite beyond the proper sphere of the conceptions of the understanding and consequently cognize itself in respect to an intuition (which cannot possibly be intellectual, nor given by the understanding), only as it appears to itself, and not as it would cognize itself, if its intuition were intellectual.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"38.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0009","text":"[[22]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-22) The “I think” expresses the act of determining my own existence. My existence is thus already given by the act of consciousness; but the mode in which I must determine my existence, that is, the mode in which I must place the manifold belonging to my existence, is not thereby given. For this purpose intuition of self is required, and this intuition possesses a form given à priori, namely, time, which is sensuous, and belongs to our receptivity of the determinable. Now, as I do not possess another intuition of self which gives the determining in me (of the spontaneity of which I am conscious), prior to the act of determination, in the same manner as time gives the determinable, it is clear that I am unable to determine my own existence as that of a spontaneous being, but I am only able to represent to myself the spontaneity of my thought, that is, of my determination, and my existence remains ever determinable in a purely sensuous manner, that is to say, like the existence of a phenomenon. But it is because of this spontaneity that I call myself an intelligence.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"38.9"}],"sha256":"ab25c680140b62917152e6fe20f905de1a45c7bce84f118af7e3183dd3ffde84","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP","title":"Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22","order":39,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_TDUP","text":"### Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22\n\nIn the metaphysical deduction, the à priori origin of categories was proved by their complete accordance with the general logical of thought; in the transcendental deduction was exhibited the possibility of the categories as à priori cognitions of objects of an intuition in general (§ 16 and 17).At present we are about to explain the possibility of cognizing, à priori, by means of the categories, all objects which can possibly be presented to our senses, not, indeed, according to the form of their intuition, but according to the laws of their conjunction or synthesis, and thus, as it were, of prescribing laws to nature and even of rendering nature possible. For if the categories were inadequate to this task, it would not be evident to us why everything that is presented to our senses must be subject to those laws which have an à priori origin in the understanding itself.\n\nI premise that by the term synthesis of apprehension I understand the combination of the manifold in an empirical intuition, whereby perception, that is, empirical consciousness of the intuition (as phenomenon), is possible.\n\nWe have à priori forms of the external and internal sensuous intuition in the representations of space and time, and to these must the synthesis of apprehension of the manifold in a phenomenon be always comformable, because the synthesis itself can only take place according to these forms. But space and time are not merely forms of sensuous intuition, but intuitions themselves (which contain a manifold), and therefore contain à priori the determination of the unity of this manifold.[[23]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-23) (See the Transcendent Æsthetic.) Therefore is unity of the synthesis of the manifold without or within us, consequently also a conjunction to which all that is to be represented as determined in space or time must correspond, given à priori along with (not in) these intuitions, as the condition of the synthesis of all apprehension of them. But this synthetical unity can be no other than that of the conjunction of the manifold of a given intuition in general, in a primitive act of consciousness, according to the categories, but applied to our sensuous intuition. Consequently all synthesis, whereby alone is even perception possible, is subject to the categories. And, as experience is cognition by means of conjoined perceptions, the categories are conditions of the possibility of experience and are therefore valid à priori for all objects of experience.\n\n[[23]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-23) Space represented as an object (as geometry really requires it to be) contains more than the mere form of the intuition; namely, a combination of the manifold given according to the form of sensibility into a representation that can be intuited; so that the form of the intuition gives us merely the manifold, but the formal intuition gives unity of representation. In the æsthetic, I regarded this unity as belonging entirely to sensibility, for the purpose of indicating that it antecedes all conceptions, although it presupposes a synthesis which does not belong to sense, through which alone, however, all our conceptions of space and time are possible. For as by means of this unity alone (the understanding determining the sensibility) space and time are given as intuitions, it follows that the unity of this intuition à priori belongs to space and time, and not to the conception of the understanding (§ 20).\n\nWhen, then, for example, I make the empirical intuition of a house by apprehension of the manifold contained therein into a perception, the necessary unity of space and of my external sensuous intuition lies at the foundation of this act, and I, as it were, draw the form of the house conformably to this synthetical unity of the manifold in space. But this very synthetical unity remains, even when I abstract the form of space, and has its seat in the understanding, and is in fact the category of the synthesis of the homogeneous in an intuition; that is to say, the category of quantity, to which the aforesaid synthesis of apprehension, that is, the perception, must be completely conformable.[[24]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-24)\n\n[[24]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-24) In this manner it is proved, that the synthesis of apprehension, which is empirical, must necessarily be conformable to the synthesis of apperception, which is intellectual, and contained à priori in the category. It is one and the same spontaneity which at one time, under the name of imagination, at another under that of understanding, produces conjunction in the manifold of intuition.\n\nTo take another example, when I perceive the freezing of water, I apprehend two states (fluidity and solidity), which, as such, stand toward each other mutually in a relation of time. But in the time, which I place as an internal intuition, at the foundation of this phenomenon, I represent to myself synthetical unity of the manifold, without which the aforesaid relation could not be given in an intuition as determined (in regard to the succession of time). Now this synthetical unity, as the à priori condition under which I conjoin the manifold of an intuition, is, if I make abstraction of the permanent form of my internal intuition (that is to say, of time), the category of cause, by means of which, when applied to my sensibility, I determine everything that occurs according to relations of time. Consequently apprehension in such an event, and the event itself, as far as regards the possibility of its perception, stands under the conception of the relation of cause and effect: and so in all other cases.\n\nCategories are conceptions which prescribe laws à priori to phenomena, consequently to nature as the complex of all phenomena (natura materialiter spectata). And now the question arises—inasmuch as these categories are not derived from nature, and do not regulate themselves according to her as their model (for in that case they would be empirical)—how it is conceivable that nature must regulate herself according to them, in other words, how the categories can determine à priori the synthesis of the manifold of nature, and yet not derive their origin from her. The following is the solution of this enigma.\n\nIt is not in the least more difficult to conceive how the laws of the phenomena of nature must harmonize with the understanding and with its à priori form—that is, its faculty of conjoining the manifold—than it is to understand how the phenomena themselves must correspond with the à priori form of our sensuous intuition. For laws do not exist in the phenomena any more than the phenomena exist as things in themselves. Laws do not exist except by relation to the subject in which the phenomena inhere, in so far as it possesses understanding, just as phenomena have no existence except by relation to the same existing subject in so far as it has senses. To things as things in themselves, conformability to law must necessarily belong independently of an understanding to cognize them. But phenomena are only representations of things which are utterly unknown in respect to what they are in themselves. But as mere representations, they stand under no law of conjunction except that which the conjoining faculty prescribes. Now that which conjoins the manifold of sensuous intuition is imagination, a mental act to which understanding contributes unity of intellectual synthesis, and sensibility, manifoldness of apprehension. Now as all possible perception depends on the synthesis of apprehension, and this empirical synthesis itself on the transcendental, consequently on the categories, it is evident that all possible perceptions, and therefore everything that can attain to empirical consciousness, that is, all phenomena of nature, must, as regards their conjunction, be subject to the categories. And nature (considered merely as nature in general) is dependent on them, as the original ground of her necessary conformability to law (as natura formaliter spectata). But the pure faculty (of the understanding) of prescribing laws à priori to phenomena by means of mere categories, is not competent to enounce other or more laws than those on which a nature in general, as a conformability to law of phenomena of space and time, depends. Particular laws, inasmuch as they concern empirically determined phenomena, cannot be entirely deduced from pure laws, although they all stand under them. Experience must be superadded in order to know these particular laws; but in regard to experience in general, and everything that can be cognized as an object thereof, these à priori laws are our only rule and guide.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","text":"### Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22\n\nIn the metaphysical deduction, the à priori origin of categories was proved by their complete accordance with the general logical of thought; in the transcendental deduction was exhibited the possibility of the categories as à priori cognitions of objects of an intuition in general (§ 16 and 17).At present we are about to explain the possibility of cognizing, à priori, by means of the categories, all objects which can possibly be presented to our senses, not, indeed, according to the form of their intuition, but according to the laws of their conjunction or synthesis, and thus, as it were, of prescribing laws to nature and even of rendering nature possible. For if the categories were inadequate to this task, it would not be evident to us why everything that is presented to our senses must be subject to those laws which have an à priori origin in the understanding itself.\n\nI premise that by the term synthesis of apprehension I understand the combination of the manifold in an empirical intuition, whereby perception, that is, empirical consciousness of the intuition (as phenomenon), is possible.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"39.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","text":"We have à priori forms of the external and internal sensuous intuition in the representations of space and time, and to these must the synthesis of apprehension of the manifold in a phenomenon be always comformable, because the synthesis itself can only take place according to these forms. But space and time are not merely forms of sensuous intuition, but intuitions themselves (which contain a manifold), and therefore contain à priori the determination of the unity of this manifold.[[23]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-23) (See the Transcendent Æsthetic.) Therefore is unity of the synthesis of the manifold without or within us, consequently also a conjunction to which all that is to be represented as determined in space or time must correspond, given à priori along with (not in) these intuitions, as the condition of the synthesis of all apprehension of them. But this synthetical unity can be no other than that of the conjunction of the manifold of a given intuition in general, in a primitive act of consciousness, according to the categories, but applied to our sensuous intuition. Consequently all synthesis, whereby alone is even perception possible, is subject to the categories. And, as experience is cognition by means of conjoined perceptions, the categories are conditions of the possibility of experience and are therefore valid à priori for all objects of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"39.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0003","text":"[[23]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-23) Space represented as an object (as geometry really requires it to be) contains more than the mere form of the intuition; namely, a combination of the manifold given according to the form of sensibility into a representation that can be intuited; so that the form of the intuition gives us merely the manifold, but the formal intuition gives unity of representation. In the æsthetic, I regarded this unity as belonging entirely to sensibility, for the purpose of indicating that it antecedes all conceptions, although it presupposes a synthesis which does not belong to sense, through which alone, however, all our conceptions of space and time are possible. For as by means of this unity alone (the understanding determining the sensibility) space and time are given as intuitions, it follows that the unity of this intuition à priori belongs to space and time, and not to the conception of the understanding (§ 20).\n\nWhen, then, for example, I make the empirical intuition of a house by apprehension of the manifold contained therein into a perception, the necessary unity of space and of my external sensuous intuition lies at the foundation of this act, and I, as it were, draw the form of the house conformably to this synthetical unity of the manifold in space. But this very synthetical unity remains, even when I abstract the form of space, and has its seat in the understanding, and is in fact the category of the synthesis of the homogeneous in an intuition; that is to say, the category of quantity, to which the aforesaid synthesis of apprehension, that is, the perception, must be completely conformable.[[24]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-24)","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"39.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0004","text":"[[24]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-24) In this manner it is proved, that the synthesis of apprehension, which is empirical, must necessarily be conformable to the synthesis of apperception, which is intellectual, and contained à priori in the category. It is one and the same spontaneity which at one time, under the name of imagination, at another under that of understanding, produces conjunction in the manifold of intuition.\n\nTo take another example, when I perceive the freezing of water, I apprehend two states (fluidity and solidity), which, as such, stand toward each other mutually in a relation of time. But in the time, which I place as an internal intuition, at the foundation of this phenomenon, I represent to myself synthetical unity of the manifold, without which the aforesaid relation could not be given in an intuition as determined (in regard to the succession of time). Now this synthetical unity, as the à priori condition under which I conjoin the manifold of an intuition, is, if I make abstraction of the permanent form of my internal intuition (that is to say, of time), the category of cause, by means of which, when applied to my sensibility, I determine everything that occurs according to relations of time. Consequently apprehension in such an event, and the event itself, as far as regards the possibility of its perception, stands under the conception of the relation of cause and effect: and so in all other cases.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"39.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0005","text":"Categories are conceptions which prescribe laws à priori to phenomena, consequently to nature as the complex of all phenomena (natura materialiter spectata). And now the question arises—inasmuch as these categories are not derived from nature, and do not regulate themselves according to her as their model (for in that case they would be empirical)—how it is conceivable that nature must regulate herself according to them, in other words, how the categories can determine à priori the synthesis of the manifold of nature, and yet not derive their origin from her. The following is the solution of this enigma.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"39.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","text":"It is not in the least more difficult to conceive how the laws of the phenomena of nature must harmonize with the understanding and with its à priori form—that is, its faculty of conjoining the manifold—than it is to understand how the phenomena themselves must correspond with the à priori form of our sensuous intuition. For laws do not exist in the phenomena any more than the phenomena exist as things in themselves. Laws do not exist except by relation to the subject in which the phenomena inhere, in so far as it possesses understanding, just as phenomena have no existence except by relation to the same existing subject in so far as it has senses. To things as things in themselves, conformability to law must necessarily belong independently of an understanding to cognize them. But phenomena are only representations of things which are utterly unknown in respect to what they are in themselves. But as mere representations, they stand under no law of conjunction except that which the conjoining faculty prescribes. Now that which conjoins the manifold of sensuous intuition is imagination, a mental act to which understanding contributes unity of intellectual synthesis, and sensibility, manifoldness of apprehension. Now as all possible perception depends on the synthesis of apprehension, and this empirical synthesis itself on the transcendental, consequently on the categories, it is evident that all possible perceptions, and therefore everything that can attain to empirical consciousness, that is, all phenomena of nature, must, as regards their conjunction, be subject to the categories. And nature (considered merely as nature in general) is dependent on them, as the original ground of her necessary conformability to law (as natura formaliter spectata). But the pure faculty (of the understanding) of prescribing laws à priori to phenomena by means of mere categories, is not competent to enounce other or more laws than those on which a nature in general, as a conformability to law of phenomena of space and time, depends. Particular laws, inasmuch as they concern empirically determined phenomena, cannot be entirely deduced from pure laws, although they all stand under them. Experience must be superadded in order to know these particular laws; but in regard to experience in general, and everything that can be cognized as an object thereof, these à priori laws are our only rule and guide.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"39.6"}],"sha256":"ccb66f3c22b1ddbb1fad5d1a23a429259ecaf7b6bb252957a506044e1693557f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC","title":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23","order":40,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_RTDC","text":"### Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23\n\nWe cannot think any object except by means of the categories; we cannot cognize any thought except by means of intuitions corresponding to these conceptions. Now all our intuitions are sensuous, and our cognition, in so far as the object of it is given, is empirical. But empirical cognition is experience; consequently no à priori cognition is possible for us, except of objects of possible experience.[[25]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-25)\n\n[[25]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-25) Lest my readers should stumble at this assertion, and the conclusions that may be too rashly drawn from it, I must remind them that the categories in the act of thought are by no means limited by the conditions of our sensuous intuition, but have an unbounded sphere of action. It is only the cognition of the object of thought, the determining of the object, which requires intuition. In the absence of intuition, our thought of an object may still have true and useful consequences in regard to the exercise of reason by the subject. But as this exercise of reason is not always directed on the determination of the object, in other words, on cognition thereof, but also on the determination of the subject and its volition, I do not intend to treat of it in this place.\n\nBut this cognition, which is limited to objects of experience, is not for that reason derived entirely, from, experience, but—and this is asserted of the pure intuitions and the pure conceptions of the understanding—there are, unquestionably, elements of cognition, which exist in the mind à priori. Now there are only two ways in which a necessary harmony of experience with the conceptions of its objects can be cogitated. Either experience makes these conceptions possible, or the conceptions make experience possible. The former of these statements will not hold good with respect to the categories (nor in regard to pure sensuous intuition), for they are à priori conceptions, and therefore independent of experience. The assertion of an empirical origin would attribute to them a sort of generatio aequivoca. Consequently, nothing remains but to adopt the second alternative (which presents us with a system, as it were, of the epigenesis of pure reason), namely, that on the part of the understanding the categories do contain the grounds of the possibility of all experience. But with respect to the questions how they make experience possible, and what are the principles of the possibility thereof with which they present us in their application to phenomena, the following section on the transcendental exercise of the faculty of judgement will inform the reader.\n\nIt is quite possible that someone may propose a species of preformation-system of pure reason—a middle way between the two—to wit, that the categories are neither innate and first à priori principles of cognition, nor derived from experience, but are merely subjective aptitudes for thought implanted in us contemporaneously with our existence, which were so ordered and disposed by our Creator, that their exercise perfectly harmonizes with the laws of nature which regulate experience. Now, not to mention that with such an hypothesis it is impossible to say at what point we must stop in the employment of predetermined aptitudes, the fact that the categories would in this case entirely lose that character of necessity which is essentially involved in the very conception of them, is a conclusive objection to it. The conception of cause, for example, which expresses the necessity of an effect under a presupposed condition, would be false, if it rested only upon such an arbitrary subjective necessity of uniting certain empirical representations according to such a rule of relation. I could not then say—“The effect is connected with its cause in the object (that is, necessarily),” but only, “I am so constituted that I can think this representation as so connected, and not otherwise.” Now this is just what the sceptic wants. For in this case, all our knowledge, depending on the supposed objective validity of our judgement, is nothing but mere illusion; nor would there be wanting people who would deny any such subjective necessity in respect to themselves, though they must feel it. At all events, we could not dispute with any one on that which merely depends on the manner in which his subject is organized.\n\nShort view of the above Deduction.\n\nThe foregoing deduction is an exposition of the pure conceptions of the understanding (and with them of all theoretical à priori cognition), as principles of the possibility of experience, but of experience as the determination of all phenomena in space and time in general—of experience, finally, from the principle of the original synthetical unity of apperception, as the form of the understanding in relation to time and space as original forms of sensibility.\n\nI consider the division by paragraphs to be necessary only up to this point, because we had to treat of the elementary conceptions. As we now proceed to the exposition of the employment of these, I shall not designate the chapters in this manner any further.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","text":"### Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23\n\nWe cannot think any object except by means of the categories; we cannot cognize any thought except by means of intuitions corresponding to these conceptions. Now all our intuitions are sensuous, and our cognition, in so far as the object of it is given, is empirical. But empirical cognition is experience; consequently no à priori cognition is possible for us, except of objects of possible experience.[[25]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-25)\n\n[[25]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-25) Lest my readers should stumble at this assertion, and the conclusions that may be too rashly drawn from it, I must remind them that the categories in the act of thought are by no means limited by the conditions of our sensuous intuition, but have an unbounded sphere of action. It is only the cognition of the object of thought, the determining of the object, which requires intuition. In the absence of intuition, our thought of an object may still have true and useful consequences in regard to the exercise of reason by the subject. But as this exercise of reason is not always directed on the determination of the object, in other words, on cognition thereof, but also on the determination of the subject and its volition, I do not intend to treat of it in this place.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"40.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","text":"But this cognition, which is limited to objects of experience, is not for that reason derived entirely, from, experience, but—and this is asserted of the pure intuitions and the pure conceptions of the understanding—there are, unquestionably, elements of cognition, which exist in the mind à priori. Now there are only two ways in which a necessary harmony of experience with the conceptions of its objects can be cogitated. Either experience makes these conceptions possible, or the conceptions make experience possible. The former of these statements will not hold good with respect to the categories (nor in regard to pure sensuous intuition), for they are à priori conceptions, and therefore independent of experience. The assertion of an empirical origin would attribute to them a sort of generatio aequivoca. Consequently, nothing remains but to adopt the second alternative (which presents us with a system, as it were, of the epigenesis of pure reason), namely, that on the part of the understanding the categories do contain the grounds of the possibility of all experience. But with respect to the questions how they make experience possible, and what are the principles of the possibility thereof with which they present us in their application to phenomena, the following section on the transcendental exercise of the faculty of judgement will inform the reader.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"40.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","text":"It is quite possible that someone may propose a species of preformation-system of pure reason—a middle way between the two—to wit, that the categories are neither innate and first à priori principles of cognition, nor derived from experience, but are merely subjective aptitudes for thought implanted in us contemporaneously with our existence, which were so ordered and disposed by our Creator, that their exercise perfectly harmonizes with the laws of nature which regulate experience. Now, not to mention that with such an hypothesis it is impossible to say at what point we must stop in the employment of predetermined aptitudes, the fact that the categories would in this case entirely lose that character of necessity which is essentially involved in the very conception of them, is a conclusive objection to it. The conception of cause, for example, which expresses the necessity of an effect under a presupposed condition, would be false, if it rested only upon such an arbitrary subjective necessity of uniting certain empirical representations according to such a rule of relation. I could not then say—“The effect is connected with its cause in the object (that is, necessarily),” but only, “I am so constituted that I can think this representation as so connected, and not otherwise.” Now this is just what the sceptic wants. For in this case, all our knowledge, depending on the supposed objective validity of our judgement, is nothing but mere illusion; nor would there be wanting people who would deny any such subjective necessity in respect to themselves, though they must feel it. At all events, we could not dispute with any one on that which merely depends on the manner in which his subject is organized.\n\nShort view of the above Deduction.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"40.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","text":"The foregoing deduction is an exposition of the pure conceptions of the understanding (and with them of all theoretical à priori cognition), as principles of the possibility of experience, but of experience as the determination of all phenomena in space and time in general—of experience, finally, from the principle of the original synthetical unity of apperception, as the form of the understanding in relation to time and space as original forms of sensibility.\n\nI consider the division by paragraphs to be necessary only up to this point, because we had to treat of the elementary conceptions. As we now proceed to the exposition of the employment of these, I shall not designate the chapters in this manner any further.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"40.4"}],"sha256":"33f4ba0d1f6c76086fdf4b2714dadb0e72065e6845f9eb071ea9ea298541304d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP","title":"BOOK II. Analytic of Principles","order":41,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_BIAP","text":"# Book Two\n\n### BOOK II. Analytic of Principles\n\nGeneral logic is constructed upon a plan which coincides exactly with the division of the higher faculties of cognition. These are, understanding, judgement, and reason. This science, accordingly, treats in its analytic of conceptions, judgements, and conclusions in exact correspondence with the functions and order of those mental powers which we include generally under the generic denomination of understanding.\n\nAs this merely formal logic makes abstraction of all content of cognition, whether pure or empirical, and occupies itself with the mere form of thought (discursive cognition), it must contain in its analytic a canon for reason. For the form of reason has its law, which, without taking into consideration the particular nature of the cognition about which it is employed, can be discovered à priori, by the simple analysis of the action of reason into its momenta.\n\nTranscendental logic, limited as it is to a determinate content, that of pure à priori cognitions, to wit, cannot imitate general logic in this division. For it is evident that the transcendental employment of reason is not objectively valid, and therefore does not belong to the logic of truth (that is, to analytic), but as a logic of illusion, occupies a particular department in the scholastic system under the name of transcendental dialectic.\n\nUnderstanding and judgement accordingly possess in transcendental logic a canon of objectively valid, and therefore true exercise, and are comprehended in the analytical department of that logic. But reason, in her endeavours to arrive by à priori means at some true statement concerning objects and to extend cognition beyond the bounds of possible experience, is altogether dialectic, and her illusory assertions cannot be constructed into a canon such as an analytic ought to contain.\n\nAccordingly, the analytic of principles will be merely a canon for the faculty of judgement, for the instruction of this faculty in its application to phenomena of the pure conceptions of the understanding, which contain the necessary condition for the establishment of à priori laws. On this account, although the subject of the following chapters is the especial principles of understanding, I shall make use of the term Doctrine of the faculty of judgement, in order to define more particularly my present purpose.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","text":"# Book Two\n\n### BOOK II. Analytic of Principles\n\nGeneral logic is constructed upon a plan which coincides exactly with the division of the higher faculties of cognition. These are, understanding, judgement, and reason. This science, accordingly, treats in its analytic of conceptions, judgements, and conclusions in exact correspondence with the functions and order of those mental powers which we include generally under the generic denomination of understanding.\n\nAs this merely formal logic makes abstraction of all content of cognition, whether pure or empirical, and occupies itself with the mere form of thought (discursive cognition), it must contain in its analytic a canon for reason. For the form of reason has its law, which, without taking into consideration the particular nature of the cognition about which it is employed, can be discovered à priori, by the simple analysis of the action of reason into its momenta.\n\nTranscendental logic, limited as it is to a determinate content, that of pure à priori cognitions, to wit, cannot imitate general logic in this division. For it is evident that the transcendental employment of reason is not objectively valid, and therefore does not belong to the logic of truth (that is, to analytic), but as a logic of illusion, occupies a particular department in the scholastic system under the name of transcendental dialectic.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"41.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","text":"Understanding and judgement accordingly possess in transcendental logic a canon of objectively valid, and therefore true exercise, and are comprehended in the analytical department of that logic. But reason, in her endeavours to arrive by à priori means at some true statement concerning objects and to extend cognition beyond the bounds of possible experience, is altogether dialectic, and her illusory assertions cannot be constructed into a canon such as an analytic ought to contain.\n\nAccordingly, the analytic of principles will be merely a canon for the faculty of judgement, for the instruction of this faculty in its application to phenomena of the pure conceptions of the understanding, which contain the necessary condition for the establishment of à priori laws. On this account, although the subject of the following chapters is the especial principles of understanding, I shall make use of the term Doctrine of the faculty of judgement, in order to define more particularly my present purpose.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"41.2"}],"sha256":"daaf5f1aae5587fcdce2dc7a22c225139851e50c7483684f95a279ca362b3727","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ","title":"INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General","order":42,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_ITFJ","text":"### INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General\n\nIf understanding in general be defined as the faculty of laws or rules, the faculty of judgement may be termed the faculty of subsumption under these rules; that is, of distinguishing whether this or that does or does not stand under a given rule (casus datae legis). General logic contains no directions or precepts for the faculty of judgement, nor can it contain any such. For as it makes abstraction of all content of cognition, no duty is left for it, except that of exposing analytically the mere form of cognition in conceptions, judgements, and conclusions, and of thereby establishing formal rules for all exercise of the understanding. Now if this logic wished to give some general direction how we should subsume under these rules, that is, how we should distinguish whether this or that did or did not stand under them, this again could not be done otherwise than by means of a rule. But this rule, precisely because it is a rule, requires for itself direction from the faculty of judgement. Thus, it is evident that the understanding is capable of being instructed by rules, but that the judgement is a peculiar talent, which does not, and cannot require tuition, but only exercise. This faculty is therefore the specific quality of the so-called mother wit, the want of which no scholastic discipline can compensate.\n\nFor although education may furnish, and, as it were, engraft upon a limited understanding rules borrowed from other minds, yet the power of employing these rules correctly must belong to the pupil himself; and no rule which we can prescribe to him with this purpose is, in the absence or deficiency of this gift of nature, secure from misuse.[[26]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-26) A physician therefore, a judge or a statesman, may have in his head many admirable pathological, juridical, or political rules, in a degree that may enable him to be a profound teacher in his particular science, and yet in the application of these rules he may very possibly blunder—either because he is wanting in natural judgement (though not in understanding) and, whilst he can comprehend the general in abstracto, cannot distinguish whether a particular case in concreto ought to rank under the former; or because his faculty of judgement has not been sufficiently exercised by examples and real practice. Indeed, the grand and only use of examples, is to sharpen the judgement. For as regards the correctness and precision of the insight of the understanding, examples are commonly injurious rather than otherwise, because, as casus in terminis they seldom adequately fulfil the conditions of the rule. Besides, they often weaken the power of our understanding to apprehend rules or laws in their universality, independently of particular circumstances of experience; and hence, accustom us to employ them more as formulae than as principles. Examples are thus the go-cart of the judgement, which he who is naturally deficient in that faculty cannot afford to dispense with.\n\n[[26]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-26) Deficiency in judgement is properly that which is called stupidity; and for such a failing we know no remedy. A dull or narrow-minded person, to whom nothing is wanting but a proper degree of understanding, may be improved by tuition, even so far as to deserve the epithet of learned. But as such persons frequently labour under a deficiency in the faculty of judgement, it is not uncommon to find men extremely learned who in the application of their science betray a lamentable degree this irremediable want.\n\nBut although general logic cannot give directions to the faculty of judgement, the case is very different as regards transcendental logic, insomuch that it appears to be the especial duty of the latter to secure and direct, by means of determinate rules, the faculty of judgement in the employment of the pure understanding. For, as a doctrine, that is, as an endeavour to enlarge the sphere of the understanding in regard to pure à priori cognitions, philosophy is worse than useless, since from all the attempts hitherto made, little or no ground has been gained. But, as a critique, in order to guard against the mistakes of the faculty of judgement (lapsus judicii) in the employment of the few pure conceptions of the understanding which we possess, although its use is in this case purely negative, philosophy is called upon to apply all its acuteness and penetration.\n\nBut transcendental philosophy has this peculiarity, that besides indicating the rule, or rather the general condition for rules, which is given in the pure conception of the understanding, it can, at the same time, indicate à priori the case to which the rule must be applied. The cause of the superiority which, in this respect, transcendental philosophy possesses above all other sciences except mathematics, lies in this: it treats of conceptions which must relate à priori to their objects, whose objective validity consequently cannot be demonstrated à posteriori, and is, at the same time, under the obligation of presenting in general but sufficient tests, the conditions under which objects can be given in harmony with those conceptions; otherwise they would be mere logical forms, without content, and not pure conceptions of the understanding.\n\nOur transcendental doctrine of the faculty of judgement will contain two chapters. The first will treat of the sensuous condition under which alone pure conceptions of the understanding can be employed—that is, of the schematism of the pure understanding. The second will treat of those synthetical judgements which are derived à priori from pure conceptions of the understanding under those conditions, and which lie à priori at the foundation of all other cognitions, that is to say, it will treat of the principles of the pure understanding.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","text":"### INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General\n\nIf understanding in general be defined as the faculty of laws or rules, the faculty of judgement may be termed the faculty of subsumption under these rules; that is, of distinguishing whether this or that does or does not stand under a given rule (casus datae legis). General logic contains no directions or precepts for the faculty of judgement, nor can it contain any such. For as it makes abstraction of all content of cognition, no duty is left for it, except that of exposing analytically the mere form of cognition in conceptions, judgements, and conclusions, and of thereby establishing formal rules for all exercise of the understanding. Now if this logic wished to give some general direction how we should subsume under these rules, that is, how we should distinguish whether this or that did or did not stand under them, this again could not be done otherwise than by means of a rule. But this rule, precisely because it is a rule, requires for itself direction from the faculty of judgement. Thus, it is evident that the understanding is capable of being instructed by rules, but that the judgement is a peculiar talent, which does not, and cannot require tuition, but only exercise. This faculty is therefore the specific quality of the so-called mother wit, the want of which no scholastic discipline can compensate.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"42.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","text":"For although education may furnish, and, as it were, engraft upon a limited understanding rules borrowed from other minds, yet the power of employing these rules correctly must belong to the pupil himself; and no rule which we can prescribe to him with this purpose is, in the absence or deficiency of this gift of nature, secure from misuse.[[26]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-26) A physician therefore, a judge or a statesman, may have in his head many admirable pathological, juridical, or political rules, in a degree that may enable him to be a profound teacher in his particular science, and yet in the application of these rules he may very possibly blunder—either because he is wanting in natural judgement (though not in understanding) and, whilst he can comprehend the general in abstracto, cannot distinguish whether a particular case in concreto ought to rank under the former; or because his faculty of judgement has not been sufficiently exercised by examples and real practice. Indeed, the grand and only use of examples, is to sharpen the judgement. For as regards the correctness and precision of the insight of the understanding, examples are commonly injurious rather than otherwise, because, as casus in terminis they seldom adequately fulfil the conditions of the rule. Besides, they often weaken the power of our understanding to apprehend rules or laws in their universality, independently of particular circumstances of experience; and hence, accustom us to employ them more as formulae than as principles. Examples are thus the go-cart of the judgement, which he who is naturally deficient in that faculty cannot afford to dispense with.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"42.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","text":"[[26]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-26) Deficiency in judgement is properly that which is called stupidity; and for such a failing we know no remedy. A dull or narrow-minded person, to whom nothing is wanting but a proper degree of understanding, may be improved by tuition, even so far as to deserve the epithet of learned. But as such persons frequently labour under a deficiency in the faculty of judgement, it is not uncommon to find men extremely learned who in the application of their science betray a lamentable degree this irremediable want.\n\nBut although general logic cannot give directions to the faculty of judgement, the case is very different as regards transcendental logic, insomuch that it appears to be the especial duty of the latter to secure and direct, by means of determinate rules, the faculty of judgement in the employment of the pure understanding. For, as a doctrine, that is, as an endeavour to enlarge the sphere of the understanding in regard to pure à priori cognitions, philosophy is worse than useless, since from all the attempts hitherto made, little or no ground has been gained. But, as a critique, in order to guard against the mistakes of the faculty of judgement (lapsus judicii) in the employment of the few pure conceptions of the understanding which we possess, although its use is in this case purely negative, philosophy is called upon to apply all its acuteness and penetration.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"42.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","text":"But transcendental philosophy has this peculiarity, that besides indicating the rule, or rather the general condition for rules, which is given in the pure conception of the understanding, it can, at the same time, indicate à priori the case to which the rule must be applied. The cause of the superiority which, in this respect, transcendental philosophy possesses above all other sciences except mathematics, lies in this: it treats of conceptions which must relate à priori to their objects, whose objective validity consequently cannot be demonstrated à posteriori, and is, at the same time, under the obligation of presenting in general but sufficient tests, the conditions under which objects can be given in harmony with those conceptions; otherwise they would be mere logical forms, without content, and not pure conceptions of the understanding.\n\nOur transcendental doctrine of the faculty of judgement will contain two chapters. The first will treat of the sensuous condition under which alone pure conceptions of the understanding can be employed—that is, of the schematism of the pure understanding. The second will treat of those synthetical judgements which are derived à priori from pure conceptions of the understanding under those conditions, and which lie à priori at the foundation of all other cognitions, that is to say, it will treat of the principles of the pure understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"42.4"}],"sha256":"8ce5ae07a72fba9439042421b56309aaa461031fe0a9c5b9c337691f2a6b977a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP","title":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding","order":43,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_CISP","text":"### TRANSCENDENTAL DOCTRINE OF THE FACULTY OF JUDGEMENT OR, ANALYTIC OF PRINCIPLES\n\n### Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\nIn all subsumptions of an object under a conception, the representation of the object must be homogeneous with the conception; in other words, the conception must contain that which is represented in the object to be subsumed under it. For this is the meaning of the expression: “An object is contained under a conception.” Thus the empirical conception of a plate is homogeneous with the pure geometrical conception of a circle, inasmuch as the roundness which is cogitated in the former is intuited in the latter.\n\nBut pure conceptions of the understanding, when compared with empirical intuitions, or even with sensuous intuitions in general, are quite heterogeneous, and never can be discovered in any intuition. How then is the subsumption of the latter under the former, and consequently the application of the categories to phenomena, possible?—For it is impossible to say, for example: “Causality can be intuited through the senses and is contained in the phenomenon.”—This natural and important question forms the real cause of the necessity of a transcendental doctrine of the faculty of judgement, with the purpose, to wit, of showing how pure conceptions of the understanding can be applied to phenomena. In all other sciences, where the conceptions by which the object is thought in the general are not so different and heterogeneous from those which represent the object in concreto—as it is given, it is quite unnecessary to institute any special inquiries concerning the application of the former to the latter.\n\nNow it is quite clear that there must be some third thing, which on the one side is homogeneous with the category, and with the phenomenon on the other, and so makes the application of the former to the latter possible. This mediating representation must be pure (without any empirical content), and yet must on the one side be intellectual, on the other sensuous. Such a representation is the transcendental schema.\n\nThe conception of the understanding contains pure synthetical unity of the manifold in general. Time, as the formal condition of the manifold of the internal sense, consequently of the conjunction of all representations, contains à priori a manifold in the pure intuition. Now a transcendental determination of time is so far homogeneous with the category, which constitutes the unity thereof, that it is universal and rests upon a rule à priori. On the other hand, it is so far homogeneous with the phenomenon, inasmuch as time is contained in every empirical representation of the manifold. Thus an application of the category to phenomena becomes possible, by means of the transcendental determination of time, which, as the schema of the conceptions of the understanding, mediates the subsumption of the latter under the former.\n\nAfter what has been proved in our deduction of the categories, no one, it is to be hoped, can hesitate as to the proper decision of the question, whether the employment of these pure conceptions of the understanding ought to be merely empirical or also transcendental; in other words, whether the categories, as conditions of a possible experience, relate à priori solely to phenomena, or whether, as conditions of the possibility of things in general, their application can be extended to objects as things in themselves. For we have there seen that conceptions are quite impossible, and utterly without signification, unless either to them, or at least to the elements of which they consist, an object be given; and that, consequently, they cannot possibly apply to objects as things in themselves without regard to the question whether and how these may be given to us; and, further, that the only manner in which objects can be given to us is by means of the modification of our sensibility; and, finally, that pure à priori conceptions, in addition to the function of the understanding in the category, must contain à priori formal conditions of sensibility (of the internal sense, namely), which again contain the general condition under which alone the category can be applied to any object. This formal and pure condition of sensibility, to which the conception of the understanding is restricted in its employment, we shall name the schema of the conception of the understanding, and the procedure of the understanding with these schemata we shall call the schematism of the pure understanding.\n\nThe schema is, in itself, always a mere product of the imagination. But, as the synthesis of imagination has for its aim no single intuition, but merely unity in the determination of sensibility, the schema is clearly distinguishable from the image. Thus, if I place five points one after another.... this is an image of the number five. On the other hand, if I only think a number in general, which may be either five or a hundred, this thought is rather the representation of a method of representing in an image a sum (e.g., a thousand) in conformity with a conception, than the image itself, an image which I should find some little difficulty in reviewing, and comparing with the conception. Now this representation of a general procedure of the imagination to present its image to a conception, I call the schema of this conception.\n\nIn truth, it is not images of objects, but schemata, which lie at the foundation of our pure sensuous conceptions. No image could ever be adequate to our conception of a triangle in general. For the generalness of the conception it never could attain to, as this includes under itself all triangles, whether right-angled, acute-angled, etc., whilst the image would always be limited to a single part of this sphere. The schema of the triangle can exist nowhere else than in thought, and it indicates a rule of the synthesis of the imagination in regard to pure figures in space. Still less is an object of experience, or an image of the object, ever to the empirical conception. On the contrary, the conception always relates immediately to the schema of the imagination, as a rule for the determination of our intuition, in conformity with a certain general conception. The conception of a dog indicates a rule, according to which my imagination can delineate the figure of a four-footed animal in general, without being limited to any particular individual form which experience presents to me, or indeed to any possible image that I can represent to myself in concreto. This schematism of our understanding in regard to phenomena and their mere form, is an art, hidden in the depths of the human soul, whose true modes of action we shall only with difficulty discover and unveil. Thus much only can we say: “The image is a product of the empirical faculty of the productive imagination—the schema of sensuous conceptions (of figures in space, for example) is a product, and, as it were, a monogram of the pure imagination à priori, whereby and according to which images first become possible, which, however, can be connected with the conception only mediately by means of the schema which they indicate, and are in themselves never fully adequate to it.” On the other hand, the schema of a pure conception of the understanding is something that cannot be reduced into any image—it is nothing else than the pure synthesis expressed by the category, conformably, to a rule of unity according to conceptions. It is a transcendental product of the imagination, a product which concerns the determination of the internal sense, according to conditions of its form (time) in respect to all representations, in so far as these representations must be conjoined à priori in one conception, conformably to the unity of apperception.\n\nWithout entering upon a dry and tedious analysis of the essential requisites of transcendental schemata of the pure conceptions of the understanding, we shall rather proceed at once to give an explanation of them according to the order of the categories, and in connection therewith.\n\nFor the external sense the pure image of all quantities (quantorum) is space; the pure image of all objects of sense in general, is time. But the pure schema of quantity (quantitatis) as a conception of the understanding, is number, a representation which comprehends the successive addition of one to one (homogeneous quantities). Thus, number is nothing else than the unity of the synthesis of the manifold in a homogeneous intuition, by means of my generating time itself in my apprehension of the intuition.\n\nReality, in the pure conception of the understanding, is that which corresponds to a sensation in general; that, consequently, the conception of which indicates a being (in time). Negation is that the conception of which represents a not-being (in time). The opposition of these two consists therefore in the difference of one and the same time, as a time filled or a time empty. Now as time is only the form of intuition, consequently of objects as phenomena, that which in objects corresponds to sensation is the transcendental matter of all objects as things in themselves (Sachheit, reality). Now every sensation has a degree or quantity by which it can fill time, that is to say, the internal sense in respect of the representation of an object, more or less, until it vanishes into nothing (= 0 = negatio). Thus there is a relation and connection between reality and negation, or rather a transition from the former to the latter, which makes every reality representable to us as a quantum; and the schema of a reality as the quantity of something in so far as it fills time, is exactly this continuous and uniform generation of the reality in time, as we descend in time from the sensation which has a certain degree, down to the vanishing thereof, or gradually ascend from negation to the quantity thereof.\n\nThe schema of substance is the permanence of the real in time; that is, the representation of it as a substratum of the empirical determination of time; a substratum which therefore remains, whilst all else changes. (Time passes not, but in it passes the existence of the changeable. To time, therefore, which is itself unchangeable and permanent, corresponds that which in the phenomenon is unchangeable in existence, that is, substance, and it is only by it that the succession and coexistence of phenomena can be determined in regard to time.)\n\nThe schema of cause and of the causality of a thing is the real which, when posited, is always followed by something else. It consists, therefore, in the succession of the manifold, in so far as that succession is subjected to a rule.\n\nThe schema of community (reciprocity of action and reaction), or the reciprocal causality of substances in respect of their accidents, is the coexistence of the determinations of the one with those of the other, according to a general rule.\n\nThe schema of possibility is the accordance of the synthesis of different representations with the conditions of time in general (as, for example, opposites cannot exist together at the same time in the same thing, but only after each other), and is therefore the determination of the representation of a thing at any time.\n\nThe schema of reality is existence in a determined time.\n\nThe schema of necessity is the existence of an object in all time.\n\nIt is clear, from all this, that the schema of the category of quantity contains and represents the generation (synthesis) of time itself, in the successive apprehension of an object; the schema of quality the synthesis of sensation with the representation of time, or the filling up of time; the schema of relation the relation of perceptions to each other in all time (that is, according to a rule of the determination of time): and finally, the schema of modality and its categories, time itself, as the correlative of the determination of an object—whether it does belong to time, and how. The schemata, therefore, are nothing but à priori determinations of time according to rules, and these, in regard to all possible objects, following the arrangement of the categories, relate to the series in time, the content in time, the order in time, and finally, to the complex or totality in time.\n\nHence it is apparent that the schematism of the understanding, by means of the transcendental synthesis of the imagination, amounts to nothing else than the unity of the manifold of intuition in the internal sense, and thus indirectly to the unity of apperception, as a function corresponding to the internal sense (a receptivity). Thus, the schemata of the pure conceptions of the understanding are the true and only conditions whereby our understanding receives an application to objects, and consequently significance. Finally, therefore, the categories are only capable of empirical use, inasmuch as they serve merely to subject phenomena to the universal rules of synthesis, by means of an à priori necessary unity (on account of the necessary union of all consciousness in one original apperception); and so to render them susceptible of a complete connection in one experience. But within this whole of possible experience lie all our cognitions, and in the universal relation to this experience consists transcendental truth, which antecedes all empirical truth, and renders the latter possible.\n\nIt is, however, evident at first sight, that although the schemata of sensibility are the sole agents in realizing the categories, they do, nevertheless, also restrict them, that is, they limit the categories by conditions which lie beyond the sphere of understanding—namely, in sensibility. Hence the schema is properly only the phenomenon, or the sensuous conception of an object in harmony with the category. (Numerus est quantitas phaenomenon—sensatio realitas phaenomenon; constans et perdurabile rerum substantia phaenomenon—aeternitas, necessitas, phaenomena, etc.) Now, if we remove a restrictive condition, we thereby amplify, it appears, the formerly limited conception. In this way, the categories in their pure signification, free from all conditions of sensibility, ought to be valid of things as they are, and not, as the schemata represent them, merely as they appear; and consequently the categories must have a significance far more extended, and wholly independent of all schemata. In truth, there does always remain to the pure conceptions of the understanding, after abstracting every sensuous condition, a value and significance, which is, however, merely logical. But in this case, no object is given them, and therefore they have no meaning sufficient to afford us a conception of an object. The notion of substance, for example, if we leave out the sensuous determination of permanence, would mean nothing more than a something which can be cogitated as subject, without the possibility of becoming a predicate to anything else. Of this representation I can make nothing, inasmuch as it does not indicate to me what determinations the thing possesses which must thus be valid as premier subject. Consequently, the categories, without schemata are merely functions of the understanding for the production of conceptions, but do not represent any object. This significance they derive from sensibility, which at the same time realizes the understanding and restricts it.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","text":"### TRANSCENDENTAL DOCTRINE OF THE FACULTY OF JUDGEMENT OR, ANALYTIC OF PRINCIPLES\n\n### Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding\n\nIn all subsumptions of an object under a conception, the representation of the object must be homogeneous with the conception; in other words, the conception must contain that which is represented in the object to be subsumed under it. For this is the meaning of the expression: “An object is contained under a conception.” Thus the empirical conception of a plate is homogeneous with the pure geometrical conception of a circle, inasmuch as the roundness which is cogitated in the former is intuited in the latter.\n\nBut pure conceptions of the understanding, when compared with empirical intuitions, or even with sensuous intuitions in general, are quite heterogeneous, and never can be discovered in any intuition. How then is the subsumption of the latter under the former, and consequently the application of the categories to phenomena, possible?—For it is impossible to say, for example: “Causality can be intuited through the senses and is contained in the phenomenon.”—This natural and important question forms the real cause of the necessity of a transcendental doctrine of the faculty of judgement, with the purpose, to wit, of showing how pure conceptions of the understanding can be applied to phenomena. In all other sciences, where the conceptions by which the object is thought in the general are not so different and heterogeneous from those which represent the object in concreto—as it is given, it is quite unnecessary to institute any special inquiries concerning the application of the former to the latter.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"43.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","text":"Now it is quite clear that there must be some third thing, which on the one side is homogeneous with the category, and with the phenomenon on the other, and so makes the application of the former to the latter possible. This mediating representation must be pure (without any empirical content), and yet must on the one side be intellectual, on the other sensuous. Such a representation is the transcendental schema.\n\nThe conception of the understanding contains pure synthetical unity of the manifold in general. Time, as the formal condition of the manifold of the internal sense, consequently of the conjunction of all representations, contains à priori a manifold in the pure intuition. Now a transcendental determination of time is so far homogeneous with the category, which constitutes the unity thereof, that it is universal and rests upon a rule à priori. On the other hand, it is so far homogeneous with the phenomenon, inasmuch as time is contained in every empirical representation of the manifold. Thus an application of the category to phenomena becomes possible, by means of the transcendental determination of time, which, as the schema of the conceptions of the understanding, mediates the subsumption of the latter under the former.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"43.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0003","text":"After what has been proved in our deduction of the categories, no one, it is to be hoped, can hesitate as to the proper decision of the question, whether the employment of these pure conceptions of the understanding ought to be merely empirical or also transcendental; in other words, whether the categories, as conditions of a possible experience, relate à priori solely to phenomena, or whether, as conditions of the possibility of things in general, their application can be extended to objects as things in themselves. For we have there seen that conceptions are quite impossible, and utterly without signification, unless either to them, or at least to the elements of which they consist, an object be given; and that, consequently, they cannot possibly apply to objects as things in themselves without regard to the question whether and how these may be given to us; and, further, that the only manner in which objects can be given to us is by means of the modification of our sensibility; and, finally, that pure à priori conceptions, in addition to the function of the understanding in the category, must contain à priori formal conditions of sensibility (of the internal sense, namely), which again contain the general condition under which alone the category can be applied to any object. This formal and pure condition of sensibility, to which the conception of the understanding is restricted in its employment, we shall name the schema of the conception of the understanding, and the procedure of the understanding with these schemata we shall call the schematism of the pure understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"43.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0004","text":"The schema is, in itself, always a mere product of the imagination. But, as the synthesis of imagination has for its aim no single intuition, but merely unity in the determination of sensibility, the schema is clearly distinguishable from the image. Thus, if I place five points one after another.... this is an image of the number five. On the other hand, if I only think a number in general, which may be either five or a hundred, this thought is rather the representation of a method of representing in an image a sum (e.g., a thousand) in conformity with a conception, than the image itself, an image which I should find some little difficulty in reviewing, and comparing with the conception. Now this representation of a general procedure of the imagination to present its image to a conception, I call the schema of this conception.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"43.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","text":"In truth, it is not images of objects, but schemata, which lie at the foundation of our pure sensuous conceptions. No image could ever be adequate to our conception of a triangle in general. For the generalness of the conception it never could attain to, as this includes under itself all triangles, whether right-angled, acute-angled, etc., whilst the image would always be limited to a single part of this sphere. The schema of the triangle can exist nowhere else than in thought, and it indicates a rule of the synthesis of the imagination in regard to pure figures in space. Still less is an object of experience, or an image of the object, ever to the empirical conception. On the contrary, the conception always relates immediately to the schema of the imagination, as a rule for the determination of our intuition, in conformity with a certain general conception. The conception of a dog indicates a rule, according to which my imagination can delineate the figure of a four-footed animal in general, without being limited to any particular individual form which experience presents to me, or indeed to any possible image that I can represent to myself in concreto. This schematism of our understanding in regard to phenomena and their mere form, is an art, hidden in the depths of the human soul, whose true modes of action we shall only with difficulty discover and unveil. Thus much only can we say: “The image is a product of the empirical faculty of the productive imagination—the schema of sensuous conceptions (of figures in space, for example) is a product, and, as it were, a monogram of the pure imagination à priori, whereby and according to which images first become possible, which, however, can be connected with the conception only mediately by means of the schema which they indicate, and are in themselves never fully adequate to it.” On the other hand, the schema of a pure conception of the understanding is something that cannot be reduced into any image—it is nothing else than the pure synthesis expressed by the category, conformably, to a rule of unity according to conceptions. It is a transcendental product of the imagination, a product which concerns the determination of the internal sense, according to conditions of its form (time) in respect to all representations, in so far as these representations must be conjoined à priori in one conception, conformably to the unity of apperception.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"43.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0006","text":"Without entering upon a dry and tedious analysis of the essential requisites of transcendental schemata of the pure conceptions of the understanding, we shall rather proceed at once to give an explanation of them according to the order of the categories, and in connection therewith.\n\nFor the external sense the pure image of all quantities (quantorum) is space; the pure image of all objects of sense in general, is time. But the pure schema of quantity (quantitatis) as a conception of the understanding, is number, a representation which comprehends the successive addition of one to one (homogeneous quantities). Thus, number is nothing else than the unity of the synthesis of the manifold in a homogeneous intuition, by means of my generating time itself in my apprehension of the intuition.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"43.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0007","text":"Reality, in the pure conception of the understanding, is that which corresponds to a sensation in general; that, consequently, the conception of which indicates a being (in time). Negation is that the conception of which represents a not-being (in time). The opposition of these two consists therefore in the difference of one and the same time, as a time filled or a time empty. Now as time is only the form of intuition, consequently of objects as phenomena, that which in objects corresponds to sensation is the transcendental matter of all objects as things in themselves (Sachheit, reality). Now every sensation has a degree or quantity by which it can fill time, that is to say, the internal sense in respect of the representation of an object, more or less, until it vanishes into nothing (= 0 = negatio). Thus there is a relation and connection between reality and negation, or rather a transition from the former to the latter, which makes every reality representable to us as a quantum; and the schema of a reality as the quantity of something in so far as it fills time, is exactly this continuous and uniform generation of the reality in time, as we descend in time from the sensation which has a certain degree, down to the vanishing thereof, or gradually ascend from negation to the quantity thereof.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"43.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0008","text":"The schema of substance is the permanence of the real in time; that is, the representation of it as a substratum of the empirical determination of time; a substratum which therefore remains, whilst all else changes. (Time passes not, but in it passes the existence of the changeable. To time, therefore, which is itself unchangeable and permanent, corresponds that which in the phenomenon is unchangeable in existence, that is, substance, and it is only by it that the succession and coexistence of phenomena can be determined in regard to time.)\n\nThe schema of cause and of the causality of a thing is the real which, when posited, is always followed by something else. It consists, therefore, in the succession of the manifold, in so far as that succession is subjected to a rule.\n\nThe schema of community (reciprocity of action and reaction), or the reciprocal causality of substances in respect of their accidents, is the coexistence of the determinations of the one with those of the other, according to a general rule.\n\nThe schema of possibility is the accordance of the synthesis of different representations with the conditions of time in general (as, for example, opposites cannot exist together at the same time in the same thing, but only after each other), and is therefore the determination of the representation of a thing at any time.\n\nThe schema of reality is existence in a determined time.\n\nThe schema of necessity is the existence of an object in all time.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"43.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0009","text":"It is clear, from all this, that the schema of the category of quantity contains and represents the generation (synthesis) of time itself, in the successive apprehension of an object; the schema of quality the synthesis of sensation with the representation of time, or the filling up of time; the schema of relation the relation of perceptions to each other in all time (that is, according to a rule of the determination of time): and finally, the schema of modality and its categories, time itself, as the correlative of the determination of an object—whether it does belong to time, and how. The schemata, therefore, are nothing but à priori determinations of time according to rules, and these, in regard to all possible objects, following the arrangement of the categories, relate to the series in time, the content in time, the order in time, and finally, to the complex or totality in time.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"43.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","text":"Hence it is apparent that the schematism of the understanding, by means of the transcendental synthesis of the imagination, amounts to nothing else than the unity of the manifold of intuition in the internal sense, and thus indirectly to the unity of apperception, as a function corresponding to the internal sense (a receptivity). Thus, the schemata of the pure conceptions of the understanding are the true and only conditions whereby our understanding receives an application to objects, and consequently significance. Finally, therefore, the categories are only capable of empirical use, inasmuch as they serve merely to subject phenomena to the universal rules of synthesis, by means of an à priori necessary unity (on account of the necessary union of all consciousness in one original apperception); and so to render them susceptible of a complete connection in one experience. But within this whole of possible experience lie all our cognitions, and in the universal relation to this experience consists transcendental truth, which antecedes all empirical truth, and renders the latter possible.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"43.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","text":"It is, however, evident at first sight, that although the schemata of sensibility are the sole agents in realizing the categories, they do, nevertheless, also restrict them, that is, they limit the categories by conditions which lie beyond the sphere of understanding—namely, in sensibility. Hence the schema is properly only the phenomenon, or the sensuous conception of an object in harmony with the category. (Numerus est quantitas phaenomenon—sensatio realitas phaenomenon; constans et perdurabile rerum substantia phaenomenon—aeternitas, necessitas, phaenomena, etc.) Now, if we remove a restrictive condition, we thereby amplify, it appears, the formerly limited conception. In this way, the categories in their pure signification, free from all conditions of sensibility, ought to be valid of things as they are, and not, as the schemata represent them, merely as they appear; and consequently the categories must have a significance far more extended, and wholly independent of all schemata. In truth, there does always remain to the pure conceptions of the understanding, after abstracting every sensuous condition, a value and significance, which is, however, merely logical. But in this case, no object is given them, and therefore they have no meaning sufficient to afford us a conception of an object. The notion of substance, for example, if we leave out the sensuous determination of permanence, would mean nothing more than a something which can be cogitated as subject, without the possibility of becoming a predicate to anything else. Of this representation I can make nothing, inasmuch as it does not indicate to me what determinations the thing possesses which must thus be valid as premier subject. Consequently, the categories, without schemata are merely functions of the understanding for the production of conceptions, but do not represent any object. This significance they derive from sensibility, which at the same time realizes the understanding and restricts it.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"43.11"}],"sha256":"d143541b3e1840dcb76b994b416ef980b3660e74e08192b0ac9ffe94a1863311","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA","title":"Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding","order":44,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_CISA","text":"### Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding\n\nIn the foregoing chapter we have merely considered the general conditions under which alone the transcendental faculty of judgement is justified in using the pure conceptions of the understanding for synthetical judgements. Our duty at present is to exhibit in systematic connection those judgements which the understanding really produces à priori. For this purpose, our table of the categories will certainly afford us the natural and safe guidance. For it is precisely the categories whose application to possible experience must constitute all pure à priori cognition of the understanding; and the relation of which to sensibility will, on that very account, present us with a complete and systematic catalogue of all the transcendental principles of the use of the understanding.\n\nPrinciples à priori are so called, not merely because they contain in themselves the grounds of other judgements, but also because they themselves are not grounded in higher and more general cognitions. This peculiarity, however, does not raise them altogether above the need of a proof. For although there could be found no higher cognition, and therefore no objective proof, and although such a principle rather serves as the foundation for all cognition of the object, this by no means hinders us from drawing a proof from the subjective sources of the possibility of the cognition of an object. Such a proof is necessary, moreover, because without it the principle might be liable to the imputation of being a mere gratuitous assertion.\n\nIn the second place, we shall limit our investigations to those principles which relate to the categories. For as to the principles of transcendental æsthetic, according to which space and time are the conditions of the possibility of things as phenomena, as also the restriction of these principles, namely, that they cannot be applied to objects as things in themselves—these, of course, do not fall within the scope of our present inquiry. In like manner, the principles of mathematical science form no part of this system, because they are all drawn from intuition, and not from the pure conception of the understanding. The possibility of these principles, however, will necessarily be considered here, inasmuch as they are synthetical judgements à priori, not indeed for the purpose of proving their accuracy and apodeictic certainty, which is unnecessary, but merely to render conceivable and deduce the possibility of such evident à priori cognitions.\n\nBut we shall have also to speak of the principle of analytical judgements, in opposition to synthetical judgements, which is the proper subject of our inquiries, because this very opposition will free the theory of the latter from all ambiguity, and place it clearly before our eyes in its true nature.\n\nSYSTEM OF THE PRINCIPLES OF THE PURE UNDERSTANDING","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","text":"### Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding\n\nIn the foregoing chapter we have merely considered the general conditions under which alone the transcendental faculty of judgement is justified in using the pure conceptions of the understanding for synthetical judgements. Our duty at present is to exhibit in systematic connection those judgements which the understanding really produces à priori. For this purpose, our table of the categories will certainly afford us the natural and safe guidance. For it is precisely the categories whose application to possible experience must constitute all pure à priori cognition of the understanding; and the relation of which to sensibility will, on that very account, present us with a complete and systematic catalogue of all the transcendental principles of the use of the understanding.\n\nPrinciples à priori are so called, not merely because they contain in themselves the grounds of other judgements, but also because they themselves are not grounded in higher and more general cognitions. This peculiarity, however, does not raise them altogether above the need of a proof. For although there could be found no higher cognition, and therefore no objective proof, and although such a principle rather serves as the foundation for all cognition of the object, this by no means hinders us from drawing a proof from the subjective sources of the possibility of the cognition of an object. Such a proof is necessary, moreover, because without it the principle might be liable to the imputation of being a mere gratuitous assertion.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"44.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","text":"In the second place, we shall limit our investigations to those principles which relate to the categories. For as to the principles of transcendental æsthetic, according to which space and time are the conditions of the possibility of things as phenomena, as also the restriction of these principles, namely, that they cannot be applied to objects as things in themselves—these, of course, do not fall within the scope of our present inquiry. In like manner, the principles of mathematical science form no part of this system, because they are all drawn from intuition, and not from the pure conception of the understanding. The possibility of these principles, however, will necessarily be considered here, inasmuch as they are synthetical judgements à priori, not indeed for the purpose of proving their accuracy and apodeictic certainty, which is unnecessary, but merely to render conceivable and deduce the possibility of such evident à priori cognitions.\n\nBut we shall have also to speak of the principle of analytical judgements, in opposition to synthetical judgements, which is the proper subject of our inquiries, because this very opposition will free the theory of the latter from all ambiguity, and place it clearly before our eyes in its true nature.\n\nSYSTEM OF THE PRINCIPLES OF THE PURE UNDERSTANDING","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"44.2"}],"sha256":"9beba086049a77d02d471a4bbd907e461b7df3db16bd0c328cf61ea9e1d3e189","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP","title":"Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements","order":45,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SISP","text":"### Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements\n\nWhatever may be the content of our cognition, and in whatever manner our cognition may be related to its object, the universal, although only negative conditions of all our judgements is that they do not contradict themselves; otherwise these judgements are in themselves (even without respect to the object) nothing. But although there may exist no contradiction in our judgement, it may nevertheless connect conceptions in such a manner that they do not correspond to the object, or without any grounds either à priori or à posteriori for arriving at such a judgement, and thus, without being self-contradictory, a judgement may nevertheless be either false or groundless.\n\nNow, the proposition: “No subject can have a predicate that contradicts it,” is called the principle of contradiction, and is a universal but purely negative criterion of all truth. But it belongs to logic alone, because it is valid of cognitions, merely as cognitions and without respect to their content, and declares that the contradiction entirely nullifies them. We can also, however, make a positive use of this principle, that is, not merely to banish falsehood and error (in so far as it rests upon contradiction), but also for the cognition of truth. For if the judgement is analytical, be it affirmative or negative, its truth must always be recognizable by means of the principle of contradiction. For the contrary of that which lies and is cogitated as conception in the cognition of the object will be always properly negatived, but the conception itself must always be affirmed of the object, inasmuch as the contrary thereof would be in contradiction to the object.\n\nWe must therefore hold the principle of contradiction to be the universal and fully sufficient Principle of all analytical cognition. But as a sufficient criterion of truth, it has no further utility or authority. For the fact that no cognition can be at variance with this principle without nullifying itself, constitutes this principle the sine qua non, but not the determining ground of the truth of our cognition. As our business at present is properly with the synthetical part of our knowledge only, we shall always be on our guard not to transgress this inviolable principle; but at the same time not to expect from it any direct assistance in the establishment of the truth of any synthetical proposition.\n\nThere exists, however, a formula of this celebrated principle—a principle merely formal and entirely without content—which contains a synthesis that has been inadvertently and quite unnecessarily mixed up with it. It is this: “It is impossible for a thing to be and not to be at the same time.” Not to mention the superfluousness of the addition of the word impossible to indicate the apodeictic certainty, which ought to be self-evident from the proposition itself, the proposition is affected by the condition of time, and as it were says: “A thing = A, which is something = B, cannot at the same time be non-B.” But both, B as well as non-B, may quite well exist in succession. For example, a man who is young cannot at the same time be old; but the same man can very well be at one time young, and at another not young, that is, old. Now the principle of contradiction as a merely logical proposition must not by any means limit its application merely to relations of time, and consequently a formula like the preceding is quite foreign to its true purpose. The misunderstanding arises in this way. We first of all separate a predicate of a thing from the conception of the thing, and afterwards connect with this predicate its opposite, and hence do not establish any contradiction with the subject, but only with its predicate, which has been conjoined with the subject synthetically—a contradiction, moreover, which obtains only when the first and second predicate are affirmed in the same time. If I say: “A man who is ignorant is not learned,” the condition “at the same time” must be added, for he who is at one time ignorant, may at another be learned. But if I say: “No ignorant man is a learned man,” the proposition is analytical, because the characteristic ignorance is now a constituent part of the conception of the subject; and in this case the negative proposition is evident immediately from the proposition of contradiction, without the necessity of adding the condition “the same time.” This is the reason why I have altered the formula of this principle—an alteration which shows very clearly the nature of an analytical proposition.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","text":"### Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements\n\nWhatever may be the content of our cognition, and in whatever manner our cognition may be related to its object, the universal, although only negative conditions of all our judgements is that they do not contradict themselves; otherwise these judgements are in themselves (even without respect to the object) nothing. But although there may exist no contradiction in our judgement, it may nevertheless connect conceptions in such a manner that they do not correspond to the object, or without any grounds either à priori or à posteriori for arriving at such a judgement, and thus, without being self-contradictory, a judgement may nevertheless be either false or groundless.\n\nNow, the proposition: “No subject can have a predicate that contradicts it,” is called the principle of contradiction, and is a universal but purely negative criterion of all truth. But it belongs to logic alone, because it is valid of cognitions, merely as cognitions and without respect to their content, and declares that the contradiction entirely nullifies them. We can also, however, make a positive use of this principle, that is, not merely to banish falsehood and error (in so far as it rests upon contradiction), but also for the cognition of truth. For if the judgement is analytical, be it affirmative or negative, its truth must always be recognizable by means of the principle of contradiction. For the contrary of that which lies and is cogitated as conception in the cognition of the object will be always properly negatived, but the conception itself must always be affirmed of the object, inasmuch as the contrary thereof would be in contradiction to the object.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"45.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","text":"We must therefore hold the principle of contradiction to be the universal and fully sufficient Principle of all analytical cognition. But as a sufficient criterion of truth, it has no further utility or authority. For the fact that no cognition can be at variance with this principle without nullifying itself, constitutes this principle the sine qua non, but not the determining ground of the truth of our cognition. As our business at present is properly with the synthetical part of our knowledge only, we shall always be on our guard not to transgress this inviolable principle; but at the same time not to expect from it any direct assistance in the establishment of the truth of any synthetical proposition.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"45.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","text":"There exists, however, a formula of this celebrated principle—a principle merely formal and entirely without content—which contains a synthesis that has been inadvertently and quite unnecessarily mixed up with it. It is this: “It is impossible for a thing to be and not to be at the same time.” Not to mention the superfluousness of the addition of the word impossible to indicate the apodeictic certainty, which ought to be self-evident from the proposition itself, the proposition is affected by the condition of time, and as it were says: “A thing = A, which is something = B, cannot at the same time be non-B.” But both, B as well as non-B, may quite well exist in succession. For example, a man who is young cannot at the same time be old; but the same man can very well be at one time young, and at another not young, that is, old. Now the principle of contradiction as a merely logical proposition must not by any means limit its application merely to relations of time, and consequently a formula like the preceding is quite foreign to its true purpose. The misunderstanding arises in this way. We first of all separate a predicate of a thing from the conception of the thing, and afterwards connect with this predicate its opposite, and hence do not establish any contradiction with the subject, but only with its predicate, which has been conjoined with the subject synthetically—a contradiction, moreover, which obtains only when the first and second predicate are affirmed in the same time. If I say: “A man who is ignorant is not learned,” the condition “at the same time” must be added, for he who is at one time ignorant, may at another be learned. But if I say: “No ignorant man is a learned man,” the proposition is analytical, because the characteristic ignorance is now a constituent part of the conception of the subject; and in this case the negative proposition is evident immediately from the proposition of contradiction, without the necessity of adding the condition “the same time.” This is the reason why I have altered the formula of this principle—an alteration which shows very clearly the nature of an analytical proposition.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"45.3"}],"sha256":"a72df754a4e1e7f6a2d25d215083e61a4851fb72ec2e4530ebbf9f9a55f7b10a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2","title":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements","order":46,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SI_2","text":"### Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements\n\nThe explanation of the possibility of synthetical judgements is a task with which general logic has nothing to do; indeed she needs not even be acquainted with its name. But in transcendental logic it is the most important matter to be dealt with—indeed the only one, if the question is of the possibility of synthetical judgements à priori, the conditions and extent of their validity. For when this question is fully decided, it can reach its aim with perfect ease, the determination, to wit, of the extent and limits of the pure understanding.\n\nIn an analytical judgement I do not go beyond the given conception, in order to arrive at some decision respecting it. If the judgement is affirmative, I predicate of the conception only that which was already cogitated in it; if negative, I merely exclude from the conception its contrary. But in synthetical judgements, I must go beyond the given conception, in order to cogitate, in relation with it, something quite different from that which was cogitated in it, a relation which is consequently never one either of identity or contradiction, and by means of which the truth or error of the judgement cannot be discerned merely from the judgement itself.\n\nGranted, then, that we must go out beyond a given conception, in order to compare it synthetically with another, a third thing is necessary, in which alone the synthesis of two conceptions can originate. Now what is this tertium quid that is to be the medium of all synthetical judgements? It is only a complex in which all our representations are contained, the internal sense to wit, and its form à priori, time.\n\nThe synthesis of our representations rests upon the imagination; their synthetical unity (which is requisite to a judgement), upon the unity of apperception. In this, therefore, is to be sought the possibility of synthetical judgements, and as all three contain the sources of à priori representations, the possibility of pure synthetical judgements also; nay, they are necessary upon these grounds, if we are to possess a knowledge of objects, which rests solely upon the synthesis of representations.\n\nIf a cognition is to have objective reality, that is, to relate to an object, and possess sense and meaning in respect to it, it is necessary that the object be given in some way or another. Without this, our conceptions are empty, and we may indeed have thought by means of them, but by such thinking we have not, in fact, cognized anything, we have merely played with representation. To give an object, if this expression be understood in the sense of “to present” the object, not mediately but immediately in intuition, means nothing else than to apply the representation of it to experience, be that experience real or only possible. Space and time themselves, pure as these conceptions are from all that is empirical, and certain as it is that they are represented fully à priori in the mind, would be completely without objective validity, and without sense and significance, if their necessary use in the objects of experience were not shown. Nay, the representation of them is a mere schema, that always relates to the reproductive imagination, which calls up the objects of experience, without which they have no meaning. And so it is with all conceptions without distinction.\n\nThe possibility of experience is, then, that which gives objective reality to all our à priori cognitions. Now experience depends upon the synthetical unity of phenomena, that is, upon a synthesis according to conceptions of the object of phenomena in general, a synthesis without which experience never could become knowledge, but would be merely a rhapsody of perceptions, never fitting together into any connected text, according to rules of a thoroughly united (possible) consciousness, and therefore never subjected to the transcendental and necessary unity of apperception. Experience has therefore for a foundation, à priori principles of its form, that is to say, general rules of unity in the synthesis of phenomena, the objective reality of which rules, as necessary conditions even of the possibility of experience can which rules, as necessary conditions—even of the possibility of experience—can always be shown in experience. But apart from this relation, à priori synthetical propositions are absolutely impossible, because they have no third term, that is, no pure object, in which the synthetical unity can exhibit the objective reality of its conceptions.\n\nAlthough, then, respecting space, or the forms which productive imagination describes therein, we do cognize much à priori in synthetical judgements, and are really in no need of experience for this purpose, such knowledge would nevertheless amount to nothing but a busy trifling with a mere chimera, were not space to be considered as the condition of the phenomena which constitute the material of external experience. Hence those pure synthetical judgements do relate, though but mediately, to possible experience, or rather to the possibility of experience, and upon that alone is founded the objective validity of their synthesis.\n\nWhile then, on the one hand, experience, as empirical synthesis, is the only possible mode of cognition which gives reality to all other synthesis; on the other hand, this latter synthesis, as cognition à priori, possesses truth, that is, accordance with its object, only in so far as it contains nothing more than what is necessary to the synthetical unity of experience.\n\nAccordingly, the supreme principle of all synthetical judgements is: “Every object is subject to the necessary conditions of the synthetical unity of the manifold of intuition in a possible experience.”\n\nÀ priori synthetical judgements are possible when we apply the formal conditions of the à priori intuition, the synthesis of the imagination, and the necessary unity of that synthesis in a transcendental apperception, to a possible cognition of experience, and say: “The conditions of the possibility of experience in general are at the same time conditions of the possibility of the objects of experience, and have, for that reason, objective validity in an à priori synthetical judgement.”","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","text":"### Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements\n\nThe explanation of the possibility of synthetical judgements is a task with which general logic has nothing to do; indeed she needs not even be acquainted with its name. But in transcendental logic it is the most important matter to be dealt with—indeed the only one, if the question is of the possibility of synthetical judgements à priori, the conditions and extent of their validity. For when this question is fully decided, it can reach its aim with perfect ease, the determination, to wit, of the extent and limits of the pure understanding.\n\nIn an analytical judgement I do not go beyond the given conception, in order to arrive at some decision respecting it. If the judgement is affirmative, I predicate of the conception only that which was already cogitated in it; if negative, I merely exclude from the conception its contrary. But in synthetical judgements, I must go beyond the given conception, in order to cogitate, in relation with it, something quite different from that which was cogitated in it, a relation which is consequently never one either of identity or contradiction, and by means of which the truth or error of the judgement cannot be discerned merely from the judgement itself.\n\nGranted, then, that we must go out beyond a given conception, in order to compare it synthetically with another, a third thing is necessary, in which alone the synthesis of two conceptions can originate. Now what is this tertium quid that is to be the medium of all synthetical judgements? It is only a complex in which all our representations are contained, the internal sense to wit, and its form à priori, time.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"46.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","text":"The synthesis of our representations rests upon the imagination; their synthetical unity (which is requisite to a judgement), upon the unity of apperception. In this, therefore, is to be sought the possibility of synthetical judgements, and as all three contain the sources of à priori representations, the possibility of pure synthetical judgements also; nay, they are necessary upon these grounds, if we are to possess a knowledge of objects, which rests solely upon the synthesis of representations.\n\nIf a cognition is to have objective reality, that is, to relate to an object, and possess sense and meaning in respect to it, it is necessary that the object be given in some way or another. Without this, our conceptions are empty, and we may indeed have thought by means of them, but by such thinking we have not, in fact, cognized anything, we have merely played with representation. To give an object, if this expression be understood in the sense of “to present” the object, not mediately but immediately in intuition, means nothing else than to apply the representation of it to experience, be that experience real or only possible. Space and time themselves, pure as these conceptions are from all that is empirical, and certain as it is that they are represented fully à priori in the mind, would be completely without objective validity, and without sense and significance, if their necessary use in the objects of experience were not shown. Nay, the representation of them is a mere schema, that always relates to the reproductive imagination, which calls up the objects of experience, without which they have no meaning. And so it is with all conceptions without distinction.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"46.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","text":"The possibility of experience is, then, that which gives objective reality to all our à priori cognitions. Now experience depends upon the synthetical unity of phenomena, that is, upon a synthesis according to conceptions of the object of phenomena in general, a synthesis without which experience never could become knowledge, but would be merely a rhapsody of perceptions, never fitting together into any connected text, according to rules of a thoroughly united (possible) consciousness, and therefore never subjected to the transcendental and necessary unity of apperception. Experience has therefore for a foundation, à priori principles of its form, that is to say, general rules of unity in the synthesis of phenomena, the objective reality of which rules, as necessary conditions even of the possibility of experience can which rules, as necessary conditions—even of the possibility of experience—can always be shown in experience. But apart from this relation, à priori synthetical propositions are absolutely impossible, because they have no third term, that is, no pure object, in which the synthetical unity can exhibit the objective reality of its conceptions.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"46.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","text":"Although, then, respecting space, or the forms which productive imagination describes therein, we do cognize much à priori in synthetical judgements, and are really in no need of experience for this purpose, such knowledge would nevertheless amount to nothing but a busy trifling with a mere chimera, were not space to be considered as the condition of the phenomena which constitute the material of external experience. Hence those pure synthetical judgements do relate, though but mediately, to possible experience, or rather to the possibility of experience, and upon that alone is founded the objective validity of their synthesis.\n\nWhile then, on the one hand, experience, as empirical synthesis, is the only possible mode of cognition which gives reality to all other synthesis; on the other hand, this latter synthesis, as cognition à priori, possesses truth, that is, accordance with its object, only in so far as it contains nothing more than what is necessary to the synthetical unity of experience.\n\nAccordingly, the supreme principle of all synthetical judgements is: “Every object is subject to the necessary conditions of the synthetical unity of the manifold of intuition in a possible experience.”\n\nÀ priori synthetical judgements are possible when we apply the formal conditions of the à priori intuition, the synthesis of the imagination, and the necessary unity of that synthesis in a transcendental apperception, to a possible cognition of experience, and say: “The conditions of the possibility of experience in general are at the same time conditions of the possibility of the objects of experience, and have, for that reason, objective validity in an à priori synthetical judgement.”","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"46.4"}],"sha256":"34e74a03848b8cb9ccb2b7b02cef1d0b8912093af51bf75b1a815fa23c089a4f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR","title":"Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding","order":47,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SISR","text":"### Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding\n\nThat principles exist at all is to be ascribed solely to the pure understanding, which is not only the faculty of rules in regard to that which happens, but is even the source of principles according to which everything that can be presented to us as an object is necessarily subject to rules, because without such rules we never could attain to cognition of an object. Even the laws of nature, if they are contemplated as principles of the empirical use of the understanding, possess also a characteristic of necessity, and we may therefore at least expect them to be determined upon grounds which are valid à priori and antecedent to all experience. But all laws of nature, without distinction, are subject to higher principles of the understanding, inasmuch as the former are merely applications of the latter to particular cases of experience. These higher principles alone therefore give the conception, which contains the necessary condition, and, as it were, the exponent of a rule; experience, on the other hand, gives the case which comes under the rule.\n\nThere is no danger of our mistaking merely empirical principles for principles of the pure understanding, or conversely; for the character of necessity, according to conceptions which distinguish the latter, and the absence of this in every empirical proposition, how extensively valid soever it may be, is a perfect safeguard against confounding them. There are, however, pure principles à priori, which nevertheless I should not ascribe to the pure understanding—for this reason, that they are not derived from pure conceptions, but (although by the mediation of the understanding) from pure intuitions. But understanding is the faculty of conceptions. Such principles mathematical science possesses, but their application to experience, consequently their objective validity, nay the possibility of such à priori synthetical cognitions (the deduction thereof) rests entirely upon the pure understanding.\n\nOn this account, I shall not reckon among my principles those of mathematics; though I shall include those upon the possibility and objective validity à priori, of principles of the mathematical science, which, consequently, are to be looked upon as the principle of these, and which proceed from conceptions to intuition, and not from intuition to conceptions.\n\nIn the application of the pure conceptions of the understanding to possible experience, the employment of their synthesis is either mathematical or dynamical, for it is directed partly on the intuition alone, partly on the existence of a phenomenon. But the à priori conditions of intuition are in relation to a possible experience absolutely necessary, those of the existence of objects of a possible empirical intuition are in themselves contingent. Hence the principles of the mathematical use of the categories will possess a character of absolute necessity, that is, will be apodeictic; those, on the other hand, of the dynamical use, the character of an à priori necessity indeed, but only under the condition of empirical thought in an experience, therefore only mediately and indirectly. Consequently they will not possess that immediate evidence which is peculiar to the former, although their application to experience does not, for that reason, lose its truth and certitude. But of this point we shall be better able to judge at the conclusion of this system of principles.\n\nThe table of the categories is naturally our guide to the table of principles, because these are nothing else than rules for the objective employment of the former. Accordingly, all principles of the pure understanding are:\n\n                                1\n                              Axioms\n                           of Intuition\n\n               2                                    3\n          Anticipations                          Analogies\n          of Perception                        of Experience\n                                4\n                          Postulates of\n                        Empirical Thought\n                           in general\n\nThese appellations I have chosen advisedly, in order that we might not lose sight of the distinctions in respect of the evidence and the employment of these principles. It will, however, soon appear that—a fact which concerns both the evidence of these principles, and the à priori determination of phenomena—according to the categories of quantity and quality (if we attend merely to the form of these), the principles of these categories are distinguishable from those of the two others, in as much as the former are possessed of an intuitive, but the latter of a merely discursive, though in both instances a complete, certitude. I shall therefore call the former mathematical, and the latter dynamical principles.[[27]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-27) It must be observed, however, that by these terms I mean just as little in the one case the principles of mathematics as those of general (physical) dynamics in the other. I have here in view merely the principles of the pure understanding, in their application to the internal sense (without distinction of the representations given therein), by means of which the sciences of mathematics and dynamics become possible. Accordingly, I have named these principles rather with reference to their application than their content; and I shall now proceed to consider them in the order in which they stand in the table.\n\n[[27]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-27) All combination (conjunctio) is either composition (compositio) or connection (nexus). The former is the synthesis of a manifold, the parts of which do not necessarily belong to each other. For example, the two triangles into which a square is divided by a diagonal, do not necessarily belong to each other, and of this kind is the synthesis of the homogeneous in everything that can be mathematically considered. This synthesis can be divided into those of aggregation and coalition, the former of which is applied to extensive, the latter to intensive quantities. The second sort of combination (nexus) is the synthesis of a manifold, in so far as its parts do belong necessarily to each other; for example, the accident to a substance, or the effect to the cause. Consequently it is a synthesis of that which though heterogeneous, is represented as connected à priori. This combination—not an arbitrary one—I entitle dynamical because it concerns the connection of the existence of the manifold. This, again, may be divided into the physical synthesis, of the phenomena divided among each other, and the metaphysical synthesis, or the connection of phenomena à priori in the faculty of cognition.\n\n1. AXIOMS OF INTUITION.\n\nThe principle of these is: All Intuitions are Extensive Quantities.\n\nPROOF.\n\nAll phenomena contain, as regards their form, an intuition in space and time, which lies à priori at the foundation of all without exception. Phenomena, therefore, cannot be apprehended, that is, received into empirical consciousness otherwise than through the synthesis of a manifold, through which the representations of a determinate space or time are generated; that is to say, through the composition of the homogeneous and the consciousness of the synthetical unity of this manifold (homogeneous). Now the consciousness of a homogeneous manifold in intuition, in so far as thereby the representation of an object is rendered possible, is the conception of a quantity (quanti). Consequently, even the perception of an object as phenomenon is possible only through the same synthetical unity of the manifold of the given sensuous intuition, through which the unity of the composition of the homogeneous manifold in the conception of a quantity is cogitated; that is to say, all phenomena are quantities, and extensive quantities, because as intuitions in space or time they must be represented by means of the same synthesis through which space and time themselves are determined.\n\nAn extensive quantity I call that wherein the representation of the parts renders possible (and therefore necessarily antecedes) the representation of the whole. I cannot represent to myself any line, however small, without drawing it in thought, that is, without generating from a point all its parts one after another, and in this way alone producing this intuition. Precisely the same is the case with every, even the smallest, portion of time. I cogitate therein only the successive progress from one moment to another, and hence, by means of the different portions of time and the addition of them, a determinate quantity of time is produced. As the pure intuition in all phenomena is either time or space, so is every phenomenon in its character of intuition an extensive quantity, inasmuch as it can only be cognized in our apprehension by successive synthesis (from part to part). All phenomena are, accordingly, to be considered as aggregates, that is, as a collection of previously given parts; which is not the case with every sort of quantities, but only with those which are represented and apprehended by us as extensive.\n\nOn this successive synthesis of the productive imagination, in the generation of figures, is founded the mathematics of extension, or geometry, with its axioms, which express the conditions of sensuous intuition à priori, under which alone the schema of a pure conception of external intuition can exist; for example, “be tween two points only one straight line is possible,” “two straight lines cannot enclose a space,” etc. These are the axioms which properly relate only to quantities (quanta) as such.\n\nBut, as regards the quantity of a thing (quantitas), that is to say, the answer to the question: “How large is this or that object?” although, in respect to this question, we have various propositions synthetical and immediately certain (indemonstrabilia); we have, in the proper sense of the term, no axioms. For example, the propositions: “If equals be added to equals, the wholes are equal”; “If equals be taken from equals, the remainders are equal”; are analytical, because I am immediately conscious of the identity of the production of the one quantity with the production of the other; whereas axioms must be à priori synthetical propositions. On the other hand, the self-evident propositions as to the relation of numbers, are certainly synthetical but not universal, like those of geometry, and for this reason cannot be called axioms, but numerical formulae. That 7 + 5 = 12 is not an analytical proposition. For neither in the representation of seven, nor of five, nor of the composition of the two numbers, do I cogitate the number twelve. (Whether I cogitate the number in the addition of both, is not at present the question; for in the case of an analytical proposition, the only point is whether I really cogitate the predicate in the representation of the subject.) But although the proposition is synthetical, it is nevertheless only a singular proposition. In so far as regard is here had merely to the synthesis of the homogeneous (the units), it cannot take place except in one manner, although our use of these numbers is afterwards general. If I say: “A triangle can be constructed with three lines, any two of which taken together are greater than the third,” I exercise merely the pure function of the productive imagination, which may draw the lines longer or shorter and construct the angles at its pleasure. On the contrary, the number seven is possible only in one manner, and so is likewise the number twelve, which results from the synthesis of seven and five. Such propositions, then, cannot be termed axioms (for in that case we should have an infinity of these), but numerical formulae.\n\nThis transcendental principle of the mathematics of phenomena greatly enlarges our à priori cognition. For it is by this principle alone that pure mathematics is rendered applicable in all its precision to objects of experience, and without it the validity of this application would not be so self-evident; on the contrary, contradictions and confusions have often arisen on this very point. Phenomena are not things in themselves. Empirical intuition is possible only through pure intuition (of space and time); consequently, what geometry affirms of the latter, is indisputably valid of the former. All evasions, such as the statement that objects of sense do not conform to the rules of construction in space (for example, to the rule of the infinite divisibility of lines or angles), must fall to the ground. For, if these objections hold good, we deny to space, and with it to all mathematics, objective validity, and no longer know wherefore, and how far, mathematics can be applied to phenomena. The synthesis of spaces and times as the essential form of all intuition, is that which renders possible the apprehension of a phenomenon, and therefore every external experience, consequently all cognition of the objects of experience; and whatever mathematics in its pure use proves of the former, must necessarily hold good of the latter. All objections are but the chicaneries of an ill-instructed reason, which erroneously thinks to liberate the objects of sense from the formal conditions of our sensibility, and represents these, although mere phenomena, as things in themselves, presented as such to our understanding. But in this case, no à priori synthetical cognition of them could be possible, consequently not through pure conceptions of space and the science which determines these conceptions, that is to say, geometry, would itself be impossible.\n\n2. ANTICIPATIONS OF PERCEPTION.\n\nThe principle of these is: In all phenomena the Real, that which is an object of sensation, has Intensive Quantity, that is, has a Degree.\n\nPROOF.\n\nPerception is empirical consciousness, that is to say, a consciousness which contains an element of sensation. Phenomena as objects of perception are not pure, that is, merely formal intuitions, like space and time, for they cannot be perceived in themselves.[[28]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-28) They contain, then, over and above the intuition, the materials for an object (through which is represented something existing in space or time), that is to say, they contain the real of sensation, as a representation merely subjective, which gives us merely the consciousness that the subject is affected, and which we refer to some external object. Now, a gradual transition from empirical consciousness to pure consciousness is possible, inasmuch as the real in this consciousness entirely vanishes, and there remains a merely formal consciousness (à priori) of the manifold in time and space; consequently there is possible a synthesis also of the production of the quantity of a sensation from its commencement, that is, from the pure intuition = 0 onwards up to a certain quantity of the sensation. Now as sensation in itself is not an objective representation, and in it is to be found neither the intuition of space nor of time, it cannot possess any extensive quantity, and yet there does belong to it a quantity (and that by means of its apprehension, in which empirical consciousness can within a certain time rise from nothing = 0 up to its given amount), consequently an intensive quantity. And thus we must ascribe intensive quantity, that is, a degree of influence on sense to all objects of perception, in so far as this perception contains sensation.\n\n[[28]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-28) They can be perceived only as phenomena, and some part of them must always belong to the non-ego; whereas pure intuitions are entirely the products of the mind itself, and as such are cognized _in themselves.—Tr_\n\nAll cognition, by means of which I am enabled to cognize and determine à priori what belongs to empirical cognition, may be called an anticipation; and without doubt this is the sense in which Epicurus employed his expression prholepsis. But as there is in phenomena something which is never cognized à priori, which on this account constitutes the proper difference between pure and empirical cognition, that is to say, sensation (as the matter of perception), it follows, that sensation is just that element in cognition which cannot be at all anticipated. On the other hand, we might very well term the pure determinations in space and time, as well in regard to figure as to quantity, anticipations of phenomena, because they represent à priori that which may always be given à posteriori in experience. But suppose that in every sensation, as sensation in general, without any particular sensation being thought of, there existed something which could be cognized à priori, this would deserve to be called anticipation in a special sense—special, because it may seem surprising to forestall experience, in that which concerns the matter of experience, and which we can only derive from itself. Yet such really is the case here.\n\nApprehension[[29]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-29), by means of sensation alone, fills only one moment, that is, if I do not take into consideration a succession of many sensations. As that in the phenomenon, the apprehension of which is not a successive synthesis advancing from parts to an entire representation, sensation has therefore no extensive quantity; the want of sensation in a moment of time would represent it as empty, consequently = 0. That which in the empirical intuition corresponds to sensation is reality (realitas phaenomenon); that which corresponds to the absence of it, negation = 0. Now every sensation is capable of a diminution, so that it can decrease, and thus gradually disappear. Therefore, between reality in a phenomenon and negation, there exists a continuous concatenation of many possible intermediate sensations, the difference of which from each other is always smaller than that between the given sensation and zero, or complete negation. That is to say, the real in a phenomenon has always a quantity, which however is not discoverable in apprehension, inasmuch as apprehension take place by means of mere sensation in one instant, and not by the successive synthesis of many sensations, and therefore does not progress from parts to the whole. Consequently, it has a quantity, but not an extensive quantity.\n\n[[29]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-29) Apprehension is the Kantian word for preception, in the largest sense in which we employ that term. It is the genus which includes under i, as species, perception proper and sensation proper—Tr\n\nNow that quantity which is apprehended only as unity, and in which plurality can be represented only by approximation to negation = 0, I term intensive quantity. Consequently, reality in a phenomenon has intensive quantity, that is, a degree. If we consider this reality as cause (be it of sensation or of another reality in the phenomenon, for example, a change), we call the degree of reality in its character of cause a momentum, for example, the momentum of weight; and for this reason, that the degree only indicates that quantity the apprehension of which is not successive, but instantaneous. This, however, I touch upon only in passing, for with causality I have at present nothing to do.\n\nAccordingly, every sensation, consequently every reality in phenomena, however small it may be, has a degree, that is, an intensive quantity, which may always be lessened, and between reality and negation there exists a continuous connection of possible realities, and possible smaller perceptions. Every colour—for example, red—has a degree, which, be it ever so small, is never the smallest, and so is it always with heat, the momentum of weight, etc.\n\nThis property of quantities, according to which no part of them is the smallest possible (no part simple), is called their continuity. Space and time are quanta continua, because no part of them can be given, without enclosing it within boundaries (points and moments), consequently, this given part is itself a space or a time. Space, therefore, consists only of spaces, and time of times. Points and moments are only boundaries, that is, the mere places or positions of their limitation. But places always presuppose intuitions which are to limit or determine them; and we cannot conceive either space or time composed of constituent parts which are given before space or time. Such quantities may also be called flowing, because synthesis (of the productive imagination) in the production of these quantities is a progression in time, the continuity of which we are accustomed to indicate by the expression flowing.\n\nAll phenomena, then, are continuous quantities, in respect both to intuition and mere perception (sensation, and with it reality). In the former case they are extensive quantities; in the latter, intensive. When the synthesis of the manifold of a phenomenon is interrupted, there results merely an aggregate of several phenomena, and not properly a phenomenon as a quantity, which is not produced by the mere continuation of the productive synthesis of a certain kind, but by the repetition of a synthesis always ceasing. For example, if I call thirteen dollars a sum or quantity of money, I employ the term quite correctly, inasmuch as I understand by thirteen dollars the value of a mark in standard silver, which is, to be sure, a continuous quantity, in which no part is the smallest, but every part might constitute a piece of money, which would contain material for still smaller pieces. If, however, by the words thirteen dollars I understand so many coins (be their value in silver what it may), it would be quite erroneous to use the expression a quantity of dollars; on the contrary, I must call them aggregate, that is, a number of coins. And as in every number we must have unity as the foundation, so a phenomenon taken as unity is a quantity, and as such always a continuous quantity (quantum continuum).\n\nNow, seeing all phenomena, whether considered as extensive or intensive, are continuous quantities, the proposition: “All change (transition of a thing from one state into another) is continuous,” might be proved here easily, and with mathematical evidence, were it not that the causality of a change lies, entirely beyond the bounds of a transcendental philosophy, and presupposes empirical principles. For of the possibility of a cause which changes the condition of things, that is, which determines them to the contrary to a certain given state, the understanding gives us à priori no knowledge; not merely because it has no insight into the possibility of it (for such insight is absent in several à priori cognitions), but because the notion of change concerns only certain determinations of phenomena, which experience alone can acquaint us with, while their cause lies in the unchangeable. But seeing that we have nothing which we could here employ but the pure fundamental conceptions of all possible experience, among which of course nothing empirical can be admitted, we dare not, without injuring the unity of our system, anticipate general physical science, which is built upon certain fundamental experiences.\n\nNevertheless, we are in no want of proofs of the great influence which the principle above developed exercises in the anticipation of perceptions, and even in supplying the want of them, so far as to shield us against the false conclusions which otherwise we might rashly draw.\n\nIf all reality in perception has a degree, between which and negation there is an endless sequence of ever smaller degrees, and if, nevertheless, every sense must have a determinate degree of receptivity for sensations; no perception, and consequently no experience is possible, which can prove, either immediately or mediately, an entire absence of all reality in a phenomenon; in other words, it is impossible ever to draw from experience a proof of the existence of empty space or of empty time. For in the first place, an entire absence of reality in a sensuous intuition cannot of course be an object of perception; secondly, such absence cannot be deduced from the contemplation of any single phenomenon, and the difference of the degrees in its reality; nor ought it ever to be admitted in explanation of any phenomenon. For if even the complete intuition of a determinate space or time is thoroughly real, that is, if no part thereof is empty, yet because every reality has its degree, which, with the extensive quantity of the phenomenon unchanged, can diminish through endless gradations down to nothing (the void), there must be infinitely graduated degrees, with which space or time is filled, and the intensive quantity in different phenomena may be smaller or greater, although the extensive quantity of the intuition remains equal and unaltered.\n\nWe shall give an example of this. Almost all natural philosophers, remarking a great difference in the quantity of the matter of different kinds in bodies with the same volume (partly on account of the momentum of gravity or weight, partly on account of the momentum of resistance to other bodies in motion), conclude unanimously that this volume (extensive quantity of the phenomenon) must be void in all bodies, although in different proportion. But who would suspect that these for the most part mathematical and mechanical inquirers into nature should ground this conclusion solely on a metaphysical hypothesis—a sort of hypothesis which they profess to disparage and avoid? Yet this they do, in assuming that the real in space (I must not here call it impenetrability or weight, because these are empirical conceptions) is always identical, and can only be distinguished according to its extensive quantity, that is, multiplicity. Now to this presupposition, for which they can have no ground in experience, and which consequently is merely metaphysical, I oppose a transcendental demonstration, which it is true will not explain the difference in the filling up of spaces, but which nevertheless completely does away with the supposed necessity of the above-mentioned presupposition that we cannot explain the said difference otherwise than by the hypothesis of empty spaces. This demonstration, moreover, has the merit of setting the understanding at liberty to conceive this distinction in a different manner, if the explanation of the fact requires any such hypothesis. For we perceive that although two equal spaces may be completely filled by matters altogether different, so that in neither of them is there left a single point wherein matter is not present, nevertheless, every reality has its degree (of resistance or of weight), which, without diminution of the extensive quantity, can become less and less ad infinitum, before it passes into nothingness and disappears. Thus an expansion which fills a space—for example, caloric, or any other reality in the phenomenal world—can decrease in its degrees to infinity, yet without leaving the smallest part of the space empty; on the contrary, filling it with those lesser degrees as completely as another phenomenon could with greater. My intention here is by no means to maintain that this is really the case with the difference of matters, in regard to their specific gravity; I wish only to prove, from a principle of the pure understanding, that the nature of our perceptions makes such a mode of explanation possible, and that it is erroneous to regard the real in a phenomenon as equal quoad its degree, and different only quoad its aggregation and extensive quantity, and this, too, on the pretended authority of an à priori principle of the understanding.\n\nNevertheless, this principle of the anticipation of perception must somewhat startle an inquirer whom initiation into transcendental philosophy has rendered cautious. We must naturally entertain some doubt whether or not the understanding can enounce any such synthetical proposition as that respecting the degree of all reality in phenomena, and consequently the possibility of the internal difference of sensation itself—abstraction being made of its empirical quality. Thus it is a question not unworthy of solution: “How the understanding can pronounce synthetically and à priori respecting phenomena, and thus anticipate these, even in that which is peculiarly and merely empirical, that, namely, which concerns sensation itself?”\n\nThe quality of sensation is in all cases merely empirical, and cannot be represented à priori (for example, colours, taste, etc.). But the real—that which corresponds to sensation—in opposition to negation = 0, only represents something the conception of which in itself contains a being (ein seyn), and signifies nothing but the synthesis in an empirical consciousness. That is to say, the empirical consciousness in the internal sense can be raised from 0 to every higher degree, so that the very same extensive quantity of intuition, an illuminated surface, for example, excites as great a sensation as an aggregate of many other surfaces less illuminated. We can therefore make complete abstraction of the extensive quantity of a phenomenon, and represent to ourselves in the mere sensation in a certain momentum, a synthesis of homogeneous ascension from 0 up to the given empirical consciousness, All sensations therefore as such are given only à posteriori, but this property thereof, namely, that they have a degree, can be known à priori. It is worthy of remark, that in respect to quantities in general, we can cognize à priori only a single quality, namely, continuity; but in respect to all quality (the real in phenomena), we cannot cognize à priori anything more than the intensive quantity thereof, namely, that they have a degree. All else is left to experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","text":"### Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding\n\nThat principles exist at all is to be ascribed solely to the pure understanding, which is not only the faculty of rules in regard to that which happens, but is even the source of principles according to which everything that can be presented to us as an object is necessarily subject to rules, because without such rules we never could attain to cognition of an object. Even the laws of nature, if they are contemplated as principles of the empirical use of the understanding, possess also a characteristic of necessity, and we may therefore at least expect them to be determined upon grounds which are valid à priori and antecedent to all experience. But all laws of nature, without distinction, are subject to higher principles of the understanding, inasmuch as the former are merely applications of the latter to particular cases of experience. These higher principles alone therefore give the conception, which contains the necessary condition, and, as it were, the exponent of a rule; experience, on the other hand, gives the case which comes under the rule.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"47.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0002","text":"There is no danger of our mistaking merely empirical principles for principles of the pure understanding, or conversely; for the character of necessity, according to conceptions which distinguish the latter, and the absence of this in every empirical proposition, how extensively valid soever it may be, is a perfect safeguard against confounding them. There are, however, pure principles à priori, which nevertheless I should not ascribe to the pure understanding—for this reason, that they are not derived from pure conceptions, but (although by the mediation of the understanding) from pure intuitions. But understanding is the faculty of conceptions. Such principles mathematical science possesses, but their application to experience, consequently their objective validity, nay the possibility of such à priori synthetical cognitions (the deduction thereof) rests entirely upon the pure understanding.\n\nOn this account, I shall not reckon among my principles those of mathematics; though I shall include those upon the possibility and objective validity à priori, of principles of the mathematical science, which, consequently, are to be looked upon as the principle of these, and which proceed from conceptions to intuition, and not from intuition to conceptions.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"47.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","text":"In the application of the pure conceptions of the understanding to possible experience, the employment of their synthesis is either mathematical or dynamical, for it is directed partly on the intuition alone, partly on the existence of a phenomenon. But the à priori conditions of intuition are in relation to a possible experience absolutely necessary, those of the existence of objects of a possible empirical intuition are in themselves contingent. Hence the principles of the mathematical use of the categories will possess a character of absolute necessity, that is, will be apodeictic; those, on the other hand, of the dynamical use, the character of an à priori necessity indeed, but only under the condition of empirical thought in an experience, therefore only mediately and indirectly. Consequently they will not possess that immediate evidence which is peculiar to the former, although their application to experience does not, for that reason, lose its truth and certitude. But of this point we shall be better able to judge at the conclusion of this system of principles.\n\nThe table of the categories is naturally our guide to the table of principles, because these are nothing else than rules for the objective employment of the former. Accordingly, all principles of the pure understanding are:\n\n                                1\n                              Axioms\n                           of Intuition\n\n               2                                    3\n          Anticipations                          Analogies\n          of Perception                        of Experience\n                                4\n                          Postulates of\n                        Empirical Thought\n                           in general","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"47.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0004","text":"These appellations I have chosen advisedly, in order that we might not lose sight of the distinctions in respect of the evidence and the employment of these principles. It will, however, soon appear that—a fact which concerns both the evidence of these principles, and the à priori determination of phenomena—according to the categories of quantity and quality (if we attend merely to the form of these), the principles of these categories are distinguishable from those of the two others, in as much as the former are possessed of an intuitive, but the latter of a merely discursive, though in both instances a complete, certitude. I shall therefore call the former mathematical, and the latter dynamical principles.[[27]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-27) It must be observed, however, that by these terms I mean just as little in the one case the principles of mathematics as those of general (physical) dynamics in the other. I have here in view merely the principles of the pure understanding, in their application to the internal sense (without distinction of the representations given therein), by means of which the sciences of mathematics and dynamics become possible. Accordingly, I have named these principles rather with reference to their application than their content; and I shall now proceed to consider them in the order in which they stand in the table.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"47.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0005","text":"[[27]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-27) All combination (conjunctio) is either composition (compositio) or connection (nexus). The former is the synthesis of a manifold, the parts of which do not necessarily belong to each other. For example, the two triangles into which a square is divided by a diagonal, do not necessarily belong to each other, and of this kind is the synthesis of the homogeneous in everything that can be mathematically considered. This synthesis can be divided into those of aggregation and coalition, the former of which is applied to extensive, the latter to intensive quantities. The second sort of combination (nexus) is the synthesis of a manifold, in so far as its parts do belong necessarily to each other; for example, the accident to a substance, or the effect to the cause. Consequently it is a synthesis of that which though heterogeneous, is represented as connected à priori. This combination—not an arbitrary one—I entitle dynamical because it concerns the connection of the existence of the manifold. This, again, may be divided into the physical synthesis, of the phenomena divided among each other, and the metaphysical synthesis, or the connection of phenomena à priori in the faculty of cognition.\n\n1. AXIOMS OF INTUITION.\n\nThe principle of these is: All Intuitions are Extensive Quantities.\n\nPROOF.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"47.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","text":"All phenomena contain, as regards their form, an intuition in space and time, which lies à priori at the foundation of all without exception. Phenomena, therefore, cannot be apprehended, that is, received into empirical consciousness otherwise than through the synthesis of a manifold, through which the representations of a determinate space or time are generated; that is to say, through the composition of the homogeneous and the consciousness of the synthetical unity of this manifold (homogeneous). Now the consciousness of a homogeneous manifold in intuition, in so far as thereby the representation of an object is rendered possible, is the conception of a quantity (quanti). Consequently, even the perception of an object as phenomenon is possible only through the same synthetical unity of the manifold of the given sensuous intuition, through which the unity of the composition of the homogeneous manifold in the conception of a quantity is cogitated; that is to say, all phenomena are quantities, and extensive quantities, because as intuitions in space or time they must be represented by means of the same synthesis through which space and time themselves are determined.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"47.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0007","text":"An extensive quantity I call that wherein the representation of the parts renders possible (and therefore necessarily antecedes) the representation of the whole. I cannot represent to myself any line, however small, without drawing it in thought, that is, without generating from a point all its parts one after another, and in this way alone producing this intuition. Precisely the same is the case with every, even the smallest, portion of time. I cogitate therein only the successive progress from one moment to another, and hence, by means of the different portions of time and the addition of them, a determinate quantity of time is produced. As the pure intuition in all phenomena is either time or space, so is every phenomenon in its character of intuition an extensive quantity, inasmuch as it can only be cognized in our apprehension by successive synthesis (from part to part). All phenomena are, accordingly, to be considered as aggregates, that is, as a collection of previously given parts; which is not the case with every sort of quantities, but only with those which are represented and apprehended by us as extensive.\n\nOn this successive synthesis of the productive imagination, in the generation of figures, is founded the mathematics of extension, or geometry, with its axioms, which express the conditions of sensuous intuition à priori, under which alone the schema of a pure conception of external intuition can exist; for example, “be tween two points only one straight line is possible,” “two straight lines cannot enclose a space,” etc. These are the axioms which properly relate only to quantities (quanta) as such.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"47.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0008","text":"But, as regards the quantity of a thing (quantitas), that is to say, the answer to the question: “How large is this or that object?” although, in respect to this question, we have various propositions synthetical and immediately certain (indemonstrabilia); we have, in the proper sense of the term, no axioms. For example, the propositions: “If equals be added to equals, the wholes are equal”; “If equals be taken from equals, the remainders are equal”; are analytical, because I am immediately conscious of the identity of the production of the one quantity with the production of the other; whereas axioms must be à priori synthetical propositions. On the other hand, the self-evident propositions as to the relation of numbers, are certainly synthetical but not universal, like those of geometry, and for this reason cannot be called axioms, but numerical formulae. That 7 + 5 = 12 is not an analytical proposition. For neither in the representation of seven, nor of five, nor of the composition of the two numbers, do I cogitate the number twelve. (Whether I cogitate the number in the addition of both, is not at present the question; for in the case of an analytical proposition, the only point is whether I really cogitate the predicate in the representation of the subject.) But although the proposition is synthetical, it is nevertheless only a singular proposition. In so far as regard is here had merely to the synthesis of the homogeneous (the units), it cannot take place except in one manner, although our use of these numbers is afterwards general. If I say: “A triangle can be constructed with three lines, any two of which taken together are greater than the third,” I exercise merely the pure function of the productive imagination, which may draw the lines longer or shorter and construct the angles at its pleasure. On the contrary, the number seven is possible only in one manner, and so is likewise the number twelve, which results from the synthesis of seven and five. Such propositions, then, cannot be termed axioms (for in that case we should have an infinity of these), but numerical formulae.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"47.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","text":"This transcendental principle of the mathematics of phenomena greatly enlarges our à priori cognition. For it is by this principle alone that pure mathematics is rendered applicable in all its precision to objects of experience, and without it the validity of this application would not be so self-evident; on the contrary, contradictions and confusions have often arisen on this very point. Phenomena are not things in themselves. Empirical intuition is possible only through pure intuition (of space and time); consequently, what geometry affirms of the latter, is indisputably valid of the former. All evasions, such as the statement that objects of sense do not conform to the rules of construction in space (for example, to the rule of the infinite divisibility of lines or angles), must fall to the ground. For, if these objections hold good, we deny to space, and with it to all mathematics, objective validity, and no longer know wherefore, and how far, mathematics can be applied to phenomena. The synthesis of spaces and times as the essential form of all intuition, is that which renders possible the apprehension of a phenomenon, and therefore every external experience, consequently all cognition of the objects of experience; and whatever mathematics in its pure use proves of the former, must necessarily hold good of the latter. All objections are but the chicaneries of an ill-instructed reason, which erroneously thinks to liberate the objects of sense from the formal conditions of our sensibility, and represents these, although mere phenomena, as things in themselves, presented as such to our understanding. But in this case, no à priori synthetical cognition of them could be possible, consequently not through pure conceptions of space and the science which determines these conceptions, that is to say, geometry, would itself be impossible.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"47.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","text":"2. ANTICIPATIONS OF PERCEPTION.\n\nThe principle of these is: In all phenomena the Real, that which is an object of sensation, has Intensive Quantity, that is, has a Degree.\n\nPROOF.\n\nPerception is empirical consciousness, that is to say, a consciousness which contains an element of sensation. Phenomena as objects of perception are not pure, that is, merely formal intuitions, like space and time, for they cannot be perceived in themselves.[[28]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-28) They contain, then, over and above the intuition, the materials for an object (through which is represented something existing in space or time), that is to say, they contain the real of sensation, as a representation merely subjective, which gives us merely the consciousness that the subject is affected, and which we refer to some external object. Now, a gradual transition from empirical consciousness to pure consciousness is possible, inasmuch as the real in this consciousness entirely vanishes, and there remains a merely formal consciousness (à priori) of the manifold in time and space; consequently there is possible a synthesis also of the production of the quantity of a sensation from its commencement, that is, from the pure intuition = 0 onwards up to a certain quantity of the sensation. Now as sensation in itself is not an objective representation, and in it is to be found neither the intuition of space nor of time, it cannot possess any extensive quantity, and yet there does belong to it a quantity (and that by means of its apprehension, in which empirical consciousness can within a certain time rise from nothing = 0 up to its given amount), consequently an intensive quantity. And thus we must ascribe intensive quantity, that is, a degree of influence on sense to all objects of perception, in so far as this perception contains sensation.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"47.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0011","text":"[[28]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-28) They can be perceived only as phenomena, and some part of them must always belong to the non-ego; whereas pure intuitions are entirely the products of the mind itself, and as such are cognized _in themselves.—Tr_\n\nAll cognition, by means of which I am enabled to cognize and determine à priori what belongs to empirical cognition, may be called an anticipation; and without doubt this is the sense in which Epicurus employed his expression prholepsis. But as there is in phenomena something which is never cognized à priori, which on this account constitutes the proper difference between pure and empirical cognition, that is to say, sensation (as the matter of perception), it follows, that sensation is just that element in cognition which cannot be at all anticipated. On the other hand, we might very well term the pure determinations in space and time, as well in regard to figure as to quantity, anticipations of phenomena, because they represent à priori that which may always be given à posteriori in experience. But suppose that in every sensation, as sensation in general, without any particular sensation being thought of, there existed something which could be cognized à priori, this would deserve to be called anticipation in a special sense—special, because it may seem surprising to forestall experience, in that which concerns the matter of experience, and which we can only derive from itself. Yet such really is the case here.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"47.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0012","text":"Apprehension[[29]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-29), by means of sensation alone, fills only one moment, that is, if I do not take into consideration a succession of many sensations. As that in the phenomenon, the apprehension of which is not a successive synthesis advancing from parts to an entire representation, sensation has therefore no extensive quantity; the want of sensation in a moment of time would represent it as empty, consequently = 0. That which in the empirical intuition corresponds to sensation is reality (realitas phaenomenon); that which corresponds to the absence of it, negation = 0. Now every sensation is capable of a diminution, so that it can decrease, and thus gradually disappear. Therefore, between reality in a phenomenon and negation, there exists a continuous concatenation of many possible intermediate sensations, the difference of which from each other is always smaller than that between the given sensation and zero, or complete negation. That is to say, the real in a phenomenon has always a quantity, which however is not discoverable in apprehension, inasmuch as apprehension take place by means of mere sensation in one instant, and not by the successive synthesis of many sensations, and therefore does not progress from parts to the whole. Consequently, it has a quantity, but not an extensive quantity.\n\n[[29]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-29) Apprehension is the Kantian word for preception, in the largest sense in which we employ that term. It is the genus which includes under i, as species, perception proper and sensation proper—Tr","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"47.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0013","text":"Now that quantity which is apprehended only as unity, and in which plurality can be represented only by approximation to negation = 0, I term intensive quantity. Consequently, reality in a phenomenon has intensive quantity, that is, a degree. If we consider this reality as cause (be it of sensation or of another reality in the phenomenon, for example, a change), we call the degree of reality in its character of cause a momentum, for example, the momentum of weight; and for this reason, that the degree only indicates that quantity the apprehension of which is not successive, but instantaneous. This, however, I touch upon only in passing, for with causality I have at present nothing to do.\n\nAccordingly, every sensation, consequently every reality in phenomena, however small it may be, has a degree, that is, an intensive quantity, which may always be lessened, and between reality and negation there exists a continuous connection of possible realities, and possible smaller perceptions. Every colour—for example, red—has a degree, which, be it ever so small, is never the smallest, and so is it always with heat, the momentum of weight, etc.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"47.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0014","text":"This property of quantities, according to which no part of them is the smallest possible (no part simple), is called their continuity. Space and time are quanta continua, because no part of them can be given, without enclosing it within boundaries (points and moments), consequently, this given part is itself a space or a time. Space, therefore, consists only of spaces, and time of times. Points and moments are only boundaries, that is, the mere places or positions of their limitation. But places always presuppose intuitions which are to limit or determine them; and we cannot conceive either space or time composed of constituent parts which are given before space or time. Such quantities may also be called flowing, because synthesis (of the productive imagination) in the production of these quantities is a progression in time, the continuity of which we are accustomed to indicate by the expression flowing.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"47.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0015","text":"All phenomena, then, are continuous quantities, in respect both to intuition and mere perception (sensation, and with it reality). In the former case they are extensive quantities; in the latter, intensive. When the synthesis of the manifold of a phenomenon is interrupted, there results merely an aggregate of several phenomena, and not properly a phenomenon as a quantity, which is not produced by the mere continuation of the productive synthesis of a certain kind, but by the repetition of a synthesis always ceasing. For example, if I call thirteen dollars a sum or quantity of money, I employ the term quite correctly, inasmuch as I understand by thirteen dollars the value of a mark in standard silver, which is, to be sure, a continuous quantity, in which no part is the smallest, but every part might constitute a piece of money, which would contain material for still smaller pieces. If, however, by the words thirteen dollars I understand so many coins (be their value in silver what it may), it would be quite erroneous to use the expression a quantity of dollars; on the contrary, I must call them aggregate, that is, a number of coins. And as in every number we must have unity as the foundation, so a phenomenon taken as unity is a quantity, and as such always a continuous quantity (quantum continuum).","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"47.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0016","text":"Now, seeing all phenomena, whether considered as extensive or intensive, are continuous quantities, the proposition: “All change (transition of a thing from one state into another) is continuous,” might be proved here easily, and with mathematical evidence, were it not that the causality of a change lies, entirely beyond the bounds of a transcendental philosophy, and presupposes empirical principles. For of the possibility of a cause which changes the condition of things, that is, which determines them to the contrary to a certain given state, the understanding gives us à priori no knowledge; not merely because it has no insight into the possibility of it (for such insight is absent in several à priori cognitions), but because the notion of change concerns only certain determinations of phenomena, which experience alone can acquaint us with, while their cause lies in the unchangeable. But seeing that we have nothing which we could here employ but the pure fundamental conceptions of all possible experience, among which of course nothing empirical can be admitted, we dare not, without injuring the unity of our system, anticipate general physical science, which is built upon certain fundamental experiences.\n\nNevertheless, we are in no want of proofs of the great influence which the principle above developed exercises in the anticipation of perceptions, and even in supplying the want of them, so far as to shield us against the false conclusions which otherwise we might rashly draw.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"47.16"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","text":"If all reality in perception has a degree, between which and negation there is an endless sequence of ever smaller degrees, and if, nevertheless, every sense must have a determinate degree of receptivity for sensations; no perception, and consequently no experience is possible, which can prove, either immediately or mediately, an entire absence of all reality in a phenomenon; in other words, it is impossible ever to draw from experience a proof of the existence of empty space or of empty time. For in the first place, an entire absence of reality in a sensuous intuition cannot of course be an object of perception; secondly, such absence cannot be deduced from the contemplation of any single phenomenon, and the difference of the degrees in its reality; nor ought it ever to be admitted in explanation of any phenomenon. For if even the complete intuition of a determinate space or time is thoroughly real, that is, if no part thereof is empty, yet because every reality has its degree, which, with the extensive quantity of the phenomenon unchanged, can diminish through endless gradations down to nothing (the void), there must be infinitely graduated degrees, with which space or time is filled, and the intensive quantity in different phenomena may be smaller or greater, although the extensive quantity of the intuition remains equal and unaltered.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"47.17"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","text":"We shall give an example of this. Almost all natural philosophers, remarking a great difference in the quantity of the matter of different kinds in bodies with the same volume (partly on account of the momentum of gravity or weight, partly on account of the momentum of resistance to other bodies in motion), conclude unanimously that this volume (extensive quantity of the phenomenon) must be void in all bodies, although in different proportion. But who would suspect that these for the most part mathematical and mechanical inquirers into nature should ground this conclusion solely on a metaphysical hypothesis—a sort of hypothesis which they profess to disparage and avoid? Yet this they do, in assuming that the real in space (I must not here call it impenetrability or weight, because these are empirical conceptions) is always identical, and can only be distinguished according to its extensive quantity, that is, multiplicity. Now to this presupposition, for which they can have no ground in experience, and which consequently is merely metaphysical, I oppose a transcendental demonstration, which it is true will not explain the difference in the filling up of spaces, but which nevertheless completely does away with the supposed necessity of the above-mentioned presupposition that we cannot explain the said difference otherwise than by the hypothesis of empty spaces. This demonstration, moreover, has the merit of setting the understanding at liberty to conceive this distinction in a different manner, if the explanation of the fact requires any such hypothesis. For we perceive that although two equal spaces may be completely filled by matters altogether different, so that in neither of them is there left a single point wherein matter is not present, nevertheless, every reality has its degree (of resistance or of weight), which, without diminution of the extensive quantity, can become less and less ad infinitum, before it passes into nothingness and disappears. Thus an expansion which fills a space—for example, caloric, or any other reality in the phenomenal world—can decrease in its degrees to infinity, yet without leaving the smallest part of the space empty; on the contrary, filling it with those lesser degrees as completely as another phenomenon could with greater. My intention here is by no means to maintain that this is really the case with the difference of matters, in regard to their specific gravity; I wish only to prove, from a principle of the pure understanding, that the nature of our perceptions makes such a mode of explanation possible, and that it is erroneous to regard the real in a phenomenon as equal quoad its degree, and different only quoad its aggregation and extensive quantity, and this, too, on the pretended authority of an à priori principle of the understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"47.18"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0019","text":"Nevertheless, this principle of the anticipation of perception must somewhat startle an inquirer whom initiation into transcendental philosophy has rendered cautious. We must naturally entertain some doubt whether or not the understanding can enounce any such synthetical proposition as that respecting the degree of all reality in phenomena, and consequently the possibility of the internal difference of sensation itself—abstraction being made of its empirical quality. Thus it is a question not unworthy of solution: “How the understanding can pronounce synthetically and à priori respecting phenomena, and thus anticipate these, even in that which is peculiarly and merely empirical, that, namely, which concerns sensation itself?”","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"47.19"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","text":"The quality of sensation is in all cases merely empirical, and cannot be represented à priori (for example, colours, taste, etc.). But the real—that which corresponds to sensation—in opposition to negation = 0, only represents something the conception of which in itself contains a being (ein seyn), and signifies nothing but the synthesis in an empirical consciousness. That is to say, the empirical consciousness in the internal sense can be raised from 0 to every higher degree, so that the very same extensive quantity of intuition, an illuminated surface, for example, excites as great a sensation as an aggregate of many other surfaces less illuminated. We can therefore make complete abstraction of the extensive quantity of a phenomenon, and represent to ourselves in the mere sensation in a certain momentum, a synthesis of homogeneous ascension from 0 up to the given empirical consciousness, All sensations therefore as such are given only à posteriori, but this property thereof, namely, that they have a degree, can be known à priori. It is worthy of remark, that in respect to quantities in general, we can cognize à priori only a single quality, namely, continuity; but in respect to all quality (the real in phenomena), we cannot cognize à priori anything more than the intensive quantity thereof, namely, that they have a degree. All else is left to experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"47.20"}],"sha256":"2c3ccab5c2ed54e19b167aeb5a8b7e23eae0b7c59e7b8b8bb1570b276a62e1e1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD","title":"Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","order":48,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_CIGD","text":"# Phenomena Noumena\n\n### Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena\n\nWe have now not only traversed the region of the pure understanding and carefully surveyed every part of it, but we have also measured it, and assigned to everything therein its proper place. But this land is an island, and enclosed by nature herself within unchangeable limits. It is the land of truth (an attractive word), surrounded by a wide and stormy ocean, the region of illusion, where many a fog-bank, many an iceberg, seems to the mariner, on his voyage of discovery, a new country, and, while constantly deluding him with vain hopes, engages him in dangerous adventures, from which he never can desist, and which yet he never can bring to a termination. But before venturing upon this sea, in order to explore it in its whole extent, and to arrive at a certainty whether anything is to be discovered there, it will not be without advantage if we cast our eyes upon the chart of the land that we are about to leave, and to ask ourselves, firstly, whether we cannot rest perfectly contented with what it contains, or whether we must not of necessity be contented with it, if we can find nowhere else a solid foundation to build upon; and, secondly, by what title we possess this land itself, and how we hold it secure against all hostile claims? Although, in the course of our analytic, we have already given sufficient answers to these questions, yet a summary recapitulation of these solutions may be useful in strengthening our conviction, by uniting in one point the momenta of the arguments.\n\nWe have seen that everything which the understanding draws from itself, without borrowing from experience, it nevertheless possesses only for the behoof and use of experience. The principles of the pure understanding, whether constitutive à priori (as the mathematical principles), or merely regulative (as the dynamical), contain nothing but the pure schema, as it were, of possible experience. For experience possesses its unity from the synthetical unity which the understanding, originally and from itself, imparts to the synthesis of the imagination in relation to apperception, and in à priori relation to and agreement with which phenomena, as data for a possible cognition, must stand. But although these rules of the understanding are not only à priori true, but the very source of all truth, that is, of the accordance of our cognition with objects, and on this ground, that they contain the basis of the possibility of experience, as the ensemble of all cognition, it seems to us not enough to propound what is true—we desire also to be told what we want to know. If, then, we learn nothing more by this critical examination than what we should have practised in the merely empirical use of the understanding, without any such subtle inquiry, the presumption is that the advantage we reap from it is not worth the labour bestowed upon it. It may certainly be answered that no rash curiosity is more prejudicial to the enlargement of our knowledge than that which must know beforehand the utility of this or that piece of information which we seek, before we have entered on the needful investigations, and before one could form the least conception of its utility, even though it were placed before our eyes. But there is one advantage in such transcendental inquiries which can be made comprehensible to the dullest and most reluctant learner—this, namely, that the understanding which is occupied merely with empirical exercise, and does not reflect on the sources of its own cognition, may exercise its functions very well and very successfully, but is quite unable to do one thing, and that of very great importance, to determine, namely, the bounds that limit its employment, and to know what lies within or without its own sphere. This purpose can be obtained only by such profound investigations as we have instituted. But if it cannot distinguish whether certain questions lie within its horizon or not, it can never be sure either as to its claims or possessions, but must lay its account with many humiliating corrections, when it transgresses, as it unavoidably will, the limits of its own territory, and loses itself in fanciful opinions and blinding illusions.\n\nThat the understanding, therefore, cannot make of its à priori principles, or even of its conceptions, other than an empirical use, is a proposition which leads to the most important results. A transcendental use is made of a conception in a fundamental proposition or principle, when it is referred to things in general and considered as things in themselves; an empirical use, when it is referred merely to phenomena, that is, to objects of a possible experience. That the latter use of a conception is the only admissible one is evident from the reasons following. For every conception are requisite, firstly, the logical form of a conception (of thought) general; and, secondly, the possibility of presenting to this an object to which it may apply. Failing this latter, it has no sense, and utterly void of content, although it may contain the logical function for constructing a conception from certain data. Now, object cannot be given to a conception otherwise than by intuition, and, even if a pure intuition antecedent to the object is à priori possible, this pure intuition can itself obtain objective validity only from empirical intuition, of which it is itself but the form. All conceptions, therefore, and with them all principles, however high the degree of their à priori possibility, relate to empirical intuitions, that is, to data towards a possible experience. Without this they possess no objective validity, but are mere play of imagination or of understanding with images or notions. Let us take, for example, the conceptions of mathematics, and first in its pure intuitions. “Space has three dimensions”—“Between two points there can be only one straight line,” etc. Although all these principles, and the representation of the object with which this science occupies itself, are generated in the mind entirely à priori, they would nevertheless have no significance if we were not always able to exhibit their significance in and by means of phenomena (empirical objects). Hence it is requisite that an abstract conception be made sensuous, that is, that an object corresponding to it in intuition be forthcoming, otherwise the conception remains, as we say, without sense, that is, without meaning. Mathematics fulfils this requirement by the construction of the figure, which is a phenomenon evident to the senses. The same science finds support and significance in number; this in its turn finds it in the fingers, or in counters, or in lines and points. The conception itself is always produced à priori, together with the synthetical principles or formulas from such conceptions; but the proper employment of them, and their application to objects, can exist nowhere but in experience, the possibility of which, as regards its form, they contain à priori.\n\nThat this is also the case with all of the categories and the principles based upon them is evident from the fact that we cannot render intelligible the possibility of an object corresponding to them without having recourse to the conditions of sensibility, consequently, to the form of phenomena, to which, as their only proper objects, their use must therefore be confined, inasmuch as, if this condition is removed, all significance, that is, all relation to an object, disappears, and no example can be found to make it comprehensible what sort of things we ought to think under such conceptions.\n\nThe conception of quantity cannot be explained except by saying that it is the determination of a thing whereby it can be cogitated how many times one is placed in it. But this “how many times” is based upon successive repetition, consequently upon time and the synthesis of the homogeneous therein. Reality, in contradistinction to negation, can be explained only by cogitating a time which is either filled therewith or is void. If I leave out the notion of permanence (which is existence in all time), there remains in the conception of substance nothing but the logical notion of subject, a notion of which I endeavour to realize by representing to myself something that can exist only as a subject. But not only am I perfectly ignorant of any conditions under which this logical prerogative can belong to a thing, I can make nothing out of the notion, and draw no inference from it, because no object to which to apply the conception is determined, and we consequently do not know whether it has any meaning at all. In like manner, if I leave out the notion of time, in which something follows upon some other thing in conformity with a rule, I can find nothing in the pure category, except that there is a something of such a sort that from it a conclusion may be drawn as to the existence of some other thing. But in this case it would not only be impossible to distinguish between a cause and an effect, but, as this power to draw conclusions requires conditions of which I am quite ignorant, the conception is not determined as to the mode in which it ought to apply to an object. The so-called principle: “Everything that is contingent has a cause,” comes with a gravity and self-assumed authority that seems to require no support from without. But, I ask, what is meant by contingent? The answer is that the non-existence of which is possible. But I should like very well to know by what means this possibility of non-existence is to be cognized, if we do not represent to ourselves a succession in the series of phenomena, and in this succession an existence which follows a non-existence, or conversely, consequently, change. For to say, that the non-existence of a thing is not self-contradictory is a lame appeal to a logical condition, which is no doubt a necessary condition of the existence of the conception, but is far from being sufficient for the real objective possibility of non-existence. I can annihilate in thought every existing substance without self-contradiction, but I cannot infer from this their objective contingency in existence, that is to say, the possibility of their non-existence in itself. As regards the category of community, it may easily be inferred that, as the pure categories of substance and causality are incapable of a definition and explanation sufficient to determine their object without the aid of intuition, the category of reciprocal causality in the relation of substances to each other (commercium) is just as little susceptible thereof. Possibility, existence, and necessity nobody has ever yet been able to explain without being guilty of manifest tautology, when the definition has been drawn entirely from the pure understanding. For the substitution of the logical possibility of the conception—the condition of which is that it be not self-contradictory, for the transcendental possibility of things—the condition of which is that there be an object corresponding to the conception, is a trick which can only deceive the inexperienced.[[38]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-38)\n\n[[38]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-38) In one word, to none of these conceptions belongs a corresponding object, and consequently their real possibility cannot be demonstrated, if we take away sensuous intuition—the only intuition which we possess—and there then remains nothing but the logical possibility, that is, the fact that the conception or thought is possible—which, however, is not the question; what we want to know being, whether it relates to an object and thus possesses any meaning.\n\nIt follows incontestably, that the pure conceptions of the understanding are incapable of transcendental, and must always be of empirical use alone, and that the principles of the pure understanding relate only to the general conditions of a possible experience, to objects of the senses, and never to things in general, apart from the mode in which we intuite them.\n\nTranscendental analytic has accordingly this important result, to wit, that the understanding is competent effect nothing à priori, except the anticipation of the form of a possible experience in general, and that, as that which is not phenomenon cannot be an object of experience, it can never overstep the limits of sensibility, within which alone objects are presented to us. Its principles are merely principles of the exposition of phenomena, and the proud name of an ontology, which professes to present synthetical cognitions à priori of things in general in a systematic doctrine, must give place to the modest title of analytic of the pure understanding.\n\nThought is the act of referring a given intuition to an object. If the mode of this intuition is unknown to us, the object is merely transcendental, and the conception of the understanding is employed only transcendentally, that is, to produce unity in the thought of a manifold in general. Now a pure category, in which all conditions of sensuous intuition—as the only intuition we possess—are abstracted, does not determine an object, but merely expresses the thought of an object in general, according to different modes. Now, to employ a conception, the function of judgement is required, by which an object is subsumed under the conception, consequently the at least formal condition, under which something can be given in intuition. Failing this condition of judgement (schema), subsumption is impossible; for there is in such a case nothing given, which may be subsumed under the conception. The merely transcendental use of the categories is therefore, in fact, no use at all and has no determined, or even, as regards its form, determinable object. Hence it follows that the pure category is incompetent to establish a synthetical à priori principle, and that the principles of the pure understanding are only of empirical and never of transcendental use, and that beyond the sphere of possible experience no synthetical à priori principles are possible.\n\nIt may be advisable, therefore, to express ourselves thus. The pure categories, apart from the formal conditions of sensibility, have a merely transcendental meaning, but are nevertheless not of transcendental use, because this is in itself impossible, inasmuch as all the conditions of any employment or use of them (in judgements) are absent, to wit, the formal conditions of the subsumption of an object under these conceptions. As, therefore, in the character of pure categories, they must be employed empirically, and cannot be employed transcendentally, they are of no use at all, when separated from sensibility, that is, they cannot be applied to an object. They are merely the pure form of the employment of the understanding in respect of objects in general and of thought, without its being at the same time possible to think or to determine any object by their means. But there lurks at the foundation of this subject an illusion which it is very difficult to avoid. The categories are not based, as regards their origin, upon sensibility, like the forms of intuition, space, and time; they seem, therefore, to be capable of an application beyond the sphere of sensuous objects. But this is not the case. They are nothing but mere forms of thought, which contain only the logical faculty of uniting à priori in consciousness the manifold given in intuition. Apart, then, from the only intuition possible for us, they have still less meaning than the pure sensuous forms, space and time, for through them an object is at least given, while a mode of connection of the manifold, when the intuition which alone gives the manifold is wanting, has no meaning at all. At the same time, when we designate certain objects as phenomena or sensuous existences, thus distinguishing our mode of intuiting them from their own nature as things in themselves, it is evident that by this very distinction we as it were place the latter, considered in this their own nature, although we do not so intuite them, in opposition to the former, or, on the other hand, we do so place other possible things, which are not objects of our senses, but are cogitated by the understanding alone, and call them intelligible existences (noumena). Now the question arises whether the pure conceptions of our understanding do possess significance in respect of these latter, and may possibly be a mode of cognizing them.\n\nBut we are met at the very commencement with an ambiguity, which may easily occasion great misapprehension. The understanding, when it terms an object in a certain relation phenomenon, at the same time forms out of this relation a representation or notion of an object in itself, and hence believes that it can form also conceptions of such objects. Now as the understanding possesses no other fundamental conceptions besides the categories, it takes for granted that an object considered as a thing in itself must be capable of being thought by means of these pure conceptions, and is thereby led to hold the perfectly undetermined conception of an intelligible existence, a something out of the sphere of our sensibility, for a determinate conception of an existence which we can cognize in some way or other by means of the understanding.\n\nIf, by the term noumenon, we understand a thing so far as it is not an object of our sensuous intuition, thus making abstraction of our mode of intuiting it, this is a noumenon in the negative sense of the word. But if we understand by it an object of a non-sensuous intuition, we in this case assume a peculiar mode of intuition, an intellectual intuition, to wit, which does not, however, belong to us, of the very possibility of which we have no notion—and this is a noumenon in the positive sense.\n\nThe doctrine of sensibility is also the doctrine of noumena in the negative sense, that is, of things which the understanding is obliged to cogitate apart from any relation to our mode of intuition, consequently not as mere phenomena, but as things in themselves. But the understanding at the same time comprehends that it cannot employ its categories for the consideration of things in themselves, because these possess significance only in relation to the unity of intuitions in space and time, and that they are competent to determine this unity by means of general à priori connecting conceptions only on account of the pure ideality of space and time. Where this unity of time is not to be met with, as is the case with noumena, the whole use, indeed the whole meaning of the categories is entirely lost, for even the possibility of things to correspond to the categories is in this case incomprehensible. On this point, I need only refer the reader to what I have said at the commencement of the General Remark appended to the foregoing chapter. Now, the possibility of a thing can never be proved from the fact that the conception of it is not self-contradictory, but only by means of an intuition corresponding to the conception. If, therefore, we wish to apply the categories to objects which cannot be regarded as phenomena, we must have an intuition different from the sensuous, and in this case the objects would be a noumena in the positive sense of the word. Now, as such an intuition, that is, an intellectual intuition, is no part of our faculty of cognition, it is absolutely impossible for the categories to possess any application beyond the limits of experience. It may be true that there are intelligible existences to which our faculty of sensuous intuition has no relation, and cannot be applied, but our conceptions of the understanding, as mere forms of thought for our sensuous intuition, do not extend to these. What, therefore, we call noumenon must be understood by us as such in a negative sense.\n\nIf I take away from an empirical intuition all thought (by means of the categories), there remains no cognition of any object; for by means of mere intuition nothing is cogitated, and, from the existence of such or such an affection of sensibility in me, it does not follow that this affection or representation has any relation to an object without me. But if I take away all intuition, there still remains the form of thought, that is, the mode of determining an object for the manifold of a possible intuition. Thus the categories do in some measure really extend further than sensuous intuition, inasmuch as they think objects in general, without regard to the mode (of sensibility) in which these objects are given. But they do not for this reason apply to and determine a wider sphere of objects, because we cannot assume that such can be given, without presupposing the possibility of another than the sensuous mode of intuition, a supposition we are not justified in making.\n\nI call a conception problematical which contains in itself no contradiction, and which is connected with other cognitions as a limitation of given conceptions, but whose objective reality cannot be cognized in any manner. The conception of a noumenon, that is, of a thing which must be cogitated not as an object of sense, but as a thing in itself (solely through the pure understanding), is not self-contradictory, for we are not entitled to maintain that sensibility is the only possible mode of intuition. Nay, further, this conception is necessary to restrain sensuous intuition within the bounds of phenomena, and thus to limit the objective validity of sensuous cognition; for things in themselves, which lie beyond its province, are called noumena for the very purpose of indicating that this cognition does not extend its application to all that the understanding thinks. But, after all, the possibility of such noumena is quite incomprehensible, and beyond the sphere of phenomena, all is for us a mere void; that is to say, we possess an understanding whose province does problematically extend beyond this sphere, but we do not possess an intuition, indeed, not even the conception of a possible intuition, by means of which objects beyond the region of sensibility could be given us, and in reference to which the understanding might be employed assertorically. The conception of a noumenon is therefore merely a limitative conception and therefore only of negative use. But it is not an arbitrary or fictitious notion, but is connected with the limitation of sensibility, without, however, being capable of presenting us with any positive datum beyond this sphere.\n\nThe division of objects into phenomena and noumena, and of the world into a mundus sensibilis and intelligibilis is therefore quite inadmissible in a positive sense, although conceptions do certainly admit of such a division; for the class of noumena have no determinate object corresponding to them, and cannot therefore possess objective validity. If we abandon the senses, how can it be made conceivable that the categories (which are the only conceptions that could serve as conceptions for noumena) have any sense or meaning at all, inasmuch as something more than the mere unity of thought, namely, a possible intuition, is requisite for their application to an object? The conception of a noumenon, considered as merely problematical, is, however, not only admissible, but, as a limitative conception of sensibility, absolutely necessary. But, in this case, a noumenon is not a particular intelligible object for our understanding; on the contrary, the kind of understanding to which it could belong is itself a problem, for we cannot form the most distant conception of the possibility of an understanding which should cognize an object, not discursively by means of categories, but intuitively in a non-sensuous intuition. Our understanding attains in this way a sort of negative extension. That is to say, it is not limited by, but rather limits, sensibility, by giving the name of noumena to things, not considered as phenomena, but as things in themselves. But it at the same time prescribes limits to itself, for it confesses itself unable to cognize these by means of the categories, and hence is compelled to cogitate them merely as an unknown something.\n\nI find, however, in the writings of modern authors, an entirely different use of the expressions, mundus sensibilis and intelligibilis, which quite departs from the meaning of the ancients—an acceptation in which, indeed, there is to be found no difficulty, but which at the same time depends on mere verbal quibbling. According to this meaning, some have chosen to call the complex of phenomena, in so far as it is intuited, mundus sensibilis, but in so far as the connection thereof is cogitated according to general laws of thought, mundus intelligibilis. Astronomy, in so far as we mean by the word the mere observation of the starry heaven, may represent the former; a system of astronomy, such as the Copernican or Newtonian, the latter. But such twisting of words is a mere sophistical subterfuge, to avoid a difficult question, by modifying its meaning to suit our own convenience. To be sure, understanding and reason are employed in the cognition of phenomena; but the question is, whether these can be applied when the object is not a phenomenon and in this sense we regard it if it is cogitated as given to the understanding alone, and not to the senses. The question therefore is whether, over and above the empirical use of the understanding, a transcendental use is possible, which applies to the noumenon as an object. This question we have answered in the negative.\n\nWhen therefore we say, the senses represent objects as they appear, the understanding as they are, the latter statement must not be understood in a transcendental, but only in an empirical signification, that is, as they must be represented in the complete connection of phenomena, and not according to what they may be, apart from their relation to possible experience, consequently not as objects of the pure understanding. For this must ever remain unknown to us. Nay, it is also quite unknown to us whether any such transcendental or extraordinary cognition is possible under any circumstances, at least, whether it is possible by means of our categories. Understanding and sensibility, with us, can determine objects only in conjunction. If we separate them, we have intuitions without conceptions, or conceptions without intuitions; in both cases, representations, which we cannot apply to any determinate object.\n\nIf, after all our inquiries and explanations, any one still hesitates to abandon the mere transcendental use of the categories, let him attempt to construct with them a synthetical proposition. It would, of course, be unnecessary for this purpose to construct an analytical proposition, for that does not extend the sphere of the understanding, but, being concerned only about what is cogitated in the conception itself, it leaves it quite undecided whether the conception has any relation to objects, or merely indicates the unity of thought—complete abstraction being made of the modi in which an object may be given: in such a proposition, it is sufficient for the understanding to know what lies in the conception—to what it applies is to it indifferent. The attempt must therefore be made with a synthetical and so-called transcendental principle, for example: “Everything that exists, exists as substance,” or, “Everything that is contingent exists as an effect of some other thing, viz., of its cause.” Now I ask, whence can the understanding draw these synthetical propositions, when the conceptions contained therein do not relate to possible experience but to things in themselves (noumena)? Where is to be found the third term, which is always requisite PURE site in a synthetical proposition, which may connect in the same proposition conceptions which have no logical (analytical) connection with each other? The proposition never will be demonstrated, nay, more, the possibility of any such pure assertion never can be shown, without making reference to the empirical use of the understanding, and thus, ipso facto, completely renouncing pure and non-sensuous judgement. Thus the conception of pure and merely intelligible objects is completely void of all principles of its application, because we cannot imagine any mode in which they might be given, and the problematical thought which leaves a place open for them serves only, like a void space, to limit the use of empirical principles, without containing at the same time any other object of cognition beyond their sphere.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0001","text":"# Phenomena Noumena\n\n### Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena\n\nWe have now not only traversed the region of the pure understanding and carefully surveyed every part of it, but we have also measured it, and assigned to everything therein its proper place. But this land is an island, and enclosed by nature herself within unchangeable limits. It is the land of truth (an attractive word), surrounded by a wide and stormy ocean, the region of illusion, where many a fog-bank, many an iceberg, seems to the mariner, on his voyage of discovery, a new country, and, while constantly deluding him with vain hopes, engages him in dangerous adventures, from which he never can desist, and which yet he never can bring to a termination. But before venturing upon this sea, in order to explore it in its whole extent, and to arrive at a certainty whether anything is to be discovered there, it will not be without advantage if we cast our eyes upon the chart of the land that we are about to leave, and to ask ourselves, firstly, whether we cannot rest perfectly contented with what it contains, or whether we must not of necessity be contented with it, if we can find nowhere else a solid foundation to build upon; and, secondly, by what title we possess this land itself, and how we hold it secure against all hostile claims? Although, in the course of our analytic, we have already given sufficient answers to these questions, yet a summary recapitulation of these solutions may be useful in strengthening our conviction, by uniting in one point the momenta of the arguments.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"48.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","text":"We have seen that everything which the understanding draws from itself, without borrowing from experience, it nevertheless possesses only for the behoof and use of experience. The principles of the pure understanding, whether constitutive à priori (as the mathematical principles), or merely regulative (as the dynamical), contain nothing but the pure schema, as it were, of possible experience. For experience possesses its unity from the synthetical unity which the understanding, originally and from itself, imparts to the synthesis of the imagination in relation to apperception, and in à priori relation to and agreement with which phenomena, as data for a possible cognition, must stand. But although these rules of the understanding are not only à priori true, but the very source of all truth, that is, of the accordance of our cognition with objects, and on this ground, that they contain the basis of the possibility of experience, as the ensemble of all cognition, it seems to us not enough to propound what is true—we desire also to be told what we want to know. If, then, we learn nothing more by this critical examination than what we should have practised in the merely empirical use of the understanding, without any such subtle inquiry, the presumption is that the advantage we reap from it is not worth the labour bestowed upon it. It may certainly be answered that no rash curiosity is more prejudicial to the enlargement of our knowledge than that which must know beforehand the utility of this or that piece of information which we seek, before we have entered on the needful investigations, and before one could form the least conception of its utility, even though it were placed before our eyes. But there is one advantage in such transcendental inquiries which can be made comprehensible to the dullest and most reluctant learner—this, namely, that the understanding which is occupied merely with empirical exercise, and does not reflect on the sources of its own cognition, may exercise its functions very well and very successfully, but is quite unable to do one thing, and that of very great importance, to determine, namely, the bounds that limit its employment, and to know what lies within or without its own sphere. This purpose can be obtained only by such profound investigations as we have instituted. But if it cannot distinguish whether certain questions lie within its horizon or not, it can never be sure either as to its claims or possessions, but must lay its account with many humiliating corrections, when it transgresses, as it unavoidably will, the limits of its own territory, and loses itself in fanciful opinions and blinding illusions.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"48.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","text":"That the understanding, therefore, cannot make of its à priori principles, or even of its conceptions, other than an empirical use, is a proposition which leads to the most important results. A transcendental use is made of a conception in a fundamental proposition or principle, when it is referred to things in general and considered as things in themselves; an empirical use, when it is referred merely to phenomena, that is, to objects of a possible experience. That the latter use of a conception is the only admissible one is evident from the reasons following. For every conception are requisite, firstly, the logical form of a conception (of thought) general; and, secondly, the possibility of presenting to this an object to which it may apply. Failing this latter, it has no sense, and utterly void of content, although it may contain the logical function for constructing a conception from certain data. Now, object cannot be given to a conception otherwise than by intuition, and, even if a pure intuition antecedent to the object is à priori possible, this pure intuition can itself obtain objective validity only from empirical intuition, of which it is itself but the form. All conceptions, therefore, and with them all principles, however high the degree of their à priori possibility, relate to empirical intuitions, that is, to data towards a possible experience. Without this they possess no objective validity, but are mere play of imagination or of understanding with images or notions. Let us take, for example, the conceptions of mathematics, and first in its pure intuitions. “Space has three dimensions”—“Between two points there can be only one straight line,” etc. Although all these principles, and the representation of the object with which this science occupies itself, are generated in the mind entirely à priori, they would nevertheless have no significance if we were not always able to exhibit their significance in and by means of phenomena (empirical objects). Hence it is requisite that an abstract conception be made sensuous, that is, that an object corresponding to it in intuition be forthcoming, otherwise the conception remains, as we say, without sense, that is, without meaning. Mathematics fulfils this requirement by the construction of the figure, which is a phenomenon evident to the senses. The same science finds support and significance in number; this in its turn finds it in the fingers, or in counters, or in lines and points. The conception itself is always produced à priori, together with the synthetical principles or formulas from such conceptions; but the proper employment of them, and their application to objects, can exist nowhere but in experience, the possibility of which, as regards its form, they contain à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"48.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0004","text":"That this is also the case with all of the categories and the principles based upon them is evident from the fact that we cannot render intelligible the possibility of an object corresponding to them without having recourse to the conditions of sensibility, consequently, to the form of phenomena, to which, as their only proper objects, their use must therefore be confined, inasmuch as, if this condition is removed, all significance, that is, all relation to an object, disappears, and no example can be found to make it comprehensible what sort of things we ought to think under such conceptions.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"48.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0005","text":"The conception of quantity cannot be explained except by saying that it is the determination of a thing whereby it can be cogitated how many times one is placed in it. But this “how many times” is based upon successive repetition, consequently upon time and the synthesis of the homogeneous therein. Reality, in contradistinction to negation, can be explained only by cogitating a time which is either filled therewith or is void. If I leave out the notion of permanence (which is existence in all time), there remains in the conception of substance nothing but the logical notion of subject, a notion of which I endeavour to realize by representing to myself something that can exist only as a subject. But not only am I perfectly ignorant of any conditions under which this logical prerogative can belong to a thing, I can make nothing out of the notion, and draw no inference from it, because no object to which to apply the conception is determined, and we consequently do not know whether it has any meaning at all. In like manner, if I leave out the notion of time, in which something follows upon some other thing in conformity with a rule, I can find nothing in the pure category, except that there is a something of such a sort that from it a conclusion may be drawn as to the existence of some other thing. But in this case it would not only be impossible to distinguish between a cause and an effect, but, as this power to draw conclusions requires conditions of which I am quite ignorant, the conception is not determined as to the mode in which it ought to apply to an object. The so-called principle: “Everything that is contingent has a cause,” comes with a gravity and self-assumed authority that seems to require no support from without. But, I ask, what is meant by contingent? The answer is that the non-existence of which is possible. But I should like very well to know by what means this possibility of non-existence is to be cognized, if we do not represent to ourselves a succession in the series of phenomena, and in this succession an existence which follows a non-existence, or conversely, consequently, change. For to say, that the non-existence of a thing is not self-contradictory is a lame appeal to a logical condition, which is no doubt a necessary condition of the existence of the conception, but is far from being sufficient for the real objective possibility of non-existence. I can annihilate in thought every existing substance without self-contradiction, but I cannot infer from this their objective contingency in existence, that is to say, the possibility of their non-existence in itself. As regards the category of community, it may easily be inferred that, as the pure categories of substance and causality are incapable of a definition and explanation sufficient to determine their object without the aid of intuition, the category of reciprocal causality in the relation of substances to each other (commercium) is just as little susceptible thereof. Possibility, existence, and necessity nobody has ever yet been able to explain without being guilty of manifest tautology, when the definition has been drawn entirely from the pure understanding. For the substitution of the logical possibility of the conception—the condition of which is that it be not self-contradictory, for the transcendental possibility of things—the condition of which is that there be an object corresponding to the conception, is a trick which can only deceive the inexperienced.[[38]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-38)","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"48.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","text":"[[38]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-38) In one word, to none of these conceptions belongs a corresponding object, and consequently their real possibility cannot be demonstrated, if we take away sensuous intuition—the only intuition which we possess—and there then remains nothing but the logical possibility, that is, the fact that the conception or thought is possible—which, however, is not the question; what we want to know being, whether it relates to an object and thus possesses any meaning.\n\nIt follows incontestably, that the pure conceptions of the understanding are incapable of transcendental, and must always be of empirical use alone, and that the principles of the pure understanding relate only to the general conditions of a possible experience, to objects of the senses, and never to things in general, apart from the mode in which we intuite them.\n\nTranscendental analytic has accordingly this important result, to wit, that the understanding is competent effect nothing à priori, except the anticipation of the form of a possible experience in general, and that, as that which is not phenomenon cannot be an object of experience, it can never overstep the limits of sensibility, within which alone objects are presented to us. Its principles are merely principles of the exposition of phenomena, and the proud name of an ontology, which professes to present synthetical cognitions à priori of things in general in a systematic doctrine, must give place to the modest title of analytic of the pure understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"48.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","text":"Thought is the act of referring a given intuition to an object. If the mode of this intuition is unknown to us, the object is merely transcendental, and the conception of the understanding is employed only transcendentally, that is, to produce unity in the thought of a manifold in general. Now a pure category, in which all conditions of sensuous intuition—as the only intuition we possess—are abstracted, does not determine an object, but merely expresses the thought of an object in general, according to different modes. Now, to employ a conception, the function of judgement is required, by which an object is subsumed under the conception, consequently the at least formal condition, under which something can be given in intuition. Failing this condition of judgement (schema), subsumption is impossible; for there is in such a case nothing given, which may be subsumed under the conception. The merely transcendental use of the categories is therefore, in fact, no use at all and has no determined, or even, as regards its form, determinable object. Hence it follows that the pure category is incompetent to establish a synthetical à priori principle, and that the principles of the pure understanding are only of empirical and never of transcendental use, and that beyond the sphere of possible experience no synthetical à priori principles are possible.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"48.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","text":"It may be advisable, therefore, to express ourselves thus. The pure categories, apart from the formal conditions of sensibility, have a merely transcendental meaning, but are nevertheless not of transcendental use, because this is in itself impossible, inasmuch as all the conditions of any employment or use of them (in judgements) are absent, to wit, the formal conditions of the subsumption of an object under these conceptions. As, therefore, in the character of pure categories, they must be employed empirically, and cannot be employed transcendentally, they are of no use at all, when separated from sensibility, that is, they cannot be applied to an object. They are merely the pure form of the employment of the understanding in respect of objects in general and of thought, without its being at the same time possible to think or to determine any object by their means. But there lurks at the foundation of this subject an illusion which it is very difficult to avoid. The categories are not based, as regards their origin, upon sensibility, like the forms of intuition, space, and time; they seem, therefore, to be capable of an application beyond the sphere of sensuous objects. But this is not the case. They are nothing but mere forms of thought, which contain only the logical faculty of uniting à priori in consciousness the manifold given in intuition. Apart, then, from the only intuition possible for us, they have still less meaning than the pure sensuous forms, space and time, for through them an object is at least given, while a mode of connection of the manifold, when the intuition which alone gives the manifold is wanting, has no meaning at all. At the same time, when we designate certain objects as phenomena or sensuous existences, thus distinguishing our mode of intuiting them from their own nature as things in themselves, it is evident that by this very distinction we as it were place the latter, considered in this their own nature, although we do not so intuite them, in opposition to the former, or, on the other hand, we do so place other possible things, which are not objects of our senses, but are cogitated by the understanding alone, and call them intelligible existences (noumena). Now the question arises whether the pure conceptions of our understanding do possess significance in respect of these latter, and may possibly be a mode of cognizing them.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"48.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","text":"But we are met at the very commencement with an ambiguity, which may easily occasion great misapprehension. The understanding, when it terms an object in a certain relation phenomenon, at the same time forms out of this relation a representation or notion of an object in itself, and hence believes that it can form also conceptions of such objects. Now as the understanding possesses no other fundamental conceptions besides the categories, it takes for granted that an object considered as a thing in itself must be capable of being thought by means of these pure conceptions, and is thereby led to hold the perfectly undetermined conception of an intelligible existence, a something out of the sphere of our sensibility, for a determinate conception of an existence which we can cognize in some way or other by means of the understanding.\n\nIf, by the term noumenon, we understand a thing so far as it is not an object of our sensuous intuition, thus making abstraction of our mode of intuiting it, this is a noumenon in the negative sense of the word. But if we understand by it an object of a non-sensuous intuition, we in this case assume a peculiar mode of intuition, an intellectual intuition, to wit, which does not, however, belong to us, of the very possibility of which we have no notion—and this is a noumenon in the positive sense.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"48.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0010","text":"The doctrine of sensibility is also the doctrine of noumena in the negative sense, that is, of things which the understanding is obliged to cogitate apart from any relation to our mode of intuition, consequently not as mere phenomena, but as things in themselves. But the understanding at the same time comprehends that it cannot employ its categories for the consideration of things in themselves, because these possess significance only in relation to the unity of intuitions in space and time, and that they are competent to determine this unity by means of general à priori connecting conceptions only on account of the pure ideality of space and time. Where this unity of time is not to be met with, as is the case with noumena, the whole use, indeed the whole meaning of the categories is entirely lost, for even the possibility of things to correspond to the categories is in this case incomprehensible. On this point, I need only refer the reader to what I have said at the commencement of the General Remark appended to the foregoing chapter. Now, the possibility of a thing can never be proved from the fact that the conception of it is not self-contradictory, but only by means of an intuition corresponding to the conception. If, therefore, we wish to apply the categories to objects which cannot be regarded as phenomena, we must have an intuition different from the sensuous, and in this case the objects would be a noumena in the positive sense of the word. Now, as such an intuition, that is, an intellectual intuition, is no part of our faculty of cognition, it is absolutely impossible for the categories to possess any application beyond the limits of experience. It may be true that there are intelligible existences to which our faculty of sensuous intuition has no relation, and cannot be applied, but our conceptions of the understanding, as mere forms of thought for our sensuous intuition, do not extend to these. What, therefore, we call noumenon must be understood by us as such in a negative sense.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"48.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0011","text":"If I take away from an empirical intuition all thought (by means of the categories), there remains no cognition of any object; for by means of mere intuition nothing is cogitated, and, from the existence of such or such an affection of sensibility in me, it does not follow that this affection or representation has any relation to an object without me. But if I take away all intuition, there still remains the form of thought, that is, the mode of determining an object for the manifold of a possible intuition. Thus the categories do in some measure really extend further than sensuous intuition, inasmuch as they think objects in general, without regard to the mode (of sensibility) in which these objects are given. But they do not for this reason apply to and determine a wider sphere of objects, because we cannot assume that such can be given, without presupposing the possibility of another than the sensuous mode of intuition, a supposition we are not justified in making.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"48.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","text":"I call a conception problematical which contains in itself no contradiction, and which is connected with other cognitions as a limitation of given conceptions, but whose objective reality cannot be cognized in any manner. The conception of a noumenon, that is, of a thing which must be cogitated not as an object of sense, but as a thing in itself (solely through the pure understanding), is not self-contradictory, for we are not entitled to maintain that sensibility is the only possible mode of intuition. Nay, further, this conception is necessary to restrain sensuous intuition within the bounds of phenomena, and thus to limit the objective validity of sensuous cognition; for things in themselves, which lie beyond its province, are called noumena for the very purpose of indicating that this cognition does not extend its application to all that the understanding thinks. But, after all, the possibility of such noumena is quite incomprehensible, and beyond the sphere of phenomena, all is for us a mere void; that is to say, we possess an understanding whose province does problematically extend beyond this sphere, but we do not possess an intuition, indeed, not even the conception of a possible intuition, by means of which objects beyond the region of sensibility could be given us, and in reference to which the understanding might be employed assertorically. The conception of a noumenon is therefore merely a limitative conception and therefore only of negative use. But it is not an arbitrary or fictitious notion, but is connected with the limitation of sensibility, without, however, being capable of presenting us with any positive datum beyond this sphere.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"48.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","text":"The division of objects into phenomena and noumena, and of the world into a mundus sensibilis and intelligibilis is therefore quite inadmissible in a positive sense, although conceptions do certainly admit of such a division; for the class of noumena have no determinate object corresponding to them, and cannot therefore possess objective validity. If we abandon the senses, how can it be made conceivable that the categories (which are the only conceptions that could serve as conceptions for noumena) have any sense or meaning at all, inasmuch as something more than the mere unity of thought, namely, a possible intuition, is requisite for their application to an object? The conception of a noumenon, considered as merely problematical, is, however, not only admissible, but, as a limitative conception of sensibility, absolutely necessary. But, in this case, a noumenon is not a particular intelligible object for our understanding; on the contrary, the kind of understanding to which it could belong is itself a problem, for we cannot form the most distant conception of the possibility of an understanding which should cognize an object, not discursively by means of categories, but intuitively in a non-sensuous intuition. Our understanding attains in this way a sort of negative extension. That is to say, it is not limited by, but rather limits, sensibility, by giving the name of noumena to things, not considered as phenomena, but as things in themselves. But it at the same time prescribes limits to itself, for it confesses itself unable to cognize these by means of the categories, and hence is compelled to cogitate them merely as an unknown something.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"48.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0014","text":"I find, however, in the writings of modern authors, an entirely different use of the expressions, mundus sensibilis and intelligibilis, which quite departs from the meaning of the ancients—an acceptation in which, indeed, there is to be found no difficulty, but which at the same time depends on mere verbal quibbling. According to this meaning, some have chosen to call the complex of phenomena, in so far as it is intuited, mundus sensibilis, but in so far as the connection thereof is cogitated according to general laws of thought, mundus intelligibilis. Astronomy, in so far as we mean by the word the mere observation of the starry heaven, may represent the former; a system of astronomy, such as the Copernican or Newtonian, the latter. But such twisting of words is a mere sophistical subterfuge, to avoid a difficult question, by modifying its meaning to suit our own convenience. To be sure, understanding and reason are employed in the cognition of phenomena; but the question is, whether these can be applied when the object is not a phenomenon and in this sense we regard it if it is cogitated as given to the understanding alone, and not to the senses. The question therefore is whether, over and above the empirical use of the understanding, a transcendental use is possible, which applies to the noumenon as an object. This question we have answered in the negative.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"48.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0015","text":"When therefore we say, the senses represent objects as they appear, the understanding as they are, the latter statement must not be understood in a transcendental, but only in an empirical signification, that is, as they must be represented in the complete connection of phenomena, and not according to what they may be, apart from their relation to possible experience, consequently not as objects of the pure understanding. For this must ever remain unknown to us. Nay, it is also quite unknown to us whether any such transcendental or extraordinary cognition is possible under any circumstances, at least, whether it is possible by means of our categories. Understanding and sensibility, with us, can determine objects only in conjunction. If we separate them, we have intuitions without conceptions, or conceptions without intuitions; in both cases, representations, which we cannot apply to any determinate object.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"48.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0016","text":"If, after all our inquiries and explanations, any one still hesitates to abandon the mere transcendental use of the categories, let him attempt to construct with them a synthetical proposition. It would, of course, be unnecessary for this purpose to construct an analytical proposition, for that does not extend the sphere of the understanding, but, being concerned only about what is cogitated in the conception itself, it leaves it quite undecided whether the conception has any relation to objects, or merely indicates the unity of thought—complete abstraction being made of the modi in which an object may be given: in such a proposition, it is sufficient for the understanding to know what lies in the conception—to what it applies is to it indifferent. The attempt must therefore be made with a synthetical and so-called transcendental principle, for example: “Everything that exists, exists as substance,” or, “Everything that is contingent exists as an effect of some other thing, viz., of its cause.” Now I ask, whence can the understanding draw these synthetical propositions, when the conceptions contained therein do not relate to possible experience but to things in themselves (noumena)? Where is to be found the third term, which is always requisite PURE site in a synthetical proposition, which may connect in the same proposition conceptions which have no logical (analytical) connection with each other? The proposition never will be demonstrated, nay, more, the possibility of any such pure assertion never can be shown, without making reference to the empirical use of the understanding, and thus, ipso facto, completely renouncing pure and non-sensuous judgement. Thus the conception of pure and merely intelligible objects is completely void of all principles of its application, because we cannot imagine any mode in which they might be given, and the problematical thought which leaves a place open for them serves only, like a void space, to limit the use of empirical principles, without containing at the same time any other object of cognition beyond their sphere.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"48.16"}],"sha256":"71fe80728215b32bb51d4be73eeb467668a7803e8c4da355498cdd8cd01eba88","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX","title":"APPENDIX","order":49,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_APPX","text":"### APPENDIX\n\nOf the Equivocal Nature or Amphiboly of the Conceptions of Reflection from the Confusion of the Transcendental with the Empirical use of the Understanding.\n\nReflection (reflexio) is not occupied about objects themselves, for the purpose of directly obtaining conceptions of them, but is that state of the mind in which we set ourselves to discover the subjective conditions under which we obtain conceptions. It is the consciousness of the relation of given representations to the different sources or faculties of cognition, by which alone their relation to each other can be rightly determined. The first question which occurs in considering our representations is to what faculty of cognition do they belong? To the understanding or to the senses? Many judgements are admitted to be true from mere habit or inclination; but, because reflection neither precedes nor follows, it is held to be a judgement that has its origin in the understanding. All judgements do not require examination, that is, investigation into the grounds of their truth. For, when they are immediately certain (for example: “Between two points there can be only one straight line”), no better or less mediate test of their truth can be found than that which they themselves contain and express. But all judgement, nay, all comparisons require reflection, that is, a distinction of the faculty of cognition to which the given conceptions belong. The act whereby I compare my representations with the faculty of cognition which originates them, and whereby I distinguish whether they are compared with each other as belonging to the pure understanding or to sensuous intuition, I term transcendental reflection. Now, the relations in which conceptions can stand to each other are those of identity and difference, agreement and opposition, of the internal and external, finally, of the determinable and the determining (matter and form). The proper determination of these relations rests on the question, to what faculty of cognition they subjectively belong, whether to sensibility or understanding? For, on the manner in which we solve this question depends the manner in which we must cogitate these relations.\n\nBefore constructing any objective judgement, we compare the conceptions that are to be placed in the judgement, and observe whether there exists identity (of many representations in one conception), if a general judgement is to be constructed, or difference, if a particular; whether there is agreement when affirmative; and opposition when negative judgements are to be constructed, and so on. For this reason we ought to call these conceptions, conceptions of comparison (conceptus comparationis). But as, when the question is not as to the logical form, but as to the content of conceptions, that is to say, whether the things themselves are identical or different, in agreement or opposition, and so on, the things can have a twofold relation to our faculty of cognition, to wit, a relation either to sensibility or to the understanding, and as on this relation depends their relation to each other, transcendental reflection, that is, the relation of given representations to one or the other faculty of cognition, can alone determine this latter relation. Thus we shall not be able to discover whether the things are identical or different, in agreement or opposition, etc., from the mere conception of the things by means of comparison (comparatio), but only by distinguishing the mode of cognition to which they belong, in other words, by means of transcendental reflection. We may, therefore, with justice say, that logical reflection is mere comparison, for in it no account is taken of the faculty of cognition to which the given conceptions belong, and they are consequently, as far as regards their origin, to be treated as homogeneous; while transcendental reflection (which applies to the objects themselves) contains the ground of the possibility of objective comparison of representations with each other, and is therefore very different from the former, because the faculties of cognition to which they belong are not even the same. Transcendental reflection is a duty which no one can neglect who wishes to establish an à priori judgement upon things. We shall now proceed to fulfil this duty, and thereby throw not a little light on the question as to the determination of the proper business of the understanding.\n\n1. Identity and Difference. When an object is presented to us several times, but always with the same internal determinations (qualitas et quantitas), it, if an object of pure understanding, is always the same, not several things, but only one thing (numerica identitas); but if a phenomenon, we do not concern ourselves with comparing the conception of the thing with the conception of some other, but, although they may be in this respect perfectly the same, the difference of place at the same time is a sufficient ground for asserting the numerical difference of these objects (of sense). Thus, in the case of two drops of water, we may make complete abstraction of all internal difference (quality and quantity), and, the fact that they are intuited at the same time in different places, is sufficient to justify us in holding them to be numerically different. Leibnitz regarded phenomena as things in themselves, consequently as intelligibilia, that is, objects of pure understanding (although, on account of the confused nature of their representations, he gave them the name of phenomena), and in this case his principle of the indiscernible (principium identatis indiscernibilium) is not to be impugned. But, as phenomena are objects of sensibility, and, as the understanding, in respect of them, must be employed empirically and not purely or transcendentally, plurality and numerical difference are given by space itself as the condition of external phenomena. For one part of space, although it may be perfectly similar and equal to another part, is still without it, and for this reason alone is different from the latter, which is added to it in order to make up a greater space. It follows that this must hold good of all things that are in the different parts of space at the same time, however similar and equal one may be to another.\n\n2. Agreement and Opposition. When reality is represented by the pure understanding (realitas noumenon), opposition between realities is incogitable—such a relation, that is, that when these realities are connected in one subject, they annihilate the effects of each other and may be represented in the formula 3 -3 = 0. On the other hand, the real in a phenomenon (realitas phaenomenon) may very well be in mutual opposition, and, when united in the same subject, the one may completely or in part annihilate the effect or consequence of the other; as in the case of two moving forces in the same straight line drawing or impelling a point in opposite directions, or in the case of a pleasure counterbalancing a certain amount of pain.\n\n3. The Internal and External. In an object of the pure understanding, only that is internal which has no relation (as regards its existence) to anything different from itself. On the other hand, the internal determinations of a substantia phaenomenon in space are nothing but relations, and it is itself nothing more than a complex of mere relations. Substance in space we are cognizant of only through forces operative in it, either drawing others towards itself (attraction), or preventing others from forcing into itself (repulsion and impenetrability). We know no other properties that make up the conception of substance phenomenal in space, and which we term matter. On the other hand, as an object of the pure understanding, every substance must have internal determination and forces. But what other internal attributes of such an object can I think than those which my internal sense presents to me? That, to wit, which in either itself thought, or something analogous to it. Hence Leibnitz, who looked upon things as noumena, after denying them everything like external relation, and therefore also composition or combination, declared that all substances, even the component parts of matter, were simple substances with powers of representation, in one word, monads.\n\n4. Matter and Form. These two conceptions lie at the foundation of all other reflection, so inseparably are they connected with every mode of exercising the understanding. The former denotes the determinable in general, the second its determination, both in a transcendental sense, abstraction being made of every difference in that which is given, and of the mode in which it is determined. Logicians formerly termed the universal, matter, the specific difference of this or that part of the universal, form. In a judgement one may call the given conceptions logical matter (for the judgement), the relation of these to each other (by means of the copula), the form of the judgement. In an object, the composite parts thereof (essentialia) are the matter; the mode in which they are connected in the object, the form. In respect to things in general, unlimited reality was regarded as the matter of all possibility, the limitation thereof (negation) as the form, by which one thing is distinguished from another according to transcendental conceptions. The understanding demands that something be given (at least in the conception), in order to be able to determine it in a certain manner. Hence, in a conception of the pure understanding, the matter precedes the form, and for this reason Leibnitz first assumed the existence of things (monads) and of an internal power of representation in them, in order to found upon this their external relation and the community their state (that is, of their representations). Hence, with him, space and time were possible—the former through the relation of substances, the latter through the connection of their determinations with each other, as causes and effects. And so would it really be, if the pure understanding were capable of an immediate application to objects, and if space and time were determinations of things in themselves. But being merely sensuous intuitions, in which we determine all objects solely as phenomena, the form of intuition (as a subjective property of sensibility) must antecede all matter (sensations), consequently space and time must antecede all phenomena and all data of experience, and rather make experience itself possible. But the intellectual philosopher could not endure that the form should precede the things themselves and determine their possibility; an objection perfectly correct, if we assume that we intuite things as they are, although with confused representation. But as sensuous intuition is a peculiar subjective condition, which is à priori at the foundation of all perception, and the form of which is primitive, the form must be given per se, and so far from matter (or the things themselves which appear) lying at the foundation of experience (as we must conclude, if we judge by mere conceptions), the very possibility of itself presupposes, on the contrary, a given formal intuition (space and time).\n\nREMARK ON THE AMPHIBOLY OF THE CONCEPTIONS OF REFLECTION.\n\nLet me be allowed to term the position which we assign to a conception either in the sensibility or in the pure understanding, the transcendental place. In this manner, the appointment of the position which must be taken by each conception according to the difference in its use, and the directions for determining this place to all conceptions according to rules, would be a transcendental topic, a doctrine which would thoroughly shield us from the surreptitious devices of the pure understanding and the delusions which thence arise, as it would always distinguish to what faculty of cognition each conception properly belonged. Every conception, every title, under which many cognitions rank together, may be called a logical place. Upon this is based the logical topic of Aristotle, of which teachers and rhetoricians could avail themselves, in order, under certain titles of thought, to observe what would best suit the matter they had to treat, and thus enable themselves to quibble and talk with fluency and an appearance of profundity.\n\nTranscendental topic, on the contrary, contains nothing more than the above-mentioned four titles of all comparison and distinction, which differ from categories in this respect, that they do not represent the object according to that which constitutes its conception (quantity, reality), but set forth merely the comparison of representations, which precedes our conceptions of things. But this comparison requires a previous reflection, that is, a determination of the place to which the representations of the things which are compared belong, whether, to wit, they are cogitated by the pure understanding, or given by sensibility.\n\nConceptions may be logically compared without the trouble of inquiring to what faculty their objects belong, whether as noumena, to the understanding, or as phenomena, to sensibility. If, however, we wish to employ these conceptions in respect of objects, previous transcendental reflection is necessary. Without this reflection I should make a very unsafe use of these conceptions, and construct pretended synthetical propositions which critical reason cannot acknowledge and which are based solely upon a transcendental amphiboly, that is, upon a substitution of an object of pure understanding for a phenomenon.\n\nFor want of this doctrine of transcendental topic, and consequently deceived by the amphiboly of the conceptions of reflection, the celebrated Leibnitz constructed an intellectual system of the world, or rather, believed himself competent to cognize the internal nature of things, by comparing all objects merely with the understanding and the abstract formal conceptions of thought. Our table of the conceptions of reflection gives us the unexpected advantage of being able to exhibit the distinctive peculiarities of his system in all its parts, and at the same time of exposing the fundamental principle of this peculiar mode of thought, which rested upon naught but a misconception. He compared all things with each other merely by means of conceptions, and naturally found no other differences than those by which the understanding distinguishes its pure conceptions one from another. The conditions of sensuous intuition, which contain in themselves their own means of distinction, he did not look upon as primitive, because sensibility was to him but a confused mode of representation and not any particular source of representations. A phenomenon was for him the representation of the thing in itself, although distinguished from cognition by the understanding only in respect of the logical form—the former with its usual want of analysis containing, according to him, a certain mixture of collateral representations in its conception of a thing, which it is the duty of the understanding to separate and distinguish. In one word, Leibnitz intellectualized phenomena, just as Locke, in his system of noogony (if I may be allowed to make use of such expressions), sensualized the conceptions of the understanding, that is to say, declared them to be nothing more than empirical or abstract conceptions of reflection. Instead of seeking in the understanding and sensibility two different sources of representations, which, however, can present us with objective judgements of things only in conjunction, each of these great men recognized but one of these faculties, which, in their opinion, applied immediately to things in themselves, the other having no duty but that of confusing or arranging the representations of the former.\n\nAccordingly, the objects of sense were compared by Leibnitz as things in general merely in the understanding.\n\n1st. He compares them in regard to their identity or difference—as judged by the understanding. As, therefore, he considered merely the conceptions of objects, and not their position in intuition, in which alone objects can be given, and left quite out of sight the transcendental locale of these conceptions—whether, that is, their object ought to be classed among phenomena, or among things in themselves, it was to be expected that he should extend the application of the principle of indiscernibles, which is valid solely of conceptions of things in general, to objects of sense (mundus phaenomenon), and that he should believe that he had thereby contributed in no small degree to extend our knowledge of nature. In truth, if I cognize in all its inner determinations a drop of water as a thing in itself, I cannot look upon one drop as different from another, if the conception of the one is completely identical with that of the other. But if it is a phenomenon in space, it has a place not merely in the understanding (among conceptions), but also in sensuous external intuition (in space), and in this case, the physical locale is a matter of indifference in regard to the internal determinations of things, and one place, B, may contain a thing which is perfectly similar and equal to another in a place, A, just as well as if the two things were in every respect different from each other. Difference of place without any other conditions, makes the plurality and distinction of objects as phenomena, not only possible in itself, but even necessary. Consequently, the above so-called law is not a law of nature. It is merely an analytical rule for the comparison of things by means of mere conceptions.\n\n2nd. The principle: “Realities (as simple affirmations) never logically contradict each other,” is a proposition perfectly true respecting the relation of conceptions, but, whether as regards nature, or things in themselves (of which we have not the slightest conception), is without any the least meaning. For real opposition, in which A -B is = 0, exists everywhere, an opposition, that is, in which one reality united with another in the same subject annihilates the effects of the other—a fact which is constantly brought before our eyes by the different antagonistic actions and operations in nature, which, nevertheless, as depending on real forces, must be called realitates phaenomena. General mechanics can even present us with the empirical condition of this opposition in an à priori rule, as it directs its attention to the opposition in the direction of forces—a condition of which the transcendental conception of reality can tell us nothing. Although M. Leibnitz did not announce this proposition with precisely the pomp of a new principle, he yet employed it for the establishment of new propositions, and his followers introduced it into their Leibnitzio-Wolfian system of philosophy. According to this principle, for example, all evils are but consequences of the limited nature of created beings, that is, negations, because these are the only opposite of reality. (In the mere conception of a thing in general this is really the case, but not in things as phenomena.) In like manner, the upholders of this system deem it not only possible, but natural also, to connect and unite all reality in one being, because they acknowledge no other sort of opposition than that of contradiction (by which the conception itself of a thing is annihilated), and find themselves unable to conceive an opposition of reciprocal destruction, so to speak, in which one real cause destroys the effect of another, and the conditions of whose representation we meet with only in sensibility.\n\n3rd. The Leibnitzian monadology has really no better foundation than on this philosopher’s mode of falsely representing the difference of the internal and external solely in relation to the understanding. Substances, in general, must have something inward, which is therefore free from external relations, consequently from that of composition also. The simple—that which can be represented by a unit—is therefore the foundation of that which is internal in things in themselves. The internal state of substances cannot therefore consist in place, shape, contact, or motion, determinations which are all external relations, and we can ascribe to them no other than that whereby we internally determine our faculty of sense itself, that is to say, the state of representation. Thus, then, were constructed the monads, which were to form the elements of the universe, the active force of which consists in representation, the effects of this force being thus entirely confined to themselves.\n\nFor the same reason, his view of the possible community of substances could not represent it but as a predetermined harmony, and by no means as a physical influence. For inasmuch as everything is occupied only internally, that is, with its own representations, the state of the representations of one substance could not stand in active and living connection with that of another, but some third cause operating on all without exception was necessary to make the different states correspond with one another. And this did not happen by means of assistance applied in each particular case (systema assistentiae), but through the unity of the idea of a cause occupied and connected with all substances, in which they necessarily receive, according to the Leibnitzian school, their existence and permanence, consequently also reciprocal correspondence, according to universal laws.\n\n4th. This philosopher’s celebrated doctrine of space and time, in which he intellectualized these forms of sensibility, originated in the same delusion of transcendental reflection. If I attempt to represent by the mere understanding, the external relations of things, I can do so only by employing the conception of their reciprocal action, and if I wish to connect one state of the same thing with another state, I must avail myself of the notion of the order of cause and effect. And thus Leibnitz regarded space as a certain order in the community of substances, and time as the dynamical sequence of their states. That which space and time possess proper to themselves and independent of things, he ascribed to a necessary confusion in our conceptions of them, whereby that which is a mere form of dynamical relations is held to be a self-existent intuition, antecedent even to things themselves. Thus space and time were the intelligible form of the connection of things (substances and their states) in themselves. But things were intelligible substances (substantiae noumena). At the same time, he made these conceptions valid of phenomena, because he did not allow to sensibility a peculiar mode of intuition, but sought all, even the empirical representation of objects, in the understanding, and left to sense naught but the despicable task of confusing and disarranging the representations of the former.\n\nBut even if we could frame any synthetical proposition concerning things in themselves by means of the pure understanding (which is impossible), it could not apply to phenomena, which do not represent things in themselves. In such a case I should be obliged in transcendental reflection to compare my conceptions only under the conditions of sensibility, and so space and time would not be determinations of things in themselves, but of phenomena. What things may be in themselves, I know not and need not know, because a thing is never presented to me otherwise than as a phenomenon.\n\nI must adopt the same mode of procedure with the other conceptions of reflection. Matter is substantia phaenomenon. That in it which is internal I seek to discover in all parts of space which it occupies, and in all the functions and operations it performs, and which are indeed never anything but phenomena of the external sense. I cannot therefore find anything that is absolutely, but only what is comparatively internal, and which itself consists of external relations. The absolutely internal in matter, and as it should be according to the pure understanding, is a mere chimera, for matter is not an object for the pure understanding. But the transcendental object, which is the foundation of the phenomenon which we call matter, is a mere nescio quid, the nature of which we could not understand, even though someone were found able to tell us. For we can understand nothing that does not bring with it something in intuition corresponding to the expressions employed. If, by the complaint of being unable to perceive the internal nature of things, it is meant that we do not comprehend by the pure understanding what the things which appear to us may be in themselves, it is a silly and unreasonable complaint; for those who talk thus really desire that we should be able to cognize, consequently to intuite, things without senses, and therefore wish that we possessed a faculty of cognition perfectly different from the human faculty, not merely in degree, but even as regards intuition and the mode thereof, so that thus we should not be men, but belong to a class of beings, the possibility of whose existence, much less their nature and constitution, we have no means of cognizing. By observation and analysis of phenomena we penetrate into the interior of nature, and no one can say what progress this knowledge may make in time. But those transcendental questions which pass beyond the limits of nature, we could never answer, even although all nature were laid open to us, because we have not the power of observing our own mind with any other intuition than that of our internal sense. For herein lies the mystery of the origin and source of our faculty of sensibility. Its application to an object, and the transcendental ground of this unity of subjective and objective, lie too deeply concealed for us, who cognize ourselves only through the internal sense, consequently as phenomena, to be able to discover in our existence anything but phenomena, the non-sensuous cause of which we at the same time earnestly desire to penetrate to.\n\nThe great utility of this critique of conclusions arrived at by the processes of mere reflection consists in its clear demonstration of the nullity of all conclusions respecting objects which are compared with each other in the understanding alone, while it at the same time confirms what we particularly insisted on, namely, that, although phenomena are not included as things in themselves among the objects of the pure understanding, they are nevertheless the only things by which our cognition can possess objective reality, that is to say, which give us intuitions to correspond with our conceptions.\n\nWhen we reflect in a purely logical manner, we do nothing more than compare conceptions in our understanding, to discover whether both have the same content, whether they are self-contradictory or not, whether anything is contained in either conception, which of the two is given, and which is merely a mode of thinking that given. But if I apply these conceptions to an object in general (in the transcendental sense), without first determining whether it is an object of sensuous or intellectual intuition, certain limitations present themselves, which forbid us to pass beyond the conceptions and render all empirical use of them impossible. And thus these limitations prove that the representation of an object as a thing in general is not only insufficient, but, without sensuous determination and independently of empirical conditions, self-contradictory; that we must therefore make abstraction of all objects, as in logic, or, admitting them, must think them under conditions of sensuous intuition; that, consequently, the intelligible requires an altogether peculiar intuition, which we do not possess, and in the absence of which it is for us nothing; while, on the other hand phenomena cannot be objects in themselves. For, when I merely think things in general, the difference in their external relations cannot constitute a difference in the things themselves; on the contrary, the former presupposes the latter, and if the conception of one of two things is not internally different from that of the other, I am merely thinking the same thing in different relations. Further, by the addition of one affirmation (reality) to the other, the positive therein is really augmented, and nothing is abstracted or withdrawn from it; hence the real in things cannot be in contradiction with or opposition to itself—and so on.\n\nThe true use of the conceptions of reflection in the employment of the understanding has, as we have shown, been so misconceived by Leibnitz, one of the most acute philosophers of either ancient or modern times, that he has been misled into the construction of a baseless system of intellectual cognition, which professes to determine its objects without the intervention of the senses. For this reason, the exposition of the cause of the amphiboly of these conceptions, as the origin of these false principles, is of great utility in determining with certainty the proper limits of the understanding.\n\nIt is right to say whatever is affirmed or denied of the whole of a conception can be affirmed or denied of any part of it (dictum de omni et nullo); but it would be absurd so to alter this logical proposition as to say whatever is not contained in a general conception is likewise not contained in the particular conceptions which rank under it; for the latter are particular conceptions, for the very reason that their content is greater than that which is cogitated in the general conception. And yet the whole intellectual system of Leibnitz is based upon this false principle, and with it must necessarily fall to the ground, together with all the ambiguous principles in reference to the employment of the understanding which have thence originated.\n\nLeibnitz’s principle of the identity of indiscernibles or indistinguishables is really based on the presupposition that, if in the conception of a thing a certain distinction is not to be found, it is also not to be met with in things themselves; that, consequently, all things are completely identical (numero eadem) which are not distinguishable from each other (as to quality or quantity) in our conceptions of them. But, as in the mere conception of anything abstraction has been made of many necessary conditions of intuition, that of which abstraction has been made is rashly held to be non-existent, and nothing is attributed to the thing but what is contained in its conception.\n\nThe conception of a cubic foot of space, however I may think it, is in itself completely identical. But two cubic feet in space are nevertheless distinct from each other from the sole fact of their being in different places (they are numero diversa); and these places are conditions of intuition, wherein the object of this conception is given, and which do not belong to the conception, but to the faculty of sensibility. In like manner, there is in the conception of a thing no contradiction when a negative is not connected with an affirmative; and merely affirmative conceptions cannot, in conjunction, produce any negation. But in sensuous intuition, wherein reality (take for example, motion) is given, we find conditions (opposite directions)—of which abstraction has been made in the conception of motion in general—which render possible a contradiction or opposition (not indeed of a logical kind)—and which from pure positives produce zero = 0. We are therefore not justified in saying that all reality is in perfect agreement and harmony, because no contradiction is discoverable among its conceptions.[[39]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-39) According to mere conceptions, that which is internal is the substratum of all relations or external determinations. When, therefore, I abstract all conditions of intuition, and confine myself solely to the conception of a thing in general, I can make abstraction of all external relations, and there must nevertheless remain a conception of that which indicates no relation, but merely internal determinations. Now it seems to follow that in everything (substance) there is something which is absolutely internal and which antecedes all external determinations, inasmuch as it renders them possible; and that therefore this substratum is something which does not contain any external relations and is consequently simple (for corporeal things are never anything but relations, at least of their parts external to each other); and, inasmuch as we know of no other absolutely internal determinations than those of the internal sense, this substratum is not only simple, but also, analogously with our internal sense, determined through representations, that is to say, all things are properly monads, or simple beings endowed with the power of representation. Now all this would be perfectly correct, if the conception of a thing were the only necessary condition of the presentation of objects of external intuition. It is, on the contrary, manifest that a permanent phenomenon in space (impenetrable extension) can contain mere relations, and nothing that is absolutely internal, and yet be the primary substratum of all external perception. By mere conceptions I cannot think anything external, without, at the same time, thinking something internal, for the reason that conceptions of relations presuppose given things, and without these are impossible. But, as an intuition there is something (that is, space, which, with all it contains, consists of purely formal, or, indeed, real relations) which is not found in the mere conception of a thing in general, and this presents to us the substratum which could not be cognized through conceptions alone, I cannot say: because a thing cannot be represented by mere conceptions without something absolutely internal, there is also, in the things themselves which are contained under these conceptions, and in their intuition nothing external to which something absolutely internal does not serve as the foundation. For, when we have made abstraction of all the conditions of intuition, there certainly remains in the mere conception nothing but the internal in general, through which alone the external is possible. But this necessity, which is grounded upon abstraction alone, does not obtain in the case of things themselves, in so far as they are given in intuition with such determinations as express mere relations, without having anything internal as their foundation; for they are not things in themselves, but only phenomena. What we cognize in matter is nothing but relations (what we call its internal determinations are but comparatively internal). But there are some self-subsistent and permanent, through which a determined object is given. That I, when abstraction is made of these relations, have nothing more to think, does not destroy the conception of a thing as phenomenon, nor the conception of an object in abstracto, but it does away with the possibility of an object that is determinable according to mere conceptions, that is, of a noumenon. It is certainly startling to hear that a thing consists solely of relations; but this thing is simply a phenomenon, and cannot be cogitated by means of the mere categories: it does itself consist in the mere relation of something in general to the senses. In the same way, we cannot cogitate relations of things in abstracto, if we commence with conceptions alone, in any other manner than that one is the cause of determinations in the other; for that is itself the conception of the understanding or category of relation. But, as in this case we make abstraction of all intuition, we lose altogether the mode in which the manifold determines to each of its parts its place, that is, the form of sensibility (space); and yet this mode antecedes all empirical causality.\n\n[[39]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-39) If any one wishes here to have recourse to the usual subterfuge, and to say, that at least realitates noumena cannot be in opposition to each other, it will be requisite for him to adduce an example of this pure and non-sensuous reality, that it may be understood whether the notion represents something or nothing. But an example cannot be found except in experience, which never presents to us anything more than phenomena; and thus the proposition means nothing more than that the conception which contains only affirmatives does not contain anything negative—a proposition nobody ever doubted.\n\nIf by intelligible objects we understand things which can be thought by means of the pure categories, without the need of the schemata of sensibility, such objects are impossible. For the condition of the objective use of all our conceptions of understanding is the mode of our sensuous intuition, whereby objects are given; and, if we make abstraction of the latter, the former can have no relation to an object. And even if we should suppose a different kind of intuition from our own, still our functions of thought would have no use or signification in respect thereof. But if we understand by the term, objects of a non-sensuous intuition, in respect of which our categories are not valid, and of which we can accordingly have no knowledge (neither intuition nor conception), in this merely negative sense noumena must be admitted. For this is no more than saying that our mode of intuition is not applicable to all things, but only to objects of our senses, that consequently its objective validity is limited, and that room is therefore left for another kind of intuition, and thus also for things that may be objects of it. But in this sense the conception of a noumenon is problematical, that is to say, it is the notion of a thing of which we can neither say that it is possible, nor that it is impossible, inasmuch as we do not know of any mode of intuition besides the sensuous, or of any other sort of conceptions than the categories—a mode of intuition and a kind of conception neither of which is applicable to a non-sensuous object. We are on this account incompetent to extend the sphere of our objects of thought beyond the conditions of our sensibility, and to assume the existence of objects of pure thought, that is, of noumena, inasmuch as these have no true positive signification. For it must be confessed of the categories that they are not of themselves sufficient for the cognition of things in themselves and, without the data of sensibility, are mere subjective forms of the unity of the understanding. Thought is certainly not a product of the senses, and in so far is not limited by them, but it does not therefore follow that it may be employed purely and without the intervention of sensibility, for it would then be without reference to an object. And we cannot call a noumenon an object of pure thought; for the representation thereof is but the problematical conception of an object for a perfectly different intuition and a perfectly different understanding from ours, both of which are consequently themselves problematical. The conception of a noumenon is therefore not the conception of an object, but merely a problematical conception inseparably connected with the limitation of our sensibility. That is to say, this conception contains the answer to the question: “Are there objects quite unconnected with, and independent of, our intuition?”—a question to which only an indeterminate answer can be given. That answer is: “Inasmuch as sensuous intuition does not apply to all things without distinction, there remains room for other and different objects.” The existence of these problematical objects is therefore not absolutely denied, in the absence of a determinate conception of them, but, as no category is valid in respect of them, neither must they be admitted as objects for our understanding.\n\nUnderstanding accordingly limits sensibility, without at the same time enlarging its own field. While, moreover, it forbids sensibility to apply its forms and modes to things in themselves and restricts it to the sphere of phenomena, it cogitates an object in itself, only, however, as a transcendental object, which is the cause of a phenomenon (consequently not itself a phenomenon), and which cannot be thought either as a quantity or as reality, or as substance (because these conceptions always require sensuous forms in which to determine an object)—an object, therefore, of which we are quite unable to say whether it can be met with in ourselves or out of us, whether it would be annihilated together with sensibility, or, if this were taken away, would continue to exist. If we wish to call this object a noumenon, because the representation of it is non-sensuous, we are at liberty to do so. But as we can apply to it none of the conceptions of our understanding, the representation is for us quite void, and is available only for the indication of the limits of our sensuous intuition, thereby leaving at the same time an empty space, which we are competent to fill by the aid neither of possible experience, nor of the pure understanding.\n\nThe critique of the pure understanding, accordingly, does not permit us to create for ourselves a new field of objects beyond those which are presented to us as phenomena, and to stray into intelligible worlds; nay, it does not even allow us to endeavour to form so much as a conception of them. The specious error which leads to this—and which is a perfectly excusable one—lies in the fact that the employment of the understanding, contrary to its proper purpose and destination, is made transcendental, and objects, that is, possible intuitions, are made to regulate themselves according to conceptions, instead of the conceptions arranging themselves according to the intuitions, on which alone their own objective validity rests. Now the reason of this again is that apperception, and with it thought, antecedes all possible determinate arrangement of representations. Accordingly we think something in general and determine it on the one hand sensuously, but, on the other, distinguish the general and in abstracto represented object from this particular mode of intuiting it. In this case there remains a mode of determining the object by mere thought, which is really but a logical form without content, which, however, seems to us to be a mode of the existence of the object in itself (noumenon), without regard to intuition which is limited to our senses.\n\nBefore ending this transcendental analytic, we must make an addition, which, although in itself of no particular importance, seems to be necessary to the completeness of the system. The highest conception, with which a transcendental philosophy commonly begins, is the division into possible and impossible. But as all division presupposes a divided conception, a still higher one must exist, and this is the conception of an object in general—problematically understood and without its being decided whether it is something or nothing. As the categories are the only conceptions which apply to objects in general, the distinguishing of an object, whether it is something or nothing, must proceed according to the order and direction of the categories.\n\n1. To the categories of quantity, that is, the conceptions of all, many, and one, the conception which annihilates all, that is, the conception of none, is opposed. And thus the object of a conception, to which no intuition can be found to correspond, is = nothing. That is, it is a conception without an object (ens rationis), like noumena, which cannot be considered possible in the sphere of reality, though they must not therefore be held to be impossible—or like certain new fundamental forces in matter, the existence of which is cogitable without contradiction, though, as examples from experience are not forthcoming, they must not be regarded as possible.\n\n2. Reality is something; negation is nothing, that is, a conception of the absence of an object, as cold, a shadow (nihil privativum).\n\n3. The mere form of intuition, without substance, is in itself no object, but the merely formal condition of an object (as phenomenon), as pure space and pure time. These are certainly something, as forms of intuition, but are not themselves objects which are intuited (ens imaginarium).\n\n4. The object of a conception which is self-contradictory, is nothing, because the conception is nothing—is impossible, as a figure composed of two straight lines (nihil negativum).\n\nThe table of this division of the conception of nothing (the corresponding division of the conception of something does not require special description) must therefore be arranged as follows:\n\n                      NOTHING\n                        AS\n\n                        1\n                As Empty Conception\n                 without object,\n                  _ens rationis_\n           2                               3\n     Empty object of               Empty intuition\n      a conception,                without object,\n     _nihil privativum              ens imaginarium_\n                        4\n                   Empty object\n                 without conception,\n                  _nihil negativum_\n\nWe see that the ens rationis is distinguished from the nihil negativum or pure nothing by the consideration that the former must not be reckoned among possibilities, because it is a mere fiction—though not self-contradictory, while the latter is completely opposed to all possibility, inasmuch as the conception annihilates itself. Both, however, are empty conceptions. On the other hand, the nihil privativum and ens imaginarium are empty data for conceptions. If light be not given to the senses, we cannot represent to ourselves darkness, and if extended objects are not perceived, we cannot represent space. Neither the negation, nor the mere form of intuition can, without something real, be an object.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0001","text":"### APPENDIX\n\nOf the Equivocal Nature or Amphiboly of the Conceptions of Reflection from the Confusion of the Transcendental with the Empirical use of the Understanding.\n\nReflection (reflexio) is not occupied about objects themselves, for the purpose of directly obtaining conceptions of them, but is that state of the mind in which we set ourselves to discover the subjective conditions under which we obtain conceptions. It is the consciousness of the relation of given representations to the different sources or faculties of cognition, by which alone their relation to each other can be rightly determined. The first question which occurs in considering our representations is to what faculty of cognition do they belong? To the understanding or to the senses? Many judgements are admitted to be true from mere habit or inclination; but, because reflection neither precedes nor follows, it is held to be a judgement that has its origin in the understanding. All judgements do not require examination, that is, investigation into the grounds of their truth. For, when they are immediately certain (for example: “Between two points there can be only one straight line”), no better or less mediate test of their truth can be found than that which they themselves contain and express. But all judgement, nay, all comparisons require reflection, that is, a distinction of the faculty of cognition to which the given conceptions belong. The act whereby I compare my representations with the faculty of cognition which originates them, and whereby I distinguish whether they are compared with each other as belonging to the pure understanding or to sensuous intuition, I term transcendental reflection. Now, the relations in which conceptions can stand to each other are those of identity and difference, agreement and opposition, of the internal and external, finally, of the determinable and the determining (matter and form). The proper determination of these relations rests on the question, to what faculty of cognition they subjectively belong, whether to sensibility or understanding? For, on the manner in which we solve this question depends the manner in which we must cogitate these relations.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"49.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","text":"Before constructing any objective judgement, we compare the conceptions that are to be placed in the judgement, and observe whether there exists identity (of many representations in one conception), if a general judgement is to be constructed, or difference, if a particular; whether there is agreement when affirmative; and opposition when negative judgements are to be constructed, and so on. For this reason we ought to call these conceptions, conceptions of comparison (conceptus comparationis). But as, when the question is not as to the logical form, but as to the content of conceptions, that is to say, whether the things themselves are identical or different, in agreement or opposition, and so on, the things can have a twofold relation to our faculty of cognition, to wit, a relation either to sensibility or to the understanding, and as on this relation depends their relation to each other, transcendental reflection, that is, the relation of given representations to one or the other faculty of cognition, can alone determine this latter relation. Thus we shall not be able to discover whether the things are identical or different, in agreement or opposition, etc., from the mere conception of the things by means of comparison (comparatio), but only by distinguishing the mode of cognition to which they belong, in other words, by means of transcendental reflection. We may, therefore, with justice say, that logical reflection is mere comparison, for in it no account is taken of the faculty of cognition to which the given conceptions belong, and they are consequently, as far as regards their origin, to be treated as homogeneous; while transcendental reflection (which applies to the objects themselves) contains the ground of the possibility of objective comparison of representations with each other, and is therefore very different from the former, because the faculties of cognition to which they belong are not even the same. Transcendental reflection is a duty which no one can neglect who wishes to establish an à priori judgement upon things. We shall now proceed to fulfil this duty, and thereby throw not a little light on the question as to the determination of the proper business of the understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"49.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0003","text":"1. Identity and Difference. When an object is presented to us several times, but always with the same internal determinations (qualitas et quantitas), it, if an object of pure understanding, is always the same, not several things, but only one thing (numerica identitas); but if a phenomenon, we do not concern ourselves with comparing the conception of the thing with the conception of some other, but, although they may be in this respect perfectly the same, the difference of place at the same time is a sufficient ground for asserting the numerical difference of these objects (of sense). Thus, in the case of two drops of water, we may make complete abstraction of all internal difference (quality and quantity), and, the fact that they are intuited at the same time in different places, is sufficient to justify us in holding them to be numerically different. Leibnitz regarded phenomena as things in themselves, consequently as intelligibilia, that is, objects of pure understanding (although, on account of the confused nature of their representations, he gave them the name of phenomena), and in this case his principle of the indiscernible (principium identatis indiscernibilium) is not to be impugned. But, as phenomena are objects of sensibility, and, as the understanding, in respect of them, must be employed empirically and not purely or transcendentally, plurality and numerical difference are given by space itself as the condition of external phenomena. For one part of space, although it may be perfectly similar and equal to another part, is still without it, and for this reason alone is different from the latter, which is added to it in order to make up a greater space. It follows that this must hold good of all things that are in the different parts of space at the same time, however similar and equal one may be to another.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"49.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0004","text":"2. Agreement and Opposition. When reality is represented by the pure understanding (realitas noumenon), opposition between realities is incogitable—such a relation, that is, that when these realities are connected in one subject, they annihilate the effects of each other and may be represented in the formula 3 -3 = 0. On the other hand, the real in a phenomenon (realitas phaenomenon) may very well be in mutual opposition, and, when united in the same subject, the one may completely or in part annihilate the effect or consequence of the other; as in the case of two moving forces in the same straight line drawing or impelling a point in opposite directions, or in the case of a pleasure counterbalancing a certain amount of pain.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"49.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0005","text":"3. The Internal and External. In an object of the pure understanding, only that is internal which has no relation (as regards its existence) to anything different from itself. On the other hand, the internal determinations of a substantia phaenomenon in space are nothing but relations, and it is itself nothing more than a complex of mere relations. Substance in space we are cognizant of only through forces operative in it, either drawing others towards itself (attraction), or preventing others from forcing into itself (repulsion and impenetrability). We know no other properties that make up the conception of substance phenomenal in space, and which we term matter. On the other hand, as an object of the pure understanding, every substance must have internal determination and forces. But what other internal attributes of such an object can I think than those which my internal sense presents to me? That, to wit, which in either itself thought, or something analogous to it. Hence Leibnitz, who looked upon things as noumena, after denying them everything like external relation, and therefore also composition or combination, declared that all substances, even the component parts of matter, were simple substances with powers of representation, in one word, monads.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"49.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0006","text":"4. Matter and Form. These two conceptions lie at the foundation of all other reflection, so inseparably are they connected with every mode of exercising the understanding. The former denotes the determinable in general, the second its determination, both in a transcendental sense, abstraction being made of every difference in that which is given, and of the mode in which it is determined. Logicians formerly termed the universal, matter, the specific difference of this or that part of the universal, form. In a judgement one may call the given conceptions logical matter (for the judgement), the relation of these to each other (by means of the copula), the form of the judgement. In an object, the composite parts thereof (essentialia) are the matter; the mode in which they are connected in the object, the form. In respect to things in general, unlimited reality was regarded as the matter of all possibility, the limitation thereof (negation) as the form, by which one thing is distinguished from another according to transcendental conceptions. The understanding demands that something be given (at least in the conception), in order to be able to determine it in a certain manner. Hence, in a conception of the pure understanding, the matter precedes the form, and for this reason Leibnitz first assumed the existence of things (monads) and of an internal power of representation in them, in order to found upon this their external relation and the community their state (that is, of their representations). Hence, with him, space and time were possible—the former through the relation of substances, the latter through the connection of their determinations with each other, as causes and effects. And so would it really be, if the pure understanding were capable of an immediate application to objects, and if space and time were determinations of things in themselves. But being merely sensuous intuitions, in which we determine all objects solely as phenomena, the form of intuition (as a subjective property of sensibility) must antecede all matter (sensations), consequently space and time must antecede all phenomena and all data of experience, and rather make experience itself possible. But the intellectual philosopher could not endure that the form should precede the things themselves and determine their possibility; an objection perfectly correct, if we assume that we intuite things as they are, although with confused representation. But as sensuous intuition is a peculiar subjective condition, which is à priori at the foundation of all perception, and the form of which is primitive, the form must be given per se, and so far from matter (or the things themselves which appear) lying at the foundation of experience (as we must conclude, if we judge by mere conceptions), the very possibility of itself presupposes, on the contrary, a given formal intuition (space and time).","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"49.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0007","text":"REMARK ON THE AMPHIBOLY OF THE CONCEPTIONS OF REFLECTION.\n\nLet me be allowed to term the position which we assign to a conception either in the sensibility or in the pure understanding, the transcendental place. In this manner, the appointment of the position which must be taken by each conception according to the difference in its use, and the directions for determining this place to all conceptions according to rules, would be a transcendental topic, a doctrine which would thoroughly shield us from the surreptitious devices of the pure understanding and the delusions which thence arise, as it would always distinguish to what faculty of cognition each conception properly belonged. Every conception, every title, under which many cognitions rank together, may be called a logical place. Upon this is based the logical topic of Aristotle, of which teachers and rhetoricians could avail themselves, in order, under certain titles of thought, to observe what would best suit the matter they had to treat, and thus enable themselves to quibble and talk with fluency and an appearance of profundity.\n\nTranscendental topic, on the contrary, contains nothing more than the above-mentioned four titles of all comparison and distinction, which differ from categories in this respect, that they do not represent the object according to that which constitutes its conception (quantity, reality), but set forth merely the comparison of representations, which precedes our conceptions of things. But this comparison requires a previous reflection, that is, a determination of the place to which the representations of the things which are compared belong, whether, to wit, they are cogitated by the pure understanding, or given by sensibility.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"49.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0008","text":"Conceptions may be logically compared without the trouble of inquiring to what faculty their objects belong, whether as noumena, to the understanding, or as phenomena, to sensibility. If, however, we wish to employ these conceptions in respect of objects, previous transcendental reflection is necessary. Without this reflection I should make a very unsafe use of these conceptions, and construct pretended synthetical propositions which critical reason cannot acknowledge and which are based solely upon a transcendental amphiboly, that is, upon a substitution of an object of pure understanding for a phenomenon.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"49.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","text":"For want of this doctrine of transcendental topic, and consequently deceived by the amphiboly of the conceptions of reflection, the celebrated Leibnitz constructed an intellectual system of the world, or rather, believed himself competent to cognize the internal nature of things, by comparing all objects merely with the understanding and the abstract formal conceptions of thought. Our table of the conceptions of reflection gives us the unexpected advantage of being able to exhibit the distinctive peculiarities of his system in all its parts, and at the same time of exposing the fundamental principle of this peculiar mode of thought, which rested upon naught but a misconception. He compared all things with each other merely by means of conceptions, and naturally found no other differences than those by which the understanding distinguishes its pure conceptions one from another. The conditions of sensuous intuition, which contain in themselves their own means of distinction, he did not look upon as primitive, because sensibility was to him but a confused mode of representation and not any particular source of representations. A phenomenon was for him the representation of the thing in itself, although distinguished from cognition by the understanding only in respect of the logical form—the former with its usual want of analysis containing, according to him, a certain mixture of collateral representations in its conception of a thing, which it is the duty of the understanding to separate and distinguish. In one word, Leibnitz intellectualized phenomena, just as Locke, in his system of noogony (if I may be allowed to make use of such expressions), sensualized the conceptions of the understanding, that is to say, declared them to be nothing more than empirical or abstract conceptions of reflection. Instead of seeking in the understanding and sensibility two different sources of representations, which, however, can present us with objective judgements of things only in conjunction, each of these great men recognized but one of these faculties, which, in their opinion, applied immediately to things in themselves, the other having no duty but that of confusing or arranging the representations of the former.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"49.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0010","text":"Accordingly, the objects of sense were compared by Leibnitz as things in general merely in the understanding.\n\n1st. He compares them in regard to their identity or difference—as judged by the understanding. As, therefore, he considered merely the conceptions of objects, and not their position in intuition, in which alone objects can be given, and left quite out of sight the transcendental locale of these conceptions—whether, that is, their object ought to be classed among phenomena, or among things in themselves, it was to be expected that he should extend the application of the principle of indiscernibles, which is valid solely of conceptions of things in general, to objects of sense (mundus phaenomenon), and that he should believe that he had thereby contributed in no small degree to extend our knowledge of nature. In truth, if I cognize in all its inner determinations a drop of water as a thing in itself, I cannot look upon one drop as different from another, if the conception of the one is completely identical with that of the other. But if it is a phenomenon in space, it has a place not merely in the understanding (among conceptions), but also in sensuous external intuition (in space), and in this case, the physical locale is a matter of indifference in regard to the internal determinations of things, and one place, B, may contain a thing which is perfectly similar and equal to another in a place, A, just as well as if the two things were in every respect different from each other. Difference of place without any other conditions, makes the plurality and distinction of objects as phenomena, not only possible in itself, but even necessary. Consequently, the above so-called law is not a law of nature. It is merely an analytical rule for the comparison of things by means of mere conceptions.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"49.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0011","text":"2nd. The principle: “Realities (as simple affirmations) never logically contradict each other,” is a proposition perfectly true respecting the relation of conceptions, but, whether as regards nature, or things in themselves (of which we have not the slightest conception), is without any the least meaning. For real opposition, in which A -B is = 0, exists everywhere, an opposition, that is, in which one reality united with another in the same subject annihilates the effects of the other—a fact which is constantly brought before our eyes by the different antagonistic actions and operations in nature, which, nevertheless, as depending on real forces, must be called realitates phaenomena. General mechanics can even present us with the empirical condition of this opposition in an à priori rule, as it directs its attention to the opposition in the direction of forces—a condition of which the transcendental conception of reality can tell us nothing. Although M. Leibnitz did not announce this proposition with precisely the pomp of a new principle, he yet employed it for the establishment of new propositions, and his followers introduced it into their Leibnitzio-Wolfian system of philosophy. According to this principle, for example, all evils are but consequences of the limited nature of created beings, that is, negations, because these are the only opposite of reality. (In the mere conception of a thing in general this is really the case, but not in things as phenomena.) In like manner, the upholders of this system deem it not only possible, but natural also, to connect and unite all reality in one being, because they acknowledge no other sort of opposition than that of contradiction (by which the conception itself of a thing is annihilated), and find themselves unable to conceive an opposition of reciprocal destruction, so to speak, in which one real cause destroys the effect of another, and the conditions of whose representation we meet with only in sensibility.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"49.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0012","text":"3rd. The Leibnitzian monadology has really no better foundation than on this philosopher’s mode of falsely representing the difference of the internal and external solely in relation to the understanding. Substances, in general, must have something inward, which is therefore free from external relations, consequently from that of composition also. The simple—that which can be represented by a unit—is therefore the foundation of that which is internal in things in themselves. The internal state of substances cannot therefore consist in place, shape, contact, or motion, determinations which are all external relations, and we can ascribe to them no other than that whereby we internally determine our faculty of sense itself, that is to say, the state of representation. Thus, then, were constructed the monads, which were to form the elements of the universe, the active force of which consists in representation, the effects of this force being thus entirely confined to themselves.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"49.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0013","text":"For the same reason, his view of the possible community of substances could not represent it but as a predetermined harmony, and by no means as a physical influence. For inasmuch as everything is occupied only internally, that is, with its own representations, the state of the representations of one substance could not stand in active and living connection with that of another, but some third cause operating on all without exception was necessary to make the different states correspond with one another. And this did not happen by means of assistance applied in each particular case (systema assistentiae), but through the unity of the idea of a cause occupied and connected with all substances, in which they necessarily receive, according to the Leibnitzian school, their existence and permanence, consequently also reciprocal correspondence, according to universal laws.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"49.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0014","text":"4th. This philosopher’s celebrated doctrine of space and time, in which he intellectualized these forms of sensibility, originated in the same delusion of transcendental reflection. If I attempt to represent by the mere understanding, the external relations of things, I can do so only by employing the conception of their reciprocal action, and if I wish to connect one state of the same thing with another state, I must avail myself of the notion of the order of cause and effect. And thus Leibnitz regarded space as a certain order in the community of substances, and time as the dynamical sequence of their states. That which space and time possess proper to themselves and independent of things, he ascribed to a necessary confusion in our conceptions of them, whereby that which is a mere form of dynamical relations is held to be a self-existent intuition, antecedent even to things themselves. Thus space and time were the intelligible form of the connection of things (substances and their states) in themselves. But things were intelligible substances (substantiae noumena). At the same time, he made these conceptions valid of phenomena, because he did not allow to sensibility a peculiar mode of intuition, but sought all, even the empirical representation of objects, in the understanding, and left to sense naught but the despicable task of confusing and disarranging the representations of the former.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"49.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0015","text":"But even if we could frame any synthetical proposition concerning things in themselves by means of the pure understanding (which is impossible), it could not apply to phenomena, which do not represent things in themselves. In such a case I should be obliged in transcendental reflection to compare my conceptions only under the conditions of sensibility, and so space and time would not be determinations of things in themselves, but of phenomena. What things may be in themselves, I know not and need not know, because a thing is never presented to me otherwise than as a phenomenon.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"49.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0016","text":"I must adopt the same mode of procedure with the other conceptions of reflection. Matter is substantia phaenomenon. That in it which is internal I seek to discover in all parts of space which it occupies, and in all the functions and operations it performs, and which are indeed never anything but phenomena of the external sense. I cannot therefore find anything that is absolutely, but only what is comparatively internal, and which itself consists of external relations. The absolutely internal in matter, and as it should be according to the pure understanding, is a mere chimera, for matter is not an object for the pure understanding. But the transcendental object, which is the foundation of the phenomenon which we call matter, is a mere nescio quid, the nature of which we could not understand, even though someone were found able to tell us. For we can understand nothing that does not bring with it something in intuition corresponding to the expressions employed. If, by the complaint of being unable to perceive the internal nature of things, it is meant that we do not comprehend by the pure understanding what the things which appear to us may be in themselves, it is a silly and unreasonable complaint; for those who talk thus really desire that we should be able to cognize, consequently to intuite, things without senses, and therefore wish that we possessed a faculty of cognition perfectly different from the human faculty, not merely in degree, but even as regards intuition and the mode thereof, so that thus we should not be men, but belong to a class of beings, the possibility of whose existence, much less their nature and constitution, we have no means of cognizing. By observation and analysis of phenomena we penetrate into the interior of nature, and no one can say what progress this knowledge may make in time. But those transcendental questions which pass beyond the limits of nature, we could never answer, even although all nature were laid open to us, because we have not the power of observing our own mind with any other intuition than that of our internal sense. For herein lies the mystery of the origin and source of our faculty of sensibility. Its application to an object, and the transcendental ground of this unity of subjective and objective, lie too deeply concealed for us, who cognize ourselves only through the internal sense, consequently as phenomena, to be able to discover in our existence anything but phenomena, the non-sensuous cause of which we at the same time earnestly desire to penetrate to.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"49.16"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0017","text":"The great utility of this critique of conclusions arrived at by the processes of mere reflection consists in its clear demonstration of the nullity of all conclusions respecting objects which are compared with each other in the understanding alone, while it at the same time confirms what we particularly insisted on, namely, that, although phenomena are not included as things in themselves among the objects of the pure understanding, they are nevertheless the only things by which our cognition can possess objective reality, that is to say, which give us intuitions to correspond with our conceptions.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"49.17"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0018","text":"When we reflect in a purely logical manner, we do nothing more than compare conceptions in our understanding, to discover whether both have the same content, whether they are self-contradictory or not, whether anything is contained in either conception, which of the two is given, and which is merely a mode of thinking that given. But if I apply these conceptions to an object in general (in the transcendental sense), without first determining whether it is an object of sensuous or intellectual intuition, certain limitations present themselves, which forbid us to pass beyond the conceptions and render all empirical use of them impossible. And thus these limitations prove that the representation of an object as a thing in general is not only insufficient, but, without sensuous determination and independently of empirical conditions, self-contradictory; that we must therefore make abstraction of all objects, as in logic, or, admitting them, must think them under conditions of sensuous intuition; that, consequently, the intelligible requires an altogether peculiar intuition, which we do not possess, and in the absence of which it is for us nothing; while, on the other hand phenomena cannot be objects in themselves. For, when I merely think things in general, the difference in their external relations cannot constitute a difference in the things themselves; on the contrary, the former presupposes the latter, and if the conception of one of two things is not internally different from that of the other, I am merely thinking the same thing in different relations. Further, by the addition of one affirmation (reality) to the other, the positive therein is really augmented, and nothing is abstracted or withdrawn from it; hence the real in things cannot be in contradiction with or opposition to itself—and so on.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"49.18"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0019","text":"The true use of the conceptions of reflection in the employment of the understanding has, as we have shown, been so misconceived by Leibnitz, one of the most acute philosophers of either ancient or modern times, that he has been misled into the construction of a baseless system of intellectual cognition, which professes to determine its objects without the intervention of the senses. For this reason, the exposition of the cause of the amphiboly of these conceptions, as the origin of these false principles, is of great utility in determining with certainty the proper limits of the understanding.\n\nIt is right to say whatever is affirmed or denied of the whole of a conception can be affirmed or denied of any part of it (dictum de omni et nullo); but it would be absurd so to alter this logical proposition as to say whatever is not contained in a general conception is likewise not contained in the particular conceptions which rank under it; for the latter are particular conceptions, for the very reason that their content is greater than that which is cogitated in the general conception. And yet the whole intellectual system of Leibnitz is based upon this false principle, and with it must necessarily fall to the ground, together with all the ambiguous principles in reference to the employment of the understanding which have thence originated.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"49.19"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0020","text":"Leibnitz’s principle of the identity of indiscernibles or indistinguishables is really based on the presupposition that, if in the conception of a thing a certain distinction is not to be found, it is also not to be met with in things themselves; that, consequently, all things are completely identical (numero eadem) which are not distinguishable from each other (as to quality or quantity) in our conceptions of them. But, as in the mere conception of anything abstraction has been made of many necessary conditions of intuition, that of which abstraction has been made is rashly held to be non-existent, and nothing is attributed to the thing but what is contained in its conception.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"49.20"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0021","text":"The conception of a cubic foot of space, however I may think it, is in itself completely identical. But two cubic feet in space are nevertheless distinct from each other from the sole fact of their being in different places (they are numero diversa); and these places are conditions of intuition, wherein the object of this conception is given, and which do not belong to the conception, but to the faculty of sensibility. In like manner, there is in the conception of a thing no contradiction when a negative is not connected with an affirmative; and merely affirmative conceptions cannot, in conjunction, produce any negation. But in sensuous intuition, wherein reality (take for example, motion) is given, we find conditions (opposite directions)—of which abstraction has been made in the conception of motion in general—which render possible a contradiction or opposition (not indeed of a logical kind)—and which from pure positives produce zero = 0. We are therefore not justified in saying that all reality is in perfect agreement and harmony, because no contradiction is discoverable among its conceptions.[[39]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-39) According to mere conceptions, that which is internal is the substratum of all relations or external determinations. When, therefore, I abstract all conditions of intuition, and confine myself solely to the conception of a thing in general, I can make abstraction of all external relations, and there must nevertheless remain a conception of that which indicates no relation, but merely internal determinations. Now it seems to follow that in everything (substance) there is something which is absolutely internal and which antecedes all external determinations, inasmuch as it renders them possible; and that therefore this substratum is something which does not contain any external relations and is consequently simple (for corporeal things are never anything but relations, at least of their parts external to each other); and, inasmuch as we know of no other absolutely internal determinations than those of the internal sense, this substratum is not only simple, but also, analogously with our internal sense, determined through representations, that is to say, all things are properly monads, or simple beings endowed with the power of representation. Now all this would be perfectly correct, if the conception of a thing were the only necessary condition of the presentation of objects of external intuition. It is, on the contrary, manifest that a permanent phenomenon in space (impenetrable extension) can contain mere relations, and nothing that is absolutely internal, and yet be the primary substratum of all external perception. By mere conceptions I cannot think anything external, without, at the same time, thinking something internal, for the reason that conceptions of relations presuppose given things, and without these are impossible. But, as an intuition there is something (that is, space, which, with all it contains, consists of purely formal, or, indeed, real relations) which is not found in the mere conception of a thing in general, and this presents to us the substratum which could not be cognized through conceptions alone, I cannot say: because a thing cannot be represented by mere conceptions without something absolutely internal, there is also, in the things themselves which are contained under these conceptions, and in their intuition nothing external to which something absolutely internal does not serve as the foundation. For, when we have made abstraction of all the conditions of intuition, there certainly remains in the mere conception nothing but the internal in general, through which alone the external is possible. But this necessity, which is grounded upon abstraction alone, does not obtain in the case of things themselves, in so far as they are given in intuition with such determinations as express mere relations, without having anything internal as their foundation; for they are not things in themselves, but only phenomena. What we cognize in matter is nothing but relations (what we call its internal determinations are but comparatively internal). But there are some self-subsistent and permanent, through which a determined object is given. That I, when abstraction is made of these relations, have nothing more to think, does not destroy the conception of a thing as phenomenon, nor the conception of an object in abstracto, but it does away with the possibility of an object that is determinable according to mere conceptions, that is, of a noumenon. It is certainly startling to hear that a thing consists solely of relations; but this thing is simply a phenomenon, and cannot be cogitated by means of the mere categories: it does itself consist in the mere relation of something in general to the senses. In the same way, we cannot cogitate relations of things in abstracto, if we commence with conceptions alone, in any other manner than that one is the cause of determinations in the other; for that is itself the conception of the understanding or category of relation. But, as in this case we make abstraction of all intuition, we lose altogether the mode in which the manifold determines to each of its parts its place, that is, the form of sensibility (space); and yet this mode antecedes all empirical causality.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"49.21"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0022","text":"[[39]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-39) If any one wishes here to have recourse to the usual subterfuge, and to say, that at least realitates noumena cannot be in opposition to each other, it will be requisite for him to adduce an example of this pure and non-sensuous reality, that it may be understood whether the notion represents something or nothing. But an example cannot be found except in experience, which never presents to us anything more than phenomena; and thus the proposition means nothing more than that the conception which contains only affirmatives does not contain anything negative—a proposition nobody ever doubted.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"49.22"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","text":"If by intelligible objects we understand things which can be thought by means of the pure categories, without the need of the schemata of sensibility, such objects are impossible. For the condition of the objective use of all our conceptions of understanding is the mode of our sensuous intuition, whereby objects are given; and, if we make abstraction of the latter, the former can have no relation to an object. And even if we should suppose a different kind of intuition from our own, still our functions of thought would have no use or signification in respect thereof. But if we understand by the term, objects of a non-sensuous intuition, in respect of which our categories are not valid, and of which we can accordingly have no knowledge (neither intuition nor conception), in this merely negative sense noumena must be admitted. For this is no more than saying that our mode of intuition is not applicable to all things, but only to objects of our senses, that consequently its objective validity is limited, and that room is therefore left for another kind of intuition, and thus also for things that may be objects of it. But in this sense the conception of a noumenon is problematical, that is to say, it is the notion of a thing of which we can neither say that it is possible, nor that it is impossible, inasmuch as we do not know of any mode of intuition besides the sensuous, or of any other sort of conceptions than the categories—a mode of intuition and a kind of conception neither of which is applicable to a non-sensuous object. We are on this account incompetent to extend the sphere of our objects of thought beyond the conditions of our sensibility, and to assume the existence of objects of pure thought, that is, of noumena, inasmuch as these have no true positive signification. For it must be confessed of the categories that they are not of themselves sufficient for the cognition of things in themselves and, without the data of sensibility, are mere subjective forms of the unity of the understanding. Thought is certainly not a product of the senses, and in so far is not limited by them, but it does not therefore follow that it may be employed purely and without the intervention of sensibility, for it would then be without reference to an object. And we cannot call a noumenon an object of pure thought; for the representation thereof is but the problematical conception of an object for a perfectly different intuition and a perfectly different understanding from ours, both of which are consequently themselves problematical. The conception of a noumenon is therefore not the conception of an object, but merely a problematical conception inseparably connected with the limitation of our sensibility. That is to say, this conception contains the answer to the question: “Are there objects quite unconnected with, and independent of, our intuition?”—a question to which only an indeterminate answer can be given. That answer is: “Inasmuch as sensuous intuition does not apply to all things without distinction, there remains room for other and different objects.” The existence of these problematical objects is therefore not absolutely denied, in the absence of a determinate conception of them, but, as no category is valid in respect of them, neither must they be admitted as objects for our understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"49.23"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","text":"Understanding accordingly limits sensibility, without at the same time enlarging its own field. While, moreover, it forbids sensibility to apply its forms and modes to things in themselves and restricts it to the sphere of phenomena, it cogitates an object in itself, only, however, as a transcendental object, which is the cause of a phenomenon (consequently not itself a phenomenon), and which cannot be thought either as a quantity or as reality, or as substance (because these conceptions always require sensuous forms in which to determine an object)—an object, therefore, of which we are quite unable to say whether it can be met with in ourselves or out of us, whether it would be annihilated together with sensibility, or, if this were taken away, would continue to exist. If we wish to call this object a noumenon, because the representation of it is non-sensuous, we are at liberty to do so. But as we can apply to it none of the conceptions of our understanding, the representation is for us quite void, and is available only for the indication of the limits of our sensuous intuition, thereby leaving at the same time an empty space, which we are competent to fill by the aid neither of possible experience, nor of the pure understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"49.24"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0025","text":"The critique of the pure understanding, accordingly, does not permit us to create for ourselves a new field of objects beyond those which are presented to us as phenomena, and to stray into intelligible worlds; nay, it does not even allow us to endeavour to form so much as a conception of them. The specious error which leads to this—and which is a perfectly excusable one—lies in the fact that the employment of the understanding, contrary to its proper purpose and destination, is made transcendental, and objects, that is, possible intuitions, are made to regulate themselves according to conceptions, instead of the conceptions arranging themselves according to the intuitions, on which alone their own objective validity rests. Now the reason of this again is that apperception, and with it thought, antecedes all possible determinate arrangement of representations. Accordingly we think something in general and determine it on the one hand sensuously, but, on the other, distinguish the general and in abstracto represented object from this particular mode of intuiting it. In this case there remains a mode of determining the object by mere thought, which is really but a logical form without content, which, however, seems to us to be a mode of the existence of the object in itself (noumenon), without regard to intuition which is limited to our senses.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"49.25"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","text":"Before ending this transcendental analytic, we must make an addition, which, although in itself of no particular importance, seems to be necessary to the completeness of the system. The highest conception, with which a transcendental philosophy commonly begins, is the division into possible and impossible. But as all division presupposes a divided conception, a still higher one must exist, and this is the conception of an object in general—problematically understood and without its being decided whether it is something or nothing. As the categories are the only conceptions which apply to objects in general, the distinguishing of an object, whether it is something or nothing, must proceed according to the order and direction of the categories.\n\n1. To the categories of quantity, that is, the conceptions of all, many, and one, the conception which annihilates all, that is, the conception of none, is opposed. And thus the object of a conception, to which no intuition can be found to correspond, is = nothing. That is, it is a conception without an object (ens rationis), like noumena, which cannot be considered possible in the sphere of reality, though they must not therefore be held to be impossible—or like certain new fundamental forces in matter, the existence of which is cogitable without contradiction, though, as examples from experience are not forthcoming, they must not be regarded as possible.\n\n2. Reality is something; negation is nothing, that is, a conception of the absence of an object, as cold, a shadow (nihil privativum).","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"49.26"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0027","text":"3. The mere form of intuition, without substance, is in itself no object, but the merely formal condition of an object (as phenomenon), as pure space and pure time. These are certainly something, as forms of intuition, but are not themselves objects which are intuited (ens imaginarium).\n\n4. The object of a conception which is self-contradictory, is nothing, because the conception is nothing—is impossible, as a figure composed of two straight lines (nihil negativum).\n\nThe table of this division of the conception of nothing (the corresponding division of the conception of something does not require special description) must therefore be arranged as follows:\n\n                      NOTHING\n                        AS\n\n                        1\n                As Empty Conception\n                 without object,\n                  _ens rationis_\n           2                               3\n     Empty object of               Empty intuition\n      a conception,                without object,\n     _nihil privativum              ens imaginarium_\n                        4\n                   Empty object\n                 without conception,\n                  _nihil negativum_","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"49.27"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0028","text":"We see that the ens rationis is distinguished from the nihil negativum or pure nothing by the consideration that the former must not be reckoned among possibilities, because it is a mere fiction—though not self-contradictory, while the latter is completely opposed to all possibility, inasmuch as the conception annihilates itself. Both, however, are empty conceptions. On the other hand, the nihil privativum and ens imaginarium are empty data for conceptions. If light be not given to the senses, we cannot represent to ourselves darkness, and if extended objects are not perceived, we cannot represent space. Neither the negation, nor the mere form of intuition can, without something real, be an object.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"49.28"}],"sha256":"583e55e22bb95e4780d012a79dda033a06aadc14b45c8b6347ae0a337e8662d4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE","title":"Experience","order":50,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_EXPE","text":"# Experience\n\n3. ANALOGIES OF EXPERIENCE.\n\nThe principle of these is: Experience is possible only through the representation of a necessary connection of Perceptions.\n\nPROOF.\n\nExperience is an empirical cognition; that is to say, a cognition which determines an object by means of perceptions. It is therefore a synthesis of perceptions, a synthesis which is not itself contained in perception, but which contains the synthetical unity of the manifold of perception in a consciousness; and this unity constitutes the essential of our cognition of objects of the senses, that is, of experience (not merely of intuition or sensation). Now in experience our perceptions come together contingently, so that no character of necessity in their connection appears, or can appear from the perceptions themselves, because apprehension is only a placing together of the manifold of empirical intuition, and no representation of a necessity in the connected existence of the phenomena which apprehension brings together, is to be discovered therein. But as experience is a cognition of objects by means of perceptions, it follows that the relation of the existence of the existence of the manifold must be represented in experience not as it is put together in time, but as it is objectively in time. And as time itself cannot be perceived, the determination of the existence of objects in time can only take place by means of their connection in time in general, consequently only by means of à priori connecting conceptions. Now as these conceptions always possess the character of necessity, experience is possible only by means of a representation of the necessary connection of perception.\n\nThe three modi of time are permanence, succession, and coexistence. Accordingly, there are three rules of all relations of time in phenomena, according to which the existence of every phenomenon is determined in respect of the unity of all time, and these antecede all experience and render it possible.\n\nThe general principle of all three analogies rests on the necessary unity of apperception in relation to all possible empirical consciousness (perception) at every time, consequently, as this unity lies à priori at the foundation of all mental operations, the principle rests on the synthetical unity of all phenomena according to their relation in time. For the original apperception relates to our internal sense (the complex of all representations), and indeed relates à priori to its form, that is to say, the relation of the manifold empirical consciousness in time. Now this manifold must be combined in original apperception according to relations of time—a necessity imposed by the à priori transcendental unity of apperception, to which is subjected all that can belong to my (i.e., my own) cognition, and therefore all that can become an object for me. This synthetical and à priori determined unity in relation of perceptions in time is therefore the rule: “All empirical determinations of time must be subject to rules of the general determination of time”; and the analogies of experience, of which we are now about to treat, must be rules of this nature.\n\nThese principles have this peculiarity, that they do not concern phenomena, and the synthesis of the empirical intuition thereof, but merely the existence of phenomena and their relation to each other in regard to this existence. Now the mode in which we apprehend a thing in a phenomenon can be determined à priori in such a manner that the rule of its synthesis can give, that is to say, can produce this à priori intuition in every empirical example. But the existence of phenomena cannot be known à priori, and although we could arrive by this path at a conclusion of the fact of some existence, we could not cognize that existence determinately, that is to say, we should be incapable of anticipating in what respect the empirical intuition of it would be distinguishable from that of others.\n\nThe two principles above mentioned, which I called mathematical, in consideration of the fact of their authorizing the application of mathematic phenomena, relate to these phenomena only in regard to their possibility, and instruct us how phenomena, as far as regards their intuition or the real in their perception, can be generated according to the rules of a mathematical synthesis. Consequently, numerical quantities, and with them the determination of a phenomenon as a quantity, can be employed in the one case as well as in the other. Thus, for example, out of 200,000 illuminations by the moon, I might compose and give à priori, that is construct, the degree of our sensations of the sun-light.[[30]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-30) We may therefore entitle these two principles constitutive.\n\n[[30]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-30) Kant’s meaning is: The two principles enunciated under the heads of “Axioms of Intuition,” and “Anticipations of Perception,” authorize the application to phenomena of determinations of size and number, that is of mathematic. For example, I may compute the light of the sun, and say that its quantity is a certain number of times greater than that of the moon. In the same way, heat is measured by the comparison of its different effects on water, &c., and on mercury in a thermometer.—Tr\n\nThe case is very different with those principles whose province it is to subject the existence of phenomena to rules à priori. For as existence does not admit of being constructed, it is clear that they must only concern the relations of existence and be merely regulative principles. In this case, therefore, neither axioms nor anticipations are to be thought of. Thus, if a perception is given us, in a certain relation of time to other (although undetermined) perceptions, we cannot then say à priori, what and how great (in quantity) the other perception necessarily connected with the former is, but only how it is connected, quoad its existence, in this given modus of time. Analogies in philosophy mean something very different from that which they represent in mathematics. In the latter they are formulae, which enounce the equality of two relations of quantity, and are always constitutive, so that if two terms of the proportion are given, the third is also given, that is, can be constructed by the aid of these formulae. But in philosophy, analogy is not the equality of two quantitative but of two qualitative relations. In this case, from three given terms, I can give à priori and cognize the relation to a fourth member, but not this fourth term itself, although I certainly possess a rule to guide me in the search for this fourth term in experience, and a mark to assist me in discovering it. An analogy of experience is therefore only a rule according to which unity of experience must arise out of perceptions in respect to objects (phenomena) not as a constitutive, but merely as a regulative principle. The same holds good also of the postulates of empirical thought in general, which relate to the synthesis of mere intuition (which concerns the form of phenomena), the synthesis of perception (which concerns the matter of phenomena), and the synthesis of experience (which concerns the relation of these perceptions). For they are only regulative principles, and clearly distinguishable from the mathematical, which are constitutive, not indeed in regard to the certainty which both possess à priori, but in the mode of evidence thereof, consequently also in the manner of demonstration.\n\nBut what has been observed of all synthetical propositions, and must be particularly remarked in this place, is this, that these analogies possess significance and validity, not as principles of the transcendental, but only as principles of the empirical use of the understanding, and their truth can therefore be proved only as such, and that consequently the phenomena must not be subjoined directly under the categories, but only under their schemata. For if the objects to which those principles must be applied were things in themselves, it would be quite impossible to cognize aught concerning them synthetically à priori. But they are nothing but phenomena; a complete knowledge of which—a knowledge to which all principles à priori must at last relate—is the only possible experience. It follows that these principles can have nothing else for their aim than the conditions of the empirical cognition in the unity of synthesis of phenomena. But this synthesis is cogitated only in the schema of the pure conception of the understanding, of whose unity, as that of a synthesis in general, the category contains the function unrestricted by any sensuous condition. These principles will therefore authorize us to connect phenomena according to an analogy, with the logical and universal unity of conceptions, and consequently to employ the categories in the principles themselves; but in the application of them to experience, we shall use only their schemata, as the key to their proper application, instead of the categories, or rather the latter as restricting conditions, under the title of “formulae” of the former.\n\nA. FIRST ANALOGY.\n\nPrinciple of the Permanence of Substance.\n\nIn all changes of phenomena, substance is permanent, and the quantum thereof in nature is neither increased nor diminished.\n\nPROOF.\n\nAll phenomena exist in time, wherein alone as substratum, that is, as the permanent form of the internal intuition, coexistence and succession can be represented. Consequently time, in which all changes of phenomena must be cogitated, remains and changes not, because it is that in which succession and coexistence can be represented only as determinations thereof. Now, time in itself cannot be an object of perception. It follows that in objects of perception, that is, in phenomena, there must be found a substratum which represents time in general, and in which all change or coexistence can be perceived by means of the relation of phenomena to it. But the substratum of all reality, that is, of all that pertains to the existence of things, is substance; all that pertains to existence can be cogitated only as a determination of substance. Consequently, the permanent, in relation to which alone can all relations of time in phenomena be determined, is substance in the world of phenomena, that is, the real in phenomena, that which, as the substratum of all change, remains ever the same. Accordingly, as this cannot change in existence, its quantity in nature can neither be increased nor diminished.\n\nOur apprehension of the manifold in a phenomenon is always successive, is Consequently always changing. By it alone we could, therefore, never determine whether this manifold, as an object of experience, is coexistent or successive, unless it had for a foundation something fixed and permanent, of the existence of which all succession and coexistence are nothing but so many modes (modi of time). Only in the permanent, then, are relations of time possible (for simultaneity and succession are the only relations in time); that is to say, the permanent is the substratum of our empirical representation of time itself, in which alone all determination of time is possible. Permanence is, in fact, just another expression for time, as the abiding correlate of all existence of phenomena, and of all change, and of all coexistence. For change does not affect time itself, but only the phenomena in time (just as coexistence cannot be regarded as a modus of time itself, seeing that in time no parts are coexistent, but all successive). If we were to attribute succession to time itself, we should be obliged to cogitate another time, in which this succession would be possible. It is only by means of the permanent that existence in different parts of the successive series of time receives a quantity, which we entitle duration. For in mere succession, existence is perpetually vanishing and recommencing, and therefore never has even the least quantity. Without the permanent, then, no relation in time is possible. Now, time in itself is not an object of perception; consequently the permanent in phenomena must be regarded as the substratum of all determination of time, and consequently also as the condition of the possibility of all synthetical unity of perceptions, that is, of experience; and all existence and all change in time can only be regarded as a mode in the existence of that which abides unchangeably. Therefore, in all phenomena, the permanent is the object in itself, that is, the substance (phenomenon); but all that changes or can change belongs only to the mode of the existence of this substance or substances, consequently to its determinations.\n\nI find that in all ages not only the philosopher, but even the common understanding, has preposited this permanence as a substratum of all change in phenomena; indeed, I am compelled to believe that they will always accept this as an indubitable fact. Only the philosopher expresses himself in a more precise and definite manner, when he says: “In all changes in the world, the substance remains, and the accidents alone are changeable.” But of this decidedly synthetical proposition, I nowhere meet with even an attempt at proof; nay, it very rarely has the good fortune to stand, as it deserves to do, at the head of the pure and entirely à priori laws of nature. In truth, the statement that substance is permanent, is tautological. For this very permanence is the ground on which we apply the category of substance to the phenomenon; and we should have been obliged to prove that in all phenomena there is something permanent, of the existence of which the changeable is nothing but a determination. But because a proof of this nature cannot be dogmatical, that is, cannot be drawn from conceptions, inasmuch as it concerns a synthetical proposition à priori, and as philosophers never reflected that such propositions are valid only in relation to possible experience, and therefore cannot be proved except by means of a deduction of the possibility of experience, it is no wonder that while it has served as the foundation of all experience (for we feel the need of it in empirical cognition), it has never been supported by proof.\n\nA philosopher was asked: “What is the weight of smoke?” He answered: “Subtract from the weight of the burnt wood the weight of the remaining ashes, and you will have the weight of the smoke.” Thus he presumed it to be incontrovertible that even in fire the matter (substance) does not perish, but that only the form of it undergoes a change. In like manner was the saying: “From nothing comes nothing,” only another inference from the principle or permanence, or rather of the ever-abiding existence of the true subject in phenomena. For if that in the phenomenon which we call substance is to be the proper substratum of all determination of time, it follows that all existence in past as well as in future time, must be determinable by means of it alone. Hence we are entitled to apply the term substance to a phenomenon, only because we suppose its existence in all time, a notion which the word permanence does not fully express, as it seems rather to be referable to future time. However, the internal necessity perpetually to be, is inseparably connected with the necessity always to have been, and so the expression may stand as it is. “Gigni de nihilo nihil; in nihilum nil posse reverti,”[[31]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-31) are two propositions which the ancients never parted, and which people nowadays sometimes mistakenly disjoin, because they imagine that the propositions apply to objects as things in themselves, and that the former might be inimical to the dependence (even in respect of its substance also) of the world upon a supreme cause. But this apprehension is entirely needless, for the question in this case is only of phenomena in the sphere of experience, the unity of which never could be possible, if we admitted the possibility that new things (in respect of their substance) should arise. For in that case, we should lose altogether that which alone can represent the unity of time, to wit, the identity of the substratum, as that through which alone all change possesses complete and thorough unity. This permanence is, however, nothing but the manner in which we represent to ourselves the existence of things in the phenomenal world.\n\n[[31]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-31) Persius, Satirae, iii.83-84.\n\nThe determinations of a substance, which are only particular modes of its existence, are called accidents. They are always real, because they concern the existence of substance (negations are only determinations, which express the non-existence of something in the substance). Now, if to this real in the substance we ascribe a particular existence (for example, to motion as an accident of matter), this existence is called inherence, in contradistinction to the existence of substance, which we call subsistence. But hence arise many misconceptions, and it would be a more accurate and just mode of expression to designate the accident only as the mode in which the existence of a substance is positively determined. Meanwhile, by reason of the conditions of the logical exercise of our understanding, it is impossible to avoid separating, as it were, that which in the existence of a substance is subject to change, whilst the substance remains, and regarding it in relation to that which is properly permanent and radical. On this account, this category of substance stands under the title of relation, rather because it is the condition thereof than because it contains in itself any relation.\n\nNow, upon this notion of permanence rests the proper notion of the conception change. Origin and extinction are not changes of that which originates or becomes extinct. Change is but a mode of existence, which follows on another mode of existence of the same object; hence all that changes is permanent, and only the condition thereof changes. Now since this mutation affects only determinations, which can have a beginning or an end, we may say, employing an expression which seems somewhat paradoxical: “Only the permanent (substance) is subject to change; the mutable suffers no change, but rather alternation, that is, when certain determinations cease, others begin.”\n\nChange, when, cannot be perceived by us except in substances, and origin or extinction in an absolute sense, that does not concern merely a determination of the permanent, cannot be a possible perception, for it is this very notion of the permanent which renders possible the representation of a transition from one state into another, and from non-being to being, which, consequently, can be empirically cognized only as alternating determinations of that which is permanent. Grant that a thing absolutely begins to be; we must then have a point of time in which it was not. But how and by what can we fix and determine this point of time, unless by that which already exists? For a void time—preceding—is not an object of perception; but if we connect this beginning with objects which existed previously, and which continue to exist till the object in question in question begins to be, then the latter can only be a determination of the former as the permanent. The same holds good of the notion of extinction, for this presupposes the empirical representation of a time, in which a phenomenon no longer exists.\n\nSubstances (in the world of phenomena) are the substratum of all determinations of time. The beginning of some, and the ceasing to be of other substances, would utterly do away with the only condition of the empirical unity of time; and in that case phenomena would relate to two different times, in which, side by side, existence would pass; which is absurd. For there is only one time in which all different times must be placed, not as coexistent, but as successive.\n\nAccordingly, permanence is a necessary condition under which alone phenomena, as things or objects, are determinable in a possible experience. But as regards the empirical criterion of this necessary permanence, and with it of the substantiality of phenomena, we shall find sufficient opportunity to speak in the sequel.\n\nB. SECOND ANALOGY.\n\nPrinciple of the Succession of Time According to the Law of Causality. All changes take place according to the law of the connection of Cause and Effect.\n\nPROOF.\n\n(That all phenomena in the succession of time are only changes, that is, a successive being and non-being of the determinations of substance, which is permanent; consequently that a being of substance itself which follows on the non-being thereof, or a non-being of substance which follows on the being thereof, in other words, that the origin or extinction of substance itself, is impossible—all this has been fully established in treating of the foregoing principle. This principle might have been expressed as follows: “All alteration (succession) of phenomena is merely change”; for the changes of substance are not origin or extinction, because the conception of change presupposes the same subject as existing with two opposite determinations, and consequently as permanent. After this premonition, we shall proceed to the proof.)\n\nI perceive that phenomena succeed one another, that is to say, a state of things exists at one time, the opposite of which existed in a former state. In this case, then, I really connect together two perceptions in time. Now connection is not an operation of mere sense and intuition, but is the product of a synthetical faculty of imagination, which determines the internal sense in respect of a relation of time. But imagination can connect these two states in two ways, so that either the one or the other may antecede in time; for time in itself cannot be an object of perception, and what in an object precedes and what follows cannot be empirically determined in relation to it. I am only conscious, then, that my imagination places one state before and the other after; not that the one state antecedes the other in the object. In other words, the objective relation of the successive phenomena remains quite undetermined by means of mere perception. Now in order that this relation may be cognized as determined, the relation between the two states must be so cogitated that it is thereby determined as necessary, which of them must be placed before and which after, and not conversely. But the conception which carries with it a necessity of synthetical unity, can be none other than a pure conception of the understanding which does not lie in mere perception; and in this case it is the conception of “the relation of cause and effect,” the former of which determines the latter in time, as its necessary consequence, and not as something which might possibly antecede (or which might in some cases not be perceived to follow). It follows that it is only because we subject the sequence of phenomena, and consequently all change, to the law of causality, that experience itself, that is, empirical cognition of phenomena, becomes possible; and consequently, that phenomena themselves, as objects of experience, are possible only by virtue of this law.\n\nOur apprehension of the manifold of phenomena is always successive. The representations of parts succeed one another. Whether they succeed one another in the object also, is a second point for reflection, which was not contained in the former. Now we may certainly give the name of object to everything, even to every representation, so far as we are conscious thereof; but what this word may mean in the case of phenomena, not merely in so far as they (as representations) are objects, but only in so far as they indicate an object, is a question requiring deeper consideration. In so far as they, regarded merely as representations, are at the same time objects of consciousness, they are not to be distinguished from apprehension, that is, reception into the synthesis of imagination, and we must therefore say: “The manifold of phenomena is always produced successively in the mind.” If phenomena were things in themselves, no man would be able to conjecture from the succession of our representations how this manifold is connected in the object; for we have to do only with our representations. How things may be in themselves, without regard to the representations through which they affect us, is utterly beyond the sphere of our cognition. Now although phenomena are not things in themselves, and are nevertheless the only thing given to us to be cognized, it is my duty to show what sort of connection in time belongs to the manifold in phenomena themselves, while the representation of this manifold in apprehension is always successive. For example, the apprehension of the manifold in the phenomenon of a house which stands before me, is successive. Now comes the question whether the manifold of this house is in itself successive—which no one will be at all willing to grant. But, so soon as I raise my conception of an object to the transcendental signification thereof, I find that the house is not a thing in itself, but only a phenomenon, that is, a representation, the transcendental object of which remains utterly unknown. What then am I to understand by the question: “How can the manifold be connected in the phenomenon itself—not considered as a thing in itself, but merely as a phenomenon?” Here that which lies in my successive apprehension is regarded as representation, whilst the phenomenon which is given me, notwithstanding that it is nothing more than a complex of these representations, is regarded as the object thereof, with which my conception, drawn from the representations of apprehension, must harmonize. It is very soon seen that, as accordance of the cognition with its object constitutes truth, the question now before us can only relate to the formal conditions of empirical truth; and that the phenomenon, in opposition to the representations of apprehension, can only be distinguished therefrom as the object of them, if it is subject to a rule which distinguishes it from every other apprehension, and which renders necessary a mode of connection of the manifold. That in the phenomenon which contains the condition of this necessary rule of apprehension, is the object.\n\nLet us now proceed to our task. That something happens, that is to say, that something or some state exists which before was not, cannot be empirically perceived, unless a phenomenon precedes, which does not contain in itself this state. For a reality which should follow upon a void time, in other words, a beginning, which no state of things precedes, can just as little be apprehended as the void time itself. Every apprehension of an event is therefore a perception which follows upon another perception. But as this is the case with all synthesis of apprehension, as I have shown above in the example of a house, my apprehension of an event is not yet sufficiently distinguished from other apprehensions. But I remark also that if in a phenomenon which contains an occurrence, I call the antecedent state of my perception, A, and the following state, B, the perception B can only follow A in apprehension, and the perception A cannot follow B, but only precede it. For example, I see a ship float down the stream of a river. My perception of its place lower down follows upon my perception of its place higher up the course of the river, and it is impossible that, in the apprehension of this phenomenon, the vessel should be perceived first below and afterwards higher up the stream. Here, therefore, the order in the sequence of perceptions in apprehension is determined; and by this order apprehension is regulated. In the former example, my perceptions in the apprehension of a house might begin at the roof and end at the foundation, or vice versa; or I might apprehend the manifold in this empirical intuition, by going from left to right, and from right to left. Accordingly, in the series of these perceptions, there was no determined order, which necessitated my beginning at a certain point, in order empirically to connect the manifold. But this rule is always to be met with in the perception of that which happens, and it makes the order of the successive perceptions in the apprehension of such a phenomenon necessary.\n\nI must, therefore, in the present case, deduce the subjective sequence of apprehension from the objective sequence of phenomena, for otherwise the former is quite undetermined, and one phenomenon is not distinguishable from another. The former alone proves nothing as to the connection of the manifold in an object, for it is quite arbitrary. The latter must consist in the order of the manifold in a phenomenon, according to which order the apprehension of one thing (that which happens) follows that of another thing (which precedes), in conformity with a rule. In this way alone can I be authorized to say of the phenomenon itself, and not merely of my own apprehension, that a certain order or sequence is to be found therein. That is, in other words, I cannot arrange my apprehension otherwise than in this order.\n\nIn conformity with this rule, then, it is necessary that in that which antecedes an event there be found the condition of a rule, according to which in this event follows always and necessarily; but I cannot reverse this and go back from the event, and determine (by apprehension) that which antecedes it. For no phenomenon goes back from the succeeding point of time to the preceding point, although it does certainly relate to a preceding point of time; from a given time, on the other hand, there is always a necessary progression to the determined succeeding time. Therefore, because there certainly is something that follows, I must of necessity connect it with something else, which antecedes, and upon which it follows, in conformity with a rule, that is necessarily, so that the event, as conditioned, affords certain indication of a condition, and this condition determines the event.\n\nLet us suppose that nothing precedes an event, upon which this event must follow in conformity with a rule. All sequence of perception would then exist only in apprehension, that is to say, would be merely subjective, and it could not thereby be objectively determined what thing ought to precede, and what ought to follow in perception. In such a case, we should have nothing but a play of representations, which would possess no application to any object. That is to say, it would not be possible through perception to distinguish one phenomenon from another, as regards relations of time; because the succession in the act of apprehension would always be of the same sort, and therefore there would be nothing in the phenomenon to determine the succession, and to render a certain sequence objectively necessary. And, in this case, I cannot say that two states in a phenomenon follow one upon the other, but only that one apprehension follows upon another. But this is merely subjective, and does not determine an object, and consequently cannot be held to be cognition of an object—not even in the phenomenal world.\n\nAccordingly, when we know in experience that something happens, we always presuppose that something precedes, whereupon it follows in conformity with a rule. For otherwise I could not say of the object that it follows; because the mere succession in my apprehension, if it be not determined by a rule in relation to something preceding, does not authorize succession in the object. Only, therefore, in reference to a rule, according to which phenomena are determined in their sequence, that is, as they happen, by the preceding state, can I make my subjective synthesis (of apprehension) objective, and it is only under this presupposition that even the experience of an event is possible.\n\nNo doubt it appears as if this were in thorough contradiction to all the notions which people have hitherto entertained in regard to the procedure of the human understanding. According to these opinions, it is by means of the perception and comparison of similar consequences following upon certain antecedent phenomena that the understanding is led to the discovery of a rule, according to which certain events always follow certain phenomena, and it is only by this process that we attain to the conception of cause. Upon such a basis, it is clear that this conception must be merely empirical, and the rule which it furnishes us with—“Everything that happens must have a cause”—would be just as contingent as experience itself. The universality and necessity of the rule or law would be perfectly spurious attributes of it. Indeed, it could not possess universal validity, inasmuch as it would not in this case be à priori, but founded on deduction. But the same is the case with this law as with other pure à priori representations (e.g., space and time), which we can draw in perfect clearness and completeness from experience, only because we had already placed them therein, and by that means, and by that alone, had rendered experience possible. Indeed, the logical clearness of this representation of a rule, determining the series of events, is possible only when we have made use thereof in experience. Nevertheless, the recognition of this rule, as a condition of the synthetical unity of phenomena in time, was the ground of experience itself and consequently preceded it à priori.\n\nIt is now our duty to show by an example that we never, even in experience, attribute to an object the notion of succession or effect (of an event—that is, the happening of something that did not exist before), and distinguish it from the subjective succession of apprehension, unless when a rule lies at the foundation, which compels us to observe this order of perception in preference to any other, and that, indeed, it is this necessity which first renders possible the representation of a succession in the object.\n\nWe have representations within us, of which also we can be conscious. But, however widely extended, however accurate and thoroughgoing this consciousness may be, these representations are still nothing more than representations, that is, internal determinations of the mind in this or that relation of time. Now how happens it that to these representations we should set an object, or that, in addition to their subjective reality, as modifications, we should still further attribute to them a certain unknown objective reality? It is clear that objective significancy cannot consist in a relation to another representation (of that which we desire to term object), for in that case the question again arises: “How does this other representation go out of itself, and obtain objective significancy over and above the subjective, which is proper to it, as a determination of a state of mind?” If we try to discover what sort of new property the relation to an object gives to our subjective representations, and what new importance they thereby receive, we shall find that this relation has no other effect than that of rendering necessary the connection of our representations in a certain manner, and of subjecting them to a rule; and that conversely, it is only because a certain order is necessary in the relations of time of our representations, that objective significancy is ascribed to them.\n\nIn the synthesis of phenomena, the manifold of our representations is always successive. Now hereby is not represented an object, for by means of this succession, which is common to all apprehension, no one thing is distinguished from another. But so soon as I perceive or assume that in this succession there is a relation to a state antecedent, from which the representation follows in accordance with a rule, so soon do I represent something as an event, or as a thing that happens; in other words, I cognize an object to which I must assign a certain determinate position in time, which cannot be altered, because of the preceding state in the object. When, therefore, I perceive that something happens, there is contained in this representation, in the first place, the fact, that something antecedes; because, it is only in relation to this that the phenomenon obtains its proper relation of time, in other words, exists after an antecedent time, in which it did not exist. But it can receive its determined place in time only by the presupposition that something existed in the foregoing state, upon which it follows inevitably and always, that is, in conformity with a rule. From all this it is evident that, in the first place, I cannot reverse the order of succession, and make that which happens precede that upon which it follows; and that, in the second place, if the antecedent state be posited, a certain determinate event inevitably and necessarily follows. Hence it follows that there exists a certain order in our representations, whereby the present gives a sure indication of some previously existing state, as a correlate, though still undetermined, of the existing event which is given—a correlate which itself relates to the event as its consequence, conditions it, and connects it necessarily with itself in the series of time.\n\nIf then it be admitted as a necessary law of sensibility, and consequently a formal condition of all perception, that the preceding necessarily determines the succeeding time (inasmuch as I cannot arrive at the succeeding except through the preceding), it must likewise be an indispensable law of empirical representation of the series of time that the phenomena of the past determine all phenomena in the succeeding time, and that the latter, as events, cannot take place, except in so far as the former determine their existence in time, that is to say, establish it according to a rule. For it is of course only in phenomena that we can empirically cognize this continuity in the connection of times.\n\nFor all experience and for the possibility of experience, understanding is indispensable, and the first step which it takes in this sphere is not to render the representation of objects clear, but to render the representation of an object in general, possible. It does this by applying the order of time to phenomena, and their existence. In other words, it assigns to each phenomenon, as a consequence, a place in relation to preceding phenomena, determined à priori in time, without which it could not harmonize with time itself, which determines a place à priori to all its parts. This determination of place cannot be derived from the relation of phenomena to absolute time (for it is not an object of perception); but, on the contrary, phenomena must reciprocally determine the places in time of one another, and render these necessary in the order of time. In other words, whatever follows or happens, must follow in conformity with a universal rule upon that which was contained in the foregoing state. Hence arises a series of phenomena, which, by means of the understanding, produces and renders necessary exactly the same order and continuous connection in the series of our possible perceptions, as is found à priori in the form of internal intuition (time), in which all our perceptions must have place.\n\nThat something happens, then, is a perception which belongs to a possible experience, which becomes real only because I look upon the phenomenon as determined in regard to its place in time, consequently as an object, which can always be found by means of a rule in the connected series of my perceptions. But this rule of the determination of a thing according to succession in time is as follows: “In what precedes may be found the condition, under which an event always (that is, necessarily) follows.” From all this it is obvious that the principle of cause and effect is the principle of possible experience, that is, of objective cognition of phenomena, in regard to their relations in the succession of time.\n\nThe proof of this fundamental proposition rests entirely on the following momenta of argument. To all empirical cognition belongs the synthesis of the manifold by the imagination, a synthesis which is always successive, that is, in which the representations therein always follow one another. But the order of succession in imagination is not determined, and the series of successive representations may be taken retrogressively as well as progressively. But if this synthesis is a synthesis of apprehension (of the manifold of a given phenomenon), then the order is determined in the object, or to speak more accurately, there is therein an order of successive synthesis which determines an object, and according to which something necessarily precedes, and when this is posited, something else necessarily follows. If, then, my perception is to contain the cognition of an event, that is, of something which really happens, it must be an empirical judgement, wherein we think that the succession is determined; that is, it presupposes another phenomenon, upon which this event follows necessarily, or in conformity with a rule. If, on the contrary, when I posited the antecedent, the event did not necessarily follow, I should be obliged to consider it merely as a subjective play of my imagination, and if in this I represented to myself anything as objective, I must look upon it as a mere dream. Thus, the relation of phenomena (as possible perceptions), according to which that which happens is, as to its existence, necessarily determined in time by something which antecedes, in conformity with a rule—in other words, the relation of cause and effect—is the condition of the objective validity of our empirical judgements in regard to the sequence of perceptions, consequently of their empirical truth, and therefore of experience. The principle of the relation of causality in the succession of phenomena is therefore valid for all objects of experience, because it is itself the ground of the possibility of experience.\n\nHere, however, a difficulty arises, which must be resolved. The principle of the connection of causality among phenomena is limited in our formula to the succession thereof, although in practice we find that the principle applies also when the phenomena exist together in the same time, and that cause and effect may be simultaneous. For example, there is heat in a room, which does not exist in the open air. I look about for the cause, and find it to be the fire, Now the fire as the cause is simultaneous with its effect, the heat of the room. In this case, then, there is no succession as regards time, between cause and effect, but they are simultaneous; and still the law holds good. The greater part of operating causes in nature are simultaneous with their effects, and the succession in time of the latter is produced only because the cause cannot achieve the total of its effect in one moment. But at the moment when the effect first arises, it is always simultaneous with the causality of its cause, because, if the cause had but a moment before ceased to be, the effect could not have arisen. Here it must be specially remembered that we must consider the order of time and not the lapse thereof. The relation remains, even though no time has elapsed. The time between the causality of the cause and its immediate effect may entirely vanish, and the cause and effect be thus simultaneous, but the relation of the one to the other remains always determinable according to time. If, for example, I consider a leaden ball, which lies upon a cushion and makes a hollow in it, as a cause, then it is simultaneous with the effect. But I distinguish the two through the relation of time of the dynamical connection of both. For if I lay the ball upon the cushion, then the hollow follows upon the before smooth surface; but supposing the cushion has, from some cause or another, a hollow, there does not thereupon follow a leaden ball.\n\nThus, the law of succession of time is in all instances the only empirical criterion of effect in relation to the causality of the antecedent cause. The glass is the cause of the rising of the water above its horizontal surface, although the two phenomena are contemporaneous. For, as soon as I draw some water with the glass from a larger vessel, an effect follows thereupon, namely, the change of the horizontal state which the water had in the large vessel into a concave, which it assumes in the glass.\n\nThis conception of causality leads us to the conception of action; that of action, to the conception of force; and through it, to the conception of substance. As I do not wish this critical essay, the sole purpose of which is to treat of the sources of our synthetical cognition à priori, to be crowded with analyses which merely explain, but do not enlarge the sphere of our conceptions, I reserve the detailed explanation of the above conceptions for a future system of pure reason. Such an analysis, indeed, executed with great particularity, may already be found in well-known works on this subject. But I cannot at present refrain from making a few remarks on the empirical criterion of a substance, in so far as it seems to be more evident and more easily recognized through the conception of action than through that of the permanence of a phenomenon.\n\nWhere action (consequently activity and force) exists, substance also must exist, and in it alone must be sought the seat of that fruitful source of phenomena. Very well. But if we are called upon to explain what we mean by substance, and wish to avoid the vice of reasoning in a circle, the answer is by no means so easy. How shall we conclude immediately from the action to the permanence of that which acts, this being nevertheless an essential and peculiar criterion of substance (phenomenon)? But after what has been said above, the solution of this question becomes easy enough, although by the common mode of procedure—merely analysing our conceptions—it would be quite impossible. The conception of action indicates the relation of the subject of causality to the effect. Now because all effect consists in that which happens, therefore in the changeable, the last subject thereof is the permanent, as the substratum of all that changes, that is, substance. For according to the principle of causality, actions are always the first ground of all change in phenomena and, consequently, cannot be a property of a subject which itself changes, because if this were the case, other actions and another subject would be necessary to determine this change. From all this it results that action alone, as an empirical criterion, is a sufficient proof of the presence of substantiality, without any necessity on my part of endeavouring to discover the permanence of substance by a comparison. Besides, by this mode of induction we could not attain to the completeness which the magnitude and strict universality of the conception requires. For that the primary subject of the causality of all arising and passing away, all origin and extinction, cannot itself (in the sphere of phenomena) arise and pass away, is a sound and safe conclusion, a conclusion which leads us to the conception of empirical necessity and permanence in existence, and consequently to the conception of a substance as phenomenon.\n\nWhen something happens, the mere fact of the occurrence, without regard to that which occurs, is an object requiring investigation. The transition from the non-being of a state into the existence of it, supposing that this state contains no quality which previously existed in the phenomenon, is a fact of itself demanding inquiry. Such an event, as has been shown in No. A, does not concern substance (for substance does not thus originate), but its condition or state. It is therefore only change, and not origin from nothing. If this origin be regarded as the effect of a foreign cause, it is termed creation, which cannot be admitted as an event among phenomena, because the very possibility of it would annihilate the unity of experience. If, however, I regard all things not as phenomena, but as things in themselves and objects of understanding alone, they, although substances, may be considered as dependent, in respect of their existence, on a foreign cause. But this would require a very different meaning in the words, a meaning which could not apply to phenomena as objects of possible experience.\n\nHow a thing can be changed, how it is possible that upon one state existing in one point of time, an opposite state should follow in another point of time—of this we have not the smallest conception à priori. There is requisite for this the knowledge of real powers, which can only be given empirically; for example, knowledge of moving forces, or, in other words, of certain successive phenomena (as movements) which indicate the presence of such forces. But the form of every change, the condition under which alone it can take place as the coming into existence of another state (be the content of the change, that is, the state which is changed, what it may), and consequently the succession of the states themselves can very well be considered à priori, in relation to the law of causality and the conditions of time.[[32]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-32)\n\n[[32]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-32) It must be remarked that I do not speak of the change of certain relations, but of the change of the state. Thus, when a body moves in a uniform manner, it does not change its state (of motion); but only when all motion increases or decreases.\n\nWhen a substance passes from one state, a, into another state, b, the point of time in which the latter exists is different from, and subsequent to that in which the former existed. In like manner, the second state, as reality (in the phenomenon), differs from the first, in which the reality of the second did not exist, as b from zero. That is to say, if the state, b, differs from the state, a, only in respect to quantity, the change is a coming into existence of b -a, which in the former state did not exist, and in relation to which that state is = O.\n\nNow the question arises how a thing passes from one state = a, into another state = b. Between two moments there is always a certain time, and between two states existing in these moments there is always a difference having a certain quantity (for all parts of phenomena are in their turn quantities). Consequently, every transition from one state into another, is always effected in a time contained between two moments, of which the first determines the state which the thing leaves, and the second determines the state into which the thing passes. Both moments, then, are limitations of the time of a change, consequently of the intermediate state between both, and as such they belong to the total of the change. Now every change has a cause, which evidences its causality in the whole time during which the charge takes place. The cause, therefore, does not produce the change all at once or in one moment, but in a time, so that, as the time gradually increases from the commencing instant, a, to its completion at b, in like manner also, the quantity of the reality (b - a) is generated through the lesser degrees which are contained between the first and last. All change is therefore possible only through a continuous action of the causality, which, in so far as it is uniform, we call a momentum. The change does not consist of these momenta, but is generated or produced by them as their effect.\n\nSuch is the law of the continuity of all change, the ground of which is that neither time itself nor any phenomenon in time consists of parts which are the smallest possible, but that, notwithstanding, the state of a thing passes in the process of a change through all these parts, as elements, to its second state. There is no smallest degree of reality in a phenomenon, just as there is no smallest degree in the quantity of time; and so the new state of reality grows up out of the former state, through all the infinite degrees thereof, the differences of which one from another, taken all together, are less than the difference between 0 and a.\n\nIt is not our business to inquire here into the utility of this principle in the investigation of nature. But how such a proposition, which appears so greatly to extend our knowledge of nature, is possible completely à priori, is indeed a question which deserves investigation, although the first view seems to demonstrate the truth and reality of the principle, and the question, how it is possible, may be considered superfluous. For there are so many groundless pretensions to the enlargement of our knowledge by pure reason that we must take it as a general rule to be mistrustful of all such, and without a thoroughgoing and radical deduction, to believe nothing of the sort even on the clearest dogmatical evidence.\n\nEvery addition to our empirical knowledge, and every advance made in the exercise of our perception, is nothing more than an extension of the determination of the internal sense, that is to say, a progression in time, be objects themselves what they may, phenomena, or pure intuitions. This progression in time determines everything, and is itself determined by nothing else. That is to say, the parts of the progression exist only in time, and by means of the synthesis thereof, and are not given antecedently to it. For this reason, every transition in perception to anything which follows upon another in time, is a determination of time by means of the production of this perception. And as this determination of time is, always and in all its parts, a quantity, the perception produced is to be considered as a quantity which proceeds through all its degrees—no one of which is the smallest possible—from zero up to its determined degree. From this we perceive the possibility of cognizing à priori a law of changes—a law, however, which concerns their form merely. We merely anticipate our own apprehension, the formal condition of which, inasmuch as it is itself to be found in the mind antecedently to all given phenomena, must certainly be capable of being cognized à priori.\n\nThus, as time contains the sensuous condition à priori of the possibility of a continuous progression of that which exists to that which follows it, the understanding, by virtue of the unity of apperception, contains the condition à priori of the possibility of a continuous determination of the position in time of all phenomena, and this by means of the series of causes and effects, the former of which necessitate the sequence of the latter, and thereby render universally and for all time, and by consequence, objectively, valid the empirical cognition of the relations of time.\n\nC. THIRD ANALOGY.\n\nPrinciple of Coexistence, According to the Law of Reciprocity or Community.\n\nAll substances, in so far as they can be perceived in space at the same time, exist in a state of complete reciprocity of action.\n\nPROOF.\n\nThings are coexistent, when in empirical intuition the perception of the one can follow upon the perception of the other, and vice versa—which cannot occur in the succession of phenomena, as we have shown in the explanation of the second principle. Thus I can perceive the moon and then the earth, or conversely, first the earth and then the moon; and for the reason that my perceptions of these objects can reciprocally follow each other, I say, they exist contemporaneously. Now coexistence is the existence of the manifold in the same time. But time itself is not an object of perception; and therefore we cannot conclude from the fact that things are placed in the same time, the other fact, that the perception of these things can follow each other reciprocally. The synthesis of the imagination in apprehension would only present to us each of these perceptions as present in the subject when the other is not present, and contrariwise; but would not show that the objects are coexistent, that is to say, that, if the one exists, the other also exists in the same time, and that this is necessarily so, in order that the perceptions may be capable of following each other reciprocally. It follows that a conception of the understanding or category of the reciprocal sequence of the determinations of phenomena (existing, as they do, apart from each other, and yet contemporaneously), is requisite to justify us in saying that the reciprocal succession of perceptions has its foundation in the object, and to enable us to represent coexistence as objective. But that relation of substances in which the one contains determinations the ground of which is in the other substance, is the relation of influence. And, when this influence is reciprocal, it is the relation of community or reciprocity. Consequently the coexistence of substances in space cannot be cognized in experience otherwise than under the precondition of their reciprocal action. This is therefore the condition of the possibility of things themselves as objects of experience.\n\nThings are coexistent, in so far as they exist in one and the same time. But how can we know that they exist in one and the same time? Only by observing that the order in the synthesis of apprehension of the manifold is arbitrary and a matter of indifference, that is to say, that it can proceed from A, through B, C, D, to E, or contrariwise from E to A. For if they were successive in time (and in the order, let us suppose, which begins with A), it is quite impossible for the apprehension in perception to begin with E and go backwards to A, inasmuch as A belongs to past time and, therefore, cannot be an object of apprehension.\n\nLet us assume that in a number of substances considered as phenomena each is completely isolated, that is, that no one acts upon another. Then I say that the coexistence of these cannot be an object of possible perception and that the existence of one cannot, by any mode of empirical synthesis, lead us to the existence of another. For we imagine them in this case to be separated by a completely void space, and thus perception, which proceeds from the one to the other in time, would indeed determine their existence by means of a following perception, but would be quite unable to distinguish whether the one phenomenon follows objectively upon the first, or is coexistent with it.\n\nBesides the mere fact of existence, then, there must be something by means of which A determines the position of B in time and, conversely, B the position of A; because only under this condition can substances be empirically represented as existing contemporaneously. Now that alone determines the position of another thing in time which is the cause of it or of its determinations. Consequently every substance (inasmuch as it can have succession predicated of it only in respect of its determinations) must contain the causality of certain determinations in another substance, and at the same time the effects of the causality of the other in itself. That is to say, substances must stand (mediately or immediately) in dynamical community with each other, if coexistence is to be cognized in any possible experience. But, in regard to objects of experience, that is absolutely necessary without which the experience of these objects would itself be impossible. Consequently it is absolutely necessary that all substances in the world of phenomena, in so far as they are coexistent, stand in a relation of complete community of reciprocal action to each other.\n\nThe word community has in our language[[33]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-33) two meanings, and contains the two notions conveyed in the Latin communio and commercium. We employ it in this place in the latter sense—that of a dynamical community, without which even the community of place (communio spatii) could not be empirically cognized. In our experiences it is easy to observe that it is only the continuous influences in all parts of space that can conduct our senses from one object to another; that the light which plays between our eyes and the heavenly bodies produces a mediating community between them and us, and thereby evidences their coexistence with us; that we cannot empirically change our position (perceive this change), unless the existence of matter throughout the whole of space rendered possible the perception of the positions we occupy; and that this perception can prove the contemporaneous existence of these places only through their reciprocal influence, and thereby also the coexistence of even the most remote objects—although in this case the proof is only mediate. Without community, every perception (of a phenomenon in space) is separated from every other and isolated, and the chain of empirical representations, that is, of experience, must, with the appearance of a new object, begin entirely de novo, without the least connection with preceding representations, and without standing towards these even in the relation of time. My intention here is by no means to combat the notion of empty space; for it may exist where our perceptions cannot exist, inasmuch as they cannot reach thereto, and where, therefore, no empirical perception of coexistence takes place. But in this case it is not an object of possible experience.\n\n[[33]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-33) German\n\nThe following remarks may be useful in the way of explanation. In the mind, all phenomena, as contents of a possible experience, must exist in community (communio) of apperception or consciousness, and in so far as it is requisite that objects be represented as coexistent and connected, in so far must they reciprocally determine the position in time of each other and thereby constitute a whole. If this subjective community is to rest upon an objective basis, or to be applied to substances as phenomena, the perception of one substance must render possible the perception of another, and conversely. For otherwise succession, which is always found in perceptions as apprehensions, would be predicated of external objects, and their representation of their coexistence be thus impossible. But this is a reciprocal influence, that is to say, a real community (commercium) of substances, without which therefore the empirical relation of coexistence would be a notion beyond the reach of our minds. By virtue of this commercium, phenomena, in so far as they are apart from, and nevertheless in connection with each other, constitute a compositum reale. Such composita are possible in many different ways. The three dynamical relations then, from which all others spring, are those of inherence, consequence, and composition.\n\nThese, then, are the three analogies of experience. They are nothing more than principles of the determination of the existence of phenomena in time, according to the three modi of this determination; to wit, the relation to time itself as a quantity (the quantity of existence, that is, duration), the relation in time as a series or succession, finally, the relation in time as the complex of all existence (simultaneity). This unity of determination in regard to time is thoroughly dynamical; that is to say, time is not considered as that in which experience determines immediately to every existence its position; for this is impossible, inasmuch as absolute time is not an object of perception, by means of which phenomena can be connected with each other. On the contrary, the rule of the understanding, through which alone the existence of phenomena can receive synthetical unity as regards relations of time, determines for every phenomenon its position in time, and consequently à priori, and with validity for all and every time.\n\nBy nature, in the empirical sense of the word, we understand the totality of phenomena connected, in respect of their existence, according to necessary rules, that is, laws. There are therefore certain laws (which are moreover à priori) which make nature possible; and all empirical laws can exist only by means of experience, and by virtue of those primitive laws through which experience itself becomes possible. The purpose of the analogies is therefore to represent to us the unity of nature in the connection of all phenomena under certain exponents, the only business of which is to express the relation of time (in so far as it contains all existence in itself) to the unity of apperception, which can exist in synthesis only according to rules. The combined expression of all is this: “All phenomena exist in one nature, and must so exist, inasmuch as without this à priori unity, no unity of experience, and consequently no determination of objects in experience, is possible.”\n\nAs regards the mode of proof which we have employed in treating of these transcendental laws of nature, and the peculiar character of it we must make one remark, which will at the same time be important as a guide in every other attempt to demonstrate the truth of intellectual and likewise synthetical propositions à priori. Had we endeavoured to prove these analogies dogmatically, that is, from conceptions; that is to say, had we employed this method in attempting to show that everything which exists, exists only in that which is permanent—that every thing or event presupposes the existence of something in a preceding state, upon which it follows in conformity with a rule—lastly, that in the manifold, which is coexistent, the states coexist in connection with each other according to a rule, all our labour would have been utterly in vain. For mere conceptions of things, analyse them as we may, cannot enable us to conclude from the existence of one object to the existence of another. What other course was left for us to pursue? This only, to demonstrate the possibility of experience as a cognition in which at last all objects must be capable of being presented to us, if the representation of them is to possess any objective reality. Now in this third, this mediating term, the essential form of which consists in the synthetical unity of the apperception of all phenomena, we found à priori conditions of the universal and necessary determination as to time of all existences in the world of phenomena, without which the empirical determination thereof as to time would itself be impossible, and we also discovered rules of synthetical unity à priori, by means of which we could anticipate experience. For want of this method, and from the fancy that it was possible to discover a dogmatical proof of the synthetical propositions which are requisite in the empirical employment of the understanding, has it happened that a proof of the principle of sufficient reason has been so often attempted, and always in vain. The other two analogies nobody has ever thought of, although they have always been silently employed by the mind,[[34]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-34) because the guiding thread furnished by the categories was wanting, the guide which alone can enable us to discover every hiatus, both in the system of conceptions and of principles.\n\n[[34]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-34) The unity of the universe, in which all phenomena to be connected, is evidently a mere consequence of the admitted principle of the community of all substances which are coexistent. For were substances isolated, they could not as parts constitute a whole, and were their connection (reciprocal action of the manifold) not necessary from the very fact of coexistence, we could not conclude from the fact of the latter as a merely ideal relation to the former as a real one. We have, however, shown in its place that community is the proper ground of the possibility of an empirical cognition of coexistence, and that we may therefore properly reason from the latter to the former as its condition.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","text":"# Experience\n\n3. ANALOGIES OF EXPERIENCE.\n\nThe principle of these is: Experience is possible only through the representation of a necessary connection of Perceptions.\n\nPROOF.\n\nExperience is an empirical cognition; that is to say, a cognition which determines an object by means of perceptions. It is therefore a synthesis of perceptions, a synthesis which is not itself contained in perception, but which contains the synthetical unity of the manifold of perception in a consciousness; and this unity constitutes the essential of our cognition of objects of the senses, that is, of experience (not merely of intuition or sensation). Now in experience our perceptions come together contingently, so that no character of necessity in their connection appears, or can appear from the perceptions themselves, because apprehension is only a placing together of the manifold of empirical intuition, and no representation of a necessity in the connected existence of the phenomena which apprehension brings together, is to be discovered therein. But as experience is a cognition of objects by means of perceptions, it follows that the relation of the existence of the existence of the manifold must be represented in experience not as it is put together in time, but as it is objectively in time. And as time itself cannot be perceived, the determination of the existence of objects in time can only take place by means of their connection in time in general, consequently only by means of à priori connecting conceptions. Now as these conceptions always possess the character of necessity, experience is possible only by means of a representation of the necessary connection of perception.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"50.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","text":"The three modi of time are permanence, succession, and coexistence. Accordingly, there are three rules of all relations of time in phenomena, according to which the existence of every phenomenon is determined in respect of the unity of all time, and these antecede all experience and render it possible.\n\nThe general principle of all three analogies rests on the necessary unity of apperception in relation to all possible empirical consciousness (perception) at every time, consequently, as this unity lies à priori at the foundation of all mental operations, the principle rests on the synthetical unity of all phenomena according to their relation in time. For the original apperception relates to our internal sense (the complex of all representations), and indeed relates à priori to its form, that is to say, the relation of the manifold empirical consciousness in time. Now this manifold must be combined in original apperception according to relations of time—a necessity imposed by the à priori transcendental unity of apperception, to which is subjected all that can belong to my (i.e., my own) cognition, and therefore all that can become an object for me. This synthetical and à priori determined unity in relation of perceptions in time is therefore the rule: “All empirical determinations of time must be subject to rules of the general determination of time”; and the analogies of experience, of which we are now about to treat, must be rules of this nature.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"50.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0003","text":"These principles have this peculiarity, that they do not concern phenomena, and the synthesis of the empirical intuition thereof, but merely the existence of phenomena and their relation to each other in regard to this existence. Now the mode in which we apprehend a thing in a phenomenon can be determined à priori in such a manner that the rule of its synthesis can give, that is to say, can produce this à priori intuition in every empirical example. But the existence of phenomena cannot be known à priori, and although we could arrive by this path at a conclusion of the fact of some existence, we could not cognize that existence determinately, that is to say, we should be incapable of anticipating in what respect the empirical intuition of it would be distinguishable from that of others.\n\nThe two principles above mentioned, which I called mathematical, in consideration of the fact of their authorizing the application of mathematic phenomena, relate to these phenomena only in regard to their possibility, and instruct us how phenomena, as far as regards their intuition or the real in their perception, can be generated according to the rules of a mathematical synthesis. Consequently, numerical quantities, and with them the determination of a phenomenon as a quantity, can be employed in the one case as well as in the other. Thus, for example, out of 200,000 illuminations by the moon, I might compose and give à priori, that is construct, the degree of our sensations of the sun-light.[[30]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-30) We may therefore entitle these two principles constitutive.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"50.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0004","text":"[[30]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-30) Kant’s meaning is: The two principles enunciated under the heads of “Axioms of Intuition,” and “Anticipations of Perception,” authorize the application to phenomena of determinations of size and number, that is of mathematic. For example, I may compute the light of the sun, and say that its quantity is a certain number of times greater than that of the moon. In the same way, heat is measured by the comparison of its different effects on water, &c., and on mercury in a thermometer.—Tr","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"50.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0005","text":"The case is very different with those principles whose province it is to subject the existence of phenomena to rules à priori. For as existence does not admit of being constructed, it is clear that they must only concern the relations of existence and be merely regulative principles. In this case, therefore, neither axioms nor anticipations are to be thought of. Thus, if a perception is given us, in a certain relation of time to other (although undetermined) perceptions, we cannot then say à priori, what and how great (in quantity) the other perception necessarily connected with the former is, but only how it is connected, quoad its existence, in this given modus of time. Analogies in philosophy mean something very different from that which they represent in mathematics. In the latter they are formulae, which enounce the equality of two relations of quantity, and are always constitutive, so that if two terms of the proportion are given, the third is also given, that is, can be constructed by the aid of these formulae. But in philosophy, analogy is not the equality of two quantitative but of two qualitative relations. In this case, from three given terms, I can give à priori and cognize the relation to a fourth member, but not this fourth term itself, although I certainly possess a rule to guide me in the search for this fourth term in experience, and a mark to assist me in discovering it. An analogy of experience is therefore only a rule according to which unity of experience must arise out of perceptions in respect to objects (phenomena) not as a constitutive, but merely as a regulative principle. The same holds good also of the postulates of empirical thought in general, which relate to the synthesis of mere intuition (which concerns the form of phenomena), the synthesis of perception (which concerns the matter of phenomena), and the synthesis of experience (which concerns the relation of these perceptions). For they are only regulative principles, and clearly distinguishable from the mathematical, which are constitutive, not indeed in regard to the certainty which both possess à priori, but in the mode of evidence thereof, consequently also in the manner of demonstration.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"50.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","text":"But what has been observed of all synthetical propositions, and must be particularly remarked in this place, is this, that these analogies possess significance and validity, not as principles of the transcendental, but only as principles of the empirical use of the understanding, and their truth can therefore be proved only as such, and that consequently the phenomena must not be subjoined directly under the categories, but only under their schemata. For if the objects to which those principles must be applied were things in themselves, it would be quite impossible to cognize aught concerning them synthetically à priori. But they are nothing but phenomena; a complete knowledge of which—a knowledge to which all principles à priori must at last relate—is the only possible experience. It follows that these principles can have nothing else for their aim than the conditions of the empirical cognition in the unity of synthesis of phenomena. But this synthesis is cogitated only in the schema of the pure conception of the understanding, of whose unity, as that of a synthesis in general, the category contains the function unrestricted by any sensuous condition. These principles will therefore authorize us to connect phenomena according to an analogy, with the logical and universal unity of conceptions, and consequently to employ the categories in the principles themselves; but in the application of them to experience, we shall use only their schemata, as the key to their proper application, instead of the categories, or rather the latter as restricting conditions, under the title of “formulae” of the former.\n\nA. FIRST ANALOGY.\n\nPrinciple of the Permanence of Substance.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"50.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","text":"In all changes of phenomena, substance is permanent, and the quantum thereof in nature is neither increased nor diminished.\n\nPROOF.\n\nAll phenomena exist in time, wherein alone as substratum, that is, as the permanent form of the internal intuition, coexistence and succession can be represented. Consequently time, in which all changes of phenomena must be cogitated, remains and changes not, because it is that in which succession and coexistence can be represented only as determinations thereof. Now, time in itself cannot be an object of perception. It follows that in objects of perception, that is, in phenomena, there must be found a substratum which represents time in general, and in which all change or coexistence can be perceived by means of the relation of phenomena to it. But the substratum of all reality, that is, of all that pertains to the existence of things, is substance; all that pertains to existence can be cogitated only as a determination of substance. Consequently, the permanent, in relation to which alone can all relations of time in phenomena be determined, is substance in the world of phenomena, that is, the real in phenomena, that which, as the substratum of all change, remains ever the same. Accordingly, as this cannot change in existence, its quantity in nature can neither be increased nor diminished.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"50.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0008","text":"Our apprehension of the manifold in a phenomenon is always successive, is Consequently always changing. By it alone we could, therefore, never determine whether this manifold, as an object of experience, is coexistent or successive, unless it had for a foundation something fixed and permanent, of the existence of which all succession and coexistence are nothing but so many modes (modi of time). Only in the permanent, then, are relations of time possible (for simultaneity and succession are the only relations in time); that is to say, the permanent is the substratum of our empirical representation of time itself, in which alone all determination of time is possible. Permanence is, in fact, just another expression for time, as the abiding correlate of all existence of phenomena, and of all change, and of all coexistence. For change does not affect time itself, but only the phenomena in time (just as coexistence cannot be regarded as a modus of time itself, seeing that in time no parts are coexistent, but all successive). If we were to attribute succession to time itself, we should be obliged to cogitate another time, in which this succession would be possible. It is only by means of the permanent that existence in different parts of the successive series of time receives a quantity, which we entitle duration. For in mere succession, existence is perpetually vanishing and recommencing, and therefore never has even the least quantity. Without the permanent, then, no relation in time is possible. Now, time in itself is not an object of perception; consequently the permanent in phenomena must be regarded as the substratum of all determination of time, and consequently also as the condition of the possibility of all synthetical unity of perceptions, that is, of experience; and all existence and all change in time can only be regarded as a mode in the existence of that which abides unchangeably. Therefore, in all phenomena, the permanent is the object in itself, that is, the substance (phenomenon); but all that changes or can change belongs only to the mode of the existence of this substance or substances, consequently to its determinations.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"50.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0009","text":"I find that in all ages not only the philosopher, but even the common understanding, has preposited this permanence as a substratum of all change in phenomena; indeed, I am compelled to believe that they will always accept this as an indubitable fact. Only the philosopher expresses himself in a more precise and definite manner, when he says: “In all changes in the world, the substance remains, and the accidents alone are changeable.” But of this decidedly synthetical proposition, I nowhere meet with even an attempt at proof; nay, it very rarely has the good fortune to stand, as it deserves to do, at the head of the pure and entirely à priori laws of nature. In truth, the statement that substance is permanent, is tautological. For this very permanence is the ground on which we apply the category of substance to the phenomenon; and we should have been obliged to prove that in all phenomena there is something permanent, of the existence of which the changeable is nothing but a determination. But because a proof of this nature cannot be dogmatical, that is, cannot be drawn from conceptions, inasmuch as it concerns a synthetical proposition à priori, and as philosophers never reflected that such propositions are valid only in relation to possible experience, and therefore cannot be proved except by means of a deduction of the possibility of experience, it is no wonder that while it has served as the foundation of all experience (for we feel the need of it in empirical cognition), it has never been supported by proof.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"50.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0010","text":"A philosopher was asked: “What is the weight of smoke?” He answered: “Subtract from the weight of the burnt wood the weight of the remaining ashes, and you will have the weight of the smoke.” Thus he presumed it to be incontrovertible that even in fire the matter (substance) does not perish, but that only the form of it undergoes a change. In like manner was the saying: “From nothing comes nothing,” only another inference from the principle or permanence, or rather of the ever-abiding existence of the true subject in phenomena. For if that in the phenomenon which we call substance is to be the proper substratum of all determination of time, it follows that all existence in past as well as in future time, must be determinable by means of it alone. Hence we are entitled to apply the term substance to a phenomenon, only because we suppose its existence in all time, a notion which the word permanence does not fully express, as it seems rather to be referable to future time. However, the internal necessity perpetually to be, is inseparably connected with the necessity always to have been, and so the expression may stand as it is. “Gigni de nihilo nihil; in nihilum nil posse reverti,”[[31]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-31) are two propositions which the ancients never parted, and which people nowadays sometimes mistakenly disjoin, because they imagine that the propositions apply to objects as things in themselves, and that the former might be inimical to the dependence (even in respect of its substance also) of the world upon a supreme cause. But this apprehension is entirely needless, for the question in this case is only of phenomena in the sphere of experience, the unity of which never could be possible, if we admitted the possibility that new things (in respect of their substance) should arise. For in that case, we should lose altogether that which alone can represent the unity of time, to wit, the identity of the substratum, as that through which alone all change possesses complete and thorough unity. This permanence is, however, nothing but the manner in which we represent to ourselves the existence of things in the phenomenal world.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"50.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0011","text":"[[31]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-31) Persius, Satirae, iii.83-84.\n\nThe determinations of a substance, which are only particular modes of its existence, are called accidents. They are always real, because they concern the existence of substance (negations are only determinations, which express the non-existence of something in the substance). Now, if to this real in the substance we ascribe a particular existence (for example, to motion as an accident of matter), this existence is called inherence, in contradistinction to the existence of substance, which we call subsistence. But hence arise many misconceptions, and it would be a more accurate and just mode of expression to designate the accident only as the mode in which the existence of a substance is positively determined. Meanwhile, by reason of the conditions of the logical exercise of our understanding, it is impossible to avoid separating, as it were, that which in the existence of a substance is subject to change, whilst the substance remains, and regarding it in relation to that which is properly permanent and radical. On this account, this category of substance stands under the title of relation, rather because it is the condition thereof than because it contains in itself any relation.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"50.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0012","text":"Now, upon this notion of permanence rests the proper notion of the conception change. Origin and extinction are not changes of that which originates or becomes extinct. Change is but a mode of existence, which follows on another mode of existence of the same object; hence all that changes is permanent, and only the condition thereof changes. Now since this mutation affects only determinations, which can have a beginning or an end, we may say, employing an expression which seems somewhat paradoxical: “Only the permanent (substance) is subject to change; the mutable suffers no change, but rather alternation, that is, when certain determinations cease, others begin.”\n\nChange, when, cannot be perceived by us except in substances, and origin or extinction in an absolute sense, that does not concern merely a determination of the permanent, cannot be a possible perception, for it is this very notion of the permanent which renders possible the representation of a transition from one state into another, and from non-being to being, which, consequently, can be empirically cognized only as alternating determinations of that which is permanent. Grant that a thing absolutely begins to be; we must then have a point of time in which it was not. But how and by what can we fix and determine this point of time, unless by that which already exists? For a void time—preceding—is not an object of perception; but if we connect this beginning with objects which existed previously, and which continue to exist till the object in question in question begins to be, then the latter can only be a determination of the former as the permanent. The same holds good of the notion of extinction, for this presupposes the empirical representation of a time, in which a phenomenon no longer exists.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"50.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0013","text":"Substances (in the world of phenomena) are the substratum of all determinations of time. The beginning of some, and the ceasing to be of other substances, would utterly do away with the only condition of the empirical unity of time; and in that case phenomena would relate to two different times, in which, side by side, existence would pass; which is absurd. For there is only one time in which all different times must be placed, not as coexistent, but as successive.\n\nAccordingly, permanence is a necessary condition under which alone phenomena, as things or objects, are determinable in a possible experience. But as regards the empirical criterion of this necessary permanence, and with it of the substantiality of phenomena, we shall find sufficient opportunity to speak in the sequel.\n\nB. SECOND ANALOGY.\n\nPrinciple of the Succession of Time According to the Law of Causality. All changes take place according to the law of the connection of Cause and Effect.\n\nPROOF.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"50.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0014","text":"(That all phenomena in the succession of time are only changes, that is, a successive being and non-being of the determinations of substance, which is permanent; consequently that a being of substance itself which follows on the non-being thereof, or a non-being of substance which follows on the being thereof, in other words, that the origin or extinction of substance itself, is impossible—all this has been fully established in treating of the foregoing principle. This principle might have been expressed as follows: “All alteration (succession) of phenomena is merely change”; for the changes of substance are not origin or extinction, because the conception of change presupposes the same subject as existing with two opposite determinations, and consequently as permanent. After this premonition, we shall proceed to the proof.)","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"50.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0015","text":"I perceive that phenomena succeed one another, that is to say, a state of things exists at one time, the opposite of which existed in a former state. In this case, then, I really connect together two perceptions in time. Now connection is not an operation of mere sense and intuition, but is the product of a synthetical faculty of imagination, which determines the internal sense in respect of a relation of time. But imagination can connect these two states in two ways, so that either the one or the other may antecede in time; for time in itself cannot be an object of perception, and what in an object precedes and what follows cannot be empirically determined in relation to it. I am only conscious, then, that my imagination places one state before and the other after; not that the one state antecedes the other in the object. In other words, the objective relation of the successive phenomena remains quite undetermined by means of mere perception. Now in order that this relation may be cognized as determined, the relation between the two states must be so cogitated that it is thereby determined as necessary, which of them must be placed before and which after, and not conversely. But the conception which carries with it a necessity of synthetical unity, can be none other than a pure conception of the understanding which does not lie in mere perception; and in this case it is the conception of “the relation of cause and effect,” the former of which determines the latter in time, as its necessary consequence, and not as something which might possibly antecede (or which might in some cases not be perceived to follow). It follows that it is only because we subject the sequence of phenomena, and consequently all change, to the law of causality, that experience itself, that is, empirical cognition of phenomena, becomes possible; and consequently, that phenomena themselves, as objects of experience, are possible only by virtue of this law.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"50.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0016","text":"Our apprehension of the manifold of phenomena is always successive. The representations of parts succeed one another. Whether they succeed one another in the object also, is a second point for reflection, which was not contained in the former. Now we may certainly give the name of object to everything, even to every representation, so far as we are conscious thereof; but what this word may mean in the case of phenomena, not merely in so far as they (as representations) are objects, but only in so far as they indicate an object, is a question requiring deeper consideration. In so far as they, regarded merely as representations, are at the same time objects of consciousness, they are not to be distinguished from apprehension, that is, reception into the synthesis of imagination, and we must therefore say: “The manifold of phenomena is always produced successively in the mind.” If phenomena were things in themselves, no man would be able to conjecture from the succession of our representations how this manifold is connected in the object; for we have to do only with our representations. How things may be in themselves, without regard to the representations through which they affect us, is utterly beyond the sphere of our cognition. Now although phenomena are not things in themselves, and are nevertheless the only thing given to us to be cognized, it is my duty to show what sort of connection in time belongs to the manifold in phenomena themselves, while the representation of this manifold in apprehension is always successive. For example, the apprehension of the manifold in the phenomenon of a house which stands before me, is successive. Now comes the question whether the manifold of this house is in itself successive—which no one will be at all willing to grant. But, so soon as I raise my conception of an object to the transcendental signification thereof, I find that the house is not a thing in itself, but only a phenomenon, that is, a representation, the transcendental object of which remains utterly unknown. What then am I to understand by the question: “How can the manifold be connected in the phenomenon itself—not considered as a thing in itself, but merely as a phenomenon?” Here that which lies in my successive apprehension is regarded as representation, whilst the phenomenon which is given me, notwithstanding that it is nothing more than a complex of these representations, is regarded as the object thereof, with which my conception, drawn from the representations of apprehension, must harmonize. It is very soon seen that, as accordance of the cognition with its object constitutes truth, the question now before us can only relate to the formal conditions of empirical truth; and that the phenomenon, in opposition to the representations of apprehension, can only be distinguished therefrom as the object of them, if it is subject to a rule which distinguishes it from every other apprehension, and which renders necessary a mode of connection of the manifold. That in the phenomenon which contains the condition of this necessary rule of apprehension, is the object.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"50.16"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0017","text":"Let us now proceed to our task. That something happens, that is to say, that something or some state exists which before was not, cannot be empirically perceived, unless a phenomenon precedes, which does not contain in itself this state. For a reality which should follow upon a void time, in other words, a beginning, which no state of things precedes, can just as little be apprehended as the void time itself. Every apprehension of an event is therefore a perception which follows upon another perception. But as this is the case with all synthesis of apprehension, as I have shown above in the example of a house, my apprehension of an event is not yet sufficiently distinguished from other apprehensions. But I remark also that if in a phenomenon which contains an occurrence, I call the antecedent state of my perception, A, and the following state, B, the perception B can only follow A in apprehension, and the perception A cannot follow B, but only precede it. For example, I see a ship float down the stream of a river. My perception of its place lower down follows upon my perception of its place higher up the course of the river, and it is impossible that, in the apprehension of this phenomenon, the vessel should be perceived first below and afterwards higher up the stream. Here, therefore, the order in the sequence of perceptions in apprehension is determined; and by this order apprehension is regulated. In the former example, my perceptions in the apprehension of a house might begin at the roof and end at the foundation, or vice versa; or I might apprehend the manifold in this empirical intuition, by going from left to right, and from right to left. Accordingly, in the series of these perceptions, there was no determined order, which necessitated my beginning at a certain point, in order empirically to connect the manifold. But this rule is always to be met with in the perception of that which happens, and it makes the order of the successive perceptions in the apprehension of such a phenomenon necessary.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"50.17"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0018","text":"I must, therefore, in the present case, deduce the subjective sequence of apprehension from the objective sequence of phenomena, for otherwise the former is quite undetermined, and one phenomenon is not distinguishable from another. The former alone proves nothing as to the connection of the manifold in an object, for it is quite arbitrary. The latter must consist in the order of the manifold in a phenomenon, according to which order the apprehension of one thing (that which happens) follows that of another thing (which precedes), in conformity with a rule. In this way alone can I be authorized to say of the phenomenon itself, and not merely of my own apprehension, that a certain order or sequence is to be found therein. That is, in other words, I cannot arrange my apprehension otherwise than in this order.\n\nIn conformity with this rule, then, it is necessary that in that which antecedes an event there be found the condition of a rule, according to which in this event follows always and necessarily; but I cannot reverse this and go back from the event, and determine (by apprehension) that which antecedes it. For no phenomenon goes back from the succeeding point of time to the preceding point, although it does certainly relate to a preceding point of time; from a given time, on the other hand, there is always a necessary progression to the determined succeeding time. Therefore, because there certainly is something that follows, I must of necessity connect it with something else, which antecedes, and upon which it follows, in conformity with a rule, that is necessarily, so that the event, as conditioned, affords certain indication of a condition, and this condition determines the event.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"50.18"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0019","text":"Let us suppose that nothing precedes an event, upon which this event must follow in conformity with a rule. All sequence of perception would then exist only in apprehension, that is to say, would be merely subjective, and it could not thereby be objectively determined what thing ought to precede, and what ought to follow in perception. In such a case, we should have nothing but a play of representations, which would possess no application to any object. That is to say, it would not be possible through perception to distinguish one phenomenon from another, as regards relations of time; because the succession in the act of apprehension would always be of the same sort, and therefore there would be nothing in the phenomenon to determine the succession, and to render a certain sequence objectively necessary. And, in this case, I cannot say that two states in a phenomenon follow one upon the other, but only that one apprehension follows upon another. But this is merely subjective, and does not determine an object, and consequently cannot be held to be cognition of an object—not even in the phenomenal world.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"50.19"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0020","text":"Accordingly, when we know in experience that something happens, we always presuppose that something precedes, whereupon it follows in conformity with a rule. For otherwise I could not say of the object that it follows; because the mere succession in my apprehension, if it be not determined by a rule in relation to something preceding, does not authorize succession in the object. Only, therefore, in reference to a rule, according to which phenomena are determined in their sequence, that is, as they happen, by the preceding state, can I make my subjective synthesis (of apprehension) objective, and it is only under this presupposition that even the experience of an event is possible.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"50.20"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0021","text":"No doubt it appears as if this were in thorough contradiction to all the notions which people have hitherto entertained in regard to the procedure of the human understanding. According to these opinions, it is by means of the perception and comparison of similar consequences following upon certain antecedent phenomena that the understanding is led to the discovery of a rule, according to which certain events always follow certain phenomena, and it is only by this process that we attain to the conception of cause. Upon such a basis, it is clear that this conception must be merely empirical, and the rule which it furnishes us with—“Everything that happens must have a cause”—would be just as contingent as experience itself. The universality and necessity of the rule or law would be perfectly spurious attributes of it. Indeed, it could not possess universal validity, inasmuch as it would not in this case be à priori, but founded on deduction. But the same is the case with this law as with other pure à priori representations (e.g., space and time), which we can draw in perfect clearness and completeness from experience, only because we had already placed them therein, and by that means, and by that alone, had rendered experience possible. Indeed, the logical clearness of this representation of a rule, determining the series of events, is possible only when we have made use thereof in experience. Nevertheless, the recognition of this rule, as a condition of the synthetical unity of phenomena in time, was the ground of experience itself and consequently preceded it à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"50.21"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0022","text":"It is now our duty to show by an example that we never, even in experience, attribute to an object the notion of succession or effect (of an event—that is, the happening of something that did not exist before), and distinguish it from the subjective succession of apprehension, unless when a rule lies at the foundation, which compels us to observe this order of perception in preference to any other, and that, indeed, it is this necessity which first renders possible the representation of a succession in the object.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"50.22"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0023","text":"We have representations within us, of which also we can be conscious. But, however widely extended, however accurate and thoroughgoing this consciousness may be, these representations are still nothing more than representations, that is, internal determinations of the mind in this or that relation of time. Now how happens it that to these representations we should set an object, or that, in addition to their subjective reality, as modifications, we should still further attribute to them a certain unknown objective reality? It is clear that objective significancy cannot consist in a relation to another representation (of that which we desire to term object), for in that case the question again arises: “How does this other representation go out of itself, and obtain objective significancy over and above the subjective, which is proper to it, as a determination of a state of mind?” If we try to discover what sort of new property the relation to an object gives to our subjective representations, and what new importance they thereby receive, we shall find that this relation has no other effect than that of rendering necessary the connection of our representations in a certain manner, and of subjecting them to a rule; and that conversely, it is only because a certain order is necessary in the relations of time of our representations, that objective significancy is ascribed to them.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"50.23"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0024","text":"In the synthesis of phenomena, the manifold of our representations is always successive. Now hereby is not represented an object, for by means of this succession, which is common to all apprehension, no one thing is distinguished from another. But so soon as I perceive or assume that in this succession there is a relation to a state antecedent, from which the representation follows in accordance with a rule, so soon do I represent something as an event, or as a thing that happens; in other words, I cognize an object to which I must assign a certain determinate position in time, which cannot be altered, because of the preceding state in the object. When, therefore, I perceive that something happens, there is contained in this representation, in the first place, the fact, that something antecedes; because, it is only in relation to this that the phenomenon obtains its proper relation of time, in other words, exists after an antecedent time, in which it did not exist. But it can receive its determined place in time only by the presupposition that something existed in the foregoing state, upon which it follows inevitably and always, that is, in conformity with a rule. From all this it is evident that, in the first place, I cannot reverse the order of succession, and make that which happens precede that upon which it follows; and that, in the second place, if the antecedent state be posited, a certain determinate event inevitably and necessarily follows. Hence it follows that there exists a certain order in our representations, whereby the present gives a sure indication of some previously existing state, as a correlate, though still undetermined, of the existing event which is given—a correlate which itself relates to the event as its consequence, conditions it, and connects it necessarily with itself in the series of time.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"50.24"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0025","text":"If then it be admitted as a necessary law of sensibility, and consequently a formal condition of all perception, that the preceding necessarily determines the succeeding time (inasmuch as I cannot arrive at the succeeding except through the preceding), it must likewise be an indispensable law of empirical representation of the series of time that the phenomena of the past determine all phenomena in the succeeding time, and that the latter, as events, cannot take place, except in so far as the former determine their existence in time, that is to say, establish it according to a rule. For it is of course only in phenomena that we can empirically cognize this continuity in the connection of times.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"50.25"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0026","text":"For all experience and for the possibility of experience, understanding is indispensable, and the first step which it takes in this sphere is not to render the representation of objects clear, but to render the representation of an object in general, possible. It does this by applying the order of time to phenomena, and their existence. In other words, it assigns to each phenomenon, as a consequence, a place in relation to preceding phenomena, determined à priori in time, without which it could not harmonize with time itself, which determines a place à priori to all its parts. This determination of place cannot be derived from the relation of phenomena to absolute time (for it is not an object of perception); but, on the contrary, phenomena must reciprocally determine the places in time of one another, and render these necessary in the order of time. In other words, whatever follows or happens, must follow in conformity with a universal rule upon that which was contained in the foregoing state. Hence arises a series of phenomena, which, by means of the understanding, produces and renders necessary exactly the same order and continuous connection in the series of our possible perceptions, as is found à priori in the form of internal intuition (time), in which all our perceptions must have place.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"50.26"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0027","text":"That something happens, then, is a perception which belongs to a possible experience, which becomes real only because I look upon the phenomenon as determined in regard to its place in time, consequently as an object, which can always be found by means of a rule in the connected series of my perceptions. But this rule of the determination of a thing according to succession in time is as follows: “In what precedes may be found the condition, under which an event always (that is, necessarily) follows.” From all this it is obvious that the principle of cause and effect is the principle of possible experience, that is, of objective cognition of phenomena, in regard to their relations in the succession of time.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"50.27"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0028","text":"The proof of this fundamental proposition rests entirely on the following momenta of argument. To all empirical cognition belongs the synthesis of the manifold by the imagination, a synthesis which is always successive, that is, in which the representations therein always follow one another. But the order of succession in imagination is not determined, and the series of successive representations may be taken retrogressively as well as progressively. But if this synthesis is a synthesis of apprehension (of the manifold of a given phenomenon), then the order is determined in the object, or to speak more accurately, there is therein an order of successive synthesis which determines an object, and according to which something necessarily precedes, and when this is posited, something else necessarily follows. If, then, my perception is to contain the cognition of an event, that is, of something which really happens, it must be an empirical judgement, wherein we think that the succession is determined; that is, it presupposes another phenomenon, upon which this event follows necessarily, or in conformity with a rule. If, on the contrary, when I posited the antecedent, the event did not necessarily follow, I should be obliged to consider it merely as a subjective play of my imagination, and if in this I represented to myself anything as objective, I must look upon it as a mere dream. Thus, the relation of phenomena (as possible perceptions), according to which that which happens is, as to its existence, necessarily determined in time by something which antecedes, in conformity with a rule—in other words, the relation of cause and effect—is the condition of the objective validity of our empirical judgements in regard to the sequence of perceptions, consequently of their empirical truth, and therefore of experience. The principle of the relation of causality in the succession of phenomena is therefore valid for all objects of experience, because it is itself the ground of the possibility of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"50.28"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0029","text":"Here, however, a difficulty arises, which must be resolved. The principle of the connection of causality among phenomena is limited in our formula to the succession thereof, although in practice we find that the principle applies also when the phenomena exist together in the same time, and that cause and effect may be simultaneous. For example, there is heat in a room, which does not exist in the open air. I look about for the cause, and find it to be the fire, Now the fire as the cause is simultaneous with its effect, the heat of the room. In this case, then, there is no succession as regards time, between cause and effect, but they are simultaneous; and still the law holds good. The greater part of operating causes in nature are simultaneous with their effects, and the succession in time of the latter is produced only because the cause cannot achieve the total of its effect in one moment. But at the moment when the effect first arises, it is always simultaneous with the causality of its cause, because, if the cause had but a moment before ceased to be, the effect could not have arisen. Here it must be specially remembered that we must consider the order of time and not the lapse thereof. The relation remains, even though no time has elapsed. The time between the causality of the cause and its immediate effect may entirely vanish, and the cause and effect be thus simultaneous, but the relation of the one to the other remains always determinable according to time. If, for example, I consider a leaden ball, which lies upon a cushion and makes a hollow in it, as a cause, then it is simultaneous with the effect. But I distinguish the two through the relation of time of the dynamical connection of both. For if I lay the ball upon the cushion, then the hollow follows upon the before smooth surface; but supposing the cushion has, from some cause or another, a hollow, there does not thereupon follow a leaden ball.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"50.29"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0030","text":"Thus, the law of succession of time is in all instances the only empirical criterion of effect in relation to the causality of the antecedent cause. The glass is the cause of the rising of the water above its horizontal surface, although the two phenomena are contemporaneous. For, as soon as I draw some water with the glass from a larger vessel, an effect follows thereupon, namely, the change of the horizontal state which the water had in the large vessel into a concave, which it assumes in the glass.\n\nThis conception of causality leads us to the conception of action; that of action, to the conception of force; and through it, to the conception of substance. As I do not wish this critical essay, the sole purpose of which is to treat of the sources of our synthetical cognition à priori, to be crowded with analyses which merely explain, but do not enlarge the sphere of our conceptions, I reserve the detailed explanation of the above conceptions for a future system of pure reason. Such an analysis, indeed, executed with great particularity, may already be found in well-known works on this subject. But I cannot at present refrain from making a few remarks on the empirical criterion of a substance, in so far as it seems to be more evident and more easily recognized through the conception of action than through that of the permanence of a phenomenon.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"50.30"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0031","text":"Where action (consequently activity and force) exists, substance also must exist, and in it alone must be sought the seat of that fruitful source of phenomena. Very well. But if we are called upon to explain what we mean by substance, and wish to avoid the vice of reasoning in a circle, the answer is by no means so easy. How shall we conclude immediately from the action to the permanence of that which acts, this being nevertheless an essential and peculiar criterion of substance (phenomenon)? But after what has been said above, the solution of this question becomes easy enough, although by the common mode of procedure—merely analysing our conceptions—it would be quite impossible. The conception of action indicates the relation of the subject of causality to the effect. Now because all effect consists in that which happens, therefore in the changeable, the last subject thereof is the permanent, as the substratum of all that changes, that is, substance. For according to the principle of causality, actions are always the first ground of all change in phenomena and, consequently, cannot be a property of a subject which itself changes, because if this were the case, other actions and another subject would be necessary to determine this change. From all this it results that action alone, as an empirical criterion, is a sufficient proof of the presence of substantiality, without any necessity on my part of endeavouring to discover the permanence of substance by a comparison. Besides, by this mode of induction we could not attain to the completeness which the magnitude and strict universality of the conception requires. For that the primary subject of the causality of all arising and passing away, all origin and extinction, cannot itself (in the sphere of phenomena) arise and pass away, is a sound and safe conclusion, a conclusion which leads us to the conception of empirical necessity and permanence in existence, and consequently to the conception of a substance as phenomenon.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"50.31"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0032","text":"When something happens, the mere fact of the occurrence, without regard to that which occurs, is an object requiring investigation. The transition from the non-being of a state into the existence of it, supposing that this state contains no quality which previously existed in the phenomenon, is a fact of itself demanding inquiry. Such an event, as has been shown in No. A, does not concern substance (for substance does not thus originate), but its condition or state. It is therefore only change, and not origin from nothing. If this origin be regarded as the effect of a foreign cause, it is termed creation, which cannot be admitted as an event among phenomena, because the very possibility of it would annihilate the unity of experience. If, however, I regard all things not as phenomena, but as things in themselves and objects of understanding alone, they, although substances, may be considered as dependent, in respect of their existence, on a foreign cause. But this would require a very different meaning in the words, a meaning which could not apply to phenomena as objects of possible experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"50.32"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0033","text":"How a thing can be changed, how it is possible that upon one state existing in one point of time, an opposite state should follow in another point of time—of this we have not the smallest conception à priori. There is requisite for this the knowledge of real powers, which can only be given empirically; for example, knowledge of moving forces, or, in other words, of certain successive phenomena (as movements) which indicate the presence of such forces. But the form of every change, the condition under which alone it can take place as the coming into existence of another state (be the content of the change, that is, the state which is changed, what it may), and consequently the succession of the states themselves can very well be considered à priori, in relation to the law of causality and the conditions of time.[[32]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-32)\n\n[[32]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-32) It must be remarked that I do not speak of the change of certain relations, but of the change of the state. Thus, when a body moves in a uniform manner, it does not change its state (of motion); but only when all motion increases or decreases.\n\nWhen a substance passes from one state, a, into another state, b, the point of time in which the latter exists is different from, and subsequent to that in which the former existed. In like manner, the second state, as reality (in the phenomenon), differs from the first, in which the reality of the second did not exist, as b from zero. That is to say, if the state, b, differs from the state, a, only in respect to quantity, the change is a coming into existence of b -a, which in the former state did not exist, and in relation to which that state is = O.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"50.33"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0034","text":"Now the question arises how a thing passes from one state = a, into another state = b. Between two moments there is always a certain time, and between two states existing in these moments there is always a difference having a certain quantity (for all parts of phenomena are in their turn quantities). Consequently, every transition from one state into another, is always effected in a time contained between two moments, of which the first determines the state which the thing leaves, and the second determines the state into which the thing passes. Both moments, then, are limitations of the time of a change, consequently of the intermediate state between both, and as such they belong to the total of the change. Now every change has a cause, which evidences its causality in the whole time during which the charge takes place. The cause, therefore, does not produce the change all at once or in one moment, but in a time, so that, as the time gradually increases from the commencing instant, a, to its completion at b, in like manner also, the quantity of the reality (b - a) is generated through the lesser degrees which are contained between the first and last. All change is therefore possible only through a continuous action of the causality, which, in so far as it is uniform, we call a momentum. The change does not consist of these momenta, but is generated or produced by them as their effect.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"50.34"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0035","text":"Such is the law of the continuity of all change, the ground of which is that neither time itself nor any phenomenon in time consists of parts which are the smallest possible, but that, notwithstanding, the state of a thing passes in the process of a change through all these parts, as elements, to its second state. There is no smallest degree of reality in a phenomenon, just as there is no smallest degree in the quantity of time; and so the new state of reality grows up out of the former state, through all the infinite degrees thereof, the differences of which one from another, taken all together, are less than the difference between 0 and a.\n\nIt is not our business to inquire here into the utility of this principle in the investigation of nature. But how such a proposition, which appears so greatly to extend our knowledge of nature, is possible completely à priori, is indeed a question which deserves investigation, although the first view seems to demonstrate the truth and reality of the principle, and the question, how it is possible, may be considered superfluous. For there are so many groundless pretensions to the enlargement of our knowledge by pure reason that we must take it as a general rule to be mistrustful of all such, and without a thoroughgoing and radical deduction, to believe nothing of the sort even on the clearest dogmatical evidence.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"50.35"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0036","text":"Every addition to our empirical knowledge, and every advance made in the exercise of our perception, is nothing more than an extension of the determination of the internal sense, that is to say, a progression in time, be objects themselves what they may, phenomena, or pure intuitions. This progression in time determines everything, and is itself determined by nothing else. That is to say, the parts of the progression exist only in time, and by means of the synthesis thereof, and are not given antecedently to it. For this reason, every transition in perception to anything which follows upon another in time, is a determination of time by means of the production of this perception. And as this determination of time is, always and in all its parts, a quantity, the perception produced is to be considered as a quantity which proceeds through all its degrees—no one of which is the smallest possible—from zero up to its determined degree. From this we perceive the possibility of cognizing à priori a law of changes—a law, however, which concerns their form merely. We merely anticipate our own apprehension, the formal condition of which, inasmuch as it is itself to be found in the mind antecedently to all given phenomena, must certainly be capable of being cognized à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"50.36"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0037","text":"Thus, as time contains the sensuous condition à priori of the possibility of a continuous progression of that which exists to that which follows it, the understanding, by virtue of the unity of apperception, contains the condition à priori of the possibility of a continuous determination of the position in time of all phenomena, and this by means of the series of causes and effects, the former of which necessitate the sequence of the latter, and thereby render universally and for all time, and by consequence, objectively, valid the empirical cognition of the relations of time.\n\nC. THIRD ANALOGY.\n\nPrinciple of Coexistence, According to the Law of Reciprocity or Community.\n\nAll substances, in so far as they can be perceived in space at the same time, exist in a state of complete reciprocity of action.\n\nPROOF.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"50.37"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0038","text":"Things are coexistent, when in empirical intuition the perception of the one can follow upon the perception of the other, and vice versa—which cannot occur in the succession of phenomena, as we have shown in the explanation of the second principle. Thus I can perceive the moon and then the earth, or conversely, first the earth and then the moon; and for the reason that my perceptions of these objects can reciprocally follow each other, I say, they exist contemporaneously. Now coexistence is the existence of the manifold in the same time. But time itself is not an object of perception; and therefore we cannot conclude from the fact that things are placed in the same time, the other fact, that the perception of these things can follow each other reciprocally. The synthesis of the imagination in apprehension would only present to us each of these perceptions as present in the subject when the other is not present, and contrariwise; but would not show that the objects are coexistent, that is to say, that, if the one exists, the other also exists in the same time, and that this is necessarily so, in order that the perceptions may be capable of following each other reciprocally. It follows that a conception of the understanding or category of the reciprocal sequence of the determinations of phenomena (existing, as they do, apart from each other, and yet contemporaneously), is requisite to justify us in saying that the reciprocal succession of perceptions has its foundation in the object, and to enable us to represent coexistence as objective. But that relation of substances in which the one contains determinations the ground of which is in the other substance, is the relation of influence. And, when this influence is reciprocal, it is the relation of community or reciprocity. Consequently the coexistence of substances in space cannot be cognized in experience otherwise than under the precondition of their reciprocal action. This is therefore the condition of the possibility of things themselves as objects of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"50.38"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0039","text":"Things are coexistent, in so far as they exist in one and the same time. But how can we know that they exist in one and the same time? Only by observing that the order in the synthesis of apprehension of the manifold is arbitrary and a matter of indifference, that is to say, that it can proceed from A, through B, C, D, to E, or contrariwise from E to A. For if they were successive in time (and in the order, let us suppose, which begins with A), it is quite impossible for the apprehension in perception to begin with E and go backwards to A, inasmuch as A belongs to past time and, therefore, cannot be an object of apprehension.\n\nLet us assume that in a number of substances considered as phenomena each is completely isolated, that is, that no one acts upon another. Then I say that the coexistence of these cannot be an object of possible perception and that the existence of one cannot, by any mode of empirical synthesis, lead us to the existence of another. For we imagine them in this case to be separated by a completely void space, and thus perception, which proceeds from the one to the other in time, would indeed determine their existence by means of a following perception, but would be quite unable to distinguish whether the one phenomenon follows objectively upon the first, or is coexistent with it.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"50.39"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0040","text":"Besides the mere fact of existence, then, there must be something by means of which A determines the position of B in time and, conversely, B the position of A; because only under this condition can substances be empirically represented as existing contemporaneously. Now that alone determines the position of another thing in time which is the cause of it or of its determinations. Consequently every substance (inasmuch as it can have succession predicated of it only in respect of its determinations) must contain the causality of certain determinations in another substance, and at the same time the effects of the causality of the other in itself. That is to say, substances must stand (mediately or immediately) in dynamical community with each other, if coexistence is to be cognized in any possible experience. But, in regard to objects of experience, that is absolutely necessary without which the experience of these objects would itself be impossible. Consequently it is absolutely necessary that all substances in the world of phenomena, in so far as they are coexistent, stand in a relation of complete community of reciprocal action to each other.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"50.40"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0041","text":"The word community has in our language[[33]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-33) two meanings, and contains the two notions conveyed in the Latin communio and commercium. We employ it in this place in the latter sense—that of a dynamical community, without which even the community of place (communio spatii) could not be empirically cognized. In our experiences it is easy to observe that it is only the continuous influences in all parts of space that can conduct our senses from one object to another; that the light which plays between our eyes and the heavenly bodies produces a mediating community between them and us, and thereby evidences their coexistence with us; that we cannot empirically change our position (perceive this change), unless the existence of matter throughout the whole of space rendered possible the perception of the positions we occupy; and that this perception can prove the contemporaneous existence of these places only through their reciprocal influence, and thereby also the coexistence of even the most remote objects—although in this case the proof is only mediate. Without community, every perception (of a phenomenon in space) is separated from every other and isolated, and the chain of empirical representations, that is, of experience, must, with the appearance of a new object, begin entirely de novo, without the least connection with preceding representations, and without standing towards these even in the relation of time. My intention here is by no means to combat the notion of empty space; for it may exist where our perceptions cannot exist, inasmuch as they cannot reach thereto, and where, therefore, no empirical perception of coexistence takes place. But in this case it is not an object of possible experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"50.41"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0042","text":"[[33]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-33) German\n\nThe following remarks may be useful in the way of explanation. In the mind, all phenomena, as contents of a possible experience, must exist in community (communio) of apperception or consciousness, and in so far as it is requisite that objects be represented as coexistent and connected, in so far must they reciprocally determine the position in time of each other and thereby constitute a whole. If this subjective community is to rest upon an objective basis, or to be applied to substances as phenomena, the perception of one substance must render possible the perception of another, and conversely. For otherwise succession, which is always found in perceptions as apprehensions, would be predicated of external objects, and their representation of their coexistence be thus impossible. But this is a reciprocal influence, that is to say, a real community (commercium) of substances, without which therefore the empirical relation of coexistence would be a notion beyond the reach of our minds. By virtue of this commercium, phenomena, in so far as they are apart from, and nevertheless in connection with each other, constitute a compositum reale. Such composita are possible in many different ways. The three dynamical relations then, from which all others spring, are those of inherence, consequence, and composition.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"50.42"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0043","text":"These, then, are the three analogies of experience. They are nothing more than principles of the determination of the existence of phenomena in time, according to the three modi of this determination; to wit, the relation to time itself as a quantity (the quantity of existence, that is, duration), the relation in time as a series or succession, finally, the relation in time as the complex of all existence (simultaneity). This unity of determination in regard to time is thoroughly dynamical; that is to say, time is not considered as that in which experience determines immediately to every existence its position; for this is impossible, inasmuch as absolute time is not an object of perception, by means of which phenomena can be connected with each other. On the contrary, the rule of the understanding, through which alone the existence of phenomena can receive synthetical unity as regards relations of time, determines for every phenomenon its position in time, and consequently à priori, and with validity for all and every time.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"50.43"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0044","text":"By nature, in the empirical sense of the word, we understand the totality of phenomena connected, in respect of their existence, according to necessary rules, that is, laws. There are therefore certain laws (which are moreover à priori) which make nature possible; and all empirical laws can exist only by means of experience, and by virtue of those primitive laws through which experience itself becomes possible. The purpose of the analogies is therefore to represent to us the unity of nature in the connection of all phenomena under certain exponents, the only business of which is to express the relation of time (in so far as it contains all existence in itself) to the unity of apperception, which can exist in synthesis only according to rules. The combined expression of all is this: “All phenomena exist in one nature, and must so exist, inasmuch as without this à priori unity, no unity of experience, and consequently no determination of objects in experience, is possible.”","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"50.44"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0045","text":"As regards the mode of proof which we have employed in treating of these transcendental laws of nature, and the peculiar character of it we must make one remark, which will at the same time be important as a guide in every other attempt to demonstrate the truth of intellectual and likewise synthetical propositions à priori. Had we endeavoured to prove these analogies dogmatically, that is, from conceptions; that is to say, had we employed this method in attempting to show that everything which exists, exists only in that which is permanent—that every thing or event presupposes the existence of something in a preceding state, upon which it follows in conformity with a rule—lastly, that in the manifold, which is coexistent, the states coexist in connection with each other according to a rule, all our labour would have been utterly in vain. For mere conceptions of things, analyse them as we may, cannot enable us to conclude from the existence of one object to the existence of another. What other course was left for us to pursue? This only, to demonstrate the possibility of experience as a cognition in which at last all objects must be capable of being presented to us, if the representation of them is to possess any objective reality. Now in this third, this mediating term, the essential form of which consists in the synthetical unity of the apperception of all phenomena, we found à priori conditions of the universal and necessary determination as to time of all existences in the world of phenomena, without which the empirical determination thereof as to time would itself be impossible, and we also discovered rules of synthetical unity à priori, by means of which we could anticipate experience. For want of this method, and from the fancy that it was possible to discover a dogmatical proof of the synthetical propositions which are requisite in the empirical employment of the understanding, has it happened that a proof of the principle of sufficient reason has been so often attempted, and always in vain. The other two analogies nobody has ever thought of, although they have always been silently employed by the mind,[[34]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-34) because the guiding thread furnished by the categories was wanting, the guide which alone can enable us to discover every hiatus, both in the system of conceptions and of principles.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"50.45"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0046","text":"[[34]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-34) The unity of the universe, in which all phenomena to be connected, is evidently a mere consequence of the admitted principle of the community of all substances which are coexistent. For were substances isolated, they could not as parts constitute a whole, and were their connection (reciprocal action of the manifold) not necessary from the very fact of coexistence, we could not conclude from the fact of the latter as a merely ideal relation to the former as a real one. We have, however, shown in its place that community is the proper ground of the possibility of an empirical cognition of coexistence, and that we may therefore properly reason from the latter to the former as its condition.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"50.46"}],"sha256":"ec84d34cf70878597afa4cfc1c35e858f50ba1b4d4466dd5bb2d988cb68d675d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI","title":"Empicisim","order":51,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_EMPI","text":"# Empicisim\n\n4. THE POSTULATES OF EMPIRICAL THOUGHT.\n\n5. That which agrees with the formal conditions (intuition and conception) of experience, is possible.\n\n6. That which coheres with the material conditions of experience (sensation), is real.\n\n7. That whose coherence with the real is determined according to universal conditions of experience is (exists) necessary.\n\nExplanation.\n\nThe categories of modality possess this peculiarity, that they do not in the least determine the object, or enlarge the conception to which they are annexed as predicates, but only express its relation to the faculty of cognition. Though my conception of a thing is in itself complete, I am still entitled to ask whether the object of it is merely possible, or whether it is also real, or, if the latter, whether it is also necessary. But hereby the object itself is not more definitely determined in thought, but the question is only in what relation it, including all its determinations, stands to the understanding and its employment in experience, to the empirical faculty of judgement, and to the reason of its application to experience.\n\nFor this very reason, too, the categories of modality are nothing more than explanations of the conceptions of possibility, reality, and necessity, as employed in experience, and at the same time, restrictions of all the categories to empirical use alone, not authorizing the transcendental employment of them. For if they are to have something more than a merely logical significance, and to be something more than a mere analytical expression of the form of thought, and to have a relation to things and their possibility, reality, or necessity, they must concern possible experience and its synthetical unity, in which alone objects of cognition can be given.\n\nThe postulate of the possibility of things requires also, that the conception of the things agree with the formal conditions of our experience in general. But this, that is to say, the objective form of experience, contains all the kinds of synthesis which are requisite for the cognition of objects. A conception which contains a synthesis must be regarded as empty and, without reference to an object, if its synthesis does not belong to experience—either as borrowed from it, and in this case it is called an empirical conception, or such as is the ground and à priori condition of experience (its form), and in this case it is a pure conception, a conception which nevertheless belongs to experience, inasmuch as its object can be found in this alone. For where shall we find the criterion or character of the possibility of an object which is cogitated by means of an à priori synthetical conception, if not in the synthesis which constitutes the form of empirical cognition of objects? That in such a conception no contradiction exists is indeed a necessary logical condition, but very far from being sufficient to establish the objective reality of the conception, that is, the possibility of such an object as is thought in the conception. Thus, in the conception of a figure which is contained within two straight lines, there is no contradiction, for the conceptions of two straight lines and of their junction contain no negation of a figure. The impossibility in such a case does not rest upon the conception in itself, but upon the construction of it in space, that is to say, upon the conditions of space and its determinations. But these have themselves objective reality, that is, they apply to possible things, because they contain à priori the form of experience in general.\n\nAnd now we shall proceed to point out the extensive utility and influence of this postulate of possibility. When I represent to myself a thing that is permanent, so that everything in it which changes belongs merely to its state or condition, from such a conception alone I never can cognize that such a thing is possible. Or, if I represent to myself something which is so constituted that, when it is posited, something else follows always and infallibly, my thought contains no self-contradiction; but whether such a property as causality is to be found in any possible thing, my thought alone affords no means of judging. Finally, I can represent to myself different things (substances) which are so constituted that the state or condition of one causes a change in the state of the other, and reciprocally; but whether such a relation is a property of things cannot be perceived from these conceptions, which contain a merely arbitrary synthesis. Only from the fact, therefore, that these conceptions express à priori the relations of perceptions in every experience, do we know that they possess objective reality, that is, transcendental truth; and that independent of experience, though not independent of all relation to form of an experience in general and its synthetical unity, in which alone objects can be empirically cognized.\n\nBut when we fashion to ourselves new conceptions of substances, forces, action, and reaction, from the material presented to us by perception, without following the example of experience in their connection, we create mere chimeras, of the possibility of which we cannot discover any criterion, because we have not taken experience for our instructress, though we have borrowed the conceptions from her. Such fictitious conceptions derive their character of possibility not, like the categories, à priori, as conceptions on which all experience depends, but only, à posteriori, as conceptions given by means of experience itself, and their possibility must either be cognized à posteriori and empirically, or it cannot be cognized at all. A substance which is permanently present in space, yet without filling it (like that tertium quid between matter and the thinking subject which some have tried to introduce into metaphysics), or a peculiar fundamental power of the mind of intuiting the future by anticipation (instead of merely inferring from past and present events), or, finally, a power of the mind to place itself in community of thought with other men, however distant they may be—these are conceptions the possibility of which has no ground to rest upon. For they are not based upon experience and its known laws; and, without experience, they are a merely arbitrary conjunction of thoughts, which, though containing no internal contradiction, has no claim to objective reality, neither, consequently, to the possibility of such an object as is thought in these conceptions. As far as concerns reality, it is self-evident that we cannot cogitate such a possibility in concreto without the aid of experience; because reality is concerned only with sensation, as the matter of experience, and not with the form of thought, with which we can no doubt indulge in shaping fancies.\n\nBut I pass by everything which derives its possibility from reality in experience, and I purpose treating here merely of the possibility of things by means of à priori conceptions. I maintain, then, that the possibility of things is not derived from such conceptions per se, but only when considered as formal and objective conditions of an experience in general.\n\nIt seems, indeed, as if the possibility of a triangle could be cognized from the conception of it alone (which is certainly independent of experience); for we can certainly give to the conception a corresponding object completely à priori, that is to say, we can construct it. But as a triangle is only the form of an object, it must remain a mere product of the imagination, and the possibility of the existence of an object corresponding to it must remain doubtful, unless we can discover some other ground, unless we know that the figure can be cogitated under the conditions upon which all objects of experience rest. Now, the facts that space is a formal condition à priori of external experience, that the formative synthesis, by which we construct a triangle in imagination, is the very same as that we employ in the apprehension of a phenomenon for the purpose of making an empirical conception of it, are what alone connect the notion of the possibility of such a thing, with the conception of it. In the same manner, the possibility of continuous quantities, indeed of quantities in general, for the conceptions of them are without exception synthetical, is never evident from the conceptions in themselves, but only when they are considered as the formal conditions of the determination of objects in experience. And where, indeed, should we look for objects to correspond to our conceptions, if not in experience, by which alone objects are presented to us? It is, however, true that without antecedent experience we can cognize and characterize the possibility of things, relatively to the formal conditions, under which something is determined in experience as an object, consequently, completely à priori. But still this is possible only in relation to experience and within its limits.\n\nThe postulate concerning the cognition of the reality of things requires perception, consequently conscious sensation, not indeed immediately, that is, of the object itself, whose existence is to be cognized, but still that the object have some connection with a real perception, in accordance with the analogies of experience, which exhibit all kinds of real connection in experience.\n\nFrom the mere conception of a thing it is impossible to conclude its existence. For, let the conception be ever so complete, and containing a statement of all the determinations of the thing, the existence of it has nothing to do with all this, but only with the question whether such a thing is given, so that the perception of it can in every case precede the conception. For the fact that the conception of it precedes the perception, merely indicates the possibility of its existence; it is perception which presents matter to the conception, that is the sole criterion of reality. Prior to the perception of the thing, however, and therefore comparatively à priori, we are able to cognize its existence, provided it stands in connection with some perceptions according to the principles of the empirical conjunction of these, that is, in conformity with the analogies of perception. For, in this case, the existence of the supposed thing is connected with our perception in a possible experience, and we are able, with the guidance of these analogies, to reason in the series of possible perceptions from a thing which we do really perceive to the thing we do not perceive. Thus, we cognize the existence of a magnetic matter penetrating all bodies from the perception of the attraction of the steel-filings by the magnet, although the constitution of our organs renders an immediate perception of this matter impossible for us. For, according to the laws of sensibility and the connected context of our perceptions, we should in an experience come also on an immediate empirical intuition of this matter, if our senses were more acute—but this obtuseness has no influence upon and cannot alter the form of possible experience in general. Our knowledge of the existence of things reaches as far as our perceptions, and what may be inferred from them according to empirical laws, extend. If we do not set out from experience, or do not proceed according to the laws of the empirical connection of phenomena, our pretensions to discover the existence of a thing which we do not immediately perceive are vain. Idealism, however, brings forward powerful objections to these rules for proving existence mediately. This is, therefore, the proper place for its refutation.\n\nREFUTATION OF IDEALISM.\n\nIdealism—I mean material idealism—is the theory which declares the existence of objects in space without us to be either () doubtful and indemonstrable, or (2) false and impossible. The first is the problematical idealism of Descartes, who admits the undoubted certainty of only one empirical assertion (assertio), to wit, “I am.” The second is the dogmatical idealism of Berkeley, who maintains that space, together with all the objects of which it is the inseparable condition, is a thing which is in itself impossible, and that consequently the objects in space are mere products of the imagination. The dogmatical theory of idealism is unavoidable, if we regard space as a property of things in themselves; for in that case it is, with all to which it serves as condition, a nonentity. But the foundation for this kind of idealism we have already destroyed in the transcendental æsthetic. Problematical idealism, which makes no such assertion, but only alleges our incapacity to prove the existence of anything besides ourselves by means of immediate experience, is a theory rational and evidencing a thorough and philosophical mode of thinking, for it observes the rule not to form a decisive judgement before sufficient proof be shown. The desired proof must therefore demonstrate that we have experience of external things, and not mere fancies. For this purpose, we must prove, that our internal and, to Descartes, indubitable experience is itself possible only under the previous assumption of external experience.\n\nTHEOREM.\n\nThe simple but empirically determined consciousness of my own existence proves the existence of external objects in space.\n\nPROOF\n\nI am conscious of my own existence as determined in time. All determination in regard to time presupposes the existence of something permanent in perception. But this permanent something cannot be something in me, for the very reason that my existence in time is itself determined by this permanent something. It follows that the perception of this permanent existence is possible only through a thing without me and not through the mere representation of a thing without me. Consequently, the determination of my existence in time is possible only through the existence of real things external to me. Now, consciousness in time is necessarily connected with the consciousness of the possibility of this determination in time. Hence it follows that consciousness in time is necessarily connected also with the existence of things without me, inasmuch as the existence of these things is the condition of determination in time. That is to say, the consciousness of my own existence is at the same time an immediate consciousness of the existence of other things without me.\n\nRemark I. The reader will observe, that in the foregoing proof the game which idealism plays is retorted upon itself, and with more justice. It assumed that the only immediate experience is internal and that from this we can only infer the existence of external things. But, as always happens, when we reason from given effects to determined causes, idealism has reasoned with too much haste and uncertainty, for it is quite possible that the cause of our representations may lie in ourselves, and that we ascribe it falsely to external things. But our proof shows that external experience is properly immediate,[[35]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-35) that only by virtue of it—not, indeed, the consciousness of our own existence, but certainly the determination of our existence in time, that is, internal experience—is possible. It is true, that the representation “I am,” which is the expression of the consciousness which can accompany all my thoughts, is that which immediately includes the existence of a subject. But in this representation we cannot find any knowledge of the subject, and therefore also no empirical knowledge, that is, experience. For experience contains, in addition to the thought of something existing, intuition, and in this case it must be internal intuition, that is, time, in relation to which the subject must be determined. But the existence of external things is absolutely requisite for this purpose, so that it follows that internal experience is itself possible only mediately and through external experience.\n\n[[35]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-35) The immediate consciousness of the existence of external things is, in the preceding theorem, not presupposed, but proved, by the possibility of this consciousness understood by us or not. The question as to the possibility of it would stand thus: “Have we an internal sense, but no external sense, and is our belief in external perception a mere delusion?” But it is evident that, in order merely to fancy to ourselves anything as external, that is, to present it to the sense in intuition we must already possess an external sense, and must thereby distinguish immediately the mere receptivity of an external intuition from the spontaneity which characterizes every act of imagination. For merely to imagine also an external sense, would annihilate the faculty of intuition itself which is to be determined by the imagination.\n\nRemark II. Now with this view all empirical use of our faculty of cognition in the determination of time is in perfect accordance. Its truth is supported by the fact that it is possible to perceive a determination of time only by means of a change in external relations (motion) to the permanent in space (for example, we become aware of the sun’s motion by observing the changes of his relation to the objects of this earth). But this is not all. We find that we possess nothing permanent that can correspond and be submitted to the conception of a substance as intuition, except matter. This idea of permanence is not itself derived from external experience, but is an à priori necessary condition of all determination of time, consequently also of the internal sense in reference to our own existence, and that through the existence of external things. In the representation “I,” the consciousness of myself is not an intuition, but a merely intellectual representation produced by the spontaneous activity of a thinking subject. It follows, that this “i” has not any predicate of intuition, which, in its character of permanence, could serve as correlate to the determination of time in the internal sense—in the same way as impenetrability is the correlate of matter as an empirical intuition.\n\nRemark III. From the fact that the existence of external things is a necessary condition of the possibility of a determined consciousness of ourselves, it does not follow that every intuitive representation of external things involves the existence of these things, for their representations may very well be the mere products of the imagination (in dreams as well as in madness); though, indeed, these are themselves created by the reproduction of previous external perceptions, which, as has been shown, are possible only through the reality of external objects. The sole aim of our remarks has, however, been to prove that internal experience in general is possible only through external experience in general. Whether this or that supposed experience be purely imaginary must be discovered from its particular determinations and by comparing these with the criteria of all real experience.\n\nFinally, as regards the third postulate, it applies to material necessity in existence, and not to merely formal and logical necessity in the connection of conceptions. Now as we cannot cognize completely à priori the existence of any object of sense, though we can do so comparatively à priori, that is, relatively to some other previously given existence—a cognition, however, which can only be of such an existence as must be contained in the complex of experience, of which the previously given perception is a part—the necessity of existence can never be cognized from conceptions, but always, on the contrary, from its connection with that which is an object of perception. But the only existence cognized, under the condition of other given phenomena, as necessary, is the existence of effects from given causes in conformity with the laws of causality. It is consequently not the necessity of the existence of things (as substances), but the necessity of the state of things that we cognize, and that not immediately, but by means of the existence of other states given in perception, according to empirical laws of causality. Hence it follows that the criterion of necessity is to be found only in the law of possible experience—that everything which happens is determined à priori in the phenomenon by its cause. Thus we cognize only the necessity of effects in nature, the causes of which are given us. Moreover, the criterion of necessity in existence possesses no application beyond the field of possible experience, and even in this it is not valid of the existence of things as substances, because these can never be considered as empirical effects, or as something that happens and has a beginning. Necessity, therefore, regards only the relations of phenomena according to the dynamical law of causality, and the possibility grounded thereon, of reasoning from some given existence (of a cause) à priori to another existence (of an effect). “Everything that happens is hypothetically necessary,” is a principle which subjects the changes that take place in the world to a law, that is, to a rule of necessary existence, without which nature herself could not possibly exist. Hence the proposition, “Nothing happens by blind chance (in mundo non datur casus),” is an à priori law of nature. The case is the same with the proposition, “Necessity in nature is not blind,” that is, it is conditioned, consequently intelligible necessity (non datur fatum). Both laws subject the play of change to “a nature of things (as phenomena),” or, which is the same thing, to the unity of the understanding, and through the understanding alone can changes belong to an experience, as the synthetical unity of phenomena. Both belong to the class of dynamical principles. The former is properly a consequence of the principle of causality—one of the analogies of experience. The latter belongs to the principles of modality, which to the determination of causality adds the conception of necessity, which is itself, however, subject to a rule of the understanding. The principle of continuity forbids any leap in the series of phenomena regarded as changes (in mundo non datur saltus); and likewise, in the complex of all empirical intuitions in space, any break or hiatus between two phenomena (non datur hiatus)—for we can so express the principle, that experience can admit nothing which proves the existence of a vacuum, or which even admits it as a part of an empirical synthesis. For, as regards a vacuum or void, which we may cogitate as out and beyond the field of possible experience (the world), such a question cannot come before the tribunal of mere understanding, which decides only upon questions that concern the employment of given phenomena for the construction of empirical cognition. It is rather a problem for ideal reason, which passes beyond the sphere of a possible experience and aims at forming a judgement of that which surrounds and circumscribes it, and the proper place for the consideration of it is the transcendental dialectic. These four propositions, “In mundo non datur hiatus, non datur saltus, non datur casus, non datur fatum,” as well as all principles of transcendental origin, we could very easily exhibit in their proper order, that is, in conformity with the order of the categories, and assign to each its proper place. But the already practised reader will do this for himself, or discover the clue to such an arrangement. But the combined result of all is simply this, to admit into the empirical synthesis nothing which might cause a break in or be foreign to the understanding and the continuous connection of all phenomena, that is, the unity of the conceptions of the understanding. For in the understanding alone is the unity of experience, in which all perceptions must have their assigned place, possible.\n\nWhether the field of possibility be greater than that of reality, and whether the field of the latter be itself greater than that of necessity, are interesting enough questions, and quite capable of synthetic solution, questions, however, which come under the jurisdiction of reason alone. For they are tantamount to asking whether all things as phenomena do without exception belong to the complex and connected whole of a single experience, of which every given perception is a part which therefore cannot be conjoined with any other phenomena—or, whether my perceptions can belong to more than one possible experience? The understanding gives to experience, according to the subjective and formal conditions, of sensibility as well as of apperception, the rules which alone make this experience possible. Other forms of intuition besides those of space and time, other forms of understanding besides the discursive forms of thought, or of cognition by means of conceptions, we can neither imagine nor make intelligible to ourselves; and even if we could, they would still not belong to experience, which is the only mode of cognition by which objects are presented to us. Whether other perceptions besides those which belong to the total of our possible experience, and consequently whether some other sphere of matter exists, the understanding has no power to decide, its proper occupation being with the synthesis of that which is given. Moreover, the poverty of the usual arguments which go to prove the existence of a vast sphere of possibility, of which all that is real (every object of experience) is but a small part, is very remarkable. “All real is possible”; from this follows naturally, according to the logical laws of conversion, the particular proposition: “Some possible is real.” Now this seems to be equivalent to: “Much is possible that is not real.” No doubt it does seem as if we ought to consider the sum of the possible to be greater than that of the real, from the fact that something must be added to the former to constitute the latter. But this notion of adding to the possible is absurd. For that which is not in the sum of the possible, and consequently requires to be added to it, is manifestly impossible. In addition to accordance with the formal conditions of experience, the understanding requires a connection with some perception; but that which is connected with this perception is real, even although it is not immediately perceived. But that another series of phenomena, in complete coherence with that which is given in perception, consequently more than one all-embracing experience is possible, is an inference which cannot be concluded from the data given us by experience, and still less without any data at all. That which is possible only under conditions which are themselves merely possible, is not possible in any respect. And yet we can find no more certain ground on which to base the discussion of the question whether the sphere of possibility is wider than that of experience.\n\nI have merely mentioned these questions, that in treating of the conception of the understanding, there might be no omission of anything that, in the common opinion, belongs to them. In reality, however, the notion of absolute possibility (possibility which is valid in every respect) is not a mere conception of the understanding, which can be employed empirically, but belongs to reason alone, which passes the bounds of all empirical use of the understanding. We have, therefore, contented ourselves with a merely critical remark, leaving the subject to be explained in the sequel.\n\nBefore concluding this fourth section, and at the same time the system of all principles of the pure understanding, it seems proper to mention the reasons which induced me to term the principles of modality postulates. This expression I do not here use in the sense which some more recent philosophers, contrary to its meaning with mathematicians, to whom the word properly belongs, attach to it—that of a proposition, namely, immediately certain, requiring neither deduction nor proof. For if, in the case of synthetical propositions, however evident they may be, we accord to them without deduction, and merely on the strength of their own pretensions, unqualified belief, all critique of the understanding is entirely lost; and, as there is no want of bold pretensions, which the common belief (though for the philosopher this is no credential) does not reject, the understanding lies exposed to every delusion and conceit, without the power of refusing its assent to those assertions, which, though illegitimate, demand acceptance as veritable axioms. When, therefore, to the conception of a thing an à priori determination is synthetically added, such a proposition must obtain, if not a proof, at least a deduction of the legitimacy of its assertion.\n\nThe principles of modality are, however, not objectively synthetical, for the predicates of possibility, reality, and necessity do not in the least augment the conception of that of which they are affirmed, inasmuch as they contribute nothing to the representation of the object. But as they are, nevertheless, always synthetical, they are so merely subjectively. That is to say, they have a reflective power, and apply to the conception of a thing, of which, in other respects, they affirm nothing, the faculty of cognition in which the conception originates and has its seat. So that if the conception merely agree with the formal conditions of experience, its object is called possible; if it is in connection with perception, and determined thereby, the object is real; if it is determined according to conceptions by means of the connection of perceptions, the object is called necessary. The principles of modality therefore predicate of a conception nothing more than the procedure of the faculty of cognition which generated it. Now a postulate in mathematics is a practical proposition which contains nothing but the synthesis by which we present an object to ourselves, and produce the conception of it, for example—“With a given line, to describe a circle upon a plane, from a given point”; and such a proposition does not admit of proof, because the procedure, which it requires, is exactly that by which alone it is possible to generate the conception of such a figure. With the same right, accordingly, can we postulate the principles of modality, because they do not augment[[36]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-36) the conception of a thing but merely indicate the manner in which it is connected with the faculty of cognition.\n\n[[36]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-36) When I think the reality of a thing, I do really think more than the possibility, but not in the thing; for that can never contain more in reality than was contained in its complete possibility. But while the notion of possibility is merely the notion of a position of thing in relation to the understanding (its empirical use), reality is the conjunction of the thing with perception.\n\nGENERAL REMARK ON THE SYSTEM OF PRINCIPLES.\n\nIt is very remarkable that we cannot perceive the possibility of a thing from the category alone, but must always have an intuition, by which to make evident the objective reality of the pure conception of the understanding. Take, for example, the categories of relation. How (1) a thing can exist only as a subject, and not as a mere determination of other things, that is, can be substance; or how (2), because something exists, some other thing must exist, consequently how a thing can be a cause; or how (3), when several things exist, from the fact that one of these things exists, some consequence to the others follows, and reciprocally, and in this way a community of substances can be possible—are questions whose solution cannot be obtained from mere conceptions. The very same is the case with the other categories; for example, how a thing can be of the same sort with many others, that is, can be a quantity, and so on. So long as we have not intuition we cannot know whether we do really think an object by the categories, and where an object can anywhere be found to cohere with them, and thus the truth is established, that the categories are not in themselves cognitions, but mere forms of thought for the construction of cognitions from given intuitions. For the same reason is it true that from categories alone no synthetical proposition can be made. For example: “In every existence there is substance,” that is, something that can exist only as a subject and not as mere predicate; or, “Everything is a quantity”—to construct propositions such as these, we require something to enable us to go out beyond the given conception and connect another with it. For the same reason the attempt to prove a synthetical proposition by means of mere conceptions, for example: “Everything that exists contingently has a cause,” has never succeeded. We could never get further than proving that, without this relation to conceptions, we could not conceive the existence of the contingent, that is, could not à priori through the understanding cognize the existence of such a thing; but it does not hence follow that this is also the condition of the possibility of the thing itself that is said to be contingent. If, accordingly; we look back to our proof of the principle of causality, we shall find that we were able to prove it as valid only of objects of possible experience, and, indeed, only as itself the principle of the possibility of experience, Consequently of the cognition of an object given in empirical intuition, and not from mere conceptions. That, however, the proposition: “Everything that is contingent must have a cause,” is evident to every one merely from conceptions, is not to be denied. But in this case the conception of the contingent is cogitated as involving not the category of modality (as that the non-existence of which can be conceived) but that of relation (as that which can exist only as the consequence of something else), and so it is really an identical proposition: “That which can exist only as a consequence, has a cause.” In fact, when we have to give examples of contingent existence, we always refer to changes, and not merely to the possibility of conceiving the opposite.[[37]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-37) But change is an event, which, as such, is possible only through a cause, and considered per se its non-existence is therefore possible, and we become cognizant of its contingency from the fact that it can exist only as the effect of a cause. Hence, if a thing is assumed to be contingent, it is an analytical proposition to say, it has a cause.\n\n[[37]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-37) We can easily conceive the non-existence of matter; but the ancients did not thence infer its contingency. But even the alternation of the existence and non-existence of a given state in a thing, in which all change consists, by no means proves the contingency of that state—the ground of proof being the reality of its opposite. For example, a body is in a state of rest after motion, but we cannot infer the contingency of the motion from the fact that the former is the opposite of the latter. For this opposite is merely a logical and not a real opposite to the other. If we wish to demonstrate the contingency of the motion, what we ought to prove is that, instead of the motion which took place in the preceding point of time, it was possible for the body to have been then in rest, not, that it is afterwards in rest; for in this case, both opposites are perfectly consistent with each other.\n\nBut it is still more remarkable that, to understand the possibility of things according to the categories and thus to demonstrate the objective reality of the latter, we require not merely intuitions, but external intuitions. If, for example, we take the pure conceptions of relation, we find that (1) for the purpose of presenting to the conception of substance something permanent in intuition corresponding thereto and thus of demonstrating the objective reality of this conception, we require an intuition (of matter) in space, because space alone is permanent and determines things as such, while time, and with it all that is in the internal sense, is in a state of continual flow; (2) in order to represent change as the intuition corresponding to the conception of causality, we require the representation of motion as change in space; in fact, it is through it alone that changes, the possibility of which no pure understanding can perceive, are capable of being intuited. Change is the connection of determinations contradictorily opposed to each other in the existence of one and the same thing. Now, how it is possible that out of a given state one quite opposite to it in the same thing should follow, reason without an example can not only not conceive, but cannot even make intelligible without intuition; and this intuition is the motion of a point in space; the existence of which in different spaces (as a consequence of opposite determinations) alone makes the intuition of change possible. For, in order to make even internal change cognitable, we require to represent time, as the form of the internal sense, figuratively by a line, and the internal change by the drawing of that line (motion), and consequently are obliged to employ external intuition to be able to represent the successive existence of ourselves in different states. The proper ground of this fact is that all change to be perceived as change presupposes something permanent in intuition, while in the internal sense no permanent intuition is to be found. Lastly, the objective possibility of the category of community cannot be conceived by mere reason, and consequently its objective reality cannot be demonstrated without an intuition, and that external in space. For how can we conceive the possibility of community, that is, when several substances exist, that some effect on the existence of the one follows from the existence of the other, and reciprocally, and therefore that, because something exists in the latter, something else must exist in the former, which could not be understood from its own existence alone? For this is the very essence of community—which is inconceivable as a property of things which are perfectly isolated. Hence, Leibnitz, in attributing to the substances of the world—as cogitated by the understanding alone—a community, required the mediating aid of a divinity; for, from their existence, such a property seemed to him with justice inconceivable. But we can very easily conceive the possibility of community (of substances as phenomena) if we represent them to ourselves as in space, consequently in external intuition. For external intuition contains in itself à priori formal external relations, as the conditions of the possibility of the real relations of action and reaction, and therefore of the possibility of community. With the same ease can it be demonstrated, that the possibility of things as quantities, and consequently the objective reality of the category of quantity, can be grounded only in external intuition, and that by its means alone is the notion of quantity appropriated by the internal sense. But I must avoid prolixity, and leave the task of illustrating this by examples to the reader’s own reflection.\n\nThe above remarks are of the greatest importance, not only for the confirmation of our previous confutation of idealism, but still more when the subject of self-cognition by mere internal consciousness and the determination of our own nature without the aid of external empirical intuitions is under discussion, for the indication of the grounds of the possibility of such a cognition.\n\nThe result of the whole of this part of the analytic of principles is, therefore: “All principles of the pure understanding are nothing more than à priori principles of the possibility of experience, and to experience alone do all à priori synthetical propositions apply and relate”; indeed, their possibility itself rests entirely on this relation.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","text":"# Empicisim\n\n4. THE POSTULATES OF EMPIRICAL THOUGHT.\n\n5. That which agrees with the formal conditions (intuition and conception) of experience, is possible.\n\n6. That which coheres with the material conditions of experience (sensation), is real.\n\n7. That whose coherence with the real is determined according to universal conditions of experience is (exists) necessary.\n\nExplanation.\n\nThe categories of modality possess this peculiarity, that they do not in the least determine the object, or enlarge the conception to which they are annexed as predicates, but only express its relation to the faculty of cognition. Though my conception of a thing is in itself complete, I am still entitled to ask whether the object of it is merely possible, or whether it is also real, or, if the latter, whether it is also necessary. But hereby the object itself is not more definitely determined in thought, but the question is only in what relation it, including all its determinations, stands to the understanding and its employment in experience, to the empirical faculty of judgement, and to the reason of its application to experience.\n\nFor this very reason, too, the categories of modality are nothing more than explanations of the conceptions of possibility, reality, and necessity, as employed in experience, and at the same time, restrictions of all the categories to empirical use alone, not authorizing the transcendental employment of them. For if they are to have something more than a merely logical significance, and to be something more than a mere analytical expression of the form of thought, and to have a relation to things and their possibility, reality, or necessity, they must concern possible experience and its synthetical unity, in which alone objects of cognition can be given.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"51.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0002","text":"The postulate of the possibility of things requires also, that the conception of the things agree with the formal conditions of our experience in general. But this, that is to say, the objective form of experience, contains all the kinds of synthesis which are requisite for the cognition of objects. A conception which contains a synthesis must be regarded as empty and, without reference to an object, if its synthesis does not belong to experience—either as borrowed from it, and in this case it is called an empirical conception, or such as is the ground and à priori condition of experience (its form), and in this case it is a pure conception, a conception which nevertheless belongs to experience, inasmuch as its object can be found in this alone. For where shall we find the criterion or character of the possibility of an object which is cogitated by means of an à priori synthetical conception, if not in the synthesis which constitutes the form of empirical cognition of objects? That in such a conception no contradiction exists is indeed a necessary logical condition, but very far from being sufficient to establish the objective reality of the conception, that is, the possibility of such an object as is thought in the conception. Thus, in the conception of a figure which is contained within two straight lines, there is no contradiction, for the conceptions of two straight lines and of their junction contain no negation of a figure. The impossibility in such a case does not rest upon the conception in itself, but upon the construction of it in space, that is to say, upon the conditions of space and its determinations. But these have themselves objective reality, that is, they apply to possible things, because they contain à priori the form of experience in general.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"51.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0003","text":"And now we shall proceed to point out the extensive utility and influence of this postulate of possibility. When I represent to myself a thing that is permanent, so that everything in it which changes belongs merely to its state or condition, from such a conception alone I never can cognize that such a thing is possible. Or, if I represent to myself something which is so constituted that, when it is posited, something else follows always and infallibly, my thought contains no self-contradiction; but whether such a property as causality is to be found in any possible thing, my thought alone affords no means of judging. Finally, I can represent to myself different things (substances) which are so constituted that the state or condition of one causes a change in the state of the other, and reciprocally; but whether such a relation is a property of things cannot be perceived from these conceptions, which contain a merely arbitrary synthesis. Only from the fact, therefore, that these conceptions express à priori the relations of perceptions in every experience, do we know that they possess objective reality, that is, transcendental truth; and that independent of experience, though not independent of all relation to form of an experience in general and its synthetical unity, in which alone objects can be empirically cognized.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"51.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0004","text":"But when we fashion to ourselves new conceptions of substances, forces, action, and reaction, from the material presented to us by perception, without following the example of experience in their connection, we create mere chimeras, of the possibility of which we cannot discover any criterion, because we have not taken experience for our instructress, though we have borrowed the conceptions from her. Such fictitious conceptions derive their character of possibility not, like the categories, à priori, as conceptions on which all experience depends, but only, à posteriori, as conceptions given by means of experience itself, and their possibility must either be cognized à posteriori and empirically, or it cannot be cognized at all. A substance which is permanently present in space, yet without filling it (like that tertium quid between matter and the thinking subject which some have tried to introduce into metaphysics), or a peculiar fundamental power of the mind of intuiting the future by anticipation (instead of merely inferring from past and present events), or, finally, a power of the mind to place itself in community of thought with other men, however distant they may be—these are conceptions the possibility of which has no ground to rest upon. For they are not based upon experience and its known laws; and, without experience, they are a merely arbitrary conjunction of thoughts, which, though containing no internal contradiction, has no claim to objective reality, neither, consequently, to the possibility of such an object as is thought in these conceptions. As far as concerns reality, it is self-evident that we cannot cogitate such a possibility in concreto without the aid of experience; because reality is concerned only with sensation, as the matter of experience, and not with the form of thought, with which we can no doubt indulge in shaping fancies.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"51.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0005","text":"But I pass by everything which derives its possibility from reality in experience, and I purpose treating here merely of the possibility of things by means of à priori conceptions. I maintain, then, that the possibility of things is not derived from such conceptions per se, but only when considered as formal and objective conditions of an experience in general.\n\nIt seems, indeed, as if the possibility of a triangle could be cognized from the conception of it alone (which is certainly independent of experience); for we can certainly give to the conception a corresponding object completely à priori, that is to say, we can construct it. But as a triangle is only the form of an object, it must remain a mere product of the imagination, and the possibility of the existence of an object corresponding to it must remain doubtful, unless we can discover some other ground, unless we know that the figure can be cogitated under the conditions upon which all objects of experience rest. Now, the facts that space is a formal condition à priori of external experience, that the formative synthesis, by which we construct a triangle in imagination, is the very same as that we employ in the apprehension of a phenomenon for the purpose of making an empirical conception of it, are what alone connect the notion of the possibility of such a thing, with the conception of it. In the same manner, the possibility of continuous quantities, indeed of quantities in general, for the conceptions of them are without exception synthetical, is never evident from the conceptions in themselves, but only when they are considered as the formal conditions of the determination of objects in experience. And where, indeed, should we look for objects to correspond to our conceptions, if not in experience, by which alone objects are presented to us? It is, however, true that without antecedent experience we can cognize and characterize the possibility of things, relatively to the formal conditions, under which something is determined in experience as an object, consequently, completely à priori. But still this is possible only in relation to experience and within its limits.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"51.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","text":"The postulate concerning the cognition of the reality of things requires perception, consequently conscious sensation, not indeed immediately, that is, of the object itself, whose existence is to be cognized, but still that the object have some connection with a real perception, in accordance with the analogies of experience, which exhibit all kinds of real connection in experience.\n\nFrom the mere conception of a thing it is impossible to conclude its existence. For, let the conception be ever so complete, and containing a statement of all the determinations of the thing, the existence of it has nothing to do with all this, but only with the question whether such a thing is given, so that the perception of it can in every case precede the conception. For the fact that the conception of it precedes the perception, merely indicates the possibility of its existence; it is perception which presents matter to the conception, that is the sole criterion of reality. Prior to the perception of the thing, however, and therefore comparatively à priori, we are able to cognize its existence, provided it stands in connection with some perceptions according to the principles of the empirical conjunction of these, that is, in conformity with the analogies of perception. For, in this case, the existence of the supposed thing is connected with our perception in a possible experience, and we are able, with the guidance of these analogies, to reason in the series of possible perceptions from a thing which we do really perceive to the thing we do not perceive. Thus, we cognize the existence of a magnetic matter penetrating all bodies from the perception of the attraction of the steel-filings by the magnet, although the constitution of our organs renders an immediate perception of this matter impossible for us. For, according to the laws of sensibility and the connected context of our perceptions, we should in an experience come also on an immediate empirical intuition of this matter, if our senses were more acute—but this obtuseness has no influence upon and cannot alter the form of possible experience in general. Our knowledge of the existence of things reaches as far as our perceptions, and what may be inferred from them according to empirical laws, extend. If we do not set out from experience, or do not proceed according to the laws of the empirical connection of phenomena, our pretensions to discover the existence of a thing which we do not immediately perceive are vain. Idealism, however, brings forward powerful objections to these rules for proving existence mediately. This is, therefore, the proper place for its refutation.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"51.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0007","text":"REFUTATION OF IDEALISM.\n\nIdealism—I mean material idealism—is the theory which declares the existence of objects in space without us to be either () doubtful and indemonstrable, or (2) false and impossible. The first is the problematical idealism of Descartes, who admits the undoubted certainty of only one empirical assertion (assertio), to wit, “I am.” The second is the dogmatical idealism of Berkeley, who maintains that space, together with all the objects of which it is the inseparable condition, is a thing which is in itself impossible, and that consequently the objects in space are mere products of the imagination. The dogmatical theory of idealism is unavoidable, if we regard space as a property of things in themselves; for in that case it is, with all to which it serves as condition, a nonentity. But the foundation for this kind of idealism we have already destroyed in the transcendental æsthetic. Problematical idealism, which makes no such assertion, but only alleges our incapacity to prove the existence of anything besides ourselves by means of immediate experience, is a theory rational and evidencing a thorough and philosophical mode of thinking, for it observes the rule not to form a decisive judgement before sufficient proof be shown. The desired proof must therefore demonstrate that we have experience of external things, and not mere fancies. For this purpose, we must prove, that our internal and, to Descartes, indubitable experience is itself possible only under the previous assumption of external experience.\n\nTHEOREM.\n\nThe simple but empirically determined consciousness of my own existence proves the existence of external objects in space.\n\nPROOF","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"51.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","text":"I am conscious of my own existence as determined in time. All determination in regard to time presupposes the existence of something permanent in perception. But this permanent something cannot be something in me, for the very reason that my existence in time is itself determined by this permanent something. It follows that the perception of this permanent existence is possible only through a thing without me and not through the mere representation of a thing without me. Consequently, the determination of my existence in time is possible only through the existence of real things external to me. Now, consciousness in time is necessarily connected with the consciousness of the possibility of this determination in time. Hence it follows that consciousness in time is necessarily connected also with the existence of things without me, inasmuch as the existence of these things is the condition of determination in time. That is to say, the consciousness of my own existence is at the same time an immediate consciousness of the existence of other things without me.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"51.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0009","text":"Remark I. The reader will observe, that in the foregoing proof the game which idealism plays is retorted upon itself, and with more justice. It assumed that the only immediate experience is internal and that from this we can only infer the existence of external things. But, as always happens, when we reason from given effects to determined causes, idealism has reasoned with too much haste and uncertainty, for it is quite possible that the cause of our representations may lie in ourselves, and that we ascribe it falsely to external things. But our proof shows that external experience is properly immediate,[[35]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-35) that only by virtue of it—not, indeed, the consciousness of our own existence, but certainly the determination of our existence in time, that is, internal experience—is possible. It is true, that the representation “I am,” which is the expression of the consciousness which can accompany all my thoughts, is that which immediately includes the existence of a subject. But in this representation we cannot find any knowledge of the subject, and therefore also no empirical knowledge, that is, experience. For experience contains, in addition to the thought of something existing, intuition, and in this case it must be internal intuition, that is, time, in relation to which the subject must be determined. But the existence of external things is absolutely requisite for this purpose, so that it follows that internal experience is itself possible only mediately and through external experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"51.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0010","text":"[[35]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-35) The immediate consciousness of the existence of external things is, in the preceding theorem, not presupposed, but proved, by the possibility of this consciousness understood by us or not. The question as to the possibility of it would stand thus: “Have we an internal sense, but no external sense, and is our belief in external perception a mere delusion?” But it is evident that, in order merely to fancy to ourselves anything as external, that is, to present it to the sense in intuition we must already possess an external sense, and must thereby distinguish immediately the mere receptivity of an external intuition from the spontaneity which characterizes every act of imagination. For merely to imagine also an external sense, would annihilate the faculty of intuition itself which is to be determined by the imagination.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"51.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0011","text":"Remark II. Now with this view all empirical use of our faculty of cognition in the determination of time is in perfect accordance. Its truth is supported by the fact that it is possible to perceive a determination of time only by means of a change in external relations (motion) to the permanent in space (for example, we become aware of the sun’s motion by observing the changes of his relation to the objects of this earth). But this is not all. We find that we possess nothing permanent that can correspond and be submitted to the conception of a substance as intuition, except matter. This idea of permanence is not itself derived from external experience, but is an à priori necessary condition of all determination of time, consequently also of the internal sense in reference to our own existence, and that through the existence of external things. In the representation “I,” the consciousness of myself is not an intuition, but a merely intellectual representation produced by the spontaneous activity of a thinking subject. It follows, that this “i” has not any predicate of intuition, which, in its character of permanence, could serve as correlate to the determination of time in the internal sense—in the same way as impenetrability is the correlate of matter as an empirical intuition.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"51.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0012","text":"Remark III. From the fact that the existence of external things is a necessary condition of the possibility of a determined consciousness of ourselves, it does not follow that every intuitive representation of external things involves the existence of these things, for their representations may very well be the mere products of the imagination (in dreams as well as in madness); though, indeed, these are themselves created by the reproduction of previous external perceptions, which, as has been shown, are possible only through the reality of external objects. The sole aim of our remarks has, however, been to prove that internal experience in general is possible only through external experience in general. Whether this or that supposed experience be purely imaginary must be discovered from its particular determinations and by comparing these with the criteria of all real experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"51.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","text":"Finally, as regards the third postulate, it applies to material necessity in existence, and not to merely formal and logical necessity in the connection of conceptions. Now as we cannot cognize completely à priori the existence of any object of sense, though we can do so comparatively à priori, that is, relatively to some other previously given existence—a cognition, however, which can only be of such an existence as must be contained in the complex of experience, of which the previously given perception is a part—the necessity of existence can never be cognized from conceptions, but always, on the contrary, from its connection with that which is an object of perception. But the only existence cognized, under the condition of other given phenomena, as necessary, is the existence of effects from given causes in conformity with the laws of causality. It is consequently not the necessity of the existence of things (as substances), but the necessity of the state of things that we cognize, and that not immediately, but by means of the existence of other states given in perception, according to empirical laws of causality. Hence it follows that the criterion of necessity is to be found only in the law of possible experience—that everything which happens is determined à priori in the phenomenon by its cause. Thus we cognize only the necessity of effects in nature, the causes of which are given us. Moreover, the criterion of necessity in existence possesses no application beyond the field of possible experience, and even in this it is not valid of the existence of things as substances, because these can never be considered as empirical effects, or as something that happens and has a beginning. Necessity, therefore, regards only the relations of phenomena according to the dynamical law of causality, and the possibility grounded thereon, of reasoning from some given existence (of a cause) à priori to another existence (of an effect). “Everything that happens is hypothetically necessary,” is a principle which subjects the changes that take place in the world to a law, that is, to a rule of necessary existence, without which nature herself could not possibly exist. Hence the proposition, “Nothing happens by blind chance (in mundo non datur casus),” is an à priori law of nature. The case is the same with the proposition, “Necessity in nature is not blind,” that is, it is conditioned, consequently intelligible necessity (non datur fatum). Both laws subject the play of change to “a nature of things (as phenomena),” or, which is the same thing, to the unity of the understanding, and through the understanding alone can changes belong to an experience, as the synthetical unity of phenomena. Both belong to the class of dynamical principles. The former is properly a consequence of the principle of causality—one of the analogies of experience. The latter belongs to the principles of modality, which to the determination of causality adds the conception of necessity, which is itself, however, subject to a rule of the understanding. The principle of continuity forbids any leap in the series of phenomena regarded as changes (in mundo non datur saltus); and likewise, in the complex of all empirical intuitions in space, any break or hiatus between two phenomena (non datur hiatus)—for we can so express the principle, that experience can admit nothing which proves the existence of a vacuum, or which even admits it as a part of an empirical synthesis. For, as regards a vacuum or void, which we may cogitate as out and beyond the field of possible experience (the world), such a question cannot come before the tribunal of mere understanding, which decides only upon questions that concern the employment of given phenomena for the construction of empirical cognition. It is rather a problem for ideal reason, which passes beyond the sphere of a possible experience and aims at forming a judgement of that which surrounds and circumscribes it, and the proper place for the consideration of it is the transcendental dialectic. These four propositions, “In mundo non datur hiatus, non datur saltus, non datur casus, non datur fatum,” as well as all principles of transcendental origin, we could very easily exhibit in their proper order, that is, in conformity with the order of the categories, and assign to each its proper place. But the already practised reader will do this for himself, or discover the clue to such an arrangement. But the combined result of all is simply this, to admit into the empirical synthesis nothing which might cause a break in or be foreign to the understanding and the continuous connection of all phenomena, that is, the unity of the conceptions of the understanding. For in the understanding alone is the unity of experience, in which all perceptions must have their assigned place, possible.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"51.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0014","text":"Whether the field of possibility be greater than that of reality, and whether the field of the latter be itself greater than that of necessity, are interesting enough questions, and quite capable of synthetic solution, questions, however, which come under the jurisdiction of reason alone. For they are tantamount to asking whether all things as phenomena do without exception belong to the complex and connected whole of a single experience, of which every given perception is a part which therefore cannot be conjoined with any other phenomena—or, whether my perceptions can belong to more than one possible experience? The understanding gives to experience, according to the subjective and formal conditions, of sensibility as well as of apperception, the rules which alone make this experience possible. Other forms of intuition besides those of space and time, other forms of understanding besides the discursive forms of thought, or of cognition by means of conceptions, we can neither imagine nor make intelligible to ourselves; and even if we could, they would still not belong to experience, which is the only mode of cognition by which objects are presented to us. Whether other perceptions besides those which belong to the total of our possible experience, and consequently whether some other sphere of matter exists, the understanding has no power to decide, its proper occupation being with the synthesis of that which is given. Moreover, the poverty of the usual arguments which go to prove the existence of a vast sphere of possibility, of which all that is real (every object of experience) is but a small part, is very remarkable. “All real is possible”; from this follows naturally, according to the logical laws of conversion, the particular proposition: “Some possible is real.” Now this seems to be equivalent to: “Much is possible that is not real.” No doubt it does seem as if we ought to consider the sum of the possible to be greater than that of the real, from the fact that something must be added to the former to constitute the latter. But this notion of adding to the possible is absurd. For that which is not in the sum of the possible, and consequently requires to be added to it, is manifestly impossible. In addition to accordance with the formal conditions of experience, the understanding requires a connection with some perception; but that which is connected with this perception is real, even although it is not immediately perceived. But that another series of phenomena, in complete coherence with that which is given in perception, consequently more than one all-embracing experience is possible, is an inference which cannot be concluded from the data given us by experience, and still less without any data at all. That which is possible only under conditions which are themselves merely possible, is not possible in any respect. And yet we can find no more certain ground on which to base the discussion of the question whether the sphere of possibility is wider than that of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"51.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0015","text":"I have merely mentioned these questions, that in treating of the conception of the understanding, there might be no omission of anything that, in the common opinion, belongs to them. In reality, however, the notion of absolute possibility (possibility which is valid in every respect) is not a mere conception of the understanding, which can be employed empirically, but belongs to reason alone, which passes the bounds of all empirical use of the understanding. We have, therefore, contented ourselves with a merely critical remark, leaving the subject to be explained in the sequel.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"51.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0016","text":"Before concluding this fourth section, and at the same time the system of all principles of the pure understanding, it seems proper to mention the reasons which induced me to term the principles of modality postulates. This expression I do not here use in the sense which some more recent philosophers, contrary to its meaning with mathematicians, to whom the word properly belongs, attach to it—that of a proposition, namely, immediately certain, requiring neither deduction nor proof. For if, in the case of synthetical propositions, however evident they may be, we accord to them without deduction, and merely on the strength of their own pretensions, unqualified belief, all critique of the understanding is entirely lost; and, as there is no want of bold pretensions, which the common belief (though for the philosopher this is no credential) does not reject, the understanding lies exposed to every delusion and conceit, without the power of refusing its assent to those assertions, which, though illegitimate, demand acceptance as veritable axioms. When, therefore, to the conception of a thing an à priori determination is synthetically added, such a proposition must obtain, if not a proof, at least a deduction of the legitimacy of its assertion.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"51.16"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0017","text":"The principles of modality are, however, not objectively synthetical, for the predicates of possibility, reality, and necessity do not in the least augment the conception of that of which they are affirmed, inasmuch as they contribute nothing to the representation of the object. But as they are, nevertheless, always synthetical, they are so merely subjectively. That is to say, they have a reflective power, and apply to the conception of a thing, of which, in other respects, they affirm nothing, the faculty of cognition in which the conception originates and has its seat. So that if the conception merely agree with the formal conditions of experience, its object is called possible; if it is in connection with perception, and determined thereby, the object is real; if it is determined according to conceptions by means of the connection of perceptions, the object is called necessary. The principles of modality therefore predicate of a conception nothing more than the procedure of the faculty of cognition which generated it. Now a postulate in mathematics is a practical proposition which contains nothing but the synthesis by which we present an object to ourselves, and produce the conception of it, for example—“With a given line, to describe a circle upon a plane, from a given point”; and such a proposition does not admit of proof, because the procedure, which it requires, is exactly that by which alone it is possible to generate the conception of such a figure. With the same right, accordingly, can we postulate the principles of modality, because they do not augment[[36]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-36) the conception of a thing but merely indicate the manner in which it is connected with the faculty of cognition.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"51.17"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0018","text":"[[36]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-36) When I think the reality of a thing, I do really think more than the possibility, but not in the thing; for that can never contain more in reality than was contained in its complete possibility. But while the notion of possibility is merely the notion of a position of thing in relation to the understanding (its empirical use), reality is the conjunction of the thing with perception.\n\nGENERAL REMARK ON THE SYSTEM OF PRINCIPLES.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"51.18"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","text":"It is very remarkable that we cannot perceive the possibility of a thing from the category alone, but must always have an intuition, by which to make evident the objective reality of the pure conception of the understanding. Take, for example, the categories of relation. How (1) a thing can exist only as a subject, and not as a mere determination of other things, that is, can be substance; or how (2), because something exists, some other thing must exist, consequently how a thing can be a cause; or how (3), when several things exist, from the fact that one of these things exists, some consequence to the others follows, and reciprocally, and in this way a community of substances can be possible—are questions whose solution cannot be obtained from mere conceptions. The very same is the case with the other categories; for example, how a thing can be of the same sort with many others, that is, can be a quantity, and so on. So long as we have not intuition we cannot know whether we do really think an object by the categories, and where an object can anywhere be found to cohere with them, and thus the truth is established, that the categories are not in themselves cognitions, but mere forms of thought for the construction of cognitions from given intuitions. For the same reason is it true that from categories alone no synthetical proposition can be made. For example: “In every existence there is substance,” that is, something that can exist only as a subject and not as mere predicate; or, “Everything is a quantity”—to construct propositions such as these, we require something to enable us to go out beyond the given conception and connect another with it. For the same reason the attempt to prove a synthetical proposition by means of mere conceptions, for example: “Everything that exists contingently has a cause,” has never succeeded. We could never get further than proving that, without this relation to conceptions, we could not conceive the existence of the contingent, that is, could not à priori through the understanding cognize the existence of such a thing; but it does not hence follow that this is also the condition of the possibility of the thing itself that is said to be contingent. If, accordingly; we look back to our proof of the principle of causality, we shall find that we were able to prove it as valid only of objects of possible experience, and, indeed, only as itself the principle of the possibility of experience, Consequently of the cognition of an object given in empirical intuition, and not from mere conceptions. That, however, the proposition: “Everything that is contingent must have a cause,” is evident to every one merely from conceptions, is not to be denied. But in this case the conception of the contingent is cogitated as involving not the category of modality (as that the non-existence of which can be conceived) but that of relation (as that which can exist only as the consequence of something else), and so it is really an identical proposition: “That which can exist only as a consequence, has a cause.” In fact, when we have to give examples of contingent existence, we always refer to changes, and not merely to the possibility of conceiving the opposite.[[37]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-37) But change is an event, which, as such, is possible only through a cause, and considered per se its non-existence is therefore possible, and we become cognizant of its contingency from the fact that it can exist only as the effect of a cause. Hence, if a thing is assumed to be contingent, it is an analytical proposition to say, it has a cause.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"51.19"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0020","text":"[[37]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-37) We can easily conceive the non-existence of matter; but the ancients did not thence infer its contingency. But even the alternation of the existence and non-existence of a given state in a thing, in which all change consists, by no means proves the contingency of that state—the ground of proof being the reality of its opposite. For example, a body is in a state of rest after motion, but we cannot infer the contingency of the motion from the fact that the former is the opposite of the latter. For this opposite is merely a logical and not a real opposite to the other. If we wish to demonstrate the contingency of the motion, what we ought to prove is that, instead of the motion which took place in the preceding point of time, it was possible for the body to have been then in rest, not, that it is afterwards in rest; for in this case, both opposites are perfectly consistent with each other.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"51.20"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0021","text":"But it is still more remarkable that, to understand the possibility of things according to the categories and thus to demonstrate the objective reality of the latter, we require not merely intuitions, but external intuitions. If, for example, we take the pure conceptions of relation, we find that (1) for the purpose of presenting to the conception of substance something permanent in intuition corresponding thereto and thus of demonstrating the objective reality of this conception, we require an intuition (of matter) in space, because space alone is permanent and determines things as such, while time, and with it all that is in the internal sense, is in a state of continual flow; (2) in order to represent change as the intuition corresponding to the conception of causality, we require the representation of motion as change in space; in fact, it is through it alone that changes, the possibility of which no pure understanding can perceive, are capable of being intuited. Change is the connection of determinations contradictorily opposed to each other in the existence of one and the same thing. Now, how it is possible that out of a given state one quite opposite to it in the same thing should follow, reason without an example can not only not conceive, but cannot even make intelligible without intuition; and this intuition is the motion of a point in space; the existence of which in different spaces (as a consequence of opposite determinations) alone makes the intuition of change possible. For, in order to make even internal change cognitable, we require to represent time, as the form of the internal sense, figuratively by a line, and the internal change by the drawing of that line (motion), and consequently are obliged to employ external intuition to be able to represent the successive existence of ourselves in different states. The proper ground of this fact is that all change to be perceived as change presupposes something permanent in intuition, while in the internal sense no permanent intuition is to be found. Lastly, the objective possibility of the category of community cannot be conceived by mere reason, and consequently its objective reality cannot be demonstrated without an intuition, and that external in space. For how can we conceive the possibility of community, that is, when several substances exist, that some effect on the existence of the one follows from the existence of the other, and reciprocally, and therefore that, because something exists in the latter, something else must exist in the former, which could not be understood from its own existence alone? For this is the very essence of community—which is inconceivable as a property of things which are perfectly isolated. Hence, Leibnitz, in attributing to the substances of the world—as cogitated by the understanding alone—a community, required the mediating aid of a divinity; for, from their existence, such a property seemed to him with justice inconceivable. But we can very easily conceive the possibility of community (of substances as phenomena) if we represent them to ourselves as in space, consequently in external intuition. For external intuition contains in itself à priori formal external relations, as the conditions of the possibility of the real relations of action and reaction, and therefore of the possibility of community. With the same ease can it be demonstrated, that the possibility of things as quantities, and consequently the objective reality of the category of quantity, can be grounded only in external intuition, and that by its means alone is the notion of quantity appropriated by the internal sense. But I must avoid prolixity, and leave the task of illustrating this by examples to the reader’s own reflection.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"51.21"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0022","text":"The above remarks are of the greatest importance, not only for the confirmation of our previous confutation of idealism, but still more when the subject of self-cognition by mere internal consciousness and the determination of our own nature without the aid of external empirical intuitions is under discussion, for the indication of the grounds of the possibility of such a cognition.\n\nThe result of the whole of this part of the analytic of principles is, therefore: “All principles of the pure understanding are nothing more than à priori principles of the possibility of experience, and to experience alone do all à priori synthetical propositions apply and relate”; indeed, their possibility itself rests entirely on this relation.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"51.22"}],"sha256":"148d178b7b7b8519aa16263b4eca42fb32c81eef73770c547f4c71dbafb8e12e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA","title":"I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","order":52,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_ITIA","text":"# Second Division\n\n### SECOND DIVISION—TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC\n\n### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC.  \nINTRODUCTION.\n\n### I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance\n\nWe termed dialectic in general a logic of appearance. This does not signify a doctrine of probability; for probability is truth, only cognized upon insufficient grounds, and though the information it gives us is imperfect, it is not therefore deceitful. Hence it must not be separated from the analytical part of logic. Still less must phenomenon and appearance be held to be identical. For truth or illusory appearance does not reside in the object, in so far as it is intuited, but in the judgement upon the object, in so far as it is thought. It is, therefore, quite correct to say that the senses do not err, not because they always judge correctly, but because they do not judge at all. Hence truth and error, consequently also, illusory appearance as the cause of error, are only to be found in a judgement, that is, in the relation of an object to our understanding. In a cognition which completely harmonizes with the laws of the understanding, no error can exist. In a representation of the senses—as not containing any judgement—there is also no error. But no power of nature can of itself deviate from its own laws. Hence neither the understanding per se (without the influence of another cause), nor the senses per se, would fall into error; the former could not, because, if it acts only according to its own laws, the effect (the judgement) must necessarily accord with these laws. But in accordance with the laws of the understanding consists the formal element in all truth. In the senses there is no judgement—neither a true nor a false one. But, as we have no source of cognition besides these two, it follows that error is caused solely by the unobserved influence of the sensibility upon the understanding. And thus it happens that the subjective grounds of a judgement blend and are confounded with the objective, and cause them to deviate from their proper determination,[[40]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-40) just as a body in motion would always of itself proceed in a straight line, but if another impetus gives to it a different direction, it will then start off into a curvilinear line of motion. To distinguish the peculiar action of the understanding from the power which mingles with it, it is necessary to consider an erroneous judgement as the diagonal between two forces, that determine the judgement in two different directions, which, as it were, form an angle, and to resolve this composite operation into the simple ones of the understanding and the sensibility. In pure à priori judgements this must be done by means of transcendental reflection, whereby, as has been already shown, each representation has its place appointed in the corresponding faculty of cognition, and consequently the influence of the one faculty upon the other is made apparent.\n\n[[40]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-40) Sensibility, subjected to the understanding, as the object upon which the understanding employs its functions, is the source of real cognitions. But, in so far as it exercises an influence upon the action of the understanding and determines it to judgement, sensibility is itself the cause of error.\n\nIt is not at present our business to treat of empirical illusory appearance (for example, optical illusion), which occurs in the empirical application of otherwise correct rules of the understanding, and in which the judgement is misled by the influence of imagination. Our purpose is to speak of transcendental illusory appearance, which influences principles—that are not even applied to experience, for in this case we should possess a sure test of their correctness—but which leads us, in disregard of all the warnings of criticism, completely beyond the empirical employment of the categories and deludes us with the chimera of an extension of the sphere of the pure understanding. We shall term those principles the application of which is confined entirely within the limits of possible experience, immanent; those, on the other hand, which transgress these limits, we shall call transcendent principles. But by these latter I do not understand principles of the transcendental use or misuse of the categories, which is in reality a mere fault of the judgement when not under due restraint from criticism, and therefore not paying sufficient attention to the limits of the sphere in which the pure understanding is allowed to exercise its functions; but real principles which exhort us to break down all those barriers, and to lay claim to a perfectly new field of cognition, which recognizes no line of demarcation. Thus transcendental and transcendent are not identical terms. The principles of the pure understanding, which we have already propounded, ought to be of empirical and not of transcendental use, that is, they are not applicable to any object beyond the sphere of experience. A principle which removes these limits, nay, which authorizes us to overstep them, is called transcendent. If our criticism can succeed in exposing the illusion in these pretended principles, those which are limited in their employment to the sphere of experience may be called, in opposition to the others, immanent principles of the pure understanding.\n\nLogical illusion, which consists merely in the imitation of the form of reason (the illusion in sophistical syllogisms), arises entirely from a want of due attention to logical rules. So soon as the attention is awakened to the case before us, this illusion totally disappears. Transcendental illusion, on the contrary, does not cease to exist, even after it has been exposed, and its nothingness clearly perceived by means of transcendental criticism. Take, for example, the illusion in the proposition: “The world must have a beginning in time.” The cause of this is as follows. In our reason, subjectively considered as a faculty of human cognition, there exist fundamental rules and maxims of its exercise, which have completely the appearance of objective principles. Now from this cause it happens that the subjective necessity of a certain connection of our conceptions, is regarded as an objective necessity of the determination of things in themselves. This illusion it is impossible to avoid, just as we cannot avoid perceiving that the sea appears to be higher at a distance than it is near the shore, because we see the former by means of higher rays than the latter, or, which is a still stronger case, as even the astronomer cannot prevent himself from seeing the moon larger at its rising than some time afterwards, although he is not deceived by this illusion.\n\nTranscendental dialectic will therefore content itself with exposing the illusory appearance in transcendental judgements, and guarding us against it; but to make it, as in the case of logical illusion, entirely disappear and cease to be illusion is utterly beyond its power. For we have here to do with a natural and unavoidable illusion, which rests upon subjective principles and imposes these upon us as objective, while logical dialectic, in the detection of sophisms, has to do merely with an error in the logical consequence of the propositions, or with an artificially constructed illusion, in imitation of the natural error. There is, therefore, a natural and unavoidable dialectic of pure reason—not that in which the bungler, from want of the requisite knowledge, involves himself, nor that which the sophist devises for the purpose of misleading, but that which is an inseparable adjunct of human reason, and which, even after its illusions have been exposed, does not cease to deceive, and continually to lead reason into momentary errors, which it becomes necessary continually to remove.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","text":"# Second Division\n\n### SECOND DIVISION—TRANSCENDENTAL LOGIC\n\n### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC.  \nINTRODUCTION.\n\n### I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance\n\nWe termed dialectic in general a logic of appearance. This does not signify a doctrine of probability; for probability is truth, only cognized upon insufficient grounds, and though the information it gives us is imperfect, it is not therefore deceitful. Hence it must not be separated from the analytical part of logic. Still less must phenomenon and appearance be held to be identical. For truth or illusory appearance does not reside in the object, in so far as it is intuited, but in the judgement upon the object, in so far as it is thought. It is, therefore, quite correct to say that the senses do not err, not because they always judge correctly, but because they do not judge at all. Hence truth and error, consequently also, illusory appearance as the cause of error, are only to be found in a judgement, that is, in the relation of an object to our understanding. In a cognition which completely harmonizes with the laws of the understanding, no error can exist. In a representation of the senses—as not containing any judgement—there is also no error. But no power of nature can of itself deviate from its own laws. Hence neither the understanding per se (without the influence of another cause), nor the senses per se, would fall into error; the former could not, because, if it acts only according to its own laws, the effect (the judgement) must necessarily accord with these laws. But in accordance with the laws of the understanding consists the formal element in all truth. In the senses there is no judgement—neither a true nor a false one.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"52.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","text":"But, as we have no source of cognition besides these two, it follows that error is caused solely by the unobserved influence of the sensibility upon the understanding. And thus it happens that the subjective grounds of a judgement blend and are confounded with the objective, and cause them to deviate from their proper determination,[[40]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-40) just as a body in motion would always of itself proceed in a straight line, but if another impetus gives to it a different direction, it will then start off into a curvilinear line of motion. To distinguish the peculiar action of the understanding from the power which mingles with it, it is necessary to consider an erroneous judgement as the diagonal between two forces, that determine the judgement in two different directions, which, as it were, form an angle, and to resolve this composite operation into the simple ones of the understanding and the sensibility. In pure à priori judgements this must be done by means of transcendental reflection, whereby, as has been already shown, each representation has its place appointed in the corresponding faculty of cognition, and consequently the influence of the one faculty upon the other is made apparent.\n\n[[40]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-40) Sensibility, subjected to the understanding, as the object upon which the understanding employs its functions, is the source of real cognitions. But, in so far as it exercises an influence upon the action of the understanding and determines it to judgement, sensibility is itself the cause of error.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"52.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","text":"It is not at present our business to treat of empirical illusory appearance (for example, optical illusion), which occurs in the empirical application of otherwise correct rules of the understanding, and in which the judgement is misled by the influence of imagination. Our purpose is to speak of transcendental illusory appearance, which influences principles—that are not even applied to experience, for in this case we should possess a sure test of their correctness—but which leads us, in disregard of all the warnings of criticism, completely beyond the empirical employment of the categories and deludes us with the chimera of an extension of the sphere of the pure understanding. We shall term those principles the application of which is confined entirely within the limits of possible experience, immanent; those, on the other hand, which transgress these limits, we shall call transcendent principles. But by these latter I do not understand principles of the transcendental use or misuse of the categories, which is in reality a mere fault of the judgement when not under due restraint from criticism, and therefore not paying sufficient attention to the limits of the sphere in which the pure understanding is allowed to exercise its functions; but real principles which exhort us to break down all those barriers, and to lay claim to a perfectly new field of cognition, which recognizes no line of demarcation. Thus transcendental and transcendent are not identical terms. The principles of the pure understanding, which we have already propounded, ought to be of empirical and not of transcendental use, that is, they are not applicable to any object beyond the sphere of experience. A principle which removes these limits, nay, which authorizes us to overstep them, is called transcendent. If our criticism can succeed in exposing the illusion in these pretended principles, those which are limited in their employment to the sphere of experience may be called, in opposition to the others, immanent principles of the pure understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"52.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","text":"Logical illusion, which consists merely in the imitation of the form of reason (the illusion in sophistical syllogisms), arises entirely from a want of due attention to logical rules. So soon as the attention is awakened to the case before us, this illusion totally disappears. Transcendental illusion, on the contrary, does not cease to exist, even after it has been exposed, and its nothingness clearly perceived by means of transcendental criticism. Take, for example, the illusion in the proposition: “The world must have a beginning in time.” The cause of this is as follows. In our reason, subjectively considered as a faculty of human cognition, there exist fundamental rules and maxims of its exercise, which have completely the appearance of objective principles. Now from this cause it happens that the subjective necessity of a certain connection of our conceptions, is regarded as an objective necessity of the determination of things in themselves. This illusion it is impossible to avoid, just as we cannot avoid perceiving that the sea appears to be higher at a distance than it is near the shore, because we see the former by means of higher rays than the latter, or, which is a still stronger case, as even the astronomer cannot prevent himself from seeing the moon larger at its rising than some time afterwards, although he is not deceived by this illusion.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"52.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","text":"Transcendental dialectic will therefore content itself with exposing the illusory appearance in transcendental judgements, and guarding us against it; but to make it, as in the case of logical illusion, entirely disappear and cease to be illusion is utterly beyond its power. For we have here to do with a natural and unavoidable illusion, which rests upon subjective principles and imposes these upon us as objective, while logical dialectic, in the detection of sophisms, has to do merely with an error in the logical consequence of the propositions, or with an artificially constructed illusion, in imitation of the natural error. There is, therefore, a natural and unavoidable dialectic of pure reason—not that in which the bungler, from want of the requisite knowledge, involves himself, nor that which the sophist devises for the purpose of misleading, but that which is an inseparable adjunct of human reason, and which, even after its illusions have been exposed, does not cease to deceive, and continually to lead reason into momentary errors, which it becomes necessary continually to remove.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"52.5"}],"sha256":"03d5c7d54f0591eed4dc19e8cc98137902c3180c0ab82e51c8195a01abe43e8f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS","title":"II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","order":53,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_IPRS","text":"### II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance\n\nA. OF REASON IN GENERAL.\n\nAll our knowledge begins with sense, proceeds thence to understanding, and ends with reason, beyond which nothing higher can be discovered in the human mind for elaborating the matter of intuition and subjecting it to the highest unity of thought. At this stage of our inquiry it is my duty to give an explanation of this, the highest faculty of cognition, and I confess I find myself here in some difficulty. Of reason, as of the understanding, there is a merely formal, that is, logical use, in which it makes abstraction of all content of cognition; but there is also a real use, inasmuch as it contains in itself the source of certain conceptions and principles, which it does not borrow either from the senses or the understanding. The former faculty has been long defined by logicians as the faculty of mediate conclusion in contradistinction to immediate conclusions (consequentiae immediatae); but the nature of the latter, which itself generates conceptions, is not to be understood from this definition. Now as a division of reason into a logical and a transcendental faculty presents itself here, it becomes necessary to seek for a higher conception of this source of cognition which shall comprehend both conceptions. In this we may expect, according to the analogy of the conceptions of the understanding, that the logical conception will give us the key to the transcendental, and that the table of the functions of the former will present us with the clue to the conceptions of reason.\n\nIn the former part of our transcendental logic, we defined the understanding to be the faculty of rules; reason may be distinguished from understanding as the faculty of principles.\n\nThe term principle is ambiguous, and commonly signifies merely a cognition that may be employed as a principle, although it is not in itself, and as regards its proper origin, entitled to the distinction. Every general proposition, even if derived from experience by the process of induction, may serve as the major in a syllogism; but it is not for that reason a principle. Mathematical axioms (for example, there can be only one straight line between two points) are general à priori cognitions, and are therefore rightly denominated principles, relatively to the cases which can be subsumed under them. But I cannot for this reason say that I cognize this property of a straight line from principles—I cognize it only in pure intuition.\n\nCognition from principles, then, is that cognition in which I cognize the particular in the general by means of conceptions. Thus every syllogism is a form of the deduction of a cognition from a principle. For the major always gives a conception, through which everything that is subsumed under the condition thereof is cognized according to a principle. Now as every general cognition may serve as the major in a syllogism, and the understanding presents us with such general à priori propositions, they may be termed principles, in respect of their possible use.\n\nBut if we consider these principles of the pure understanding in relation to their origin, we shall find them to be anything rather than cognitions from conceptions. For they would not even be possible à priori, if we could not rely on the assistance of pure intuition (in mathematics), or on that of the conditions of a possible experience. That everything that happens has a cause, cannot be concluded from the general conception of that which happens; on the contrary the principle of causality instructs us as to the mode of obtaining from that which happens a determinate empirical conception.\n\nSynthetical cognitions from conceptions the understanding cannot supply, and they alone are entitled to be called principles. At the same time, all general propositions may be termed comparative principles.\n\nIt has been a long-cherished wish—that (who knows how late), may one day, be happily accomplished—that the principles of the endless variety of civil laws should be investigated and exposed; for in this way alone can we find the secret of simplifying legislation. But in this case, laws are nothing more than limitations of our freedom upon conditions under which it subsists in perfect harmony with itself; they consequently have for their object that which is completely our own work, and of which we ourselves may be the cause by means of these conceptions. But how objects as things in themselves—how the nature of things is subordinated to principles and is to be determined, according to conceptions, is a question which it seems well nigh impossible to answer. Be this, however, as it may—for on this point our investigation is yet to be made—it is at least manifest from what we have said that cognition from principles is something very different from cognition by means of the understanding, which may indeed precede other cognitions in the form of a principle, but in itself—in so far as it is synthetical—is neither based upon mere thought, nor contains a general proposition drawn from conceptions alone.\n\nThe understanding may be a faculty for the production of unity of phenomena by virtue of rules; the reason is a faculty for the production of unity of rules (of the understanding) under principles. Reason, therefore, never applies directly to experience, or to any sensuous object; its object is, on the contrary, the understanding, to the manifold cognition of which it gives a unity à priori by means of conceptions—a unity which may be called rational unity, and which is of a nature very different from that of the unity produced by the understanding.\n\nThe above is the general conception of the faculty of reason, in so far as it has been possible to make it comprehensible in the absence of examples. These will be given in the sequel.\n\nB. OF THE LOGICAL USE OF REASON.\n\nA distinction is commonly made between that which is immediately cognized and that which is inferred or concluded. That in a figure which is bounded by three straight lines there are three angles, is an immediate cognition; but that these angles are together equal to two right angles, is an inference or conclusion. Now, as we are constantly employing this mode of thought and have thus become quite accustomed to it, we no longer remark the above distinction, and, as in the case of the so-called deceptions of sense, consider as immediately perceived, what has really been inferred. In every reasoning or syllogism, there is a fundamental proposition, afterwards a second drawn from it, and finally the conclusion, which connects the truth in the first with the truth in the second—and that infallibly. If the judgement concluded is so contained in the first proposition that it can be deduced from it without the meditation of a third notion, the conclusion is called immediate (consequentia immediata); I prefer the term conclusion of the understanding. But if, in addition to the fundamental cognition, a second judgement is necessary for the production of the conclusion, it is called a conclusion of the reason. In the proposition: All men are mortal, are contained the propositions: Some men are mortal, Nothing that is not mortal is a man, and these are therefore immediate conclusions from the first. On the other hand, the proposition: all the learned are mortal, is not contained in the main proposition (for the conception of a learned man does not occur in it), and it can be deduced from the main proposition only by means of a mediating judgement.\n\nIn every syllogism I first cogitate a rule (the major) by means of the understanding. In the next place I subsume a cognition under the condition of the rule (and this is the minor) by means of the judgement. And finally I determine my cognition by means of the predicate of the rule (this is the conclusio), consequently, I determine it à priori by means of the reason. The relations, therefore, which the major proposition, as the rule, represents between a cognition and its condition, constitute the different kinds of syllogisms. These are just threefold—analogously with all judgements, in so far as they differ in the mode of expressing the relation of a cognition in the understanding—namely, categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive.\n\nWhen as often happens, the conclusion is a judgement which may follow from other given judgements, through which a perfectly different object is cogitated, I endeavour to discover in the understanding whether the assertion in this conclusion does not stand under certain conditions according to a general rule. If I find such a condition, and if the object mentioned in the conclusion can be subsumed under the given condition, then this conclusion follows from a rule which is also valid for other objects of cognition. From this we see that reason endeavours to subject the great variety of the cognitions of the understanding to the smallest possible number of principles (general conditions), and thus to produce in it the highest unity.\n\nC. OF THE PURE USE OF REASON.\n\nCan we isolate reason, and, if so, is it in this case a peculiar source of conceptions and judgements which spring from it alone, and through which it can be applied to objects; or is it merely a subordinate faculty, whose duty it is to give a certain form to given cognitions—a form which is called logical, and through which the cognitions of the understanding are subordinated to each other, and lower rules to higher (those, to wit, whose condition comprises in its sphere the condition of the others), in so far as this can be done by comparison? This is the question which we have at present to answer. Manifold variety of rules and unity of principles is a requirement of reason, for the purpose of bringing the understanding into complete accordance with itself, just as understanding subjects the manifold content of intuition to conceptions, and thereby introduces connection into it. But this principle prescribes no law to objects, and does not contain any ground of the possibility of cognizing or of determining them as such, but is merely a subjective law for the proper arrangement of the content of the understanding. The purpose of this law is, by a comparison of the conceptions of the understanding, to reduce them to the smallest possible number, although, at the same time, it does not justify us in demanding from objects themselves such a uniformity as might contribute to the convenience and the enlargement of the sphere of the understanding, or in expecting that it will itself thus receive from them objective validity. In one word, the question is: “does reason in itself, that is, does pure reason contain à priori synthetical principles and rules, and what are those principles?”\n\nThe formal and logical procedure of reason in syllogisms gives us sufficient information in regard to the ground on which the transcendental principle of reason in its pure synthetical cognition will rest.\n\n1. Reason, as observed in the syllogistic process, is not applicable to intuitions, for the purpose of subjecting them to rules—for this is the province of the understanding with its categories—but to conceptions and judgements. If pure reason does apply to objects and the intuition of them, it does so not immediately, but mediately—through the understanding and its judgements, which have a direct relation to the senses and their intuition, for the purpose of determining their objects. The unity of reason is therefore not the unity of a possible experience, but is essentially different from this unity, which is that of the understanding. That everything which happens has a cause, is not a principle cognized and prescribed by reason. This principle makes the unity of experience possible and borrows nothing from reason, which, without a reference to possible experience, could never have produced by means of mere conceptions any such synthetical unity.\n\n2. Reason, in its logical use, endeavours to discover the general condition of its judgement (the conclusion), and a syllogism is itself nothing but a judgement by means of the subsumption of its condition under a general rule (the major). Now as this rule may itself be subjected to the same process of reason, and thus the condition of the condition be sought (by means of a prosyllogism) as long as the process can be continued, it is very manifest that the peculiar principle of reason in its logical use is to find for the conditioned cognition of the understanding the unconditioned whereby the unity of the former is completed.\n\nBut this logical maxim cannot be a principle of pure reason, unless we admit that, if the conditioned is given, the whole series of conditions subordinated to one another—a series which is consequently itself unconditioned—is also given, that is, contained in the object and its connection.\n\nBut this principle of pure reason is evidently synthetical; for, analytically, the conditioned certainly relates to some condition, but not to the unconditioned. From this principle also there must originate different synthetical propositions, of which the pure understanding is perfectly ignorant, for it has to do only with objects of a possible experience, the cognition and synthesis of which is always conditioned. The unconditioned, if it does really exist, must be especially considered in regard to the determinations which distinguish it from whatever is conditioned, and will thus afford us material for many à priori synthetical propositions.\n\nThe principles resulting from this highest principle of pure reason will, however, be transcendent in relation to phenomena, that is to say, it will be impossible to make any adequate empirical use of this principle. It is therefore completely different from all principles of the understanding, the use made of which is entirely immanent, their object and purpose being merely the possibility of experience. Now our duty in the transcendental dialectic is as follows. To discover whether the principle that the series of conditions (in the synthesis of phenomena, or of thought in general) extends to the unconditioned is objectively true, or not; what consequences result therefrom affecting the empirical use of the understanding, or rather whether there exists any such objectively valid proposition of reason, and whether it is not, on the contrary, a merely logical precept which directs us to ascend perpetually to still higher conditions, to approach completeness in the series of them, and thus to introduce into our cognition the highest possible unity of reason. We must ascertain, I say, whether this requirement of reason has not been regarded, by a misunderstanding, as a transcendental principle of pure reason, which postulates a thorough completeness in the series of conditions in objects themselves. We must show, moreover, the misconceptions and illusions that intrude into syllogisms, the major proposition of which pure reason has supplied—a proposition which has perhaps more of the character of a petitio than of a postulatum—and that proceed from experience upwards to its conditions. The solution of these problems is our task in transcendental dialectic, which we are about to expose even at its source, that lies deep in human reason. We shall divide it into two parts, the first of which will treat of the transcendent conceptions of pure reason, the second of transcendent and dialectical syllogisms.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0001","text":"### II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance\n\nA. OF REASON IN GENERAL.\n\nAll our knowledge begins with sense, proceeds thence to understanding, and ends with reason, beyond which nothing higher can be discovered in the human mind for elaborating the matter of intuition and subjecting it to the highest unity of thought. At this stage of our inquiry it is my duty to give an explanation of this, the highest faculty of cognition, and I confess I find myself here in some difficulty. Of reason, as of the understanding, there is a merely formal, that is, logical use, in which it makes abstraction of all content of cognition; but there is also a real use, inasmuch as it contains in itself the source of certain conceptions and principles, which it does not borrow either from the senses or the understanding. The former faculty has been long defined by logicians as the faculty of mediate conclusion in contradistinction to immediate conclusions (consequentiae immediatae); but the nature of the latter, which itself generates conceptions, is not to be understood from this definition. Now as a division of reason into a logical and a transcendental faculty presents itself here, it becomes necessary to seek for a higher conception of this source of cognition which shall comprehend both conceptions. In this we may expect, according to the analogy of the conceptions of the understanding, that the logical conception will give us the key to the transcendental, and that the table of the functions of the former will present us with the clue to the conceptions of reason.\n\nIn the former part of our transcendental logic, we defined the understanding to be the faculty of rules; reason may be distinguished from understanding as the faculty of principles.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"53.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0002","text":"The term principle is ambiguous, and commonly signifies merely a cognition that may be employed as a principle, although it is not in itself, and as regards its proper origin, entitled to the distinction. Every general proposition, even if derived from experience by the process of induction, may serve as the major in a syllogism; but it is not for that reason a principle. Mathematical axioms (for example, there can be only one straight line between two points) are general à priori cognitions, and are therefore rightly denominated principles, relatively to the cases which can be subsumed under them. But I cannot for this reason say that I cognize this property of a straight line from principles—I cognize it only in pure intuition.\n\nCognition from principles, then, is that cognition in which I cognize the particular in the general by means of conceptions. Thus every syllogism is a form of the deduction of a cognition from a principle. For the major always gives a conception, through which everything that is subsumed under the condition thereof is cognized according to a principle. Now as every general cognition may serve as the major in a syllogism, and the understanding presents us with such general à priori propositions, they may be termed principles, in respect of their possible use.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"53.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0003","text":"But if we consider these principles of the pure understanding in relation to their origin, we shall find them to be anything rather than cognitions from conceptions. For they would not even be possible à priori, if we could not rely on the assistance of pure intuition (in mathematics), or on that of the conditions of a possible experience. That everything that happens has a cause, cannot be concluded from the general conception of that which happens; on the contrary the principle of causality instructs us as to the mode of obtaining from that which happens a determinate empirical conception.\n\nSynthetical cognitions from conceptions the understanding cannot supply, and they alone are entitled to be called principles. At the same time, all general propositions may be termed comparative principles.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"53.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0004","text":"It has been a long-cherished wish—that (who knows how late), may one day, be happily accomplished—that the principles of the endless variety of civil laws should be investigated and exposed; for in this way alone can we find the secret of simplifying legislation. But in this case, laws are nothing more than limitations of our freedom upon conditions under which it subsists in perfect harmony with itself; they consequently have for their object that which is completely our own work, and of which we ourselves may be the cause by means of these conceptions. But how objects as things in themselves—how the nature of things is subordinated to principles and is to be determined, according to conceptions, is a question which it seems well nigh impossible to answer. Be this, however, as it may—for on this point our investigation is yet to be made—it is at least manifest from what we have said that cognition from principles is something very different from cognition by means of the understanding, which may indeed precede other cognitions in the form of a principle, but in itself—in so far as it is synthetical—is neither based upon mere thought, nor contains a general proposition drawn from conceptions alone.\n\nThe understanding may be a faculty for the production of unity of phenomena by virtue of rules; the reason is a faculty for the production of unity of rules (of the understanding) under principles. Reason, therefore, never applies directly to experience, or to any sensuous object; its object is, on the contrary, the understanding, to the manifold cognition of which it gives a unity à priori by means of conceptions—a unity which may be called rational unity, and which is of a nature very different from that of the unity produced by the understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"53.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0005","text":"The above is the general conception of the faculty of reason, in so far as it has been possible to make it comprehensible in the absence of examples. These will be given in the sequel.\n\nB. OF THE LOGICAL USE OF REASON.\n\nA distinction is commonly made between that which is immediately cognized and that which is inferred or concluded. That in a figure which is bounded by three straight lines there are three angles, is an immediate cognition; but that these angles are together equal to two right angles, is an inference or conclusion. Now, as we are constantly employing this mode of thought and have thus become quite accustomed to it, we no longer remark the above distinction, and, as in the case of the so-called deceptions of sense, consider as immediately perceived, what has really been inferred. In every reasoning or syllogism, there is a fundamental proposition, afterwards a second drawn from it, and finally the conclusion, which connects the truth in the first with the truth in the second—and that infallibly. If the judgement concluded is so contained in the first proposition that it can be deduced from it without the meditation of a third notion, the conclusion is called immediate (consequentia immediata); I prefer the term conclusion of the understanding. But if, in addition to the fundamental cognition, a second judgement is necessary for the production of the conclusion, it is called a conclusion of the reason. In the proposition: All men are mortal, are contained the propositions: Some men are mortal, Nothing that is not mortal is a man, and these are therefore immediate conclusions from the first. On the other hand, the proposition: all the learned are mortal, is not contained in the main proposition (for the conception of a learned man does not occur in it), and it can be deduced from the main proposition only by means of a mediating judgement.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"53.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0006","text":"In every syllogism I first cogitate a rule (the major) by means of the understanding. In the next place I subsume a cognition under the condition of the rule (and this is the minor) by means of the judgement. And finally I determine my cognition by means of the predicate of the rule (this is the conclusio), consequently, I determine it à priori by means of the reason. The relations, therefore, which the major proposition, as the rule, represents between a cognition and its condition, constitute the different kinds of syllogisms. These are just threefold—analogously with all judgements, in so far as they differ in the mode of expressing the relation of a cognition in the understanding—namely, categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive.\n\nWhen as often happens, the conclusion is a judgement which may follow from other given judgements, through which a perfectly different object is cogitated, I endeavour to discover in the understanding whether the assertion in this conclusion does not stand under certain conditions according to a general rule. If I find such a condition, and if the object mentioned in the conclusion can be subsumed under the given condition, then this conclusion follows from a rule which is also valid for other objects of cognition. From this we see that reason endeavours to subject the great variety of the cognitions of the understanding to the smallest possible number of principles (general conditions), and thus to produce in it the highest unity.\n\nC. OF THE PURE USE OF REASON.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"53.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","text":"Can we isolate reason, and, if so, is it in this case a peculiar source of conceptions and judgements which spring from it alone, and through which it can be applied to objects; or is it merely a subordinate faculty, whose duty it is to give a certain form to given cognitions—a form which is called logical, and through which the cognitions of the understanding are subordinated to each other, and lower rules to higher (those, to wit, whose condition comprises in its sphere the condition of the others), in so far as this can be done by comparison? This is the question which we have at present to answer. Manifold variety of rules and unity of principles is a requirement of reason, for the purpose of bringing the understanding into complete accordance with itself, just as understanding subjects the manifold content of intuition to conceptions, and thereby introduces connection into it. But this principle prescribes no law to objects, and does not contain any ground of the possibility of cognizing or of determining them as such, but is merely a subjective law for the proper arrangement of the content of the understanding. The purpose of this law is, by a comparison of the conceptions of the understanding, to reduce them to the smallest possible number, although, at the same time, it does not justify us in demanding from objects themselves such a uniformity as might contribute to the convenience and the enlargement of the sphere of the understanding, or in expecting that it will itself thus receive from them objective validity. In one word, the question is: “does reason in itself, that is, does pure reason contain à priori synthetical principles and rules, and what are those principles?”","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"53.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0008","text":"The formal and logical procedure of reason in syllogisms gives us sufficient information in regard to the ground on which the transcendental principle of reason in its pure synthetical cognition will rest.\n\n1. Reason, as observed in the syllogistic process, is not applicable to intuitions, for the purpose of subjecting them to rules—for this is the province of the understanding with its categories—but to conceptions and judgements. If pure reason does apply to objects and the intuition of them, it does so not immediately, but mediately—through the understanding and its judgements, which have a direct relation to the senses and their intuition, for the purpose of determining their objects. The unity of reason is therefore not the unity of a possible experience, but is essentially different from this unity, which is that of the understanding. That everything which happens has a cause, is not a principle cognized and prescribed by reason. This principle makes the unity of experience possible and borrows nothing from reason, which, without a reference to possible experience, could never have produced by means of mere conceptions any such synthetical unity.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"53.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","text":"2. Reason, in its logical use, endeavours to discover the general condition of its judgement (the conclusion), and a syllogism is itself nothing but a judgement by means of the subsumption of its condition under a general rule (the major). Now as this rule may itself be subjected to the same process of reason, and thus the condition of the condition be sought (by means of a prosyllogism) as long as the process can be continued, it is very manifest that the peculiar principle of reason in its logical use is to find for the conditioned cognition of the understanding the unconditioned whereby the unity of the former is completed.\n\nBut this logical maxim cannot be a principle of pure reason, unless we admit that, if the conditioned is given, the whole series of conditions subordinated to one another—a series which is consequently itself unconditioned—is also given, that is, contained in the object and its connection.\n\nBut this principle of pure reason is evidently synthetical; for, analytically, the conditioned certainly relates to some condition, but not to the unconditioned. From this principle also there must originate different synthetical propositions, of which the pure understanding is perfectly ignorant, for it has to do only with objects of a possible experience, the cognition and synthesis of which is always conditioned. The unconditioned, if it does really exist, must be especially considered in regard to the determinations which distinguish it from whatever is conditioned, and will thus afford us material for many à priori synthetical propositions.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"53.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","text":"The principles resulting from this highest principle of pure reason will, however, be transcendent in relation to phenomena, that is to say, it will be impossible to make any adequate empirical use of this principle. It is therefore completely different from all principles of the understanding, the use made of which is entirely immanent, their object and purpose being merely the possibility of experience. Now our duty in the transcendental dialectic is as follows. To discover whether the principle that the series of conditions (in the synthesis of phenomena, or of thought in general) extends to the unconditioned is objectively true, or not; what consequences result therefrom affecting the empirical use of the understanding, or rather whether there exists any such objectively valid proposition of reason, and whether it is not, on the contrary, a merely logical precept which directs us to ascend perpetually to still higher conditions, to approach completeness in the series of them, and thus to introduce into our cognition the highest possible unity of reason. We must ascertain, I say, whether this requirement of reason has not been regarded, by a misunderstanding, as a transcendental principle of pure reason, which postulates a thorough completeness in the series of conditions in objects themselves. We must show, moreover, the misconceptions and illusions that intrude into syllogisms, the major proposition of which pure reason has supplied—a proposition which has perhaps more of the character of a petitio than of a postulatum—and that proceed from experience upwards to its conditions. The solution of these problems is our task in transcendental dialectic, which we are about to expose even at its source, that lies deep in human reason. We shall divide it into two parts, the first of which will treat of the transcendent conceptions of pure reason, the second of transcendent and dialectical syllogisms.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"53.10"}],"sha256":"00efac110ffbf57c5aac190b99bd9e7bd3063edc5ad5aadd71cf120e51342871","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI","title":"TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK I—OF THE CONCEPTIONS OF PURE REASON.","order":54,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_TDBI","text":"### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK I—OF THE CONCEPTIONS OF PURE REASON.\n\nThe conceptions of pure reason—we do not here speak of the possibility of them—are not obtained by reflection, but by inference or conclusion. The conceptions of understanding are also cogitated à priori antecedently to experience, and render it possible; but they contain nothing but the unity of reflection upon phenomena, in so far as these must necessarily belong to a possible empirical consciousness. Through them alone are cognition and the determination of an object possible. It is from them, accordingly, that we receive material for reasoning, and antecedently to them we possess no à priori conceptions of objects from which they might be deduced, On the other hand, the sole basis of their objective reality consists in the necessity imposed on them, as containing the intellectual form of all experience, of restricting their application and influence to the sphere of experience.\n\nBut the term, conception of reason, or rational conception, itself indicates that it does not confine itself within the limits of experience, because its object-matter is a cognition, of which every empirical cognition is but a part—nay, the whole of possible experience may be itself but a part of it—a cognition to which no actual experience ever fully attains, although it does always pertain to it. The aim of rational conceptions is the comprehension, as that of the conceptions of understanding is the understanding of perceptions. If they contain the unconditioned, they relate to that to which all experience is subordinate, but which is never itself an object of experience—that towards which reason tends in all its conclusions from experience, and by the standard of which it estimates the degree of their empirical use, but which is never itself an element in an empirical synthesis. If, notwithstanding, such conceptions possess objective validity, they may be called conceptus ratiocinati (conceptions legitimately concluded); in cases where they do not, they have been admitted on account of having the appearance of being correctly concluded, and may be called conceptus ratiocinantes (sophistical conceptions). But as this can only be sufficiently demonstrated in that part of our treatise which relates to the dialectical conclusions of reason, we shall omit any consideration of it in this place. As we called the pure conceptions of the understanding categories, we shall also distinguish those of pure reason by a new name and call them transcendental ideas. These terms, however, we must in the first place explain and justify.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","text":"### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK I—OF THE CONCEPTIONS OF PURE REASON.\n\nThe conceptions of pure reason—we do not here speak of the possibility of them—are not obtained by reflection, but by inference or conclusion. The conceptions of understanding are also cogitated à priori antecedently to experience, and render it possible; but they contain nothing but the unity of reflection upon phenomena, in so far as these must necessarily belong to a possible empirical consciousness. Through them alone are cognition and the determination of an object possible. It is from them, accordingly, that we receive material for reasoning, and antecedently to them we possess no à priori conceptions of objects from which they might be deduced, On the other hand, the sole basis of their objective reality consists in the necessity imposed on them, as containing the intellectual form of all experience, of restricting their application and influence to the sphere of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"54.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","text":"But the term, conception of reason, or rational conception, itself indicates that it does not confine itself within the limits of experience, because its object-matter is a cognition, of which every empirical cognition is but a part—nay, the whole of possible experience may be itself but a part of it—a cognition to which no actual experience ever fully attains, although it does always pertain to it. The aim of rational conceptions is the comprehension, as that of the conceptions of understanding is the understanding of perceptions. If they contain the unconditioned, they relate to that to which all experience is subordinate, but which is never itself an object of experience—that towards which reason tends in all its conclusions from experience, and by the standard of which it estimates the degree of their empirical use, but which is never itself an element in an empirical synthesis. If, notwithstanding, such conceptions possess objective validity, they may be called conceptus ratiocinati (conceptions legitimately concluded); in cases where they do not, they have been admitted on account of having the appearance of being correctly concluded, and may be called conceptus ratiocinantes (sophistical conceptions). But as this can only be sufficiently demonstrated in that part of our treatise which relates to the dialectical conclusions of reason, we shall omit any consideration of it in this place. As we called the pure conceptions of the understanding categories, we shall also distinguish those of pure reason by a new name and call them transcendental ideas. These terms, however, we must in the first place explain and justify.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"54.2"}],"sha256":"c9e7c636a3582d9d9d78e6293f73bcb2a9983f844f2b81e9394aff41afeebbb4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG","title":"Section I—Of Ideas in General","order":55,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIIG","text":"### Section I—Of Ideas in General\n\nDespite the great wealth of words which European languages possess, the thinker finds himself often at a loss for an expression exactly suited to his conception, for want of which he is unable to make himself intelligible either to others or to himself. To coin new words is a pretension to legislation in language which is seldom successful; and, before recourse is taken to so desperate an expedient, it is advisable to examine the dead and learned languages, with the hope and the probability that we may there meet with some adequate expression of the notion we have in our minds. In this case, even if the original meaning of the word has become somewhat uncertain, from carelessness or want of caution on the part of the authors of it, it is always better to adhere to and confirm its proper meaning—even although it may be doubtful whether it was formerly used in exactly this sense—than to make our labour vain by want of sufficient care to render ourselves intelligible.\n\nFor this reason, when it happens that there exists only a single word to express a certain conception, and this word, in its usual acceptation, is thoroughly adequate to the conception, the accurate distinction of which from related conceptions is of great importance, we ought not to employ the expression improvidently, or, for the sake of variety and elegance of style, use it as a synonym for other cognate words. It is our duty, on the contrary, carefully to preserve its peculiar signification, as otherwise it easily happens that when the attention of the reader is no longer particularly attracted to the expression, and it is lost amid the multitude of other words of very different import, the thought which it conveyed, and which it alone conveyed, is lost with it.\n\nPlato employed the expression idea in a way that plainly showed he meant by it something which is never derived from the senses, but which far transcends even the conceptions of the understanding (with which Aristotle occupied himself), inasmuch as in experience nothing perfectly corresponding to them could be found. Ideas are, according to him, archetypes of things themselves, and not merely keys to possible experiences, like the categories. In his view they flow from the highest reason, by which they have been imparted to human reason, which, however, exists no longer in its original state, but is obliged with great labour to recall by reminiscence—which is called philosophy—the old but now sadly obscured ideas. I will not here enter upon any literary investigation of the sense which this sublime philosopher attached to this expression. I shall content myself with remarking that it is nothing unusual, in common conversation as well as in written works, by comparing the thoughts which an author has delivered upon a subject, to understand him better than he understood himself inasmuch as he may not have sufficiently determined his conception, and thus have sometimes spoken, nay even thought, in opposition to his own opinions.\n\nPlato perceived very clearly that our faculty of cognition has the feeling of a much higher vocation than that of merely spelling out phenomena according to synthetical unity, for the purpose of being able to read them as experience, and that our reason naturally raises itself to cognitions far too elevated to admit of the possibility of an object given by experience corresponding to them—cognitions which are nevertheless real, and are not mere phantoms of the brain.\n\nThis philosopher found his ideas especially in all that is practical,[[41]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-41) that is, which rests upon freedom, which in its turn ranks under cognitions that are the peculiar product of reason. He who would derive from experience the conceptions of virtue, who would make (as many have really done) that, which at best can but serve as an imperfectly illustrative example, a model for or the formation of a perfectly adequate idea on the subject, would in fact transform virtue into a nonentity changeable according to time and circumstance and utterly incapable of being employed as a rule. On the contrary, every one is conscious that, when any one is held up to him as a model of virtue, he compares this so-called model with the true original which he possesses in his own mind and values him according to this standard. But this standard is the idea of virtue, in relation to which all possible objects of experience are indeed serviceable as examples—proofs of the practicability in a certain degree of that which the conception of virtue demands—but certainly not as archetypes. That the actions of man will never be in perfect accordance with all the requirements of the pure ideas of reason, does not prove the thought to be chimerical. For only through this idea are all judgements as to moral merit or demerit possible; it consequently lies at the foundation of every approach to moral perfection, however far removed from it the obstacles in human nature—indeterminable as to degree—may keep us.\n\n[[41]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-41) He certainly extended the application of his conception to speculative cognitions also, provided they were given pure and completely à priori, nay, even to mathematics, although this science cannot possess an object otherwhere than in Possible experience. I cannot follow him in this, and as little can I follow him in his mystical deduction of these ideas, or in his hypostatization of them; although, in truth, the elevated and exaggerated language which he employed in describing them is quite capable of an interpretation more subdued and more in accordance with fact and the nature of things.\n\nThe Platonic Republic has become proverbial as an example—and a striking one—of imaginary perfection, such as can exist only in the brain of the idle thinker; and Brucker ridicules the philosopher for maintaining that a prince can never govern well, unless he is participant in the ideas. But we should do better to follow up this thought and, where this admirable thinker leaves us without assistance, employ new efforts to place it in clearer light, rather than carelessly fling it aside as useless, under the very miserable and pernicious pretext of impracticability. A constitution of the greatest possible human freedom according to laws, by which the liberty of every individual can consist with the liberty of every other (not of the greatest possible happiness, for this follows necessarily from the former), is, to say the least, a necessary idea, which must be placed at the foundation not only of the first plan of the constitution of a state, but of all its laws. And, in this, it not necessary at the outset to take account of the obstacles which lie in our way—obstacles which perhaps do not necessarily arise from the character of human nature, but rather from the previous neglect of true ideas in legislation. For there is nothing more pernicious and more unworthy of a philosopher, than the vulgar appeal to a so-called adverse experience, which indeed would not have existed, if those institutions had been established at the proper time and in accordance with ideas; while, instead of this, conceptions, crude for the very reason that they have been drawn from experience, have marred and frustrated all our better views and intentions. The more legislation and government are in harmony with this idea, the more rare do punishments become and thus it is quite reasonable to maintain, as Plato did, that in a perfect state no punishments at all would be necessary. Now although a perfect state may never exist, the idea is not on that account the less just, which holds up this maximum as the archetype or standard of a constitution, in order to bring legislative government always nearer and nearer to the greatest possible perfection. For at what precise degree human nature must stop in its progress, and how wide must be the chasm which must necessarily exist between the idea and its realization, are problems which no one can or ought to determine—and for this reason, that it is the destination of freedom to overstep all assigned limits between itself and the idea.\n\nBut not only in that wherein human reason is a real causal agent and where ideas are operative causes (of actions and their objects), that is to say, in the region of ethics, but also in regard to nature herself, Plato saw clear proofs of an origin from ideas. A plant, and animal, the regular order of nature—probably also the disposition of the whole universe—give manifest evidence that they are possible only by means of and according to ideas; that, indeed, no one creature, under the individual conditions of its existence, perfectly harmonizes with the idea of the most perfect of its kind—just as little as man with the idea of humanity, which nevertheless he bears in his soul as the archetypal standard of his actions; that, notwithstanding, these ideas are in the highest sense individually, unchangeably, and completely determined, and are the original causes of things; and that the totality of connected objects in the universe is alone fully adequate to that idea. Setting aside the exaggerations of expression in the writings of this philosopher, the mental power exhibited in this ascent from the ectypal mode of regarding the physical world to the architectonic connection thereof according to ends, that is, ideas, is an effort which deserves imitation and claims respect. But as regards the principles of ethics, of legislation, and of religion, spheres in which ideas alone render experience possible, although they never attain to full expression therein, he has vindicated for himself a position of peculiar merit, which is not appreciated only because it is judged by the very empirical rules, the validity of which as principles is destroyed by ideas. For as regards nature, experience presents us with rules and is the source of truth, but in relation to ethical laws experience is the parent of illusion, and it is in the highest degree reprehensible to limit or to deduce the laws which dictate what I ought to do, from what is done.\n\nWe must, however, omit the consideration of these important subjects, the development of which is in reality the peculiar duty and dignity of philosophy, and confine ourselves for the present to the more humble but not less useful task of preparing a firm foundation for those majestic edifices of moral science. For this foundation has been hitherto insecure from the many subterranean passages which reason in its confident but vain search for treasures has made in all directions. Our present duty is to make ourselves perfectly acquainted with the transcendental use made of pure reason, its principles and ideas, that we may be able properly to determine and value its influence and real worth. But before bringing these introductory remarks to a close, I beg those who really have philosophy at heart—and their number is but small—if they shall find themselves convinced by the considerations following as well as by those above, to exert themselves to preserve to the expression idea its original signification, and to take care that it be not lost among those other expressions by which all sorts of representations are loosely designated—that the interests of science may not thereby suffer. We are in no want of words to denominate adequately every mode of representation, without the necessity of encroaching upon terms which are proper to others. The following is a graduated list of them. The genus is representation in general (representatio). Under it stands representation with consciousness (perceptio). A perception which relates solely to the subject as a modification of its state, is a sensation (sensatio), an objective perception is a cognition (cognitio). A cognition is either an intuition or a conception (intuitus vel conceptus). The former has an immediate relation to the object and is singular and individual; the latter has but a mediate relation, by means of a characteristic mark which may be common to several things. A conception is either empirical or pure. A pure conception, in so far as it has its origin in the understanding alone, and is not the conception of a pure sensuous image, is called notio. A conception formed from notions, which transcends the possibility of experience, is an idea, or a conception of reason. To one who has accustomed himself to these distinctions, it must be quite intolerable to hear the representation of the colour red called an idea. It ought not even to be called a notion or conception of understanding.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","text":"### Section I—Of Ideas in General\n\nDespite the great wealth of words which European languages possess, the thinker finds himself often at a loss for an expression exactly suited to his conception, for want of which he is unable to make himself intelligible either to others or to himself. To coin new words is a pretension to legislation in language which is seldom successful; and, before recourse is taken to so desperate an expedient, it is advisable to examine the dead and learned languages, with the hope and the probability that we may there meet with some adequate expression of the notion we have in our minds. In this case, even if the original meaning of the word has become somewhat uncertain, from carelessness or want of caution on the part of the authors of it, it is always better to adhere to and confirm its proper meaning—even although it may be doubtful whether it was formerly used in exactly this sense—than to make our labour vain by want of sufficient care to render ourselves intelligible.\n\nFor this reason, when it happens that there exists only a single word to express a certain conception, and this word, in its usual acceptation, is thoroughly adequate to the conception, the accurate distinction of which from related conceptions is of great importance, we ought not to employ the expression improvidently, or, for the sake of variety and elegance of style, use it as a synonym for other cognate words. It is our duty, on the contrary, carefully to preserve its peculiar signification, as otherwise it easily happens that when the attention of the reader is no longer particularly attracted to the expression, and it is lost amid the multitude of other words of very different import, the thought which it conveyed, and which it alone conveyed, is lost with it.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"55.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","text":"Plato employed the expression idea in a way that plainly showed he meant by it something which is never derived from the senses, but which far transcends even the conceptions of the understanding (with which Aristotle occupied himself), inasmuch as in experience nothing perfectly corresponding to them could be found. Ideas are, according to him, archetypes of things themselves, and not merely keys to possible experiences, like the categories. In his view they flow from the highest reason, by which they have been imparted to human reason, which, however, exists no longer in its original state, but is obliged with great labour to recall by reminiscence—which is called philosophy—the old but now sadly obscured ideas. I will not here enter upon any literary investigation of the sense which this sublime philosopher attached to this expression. I shall content myself with remarking that it is nothing unusual, in common conversation as well as in written works, by comparing the thoughts which an author has delivered upon a subject, to understand him better than he understood himself inasmuch as he may not have sufficiently determined his conception, and thus have sometimes spoken, nay even thought, in opposition to his own opinions.\n\nPlato perceived very clearly that our faculty of cognition has the feeling of a much higher vocation than that of merely spelling out phenomena according to synthetical unity, for the purpose of being able to read them as experience, and that our reason naturally raises itself to cognitions far too elevated to admit of the possibility of an object given by experience corresponding to them—cognitions which are nevertheless real, and are not mere phantoms of the brain.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"55.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","text":"This philosopher found his ideas especially in all that is practical,[[41]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-41) that is, which rests upon freedom, which in its turn ranks under cognitions that are the peculiar product of reason. He who would derive from experience the conceptions of virtue, who would make (as many have really done) that, which at best can but serve as an imperfectly illustrative example, a model for or the formation of a perfectly adequate idea on the subject, would in fact transform virtue into a nonentity changeable according to time and circumstance and utterly incapable of being employed as a rule. On the contrary, every one is conscious that, when any one is held up to him as a model of virtue, he compares this so-called model with the true original which he possesses in his own mind and values him according to this standard. But this standard is the idea of virtue, in relation to which all possible objects of experience are indeed serviceable as examples—proofs of the practicability in a certain degree of that which the conception of virtue demands—but certainly not as archetypes. That the actions of man will never be in perfect accordance with all the requirements of the pure ideas of reason, does not prove the thought to be chimerical. For only through this idea are all judgements as to moral merit or demerit possible; it consequently lies at the foundation of every approach to moral perfection, however far removed from it the obstacles in human nature—indeterminable as to degree—may keep us.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"55.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","text":"[[41]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-41) He certainly extended the application of his conception to speculative cognitions also, provided they were given pure and completely à priori, nay, even to mathematics, although this science cannot possess an object otherwhere than in Possible experience. I cannot follow him in this, and as little can I follow him in his mystical deduction of these ideas, or in his hypostatization of them; although, in truth, the elevated and exaggerated language which he employed in describing them is quite capable of an interpretation more subdued and more in accordance with fact and the nature of things.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"55.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","text":"The Platonic Republic has become proverbial as an example—and a striking one—of imaginary perfection, such as can exist only in the brain of the idle thinker; and Brucker ridicules the philosopher for maintaining that a prince can never govern well, unless he is participant in the ideas. But we should do better to follow up this thought and, where this admirable thinker leaves us without assistance, employ new efforts to place it in clearer light, rather than carelessly fling it aside as useless, under the very miserable and pernicious pretext of impracticability. A constitution of the greatest possible human freedom according to laws, by which the liberty of every individual can consist with the liberty of every other (not of the greatest possible happiness, for this follows necessarily from the former), is, to say the least, a necessary idea, which must be placed at the foundation not only of the first plan of the constitution of a state, but of all its laws. And, in this, it not necessary at the outset to take account of the obstacles which lie in our way—obstacles which perhaps do not necessarily arise from the character of human nature, but rather from the previous neglect of true ideas in legislation. For there is nothing more pernicious and more unworthy of a philosopher, than the vulgar appeal to a so-called adverse experience, which indeed would not have existed, if those institutions had been established at the proper time and in accordance with ideas; while, instead of this, conceptions, crude for the very reason that they have been drawn from experience, have marred and frustrated all our better views and intentions. The more legislation and government are in harmony with this idea, the more rare do punishments become and thus it is quite reasonable to maintain, as Plato did, that in a perfect state no punishments at all would be necessary. Now although a perfect state may never exist, the idea is not on that account the less just, which holds up this maximum as the archetype or standard of a constitution, in order to bring legislative government always nearer and nearer to the greatest possible perfection. For at what precise degree human nature must stop in its progress, and how wide must be the chasm which must necessarily exist between the idea and its realization, are problems which no one can or ought to determine—and for this reason, that it is the destination of freedom to overstep all assigned limits between itself and the idea.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"55.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","text":"But not only in that wherein human reason is a real causal agent and where ideas are operative causes (of actions and their objects), that is to say, in the region of ethics, but also in regard to nature herself, Plato saw clear proofs of an origin from ideas. A plant, and animal, the regular order of nature—probably also the disposition of the whole universe—give manifest evidence that they are possible only by means of and according to ideas; that, indeed, no one creature, under the individual conditions of its existence, perfectly harmonizes with the idea of the most perfect of its kind—just as little as man with the idea of humanity, which nevertheless he bears in his soul as the archetypal standard of his actions; that, notwithstanding, these ideas are in the highest sense individually, unchangeably, and completely determined, and are the original causes of things; and that the totality of connected objects in the universe is alone fully adequate to that idea. Setting aside the exaggerations of expression in the writings of this philosopher, the mental power exhibited in this ascent from the ectypal mode of regarding the physical world to the architectonic connection thereof according to ends, that is, ideas, is an effort which deserves imitation and claims respect. But as regards the principles of ethics, of legislation, and of religion, spheres in which ideas alone render experience possible, although they never attain to full expression therein, he has vindicated for himself a position of peculiar merit, which is not appreciated only because it is judged by the very empirical rules, the validity of which as principles is destroyed by ideas. For as regards nature, experience presents us with rules and is the source of truth, but in relation to ethical laws experience is the parent of illusion, and it is in the highest degree reprehensible to limit or to deduce the laws which dictate what I ought to do, from what is done.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"55.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","text":"We must, however, omit the consideration of these important subjects, the development of which is in reality the peculiar duty and dignity of philosophy, and confine ourselves for the present to the more humble but not less useful task of preparing a firm foundation for those majestic edifices of moral science. For this foundation has been hitherto insecure from the many subterranean passages which reason in its confident but vain search for treasures has made in all directions. Our present duty is to make ourselves perfectly acquainted with the transcendental use made of pure reason, its principles and ideas, that we may be able properly to determine and value its influence and real worth. But before bringing these introductory remarks to a close, I beg those who really have philosophy at heart—and their number is but small—if they shall find themselves convinced by the considerations following as well as by those above, to exert themselves to preserve to the expression idea its original signification, and to take care that it be not lost among those other expressions by which all sorts of representations are loosely designated—that the interests of science may not thereby suffer. We are in no want of words to denominate adequately every mode of representation, without the necessity of encroaching upon terms which are proper to others. The following is a graduated list of them. The genus is representation in general (representatio). Under it stands representation with consciousness (perceptio). A perception which relates solely to the subject as a modification of its state, is a sensation (sensatio), an objective perception is a cognition (cognitio). A cognition is either an intuition or a conception (intuitus vel conceptus). The former has an immediate relation to the object and is singular and individual; the latter has but a mediate relation, by means of a characteristic mark which may be common to several things. A conception is either empirical or pure. A pure conception, in so far as it has its origin in the understanding alone, and is not the conception of a pure sensuous image, is called notio. A conception formed from notions, which transcends the possibility of experience, is an idea, or a conception of reason. To one who has accustomed himself to these distinctions, it must be quite intolerable to hear the representation of the colour red called an idea. It ought not even to be called a notion or conception of understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"55.7"}],"sha256":"6e90b85d9d0755c3d71ae92dfa14ca643f3298bc5ac684ad02330287d06c76ae","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI","title":"Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas","order":56,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SITI","text":"### Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas\n\nTranscendental analytic showed us how the mere logical form of our cognition can contain the origin of pure conceptions à priori, conceptions which represent objects antecedently to all experience, or rather, indicate the synthetical unity which alone renders possible an empirical cognition of objects. The form of judgements—converted into a conception of the synthesis of intuitions—produced the categories which direct the employment of the understanding in experience. This consideration warrants us to expect that the form of syllogisms, when applied to synthetical unity of intuitions, following the rule of the categories, will contain the origin of particular à priori conceptions, which we may call pure conceptions of reason or transcendental ideas, and which will determine the use of the understanding in the totality of experience according to principles.\n\nThe function of reason in arguments consists in the universality of a cognition according to conceptions, and the syllogism itself is a judgement which is determined à priori in the whole extent of its condition. The proposition: “Caius is mortal,” is one which may be obtained from experience by the aid of the understanding alone; but my wish is to find a conception which contains the condition under which the predicate of this judgement is given—in this case, the conception of man—and after subsuming under this condition, taken in its whole extent (all men are mortal), I determine according to it the cognition of the object thought, and say: “Caius is mortal.”\n\nHence, in the conclusion of a syllogism we restrict a predicate to a certain object, after having thought it in the major in its whole extent under a certain condition. This complete quantity of the extent in relation to such a condition is called universality (universalitas). To this corresponds totality (universitas) of conditions in the synthesis of intuitions. The transcendental conception of reason is therefore nothing else than the conception of the totality of the conditions of a given conditioned. Now as the unconditioned alone renders possible totality of conditions, and, conversely, the totality of conditions is itself always unconditioned; a pure rational conception in general can be defined and explained by means of the conception of the unconditioned, in so far as it contains a basis for the synthesis of the conditioned.\n\nTo the number of modes of relation which the understanding cogitates by means of the categories, the number of pure rational conceptions will correspond. We must therefore seek for, first, an unconditioned of the categorical synthesis in a subject; secondly, of the hypothetical synthesis of the members of a series; thirdly, of the disjunctive synthesis of parts in a system.\n\nThere are exactly the same number of modes of syllogisms, each of which proceeds through prosyllogisms to the unconditioned—one to the subject which cannot be employed as predicate, another to the presupposition which supposes nothing higher than itself, and the third to an aggregate of the members of the complete division of a conception. Hence the pure rational conceptions of totality in the synthesis of conditions have a necessary foundation in the nature of human reason—at least as modes of elevating the unity of the understanding to the unconditioned. They may have no valid application, corresponding to their transcendental employment, in concreto, and be thus of no greater utility than to direct the understanding how, while extending them as widely as possible, to maintain its exercise and application in perfect consistence and harmony.\n\nBut, while speaking here of the totality of conditions and of the unconditioned as the common title of all conceptions of reason, we again light upon an expression which we find it impossible to dispense with, and which nevertheless, owing to the ambiguity attaching to it from long abuse, we cannot employ with safety. The word absolute is one of the few words which, in its original signification, was perfectly adequate to the conception it was intended to convey—a conception which no other word in the same language exactly suits, and the loss—or, which is the same thing, the incautious and loose employment—of which must be followed by the loss of the conception itself. And, as it is a conception which occupies much of the attention of reason, its loss would be greatly to the detriment of all transcendental philosophy. The word absolute is at present frequently used to denote that something can be predicated of a thing considered in itself and intrinsically. In this sense absolutely possible would signify that which is possible in itself (interne)—which is, in fact, the least that one can predicate of an object. On the other hand, it is sometimes employed to indicate that a thing is valid in all respects—for example, absolute sovereignty. Absolutely possible would in this sense signify that which is possible in all relations and in every respect; and this is the most that can be predicated of the possibility of a thing. Now these significations do in truth frequently coincide. Thus, for example, that which is intrinsically impossible, is also impossible in all relations, that is, absolutely impossible. But in most cases they differ from each other toto caelo, and I can by no means conclude that, because a thing is in itself possible, it is also possible in all relations, and therefore absolutely. Nay, more, I shall in the sequel show that absolute necessity does not by any means depend on internal necessity, and that, therefore, it must not be considered as synonymous with it. Of an opposite which is intrinsically impossible, we may affirm that it is in all respects impossible, and that, consequently, the thing itself, of which this is the opposite, is absolutely necessary; but I cannot reason conversely and say, the opposite of that which is absolutely necessary is intrinsically impossible, that is, that the absolute necessity of things is an internal necessity. For this internal necessity is in certain cases a mere empty word with which the least conception cannot be connected, while the conception of the necessity of a thing in all relations possesses very peculiar determinations. Now as the loss of a conception of great utility in speculative science cannot be a matter of indifference to the philosopher, I trust that the proper determination and careful preservation of the expression on which the conception depends will likewise be not indifferent to him.\n\nIn this enlarged signification, then, shall I employ the word absolute, in opposition to that which is valid only in some particular respect; for the latter is restricted by conditions, the former is valid without any restriction whatever.\n\nNow the transcendental conception of reason has for its object nothing else than absolute totality in the synthesis of conditions and does not rest satisfied till it has attained to the absolutely, that is, in all respects and relations, unconditioned. For pure reason leaves to the understanding everything that immediately relates to the object of intuition or rather to their synthesis in imagination. The former restricts itself to the absolute totality in the employment of the conceptions of the understanding and aims at carrying out the synthetical unity which is cogitated in the category, even to the unconditioned. This unity may hence be called the rational unity of phenomena, as the other, which the category expresses, may be termed the unity of the understanding. Reason, therefore, has an immediate relation to the use of the understanding, not indeed in so far as the latter contains the ground of possible experience (for the conception of the absolute totality of conditions is not a conception that can be employed in experience, because no experience is unconditioned), but solely for the purpose of directing it to a certain unity, of which the understanding has no conception, and the aim of which is to collect into an absolute whole all acts of the understanding. Hence the objective employment of the pure conceptions of reason is always transcendent, while that of the pure conceptions of the understanding must, according to their nature, be always immanent, inasmuch as they are limited to possible experience.\n\nI understand by idea a necessary conception of reason, to which no corresponding object can be discovered in the world of sense. Accordingly, the pure conceptions of reason at present under consideration are transcendental ideas. They are conceptions of pure reason, for they regard all empirical cognition as determined by means of an absolute totality of conditions. They are not mere fictions, but natural and necessary products of reason, and have hence a necessary relation to the whole sphere of the exercise of the understanding. And, finally, they are transcendent, and overstep the limits of all experiences, in which, consequently, no object can ever be presented that would be perfectly adequate to a transcendental idea. When we use the word idea, we say, as regards its object (an object of the pure understanding), a great deal, but as regards its subject (that is, in respect of its reality under conditions of experience), exceedingly little, because the idea, as the conception of a maximum, can never be completely and adequately presented in concreto. Now, as in the merely speculative employment of reason the latter is properly the sole aim, and as in this case the approximation to a conception, which is never attained in practice, is the same thing as if the conception were non-existent—it is commonly said of the conception of this kind, “it is only an idea.” So we might very well say, “the absolute totality of all phenomena is only an idea,” for, as we never can present an adequate representation of it, it remains for us a problem incapable of solution. On the other hand, as in the practical use of the understanding we have only to do with action and practice according to rules, an idea of pure reason can always be given really in concreto, although only partially, nay, it is the indispensable condition of all practical employment of reason. The practice or execution of the idea is always limited and defective, but nevertheless within indeterminable boundaries, consequently always under the influence of the conception of an absolute perfection. And thus the practical idea is always in the highest degree fruitful, and in relation to real actions indispensably necessary. In the idea, pure reason possesses even causality and the power of producing that which its conception contains. Hence we cannot say of wisdom, in a disparaging way, “it is only an idea.” For, for the very reason that it is the idea of the necessary unity of all possible aims, it must be for all practical exertions and endeavours the primitive condition and rule—a rule which, if not constitutive, is at least limitative.\n\nNow, although we must say of the transcendental conceptions of reason, “they are only ideas,” we must not, on this account, look upon them as superfluous and nugatory. For, although no object can be determined by them, they can be of great utility, unobserved and at the basis of the edifice of the understanding, as the canon for its extended and self-consistent exercise—a canon which, indeed, does not enable it to cognize more in an object than it would cognize by the help of its own conceptions, but which guides it more securely in its cognition. Not to mention that they perhaps render possible a transition from our conceptions of nature and the non-ego to the practical conceptions, and thus produce for even ethical ideas keeping, so to speak, and connection with the speculative cognitions of reason. The explication of all this must be looked for in the sequel.\n\nBut setting aside, in conformity with our original purpose, the consideration of the practical ideas, we proceed to contemplate reason in its speculative use alone, nay, in a still more restricted sphere, to wit, in the transcendental use; and here must strike into the same path which we followed in our deduction of the categories. That is to say, we shall consider the logical form of the cognition of reason, that we may see whether reason may not be thereby a source of conceptions which enables us to regard objects in themselves as determined synthetically à priori, in relation to one or other of the functions of reason.\n\nReason, considered as the faculty of a certain logical form of cognition, is the faculty of conclusion, that is, of mediate judgement—by means of the subsumption of the condition of a possible judgement under the condition of a given judgement. The given judgement is the general rule (major). The subsumption of the condition of another possible judgement under the condition of the rule is the minor. The actual judgement, which enounces the assertion of the rule in the subsumed case, is the conclusion (conclusio). The rule predicates something generally under a certain condition. The condition of the rule is satisfied in some particular case. It follows that what was valid in general under that condition must also be considered as valid in the particular case which satisfies this condition. It is very plain that reason attains to a cognition, by means of acts of the understanding which constitute a series of conditions. When I arrive at the proposition, “All bodies are changeable,” by beginning with the more remote cognition (in which the conception of body does not appear, but which nevertheless contains the condition of that conception), “All compound is changeable,” by proceeding from this to a less remote cognition, which stands under the condition of the former, “Bodies are compound,” and hence to a third, which at length connects for me the remote cognition (changeable) with the one before me, “Consequently, bodies are changeable”—I have arrived at a cognition (conclusion) through a series of conditions (premisses). Now every series, whose exponent (of the categorical or hypothetical judgement) is given, can be continued; consequently the same procedure of reason conducts us to the ratiocinatio polysyllogistica, which is a series of syllogisms, that can be continued either on the side of the conditions (per prosyllogismos) or of the conditioned (per episyllogismos) to an indefinite extent.\n\nBut we very soon perceive that the chain or series of prosyllogisms, that is, of deduced cognitions on the side of the grounds or conditions of a given cognition, in other words, the ascending series of syllogisms must have a very different relation to the faculty of reason from that of the descending series, that is, the progressive procedure of reason on the side of the conditioned by means of episyllogisms. For, as in the former case the cognition (conclusio) is given only as conditioned, reason can attain to this cognition only under the presupposition that all the members of the series on the side of the conditions are given (totality in the series of premisses), because only under this supposition is the judgement we may be considering possible à priori; while on the side of the conditioned or the inferences, only an incomplete and becoming, and not a presupposed or given series, consequently only a potential progression, is cogitated. Hence, when a cognition is contemplated as conditioned, reason is compelled to consider the series of conditions in an ascending line as completed and given in their totality. But if the very same condition is considered at the same time as the condition of other cognitions, which together constitute a series of inferences or consequences in a descending line, reason may preserve a perfect indifference, as to how far this progression may extend _a parte posteriori_, and whether the totality of this series is possible, because it stands in no need of such a series for the purpose of arriving at the conclusion before it, inasmuch as this conclusion is sufficiently guaranteed and determined on grounds a parte priori. It may be the case, that upon the side of the conditions the series of premisses has a first or highest condition, or it may not possess this, and so be a parte priori unlimited; but it must, nevertheless, contain totality of conditions, even admitting that we never could succeed in completely apprehending it; and the whole series must be unconditionally true, if the conditioned, which is considered as an inference resulting from it, is to be held as true. This is a requirement of reason, which announces its cognition as determined à priori and as necessary, either in itself—and in this case it needs no grounds to rest upon—or, if it is deduced, as a member of a series of grounds, which is itself unconditionally true.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","text":"### Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas\n\nTranscendental analytic showed us how the mere logical form of our cognition can contain the origin of pure conceptions à priori, conceptions which represent objects antecedently to all experience, or rather, indicate the synthetical unity which alone renders possible an empirical cognition of objects. The form of judgements—converted into a conception of the synthesis of intuitions—produced the categories which direct the employment of the understanding in experience. This consideration warrants us to expect that the form of syllogisms, when applied to synthetical unity of intuitions, following the rule of the categories, will contain the origin of particular à priori conceptions, which we may call pure conceptions of reason or transcendental ideas, and which will determine the use of the understanding in the totality of experience according to principles.\n\nThe function of reason in arguments consists in the universality of a cognition according to conceptions, and the syllogism itself is a judgement which is determined à priori in the whole extent of its condition. The proposition: “Caius is mortal,” is one which may be obtained from experience by the aid of the understanding alone; but my wish is to find a conception which contains the condition under which the predicate of this judgement is given—in this case, the conception of man—and after subsuming under this condition, taken in its whole extent (all men are mortal), I determine according to it the cognition of the object thought, and say: “Caius is mortal.”","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"56.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","text":"Hence, in the conclusion of a syllogism we restrict a predicate to a certain object, after having thought it in the major in its whole extent under a certain condition. This complete quantity of the extent in relation to such a condition is called universality (universalitas). To this corresponds totality (universitas) of conditions in the synthesis of intuitions. The transcendental conception of reason is therefore nothing else than the conception of the totality of the conditions of a given conditioned. Now as the unconditioned alone renders possible totality of conditions, and, conversely, the totality of conditions is itself always unconditioned; a pure rational conception in general can be defined and explained by means of the conception of the unconditioned, in so far as it contains a basis for the synthesis of the conditioned.\n\nTo the number of modes of relation which the understanding cogitates by means of the categories, the number of pure rational conceptions will correspond. We must therefore seek for, first, an unconditioned of the categorical synthesis in a subject; secondly, of the hypothetical synthesis of the members of a series; thirdly, of the disjunctive synthesis of parts in a system.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"56.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0003","text":"There are exactly the same number of modes of syllogisms, each of which proceeds through prosyllogisms to the unconditioned—one to the subject which cannot be employed as predicate, another to the presupposition which supposes nothing higher than itself, and the third to an aggregate of the members of the complete division of a conception. Hence the pure rational conceptions of totality in the synthesis of conditions have a necessary foundation in the nature of human reason—at least as modes of elevating the unity of the understanding to the unconditioned. They may have no valid application, corresponding to their transcendental employment, in concreto, and be thus of no greater utility than to direct the understanding how, while extending them as widely as possible, to maintain its exercise and application in perfect consistence and harmony.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"56.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0004","text":"But, while speaking here of the totality of conditions and of the unconditioned as the common title of all conceptions of reason, we again light upon an expression which we find it impossible to dispense with, and which nevertheless, owing to the ambiguity attaching to it from long abuse, we cannot employ with safety. The word absolute is one of the few words which, in its original signification, was perfectly adequate to the conception it was intended to convey—a conception which no other word in the same language exactly suits, and the loss—or, which is the same thing, the incautious and loose employment—of which must be followed by the loss of the conception itself. And, as it is a conception which occupies much of the attention of reason, its loss would be greatly to the detriment of all transcendental philosophy. The word absolute is at present frequently used to denote that something can be predicated of a thing considered in itself and intrinsically. In this sense absolutely possible would signify that which is possible in itself (interne)—which is, in fact, the least that one can predicate of an object. On the other hand, it is sometimes employed to indicate that a thing is valid in all respects—for example, absolute sovereignty. Absolutely possible would in this sense signify that which is possible in all relations and in every respect; and this is the most that can be predicated of the possibility of a thing. Now these significations do in truth frequently coincide. Thus, for example, that which is intrinsically impossible, is also impossible in all relations, that is, absolutely impossible. But in most cases they differ from each other toto caelo, and I can by no means conclude that, because a thing is in itself possible, it is also possible in all relations, and therefore absolutely. Nay, more, I shall in the sequel show that absolute necessity does not by any means depend on internal necessity, and that, therefore, it must not be considered as synonymous with it. Of an opposite which is intrinsically impossible, we may affirm that it is in all respects impossible, and that, consequently, the thing itself, of which this is the opposite, is absolutely necessary; but I cannot reason conversely and say, the opposite of that which is absolutely necessary is intrinsically impossible, that is, that the absolute necessity of things is an internal necessity. For this internal necessity is in certain cases a mere empty word with which the least conception cannot be connected, while the conception of the necessity of a thing in all relations possesses very peculiar determinations. Now as the loss of a conception of great utility in speculative science cannot be a matter of indifference to the philosopher, I trust that the proper determination and careful preservation of the expression on which the conception depends will likewise be not indifferent to him.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"56.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","text":"In this enlarged signification, then, shall I employ the word absolute, in opposition to that which is valid only in some particular respect; for the latter is restricted by conditions, the former is valid without any restriction whatever.\n\nNow the transcendental conception of reason has for its object nothing else than absolute totality in the synthesis of conditions and does not rest satisfied till it has attained to the absolutely, that is, in all respects and relations, unconditioned. For pure reason leaves to the understanding everything that immediately relates to the object of intuition or rather to their synthesis in imagination. The former restricts itself to the absolute totality in the employment of the conceptions of the understanding and aims at carrying out the synthetical unity which is cogitated in the category, even to the unconditioned. This unity may hence be called the rational unity of phenomena, as the other, which the category expresses, may be termed the unity of the understanding. Reason, therefore, has an immediate relation to the use of the understanding, not indeed in so far as the latter contains the ground of possible experience (for the conception of the absolute totality of conditions is not a conception that can be employed in experience, because no experience is unconditioned), but solely for the purpose of directing it to a certain unity, of which the understanding has no conception, and the aim of which is to collect into an absolute whole all acts of the understanding. Hence the objective employment of the pure conceptions of reason is always transcendent, while that of the pure conceptions of the understanding must, according to their nature, be always immanent, inasmuch as they are limited to possible experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"56.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","text":"I understand by idea a necessary conception of reason, to which no corresponding object can be discovered in the world of sense. Accordingly, the pure conceptions of reason at present under consideration are transcendental ideas. They are conceptions of pure reason, for they regard all empirical cognition as determined by means of an absolute totality of conditions. They are not mere fictions, but natural and necessary products of reason, and have hence a necessary relation to the whole sphere of the exercise of the understanding. And, finally, they are transcendent, and overstep the limits of all experiences, in which, consequently, no object can ever be presented that would be perfectly adequate to a transcendental idea. When we use the word idea, we say, as regards its object (an object of the pure understanding), a great deal, but as regards its subject (that is, in respect of its reality under conditions of experience), exceedingly little, because the idea, as the conception of a maximum, can never be completely and adequately presented in concreto. Now, as in the merely speculative employment of reason the latter is properly the sole aim, and as in this case the approximation to a conception, which is never attained in practice, is the same thing as if the conception were non-existent—it is commonly said of the conception of this kind, “it is only an idea.” So we might very well say, “the absolute totality of all phenomena is only an idea,” for, as we never can present an adequate representation of it, it remains for us a problem incapable of solution. On the other hand, as in the practical use of the understanding we have only to do with action and practice according to rules, an idea of pure reason can always be given really in concreto, although only partially, nay, it is the indispensable condition of all practical employment of reason. The practice or execution of the idea is always limited and defective, but nevertheless within indeterminable boundaries, consequently always under the influence of the conception of an absolute perfection. And thus the practical idea is always in the highest degree fruitful, and in relation to real actions indispensably necessary. In the idea, pure reason possesses even causality and the power of producing that which its conception contains. Hence we cannot say of wisdom, in a disparaging way, “it is only an idea.” For, for the very reason that it is the idea of the necessary unity of all possible aims, it must be for all practical exertions and endeavours the primitive condition and rule—a rule which, if not constitutive, is at least limitative.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"56.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","text":"Now, although we must say of the transcendental conceptions of reason, “they are only ideas,” we must not, on this account, look upon them as superfluous and nugatory. For, although no object can be determined by them, they can be of great utility, unobserved and at the basis of the edifice of the understanding, as the canon for its extended and self-consistent exercise—a canon which, indeed, does not enable it to cognize more in an object than it would cognize by the help of its own conceptions, but which guides it more securely in its cognition. Not to mention that they perhaps render possible a transition from our conceptions of nature and the non-ego to the practical conceptions, and thus produce for even ethical ideas keeping, so to speak, and connection with the speculative cognitions of reason. The explication of all this must be looked for in the sequel.\n\nBut setting aside, in conformity with our original purpose, the consideration of the practical ideas, we proceed to contemplate reason in its speculative use alone, nay, in a still more restricted sphere, to wit, in the transcendental use; and here must strike into the same path which we followed in our deduction of the categories. That is to say, we shall consider the logical form of the cognition of reason, that we may see whether reason may not be thereby a source of conceptions which enables us to regard objects in themselves as determined synthetically à priori, in relation to one or other of the functions of reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"56.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0008","text":"Reason, considered as the faculty of a certain logical form of cognition, is the faculty of conclusion, that is, of mediate judgement—by means of the subsumption of the condition of a possible judgement under the condition of a given judgement. The given judgement is the general rule (major). The subsumption of the condition of another possible judgement under the condition of the rule is the minor. The actual judgement, which enounces the assertion of the rule in the subsumed case, is the conclusion (conclusio). The rule predicates something generally under a certain condition. The condition of the rule is satisfied in some particular case. It follows that what was valid in general under that condition must also be considered as valid in the particular case which satisfies this condition. It is very plain that reason attains to a cognition, by means of acts of the understanding which constitute a series of conditions. When I arrive at the proposition, “All bodies are changeable,” by beginning with the more remote cognition (in which the conception of body does not appear, but which nevertheless contains the condition of that conception), “All compound is changeable,” by proceeding from this to a less remote cognition, which stands under the condition of the former, “Bodies are compound,” and hence to a third, which at length connects for me the remote cognition (changeable) with the one before me, “Consequently, bodies are changeable”—I have arrived at a cognition (conclusion) through a series of conditions (premisses). Now every series, whose exponent (of the categorical or hypothetical judgement) is given, can be continued; consequently the same procedure of reason conducts us to the ratiocinatio polysyllogistica, which is a series of syllogisms, that can be continued either on the side of the conditions (per prosyllogismos) or of the conditioned (per episyllogismos) to an indefinite extent.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"56.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","text":"But we very soon perceive that the chain or series of prosyllogisms, that is, of deduced cognitions on the side of the grounds or conditions of a given cognition, in other words, the ascending series of syllogisms must have a very different relation to the faculty of reason from that of the descending series, that is, the progressive procedure of reason on the side of the conditioned by means of episyllogisms. For, as in the former case the cognition (conclusio) is given only as conditioned, reason can attain to this cognition only under the presupposition that all the members of the series on the side of the conditions are given (totality in the series of premisses), because only under this supposition is the judgement we may be considering possible à priori; while on the side of the conditioned or the inferences, only an incomplete and becoming, and not a presupposed or given series, consequently only a potential progression, is cogitated. Hence, when a cognition is contemplated as conditioned, reason is compelled to consider the series of conditions in an ascending line as completed and given in their totality. But if the very same condition is considered at the same time as the condition of other cognitions, which together constitute a series of inferences or consequences in a descending line, reason may preserve a perfect indifference, as to how far this progression may extend _a parte posteriori_, and whether the totality of this series is possible, because it stands in no need of such a series for the purpose of arriving at the conclusion before it, inasmuch as this conclusion is sufficiently guaranteed and determined on grounds a parte priori. It may be the case, that upon the side of the conditions the series of premisses has a first or highest condition, or it may not possess this, and so be a parte priori unlimited; but it must, nevertheless, contain totality of conditions, even admitting that we never could succeed in completely apprehending it; and the whole series must be unconditionally true, if the conditioned, which is considered as an inference resulting from it, is to be held as true. This is a requirement of reason, which announces its cognition as determined à priori and as necessary, either in itself—and in this case it needs no grounds to rest upon—or, if it is deduced, as a member of a series of grounds, which is itself unconditionally true.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"56.9"}],"sha256":"15693e1d71877707723885318d648022509dc3ed2892dc7b919776a20e75e38f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST","title":"Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas","order":57,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIST","text":"### Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas\n\nWe are not at present engaged with a logical dialectic, which makes complete abstraction of the content of cognition and aims only at unveiling the illusory appearance in the form of syllogisms. Our subject is transcendental dialectic, which must contain, completely à priori, the origin of certain cognitions drawn from pure reason, and the origin of certain deduced conceptions, the object of which cannot be given empirically and which therefore lie beyond the sphere of the faculty of understanding. We have observed, from the natural relation which the transcendental use of our cognition, in syllogisms as well as in judgements, must have to the logical, that there are three kinds of dialectical arguments, corresponding to the three modes of conclusion, by which reason attains to cognitions on principles; and that in all it is the business of reason to ascend from the conditioned synthesis, beyond which the understanding never proceeds, to the unconditioned which the understanding never can reach.\n\nNow the most general relations which can exist in our representations are: 1st, the relation to the subject; 2nd, the relation to objects, either as phenomena, or as objects of thought in general. If we connect this subdivision with the main division, all the relations of our representations, of which we can form either a conception or an idea, are threefold: 1. The relation to the subject; 2. The relation to the manifold of the object as a phenomenon; 3. The relation to all things in general.\n\nNow all pure conceptions have to do in general with the synthetical unity of representations; conceptions of pure reason (transcendental ideas), on the other hand, with the unconditional synthetical unity of all conditions. It follows that all transcendental ideas arrange themselves in three classes, the first of which contains the absolute (unconditioned) unity of the thinking subject, the second the absolute unity of the series of the conditions of a phenomenon, the third the absolute unity of the condition of all objects of thought in general.\n\nThe thinking subject is the object-matter of Psychology; the sum total of all phenomena (the world) is the object-matter of Cosmology; and the thing which contains the highest condition of the possibility of all that is cogitable (the being of all beings) is the object-matter of all Theology. Thus pure reason presents us with the idea of a transcendental doctrine of the soul (psychologia rationalis), of a transcendental science of the world (cosmologia rationalis), and finally of a transcendental doctrine of God (theologia transcendentalis). Understanding cannot originate even the outline of any of these sciences, even when connected with the highest logical use of reason, that is, all cogitable syllogisms—for the purpose of proceeding from one object (phenomenon) to all others, even to the utmost limits of the empirical synthesis. They are, on the contrary, pure and genuine products, or problems, of pure reason.\n\nWhat modi of the pure conceptions of reason these transcendental ideas are will be fully exposed in the following chapter. They follow the guiding thread of the categories. For pure reason never relates immediately to objects, but to the conceptions of these contained in the understanding. In like manner, it will be made manifest in the detailed explanation of these ideas—how reason, merely through the synthetical use of the same function which it employs in a categorical syllogism, necessarily attains to the conception of the absolute unity of the thinking subject—how the logical procedure in hypothetical ideas necessarily produces the idea of the absolutely unconditioned in a series of given conditions, and finally—how the mere form of the disjunctive syllogism involves the highest conception of a being of all beings: a thought which at first sight seems in the highest degree paradoxical.\n\nAn objective deduction, such as we were able to present in the case of the categories, is impossible as regards these transcendental ideas. For they have, in truth, no relation to any object, in experience, for the very reason that they are only ideas. But a subjective deduction of them from the nature of our reason is possible, and has been given in the present chapter.\n\nIt is easy to perceive that the sole aim of pure reason is the absolute totality of the synthesis on the side of the conditions, and that it does not concern itself with the absolute completeness on the Part of the conditioned. For of the former alone does she stand in need, in order to preposit the whole series of conditions, and thus present them to the understanding à priori. But if we once have a completely (and unconditionally) given condition, there is no further necessity, in proceeding with the series, for a conception of reason; for the understanding takes of itself every step downward, from the condition to the conditioned. Thus the transcendental ideas are available only for ascending in the series of conditions, till we reach the unconditioned, that is, principles. As regards descending to the conditioned, on the other hand, we find that there is a widely extensive logical use which reason makes of the laws of the understanding, but that a transcendental use thereof is impossible; and that when we form an idea of the absolute totality of such a synthesis, for example, of the whole series of all future changes in the world, this idea is a mere ens rationis, an arbitrary fiction of thought, and not a necessary presupposition of reason. For the possibility of the conditioned presupposes the totality of its conditions, but not of its consequences. Consequently, this conception is not a transcendental idea—and it is with these alone that we are at present occupied.\n\nFinally, it is obvious that there exists among the transcendental ideas a certain connection and unity, and that pure reason, by means of them, collects all its cognitions into one system. From the cognition of self to the cognition of the world, and through these to the supreme being, the progression is so natural, that it seems to resemble the logical march of reason from the premisses to the conclusion.[[42]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-42) Now whether there lies unobserved at the foundation of these ideas an analogy of the same kind as exists between the logical and transcendental procedure of reason, is another of those questions, the answer to which we must not expect till we arrive at a more advanced stage in our inquiries. In this cursory and preliminary view, we have, meanwhile, reached our aim. For we have dispelled the ambiguity which attached to the transcendental conceptions of reason, from their being commonly mixed up with other conceptions in the systems of philosophers, and not properly distinguished from the conceptions of the understanding; we have exposed their origin and, thereby, at the same time their determinate number, and presented them in a systematic connection, and have thus marked out and enclosed a definite sphere for pure reason.\n\n[[42]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-42) The science of Metaphysics has for the proper object of its inquiries only three grand ideas: GOD, FREEDOM, and IMMORTALITY, and it aims at showing, that the second conception, conjoined with the first, must lead to the third, as a necessary conclusion. All the other subjects with which it occupies itself, are merely means for the attainment and realization of these ideas. It does not require these ideas for the construction of a science of nature, but, on the contrary, for the purpose of passing beyond the sphere of nature. A complete insight into and comprehension of them would render Theology, Ethics, and, through the conjunction of both, Religion, solely dependent on the speculative faculty of reason. In a systematic representation of these ideas the above-mentioned arrangement—the synthetical one—would be the most suitable; but in the investigation which must necessarily precede it, the analytical, which reverses this arrangement, would be better adapted to our purpose, as in it we should proceed from that which experience immediately presents to us—psychology, to cosmology, and thence to theology.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","text":"### Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas\n\nWe are not at present engaged with a logical dialectic, which makes complete abstraction of the content of cognition and aims only at unveiling the illusory appearance in the form of syllogisms. Our subject is transcendental dialectic, which must contain, completely à priori, the origin of certain cognitions drawn from pure reason, and the origin of certain deduced conceptions, the object of which cannot be given empirically and which therefore lie beyond the sphere of the faculty of understanding. We have observed, from the natural relation which the transcendental use of our cognition, in syllogisms as well as in judgements, must have to the logical, that there are three kinds of dialectical arguments, corresponding to the three modes of conclusion, by which reason attains to cognitions on principles; and that in all it is the business of reason to ascend from the conditioned synthesis, beyond which the understanding never proceeds, to the unconditioned which the understanding never can reach.\n\nNow the most general relations which can exist in our representations are: 1st, the relation to the subject; 2nd, the relation to objects, either as phenomena, or as objects of thought in general. If we connect this subdivision with the main division, all the relations of our representations, of which we can form either a conception or an idea, are threefold: 1. The relation to the subject; 2. The relation to the manifold of the object as a phenomenon; 3. The relation to all things in general.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"57.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","text":"Now all pure conceptions have to do in general with the synthetical unity of representations; conceptions of pure reason (transcendental ideas), on the other hand, with the unconditional synthetical unity of all conditions. It follows that all transcendental ideas arrange themselves in three classes, the first of which contains the absolute (unconditioned) unity of the thinking subject, the second the absolute unity of the series of the conditions of a phenomenon, the third the absolute unity of the condition of all objects of thought in general.\n\nThe thinking subject is the object-matter of Psychology; the sum total of all phenomena (the world) is the object-matter of Cosmology; and the thing which contains the highest condition of the possibility of all that is cogitable (the being of all beings) is the object-matter of all Theology. Thus pure reason presents us with the idea of a transcendental doctrine of the soul (psychologia rationalis), of a transcendental science of the world (cosmologia rationalis), and finally of a transcendental doctrine of God (theologia transcendentalis). Understanding cannot originate even the outline of any of these sciences, even when connected with the highest logical use of reason, that is, all cogitable syllogisms—for the purpose of proceeding from one object (phenomenon) to all others, even to the utmost limits of the empirical synthesis. They are, on the contrary, pure and genuine products, or problems, of pure reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"57.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","text":"What modi of the pure conceptions of reason these transcendental ideas are will be fully exposed in the following chapter. They follow the guiding thread of the categories. For pure reason never relates immediately to objects, but to the conceptions of these contained in the understanding. In like manner, it will be made manifest in the detailed explanation of these ideas—how reason, merely through the synthetical use of the same function which it employs in a categorical syllogism, necessarily attains to the conception of the absolute unity of the thinking subject—how the logical procedure in hypothetical ideas necessarily produces the idea of the absolutely unconditioned in a series of given conditions, and finally—how the mere form of the disjunctive syllogism involves the highest conception of a being of all beings: a thought which at first sight seems in the highest degree paradoxical.\n\nAn objective deduction, such as we were able to present in the case of the categories, is impossible as regards these transcendental ideas. For they have, in truth, no relation to any object, in experience, for the very reason that they are only ideas. But a subjective deduction of them from the nature of our reason is possible, and has been given in the present chapter.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"57.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","text":"It is easy to perceive that the sole aim of pure reason is the absolute totality of the synthesis on the side of the conditions, and that it does not concern itself with the absolute completeness on the Part of the conditioned. For of the former alone does she stand in need, in order to preposit the whole series of conditions, and thus present them to the understanding à priori. But if we once have a completely (and unconditionally) given condition, there is no further necessity, in proceeding with the series, for a conception of reason; for the understanding takes of itself every step downward, from the condition to the conditioned. Thus the transcendental ideas are available only for ascending in the series of conditions, till we reach the unconditioned, that is, principles. As regards descending to the conditioned, on the other hand, we find that there is a widely extensive logical use which reason makes of the laws of the understanding, but that a transcendental use thereof is impossible; and that when we form an idea of the absolute totality of such a synthesis, for example, of the whole series of all future changes in the world, this idea is a mere ens rationis, an arbitrary fiction of thought, and not a necessary presupposition of reason. For the possibility of the conditioned presupposes the totality of its conditions, but not of its consequences. Consequently, this conception is not a transcendental idea—and it is with these alone that we are at present occupied.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"57.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","text":"Finally, it is obvious that there exists among the transcendental ideas a certain connection and unity, and that pure reason, by means of them, collects all its cognitions into one system. From the cognition of self to the cognition of the world, and through these to the supreme being, the progression is so natural, that it seems to resemble the logical march of reason from the premisses to the conclusion.[[42]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-42) Now whether there lies unobserved at the foundation of these ideas an analogy of the same kind as exists between the logical and transcendental procedure of reason, is another of those questions, the answer to which we must not expect till we arrive at a more advanced stage in our inquiries. In this cursory and preliminary view, we have, meanwhile, reached our aim. For we have dispelled the ambiguity which attached to the transcendental conceptions of reason, from their being commonly mixed up with other conceptions in the systems of philosophers, and not properly distinguished from the conceptions of the understanding; we have exposed their origin and, thereby, at the same time their determinate number, and presented them in a systematic connection, and have thus marked out and enclosed a definite sphere for pure reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"57.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0006","text":"[[42]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-42) The science of Metaphysics has for the proper object of its inquiries only three grand ideas: GOD, FREEDOM, and IMMORTALITY, and it aims at showing, that the second conception, conjoined with the first, must lead to the third, as a necessary conclusion. All the other subjects with which it occupies itself, are merely means for the attainment and realization of these ideas. It does not require these ideas for the construction of a science of nature, but, on the contrary, for the purpose of passing beyond the sphere of nature. A complete insight into and comprehension of them would render Theology, Ethics, and, through the conjunction of both, Religion, solely dependent on the speculative faculty of reason. In a systematic representation of these ideas the above-mentioned arrangement—the synthetical one—would be the most suitable; but in the investigation which must necessarily precede it, the analytical, which reverses this arrangement, would be better adapted to our purpose, as in it we should proceed from that which experience immediately presents to us—psychology, to cosmology, and thence to theology.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"57.6"}],"sha256":"ea9e1d80dd5869c3daec6039cc5f368b3c64c805042db7816321d216a5627304","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2","title":"TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK II—OF THE DIALECTICAL PROCEDURE OF PURE REASON","order":58,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_TD_2","text":"# Dialectic\n\n### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK II—OF THE DIALECTICAL PROCEDURE OF PURE REASON\n\nIt may be said that the object of a merely transcendental idea is something of which we have no conception, although the idea may be a necessary product of reason according to its original laws. For, in fact, a conception of an object that is adequate to the idea given by reason, is impossible. For such an object must be capable of being presented and intuited in a Possible experience. But we should express our meaning better, and with less risk of being misunderstood, if we said that we can have no knowledge of an object, which perfectly corresponds to an idea, although we may possess a problematical conception thereof.\n\nNow the transcendental (subjective) reality at least of the pure conceptions of reason rests upon the fact that we are led to such ideas by a necessary procedure of reason. There must therefore be syllogisms which contain no empirical premisses, and by means of which we conclude from something that we do know, to something of which we do not even possess a conception, to which we, nevertheless, by an unavoidable illusion, ascribe objective reality. Such arguments are, as regards their result, rather to be termed sophisms than syllogisms, although indeed, as regards their origin, they are very well entitled to the latter name, inasmuch as they are not fictions or accidental products of reason, but are necessitated by its very nature. They are sophisms, not of men, but of pure reason herself, from which the Wisest cannot free himself. After long labour he may be able to guard against the error, but he can never be thoroughly rid of the illusion which continually mocks and misleads him.\n\nOf these dialectical arguments there are three kinds, corresponding to the number of the ideas which their conclusions present. In the argument or syllogism of the first class, I conclude, from the transcendental conception of the subject which contains no manifold, the absolute unity of the subject itself, of which I cannot in this manner attain to a conception. This dialectical argument I shall call the transcendental paralogism. The second class of sophistical arguments is occupied with the transcendental conception of the absolute totality of the series of conditions for a given phenomenon, and I conclude, from the fact that I have always a self-contradictory conception of the unconditioned synthetical unity of the series upon one side, the truth of the opposite unity, of which I have nevertheless no conception. The condition of reason in these dialectical arguments, I shall term the antinomy of pure reason. Finally, according to the third kind of sophistical argument, I conclude, from the totality of the conditions of thinking objects in general, in so far as they can be given, the absolute synthetical unity of all conditions of the possibility of things in general; that is, from things which I do not know in their mere transcendental conception, I conclude a being of all beings which I know still less by means of a transcendental conception, and of whose unconditioned necessity I can form no conception whatever. This dialectical argument I shall call the ideal of pure reason.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","text":"# Dialectic\n\n### TRANSCENDENTAL DIALECTIC—BOOK II—OF THE DIALECTICAL PROCEDURE OF PURE REASON\n\nIt may be said that the object of a merely transcendental idea is something of which we have no conception, although the idea may be a necessary product of reason according to its original laws. For, in fact, a conception of an object that is adequate to the idea given by reason, is impossible. For such an object must be capable of being presented and intuited in a Possible experience. But we should express our meaning better, and with less risk of being misunderstood, if we said that we can have no knowledge of an object, which perfectly corresponds to an idea, although we may possess a problematical conception thereof.\n\nNow the transcendental (subjective) reality at least of the pure conceptions of reason rests upon the fact that we are led to such ideas by a necessary procedure of reason. There must therefore be syllogisms which contain no empirical premisses, and by means of which we conclude from something that we do know, to something of which we do not even possess a conception, to which we, nevertheless, by an unavoidable illusion, ascribe objective reality. Such arguments are, as regards their result, rather to be termed sophisms than syllogisms, although indeed, as regards their origin, they are very well entitled to the latter name, inasmuch as they are not fictions or accidental products of reason, but are necessitated by its very nature. They are sophisms, not of men, but of pure reason herself, from which the Wisest cannot free himself. After long labour he may be able to guard against the error, but he can never be thoroughly rid of the illusion which continually mocks and misleads him.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"58.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","text":"Of these dialectical arguments there are three kinds, corresponding to the number of the ideas which their conclusions present. In the argument or syllogism of the first class, I conclude, from the transcendental conception of the subject which contains no manifold, the absolute unity of the subject itself, of which I cannot in this manner attain to a conception. This dialectical argument I shall call the transcendental paralogism. The second class of sophistical arguments is occupied with the transcendental conception of the absolute totality of the series of conditions for a given phenomenon, and I conclude, from the fact that I have always a self-contradictory conception of the unconditioned synthetical unity of the series upon one side, the truth of the opposite unity, of which I have nevertheless no conception. The condition of reason in these dialectical arguments, I shall term the antinomy of pure reason. Finally, according to the third kind of sophistical argument, I conclude, from the totality of the conditions of thinking objects in general, in so far as they can be given, the absolute synthetical unity of all conditions of the possibility of things in general; that is, from things which I do not know in their mere transcendental conception, I conclude a being of all beings which I know still less by means of a transcendental conception, and of whose unconditioned necessity I can form no conception whatever. This dialectical argument I shall call the ideal of pure reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"58.2"}],"sha256":"e04382bbfec7d11858b9fc85285adb9b8dba2cac8c92b827753e76845e0f7df2","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP","title":"Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason","order":59,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_CIPP","text":"### Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason\n\nThe logical paralogism consists in the falsity of an argument in respect of its form, be the content what it may. But a transcendental paralogism has a transcendental foundation, and concludes falsely, while the form is correct and unexceptionable. In this manner the paralogism has its foundation in the nature of human reason, and is the parent of an unavoidable, though not insoluble, mental illusion.\n\nWe now come to a conception which was not inserted in the general list of transcendental conceptions, and yet must be reckoned with them, but at the same time without in the least altering, or indicating a deficiency in that table. This is the conception, or, if the term is preferred, the judgement, “I think.” But it is readily perceived that this thought is as it were the vehicle of all conceptions in general, and consequently of transcendental conceptions also, and that it is therefore regarded as a transcendental conception, although it can have no peculiar claim to be so ranked, inasmuch as its only use is to indicate that all thought is accompanied by consciousness. At the same time, pure as this conception is from empirical content (impressions of the senses), it enables us to distinguish two different kinds of objects. “I,” as thinking, am an object of the internal sense, and am called soul. That which is an object of the external senses is called body. Thus the expression, “I,” as a thinking being, designates the object-matter of psychology, which may be called “the rational doctrine of the soul,” inasmuch as in this science I desire to know nothing of the soul but what, independently of all experience (which determines me in concreto), may be concluded from this conception “I,” in so far as it appears in all thought.\n\nNow, the rational doctrine of the soul is really an undertaking of this kind. For if the smallest empirical element of thought, if any particular perception of my internal state, were to be introduced among the grounds of cognition of this science, it would not be a rational, but an empirical doctrine of the soul. We have thus before us a pretended science, raised upon the single proposition, “I think,” whose foundation or want of foundation we may very properly, and agreeably with the nature of a transcendental philosophy, here examine. It ought not to be objected that in this proposition, which expresses the perception of one’s self, an internal experience is asserted, and that consequently the rational doctrine of the soul which is founded upon it, is not pure, but partly founded upon an empirical principle. For this internal perception is nothing more than the mere apperception, “I think,” which in fact renders all transcendental conceptions possible, in which we say, “I think substance, cause, etc.” For internal experience in general and its possibility, or perception in general, and its relation to other perceptions, unless some particular distinction or determination thereof is empirically given, cannot be regarded as empirical cognition, but as cognition of the empirical, and belongs to the investigation of the possibility of every experience, which is certainly transcendental. The smallest object of experience (for example, only pleasure or pain), that should be included in the general representation of self-consciousness, would immediately change the rational into an empirical psychology.\n\n“I think” is therefore the only text of rational psychology, from which it must develop its whole system. It is manifest that this thought, when applied to an object (myself), can contain nothing but transcendental predicates thereof; because the least empirical predicate would destroy the purity of the science and its independence of all experience.\n\nBut we shall have to follow here the guidance of the categories—only, as in the present case a thing, “I,” as thinking being, is at first given, we shall—not indeed change the order of the categories as it stands in the table—but begin at the category of substance, by which at the a thing in itself is represented and proceeds backwards through the series. The topic of the rational doctrine of the soul, from which everything else it may contain must be deduced, is accordingly as follows:\n\n            1                          2\n  The Soul is SUBSTANCE       As regards its quality\n                                it is SIMPLE\n\n                      3\n          As regards the different\n          times in which it exists,\n          it is numerically identical,\n          that is UNITY, not Plurality.\n\n                       4\n  It is in relation to possible objects in space[[43]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-43)\n\n[[43]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-43) The reader, who may not so easily perceive the psychological sense of these expressions, taken here in their transcendental abstraction, and cannot guess why the latter attribute of the soul belongs to the category of existence, will find the expressions sufficiently explained and justified in the sequel. I have, moreover, to apologize for the Latin terms which have been employed, instead of their German synonyms, contrary to the rules of correct writing. But I judged it better to sacrifice elegance to perspicuity.\n\nFrom these elements originate all the conceptions of pure psychology, by combination alone, without the aid of any other principle. This substance, merely as an object of the internal sense, gives the conception of Immateriality; as simple substance, that of Incorruptibility; its identity, as intellectual substance, gives the conception of Personality; all these three together, Spirituality. Its relation to objects in space gives us the conception of connection (commercium) with bodies. Thus it represents thinking substance as the principle of life in matter, that is, as a soul (anima), and as the ground of Animality; and this, limited and determined by the conception of spirituality, gives us that of Immortality.\n\nNow to these conceptions relate four paralogisms of a transcendental psychology, which is falsely held to be a science of pure reason, touching the nature of our thinking being. We can, however, lay at the foundation of this science nothing but the simple and in itself perfectly contentless representation “i” which cannot even be called a conception, but merely a consciousness which accompanies all conceptions. By this “I,” or “He,” or “It,” who or which thinks, nothing more is represented than a transcendental subject of thought = x, which is cognized only by means of the thoughts that are its predicates, and of which, apart from these, we cannot form the least conception. Hence in a perpetual circle, inasmuch as we must always employ it, in order to frame any judgement respecting it. And this inconvenience we find it impossible to rid ourselves of, because consciousness in itself is not so much a representation distinguishing a particular object, as a form of representation in general, in so far as it may be termed cognition; for in and by cognition alone do I think anything.\n\nIt must, however, appear extraordinary at first sight that the condition under which I think, and which is consequently a property of my subject, should be held to be likewise valid for every existence which thinks, and that we can presume to base upon a seemingly empirical proposition a judgement which is apodeictic and universal, to wit, that everything which thinks is constituted as the voice of my consciousness declares it to be, that is, as a self-conscious being. The cause of this belief is to be found in the fact that we necessarily attribute to things à priori all the properties which constitute conditions under which alone we can cogitate them. Now I cannot obtain the least representation of a thinking being by means of external experience, but solely through self-consciousness. Such objects are consequently nothing more than the transference of this consciousness of mine to other things which can only thus be represented as thinking beings. The proposition, “I think,” is, in the present case, understood in a problematical sense, not in so far as it contains a perception of an existence (like the Cartesian “Cogito, ergo sum”), but in regard to its mere possibility—for the purpose of discovering what properties may be inferred from so simple a proposition and predicated of the subject of it.\n\nIf at the foundation of our pure rational cognition of thinking beings there lay more than the mere Cogito—if we could likewise call in aid observations on the play of our thoughts, and the thence derived natural laws of the thinking self, there would arise an empirical psychology which would be a kind of physiology of the internal sense and might possibly be capable of explaining the phenomena of that sense. But it could never be available for discovering those properties which do not belong to possible experience (such as the quality of simplicity), nor could it make any apodeictic enunciation on the nature of thinking beings: it would therefore not be a rational psychology.\n\nNow, as the proposition “I think” (in the problematical sense) contains the form of every judgement in general and is the constant accompaniment of all the categories, it is manifest that conclusions are drawn from it only by a transcendental employment of the understanding. This use of the understanding excludes all empirical elements; and we cannot, as has been shown above, have any favourable conception beforehand of its procedure. We shall therefore follow with a critical eye this proposition through all the predicaments of pure psychology; but we shall, for brevity’s sake, allow this examination to proceed in an uninterrupted connection.\n\nBefore entering on this task, however, the following general remark may help to quicken our attention to this mode of argument. It is not merely through my thinking that I cognize an object, but only through my determining a given intuition in relation to the unity of consciousness in which all thinking consists. It follows that I cognize myself, not through my being conscious of myself as thinking, but only when I am conscious of the intuition of myself as determined in relation to the function of thought. All the modi of self-consciousness in thought are hence not conceptions of objects (conceptions of the understanding—categories); they are mere logical functions, which do not present to thought an object to be cognized, and cannot therefore present my Self as an object. Not the consciousness of the determining, but only that of the determinable self, that is, of my internal intuition (in so far as the manifold contained in it can be connected conformably with the general condition of the unity of apperception in thought), is the object.\n\n1. In all judgements I am the determining subject of that relation which constitutes a judgement. But that the I which thinks, must be considered as in thought always a subject, and as a thing which cannot be a predicate to thought, is an apodeictic and identical proposition. But this proposition does not signify that I, as an object, am, for myself, a self-subsistent being or substance. This latter statement—an ambitious one—requires to be supported by data which are not to be discovered in thought; and are perhaps (in so far as I consider the thinking self merely as such) not to be discovered in the thinking self at all.\n\n2. That the I or Ego of apperception, and consequently in all thought, is singular or simple, and cannot be resolved into a plurality of subjects, and therefore indicates a logically simple subject—this is self-evident from the very conception of an Ego, and is consequently an analytical proposition. But this is not tantamount to declaring that the thinking Ego is a simple substance—for this would be a synthetical proposition. The conception of substance always relates to intuitions, which with me cannot be other than sensuous, and which consequently lie completely out of the sphere of the understanding and its thought: but to this sphere belongs the affirmation that the Ego is simple in thought. It would indeed be surprising, if the conception of “substance,” which in other cases requires so much labour to distinguish from the other elements presented by intuition—so much trouble, too, to discover whether it can be simple (as in the case of the parts of matter)—should be presented immediately to me, as if by revelation, in the poorest mental representation of all.\n\n3. The proposition of the identity of my Self amidst all the manifold representations of which I am conscious, is likewise a proposition lying in the conceptions themselves, and is consequently analytical. But this identity of the subject, of which I am conscious in all its representations, does not relate to or concern the intuition of the subject, by which it is given as an object. This proposition cannot therefore enounce the identity of the person, by which is understood the consciousness of the identity of its own substance as a thinking being in all change and variation of circumstances. To prove this, we should require not a mere analysis of the proposition, but synthetical judgements based upon a given intuition.\n\n4. I distinguish my own existence, as that of a thinking being, from that of other things external to me—among which my body also is reckoned. This is also an analytical proposition, for other things are exactly those which I think as different or distinguished from myself. But whether this consciousness of myself is possible without things external to me; and whether therefore I can exist merely as a thinking being (without being man)—cannot be known or inferred from this proposition.\n\nThus we have gained nothing as regards the cognition of myself as object, by the analysis of the consciousness of my Self in thought. The logical exposition of thought in general is mistaken for a metaphysical determination of the object.\n\nOur Critique would be an investigation utterly superfluous, if there existed a possibility of proving à priori, that all thinking beings are in themselves simple substances, as such, therefore, possess the inseparable attribute of personality, and are conscious of their existence apart from and unconnected with matter. For we should thus have taken a step beyond the world of sense, and have penetrated into the sphere of noumena; and in this case the right could not be denied us of extending our knowledge in this sphere, of establishing ourselves, and, under a favouring star, appropriating to ourselves possessions in it. For the proposition: “Every thinking being, as such, is simple substance,” is an à priori synthetical proposition; because in the first place it goes beyond the conception which is the subject of it, and adds to the mere notion of a thinking being the mode of its existence, and in the second place annexes a predicate (that of simplicity) to the latter conception—a predicate which it could not have discovered in the sphere of experience. It would follow that à priori synthetical propositions are possible and legitimate, not only, as we have maintained, in relation to objects of possible experience, and as principles of the possibility of this experience itself, but are applicable to things in themselves—an inference which makes an end of the whole of this Critique, and obliges us to fall back on the old mode of metaphysical procedure. But indeed the danger is not so great, if we look a little closer into the question.\n\nThere lurks in the procedure of rational Psychology a paralogism, which is represented in the following syllogism:\n\nThat which cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subject, does not exist otherwise than as subject, and is therefore substance.\n\nA thinking being, considered merely as such, cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subject.\n\nTherefore it exists also as such, that is, as substance.\n\nIn the major we speak of a being that can be cogitated generally and in every relation, consequently as it may be given in intuition. But in the minor we speak of the same being only in so far as it regards itself as subject, relatively to thought and the unity of consciousness, but not in relation to intuition, by which it is presented as an object to thought. Thus the conclusion is here arrived at by a Sophisma figurae dictionis.[[44]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-44)\n\n[[44]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-44) Thought is taken in the two premisses in two totally different senses. In the major it is considered as relating and applying to objects in general, consequently to objects of intuition also. In the minor, we understand it as relating merely to self-consciousness. In this sense, we do not cogitate an object, but merely the relation to the self-consciousness of the subject, as the form of thought. In the former premiss we speak of things which cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subjects. In the second, we do not speak of things, but of thought (all objects being abstracted), in which the Ego is always the subject of consciousness. Hence the conclusion cannot be, “I cannot exist otherwise than as subject”; but only “I can, in cogitating my existence, employ my Ego only as the subject of the judgement.” But this is an identical proposition, and throws no light on the mode of my existence.\n\nThat this famous argument is a mere paralogism, will be plain to any one who will consider the general remark which precedes our exposition of the principles of the pure understanding, and the section on noumena. For it was there proved that the conception of a thing, which can exist per se—only as a subject and never as a predicate, possesses no objective reality; that is to say, we can never know whether there exists any object to correspond to the conception; consequently, the conception is nothing more than a conception, and from it we derive no proper knowledge. If this conception is to indicate by the term substance, an object that can be given, if it is to become a cognition, we must have at the foundation of the cognition a permanent intuition, as the indispensable condition of its objective reality. For through intuition alone can an object be given. But in internal intuition there is nothing permanent, for the Ego is but the consciousness of my thought. If then, we appeal merely to thought, we cannot discover the necessary condition of the application of the conception of substance—that is, of a subject existing per se—to the subject as a thinking being. And thus the conception of the simple nature of substance, which is connected with the objective reality of this conception, is shown to be also invalid, and to be, in fact, nothing more than the logical qualitative unity of self-consciousness in thought; whilst we remain perfectly ignorant whether the subject is composite or not.\n\nRefutation of the Argument of Mendelssohn for the Substantiality or Permanence of the Soul.\n\nThis acute philosopher easily perceived the insufficiency of the common argument which attempts to prove that the soul—it being granted that it is a simple being—cannot perish by dissolution or decomposition; he saw it is not impossible for it to cease to be by extinction, or disappearance. He endeavoured to prove in his Phaedo, that the soul cannot be annihilated, by showing that a simple being cannot cease to exist. Inasmuch as, he said, a simple existence cannot diminish, nor gradually lose portions of its being, and thus be by degrees reduced to nothing (for it possesses no parts, and therefore no multiplicity), between the moment in which it is, and the moment in which it is not, no time can be discovered—which is impossible. But this philosopher did not consider that, granting the soul to possess this simple nature, which contains no parts external to each other and consequently no extensive quantity, we cannot refuse to it any less than to any other being, intensive quantity, that is, a degree of reality in regard to all its faculties, nay, to all that constitutes its existence. But this degree of reality can become less and less through an infinite series of smaller degrees. It follows, therefore, that this supposed substance—this thing, the permanence of which is not assured in any other way, may, if not by decomposition, by gradual loss (remissio) of its powers (consequently by elanguescence, if I may employ this expression), be changed into nothing. For consciousness itself has always a degree, which may be lessened.[[45]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-45)Consequently the faculty of being conscious may be diminished; and so with all other faculties. The permanence of the soul, therefore, as an object of the internal sense, remains undemonstrated, nay, even indemonstrable. Its permanence in life is evident, per se, inasmuch as the thinking being (as man) is to itself, at the same time, an object of the external senses. But this does not authorize the rational psychologist to affirm, from mere conceptions, its permanence beyond life.[[46]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-46)\n\n[[45]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-45) Clearness is not, as logicians maintain, the consciousness of a representation. For a certain degree of consciousness, which may not, however, be sufficient for recollection, is to be met with in many dim representations. For without any consciousness at all, we should not be able to recognize any difference in the obscure representations we connect; as we really can do with many conceptions, such as those of right and justice, and those of the musician, who strikes at once several notes in improvising a piece of music. But a representation is clear, in which our consciousness is sufficient for the consciousness of the difference of this representation from others. If we are only conscious that there is a difference, but are not conscious of the difference—that is, what the difference is—the representation must be termed obscure. There is, consequently, an infinite series of degrees of consciousness down to its entire disappearance.\n\n[[46]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-46) There are some who think they have done enough to establish a new possibility in the mode of the existence of souls, when they have shown that there is no contradiction in their hypotheses on this subject. Such are those who affirm the possibility of thought—of which they have no other knowledge than what they derive from its use in connecting empirical intuitions presented in this our human life—after this life has ceased. But it is very easy to embarrass them by the introduction of counter-possibilities, which rest upon quite as good a foundation. Such, for example, is the possibility of the division of a simple substance into several substances; and conversely, of the coalition of several into one simple substance. For, although divisibility presupposes composition, it does not necessarily require a composition of substances, but only of the degrees (of the several faculties) of one and the same substance. Now we can cogitate all the powers and faculties of the soul—even that of consciousness—as diminished by one half, the substance still remaining. In the same way we can represent to ourselves without contradiction, this obliterated half as preserved, not in the soul, but without it; and we can believe that, as in this case every thing that is real in the soul, and has a degree—consequently its entire existence—has been halved, a particular substance would arise out of the soul. For the multiplicity, which has been divided, formerly existed, but not as a multiplicity of substances, but of every reality as the quantum of existence in it; and the unity of substance was merely a mode of existence, which by this division alone has been transformed into a plurality of subsistence. In the same manner several simple substances might coalesce into one, without anything being lost except the plurality of subsistence, inasmuch as the one substance would contain the degree of reality of all the former substances. Perhaps, indeed, the simple substances, which appear under the form of matter, might (not indeed by a mechanical or chemical influence upon each other, but by an unknown influence, of which the former would be but the phenomenal appearance), by means of such a dynamical division of the parent-souls, as intensive quantities, produce other souls, while the former repaired the loss thus sustained with new matter of the same sort. I am far from allowing any value to such chimeras; and the principles of our analytic have clearly proved that no other than an empirical use of the categories—that of substance, for example—is possible. But if the rationalist is bold enough to construct, on the mere authority of the faculty of thought—without any intuition, whereby an object is given—a self-subsistent being, merely because the unity of apperception in thought cannot allow him to believe it a composite being, instead of declaring, as he ought to do, that he is unable to explain the possibility of a thinking nature; what ought to hinder the materialist, with as complete an independence of experience, to employ the principle of the rationalist in a directly opposite manner—still preserving the formal unity required by his opponent?\n\nIf, now, we take the above propositions—as they must be accepted as valid for all thinking beings in the system of rational psychology—in synthetical connection, and proceed, from the category of relation, with the proposition: “All thinking beings are, as such, substances,” backwards through the series, till the circle is completed; we come at last to their existence, of which, in this system of rational psychology, substances are held to be conscious, independently of external things; nay, it is asserted that, in relation to the permanence which is a necessary characteristic of substance, they can of themselves determine external things. It follows that idealism—at least problematical idealism, is perfectly unavoidable in this rationalistic system. And, if the existence of outward things is not held to be requisite to the determination of the existence of a substance in time, the existence of these outward things at all, is a gratuitous assumption which remains without the possibility of a proof.\n\nBut if we proceed analytically—the “I think” as a proposition containing in itself an existence as given, consequently modality being the principle—and dissect this proposition, in order to ascertain its content, and discover whether and how this Ego determines its existence in time and space without the aid of anything external; the propositions of rationalistic psychology would not begin with the conception of a thinking being, but with a reality, and the properties of a thinking being in general would be deduced from the mode in which this reality is cogitated, after everything empirical had been abstracted; as is shown in the following table:\n\n                        1\n                      I think,\n\n            2                             3\n        as Subject,              as simple Subject,\n\n                        4\n               as identical Subject,\n           in every state of my thought.\n\nNow, inasmuch as it is not determined in this second proposition, whether I can exist and be cogitated only as subject, and not also as a predicate of another being, the conception of a subject is here taken in a merely logical sense; and it remains undetermined, whether substance is to be cogitated under the conception or not. But in the third proposition, the absolute unity of apperception—the simple Ego in the representation to which all connection and separation, which constitute thought, relate, is of itself important; even although it presents us with no information about the constitution or subsistence of the subject. Apperception is something real, and the simplicity of its nature is given in the very fact of its possibility. Now in space there is nothing real that is at the same time simple; for points, which are the only simple things in space, are merely limits, but not constituent parts of space. From this follows the impossibility of a definition on the basis of materialism of the constitution of my Ego as a merely thinking subject. But, because my existence is considered in the first proposition as given, for it does not mean, “Every thinking being exists” (for this would be predicating of them absolute necessity), but only, “I exist thinking”; the proposition is quite empirical, and contains the determinability of my existence merely in relation to my representations in time. But as I require for this purpose something that is permanent, such as is not given in internal intuition; the mode of my existence, whether as substance or as accident, cannot be determined by means of this simple self-consciousness. Thus, if materialism is inadequate to explain the mode in which I exist, spiritualism is likewise as insufficient; and the conclusion is that we are utterly unable to attain to any knowledge of the constitution of the soul, in so far as relates to the possibility of its existence apart from external objects.\n\nAnd, indeed, how should it be possible, merely by the aid of the unity of consciousness—which we cognize only for the reason that it is indispensable to the possibility of experience—to pass the bounds of experience (our existence in this life); and to extend our cognition to the nature of all thinking beings by means of the empirical—but in relation to every sort of intuition, perfectly undetermined—proposition, “I think”?\n\nThere does not then exist any rational psychology as a doctrine furnishing any addition to our knowledge of ourselves. It is nothing more than a discipline, which sets impassable limits to speculative reason in this region of thought, to prevent it, on the one hand, from throwing itself into the arms of a soulless materialism, and, on the other, from losing itself in the mazes of a baseless spiritualism. It teaches us to consider this refusal of our reason to give any satisfactory answer to questions which reach beyond the limits of this our human life, as a hint to abandon fruitless speculation; and to direct, to a practical use, our knowledge of ourselves—which, although applicable only to objects of experience, receives its principles from a higher source, and regulates its procedure as if our destiny reached far beyond the boundaries of experience and life.\n\nFrom all this it is evident that rational psychology has its origin in a mere misunderstanding. The unity of consciousness, which lies at the basis of the categories, is considered to be an intuition of the subject as an object; and the category of substance is applied to the intuition. But this unity is nothing more than the unity in thought, by which no object is given; to which therefore the category of substance—which always presupposes a given intuition—cannot be applied. Consequently, the subject cannot be cognized. The subject of the categories cannot, therefore, for the very reason that it cogitates these, frame any conception of itself as an object of the categories; for, to cogitate these, it must lay at the foundation its own pure self-consciousness—the very thing that it wishes to explain and describe. In like manner, the subject, in which the representation of time has its basis, cannot determine, for this very reason, its own existence in time. Now, if the latter is impossible, the former, as an attempt to determine itself by means of the categories as a thinking being in general, is no less so.[[47]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-47)\n\n[[47]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-47) The “I think” is, as has been already stated, an empirical proposition, and contains the proposition, “I exist.” But I cannot say, “Everything, which thinks, exists”; for in this case the property of thought would constitute all beings possessing it, necessary beings. Hence my existence cannot be considered as an inference from the proposition, “I think,” as Descartes maintained—because in this case the major premiss, “Everything, which thinks, exists,” must precede—but the two propositions are identical. The proposition, “I think,” expresses an undetermined empirical intuition, that perception (proving consequently that sensation, which must belong to sensibility, lies at the foundation of this proposition); but it precedes experience, whose province it is to determine an object of perception by means of the categories in relation to time; and existence in this proposition is not a category, as it does not apply to an undetermined given object, but only to one of which we have a conception, and about which we wish to know whether it does or does not exist, out of, and apart from this conception. An undetermined perception signifies here merely something real that has been given, only, however, to thought in general—but not as a phenomenon, nor as a thing in itself (noumenon), but only as something that really exists, and is designated as such in the proposition, “I think.” For it must be remarked that, when I call the proposition, “I think,” an empirical proposition, I do not thereby mean that the Ego in the proposition is an empirical representation; on the contrary, it is purely intellectual, because it belongs to thought in general. But without some empirical representation, which presents to the mind material for thought, the mental act, “I think,” would not take place; and the empirical is only the condition of the application or employment of the pure intellectual faculty.\n\nThus, then, appears the vanity of the hope of establishing a cognition which is to extend its rule beyond the limits of experience—a cognition which is one of the highest interests of humanity; and thus is proved the futility of the attempt of speculative philosophy in this region of thought. But, in this interest of thought, the severity of criticism has rendered to reason a not unimportant service, by the demonstration of the impossibility of making any dogmatical affirmation concerning an object of experience beyond the boundaries of experience. She has thus fortified reason against all affirmations of the contrary. Now, this can be accomplished in only two ways. Either our proposition must be proved apodeictically; or, if this is unsuccessful, the sources of this inability must be sought for, and, if these are discovered to exist in the natural and necessary limitation of our reason, our opponents must submit to the same law of renunciation and refrain from advancing claims to dogmatic assertion.\n\nBut the right, say rather the necessity to admit a future life, upon principles of the practical conjoined with the speculative use of reason, has lost nothing by this renunciation; for the merely speculative proof has never had any influence upon the common reason of men. It stands upon the point of a hair, so that even the schools have been able to preserve it from falling only by incessantly discussing it and spinning it like a top; and even in their eyes it has never been able to present any safe foundation for the erection of a theory. The proofs which have been current among men, preserve their value undiminished; nay, rather gain in clearness and unsophisticated power, by the rejection of the dogmatical assumptions of speculative reason. For reason is thus confined within her own peculiar province—the arrangement of ends or aims, which is at the same time the arrangement of nature; and, as a practical faculty, without limiting itself to the latter, it is justified in extending the former, and with it our own existence, beyond the boundaries of experience and life. If we turn our attention to the analogy of the nature of living beings in this world, in the consideration of which reason is obliged to accept as a principle that no organ, no faculty, no appetite is useless, and that nothing is superfluous, nothing disproportionate to its use, nothing unsuited to its end; but that, on the contrary, everything is perfectly conformed to its destination in life—we shall find that man, who alone is the final end and aim of this order, is still the only animal that seems to be excepted from it. For his natural gifts—not merely as regards the talents and motives that may incite him to employ them, but especially the moral law in him—stretch so far beyond all mere earthly utility and advantage, that he feels himself bound to prize the mere consciousness of probity, apart from all advantageous consequences—even the shadowy gift of posthumous fame—above everything; and he is conscious of an inward call to constitute himself, by his conduct in this world—without regard to mere sublunary interests—the citizen of a better. This mighty, irresistible proof—accompanied by an ever-increasing knowledge of the conformability to a purpose in everything we see around us, by the conviction of the boundless immensity of creation, by the consciousness of a certain illimitableness in the possible extension of our knowledge, and by a desire commensurate therewith—remains to humanity, even after the theoretical cognition of ourselves has failed to establish the necessity of an existence after death.\n\nConclusion of the Solution of the Psychological Paralogism.\n\nThe dialectical illusion in rational psychology arises from our confounding an idea of reason (of a pure intelligence) with the conception—in every respect undetermined—of a thinking being in general. I cogitate myself in behalf of a possible experience, at the same time making abstraction of all actual experience; and infer therefrom that I can be conscious of myself apart from experience and its empirical conditions. I consequently confound the possible abstraction of my empirically determined existence with the supposed consciousness of a possible separate existence of my thinking self; and I believe that I cognize what is substantial in myself as a transcendental subject, when I have nothing more in thought than the unity of consciousness, which lies at the basis of all determination of cognition.\n\nThe task of explaining the community of the soul with the body does not properly belong to the psychology of which we are here speaking; because it proposes to prove the personality of the soul apart from this communion (after death), and is therefore transcendent in the proper sense of the word, although occupying itself with an object of experience—only in so far, however, as it ceases to be an object of experience. But a sufficient answer may be found to the question in our system. The difficulty which lies in the execution of this task consists, as is well known, in the presupposed heterogeneity of the object of the internal sense (the soul) and the objects of the external senses; inasmuch as the formal condition of the intuition of the one is time, and of that of the other space also. But if we consider that both kinds of objects do not differ internally, but only in so far as the one appears externally to the other—consequently, that what lies at the basis of phenomena, as a thing in itself, may not be heterogeneous; this difficulty disappears. There then remains no other difficulty than is to be found in the question—how a community of substances is possible; a question which lies out of the region of psychology, and which the reader, after what in our analytic has been said of primitive forces and faculties, will easily judge to be also beyond the region of human cognition.\n\nGENERAL REMARK\n\nOn the Transition from Rational Psychology to Cosmology.\n\nThe proposition, “I think,” or, “I exist thinking,” is an empirical proposition. But such a proposition must be based on empirical intuition, and the object cogitated as a phenomenon; and thus our theory appears to maintain that the soul, even in thought, is merely a phenomenon; and in this way our consciousness itself, in fact, abuts upon nothing.\n\nThought, per se, is merely the purely spontaneous logical function which operates to connect the manifold of a possible intuition; and it does not represent the subject of consciousness as a phenomenon—for this reason alone, that it pays no attention to the question whether the mode of intuiting it is sensuous or intellectual. I therefore do not represent myself in thought either as I am, or as I appear to myself; I merely cogitate myself as an object in general, of the mode of intuiting which I make abstraction. When I represent myself as the subject of thought, or as the ground of thought, these modes of representation are not related to the categories of substance or of cause; for these are functions of thought applicable only to our sensuous intuition. The application of these categories to the Ego would, however, be necessary, if I wished to make myself an object of knowledge. But I wish to be conscious of myself only as thinking; in what mode my Self is given in intuition, I do not consider, and it may be that I, who think, am a phenomenon—although not in so far as I am a thinking being; but in the consciousness of myself in mere thought I am a being, though this consciousness does not present to me any property of this being as material for thought.\n\nBut the proposition, “I think,” in so far as it declares, “I exist thinking,” is not the mere representation of a logical function. It determines the subject (which is in this case an object also) in relation to existence; and it cannot be given without the aid of the internal sense, whose intuition presents to us an object, not as a thing in itself, but always as a phenomenon. In this proposition there is therefore something more to be found than the mere spontaneity of thought; there is also the receptivity of intuition, that is, my thought of myself applied to the empirical intuition of myself. Now, in this intuition the thinking self must seek the conditions of the employment of its logical functions as categories of substance, cause, and so forth; not merely for the purpose of distinguishing itself as an object in itself by means of the representation “I,” but also for the purpose of determining the mode of its existence, that is, of cognizing itself as noumenon. But this is impossible, for the internal empirical intuition is sensuous, and presents us with nothing but phenomenal data, which do not assist the object of pure consciousness in its attempt to cognize itself as a separate existence, but are useful only as contributions to experience.\n\nBut, let it be granted that we could discover, not in experience, but in certain firmly-established à priori laws of the use of pure reason—laws relating to our existence, authority to consider ourselves as legislating à priori in relation to our own existence and as determining this existence; we should, on this supposition, find ourselves possessed of a spontaneity, by which our actual existence would be determinable, without the aid of the conditions of empirical intuition. We should also become aware that in the consciousness of our existence there was an à priori content, which would serve to determine our own existence—an existence only sensuously determinable—relatively, however, to a certain internal faculty in relation to an intelligible world.\n\nBut this would not give the least help to the attempts of rational psychology. For this wonderful faculty, which the consciousness of the moral law in me reveals, would present me with a principle of the determination of my own existence which is purely intellectual—but by what predicates? By none other than those which are given in sensuous intuition. Thus I should find myself in the same position in rational psychology which I formerly occupied, that is to say, I should find myself still in need of sensuous intuitions, in order to give significance to my conceptions of substance and cause, by means of which alone I can possess a knowledge of myself: but these intuitions can never raise me above the sphere of experience. I should be justified, however, in applying these conceptions, in regard to their practical use, which is always directed to objects of experience—in conformity with their analogical significance when employed theoretically—to freedom and its subject. At the same time, I should understand by them merely the logical functions of subject and predicate, of principle and consequence, in conformity with which all actions are so determined, that they are capable of being explained along with the laws of nature, conformably to the categories of substance and cause, although they originate from a very different principle. We have made these observations for the purpose of guarding against misunderstanding, to which the doctrine of our intuition of self as a phenomenon is exposed. We shall have occasion to perceive their utility in the sequel.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0001","text":"### Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason\n\nThe logical paralogism consists in the falsity of an argument in respect of its form, be the content what it may. But a transcendental paralogism has a transcendental foundation, and concludes falsely, while the form is correct and unexceptionable. In this manner the paralogism has its foundation in the nature of human reason, and is the parent of an unavoidable, though not insoluble, mental illusion.\n\nWe now come to a conception which was not inserted in the general list of transcendental conceptions, and yet must be reckoned with them, but at the same time without in the least altering, or indicating a deficiency in that table. This is the conception, or, if the term is preferred, the judgement, “I think.” But it is readily perceived that this thought is as it were the vehicle of all conceptions in general, and consequently of transcendental conceptions also, and that it is therefore regarded as a transcendental conception, although it can have no peculiar claim to be so ranked, inasmuch as its only use is to indicate that all thought is accompanied by consciousness. At the same time, pure as this conception is from empirical content (impressions of the senses), it enables us to distinguish two different kinds of objects. “I,” as thinking, am an object of the internal sense, and am called soul. That which is an object of the external senses is called body. Thus the expression, “I,” as a thinking being, designates the object-matter of psychology, which may be called “the rational doctrine of the soul,” inasmuch as in this science I desire to know nothing of the soul but what, independently of all experience (which determines me in concreto), may be concluded from this conception “I,” in so far as it appears in all thought.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"59.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0002","text":"Now, the rational doctrine of the soul is really an undertaking of this kind. For if the smallest empirical element of thought, if any particular perception of my internal state, were to be introduced among the grounds of cognition of this science, it would not be a rational, but an empirical doctrine of the soul. We have thus before us a pretended science, raised upon the single proposition, “I think,” whose foundation or want of foundation we may very properly, and agreeably with the nature of a transcendental philosophy, here examine. It ought not to be objected that in this proposition, which expresses the perception of one’s self, an internal experience is asserted, and that consequently the rational doctrine of the soul which is founded upon it, is not pure, but partly founded upon an empirical principle. For this internal perception is nothing more than the mere apperception, “I think,” which in fact renders all transcendental conceptions possible, in which we say, “I think substance, cause, etc.” For internal experience in general and its possibility, or perception in general, and its relation to other perceptions, unless some particular distinction or determination thereof is empirically given, cannot be regarded as empirical cognition, but as cognition of the empirical, and belongs to the investigation of the possibility of every experience, which is certainly transcendental. The smallest object of experience (for example, only pleasure or pain), that should be included in the general representation of self-consciousness, would immediately change the rational into an empirical psychology.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"59.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0003","text":"“I think” is therefore the only text of rational psychology, from which it must develop its whole system. It is manifest that this thought, when applied to an object (myself), can contain nothing but transcendental predicates thereof; because the least empirical predicate would destroy the purity of the science and its independence of all experience.\n\nBut we shall have to follow here the guidance of the categories—only, as in the present case a thing, “I,” as thinking being, is at first given, we shall—not indeed change the order of the categories as it stands in the table—but begin at the category of substance, by which at the a thing in itself is represented and proceeds backwards through the series. The topic of the rational doctrine of the soul, from which everything else it may contain must be deduced, is accordingly as follows:\n\n            1                          2\n  The Soul is SUBSTANCE       As regards its quality\n                                it is SIMPLE\n\n                      3\n          As regards the different\n          times in which it exists,\n          it is numerically identical,\n          that is UNITY, not Plurality.\n\n                       4\n  It is in relation to possible objects in space[[43]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-43)","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"59.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0004","text":"[[43]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-43) The reader, who may not so easily perceive the psychological sense of these expressions, taken here in their transcendental abstraction, and cannot guess why the latter attribute of the soul belongs to the category of existence, will find the expressions sufficiently explained and justified in the sequel. I have, moreover, to apologize for the Latin terms which have been employed, instead of their German synonyms, contrary to the rules of correct writing. But I judged it better to sacrifice elegance to perspicuity.\n\nFrom these elements originate all the conceptions of pure psychology, by combination alone, without the aid of any other principle. This substance, merely as an object of the internal sense, gives the conception of Immateriality; as simple substance, that of Incorruptibility; its identity, as intellectual substance, gives the conception of Personality; all these three together, Spirituality. Its relation to objects in space gives us the conception of connection (commercium) with bodies. Thus it represents thinking substance as the principle of life in matter, that is, as a soul (anima), and as the ground of Animality; and this, limited and determined by the conception of spirituality, gives us that of Immortality.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"59.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0005","text":"Now to these conceptions relate four paralogisms of a transcendental psychology, which is falsely held to be a science of pure reason, touching the nature of our thinking being. We can, however, lay at the foundation of this science nothing but the simple and in itself perfectly contentless representation “i” which cannot even be called a conception, but merely a consciousness which accompanies all conceptions. By this “I,” or “He,” or “It,” who or which thinks, nothing more is represented than a transcendental subject of thought = x, which is cognized only by means of the thoughts that are its predicates, and of which, apart from these, we cannot form the least conception. Hence in a perpetual circle, inasmuch as we must always employ it, in order to frame any judgement respecting it. And this inconvenience we find it impossible to rid ourselves of, because consciousness in itself is not so much a representation distinguishing a particular object, as a form of representation in general, in so far as it may be termed cognition; for in and by cognition alone do I think anything.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"59.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0006","text":"It must, however, appear extraordinary at first sight that the condition under which I think, and which is consequently a property of my subject, should be held to be likewise valid for every existence which thinks, and that we can presume to base upon a seemingly empirical proposition a judgement which is apodeictic and universal, to wit, that everything which thinks is constituted as the voice of my consciousness declares it to be, that is, as a self-conscious being. The cause of this belief is to be found in the fact that we necessarily attribute to things à priori all the properties which constitute conditions under which alone we can cogitate them. Now I cannot obtain the least representation of a thinking being by means of external experience, but solely through self-consciousness. Such objects are consequently nothing more than the transference of this consciousness of mine to other things which can only thus be represented as thinking beings. The proposition, “I think,” is, in the present case, understood in a problematical sense, not in so far as it contains a perception of an existence (like the Cartesian “Cogito, ergo sum”), but in regard to its mere possibility—for the purpose of discovering what properties may be inferred from so simple a proposition and predicated of the subject of it.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"59.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0007","text":"If at the foundation of our pure rational cognition of thinking beings there lay more than the mere Cogito—if we could likewise call in aid observations on the play of our thoughts, and the thence derived natural laws of the thinking self, there would arise an empirical psychology which would be a kind of physiology of the internal sense and might possibly be capable of explaining the phenomena of that sense. But it could never be available for discovering those properties which do not belong to possible experience (such as the quality of simplicity), nor could it make any apodeictic enunciation on the nature of thinking beings: it would therefore not be a rational psychology.\n\nNow, as the proposition “I think” (in the problematical sense) contains the form of every judgement in general and is the constant accompaniment of all the categories, it is manifest that conclusions are drawn from it only by a transcendental employment of the understanding. This use of the understanding excludes all empirical elements; and we cannot, as has been shown above, have any favourable conception beforehand of its procedure. We shall therefore follow with a critical eye this proposition through all the predicaments of pure psychology; but we shall, for brevity’s sake, allow this examination to proceed in an uninterrupted connection.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"59.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","text":"Before entering on this task, however, the following general remark may help to quicken our attention to this mode of argument. It is not merely through my thinking that I cognize an object, but only through my determining a given intuition in relation to the unity of consciousness in which all thinking consists. It follows that I cognize myself, not through my being conscious of myself as thinking, but only when I am conscious of the intuition of myself as determined in relation to the function of thought. All the modi of self-consciousness in thought are hence not conceptions of objects (conceptions of the understanding—categories); they are mere logical functions, which do not present to thought an object to be cognized, and cannot therefore present my Self as an object. Not the consciousness of the determining, but only that of the determinable self, that is, of my internal intuition (in so far as the manifold contained in it can be connected conformably with the general condition of the unity of apperception in thought), is the object.\n\n1. In all judgements I am the determining subject of that relation which constitutes a judgement. But that the I which thinks, must be considered as in thought always a subject, and as a thing which cannot be a predicate to thought, is an apodeictic and identical proposition. But this proposition does not signify that I, as an object, am, for myself, a self-subsistent being or substance. This latter statement—an ambitious one—requires to be supported by data which are not to be discovered in thought; and are perhaps (in so far as I consider the thinking self merely as such) not to be discovered in the thinking self at all.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"59.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0009","text":"2. That the I or Ego of apperception, and consequently in all thought, is singular or simple, and cannot be resolved into a plurality of subjects, and therefore indicates a logically simple subject—this is self-evident from the very conception of an Ego, and is consequently an analytical proposition. But this is not tantamount to declaring that the thinking Ego is a simple substance—for this would be a synthetical proposition. The conception of substance always relates to intuitions, which with me cannot be other than sensuous, and which consequently lie completely out of the sphere of the understanding and its thought: but to this sphere belongs the affirmation that the Ego is simple in thought. It would indeed be surprising, if the conception of “substance,” which in other cases requires so much labour to distinguish from the other elements presented by intuition—so much trouble, too, to discover whether it can be simple (as in the case of the parts of matter)—should be presented immediately to me, as if by revelation, in the poorest mental representation of all.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"59.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","text":"3. The proposition of the identity of my Self amidst all the manifold representations of which I am conscious, is likewise a proposition lying in the conceptions themselves, and is consequently analytical. But this identity of the subject, of which I am conscious in all its representations, does not relate to or concern the intuition of the subject, by which it is given as an object. This proposition cannot therefore enounce the identity of the person, by which is understood the consciousness of the identity of its own substance as a thinking being in all change and variation of circumstances. To prove this, we should require not a mere analysis of the proposition, but synthetical judgements based upon a given intuition.\n\n4. I distinguish my own existence, as that of a thinking being, from that of other things external to me—among which my body also is reckoned. This is also an analytical proposition, for other things are exactly those which I think as different or distinguished from myself. But whether this consciousness of myself is possible without things external to me; and whether therefore I can exist merely as a thinking being (without being man)—cannot be known or inferred from this proposition.\n\nThus we have gained nothing as regards the cognition of myself as object, by the analysis of the consciousness of my Self in thought. The logical exposition of thought in general is mistaken for a metaphysical determination of the object.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"59.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0011","text":"Our Critique would be an investigation utterly superfluous, if there existed a possibility of proving à priori, that all thinking beings are in themselves simple substances, as such, therefore, possess the inseparable attribute of personality, and are conscious of their existence apart from and unconnected with matter. For we should thus have taken a step beyond the world of sense, and have penetrated into the sphere of noumena; and in this case the right could not be denied us of extending our knowledge in this sphere, of establishing ourselves, and, under a favouring star, appropriating to ourselves possessions in it. For the proposition: “Every thinking being, as such, is simple substance,” is an à priori synthetical proposition; because in the first place it goes beyond the conception which is the subject of it, and adds to the mere notion of a thinking being the mode of its existence, and in the second place annexes a predicate (that of simplicity) to the latter conception—a predicate which it could not have discovered in the sphere of experience. It would follow that à priori synthetical propositions are possible and legitimate, not only, as we have maintained, in relation to objects of possible experience, and as principles of the possibility of this experience itself, but are applicable to things in themselves—an inference which makes an end of the whole of this Critique, and obliges us to fall back on the old mode of metaphysical procedure. But indeed the danger is not so great, if we look a little closer into the question.\n\nThere lurks in the procedure of rational Psychology a paralogism, which is represented in the following syllogism:","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"59.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0012","text":"That which cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subject, does not exist otherwise than as subject, and is therefore substance.\n\nA thinking being, considered merely as such, cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subject.\n\nTherefore it exists also as such, that is, as substance.\n\nIn the major we speak of a being that can be cogitated generally and in every relation, consequently as it may be given in intuition. But in the minor we speak of the same being only in so far as it regards itself as subject, relatively to thought and the unity of consciousness, but not in relation to intuition, by which it is presented as an object to thought. Thus the conclusion is here arrived at by a Sophisma figurae dictionis.[[44]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-44)\n\n[[44]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-44) Thought is taken in the two premisses in two totally different senses. In the major it is considered as relating and applying to objects in general, consequently to objects of intuition also. In the minor, we understand it as relating merely to self-consciousness. In this sense, we do not cogitate an object, but merely the relation to the self-consciousness of the subject, as the form of thought. In the former premiss we speak of things which cannot be cogitated otherwise than as subjects. In the second, we do not speak of things, but of thought (all objects being abstracted), in which the Ego is always the subject of consciousness. Hence the conclusion cannot be, “I cannot exist otherwise than as subject”; but only “I can, in cogitating my existence, employ my Ego only as the subject of the judgement.” But this is an identical proposition, and throws no light on the mode of my existence.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"59.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","text":"That this famous argument is a mere paralogism, will be plain to any one who will consider the general remark which precedes our exposition of the principles of the pure understanding, and the section on noumena. For it was there proved that the conception of a thing, which can exist per se—only as a subject and never as a predicate, possesses no objective reality; that is to say, we can never know whether there exists any object to correspond to the conception; consequently, the conception is nothing more than a conception, and from it we derive no proper knowledge. If this conception is to indicate by the term substance, an object that can be given, if it is to become a cognition, we must have at the foundation of the cognition a permanent intuition, as the indispensable condition of its objective reality. For through intuition alone can an object be given. But in internal intuition there is nothing permanent, for the Ego is but the consciousness of my thought. If then, we appeal merely to thought, we cannot discover the necessary condition of the application of the conception of substance—that is, of a subject existing per se—to the subject as a thinking being. And thus the conception of the simple nature of substance, which is connected with the objective reality of this conception, is shown to be also invalid, and to be, in fact, nothing more than the logical qualitative unity of self-consciousness in thought; whilst we remain perfectly ignorant whether the subject is composite or not.\n\nRefutation of the Argument of Mendelssohn for the Substantiality or Permanence of the Soul.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"59.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0014","text":"This acute philosopher easily perceived the insufficiency of the common argument which attempts to prove that the soul—it being granted that it is a simple being—cannot perish by dissolution or decomposition; he saw it is not impossible for it to cease to be by extinction, or disappearance. He endeavoured to prove in his Phaedo, that the soul cannot be annihilated, by showing that a simple being cannot cease to exist. Inasmuch as, he said, a simple existence cannot diminish, nor gradually lose portions of its being, and thus be by degrees reduced to nothing (for it possesses no parts, and therefore no multiplicity), between the moment in which it is, and the moment in which it is not, no time can be discovered—which is impossible. But this philosopher did not consider that, granting the soul to possess this simple nature, which contains no parts external to each other and consequently no extensive quantity, we cannot refuse to it any less than to any other being, intensive quantity, that is, a degree of reality in regard to all its faculties, nay, to all that constitutes its existence. But this degree of reality can become less and less through an infinite series of smaller degrees. It follows, therefore, that this supposed substance—this thing, the permanence of which is not assured in any other way, may, if not by decomposition, by gradual loss (remissio) of its powers (consequently by elanguescence, if I may employ this expression), be changed into nothing. For consciousness itself has always a degree, which may be lessened.[[45]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-45)Consequently the faculty of being conscious may be diminished; and so with all other faculties. The permanence of the soul, therefore, as an object of the internal sense, remains undemonstrated, nay, even indemonstrable. Its permanence in life is evident, per se, inasmuch as the thinking being (as man) is to itself, at the same time, an object of the external senses. But this does not authorize the rational psychologist to affirm, from mere conceptions, its permanence beyond life.[[46]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-46)","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"59.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0015","text":"[[45]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-45) Clearness is not, as logicians maintain, the consciousness of a representation. For a certain degree of consciousness, which may not, however, be sufficient for recollection, is to be met with in many dim representations. For without any consciousness at all, we should not be able to recognize any difference in the obscure representations we connect; as we really can do with many conceptions, such as those of right and justice, and those of the musician, who strikes at once several notes in improvising a piece of music. But a representation is clear, in which our consciousness is sufficient for the consciousness of the difference of this representation from others. If we are only conscious that there is a difference, but are not conscious of the difference—that is, what the difference is—the representation must be termed obscure. There is, consequently, an infinite series of degrees of consciousness down to its entire disappearance.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"59.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0016","text":"[[46]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-46) There are some who think they have done enough to establish a new possibility in the mode of the existence of souls, when they have shown that there is no contradiction in their hypotheses on this subject. Such are those who affirm the possibility of thought—of which they have no other knowledge than what they derive from its use in connecting empirical intuitions presented in this our human life—after this life has ceased. But it is very easy to embarrass them by the introduction of counter-possibilities, which rest upon quite as good a foundation. Such, for example, is the possibility of the division of a simple substance into several substances; and conversely, of the coalition of several into one simple substance. For, although divisibility presupposes composition, it does not necessarily require a composition of substances, but only of the degrees (of the several faculties) of one and the same substance. Now we can cogitate all the powers and faculties of the soul—even that of consciousness—as diminished by one half, the substance still remaining. In the same way we can represent to ourselves without contradiction, this obliterated half as preserved, not in the soul, but without it; and we can believe that, as in this case every thing that is real in the soul, and has a degree—consequently its entire existence—has been halved, a particular substance would arise out of the soul. For the multiplicity, which has been divided, formerly existed, but not as a multiplicity of substances, but of every reality as the quantum of existence in it; and the unity of substance was merely a mode of existence, which by this division alone has been transformed into a plurality of subsistence. In the same manner several simple substances might coalesce into one, without anything being lost except the plurality of subsistence, inasmuch as the one substance would contain the degree of reality of all the former substances. Perhaps, indeed, the simple substances, which appear under the form of matter, might (not indeed by a mechanical or chemical influence upon each other, but by an unknown influence, of which the former would be but the phenomenal appearance), by means of such a dynamical division of the parent-souls, as intensive quantities, produce other souls, while the former repaired the loss thus sustained with new matter of the same sort. I am far from allowing any value to such chimeras; and the principles of our analytic have clearly proved that no other than an empirical use of the categories—that of substance, for example—is possible. But if the rationalist is bold enough to construct, on the mere authority of the faculty of thought—without any intuition, whereby an object is given—a self-subsistent being, merely because the unity of apperception in thought cannot allow him to believe it a composite being, instead of declaring, as he ought to do, that he is unable to explain the possibility of a thinking nature; what ought to hinder the materialist, with as complete an independence of experience, to employ the principle of the rationalist in a directly opposite manner—still preserving the formal unity required by his opponent?","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"59.16"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0017","text":"If, now, we take the above propositions—as they must be accepted as valid for all thinking beings in the system of rational psychology—in synthetical connection, and proceed, from the category of relation, with the proposition: “All thinking beings are, as such, substances,” backwards through the series, till the circle is completed; we come at last to their existence, of which, in this system of rational psychology, substances are held to be conscious, independently of external things; nay, it is asserted that, in relation to the permanence which is a necessary characteristic of substance, they can of themselves determine external things. It follows that idealism—at least problematical idealism, is perfectly unavoidable in this rationalistic system. And, if the existence of outward things is not held to be requisite to the determination of the existence of a substance in time, the existence of these outward things at all, is a gratuitous assumption which remains without the possibility of a proof.\n\nBut if we proceed analytically—the “I think” as a proposition containing in itself an existence as given, consequently modality being the principle—and dissect this proposition, in order to ascertain its content, and discover whether and how this Ego determines its existence in time and space without the aid of anything external; the propositions of rationalistic psychology would not begin with the conception of a thinking being, but with a reality, and the properties of a thinking being in general would be deduced from the mode in which this reality is cogitated, after everything empirical had been abstracted; as is shown in the following table:\n\n                        1\n                      I think,\n\n            2                             3\n        as Subject,              as simple Subject,","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"59.17"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0018","text":"4\n               as identical Subject,\n           in every state of my thought.\n\nNow, inasmuch as it is not determined in this second proposition, whether I can exist and be cogitated only as subject, and not also as a predicate of another being, the conception of a subject is here taken in a merely logical sense; and it remains undetermined, whether substance is to be cogitated under the conception or not. But in the third proposition, the absolute unity of apperception—the simple Ego in the representation to which all connection and separation, which constitute thought, relate, is of itself important; even although it presents us with no information about the constitution or subsistence of the subject. Apperception is something real, and the simplicity of its nature is given in the very fact of its possibility. Now in space there is nothing real that is at the same time simple; for points, which are the only simple things in space, are merely limits, but not constituent parts of space. From this follows the impossibility of a definition on the basis of materialism of the constitution of my Ego as a merely thinking subject. But, because my existence is considered in the first proposition as given, for it does not mean, “Every thinking being exists” (for this would be predicating of them absolute necessity), but only, “I exist thinking”; the proposition is quite empirical, and contains the determinability of my existence merely in relation to my representations in time. But as I require for this purpose something that is permanent, such as is not given in internal intuition; the mode of my existence, whether as substance or as accident, cannot be determined by means of this simple self-consciousness. Thus, if materialism is inadequate to explain the mode in which I exist, spiritualism is likewise as insufficient; and the conclusion is that we are utterly unable to attain to any knowledge of the constitution of the soul, in so far as relates to the possibility of its existence apart from external objects.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"59.18"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","text":"And, indeed, how should it be possible, merely by the aid of the unity of consciousness—which we cognize only for the reason that it is indispensable to the possibility of experience—to pass the bounds of experience (our existence in this life); and to extend our cognition to the nature of all thinking beings by means of the empirical—but in relation to every sort of intuition, perfectly undetermined—proposition, “I think”?\n\nThere does not then exist any rational psychology as a doctrine furnishing any addition to our knowledge of ourselves. It is nothing more than a discipline, which sets impassable limits to speculative reason in this region of thought, to prevent it, on the one hand, from throwing itself into the arms of a soulless materialism, and, on the other, from losing itself in the mazes of a baseless spiritualism. It teaches us to consider this refusal of our reason to give any satisfactory answer to questions which reach beyond the limits of this our human life, as a hint to abandon fruitless speculation; and to direct, to a practical use, our knowledge of ourselves—which, although applicable only to objects of experience, receives its principles from a higher source, and regulates its procedure as if our destiny reached far beyond the boundaries of experience and life.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"59.19"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0020","text":"From all this it is evident that rational psychology has its origin in a mere misunderstanding. The unity of consciousness, which lies at the basis of the categories, is considered to be an intuition of the subject as an object; and the category of substance is applied to the intuition. But this unity is nothing more than the unity in thought, by which no object is given; to which therefore the category of substance—which always presupposes a given intuition—cannot be applied. Consequently, the subject cannot be cognized. The subject of the categories cannot, therefore, for the very reason that it cogitates these, frame any conception of itself as an object of the categories; for, to cogitate these, it must lay at the foundation its own pure self-consciousness—the very thing that it wishes to explain and describe. In like manner, the subject, in which the representation of time has its basis, cannot determine, for this very reason, its own existence in time. Now, if the latter is impossible, the former, as an attempt to determine itself by means of the categories as a thinking being in general, is no less so.[[47]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-47)","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"59.20"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0021","text":"[[47]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-47) The “I think” is, as has been already stated, an empirical proposition, and contains the proposition, “I exist.” But I cannot say, “Everything, which thinks, exists”; for in this case the property of thought would constitute all beings possessing it, necessary beings. Hence my existence cannot be considered as an inference from the proposition, “I think,” as Descartes maintained—because in this case the major premiss, “Everything, which thinks, exists,” must precede—but the two propositions are identical. The proposition, “I think,” expresses an undetermined empirical intuition, that perception (proving consequently that sensation, which must belong to sensibility, lies at the foundation of this proposition); but it precedes experience, whose province it is to determine an object of perception by means of the categories in relation to time; and existence in this proposition is not a category, as it does not apply to an undetermined given object, but only to one of which we have a conception, and about which we wish to know whether it does or does not exist, out of, and apart from this conception. An undetermined perception signifies here merely something real that has been given, only, however, to thought in general—but not as a phenomenon, nor as a thing in itself (noumenon), but only as something that really exists, and is designated as such in the proposition, “I think.” For it must be remarked that, when I call the proposition, “I think,” an empirical proposition, I do not thereby mean that the Ego in the proposition is an empirical representation; on the contrary, it is purely intellectual, because it belongs to thought in general. But without some empirical representation, which presents to the mind material for thought, the mental act, “I think,” would not take place; and the empirical is only the condition of the application or employment of the pure intellectual faculty.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"59.21"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0022","text":"Thus, then, appears the vanity of the hope of establishing a cognition which is to extend its rule beyond the limits of experience—a cognition which is one of the highest interests of humanity; and thus is proved the futility of the attempt of speculative philosophy in this region of thought. But, in this interest of thought, the severity of criticism has rendered to reason a not unimportant service, by the demonstration of the impossibility of making any dogmatical affirmation concerning an object of experience beyond the boundaries of experience. She has thus fortified reason against all affirmations of the contrary. Now, this can be accomplished in only two ways. Either our proposition must be proved apodeictically; or, if this is unsuccessful, the sources of this inability must be sought for, and, if these are discovered to exist in the natural and necessary limitation of our reason, our opponents must submit to the same law of renunciation and refrain from advancing claims to dogmatic assertion.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"59.22"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0023","text":"But the right, say rather the necessity to admit a future life, upon principles of the practical conjoined with the speculative use of reason, has lost nothing by this renunciation; for the merely speculative proof has never had any influence upon the common reason of men. It stands upon the point of a hair, so that even the schools have been able to preserve it from falling only by incessantly discussing it and spinning it like a top; and even in their eyes it has never been able to present any safe foundation for the erection of a theory. The proofs which have been current among men, preserve their value undiminished; nay, rather gain in clearness and unsophisticated power, by the rejection of the dogmatical assumptions of speculative reason. For reason is thus confined within her own peculiar province—the arrangement of ends or aims, which is at the same time the arrangement of nature; and, as a practical faculty, without limiting itself to the latter, it is justified in extending the former, and with it our own existence, beyond the boundaries of experience and life. If we turn our attention to the analogy of the nature of living beings in this world, in the consideration of which reason is obliged to accept as a principle that no organ, no faculty, no appetite is useless, and that nothing is superfluous, nothing disproportionate to its use, nothing unsuited to its end; but that, on the contrary, everything is perfectly conformed to its destination in life—we shall find that man, who alone is the final end and aim of this order, is still the only animal that seems to be excepted from it. For his natural gifts—not merely as regards the talents and motives that may incite him to employ them, but especially the moral law in him—stretch so far beyond all mere earthly utility and advantage, that he feels himself bound to prize the mere consciousness of probity, apart from all advantageous consequences—even the shadowy gift of posthumous fame—above everything; and he is conscious of an inward call to constitute himself, by his conduct in this world—without regard to mere sublunary interests—the citizen of a better. This mighty, irresistible proof—accompanied by an ever-increasing knowledge of the conformability to a purpose in everything we see around us, by the conviction of the boundless immensity of creation, by the consciousness of a certain illimitableness in the possible extension of our knowledge, and by a desire commensurate therewith—remains to humanity, even after the theoretical cognition of ourselves has failed to establish the necessity of an existence after death.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"59.23"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0024","text":"Conclusion of the Solution of the Psychological Paralogism.\n\nThe dialectical illusion in rational psychology arises from our confounding an idea of reason (of a pure intelligence) with the conception—in every respect undetermined—of a thinking being in general. I cogitate myself in behalf of a possible experience, at the same time making abstraction of all actual experience; and infer therefrom that I can be conscious of myself apart from experience and its empirical conditions. I consequently confound the possible abstraction of my empirically determined existence with the supposed consciousness of a possible separate existence of my thinking self; and I believe that I cognize what is substantial in myself as a transcendental subject, when I have nothing more in thought than the unity of consciousness, which lies at the basis of all determination of cognition.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"59.24"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0025","text":"The task of explaining the community of the soul with the body does not properly belong to the psychology of which we are here speaking; because it proposes to prove the personality of the soul apart from this communion (after death), and is therefore transcendent in the proper sense of the word, although occupying itself with an object of experience—only in so far, however, as it ceases to be an object of experience. But a sufficient answer may be found to the question in our system. The difficulty which lies in the execution of this task consists, as is well known, in the presupposed heterogeneity of the object of the internal sense (the soul) and the objects of the external senses; inasmuch as the formal condition of the intuition of the one is time, and of that of the other space also. But if we consider that both kinds of objects do not differ internally, but only in so far as the one appears externally to the other—consequently, that what lies at the basis of phenomena, as a thing in itself, may not be heterogeneous; this difficulty disappears. There then remains no other difficulty than is to be found in the question—how a community of substances is possible; a question which lies out of the region of psychology, and which the reader, after what in our analytic has been said of primitive forces and faculties, will easily judge to be also beyond the region of human cognition.\n\nGENERAL REMARK\n\nOn the Transition from Rational Psychology to Cosmology.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"59.25"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0026","text":"The proposition, “I think,” or, “I exist thinking,” is an empirical proposition. But such a proposition must be based on empirical intuition, and the object cogitated as a phenomenon; and thus our theory appears to maintain that the soul, even in thought, is merely a phenomenon; and in this way our consciousness itself, in fact, abuts upon nothing.\n\nThought, per se, is merely the purely spontaneous logical function which operates to connect the manifold of a possible intuition; and it does not represent the subject of consciousness as a phenomenon—for this reason alone, that it pays no attention to the question whether the mode of intuiting it is sensuous or intellectual. I therefore do not represent myself in thought either as I am, or as I appear to myself; I merely cogitate myself as an object in general, of the mode of intuiting which I make abstraction. When I represent myself as the subject of thought, or as the ground of thought, these modes of representation are not related to the categories of substance or of cause; for these are functions of thought applicable only to our sensuous intuition. The application of these categories to the Ego would, however, be necessary, if I wished to make myself an object of knowledge. But I wish to be conscious of myself only as thinking; in what mode my Self is given in intuition, I do not consider, and it may be that I, who think, am a phenomenon—although not in so far as I am a thinking being; but in the consciousness of myself in mere thought I am a being, though this consciousness does not present to me any property of this being as material for thought.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"59.26"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0027","text":"But the proposition, “I think,” in so far as it declares, “I exist thinking,” is not the mere representation of a logical function. It determines the subject (which is in this case an object also) in relation to existence; and it cannot be given without the aid of the internal sense, whose intuition presents to us an object, not as a thing in itself, but always as a phenomenon. In this proposition there is therefore something more to be found than the mere spontaneity of thought; there is also the receptivity of intuition, that is, my thought of myself applied to the empirical intuition of myself. Now, in this intuition the thinking self must seek the conditions of the employment of its logical functions as categories of substance, cause, and so forth; not merely for the purpose of distinguishing itself as an object in itself by means of the representation “I,” but also for the purpose of determining the mode of its existence, that is, of cognizing itself as noumenon. But this is impossible, for the internal empirical intuition is sensuous, and presents us with nothing but phenomenal data, which do not assist the object of pure consciousness in its attempt to cognize itself as a separate existence, but are useful only as contributions to experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"59.27"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0028","text":"But, let it be granted that we could discover, not in experience, but in certain firmly-established à priori laws of the use of pure reason—laws relating to our existence, authority to consider ourselves as legislating à priori in relation to our own existence and as determining this existence; we should, on this supposition, find ourselves possessed of a spontaneity, by which our actual existence would be determinable, without the aid of the conditions of empirical intuition. We should also become aware that in the consciousness of our existence there was an à priori content, which would serve to determine our own existence—an existence only sensuously determinable—relatively, however, to a certain internal faculty in relation to an intelligible world.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"59.28"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0029","text":"But this would not give the least help to the attempts of rational psychology. For this wonderful faculty, which the consciousness of the moral law in me reveals, would present me with a principle of the determination of my own existence which is purely intellectual—but by what predicates? By none other than those which are given in sensuous intuition. Thus I should find myself in the same position in rational psychology which I formerly occupied, that is to say, I should find myself still in need of sensuous intuitions, in order to give significance to my conceptions of substance and cause, by means of which alone I can possess a knowledge of myself: but these intuitions can never raise me above the sphere of experience. I should be justified, however, in applying these conceptions, in regard to their practical use, which is always directed to objects of experience—in conformity with their analogical significance when employed theoretically—to freedom and its subject. At the same time, I should understand by them merely the logical functions of subject and predicate, of principle and consequence, in conformity with which all actions are so determined, that they are capable of being explained along with the laws of nature, conformably to the categories of substance and cause, although they originate from a very different principle. We have made these observations for the purpose of guarding against misunderstanding, to which the doctrine of our intuition of self as a phenomenon is exposed. We shall have occasion to perceive their utility in the sequel.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"59.29"}],"sha256":"fb3f722c22cea010a92e2b94749640eba4d262626eae0848adb8c4bd375e335c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP","title":"Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason","order":60,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_CIAP","text":"### Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason\n\nWe showed in the introduction to this part of our work, that all transcendental illusion of pure reason arose from dialectical arguments, the schema of which logic gives us in its three formal species of syllogisms—just as the categories find their logical schema in the four functions of all judgements. The first kind of these sophistical arguments related to the unconditioned unity of the subjective conditions of all representations in general (of the subject or soul), in correspondence with the categorical syllogisms, the major of which, as the principle, enounces the relation of a predicate to a subject. The second kind of dialectical argument will therefore be concerned, following the analogy with hypothetical syllogisms, with the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions in the phenomenon; and, in this way, the theme of the third kind to be treated of in the following chapter will be the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions of the possibility of objects in general.\n\nBut it is worthy of remark that the transcendental paralogism produced in the mind only a one-third illusion, in regard to the idea of the subject of our thought; and the conceptions of reason gave no ground to maintain the contrary proposition. The advantage is completely on the side of Pneumatism; although this theory itself passes into naught, in the crucible of pure reason.\n\nVery different is the case when we apply reason to the objective synthesis of phenomena. Here, certainly, reason establishes, with much plausibility, its principle of unconditioned unity; but it very soon falls into such contradictions that it is compelled, in relation to cosmology, to renounce its pretensions.\n\nFor here a new phenomenon of human reason meets us—a perfectly natural antithetic, which does not require to be sought for by subtle sophistry, but into which reason of itself unavoidably falls. It is thereby preserved, to be sure, from the slumber of a fancied conviction—which a merely one-sided illusion produces; but it is at the same time compelled, either, on the one hand, to abandon itself to a despairing scepticism, or, on the other, to assume a dogmatical confidence and obstinate persistence in certain assertions, without granting a fair hearing to the other side of the question. Either is the death of a sound philosophy, although the former might perhaps deserve the title of the euthanasia of pure reason.\n\nBefore entering this region of discord and confusion, which the conflict of the laws of pure reason (antinomy) produces, we shall present the reader with some considerations, in explanation and justification of the method we intend to follow in our treatment of this subject. I term all transcendental ideas, in so far as they relate to the absolute totality in the synthesis of phenomena, cosmical conceptions; partly on account of this unconditioned totality, on which the conception of the world-whole is based—a conception, which is itself an idea—partly because they relate solely to the synthesis of phenomena—the empirical synthesis; while, on the other hand, the absolute totality in the synthesis of the conditions of all possible things gives rise to an ideal of pure reason, which is quite distinct from the cosmical conception, although it stands in relation with it. Hence, as the paralogisms of pure reason laid the foundation for a dialectical psychology, the antinomy of pure reason will present us with the transcendental principles of a pretended pure (rational) cosmology—not, however, to declare it valid and to appropriate it, but—as the very term of a conflict of reason sufficiently indicates, to present it as an idea which cannot be reconciled with phenomena and experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","text":"### Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason\n\nWe showed in the introduction to this part of our work, that all transcendental illusion of pure reason arose from dialectical arguments, the schema of which logic gives us in its three formal species of syllogisms—just as the categories find their logical schema in the four functions of all judgements. The first kind of these sophistical arguments related to the unconditioned unity of the subjective conditions of all representations in general (of the subject or soul), in correspondence with the categorical syllogisms, the major of which, as the principle, enounces the relation of a predicate to a subject. The second kind of dialectical argument will therefore be concerned, following the analogy with hypothetical syllogisms, with the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions in the phenomenon; and, in this way, the theme of the third kind to be treated of in the following chapter will be the unconditioned unity of the objective conditions of the possibility of objects in general.\n\nBut it is worthy of remark that the transcendental paralogism produced in the mind only a one-third illusion, in regard to the idea of the subject of our thought; and the conceptions of reason gave no ground to maintain the contrary proposition. The advantage is completely on the side of Pneumatism; although this theory itself passes into naught, in the crucible of pure reason.\n\nVery different is the case when we apply reason to the objective synthesis of phenomena. Here, certainly, reason establishes, with much plausibility, its principle of unconditioned unity; but it very soon falls into such contradictions that it is compelled, in relation to cosmology, to renounce its pretensions.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"60.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","text":"For here a new phenomenon of human reason meets us—a perfectly natural antithetic, which does not require to be sought for by subtle sophistry, but into which reason of itself unavoidably falls. It is thereby preserved, to be sure, from the slumber of a fancied conviction—which a merely one-sided illusion produces; but it is at the same time compelled, either, on the one hand, to abandon itself to a despairing scepticism, or, on the other, to assume a dogmatical confidence and obstinate persistence in certain assertions, without granting a fair hearing to the other side of the question. Either is the death of a sound philosophy, although the former might perhaps deserve the title of the euthanasia of pure reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"60.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","text":"Before entering this region of discord and confusion, which the conflict of the laws of pure reason (antinomy) produces, we shall present the reader with some considerations, in explanation and justification of the method we intend to follow in our treatment of this subject. I term all transcendental ideas, in so far as they relate to the absolute totality in the synthesis of phenomena, cosmical conceptions; partly on account of this unconditioned totality, on which the conception of the world-whole is based—a conception, which is itself an idea—partly because they relate solely to the synthesis of phenomena—the empirical synthesis; while, on the other hand, the absolute totality in the synthesis of the conditions of all possible things gives rise to an ideal of pure reason, which is quite distinct from the cosmical conception, although it stands in relation with it. Hence, as the paralogisms of pure reason laid the foundation for a dialectical psychology, the antinomy of pure reason will present us with the transcendental principles of a pretended pure (rational) cosmology—not, however, to declare it valid and to appropriate it, but—as the very term of a conflict of reason sufficiently indicates, to present it as an idea which cannot be reconciled with phenomena and experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"60.3"}],"sha256":"55bb09483c71f653ac54244fa07f7138f41cc16799f3d88b608f6b1ee21fbb26","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC","title":"Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas","order":61,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SISC","text":"# Cosmos\n\n### Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas\n\nThat we may be able to enumerate with systematic precision these ideas according to a principle, we must remark, in the first place, that it is from the understanding alone that pure and transcendental conceptions take their origin; that the reason does not properly give birth to any conception, but only frees the conception of the understanding from the unavoidable limitation of a possible experience, and thus endeavours to raise it above the empirical, though it must still be in connection with it. This happens from the fact that, for a given conditioned, reason demands absolute totality on the side of the conditions (to which the understanding submits all phenomena), and thus makes of the category a transcendental idea. This it does that it may be able to give absolute completeness to the empirical synthesis, by continuing it to the unconditioned (which is not to be found in experience, but only in the idea). Reason requires this according to the principle: If the conditioned is given the whole of the conditions, and consequently the absolutely unconditioned, is also given, whereby alone the former was possible. First, then, the transcendental ideas are properly nothing but categories elevated to the unconditioned; and they may be arranged in a table according to the titles of the latter. But, secondly, all the categories are not available for this purpose, but only those in which the synthesis constitutes a series—of conditions subordinated to, not co-ordinated with, each other. Absolute totality is required of reason only in so far as concerns the ascending series of the conditions of a conditioned; not, consequently, when the question relates to the descending series of consequences, or to the aggregate of the co-ordinated conditions of these consequences. For, in relation to a given conditioned, conditions are presupposed and considered to be given along with it. On the other hand, as the consequences do not render possible their conditions, but rather presuppose them—in the consideration of the procession of consequences (or in the descent from the given condition to the conditioned), we may be quite unconcerned whether the series ceases or not; and their totality is not a necessary demand of reason.\n\nThus we cogitate—and necessarily—a given time completely elapsed up to a given moment, although that time is not determinable by us. But as regards time future, which is not the condition of arriving at the present, in order to conceive it; it is quite indifferent whether we consider future time as ceasing at some point, or as prolonging itself to infinity. Take, for example, the series m, n, o, in which n is given as conditioned in relation to m, but at the same time as the condition of o, and let the series proceed upwards from the conditioned n to m (l, k, i, etc.), and also downwards from the condition n to the conditioned o (p, q, r, etc.)—I must presuppose the former series, to be able to consider n as given, and n is according to reason (the totality of conditions) possible only by means of that series. But its possibility does not rest on the following series o, p, q, r, which for this reason cannot be regarded as given, but only as capable of being given (dabilis).\n\nI shall term the synthesis of the series on the side of the conditions—from that nearest to the given phenomenon up to the more remote—regressive; that which proceeds on the side of the conditioned, from the immediate consequence to the more remote, I shall call the progressive synthesis. The former proceeds in antecedentia, the latter in consequentia. The cosmological ideas are therefore occupied with the totality of the regressive synthesis, and proceed in antecedentia, not in consequentia. When the latter takes place, it is an arbitrary and not a necessary problem of pure reason; for we require, for the complete understanding of what is given in a phenomenon, not the consequences which succeed, but the grounds or principles which precede.\n\nIn order to construct the table of ideas in correspondence with the table of categories, we take first the two primitive quanta of all our intuitions, time and space. Time is in itself a series (and the formal condition of all series), and hence, in relation to a given present, we must distinguish à priori in it the antecedentia as conditions (time past) from the consequentia (time future). Consequently, the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the series of the conditions of a given conditioned, relates merely to all past time. According to the idea of reason, the whole past time, as the condition of the given moment, is necessarily cogitated as given. But, as regards space, there exists in it no distinction between progressus and regressus; for it is an aggregate and not a series—its parts existing together at the same time. I can consider a given point of time in relation to past time only as conditioned, because this given moment comes into existence only through the past time rather through the passing of the preceding time. But as the parts of space are not subordinated, but co-ordinated to each other, one part cannot be the condition of the possibility of the other; and space is not in itself, like time, a series. But the synthesis of the manifold parts of space—(the syntheses whereby we apprehend space)—is nevertheless successive; it takes place, therefore, in time, and contains a series. And as in this series of aggregated spaces (for example, the feet in a rood), beginning with a given portion of space, those which continue to be annexed form the condition of the limits of the former—the measurement of a space must also be regarded as a synthesis of the series of the conditions of a given conditioned. It differs, however, in this respect from that of time, that the side of the conditioned is not in itself distinguishable from the side of the condition; and, consequently, regressus and progressus in space seem to be identical. But, inasmuch as one part of space is not given, but only limited, by and through another, we must also consider every limited space as conditioned, in so far as it presupposes some other space as the condition of its limitation, and so on. As regards limitation, therefore, our procedure in space is also a regressus, and the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the synthesis in a series of conditions applies to space also; and I am entitled to demand the absolute totality of the phenomenal synthesis in space as well as in time. Whether my demand can be satisfied is a question to be answered in the sequel.\n\nSecondly, the real in space—that is, matter—is conditioned. Its internal conditions are its parts, and the parts of parts its remote conditions; so that in this case we find a regressive synthesis, the absolute totality of which is a demand of reason. But this cannot be obtained otherwise than by a complete division of parts, whereby the real in matter becomes either nothing or that which is not matter, that is to say, the simple. Consequently we find here also a series of conditions and a progress to the unconditioned.\n\nThirdly, as regards the categories of a real relation between phenomena, the category of substance and its accidents is not suitable for the formation of a transcendental idea; that is to say, reason has no ground, in regard to it, to proceed regressively with conditions. For accidents (in so far as they inhere in a substance) are co-ordinated with each other, and do not constitute a series. And, in relation to substance, they are not properly subordinated to it, but are the mode of existence of the substance itself. The conception of the substantial might nevertheless seem to be an idea of the transcendental reason. But, as this signifies nothing more than the conception of an object in general, which subsists in so far as we cogitate in it merely a transcendental subject without any predicates; and as the question here is of an unconditioned in the series of phenomena—it is clear that the substantial can form no member thereof. The same holds good of substances in community, which are mere aggregates and do not form a series. For they are not subordinated to each other as conditions of the possibility of each other; which, however, may be affirmed of spaces, the limits of which are never determined in themselves, but always by some other space. It is, therefore, only in the category of causality that we can find a series of causes to a given effect, and in which we ascend from the latter, as the conditioned, to the former as the conditions, and thus answer the question of reason.\n\nFourthly, the conceptions of the possible, the actual, and the necessary do not conduct us to any series—excepting only in so far as the contingent in existence must always be regarded as conditioned, and as indicating, according to a law of the understanding, a condition, under which it is necessary to rise to a higher, till in the totality of the series, reason arrives at unconditioned necessity.\n\nThere are, accordingly, only four cosmological ideas, corresponding with the four titles of the categories. For we can select only such as necessarily furnish us with a series in the synthesis of the manifold.\n\n                      1\n            The absolute Completeness\n                    of the\n                 COMPOSITION\n     of the given totality of all phenomena.\n\n                      2\n            The absolute Completeness\n                    of the\n                   DIVISION\n     of given totality in a phenomenon.\n\n                       3\n            The absolute Completeness\n                     of the\n                   ORIGINATION\n                  of a phenomenon.\n\n                       4\n            The absolute Completeness\n         of the DEPENDENCE of the EXISTENCE\n        of what is changeable in a phenomenon.\n\nWe must here remark, in the first place, that the idea of absolute totality relates to nothing but the exposition of phenomena, and therefore not to the pure conception of a totality of things. Phenomena are here, therefore, regarded as given, and reason requires the absolute completeness of the conditions of their possibility, in so far as these conditions constitute a series—consequently an absolutely (that is, in every respect) complete synthesis, whereby a phenomenon can be explained according to the laws of the understanding.\n\nSecondly, it is properly the unconditioned alone that reason seeks in this serially and regressively conducted synthesis of conditions. It wishes, to speak in another way, to attain to completeness in the series of premisses, so as to render it unnecessary to presuppose others. This unconditioned is always contained in the absolute totality of the series, when we endeavour to form a representation of it in thought. But this absolutely complete synthesis is itself but an idea; for it is impossible, at least before hand, to know whether any such synthesis is possible in the case of phenomena. When we represent all existence in thought by means of pure conceptions of the understanding, without any conditions of sensuous intuition, we may say with justice that for a given conditioned the whole series of conditions subordinated to each other is also given; for the former is only given through the latter. But we find in the case of phenomena a particular limitation of the mode in which conditions are given, that is, through the successive synthesis of the manifold of intuition, which must be complete in the regress. Now whether this completeness is sensuously possible, is a problem. But the idea of it lies in the reason—be it possible or impossible to connect with the idea adequate empirical conceptions. Therefore, as in the absolute totality of the regressive synthesis of the manifold in a phenomenon (following the guidance of the categories, which represent it as a series of conditions to a given conditioned) the unconditioned is necessarily contained—it being still left unascertained whether and how this totality exists; reason sets out from the idea of totality, although its proper and final aim is the unconditioned—of the whole series, or of a part thereof.\n\nThis unconditioned may be cogitated—either as existing only in the entire series, all the members of which therefore would be without exception conditioned and only the totality absolutely unconditioned—and in this case the regressus is called infinite; or the absolutely unconditioned is only a part of the series, to which the other members are subordinated, but which Is not itself submitted to any other condition.[[48]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-48) In the former case the series is a parte priori unlimited (without beginning), that is, infinite, and nevertheless completely given. But the regress in it is never completed, and can only be called potentially infinite. In the second case there exists a first in the series. This first is called, in relation to past time, the beginning of the world; in relation to space, the limit of the world; in relation to the parts of a given limited whole, the simple; in relation to causes, absolute spontaneity (liberty); and in relation to the existence of changeable things, absolute physical necessity.\n\n[[48]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-48) The absolute totality of the series of conditions to a given conditioned is always unconditioned; because beyond it there exist no other conditions, on which it might depend. But the absolute totality of such a series is only an idea, or rather a problematical conception, the possibility of which must be investigated—particularly in relation to the mode in which the unconditioned, as the transcendental idea which is the real subject of inquiry, may be contained therein.\n\nWe possess two expressions, world and nature, which are generally interchanged. The first denotes the mathematical total of all phenomena and the totality of their synthesis—in its progress by means of composition, as well as by division. And the world is termed nature,[[49]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-49) when it is regarded as a dynamical whole—when our attention is not directed to the aggregation in space and time, for the purpose of cogitating it as a quantity, but to the unity in the existence of phenomena. In this case the condition of that which happens is called a cause; the unconditioned causality of the cause in a phenomenon is termed liberty; the conditioned cause is called in a more limited sense a natural cause. The conditioned in existence is termed contingent, and the unconditioned necessary. The unconditioned necessity of phenomena may be called natural necessity.\n\n[[49]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-49) Nature, understood adjective (formaliter), signifies the complex of the determinations of a thing, connected according to an internal principle of causality. On the other hand, we understand by nature, substantive (materialiter), the sum total of phenomena, in so far as they, by virtue of an internal principle of causality, are connected with each other throughout. In the former sense we speak of the nature of liquid matter, of fire, etc., and employ the word only adjective; while, if speaking of the objects of nature, we have in our minds the idea of a subsisting whole.\n\nThe ideas which we are at present engaged in discussing I have called cosmological ideas; partly because by the term world is understood the entire content of all phenomena, and our ideas are directed solely to the unconditioned among phenomena; partly also, because world, in the transcendental sense, signifies the absolute totality of the content of existing things, and we are directing our attention only to the completeness of the synthesis—although, properly, only in regression. In regard to the fact that these ideas are all transcendent, and, although they do not transcend phenomena as regards their mode, but are concerned solely with the world of sense (and not with noumena), nevertheless carry their synthesis to a degree far above all possible experience—it still seems to me that we can, with perfect propriety, designate them cosmical conceptions. As regards the distinction between the mathematically and the dynamically unconditioned which is the aim of the regression of the synthesis, I should call the two former, in a more limited signification, cosmical conceptions, the remaining two transcendent physical conceptions. This distinction does not at present seem to be of particular importance, but we shall afterwards find it to be of some value.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","text":"# Cosmos\n\n### Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas\n\nThat we may be able to enumerate with systematic precision these ideas according to a principle, we must remark, in the first place, that it is from the understanding alone that pure and transcendental conceptions take their origin; that the reason does not properly give birth to any conception, but only frees the conception of the understanding from the unavoidable limitation of a possible experience, and thus endeavours to raise it above the empirical, though it must still be in connection with it. This happens from the fact that, for a given conditioned, reason demands absolute totality on the side of the conditions (to which the understanding submits all phenomena), and thus makes of the category a transcendental idea. This it does that it may be able to give absolute completeness to the empirical synthesis, by continuing it to the unconditioned (which is not to be found in experience, but only in the idea). Reason requires this according to the principle: If the conditioned is given the whole of the conditions, and consequently the absolutely unconditioned, is also given, whereby alone the former was possible. First, then, the transcendental ideas are properly nothing but categories elevated to the unconditioned; and they may be arranged in a table according to the titles of the latter. But, secondly, all the categories are not available for this purpose, but only those in which the synthesis constitutes a series—of conditions subordinated to, not co-ordinated with, each other. Absolute totality is required of reason only in so far as concerns the ascending series of the conditions of a conditioned; not, consequently, when the question relates to the descending series of consequences, or to the aggregate of the co-ordinated conditions of these consequences. For, in relation to a given conditioned, conditions are presupposed and considered to be given along with it. On the other hand, as the consequences do not render possible their conditions, but rather presuppose them—in the consideration of the procession of consequences (or in the descent from the given condition to the conditioned), we may be quite unconcerned whether the series ceases or not; and their totality is not a necessary demand of reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"61.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","text":"Thus we cogitate—and necessarily—a given time completely elapsed up to a given moment, although that time is not determinable by us. But as regards time future, which is not the condition of arriving at the present, in order to conceive it; it is quite indifferent whether we consider future time as ceasing at some point, or as prolonging itself to infinity. Take, for example, the series m, n, o, in which n is given as conditioned in relation to m, but at the same time as the condition of o, and let the series proceed upwards from the conditioned n to m (l, k, i, etc.), and also downwards from the condition n to the conditioned o (p, q, r, etc.)—I must presuppose the former series, to be able to consider n as given, and n is according to reason (the totality of conditions) possible only by means of that series. But its possibility does not rest on the following series o, p, q, r, which for this reason cannot be regarded as given, but only as capable of being given (dabilis).\n\nI shall term the synthesis of the series on the side of the conditions—from that nearest to the given phenomenon up to the more remote—regressive; that which proceeds on the side of the conditioned, from the immediate consequence to the more remote, I shall call the progressive synthesis. The former proceeds in antecedentia, the latter in consequentia. The cosmological ideas are therefore occupied with the totality of the regressive synthesis, and proceed in antecedentia, not in consequentia. When the latter takes place, it is an arbitrary and not a necessary problem of pure reason; for we require, for the complete understanding of what is given in a phenomenon, not the consequences which succeed, but the grounds or principles which precede.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"61.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0003","text":"In order to construct the table of ideas in correspondence with the table of categories, we take first the two primitive quanta of all our intuitions, time and space. Time is in itself a series (and the formal condition of all series), and hence, in relation to a given present, we must distinguish à priori in it the antecedentia as conditions (time past) from the consequentia (time future). Consequently, the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the series of the conditions of a given conditioned, relates merely to all past time. According to the idea of reason, the whole past time, as the condition of the given moment, is necessarily cogitated as given. But, as regards space, there exists in it no distinction between progressus and regressus; for it is an aggregate and not a series—its parts existing together at the same time. I can consider a given point of time in relation to past time only as conditioned, because this given moment comes into existence only through the past time rather through the passing of the preceding time. But as the parts of space are not subordinated, but co-ordinated to each other, one part cannot be the condition of the possibility of the other; and space is not in itself, like time, a series. But the synthesis of the manifold parts of space—(the syntheses whereby we apprehend space)—is nevertheless successive; it takes place, therefore, in time, and contains a series. And as in this series of aggregated spaces (for example, the feet in a rood), beginning with a given portion of space, those which continue to be annexed form the condition of the limits of the former—the measurement of a space must also be regarded as a synthesis of the series of the conditions of a given conditioned. It differs, however, in this respect from that of time, that the side of the conditioned is not in itself distinguishable from the side of the condition; and, consequently, regressus and progressus in space seem to be identical. But, inasmuch as one part of space is not given, but only limited, by and through another, we must also consider every limited space as conditioned, in so far as it presupposes some other space as the condition of its limitation, and so on. As regards limitation, therefore, our procedure in space is also a regressus, and the transcendental idea of the absolute totality of the synthesis in a series of conditions applies to space also; and I am entitled to demand the absolute totality of the phenomenal synthesis in space as well as in time. Whether my demand can be satisfied is a question to be answered in the sequel.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"61.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0004","text":"Secondly, the real in space—that is, matter—is conditioned. Its internal conditions are its parts, and the parts of parts its remote conditions; so that in this case we find a regressive synthesis, the absolute totality of which is a demand of reason. But this cannot be obtained otherwise than by a complete division of parts, whereby the real in matter becomes either nothing or that which is not matter, that is to say, the simple. Consequently we find here also a series of conditions and a progress to the unconditioned.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"61.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","text":"Thirdly, as regards the categories of a real relation between phenomena, the category of substance and its accidents is not suitable for the formation of a transcendental idea; that is to say, reason has no ground, in regard to it, to proceed regressively with conditions. For accidents (in so far as they inhere in a substance) are co-ordinated with each other, and do not constitute a series. And, in relation to substance, they are not properly subordinated to it, but are the mode of existence of the substance itself. The conception of the substantial might nevertheless seem to be an idea of the transcendental reason. But, as this signifies nothing more than the conception of an object in general, which subsists in so far as we cogitate in it merely a transcendental subject without any predicates; and as the question here is of an unconditioned in the series of phenomena—it is clear that the substantial can form no member thereof. The same holds good of substances in community, which are mere aggregates and do not form a series. For they are not subordinated to each other as conditions of the possibility of each other; which, however, may be affirmed of spaces, the limits of which are never determined in themselves, but always by some other space. It is, therefore, only in the category of causality that we can find a series of causes to a given effect, and in which we ascend from the latter, as the conditioned, to the former as the conditions, and thus answer the question of reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"61.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","text":"Fourthly, the conceptions of the possible, the actual, and the necessary do not conduct us to any series—excepting only in so far as the contingent in existence must always be regarded as conditioned, and as indicating, according to a law of the understanding, a condition, under which it is necessary to rise to a higher, till in the totality of the series, reason arrives at unconditioned necessity.\n\nThere are, accordingly, only four cosmological ideas, corresponding with the four titles of the categories. For we can select only such as necessarily furnish us with a series in the synthesis of the manifold.\n\n                      1\n            The absolute Completeness\n                    of the\n                 COMPOSITION\n     of the given totality of all phenomena.\n\n                      2\n            The absolute Completeness\n                    of the\n                   DIVISION\n     of given totality in a phenomenon.\n\n                       3\n            The absolute Completeness\n                     of the\n                   ORIGINATION\n                  of a phenomenon.\n\n                       4\n            The absolute Completeness\n         of the DEPENDENCE of the EXISTENCE\n        of what is changeable in a phenomenon.\n\nWe must here remark, in the first place, that the idea of absolute totality relates to nothing but the exposition of phenomena, and therefore not to the pure conception of a totality of things. Phenomena are here, therefore, regarded as given, and reason requires the absolute completeness of the conditions of their possibility, in so far as these conditions constitute a series—consequently an absolutely (that is, in every respect) complete synthesis, whereby a phenomenon can be explained according to the laws of the understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"61.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","text":"Secondly, it is properly the unconditioned alone that reason seeks in this serially and regressively conducted synthesis of conditions. It wishes, to speak in another way, to attain to completeness in the series of premisses, so as to render it unnecessary to presuppose others. This unconditioned is always contained in the absolute totality of the series, when we endeavour to form a representation of it in thought. But this absolutely complete synthesis is itself but an idea; for it is impossible, at least before hand, to know whether any such synthesis is possible in the case of phenomena. When we represent all existence in thought by means of pure conceptions of the understanding, without any conditions of sensuous intuition, we may say with justice that for a given conditioned the whole series of conditions subordinated to each other is also given; for the former is only given through the latter. But we find in the case of phenomena a particular limitation of the mode in which conditions are given, that is, through the successive synthesis of the manifold of intuition, which must be complete in the regress. Now whether this completeness is sensuously possible, is a problem. But the idea of it lies in the reason—be it possible or impossible to connect with the idea adequate empirical conceptions. Therefore, as in the absolute totality of the regressive synthesis of the manifold in a phenomenon (following the guidance of the categories, which represent it as a series of conditions to a given conditioned) the unconditioned is necessarily contained—it being still left unascertained whether and how this totality exists; reason sets out from the idea of totality, although its proper and final aim is the unconditioned—of the whole series, or of a part thereof.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"61.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0008","text":"This unconditioned may be cogitated—either as existing only in the entire series, all the members of which therefore would be without exception conditioned and only the totality absolutely unconditioned—and in this case the regressus is called infinite; or the absolutely unconditioned is only a part of the series, to which the other members are subordinated, but which Is not itself submitted to any other condition.[[48]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-48) In the former case the series is a parte priori unlimited (without beginning), that is, infinite, and nevertheless completely given. But the regress in it is never completed, and can only be called potentially infinite. In the second case there exists a first in the series. This first is called, in relation to past time, the beginning of the world; in relation to space, the limit of the world; in relation to the parts of a given limited whole, the simple; in relation to causes, absolute spontaneity (liberty); and in relation to the existence of changeable things, absolute physical necessity.\n\n[[48]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-48) The absolute totality of the series of conditions to a given conditioned is always unconditioned; because beyond it there exist no other conditions, on which it might depend. But the absolute totality of such a series is only an idea, or rather a problematical conception, the possibility of which must be investigated—particularly in relation to the mode in which the unconditioned, as the transcendental idea which is the real subject of inquiry, may be contained therein.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"61.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0009","text":"We possess two expressions, world and nature, which are generally interchanged. The first denotes the mathematical total of all phenomena and the totality of their synthesis—in its progress by means of composition, as well as by division. And the world is termed nature,[[49]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-49) when it is regarded as a dynamical whole—when our attention is not directed to the aggregation in space and time, for the purpose of cogitating it as a quantity, but to the unity in the existence of phenomena. In this case the condition of that which happens is called a cause; the unconditioned causality of the cause in a phenomenon is termed liberty; the conditioned cause is called in a more limited sense a natural cause. The conditioned in existence is termed contingent, and the unconditioned necessary. The unconditioned necessity of phenomena may be called natural necessity.\n\n[[49]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-49) Nature, understood adjective (formaliter), signifies the complex of the determinations of a thing, connected according to an internal principle of causality. On the other hand, we understand by nature, substantive (materialiter), the sum total of phenomena, in so far as they, by virtue of an internal principle of causality, are connected with each other throughout. In the former sense we speak of the nature of liquid matter, of fire, etc., and employ the word only adjective; while, if speaking of the objects of nature, we have in our minds the idea of a subsisting whole.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"61.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0010","text":"The ideas which we are at present engaged in discussing I have called cosmological ideas; partly because by the term world is understood the entire content of all phenomena, and our ideas are directed solely to the unconditioned among phenomena; partly also, because world, in the transcendental sense, signifies the absolute totality of the content of existing things, and we are directing our attention only to the completeness of the synthesis—although, properly, only in regression. In regard to the fact that these ideas are all transcendent, and, although they do not transcend phenomena as regards their mode, but are concerned solely with the world of sense (and not with noumena), nevertheless carry their synthesis to a degree far above all possible experience—it still seems to me that we can, with perfect propriety, designate them cosmical conceptions. As regards the distinction between the mathematically and the dynamically unconditioned which is the aim of the regression of the synthesis, I should call the two former, in a more limited signification, cosmical conceptions, the remaining two transcendent physical conceptions. This distinction does not at present seem to be of particular importance, but we shall afterwards find it to be of some value.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"61.10"}],"sha256":"364c74cedaa5a26aa8d44a48de0bbb2577de8f89bba8c3120ef8d3afdb2b6c07","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP","title":"Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason","order":62,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIAP","text":"### Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason\n\nThetic is the term applied to every collection of dogmatical propositions. By antithetic I do not understand dogmatical assertions of the opposite, but the self-contradiction of seemingly dogmatical cognitions (thesis cum antithesis), in none of which we can discover any decided superiority. Antithetic is not, therefore, occupied with one-sided statements, but is engaged in considering the contradictory nature of the general cognitions of reason and its causes. Transcendental antithetic is an investigation into the antinomy of pure reason, its causes and result. If we employ our reason not merely in the application of the principles of the understanding to objects of experience, but venture with it beyond these boundaries, there arise certain sophistical propositions or theorems. These assertions have the following peculiarities: They can find neither confirmation nor confutation in experience; and each is in itself not only self-consistent, but possesses conditions of its necessity in the very nature of reason—only that, unluckily, there exist just as valid and necessary grounds for maintaining the contrary proposition.\n\nThe questions which naturally arise in the consideration of this dialectic of pure reason, are therefore: 1st. In what propositions is pure reason unavoidably subject to an antinomy? 2nd. What are the causes of this antinomy? 3rd. Whether and in what way can reason free itself from this self-contradiction?\n\nA dialectical proposition or theorem of pure reason must, according to what has been said, be distinguishable from all sophistical propositions, by the fact that it is not an answer to an arbitrary question, which may be raised at the mere pleasure of any person, but to one which human reason must necessarily encounter in its progress. In the second place, a dialectical proposition, with its opposite, does not carry the appearance of a merely artificial illusion, which disappears as soon as it is investigated, but a natural and unavoidable illusion, which, even when we are no longer deceived by it, continues to mock us and, although rendered harmless, can never be completely removed.\n\nThis dialectical doctrine will not relate to the unity of understanding in empirical conceptions, but to the unity of reason in pure ideas. The conditions of this doctrine are—inasmuch as it must, as a synthesis according to rules, be conformable to the understanding, and at the same time as the absolute unity of the synthesis, to the reason—that, if it is adequate to the unity of reason, it is too great for the understanding, if according with the understanding, it is too small for the reason. Hence arises a mutual opposition, which cannot be avoided, do what we will.\n\nThese sophistical assertions of dialectic open, as it were, a battle-field, where that side obtains the victory which has been permitted to make the attack, and he is compelled to yield who has been unfortunately obliged to stand on the defensive. And hence, champions of ability, whether on the right or on the wrong side, are certain to carry away the crown of victory, if they only take care to have the right to make the last attack, and are not obliged to sustain another onset from their opponent. We can easily believe that this arena has been often trampled by the feet of combatants, that many victories have been obtained on both sides, but that the last victory, decisive of the affair between the contending parties, was won by him who fought for the right, only if his adversary was forbidden to continue the tourney. As impartial umpires, we must lay aside entirely the consideration whether the combatants are fighting for the right or for the wrong side, for the true or for the false, and allow the combat to be first decided. Perhaps, after they have wearied more than injured each other, they will discover the nothingness of their cause of quarrel and part good friends.\n\nThis method of watching, or rather of originating, a conflict of assertions, not for the purpose of finally deciding in favour of either side, but to discover whether the object of the struggle is not a mere illusion, which each strives in vain to reach, but which would be no gain even when reached—this procedure, I say, may be termed the sceptical method. It is thoroughly distinct from scepticism—the principle of a technical and scientific ignorance, which undermines the foundations of all knowledge, in order, if possible, to destroy our belief and confidence therein. For the sceptical method aims at certainty, by endeavouring to discover in a conflict of this kind, conducted honestly and intelligently on both sides, the point of misunderstanding; just as wise legislators derive, from the embarrassment of judges in lawsuits, information in regard to the defective and ill-defined parts of their statutes. The antinomy which reveals itself in the application of laws, is for our limited wisdom the best criterion of legislation. For the attention of reason, which in abstract speculation does not easily become conscious of its errors, is thus roused to the momenta in the determination of its principles.\n\nBut this sceptical method is essentially peculiar to transcendental philosophy, and can perhaps be dispensed with in every other field of investigation. In mathematics its use would be absurd; because in it no false assertions can long remain hidden, inasmuch as its demonstrations must always proceed under the guidance of pure intuition, and by means of an always evident synthesis. In experimental philosophy, doubt and delay may be very useful; but no misunderstanding is possible, which cannot be easily removed; and in experience means of solving the difficulty and putting an end to the dissension must at last be found, whether sooner or later. Moral philosophy can always exhibit its principles, with their practical consequences, in concreto—at least in possible experiences, and thus escape the mistakes and ambiguities of abstraction. But transcendental propositions, which lay claim to insight beyond the region of possible experience, cannot, on the one hand, exhibit their abstract synthesis in any à priori intuition, nor, on the other, expose a lurking error by the help of experience. Transcendental reason, therefore, presents us with no other criterion than that of an attempt to reconcile such assertions, and for this purpose to permit a free and unrestrained conflict between them. And this we now proceed to arrange.[[50]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-50)\n\n[[50]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-50) The antinomies stand in the order of the four transcendental ideas above detailed.\n\nFIRST CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nThe world has a beginning in time, and is also limited in regard to space.\n\nPROOF.\n\nGranted that the world has no beginning in time; up to every given moment of time, an eternity must have elapsed, and therewith passed away an infinite series of successive conditions or states of things in the world. Now the infinity of a series consists in the fact that it never can be completed by means of a successive synthesis. It follows that an infinite series already elapsed is impossible and that, consequently, a beginning of the world is a necessary condition of its existence. And this was the first thing to be proved.\n\nAs regards the second, let us take the opposite for granted. In this case, the world must be an infinite given total of coexistent things. Now we cannot cogitate the dimensions of a quantity, which is not given within certain limits of an intuition,[[51]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-51) in any other way than by means of the synthesis of its parts, and the total of such a quantity only by means of a completed synthesis, or the repeated addition of unity to itself. Accordingly, to cogitate the world, which fills all spaces, as a whole, the successive synthesis of the parts of an infinite world must be looked upon as completed, that is to say, an infinite time must be regarded as having elapsed in the enumeration of all co-existing things; which is impossible. For this reason an infinite aggregate of actual things cannot be considered as a given whole, consequently, not as a contemporaneously given whole. The world is consequently, as regards extension in space, not infinite, but enclosed in limits. And this was the second thing to be proved.\n\n[[51]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-51) We may consider an undetermined quantity as a whole, when it is enclosed within limits, although we cannot construct or ascertain its totality by measurement, that is, by the successive synthesis of its parts. For its limits of themselves determine its completeness as a whole.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nThe world has no beginning, and no limits in space, but is, in relation both to time and space, infinite.\n\nPROOF.\n\nFor let it be granted that it has a beginning. A beginning is an existence which is preceded by a time in which the thing does not exist. On the above supposition, it follows that there must have been a time in which the world did not exist, that is, a void time. But in a void time the origination of a thing is impossible; because no part of any such time contains a distinctive condition of being, in preference to that of non-being (whether the supposed thing originate of itself, or by means of some other cause). Consequently, many series of things may have a beginning in the world, but the world itself cannot have a beginning, and is, therefore, in relation to past time, infinite.\n\nAs regards the second statement, let us first take the opposite for granted—that the world is finite and limited in space; it follows that it must exist in a void space, which is not limited. We should therefore meet not only with a relation of things in space, but also a relation of things to space. Now, as the world is an absolute whole, out of and beyond which no object of intuition, and consequently no correlate to which can be discovered, this relation of the world to a void space is merely a relation to no object. But such a relation, and consequently the limitation of the world by void space, is nothing. Consequently, the world, as regards space, is not limited, that is, it is infinite in regard to extension.[[52]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-52)\n\n[[52]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-52) Space is merely the form of external intuition (formal intuition), and not a real object which can be externally perceived. Space, prior to all things which determine it (fill or limit it), or, rather, which present an empirical intuition conformable to it, is, under the title of absolute space, nothing but the mere possibility of external phenomena, in so far as they either exist in themselves, or can annex themselves to given intuitions. Empirical intuition is therefore not a composition of phenomena and space (of perception and empty intuition). The one is not the correlate of the other in a synthesis, but they are vitally connected in the same empirical intuition, as matter and form. If we wish to set one of these two apart from the other—space from phenomena—there arise all sorts of empty determinations of external intuition, which are very far from being possible perceptions. For example, motion or rest of the world in an infinite empty space, or a determination of the mutual relation of both, cannot possibly be perceived, and is therefore merely the predicate of a notional entity.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE FIRST ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.\n\nIn bringing forward these conflicting arguments, I have not been on the search for sophisms, for the purpose of availing myself of special pleading, which takes advantage of the carelessness of the opposite party, appeals to a misunderstood statute, and erects its unrighteous claims upon an unfair interpretation. Both proofs originate fairly from the nature of the case, and the advantage presented by the mistakes of the dogmatists of both parties has been completely set aside.\n\nThe thesis might also have been unfairly demonstrated, by the introduction of an erroneous conception of the infinity of a given quantity. A quantity is infinite, if a greater than itself cannot possibly exist. The quantity is measured by the number of given units—which are taken as a standard—contained in it. Now no number can be the greatest, because one or more units can always be added. It follows that an infinite given quantity, consequently an infinite world (both as regards time and extension) is impossible. It is, therefore, limited in both respects. In this manner I might have conducted my proof; but the conception given in it does not agree with the true conception of an infinite whole. In this there is no representation of its quantity, it is not said how large it is; consequently its conception is not the conception of a maximum. We cogitate in it merely its relation to an arbitrarily assumed unit, in relation to which it is greater than any number. Now, just as the unit which is taken is greater or smaller, the infinite will be greater or smaller; but the infinity, which consists merely in the relation to this given unit, must remain always the same, although the absolute quantity of the whole is not thereby cognized.\n\nThe true (transcendental) conception of infinity is: that the successive synthesis of unity in the measurement of a given quantum can never be completed.[[53]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-53) Hence it follows, without possibility of mistake, that an eternity of actual successive states up to a given (the present) moment cannot have elapsed, and that the world must therefore have a beginning.\n\n[[53]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-53) The quantum in this sense contains a congeries of given units, which is greater than any number—and this is the mathematical conception of the infinite.\n\nIn regard to the second part of the thesis, the difficulty as to an infinite and yet elapsed series disappears; for the manifold of a world infinite in extension is contemporaneously given. But, in order to cogitate the total of this manifold, as we cannot have the aid of limits constituting by themselves this total in intuition, we are obliged to give some account of our conception, which in this case cannot proceed from the whole to the determined quantity of the parts, but must demonstrate the possibility of a whole by means of a successive synthesis of the parts. But as this synthesis must constitute a series that cannot be completed, it is impossible for us to cogitate prior to it, and consequently not by means of it, a totality. For the conception of totality itself is in the present case the representation of a completed synthesis of the parts; and this completion, and consequently its conception, is impossible.\n\nON THE ANTITHESIS.\n\nThe proof in favour of the infinity of the cosmical succession and the cosmical content is based upon the consideration that, in the opposite case, a void time and a void space must constitute the limits of the world. Now I am not unaware, that there are some ways of escaping this conclusion. It may, for example, be alleged, that a limit to the world, as regards both space and time, is quite possible, without at the same time holding the existence of an absolute time before the beginning of the world, or an absolute space extending beyond the actual world—which is impossible. I am quite well satisfied with the latter part of this opinion of the philosophers of the Leibnitzian school. Space is merely the form of external intuition, but not a real object which can itself be externally intuited; it is not a correlate of phenomena, it is the form of phenomena itself. Space, therefore, cannot be regarded as absolutely and in itself something determinative of the existence of things, because it is not itself an object, but only the form of possible objects. Consequently, things, as phenomena, determine space; that is to say, they render it possible that, of all the possible predicates of space (size and relation), certain may belong to reality. But we cannot affirm the converse, that space, as something self-subsistent, can determine real things in regard to size or shape, for it is in itself not a real thing. Space (filled or void)[[54]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-54) may therefore be limited by phenomena, but phenomena cannot be limited by an empty space without them. This is true of time also. All this being granted, it is nevertheless indisputable, that we must assume these two nonentities, void space without and void time before the world, if we assume the existence of cosmical limits, relatively to space or time.\n\n[[54]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-54) It is evident that what is meant here is, that empty space, in so far as it is limited by phenomena—space, that is, within the world—does not at least contradict transcendental principles, and may therefore, as regards them, be admitted, although its possibility cannot on that account be affirmed.\n\nFor, as regards the subterfuge adopted by those who endeavour to evade the consequence—that, if the world is limited as to space and time, the infinite void must determine the existence of actual things in regard to their dimensions—it arises solely from the fact that instead of a sensuous world, an intelligible world—of which nothing is known—is cogitated; instead of a real beginning (an existence, which is preceded by a period in which nothing exists), an existence which presupposes no other condition than that of time; and, instead of limits of extension, boundaries of the universe. But the question relates to the mundus phaenomenon, and its quantity; and in this case we cannot make abstraction of the conditions of sensibility, without doing away with the essential reality of this world itself. The world of sense, if it is limited, must necessarily lie in the infinite void. If this, and with it space as the à priori condition of the possibility of phenomena, is left out of view, the whole world of sense disappears. In our problem is this alone considered as given. The mundus intelligibilis is nothing but the general conception of a world, in which abstraction has been made of all conditions of intuition, and in relation to which no synthetical proposition—either affirmative or negative—is possible.\n\nSECOND CONFLICT OF TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nEvery composite substance in the world consists of simple parts; and there exists nothing that is not either itself simple, or composed of simple parts.\n\nPROOF.\n\nFor, grant that composite substances do not consist of simple parts; in this case, if all combination or composition were annihilated in thought, no composite part, and (as, by the supposition, there do not exist simple parts) no simple part would exist. Consequently, no substance; consequently, nothing would exist. Either, then, it is impossible to annihilate composition in thought; or, after such annihilation, there must remain something that subsists without composition, that is, something that is simple. But in the former case the composite could not itself consist of substances, because with substances composition is merely a contingent relation, apart from which they must still exist as self-subsistent beings. Now, as this case contradicts the supposition, the second must contain the truth—that the substantial composite in the world consists of simple parts.\n\nIt follows, as an immediate inference, that the things in the world are all, without exception, simple beings—that composition is merely an external condition pertaining to them—and that, although we never can separate and isolate the elementary substances from the state of composition, reason must cogitate these as the primary subjects of all composition, and consequently, as prior thereto—and as simple substances.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nNo composite thing in the world consists of simple parts; and there does not exist in the world any simple substance.\n\nPROOF.\n\nLet it be supposed that a composite thing (as substance) consists of simple parts. Inasmuch as all external relation, consequently all composition of substances, is possible only in space; the space, occupied by that which is composite, must consist of the same number of parts as is contained in the composite. But space does not consist of simple parts, but of spaces. Therefore, every part of the composite must occupy a space. But the absolutely primary parts of what is composite are simple. It follows that what is simple occupies a space. Now, as everything real that occupies a space, contains a manifold the parts of which are external to each other, and is consequently composite—and a real composite, not of accidents (for these cannot exist external to each other apart from substance), but of substances—it follows that the simple must be a substantial composite, which is self-contradictory.\n\nThe second proposition of the antithesis—that there exists in the world nothing that is simple—is here equivalent to the following: The existence of the absolutely simple cannot be demonstrated from any experience or perception either external or internal; and the absolutely simple is a mere idea, the objective reality of which cannot be demonstrated in any possible experience; it is consequently, in the exposition of phenomena, without application and object. For, let us take for granted that an object may be found in experience for this transcendental idea; the empirical intuition of such an object must then be recognized to contain absolutely no manifold with its parts external to each other, and connected into unity. Now, as we cannot reason from the non-consciousness of such a manifold to the impossibility of its existence in the intuition of an object, and as the proof of this impossibility is necessary for the establishment and proof of absolute simplicity; it follows that this simplicity cannot be inferred from any perception whatever. As, therefore, an absolutely simple object cannot be given in any experience, and the world of sense must be considered as the sum total of all possible experiences: nothing simple exists in the world.\n\nThis second proposition in the antithesis has a more extended aim than the first. The first merely banishes the simple from the intuition of the composite; while the second drives it entirely out of nature. Hence we were unable to demonstrate it from the conception of a given object of external intuition (of the composite), but we were obliged to prove it from the relation of a given object to a possible experience in general.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE SECOND ANTINOMY. THESIS.\n\nWhen I speak of a whole, which necessarily consists of simple parts, I understand thereby only a substantial whole, as the true composite; that is to say, I understand that contingent unity of the manifold which is given as perfectly isolated (at least in thought), placed in reciprocal connection, and thus constituted a unity. Space ought not to be called a compositum but a totum, for its parts are possible in the whole, and not the whole by means of the parts. It might perhaps be called a compositum ideale, but not a compositum reale. But this is of no importance. As space is not a composite of substances (and not even of real accidents), if I abstract all composition therein—nothing, not even a point, remains; for a point is possible only as the limit of a space—consequently of a composite. Space and time, therefore, do not consist of simple parts. That which belongs only to the condition or state of a substance, even although it possesses a quantity (motion or change, for example), likewise does not consist of simple parts. That is to say, a certain degree of change does not originate from the addition of many simple changes. Our inference of the simple from the composite is valid only of self-subsisting things. But the accidents of a state are not self-subsistent. The proof, then, for the necessity of the simple, as the component part of all that is substantial and composite, may prove a failure, and the whole case of this thesis be lost, if we carry the proposition too far, and wish to make it valid of everything that is composite without distinction—as indeed has really now and then happened. Besides, I am here speaking only of the simple, in so far as it is necessarily given in the composite—the latter being capable of solution into the former as its component parts. The proper signification of the word monas (as employed by Leibnitz) ought to relate to the simple, given immediately as simple substance (for example, in consciousness), and not as an element of the composite. As an element, the term atomus would be more appropriate. And as I wish to prove the existence of simple substances, only in relation to, and as the elements of, the composite, I might term the antithesis of the second Antinomy, transcendental Atomistic. But as this word has long been employed to designate a particular theory of corporeal phenomena (moleculae), and thus presupposes a basis of empirical conceptions, I prefer calling it the dialectical principle of Monadology.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nAgainst the assertion of the infinite subdivisibility of matter whose ground of proof is purely mathematical, objections have been alleged by the Monadists. These objections lay themselves open, at first sight, to suspicion, from the fact that they do not recognize the clearest mathematical proofs as propositions relating to the constitution of space, in so far as it is really the formal condition of the possibility of all matter, but regard them merely as inferences from abstract but arbitrary conceptions, which cannot have any application to real things. Just as if it were possible to imagine another mode of intuition than that given in the primitive intuition of space; and just as if its à priori determinations did not apply to everything, the existence of which is possible, from the fact alone of its filling space. If we listen to them, we shall find ourselves required to cogitate, in addition to the mathematical point, which is simple—not, however, a part, but a mere limit of space—physical points, which are indeed likewise simple, but possess the peculiar property, as parts of space, of filling it merely by their aggregation. I shall not repeat here the common and clear refutations of this absurdity, which are to be found everywhere in numbers: every one knows that it is impossible to undermine the evidence of mathematics by mere discursive conceptions; I shall only remark that, if in this case philosophy endeavours to gain an advantage over mathematics by sophistical artifices, it is because it forgets that the discussion relates solely to Phenomena and their conditions. It is not sufficient to find the conception of the simple for the pure conception of the composite, but we must discover for the intuition of the composite (matter), the intuition of the simple. Now this, according to the laws of sensibility, and consequently in the case of objects of sense, is utterly impossible. In the case of a whole composed of substances, which is cogitated solely by the pure understanding, it may be necessary to be in possession of the simple before composition is possible. But this does not hold good of the Totum substantiale phaenomenon, which, as an empirical intuition in space, possesses the necessary property of containing no simple part, for the very reason that no part of space is simple. Meanwhile, the Monadists have been subtle enough to escape from this difficulty, by presupposing intuition and the dynamical relation of substances as the condition of the possibility of space, instead of regarding space as the condition of the possibility of the objects of external intuition, that is, of bodies. Now we have a conception of bodies only as phenomena, and, as such, they necessarily presuppose space as the condition of all external phenomena. The evasion is therefore in vain; as, indeed, we have sufficiently shown in our Æsthetic. If bodies were things in themselves, the proof of the Monadists would be unexceptionable.\n\nThe second dialectical assertion possesses the peculiarity of having opposed to it a dogmatical proposition, which, among all such sophistical statements, is the only one that undertakes to prove in the case of an object of experience, that which is properly a transcendental idea—the absolute simplicity of substance. The proposition is that the object of the internal sense, the thinking Ego, is an absolute simple substance. Without at present entering upon this subject—as it has been considered at length in a former chapter—I shall merely remark that, if something is cogitated merely as an object, without the addition of any synthetical determination of its intuition—as happens in the case of the bare representation, _I_—it is certain that no manifold and no composition can be perceived in such a representation. As, moreover, the predicates whereby I cogitate this object are merely intuitions of the internal sense, there cannot be discovered in them anything to prove the existence of a manifold whose parts are external to each other, and, consequently, nothing to prove the existence of real composition. Consciousness, therefore, is so constituted that, inasmuch as the thinking subject is at the same time its own object, it cannot divide itself—although it can divide its inhering determinations. For every object in relation to itself is absolute unity. Nevertheless, if the subject is regarded externally, as an object of intuition, it must, in its character of phenomenon, possess the property of composition. And it must always be regarded in this manner, if we wish to know whether there is or is not contained in it a manifold whose parts are external to each other.\n\nTHIRD CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nCausality according to the laws of nature, is not the only causality operating to originate the phenomena of the world. A causality of freedom is also necessary to account fully for these phenomena.\n\nPROOF.\n\nLet it be supposed, that there is no other kind of causality than that according to the laws of nature. Consequently, everything that happens presupposes a previous condition, which it follows with absolute certainty, in conformity with a rule. But this previous condition must itself be something that has happened (that has arisen in time, as it did not exist before), for, if it has always been in existence, its consequence or effect would not thus originate for the first time, but would likewise have always existed. The causality, therefore, of a cause, whereby something happens, is itself a thing that has happened. Now this again presupposes, in conformity with the law of nature, a previous condition and its causality, and this another anterior to the former, and so on. If, then, everything happens solely in accordance with the laws of nature, there cannot be any real first beginning of things, but only a subaltern or comparative beginning. There cannot, therefore, be a completeness of series on the side of the causes which originate the one from the other. But the law of nature is that nothing can happen without a sufficient à priori determined cause. The proposition therefore—if all causality is possible only in accordance with the laws of nature—is, when stated in this unlimited and general manner, self-contradictory. It follows that this cannot be the only kind of causality.\n\nFrom what has been said, it follows that a causality must be admitted, by means of which something happens, without its cause being determined according to necessary laws by some other cause preceding. That is to say, there must exist an absolute spontaneity of cause, which of itself originates a series of phenomena which proceeds according to natural laws—consequently transcendental freedom, without which even in the course of nature the succession of phenomena on the side of causes is never complete.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nThere is no such thing as freedom, but everything in the world happens solely according to the laws of nature.\n\nPROOF.\n\nGranted, that there does exist freedom in the transcendental sense, as a peculiar kind of causality, operating to produce events in the world—a faculty, that is to say, of originating a state, and consequently a series of consequences from that state. In this case, not only the series originated by this spontaneity, but the determination of this spontaneity itself to the production of the series, that is to say, the causality itself must have an absolute commencement, such that nothing can precede to determine this action according to unvarying laws. But every beginning of action presupposes in the acting cause a state of inaction; and a dynamically primal beginning of action presupposes a state, which has no connection—as regards causality—with the preceding state of the cause—which does not, that is, in any wise result from it. Transcendental freedom is therefore opposed to the natural law of cause and effect, and such a conjunction of successive states in effective causes is destructive of the possibility of unity in experience and for that reason not to be found in experience—is consequently a mere fiction of thought.\n\nWe have, therefore, nothing but nature to which we must look for connection and order in cosmical events. Freedom—independence of the laws of nature—is certainly a deliverance from restraint, but it is also a relinquishing of the guidance of law and rule. For it cannot be alleged that, instead of the laws of nature, laws of freedom may be introduced into the causality of the course of nature. For, if freedom were determined according to laws, it would be no longer freedom, but merely nature. Nature, therefore, and transcendental freedom are distinguishable as conformity to law and lawlessness. The former imposes upon understanding the difficulty of seeking the origin of events ever higher and higher in the series of causes, inasmuch as causality is always conditioned thereby; while it compensates this labour by the guarantee of a unity complete and in conformity with law. The latter, on the contrary, holds out to the understanding the promise of a point of rest in the chain of causes, by conducting it to an unconditioned causality, which professes to have the power of spontaneous origination, but which, in its own utter blindness, deprives it of the guidance of rules, by which alone a completely connected experience is possible.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE THIRD ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.\n\nThe transcendental idea of freedom is far from constituting the entire content of the psychological conception so termed, which is for the most part empirical. It merely presents us with the conception of spontaneity of action, as the proper ground for imputing freedom to the cause of a certain class of objects. It is, however, the true stumbling-stone to philosophy, which meets with unconquerable difficulties in the way of its admitting this kind of unconditioned causality. That element in the question of the freedom of the will, which has for so long a time placed speculative reason in such perplexity, is properly only transcendental, and concerns the question, whether there must be held to exist a faculty of spontaneous origination of a series of successive things or states. How such a faculty is possible is not a necessary inquiry; for in the case of natural causality itself, we are obliged to content ourselves with the à priori knowledge that such a causality must be presupposed, although we are quite incapable of comprehending how the being of one thing is possible through the being of another, but must for this information look entirely to experience. Now we have demonstrated this necessity of a free first beginning of a series of phenomena, only in so far as it is required for the comprehension of an origin of the world, all following states being regarded as a succession according to laws of nature alone. But, as there has thus been proved the existence of a faculty which can of itself originate a series in time—although we are unable to explain how it can exist—we feel ourselves authorized to admit, even in the midst of the natural course of events, a beginning, as regards causality, of different successions of phenomena, and at the same time to attribute to all substances a faculty of free action. But we ought in this case not to allow ourselves to fall into a common misunderstanding, and to suppose that, because a successive series in the world can only have a comparatively first beginning—another state or condition of things always preceding—an absolutely first beginning of a series in the course of nature is impossible. For we are not speaking here of an absolutely first beginning in relation to time, but as regards causality alone. When, for example, I, completely of my own free will, and independently of the necessarily determinative influence of natural causes, rise from my chair, there commences with this event, including its material consequences in infinitum, an absolutely new series; although, in relation to time, this event is merely the continuation of a preceding series. For this resolution and act of mine do not form part of the succession of effects in nature, and are not mere continuations of it; on the contrary, the determining causes of nature cease to operate in reference to this event, which certainly succeeds the acts of nature, but does not proceed from them. For these reasons, the action of a free agent must be termed, in regard to causality, if not in relation to time, an absolutely primal beginning of a series of phenomena.\n\nThe justification of this need of reason to rest upon a free act as the first beginning of the series of natural causes is evident from the fact, that all philosophers of antiquity (with the exception of the Epicurean school) felt themselves obliged, when constructing a theory of the motions of the universe, to accept a prime mover, that is, a freely acting cause, which spontaneously and prior to all other causes evolved this series of states. They always felt the need of going beyond mere nature, for the purpose of making a first beginning comprehensible.\n\nON THE ANTITHESIS.\n\nThe assertor of the all-sufficiency of nature in regard to causality (transcendental Physiocracy), in opposition to the doctrine of freedom, would defend his view of the question somewhat in the following manner. He would say, in answer to the sophistical arguments of the opposite party: If you do not accept a mathematical first, in relation to time, you have no need to seek a dynamical first, in regard to causality. Who compelled you to imagine an absolutely primal condition of the world, and therewith an absolute beginning of the gradually progressing successions of phenomena—and, as some foundation for this fancy of yours, to set bounds to unlimited nature? Inasmuch as the substances in the world have always existed—at least the unity of experience renders such a supposition quite necessary—there is no difficulty in believing also, that the changes in the conditions of these substances have always existed; and, consequently, that a first beginning, mathematical or dynamical, is by no means required. The possibility of such an infinite derivation, without any initial member from which all the others result, is certainly quite incomprehensible. But, if you are rash enough to deny the enigmatical secrets of nature for this reason, you will find yourselves obliged to deny also the existence of many fundamental properties of natural objects (such as fundamental forces), which you can just as little comprehend; and even the possibility of so simple a conception as that of change must present to you insuperable difficulties. For if experience did not teach you that it was real, you never could conceive à priori the possibility of this ceaseless sequence of being and non-being.\n\nBut if the existence of a transcendental faculty of freedom is granted—a faculty of originating changes in the world—this faculty must at least exist out of and apart from the world; although it is certainly a bold assumption, that, over and above the complete content of all possible intuitions, there still exists an object which cannot be presented in any possible perception. But, to attribute to substances in the world itself such a faculty, is quite inadmissible; for, in this case; the connection of phenomena reciprocally determining and determined according to general laws, which is termed nature, and along with it the criteria of empirical truth, which enable us to distinguish experience from mere visionary dreaming, would almost entirely disappear. In proximity with such a lawless faculty of freedom, a system of nature is hardly cogitable; for the laws of the latter would be continually subject to the intrusive influences of the former, and the course of phenomena, which would otherwise proceed regularly and uniformly, would become thereby confused and disconnected.\n\nFOURTH CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nThere exists either in, or in connection with the world—either as a part of it, or as the cause of it—an absolutely necessary being.\n\nPROOF.\n\nThe world of sense, as the sum total of all phenomena, contains a series of changes. For, without such a series, the mental representation of the series of time itself, as the condition of the possibility of the sensuous world, could not be presented to us.[[55]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-55) But every change stands under its condition, which precedes it in time and renders it necessary. Now the existence of a given condition presupposes a complete series of conditions up to the absolutely unconditioned, which alone is absolutely necessary. It follows that something that is absolutely necessary must exist, if change exists as its consequence. But this necessary thing itself belongs to the sensuous world. For suppose it to exist out of and apart from it, the series of cosmical changes would receive from it a beginning, and yet this necessary cause would not itself belong to the world of sense. But this is impossible. For, as the beginning of a series in time is determined only by that which precedes it in time, the supreme condition of the beginning of a series of changes must exist in the time in which this series itself did not exist; for a beginning supposes a time preceding, in which the thing that begins to be was not in existence. The causality of the necessary cause of changes, and consequently the cause itself, must for these reasons belong to time—and to phenomena, time being possible only as the form of phenomena. Consequently, it cannot be cogitated as separated from the world of sense—the sum total of all phenomena. There is, therefore, contained in the world, something that is absolutely necessary—whether it be the whole cosmical series itself, or only a part of it.\n\n[[55]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-55) Objectively, time, as the formal condition of the possibility of change, precedes all changes; but subjectively, and in consciousness, the representation of time, like every other, is given solely by occasion of perception.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nAn absolutely necessary being does not exist, either in the world, or out of it—as its cause.\n\nPROOF.\n\nGrant that either the world itself is necessary, or that there is contained in it a necessary existence. Two cases are possible. First, there must either be in the series of cosmical changes a beginning, which is unconditionally necessary, and therefore uncaused—which is at variance with the dynamical law of the determination of all phenomena in time; or, secondly, the series itself is without beginning, and, although contingent and conditioned in all its parts, is nevertheless absolutely necessary and unconditioned as a whole—which is self-contradictory. For the existence of an aggregate cannot be necessary, if no single part of it possesses necessary existence.\n\nGrant, on the other hand, that an absolutely necessary cause exists out of and apart from the world. This cause, as the highest member in the series of the causes of cosmical changes, must originate or begin[[56]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-56) the existence of the latter and their series. In this case it must also begin to act, and its causality would therefore belong to time, and consequently to the sum total of phenomena, that is, to the world. It follows that the cause cannot be out of the world; which is contradictory to the hypothesis. Therefore, neither in the world, nor out of it (but in causal connection with it), does there exist any absolutely necessary being.\n\n[[56]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-56) The word begin is taken in two senses. The first is active—the cause being regarded as beginning a series of conditions as its effect (infit). The second is passive—the causality in the cause itself beginning to operate (fit). I reason here from the first to the second.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE FOURTH ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.\n\nTo demonstrate the existence of a necessary being, I cannot be permitted in this place to employ any other than the cosmological argument, which ascends from the conditioned in phenomena to the unconditioned in conception—the unconditioned being considered the necessary condition of the absolute totality of the series. The proof, from the mere idea of a supreme being, belongs to another principle of reason and requires separate discussion.\n\nThe pure cosmological proof demonstrates the existence of a necessary being, but at the same time leaves it quite unsettled, whether this being is the world itself, or quite distinct from it. To establish the truth of the latter view, principles are requisite, which are not cosmological and do not proceed in the series of phenomena. We should require to introduce into our proof conceptions of contingent beings—regarded merely as objects of the understanding, and also a principle which enables us to connect these, by means of mere conceptions, with a necessary being. But the proper place for all such arguments is a transcendent philosophy, which has unhappily not yet been established.\n\nBut, if we begin our proof cosmologically, by laying at the foundation of it the series of phenomena, and the regress in it according to empirical laws of causality, we are not at liberty to break off from this mode of demonstration and to pass over to something which is not itself a member of the series. The condition must be taken in exactly the same signification as the relation of the conditioned to its condition in the series has been taken, for the series must conduct us in an unbroken regress to this supreme condition. But if this relation is sensuous, and belongs to the possible empirical employment of understanding, the supreme condition or cause must close the regressive series according to the laws of sensibility and consequently, must belong to the series of time. It follows that this necessary existence must be regarded as the highest member of the cosmical series.\n\nCertain philosophers have, nevertheless, allowed themselves the liberty of making such a saltus (metabasis eis allo gonos). From the changes in the world they have concluded their empirical contingency, that is, their dependence on empirically-determined causes, and they thus admitted an ascending series of empirical conditions: and in this they are quite right. But as they could not find in this series any primal beginning or any highest member, they passed suddenly from the empirical conception of contingency to the pure category, which presents us with a series—not sensuous, but intellectual—whose completeness does certainly rest upon the existence of an absolutely necessary cause. Nay, more, this intellectual series is not tied to any sensuous conditions; and is therefore free from the condition of time, which requires it spontaneously to begin its causality in time. But such a procedure is perfectly inadmissible, as will be made plain from what follows.\n\nIn the pure sense of the categories, that is contingent the contradictory opposite of which is possible. Now we cannot reason from empirical contingency to intellectual. The opposite of that which is changed—the opposite of its state—is actual at another time, and is therefore possible. Consequently, it is not the contradictory opposite of the former state. To be that, it is necessary that, in the same time in which the preceding state existed, its opposite could have existed in its place; but such a cognition is not given us in the mere phenomenon of change. A body that was in motion = A, comes into a state of rest = non-A. Now it cannot be concluded from the fact that a state opposite to the state A follows it, that the contradictory opposite of A is possible; and that A is therefore contingent. To prove this, we should require to know that the state of rest could have existed in the very same time in which the motion took place. Now we know nothing more than that the state of rest was actual in the time that followed the state of motion; consequently, that it was also possible. But motion at one time, and rest at another time, are not contradictorily opposed to each other. It follows from what has been said that the succession of opposite determinations, that is, change, does not demonstrate the fact of contingency as represented in the conceptions of the pure understanding; and that it cannot, therefore, conduct us to the fact of the existence of a necessary being. Change proves merely empirical contingency, that is to say, that the new state could not have existed without a cause, which belongs to the preceding time. This cause—even although it is regarded as absolutely necessary—must be presented to us in time, and must belong to the series of phenomena.\n\nON THE ANTITHESIS.\n\nThe difficulties which meet us, in our attempt to rise through the series of phenomena to the existence of an absolutely necessary supreme cause, must not originate from our inability to establish the truth of our mere conceptions of the necessary existence of a thing. That is to say, our objections not be ontological, but must be directed against the causal connection with a series of phenomena of a condition which is itself unconditioned. In one word, they must be cosmological and relate to empirical laws. We must show that the regress in the series of causes (in the world of sense) cannot conclude with an empirically unconditioned condition, and that the cosmological argument from the contingency of the cosmical state—a contingency alleged to arise from change—does not justify us in accepting a first cause, that is, a prime originator of the cosmical series.\n\nThe reader will observe in this antinomy a very remarkable contrast. The very same grounds of proof which established in the thesis the existence of a supreme being, demonstrated in the antithesis—and with equal strictness—the non-existence of such a being. We found, first, that a necessary being exists, because the whole time past contains the series of all conditions, and with it, therefore, the unconditioned (the necessary); secondly, that there does not exist any necessary being, for the same reason, that the whole time past contains the series of all conditions—which are themselves, therefore, in the aggregate, conditioned. The cause of this seeming incongruity is as follows. We attend, in the first argument, solely to the absolute totality of the series of conditions, the one of which determines the other in time, and thus arrive at a necessary unconditioned. In the second, we consider, on the contrary, the contingency of everything that is determined in the series of time—for every event is preceded by a time, in which the condition itself must be determined as conditioned—and thus everything that is unconditioned or absolutely necessary disappears. In both, the mode of proof is quite in accordance with the common procedure of human reason, which often falls into discord with itself, from considering an object from two different points of view. Herr von Mairan regarded the controversy between two celebrated astronomers, which arose from a similar difficulty as to the choice of a proper standpoint, as a phenomenon of sufficient importance to warrant a separate treatise on the subject. The one concluded: the moon revolves on its own axis, because it constantly presents the same side to the earth; the other declared that the moon does not revolve on its own axis, for the same reason. Both conclusions were perfectly correct, according to the point of view from which the motions of the moon were considered.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","text":"### Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason\n\nThetic is the term applied to every collection of dogmatical propositions. By antithetic I do not understand dogmatical assertions of the opposite, but the self-contradiction of seemingly dogmatical cognitions (thesis cum antithesis), in none of which we can discover any decided superiority. Antithetic is not, therefore, occupied with one-sided statements, but is engaged in considering the contradictory nature of the general cognitions of reason and its causes. Transcendental antithetic is an investigation into the antinomy of pure reason, its causes and result. If we employ our reason not merely in the application of the principles of the understanding to objects of experience, but venture with it beyond these boundaries, there arise certain sophistical propositions or theorems. These assertions have the following peculiarities: They can find neither confirmation nor confutation in experience; and each is in itself not only self-consistent, but possesses conditions of its necessity in the very nature of reason—only that, unluckily, there exist just as valid and necessary grounds for maintaining the contrary proposition.\n\nThe questions which naturally arise in the consideration of this dialectic of pure reason, are therefore: 1st. In what propositions is pure reason unavoidably subject to an antinomy? 2nd. What are the causes of this antinomy? 3rd. Whether and in what way can reason free itself from this self-contradiction?","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"62.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","text":"A dialectical proposition or theorem of pure reason must, according to what has been said, be distinguishable from all sophistical propositions, by the fact that it is not an answer to an arbitrary question, which may be raised at the mere pleasure of any person, but to one which human reason must necessarily encounter in its progress. In the second place, a dialectical proposition, with its opposite, does not carry the appearance of a merely artificial illusion, which disappears as soon as it is investigated, but a natural and unavoidable illusion, which, even when we are no longer deceived by it, continues to mock us and, although rendered harmless, can never be completely removed.\n\nThis dialectical doctrine will not relate to the unity of understanding in empirical conceptions, but to the unity of reason in pure ideas. The conditions of this doctrine are—inasmuch as it must, as a synthesis according to rules, be conformable to the understanding, and at the same time as the absolute unity of the synthesis, to the reason—that, if it is adequate to the unity of reason, it is too great for the understanding, if according with the understanding, it is too small for the reason. Hence arises a mutual opposition, which cannot be avoided, do what we will.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"62.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0003","text":"These sophistical assertions of dialectic open, as it were, a battle-field, where that side obtains the victory which has been permitted to make the attack, and he is compelled to yield who has been unfortunately obliged to stand on the defensive. And hence, champions of ability, whether on the right or on the wrong side, are certain to carry away the crown of victory, if they only take care to have the right to make the last attack, and are not obliged to sustain another onset from their opponent. We can easily believe that this arena has been often trampled by the feet of combatants, that many victories have been obtained on both sides, but that the last victory, decisive of the affair between the contending parties, was won by him who fought for the right, only if his adversary was forbidden to continue the tourney. As impartial umpires, we must lay aside entirely the consideration whether the combatants are fighting for the right or for the wrong side, for the true or for the false, and allow the combat to be first decided. Perhaps, after they have wearied more than injured each other, they will discover the nothingness of their cause of quarrel and part good friends.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"62.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","text":"This method of watching, or rather of originating, a conflict of assertions, not for the purpose of finally deciding in favour of either side, but to discover whether the object of the struggle is not a mere illusion, which each strives in vain to reach, but which would be no gain even when reached—this procedure, I say, may be termed the sceptical method. It is thoroughly distinct from scepticism—the principle of a technical and scientific ignorance, which undermines the foundations of all knowledge, in order, if possible, to destroy our belief and confidence therein. For the sceptical method aims at certainty, by endeavouring to discover in a conflict of this kind, conducted honestly and intelligently on both sides, the point of misunderstanding; just as wise legislators derive, from the embarrassment of judges in lawsuits, information in regard to the defective and ill-defined parts of their statutes. The antinomy which reveals itself in the application of laws, is for our limited wisdom the best criterion of legislation. For the attention of reason, which in abstract speculation does not easily become conscious of its errors, is thus roused to the momenta in the determination of its principles.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"62.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","text":"But this sceptical method is essentially peculiar to transcendental philosophy, and can perhaps be dispensed with in every other field of investigation. In mathematics its use would be absurd; because in it no false assertions can long remain hidden, inasmuch as its demonstrations must always proceed under the guidance of pure intuition, and by means of an always evident synthesis. In experimental philosophy, doubt and delay may be very useful; but no misunderstanding is possible, which cannot be easily removed; and in experience means of solving the difficulty and putting an end to the dissension must at last be found, whether sooner or later. Moral philosophy can always exhibit its principles, with their practical consequences, in concreto—at least in possible experiences, and thus escape the mistakes and ambiguities of abstraction. But transcendental propositions, which lay claim to insight beyond the region of possible experience, cannot, on the one hand, exhibit their abstract synthesis in any à priori intuition, nor, on the other, expose a lurking error by the help of experience. Transcendental reason, therefore, presents us with no other criterion than that of an attempt to reconcile such assertions, and for this purpose to permit a free and unrestrained conflict between them. And this we now proceed to arrange.[[50]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-50)\n\n[[50]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-50) The antinomies stand in the order of the four transcendental ideas above detailed.\n\nFIRST CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nThe world has a beginning in time, and is also limited in regard to space.\n\nPROOF.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"62.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0006","text":"Granted that the world has no beginning in time; up to every given moment of time, an eternity must have elapsed, and therewith passed away an infinite series of successive conditions or states of things in the world. Now the infinity of a series consists in the fact that it never can be completed by means of a successive synthesis. It follows that an infinite series already elapsed is impossible and that, consequently, a beginning of the world is a necessary condition of its existence. And this was the first thing to be proved.\n\nAs regards the second, let us take the opposite for granted. In this case, the world must be an infinite given total of coexistent things. Now we cannot cogitate the dimensions of a quantity, which is not given within certain limits of an intuition,[[51]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-51) in any other way than by means of the synthesis of its parts, and the total of such a quantity only by means of a completed synthesis, or the repeated addition of unity to itself. Accordingly, to cogitate the world, which fills all spaces, as a whole, the successive synthesis of the parts of an infinite world must be looked upon as completed, that is to say, an infinite time must be regarded as having elapsed in the enumeration of all co-existing things; which is impossible. For this reason an infinite aggregate of actual things cannot be considered as a given whole, consequently, not as a contemporaneously given whole. The world is consequently, as regards extension in space, not infinite, but enclosed in limits. And this was the second thing to be proved.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"62.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0007","text":"[[51]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-51) We may consider an undetermined quantity as a whole, when it is enclosed within limits, although we cannot construct or ascertain its totality by measurement, that is, by the successive synthesis of its parts. For its limits of themselves determine its completeness as a whole.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nThe world has no beginning, and no limits in space, but is, in relation both to time and space, infinite.\n\nPROOF.\n\nFor let it be granted that it has a beginning. A beginning is an existence which is preceded by a time in which the thing does not exist. On the above supposition, it follows that there must have been a time in which the world did not exist, that is, a void time. But in a void time the origination of a thing is impossible; because no part of any such time contains a distinctive condition of being, in preference to that of non-being (whether the supposed thing originate of itself, or by means of some other cause). Consequently, many series of things may have a beginning in the world, but the world itself cannot have a beginning, and is, therefore, in relation to past time, infinite.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"62.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0008","text":"As regards the second statement, let us first take the opposite for granted—that the world is finite and limited in space; it follows that it must exist in a void space, which is not limited. We should therefore meet not only with a relation of things in space, but also a relation of things to space. Now, as the world is an absolute whole, out of and beyond which no object of intuition, and consequently no correlate to which can be discovered, this relation of the world to a void space is merely a relation to no object. But such a relation, and consequently the limitation of the world by void space, is nothing. Consequently, the world, as regards space, is not limited, that is, it is infinite in regard to extension.[[52]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-52)","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"62.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0009","text":"[[52]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-52) Space is merely the form of external intuition (formal intuition), and not a real object which can be externally perceived. Space, prior to all things which determine it (fill or limit it), or, rather, which present an empirical intuition conformable to it, is, under the title of absolute space, nothing but the mere possibility of external phenomena, in so far as they either exist in themselves, or can annex themselves to given intuitions. Empirical intuition is therefore not a composition of phenomena and space (of perception and empty intuition). The one is not the correlate of the other in a synthesis, but they are vitally connected in the same empirical intuition, as matter and form. If we wish to set one of these two apart from the other—space from phenomena—there arise all sorts of empty determinations of external intuition, which are very far from being possible perceptions. For example, motion or rest of the world in an infinite empty space, or a determination of the mutual relation of both, cannot possibly be perceived, and is therefore merely the predicate of a notional entity.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE FIRST ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.\n\nIn bringing forward these conflicting arguments, I have not been on the search for sophisms, for the purpose of availing myself of special pleading, which takes advantage of the carelessness of the opposite party, appeals to a misunderstood statute, and erects its unrighteous claims upon an unfair interpretation. Both proofs originate fairly from the nature of the case, and the advantage presented by the mistakes of the dogmatists of both parties has been completely set aside.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"62.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0010","text":"The thesis might also have been unfairly demonstrated, by the introduction of an erroneous conception of the infinity of a given quantity. A quantity is infinite, if a greater than itself cannot possibly exist. The quantity is measured by the number of given units—which are taken as a standard—contained in it. Now no number can be the greatest, because one or more units can always be added. It follows that an infinite given quantity, consequently an infinite world (both as regards time and extension) is impossible. It is, therefore, limited in both respects. In this manner I might have conducted my proof; but the conception given in it does not agree with the true conception of an infinite whole. In this there is no representation of its quantity, it is not said how large it is; consequently its conception is not the conception of a maximum. We cogitate in it merely its relation to an arbitrarily assumed unit, in relation to which it is greater than any number. Now, just as the unit which is taken is greater or smaller, the infinite will be greater or smaller; but the infinity, which consists merely in the relation to this given unit, must remain always the same, although the absolute quantity of the whole is not thereby cognized.\n\nThe true (transcendental) conception of infinity is: that the successive synthesis of unity in the measurement of a given quantum can never be completed.[[53]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-53) Hence it follows, without possibility of mistake, that an eternity of actual successive states up to a given (the present) moment cannot have elapsed, and that the world must therefore have a beginning.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"62.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0011","text":"[[53]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-53) The quantum in this sense contains a congeries of given units, which is greater than any number—and this is the mathematical conception of the infinite.\n\nIn regard to the second part of the thesis, the difficulty as to an infinite and yet elapsed series disappears; for the manifold of a world infinite in extension is contemporaneously given. But, in order to cogitate the total of this manifold, as we cannot have the aid of limits constituting by themselves this total in intuition, we are obliged to give some account of our conception, which in this case cannot proceed from the whole to the determined quantity of the parts, but must demonstrate the possibility of a whole by means of a successive synthesis of the parts. But as this synthesis must constitute a series that cannot be completed, it is impossible for us to cogitate prior to it, and consequently not by means of it, a totality. For the conception of totality itself is in the present case the representation of a completed synthesis of the parts; and this completion, and consequently its conception, is impossible.\n\nON THE ANTITHESIS.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"62.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0012","text":"The proof in favour of the infinity of the cosmical succession and the cosmical content is based upon the consideration that, in the opposite case, a void time and a void space must constitute the limits of the world. Now I am not unaware, that there are some ways of escaping this conclusion. It may, for example, be alleged, that a limit to the world, as regards both space and time, is quite possible, without at the same time holding the existence of an absolute time before the beginning of the world, or an absolute space extending beyond the actual world—which is impossible. I am quite well satisfied with the latter part of this opinion of the philosophers of the Leibnitzian school. Space is merely the form of external intuition, but not a real object which can itself be externally intuited; it is not a correlate of phenomena, it is the form of phenomena itself. Space, therefore, cannot be regarded as absolutely and in itself something determinative of the existence of things, because it is not itself an object, but only the form of possible objects. Consequently, things, as phenomena, determine space; that is to say, they render it possible that, of all the possible predicates of space (size and relation), certain may belong to reality. But we cannot affirm the converse, that space, as something self-subsistent, can determine real things in regard to size or shape, for it is in itself not a real thing. Space (filled or void)[[54]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-54) may therefore be limited by phenomena, but phenomena cannot be limited by an empty space without them. This is true of time also. All this being granted, it is nevertheless indisputable, that we must assume these two nonentities, void space without and void time before the world, if we assume the existence of cosmical limits, relatively to space or time.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"62.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0013","text":"[[54]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-54) It is evident that what is meant here is, that empty space, in so far as it is limited by phenomena—space, that is, within the world—does not at least contradict transcendental principles, and may therefore, as regards them, be admitted, although its possibility cannot on that account be affirmed.\n\nFor, as regards the subterfuge adopted by those who endeavour to evade the consequence—that, if the world is limited as to space and time, the infinite void must determine the existence of actual things in regard to their dimensions—it arises solely from the fact that instead of a sensuous world, an intelligible world—of which nothing is known—is cogitated; instead of a real beginning (an existence, which is preceded by a period in which nothing exists), an existence which presupposes no other condition than that of time; and, instead of limits of extension, boundaries of the universe. But the question relates to the mundus phaenomenon, and its quantity; and in this case we cannot make abstraction of the conditions of sensibility, without doing away with the essential reality of this world itself. The world of sense, if it is limited, must necessarily lie in the infinite void. If this, and with it space as the à priori condition of the possibility of phenomena, is left out of view, the whole world of sense disappears. In our problem is this alone considered as given. The mundus intelligibilis is nothing but the general conception of a world, in which abstraction has been made of all conditions of intuition, and in relation to which no synthetical proposition—either affirmative or negative—is possible.\n\nSECOND CONFLICT OF TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"62.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0014","text":"Every composite substance in the world consists of simple parts; and there exists nothing that is not either itself simple, or composed of simple parts.\n\nPROOF.\n\nFor, grant that composite substances do not consist of simple parts; in this case, if all combination or composition were annihilated in thought, no composite part, and (as, by the supposition, there do not exist simple parts) no simple part would exist. Consequently, no substance; consequently, nothing would exist. Either, then, it is impossible to annihilate composition in thought; or, after such annihilation, there must remain something that subsists without composition, that is, something that is simple. But in the former case the composite could not itself consist of substances, because with substances composition is merely a contingent relation, apart from which they must still exist as self-subsistent beings. Now, as this case contradicts the supposition, the second must contain the truth—that the substantial composite in the world consists of simple parts.\n\nIt follows, as an immediate inference, that the things in the world are all, without exception, simple beings—that composition is merely an external condition pertaining to them—and that, although we never can separate and isolate the elementary substances from the state of composition, reason must cogitate these as the primary subjects of all composition, and consequently, as prior thereto—and as simple substances.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nNo composite thing in the world consists of simple parts; and there does not exist in the world any simple substance.\n\nPROOF.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"62.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0015","text":"Let it be supposed that a composite thing (as substance) consists of simple parts. Inasmuch as all external relation, consequently all composition of substances, is possible only in space; the space, occupied by that which is composite, must consist of the same number of parts as is contained in the composite. But space does not consist of simple parts, but of spaces. Therefore, every part of the composite must occupy a space. But the absolutely primary parts of what is composite are simple. It follows that what is simple occupies a space. Now, as everything real that occupies a space, contains a manifold the parts of which are external to each other, and is consequently composite—and a real composite, not of accidents (for these cannot exist external to each other apart from substance), but of substances—it follows that the simple must be a substantial composite, which is self-contradictory.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"62.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0016","text":"The second proposition of the antithesis—that there exists in the world nothing that is simple—is here equivalent to the following: The existence of the absolutely simple cannot be demonstrated from any experience or perception either external or internal; and the absolutely simple is a mere idea, the objective reality of which cannot be demonstrated in any possible experience; it is consequently, in the exposition of phenomena, without application and object. For, let us take for granted that an object may be found in experience for this transcendental idea; the empirical intuition of such an object must then be recognized to contain absolutely no manifold with its parts external to each other, and connected into unity. Now, as we cannot reason from the non-consciousness of such a manifold to the impossibility of its existence in the intuition of an object, and as the proof of this impossibility is necessary for the establishment and proof of absolute simplicity; it follows that this simplicity cannot be inferred from any perception whatever. As, therefore, an absolutely simple object cannot be given in any experience, and the world of sense must be considered as the sum total of all possible experiences: nothing simple exists in the world.\n\nThis second proposition in the antithesis has a more extended aim than the first. The first merely banishes the simple from the intuition of the composite; while the second drives it entirely out of nature. Hence we were unable to demonstrate it from the conception of a given object of external intuition (of the composite), but we were obliged to prove it from the relation of a given object to a possible experience in general.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE SECOND ANTINOMY. THESIS.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"62.16"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0017","text":"When I speak of a whole, which necessarily consists of simple parts, I understand thereby only a substantial whole, as the true composite; that is to say, I understand that contingent unity of the manifold which is given as perfectly isolated (at least in thought), placed in reciprocal connection, and thus constituted a unity. Space ought not to be called a compositum but a totum, for its parts are possible in the whole, and not the whole by means of the parts. It might perhaps be called a compositum ideale, but not a compositum reale. But this is of no importance. As space is not a composite of substances (and not even of real accidents), if I abstract all composition therein—nothing, not even a point, remains; for a point is possible only as the limit of a space—consequently of a composite. Space and time, therefore, do not consist of simple parts. That which belongs only to the condition or state of a substance, even although it possesses a quantity (motion or change, for example), likewise does not consist of simple parts. That is to say, a certain degree of change does not originate from the addition of many simple changes. Our inference of the simple from the composite is valid only of self-subsisting things. But the accidents of a state are not self-subsistent. The proof, then, for the necessity of the simple, as the component part of all that is substantial and composite, may prove a failure, and the whole case of this thesis be lost, if we carry the proposition too far, and wish to make it valid of everything that is composite without distinction—as indeed has really now and then happened. Besides, I am here speaking only of the simple, in so far as it is necessarily given in the composite—the latter being capable of solution into the former as its component parts. The proper signification of the word monas (as employed by Leibnitz) ought to relate to the simple, given immediately as simple substance (for example, in consciousness), and not as an element of the composite. As an element, the term atomus would be more appropriate. And as I wish to prove the existence of simple substances, only in relation to, and as the elements of, the composite, I might term the antithesis of the second Antinomy, transcendental Atomistic. But as this word has long been employed to designate a particular theory of corporeal phenomena (moleculae), and thus presupposes a basis of empirical conceptions, I prefer calling it the dialectical principle of Monadology.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"62.17"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0018","text":"ANTITHESIS.\n\nAgainst the assertion of the infinite subdivisibility of matter whose ground of proof is purely mathematical, objections have been alleged by the Monadists. These objections lay themselves open, at first sight, to suspicion, from the fact that they do not recognize the clearest mathematical proofs as propositions relating to the constitution of space, in so far as it is really the formal condition of the possibility of all matter, but regard them merely as inferences from abstract but arbitrary conceptions, which cannot have any application to real things. Just as if it were possible to imagine another mode of intuition than that given in the primitive intuition of space; and just as if its à priori determinations did not apply to everything, the existence of which is possible, from the fact alone of its filling space. If we listen to them, we shall find ourselves required to cogitate, in addition to the mathematical point, which is simple—not, however, a part, but a mere limit of space—physical points, which are indeed likewise simple, but possess the peculiar property, as parts of space, of filling it merely by their aggregation. I shall not repeat here the common and clear refutations of this absurdity, which are to be found everywhere in numbers: every one knows that it is impossible to undermine the evidence of mathematics by mere discursive conceptions; I shall only remark that, if in this case philosophy endeavours to gain an advantage over mathematics by sophistical artifices, it is because it forgets that the discussion relates solely to Phenomena and their conditions.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"62.18"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0019","text":"It is not sufficient to find the conception of the simple for the pure conception of the composite, but we must discover for the intuition of the composite (matter), the intuition of the simple. Now this, according to the laws of sensibility, and consequently in the case of objects of sense, is utterly impossible. In the case of a whole composed of substances, which is cogitated solely by the pure understanding, it may be necessary to be in possession of the simple before composition is possible. But this does not hold good of the Totum substantiale phaenomenon, which, as an empirical intuition in space, possesses the necessary property of containing no simple part, for the very reason that no part of space is simple. Meanwhile, the Monadists have been subtle enough to escape from this difficulty, by presupposing intuition and the dynamical relation of substances as the condition of the possibility of space, instead of regarding space as the condition of the possibility of the objects of external intuition, that is, of bodies. Now we have a conception of bodies only as phenomena, and, as such, they necessarily presuppose space as the condition of all external phenomena. The evasion is therefore in vain; as, indeed, we have sufficiently shown in our Æsthetic. If bodies were things in themselves, the proof of the Monadists would be unexceptionable.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"62.19"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0020","text":"The second dialectical assertion possesses the peculiarity of having opposed to it a dogmatical proposition, which, among all such sophistical statements, is the only one that undertakes to prove in the case of an object of experience, that which is properly a transcendental idea—the absolute simplicity of substance. The proposition is that the object of the internal sense, the thinking Ego, is an absolute simple substance. Without at present entering upon this subject—as it has been considered at length in a former chapter—I shall merely remark that, if something is cogitated merely as an object, without the addition of any synthetical determination of its intuition—as happens in the case of the bare representation, _I_—it is certain that no manifold and no composition can be perceived in such a representation. As, moreover, the predicates whereby I cogitate this object are merely intuitions of the internal sense, there cannot be discovered in them anything to prove the existence of a manifold whose parts are external to each other, and, consequently, nothing to prove the existence of real composition. Consciousness, therefore, is so constituted that, inasmuch as the thinking subject is at the same time its own object, it cannot divide itself—although it can divide its inhering determinations. For every object in relation to itself is absolute unity. Nevertheless, if the subject is regarded externally, as an object of intuition, it must, in its character of phenomenon, possess the property of composition. And it must always be regarded in this manner, if we wish to know whether there is or is not contained in it a manifold whose parts are external to each other.\n\nTHIRD CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"62.20"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0021","text":"Causality according to the laws of nature, is not the only causality operating to originate the phenomena of the world. A causality of freedom is also necessary to account fully for these phenomena.\n\nPROOF.\n\nLet it be supposed, that there is no other kind of causality than that according to the laws of nature. Consequently, everything that happens presupposes a previous condition, which it follows with absolute certainty, in conformity with a rule. But this previous condition must itself be something that has happened (that has arisen in time, as it did not exist before), for, if it has always been in existence, its consequence or effect would not thus originate for the first time, but would likewise have always existed. The causality, therefore, of a cause, whereby something happens, is itself a thing that has happened. Now this again presupposes, in conformity with the law of nature, a previous condition and its causality, and this another anterior to the former, and so on. If, then, everything happens solely in accordance with the laws of nature, there cannot be any real first beginning of things, but only a subaltern or comparative beginning. There cannot, therefore, be a completeness of series on the side of the causes which originate the one from the other. But the law of nature is that nothing can happen without a sufficient à priori determined cause. The proposition therefore—if all causality is possible only in accordance with the laws of nature—is, when stated in this unlimited and general manner, self-contradictory. It follows that this cannot be the only kind of causality.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"62.21"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0022","text":"From what has been said, it follows that a causality must be admitted, by means of which something happens, without its cause being determined according to necessary laws by some other cause preceding. That is to say, there must exist an absolute spontaneity of cause, which of itself originates a series of phenomena which proceeds according to natural laws—consequently transcendental freedom, without which even in the course of nature the succession of phenomena on the side of causes is never complete.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nThere is no such thing as freedom, but everything in the world happens solely according to the laws of nature.\n\nPROOF.\n\nGranted, that there does exist freedom in the transcendental sense, as a peculiar kind of causality, operating to produce events in the world—a faculty, that is to say, of originating a state, and consequently a series of consequences from that state. In this case, not only the series originated by this spontaneity, but the determination of this spontaneity itself to the production of the series, that is to say, the causality itself must have an absolute commencement, such that nothing can precede to determine this action according to unvarying laws. But every beginning of action presupposes in the acting cause a state of inaction; and a dynamically primal beginning of action presupposes a state, which has no connection—as regards causality—with the preceding state of the cause—which does not, that is, in any wise result from it. Transcendental freedom is therefore opposed to the natural law of cause and effect, and such a conjunction of successive states in effective causes is destructive of the possibility of unity in experience and for that reason not to be found in experience—is consequently a mere fiction of thought.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"62.22"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0023","text":"We have, therefore, nothing but nature to which we must look for connection and order in cosmical events. Freedom—independence of the laws of nature—is certainly a deliverance from restraint, but it is also a relinquishing of the guidance of law and rule. For it cannot be alleged that, instead of the laws of nature, laws of freedom may be introduced into the causality of the course of nature. For, if freedom were determined according to laws, it would be no longer freedom, but merely nature. Nature, therefore, and transcendental freedom are distinguishable as conformity to law and lawlessness. The former imposes upon understanding the difficulty of seeking the origin of events ever higher and higher in the series of causes, inasmuch as causality is always conditioned thereby; while it compensates this labour by the guarantee of a unity complete and in conformity with law. The latter, on the contrary, holds out to the understanding the promise of a point of rest in the chain of causes, by conducting it to an unconditioned causality, which professes to have the power of spontaneous origination, but which, in its own utter blindness, deprives it of the guidance of rules, by which alone a completely connected experience is possible.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE THIRD ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"62.23"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0024","text":"The transcendental idea of freedom is far from constituting the entire content of the psychological conception so termed, which is for the most part empirical. It merely presents us with the conception of spontaneity of action, as the proper ground for imputing freedom to the cause of a certain class of objects. It is, however, the true stumbling-stone to philosophy, which meets with unconquerable difficulties in the way of its admitting this kind of unconditioned causality. That element in the question of the freedom of the will, which has for so long a time placed speculative reason in such perplexity, is properly only transcendental, and concerns the question, whether there must be held to exist a faculty of spontaneous origination of a series of successive things or states. How such a faculty is possible is not a necessary inquiry; for in the case of natural causality itself, we are obliged to content ourselves with the à priori knowledge that such a causality must be presupposed, although we are quite incapable of comprehending how the being of one thing is possible through the being of another, but must for this information look entirely to experience. Now we have demonstrated this necessity of a free first beginning of a series of phenomena, only in so far as it is required for the comprehension of an origin of the world, all following states being regarded as a succession according to laws of nature alone. But, as there has thus been proved the existence of a faculty which can of itself originate a series in time—although we are unable to explain how it can exist—we feel ourselves authorized to admit, even in the midst of the natural course of events, a beginning, as regards causality, of different successions of phenomena, and at the same time to attribute to all substances a faculty of free action. But we ought in this case not to allow ourselves to fall into a common misunderstanding, and to suppose that, because a successive series in the world can only have a comparatively first beginning—another state or condition of things always preceding—an absolutely first beginning of a series in the course of nature is impossible. For we are not speaking here of an absolutely first beginning in relation to time, but as regards causality alone. When, for example, I, completely of my own free will, and independently of the necessarily determinative influence of natural causes, rise from my chair, there commences with this event, including its material consequences in infinitum, an absolutely new series; although, in relation to time, this event is merely the continuation of a preceding series. For this resolution and act of mine do not form part of the succession of effects in nature, and are not mere continuations of it; on the contrary, the determining causes of nature cease to operate in reference to this event, which certainly succeeds the acts of nature, but does not proceed from them. For these reasons, the action of a free agent must be termed, in regard to causality, if not in relation to time, an absolutely primal beginning of a series of phenomena.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"62.24"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0025","text":"The justification of this need of reason to rest upon a free act as the first beginning of the series of natural causes is evident from the fact, that all philosophers of antiquity (with the exception of the Epicurean school) felt themselves obliged, when constructing a theory of the motions of the universe, to accept a prime mover, that is, a freely acting cause, which spontaneously and prior to all other causes evolved this series of states. They always felt the need of going beyond mere nature, for the purpose of making a first beginning comprehensible.\n\nON THE ANTITHESIS.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"62.25"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0026","text":"The assertor of the all-sufficiency of nature in regard to causality (transcendental Physiocracy), in opposition to the doctrine of freedom, would defend his view of the question somewhat in the following manner. He would say, in answer to the sophistical arguments of the opposite party: If you do not accept a mathematical first, in relation to time, you have no need to seek a dynamical first, in regard to causality. Who compelled you to imagine an absolutely primal condition of the world, and therewith an absolute beginning of the gradually progressing successions of phenomena—and, as some foundation for this fancy of yours, to set bounds to unlimited nature? Inasmuch as the substances in the world have always existed—at least the unity of experience renders such a supposition quite necessary—there is no difficulty in believing also, that the changes in the conditions of these substances have always existed; and, consequently, that a first beginning, mathematical or dynamical, is by no means required. The possibility of such an infinite derivation, without any initial member from which all the others result, is certainly quite incomprehensible. But, if you are rash enough to deny the enigmatical secrets of nature for this reason, you will find yourselves obliged to deny also the existence of many fundamental properties of natural objects (such as fundamental forces), which you can just as little comprehend; and even the possibility of so simple a conception as that of change must present to you insuperable difficulties. For if experience did not teach you that it was real, you never could conceive à priori the possibility of this ceaseless sequence of being and non-being.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"62.26"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0027","text":"But if the existence of a transcendental faculty of freedom is granted—a faculty of originating changes in the world—this faculty must at least exist out of and apart from the world; although it is certainly a bold assumption, that, over and above the complete content of all possible intuitions, there still exists an object which cannot be presented in any possible perception. But, to attribute to substances in the world itself such a faculty, is quite inadmissible; for, in this case; the connection of phenomena reciprocally determining and determined according to general laws, which is termed nature, and along with it the criteria of empirical truth, which enable us to distinguish experience from mere visionary dreaming, would almost entirely disappear. In proximity with such a lawless faculty of freedom, a system of nature is hardly cogitable; for the laws of the latter would be continually subject to the intrusive influences of the former, and the course of phenomena, which would otherwise proceed regularly and uniformly, would become thereby confused and disconnected.\n\nFOURTH CONFLICT OF THE TRANSCENDENTAL IDEAS. THESIS.\n\nThere exists either in, or in connection with the world—either as a part of it, or as the cause of it—an absolutely necessary being.\n\nPROOF.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"62.27"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0028","text":"The world of sense, as the sum total of all phenomena, contains a series of changes. For, without such a series, the mental representation of the series of time itself, as the condition of the possibility of the sensuous world, could not be presented to us.[[55]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-55) But every change stands under its condition, which precedes it in time and renders it necessary. Now the existence of a given condition presupposes a complete series of conditions up to the absolutely unconditioned, which alone is absolutely necessary. It follows that something that is absolutely necessary must exist, if change exists as its consequence. But this necessary thing itself belongs to the sensuous world. For suppose it to exist out of and apart from it, the series of cosmical changes would receive from it a beginning, and yet this necessary cause would not itself belong to the world of sense. But this is impossible. For, as the beginning of a series in time is determined only by that which precedes it in time, the supreme condition of the beginning of a series of changes must exist in the time in which this series itself did not exist; for a beginning supposes a time preceding, in which the thing that begins to be was not in existence. The causality of the necessary cause of changes, and consequently the cause itself, must for these reasons belong to time—and to phenomena, time being possible only as the form of phenomena. Consequently, it cannot be cogitated as separated from the world of sense—the sum total of all phenomena. There is, therefore, contained in the world, something that is absolutely necessary—whether it be the whole cosmical series itself, or only a part of it.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"62.28"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0029","text":"[[55]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-55) Objectively, time, as the formal condition of the possibility of change, precedes all changes; but subjectively, and in consciousness, the representation of time, like every other, is given solely by occasion of perception.\n\nANTITHESIS.\n\nAn absolutely necessary being does not exist, either in the world, or out of it—as its cause.\n\nPROOF.\n\nGrant that either the world itself is necessary, or that there is contained in it a necessary existence. Two cases are possible. First, there must either be in the series of cosmical changes a beginning, which is unconditionally necessary, and therefore uncaused—which is at variance with the dynamical law of the determination of all phenomena in time; or, secondly, the series itself is without beginning, and, although contingent and conditioned in all its parts, is nevertheless absolutely necessary and unconditioned as a whole—which is self-contradictory. For the existence of an aggregate cannot be necessary, if no single part of it possesses necessary existence.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"62.29"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0030","text":"Grant, on the other hand, that an absolutely necessary cause exists out of and apart from the world. This cause, as the highest member in the series of the causes of cosmical changes, must originate or begin[[56]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-56) the existence of the latter and their series. In this case it must also begin to act, and its causality would therefore belong to time, and consequently to the sum total of phenomena, that is, to the world. It follows that the cause cannot be out of the world; which is contradictory to the hypothesis. Therefore, neither in the world, nor out of it (but in causal connection with it), does there exist any absolutely necessary being.\n\n[[56]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-56) The word begin is taken in two senses. The first is active—the cause being regarded as beginning a series of conditions as its effect (infit). The second is passive—the causality in the cause itself beginning to operate (fit). I reason here from the first to the second.\n\nOBSERVATIONS ON THE FOURTH ANTINOMY. ON THE THESIS.\n\nTo demonstrate the existence of a necessary being, I cannot be permitted in this place to employ any other than the cosmological argument, which ascends from the conditioned in phenomena to the unconditioned in conception—the unconditioned being considered the necessary condition of the absolute totality of the series. The proof, from the mere idea of a supreme being, belongs to another principle of reason and requires separate discussion.","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"62.30"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0031","text":"The pure cosmological proof demonstrates the existence of a necessary being, but at the same time leaves it quite unsettled, whether this being is the world itself, or quite distinct from it. To establish the truth of the latter view, principles are requisite, which are not cosmological and do not proceed in the series of phenomena. We should require to introduce into our proof conceptions of contingent beings—regarded merely as objects of the understanding, and also a principle which enables us to connect these, by means of mere conceptions, with a necessary being. But the proper place for all such arguments is a transcendent philosophy, which has unhappily not yet been established.\n\nBut, if we begin our proof cosmologically, by laying at the foundation of it the series of phenomena, and the regress in it according to empirical laws of causality, we are not at liberty to break off from this mode of demonstration and to pass over to something which is not itself a member of the series. The condition must be taken in exactly the same signification as the relation of the conditioned to its condition in the series has been taken, for the series must conduct us in an unbroken regress to this supreme condition. But if this relation is sensuous, and belongs to the possible empirical employment of understanding, the supreme condition or cause must close the regressive series according to the laws of sensibility and consequently, must belong to the series of time. It follows that this necessary existence must be regarded as the highest member of the cosmical series.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"62.31"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0032","text":"Certain philosophers have, nevertheless, allowed themselves the liberty of making such a saltus (metabasis eis allo gonos). From the changes in the world they have concluded their empirical contingency, that is, their dependence on empirically-determined causes, and they thus admitted an ascending series of empirical conditions: and in this they are quite right. But as they could not find in this series any primal beginning or any highest member, they passed suddenly from the empirical conception of contingency to the pure category, which presents us with a series—not sensuous, but intellectual—whose completeness does certainly rest upon the existence of an absolutely necessary cause. Nay, more, this intellectual series is not tied to any sensuous conditions; and is therefore free from the condition of time, which requires it spontaneously to begin its causality in time. But such a procedure is perfectly inadmissible, as will be made plain from what follows.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"62.32"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0033","text":"In the pure sense of the categories, that is contingent the contradictory opposite of which is possible. Now we cannot reason from empirical contingency to intellectual. The opposite of that which is changed—the opposite of its state—is actual at another time, and is therefore possible. Consequently, it is not the contradictory opposite of the former state. To be that, it is necessary that, in the same time in which the preceding state existed, its opposite could have existed in its place; but such a cognition is not given us in the mere phenomenon of change. A body that was in motion = A, comes into a state of rest = non-A. Now it cannot be concluded from the fact that a state opposite to the state A follows it, that the contradictory opposite of A is possible; and that A is therefore contingent. To prove this, we should require to know that the state of rest could have existed in the very same time in which the motion took place. Now we know nothing more than that the state of rest was actual in the time that followed the state of motion; consequently, that it was also possible. But motion at one time, and rest at another time, are not contradictorily opposed to each other. It follows from what has been said that the succession of opposite determinations, that is, change, does not demonstrate the fact of contingency as represented in the conceptions of the pure understanding; and that it cannot, therefore, conduct us to the fact of the existence of a necessary being. Change proves merely empirical contingency, that is to say, that the new state could not have existed without a cause, which belongs to the preceding time. This cause—even although it is regarded as absolutely necessary—must be presented to us in time, and must belong to the series of phenomena.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"62.33"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0034","text":"ON THE ANTITHESIS.\n\nThe difficulties which meet us, in our attempt to rise through the series of phenomena to the existence of an absolutely necessary supreme cause, must not originate from our inability to establish the truth of our mere conceptions of the necessary existence of a thing. That is to say, our objections not be ontological, but must be directed against the causal connection with a series of phenomena of a condition which is itself unconditioned. In one word, they must be cosmological and relate to empirical laws. We must show that the regress in the series of causes (in the world of sense) cannot conclude with an empirically unconditioned condition, and that the cosmological argument from the contingency of the cosmical state—a contingency alleged to arise from change—does not justify us in accepting a first cause, that is, a prime originator of the cosmical series.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"62.34"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0035","text":"The reader will observe in this antinomy a very remarkable contrast. The very same grounds of proof which established in the thesis the existence of a supreme being, demonstrated in the antithesis—and with equal strictness—the non-existence of such a being. We found, first, that a necessary being exists, because the whole time past contains the series of all conditions, and with it, therefore, the unconditioned (the necessary); secondly, that there does not exist any necessary being, for the same reason, that the whole time past contains the series of all conditions—which are themselves, therefore, in the aggregate, conditioned. The cause of this seeming incongruity is as follows. We attend, in the first argument, solely to the absolute totality of the series of conditions, the one of which determines the other in time, and thus arrive at a necessary unconditioned. In the second, we consider, on the contrary, the contingency of everything that is determined in the series of time—for every event is preceded by a time, in which the condition itself must be determined as conditioned—and thus everything that is unconditioned or absolutely necessary disappears. In both, the mode of proof is quite in accordance with the common procedure of human reason, which often falls into discord with itself, from considering an object from two different points of view. Herr von Mairan regarded the controversy between two celebrated astronomers, which arose from a similar difficulty as to the choice of a proper standpoint, as a phenomenon of sufficient importance to warrant a separate treatise on the subject. The one concluded: the moon revolves on its own axis, because it constantly presents the same side to the earth; the other declared that the moon does not revolve on its own axis, for the same reason. Both conclusions were perfectly correct, according to the point of view from which the motions of the moon were considered.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"62.35"}],"sha256":"6c421a4bdf528dbab56802909efede880da6319b1a7e8b8027ae1835b60d687e","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR","title":"Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions","order":63,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIIR","text":"# Contradiction\n\n### Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions\n\nWe have thus completely before us the dialectical procedure of the cosmological ideas. No possible experience can present us with an object adequate to them in extent. Nay, more, reason itself cannot cogitate them as according with the general laws of experience. And yet they are not arbitrary fictions of thought. On the contrary, reason, in its uninterrupted progress in the empirical synthesis, is necessarily conducted to them, when it endeavours to free from all conditions and to comprehend in its unconditioned totality that which can only be determined conditionally in accordance with the laws of experience. These dialectical propositions are so many attempts to solve four natural and unavoidable problems of reason. There are neither more, nor can there be less, than this number, because there are no other series of synthetical hypotheses, limiting à priori the empirical synthesis.\n\nThe brilliant claims of reason striving to extend its dominion beyond the limits of experience, have been represented above only in dry formulae, which contain merely the grounds of its pretensions. They have, besides, in conformity with the character of a transcendental philosophy, been freed from every empirical element; although the full splendour of the promises they hold out, and the anticipations they excite, manifests itself only when in connection with empirical cognitions. In the application of them, however, and in the advancing enlargement of the employment of reason, while struggling to rise from the region of experience and to soar to those sublime ideas, philosophy discovers a value and a dignity, which, if it could but make good its assertions, would raise it far above all other departments of human knowledge—professing, as it does, to present a sure foundation for our highest hopes and the ultimate aims of all the exertions of reason. The questions: whether the world has a beginning and a limit to its extension in space; whether there exists anywhere, or perhaps, in my own thinking Self, an indivisible and indestructible unity—or whether nothing but what is divisible and transitory exists; whether I am a free agent, or, like other beings, am bound in the chains of nature and fate; whether, finally, there is a supreme cause of the world, or all our thought and speculation must end with nature and the order of external things—are questions for the solution of which the mathematician would willingly exchange his whole science; for in it there is no satisfaction for the highest aspirations and most ardent desires of humanity. Nay, it may even be said that the true value of mathematics—that pride of human reason—consists in this: that she guides reason to the knowledge of nature—in her greater as well as in her less manifestations—in her beautiful order and regularity—guides her, moreover, to an insight into the wonderful unity of the moving forces in the operations of nature, far beyond the expectations of a philosophy building only on experience; and that she thus encourages philosophy to extend the province of reason beyond all experience, and at the same time provides it with the most excellent materials for supporting its investigations, in so far as their nature admits, by adequate and accordant intuitions.\n\nUnfortunately for speculation—but perhaps fortunately for the practical interests of humanity—reason, in the midst of her highest anticipations, finds herself hemmed in by a press of opposite and contradictory conclusions, from which neither her honour nor her safety will permit her to draw back. Nor can she regard these conflicting trains of reasoning with indifference as mere passages at arms, still less can she command peace; for in the subject of the conflict she has a deep interest. There is no other course left open to her than to reflect with herself upon the origin of this disunion in reason—whether it may not arise from a mere misunderstanding. After such an inquiry, arrogant claims would have to be given up on both sides; but the sovereignty of reason over understanding and sense would be based upon a sure foundation.\n\nWe shall at present defer this radical inquiry and, in the meantime, consider for a little what side in the controversy we should most willingly take, if we were obliged to become partisans at all. As, in this case, we leave out of sight altogether the logical criterion of truth, and merely consult our own interest in reference to the question, these considerations, although inadequate to settle the question of right in either party, will enable us to comprehend how those who have taken part in the struggle, adopt the one view rather than the other—no special insight into the subject, however, having influenced their choice. They will, at the same time, explain to us many other things by the way—for example, the fiery zeal on the one side and the cold maintenance of their cause on the other; why the one party has met with the warmest approbations, and the other has always been repulsed by irreconcilable prejudices.\n\nThere is one thing, however, that determines the proper point of view, from which alone this preliminary inquiry can be instituted and carried on with the proper completeness—and that is the comparison of the principles from which both sides, thesis and antithesis, proceed. My readers would remark in the propositions of the antithesis a complete uniformity in the mode of thought and a perfect unity of principle. Its principle was that of pure empiricism, not only in the explication of the phenomena in the world, but also in the solution of the transcendental ideas, even of that of the universe itself. The affirmations of the thesis, on the contrary, were based, in addition to the empirical mode of explanation employed in the series of phenomena, on intellectual propositions; and its principles were in so far not simple. I shall term the thesis, in view of its essential characteristic, the dogmatism of pure reason.\n\nOn the side of Dogmatism, or of the thesis, therefore, in the determination of the cosmological ideas, we find:\n\n1. A practical interest, which must be very dear to every right-thinking man. That the word has a beginning—that the nature of my thinking self is simple, and therefore indestructible—that I am a free agent, and raised above the compulsion of nature and her laws—and, finally, that the entire order of things, which form the world, is dependent upon a Supreme Being, from whom the whole receives unity and connection—these are so many foundation-stones of morality and religion. The antithesis deprives us of all these supports—or, at least, seems so to deprive us.\n\n2. A speculative interest of reason manifests itself on this side. For, if we take the transcendental ideas and employ them in the manner which the thesis directs, we can exhibit completely à priori the entire chain of conditions, and understand the derivation of the conditioned—beginning from the unconditioned. This the antithesis does not do; and for this reason does not meet with so welcome a reception. For it can give no answer to our question respecting the conditions of its synthesis—except such as must be supplemented by another question, and so on to infinity. According to it, we must rise from a given beginning to one still higher; every part conducts us to a still smaller one; every event is preceded by another event which is its cause; and the conditions of existence rest always upon other and still higher conditions, and find neither end nor basis in some self-subsistent thing as the primal being.\n\n3. This side has also the advantage of popularity; and this constitutes no small part of its claim to favour. The common understanding does not find the least difficulty in the idea of the unconditioned beginning of all synthesis—accustomed, as it is, rather to follow our consequences than to seek for a proper basis for cognition. In the conception of an absolute first, moreover—the possibility of which it does not inquire into—it is highly gratified to find a firmly-established point of departure for its attempts at theory; while in the restless and continuous ascent from the conditioned to the condition, always with one foot in the air, it can find no satisfaction.\n\nOn the side of the antithesis, or Empiricism, in the determination of the cosmological ideas:\n\n1. We cannot discover any such practical interest arising from pure principles of reason as morality and religion present. On the contrary, pure empiricism seems to empty them of all their power and influence. If there does not exist a Supreme Being distinct from the world—if the world is without beginning, consequently without a Creator—if our wills are not free, and the soul is divisible and subject to corruption just like matter—the ideas and principles of morality lose all validity and fall with the transcendental ideas which constituted their theoretical support.\n\n2. But empiricism, in compensation, holds out to reason, in its speculative interests, certain important advantages, far exceeding any that the dogmatist can promise us. For, when employed by the empiricist, understanding is always upon its proper ground of investigation—the field of possible experience, the laws of which it can explore, and thus extend its cognition securely and with clear intelligence without being stopped by limits in any direction. Here can it and ought it to find and present to intuition its proper object—not only in itself, but in all its relations; or, if it employ conceptions, upon this ground it can always present the corresponding images in clear and unmistakable intuitions. It is quite unnecessary for it to renounce the guidance of nature, to attach itself to ideas, the objects of which it cannot know; because, as mere intellectual entities, they cannot be presented in any intuition. On the contrary, it is not even permitted to abandon its proper occupation, under the pretence that it has been brought to a conclusion (for it never can be), and to pass into the region of idealizing reason and transcendent conceptions, which it is not required to observe and explore the laws of nature, but merely to think and to imagine—secure from being contradicted by facts, because they have not been called as witnesses, but passed by, or perhaps subordinated to the so-called higher interests and considerations of pure reason.\n\nHence the empiricist will never allow himself to accept any epoch of nature for the first—the absolutely primal state; he will not believe that there can be limits to his outlook into her wide domains, nor pass from the objects of nature, which he can satisfactorily explain by means of observation and mathematical thought—which he can determine synthetically in intuition, to those which neither sense nor imagination can ever present in concreto; he will not concede the existence of a faculty in nature, operating independently of the laws of nature—a concession which would introduce uncertainty into the procedure of the understanding, which is guided by necessary laws to the observation of phenomena; nor, finally, will he permit himself to seek a cause beyond nature, inasmuch as we know nothing but it, and from it alone receive an objective basis for all our conceptions and instruction in the unvarying laws of things.\n\nIn truth, if the empirical philosopher had no other purpose in the establishment of his antithesis than to check the presumption of a reason which mistakes its true destination, which boasts of its insight and its knowledge, just where all insight and knowledge cease to exist, and regards that which is valid only in relation to a practical interest, as an advancement of the speculative interests of the mind (in order, when it is convenient for itself, to break the thread of our physical investigations, and, under pretence of extending our cognition, connect them with transcendental ideas, by means of which we really know only that we know nothing)—if, I say, the empiricist rested satisfied with this benefit, the principle advanced by him would be a maxim recommending moderation in the pretensions of reason and modesty in its affirmations, and at the same time would direct us to the right mode of extending the province of the understanding, by the help of the only true teacher, experience. In obedience to this advice, intellectual hypotheses and faith would not be called in aid of our practical interests; nor should we introduce them under the pompous titles of science and insight. For speculative cognition cannot find an objective basis any other where than in experience; and, when we overstep its limits our synthesis, which requires ever new cognitions independent of experience, has no substratum of intuition upon which to build.\n\nBut if—as often happens—empiricism, in relation to ideas, becomes itself dogmatic and boldly denies that which is above the sphere of its phenomenal cognition, it falls itself into the error of intemperance—an error which is here all the more reprehensible, as thereby the practical interest of reason receives an irreparable injury.\n\nAnd this constitutes the opposition between Epicureanism[[57]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-57) and Platonism.\n\n[[57]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-57) It is, however, still a matter of doubt whether Epicurus ever propounded these principles as directions for the objective employment of the understanding. If, indeed, they were nothing more than maxims for the speculative exercise of reason, he gives evidence therein a more genuine philosophic spirit than any of the philosophers of antiquity. That, in the explanation of phenomena, we must proceed as if the field of inquiry had neither limits in space nor commencement in time; that we must be satisfied with the teaching of experience in reference to the material of which the world is posed; that we must not look for any other mode of the origination of events than that which is determined by the unalterable laws of nature; and finally, that we not employ the hypothesis of a cause distinct from the world to account for a phenomenon or for the world itself—are principles for the extension of speculative philosophy, and the discovery of the true sources of the principles of morals, which, however little conformed to in the present day, are undoubtedly correct. At the same time, any one desirous of ignoring, in mere speculation, these dogmatical propositions, need not for that reason be accused of denying them.\n\nBoth Epicurus and Plato assert more in their systems than they know. The former encourages and advances science—although to the prejudice of the practical; the latter presents us with excellent principles for the investigation of the practical, but, in relation to everything regarding which we can attain to speculative cognition, permits reason to append idealistic explanations of natural phenomena, to the great injury of physical investigation.\n\n3. In regard to the third motive for the preliminary choice of a party in this war of assertions, it seems very extraordinary that empiricism should be utterly unpopular. We should be inclined to believe that the common understanding would receive it with pleasure—promising as it does to satisfy it without passing the bounds of experience and its connected order; while transcendental dogmatism obliges it to rise to conceptions which far surpass the intelligence and ability of the most practised thinkers. But in this, in truth, is to be found its real motive. For the common understanding thus finds itself in a situation where not even the most learned can have the advantage of it. If it understands little or nothing about these transcendental conceptions, no one can boast of understanding any more; and although it may not express itself in so scholastically correct a manner as others, it can busy itself with reasoning and arguments without end, wandering among mere ideas, about which one can always be very eloquent, because we know nothing about them; while, in the observation and investigation of nature, it would be forced to remain dumb and to confess its utter ignorance. Thus indolence and vanity form of themselves strong recommendations of these principles. Besides, although it is a hard thing for a philosopher to assume a principle, of which he can give to himself no reasonable account, and still more to employ conceptions, the objective reality of which cannot be established, nothing is more usual with the common understanding. It wants something which will allow it to go to work with confidence. The difficulty of even comprehending a supposition does not disquiet it, because—not knowing what comprehending means—it never even thinks of the supposition it may be adopting as a principle; and regards as known that with which it has become familiar from constant use. And, at last, all speculative interests disappear before the practical interests which it holds dear; and it fancies that it understands and knows what its necessities and hopes incite it to assume or to believe. Thus the empiricism of transcendentally idealizing reason is robbed of all popularity; and, however prejudicial it may be to the highest practical principles, there is no fear that it will ever pass the limits of the schools, or acquire any favour or influence in society or with the multitude.\n\nHuman reason is by nature architectonic. That is to say, it regards all cognitions as parts of a possible system, and hence accepts only such principles as at least do not incapacitate a cognition to which we may have attained from being placed along with others in a general system. But the propositions of the antithesis are of a character which renders the completion of an edifice of cognitions impossible. According to these, beyond one state or epoch of the world there is always to be found one more ancient; in every part always other parts themselves divisible; preceding every event another, the origin of which must itself be sought still higher; and everything in existence is conditioned, and still not dependent on an unconditioned and primal existence. As, therefore, the antithesis will not concede the existence of a first beginning which might be available as a foundation, a complete edifice of cognition, in the presence of such hypothesis, is utterly impossible. Thus the architectonic interest of reason, which requires a unity—not empirical, but à priori and rational—forms a natural recommendation for the assertions of the thesis in our antinomy.\n\nBut if any one could free himself entirely from all considerations of interest, and weigh without partiality the assertions of reason, attending only to their content, irrespective of the consequences which follow from them; such a person, on the supposition that he knew no other way out of the confusion than to settle the truth of one or other of the conflicting doctrines, would live in a state of continual hesitation. Today, he would feel convinced that the human will is free; to-morrow, considering the indissoluble chain of nature, he would look on freedom as a mere illusion and declare nature to be all-in-all. But, if he were called to action, the play of the merely speculative reason would disappear like the shapes of a dream, and practical interest would dictate his choice of principles. But, as it well befits a reflective and inquiring being to devote certain periods of time to the examination of its own reason—to divest itself of all partiality, and frankly to communicate its observations for the judgement and opinion of others; so no one can be blamed for, much less prevented from, placing both parties on their trial, with permission to end themselves, free from intimidation, before intimidation, before a sworn jury of equal condition with themselves—the condition of weak and fallible men.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","text":"# Contradiction\n\n### Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions\n\nWe have thus completely before us the dialectical procedure of the cosmological ideas. No possible experience can present us with an object adequate to them in extent. Nay, more, reason itself cannot cogitate them as according with the general laws of experience. And yet they are not arbitrary fictions of thought. On the contrary, reason, in its uninterrupted progress in the empirical synthesis, is necessarily conducted to them, when it endeavours to free from all conditions and to comprehend in its unconditioned totality that which can only be determined conditionally in accordance with the laws of experience. These dialectical propositions are so many attempts to solve four natural and unavoidable problems of reason. There are neither more, nor can there be less, than this number, because there are no other series of synthetical hypotheses, limiting à priori the empirical synthesis.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"63.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0002","text":"The brilliant claims of reason striving to extend its dominion beyond the limits of experience, have been represented above only in dry formulae, which contain merely the grounds of its pretensions. They have, besides, in conformity with the character of a transcendental philosophy, been freed from every empirical element; although the full splendour of the promises they hold out, and the anticipations they excite, manifests itself only when in connection with empirical cognitions. In the application of them, however, and in the advancing enlargement of the employment of reason, while struggling to rise from the region of experience and to soar to those sublime ideas, philosophy discovers a value and a dignity, which, if it could but make good its assertions, would raise it far above all other departments of human knowledge—professing, as it does, to present a sure foundation for our highest hopes and the ultimate aims of all the exertions of reason. The questions: whether the world has a beginning and a limit to its extension in space; whether there exists anywhere, or perhaps, in my own thinking Self, an indivisible and indestructible unity—or whether nothing but what is divisible and transitory exists; whether I am a free agent, or, like other beings, am bound in the chains of nature and fate; whether, finally, there is a supreme cause of the world, or all our thought and speculation must end with nature and the order of external things—are questions for the solution of which the mathematician would willingly exchange his whole science; for in it there is no satisfaction for the highest aspirations and most ardent desires of humanity. Nay, it may even be said that the true value of mathematics—that pride of human reason—consists in this: that she guides reason to the knowledge of nature—in her greater as well as in her less manifestations—in her beautiful order and regularity—guides her, moreover, to an insight into the wonderful unity of the moving forces in the operations of nature, far beyond the expectations of a philosophy building only on experience; and that she thus encourages philosophy to extend the province of reason beyond all experience, and at the same time provides it with the most excellent materials for supporting its investigations, in so far as their nature admits, by adequate and accordant intuitions.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"63.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","text":"Unfortunately for speculation—but perhaps fortunately for the practical interests of humanity—reason, in the midst of her highest anticipations, finds herself hemmed in by a press of opposite and contradictory conclusions, from which neither her honour nor her safety will permit her to draw back. Nor can she regard these conflicting trains of reasoning with indifference as mere passages at arms, still less can she command peace; for in the subject of the conflict she has a deep interest. There is no other course left open to her than to reflect with herself upon the origin of this disunion in reason—whether it may not arise from a mere misunderstanding. After such an inquiry, arrogant claims would have to be given up on both sides; but the sovereignty of reason over understanding and sense would be based upon a sure foundation.\n\nWe shall at present defer this radical inquiry and, in the meantime, consider for a little what side in the controversy we should most willingly take, if we were obliged to become partisans at all. As, in this case, we leave out of sight altogether the logical criterion of truth, and merely consult our own interest in reference to the question, these considerations, although inadequate to settle the question of right in either party, will enable us to comprehend how those who have taken part in the struggle, adopt the one view rather than the other—no special insight into the subject, however, having influenced their choice. They will, at the same time, explain to us many other things by the way—for example, the fiery zeal on the one side and the cold maintenance of their cause on the other; why the one party has met with the warmest approbations, and the other has always been repulsed by irreconcilable prejudices.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"63.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","text":"There is one thing, however, that determines the proper point of view, from which alone this preliminary inquiry can be instituted and carried on with the proper completeness—and that is the comparison of the principles from which both sides, thesis and antithesis, proceed. My readers would remark in the propositions of the antithesis a complete uniformity in the mode of thought and a perfect unity of principle. Its principle was that of pure empiricism, not only in the explication of the phenomena in the world, but also in the solution of the transcendental ideas, even of that of the universe itself. The affirmations of the thesis, on the contrary, were based, in addition to the empirical mode of explanation employed in the series of phenomena, on intellectual propositions; and its principles were in so far not simple. I shall term the thesis, in view of its essential characteristic, the dogmatism of pure reason.\n\nOn the side of Dogmatism, or of the thesis, therefore, in the determination of the cosmological ideas, we find:\n\n1. A practical interest, which must be very dear to every right-thinking man. That the word has a beginning—that the nature of my thinking self is simple, and therefore indestructible—that I am a free agent, and raised above the compulsion of nature and her laws—and, finally, that the entire order of things, which form the world, is dependent upon a Supreme Being, from whom the whole receives unity and connection—these are so many foundation-stones of morality and religion. The antithesis deprives us of all these supports—or, at least, seems so to deprive us.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"63.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0005","text":"2. A speculative interest of reason manifests itself on this side. For, if we take the transcendental ideas and employ them in the manner which the thesis directs, we can exhibit completely à priori the entire chain of conditions, and understand the derivation of the conditioned—beginning from the unconditioned. This the antithesis does not do; and for this reason does not meet with so welcome a reception. For it can give no answer to our question respecting the conditions of its synthesis—except such as must be supplemented by another question, and so on to infinity. According to it, we must rise from a given beginning to one still higher; every part conducts us to a still smaller one; every event is preceded by another event which is its cause; and the conditions of existence rest always upon other and still higher conditions, and find neither end nor basis in some self-subsistent thing as the primal being.\n\n3. This side has also the advantage of popularity; and this constitutes no small part of its claim to favour. The common understanding does not find the least difficulty in the idea of the unconditioned beginning of all synthesis—accustomed, as it is, rather to follow our consequences than to seek for a proper basis for cognition. In the conception of an absolute first, moreover—the possibility of which it does not inquire into—it is highly gratified to find a firmly-established point of departure for its attempts at theory; while in the restless and continuous ascent from the conditioned to the condition, always with one foot in the air, it can find no satisfaction.\n\nOn the side of the antithesis, or Empiricism, in the determination of the cosmological ideas:","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"63.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0006","text":"1. We cannot discover any such practical interest arising from pure principles of reason as morality and religion present. On the contrary, pure empiricism seems to empty them of all their power and influence. If there does not exist a Supreme Being distinct from the world—if the world is without beginning, consequently without a Creator—if our wills are not free, and the soul is divisible and subject to corruption just like matter—the ideas and principles of morality lose all validity and fall with the transcendental ideas which constituted their theoretical support.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"63.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","text":"2. But empiricism, in compensation, holds out to reason, in its speculative interests, certain important advantages, far exceeding any that the dogmatist can promise us. For, when employed by the empiricist, understanding is always upon its proper ground of investigation—the field of possible experience, the laws of which it can explore, and thus extend its cognition securely and with clear intelligence without being stopped by limits in any direction. Here can it and ought it to find and present to intuition its proper object—not only in itself, but in all its relations; or, if it employ conceptions, upon this ground it can always present the corresponding images in clear and unmistakable intuitions. It is quite unnecessary for it to renounce the guidance of nature, to attach itself to ideas, the objects of which it cannot know; because, as mere intellectual entities, they cannot be presented in any intuition. On the contrary, it is not even permitted to abandon its proper occupation, under the pretence that it has been brought to a conclusion (for it never can be), and to pass into the region of idealizing reason and transcendent conceptions, which it is not required to observe and explore the laws of nature, but merely to think and to imagine—secure from being contradicted by facts, because they have not been called as witnesses, but passed by, or perhaps subordinated to the so-called higher interests and considerations of pure reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"63.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0008","text":"Hence the empiricist will never allow himself to accept any epoch of nature for the first—the absolutely primal state; he will not believe that there can be limits to his outlook into her wide domains, nor pass from the objects of nature, which he can satisfactorily explain by means of observation and mathematical thought—which he can determine synthetically in intuition, to those which neither sense nor imagination can ever present in concreto; he will not concede the existence of a faculty in nature, operating independently of the laws of nature—a concession which would introduce uncertainty into the procedure of the understanding, which is guided by necessary laws to the observation of phenomena; nor, finally, will he permit himself to seek a cause beyond nature, inasmuch as we know nothing but it, and from it alone receive an objective basis for all our conceptions and instruction in the unvarying laws of things.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"63.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","text":"In truth, if the empirical philosopher had no other purpose in the establishment of his antithesis than to check the presumption of a reason which mistakes its true destination, which boasts of its insight and its knowledge, just where all insight and knowledge cease to exist, and regards that which is valid only in relation to a practical interest, as an advancement of the speculative interests of the mind (in order, when it is convenient for itself, to break the thread of our physical investigations, and, under pretence of extending our cognition, connect them with transcendental ideas, by means of which we really know only that we know nothing)—if, I say, the empiricist rested satisfied with this benefit, the principle advanced by him would be a maxim recommending moderation in the pretensions of reason and modesty in its affirmations, and at the same time would direct us to the right mode of extending the province of the understanding, by the help of the only true teacher, experience. In obedience to this advice, intellectual hypotheses and faith would not be called in aid of our practical interests; nor should we introduce them under the pompous titles of science and insight. For speculative cognition cannot find an objective basis any other where than in experience; and, when we overstep its limits our synthesis, which requires ever new cognitions independent of experience, has no substratum of intuition upon which to build.\n\nBut if—as often happens—empiricism, in relation to ideas, becomes itself dogmatic and boldly denies that which is above the sphere of its phenomenal cognition, it falls itself into the error of intemperance—an error which is here all the more reprehensible, as thereby the practical interest of reason receives an irreparable injury.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"63.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0010","text":"And this constitutes the opposition between Epicureanism[[57]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-57) and Platonism.\n\n[[57]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-57) It is, however, still a matter of doubt whether Epicurus ever propounded these principles as directions for the objective employment of the understanding. If, indeed, they were nothing more than maxims for the speculative exercise of reason, he gives evidence therein a more genuine philosophic spirit than any of the philosophers of antiquity. That, in the explanation of phenomena, we must proceed as if the field of inquiry had neither limits in space nor commencement in time; that we must be satisfied with the teaching of experience in reference to the material of which the world is posed; that we must not look for any other mode of the origination of events than that which is determined by the unalterable laws of nature; and finally, that we not employ the hypothesis of a cause distinct from the world to account for a phenomenon or for the world itself—are principles for the extension of speculative philosophy, and the discovery of the true sources of the principles of morals, which, however little conformed to in the present day, are undoubtedly correct. At the same time, any one desirous of ignoring, in mere speculation, these dogmatical propositions, need not for that reason be accused of denying them.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"63.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0011","text":"Both Epicurus and Plato assert more in their systems than they know. The former encourages and advances science—although to the prejudice of the practical; the latter presents us with excellent principles for the investigation of the practical, but, in relation to everything regarding which we can attain to speculative cognition, permits reason to append idealistic explanations of natural phenomena, to the great injury of physical investigation.\n\n3. In regard to the third motive for the preliminary choice of a party in this war of assertions, it seems very extraordinary that empiricism should be utterly unpopular. We should be inclined to believe that the common understanding would receive it with pleasure—promising as it does to satisfy it without passing the bounds of experience and its connected order; while transcendental dogmatism obliges it to rise to conceptions which far surpass the intelligence and ability of the most practised thinkers. But in this, in truth, is to be found its real motive. For the common understanding thus finds itself in a situation where not even the most learned can have the advantage of it. If it understands little or nothing about these transcendental conceptions, no one can boast of understanding any more; and although it may not express itself in so scholastically correct a manner as others, it can busy itself with reasoning and arguments without end, wandering among mere ideas, about which one can always be very eloquent, because we know nothing about them; while, in the observation and investigation of nature, it would be forced to remain dumb and to confess its utter ignorance. Thus indolence and vanity form of themselves strong recommendations of these principles.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"63.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0012","text":"Besides, although it is a hard thing for a philosopher to assume a principle, of which he can give to himself no reasonable account, and still more to employ conceptions, the objective reality of which cannot be established, nothing is more usual with the common understanding. It wants something which will allow it to go to work with confidence. The difficulty of even comprehending a supposition does not disquiet it, because—not knowing what comprehending means—it never even thinks of the supposition it may be adopting as a principle; and regards as known that with which it has become familiar from constant use. And, at last, all speculative interests disappear before the practical interests which it holds dear; and it fancies that it understands and knows what its necessities and hopes incite it to assume or to believe. Thus the empiricism of transcendentally idealizing reason is robbed of all popularity; and, however prejudicial it may be to the highest practical principles, there is no fear that it will ever pass the limits of the schools, or acquire any favour or influence in society or with the multitude.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"63.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","text":"Human reason is by nature architectonic. That is to say, it regards all cognitions as parts of a possible system, and hence accepts only such principles as at least do not incapacitate a cognition to which we may have attained from being placed along with others in a general system. But the propositions of the antithesis are of a character which renders the completion of an edifice of cognitions impossible. According to these, beyond one state or epoch of the world there is always to be found one more ancient; in every part always other parts themselves divisible; preceding every event another, the origin of which must itself be sought still higher; and everything in existence is conditioned, and still not dependent on an unconditioned and primal existence. As, therefore, the antithesis will not concede the existence of a first beginning which might be available as a foundation, a complete edifice of cognition, in the presence of such hypothesis, is utterly impossible. Thus the architectonic interest of reason, which requires a unity—not empirical, but à priori and rational—forms a natural recommendation for the assertions of the thesis in our antinomy.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"63.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0014","text":"But if any one could free himself entirely from all considerations of interest, and weigh without partiality the assertions of reason, attending only to their content, irrespective of the consequences which follow from them; such a person, on the supposition that he knew no other way out of the confusion than to settle the truth of one or other of the conflicting doctrines, would live in a state of continual hesitation. Today, he would feel convinced that the human will is free; to-morrow, considering the indissoluble chain of nature, he would look on freedom as a mere illusion and declare nature to be all-in-all. But, if he were called to action, the play of the merely speculative reason would disappear like the shapes of a dream, and practical interest would dictate his choice of principles. But, as it well befits a reflective and inquiring being to devote certain periods of time to the examination of its own reason—to divest itself of all partiality, and frankly to communicate its observations for the judgement and opinion of others; so no one can be blamed for, much less prevented from, placing both parties on their trial, with permission to end themselves, free from intimidation, before intimidation, before a sworn jury of equal condition with themselves—the condition of weak and fallible men.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"63.14"}],"sha256":"922cfa9a168eaec5543e7b527248e1a87fa6ac349e8cb941bfa8ff1543236b53","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI","title":"Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems","order":64,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SINI","text":"### Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems\n\nTo avow an ability to solve all problems and to answer all questions would be a profession certain to convict any philosopher of extravagant boasting and self-conceit, and at once to destroy the confidence that might otherwise have been reposed in him. There are, however, sciences so constituted that every question arising within their sphere must necessarily be capable of receiving an answer from the knowledge already possessed, for the answer must be received from the same sources whence the question arose. In such sciences it is not allowable to excuse ourselves on the plea of necessary and unavoidable ignorance; a solution is absolutely requisite. The rule of right and wrong must help us to the knowledge of what is right or wrong in all possible cases; otherwise, the idea of obligation or duty would be utterly null, for we cannot have any obligation to that which we cannot know. On the other hand, in our investigations of the phenomena of nature, much must remain uncertain, and many questions continue insoluble; because what we know of nature is far from being sufficient to explain all the phenomena that are presented to our observation. Now the question is: Whether there is in transcendental philosophy any question, relating to an object presented to pure reason, which is unanswerable by this reason; and whether we must regard the subject of the question as quite uncertain, so far as our knowledge extends, and must give it a place among those subjects, of which we have just so much conception as is sufficient to enable us to raise a question—faculty or materials failing us, however, when we attempt an answer.\n\nNow I maintain that, among all speculative cognition, the peculiarity of transcendental philosophy is that there is no question, relating to an object presented to pure reason, which is insoluble by this reason; and that the profession of unavoidable ignorance—the problem being alleged to be beyond the reach of our faculties—cannot free us from the obligation to present a complete and satisfactory answer. For the very conception which enables us to raise the question must give us the power of answering it; inasmuch as the object, as in the case of right and wrong, is not to be discovered out of the conception.\n\nBut, in transcendental philosophy, it is only the cosmological questions to which we can demand a satisfactory answer in relation to the constitution of their object; and the philosopher is not permitted to avail himself of the pretext of necessary ignorance and impenetrable obscurity. These questions relate solely to the cosmological ideas. For the object must be given in experience, and the question relates to the adequateness of the object to an idea. If the object is transcendental and therefore itself unknown; if the question, for example, is whether the object—the something, the phenomenon of which (internal—in ourselves) is thought—that is to say, the soul, is in itself a simple being; or whether there is a cause of all things, which is absolutely necessary—in such cases we are seeking for our idea an object, of which we may confess that it is unknown to us, though we must not on that account assert that it is impossible.[[58]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-58) The cosmological ideas alone posses the peculiarity that we can presuppose the object of them and the empirical synthesis requisite for the conception of that object to be given; and the question, which arises from these ideas, relates merely to the progress of this synthesis, in so far as it must contain absolute totality—which, however, is not empirical, as it cannot be given in any experience. Now, as the question here is solely in regard to a thing as the object of a possible experience and not as a thing in itself, the answer to the transcendental cosmological question need not be sought out of the idea, for the question does not regard an object in itself. The question in relation to a possible experience is not, “What can be given in an experience in concreto” but “what is contained in the idea, to which the empirical synthesis must approximate.” The question must therefore be capable of solution from the idea alone. For the idea is a creation of reason itself, which therefore cannot disclaim the obligation to answer or refer us to the unknown object.\n\n[[58]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-58) The question, “What is the constitution of a transcendental object?” is unanswerable—we are unable to say what it is; but we can perceive that the question itself is nothing; because it does not relate to any object that can be presented to us. For this reason, we must consider all the questions raised in transcendental psychology as answerable and as really answered; for they relate to the transcendental subject of all internal phenomena, which is not itself phenomenon and consequently not given as an object, in which, moreover, none of the categories—and it is to them that the question is properly directed—find any conditions of its application. Here, therefore, is a case where no answer is the only proper answer. For a question regarding the constitution of a something which cannot be cogitated by any determined predicate, being completely beyond the sphere of objects and experience, is perfectly null and void.\n\nIt is not so extraordinary, as it at first sight appears, that a science should demand and expect satisfactory answers to all the questions that may arise within its own sphere (questiones domesticae), although, up to a certain time, these answers may not have been discovered. There are, in addition to transcendental philosophy, only two pure sciences of reason; the one with a speculative, the other with a practical content—pure mathematics and pure ethics. Has any one ever heard it alleged that, from our complete and necessary ignorance of the conditions, it is uncertain what exact relation the diameter of a circle bears to the circle in rational or irrational numbers? By the former the sum cannot be given exactly, by the latter only approximately; and therefore we decide that the impossibility of a solution of the question is evident. Lambert presented us with a demonstration of this. In the general principles of morals there can be nothing uncertain, for the propositions are either utterly without meaning, or must originate solely in our rational conceptions. On the other hand, there must be in physical science an infinite number of conjectures, which can never become certainties; because the phenomena of nature are not given as objects dependent on our conceptions. The key to the solution of such questions cannot, therefore, be found in our conceptions, or in pure thought, but must lie without us and for that reason is in many cases not to be discovered; and consequently a satisfactory explanation cannot be expected. The questions of transcendental analytic, which relate to the deduction of our pure cognition, are not to be regarded as of the same kind as those mentioned above; for we are not at present treating of the certainty of judgements in relation to the origin of our conceptions, but only of that certainty in relation to objects.\n\nWe cannot, therefore, escape the responsibility of at least a critical solution of the questions of reason, by complaints of the limited nature of our faculties, and the seemingly humble confession that it is beyond the power of our reason to decide, whether the world has existed from all eternity or had a beginning—whether it is infinitely extended, or enclosed within certain limits—whether anything in the world is simple, or whether everything must be capable of infinite divisibility—whether freedom can originate phenomena, or whether everything is absolutely dependent on the laws and order of nature—and, finally, whether there exists a being that is completely unconditioned and necessary, or whether the existence of everything is conditioned and consequently dependent on something external to itself, and therefore in its own nature contingent. For all these questions relate to an object, which can be given nowhere else than in thought. This object is the absolutely unconditioned totality of the synthesis of phenomena. If the conceptions in our minds do not assist us to some certain result in regard to these problems, we must not defend ourselves on the plea that the object itself remains hidden from and unknown to us. For no such thing or object can be given—it is not to be found out of the idea in our minds. We must seek the cause of our failure in our idea itself, which is an insoluble problem and in regard to which we obstinately assume that there exists a real object corresponding and adequate to it. A clear explanation of the dialectic which lies in our conception, will very soon enable us to come to a satisfactory decision in regard to such a question.\n\nThe pretext that we are unable to arrive at certainty in regard to these problems may be met with this question, which requires at least a plain answer: “From what source do the ideas originate, the solution of which involves you in such difficulties? Are you seeking for an explanation of certain phenomena; and do you expect these ideas to give you the principles or the rules of this explanation?” Let it be granted, that all nature was laid open before you; that nothing was hid from your senses and your consciousness. Still, you could not cognize in concreto the object of your ideas in any experience. For what is demanded is not only this full and complete intuition, but also a complete synthesis and the consciousness of its absolute totality; and this is not possible by means of any empirical cognition. It follows that your question—your idea—is by no means necessary for the explanation of any phenomenon; and the idea cannot have been in any sense given by the object itself. For such an object can never be presented to us, because it cannot be given by any possible experience. Whatever perceptions you may attain to, you are still surrounded by conditions—in space, or in time—and you cannot discover anything unconditioned; nor can you decide whether this unconditioned is to be placed in an absolute beginning of the synthesis, or in an absolute totality of the series without beginning. A whole, in the empirical signification of the term, is always merely comparative. The absolute whole of quantity (the universe), of division, of derivation, of the condition of existence, with the question—whether it is to be produced by finite or infinite synthesis, no possible experience can instruct us concerning. You will not, for example, be able to explain the phenomena of a body in the least degree better, whether you believe it to consist of simple, or of composite parts; for a simple phenomenon—and just as little an infinite series of composition—can never be presented to your perception. Phenomena require and admit of explanation, only in so far as the conditions of that explanation are given in perception; but the sum total of that which is given in phenomena, considered as an absolute whole, is itself a perception—and we cannot therefore seek for explanations of this whole beyond itself, in other perceptions. The explanation of this whole is the proper object of the transcendental problems of pure reason.\n\nAlthough, therefore, the solution of these problems is unattainable through experience, we must not permit ourselves to say that it is uncertain how the object of our inquiries is constituted. For the object is in our own mind and cannot be discovered in experience; and we have only to take care that our thoughts are consistent with each other, and to avoid falling into the amphiboly of regarding our idea as a representation of an object empirically given, and therefore to be cognized according to the laws of experience. A dogmatical solution is therefore not only unsatisfactory but impossible. The critical solution, which may be a perfectly certain one, does not consider the question objectively, but proceeds by inquiring into the basis of the cognition upon which the question rests.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0001","text":"### Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems\n\nTo avow an ability to solve all problems and to answer all questions would be a profession certain to convict any philosopher of extravagant boasting and self-conceit, and at once to destroy the confidence that might otherwise have been reposed in him. There are, however, sciences so constituted that every question arising within their sphere must necessarily be capable of receiving an answer from the knowledge already possessed, for the answer must be received from the same sources whence the question arose. In such sciences it is not allowable to excuse ourselves on the plea of necessary and unavoidable ignorance; a solution is absolutely requisite. The rule of right and wrong must help us to the knowledge of what is right or wrong in all possible cases; otherwise, the idea of obligation or duty would be utterly null, for we cannot have any obligation to that which we cannot know. On the other hand, in our investigations of the phenomena of nature, much must remain uncertain, and many questions continue insoluble; because what we know of nature is far from being sufficient to explain all the phenomena that are presented to our observation. Now the question is: Whether there is in transcendental philosophy any question, relating to an object presented to pure reason, which is unanswerable by this reason; and whether we must regard the subject of the question as quite uncertain, so far as our knowledge extends, and must give it a place among those subjects, of which we have just so much conception as is sufficient to enable us to raise a question—faculty or materials failing us, however, when we attempt an answer.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"64.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0002","text":"Now I maintain that, among all speculative cognition, the peculiarity of transcendental philosophy is that there is no question, relating to an object presented to pure reason, which is insoluble by this reason; and that the profession of unavoidable ignorance—the problem being alleged to be beyond the reach of our faculties—cannot free us from the obligation to present a complete and satisfactory answer. For the very conception which enables us to raise the question must give us the power of answering it; inasmuch as the object, as in the case of right and wrong, is not to be discovered out of the conception.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"64.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","text":"But, in transcendental philosophy, it is only the cosmological questions to which we can demand a satisfactory answer in relation to the constitution of their object; and the philosopher is not permitted to avail himself of the pretext of necessary ignorance and impenetrable obscurity. These questions relate solely to the cosmological ideas. For the object must be given in experience, and the question relates to the adequateness of the object to an idea. If the object is transcendental and therefore itself unknown; if the question, for example, is whether the object—the something, the phenomenon of which (internal—in ourselves) is thought—that is to say, the soul, is in itself a simple being; or whether there is a cause of all things, which is absolutely necessary—in such cases we are seeking for our idea an object, of which we may confess that it is unknown to us, though we must not on that account assert that it is impossible.[[58]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-58) The cosmological ideas alone posses the peculiarity that we can presuppose the object of them and the empirical synthesis requisite for the conception of that object to be given; and the question, which arises from these ideas, relates merely to the progress of this synthesis, in so far as it must contain absolute totality—which, however, is not empirical, as it cannot be given in any experience. Now, as the question here is solely in regard to a thing as the object of a possible experience and not as a thing in itself, the answer to the transcendental cosmological question need not be sought out of the idea, for the question does not regard an object in itself. The question in relation to a possible experience is not, “What can be given in an experience in concreto” but “what is contained in the idea, to which the empirical synthesis must approximate.” The question must therefore be capable of solution from the idea alone. For the idea is a creation of reason itself, which therefore cannot disclaim the obligation to answer or refer us to the unknown object.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"64.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0004","text":"[[58]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-58) The question, “What is the constitution of a transcendental object?” is unanswerable—we are unable to say what it is; but we can perceive that the question itself is nothing; because it does not relate to any object that can be presented to us. For this reason, we must consider all the questions raised in transcendental psychology as answerable and as really answered; for they relate to the transcendental subject of all internal phenomena, which is not itself phenomenon and consequently not given as an object, in which, moreover, none of the categories—and it is to them that the question is properly directed—find any conditions of its application. Here, therefore, is a case where no answer is the only proper answer. For a question regarding the constitution of a something which cannot be cogitated by any determined predicate, being completely beyond the sphere of objects and experience, is perfectly null and void.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"64.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0005","text":"It is not so extraordinary, as it at first sight appears, that a science should demand and expect satisfactory answers to all the questions that may arise within its own sphere (questiones domesticae), although, up to a certain time, these answers may not have been discovered. There are, in addition to transcendental philosophy, only two pure sciences of reason; the one with a speculative, the other with a practical content—pure mathematics and pure ethics. Has any one ever heard it alleged that, from our complete and necessary ignorance of the conditions, it is uncertain what exact relation the diameter of a circle bears to the circle in rational or irrational numbers? By the former the sum cannot be given exactly, by the latter only approximately; and therefore we decide that the impossibility of a solution of the question is evident. Lambert presented us with a demonstration of this. In the general principles of morals there can be nothing uncertain, for the propositions are either utterly without meaning, or must originate solely in our rational conceptions. On the other hand, there must be in physical science an infinite number of conjectures, which can never become certainties; because the phenomena of nature are not given as objects dependent on our conceptions. The key to the solution of such questions cannot, therefore, be found in our conceptions, or in pure thought, but must lie without us and for that reason is in many cases not to be discovered; and consequently a satisfactory explanation cannot be expected. The questions of transcendental analytic, which relate to the deduction of our pure cognition, are not to be regarded as of the same kind as those mentioned above; for we are not at present treating of the certainty of judgements in relation to the origin of our conceptions, but only of that certainty in relation to objects.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"64.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","text":"We cannot, therefore, escape the responsibility of at least a critical solution of the questions of reason, by complaints of the limited nature of our faculties, and the seemingly humble confession that it is beyond the power of our reason to decide, whether the world has existed from all eternity or had a beginning—whether it is infinitely extended, or enclosed within certain limits—whether anything in the world is simple, or whether everything must be capable of infinite divisibility—whether freedom can originate phenomena, or whether everything is absolutely dependent on the laws and order of nature—and, finally, whether there exists a being that is completely unconditioned and necessary, or whether the existence of everything is conditioned and consequently dependent on something external to itself, and therefore in its own nature contingent. For all these questions relate to an object, which can be given nowhere else than in thought. This object is the absolutely unconditioned totality of the synthesis of phenomena. If the conceptions in our minds do not assist us to some certain result in regard to these problems, we must not defend ourselves on the plea that the object itself remains hidden from and unknown to us. For no such thing or object can be given—it is not to be found out of the idea in our minds. We must seek the cause of our failure in our idea itself, which is an insoluble problem and in regard to which we obstinately assume that there exists a real object corresponding and adequate to it. A clear explanation of the dialectic which lies in our conception, will very soon enable us to come to a satisfactory decision in regard to such a question.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"64.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","text":"The pretext that we are unable to arrive at certainty in regard to these problems may be met with this question, which requires at least a plain answer: “From what source do the ideas originate, the solution of which involves you in such difficulties? Are you seeking for an explanation of certain phenomena; and do you expect these ideas to give you the principles or the rules of this explanation?” Let it be granted, that all nature was laid open before you; that nothing was hid from your senses and your consciousness. Still, you could not cognize in concreto the object of your ideas in any experience. For what is demanded is not only this full and complete intuition, but also a complete synthesis and the consciousness of its absolute totality; and this is not possible by means of any empirical cognition. It follows that your question—your idea—is by no means necessary for the explanation of any phenomenon; and the idea cannot have been in any sense given by the object itself. For such an object can never be presented to us, because it cannot be given by any possible experience. Whatever perceptions you may attain to, you are still surrounded by conditions—in space, or in time—and you cannot discover anything unconditioned; nor can you decide whether this unconditioned is to be placed in an absolute beginning of the synthesis, or in an absolute totality of the series without beginning. A whole, in the empirical signification of the term, is always merely comparative. The absolute whole of quantity (the universe), of division, of derivation, of the condition of existence, with the question—whether it is to be produced by finite or infinite synthesis, no possible experience can instruct us concerning. You will not, for example, be able to explain the phenomena of a body in the least degree better, whether you believe it to consist of simple, or of composite parts; for a simple phenomenon—and just as little an infinite series of composition—can never be presented to your perception. Phenomena require and admit of explanation, only in so far as the conditions of that explanation are given in perception; but the sum total of that which is given in phenomena, considered as an absolute whole, is itself a perception—and we cannot therefore seek for explanations of this whole beyond itself, in other perceptions. The explanation of this whole is the proper object of the transcendental problems of pure reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"64.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","text":"Although, therefore, the solution of these problems is unattainable through experience, we must not permit ourselves to say that it is uncertain how the object of our inquiries is constituted. For the object is in our own mind and cannot be discovered in experience; and we have only to take care that our thoughts are consistent with each other, and to avoid falling into the amphiboly of regarding our idea as a representation of an object empirically given, and therefore to be cognized according to the laws of experience. A dogmatical solution is therefore not only unsatisfactory but impossible. The critical solution, which may be a perfectly certain one, does not consider the question objectively, but proceeds by inquiring into the basis of the cognition upon which the question rests.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"64.8"}],"sha256":"8c86d8e38c71f8e388bd562b47ca0e69b8606b07aa2f907811a745f2b4aabf9f","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE","title":"Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas","order":65,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SVSE","text":"### Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas\n\nWe should be quite willing to desist from the demand of a dogmatical answer to our questions, if we understood beforehand that, be the answer what it may, it would only serve to increase our ignorance, to throw us from one incomprehensibility into another, from one obscurity into another still greater, and perhaps lead us into irreconcilable contradictions. If a dogmatical affirmative or negative answer is demanded, is it at all prudent to set aside the probable grounds of a solution which lie before us and to take into consideration what advantage we shall gain, if the answer is to favour the one side or the other? If it happens that in both cases the answer is mere nonsense, we have in this an irresistible summons to institute a critical investigation of the question, for the purpose of discovering whether it is based on a groundless presupposition and relates to an idea, the falsity of which would be more easily exposed in its application and consequences than in the mere representation of its content. This is the great utility of the sceptical mode of treating the questions addressed by pure reason to itself. By this method we easily rid ourselves of the confusions of dogmatism, and establish in its place a temperate criticism, which, as a genuine cathartic, will successfully remove the presumptuous notions of philosophy and their consequence—the vain pretension to universal science.\n\nIf, then, I could understand the nature of a cosmological idea and perceive, before I entered on the discussion of the subject at all, that, whatever side of the question regarding the unconditioned of the regressive synthesis of phenomena it favoured—it must either be too great or too small for every conception of the understanding—I would be able to comprehend how the idea, which relates to an object of experience—an experience which must be adequate to and in accordance with a possible conception of the understanding—must be completely void and without significance, inasmuch as its object is inadequate, consider it as we may. And this is actually the case with all cosmological conceptions, which, for the reason above mentioned, involve reason, so long as it remains attached to them, in an unavoidable antinomy. For suppose:\n\nFirst, that the world has no beginning—in this case it is too large for our conception; for this conception, which consists in a successive regress, cannot overtake the whole eternity that has elapsed. Grant that it has a beginning, it is then too small for the conception of the understanding. For, as a beginning presupposes a time preceding, it cannot be unconditioned; and the law of the empirical employment of the understanding imposes the necessity of looking for a higher condition of time; and the world is, therefore, evidently too small for this law.\n\nThe same is the case with the double answer to the question regarding the extent, in space, of the world. For, if it is infinite and unlimited, it must be too large for every possible empirical conception. If it is finite and limited, we have a right to ask: “What determines these limits?” Void space is not a self-subsistent correlate of things, and cannot be a final condition—and still less an empirical condition, forming a part of a possible experience. For how can we have any experience or perception of an absolute void? But the absolute totality of the empirical synthesis requires that the unconditioned be an empirical conception. Consequently, a finite world is too small for our conception.\n\nSecondly, if every phenomenon (matter) in space consists of an infinite number of parts, the regress of the division is always too great for our conception; and if the division of space must cease with some member of the division (the simple), it is too small for the idea of the unconditioned. For the member at which we have discontinued our division still admits a regress to many more parts contained in the object.\n\nThirdly, suppose that every event in the world happens in accordance with the laws of nature; the causality of a cause must itself be an event and necessitates a regress to a still higher cause, and consequently the unceasing prolongation of the series of conditions a parte priori. Operative nature is therefore too large for every conception we can form in the synthesis of cosmical events.\n\nIf we admit the existence of spontaneously produced events, that is, of free agency, we are driven, in our search for sufficient reasons, on an unavoidable law of nature and are compelled to appeal to the empirical law of causality, and we find that any such totality of connection in our synthesis is too small for our necessary empirical conception.\n\nFourthly, if we assume the existence of an absolutely necessary being—whether it be the world or something in the world, or the cause of the world—we must place it in a time at an infinite distance from any given moment; for, otherwise, it must be dependent on some other and higher existence. Such an existence is, in this case, too large for our empirical conception, and unattainable by the continued regress of any synthesis.\n\nBut if we believe that everything in the world—be it condition or conditioned—is contingent; every given existence is too small for our conception. For in this case we are compelled to seek for some other existence upon which the former depends.\n\nWe have said that in all these cases the cosmological idea is either too great or too small for the empirical regress in a synthesis, and consequently for every possible conception of the understanding. Why did we not express ourselves in a manner exactly the reverse of this and, instead of accusing the cosmological idea of over stepping or of falling short of its true aim, possible experience, say that, in the first case, the empirical conception is always too small for the idea, and in the second too great, and thus attach the blame of these contradictions to the empirical regress? The reason is this. Possible experience can alone give reality to our conceptions; without it a conception is merely an idea, without truth or relation to an object. Hence a possible empirical conception must be the standard by which we are to judge whether an idea is anything more than an idea and fiction of thought, or whether it relates to an object in the world. If we say of a thing that in relation to some other thing it is too large or too small, the former is considered as existing for the sake of the latter, and requiring to be adapted to it. Among the trivial subjects of discussion in the old schools of dialectics was this question: “If a ball cannot pass through a hole, shall we say that the ball is too large or the hole too small?” In this case it is indifferent what expression we employ; for we do not know which exists for the sake of the other. On the other hand, we cannot say: “The man is too long for his coat”; but: “The coat is too short for the man.”\n\nWe are thus led to the well-founded suspicion that the cosmological ideas, and all the conflicting sophistical assertions connected with them, are based upon a false and fictitious conception of the mode in which the object of these ideas is presented to us; and this suspicion will probably direct us how to expose the illusion that has so long led us astray from the truth.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","text":"### Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas\n\nWe should be quite willing to desist from the demand of a dogmatical answer to our questions, if we understood beforehand that, be the answer what it may, it would only serve to increase our ignorance, to throw us from one incomprehensibility into another, from one obscurity into another still greater, and perhaps lead us into irreconcilable contradictions. If a dogmatical affirmative or negative answer is demanded, is it at all prudent to set aside the probable grounds of a solution which lie before us and to take into consideration what advantage we shall gain, if the answer is to favour the one side or the other? If it happens that in both cases the answer is mere nonsense, we have in this an irresistible summons to institute a critical investigation of the question, for the purpose of discovering whether it is based on a groundless presupposition and relates to an idea, the falsity of which would be more easily exposed in its application and consequences than in the mere representation of its content. This is the great utility of the sceptical mode of treating the questions addressed by pure reason to itself. By this method we easily rid ourselves of the confusions of dogmatism, and establish in its place a temperate criticism, which, as a genuine cathartic, will successfully remove the presumptuous notions of philosophy and their consequence—the vain pretension to universal science.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"65.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","text":"If, then, I could understand the nature of a cosmological idea and perceive, before I entered on the discussion of the subject at all, that, whatever side of the question regarding the unconditioned of the regressive synthesis of phenomena it favoured—it must either be too great or too small for every conception of the understanding—I would be able to comprehend how the idea, which relates to an object of experience—an experience which must be adequate to and in accordance with a possible conception of the understanding—must be completely void and without significance, inasmuch as its object is inadequate, consider it as we may. And this is actually the case with all cosmological conceptions, which, for the reason above mentioned, involve reason, so long as it remains attached to them, in an unavoidable antinomy. For suppose:\n\nFirst, that the world has no beginning—in this case it is too large for our conception; for this conception, which consists in a successive regress, cannot overtake the whole eternity that has elapsed. Grant that it has a beginning, it is then too small for the conception of the understanding. For, as a beginning presupposes a time preceding, it cannot be unconditioned; and the law of the empirical employment of the understanding imposes the necessity of looking for a higher condition of time; and the world is, therefore, evidently too small for this law.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"65.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","text":"The same is the case with the double answer to the question regarding the extent, in space, of the world. For, if it is infinite and unlimited, it must be too large for every possible empirical conception. If it is finite and limited, we have a right to ask: “What determines these limits?” Void space is not a self-subsistent correlate of things, and cannot be a final condition—and still less an empirical condition, forming a part of a possible experience. For how can we have any experience or perception of an absolute void? But the absolute totality of the empirical synthesis requires that the unconditioned be an empirical conception. Consequently, a finite world is too small for our conception.\n\nSecondly, if every phenomenon (matter) in space consists of an infinite number of parts, the regress of the division is always too great for our conception; and if the division of space must cease with some member of the division (the simple), it is too small for the idea of the unconditioned. For the member at which we have discontinued our division still admits a regress to many more parts contained in the object.\n\nThirdly, suppose that every event in the world happens in accordance with the laws of nature; the causality of a cause must itself be an event and necessitates a regress to a still higher cause, and consequently the unceasing prolongation of the series of conditions a parte priori. Operative nature is therefore too large for every conception we can form in the synthesis of cosmical events.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"65.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","text":"If we admit the existence of spontaneously produced events, that is, of free agency, we are driven, in our search for sufficient reasons, on an unavoidable law of nature and are compelled to appeal to the empirical law of causality, and we find that any such totality of connection in our synthesis is too small for our necessary empirical conception.\n\nFourthly, if we assume the existence of an absolutely necessary being—whether it be the world or something in the world, or the cause of the world—we must place it in a time at an infinite distance from any given moment; for, otherwise, it must be dependent on some other and higher existence. Such an existence is, in this case, too large for our empirical conception, and unattainable by the continued regress of any synthesis.\n\nBut if we believe that everything in the world—be it condition or conditioned—is contingent; every given existence is too small for our conception. For in this case we are compelled to seek for some other existence upon which the former depends.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"65.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","text":"We have said that in all these cases the cosmological idea is either too great or too small for the empirical regress in a synthesis, and consequently for every possible conception of the understanding. Why did we not express ourselves in a manner exactly the reverse of this and, instead of accusing the cosmological idea of over stepping or of falling short of its true aim, possible experience, say that, in the first case, the empirical conception is always too small for the idea, and in the second too great, and thus attach the blame of these contradictions to the empirical regress? The reason is this. Possible experience can alone give reality to our conceptions; without it a conception is merely an idea, without truth or relation to an object. Hence a possible empirical conception must be the standard by which we are to judge whether an idea is anything more than an idea and fiction of thought, or whether it relates to an object in the world. If we say of a thing that in relation to some other thing it is too large or too small, the former is considered as existing for the sake of the latter, and requiring to be adapted to it. Among the trivial subjects of discussion in the old schools of dialectics was this question: “If a ball cannot pass through a hole, shall we say that the ball is too large or the hole too small?” In this case it is indifferent what expression we employ; for we do not know which exists for the sake of the other. On the other hand, we cannot say: “The man is too long for his coat”; but: “The coat is too short for the man.”\n\nWe are thus led to the well-founded suspicion that the cosmological ideas, and all the conflicting sophistical assertions connected with them, are based upon a false and fictitious conception of the mode in which the object of these ideas is presented to us; and this suspicion will probably direct us how to expose the illusion that has so long led us astray from the truth.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"65.5"}],"sha256":"39f0013b5ff005979def4dfcd48b119492c1e0aed108742889ff78905b6827ad","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI","title":"Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic","order":66,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SVTI","text":"### Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic\n\nIn the transcendental æsthetic we proved that everything intuited in space and time, all objects of a possible experience, are nothing but phenomena, that is, mere representations; and that these, as presented to us—as extended bodies, or as series of changes—have no self-subsistent existence apart from human thought. This doctrine I call Transcendental Idealism.[[59]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-59) The realist in the transcendental sense regards these modifications of our sensibility, these mere representations, as things subsisting in themselves.\n\n[[59]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-59) I have elsewhere termed this theory formal idealism, to distinguish it from material idealism, which doubts or denies the existence of external things. To avoid ambiguity, it seems advisable in many cases to employ this term instead of that mentioned in the text.\n\nIt would be unjust to accuse us of holding the long-decried theory of empirical idealism, which, while admitting the reality of space, denies, or at least doubts, the existence of bodies extended in it, and thus leaves us without a sufficient criterion of reality and illusion. The supporters of this theory find no difficulty in admitting the reality of the phenomena of the internal sense in time; nay, they go the length of maintaining that this internal experience is of itself a sufficient proof of the real existence of its object as a thing in itself.\n\nTranscendental idealism allows that the objects of external intuition—as intuited in space, and all changes in time—as represented by the internal sense, are real. For, as space is the form of that intuition which we call external, and, without objects in space, no empirical representation could be given us, we can and ought to regard extended bodies in it as real. The case is the same with representations in time. But time and space, with all phenomena therein, are not in themselves things. They are nothing but representations and cannot exist out of and apart from the mind. Nay, the sensuous internal intuition of the mind (as the object of consciousness), the determination of which is represented by the succession of different states in time, is not the real, proper self, as it exists in itself—not the transcendental subject—but only a phenomenon, which is presented to the sensibility of this, to us, unknown being. This internal phenomenon cannot be admitted to be a self-subsisting thing; for its condition is time, and time cannot be the condition of a thing in itself. But the empirical truth of phenomena in space and time is guaranteed beyond the possibility of doubt, and sufficiently distinguished from the illusion of dreams or fancy—although both have a proper and thorough connection in an experience according to empirical laws. The objects of experience then are not things in themselves, but are given only in experience, and have no existence apart from and independently of experience. That there may be inhabitants in the moon, although no one has ever observed them, must certainly be admitted; but this assertion means only, that we may in the possible progress of experience discover them at some future time. For that which stands in connection with a perception according to the laws of the progress of experience is real. They are therefore really existent, if they stand in empirical connection with my actual or real consciousness, although they are not in themselves real, that is, apart from the progress of experience.\n\nThere is nothing actually given—we can be conscious of nothing as real, except a perception and the empirical progression from it to other possible perceptions. For phenomena, as mere representations, are real only in perception; and perception is, in fact, nothing but the reality of an empirical representation, that is, a phenomenon. To call a phenomenon a real thing prior to perception means either that we must meet with this phenomenon in the progress of experience, or it means nothing at all. For I can say only of a thing in itself that it exists without relation to the senses and experience. But we are speaking here merely of phenomena in space and time, both of which are determinations of sensibility, and not of things in themselves. It follows that phenomena are not things in themselves, but are mere representations, which if not given in us—in perception—are non-existent.\n\nThe faculty of sensuous intuition is properly a receptivity—a capacity of being affected in a certain manner by representations, the relation of which to each other is a pure intuition of space and time—the pure forms of sensibility. These representations, in so far as they are connected and determinable in this relation (in space and time) according to laws of the unity of experience, are called objects. The non-sensuous cause of these representations is completely unknown to us and hence cannot be intuited as an object. For such an object could not be represented either in space or in time; and without these conditions intuition or representation is impossible. We may, at the same time, term the non-sensuous cause of phenomena the transcendental object—but merely as a mental correlate to sensibility, considered as a receptivity. To this transcendental object we may attribute the whole connection and extent of our possible perceptions, and say that it is given and exists in itself prior to all experience. But the phenomena, corresponding to it, are not given as things in themselves, but in experience alone. For they are mere representations, receiving from perceptions alone significance and relation to a real object, under the condition that this or that perception—indicating an object—is in complete connection with all others in accordance with the rules of the unity of experience. Thus we can say: “The things that really existed in past time are given in the transcendental object of experience.” But these are to me real objects, only in so far as I can represent to my own mind, that a regressive series of possible perceptions—following the indications of history, or the footsteps of cause and effect—in accordance with empirical laws—that, in one word, the course of the world conducts us to an elapsed series of time as the condition of the present time. This series in past time is represented as real, not in itself, but only in connection with a possible experience. Thus, when I say that certain events occurred in past time, I merely assert the possibility of prolonging the chain of experience, from the present perception, upwards to the conditions that determine it according to time.\n\nIf I represent to myself all objects existing in all space and time, I do not thereby place these in space and time prior to all experience; on the contrary, such a representation is nothing more than the notion of a possible experience, in its absolute completeness. In experience alone are those objects, which are nothing but representations, given. But, when I say they existed prior to my experience, this means only that I must begin with the perception present to me and follow the track indicated until I discover them in some part or region of experience. The cause of the empirical condition of this progression—and consequently at what member therein I must stop, and at what point in the regress I am to find this member—is transcendental, and hence necessarily incognizable. But with this we have not to do; our concern is only with the law of progression in experience, in which objects, that is, phenomena, are given. It is a matter of indifference, whether I say, “I may in the progress of experience discover stars, at a hundred times greater distance than the most distant of those now visible,” or, “Stars at this distance may be met in space, although no one has, or ever will discover them.” For, if they are given as things in themselves, without any relation to possible experience, they are for me non-existent, consequently, are not objects, for they are not contained in the regressive series of experience. But, if these phenomena must be employed in the construction or support of the cosmological idea of an absolute whole, and when we are discussing a question that oversteps the limits of possible experience, the proper distinction of the different theories of the reality of sensuous objects is of great importance, in order to avoid the illusion which must necessarily arise from the misinterpretation of our empirical conceptions.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","text":"### Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic\n\nIn the transcendental æsthetic we proved that everything intuited in space and time, all objects of a possible experience, are nothing but phenomena, that is, mere representations; and that these, as presented to us—as extended bodies, or as series of changes—have no self-subsistent existence apart from human thought. This doctrine I call Transcendental Idealism.[[59]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-59) The realist in the transcendental sense regards these modifications of our sensibility, these mere representations, as things subsisting in themselves.\n\n[[59]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-59) I have elsewhere termed this theory formal idealism, to distinguish it from material idealism, which doubts or denies the existence of external things. To avoid ambiguity, it seems advisable in many cases to employ this term instead of that mentioned in the text.\n\nIt would be unjust to accuse us of holding the long-decried theory of empirical idealism, which, while admitting the reality of space, denies, or at least doubts, the existence of bodies extended in it, and thus leaves us without a sufficient criterion of reality and illusion. The supporters of this theory find no difficulty in admitting the reality of the phenomena of the internal sense in time; nay, they go the length of maintaining that this internal experience is of itself a sufficient proof of the real existence of its object as a thing in itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"66.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","text":"Transcendental idealism allows that the objects of external intuition—as intuited in space, and all changes in time—as represented by the internal sense, are real. For, as space is the form of that intuition which we call external, and, without objects in space, no empirical representation could be given us, we can and ought to regard extended bodies in it as real. The case is the same with representations in time. But time and space, with all phenomena therein, are not in themselves things. They are nothing but representations and cannot exist out of and apart from the mind. Nay, the sensuous internal intuition of the mind (as the object of consciousness), the determination of which is represented by the succession of different states in time, is not the real, proper self, as it exists in itself—not the transcendental subject—but only a phenomenon, which is presented to the sensibility of this, to us, unknown being. This internal phenomenon cannot be admitted to be a self-subsisting thing; for its condition is time, and time cannot be the condition of a thing in itself. But the empirical truth of phenomena in space and time is guaranteed beyond the possibility of doubt, and sufficiently distinguished from the illusion of dreams or fancy—although both have a proper and thorough connection in an experience according to empirical laws. The objects of experience then are not things in themselves, but are given only in experience, and have no existence apart from and independently of experience. That there may be inhabitants in the moon, although no one has ever observed them, must certainly be admitted; but this assertion means only, that we may in the possible progress of experience discover them at some future time. For that which stands in connection with a perception according to the laws of the progress of experience is real. They are therefore really existent, if they stand in empirical connection with my actual or real consciousness, although they are not in themselves real, that is, apart from the progress of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"66.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0003","text":"There is nothing actually given—we can be conscious of nothing as real, except a perception and the empirical progression from it to other possible perceptions. For phenomena, as mere representations, are real only in perception; and perception is, in fact, nothing but the reality of an empirical representation, that is, a phenomenon. To call a phenomenon a real thing prior to perception means either that we must meet with this phenomenon in the progress of experience, or it means nothing at all. For I can say only of a thing in itself that it exists without relation to the senses and experience. But we are speaking here merely of phenomena in space and time, both of which are determinations of sensibility, and not of things in themselves. It follows that phenomena are not things in themselves, but are mere representations, which if not given in us—in perception—are non-existent.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"66.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","text":"The faculty of sensuous intuition is properly a receptivity—a capacity of being affected in a certain manner by representations, the relation of which to each other is a pure intuition of space and time—the pure forms of sensibility. These representations, in so far as they are connected and determinable in this relation (in space and time) according to laws of the unity of experience, are called objects. The non-sensuous cause of these representations is completely unknown to us and hence cannot be intuited as an object. For such an object could not be represented either in space or in time; and without these conditions intuition or representation is impossible. We may, at the same time, term the non-sensuous cause of phenomena the transcendental object—but merely as a mental correlate to sensibility, considered as a receptivity. To this transcendental object we may attribute the whole connection and extent of our possible perceptions, and say that it is given and exists in itself prior to all experience. But the phenomena, corresponding to it, are not given as things in themselves, but in experience alone. For they are mere representations, receiving from perceptions alone significance and relation to a real object, under the condition that this or that perception—indicating an object—is in complete connection with all others in accordance with the rules of the unity of experience. Thus we can say: “The things that really existed in past time are given in the transcendental object of experience.” But these are to me real objects, only in so far as I can represent to my own mind, that a regressive series of possible perceptions—following the indications of history, or the footsteps of cause and effect—in accordance with empirical laws—that, in one word, the course of the world conducts us to an elapsed series of time as the condition of the present time. This series in past time is represented as real, not in itself, but only in connection with a possible experience. Thus, when I say that certain events occurred in past time, I merely assert the possibility of prolonging the chain of experience, from the present perception, upwards to the conditions that determine it according to time.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"66.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","text":"If I represent to myself all objects existing in all space and time, I do not thereby place these in space and time prior to all experience; on the contrary, such a representation is nothing more than the notion of a possible experience, in its absolute completeness. In experience alone are those objects, which are nothing but representations, given. But, when I say they existed prior to my experience, this means only that I must begin with the perception present to me and follow the track indicated until I discover them in some part or region of experience. The cause of the empirical condition of this progression—and consequently at what member therein I must stop, and at what point in the regress I am to find this member—is transcendental, and hence necessarily incognizable. But with this we have not to do; our concern is only with the law of progression in experience, in which objects, that is, phenomena, are given. It is a matter of indifference, whether I say, “I may in the progress of experience discover stars, at a hundred times greater distance than the most distant of those now visible,” or, “Stars at this distance may be met in space, although no one has, or ever will discover them.” For, if they are given as things in themselves, without any relation to possible experience, they are for me non-existent, consequently, are not objects, for they are not contained in the regressive series of experience. But, if these phenomena must be employed in the construction or support of the cosmological idea of an absolute whole, and when we are discussing a question that oversteps the limits of possible experience, the proper distinction of the different theories of the reality of sensuous objects is of great importance, in order to avoid the illusion which must necessarily arise from the misinterpretation of our empirical conceptions.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"66.5"}],"sha256":"c1ae859e4e94200e140dd6cd5a9dd4da1dfe7edbe9b3248de17fd04bad6ee27a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS","title":"Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem","order":67,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SVCS","text":"# Cosmos Solution\n\n### Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem\n\nThe antinomy of pure reason is based upon the following dialectical argument: “If that which is conditioned is given, the whole series of its conditions is also given; but sensuous objects are given as conditioned; consequently...” This syllogism, the major of which seems so natural and evident, introduces as many cosmological ideas as there are different kinds of conditions in the synthesis of phenomena, in so far as these conditions constitute a series. These ideas require absolute totality in the series, and thus place reason in inextricable embarrassment. Before proceeding to expose the fallacy in this dialectical argument, it will be necessary to have a correct understanding of certain conceptions that appear in it.\n\nIn the first place, the following proposition is evident, and indubitably certain: “If the conditioned is given, a regress in the series of all its conditions is thereby imperatively required.” For the very conception of a conditioned is a conception of something related to a condition, and, if this condition is itself conditioned, to another condition—and so on through all the members of the series. This proposition is, therefore, analytical and has nothing to fear from transcendental criticism. It is a logical postulate of reason: to pursue, as far as possible, the connection of a conception with its conditions.\n\nIf, in the second place, both the conditioned and the condition are things in themselves, and if the former is given, not only is the regress to the latter requisite, but the latter is really given with the former. Now, as this is true of all the members of the series, the entire series of conditions, and with them the unconditioned, is at the same time given in the very fact of the conditioned, the existence of which is possible only in and through that series, being given. In this case, the synthesis of the conditioned with its condition, is a synthesis of the understanding merely, which represents things as they are, without regarding whether and how we can cognize them. But if I have to do with phenomena, which, in their character of mere representations, are not given, if I do not attain to a cognition of them (in other words, to themselves, for they are nothing more than empirical cognitions), I am not entitled to say: “If the conditioned is given, all its conditions (as phenomena) are also given.” I cannot, therefore, from the fact of a conditioned being given, infer the absolute totality of the series of its conditions. For phenomena are nothing but an empirical synthesis in apprehension or perception, and are therefore given only in it. Now, in speaking of phenomena it does not follow that, if the conditioned is given, the synthesis which constitutes its empirical condition is also thereby given and presupposed; such a synthesis can be established only by an actual regress in the series of conditions. But we are entitled to say in this case that a regress to the conditions of a conditioned, in other words, that a continuous empirical synthesis is enjoined; that, if the conditions are not given, they are at least required; and that we are certain to discover the conditions in this regress.\n\nWe can now see that the major, in the above cosmological syllogism, takes the conditioned in the transcendental signification which it has in the pure category, while the minor speaks of it in the empirical signification which it has in the category as applied to phenomena. There is, therefore, a dialectical fallacy in the syllogism—a sophisma figurae dictionis. But this fallacy is not a consciously devised one, but a perfectly natural illusion of the common reason of man. For, when a thing is given as conditioned, we presuppose in the major its conditions and their series, unperceived, as it were, and unseen; because this is nothing more than the logical requirement of complete and satisfactory premisses for a given conclusion. In this case, time is altogether left out in the connection of the conditioned with the condition; they are supposed to be given in themselves, and contemporaneously. It is, moreover, just as natural to regard phenomena (in the minor) as things in themselves and as objects presented to the pure understanding, as in the major, in which complete abstraction was made of all conditions of intuition. But it is under these conditions alone that objects are given. Now we overlooked a remarkable distinction between the conceptions. The synthesis of the conditioned with its condition, and the complete series of the latter (in the major) are not limited by time, and do not contain the conception of succession. On the contrary, the empirical synthesis and the series of conditions in the phenomenal world—subsumed in the minor—are necessarily successive and given in time alone. It follows that I cannot presuppose in the minor, as I did in the major, the absolute totality of the synthesis and of the series therein represented; for in the major all the members of the series are given as things in themselves—without any limitations or conditions of time, while in the minor they are possible only in and through a successive regress, which cannot exist, except it be actually carried into execution in the world of phenomena.\n\nAfter this proof of the viciousness of the argument commonly employed in maintaining cosmological assertions, both parties may now be justly dismissed, as advancing claims without grounds or title. But the process has not been ended by convincing them that one or both were in the wrong and had maintained an assertion which was without valid grounds of proof. Nothing seems to be clearer than that, if one maintains: “The world has a beginning,” and another: “The world has no beginning,” one of the two must be right. But it is likewise clear that, if the evidence on both sides is equal, it is impossible to discover on what side the truth lies; and the controversy continues, although the parties have been recommended to peace before the tribunal of reason. There remains, then, no other means of settling the question than to convince the parties, who refute each other with such conclusiveness and ability, that they are disputing about nothing, and that a transcendental illusion has been mocking them with visions of reality where there is none. The mode of adjusting a dispute which cannot be decided upon its own merits, we shall now proceed to lay before our readers.\n\n---\n\nZeno of Elea, a subtle dialectician, was severely reprimanded by Plato as a sophist, who, merely from the base motive of exhibiting his skill in discussion, maintained and subverted the same proposition by arguments as powerful and convincing on the one side as on the other. He maintained, for example, that God (who was probably nothing more, in his view, than the world) is neither finite nor infinite, neither in motion nor in rest, neither similar nor dissimilar to any other thing. It seemed to those philosophers who criticized his mode of discussion that his purpose was to deny completely both of two self-contradictory propositions—which is absurd. But I cannot believe that there is any justice in this accusation. The first of these propositions I shall presently consider in a more detailed manner. With regard to the others, if by the word of God he understood merely the Universe, his meaning must have been—that it cannot be permanently present in one place—that is, at rest—nor be capable of changing its place—that is, of moving—because all places are in the universe, and the universe itself is, therefore, in no place. Again, if the universe contains in itself everything that exists, it cannot be similar or dissimilar to any other thing, because there is, in fact, no other thing with which it can be compared. If two opposite judgements presuppose a contingent impossible, or arbitrary condition, both—in spite of their opposition (which is, however, not properly or really a contradiction)—fall away; because the condition, which ensured the validity of both, has itself disappeared.\n\nIf we say: “Everybody has either a good or a bad smell,” we have omitted a third possible judgement—it has no smell at all; and thus both conflicting statements may be false. If we say: “It is either good-smelling or not good-smelling (vel suaveolens vel non-suaveolens),” both judgements are contradictorily opposed; and the contradictory opposite of the former judgement—some bodies are not good-smelling—embraces also those bodies which have no smell at all. In the preceding pair of opposed judgements (per disparata), the contingent condition of the conception of body (smell) attached to both conflicting statements, instead of having been omitted in the latter, which is consequently not the contradictory opposite of the former.\n\nIf, accordingly, we say: “The world is either infinite in extension, or it is not infinite (non est infinitus)”; and if the former proposition is false, its contradictory opposite—the world is not infinite—must be true. And thus I should deny the existence of an infinite, without, however affirming the existence of a finite world. But if we construct our proposition thus: “The world is either infinite or finite (non-infinite),” both statements may be false. For, in this case, we consider the world as per se determined in regard to quantity, and while, in the one judgement, we deny its infinite and consequently, perhaps, its independent existence; in the other, we append to the world, regarded as a thing in itself, a certain determination—that of finitude; and the latter may be false as well as the former, if the world is not given as a thing in itself, and thus neither as finite nor as infinite in quantity. This kind of opposition I may be allowed to term dialectical; that of contradictories may be called analytical opposition. Thus then, of two dialectically opposed judgements both may be false, from the fact, that the one is not a mere contradictory of the other, but actually enounces more than is requisite for a full and complete contradiction.\n\nWhen we regard the two propositions—“The world is infinite in quantity,” and, “The world is finite in quantity,” as contradictory opposites, we are assuming that the world—the complete series of phenomena—is a thing in itself. For it remains as a permanent quantity, whether I deny the infinite or the finite regress in the series of its phenomena. But if we dismiss this assumption—this transcendental illusion—and deny that it is a thing in itself, the contradictory opposition is metamorphosed into a merely dialectical one; and the world, as not existing in itself—independently of the regressive series of my representations—exists in like manner neither as a whole which is infinite nor as a whole which is finite in itself. The universe exists for me only in the empirical regress of the series of phenomena and not per se. If, then, it is always conditioned, it is never completely or as a whole; and it is, therefore, not an unconditioned whole and does not exist as such, either with an infinite, or with a finite quantity.\n\nWhat we have here said of the first cosmological idea—that of the absolute totality of quantity in phenomena—applies also to the others. The series of conditions is discoverable only in the regressive synthesis itself, and not in the phenomenon considered as a thing in itself—given prior to all regress. Hence I am compelled to say: “The aggregate of parts in a given phenomenon is in itself neither finite nor infinite; and these parts are given only in the regressive synthesis of decomposition—a synthesis which is never given in absolute completeness, either as finite, or as infinite.” The same is the case with the series of subordinated causes, or of the conditioned up to the unconditioned and necessary existence, which can never be regarded as in itself, and in its totality, either as finite or as infinite; because, as a series of subordinate representations, it subsists only in the dynamical regress and cannot be regarded as existing previously to this regress, or as a self-subsistent series of things.\n\nThus the antinomy of pure reason in its cosmological ideas disappears. For the above demonstration has established the fact that it is merely the product of a dialectical and illusory opposition, which arises from the application of the idea of absolute totality—admissible only as a condition of things in themselves—to phenomena, which exist only in our representations, and—when constituting a series—in a successive regress. This antinomy of reason may, however, be really profitable to our speculative interests, not in the way of contributing any dogmatical addition, but as presenting to us another material support in our critical investigations. For it furnishes us with an indirect proof of the transcendental ideality of phenomena, if our minds were not completely satisfied with the direct proof set forth in the Trancendental Æsthetic. The proof would proceed in the following dilemma. If the world is a whole existing in itself, it must be either finite or infinite. But it is neither finite nor infinite—as has been shown, on the one side, by the thesis, on the other, by the antithesis. Therefore the world—the content of all phenomena—is not a whole existing in itself. It follows that phenomena are nothing, apart from our representations. And this is what we mean by transcendental ideality.\n\nThis remark is of some importance. It enables us to see that the proofs of the fourfold antinomy are not mere sophistries—are not fallacious, but grounded on the nature of reason, and valid—under the supposition that phenomena are things in themselves. The opposition of the judgements which follow makes it evident that a fallacy lay in the initial supposition, and thus helps us to discover the true constitution of objects of sense. This transcendental dialectic does not favour scepticism, although it presents us with a triumphant demonstration of the advantages of the sceptical method, the great utility of which is apparent in the antinomy, where the arguments of reason were allowed to confront each other in undiminished force. And although the result of these conflicts of reason is not what we expected—although we have obtained no positive dogmatical addition to metaphysical science—we have still reaped a great advantage in the correction of our judgements on these subjects of thought.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0001","text":"# Cosmos Solution\n\n### Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem\n\nThe antinomy of pure reason is based upon the following dialectical argument: “If that which is conditioned is given, the whole series of its conditions is also given; but sensuous objects are given as conditioned; consequently...” This syllogism, the major of which seems so natural and evident, introduces as many cosmological ideas as there are different kinds of conditions in the synthesis of phenomena, in so far as these conditions constitute a series. These ideas require absolute totality in the series, and thus place reason in inextricable embarrassment. Before proceeding to expose the fallacy in this dialectical argument, it will be necessary to have a correct understanding of certain conceptions that appear in it.\n\nIn the first place, the following proposition is evident, and indubitably certain: “If the conditioned is given, a regress in the series of all its conditions is thereby imperatively required.” For the very conception of a conditioned is a conception of something related to a condition, and, if this condition is itself conditioned, to another condition—and so on through all the members of the series. This proposition is, therefore, analytical and has nothing to fear from transcendental criticism. It is a logical postulate of reason: to pursue, as far as possible, the connection of a conception with its conditions.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"67.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","text":"If, in the second place, both the conditioned and the condition are things in themselves, and if the former is given, not only is the regress to the latter requisite, but the latter is really given with the former. Now, as this is true of all the members of the series, the entire series of conditions, and with them the unconditioned, is at the same time given in the very fact of the conditioned, the existence of which is possible only in and through that series, being given. In this case, the synthesis of the conditioned with its condition, is a synthesis of the understanding merely, which represents things as they are, without regarding whether and how we can cognize them. But if I have to do with phenomena, which, in their character of mere representations, are not given, if I do not attain to a cognition of them (in other words, to themselves, for they are nothing more than empirical cognitions), I am not entitled to say: “If the conditioned is given, all its conditions (as phenomena) are also given.” I cannot, therefore, from the fact of a conditioned being given, infer the absolute totality of the series of its conditions. For phenomena are nothing but an empirical synthesis in apprehension or perception, and are therefore given only in it. Now, in speaking of phenomena it does not follow that, if the conditioned is given, the synthesis which constitutes its empirical condition is also thereby given and presupposed; such a synthesis can be established only by an actual regress in the series of conditions. But we are entitled to say in this case that a regress to the conditions of a conditioned, in other words, that a continuous empirical synthesis is enjoined; that, if the conditions are not given, they are at least required; and that we are certain to discover the conditions in this regress.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"67.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","text":"We can now see that the major, in the above cosmological syllogism, takes the conditioned in the transcendental signification which it has in the pure category, while the minor speaks of it in the empirical signification which it has in the category as applied to phenomena. There is, therefore, a dialectical fallacy in the syllogism—a sophisma figurae dictionis. But this fallacy is not a consciously devised one, but a perfectly natural illusion of the common reason of man. For, when a thing is given as conditioned, we presuppose in the major its conditions and their series, unperceived, as it were, and unseen; because this is nothing more than the logical requirement of complete and satisfactory premisses for a given conclusion. In this case, time is altogether left out in the connection of the conditioned with the condition; they are supposed to be given in themselves, and contemporaneously. It is, moreover, just as natural to regard phenomena (in the minor) as things in themselves and as objects presented to the pure understanding, as in the major, in which complete abstraction was made of all conditions of intuition. But it is under these conditions alone that objects are given. Now we overlooked a remarkable distinction between the conceptions. The synthesis of the conditioned with its condition, and the complete series of the latter (in the major) are not limited by time, and do not contain the conception of succession. On the contrary, the empirical synthesis and the series of conditions in the phenomenal world—subsumed in the minor—are necessarily successive and given in time alone. It follows that I cannot presuppose in the minor, as I did in the major, the absolute totality of the synthesis and of the series therein represented; for in the major all the members of the series are given as things in themselves—without any limitations or conditions of time, while in the minor they are possible only in and through a successive regress, which cannot exist, except it be actually carried into execution in the world of phenomena.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"67.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0004","text":"After this proof of the viciousness of the argument commonly employed in maintaining cosmological assertions, both parties may now be justly dismissed, as advancing claims without grounds or title. But the process has not been ended by convincing them that one or both were in the wrong and had maintained an assertion which was without valid grounds of proof. Nothing seems to be clearer than that, if one maintains: “The world has a beginning,” and another: “The world has no beginning,” one of the two must be right. But it is likewise clear that, if the evidence on both sides is equal, it is impossible to discover on what side the truth lies; and the controversy continues, although the parties have been recommended to peace before the tribunal of reason. There remains, then, no other means of settling the question than to convince the parties, who refute each other with such conclusiveness and ability, that they are disputing about nothing, and that a transcendental illusion has been mocking them with visions of reality where there is none. The mode of adjusting a dispute which cannot be decided upon its own merits, we shall now proceed to lay before our readers.\n\n---","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"67.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0005","text":"Zeno of Elea, a subtle dialectician, was severely reprimanded by Plato as a sophist, who, merely from the base motive of exhibiting his skill in discussion, maintained and subverted the same proposition by arguments as powerful and convincing on the one side as on the other. He maintained, for example, that God (who was probably nothing more, in his view, than the world) is neither finite nor infinite, neither in motion nor in rest, neither similar nor dissimilar to any other thing. It seemed to those philosophers who criticized his mode of discussion that his purpose was to deny completely both of two self-contradictory propositions—which is absurd. But I cannot believe that there is any justice in this accusation. The first of these propositions I shall presently consider in a more detailed manner. With regard to the others, if by the word of God he understood merely the Universe, his meaning must have been—that it cannot be permanently present in one place—that is, at rest—nor be capable of changing its place—that is, of moving—because all places are in the universe, and the universe itself is, therefore, in no place. Again, if the universe contains in itself everything that exists, it cannot be similar or dissimilar to any other thing, because there is, in fact, no other thing with which it can be compared. If two opposite judgements presuppose a contingent impossible, or arbitrary condition, both—in spite of their opposition (which is, however, not properly or really a contradiction)—fall away; because the condition, which ensured the validity of both, has itself disappeared.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"67.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0006","text":"If we say: “Everybody has either a good or a bad smell,” we have omitted a third possible judgement—it has no smell at all; and thus both conflicting statements may be false. If we say: “It is either good-smelling or not good-smelling (vel suaveolens vel non-suaveolens),” both judgements are contradictorily opposed; and the contradictory opposite of the former judgement—some bodies are not good-smelling—embraces also those bodies which have no smell at all. In the preceding pair of opposed judgements (per disparata), the contingent condition of the conception of body (smell) attached to both conflicting statements, instead of having been omitted in the latter, which is consequently not the contradictory opposite of the former.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"67.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0007","text":"If, accordingly, we say: “The world is either infinite in extension, or it is not infinite (non est infinitus)”; and if the former proposition is false, its contradictory opposite—the world is not infinite—must be true. And thus I should deny the existence of an infinite, without, however affirming the existence of a finite world. But if we construct our proposition thus: “The world is either infinite or finite (non-infinite),” both statements may be false. For, in this case, we consider the world as per se determined in regard to quantity, and while, in the one judgement, we deny its infinite and consequently, perhaps, its independent existence; in the other, we append to the world, regarded as a thing in itself, a certain determination—that of finitude; and the latter may be false as well as the former, if the world is not given as a thing in itself, and thus neither as finite nor as infinite in quantity. This kind of opposition I may be allowed to term dialectical; that of contradictories may be called analytical opposition. Thus then, of two dialectically opposed judgements both may be false, from the fact, that the one is not a mere contradictory of the other, but actually enounces more than is requisite for a full and complete contradiction.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"67.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","text":"When we regard the two propositions—“The world is infinite in quantity,” and, “The world is finite in quantity,” as contradictory opposites, we are assuming that the world—the complete series of phenomena—is a thing in itself. For it remains as a permanent quantity, whether I deny the infinite or the finite regress in the series of its phenomena. But if we dismiss this assumption—this transcendental illusion—and deny that it is a thing in itself, the contradictory opposition is metamorphosed into a merely dialectical one; and the world, as not existing in itself—independently of the regressive series of my representations—exists in like manner neither as a whole which is infinite nor as a whole which is finite in itself. The universe exists for me only in the empirical regress of the series of phenomena and not per se. If, then, it is always conditioned, it is never completely or as a whole; and it is, therefore, not an unconditioned whole and does not exist as such, either with an infinite, or with a finite quantity.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"67.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0009","text":"What we have here said of the first cosmological idea—that of the absolute totality of quantity in phenomena—applies also to the others. The series of conditions is discoverable only in the regressive synthesis itself, and not in the phenomenon considered as a thing in itself—given prior to all regress. Hence I am compelled to say: “The aggregate of parts in a given phenomenon is in itself neither finite nor infinite; and these parts are given only in the regressive synthesis of decomposition—a synthesis which is never given in absolute completeness, either as finite, or as infinite.” The same is the case with the series of subordinated causes, or of the conditioned up to the unconditioned and necessary existence, which can never be regarded as in itself, and in its totality, either as finite or as infinite; because, as a series of subordinate representations, it subsists only in the dynamical regress and cannot be regarded as existing previously to this regress, or as a self-subsistent series of things.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"67.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0010","text":"Thus the antinomy of pure reason in its cosmological ideas disappears. For the above demonstration has established the fact that it is merely the product of a dialectical and illusory opposition, which arises from the application of the idea of absolute totality—admissible only as a condition of things in themselves—to phenomena, which exist only in our representations, and—when constituting a series—in a successive regress. This antinomy of reason may, however, be really profitable to our speculative interests, not in the way of contributing any dogmatical addition, but as presenting to us another material support in our critical investigations. For it furnishes us with an indirect proof of the transcendental ideality of phenomena, if our minds were not completely satisfied with the direct proof set forth in the Trancendental Æsthetic. The proof would proceed in the following dilemma. If the world is a whole existing in itself, it must be either finite or infinite. But it is neither finite nor infinite—as has been shown, on the one side, by the thesis, on the other, by the antithesis. Therefore the world—the content of all phenomena—is not a whole existing in itself. It follows that phenomena are nothing, apart from our representations. And this is what we mean by transcendental ideality.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"67.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0011","text":"This remark is of some importance. It enables us to see that the proofs of the fourfold antinomy are not mere sophistries—are not fallacious, but grounded on the nature of reason, and valid—under the supposition that phenomena are things in themselves. The opposition of the judgements which follow makes it evident that a fallacy lay in the initial supposition, and thus helps us to discover the true constitution of objects of sense. This transcendental dialectic does not favour scepticism, although it presents us with a triumphant demonstration of the advantages of the sceptical method, the great utility of which is apparent in the antinomy, where the arguments of reason were allowed to confront each other in undiminished force. And although the result of these conflicts of reason is not what we expected—although we have obtained no positive dogmatical addition to metaphysical science—we have still reaped a great advantage in the correction of our judgements on these subjects of thought.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"67.11"}],"sha256":"cced6fe22d5d47488f1031a216a8e89726fb6f42aab2dc0ef4fdd5d087f8b785","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP","title":"Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas","order":68,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SVRP","text":"### Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas\n\nThe cosmological principle of totality could not give us any certain knowledge in regard to the maximum in the series of conditions in the world of sense, considered as a thing in itself. The actual regress in the series is the only means of approaching this maximum. This principle of pure reason, therefore, may still be considered as valid—not as an axiom enabling us to cogitate totality in the object as actual, but as a problem for the understanding, which requires it to institute and to continue, in conformity with the idea of totality in the mind, the regress in the series of the conditions of a given conditioned. For in the world of sense, that is, in space and time, every condition which we discover in our investigation of phenomena is itself conditioned; because sensuous objects are not things in themselves (in which case an absolutely unconditioned might be reached in the progress of cognition), but are merely empirical representations the conditions of which must always be found in intuition. The principle of reason is therefore properly a mere rule—prescribing a regress in the series of conditions for given phenomena, and prohibiting any pause or rest on an absolutely unconditioned. It is, therefore, not a principle of the possibility of experience or of the empirical cognition of sensuous objects—consequently not a principle of the understanding; for every experience is confined within certain proper limits determined by the given intuition. Still less is it a constitutive principle of reason authorizing us to extend our conception of the sensuous world beyond all possible experience. It is merely a principle for the enlargement and extension of experience as far as is possible for human faculties. It forbids us to consider any empirical limits as absolute. It is, hence, a principle of reason, which, as a rule, dictates how we ought to proceed in our empirical regress, but is unable to anticipate or indicate prior to the empirical regress what is given in the object itself. I have termed it for this reason a regulative principle of reason; while the principle of the absolute totality of the series of conditions, as existing in itself and given in the object, is a constitutive cosmological principle. This distinction will at once demonstrate the falsehood of the constitutive principle, and prevent us from attributing (by a transcendental subreptio) objective reality to an idea, which is valid only as a rule.\n\nIn order to understand the proper meaning of this rule of pure reason, we must notice first that it cannot tell us what the object is, but only how the empirical regress is to be proceeded with in order to attain to the complete conception of the object. If it gave us any information in respect to the former statement, it would be a constitutive principle—a principle impossible from the nature of pure reason. It will not therefore enable us to establish any such conclusions as: “The series of conditions for a given conditioned is in itself finite,” or, “It is infinite.” For, in this case, we should be cogitating in the mere idea of absolute totality, an object which is not and cannot be given in experience; inasmuch as we should be attributing a reality objective and independent of the empirical synthesis, to a series of phenomena. This idea of reason cannot then be regarded as valid—except as a rule for the regressive synthesis in the series of conditions, according to which we must proceed from the conditioned, through all intermediate and subordinate conditions, up to the unconditioned; although this goal is unattained and unattainable. For the absolutely unconditioned cannot be discovered in the sphere of experience.\n\nWe now proceed to determine clearly our notion of a synthesis which can never be complete. There are two terms commonly employed for this purpose. These terms are regarded as expressions of different and distinguishable notions, although the ground of the distinction has never been clearly exposed. The term employed by the mathematicians is progressus in infinitum. The philosophers prefer the expression progressus in indefinitum. Without detaining the reader with an examination of the reasons for such a distinction, or with remarks on the right or wrong use of the terms, I shall endeavour clearly to determine these conceptions, so far as is necessary for the purpose in this Critique.\n\nWe may, with propriety, say of a straight line, that it may be produced to infinity. In this case the distinction between a progressus in infinitum and a progressus in indefinitum is a mere piece of subtlety. For, although when we say, “Produce a straight line,” it is more correct to say in indefinitum than in infinitum; because the former means, “Produce it as far as you please,” the second, “You must not cease to produce it”; the expression in infinitum is, when we are speaking of the power to do it, perfectly correct, for we can always make it longer if we please—on to infinity. And this remark holds good in all cases, when we speak of a progressus, that is, an advancement from the condition to the conditioned; this possible advancement always proceeds to infinity. We may proceed from a given pair in the descending line of generation from father to son, and cogitate a never-ending line of descendants from it. For in such a case reason does not demand absolute totality in the series, because it does not presuppose it as a condition and as given (datum), but merely as conditioned, and as capable of being given (dabile).\n\nVery different is the case with the problem: “How far the regress, which ascends from the given conditioned to the conditions, must extend”; whether I can say: “It is a regress in infinitum,” or only “in indefinitum”; and whether, for example, setting out from the human beings at present alive in the world, I may ascend in the series of their ancestors, in infinitum—or whether all that can be said is, that so far as I have proceeded, I have discovered no empirical ground for considering the series limited, so that I am justified, and indeed, compelled to search for ancestors still further back, although I am not obliged by the idea of reason to presuppose them.\n\nMy answer to this question is: “If the series is given in empirical intuition as a whole, the regress in the series of its internal conditions proceeds in infinitum; but, if only one member of the series is given, from which the regress is to proceed to absolute totality, the regress is possible only in indefinitum.” For example, the division of a portion of matter given within certain limits—of a body, that is—proceeds in infinitum. For, as the condition of this whole is its part, and the condition of the part a part of the part, and so on, and as in this regress of decomposition an unconditioned indivisible member of the series of conditions is not to be found; there are no reasons or grounds in experience for stopping in the division, but, on the contrary, the more remote members of the division are actually and empirically given prior to this division. That is to say, the division proceeds to infinity. On the other hand, the series of ancestors of any given human being is not given, in its absolute totality, in any experience, and yet the regress proceeds from every genealogical member of this series to one still higher, and does not meet with any empirical limit presenting an absolutely unconditioned member of the series. But as the members of such a series are not contained in the empirical intuition of the whole, prior to the regress, this regress does not proceed to infinity, but only in indefinitum, that is, we are called upon to discover other and higher members, which are themselves always conditioned.\n\nIn neither case—the regressus in infinitum, nor the regressus in indefinitum, is the series of conditions to be considered as actually infinite in the object itself. This might be true of things in themselves, but it cannot be asserted of phenomena, which, as conditions of each other, are only given in the empirical regress itself. Hence, the question no longer is, “What is the quantity of this series of conditions in itself—is it finite or infinite?” for it is nothing in itself; but, “How is the empirical regress to be commenced, and how far ought we to proceed with it?” And here a signal distinction in the application of this rule becomes apparent. If the whole is given empirically, it is possible to recede in the series of its internal conditions to infinity. But if the whole is not given, and can only be given by and through the empirical regress, I can only say: “It is possible to infinity, to proceed to still higher conditions in the series.” In the first case, I am justified in asserting that more members are empirically given in the object than I attain to in the regress (of decomposition). In the second case, I am justified only in saying, that I can always proceed further in the regress, because no member of the series is given as absolutely conditioned, and thus a higher member is possible, and an inquiry with regard to it is necessary. In the one case it is necessary to find other members of the series, in the other it is necessary to inquire for others, inasmuch as experience presents no absolute limitation of the regress. For, either you do not possess a perception which absolutely limits your empirical regress, and in this case the regress cannot be regarded as complete; or, you do possess such a limitative perception, in which case it is not a part of your series (for that which limits must be distinct from that which is limited by it), and it is incumbent on you to continue your regress up to this condition, and so on.\n\nThese remarks will be placed in their proper light by their application in the following section.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","text":"### Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas\n\nThe cosmological principle of totality could not give us any certain knowledge in regard to the maximum in the series of conditions in the world of sense, considered as a thing in itself. The actual regress in the series is the only means of approaching this maximum. This principle of pure reason, therefore, may still be considered as valid—not as an axiom enabling us to cogitate totality in the object as actual, but as a problem for the understanding, which requires it to institute and to continue, in conformity with the idea of totality in the mind, the regress in the series of the conditions of a given conditioned. For in the world of sense, that is, in space and time, every condition which we discover in our investigation of phenomena is itself conditioned; because sensuous objects are not things in themselves (in which case an absolutely unconditioned might be reached in the progress of cognition), but are merely empirical representations the conditions of which must always be found in intuition. The principle of reason is therefore properly a mere rule—prescribing a regress in the series of conditions for given phenomena, and prohibiting any pause or rest on an absolutely unconditioned. It is, therefore, not a principle of the possibility of experience or of the empirical cognition of sensuous objects—consequently not a principle of the understanding; for every experience is confined within certain proper limits determined by the given intuition. Still less is it a constitutive principle of reason authorizing us to extend our conception of the sensuous world beyond all possible experience. It is merely a principle for the enlargement and extension of experience as far as is possible for human faculties. It forbids us to consider any empirical limits as absolute. It is, hence, a principle of reason, which, as a rule, dictates how we ought to proceed in our empirical regress, but is unable to anticipate or indicate prior to the empirical regress what is given in the object itself. I have termed it for this reason a regulative principle of reason; while the principle of the absolute totality of the series of conditions, as existing in itself and given in the object, is a constitutive cosmological principle. This distinction will at once demonstrate the falsehood of the constitutive principle, and prevent us from attributing (by a transcendental subreptio) objective reality to an idea, which is valid only as a rule.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"68.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","text":"In order to understand the proper meaning of this rule of pure reason, we must notice first that it cannot tell us what the object is, but only how the empirical regress is to be proceeded with in order to attain to the complete conception of the object. If it gave us any information in respect to the former statement, it would be a constitutive principle—a principle impossible from the nature of pure reason. It will not therefore enable us to establish any such conclusions as: “The series of conditions for a given conditioned is in itself finite,” or, “It is infinite.” For, in this case, we should be cogitating in the mere idea of absolute totality, an object which is not and cannot be given in experience; inasmuch as we should be attributing a reality objective and independent of the empirical synthesis, to a series of phenomena. This idea of reason cannot then be regarded as valid—except as a rule for the regressive synthesis in the series of conditions, according to which we must proceed from the conditioned, through all intermediate and subordinate conditions, up to the unconditioned; although this goal is unattained and unattainable. For the absolutely unconditioned cannot be discovered in the sphere of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"68.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0003","text":"We now proceed to determine clearly our notion of a synthesis which can never be complete. There are two terms commonly employed for this purpose. These terms are regarded as expressions of different and distinguishable notions, although the ground of the distinction has never been clearly exposed. The term employed by the mathematicians is progressus in infinitum. The philosophers prefer the expression progressus in indefinitum. Without detaining the reader with an examination of the reasons for such a distinction, or with remarks on the right or wrong use of the terms, I shall endeavour clearly to determine these conceptions, so far as is necessary for the purpose in this Critique.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"68.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0004","text":"We may, with propriety, say of a straight line, that it may be produced to infinity. In this case the distinction between a progressus in infinitum and a progressus in indefinitum is a mere piece of subtlety. For, although when we say, “Produce a straight line,” it is more correct to say in indefinitum than in infinitum; because the former means, “Produce it as far as you please,” the second, “You must not cease to produce it”; the expression in infinitum is, when we are speaking of the power to do it, perfectly correct, for we can always make it longer if we please—on to infinity. And this remark holds good in all cases, when we speak of a progressus, that is, an advancement from the condition to the conditioned; this possible advancement always proceeds to infinity. We may proceed from a given pair in the descending line of generation from father to son, and cogitate a never-ending line of descendants from it. For in such a case reason does not demand absolute totality in the series, because it does not presuppose it as a condition and as given (datum), but merely as conditioned, and as capable of being given (dabile).","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"68.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0005","text":"Very different is the case with the problem: “How far the regress, which ascends from the given conditioned to the conditions, must extend”; whether I can say: “It is a regress in infinitum,” or only “in indefinitum”; and whether, for example, setting out from the human beings at present alive in the world, I may ascend in the series of their ancestors, in infinitum—or whether all that can be said is, that so far as I have proceeded, I have discovered no empirical ground for considering the series limited, so that I am justified, and indeed, compelled to search for ancestors still further back, although I am not obliged by the idea of reason to presuppose them.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"68.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","text":"My answer to this question is: “If the series is given in empirical intuition as a whole, the regress in the series of its internal conditions proceeds in infinitum; but, if only one member of the series is given, from which the regress is to proceed to absolute totality, the regress is possible only in indefinitum.” For example, the division of a portion of matter given within certain limits—of a body, that is—proceeds in infinitum. For, as the condition of this whole is its part, and the condition of the part a part of the part, and so on, and as in this regress of decomposition an unconditioned indivisible member of the series of conditions is not to be found; there are no reasons or grounds in experience for stopping in the division, but, on the contrary, the more remote members of the division are actually and empirically given prior to this division. That is to say, the division proceeds to infinity. On the other hand, the series of ancestors of any given human being is not given, in its absolute totality, in any experience, and yet the regress proceeds from every genealogical member of this series to one still higher, and does not meet with any empirical limit presenting an absolutely unconditioned member of the series. But as the members of such a series are not contained in the empirical intuition of the whole, prior to the regress, this regress does not proceed to infinity, but only in indefinitum, that is, we are called upon to discover other and higher members, which are themselves always conditioned.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"68.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","text":"In neither case—the regressus in infinitum, nor the regressus in indefinitum, is the series of conditions to be considered as actually infinite in the object itself. This might be true of things in themselves, but it cannot be asserted of phenomena, which, as conditions of each other, are only given in the empirical regress itself. Hence, the question no longer is, “What is the quantity of this series of conditions in itself—is it finite or infinite?” for it is nothing in itself; but, “How is the empirical regress to be commenced, and how far ought we to proceed with it?” And here a signal distinction in the application of this rule becomes apparent. If the whole is given empirically, it is possible to recede in the series of its internal conditions to infinity. But if the whole is not given, and can only be given by and through the empirical regress, I can only say: “It is possible to infinity, to proceed to still higher conditions in the series.” In the first case, I am justified in asserting that more members are empirically given in the object than I attain to in the regress (of decomposition). In the second case, I am justified only in saying, that I can always proceed further in the regress, because no member of the series is given as absolutely conditioned, and thus a higher member is possible, and an inquiry with regard to it is necessary. In the one case it is necessary to find other members of the series, in the other it is necessary to inquire for others, inasmuch as experience presents no absolute limitation of the regress. For, either you do not possess a perception which absolutely limits your empirical regress, and in this case the regress cannot be regarded as complete; or, you do possess such a limitative perception, in which case it is not a part of your series (for that which limits must be distinct from that which is limited by it), and it is incumbent on you to continue your regress up to this condition, and so on.\n\nThese remarks will be placed in their proper light by their application in the following section.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"68.7"}],"sha256":"90bdc4db5dd89ebc273654baa4a87564726004b77119d703f088f1e71d64d80d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU","title":"Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas","order":69,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIEU","text":"### Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas\n\nWe have shown that no transcendental use can be made either of the conceptions of reason or of understanding. We have shown, likewise, that the demand of absolute totality in the series of conditions in the world of sense arises from a transcendental employment of reason, resting on the opinion that phenomena are to be regarded as things in themselves. It follows that we are not required to answer the question respecting the absolute quantity of a series—whether it is in itself limited or unlimited. We are only called upon to determine how far we must proceed in the empirical regress from condition to condition, in order to discover, in conformity with the rule of reason, a full and correct answer to the questions proposed by reason itself.\n\nThis principle of reason is hence valid only as a rule for the extension of a possible experience—its invalidity as a principle constitutive of phenomena in themselves having been sufficiently demonstrated. And thus, too, the antinomial conflict of reason with itself is completely put an end to; inasmuch as we have not only presented a critical solution of the fallacy lurking in the opposite statements of reason, but have shown the true meaning of the ideas which gave rise to these statements. The dialectical principle of reason has, therefore, been changed into a doctrinal principle. But in fact, if this principle, in the subjective signification which we have shown to be its only true sense, may be guaranteed as a principle of the unceasing extension of the employment of our understanding, its influence and value are just as great as if it were an axiom for the à priori determination of objects. For such an axiom could not exert a stronger influence on the extension and rectification of our knowledge, otherwise than by procuring for the principles of the understanding the most widely expanded employment in the field of experience.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","text":"### Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas\n\nWe have shown that no transcendental use can be made either of the conceptions of reason or of understanding. We have shown, likewise, that the demand of absolute totality in the series of conditions in the world of sense arises from a transcendental employment of reason, resting on the opinion that phenomena are to be regarded as things in themselves. It follows that we are not required to answer the question respecting the absolute quantity of a series—whether it is in itself limited or unlimited. We are only called upon to determine how far we must proceed in the empirical regress from condition to condition, in order to discover, in conformity with the rule of reason, a full and correct answer to the questions proposed by reason itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"69.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","text":"This principle of reason is hence valid only as a rule for the extension of a possible experience—its invalidity as a principle constitutive of phenomena in themselves having been sufficiently demonstrated. And thus, too, the antinomial conflict of reason with itself is completely put an end to; inasmuch as we have not only presented a critical solution of the fallacy lurking in the opposite statements of reason, but have shown the true meaning of the ideas which gave rise to these statements. The dialectical principle of reason has, therefore, been changed into a doctrinal principle. But in fact, if this principle, in the subjective signification which we have shown to be its only true sense, may be guaranteed as a principle of the unceasing extension of the employment of our understanding, its influence and value are just as great as if it were an axiom for the à priori determination of objects. For such an axiom could not exert a stronger influence on the extension and rectification of our knowledge, otherwise than by procuring for the principles of the understanding the most widely expanded employment in the field of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"69.2"}],"sha256":"2d6dae16a6cf0948500b3eff00c01a164f6ef7af3615072448fdb32c0e581d71","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI","title":"I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe","order":70,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_ISCI","text":"### I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe\n\nHere, as well as in the case of the other cosmological problems, the ground of the regulative principle of reason is the proposition that in our empirical regress no experience of an absolute limit, and consequently no experience of a condition, which is itself absolutely unconditioned, is discoverable. And the truth of this proposition itself rests upon the consideration that such an experience must represent to us phenomena as limited by nothing or the mere void, on which our continued regress by means of perception must abut—which is impossible.\n\nNow this proposition, which declares that every condition attained in the empirical regress must itself be considered empirically conditioned, contains the rule in terminis, which requires me, to whatever extent I may have proceeded in the ascending series, always to look for some higher member in the series—whether this member is to become known to me through experience, or not.\n\nNothing further is necessary, then, for the solution of the first cosmological problem, than to decide, whether, in the regress to the unconditioned quantity of the universe (as regards space and time), this never limited ascent ought to be called a regressus in infinitum or indefinitum.\n\nThe general representation which we form in our minds of the series of all past states or conditions of the world, or of all the things which at present exist in it, is itself nothing more than a possible empirical regress, which is cogitated—although in an undetermined manner—in the mind, and which gives rise to the conception of a series of conditions for a given object.[[60]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-60) Now I have a conception of the universe, but not an intuition—that is, not an intuition of it as a whole. Thus I cannot infer the magnitude of the regress from the quantity or magnitude of the world, and determine the former by means of the latter; on the contrary, I must first of all form a conception of the quantity or magnitude of the world from the magnitude of the empirical regress. But of this regress I know nothing more than that I ought to proceed from every given member of the series of conditions to one still higher. But the quantity of the universe is not thereby determined, and we cannot affirm that this regress proceeds in infinitum. Such an affirmation would anticipate the members of the series which have not yet been reached, and represent the number of them as beyond the grasp of any empirical synthesis; it would consequently determine the cosmical quantity prior to the regress (although only in a negative manner)—which is impossible. For the world is not given in its totality in any intuition: consequently, its quantity cannot be given prior to the regress. It follows that we are unable to make any declaration respecting the cosmical quantity in itself—not even that the regress in it is a regress in infinitum; we must only endeavour to attain to a conception of the quantity of the universe, in conformity with the rule which determines the empirical regress in it. But this rule merely requires us never to admit an absolute limit to our series—how far soever we may have proceeded in it, but always, on the contrary, to subordinate every phenomenon to some other as its condition, and consequently to proceed to this higher phenomenon. Such a regress is, therefore, the regressus in indefinitum, which, as not determining a quantity in the object, is clearly distinguishable from the regressus in infinitum.\n\n[[60]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-60) The cosmical series can neither be greater nor smaller than the possible empirical regress, upon which its conception is based. And as this regress cannot be a determinate infinite regress, still less a determinate finite (absolutely limited), it is evident that we cannot regard the world as either finite or infinite, because the regress, which gives us the representation of the world, is neither finite nor infinite.\n\nIt follows from what we have said that we are not justified in declaring the world to be infinite in space, or as regards past time. For this conception of an infinite given quantity is empirical; but we cannot apply the conception of an infinite quantity to the world as an object of the senses. I cannot say, “The regress from a given perception to everything limited either in space or time, proceeds in infinitum,” for this presupposes an infinite cosmical quantity; neither can I say, “It is finite,” for an absolute limit is likewise impossible in experience. It follows that I am not entitled to make any assertion at all respecting the whole object of experience—the world of sense; I must limit my declarations to the rule according to which experience or empirical knowledge is to be attained.\n\nTo the question, therefore, respecting the cosmical quantity, the first and negative answer is: “The world has no beginning in time, and no absolute limit in space.”\n\nFor, in the contrary case, it would be limited by a void time on the one hand, and by a void space on the other. Now, since the world, as a phenomenon, cannot be thus limited in itself for a phenomenon is not a thing in itself; it must be possible for us to have a perception of this limitation by a void time and a void space. But such a perception—such an experience is impossible; because it has no content. Consequently, an absolute cosmical limit is empirically, and therefore absolutely, impossible.[[61]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-61)\n\n[[61]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-61) The reader will remark that the proof presented above is very different from the dogmatical demonstration given in the antithesis of the first antinomy. In that demonstration, it was taken for granted that the world is a thing in itself—given in its totality prior to all regress, and a determined position in space and time was denied to it—if it was not considered as occupying all time and all space. Hence our conclusion differed from that given above; for we inferred in the antithesis the actual infinity of the world.\n\nFrom this follows the affirmative answer: “The regress in the series of phenomena—as a determination of the cosmical quantity, proceeds in indefinitum.” This is equivalent to saying: “The world of sense has no absolute quantity, but the empirical regress (through which alone the world of sense is presented to us on the side of its conditions) rests upon a rule, which requires it to proceed from every member of the series, as conditioned, to one still more remote (whether through personal experience, or by means of history, or the chain of cause and effect), and not to cease at any point in this extension of the possible empirical employment of the understanding.” And this is the proper and only use which reason can make of its principles.\n\nThe above rule does not prescribe an unceasing regress in one kind of phenomena. It does not, for example, forbid us, in our ascent from an individual human being through the line of his ancestors, to expect that we shall discover at some point of the regress a primeval pair, or to admit, in the series of heavenly bodies, a sun at the farthest possible distance from some centre. All that it demands is a perpetual progress from phenomena to phenomena, even although an actual perception is not presented by them (as in the case of our perceptions being so weak as that we are unable to become conscious of them), since they, nevertheless, belong to possible experience.\n\nEvery beginning is in time, and all limits to extension are in space. But space and time are in the world of sense. Consequently phenomena in the world are conditionally limited, but the world itself is not limited, either conditionally or unconditionally.\n\nFor this reason, and because neither the world nor the cosmical series of conditions to a given conditioned can be completely given, our conception of the cosmical quantity is given only in and through the regress and not prior to it—in a collective intuition. But the regress itself is really nothing more than the determining of the cosmical quantity, and cannot therefore give us any determined conception of it—still less a conception of a quantity which is, in relation to a certain standard, infinite. The regress does not, therefore, proceed to infinity (an infinity given), but only to an indefinite extent, for or the of presenting to us a quantity—realized only in and through the regress itself.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","text":"### I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe\n\nHere, as well as in the case of the other cosmological problems, the ground of the regulative principle of reason is the proposition that in our empirical regress no experience of an absolute limit, and consequently no experience of a condition, which is itself absolutely unconditioned, is discoverable. And the truth of this proposition itself rests upon the consideration that such an experience must represent to us phenomena as limited by nothing or the mere void, on which our continued regress by means of perception must abut—which is impossible.\n\nNow this proposition, which declares that every condition attained in the empirical regress must itself be considered empirically conditioned, contains the rule in terminis, which requires me, to whatever extent I may have proceeded in the ascending series, always to look for some higher member in the series—whether this member is to become known to me through experience, or not.\n\nNothing further is necessary, then, for the solution of the first cosmological problem, than to decide, whether, in the regress to the unconditioned quantity of the universe (as regards space and time), this never limited ascent ought to be called a regressus in infinitum or indefinitum.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"70.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","text":"The general representation which we form in our minds of the series of all past states or conditions of the world, or of all the things which at present exist in it, is itself nothing more than a possible empirical regress, which is cogitated—although in an undetermined manner—in the mind, and which gives rise to the conception of a series of conditions for a given object.[[60]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-60) Now I have a conception of the universe, but not an intuition—that is, not an intuition of it as a whole. Thus I cannot infer the magnitude of the regress from the quantity or magnitude of the world, and determine the former by means of the latter; on the contrary, I must first of all form a conception of the quantity or magnitude of the world from the magnitude of the empirical regress. But of this regress I know nothing more than that I ought to proceed from every given member of the series of conditions to one still higher. But the quantity of the universe is not thereby determined, and we cannot affirm that this regress proceeds in infinitum. Such an affirmation would anticipate the members of the series which have not yet been reached, and represent the number of them as beyond the grasp of any empirical synthesis; it would consequently determine the cosmical quantity prior to the regress (although only in a negative manner)—which is impossible. For the world is not given in its totality in any intuition: consequently, its quantity cannot be given prior to the regress. It follows that we are unable to make any declaration respecting the cosmical quantity in itself—not even that the regress in it is a regress in infinitum; we must only endeavour to attain to a conception of the quantity of the universe, in conformity with the rule which determines the empirical regress in it. But this rule merely requires us never to admit an absolute limit to our series—how far soever we may have proceeded in it, but always, on the contrary, to subordinate every phenomenon to some other as its condition, and consequently to proceed to this higher phenomenon. Such a regress is, therefore, the regressus in indefinitum, which, as not determining a quantity in the object, is clearly distinguishable from the regressus in infinitum.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"70.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","text":"[[60]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-60) The cosmical series can neither be greater nor smaller than the possible empirical regress, upon which its conception is based. And as this regress cannot be a determinate infinite regress, still less a determinate finite (absolutely limited), it is evident that we cannot regard the world as either finite or infinite, because the regress, which gives us the representation of the world, is neither finite nor infinite.\n\nIt follows from what we have said that we are not justified in declaring the world to be infinite in space, or as regards past time. For this conception of an infinite given quantity is empirical; but we cannot apply the conception of an infinite quantity to the world as an object of the senses. I cannot say, “The regress from a given perception to everything limited either in space or time, proceeds in infinitum,” for this presupposes an infinite cosmical quantity; neither can I say, “It is finite,” for an absolute limit is likewise impossible in experience. It follows that I am not entitled to make any assertion at all respecting the whole object of experience—the world of sense; I must limit my declarations to the rule according to which experience or empirical knowledge is to be attained.\n\nTo the question, therefore, respecting the cosmical quantity, the first and negative answer is: “The world has no beginning in time, and no absolute limit in space.”","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"70.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","text":"For, in the contrary case, it would be limited by a void time on the one hand, and by a void space on the other. Now, since the world, as a phenomenon, cannot be thus limited in itself for a phenomenon is not a thing in itself; it must be possible for us to have a perception of this limitation by a void time and a void space. But such a perception—such an experience is impossible; because it has no content. Consequently, an absolute cosmical limit is empirically, and therefore absolutely, impossible.[[61]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-61)\n\n[[61]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-61) The reader will remark that the proof presented above is very different from the dogmatical demonstration given in the antithesis of the first antinomy. In that demonstration, it was taken for granted that the world is a thing in itself—given in its totality prior to all regress, and a determined position in space and time was denied to it—if it was not considered as occupying all time and all space. Hence our conclusion differed from that given above; for we inferred in the antithesis the actual infinity of the world.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"70.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","text":"From this follows the affirmative answer: “The regress in the series of phenomena—as a determination of the cosmical quantity, proceeds in indefinitum.” This is equivalent to saying: “The world of sense has no absolute quantity, but the empirical regress (through which alone the world of sense is presented to us on the side of its conditions) rests upon a rule, which requires it to proceed from every member of the series, as conditioned, to one still more remote (whether through personal experience, or by means of history, or the chain of cause and effect), and not to cease at any point in this extension of the possible empirical employment of the understanding.” And this is the proper and only use which reason can make of its principles.\n\nThe above rule does not prescribe an unceasing regress in one kind of phenomena. It does not, for example, forbid us, in our ascent from an individual human being through the line of his ancestors, to expect that we shall discover at some point of the regress a primeval pair, or to admit, in the series of heavenly bodies, a sun at the farthest possible distance from some centre. All that it demands is a perpetual progress from phenomena to phenomena, even although an actual perception is not presented by them (as in the case of our perceptions being so weak as that we are unable to become conscious of them), since they, nevertheless, belong to possible experience.\n\nEvery beginning is in time, and all limits to extension are in space. But space and time are in the world of sense. Consequently phenomena in the world are conditionally limited, but the world itself is not limited, either conditionally or unconditionally.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"70.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0006","text":"For this reason, and because neither the world nor the cosmical series of conditions to a given conditioned can be completely given, our conception of the cosmical quantity is given only in and through the regress and not prior to it—in a collective intuition. But the regress itself is really nothing more than the determining of the cosmical quantity, and cannot therefore give us any determined conception of it—still less a conception of a quantity which is, in relation to a certain standard, infinite. The regress does not, therefore, proceed to infinity (an infinity given), but only to an indefinite extent, for or the of presenting to us a quantity—realized only in and through the regress itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"70.6"}],"sha256":"f73411a4d5bc48f7215cb95891b5612b9a21b87ff20877c53fe901c9ad6bffca","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2","title":"II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition","order":71,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_IS_2","text":"### II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition\n\nWhen I divide a whole which is given in intuition, I proceed from a conditioned to its conditions. The division of the parts of the whole (subdivisio or decompositio) is a regress in the series of these conditions. The absolute totality of this series would be actually attained and given to the mind, if the regress could arrive at simple parts. But if all the parts in a continuous decomposition are themselves divisible, the division, that is to say, the regress, proceeds from the conditioned to its conditions in infinitum; because the conditions (the parts) are themselves contained in the conditioned, and, as the latter is given in a limited intuition, the former are all given along with it. This regress cannot, therefore, be called a regressus in indefinitum, as happened in the case of the preceding cosmological idea, the regress in which proceeded from the conditioned to the conditions not given contemporaneously and along with it, but discoverable only through the empirical regress. We are not, however, entitled to affirm of a whole of this kind, which is divisible in infinitum, that it consists of an infinite number of parts. For, although all the parts are contained in the intuition of the whole, the whole division is not contained therein. The division is contained only in the progressing decomposition—in the regress itself, which is the condition of the possibility and actuality of the series. Now, as this regress is infinite, all the members (parts) to which it attains must be contained in the given whole as an aggregate. But the complete series of division is not contained therein. For this series, being infinite in succession and always incomplete, cannot represent an infinite number of members, and still less a composition of these members into a whole.\n\nTo apply this remark to space. Every limited part of space presented to intuition is a whole, the parts of which are always spaces—to whatever extent subdivided. Every limited space is hence divisible to infinity.\n\nLet us again apply the remark to an external phenomenon enclosed in limits, that is, a body. The divisibility of a body rests upon the divisibility of space, which is the condition of the possibility of the body as an extended whole. A body is consequently divisible to infinity, though it does not, for that reason, consist of an infinite number of parts.\n\nIt certainly seems that, as a body must be cogitated as substance in space, the law of divisibility would not be applicable to it as substance. For we may and ought to grant, in the case of space, that division or decomposition, to any extent, never can utterly annihilate composition (that is to say, the smallest part of space must still consist of spaces); otherwise space would entirely cease to exist—which is impossible. But, the assertion on the other hand that when all composition in matter is annihilated in thought, nothing remains, does not seem to harmonize with the conception of substance, which must be properly the subject of all composition and must remain, even after the conjunction of its attributes in space—which constituted a body—is annihilated in thought. But this is not the case with substance in the phenomenal world, which is not a thing in itself cogitated by the pure category. Phenomenal substance is not an absolute subject; it is merely a permanent sensuous image, and nothing more than an intuition, in which the unconditioned is not to be found.\n\nBut, although this rule of progress to infinity is legitimate and applicable to the subdivision of a phenomenon, as a mere occupation or filling of space, it is not applicable to a whole consisting of a number of distinct parts and constituting a quantum discretum—that is to say, an organized body. It cannot be admitted that every part in an organized whole is itself organized, and that, in analysing it to infinity, we must always meet with organized parts; although we may allow that the parts of the matter which we decompose in infinitum, may be organized. For the infinity of the division of a phenomenon in space rests altogether on the fact that the divisibility of a phenomenon is given only in and through this infinity, that is, an undetermined number of parts is given, while the parts themselves are given and determined only in and through the subdivision; in a word, the infinity of the division necessarily presupposes that the whole is not already divided in se. Hence our division determines a number of parts in the whole—a number which extends just as far as the actual regress in the division; while, on the other hand, the very notion of a body organized to infinity represents the whole as already and in itself divided. We expect, therefore, to find in it a determinate, but at the same time, infinite, number of parts—which is self-contradictory. For we should thus have a whole containing a series of members which could not be completed in any regress—which is infinite, and at the same time complete in an organized composite. Infinite divisibility is applicable only to a quantum continuum, and is based entirely on the infinite divisibility of space, But in a quantum discretum the multitude of parts or units is always determined, and hence always equal to some number. To what extent a body may be organized, experience alone can inform us; and although, so far as our experience of this or that body has extended, we may not have discovered any inorganic part, such parts must exist in possible experience. But how far the transcendental division of a phenomenon must extend, we cannot know from experience—it is a question which experience cannot answer; it is answered only by the principle of reason which forbids us to consider the empirical regress, in the analysis of extended body, as ever absolutely complete.\n\nConcluding Remark on the Solution of the Transcendental Mathematical Ideas—and Introductory to the Solution of the Dynamical Ideas.\n\nWe presented the antinomy of pure reason in a tabular form, and we endeavoured to show the ground of this self-contradiction on the part of reason, and the only means of bringing it to a conclusion—namely, by declaring both contradictory statements to be false. We represented in these antinomies the conditions of phenomena as belonging to the conditioned according to relations of space and time—which is the usual supposition of the common understanding. In this respect, all dialectical representations of totality, in the series of conditions to a given conditioned, were perfectly homogeneous. The condition was always a member of the series along with the conditioned, and thus the homogeneity of the whole series was assured. In this case the regress could never be cogitated as complete; or, if this was the case, a member really conditioned was falsely regarded as a primal member, consequently as unconditioned. In such an antinomy, therefore, we did not consider the object, that is, the conditioned, but the series of conditions belonging to the object, and the magnitude of that series. And thus arose the difficulty—a difficulty not to be settled by any decision regarding the claims of the two parties, but simply by cutting the knot—by declaring the series proposed by reason to be either too long or too short for the understanding, which could in neither case make its conceptions adequate with the ideas.\n\nBut we have overlooked, up to this point, an essential difference existing between the conceptions of the understanding which reason endeavours to raise to the rank of ideas—two of these indicating a mathematical, and two a dynamical synthesis of phenomena. Hitherto, it was necessary to signalize this distinction; for, just as in our general representation of all transcendental ideas, we considered them under phenomenal conditions, so, in the two mathematical ideas, our discussion is concerned solely with an object in the world of phenomena. But as we are now about to proceed to the consideration of the dynamical conceptions of the understanding, and their adequateness with ideas, we must not lose sight of this distinction. We shall find that it opens up to us an entirely new view of the conflict in which reason is involved. For, while in the first two antinomies, both parties were dismissed, on the ground of having advanced statements based upon false hypothesis; in the present case the hope appears of discovering a hypothesis which may be consistent with the demands of reason, and, the judge completing the statement of the grounds of claim, which both parties had left in an unsatisfactory state, the question may be settled on its own merits, not by dismissing the claimants, but by a comparison of the arguments on both sides. If we consider merely their extension, and whether they are adequate with ideas, the series of conditions may be regarded as all homogeneous. But the conception of the understanding which lies at the basis of these ideas, contains either a synthesis of the homogeneous (presupposed in every quantity—in its composition as well as in its division) or of the heterogeneous, which is the case in the dynamical synthesis of cause and effect, as well as of the necessary and the contingent.\n\nThus it happens that in the mathematical series of phenomena no other than a sensuous condition is admissible—a condition which is itself a member of the series; while the dynamical series of sensuous conditions admits a heterogeneous condition, which is not a member of the series, but, as purely intelligible, lies out of and beyond it. And thus reason is satisfied, and an unconditioned placed at the head of the series of phenomena, without introducing confusion into or discontinuing it, contrary to the principles of the understanding.\n\nNow, from the fact that the dynamical ideas admit a condition of phenomena which does not form a part of the series of phenomena, arises a result which we should not have expected from an antinomy. In former cases, the result was that both contradictory dialectical statements were declared to be false. In the present case, we find the conditioned in the dynamical series connected with an empirically unconditioned, but non-sensuous condition; and thus satisfaction is done to the understanding on the one hand and to the reason on the other.[[62]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-62) While, moreover, the dialectical arguments for unconditioned totality in mere phenomena fall to the ground, both propositions of reason may be shown to be true in their proper signification. This could not happen in the case of the cosmological ideas which demanded a mathematically unconditioned unity; for no condition could be placed at the head of the series of phenomena, except one which was itself a phenomenon and consequently a member of the series.\n\n[[62]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-62) For the understanding cannot admit among phenomena a condition which is itself empirically unconditioned. But if it is possible to cogitate an intelligible condition—one which is not a member of the series of phenomena—for a conditioned phenomenon, without breaking the series of empirical conditions, such a condition may be admissible as empirically unconditioned, and the empirical regress continue regular, unceasing, and intact.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","text":"### II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition\n\nWhen I divide a whole which is given in intuition, I proceed from a conditioned to its conditions. The division of the parts of the whole (subdivisio or decompositio) is a regress in the series of these conditions. The absolute totality of this series would be actually attained and given to the mind, if the regress could arrive at simple parts. But if all the parts in a continuous decomposition are themselves divisible, the division, that is to say, the regress, proceeds from the conditioned to its conditions in infinitum; because the conditions (the parts) are themselves contained in the conditioned, and, as the latter is given in a limited intuition, the former are all given along with it. This regress cannot, therefore, be called a regressus in indefinitum, as happened in the case of the preceding cosmological idea, the regress in which proceeded from the conditioned to the conditions not given contemporaneously and along with it, but discoverable only through the empirical regress. We are not, however, entitled to affirm of a whole of this kind, which is divisible in infinitum, that it consists of an infinite number of parts. For, although all the parts are contained in the intuition of the whole, the whole division is not contained therein. The division is contained only in the progressing decomposition—in the regress itself, which is the condition of the possibility and actuality of the series. Now, as this regress is infinite, all the members (parts) to which it attains must be contained in the given whole as an aggregate. But the complete series of division is not contained therein. For this series, being infinite in succession and always incomplete, cannot represent an infinite number of members, and still less a composition of these members into a whole.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"71.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","text":"To apply this remark to space. Every limited part of space presented to intuition is a whole, the parts of which are always spaces—to whatever extent subdivided. Every limited space is hence divisible to infinity.\n\nLet us again apply the remark to an external phenomenon enclosed in limits, that is, a body. The divisibility of a body rests upon the divisibility of space, which is the condition of the possibility of the body as an extended whole. A body is consequently divisible to infinity, though it does not, for that reason, consist of an infinite number of parts.\n\nIt certainly seems that, as a body must be cogitated as substance in space, the law of divisibility would not be applicable to it as substance. For we may and ought to grant, in the case of space, that division or decomposition, to any extent, never can utterly annihilate composition (that is to say, the smallest part of space must still consist of spaces); otherwise space would entirely cease to exist—which is impossible. But, the assertion on the other hand that when all composition in matter is annihilated in thought, nothing remains, does not seem to harmonize with the conception of substance, which must be properly the subject of all composition and must remain, even after the conjunction of its attributes in space—which constituted a body—is annihilated in thought. But this is not the case with substance in the phenomenal world, which is not a thing in itself cogitated by the pure category. Phenomenal substance is not an absolute subject; it is merely a permanent sensuous image, and nothing more than an intuition, in which the unconditioned is not to be found.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"71.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","text":"But, although this rule of progress to infinity is legitimate and applicable to the subdivision of a phenomenon, as a mere occupation or filling of space, it is not applicable to a whole consisting of a number of distinct parts and constituting a quantum discretum—that is to say, an organized body. It cannot be admitted that every part in an organized whole is itself organized, and that, in analysing it to infinity, we must always meet with organized parts; although we may allow that the parts of the matter which we decompose in infinitum, may be organized. For the infinity of the division of a phenomenon in space rests altogether on the fact that the divisibility of a phenomenon is given only in and through this infinity, that is, an undetermined number of parts is given, while the parts themselves are given and determined only in and through the subdivision; in a word, the infinity of the division necessarily presupposes that the whole is not already divided in se. Hence our division determines a number of parts in the whole—a number which extends just as far as the actual regress in the division; while, on the other hand, the very notion of a body organized to infinity represents the whole as already and in itself divided. We expect, therefore, to find in it a determinate, but at the same time, infinite, number of parts—which is self-contradictory. For we should thus have a whole containing a series of members which could not be completed in any regress—which is infinite, and at the same time complete in an organized composite. Infinite divisibility is applicable only to a quantum continuum, and is based entirely on the infinite divisibility of space, But in a quantum discretum the multitude of parts or units is always determined, and hence always equal to some number. To what extent a body may be organized, experience alone can inform us; and although, so far as our experience of this or that body has extended, we may not have discovered any inorganic part, such parts must exist in possible experience. But how far the transcendental division of a phenomenon must extend, we cannot know from experience—it is a question which experience cannot answer; it is answered only by the principle of reason which forbids us to consider the empirical regress, in the analysis of extended body, as ever absolutely complete.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"71.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","text":"Concluding Remark on the Solution of the Transcendental Mathematical Ideas—and Introductory to the Solution of the Dynamical Ideas.\n\nWe presented the antinomy of pure reason in a tabular form, and we endeavoured to show the ground of this self-contradiction on the part of reason, and the only means of bringing it to a conclusion—namely, by declaring both contradictory statements to be false. We represented in these antinomies the conditions of phenomena as belonging to the conditioned according to relations of space and time—which is the usual supposition of the common understanding. In this respect, all dialectical representations of totality, in the series of conditions to a given conditioned, were perfectly homogeneous. The condition was always a member of the series along with the conditioned, and thus the homogeneity of the whole series was assured. In this case the regress could never be cogitated as complete; or, if this was the case, a member really conditioned was falsely regarded as a primal member, consequently as unconditioned. In such an antinomy, therefore, we did not consider the object, that is, the conditioned, but the series of conditions belonging to the object, and the magnitude of that series. And thus arose the difficulty—a difficulty not to be settled by any decision regarding the claims of the two parties, but simply by cutting the knot—by declaring the series proposed by reason to be either too long or too short for the understanding, which could in neither case make its conceptions adequate with the ideas.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"71.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0005","text":"But we have overlooked, up to this point, an essential difference existing between the conceptions of the understanding which reason endeavours to raise to the rank of ideas—two of these indicating a mathematical, and two a dynamical synthesis of phenomena. Hitherto, it was necessary to signalize this distinction; for, just as in our general representation of all transcendental ideas, we considered them under phenomenal conditions, so, in the two mathematical ideas, our discussion is concerned solely with an object in the world of phenomena. But as we are now about to proceed to the consideration of the dynamical conceptions of the understanding, and their adequateness with ideas, we must not lose sight of this distinction. We shall find that it opens up to us an entirely new view of the conflict in which reason is involved. For, while in the first two antinomies, both parties were dismissed, on the ground of having advanced statements based upon false hypothesis; in the present case the hope appears of discovering a hypothesis which may be consistent with the demands of reason, and, the judge completing the statement of the grounds of claim, which both parties had left in an unsatisfactory state, the question may be settled on its own merits, not by dismissing the claimants, but by a comparison of the arguments on both sides. If we consider merely their extension, and whether they are adequate with ideas, the series of conditions may be regarded as all homogeneous. But the conception of the understanding which lies at the basis of these ideas, contains either a synthesis of the homogeneous (presupposed in every quantity—in its composition as well as in its division) or of the heterogeneous, which is the case in the dynamical synthesis of cause and effect, as well as of the necessary and the contingent.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"71.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","text":"Thus it happens that in the mathematical series of phenomena no other than a sensuous condition is admissible—a condition which is itself a member of the series; while the dynamical series of sensuous conditions admits a heterogeneous condition, which is not a member of the series, but, as purely intelligible, lies out of and beyond it. And thus reason is satisfied, and an unconditioned placed at the head of the series of phenomena, without introducing confusion into or discontinuing it, contrary to the principles of the understanding.\n\nNow, from the fact that the dynamical ideas admit a condition of phenomena which does not form a part of the series of phenomena, arises a result which we should not have expected from an antinomy. In former cases, the result was that both contradictory dialectical statements were declared to be false. In the present case, we find the conditioned in the dynamical series connected with an empirically unconditioned, but non-sensuous condition; and thus satisfaction is done to the understanding on the one hand and to the reason on the other.[[62]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-62) While, moreover, the dialectical arguments for unconditioned totality in mere phenomena fall to the ground, both propositions of reason may be shown to be true in their proper signification. This could not happen in the case of the cosmological ideas which demanded a mathematically unconditioned unity; for no condition could be placed at the head of the series of phenomena, except one which was itself a phenomenon and consequently a member of the series.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"71.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0007","text":"[[62]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-62) For the understanding cannot admit among phenomena a condition which is itself empirically unconditioned. But if it is possible to cogitate an intelligible condition—one which is not a member of the series of phenomena—for a conditioned phenomenon, without breaking the series of empirical conditions, such a condition may be admissible as empirically unconditioned, and the empirical regress continue regular, unceasing, and intact.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"71.7"}],"sha256":"525cbebc80b6b4246fb3a557038792c74e98fe78d6300deacc93e9ce5c6fb8b1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3","title":"III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes","order":72,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_IS_3","text":"### III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes\n\nThere are only two modes of causality cogitable—the causality of nature or of freedom. The first is the conjunction of a particular state with another preceding it in the world of sense, the former following the latter by virtue of a law. Now, as the causality of phenomena is subject to conditions of time, and the preceding state, if it had always existed, could not have produced an effect which would make its first appearance at a particular time, the causality of a cause must itself be an effect—must itself have begun to be, and therefore, according to the principle of the understanding, itself requires a cause.\n\nWe must understand, on the contrary, by the term freedom, in the cosmological sense, a faculty of the spontaneous origination of a state; the causality of which, therefore, is not subordinated to another cause determining it in time. Freedom is in this sense a pure transcendental idea, which, in the first place, contains no empirical element; the object of which, in the second place, cannot be given or determined in any experience, because it is a universal law of the very possibility of experience, that everything which happens must have a cause, that consequently the causality of a cause, being itself something that has happened, must also have a cause. In this view of the case, the whole field of experience, how far soever it may extend, contains nothing that is not subject to the laws of nature. But, as we cannot by this means attain to an absolute totality of conditions in reference to the series of causes and effects, reason creates the idea of a spontaneity, which can begin to act of itself, and without any external cause determining it to action, according to the natural law of causality.\n\nIt is especially remarkable that the practical conception of freedom is based upon the transcendental idea, and that the question of the possibility of the former is difficult only as it involves the consideration of the truth of the latter. Freedom, in the practical sense, is the independence of the will of coercion by sensuous impulses. A will is sensuous, in so far as it is pathologically affected (by sensuous impulses); it is termed animal (arbitrium brutum), when it is pathologically necessitated. The human will is certainly an arbitrium sensitivum, not brutum, but liberum; because sensuousness does not necessitate its action, a faculty existing in man of self-determination, independently of all sensuous coercion.\n\nIt is plain that, if all causality in the world of sense were natural—and natural only—every event would be determined by another according to necessary laws, and that, consequently, phenomena, in so far as they determine the will, must necessitate every action as a natural effect from themselves; and thus all practical freedom would fall to the ground with the transcendental idea. For the latter presupposes that although a certain thing has not happened, it ought to have happened, and that, consequently, its phenomenal cause was not so powerful and determinative as to exclude the causality of our will—a causality capable of producing effects independently of and even in opposition to the power of natural causes, and capable, consequently, of spontaneously originating a series of events.\n\nHere, too, we find it to be the case, as we generally found in the self-contradictions and perplexities of a reason which strives to pass the bounds of possible experience, that the problem is properly not physiological, but transcendental. The question of the possibility of freedom does indeed concern psychology; but, as it rests upon dialectical arguments of pure reason, its solution must engage the attention of transcendental philosophy. Before attempting this solution, a task which transcendental philosophy cannot decline, it will be advisable to make a remark with regard to its procedure in the settlement of the question.\n\nIf phenomena were things in themselves, and time and space forms of the existence of things, condition and conditioned would always be members of the same series; and thus would arise in the present case the antinomy common to all transcendental ideas—that their series is either too great or too small for the understanding. The dynamical ideas, which we are about to discuss in this and the following section, possess the peculiarity of relating to an object, not considered as a quantity, but as an existence; and thus, in the discussion of the present question, we may make abstraction of the quantity of the series of conditions, and consider merely the dynamical relation of the condition to the conditioned. The question, then, suggests itself, whether freedom is possible; and, if it is, whether it can consist with the universality of the natural law of causality; and, consequently, whether we enounce a proper disjunctive proposition when we say: “Every effect must have its origin either in nature or in freedom,” or whether both cannot exist together in the same event in different relations. The principle of an unbroken connection between all events in the phenomenal world, in accordance with the unchangeable laws of nature, is a well-established principle of transcendental analytic which admits of no exception. The question, therefore, is: “Whether an effect, determined according to the laws of nature, can at the same time be produced by a free agent, or whether freedom and nature mutually exclude each other?” And here, the common but fallacious hypothesis of the absolute reality of phenomena manifests its injurious influence in embarrassing the procedure of reason. For if phenomena are things in themselves, freedom is impossible. In this case, nature is the complete and all-sufficient cause of every event; and condition and conditioned, cause and effect are contained in the same series, and necessitated by the same law. If, on the contrary, phenomena are held to be, as they are in fact, nothing more than mere representations, connected with each other in accordance with empirical laws, they must have a ground which is not phenomenal. But the causality of such an intelligible cause is not determined or determinable by phenomena; although its effects, as phenomena, must be determined by other phenomenal existences. This cause and its causality exist therefore out of and apart from the series of phenomena; while its effects do exist and are discoverable in the series of empirical conditions. Such an effect may therefore be considered to be free in relation to its intelligible cause, and necessary in relation to the phenomena from which it is a necessary consequence—a distinction which, stated in this perfectly general and abstract manner, must appear in the highest degree subtle and obscure. The sequel will explain. It is sufficient, at present, to remark that, as the complete and unbroken connection of phenomena is an unalterable law of nature, freedom is impossible—on the supposition that phenomena are absolutely real. Hence those philosophers who adhere to the common opinion on this subject can never succeed in reconciling the ideas of nature and freedom.\n\n_Possibility of Freedom in Harmony with the Universal Law of Natural Necessity._\n\nThat element in a sensuous object which is not itself sensuous, I may be allowed to term intelligible. If, accordingly, an object which must be regarded as a sensuous phenomenon possesses a faculty which is not an object of sensuous intuition, but by means of which it is capable of being the cause of phenomena, the causality of an object or existence of this kind may be regarded from two different points of view. It may be considered to be intelligible, as regards its action—the action of a thing which is a thing in itself, and sensuous, as regards its effects—the effects of a phenomenon belonging to the sensuous world. We should accordingly, have to form both an empirical and an intellectual conception of the causality of such a faculty or power—both, however, having reference to the same effect. This twofold manner of cogitating a power residing in a sensuous object does not run counter to any of the conceptions which we ought to form of the world of phenomena or of a possible experience. Phenomena—not being things in themselves—must have a transcendental object as a foundation, which determines them as mere representations; and there seems to be no reason why we should not ascribe to this transcendental object, in addition to the property of self-phenomenization, a causality whose effects are to be met with in the world of phenomena, although it is not itself a phenomenon. But every effective cause must possess a character, that is to say, a law of its causality, without which it would cease to be a cause. In the above case, then, every sensuous object would possess an empirical character, which guaranteed that its actions, as phenomena, stand in complete and harmonious connection, conformably to unvarying natural laws, with all other phenomena, and can be deduced from these, as conditions, and that they do thus, in connection with these, constitute a series in the order of nature. This sensuous object must, in the second place, possess an intelligible character, which guarantees it to be the cause of those actions, as phenomena, although it is not itself a phenomenon nor subordinate to the conditions of the world of sense. The former may be termed the character of the thing as a phenomenon, the latter the character of the thing as a thing in itself.\n\nNow this active subject would, in its character of intelligible subject, be subordinate to no conditions of time, for time is only a condition of phenomena, and not of things in themselves. No action would begin or cease to be in this subject; it would consequently be free from the law of all determination of time—the law of change, namely, that everything which happens must have a cause in the phenomena of a preceding state. In one word, the causality of the subject, in so far as it is intelligible, would not form part of the series of empirical conditions which determine and necessitate an event in the world of sense. Again, this intelligible character of a thing cannot be immediately cognized, because we can perceive nothing but phenomena, but it must be capable of being cogitated in harmony with the empirical character; for we always find ourselves compelled to place, in thought, a transcendental object at the basis of phenomena although we can never know what this object is in itself.\n\nIn virtue of its empirical character, this subject would at the same time be subordinate to all the empirical laws of causality, and, as a phenomenon and member of the sensuous world, its effects would have to be accounted for by a reference to preceding phenomena. Eternal phenomena must be capable of influencing it; and its actions, in accordance with natural laws, must explain to us how its empirical character, that is, the law of its causality, is to be cognized in and by means of experience. In a word, all requisites for a complete and necessary determination of these actions must be presented to us by experience.\n\nIn virtue of its intelligible character, on the other hand (although we possess only a general conception of this character), the subject must be regarded as free from all sensuous influences, and from all phenomenal determination. Moreover, as nothing happens in this subject—for it is a noumenon, and there does not consequently exist in it any change, demanding the dynamical determination of time, and for the same reason no connection with phenomena as causes—this active existence must in its actions be free from and independent of natural necessity, for this necessity exists only in the world of phenomena. It would be quite correct to say that it originates or begins its effects in the world of sense from itself, although the action productive of these effects does not begin in itself. We should not be in this case affirming that these sensuous effects began to exist of themselves, because they are always determined by prior empirical conditions—by virtue of the empirical character, which is the phenomenon of the intelligible character—and are possible only as constituting a continuation of the series of natural causes. And thus nature and freedom, each in the complete and absolute signification of these terms, can exist, without contradiction or disagreement, in the same action.\n\n_Exposition of the Cosmological Idea of Freedom in Harmony with the Universal Law of Natural Necessity._\n\nI have thought it advisable to lay before the reader at first merely a sketch of the solution of this transcendental problem, in order to enable him to form with greater ease a clear conception of the course which reason must adopt in the solution. I shall now proceed to exhibit the several momenta of this solution, and to consider them in their order.\n\nThe natural law that everything which happens must have a cause, that the causality of this cause, that is, the action of the cause (which cannot always have existed, but must be itself an event, for it precedes in time some effect which it has originated), must have itself a phenomenal cause, by which it is determined and, and, consequently, all events are empirically determined in an order of nature—this law, I say, which lies at the foundation of the possibility of experience, and of a connected system of phenomena or nature is a law of the understanding, from which no departure, and to which no exception, can be admitted. For to except even a single phenomenon from its operation is to exclude it from the sphere of possible experience and thus to admit it to be a mere fiction of thought or phantom of the brain.\n\nThus we are obliged to acknowledge the existence of a chain of causes, in which, however, absolute totality cannot be found. But we need not detain ourselves with this question, for it has already been sufficiently answered in our discussion of the antinomies into which reason falls, when it attempts to reach the unconditioned in the series of phenomena. If we permit ourselves to be deceived by the illusion of transcendental idealism, we shall find that neither nature nor freedom exists. Now the question is: “Whether, admitting the existence of natural necessity in the world of phenomena, it is possible to consider an effect as at the same time an effect of nature and an effect of freedom—or, whether these two modes of causality are contradictory and incompatible?”\n\nNo phenomenal cause can absolutely and of itself begin a series. Every action, in so far as it is productive of an event, is itself an event or occurrence, and presupposes another preceding state, in which its cause existed. Thus everything that happens is but a continuation of a series, and an absolute beginning is impossible in the sensuous world. The actions of natural causes are, accordingly, themselves effects, and presuppose causes preceding them in time. A primal action which forms an absolute beginning, is beyond the causal power of phenomena.\n\nNow, is it absolutely necessary that, granting that all effects are phenomena, the causality of the cause of these effects must also be a phenomenon and belong to the empirical world? Is it not rather possible that, although every effect in the phenomenal world must be connected with an empirical cause, according to the universal law of nature, this empirical causality may be itself the effect of a non-empirical and intelligible causality—its connection with natural causes remaining nevertheless intact? Such a causality would be considered, in reference to phenomena, as the primal action of a cause, which is in so far, therefore, not phenomenal, but, by reason of this faculty or power, intelligible; although it must, at the same time, as a link in the chain of nature, be regarded as belonging to the sensuous world.\n\nA belief in the reciprocal causality of phenomena is necessary, if we are required to look for and to present the natural conditions of natural events, that is to say, their causes. This being admitted as unexceptionably valid, the requirements of the understanding, which recognizes nothing but nature in the region of phenomena, are satisfied, and our physical explanations of physical phenomena may proceed in their regular course, without hindrance and without opposition. But it is no stumbling-block in the way, even assuming the idea to be a pure fiction, to admit that there are some natural causes in the possession of a faculty which is not empirical, but intelligible, inasmuch as it is not determined to action by empirical conditions, but purely and solely upon grounds brought forward by the understanding—this action being still, when the cause is phenomenized, in perfect accordance with the laws of empirical causality. Thus the acting subject, as a causal phenomenon, would continue to preserve a complete connection with nature and natural conditions; and the phenomenon only of the subject (with all its phenomenal causality) would contain certain conditions, which, if we ascend from the empirical to the transcendental object, must necessarily be regarded as intelligible. For, if we attend, in our inquiries with regard to causes in the world of phenomena, to the directions of nature alone, we need not trouble ourselves about the relation in which the transcendental subject, which is completely unknown to us, stands to these phenomena and their connection in nature. The intelligible ground of phenomena in this subject does not concern empirical questions. It has to do only with pure thought; and, although the effects of this thought and action of the pure understanding are discoverable in phenomena, these phenomena must nevertheless be capable of a full and complete explanation, upon purely physical grounds and in accordance with natural laws. And in this case we attend solely to their empirical and omit all consideration of their intelligible character (which is the transcendental cause of the former) as completely unknown, except in so far as it is exhibited by the latter as its empirical symbol. Now let us apply this to experience. Man is a phenomenon of the sensuous world and, at the same time, therefore, a natural cause, the causality of which must be regulated by empirical laws. As such, he must possess an empirical character, like all other natural phenomena. We remark this empirical character in his actions, which reveal the presence of certain powers and faculties. If we consider inanimate or merely animal nature, we can discover no reason for ascribing to ourselves any other than a faculty which is determined in a purely sensuous manner. But man, to whom nature reveals herself only through sense, cognizes himself not only by his senses, but also through pure apperception; and this in actions and internal determinations, which he cannot regard as sensuous impressions. He is thus to himself, on the one hand, a phenomenon, but on the other hand, in respect of certain faculties, a purely intelligible object—intelligible, because its action cannot be ascribed to sensuous receptivity. These faculties are understanding and reason. The latter, especially, is in a peculiar manner distinct from all empirically-conditioned faculties, for it employs ideas alone in the consideration of its objects, and by means of these determines the understanding, which then proceeds to make an empirical use of its own conceptions, which, like the ideas of reason, are pure and non-empirical.\n\nThat reason possesses the faculty of causality, or that at least we are compelled so to represent it, is evident from the imperatives, which in the sphere of the practical we impose on many of our executive powers. The words I ought express a species of necessity, and imply a connection with grounds which nature does not and cannot present to the mind of man. Understanding knows nothing in nature but that which is, or has been, or will be. It would be absurd to say that anything in nature ought to be other than it is in the relations of time in which it stands; indeed, the ought, when we consider merely the course of nature, has neither application nor meaning. The question, “What ought to happen in the sphere of nature?” is just as absurd as the question, “What ought to be the properties of a circle?” All that we are entitled to ask is, “What takes place in nature?” or, in the latter case, “What are the properties of a circle?”\n\nBut the idea of an ought or of duty indicates a possible action, the ground of which is a pure conception; while the ground of a merely natural action is, on the contrary, always a phenomenon. This action must certainly be possible under physical conditions, if it is prescribed by the moral imperative ought; but these physical or natural conditions do not concern the determination of the will itself, they relate to its effects alone, and the consequences of the effect in the world of phenomena. Whatever number of motives nature may present to my will, whatever sensuous impulses—the moral ought it is beyond their power to produce. They may produce a volition, which, so far from being necessary, is always conditioned—a volition to which the ought enunciated by reason, sets an aim and a standard, gives permission or prohibition. Be the object what it may, purely sensuous—as pleasure, or presented by pure reason—as good, reason will not yield to grounds which have an empirical origin. Reason will not follow the order of things presented by experience, but, with perfect spontaneity, rearranges them according to ideas, with which it compels empirical conditions to agree. It declares, in the name of these ideas, certain actions to be necessary which nevertheless have not taken place and which perhaps never will take place; and yet presupposes that it possesses the faculty of causality in relation to these actions. For, in the absence of this supposition, it could not expect its ideas to produce certain effects in the world of experience.\n\nNow, let us stop here and admit it to be at least possible that reason does stand in a really causal relation to phenomena. In this case it must—pure reason as it is—exhibit an empirical character. For every cause supposes a rule, according to which certain phenomena follow as effects from the cause, and every rule requires uniformity in these effects; and this is the proper ground of the conception of a cause—as a faculty or power. Now this conception (of a cause) may be termed the empirical character of reason; and this character is a permanent one, while the effects produced appear, in conformity with the various conditions which accompany and partly limit them, in various forms.\n\nThus the volition of every man has an empirical character, which is nothing more than the causality of his reason, in so far as its effects in the phenomenal world manifest the presence of a rule, according to which we are enabled to examine, in their several kinds and degrees, the actions of this causality and the rational grounds for these actions, and in this way to decide upon the subjective principles of the volition. Now we learn what this empirical character is only from phenomenal effects, and from the rule of these which is presented by experience; and for this reason all the actions of man in the world of phenomena are determined by his empirical character, and the co-operative causes of nature. If, then, we could investigate all the phenomena of human volition to their lowest foundation in the mind, there would be no action which we could not anticipate with certainty, and recognize to be absolutely necessary from its preceding conditions. So far as relates to this empirical character, therefore, there can be no freedom; and it is only in the light of this character that we can consider the human will, when we confine ourselves to simple observation and, as is the case in anthropology, institute a physiological investigation of the motive causes of human actions.\n\nBut when we consider the same actions in relation to reason—not for the purpose of explaining their origin, that is, in relation to speculative reason, but to practical reason, as the producing cause of these actions—we shall discover a rule and an order very different from those of nature and experience. For the declaration of this mental faculty may be that what has and could not but take place in the course of nature, ought not to have taken place. Sometimes, too, we discover, or believe that we discover, that the ideas of reason did actually stand in a causal relation to certain actions of man; and that these actions have taken place because they were determined, not by empirical causes, but by the act of the will upon grounds of reason.\n\nNow, granting that reason stands in a causal relation to phenomena; can an action of reason be called free, when we know that, sensuously, in its empirical character, it is completely determined and absolutely necessary? But this empirical character is itself determined by the intelligible character. The latter we cannot cognize; we can only indicate it by means of phenomena, which enable us to have an immediate cognition only of the empirical character.[[63]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-63) An action, then, in so far as it is to be ascribed to an intelligible cause, does not result from it in accordance with empirical laws. That is to say, not the conditions of pure reason, but only their effects in the internal sense, precede the act. Pure reason, as a purely intelligible faculty, is not subject to the conditions of time. The causality of reason in its intelligible character does not begin to be; it does not make its appearance at a certain time, for the purpose of producing an effect. If this were not the case, the causality of reason would be subservient to the natural law of phenomena, which determines them according to time, and as a series of causes and effects in time; it would consequently cease to be freedom and become a part of nature. We are therefore justified in saying: “If reason stands in a causal relation to phenomena, it is a faculty which originates the sensuous condition of an empirical series of effects.” For the condition, which resides in the reason, is non-sensuous, and therefore cannot be originated, or begin to be. And thus we find—what we could not discover in any empirical series—a condition of a successive series of events itself empirically unconditioned. For, in the present case, the condition stands out of and beyond the series of phenomena—it is intelligible, and it consequently cannot be subjected to any sensuous condition, or to any time-determination by a preceding cause.\n\n[[63]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-63) The real morality of actions—their merit or demerit, and even that of our own conduct, is completely unknown to us. Our estimates can relate only to their empirical character. How much is the result of the action of free will, how much is to be ascribed to nature and to blameless error, or to a happy constitution of temperament (merito fortunae), no one can discover, nor, for this reason, determine with perfect justice.\n\nBut, in another respect, the same cause belongs also to the series of phenomena. Man is himself a phenomenon. His will has an empirical character, which is the empirical cause of all his actions. There is no condition—determining man and his volition in conformity with this character—which does not itself form part of the series of effects in nature, and is subject to their law—the law according to which an empirically undetermined cause of an event in time cannot exist. For this reason no given action can have an absolute and spontaneous origination, all actions being phenomena, and belonging to the world of experience. But it cannot be said of reason, that the state in which it determines the will is always preceded by some other state determining it. For reason is not a phenomenon, and therefore not subject to sensuous conditions; and, consequently, even in relation to its causality, the sequence or conditions of time do not influence reason, nor can the dynamical law of nature, which determines the sequence of time according to certain rules, be applied to it.\n\nReason is consequently the permanent condition of all actions of the human will. Each of these is determined in the empirical character of the man, even before it has taken place. The intelligible character, of which the former is but the sensuous schema, knows no before or after; and every action, irrespective of the time-relation in which it stands with other phenomena, is the immediate effect of the intelligible character of pure reason, which, consequently, enjoys freedom of action, and is not dynamically determined either by internal or external preceding conditions. This freedom must not be described, in a merely negative manner, as independence of empirical conditions, for in this case the faculty of reason would cease to be a cause of phenomena; but it must be regarded, positively, as a faculty which can spontaneously originate a series of events. At the same time, it must not be supposed that any beginning can take place in reason; on the contrary, reason, as the unconditioned condition of all action of the will, admits of no time-conditions, although its effect does really begin in a series of phenomena—a beginning which is not, however, absolutely primal.\n\nI shall illustrate this regulative principle of reason by an example, from its employment in the world of experience; proved it cannot be by any amount of experience, or by any number of facts, for such arguments cannot establish the truth of transcendental propositions. Let us take a voluntary action—for example, a falsehood—by means of which a man has introduced a certain degree of confusion into the social life of humanity, which is judged according to the motives from which it originated, and the blame of which and of the evil consequences arising from it, is imputed to the offender. We at first proceed to examine the empirical character of the offence, and for this purpose we endeavour to penetrate to the sources of that character, such as a defective education, bad company, a shameless and wicked disposition, frivolity, and want of reflection—not forgetting also the occasioning causes which prevailed at the moment of the transgression. In this the procedure is exactly the same as that pursued in the investigation of the series of causes which determine a given physical effect. Now, although we believe the action to have been determined by all these circumstances, we do not the less blame the offender. We do not blame him for his unhappy disposition, nor for the circumstances which influenced him, nay, not even for his former course of life; for we presuppose that all these considerations may be set aside, that the series of preceding conditions may be regarded as having never existed, and that the action may be considered as completely unconditioned in relation to any state preceding, just as if the agent commenced with it an entirely new series of effects. Our blame of the offender is grounded upon a law of reason, which requires us to regard this faculty as a cause, which could have and ought to have otherwise determined the behaviour of the culprit, independently of all empirical conditions. This causality of reason we do not regard as a co-operating agency, but as complete in itself. It matters not whether the sensuous impulses favoured or opposed the action of this causality, the offence is estimated according to its intelligible character—the offender is decidedly worthy of blame, the moment he utters a falsehood. It follows that we regard reason, in spite of the empirical conditions of the act, as completely free, and therefore, as in the present case, culpable.\n\nThe above judgement is complete evidence that we are accustomed to think that reason is not affected by sensuous conditions, that in it no change takes place—although its phenomena, in other words, the mode in which it appears in its effects, are subject to change—that in it no preceding state determines the following, and, consequently, that it does not form a member of the series of sensuous conditions which necessitate phenomena according to natural laws. Reason is present and the same in all human actions and at all times; but it does not itself exist in time, and therefore does not enter upon any state in which it did not formerly exist. It is, relatively to new states or conditions, determining, but not determinable. Hence we cannot ask: “Why did not reason determine itself in a different manner?” The question ought to be thus stated: “Why did not reason employ its power of causality to determine certain phenomena in a different manner?” But this is a question which admits of no answer. For a different intelligible character would have exhibited a different empirical character; and, when we say that, in spite of the course which his whole former life has taken, the offender could have refrained from uttering the falsehood, this means merely that the act was subject to the power and authority—permissive or prohibitive—of reason. Now, reason is not subject in its causality to any conditions of phenomena or of time; and a difference in time may produce a difference in the relation of phenomena to each other—for these are not things and therefore not causes in themselves—but it cannot produce any difference in the relation in which the action stands to the faculty of reason.\n\nThus, then, in our investigation into free actions and the causal power which produced them, we arrive at an intelligible cause, beyond which, however, we cannot go; although we can recognize that it is free, that is, independent of all sensuous conditions, and that, in this way, it may be the sensuously unconditioned condition of phenomena. But for what reason the intelligible character generates such and such phenomena and exhibits such and such an empirical character under certain circumstances, it is beyond the power of our reason to decide. The question is as much above the power and the sphere of reason as the following would be: “Why does the transcendental object of our external sensuous intuition allow of no other form than that of intuition in space?” But the problem, which we were called upon to solve, does not require us to entertain any such questions. The problem was merely this—whether freedom and natural necessity can exist without opposition in the same action. To this question we have given a sufficient answer; for we have shown that, as the former stands in a relation to a different kind of condition from those of the latter, the law of the one does not affect the law of the other and that, consequently, both can exist together in independence of and without interference with each other.\n\nThe reader must be careful to remark that my intention in the above remarks has not been to prove the actual existence of freedom, as a faculty in which resides the cause of certain sensuous phenomena. For, not to mention that such an argument would not have a transcendental character, nor have been limited to the discussion of pure conceptions—all attempts at inferring from experience what cannot be cogitated in accordance with its laws, must ever be unsuccessful. Nay, more, I have not even aimed at demonstrating the possibility of freedom; for this too would have been a vain endeavour, inasmuch as it is beyond the power of the mind to cognize the possibility of a reality or of a causal power by the aid of mere à priori conceptions. Freedom has been considered in the foregoing remarks only as a transcendental idea, by means of which reason aims at originating a series of conditions in the world of phenomena with the help of that which is sensuously unconditioned, involving itself, however, in an antinomy with the laws which itself prescribes for the conduct of the understanding. That this antinomy is based upon a mere illusion, and that nature and freedom are at least not opposed—this was the only thing in our power to prove, and the question which it was our task to solve.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0001","text":"### III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes\n\nThere are only two modes of causality cogitable—the causality of nature or of freedom. The first is the conjunction of a particular state with another preceding it in the world of sense, the former following the latter by virtue of a law. Now, as the causality of phenomena is subject to conditions of time, and the preceding state, if it had always existed, could not have produced an effect which would make its first appearance at a particular time, the causality of a cause must itself be an effect—must itself have begun to be, and therefore, according to the principle of the understanding, itself requires a cause.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"72.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0002","text":"We must understand, on the contrary, by the term freedom, in the cosmological sense, a faculty of the spontaneous origination of a state; the causality of which, therefore, is not subordinated to another cause determining it in time. Freedom is in this sense a pure transcendental idea, which, in the first place, contains no empirical element; the object of which, in the second place, cannot be given or determined in any experience, because it is a universal law of the very possibility of experience, that everything which happens must have a cause, that consequently the causality of a cause, being itself something that has happened, must also have a cause. In this view of the case, the whole field of experience, how far soever it may extend, contains nothing that is not subject to the laws of nature. But, as we cannot by this means attain to an absolute totality of conditions in reference to the series of causes and effects, reason creates the idea of a spontaneity, which can begin to act of itself, and without any external cause determining it to action, according to the natural law of causality.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"72.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0003","text":"It is especially remarkable that the practical conception of freedom is based upon the transcendental idea, and that the question of the possibility of the former is difficult only as it involves the consideration of the truth of the latter. Freedom, in the practical sense, is the independence of the will of coercion by sensuous impulses. A will is sensuous, in so far as it is pathologically affected (by sensuous impulses); it is termed animal (arbitrium brutum), when it is pathologically necessitated. The human will is certainly an arbitrium sensitivum, not brutum, but liberum; because sensuousness does not necessitate its action, a faculty existing in man of self-determination, independently of all sensuous coercion.\n\nIt is plain that, if all causality in the world of sense were natural—and natural only—every event would be determined by another according to necessary laws, and that, consequently, phenomena, in so far as they determine the will, must necessitate every action as a natural effect from themselves; and thus all practical freedom would fall to the ground with the transcendental idea. For the latter presupposes that although a certain thing has not happened, it ought to have happened, and that, consequently, its phenomenal cause was not so powerful and determinative as to exclude the causality of our will—a causality capable of producing effects independently of and even in opposition to the power of natural causes, and capable, consequently, of spontaneously originating a series of events.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"72.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0004","text":"Here, too, we find it to be the case, as we generally found in the self-contradictions and perplexities of a reason which strives to pass the bounds of possible experience, that the problem is properly not physiological, but transcendental. The question of the possibility of freedom does indeed concern psychology; but, as it rests upon dialectical arguments of pure reason, its solution must engage the attention of transcendental philosophy. Before attempting this solution, a task which transcendental philosophy cannot decline, it will be advisable to make a remark with regard to its procedure in the settlement of the question.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"72.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","text":"If phenomena were things in themselves, and time and space forms of the existence of things, condition and conditioned would always be members of the same series; and thus would arise in the present case the antinomy common to all transcendental ideas—that their series is either too great or too small for the understanding. The dynamical ideas, which we are about to discuss in this and the following section, possess the peculiarity of relating to an object, not considered as a quantity, but as an existence; and thus, in the discussion of the present question, we may make abstraction of the quantity of the series of conditions, and consider merely the dynamical relation of the condition to the conditioned. The question, then, suggests itself, whether freedom is possible; and, if it is, whether it can consist with the universality of the natural law of causality; and, consequently, whether we enounce a proper disjunctive proposition when we say: “Every effect must have its origin either in nature or in freedom,” or whether both cannot exist together in the same event in different relations. The principle of an unbroken connection between all events in the phenomenal world, in accordance with the unchangeable laws of nature, is a well-established principle of transcendental analytic which admits of no exception. The question, therefore, is: “Whether an effect, determined according to the laws of nature, can at the same time be produced by a free agent, or whether freedom and nature mutually exclude each other?” And here, the common but fallacious hypothesis of the absolute reality of phenomena manifests its injurious influence in embarrassing the procedure of reason. For if phenomena are things in themselves, freedom is impossible. In this case, nature is the complete and all-sufficient cause of every event; and condition and conditioned, cause and effect are contained in the same series, and necessitated by the same law. If, on the contrary, phenomena are held to be, as they are in fact, nothing more than mere representations, connected with each other in accordance with empirical laws, they must have a ground which is not phenomenal. But the causality of such an intelligible cause is not determined or determinable by phenomena; although its effects, as phenomena, must be determined by other phenomenal existences. This cause and its causality exist therefore out of and apart from the series of phenomena; while its effects do exist and are discoverable in the series of empirical conditions. Such an effect may therefore be considered to be free in relation to its intelligible cause, and necessary in relation to the phenomena from which it is a necessary consequence—a distinction which, stated in this perfectly general and abstract manner, must appear in the highest degree subtle and obscure. The sequel will explain. It is sufficient, at present, to remark that, as the complete and unbroken connection of phenomena is an unalterable law of nature, freedom is impossible—on the supposition that phenomena are absolutely real. Hence those philosophers who adhere to the common opinion on this subject can never succeed in reconciling the ideas of nature and freedom.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"72.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0006","text":"_Possibility of Freedom in Harmony with the Universal Law of Natural Necessity._\n\nThat element in a sensuous object which is not itself sensuous, I may be allowed to term intelligible. If, accordingly, an object which must be regarded as a sensuous phenomenon possesses a faculty which is not an object of sensuous intuition, but by means of which it is capable of being the cause of phenomena, the causality of an object or existence of this kind may be regarded from two different points of view. It may be considered to be intelligible, as regards its action—the action of a thing which is a thing in itself, and sensuous, as regards its effects—the effects of a phenomenon belonging to the sensuous world. We should accordingly, have to form both an empirical and an intellectual conception of the causality of such a faculty or power—both, however, having reference to the same effect. This twofold manner of cogitating a power residing in a sensuous object does not run counter to any of the conceptions which we ought to form of the world of phenomena or of a possible experience. Phenomena—not being things in themselves—must have a transcendental object as a foundation, which determines them as mere representations; and there seems to be no reason why we should not ascribe to this transcendental object, in addition to the property of self-phenomenization, a causality whose effects are to be met with in the world of phenomena, although it is not itself a phenomenon. But every effective cause must possess a character, that is to say, a law of its causality, without which it would cease to be a cause. In the above case, then, every sensuous object would possess an empirical character, which guaranteed that its actions, as phenomena, stand in complete and harmonious connection, conformably to unvarying natural laws, with all other phenomena, and can be deduced from these, as conditions, and that they do thus, in connection with these, constitute a series in the order of nature. This sensuous object must, in the second place, possess an intelligible character, which guarantees it to be the cause of those actions, as phenomena, although it is not itself a phenomenon nor subordinate to the conditions of the world of sense. The former may be termed the character of the thing as a phenomenon, the latter the character of the thing as a thing in itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"72.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","text":"Now this active subject would, in its character of intelligible subject, be subordinate to no conditions of time, for time is only a condition of phenomena, and not of things in themselves. No action would begin or cease to be in this subject; it would consequently be free from the law of all determination of time—the law of change, namely, that everything which happens must have a cause in the phenomena of a preceding state. In one word, the causality of the subject, in so far as it is intelligible, would not form part of the series of empirical conditions which determine and necessitate an event in the world of sense. Again, this intelligible character of a thing cannot be immediately cognized, because we can perceive nothing but phenomena, but it must be capable of being cogitated in harmony with the empirical character; for we always find ourselves compelled to place, in thought, a transcendental object at the basis of phenomena although we can never know what this object is in itself.\n\nIn virtue of its empirical character, this subject would at the same time be subordinate to all the empirical laws of causality, and, as a phenomenon and member of the sensuous world, its effects would have to be accounted for by a reference to preceding phenomena. Eternal phenomena must be capable of influencing it; and its actions, in accordance with natural laws, must explain to us how its empirical character, that is, the law of its causality, is to be cognized in and by means of experience. In a word, all requisites for a complete and necessary determination of these actions must be presented to us by experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"72.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","text":"In virtue of its intelligible character, on the other hand (although we possess only a general conception of this character), the subject must be regarded as free from all sensuous influences, and from all phenomenal determination. Moreover, as nothing happens in this subject—for it is a noumenon, and there does not consequently exist in it any change, demanding the dynamical determination of time, and for the same reason no connection with phenomena as causes—this active existence must in its actions be free from and independent of natural necessity, for this necessity exists only in the world of phenomena. It would be quite correct to say that it originates or begins its effects in the world of sense from itself, although the action productive of these effects does not begin in itself. We should not be in this case affirming that these sensuous effects began to exist of themselves, because they are always determined by prior empirical conditions—by virtue of the empirical character, which is the phenomenon of the intelligible character—and are possible only as constituting a continuation of the series of natural causes. And thus nature and freedom, each in the complete and absolute signification of these terms, can exist, without contradiction or disagreement, in the same action.\n\n_Exposition of the Cosmological Idea of Freedom in Harmony with the Universal Law of Natural Necessity._\n\nI have thought it advisable to lay before the reader at first merely a sketch of the solution of this transcendental problem, in order to enable him to form with greater ease a clear conception of the course which reason must adopt in the solution. I shall now proceed to exhibit the several momenta of this solution, and to consider them in their order.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"72.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0009","text":"The natural law that everything which happens must have a cause, that the causality of this cause, that is, the action of the cause (which cannot always have existed, but must be itself an event, for it precedes in time some effect which it has originated), must have itself a phenomenal cause, by which it is determined and, and, consequently, all events are empirically determined in an order of nature—this law, I say, which lies at the foundation of the possibility of experience, and of a connected system of phenomena or nature is a law of the understanding, from which no departure, and to which no exception, can be admitted. For to except even a single phenomenon from its operation is to exclude it from the sphere of possible experience and thus to admit it to be a mere fiction of thought or phantom of the brain.\n\nThus we are obliged to acknowledge the existence of a chain of causes, in which, however, absolute totality cannot be found. But we need not detain ourselves with this question, for it has already been sufficiently answered in our discussion of the antinomies into which reason falls, when it attempts to reach the unconditioned in the series of phenomena. If we permit ourselves to be deceived by the illusion of transcendental idealism, we shall find that neither nature nor freedom exists. Now the question is: “Whether, admitting the existence of natural necessity in the world of phenomena, it is possible to consider an effect as at the same time an effect of nature and an effect of freedom—or, whether these two modes of causality are contradictory and incompatible?”","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"72.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0010","text":"No phenomenal cause can absolutely and of itself begin a series. Every action, in so far as it is productive of an event, is itself an event or occurrence, and presupposes another preceding state, in which its cause existed. Thus everything that happens is but a continuation of a series, and an absolute beginning is impossible in the sensuous world. The actions of natural causes are, accordingly, themselves effects, and presuppose causes preceding them in time. A primal action which forms an absolute beginning, is beyond the causal power of phenomena.\n\nNow, is it absolutely necessary that, granting that all effects are phenomena, the causality of the cause of these effects must also be a phenomenon and belong to the empirical world? Is it not rather possible that, although every effect in the phenomenal world must be connected with an empirical cause, according to the universal law of nature, this empirical causality may be itself the effect of a non-empirical and intelligible causality—its connection with natural causes remaining nevertheless intact? Such a causality would be considered, in reference to phenomena, as the primal action of a cause, which is in so far, therefore, not phenomenal, but, by reason of this faculty or power, intelligible; although it must, at the same time, as a link in the chain of nature, be regarded as belonging to the sensuous world.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"72.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0011","text":"A belief in the reciprocal causality of phenomena is necessary, if we are required to look for and to present the natural conditions of natural events, that is to say, their causes. This being admitted as unexceptionably valid, the requirements of the understanding, which recognizes nothing but nature in the region of phenomena, are satisfied, and our physical explanations of physical phenomena may proceed in their regular course, without hindrance and without opposition. But it is no stumbling-block in the way, even assuming the idea to be a pure fiction, to admit that there are some natural causes in the possession of a faculty which is not empirical, but intelligible, inasmuch as it is not determined to action by empirical conditions, but purely and solely upon grounds brought forward by the understanding—this action being still, when the cause is phenomenized, in perfect accordance with the laws of empirical causality. Thus the acting subject, as a causal phenomenon, would continue to preserve a complete connection with nature and natural conditions; and the phenomenon only of the subject (with all its phenomenal causality) would contain certain conditions, which, if we ascend from the empirical to the transcendental object, must necessarily be regarded as intelligible. For, if we attend, in our inquiries with regard to causes in the world of phenomena, to the directions of nature alone, we need not trouble ourselves about the relation in which the transcendental subject, which is completely unknown to us, stands to these phenomena and their connection in nature. The intelligible ground of phenomena in this subject does not concern empirical questions. It has to do only with pure thought; and, although the effects of this thought and action of the pure understanding are discoverable in phenomena, these phenomena must nevertheless be capable of a full and complete explanation, upon purely physical grounds and in accordance with natural laws. And in this case we attend solely to their empirical and omit all consideration of their intelligible character (which is the transcendental cause of the former) as completely unknown, except in so far as it is exhibited by the latter as its empirical symbol. Now let us apply this to experience. Man is a phenomenon of the sensuous world and, at the same time, therefore, a natural cause, the causality of which must be regulated by empirical laws. As such, he must possess an empirical character, like all other natural phenomena. We remark this empirical character in his actions, which reveal the presence of certain powers and faculties. If we consider inanimate or merely animal nature, we can discover no reason for ascribing to ourselves any other than a faculty which is determined in a purely sensuous manner. But man, to whom nature reveals herself only through sense, cognizes himself not only by his senses, but also through pure apperception; and this in actions and internal determinations, which he cannot regard as sensuous impressions. He is thus to himself, on the one hand, a phenomenon, but on the other hand, in respect of certain faculties, a purely intelligible object—intelligible, because its action cannot be ascribed to sensuous receptivity. These faculties are understanding and reason. The latter, especially, is in a peculiar manner distinct from all empirically-conditioned faculties, for it employs ideas alone in the consideration of its objects, and by means of these determines the understanding, which then proceeds to make an empirical use of its own conceptions, which, like the ideas of reason, are pure and non-empirical.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"72.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0012","text":"That reason possesses the faculty of causality, or that at least we are compelled so to represent it, is evident from the imperatives, which in the sphere of the practical we impose on many of our executive powers. The words I ought express a species of necessity, and imply a connection with grounds which nature does not and cannot present to the mind of man. Understanding knows nothing in nature but that which is, or has been, or will be. It would be absurd to say that anything in nature ought to be other than it is in the relations of time in which it stands; indeed, the ought, when we consider merely the course of nature, has neither application nor meaning. The question, “What ought to happen in the sphere of nature?” is just as absurd as the question, “What ought to be the properties of a circle?” All that we are entitled to ask is, “What takes place in nature?” or, in the latter case, “What are the properties of a circle?”","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"72.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0013","text":"But the idea of an ought or of duty indicates a possible action, the ground of which is a pure conception; while the ground of a merely natural action is, on the contrary, always a phenomenon. This action must certainly be possible under physical conditions, if it is prescribed by the moral imperative ought; but these physical or natural conditions do not concern the determination of the will itself, they relate to its effects alone, and the consequences of the effect in the world of phenomena. Whatever number of motives nature may present to my will, whatever sensuous impulses—the moral ought it is beyond their power to produce. They may produce a volition, which, so far from being necessary, is always conditioned—a volition to which the ought enunciated by reason, sets an aim and a standard, gives permission or prohibition. Be the object what it may, purely sensuous—as pleasure, or presented by pure reason—as good, reason will not yield to grounds which have an empirical origin. Reason will not follow the order of things presented by experience, but, with perfect spontaneity, rearranges them according to ideas, with which it compels empirical conditions to agree. It declares, in the name of these ideas, certain actions to be necessary which nevertheless have not taken place and which perhaps never will take place; and yet presupposes that it possesses the faculty of causality in relation to these actions. For, in the absence of this supposition, it could not expect its ideas to produce certain effects in the world of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"72.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0014","text":"Now, let us stop here and admit it to be at least possible that reason does stand in a really causal relation to phenomena. In this case it must—pure reason as it is—exhibit an empirical character. For every cause supposes a rule, according to which certain phenomena follow as effects from the cause, and every rule requires uniformity in these effects; and this is the proper ground of the conception of a cause—as a faculty or power. Now this conception (of a cause) may be termed the empirical character of reason; and this character is a permanent one, while the effects produced appear, in conformity with the various conditions which accompany and partly limit them, in various forms.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"72.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0015","text":"Thus the volition of every man has an empirical character, which is nothing more than the causality of his reason, in so far as its effects in the phenomenal world manifest the presence of a rule, according to which we are enabled to examine, in their several kinds and degrees, the actions of this causality and the rational grounds for these actions, and in this way to decide upon the subjective principles of the volition. Now we learn what this empirical character is only from phenomenal effects, and from the rule of these which is presented by experience; and for this reason all the actions of man in the world of phenomena are determined by his empirical character, and the co-operative causes of nature. If, then, we could investigate all the phenomena of human volition to their lowest foundation in the mind, there would be no action which we could not anticipate with certainty, and recognize to be absolutely necessary from its preceding conditions. So far as relates to this empirical character, therefore, there can be no freedom; and it is only in the light of this character that we can consider the human will, when we confine ourselves to simple observation and, as is the case in anthropology, institute a physiological investigation of the motive causes of human actions.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"72.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0016","text":"But when we consider the same actions in relation to reason—not for the purpose of explaining their origin, that is, in relation to speculative reason, but to practical reason, as the producing cause of these actions—we shall discover a rule and an order very different from those of nature and experience. For the declaration of this mental faculty may be that what has and could not but take place in the course of nature, ought not to have taken place. Sometimes, too, we discover, or believe that we discover, that the ideas of reason did actually stand in a causal relation to certain actions of man; and that these actions have taken place because they were determined, not by empirical causes, but by the act of the will upon grounds of reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"72.16"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0017","text":"Now, granting that reason stands in a causal relation to phenomena; can an action of reason be called free, when we know that, sensuously, in its empirical character, it is completely determined and absolutely necessary? But this empirical character is itself determined by the intelligible character. The latter we cannot cognize; we can only indicate it by means of phenomena, which enable us to have an immediate cognition only of the empirical character.[[63]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-63) An action, then, in so far as it is to be ascribed to an intelligible cause, does not result from it in accordance with empirical laws. That is to say, not the conditions of pure reason, but only their effects in the internal sense, precede the act. Pure reason, as a purely intelligible faculty, is not subject to the conditions of time. The causality of reason in its intelligible character does not begin to be; it does not make its appearance at a certain time, for the purpose of producing an effect. If this were not the case, the causality of reason would be subservient to the natural law of phenomena, which determines them according to time, and as a series of causes and effects in time; it would consequently cease to be freedom and become a part of nature. We are therefore justified in saying: “If reason stands in a causal relation to phenomena, it is a faculty which originates the sensuous condition of an empirical series of effects.” For the condition, which resides in the reason, is non-sensuous, and therefore cannot be originated, or begin to be. And thus we find—what we could not discover in any empirical series—a condition of a successive series of events itself empirically unconditioned. For, in the present case, the condition stands out of and beyond the series of phenomena—it is intelligible, and it consequently cannot be subjected to any sensuous condition, or to any time-determination by a preceding cause.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"72.17"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0018","text":"[[63]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-63) The real morality of actions—their merit or demerit, and even that of our own conduct, is completely unknown to us. Our estimates can relate only to their empirical character. How much is the result of the action of free will, how much is to be ascribed to nature and to blameless error, or to a happy constitution of temperament (merito fortunae), no one can discover, nor, for this reason, determine with perfect justice.\n\nBut, in another respect, the same cause belongs also to the series of phenomena. Man is himself a phenomenon. His will has an empirical character, which is the empirical cause of all his actions. There is no condition—determining man and his volition in conformity with this character—which does not itself form part of the series of effects in nature, and is subject to their law—the law according to which an empirically undetermined cause of an event in time cannot exist. For this reason no given action can have an absolute and spontaneous origination, all actions being phenomena, and belonging to the world of experience. But it cannot be said of reason, that the state in which it determines the will is always preceded by some other state determining it. For reason is not a phenomenon, and therefore not subject to sensuous conditions; and, consequently, even in relation to its causality, the sequence or conditions of time do not influence reason, nor can the dynamical law of nature, which determines the sequence of time according to certain rules, be applied to it.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"72.18"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0019","text":"Reason is consequently the permanent condition of all actions of the human will. Each of these is determined in the empirical character of the man, even before it has taken place. The intelligible character, of which the former is but the sensuous schema, knows no before or after; and every action, irrespective of the time-relation in which it stands with other phenomena, is the immediate effect of the intelligible character of pure reason, which, consequently, enjoys freedom of action, and is not dynamically determined either by internal or external preceding conditions. This freedom must not be described, in a merely negative manner, as independence of empirical conditions, for in this case the faculty of reason would cease to be a cause of phenomena; but it must be regarded, positively, as a faculty which can spontaneously originate a series of events. At the same time, it must not be supposed that any beginning can take place in reason; on the contrary, reason, as the unconditioned condition of all action of the will, admits of no time-conditions, although its effect does really begin in a series of phenomena—a beginning which is not, however, absolutely primal.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"72.19"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0020","text":"I shall illustrate this regulative principle of reason by an example, from its employment in the world of experience; proved it cannot be by any amount of experience, or by any number of facts, for such arguments cannot establish the truth of transcendental propositions. Let us take a voluntary action—for example, a falsehood—by means of which a man has introduced a certain degree of confusion into the social life of humanity, which is judged according to the motives from which it originated, and the blame of which and of the evil consequences arising from it, is imputed to the offender. We at first proceed to examine the empirical character of the offence, and for this purpose we endeavour to penetrate to the sources of that character, such as a defective education, bad company, a shameless and wicked disposition, frivolity, and want of reflection—not forgetting also the occasioning causes which prevailed at the moment of the transgression. In this the procedure is exactly the same as that pursued in the investigation of the series of causes which determine a given physical effect. Now, although we believe the action to have been determined by all these circumstances, we do not the less blame the offender. We do not blame him for his unhappy disposition, nor for the circumstances which influenced him, nay, not even for his former course of life; for we presuppose that all these considerations may be set aside, that the series of preceding conditions may be regarded as having never existed, and that the action may be considered as completely unconditioned in relation to any state preceding, just as if the agent commenced with it an entirely new series of effects. Our blame of the offender is grounded upon a law of reason, which requires us to regard this faculty as a cause, which could have and ought to have otherwise determined the behaviour of the culprit, independently of all empirical conditions. This causality of reason we do not regard as a co-operating agency, but as complete in itself. It matters not whether the sensuous impulses favoured or opposed the action of this causality, the offence is estimated according to its intelligible character—the offender is decidedly worthy of blame, the moment he utters a falsehood. It follows that we regard reason, in spite of the empirical conditions of the act, as completely free, and therefore, as in the present case, culpable.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"72.20"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0021","text":"The above judgement is complete evidence that we are accustomed to think that reason is not affected by sensuous conditions, that in it no change takes place—although its phenomena, in other words, the mode in which it appears in its effects, are subject to change—that in it no preceding state determines the following, and, consequently, that it does not form a member of the series of sensuous conditions which necessitate phenomena according to natural laws. Reason is present and the same in all human actions and at all times; but it does not itself exist in time, and therefore does not enter upon any state in which it did not formerly exist. It is, relatively to new states or conditions, determining, but not determinable. Hence we cannot ask: “Why did not reason determine itself in a different manner?” The question ought to be thus stated: “Why did not reason employ its power of causality to determine certain phenomena in a different manner?” But this is a question which admits of no answer. For a different intelligible character would have exhibited a different empirical character; and, when we say that, in spite of the course which his whole former life has taken, the offender could have refrained from uttering the falsehood, this means merely that the act was subject to the power and authority—permissive or prohibitive—of reason. Now, reason is not subject in its causality to any conditions of phenomena or of time; and a difference in time may produce a difference in the relation of phenomena to each other—for these are not things and therefore not causes in themselves—but it cannot produce any difference in the relation in which the action stands to the faculty of reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"72.21"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0022","text":"Thus, then, in our investigation into free actions and the causal power which produced them, we arrive at an intelligible cause, beyond which, however, we cannot go; although we can recognize that it is free, that is, independent of all sensuous conditions, and that, in this way, it may be the sensuously unconditioned condition of phenomena. But for what reason the intelligible character generates such and such phenomena and exhibits such and such an empirical character under certain circumstances, it is beyond the power of our reason to decide. The question is as much above the power and the sphere of reason as the following would be: “Why does the transcendental object of our external sensuous intuition allow of no other form than that of intuition in space?” But the problem, which we were called upon to solve, does not require us to entertain any such questions. The problem was merely this—whether freedom and natural necessity can exist without opposition in the same action. To this question we have given a sufficient answer; for we have shown that, as the former stands in a relation to a different kind of condition from those of the latter, the law of the one does not affect the law of the other and that, consequently, both can exist together in independence of and without interference with each other.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"72.22"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","text":"The reader must be careful to remark that my intention in the above remarks has not been to prove the actual existence of freedom, as a faculty in which resides the cause of certain sensuous phenomena. For, not to mention that such an argument would not have a transcendental character, nor have been limited to the discussion of pure conceptions—all attempts at inferring from experience what cannot be cogitated in accordance with its laws, must ever be unsuccessful. Nay, more, I have not even aimed at demonstrating the possibility of freedom; for this too would have been a vain endeavour, inasmuch as it is beyond the power of the mind to cognize the possibility of a reality or of a causal power by the aid of mere à priori conceptions. Freedom has been considered in the foregoing remarks only as a transcendental idea, by means of which reason aims at originating a series of conditions in the world of phenomena with the help of that which is sensuously unconditioned, involving itself, however, in an antinomy with the laws which itself prescribes for the conduct of the understanding. That this antinomy is based upon a mere illusion, and that nature and freedom are at least not opposed—this was the only thing in our power to prove, and the question which it was our task to solve.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"72.23"}],"sha256":"1b27a442c6c7f17ce7571413b7f30b39cb02b39294af0cd7ac06eb54243b150d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4","title":"IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences","order":73,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_IS_4","text":"### IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences\n\nIn the preceding remarks, we considered the changes in the world of sense as constituting a dynamical series, in which each member is subordinated to another—as its cause. Our present purpose is to avail ourselves of this series of states or conditions as a guide to an existence which may be the highest condition of all changeable phenomena, that is, to a necessary being. Our endeavour to reach, not the unconditioned causality, but the unconditioned existence, of substance. The series before us is therefore a series of conceptions, and not of intuitions (in which the one intuition is the condition of the other).\n\nBut it is evident that, as all phenomena are subject to change and conditioned in their existence, the series of dependent existences cannot embrace an unconditioned member, the existence of which would be absolutely necessary. It follows that, if phenomena were things in themselves, and—as an immediate consequence from this supposition—condition and conditioned belonged to the same series of phenomena, the existence of a necessary being, as the condition of the existence of sensuous phenomena, would be perfectly impossible.\n\nAn important distinction, however, exists between the dynamical and the mathematical regress. The latter is engaged solely with the combination of parts into a whole, or with the division of a whole into its parts; and therefore are the conditions of its series parts of the series, and to be consequently regarded as homogeneous, and for this reason, as consisting, without exception, of phenomena. If the former regress, on the contrary, the aim of which is not to establish the possibility of an unconditioned whole consisting of given parts, or of an unconditioned part of a given whole, but to demonstrate the possibility of the deduction of a certain state from its cause, or of the contingent existence of substance from that which exists necessarily, it is not requisite that the condition should form part of an empirical series along with the conditioned.\n\nIn the case of the apparent antinomy with which we are at present dealing, there exists a way of escape from the difficulty; for it is not impossible that both of the contradictory statements may be true in different relations. All sensuous phenomena may be contingent, and consequently possess only an empirically conditioned existence, and yet there may also exist a non-empirical condition of the whole series, or, in other words, a necessary being. For this necessary being, as an intelligible condition, would not form a member—not even the highest member—of the series; the whole world of sense would be left in its empirically determined existence uninterfered with and uninfluenced. This would also form a ground of distinction between the modes of solution employed for the third and fourth antinomies. For, while in the consideration of freedom in the former antinomy, the thing itself—the cause (substantia phaenomenon)—was regarded as belonging to the series of conditions, and only its causality to the intelligible world—we are obliged in the present case to cogitate this necessary being as purely intelligible and as existing entirely apart from the world of sense (as an ens extramundanum); for otherwise it would be subject to the phenomenal law of contingency and dependence.\n\nIn relation to the present problem, therefore, the regulative principle of reason is that everything in the sensuous world possesses an empirically conditioned existence—that no property of the sensuous world possesses unconditioned necessity—that we are bound to expect, and, so far as is possible, to seek for the empirical condition of every member in the series of conditions—and that there is no sufficient reason to justify us in deducing any existence from a condition which lies out of and beyond the empirical series, or in regarding any existence as independent and self-subsistent; although this should not prevent us from recognizing the possibility of the whole series being based upon a being which is intelligible, and for this reason free from all empirical conditions.\n\nBut it has been far from my intention, in these remarks, to prove the existence of this unconditioned and necessary being, or even to evidence the possibility of a purely intelligible condition of the existence of all sensuous phenomena. As bounds were set to reason, to prevent it from leaving the guiding thread of empirical conditions and losing itself in transcendent theories which are incapable of concrete presentation; so it was my purpose, on the other hand, to set bounds to the law of the purely empirical understanding, and to protest against any attempts on its part at deciding on the possibility of things, or declaring the existence of the intelligible to be impossible, merely on the ground that it is not available for the explanation and exposition of phenomena. It has been shown, at the same time, that the contingency of all the phenomena of nature and their empirical conditions is quite consistent with the arbitrary hypothesis of a necessary, although purely intelligible condition, that no real contradiction exists between them and that, consequently, both may be true. The existence of such an absolutely necessary being may be impossible; but this can never be demonstrated from the universal contingency and dependence of sensuous phenomena, nor from the principle which forbids us to discontinue the series at some member of it, or to seek for its cause in some sphere of existence beyond the world of nature. Reason goes its way in the empirical world, and follows, too, its peculiar path in the sphere of the transcendental.\n\nThe sensuous world contains nothing but phenomena, which are mere representations, and always sensuously conditioned; things in themselves are not, and cannot be, objects to us. It is not to be wondered at, therefore, that we are not justified in leaping from some member of an empirical series beyond the world of sense, as if empirical representations were things in themselves, existing apart from their transcendental ground in the human mind, and the cause of whose existence may be sought out of the empirical series. This would certainly be the case with contingent things; but it cannot be with mere representations of things, the contingency of which is itself merely a phenomenon and can relate to no other regress than that which determines phenomena, that is, the empirical. But to cogitate an intelligible ground of phenomena, as free, moreover, from the contingency of the latter, conflicts neither with the unlimited nature of the empirical regress, nor with the complete contingency of phenomena. And the demonstration of this was the only thing necessary for the solution of this apparent antinomy. For if the condition of every conditioned—as regards its existence—is sensuous, and for this reason a part of the same series, it must be itself conditioned, as was shown in the antithesis of the fourth antinomy. The embarrassments into which a reason, which postulates the unconditioned, necessarily falls, must, therefore, continue to exist; or the unconditioned must be placed in the sphere of the intelligible. In this way, its necessity does not require, nor does it even permit, the presence of an empirical condition: and it is, consequently, unconditionally necessary.\n\nThe empirical employment of reason is not affected by the assumption of a purely intelligible being; it continues its operations on the principle of the contingency of all phenomena, proceeding from empirical conditions to still higher and higher conditions, themselves empirical. Just as little does this regulative principle exclude the assumption of an intelligible cause, when the question regards merely the pure employment of reason—in relation to ends or aims. For, in this case, an intelligible cause signifies merely the transcendental and to us unknown ground of the possibility of sensuous phenomena, and its existence, necessary and independent of all sensuous conditions, is not inconsistent with the contingency of phenomena, or with the unlimited possibility of regress which exists in the series of empirical conditions.\n\nConcluding Remarks on the Antinomy of Pure Reason.\n\nSo long as the object of our rational conceptions is the totality of conditions in the world of phenomena, and the satisfaction, from this source, of the requirements of reason, so long are our ideas transcendental and cosmological. But when we set the unconditioned—which is the aim of all our inquiries—in a sphere which lies out of the world of sense and possible experience, our ideas become transcendent. They are then not merely serviceable towards the completion of the exercise of reason (which remains an idea, never executed, but always to be pursued); they detach themselves completely from experience and construct for themselves objects, the material of which has not been presented by experience, and the objective reality of which is not based upon the completion of the empirical series, but upon pure à priori conceptions. The intelligible object of these transcendent ideas may be conceded, as a transcendental object. But we cannot cogitate it as a thing determinable by certain distinct predicates relating to its internal nature, for it has no connection with empirical conceptions; nor are we justified in affirming the existence of any such object. It is, consequently, a mere product of the mind alone. Of all the cosmological ideas, however, it is that occasioning the fourth antinomy which compels us to venture upon this step. For the existence of phenomena, always conditioned and never self-subsistent, requires us to look for an object different from phenomena—an intelligible object, with which all contingency must cease. But, as we have allowed ourselves to assume the existence of a self-subsistent reality out of the field of experience, and are therefore obliged to regard phenomena as merely a contingent mode of representing intelligible objects employed by beings which are themselves intelligences—no other course remains for us than to follow analogy and employ the same mode in forming some conception of intelligible things, of which we have not the least knowledge, which nature taught us to use in the formation of empirical conceptions. Experience made us acquainted with the contingent. But we are at present engaged in the discussion of things which are not objects of experience; and must, therefore, deduce our knowledge of them from that which is necessary absolutely and in itself, that is, from pure conceptions. Hence the first step which we take out of the world of sense obliges us to begin our system of new cognition with the investigation of a necessary being, and to deduce from our conceptions of it all our conceptions of intelligible things. This we propose to attempt in the following chapter.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0001","text":"### IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences\n\nIn the preceding remarks, we considered the changes in the world of sense as constituting a dynamical series, in which each member is subordinated to another—as its cause. Our present purpose is to avail ourselves of this series of states or conditions as a guide to an existence which may be the highest condition of all changeable phenomena, that is, to a necessary being. Our endeavour to reach, not the unconditioned causality, but the unconditioned existence, of substance. The series before us is therefore a series of conceptions, and not of intuitions (in which the one intuition is the condition of the other).\n\nBut it is evident that, as all phenomena are subject to change and conditioned in their existence, the series of dependent existences cannot embrace an unconditioned member, the existence of which would be absolutely necessary. It follows that, if phenomena were things in themselves, and—as an immediate consequence from this supposition—condition and conditioned belonged to the same series of phenomena, the existence of a necessary being, as the condition of the existence of sensuous phenomena, would be perfectly impossible.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"73.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","text":"An important distinction, however, exists between the dynamical and the mathematical regress. The latter is engaged solely with the combination of parts into a whole, or with the division of a whole into its parts; and therefore are the conditions of its series parts of the series, and to be consequently regarded as homogeneous, and for this reason, as consisting, without exception, of phenomena. If the former regress, on the contrary, the aim of which is not to establish the possibility of an unconditioned whole consisting of given parts, or of an unconditioned part of a given whole, but to demonstrate the possibility of the deduction of a certain state from its cause, or of the contingent existence of substance from that which exists necessarily, it is not requisite that the condition should form part of an empirical series along with the conditioned.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"73.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","text":"In the case of the apparent antinomy with which we are at present dealing, there exists a way of escape from the difficulty; for it is not impossible that both of the contradictory statements may be true in different relations. All sensuous phenomena may be contingent, and consequently possess only an empirically conditioned existence, and yet there may also exist a non-empirical condition of the whole series, or, in other words, a necessary being. For this necessary being, as an intelligible condition, would not form a member—not even the highest member—of the series; the whole world of sense would be left in its empirically determined existence uninterfered with and uninfluenced. This would also form a ground of distinction between the modes of solution employed for the third and fourth antinomies. For, while in the consideration of freedom in the former antinomy, the thing itself—the cause (substantia phaenomenon)—was regarded as belonging to the series of conditions, and only its causality to the intelligible world—we are obliged in the present case to cogitate this necessary being as purely intelligible and as existing entirely apart from the world of sense (as an ens extramundanum); for otherwise it would be subject to the phenomenal law of contingency and dependence.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"73.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","text":"In relation to the present problem, therefore, the regulative principle of reason is that everything in the sensuous world possesses an empirically conditioned existence—that no property of the sensuous world possesses unconditioned necessity—that we are bound to expect, and, so far as is possible, to seek for the empirical condition of every member in the series of conditions—and that there is no sufficient reason to justify us in deducing any existence from a condition which lies out of and beyond the empirical series, or in regarding any existence as independent and self-subsistent; although this should not prevent us from recognizing the possibility of the whole series being based upon a being which is intelligible, and for this reason free from all empirical conditions.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"73.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","text":"But it has been far from my intention, in these remarks, to prove the existence of this unconditioned and necessary being, or even to evidence the possibility of a purely intelligible condition of the existence of all sensuous phenomena. As bounds were set to reason, to prevent it from leaving the guiding thread of empirical conditions and losing itself in transcendent theories which are incapable of concrete presentation; so it was my purpose, on the other hand, to set bounds to the law of the purely empirical understanding, and to protest against any attempts on its part at deciding on the possibility of things, or declaring the existence of the intelligible to be impossible, merely on the ground that it is not available for the explanation and exposition of phenomena. It has been shown, at the same time, that the contingency of all the phenomena of nature and their empirical conditions is quite consistent with the arbitrary hypothesis of a necessary, although purely intelligible condition, that no real contradiction exists between them and that, consequently, both may be true. The existence of such an absolutely necessary being may be impossible; but this can never be demonstrated from the universal contingency and dependence of sensuous phenomena, nor from the principle which forbids us to discontinue the series at some member of it, or to seek for its cause in some sphere of existence beyond the world of nature. Reason goes its way in the empirical world, and follows, too, its peculiar path in the sphere of the transcendental.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"73.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0006","text":"The sensuous world contains nothing but phenomena, which are mere representations, and always sensuously conditioned; things in themselves are not, and cannot be, objects to us. It is not to be wondered at, therefore, that we are not justified in leaping from some member of an empirical series beyond the world of sense, as if empirical representations were things in themselves, existing apart from their transcendental ground in the human mind, and the cause of whose existence may be sought out of the empirical series. This would certainly be the case with contingent things; but it cannot be with mere representations of things, the contingency of which is itself merely a phenomenon and can relate to no other regress than that which determines phenomena, that is, the empirical. But to cogitate an intelligible ground of phenomena, as free, moreover, from the contingency of the latter, conflicts neither with the unlimited nature of the empirical regress, nor with the complete contingency of phenomena. And the demonstration of this was the only thing necessary for the solution of this apparent antinomy. For if the condition of every conditioned—as regards its existence—is sensuous, and for this reason a part of the same series, it must be itself conditioned, as was shown in the antithesis of the fourth antinomy. The embarrassments into which a reason, which postulates the unconditioned, necessarily falls, must, therefore, continue to exist; or the unconditioned must be placed in the sphere of the intelligible. In this way, its necessity does not require, nor does it even permit, the presence of an empirical condition: and it is, consequently, unconditionally necessary.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"73.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","text":"The empirical employment of reason is not affected by the assumption of a purely intelligible being; it continues its operations on the principle of the contingency of all phenomena, proceeding from empirical conditions to still higher and higher conditions, themselves empirical. Just as little does this regulative principle exclude the assumption of an intelligible cause, when the question regards merely the pure employment of reason—in relation to ends or aims. For, in this case, an intelligible cause signifies merely the transcendental and to us unknown ground of the possibility of sensuous phenomena, and its existence, necessary and independent of all sensuous conditions, is not inconsistent with the contingency of phenomena, or with the unlimited possibility of regress which exists in the series of empirical conditions.\n\nConcluding Remarks on the Antinomy of Pure Reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"73.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","text":"So long as the object of our rational conceptions is the totality of conditions in the world of phenomena, and the satisfaction, from this source, of the requirements of reason, so long are our ideas transcendental and cosmological. But when we set the unconditioned—which is the aim of all our inquiries—in a sphere which lies out of the world of sense and possible experience, our ideas become transcendent. They are then not merely serviceable towards the completion of the exercise of reason (which remains an idea, never executed, but always to be pursued); they detach themselves completely from experience and construct for themselves objects, the material of which has not been presented by experience, and the objective reality of which is not based upon the completion of the empirical series, but upon pure à priori conceptions. The intelligible object of these transcendent ideas may be conceded, as a transcendental object. But we cannot cogitate it as a thing determinable by certain distinct predicates relating to its internal nature, for it has no connection with empirical conceptions; nor are we justified in affirming the existence of any such object. It is, consequently, a mere product of the mind alone. Of all the cosmological ideas, however, it is that occasioning the fourth antinomy which compels us to venture upon this step. For the existence of phenomena, always conditioned and never self-subsistent, requires us to look for an object different from phenomena—an intelligible object, with which all contingency must cease. But, as we have allowed ourselves to assume the existence of a self-subsistent reality out of the field of experience, and are therefore obliged to regard phenomena as merely a contingent mode of representing intelligible objects employed by beings which are themselves intelligences—no other course remains for us than to follow analogy and employ the same mode in forming some conception of intelligible things, of which we have not the least knowledge, which nature taught us to use in the formation of empirical conceptions. Experience made us acquainted with the contingent. But we are at present engaged in the discussion of things which are not objects of experience; and must, therefore, deduce our knowledge of them from that which is necessary absolutely and in itself, that is, from pure conceptions. Hence the first step which we take out of the world of sense obliges us to begin our system of new cognition with the investigation of a necessary being, and to deduce from our conceptions of it all our conceptions of intelligible things. This we propose to attempt in the following chapter.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"73.8"}],"sha256":"1a4c314ef12dd92fbcdb3dea6e45aa9f4ce0de20a7f58df8030e5b5aa0350d98","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3","title":"Section I. Of the Ideal in General","order":74,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SI_3","text":"# Ideal Of Pure Reason\n\n### Chapter III. The Ideal of Pure Reason\n\n### Section I. Of the Ideal in General\n\nWe have seen that pure conceptions do not present objects to the mind, except under sensuous conditions; because the conditions of objective reality do not exist in these conceptions, which contain, in fact, nothing but the mere form of thought. They may, however, when applied to phenomena, be presented in concreto; for it is phenomena that present to them the materials for the formation of empirical conceptions, which are nothing more than concrete forms of the conceptions of the understanding. But ideas are still further removed from objective reality than categories; for no phenomenon can ever present them to the human mind in concreto. They contain a certain perfection, attainable by no possible empirical cognition; and they give to reason a systematic unity, to which the unity of experience attempts to approximate, but can never completely attain.\n\nBut still further removed than the idea from objective reality is the Ideal, by which term I understand the idea, not in concreto, but in individuo—as an individual thing, determinable or determined by the idea alone. The idea of humanity in its complete perfection supposes not only the advancement of all the powers and faculties, which constitute our conception of human nature, to a complete attainment of their final aims, but also everything which is requisite for the complete determination of the idea; for of all contradictory predicates, only one can conform with the idea of the perfect man. What I have termed an ideal was in Plato’s philosophy an idea of the divine mind—an individual object present to its pure intuition, the most perfect of every kind of possible beings, and the archetype of all phenomenal existences.\n\nWithout rising to these speculative heights, we are bound to confess that human reason contains not only ideas, but ideals, which possess, not, like those of Plato, creative, but certainly practical power—as regulative principles, and form the basis of the perfectibility of certain actions. Moral conceptions are not perfectly pure conceptions of reason, because an empirical element—of pleasure or pain—lies at the foundation of them. In relation, however, to the principle, whereby reason sets bounds to a freedom which is in itself without law, and consequently when we attend merely to their form, they may be considered as pure conceptions of reason. Virtue and wisdom in their perfect purity are ideas. But the wise man of the Stoics is an ideal, that is to say, a human being existing only in thought and in complete conformity with the idea of wisdom. As the idea provides a rule, so the ideal serves as an archetype for the perfect and complete determination of the copy. Thus the conduct of this wise and divine man serves us as a standard of action, with which we may compare and judge ourselves, which may help us to reform ourselves, although the perfection it demands can never be attained by us. Although we cannot concede objective reality to these ideals, they are not to be considered as chimeras; on the contrary, they provide reason with a standard, which enables it to estimate, by comparison, the degree of incompleteness in the objects presented to it. But to aim at realizing the ideal in an example in the world of experience—to describe, for instance, the character of the perfectly wise man in a romance—is impracticable. Nay more, there is something absurd in the attempt; and the result must be little edifying, as the natural limitations, which are continually breaking in upon the perfection and completeness of the idea, destroy the illusion in the story and throw an air of suspicion even on what is good in the idea, which hence appears fictitious and unreal.\n\nSuch is the constitution of the ideal of reason, which is always based upon determinate conceptions, and serves as a rule and a model for limitation or of criticism. Very different is the nature of the ideals of the imagination. Of these it is impossible to present an intelligible conception; they are a kind of monogram, drawn according to no determinate rule, and forming rather a vague picture—the production of many diverse experiences—than a determinate image. Such are the ideals which painters and physiognomists profess to have in their minds, and which can serve neither as a model for production nor as a standard for appreciation. They may be termed, though improperly, sensuous ideals, as they are declared to be models of certain possible empirical intuitions. They cannot, however, furnish rules or standards for explanation or examination.\n\nIn its ideals, reason aims at complete and perfect determination according to à priori rules; and hence it cogitates an object, which must be completely determinable in conformity with principles, although all empirical conditions are absent, and the conception of the object is on this account transcendent.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","text":"# Ideal Of Pure Reason\n\n### Chapter III. The Ideal of Pure Reason\n\n### Section I. Of the Ideal in General\n\nWe have seen that pure conceptions do not present objects to the mind, except under sensuous conditions; because the conditions of objective reality do not exist in these conceptions, which contain, in fact, nothing but the mere form of thought. They may, however, when applied to phenomena, be presented in concreto; for it is phenomena that present to them the materials for the formation of empirical conceptions, which are nothing more than concrete forms of the conceptions of the understanding. But ideas are still further removed from objective reality than categories; for no phenomenon can ever present them to the human mind in concreto. They contain a certain perfection, attainable by no possible empirical cognition; and they give to reason a systematic unity, to which the unity of experience attempts to approximate, but can never completely attain.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"74.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","text":"But still further removed than the idea from objective reality is the Ideal, by which term I understand the idea, not in concreto, but in individuo—as an individual thing, determinable or determined by the idea alone. The idea of humanity in its complete perfection supposes not only the advancement of all the powers and faculties, which constitute our conception of human nature, to a complete attainment of their final aims, but also everything which is requisite for the complete determination of the idea; for of all contradictory predicates, only one can conform with the idea of the perfect man. What I have termed an ideal was in Plato’s philosophy an idea of the divine mind—an individual object present to its pure intuition, the most perfect of every kind of possible beings, and the archetype of all phenomenal existences.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"74.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","text":"Without rising to these speculative heights, we are bound to confess that human reason contains not only ideas, but ideals, which possess, not, like those of Plato, creative, but certainly practical power—as regulative principles, and form the basis of the perfectibility of certain actions. Moral conceptions are not perfectly pure conceptions of reason, because an empirical element—of pleasure or pain—lies at the foundation of them. In relation, however, to the principle, whereby reason sets bounds to a freedom which is in itself without law, and consequently when we attend merely to their form, they may be considered as pure conceptions of reason. Virtue and wisdom in their perfect purity are ideas. But the wise man of the Stoics is an ideal, that is to say, a human being existing only in thought and in complete conformity with the idea of wisdom. As the idea provides a rule, so the ideal serves as an archetype for the perfect and complete determination of the copy. Thus the conduct of this wise and divine man serves us as a standard of action, with which we may compare and judge ourselves, which may help us to reform ourselves, although the perfection it demands can never be attained by us. Although we cannot concede objective reality to these ideals, they are not to be considered as chimeras; on the contrary, they provide reason with a standard, which enables it to estimate, by comparison, the degree of incompleteness in the objects presented to it. But to aim at realizing the ideal in an example in the world of experience—to describe, for instance, the character of the perfectly wise man in a romance—is impracticable. Nay more, there is something absurd in the attempt; and the result must be little edifying, as the natural limitations, which are continually breaking in upon the perfection and completeness of the idea, destroy the illusion in the story and throw an air of suspicion even on what is good in the idea, which hence appears fictitious and unreal.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"74.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","text":"Such is the constitution of the ideal of reason, which is always based upon determinate conceptions, and serves as a rule and a model for limitation or of criticism. Very different is the nature of the ideals of the imagination. Of these it is impossible to present an intelligible conception; they are a kind of monogram, drawn according to no determinate rule, and forming rather a vague picture—the production of many diverse experiences—than a determinate image. Such are the ideals which painters and physiognomists profess to have in their minds, and which can serve neither as a model for production nor as a standard for appreciation. They may be termed, though improperly, sensuous ideals, as they are declared to be models of certain possible empirical intuitions. They cannot, however, furnish rules or standards for explanation or examination.\n\nIn its ideals, reason aims at complete and perfect determination according to à priori rules; and hence it cogitates an object, which must be completely determinable in conformity with principles, although all empirical conditions are absent, and the conception of the object is on this account transcendent.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"74.4"}],"sha256":"41444f6d98e3d619370c586f4fa99b66659d61fcb3b1c4519cc1211747266762","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4","title":"Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)","order":75,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SI_4","text":"### Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)\n\nEvery conception is, in relation to that which is not contained in it, undetermined and subject to the principle of determinability. This principle is that, of every two contradictorily opposed predicates, only one can belong to a conception. It is a purely logical principle, itself based upon the principle of contradiction; inasmuch as it makes complete abstraction of the content and attends merely to the logical form of the cognition.\n\nBut again, everything, as regards its possibility, is also subject to the principle of complete determination, according to which one of all the possible contradictory predicates of things must belong to it. This principle is not based merely upon that of contradiction; for, in addition to the relation between two contradictory predicates, it regards everything as standing in a relation to the sum of possibilities, as the sum total of all predicates of things, and, while presupposing this sum as an à priori condition, presents to the mind everything as receiving the possibility of its individual existence from the relation it bears to, and the share it possesses in, the aforesaid sum of possibilities.[[64]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-64) The principle of complete determination relates the content and not to the logical form. It is the principle of the synthesis of all the predicates which are required to constitute the complete conception of a thing, and not a mere principle analytical representation, which enounces that one of two contradictory predicates must belong to a conception. It contains, moreover, a transcendental presupposition—that, namely, of the material for all possibility, which must contain à priori the data for this or that particular possibility.\n\n[[64]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-64) Thus this principle declares everything to possess a relation to a common correlate—the sum-total of possibility, which, if discovered to exist in the idea of one individual thing, would establish the affinity of all possible things, from the identity of the ground of their complete determination. The determinability of every conception is subordinate to the universality (Allgemeinheit, universalitas) of the principle of excluded middle; the determination of a thing to the totality (Allheit, universitas) of all possible predicates.\n\nThe proposition, everything which exists is completely determined, means not only that one of every pair of given contradictory attributes, but that one of all possible attributes, is always predicable of the thing; in it the predicates are not merely compared logically with each other, but the thing itself is transcendentally compared with the sum-total of all possible predicates. The proposition is equivalent to saying: “To attain to a complete knowledge of a thing, it is necessary to possess a knowledge of everything that is possible, and to determine it thereby in a positive or negative manner.” The conception of complete determination is consequently a conception which cannot be presented in its totality in concreto, and is therefore based upon an idea, which has its seat in the reason—the faculty which prescribes to the understanding the laws of its harmonious and perfect exercise.\n\nNow, although this idea of the sum-total of all possibility, in so far as it forms the condition of the complete determination of everything, is itself undetermined in relation to the predicates which may constitute this sum-total, and we cogitate in it merely the sum-total of all possible predicates—we nevertheless find, upon closer examination, that this idea, as a primitive conception of the mind, excludes a large number of predicates—those deduced and those irreconcilable with others, and that it is evolved as a conception completely determined à priori. Thus it becomes the conception of an individual object, which is completely determined by and through the mere idea, and must consequently be termed an ideal of pure reason.\n\nWhen we consider all possible predicates, not merely logically, but transcendentally, that is to say, with reference to the content which may be cogitated as existing in them à priori, we shall find that some indicate a being, others merely a non-being. The logical negation expressed in the word not does not properly belong to a conception, but only to the relation of one conception to another in a judgement, and is consequently quite insufficient to present to the mind the content of a conception. The expression not mortal does not indicate that a non-being is cogitated in the object; it does not concern the content at all. A transcendental negation, on the contrary, indicates non-being in itself, and is opposed to transcendental affirmation, the conception of which of itself expresses a being. Hence this affirmation indicates a reality, because in and through it objects are considered to be something—to be things; while the opposite negation, on the other hand, indicates a mere want, or privation, or absence, and, where such negations alone are attached to a representation, the non-existence of anything corresponding to the representation.\n\nNow a negation cannot be cogitated as determined, without cogitating at the same time the opposite affirmation. The man born blind has not the least notion of darkness, because he has none of light; the vagabond knows nothing of poverty, because he has never known what it is to be in comfort;[[65]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-65) the ignorant man has no conception of his ignorance, because he has no conception of knowledge. All conceptions of negatives are accordingly derived or deduced conceptions; and realities contain the data, and, so to speak, the material or transcendental content of the possibility and complete determination of all things.\n\n[[65]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-65) The investigations and calculations of astronomers have taught us much that is wonderful; but the most important lesson we have received from them is the discovery of the abyss of our ignorance in relation to the universe—an ignorance the magnitude of which reason, without the information thus derived, could never have conceived. This discovery of our deficiencies must produce a great change in the determination of the aims of human reason.\n\nIf, therefore, a transcendental substratum lies at the foundation of the complete determination of things—a substratum which is to form the fund from which all possible predicates of things are to be supplied, this substratum cannot be anything else than the idea of a sum-total of reality (omnitudo realitatis). In this view, negations are nothing but limitations—a term which could not, with propriety, be applied to them, if the unlimited (the all) did not form the true basis of our conception.\n\nThis conception of a sum-total of reality is the conception of a thing in itself, regarded as completely determined; and the conception of an ens realissimum is the conception of an individual being, inasmuch as it is determined by that predicate of all possible contradictory predicates, which indicates and belongs to being. It is, therefore, a transcendental ideal which forms the basis of the complete determination of everything that exists, and is the highest material condition of its possibility—a condition on which must rest the cogitation of all objects with respect to their content. Nay, more, this ideal is the only proper ideal of which the human mind is capable; because in this case alone a general conception of a thing is completely determined by and through itself, and cognized as the representation of an individuum.\n\nThe logical determination of a conception is based upon a disjunctive syllogism, the major of which contains the logical division of the extent of a general conception, the minor limits this extent to a certain part, while the conclusion determines the conception by this part. The general conception of a reality cannot be divided à priori, because, without the aid of experience, we cannot know any determinate kinds of reality, standing under the former as the genus. The transcendental principle of the complete determination of all things is therefore merely the representation of the sum-total of all reality; it is not a conception which is the genus of all predicates under itself, but one which comprehends them all within itself. The complete determination of a thing is consequently based upon the limitation of this total of reality, so much being predicated of the thing, while all that remains over is excluded—a procedure which is in exact agreement with that of the disjunctive syllogism and the determination of the objects in the conclusion by one of the members of the division. It follows that reason, in laying the transcendental ideal at the foundation of its determination of all possible things, takes a course in exact analogy with that which it pursues in disjunctive syllogisms—a proposition which formed the basis of the systematic division of all transcendental ideas, according to which they are produced in complete parallelism with the three modes of syllogistic reasoning employed by the human mind.\n\nIt is self-evident that reason, in cogitating the necessary complete determination of things, does not presuppose the existence of a being corresponding to its ideal, but merely the idea of the ideal—for the purpose of deducing from the unconditional totality of complete determination, The ideal is therefore the prototype of all things, which, as defective copies (ectypa), receive from it the material of their possibility, and approximate to it more or less, though it is impossible that they can ever attain to its perfection.\n\nThe possibility of things must therefore be regarded as derived—except that of the thing which contains in itself all reality, which must be considered to be primitive and original. For all negations—and they are the only predicates by means of which all other things can be distinguished from the ens realissimum—are mere limitations of a greater and a higher—nay, the highest reality; and they consequently presuppose this reality, and are, as regards their content, derived from it. The manifold nature of things is only an infinitely various mode of limiting the conception of the highest reality, which is their common substratum; just as all figures are possible only as different modes of limiting infinite space. The object of the ideal of reason—an object existing only in reason itself—is also termed the primal being (ens originarium); as having no existence superior to him, the supreme being (ens summum); and as being the condition of all other beings, which rank under it, the being of all beings (ens entium). But none of these terms indicate the objective relation of an actually existing object to other things, but merely that of an idea to conceptions; and all our investigations into this subject still leave us in perfect uncertainty with regard to the existence of this being.\n\nA primal being cannot be said to consist of many other beings with an existence which is derivative, for the latter presuppose the former, and therefore cannot be constitutive parts of it. It follows that the ideal of the primal being must be cogitated as simple.\n\nThe deduction of the possibility of all other things from this primal being cannot, strictly speaking, be considered as a limitation, or as a kind of division of its reality; for this would be regarding the primal being as a mere aggregate—which has been shown to be impossible, although it was so represented in our first rough sketch. The highest reality must be regarded rather as the ground than as the sum-total of the possibility of all things, and the manifold nature of things be based, not upon the limitation of the primal being itself, but upon the complete series of effects which flow from it. And thus all our powers of sense, as well as all phenomenal reality, may be with propriety regarded as belonging to this series of effects, while they could not have formed parts of the idea, considered as an aggregate. Pursuing this track, and hypostatizing this idea, we shall find ourselves authorized to determine our notion of the Supreme Being by means of the mere conception of a highest reality, as one, simple, all-sufficient, eternal, and so on—in one word, to determine it in its unconditioned completeness by the aid of every possible predicate. The conception of such a being is the conception of God in its transcendental sense, and thus the ideal of pure reason is the object-matter of a transcendental theology.\n\nBut, by such an employment of the transcendental idea, we should be over stepping the limits of its validity and purpose. For reason placed it, as the conception of all reality, at the basis of the complete determination of things, without requiring that this conception be regarded as the conception of an objective existence. Such an existence would be purely fictitious, and the hypostatizing of the content of the idea into an ideal, as an individual being, is a step perfectly unauthorized. Nay, more, we are not even called upon to assume the possibility of such an hypothesis, as none of the deductions drawn from such an ideal would affect the complete determination of things in general—for the sake of which alone is the idea necessary.\n\nIt is not sufficient to circumscribe the procedure and the dialectic of reason; we must also endeavour to discover the sources of this dialectic, that we may have it in our power to give a rational explanation of this illusion, as a phenomenon of the human mind. For the ideal, of which we are at present speaking, is based, not upon an arbitrary, but upon a natural, idea. The question hence arises: How happens it that reason regards the possibility of all things as deduced from a single possibility, that, to wit, of the highest reality, and presupposes this as existing in an individual and primal being?\n\nThe answer is ready; it is at once presented by the procedure of transcendental analytic. The possibility of sensuous objects is a relation of these objects to thought, in which something (the empirical form) may be cogitated à priori; while that which constitutes the matter—the reality of the phenomenon (that element which corresponds to sensation)—must be given from without, as otherwise it could not even be cogitated by, nor could its possibility be presentable to the mind. Now, a sensuous object is completely determined, when it has been compared with all phenomenal predicates, and represented by means of these either positively or negatively. But, as that which constitutes the thing itself—the real in a phenomenon, must be given, and that, in which the real of all phenomena is given, is experience, one, sole, and all-embracing—the material of the possibility of all sensuous objects must be presupposed as given in a whole, and it is upon the limitation of this whole that the possibility of all empirical objects, their distinction from each other and their complete determination, are based. Now, no other objects are presented to us besides sensuous objects, and these can be given only in connection with a possible experience; it follows that a thing is not an object to us, unless it presupposes the whole or sum-total of empirical reality as the condition of its possibility. Now, a natural illusion leads us to consider this principle, which is valid only of sensuous objects, as valid with regard to things in general. And thus we are induced to hold the empirical principle of our conceptions of the possibility of things, as phenomena, by leaving out this limitative condition, to be a transcendental principle of the possibility of things in general.\n\nWe proceed afterwards to hypostatize this idea of the sum-total of all reality, by changing the distributive unity of the empirical exercise of the understanding into the collective unity of an empirical whole—a dialectical illusion, and by cogitating this whole or sum of experience as an individual thing, containing in itself all empirical reality. This individual thing or being is then, by means of the above-mentioned transcendental subreption, substituted for our notion of a thing which stands at the head of the possibility of all things, the real conditions of whose complete determination it presents.[[66]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-66)\n\n[[66]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-66) This ideal of the ens realissimum—although merely a mental representation—is first objectivized, that is, has an objective existence attributed to it, then hypostatized, and finally, by the natural progress of reason to the completion of unity, personified, as we shall show presently. For the regulative unity of experience is not based upon phenomena themselves, but upon the connection of the variety of phenomena by the understanding in a consciousness, and thus the unity of the supreme reality and the complete determinability of all things, seem to reside in a supreme understanding, and, consequently, in a conscious intelligence.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0001","text":"### Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)\n\nEvery conception is, in relation to that which is not contained in it, undetermined and subject to the principle of determinability. This principle is that, of every two contradictorily opposed predicates, only one can belong to a conception. It is a purely logical principle, itself based upon the principle of contradiction; inasmuch as it makes complete abstraction of the content and attends merely to the logical form of the cognition.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"75.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","text":"But again, everything, as regards its possibility, is also subject to the principle of complete determination, according to which one of all the possible contradictory predicates of things must belong to it. This principle is not based merely upon that of contradiction; for, in addition to the relation between two contradictory predicates, it regards everything as standing in a relation to the sum of possibilities, as the sum total of all predicates of things, and, while presupposing this sum as an à priori condition, presents to the mind everything as receiving the possibility of its individual existence from the relation it bears to, and the share it possesses in, the aforesaid sum of possibilities.[[64]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-64) The principle of complete determination relates the content and not to the logical form. It is the principle of the synthesis of all the predicates which are required to constitute the complete conception of a thing, and not a mere principle analytical representation, which enounces that one of two contradictory predicates must belong to a conception. It contains, moreover, a transcendental presupposition—that, namely, of the material for all possibility, which must contain à priori the data for this or that particular possibility.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"75.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0003","text":"[[64]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-64) Thus this principle declares everything to possess a relation to a common correlate—the sum-total of possibility, which, if discovered to exist in the idea of one individual thing, would establish the affinity of all possible things, from the identity of the ground of their complete determination. The determinability of every conception is subordinate to the universality (Allgemeinheit, universalitas) of the principle of excluded middle; the determination of a thing to the totality (Allheit, universitas) of all possible predicates.\n\nThe proposition, everything which exists is completely determined, means not only that one of every pair of given contradictory attributes, but that one of all possible attributes, is always predicable of the thing; in it the predicates are not merely compared logically with each other, but the thing itself is transcendentally compared with the sum-total of all possible predicates. The proposition is equivalent to saying: “To attain to a complete knowledge of a thing, it is necessary to possess a knowledge of everything that is possible, and to determine it thereby in a positive or negative manner.” The conception of complete determination is consequently a conception which cannot be presented in its totality in concreto, and is therefore based upon an idea, which has its seat in the reason—the faculty which prescribes to the understanding the laws of its harmonious and perfect exercise.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"75.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0004","text":"Now, although this idea of the sum-total of all possibility, in so far as it forms the condition of the complete determination of everything, is itself undetermined in relation to the predicates which may constitute this sum-total, and we cogitate in it merely the sum-total of all possible predicates—we nevertheless find, upon closer examination, that this idea, as a primitive conception of the mind, excludes a large number of predicates—those deduced and those irreconcilable with others, and that it is evolved as a conception completely determined à priori. Thus it becomes the conception of an individual object, which is completely determined by and through the mere idea, and must consequently be termed an ideal of pure reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"75.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0005","text":"When we consider all possible predicates, not merely logically, but transcendentally, that is to say, with reference to the content which may be cogitated as existing in them à priori, we shall find that some indicate a being, others merely a non-being. The logical negation expressed in the word not does not properly belong to a conception, but only to the relation of one conception to another in a judgement, and is consequently quite insufficient to present to the mind the content of a conception. The expression not mortal does not indicate that a non-being is cogitated in the object; it does not concern the content at all. A transcendental negation, on the contrary, indicates non-being in itself, and is opposed to transcendental affirmation, the conception of which of itself expresses a being. Hence this affirmation indicates a reality, because in and through it objects are considered to be something—to be things; while the opposite negation, on the other hand, indicates a mere want, or privation, or absence, and, where such negations alone are attached to a representation, the non-existence of anything corresponding to the representation.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"75.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","text":"Now a negation cannot be cogitated as determined, without cogitating at the same time the opposite affirmation. The man born blind has not the least notion of darkness, because he has none of light; the vagabond knows nothing of poverty, because he has never known what it is to be in comfort;[[65]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-65) the ignorant man has no conception of his ignorance, because he has no conception of knowledge. All conceptions of negatives are accordingly derived or deduced conceptions; and realities contain the data, and, so to speak, the material or transcendental content of the possibility and complete determination of all things.\n\n[[65]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-65) The investigations and calculations of astronomers have taught us much that is wonderful; but the most important lesson we have received from them is the discovery of the abyss of our ignorance in relation to the universe—an ignorance the magnitude of which reason, without the information thus derived, could never have conceived. This discovery of our deficiencies must produce a great change in the determination of the aims of human reason.\n\nIf, therefore, a transcendental substratum lies at the foundation of the complete determination of things—a substratum which is to form the fund from which all possible predicates of things are to be supplied, this substratum cannot be anything else than the idea of a sum-total of reality (omnitudo realitatis). In this view, negations are nothing but limitations—a term which could not, with propriety, be applied to them, if the unlimited (the all) did not form the true basis of our conception.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"75.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","text":"This conception of a sum-total of reality is the conception of a thing in itself, regarded as completely determined; and the conception of an ens realissimum is the conception of an individual being, inasmuch as it is determined by that predicate of all possible contradictory predicates, which indicates and belongs to being. It is, therefore, a transcendental ideal which forms the basis of the complete determination of everything that exists, and is the highest material condition of its possibility—a condition on which must rest the cogitation of all objects with respect to their content. Nay, more, this ideal is the only proper ideal of which the human mind is capable; because in this case alone a general conception of a thing is completely determined by and through itself, and cognized as the representation of an individuum.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"75.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0008","text":"The logical determination of a conception is based upon a disjunctive syllogism, the major of which contains the logical division of the extent of a general conception, the minor limits this extent to a certain part, while the conclusion determines the conception by this part. The general conception of a reality cannot be divided à priori, because, without the aid of experience, we cannot know any determinate kinds of reality, standing under the former as the genus. The transcendental principle of the complete determination of all things is therefore merely the representation of the sum-total of all reality; it is not a conception which is the genus of all predicates under itself, but one which comprehends them all within itself. The complete determination of a thing is consequently based upon the limitation of this total of reality, so much being predicated of the thing, while all that remains over is excluded—a procedure which is in exact agreement with that of the disjunctive syllogism and the determination of the objects in the conclusion by one of the members of the division. It follows that reason, in laying the transcendental ideal at the foundation of its determination of all possible things, takes a course in exact analogy with that which it pursues in disjunctive syllogisms—a proposition which formed the basis of the systematic division of all transcendental ideas, according to which they are produced in complete parallelism with the three modes of syllogistic reasoning employed by the human mind.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"75.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","text":"It is self-evident that reason, in cogitating the necessary complete determination of things, does not presuppose the existence of a being corresponding to its ideal, but merely the idea of the ideal—for the purpose of deducing from the unconditional totality of complete determination, The ideal is therefore the prototype of all things, which, as defective copies (ectypa), receive from it the material of their possibility, and approximate to it more or less, though it is impossible that they can ever attain to its perfection.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"75.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0010","text":"The possibility of things must therefore be regarded as derived—except that of the thing which contains in itself all reality, which must be considered to be primitive and original. For all negations—and they are the only predicates by means of which all other things can be distinguished from the ens realissimum—are mere limitations of a greater and a higher—nay, the highest reality; and they consequently presuppose this reality, and are, as regards their content, derived from it. The manifold nature of things is only an infinitely various mode of limiting the conception of the highest reality, which is their common substratum; just as all figures are possible only as different modes of limiting infinite space. The object of the ideal of reason—an object existing only in reason itself—is also termed the primal being (ens originarium); as having no existence superior to him, the supreme being (ens summum); and as being the condition of all other beings, which rank under it, the being of all beings (ens entium). But none of these terms indicate the objective relation of an actually existing object to other things, but merely that of an idea to conceptions; and all our investigations into this subject still leave us in perfect uncertainty with regard to the existence of this being.\n\nA primal being cannot be said to consist of many other beings with an existence which is derivative, for the latter presuppose the former, and therefore cannot be constitutive parts of it. It follows that the ideal of the primal being must be cogitated as simple.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"75.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0011","text":"The deduction of the possibility of all other things from this primal being cannot, strictly speaking, be considered as a limitation, or as a kind of division of its reality; for this would be regarding the primal being as a mere aggregate—which has been shown to be impossible, although it was so represented in our first rough sketch. The highest reality must be regarded rather as the ground than as the sum-total of the possibility of all things, and the manifold nature of things be based, not upon the limitation of the primal being itself, but upon the complete series of effects which flow from it. And thus all our powers of sense, as well as all phenomenal reality, may be with propriety regarded as belonging to this series of effects, while they could not have formed parts of the idea, considered as an aggregate. Pursuing this track, and hypostatizing this idea, we shall find ourselves authorized to determine our notion of the Supreme Being by means of the mere conception of a highest reality, as one, simple, all-sufficient, eternal, and so on—in one word, to determine it in its unconditioned completeness by the aid of every possible predicate. The conception of such a being is the conception of God in its transcendental sense, and thus the ideal of pure reason is the object-matter of a transcendental theology.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"75.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","text":"But, by such an employment of the transcendental idea, we should be over stepping the limits of its validity and purpose. For reason placed it, as the conception of all reality, at the basis of the complete determination of things, without requiring that this conception be regarded as the conception of an objective existence. Such an existence would be purely fictitious, and the hypostatizing of the content of the idea into an ideal, as an individual being, is a step perfectly unauthorized. Nay, more, we are not even called upon to assume the possibility of such an hypothesis, as none of the deductions drawn from such an ideal would affect the complete determination of things in general—for the sake of which alone is the idea necessary.\n\nIt is not sufficient to circumscribe the procedure and the dialectic of reason; we must also endeavour to discover the sources of this dialectic, that we may have it in our power to give a rational explanation of this illusion, as a phenomenon of the human mind. For the ideal, of which we are at present speaking, is based, not upon an arbitrary, but upon a natural, idea. The question hence arises: How happens it that reason regards the possibility of all things as deduced from a single possibility, that, to wit, of the highest reality, and presupposes this as existing in an individual and primal being?","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"75.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","text":"The answer is ready; it is at once presented by the procedure of transcendental analytic. The possibility of sensuous objects is a relation of these objects to thought, in which something (the empirical form) may be cogitated à priori; while that which constitutes the matter—the reality of the phenomenon (that element which corresponds to sensation)—must be given from without, as otherwise it could not even be cogitated by, nor could its possibility be presentable to the mind. Now, a sensuous object is completely determined, when it has been compared with all phenomenal predicates, and represented by means of these either positively or negatively. But, as that which constitutes the thing itself—the real in a phenomenon, must be given, and that, in which the real of all phenomena is given, is experience, one, sole, and all-embracing—the material of the possibility of all sensuous objects must be presupposed as given in a whole, and it is upon the limitation of this whole that the possibility of all empirical objects, their distinction from each other and their complete determination, are based. Now, no other objects are presented to us besides sensuous objects, and these can be given only in connection with a possible experience; it follows that a thing is not an object to us, unless it presupposes the whole or sum-total of empirical reality as the condition of its possibility. Now, a natural illusion leads us to consider this principle, which is valid only of sensuous objects, as valid with regard to things in general. And thus we are induced to hold the empirical principle of our conceptions of the possibility of things, as phenomena, by leaving out this limitative condition, to be a transcendental principle of the possibility of things in general.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"75.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","text":"We proceed afterwards to hypostatize this idea of the sum-total of all reality, by changing the distributive unity of the empirical exercise of the understanding into the collective unity of an empirical whole—a dialectical illusion, and by cogitating this whole or sum of experience as an individual thing, containing in itself all empirical reality. This individual thing or being is then, by means of the above-mentioned transcendental subreption, substituted for our notion of a thing which stands at the head of the possibility of all things, the real conditions of whose complete determination it presents.[[66]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-66)\n\n[[66]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-66) This ideal of the ens realissimum—although merely a mental representation—is first objectivized, that is, has an objective existence attributed to it, then hypostatized, and finally, by the natural progress of reason to the completion of unity, personified, as we shall show presently. For the regulative unity of experience is not based upon phenomena themselves, but upon the connection of the variety of phenomena by the understanding in a consciousness, and thus the unity of the supreme reality and the complete determinability of all things, seem to reside in a supreme understanding, and, consequently, in a conscious intelligence.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"75.14"}],"sha256":"444e902bd03baae73a2314622bfda7799aff958fd7ad4a165aa34ffae5216967","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE","title":"Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being","order":76,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIAE","text":"### Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being\n\nNotwithstanding the pressing necessity which reason feels, to form some presupposition that shall serve the understanding as a proper basis for the complete determination of its conceptions, the idealistic and factitious nature of such a presupposition is too evident to allow reason for a moment to persuade itself into a belief of the objective existence of a mere creation of its own thought. But there are other considerations which compel reason to seek out some resting place in the regress from the conditioned to the unconditioned, which is not given as an actual existence from the mere conception of it, although it alone can give completeness to the series of conditions. And this is the natural course of every human reason, even of the most uneducated, although the path at first entered it does not always continue to follow. It does not begin from conceptions, but from common experience, and requires a basis in actual existence. But this basis is insecure, unless it rests upon the immovable rock of the absolutely necessary. And this foundation is itself unworthy of trust, if it leave under and above it empty space, if it do not fill all, and leave no room for a why or a wherefore, if it be not, in one word, infinite in its reality.\n\nIf we admit the existence of some one thing, whatever it may be, we must also admit that there is something which exists necessarily. For what is contingent exists only under the condition of some other thing, which is its cause; and from this we must go on to conclude the existence of a cause which is not contingent, and which consequently exists necessarily and unconditionally. Such is the argument by which reason justifies its advances towards a primal being.\n\nNow reason looks round for the conception of a being that may be admitted, without inconsistency, to be worthy of the attribute of absolute necessity, not for the purpose of inferring à priori, from the conception of such a being, its objective existence (for if reason allowed itself to take this course, it would not require a basis in given and actual existence, but merely the support of pure conceptions), but for the purpose of discovering, among all our conceptions of possible things, that conception which possesses no element inconsistent with the idea of absolute necessity. For that there must be some absolutely necessary existence, it regards as a truth already established. Now, if it can remove every existence incapable of supporting the attribute of absolute necessity, excepting one—this must be the absolutely necessary being, whether its necessity is comprehensible by us, that is, deducible from the conception of it alone, or not.\n\nNow that, the conception of which contains a therefore to every wherefore, which is not defective in any respect whatever, which is all-sufficient as a condition, seems to be the being of which we can justly predicate absolute necessity—for this reason, that, possessing the conditions of all that is possible, it does not and cannot itself require any condition. And thus it satisfies, in one respect at least, the requirements of the conception of absolute necessity. In this view, it is superior to all other conceptions, which, as deficient and incomplete, do not possess the characteristic of independence of all higher conditions. It is true that we cannot infer from this that what does not contain in itself the supreme and complete condition—the condition of all other things—must possess only a conditioned existence; but as little can we assert the contrary, for this supposed being does not possess the only characteristic which can enable reason to cognize by means of an à priori conception the unconditioned and necessary nature of its existence.\n\nThe conception of an ens realissimum is that which best agrees with the conception of an unconditioned and necessary being. The former conception does not satisfy all the requirements of the latter; but we have no choice, we are obliged to adhere to it, for we find that we cannot do without the existence of a necessary being; and even although we admit it, we find it out of our power to discover in the whole sphere of possibility any being that can advance well-grounded claims to such a distinction.\n\nThe following is, therefore, the natural course of human reason. It begins by persuading itself of the existence of some necessary being. In this being it recognizes the characteristics of unconditioned existence. It then seeks the conception of that which is independent of all conditions, and finds it in that which is itself the sufficient condition of all other things—in other words, in that which contains all reality. But the unlimited all is an absolute unity, and is conceived by the mind as a being one and supreme; and thus reason concludes that the Supreme Being, as the primal basis of all things, possesses an existence which is absolutely necessary.\n\nThis conception must be regarded as in some degree satisfactory, if we admit the existence of a necessary being, and consider that there exists a necessity for a definite and final answer to these questions. In such a case, we cannot make a better choice, or rather we have no choice at all, but feel ourselves obliged to declare in favour of the absolute unity of complete reality, as the highest source of the possibility of things. But if there exists no motive for coming to a definite conclusion, and we may leave the question unanswered till we have fully weighed both sides—in other words, when we are merely called upon to decide how much we happen to know about the question, and how much we merely flatter ourselves that we know—the above conclusion does not appear to be so great advantage, but, on the contrary, seems defective in the grounds upon which it is supported.\n\nFor, admitting the truth of all that has been said, that, namely, the inference from a given existence (my own, for example) to the existence of an unconditioned and necessary being is valid and unassailable; that, in the second place, we must consider a being which contains all reality, and consequently all the conditions of other things, to be absolutely unconditioned; and admitting too, that we have thus discovered the conception of a thing to which may be attributed, without inconsistency, absolute necessity—it does not follow from all this that the conception of a limited being, in which the supreme reality does not reside, is therefore incompatible with the idea of absolute necessity. For, although I do not discover the element of the unconditioned in the conception of such a being—an element which is manifestly existent in the sum-total of all conditions—I am not entitled to conclude that its existence is therefore conditioned; just as I am not entitled to affirm, in a hypothetical syllogism, that where a certain condition does not exist (in the present, completeness, as far as pure conceptions are concerned), the conditioned does not exist either. On the contrary, we are free to consider all limited beings as likewise unconditionally necessary, although we are unable to infer this from the general conception which we have of them. Thus conducted, this argument is incapable of giving us the least notion of the properties of a necessary being, and must be in every respect without result.\n\nThis argument continues, however, to possess a weight and an authority, which, in spite of its objective insufficiency, it has never been divested of. For, granting that certain responsibilities lie upon us, which, as based on the ideas of reason, deserve to be respected and submitted to, although they are incapable of a real or practical application to our nature, or, in other words, would be responsibilities without motives, except upon the supposition of a Supreme Being to give effect and influence to the practical laws: in such a case we should be bound to obey our conceptions, which, although objectively insufficient, do, according to the standard of reason, preponderate over and are superior to any claims that may be advanced from any other quarter. The equilibrium of doubt would in this case be destroyed by a practical addition; indeed, Reason would be compelled to condemn herself, if she refused to comply with the demands of the judgement, no superior to which we know—however defective her understanding of the grounds of these demands might be.\n\nThis argument, although in fact transcendental, inasmuch as it rests upon the intrinsic insufficiency of the contingent, is so simple and natural, that the commonest understanding can appreciate its value. We see things around us change, arise, and pass away; they, or their condition, must therefore have a cause. The same demand must again be made of the cause itself—as a datum of experience. Now it is natural that we should place the highest causality just where we place supreme causality, in that being, which contains the conditions of all possible effects, and the conception of which is so simple as that of an all-embracing reality. This highest cause, then, we regard as absolutely necessary, because we find it absolutely necessary to rise to it, and do not discover any reason for proceeding beyond it. Thus, among all nations, through the darkest polytheism glimmer some faint sparks of monotheism, to which these idolaters have been led, not from reflection and profound thought, but by the study and natural progress of the common understanding.\n\nThere are only three modes of proving the existence of a Deity, on the grounds of speculative reason.\n\nAll the paths conducting to this end begin either from determinate experience and the peculiar constitution of the world of sense, and rise, according to the laws of causality, from it to the highest cause existing apart from the world—or from a purely indeterminate experience, that is, some empirical existence—or abstraction is made of all experience, and the existence of a supreme cause is concluded from à priori conceptions alone. The first is the physico-theological argument, the second the cosmological, the third the ontological. More there are not, and more there cannot be.\n\nI shall show it is as unsuccessful on the one path—the empirical—as on the other—the transcendental, and that it stretches its wings in vain, to soar beyond the world of sense by the mere might of speculative thought. As regards the order in which we must discuss those arguments, it will be exactly the reverse of that in which reason, in the progress of its development, attains to them—the order in which they are placed above. For it will be made manifest to the reader that, although experience presents the occasion and the starting-point, it is the transcendental idea of reason which guides it in its pilgrimage and is the goal of all its struggles. I shall therefore begin with an examination of the transcendental argument, and afterwards inquire what additional strength has accrued to this mode of proof from the addition of the empirical element.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","text":"### Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being\n\nNotwithstanding the pressing necessity which reason feels, to form some presupposition that shall serve the understanding as a proper basis for the complete determination of its conceptions, the idealistic and factitious nature of such a presupposition is too evident to allow reason for a moment to persuade itself into a belief of the objective existence of a mere creation of its own thought. But there are other considerations which compel reason to seek out some resting place in the regress from the conditioned to the unconditioned, which is not given as an actual existence from the mere conception of it, although it alone can give completeness to the series of conditions. And this is the natural course of every human reason, even of the most uneducated, although the path at first entered it does not always continue to follow. It does not begin from conceptions, but from common experience, and requires a basis in actual existence. But this basis is insecure, unless it rests upon the immovable rock of the absolutely necessary. And this foundation is itself unworthy of trust, if it leave under and above it empty space, if it do not fill all, and leave no room for a why or a wherefore, if it be not, in one word, infinite in its reality.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"76.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","text":"If we admit the existence of some one thing, whatever it may be, we must also admit that there is something which exists necessarily. For what is contingent exists only under the condition of some other thing, which is its cause; and from this we must go on to conclude the existence of a cause which is not contingent, and which consequently exists necessarily and unconditionally. Such is the argument by which reason justifies its advances towards a primal being.\n\nNow reason looks round for the conception of a being that may be admitted, without inconsistency, to be worthy of the attribute of absolute necessity, not for the purpose of inferring à priori, from the conception of such a being, its objective existence (for if reason allowed itself to take this course, it would not require a basis in given and actual existence, but merely the support of pure conceptions), but for the purpose of discovering, among all our conceptions of possible things, that conception which possesses no element inconsistent with the idea of absolute necessity. For that there must be some absolutely necessary existence, it regards as a truth already established. Now, if it can remove every existence incapable of supporting the attribute of absolute necessity, excepting one—this must be the absolutely necessary being, whether its necessity is comprehensible by us, that is, deducible from the conception of it alone, or not.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"76.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","text":"Now that, the conception of which contains a therefore to every wherefore, which is not defective in any respect whatever, which is all-sufficient as a condition, seems to be the being of which we can justly predicate absolute necessity—for this reason, that, possessing the conditions of all that is possible, it does not and cannot itself require any condition. And thus it satisfies, in one respect at least, the requirements of the conception of absolute necessity. In this view, it is superior to all other conceptions, which, as deficient and incomplete, do not possess the characteristic of independence of all higher conditions. It is true that we cannot infer from this that what does not contain in itself the supreme and complete condition—the condition of all other things—must possess only a conditioned existence; but as little can we assert the contrary, for this supposed being does not possess the only characteristic which can enable reason to cognize by means of an à priori conception the unconditioned and necessary nature of its existence.\n\nThe conception of an ens realissimum is that which best agrees with the conception of an unconditioned and necessary being. The former conception does not satisfy all the requirements of the latter; but we have no choice, we are obliged to adhere to it, for we find that we cannot do without the existence of a necessary being; and even although we admit it, we find it out of our power to discover in the whole sphere of possibility any being that can advance well-grounded claims to such a distinction.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"76.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0004","text":"The following is, therefore, the natural course of human reason. It begins by persuading itself of the existence of some necessary being. In this being it recognizes the characteristics of unconditioned existence. It then seeks the conception of that which is independent of all conditions, and finds it in that which is itself the sufficient condition of all other things—in other words, in that which contains all reality. But the unlimited all is an absolute unity, and is conceived by the mind as a being one and supreme; and thus reason concludes that the Supreme Being, as the primal basis of all things, possesses an existence which is absolutely necessary.\n\nThis conception must be regarded as in some degree satisfactory, if we admit the existence of a necessary being, and consider that there exists a necessity for a definite and final answer to these questions. In such a case, we cannot make a better choice, or rather we have no choice at all, but feel ourselves obliged to declare in favour of the absolute unity of complete reality, as the highest source of the possibility of things. But if there exists no motive for coming to a definite conclusion, and we may leave the question unanswered till we have fully weighed both sides—in other words, when we are merely called upon to decide how much we happen to know about the question, and how much we merely flatter ourselves that we know—the above conclusion does not appear to be so great advantage, but, on the contrary, seems defective in the grounds upon which it is supported.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"76.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","text":"For, admitting the truth of all that has been said, that, namely, the inference from a given existence (my own, for example) to the existence of an unconditioned and necessary being is valid and unassailable; that, in the second place, we must consider a being which contains all reality, and consequently all the conditions of other things, to be absolutely unconditioned; and admitting too, that we have thus discovered the conception of a thing to which may be attributed, without inconsistency, absolute necessity—it does not follow from all this that the conception of a limited being, in which the supreme reality does not reside, is therefore incompatible with the idea of absolute necessity. For, although I do not discover the element of the unconditioned in the conception of such a being—an element which is manifestly existent in the sum-total of all conditions—I am not entitled to conclude that its existence is therefore conditioned; just as I am not entitled to affirm, in a hypothetical syllogism, that where a certain condition does not exist (in the present, completeness, as far as pure conceptions are concerned), the conditioned does not exist either. On the contrary, we are free to consider all limited beings as likewise unconditionally necessary, although we are unable to infer this from the general conception which we have of them. Thus conducted, this argument is incapable of giving us the least notion of the properties of a necessary being, and must be in every respect without result.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"76.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","text":"This argument continues, however, to possess a weight and an authority, which, in spite of its objective insufficiency, it has never been divested of. For, granting that certain responsibilities lie upon us, which, as based on the ideas of reason, deserve to be respected and submitted to, although they are incapable of a real or practical application to our nature, or, in other words, would be responsibilities without motives, except upon the supposition of a Supreme Being to give effect and influence to the practical laws: in such a case we should be bound to obey our conceptions, which, although objectively insufficient, do, according to the standard of reason, preponderate over and are superior to any claims that may be advanced from any other quarter. The equilibrium of doubt would in this case be destroyed by a practical addition; indeed, Reason would be compelled to condemn herself, if she refused to comply with the demands of the judgement, no superior to which we know—however defective her understanding of the grounds of these demands might be.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"76.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","text":"This argument, although in fact transcendental, inasmuch as it rests upon the intrinsic insufficiency of the contingent, is so simple and natural, that the commonest understanding can appreciate its value. We see things around us change, arise, and pass away; they, or their condition, must therefore have a cause. The same demand must again be made of the cause itself—as a datum of experience. Now it is natural that we should place the highest causality just where we place supreme causality, in that being, which contains the conditions of all possible effects, and the conception of which is so simple as that of an all-embracing reality. This highest cause, then, we regard as absolutely necessary, because we find it absolutely necessary to rise to it, and do not discover any reason for proceeding beyond it. Thus, among all nations, through the darkest polytheism glimmer some faint sparks of monotheism, to which these idolaters have been led, not from reflection and profound thought, but by the study and natural progress of the common understanding.\n\nThere are only three modes of proving the existence of a Deity, on the grounds of speculative reason.\n\nAll the paths conducting to this end begin either from determinate experience and the peculiar constitution of the world of sense, and rise, according to the laws of causality, from it to the highest cause existing apart from the world—or from a purely indeterminate experience, that is, some empirical existence—or abstraction is made of all experience, and the existence of a supreme cause is concluded from à priori conceptions alone. The first is the physico-theological argument, the second the cosmological, the third the ontological. More there are not, and more there cannot be.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"76.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0008","text":"I shall show it is as unsuccessful on the one path—the empirical—as on the other—the transcendental, and that it stretches its wings in vain, to soar beyond the world of sense by the mere might of speculative thought. As regards the order in which we must discuss those arguments, it will be exactly the reverse of that in which reason, in the progress of its development, attains to them—the order in which they are placed above. For it will be made manifest to the reader that, although experience presents the occasion and the starting-point, it is the transcendental idea of reason which guides it in its pilgrimage and is the goal of all its struggles. I shall therefore begin with an examination of the transcendental argument, and afterwards inquire what additional strength has accrued to this mode of proof from the addition of the empirical element.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"76.8"}],"sha256":"ce44483cdcc6f9d05da89c4346bcba1617f915b523517b8702558c6673c03616","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO","title":"Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God","order":77,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIIO","text":"### Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God\n\nIt is evident from what has been said that the conception of an absolutely necessary being is a mere idea, the objective reality of which is far from being established by the mere fact that it is a need of reason. On the contrary, this idea serves merely to indicate a certain unattainable perfection, and rather limits the operations than, by the presentation of new objects, extends the sphere of the understanding. But a strange anomaly meets us at the very threshold; for the inference from a given existence in general to an absolutely necessary existence seems to be correct and unavoidable, while the conditions of the understanding refuse to aid us in forming any conception of such a being.\n\nPhilosophers have always talked of an absolutely necessary being, and have nevertheless declined to take the trouble of conceiving whether—and how—a being of this nature is even cogitable, not to mention that its existence is actually demonstrable. A verbal definition of the conception is certainly easy enough: it is something the non-existence of which is impossible. But does this definition throw any light upon the conditions which render it impossible to cogitate the non-existence of a thing—conditions which we wish to ascertain, that we may discover whether we think anything in the conception of such a being or not? For the mere fact that I throw away, by means of the word unconditioned, all the conditions which the understanding habitually requires in order to regard anything as necessary, is very far from making clear whether by means of the conception of the unconditionally necessary I think of something, or really of nothing at all.\n\nNay, more, this chance-conception, now become so current, many have endeavoured to explain by examples which seemed to render any inquiries regarding its intelligibility quite needless. Every geometrical proposition—a triangle has three angles—it was said, is absolutely necessary; and thus people talked of an object which lay out of the sphere of our understanding as if it were perfectly plain what the conception of such a being meant.\n\nAll the examples adduced have been drawn, without exception, from judgements, and not from things. But the unconditioned necessity of a judgement does not form the absolute necessity of a thing. On the contrary, the absolute necessity of a judgement is only a conditioned necessity of a thing, or of the predicate in a judgement. The proposition above-mentioned does not enounce that three angles necessarily exist, but, upon condition that a triangle exists, three angles must necessarily exist—in it. And thus this logical necessity has been the source of the greatest delusions. Having formed an à priori conception of a thing, the content of which was made to embrace existence, we believed ourselves safe in concluding that, because existence belongs necessarily to the object of the conception (that is, under the condition of my positing this thing as given), the existence of the thing is also posited necessarily, and that it is therefore absolutely necessary—merely because its existence has been cogitated in the conception.\n\nIf, in an identical judgement, I annihilate the predicate in thought, and retain the subject, a contradiction is the result; and hence I say, the former belongs necessarily to the latter. But if I suppress both subject and predicate in thought, no contradiction arises; for there is nothing at all, and therefore no means of forming a contradiction. To suppose the existence of a triangle and not that of its three angles, is self-contradictory; but to suppose the non-existence of both triangle and angles is perfectly admissible. And so is it with the conception of an absolutely necessary being. Annihilate its existence in thought, and you annihilate the thing itself with all its predicates; how then can there be any room for contradiction? Externally, there is nothing to give rise to a contradiction, for a thing cannot be necessary externally; nor internally, for, by the annihilation or suppression of the thing itself, its internal properties are also annihilated. God is omnipotent—that is a necessary judgement. His omnipotence cannot be denied, if the existence of a Deity is posited—the existence, that is, of an infinite being, the two conceptions being identical. But when you say, God does not exist, neither omnipotence nor any other predicate is affirmed; they must all disappear with the subject, and in this judgement there cannot exist the least self-contradiction.\n\nYou have thus seen that when the predicate of a judgement is annihilated in thought along with the subject, no internal contradiction can arise, be the predicate what it may. There is no possibility of evading the conclusion—you find yourselves compelled to declare: There are certain subjects which cannot be annihilated in thought. But this is nothing more than saying: There exist subjects which are absolutely necessary—the very hypothesis which you are called upon to establish. For I find myself unable to form the slightest conception of a thing which when annihilated in thought with all its predicates, leaves behind a contradiction; and contradiction is the only criterion of impossibility in the sphere of pure à priori conceptions.\n\nAgainst these general considerations, the justice of which no one can dispute, one argument is adduced, which is regarded as furnishing a satisfactory demonstration from the fact. It is affirmed that there is one and only one conception, in which the non-being or annihilation of the object is self-contradictory, and this is the conception of an ens realissimum. It possesses, you say, all reality, and you feel yourselves justified in admitting the possibility of such a being. (This I am willing to grant for the present, although the existence of a conception which is not self-contradictory is far from being sufficient to prove the possibility of an object.)[[67]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-67) Now the notion of all reality embraces in it that of existence; the notion of existence lies, therefore, in the conception of this possible thing. If this thing is annihilated in thought, the internal possibility of the thing is also annihilated, which is self-contradictory.\n\n[[67]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-67) A conception is always possible, if it is not self-contradictory. This is the logical criterion of possibility, distinguishing the object of such a conception from the nihil negativum. But it may be, notwithstanding, an empty conception, unless the objective reality of this synthesis, but which it is generated, is demonstrated; and a proof of this kind must be based upon principles of possible experience, and not upon the principle of analysis or contradiction. This remark may be serviceable as a warning against concluding, from the possibility of a conception—which is logical—the possibility of a thing—which is real.\n\nI answer: It is absurd to introduce—under whatever term disguised—into the conception of a thing, which is to be cogitated solely in reference to its possibility, the conception of its existence. If this is admitted, you will have apparently gained the day, but in reality have enounced nothing but a mere tautology. I ask, is the proposition, this or that thing (which I am admitting to be possible) exists, an analytical or a synthetical proposition? If the former, there is no addition made to the subject of your thought by the affirmation of its existence; but then the conception in your minds is identical with the thing itself, or you have supposed the existence of a thing to be possible, and then inferred its existence from its internal possibility—which is but a miserable tautology. The word reality in the conception of the thing, and the word existence in the conception of the predicate, will not help you out of the difficulty. For, supposing you were to term all positing of a thing reality, you have thereby posited the thing with all its predicates in the conception of the subject and assumed its actual existence, and this you merely repeat in the predicate. But if you confess, as every reasonable person must, that every existential proposition is synthetical, how can it be maintained that the predicate of existence cannot be denied without contradiction?—a property which is the characteristic of analytical propositions, alone.\n\nI should have a reasonable hope of putting an end for ever to this sophistical mode of argumentation, by a strict definition of the conception of existence, did not my own experience teach me that the illusion arising from our confounding a logical with a real predicate (a predicate which aids in the determination of a thing) resists almost all the endeavours of explanation and illustration. A logical predicate may be what you please, even the subject may be predicated of itself; for logic pays no regard to the content of a judgement. But the determination of a conception is a predicate, which adds to and enlarges the conception. It must not, therefore, be contained in the conception.\n\nBeing is evidently not a real predicate, that is, a conception of something which is added to the conception of some other thing. It is merely the positing of a thing, or of certain determinations in it. Logically, it is merely the copula of a judgement. The proposition, God is omnipotent, contains two conceptions, which have a certain object or content; the word is, is no additional predicate—it merely indicates the relation of the predicate to the subject. Now, if I take the subject (God) with all its predicates (omnipotence being one), and say: God is, or, There is a God, I add no new predicate to the conception of God, I merely posit or affirm the existence of the subject with all its predicates—I posit the object in relation to my conception. The content of both is the same; and there is no addition made to the conception, which expresses merely the possibility of the object, by my cogitating the object—in the expression, it is—as absolutely given or existing. Thus the real contains no more than the possible. A hundred real dollars contain no more than a hundred possible dollars. For, as the latter indicate the conception, and the former the object, on the supposition that the content of the former was greater than that of the latter, my conception would not be an expression of the whole object, and would consequently be an inadequate conception of it. But in reckoning my wealth there may be said to be more in a hundred real dollars than in a hundred possible dollars—that is, in the mere conception of them. For the real object—the dollars—is not analytically contained in my conception, but forms a synthetical addition to my conception (which is merely a determination of my mental state), although this objective reality—this existence—apart from my conceptions, does not in the least degree increase the aforesaid hundred dollars.\n\nBy whatever and by whatever number of predicates—even to the complete determination of it—I may cogitate a thing, I do not in the least augment the object of my conception by the addition of the statement: This thing exists. Otherwise, not exactly the same, but something more than what was cogitated in my conception, would exist, and I could not affirm that the exact object of my conception had real existence. If I cogitate a thing as containing all modes of reality except one, the mode of reality which is absent is not added to the conception of the thing by the affirmation that the thing exists; on the contrary, the thing exists—if it exist at all—with the same defect as that cogitated in its conception; otherwise not that which was cogitated, but something different, exists. Now, if I cogitate a being as the highest reality, without defect or imperfection, the question still remains—whether this being exists or not? For, although no element is wanting in the possible real content of my conception, there is a defect in its relation to my mental state, that is, I am ignorant whether the cognition of the object indicated by the conception is possible à posteriori. And here the cause of the present difficulty becomes apparent. If the question regarded an object of sense merely, it would be impossible for me to confound the conception with the existence of a thing. For the conception merely enables me to cogitate an object as according with the general conditions of experience; while the existence of the object permits me to cogitate it as contained in the sphere of actual experience. At the same time, this connection with the world of experience does not in the least augment the conception, although a possible perception has been added to the experience of the mind. But if we cogitate existence by the pure category alone, it is not to be wondered at, that we should find ourselves unable to present any criterion sufficient to distinguish it from mere possibility.\n\nWhatever be the content of our conception of an object, it is necessary to go beyond it, if we wish to predicate existence of the object. In the case of sensuous objects, this is attained by their connection according to empirical laws with some one of my perceptions; but there is no means of cognizing the existence of objects of pure thought, because it must be cognized completely à priori. But all our knowledge of existence (be it immediately by perception, or by inferences connecting some object with a perception) belongs entirely to the sphere of experience—which is in perfect unity with itself; and although an existence out of this sphere cannot be absolutely declared to be impossible, it is a hypothesis the truth of which we have no means of ascertaining.\n\nThe notion of a Supreme Being is in many respects a highly useful idea; but for the very reason that it is an idea, it is incapable of enlarging our cognition with regard to the existence of things. It is not even sufficient to instruct us as to the possibility of a being which we do not know to exist. The analytical criterion of possibility, which consists in the absence of contradiction in propositions, cannot be denied it. But the connection of real properties in a thing is a synthesis of the possibility of which an à priori judgement cannot be formed, because these realities are not presented to us specifically; and even if this were to happen, a judgement would still be impossible, because the criterion of the possibility of synthetical cognitions must be sought for in the world of experience, to which the object of an idea cannot belong. And thus the celebrated Leibnitz has utterly failed in his attempt to establish upon à priori grounds the possibility of this sublime ideal being.\n\nThe celebrated ontological or Cartesian argument for the existence of a Supreme Being is therefore insufficient; and we may as well hope to increase our stock of knowledge by the aid of mere ideas, as the merchant to augment his wealth by the addition of noughts to his cash account.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","text":"### Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God\n\nIt is evident from what has been said that the conception of an absolutely necessary being is a mere idea, the objective reality of which is far from being established by the mere fact that it is a need of reason. On the contrary, this idea serves merely to indicate a certain unattainable perfection, and rather limits the operations than, by the presentation of new objects, extends the sphere of the understanding. But a strange anomaly meets us at the very threshold; for the inference from a given existence in general to an absolutely necessary existence seems to be correct and unavoidable, while the conditions of the understanding refuse to aid us in forming any conception of such a being.\n\nPhilosophers have always talked of an absolutely necessary being, and have nevertheless declined to take the trouble of conceiving whether—and how—a being of this nature is even cogitable, not to mention that its existence is actually demonstrable. A verbal definition of the conception is certainly easy enough: it is something the non-existence of which is impossible. But does this definition throw any light upon the conditions which render it impossible to cogitate the non-existence of a thing—conditions which we wish to ascertain, that we may discover whether we think anything in the conception of such a being or not? For the mere fact that I throw away, by means of the word unconditioned, all the conditions which the understanding habitually requires in order to regard anything as necessary, is very far from making clear whether by means of the conception of the unconditionally necessary I think of something, or really of nothing at all.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"77.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","text":"Nay, more, this chance-conception, now become so current, many have endeavoured to explain by examples which seemed to render any inquiries regarding its intelligibility quite needless. Every geometrical proposition—a triangle has three angles—it was said, is absolutely necessary; and thus people talked of an object which lay out of the sphere of our understanding as if it were perfectly plain what the conception of such a being meant.\n\nAll the examples adduced have been drawn, without exception, from judgements, and not from things. But the unconditioned necessity of a judgement does not form the absolute necessity of a thing. On the contrary, the absolute necessity of a judgement is only a conditioned necessity of a thing, or of the predicate in a judgement. The proposition above-mentioned does not enounce that three angles necessarily exist, but, upon condition that a triangle exists, three angles must necessarily exist—in it. And thus this logical necessity has been the source of the greatest delusions. Having formed an à priori conception of a thing, the content of which was made to embrace existence, we believed ourselves safe in concluding that, because existence belongs necessarily to the object of the conception (that is, under the condition of my positing this thing as given), the existence of the thing is also posited necessarily, and that it is therefore absolutely necessary—merely because its existence has been cogitated in the conception.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"77.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0003","text":"If, in an identical judgement, I annihilate the predicate in thought, and retain the subject, a contradiction is the result; and hence I say, the former belongs necessarily to the latter. But if I suppress both subject and predicate in thought, no contradiction arises; for there is nothing at all, and therefore no means of forming a contradiction. To suppose the existence of a triangle and not that of its three angles, is self-contradictory; but to suppose the non-existence of both triangle and angles is perfectly admissible. And so is it with the conception of an absolutely necessary being. Annihilate its existence in thought, and you annihilate the thing itself with all its predicates; how then can there be any room for contradiction? Externally, there is nothing to give rise to a contradiction, for a thing cannot be necessary externally; nor internally, for, by the annihilation or suppression of the thing itself, its internal properties are also annihilated. God is omnipotent—that is a necessary judgement. His omnipotence cannot be denied, if the existence of a Deity is posited—the existence, that is, of an infinite being, the two conceptions being identical. But when you say, God does not exist, neither omnipotence nor any other predicate is affirmed; they must all disappear with the subject, and in this judgement there cannot exist the least self-contradiction.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"77.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0004","text":"You have thus seen that when the predicate of a judgement is annihilated in thought along with the subject, no internal contradiction can arise, be the predicate what it may. There is no possibility of evading the conclusion—you find yourselves compelled to declare: There are certain subjects which cannot be annihilated in thought. But this is nothing more than saying: There exist subjects which are absolutely necessary—the very hypothesis which you are called upon to establish. For I find myself unable to form the slightest conception of a thing which when annihilated in thought with all its predicates, leaves behind a contradiction; and contradiction is the only criterion of impossibility in the sphere of pure à priori conceptions.\n\nAgainst these general considerations, the justice of which no one can dispute, one argument is adduced, which is regarded as furnishing a satisfactory demonstration from the fact. It is affirmed that there is one and only one conception, in which the non-being or annihilation of the object is self-contradictory, and this is the conception of an ens realissimum. It possesses, you say, all reality, and you feel yourselves justified in admitting the possibility of such a being. (This I am willing to grant for the present, although the existence of a conception which is not self-contradictory is far from being sufficient to prove the possibility of an object.)[[67]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-67) Now the notion of all reality embraces in it that of existence; the notion of existence lies, therefore, in the conception of this possible thing. If this thing is annihilated in thought, the internal possibility of the thing is also annihilated, which is self-contradictory.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"77.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0005","text":"[[67]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-67) A conception is always possible, if it is not self-contradictory. This is the logical criterion of possibility, distinguishing the object of such a conception from the nihil negativum. But it may be, notwithstanding, an empty conception, unless the objective reality of this synthesis, but which it is generated, is demonstrated; and a proof of this kind must be based upon principles of possible experience, and not upon the principle of analysis or contradiction. This remark may be serviceable as a warning against concluding, from the possibility of a conception—which is logical—the possibility of a thing—which is real.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"77.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","text":"I answer: It is absurd to introduce—under whatever term disguised—into the conception of a thing, which is to be cogitated solely in reference to its possibility, the conception of its existence. If this is admitted, you will have apparently gained the day, but in reality have enounced nothing but a mere tautology. I ask, is the proposition, this or that thing (which I am admitting to be possible) exists, an analytical or a synthetical proposition? If the former, there is no addition made to the subject of your thought by the affirmation of its existence; but then the conception in your minds is identical with the thing itself, or you have supposed the existence of a thing to be possible, and then inferred its existence from its internal possibility—which is but a miserable tautology. The word reality in the conception of the thing, and the word existence in the conception of the predicate, will not help you out of the difficulty. For, supposing you were to term all positing of a thing reality, you have thereby posited the thing with all its predicates in the conception of the subject and assumed its actual existence, and this you merely repeat in the predicate. But if you confess, as every reasonable person must, that every existential proposition is synthetical, how can it be maintained that the predicate of existence cannot be denied without contradiction?—a property which is the characteristic of analytical propositions, alone.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"77.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0007","text":"I should have a reasonable hope of putting an end for ever to this sophistical mode of argumentation, by a strict definition of the conception of existence, did not my own experience teach me that the illusion arising from our confounding a logical with a real predicate (a predicate which aids in the determination of a thing) resists almost all the endeavours of explanation and illustration. A logical predicate may be what you please, even the subject may be predicated of itself; for logic pays no regard to the content of a judgement. But the determination of a conception is a predicate, which adds to and enlarges the conception. It must not, therefore, be contained in the conception.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"77.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","text":"Being is evidently not a real predicate, that is, a conception of something which is added to the conception of some other thing. It is merely the positing of a thing, or of certain determinations in it. Logically, it is merely the copula of a judgement. The proposition, God is omnipotent, contains two conceptions, which have a certain object or content; the word is, is no additional predicate—it merely indicates the relation of the predicate to the subject. Now, if I take the subject (God) with all its predicates (omnipotence being one), and say: God is, or, There is a God, I add no new predicate to the conception of God, I merely posit or affirm the existence of the subject with all its predicates—I posit the object in relation to my conception. The content of both is the same; and there is no addition made to the conception, which expresses merely the possibility of the object, by my cogitating the object—in the expression, it is—as absolutely given or existing. Thus the real contains no more than the possible. A hundred real dollars contain no more than a hundred possible dollars. For, as the latter indicate the conception, and the former the object, on the supposition that the content of the former was greater than that of the latter, my conception would not be an expression of the whole object, and would consequently be an inadequate conception of it. But in reckoning my wealth there may be said to be more in a hundred real dollars than in a hundred possible dollars—that is, in the mere conception of them. For the real object—the dollars—is not analytically contained in my conception, but forms a synthetical addition to my conception (which is merely a determination of my mental state), although this objective reality—this existence—apart from my conceptions, does not in the least degree increase the aforesaid hundred dollars.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"77.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","text":"By whatever and by whatever number of predicates—even to the complete determination of it—I may cogitate a thing, I do not in the least augment the object of my conception by the addition of the statement: This thing exists. Otherwise, not exactly the same, but something more than what was cogitated in my conception, would exist, and I could not affirm that the exact object of my conception had real existence. If I cogitate a thing as containing all modes of reality except one, the mode of reality which is absent is not added to the conception of the thing by the affirmation that the thing exists; on the contrary, the thing exists—if it exist at all—with the same defect as that cogitated in its conception; otherwise not that which was cogitated, but something different, exists. Now, if I cogitate a being as the highest reality, without defect or imperfection, the question still remains—whether this being exists or not? For, although no element is wanting in the possible real content of my conception, there is a defect in its relation to my mental state, that is, I am ignorant whether the cognition of the object indicated by the conception is possible à posteriori. And here the cause of the present difficulty becomes apparent. If the question regarded an object of sense merely, it would be impossible for me to confound the conception with the existence of a thing. For the conception merely enables me to cogitate an object as according with the general conditions of experience; while the existence of the object permits me to cogitate it as contained in the sphere of actual experience. At the same time, this connection with the world of experience does not in the least augment the conception, although a possible perception has been added to the experience of the mind. But if we cogitate existence by the pure category alone, it is not to be wondered at, that we should find ourselves unable to present any criterion sufficient to distinguish it from mere possibility.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"77.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","text":"Whatever be the content of our conception of an object, it is necessary to go beyond it, if we wish to predicate existence of the object. In the case of sensuous objects, this is attained by their connection according to empirical laws with some one of my perceptions; but there is no means of cognizing the existence of objects of pure thought, because it must be cognized completely à priori. But all our knowledge of existence (be it immediately by perception, or by inferences connecting some object with a perception) belongs entirely to the sphere of experience—which is in perfect unity with itself; and although an existence out of this sphere cannot be absolutely declared to be impossible, it is a hypothesis the truth of which we have no means of ascertaining.\n\nThe notion of a Supreme Being is in many respects a highly useful idea; but for the very reason that it is an idea, it is incapable of enlarging our cognition with regard to the existence of things. It is not even sufficient to instruct us as to the possibility of a being which we do not know to exist. The analytical criterion of possibility, which consists in the absence of contradiction in propositions, cannot be denied it. But the connection of real properties in a thing is a synthesis of the possibility of which an à priori judgement cannot be formed, because these realities are not presented to us specifically; and even if this were to happen, a judgement would still be impossible, because the criterion of the possibility of synthetical cognitions must be sought for in the world of experience, to which the object of an idea cannot belong. And thus the celebrated Leibnitz has utterly failed in his attempt to establish upon à priori grounds the possibility of this sublime ideal being.\n\nThe celebrated ontological or Cartesian argument for the existence of a Supreme Being is therefore insufficient; and we may as well hope to increase our stock of knowledge by the aid of mere ideas, as the merchant to augment his wealth by the addition of noughts to his cash account.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"77.10"}],"sha256":"d7f878c571d10b45448007f4587b78e37a095cf1488c4c2b566464499382480d","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC","title":"Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God","order":78,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SVIC","text":"# Theology\n\n### Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God\n\nIt was by no means a natural course of proceeding, but, on the contrary, an invention entirely due to the subtlety of the schools, to attempt to draw from a mere idea a proof of the existence of an object corresponding to it. Such a course would never have been pursued, were it not for that need of reason which requires it to suppose the existence of a necessary being as a basis for the empirical regress, and that, as this necessity must be unconditioned and à priori, reason is bound to discover a conception which shall satisfy, if possible, this requirement, and enable us to attain to the à priori cognition of such a being. This conception was thought to be found in the idea of an ens realissimum, and thus this idea was employed for the attainment of a better defined knowledge of a necessary being, of the existence of which we were convinced, or persuaded, on other grounds. Thus reason was seduced from her natural courage; and, instead of concluding with the conception of an ens realissimum, an attempt was made to begin with it, for the purpose of inferring from it that idea of a necessary existence which it was in fact called in to complete. Thus arose that unfortunate ontological argument, which neither satisfies the healthy common sense of humanity, nor sustains the scientific examination of the philosopher.\n\nThe cosmological proof, which we are about to examine, retains the connection between absolute necessity and the highest reality; but, instead of reasoning from this highest reality to a necessary existence, like the preceding argument, it concludes from the given unconditioned necessity of some being its unlimited reality. The track it pursues, whether rational or sophistical, is at least natural, and not only goes far to persuade the common understanding, but shows itself deserving of respect from the speculative intellect; while it contains, at the same time, the outlines of all the arguments employed in natural theology—arguments which always have been, and still will be, in use and authority. These, however adorned, and hid under whatever embellishments of rhetoric and sentiment, are at bottom identical with the arguments we are at present to discuss. This proof, termed by Leibnitz the argumentum a contingentia mundi, I shall now lay before the reader, and subject to a strict examination.\n\nIt is framed in the following manner: If something exists, an absolutely necessary being must likewise exist. Now I, at least, exist. Consequently, there exists an absolutely necessary being. The minor contains an experience, the major reasons from a general experience to the existence of a necessary being.[[68]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-68) Thus this argument really begins at experience, and is not completely à priori, or ontological. The object of all possible experience being the world, it is called the cosmological proof. It contains no reference to any peculiar property of sensuous objects, by which this world of sense might be distinguished from other possible worlds; and in this respect it differs from the physico-theological proof, which is based upon the consideration of the peculiar constitution of our sensuous world.\n\n[[68]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-68) This inference is too well known to require more detailed discussion. It is based upon the spurious transcendental law of causality, that everything which is contingent has a cause, which, if itself contingent, must also have a cause; and so on, till the series of subordinated causes must end with an absolutely necessary cause, without which it would not possess completeness.\n\nThe proof proceeds thus: A necessary being can be determined only in one way, that is, it can be determined by only one of all possible opposed predicates; consequently, it must be completely determined in and by its conception. But there is only a single conception of a thing possible, which completely determines the thing à priori: that is, the conception of the ens realissimum. It follows that the conception of the ens realissimum is the only conception by and in which we can cogitate a necessary being. Consequently, a Supreme Being necessarily exists.\n\nIn this cosmological argument are assembled so many sophistical propositions that speculative reason seems to have exerted in it all her dialectical skill to produce a transcendental illusion of the most extreme character. We shall postpone an investigation of this argument for the present, and confine ourselves to exposing the stratagem by which it imposes upon us an old argument in a new dress, and appeals to the agreement of two witnesses, the one with the credentials of pure reason, and the other with those of empiricism; while, in fact, it is only the former who has changed his dress and voice, for the purpose of passing himself off for an additional witness. That it may possess a secure foundation, it bases its conclusions upon experience, and thus appears to be completely distinct from the ontological argument, which places its confidence entirely in pure à priori conceptions. But this experience merely aids reason in making one step—to the existence of a necessary being. What the properties of this being are cannot be learned from experience; and therefore reason abandons it altogether, and pursues its inquiries in the sphere of pure conception, for the purpose of discovering what the properties of an absolutely necessary being ought to be, that is, what among all possible things contain the conditions (requisita) of absolute necessity. Reason believes that it has discovered these requisites in the conception of an ens realissimum—and in it alone, and hence concludes: The ens realissimum is an absolutely necessary being. But it is evident that reason has here presupposed that the conception of an ens realissimum is perfectly adequate to the conception of a being of absolute necessity, that is, that we may infer the existence of the latter from that of the former—a proposition which formed the basis of the ontological argument, and which is now employed in the support of the cosmological argument, contrary to the wish and professions of its inventors. For the existence of an absolutely necessary being is given in conceptions alone. But if I say: “The conception of the ens realissimum is a conception of this kind, and in fact the only conception which is adequate to our idea of a necessary being,” I am obliged to admit, that the latter may be inferred from the former. Thus it is properly the ontological argument which figures in the cosmological, and constitutes the whole strength of the latter; while the spurious basis of experience has been of no further use than to conduct us to the conception of absolute necessity, being utterly insufficient to demonstrate the presence of this attribute in any determinate existence or thing. For when we propose to ourselves an aim of this character, we must abandon the sphere of experience, and rise to that of pure conceptions, which we examine with the purpose of discovering whether any one contains the conditions of the possibility of an absolutely necessary being. But if the possibility of such a being is thus demonstrated, its existence is also proved; for we may then assert that, of all possible beings there is one which possesses the attribute of necessity—in other words, this being possesses an absolutely necessary existence.\n\nAll illusions in an argument are more easily detected when they are presented in the formal manner employed by the schools, which we now proceed to do.\n\nIf the proposition: “Every absolutely necessary being is likewise an ens realissimum,” is correct (and it is this which constitutes the nervus probandi of the cosmological argument), it must, like all affirmative judgements, be capable of conversion—the conversio per accidens, at least. It follows, then, that some entia realissima are absolutely necessary beings. But no ens realissimum is in any respect different from another, and what is valid of some is valid of all. In this present case, therefore, I may employ simple conversion, and say: “Every ens realissimum is a necessary being.” But as this proposition is determined à priori by the conceptions contained in it, the mere conception of an ens realissimum must possess the additional attribute of absolute necessity. But this is exactly what was maintained in the ontological argument, and not recognized by the cosmological, although it formed the real ground of its disguised and illusory reasoning.\n\nThus the second mode employed by speculative reason of demonstrating the existence of a Supreme Being, is not only, like the first, illusory and inadequate, but possesses the additional blemish of an ignoratio elenchi—professing to conduct us by a new road to the desired goal, but bringing us back, after a short circuit, to the old path which we had deserted at its call.\n\nI mentioned above that this cosmological argument contains a perfect nest of dialectical assumptions, which transcendental criticism does not find it difficult to expose and to dissipate. I shall merely enumerate these, leaving it to the reader, who must by this time be well practised in such matters, to investigate the fallacies residing therein.\n\nThe following fallacies, for example, are discoverable in this mode of proof: 1. The transcendental principle: “Everything that is contingent must have a cause”—a principle without significance, except in the sensuous world. For the purely intellectual conception of the contingent cannot produce any synthetical proposition, like that of causality, which is itself without significance or distinguishing characteristic except in the phenomenal world. But in the present case it is employed to help us beyond the limits of its sphere. 2. “From the impossibility of an infinite ascending series of causes in the world of sense a first cause is inferred”; a conclusion which the principles of the employment of reason do not justify even in the sphere of experience, and still less when an attempt is made to pass the limits of this sphere. 3. Reason allows itself to be satisfied upon insufficient grounds, with regard to the completion of this series. It removes all conditions (without which, however, no conception of Necessity can take place); and, as after this it is beyond our power to form any other conceptions, it accepts this as a completion of the conception it wishes to form of the series. 4. The logical possibility of a conception of the total of reality (the criterion of this possibility being the absence of contradiction) is confounded with the transcendental, which requires a principle of the practicability of such a synthesis—a principle which again refers us to the world of experience. And so on.\n\nThe aim of the cosmological argument is to avoid the necessity of proving the existence of a necessary being priori from mere conceptions—a proof which must be ontological, and of which we feel ourselves quite incapable. With this purpose, we reason from an actual existence—an experience in general, to an absolutely necessary condition of that existence. It is in this case unnecessary to demonstrate its possibility. For after having proved that it exists, the question regarding its possibility is superfluous. Now, when we wish to define more strictly the nature of this necessary being, we do not look out for some being the conception of which would enable us to comprehend the necessity of its being—for if we could do this, an empirical presupposition would be unnecessary; no, we try to discover merely the negative condition (conditio sine qua non), without which a being would not be absolutely necessary. Now this would be perfectly admissible in every sort of reasoning, from a consequence to its principle; but in the present case it unfortunately happens that the condition of absolute necessity can be discovered in but a single being, the conception of which must consequently contain all that is requisite for demonstrating the presence of absolute necessity, and thus entitle me to infer this absolute necessity à priori. That is, it must be possible to reason conversely, and say: The thing, to which the conception of the highest reality belongs, is absolutely necessary. But if I cannot reason thus—and I cannot, unless I believe in the sufficiency of the ontological argument—I find insurmountable obstacles in my new path, and am really no farther than the point from which I set out. The conception of a Supreme Being satisfies all questions à priori regarding the internal determinations of a thing, and is for this reason an ideal without equal or parallel, the general conception of it indicating it as at the same time an ens individuum among all possible things. But the conception does not satisfy the question regarding its existence—which was the purpose of all our inquiries; and, although the existence of a necessary being were admitted, we should find it impossible to answer the question: What of all things in the world must be regarded as such?\n\nIt is certainly allowable to admit the existence of an all-sufficient being—a cause of all possible effects—for the purpose of enabling reason to introduce unity into its mode and grounds of explanation with regard to phenomena. But to assert that such a being necessarily exists, is no longer the modest enunciation of an admissible hypothesis, but the boldest declaration of an apodeictic certainty; for the cognition of that which is absolutely necessary must itself possess that character.\n\nThe aim of the transcendental ideal formed by the mind is either to discover a conception which shall harmonize with the idea of absolute necessity, or a conception which shall contain that idea. If the one is possible, so is the other; for reason recognizes that alone as absolutely necessary which is necessary from its conception. But both attempts are equally beyond our power—we find it impossible to satisfy the understanding upon this point, and as impossible to induce it to remain at rest in relation to this incapacity.\n\nUnconditioned necessity, which, as the ultimate support and stay of all existing things, is an indispensable requirement of the mind, is an abyss on the verge of which human reason trembles in dismay. Even the idea of eternity, terrible and sublime as it is, as depicted by Haller, does not produce upon the mental vision such a feeling of awe and terror; for, although it measures the duration of things, it does not support them. We cannot bear, nor can we rid ourselves of the thought that a being, which we regard as the greatest of all possible existences, should say to himself: I am from eternity to eternity; beside me there is nothing, except that which exists by my will; whence then am I? Here all sinks away from under us; and the greatest, as the smallest, perfection, hovers without stay or footing in presence of the speculative reason, which finds it as easy to part with the one as with the other.\n\nMany physical powers, which evidence their existence by their effects, are perfectly inscrutable in their nature; they elude all our powers of observation. The transcendental object which forms the basis of phenomena, and, in connection with it, the reason why our sensibility possesses this rather than that particular kind of conditions, are and must ever remain hidden from our mental vision; the fact is there, the reason of the fact we cannot see. But an ideal of pure reason cannot be termed mysterious or inscrutable, because the only credential of its reality is the need of it felt by reason, for the purpose of giving completeness to the world of synthetical unity. An ideal is not even given as a cogitable object, and therefore cannot be inscrutable; on the contrary, it must, as a mere idea, be based on the constitution of reason itself, and on this account must be capable of explanation and solution. For the very essence of reason consists in its ability to give an account, of all our conceptions, opinions, and assertions—upon objective, or, when they happen to be illusory and fallacious, upon subjective grounds.\n\nDetection and Explanation of the Dialectical Illusion in all Transcendental Arguments for the Existence of a Necessary Being.\n\nBoth of the above arguments are transcendental; in other words, they do not proceed upon empirical principles. For, although the cosmological argument professed to lay a basis of experience for its edifice of reasoning, it did not ground its procedure upon the peculiar constitution of experience, but upon pure principles of reason—in relation to an existence given by empirical consciousness; utterly abandoning its guidance, however, for the purpose of supporting its assertions entirely upon pure conceptions. Now what is the cause, in these transcendental arguments, of the dialectical, but natural, illusion, which connects the conceptions of necessity and supreme reality, and hypostatizes that which cannot be anything but an idea? What is the cause of this unavoidable step on the part of reason, of admitting that some one among all existing things must be necessary, while it falls back from the assertion of the existence of such a being as from an abyss? And how does reason proceed to explain this anomaly to itself, and from the wavering condition of a timid and reluctant approbation—always again withdrawn—arrive at a calm and settled insight into its cause?\n\nIt is something very remarkable that, on the supposition that something exists, I cannot avoid the inference that something exists necessarily. Upon this perfectly natural—but not on that account reliable—inference does the cosmological argument rest. But, let me form any conception whatever of a thing, I find that I cannot cogitate the existence of the thing as absolutely necessary, and that nothing prevents me—be the thing or being what it may—from cogitating its non-existence. I may thus be obliged to admit that all existing things have a necessary basis, while I cannot cogitate any single or individual thing as necessary. In other words, I can never complete the regress through the conditions of existence, without admitting the existence of a necessary being; but, on the other hand, I cannot make a commencement from this being.\n\nIf I must cogitate something as existing necessarily as the basis of existing things, and yet am not permitted to cogitate any individual thing as in itself necessary, the inevitable inference is that necessity and contingency are not properties of things themselves—otherwise an internal contradiction would result; that consequently neither of these principles are objective, but merely subjective principles of reason—the one requiring us to seek for a necessary ground for everything that exists, that is, to be satisfied with no other explanation than that which is complete à priori, the other forbidding us ever to hope for the attainment of this completeness, that is, to regard no member of the empirical world as unconditioned. In this mode of viewing them, both principles, in their purely heuristic and regulative character, and as concerning merely the formal interest of reason, are quite consistent with each other. The one says: “You must philosophize upon nature,” as if there existed a necessary primal basis of all existing things, solely for the purpose of introducing systematic unity into your knowledge, by pursuing an idea of this character—a foundation which is arbitrarily admitted to be ultimate; while the other warns you to consider no individual determination, concerning the existence of things, as such an ultimate foundation, that is, as absolutely necessary, but to keep the way always open for further progress in the deduction, and to treat every determination as determined by some other. But if all that we perceive must be regarded as conditionally necessary, it is impossible that anything which is empirically given should be absolutely necessary.\n\nIt follows from this that you must accept the absolutely necessary as out of and beyond the world, inasmuch as it is useful only as a principle of the highest possible unity in experience, and you cannot discover any such necessary existence in the would, the second rule requiring you to regard all empirical causes of unity as themselves deduced.\n\nThe philosophers of antiquity regarded all the forms of nature as contingent; while matter was considered by them, in accordance with the judgement of the common reason of mankind, as primal and necessary. But if they had regarded matter, not relatively—as the substratum of phenomena, but absolutely and in itself—as an independent existence, this idea of absolute necessity would have immediately disappeared. For there is nothing absolutely connecting reason with such an existence; on the contrary, it can annihilate it in thought, always and without self-contradiction. But in thought alone lay the idea of absolute necessity. A regulative principle must, therefore, have been at the foundation of this opinion. In fact, extension and impenetrability—which together constitute our conception of matter—form the supreme empirical principle of the unity of phenomena, and this principle, in so far as it is empirically unconditioned, possesses the property of a regulative principle. But, as every determination of matter which constitutes what is real in it—and consequently impenetrability—is an effect, which must have a cause, and is for this reason always derived, the notion of matter cannot harmonize with the idea of a necessary being, in its character of the principle of all derived unity. For every one of its real properties, being derived, must be only conditionally necessary, and can therefore be annihilated in thought; and thus the whole existence of matter can be so annihilated or suppressed. If this were not the case, we should have found in the world of phenomena the highest ground or condition of unity—which is impossible, according to the second regulative principle. It follows that matter, and, in general, all that forms part of the world of sense, cannot be a necessary primal being, nor even a principle of empirical unity, but that this being or principle must have its place assigned without the world. And, in this way, we can proceed in perfect confidence to deduce the phenomena of the world and their existence from other phenomena, just as if there existed no necessary being; and we can at the same time, strive without ceasing towards the attainment of completeness for our deduction, just as if such a being—the supreme condition of all existences—were presupposed by the mind.\n\nThese remarks will have made it evident to the reader that the ideal of the Supreme Being, far from being an enouncement of the existence of a being in itself necessary, is nothing more than a regulative principle of reason, requiring us to regard all connection existing between phenomena as if it had its origin from an all-sufficient necessary cause, and basing upon this the rule of a systematic and necessary unity in the explanation of phenomena. We cannot, at the same time, avoid regarding, by a transcendental subreptio, this formal principle as constitutive, and hypostatizing this unity. Precisely similar is the case with our notion of space. Space is the primal condition of all forms, which are properly just so many different limitations of it; and thus, although it is merely a principle of sensibility, we cannot help regarding it as an absolutely necessary and self-subsistent thing—as an object given à priori in itself. In the same way, it is quite natural that, as the systematic unity of nature cannot be established as a principle for the empirical employment of reason, unless it is based upon the idea of an ens realissimum, as the supreme cause, we should regard this idea as a real object, and this object, in its character of supreme condition, as absolutely necessary, and that in this way a regulative should be transformed into a constitutive principle. This interchange becomes evident when I regard this supreme being, which, relatively to the world, was absolutely (unconditionally) necessary, as a thing per se. In this case, I find it impossible to represent this necessity in or by any conception, and it exists merely in my own mind, as the formal condition of thought, but not as a material and hypostatic condition of existence.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0001","text":"# Theology\n\n### Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God\n\nIt was by no means a natural course of proceeding, but, on the contrary, an invention entirely due to the subtlety of the schools, to attempt to draw from a mere idea a proof of the existence of an object corresponding to it. Such a course would never have been pursued, were it not for that need of reason which requires it to suppose the existence of a necessary being as a basis for the empirical regress, and that, as this necessity must be unconditioned and à priori, reason is bound to discover a conception which shall satisfy, if possible, this requirement, and enable us to attain to the à priori cognition of such a being. This conception was thought to be found in the idea of an ens realissimum, and thus this idea was employed for the attainment of a better defined knowledge of a necessary being, of the existence of which we were convinced, or persuaded, on other grounds. Thus reason was seduced from her natural courage; and, instead of concluding with the conception of an ens realissimum, an attempt was made to begin with it, for the purpose of inferring from it that idea of a necessary existence which it was in fact called in to complete. Thus arose that unfortunate ontological argument, which neither satisfies the healthy common sense of humanity, nor sustains the scientific examination of the philosopher.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"78.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0002","text":"The cosmological proof, which we are about to examine, retains the connection between absolute necessity and the highest reality; but, instead of reasoning from this highest reality to a necessary existence, like the preceding argument, it concludes from the given unconditioned necessity of some being its unlimited reality. The track it pursues, whether rational or sophistical, is at least natural, and not only goes far to persuade the common understanding, but shows itself deserving of respect from the speculative intellect; while it contains, at the same time, the outlines of all the arguments employed in natural theology—arguments which always have been, and still will be, in use and authority. These, however adorned, and hid under whatever embellishments of rhetoric and sentiment, are at bottom identical with the arguments we are at present to discuss. This proof, termed by Leibnitz the argumentum a contingentia mundi, I shall now lay before the reader, and subject to a strict examination.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"78.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","text":"It is framed in the following manner: If something exists, an absolutely necessary being must likewise exist. Now I, at least, exist. Consequently, there exists an absolutely necessary being. The minor contains an experience, the major reasons from a general experience to the existence of a necessary being.[[68]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-68) Thus this argument really begins at experience, and is not completely à priori, or ontological. The object of all possible experience being the world, it is called the cosmological proof. It contains no reference to any peculiar property of sensuous objects, by which this world of sense might be distinguished from other possible worlds; and in this respect it differs from the physico-theological proof, which is based upon the consideration of the peculiar constitution of our sensuous world.\n\n[[68]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-68) This inference is too well known to require more detailed discussion. It is based upon the spurious transcendental law of causality, that everything which is contingent has a cause, which, if itself contingent, must also have a cause; and so on, till the series of subordinated causes must end with an absolutely necessary cause, without which it would not possess completeness.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"78.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0004","text":"The proof proceeds thus: A necessary being can be determined only in one way, that is, it can be determined by only one of all possible opposed predicates; consequently, it must be completely determined in and by its conception. But there is only a single conception of a thing possible, which completely determines the thing à priori: that is, the conception of the ens realissimum. It follows that the conception of the ens realissimum is the only conception by and in which we can cogitate a necessary being. Consequently, a Supreme Being necessarily exists.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"78.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0005","text":"In this cosmological argument are assembled so many sophistical propositions that speculative reason seems to have exerted in it all her dialectical skill to produce a transcendental illusion of the most extreme character. We shall postpone an investigation of this argument for the present, and confine ourselves to exposing the stratagem by which it imposes upon us an old argument in a new dress, and appeals to the agreement of two witnesses, the one with the credentials of pure reason, and the other with those of empiricism; while, in fact, it is only the former who has changed his dress and voice, for the purpose of passing himself off for an additional witness. That it may possess a secure foundation, it bases its conclusions upon experience, and thus appears to be completely distinct from the ontological argument, which places its confidence entirely in pure à priori conceptions. But this experience merely aids reason in making one step—to the existence of a necessary being. What the properties of this being are cannot be learned from experience; and therefore reason abandons it altogether, and pursues its inquiries in the sphere of pure conception, for the purpose of discovering what the properties of an absolutely necessary being ought to be, that is, what among all possible things contain the conditions (requisita) of absolute necessity. Reason believes that it has discovered these requisites in the conception of an ens realissimum—and in it alone, and hence concludes: The ens realissimum is an absolutely necessary being. But it is evident that reason has here presupposed that the conception of an ens realissimum is perfectly adequate to the conception of a being of absolute necessity, that is, that we may infer the existence of the latter from that of the former—a proposition which formed the basis of the ontological argument, and which is now employed in the support of the cosmological argument, contrary to the wish and professions of its inventors. For the existence of an absolutely necessary being is given in conceptions alone. But if I say: “The conception of the ens realissimum is a conception of this kind, and in fact the only conception which is adequate to our idea of a necessary being,” I am obliged to admit, that the latter may be inferred from the former. Thus it is properly the ontological argument which figures in the cosmological, and constitutes the whole strength of the latter; while the spurious basis of experience has been of no further use than to conduct us to the conception of absolute necessity, being utterly insufficient to demonstrate the presence of this attribute in any determinate existence or thing. For when we propose to ourselves an aim of this character, we must abandon the sphere of experience, and rise to that of pure conceptions, which we examine with the purpose of discovering whether any one contains the conditions of the possibility of an absolutely necessary being. But if the possibility of such a being is thus demonstrated, its existence is also proved; for we may then assert that, of all possible beings there is one which possesses the attribute of necessity—in other words, this being possesses an absolutely necessary existence.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"78.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","text":"All illusions in an argument are more easily detected when they are presented in the formal manner employed by the schools, which we now proceed to do.\n\nIf the proposition: “Every absolutely necessary being is likewise an ens realissimum,” is correct (and it is this which constitutes the nervus probandi of the cosmological argument), it must, like all affirmative judgements, be capable of conversion—the conversio per accidens, at least. It follows, then, that some entia realissima are absolutely necessary beings. But no ens realissimum is in any respect different from another, and what is valid of some is valid of all. In this present case, therefore, I may employ simple conversion, and say: “Every ens realissimum is a necessary being.” But as this proposition is determined à priori by the conceptions contained in it, the mere conception of an ens realissimum must possess the additional attribute of absolute necessity. But this is exactly what was maintained in the ontological argument, and not recognized by the cosmological, although it formed the real ground of its disguised and illusory reasoning.\n\nThus the second mode employed by speculative reason of demonstrating the existence of a Supreme Being, is not only, like the first, illusory and inadequate, but possesses the additional blemish of an ignoratio elenchi—professing to conduct us by a new road to the desired goal, but bringing us back, after a short circuit, to the old path which we had deserted at its call.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"78.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","text":"I mentioned above that this cosmological argument contains a perfect nest of dialectical assumptions, which transcendental criticism does not find it difficult to expose and to dissipate. I shall merely enumerate these, leaving it to the reader, who must by this time be well practised in such matters, to investigate the fallacies residing therein.\n\nThe following fallacies, for example, are discoverable in this mode of proof: 1. The transcendental principle: “Everything that is contingent must have a cause”—a principle without significance, except in the sensuous world. For the purely intellectual conception of the contingent cannot produce any synthetical proposition, like that of causality, which is itself without significance or distinguishing characteristic except in the phenomenal world. But in the present case it is employed to help us beyond the limits of its sphere. 2. “From the impossibility of an infinite ascending series of causes in the world of sense a first cause is inferred”; a conclusion which the principles of the employment of reason do not justify even in the sphere of experience, and still less when an attempt is made to pass the limits of this sphere. 3. Reason allows itself to be satisfied upon insufficient grounds, with regard to the completion of this series. It removes all conditions (without which, however, no conception of Necessity can take place); and, as after this it is beyond our power to form any other conceptions, it accepts this as a completion of the conception it wishes to form of the series. 4. The logical possibility of a conception of the total of reality (the criterion of this possibility being the absence of contradiction) is confounded with the transcendental, which requires a principle of the practicability of such a synthesis—a principle which again refers us to the world of experience. And so on.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"78.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0008","text":"The aim of the cosmological argument is to avoid the necessity of proving the existence of a necessary being priori from mere conceptions—a proof which must be ontological, and of which we feel ourselves quite incapable. With this purpose, we reason from an actual existence—an experience in general, to an absolutely necessary condition of that existence. It is in this case unnecessary to demonstrate its possibility. For after having proved that it exists, the question regarding its possibility is superfluous. Now, when we wish to define more strictly the nature of this necessary being, we do not look out for some being the conception of which would enable us to comprehend the necessity of its being—for if we could do this, an empirical presupposition would be unnecessary; no, we try to discover merely the negative condition (conditio sine qua non), without which a being would not be absolutely necessary. Now this would be perfectly admissible in every sort of reasoning, from a consequence to its principle; but in the present case it unfortunately happens that the condition of absolute necessity can be discovered in but a single being, the conception of which must consequently contain all that is requisite for demonstrating the presence of absolute necessity, and thus entitle me to infer this absolute necessity à priori. That is, it must be possible to reason conversely, and say: The thing, to which the conception of the highest reality belongs, is absolutely necessary. But if I cannot reason thus—and I cannot, unless I believe in the sufficiency of the ontological argument—I find insurmountable obstacles in my new path, and am really no farther than the point from which I set out. The conception of a Supreme Being satisfies all questions à priori regarding the internal determinations of a thing, and is for this reason an ideal without equal or parallel, the general conception of it indicating it as at the same time an ens individuum among all possible things. But the conception does not satisfy the question regarding its existence—which was the purpose of all our inquiries; and, although the existence of a necessary being were admitted, we should find it impossible to answer the question: What of all things in the world must be regarded as such?","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"78.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0009","text":"It is certainly allowable to admit the existence of an all-sufficient being—a cause of all possible effects—for the purpose of enabling reason to introduce unity into its mode and grounds of explanation with regard to phenomena. But to assert that such a being necessarily exists, is no longer the modest enunciation of an admissible hypothesis, but the boldest declaration of an apodeictic certainty; for the cognition of that which is absolutely necessary must itself possess that character.\n\nThe aim of the transcendental ideal formed by the mind is either to discover a conception which shall harmonize with the idea of absolute necessity, or a conception which shall contain that idea. If the one is possible, so is the other; for reason recognizes that alone as absolutely necessary which is necessary from its conception. But both attempts are equally beyond our power—we find it impossible to satisfy the understanding upon this point, and as impossible to induce it to remain at rest in relation to this incapacity.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"78.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0010","text":"Unconditioned necessity, which, as the ultimate support and stay of all existing things, is an indispensable requirement of the mind, is an abyss on the verge of which human reason trembles in dismay. Even the idea of eternity, terrible and sublime as it is, as depicted by Haller, does not produce upon the mental vision such a feeling of awe and terror; for, although it measures the duration of things, it does not support them. We cannot bear, nor can we rid ourselves of the thought that a being, which we regard as the greatest of all possible existences, should say to himself: I am from eternity to eternity; beside me there is nothing, except that which exists by my will; whence then am I? Here all sinks away from under us; and the greatest, as the smallest, perfection, hovers without stay or footing in presence of the speculative reason, which finds it as easy to part with the one as with the other.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"78.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0011","text":"Many physical powers, which evidence their existence by their effects, are perfectly inscrutable in their nature; they elude all our powers of observation. The transcendental object which forms the basis of phenomena, and, in connection with it, the reason why our sensibility possesses this rather than that particular kind of conditions, are and must ever remain hidden from our mental vision; the fact is there, the reason of the fact we cannot see. But an ideal of pure reason cannot be termed mysterious or inscrutable, because the only credential of its reality is the need of it felt by reason, for the purpose of giving completeness to the world of synthetical unity. An ideal is not even given as a cogitable object, and therefore cannot be inscrutable; on the contrary, it must, as a mere idea, be based on the constitution of reason itself, and on this account must be capable of explanation and solution. For the very essence of reason consists in its ability to give an account, of all our conceptions, opinions, and assertions—upon objective, or, when they happen to be illusory and fallacious, upon subjective grounds.\n\nDetection and Explanation of the Dialectical Illusion in all Transcendental Arguments for the Existence of a Necessary Being.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"78.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0012","text":"Both of the above arguments are transcendental; in other words, they do not proceed upon empirical principles. For, although the cosmological argument professed to lay a basis of experience for its edifice of reasoning, it did not ground its procedure upon the peculiar constitution of experience, but upon pure principles of reason—in relation to an existence given by empirical consciousness; utterly abandoning its guidance, however, for the purpose of supporting its assertions entirely upon pure conceptions. Now what is the cause, in these transcendental arguments, of the dialectical, but natural, illusion, which connects the conceptions of necessity and supreme reality, and hypostatizes that which cannot be anything but an idea? What is the cause of this unavoidable step on the part of reason, of admitting that some one among all existing things must be necessary, while it falls back from the assertion of the existence of such a being as from an abyss? And how does reason proceed to explain this anomaly to itself, and from the wavering condition of a timid and reluctant approbation—always again withdrawn—arrive at a calm and settled insight into its cause?","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"78.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","text":"It is something very remarkable that, on the supposition that something exists, I cannot avoid the inference that something exists necessarily. Upon this perfectly natural—but not on that account reliable—inference does the cosmological argument rest. But, let me form any conception whatever of a thing, I find that I cannot cogitate the existence of the thing as absolutely necessary, and that nothing prevents me—be the thing or being what it may—from cogitating its non-existence. I may thus be obliged to admit that all existing things have a necessary basis, while I cannot cogitate any single or individual thing as necessary. In other words, I can never complete the regress through the conditions of existence, without admitting the existence of a necessary being; but, on the other hand, I cannot make a commencement from this being.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"78.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","text":"If I must cogitate something as existing necessarily as the basis of existing things, and yet am not permitted to cogitate any individual thing as in itself necessary, the inevitable inference is that necessity and contingency are not properties of things themselves—otherwise an internal contradiction would result; that consequently neither of these principles are objective, but merely subjective principles of reason—the one requiring us to seek for a necessary ground for everything that exists, that is, to be satisfied with no other explanation than that which is complete à priori, the other forbidding us ever to hope for the attainment of this completeness, that is, to regard no member of the empirical world as unconditioned. In this mode of viewing them, both principles, in their purely heuristic and regulative character, and as concerning merely the formal interest of reason, are quite consistent with each other. The one says: “You must philosophize upon nature,” as if there existed a necessary primal basis of all existing things, solely for the purpose of introducing systematic unity into your knowledge, by pursuing an idea of this character—a foundation which is arbitrarily admitted to be ultimate; while the other warns you to consider no individual determination, concerning the existence of things, as such an ultimate foundation, that is, as absolutely necessary, but to keep the way always open for further progress in the deduction, and to treat every determination as determined by some other. But if all that we perceive must be regarded as conditionally necessary, it is impossible that anything which is empirically given should be absolutely necessary.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"78.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0015","text":"It follows from this that you must accept the absolutely necessary as out of and beyond the world, inasmuch as it is useful only as a principle of the highest possible unity in experience, and you cannot discover any such necessary existence in the would, the second rule requiring you to regard all empirical causes of unity as themselves deduced.\n\nThe philosophers of antiquity regarded all the forms of nature as contingent; while matter was considered by them, in accordance with the judgement of the common reason of mankind, as primal and necessary. But if they had regarded matter, not relatively—as the substratum of phenomena, but absolutely and in itself—as an independent existence, this idea of absolute necessity would have immediately disappeared. For there is nothing absolutely connecting reason with such an existence; on the contrary, it can annihilate it in thought, always and without self-contradiction. But in thought alone lay the idea of absolute necessity. A regulative principle must, therefore, have been at the foundation of this opinion. In fact, extension and impenetrability—which together constitute our conception of matter—form the supreme empirical principle of the unity of phenomena, and this principle, in so far as it is empirically unconditioned, possesses the property of a regulative principle. But, as every determination of matter which constitutes what is real in it—and consequently impenetrability—is an effect, which must have a cause, and is for this reason always derived, the notion of matter cannot harmonize with the idea of a necessary being, in its character of the principle of all derived unity. For every one of its real properties, being derived, must be only conditionally necessary, and can therefore be annihilated in thought; and thus the whole existence of matter can be so annihilated or suppressed. If this were not the case, we should have found in the world of phenomena the highest ground or condition of unity—which is impossible, according to the second regulative principle. It follows that matter, and, in general, all that forms part of the world of sense, cannot be a necessary primal being, nor even a principle of empirical unity, but that this being or principle must have its place assigned without the world. And, in this way, we can proceed in perfect confidence to deduce the phenomena of the world and their existence from other phenomena, just as if there existed no necessary being; and we can at the same time, strive without ceasing towards the attainment of completeness for our deduction, just as if such a being—the supreme condition of all existences—were presupposed by the mind.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"78.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","text":"These remarks will have made it evident to the reader that the ideal of the Supreme Being, far from being an enouncement of the existence of a being in itself necessary, is nothing more than a regulative principle of reason, requiring us to regard all connection existing between phenomena as if it had its origin from an all-sufficient necessary cause, and basing upon this the rule of a systematic and necessary unity in the explanation of phenomena. We cannot, at the same time, avoid regarding, by a transcendental subreptio, this formal principle as constitutive, and hypostatizing this unity. Precisely similar is the case with our notion of space. Space is the primal condition of all forms, which are properly just so many different limitations of it; and thus, although it is merely a principle of sensibility, we cannot help regarding it as an absolutely necessary and self-subsistent thing—as an object given à priori in itself. In the same way, it is quite natural that, as the systematic unity of nature cannot be established as a principle for the empirical employment of reason, unless it is based upon the idea of an ens realissimum, as the supreme cause, we should regard this idea as a real object, and this object, in its character of supreme condition, as absolutely necessary, and that in this way a regulative should be transformed into a constitutive principle. This interchange becomes evident when I regard this supreme being, which, relatively to the world, was absolutely (unconditionally) necessary, as a thing per se. In this case, I find it impossible to represent this necessity in or by any conception, and it exists merely in my own mind, as the formal condition of thought, but not as a material and hypostatic condition of existence.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"78.16"}],"sha256":"086420241ee96047bc577b469705f70aff3ae3d98c3fb21fed899fd3e1e2f12c","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP","title":"Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof","order":79,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SVIP","text":"### Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof\n\nIf, then, neither a pure conception nor the general experience of an existing being can provide a sufficient basis for the proof of the existence of the Deity, we can make the attempt by the only other mode—that of grounding our argument upon a determinate experience of the phenomena of the present world, their constitution and disposition, and discover whether we can thus attain to a sound conviction of the existence of a Supreme Being. This argument we shall term the physico-theological argument. If it is shown to be insufficient, speculative reason cannot present us with any satisfactory proof of the existence of a being corresponding to our transcendental idea.\n\nIt is evident from the remarks that have been made in the preceding sections, that an answer to this question will be far from being difficult or unconvincing. For how can any experience be adequate with an idea? The very essence of an idea consists in the fact that no experience can ever be discovered congruent or adequate with it. The transcendental idea of a necessary and all-sufficient being is so immeasurably great, so high above all that is empirical, which is always conditioned, that we hope in vain to find materials in the sphere of experience sufficiently ample for our conception, and in vain seek the unconditioned among things that are conditioned, while examples, nay, even guidance is denied us by the laws of empirical synthesis.\n\nIf the Supreme Being forms a link in the chain of empirical conditions, it must be a member of the empirical series, and, like the lower members which it precedes, have its origin in some higher member of the series. If, on the other hand, we disengage it from the chain, and cogitate it as an intelligible being, apart from the series of natural causes—how shall reason bridge the abyss that separates the latter from the former? All laws respecting the regress from effects to causes, all synthetical additions to our knowledge relate solely to possible experience and the objects of the sensuous world, and, apart from them, are without significance.\n\nThe world around us opens before our view so magnificent a spectacle of order, variety, beauty, and conformity to ends, that whether we pursue our observations into the infinity of space in the one direction, or into its illimitable divisions in the other, whether we regard the world in its greatest or its least manifestations—even after we have attained to the highest summit of knowledge which our weak minds can reach, we find that language in the presence of wonders so inconceivable has lost its force, and number its power to reckon, nay, even thought fails to conceive adequately, and our conception of the whole dissolves into an astonishment without power of expression—all the more eloquent that it is dumb. Everywhere around us we observe a chain of causes and effects, of means and ends, of death and birth; and, as nothing has entered of itself into the condition in which we find it, we are constantly referred to some other thing, which itself suggests the same inquiry regarding its cause, and thus the universe must sink into the abyss of nothingness, unless we admit that, besides this infinite chain of contingencies, there exists something that is primal and self-subsistent—something which, as the cause of this phenomenal world, secures its continuance and preservation.\n\nThis highest cause—what magnitude shall we attribute to it? Of the content of the world we are ignorant; still less can we estimate its magnitude by comparison with the sphere of the possible. But this supreme cause being a necessity of the human mind, what is there to prevent us from attributing to it such a degree of perfection as to place it above the sphere of all that is possible? This we can easily do, although only by the aid of the faint outline of an abstract conception, by representing this being to ourselves as containing in itself, as an individual substance, all possible perfection—a conception which satisfies that requirement of reason which demands parsimony in principles, which is free from self-contradiction, which even contributes to the extension of the employment of reason in experience, by means of the guidance afforded by this idea to order and system, and which in no respect conflicts with any law of experience.\n\nThis argument always deserves to be mentioned with respect. It is the oldest, the clearest, and that most in conformity with the common reason of humanity. It animates the study of nature, as it itself derives its existence and draws ever new strength from that source. It introduces aims and ends into a sphere in which our observation could not of itself have discovered them, and extends our knowledge of nature, by directing our attention to a unity, the principle of which lies beyond nature. This knowledge of nature again reacts upon this idea—its cause; and thus our belief in a divine author of the universe rises to the power of an irresistible conviction.\n\nFor these reasons it would be utterly hopeless to attempt to rob this argument of the authority it has always enjoyed. The mind, unceasingly elevated by these considerations, which, although empirical, are so remarkably powerful, and continually adding to their force, will not suffer itself to be depressed by the doubts suggested by subtle speculation; it tears itself out of this state of uncertainty, the moment it casts a look upon the wondrous forms of nature and the majesty of the universe, and rises from height to height, from condition to condition, till it has elevated itself to the supreme and unconditioned author of all.\n\nBut although we have nothing to object to the reasonableness and utility of this procedure, but have rather to commend and encourage it, we cannot approve of the claims which this argument advances to demonstrative certainty and to a reception upon its own merits, apart from favour or support by other arguments. Nor can it injure the cause of morality to endeavour to lower the tone of the arrogant sophist, and to teach him that modesty and moderation which are the properties of a belief that brings calm and content into the mind, without prescribing to it an unworthy subjection. I maintain, then, that the physico-theological argument is insufficient of itself to prove the existence of a Supreme Being, that it must entrust this to the ontological argument—to which it serves merely as an introduction, and that, consequently, this argument contains the only possible ground of proof (possessed by speculative reason) for the existence of this being.\n\nThe chief momenta in the physico-theological argument are as follow: 1. We observe in the world manifest signs of an arrangement full of purpose, executed with great wisdom, and argument in whole of a content indescribably various, and of an extent without limits. 2. This arrangement of means and ends is entirely foreign to the things existing in the world—it belongs to them merely as a contingent attribute; in other words, the nature of different things could not of itself, whatever means were employed, harmoniously tend towards certain purposes, were they not chosen and directed for these purposes by a rational and disposing principle, in accordance with certain fundamental ideas. 3. There exists, therefore, a sublime and wise cause (or several), which is not merely a blind, all-powerful nature, producing the beings and events which fill the world in unconscious fecundity, but a free and intelligent cause of the world. 4. The unity of this cause may be inferred from the unity of the reciprocal relation existing between the parts of the world, as portions of an artistic edifice—an inference which all our observation favours, and all principles of analogy support.\n\nIn the above argument, it is inferred from the analogy of certain products of nature with those of human art, when it compels Nature to bend herself to its purposes, as in the case of a house, a ship, or a watch, that the same kind of causality—namely, understanding and will—resides in nature. It is also declared that the internal possibility of this freely-acting nature (which is the source of all art, and perhaps also of human reason) is derivable from another and superhuman art—a conclusion which would perhaps be found incapable of standing the test of subtle transcendental criticism. But to neither of these opinions shall we at present object. We shall only remark that it must be confessed that, if we are to discuss the subject of cause at all, we cannot proceed more securely than with the guidance of the analogy subsisting between nature and such products of design—these being the only products whose causes and modes of organization are completely known to us. Reason would be unable to satisfy her own requirements, if she passed from a causality which she does know, to obscure and indemonstrable principles of explanation which she does not know.\n\nAccording to the physico-theological argument, the connection and harmony existing in the world evidence the contingency of the form merely, but not of the matter, that is, of the substance of the world. To establish the truth of the latter opinion, it would be necessary to prove that all things would be in themselves incapable of this harmony and order, unless they were, even as regards their substance, the product of a supreme wisdom. But this would require very different grounds of proof from those presented by the analogy with human art. This proof can at most, therefore, demonstrate the existence of an architect of the world, whose efforts are limited by the capabilities of the material with which he works, but not of a creator of the world, to whom all things are subject. Thus this argument is utterly insufficient for the task before us—a demonstration of the existence of an all-sufficient being. If we wish to prove the contingency of matter, we must have recourse to a transcendental argument, which the physico-theological was constructed expressly to avoid.\n\nWe infer, from the order and design visible in the universe, as a disposition of a thoroughly contingent character, the existence of a cause proportionate thereto. The conception of this cause must contain certain determinate qualities, and it must therefore be regarded as the conception of a being which possesses all power, wisdom, and so on, in one word, all perfection—the conception, that is, of an all-sufficient being. For the predicates of very great, astonishing, or immeasurable power and excellence, give us no determinate conception of the thing, nor do they inform us what the thing may be in itself. They merely indicate the relation existing between the magnitude of the object and the observer, who compares it with himself and with his own power of comprehension, and are mere expressions of praise and reverence, by which the object is either magnified, or the observing subject depreciated in relation to the object. Where we have to do with the magnitude (of the perfection) of a thing, we can discover no determinate conception, except that which comprehends all possible perfection or completeness, and it is only the total (omnitudo) of reality which is completely determined in and through its conception alone.\n\nNow it cannot be expected that any one will be bold enough to declare that he has a perfect insight into the relation which the magnitude of the world he contemplates bears (in its extent as well as in its content) to omnipotence, into that of the order and design in the world to the highest wisdom, and that of the unity of the world to the absolute unity of a Supreme Being. Physico-theology is therefore incapable of presenting a determinate conception of a supreme cause of the world, and is therefore insufficient as a principle of theology—a theology which is itself to be the basis of religion.\n\nThe attainment of absolute totality is completely impossible on the path of empiricism. And yet this is the path pursued in the physico-theological argument. What means shall we employ to bridge the abyss?\n\nAfter elevating ourselves to admiration of the magnitude of the power, wisdom, and other attributes of the author of the world, and finding we can advance no further, we leave the argument on empirical grounds, and proceed to infer the contingency of the world from the order and conformity to aims that are observable in it. From this contingency we infer, by the help of transcendental conceptions alone, the existence of something absolutely necessary; and, still advancing, proceed from the conception of the absolute necessity of the first cause to the completely determined or determining conception thereof—the conception of an all-embracing reality. Thus the physico-theological, failing in its undertaking, recurs in its embarrassment to the cosmological argument; and, as this is merely the ontological argument in disguise, it executes its design solely by the aid of pure reason, although it at first professed to have no connection with this faculty and to base its entire procedure upon experience alone.\n\nThe physico-theologians have therefore no reason to regard with such contempt the transcendental mode of argument, and to look down upon it, with the conceit of clear-sighted observers of nature, as the brain-cobweb of obscure speculatists. For, if they reflect upon and examine their own arguments, they will find that, after following for some time the path of nature and experience, and discovering themselves no nearer their object, they suddenly leave this path and pass into the region of pure possibility, where they hope to reach upon the wings of ideas what had eluded all their empirical investigations. Gaining, as they think, a firm footing after this immense leap, they extend their determinate conception—into the possession of which they have come, they know not how—over the whole sphere of creation, and explain their ideal, which is entirely a product of pure reason, by illustrations drawn from experience—though in a degree miserably unworthy of the grandeur of the object, while they refuse to acknowledge that they have arrived at this cognition or hypothesis by a very different road from that of experience.\n\nThus the physico-theological is based upon the cosmological, and this upon the ontological proof of the existence of a Supreme Being; and as besides these three there is no other path open to speculative reason, the ontological proof, on the ground of pure conceptions of reason, is the only possible one, if any proof of a proposition so far transcending the empirical exercise of the understanding is possible at all.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0001","text":"### Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof\n\nIf, then, neither a pure conception nor the general experience of an existing being can provide a sufficient basis for the proof of the existence of the Deity, we can make the attempt by the only other mode—that of grounding our argument upon a determinate experience of the phenomena of the present world, their constitution and disposition, and discover whether we can thus attain to a sound conviction of the existence of a Supreme Being. This argument we shall term the physico-theological argument. If it is shown to be insufficient, speculative reason cannot present us with any satisfactory proof of the existence of a being corresponding to our transcendental idea.\n\nIt is evident from the remarks that have been made in the preceding sections, that an answer to this question will be far from being difficult or unconvincing. For how can any experience be adequate with an idea? The very essence of an idea consists in the fact that no experience can ever be discovered congruent or adequate with it. The transcendental idea of a necessary and all-sufficient being is so immeasurably great, so high above all that is empirical, which is always conditioned, that we hope in vain to find materials in the sphere of experience sufficiently ample for our conception, and in vain seek the unconditioned among things that are conditioned, while examples, nay, even guidance is denied us by the laws of empirical synthesis.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"79.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0002","text":"If the Supreme Being forms a link in the chain of empirical conditions, it must be a member of the empirical series, and, like the lower members which it precedes, have its origin in some higher member of the series. If, on the other hand, we disengage it from the chain, and cogitate it as an intelligible being, apart from the series of natural causes—how shall reason bridge the abyss that separates the latter from the former? All laws respecting the regress from effects to causes, all synthetical additions to our knowledge relate solely to possible experience and the objects of the sensuous world, and, apart from them, are without significance.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"79.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0003","text":"The world around us opens before our view so magnificent a spectacle of order, variety, beauty, and conformity to ends, that whether we pursue our observations into the infinity of space in the one direction, or into its illimitable divisions in the other, whether we regard the world in its greatest or its least manifestations—even after we have attained to the highest summit of knowledge which our weak minds can reach, we find that language in the presence of wonders so inconceivable has lost its force, and number its power to reckon, nay, even thought fails to conceive adequately, and our conception of the whole dissolves into an astonishment without power of expression—all the more eloquent that it is dumb. Everywhere around us we observe a chain of causes and effects, of means and ends, of death and birth; and, as nothing has entered of itself into the condition in which we find it, we are constantly referred to some other thing, which itself suggests the same inquiry regarding its cause, and thus the universe must sink into the abyss of nothingness, unless we admit that, besides this infinite chain of contingencies, there exists something that is primal and self-subsistent—something which, as the cause of this phenomenal world, secures its continuance and preservation.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"79.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0004","text":"This highest cause—what magnitude shall we attribute to it? Of the content of the world we are ignorant; still less can we estimate its magnitude by comparison with the sphere of the possible. But this supreme cause being a necessity of the human mind, what is there to prevent us from attributing to it such a degree of perfection as to place it above the sphere of all that is possible? This we can easily do, although only by the aid of the faint outline of an abstract conception, by representing this being to ourselves as containing in itself, as an individual substance, all possible perfection—a conception which satisfies that requirement of reason which demands parsimony in principles, which is free from self-contradiction, which even contributes to the extension of the employment of reason in experience, by means of the guidance afforded by this idea to order and system, and which in no respect conflicts with any law of experience.\n\nThis argument always deserves to be mentioned with respect. It is the oldest, the clearest, and that most in conformity with the common reason of humanity. It animates the study of nature, as it itself derives its existence and draws ever new strength from that source. It introduces aims and ends into a sphere in which our observation could not of itself have discovered them, and extends our knowledge of nature, by directing our attention to a unity, the principle of which lies beyond nature. This knowledge of nature again reacts upon this idea—its cause; and thus our belief in a divine author of the universe rises to the power of an irresistible conviction.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"79.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","text":"For these reasons it would be utterly hopeless to attempt to rob this argument of the authority it has always enjoyed. The mind, unceasingly elevated by these considerations, which, although empirical, are so remarkably powerful, and continually adding to their force, will not suffer itself to be depressed by the doubts suggested by subtle speculation; it tears itself out of this state of uncertainty, the moment it casts a look upon the wondrous forms of nature and the majesty of the universe, and rises from height to height, from condition to condition, till it has elevated itself to the supreme and unconditioned author of all.\n\nBut although we have nothing to object to the reasonableness and utility of this procedure, but have rather to commend and encourage it, we cannot approve of the claims which this argument advances to demonstrative certainty and to a reception upon its own merits, apart from favour or support by other arguments. Nor can it injure the cause of morality to endeavour to lower the tone of the arrogant sophist, and to teach him that modesty and moderation which are the properties of a belief that brings calm and content into the mind, without prescribing to it an unworthy subjection. I maintain, then, that the physico-theological argument is insufficient of itself to prove the existence of a Supreme Being, that it must entrust this to the ontological argument—to which it serves merely as an introduction, and that, consequently, this argument contains the only possible ground of proof (possessed by speculative reason) for the existence of this being.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"79.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0006","text":"The chief momenta in the physico-theological argument are as follow: 1. We observe in the world manifest signs of an arrangement full of purpose, executed with great wisdom, and argument in whole of a content indescribably various, and of an extent without limits. 2. This arrangement of means and ends is entirely foreign to the things existing in the world—it belongs to them merely as a contingent attribute; in other words, the nature of different things could not of itself, whatever means were employed, harmoniously tend towards certain purposes, were they not chosen and directed for these purposes by a rational and disposing principle, in accordance with certain fundamental ideas. 3. There exists, therefore, a sublime and wise cause (or several), which is not merely a blind, all-powerful nature, producing the beings and events which fill the world in unconscious fecundity, but a free and intelligent cause of the world. 4. The unity of this cause may be inferred from the unity of the reciprocal relation existing between the parts of the world, as portions of an artistic edifice—an inference which all our observation favours, and all principles of analogy support.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"79.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0007","text":"In the above argument, it is inferred from the analogy of certain products of nature with those of human art, when it compels Nature to bend herself to its purposes, as in the case of a house, a ship, or a watch, that the same kind of causality—namely, understanding and will—resides in nature. It is also declared that the internal possibility of this freely-acting nature (which is the source of all art, and perhaps also of human reason) is derivable from another and superhuman art—a conclusion which would perhaps be found incapable of standing the test of subtle transcendental criticism. But to neither of these opinions shall we at present object. We shall only remark that it must be confessed that, if we are to discuss the subject of cause at all, we cannot proceed more securely than with the guidance of the analogy subsisting between nature and such products of design—these being the only products whose causes and modes of organization are completely known to us. Reason would be unable to satisfy her own requirements, if she passed from a causality which she does know, to obscure and indemonstrable principles of explanation which she does not know.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"79.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","text":"According to the physico-theological argument, the connection and harmony existing in the world evidence the contingency of the form merely, but not of the matter, that is, of the substance of the world. To establish the truth of the latter opinion, it would be necessary to prove that all things would be in themselves incapable of this harmony and order, unless they were, even as regards their substance, the product of a supreme wisdom. But this would require very different grounds of proof from those presented by the analogy with human art. This proof can at most, therefore, demonstrate the existence of an architect of the world, whose efforts are limited by the capabilities of the material with which he works, but not of a creator of the world, to whom all things are subject. Thus this argument is utterly insufficient for the task before us—a demonstration of the existence of an all-sufficient being. If we wish to prove the contingency of matter, we must have recourse to a transcendental argument, which the physico-theological was constructed expressly to avoid.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"79.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","text":"We infer, from the order and design visible in the universe, as a disposition of a thoroughly contingent character, the existence of a cause proportionate thereto. The conception of this cause must contain certain determinate qualities, and it must therefore be regarded as the conception of a being which possesses all power, wisdom, and so on, in one word, all perfection—the conception, that is, of an all-sufficient being. For the predicates of very great, astonishing, or immeasurable power and excellence, give us no determinate conception of the thing, nor do they inform us what the thing may be in itself. They merely indicate the relation existing between the magnitude of the object and the observer, who compares it with himself and with his own power of comprehension, and are mere expressions of praise and reverence, by which the object is either magnified, or the observing subject depreciated in relation to the object. Where we have to do with the magnitude (of the perfection) of a thing, we can discover no determinate conception, except that which comprehends all possible perfection or completeness, and it is only the total (omnitudo) of reality which is completely determined in and through its conception alone.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"79.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0010","text":"Now it cannot be expected that any one will be bold enough to declare that he has a perfect insight into the relation which the magnitude of the world he contemplates bears (in its extent as well as in its content) to omnipotence, into that of the order and design in the world to the highest wisdom, and that of the unity of the world to the absolute unity of a Supreme Being. Physico-theology is therefore incapable of presenting a determinate conception of a supreme cause of the world, and is therefore insufficient as a principle of theology—a theology which is itself to be the basis of religion.\n\nThe attainment of absolute totality is completely impossible on the path of empiricism. And yet this is the path pursued in the physico-theological argument. What means shall we employ to bridge the abyss?","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"79.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","text":"After elevating ourselves to admiration of the magnitude of the power, wisdom, and other attributes of the author of the world, and finding we can advance no further, we leave the argument on empirical grounds, and proceed to infer the contingency of the world from the order and conformity to aims that are observable in it. From this contingency we infer, by the help of transcendental conceptions alone, the existence of something absolutely necessary; and, still advancing, proceed from the conception of the absolute necessity of the first cause to the completely determined or determining conception thereof—the conception of an all-embracing reality. Thus the physico-theological, failing in its undertaking, recurs in its embarrassment to the cosmological argument; and, as this is merely the ontological argument in disguise, it executes its design solely by the aid of pure reason, although it at first professed to have no connection with this faculty and to base its entire procedure upon experience alone.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"79.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","text":"The physico-theologians have therefore no reason to regard with such contempt the transcendental mode of argument, and to look down upon it, with the conceit of clear-sighted observers of nature, as the brain-cobweb of obscure speculatists. For, if they reflect upon and examine their own arguments, they will find that, after following for some time the path of nature and experience, and discovering themselves no nearer their object, they suddenly leave this path and pass into the region of pure possibility, where they hope to reach upon the wings of ideas what had eluded all their empirical investigations. Gaining, as they think, a firm footing after this immense leap, they extend their determinate conception—into the possession of which they have come, they know not how—over the whole sphere of creation, and explain their ideal, which is entirely a product of pure reason, by illustrations drawn from experience—though in a degree miserably unworthy of the grandeur of the object, while they refuse to acknowledge that they have arrived at this cognition or hypothesis by a very different road from that of experience.\n\nThus the physico-theological is based upon the cosmological, and this upon the ontological proof of the existence of a Supreme Being; and as besides these three there is no other path open to speculative reason, the ontological proof, on the ground of pure conceptions of reason, is the only possible one, if any proof of a proposition so far transcending the empirical exercise of the understanding is possible at all.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"79.12"}],"sha256":"3320c68759bdc68a5906d596d01ed8d9c74f72b0ca1b5d533a43437d007deabf","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA","title":"Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason","order":80,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SVCA","text":"### Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason\n\nIf by the term theology I understand the cognition of a primal being, that cognition is based either upon reason alone (theologia rationalis) or upon revelation (theologia revelata). The former cogitates its object either by means of pure transcendental conceptions, as an ens originarium, realissimum, ens entium, and is termed transcendental theology; or, by means of a conception derived from the nature of our own mind, as a supreme intelligence, and must then be entitled natural theology. The person who believes in a transcendental theology alone, is termed a deist; he who acknowledges the possibility of a natural theology also, a theist. The former admits that we can cognize by pure reason alone the existence of a Supreme Being, but at the same time maintains that our conception of this being is purely transcendental, and that all we can say of it is that it possesses all reality, without being able to define it more closely. The second asserts that reason is capable of presenting us, from the analogy with nature, with a more definite conception of this being, and that its operations, as the cause of all things, are the results of intelligence and free will. The former regards the Supreme Being as the cause of the world—whether by the necessity of his nature, or as a free agent, is left undetermined; the latter considers this being as the author of the world.\n\nTranscendental theology aims either at inferring the existence of a Supreme Being from a general experience, without any closer reference to the world to which this experience belongs, and in this case it is called cosmotheology; or it endeavours to cognize the existence of such a being, through mere conceptions, without the aid of experience, and is then termed ontotheology.\n\nNatural theology infers the attributes and the existence of an author of the world, from the constitution of, the order and unity observable in, the world, in which two modes of causality must be admitted to exist—those of nature and freedom. Thus it rises from this world to a supreme intelligence, either as the principle of all natural, or of all moral order and perfection. In the former case it is termed physico-theology, in the latter, ethical or moral-theology.[[69]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-69)\n\n[[69]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-69) Not theological ethics; for this science contains ethical laws, which presuppose the existence of a Supreme Governor of the world; while moral-theology, on the contrary, is the expression of a conviction of the existence of a Supreme Being, founded upon ethical laws.\n\nAs we are wont to understand by the term God not merely an eternal nature, the operations of which are insensate and blind, but a Supreme Being, who is the free and intelligent author of all things, and as it is this latter view alone that can be of interest to humanity, we might, in strict rigour, deny to the deist any belief in God at all, and regard him merely as a maintainer of the existence of a primal being or thing—the supreme cause of all other things. But, as no one ought to be blamed, merely because he does not feel himself justified in maintaining a certain opinion, as if he altogether denied its truth and asserted the opposite, it is more correct—as it is less harsh—to say, the deist believes in a God, the theist in a living God (summa intelligentia). We shall now proceed to investigate the sources of all these attempts of reason to establish the existence of a Supreme Being.\n\nIt may be sufficient in this place to define theoretical knowledge or cognition as knowledge of that which is, and practical knowledge as knowledge of that which ought to be. In this view, the theoretical employment of reason is that by which I cognize à priori (as necessary) that something is, while the practical is that by which I cognize à priori what ought to happen. Now, if it is an indubitably certain, though at the same time an entirely conditioned truth, that something is, or ought to happen, either a certain determinate condition of this truth is absolutely necessary, or such a condition may be arbitrarily presupposed. In the former case the condition is postulated (per thesin), in the latter supposed (per hypothesin). There are certain practical laws—those of morality—which are absolutely necessary. Now, if these laws necessarily presuppose the existence of some being, as the condition of the possibility of their obligatory power, this being must be postulated, because the conditioned, from which we reason to this determinate condition, is itself cognized à priori as absolutely necessary. We shall at some future time show that the moral laws not merely presuppose the existence of a Supreme Being, but also, as themselves absolutely necessary in a different relation, demand or postulate it—although only from a practical point of view. The discussion of this argument we postpone for the present.\n\nWhen the question relates merely to that which is, not to that which ought to be, the conditioned which is presented in experience is always cogitated as contingent. For this reason its condition cannot be regarded as absolutely necessary, but merely as relatively necessary, or rather as needful; the condition is in itself and à priori a mere arbitrary presupposition in aid of the cognition, by reason, of the conditioned. If, then, we are to possess a theoretical cognition of the absolute necessity of a thing, we cannot attain to this cognition otherwise than à priori by means of conceptions; while it is impossible in this way to cognize the existence of a cause which bears any relation to an existence given in experience.\n\nTheoretical cognition is speculative when it relates to an object or certain conceptions of an object which is not given and cannot be discovered by means of experience. It is opposed to the cognition of nature, which concerns only those objects or predicates which can be presented in a possible experience.\n\nThe principle that everything which happens (the empirically contingent) must have a cause, is a principle of the cognition of nature, but not of speculative cognition. For, if we change it into an abstract principle, and deprive it of its reference to experience and the empirical, we shall find that it cannot with justice be regarded any longer as a synthetical proposition, and that it is impossible to discover any mode of transition from that which exists to something entirely different—termed cause. Nay, more, the conception of a cause likewise that of the contingent—loses, in this speculative mode of employing it, all significance, for its objective reality and meaning are comprehensible from experience alone.\n\nWhen from the existence of the universe and the things in it the existence of a cause of the universe is inferred, reason is proceeding not in the natural, but in the speculative method. For the principle of the former enounces, not that things themselves or substances, but only that which happens or their states—as empirically contingent, have a cause: the assertion that the existence of substance itself is contingent is not justified by experience, it is the assertion of a reason employing its principles in a speculative manner. If, again, I infer from the form of the universe, from the way in which all things are connected and act and react upon each other, the existence of a cause entirely distinct from the universe—this would again be a judgement of purely speculative reason; because the object in this case—the cause—can never be an object of possible experience. In both these cases the principle of causality, which is valid only in the field of experience—useless and even meaningless beyond this region, would be diverted from its proper destination.\n\nNow I maintain that all attempts of reason to establish a theology by the aid of speculation alone are fruitless, that the principles of reason as applied to nature do not conduct us to any theological truths, and, consequently, that a rational theology can have no existence, unless it is founded upon the laws of morality. For all synthetical principles of the understanding are valid only as immanent in experience; while the cognition of a Supreme Being necessitates their being employed transcendentally, and of this the understanding is quite incapable. If the empirical law of causality is to conduct us to a Supreme Being, this being must belong to the chain of empirical objects—in which case it would be, like all phenomena, itself conditioned. If the possibility of passing the limits of experience be admitted, by means of the dynamical law of the relation of an effect to its cause, what kind of conception shall we obtain by this procedure? Certainly not the conception of a Supreme Being, because experience never presents us with the greatest of all possible effects, and it is only an effect of this character that could witness to the existence of a corresponding cause. If, for the purpose of fully satisfying the requirements of Reason, we recognize her right to assert the existence of a perfect and absolutely necessary being, this can be admitted only from favour, and cannot be regarded as the result of irresistible demonstration. The physico-theological proof may add weight to others—if other proofs there are—by connecting speculation with experience; but in itself it rather prepares the mind for theological cognition, and gives it a right and natural direction, than establishes a sure foundation for theology.\n\nIt is now perfectly evident that transcendental questions admit only of transcendental answers—those presented à priori by pure conceptions without the least empirical admixture. But the question in the present case is evidently synthetical—it aims at the extension of our cognition beyond the bounds of experience—it requires an assurance respecting the existence of a being corresponding with the idea in our minds, to which no experience can ever be adequate. Now it has been abundantly proved that all à priori synthetical cognition is possible only as the expression of the formal conditions of a possible experience; and that the validity of all principles depends upon their immanence in the field of experience, that is, their relation to objects of empirical cognition or phenomena. Thus all transcendental procedure in reference to speculative theology is without result.\n\nIf any one prefers doubting the conclusiveness of the proofs of our analytic to losing the persuasion of the validity of these old and time honoured arguments, he at least cannot decline answering the question—how he can pass the limits of all possible experience by the help of mere ideas. If he talks of new arguments, or of improvements upon old arguments, I request him to spare me. There is certainly no great choice in this sphere of discussion, as all speculative arguments must at last look for support to the ontological, and I have, therefore, very little to fear from the argumentative fecundity of the dogmatical defenders of a non-sensuous reason. Without looking upon myself as a remarkably combative person, I shall not decline the challenge to detect the fallacy and destroy the pretensions of every attempt of speculative theology. And yet the hope of better fortune never deserts those who are accustomed to the dogmatical mode of procedure. I shall, therefore, restrict myself to the simple and equitable demand that such reasoners will demonstrate, from the nature of the human mind as well as from that of the other sources of knowledge, how we are to proceed to extend our cognition completely à priori, and to carry it to that point where experience abandons us, and no means exist of guaranteeing the objective reality of our conceptions. In whatever way the understanding may have attained to a conception, the existence of the object of the conception cannot be discovered in it by analysis, because the cognition of the existence of the object depends upon the object’s being posited and given in itself apart from the conception. But it is utterly impossible to go beyond our conception, without the aid of experience—which presents to the mind nothing but phenomena, or to attain by the help of mere conceptions to a conviction of the existence of new kinds of objects or supernatural beings.\n\nBut although pure speculative reason is far from sufficient to demonstrate the existence of a Supreme Being, it is of the highest utility in correcting our conception of this being—on the supposition that we can attain to the cognition of it by some other means—in making it consistent with itself and with all other conceptions of intelligible objects, clearing it from all that is incompatible with the conception of an ens summun, and eliminating from it all limitations or admixtures of empirical elements.\n\nTranscendental theology is still therefore, notwithstanding its objective insufficiency, of importance in a negative respect; it is useful as a test of the procedure of reason when engaged with pure ideas, no other than a transcendental standard being in this case admissible. For if, from a practical point of view, the hypothesis of a Supreme and All-sufficient Being is to maintain its validity without opposition, it must be of the highest importance to define this conception in a correct and rigorous manner—as the transcendental conception of a necessary being, to eliminate all phenomenal elements (anthropomorphism in its most extended signification), and at the same time to overflow all contradictory assertions—be they atheistic, deistic, or anthropomorphic. This is of course very easy; as the same arguments which demonstrated the inability of human reason to affirm the existence of a Supreme Being must be alike sufficient to prove the invalidity of its denial. For it is impossible to gain from the pure speculation of reason demonstration that there exists no Supreme Being, as the ground of all that exists, or that this being possesses none of those properties which we regard as analogical with the dynamical qualities of a thinking being, or that, as the anthropomorphists would have us believe, it is subject to all the limitations which sensibility imposes upon those intelligences which exist in the world of experience.\n\nA Supreme Being is, therefore, for the speculative reason, a mere ideal, though a faultless one—a conception which perfects and crowns the system of human cognition, but the objective reality of which can neither be proved nor disproved by pure reason. If this defect is ever supplied by a moral theology, the problematic transcendental theology which has preceded, will have been at least serviceable as demonstrating the mental necessity existing for the conception, by the complete determination of it which it has furnished, and the ceaseless testing of the conclusions of a reason often deceived by sense, and not always in harmony with its own ideas. The attributes of necessity, infinitude, unity, existence apart from the world (and not as a world soul), eternity (free from conditions of time), omnipresence (free from conditions of space), omnipotence, and others, are pure transcendental predicates; and thus the accurate conception of a Supreme Being, which every theology requires, is furnished by transcendental theology alone.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0001","text":"### Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason\n\nIf by the term theology I understand the cognition of a primal being, that cognition is based either upon reason alone (theologia rationalis) or upon revelation (theologia revelata). The former cogitates its object either by means of pure transcendental conceptions, as an ens originarium, realissimum, ens entium, and is termed transcendental theology; or, by means of a conception derived from the nature of our own mind, as a supreme intelligence, and must then be entitled natural theology. The person who believes in a transcendental theology alone, is termed a deist; he who acknowledges the possibility of a natural theology also, a theist. The former admits that we can cognize by pure reason alone the existence of a Supreme Being, but at the same time maintains that our conception of this being is purely transcendental, and that all we can say of it is that it possesses all reality, without being able to define it more closely. The second asserts that reason is capable of presenting us, from the analogy with nature, with a more definite conception of this being, and that its operations, as the cause of all things, are the results of intelligence and free will. The former regards the Supreme Being as the cause of the world—whether by the necessity of his nature, or as a free agent, is left undetermined; the latter considers this being as the author of the world.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"80.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0002","text":"Transcendental theology aims either at inferring the existence of a Supreme Being from a general experience, without any closer reference to the world to which this experience belongs, and in this case it is called cosmotheology; or it endeavours to cognize the existence of such a being, through mere conceptions, without the aid of experience, and is then termed ontotheology.\n\nNatural theology infers the attributes and the existence of an author of the world, from the constitution of, the order and unity observable in, the world, in which two modes of causality must be admitted to exist—those of nature and freedom. Thus it rises from this world to a supreme intelligence, either as the principle of all natural, or of all moral order and perfection. In the former case it is termed physico-theology, in the latter, ethical or moral-theology.[[69]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-69)\n\n[[69]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-69) Not theological ethics; for this science contains ethical laws, which presuppose the existence of a Supreme Governor of the world; while moral-theology, on the contrary, is the expression of a conviction of the existence of a Supreme Being, founded upon ethical laws.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"80.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0003","text":"As we are wont to understand by the term God not merely an eternal nature, the operations of which are insensate and blind, but a Supreme Being, who is the free and intelligent author of all things, and as it is this latter view alone that can be of interest to humanity, we might, in strict rigour, deny to the deist any belief in God at all, and regard him merely as a maintainer of the existence of a primal being or thing—the supreme cause of all other things. But, as no one ought to be blamed, merely because he does not feel himself justified in maintaining a certain opinion, as if he altogether denied its truth and asserted the opposite, it is more correct—as it is less harsh—to say, the deist believes in a God, the theist in a living God (summa intelligentia). We shall now proceed to investigate the sources of all these attempts of reason to establish the existence of a Supreme Being.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"80.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","text":"It may be sufficient in this place to define theoretical knowledge or cognition as knowledge of that which is, and practical knowledge as knowledge of that which ought to be. In this view, the theoretical employment of reason is that by which I cognize à priori (as necessary) that something is, while the practical is that by which I cognize à priori what ought to happen. Now, if it is an indubitably certain, though at the same time an entirely conditioned truth, that something is, or ought to happen, either a certain determinate condition of this truth is absolutely necessary, or such a condition may be arbitrarily presupposed. In the former case the condition is postulated (per thesin), in the latter supposed (per hypothesin). There are certain practical laws—those of morality—which are absolutely necessary. Now, if these laws necessarily presuppose the existence of some being, as the condition of the possibility of their obligatory power, this being must be postulated, because the conditioned, from which we reason to this determinate condition, is itself cognized à priori as absolutely necessary. We shall at some future time show that the moral laws not merely presuppose the existence of a Supreme Being, but also, as themselves absolutely necessary in a different relation, demand or postulate it—although only from a practical point of view. The discussion of this argument we postpone for the present.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"80.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","text":"When the question relates merely to that which is, not to that which ought to be, the conditioned which is presented in experience is always cogitated as contingent. For this reason its condition cannot be regarded as absolutely necessary, but merely as relatively necessary, or rather as needful; the condition is in itself and à priori a mere arbitrary presupposition in aid of the cognition, by reason, of the conditioned. If, then, we are to possess a theoretical cognition of the absolute necessity of a thing, we cannot attain to this cognition otherwise than à priori by means of conceptions; while it is impossible in this way to cognize the existence of a cause which bears any relation to an existence given in experience.\n\nTheoretical cognition is speculative when it relates to an object or certain conceptions of an object which is not given and cannot be discovered by means of experience. It is opposed to the cognition of nature, which concerns only those objects or predicates which can be presented in a possible experience.\n\nThe principle that everything which happens (the empirically contingent) must have a cause, is a principle of the cognition of nature, but not of speculative cognition. For, if we change it into an abstract principle, and deprive it of its reference to experience and the empirical, we shall find that it cannot with justice be regarded any longer as a synthetical proposition, and that it is impossible to discover any mode of transition from that which exists to something entirely different—termed cause. Nay, more, the conception of a cause likewise that of the contingent—loses, in this speculative mode of employing it, all significance, for its objective reality and meaning are comprehensible from experience alone.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"80.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","text":"When from the existence of the universe and the things in it the existence of a cause of the universe is inferred, reason is proceeding not in the natural, but in the speculative method. For the principle of the former enounces, not that things themselves or substances, but only that which happens or their states—as empirically contingent, have a cause: the assertion that the existence of substance itself is contingent is not justified by experience, it is the assertion of a reason employing its principles in a speculative manner. If, again, I infer from the form of the universe, from the way in which all things are connected and act and react upon each other, the existence of a cause entirely distinct from the universe—this would again be a judgement of purely speculative reason; because the object in this case—the cause—can never be an object of possible experience. In both these cases the principle of causality, which is valid only in the field of experience—useless and even meaningless beyond this region, would be diverted from its proper destination.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"80.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0007","text":"Now I maintain that all attempts of reason to establish a theology by the aid of speculation alone are fruitless, that the principles of reason as applied to nature do not conduct us to any theological truths, and, consequently, that a rational theology can have no existence, unless it is founded upon the laws of morality. For all synthetical principles of the understanding are valid only as immanent in experience; while the cognition of a Supreme Being necessitates their being employed transcendentally, and of this the understanding is quite incapable. If the empirical law of causality is to conduct us to a Supreme Being, this being must belong to the chain of empirical objects—in which case it would be, like all phenomena, itself conditioned. If the possibility of passing the limits of experience be admitted, by means of the dynamical law of the relation of an effect to its cause, what kind of conception shall we obtain by this procedure? Certainly not the conception of a Supreme Being, because experience never presents us with the greatest of all possible effects, and it is only an effect of this character that could witness to the existence of a corresponding cause. If, for the purpose of fully satisfying the requirements of Reason, we recognize her right to assert the existence of a perfect and absolutely necessary being, this can be admitted only from favour, and cannot be regarded as the result of irresistible demonstration. The physico-theological proof may add weight to others—if other proofs there are—by connecting speculation with experience; but in itself it rather prepares the mind for theological cognition, and gives it a right and natural direction, than establishes a sure foundation for theology.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"80.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0008","text":"It is now perfectly evident that transcendental questions admit only of transcendental answers—those presented à priori by pure conceptions without the least empirical admixture. But the question in the present case is evidently synthetical—it aims at the extension of our cognition beyond the bounds of experience—it requires an assurance respecting the existence of a being corresponding with the idea in our minds, to which no experience can ever be adequate. Now it has been abundantly proved that all à priori synthetical cognition is possible only as the expression of the formal conditions of a possible experience; and that the validity of all principles depends upon their immanence in the field of experience, that is, their relation to objects of empirical cognition or phenomena. Thus all transcendental procedure in reference to speculative theology is without result.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"80.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","text":"If any one prefers doubting the conclusiveness of the proofs of our analytic to losing the persuasion of the validity of these old and time honoured arguments, he at least cannot decline answering the question—how he can pass the limits of all possible experience by the help of mere ideas. If he talks of new arguments, or of improvements upon old arguments, I request him to spare me. There is certainly no great choice in this sphere of discussion, as all speculative arguments must at last look for support to the ontological, and I have, therefore, very little to fear from the argumentative fecundity of the dogmatical defenders of a non-sensuous reason. Without looking upon myself as a remarkably combative person, I shall not decline the challenge to detect the fallacy and destroy the pretensions of every attempt of speculative theology. And yet the hope of better fortune never deserts those who are accustomed to the dogmatical mode of procedure. I shall, therefore, restrict myself to the simple and equitable demand that such reasoners will demonstrate, from the nature of the human mind as well as from that of the other sources of knowledge, how we are to proceed to extend our cognition completely à priori, and to carry it to that point where experience abandons us, and no means exist of guaranteeing the objective reality of our conceptions. In whatever way the understanding may have attained to a conception, the existence of the object of the conception cannot be discovered in it by analysis, because the cognition of the existence of the object depends upon the object’s being posited and given in itself apart from the conception. But it is utterly impossible to go beyond our conception, without the aid of experience—which presents to the mind nothing but phenomena, or to attain by the help of mere conceptions to a conviction of the existence of new kinds of objects or supernatural beings.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"80.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0010","text":"But although pure speculative reason is far from sufficient to demonstrate the existence of a Supreme Being, it is of the highest utility in correcting our conception of this being—on the supposition that we can attain to the cognition of it by some other means—in making it consistent with itself and with all other conceptions of intelligible objects, clearing it from all that is incompatible with the conception of an ens summun, and eliminating from it all limitations or admixtures of empirical elements.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"80.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","text":"Transcendental theology is still therefore, notwithstanding its objective insufficiency, of importance in a negative respect; it is useful as a test of the procedure of reason when engaged with pure ideas, no other than a transcendental standard being in this case admissible. For if, from a practical point of view, the hypothesis of a Supreme and All-sufficient Being is to maintain its validity without opposition, it must be of the highest importance to define this conception in a correct and rigorous manner—as the transcendental conception of a necessary being, to eliminate all phenomenal elements (anthropomorphism in its most extended signification), and at the same time to overflow all contradictory assertions—be they atheistic, deistic, or anthropomorphic. This is of course very easy; as the same arguments which demonstrated the inability of human reason to affirm the existence of a Supreme Being must be alike sufficient to prove the invalidity of its denial. For it is impossible to gain from the pure speculation of reason demonstration that there exists no Supreme Being, as the ground of all that exists, or that this being possesses none of those properties which we regard as analogical with the dynamical qualities of a thinking being, or that, as the anthropomorphists would have us believe, it is subject to all the limitations which sensibility imposes upon those intelligences which exist in the world of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"80.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","text":"A Supreme Being is, therefore, for the speculative reason, a mere ideal, though a faultless one—a conception which perfects and crowns the system of human cognition, but the objective reality of which can neither be proved nor disproved by pure reason. If this defect is ever supplied by a moral theology, the problematic transcendental theology which has preceded, will have been at least serviceable as demonstrating the mental necessity existing for the conception, by the complete determination of it which it has furnished, and the ceaseless testing of the conclusions of a reason often deceived by sense, and not always in harmony with its own ideas. The attributes of necessity, infinitude, unity, existence apart from the world (and not as a world soul), eternity (free from conditions of time), omnipresence (free from conditions of space), omnipotence, and others, are pure transcendental predicates; and thus the accurate conception of a Supreme Being, which every theology requires, is furnished by transcendental theology alone.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"80.12"}],"sha256":"4171202f67e4b055c1a76fa1bd006ea0e4a1e6faeca4f8a6515c6e6c9f27dc57","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2","title":"Appendix","order":81,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_APP2","text":"# Appendix\n\n### APPENDIX. Of the Regulative Employment of the Ideas of Pure Reason\n\nThe result of all the dialectical attempts of pure reason not only confirms the truth of what we have already proved in our Transcendental Analytic, namely, that all inferences which would lead us beyond the limits of experience are fallacious and groundless, but it at the same time teaches us this important lesson, that human reason has a natural inclination to overstep these limits, and that transcendental ideas are as much the natural property of the reason as categories are of the understanding. There exists this difference, however, that while the categories never mislead us, outward objects being always in perfect harmony therewith, ideas are the parents of irresistible illusions, the severest and most subtle criticism being required to save us from the fallacies which they induce.\n\nWhatever is grounded in the nature of our powers will be found to be in harmony with the final purpose and proper employment of these powers, when once we have discovered their true direction and aim. We are entitled to suppose, therefore, that there exists a mode of employing transcendental ideas which is proper and immanent; although, when we mistake their meaning, and regard them as conceptions of actual things, their mode of application is transcendent and delusive. For it is not the idea itself, but only the employment of the idea in relation to possible experience, that is transcendent or immanent. An idea is employed transcendently, when it is applied to an object falsely believed to be adequate with and to correspond to it; immanently, when it is applied solely to the employment of the understanding in the sphere of experience. Thus all errors of subreptio—of misapplication, are to be ascribed to defects of judgement, and not to understanding or reason.\n\nReason never has an immediate relation to an object; it relates immediately to the understanding alone. It is only through the understanding that it can be employed in the field of experience. It does not form conceptions of objects, it merely arranges them and gives to them that unity which they are capable of possessing when the sphere of their application has been extended as widely as possible. Reason avails itself of the conception of the understanding for the sole purpose of producing totality in the different series. This totality the understanding does not concern itself with; its only occupation is the connection of experiences, by which series of conditions in accordance with conceptions are established. The object of reason is, therefore, the understanding and its proper destination. As the latter brings unity into the diversity of objects by means of its conceptions, so the former brings unity into the diversity of conceptions by means of ideas; as it sets the final aim of a collective unity to the operations of the understanding, which without this occupies itself with a distributive unity alone.\n\nI accordingly maintain that transcendental ideas can never be employed as constitutive ideas, that they cannot be conceptions of objects, and that, when thus considered, they assume a fallacious and dialectical character. But, on the other hand, they are capable of an admirable and indispensably necessary application to objects—as regulative ideas, directing the understanding to a certain aim, the guiding lines towards which all its laws follow, and in which they all meet in one point. This point—though a mere idea (focus imaginarius), that is, not a point from which the conceptions of the understanding do really proceed, for it lies beyond the sphere of possible experience—serves, notwithstanding, to give to these conceptions the greatest possible unity combined with the greatest possible extension. Hence arises the natural illusion which induces us to believe that these lines proceed from an object which lies out of the sphere of empirical cognition, just as objects reflected in a mirror appear to be behind it. But this illusion—which we may hinder from imposing upon us—is necessary and unavoidable, if we desire to see, not only those objects which lie before us, but those which are at a great distance behind us; that is to say, when, in the present case, we direct the aims of the understanding, beyond every given experience, towards an extension as great as can possibly be attained.\n\nIf we review our cognitions in their entire extent, we shall find that the peculiar business of reason is to arrange them into a system, that is to say, to give them connection according to a principle. This unity presupposes an idea—the idea of the form of a whole (of cognition), preceding the determinate cognition of the parts, and containing the conditions which determine à priori to every part its place and relation to the other parts of the whole system. This idea, accordingly, demands complete unity in the cognition of the understanding—not the unity of a contingent aggregate, but that of a system connected according to necessary laws. It cannot be affirmed with propriety that this idea is a conception of an object; it is merely a conception of the complete unity of the conceptions of objects, in so far as this unity is available to the understanding as a rule. Such conceptions of reason are not derived from nature; on the contrary, we employ them for the interrogation and investigation of nature, and regard our cognition as defective so long as it is not adequate to them. We admit that such a thing as pure earth, pure water, or pure air, is not to be discovered. And yet we require these conceptions (which have their origin in the reason, so far as regards their absolute purity and completeness) for the purpose of determining the share which each of these natural causes has in every phenomenon. Thus the different kinds of matter are all referred to earths, as mere weight; to salts and inflammable bodies, as pure force; and finally, to water and air, as the vehicula of the former, or the machines employed by them in their operations—for the purpose of explaining the chemical action and reaction of bodies in accordance with the idea of a mechanism. For, although not actually so expressed, the influence of such ideas of reason is very observable in the procedure of natural philosophers.\n\nIf reason is the faculty of deducing the particular from the general, and if the general be certain in se and given, it is only necessary that the judgement should subsume the particular under the general, the particular being thus necessarily determined. I shall term this the demonstrative or apodeictic employment of reason. If, however, the general is admitted as problematical only, and is a mere idea, the particular case is certain, but the universality of the rule which applies to this particular case remains a problem. Several particular cases, the certainty of which is beyond doubt, are then taken and examined, for the purpose of discovering whether the rule is applicable to them; and if it appears that all the particular cases which can be collected follow from the rule, its universality is inferred, and at the same time, all the causes which have not, or cannot be presented to our observation, are concluded to be of the same character with those which we have observed. This I shall term the hypothetical employment of the reason.\n\nThe hypothetical exercise of reason by the aid of ideas employed as problematical conceptions is properly not constitutive. That is to say, if we consider the subject strictly, the truth of the rule, which has been employed as an hypothesis, does not follow from the use that is made of it by reason. For how can we know all the possible cases that may arise? some of which may, however, prove exceptions to the universality of the rule. This employment of reason is merely regulative, and its sole aim is the introduction of unity into the aggregate of our particular cognitions, and thereby the approximating of the rule to universality.\n\nThe object of the hypothetical employment of reason is therefore the systematic unity of cognitions; and this unity is the criterion of the truth of a rule. On the other hand, this systematic unity—as a mere idea—is in fact merely a unity projected, not to be regarded as given, but only in the light of a problem—a problem which serves, however, as a principle for the various and particular exercise of the understanding in experience, directs it with regard to those cases which are not presented to our observation, and introduces harmony and consistency into all its operations.\n\nAll that we can be certain of from the above considerations is that this systematic unity is a logical principle, whose aim is to assist the understanding, where it cannot of itself attain to rules, by means of ideas, to bring all these various rules under one principle, and thus to ensure the most complete consistency and connection that can be attained. But the assertion that objects and the understanding by which they are cognized are so constituted as to be determined to systematic unity, that this may be postulated à priori, without any reference to the interest of reason, and that we are justified in declaring all possible cognitions—empirical and others—to possess systematic unity, and to be subject to general principles from which, notwithstanding their various character, they are all derivable,—such an assertion can be founded only upon a transcendental principle of reason, which would render this systematic unity not subjectively and logically—in its character of a method, but objectively necessary.\n\nWe shall illustrate this by an example. The conceptions of the understanding make us acquainted, among many other kinds of unity, with that of the causality of a substance, which is termed power. The different phenomenal manifestations of the same substance appear at first view to be so very dissimilar that we are inclined to assume the existence of just as many different powers as there are different effects—as, in the case of the human mind, we have feeling, consciousness, imagination, memory, wit, analysis, pleasure, desire and so on. Now we are required by a logical maxim to reduce these differences to as small a number as possible, by comparing them and discovering the hidden identity which exists. We must inquire, for example, whether or not imagination (connected with consciousness), memory, wit, and analysis are not merely different forms of understanding and reason. The idea of a fundamental power, the existence of which no effort of logic can assure us of, is the problem to be solved, for the systematic representation of the existing variety of powers. The logical principle of reason requires us to produce as great a unity as is possible in the system of our cognitions; and the more the phenomena of this and the other power are found to be identical, the more probable does it become, that they are nothing but different manifestations of one and the same power, which may be called, relatively speaking, a fundamental power. And so with other cases.\n\nThese relatively fundamental powers must again be compared with each other, to discover, if possible, the one radical and absolutely fundamental power of which they are but the manifestations. But this unity is purely hypothetical. It is not maintained, that this unity does really exist, but that we must, in the interest of reason, that is, for the establishment of principles for the various rules presented by experience, try to discover and introduce it, so far as is practicable, into the sphere of our cognitions.\n\nBut the transcendental employment of the understanding would lead us to believe that this idea of a fundamental power is not problematical, but that it possesses objective reality, and thus the systematic unity of the various powers or forces in a substance is demanded by the understanding and erected into an apodeictic or necessary principle. For, without having attempted to discover the unity of the various powers existing in nature, nay, even after all our attempts have failed, we notwithstanding presuppose that it does exist, and may be, sooner or later, discovered. And this reason does, not only, as in the case above adduced, with regard to the unity of substance, but where many substances, although all to a certain extent homogeneous, are discoverable, as in the case of matter in general. Here also does reason presuppose the existence of the systematic unity of various powers—inasmuch as particular laws of nature are subordinate to general laws; and parsimony in principles is not merely an economical principle of reason, but an essential law of nature.\n\nWe cannot understand, in fact, how a logical principle of unity can of right exist, unless we presuppose a transcendental principle, by which such a systematic unit—as a property of objects themselves—is regarded as necessary à priori. For with what right can reason, in its logical exercise, require us to regard the variety of forces which nature displays, as in effect a disguised unity, and to deduce them from one fundamental force or power, when she is free to admit that it is just as possible that all forces should be different in kind, and that a systematic unity is not conformable to the design of nature? In this view of the case, reason would be proceeding in direct opposition to her own destination, by setting as an aim an idea which entirely conflicts with the procedure and arrangement of nature. Neither can we assert that reason has previously inferred this unity from the contingent nature of phenomena. For the law of reason which requires us to seek for this unity is a necessary law, inasmuch as without it we should not possess a faculty of reason, nor without reason a consistent and self-accordant mode of employing the understanding, nor, in the absence of this, any proper and sufficient criterion of empirical truth. In relation to this criterion, therefore, we must suppose the idea of the systematic unity of nature to possess objective validity and necessity.\n\nWe find this transcendental presupposition lurking in different forms in the principles of philosophers, although they have neither recognized it nor confessed to themselves its presence. That the diversities of individual things do not exclude identity of species, that the various species must be considered as merely different determinations of a few genera, and these again as divisions of still higher races, and so on—that, accordingly, a certain systematic unity of all possible empirical conceptions, in so far as they can be deduced from higher and more general conceptions, must be sought for, is a scholastic maxim or logical principle, without which reason could not be employed by us. For we can infer the particular from the general, only in so far as general properties of things constitute the foundation upon which the particular rest.\n\nThat the same unity exists in nature is presupposed by philosophers in the well-known scholastic maxim, which forbids us unnecessarily to augment the number of entities or principles (entia praeter necessitatem non esse multiplicanda). This maxim asserts that nature herself assists in the establishment of this unity of reason, and that the seemingly infinite diversity of phenomena should not deter us from the expectation of discovering beneath this diversity a unity of fundamental properties, of which the aforesaid variety is but a more or less determined form. This unity, although a mere idea, thinkers have found it necessary rather to moderate the desire than to encourage it. It was considered a great step when chemists were able to reduce all salts to two main genera—acids and alkalis; and they regard this difference as itself a mere variety, or different manifestation of one and the same fundamental material. The different kinds of earths (stones and even metals) chemists have endeavoured to reduce to three, and afterwards to two; but still, not content with this advance, they cannot but think that behind these diversities there lurks but one genus—nay, that even salts and earths have a common principle. It might be conjectured that this is merely an economical plan of reason, for the purpose of sparing itself trouble, and an attempt of a purely hypothetical character, which, when successful, gives an appearance of probability to the principle of explanation employed by the reason. But a selfish purpose of this kind is easily to be distinguished from the idea, according to which every one presupposes that this unity is in accordance with the laws of nature, and that reason does not in this case request, but requires, although we are quite unable to determine the proper limits of this unity.\n\nIf the diversity existing in phenomena—a diversity not of form (for in this they may be similar) but of content—were so great that the subtlest human reason could never by comparison discover in them the least similarity (which is not impossible), in this case the logical law of genera would be without foundation, the conception of a genus, nay, all general conceptions would be impossible, and the faculty of the understanding, the exercise of which is restricted to the world of conceptions, could not exist. The logical principle of genera, accordingly, if it is to be applied to nature (by which I mean objects presented to our senses), presupposes a transcendental principle. In accordance with this principle, homogeneity is necessarily presupposed in the variety of phenomena (although we are unable to determine à priori the degree of this homogeneity), because without it no empirical conceptions, and consequently no experience, would be possible.\n\nThe logical principle of genera, which demands identity in phenomena, is balanced by another principle—that of species, which requires variety and diversity in things, notwithstanding their accordance in the same genus, and directs the understanding to attend to the one no less than to the other. This principle (of the faculty of distinction) acts as a check upon the reason and reason exhibits in this respect a double and conflicting interest—on the one hand, the interest in the extent (the interest of generality) in relation to genera; on the other, that of the content (the interest of individuality) in relation to the variety of species. In the former case, the understanding cogitates more under its conceptions, in the latter it cogitates more in them. This distinction manifests itself likewise in the habits of thought peculiar to natural philosophers, some of whom—the remarkably speculative heads—may be said to be hostile to heterogeneity in phenomena, and have their eyes always fixed on the unity of genera, while others—with a strong empirical tendency—aim unceasingly at the analysis of phenomena, and almost destroy in us the hope of ever being able to estimate the character of these according to general principles.\n\nThe latter mode of thought is evidently based upon a logical principle, the aim of which is the systematic completeness of all cognitions. This principle authorizes me, beginning at the genus, to descend to the various and diverse contained under it; and in this way extension, as in the former case unity, is assured to the system. For if we merely examine the sphere of the conception which indicates a genus, we cannot discover how far it is possible to proceed in the division of that sphere; just as it is impossible, from the consideration of the space occupied by matter, to determine how far we can proceed in the division of it. Hence every genus must contain different species, and these again different subspecies; and as each of the latter must itself contain a sphere (must be of a certain extent, as a conceptus communis), reason demands that no species or sub-species is to be considered as the lowest possible. For a species or sub-species, being always a conception, which contains only what is common to a number of different things, does not completely determine any individual thing, or relate immediately to it, and must consequently contain other conceptions, that is, other sub-species under it. This law of specification may be thus expressed: entium varietates non temere sunt minuendae.\n\nBut it is easy to see that this logical law would likewise be without sense or application, were it not based upon a transcendental law of specification, which certainly does not require that the differences existing in phenomena should be infinite in number, for the logical principle, which merely maintains the indeterminateness of the logical sphere of a conception, in relation to its possible division, does not authorize this statement; while it does impose upon the understanding the duty of searching for subspecies to every species, and minor differences in every difference. For, were there no lower conceptions, neither could there be any higher. Now the understanding cognizes only by means of conceptions; consequently, how far soever it may proceed in division, never by mere intuition, but always by lower and lower conceptions. The cognition of phenomena in their complete determination (which is possible only by means of the understanding) requires an unceasingly continued specification of conceptions, and a progression to ever smaller differences, of which abstraction had been made in the conception of the species, and still more in that of the genus.\n\nThis law of specification cannot be deduced from experience; it can never present us with a principle of so universal an application. Empirical specification very soon stops in its distinction of diversities, and requires the guidance of the transcendental law, as a principle of the reason—a law which imposes on us the necessity of never ceasing in our search for differences, even although these may not present themselves to the senses. That absorbent earths are of different kinds could only be discovered by obeying the anticipatory law of reason, which imposes upon the understanding the task of discovering the differences existing between these earths, and supposes that nature is richer in substances than our senses would indicate. The faculty of the understanding belongs to us just as much under the presupposition of differences in the objects of nature, as under the condition that these objects are homogeneous, because we could not possess conceptions, nor make any use of our understanding, were not the phenomena included under these conceptions in some respects dissimilar, as well as similar, in their character.\n\nReason thus prepares the sphere of the understanding for the operations of this faculty: 1. By the principle of the homogeneity of the diverse in higher genera; 2. By the principle of the variety of the homogeneous in lower species; and, to complete the systematic unity, it adds, 3. A law of the affinity of all conceptions which prescribes a continuous transition from one species to every other by the gradual increase of diversity. We may term these the principles of the homogeneity, the specification, and the continuity of forms. The latter results from the union of the two former, inasmuch as we regard the systematic connection as complete in thought, in the ascent to higher genera, as well as in the descent to lower species. For all diversities must be related to each other, as they all spring from one highest genus, descending through the different gradations of a more and more extended determination.\n\nWe may illustrate the systematic unity produced by the three logical principles in the following manner. Every conception may be regarded as a point, which, as the standpoint of a spectator, has a certain horizon, which may be said to enclose a number of things that may be viewed, so to speak, from that centre. Within this horizon there must be an infinite number of other points, each of which has its own horizon, smaller and more circumscribed; in other words, every species contains sub-species, according to the principle of specification, and the logical horizon consists of smaller horizons (subspecies), but not of points (individuals), which possess no extent. But different horizons or genera, which include under them so many conceptions, may have one common horizon, from which, as from a mid-point, they may be surveyed; and we may proceed thus, till we arrive at the highest genus, or universal and true horizon, which is determined by the highest conception, and which contains under itself all differences and varieties, as genera, species, and subspecies.\n\nTo this highest standpoint I am conducted by the law of homogeneity, as to all lower and more variously-determined conceptions by the law of specification. Now as in this way there exists no void in the whole extent of all possible conceptions, and as out of the sphere of these the mind can discover nothing, there arises from the presupposition of the universal horizon above mentioned, and its complete division, the principle: Non datur vacuum formarum. This principle asserts that there are not different primitive and highest genera, which stand isolated, so to speak, from each other, but all the various genera are mere divisions and limitations of one highest and universal genus; and hence follows immediately the principle: Datur continuum formarum. This principle indicates that all differences of species limit each other, and do not admit of transition from one to another by a saltus, but only through smaller degrees of the difference between the one species and the other. In one word, there are no species or sub-species which (in the view of reason) are the nearest possible to each other; intermediate species or sub-species being always possible, the difference of which from each of the former is always smaller than the difference existing between these.\n\nThe first law, therefore, directs us to avoid the notion that there exist different primal genera, and enounces the fact of perfect homogeneity; the second imposes a check upon this tendency to unity and prescribes the distinction of sub-species, before proceeding to apply our general conceptions to individuals. The third unites both the former, by enouncing the fact of homogeneity as existing even in the most various diversity, by means of the gradual transition from one species to another. Thus it indicates a relationship between the different branches or species, in so far as they all spring from the same stem.\n\nBut this logical law of the continuum specierum (formarum logicarum) presupposes a transcendental principle (lex continui in natura), without which the understanding might be led into error, by following the guidance of the former, and thus perhaps pursuing a path contrary to that prescribed by nature. This law must, consequently, be based upon pure transcendental, and not upon empirical, considerations. For, in the latter case, it would come later than the system; whereas it is really itself the parent of all that is systematic in our cognition of nature. These principles are not mere hypotheses employed for the purpose of experimenting upon nature; although when any such connection is discovered, it forms a solid ground for regarding the hypothetical unity as valid in the sphere of nature—and thus they are in this respect not without their use. But we go farther, and maintain that it is manifest that these principles of parsimony in fundamental causes, variety in effects, and affinity in phenomena, are in accordance both with reason and nature, and that they are not mere methods or plans devised for the purpose of assisting us in our observation of the external world.\n\nBut it is plain that this continuity of forms is a mere idea, to which no adequate object can be discovered in experience. And this for two reasons. First, because the species in nature are really divided, and hence form quanta discreta; and, if the gradual progression through their affinity were continuous, the intermediate members lying between two given species must be infinite in number, which is impossible. Secondly, because we cannot make any determinate empirical use of this law, inasmuch as it does not present us with any criterion of affinity which could aid us in determining how far we ought to pursue the graduation of differences: it merely contains a general indication that it is our duty to seek for and, if possible, to discover them.\n\nWhen we arrange these principles of systematic unity in the order conformable to their employment in experience, they will stand thus: Variety, Affinity, Unity, each of them, as ideas, being taken in the highest degree of their completeness. Reason presupposes the existence of cognitions of the understanding, which have a direct relation to experience, and aims at the ideal unity of these cognitions—a unity which far transcends all experience or empirical notions. The affinity of the diverse, notwithstanding the differences existing between its parts, has a relation to things, but a still closer one to the mere properties and powers of things. For example, imperfect experience may represent the orbits of the planets as circular. But we discover variations from this course, and we proceed to suppose that the planets revolve in a path which, if not a circle, is of a character very similar to it. That is to say, the movements of those planets which do not form a circle will approximate more or less to the properties of a circle, and probably form an ellipse. The paths of comets exhibit still greater variations, for, so far as our observation extends, they do not return upon their own course in a circle or ellipse. But we proceed to the conjecture that comets describe a parabola, a figure which is closely allied to the ellipse. In fact, a parabola is merely an ellipse, with its longer axis produced to an indefinite extent. Thus these principles conduct us to a unity in the genera of the forms of these orbits, and, proceeding farther, to a unity as regards the cause of the motions of the heavenly bodies—that is, gravitation. But we go on extending our conquests over nature, and endeavour to explain all seeming deviations from these rules, and even make additions to our system which no experience can ever substantiate—for example, the theory, in affinity with that of ellipses, of hyperbolic paths of comets, pursuing which, these bodies leave our solar system and, passing from sun to sun, unite the most distant parts of the infinite universe, which is held together by the same moving power.\n\nThe most remarkable circumstance connected with these principles is that they seem to be transcendental, and, although only containing ideas for the guidance of the empirical exercise of reason, and although this empirical employment stands to these ideas in an asymptotic relation alone (to use a mathematical term), that is, continually approximate, without ever being able to attain to them, they possess, notwithstanding, as à priori synthetical propositions, objective though undetermined validity, and are available as rules for possible experience. In the elaboration of our experience, they may also be employed with great advantage, as heuristic[[70]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-70) principles. A transcendental deduction of them cannot be made; such a deduction being always impossible in the case of ideas, as has been already shown.\n\n[[70]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-70) From the Greek, eurhioko.\n\nWe distinguished, in the Transcendental Analytic, the dynamical principles of the understanding, which are regulative principles of intuition, from the mathematical, which are constitutive principles of intuition. These dynamical laws are, however, constitutive in relation to experience, inasmuch as they render the conceptions without which experience could not exist possible à priori. But the principles of pure reason cannot be constitutive even in regard to empirical conceptions, because no sensuous schema corresponding to them can be discovered, and they cannot therefore have an object in concreto. Now, if I grant that they cannot be employed in the sphere of experience, as constitutive principles, how shall I secure for them employment and objective validity as regulative principles, and in what way can they be so employed?\n\nThe understanding is the object of reason, as sensibility is the object of the understanding. The production of systematic unity in all the empirical operations of the understanding is the proper occupation of reason; just as it is the business of the understanding to connect the various content of phenomena by means of conceptions, and subject them to empirical laws. But the operations of the understanding are, without the schemata of sensibility, undetermined; and, in the same manner, the unity of reason is perfectly undetermined as regards the conditions under which, and the extent to which, the understanding ought to carry the systematic connection of its conceptions. But, although it is impossible to discover in intuition a schema for the complete systematic unity of all the conceptions of the understanding, there must be some analogon of this schema. This analogon is the idea of the maximum of the division and the connection of our cognition in one principle. For we may have a determinate notion of a maximum and an absolutely perfect, all the restrictive conditions which are connected with an indeterminate and various content having been abstracted. Thus the idea of reason is analogous with a sensuous schema, with this difference, that the application of the categories to the schema of reason does not present a cognition of any object (as is the case with the application of the categories to sensuous schemata), but merely provides us with a rule or principle for the systematic unity of the exercise of the understanding. Now, as every principle which imposes upon the exercise of the understanding à priori compliance with the rule of systematic unity also relates, although only in an indirect manner, to an object of experience, the principles of pure reason will also possess objective reality and validity in relation to experience. But they will not aim at determining our knowledge in regard to any empirical object; they will merely indicate the procedure, following which the empirical and determinate exercise of the understanding may be in complete harmony and connection with itself—a result which is produced by its being brought into harmony with the principle of systematic unity, so far as that is possible, and deduced from it.\n\nI term all subjective principles, which are not derived from observation of the constitution of an object, but from the interest which Reason has in producing a certain completeness in her cognition of that object, maxims of reason. Thus there are maxims of speculative reason, which are based solely upon its speculative interest, although they appear to be objective principles.\n\nWhen principles which are really regulative are regarded as constitutive, and employed as objective principles, contradictions must arise; but if they are considered as mere maxims, there is no room for contradictions of any kind, as they then merely indicate the different interests of reason, which occasion differences in the mode of thought. In effect, Reason has only one single interest, and the seeming contradiction existing between her maxims merely indicates a difference in, and a reciprocal limitation of, the methods by which this interest is satisfied.\n\nThis reasoner has at heart the interest of diversity—in accordance with the principle of specification; another, the interest of unity—in accordance with the principle of aggregation. Each believes that his judgement rests upon a thorough insight into the subject he is examining, and yet it has been influenced solely by a greater or less degree of adherence to some one of the two principles, neither of which are objective, but originate solely from the interest of reason, and on this account to be termed maxims rather than principles. When I observe intelligent men disputing about the distinctive characteristics of men, animals, or plants, and even of minerals, those on the one side assuming the existence of certain national characteristics, certain well-defined and hereditary distinctions of family, race, and so on, while the other side maintain that nature has endowed all races of men with the same faculties and dispositions, and that all differences are but the result of external and accidental circumstances—I have only to consider for a moment the real nature of the subject of discussion, to arrive at the conclusion that it is a subject far too deep for us to judge of, and that there is little probability of either party being able to speak from a perfect insight into and understanding of the nature of the subject itself. Both have, in reality, been struggling for the twofold interest of reason; the one maintaining the one interest, the other the other. But this difference between the maxims of diversity and unity may easily be reconciled and adjusted; although, so long as they are regarded as objective principles, they must occasion not only contradictions and polemic, but place hinderances in the way of the advancement of truth, until some means is discovered of reconciling these conflicting interests, and bringing reason into union and harmony with itself.\n\nThe same is the case with the so-called law discovered by Leibnitz, and supported with remarkable ability by Bonnet—the law of the continuous gradation of created beings, which is nothing more than an inference from the principle of affinity; for observation and study of the order of nature could never present it to the mind as an objective truth. The steps of this ladder, as they appear in experience, are too far apart from each other, and the so-called petty differences between different kinds of animals are in nature commonly so wide separations that no confidence can be placed in such views (particularly when we reflect on the great variety of things, and the ease with which we can discover resemblances), and no faith in the laws which are said to express the aims and purposes of nature. On the other hand, the method of investigating the order of nature in the light of this principle, and the maxim which requires us to regard this order—it being still undetermined how far it extends—as really existing in nature, is beyond doubt a legitimate and excellent principle of reason—a principle which extends farther than any experience or observation of ours and which, without giving us any positive knowledge of anything in the region of experience, guides us to the goal of systematic unity.\n\n_Of the Ultimate End of the Natural Dialectic of Human Reason._\n\nThe ideas of pure reason cannot be, of themselves and in their own nature, dialectical; it is from their misemployment alone that fallacies and illusions arise. For they originate in the nature of reason itself, and it is impossible that this supreme tribunal for all the rights and claims of speculation should be itself undeserving of confidence and promotive of error. It is to be expected, therefore, that these ideas have a genuine and legitimate aim. It is true, the mob of sophists raise against reason the cry of inconsistency and contradiction, and affect to despise the government of that faculty, because they cannot understand its constitution, while it is to its beneficial influences alone that they owe the position and the intelligence which enable them to criticize and to blame its procedure.\n\nWe cannot employ an à priori conception with certainty, until we have made a transcendental deduction therefore. The ideas of pure reason do not admit of the same kind of deduction as the categories. But if they are to possess the least objective validity, and to represent anything but mere creations of thought (entia rationis ratiocinantis), a deduction of them must be possible. This deduction will complete the critical task imposed upon pure reason; and it is to this part Of our labours that we now proceed.\n\nThere is a great difference between a thing’s being presented to the mind as an object in an absolute sense, or merely as an ideal object. In the former case I employ my conceptions to determine the object; in the latter case nothing is present to the mind but a mere schema, which does not relate directly to an object, not even in a hypothetical sense, but which is useful only for the purpose of representing other objects to the mind, in a mediate and indirect manner, by means of their relation to the idea in the intellect. Thus I say the conception of a supreme intelligence is a mere idea; that is to say, its objective reality does not consist in the fact that it has an immediate relation to an object (for in this sense we have no means of establishing its objective validity), it is merely a schema constructed according to the necessary conditions of the unity of reason—the schema of a thing in general, which is useful towards the production of the highest degree of systematic unity in the empirical exercise of reason, in which we deduce this or that object of experience from the imaginary object of this idea, as the ground or cause of the said object of experience. In this way, the idea is properly a heuristic, and not an ostensive, conception; it does not give us any information respecting the constitution of an object, it merely indicates how, under the guidance of the idea, we ought to investigate the constitution and the relations of objects in the world of experience. Now, if it can be shown that the three kinds of transcendental ideas (psychological, cosmological, and theological), although not relating directly to any object nor determining it, do nevertheless, on the supposition of the existence of an ideal object, produce systematic unity in the laws of the empirical employment of the reason, and extend our empirical cognition, without ever being inconsistent or in opposition with it—it must be a necessary maxim of reason to regulate its procedure according to these ideas. And this forms the transcendental deduction of all speculative ideas, not as constitutive principles of the extension of our cognition beyond the limits of our experience, but as regulative principles of the systematic unity of empirical cognition, which is by the aid of these ideas arranged and emended within its own proper limits, to an extent unattainable by the operation of the principles of the understanding alone.\n\nI shall make this plainer. Guided by the principles involved in these ideas, we must, in the first place, so connect all the phenomena, actions, and feelings of the mind, as if it were a simple substance, which, endowed with personal identity, possesses a permanent existence (in this life at least), while its states, among which those of the body are to be included as external conditions, are in continual change. Secondly, in cosmology, we must investigate the conditions of all natural phenomena, internal as well as external, as if they belonged to a chain infinite and without any prime or supreme member, while we do not, on this account, deny the existence of intelligible grounds of these phenomena, although we never employ them to explain phenomena, for the simple reason that they are not objects of our cognition. Thirdly, in the sphere of theology, we must regard the whole system of possible experience as forming an absolute, but dependent and sensuously-conditioned unity, and at the same time as based upon a sole, supreme, and all-sufficient ground existing apart from the world itself—a ground which is a self-subsistent, primeval and creative reason, in relation to which we so employ our reason in the field of experience, as if all objects drew their origin from that archetype of all reason. In other words, we ought not to deduce the internal phenomena of the mind from a simple thinking substance, but deduce them from each other under the guidance of the regulative idea of a simple being; we ought not to deduce the phenomena, order, and unity of the universe from a supreme intelligence, but merely draw from this idea of a supremely wise cause the rules which must guide reason in its connection of causes and effects.\n\nNow there is nothing to hinder us from admitting these ideas to possess an objective and hyperbolic existence, except the cosmological ideas, which lead reason into an antinomy: the psychological and theological ideas are not antinomial. They contain no contradiction; and how, then, can any one dispute their objective reality, since he who denies it knows as little about their possibility as we who affirm? And yet, when we wish to admit the existence of a thing, it is not sufficient to convince ourselves that there is no positive obstacle in the way; for it cannot be allowable to regard mere creations of thought, which transcend, though they do not contradict, all our conceptions, as real and determinate objects, solely upon the authority of a speculative reason striving to compass its own aims. They cannot, therefore, be admitted to be real in themselves; they can only possess a comparative reality—that of a schema of the regulative principle of the systematic unity of all cognition. They are to be regarded not as actual things, but as in some measure analogous to them. We abstract from the object of the idea all the conditions which limit the exercise of our understanding, but which, on the other hand, are the sole conditions of our possessing a determinate conception of any given thing. And thus we cogitate a something, of the real nature of which we have not the least conception, but which we represent to ourselves as standing in a relation to the whole system of phenomena, analogous to that in which phenomena stand to each other.\n\nBy admitting these ideal beings, we do not really extend our cognitions beyond the objects of possible experience; we extend merely the empirical unity of our experience, by the aid of systematic unity, the schema of which is furnished by the idea, which is therefore valid—not as a constitutive, but as a regulative principle. For although we posit a thing corresponding to the idea—a something, an actual existence—we do not on that account aim at the extension of our cognition by means of transcendent conceptions. This existence is purely ideal, and not objective; it is the mere expression of the systematic unity which is to be the guide of reason in the field of experience. There are no attempts made at deciding what the ground of this unity may be, or what the real nature of this imaginary being.\n\nThus the transcendental and only determinate conception of God, which is presented to us by speculative reason, is in the strictest sense deistic. In other words, reason does not assure us of the objective validity of the conception; it merely gives us the idea of something, on which the supreme and necessary unity of all experience is based. This something we cannot, following the analogy of a real substance, cogitate otherwise than as the cause of all things operating in accordance with rational laws, if we regard it as an individual object; although we should rest contented with the idea alone as a regulative principle of reason, and make no attempt at completing the sum of the conditions imposed by thought. This attempt is, indeed, inconsistent with the grand aim of complete systematic unity in the sphere of cognition—a unity to which no bounds are set by reason.\n\nHence it happens that, admitting a divine being, I can have no conception of the internal possibility of its perfection, or of the necessity of its existence. The only advantage of this admission is that it enables me to answer all other questions relating to the contingent, and to give reason the most complete satisfaction as regards the unity which it aims at attaining in the world of experience. But I cannot satisfy reason with regard to this hypothesis itself; and this proves that it is not its intelligence and insight into the subject, but its speculative interest alone which induces it to proceed from a point lying far beyond the sphere of our cognition, for the purpose of being able to consider all objects as parts of a systematic whole.\n\nHere a distinction presents itself, in regard to the way in which we may cogitate a presupposition—a distinction which is somewhat subtle, but of great importance in transcendental philosophy. I may have sufficient grounds to admit something, or the existence of something, in a relative point of view (suppositio relativa), without being justified in admitting it in an absolute sense (suppositio absoluta). This distinction is undoubtedly requisite, in the case of a regulative principle, the necessity of which we recognize, though we are ignorant of the source and cause of that necessity, and which we assume to be based upon some ultimate ground, for the purpose of being able to cogitate the universality of the principle in a more determinate way. For example, I cogitate the existence of a being corresponding to a pure transcendental idea. But I cannot admit that this being exists absolutely and in itself, because all of the conceptions by which I can cogitate an object in a determinate manner fall short of assuring me of its existence; nay, the conditions of the objective validity of my conceptions are excluded by the idea—by the very fact of its being an idea. The conceptions of reality, substance, causality, nay, even that of necessity in existence, have no significance out of the sphere of empirical cognition, and cannot, beyond that sphere, determine any object. They may, accordingly, be employed to explain the possibility of things in the world of sense, but they are utterly inadequate to explain the possibility of the universe itself considered as a whole; because in this case the ground of explanation must lie out of and beyond the world, and cannot, therefore, be an object of possible experience. Now, I may admit the existence of an incomprehensible being of this nature—the object of a mere idea, relatively to the world of sense; although I have no ground to admit its existence absolutely and in itself. For if an idea (that of a systematic and complete unity, of which I shall presently speak more particularly) lies at the foundation of the most extended empirical employment of reason, and if this idea cannot be adequately represented in concreto, although it is indispensably necessary for the approximation of empirical unity to the highest possible degree—I am not only authorized, but compelled, to realize this idea, that is, to posit a real object corresponding thereto. But I cannot profess to know this object; it is to me merely a something, to which, as the ground of systematic unity in cognition, I attribute such properties as are analogous to the conceptions employed by the understanding in the sphere of experience. Following the analogy of the notions of reality, substance, causality, and necessity, I cogitate a being, which possesses all these attributes in the highest degree; and, as this idea is the offspring of my reason alone, I cogitate this being as self-subsistent reason, and as the cause of the universe operating by means of ideas of the greatest possible harmony and unity. Thus I abstract all conditions that would limit my idea, solely for the purpose of rendering systematic unity possible in the world of empirical diversity, and thus securing the widest possible extension for the exercise of reason in that sphere. This I am enabled to do, by regarding all connections and relations in the world of sense, as if they were the dispositions of a supreme reason, of which our reason is but a faint image. I then proceed to cogitate this Supreme Being by conceptions which have, properly, no meaning or application, except in the world of sense. But as I am authorized to employ the transcendental hypothesis of such a being in a relative respect alone, that is, as the substratum of the greatest possible unity in experience—I may attribute to a being which I regard as distinct from the world, such properties as belong solely to the sphere of sense and experience. For I do not desire, and am not justified in desiring, to cognize this object of my idea, as it exists in itself; for I possess no conceptions sufficient for this task, those of reality, substance, causality, nay, even that of necessity in existence, losing all significance, and becoming merely the signs of conceptions, without content and without applicability, when I attempt to carry them beyond the limits of the world of sense. I cogitate merely the relation of a perfectly unknown being to the greatest possible systematic unity of experience, solely for the purpose of employing it as the schema of the regulative principle which directs reason in its empirical exercise.\n\nIt is evident, at the first view, that we cannot presuppose the reality of this transcendental object, by means of the conceptions of reality, substance, causality, and so on, because these conceptions cannot be applied to anything that is distinct from the world of sense. Thus the supposition of a Supreme Being or cause is purely relative; it is cogitated only in behalf of the systematic unity of experience; such a being is but a something, of whose existence in itself we have not the least conception. Thus, too, it becomes sufficiently manifest why we required the idea of a necessary being in relation to objects given by sense, although we can never have the least conception of this being, or of its absolute necessity.\n\nAnd now we can clearly perceive the result of our transcendental dialectic, and the proper aim of the ideas of pure reason—which become dialectical solely from misunderstanding and inconsiderateness. Pure reason is, in fact, occupied with itself, and not with any object. Objects are not presented to it to be embraced in the unity of an empirical conception; it is only the cognitions of the understanding that are presented to it, for the purpose of receiving the unity of a rational conception, that is, of being connected according to a principle. The unity of reason is the unity of system; and this systematic unity is not an objective principle, extending its dominion over objects, but a subjective maxim, extending its authority over the empirical cognition of objects. The systematic connection which reason gives to the empirical employment of the understanding not only advances the extension of that employment, but ensures its correctness, and thus the principle of a systematic unity of this nature is also objective, although only in an indefinite respect (principium vagum). It is not, however, a constitutive principle, determining an object to which it directly relates; it is merely a regulative principle or maxim, advancing and strengthening the empirical exercise of reason, by the opening up of new paths of which the understanding is ignorant, while it never conflicts with the laws of its exercise in the sphere of experience.\n\nBut reason cannot cogitate this systematic unity, without at the same time cogitating an object of the idea—an object that cannot be presented in any experience, which contains no concrete example of a complete systematic unity. This being (ens rationis ratiocinatae) is therefore a mere idea and is not assumed to be a thing which is real absolutely and in itself. On the contrary, it forms merely the problematical foundation of the connection which the mind introduces among the phenomena of the sensuous world. We look upon this connection, in the light of the above-mentioned idea, as if it drew its origin from the supposed being which corresponds to the idea. And yet all we aim at is the possession of this idea as a secure foundation for the systematic unity of experience—a unity indispensable to reason, advantageous to the understanding, and promotive of the interests of empirical cognition.\n\nWe mistake the true meaning of this idea when we regard it as an enouncement, or even as a hypothetical declaration of the existence of a real thing, which we are to regard as the origin or ground of a systematic constitution of the universe. On the contrary, it is left completely undetermined what the nature or properties of this so-called ground may be. The idea is merely to be adopted as a point of view, from which this unity, so essential to reason and so beneficial to the understanding, may be regarded as radiating. In one word, this transcendental thing is merely the schema of a regulative principle, by means of which Reason, so far as in her lies, extends the dominion of systematic unity over the whole sphere of experience.\n\nThe first object of an idea of this kind is the ego, considered merely as a thinking nature or soul. If I wish to investigate the properties of a thinking being, I must interrogate experience. But I find that I can apply none of the categories to this object, the schema of these categories, which is the condition of their application, being given only in sensuous intuition. But I cannot thus attain to the cognition of a systematic unity of all the phenomena of the internal sense. Instead, therefore, of an empirical conception of what the soul really is, reason takes the conception of the empirical unity of all thought, and, by cogitating this unity as unconditioned and primitive, constructs the rational conception or idea of a simple substance which is in itself unchangeable, possessing personal identity, and in connection with other real things external to it; in one word, it constructs the idea of a simple self-subsistent intelligence. But the real aim of reason in this procedure is the attainment of principles of systematic unity for the explanation of the phenomena of the soul. That is, reason desires to be able to represent all the determinations of the internal sense as existing in one subject, all powers as deduced from one fundamental power, all changes as mere varieties in the condition of a being which is permanent and always the same, and all phenomena in space as entirely different in their nature from the procedure of thought. Essential simplicity (with the other attributes predicated of the ego) is regarded as the mere schema of this regulative principle; it is not assumed that it is the actual ground of the properties of the soul. For these properties may rest upon quite different grounds, of which we are completely ignorant; just as the above predicates could not give us any knowledge of the soul as it is in itself, even if we regarded them as valid in respect of it, inasmuch as they constitute a mere idea, which cannot be represented in concreto. Nothing but good can result from a psychological idea of this kind, if we only take proper care not to consider it as more than an idea; that is, if we regard it as valid merely in relation to the employment of reason, in the sphere of the phenomena of the soul. Under the guidance of this idea, or principle, no empirical laws of corporeal phenomena are called in to explain that which is a phenomenon of the internal sense alone; no windy hypotheses of the generation, annihilation, and palingenesis of souls are admitted. Thus the consideration of this object of the internal sense is kept pure, and unmixed with heterogeneous elements; while the investigation of reason aims at reducing all the grounds of explanation employed in this sphere of knowledge to a single principle. All this is best effected, nay, cannot be effected otherwise than by means of such a schema, which requires us to regard this ideal thing as an actual existence. The psychological idea is, therefore, meaningless and inapplicable, except as the schema of a regulative conception. For, if I ask whether the soul is not really of a spiritual nature—it is a question which has no meaning. From such a conception has been abstracted, not merely all corporeal nature, but all nature, that is, all the predicates of a possible experience; and consequently, all the conditions which enable us to cogitate an object to this conception have disappeared. But, if these conditions are absent, it is evident that the conception is meaningless.\n\nThe second regulative idea of speculative reason is the conception of the universe. For nature is properly the only object presented to us, in regard to which reason requires regulative principles. Nature is twofold—thinking and corporeal nature. To cogitate the latter in regard to its internal possibility, that is, to determine the application of the categories to it, no idea is required—no representation which transcends experience. In this sphere, therefore, an idea is impossible, sensuous intuition being our only guide; while, in the sphere of psychology, we require the fundamental idea (I), which contains à priori a certain form of thought namely, the unity of the ego. Pure reason has, therefore, nothing left but nature in general, and the completeness of conditions in nature in accordance with some principle. The absolute totality of the series of these conditions is an idea, which can never be fully realized in the empirical exercise of reason, while it is serviceable as a rule for the procedure of reason in relation to that totality. It requires us, in the explanation of given phenomena (in the regress or ascent in the series), to proceed as if the series were infinite in itself, that is, were prolonged in indefinitum; while on the other hand, where reason is regarded as itself the determining cause (in the region of freedom), we are required to proceed as if we had not before us an object of sense, but of the pure understanding. In this latter case, the conditions do not exist in the series of phenomena, but may be placed quite out of and beyond it, and the series of conditions may be regarded as if it had an absolute beginning from an intelligible cause. All this proves that the cosmological ideas are nothing but regulative principles, and not constitutive; and that their aim is not to realize an actual totality in such series. The full discussion of this subject will be found in its proper place in the chapter on the antinomy of pure reason.\n\nThe third idea of pure reason, containing the hypothesis of a being which is valid merely as a relative hypothesis, is that of the one and all-sufficient cause of all cosmological series, in other words, the idea of God. We have not the slightest ground absolutely to admit the existence of an object corresponding to this idea; for what can empower or authorize us to affirm the existence of a being of the highest perfection—a being whose existence is absolutely necessary—merely because we possess the conception of such a being? The answer is: It is the existence of the world which renders this hypothesis necessary. But this answer makes it perfectly evident that the idea of this being, like all other speculative ideas, is essentially nothing more than a demand upon reason that it shall regulate the connection which it and its subordinate faculties introduce into the phenomena of the world by principles of systematic unity and, consequently, that it shall regard all phenomena as originating from one all-embracing being, as the supreme and all-sufficient cause. From this it is plain that the only aim of reason in this procedure is the establishment of its own formal rule for the extension of its dominion in the world of experience; that it does not aim at an extension of its cognition beyond the limits of experience; and that, consequently, this idea does not contain any constitutive principle.\n\nThe highest formal unity, which is based upon ideas alone, is the unity of all things—a unity in accordance with an aim or purpose; and the speculative interest of reason renders it necessary to regard all order in the world as if it originated from the intention and design of a supreme reason. This principle unfolds to the view of reason in the sphere of experience new and enlarged prospects, and invites it to connect the phenomena of the world according to teleological laws, and in this way to attain to the highest possible degree of systematic unity. The hypothesis of a supreme intelligence, as the sole cause of the universe—an intelligence which has for us no more than an ideal existence—is accordingly always of the greatest service to reason. Thus, if we presuppose, in relation to the figure of the earth (which is round, but somewhat flattened at the poles),[[71]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-71) or that of mountains or seas, wise designs on the part of an author of the universe, we cannot fail to make, by the light of this supposition, a great number of interesting discoveries. If we keep to this hypothesis, as a principle which is purely regulative, even error cannot be very detrimental. For, in this case, error can have no more serious consequences than that, where we expected to discover a teleological connection (nexus finalis), only a mechanical or physical connection appears. In such a case, we merely fail to find the additional form of unity we expected, but we do not lose the rational unity which the mind requires in its procedure in experience. But even a miscarriage of this sort cannot affect the law in its general and teleological relations. For although we may convict an anatomist of an error, when he connects the limb of some animal with a certain purpose, it is quite impossible to prove in a single case that any arrangement of nature, be it what it may, is entirely without aim or design. And thus medical physiology, by the aid of a principle presented to it by pure reason, extends its very limited empirical knowledge of the purposes of the different parts of an organized body so far that it may be asserted with the utmost confidence, and with the approbation of all reflecting men, that every organ or bodily part of an animal has its use and answers a certain design. Now, this is a supposition which, if regarded as of a constitutive character, goes much farther than any experience or observation of ours can justify. Hence it is evident that it is nothing more than a regulative principle of reason, which aims at the highest degree of systematic unity, by the aid of the idea of a causality according to design in a supreme cause—a cause which it regards as the highest intelligence.\n\n[[71]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-71) The advantages which a circular form, in the case of the earth, has over every other, are well known. But few are aware that the slight flattening at the poles, which gives it the figure of a spheroid, is the only cause which prevents the elevations of continents or even of mountains, perhaps thrown up by some internal convulsion, from continually altering the position of the axis of the earth—and that to some considerable degree in a short time. The great protuberance of the earth under the Equator serves to overbalance the impetus of all other masses of earth, and thus to preserve the axis of the earth, so far as we can observe, in its present position. And yet this wise arrangement has been unthinkingly explained from the equilibrium of the formerly fluid mass.\n\nIf, however, we neglect this restriction of the idea to a purely regulative influence, reason is betrayed into numerous errors. For it has then left the ground of experience, in which alone are to be found the criteria of truth, and has ventured into the region of the incomprehensible and unsearchable, on the heights of which it loses its power and collectedness, because it has completely severed its connection with experience.\n\nThe first error which arises from our employing the idea of a Supreme Being as a constitutive (in repugnance to the very nature of an idea), and not as a regulative principle, is the error of inactive reason (ignava ratio).[[72]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-72) We may so term every principle which requires us to regard our investigations of nature as absolutely complete, and allows reason to cease its inquiries, as if it had fully executed its task. Thus the psychological idea of the ego, when employed as a constitutive principle for the explanation of the phenomena of the soul, and for the extension of our knowledge regarding this subject beyond the limits of experience—even to the condition of the soul after death—is convenient enough for the purposes of pure reason, but detrimental and even ruinous to its interests in the sphere of nature and experience. The dogmatizing spiritualist explains the unchanging unity of our personality through all changes of condition from the unity of a thinking substance, the interest which we take in things and events that can happen only after our death, from a consciousness of the immaterial nature of our thinking subject, and so on. Thus he dispenses with all empirical investigations into the cause of these internal phenomena, and with all possible explanations of them upon purely natural grounds; while, at the dictation of a transcendent reason, he passes by the immanent sources of cognition in experience, greatly to his own ease and convenience, but to the sacrifice of all, genuine insight and intelligence. These prejudicial consequences become still more evident, in the case of the dogmatical treatment of our idea of a Supreme Intelligence, and the theological system of nature (physico-theology) which is falsely based upon it. For, in this case, the aims which we observe in nature, and often those which we merely fancy to exist, make the investigation of causes a very easy task, by directing us to refer such and such phenomena immediately to the unsearchable will and counsel of the Supreme Wisdom, while we ought to investigate their causes in the general laws of the mechanism of matter. We are thus recommended to consider the labour of reason as ended, when we have merely dispensed with its employment, which is guided surely and safely only by the order of nature and the series of changes in the world—which are arranged according to immanent and general laws. This error may be avoided, if we do not merely consider from the view-point of final aims certain parts of nature, such as the division and structure of a continent, the constitution and direction of certain mountain-chains, or even the organization existing in the vegetable and animal kingdoms, but look upon this systematic unity of nature in a perfectly general way, in relation to the idea of a Supreme Intelligence. If we pursue this advice, we lay as a foundation for all investigation the conformity to aims of all phenomena of nature in accordance with universal laws, for which no particular arrangement of nature is exempt, but only cognized by us with more or less difficulty; and we possess a regulative principle of the systematic unity of a teleological connection, which we do not attempt to anticipate or predetermine. All that we do, and ought to do, is to follow out the physico-mechanical connection in nature according to general laws, with the hope of discovering, sooner or later, the teleological connection also. Thus, and thus only, can the principle of final unity aid in the extension of the employment of reason in the sphere of experience, without being in any case detrimental to its interests.\n\n[[72]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-72) This was the term applied by the old dialecticians to a sophistical argument, which ran thus: If it is your fate to die of this disease, you will die, whether you employ a physician or not. Cicero says that this mode of reasoning has received this appellation, because, if followed, it puts an end to the employment of reason in the affairs of life. For a similar reason, I have applied this designation to the sophistical argument of pure reason.\n\nThe second error which arises from the misconception of the principle of systematic unity is that of perverted reason (perversa ratio, usteron roteron rationis). The idea of systematic unity is available as a regulative principle in the connection of phenomena according to general natural laws; and, how far soever we have to travel upon the path of experience to discover some fact or event, this idea requires us to believe that we have approached all the more nearly to the completion of its use in the sphere of nature, although that completion can never be attained. But this error reverses the procedure of reason. We begin by hypostatizing the principle of systematic unity, and by giving an anthropomorphic determination to the conception of a Supreme Intelligence, and then proceed forcibly to impose aims upon nature. Thus not only does teleology, which ought to aid in the completion of unity in accordance with general laws, operate to the destruction of its influence, but it hinders reason from attaining its proper aim, that is, the proof, upon natural grounds, of the existence of a supreme intelligent cause. For, if we cannot presuppose supreme finality in nature à priori, that is, as essentially belonging to nature, how can we be directed to endeavour to discover this unity and, rising gradually through its different degrees, to approach the supreme perfection of an author of all—a perfection which is absolutely necessary, and therefore cognizable à priori? The regulative principle directs us to presuppose systematic unity absolutely and, consequently, as following from the essential nature of things—but only as a unity of nature, not merely cognized empirically, but presupposed à priori, although only in an indeterminate manner. But if I insist on basing nature upon the foundation of a supreme ordaining Being, the unity of nature is in effect lost. For, in this case, it is quite foreign and unessential to the nature of things, and cannot be cognized from the general laws of nature. And thus arises a vicious circular argument, what ought to have been proved having been presupposed.\n\nTo take the regulative principle of systematic unity in nature for a constitutive principle, and to hypostatize and make a cause out of that which is properly the ideal ground of the consistent and harmonious exercise of reason, involves reason in inextricable embarrassments. The investigation of nature pursues its own path under the guidance of the chain of natural causes, in accordance with the general laws of nature, and ever follows the light of the idea of an author of the universe—not for the purpose of deducing the finality, which it constantly pursues, from this Supreme Being, but to attain to the cognition of his existence from the finality which it seeks in the existence of the phenomena of nature, and, if possible, in that of all things to cognize this being, consequently, as absolutely necessary. Whether this latter purpose succeed or not, the idea is and must always be a true one, and its employment, when merely regulative, must always be accompanied by truthful and beneficial results.\n\nComplete unity, in conformity with aims, constitutes absolute perfection. But if we do not find this unity in the nature of the things which go to constitute the world of experience, that is, of objective cognition, consequently in the universal and necessary laws of nature, how can we infer from this unity the idea of the supreme and absolutely necessary perfection of a primal being, which is the origin of all causality? The greatest systematic unity, and consequently teleological unity, constitutes the very foundation of the possibility of the most extended employment of human reason. The idea of unity is therefore essentially and indissolubly connected with the nature of our reason. This idea is a legislative one; and hence it is very natural that we should assume the existence of a legislative reason corresponding to it, from which the systematic unity of nature—the object of the operations of reason—must be derived.\n\nIn the course of our discussion of the antinomies, we stated that it is always possible to answer all the questions which pure reason may raise; and that the plea of the limited nature of our cognition, which is unavoidable and proper in many questions regarding natural phenomena, cannot in this case be admitted, because the questions raised do not relate to the nature of things, but are necessarily originated by the nature of reason itself, and relate to its own internal constitution. We can now establish this assertion, which at first sight appeared so rash, in relation to the two questions in which reason takes the greatest interest, and thus complete our discussion of the dialectic of pure reason.\n\nIf, then, the question is asked, in relation to transcendental theology,[[73]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-73) first, whether there is anything distinct from the world, which contains the ground of cosmical order and connection according to general laws? The answer is: Certainly. For the world is a sum of phenomena; there must, therefore, be some transcendental basis of these phenomena, that is, a basis cogitable by the pure understanding alone. If, secondly, the question is asked whether this being is substance, whether it is of the greatest reality, whether it is necessary, and so forth? I answer that this question is utterly without meaning. For all the categories which aid me in forming a conception of an object cannot be employed except in the world of sense, and are without meaning when not applied to objects of actual or possible experience. Out of this sphere, they are not properly conceptions, but the mere marks or indices of conceptions, which we may admit, although they cannot, without the help of experience, help us to understand any subject or thing. If, thirdly, the question is whether we may not cogitate this being, which is distinct from the world, in analogy with the objects of experience? The answer is: Undoubtedly, but only as an ideal, and not as a real object. That is, we must cogitate it only as an unknown substratum of the systematic unity, order, and finality of the world—a unity which reason must employ as the regulative principle of its investigation of nature. Nay, more, we may admit into the idea certain anthropomorphic elements, which are promotive of the interests of this regulative principle. For it is no more than an idea, which does not relate directly to a being distinct from the world, but to the regulative principle of the systematic unity of the world, by means, however, of a schema of this unity—the schema of a Supreme Intelligence, who is the wisely-designing author of the universe. What this basis of cosmical unity may be in itself, we know not—we cannot discover from the idea; we merely know how we ought to employ the idea of this unity, in relation to the systematic operation of reason in the sphere of experience.\n\n[[73]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-73) After what has been said of the psychological idea of the ego and its proper employment as a regulative principle of the operations of reason, I need not enter into details regarding the transcendental illusion by which the systematic unity of all the various phenomena of the internal sense is hypostatized. The procedure is in this case very similar to that which has been discussed in our remarks on the theological ideal.\n\nBut, it will be asked again, can we on these grounds, admit the existence of a wise and omnipotent author of the world? Without doubt; and not only so, but we must assume the existence of such a being. But do we thus extend the limits of our knowledge beyond the field of possible experience? By no means. For we have merely presupposed a something, of which we have no conception, which we do not know as it is in itself; but, in relation to the systematic disposition of the universe, which we must presuppose in all our observation of nature, we have cogitated this unknown being in analogy with an intelligent existence (an empirical conception), that is to say, we have endowed it with those attributes, which, judging from the nature of our own reason, may contain the ground of such a systematic unity. This idea is therefore valid only relatively to the employment in experience of our reason. But if we attribute to it absolute and objective validity, we overlook the fact that it is merely an ideal being that we cogitate; and, by setting out from a basis which is not determinable by considerations drawn from experience, we place ourselves in a position which incapacitates us from applying this principle to the empirical employment of reason.\n\nBut, it will be asked further, can I make any use of this conception and hypothesis in my investigations into the world and nature? Yes, for this very purpose was the idea established by reason as a fundamental basis. But may I regard certain arrangements, which seemed to have been made in conformity with some fixed aim, as the arrangements of design, and look upon them as proceeding from the divine will, with the intervention, however, of certain other particular arrangements disposed to that end? Yes, you may do so; but at the same time you must regard it as indifferent, whether it is asserted that divine wisdom has disposed all things in conformity with his highest aims, or that the idea of supreme wisdom is a regulative principle in the investigation of nature, and at the same time a principle of the systematic unity of nature according to general laws, even in those cases where we are unable to discover that unity. In other words, it must be perfectly indifferent to you whether you say, when you have discovered this unity: God has wisely willed it so; or: Nature has wisely arranged this. For it was nothing but the systematic unity, which reason requires as a basis for the investigation of nature, that justified you in accepting the idea of a supreme intelligence as a schema for a regulative principle; and, the farther you advance in the discovery of design and finality, the more certain the validity of your idea. But, as the whole aim of this regulative principle was the discovery of a necessary and systematic unity in nature, we have, in so far as we attain this, to attribute our success to the idea of a Supreme Being; while, at the same time, we cannot, without involving ourselves in contradictions, overlook the general laws of nature, as it was in reference to them alone that this idea was employed. We cannot, I say, overlook the general laws of nature, and regard this conformity to aims observable in nature as contingent or hyperphysical in its origin; inasmuch as there is no ground which can justify us in the admission of a being with such properties distinct from and above nature. All that we are authorized to assert is that this idea may be employed as a principle, and that the properties of the being which is assumed to correspond to it may be regarded as systematically connected in analogy with the causal determination of phenomena.\n\nFor the same reasons we are justified in introducing into the idea of the supreme cause other anthropomorphic elements (for without these we could not predicate anything of it); we may regard it as allowable to cogitate this cause as a being with understanding, the feelings of pleasure and displeasure, and faculties of desire and will corresponding to these. At the same time, we may attribute to this being infinite perfection—a perfection which necessarily transcends that which our knowledge of the order and design in the world authorize us to predicate of it. For the regulative law of systematic unity requires us to study nature on the supposition that systematic and final unity in infinitum is everywhere discoverable, even in the highest diversity. For, although we may discover little of this cosmical perfection, it belongs to the legislative prerogative of reason to require us always to seek for and to expect it; while it must always be beneficial to institute all inquiries into nature in accordance with this principle. But it is evident that, by this idea of a supreme author of all, which I place as the foundation of all inquiries into nature, I do not mean to assert the existence of such a being, or that I have any knowledge of its existence; and, consequently, I do not really deduce anything from the existence of this being, but merely from its idea, that is to say, from the nature of things in this world, in accordance with this idea. A certain dim consciousness of the true use of this idea seems to have dictated to the philosophers of all times the moderate language used by them regarding the cause of the world. We find them employing the expressions wisdom and care of nature, and divine wisdom, as synonymous—nay, in purely speculative discussions, preferring the former, because it does not carry the appearance of greater pretensions than such as we are entitled to make, and at the same time directs reason to its proper field of action—nature and her phenomena.\n\nThus, pure reason, which at first seemed to promise us nothing less than the extension of our cognition beyond the limits of experience, is found, when thoroughly examined, to contain nothing but regulative principles, the virtue and function of which is to introduce into our cognition a higher degree of unity than the understanding could of itself. These principles, by placing the goal of all our struggles at so great a distance, realize for us the most thorough connection between the different parts of our cognition, and the highest degree of systematic unity. But, on the other hand, if misunderstood and employed as constitutive principles of transcendent cognition, they become the parents of illusions and contradictions, while pretending to introduce us to new regions of knowledge.\n\nThus all human cognition begins with intuitions, proceeds from thence to conceptions, and ends with ideas. Although it possesses, in relation to all three elements, à priori sources of cognition, which seemed to transcend the limits of all experience, a thoroughgoing criticism demonstrates that speculative reason can never, by the aid of these elements, pass the bounds of possible experience, and that the proper destination of this highest faculty of cognition is to employ all methods, and all the principles of these methods, for the purpose of penetrating into the innermost secrets of nature, by the aid of the principles of unity (among all kinds of which teleological unity is the highest), while it ought not to attempt to soar above the sphere of experience, beyond which there lies nought for us but the void inane. The critical examination, in our Transcendental Analytic, of all the propositions which professed to extend cognition beyond the sphere of experience, completely demonstrated that they can only conduct us to a possible experience. If we were not distrustful even of the clearest abstract theorems, if we were not allured by specious and inviting prospects to escape from the constraining power of their evidence, we might spare ourselves the laborious examination of all the dialectical arguments which a transcendent reason adduces in support of its pretensions; for we should know with the most complete certainty that, however honest such professions might be, they are null and valueless, because they relate to a kind of knowledge to which no man can by any possibility attain. But, as there is no end to discussion, if we cannot discover the true cause of the illusions by which even the wisest are deceived, and as the analysis of all our transcendent cognition into its elements is of itself of no slight value as a psychological study, while it is a duty incumbent on every philosopher—it was found necessary to investigate the dialectical procedure of reason in its primary sources. And as the inferences of which this dialectic is the parent are not only deceitful, but naturally possess a profound interest for humanity, it was advisable at the same time, to give a full account of the momenta of this dialectical procedure, and to deposit it in the archives of human reason, as a warning to all future metaphysicians to avoid these causes of speculative error.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0001","text":"# Appendix\n\n### APPENDIX. Of the Regulative Employment of the Ideas of Pure Reason\n\nThe result of all the dialectical attempts of pure reason not only confirms the truth of what we have already proved in our Transcendental Analytic, namely, that all inferences which would lead us beyond the limits of experience are fallacious and groundless, but it at the same time teaches us this important lesson, that human reason has a natural inclination to overstep these limits, and that transcendental ideas are as much the natural property of the reason as categories are of the understanding. There exists this difference, however, that while the categories never mislead us, outward objects being always in perfect harmony therewith, ideas are the parents of irresistible illusions, the severest and most subtle criticism being required to save us from the fallacies which they induce.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"81.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","text":"Whatever is grounded in the nature of our powers will be found to be in harmony with the final purpose and proper employment of these powers, when once we have discovered their true direction and aim. We are entitled to suppose, therefore, that there exists a mode of employing transcendental ideas which is proper and immanent; although, when we mistake their meaning, and regard them as conceptions of actual things, their mode of application is transcendent and delusive. For it is not the idea itself, but only the employment of the idea in relation to possible experience, that is transcendent or immanent. An idea is employed transcendently, when it is applied to an object falsely believed to be adequate with and to correspond to it; immanently, when it is applied solely to the employment of the understanding in the sphere of experience. Thus all errors of subreptio—of misapplication, are to be ascribed to defects of judgement, and not to understanding or reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"81.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0003","text":"Reason never has an immediate relation to an object; it relates immediately to the understanding alone. It is only through the understanding that it can be employed in the field of experience. It does not form conceptions of objects, it merely arranges them and gives to them that unity which they are capable of possessing when the sphere of their application has been extended as widely as possible. Reason avails itself of the conception of the understanding for the sole purpose of producing totality in the different series. This totality the understanding does not concern itself with; its only occupation is the connection of experiences, by which series of conditions in accordance with conceptions are established. The object of reason is, therefore, the understanding and its proper destination. As the latter brings unity into the diversity of objects by means of its conceptions, so the former brings unity into the diversity of conceptions by means of ideas; as it sets the final aim of a collective unity to the operations of the understanding, which without this occupies itself with a distributive unity alone.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"81.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","text":"I accordingly maintain that transcendental ideas can never be employed as constitutive ideas, that they cannot be conceptions of objects, and that, when thus considered, they assume a fallacious and dialectical character. But, on the other hand, they are capable of an admirable and indispensably necessary application to objects—as regulative ideas, directing the understanding to a certain aim, the guiding lines towards which all its laws follow, and in which they all meet in one point. This point—though a mere idea (focus imaginarius), that is, not a point from which the conceptions of the understanding do really proceed, for it lies beyond the sphere of possible experience—serves, notwithstanding, to give to these conceptions the greatest possible unity combined with the greatest possible extension. Hence arises the natural illusion which induces us to believe that these lines proceed from an object which lies out of the sphere of empirical cognition, just as objects reflected in a mirror appear to be behind it. But this illusion—which we may hinder from imposing upon us—is necessary and unavoidable, if we desire to see, not only those objects which lie before us, but those which are at a great distance behind us; that is to say, when, in the present case, we direct the aims of the understanding, beyond every given experience, towards an extension as great as can possibly be attained.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"81.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0005","text":"If we review our cognitions in their entire extent, we shall find that the peculiar business of reason is to arrange them into a system, that is to say, to give them connection according to a principle. This unity presupposes an idea—the idea of the form of a whole (of cognition), preceding the determinate cognition of the parts, and containing the conditions which determine à priori to every part its place and relation to the other parts of the whole system. This idea, accordingly, demands complete unity in the cognition of the understanding—not the unity of a contingent aggregate, but that of a system connected according to necessary laws. It cannot be affirmed with propriety that this idea is a conception of an object; it is merely a conception of the complete unity of the conceptions of objects, in so far as this unity is available to the understanding as a rule. Such conceptions of reason are not derived from nature; on the contrary, we employ them for the interrogation and investigation of nature, and regard our cognition as defective so long as it is not adequate to them. We admit that such a thing as pure earth, pure water, or pure air, is not to be discovered. And yet we require these conceptions (which have their origin in the reason, so far as regards their absolute purity and completeness) for the purpose of determining the share which each of these natural causes has in every phenomenon. Thus the different kinds of matter are all referred to earths, as mere weight; to salts and inflammable bodies, as pure force; and finally, to water and air, as the vehicula of the former, or the machines employed by them in their operations—for the purpose of explaining the chemical action and reaction of bodies in accordance with the idea of a mechanism. For, although not actually so expressed, the influence of such ideas of reason is very observable in the procedure of natural philosophers.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"81.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0006","text":"If reason is the faculty of deducing the particular from the general, and if the general be certain in se and given, it is only necessary that the judgement should subsume the particular under the general, the particular being thus necessarily determined. I shall term this the demonstrative or apodeictic employment of reason. If, however, the general is admitted as problematical only, and is a mere idea, the particular case is certain, but the universality of the rule which applies to this particular case remains a problem. Several particular cases, the certainty of which is beyond doubt, are then taken and examined, for the purpose of discovering whether the rule is applicable to them; and if it appears that all the particular cases which can be collected follow from the rule, its universality is inferred, and at the same time, all the causes which have not, or cannot be presented to our observation, are concluded to be of the same character with those which we have observed. This I shall term the hypothetical employment of the reason.\n\nThe hypothetical exercise of reason by the aid of ideas employed as problematical conceptions is properly not constitutive. That is to say, if we consider the subject strictly, the truth of the rule, which has been employed as an hypothesis, does not follow from the use that is made of it by reason. For how can we know all the possible cases that may arise? some of which may, however, prove exceptions to the universality of the rule. This employment of reason is merely regulative, and its sole aim is the introduction of unity into the aggregate of our particular cognitions, and thereby the approximating of the rule to universality.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"81.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0007","text":"The object of the hypothetical employment of reason is therefore the systematic unity of cognitions; and this unity is the criterion of the truth of a rule. On the other hand, this systematic unity—as a mere idea—is in fact merely a unity projected, not to be regarded as given, but only in the light of a problem—a problem which serves, however, as a principle for the various and particular exercise of the understanding in experience, directs it with regard to those cases which are not presented to our observation, and introduces harmony and consistency into all its operations.\n\nAll that we can be certain of from the above considerations is that this systematic unity is a logical principle, whose aim is to assist the understanding, where it cannot of itself attain to rules, by means of ideas, to bring all these various rules under one principle, and thus to ensure the most complete consistency and connection that can be attained. But the assertion that objects and the understanding by which they are cognized are so constituted as to be determined to systematic unity, that this may be postulated à priori, without any reference to the interest of reason, and that we are justified in declaring all possible cognitions—empirical and others—to possess systematic unity, and to be subject to general principles from which, notwithstanding their various character, they are all derivable,—such an assertion can be founded only upon a transcendental principle of reason, which would render this systematic unity not subjectively and logically—in its character of a method, but objectively necessary.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"81.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0008","text":"We shall illustrate this by an example. The conceptions of the understanding make us acquainted, among many other kinds of unity, with that of the causality of a substance, which is termed power. The different phenomenal manifestations of the same substance appear at first view to be so very dissimilar that we are inclined to assume the existence of just as many different powers as there are different effects—as, in the case of the human mind, we have feeling, consciousness, imagination, memory, wit, analysis, pleasure, desire and so on. Now we are required by a logical maxim to reduce these differences to as small a number as possible, by comparing them and discovering the hidden identity which exists. We must inquire, for example, whether or not imagination (connected with consciousness), memory, wit, and analysis are not merely different forms of understanding and reason. The idea of a fundamental power, the existence of which no effort of logic can assure us of, is the problem to be solved, for the systematic representation of the existing variety of powers. The logical principle of reason requires us to produce as great a unity as is possible in the system of our cognitions; and the more the phenomena of this and the other power are found to be identical, the more probable does it become, that they are nothing but different manifestations of one and the same power, which may be called, relatively speaking, a fundamental power. And so with other cases.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"81.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0009","text":"These relatively fundamental powers must again be compared with each other, to discover, if possible, the one radical and absolutely fundamental power of which they are but the manifestations. But this unity is purely hypothetical. It is not maintained, that this unity does really exist, but that we must, in the interest of reason, that is, for the establishment of principles for the various rules presented by experience, try to discover and introduce it, so far as is practicable, into the sphere of our cognitions.\n\nBut the transcendental employment of the understanding would lead us to believe that this idea of a fundamental power is not problematical, but that it possesses objective reality, and thus the systematic unity of the various powers or forces in a substance is demanded by the understanding and erected into an apodeictic or necessary principle. For, without having attempted to discover the unity of the various powers existing in nature, nay, even after all our attempts have failed, we notwithstanding presuppose that it does exist, and may be, sooner or later, discovered. And this reason does, not only, as in the case above adduced, with regard to the unity of substance, but where many substances, although all to a certain extent homogeneous, are discoverable, as in the case of matter in general. Here also does reason presuppose the existence of the systematic unity of various powers—inasmuch as particular laws of nature are subordinate to general laws; and parsimony in principles is not merely an economical principle of reason, but an essential law of nature.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"81.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0010","text":"We cannot understand, in fact, how a logical principle of unity can of right exist, unless we presuppose a transcendental principle, by which such a systematic unit—as a property of objects themselves—is regarded as necessary à priori. For with what right can reason, in its logical exercise, require us to regard the variety of forces which nature displays, as in effect a disguised unity, and to deduce them from one fundamental force or power, when she is free to admit that it is just as possible that all forces should be different in kind, and that a systematic unity is not conformable to the design of nature? In this view of the case, reason would be proceeding in direct opposition to her own destination, by setting as an aim an idea which entirely conflicts with the procedure and arrangement of nature. Neither can we assert that reason has previously inferred this unity from the contingent nature of phenomena. For the law of reason which requires us to seek for this unity is a necessary law, inasmuch as without it we should not possess a faculty of reason, nor without reason a consistent and self-accordant mode of employing the understanding, nor, in the absence of this, any proper and sufficient criterion of empirical truth. In relation to this criterion, therefore, we must suppose the idea of the systematic unity of nature to possess objective validity and necessity.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"81.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0011","text":"We find this transcendental presupposition lurking in different forms in the principles of philosophers, although they have neither recognized it nor confessed to themselves its presence. That the diversities of individual things do not exclude identity of species, that the various species must be considered as merely different determinations of a few genera, and these again as divisions of still higher races, and so on—that, accordingly, a certain systematic unity of all possible empirical conceptions, in so far as they can be deduced from higher and more general conceptions, must be sought for, is a scholastic maxim or logical principle, without which reason could not be employed by us. For we can infer the particular from the general, only in so far as general properties of things constitute the foundation upon which the particular rest.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"81.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0012","text":"That the same unity exists in nature is presupposed by philosophers in the well-known scholastic maxim, which forbids us unnecessarily to augment the number of entities or principles (entia praeter necessitatem non esse multiplicanda). This maxim asserts that nature herself assists in the establishment of this unity of reason, and that the seemingly infinite diversity of phenomena should not deter us from the expectation of discovering beneath this diversity a unity of fundamental properties, of which the aforesaid variety is but a more or less determined form. This unity, although a mere idea, thinkers have found it necessary rather to moderate the desire than to encourage it. It was considered a great step when chemists were able to reduce all salts to two main genera—acids and alkalis; and they regard this difference as itself a mere variety, or different manifestation of one and the same fundamental material. The different kinds of earths (stones and even metals) chemists have endeavoured to reduce to three, and afterwards to two; but still, not content with this advance, they cannot but think that behind these diversities there lurks but one genus—nay, that even salts and earths have a common principle. It might be conjectured that this is merely an economical plan of reason, for the purpose of sparing itself trouble, and an attempt of a purely hypothetical character, which, when successful, gives an appearance of probability to the principle of explanation employed by the reason. But a selfish purpose of this kind is easily to be distinguished from the idea, according to which every one presupposes that this unity is in accordance with the laws of nature, and that reason does not in this case request, but requires, although we are quite unable to determine the proper limits of this unity.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"81.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0013","text":"If the diversity existing in phenomena—a diversity not of form (for in this they may be similar) but of content—were so great that the subtlest human reason could never by comparison discover in them the least similarity (which is not impossible), in this case the logical law of genera would be without foundation, the conception of a genus, nay, all general conceptions would be impossible, and the faculty of the understanding, the exercise of which is restricted to the world of conceptions, could not exist. The logical principle of genera, accordingly, if it is to be applied to nature (by which I mean objects presented to our senses), presupposes a transcendental principle. In accordance with this principle, homogeneity is necessarily presupposed in the variety of phenomena (although we are unable to determine à priori the degree of this homogeneity), because without it no empirical conceptions, and consequently no experience, would be possible.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"81.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0014","text":"The logical principle of genera, which demands identity in phenomena, is balanced by another principle—that of species, which requires variety and diversity in things, notwithstanding their accordance in the same genus, and directs the understanding to attend to the one no less than to the other. This principle (of the faculty of distinction) acts as a check upon the reason and reason exhibits in this respect a double and conflicting interest—on the one hand, the interest in the extent (the interest of generality) in relation to genera; on the other, that of the content (the interest of individuality) in relation to the variety of species. In the former case, the understanding cogitates more under its conceptions, in the latter it cogitates more in them. This distinction manifests itself likewise in the habits of thought peculiar to natural philosophers, some of whom—the remarkably speculative heads—may be said to be hostile to heterogeneity in phenomena, and have their eyes always fixed on the unity of genera, while others—with a strong empirical tendency—aim unceasingly at the analysis of phenomena, and almost destroy in us the hope of ever being able to estimate the character of these according to general principles.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"81.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0015","text":"The latter mode of thought is evidently based upon a logical principle, the aim of which is the systematic completeness of all cognitions. This principle authorizes me, beginning at the genus, to descend to the various and diverse contained under it; and in this way extension, as in the former case unity, is assured to the system. For if we merely examine the sphere of the conception which indicates a genus, we cannot discover how far it is possible to proceed in the division of that sphere; just as it is impossible, from the consideration of the space occupied by matter, to determine how far we can proceed in the division of it. Hence every genus must contain different species, and these again different subspecies; and as each of the latter must itself contain a sphere (must be of a certain extent, as a conceptus communis), reason demands that no species or sub-species is to be considered as the lowest possible. For a species or sub-species, being always a conception, which contains only what is common to a number of different things, does not completely determine any individual thing, or relate immediately to it, and must consequently contain other conceptions, that is, other sub-species under it. This law of specification may be thus expressed: entium varietates non temere sunt minuendae.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"81.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0016","text":"But it is easy to see that this logical law would likewise be without sense or application, were it not based upon a transcendental law of specification, which certainly does not require that the differences existing in phenomena should be infinite in number, for the logical principle, which merely maintains the indeterminateness of the logical sphere of a conception, in relation to its possible division, does not authorize this statement; while it does impose upon the understanding the duty of searching for subspecies to every species, and minor differences in every difference. For, were there no lower conceptions, neither could there be any higher. Now the understanding cognizes only by means of conceptions; consequently, how far soever it may proceed in division, never by mere intuition, but always by lower and lower conceptions. The cognition of phenomena in their complete determination (which is possible only by means of the understanding) requires an unceasingly continued specification of conceptions, and a progression to ever smaller differences, of which abstraction had been made in the conception of the species, and still more in that of the genus.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"81.16"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0017","text":"This law of specification cannot be deduced from experience; it can never present us with a principle of so universal an application. Empirical specification very soon stops in its distinction of diversities, and requires the guidance of the transcendental law, as a principle of the reason—a law which imposes on us the necessity of never ceasing in our search for differences, even although these may not present themselves to the senses. That absorbent earths are of different kinds could only be discovered by obeying the anticipatory law of reason, which imposes upon the understanding the task of discovering the differences existing between these earths, and supposes that nature is richer in substances than our senses would indicate. The faculty of the understanding belongs to us just as much under the presupposition of differences in the objects of nature, as under the condition that these objects are homogeneous, because we could not possess conceptions, nor make any use of our understanding, were not the phenomena included under these conceptions in some respects dissimilar, as well as similar, in their character.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"81.17"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0018","text":"Reason thus prepares the sphere of the understanding for the operations of this faculty: 1. By the principle of the homogeneity of the diverse in higher genera; 2. By the principle of the variety of the homogeneous in lower species; and, to complete the systematic unity, it adds, 3. A law of the affinity of all conceptions which prescribes a continuous transition from one species to every other by the gradual increase of diversity. We may term these the principles of the homogeneity, the specification, and the continuity of forms. The latter results from the union of the two former, inasmuch as we regard the systematic connection as complete in thought, in the ascent to higher genera, as well as in the descent to lower species. For all diversities must be related to each other, as they all spring from one highest genus, descending through the different gradations of a more and more extended determination.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"81.18"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0019","text":"We may illustrate the systematic unity produced by the three logical principles in the following manner. Every conception may be regarded as a point, which, as the standpoint of a spectator, has a certain horizon, which may be said to enclose a number of things that may be viewed, so to speak, from that centre. Within this horizon there must be an infinite number of other points, each of which has its own horizon, smaller and more circumscribed; in other words, every species contains sub-species, according to the principle of specification, and the logical horizon consists of smaller horizons (subspecies), but not of points (individuals), which possess no extent. But different horizons or genera, which include under them so many conceptions, may have one common horizon, from which, as from a mid-point, they may be surveyed; and we may proceed thus, till we arrive at the highest genus, or universal and true horizon, which is determined by the highest conception, and which contains under itself all differences and varieties, as genera, species, and subspecies.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"81.19"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0020","text":"To this highest standpoint I am conducted by the law of homogeneity, as to all lower and more variously-determined conceptions by the law of specification. Now as in this way there exists no void in the whole extent of all possible conceptions, and as out of the sphere of these the mind can discover nothing, there arises from the presupposition of the universal horizon above mentioned, and its complete division, the principle: Non datur vacuum formarum. This principle asserts that there are not different primitive and highest genera, which stand isolated, so to speak, from each other, but all the various genera are mere divisions and limitations of one highest and universal genus; and hence follows immediately the principle: Datur continuum formarum. This principle indicates that all differences of species limit each other, and do not admit of transition from one to another by a saltus, but only through smaller degrees of the difference between the one species and the other. In one word, there are no species or sub-species which (in the view of reason) are the nearest possible to each other; intermediate species or sub-species being always possible, the difference of which from each of the former is always smaller than the difference existing between these.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"81.20"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0021","text":"The first law, therefore, directs us to avoid the notion that there exist different primal genera, and enounces the fact of perfect homogeneity; the second imposes a check upon this tendency to unity and prescribes the distinction of sub-species, before proceeding to apply our general conceptions to individuals. The third unites both the former, by enouncing the fact of homogeneity as existing even in the most various diversity, by means of the gradual transition from one species to another. Thus it indicates a relationship between the different branches or species, in so far as they all spring from the same stem.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"81.21"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0022","text":"But this logical law of the continuum specierum (formarum logicarum) presupposes a transcendental principle (lex continui in natura), without which the understanding might be led into error, by following the guidance of the former, and thus perhaps pursuing a path contrary to that prescribed by nature. This law must, consequently, be based upon pure transcendental, and not upon empirical, considerations. For, in the latter case, it would come later than the system; whereas it is really itself the parent of all that is systematic in our cognition of nature. These principles are not mere hypotheses employed for the purpose of experimenting upon nature; although when any such connection is discovered, it forms a solid ground for regarding the hypothetical unity as valid in the sphere of nature—and thus they are in this respect not without their use. But we go farther, and maintain that it is manifest that these principles of parsimony in fundamental causes, variety in effects, and affinity in phenomena, are in accordance both with reason and nature, and that they are not mere methods or plans devised for the purpose of assisting us in our observation of the external world.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"81.22"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0023","text":"But it is plain that this continuity of forms is a mere idea, to which no adequate object can be discovered in experience. And this for two reasons. First, because the species in nature are really divided, and hence form quanta discreta; and, if the gradual progression through their affinity were continuous, the intermediate members lying between two given species must be infinite in number, which is impossible. Secondly, because we cannot make any determinate empirical use of this law, inasmuch as it does not present us with any criterion of affinity which could aid us in determining how far we ought to pursue the graduation of differences: it merely contains a general indication that it is our duty to seek for and, if possible, to discover them.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"81.23"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0024","text":"When we arrange these principles of systematic unity in the order conformable to their employment in experience, they will stand thus: Variety, Affinity, Unity, each of them, as ideas, being taken in the highest degree of their completeness. Reason presupposes the existence of cognitions of the understanding, which have a direct relation to experience, and aims at the ideal unity of these cognitions—a unity which far transcends all experience or empirical notions. The affinity of the diverse, notwithstanding the differences existing between its parts, has a relation to things, but a still closer one to the mere properties and powers of things. For example, imperfect experience may represent the orbits of the planets as circular. But we discover variations from this course, and we proceed to suppose that the planets revolve in a path which, if not a circle, is of a character very similar to it. That is to say, the movements of those planets which do not form a circle will approximate more or less to the properties of a circle, and probably form an ellipse. The paths of comets exhibit still greater variations, for, so far as our observation extends, they do not return upon their own course in a circle or ellipse. But we proceed to the conjecture that comets describe a parabola, a figure which is closely allied to the ellipse. In fact, a parabola is merely an ellipse, with its longer axis produced to an indefinite extent. Thus these principles conduct us to a unity in the genera of the forms of these orbits, and, proceeding farther, to a unity as regards the cause of the motions of the heavenly bodies—that is, gravitation. But we go on extending our conquests over nature, and endeavour to explain all seeming deviations from these rules, and even make additions to our system which no experience can ever substantiate—for example, the theory, in affinity with that of ellipses, of hyperbolic paths of comets, pursuing which, these bodies leave our solar system and, passing from sun to sun, unite the most distant parts of the infinite universe, which is held together by the same moving power.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"81.24"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0025","text":"The most remarkable circumstance connected with these principles is that they seem to be transcendental, and, although only containing ideas for the guidance of the empirical exercise of reason, and although this empirical employment stands to these ideas in an asymptotic relation alone (to use a mathematical term), that is, continually approximate, without ever being able to attain to them, they possess, notwithstanding, as à priori synthetical propositions, objective though undetermined validity, and are available as rules for possible experience. In the elaboration of our experience, they may also be employed with great advantage, as heuristic[[70]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-70) principles. A transcendental deduction of them cannot be made; such a deduction being always impossible in the case of ideas, as has been already shown.\n\n[[70]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-70) From the Greek, eurhioko.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"81.25"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0026","text":"We distinguished, in the Transcendental Analytic, the dynamical principles of the understanding, which are regulative principles of intuition, from the mathematical, which are constitutive principles of intuition. These dynamical laws are, however, constitutive in relation to experience, inasmuch as they render the conceptions without which experience could not exist possible à priori. But the principles of pure reason cannot be constitutive even in regard to empirical conceptions, because no sensuous schema corresponding to them can be discovered, and they cannot therefore have an object in concreto. Now, if I grant that they cannot be employed in the sphere of experience, as constitutive principles, how shall I secure for them employment and objective validity as regulative principles, and in what way can they be so employed?","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"81.26"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0027","text":"The understanding is the object of reason, as sensibility is the object of the understanding. The production of systematic unity in all the empirical operations of the understanding is the proper occupation of reason; just as it is the business of the understanding to connect the various content of phenomena by means of conceptions, and subject them to empirical laws. But the operations of the understanding are, without the schemata of sensibility, undetermined; and, in the same manner, the unity of reason is perfectly undetermined as regards the conditions under which, and the extent to which, the understanding ought to carry the systematic connection of its conceptions. But, although it is impossible to discover in intuition a schema for the complete systematic unity of all the conceptions of the understanding, there must be some analogon of this schema. This analogon is the idea of the maximum of the division and the connection of our cognition in one principle. For we may have a determinate notion of a maximum and an absolutely perfect, all the restrictive conditions which are connected with an indeterminate and various content having been abstracted. Thus the idea of reason is analogous with a sensuous schema, with this difference, that the application of the categories to the schema of reason does not present a cognition of any object (as is the case with the application of the categories to sensuous schemata), but merely provides us with a rule or principle for the systematic unity of the exercise of the understanding. Now, as every principle which imposes upon the exercise of the understanding à priori compliance with the rule of systematic unity also relates, although only in an indirect manner, to an object of experience, the principles of pure reason will also possess objective reality and validity in relation to experience. But they will not aim at determining our knowledge in regard to any empirical object; they will merely indicate the procedure, following which the empirical and determinate exercise of the understanding may be in complete harmony and connection with itself—a result which is produced by its being brought into harmony with the principle of systematic unity, so far as that is possible, and deduced from it.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"81.27"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0028","text":"I term all subjective principles, which are not derived from observation of the constitution of an object, but from the interest which Reason has in producing a certain completeness in her cognition of that object, maxims of reason. Thus there are maxims of speculative reason, which are based solely upon its speculative interest, although they appear to be objective principles.\n\nWhen principles which are really regulative are regarded as constitutive, and employed as objective principles, contradictions must arise; but if they are considered as mere maxims, there is no room for contradictions of any kind, as they then merely indicate the different interests of reason, which occasion differences in the mode of thought. In effect, Reason has only one single interest, and the seeming contradiction existing between her maxims merely indicates a difference in, and a reciprocal limitation of, the methods by which this interest is satisfied.","kind":"passage","sequence":28,"address":"81.28"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0029","text":"This reasoner has at heart the interest of diversity—in accordance with the principle of specification; another, the interest of unity—in accordance with the principle of aggregation. Each believes that his judgement rests upon a thorough insight into the subject he is examining, and yet it has been influenced solely by a greater or less degree of adherence to some one of the two principles, neither of which are objective, but originate solely from the interest of reason, and on this account to be termed maxims rather than principles. When I observe intelligent men disputing about the distinctive characteristics of men, animals, or plants, and even of minerals, those on the one side assuming the existence of certain national characteristics, certain well-defined and hereditary distinctions of family, race, and so on, while the other side maintain that nature has endowed all races of men with the same faculties and dispositions, and that all differences are but the result of external and accidental circumstances—I have only to consider for a moment the real nature of the subject of discussion, to arrive at the conclusion that it is a subject far too deep for us to judge of, and that there is little probability of either party being able to speak from a perfect insight into and understanding of the nature of the subject itself. Both have, in reality, been struggling for the twofold interest of reason; the one maintaining the one interest, the other the other. But this difference between the maxims of diversity and unity may easily be reconciled and adjusted; although, so long as they are regarded as objective principles, they must occasion not only contradictions and polemic, but place hinderances in the way of the advancement of truth, until some means is discovered of reconciling these conflicting interests, and bringing reason into union and harmony with itself.","kind":"passage","sequence":29,"address":"81.29"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0030","text":"The same is the case with the so-called law discovered by Leibnitz, and supported with remarkable ability by Bonnet—the law of the continuous gradation of created beings, which is nothing more than an inference from the principle of affinity; for observation and study of the order of nature could never present it to the mind as an objective truth. The steps of this ladder, as they appear in experience, are too far apart from each other, and the so-called petty differences between different kinds of animals are in nature commonly so wide separations that no confidence can be placed in such views (particularly when we reflect on the great variety of things, and the ease with which we can discover resemblances), and no faith in the laws which are said to express the aims and purposes of nature. On the other hand, the method of investigating the order of nature in the light of this principle, and the maxim which requires us to regard this order—it being still undetermined how far it extends—as really existing in nature, is beyond doubt a legitimate and excellent principle of reason—a principle which extends farther than any experience or observation of ours and which, without giving us any positive knowledge of anything in the region of experience, guides us to the goal of systematic unity.\n\n_Of the Ultimate End of the Natural Dialectic of Human Reason._","kind":"passage","sequence":30,"address":"81.30"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0031","text":"The ideas of pure reason cannot be, of themselves and in their own nature, dialectical; it is from their misemployment alone that fallacies and illusions arise. For they originate in the nature of reason itself, and it is impossible that this supreme tribunal for all the rights and claims of speculation should be itself undeserving of confidence and promotive of error. It is to be expected, therefore, that these ideas have a genuine and legitimate aim. It is true, the mob of sophists raise against reason the cry of inconsistency and contradiction, and affect to despise the government of that faculty, because they cannot understand its constitution, while it is to its beneficial influences alone that they owe the position and the intelligence which enable them to criticize and to blame its procedure.\n\nWe cannot employ an à priori conception with certainty, until we have made a transcendental deduction therefore. The ideas of pure reason do not admit of the same kind of deduction as the categories. But if they are to possess the least objective validity, and to represent anything but mere creations of thought (entia rationis ratiocinantis), a deduction of them must be possible. This deduction will complete the critical task imposed upon pure reason; and it is to this part Of our labours that we now proceed.","kind":"passage","sequence":31,"address":"81.31"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0032","text":"There is a great difference between a thing’s being presented to the mind as an object in an absolute sense, or merely as an ideal object. In the former case I employ my conceptions to determine the object; in the latter case nothing is present to the mind but a mere schema, which does not relate directly to an object, not even in a hypothetical sense, but which is useful only for the purpose of representing other objects to the mind, in a mediate and indirect manner, by means of their relation to the idea in the intellect. Thus I say the conception of a supreme intelligence is a mere idea; that is to say, its objective reality does not consist in the fact that it has an immediate relation to an object (for in this sense we have no means of establishing its objective validity), it is merely a schema constructed according to the necessary conditions of the unity of reason—the schema of a thing in general, which is useful towards the production of the highest degree of systematic unity in the empirical exercise of reason, in which we deduce this or that object of experience from the imaginary object of this idea, as the ground or cause of the said object of experience. In this way, the idea is properly a heuristic, and not an ostensive, conception; it does not give us any information respecting the constitution of an object, it merely indicates how, under the guidance of the idea, we ought to investigate the constitution and the relations of objects in the world of experience. Now, if it can be shown that the three kinds of transcendental ideas (psychological, cosmological, and theological), although not relating directly to any object nor determining it, do nevertheless, on the supposition of the existence of an ideal object, produce systematic unity in the laws of the empirical employment of the reason, and extend our empirical cognition, without ever being inconsistent or in opposition with it—it must be a necessary maxim of reason to regulate its procedure according to these ideas. And this forms the transcendental deduction of all speculative ideas, not as constitutive principles of the extension of our cognition beyond the limits of our experience, but as regulative principles of the systematic unity of empirical cognition, which is by the aid of these ideas arranged and emended within its own proper limits, to an extent unattainable by the operation of the principles of the understanding alone.","kind":"passage","sequence":32,"address":"81.32"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0033","text":"I shall make this plainer. Guided by the principles involved in these ideas, we must, in the first place, so connect all the phenomena, actions, and feelings of the mind, as if it were a simple substance, which, endowed with personal identity, possesses a permanent existence (in this life at least), while its states, among which those of the body are to be included as external conditions, are in continual change. Secondly, in cosmology, we must investigate the conditions of all natural phenomena, internal as well as external, as if they belonged to a chain infinite and without any prime or supreme member, while we do not, on this account, deny the existence of intelligible grounds of these phenomena, although we never employ them to explain phenomena, for the simple reason that they are not objects of our cognition. Thirdly, in the sphere of theology, we must regard the whole system of possible experience as forming an absolute, but dependent and sensuously-conditioned unity, and at the same time as based upon a sole, supreme, and all-sufficient ground existing apart from the world itself—a ground which is a self-subsistent, primeval and creative reason, in relation to which we so employ our reason in the field of experience, as if all objects drew their origin from that archetype of all reason. In other words, we ought not to deduce the internal phenomena of the mind from a simple thinking substance, but deduce them from each other under the guidance of the regulative idea of a simple being; we ought not to deduce the phenomena, order, and unity of the universe from a supreme intelligence, but merely draw from this idea of a supremely wise cause the rules which must guide reason in its connection of causes and effects.","kind":"passage","sequence":33,"address":"81.33"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0034","text":"Now there is nothing to hinder us from admitting these ideas to possess an objective and hyperbolic existence, except the cosmological ideas, which lead reason into an antinomy: the psychological and theological ideas are not antinomial. They contain no contradiction; and how, then, can any one dispute their objective reality, since he who denies it knows as little about their possibility as we who affirm? And yet, when we wish to admit the existence of a thing, it is not sufficient to convince ourselves that there is no positive obstacle in the way; for it cannot be allowable to regard mere creations of thought, which transcend, though they do not contradict, all our conceptions, as real and determinate objects, solely upon the authority of a speculative reason striving to compass its own aims. They cannot, therefore, be admitted to be real in themselves; they can only possess a comparative reality—that of a schema of the regulative principle of the systematic unity of all cognition. They are to be regarded not as actual things, but as in some measure analogous to them. We abstract from the object of the idea all the conditions which limit the exercise of our understanding, but which, on the other hand, are the sole conditions of our possessing a determinate conception of any given thing. And thus we cogitate a something, of the real nature of which we have not the least conception, but which we represent to ourselves as standing in a relation to the whole system of phenomena, analogous to that in which phenomena stand to each other.","kind":"passage","sequence":34,"address":"81.34"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0035","text":"By admitting these ideal beings, we do not really extend our cognitions beyond the objects of possible experience; we extend merely the empirical unity of our experience, by the aid of systematic unity, the schema of which is furnished by the idea, which is therefore valid—not as a constitutive, but as a regulative principle. For although we posit a thing corresponding to the idea—a something, an actual existence—we do not on that account aim at the extension of our cognition by means of transcendent conceptions. This existence is purely ideal, and not objective; it is the mere expression of the systematic unity which is to be the guide of reason in the field of experience. There are no attempts made at deciding what the ground of this unity may be, or what the real nature of this imaginary being.\n\nThus the transcendental and only determinate conception of God, which is presented to us by speculative reason, is in the strictest sense deistic. In other words, reason does not assure us of the objective validity of the conception; it merely gives us the idea of something, on which the supreme and necessary unity of all experience is based. This something we cannot, following the analogy of a real substance, cogitate otherwise than as the cause of all things operating in accordance with rational laws, if we regard it as an individual object; although we should rest contented with the idea alone as a regulative principle of reason, and make no attempt at completing the sum of the conditions imposed by thought. This attempt is, indeed, inconsistent with the grand aim of complete systematic unity in the sphere of cognition—a unity to which no bounds are set by reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":35,"address":"81.35"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0036","text":"Hence it happens that, admitting a divine being, I can have no conception of the internal possibility of its perfection, or of the necessity of its existence. The only advantage of this admission is that it enables me to answer all other questions relating to the contingent, and to give reason the most complete satisfaction as regards the unity which it aims at attaining in the world of experience. But I cannot satisfy reason with regard to this hypothesis itself; and this proves that it is not its intelligence and insight into the subject, but its speculative interest alone which induces it to proceed from a point lying far beyond the sphere of our cognition, for the purpose of being able to consider all objects as parts of a systematic whole.","kind":"passage","sequence":36,"address":"81.36"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0037","text":"Here a distinction presents itself, in regard to the way in which we may cogitate a presupposition—a distinction which is somewhat subtle, but of great importance in transcendental philosophy. I may have sufficient grounds to admit something, or the existence of something, in a relative point of view (suppositio relativa), without being justified in admitting it in an absolute sense (suppositio absoluta). This distinction is undoubtedly requisite, in the case of a regulative principle, the necessity of which we recognize, though we are ignorant of the source and cause of that necessity, and which we assume to be based upon some ultimate ground, for the purpose of being able to cogitate the universality of the principle in a more determinate way. For example, I cogitate the existence of a being corresponding to a pure transcendental idea. But I cannot admit that this being exists absolutely and in itself, because all of the conceptions by which I can cogitate an object in a determinate manner fall short of assuring me of its existence; nay, the conditions of the objective validity of my conceptions are excluded by the idea—by the very fact of its being an idea. The conceptions of reality, substance, causality, nay, even that of necessity in existence, have no significance out of the sphere of empirical cognition, and cannot, beyond that sphere, determine any object. They may, accordingly, be employed to explain the possibility of things in the world of sense, but they are utterly inadequate to explain the possibility of the universe itself considered as a whole; because in this case the ground of explanation must lie out of and beyond the world, and cannot, therefore, be an object of possible experience. Now, I may admit the existence of an incomprehensible being of this nature—the object of a mere idea, relatively to the world of sense; although I have no ground to admit its existence absolutely and in itself. For if an idea (that of a systematic and complete unity, of which I shall presently speak more particularly) lies at the foundation of the most extended empirical employment of reason, and if this idea cannot be adequately represented in concreto, although it is indispensably necessary for the approximation of empirical unity to the highest possible degree—I am not only authorized, but compelled, to realize this idea, that is, to posit a real object corresponding thereto. But I cannot profess to know this object; it is to me merely a something, to which, as the ground of systematic unity in cognition, I attribute such properties as are analogous to the conceptions employed by the understanding in the sphere of experience. Following the analogy of the notions of reality, substance, causality, and necessity, I cogitate a being, which possesses all these attributes in the highest degree; and, as this idea is the offspring of my reason alone, I cogitate this being as self-subsistent reason, and as the cause of the universe operating by means of ideas of the greatest possible harmony and unity. Thus I abstract all conditions that would limit my idea, solely for the purpose of rendering systematic unity possible in the world of empirical diversity, and thus securing the widest possible extension for the exercise of reason in that sphere. This I am enabled to do, by regarding all connections and relations in the world of sense, as if they were the dispositions of a supreme reason, of which our reason is but a faint image. I then proceed to cogitate this Supreme Being by conceptions which have, properly, no meaning or application, except in the world of sense. But as I am authorized to employ the transcendental hypothesis of such a being in a relative respect alone, that is, as the substratum of the greatest possible unity in experience—I may attribute to a being which I regard as distinct from the world, such properties as belong solely to the sphere of sense and experience. For I do not desire, and am not justified in desiring, to cognize this object of my idea, as it exists in itself; for I possess no conceptions sufficient for this task, those of reality, substance, causality, nay, even that of necessity in existence, losing all significance, and becoming merely the signs of conceptions, without content and without applicability, when I attempt to carry them beyond the limits of the world of sense. I cogitate merely the relation of a perfectly unknown being to the greatest possible systematic unity of experience, solely for the purpose of employing it as the schema of the regulative principle which directs reason in its empirical exercise.","kind":"passage","sequence":37,"address":"81.37"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0038","text":"It is evident, at the first view, that we cannot presuppose the reality of this transcendental object, by means of the conceptions of reality, substance, causality, and so on, because these conceptions cannot be applied to anything that is distinct from the world of sense. Thus the supposition of a Supreme Being or cause is purely relative; it is cogitated only in behalf of the systematic unity of experience; such a being is but a something, of whose existence in itself we have not the least conception. Thus, too, it becomes sufficiently manifest why we required the idea of a necessary being in relation to objects given by sense, although we can never have the least conception of this being, or of its absolute necessity.","kind":"passage","sequence":38,"address":"81.38"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0039","text":"And now we can clearly perceive the result of our transcendental dialectic, and the proper aim of the ideas of pure reason—which become dialectical solely from misunderstanding and inconsiderateness. Pure reason is, in fact, occupied with itself, and not with any object. Objects are not presented to it to be embraced in the unity of an empirical conception; it is only the cognitions of the understanding that are presented to it, for the purpose of receiving the unity of a rational conception, that is, of being connected according to a principle. The unity of reason is the unity of system; and this systematic unity is not an objective principle, extending its dominion over objects, but a subjective maxim, extending its authority over the empirical cognition of objects. The systematic connection which reason gives to the empirical employment of the understanding not only advances the extension of that employment, but ensures its correctness, and thus the principle of a systematic unity of this nature is also objective, although only in an indefinite respect (principium vagum). It is not, however, a constitutive principle, determining an object to which it directly relates; it is merely a regulative principle or maxim, advancing and strengthening the empirical exercise of reason, by the opening up of new paths of which the understanding is ignorant, while it never conflicts with the laws of its exercise in the sphere of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":39,"address":"81.39"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0040","text":"But reason cannot cogitate this systematic unity, without at the same time cogitating an object of the idea—an object that cannot be presented in any experience, which contains no concrete example of a complete systematic unity. This being (ens rationis ratiocinatae) is therefore a mere idea and is not assumed to be a thing which is real absolutely and in itself. On the contrary, it forms merely the problematical foundation of the connection which the mind introduces among the phenomena of the sensuous world. We look upon this connection, in the light of the above-mentioned idea, as if it drew its origin from the supposed being which corresponds to the idea. And yet all we aim at is the possession of this idea as a secure foundation for the systematic unity of experience—a unity indispensable to reason, advantageous to the understanding, and promotive of the interests of empirical cognition.\n\nWe mistake the true meaning of this idea when we regard it as an enouncement, or even as a hypothetical declaration of the existence of a real thing, which we are to regard as the origin or ground of a systematic constitution of the universe. On the contrary, it is left completely undetermined what the nature or properties of this so-called ground may be. The idea is merely to be adopted as a point of view, from which this unity, so essential to reason and so beneficial to the understanding, may be regarded as radiating. In one word, this transcendental thing is merely the schema of a regulative principle, by means of which Reason, so far as in her lies, extends the dominion of systematic unity over the whole sphere of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":40,"address":"81.40"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0041","text":"The first object of an idea of this kind is the ego, considered merely as a thinking nature or soul. If I wish to investigate the properties of a thinking being, I must interrogate experience. But I find that I can apply none of the categories to this object, the schema of these categories, which is the condition of their application, being given only in sensuous intuition. But I cannot thus attain to the cognition of a systematic unity of all the phenomena of the internal sense. Instead, therefore, of an empirical conception of what the soul really is, reason takes the conception of the empirical unity of all thought, and, by cogitating this unity as unconditioned and primitive, constructs the rational conception or idea of a simple substance which is in itself unchangeable, possessing personal identity, and in connection with other real things external to it; in one word, it constructs the idea of a simple self-subsistent intelligence. But the real aim of reason in this procedure is the attainment of principles of systematic unity for the explanation of the phenomena of the soul. That is, reason desires to be able to represent all the determinations of the internal sense as existing in one subject, all powers as deduced from one fundamental power, all changes as mere varieties in the condition of a being which is permanent and always the same, and all phenomena in space as entirely different in their nature from the procedure of thought. Essential simplicity (with the other attributes predicated of the ego) is regarded as the mere schema of this regulative principle; it is not assumed that it is the actual ground of the properties of the soul. For these properties may rest upon quite different grounds, of which we are completely ignorant; just as the above predicates could not give us any knowledge of the soul as it is in itself, even if we regarded them as valid in respect of it, inasmuch as they constitute a mere idea, which cannot be represented in concreto. Nothing but good can result from a psychological idea of this kind, if we only take proper care not to consider it as more than an idea; that is, if we regard it as valid merely in relation to the employment of reason, in the sphere of the phenomena of the soul. Under the guidance of this idea, or principle, no empirical laws of corporeal phenomena are called in to explain that which is a phenomenon of the internal sense alone; no windy hypotheses of the generation, annihilation, and palingenesis of souls are admitted. Thus the consideration of this object of the internal sense is kept pure, and unmixed with heterogeneous elements; while the investigation of reason aims at reducing all the grounds of explanation employed in this sphere of knowledge to a single principle. All this is best effected, nay, cannot be effected otherwise than by means of such a schema, which requires us to regard this ideal thing as an actual existence. The psychological idea is, therefore, meaningless and inapplicable, except as the schema of a regulative conception. For, if I ask whether the soul is not really of a spiritual nature—it is a question which has no meaning. From such a conception has been abstracted, not merely all corporeal nature, but all nature, that is, all the predicates of a possible experience; and consequently, all the conditions which enable us to cogitate an object to this conception have disappeared. But, if these conditions are absent, it is evident that the conception is meaningless.","kind":"passage","sequence":41,"address":"81.41"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0042","text":"The second regulative idea of speculative reason is the conception of the universe. For nature is properly the only object presented to us, in regard to which reason requires regulative principles. Nature is twofold—thinking and corporeal nature. To cogitate the latter in regard to its internal possibility, that is, to determine the application of the categories to it, no idea is required—no representation which transcends experience. In this sphere, therefore, an idea is impossible, sensuous intuition being our only guide; while, in the sphere of psychology, we require the fundamental idea (I), which contains à priori a certain form of thought namely, the unity of the ego. Pure reason has, therefore, nothing left but nature in general, and the completeness of conditions in nature in accordance with some principle. The absolute totality of the series of these conditions is an idea, which can never be fully realized in the empirical exercise of reason, while it is serviceable as a rule for the procedure of reason in relation to that totality. It requires us, in the explanation of given phenomena (in the regress or ascent in the series), to proceed as if the series were infinite in itself, that is, were prolonged in indefinitum; while on the other hand, where reason is regarded as itself the determining cause (in the region of freedom), we are required to proceed as if we had not before us an object of sense, but of the pure understanding. In this latter case, the conditions do not exist in the series of phenomena, but may be placed quite out of and beyond it, and the series of conditions may be regarded as if it had an absolute beginning from an intelligible cause. All this proves that the cosmological ideas are nothing but regulative principles, and not constitutive; and that their aim is not to realize an actual totality in such series. The full discussion of this subject will be found in its proper place in the chapter on the antinomy of pure reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":42,"address":"81.42"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0043","text":"The third idea of pure reason, containing the hypothesis of a being which is valid merely as a relative hypothesis, is that of the one and all-sufficient cause of all cosmological series, in other words, the idea of God. We have not the slightest ground absolutely to admit the existence of an object corresponding to this idea; for what can empower or authorize us to affirm the existence of a being of the highest perfection—a being whose existence is absolutely necessary—merely because we possess the conception of such a being? The answer is: It is the existence of the world which renders this hypothesis necessary. But this answer makes it perfectly evident that the idea of this being, like all other speculative ideas, is essentially nothing more than a demand upon reason that it shall regulate the connection which it and its subordinate faculties introduce into the phenomena of the world by principles of systematic unity and, consequently, that it shall regard all phenomena as originating from one all-embracing being, as the supreme and all-sufficient cause. From this it is plain that the only aim of reason in this procedure is the establishment of its own formal rule for the extension of its dominion in the world of experience; that it does not aim at an extension of its cognition beyond the limits of experience; and that, consequently, this idea does not contain any constitutive principle.","kind":"passage","sequence":43,"address":"81.43"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0044","text":"The highest formal unity, which is based upon ideas alone, is the unity of all things—a unity in accordance with an aim or purpose; and the speculative interest of reason renders it necessary to regard all order in the world as if it originated from the intention and design of a supreme reason. This principle unfolds to the view of reason in the sphere of experience new and enlarged prospects, and invites it to connect the phenomena of the world according to teleological laws, and in this way to attain to the highest possible degree of systematic unity. The hypothesis of a supreme intelligence, as the sole cause of the universe—an intelligence which has for us no more than an ideal existence—is accordingly always of the greatest service to reason. Thus, if we presuppose, in relation to the figure of the earth (which is round, but somewhat flattened at the poles),[[71]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-71) or that of mountains or seas, wise designs on the part of an author of the universe, we cannot fail to make, by the light of this supposition, a great number of interesting discoveries. If we keep to this hypothesis, as a principle which is purely regulative, even error cannot be very detrimental. For, in this case, error can have no more serious consequences than that, where we expected to discover a teleological connection (nexus finalis), only a mechanical or physical connection appears. In such a case, we merely fail to find the additional form of unity we expected, but we do not lose the rational unity which the mind requires in its procedure in experience. But even a miscarriage of this sort cannot affect the law in its general and teleological relations. For although we may convict an anatomist of an error, when he connects the limb of some animal with a certain purpose, it is quite impossible to prove in a single case that any arrangement of nature, be it what it may, is entirely without aim or design. And thus medical physiology, by the aid of a principle presented to it by pure reason, extends its very limited empirical knowledge of the purposes of the different parts of an organized body so far that it may be asserted with the utmost confidence, and with the approbation of all reflecting men, that every organ or bodily part of an animal has its use and answers a certain design. Now, this is a supposition which, if regarded as of a constitutive character, goes much farther than any experience or observation of ours can justify. Hence it is evident that it is nothing more than a regulative principle of reason, which aims at the highest degree of systematic unity, by the aid of the idea of a causality according to design in a supreme cause—a cause which it regards as the highest intelligence.","kind":"passage","sequence":44,"address":"81.44"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0045","text":"[[71]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-71) The advantages which a circular form, in the case of the earth, has over every other, are well known. But few are aware that the slight flattening at the poles, which gives it the figure of a spheroid, is the only cause which prevents the elevations of continents or even of mountains, perhaps thrown up by some internal convulsion, from continually altering the position of the axis of the earth—and that to some considerable degree in a short time. The great protuberance of the earth under the Equator serves to overbalance the impetus of all other masses of earth, and thus to preserve the axis of the earth, so far as we can observe, in its present position. And yet this wise arrangement has been unthinkingly explained from the equilibrium of the formerly fluid mass.\n\nIf, however, we neglect this restriction of the idea to a purely regulative influence, reason is betrayed into numerous errors. For it has then left the ground of experience, in which alone are to be found the criteria of truth, and has ventured into the region of the incomprehensible and unsearchable, on the heights of which it loses its power and collectedness, because it has completely severed its connection with experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":45,"address":"81.45"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","text":"The first error which arises from our employing the idea of a Supreme Being as a constitutive (in repugnance to the very nature of an idea), and not as a regulative principle, is the error of inactive reason (ignava ratio).[[72]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-72) We may so term every principle which requires us to regard our investigations of nature as absolutely complete, and allows reason to cease its inquiries, as if it had fully executed its task. Thus the psychological idea of the ego, when employed as a constitutive principle for the explanation of the phenomena of the soul, and for the extension of our knowledge regarding this subject beyond the limits of experience—even to the condition of the soul after death—is convenient enough for the purposes of pure reason, but detrimental and even ruinous to its interests in the sphere of nature and experience. The dogmatizing spiritualist explains the unchanging unity of our personality through all changes of condition from the unity of a thinking substance, the interest which we take in things and events that can happen only after our death, from a consciousness of the immaterial nature of our thinking subject, and so on. Thus he dispenses with all empirical investigations into the cause of these internal phenomena, and with all possible explanations of them upon purely natural grounds; while, at the dictation of a transcendent reason, he passes by the immanent sources of cognition in experience, greatly to his own ease and convenience, but to the sacrifice of all, genuine insight and intelligence. These prejudicial consequences become still more evident, in the case of the dogmatical treatment of our idea of a Supreme Intelligence, and the theological system of nature (physico-theology) which is falsely based upon it. For, in this case, the aims which we observe in nature, and often those which we merely fancy to exist, make the investigation of causes a very easy task, by directing us to refer such and such phenomena immediately to the unsearchable will and counsel of the Supreme Wisdom, while we ought to investigate their causes in the general laws of the mechanism of matter. We are thus recommended to consider the labour of reason as ended, when we have merely dispensed with its employment, which is guided surely and safely only by the order of nature and the series of changes in the world—which are arranged according to immanent and general laws. This error may be avoided, if we do not merely consider from the view-point of final aims certain parts of nature, such as the division and structure of a continent, the constitution and direction of certain mountain-chains, or even the organization existing in the vegetable and animal kingdoms, but look upon this systematic unity of nature in a perfectly general way, in relation to the idea of a Supreme Intelligence. If we pursue this advice, we lay as a foundation for all investigation the conformity to aims of all phenomena of nature in accordance with universal laws, for which no particular arrangement of nature is exempt, but only cognized by us with more or less difficulty; and we possess a regulative principle of the systematic unity of a teleological connection, which we do not attempt to anticipate or predetermine. All that we do, and ought to do, is to follow out the physico-mechanical connection in nature according to general laws, with the hope of discovering, sooner or later, the teleological connection also. Thus, and thus only, can the principle of final unity aid in the extension of the employment of reason in the sphere of experience, without being in any case detrimental to its interests.","kind":"passage","sequence":46,"address":"81.46"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0047","text":"[[72]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-72) This was the term applied by the old dialecticians to a sophistical argument, which ran thus: If it is your fate to die of this disease, you will die, whether you employ a physician or not. Cicero says that this mode of reasoning has received this appellation, because, if followed, it puts an end to the employment of reason in the affairs of life. For a similar reason, I have applied this designation to the sophistical argument of pure reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":47,"address":"81.47"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0048","text":"The second error which arises from the misconception of the principle of systematic unity is that of perverted reason (perversa ratio, usteron roteron rationis). The idea of systematic unity is available as a regulative principle in the connection of phenomena according to general natural laws; and, how far soever we have to travel upon the path of experience to discover some fact or event, this idea requires us to believe that we have approached all the more nearly to the completion of its use in the sphere of nature, although that completion can never be attained. But this error reverses the procedure of reason. We begin by hypostatizing the principle of systematic unity, and by giving an anthropomorphic determination to the conception of a Supreme Intelligence, and then proceed forcibly to impose aims upon nature. Thus not only does teleology, which ought to aid in the completion of unity in accordance with general laws, operate to the destruction of its influence, but it hinders reason from attaining its proper aim, that is, the proof, upon natural grounds, of the existence of a supreme intelligent cause. For, if we cannot presuppose supreme finality in nature à priori, that is, as essentially belonging to nature, how can we be directed to endeavour to discover this unity and, rising gradually through its different degrees, to approach the supreme perfection of an author of all—a perfection which is absolutely necessary, and therefore cognizable à priori? The regulative principle directs us to presuppose systematic unity absolutely and, consequently, as following from the essential nature of things—but only as a unity of nature, not merely cognized empirically, but presupposed à priori, although only in an indeterminate manner. But if I insist on basing nature upon the foundation of a supreme ordaining Being, the unity of nature is in effect lost. For, in this case, it is quite foreign and unessential to the nature of things, and cannot be cognized from the general laws of nature. And thus arises a vicious circular argument, what ought to have been proved having been presupposed.","kind":"passage","sequence":48,"address":"81.48"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0049","text":"To take the regulative principle of systematic unity in nature for a constitutive principle, and to hypostatize and make a cause out of that which is properly the ideal ground of the consistent and harmonious exercise of reason, involves reason in inextricable embarrassments. The investigation of nature pursues its own path under the guidance of the chain of natural causes, in accordance with the general laws of nature, and ever follows the light of the idea of an author of the universe—not for the purpose of deducing the finality, which it constantly pursues, from this Supreme Being, but to attain to the cognition of his existence from the finality which it seeks in the existence of the phenomena of nature, and, if possible, in that of all things to cognize this being, consequently, as absolutely necessary. Whether this latter purpose succeed or not, the idea is and must always be a true one, and its employment, when merely regulative, must always be accompanied by truthful and beneficial results.","kind":"passage","sequence":49,"address":"81.49"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0050","text":"Complete unity, in conformity with aims, constitutes absolute perfection. But if we do not find this unity in the nature of the things which go to constitute the world of experience, that is, of objective cognition, consequently in the universal and necessary laws of nature, how can we infer from this unity the idea of the supreme and absolutely necessary perfection of a primal being, which is the origin of all causality? The greatest systematic unity, and consequently teleological unity, constitutes the very foundation of the possibility of the most extended employment of human reason. The idea of unity is therefore essentially and indissolubly connected with the nature of our reason. This idea is a legislative one; and hence it is very natural that we should assume the existence of a legislative reason corresponding to it, from which the systematic unity of nature—the object of the operations of reason—must be derived.\n\nIn the course of our discussion of the antinomies, we stated that it is always possible to answer all the questions which pure reason may raise; and that the plea of the limited nature of our cognition, which is unavoidable and proper in many questions regarding natural phenomena, cannot in this case be admitted, because the questions raised do not relate to the nature of things, but are necessarily originated by the nature of reason itself, and relate to its own internal constitution. We can now establish this assertion, which at first sight appeared so rash, in relation to the two questions in which reason takes the greatest interest, and thus complete our discussion of the dialectic of pure reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":50,"address":"81.50"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0051","text":"If, then, the question is asked, in relation to transcendental theology,[[73]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-73) first, whether there is anything distinct from the world, which contains the ground of cosmical order and connection according to general laws? The answer is: Certainly. For the world is a sum of phenomena; there must, therefore, be some transcendental basis of these phenomena, that is, a basis cogitable by the pure understanding alone. If, secondly, the question is asked whether this being is substance, whether it is of the greatest reality, whether it is necessary, and so forth? I answer that this question is utterly without meaning. For all the categories which aid me in forming a conception of an object cannot be employed except in the world of sense, and are without meaning when not applied to objects of actual or possible experience. Out of this sphere, they are not properly conceptions, but the mere marks or indices of conceptions, which we may admit, although they cannot, without the help of experience, help us to understand any subject or thing. If, thirdly, the question is whether we may not cogitate this being, which is distinct from the world, in analogy with the objects of experience? The answer is: Undoubtedly, but only as an ideal, and not as a real object. That is, we must cogitate it only as an unknown substratum of the systematic unity, order, and finality of the world—a unity which reason must employ as the regulative principle of its investigation of nature. Nay, more, we may admit into the idea certain anthropomorphic elements, which are promotive of the interests of this regulative principle. For it is no more than an idea, which does not relate directly to a being distinct from the world, but to the regulative principle of the systematic unity of the world, by means, however, of a schema of this unity—the schema of a Supreme Intelligence, who is the wisely-designing author of the universe. What this basis of cosmical unity may be in itself, we know not—we cannot discover from the idea; we merely know how we ought to employ the idea of this unity, in relation to the systematic operation of reason in the sphere of experience.","kind":"passage","sequence":51,"address":"81.51"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0052","text":"[[73]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-73) After what has been said of the psychological idea of the ego and its proper employment as a regulative principle of the operations of reason, I need not enter into details regarding the transcendental illusion by which the systematic unity of all the various phenomena of the internal sense is hypostatized. The procedure is in this case very similar to that which has been discussed in our remarks on the theological ideal.\n\nBut, it will be asked again, can we on these grounds, admit the existence of a wise and omnipotent author of the world? Without doubt; and not only so, but we must assume the existence of such a being. But do we thus extend the limits of our knowledge beyond the field of possible experience? By no means. For we have merely presupposed a something, of which we have no conception, which we do not know as it is in itself; but, in relation to the systematic disposition of the universe, which we must presuppose in all our observation of nature, we have cogitated this unknown being in analogy with an intelligent existence (an empirical conception), that is to say, we have endowed it with those attributes, which, judging from the nature of our own reason, may contain the ground of such a systematic unity. This idea is therefore valid only relatively to the employment in experience of our reason. But if we attribute to it absolute and objective validity, we overlook the fact that it is merely an ideal being that we cogitate; and, by setting out from a basis which is not determinable by considerations drawn from experience, we place ourselves in a position which incapacitates us from applying this principle to the empirical employment of reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":52,"address":"81.52"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0053","text":"But, it will be asked further, can I make any use of this conception and hypothesis in my investigations into the world and nature? Yes, for this very purpose was the idea established by reason as a fundamental basis. But may I regard certain arrangements, which seemed to have been made in conformity with some fixed aim, as the arrangements of design, and look upon them as proceeding from the divine will, with the intervention, however, of certain other particular arrangements disposed to that end? Yes, you may do so; but at the same time you must regard it as indifferent, whether it is asserted that divine wisdom has disposed all things in conformity with his highest aims, or that the idea of supreme wisdom is a regulative principle in the investigation of nature, and at the same time a principle of the systematic unity of nature according to general laws, even in those cases where we are unable to discover that unity. In other words, it must be perfectly indifferent to you whether you say, when you have discovered this unity: God has wisely willed it so; or: Nature has wisely arranged this. For it was nothing but the systematic unity, which reason requires as a basis for the investigation of nature, that justified you in accepting the idea of a supreme intelligence as a schema for a regulative principle; and, the farther you advance in the discovery of design and finality, the more certain the validity of your idea. But, as the whole aim of this regulative principle was the discovery of a necessary and systematic unity in nature, we have, in so far as we attain this, to attribute our success to the idea of a Supreme Being; while, at the same time, we cannot, without involving ourselves in contradictions, overlook the general laws of nature, as it was in reference to them alone that this idea was employed. We cannot, I say, overlook the general laws of nature, and regard this conformity to aims observable in nature as contingent or hyperphysical in its origin; inasmuch as there is no ground which can justify us in the admission of a being with such properties distinct from and above nature. All that we are authorized to assert is that this idea may be employed as a principle, and that the properties of the being which is assumed to correspond to it may be regarded as systematically connected in analogy with the causal determination of phenomena.","kind":"passage","sequence":53,"address":"81.53"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0054","text":"For the same reasons we are justified in introducing into the idea of the supreme cause other anthropomorphic elements (for without these we could not predicate anything of it); we may regard it as allowable to cogitate this cause as a being with understanding, the feelings of pleasure and displeasure, and faculties of desire and will corresponding to these. At the same time, we may attribute to this being infinite perfection—a perfection which necessarily transcends that which our knowledge of the order and design in the world authorize us to predicate of it. For the regulative law of systematic unity requires us to study nature on the supposition that systematic and final unity in infinitum is everywhere discoverable, even in the highest diversity. For, although we may discover little of this cosmical perfection, it belongs to the legislative prerogative of reason to require us always to seek for and to expect it; while it must always be beneficial to institute all inquiries into nature in accordance with this principle. But it is evident that, by this idea of a supreme author of all, which I place as the foundation of all inquiries into nature, I do not mean to assert the existence of such a being, or that I have any knowledge of its existence; and, consequently, I do not really deduce anything from the existence of this being, but merely from its idea, that is to say, from the nature of things in this world, in accordance with this idea. A certain dim consciousness of the true use of this idea seems to have dictated to the philosophers of all times the moderate language used by them regarding the cause of the world. We find them employing the expressions wisdom and care of nature, and divine wisdom, as synonymous—nay, in purely speculative discussions, preferring the former, because it does not carry the appearance of greater pretensions than such as we are entitled to make, and at the same time directs reason to its proper field of action—nature and her phenomena.","kind":"passage","sequence":54,"address":"81.54"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0055","text":"Thus, pure reason, which at first seemed to promise us nothing less than the extension of our cognition beyond the limits of experience, is found, when thoroughly examined, to contain nothing but regulative principles, the virtue and function of which is to introduce into our cognition a higher degree of unity than the understanding could of itself. These principles, by placing the goal of all our struggles at so great a distance, realize for us the most thorough connection between the different parts of our cognition, and the highest degree of systematic unity. But, on the other hand, if misunderstood and employed as constitutive principles of transcendent cognition, they become the parents of illusions and contradictions, while pretending to introduce us to new regions of knowledge.","kind":"passage","sequence":55,"address":"81.55"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0056","text":"Thus all human cognition begins with intuitions, proceeds from thence to conceptions, and ends with ideas. Although it possesses, in relation to all three elements, à priori sources of cognition, which seemed to transcend the limits of all experience, a thoroughgoing criticism demonstrates that speculative reason can never, by the aid of these elements, pass the bounds of possible experience, and that the proper destination of this highest faculty of cognition is to employ all methods, and all the principles of these methods, for the purpose of penetrating into the innermost secrets of nature, by the aid of the principles of unity (among all kinds of which teleological unity is the highest), while it ought not to attempt to soar above the sphere of experience, beyond which there lies nought for us but the void inane. The critical examination, in our Transcendental Analytic, of all the propositions which professed to extend cognition beyond the sphere of experience, completely demonstrated that they can only conduct us to a possible experience. If we were not distrustful even of the clearest abstract theorems, if we were not allured by specious and inviting prospects to escape from the constraining power of their evidence, we might spare ourselves the laborious examination of all the dialectical arguments which a transcendent reason adduces in support of its pretensions; for we should know with the most complete certainty that, however honest such professions might be, they are null and valueless, because they relate to a kind of knowledge to which no man can by any possibility attain. But, as there is no end to discussion, if we cannot discover the true cause of the illusions by which even the wisest are deceived, and as the analysis of all our transcendent cognition into its elements is of itself of no slight value as a psychological study, while it is a duty incumbent on every philosopher—it was found necessary to investigate the dialectical procedure of reason in its primary sources. And as the inferences of which this dialectic is the parent are not only deceitful, but naturally possess a profound interest for humanity, it was advisable at the same time, to give a full account of the momenta of this dialectical procedure, and to deposit it in the archives of human reason, as a warning to all future metaphysicians to avoid these causes of speculative error.","kind":"passage","sequence":56,"address":"81.56"}],"sha256":"614be374a176c929c1f532808079fa282d32c7d13225558aa8871b0f34a1904a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM","title":"II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method","order":82,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_ITDM","text":"## II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method\n\nIf we regard the sum of the cognition of pure speculative reason as an edifice, the idea of which, at least, exists in the human mind, it may be said that we have in the Transcendental Doctrine of Elements examined the materials and determined to what edifice these belong, and what its height and stability. We have found, indeed, that, although we had purposed to build for ourselves a tower which should reach to Heaven, the supply of materials sufficed merely for a habitation, which was spacious enough for all terrestrial purposes, and high enough to enable us to survey the level plain of experience, but that the bold undertaking designed necessarily failed for want of materials—not to mention the confusion of tongues, which gave rise to endless disputes among the labourers on the plan of the edifice, and at last scattered them over all the world, each to erect a separate building for himself, according to his own plans and his own inclinations. Our present task relates not to the materials, but to the plan of an edifice; and, as we have had sufficient warning not to venture blindly upon a design which may be found to transcend our natural powers, while, at the same time, we cannot give up the intention of erecting a secure abode for the mind, we must proportion our design to the material which is presented to us, and which is, at the same time, sufficient for all our wants.\n\nI understand, then, by the transcendental doctrine of method, the determination of the formal conditions of a complete system of pure reason. We shall accordingly have to treat of the discipline, the canon, the architectonic, and, finally, the history of pure reason. This part of our Critique will accomplish, from the transcendental point of view, what has been usually attempted, but miserably executed, under the name of practical logic. It has been badly executed, I say, because general logic, not being limited to any particular kind of cognition (not even to the pure cognition of the understanding) nor to any particular objects, it cannot, without borrowing from other sciences, do more than present merely the titles or signs of possible methods and the technical expressions, which are employed in the systematic parts of all sciences; and thus the pupil is made acquainted with names, the meaning and application of which he is to learn only at some future time.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","text":"## II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method\n\nIf we regard the sum of the cognition of pure speculative reason as an edifice, the idea of which, at least, exists in the human mind, it may be said that we have in the Transcendental Doctrine of Elements examined the materials and determined to what edifice these belong, and what its height and stability. We have found, indeed, that, although we had purposed to build for ourselves a tower which should reach to Heaven, the supply of materials sufficed merely for a habitation, which was spacious enough for all terrestrial purposes, and high enough to enable us to survey the level plain of experience, but that the bold undertaking designed necessarily failed for want of materials—not to mention the confusion of tongues, which gave rise to endless disputes among the labourers on the plan of the edifice, and at last scattered them over all the world, each to erect a separate building for himself, according to his own plans and his own inclinations. Our present task relates not to the materials, but to the plan of an edifice; and, as we have had sufficient warning not to venture blindly upon a design which may be found to transcend our natural powers, while, at the same time, we cannot give up the intention of erecting a secure abode for the mind, we must proportion our design to the material which is presented to us, and which is, at the same time, sufficient for all our wants.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"82.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","text":"I understand, then, by the transcendental doctrine of method, the determination of the formal conditions of a complete system of pure reason. We shall accordingly have to treat of the discipline, the canon, the architectonic, and, finally, the history of pure reason. This part of our Critique will accomplish, from the transcendental point of view, what has been usually attempted, but miserably executed, under the name of practical logic. It has been badly executed, I say, because general logic, not being limited to any particular kind of cognition (not even to the pure cognition of the understanding) nor to any particular objects, it cannot, without borrowing from other sciences, do more than present merely the titles or signs of possible methods and the technical expressions, which are employed in the systematic parts of all sciences; and thus the pupil is made acquainted with names, the meaning and application of which he is to learn only at some future time.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"82.2"}],"sha256":"52965b3cc33c420c52fa0680303555fc3445b49a3d584102e39b698a6315d367","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP","title":"Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason","order":83,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_CIDP","text":"### Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason\n\nNegative judgements—those which are so not merely as regards their logical form, but in respect of their content—are not commonly held in especial respect. They are, on the contrary, regarded as jealous enemies of our insatiable desire for knowledge; and it almost requires an apology to induce us to tolerate, much less to prize and to respect them.\n\nAll propositions, indeed, may be logically expressed in a negative form; but, in relation to the content of our cognition, the peculiar province of negative judgements is solely to prevent error. For this reason, too, negative propositions, which are framed for the purpose of correcting false cognitions where error is absolutely impossible, are undoubtedly true, but inane and senseless; that is, they are in reality purposeless and, for this reason, often very ridiculous. Such is the proposition of the schoolman that Alexander could not have subdued any countries without an army.\n\nBut where the limits of our possible cognition are very much contracted, the attraction to new fields of knowledge great, the illusions to which the mind is subject of the most deceptive character, and the evil consequences of error of no inconsiderable magnitude—the negative element in knowledge, which is useful only to guard us against error, is of far more importance than much of that positive instruction which makes additions to the sum of our knowledge. The restraint which is employed to repress, and finally to extirpate the constant inclination to depart from certain rules, is termed discipline. It is distinguished from culture, which aims at the formation of a certain degree of skill, without attempting to repress or to destroy any other mental power, already existing. In the cultivation of a talent, which has given evidence of an impulse towards self-development, discipline takes a negative,[[74]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-74) culture and doctrine a positive, part.\n\n[[74]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-74) I am well aware that, in the language of the schools, the term discipline is usually employed as synonymous with instruction. But there are so many cases in which it is necessary to distinguish the notion of the former, as a course of corrective training, from that of the latter, as the communication of knowledge, and the nature of things itself demands the appropriation of the most suitable expressions for this distinction, that it is my desire that the former terms should never be employed in any other than a negative signification.\n\nThat natural dispositions and talents (such as imagination and wit), which ask a free and unlimited development, require in many respects the corrective influence of discipline, every one will readily grant. But it may well appear strange that reason, whose proper duty it is to prescribe rules of discipline to all the other powers of the mind, should itself require this corrective. It has, in fact, hitherto escaped this humiliation, only because, in presence of its magnificent pretensions and high position, no one could readily suspect it to be capable of substituting fancies for conceptions, and words for things.\n\nReason, when employed in the field of experience, does not stand in need of criticism, because its principles are subjected to the continual test of empirical observations. Nor is criticism requisite in the sphere of mathematics, where the conceptions of reason must always be presented in concreto in pure intuition, and baseless or arbitrary assertions are discovered without difficulty. But where reason is not held in a plain track by the influence of empirical or of pure intuition, that is, when it is employed in the transcendental sphere of pure conceptions, it stands in great need of discipline, to restrain its propensity to overstep the limits of possible experience and to keep it from wandering into error. In fact, the utility of the philosophy of pure reason is entirely of this negative character. Particular errors may be corrected by particular animadversions, and the causes of these errors may be eradicated by criticism. But where we find, as in the case of pure reason, a complete system of illusions and fallacies, closely connected with each other and depending upon grand general principles, there seems to be required a peculiar and negative code of mental legislation, which, under the denomination of a discipline, and founded upon the nature of reason and the objects of its exercise, shall constitute a system of thorough examination and testing, which no fallacy will be able to withstand or escape from, under whatever disguise or concealment it may lurk.\n\nBut the reader must remark that, in this the second division of our transcendental Critique the discipline of pure reason is not directed to the content, but to the method of the cognition of pure reason. The former task has been completed in the doctrine of elements. But there is so much similarity in the mode of employing the faculty of reason, whatever be the object to which it is applied, while, at the same time, its employment in the transcendental sphere is so essentially different in kind from every other, that, without the warning negative influence of a discipline specially directed to that end, the errors are unavoidable which spring from the unskillful employment of the methods which are originated by reason but which are out of place in this sphere.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0001","text":"### Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason\n\nNegative judgements—those which are so not merely as regards their logical form, but in respect of their content—are not commonly held in especial respect. They are, on the contrary, regarded as jealous enemies of our insatiable desire for knowledge; and it almost requires an apology to induce us to tolerate, much less to prize and to respect them.\n\nAll propositions, indeed, may be logically expressed in a negative form; but, in relation to the content of our cognition, the peculiar province of negative judgements is solely to prevent error. For this reason, too, negative propositions, which are framed for the purpose of correcting false cognitions where error is absolutely impossible, are undoubtedly true, but inane and senseless; that is, they are in reality purposeless and, for this reason, often very ridiculous. Such is the proposition of the schoolman that Alexander could not have subdued any countries without an army.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"83.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","text":"But where the limits of our possible cognition are very much contracted, the attraction to new fields of knowledge great, the illusions to which the mind is subject of the most deceptive character, and the evil consequences of error of no inconsiderable magnitude—the negative element in knowledge, which is useful only to guard us against error, is of far more importance than much of that positive instruction which makes additions to the sum of our knowledge. The restraint which is employed to repress, and finally to extirpate the constant inclination to depart from certain rules, is termed discipline. It is distinguished from culture, which aims at the formation of a certain degree of skill, without attempting to repress or to destroy any other mental power, already existing. In the cultivation of a talent, which has given evidence of an impulse towards self-development, discipline takes a negative,[[74]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-74) culture and doctrine a positive, part.\n\n[[74]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-74) I am well aware that, in the language of the schools, the term discipline is usually employed as synonymous with instruction. But there are so many cases in which it is necessary to distinguish the notion of the former, as a course of corrective training, from that of the latter, as the communication of knowledge, and the nature of things itself demands the appropriation of the most suitable expressions for this distinction, that it is my desire that the former terms should never be employed in any other than a negative signification.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"83.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","text":"That natural dispositions and talents (such as imagination and wit), which ask a free and unlimited development, require in many respects the corrective influence of discipline, every one will readily grant. But it may well appear strange that reason, whose proper duty it is to prescribe rules of discipline to all the other powers of the mind, should itself require this corrective. It has, in fact, hitherto escaped this humiliation, only because, in presence of its magnificent pretensions and high position, no one could readily suspect it to be capable of substituting fancies for conceptions, and words for things.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"83.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","text":"Reason, when employed in the field of experience, does not stand in need of criticism, because its principles are subjected to the continual test of empirical observations. Nor is criticism requisite in the sphere of mathematics, where the conceptions of reason must always be presented in concreto in pure intuition, and baseless or arbitrary assertions are discovered without difficulty. But where reason is not held in a plain track by the influence of empirical or of pure intuition, that is, when it is employed in the transcendental sphere of pure conceptions, it stands in great need of discipline, to restrain its propensity to overstep the limits of possible experience and to keep it from wandering into error. In fact, the utility of the philosophy of pure reason is entirely of this negative character. Particular errors may be corrected by particular animadversions, and the causes of these errors may be eradicated by criticism. But where we find, as in the case of pure reason, a complete system of illusions and fallacies, closely connected with each other and depending upon grand general principles, there seems to be required a peculiar and negative code of mental legislation, which, under the denomination of a discipline, and founded upon the nature of reason and the objects of its exercise, shall constitute a system of thorough examination and testing, which no fallacy will be able to withstand or escape from, under whatever disguise or concealment it may lurk.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"83.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","text":"But the reader must remark that, in this the second division of our transcendental Critique the discipline of pure reason is not directed to the content, but to the method of the cognition of pure reason. The former task has been completed in the doctrine of elements. But there is so much similarity in the mode of employing the faculty of reason, whatever be the object to which it is applied, while, at the same time, its employment in the transcendental sphere is so essentially different in kind from every other, that, without the warning negative influence of a discipline specially directed to that end, the errors are unavoidable which spring from the unskillful employment of the methods which are originated by reason but which are out of place in this sphere.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"83.5"}],"sha256":"765dda2950ca6b977f0b45fe32b2ace815baefd7c5dd5730bcac840c5abb7fe4","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP","title":"Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism","order":84,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIDP","text":"### Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism\n\nThe science of mathematics presents the most brilliant example of the extension of the sphere of pure reason without the aid of experience. Examples are always contagious; and they exert an especial influence on the same faculty, which naturally flatters itself that it will have the same good fortune in other case as fell to its lot in one fortunate instance. Hence pure reason hopes to be able to extend its empire in the transcendental sphere with equal success and security, especially when it applies the same method which was attended with such brilliant results in the science of mathematics. It is, therefore, of the highest importance for us to know whether the method of arriving at demonstrative certainty, which is termed mathematical, be identical with that by which we endeavour to attain the same degree of certainty in philosophy, and which is termed in that science dogmatical.\n\nPhilosophical cognition is the cognition of reason by means of conceptions; mathematical cognition is cognition by means of the construction of conceptions. The construction of a conception is the presentation à priori of the intuition which corresponds to the conception. For this purpose a non-empirical intuition is requisite, which, as an intuition, is an individual object; while, as the construction of a conception (a general representation), it must be seen to be universally valid for all the possible intuitions which rank under that conception. Thus I construct a triangle, by the presentation of the object which corresponds to this conception, either by mere imagination, in pure intuition, or upon paper, in empirical intuition, in both cases completely à priori, without borrowing the type of that figure from any experience. The individual figure drawn upon paper is empirical; but it serves, notwithstanding, to indicate the conception, even in its universality, because in this empirical intuition we keep our eye merely on the act of the construction of the conception, and pay no attention to the various modes of determining it, for example, its size, the length of its sides, the size of its angles, these not in the least affecting the essential character of the conception.\n\nPhilosophical cognition, accordingly, regards the particular only in the general; mathematical the general in the particular, nay, in the individual. This is done, however, entirely à priori and by means of pure reason, so that, as this individual figure is determined under certain universal conditions of construction, the object of the conception, to which this individual figure corresponds as its schema, must be cogitated as universally determined.\n\nThe essential difference of these two modes of cognition consists, therefore, in this formal quality; it does not regard the difference of the matter or objects of both. Those thinkers who aim at distinguishing philosophy from mathematics by asserting that the former has to do with quality merely, and the latter with quantity, have mistaken the effect for the cause. The reason why mathematical cognition can relate only to quantity is to be found in its form alone. For it is the conception of quantities only that is capable of being constructed, that is, presented à priori in intuition; while qualities cannot be given in any other than an empirical intuition. Hence the cognition of qualities by reason is possible only through conceptions. No one can find an intuition which shall correspond to the conception of reality, except in experience; it cannot be presented to the mind à priori and antecedently to the empirical consciousness of a reality. We can form an intuition, by means of the mere conception of it, of a cone, without the aid of experience; but the colour of the cone we cannot know except from experience. I cannot present an intuition of a cause, except in an example which experience offers to me. Besides, philosophy, as well as mathematics, treats of quantities; as, for example, of totality, infinity, and so on. Mathematics, too, treats of the difference of lines and surfaces—as spaces of different quality, of the continuity of extension—as a quality thereof. But, although in such cases they have a common object, the mode in which reason considers that object is very different in philosophy from what it is in mathematics. The former confines itself to the general conceptions; the latter can do nothing with a mere conception, it hastens to intuition. In this intuition it regards the conception in concreto, not empirically, but in an à priori intuition, which it has constructed; and in which, all the results which follow from the general conditions of the construction of the conception are in all cases valid for the object of the constructed conception.\n\nSuppose that the conception of a triangle is given to a philosopher and that he is required to discover, by the philosophical method, what relation the sum of its angles bears to a right angle. He has nothing before him but the conception of a figure enclosed within three right lines, and, consequently, with the same number of angles. He may analyse the conception of a right line, of an angle, or of the number three as long as he pleases, but he will not discover any properties not contained in these conceptions. But, if this question is proposed to a geometrician, he at once begins by constructing a triangle. He knows that two right angles are equal to the sum of all the contiguous angles which proceed from one point in a straight line; and he goes on to produce one side of his triangle, thus forming two adjacent angles which are together equal to two right angles. He then divides the exterior of these angles, by drawing a line parallel with the opposite side of the triangle, and immediately perceives that he has thus got an exterior adjacent angle which is equal to the interior. Proceeding in this way, through a chain of inferences, and always on the ground of intuition, he arrives at a clear and universally valid solution of the question.\n\nBut mathematics does not confine itself to the construction of quantities (quanta), as in the case of geometry; it occupies itself with pure quantity also (quantitas), as in the case of algebra, where complete abstraction is made of the properties of the object indicated by the conception of quantity. In algebra, a certain method of notation by signs is adopted, and these indicate the different possible constructions of quantities, the extraction of roots, and so on. After having thus denoted the general conception of quantities, according to their different relations, the different operations by which quantity or number is increased or diminished are presented in intuition in accordance with general rules. Thus, when one quantity is to be divided by another, the signs which denote both are placed in the form peculiar to the operation of division; and thus algebra, by means of a symbolical construction of quantity, just as geometry, with its ostensive or geometrical construction (a construction of the objects themselves), arrives at results which discursive cognition cannot hope to reach by the aid of mere conceptions.\n\nNow, what is the cause of this difference in the fortune of the philosopher and the mathematician, the former of whom follows the path of conceptions, while the latter pursues that of intuitions, which he represents, à priori, in correspondence with his conceptions? The cause is evident from what has been already demonstrated in the introduction to this Critique. We do not, in the present case, want to discover analytical propositions, which may be produced merely by analysing our conceptions—for in this the philosopher would have the advantage over his rival; we aim at the discovery of synthetical propositions—such synthetical propositions, moreover, as can be cognized à priori. I must not confine myself to that which I actually cogitate in my conception of a triangle, for this is nothing more than the mere definition; I must try to go beyond that, and to arrive at properties which are not contained in, although they belong to, the conception. Now, this is impossible, unless I determine the object present to my mind according to the conditions, either of empirical, or of pure, intuition. In the former case, I should have an empirical proposition (arrived at by actual measurement of the angles of the triangle), which would possess neither universality nor necessity; but that would be of no value. In the latter, I proceed by geometrical construction, by means of which I collect, in a pure intuition, just as I would in an empirical intuition, all the various properties which belong to the schema of a triangle in general, and consequently to its conception, and thus construct synthetical propositions which possess the attribute of universality.\n\nIt would be vain to philosophize upon the triangle, that is, to reflect on it discursively; I should get no further than the definition with which I had been obliged to set out. There are certainly transcendental synthetical propositions which are framed by means of pure conceptions, and which form the peculiar distinction of philosophy; but these do not relate to any particular thing, but to a thing in general, and enounce the conditions under which the perception of it may become a part of possible experience. But the science of mathematics has nothing to do with such questions, nor with the question of existence in any fashion; it is concerned merely with the properties of objects in themselves, only in so far as these are connected with the conception of the objects.\n\nIn the above example, we merely attempted to show the great difference which exists between the discursive employment of reason in the sphere of conceptions, and its intuitive exercise by means of the construction of conceptions. The question naturally arises: What is the cause which necessitates this twofold exercise of reason, and how are we to discover whether it is the philosophical or the mathematical method which reason is pursuing in an argument?\n\nAll our knowledge relates, finally, to possible intuitions, for it is these alone that present objects to the mind. An à priori or non-empirical conception contains either a pure intuition—and in this case it can be constructed; or it contains nothing but the synthesis of possible intuitions, which are not given à priori. In this latter case, it may help us to form synthetical à priori judgements, but only in the discursive method, by conceptions, not in the intuitive, by means of the construction of conceptions.\n\nThe only à priori intuition is that of the pure form of phenomena—space and time. A conception of space and time as quanta may be presented à priori in intuition, that is, constructed, either alone with their quality (figure), or as pure quantity (the mere synthesis of the homogeneous), by means of number. But the matter of phenomena, by which things are given in space and time, can be presented only in perception, à posteriori. The only conception which represents à priori this empirical content of phenomena is the conception of a thing in general; and the à priori synthetical cognition of this conception can give us nothing more than the rule for the synthesis of that which may be contained in the corresponding à posteriori perception; it is utterly inadequate to present an à priori intuition of the real object, which must necessarily be empirical.\n\nSynthetical propositions, which relate to things in general, an à priori intuition of which is impossible, are transcendental. For this reason transcendental propositions cannot be framed by means of the construction of conceptions; they are à priori, and based entirely on conceptions themselves. They contain merely the rule, by which we are to seek in the world of perception or experience the synthetical unity of that which cannot be intuited à priori. But they are incompetent to present any of the conceptions which appear in them in an à priori intuition; these can be given only à posteriori, in experience, which, however, is itself possible only through these synthetical principles.\n\nIf we are to form a synthetical judgement regarding a conception, we must go beyond it, to the intuition in which it is given. If we keep to what is contained in the conception, the judgement is merely analytical—it is merely an explanation of what we have cogitated in the conception. But I can pass from the conception to the pure or empirical intuition which corresponds to it. I can proceed to examine my conception in concreto, and to cognize, either à priori or à posteriori, what I find in the object of the conception. The former—à priori cognition—is rational-mathematical cognition by means of the construction of the conception; the latter—à posteriori cognition—is purely empirical cognition, which does not possess the attributes of necessity and universality. Thus I may analyse the conception I have of gold; but I gain no new information from this analysis, I merely enumerate the different properties which I had connected with the notion indicated by the word. My knowledge has gained in logical clearness and arrangement, but no addition has been made to it. But if I take the matter which is indicated by this name, and submit it to the examination of my senses, I am enabled to form several synthetical—although still empirical—propositions. The mathematical conception of a triangle I should construct, that is, present à priori in intuition, and in this way attain to rational-synthetical cognition. But when the transcendental conception of reality, or substance, or power is presented to my mind, I find that it does not relate to or indicate either an empirical or pure intuition, but that it indicates merely the synthesis of empirical intuitions, which cannot of course be given à priori. The synthesis in such a conception cannot proceed à priori—without the aid of experience—to the intuition which corresponds to the conception; and, for this reason, none of these conceptions can produce a determinative synthetical proposition, they can never present more than a principle of the synthesis[[75]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-75) of possible empirical intuitions. A transcendental proposition is, therefore, a synthetical cognition of reason by means of pure conceptions and the discursive method, and it renders possible all synthetical unity in empirical cognition, though it cannot present us with any intuition à priori.\n\n[[75]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-75) In the case of the conception of cause, I do really go beyond the empirical conception of an event—but not to the intuition which presents this conception in concreto, but only to the time-conditions, which may be found in experience to correspond to the conception. My procedure is, therefore, strictly according to conceptions; I cannot in a case of this kind employ the construction of conceptions, because the conception is merely a rule for the synthesis of perceptions, which are not pure intuitions, and which, therefore, cannot be given à priori.\n\nThere is thus a twofold exercise of reason. Both modes have the properties of universality and an à priori origin in common, but are, in their procedure, of widely different character. The reason of this is that in the world of phenomena, in which alone objects are presented to our minds, there are two main elements—the form of intuition (space and time), which can be cognized and determined completely à priori, and the matter or content—that which is presented in space and time, and which, consequently, contains a something—an existence corresponding to our powers of sensation. As regards the latter, which can never be given in a determinate mode except by experience, there are no à priori notions which relate to it, except the undetermined conceptions of the synthesis of possible sensations, in so far as these belong (in a possible experience) to the unity of consciousness. As regards the former, we can determine our conceptions à priori in intuition, inasmuch as we are ourselves the creators of the objects of the conceptions in space and time—these objects being regarded simply as quanta. In the one case, reason proceeds according to conceptions and can do nothing more than subject phenomena to these—which can only be determined empirically, that is, à posteriori—in conformity, however, with those conceptions as the rules of all empirical synthesis. In the other case, reason proceeds by the construction of conceptions; and, as these conceptions relate to an à priori intuition, they may be given and determined in pure intuition à priori, and without the aid of empirical data. The examination and consideration of everything that exists in space or time—whether it is a quantum or not, in how far the particular something (which fills space or time) is a primary substratum, or a mere determination of some other existence, whether it relates to anything else—either as cause or effect, whether its existence is isolated or in reciprocal connection with and dependence upon others, the possibility of this existence, its reality and necessity or opposites—all these form part of the cognition of reason on the ground of conceptions, and this cognition is termed philosophical. But to determine à priori an intuition in space (its figure), to divide time into periods, or merely to cognize the quantity of an intuition in space and time, and to determine it by number—all this is an operation of reason by means of the construction of conceptions, and is called mathematical.\n\nThe success which attends the efforts of reason in the sphere of mathematics naturally fosters the expectation that the same good fortune will be its lot, if it applies the mathematical method in other regions of mental endeavour besides that of quantities. Its success is thus great, because it can support all its conceptions by à priori intuitions and, in this way, make itself a master, as it were, over nature; while pure philosophy, with its à priori discursive conceptions, bungles about in the world of nature, and cannot accredit or show any à priori evidence of the reality of these conceptions. Masters in the science of mathematics are confident of the success of this method; indeed, it is a common persuasion that it is capable of being applied to any subject of human thought. They have hardly ever reflected or philosophized on their favourite science—a task of great difficulty; and the specific difference between the two modes of employing the faculty of reason has never entered their thoughts. Rules current in the field of common experience, and which common sense stamps everywhere with its approval, are regarded by them as axiomatic. From what source the conceptions of space and time, with which (as the only primitive quanta) they have to deal, enter their minds, is a question which they do not trouble themselves to answer; and they think it just as unnecessary to examine into the origin of the pure conceptions of the understanding and the extent of their validity. All they have to do with them is to employ them. In all this they are perfectly right, if they do not overstep the limits of the sphere of nature. But they pass, unconsciously, from the world of sense to the insecure ground of pure transcendental conceptions (instabilis tellus, innabilis unda), where they can neither stand nor swim, and where the tracks of their footsteps are obliterated by time; while the march of mathematics is pursued on a broad and magnificent highway, which the latest posterity shall frequent without fear of danger or impediment.\n\nAs we have taken upon us the task of determining, clearly and certainly, the limits of pure reason in the sphere of transcendentalism, and as the efforts of reason in this direction are persisted in, even after the plainest and most expressive warnings, hope still beckoning us past the limits of experience into the splendours of the intellectual world—it becomes necessary to cut away the last anchor of this fallacious and fantastic hope. We shall, accordingly, show that the mathematical method is unattended in the sphere of philosophy by the least advantage—except, perhaps, that it more plainly exhibits its own inadequacy—that geometry and philosophy are two quite different things, although they go hand in hand in the field of natural science, and, consequently, that the procedure of the one can never be imitated by the other.\n\nThe evidence of mathematics rests upon definitions, axioms, and demonstrations. I shall be satisfied with showing that none of these forms can be employed or imitated in philosophy in the sense in which they are understood by mathematicians; and that the geometrician, if he employs his method in philosophy, will succeed only in building card-castles, while the employment of the philosophical method in mathematics can result in nothing but mere verbiage. The essential business of philosophy, indeed, is to mark out the limits of the science; and even the mathematician, unless his talent is naturally circumscribed and limited to this particular department of knowledge, cannot turn a deaf ear to the warnings of philosophy, or set himself above its direction.\n\nI. Of Definitions. A definition is, as the term itself indicates, the representation, upon primary grounds, of the complete conception of a thing within its own limits.[[76]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-76) Accordingly, an empirical conception cannot be defined, it can only be explained. For, as there are in such a conception only a certain number of marks or signs, which denote a certain class of sensuous objects, we can never be sure that we do not cogitate under the word which indicates the same object, at one time a greater, at another a smaller number of signs. Thus, one person may cogitate in his conception of gold, in addition to its properties of weight, colour, malleability, that of resisting rust, while another person may be ignorant of this quality. We employ certain signs only so long as we require them for the sake of distinction; new observations abstract some and add new ones, so that an empirical conception never remains within permanent limits. It is, in fact, useless to define a conception of this kind. If, for example, we are speaking of water and its properties, we do not stop at what we actually think by the word water, but proceed to observation and experiment; and the word, with the few signs attached to it, is more properly a designation than a conception of the thing. A definition in this case would evidently be nothing more than a determination of the word. In the second place, no à priori conception, such as those of substance, cause, right, fitness, and so on, can be defined. For I can never be sure, that the clear representation of a given conception (which is given in a confused state) has been fully developed, until I know that the representation is adequate with its object. But, inasmuch as the conception, as it is presented to the mind, may contain a number of obscure representations, which we do not observe in our analysis, although we employ them in our application of the conception, I can never be sure that my analysis is complete, while examples may make this probable, although they can never demonstrate the fact. Instead of the word definition, I should rather employ the term exposition—a more modest expression, which the critic may accept without surrendering his doubts as to the completeness of the analysis of any such conception. As, therefore, neither empirical nor à priori conceptions are capable of definition, we have to see whether the only other kind of conceptions—arbitrary conceptions—can be subjected to this mental operation. Such a conception can always be defined; for I must know thoroughly what I wished to cogitate in it, as it was I who created it, and it was not given to my mind either by the nature of my understanding or by experience. At the same time, I cannot say that, by such a definition, I have defined a real object. If the conception is based upon empirical conditions, if, for example, I have a conception of a clock for a ship, this arbitrary conception does not assure me of the existence or even of the possibility of the object. My definition of such a conception would with more propriety be termed a declaration of a project than a definition of an object. There are no other conceptions which can bear definition, except those which contain an arbitrary synthesis, which can be constructed à priori. Consequently, the science of mathematics alone possesses definitions. For the object here thought is presented à priori in intuition; and thus it can never contain more or less than the conception, because the conception of the object has been given by the definition—and primarily, that is, without deriving the definition from any other source. Philosophical definitions are, therefore, merely expositions of given conceptions, while mathematical definitions are constructions of conceptions originally formed by the mind itself; the former are produced by analysis, the completeness of which is never demonstratively certain, the latter by a synthesis. In a mathematical definition the conception is formed, in a philosophical definition it is only explained. From this it follows:\n\n[[76]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-76) The definition must describe the conception completely that is, omit none of the marks or signs of which it composed; within its own limits, that is, it must be precise, and enumerate no more signs than belong to the conception; and on primary grounds, that is to say, the limitations of the bounds of the conception must not be deduced from other conceptions, as in this case a proof would be necessary, and the so-called definition would be incapable of taking its place at the head of all the judgements we have to form regarding an object.\n\n(a) That we must not imitate, in philosophy, the mathematical usage of commencing with definitions—except by way of hypothesis or experiment. For, as all so-called philosophical definitions are merely analyses of given conceptions, these conceptions, although only in a confused form, must precede the analysis; and the incomplete exposition must precede the complete, so that we may be able to draw certain inferences from the characteristics which an incomplete analysis has enabled us to discover, before we attain to the complete exposition or definition of the conception. In one word, a full and clear definition ought, in philosophy, rather to form the conclusion than the commencement of our labours.[[77]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-77) In mathematics, on the contrary, we cannot have a conception prior to the definition; it is the definition which gives us the conception, and it must for this reason form the commencement of every chain of mathematical reasoning.\n\n[[77]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-77) Philosophy abounds in faulty definitions, especially such as contain some of the elements requisite to form a complete definition. If a conception could not be employed in reasoning before it had been defined, it would fare ill with all philosophical thought. But, as incompletely defined conceptions may always be employed without detriment to truth, so far as our analysis of the elements contained in them proceeds, imperfect definitions, that is, propositions which are properly not definitions, but merely approximations thereto, may be used with great advantage. In mathematics, definition belongs ad esse, in philosophy ad melius esse. It is a difficult task to construct a proper definition. Jurists are still without a complete definition of the idea of right.\n\n(b) Mathematical definitions cannot be erroneous. For the conception is given only in and through the definition, and thus it contains only what has been cogitated in the definition. But although a definition cannot be incorrect, as regards its content, an error may sometimes, although seldom, creep into the form. This error consists in a want of precision. Thus the common definition of a circle—that it is a curved line, every point in which is equally distant from another point called the centre—is faulty, from the fact that the determination indicated by the word curved is superfluous. For there ought to be a particular theorem, which may be easily proved from the definition, to the effect that every line, which has all its points at equal distances from another point, must be a curved line—that is, that not even the smallest part of it can be straight. Analytical definitions, on the other hand, may be erroneous in many respects, either by the introduction of signs which do not actually exist in the conception, or by wanting in that completeness which forms the essential of a definition. In the latter case, the definition is necessarily defective, because we can never be fully certain of the completeness of our analysis. For these reasons, the method of definition employed in mathematics cannot be imitated in philosophy.\n\n2. Of Axioms. These, in so far as they are immediately certain, are à priori synthetical principles. Now, one conception cannot be connected synthetically and yet immediately with another; because, if we wish to proceed out of and beyond a conception, a third mediating cognition is necessary. And, as philosophy is a cognition of reason by the aid of conceptions alone, there is to be found in it no principle which deserves to be called an axiom. Mathematics, on the other hand, may possess axioms, because it can always connect the predicates of an object à priori, and without any mediating term, by means of the construction of conceptions in intuition. Such is the case with the proposition: Three points can always lie in a plane. On the other hand, no synthetical principle which is based upon conceptions, can ever be immediately certain (for example, the proposition: Everything that happens has a cause), because I require a mediating term to connect the two conceptions of event and cause—namely, the condition of time-determination in an experience, and I cannot cognize any such principle immediately and from conceptions alone. Discursive principles are, accordingly, very different from intuitive principles or axioms. The former always require deduction, which in the case of the latter may be altogether dispensed with. Axioms are, for this reason, always self-evident, while philosophical principles, whatever may be the degree of certainty they possess, cannot lay any claim to such a distinction. No synthetical proposition of pure transcendental reason can be so evident, as is often rashly enough declared, as the statement, twice two are four. It is true that in the Analytic I introduced into the list of principles of the pure understanding, certain axioms of intuition; but the principle there discussed was not itself an axiom, but served merely to present the principle of the possibility of axioms in general, while it was really nothing more than a principle based upon conceptions. For it is one part of the duty of transcendental philosophy to establish the possibility of mathematics itself. Philosophy possesses, then, no axioms, and has no right to impose its à priori principles upon thought, until it has established their authority and validity by a thoroughgoing deduction.\n\n3. Of Demonstrations. Only an apodeictic proof, based upon intuition, can be termed a demonstration. Experience teaches us what is, but it cannot convince us that it might not have been otherwise. Hence a proof upon empirical grounds cannot be apodeictic. À priori conceptions, in discursive cognition, can never produce intuitive certainty or evidence, however certain the judgement they present may be. Mathematics alone, therefore, contains demonstrations, because it does not deduce its cognition from conceptions, but from the construction of conceptions, that is, from intuition, which can be given à priori in accordance with conceptions. The method of algebra, in equations, from which the correct answer is deduced by reduction, is a kind of construction—not geometrical, but by symbols—in which all conceptions, especially those of the relations of quantities, are represented in intuition by signs; and thus the conclusions in that science are secured from errors by the fact that every proof is submitted to ocular evidence. Philosophical cognition does not possess this advantage, it being required to consider the general always in abstracto (by means of conceptions), while mathematics can always consider it in concreto (in an individual intuition), and at the same time by means of à priori representation, whereby all errors are rendered manifest to the senses. The former—discursive proofs—ought to be termed acroamatic proofs, rather than demonstrations, as only words are employed in them, while demonstrations proper, as the term itself indicates, always require a reference to the intuition of the object.\n\nIt follows from all these considerations that it is not consonant with the nature of philosophy, especially in the sphere of pure reason, to employ the dogmatical method, and to adorn itself with the titles and insignia of mathematical science. It does not belong to that order, and can only hope for a fraternal union with that science. Its attempts at mathematical evidence are vain pretensions, which can only keep it back from its true aim, which is to detect the illusory procedure of reason when transgressing its proper limits, and by fully explaining and analysing our conceptions, to conduct us from the dim regions of speculation to the clear region of modest self-knowledge. Reason must not, therefore, in its transcendental endeavours, look forward with such confidence, as if the path it is pursuing led straight to its aim, nor reckon with such security upon its premisses, as to consider it unnecessary to take a step back, or to keep a strict watch for errors, which, overlooked in the principles, may be detected in the arguments themselves—in which case it may be requisite either to determine these principles with greater strictness, or to change them entirely.\n\nI divide all apodeictic propositions, whether demonstrable or immediately certain, into dogmata and mathemata. A direct synthetical proposition, based on conceptions, is a dogma; a proposition of the same kind, based on the construction of conceptions, is a mathema. Analytical judgements do not teach us any more about an object than what was contained in the conception we had of it; because they do not extend our cognition beyond our conception of an object, they merely elucidate the conception. They cannot therefore be with propriety termed dogmas. Of the two kinds of à priori synthetical propositions above mentioned, only those which are employed in philosophy can, according to the general mode of speech, bear this name; those of arithmetic or geometry would not be rightly so denominated. Thus the customary mode of speaking confirms the explanation given above, and the conclusion arrived at, that only those judgements which are based upon conceptions, not on the construction of conceptions, can be termed dogmatical.\n\nThus, pure reason, in the sphere of speculation, does not contain a single direct synthetical judgement based upon conceptions. By means of ideas, it is, as we have shown, incapable of producing synthetical judgements, which are objectively valid; by means of the conceptions of the understanding, it establishes certain indubitable principles, not, however, directly on the basis of conceptions, but only indirectly by means of the relation of these conceptions to something of a purely contingent nature, namely, possible experience. When experience is presupposed, these principles are apodeictically certain, but in themselves, and directly, they cannot even be cognized à priori. Thus the given conceptions of cause and event will not be sufficient for the demonstration of the proposition: Every event has a cause. For this reason, it is not a dogma; although from another point of view, that of experience, it is capable of being proved to demonstration. The proper term for such a proposition is principle, and not theorem (although it does require to be proved), because it possesses the remarkable peculiarity of being the condition of the possibility of its own ground of proof, that is, experience, and of forming a necessary presupposition in all empirical observation.\n\nIf then, in the speculative sphere of pure reason, no dogmata are to be found; all dogmatical methods, whether borrowed from mathematics, or invented by philosophical thinkers, are alike inappropriate and inefficient. They only serve to conceal errors and fallacies, and to deceive philosophy, whose duty it is to see that reason pursues a safe and straight path. A philosophical method may, however, be systematical. For our reason is, subjectively considered, itself a system, and, in the sphere of mere conceptions, a system of investigation according to principles of unity, the material being supplied by experience alone. But this is not the proper place for discussing the peculiar method of transcendental philosophy, as our present task is simply to examine whether our faculties are capable of erecting an edifice on the basis of pure reason, and how far they may proceed with the materials at their command.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0001","text":"### Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism\n\nThe science of mathematics presents the most brilliant example of the extension of the sphere of pure reason without the aid of experience. Examples are always contagious; and they exert an especial influence on the same faculty, which naturally flatters itself that it will have the same good fortune in other case as fell to its lot in one fortunate instance. Hence pure reason hopes to be able to extend its empire in the transcendental sphere with equal success and security, especially when it applies the same method which was attended with such brilliant results in the science of mathematics. It is, therefore, of the highest importance for us to know whether the method of arriving at demonstrative certainty, which is termed mathematical, be identical with that by which we endeavour to attain the same degree of certainty in philosophy, and which is termed in that science dogmatical.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"84.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0002","text":"Philosophical cognition is the cognition of reason by means of conceptions; mathematical cognition is cognition by means of the construction of conceptions. The construction of a conception is the presentation à priori of the intuition which corresponds to the conception. For this purpose a non-empirical intuition is requisite, which, as an intuition, is an individual object; while, as the construction of a conception (a general representation), it must be seen to be universally valid for all the possible intuitions which rank under that conception. Thus I construct a triangle, by the presentation of the object which corresponds to this conception, either by mere imagination, in pure intuition, or upon paper, in empirical intuition, in both cases completely à priori, without borrowing the type of that figure from any experience. The individual figure drawn upon paper is empirical; but it serves, notwithstanding, to indicate the conception, even in its universality, because in this empirical intuition we keep our eye merely on the act of the construction of the conception, and pay no attention to the various modes of determining it, for example, its size, the length of its sides, the size of its angles, these not in the least affecting the essential character of the conception.\n\nPhilosophical cognition, accordingly, regards the particular only in the general; mathematical the general in the particular, nay, in the individual. This is done, however, entirely à priori and by means of pure reason, so that, as this individual figure is determined under certain universal conditions of construction, the object of the conception, to which this individual figure corresponds as its schema, must be cogitated as universally determined.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"84.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0003","text":"The essential difference of these two modes of cognition consists, therefore, in this formal quality; it does not regard the difference of the matter or objects of both. Those thinkers who aim at distinguishing philosophy from mathematics by asserting that the former has to do with quality merely, and the latter with quantity, have mistaken the effect for the cause. The reason why mathematical cognition can relate only to quantity is to be found in its form alone. For it is the conception of quantities only that is capable of being constructed, that is, presented à priori in intuition; while qualities cannot be given in any other than an empirical intuition. Hence the cognition of qualities by reason is possible only through conceptions. No one can find an intuition which shall correspond to the conception of reality, except in experience; it cannot be presented to the mind à priori and antecedently to the empirical consciousness of a reality. We can form an intuition, by means of the mere conception of it, of a cone, without the aid of experience; but the colour of the cone we cannot know except from experience. I cannot present an intuition of a cause, except in an example which experience offers to me. Besides, philosophy, as well as mathematics, treats of quantities; as, for example, of totality, infinity, and so on. Mathematics, too, treats of the difference of lines and surfaces—as spaces of different quality, of the continuity of extension—as a quality thereof. But, although in such cases they have a common object, the mode in which reason considers that object is very different in philosophy from what it is in mathematics. The former confines itself to the general conceptions; the latter can do nothing with a mere conception, it hastens to intuition. In this intuition it regards the conception in concreto, not empirically, but in an à priori intuition, which it has constructed; and in which, all the results which follow from the general conditions of the construction of the conception are in all cases valid for the object of the constructed conception.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"84.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0004","text":"Suppose that the conception of a triangle is given to a philosopher and that he is required to discover, by the philosophical method, what relation the sum of its angles bears to a right angle. He has nothing before him but the conception of a figure enclosed within three right lines, and, consequently, with the same number of angles. He may analyse the conception of a right line, of an angle, or of the number three as long as he pleases, but he will not discover any properties not contained in these conceptions. But, if this question is proposed to a geometrician, he at once begins by constructing a triangle. He knows that two right angles are equal to the sum of all the contiguous angles which proceed from one point in a straight line; and he goes on to produce one side of his triangle, thus forming two adjacent angles which are together equal to two right angles. He then divides the exterior of these angles, by drawing a line parallel with the opposite side of the triangle, and immediately perceives that he has thus got an exterior adjacent angle which is equal to the interior. Proceeding in this way, through a chain of inferences, and always on the ground of intuition, he arrives at a clear and universally valid solution of the question.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"84.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0005","text":"But mathematics does not confine itself to the construction of quantities (quanta), as in the case of geometry; it occupies itself with pure quantity also (quantitas), as in the case of algebra, where complete abstraction is made of the properties of the object indicated by the conception of quantity. In algebra, a certain method of notation by signs is adopted, and these indicate the different possible constructions of quantities, the extraction of roots, and so on. After having thus denoted the general conception of quantities, according to their different relations, the different operations by which quantity or number is increased or diminished are presented in intuition in accordance with general rules. Thus, when one quantity is to be divided by another, the signs which denote both are placed in the form peculiar to the operation of division; and thus algebra, by means of a symbolical construction of quantity, just as geometry, with its ostensive or geometrical construction (a construction of the objects themselves), arrives at results which discursive cognition cannot hope to reach by the aid of mere conceptions.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"84.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0006","text":"Now, what is the cause of this difference in the fortune of the philosopher and the mathematician, the former of whom follows the path of conceptions, while the latter pursues that of intuitions, which he represents, à priori, in correspondence with his conceptions? The cause is evident from what has been already demonstrated in the introduction to this Critique. We do not, in the present case, want to discover analytical propositions, which may be produced merely by analysing our conceptions—for in this the philosopher would have the advantage over his rival; we aim at the discovery of synthetical propositions—such synthetical propositions, moreover, as can be cognized à priori. I must not confine myself to that which I actually cogitate in my conception of a triangle, for this is nothing more than the mere definition; I must try to go beyond that, and to arrive at properties which are not contained in, although they belong to, the conception. Now, this is impossible, unless I determine the object present to my mind according to the conditions, either of empirical, or of pure, intuition. In the former case, I should have an empirical proposition (arrived at by actual measurement of the angles of the triangle), which would possess neither universality nor necessity; but that would be of no value. In the latter, I proceed by geometrical construction, by means of which I collect, in a pure intuition, just as I would in an empirical intuition, all the various properties which belong to the schema of a triangle in general, and consequently to its conception, and thus construct synthetical propositions which possess the attribute of universality.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"84.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0007","text":"It would be vain to philosophize upon the triangle, that is, to reflect on it discursively; I should get no further than the definition with which I had been obliged to set out. There are certainly transcendental synthetical propositions which are framed by means of pure conceptions, and which form the peculiar distinction of philosophy; but these do not relate to any particular thing, but to a thing in general, and enounce the conditions under which the perception of it may become a part of possible experience. But the science of mathematics has nothing to do with such questions, nor with the question of existence in any fashion; it is concerned merely with the properties of objects in themselves, only in so far as these are connected with the conception of the objects.\n\nIn the above example, we merely attempted to show the great difference which exists between the discursive employment of reason in the sphere of conceptions, and its intuitive exercise by means of the construction of conceptions. The question naturally arises: What is the cause which necessitates this twofold exercise of reason, and how are we to discover whether it is the philosophical or the mathematical method which reason is pursuing in an argument?\n\nAll our knowledge relates, finally, to possible intuitions, for it is these alone that present objects to the mind. An à priori or non-empirical conception contains either a pure intuition—and in this case it can be constructed; or it contains nothing but the synthesis of possible intuitions, which are not given à priori. In this latter case, it may help us to form synthetical à priori judgements, but only in the discursive method, by conceptions, not in the intuitive, by means of the construction of conceptions.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"84.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","text":"The only à priori intuition is that of the pure form of phenomena—space and time. A conception of space and time as quanta may be presented à priori in intuition, that is, constructed, either alone with their quality (figure), or as pure quantity (the mere synthesis of the homogeneous), by means of number. But the matter of phenomena, by which things are given in space and time, can be presented only in perception, à posteriori. The only conception which represents à priori this empirical content of phenomena is the conception of a thing in general; and the à priori synthetical cognition of this conception can give us nothing more than the rule for the synthesis of that which may be contained in the corresponding à posteriori perception; it is utterly inadequate to present an à priori intuition of the real object, which must necessarily be empirical.\n\nSynthetical propositions, which relate to things in general, an à priori intuition of which is impossible, are transcendental. For this reason transcendental propositions cannot be framed by means of the construction of conceptions; they are à priori, and based entirely on conceptions themselves. They contain merely the rule, by which we are to seek in the world of perception or experience the synthetical unity of that which cannot be intuited à priori. But they are incompetent to present any of the conceptions which appear in them in an à priori intuition; these can be given only à posteriori, in experience, which, however, is itself possible only through these synthetical principles.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"84.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0009","text":"If we are to form a synthetical judgement regarding a conception, we must go beyond it, to the intuition in which it is given. If we keep to what is contained in the conception, the judgement is merely analytical—it is merely an explanation of what we have cogitated in the conception. But I can pass from the conception to the pure or empirical intuition which corresponds to it. I can proceed to examine my conception in concreto, and to cognize, either à priori or à posteriori, what I find in the object of the conception. The former—à priori cognition—is rational-mathematical cognition by means of the construction of the conception; the latter—à posteriori cognition—is purely empirical cognition, which does not possess the attributes of necessity and universality. Thus I may analyse the conception I have of gold; but I gain no new information from this analysis, I merely enumerate the different properties which I had connected with the notion indicated by the word. My knowledge has gained in logical clearness and arrangement, but no addition has been made to it. But if I take the matter which is indicated by this name, and submit it to the examination of my senses, I am enabled to form several synthetical—although still empirical—propositions. The mathematical conception of a triangle I should construct, that is, present à priori in intuition, and in this way attain to rational-synthetical cognition. But when the transcendental conception of reality, or substance, or power is presented to my mind, I find that it does not relate to or indicate either an empirical or pure intuition, but that it indicates merely the synthesis of empirical intuitions, which cannot of course be given à priori. The synthesis in such a conception cannot proceed à priori—without the aid of experience—to the intuition which corresponds to the conception; and, for this reason, none of these conceptions can produce a determinative synthetical proposition, they can never present more than a principle of the synthesis[[75]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-75) of possible empirical intuitions. A transcendental proposition is, therefore, a synthetical cognition of reason by means of pure conceptions and the discursive method, and it renders possible all synthetical unity in empirical cognition, though it cannot present us with any intuition à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"84.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0010","text":"[[75]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-75) In the case of the conception of cause, I do really go beyond the empirical conception of an event—but not to the intuition which presents this conception in concreto, but only to the time-conditions, which may be found in experience to correspond to the conception. My procedure is, therefore, strictly according to conceptions; I cannot in a case of this kind employ the construction of conceptions, because the conception is merely a rule for the synthesis of perceptions, which are not pure intuitions, and which, therefore, cannot be given à priori.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"84.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0011","text":"There is thus a twofold exercise of reason. Both modes have the properties of universality and an à priori origin in common, but are, in their procedure, of widely different character. The reason of this is that in the world of phenomena, in which alone objects are presented to our minds, there are two main elements—the form of intuition (space and time), which can be cognized and determined completely à priori, and the matter or content—that which is presented in space and time, and which, consequently, contains a something—an existence corresponding to our powers of sensation. As regards the latter, which can never be given in a determinate mode except by experience, there are no à priori notions which relate to it, except the undetermined conceptions of the synthesis of possible sensations, in so far as these belong (in a possible experience) to the unity of consciousness. As regards the former, we can determine our conceptions à priori in intuition, inasmuch as we are ourselves the creators of the objects of the conceptions in space and time—these objects being regarded simply as quanta. In the one case, reason proceeds according to conceptions and can do nothing more than subject phenomena to these—which can only be determined empirically, that is, à posteriori—in conformity, however, with those conceptions as the rules of all empirical synthesis. In the other case, reason proceeds by the construction of conceptions; and, as these conceptions relate to an à priori intuition, they may be given and determined in pure intuition à priori, and without the aid of empirical data. The examination and consideration of everything that exists in space or time—whether it is a quantum or not, in how far the particular something (which fills space or time) is a primary substratum, or a mere determination of some other existence, whether it relates to anything else—either as cause or effect, whether its existence is isolated or in reciprocal connection with and dependence upon others, the possibility of this existence, its reality and necessity or opposites—all these form part of the cognition of reason on the ground of conceptions, and this cognition is termed philosophical. But to determine à priori an intuition in space (its figure), to divide time into periods, or merely to cognize the quantity of an intuition in space and time, and to determine it by number—all this is an operation of reason by means of the construction of conceptions, and is called mathematical.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"84.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0012","text":"The success which attends the efforts of reason in the sphere of mathematics naturally fosters the expectation that the same good fortune will be its lot, if it applies the mathematical method in other regions of mental endeavour besides that of quantities. Its success is thus great, because it can support all its conceptions by à priori intuitions and, in this way, make itself a master, as it were, over nature; while pure philosophy, with its à priori discursive conceptions, bungles about in the world of nature, and cannot accredit or show any à priori evidence of the reality of these conceptions. Masters in the science of mathematics are confident of the success of this method; indeed, it is a common persuasion that it is capable of being applied to any subject of human thought. They have hardly ever reflected or philosophized on their favourite science—a task of great difficulty; and the specific difference between the two modes of employing the faculty of reason has never entered their thoughts. Rules current in the field of common experience, and which common sense stamps everywhere with its approval, are regarded by them as axiomatic. From what source the conceptions of space and time, with which (as the only primitive quanta) they have to deal, enter their minds, is a question which they do not trouble themselves to answer; and they think it just as unnecessary to examine into the origin of the pure conceptions of the understanding and the extent of their validity. All they have to do with them is to employ them. In all this they are perfectly right, if they do not overstep the limits of the sphere of nature. But they pass, unconsciously, from the world of sense to the insecure ground of pure transcendental conceptions (instabilis tellus, innabilis unda), where they can neither stand nor swim, and where the tracks of their footsteps are obliterated by time; while the march of mathematics is pursued on a broad and magnificent highway, which the latest posterity shall frequent without fear of danger or impediment.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"84.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","text":"As we have taken upon us the task of determining, clearly and certainly, the limits of pure reason in the sphere of transcendentalism, and as the efforts of reason in this direction are persisted in, even after the plainest and most expressive warnings, hope still beckoning us past the limits of experience into the splendours of the intellectual world—it becomes necessary to cut away the last anchor of this fallacious and fantastic hope. We shall, accordingly, show that the mathematical method is unattended in the sphere of philosophy by the least advantage—except, perhaps, that it more plainly exhibits its own inadequacy—that geometry and philosophy are two quite different things, although they go hand in hand in the field of natural science, and, consequently, that the procedure of the one can never be imitated by the other.\n\nThe evidence of mathematics rests upon definitions, axioms, and demonstrations. I shall be satisfied with showing that none of these forms can be employed or imitated in philosophy in the sense in which they are understood by mathematicians; and that the geometrician, if he employs his method in philosophy, will succeed only in building card-castles, while the employment of the philosophical method in mathematics can result in nothing but mere verbiage. The essential business of philosophy, indeed, is to mark out the limits of the science; and even the mathematician, unless his talent is naturally circumscribed and limited to this particular department of knowledge, cannot turn a deaf ear to the warnings of philosophy, or set himself above its direction.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"84.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","text":"I. Of Definitions. A definition is, as the term itself indicates, the representation, upon primary grounds, of the complete conception of a thing within its own limits.[[76]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-76) Accordingly, an empirical conception cannot be defined, it can only be explained. For, as there are in such a conception only a certain number of marks or signs, which denote a certain class of sensuous objects, we can never be sure that we do not cogitate under the word which indicates the same object, at one time a greater, at another a smaller number of signs. Thus, one person may cogitate in his conception of gold, in addition to its properties of weight, colour, malleability, that of resisting rust, while another person may be ignorant of this quality. We employ certain signs only so long as we require them for the sake of distinction; new observations abstract some and add new ones, so that an empirical conception never remains within permanent limits. It is, in fact, useless to define a conception of this kind. If, for example, we are speaking of water and its properties, we do not stop at what we actually think by the word water, but proceed to observation and experiment; and the word, with the few signs attached to it, is more properly a designation than a conception of the thing. A definition in this case would evidently be nothing more than a determination of the word. In the second place, no à priori conception, such as those of substance, cause, right, fitness, and so on, can be defined. For I can never be sure, that the clear representation of a given conception (which is given in a confused state) has been fully developed, until I know that the representation is adequate with its object. But, inasmuch as the conception, as it is presented to the mind, may contain a number of obscure representations, which we do not observe in our analysis, although we employ them in our application of the conception, I can never be sure that my analysis is complete, while examples may make this probable, although they can never demonstrate the fact. Instead of the word definition, I should rather employ the term exposition—a more modest expression, which the critic may accept without surrendering his doubts as to the completeness of the analysis of any such conception. As, therefore, neither empirical nor à priori conceptions are capable of definition, we have to see whether the only other kind of conceptions—arbitrary conceptions—can be subjected to this mental operation. Such a conception can always be defined; for I must know thoroughly what I wished to cogitate in it, as it was I who created it, and it was not given to my mind either by the nature of my understanding or by experience. At the same time, I cannot say that, by such a definition, I have defined a real object. If the conception is based upon empirical conditions, if, for example, I have a conception of a clock for a ship, this arbitrary conception does not assure me of the existence or even of the possibility of the object. My definition of such a conception would with more propriety be termed a declaration of a project than a definition of an object. There are no other conceptions which can bear definition, except those which contain an arbitrary synthesis, which can be constructed à priori. Consequently, the science of mathematics alone possesses definitions. For the object here thought is presented à priori in intuition; and thus it can never contain more or less than the conception, because the conception of the object has been given by the definition—and primarily, that is, without deriving the definition from any other source. Philosophical definitions are, therefore, merely expositions of given conceptions, while mathematical definitions are constructions of conceptions originally formed by the mind itself; the former are produced by analysis, the completeness of which is never demonstratively certain, the latter by a synthesis. In a mathematical definition the conception is formed, in a philosophical definition it is only explained. From this it follows:","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"84.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0015","text":"[[76]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-76) The definition must describe the conception completely that is, omit none of the marks or signs of which it composed; within its own limits, that is, it must be precise, and enumerate no more signs than belong to the conception; and on primary grounds, that is to say, the limitations of the bounds of the conception must not be deduced from other conceptions, as in this case a proof would be necessary, and the so-called definition would be incapable of taking its place at the head of all the judgements we have to form regarding an object.\n\n(a) That we must not imitate, in philosophy, the mathematical usage of commencing with definitions—except by way of hypothesis or experiment. For, as all so-called philosophical definitions are merely analyses of given conceptions, these conceptions, although only in a confused form, must precede the analysis; and the incomplete exposition must precede the complete, so that we may be able to draw certain inferences from the characteristics which an incomplete analysis has enabled us to discover, before we attain to the complete exposition or definition of the conception. In one word, a full and clear definition ought, in philosophy, rather to form the conclusion than the commencement of our labours.[[77]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-77) In mathematics, on the contrary, we cannot have a conception prior to the definition; it is the definition which gives us the conception, and it must for this reason form the commencement of every chain of mathematical reasoning.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"84.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0016","text":"[[77]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-77) Philosophy abounds in faulty definitions, especially such as contain some of the elements requisite to form a complete definition. If a conception could not be employed in reasoning before it had been defined, it would fare ill with all philosophical thought. But, as incompletely defined conceptions may always be employed without detriment to truth, so far as our analysis of the elements contained in them proceeds, imperfect definitions, that is, propositions which are properly not definitions, but merely approximations thereto, may be used with great advantage. In mathematics, definition belongs ad esse, in philosophy ad melius esse. It is a difficult task to construct a proper definition. Jurists are still without a complete definition of the idea of right.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"84.16"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0017","text":"(b) Mathematical definitions cannot be erroneous. For the conception is given only in and through the definition, and thus it contains only what has been cogitated in the definition. But although a definition cannot be incorrect, as regards its content, an error may sometimes, although seldom, creep into the form. This error consists in a want of precision. Thus the common definition of a circle—that it is a curved line, every point in which is equally distant from another point called the centre—is faulty, from the fact that the determination indicated by the word curved is superfluous. For there ought to be a particular theorem, which may be easily proved from the definition, to the effect that every line, which has all its points at equal distances from another point, must be a curved line—that is, that not even the smallest part of it can be straight. Analytical definitions, on the other hand, may be erroneous in many respects, either by the introduction of signs which do not actually exist in the conception, or by wanting in that completeness which forms the essential of a definition. In the latter case, the definition is necessarily defective, because we can never be fully certain of the completeness of our analysis. For these reasons, the method of definition employed in mathematics cannot be imitated in philosophy.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"84.17"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","text":"2. Of Axioms. These, in so far as they are immediately certain, are à priori synthetical principles. Now, one conception cannot be connected synthetically and yet immediately with another; because, if we wish to proceed out of and beyond a conception, a third mediating cognition is necessary. And, as philosophy is a cognition of reason by the aid of conceptions alone, there is to be found in it no principle which deserves to be called an axiom. Mathematics, on the other hand, may possess axioms, because it can always connect the predicates of an object à priori, and without any mediating term, by means of the construction of conceptions in intuition. Such is the case with the proposition: Three points can always lie in a plane. On the other hand, no synthetical principle which is based upon conceptions, can ever be immediately certain (for example, the proposition: Everything that happens has a cause), because I require a mediating term to connect the two conceptions of event and cause—namely, the condition of time-determination in an experience, and I cannot cognize any such principle immediately and from conceptions alone. Discursive principles are, accordingly, very different from intuitive principles or axioms. The former always require deduction, which in the case of the latter may be altogether dispensed with. Axioms are, for this reason, always self-evident, while philosophical principles, whatever may be the degree of certainty they possess, cannot lay any claim to such a distinction. No synthetical proposition of pure transcendental reason can be so evident, as is often rashly enough declared, as the statement, twice two are four. It is true that in the Analytic I introduced into the list of principles of the pure understanding, certain axioms of intuition; but the principle there discussed was not itself an axiom, but served merely to present the principle of the possibility of axioms in general, while it was really nothing more than a principle based upon conceptions. For it is one part of the duty of transcendental philosophy to establish the possibility of mathematics itself. Philosophy possesses, then, no axioms, and has no right to impose its à priori principles upon thought, until it has established their authority and validity by a thoroughgoing deduction.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"84.18"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0019","text":"3. Of Demonstrations. Only an apodeictic proof, based upon intuition, can be termed a demonstration. Experience teaches us what is, but it cannot convince us that it might not have been otherwise. Hence a proof upon empirical grounds cannot be apodeictic. À priori conceptions, in discursive cognition, can never produce intuitive certainty or evidence, however certain the judgement they present may be. Mathematics alone, therefore, contains demonstrations, because it does not deduce its cognition from conceptions, but from the construction of conceptions, that is, from intuition, which can be given à priori in accordance with conceptions. The method of algebra, in equations, from which the correct answer is deduced by reduction, is a kind of construction—not geometrical, but by symbols—in which all conceptions, especially those of the relations of quantities, are represented in intuition by signs; and thus the conclusions in that science are secured from errors by the fact that every proof is submitted to ocular evidence. Philosophical cognition does not possess this advantage, it being required to consider the general always in abstracto (by means of conceptions), while mathematics can always consider it in concreto (in an individual intuition), and at the same time by means of à priori representation, whereby all errors are rendered manifest to the senses. The former—discursive proofs—ought to be termed acroamatic proofs, rather than demonstrations, as only words are employed in them, while demonstrations proper, as the term itself indicates, always require a reference to the intuition of the object.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"84.19"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0020","text":"It follows from all these considerations that it is not consonant with the nature of philosophy, especially in the sphere of pure reason, to employ the dogmatical method, and to adorn itself with the titles and insignia of mathematical science. It does not belong to that order, and can only hope for a fraternal union with that science. Its attempts at mathematical evidence are vain pretensions, which can only keep it back from its true aim, which is to detect the illusory procedure of reason when transgressing its proper limits, and by fully explaining and analysing our conceptions, to conduct us from the dim regions of speculation to the clear region of modest self-knowledge. Reason must not, therefore, in its transcendental endeavours, look forward with such confidence, as if the path it is pursuing led straight to its aim, nor reckon with such security upon its premisses, as to consider it unnecessary to take a step back, or to keep a strict watch for errors, which, overlooked in the principles, may be detected in the arguments themselves—in which case it may be requisite either to determine these principles with greater strictness, or to change them entirely.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"84.20"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0021","text":"I divide all apodeictic propositions, whether demonstrable or immediately certain, into dogmata and mathemata. A direct synthetical proposition, based on conceptions, is a dogma; a proposition of the same kind, based on the construction of conceptions, is a mathema. Analytical judgements do not teach us any more about an object than what was contained in the conception we had of it; because they do not extend our cognition beyond our conception of an object, they merely elucidate the conception. They cannot therefore be with propriety termed dogmas. Of the two kinds of à priori synthetical propositions above mentioned, only those which are employed in philosophy can, according to the general mode of speech, bear this name; those of arithmetic or geometry would not be rightly so denominated. Thus the customary mode of speaking confirms the explanation given above, and the conclusion arrived at, that only those judgements which are based upon conceptions, not on the construction of conceptions, can be termed dogmatical.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"84.21"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","text":"Thus, pure reason, in the sphere of speculation, does not contain a single direct synthetical judgement based upon conceptions. By means of ideas, it is, as we have shown, incapable of producing synthetical judgements, which are objectively valid; by means of the conceptions of the understanding, it establishes certain indubitable principles, not, however, directly on the basis of conceptions, but only indirectly by means of the relation of these conceptions to something of a purely contingent nature, namely, possible experience. When experience is presupposed, these principles are apodeictically certain, but in themselves, and directly, they cannot even be cognized à priori. Thus the given conceptions of cause and event will not be sufficient for the demonstration of the proposition: Every event has a cause. For this reason, it is not a dogma; although from another point of view, that of experience, it is capable of being proved to demonstration. The proper term for such a proposition is principle, and not theorem (although it does require to be proved), because it possesses the remarkable peculiarity of being the condition of the possibility of its own ground of proof, that is, experience, and of forming a necessary presupposition in all empirical observation.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"84.22"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","text":"If then, in the speculative sphere of pure reason, no dogmata are to be found; all dogmatical methods, whether borrowed from mathematics, or invented by philosophical thinkers, are alike inappropriate and inefficient. They only serve to conceal errors and fallacies, and to deceive philosophy, whose duty it is to see that reason pursues a safe and straight path. A philosophical method may, however, be systematical. For our reason is, subjectively considered, itself a system, and, in the sphere of mere conceptions, a system of investigation according to principles of unity, the material being supplied by experience alone. But this is not the proper place for discussing the peculiar method of transcendental philosophy, as our present task is simply to examine whether our faculties are capable of erecting an edifice on the basis of pure reason, and how far they may proceed with the materials at their command.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"84.23"}],"sha256":"c779dfd53d23c0bdc20886801035b7c1611ae4ed26413c2c6c09273df3583855","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5","title":"Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics","order":85,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SI_5","text":"# Discipline\n\n### Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics\n\nReason must be subject, in all its operations, to criticism, which must always be permitted to exercise its functions without restraint; otherwise its interests are imperilled and its influence obnoxious to suspicion. There is nothing, however useful, however sacred it may be, that can claim exemption from the searching examination of this supreme tribunal, which has no respect of persons. The very existence of reason depends upon this freedom; for the voice of reason is not that of a dictatorial and despotic power, it is rather like the vote of the citizens of a free state, every member of which must have the privilege of giving free expression to his doubts, and possess even the right of veto.\n\nBut while reason can never decline to submit itself to the tribunal of criticism, it has not always cause to dread the judgement of this court. Pure reason, however, when engaged in the sphere of dogmatism, is not so thoroughly conscious of a strict observance of its highest laws, as to appear before a higher judicial reason with perfect confidence. On the contrary, it must renounce its magnificent dogmatical pretensions in philosophy.\n\nVery different is the case when it has to defend itself, not before a judge, but against an equal. If dogmatical assertions are advanced on the negative side, in opposition to those made by reason on the positive side, its justification kat authrhopon is complete, although the proof of its propositions is kat aletheian unsatisfactory.\n\nBy the polemic of pure reason I mean the defence of its propositions made by reason, in opposition to the dogmatical counter-propositions advanced by other parties. The question here is not whether its own statements may not also be false; it merely regards the fact that reason proves that the opposite cannot be established with demonstrative certainty, nor even asserted with a higher degree of probability. Reason does not hold her possessions upon sufferance; for, although she cannot show a perfectly satisfactory title to them, no one can prove that she is not the rightful possessor.\n\nIt is a melancholy reflection that reason, in its highest exercise, falls into an antithetic; and that the supreme tribunal for the settlement of differences should not be at union with itself. It is true that we had to discuss the question of an apparent antithetic, but we found that it was based upon a misconception. In conformity with the common prejudice, phenomena were regarded as things in themselves, and thus an absolute completeness in their synthesis was required in the one mode or in the other (it was shown to be impossible in both); a demand entirely out of place in regard to phenomena. There was, then, no real self-contradiction of reason in the propositions: The series of phenomena given in themselves has an absolutely first beginning; and: This series is absolutely and in itself without beginning. The two propositions are perfectly consistent with each other, because phenomena as phenomena are in themselves nothing, and consequently the hypothesis that they are things in themselves must lead to self-contradictory inferences.\n\nBut there are cases in which a similar misunderstanding cannot be provided against, and the dispute must remain unsettled. Take, for example, the theistic proposition: There is a Supreme Being; and on the other hand, the atheistic counter-statement: There exists no Supreme Being; or, in psychology: Everything that thinks possesses the attribute of absolute and permanent unity, which is utterly different from the transitory unity of material phenomena; and the counter-proposition: The soul is not an immaterial unity, and its nature is transitory, like that of phenomena. The objects of these questions contain no heterogeneous or contradictory elements, for they relate to things in themselves, and not to phenomena. There would arise, indeed, a real contradiction, if reason came forward with a statement on the negative side of these questions alone. As regards the criticism to which the grounds of proof on the affirmative side must be subjected, it may be freely admitted, without necessitating the surrender of the affirmative propositions, which have, at least, the interest of reason in their favour—an advantage which the opposite party cannot lay claim to.\n\nI cannot agree with the opinion of several admirable thinkers—Sulzer among the rest—that, in spite of the weakness of the arguments hitherto in use, we may hope, one day, to see sufficient demonstrations of the two cardinal propositions of pure reason—the existence of a Supreme Being, and the immortality of the soul. I am certain, on the contrary, that this will never be the case. For on what ground can reason base such synthetical propositions, which do not relate to the objects of experience and their internal possibility? But it is also demonstratively certain that no one will ever be able to maintain the contrary with the least show of probability. For, as he can attempt such a proof solely upon the basis of pure reason, he is bound to prove that a Supreme Being, and a thinking subject in the character of a pure intelligence, are impossible. But where will he find the knowledge which can enable him to enounce synthetical judgements in regard to things which transcend the region of experience? We may, therefore, rest assured that the opposite never will be demonstrated. We need not, then, have recourse to scholastic arguments; we may always admit the truth of those propositions which are consistent with the speculative interests of reason in the sphere of experience, and form, moreover, the only means of uniting the speculative with the practical interest. Our opponent, who must not be considered here as a critic solely, we can be ready to meet with a non liquet which cannot fail to disconcert him; while we cannot deny his right to a similar retort, as we have on our side the advantage of the support of the subjective maxim of reason, and can therefore look upon all his sophistical arguments with calm indifference.\n\nFrom this point of view, there is properly no antithetic of pure reason. For the only arena for such a struggle would be upon the field of pure theology and psychology; but on this ground there can appear no combatant whom we need to fear. Ridicule and boasting can be his only weapons; and these may be laughed at, as mere child’s play. This consideration restores to Reason her courage; for what source of confidence could be found, if she, whose vocation it is to destroy error, were at variance with herself and without any reasonable hope of ever reaching a state of permanent repose?\n\nEverything in nature is good for some purpose. Even poisons are serviceable; they destroy the evil effects of other poisons generated in our system, and must always find a place in every complete pharmacopoeia. The objections raised against the fallacies and sophistries of speculative reason, are objections given by the nature of this reason itself, and must therefore have a destination and purpose which can only be for the good of humanity. For what purpose has Providence raised many objects, in which we have the deepest interest, so far above us, that we vainly try to cognize them with certainty, and our powers of mental vision are rather excited than satisfied by the glimpses we may chance to seize? It is very doubtful whether it is for our benefit to advance bold affirmations regarding subjects involved in such obscurity; perhaps it would even be detrimental to our best interests. But it is undoubtedly always beneficial to leave the investigating, as well as the critical reason, in perfect freedom, and permit it to take charge of its own interests, which are advanced as much by its limitation, as by its extension of its views, and which always suffer by the interference of foreign powers forcing it, against its natural tendencies, to bend to certain preconceived designs.\n\nAllow your opponent to say what he thinks reasonable, and combat him only with the weapons of reason. Have no anxiety for the practical interests of humanity—these are never imperilled in a purely speculative dispute. Such a dispute serves merely to disclose the antinomy of reason, which, as it has its source in the nature of reason, ought to be thoroughly investigated. Reason is benefited by the examination of a subject on both sides, and its judgements are corrected by being limited. It is not the matter that may give occasion to dispute, but the manner. For it is perfectly permissible to employ, in the presence of reason, the language of a firmly rooted faith, even after we have been obliged to renounce all pretensions to knowledge.\n\nIf we were to ask the dispassionate David Hume—a philosopher endowed, in a degree that few are, with a well-balanced judgement: What motive induced you to spend so much labour and thought in undermining the consoling and beneficial persuasion that reason is capable of assuring us of the existence, and presenting us with a determinate conception of a Supreme Being?—his answer would be: Nothing but the desire of teaching reason to know its own powers better, and, at the same time, a dislike of the procedure by which that faculty was compelled to support foregone conclusions, and prevented from confessing the internal weaknesses which it cannot but feel when it enters upon a rigid self-examination. If, on the other hand, we were to ask Priestley—a philosopher who had no taste for transcendental speculation, but was entirely devoted to the principles of empiricism—what his motives were for overturning those two main pillars of religion—the doctrines of the freedom of the will and the immortality of the soul (in his view the hope of a future life is but the expectation of the miracle of resurrection)—this philosopher, himself a zealous and pious teacher of religion, could give no other answer than this: I acted in the interest of reason, which always suffers, when certain objects are explained and judged by a reference to other supposed laws than those of material nature—the only laws which we know in a determinate manner. It would be unfair to decry the latter philosopher, who endeavoured to harmonize his paradoxical opinions with the interests of religion, and to undervalue an honest and reflecting man, because he finds himself at a loss the moment he has left the field of natural science. The same grace must be accorded to Hume, a man not less well-disposed, and quite as blameless in his moral character, and who pushed his abstract speculations to an extreme length, because, as he rightly believed, the object of them lies entirely beyond the bounds of natural science, and within the sphere of pure ideas.\n\nWhat is to be done to provide against the danger which seems in the present case to menace the best interests of humanity? The course to be pursued in reference to this subject is a perfectly plain and natural one. Let each thinker pursue his own path; if he shows talent, if he gives evidence of profound thought, in one word, if he shows that he possesses the power of reasoning—reason is always the gainer. If you have recourse to other means, if you attempt to coerce reason, if you raise the cry of treason to humanity, if you excite the feelings of the crowd, which can neither understand nor sympathize with such subtle speculations—you will only make yourselves ridiculous. For the question does not concern the advantage or disadvantage which we are expected to reap from such inquiries; the question is merely how far reason can advance in the field of speculation, apart from all kinds of interest, and whether we may depend upon the exertions of speculative reason, or must renounce all reliance on it. Instead of joining the combatants, it is your part to be a tranquil spectator of the struggle—a laborious struggle for the parties engaged, but attended, in its progress as well as in its result, with the most advantageous consequences for the interests of thought and knowledge. It is absurd to expect to be enlightened by Reason, and at the same time to prescribe to her what side of the question she must adopt. Moreover, reason is sufficiently held in check by its own power, the limits imposed on it by its own nature are sufficient; it is unnecessary for you to place over it additional guards, as if its power were dangerous to the constitution of the intellectual state. In the dialectic of reason there is no victory gained which need in the least disturb your tranquility.\n\nThe strife of dialectic is a necessity of reason, and we cannot but wish that it had been conducted long ere this with that perfect freedom which ought to be its essential condition. In this case, we should have had at an earlier period a matured and profound criticism, which must have put an end to all dialectical disputes, by exposing the illusions and prejudices in which they originated.\n\nThere is in human nature an unworthy propensity—a propensity which, like everything that springs from nature, must in its final purpose be conducive to the good of humanity—to conceal our real sentiments, and to give expression only to certain received opinions, which are regarded as at once safe and promotive of the common good. It is true, this tendency, not only to conceal our real sentiments, but to profess those which may gain us favour in the eyes of society, has not only civilized, but, in a certain measure, moralized us; as no one can break through the outward covering of respectability, honour, and morality, and thus the seemingly-good examples which we see around us form an excellent school for moral improvement, so long as our belief in their genuineness remains unshaken. But this disposition to represent ourselves as better than we are, and to utter opinions which are not our own, can be nothing more than a kind of provisionary arrangement of nature to lead us from the rudeness of an uncivilized state, and to teach us how to assume at least the appearance and manner of the good we see. But when true principles have been developed, and have obtained a sure foundation in our habit of thought, this conventionalism must be attacked with earnest vigour, otherwise it corrupts the heart, and checks the growth of good dispositions with the mischievous weed of fair appearances.\n\nI am sorry to remark the same tendency to misrepresentation and hypocrisy in the sphere of speculative discussion, where there is less temptation to restrain the free expression of thought. For what can be more prejudicial to the interests of intelligence than to falsify our real sentiments, to conceal the doubts which we feel in regard to our statements, or to maintain the validity of grounds of proof which we well know to be insufficient? So long as mere personal vanity is the source of these unworthy artifices—and this is generally the case in speculative discussions, which are mostly destitute of practical interest, and are incapable of complete demonstration—the vanity of the opposite party exaggerates as much on the other side; and thus the result is the same, although it is not brought about so soon as if the dispute had been conducted in a sincere and upright spirit. But where the mass entertains the notion that the aim of certain subtle speculators is nothing less than to shake the very foundations of public welfare and morality—it seems not only prudent, but even praise worthy, to maintain the good cause by illusory arguments, rather than to give to our supposed opponents the advantage of lowering our declarations to the moderate tone of a merely practical conviction, and of compelling us to confess our inability to attain to apodeictic certainty in speculative subjects. But we ought to reflect that there is nothing, in the world more fatal to the maintenance of a good cause than deceit, misrepresentation, and falsehood. That the strictest laws of honesty should be observed in the discussion of a purely speculative subject is the least requirement that can be made. If we could reckon with security even upon so little, the conflict of speculative reason regarding the important questions of God, immortality, and freedom, would have been either decided long ago, or would very soon be brought to a conclusion. But, in general, the uprightness of the defence stands in an inverse ratio to the goodness of the cause; and perhaps more honesty and fairness are shown by those who deny than by those who uphold these doctrines.\n\nI shall persuade myself, then, that I have readers who do not wish to see a righteous cause defended by unfair arguments. Such will now recognize the fact that, according to the principles of this Critique, if we consider not what is, but what ought to be the case, there can be really no polemic of pure reason. For how can two persons dispute about a thing, the reality of which neither can present in actual or even in possible experience? Each adopts the plan of meditating on his idea for the purpose of drawing from the idea, if he can, what is more than the idea, that is, the reality of the object which it indicates. How shall they settle the dispute, since neither is able to make his assertions directly comprehensible and certain, but must restrict himself to attacking and confuting those of his opponent? All statements enounced by pure reason transcend the conditions of possible experience, beyond the sphere of which we can discover no criterion of truth, while they are at the same time framed in accordance with the laws of the understanding, which are applicable only to experience; and thus it is the fate of all such speculative discussions that while the one party attacks the weaker side of his opponent, he infallibly lays open his own weaknesses.\n\nThe critique of pure reason may be regarded as the highest tribunal for all speculative disputes; for it is not involved in these disputes, which have an immediate relation to certain objects and not to the laws of the mind, but is instituted for the purpose of determining the rights and limits of reason.\n\nWithout the control of criticism, reason is, as it were, in a state of nature, and can only establish its claims and assertions by war. Criticism, on the contrary, deciding all questions according to the fundamental laws of its own institution, secures to us the peace of law and order, and enables us to discuss all differences in the more tranquil manner of a legal process. In the former case, disputes are ended by victory, which both sides may claim and which is followed by a hollow armistice; in the latter, by a sentence, which, as it strikes at the root of all speculative differences, ensures to all concerned a lasting peace. The endless disputes of a dogmatizing reason compel us to look for some mode of arriving at a settled decision by a critical investigation of reason itself; just as Hobbes maintains that the state of nature is a state of injustice and violence, and that we must leave it and submit ourselves to the constraint of law, which indeed limits individual freedom, but only that it may consist with the freedom of others and with the common good of all.\n\nThis freedom will, among other things, permit of our openly stating the difficulties and doubts which we are ourselves unable to solve, without being decried on that account as turbulent and dangerous citizens. This privilege forms part of the native rights of human reason, which recognizes no other judge than the universal reason of humanity; and as this reason is the source of all progress and improvement, such a privilege is to be held sacred and inviolable. It is unwise, moreover, to denounce as dangerous any bold assertions against, or rash attacks upon, an opinion which is held by the largest and most moral class of the community; for that would be giving them an importance which they do not deserve. When I hear that the freedom of the will, the hope of a future life, and the existence of God have been overthrown by the arguments of some able writer, I feel a strong desire to read his book; for I expect that he will add to my knowledge and impart greater clearness and distinctness to my views by the argumentative power shown in his writings. But I am perfectly certain, even before I have opened the book, that he has not succeeded in a single point, not because I believe I am in possession of irrefutable demonstrations of these important propositions, but because this transcendental critique, which has disclosed to me the power and the limits of pure reason, has fully convinced me that, as it is insufficient to establish the affirmative, it is as powerless, and even more so, to assure us of the truth of the negative answer to these questions. From what source does this free-thinker derive his knowledge that there is, for example, no Supreme Being? This proposition lies out of the field of possible experience, and, therefore, beyond the limits of human cognition. But I would not read at, all the answer which the dogmatical maintainer of the good cause makes to his opponent, because I know well beforehand, that he will merely attack the fallacious grounds of his adversary, without being able to establish his own assertions. Besides, a new illusory argument, in the construction of which talent and acuteness are shown, is suggestive of new ideas and new trains of reasoning, and in this respect the old and everyday sophistries are quite useless. Again, the dogmatical opponent of religion gives employment to criticism, and enables us to test and correct its principles, while there is no occasion for anxiety in regard to the influence and results of his reasoning.\n\nBut, it will be said, must we not warn the youth entrusted to academical care against such writings, must we not preserve them from the knowledge of these dangerous assertions, until their judgement is ripened, or rather until the doctrines which we wish to inculcate are so firmly rooted in their minds as to withstand all attempts at instilling the contrary dogmas, from whatever quarter they may come?\n\nIf we are to confine ourselves to the dogmatical procedure in the sphere of pure reason, and find ourselves unable to settle such disputes otherwise than by becoming a party in them, and setting counter-assertions against the statements advanced by our opponents, there is certainly no plan more advisable for the moment, but, at the same time, none more absurd and inefficient for the future, than this retaining of the youthful mind under guardianship for a time, and thus preserving it—for so long at least—from seduction into error. But when, at a later period, either curiosity, or the prevalent fashion of thought places such writings in their hands, will the so-called convictions of their youth stand firm? The young thinker, who has in his armoury none but dogmatical weapons with which to resist the attacks of his opponent, and who cannot detect the latent dialectic which lies in his own opinions as well as in those of the opposite party, sees the advance of illusory arguments and grounds of proof which have the advantage of novelty, against as illusory grounds of proof destitute of this advantage, and which, perhaps, excite the suspicion that the natural credulity of his youth has been abused by his instructors. He thinks he can find no better means of showing that he has out grown the discipline of his minority than by despising those well-meant warnings, and, knowing no system of thought but that of dogmatism, he drinks deep draughts of the poison that is to sap the principles in which his early years were trained.\n\nExactly the opposite of the system here recommended ought to be pursued in academical instruction. This can only be effected, however, by a thorough training in the critical investigation of pure reason. For, in order to bring the principles of this critique into exercise as soon as possible, and to demonstrate their perfect even in the presence of the highest degree of dialectical illusion, the student ought to examine the assertions made on both sides of speculative questions step by step, and to test them by these principles. It cannot be a difficult task for him to show the fallacies inherent in these propositions, and thus he begins early to feel his own power of securing himself against the influence of such sophistical arguments, which must finally lose, for him, all their illusory power. And, although the same blows which overturn the edifice of his opponent are as fatal to his own speculative structures, if such he has wished to rear; he need not feel any sorrow in regard to this seeming misfortune, as he has now before him a fair prospect into the practical region in which he may reasonably hope to find a more secure foundation for a rational system.\n\nThere is, accordingly, no proper polemic in the sphere of pure reason. Both parties beat the air and fight with their own shadows, as they pass beyond the limits of nature, and can find no tangible point of attack—no firm footing for their dogmatical conflict. Fight as vigorously as they may, the shadows which they hew down, immediately start up again, like the heroes in Walhalla, and renew the bloodless and unceasing contest.\n\nBut neither can we admit that there is any proper sceptical employment of pure reason, such as might be based upon the principle of neutrality in all speculative disputes. To excite reason against itself, to place weapons in the hands of the party on the one side as well as in those of the other, and to remain an undisturbed and sarcastic spectator of the fierce struggle that ensues, seems, from the dogmatical point of view, to be a part fitting only a malevolent disposition. But, when the sophist evidences an invincible obstinacy and blindness, and a pride which no criticism can moderate, there is no other practicable course than to oppose to this pride and obstinacy similar feelings and pretensions on the other side, equally well or ill founded, so that reason, staggered by the reflections thus forced upon it, finds it necessary to moderate its confidence in such pretensions and to listen to the advice of criticism. But we cannot stop at these doubts, much less regard the conviction of our ignorance, not only as a cure for the conceit natural to dogmatism, but as the settlement of the disputes in which reason is involved with itself. On the contrary, scepticism is merely a means of awakening reason from its dogmatic dreams and exciting it to a more careful investigation into its own powers and pretensions. But, as scepticism appears to be the shortest road to a permanent peace in the domain of philosophy, and as it is the track pursued by the many who aim at giving a philosophical colouring to their contemptuous dislike of all inquiries of this kind, I think it necessary to present to my readers this mode of thought in its true light.\n\n_Scepticism not a Permanent State for Human Reason._\n\nThe consciousness of ignorance—unless this ignorance is recognized to be absolutely necessary ought, instead of forming the conclusion of my inquiries, to be the strongest motive to the pursuit of them. All ignorance is either ignorance of things or of the limits of knowledge. If my ignorance is accidental and not necessary, it must incite me, in the first case, to a dogmatical inquiry regarding the objects of which I am ignorant; in the second, to a critical investigation into the bounds of all possible knowledge. But that my ignorance is absolutely necessary and unavoidable, and that it consequently absolves from the duty of all further investigation, is a fact which cannot be made out upon empirical grounds—from observation—but upon critical grounds alone, that is, by a thoroughgoing investigation into the primary sources of cognition. It follows that the determination of the bounds of reason can be made only on à priori grounds; while the empirical limitation of reason, which is merely an indeterminate cognition of an ignorance that can never be completely removed, can take place only à posteriori. In other words, our empirical knowledge is limited by that which yet remains for us to know. The former cognition of our ignorance, which is possible only on a rational basis, is a science; the latter is merely a perception, and we cannot say how far the inferences drawn from it may extend. If I regard the earth, as it really appears to my senses, as a flat surface, I am ignorant how far this surface extends. But experience teaches me that, how far soever I go, I always see before me a space in which I can proceed farther; and thus I know the limits—merely visual—of my actual knowledge of the earth, although I am ignorant of the limits of the earth itself. But if I have got so far as to know that the earth is a sphere, and that its surface is spherical, I can cognize à priori and determine upon principles, from my knowledge of a small part of this surface—say to the extent of a degree—the diameter and circumference of the earth; and although I am ignorant of the objects which this surface contains, I have a perfect knowledge of its limits and extent.\n\nThe sum of all the possible objects of our cognition seems to us to be a level surface, with an apparent horizon—that which forms the limit of its extent, and which has been termed by us the idea of unconditioned totality. To reach this limit by empirical means is impossible, and all attempts to determine it à priori according to a principle, are alike in vain. But all the questions raised by pure reason relate to that which lies beyond this horizon, or, at least, in its boundary line.\n\nThe celebrated David Hume was one of those geographers of human reason who believe that they have given a sufficient answer to all such questions by declaring them to lie beyond the horizon of our knowledge—a horizon which, however, Hume was unable to determine. His attention especially was directed to the principle of causality; and he remarked with perfect justice that the truth of this principle, and even the objective validity of the conception of a cause, was not commonly based upon clear insight, that is, upon à priori cognition. Hence he concluded that this law does not derive its authority from its universality and necessity, but merely from its general applicability in the course of experience, and a kind of subjective necessity thence arising, which he termed habit. From the inability of reason to establish this principle as a necessary law for the acquisition of all experience, he inferred the nullity of all the attempts of reason to pass the region of the empirical.\n\nThis procedure of subjecting the facta of reason to examination, and, if necessary, to disapproval, may be termed the censura of reason. This censura must inevitably lead us to doubts regarding all transcendent employment of principles. But this is only the second step in our inquiry. The first step in regard to the subjects of pure reason, and which marks the infancy of that faculty, is that of dogmatism. The second, which we have just mentioned, is that of scepticism, and it gives evidence that our judgement has been improved by experience. But a third step is necessary—indicative of the maturity and manhood of the judgement, which now lays a firm foundation upon universal and necessary principles. This is the period of criticism, in which we do not examine the facta of reason, but reason itself, in the whole extent of its powers, and in regard to its capability of à priori cognition; and thus we determine not merely the empirical and ever-shifting bounds of our knowledge, but its necessary and eternal limits. We demonstrate from indubitable principles, not merely our ignorance in respect to this or that subject, but in regard to all possible questions of a certain class. Thus scepticism is a resting place for reason, in which it may reflect on its dogmatical wanderings and gain some knowledge of the region in which it happens to be, that it may pursue its way with greater certainty; but it cannot be its permanent dwelling-place. It must take up its abode only in the region of complete certitude, whether this relates to the cognition of objects themselves, or to the limits which bound all our cognition.\n\nReason is not to be considered as an indefinitely extended plane, of the bounds of which we have only a general knowledge; it ought rather to be compared to a sphere, the radius of which may be found from the curvature of its surface—that is, the nature of à priori synthetical propositions—and, consequently, its circumference and extent. Beyond the sphere of experience there are no objects which it can cognize; nay, even questions regarding such supposititious objects relate only to the subjective principles of a complete determination of the relations which exist between the understanding-conceptions which lie within this sphere.\n\nWe are actually in possession of à priori synthetical cognitions, as is proved by the existence of the principles of the understanding, which anticipate experience. If any one cannot comprehend the possibility of these principles, he may have some reason to doubt whether they are really à priori; but he cannot on this account declare them to be impossible, and affirm the nullity of the steps which reason may have taken under their guidance. He can only say: If we perceived their origin and their authenticity, we should be able to determine the extent and limits of reason; but, till we can do this, all propositions regarding the latter are mere random assertions. In this view, the doubt respecting all dogmatical philosophy, which proceeds without the guidance of criticism, is well grounded; but we cannot therefore deny to reason the ability to construct a sound philosophy, when the way has been prepared by a thorough critical investigation. All the conceptions produced, and all the questions raised, by pure reason, do not lie in the sphere of experience, but in that of reason itself, and hence they must be solved, and shown to be either valid or inadmissible, by that faculty. We have no right to decline the solution of such problems, on the ground that the solution can be discovered only from the nature of things, and under pretence of the limitation of human faculties, for reason is the sole creator of all these ideas, and is therefore bound either to establish their validity or to expose their illusory nature.\n\nThe polemic of scepticism is properly directed against the dogmatist, who erects a system of philosophy without having examined the fundamental objective principles on which it is based, for the purpose of evidencing the futility of his designs, and thus bringing him to a knowledge of his own powers. But, in itself, scepticism does not give us any certain information in regard to the bounds of our knowledge. All unsuccessful dogmatical attempts of reason are facia, which it is always useful to submit to the censure of the sceptic. But this cannot help us to any decision regarding the expectations which reason cherishes of better success in future endeavours; the investigations of scepticism cannot, therefore, settle the dispute regarding the rights and powers of human reason.\n\nHume is perhaps the ablest and most ingenious of all sceptical philosophers, and his writings have, undoubtedly, exerted the most powerful influence in awakening reason to a thorough investigation into its own powers. It will, therefore, well repay our labours to consider for a little the course of reasoning which he followed and the errors into which he strayed, although setting out on the path of truth and certitude.\n\nHume was probably aware, although he never clearly developed the notion, that we proceed in judgements of a certain class beyond our conception of the object. I have termed this kind of judgement synthetical. As regard the manner in which I pass beyond my conception by the aid of experience, no doubts can be entertained. Experience is itself a synthesis of perceptions; and it employs perceptions to increment the conception, which I obtain by means of another perception. But we feel persuaded that we are able to proceed beyond a conception, and to extend our cognition à priori. We attempt this in two ways—either, through the pure understanding, in relation to that which may become an object of experience, or, through pure reason, in relation to such properties of things, or of the existence of things, as can never be presented in any experience. This sceptical philosopher did not distinguish these two kinds of judgements, as he ought to have done, but regarded this augmentation of conceptions, and, if we may so express ourselves, the spontaneous generation of understanding and reason, independently of the impregnation of experience, as altogether impossible. The so-called à priori principles of these faculties he consequently held to be invalid and imaginary, and regarded them as nothing but subjective habits of thought originating in experience, and therefore purely empirical and contingent rules, to which we attribute a spurious necessity and universality. In support of this strange assertion, he referred us to the generally acknowledged principle of the relation between cause and effect. No faculty of the mind can conduct us from the conception of a thing to the existence of something else; and hence he believed he could infer that, without experience, we possess no source from which we can augment a conception, and no ground sufficient to justify us in framing a judgement that is to extend our cognition à priori. That the light of the sun, which shines upon a piece of wax, at the same time melts it, while it hardens clay, no power of the understanding could infer from the conceptions which we previously possessed of these substances; much less is there any à priori law that could conduct us to such a conclusion, which experience alone can certify. On the other hand, we have seen in our discussion of transcendental logic, that, although we can never proceed immediately beyond the content of the conception which is given us, we can always cognize completely à priori—in relation, however, to a third term, namely, possible experience—the law of its connection with other things. For example, if I observe that a piece of wax melts, I can cognize à priori that there must have been something (the sun’s heat) preceding, which this law; although, without the aid of experience, I could not cognize à priori and in a determinate manner either the cause from the effect, or the effect from the cause. Hume was, therefore, wrong in inferring, from the contingency of the determination according to law, the contingency of the law itself; and the passing beyond the conception of a thing to possible experience (which is an à priori proceeding, constituting the objective reality of the conception), he confounded with our synthesis of objects in actual experience, which is always, of course, empirical. Thus, too, he regarded the principle of affinity, which has its seat in the understanding and indicates a necessary connection, as a mere rule of association, lying in the imitative faculty of imagination, which can present only contingent, and not objective connections.\n\nThe sceptical errors of this remarkably acute thinker arose principally from a defect, which was common to him with the dogmatists, namely, that he had never made a systematic review of all the different kinds of à priori synthesis performed by the understanding. Had he done so, he would have found, to take one example among many, that the principle of permanence was of this character, and that it, as well as the principle of causality, anticipates experience. In this way he might have been able to describe the determinate limits of the à priori operations of understanding and reason. But he merely declared the understanding to be limited, instead of showing what its limits were; he created a general mistrust in the power of our faculties, without giving us any determinate knowledge of the bounds of our necessary and unavoidable ignorance; he examined and condemned some of the principles of the understanding, without investigating all its powers with the completeness necessary to criticism. He denies, with truth, certain powers to the understanding, but he goes further, and declares it to be utterly inadequate to the à priori extension of knowledge, although he has not fully examined all the powers which reside in the faculty; and thus the fate which always overtakes scepticism meets him too. That is to say, his own declarations are doubted, for his objections were based upon facta, which are contingent, and not upon principles, which can alone demonstrate the necessary invalidity of all dogmatical assertions.\n\nAs Hume makes no distinction between the well-grounded claims of the understanding and the dialectical pretensions of reason, against which, however, his attacks are mainly directed, reason does not feel itself shut out from all attempts at the extension of à priori cognition, and hence it refuses, in spite of a few checks in this or that quarter, to relinquish such efforts. For one naturally arms oneself to resist an attack, and becomes more obstinate in the resolve to establish the claims he has advanced. But a complete review of the powers of reason, and the conviction thence arising that we are in possession of a limited field of action, while we must admit the vanity of higher claims, puts an end to all doubt and dispute, and induces reason to rest satisfied with the undisturbed possession of its limited domain.\n\nTo the uncritical dogmatist, who has not surveyed the sphere of his understanding, nor determined, in accordance with principles, the limits of possible cognition, who, consequently, is ignorant of his own powers, and believes he will discover them by the attempts he makes in the field of cognition, these attacks of scepticism are not only dangerous, but destructive. For if there is one proposition in his chain of reasoning which he cannot prove, or the fallacy in which he cannot evolve in accordance with a principle, suspicion falls on all his statements, however plausible they may appear.\n\nAnd thus scepticism, the bane of dogmatical philosophy, conducts us to a sound investigation into the understanding and the reason. When we are thus far advanced, we need fear no further attacks; for the limits of our domain are clearly marked out, and we can make no claims nor become involved in any disputes regarding the region that lies beyond these limits. Thus the sceptical procedure in philosophy does not present any solution of the problems of reason, but it forms an excellent exercise for its powers, awakening its circumspection, and indicating the means whereby it may most fully establish its claims to its legitimate possessions.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0001","text":"# Discipline\n\n### Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics\n\nReason must be subject, in all its operations, to criticism, which must always be permitted to exercise its functions without restraint; otherwise its interests are imperilled and its influence obnoxious to suspicion. There is nothing, however useful, however sacred it may be, that can claim exemption from the searching examination of this supreme tribunal, which has no respect of persons. The very existence of reason depends upon this freedom; for the voice of reason is not that of a dictatorial and despotic power, it is rather like the vote of the citizens of a free state, every member of which must have the privilege of giving free expression to his doubts, and possess even the right of veto.\n\nBut while reason can never decline to submit itself to the tribunal of criticism, it has not always cause to dread the judgement of this court. Pure reason, however, when engaged in the sphere of dogmatism, is not so thoroughly conscious of a strict observance of its highest laws, as to appear before a higher judicial reason with perfect confidence. On the contrary, it must renounce its magnificent dogmatical pretensions in philosophy.\n\nVery different is the case when it has to defend itself, not before a judge, but against an equal. If dogmatical assertions are advanced on the negative side, in opposition to those made by reason on the positive side, its justification kat authrhopon is complete, although the proof of its propositions is kat aletheian unsatisfactory.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"85.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0002","text":"By the polemic of pure reason I mean the defence of its propositions made by reason, in opposition to the dogmatical counter-propositions advanced by other parties. The question here is not whether its own statements may not also be false; it merely regards the fact that reason proves that the opposite cannot be established with demonstrative certainty, nor even asserted with a higher degree of probability. Reason does not hold her possessions upon sufferance; for, although she cannot show a perfectly satisfactory title to them, no one can prove that she is not the rightful possessor.\n\nIt is a melancholy reflection that reason, in its highest exercise, falls into an antithetic; and that the supreme tribunal for the settlement of differences should not be at union with itself. It is true that we had to discuss the question of an apparent antithetic, but we found that it was based upon a misconception. In conformity with the common prejudice, phenomena were regarded as things in themselves, and thus an absolute completeness in their synthesis was required in the one mode or in the other (it was shown to be impossible in both); a demand entirely out of place in regard to phenomena. There was, then, no real self-contradiction of reason in the propositions: The series of phenomena given in themselves has an absolutely first beginning; and: This series is absolutely and in itself without beginning. The two propositions are perfectly consistent with each other, because phenomena as phenomena are in themselves nothing, and consequently the hypothesis that they are things in themselves must lead to self-contradictory inferences.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"85.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0003","text":"But there are cases in which a similar misunderstanding cannot be provided against, and the dispute must remain unsettled. Take, for example, the theistic proposition: There is a Supreme Being; and on the other hand, the atheistic counter-statement: There exists no Supreme Being; or, in psychology: Everything that thinks possesses the attribute of absolute and permanent unity, which is utterly different from the transitory unity of material phenomena; and the counter-proposition: The soul is not an immaterial unity, and its nature is transitory, like that of phenomena. The objects of these questions contain no heterogeneous or contradictory elements, for they relate to things in themselves, and not to phenomena. There would arise, indeed, a real contradiction, if reason came forward with a statement on the negative side of these questions alone. As regards the criticism to which the grounds of proof on the affirmative side must be subjected, it may be freely admitted, without necessitating the surrender of the affirmative propositions, which have, at least, the interest of reason in their favour—an advantage which the opposite party cannot lay claim to.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"85.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","text":"I cannot agree with the opinion of several admirable thinkers—Sulzer among the rest—that, in spite of the weakness of the arguments hitherto in use, we may hope, one day, to see sufficient demonstrations of the two cardinal propositions of pure reason—the existence of a Supreme Being, and the immortality of the soul. I am certain, on the contrary, that this will never be the case. For on what ground can reason base such synthetical propositions, which do not relate to the objects of experience and their internal possibility? But it is also demonstratively certain that no one will ever be able to maintain the contrary with the least show of probability. For, as he can attempt such a proof solely upon the basis of pure reason, he is bound to prove that a Supreme Being, and a thinking subject in the character of a pure intelligence, are impossible. But where will he find the knowledge which can enable him to enounce synthetical judgements in regard to things which transcend the region of experience? We may, therefore, rest assured that the opposite never will be demonstrated. We need not, then, have recourse to scholastic arguments; we may always admit the truth of those propositions which are consistent with the speculative interests of reason in the sphere of experience, and form, moreover, the only means of uniting the speculative with the practical interest. Our opponent, who must not be considered here as a critic solely, we can be ready to meet with a non liquet which cannot fail to disconcert him; while we cannot deny his right to a similar retort, as we have on our side the advantage of the support of the subjective maxim of reason, and can therefore look upon all his sophistical arguments with calm indifference.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"85.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0005","text":"From this point of view, there is properly no antithetic of pure reason. For the only arena for such a struggle would be upon the field of pure theology and psychology; but on this ground there can appear no combatant whom we need to fear. Ridicule and boasting can be his only weapons; and these may be laughed at, as mere child’s play. This consideration restores to Reason her courage; for what source of confidence could be found, if she, whose vocation it is to destroy error, were at variance with herself and without any reasonable hope of ever reaching a state of permanent repose?","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"85.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0006","text":"Everything in nature is good for some purpose. Even poisons are serviceable; they destroy the evil effects of other poisons generated in our system, and must always find a place in every complete pharmacopoeia. The objections raised against the fallacies and sophistries of speculative reason, are objections given by the nature of this reason itself, and must therefore have a destination and purpose which can only be for the good of humanity. For what purpose has Providence raised many objects, in which we have the deepest interest, so far above us, that we vainly try to cognize them with certainty, and our powers of mental vision are rather excited than satisfied by the glimpses we may chance to seize? It is very doubtful whether it is for our benefit to advance bold affirmations regarding subjects involved in such obscurity; perhaps it would even be detrimental to our best interests. But it is undoubtedly always beneficial to leave the investigating, as well as the critical reason, in perfect freedom, and permit it to take charge of its own interests, which are advanced as much by its limitation, as by its extension of its views, and which always suffer by the interference of foreign powers forcing it, against its natural tendencies, to bend to certain preconceived designs.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"85.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","text":"Allow your opponent to say what he thinks reasonable, and combat him only with the weapons of reason. Have no anxiety for the practical interests of humanity—these are never imperilled in a purely speculative dispute. Such a dispute serves merely to disclose the antinomy of reason, which, as it has its source in the nature of reason, ought to be thoroughly investigated. Reason is benefited by the examination of a subject on both sides, and its judgements are corrected by being limited. It is not the matter that may give occasion to dispute, but the manner. For it is perfectly permissible to employ, in the presence of reason, the language of a firmly rooted faith, even after we have been obliged to renounce all pretensions to knowledge.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"85.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0008","text":"If we were to ask the dispassionate David Hume—a philosopher endowed, in a degree that few are, with a well-balanced judgement: What motive induced you to spend so much labour and thought in undermining the consoling and beneficial persuasion that reason is capable of assuring us of the existence, and presenting us with a determinate conception of a Supreme Being?—his answer would be: Nothing but the desire of teaching reason to know its own powers better, and, at the same time, a dislike of the procedure by which that faculty was compelled to support foregone conclusions, and prevented from confessing the internal weaknesses which it cannot but feel when it enters upon a rigid self-examination. If, on the other hand, we were to ask Priestley—a philosopher who had no taste for transcendental speculation, but was entirely devoted to the principles of empiricism—what his motives were for overturning those two main pillars of religion—the doctrines of the freedom of the will and the immortality of the soul (in his view the hope of a future life is but the expectation of the miracle of resurrection)—this philosopher, himself a zealous and pious teacher of religion, could give no other answer than this: I acted in the interest of reason, which always suffers, when certain objects are explained and judged by a reference to other supposed laws than those of material nature—the only laws which we know in a determinate manner. It would be unfair to decry the latter philosopher, who endeavoured to harmonize his paradoxical opinions with the interests of religion, and to undervalue an honest and reflecting man, because he finds himself at a loss the moment he has left the field of natural science. The same grace must be accorded to Hume, a man not less well-disposed, and quite as blameless in his moral character, and who pushed his abstract speculations to an extreme length, because, as he rightly believed, the object of them lies entirely beyond the bounds of natural science, and within the sphere of pure ideas.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"85.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0009","text":"What is to be done to provide against the danger which seems in the present case to menace the best interests of humanity? The course to be pursued in reference to this subject is a perfectly plain and natural one. Let each thinker pursue his own path; if he shows talent, if he gives evidence of profound thought, in one word, if he shows that he possesses the power of reasoning—reason is always the gainer. If you have recourse to other means, if you attempt to coerce reason, if you raise the cry of treason to humanity, if you excite the feelings of the crowd, which can neither understand nor sympathize with such subtle speculations—you will only make yourselves ridiculous. For the question does not concern the advantage or disadvantage which we are expected to reap from such inquiries; the question is merely how far reason can advance in the field of speculation, apart from all kinds of interest, and whether we may depend upon the exertions of speculative reason, or must renounce all reliance on it. Instead of joining the combatants, it is your part to be a tranquil spectator of the struggle—a laborious struggle for the parties engaged, but attended, in its progress as well as in its result, with the most advantageous consequences for the interests of thought and knowledge. It is absurd to expect to be enlightened by Reason, and at the same time to prescribe to her what side of the question she must adopt. Moreover, reason is sufficiently held in check by its own power, the limits imposed on it by its own nature are sufficient; it is unnecessary for you to place over it additional guards, as if its power were dangerous to the constitution of the intellectual state. In the dialectic of reason there is no victory gained which need in the least disturb your tranquility.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"85.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0010","text":"The strife of dialectic is a necessity of reason, and we cannot but wish that it had been conducted long ere this with that perfect freedom which ought to be its essential condition. In this case, we should have had at an earlier period a matured and profound criticism, which must have put an end to all dialectical disputes, by exposing the illusions and prejudices in which they originated.\n\nThere is in human nature an unworthy propensity—a propensity which, like everything that springs from nature, must in its final purpose be conducive to the good of humanity—to conceal our real sentiments, and to give expression only to certain received opinions, which are regarded as at once safe and promotive of the common good. It is true, this tendency, not only to conceal our real sentiments, but to profess those which may gain us favour in the eyes of society, has not only civilized, but, in a certain measure, moralized us; as no one can break through the outward covering of respectability, honour, and morality, and thus the seemingly-good examples which we see around us form an excellent school for moral improvement, so long as our belief in their genuineness remains unshaken. But this disposition to represent ourselves as better than we are, and to utter opinions which are not our own, can be nothing more than a kind of provisionary arrangement of nature to lead us from the rudeness of an uncivilized state, and to teach us how to assume at least the appearance and manner of the good we see. But when true principles have been developed, and have obtained a sure foundation in our habit of thought, this conventionalism must be attacked with earnest vigour, otherwise it corrupts the heart, and checks the growth of good dispositions with the mischievous weed of fair appearances.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"85.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0011","text":"I am sorry to remark the same tendency to misrepresentation and hypocrisy in the sphere of speculative discussion, where there is less temptation to restrain the free expression of thought. For what can be more prejudicial to the interests of intelligence than to falsify our real sentiments, to conceal the doubts which we feel in regard to our statements, or to maintain the validity of grounds of proof which we well know to be insufficient? So long as mere personal vanity is the source of these unworthy artifices—and this is generally the case in speculative discussions, which are mostly destitute of practical interest, and are incapable of complete demonstration—the vanity of the opposite party exaggerates as much on the other side; and thus the result is the same, although it is not brought about so soon as if the dispute had been conducted in a sincere and upright spirit. But where the mass entertains the notion that the aim of certain subtle speculators is nothing less than to shake the very foundations of public welfare and morality—it seems not only prudent, but even praise worthy, to maintain the good cause by illusory arguments, rather than to give to our supposed opponents the advantage of lowering our declarations to the moderate tone of a merely practical conviction, and of compelling us to confess our inability to attain to apodeictic certainty in speculative subjects. But we ought to reflect that there is nothing, in the world more fatal to the maintenance of a good cause than deceit, misrepresentation, and falsehood. That the strictest laws of honesty should be observed in the discussion of a purely speculative subject is the least requirement that can be made. If we could reckon with security even upon so little, the conflict of speculative reason regarding the important questions of God, immortality, and freedom, would have been either decided long ago, or would very soon be brought to a conclusion. But, in general, the uprightness of the defence stands in an inverse ratio to the goodness of the cause; and perhaps more honesty and fairness are shown by those who deny than by those who uphold these doctrines.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"85.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","text":"I shall persuade myself, then, that I have readers who do not wish to see a righteous cause defended by unfair arguments. Such will now recognize the fact that, according to the principles of this Critique, if we consider not what is, but what ought to be the case, there can be really no polemic of pure reason. For how can two persons dispute about a thing, the reality of which neither can present in actual or even in possible experience? Each adopts the plan of meditating on his idea for the purpose of drawing from the idea, if he can, what is more than the idea, that is, the reality of the object which it indicates. How shall they settle the dispute, since neither is able to make his assertions directly comprehensible and certain, but must restrict himself to attacking and confuting those of his opponent? All statements enounced by pure reason transcend the conditions of possible experience, beyond the sphere of which we can discover no criterion of truth, while they are at the same time framed in accordance with the laws of the understanding, which are applicable only to experience; and thus it is the fate of all such speculative discussions that while the one party attacks the weaker side of his opponent, he infallibly lays open his own weaknesses.\n\nThe critique of pure reason may be regarded as the highest tribunal for all speculative disputes; for it is not involved in these disputes, which have an immediate relation to certain objects and not to the laws of the mind, but is instituted for the purpose of determining the rights and limits of reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"85.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","text":"Without the control of criticism, reason is, as it were, in a state of nature, and can only establish its claims and assertions by war. Criticism, on the contrary, deciding all questions according to the fundamental laws of its own institution, secures to us the peace of law and order, and enables us to discuss all differences in the more tranquil manner of a legal process. In the former case, disputes are ended by victory, which both sides may claim and which is followed by a hollow armistice; in the latter, by a sentence, which, as it strikes at the root of all speculative differences, ensures to all concerned a lasting peace. The endless disputes of a dogmatizing reason compel us to look for some mode of arriving at a settled decision by a critical investigation of reason itself; just as Hobbes maintains that the state of nature is a state of injustice and violence, and that we must leave it and submit ourselves to the constraint of law, which indeed limits individual freedom, but only that it may consist with the freedom of others and with the common good of all.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"85.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0014","text":"This freedom will, among other things, permit of our openly stating the difficulties and doubts which we are ourselves unable to solve, without being decried on that account as turbulent and dangerous citizens. This privilege forms part of the native rights of human reason, which recognizes no other judge than the universal reason of humanity; and as this reason is the source of all progress and improvement, such a privilege is to be held sacred and inviolable. It is unwise, moreover, to denounce as dangerous any bold assertions against, or rash attacks upon, an opinion which is held by the largest and most moral class of the community; for that would be giving them an importance which they do not deserve. When I hear that the freedom of the will, the hope of a future life, and the existence of God have been overthrown by the arguments of some able writer, I feel a strong desire to read his book; for I expect that he will add to my knowledge and impart greater clearness and distinctness to my views by the argumentative power shown in his writings. But I am perfectly certain, even before I have opened the book, that he has not succeeded in a single point, not because I believe I am in possession of irrefutable demonstrations of these important propositions, but because this transcendental critique, which has disclosed to me the power and the limits of pure reason, has fully convinced me that, as it is insufficient to establish the affirmative, it is as powerless, and even more so, to assure us of the truth of the negative answer to these questions. From what source does this free-thinker derive his knowledge that there is, for example, no Supreme Being? This proposition lies out of the field of possible experience, and, therefore, beyond the limits of human cognition. But I would not read at, all the answer which the dogmatical maintainer of the good cause makes to his opponent, because I know well beforehand, that he will merely attack the fallacious grounds of his adversary, without being able to establish his own assertions. Besides, a new illusory argument, in the construction of which talent and acuteness are shown, is suggestive of new ideas and new trains of reasoning, and in this respect the old and everyday sophistries are quite useless. Again, the dogmatical opponent of religion gives employment to criticism, and enables us to test and correct its principles, while there is no occasion for anxiety in regard to the influence and results of his reasoning.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"85.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0015","text":"But, it will be said, must we not warn the youth entrusted to academical care against such writings, must we not preserve them from the knowledge of these dangerous assertions, until their judgement is ripened, or rather until the doctrines which we wish to inculcate are so firmly rooted in their minds as to withstand all attempts at instilling the contrary dogmas, from whatever quarter they may come?\n\nIf we are to confine ourselves to the dogmatical procedure in the sphere of pure reason, and find ourselves unable to settle such disputes otherwise than by becoming a party in them, and setting counter-assertions against the statements advanced by our opponents, there is certainly no plan more advisable for the moment, but, at the same time, none more absurd and inefficient for the future, than this retaining of the youthful mind under guardianship for a time, and thus preserving it—for so long at least—from seduction into error. But when, at a later period, either curiosity, or the prevalent fashion of thought places such writings in their hands, will the so-called convictions of their youth stand firm? The young thinker, who has in his armoury none but dogmatical weapons with which to resist the attacks of his opponent, and who cannot detect the latent dialectic which lies in his own opinions as well as in those of the opposite party, sees the advance of illusory arguments and grounds of proof which have the advantage of novelty, against as illusory grounds of proof destitute of this advantage, and which, perhaps, excite the suspicion that the natural credulity of his youth has been abused by his instructors. He thinks he can find no better means of showing that he has out grown the discipline of his minority than by despising those well-meant warnings, and, knowing no system of thought but that of dogmatism, he drinks deep draughts of the poison that is to sap the principles in which his early years were trained.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"85.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0016","text":"Exactly the opposite of the system here recommended ought to be pursued in academical instruction. This can only be effected, however, by a thorough training in the critical investigation of pure reason. For, in order to bring the principles of this critique into exercise as soon as possible, and to demonstrate their perfect even in the presence of the highest degree of dialectical illusion, the student ought to examine the assertions made on both sides of speculative questions step by step, and to test them by these principles. It cannot be a difficult task for him to show the fallacies inherent in these propositions, and thus he begins early to feel his own power of securing himself against the influence of such sophistical arguments, which must finally lose, for him, all their illusory power. And, although the same blows which overturn the edifice of his opponent are as fatal to his own speculative structures, if such he has wished to rear; he need not feel any sorrow in regard to this seeming misfortune, as he has now before him a fair prospect into the practical region in which he may reasonably hope to find a more secure foundation for a rational system.\n\nThere is, accordingly, no proper polemic in the sphere of pure reason. Both parties beat the air and fight with their own shadows, as they pass beyond the limits of nature, and can find no tangible point of attack—no firm footing for their dogmatical conflict. Fight as vigorously as they may, the shadows which they hew down, immediately start up again, like the heroes in Walhalla, and renew the bloodless and unceasing contest.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"85.16"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0017","text":"But neither can we admit that there is any proper sceptical employment of pure reason, such as might be based upon the principle of neutrality in all speculative disputes. To excite reason against itself, to place weapons in the hands of the party on the one side as well as in those of the other, and to remain an undisturbed and sarcastic spectator of the fierce struggle that ensues, seems, from the dogmatical point of view, to be a part fitting only a malevolent disposition. But, when the sophist evidences an invincible obstinacy and blindness, and a pride which no criticism can moderate, there is no other practicable course than to oppose to this pride and obstinacy similar feelings and pretensions on the other side, equally well or ill founded, so that reason, staggered by the reflections thus forced upon it, finds it necessary to moderate its confidence in such pretensions and to listen to the advice of criticism. But we cannot stop at these doubts, much less regard the conviction of our ignorance, not only as a cure for the conceit natural to dogmatism, but as the settlement of the disputes in which reason is involved with itself. On the contrary, scepticism is merely a means of awakening reason from its dogmatic dreams and exciting it to a more careful investigation into its own powers and pretensions. But, as scepticism appears to be the shortest road to a permanent peace in the domain of philosophy, and as it is the track pursued by the many who aim at giving a philosophical colouring to their contemptuous dislike of all inquiries of this kind, I think it necessary to present to my readers this mode of thought in its true light.\n\n_Scepticism not a Permanent State for Human Reason._","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"85.17"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0018","text":"The consciousness of ignorance—unless this ignorance is recognized to be absolutely necessary ought, instead of forming the conclusion of my inquiries, to be the strongest motive to the pursuit of them. All ignorance is either ignorance of things or of the limits of knowledge. If my ignorance is accidental and not necessary, it must incite me, in the first case, to a dogmatical inquiry regarding the objects of which I am ignorant; in the second, to a critical investigation into the bounds of all possible knowledge. But that my ignorance is absolutely necessary and unavoidable, and that it consequently absolves from the duty of all further investigation, is a fact which cannot be made out upon empirical grounds—from observation—but upon critical grounds alone, that is, by a thoroughgoing investigation into the primary sources of cognition. It follows that the determination of the bounds of reason can be made only on à priori grounds; while the empirical limitation of reason, which is merely an indeterminate cognition of an ignorance that can never be completely removed, can take place only à posteriori. In other words, our empirical knowledge is limited by that which yet remains for us to know. The former cognition of our ignorance, which is possible only on a rational basis, is a science; the latter is merely a perception, and we cannot say how far the inferences drawn from it may extend. If I regard the earth, as it really appears to my senses, as a flat surface, I am ignorant how far this surface extends. But experience teaches me that, how far soever I go, I always see before me a space in which I can proceed farther; and thus I know the limits—merely visual—of my actual knowledge of the earth, although I am ignorant of the limits of the earth itself. But if I have got so far as to know that the earth is a sphere, and that its surface is spherical, I can cognize à priori and determine upon principles, from my knowledge of a small part of this surface—say to the extent of a degree—the diameter and circumference of the earth; and although I am ignorant of the objects which this surface contains, I have a perfect knowledge of its limits and extent.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"85.18"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0019","text":"The sum of all the possible objects of our cognition seems to us to be a level surface, with an apparent horizon—that which forms the limit of its extent, and which has been termed by us the idea of unconditioned totality. To reach this limit by empirical means is impossible, and all attempts to determine it à priori according to a principle, are alike in vain. But all the questions raised by pure reason relate to that which lies beyond this horizon, or, at least, in its boundary line.\n\nThe celebrated David Hume was one of those geographers of human reason who believe that they have given a sufficient answer to all such questions by declaring them to lie beyond the horizon of our knowledge—a horizon which, however, Hume was unable to determine. His attention especially was directed to the principle of causality; and he remarked with perfect justice that the truth of this principle, and even the objective validity of the conception of a cause, was not commonly based upon clear insight, that is, upon à priori cognition. Hence he concluded that this law does not derive its authority from its universality and necessity, but merely from its general applicability in the course of experience, and a kind of subjective necessity thence arising, which he termed habit. From the inability of reason to establish this principle as a necessary law for the acquisition of all experience, he inferred the nullity of all the attempts of reason to pass the region of the empirical.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"85.19"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","text":"This procedure of subjecting the facta of reason to examination, and, if necessary, to disapproval, may be termed the censura of reason. This censura must inevitably lead us to doubts regarding all transcendent employment of principles. But this is only the second step in our inquiry. The first step in regard to the subjects of pure reason, and which marks the infancy of that faculty, is that of dogmatism. The second, which we have just mentioned, is that of scepticism, and it gives evidence that our judgement has been improved by experience. But a third step is necessary—indicative of the maturity and manhood of the judgement, which now lays a firm foundation upon universal and necessary principles. This is the period of criticism, in which we do not examine the facta of reason, but reason itself, in the whole extent of its powers, and in regard to its capability of à priori cognition; and thus we determine not merely the empirical and ever-shifting bounds of our knowledge, but its necessary and eternal limits. We demonstrate from indubitable principles, not merely our ignorance in respect to this or that subject, but in regard to all possible questions of a certain class. Thus scepticism is a resting place for reason, in which it may reflect on its dogmatical wanderings and gain some knowledge of the region in which it happens to be, that it may pursue its way with greater certainty; but it cannot be its permanent dwelling-place. It must take up its abode only in the region of complete certitude, whether this relates to the cognition of objects themselves, or to the limits which bound all our cognition.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"85.20"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0021","text":"Reason is not to be considered as an indefinitely extended plane, of the bounds of which we have only a general knowledge; it ought rather to be compared to a sphere, the radius of which may be found from the curvature of its surface—that is, the nature of à priori synthetical propositions—and, consequently, its circumference and extent. Beyond the sphere of experience there are no objects which it can cognize; nay, even questions regarding such supposititious objects relate only to the subjective principles of a complete determination of the relations which exist between the understanding-conceptions which lie within this sphere.","kind":"passage","sequence":21,"address":"85.21"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0022","text":"We are actually in possession of à priori synthetical cognitions, as is proved by the existence of the principles of the understanding, which anticipate experience. If any one cannot comprehend the possibility of these principles, he may have some reason to doubt whether they are really à priori; but he cannot on this account declare them to be impossible, and affirm the nullity of the steps which reason may have taken under their guidance. He can only say: If we perceived their origin and their authenticity, we should be able to determine the extent and limits of reason; but, till we can do this, all propositions regarding the latter are mere random assertions. In this view, the doubt respecting all dogmatical philosophy, which proceeds without the guidance of criticism, is well grounded; but we cannot therefore deny to reason the ability to construct a sound philosophy, when the way has been prepared by a thorough critical investigation. All the conceptions produced, and all the questions raised, by pure reason, do not lie in the sphere of experience, but in that of reason itself, and hence they must be solved, and shown to be either valid or inadmissible, by that faculty. We have no right to decline the solution of such problems, on the ground that the solution can be discovered only from the nature of things, and under pretence of the limitation of human faculties, for reason is the sole creator of all these ideas, and is therefore bound either to establish their validity or to expose their illusory nature.","kind":"passage","sequence":22,"address":"85.22"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0023","text":"The polemic of scepticism is properly directed against the dogmatist, who erects a system of philosophy without having examined the fundamental objective principles on which it is based, for the purpose of evidencing the futility of his designs, and thus bringing him to a knowledge of his own powers. But, in itself, scepticism does not give us any certain information in regard to the bounds of our knowledge. All unsuccessful dogmatical attempts of reason are facia, which it is always useful to submit to the censure of the sceptic. But this cannot help us to any decision regarding the expectations which reason cherishes of better success in future endeavours; the investigations of scepticism cannot, therefore, settle the dispute regarding the rights and powers of human reason.\n\nHume is perhaps the ablest and most ingenious of all sceptical philosophers, and his writings have, undoubtedly, exerted the most powerful influence in awakening reason to a thorough investigation into its own powers. It will, therefore, well repay our labours to consider for a little the course of reasoning which he followed and the errors into which he strayed, although setting out on the path of truth and certitude.","kind":"passage","sequence":23,"address":"85.23"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0024","text":"Hume was probably aware, although he never clearly developed the notion, that we proceed in judgements of a certain class beyond our conception of the object. I have termed this kind of judgement synthetical. As regard the manner in which I pass beyond my conception by the aid of experience, no doubts can be entertained. Experience is itself a synthesis of perceptions; and it employs perceptions to increment the conception, which I obtain by means of another perception. But we feel persuaded that we are able to proceed beyond a conception, and to extend our cognition à priori. We attempt this in two ways—either, through the pure understanding, in relation to that which may become an object of experience, or, through pure reason, in relation to such properties of things, or of the existence of things, as can never be presented in any experience. This sceptical philosopher did not distinguish these two kinds of judgements, as he ought to have done, but regarded this augmentation of conceptions, and, if we may so express ourselves, the spontaneous generation of understanding and reason, independently of the impregnation of experience, as altogether impossible. The so-called à priori principles of these faculties he consequently held to be invalid and imaginary, and regarded them as nothing but subjective habits of thought originating in experience, and therefore purely empirical and contingent rules, to which we attribute a spurious necessity and universality. In support of this strange assertion, he referred us to the generally acknowledged principle of the relation between cause and effect. No faculty of the mind can conduct us from the conception of a thing to the existence of something else; and hence he believed he could infer that, without experience, we possess no source from which we can augment a conception, and no ground sufficient to justify us in framing a judgement that is to extend our cognition à priori. That the light of the sun, which shines upon a piece of wax, at the same time melts it, while it hardens clay, no power of the understanding could infer from the conceptions which we previously possessed of these substances; much less is there any à priori law that could conduct us to such a conclusion, which experience alone can certify. On the other hand, we have seen in our discussion of transcendental logic, that, although we can never proceed immediately beyond the content of the conception which is given us, we can always cognize completely à priori—in relation, however, to a third term, namely, possible experience—the law of its connection with other things. For example, if I observe that a piece of wax melts, I can cognize à priori that there must have been something (the sun’s heat) preceding, which this law; although, without the aid of experience, I could not cognize à priori and in a determinate manner either the cause from the effect, or the effect from the cause. Hume was, therefore, wrong in inferring, from the contingency of the determination according to law, the contingency of the law itself; and the passing beyond the conception of a thing to possible experience (which is an à priori proceeding, constituting the objective reality of the conception), he confounded with our synthesis of objects in actual experience, which is always, of course, empirical. Thus, too, he regarded the principle of affinity, which has its seat in the understanding and indicates a necessary connection, as a mere rule of association, lying in the imitative faculty of imagination, which can present only contingent, and not objective connections.","kind":"passage","sequence":24,"address":"85.24"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0025","text":"The sceptical errors of this remarkably acute thinker arose principally from a defect, which was common to him with the dogmatists, namely, that he had never made a systematic review of all the different kinds of à priori synthesis performed by the understanding. Had he done so, he would have found, to take one example among many, that the principle of permanence was of this character, and that it, as well as the principle of causality, anticipates experience. In this way he might have been able to describe the determinate limits of the à priori operations of understanding and reason. But he merely declared the understanding to be limited, instead of showing what its limits were; he created a general mistrust in the power of our faculties, without giving us any determinate knowledge of the bounds of our necessary and unavoidable ignorance; he examined and condemned some of the principles of the understanding, without investigating all its powers with the completeness necessary to criticism. He denies, with truth, certain powers to the understanding, but he goes further, and declares it to be utterly inadequate to the à priori extension of knowledge, although he has not fully examined all the powers which reside in the faculty; and thus the fate which always overtakes scepticism meets him too. That is to say, his own declarations are doubted, for his objections were based upon facta, which are contingent, and not upon principles, which can alone demonstrate the necessary invalidity of all dogmatical assertions.","kind":"passage","sequence":25,"address":"85.25"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0026","text":"As Hume makes no distinction between the well-grounded claims of the understanding and the dialectical pretensions of reason, against which, however, his attacks are mainly directed, reason does not feel itself shut out from all attempts at the extension of à priori cognition, and hence it refuses, in spite of a few checks in this or that quarter, to relinquish such efforts. For one naturally arms oneself to resist an attack, and becomes more obstinate in the resolve to establish the claims he has advanced. But a complete review of the powers of reason, and the conviction thence arising that we are in possession of a limited field of action, while we must admit the vanity of higher claims, puts an end to all doubt and dispute, and induces reason to rest satisfied with the undisturbed possession of its limited domain.\n\nTo the uncritical dogmatist, who has not surveyed the sphere of his understanding, nor determined, in accordance with principles, the limits of possible cognition, who, consequently, is ignorant of his own powers, and believes he will discover them by the attempts he makes in the field of cognition, these attacks of scepticism are not only dangerous, but destructive. For if there is one proposition in his chain of reasoning which he cannot prove, or the fallacy in which he cannot evolve in accordance with a principle, suspicion falls on all his statements, however plausible they may appear.","kind":"passage","sequence":26,"address":"85.26"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0027","text":"And thus scepticism, the bane of dogmatical philosophy, conducts us to a sound investigation into the understanding and the reason. When we are thus far advanced, we need fear no further attacks; for the limits of our domain are clearly marked out, and we can make no claims nor become involved in any disputes regarding the region that lies beyond these limits. Thus the sceptical procedure in philosophy does not present any solution of the problems of reason, but it forms an excellent exercise for its powers, awakening its circumspection, and indicating the means whereby it may most fully establish its claims to its legitimate possessions.","kind":"passage","sequence":27,"address":"85.27"}],"sha256":"7ec632fa786180f4585260f19f4176486a8ace49c0011629c51b8b9fdd1d7230","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6","title":"Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis","order":86,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SI_6","text":"### Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis\n\nThis critique of reason has now taught us that all its efforts to extend the bounds of knowledge, by means of pure speculation, are utterly fruitless. So much the wider field, it may appear, lies open to hypothesis; as, where we cannot know with certainty, we are at liberty to make guesses and to form suppositions.\n\nImagination may be allowed, under the strict surveillance of reason, to invent suppositions; but, these must be based on something that is perfectly certain—and that is the possibility of the object. If we are well assured upon this point, it is allowable to have recourse to supposition in regard to the reality of the object; but this supposition must, unless it is utterly groundless, be connected, as its ground of explanation, with that which is really given and absolutely certain. Such a supposition is termed a hypothesis.\n\nIt is beyond our power to form the least conception à priori of the possibility of dynamical connection in phenomena; and the category of the pure understanding will not enable us to excogitate any such connection, but merely helps us to understand it, when we meet with it in experience. For this reason we cannot, in accordance with the categories, imagine or invent any object or any property of an object not given, or that may not be given in experience, and employ it in a hypothesis; otherwise, we should be basing our chain of reasoning upon mere chimerical fancies, and not upon conceptions of things. Thus, we have no right to assume the existence of new powers, not existing in nature—for example, an understanding with a non-sensuous intuition, a force of attraction without contact, or some new kind of substances occupying space, and yet without the property of impenetrability—and, consequently, we cannot assume that there is any other kind of community among substances than that observable in experience, any kind of presence than that in space, or any kind of duration than that in time. In one word, the conditions of possible experience are for reason the only conditions of the possibility of things; reason cannot venture to form, independently of these conditions, any conceptions of things, because such conceptions, although not self-contradictory, are without object and without application.\n\nThe conceptions of reason are, as we have already shown, mere ideas, and do not relate to any object in any kind of experience. At the same time, they do not indicate imaginary or possible objects. They are purely problematical in their nature and, as aids to the heuristic exercise of the faculties, form the basis of the regulative principles for the systematic employment of the understanding in the field of experience. If we leave this ground of experience, they become mere fictions of thought, the possibility of which is quite indemonstrable; and they cannot, consequently, be employed as hypotheses in the explanation of real phenomena. It is quite admissible to cogitate the soul as simple, for the purpose of enabling ourselves to employ the idea of a perfect and necessary unity of all the faculties of the mind as the principle of all our inquiries into its internal phenomena, although we cannot cognize this unity in concreto. But to assume that the soul is a simple substance (a transcendental conception) would be enouncing a proposition which is not only indemonstrable—as many physical hypotheses are—but a proposition which is purely arbitrary, and in the highest degree rash. The simple is never presented in experience; and, if by substance is here meant the permanent object of sensuous intuition, the possibility of a simple phenomenon is perfectly inconceivable. Reason affords no good grounds for admitting the existence of intelligible beings, or of intelligible properties of sensuous things, although—as we have no conception either of their possibility or of their impossibility—it will always be out of our power to affirm dogmatically that they do not exist. In the explanation of given phenomena, no other things and no other grounds of explanation can be employed than those which stand in connection with the given phenomena according to the known laws of experience. A transcendental hypothesis, in which a mere idea of reason is employed to explain the phenomena of nature, would not give us any better insight into a phenomenon, as we should be trying to explain what we do not sufficiently understand from known empirical principles, by what we do not understand at all. The principles of such a hypothesis might conduce to the satisfaction of reason, but it would not assist the understanding in its application to objects. Order and conformity to aims in the sphere of nature must be themselves explained upon natural grounds and according to natural laws; and the wildest hypotheses, if they are only physical, are here more admissible than a hyperphysical hypothesis, such as that of a divine author. For such a hypothesis would introduce the principle of ignava ratio, which requires us to give up the search for causes that might be discovered in the course of experience and to rest satisfied with a mere idea. As regards the absolute totality of the grounds of explanation in the series of these causes, this can be no hindrance to the understanding in the case of phenomena; because, as they are to us nothing more than phenomena, we have no right to look for anything like completeness in the synthesis of the series of their conditions.\n\nTranscendental hypotheses are therefore inadmissible; and we cannot use the liberty of employing, in the absence of physical, hyperphysical grounds of explanation. And this for two reasons; first, because such hypothesis do not advance reason, but rather stop it in its progress; secondly, because this licence would render fruitless all its exertions in its own proper sphere, which is that of experience. For, when the explanation of natural phenomena happens to be difficult, we have constantly at hand a transcendental ground of explanation, which lifts us above the necessity of investigating nature; and our inquiries are brought to a close, not because we have obtained all the requisite knowledge, but because we abut upon a principle which is incomprehensible and which, indeed, is so far back in the track of thought as to contain the conception of the absolutely primal being.\n\nThe next requisite for the admissibility of a hypothesis is its sufficiency. That is, it must determine à priori the consequences which are given in experience and which are supposed to follow from the hypothesis itself. If we require to employ auxiliary hypotheses, the suspicion naturally arises that they are mere fictions; because the necessity for each of them requires the same justification as in the case of the original hypothesis, and thus their testimony is invalid. If we suppose the existence of an infinitely perfect cause, we possess sufficient grounds for the explanation of the conformity to aims, the order and the greatness which we observe in the universe; but we find ourselves obliged, when we observe the evil in the world and the exceptions to these laws, to employ new hypothesis in support of the original one. We employ the idea of the simple nature of the human soul as the foundation of all the theories we may form of its phenomena; but when we meet with difficulties in our way, when we observe in the soul phenomena similar to the changes which take place in matter, we require to call in new auxiliary hypotheses. These may, indeed, not be false, but we do not know them to be true, because the only witness to their certitude is the hypothesis which they themselves have been called in to explain.\n\nWe are not discussing the above-mentioned assertions regarding the immaterial unity of the soul and the existence of a Supreme Being as dogmata, which certain philosophers profess to demonstrate à priori, but purely as hypotheses. In the former case, the dogmatist must take care that his arguments possess the apodeictic certainty of a demonstration. For the assertion that the reality of such ideas is probable is as absurd as a proof of the probability of a proposition in geometry. Pure abstract reason, apart from all experience, can either cognize nothing at all; and hence the judgements it enounces are never mere opinions, they are either apodeictic certainties, or declarations that nothing can be known on the subject. Opinions and probable judgements on the nature of things can only be employed to explain given phenomena, or they may relate to the effect, in accordance with empirical laws, of an actually existing cause. In other words, we must restrict the sphere of opinion to the world of experience and nature. Beyond this region opinion is mere invention; unless we are groping about for the truth on a path not yet fully known, and have some hopes of stumbling upon it by chance.\n\nBut, although hypotheses are inadmissible in answers to the questions of pure speculative reason, they may be employed in the defence of these answers. That is to say, hypotheses are admissible in polemic, but not in the sphere of dogmatism. By the defence of statements of this character, I do not mean an attempt at discovering new grounds for their support, but merely the refutation of the arguments of opponents. All à priori synthetical propositions possess the peculiarity that, although the philosopher who maintains the reality of the ideas contained in the proposition is not in possession of sufficient knowledge to establish the certainty of his statements, his opponent is as little able to prove the truth of the opposite. This equality of fortune does not allow the one party to be superior to the other in the sphere of speculative cognition; and it is this sphere, accordingly, that is the proper arena of these endless speculative conflicts. But we shall afterwards show that, in relation to its practical exercise, Reason has the right of admitting what, in the field of pure speculation, she would not be justified in supposing, except upon perfectly sufficient grounds; because all such suppositions destroy the necessary completeness of speculation—a condition which the practical reason, however, does not consider to be requisite. In this sphere, therefore, Reason is mistress of a possession, her title to which she does not require to prove—which, in fact, she could not do. The burden of proof accordingly rests upon the opponent. But as he has just as little knowledge regarding the subject discussed, and is as little able to prove the non-existence of the object of an idea, as the philosopher on the other side is to demonstrate its reality, it is evident that there is an advantage on the side of the philosopher who maintains his proposition as a practically necessary supposition (melior est conditio possidentis). For he is at liberty to employ, in self-defence, the same weapons as his opponent makes use of in attacking him; that is, he has a right to use hypotheses not for the purpose of supporting the arguments in favour of his own propositions, but to show that his opponent knows no more than himself regarding the subject under discussion and cannot boast of any speculative advantage.\n\nHypotheses are, therefore, admissible in the sphere of pure reason only as weapons for self-defence, and not as supports to dogmatical assertions. But the opposing party we must always seek for in ourselves. For speculative reason is, in the sphere of transcendentalism, dialectical in its own nature. The difficulties and objections we have to fear lie in ourselves. They are like old but never superannuated claims; and we must seek them out, and settle them once and for ever, if we are to expect a permanent peace. External tranquility is hollow and unreal. The root of these contradictions, which lies in the nature of human reason, must be destroyed; and this can only be done by giving it, in the first instance, freedom to grow, nay, by nourishing it, that it may send out shoots, and thus betray its own existence. It is our duty, therefore, to try to discover new objections, to put weapons in the bands of our opponent, and to grant him the most favourable position in the arena that he can wish. We have nothing to fear from these concessions; on the contrary, we may rather hope that we shall thus make ourselves master of a possession which no one will ever venture to dispute.\n\nThe thinker requires, to be fully equipped, the hypotheses of pure reason, which, although but leaden weapons (for they have not been steeled in the armoury of experience), are as useful as any that can be employed by his opponents. If, accordingly, we have assumed, from a non-speculative point of view, the immaterial nature of the soul, and are met by the objection that experience seems to prove that the growth and decay of our mental faculties are mere modifications of the sensuous organism—we can weaken the force of this objection by the assumption that the body is nothing but the fundamental phenomenon, to which, as a necessary condition, all sensibility, and consequently all thought, relates in the present state of our existence; and that the separation of soul and body forms the conclusion of the sensuous exercise of our power of cognition and the beginning of the intellectual. The body would, in this view of the question, be regarded, not as the cause of thought, but merely as its restrictive condition, as promotive of the sensuous and animal, but as a hindrance to the pure and spiritual life; and the dependence of the animal life on the constitution of the body, would not prove that the whole life of man was also dependent on the state of the organism. We might go still farther, and discover new objections, or carry out to their extreme consequences those which have already been adduced.\n\nGeneration, in the human race as well as among the irrational animals, depends on so many accidents—of occasion, of proper sustenance, of the laws enacted by the government of a country of vice even, that it is difficult to believe in the eternal existence of a being whose life has begun under circumstances so mean and trivial, and so entirely dependent upon our own control. As regards the continuance of the existence of the whole race, we need have no difficulties, for accident in single cases is subject to general laws; but, in the case of each individual, it would seem as if we could hardly expect so wonderful an effect from causes so insignificant. But, in answer to these objections, we may adduce the transcendental hypothesis that all life is properly intelligible, and not subject to changes of time, and that it neither began in birth, nor will end in death. We may assume that this life is nothing more than a sensuous representation of pure spiritual life; that the whole world of sense is but an image, hovering before the faculty of cognition which we exercise in this sphere, and with no more objective reality than a dream; and that if we could intuite ourselves and other things as they really are, we should see ourselves in a world of spiritual natures, our connection with which did not begin at our birth and will not cease with the destruction of the body. And so on.\n\nWe cannot be said to know what has been above asserted, nor do we seriously maintain the truth of these assertions; and the notions therein indicated are not even ideas of reason, they are purely fictitious conceptions. But this hypothetical procedure is in perfect conformity with the laws of reason. Our opponent mistakes the absence of empirical conditions for a proof of the complete impossibility of all that we have asserted; and we have to show him that he has not exhausted the whole sphere of possibility and that he can as little compass that sphere by the laws of experience and nature, as we can lay a secure foundation for the operations of reason beyond the region of experience. Such hypothetical defences against the pretensions of an opponent must not be regarded as declarations of opinion. The philosopher abandons them, so soon as the opposite party renounces its dogmatical conceit. To maintain a simply negative position in relation to propositions which rest on an insecure foundation, well befits the moderation of a true philosopher; but to uphold the objections urged against an opponent as proofs of the opposite statement is a proceeding just as unwarrantable and arrogant as it is to attack the position of a philosopher who advances affirmative propositions regarding such a subject.\n\nIt is evident, therefore, that hypotheses, in the speculative sphere, are valid, not as independent propositions, but only relatively to opposite transcendent assumptions. For, to make the principles of possible experience conditions of the possibility of things in general is just as transcendent a procedure as to maintain the objective reality of ideas which can be applied to no objects except such as lie without the limits of possible experience. The judgements enounced by pure reason must be necessary, or they must not be enounced at all. Reason cannot trouble herself with opinions. But the hypotheses we have been discussing are merely problematical judgements, which can neither be confuted nor proved; while, therefore, they are not personal opinions, they are indispensable as answers to objections which are liable to be raised. But we must take care to confine them to this function, and guard against any assumption on their part of absolute validity, a proceeding which would involve reason in inextricable difficulties and contradictions.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","text":"### Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis\n\nThis critique of reason has now taught us that all its efforts to extend the bounds of knowledge, by means of pure speculation, are utterly fruitless. So much the wider field, it may appear, lies open to hypothesis; as, where we cannot know with certainty, we are at liberty to make guesses and to form suppositions.\n\nImagination may be allowed, under the strict surveillance of reason, to invent suppositions; but, these must be based on something that is perfectly certain—and that is the possibility of the object. If we are well assured upon this point, it is allowable to have recourse to supposition in regard to the reality of the object; but this supposition must, unless it is utterly groundless, be connected, as its ground of explanation, with that which is really given and absolutely certain. Such a supposition is termed a hypothesis.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"86.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0002","text":"It is beyond our power to form the least conception à priori of the possibility of dynamical connection in phenomena; and the category of the pure understanding will not enable us to excogitate any such connection, but merely helps us to understand it, when we meet with it in experience. For this reason we cannot, in accordance with the categories, imagine or invent any object or any property of an object not given, or that may not be given in experience, and employ it in a hypothesis; otherwise, we should be basing our chain of reasoning upon mere chimerical fancies, and not upon conceptions of things. Thus, we have no right to assume the existence of new powers, not existing in nature—for example, an understanding with a non-sensuous intuition, a force of attraction without contact, or some new kind of substances occupying space, and yet without the property of impenetrability—and, consequently, we cannot assume that there is any other kind of community among substances than that observable in experience, any kind of presence than that in space, or any kind of duration than that in time. In one word, the conditions of possible experience are for reason the only conditions of the possibility of things; reason cannot venture to form, independently of these conditions, any conceptions of things, because such conceptions, although not self-contradictory, are without object and without application.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"86.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","text":"The conceptions of reason are, as we have already shown, mere ideas, and do not relate to any object in any kind of experience. At the same time, they do not indicate imaginary or possible objects. They are purely problematical in their nature and, as aids to the heuristic exercise of the faculties, form the basis of the regulative principles for the systematic employment of the understanding in the field of experience. If we leave this ground of experience, they become mere fictions of thought, the possibility of which is quite indemonstrable; and they cannot, consequently, be employed as hypotheses in the explanation of real phenomena. It is quite admissible to cogitate the soul as simple, for the purpose of enabling ourselves to employ the idea of a perfect and necessary unity of all the faculties of the mind as the principle of all our inquiries into its internal phenomena, although we cannot cognize this unity in concreto. But to assume that the soul is a simple substance (a transcendental conception) would be enouncing a proposition which is not only indemonstrable—as many physical hypotheses are—but a proposition which is purely arbitrary, and in the highest degree rash. The simple is never presented in experience; and, if by substance is here meant the permanent object of sensuous intuition, the possibility of a simple phenomenon is perfectly inconceivable. Reason affords no good grounds for admitting the existence of intelligible beings, or of intelligible properties of sensuous things, although—as we have no conception either of their possibility or of their impossibility—it will always be out of our power to affirm dogmatically that they do not exist. In the explanation of given phenomena, no other things and no other grounds of explanation can be employed than those which stand in connection with the given phenomena according to the known laws of experience. A transcendental hypothesis, in which a mere idea of reason is employed to explain the phenomena of nature, would not give us any better insight into a phenomenon, as we should be trying to explain what we do not sufficiently understand from known empirical principles, by what we do not understand at all. The principles of such a hypothesis might conduce to the satisfaction of reason, but it would not assist the understanding in its application to objects. Order and conformity to aims in the sphere of nature must be themselves explained upon natural grounds and according to natural laws; and the wildest hypotheses, if they are only physical, are here more admissible than a hyperphysical hypothesis, such as that of a divine author. For such a hypothesis would introduce the principle of ignava ratio, which requires us to give up the search for causes that might be discovered in the course of experience and to rest satisfied with a mere idea. As regards the absolute totality of the grounds of explanation in the series of these causes, this can be no hindrance to the understanding in the case of phenomena; because, as they are to us nothing more than phenomena, we have no right to look for anything like completeness in the synthesis of the series of their conditions.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"86.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0004","text":"Transcendental hypotheses are therefore inadmissible; and we cannot use the liberty of employing, in the absence of physical, hyperphysical grounds of explanation. And this for two reasons; first, because such hypothesis do not advance reason, but rather stop it in its progress; secondly, because this licence would render fruitless all its exertions in its own proper sphere, which is that of experience. For, when the explanation of natural phenomena happens to be difficult, we have constantly at hand a transcendental ground of explanation, which lifts us above the necessity of investigating nature; and our inquiries are brought to a close, not because we have obtained all the requisite knowledge, but because we abut upon a principle which is incomprehensible and which, indeed, is so far back in the track of thought as to contain the conception of the absolutely primal being.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"86.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0005","text":"The next requisite for the admissibility of a hypothesis is its sufficiency. That is, it must determine à priori the consequences which are given in experience and which are supposed to follow from the hypothesis itself. If we require to employ auxiliary hypotheses, the suspicion naturally arises that they are mere fictions; because the necessity for each of them requires the same justification as in the case of the original hypothesis, and thus their testimony is invalid. If we suppose the existence of an infinitely perfect cause, we possess sufficient grounds for the explanation of the conformity to aims, the order and the greatness which we observe in the universe; but we find ourselves obliged, when we observe the evil in the world and the exceptions to these laws, to employ new hypothesis in support of the original one. We employ the idea of the simple nature of the human soul as the foundation of all the theories we may form of its phenomena; but when we meet with difficulties in our way, when we observe in the soul phenomena similar to the changes which take place in matter, we require to call in new auxiliary hypotheses. These may, indeed, not be false, but we do not know them to be true, because the only witness to their certitude is the hypothesis which they themselves have been called in to explain.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"86.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0006","text":"We are not discussing the above-mentioned assertions regarding the immaterial unity of the soul and the existence of a Supreme Being as dogmata, which certain philosophers profess to demonstrate à priori, but purely as hypotheses. In the former case, the dogmatist must take care that his arguments possess the apodeictic certainty of a demonstration. For the assertion that the reality of such ideas is probable is as absurd as a proof of the probability of a proposition in geometry. Pure abstract reason, apart from all experience, can either cognize nothing at all; and hence the judgements it enounces are never mere opinions, they are either apodeictic certainties, or declarations that nothing can be known on the subject. Opinions and probable judgements on the nature of things can only be employed to explain given phenomena, or they may relate to the effect, in accordance with empirical laws, of an actually existing cause. In other words, we must restrict the sphere of opinion to the world of experience and nature. Beyond this region opinion is mere invention; unless we are groping about for the truth on a path not yet fully known, and have some hopes of stumbling upon it by chance.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"86.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","text":"But, although hypotheses are inadmissible in answers to the questions of pure speculative reason, they may be employed in the defence of these answers. That is to say, hypotheses are admissible in polemic, but not in the sphere of dogmatism. By the defence of statements of this character, I do not mean an attempt at discovering new grounds for their support, but merely the refutation of the arguments of opponents. All à priori synthetical propositions possess the peculiarity that, although the philosopher who maintains the reality of the ideas contained in the proposition is not in possession of sufficient knowledge to establish the certainty of his statements, his opponent is as little able to prove the truth of the opposite. This equality of fortune does not allow the one party to be superior to the other in the sphere of speculative cognition; and it is this sphere, accordingly, that is the proper arena of these endless speculative conflicts. But we shall afterwards show that, in relation to its practical exercise, Reason has the right of admitting what, in the field of pure speculation, she would not be justified in supposing, except upon perfectly sufficient grounds; because all such suppositions destroy the necessary completeness of speculation—a condition which the practical reason, however, does not consider to be requisite. In this sphere, therefore, Reason is mistress of a possession, her title to which she does not require to prove—which, in fact, she could not do. The burden of proof accordingly rests upon the opponent. But as he has just as little knowledge regarding the subject discussed, and is as little able to prove the non-existence of the object of an idea, as the philosopher on the other side is to demonstrate its reality, it is evident that there is an advantage on the side of the philosopher who maintains his proposition as a practically necessary supposition (melior est conditio possidentis). For he is at liberty to employ, in self-defence, the same weapons as his opponent makes use of in attacking him; that is, he has a right to use hypotheses not for the purpose of supporting the arguments in favour of his own propositions, but to show that his opponent knows no more than himself regarding the subject under discussion and cannot boast of any speculative advantage.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"86.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0008","text":"Hypotheses are, therefore, admissible in the sphere of pure reason only as weapons for self-defence, and not as supports to dogmatical assertions. But the opposing party we must always seek for in ourselves. For speculative reason is, in the sphere of transcendentalism, dialectical in its own nature. The difficulties and objections we have to fear lie in ourselves. They are like old but never superannuated claims; and we must seek them out, and settle them once and for ever, if we are to expect a permanent peace. External tranquility is hollow and unreal. The root of these contradictions, which lies in the nature of human reason, must be destroyed; and this can only be done by giving it, in the first instance, freedom to grow, nay, by nourishing it, that it may send out shoots, and thus betray its own existence. It is our duty, therefore, to try to discover new objections, to put weapons in the bands of our opponent, and to grant him the most favourable position in the arena that he can wish. We have nothing to fear from these concessions; on the contrary, we may rather hope that we shall thus make ourselves master of a possession which no one will ever venture to dispute.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"86.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0009","text":"The thinker requires, to be fully equipped, the hypotheses of pure reason, which, although but leaden weapons (for they have not been steeled in the armoury of experience), are as useful as any that can be employed by his opponents. If, accordingly, we have assumed, from a non-speculative point of view, the immaterial nature of the soul, and are met by the objection that experience seems to prove that the growth and decay of our mental faculties are mere modifications of the sensuous organism—we can weaken the force of this objection by the assumption that the body is nothing but the fundamental phenomenon, to which, as a necessary condition, all sensibility, and consequently all thought, relates in the present state of our existence; and that the separation of soul and body forms the conclusion of the sensuous exercise of our power of cognition and the beginning of the intellectual. The body would, in this view of the question, be regarded, not as the cause of thought, but merely as its restrictive condition, as promotive of the sensuous and animal, but as a hindrance to the pure and spiritual life; and the dependence of the animal life on the constitution of the body, would not prove that the whole life of man was also dependent on the state of the organism. We might go still farther, and discover new objections, or carry out to their extreme consequences those which have already been adduced.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"86.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0010","text":"Generation, in the human race as well as among the irrational animals, depends on so many accidents—of occasion, of proper sustenance, of the laws enacted by the government of a country of vice even, that it is difficult to believe in the eternal existence of a being whose life has begun under circumstances so mean and trivial, and so entirely dependent upon our own control. As regards the continuance of the existence of the whole race, we need have no difficulties, for accident in single cases is subject to general laws; but, in the case of each individual, it would seem as if we could hardly expect so wonderful an effect from causes so insignificant. But, in answer to these objections, we may adduce the transcendental hypothesis that all life is properly intelligible, and not subject to changes of time, and that it neither began in birth, nor will end in death. We may assume that this life is nothing more than a sensuous representation of pure spiritual life; that the whole world of sense is but an image, hovering before the faculty of cognition which we exercise in this sphere, and with no more objective reality than a dream; and that if we could intuite ourselves and other things as they really are, we should see ourselves in a world of spiritual natures, our connection with which did not begin at our birth and will not cease with the destruction of the body. And so on.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"86.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","text":"We cannot be said to know what has been above asserted, nor do we seriously maintain the truth of these assertions; and the notions therein indicated are not even ideas of reason, they are purely fictitious conceptions. But this hypothetical procedure is in perfect conformity with the laws of reason. Our opponent mistakes the absence of empirical conditions for a proof of the complete impossibility of all that we have asserted; and we have to show him that he has not exhausted the whole sphere of possibility and that he can as little compass that sphere by the laws of experience and nature, as we can lay a secure foundation for the operations of reason beyond the region of experience. Such hypothetical defences against the pretensions of an opponent must not be regarded as declarations of opinion. The philosopher abandons them, so soon as the opposite party renounces its dogmatical conceit. To maintain a simply negative position in relation to propositions which rest on an insecure foundation, well befits the moderation of a true philosopher; but to uphold the objections urged against an opponent as proofs of the opposite statement is a proceeding just as unwarrantable and arrogant as it is to attack the position of a philosopher who advances affirmative propositions regarding such a subject.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"86.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0012","text":"It is evident, therefore, that hypotheses, in the speculative sphere, are valid, not as independent propositions, but only relatively to opposite transcendent assumptions. For, to make the principles of possible experience conditions of the possibility of things in general is just as transcendent a procedure as to maintain the objective reality of ideas which can be applied to no objects except such as lie without the limits of possible experience. The judgements enounced by pure reason must be necessary, or they must not be enounced at all. Reason cannot trouble herself with opinions. But the hypotheses we have been discussing are merely problematical judgements, which can neither be confuted nor proved; while, therefore, they are not personal opinions, they are indispensable as answers to objections which are liable to be raised. But we must take care to confine them to this function, and guard against any assumption on their part of absolute validity, a proceeding which would involve reason in inextricable difficulties and contradictions.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"86.12"}],"sha256":"8c38afd67de3f3977af09bbb173b504a137020a7c571d1ef33e71fcc8b8ee63b","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7","title":"Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs","order":87,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SI_7","text":"# Proofs\n\n### Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs\n\nIt is a peculiarity, which distinguishes the proofs of transcendental synthetical propositions from those of all other à priori synthetical cognitions, that reason, in the case of the former, does not apply its conceptions directly to an object, but is first obliged to prove, à priori, the objective validity of these conceptions and the possibility of their syntheses. This is not merely a prudential rule, it is essential to the very possibility of the proof of a transcendental proposition. If I am required to pass, à priori, beyond the conception of an object, I find that it is utterly impossible without the guidance of something which is not contained in the conception. In mathematics, it is à priori intuition that guides my synthesis; and, in this case, all our conclusions may be drawn immediately from pure intuition. In transcendental cognition, so long as we are dealing only with conceptions of the understanding, we are guided by possible experience. That is to say, a proof in the sphere of transcendental cognition does not show that the given conception (that of an event, for example) leads directly to another conception (that of a cause)—for this would be a saltus which nothing can justify; but it shows that experience itself, and consequently the object of experience, is impossible without the connection indicated by these conceptions. It follows that such a proof must demonstrate the possibility of arriving, synthetically and à priori, at a certain knowledge of things, which was not contained in our conceptions of these things. Unless we pay particular attention to this requirement, our proofs, instead of pursuing the straight path indicated by reason, follow the tortuous road of mere subjective association. The illusory conviction, which rests upon subjective causes of association, and which is considered as resulting from the perception of a real and objective natural affinity, is always open to doubt and suspicion. For this reason, all the attempts which have been made to prove the principle of sufficient reason, have, according to the universal admission of philosophers, been quite unsuccessful; and, before the appearance of transcendental criticism, it was considered better, as this principle could not be abandoned, to appeal boldly to the common sense of mankind (a proceeding which always proves that the problem, which reason ought to solve, is one in which philosophers find great difficulties), rather than attempt to discover new dogmatical proofs.\n\nBut, if the proposition to be proved is a proposition of pure reason, and if I aim at passing beyond my empirical conceptions by the aid of mere ideas, it is necessary that the proof should first show that such a step in synthesis is possible (which it is not), before it proceeds to prove the truth of the proposition itself. The so-called proof of the simple nature of the soul from the unity of apperception, is a very plausible one. But it contains no answer to the objection, that, as the notion of absolute simplicity is not a conception which is directly applicable to a perception, but is an idea which must be inferred—if at all—from observation, it is by no means evident how the mere fact of consciousness, which is contained in all thought, although in so far a simple representation, can conduct me to the consciousness and cognition of a thing which is purely a thinking substance. When I represent to my mind the power of my body as in motion, my body in this thought is so far absolute unity, and my representation of it is a simple one; and hence I can indicate this representation by the motion of a point, because I have made abstraction of the size or volume of the body. But I cannot hence infer that, given merely the moving power of a body, the body may be cogitated as simple substance, merely because the representation in my mind takes no account of its content in space, and is consequently simple. The simple, in abstraction, is very different from the objectively simple; and hence the Ego, which is simple in the first sense, may, in the second sense, as indicating the soul itself, be a very complex conception, with a very various content. Thus it is evident that in all such arguments there lurks a paralogism. We guess (for without some such surmise our suspicion would not be excited in reference to a proof of this character) at the presence of the paralogism, by keeping ever before us a criterion of the possibility of those synthetical propositions which aim at proving more than experience can teach us. This criterion is obtained from the observation that such proofs do not lead us directly from the subject of the proposition to be proved to the required predicate, but find it necessary to presuppose the possibility of extending our cognition à priori by means of ideas. We must, accordingly, always use the greatest caution; we require, before attempting any proof, to consider how it is possible to extend the sphere of cognition by the operations of pure reason, and from what source we are to derive knowledge, which is not obtained from the analysis of conceptions, nor relates, by anticipation, to possible experience. We shall thus spare ourselves much severe and fruitless labour, by not expecting from reason what is beyond its power, or rather by subjecting it to discipline, and teaching it to moderate its vehement desires for the extension of the sphere of cognition.\n\nThe first rule for our guidance is, therefore, not to attempt a transcendental proof, before we have considered from what source we are to derive the principles upon which the proof is to be based, and what right we have to expect that our conclusions from these principles will be veracious. If they are principles of the understanding, it is vain to expect that we should attain by their means to ideas of pure reason; for these principles are valid only in regard to objects of possible experience. If they are principles of pure reason, our labour is alike in vain. For the principles of reason, if employed as objective, are without exception dialectical and possess no validity or truth, except as regulative principles of the systematic employment of reason in experience. But when such delusive proof are presented to us, it is our duty to meet them with the non liquet of a matured judgement; and, although we are unable to expose the particular sophism upon which the proof is based, we have a right to demand a deduction of the principles employed in it; and, if these principles have their origin in pure reason alone, such a deduction is absolutely impossible. And thus it is unnecessary that we should trouble ourselves with the exposure and confutation of every sophistical illusion; we may, at once, bring all dialectic, which is inexhaustible in the production of fallacies, before the bar of critical reason, which tests the principles upon which all dialectical procedure is based. The second peculiarity of transcendental proof is that a transcendental proposition cannot rest upon more than a single proof. If I am drawing conclusions, not from conceptions, but from intuition corresponding to a conception, be it pure intuition, as in mathematics, or empirical, as in natural science, the intuition which forms the basis of my inferences presents me with materials for many synthetical propositions, which I can connect in various modes, while, as it is allowable to proceed from different points in the intention, I can arrive by different paths at the same proposition.\n\nBut every transcendental proposition sets out from a conception, and posits the synthetical condition of the possibility of an object according to this conception. There must, therefore, be but one ground of proof, because it is the conception alone which determines the object; and thus the proof cannot contain anything more than the determination of the object according to the conception. In our Transcendental Analytic, for example, we inferred the principle: Every event has a cause, from the only condition of the objective possibility of our conception of an event. This is that an event cannot be determined in time, and consequently cannot form a part of experience, unless it stands under this dynamical law. This is the only possible ground of proof; for our conception of an event possesses objective validity, that is, is a true conception, only because the law of causality determines an object to which it can refer. Other arguments in support of this principle have been attempted—such as that from the contingent nature of a phenomenon; but when this argument is considered, we can discover no criterion of contingency, except the fact of an event—of something happening, that is to say, the existence which is preceded by the non-existence of an object, and thus we fall back on the very thing to be proved. If the proposition: “Every thinking being is simple,” is to be proved, we keep to the conception of the ego, which is simple, and to which all thought has a relation. The same is the case with the transcendental proof of the existence of a Deity, which is based solely upon the harmony and reciprocal fitness of the conceptions of an ens realissimum and a necessary being, and cannot be attempted in any other manner.\n\nThis caution serves to simplify very much the criticism of all propositions of reason. When reason employs conceptions alone, only one proof of its thesis is possible, if any. When, therefore, the dogmatist advances with ten arguments in favour of a proposition, we may be sure that not one of them is conclusive. For if he possessed one which proved the proposition he brings forward to demonstration—as must always be the case with the propositions of pure reason—what need is there for any more? His intention can only be similar to that of the advocate who had different arguments for different judges; this availing himself of the weakness of those who examine his arguments, who, without going into any profound investigation, adopt the view of the case which seems most probable at first sight and decide according to it.\n\nThe third rule for the guidance of pure reason in the conduct of a proof is that all transcendental proofs must never be apagogic or indirect, but always ostensive or direct. The direct or ostensive proof not only establishes the truth of the proposition to be proved, but exposes the grounds of its truth; the apagogic, on the other hand, may assure us of the truth of the proposition, but it cannot enable us to comprehend the grounds of its possibility. The latter is, accordingly, rather an auxiliary to an argument, than a strictly philosophical and rational mode of procedure. In one respect, however, they have an advantage over direct proofs, from the fact that the mode of arguing by contradiction, which they employ, renders our understanding of the question more clear, and approximates the proof to the certainty of an intuitional demonstration.\n\nThe true reason why indirect proofs are employed in different sciences is this. When the grounds upon which we seek to base a cognition are too various or too profound, we try whether or not we may not discover the truth of our cognition from its consequences. The modus ponens of reasoning from the truth of its inferences to the truth of a proposition would be admissible if all the inferences that can be drawn from it are known to be true; for in this case there can be only one possible ground for these inferences, and that is the true one. But this is a quite impracticable procedure, as it surpasses all our powers to discover all the possible inferences that can be drawn from a proposition. But this mode of reasoning is employed, under favour, when we wish to prove the truth of an hypothesis; in which case we admit the truth of the conclusion—which is supported by analogy—that, if all the inferences we have drawn and examined agree with the proposition assumed, all other possible inferences will also agree with it. But, in this way, an hypothesis can never be established as a demonstrated truth. The modus tollens of reasoning from known inferences to the unknown proposition, is not only a rigorous, but a very easy mode of proof. For, if it can be shown that but one inference from a proposition is false, then the proposition must itself be false. Instead, then, of examining, in an ostensive argument, the whole series of the grounds on which the truth of a proposition rests, we need only take the opposite of this proposition, and if one inference from it be false, then must the opposite be itself false; and, consequently, the proposition which we wished to prove must be true.\n\nThe apagogic method of proof is admissible only in those sciences where it is impossible to mistake a subjective representation for an objective cognition. Where this is possible, it is plain that the opposite of a given proposition may contradict merely the subjective conditions of thought, and not the objective cognition; or it may happen that both propositions contradict each other only under a subjective condition, which is incorrectly considered to be objective, and, as the condition is itself false, both propositions may be false, and it will, consequently, be impossible to conclude the truth of the one from the falseness of the other.\n\nIn mathematics such subreptions are impossible; and it is in this science, accordingly, that the indirect mode of proof has its true place. In the science of nature, where all assertion is based upon empirical intuition, such subreptions may be guarded against by the repeated comparison of observations; but this mode of proof is of little value in this sphere of knowledge. But the transcendental efforts of pure reason are all made in the sphere of the subjective, which is the real medium of all dialectical illusion; and thus reason endeavours, in its premisses, to impose upon us subjective representations for objective cognitions. In the transcendental sphere of pure reason, then, and in the case of synthetical propositions, it is inadmissible to support a statement by disproving the counter-statement. For only two cases are possible; either, the counter-statement is nothing but the enouncement of the inconsistency of the opposite opinion with the subjective conditions of reason, which does not affect the real case (for example, we cannot comprehend the unconditioned necessity of the existence of a being, and hence every speculative proof of the existence of such a being must be opposed on subjective grounds, while the possibility of this being in itself cannot with justice be denied); or, both propositions, being dialectical in their nature, are based upon an impossible conception. In this latter case the rule applies: non entis nulla sunt predicata; that is to say, what we affirm and what we deny, respecting such an object, are equally untrue, and the apagogic mode of arriving at the truth is in this case impossible. If, for example, we presuppose that the world of sense is given in itself in its totality, it is false, either that it is infinite, or that it is finite and limited in space. Both are false, because the hypothesis is false. For the notion of phenomena (as mere representations) which are given in themselves (as objects) is self-contradictory; and the infinitude of this imaginary whole would, indeed, be unconditioned, but would be inconsistent (as everything in the phenomenal world is conditioned) with the unconditioned determination and finitude of quantities which is presupposed in our conception.\n\nThe apagogic mode of proof is the true source of those illusions which have always had so strong an attraction for the admirers of dogmatical philosophy. It may be compared to a champion who maintains the honour and claims of the party he has adopted by offering battle to all who doubt the validity of these claims and the purity of that honour; while nothing can be proved in this way, except the respective strength of the combatants, and the advantage, in this respect, is always on the side of the attacking party. Spectators, observing that each party is alternately conqueror and conquered, are led to regard the subject of dispute as beyond the power of man to decide upon. But such an opinion cannot be justified; and it is sufficient to apply to these reasoners the remark:\n\n_Non defensoribus istis  \nTempus eget._\n\nEach must try to establish his assertions by a transcendental deduction of the grounds of proof employed in his argument, and thus enable us to see in what way the claims of reason may be supported. If an opponent bases his assertions upon subjective grounds, he may be refuted with ease; not, however to the advantage of the dogmatist, who likewise depends upon subjective sources of cognition and is in like manner driven into a corner by his opponent. But, if parties employ the direct method of procedure, they will soon discover the difficulty, nay, the impossibility of proving their assertions, and will be forced to appeal to prescription and precedence; or they will, by the help of criticism, discover with ease the dogmatical illusions by which they had been mocked, and compel reason to renounce its exaggerated pretensions to speculative insight and to confine itself within the limits of its proper sphere—that of practical principles.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","text":"# Proofs\n\n### Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs\n\nIt is a peculiarity, which distinguishes the proofs of transcendental synthetical propositions from those of all other à priori synthetical cognitions, that reason, in the case of the former, does not apply its conceptions directly to an object, but is first obliged to prove, à priori, the objective validity of these conceptions and the possibility of their syntheses. This is not merely a prudential rule, it is essential to the very possibility of the proof of a transcendental proposition. If I am required to pass, à priori, beyond the conception of an object, I find that it is utterly impossible without the guidance of something which is not contained in the conception. In mathematics, it is à priori intuition that guides my synthesis; and, in this case, all our conclusions may be drawn immediately from pure intuition. In transcendental cognition, so long as we are dealing only with conceptions of the understanding, we are guided by possible experience. That is to say, a proof in the sphere of transcendental cognition does not show that the given conception (that of an event, for example) leads directly to another conception (that of a cause)—for this would be a saltus which nothing can justify; but it shows that experience itself, and consequently the object of experience, is impossible without the connection indicated by these conceptions. It follows that such a proof must demonstrate the possibility of arriving, synthetically and à priori, at a certain knowledge of things, which was not contained in our conceptions of these things. Unless we pay particular attention to this requirement, our proofs, instead of pursuing the straight path indicated by reason, follow the tortuous road of mere subjective association. The illusory conviction, which rests upon subjective causes of association, and which is considered as resulting from the perception of a real and objective natural affinity, is always open to doubt and suspicion. For this reason, all the attempts which have been made to prove the principle of sufficient reason, have, according to the universal admission of philosophers, been quite unsuccessful; and, before the appearance of transcendental criticism, it was considered better, as this principle could not be abandoned, to appeal boldly to the common sense of mankind (a proceeding which always proves that the problem, which reason ought to solve, is one in which philosophers find great difficulties), rather than attempt to discover new dogmatical proofs.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"87.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0002","text":"But, if the proposition to be proved is a proposition of pure reason, and if I aim at passing beyond my empirical conceptions by the aid of mere ideas, it is necessary that the proof should first show that such a step in synthesis is possible (which it is not), before it proceeds to prove the truth of the proposition itself. The so-called proof of the simple nature of the soul from the unity of apperception, is a very plausible one. But it contains no answer to the objection, that, as the notion of absolute simplicity is not a conception which is directly applicable to a perception, but is an idea which must be inferred—if at all—from observation, it is by no means evident how the mere fact of consciousness, which is contained in all thought, although in so far a simple representation, can conduct me to the consciousness and cognition of a thing which is purely a thinking substance. When I represent to my mind the power of my body as in motion, my body in this thought is so far absolute unity, and my representation of it is a simple one; and hence I can indicate this representation by the motion of a point, because I have made abstraction of the size or volume of the body. But I cannot hence infer that, given merely the moving power of a body, the body may be cogitated as simple substance, merely because the representation in my mind takes no account of its content in space, and is consequently simple. The simple, in abstraction, is very different from the objectively simple; and hence the Ego, which is simple in the first sense, may, in the second sense, as indicating the soul itself, be a very complex conception, with a very various content. Thus it is evident that in all such arguments there lurks a paralogism. We guess (for without some such surmise our suspicion would not be excited in reference to a proof of this character) at the presence of the paralogism, by keeping ever before us a criterion of the possibility of those synthetical propositions which aim at proving more than experience can teach us. This criterion is obtained from the observation that such proofs do not lead us directly from the subject of the proposition to be proved to the required predicate, but find it necessary to presuppose the possibility of extending our cognition à priori by means of ideas. We must, accordingly, always use the greatest caution; we require, before attempting any proof, to consider how it is possible to extend the sphere of cognition by the operations of pure reason, and from what source we are to derive knowledge, which is not obtained from the analysis of conceptions, nor relates, by anticipation, to possible experience. We shall thus spare ourselves much severe and fruitless labour, by not expecting from reason what is beyond its power, or rather by subjecting it to discipline, and teaching it to moderate its vehement desires for the extension of the sphere of cognition.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"87.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","text":"The first rule for our guidance is, therefore, not to attempt a transcendental proof, before we have considered from what source we are to derive the principles upon which the proof is to be based, and what right we have to expect that our conclusions from these principles will be veracious. If they are principles of the understanding, it is vain to expect that we should attain by their means to ideas of pure reason; for these principles are valid only in regard to objects of possible experience. If they are principles of pure reason, our labour is alike in vain. For the principles of reason, if employed as objective, are without exception dialectical and possess no validity or truth, except as regulative principles of the systematic employment of reason in experience. But when such delusive proof are presented to us, it is our duty to meet them with the non liquet of a matured judgement; and, although we are unable to expose the particular sophism upon which the proof is based, we have a right to demand a deduction of the principles employed in it; and, if these principles have their origin in pure reason alone, such a deduction is absolutely impossible. And thus it is unnecessary that we should trouble ourselves with the exposure and confutation of every sophistical illusion; we may, at once, bring all dialectic, which is inexhaustible in the production of fallacies, before the bar of critical reason, which tests the principles upon which all dialectical procedure is based. The second peculiarity of transcendental proof is that a transcendental proposition cannot rest upon more than a single proof. If I am drawing conclusions, not from conceptions, but from intuition corresponding to a conception, be it pure intuition, as in mathematics, or empirical, as in natural science, the intuition which forms the basis of my inferences presents me with materials for many synthetical propositions, which I can connect in various modes, while, as it is allowable to proceed from different points in the intention, I can arrive by different paths at the same proposition.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"87.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","text":"But every transcendental proposition sets out from a conception, and posits the synthetical condition of the possibility of an object according to this conception. There must, therefore, be but one ground of proof, because it is the conception alone which determines the object; and thus the proof cannot contain anything more than the determination of the object according to the conception. In our Transcendental Analytic, for example, we inferred the principle: Every event has a cause, from the only condition of the objective possibility of our conception of an event. This is that an event cannot be determined in time, and consequently cannot form a part of experience, unless it stands under this dynamical law. This is the only possible ground of proof; for our conception of an event possesses objective validity, that is, is a true conception, only because the law of causality determines an object to which it can refer. Other arguments in support of this principle have been attempted—such as that from the contingent nature of a phenomenon; but when this argument is considered, we can discover no criterion of contingency, except the fact of an event—of something happening, that is to say, the existence which is preceded by the non-existence of an object, and thus we fall back on the very thing to be proved. If the proposition: “Every thinking being is simple,” is to be proved, we keep to the conception of the ego, which is simple, and to which all thought has a relation. The same is the case with the transcendental proof of the existence of a Deity, which is based solely upon the harmony and reciprocal fitness of the conceptions of an ens realissimum and a necessary being, and cannot be attempted in any other manner.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"87.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0005","text":"This caution serves to simplify very much the criticism of all propositions of reason. When reason employs conceptions alone, only one proof of its thesis is possible, if any. When, therefore, the dogmatist advances with ten arguments in favour of a proposition, we may be sure that not one of them is conclusive. For if he possessed one which proved the proposition he brings forward to demonstration—as must always be the case with the propositions of pure reason—what need is there for any more? His intention can only be similar to that of the advocate who had different arguments for different judges; this availing himself of the weakness of those who examine his arguments, who, without going into any profound investigation, adopt the view of the case which seems most probable at first sight and decide according to it.\n\nThe third rule for the guidance of pure reason in the conduct of a proof is that all transcendental proofs must never be apagogic or indirect, but always ostensive or direct. The direct or ostensive proof not only establishes the truth of the proposition to be proved, but exposes the grounds of its truth; the apagogic, on the other hand, may assure us of the truth of the proposition, but it cannot enable us to comprehend the grounds of its possibility. The latter is, accordingly, rather an auxiliary to an argument, than a strictly philosophical and rational mode of procedure. In one respect, however, they have an advantage over direct proofs, from the fact that the mode of arguing by contradiction, which they employ, renders our understanding of the question more clear, and approximates the proof to the certainty of an intuitional demonstration.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"87.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0006","text":"The true reason why indirect proofs are employed in different sciences is this. When the grounds upon which we seek to base a cognition are too various or too profound, we try whether or not we may not discover the truth of our cognition from its consequences. The modus ponens of reasoning from the truth of its inferences to the truth of a proposition would be admissible if all the inferences that can be drawn from it are known to be true; for in this case there can be only one possible ground for these inferences, and that is the true one. But this is a quite impracticable procedure, as it surpasses all our powers to discover all the possible inferences that can be drawn from a proposition. But this mode of reasoning is employed, under favour, when we wish to prove the truth of an hypothesis; in which case we admit the truth of the conclusion—which is supported by analogy—that, if all the inferences we have drawn and examined agree with the proposition assumed, all other possible inferences will also agree with it. But, in this way, an hypothesis can never be established as a demonstrated truth. The modus tollens of reasoning from known inferences to the unknown proposition, is not only a rigorous, but a very easy mode of proof. For, if it can be shown that but one inference from a proposition is false, then the proposition must itself be false. Instead, then, of examining, in an ostensive argument, the whole series of the grounds on which the truth of a proposition rests, we need only take the opposite of this proposition, and if one inference from it be false, then must the opposite be itself false; and, consequently, the proposition which we wished to prove must be true.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"87.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0007","text":"The apagogic method of proof is admissible only in those sciences where it is impossible to mistake a subjective representation for an objective cognition. Where this is possible, it is plain that the opposite of a given proposition may contradict merely the subjective conditions of thought, and not the objective cognition; or it may happen that both propositions contradict each other only under a subjective condition, which is incorrectly considered to be objective, and, as the condition is itself false, both propositions may be false, and it will, consequently, be impossible to conclude the truth of the one from the falseness of the other.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"87.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","text":"In mathematics such subreptions are impossible; and it is in this science, accordingly, that the indirect mode of proof has its true place. In the science of nature, where all assertion is based upon empirical intuition, such subreptions may be guarded against by the repeated comparison of observations; but this mode of proof is of little value in this sphere of knowledge. But the transcendental efforts of pure reason are all made in the sphere of the subjective, which is the real medium of all dialectical illusion; and thus reason endeavours, in its premisses, to impose upon us subjective representations for objective cognitions. In the transcendental sphere of pure reason, then, and in the case of synthetical propositions, it is inadmissible to support a statement by disproving the counter-statement. For only two cases are possible; either, the counter-statement is nothing but the enouncement of the inconsistency of the opposite opinion with the subjective conditions of reason, which does not affect the real case (for example, we cannot comprehend the unconditioned necessity of the existence of a being, and hence every speculative proof of the existence of such a being must be opposed on subjective grounds, while the possibility of this being in itself cannot with justice be denied); or, both propositions, being dialectical in their nature, are based upon an impossible conception. In this latter case the rule applies: non entis nulla sunt predicata; that is to say, what we affirm and what we deny, respecting such an object, are equally untrue, and the apagogic mode of arriving at the truth is in this case impossible. If, for example, we presuppose that the world of sense is given in itself in its totality, it is false, either that it is infinite, or that it is finite and limited in space. Both are false, because the hypothesis is false. For the notion of phenomena (as mere representations) which are given in themselves (as objects) is self-contradictory; and the infinitude of this imaginary whole would, indeed, be unconditioned, but would be inconsistent (as everything in the phenomenal world is conditioned) with the unconditioned determination and finitude of quantities which is presupposed in our conception.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"87.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","text":"The apagogic mode of proof is the true source of those illusions which have always had so strong an attraction for the admirers of dogmatical philosophy. It may be compared to a champion who maintains the honour and claims of the party he has adopted by offering battle to all who doubt the validity of these claims and the purity of that honour; while nothing can be proved in this way, except the respective strength of the combatants, and the advantage, in this respect, is always on the side of the attacking party. Spectators, observing that each party is alternately conqueror and conquered, are led to regard the subject of dispute as beyond the power of man to decide upon. But such an opinion cannot be justified; and it is sufficient to apply to these reasoners the remark:\n\n_Non defensoribus istis  \nTempus eget._\n\nEach must try to establish his assertions by a transcendental deduction of the grounds of proof employed in his argument, and thus enable us to see in what way the claims of reason may be supported. If an opponent bases his assertions upon subjective grounds, he may be refuted with ease; not, however to the advantage of the dogmatist, who likewise depends upon subjective sources of cognition and is in like manner driven into a corner by his opponent. But, if parties employ the direct method of procedure, they will soon discover the difficulty, nay, the impossibility of proving their assertions, and will be forced to appeal to prescription and precedence; or they will, by the help of criticism, discover with ease the dogmatical illusions by which they had been mocked, and compel reason to renounce its exaggerated pretensions to speculative insight and to confine itself within the limits of its proper sphere—that of practical principles.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"87.9"}],"sha256":"4f0b03e8774a5b7964093018e331e0e07bfeb3117d9f8472c031c761f64a0c97","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP","title":"Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason","order":88,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_CICP","text":"### Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason\n\nIt is a humiliating consideration for human reason that it is incompetent to discover truth by means of pure speculation, but, on the contrary, stands in need of discipline to check its deviations from the straight path and to expose the illusions which it originates. But, on the other hand, this consideration ought to elevate and to give it confidence, for this discipline is exercised by itself alone, and it is subject to the censure of no other power. The bounds, moreover, which it is forced to set to its speculative exercise, form likewise a check upon the fallacious pretensions of opponents; and thus what remains of its possessions, after these exaggerated claims have been disallowed, is secure from attack or usurpation. The greatest, and perhaps the only, use of all philosophy of pure reason is, accordingly, of a purely negative character. It is not an organon for the extension, but a discipline for the determination, of the limits of its exercise; and without laying claim to the discovery of new truth, it has the modest merit of guarding against error.\n\nAt the same time, there must be some source of positive cognitions which belong to the domain of pure reason and which become the causes of error only from our mistaking their true character, while they form the goal towards which reason continually strives. How else can we account for the inextinguishable desire in the human mind to find a firm footing in some region beyond the limits of the world of experience? It hopes to attain to the possession of a knowledge in which it has the deepest interest. It enters upon the path of pure speculation; but in vain. We have some reason, however, to expect that, in the only other way that lies open to it—the path of practical reason—it may meet with better success.\n\nI understand by a canon a list of the à priori principles of the proper employment of certain faculties of cognition. Thus general logic, in its analytical department, is a formal canon for the faculties of understanding and reason. In the same way, Transcendental Analytic was seen to be a canon of the pure understanding; for it alone is competent to enounce true à priori synthetical cognitions. But, when no proper employment of a faculty of cognition is possible, no canon can exist. But the synthetical cognition of pure speculative reason is, as has been shown, completely impossible. There cannot, therefore, exist any canon for the speculative exercise of this faculty—for its speculative exercise is entirely dialectical; and, consequently, transcendental logic, in this respect, is merely a discipline, and not a canon. If, then, there is any proper mode of employing the faculty of pure reason—in which case there must be a canon for this faculty—this canon will relate, not to the speculative, but to the practical use of reason. This canon we now proceed to investigate.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","text":"### Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason\n\nIt is a humiliating consideration for human reason that it is incompetent to discover truth by means of pure speculation, but, on the contrary, stands in need of discipline to check its deviations from the straight path and to expose the illusions which it originates. But, on the other hand, this consideration ought to elevate and to give it confidence, for this discipline is exercised by itself alone, and it is subject to the censure of no other power. The bounds, moreover, which it is forced to set to its speculative exercise, form likewise a check upon the fallacious pretensions of opponents; and thus what remains of its possessions, after these exaggerated claims have been disallowed, is secure from attack or usurpation. The greatest, and perhaps the only, use of all philosophy of pure reason is, accordingly, of a purely negative character. It is not an organon for the extension, but a discipline for the determination, of the limits of its exercise; and without laying claim to the discovery of new truth, it has the modest merit of guarding against error.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"88.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","text":"At the same time, there must be some source of positive cognitions which belong to the domain of pure reason and which become the causes of error only from our mistaking their true character, while they form the goal towards which reason continually strives. How else can we account for the inextinguishable desire in the human mind to find a firm footing in some region beyond the limits of the world of experience? It hopes to attain to the possession of a knowledge in which it has the deepest interest. It enters upon the path of pure speculation; but in vain. We have some reason, however, to expect that, in the only other way that lies open to it—the path of practical reason—it may meet with better success.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"88.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","text":"I understand by a canon a list of the à priori principles of the proper employment of certain faculties of cognition. Thus general logic, in its analytical department, is a formal canon for the faculties of understanding and reason. In the same way, Transcendental Analytic was seen to be a canon of the pure understanding; for it alone is competent to enounce true à priori synthetical cognitions. But, when no proper employment of a faculty of cognition is possible, no canon can exist. But the synthetical cognition of pure speculative reason is, as has been shown, completely impossible. There cannot, therefore, exist any canon for the speculative exercise of this faculty—for its speculative exercise is entirely dialectical; and, consequently, transcendental logic, in this respect, is merely a discipline, and not a canon. If, then, there is any proper mode of employing the faculty of pure reason—in which case there must be a canon for this faculty—this canon will relate, not to the speculative, but to the practical use of reason. This canon we now proceed to investigate.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"88.3"}],"sha256":"b1e99aad47421a0a6b53a04b910e26c66a4373b5a2d4ef4ae51f280ed66d4750","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE","title":"Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason","order":89,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIUE","text":"### Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason\n\nThere exists in the faculty of reason a natural desire to venture beyond the field of experience, to attempt to reach the utmost bounds of all cognition by the help of ideas alone, and not to rest satisfied until it has fulfilled its course and raised the sum of its cognitions into a self-subsistent systematic whole. Is the motive for this endeavour to be found in its speculative, or in its practical interests alone?\n\nSetting aside, at present, the results of the labours of pure reason in its speculative exercise, I shall merely inquire regarding the problems the solution of which forms its ultimate aim, whether reached or not, and in relation to which all other aims are but partial and intermediate. These highest aims must, from the nature of reason, possess complete unity; otherwise the highest interest of humanity could not be successfully promoted.\n\nThe transcendental speculation of reason relates to three things: the freedom of the will, the immortality of the soul, and the existence of God. The speculative interest which reason has in those questions is very small; and, for its sake alone, we should not undertake the labour of transcendental investigation—a labour full of toil and ceaseless struggle. We should be loth to undertake this labour, because the discoveries we might make would not be of the smallest use in the sphere of concrete or physical investigation. We may find out that the will is free, but this knowledge only relates to the intelligible cause of our volition. As regards the phenomena or expressions of this will, that is, our actions, we are bound, in obedience to an inviolable maxim, without which reason cannot be employed in the sphere of experience, to explain these in the same way as we explain all the other phenomena of nature, that is to say, according to its unchangeable laws. We may have discovered the spirituality and immortality of the soul, but we cannot employ this knowledge to explain the phenomena of this life, nor the peculiar nature of the future, because our conception of an incorporeal nature is purely negative and does not add anything to our knowledge, and the only inferences to be drawn from it are purely fictitious. If, again, we prove the existence of a supreme intelligence, we should be able from it to make the conformity to aims existing in the arrangement of the world comprehensible; but we should not be justified in deducing from it any particular arrangement or disposition, or inferring any where it is not perceived. For it is a necessary rule of the speculative use of reason that we must not overlook natural causes, or refuse to listen to the teaching of experience, for the sake of deducing what we know and perceive from something that transcends all our knowledge. In one word, these three propositions are, for the speculative reason, always transcendent, and cannot be employed as immanent principles in relation to the objects of experience; they are, consequently, of no use to us in this sphere, being but the valueless results of the severe but unprofitable efforts of reason.\n\nIf, then, the actual cognition of these three cardinal propositions is perfectly useless, while Reason uses her utmost endeavours to induce us to admit them, it is plain that their real value and importance relate to our practical, and not to our speculative interest.\n\nI term all that is possible through free will, practical. But if the conditions of the exercise of free volition are empirical, reason can have only a regulative, and not a constitutive, influence upon it, and is serviceable merely for the introduction of unity into its empirical laws. In the moral philosophy of prudence, for example, the sole business of reason is to bring about a union of all the ends, which are aimed at by our inclinations, into one ultimate end—that of happiness—and to show the agreement which should exist among the means of attaining that end. In this sphere, accordingly, reason cannot present to us any other than pragmatical laws of free action, for our guidance towards the aims set up by the senses, and is incompetent to give us laws which are pure and determined completely à priori. On the other hand, pure practical laws, the ends of which have been given by reason entirely à priori, and which are not empirically conditioned, but are, on the contrary, absolutely imperative in their nature, would be products of pure reason. Such are the moral laws; and these alone belong to the sphere of the practical exercise of reason, and admit of a canon.\n\nAll the powers of reason, in the sphere of what may be termed pure philosophy, are, in fact, directed to the three above-mentioned problems alone. These again have a still higher end—the answer to the question, what we ought to do, if the will is free, if there is a God and a future world. Now, as this problem relates to our in reference to the highest aim of humanity, it is evident that the ultimate intention of nature, in the constitution of our reason, has been directed to the moral alone.\n\nWe must take care, however, in turning our attention to an object which is foreign[[78]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-78) to the sphere of transcendental philosophy, not to injure the unity of our system by digressions, nor, on the other hand, to fail in clearness, by saying too little on the new subject of discussion. I hope to avoid both extremes, by keeping as close as possible to the transcendental, and excluding all psychological, that is, empirical, elements.\n\n[[78]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-78) All practical conceptions relate to objects of pleasure and pain, and consequently—in an indirect manner, at least—to objects of feeling. But as feeling is not a faculty of representation, but lies out of the sphere of our powers of cognition, the elements of our judgements, in so far as they relate to pleasure or pain, that is, the elements of our practical judgements, do not belong to transcendental philosophy, which has to do with pure à priori cognitions alone.\n\nI have to remark, in the first place, that at present I treat of the conception of freedom in the practical sense only, and set aside the corresponding transcendental conception, which cannot be employed as a ground of explanation in the phenomenal world, but is itself a problem for pure reason. A will is purely animal (arbitrium brutum) when it is determined by sensuous impulses or instincts only, that is, when it is determined in a pathological manner. A will, which can be determined independently of sensuous impulses, consequently by motives presented by reason alone, is called a free will (arbitrium liberum); and everything which is connected with this free will, either as principle or consequence, is termed practical. The existence of practical freedom can be proved from experience alone. For the human will is not determined by that alone which immediately affects the senses; on the contrary, we have the power, by calling up the notion of what is useful or hurtful in a more distant relation, of overcoming the immediate impressions on our sensuous faculty of desire. But these considerations of what is desirable in relation to our whole state, that is, is in the end good and useful, are based entirely upon reason. This faculty, accordingly, enounces laws, which are imperative or objective laws of freedom and which tell us what ought to take place, thus distinguishing themselves from the laws of nature, which relate to that which does take place. The laws of freedom or of free will are hence termed practical laws.\n\nWhether reason is not itself, in the actual delivery of these laws, determined in its turn by other influences, and whether the action which, in relation to sensuous impulses, we call free, may not, in relation to higher and more remote operative causes, really form a part of nature—these are questions which do not here concern us. They are purely speculative questions; and all we have to do, in the practical sphere, is to inquire into the rule of conduct which reason has to present. Experience demonstrates to us the existence of practical freedom as one of the causes which exist in nature, that is, it shows the causal power of reason in the determination of the will. The idea of transcendental freedom, on the contrary, requires that reason—in relation to its causal power of commencing a series of phenomena—should be independent of all sensuous determining causes; and thus it seems to be in opposition to the law of nature and to all possible experience. It therefore remains a problem for the human mind. But this problem does not concern reason in its practical use; and we have, therefore, in a canon of pure reason, to do with only two questions, which relate to the practical interest of pure reason: Is there a God? and, Is there a future life? The question of transcendental freedom is purely speculative, and we may therefore set it entirely aside when we come to treat of practical reason. Besides, we have already discussed this subject in the antinomy of pure reason.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0001","text":"### Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason\n\nThere exists in the faculty of reason a natural desire to venture beyond the field of experience, to attempt to reach the utmost bounds of all cognition by the help of ideas alone, and not to rest satisfied until it has fulfilled its course and raised the sum of its cognitions into a self-subsistent systematic whole. Is the motive for this endeavour to be found in its speculative, or in its practical interests alone?\n\nSetting aside, at present, the results of the labours of pure reason in its speculative exercise, I shall merely inquire regarding the problems the solution of which forms its ultimate aim, whether reached or not, and in relation to which all other aims are but partial and intermediate. These highest aims must, from the nature of reason, possess complete unity; otherwise the highest interest of humanity could not be successfully promoted.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"89.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","text":"The transcendental speculation of reason relates to three things: the freedom of the will, the immortality of the soul, and the existence of God. The speculative interest which reason has in those questions is very small; and, for its sake alone, we should not undertake the labour of transcendental investigation—a labour full of toil and ceaseless struggle. We should be loth to undertake this labour, because the discoveries we might make would not be of the smallest use in the sphere of concrete or physical investigation. We may find out that the will is free, but this knowledge only relates to the intelligible cause of our volition. As regards the phenomena or expressions of this will, that is, our actions, we are bound, in obedience to an inviolable maxim, without which reason cannot be employed in the sphere of experience, to explain these in the same way as we explain all the other phenomena of nature, that is to say, according to its unchangeable laws. We may have discovered the spirituality and immortality of the soul, but we cannot employ this knowledge to explain the phenomena of this life, nor the peculiar nature of the future, because our conception of an incorporeal nature is purely negative and does not add anything to our knowledge, and the only inferences to be drawn from it are purely fictitious. If, again, we prove the existence of a supreme intelligence, we should be able from it to make the conformity to aims existing in the arrangement of the world comprehensible; but we should not be justified in deducing from it any particular arrangement or disposition, or inferring any where it is not perceived. For it is a necessary rule of the speculative use of reason that we must not overlook natural causes, or refuse to listen to the teaching of experience, for the sake of deducing what we know and perceive from something that transcends all our knowledge. In one word, these three propositions are, for the speculative reason, always transcendent, and cannot be employed as immanent principles in relation to the objects of experience; they are, consequently, of no use to us in this sphere, being but the valueless results of the severe but unprofitable efforts of reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"89.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","text":"If, then, the actual cognition of these three cardinal propositions is perfectly useless, while Reason uses her utmost endeavours to induce us to admit them, it is plain that their real value and importance relate to our practical, and not to our speculative interest.\n\nI term all that is possible through free will, practical. But if the conditions of the exercise of free volition are empirical, reason can have only a regulative, and not a constitutive, influence upon it, and is serviceable merely for the introduction of unity into its empirical laws. In the moral philosophy of prudence, for example, the sole business of reason is to bring about a union of all the ends, which are aimed at by our inclinations, into one ultimate end—that of happiness—and to show the agreement which should exist among the means of attaining that end. In this sphere, accordingly, reason cannot present to us any other than pragmatical laws of free action, for our guidance towards the aims set up by the senses, and is incompetent to give us laws which are pure and determined completely à priori. On the other hand, pure practical laws, the ends of which have been given by reason entirely à priori, and which are not empirically conditioned, but are, on the contrary, absolutely imperative in their nature, would be products of pure reason. Such are the moral laws; and these alone belong to the sphere of the practical exercise of reason, and admit of a canon.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"89.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","text":"All the powers of reason, in the sphere of what may be termed pure philosophy, are, in fact, directed to the three above-mentioned problems alone. These again have a still higher end—the answer to the question, what we ought to do, if the will is free, if there is a God and a future world. Now, as this problem relates to our in reference to the highest aim of humanity, it is evident that the ultimate intention of nature, in the constitution of our reason, has been directed to the moral alone.\n\nWe must take care, however, in turning our attention to an object which is foreign[[78]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-78) to the sphere of transcendental philosophy, not to injure the unity of our system by digressions, nor, on the other hand, to fail in clearness, by saying too little on the new subject of discussion. I hope to avoid both extremes, by keeping as close as possible to the transcendental, and excluding all psychological, that is, empirical, elements.\n\n[[78]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-78) All practical conceptions relate to objects of pleasure and pain, and consequently—in an indirect manner, at least—to objects of feeling. But as feeling is not a faculty of representation, but lies out of the sphere of our powers of cognition, the elements of our judgements, in so far as they relate to pleasure or pain, that is, the elements of our practical judgements, do not belong to transcendental philosophy, which has to do with pure à priori cognitions alone.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"89.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","text":"I have to remark, in the first place, that at present I treat of the conception of freedom in the practical sense only, and set aside the corresponding transcendental conception, which cannot be employed as a ground of explanation in the phenomenal world, but is itself a problem for pure reason. A will is purely animal (arbitrium brutum) when it is determined by sensuous impulses or instincts only, that is, when it is determined in a pathological manner. A will, which can be determined independently of sensuous impulses, consequently by motives presented by reason alone, is called a free will (arbitrium liberum); and everything which is connected with this free will, either as principle or consequence, is termed practical. The existence of practical freedom can be proved from experience alone. For the human will is not determined by that alone which immediately affects the senses; on the contrary, we have the power, by calling up the notion of what is useful or hurtful in a more distant relation, of overcoming the immediate impressions on our sensuous faculty of desire. But these considerations of what is desirable in relation to our whole state, that is, is in the end good and useful, are based entirely upon reason. This faculty, accordingly, enounces laws, which are imperative or objective laws of freedom and which tell us what ought to take place, thus distinguishing themselves from the laws of nature, which relate to that which does take place. The laws of freedom or of free will are hence termed practical laws.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"89.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","text":"Whether reason is not itself, in the actual delivery of these laws, determined in its turn by other influences, and whether the action which, in relation to sensuous impulses, we call free, may not, in relation to higher and more remote operative causes, really form a part of nature—these are questions which do not here concern us. They are purely speculative questions; and all we have to do, in the practical sphere, is to inquire into the rule of conduct which reason has to present. Experience demonstrates to us the existence of practical freedom as one of the causes which exist in nature, that is, it shows the causal power of reason in the determination of the will. The idea of transcendental freedom, on the contrary, requires that reason—in relation to its causal power of commencing a series of phenomena—should be independent of all sensuous determining causes; and thus it seems to be in opposition to the law of nature and to all possible experience. It therefore remains a problem for the human mind. But this problem does not concern reason in its practical use; and we have, therefore, in a canon of pure reason, to do with only two questions, which relate to the practical interest of pure reason: Is there a God? and, Is there a future life? The question of transcendental freedom is purely speculative, and we may therefore set it entirely aside when we come to treat of practical reason. Besides, we have already discussed this subject in the antinomy of pure reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"89.6"}],"sha256":"5427f563f592c1b63bf533ebe40ee89d25b400e89439ce54242384ed7597c2b1","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS","title":"Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason","order":90,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIIS","text":"### Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason\n\nReason conducted us, in its speculative use, through the field of experience and, as it can never find complete satisfaction in that sphere, from thence to speculative ideas—which, however, in the end brought us back again to experience, and thus fulfilled the purpose of reason, in a manner which, though useful, was not at all in accordance with our expectations. It now remains for us to consider whether pure reason can be employed in a practical sphere, and whether it will here conduct us to those ideas which attain the highest ends of pure reason, as we have just stated them. We shall thus ascertain whether, from the point of view of its practical interest, reason may not be able to supply us with that which, on the speculative side, it wholly denies us.\n\nThe whole interest of reason, speculative as well as practical, is centred in the three following questions:\n\n1. WHAT CAN I KNOW?  \n2. WHAT OUGHT I TO DO?  \n3. WHAT MAY I HOPE?  \n\nThe first question is purely speculative. We have, as I flatter myself, exhausted all the replies of which it is susceptible, and have at last found the reply with which reason must content itself, and with which it ought to be content, so long as it pays no regard to the practical. But from the two great ends to the attainment of which all these efforts of pure reason were in fact directed, we remain just as far removed as if we had consulted our ease and declined the task at the outset. So far, then, as knowledge is concerned, thus much, at least, is established, that, in regard to those two problems, it lies beyond our reach.\n\nThe second question is purely practical. As such it may indeed fall within the province of pure reason, but still it is not transcendental, but moral, and consequently cannot in itself form the subject of our criticism.\n\nThe third question: If I act as I ought to do, what may I then hope?—is at once practical and theoretical. The practical forms a clue to the answer of the theoretical, and—in its highest form—speculative question. For all hoping has happiness for its object and stands in precisely the same relation to the practical and the law of morality as knowing to the theoretical cognition of things and the law of nature. The former arrives finally at the conclusion that something is (which determines the ultimate end), because something ought to take place; the latter, that something is (which operates as the highest cause), because something does take place.\n\nHappiness is the satisfaction of all our desires; extensive, in regard to their multiplicity; intensive, in regard to their degree; and protensive, in regard to their duration. The practical law based on the motive of happiness I term a pragmatical law (or prudential rule); but that law, assuming such to exist, which has no other motive than the worthiness of being happy, I term a moral or ethical law. The first tells us what we have to do, if we wish to become possessed of happiness; the second dictates how we ought to act, in order to deserve happiness. The first is based upon empirical principles; for it is only by experience that I can learn either what inclinations exist which desire satisfaction, or what are the natural means of satisfying them. The second takes no account of our desires or the means of satisfying them, and regards only the freedom of a rational being, and the necessary conditions under which alone this freedom can harmonize with the distribution of happiness according to principles. This second law may therefore rest upon mere ideas of pure reason, and may be cognized à priori.\n\nI assume that there are pure moral laws which determine, entirely à priori (without regard to empirical motives, that is, to happiness), the conduct of a rational being, or in other words, to use which it makes of its freedom, and that these laws are absolutely imperative (not merely hypothetically, on the supposition of other empirical ends), and therefore in all respects necessary. I am warranted in assuming this, not only by the arguments of the most enlightened moralists, but by the moral judgement of every man who will make the attempt to form a distinct conception of such a law.\n\nPure reason, then, contains, not indeed in its speculative, but in its practical, or, more strictly, its moral use, principles of the possibility of experience, of such actions, namely, as, in accordance with ethical precepts, might be met with in the history of man. For since reason commands that such actions should take place, it must be possible for them to take place; and hence a particular kind of systematic unity—the moral—must be possible. We have found, it is true, that the systematic unity of nature could not be established according to speculative principles of reason, because, while reason possesses a causal power in relation to freedom, it has none in relation to the whole sphere of nature; and, while moral principles of reason can produce free actions, they cannot produce natural laws. It is, then, in its practical, but especially in its moral use, that the principles of pure reason possess objective reality.\n\nI call the world a moral world, in so far as it may be in accordance with all the ethical laws—which, by virtue of the freedom of reasonable beings, it can be, and according to the necessary laws of morality it ought to be. But this world must be conceived only as an intelligible world, inasmuch as abstraction is therein made of all conditions (ends), and even of all impediments to morality (the weakness or pravity of human nature). So far, then, it is a mere idea—though still a practical idea—which may have, and ought to have, an influence on the world of sense, so as to bring it as far as possible into conformity with itself. The idea of a moral world has, therefore, objective reality, not as referring to an object of intelligible intuition—for of such an object we can form no conception whatever—but to the world of sense—conceived, however, as an object of pure reason in its practical use—and to a corpus mysticum of rational beings in it, in so far as the liberum arbitrium of the individual is placed, under and by virtue of moral laws, in complete systematic unity both with itself and with the freedom of all others.\n\nThat is the answer to the first of the two questions of pure reason which relate to its practical interest: Do that which will render thee worthy of happiness. The second question is this: If I conduct myself so as not to be unworthy of happiness, may I hope thereby to obtain happiness? In order to arrive at the solution of this question, we must inquire whether the principles of pure reason, which prescribe à priori the law, necessarily also connect this hope with it.\n\nI say, then, that just as the moral principles are necessary according to reason in its practical use, so it is equally necessary according to reason in its theoretical use to assume that every one has ground to hope for happiness in the measure in which he has made himself worthy of it in his conduct, and that therefore the system of morality is inseparably (though only in the idea of pure reason) connected with that of happiness.\n\nNow in an intelligible, that is, in the moral world, in the conception of which we make abstraction of all the impediments to morality (sensuous desires), such a system of happiness, connected with and proportioned to morality, may be conceived as necessary, because freedom of volition—partly incited, and partly restrained by moral laws—would be itself the cause of general happiness; and thus rational beings, under the guidance of such principles, would be themselves the authors both of their own enduring welfare and that of others. But such a system of self-rewarding morality is only an idea, the carrying out of which depends upon the condition that every one acts as he ought; in other words, that all actions of reasonable beings be such as they would be if they sprung from a Supreme Will, comprehending in, or under, itself all particular wills. But since the moral law is binding on each individual in the use of his freedom of volition, even if others should not act in conformity with this law, neither the nature of things, nor the causality of actions and their relation to morality, determine how the consequences of these actions will be related to happiness; and the necessary connection of the hope of happiness with the unceasing endeavour to become worthy of happiness, cannot be cognized by reason, if we take nature alone for our guide. This connection can be hoped for only on the assumption that the cause of nature is a supreme reason, which governs according to moral laws.\n\nI term the idea of an intelligence in which the morally most perfect will, united with supreme blessedness, is the cause of all happiness in the world, so far as happiness stands in strict relation to morality (as the worthiness of being happy), the ideal of the supreme Good. It is only, then, in the ideal of the supreme original good, that pure reason can find the ground of the practically necessary connection of both elements of the highest derivative good, and accordingly of an intelligible, that is, moral world. Now since we are necessitated by reason to conceive ourselves as belonging to such a world, while the senses present to us nothing but a world of phenomena, we must assume the former as a consequence of our conduct in the world of sense (since the world of sense gives us no hint of it), and therefore as future in relation to us. Thus God and a future life are two hypotheses which, according to the principles of pure reason, are inseparable from the obligation which this reason imposes upon us.\n\nMorality per se constitutes a system. But we can form no system of happiness, except in so far as it is dispensed in strict proportion to morality. But this is only possible in the intelligible world, under a wise author and ruler. Such a ruler, together with life in such a world, which we must look upon as future, reason finds itself compelled to assume; or it must regard the moral laws as idle dreams, since the necessary consequence which this same reason connects with them must, without this hypothesis, fall to the ground. Hence also the moral laws are universally regarded as commands, which they could not be did they not connect à priori adequate consequences with their dictates, and thus carry with them promises and threats. But this, again, they could not do, did they not reside in a necessary being, as the Supreme Good, which alone can render such a teleological unity possible.\n\nLeibnitz termed the world, when viewed in relation to the rational beings which it contains, and the moral relations in which they stand to each other, under the government of the Supreme Good, the kingdom of Grace, and distinguished it from the kingdom of Nature, in which these rational beings live, under moral laws, indeed, but expect no other consequences from their actions than such as follow according to the course of nature in the world of sense. To view ourselves, therefore, as in the kingdom of grace, in which all happiness awaits us, except in so far as we ourselves limit our participation in it by actions which render us unworthy of happiness, is a practically necessary idea of reason.\n\nPractical laws, in so far as they are subjective grounds of actions, that is, subjective principles, are termed maxims. The judgements of moral according to in its purity and ultimate results are framed according ideas; the observance of its laws, according to maxims.\n\nThe whole course of our life must be subject to moral maxims; but this is impossible, unless with the moral law, which is a mere idea, reason connects an efficient cause which ordains to all conduct which is in conformity with the moral law an issue either in this or in another life, which is in exact conformity with our highest aims. Thus, without a God and without a world, invisible to us now, but hoped for, the glorious ideas of morality are, indeed, objects of approbation and of admiration, but cannot be the springs of purpose and action. For they do not satisfy all the aims which are natural to every rational being, and which are determined à priori by pure reason itself, and necessary.\n\nHappiness alone is, in the view of reason, far from being the complete good. Reason does not approve of it (however much inclination may desire it), except as united with desert. On the other hand, morality alone, and with it, mere desert, is likewise far from being the complete good. To make it complete, he who conducts himself in a manner not unworthy of happiness, must be able to hope for the possession of happiness. Even reason, unbiased by private ends, or interested considerations, cannot judge otherwise, if it puts itself in the place of a being whose business it is to dispense all happiness to others. For in the practical idea both points are essentially combined, though in such a way that participation in happiness is rendered possible by the moral disposition, as its condition, and not conversely, the moral disposition by the prospect of happiness. For a disposition which should require the prospect of happiness as its necessary condition would not be moral, and hence also would not be worthy of complete happiness—a happiness which, in the view of reason, recognizes no limitation but such as arises from our own immoral conduct.\n\nHappiness, therefore, in exact proportion with the morality of rational beings (whereby they are made worthy of happiness), constitutes alone the supreme good of a world into which we absolutely must transport ourselves according to the commands of pure but practical reason. This world is, it is true, only an intelligible world; for of such a systematic unity of ends as it requires, the world of sense gives us no hint. Its reality can be based on nothing else but the hypothesis of a supreme original good. In it independent reason, equipped with all the sufficiency of a supreme cause, founds, maintains, and fulfils the universal order of things, with the most perfect teleological harmony, however much this order may be hidden from us in the world of sense.\n\nThis moral theology has the peculiar advantage, in contrast with speculative theology, of leading inevitably to the conception of a sole, perfect, and rational First Cause, whereof speculative theology does not give us any indication on objective grounds, far less any convincing evidence. For we find neither in transcendental nor in natural theology, however far reason may lead us in these, any ground to warrant us in assuming the existence of one only Being, which stands at the head of all natural causes, and on which these are entirely dependent. On the other hand, if we take our stand on moral unity as a necessary law of the universe, and from this point of view consider what is necessary to give this law adequate efficiency and, for us, obligatory force, we must come to the conclusion that there is one only supreme will, which comprehends all these laws in itself. For how, under different wills, should we find complete unity of ends? This will must be omnipotent, that all nature and its relation to morality in the world may be subject to it; omniscient, that it may have knowledge of the most secret feelings and their moral worth; omnipresent, that it may be at hand to supply every necessity to which the highest weal of the world may give rise; eternal, that this harmony of nature and liberty may never fail; and so on.\n\nBut this systematic unity of ends in this world of intelligences—which, as mere nature, is only a world of sense, but, as a system of freedom of volition, may be termed an intelligible, that is, moral world (regnum gratiae)—leads inevitably also to the teleological unity of all things which constitute this great whole, according to universal natural laws—just as the unity of the former is according to universal and necessary moral laws—and unites the practical with the speculative reason. The world must be represented as having originated from an idea, if it is to harmonize with that use of reason without which we cannot even consider ourselves as worthy of reason—namely, the moral use, which rests entirely on the idea of the supreme good. Hence the investigation of nature receives a teleological direction, and becomes, in its widest extension, physico-theology. But this, taking its rise in moral order as a unity founded on the essence of freedom, and not accidentally instituted by external commands, establishes the teleological view of nature on grounds which must be inseparably connected with the internal possibility of things. This gives rise to a transcendental theology, which takes the ideal of the highest ontological perfection as a principle of systematic unity; and this principle connects all things according to universal and necessary natural laws, because all things have their origin in the absolute necessity of the one only Primal Being.\n\nWhat use can we make of our understanding, even in respect of experience, if we do not propose ends to ourselves? But the highest ends are those of morality, and it is only pure reason that can give us the knowledge of these. Though supplied with these, and putting ourselves under their guidance, we can make no teleological use of the knowledge of nature, as regards cognition, unless nature itself has established teleological unity. For without this unity we should not even possess reason, because we should have no school for reason, and no cultivation through objects which afford the materials for its conceptions. But teleological unity is a necessary unity, and founded on the essence of the individual will itself. Hence this will, which is the condition of the application of this unity in concreto, must be so likewise. In this way the transcendental enlargement of our rational cognition would be, not the cause, but merely the effect of the practical teleology which pure reason imposes upon us.\n\nHence, also, we find in the history of human reason that, before the moral conceptions were sufficiently purified and determined, and before men had attained to a perception of the systematic unity of ends according to these conceptions and from necessary principles, the knowledge of nature, and even a considerable amount of intellectual culture in many other sciences, could produce only rude and vague conceptions of the Deity, sometimes even admitting of an astonishing indifference with regard to this question altogether. But the more enlarged treatment of moral ideas, which was rendered necessary by the extreme pure moral law of our religion, awakened the interest, and thereby quickened the perceptions of reason in relation to this object. In this way, and without the help either of an extended acquaintance with nature, or of a reliable transcendental insight (for these have been wanting in all ages), a conception of the Divine Being was arrived at, which we now hold to be the correct one, not because speculative reason convinces us of its correctness, but because it accords with the moral principles of reason. Thus it is to pure reason, but only in its practical use, that we must ascribe the merit of having connected with our highest interest a cognition, of which mere speculation was able only to form a conjecture, but the validity of which it was unable to establish—and of having thereby rendered it, not indeed a demonstrated dogma, but a hypothesis absolutely necessary to the essential ends of reason.\n\nBut if practical reason has reached this elevation, and has attained to the conception of a sole Primal Being as the supreme good, it must not, therefore, imagine that it has transcended the empirical conditions of its application, and risen to the immediate cognition of new objects; it must not presume to start from the conception which it has gained, and to deduce from it the moral laws themselves. For it was these very laws, the internal practical necessity of which led us to the hypothesis of an independent cause, or of a wise ruler of the universe, who should give them effect. Hence we are not entitled to regard them as accidental and derived from the mere will of the ruler, especially as we have no conception of such a will, except as formed in accordance with these laws. So far, then, as practical reason has the right to conduct us, we shall not look upon actions as binding on us, because they are the commands of God, but we shall regard them as divine commands, because we are internally bound by them. We shall study freedom under the teleological unity which accords with principles of reason; we shall look upon ourselves as acting in conformity with the divine will only in so far as we hold sacred the moral law which reason teaches us from the nature of actions themselves, and we shall believe that we can obey that will only by promoting the weal of the universe in ourselves and in others. Moral theology is, therefore, only of immanent use. It teaches us to fulfil our destiny here in the world, by placing ourselves in harmony with the general system of ends, and warns us against the fanaticism, nay, the crime of depriving reason of its legislative authority in the moral conduct of life, for the purpose of directly connecting this authority with the idea of the Supreme Being. For this would be, not an immanent, but a transcendent use of moral theology, and, like the transcendent use of mere speculation, would inevitably pervert and frustrate the ultimate ends of reason.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0001","text":"### Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason\n\nReason conducted us, in its speculative use, through the field of experience and, as it can never find complete satisfaction in that sphere, from thence to speculative ideas—which, however, in the end brought us back again to experience, and thus fulfilled the purpose of reason, in a manner which, though useful, was not at all in accordance with our expectations. It now remains for us to consider whether pure reason can be employed in a practical sphere, and whether it will here conduct us to those ideas which attain the highest ends of pure reason, as we have just stated them. We shall thus ascertain whether, from the point of view of its practical interest, reason may not be able to supply us with that which, on the speculative side, it wholly denies us.\n\nThe whole interest of reason, speculative as well as practical, is centred in the three following questions:\n\n1. WHAT CAN I KNOW?  \n2. WHAT OUGHT I TO DO?  \n3. WHAT MAY I HOPE?  \n\nThe first question is purely speculative. We have, as I flatter myself, exhausted all the replies of which it is susceptible, and have at last found the reply with which reason must content itself, and with which it ought to be content, so long as it pays no regard to the practical. But from the two great ends to the attainment of which all these efforts of pure reason were in fact directed, we remain just as far removed as if we had consulted our ease and declined the task at the outset. So far, then, as knowledge is concerned, thus much, at least, is established, that, in regard to those two problems, it lies beyond our reach.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"90.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0002","text":"The second question is purely practical. As such it may indeed fall within the province of pure reason, but still it is not transcendental, but moral, and consequently cannot in itself form the subject of our criticism.\n\nThe third question: If I act as I ought to do, what may I then hope?—is at once practical and theoretical. The practical forms a clue to the answer of the theoretical, and—in its highest form—speculative question. For all hoping has happiness for its object and stands in precisely the same relation to the practical and the law of morality as knowing to the theoretical cognition of things and the law of nature. The former arrives finally at the conclusion that something is (which determines the ultimate end), because something ought to take place; the latter, that something is (which operates as the highest cause), because something does take place.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"90.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0003","text":"Happiness is the satisfaction of all our desires; extensive, in regard to their multiplicity; intensive, in regard to their degree; and protensive, in regard to their duration. The practical law based on the motive of happiness I term a pragmatical law (or prudential rule); but that law, assuming such to exist, which has no other motive than the worthiness of being happy, I term a moral or ethical law. The first tells us what we have to do, if we wish to become possessed of happiness; the second dictates how we ought to act, in order to deserve happiness. The first is based upon empirical principles; for it is only by experience that I can learn either what inclinations exist which desire satisfaction, or what are the natural means of satisfying them. The second takes no account of our desires or the means of satisfying them, and regards only the freedom of a rational being, and the necessary conditions under which alone this freedom can harmonize with the distribution of happiness according to principles. This second law may therefore rest upon mere ideas of pure reason, and may be cognized à priori.\n\nI assume that there are pure moral laws which determine, entirely à priori (without regard to empirical motives, that is, to happiness), the conduct of a rational being, or in other words, to use which it makes of its freedom, and that these laws are absolutely imperative (not merely hypothetically, on the supposition of other empirical ends), and therefore in all respects necessary. I am warranted in assuming this, not only by the arguments of the most enlightened moralists, but by the moral judgement of every man who will make the attempt to form a distinct conception of such a law.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"90.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0004","text":"Pure reason, then, contains, not indeed in its speculative, but in its practical, or, more strictly, its moral use, principles of the possibility of experience, of such actions, namely, as, in accordance with ethical precepts, might be met with in the history of man. For since reason commands that such actions should take place, it must be possible for them to take place; and hence a particular kind of systematic unity—the moral—must be possible. We have found, it is true, that the systematic unity of nature could not be established according to speculative principles of reason, because, while reason possesses a causal power in relation to freedom, it has none in relation to the whole sphere of nature; and, while moral principles of reason can produce free actions, they cannot produce natural laws. It is, then, in its practical, but especially in its moral use, that the principles of pure reason possess objective reality.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"90.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0005","text":"I call the world a moral world, in so far as it may be in accordance with all the ethical laws—which, by virtue of the freedom of reasonable beings, it can be, and according to the necessary laws of morality it ought to be. But this world must be conceived only as an intelligible world, inasmuch as abstraction is therein made of all conditions (ends), and even of all impediments to morality (the weakness or pravity of human nature). So far, then, it is a mere idea—though still a practical idea—which may have, and ought to have, an influence on the world of sense, so as to bring it as far as possible into conformity with itself. The idea of a moral world has, therefore, objective reality, not as referring to an object of intelligible intuition—for of such an object we can form no conception whatever—but to the world of sense—conceived, however, as an object of pure reason in its practical use—and to a corpus mysticum of rational beings in it, in so far as the liberum arbitrium of the individual is placed, under and by virtue of moral laws, in complete systematic unity both with itself and with the freedom of all others.\n\nThat is the answer to the first of the two questions of pure reason which relate to its practical interest: Do that which will render thee worthy of happiness. The second question is this: If I conduct myself so as not to be unworthy of happiness, may I hope thereby to obtain happiness? In order to arrive at the solution of this question, we must inquire whether the principles of pure reason, which prescribe à priori the law, necessarily also connect this hope with it.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"90.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","text":"I say, then, that just as the moral principles are necessary according to reason in its practical use, so it is equally necessary according to reason in its theoretical use to assume that every one has ground to hope for happiness in the measure in which he has made himself worthy of it in his conduct, and that therefore the system of morality is inseparably (though only in the idea of pure reason) connected with that of happiness.\n\nNow in an intelligible, that is, in the moral world, in the conception of which we make abstraction of all the impediments to morality (sensuous desires), such a system of happiness, connected with and proportioned to morality, may be conceived as necessary, because freedom of volition—partly incited, and partly restrained by moral laws—would be itself the cause of general happiness; and thus rational beings, under the guidance of such principles, would be themselves the authors both of their own enduring welfare and that of others. But such a system of self-rewarding morality is only an idea, the carrying out of which depends upon the condition that every one acts as he ought; in other words, that all actions of reasonable beings be such as they would be if they sprung from a Supreme Will, comprehending in, or under, itself all particular wills. But since the moral law is binding on each individual in the use of his freedom of volition, even if others should not act in conformity with this law, neither the nature of things, nor the causality of actions and their relation to morality, determine how the consequences of these actions will be related to happiness; and the necessary connection of the hope of happiness with the unceasing endeavour to become worthy of happiness, cannot be cognized by reason, if we take nature alone for our guide. This connection can be hoped for only on the assumption that the cause of nature is a supreme reason, which governs according to moral laws.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"90.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","text":"I term the idea of an intelligence in which the morally most perfect will, united with supreme blessedness, is the cause of all happiness in the world, so far as happiness stands in strict relation to morality (as the worthiness of being happy), the ideal of the supreme Good. It is only, then, in the ideal of the supreme original good, that pure reason can find the ground of the practically necessary connection of both elements of the highest derivative good, and accordingly of an intelligible, that is, moral world. Now since we are necessitated by reason to conceive ourselves as belonging to such a world, while the senses present to us nothing but a world of phenomena, we must assume the former as a consequence of our conduct in the world of sense (since the world of sense gives us no hint of it), and therefore as future in relation to us. Thus God and a future life are two hypotheses which, according to the principles of pure reason, are inseparable from the obligation which this reason imposes upon us.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"90.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0008","text":"Morality per se constitutes a system. But we can form no system of happiness, except in so far as it is dispensed in strict proportion to morality. But this is only possible in the intelligible world, under a wise author and ruler. Such a ruler, together with life in such a world, which we must look upon as future, reason finds itself compelled to assume; or it must regard the moral laws as idle dreams, since the necessary consequence which this same reason connects with them must, without this hypothesis, fall to the ground. Hence also the moral laws are universally regarded as commands, which they could not be did they not connect à priori adequate consequences with their dictates, and thus carry with them promises and threats. But this, again, they could not do, did they not reside in a necessary being, as the Supreme Good, which alone can render such a teleological unity possible.\n\nLeibnitz termed the world, when viewed in relation to the rational beings which it contains, and the moral relations in which they stand to each other, under the government of the Supreme Good, the kingdom of Grace, and distinguished it from the kingdom of Nature, in which these rational beings live, under moral laws, indeed, but expect no other consequences from their actions than such as follow according to the course of nature in the world of sense. To view ourselves, therefore, as in the kingdom of grace, in which all happiness awaits us, except in so far as we ourselves limit our participation in it by actions which render us unworthy of happiness, is a practically necessary idea of reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"90.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0009","text":"Practical laws, in so far as they are subjective grounds of actions, that is, subjective principles, are termed maxims. The judgements of moral according to in its purity and ultimate results are framed according ideas; the observance of its laws, according to maxims.\n\nThe whole course of our life must be subject to moral maxims; but this is impossible, unless with the moral law, which is a mere idea, reason connects an efficient cause which ordains to all conduct which is in conformity with the moral law an issue either in this or in another life, which is in exact conformity with our highest aims. Thus, without a God and without a world, invisible to us now, but hoped for, the glorious ideas of morality are, indeed, objects of approbation and of admiration, but cannot be the springs of purpose and action. For they do not satisfy all the aims which are natural to every rational being, and which are determined à priori by pure reason itself, and necessary.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"90.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0010","text":"Happiness alone is, in the view of reason, far from being the complete good. Reason does not approve of it (however much inclination may desire it), except as united with desert. On the other hand, morality alone, and with it, mere desert, is likewise far from being the complete good. To make it complete, he who conducts himself in a manner not unworthy of happiness, must be able to hope for the possession of happiness. Even reason, unbiased by private ends, or interested considerations, cannot judge otherwise, if it puts itself in the place of a being whose business it is to dispense all happiness to others. For in the practical idea both points are essentially combined, though in such a way that participation in happiness is rendered possible by the moral disposition, as its condition, and not conversely, the moral disposition by the prospect of happiness. For a disposition which should require the prospect of happiness as its necessary condition would not be moral, and hence also would not be worthy of complete happiness—a happiness which, in the view of reason, recognizes no limitation but such as arises from our own immoral conduct.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"90.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0011","text":"Happiness, therefore, in exact proportion with the morality of rational beings (whereby they are made worthy of happiness), constitutes alone the supreme good of a world into which we absolutely must transport ourselves according to the commands of pure but practical reason. This world is, it is true, only an intelligible world; for of such a systematic unity of ends as it requires, the world of sense gives us no hint. Its reality can be based on nothing else but the hypothesis of a supreme original good. In it independent reason, equipped with all the sufficiency of a supreme cause, founds, maintains, and fulfils the universal order of things, with the most perfect teleological harmony, however much this order may be hidden from us in the world of sense.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"90.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0012","text":"This moral theology has the peculiar advantage, in contrast with speculative theology, of leading inevitably to the conception of a sole, perfect, and rational First Cause, whereof speculative theology does not give us any indication on objective grounds, far less any convincing evidence. For we find neither in transcendental nor in natural theology, however far reason may lead us in these, any ground to warrant us in assuming the existence of one only Being, which stands at the head of all natural causes, and on which these are entirely dependent. On the other hand, if we take our stand on moral unity as a necessary law of the universe, and from this point of view consider what is necessary to give this law adequate efficiency and, for us, obligatory force, we must come to the conclusion that there is one only supreme will, which comprehends all these laws in itself. For how, under different wills, should we find complete unity of ends? This will must be omnipotent, that all nature and its relation to morality in the world may be subject to it; omniscient, that it may have knowledge of the most secret feelings and their moral worth; omnipresent, that it may be at hand to supply every necessity to which the highest weal of the world may give rise; eternal, that this harmony of nature and liberty may never fail; and so on.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"90.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0013","text":"But this systematic unity of ends in this world of intelligences—which, as mere nature, is only a world of sense, but, as a system of freedom of volition, may be termed an intelligible, that is, moral world (regnum gratiae)—leads inevitably also to the teleological unity of all things which constitute this great whole, according to universal natural laws—just as the unity of the former is according to universal and necessary moral laws—and unites the practical with the speculative reason. The world must be represented as having originated from an idea, if it is to harmonize with that use of reason without which we cannot even consider ourselves as worthy of reason—namely, the moral use, which rests entirely on the idea of the supreme good. Hence the investigation of nature receives a teleological direction, and becomes, in its widest extension, physico-theology. But this, taking its rise in moral order as a unity founded on the essence of freedom, and not accidentally instituted by external commands, establishes the teleological view of nature on grounds which must be inseparably connected with the internal possibility of things. This gives rise to a transcendental theology, which takes the ideal of the highest ontological perfection as a principle of systematic unity; and this principle connects all things according to universal and necessary natural laws, because all things have their origin in the absolute necessity of the one only Primal Being.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"90.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0014","text":"What use can we make of our understanding, even in respect of experience, if we do not propose ends to ourselves? But the highest ends are those of morality, and it is only pure reason that can give us the knowledge of these. Though supplied with these, and putting ourselves under their guidance, we can make no teleological use of the knowledge of nature, as regards cognition, unless nature itself has established teleological unity. For without this unity we should not even possess reason, because we should have no school for reason, and no cultivation through objects which afford the materials for its conceptions. But teleological unity is a necessary unity, and founded on the essence of the individual will itself. Hence this will, which is the condition of the application of this unity in concreto, must be so likewise. In this way the transcendental enlargement of our rational cognition would be, not the cause, but merely the effect of the practical teleology which pure reason imposes upon us.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"90.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0015","text":"Hence, also, we find in the history of human reason that, before the moral conceptions were sufficiently purified and determined, and before men had attained to a perception of the systematic unity of ends according to these conceptions and from necessary principles, the knowledge of nature, and even a considerable amount of intellectual culture in many other sciences, could produce only rude and vague conceptions of the Deity, sometimes even admitting of an astonishing indifference with regard to this question altogether. But the more enlarged treatment of moral ideas, which was rendered necessary by the extreme pure moral law of our religion, awakened the interest, and thereby quickened the perceptions of reason in relation to this object. In this way, and without the help either of an extended acquaintance with nature, or of a reliable transcendental insight (for these have been wanting in all ages), a conception of the Divine Being was arrived at, which we now hold to be the correct one, not because speculative reason convinces us of its correctness, but because it accords with the moral principles of reason. Thus it is to pure reason, but only in its practical use, that we must ascribe the merit of having connected with our highest interest a cognition, of which mere speculation was able only to form a conjecture, but the validity of which it was unable to establish—and of having thereby rendered it, not indeed a demonstrated dogma, but a hypothesis absolutely necessary to the essential ends of reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"90.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","text":"But if practical reason has reached this elevation, and has attained to the conception of a sole Primal Being as the supreme good, it must not, therefore, imagine that it has transcended the empirical conditions of its application, and risen to the immediate cognition of new objects; it must not presume to start from the conception which it has gained, and to deduce from it the moral laws themselves. For it was these very laws, the internal practical necessity of which led us to the hypothesis of an independent cause, or of a wise ruler of the universe, who should give them effect. Hence we are not entitled to regard them as accidental and derived from the mere will of the ruler, especially as we have no conception of such a will, except as formed in accordance with these laws. So far, then, as practical reason has the right to conduct us, we shall not look upon actions as binding on us, because they are the commands of God, but we shall regard them as divine commands, because we are internally bound by them. We shall study freedom under the teleological unity which accords with principles of reason; we shall look upon ourselves as acting in conformity with the divine will only in so far as we hold sacred the moral law which reason teaches us from the nature of actions themselves, and we shall believe that we can obey that will only by promoting the weal of the universe in ourselves and in others. Moral theology is, therefore, only of immanent use. It teaches us to fulfil our destiny here in the world, by placing ourselves in harmony with the general system of ends, and warns us against the fanaticism, nay, the crime of depriving reason of its legislative authority in the moral conduct of life, for the purpose of directly connecting this authority with the idea of the Supreme Being. For this would be, not an immanent, but a transcendent use of moral theology, and, like the transcendent use of mere speculation, would inevitably pervert and frustrate the ultimate ends of reason.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"90.16"}],"sha256":"4b26924e1323b1499dbabe9c61f411f0232068cf9832044f7aea158a10ffd920","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK","title":"Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief","order":91,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_SIOK","text":"### Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief\n\nThe holding of a thing to be true is a phenomenon in our understanding which may rest on objective grounds, but requires, also, subjective causes in the mind of the person judging. If a judgement is valid for every rational being, then its ground is objectively sufficient, and it is termed a conviction. If, on the other hand, it has its ground in the particular character of the subject, it is termed a persuasion.\n\nPersuasion is a mere illusion, the ground of the judgement, which lies solely in the subject, being regarded as objective. Hence a judgement of this kind has only private validity—is only valid for the individual who judges, and the holding of a thing to be true in this way cannot be communicated. But truth depends upon agreement with the object, and consequently the judgements of all understandings, if true, must be in agreement with each other (consentientia uni tertio consentiunt inter se). Conviction may, therefore, be distinguished, from an external point of view, from persuasion, by the possibility of communicating it and by showing its validity for the reason of every man; for in this case the presumption, at least, arises that the agreement of all judgements with each other, in spite of the different characters of individuals, rests upon the common ground of the agreement of each with the object, and thus the correctness of the judgement is established.\n\nPersuasion, accordingly, cannot be subjectively distinguished from conviction, that is, so long as the subject views its judgement simply as a phenomenon of its own mind. But if we inquire whether the grounds of our judgement, which are valid for us, produce the same effect on the reason of others as on our own, we have then the means, though only subjective means, not, indeed, of producing conviction, but of detecting the merely private validity of the judgement; in other words, of discovering that there is in it the element of mere persuasion.\n\nIf we can, in addition to this, develop the subjective causes of the judgement, which we have taken for its objective grounds, and thus explain the deceptive judgement as a phenomenon in our mind, apart altogether from the objective character of the object, we can then expose the illusion and need be no longer deceived by it, although, if its subjective cause lies in our nature, we cannot hope altogether to escape its influence.\n\nI can only maintain, that is, affirm as necessarily valid for every one, that which produces conviction. Persuasion I may keep for myself, if it is agreeable to me; but I cannot, and ought not, to attempt to impose it as binding upon others.\n\nHolding for true, or the subjective validity of a judgement in relation to conviction (which is, at the same time, objectively valid), has the three following degrees: opinion, belief, and knowledge. Opinion is a consciously insufficient judgement, subjectively as well as objectively. Belief is subjectively sufficient, but is recognized as being objectively insufficient. Knowledge is both subjectively and objectively sufficient. Subjective sufficiency is termed conviction (for myself); objective sufficiency is termed certainty (for all). I need not dwell longer on the explanation of such simple conceptions.\n\nI must never venture to be of opinion, without knowing something, at least, by which my judgement, in itself merely problematical, is brought into connection with the truth—which connection, although not perfect, is still something more than an arbitrary fiction. Moreover, the law of such a connection must be certain. For if, in relation to this law, I have nothing more than opinion, my judgement is but a play of the imagination, without the least relation to truth. In the judgements of pure reason, opinion has no place. For, as they do not rest on empirical grounds and as the sphere of pure reason is that of necessary truth and à priori cognition, the principle of connection in it requires universality and necessity, and consequently perfect certainty—otherwise we should have no guide to the truth at all. Hence it is absurd to have an opinion in pure mathematics; we must know, or abstain from forming a judgement altogether. The case is the same with the maxims of morality. For we must not hazard an action on the mere opinion that it is allowed, but we must know it to be so. In the transcendental sphere of reason, on the other hand, the term opinion is too weak, while the word knowledge is too strong. From the merely speculative point of view, therefore, we cannot form a judgement at all. For the subjective grounds of a judgement, such as produce belief, cannot be admitted in speculative inquiries, inasmuch as they cannot stand without empirical support and are incapable of being communicated to others in equal measure.\n\nBut it is only from the practical point of view that a theoretically insufficient judgement can be termed belief. Now the practical reference is either to skill or to morality; to the former, when the end proposed is arbitrary and accidental, to the latter, when it is absolutely necessary.\n\nIf we propose to ourselves any end whatever, the conditions of its attainment are hypothetically necessary. The necessity is subjectively, but still only comparatively, sufficient, if I am acquainted with no other conditions under which the end can be attained. On the other hand, it is sufficient, absolutely and for every one, if I know for certain that no one can be acquainted with any other conditions under which the attainment of the proposed end would be possible. In the former case my supposition—my judgement with regard to certain conditions—is a merely accidental belief; in the latter it is a necessary belief. The physician must pursue some course in the case of a patient who is in danger, but is ignorant of the nature of the disease. He observes the symptoms, and concludes, according to the best of his judgement, that it is a case of phthisis. His belief is, even in his own judgement, only contingent: another man might, perhaps come nearer the truth. Such a belief, contingent indeed, but still forming the ground of the actual use of means for the attainment of certain ends, I term Pragmatical belief.\n\nThe usual test, whether that which any one maintains is merely his persuasion, or his subjective conviction at least, that is, his firm belief, is a bet. It frequently happens that a man delivers his opinions with so much boldness and assurance, that he appears to be under no apprehension as to the possibility of his being in error. The offer of a bet startles him, and makes him pause. Sometimes it turns out that his persuasion may be valued at a ducat, but not at ten. For he does not hesitate, perhaps, to venture a ducat, but if it is proposed to stake ten, he immediately becomes aware of the possibility of his being mistaken—a possibility which has hitherto escaped his observation. If we imagine to ourselves that we have to stake the happiness of our whole life on the truth of any proposition, our judgement drops its air of triumph, we take the alarm, and discover the actual strength of our belief. Thus pragmatical belief has degrees, varying in proportion to the interests at stake.\n\nNow, in cases where we cannot enter upon any course of action in reference to some object, and where, accordingly, our judgement is purely theoretical, we can still represent to ourselves, in thought, the possibility of a course of action, for which we suppose that we have sufficient grounds, if any means existed of ascertaining the truth of the matter. Thus we find in purely theoretical judgements an analogon of practical judgements, to which the word belief may properly be applied, and which we may term doctrinal belief. I should not hesitate to stake my all on the truth of the proposition—if there were any possibility of bringing it to the test of experience—that, at least, some one of the planets, which we see, is inhabited. Hence I say that I have not merely the opinion, but the strong belief, on the correctness of which I would stake even many of the advantages of life, that there are inhabitants in other worlds.\n\nNow we must admit that the doctrine of the existence of God belongs to doctrinal belief. For, although in respect to the theoretical cognition of the universe I do not require to form any theory which necessarily involves this idea, as the condition of my explanation of the phenomena which the universe presents, but, on the contrary, am rather bound so to use my reason as if everything were mere nature, still teleological unity is so important a condition of the application of my reason to nature, that it is impossible for me to ignore it—especially since, in addition to these considerations, abundant examples of it are supplied by experience. But the sole condition, so far as my knowledge extends, under which this unity can be my guide in the investigation of nature, is the assumption that a supreme intelligence has ordered all things according to the wisest ends. Consequently, the hypothesis of a wise author of the universe is necessary for my guidance in the investigation of nature—is the condition under which alone I can fulfil an end which is contingent indeed, but by no means unimportant. Moreover, since the result of my attempts so frequently confirms the utility of this assumption, and since nothing decisive can be adduced against it, it follows that it would be saying far too little to term my judgement, in this case, a mere opinion, and that, even in this theoretical connection, I may assert that I firmly believe in God. Still, if we use words strictly, this must not be called a practical, but a doctrinal belief, which the theology of nature (physico-theology) must also produce in my mind. In the wisdom of a Supreme Being, and in the shortness of life, so inadequate to the development of the glorious powers of human nature, we may find equally sufficient grounds for a doctrinal belief in the future life of the human soul.\n\nThe expression of belief is, in such cases, an expression of modesty from the objective point of view, but, at the same time, of firm confidence, from the subjective. If I should venture to term this merely theoretical judgement even so much as a hypothesis which I am entitled to assume; a more complete conception, with regard to another world and to the cause of the world, might then be justly required of me than I am, in reality, able to give. For, if I assume anything, even as a mere hypothesis, I must, at least, know so much of the properties of such a being as will enable me, not to form the conception, but to imagine the existence of it. But the word belief refers only to the guidance which an idea gives me, and to its subjective influence on the conduct of my reason, which forces me to hold it fast, though I may not be in a position to give a speculative account of it.\n\nBut mere doctrinal belief is, to some extent, wanting in stability. We often quit our hold of it, in consequence of the difficulties which occur in speculation, though in the end we inevitably return to it again.\n\nIt is quite otherwise with moral belief. For in this sphere action is absolutely necessary, that is, I must act in obedience to the moral law in all points. The end is here incontrovertibly established, and there is only one condition possible, according to the best of my perception, under which this end can harmonize with all other ends, and so have practical validity—namely, the existence of a God and of a future world. I know also, to a certainty, that no one can be acquainted with any other conditions which conduct to the same unity of ends under the moral law. But since the moral precept is, at the same time, my maxim (as reason requires that it should be), I am irresistibly constrained to believe in the existence of God and in a future life; and I am sure that nothing can make me waver in this belief, since I should thereby overthrow my moral maxims, the renunciation of which would render me hateful in my own eyes.\n\nThus, while all the ambitious attempts of reason to penetrate beyond the limits of experience end in disappointment, there is still enough left to satisfy us in a practical point of view. No one, it is true, will be able to boast that he knows that there is a God and a future life; for, if he knows this, he is just the man whom I have long wished to find. All knowledge, regarding an object of mere reason, can be communicated; and I should thus be enabled to hope that my own knowledge would receive this wonderful extension, through the instrumentality of his instruction. No, my conviction is not logical, but moral certainty; and since it rests on subjective grounds (of the moral sentiment), I must not even say: It is morally certain that there is a God, etc., but: I am morally certain, that is, my belief in God and in another world is so interwoven with my moral nature that I am under as little apprehension of having the former torn from me as of losing the latter.\n\nThe only point in this argument that may appear open to suspicion is that this rational belief presupposes the existence of moral sentiments. If we give up this assumption, and take a man who is entirely indifferent with regard to moral laws, the question which reason proposes, becomes then merely a problem for speculation and may, indeed, be supported by strong grounds from analogy, but not by such as will compel the most obstinate scepticism to give way.[[79]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-79) But in these questions no man is free from all interest. For though the want of good sentiments may place him beyond the influence of moral interests, still even in this case enough may be left to make him fear the existence of God and a future life. For he cannot pretend to any certainty of the non-existence of God and of a future life, unless—since it could only be proved by mere reason, and therefore apodeictically—he is prepared to establish the impossibility of both, which certainly no reasonable man would undertake to do. This would be a negative belief, which could not, indeed, produce morality and good sentiments, but still could produce an analogon of these, by operating as a powerful restraint on the outbreak of evil dispositions.\n\n[[79]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-79) The human mind (as, I believe, every rational being must of necessity do) takes a natural interest in morality, although this interest is not undivided, and may not be practically in preponderance. If you strengthen and increase it, you will find the reason become docile, more enlightened, and more capable of uniting the speculative interest with the practical. But if you do not take care at the outset, or at least midway, to make men good, you will never force them into an honest belief.\n\nBut, it will be said, is this all that pure reason can effect, in opening up prospects beyond the limits of experience? Nothing more than two articles of belief? Common sense could have done as much as this, without taking the philosophers to counsel in the matter!\n\nI shall not here eulogize philosophy for the benefits which the laborious efforts of its criticism have conferred on human reason—even granting that its merit should turn out in the end to be only negative—for on this point something more will be said in the next section. But, I ask, do you require that that knowledge which concerns all men, should transcend the common understanding, and should only be revealed to you by philosophers? The very circumstance which has called forth your censure, is the best confirmation of the correctness of our previous assertions, since it discloses, what could not have been foreseen, that Nature is not chargeable with any partial distribution of her gifts in those matters which concern all men without distinction and that, in respect to the essential ends of human nature, we cannot advance further with the help of the highest philosophy, than under the guidance which nature has vouchsafed to the meanest understanding.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","text":"### Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief\n\nThe holding of a thing to be true is a phenomenon in our understanding which may rest on objective grounds, but requires, also, subjective causes in the mind of the person judging. If a judgement is valid for every rational being, then its ground is objectively sufficient, and it is termed a conviction. If, on the other hand, it has its ground in the particular character of the subject, it is termed a persuasion.\n\nPersuasion is a mere illusion, the ground of the judgement, which lies solely in the subject, being regarded as objective. Hence a judgement of this kind has only private validity—is only valid for the individual who judges, and the holding of a thing to be true in this way cannot be communicated. But truth depends upon agreement with the object, and consequently the judgements of all understandings, if true, must be in agreement with each other (consentientia uni tertio consentiunt inter se). Conviction may, therefore, be distinguished, from an external point of view, from persuasion, by the possibility of communicating it and by showing its validity for the reason of every man; for in this case the presumption, at least, arises that the agreement of all judgements with each other, in spite of the different characters of individuals, rests upon the common ground of the agreement of each with the object, and thus the correctness of the judgement is established.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"91.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0002","text":"Persuasion, accordingly, cannot be subjectively distinguished from conviction, that is, so long as the subject views its judgement simply as a phenomenon of its own mind. But if we inquire whether the grounds of our judgement, which are valid for us, produce the same effect on the reason of others as on our own, we have then the means, though only subjective means, not, indeed, of producing conviction, but of detecting the merely private validity of the judgement; in other words, of discovering that there is in it the element of mere persuasion.\n\nIf we can, in addition to this, develop the subjective causes of the judgement, which we have taken for its objective grounds, and thus explain the deceptive judgement as a phenomenon in our mind, apart altogether from the objective character of the object, we can then expose the illusion and need be no longer deceived by it, although, if its subjective cause lies in our nature, we cannot hope altogether to escape its influence.\n\nI can only maintain, that is, affirm as necessarily valid for every one, that which produces conviction. Persuasion I may keep for myself, if it is agreeable to me; but I cannot, and ought not, to attempt to impose it as binding upon others.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"91.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","text":"Holding for true, or the subjective validity of a judgement in relation to conviction (which is, at the same time, objectively valid), has the three following degrees: opinion, belief, and knowledge. Opinion is a consciously insufficient judgement, subjectively as well as objectively. Belief is subjectively sufficient, but is recognized as being objectively insufficient. Knowledge is both subjectively and objectively sufficient. Subjective sufficiency is termed conviction (for myself); objective sufficiency is termed certainty (for all). I need not dwell longer on the explanation of such simple conceptions.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"91.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","text":"I must never venture to be of opinion, without knowing something, at least, by which my judgement, in itself merely problematical, is brought into connection with the truth—which connection, although not perfect, is still something more than an arbitrary fiction. Moreover, the law of such a connection must be certain. For if, in relation to this law, I have nothing more than opinion, my judgement is but a play of the imagination, without the least relation to truth. In the judgements of pure reason, opinion has no place. For, as they do not rest on empirical grounds and as the sphere of pure reason is that of necessary truth and à priori cognition, the principle of connection in it requires universality and necessity, and consequently perfect certainty—otherwise we should have no guide to the truth at all. Hence it is absurd to have an opinion in pure mathematics; we must know, or abstain from forming a judgement altogether. The case is the same with the maxims of morality. For we must not hazard an action on the mere opinion that it is allowed, but we must know it to be so. In the transcendental sphere of reason, on the other hand, the term opinion is too weak, while the word knowledge is too strong. From the merely speculative point of view, therefore, we cannot form a judgement at all. For the subjective grounds of a judgement, such as produce belief, cannot be admitted in speculative inquiries, inasmuch as they cannot stand without empirical support and are incapable of being communicated to others in equal measure.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"91.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0005","text":"But it is only from the practical point of view that a theoretically insufficient judgement can be termed belief. Now the practical reference is either to skill or to morality; to the former, when the end proposed is arbitrary and accidental, to the latter, when it is absolutely necessary.\n\nIf we propose to ourselves any end whatever, the conditions of its attainment are hypothetically necessary. The necessity is subjectively, but still only comparatively, sufficient, if I am acquainted with no other conditions under which the end can be attained. On the other hand, it is sufficient, absolutely and for every one, if I know for certain that no one can be acquainted with any other conditions under which the attainment of the proposed end would be possible. In the former case my supposition—my judgement with regard to certain conditions—is a merely accidental belief; in the latter it is a necessary belief. The physician must pursue some course in the case of a patient who is in danger, but is ignorant of the nature of the disease. He observes the symptoms, and concludes, according to the best of his judgement, that it is a case of phthisis. His belief is, even in his own judgement, only contingent: another man might, perhaps come nearer the truth. Such a belief, contingent indeed, but still forming the ground of the actual use of means for the attainment of certain ends, I term Pragmatical belief.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"91.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0006","text":"The usual test, whether that which any one maintains is merely his persuasion, or his subjective conviction at least, that is, his firm belief, is a bet. It frequently happens that a man delivers his opinions with so much boldness and assurance, that he appears to be under no apprehension as to the possibility of his being in error. The offer of a bet startles him, and makes him pause. Sometimes it turns out that his persuasion may be valued at a ducat, but not at ten. For he does not hesitate, perhaps, to venture a ducat, but if it is proposed to stake ten, he immediately becomes aware of the possibility of his being mistaken—a possibility which has hitherto escaped his observation. If we imagine to ourselves that we have to stake the happiness of our whole life on the truth of any proposition, our judgement drops its air of triumph, we take the alarm, and discover the actual strength of our belief. Thus pragmatical belief has degrees, varying in proportion to the interests at stake.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"91.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0007","text":"Now, in cases where we cannot enter upon any course of action in reference to some object, and where, accordingly, our judgement is purely theoretical, we can still represent to ourselves, in thought, the possibility of a course of action, for which we suppose that we have sufficient grounds, if any means existed of ascertaining the truth of the matter. Thus we find in purely theoretical judgements an analogon of practical judgements, to which the word belief may properly be applied, and which we may term doctrinal belief. I should not hesitate to stake my all on the truth of the proposition—if there were any possibility of bringing it to the test of experience—that, at least, some one of the planets, which we see, is inhabited. Hence I say that I have not merely the opinion, but the strong belief, on the correctness of which I would stake even many of the advantages of life, that there are inhabitants in other worlds.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"91.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0008","text":"Now we must admit that the doctrine of the existence of God belongs to doctrinal belief. For, although in respect to the theoretical cognition of the universe I do not require to form any theory which necessarily involves this idea, as the condition of my explanation of the phenomena which the universe presents, but, on the contrary, am rather bound so to use my reason as if everything were mere nature, still teleological unity is so important a condition of the application of my reason to nature, that it is impossible for me to ignore it—especially since, in addition to these considerations, abundant examples of it are supplied by experience. But the sole condition, so far as my knowledge extends, under which this unity can be my guide in the investigation of nature, is the assumption that a supreme intelligence has ordered all things according to the wisest ends. Consequently, the hypothesis of a wise author of the universe is necessary for my guidance in the investigation of nature—is the condition under which alone I can fulfil an end which is contingent indeed, but by no means unimportant. Moreover, since the result of my attempts so frequently confirms the utility of this assumption, and since nothing decisive can be adduced against it, it follows that it would be saying far too little to term my judgement, in this case, a mere opinion, and that, even in this theoretical connection, I may assert that I firmly believe in God. Still, if we use words strictly, this must not be called a practical, but a doctrinal belief, which the theology of nature (physico-theology) must also produce in my mind. In the wisdom of a Supreme Being, and in the shortness of life, so inadequate to the development of the glorious powers of human nature, we may find equally sufficient grounds for a doctrinal belief in the future life of the human soul.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"91.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0009","text":"The expression of belief is, in such cases, an expression of modesty from the objective point of view, but, at the same time, of firm confidence, from the subjective. If I should venture to term this merely theoretical judgement even so much as a hypothesis which I am entitled to assume; a more complete conception, with regard to another world and to the cause of the world, might then be justly required of me than I am, in reality, able to give. For, if I assume anything, even as a mere hypothesis, I must, at least, know so much of the properties of such a being as will enable me, not to form the conception, but to imagine the existence of it. But the word belief refers only to the guidance which an idea gives me, and to its subjective influence on the conduct of my reason, which forces me to hold it fast, though I may not be in a position to give a speculative account of it.\n\nBut mere doctrinal belief is, to some extent, wanting in stability. We often quit our hold of it, in consequence of the difficulties which occur in speculation, though in the end we inevitably return to it again.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"91.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","text":"It is quite otherwise with moral belief. For in this sphere action is absolutely necessary, that is, I must act in obedience to the moral law in all points. The end is here incontrovertibly established, and there is only one condition possible, according to the best of my perception, under which this end can harmonize with all other ends, and so have practical validity—namely, the existence of a God and of a future world. I know also, to a certainty, that no one can be acquainted with any other conditions which conduct to the same unity of ends under the moral law. But since the moral precept is, at the same time, my maxim (as reason requires that it should be), I am irresistibly constrained to believe in the existence of God and in a future life; and I am sure that nothing can make me waver in this belief, since I should thereby overthrow my moral maxims, the renunciation of which would render me hateful in my own eyes.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"91.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","text":"Thus, while all the ambitious attempts of reason to penetrate beyond the limits of experience end in disappointment, there is still enough left to satisfy us in a practical point of view. No one, it is true, will be able to boast that he knows that there is a God and a future life; for, if he knows this, he is just the man whom I have long wished to find. All knowledge, regarding an object of mere reason, can be communicated; and I should thus be enabled to hope that my own knowledge would receive this wonderful extension, through the instrumentality of his instruction. No, my conviction is not logical, but moral certainty; and since it rests on subjective grounds (of the moral sentiment), I must not even say: It is morally certain that there is a God, etc., but: I am morally certain, that is, my belief in God and in another world is so interwoven with my moral nature that I am under as little apprehension of having the former torn from me as of losing the latter.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"91.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0012","text":"The only point in this argument that may appear open to suspicion is that this rational belief presupposes the existence of moral sentiments. If we give up this assumption, and take a man who is entirely indifferent with regard to moral laws, the question which reason proposes, becomes then merely a problem for speculation and may, indeed, be supported by strong grounds from analogy, but not by such as will compel the most obstinate scepticism to give way.[[79]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-79) But in these questions no man is free from all interest. For though the want of good sentiments may place him beyond the influence of moral interests, still even in this case enough may be left to make him fear the existence of God and a future life. For he cannot pretend to any certainty of the non-existence of God and of a future life, unless—since it could only be proved by mere reason, and therefore apodeictically—he is prepared to establish the impossibility of both, which certainly no reasonable man would undertake to do. This would be a negative belief, which could not, indeed, produce morality and good sentiments, but still could produce an analogon of these, by operating as a powerful restraint on the outbreak of evil dispositions.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"91.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0013","text":"[[79]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-79) The human mind (as, I believe, every rational being must of necessity do) takes a natural interest in morality, although this interest is not undivided, and may not be practically in preponderance. If you strengthen and increase it, you will find the reason become docile, more enlightened, and more capable of uniting the speculative interest with the practical. But if you do not take care at the outset, or at least midway, to make men good, you will never force them into an honest belief.\n\nBut, it will be said, is this all that pure reason can effect, in opening up prospects beyond the limits of experience? Nothing more than two articles of belief? Common sense could have done as much as this, without taking the philosophers to counsel in the matter!","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"91.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0014","text":"I shall not here eulogize philosophy for the benefits which the laborious efforts of its criticism have conferred on human reason—even granting that its merit should turn out in the end to be only negative—for on this point something more will be said in the next section. But, I ask, do you require that that knowledge which concerns all men, should transcend the common understanding, and should only be revealed to you by philosophers? The very circumstance which has called forth your censure, is the best confirmation of the correctness of our previous assertions, since it discloses, what could not have been foreseen, that Nature is not chargeable with any partial distribution of her gifts in those matters which concern all men without distinction and that, in respect to the essential ends of human nature, we cannot advance further with the help of the highest philosophy, than under the guidance which nature has vouchsafed to the meanest understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"91.14"}],"sha256":"4648174a604ecb36abf61c40d1604006f3495200ba76ed7a75c24deea3f18a66","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2","title":"Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason","order":92,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_CI_2","text":"# Architectonic\n\n### Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason\n\nBy the term architectonic I mean the art of constructing a system. Without systematic unity, our knowledge cannot become science; it will be an aggregate, and not a system. Thus architectonic is the doctrine of the scientific in cognition, and therefore necessarily forms part of our methodology.\n\nReason cannot permit our knowledge to remain in an unconnected and rhapsodistic state, but requires that the sum of our cognitions should constitute a system. It is thus alone that they can advance the ends of reason. By a system I mean the unity of various cognitions under one idea. This idea is the conception—given by reason—of the form of a whole, in so far as the conception determines à priori not only the limits of its content, but the place which each of its parts is to occupy. The scientific idea contains, therefore, the end and the form of the whole which is in accordance with that end. The unity of the end, to which all the parts of the system relate, and through which all have a relation to each other, communicates unity to the whole system, so that the absence of any part can be immediately detected from our knowledge of the rest; and it determines à priori the limits of the system, thus excluding all contingent or arbitrary additions. The whole is thus an organism (articulatio), and not an aggregate (coacervatio); it may grow from within (per intussusceptionem), but it cannot increase by external additions (per appositionem). It is, thus, like an animal body, the growth of which does not add any limb, but, without changing their proportions, makes each in its sphere stronger and more active.\n\nWe require, for the execution of the idea of a system, a schema, that is, a content and an arrangement of parts determined à priori by the principle which the aim of the system prescribes. A schema which is not projected in accordance with an idea, that is, from the standpoint of the highest aim of reason, but merely empirically, in accordance with accidental aims and purposes (the number of which cannot be predetermined), can give us nothing more than technical unity. But the schema which is originated from an idea (in which case reason presents us with aims à priori, and does not look for them to experience), forms the basis of architectonical unity. A science, in the proper acceptation of that term, cannot be formed technically, that is, from observation of the similarity existing between different objects, and the purely contingent use we make of our knowledge in concreto with reference to all kinds of arbitrary external aims; its constitution must be framed on architectonical principles, that is, its parts must be shown to possess an essential affinity, and be capable of being deduced from one supreme and internal aim or end, which forms the condition of the possibility of the scientific whole. The schema of a science must give à priori the plan of it (monogramma), and the division of the whole into parts, in conformity with the idea of the science; and it must also distinguish this whole from all others, according to certain understood principles.\n\nNo one will attempt to construct a science, unless he have some idea to rest on as a proper basis. But, in the elaboration of the science, he finds that the schema, nay, even the definition which he at first gave of the science, rarely corresponds with his idea; for this idea lies, like a germ, in our reason, its parts undeveloped and hid even from microscopical observation. For this reason, we ought to explain and define sciences, not according to the description which the originator gives of them, but according to the idea which we find based in reason itself, and which is suggested by the natural unity of the parts of the science already accumulated. For it will often be found that the originator of a science and even his latest successors remain attached to an erroneous idea, which they cannot render clear to themselves, and that they thus fail in determining the true content, the articulation or systematic unity, and the limits of their science.\n\nIt is unfortunate that, only after having occupied ourselves for a long time in the collection of materials, under the guidance of an idea which lies undeveloped in the mind, but not according to any definite plan of arrangement—nay, only after we have spent much time and labour in the technical disposition of our materials, does it become possible to view the idea of a science in a clear light, and to project, according to architectonical principles, a plan of the whole, in accordance with the aims of reason. Systems seem, like certain worms, to be formed by a kind of generatio aequivoca—by the mere confluence of conceptions, and to gain completeness only with the progress of time. But the schema or germ of all lies in reason; and thus is not only every system organized according to its own idea, but all are united into one grand system of human knowledge, of which they form members. For this reason, it is possible to frame an architectonic of all human cognition, the formation of which, at the present time, considering the immense materials collected or to be found in the ruins of old systems, would not indeed be very difficult. Our purpose at present is merely to sketch the plan of the architectonic of all cognition given by pure reason; and we begin from the point where the main root of human knowledge divides into two, one of which is reason. By reason I understand here the whole higher faculty of cognition, the rational being placed in contradistinction to the empirical.\n\nIf I make complete abstraction of the content of cognition, objectively considered, all cognition is, from a subjective point of view, either historical or rational. Historical cognition is cognitio ex datis, rational, cognitio ex principiis. Whatever may be the original source of a cognition, it is, in relation to the person who possesses it, merely historical, if he knows only what has been given him from another quarter, whether that knowledge was communicated by direct experience or by instruction. Thus the person who has learned a system of philosophy—say the Wolfian—although he has a perfect knowledge of all the principles, definitions, and arguments in that philosophy, as well as of the divisions that have been made of the system, possesses really no more than an historical knowledge of the Wolfian system; he knows only what has been told him, his judgements are only those which he has received from his teachers. Dispute the validity of a definition, and he is completely at a loss to find another. He has formed his mind on another’s; but the imitative faculty is not the productive. His knowledge has not been drawn from reason; and although, objectively considered, it is rational knowledge, subjectively, it is merely historical. He has learned this or that philosophy and is merely a plaster cast of a living man. Rational cognitions which are objective, that is, which have their source in reason, can be so termed from a subjective point of view, only when they have been drawn by the individual himself from the sources of reason, that is, from principles; and it is in this way alone that criticism, or even the rejection of what has been already learned, can spring up in the mind.\n\nAll rational cognition is, again, based either on conceptions, or on the construction of conceptions. The former is termed philosophical, the latter mathematical. I have already shown the essential difference of these two methods of cognition in the first chapter. A cognition may be objectively philosophical and subjectively historical—as is the case with the majority of scholars and those who cannot look beyond the limits of their system, and who remain in a state of pupilage all their lives. But it is remarkable that mathematical knowledge, when committed to memory, is valid, from the subjective point of view, as rational knowledge also, and that the same distinction cannot be drawn here as in the case of philosophical cognition. The reason is that the only way of arriving at this knowledge is through the essential principles of reason, and thus it is always certain and indisputable; because reason is employed in concreto—but at the same time à priori—that is, in pure and, therefore, infallible intuition; and thus all causes of illusion and error are excluded. Of all the à priori sciences of reason, therefore, mathematics alone can be learned. Philosophy—unless it be in an historical manner—cannot be learned; we can at most learn to philosophize.\n\nPhilosophy is the system of all philosophical cognition. We must use this term in an objective sense, if we understand by it the archetype of all attempts at philosophizing, and the standard by which all subjective philosophies are to be judged. In this sense, philosophy is merely the idea of a possible science, which does not exist in concreto, but to which we endeavour in various ways to approximate, until we have discovered the right path to pursue—a path overgrown by the errors and illusions of sense—and the image we have hitherto tried in vain to shape has become a perfect copy of the great prototype. Until that time, we cannot learn philosophy—it does not exist; if it does, where is it, who possesses it, and how shall we know it? We can only learn to philosophize; in other words, we can only exercise our powers of reasoning in accordance with general principles, retaining at the same time, the right of investigating the sources of these principles, of testing, and even of rejecting them.\n\nUntil then, our conception of philosophy is only a scholastic conception—a conception, that is, of a system of cognition which we are trying to elaborate into a science; all that we at present know being the systematic unity of this cognition, and consequently the logical completeness of the cognition for the desired end. But there is also a cosmical conception (conceptus cosmicus) of philosophy, which has always formed the true basis of this term, especially when philosophy was personified and presented to us in the ideal of a philosopher. In this view philosophy is the science of the relation of all cognition to the ultimate and essential aims of human reason (teleologia rationis humanae), and the philosopher is not merely an artist—who occupies himself with conceptions—but a lawgiver, legislating for human reason. In this sense of the word, it would be in the highest degree arrogant to assume the title of philosopher, and to pretend that we had reached the perfection of the prototype which lies in the idea alone.\n\nThe mathematician, the natural philosopher, and the logician—how far soever the first may have advanced in rational, and the two latter in philosophical knowledge—are merely artists, engaged in the arrangement and formation of conceptions; they cannot be termed philosophers. Above them all, there is the ideal teacher, who employs them as instruments for the advancement of the essential aims of human reason. Him alone can we call philosopher; but he nowhere exists. But the idea of his legislative power resides in the mind of every man, and it alone teaches us what kind of systematic unity philosophy demands in view of the ultimate aims of reason. This idea is, therefore, a cosmical conception.[[80]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-80)\n\n[[80]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-80) By a cosmical conception, I mean one in which all men necessarily take an interest; the aim of a science must accordingly be determined according to scholastic conceptions, if it is regarded merely as a means to certain arbitrarily proposed ends.\n\nIn view of the complete systematic unity of reason, there can only be one ultimate end of all the operations of the mind. To this all other aims are subordinate, and nothing more than means for its attainment. This ultimate end is the destination of man, and the philosophy which relates to it is termed moral philosophy. The superior position occupied by moral philosophy, above all other spheres for the operations of reason, sufficiently indicates the reason why the ancients always included the idea—and in an especial manner—of moralist in that of philosopher. Even at the present day, we call a man who appears to have the power of self-government, even although his knowledge may be very limited, by the name of philosopher.\n\nThe legislation of human reason, or philosophy, has two objects—nature and freedom—and thus contains not only the laws of nature, but also those of ethics, at first in two separate systems, which, finally, merge into one grand philosophical system of cognition. The philosophy of nature relates to that which is, that of ethics to that which ought to be.\n\nBut all philosophy is either cognition on the basis of pure reason, or the cognition of reason on the basis of empirical principles. The former is termed pure, the latter empirical philosophy.\n\nThe philosophy of pure reason is either propædeutic, that is, an inquiry into the powers of reason in regard to pure à priori cognition, and is termed critical philosophy; or it is, secondly, the system of pure reason—a science containing the systematic presentation of the whole body of philosophical knowledge, true as well as illusory, given by pure reason—and is called metaphysic. This name may, however, be also given to the whole system of pure philosophy, critical philosophy included, and may designate the investigation into the sources or possibility of à priori cognition, as well as the presentation of the à priori cognitions which form a system of pure philosophy—excluding, at the same time, all empirical and mathematical elements.\n\nMetaphysic is divided into that of the speculative and that of the practical use of pure reason, and is, accordingly, either the metaphysic of nature, or the metaphysic of ethics. The former contains all the pure rational principles—based upon conceptions alone (and thus excluding mathematics)—of all theoretical cognition; the latter, the principles which determine and necessitate à priori all action. Now moral philosophy alone contains a code of laws—for the regulation of our actions—which are deduced from principles entirely à priori. Hence the metaphysic of ethics is the only pure moral philosophy, as it is not based upon anthropological or other empirical considerations. The metaphysic of speculative reason is what is commonly called metaphysic in the more limited sense. But as pure moral philosophy properly forms a part of this system of cognition, we must allow it to retain the name of metaphysic, although it is not requisite that we should insist on so terming it in our present discussion.\n\nIt is of the highest importance to separate those cognitions which differ from others both in kind and in origin, and to take great care that they are not confounded with those with which they are generally found connected. What the chemist does in the analysis of substances, what the mathematician in pure mathematics, is, in a still higher degree, the duty of the philosopher, that the value of each different kind of cognition, and the part it takes in the operations of the mind, may be clearly defined. Human reason has never wanted a metaphysic of some kind, since it attained the power of thought, or rather of reflection; but it has never been able to keep this sphere of thought and cognition pure from all admixture of foreign elements. The idea of a science of this kind is as old as speculation itself; and what mind does not speculate—either in the scholastic or in the popular fashion? At the same time, it must be admitted that even thinkers by profession have been unable clearly to explain the distinction between the two elements of our cognition—the one completely à priori, the other à posteriori; and hence the proper definition of a peculiar kind of cognition, and with it the just idea of a science which has so long and so deeply engaged the attention of the human mind, has never been established. When it was said: “Metaphysic is the science of the first principles of human cognition,” this definition did not signalize a peculiarity in kind, but only a difference in degree; these first principles were thus declared to be more general than others, but no criterion of distinction from empirical principles was given. Of these some are more general, and therefore higher, than others; and—as we cannot distinguish what is completely à priori from that which is known to be à posteriori—where shall we draw the line which is to separate the higher and so-called first principles, from the lower and subordinate principles of cognition? What would be said if we were asked to be satisfied with a division of the epochs of the world into the earlier centuries and those following them? “Does the fifth, or the tenth century belong to the earlier centuries?” it would be asked. In the same way I ask: Does the conception of extension belong to metaphysics? You answer, “Yes.” Well, that of body too? “Yes.” And that of a fluid body? You stop, you are unprepared to admit this; for if you do, everything will belong to metaphysics. From this it is evident that the mere degree of subordination—of the particular to the general—cannot determine the limits of a science; and that, in the present case, we must expect to find a difference in the conceptions of metaphysics both in kind and in origin. The fundamental idea of metaphysics was obscured on another side by the fact that this kind of à priori cognition showed a certain similarity in character with the science of mathematics. Both have the property in common of possessing an à priori origin; but, in the one, our knowledge is based upon conceptions, in the other, on the construction of conceptions. Thus a decided dissimilarity between philosophical and mathematical cognition comes out—a dissimilarity which was always felt, but which could not be made distinct for want of an insight into the criteria of the difference. And thus it happened that, as philosophers themselves failed in the proper development of the idea of their science, the elaboration of the science could not proceed with a definite aim, or under trustworthy guidance. Thus, too, philosophers, ignorant of the path they ought to pursue and always disputing with each other regarding the discoveries which each asserted he had made, brought their science into disrepute with the rest of the world, and finally, even among themselves.\n\nAll pure à priori cognition forms, therefore, in view of the peculiar faculty which originates it, a peculiar and distinct unity; and metaphysic is the term applied to the philosophy which attempts to represent that cognition in this systematic unity. The speculative part of metaphysic, which has especially appropriated this appellation—that which we have called the metaphysic of nature—and which considers everything, as it is (not as it ought to be), by means of à priori conceptions, is divided in the following manner.\n\nMetaphysic, in the more limited acceptation of the term, consists of two parts—transcendental philosophy and the physiology of pure reason. The former presents the system of all the conceptions and principles belonging to the understanding and the reason, and which relate to objects in general, but not to any particular given objects (Ontologia); the latter has nature for its subject-matter, that is, the sum of given objects—whether given to the senses, or, if we will, to some other kind of intuition—and is accordingly physiology, although only rationalis. But the use of the faculty of reason in this rational mode of regarding nature is either physical or hyperphysical, or, more properly speaking, immanent or transcendent. The former relates to nature, in so far as our knowledge regarding it may be applied in experience (in concreto); the latter to that connection of the objects of experience, which transcends all experience. Transcendent physiology has, again, an internal and an external connection with its object, both, however, transcending possible experience; the former is the physiology of nature as a whole, or transcendental cognition of the world, the latter of the connection of the whole of nature with a being above nature, or transcendental cognition of God.\n\nImmanent physiology, on the contrary, considers nature as the sum of all sensuous objects, consequently, as it is presented to us—but still according to à priori conditions, for it is under these alone that nature can be presented to our minds at all. The objects of immanent physiology are of two kinds: 1. Those of the external senses, or corporeal nature; 2. The object of the internal sense, the soul, or, in accordance with our fundamental conceptions of it, thinking nature. The metaphysics of corporeal nature is called physics; but, as it must contain only the principles of an à priori cognition of nature, we must term it rational physics. The metaphysics of thinking nature is called psychology, and for the same reason is to be regarded as merely the rational cognition of the soul.\n\nThus the whole system of metaphysics consists of four principal parts: 1. Ontology; 2. Rational Physiology; 3. Rational cosmology; and 4. Rational theology. The second part—that of the rational doctrine of nature—may be subdivided into two, physica rationalis[[81]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-81) and psychologia rationalis.\n\n[[81]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-81) It must not be supposed that I mean by this appellation what is generally called physica general is, and which is rather mathematics than a philosophy of nature. For the metaphysic of nature is completely different from mathematics, nor is it so rich in results, although it is of great importance as a critical test of the application of pure understanding—cognition to nature. For want of its guidance, even mathematicians, adopting certain common notions—which are, in fact, metaphysical—have unconsciously crowded their theories of nature with hypotheses, the fallacy of which becomes evident upon the application of the principles of this metaphysic, without detriment, however, to the employment of mathematics in this sphere of cognition.\n\nThe fundamental idea of a philosophy of pure reason of necessity dictates this division; it is, therefore, architectonical—in accordance with the highest aims of reason, and not merely technical, or according to certain accidentally-observed similarities existing between the different parts of the whole science. For this reason, also, is the division immutable and of legislative authority. But the reader may observe in it a few points to which he ought to demur, and which may weaken his conviction of its truth and legitimacy.\n\nIn the first place, how can I desire an à priori cognition or metaphysic of objects, in so far as they are given à posteriori? and how is it possible to cognize the nature of things according to à priori principles, and to attain to a rational physiology? The answer is this. We take from experience nothing more than is requisite to present us with an object (in general) of the external or of the internal sense; in the former case, by the mere conception of matter (impenetrable and inanimate extension), in the latter, by the conception of a thinking being—given in the internal empirical representation, I think. As to the rest, we must not employ in our metaphysic of these objects any empirical principles (which add to the content of our conceptions by means of experience), for the purpose of forming by their help any judgements respecting these objects.\n\nSecondly, what place shall we assign to empirical psychology, which has always been considered a part of metaphysics, and from which in our time such important philosophical results have been expected, after the hope of constructing an à priori system of knowledge had been abandoned? I answer: It must be placed by the side of empirical physics or physics proper; that is, must be regarded as forming a part of applied philosophy, the à priori principles of which are contained in pure philosophy, which is therefore connected, although it must not be confounded, with psychology. Empirical psychology must therefore be banished from the sphere of metaphysics, and is indeed excluded by the very idea of that science. In conformity, however, with scholastic usage, we must permit it to occupy a place in metaphysics—but only as an appendix to it. We adopt this course from motives of economy; as psychology is not as yet full enough to occupy our attention as an independent study, while it is, at the same time, of too great importance to be entirely excluded or placed where it has still less affinity than it has with the subject of metaphysics. It is a stranger who has been long a guest; and we make it welcome to stay, until it can take up a more suitable abode in a complete system of anthropology—the pendant to empirical physics.\n\nThe above is the general idea of metaphysics, which, as more was expected from it than could be looked for with justice, and as these pleasant expectations were unfortunately never realized, fell into general disrepute. Our Critique must have fully convinced the reader that, although metaphysics cannot form the foundation of religion, it must always be one of its most important bulwarks, and that human reason, which naturally pursues a dialectical course, cannot do without this science, which checks its tendencies towards dialectic and, by elevating reason to a scientific and clear self-knowledge, prevents the ravages which a lawless speculative reason would infallibly commit in the sphere of morals as well as in that of religion. We may be sure, therefore, whatever contempt may be thrown upon metaphysics by those who judge a science not by its own nature, but according to the accidental effects it may have produced, that it can never be completely abandoned, that we must always return to it as to a beloved one who has been for a time estranged, because the questions with which it is engaged relate to the highest aims of humanity, and reason must always labour either to attain to settled views in regard to these, or to destroy those which others have already established.\n\nMetaphysic, therefore—that of nature, as well as that of ethics, but in an especial manner the criticism which forms the propædeutic to all the operations of reason—forms properly that department of knowledge which may be termed, in the truest sense of the word, philosophy. The path which it pursues is that of science, which, when it has once been discovered, is never lost, and never misleads. Mathematics, natural science, the common experience of men, have a high value as means, for the most part, to accidental ends—but at last also, to those which are necessary and essential to the existence of humanity. But to guide them to this high goal, they require the aid of rational cognition on the basis of pure conceptions, which, be it termed as it may, is properly nothing but metaphysics.\n\nFor the same reason, metaphysics forms likewise the completion of the culture of human reason. In this respect, it is indispensable, setting aside altogether the influence which it exerts as a science. For its subject-matter is the elements and highest maxims of reason, which form the basis of the possibility of some sciences and of the use of all. That, as a purely speculative science, it is more useful in preventing error than in the extension of knowledge, does not detract from its value; on the contrary, the supreme office of censor which it occupies assures to it the highest authority and importance. This office it administers for the purpose of securing order, harmony, and well-being to science, and of directing its noble and fruitful labours to the highest possible aim—the happiness of all mankind.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","text":"# Architectonic\n\n### Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason\n\nBy the term architectonic I mean the art of constructing a system. Without systematic unity, our knowledge cannot become science; it will be an aggregate, and not a system. Thus architectonic is the doctrine of the scientific in cognition, and therefore necessarily forms part of our methodology.\n\nReason cannot permit our knowledge to remain in an unconnected and rhapsodistic state, but requires that the sum of our cognitions should constitute a system. It is thus alone that they can advance the ends of reason. By a system I mean the unity of various cognitions under one idea. This idea is the conception—given by reason—of the form of a whole, in so far as the conception determines à priori not only the limits of its content, but the place which each of its parts is to occupy. The scientific idea contains, therefore, the end and the form of the whole which is in accordance with that end. The unity of the end, to which all the parts of the system relate, and through which all have a relation to each other, communicates unity to the whole system, so that the absence of any part can be immediately detected from our knowledge of the rest; and it determines à priori the limits of the system, thus excluding all contingent or arbitrary additions. The whole is thus an organism (articulatio), and not an aggregate (coacervatio); it may grow from within (per intussusceptionem), but it cannot increase by external additions (per appositionem). It is, thus, like an animal body, the growth of which does not add any limb, but, without changing their proportions, makes each in its sphere stronger and more active.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"92.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0002","text":"We require, for the execution of the idea of a system, a schema, that is, a content and an arrangement of parts determined à priori by the principle which the aim of the system prescribes. A schema which is not projected in accordance with an idea, that is, from the standpoint of the highest aim of reason, but merely empirically, in accordance with accidental aims and purposes (the number of which cannot be predetermined), can give us nothing more than technical unity. But the schema which is originated from an idea (in which case reason presents us with aims à priori, and does not look for them to experience), forms the basis of architectonical unity. A science, in the proper acceptation of that term, cannot be formed technically, that is, from observation of the similarity existing between different objects, and the purely contingent use we make of our knowledge in concreto with reference to all kinds of arbitrary external aims; its constitution must be framed on architectonical principles, that is, its parts must be shown to possess an essential affinity, and be capable of being deduced from one supreme and internal aim or end, which forms the condition of the possibility of the scientific whole. The schema of a science must give à priori the plan of it (monogramma), and the division of the whole into parts, in conformity with the idea of the science; and it must also distinguish this whole from all others, according to certain understood principles.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"92.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0003","text":"No one will attempt to construct a science, unless he have some idea to rest on as a proper basis. But, in the elaboration of the science, he finds that the schema, nay, even the definition which he at first gave of the science, rarely corresponds with his idea; for this idea lies, like a germ, in our reason, its parts undeveloped and hid even from microscopical observation. For this reason, we ought to explain and define sciences, not according to the description which the originator gives of them, but according to the idea which we find based in reason itself, and which is suggested by the natural unity of the parts of the science already accumulated. For it will often be found that the originator of a science and even his latest successors remain attached to an erroneous idea, which they cannot render clear to themselves, and that they thus fail in determining the true content, the articulation or systematic unity, and the limits of their science.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"92.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0004","text":"It is unfortunate that, only after having occupied ourselves for a long time in the collection of materials, under the guidance of an idea which lies undeveloped in the mind, but not according to any definite plan of arrangement—nay, only after we have spent much time and labour in the technical disposition of our materials, does it become possible to view the idea of a science in a clear light, and to project, according to architectonical principles, a plan of the whole, in accordance with the aims of reason. Systems seem, like certain worms, to be formed by a kind of generatio aequivoca—by the mere confluence of conceptions, and to gain completeness only with the progress of time. But the schema or germ of all lies in reason; and thus is not only every system organized according to its own idea, but all are united into one grand system of human knowledge, of which they form members. For this reason, it is possible to frame an architectonic of all human cognition, the formation of which, at the present time, considering the immense materials collected or to be found in the ruins of old systems, would not indeed be very difficult. Our purpose at present is merely to sketch the plan of the architectonic of all cognition given by pure reason; and we begin from the point where the main root of human knowledge divides into two, one of which is reason. By reason I understand here the whole higher faculty of cognition, the rational being placed in contradistinction to the empirical.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"92.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","text":"If I make complete abstraction of the content of cognition, objectively considered, all cognition is, from a subjective point of view, either historical or rational. Historical cognition is cognitio ex datis, rational, cognitio ex principiis. Whatever may be the original source of a cognition, it is, in relation to the person who possesses it, merely historical, if he knows only what has been given him from another quarter, whether that knowledge was communicated by direct experience or by instruction. Thus the person who has learned a system of philosophy—say the Wolfian—although he has a perfect knowledge of all the principles, definitions, and arguments in that philosophy, as well as of the divisions that have been made of the system, possesses really no more than an historical knowledge of the Wolfian system; he knows only what has been told him, his judgements are only those which he has received from his teachers. Dispute the validity of a definition, and he is completely at a loss to find another. He has formed his mind on another’s; but the imitative faculty is not the productive. His knowledge has not been drawn from reason; and although, objectively considered, it is rational knowledge, subjectively, it is merely historical. He has learned this or that philosophy and is merely a plaster cast of a living man. Rational cognitions which are objective, that is, which have their source in reason, can be so termed from a subjective point of view, only when they have been drawn by the individual himself from the sources of reason, that is, from principles; and it is in this way alone that criticism, or even the rejection of what has been already learned, can spring up in the mind.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"92.5"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0006","text":"All rational cognition is, again, based either on conceptions, or on the construction of conceptions. The former is termed philosophical, the latter mathematical. I have already shown the essential difference of these two methods of cognition in the first chapter. A cognition may be objectively philosophical and subjectively historical—as is the case with the majority of scholars and those who cannot look beyond the limits of their system, and who remain in a state of pupilage all their lives. But it is remarkable that mathematical knowledge, when committed to memory, is valid, from the subjective point of view, as rational knowledge also, and that the same distinction cannot be drawn here as in the case of philosophical cognition. The reason is that the only way of arriving at this knowledge is through the essential principles of reason, and thus it is always certain and indisputable; because reason is employed in concreto—but at the same time à priori—that is, in pure and, therefore, infallible intuition; and thus all causes of illusion and error are excluded. Of all the à priori sciences of reason, therefore, mathematics alone can be learned. Philosophy—unless it be in an historical manner—cannot be learned; we can at most learn to philosophize.","kind":"passage","sequence":6,"address":"92.6"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","text":"Philosophy is the system of all philosophical cognition. We must use this term in an objective sense, if we understand by it the archetype of all attempts at philosophizing, and the standard by which all subjective philosophies are to be judged. In this sense, philosophy is merely the idea of a possible science, which does not exist in concreto, but to which we endeavour in various ways to approximate, until we have discovered the right path to pursue—a path overgrown by the errors and illusions of sense—and the image we have hitherto tried in vain to shape has become a perfect copy of the great prototype. Until that time, we cannot learn philosophy—it does not exist; if it does, where is it, who possesses it, and how shall we know it? We can only learn to philosophize; in other words, we can only exercise our powers of reasoning in accordance with general principles, retaining at the same time, the right of investigating the sources of these principles, of testing, and even of rejecting them.","kind":"passage","sequence":7,"address":"92.7"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","text":"Until then, our conception of philosophy is only a scholastic conception—a conception, that is, of a system of cognition which we are trying to elaborate into a science; all that we at present know being the systematic unity of this cognition, and consequently the logical completeness of the cognition for the desired end. But there is also a cosmical conception (conceptus cosmicus) of philosophy, which has always formed the true basis of this term, especially when philosophy was personified and presented to us in the ideal of a philosopher. In this view philosophy is the science of the relation of all cognition to the ultimate and essential aims of human reason (teleologia rationis humanae), and the philosopher is not merely an artist—who occupies himself with conceptions—but a lawgiver, legislating for human reason. In this sense of the word, it would be in the highest degree arrogant to assume the title of philosopher, and to pretend that we had reached the perfection of the prototype which lies in the idea alone.","kind":"passage","sequence":8,"address":"92.8"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0009","text":"The mathematician, the natural philosopher, and the logician—how far soever the first may have advanced in rational, and the two latter in philosophical knowledge—are merely artists, engaged in the arrangement and formation of conceptions; they cannot be termed philosophers. Above them all, there is the ideal teacher, who employs them as instruments for the advancement of the essential aims of human reason. Him alone can we call philosopher; but he nowhere exists. But the idea of his legislative power resides in the mind of every man, and it alone teaches us what kind of systematic unity philosophy demands in view of the ultimate aims of reason. This idea is, therefore, a cosmical conception.[[80]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-80)\n\n[[80]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-80) By a cosmical conception, I mean one in which all men necessarily take an interest; the aim of a science must accordingly be determined according to scholastic conceptions, if it is regarded merely as a means to certain arbitrarily proposed ends.","kind":"passage","sequence":9,"address":"92.9"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","text":"In view of the complete systematic unity of reason, there can only be one ultimate end of all the operations of the mind. To this all other aims are subordinate, and nothing more than means for its attainment. This ultimate end is the destination of man, and the philosophy which relates to it is termed moral philosophy. The superior position occupied by moral philosophy, above all other spheres for the operations of reason, sufficiently indicates the reason why the ancients always included the idea—and in an especial manner—of moralist in that of philosopher. Even at the present day, we call a man who appears to have the power of self-government, even although his knowledge may be very limited, by the name of philosopher.\n\nThe legislation of human reason, or philosophy, has two objects—nature and freedom—and thus contains not only the laws of nature, but also those of ethics, at first in two separate systems, which, finally, merge into one grand philosophical system of cognition. The philosophy of nature relates to that which is, that of ethics to that which ought to be.\n\nBut all philosophy is either cognition on the basis of pure reason, or the cognition of reason on the basis of empirical principles. The former is termed pure, the latter empirical philosophy.","kind":"passage","sequence":10,"address":"92.10"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","text":"The philosophy of pure reason is either propædeutic, that is, an inquiry into the powers of reason in regard to pure à priori cognition, and is termed critical philosophy; or it is, secondly, the system of pure reason—a science containing the systematic presentation of the whole body of philosophical knowledge, true as well as illusory, given by pure reason—and is called metaphysic. This name may, however, be also given to the whole system of pure philosophy, critical philosophy included, and may designate the investigation into the sources or possibility of à priori cognition, as well as the presentation of the à priori cognitions which form a system of pure philosophy—excluding, at the same time, all empirical and mathematical elements.\n\nMetaphysic is divided into that of the speculative and that of the practical use of pure reason, and is, accordingly, either the metaphysic of nature, or the metaphysic of ethics. The former contains all the pure rational principles—based upon conceptions alone (and thus excluding mathematics)—of all theoretical cognition; the latter, the principles which determine and necessitate à priori all action. Now moral philosophy alone contains a code of laws—for the regulation of our actions—which are deduced from principles entirely à priori. Hence the metaphysic of ethics is the only pure moral philosophy, as it is not based upon anthropological or other empirical considerations. The metaphysic of speculative reason is what is commonly called metaphysic in the more limited sense. But as pure moral philosophy properly forms a part of this system of cognition, we must allow it to retain the name of metaphysic, although it is not requisite that we should insist on so terming it in our present discussion.","kind":"passage","sequence":11,"address":"92.11"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0012","text":"It is of the highest importance to separate those cognitions which differ from others both in kind and in origin, and to take great care that they are not confounded with those with which they are generally found connected. What the chemist does in the analysis of substances, what the mathematician in pure mathematics, is, in a still higher degree, the duty of the philosopher, that the value of each different kind of cognition, and the part it takes in the operations of the mind, may be clearly defined. Human reason has never wanted a metaphysic of some kind, since it attained the power of thought, or rather of reflection; but it has never been able to keep this sphere of thought and cognition pure from all admixture of foreign elements. The idea of a science of this kind is as old as speculation itself; and what mind does not speculate—either in the scholastic or in the popular fashion? At the same time, it must be admitted that even thinkers by profession have been unable clearly to explain the distinction between the two elements of our cognition—the one completely à priori, the other à posteriori; and hence the proper definition of a peculiar kind of cognition, and with it the just idea of a science which has so long and so deeply engaged the attention of the human mind, has never been established. When it was said: “Metaphysic is the science of the first principles of human cognition,” this definition did not signalize a peculiarity in kind, but only a difference in degree; these first principles were thus declared to be more general than others, but no criterion of distinction from empirical principles was given. Of these some are more general, and therefore higher, than others; and—as we cannot distinguish what is completely à priori from that which is known to be à posteriori—where shall we draw the line which is to separate the higher and so-called first principles, from the lower and subordinate principles of cognition? What would be said if we were asked to be satisfied with a division of the epochs of the world into the earlier centuries and those following them? “Does the fifth, or the tenth century belong to the earlier centuries?” it would be asked. In the same way I ask: Does the conception of extension belong to metaphysics? You answer, “Yes.” Well, that of body too? “Yes.” And that of a fluid body? You stop, you are unprepared to admit this; for if you do, everything will belong to metaphysics. From this it is evident that the mere degree of subordination—of the particular to the general—cannot determine the limits of a science; and that, in the present case, we must expect to find a difference in the conceptions of metaphysics both in kind and in origin. The fundamental idea of metaphysics was obscured on another side by the fact that this kind of à priori cognition showed a certain similarity in character with the science of mathematics. Both have the property in common of possessing an à priori origin; but, in the one, our knowledge is based upon conceptions, in the other, on the construction of conceptions. Thus a decided dissimilarity between philosophical and mathematical cognition comes out—a dissimilarity which was always felt, but which could not be made distinct for want of an insight into the criteria of the difference. And thus it happened that, as philosophers themselves failed in the proper development of the idea of their science, the elaboration of the science could not proceed with a definite aim, or under trustworthy guidance. Thus, too, philosophers, ignorant of the path they ought to pursue and always disputing with each other regarding the discoveries which each asserted he had made, brought their science into disrepute with the rest of the world, and finally, even among themselves.","kind":"passage","sequence":12,"address":"92.12"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0013","text":"All pure à priori cognition forms, therefore, in view of the peculiar faculty which originates it, a peculiar and distinct unity; and metaphysic is the term applied to the philosophy which attempts to represent that cognition in this systematic unity. The speculative part of metaphysic, which has especially appropriated this appellation—that which we have called the metaphysic of nature—and which considers everything, as it is (not as it ought to be), by means of à priori conceptions, is divided in the following manner.","kind":"passage","sequence":13,"address":"92.13"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0014","text":"Metaphysic, in the more limited acceptation of the term, consists of two parts—transcendental philosophy and the physiology of pure reason. The former presents the system of all the conceptions and principles belonging to the understanding and the reason, and which relate to objects in general, but not to any particular given objects (Ontologia); the latter has nature for its subject-matter, that is, the sum of given objects—whether given to the senses, or, if we will, to some other kind of intuition—and is accordingly physiology, although only rationalis. But the use of the faculty of reason in this rational mode of regarding nature is either physical or hyperphysical, or, more properly speaking, immanent or transcendent. The former relates to nature, in so far as our knowledge regarding it may be applied in experience (in concreto); the latter to that connection of the objects of experience, which transcends all experience. Transcendent physiology has, again, an internal and an external connection with its object, both, however, transcending possible experience; the former is the physiology of nature as a whole, or transcendental cognition of the world, the latter of the connection of the whole of nature with a being above nature, or transcendental cognition of God.","kind":"passage","sequence":14,"address":"92.14"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0015","text":"Immanent physiology, on the contrary, considers nature as the sum of all sensuous objects, consequently, as it is presented to us—but still according to à priori conditions, for it is under these alone that nature can be presented to our minds at all. The objects of immanent physiology are of two kinds: 1. Those of the external senses, or corporeal nature; 2. The object of the internal sense, the soul, or, in accordance with our fundamental conceptions of it, thinking nature. The metaphysics of corporeal nature is called physics; but, as it must contain only the principles of an à priori cognition of nature, we must term it rational physics. The metaphysics of thinking nature is called psychology, and for the same reason is to be regarded as merely the rational cognition of the soul.\n\nThus the whole system of metaphysics consists of four principal parts: 1. Ontology; 2. Rational Physiology; 3. Rational cosmology; and 4. Rational theology. The second part—that of the rational doctrine of nature—may be subdivided into two, physica rationalis[[81]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknote-81) and psychologia rationalis.","kind":"passage","sequence":15,"address":"92.15"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0016","text":"[[81]](https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/4280/pg4280-images.html#linknoteref-81) It must not be supposed that I mean by this appellation what is generally called physica general is, and which is rather mathematics than a philosophy of nature. For the metaphysic of nature is completely different from mathematics, nor is it so rich in results, although it is of great importance as a critical test of the application of pure understanding—cognition to nature. For want of its guidance, even mathematicians, adopting certain common notions—which are, in fact, metaphysical—have unconsciously crowded their theories of nature with hypotheses, the fallacy of which becomes evident upon the application of the principles of this metaphysic, without detriment, however, to the employment of mathematics in this sphere of cognition.\n\nThe fundamental idea of a philosophy of pure reason of necessity dictates this division; it is, therefore, architectonical—in accordance with the highest aims of reason, and not merely technical, or according to certain accidentally-observed similarities existing between the different parts of the whole science. For this reason, also, is the division immutable and of legislative authority. But the reader may observe in it a few points to which he ought to demur, and which may weaken his conviction of its truth and legitimacy.","kind":"passage","sequence":16,"address":"92.16"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0017","text":"In the first place, how can I desire an à priori cognition or metaphysic of objects, in so far as they are given à posteriori? and how is it possible to cognize the nature of things according to à priori principles, and to attain to a rational physiology? The answer is this. We take from experience nothing more than is requisite to present us with an object (in general) of the external or of the internal sense; in the former case, by the mere conception of matter (impenetrable and inanimate extension), in the latter, by the conception of a thinking being—given in the internal empirical representation, I think. As to the rest, we must not employ in our metaphysic of these objects any empirical principles (which add to the content of our conceptions by means of experience), for the purpose of forming by their help any judgements respecting these objects.","kind":"passage","sequence":17,"address":"92.17"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0018","text":"Secondly, what place shall we assign to empirical psychology, which has always been considered a part of metaphysics, and from which in our time such important philosophical results have been expected, after the hope of constructing an à priori system of knowledge had been abandoned? I answer: It must be placed by the side of empirical physics or physics proper; that is, must be regarded as forming a part of applied philosophy, the à priori principles of which are contained in pure philosophy, which is therefore connected, although it must not be confounded, with psychology. Empirical psychology must therefore be banished from the sphere of metaphysics, and is indeed excluded by the very idea of that science. In conformity, however, with scholastic usage, we must permit it to occupy a place in metaphysics—but only as an appendix to it. We adopt this course from motives of economy; as psychology is not as yet full enough to occupy our attention as an independent study, while it is, at the same time, of too great importance to be entirely excluded or placed where it has still less affinity than it has with the subject of metaphysics. It is a stranger who has been long a guest; and we make it welcome to stay, until it can take up a more suitable abode in a complete system of anthropology—the pendant to empirical physics.","kind":"passage","sequence":18,"address":"92.18"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","text":"The above is the general idea of metaphysics, which, as more was expected from it than could be looked for with justice, and as these pleasant expectations were unfortunately never realized, fell into general disrepute. Our Critique must have fully convinced the reader that, although metaphysics cannot form the foundation of religion, it must always be one of its most important bulwarks, and that human reason, which naturally pursues a dialectical course, cannot do without this science, which checks its tendencies towards dialectic and, by elevating reason to a scientific and clear self-knowledge, prevents the ravages which a lawless speculative reason would infallibly commit in the sphere of morals as well as in that of religion. We may be sure, therefore, whatever contempt may be thrown upon metaphysics by those who judge a science not by its own nature, but according to the accidental effects it may have produced, that it can never be completely abandoned, that we must always return to it as to a beloved one who has been for a time estranged, because the questions with which it is engaged relate to the highest aims of humanity, and reason must always labour either to attain to settled views in regard to these, or to destroy those which others have already established.","kind":"passage","sequence":19,"address":"92.19"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0020","text":"Metaphysic, therefore—that of nature, as well as that of ethics, but in an especial manner the criticism which forms the propædeutic to all the operations of reason—forms properly that department of knowledge which may be termed, in the truest sense of the word, philosophy. The path which it pursues is that of science, which, when it has once been discovered, is never lost, and never misleads. Mathematics, natural science, the common experience of men, have a high value as means, for the most part, to accidental ends—but at last also, to those which are necessary and essential to the existence of humanity. But to guide them to this high goal, they require the aid of rational cognition on the basis of pure conceptions, which, be it termed as it may, is properly nothing but metaphysics.\n\nFor the same reason, metaphysics forms likewise the completion of the culture of human reason. In this respect, it is indispensable, setting aside altogether the influence which it exerts as a science. For its subject-matter is the elements and highest maxims of reason, which form the basis of the possibility of some sciences and of the use of all. That, as a purely speculative science, it is more useful in preventing error than in the extension of knowledge, does not detract from its value; on the contrary, the supreme office of censor which it occupies assures to it the highest authority and importance. This office it administers for the purpose of securing order, harmony, and well-being to science, and of directing its noble and fruitful labours to the highest possible aim—the happiness of all mankind.","kind":"passage","sequence":20,"address":"92.20"}],"sha256":"23ab1741822a186f4b472f4d1172f71f11da1eb317a12c3e1a5e41b728653273","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP","title":"Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","order":93,"chapter_id":"CH_B337_CIHP","text":"### Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason\n\nThis title is placed here merely for the purpose of designating a division of the system of pure reason of which I do not intend to treat at present. I shall content myself with casting a cursory glance, from a purely transcendental point of view—that of the nature of pure reason—on the labours of philosophers up to the present time. They have aimed at erecting an edifice of philosophy; but to my eye this edifice appears to be in a very ruinous condition.\n\nIt is very remarkable, although naturally it could not have been otherwise, that, in the infancy of philosophy, the study of the nature of God and the constitution of a future world formed the commencement, rather than the conclusion, as we should have it, of the speculative efforts of the human mind. However rude the religious conceptions generated by the remains of the old manners and customs of a less cultivated time, the intelligent classes were not thereby prevented from devoting themselves to free inquiry into the existence and nature of God; and they easily saw that there could be no surer way of pleasing the invisible ruler of the world, and of attaining to happiness in another world at least, than a good and honest course of life in this. Thus theology and morals formed the two chief motives, or rather the points of attraction in all abstract inquiries. But it was the former that especially occupied the attention of speculative reason, and which afterwards became so celebrated under the name of metaphysics.\n\nI shall not at present indicate the periods of time at which the greatest changes in metaphysics took place, but shall merely give a hasty sketch of the different ideas which occasioned the most important revolutions in this sphere of thought. There are three different ends in relation to which these revolutions have taken place.\n\n1. In relation to the object of the cognition of reason, philosophers may be divided into sensualists and intellectualists. Epicurus may be regarded as the head of the former, Plato of the latter. The distinction here signalized, subtle as it is, dates from the earliest times, and was long maintained. The former asserted that reality resides in sensuous objects alone, and that everything else is merely imaginary; the latter, that the senses are the parents of illusion and that truth is to be found in the understanding alone. The former did not deny to the conceptions of the understanding a certain kind of reality; but with them it was merely logical, with the others it was mystical. The former admitted intellectual conceptions, but declared that sensuous objects alone possessed real existence. The latter maintained that all real objects were intelligible, and believed that the pure understanding possessed a faculty of intuition apart from sense, which, in their opinion, served only to confuse the ideas of the understanding.\n\n2. In relation to the origin of the pure cognitions of reason, we find one school maintaining that they are derived entirely from experience, and another that they have their origin in reason alone. Aristotle may be regarded as the head of the empiricists, and Plato of the noologists. Locke, the follower of Aristotle in modern times, and Leibnitz of Plato (although he cannot be said to have imitated him in his mysticism), have not been able to bring this question to a settled conclusion. The procedure of Epicurus in his sensual system, in which he always restricted his conclusions to the sphere of experience, was much more consequent than that of Aristotle and Locke. The latter especially, after having derived all the conceptions and principles of the mind from experience, goes so far, in the employment of these conceptions and principles, as to maintain that we can prove the existence of God and the immortality of the soul—both of them objects lying beyond the limits of possible experience—with the same force of demonstration, as any mathematical proposition.\n\n3. In relation to method. Method is procedure according to principles. We may divide the methods at present employed in the field of inquiry into the naturalistic and the scientific. The naturalist of pure reason lays it down as his principle that common reason, without the aid of science—which he calls sound reason, or common sense—can give a more satisfactory answer to the most important questions of metaphysics than speculation is able to do. He must maintain, therefore, that we can determine the content and circumference of the moon more certainly by the naked eye, than by the aid of mathematical reasoning. But this system is mere misology reduced to principles; and, what is the most absurd thing in this doctrine, the neglect of all scientific means is paraded as a peculiar method of extending our cognition. As regards those who are naturalists because they know no better, they are certainly not to be blamed. They follow common sense, without parading their ignorance as a method which is to teach us the wonderful secret, how we are to find the truth which lies at the bottom of the well of Democritus.\n\nQuod sapio satis est mihi, non ego curo Esse quod  \nArcesilas aerumnosique Solones. PERSIUS  \n—Satirae, iii. 78-79.  \n\nis their motto, under which they may lead a pleasant and praiseworthy life, without troubling themselves with science or troubling science with them.\n\nAs regards those who wish to pursue a scientific method, they have now the choice of following either the dogmatical or the sceptical, while they are bound never to desert the systematic mode of procedure. When I mention, in relation to the former, the celebrated Wolf, and as regards the latter, David Hume, I may leave, in accordance with my present intention, all others unnamed. The critical path alone is still open. If my reader has been kind and patient enough to accompany me on this hitherto untravelled route, he can now judge whether, if he and others will contribute their exertions towards making this narrow footpath a high road of thought, that which many centuries have failed to accomplish may not be executed before the close of the present—namely, to bring Reason to perfect contentment in regard to that which has always, but without permanent results, occupied her powers and engaged her ardent desire for knowledge.","paragraphs":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","text":"### Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason\n\nThis title is placed here merely for the purpose of designating a division of the system of pure reason of which I do not intend to treat at present. I shall content myself with casting a cursory glance, from a purely transcendental point of view—that of the nature of pure reason—on the labours of philosophers up to the present time. They have aimed at erecting an edifice of philosophy; but to my eye this edifice appears to be in a very ruinous condition.\n\nIt is very remarkable, although naturally it could not have been otherwise, that, in the infancy of philosophy, the study of the nature of God and the constitution of a future world formed the commencement, rather than the conclusion, as we should have it, of the speculative efforts of the human mind. However rude the religious conceptions generated by the remains of the old manners and customs of a less cultivated time, the intelligent classes were not thereby prevented from devoting themselves to free inquiry into the existence and nature of God; and they easily saw that there could be no surer way of pleasing the invisible ruler of the world, and of attaining to happiness in another world at least, than a good and honest course of life in this. Thus theology and morals formed the two chief motives, or rather the points of attraction in all abstract inquiries. But it was the former that especially occupied the attention of speculative reason, and which afterwards became so celebrated under the name of metaphysics.","kind":"passage","sequence":1,"address":"93.1"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","text":"I shall not at present indicate the periods of time at which the greatest changes in metaphysics took place, but shall merely give a hasty sketch of the different ideas which occasioned the most important revolutions in this sphere of thought. There are three different ends in relation to which these revolutions have taken place.\n\n1. In relation to the object of the cognition of reason, philosophers may be divided into sensualists and intellectualists. Epicurus may be regarded as the head of the former, Plato of the latter. The distinction here signalized, subtle as it is, dates from the earliest times, and was long maintained. The former asserted that reality resides in sensuous objects alone, and that everything else is merely imaginary; the latter, that the senses are the parents of illusion and that truth is to be found in the understanding alone. The former did not deny to the conceptions of the understanding a certain kind of reality; but with them it was merely logical, with the others it was mystical. The former admitted intellectual conceptions, but declared that sensuous objects alone possessed real existence. The latter maintained that all real objects were intelligible, and believed that the pure understanding possessed a faculty of intuition apart from sense, which, in their opinion, served only to confuse the ideas of the understanding.","kind":"passage","sequence":2,"address":"93.2"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","text":"2. In relation to the origin of the pure cognitions of reason, we find one school maintaining that they are derived entirely from experience, and another that they have their origin in reason alone. Aristotle may be regarded as the head of the empiricists, and Plato of the noologists. Locke, the follower of Aristotle in modern times, and Leibnitz of Plato (although he cannot be said to have imitated him in his mysticism), have not been able to bring this question to a settled conclusion. The procedure of Epicurus in his sensual system, in which he always restricted his conclusions to the sphere of experience, was much more consequent than that of Aristotle and Locke. The latter especially, after having derived all the conceptions and principles of the mind from experience, goes so far, in the employment of these conceptions and principles, as to maintain that we can prove the existence of God and the immortality of the soul—both of them objects lying beyond the limits of possible experience—with the same force of demonstration, as any mathematical proposition.","kind":"passage","sequence":3,"address":"93.3"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","text":"3. In relation to method. Method is procedure according to principles. We may divide the methods at present employed in the field of inquiry into the naturalistic and the scientific. The naturalist of pure reason lays it down as his principle that common reason, without the aid of science—which he calls sound reason, or common sense—can give a more satisfactory answer to the most important questions of metaphysics than speculation is able to do. He must maintain, therefore, that we can determine the content and circumference of the moon more certainly by the naked eye, than by the aid of mathematical reasoning. But this system is mere misology reduced to principles; and, what is the most absurd thing in this doctrine, the neglect of all scientific means is paraded as a peculiar method of extending our cognition. As regards those who are naturalists because they know no better, they are certainly not to be blamed. They follow common sense, without parading their ignorance as a method which is to teach us the wonderful secret, how we are to find the truth which lies at the bottom of the well of Democritus.\n\nQuod sapio satis est mihi, non ego curo Esse quod  \nArcesilas aerumnosique Solones. PERSIUS  \n—Satirae, iii. 78-79.  \n\nis their motto, under which they may lead a pleasant and praiseworthy life, without troubling themselves with science or troubling science with them.","kind":"passage","sequence":4,"address":"93.4"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","text":"As regards those who wish to pursue a scientific method, they have now the choice of following either the dogmatical or the sceptical, while they are bound never to desert the systematic mode of procedure. When I mention, in relation to the former, the celebrated Wolf, and as regards the latter, David Hume, I may leave, in accordance with my present intention, all others unnamed. The critical path alone is still open. If my reader has been kind and patient enough to accompany me on this hitherto untravelled route, he can now judge whether, if he and others will contribute their exertions towards making this narrow footpath a high road of thought, that which many centuries have failed to accomplish may not be executed before the close of the present—namely, to bring Reason to perfect contentment in regard to that which has always, but without permanent results, occupied her powers and engaged her ardent desire for knowledge.","kind":"passage","sequence":5,"address":"93.5"}],"sha256":"dc65df16f7fcb9f6acbb722e9be9052e62e77da188de9676ab25b71e7ef4c87a","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"passage_basis":"frozen-grounding"}],"units":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS","layer":"CH","title":"§ 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.","order":1,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"97c78a1116d88b2b545f670ce0a7ace665d0e2cc4d42ba1877fb817ebb644cee","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:AS1","text":"The problem is to determine how synthetic a priori propositions are possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:AS2","text":"Pure a priori intuitions are identified as the enabling ground of such propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:AS3","text":"Space and time supply elements not contained in the given concept but available a priori in corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:AS4","text":"Synthetic judgment arises by uniting the conceptual content with what intuition contributes beyond the concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:AS5","text":"The validity generated by pure intuition extends only to objects of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:AS6","text":"The solution therefore establishes synthetic a priori cognition while simultaneously limiting it to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:A1","text":"Transcendental philosophy must explain the possibility of synthetic a priori propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:A2","text":"Space and time are pure a priori intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:A3","text":"A pure intuition can contain a priori content not contained in the corresponding concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:A4","text":"Synthetic judgment is produced by uniting conceptual content with a priori intuitive content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:A5","text":"The pure intuitions of space and time condition cognition of objects of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:A6","text":"Judgments grounded in these pure intuitions are valid only for objects of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:A7","text":"The validity of a cognition is determined by the domain in which its enabling intuition can apply.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:NX1","text":"Synthetic a priori judgment is not generated by analysis of the given concept alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:NX2","text":"Pure intuition is not empirical content received from particular experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:NX3","text":"Space and time are not merely contingent properties abstracted from experienced objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:NX4","text":"The cognition enabled by pure intuition does not reach beyond objects of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:NX5","text":"Synthetic a priori judgments are not universally valid for objects independently of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:NX6","text":"The transcendental solution does not establish unrestricted knowledge of objects as they may be apart from sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:D1","text":"A judgment can be a priori while exceeding the content of its subject concept.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A2","CH_B337_1CTS:A3","CH_B337_1CTS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:D2","text":"The possibility of synthetic a priori cognition depends on the prior availability of pure intuitive forms.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A1","CH_B337_1CTS:A2","CH_B337_1CTS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:D3","text":"Intuition supplies the synthetic increment that concepts alone cannot provide.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A3","CH_B337_1CTS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:D4","text":"The same conditions that explain synthetic a priori validity also delimit its object-domain.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A5","CH_B337_1CTS:A6","CH_B337_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:D5","text":"The solution to the general problem is partial because it resolves the possibility of synthetic a priori cognition only within sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A1","CH_B337_1CTS:A5","CH_B337_1CTS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:SD1","text":"The ground of synthetic a priori knowledge is simultaneously the principle of its limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A2","CH_B337_1CTS:A5","CH_B337_1CTS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:SD2","text":"A priori validity does not entail validity for every possible object; it entails validity only within the field constituted by the relevant intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A3","CH_B337_1CTS:A6","CH_B337_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:SD3","text":"Transcendental explanation converts the question of knowledge into a question of enabling conditions and thereby blocks an inference from a priori necessity to metaphysical reach.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A1","CH_B337_1CTS:A2","CH_B337_1CTS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:SD4","text":"The argument establishes a bounded form of objectivity: cognition is objectively valid insofar as objects can be given under the forms of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A5","CH_B337_1CTS:A6","CH_B337_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:DNX1","text":"The system must exclude the possibility that concepts independently contain all grounds required for synthetic expansion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:DNX2","text":"The system must exclude the possibility that a priori cognition derives its content from accumulated empirical encounters.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:DNX3","text":"The system must exclude the possibility that the forms of sensibility provide access to objects outside the conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:DNX4","text":"The system must exclude the identification of a priori necessity with knowledge of things independently of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:DNX5","text":"The system must exclude a conception of transcendental philosophy as an unrestricted theory of all objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:SNX1","text":"Unconditioned object-knowledge is unavailable wherever no corresponding form of possible intuition is supplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses a transition from the formal conditions of appearance to knowledge of reality beyond appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:SNX3","text":"The possibility of synthetic a priori cognition is purchased by the renunciation of absolute cognitive scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:SNX4","text":"The system leaves the status of objects apart from possible experience outside the domain established by the Transcendental Æsthetic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:FGS1","text":"General problem: synthetic a priori propositions require a ground that is both independent of experience and capable of adding content beyond concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:FGS2","text":"Grounding condition: space and time function as pure a priori intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:FGS3","text":"Synthetic production: pure intuition contributes content absent from the given concept and permits its synthetic union with that concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:FGS4","text":"Validity condition: the resulting judgment is valid for objects insofar as they can be given within sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:FGS5","text":"Limiting closure: because the enabling conditions belong to sensibility, the judgment cannot validly extend beyond objects of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:FGS6","text":"Formal machine: Problem → Pure a priori intuition → Excess over concept → Synthetic union → Sensible objectivity → Restriction to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:AF1","text":"Synthetic a priori problem → identification of pure intuitions → explanation of intuitive surplus over concepts → synthesis in judgment → validity for sensible objects → restriction to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:ME1","text":"The source of a priori cognition is relocated from conceptual analysis to the structure of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:ME2","text":"The opposition between a priori necessity and experiential validity is dissolved by distinguishing the source of cognition from the domain of its application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:ME3","text":"The object-field is divided between objects available within possible experience and objects not legitimized by the pure intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:ME4","text":"The scope of transcendental philosophy is redrawn as both explanatory and restrictive: it explains synthetic a priori cognition by specifying its limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:ME5","text":"The conclusion prevents the success of the transcendental account from being converted into a claim of unrestricted metaphysical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:ALT1","text":"Surface — Synthetic a priori judgments are possible because space and time provide a priori intuitive content beyond concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:ALT2","text":"Structural — Cognition is organized by the interaction of conceptual forms with pure forms of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument answers a possibility-question by identifying enabling conditions rather than by extending cognition beyond its conditions of validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects are cognitively accessible only as objects that can be given within spatial and temporal sensibility; the argument therefore presupposes a distinction between appearance-conditioned objectivity and any object considered beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central transition from the problem of synthetic a priori judgment to pure intuition as its ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:MRT2","text":"The system preserves the decisive distinction between what a concept contains and what intuition contributes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:MRT3","text":"The restriction to objects of sense is structurally necessary and not an incidental qualification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:MRT4","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined regarding the detailed constitution of space and time and the full relation between sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:MRT5","text":"The argument is internally coherent insofar as its positive explanation and negative limitation arise from the same transcendental conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:MRT6","text":"Its strongest point is the conversion of limitation into the basis of validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_1CTS:MRT7","text":"Its unresolved boundary is the status of objects or cognition not available within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_1CTS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 17","title":"§ 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic.","context":"Chapter 17"}},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET","layer":"CH","title":"§ 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","order":2,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"a727a74cad562090a32a3fb7ec674546233894715f1594678ec8a5035808cb07","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:AS1","text":"The problem is whether space is a real existence, a relation belonging to things in themselves, or a form belonging to the mind’s intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:AS2","text":"The argument is a metaphysical exposition that seeks what is given a priori in the conception of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:AS3","text":"External experience is shown to presuppose spatial representation rather than generate it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:AS4","text":"Space is established as the necessary a priori condition of external phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:AS5","text":"The unity and infinite extensibility of space establish it as a pure intuition rather than a general concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:AS6","text":"Geometrical necessity is grounded in this a priori intuition and therefore possesses apodeictic certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:A1","text":"External sense represents objects as outside the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:A2","text":"External objects are represented in spatial relations of shape, dimension, separation, and position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:A3","text":"The representation of space precedes the synthesis of sensations as external and separately located.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:A4","text":"Space is a necessary a priori representation and the condition of the possibility of external phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:A5","text":"Space belongs to the form of intuition rather than to things as they are in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:A6","text":"Space is a pure intuition, not a discursive or general conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:A7","text":"There is one all-encompassing space, of which determinate spaces are parts or limitations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:A8","text":"Geometrical principles are grounded in a priori spatial intuition and thereby possess apodeictic certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:A9","text":"Space is represented as an infinite given quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:NX1","text":"Space is not derived from outward experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:NX2","text":"External experience is not possible independently of spatial representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:NX3","text":"Space is not a determination dependent on external phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:NX4","text":"Space is not a discursive or general concept formed by abstraction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:NX5","text":"Space is not composed from multiple independently given spaces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:NX6","text":"Spatial parts do not antecede the one space as components of an aggregate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:NX7","text":"Space is not an object of internal sense, just as time is not an object of external intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:NX8","text":"Space is not a property belonging unrestrictedly to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:NX9","text":"The non-existence of space cannot be represented, although space can be represented as empty of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:D1","text":"Externality and spatial separation are possible only because spatial representation precedes the organization of sensation.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A1","CH_B337_2MET:A2","CH_B337_2MET:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:D2","text":"Space has transcendental validity for phenomena without having independent validity as a property of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A4","CH_B337_2MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:D3","text":"The possibility of empty space but not spaceless representation distinguishes the formal condition of appearance from the objects occupying that condition.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A4","CH_B337_2MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:D4","text":"Spatial multiplicity is derivative because determinate spaces arise through limitation of one prior space.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A6","CH_B337_2MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:D5","text":"Geometry cannot derive its first principles from general concepts of lines and figures.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A6","CH_B337_2MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:D6","text":"The infinite divisibility and extendibility of space indicate that its original representation is intuitive rather than conceptual.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A6","CH_B337_2MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:D7","text":"The source of spatial representation cannot be located in the empirical relations that spatial representation itself makes possible.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A3","CH_B337_2MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:SD1","text":"External objectivity is relocated from an independently spatial world to the subject’s form of sensibility while remaining valid for all possible external appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A1","CH_B337_2MET:A4","CH_B337_2MET:A5","CH_B337_2MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:SD2","text":"Geometrical necessity is neither empirical induction nor knowledge of things in themselves; it is necessity imposed by the form structuring possible external experience.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A4","CH_B337_2MET:A5","CH_B337_2MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:SD3","text":"The system produces a one-to-many spatial ontology in which determinate regions emerge through limitation of a single infinite intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A6","CH_B337_2MET:A7","CH_B337_2MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:SD4","text":"The distinction between empty space and the impossibility of spaceless representation separates formal precondition from empirical occupancy.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A4","CH_B337_2MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:SD5","text":"The account must distinguish transcendental subjectivity from private arbitrariness, since spatial form is subjective in origin but universal in its validity for external experience.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A4","CH_B337_2MET:A5","CH_B337_2MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:DNX1","text":"No pre-spatial external given can serve as the ground of spatial representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:DNX2","text":"No independently existing absolute container of objects is admitted as the ground of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:DNX3","text":"Space cannot be treated as one object among the objects that appear within it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:DNX4","text":"Spatial plurality cannot be fundamental; it must result from delimitation within unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:DNX5","text":"Geometrical necessity cannot be grounded in induction, abstraction, or accumulated empirical comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:DNX6","text":"The spatial form of appearance cannot be extended without qualification to reality considered apart from appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:DNX7","text":"The ultimate origin of the mind’s spatial form is not supplied as an empirical or object-level explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:SNX1","text":"No standpoint outside sensibility is available from which space could be compared with things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:SNX2","text":"No external world already articulated spatially and independently of representation is admissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:SNX3","text":"Space cannot be given as a positive object whose own spatial nature could be investigated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:SNX4","text":"The system secures the function and necessity of spatial form without fully disclosing why the subject has this form of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:FGS1","text":"Competing accounts of space are posed: independent reality, relation belonging to things in themselves, or form of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:FGS2","text":"The possibility of representing sensations as external, separate, and differently located requires a prior representation of space.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A1","CH_B337_2MET:A2","CH_B337_2MET:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:FGS3","text":"Because external experience presupposes space, space is established as an a priori condition rather than an empirical product.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A3","CH_B337_2MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:FGS4","text":"Because space remains representable without objects but not replaceable by the absence of space, it functions as the form of external appearance rather than as its empirical content.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A4","CH_B337_2MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:FGS5","text":"Because there is one space and determinate spaces are only its limitations, space is identified as a pure intuition rather than a general concept.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A6","CH_B337_2MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:FGS6","text":"Because every part of space can be extended indefinitely, space is identified as an infinite given quantity.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A6","CH_B337_2MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:FGS7","text":"Because geometry operates through this a priori intuition, its principles possess apodeictic certainty rather than merely empirical generality.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A6","CH_B337_2MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:AF1","text":"Competing ontological hypotheses → metaphysical exposition → antecedence of spatial representation → necessary a priori condition of external experience → pure intuition through unity → infinite given quantity → apodeictic geometry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:ME1","text":"The source of spatiality is shifted from empirical relations to the conditions that make empirical relations possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:ME2","text":"Space is reclassified from an independently existing container or property into the form of external intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:ME3","text":"The opposition between subjective form and objective validity is reorganized: space is subject-dependent in origin but universally valid for appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:ME4","text":"The plurality of spaces is reclassified as regional limitation within one infinite space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:ME5","text":"Geometry is reclassified from empirical generalization or conceptual deduction into the articulation of a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:ME6","text":"The domain of spatial predicates is restricted to phenomena rather than things considered in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, external objects are represented through spatial shape, dimension, separation, and position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, space functions as the prior condition organizing external sensation and determining the possibility of spatial experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, the inquiry is redirected from the nature of space as an object to the conditions under which spatial objects can appear and geometry can operate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:ALT4","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, space and time are assigned distinct domains of sensibility: space structures external intuition, while time structures internal determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central movement from the failure of empiricism to the a priori status of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:MRT2","text":"The system is internally coherent if transcendental validity for phenomena is distinguished from applicability to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:MRT3","text":"Its strongest steps are the antecedence argument and the unity-versus-concept distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:MRT4","text":"The relation between subjective constitution and universal geometrical validity remains functionally specified rather than genetically explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:MRT5","text":"The argument compresses the transition from the inability to represent spacelessness to the necessity of space as a priori condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_2MET:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real sequence: ontological alternatives → a priori condition → pure intuition → infinite unity → geometrical apodeicticity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_2MET:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 09","title":"§ 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","context":"Chapter 09"}},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC","layer":"CH","title":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.","order":3,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"c61a4a662d02cecea9c6b22268ca98fdab8c05771d05479f5d89a83b427aea38","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AS1","text":"The problem is to determine what representation of space makes geometrical synthetic a priori cognition possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AS2","text":"A transcendental exposition explains a conception by exhibiting it as the principle of other synthetic a priori cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AS3","text":"Geometry exceeds mere conceptual analysis while retaining apodeictic necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AS4","text":"Its possibility therefore requires an a priori intuition rather than a mere concept or empirical perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AS5","text":"This intuition can precede objects only as a formal capacity belonging to the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AS6","text":"Space is consequently the form of external sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AS7","text":"Any rival explanation is inadequate if it cannot render the possibility of synthetic a priori geometry comprehensible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A1","text":"A transcendental exposition must explain a conception as the principle of possible synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A2","text":"The cognitions derived from the conception must actually flow from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A3","text":"The cognitions must be possible only under the proposed mode of explaining the conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A4","text":"Geometry produces synthetic cognitions concerning space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A5","text":"Geometrical cognitions are a priori and apodeictically necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A6","text":"A mere concept cannot yield propositions that extend beyond the concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A7","text":"The representation grounding geometry must therefore be an intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A8","text":"Because geometrical cognition precedes perception of objects, its grounding intuition must be a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A9","text":"An a priori intuition must be pure rather than empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A10","text":"An intuition preceding objects can exist in the human mind only as a condition belonging to the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A11","text":"The subject’s relevant condition is its formal capacity to be affected by objects and thereby obtain immediate representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A12","text":"Space is the form of external sense in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:A13","text":"An explanation of space is adequate only if it makes synthetic a priori geometry possible and comprehensible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:NX1","text":"Space is not represented merely through a concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:NX2","text":"Geometrical principles are not empirical judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:NX3","text":"Geometrical principles are not conclusions derived from empirical judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:NX4","text":"The intuition grounding geometry is not empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:NX5","text":"The required intuition is not produced by prior perception of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:NX6","text":"External intuition prior to objects is not explained as an independently existing object-content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:NX7","text":"A rival account is not sufficient merely because it resembles the proposed explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:NX8","text":"An explanation that fails to establish the possibility of geometry is not a transcendental explanation of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:D1","text":"Geometry requires an intuitional ground because its propositions extend beyond the content of concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A4","CH_B337_3TEC:A6","CH_B337_3TEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:D2","text":"The necessity of geometrical principles excludes their derivation from experience.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A5","CH_B337_3TEC:A8","CH_B337_3TEC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:D3","text":"The intuition of space must be available before particular objects are perceived.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A4","CH_B337_3TEC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:D4","text":"The pre-objective availability of spatial intuition requires that space belong to the subject’s mode of receptivity.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A8","CH_B337_3TEC:A10","CH_B337_3TEC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:D5","text":"Space is external not as an independently given thing but as the form through which external objects can be immediately represented.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A10","CH_B337_3TEC:A11","CH_B337_3TEC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:D6","text":"The proposed account explains geometry by connecting synthetic extension with a priori intuition rather than with conceptual analysis or empirical abstraction.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A6","CH_B337_3TEC:A7","CH_B337_3TEC:A9","CH_B337_3TEC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:D7","text":"Competing explanations can be assessed by whether they preserve the possibility of synthetic a priori geometrical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A1","CH_B337_3TEC:A3","CH_B337_3TEC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:SD1","text":"The possibility of objective geometrical knowledge is grounded in a subjective form of receptivity.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A5","CH_B337_3TEC:A7","CH_B337_3TEC:A10","CH_B337_3TEC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:SD2","text":"The distinction between subject and object is reorganized: objects are externally encountered only through a spatial form supplied by the subject.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A10","CH_B337_3TEC:A11","CH_B337_3TEC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:SD3","text":"The necessity of geometry is transferred from objects as independently given entities to the a priori conditions under which objects can be represented.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A5","CH_B337_3TEC:A8","CH_B337_3TEC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:SD4","text":"Transcendental explanation functions as a criterion of explanatory adequacy rather than as a description of the psychological origin of spatial representation.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A1","CH_B337_3TEC:A3","CH_B337_3TEC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:SD5","text":"Spatial form is both subject-dependent in origin and universally valid for the representation of external objects.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A10","CH_B337_3TEC:A11","CH_B337_3TEC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:DNX1","text":"Geometry cannot be grounded in a world whose spatial structure is simply copied through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:DNX2","text":"Synthetic a priori necessity cannot be explained by accumulation, abstraction, or generalization from perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:DNX3","text":"Space cannot be treated as a property inferred from already constituted objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:DNX4","text":"The subject cannot be reduced to a passive receiver of spatial determinations originating wholly in objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:DNX5","text":"The validity of geometry cannot depend on an explanation that leaves the source of its necessity unexplained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:DNX6","text":"The account cannot separate the form of intuition from the possibility of external object-representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:SNX1","text":"The text excludes a space existing for cognition independently of the conditions through which a subject can represent objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:SNX2","text":"It excludes a purely conceptual rationalism incapable of generating synthetic content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:SNX3","text":"It excludes an empiricism incapable of accounting for apodeictic necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:SNX4","text":"It excludes a complete separation between the form of externality and the subject’s receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:SNX5","text":"It leaves unavailable any account in which geometry is necessary while its enabling representation remains contingent or unexplained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:FGS1","text":"Synthetic extension beyond concepts + geometrical necessity → demand for an intuitional ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:FGS2","text":"Geometrical necessity + independence from perception → demand for a pure a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:FGS3","text":"Pure a priori intuition preceding objects → localization in the subject’s formal receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:FGS4","text":"Formal receptivity to external objects → space as the form of external sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:FGS5","text":"Space as the form of external sense → intelligibility of synthetic a priori geometry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:FGS6","text":"Failure to generate this intelligibility → rejection of the competing explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AF1","text":"Synthetic a priori geometry → insufficiency of conceptual derivation → requirement of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AF2","text":"Apodeictic necessity → insufficiency of empirical derivation → requirement of purity and a priority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AF3","text":"Pre-objective intuition → localization in the subject → formal capacity of external receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AF4","text":"Formal external receptivity → space as form of external sense → possibility of geometry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:AF5","text":"Possibility of geometry → criterion for distinguishing the transcendental explanation from rival accounts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:ME1","text":"Space is reclassified from an independently encountered content into a condition of external representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:ME2","text":"Geometry is reclassified from an empirical or conceptual science into a science grounded in pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:ME3","text":"Subjectivity is reclassified from a merely receptive endpoint into the source of the formal condition of receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:ME4","text":"Necessity is relocated from observed spatial regularities to the a priori form through which objects can be represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:ME5","text":"Rival theories are measured by generative explanatory power rather than by superficial similarity to the proposed account.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:ALT1","text":"Surface — Geometry contains synthetic, a priori, and apodeictically necessary propositions concerning space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:ALT2","text":"Structural — Such propositions require a pure intuition that precedes empirical objects and makes spatial determination possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The correct explanation of space is selected by its capacity to generate the possibility of geometry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Space is not treated as an object prior to subjects but as an a priori form of the subject’s external receptivity, while objects are encountered through it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the problem of geometry to the conception of space as pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between conceptual, empirical, and intuitional grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:MRT3","text":"The formal schema preserves the text’s decisive transition from synthetic extension to subjective form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent if the subject’s formal receptivity is accepted as the ground of external intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:MRT5","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the further status of objects apart from their spatial representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:MRT6","text":"The argument compresses the justification of the subject-form claim into the failure of alternative explanations to account for geometry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_3TEC:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the real argumentative movement: geometry → intuition → a priority → subjectivity → external sense → explanatory validation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_3TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 10","title":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space.","context":"Chapter 10"}},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC","layer":"CH","title":"§ 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.","order":4,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"db041e76c1ff8387104a98c38a2ab718678eb42576d01113f9c35299211fef9a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AS1","text":"The argument addresses whether space belongs to objects independently of the conditions under which they are intuited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AS2","text":"It rejects space as an intrinsic property or relation of things considered in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AS3","text":"It reconstructs space as the a priori form of human external sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AS4","text":"It derives the possibility of spatial synthetic a priori cognition from the priority of receptivity over actual perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AS5","text":"It distinguishes the empirical reality of space for appearances from its transcendental ideality regarding things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AS6","text":"It limits spatial universality to objects as externally intuited phenomena and excludes sensations and noumenal objects from equivalent status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AS7","text":"The argument is transcendental-critical and proceeds from rejected realism through subjective conditions to a dual account of spatial objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A1","text":"Space is not a determination belonging to objects considered as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A2","text":"Space is the form of all phenomena of external sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A3","text":"Space is the subjective condition under which external intuition is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A4","text":"The subject’s receptivity to affection precedes actual intuition of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A5","text":"A form that precedes actual perception can be given a priori as pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A6","text":"Every object externally intuited by the subject must be determined within space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A7","text":"The conditions of sensibility ground only the possibility of phenomena, not the possibility of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A8","text":"Space has empirical reality with respect to all possible external experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A9","text":"Space has transcendental ideality when objects are considered independently of the constitution of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A10","text":"Spatial judgments possess unrestricted validity only when their objects are specified as external phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A11","text":"Space is the only subjective representation referring to externality from which synthetic a priori propositions can be derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A12","text":"Sensations such as colour, sound, and heat are subjective changes rather than intuitions that by themselves cognize objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:A13","text":"The thing in itself corresponding to an object represented in space cannot be known through that spatial representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:NX1","text":"Space is not an intrinsic property of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:NX2","text":"Space is not a relation among things considered in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:NX3","text":"Space is not a condition governing the existence of things independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:NX4","text":"Space has no determinate meaning when the condition of external sensibility is withdrawn.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:NX5","text":"Spatial validity does not extend without qualification to all things or to every possible subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:NX6","text":"Colour, taste, sound, and heat are not adequate analogies for the ideality of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:NX7","text":"Sensations are not objective a priori representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:NX8","text":"An object represented in space is not thereby known as a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:NX9","text":"Empirical understanding must not convert a phenomenon into a noumenal object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:NX10","text":"External objectivity is not equivalent to mind-independent spatial ontology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:D1","text":"Space can be represented before actual experience because it is supplied by the subject’s prior receptivity rather than abstracted from perceived objects.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A3","CH_B337_4CFC:A4","CH_B337_4CFC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:D2","text":"Spatial relations can ground synthetic a priori principles because every externally intuited object is received through the same spatial form.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A5","CH_B337_4CFC:A6","CH_B337_4CFC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:D3","text":"The judgment that objects occupy space is universally valid when its objects are restricted to external phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A6","CH_B337_4CFC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:D4","text":"The same spatial representation can be empirically objective for experience and transcendentally non-objective with respect to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A7","CH_B337_4CFC:A8","CH_B337_4CFC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:D5","text":"Withdrawing the conditions of sensibility removes the applicability of space without invalidating external experience within those conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A3","CH_B337_4CFC:A7","CH_B337_4CFC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:D6","text":"Sensory qualities cannot establish the ideality of space because they do not independently constitute object-directed intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A11","CH_B337_4CFC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:D7","text":"The spatially represented object is knowable only as phenomenon, while its real correlate remains inaccessible through spatial cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A8","CH_B337_4CFC:A9","CH_B337_4CFC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:D8","text":"Claims about the spatial form of other thinking beings remain undecidable because the universality established concerns the constitution of human sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A3","CH_B337_4CFC:A7","CH_B337_4CFC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SD1","text":"Objectivity is relocated from independence from the subject to universal validity within the subject’s conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A3","CH_B337_4CFC:A8","CH_B337_4CFC:A9","CH_B337_4CFC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SD2","text":"Spatial science is secured as knowledge of appearances while being denied access to objects considered independently of appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A6","CH_B337_4CFC:A8","CH_B337_4CFC:A9","CH_B337_4CFC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SD3","text":"The argument makes universality conditional without reducing it to individual variation: spatial laws are universal for human sensibility but not asserted for every possible knower.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A3","CH_B337_4CFC:A7","CH_B337_4CFC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SD4","text":"The distinction between phenomenon and thing in itself functions as a prohibition against treating the structure of representation as the structure of reality independently considered.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A1","CH_B337_4CFC:A7","CH_B337_4CFC:A9","CH_B337_4CFC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SD5","text":"The system preserves empirical externality by denying that empirical externality entails transcendental realism.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A7","CH_B337_4CFC:A8","CH_B337_4CFC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SD6","text":"The argument establishes a two-level ontology of cognition in which spatial determination is necessary for experienced objects but insufficient for determining objects in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A2","CH_B337_4CFC:A6","CH_B337_4CFC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:DNX1","text":"The system cannot be grounded in an unrestricted ontology of independently spatial things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:DNX2","text":"The human form of external intuition cannot be elevated into the condition of possibility of things as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:DNX3","text":"Externality cannot be defined independently of the subject’s mode of receiving objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:DNX4","text":"No removal of subjective conditions can yield a more adequate spatial representation of the thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:DNX5","text":"The distinction between appearance and thing in itself cannot be collapsed into a distinction between illusion and reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:DNX6","text":"Individual differences in sensation cannot be used to undermine the a priori status of spatial form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:DNX7","text":"The system cannot claim that every possible subject must possess the same spatial form as human sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:DNX8","text":"The unknown correlate of representation cannot be converted into a further object of experience by conceptual abstraction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SNX1","text":"A perspective-free spatial world is unavailable as an object of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SNX2","text":"The thing in itself is required as the limiting correlate of appearance but is excluded from positive spatial knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SNX3","text":"No subject-independent criterion is available for determining whether other forms of intuition share the human form of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SNX4","text":"The argument forecloses the possibility of deriving the conditions of appearance from the objects that appear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SNX5","text":"The system leaves inaccessible the positive reality corresponding to spatially represented objects once the conditions of sensibility are abstracted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:SNX6","text":"The possibility of knowing objects without the mediating structure of sensibility is treated as inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:FGS1","text":"The failure of intrinsic and relational determinations of things in themselves initiates a transcendental analysis of the subject’s receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:FGS2","text":"Prior receptivity supplies a stable form before particular objects are perceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:FGS3","text":"That prior form is identified as pure spatial intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:FGS4","text":"Pure spatial intuition orders every object that can be externally intuited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:FGS5","text":"Universal spatial ordering generates synthetic a priori principles for external experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:FGS6","text":"Restricting judgments to external phenomena converts conditional assertions into universally valid judgments within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:FGS7","text":"Abstracting from sensibility removes the applicability of space to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:FGS8","text":"The resulting structure yields empirical reality for appearances and transcendental ideality for things considered independently of appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:FGS9","text":"The exclusion of sensations and noumenal objects prevents the system from extending spatial a priori cognition beyond its legitimate domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AF1","text":"The argument moves from the rejection of space as an intrinsic determination of things in themselves to the analysis of the subject’s receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AF2","text":"Receptivity is reframed as the source of a form that precedes actual perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AF3","text":"The prior form is identified with pure intuition and made the condition of all external object-intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AF4","text":"The condition of intuition is used to explain synthetic a priori spatial cognition and qualified universal judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AF5","text":"The argument then separates empirical reality from transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:AF6","text":"It closes by excluding sensations as equivalent forms and by denying spatial access to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ME1","text":"Space is reclassified from an intrinsic feature of reality into the form under which external reality can appear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ME2","text":"The opposition between realism and subjectivism is displaced by the distinction between empirical reality and transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ME3","text":"Universal validity is indexed to a condition of judgment rather than asserted without qualification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ME4","text":"The external object is divided into its phenomenal presentation and its unknowable thing-in-itself correlate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ME5","text":"Sensory qualities are separated from formal intuition and thereby removed as explanatory models for spatial ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ME6","text":"The domain of legitimate spatial knowledge is redrawn around possible human external experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ME7","text":"The claim that objects are external no longer entails that their spatial form belongs to them independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ALT1","text":"Surface: External objects are spatially ordered, but only insofar as they are objects of external intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ALT2","text":"Structural: The ordering of objects is generated by the subject’s a priori form of sensibility rather than by properties abstracted from objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument converts disputes about the reality of space into a distinction between the domain of possible experience and the domain of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ALT4","text":"Ontological: Spatially cognizable objects are phenomena, while the thing in itself remains a necessary but unknowable correlate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:ALT5","text":"Epistemic: Synthetic a priori cognition is possible only because the subject contributes the form within which external objects can be encountered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the rejection of transcendental realism to the critical account of space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the decisive distinction between empirical reality and transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined regarding the positive nature of the thing in itself, since it defines that correlate primarily through epistemic exclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:MRT4","text":"Its internal coherence depends on maintaining the difference between subjective origin and objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:MRT5","text":"Its strongest point is the explanation of how spatial judgments can be universally valid without describing things independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:MRT6","text":"A latent tension remains between calling space objectively valid and locating its source entirely in the subject’s form of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:MRT7","text":"A second tension remains between the universal validity of spatial form for human experience and the undecidability of spatial form for other thinking beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_4CFC:MRT8","text":"The argument relies on compression at the point where receptivity is used to establish pure intuition and its synthetic a priori consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_4CFC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 11","title":"§ 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions.","context":"Chapter 11"}},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET","layer":"CH","title":"§ 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","order":5,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"8f13ec21e722acb219fa770513605850027b9e5add55e028aab51d0bfb1773d7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:AS1","text":"The problem is to determine whether time is derived from experience or makes experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:AS2","text":"Time is reframed from an empirical abstraction into an a priori condition of sensuous representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:AS3","text":"The ground of temporal judgment is the necessary representation of one time underlying all phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:AS4","text":"The apodeictic principles of temporal relation are grounded in this a priori representation rather than extracted from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:AS5","text":"Time is identified as a singular pure intuition rather than a general discursive conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:AS6","text":"The argument moves from transcendental necessity to temporal axioms, then to the singularity, unlimitedness, and part-whole structure of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A1","text":"Time is not an empirical conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A2","text":"The representation of time is an a priori condition of representing coexistence and succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A3","text":"Time is a necessary representation underlying all sensuous intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A4","text":"Phenomena cannot be represented apart from time, whereas time can be represented without phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A5","text":"Time is the universal condition under which the reality of phenomena is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A6","text":"The fundamental relations of time possess apodeictic validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A7","text":"Temporal axioms cannot be derived from experience because experience supplies neither strict universality nor apodeictic certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A8","text":"Temporal principles are rules through which experience is possible and therefore instruct us concerning experience rather than originating from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A9","text":"Time is not a discursive general conception but a pure form of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A10","text":"Different times are parts of one and the same time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A11","text":"The non-coexistence of different times is immediately contained in the intuition of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A12","text":"Every determinate quantity of time is produced by limiting one underlying unlimited time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A13","text":"The complete representation of time cannot be furnished by concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:A14","text":"Concepts require immediate intuition as their basis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:NX1","text":"Time is not learned from empirical succession or coexistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:NX2","text":"Time is not removable from the representation of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:NX3","text":"Time is not itself annihilable as phenomena are annihilable in thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:NX4","text":"Temporal necessity is not reducible to the regularity of common experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:NX5","text":"Time is not a discursive or general conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:NX6","text":"Different times are not coexistent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:NX7","text":"The complete representation of time is not constructed from partial conceptual representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:NX8","text":"Concepts do not independently provide the immediate basis of temporal representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:D1","text":"Coexistence and succession are representable only because time is presupposed as their common ground.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:D2","text":"The possibility of temporal experience depends on a condition that experience itself cannot establish.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A2","CH_B337_5MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:D3","text":"The annihilation of all phenomena in thought does not eliminate the temporal condition of their possible representation.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A4","CH_B337_5MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:D4","text":"The principles that time has one dimension and that different times succeed rather than coexist are necessary rules of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A6","CH_B337_5MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:D5","text":"The synthetical character of temporal succession prevents its derivation from conceptual analysis alone.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A9","CH_B337_5MET:A11","CH_B337_5MET:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:D6","text":"Temporal parts are not independently existing times but determinations within a single temporal intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A9","CH_B337_5MET:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:D7","text":"Determinate temporal quantities presuppose an unlimited temporal whole that they delimit.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:D8","text":"Conceptual determination of time remains dependent on intuitive givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A13","CH_B337_5MET:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:SD1","text":"Time functions as a transcendental medium: it is not one object within experience but the condition that permits objects to be temporally related at all.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A2","CH_B337_5MET:A3","CH_B337_5MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:SD2","text":"The distinction between a priori condition and empirical content establishes a one-way dependence in which phenomena depend on time while time does not depend on phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A4","CH_B337_5MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:SD3","text":"Temporal necessity is secured by relocating the source of universality from observed regularity to the form of intuition itself.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A6","CH_B337_5MET:A7","CH_B337_5MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:SD4","text":"The unity of time makes temporal plurality a derivative articulation rather than a plurality of independently given temporal entities.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A9","CH_B337_5MET:A10","CH_B337_5MET:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:SD5","text":"The argument imposes a limit on conceptual knowledge: temporal cognition requires conceptual determination, but its ultimate ground remains intuitive rather than conceptual.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A13","CH_B337_5MET:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:DNX1","text":"Experience is not self-grounding with respect to the temporal form through which it is organized.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A2","CH_B337_5MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:DNX2","text":"Temporal order is not an external relation imposed upon otherwise non-temporal phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A3","CH_B337_5MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:DNX3","text":"The unity of time is not assembled from independently given temporal units.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A9","CH_B337_5MET:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:DNX4","text":"Infinity is not an independently completed temporal object presented alongside finite times.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:DNX5","text":"Conceptual universality does not constitute the primary mode in which time is given.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A9","CH_B337_5MET:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:DNX6","text":"Temporal necessity is not metaphysical knowledge of time as a substance existing independently of representation.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A3","CH_B337_5MET:A5","CH_B337_5MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:SNX1","text":"There is no experience of phenomena outside the temporal form that conditions their appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A2","CH_B337_5MET:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:SNX2","text":"There is no temporal plurality whose members possess fully independent existence apart from one underlying time.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A10","CH_B337_5MET:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:SNX3","text":"There is no complete conceptual capture of time that would dispense with intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A13","CH_B337_5MET:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:SNX4","text":"There is no empirical route from observed temporal regularity to apodeictic temporal necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A6","CH_B337_5MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:FGS1","text":"Empirical representations of coexistence and succession require a prior temporal form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:FGS2","text":"The prior temporal form is necessary for every sensuous intuition and can be represented without phenomenal content.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A2","CH_B337_5MET:A3","CH_B337_5MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:FGS3","text":"Because time conditions experience without being derived from it, temporal relations acquire universality and apodeictic necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A5","CH_B337_5MET:A6","CH_B337_5MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:FGS4","text":"Because temporal relations are synthetical and immediately intuited, time is classified as a pure intuition rather than a general concept.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A9","CH_B337_5MET:A11","CH_B337_5MET:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:FGS5","text":"Because time is one intuition, its distinct moments are parts produced through limitation of one unlimited temporal whole.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A10","CH_B337_5MET:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:FGS6","text":"Because conceptual representations are partial, the determination of time remains dependent on immediate intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A13","CH_B337_5MET:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:AF1","text":"Empirical appearance of coexistence and succession → presupposition of time → a priori necessity → apodeictic temporal principles → pure intuition → unified unlimited time → determinate times through limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:AF2","text":"Failure of empirical derivation → transcendental grounding → distinction between intuition and concept → restriction of conceptual construction by immediate givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:ME1","text":"Temporal order is reclassified from an observed feature of phenomena into the condition of their possible observation.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A2","CH_B337_5MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:ME2","text":"The opposition between experience and necessity is displaced: experience becomes possible only through a non-empirical rule of temporal ordering.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A6","CH_B337_5MET:A7","CH_B337_5MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:ME3","text":"The distinction between intuition and concept becomes decisive for explaining why temporal unity cannot be reduced to conceptual generality.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A9","CH_B337_5MET:A13","CH_B337_5MET:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:ME4","text":"Temporal multiplicity is mapped as limitation within unity rather than coexistence of independent temporal wholes.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A10","CH_B337_5MET:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:ME5","text":"The boundary of temporal cognition is relocated from lack of empirical data to the irreducibility of intuition as its ground.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A13","CH_B337_5MET:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:ALT1","text":"Surface — Time orders phenomena as coexistence and succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:ALT2","text":"Structural — Time is the necessary form through which sensuous experience and temporal judgment become possible.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A2","CH_B337_5MET:A3","CH_B337_5MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument defeats empirical and conceptual derivations by shifting the question from the origin of temporal content to the condition of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A7","CH_B337_5MET:A9","CH_B337_5MET:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Time is treated neither as an empirical object nor as a substance but as the singular, unlimited form within which phenomenal reality can appear.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A5","CH_B337_5MET:A9","CH_B337_5MET:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the rejection of empirical derivation to the a priori status of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:MRT2","text":"The system preserves the transition from necessary representation to apodeictic temporal principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:MRT3","text":"The distinction between pure intuition and general conception is indispensable for reconstructing the argument’s middle step.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:MRT4","text":"The relation between unlimited time and determinate temporal quantities preserves the final part-whole argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:MRT5","text":"The system is internally coherent within a transcendental framework, provided “reality” is restricted to phenomenal reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:MRT6","text":"The principal unresolved tension is that time is described as both the condition of all phenomenal reality and as something representable without phenomena, leaving its status relative to non-phenomenal reality unelaborated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction depends on accepting the distinction between empirical content and a priori form rather than independently proving that distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_5MET:MRT8","text":"The extracted structure preserves the argument’s real sequence: transcendental necessity → apodeictic validity → intuitive singularity → unlimited unity → determinate limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_5MET:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 12","title":"§ 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception.","context":"Chapter 12"}},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC","layer":"CH","title":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.","order":6,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"aba7a3e83994c7f5a9d5c12a44156ea6d337573d3daf6ef01f232fb15777500c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:AS1","text":"The possibility of change requires an account of how opposed determinations can belong to one and the same object successively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:AS2","text":"Change is reframed as a temporal relation rather than as a relation intelligible through concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:AS3","text":"Time is established as an internal a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:AS4","text":"The temporal ordering of opposed determinations makes change conceptually comprehensible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:AS5","text":"The same condition explains the possibility of synthetic a priori knowledge concerning motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:AS6","text":"The argument proceeds from the intelligibility of change to the transcendental status of time and then to the scope of a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:A1","text":"Change consists in the succession of opposed determinations in one and the same object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:A2","text":"Motion is change understood as change of place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:A3","text":"Change and motion are possible only through and within the representation of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:A4","text":"Time is an internal a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:A5","text":"Concepts alone cannot render the possibility of change comprehensible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:A6","text":"Temporal succession permits opposed determinations to belong to one object without simultaneous contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:A7","text":"The possibility of synthetic a priori knowledge concerning motion depends on the representation of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:NX1","text":"Time is not derived from experience of change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:NX2","text":"Change is not intelligible through concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:NX3","text":"Contradictory determinations are not jointly instantiated in one object at the same time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:NX4","text":"Motion is not conceptually independent of temporal representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:NX5","text":"Synthetic a priori knowledge of motion is not possible without an a priori intuition of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:D1","text":"If change is possible only through time, then time is a condition of the possibility of change rather than a feature inferred from changing objects.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A1","CH_B337_6TEC:A3","CH_B337_6TEC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:D2","text":"If opposed determinations are ordered successively, their opposition becomes compatible with the identity of the object across different temporal positions.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A1","CH_B337_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:D3","text":"If motion is a species of change, then the intelligibility of motion inherits its dependence on time.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A2","CH_B337_6TEC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:D4","text":"The a priori representation of time supplies the formal basis for knowledge that combines determinations synthetically rather than merely analyzing concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A4","CH_B337_6TEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:D5","text":"The general doctrine of motion is possible as synthetic a priori knowledge because temporal succession makes change intelligible.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A6","CH_B337_6TEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:SD1","text":"Time functions as the transcendental medium in which identity through alteration becomes possible.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A1","CH_B337_6TEC:A3","CH_B337_6TEC:A4","CH_B337_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:SD2","text":"The argument converts the problem of explaining motion into the problem of identifying the a priori condition that orders opposed determinations.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A2","CH_B337_6TEC:A5","CH_B337_6TEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:SD3","text":"The possibility of synthetic a priori knowledge is tied to the formal structure of intuition rather than to the isolated power of conceptual thought.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A4","CH_B337_6TEC:A5","CH_B337_6TEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:DNX1","text":"The intelligibility of change does not originate in the empirical observation of successive states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:DNX2","text":"Temporal succession cannot be reduced to a logical relation among predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:DNX3","text":"The identity of an object across change is not secured by excluding all opposed determinations, but by assigning them different temporal positions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:DNX4","text":"A priori knowledge of motion cannot be grounded exclusively in formal conceptual analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:SNX1","text":"The argument excludes a timeless account of alteration in which opposed determinations would be intelligible without temporal ordering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:SNX2","text":"The argument excludes the reduction of time to an empirical sequence abstracted from observed changes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:SNX3","text":"The argument leaves no room for synthetic knowledge of motion that lacks an intuitive, a priori ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:FGS1","text":"Opposed determinations are attributed to one object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:FGS2","text":"Their compatibility is secured by ordering them successively rather than simultaneously.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:FGS3","text":"The ordering condition is identified as time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:FGS4","text":"Time is characterized as an internal a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:FGS5","text":"Change becomes intelligible through this temporal form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:FGS6","text":"Motion inherits the same condition as a form of change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:FGS7","text":"The formal possibility of synthetic a priori knowledge concerning motion follows from the temporal condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:AF1","text":"Contradictory determinations in one object → temporal succession → time as internal a priori intuition → intelligibility of change → intelligibility of motion → synthetic a priori knowledge of motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:ME1","text":"Change is reclassified from an unexplained conjunction of predicates into a succession structured by time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:ME2","text":"Time is repositioned from an apparent feature of changing objects to a condition of their alterability and cognizability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:ME3","text":"The distinction between conceptual analysis and intuitive synthesis becomes decisive for explaining a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:ME4","text":"Motion is incorporated into the transcendental analysis of cognition rather than treated as an independently intelligible empirical phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:ALT1","text":"Surface — Change and motion involve successive differences in the determinations or locations of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:ALT2","text":"Structural — Temporal succession mediates the relation between opposed determinations and preserves their attribution to one object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The explanation of motion is shifted from conceptual description to the identification of its condition of possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Time is presupposed as an internal a priori form through which alteration and succession can be represented at all.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the passage’s central argument from change to time to synthetic a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between conceptual comprehension and intuitive temporal ordering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the full relation between internal time and objects considered independently of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:MRT4","text":"The argument is internally coherent insofar as temporal succession resolves the apparent contradiction between opposed determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:MRT5","text":"Its strongest move is the dependence of motion on the prior possibility of temporal representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_6TEC:MRT6","text":"Its main compression lies in asserting, rather than fully demonstrating here, why time must have the structure of an a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_6TEC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 13","title":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time.","context":"Chapter 13"}},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC","layer":"CH","title":"§ 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.","order":7,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"16b4e6208d19cc4003cd06759a99941b45a2d1367ed6936e86acfc79261a0f23","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:AS1","text":"The argument determines whether time is an independently subsisting reality, an objective property of things, or a condition of human intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:AS2","text":"The two objectivist accounts of time fail because they cannot explain time’s a priori precedence over objects or its availability in synthetic a priori judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:AS3","text":"Time is reframed as the subjective form of internal sense through which the mind apprehends itself and its internal states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:AS4","text":"Because every representation belongs to an internal state, time becomes the a priori condition of all phenomena, including external phenomena mediately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:AS5","text":"The argument distinguishes empirical reality, which time possesses with respect to experience, from absolute reality, which time lacks independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:AS6","text":"The conclusion establishes transcendental ideality and restricts temporal validity to phenomena while excluding temporal predicates from things considered in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A1","text":"Time does not subsist independently of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A2","text":"Time does not inhere in things as an objective determination independent of the conditions of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A3","text":"Time is the form of internal sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A4","text":"Internal sense determines the relations among representations in the subject’s internal state rather than the shape or position of outward phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A5","text":"Time is the a priori formal condition of all phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A6","text":"Time conditions internal phenomena immediately and external phenomena mediately through internal representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A7","text":"Every object available in experience is presented under temporal conditions because human intuition is sensuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A8","text":"Time has empirical reality with respect to all objects that can be presented to the senses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A9","text":"Time has transcendental ideality because it is nothing independently of the subjective conditions of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A10","text":"The proposition that all things are in time is valid only when “things” means things as phenomena or objects of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A11","text":"Temporal properties cannot be ascribed to things in themselves on the basis of sensuous experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:A12","text":"The representation of time is itself an intuition whose relations may be represented through the analogy of a one-dimensional line, except that temporal parts succeed rather than coexist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:NX1","text":"Time is not an independently existing object or substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:NX2","text":"Time is not an objective determination inhering in things as they are in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:NX3","text":"Time is not a determination of outward phenomena in the manner of shape or position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:NX4","text":"Time is not absolutely real independently of the mind or subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:NX5","text":"It is not valid without qualification to say that all things are in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:NX6","text":"Time is not a property that can be transferred from phenomena to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:NX7","text":"The transcendental ideality of time cannot be established by analogies that presuppose the objective reality they are meant to prove.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:D1","text":"The failure of independent subsistence and objective inherence requires time to be grounded in the subject’s mode of intuition rather than eliminated as unreal.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A1","CH_B337_7CAC:A2","CH_B337_7CAC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:D2","text":"Time orders representations internally rather than constituting a spatial feature of external objects.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A3","CH_B337_7CAC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:D3","text":"Time has a wider phenomenal scope than space because all representations are internal states, whereas space conditions only external intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A4","CH_B337_7CAC:A5","CH_B337_7CAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:D4","text":"External phenomena are temporally ordered through the mediation of the internal sense.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A5","CH_B337_7CAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:D5","text":"The universality of temporal determination concerns every possible object of experience rather than every conceivable thing without qualification.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A7","CH_B337_7CAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:D6","text":"Time can be empirically objective without being absolutely real.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A8","CH_B337_7CAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:D7","text":"The qualification of objects as phenomena converts the apparently contradictory claims that time is subjective and that all experienced objects are necessarily temporal into compatible claims.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A7","CH_B337_7CAC:A8","CH_B337_7CAC:A9","CH_B337_7CAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:D8","text":"Temporal succession is represented intuitively through a linear schema, but the coexistence of parts in the spatial analogy must be excluded from the concept of time.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:D9","text":"No temporal predicate derived from experience can establish the temporal character of things considered independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A9","CH_B337_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:SD1","text":"The system separates phenomenal objectivity from absolute ontology: time is objectively valid within experience while remaining non-absolute beyond the conditions of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A7","CH_B337_7CAC:A8","CH_B337_7CAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:SD2","text":"Subjectivity is transformed from a limitation on objectivity into the condition through which phenomenal objectivity becomes possible.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A3","CH_B337_7CAC:A5","CH_B337_7CAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:SD3","text":"The temporal structure of external experience is not independently discovered in external things but generated through the internal form of representation.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A4","CH_B337_7CAC:A6","CH_B337_7CAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:SD4","text":"The distinction between phenomena and things in themselves functions as the system’s boundary against metaphysical extension of experiential categories.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A9","CH_B337_7CAC:A10","CH_B337_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:SD5","text":"The argument makes time universally constitutive for experience while denying it universal ontological scope.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A5","CH_B337_7CAC:A7","CH_B337_7CAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:SD6","text":"The line analogy makes temporal order representable without reducing succession to spatial coexistence.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:DNX1","text":"The system excludes any standpoint from which time could be known apart from the conditions of sensuous intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A3","CH_B337_7CAC:A9","CH_B337_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the identity of phenomenal temporality with the intrinsic constitution of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A8","CH_B337_7CAC:A9","CH_B337_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:DNX3","text":"The system excludes a conception of objectivity that is independent of the subject’s mode of access to objects.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A2","CH_B337_7CAC:A3","CH_B337_7CAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the inference from necessary temporal experience to an absolute temporal order of reality.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A7","CH_B337_7CAC:A9","CH_B337_7CAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:DNX5","text":"The system excludes any non-sensuous presentation of objects as a basis for human temporal cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A7","CH_B337_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:SNX1","text":"A subject-independent time governing things in themselves is rendered unavailable as a legitimate object of human knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A9","CH_B337_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:SNX2","text":"The possibility of an unconditioned temporal ontology is foreclosed by the restriction of temporal validity to phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A8","CH_B337_7CAC:A9","CH_B337_7CAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:SNX3","text":"The argument denies access to reality stripped of the conditions through which reality can appear.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A3","CH_B337_7CAC:A9","CH_B337_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:SNX4","text":"The absolute “outside” of temporal intuition cannot be represented as a temporal domain without reinstating the conditions the argument excludes.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A9","CH_B337_7CAC:A10","CH_B337_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:FGS1","text":"The argument begins by testing whether time can be either an independently subsisting reality or an objective determination inherent in things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:FGS2","text":"The objectivist alternatives are rejected because independent subsistence lacks a corresponding object of perception, while inherence in things cannot explain time’s a priori precedence or synthetic a priori applicability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:FGS3","text":"Time is therefore assigned to the form of internal sense, where it orders the subject’s representations and internal states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:FGS4","text":"Because every representation belongs to an internal state, the condition of internal sense extends to all phenomena, making time immediate for inner phenomena and mediate for outer phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:FGS5","text":"The universal temporal structure of experience is then separated from absolute reality by distinguishing phenomenal objects from things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:FGS6","text":"The resulting system preserves empirical objectivity within experience, establishes transcendental ideality beyond experience, and blocks arguments that presuppose the absolute reality they seek to demonstrate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:AF1","text":"Independent subsistence or objective inherence → failure of objectivist accounts → internal-sense grounding → universal phenomenal applicability → empirical reality → transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:AF2","text":"Unqualified temporal ontology → phenomenal qualification of objects → restriction of temporal predicates to experience → exclusion of things in themselves → rejection of presuppositional analogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:ME1","text":"Time is reclassified from an object or inherent property into a condition of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:ME2","text":"The opposition between subjective validity and objective validity is replaced by the distinction between phenomenal objectivity and absolute reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:ME3","text":"External temporality is redescribed as mediately conditioned by internal intuition rather than as an independently given feature of outward things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:ME4","text":"The scope of time is made universal across phenomena, while the scope of space remains restricted to external phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:ME5","text":"Metaphysical claims about things in themselves are separated from valid claims about objects of sensuous experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:ME6","text":"The line analogy becomes a representational schema for succession rather than evidence that time is spatial or externally objective.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:ALT1","text":"Surface: The explicit issue is whether time exists independently, inheres in things, or belongs to the subject’s intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:ALT2","text":"Structural: The argument establishes internal sense as the mediating mechanism through which temporal order applies to both inner and outer phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument resolves the conflict between subjectivity and objectivity by redefining objectivity as validity within the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Time is empirically real for phenomena but transcendently ideal because no temporal determination is available for things in themselves independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from rejection of absolute time to the distinction between empirical reality and transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:MRT2","text":"The reconstructed system preserves the central sequence from objectivist alternatives through internal sense to phenomenal universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:MRT3","text":"The strongest structural point is the compatibility of empirical objectivity with transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:MRT4","text":"Internal coherence depends on maintaining the distinction between objects as phenomena and things considered in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:MRT5","text":"The system leaves underdeveloped why internal sense must have the asserted formal structure and how the claim of a priori validity is itself established beyond the stated regress against objectivism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:MRT6","text":"A latent tension remains between the subjectivity of time and its necessary objectivity for all experience, although the distinction between levels is designed to contain it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_7CAC:MRT7","text":"A secondary tension remains between representing time through an external spatial line and denying that temporal succession is reducible to spatial coexistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_7CAC:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 14","title":"§ 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions.","context":"Chapter 14"}},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU","layer":"CH","title":"§ 8. Elucidation.","order":8,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"99d1d94a361ed5198533efc0c7dcd33143a555c824b905446a37725406205cb7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AS1","text":"The reality of change appears to require the absolute reality of time because change is experienced only as temporal succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AS2","text":"The argument relocates time from an independently real object to the necessary form of inner intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AS3","text":"Time is empirically real because all inner experience is necessarily temporally ordered, although it lacks reality as a property of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AS4","text":"The argument is transcendental-diagnostic: it explains the conditions that make temporal experience and synthetic a priori cognition possible while restricting their validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AS5","text":"Space and time ground mathematics as pure forms of intuition, yet their applicability is limited to sensuous phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AS6","text":"The transcendental ideality of space and time avoids both the reification of empty absolute entities and the empiricist reduction that dissolves mathematical necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AS7","text":"The system closes by excluding motion and change from the a priori contents of transcendental æsthetic because each requires empirical perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A1","text":"Change is possible for human cognition only under the representation of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A2","text":"Time is the form of internal intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A3","text":"Time has empirical reality as a necessary condition of inner experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A4","text":"Time has no absolute or transcendental reality independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A5","text":"The temporal succession of representations expresses the form of inner sense rather than an objective temporal determination of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A6","text":"Every phenomenon has a thing-in-itself aspect whose nature remains problematic and an appearing aspect determined by subjective intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A7","text":"Space and time are pure forms of all sensible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A8","text":"Pure forms of intuition make synthetic a priori cognition possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A9","text":"The objective validity of space and time extends only to objects considered as sensuous phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A10","text":"Empirical certainty remains valid even if space and time belong only to the constitution of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A11","text":"Treating space and time as independently subsisting entities reifies them as empty infinite containers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A12","text":"Treating space and time as empirical relations abstracted from experience eliminates the apodeictic validity of mathematics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A13","text":"Motion requires empirical perception of something movable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:A14","text":"Change requires empirical perception of an existing object and the succession of its determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:NX1","text":"Time is not a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:NX2","text":"Time is not an object represented independently of the subject that intuits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:NX3","text":"Time is not an objective determination inhering in things as they are in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:NX4","text":"The reality of inner experience is not evidence that time possesses absolute reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:NX5","text":"The immediacy of consciousness is not access to the self as a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:NX6","text":"Space and time are not valid for objects outside the sphere of possible sensuous experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:NX7","text":"Space and time are not self-subsisting substances or containers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:NX8","text":"Space and time are not imaginative abstractions derived from empirical relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:NX9","text":"Motion and change are not pure a priori data of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:D1","text":"The actuality of experienced change establishes the empirical reality of time without establishing its absolute reality.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A1","CH_B337_8ELU:A2","CH_B337_8ELU:A3","CH_B337_8ELU:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:D2","text":"Inner experience is temporally necessary for human cognition while remaining incapable of proving that time belongs to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A2","CH_B337_8ELU:A5","CH_B337_8ELU:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:D3","text":"Mathematical cognition can be synthetic and a priori because spatial and temporal relations are given through pure intuition rather than inferred from experience.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:D4","text":"Mathematical necessity applies objectively to appearances because appearances are necessarily received under spatial and temporal forms.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:A8","CH_B337_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:D5","text":"The ideality of space and time does not weaken empirical science because empirical objects are constituted within the very forms that ideality identifies.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A3","CH_B337_8ELU:A9","CH_B337_8ELU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:D6","text":"Motion and change cannot enlarge transcendental æsthetic beyond space and time because both presuppose empirical objects given in those forms.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A13","CH_B337_8ELU:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:SD1","text":"The system preserves empirical realism by making appearances fully real for experience while denying metaphysical realism about the forms through which they appear.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A3","CH_B337_8ELU:A4","CH_B337_8ELU:A9","CH_B337_8ELU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:SD2","text":"The same subjective conditions that limit knowledge to phenomena also supply the necessity required for mathematics and empirical lawfulness.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:A8","CH_B337_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:SD3","text":"Absolute realism and empiricist relationalism are presented as reciprocal failures because one preserves mathematical scope by hypostatizing forms, while the other avoids hypostatization by sacrificing mathematical necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A11","CH_B337_8ELU:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:SD4","text":"The distinction between form and thing in itself converts an apparent ontological question about time into a transcendental question concerning conditions of representation.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A2","CH_B337_8ELU:A4","CH_B337_8ELU:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:DNX1","text":"No direct intuition of temporal order as a feature of things in themselves is available to human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:DNX2","text":"Consciousness of an inner state does not escape the phenomenal conditions that govern consciousness itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:DNX3","text":"Mathematical necessity cannot originate from experience if it is to retain apodeictic validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:DNX4","text":"Subjective form is not arbitrary psychological invention, because it necessarily structures every possible human intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:DNX5","text":"No cognition can legitimately transfer the forms of sensibility beyond the conditions under which objects are given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:SNX1","text":"The temporal character of things in themselves is foreclosed as an object of determinate knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:SNX2","text":"A non-sensible mode of intuition is invoked only as a limiting possibility and remains unavailable as a standpoint for human knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:SNX3","text":"The argument excludes any unconditioned standpoint from which the relation between representation and thing in itself could be positively known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:FGS1","text":"Experienced change → requirement of temporal ordering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:FGS2","text":"Temporal ordering → form of inner intuition rather than property of objects in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:FGS3","text":"Form of intuition → empirical reality of temporal experience and denial of absolute temporal reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:FGS4","text":"Pure forms of intuition → possibility of synthetic a priori mathematics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:FGS5","text":"Phenomenal restriction → objective validity within experience and prohibition of transcendent application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:FGS6","text":"Comparison with substantialism and empiricism → transcendental ideality as the position that retains both empirical validity and mathematical necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AF1","text":"Reality of change → claim that time must be real.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AF2","text":"Claim of temporal reality → distinction between empirical reality and absolute reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AF3","text":"Distinction between realities → relocation of time into inner intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AF4","text":"Relocation into intuition → restriction of space and time to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AF5","text":"Restriction to phenomena → preservation of mathematics and empirical knowledge without metaphysical hypostatization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:AF6","text":"Exhaustion of pure forms → exclusion of motion and change as empirical concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:ME1","text":"The opposition between the reality and ideality of time is reorganized into empirical reality for appearances and transcendental ideality relative to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:ME2","text":"Inner consciousness is reclassified from privileged metaphysical access to a phenomenal domain governed by the same formal conditions as external appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:ME3","text":"Mathematics is detached from both a realist ontology of empty spatial-temporal entities and an empiricist derivation from observed relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:ME4","text":"Motion and change are reclassified from candidates for pure sensible knowledge into empirical determinations requiring given objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text answers the objection that real change proves the absolute reality of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text distinguishes phenomenal objects from things in themselves and assigns temporal-spatial form to the subject’s sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text preserves the authority of mathematics and experience by restricting, rather than abolishing, their objective domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Time is necessary for human appearance and experience but unavailable as a determinate feature of being independently of human sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central transition from real change to empirically real but transcendentally ideal time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:MRT2","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the restriction of mathematical validity to phenomena and the preservation of its a priori necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:MRT3","text":"The system depends on the distinction between appearances and things in themselves, whose positive relation remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it shows that subjective conditions can be necessary conditions of objective experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:MRT5","text":"The system compresses the transition from necessary human form to universally valid empirical objectivity and does not independently establish that transition here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:MRT6","text":"The counterfactual possibility of non-sensible intuition limits the argument without supplying a positive account of such intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_8ELU:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if empirical reality, transcendental ideality, phenomenal validity, and mathematical necessity remain jointly held rather than treated as separable theses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_8ELU:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 15","title":"§ 8. Elucidation.","context":"Chapter 15"}},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT","layer":"CH","title":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.","order":9,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"9cc95756929ec688c6df98012f301bffda99167943377422ac8838181b6e7c4b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AS1","text":"The problem is to secure necessary synthetic cognition while denying that sensuous cognition reaches objects as they are in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AS2","text":"The text recasts sensuous objectivity as phenomenal representation structured by the subject’s receptive forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AS3","text":"The Leibnitz-Wolfian reduction of sensibility to confused intellection is rejected because it erases the difference in origin and content between intuition and intellectual cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AS4","text":"The necessity of geometry is used transcendentally to establish that space must be an a priori subjective condition of external intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AS5","text":"The relational character of external and internal intuition extends the phenomenal restriction from bodies to the subject’s self-knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AS6","text":"The distinction between phenomenon and illusion preserves empirical objectivity while excluding space and time from things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AS7","text":"The contrast between derivative sensibility and original intellectual intuition confines spatial-temporal intuition to dependent knowers and blocks its application to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A1","text":"Sensibility is a receptive mode of cognition in which the subject is affected by objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A2","text":"Sensuous intuition represents objects only as phenomena relative to the subject’s receptivity, not as they are in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A3","text":"Space and time are necessary a priori forms of human sensibility, while sensation supplies its empirical matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A4","text":"The nature of objects considered independently of sensibility is unknowable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A5","text":"Logical differences of clearness and obscurity do not determine the transcendental origin or content of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A6","text":"Empirical invariance within universal human experience does not establish cognition of a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A7","text":"Necessary and universally valid synthetic geometrical cognition cannot arise from empirical intuition or from concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A8","text":"Pure intuition can ground synthetic cognition of external objects only as a subjective form that universally conditions how such objects are given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A9","text":"Intuition supplies representations of relations rather than cognition of an object’s intrinsic nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A10","text":"Inner intuition orders the subject’s manifold in time through self-affection, so the subject intuits itself as appearance rather than as it is.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A11","text":"Phenomenal predicates are objectively valid for objects as related to sensibility and are not therefore illusory appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:A12","text":"Original intellectual intuition gives its object, whereas sensuous intuition is derivative and dependent upon an object affecting the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:NX1","text":"Sensibility is not a confused or incomplete cognition of objects as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:NX2","text":"The distinction between sensuous and intellectual cognition is not merely a logical difference in clearness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:NX3","text":"An empirically stable object is not thereby a noumenal object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:NX4","text":"Synthetic geometrical necessity is not derivable from empirical experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:NX5","text":"Synthetic geometrical construction is not derivable from concepts without intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:NX6","text":"External objects and the self are not given through sensibility as they are independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:NX7","text":"A phenomenon is not an illusory appearance or a merely deceptive seeming.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:NX8","text":"Space and time are not objective properties or conditions of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:NX9","text":"Human self-consciousness is not an original intellectual intuition of the self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:NX10","text":"The spatial and temporal conditions of sensibility are not predicates admissible of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:D1","text":"Complete empirical clarity can yield only more complete knowledge of human sensibility under space and time, never knowledge of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A2","CH_B337_9GRT:A3","CH_B337_9GRT:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:D2","text":"The transcendental object remains unknown even when an empirical object is universally and consistently determined in experience.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A2","CH_B337_9GRT:A4","CH_B337_9GRT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:D3","text":"Geometrical construction demonstrates the subjective a priori status of space because its necessary synthetic content requires pure intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A3","CH_B337_9GRT:A7","CH_B337_9GRT:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:D4","text":"If the subject’s sensible constitution is abstracted, spatial-temporal objects as represented disappear with space and time themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A2","CH_B337_9GRT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:D5","text":"External sense can disclose only the subject-relative spatial relations of objects, not their intrinsic constitution.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A2","CH_B337_9GRT:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:D6","text":"The subject can cognize itself only as temporally affected by its own activity and not as a spontaneously self-given object.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A1","CH_B337_9GRT:A3","CH_B337_9GRT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:D7","text":"Spatial, temporal, and sensible predicates can be attributed to empirical objects without being attributed to those objects as things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A2","CH_B337_9GRT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:D8","text":"Treating space and time as objective forms of all things would subject God to the conditions of derivative sensibility and convert them into independent entities lacking determinate ontological status.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A3","CH_B337_9GRT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:D9","text":"Even if all finite thinkers shared spatial-temporal intuition, that universality would not convert derivative sensibility into intellectual intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A1","CH_B337_9GRT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:SD1","text":"Transcendental ideality and empirical reality are mutually required: objects are empirically real as appearances precisely because their predicates are restricted to the subject-object relation.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A2","CH_B337_9GRT:A3","CH_B337_9GRT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:SD2","text":"The conditions that make a priori knowledge possible also establish the boundary beyond which such knowledge cannot pass.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A3","CH_B337_9GRT:A4","CH_B337_9GRT:A7","CH_B337_9GRT:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:SD3","text":"The distinction between external objects and the self does not divide noumenal from phenomenal cognition, because both are given only through forms of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A2","CH_B337_9GRT:A9","CH_B337_9GRT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:SD4","text":"The theory reverses ordinary realism: making space and time mind-independent does not secure reality but risks reducing bodies, selves, and time itself to mere appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A3","CH_B337_9GRT:A11","CH_B337_9GRT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:SD5","text":"The object of transcendental investigation is displaced from what things are in themselves to the conditions under which objects can appear and be known.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A2","CH_B337_9GRT:A4","CH_B337_9GRT:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:DNX1","text":"The system depends on the impossibility of comparing appearances with things in themselves from a cognition independent of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:DNX2","text":"The system excludes any inferential passage from empirical completeness, universality, or persistence to noumenal access.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:DNX3","text":"The system excludes an immediately self-present subject that would know itself prior to temporal self-affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a finite cognition that would be both receptive of objects and originarily productive of them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:DNX5","text":"The system excludes unrestricted application of spatial, temporal, and sensible predicates beyond the domain of possible sensuous experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:SNX1","text":"The framework is organized around an unpresentable ground: an object must affect sensibility while remaining unknowable apart from the phenomenal relation produced by that affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:SNX2","text":"The unavailable standpoint is one that could both condition all possible experience and survey the correspondence between appearance and thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:SNX3","text":"The terminally excluded cognition is finite intellectual intuition, in which the knower would possess immediate access to itself and to objects as they are.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:FGS1","text":"Object-affection → receptive sensibility → sensation ordered by space and time → phenomenal intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:FGS2","text":"Phenomenal intuition → relational representation only → exclusion of cognition of intrinsic object-nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:FGS3","text":"Necessary synthetic geometry → insufficiency of concepts and experience → requirement of pure intuition → subjectivity of spatial form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:FGS4","text":"Temporal inner sense → self-affection → phenomenal self-intuition rather than intellectual self-presence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:FGS5","text":"Subjective sensible forms + relational predicates → empirical objectivity without illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:FGS6","text":"Objective-form hypothesis → theological and ontological absurdity → reductive confirmation of transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:FGS7","text":"Derivative intuition contrasted with original intuition → restriction of space and time to dependent knowers → exclusion of their application to God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AF1","text":"Phenomenal restriction of sensibility → rejection of the confused-noumenal model of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AF2","text":"Necessary synthetic geometry → need for pure intuition → space as a subjective a priori condition of external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AF3","text":"Relational content of external and internal intuition → impossibility of intrinsic cognition of bodies and self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AF4","text":"Subjective ideality of forms + relational validity of predicates → empirical reality of phenomena rather than illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AF5","text":"Objective space-time hypothesis → reification of forms and theological contradiction → reductive exclusion of transcendental realism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:AF6","text":"Derivative versus original intuition → finite scope of sensibility → final restriction of the doctrine to dependent cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:ME1","text":"Reality is redistributed into empirically real phenomena and transcendentally unknowable things in themselves rather than divided into reality and mere seeming.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:ME2","text":"The external object and the self are reclassified as differently ordered appearances within one sensible regime.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:ME3","text":"Geometry is reclassified from knowledge of mind-independent spatial objects into evidence for the subjective conditions of possible external experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:ME4","text":"The distinction between essential and accidental sensible features is restricted to empirical comparison and denied transcendental force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:ME5","text":"Natural theology becomes a limit-test for claims about space and time rather than an independent proof of their subjective ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:ALT1","text":"Surface — Space, time, bodies, and the self are represented as phenomena structured through sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:ALT2","text":"Structural — Receptive sensibility combines empirical affection with a priori forms, thereby generating the domain in which objects can appear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — A priori geometry and the reductio of objective space and time are deployed to convert a theory of knowledge into a critique of transcendental realism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Time is the form through which the subject appears to itself, while original intuition marks an unavailable mode of non-dependent cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from receptive sensibility to the phenomenal restriction of objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:MRT2","text":"The proof-sequence is preserved because the necessity of geometry supplies the positive argument and relational intuition supplies its confirming limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as subjective forms explain both a priori validity and the impossibility of noumenal cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:MRT4","text":"The relation by which an unknowable object affects sensibility remains structurally underdetermined, although object-dependence is retained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:MRT5","text":"The claim that relational representation cannot disclose intrinsic nature functions as a governing limit-principle rather than as a separately established deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:MRT6","text":"The inference from geometrical necessity to the subjectivity of space depends on the premise that synthetic construction cannot concern things in themselves unless its intuitive condition belongs to the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:MRT7","text":"The distinction between phenomenon and illusion depends on retaining objective validity within the subject-object relation while refusing noumenal predication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_9GRT:MRT8","text":"The theological contrast closes the system by exposing consequences of the rejected alternative, but it is explicitly presented as illustration rather than proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_9GRT:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 16","title":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic.","context":"Chapter 16"}},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS","layer":"CH","title":"Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20","order":10,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"e02086c8d1897b4fe8060b3ab1d083ec33beebc4f6b1bd5056a48bf68a4db727","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AS1","text":"Pure categories require sensuous intuition to acquire determinate objective application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AS2","text":"The argument is a transcendental deduction from the conditions under which understanding can determine sensibility a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AS3","text":"The unity of apperception grounds intellectual synthesis, but productive imagination mediates that synthesis into sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AS4","text":"The application of categories is thereby secured only for appearances, because human intuition is exclusively sensuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AS5","text":"The apparent paradox of self-affection is resolved by distinguishing the spontaneous understanding from the receptive inner sense it determines.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AS6","text":"Self-consciousness establishes only the thought that the subject exists; determinate self-cognition remains temporal intuition of the self as appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A1","text":"Categories are mere forms of thought and cannot by themselves cognize a determinate object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A2","text":"Human intuition is sensuous rather than intellectual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A3","text":"The synthetic unity of apperception is the ground of a priori cognition insofar as that cognition depends on understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A4","text":"Sensibility supplies a priori forms of intuition while remaining receptive to determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A5","text":"The inner sense contains the form of intuition but no original synthetic conjunction of its manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A6","text":"The understanding is spontaneous and can determine the inner sense in conformity with the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A7","text":"Productive imagination is the spontaneous synthesis through which understanding determines sensibility a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A8","text":"Figurative synthesis is distinct from merely intellectual synthesis because it conjoins sensuous intuition under categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A9","text":"Figurative synthesis confers objective reality on categories only with respect to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A10","text":"Apperception is the source of synthetic conjunction and is not identical with inner sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A11","text":"Cognition of the self requires an intuition that determines the manifold belonging to the thought of the self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A12","text":"The thought “I am” expresses existence without supplying an intuition that determines the mode of that existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A13","text":"Time is represented through successive figurative synthesis and is cognized through figurations and changes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:A14","text":"Reproductive imagination follows empirical association and cannot explain the possibility of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:NX1","text":"Categories are not independently object-cognizing contents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:NX2","text":"Objective validity is not validity for things as they are in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:NX3","text":"Intellectual synthesis is not figurative synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:NX4","text":"Productive imagination is not reproductive association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:NX5","text":"Inner sense is not apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:NX6","text":"Self-consciousness is not self-cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:NX7","text":"The self intuited in inner sense is not the self as it is in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:NX8","text":"The spontaneity of thought is not an intellectual intuition of the spontaneous subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:D1","text":"The categories can apply objectively only where a sensuous manifold can be synthesized under their unity.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A1","CH_B337_ACOS:A2","CH_B337_ACOS:A7","CH_B337_ACOS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:D2","text":"Determinate intuition arises only when the understanding affects receptive inner sense through productive imagination.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A4","CH_B337_ACOS:A5","CH_B337_ACOS:A6","CH_B337_ACOS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:D3","text":"The passivity of inner sense is compatible with self-affection because the determining activity belongs to understanding rather than to inner sense itself.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A4","CH_B337_ACOS:A5","CH_B337_ACOS:A6","CH_B337_ACOS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:D4","text":"The temporal ordering of inner representations is an achieved synthesis rather than a ready-made conjunction found in inner sense.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A5","CH_B337_ACOS:A6","CH_B337_ACOS:A7","CH_B337_ACOS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:D5","text":"The subject is conscious of itself both as the active unity that thinks and as the passive appearance that it intuits.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A6","CH_B337_ACOS:A10","CH_B337_ACOS:A11","CH_B337_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:D6","text":"The assertion of existence in “I am” exceeds mere phenomenal illusion without yielding knowledge of what the existing subject is in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A11","CH_B337_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:D7","text":"Empirical laws of association cannot supply the transcendental ground of necessary a priori synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A3","CH_B337_ACOS:A7","CH_B337_ACOS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:SD1","text":"Objective cognition is redefined as category-governed constitution of possible experience rather than access to objects independently of conditions of receptivity.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A1","CH_B337_ACOS:A2","CH_B337_ACOS:A4","CH_B337_ACOS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:SD2","text":"The same subject necessarily occupies two functional positions: it determines as understanding and is determined as sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A4","CH_B337_ACOS:A6","CH_B337_ACOS:A10","CH_B337_ACOS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:SD3","text":"Self-knowledge is structurally delayed and mediated because the subject can know itself only through the temporal effects of its own synthetic activity.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A5","CH_B337_ACOS:A7","CH_B337_ACOS:A11","CH_B337_ACOS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:SD4","text":"The representation of inner time depends on operations that can be exhibited through outer figuration, making internal cognition dependent on a transposition into spatially representable synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A7","CH_B337_ACOS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:SD5","text":"Transcendental philosophy is bounded away from empirical psychology because its explanatory unit is productive synthesis rather than associative reproduction.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A3","CH_B337_ACOS:A7","CH_B337_ACOS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:DNX1","text":"No cognition can arise from conceptual spontaneity alone without a receptive manifold to be determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:DNX2","text":"No self-presence gives the subject immediate intuition of its own spontaneous activity as it is in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:DNX3","text":"No synthesis is simply found in the inner sense as an already unified datum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:DNX4","text":"No empirical association can bear the necessity attributed to a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:DNX5","text":"No transition from the thought of existence to determinate knowledge of existence is licensed without temporal intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:SNX1","text":"Human cognition excludes intellectual intuition, which would unite the act of determining with direct intuition of the determining subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:SNX2","text":"The subject as it is in itself remains unavailable precisely where self-consciousness seems most immediate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:SNX3","text":"The possibility of a non-phenomenal object of categories is foreclosed by the dependence of all human objective cognition on sensuous givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:FGS1","text":"Category as thought-form + sensuous manifold as given material → need for synthesis capable of determining intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:FGS2","text":"Unity of apperception → rule of necessary conjunction → purely intellectual synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:FGS3","text":"Receptive inner sense + spontaneous understanding → self-affection requiring mediating productive imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:FGS4","text":"Productive imagination + categorical unity → figurative synthesis of sensuous manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:FGS5","text":"Figurative synthesis → determinate appearance → objective reality of categories within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:FGS6","text":"Thought of self + temporal intuition of self-affection → phenomenal self-cognition rather than cognition of the self in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:FGS7","text":"“I am” without determining intuition → consciousness of existence without determinate ontological self-knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AF1","text":"Mere categories without objects → need for sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AF2","text":"Sensuous intuition without original conjunction → need for apperceptive unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AF3","text":"Apperceptive unity without sensible determination → productive imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AF4","text":"Productive imagination → figurative synthesis → categorical validity for phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AF5","text":"Self-affection paradox → distinction between understanding and inner sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:AF6","text":"Temporal self-intuition → cognition of the self as appearance → restriction of self-knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:ME1","text":"Categories are relocated from predicates of independently known being to conditions governing the unity of possible phenomenal objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:ME2","text":"Imagination is reclassified from an ancillary image-making faculty into the transcendental mediator between understanding and sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:ME3","text":"Inner observation is reclassified from direct access to an inward substance into self-affection structured by time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:ME4","text":"The distinction between active subject and passive object is redrawn within one and the same subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:ME5","text":"External figuration becomes an operative condition for representing temporal succession in internal cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:ALT1","text":"Surface — Categories receive application to objects of sense only through figurative synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:ALT2","text":"Structural — Understanding determines receptive inner sense through productive imagination under the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The deduction secures objective validity by restricting it to the conditions of possible experience rather than extending it beyond them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The subject exists as a thinking activity but can determine itself only as temporally appearing to itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the central deduction from the insufficiency of categories alone to their phenomenal application through figurative synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:MRT2","text":"The distinction between apperception, inner sense, and productive imagination is indispensable because its removal collapses the account of self-affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:MRT3","text":"The structure depends on an under-explained mediation: productive imagination must belong to sensibility while exercising the spontaneity of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:MRT4","text":"The claim that “I am” asserts existence while withholding determinate self-knowledge preserves a distinction whose positive warrant is compressed rather than independently demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:MRT5","text":"The proof of temporal self-appearance relies on external line-drawing and perceived alteration, creating a residual dependence of inner cognition on outer representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ACOS:MRT6","text":"The system is coherent if objective cognition is restricted to appearances and self-knowledge is treated as necessarily mediated by sensuous time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ACOS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 38","title":"Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20","context":"Chapter 38"}},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2","layer":"CH","title":"Appendix","order":11,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"103176f31abd6dc069dcc54a11a6e6c85d6d2c95e57b9094b4e50d63d58a1b93","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:AS1","text":"The failure of every speculative inference beyond possible experience creates the problem of how reason’s natural transcendental ideas can have any legitimate employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:AS2","text":"The argument resolves this problem by locating dialectic in the transcendent employment of ideas rather than in the ideas themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:AS3","text":"Transcendental ideas are legitimate only as regulative rules for systematizing empirical cognition, never as constitutive concepts of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:AS4","text":"Reason relates immediately to the understanding and only mediately to objects, projecting totality where the understanding supplies distributive connections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:AS5","text":"The argument is critical-transcendental in its restriction of knowledge, diagnostic in its account of illusion, and reconstructive in its recovery of ideas as heuristic maxims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:AS6","text":"The movement proceeds from dialectical failure through the distinction between idea and employment, then through systematic unity and its transcendental presuppositions, to domain-specific applications and pathologies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:AS7","text":"The terminal position confines speculative knowledge to possible experience while assigning reason the indefinite task of maximizing empirical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A1","text":"Speculative cognition cannot validly extend beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A2","text":"Transcendental ideas arise necessarily from the nature of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A3","text":"Transcendence and dialectic belong to the constitutive misemployment of an idea, not to the idea itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A4","text":"Reason relates immediately only to the understanding and through it mediately to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A5","text":"The proper function of reason is to organize the understanding’s cognitions into maximal systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A6","text":"Transcendental ideas lack sensuous schemata and therefore cannot determine objects in concreto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A7","text":"The legitimate employment of transcendental ideas is regulative and heuristic, arising from reason’s interest in completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A8","text":"A regulative idea projects a maximum of unity as a focus imaginarius rather than presenting a given whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A9","text":"Regulative principles possess objective but indeterminate validity through their indirect governance of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A10","text":"Hypothetical reason can approximate a universal rule through particular cases but cannot thereby prove the rule’s truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A11","text":"Coherent empirical cognition requires the a priori presupposition that nature is systematically unifiable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A12","text":"Systematic inquiry must seek homogeneity among diverse phenomena by subsuming them under higher genera.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A13","text":"Systematic inquiry must seek specification within homogeneous classes by pursuing lower species and differences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A14","text":"Systematic inquiry must seek affinity by treating unity and diversity as connected through progressively mediated transitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A15","text":"Complete systematic unity is an asymptotic target that experience can approach but never exhibit adequately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A16","text":"An ideal object may be posited only relatively and analogically as the schema of systematic unity, never known absolutely as a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:A17","text":"Teleological unity may guide inquiry only under universal natural laws and may never replace physico-mechanical explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX1","text":"A transcendental idea is not a conception of an actual supersensible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX2","text":"Transcendental ideas are not intrinsically dialectical or deceptive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX3","text":"Reason does not directly cognize objects or generate empirical concepts of them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX4","text":"Systematic unity is not a contingent aggregate or an empirically given totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX5","text":"Confirmation by collected cases does not establish the necessary truth of a hypothetical universal rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX6","text":"The principles of systematic unity are not empirical inductions or merely economical devices for reducing intellectual labor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX7","text":"Homogeneity does not authorize the suppression of real differences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX8","text":"Specification does not assert that an actually infinite number of species exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX9","text":"Continuity of forms is not an experienced continuum and supplies no determinate empirical criterion of affinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX10","text":"Categories such as substance, causality, reality, and necessity do not determine objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX11","text":"The absence of contradiction in an ideal object does not prove its real existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX12","text":"A supreme intelligence is not an empirically explanatory cause that may terminate investigation into natural laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:NX13","text":"The maxims of unity and diversity are not competing objective doctrines about the intrinsic constitution of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0028","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D1","text":"Reason systematizes the understanding’s conceptual operations in the same mediated manner that the understanding organizes sensible content.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A4","CH_B337_APP2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D2","text":"The object-like appearance generated by projected totality is unavoidable, although criticism can prevent that appearance from being accepted as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A2","CH_B337_APP2:A3","CH_B337_APP2:A6","CH_B337_APP2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D3","text":"The focus imaginarius enlarges and coordinates empirical inquiry precisely because it lies beyond every attainable empirical standpoint.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A5","CH_B337_APP2:A7","CH_B337_APP2:A8","CH_B337_APP2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D4","text":"Hypothetical inquiry treats the general rule as problematic, uses certain particulars to test its reach, and converts increasing confirmation into approximation rather than proof.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A7","CH_B337_APP2:A10","CH_B337_APP2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D5","text":"The logical demand for unity, differentiation, and affinity can rightfully guide the investigation of nature only by presupposing nature’s transcendental conformity to systematic cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A9","CH_B337_APP2:A11","CH_B337_APP2:A12","CH_B337_APP2:A13","CH_B337_APP2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D6","text":"Empirical concepts and experience are possible only if phenomena exhibit both sufficient similarity for classification and sufficient difference for determination.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A11","CH_B337_APP2:A12","CH_B337_APP2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D7","text":"The conceptual system is regulated by ascent toward higher genera, descent toward lower species, and mediation between differences through affinity.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A12","CH_B337_APP2:A13","CH_B337_APP2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D8","text":"Doctrinal conflicts between unity and diversity become reconcilable when their opposed claims are reclassified as reciprocally limiting maxims of one systematic interest.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A7","CH_B337_APP2:A12","CH_B337_APP2:A13","CH_B337_APP2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D9","text":"The psychological idea organizes internal phenomena as if they belonged to one permanent subject and derived from one fundamental power without establishing a simple soul as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A5","CH_B337_APP2:A6","CH_B337_APP2:A7","CH_B337_APP2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0041","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D10","text":"The cosmological idea directs empirical explanation through an indefinite series of conditions without converting absolute totality or an intelligible beginning into an empirical object.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A1","CH_B337_APP2:A7","CH_B337_APP2:A15","CH_B337_APP2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0042","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D11","text":"The theological idea organizes nature as if its systematic and purposive unity originated in a supreme intelligence while leaving that intelligence unknowable in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A7","CH_B337_APP2:A16","CH_B337_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D12","text":"The available deduction of speculative ideas is functional rather than object-determining: it establishes their necessity for systematic empirical employment without establishing corresponding objects.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A6","CH_B337_APP2:A7","CH_B337_APP2:A9","CH_B337_APP2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D13","text":"Constitutive employment produces ignava ratio by substituting a transcendent soul or divine cause for continued investigation of immanent natural conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A3","CH_B337_APP2:A6","CH_B337_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:D14","text":"Constitutive employment produces perversa ratio by hypostatizing systematic unity as a supreme cause and then deriving from that assumed cause the unity that was presupposed.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A3","CH_B337_APP2:A11","CH_B337_APP2:A16","CH_B337_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0048","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SD1","text":"Critique does not abolish transcendental ideas; it converts failed metaphysical object-claims into rules for empirical method.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A1","CH_B337_APP2:A2","CH_B337_APP2:A3","CH_B337_APP2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SD2","text":"The boundary of possible experience is productive because it blocks transcendent cognition while maximizing the internal extension and coherence of empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A1","CH_B337_APP2:A5","CH_B337_APP2:A7","CH_B337_APP2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SD3","text":"Reason requires functionally object-like schemas while withholding ontological commitment from them, making analogical “as if” representation the central mediating operation.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A6","CH_B337_APP2:A8","CH_B337_APP2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SD4","text":"The subjective interest of reason becomes an objective but indeterminate anticipation that nature will support systematic inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A7","CH_B337_APP2:A9","CH_B337_APP2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SD5","text":"Unity and difference become co-constitutive conditions of conceptual cognition, while continuity functions as their regulative mediation rather than their empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A12","CH_B337_APP2:A13","CH_B337_APP2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SD6","text":"Soul, world-totality, and God become domain-specific projections of one operation: representing unconditioned systematic unity through an ideal object.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A5","CH_B337_APP2:A8","CH_B337_APP2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SD7","text":"Because transcendental illusion arises naturally from the necessary projection of totality, critique must remain a permanent discipline rather than a completed elimination of error.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A2","CH_B337_APP2:A3","CH_B337_APP2:A8","CH_B337_APP2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SD8","text":"The system temporalizes rational inquiry as indefinite convergence toward a completeness that can regulate every stage but can never become present.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A8","CH_B337_APP2:A10","CH_B337_APP2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SD9","text":"Teleology is legitimate only as a heuristic intensification of lawful inquiry; when converted into causal explanation, it becomes either circular or inquiry-terminating.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A3","CH_B337_APP2:A16","CH_B337_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:DNX1","text":"Reason’s natural constitution cannot be ordered toward unavoidable constitutive falsehood, or the recovery of transcendental ideas as legitimate functions would lose its ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:DNX2","text":"Nature cannot be radically heterogeneous, because classification, general concepts, and inference from the general to the particular would become impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:DNX3","text":"Nature cannot be wholly undifferentiated, because determination, species-concepts, and the empirical application of understanding would become impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:DNX4","text":"The demand for systematic unity cannot be merely optional convenience, because its contingency would remove its authority over empirical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:DNX5","text":"Conceptual determinacy cannot survive the removal of the sensible conditions that give categories their content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:DNX6","text":"Heuristic success cannot bridge the gap between the relative realization of an idea and the absolute existence of its imagined referent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0034","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0035","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:DNX7","text":"No empirical series can present the completed unconditioned totality demanded by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:DNX8","text":"Purposive interpretation cannot authorize any exemption from universal natural law or any suspension of mechanical investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SNX1","text":"The absent adequate object of complete systematic unity structures the entire system as its indispensable but unpresentable center.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0040","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SNX2","text":"The ground of the correspondence between reason’s demand for unity and nature’s presumed systematicity remains unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SNX3","text":"The real constitution of the soul, the world as a completed whole, and the supreme ground of nature remain inaccessible behind their regulative schemas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0042","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0051","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SNX4","text":"No determinate criterion fixes how far homogeneity, specification, affinity, or purposiveness extends in nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0054","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SNX5","text":"Final theoretical closure is excluded because every apparent completion must remain open to further unification, differentiation, and mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:SNX6","text":"The foreclosed possibility organizing the argument is speculative knowledge of the unconditioned; beyond possible experience, no cognitively determinable object remains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:FGS1","text":"Reason’s natural demand for unconditioned totality generates transcendental ideas and the temptation to treat them as objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:FGS2","text":"Objectification of an idea combines with the absence of a sensuous schema to produce transcendent inference, illusion, contradiction, or empty predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0055","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:FGS3","text":"Critical analysis relocates the error from the idea to its employment and separates constitutive determination from regulative direction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:FGS4","text":"Regulative redirection establishes the sequence reason → understanding → empirical cognition rather than reason → supersensible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:FGS5","text":"The projected maximum of unity generates the maxims of homogeneity, specification, and affinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:FGS6","text":"These maxims require the transcendental presupposition of nature’s systematicity and thereby acquire indirect, indeterminate objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:FGS7","text":"Relative as-if schemas organize psychological, cosmological, and theological inquiry without extending cognition beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:FGS8","text":"Regulative employment yields indefinite empirical expansion, whereas constitutive relapse yields ignava ratio, perversa ratio, antinomy, or hypostatization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0042","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0048","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:AF1","text":"Dialectical failure → natural origin of transcendental ideas → distinction between idea and employment → reason’s mediation of the understanding → projected systematic unity → transcendental presupposition of nature → relative as-if schemas → diagnosis of constitutive misuse → asymptotic empirical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ME1","text":"Metaphysical claims about supersensible objects are reclassified as methodological rules governing empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ME2","text":"The limit of experience is redrawn from a merely prohibitive boundary into the condition for reason’s legitimate empirical productivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ME3","text":"Objective validity divides into determinate constitutive validity and indirect, indeterminate regulative validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ME4","text":"Reason is repositioned from a faculty that cognizes higher objects to a faculty that systematizes the understanding’s cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ME5","text":"Unity and diversity cease to be rival ontologies and become reciprocally limiting operations within one systematic project.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ME6","text":"Homogeneity, specification, and continuity become anticipatory research rules rather than discovered descriptions of nature’s completed structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ME7","text":"Soul, world-totality, and God are reclassified from knowable entities into ideal schemas of psychological, cosmological, and teleological unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ME8","text":"Teleology and mechanism cease to be alternative causal orders: mechanism governs explanation, while teleology regulates the search for systematic connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ME9","text":"Transcendental illusion is reclassified from an eliminable accident into a permanent structural effect whose authority can be blocked but whose appearance persists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ALT1","text":"Surface — The immediate issue is the legitimate employment of transcendental ideas after their constitutive claims have failed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ALT2","text":"Structural — Sensibility supplies schemata, understanding determines experienced objects, and reason organizes the understanding under projected systematic wholes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0027","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critique converts metaphysical disputes into methodological differences, coordinates opposed maxims, and prevents ideal explanations from terminating empirical research.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0029","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects of knowledge remain phenomenal, unconditioned referents remain unknowable, and rational completion exists only as an indefinitely receding horizon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0042","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the restriction of transcendent cognition, the recovery of regulative ideas, and the threefold application to soul, world, and God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0056","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:MRT2","text":"The exact status of systematic unity remains underdetermined because it is alternately treated as a subjective maxim, an objective but indefinite principle, and a transcendental presupposition of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0039","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent where the distinction between constitutive determination and regulative direction governs every application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:MRT4","text":"The structure is most determinate where the absence of sensuous schemata blocks object-cognition while indirect relation to experience secures heuristic validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:MRT5","text":"The deduction remains compressed because a category-like transcendental deduction is denied while a functional transcendental deduction of regulative employment is nevertheless claimed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0025","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0031","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:MRT6","text":"The distinction between relative and absolute supposition does not fully stabilize the oscillation between requiring the assumption of a supreme being and denying any assertion of its existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0037","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0052","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:MRT7","text":"The principle of continuity remains structurally strained by the admission that natural species are discrete and that no adequate empirical object or criterion of continuity exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:MRT8","text":"The recovery of reason depends on the unproved commitment that a natural cognitive power must possess a proper purpose compatible with truth rather than being constitutively deceptive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APP2:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if the transcendental restriction, regulative recovery, transcendental presupposition, domain applications, and pathologies remain in that dependency order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0033","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APP2:P0046","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 81","title":"Appendix","context":"Chapter 81"}},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX","layer":"CH","title":"Appendix","order":12,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"c280276e8c5aa016d5ed88a23fd41e76e5a643b10462cdd29fef9d028a21e0b4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:AS1","text":"Objective judgments become amphibolous when representations are compared without determining whether they originate in sensibility or understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:AS2","text":"Transcendental reflection assigns representations to their cognitive faculty and thereby supplies the condition of valid objective comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:AS3","text":"The argument is a diagnostic transcendental critique that reconstructs metaphysical error as a confusion between logical comparison and objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:AS4","text":"The four relations of identity and difference, agreement and opposition, internal and external, and matter and form produce different results according to their transcendental place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:AS5","text":"Leibnizian metaphysics is derived from transferring relations valid for pure concepts to phenomena, while Locke exemplifies the inverse reduction of understanding to sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:AS6","text":"The correction restricts objective cognition to phenomena and retains the noumenon only as a problematic marker of the limits of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:AS7","text":"The movement proceeds from unreflected comparison through four tests of cognitive location, then through the dismantling of intellectualized metaphysics, and finally to a classification of empty object-reference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A1","text":"Reflection determines the subjective conditions and cognitive sources under which conceptions are obtained rather than directly determining objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A2","text":"Logical reflection compares conceptions while treating their cognitive origin as homogeneous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A3","text":"Objective comparison and a priori judgment about things require transcendental reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A4","text":"Sensibility and understanding are irreducibly distinct sources of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A5","text":"Objective judgments arise only through the conjunction of sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A6","text":"Identity and difference, agreement and opposition, internal and external, and matter and form constitute the transcendental titles of comparison and distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A7","text":"Within pure understanding, matter as the determinable precedes form as determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A8","text":"Within sensibility, space and time are primitive a priori forms that precede sensations and make experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A9","text":"Difference of spatial position is sufficient to establish the numerical difference of simultaneous phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A10","text":"Phenomenal realities can oppose and cancel one another without logical contradiction between their concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A11","text":"Phenomenal matter is cognized through forces and relations, including determinations that are only comparatively internal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A12","text":"Categories possess objective validity only when objects are given under conditions of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A13","text":"Phenomena alone supply the intuitions through which human cognition acquires objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A14","text":"Things as they may be in themselves are not objects of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A15","text":"Abstraction from a condition of intuition does not establish that the corresponding determination is absent from the object as phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A16","text":"Because apperception and thought precede determinate sensuous representation, a contentless logical form can appear to determine an object in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:A17","text":"The problematic conception of an object in general precedes the division into possible and impossible and is differentiated as something or nothing according to the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX1","text":"Mere logical comparison cannot determine objective identity, opposition, interiority, or form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX2","text":"Sensibility is not a confused or analytically deficient mode of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX3","text":"Neither sensibility nor understanding applies immediately and independently to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX4","text":"A phenomenon is not a representation of a thing in itself differing only by logical confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX5","text":"The identity of indiscernibles is not a law governing objects of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX6","text":"The absence of conceptual contradiction does not establish harmony among phenomenal realities or among alleged noumenal realities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX7","text":"Phenomenal matter is not cognized as an absolutely internal, simple, representation-bearing substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX8","text":"Space and time are not orders or determinations belonging to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX9","text":"Pure categories without sensuous schemata do not constitute objects of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX10","text":"A noumenon is not a positively cognizable object of pure thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX11","text":"The transcendental object cannot be determined as quantity, reality, substance, or any other categorial object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX12","text":"Critique does not authorize the construction of an intelligible world beyond phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX13","text":"What is omitted from a general conception cannot thereby be declared absent from the particulars given under conditions of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:NX14","text":"Privation, pure form, non-instantiated thought, and contradiction are not interchangeable kinds of nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:D1","text":"Failure to assign transcendental place converts formal comparison into amphiboly by substituting an object of pure understanding for a phenomenon.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A2","CH_B337_APPX:A3","CH_B337_APPX:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:D2","text":"The identity of indiscernibles remains an analytical rule for concepts but fails as a law of phenomena because spatial location individuates sensuous objects.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A3","CH_B337_APPX:A6","CH_B337_APPX:A9","CH_B337_APPX:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:D3","text":"Logical compatibility among affirmative concepts cannot exclude real opposition among forces given in sensuous intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A3","CH_B337_APPX:A6","CH_B337_APPX:A10","CH_B337_APPX:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:D4","text":"The inference from conceptual interiority to simple representational monads converts phenomenal relations into closed substances and thereby requires pre-established harmony to coordinate them.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A6","CH_B337_APPX:A11","CH_B337_APPX:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:D5","text":"Treating matter as prior to form intellectualizes space and time by reducing them to orders among substances and their states.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A3","CH_B337_APPX:A6","CH_B337_APPX:A7","CH_B337_APPX:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:D6","text":"Leibnizian intellectualization and Lockean sensualization are inverse forms of the same one-source model of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A4","CH_B337_APPX:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:D7","text":"An object in general detached from sensuous determination supplies no objective employment for the categories.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A12","CH_B337_APPX:A13","CH_B337_APPX:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:D8","text":"A noumenon cognizable through pure categories is impossible for human cognition, while a noumenon understood negatively remains problematic rather than positively possible or impossible.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A12","CH_B337_APPX:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:D9","text":"Understanding limits the objective reach of sensibility without thereby enlarging its own domain beyond phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A12","CH_B337_APPX:A13","CH_B337_APPX:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:D10","text":"Empirical inquiry can penetrate indefinitely into the relational interior of nature without converging upon the non-sensuous ground of appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A11","CH_B337_APPX:A13","CH_B337_APPX:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:D11","text":"The categorical division of nothing yields the empty conception without object as ens rationis, privative absence as nihil privativum, empty intuition without object as ens imaginarium, and the self-annihilating conception as nihil negativum.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SD1","text":"Objective validity becomes jurisdictional: a relation is valid of an object only after the representation has been assigned to its proper cognitive faculty.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A1","CH_B337_APPX:A2","CH_B337_APPX:A3","CH_B337_APPX:A4","CH_B337_APPX:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SD2","text":"The principles of indiscernibility, noumenal harmony, monadology, pre-established harmony, and relational theories of space and time become coordinated outputs of a single amphiboly rather than independent doctrines.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A3","CH_B337_APPX:A6","CH_B337_APPX:A7","CH_B337_APPX:A8","CH_B337_APPX:A9","CH_B337_APPX:A10","CH_B337_APPX:A11","CH_B337_APPX:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SD3","text":"Rationalist intellectualization and empiricist sensualization form a structural symmetry because each abolishes one of the two conditions jointly required for objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A4","CH_B337_APPX:A5","CH_B337_APPX:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SD4","text":"The critical boundary is asymmetrical: sensibility is denied universal ontological reach, but understanding gains no positive intelligible domain in compensation.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A12","CH_B337_APPX:A13","CH_B337_APPX:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SD5","text":"Transcendental illusion is generated when an absence produced by abstraction is converted into a property of the object and a logical form is hypostatized as an ontological determination.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A15","CH_B337_APPX:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SD6","text":"Object-reference becomes graded rather than binary: sensuous instantiation yields objective reality, non-instantiated but noncontradictory thought yields problematic emptiness, privation and pure form yield empty data, and contradiction yields impossibility.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A12","CH_B337_APPX:A13","CH_B337_APPX:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SD7","text":"Temporal progress within experience remains open-ended, while passage from empirical knowledge to knowledge of things in themselves is structurally closed.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A11","CH_B337_APPX:A13","CH_B337_APPX:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:DNX1","text":"Formal homogeneity among representations cannot be assumed to imply ontological homogeneity among their objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:DNX2","text":"Completeness of a concept cannot be assumed to equal completeness of the conditions under which its object is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:DNX3","text":"Thought cannot authorize its own objective application independently of receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:DNX4","text":"Logical noncontradiction cannot be treated as sufficient proof of real possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:DNX5","text":"Sensuous conditions cannot be construed as removable distortions concealing an otherwise directly accessible intelligible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:DNX6","text":"Relationally constituted phenomena cannot be required to possess an absolutely internal substrate cognizable through pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:DNX7","text":"Epistemic limitation cannot be converted either into denial of all non-sensuous reality or into affirmation of a positive noumenal world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SNX1","text":"Human intellectual intuition is absent, and no conceptual operation can replace it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SNX2","text":"The origin of sensibility and the ground of the unity between subjective forms and objective appearance remain inaccessible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SNX3","text":"No category-valid positive account can be given of the non-sensuous ground designated as the transcendental object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SNX4","text":"The intelligible world is foreclosed as a field of cognition while remaining uneliminated as an empty boundary-space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:SNX5","text":"The system admits no standpoint from which appearance can be compared directly with its in-itself ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:FGS1","text":"Given representations → assignment to sensibility or understanding → faculty-specific comparison → objectively valid judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:FGS2","text":"Comparison without source-assignment → homogeneous treatment of representations → transcendental substitution → amphiboly → pretended synthetic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:FGS3","text":"Pure-understanding placement → conceptual identity, non-opposed affirmation, absolute interiority, matter-before-form; sensuous placement → spatial plurality, real opposition, relational interiority, form-before-matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:FGS4","text":"Single-source reduction → Leibnizian intellectualization of phenomena or Lockean sensualization of concepts of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:FGS5","text":"Amphiboly → generalized indiscernibility + denial of real opposition + monadic closure → pre-established harmony + intellectualized space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:FGS6","text":"Critical correction → categories restricted to sensuously given objects → phenomena retained as objectively cognizable → noumena reduced to problematic limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:FGS7","text":"Failed or empty object-reference → categorical differentiation into non-instantiation, privation, pure form, and contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:AF1","text":"Unreflected comparison → ambiguity of cognitive source → requirement of transcendental place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:AF2","text":"Transcendental place → four relations of reflection → divergent determinations according to faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:AF3","text":"Faculty-dependent divergence → diagnosis of Leibnizian amphiboly → collapse of one-source intellectual metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:AF4","text":"Invalid positive noumena → restriction to phenomena → noumenon retained as negative limit → empty reference classified as modes of nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ME1","text":"Objective validity is relocated from the internal form of concepts to the relation between concepts and their conditions of givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ME2","text":"Numerical identity is divided into conceptual identity and sensuously individuated plurality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ME3","text":"Opposition is divided into logical contradiction and real phenomenal cancellation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ME4","text":"Interiority is divided into absolute conceptual interiority and comparatively internal phenomenal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ME5","text":"Matter and form cease to have a single priority-order because intellectual determination and sensuous intuition reverse that order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ME6","text":"Rationalism and empiricism are reclassified as inverse reductions of a two-source cognitive architecture.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ME7","text":"The noumenon is reclassified from a possible metaphysical object into a problematic boundary-concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ME8","text":"Metaphysics is reorganized from cognition of supersensible objects into regulation of the limits of objective use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ME9","text":"Nothing is differentiated into distinct failures of object-reference rather than treated as a single absence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ALT1","text":"Surface: The four conceptions of reflection are tested against Leibnizian doctrines of identity, opposition, monads, harmony, space, and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ALT2","text":"Structural: Objective cognition is generated by the conjunction of two irreducible faculties whose representations require transcendental localization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Transcendental topic functions as a jurisdictional procedure that prevents logical rules from being converted into metaphysical claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Appearance is the exclusive domain of objective human cognition, a priori forms precede experience, empirical inquiry remains indefinitely extensible, and the in-itself remains indeterminate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central sequence from transcendental reflection through the critique of amphiboly to the negative conception of noumena and the division of nothing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:MRT2","text":"The claimed exhaustiveness of the four titles of reflection and of the categorical division of nothing remains asserted rather than independently derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent only if sensibility and understanding remain irreducible yet jointly necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:MRT4","text":"The strongest structural support lies in the repeated demonstration that each reflective relation changes when transferred from pure comparison to sensuous objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:MRT5","text":"The reconstruction of Leibniz compresses several doctrines into consequences of one amphiboly without independently establishing every historical dependency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:MRT6","text":"The designation of the transcendental object as the cause of phenomena uses causal language where categorial determination has simultaneously been prohibited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:MRT7","text":"The requirement to admit noumena negatively while refusing to determine their possibility remains coherent only through a strict distinction between admitting a limit-concept and affirming an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:MRT8","text":"The table of nothing follows from the categorical ordering of empty reference but remains only weakly integrated with the preceding critique of Leibniz.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0026","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_APPX:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the argument’s actual movement from source-confusion to critical restriction rather than merely reproducing its surface sequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_APPX:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 49","title":"Appendix","context":"Chapter 49"}},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA","layer":"CH","title":"All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16","order":13,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"a052bdcb914aeea43564ffa75b195727ecda14c3b2054956393d85d7c41a00a7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:AS1","text":"The problem is how the manifold content of a sensuous intuition can be united in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:AS2","text":"The unity of intuition requires its subordination to the original synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:AS3","text":"The act that brings manifold representations under one apperception is the logical function of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:AS4","text":"The categories are these logical functions insofar as they determine the manifold of a given intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:AS5","text":"Therefore, every manifold given in one empirical intuition is necessarily subject to the categories of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:A1","text":"The manifold content of a sensuous intuition comes under the original synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:A2","text":"The unity of intuition is possible only through its relation to the original synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:A3","text":"The understanding brings the manifold of given representations under one apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:A4","text":"The act of bringing manifold representations under one apperception is the logical function of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:A5","text":"The categories are the logical functions of judgement insofar as they determine the manifold of a given intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:A6","text":"Manifold given in one empirical intuition is determined in relation to a logical function of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:A7","text":"The determination of the manifold in relation to a logical function of judgement brings it into union in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:NX1","text":"The unity of intuition is not possible through the mere givenness of manifold content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:NX2","text":"The unity of consciousness is not produced by sensibility independently of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:NX3","text":"The categories are not independent entities added to intuition from outside the functions of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:NX4","text":"The categories are not merely empirical classifications of already unified intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:NX5","text":"The manifold of intuition is not unified without determination by a logical function of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:NX6","text":"Apperception is not a passive reception of manifold representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:D1","text":"The unity of an intuition presupposes an act that synthesizes its manifold under one consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A1","CH_B337_ASIA:A2","CH_B337_ASIA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:D2","text":"Because synthesis under one apperception is a function of judgement, the unity of intuition has a logical form.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A3","CH_B337_ASIA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:D3","text":"The categories necessarily apply to intuition because they are the forms through which judgement determines intuitive manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A5","CH_B337_ASIA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:D4","text":"The relation between intuition and categories is necessary wherever manifold content is given as one empirical intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A5","CH_B337_ASIA:A6","CH_B337_ASIA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:D5","text":"The unity of empirical intuition is simultaneously a unity of synthesis and a determination according to the functions of judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A2","CH_B337_ASIA:A4","CH_B337_ASIA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:SD1","text":"The categories function as necessary conditions for the intelligible unity of empirical intuition rather than as optional conceptual interpretations.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:D2","CH_B337_ASIA:D3","CH_B337_ASIA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:SD2","text":"The distinction between sensibility and understanding does not prevent their structural cooperation in the constitution of unified experience.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A1","CH_B337_ASIA:A3","CH_B337_ASIA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:SD3","text":"The unity of consciousness is generated through a chain in which synthesis, judgement, and categorical determination are functionally identical at different levels.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A1","CH_B337_ASIA:A4","CH_B337_ASIA:A5","CH_B337_ASIA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:SD4","text":"The argument converts the applicability of categories from a disputed relation between concepts and intuition into a condition of there being one unified empirical intuition at all.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:D1","CH_B337_ASIA:D3","CH_B337_ASIA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:DNX1","text":"The manifold cannot count as one intuition merely by being presented to sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:DNX2","text":"Conscious unity cannot be explained by an undifferentiated relation between representations and an observing subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:DNX3","text":"The logical functions of judgement cannot be confined to abstract thought while leaving intuition structurally untouched.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:DNX4","text":"Categorical determination cannot be treated as posterior to the constitution of unified empirical experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:DNX5","text":"Apperception cannot be reduced to the numerical coexistence of representations without synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:SNX1","text":"The possibility of a unified empirical intuition cannot be grounded in sensuous receptivity alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:SNX2","text":"There is no unified experience prior to the synthesis that relates manifold content to one apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:SNX3","text":"The argument excludes a domain of empirical intuition wholly external to the functions of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:SNX4","text":"The text leaves no admissible account of experience in which categories are dispensable to the unity of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:FGS1","text":"Manifold content is given in sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:FGS2","text":"The manifold is brought under the original synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:FGS3","text":"The bringing-under-one-apperception is identified as an act of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:FGS4","text":"The act of the understanding is identified with the logical function of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:FGS5","text":"The logical functions of judgement become categories when applied to the manifold of a given intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:FGS6","text":"Categorical determination unites the manifold in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:FGS7","text":"Therefore, given empirical intuition entails necessary subjection of its manifold to the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:AF1","text":"Manifold content in intuition → synthetical unity of apperception → function of judgement → categorical determination → unity in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:AF2","text":"Problem of intuitive unity → transcendental condition of apperception → logical structure of synthesis → necessary categorical applicability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:AF3","text":"Given manifold → determination under judgement → union in consciousness → empirical intuition subject to categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:ME1","text":"Intuition is reclassified from mere reception of manifold content into a unity constituted through synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:ME2","text":"The categories are reclassified from abstract logical forms into conditions governing the unity of given intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:ME3","text":"The boundary between sensibility and understanding is redrawn as a functional dependence rather than an absolute separation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:ME4","text":"The question of categorical applicability is relocated from empirical comparison to the conditions of unified consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:ME5","text":"The manifold, apperception, judgement, and categories are organized as successive articulations of one constitutive process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:ALT1","text":"Surface — Manifold content in empirical intuition is subject to the categories of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:ALT2","text":"Structural — Unity of intuition is produced through synthesis under one apperception and determination by judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The necessity of categorical application is established by tracing it to the conditions required for unified experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Experience is constituted as an ordered unity rather than received as a pre-unified manifold; the argument presupposes a transcendental, non-empirical order of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from manifold intuition to categorical determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction depends on retaining the identity between the act of synthesis under apperception and the logical function of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:MRT3","text":"The argument remains underdetermined concerning the detailed derivation of the individual categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent if the necessity of unity in consciousness is accepted as the governing condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:MRT5","text":"Its strongest point is the conversion of categorical applicability into a condition of unified intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:MRT6","text":"Its compressed transition from logical functions of judgement to categories applied to intuition carries the principal argumentative burden.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ASIA:MRT7","text":"The extracted structure preserves the original movement from apperceptive synthesis through judgement to necessary categorical subjection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ASIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 35","title":"All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16","context":"Chapter 35"}},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC","layer":"CH","title":"BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2","order":14,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"d40470ffe6e4464dc42c59b2634b60b8bf312427d2fc47c71cc1197f8ba48967","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:AS1","text":"The inquiry rejects the ordinary analysis of concepts according to their contents as insufficient to explain their a priori status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:AS2","text":"The object of analysis is shifted from concepts to the faculty of understanding that produces them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:AS3","text":"The possibility of a priori conceptions is investigated by locating their source in the understanding itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:AS4","text":"Transcendental philosophy is assigned the task of analysing the pure use of this faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:AS5","text":"Pure conceptions are traced back to their germs in the understanding and followed through their development under experiential occasions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:AS6","text":"The understanding removes empirical conditions from these conceptions and presents them in unalloyed purity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:A1","text":"The ordinary dissection of concepts by their contents is distinct from an analysis of the faculty that generates them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:A2","text":"A priori conceptions must have their birthplace in the understanding itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:A3","text":"The understanding possesses a pure use that can be analysed independently of empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:A4","text":"The possibility of a priori conceptions is established through an inquiry into their source and mode of production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:A5","text":"Transcendental philosophy properly investigates the conditions under which conceptions are possible a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:A6","text":"The logical treatment of conceptions belongs to philosophy in general rather than constituting the whole of transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:A7","text":"Pure conceptions exist in germ within the human understanding before their development through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:A8","text":"Experience occasions the development of pure conceptions without constituting their ultimate origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:A9","text":"The understanding can distinguish and remove empirical conditions attached to conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:A10","text":"A conception becomes unalloyed when its empirical conditions have been removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:NX1","text":"The Analytic of Conceptions is not the ordinary analysis of concepts according to their content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:NX2","text":"The inquiry is not limited to making already-present conceptions clear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:NX3","text":"A priori conceptions are not treated as originating in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:NX4","text":"Transcendental philosophy is not reducible to the logical treatment of conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:NX5","text":"Pure conceptions are not identical with the empirical conditions under which they become manifest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:NX6","text":"The understanding is not treated merely as a passive recipient of conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:D1","text":"The analysis of conceptual content cannot by itself establish the possibility of a priori conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A1","CH_B337_BIAC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:D2","text":"The source of a conception must be distinguished from the experiential occasion through which it is developed.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A2","CH_B337_BIAC:A7","CH_B337_BIAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:D3","text":"The investigation of a priori conceptions requires a genetic analysis of the understanding rather than a merely logical classification of concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A4","CH_B337_BIAC:A5","CH_B337_BIAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:D4","text":"Experience functions as a trigger or occasion for development, not as the constitutive ground of pure conception.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A7","CH_B337_BIAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:D5","text":"Purity is produced through the understanding’s abstraction from empirical conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A3","CH_B337_BIAC:A9","CH_B337_BIAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:D6","text":"The proper object of transcendental analysis is the relation between the understanding’s endogenous resources and their empirical activation.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A2","CH_B337_BIAC:A5","CH_B337_BIAC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:SD1","text":"The text relocates the criterion of a priori validity from the content of concepts to the productive structure of the understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A1","CH_B337_BIAC:A2","CH_B337_BIAC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:SD2","text":"Experience is subordinated to a two-stage role: it occasions the manifestation of conceptions while the understanding supplies their nonempirical ground.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A7","CH_B337_BIAC:A8","CH_B337_BIAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:SD3","text":"Transcendental philosophy becomes a reconstruction of the passage from latent intellectual capacities to purified conceptual forms.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A3","CH_B337_BIAC:A5","CH_B337_BIAC:A7","CH_B337_BIAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:SD4","text":"The distinction between genesis and purification makes possible a conception of knowledge in which empirical development does not erase a priori origin.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A2","CH_B337_BIAC:A8","CH_B337_BIAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:DNX1","text":"The possibility of a priori conceptions cannot depend entirely on their historical or psychological emergence in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:DNX2","text":"The understanding cannot be explained solely through the concepts that appear in consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:DNX3","text":"Empirical manifestation cannot be treated as identical with transcendental constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:DNX4","text":"Clarification of conceptual content cannot substitute for analysis of the conditions that make such content possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:DNX5","text":"Purity cannot be secured by simply reproducing concepts in the form in which experience presents them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:DNX6","text":"The logical order of concepts cannot exhaust the genetic order of their production in the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a model in which experience is the sole origin of every conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a model in which the understanding is only a formal classificatory instrument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:SNX3","text":"The system excludes the possibility that empirical development determines the essential structure of pure conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:SNX4","text":"The argument depends on the absence of any purely external source capable of grounding a priori conceptuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:SNX5","text":"The argument leaves no place for a conception of purity that is given independently of the understanding’s own activity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:FGS1","text":"Ordinary conceptual analysis → insufficiency for establishing a priori status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:FGS2","text":"Insufficiency of content-analysis → displacement of inquiry toward the faculty of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:FGS3","text":"Analysis of the understanding → identification of endogenous germs of conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:FGS4","text":"Endogenous germs + experiential occasions → developmental manifestation of conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:FGS5","text":"Developmental manifestation − empirical conditions → purified conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:FGS6","text":"Purified conception + analysis of its conditions → transcendental account of a priori possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:AF1","text":"Ordinary concept-analysis → failure to ground a priori possibility → faculty-analysis → endogenous origin → experiential development → removal of empirical conditions → pure conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:AF2","text":"Conceptual content → productive faculty → germinal structure → empirical occasion → purification → transcendental determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:ME1","text":"The object of analysis is reclassified from the concept as product to the understanding as generative source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:ME2","text":"The opposition between a priori and empirical is reorganized into a distinction between origin and occasion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:ME3","text":"Conceptual purity becomes an operation performed by the understanding rather than an immediately given feature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:ME4","text":"Transcendental philosophy is separated from general logical analysis by assigning each a different level of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:ME5","text":"The conceptual field is ordered genetically: latent capacity precedes experiential activation, which precedes purification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text defines what is meant by an Analytic of Conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:ALT2","text":"Structural — The argument distinguishes the understanding’s generative activity from the empirical conditions in which conceptions develop.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text redirects philosophical method from dissecting conceptual contents to reconstructing the conditions of their possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — A priori conceptions are treated as germinal structures that precede experience in origin but require experience for development and manifestation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central movement from ordinary conceptual analysis to transcendental analysis of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:MRT2","text":"The system preserves the distinction between a priori origin, empirical occasion, and subsequent purification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:MRT3","text":"The strongest structural point is the separation of the source of conception from the conditions under which conception becomes explicit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:MRT4","text":"The principal underdetermination concerns how the understanding removes empirical conditions and how germinal conceptions become determinate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:MRT5","text":"The system contains a tension between the independence of pure conceptions from experience and their dependence on experience for development.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction remains internally coherent if experience is treated as occasion rather than ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAC:MRT7","text":"The argument’s compactness depends on leaving the mechanism of germination, development, and purification formally unspecified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 23","title":"BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2","context":"Chapter 23"}},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP","layer":"CH","title":"BOOK II. Analytic of Principles","order":15,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"e552eddcbfe60154e10dbf9577e41ad715e53f3c8f01d67e1074ecbab0fa27d9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:AS1","text":"General logic is organized according to the higher faculties of cognition and analyzes their formal operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:AS2","text":"Because general logic abstracts from all cognitive content, it can formulate an a priori canon for the formal use of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:AS3","text":"Transcendental logic concerns the determinate content of pure a priori cognition and therefore cannot reproduce general logic’s division without modification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:AS4","text":"Reason’s transcendental employment seeks cognition of objects beyond possible experience and consequently lacks objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:AS5","text":"Understanding and judgement retain an objectively valid analytical use, while reason’s invalid employment belongs to transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:AS6","text":"The Analytic of Principles therefore becomes a canon for judgement in applying pure concepts of understanding to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:A1","text":"General logic is structured by the faculties of understanding, judgement, and reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:A2","text":"General logic abstracts from the content of cognition and treats only the form of discursive thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:A3","text":"The formal law of reason can be discovered a priori through analysis of reason’s operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:A4","text":"Transcendental logic is restricted to the determinate content of pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:A5","text":"The transcendental employment of reason lacks objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:A6","text":"Objective validity is the condition for belonging to the logic of truth, or analytic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:A7","text":"Understanding and judgement possess objectively valid uses within transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:A8","text":"Reason’s attempt to extend cognition beyond possible experience produces dialectical and illusory assertions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:A9","text":"The Analytic of Principles functions as a canon for judgement rather than as a canon for reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:A10","text":"Pure concepts of understanding supply necessary conditions for establishing a priori laws when applied to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:A11","text":"The designation “Doctrine of the faculty of judgement” specifies the operative purpose of the Analytic of Principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:NX1","text":"Transcendental logic is not a purely formal logic that abstracts from all content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:NX2","text":"Transcendental logic cannot reproduce general logic’s undifferentiated treatment of the faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:NX3","text":"The transcendental employment of reason is not part of the logic of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:NX4","text":"Reason’s a priori assertions about objects beyond possible experience cannot constitute an analytic canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:NX5","text":"The Analytic of Principles is not a doctrine governing reason’s speculative extension of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:NX6","text":"The principles of understanding are not presented as self-sufficient object-cognitions independent of judgement and phenomenal application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:D1","text":"The formal validity of reasoning does not guarantee the objective validity of the objects to which reason applies its form.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A2","CH_B337_BIAP:A5","CH_B337_BIAP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:D2","text":"Transcendental logic must distinguish valid cognition from merely formal coherence because it retains determinate cognitive content.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A2","CH_B337_BIAP:A4","CH_B337_BIAP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:D3","text":"Reason’s formal capacity remains analyzable a priori even though its transcendental employment cannot yield objectively valid cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A3","CH_B337_BIAP:A5","CH_B337_BIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:D4","text":"The division between analytic and dialectic is determined by objective validity rather than by the mere presence of a priori reasoning.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A5","CH_B337_BIAP:A6","CH_B337_BIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:D5","text":"Judgement becomes the mediating faculty through which pure concepts of understanding acquire application to phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A7","CH_B337_BIAP:A9","CH_B337_BIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:D6","text":"The principles of understanding require a doctrine of judgement because their validity depends on their application rather than on isolated possession.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A7","CH_B337_BIAP:A9","CH_B337_BIAP:A10","CH_B337_BIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:SD1","text":"The architecture of transcendental logic is asymmetrical: understanding and judgement generate valid cognition, while reason is retained primarily as the source of transcendental illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A5","CH_B337_BIAP:A7","CH_B337_BIAP:A8","CH_B337_BIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:SD2","text":"The boundary of possible experience functions as the criterion separating legitimate principle-use from speculative overreach.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A5","CH_B337_BIAP:A6","CH_B337_BIAP:A8","CH_B337_BIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:SD3","text":"The validity of pure principles is neither merely formal nor independently metaphysical; it is established through their judged application to phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A2","CH_B337_BIAP:A4","CH_B337_BIAP:A7","CH_B337_BIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:SD4","text":"The Analytic of Principles converts the question of pure cognition into a question of lawful application by a determining faculty.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A4","CH_B337_BIAP:A9","CH_B337_BIAP:A10","CH_B337_BIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:DNX1","text":"Formal rationality alone is insufficient to establish cognition of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:DNX2","text":"A priori cognition is not thereby entitled to exceed the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:DNX3","text":"The faculties of cognition do not possess equal jurisdiction over objective truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:DNX4","text":"Pure concepts do not establish a priori laws without the mediating operation of judgement upon phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:SNX1","text":"No purely formal system can secure objective cognition without reference to determinate conditions of validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:SNX2","text":"No unbounded faculty of reason is admissible as a source of objectively valid knowledge of supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:SNX3","text":"No complete symmetry among understanding, judgement, and reason survives the distinction between analytic validity and dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:FGS1","text":"Faculty division → formal analysis of cognition → specification of the domain of general logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:FGS2","text":"Determinate pure content → restriction of transcendental logic → separation from general logic’s undifferentiated formal method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:FGS3","text":"Reason’s extension beyond possible experience → loss of objective validity → transfer from analytic to dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:FGS4","text":"Objective validity of understanding and judgement → preservation within analytic → formation of a canon for valid application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:FGS5","text":"Pure concepts of understanding + judgement applied to phenomena → necessary conditions for a priori laws → Doctrine of the faculty of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:AF1","text":"Faculty-based organization of logic → distinction between formal and transcendental content → invalidity of reason’s transcendent use → preservation of understanding and judgement as valid faculties → Analytic of Principles as a canon for judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:ME1","text":"The field of logic is divided into formal cognition, transcendental analytic, and transcendental dialectic according to content and objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:ME2","text":"Reason is reclassified from a possible source of valid principles into the locus of dialectical illusion when it exceeds possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:ME3","text":"Judgement becomes the central site where pure conceptual conditions are connected to phenomenal cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:ME4","text":"The opposition between pure concepts and experience is replaced by a mediating structure in which concepts yield laws only through application to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The Analytic of Principles instructs judgement in applying pure concepts of understanding to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Objective validity distributes different jurisdictions among understanding, judgement, and reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument blocks speculative expansion by relocating reason’s transcendent use from analytic certification to dialectical diagnosis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Valid a priori cognition is restricted to the conditions under which objects can appear within possible experience; cognition cannot legitimately outrun that horizon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the distinction between general logic, transcendental analytic, and transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:MRT2","text":"The system is internally coherent if objective validity is treated as the governing criterion of analytic inclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction depends on preserving the distinction between reason’s formal law and reason’s transcendental employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:MRT4","text":"The central unresolved tension is that reason belongs to the general logical division yet cannot receive an equivalent canon within transcendental analytic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:MRT5","text":"A second tension remains between principles attributed to understanding and the designation of the work as a doctrine of judgement; the text resolves this functionally through application to phenomena rather than by collapsing the faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_BIAP:MRT6","text":"The extracted structure preserves the argument’s movement from faculty division, through validity restriction, to the mediating role of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_BIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 41","title":"BOOK II. Analytic of Principles","context":"Chapter 41"}},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE","layer":"CH","title":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18","order":16,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"30ee0d3147e1819ce10f87e6c7dde20a4e98232c5a3e3e5f382477d33fefb00d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AS1","text":"The text separates merely thinking an object from cognizing an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AS2","text":"It establishes cognition as the conjunction of a concept and a corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AS3","text":"Because human intuition is sensuous, concepts acquire cognitive significance only through sensuous givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AS4","text":"It distinguishes pure intuition, which supplies only the formal conditions of phenomena, from empirical intuition, which supplies objects as perceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AS5","text":"It restricts the categories to their function in making empirical cognition, and therefore experience, possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AS6","text":"It tests the boundary through the supposed non-sensuous object and shows that negative predicates and category forms cannot produce knowledge without corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A1","text":"Cognition of an object requires both a conception through which the object is thought and an intuition through which the object is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A2","text":"A conception without a corresponding intuition is only a formally determinate thought and does not yield cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A3","text":"All intuition available to human cognition is sensuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A4","text":"Sensuous intuition is either pure intuition of space and time or empirical intuition involving sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A5","text":"Pure intuition yields a priori cognition only of the formal aspect of objects as phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A6","text":"Objects in space and time are given only as perceptions and therefore only through empirical representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A7","text":"Pure concepts of the understanding produce cognition through pure intuition only insofar as they can be applied to empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A8","text":"Experience is empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A9","text":"The categories function to make empirical cognition possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A10","text":"The only legitimate cognitive use of the categories is their application to objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A11","text":"Space and time are valid only for objects of sense and possess no reality apart from sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A12","text":"The categories formally extend to objects of intuition in general, provided the intuition is sensuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A13","text":"Extending the categories beyond the range of human intuition supplies no cognitive advantage when no corresponding intuition is available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A14","text":"Objective determination requires the application of the synthetic unity of apperception to an intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:A15","text":"Merely specifying what a supposed object is not does not establish either its possibility or cognition of what it is.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:NX1","text":"Thought is not cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:NX2","text":"A category applied without corresponding intuition does not cognize an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:NX3","text":"Pure intuition does not by itself provide cognition of things independently of phenomenal form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:NX4","text":"Mathematical a priori cognition does not establish that independently existing things conform to its forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:NX5","text":"The categories do not cognize things merely through their application to pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:NX6","text":"The categories are not legitimately usable beyond objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:NX7","text":"Space and time do not describe reality independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:NX8","text":"Formal extension of the categories beyond human intuition does not generate objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:NX9","text":"Negative predicates such as “not extended” or “not temporal” do not constitute proper knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:NX10","text":"Supposing a non-sensuously intuited object does not establish the possibility of such an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:NX11","text":"No category is cognitively applicable to an object for which no corresponding intuition can be supplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:D1","text":"A concept can possess logical form without possessing objective cognitive content.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A1","CH_B337_CAOE:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:D2","text":"Since human intuition is sensuous, the cognitive use of concepts is constrained by the conditions under which sensuous objects are given.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A3","CH_B337_CAOE:A4","CH_B337_CAOE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:D3","text":"A priori mathematical cognition determines phenomenal form without establishing the existence of objects independently of that form.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A5","CH_B337_CAOE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:D4","text":"The categories obtain objective significance only when their formal functions are connected to empirical intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A7","CH_B337_CAOE:A9","CH_B337_CAOE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:D5","text":"The restriction of the categories is not a restriction of their conceptual form but of their objectively valid application.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A10","CH_B337_CAOE:A12","CH_B337_CAOE:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:D6","text":"The absence of corresponding intuition prevents the synthetic unity of apperception from determining an object.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A2","CH_B337_CAOE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:D7","text":"A supposed non-sensuous object can be negatively delimited without being positively cognized.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A3","CH_B337_CAOE:A14","CH_B337_CAOE:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:D8","text":"The concept of substance cannot establish its own objective correspondence without an empirical occasion for its application.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A1","CH_B337_CAOE:A2","CH_B337_CAOE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SD1","text":"The system separates the formal scope of a concept from the conditions under which that concept possesses objective reality.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A10","CH_B337_CAOE:A12","CH_B337_CAOE:A13","CH_B337_CAOE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SD2","text":"The categories function as conditions of possible experience rather than as an ontological inventory of all possible beings.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A8","CH_B337_CAOE:A9","CH_B337_CAOE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SD3","text":"Empirical intuition is the terminal criterion for objective significance, while pure intuition supplies only formal conditions of appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A5","CH_B337_CAOE:A6","CH_B337_CAOE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SD4","text":"Mathematical necessity is conditional upon the possibility of objects appearing under the forms of sensibility and is not an independent proof of existence.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A4","CH_B337_CAOE:A5","CH_B337_CAOE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SD5","text":"Negative determination of a non-sensuous object marks an epistemic boundary rather than producing a positive metaphysical description.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A13","CH_B337_CAOE:A14","CH_B337_CAOE:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SD6","text":"The argument does not establish that non-sensuous objects are impossible; it establishes that their possibility and categorial determination cannot be discovered through the available conditions of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A3","CH_B337_CAOE:A13","CH_B337_CAOE:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SD7","text":"The apparent conflict between the general formal reach of the categories and their restricted legitimate use is resolved by distinguishing formal applicability from objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A10","CH_B337_CAOE:A12","CH_B337_CAOE:A13","CH_B337_CAOE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:DNX1","text":"The system depends on the falsity of the assumption that conceptual determination alone can constitute object-directed knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:DNX2","text":"It excludes an intellectual intuition as an available human route to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:DNX3","text":"It excludes the inference from formal conditions of representation to the existence of independently corresponding things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:DNX4","text":"It excludes ontology from being derived directly from the categories without an intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:DNX5","text":"It excludes the transformation of absence of sensory predicates into positive determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:DNX6","text":"It excludes the possibility that category-extension alone can generate objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:DNX7","text":"It excludes the possibility of establishing an object’s possibility without being able to supply an intuition corresponding to its concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:DNX8","text":"It excludes the conversion of a merely thinkable object into a cognized object through logical coherence alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SNX1","text":"Positive knowledge of reality beyond the conditions of sensibility is structurally unavailable to the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SNX2","text":"The possibility of a non-sensuous object remains undecided rather than positively affirmed or denied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SNX3","text":"The transition from conditions of representation to independently existing being is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SNX4","text":"No alternative source of intuition is admitted that could restore objective meaning beyond empirical sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:SNX5","text":"The argument is organized around the absence of a positive intuition capable of filling the concept of an extra-sensory object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:FGS1","text":"Conceptual representation + corresponding intuition → cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:FGS2","text":"Human intuition = sensuous intuition → pure intuition / empirical intuition distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:FGS3","text":"Pure intuition → a priori determination of phenomenal form; empirical intuition → givenness of objects through sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:FGS4","text":"Category applied to pure form alone → formal thought; category connected to empirical intuition → empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:FGS5","text":"Empirical cognition = experience → legitimate category use restricted to objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:FGS6","text":"Extension beyond available intuition → empty conception → failure of objective determination → failure of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:FGS7","text":"Negative description of a non-sensuous object → exclusion of sensible predicates without positive content → no established possibility or category applicability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AF1","text":"Thinking an object → distinguishing thought from cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AF2","text":"Distinction between thought and cognition → concept-plus-intuition criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AF3","text":"Concept-plus-intuition criterion → sensuous restriction of human intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AF4","text":"Sensuous restriction → empirical grounding of objective cognition and limitation of mathematical a priori knowledge to phenomenal form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AF5","text":"Empirical grounding → categories restricted to the production of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:AF6","text":"Attempted extension beyond experience → empty concepts, apophatic predicates, and suspension of objective knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:ME1","text":"Categories are reclassified from general determinations of being into conditions of empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:ME2","text":"The distinction between a priori cognition and empirical givenness becomes a distinction between phenomenal form and object-directed content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:ME3","text":"Intuition, rather than conceptual coherence, becomes the criterion of objective significance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:ME4","text":"The boundary of knowledge is relocated from the internal completeness of concepts to their possible application to intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:ME5","text":"Negative delimitation is separated from positive cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:ME6","text":"The extra-sensory is transformed from a possible field of categorial knowledge into a field of epistemic indeterminacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:ME7","text":"The apparent opposition between the universal scope of categories and their experiential restriction is redrawn as a distinction between formal reach and legitimate use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:ALT1","text":"Surface: The text distinguishes thinking an object from cognizing an object and identifies the two components of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:ALT2","text":"Structural: Cognition is organized by the relation among concept, intuition, sensibility, apperception, and empirical experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument converts metaphysical extension into a question of legitimate application and blocks illegitimate extrapolation from conceptual form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Space and time are treated as conditions of sensuous presentation rather than realities independently valid of sensibility, while non-sensuous being remains undetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central conclusion that the categories have legitimate cognitive use only in relation to objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:MRT2","text":"The extracted structure preserves the movement from concept–intuition duality through sensuousness and empirical givenness to the restriction of category use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent only if formal extension of the categories is distinguished from objectively valid application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:MRT4","text":"The status and possibility of non-sensuous objects remain underdetermined; the argument establishes only the impossibility of cognizing them under the stated conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:MRT5","text":"The strongest structural dependency is the claim that objective determination requires a corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:MRT6","text":"The system depends on the stipulated limits of human sensibility and does not derive those limits within the passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CAOE:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s central fault line: categories possess a wider formal scope than the range within which they can produce cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CAOE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 37","title":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18","context":"Chapter 37"}},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason","order":17,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"6bc4a4caddddf957e7801cc23dc4594af71a2150e13243a6de545b5d653aaefb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:AS1","text":"The argument locates transcendental illusion in dialectical reasoning structured by the formal species of syllogism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:AS2","text":"It shifts from the subjective unity of the thinking subject to the objective unity of conditions within phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:AS3","text":"It claims that reason’s demand for unconditioned unity in objective synthesis necessarily generates contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:AS4","text":"It distinguishes this antinomy from a one-sided paralogistic illusion because both opposed positions acquire apparent rational support.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:AS5","text":"It rejects both despairing scepticism and dogmatic persistence as adequate responses to the conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:AS6","text":"It separates cosmical conceptions from the ideal of pure reason and treats pure rational cosmology as an unvalidated idea irreconcilable with phenomena and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A1","text":"Transcendental illusion in pure reason arises through dialectical arguments whose forms correspond to the formal species of syllogism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A2","text":"The categorical form generates the problem of the unconditioned unity of the subjective conditions of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A3","text":"The hypothetical form generates the problem of the unconditioned unity of objective conditions in phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A4","text":"A third dialectical form concerns the unconditioned unity of the conditions of the possibility of objects in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A5","text":"The transcendental paralogism produces only a one-third illusion concerning the subject of thought and gives no sufficient ground for maintaining the contrary proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A6","text":"In the case of objective synthesis of phenomena, reason establishes its principle of unconditioned unity with considerable plausibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A7","text":"The application of this principle to phenomena generates contradictions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A8","text":"The antinomy is a natural and unavoidable conflict into which reason falls by its own operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A9","text":"The antinomy compels reason toward either despairing scepticism or dogmatic persistence in one-sided assertions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A10","text":"Both scepticism and dogmatic persistence destroy sound philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A11","text":"Cosmical conceptions concern the absolute totality of the empirical synthesis of phenomena and ground the conception of the world-whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A12","text":"The ideal of pure reason concerns the absolute totality of the conditions of all possible things and is distinct from the cosmical conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:A13","text":"The antinomy presents the principles of a pretended pure rational cosmology as an idea generated by conflict rather than as a valid doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:NX1","text":"The antinomy is not a merely one-sided illusion of the kind produced by the paralogism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:NX2","text":"The antinomy is not produced by subtle or artificially constructed sophistry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:NX3","text":"The apparent plausibility of reason’s principle does not establish the validity of its unconditioned application to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:NX4","text":"The conflict cannot be resolved by granting one side exclusive authority while refusing the other a fair hearing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:NX5","text":"Despairing scepticism is not an adequate philosophical resolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:NX6","text":"Dogmatic persistence in one-sided assertions is not an adequate philosophical resolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:NX7","text":"The cosmical conception is not identical with the ideal of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:NX8","text":"Pure rational cosmology is not to be declared valid or appropriated as a doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:NX9","text":"The principles of pure rational cosmology cannot be reconciled with phenomena and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:D1","text":"The formal classification of syllogisms generates a sequence from subjective unity, through phenomenal objective conditions, to the conditions of possible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A1","CH_B337_CIAP:A2","CH_B337_CIAP:A3","CH_B337_CIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:D2","text":"The paralogism differs structurally from the antinomy because it lacks an equally grounded contrary proposition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:D3","text":"The objective application of reason produces a stronger dialectical conflict than the subjective paralogism.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A5","CH_B337_CIAP:A6","CH_B337_CIAP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:D4","text":"Contradiction in cosmology is generated by reason’s own demand for unconditioned unity rather than by an external attack on reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A6","CH_B337_CIAP:A7","CH_B337_CIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:D5","text":"The antinomy converts the pursuit of certainty into a forced choice between sceptical withdrawal and dogmatic assertion.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A8","CH_B337_CIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:D6","text":"The naturalness of the antinomy protects reason from remaining asleep in a merely one-sided conviction while simultaneously exposing it to philosophical breakdown.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A8","CH_B337_CIAP:A9","CH_B337_CIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:D7","text":"The distinction between cosmical conception and ideal of pure reason prevents the totality of phenomenal conditions from being identified with the totality of conditions for all possible things.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A11","CH_B337_CIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:D8","text":"The antinomy transforms rational cosmology from a candidate doctrine into an object requiring critical examination.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A7","CH_B337_CIAP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:D9","text":"The method of treatment must explain the conflict before entering into the individual laws and claims of pure reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A8","CH_B337_CIAP:A10","CH_B337_CIAP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:SD1","text":"The formal logic of syllogism functions as a generative architecture for successive domains of transcendental illusion rather than merely as a taxonomy of errors.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A1","CH_B337_CIAP:A2","CH_B337_CIAP:A3","CH_B337_CIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:SD2","text":"The antinomy makes contradiction the diagnostic manifestation of reason’s attempt to totalize the conditions of phenomenal synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A6","CH_B337_CIAP:A7","CH_B337_CIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:SD3","text":"The conflict is simultaneously epistemically protective and philosophically destructive: it prevents unexamined conviction but drives reason toward two inadequate extremes.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A8","CH_B337_CIAP:A9","CH_B337_CIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:SD4","text":"Critical analysis replaces the attempted possession of pure cosmology with the presentation of cosmology as an internally generated and experience-incompatible idea.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A7","CH_B337_CIAP:A10","CH_B337_CIAP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:SD5","text":"The distinction between cosmical conceptions and the ideal of pure reason prevents one totalizing demand from absorbing both phenomenal synthesis and the possibility of objects in general.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A4","CH_B337_CIAP:A11","CH_B337_CIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:SD6","text":"The passage establishes the diagnosis and method of the antinomy without yet supplying the positive resolution of the individual cosmological conflicts.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A8","CH_B337_CIAP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:DNX1","text":"Unconditioned totality is excluded from being treated as an ordinary empirical object within phenomenal experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:DNX2","text":"Plausibility is excluded as a sufficient criterion for the validity of reason’s unconditioned principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:DNX3","text":"The antinomy is excluded from being explained as an accidental or externally imposed defect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:DNX4","text":"One-sided doctrinal closure is excluded as a legitimate response to an internally symmetrical conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:DNX5","text":"Scepticism and dogmatism are excluded as the only acceptable forms of philosophical settlement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:DNX6","text":"Empirical phenomenal totality is excluded from being conflated with the totality of the conditions of all possible things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:DNX7","text":"Pure rational cosmology is excluded from functioning as an established science or possessed doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:SNX1","text":"Direct cognitive possession of absolute totality as a phenomenal object is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:SNX2","text":"Final philosophical closure through the selection of one side of the antinomy is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:SNX3","text":"The possibility that contradiction can be removed by retreating into scepticism is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:SNX4","text":"The possibility that the antinomy is merely accidental sophistry is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:SNX5","text":"The positive ground on which the antinomy can be critically resolved remains unformalized in this passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:FGS1","text":"Formal syllogism types → corresponding transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:FGS2","text":"Categorical form → unconditioned unity of the subjective conditions of representation → paralogistic illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:FGS3","text":"Hypothetical form → unconditioned unity of objective phenomenal conditions → cosmological antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:FGS4","text":"Demand for unconditioned unity → plausible principle of reason → contradictory consequences in phenomenal synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:FGS5","text":"Contradiction → forced alternatives of scepticism or dogmatism → failure of sound philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:FGS6","text":"Methodological distinction between empirical cosmical totality and the ideal of pure reason → suspension of doctrinal cosmology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:FGS7","text":"Suspension of doctrinal cosmology → presentation of the antinomy as an irreconcilable idea requiring critical treatment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:AF1","text":"Formal syllogism taxonomy → subjective paralogism as contrast → objective phenomenal synthesis → plausible unconditioned unity → unavoidable contradiction → scepticism/dogmatism dilemma → distinction between cosmical conception and ideal of pure reason → critique of pure rational cosmology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:AF2","text":"Apparent certainty → internally generated conflict → methodological suspension of appropriation → critical treatment of the antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:ME1","text":"The antinomy is reclassified from an error imposed on reason to a conflict produced by reason’s own operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:ME2","text":"The distinction between one-sided paralogistic illusion and symmetrical antinomy becomes structurally visible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:ME3","text":"Scepticism and dogmatism are redrawn as symmetrical failures rather than opposed philosophical victories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:ME4","text":"The world-whole of empirical synthesis is separated from the totality of conditions for all possible things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:ME5","text":"Pure rational cosmology is transformed from a doctrinal object into a critical problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:ME6","text":"Contradiction becomes evidence of a boundary in the use of reason rather than a reason to abandon rational inquiry altogether.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:ALT1","text":"Surface — Reason’s application to the objective synthesis of phenomena produces an antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The antinomy is generated by the relation between syllogistic form, unconditioned unity, and the conditions of phenomenal synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument replaces the choice between dogmatic assertion and sceptical withdrawal with a critical analysis of the conflict itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The system distinguishes conditioned phenomenal experience from the unconditioned totality of conditions and treats their conflation as the source of cosmological illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the passage’s central movement from syllogistic form to cosmological antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the decisive contrast between the asymmetrical paralogism and the symmetrical antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:MRT3","text":"The system is strongest in its mapping of logical form onto domains of transcendental illusion and in its rejection of both sceptical and dogmatic closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:MRT4","text":"The individual contradictions that constitute the antinomy remain underdetermined because the passage introduces the method rather than presenting the particular antinomies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:MRT5","text":"The distinction between cosmical conception and ideal of pure reason is structurally clear, while the precise relation between them remains compressed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIAP:MRT6","text":"The system is internally coherent as a diagnosis and methodological setup, but its positive resolution depends on material not contained in this passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 60","title":"Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 60"}},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason","order":18,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"b2ebb07dd44285e59661821714414dfd3f73a5c3932800d2731933a97be91eeb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:AS1","text":"Pure reason cannot securely discover truth through speculation alone and therefore requires self-imposed discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:AS2","text":"The primary function of pure philosophy is negative: it determines the limits of reason and guards against error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:AS3","text":"The limits imposed on speculative reason also disqualify the exaggerated claims of its opponents and secure reason’s remaining domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:AS4","text":"Reason nevertheless contains a positive orientation toward cognitions beyond experience, because it seeks an ultimate and secure ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:AS5","text":"Speculative reason cannot satisfy this orientation, so the possibility of its proper employment must be sought in practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:AS6","text":"A canon can govern only a legitimate employment of a cognitive faculty; because speculative reason has no legitimate synthetic employment, its canon belongs exclusively to practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A1","text":"Pure speculation is incompetent to establish truth without disciplinary limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A2","text":"Pure reason can discipline itself without submission to an external authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A3","text":"The limits of speculative reason prevent both its own deceptive claims and the usurpation of its legitimate possessions by opponents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A4","text":"The principal use of the philosophy of pure reason is the prevention of error rather than the extension of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A5","text":"Pure reason contains positive cognitions whose misclassification generates speculative error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A6","text":"Human reason is persistently directed toward knowledge beyond the limits of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A7","text":"Speculative reason cannot attain the knowledge toward which this direction strives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A8","text":"A canon is a set of a priori principles governing the proper employment of a cognitive faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A9","text":"A canon cannot exist where no proper employment of the relevant faculty is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A10","text":"The synthetic cognition of pure speculative reason is impossible, rendering its speculative exercise dialectical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A11","text":"If pure reason has a proper employment, that employment is practical rather than speculative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:A12","text":"The canon of pure reason therefore concerns its practical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:NX1","text":"Pure reason is not an unrestricted organon for extending knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:NX2","text":"Pure speculation is not a reliable path to truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:NX3","text":"The negative function of reason is not the discovery of new speculative truths.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:NX4","text":"The discipline of pure reason is not imposed by an external tribunal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:NX5","text":"The speculative exercise of pure reason is not a legitimate canon-governed employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:NX6","text":"Transcendental logic, with respect to speculative reason, is not a canon but a discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:NX7","text":"Synthetic cognition through pure speculative reason is not possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:NX8","text":"The desire for cognition beyond experience is not thereby evidence that speculative reason can satisfy it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:D1","text":"Because speculation deviates without discipline and reason can discipline itself, critique must function as an internal corrective rather than an externally imposed restriction.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A1","CH_B337_CICP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:D2","text":"Because reason’s limits block both its own exaggerations and opposing usurpations, limitation preserves a secure residual domain of rational cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:D3","text":"Because pure reason’s principal use is error prevention, the delimitation of reason has greater justificatory force than speculative expansion.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A1","CH_B337_CICP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:D4","text":"Because reason contains positive cognitions that become erroneous through misclassification, speculative error arises from using legitimate materials under an illegitimate mode.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A5","CH_B337_CICP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:D5","text":"Because reason seeks knowledge beyond experience while speculation cannot attain it, the failure of speculation does not eliminate reason’s orientation toward supersensible cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A6","CH_B337_CICP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:D6","text":"Because a canon requires a proper employment and speculative reason lacks one, no speculative canon can be established.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A9","CH_B337_CICP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:D7","text":"Because speculative reason has no possible synthetic cognition, its speculative activity is necessarily dialectical rather than genuinely cognitive.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A9","CH_B337_CICP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:D8","text":"Because a proper employment of pure reason must be practical if it exists at all, the investigation of reason’s canon must shift from speculation to practice.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A11","CH_B337_CICP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:SD1","text":"The restriction of speculative reason is simultaneously a defense of reason, because critique converts the admission of incapacity into jurisdiction over the limits of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A1","CH_B337_CICP:A2","CH_B337_CICP:A3","CH_B337_CICP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:SD2","text":"The text transforms the failure of metaphysical speculation from a total defeat of reason into a reallocation of reason’s legitimate use.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A5","CH_B337_CICP:A7","CH_B337_CICP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:SD3","text":"Pure reason is divided into a negative speculative function that polices cognition and a positive practical function that may orient reason toward its ultimate interests.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A4","CH_B337_CICP:A6","CH_B337_CICP:A10","CH_B337_CICP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:SD4","text":"The concept of a canon becomes a criterion of legitimacy: a faculty is canon-governed only where its employment can produce proper cognition or action.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A8","CH_B337_CICP:A9","CH_B337_CICP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:SD5","text":"The boundary of experience is not presented as the extinction of reason’s highest aims but as the point at which their mode of access must change.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A6","CH_B337_CICP:A7","CH_B337_CICP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:DNX1","text":"Reason cannot validate its highest claims merely by desiring them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:DNX2","text":"The persistence of a metaphysical question does not establish the speculative possibility of its answer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:DNX3","text":"Positive content is not sufficient for cognition; the mode in which that content is employed determines whether it becomes knowledge or error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:DNX4","text":"Self-critique cannot be replaced by an appeal to an external authority capable of settling pure reason’s speculative disputes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:DNX5","text":"The failure of speculative synthesis cannot be repaired by producing further speculative principles within the same faculty and mode.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:DNX6","text":"The limits of experience do not license either unrestricted transcendence or the abolition of reason’s supersensible orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:SNX1","text":"The text excludes the possibility that reason’s ultimate vocation can be fulfilled by speculative possession of supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:SNX2","text":"The text excludes a final authority outside reason that could discipline pure reason more adequately than reason itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:SNX3","text":"The text leaves unavailable any unified speculative canon for pure reason as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:SNX4","text":"The text suspends the assumption that the failure of theoretical knowledge entails the failure of reason’s deepest interests.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:SNX5","text":"The text organizes its positive doctrine around the absence of speculative access to the domain toward which reason is nevertheless directed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:FGS1","text":"Unchecked speculative impulse → illusion and deviation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:FGS2","text":"Self-imposed critique → determination of speculative limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:FGS3","text":"Determination of limits → rejection of exaggerated claims and protection of legitimate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:FGS4","text":"Recognition of reason’s persistent supersensible aim → distinction between positive orientation and speculative misuse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:FGS5","text":"Demonstration of the impossibility of speculative synthetic cognition → classification of speculative reason as dialectical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:FGS6","text":"Absence of a proper speculative employment → impossibility of a speculative canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:FGS7","text":"Retention of a possible proper employment → transfer of the canon from speculative to practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:AF1","text":"Speculative incapacity → self-discipline → limitation of reason → protection of legitimate cognition → preservation of supersensible orientation → failure of speculative access → practical relocation of the canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:ME1","text":"Philosophy is reclassified from an organon of speculative expansion into a discipline of cognitive delimitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:ME2","text":"The boundary of reason becomes a protective jurisdiction rather than merely a deficit or restriction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:ME3","text":"Speculative failure and rational legitimacy are separated: reason can fail to know supersensible objects without becoming irrational as a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:ME4","text":"The opposition between positive cognition and negative critique is reorganized: critique preserves the conditions under which positive use may remain legitimate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:ME5","text":"The canon of reason is displaced from theoretical cognition to practical employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:ME6","text":"The desire for transcendence is reclassified from evidence of speculative knowledge into a problem requiring another mode of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:ALT1","text":"Surface — Pure speculative reason cannot establish synthetic cognition and must be disciplined by limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:ALT2","text":"Structural — A cognitive faculty possesses a canon only when a proper employment is possible; speculative reason fails this condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critique defeats both speculative overreach and the external appropriation of reason by converting limitation into a defensive strategy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human reason is constituted by an enduring orientation beyond experience, but its fulfillment requires a transition from theoretical speculation to practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from speculative incapacity to practical canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:MRT2","text":"The central inference depends on the distinction between a faculty’s positive orientation and the illegitimate speculative use of that orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if the impossibility of speculative synthetic cognition is accepted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:MRT4","text":"The strongest structural move is the conversion of limitation into both protection and jurisdiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:MRT5","text":"The practical content of the canon remains underdetermined in this passage because its investigation is announced rather than developed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:MRT6","text":"The transition from speculative failure to practical possibility is asserted as a legitimate shift but is not fully demonstrated here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CICP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement: discipline → limitation → protection → speculative exclusion → practical redirection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CICP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 88","title":"Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 88"}},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason","order":19,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"a37c4ff2ad2ec354fbd3c079a7d503d8856ae320a99bb708b84ba6105ae492c9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:AS1","text":"Negative judgments are treated as epistemically inferior although their proper function is the prevention of error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:AS2","text":"When cognition is narrowly bounded, desire expands, illusion becomes deceptive, and error becomes costly, negative safeguards acquire priority over many positive additions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:AS3","text":"Discipline is defined as corrective restraint, in contrast to culture and doctrine as positive formation or instruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:AS4","text":"Reason itself requires discipline because its prestige conceals its capacity to substitute fancies for concepts and words for things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:AS5","text":"Empirical and mathematical uses of reason are externally constrained, whereas transcendental use of pure concepts lacks those constraints and can exceed possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:AS6","text":"Pure reason therefore requires a systematic negative discipline directed at its methods rather than its content, because interconnected errors cannot be eliminated through isolated corrections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A1","text":"Negative judgments concerning the content of cognition primarily function to prevent error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A2","text":"The importance of negative cognition increases when possible cognition is restricted, illusion is deceptive, and error has serious consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A3","text":"Discipline is corrective restraint against the tendency to depart from governing rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A4","text":"Culture and doctrine positively develop capacities or communicate knowledge without functioning primarily as corrective restraint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A5","text":"Reason can generate fancies in place of concepts and words in place of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A6","text":"Reason employed within experience is tested by empirical observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A7","text":"Reason employed in mathematics is constrained by the presentation of its conceptions in pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A8","text":"Pure-concept reason employed transcendentally is not held within the same empirical or intuitive constraints.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A9","text":"Transcendental pure reason has a propensity to exceed the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A10","text":"The errors of pure reason form an interconnected system dependent upon general principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A11","text":"An interconnected system of errors requires a general negative code of examination and testing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A12","text":"The discipline of pure reason concerns the method of cognition rather than the content of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:A13","text":"Methods generated by reason can become invalid when transferred from their proper sphere into transcendental use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:NX1","text":"Negative judgments are not defined as additions to the stock of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:NX2","text":"Error-preventing propositions are not all equally purposive; where error is impossible, negation becomes empty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:NX3","text":"Discipline is not identical with positive skill formation, culture, or instruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:NX4","text":"Reason is not exempt from corrective examination merely because it legislates rules for other mental powers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:NX5","text":"The same critical demand does not apply uniformly to empirical, mathematical, and transcendental uses of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:NX6","text":"The discipline of pure reason is not directed toward supplying or revising the content of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:NX7","text":"A general system of fallacies is not adequately addressed through isolated corrections of individual errors.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:NX8","text":"A method’s origin in reason does not make it valid in every sphere of application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:D1","text":"Under conditions of restricted cognition and heightened epistemic danger, preventing error can be more valuable than adding information.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A1","CH_B337_CIDP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:D2","text":"Corrective restraint constitutes a distinct epistemic function rather than a deficient form of instruction.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A3","CH_B337_CIDP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:D3","text":"Reason’s elevated status increases the likelihood that its own errors remain unexamined.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A3","CH_B337_CIDP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:D4","text":"The need for criticism is determined by the presence or absence of external constraints on the use of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A6","CH_B337_CIDP:A7","CH_B337_CIDP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:D5","text":"Transcendental reason becomes especially vulnerable to error when it operates without empirical or intuitive constraint.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A8","CH_B337_CIDP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:D6","text":"Because pure-reason errors depend on general principles, their correction must address the generative structure of those errors.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A10","CH_B337_CIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:D7","text":"The philosophical utility of pure reason is primarily regulatory and error-preventive rather than knowledge-expansive.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A1","CH_B337_CIDP:A9","CH_B337_CIDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:D8","text":"Critique must examine the employment of methods when the problem lies in their misapplication rather than in the objects considered.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A12","CH_B337_CIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:D9","text":"The distinction between discipline and instruction is required because correction and knowledge-communication perform different operations.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A3","CH_B337_CIDP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:SD1","text":"Reason becomes an object of discipline precisely because the faculty that prescribes rules for cognition can itself misuse its rules.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A3","CH_B337_CIDP:A5","CH_B337_CIDP:A8","CH_B337_CIDP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:SD2","text":"Negative critique is converted from a mere prohibition into a complete regulatory architecture for governing the use of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A1","CH_B337_CIDP:A3","CH_B337_CIDP:A11","CH_B337_CIDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:SD3","text":"The boundary of possible experience functions as a validity condition for methods of reasoning, not merely as a limit on conclusions.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A8","CH_B337_CIDP:A9","CH_B337_CIDP:A12","CH_B337_CIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:SD4","text":"The transfer of methods from constrained domains into transcendental use produces a structural mechanism of illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A6","CH_B337_CIDP:A7","CH_B337_CIDP:A8","CH_B337_CIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:SD5","text":"Systematic fallacy requires a method capable of testing errors across disguises rather than merely refuting visible conclusions.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A10","CH_B337_CIDP:A11","CH_B337_CIDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:SD6","text":"Pure reason preserves its legitimate authority through self-limitation rather than through unrestricted speculative expansion.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A5","CH_B337_CIDP:A9","CH_B337_CIDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:DNX1","text":"The value of cognition is not measured solely by the quantity of positive knowledge added.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:DNX2","text":"Reason is not self-authenticating across all modes of employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:DNX3","text":"A method does not retain validity merely because reason itself originated it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:DNX4","text":"Pure concepts do not by themselves secure legitimate cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:DNX5","text":"Error is not necessarily local, accidental, or separable from the general principles that generate it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:DNX6","text":"Corrective discipline does not require the destruction of a mental power; it requires the restraint of its tendency to depart from rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:DNX7","text":"Positive cultivation cannot substitute for negative regulation when the faculty’s governing methods generate illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:SNX1","text":"Unrestricted speculative expansion of reason is structurally unavailable as a legitimate epistemic path.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:SNX2","text":"A domain-invariant method of reason is excluded by the difference between empirical, mathematical, and transcendental employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:SNX3","text":"A positive completion of pure reason is not the organizing aim of the system; the organizing aim is the control of its overreach.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:SNX4","text":"The transcendental sphere cannot borrow empirical observation or pure intuition as an automatic safeguard against error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:SNX5","text":"Reason cannot remain an uncriticized universal legislator while legislating for every other cognitive power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:FGS1","text":"Restricted cognition plus expansive desire, deceptive illusion, and costly error produce the need for negative epistemic protection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:FGS2","text":"The need to prevent error produces the distinction between corrective discipline and positive culture or instruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:FGS3","text":"The prestige of reason conceals its capacity for substitution, thereby making reason itself subject to disciplinary correction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:FGS4","text":"External testing in experience and mathematics contrasts with the absence of equivalent constraint in transcendental pure-concept reasoning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:FGS5","text":"Unconstrained transcendental reasoning generates overreach beyond possible experience and organizes illusion through general principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:FGS6","text":"Systematic illusion requires a general negative code of examination rather than isolated refutations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:FGS7","text":"Because the error lies in the employment of methods, the resulting discipline regulates method rather than cognitive content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:AF1","text":"Devaluation of negative judgment → restriction of possible cognition → increased epistemic risk → priority of error prevention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:AF2","text":"Distinction between discipline and culture → extension of corrective demand to reason → exposure of reason’s concealed fallibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:AF3","text":"Separation of empirically or intuitively constrained reason from transcendental pure-concept reason → diagnosis of overreach beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:AF4","text":"Interconnected fallacies generated by general principles → requirement for a general negative code → method-focused discipline of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:ME1","text":"Negative judgment is reclassified from deficient knowledge into necessary epistemic protection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:ME2","text":"Discipline becomes an autonomous negative operation rather than a synonym for instruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:ME3","text":"Reason is repositioned from universal legislator to a faculty requiring legislation over its own use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:ME4","text":"Critique shifts from correcting cognitive contents to regulating the methods by which those contents are produced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:ME5","text":"The boundary between possible experience and transcendental speculation becomes an operative boundary of methodological validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:ME6","text":"Error is reclassified from a set of isolated mistakes into an interconnected generative system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:ME7","text":"The strategic aim of philosophy shifts from indefinite expansion of knowledge toward the prevention of systematic illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:ALT1","text":"Surface — Negative judgments prevent error, and discipline restrains departures from rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The validity of reason depends on the constraints governing its mode of employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critique must replace unrestricted cognitive expansion with systematic control of methods and boundaries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Cognition is bounded by possible experience, while transcendental reason tends to project concepts beyond the conditions under which objects can be legitimately known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument’s central movement from the devaluation of negation to the necessity of disciplinary critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:MRT2","text":"The extracted structure preserves the distinction between negative discipline and positive culture or instruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:MRT3","text":"The system is strongest where it distinguishes externally constrained reason from transcendental reason and connects that distinction to different critical requirements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:MRT4","text":"The exact contents and procedures of the proposed negative code remain underdetermined; the text establishes its necessity and scope rather than fully specifying its operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:MRT5","text":"A structural tension remains between reason as the legislator of cognitive rules and reason as a faculty requiring discipline; the text establishes this reflexivity without fully specifying its ultimate criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:MRT6","text":"A second tension remains between the non-instructional character of discipline and its formulation as a code or legislation, which gives the negative system an implicit constructive form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIDP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement of the text, but the chapter’s reliance on broad distinctions between experience, mathematics, and transcendental cognition leaves their internal boundaries compressed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 83","title":"Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 83"}},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","order":20,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"a9999b2681b934593c2f262a0c2cfa5103503aee289d2ade2580b2bd815c850c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:AS1","text":"The central problem is to determine the rightful domain and immutable limits of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:AS2","text":"Pure concepts and principles possess objective validity only as conditions of possible experience, never as cognition of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:AS3","text":"The argument is transcendental and limitative in proof, diagnostic in its account of illusion, and polemical in its rejection of pure ontology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:AS4","text":"The proof moves from the conditions under which concepts acquire objects to the loss of meaning and application when intuition and schemata are removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:AS5","text":"The distinction between phenomena and things considered apart from human intuition generates the problem of noumena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:AS6","text":"The ambiguity of noumenon is resolved by retaining its negative, limitative sense and rejecting its positive sense as an object of intellectual intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:AS7","text":"The argument moves from securing possible experience through diagnosing transcendental illusion to closing positive noumenal cognition while preserving an empty boundary beyond phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A1","text":"Objects can be determined for human cognition only through the conjunction of sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A2","text":"Objects are given to human cognition only through sensible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A3","text":"A concept has objective validity only if an object corresponding to it can be given in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A4","text":"Experience receives its formal unity from the understanding’s a priori synthesis of the manifold in relation to apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A5","text":"Categories are forms for unifying a given manifold in consciousness, not means of giving objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A6","text":"A category can be used in judgment only through a schema enabling the subsumption of an intuited object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A7","text":"Pure intuition acquires objective validity only as the form of empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A8","text":"Empirical use refers concepts to phenomena as objects of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A9","text":"Transcendental use refers concepts to things in general considered as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A10","text":"Pure concepts and principles of the understanding admit only empirical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A11","text":"Freedom from contradiction establishes only the logical possibility of a concept, not the real possibility of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A12","text":"For human understanding, the third term required for synthetic a priori connection is available only through possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A13","text":"Calling an object a phenomenon necessarily produces the thought of that object apart from the human mode of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A14","text":"A noumenon in the negative sense is a thing considered insofar as it is not an object of sensible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A15","text":"A noumenon in the positive sense is an object of non-sensible intellectual intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A16","text":"Intellectual intuition neither belongs to human cognition nor has a possibility comprehensible to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:A17","text":"The negative noumenon is necessary only as a problematic limit-concept that restricts sensibility without supplying positive cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:NX1","text":"The a priori origin of the categories does not establish their application beyond sensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:NX2","text":"The transcendental meaning of a pure category is not a transcendental use of that category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:NX3","text":"The logical notions of subject, consequence, and non-contradiction are not sufficient determinations of substance, causality, or objective possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:NX4","text":"Conceptual consistency is not proof that a corresponding object is really possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:NX5","text":"Phenomena and noumena do not constitute two positively cognizable classes of objects or worlds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:NX6","text":"A negative noumenon is not a determinate intelligible object available to the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:NX7","text":"The claim that understanding represents objects “as they are” does not signify cognition of things apart from possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:NX8","text":"The lawful intellectual organization of phenomena is not a mundus intelligibilis in the transcendental sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:NX9","text":"Analytical consistency cannot establish objective reference, and synthetic transcendental principles cannot be demonstrated without empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:NX10","text":"Sensible intuition is not asserted to be the only possible intuition absolutely; it is only the sole intuition available and intelligible to human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:D1","text":"Even concepts generated wholly a priori obtain significance and objective employment only through possible empirical exhibition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A2","CH_B337_CIGD:A3","CH_B337_CIGD:A7","CH_B337_CIGD:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:D2","text":"Categories separated from sensibility retain logical form but lose subsumable objects, objective validity, and determinate use.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A3","CH_B337_CIGD:A5","CH_B337_CIGD:A6","CH_B337_CIGD:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:D3","text":"Removing temporal and sensible schemata from the categories leaves only indeterminate logical functions from which no objective inference can be drawn.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A2","CH_B337_CIGD:A5","CH_B337_CIGD:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:D4","text":"The independence of the categories from sensibility in origin generates the illusion that they are independent of sensibility in application.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A5","CH_B337_CIGD:A9","CH_B337_CIGD:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:D5","text":"Pure understanding can articulate the formal conditions of experience but cannot provide an ontology of things in general.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A4","CH_B337_CIGD:A10","CH_B337_CIGD:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:D6","text":"Positive noumena cannot be cognized because their required mode of intuition is unavailable and its possibility is unintelligible to us.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A3","CH_B337_CIGD:A15","CH_B337_CIGD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:D7","text":"The negative noumenon extends understanding only by enabling it to limit both sensibility and its own claims.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A13","CH_B337_CIGD:A14","CH_B337_CIGD:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:D8","text":"No synthetic principle concerning noumena can be established because no intuition supplies the mediating term required to connect its concepts objectively.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A9","CH_B337_CIGD:A12","CH_B337_CIGD:A15","CH_B337_CIGD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:D9","text":"The distinction between phenomena and noumena is admissible as a conceptual limitation but inadmissible as a positive division of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A14","CH_B337_CIGD:A15","CH_B337_CIGD:A16","CH_B337_CIGD:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SD1","text":"Transcendental critique produces boundary-knowledge rather than knowledge of a further domain.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A8","CH_B337_CIGD:A9","CH_B337_CIGD:A10","CH_B337_CIGD:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SD2","text":"A priori cognition does not compete with empirical cognition; it constitutes the form of experience and thereby receives an empirical restriction on its validity.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A4","CH_B337_CIGD:A7","CH_B337_CIGD:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SD3","text":"The phenomenon–noumenon relation is asymmetrical: phenomena form a cognizable domain, whereas noumena mark only what that domain cannot claim to exhaust.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A13","CH_B337_CIGD:A14","CH_B337_CIGD:A16","CH_B337_CIGD:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SD4","text":"Thought exceeds cognition only problematically, by retaining an indeterminate place for what cannot be presented as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A3","CH_B337_CIGD:A5","CH_B337_CIGD:A13","CH_B337_CIGD:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SD5","text":"Transcendental illusion arises when the logical reach of forms of thought is converted into objective knowledge of a wider domain.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A3","CH_B337_CIGD:A5","CH_B337_CIGD:A9","CH_B337_CIGD:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SD6","text":"Metaphysics is transformed from a doctrine of supersensible objects into an analytic of the conditions and jurisdiction of understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A10","CH_B337_CIGD:A12","CH_B337_CIGD:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SD7","text":"The system excludes both dogmatic noumenalism and the dogmatic claim that sensible appearance exhausts every possible mode of reality.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A13","CH_B337_CIGD:A14","CH_B337_CIGD:A15","CH_B337_CIGD:A16","CH_B337_CIGD:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:DNX1","text":"Pure thought must not be capable of giving itself an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:DNX2","text":"The legitimate scope of a concept must not be inferable from its non-sensible origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:DNX3","text":"The thinkability of a concept must not guarantee the possibility of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:DNX4","text":"Categories must not be capable of generating their own manifold, intuition, or schema.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:DNX5","text":"The distinction between appearance and thing in itself must not establish an inventory of two worlds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:DNX6","text":"A boundary of cognition must not disclose the positive constitution of what lies beyond it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:DNX7","text":"Successful empirical employment of understanding must not by itself provide knowledge of the limits governing that employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SNX1","text":"The missing faculty around which the system is organized is intellectual intuition: the only condition that could make positive noumenal cognition possible is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SNX2","text":"The missing mediation is a non-empirical third term capable of grounding synthetic judgments about things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SNX3","text":"The noumenal beyond must remain an empty place rather than become an alternative field of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SNX4","text":"The argument depends on a remainder that cannot be identified with appearance but cannot be assigned positive predicates without collapsing the critical limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:SNX5","text":"No absolute standpoint is available from which sensible cognition and a positive intelligible world could be surveyed as two completed domains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:FGS1","text":"Sensible manifold → synthesis of imagination → unity under apperception → possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:FGS2","text":"Concept + corresponding intuition + schema → subsumption in judgment → objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:FGS3","text":"Category − sensible intuition − schema → logical form without a determinable object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:FGS4","text":"A priori origin + abstraction from sensibility → appearance of transcendental reach → critical restriction to empirical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:FGS5","text":"Phenomenon → abstraction from the human mode of intuition → thought of the thing in itself → negative noumenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:FGS6","text":"Positive noumenon → required intellectual intuition → unavailable condition → impossibility of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:FGS7","text":"Negative noumenon + restricted categories → limitation of sensibility + self-limitation of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:FGS8","text":"Attempted synthetic noumenal judgment → missing intuitive third term → failed transcendental principle → rejection of positive noumenal division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:FGS9","text":"Empirical restriction + necessary limit-concept → analytic of pure understanding rather than ontology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:AF1","text":"Survey of the secure cognitive domain → question of title and boundary → necessity of transcendental critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:AF2","text":"Conditions of objective meaning → dependence on intuition and schema → restriction of categories to possible experience → demotion of ontology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:AF3","text":"Phenomenon–thing-in-itself distinction → ambiguity of noumenon → negative/positive division → rejection of positive noumena → retention of the negative limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:AF4","text":"Proposed noumenal synthesis → demand for a mediating third term → failure outside experience → final closure of transcendental use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ME1","text":"The domain of truth is redrawn as possible experience rather than reality in its unrestricted totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ME2","text":"The opposition between a priori and empirical cognition is reorganized: the a priori supplies the form of experience rather than access beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ME3","text":"Categories are reclassified from predicates of being in general into conditions for organizing sensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ME4","text":"Thought and cognition become separable: an object may be thought problematically without being objectively known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ME5","text":"Noumenon is reclassified from a supersensible object-class into a limit-operator governing the claims of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ME6","text":"The apparent dualism of sensible and intelligible worlds is replaced by an asymmetry between a determinate field of cognition and an indeterminate boundary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ME7","text":"Critical philosophy becomes a jurisdictional map of valid use, invalid extension, and unavoidable illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ALT1","text":"Surface: The explicit issue is whether categories and principles can apply to noumena as well as phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ALT2","text":"Structural: Objective cognition is generated only by the coordinated functions of sensible givenness, categorial synthesis, schema, and judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Critique replaces speculative expansion with prior determination of the legitimacy and boundaries of cognitive claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ALT4","text":"Ontological: Objects as appearances are distinguished from objects considered apart from human intuition, while every positive ontology of the latter is withheld.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:ALT5","text":"Temporal: The objective application of quantity, reality, substance, causality, contingency, and related categories depends on temporal determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the restriction of categories, the noumenon distinction, and the rejection of positive transcendental cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:MRT2","text":"The status of the thing in itself remains underdetermined between the same object considered apart from human intuition and a merely possible non-sensible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:MRT3","text":"The system remains internally coherent only if thought is distinguished from cognition, transcendental meaning from transcendental use, and logical possibility from objective possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:MRT4","text":"The strongest inferential convergence lies in the shared requirement of intuition, schema, subsumption, and a third term for objective synthetic judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:MRT5","text":"A structural fault line remains between the claim that categories think objects in general beyond sensibility and the claim that they lose meaning and application when separated from sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:MRT6","text":"A second tension lies in declaring the negative noumenon necessary while denying any comprehension of its objective possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:MRT7","text":"The chapter depends on prior accounts of sensible intuition, apperception, and schematism that it recapitulates rather than independently re-establishes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:MRT8","text":"The closure applies to human cognition and its categories, not to every possible intellect or mode of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIGD:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the actual movement from cognitive jurisdiction through semantic deprivation to negative noumenal limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIGD:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 48","title":"Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena","context":"Chapter 48"}},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","order":21,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"685e1b6eb6e27c59219a9bf14b102e438384c03e1ba87dfe5e0afe3b437b6fe8","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AS1","text":"The chapter treats the history of philosophy as a transcendental diagnostic of pure reason rather than as a chronological history of doctrines.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AS2","text":"The historical edifice of philosophy is judged ruinous because its basic disputes have not yielded permanent rational settlement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AS3","text":"Metaphysical conflict is reduced to three generative divisions: the object of rational cognition, the origin of pure cognition, and the method of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AS4","text":"The traditional schools fail because they absolutize one pole of cognition, derive claims exceeding their own premises, or mistake the rejection of science for a method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AS5","text":"Systematic scientific procedure excludes naturalistic, dogmatic, and sceptical closure as adequate final positions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AS6","text":"Criticism is presented as the remaining route through which reason may transform an untravelled path into a stable public road.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AS7","text":"The argument moves from philosophical ruin through classificatory diagnosis and methodological exclusion toward the conditional possibility of reason’s contentment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A1","text":"The history of philosophy can be assessed from the transcendental standpoint of the nature of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A2","text":"The accumulated philosophical edifice lacks secure rational completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A3","text":"The decisive revolutions of metaphysics are intelligible through the object, origin, and method of rational cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A4","text":"Sensualism locates reality in sensuous objects, whereas intellectualism locates truth in the understanding and treats sensibility as deceptive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A5","text":"Empiricism derives pure cognition from experience, whereas noology derives it from reason alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A6","text":"A theory that derives cognition from experience cannot coherently use that cognition to demonstrate objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A7","text":"Method is procedure governed by principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A8","text":"Common reason becomes misology when its independence from science is elevated into a method for extending metaphysical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A9","text":"Scientific inquiry into pure reason must remain systematic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A10","text":"Dogmatism and scepticism are available scientific procedures but do not exhaust the possible method of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A11","text":"The critical path is the remaining method through which reason may attain lasting satisfaction concerning its metaphysical interests.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:A12","text":"The completion of critical reason requires collective continuation rather than the isolated achievement of a single thinker.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:NX1","text":"The chapter is not a full history of philosophy or a completed division of the system of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:NX2","text":"Metaphysical reality is not secured by sensuous objects alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:NX3","text":"Metaphysical truth is not secured by an understanding assumed to possess intuition independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:NX4","text":"The origin of pure cognition is not settled by the inherited opposition between experience and reason alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:NX5","text":"Empirical concepts and principles are not entitled to establish demonstrative knowledge of God or the immortality of the soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:NX6","text":"Common sense is not a scientific instrument for resolving the primary questions of metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:NX7","text":"The abandonment of scientific means is not a method of extending cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:NX8","text":"Scientific inquiry is not permitted to abandon systematic procedure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:NX9","text":"Dogmatism and scepticism are not the final methodological destiny of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:D1","text":"Philosophical history becomes a diagnostic matrix for locating failures of pure reason rather than a succession of doctrines to be merely reported.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A1","CH_B337_CIHP:A2","CH_B337_CIHP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:D2","text":"The legitimacy of a metaphysical claim depends on the coherence between its claimed origin and the range of objects to which it is applied.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A5","CH_B337_CIHP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:D3","text":"The empiricist attempt to prove transcendent objects exposes a structural self-transgression rather than merely an erroneous conclusion.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A5","CH_B337_CIHP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:D4","text":"Naturalism can remain harmless as an untheorized practical reliance on common sense but becomes irrational when it asserts itself as a superior theory of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A7","CH_B337_CIHP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:D5","text":"The demand for systematicity converts method from a preference among schools into a condition of scientific rational inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A7","CH_B337_CIHP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:D6","text":"Criticism appears as the residual method produced after naturalism, dogmatism, and scepticism have been bounded as insufficient.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A8","CH_B337_CIHP:A9","CH_B337_CIHP:A10","CH_B337_CIHP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:D7","text":"Reason’s satisfaction is represented as durable only when critical method becomes collectively reproducible.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A11","CH_B337_CIHP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:SD1","text":"The plurality of metaphysical systems is reclassified as evidence of an unresolved problem in reason’s self-governance rather than as evidence of progressive doctrinal accumulation.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A1","CH_B337_CIHP:A2","CH_B337_CIHP:A3","CH_B337_CIHP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:SD2","text":"Metaphysical failure is generated whenever an account of cognition silently exceeds the limits imposed by its own account of cognitive origin.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A3","CH_B337_CIHP:A5","CH_B337_CIHP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:SD3","text":"The transition from doctrinal conflict to critique requires replacing competition among answers with inquiry into the conditions governing admissible answers.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A1","CH_B337_CIHP:A3","CH_B337_CIHP:A9","CH_B337_CIHP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:SD4","text":"Reason’s historical progress is not automatic because centuries of metaphysical labor may reproduce unresolved divisions unless method itself is transformed.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A2","CH_B337_CIHP:A9","CH_B337_CIHP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:SD5","text":"Critical philosophy is constituted as a collective infrastructural project whose success consists in making rational procedure shareable and durable.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A11","CH_B337_CIHP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:DNX1","text":"No metaphysical position is self-validating merely because it privileges sense, understanding, experience, or ordinary reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:DNX2","text":"No claim to transcendent knowledge is admissible merely because its concepts were successfully employed within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:DNX3","text":"Historical duration, canonical authority, and repeated philosophical labor do not constitute proof of rational progress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:DNX4","text":"The solution to metaphysical conflict cannot consist in adding another unexamined doctrine to the existing field of doctrines.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:DNX5","text":"A method that does not determine its own principles cannot secure the scientific status of its conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:SNX1","text":"Immediate access to ultimate metaphysical objects is foreclosed, whether such access is attributed to sensation, pure understanding, empirical inference, or common sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:SNX2","text":"Reason cannot reach permanent contentment through the mere victory of one inherited school over another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:SNX3","text":"The unexamined authority of reason is itself unavailable as a foundation for reason’s metaphysical claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:SNX4","text":"The completed critical system is withheld from the chapter; only the necessity and opening of its route are asserted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:FGS1","text":"Ruinous philosophical inheritance → transcendental abstraction from historical chronology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:FGS2","text":"Transcendental abstraction → classification of disputes by object, origin, and method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:FGS3","text":"Classification by object → exposure of the sensualist and intellectualist absolutization of one cognitive pole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:FGS4","text":"Classification by origin → exposure of empiricist overreach beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:FGS5","text":"Classification by method → exclusion of naturalistic anti-science and imposition of systematicity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:FGS6","text":"Systematicity plus the insufficiency of dogmatism and scepticism → critical method as the remaining path.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:FGS7","text":"Critical method plus collective continuation → possible conversion of an isolated path into a public road toward reason’s contentment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AF1","text":"Ruinous philosophical edifice → transcendental diagnosis of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AF2","text":"Transcendental diagnosis → threefold differentiation of object, origin, and method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AF3","text":"Differentiation of origin and scope → disclosure of metaphysical overreach.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AF4","text":"Differentiation of methods → exclusion of naturalism and compulsory systematicity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AF5","text":"Exhaustion of inherited scientific alternatives → critical path.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:AF6","text":"Critical path plus collective labor → possible permanent satisfaction of reason’s metaphysical demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ME1","text":"The history of metaphysics is redrawn from a temporal sequence into a structured field of recurrent rational errors.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ME2","text":"Disputes about the object of cognition, its origin, and its method become separable dimensions rather than interchangeable philosophical positions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ME3","text":"The opposition between empiricism and rationalism becomes assessable by the relation between a theory’s declared source of cognition and its authorized scope of application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ME4","text":"Common sense is reclassified from a possible practical orientation into an inadmissible theoretical substitute when it claims scientific metaphysical authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ME5","text":"Criticism is positioned neither as another historical school nor as a compromise between dogmatism and scepticism, but as a transformation of the rules governing metaphysical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ME6","text":"Rational completion is recast from possession of a doctrine into the construction of a stable, collective route of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The chapter surveys the principal historical oppositions concerning metaphysical objects, cognitive origins, and philosophical methods.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The chapter identifies incoherence between the source attributed to cognition and the domain claimed for its legitimate use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The chapter converts historical comparison into a procedure for excluding inadequate methods and reserving a critical alternative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ALT4","text":"Epistemic — The chapter presupposes that cognition requires principles delimiting its proper object, source, and method of employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ALT5","text":"Temporal — The chapter treats the long duration of metaphysical conflict as non-progressive unless a new method interrupts its reproduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:ALT6","text":"Political-Public — The chapter represents rational completion as dependent on the collective widening and maintenance of a critical route.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s movement from historical ruin to the conditional opening of criticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:MRT2","text":"The system is strongest where it binds the source of cognition to the permissible scope of metaphysical assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined because the chapter asserts the critical method’s priority without supplying its positive principles or procedures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:MRT4","text":"The classification is structurally compressed because the same figures can occupy different divisions without an explicit account of the relation among those divisions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:MRT5","text":"The exclusion of dogmatism and scepticism establishes criticism residually, so the critical path initially derives force from the failure of alternatives more than from a completed internal demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:MRT6","text":"The chapter’s final promise depends on an unresolved transition from an individual critical path to collective rational contentment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIHP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if criticism is treated as a methodological reconstitution of reason rather than as a further doctrine within the inherited metaphysical field.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIHP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 93","title":"Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 93"}},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason","order":22,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"51f38f9582c27a23cddb1289fd519e331f4b8c9f5c5c67b5201d77d670342fb3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:AS1","text":"Pure rational psychology attempts to infer the soul’s substantiality, simplicity, personality, separability, and permanence from “I think” alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:AS2","text":"The argument is diagnostic and transcendental: it locates the resulting errors in reason’s necessary operations rather than in contingent empirical mistakes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:AS3","text":"The decisive distinction separates the logical unity of apperception from an intuition through which the self could be cognized as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:AS4","text":"Each purported property of the soul results from converting a logical feature of self-consciousness into a metaphysical determination of the thinking being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:AS5","text":"The objective use of the categories requires intuition, while mere apperception supplies no intuition adequate to determine the transcendental subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:AS6","text":"The paralogisms therefore establish neither spiritualism nor materialism, but the speculative indeterminacy of the soul’s constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:AS7","text":"Rational psychology is reconstructed from a doctrine of the soul into a discipline limiting dogmatic claims about the self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:AS8","text":"Speculative closure redirects claims concerning future existence from theoretical cognition to the practical use of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A1","text":"“I think” is the necessary accompaniment and vehicle of every representation that counts as thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A2","text":"In mere apperception, “I” designates only the transcendental subject of thought = x and supplies no determinate empirical content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A3","text":"An object is cognized only when a given intuition is determined in relation to the unity of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A4","text":"Categories acquire objective significance only through application to intuition within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A5","text":"Modes of self-consciousness in thought are logical functions and do not present the self as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A6","text":"Inner intuition is sensuous and temporal and contains no permanent presentation of the thinking subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A7","text":"“I think,” insofar as it means “I exist thinking,” is an empirical proposition grounded in an undetermined inner perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A8","text":"The Ego represented in “I think” is purely intellectual rather than an empirical representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A9","text":"Rational psychology must derive all its claims from “I think” while excluding every determinate empirical predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A10","text":"Necessary status as the subject of judgment does not determine the self as a subsisting substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A11","text":"The logical simplicity of apperception does not determine the self as a simple substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A12","text":"The identity of apperception across representations does not determine the identity of a person or substance through change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A13","text":"The distinction between the thinking self and external objects does not determine the possibility of existence apart from those objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A14","text":"Metaphysical predicates of the soul are synthetic determinations and cannot be obtained through analysis of “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A15","text":"Abstraction from the empirical conditions of self-representation does not establish possible existence apart from those conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A16","text":"Transcendental illusion arises when necessary conditions for thinking an object are transferred into properties of the object as it exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A17","text":"Consciousness and the other faculties possess intensive degree and can be conceived as diminishing toward disappearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:A18","text":"Practical extension of existence beyond experience is distinct from theoretical cognition of the soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX1","text":"The psychological paralogism is not reducible to an ordinary content-neutral defect of logical form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX2","text":"Mere apperception is neither an intuition of the self nor a determinate concept of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX3","text":"The problematical use of “I think” is not the Cartesian inference from thought to a separately cognized substantial existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX4","text":"Being necessarily represented as the subject of judgment is not being known as substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX5","text":"Logical indivisibility of the Ego is not knowledge of an incorruptible simple substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX6","text":"Continuity of apperception is not proof of personal identity through all temporal change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX7","text":"Distinguishability from the body is not proof of possible existence without the body.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX8","text":"Empirical psychology is not rational psychology and cannot yield apodeictic knowledge of non-empirical properties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX9","text":"Mere absence of contradiction does not establish objective possibility or actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX10","text":"Simplicity does not prove the permanence or immortality of the soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX11","text":"Neither a soulless materialism nor a dogmatic spiritualism is warranted by self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX12","text":"The difference between inner and outer appearances does not prove an intrinsic heterogeneity between soul and body as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:NX13","text":"Critical renunciation of speculative immortality does not abolish the practical ground for admitting a future life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:D1","text":"Rational psychology’s sole admissible source is too indeterminate to yield metaphysical predicates, while adding object-determining content would convert the project into empirical psychology.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A2","CH_B337_CIPP:A3","CH_B337_CIPP:A5","CH_B337_CIPP:A9","CH_B337_CIPP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:D2","text":"The inference to substance equivocates between a being that can be represented as subject under every objective relation and the Ego that functions only as the subject of judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A3","CH_B337_CIPP:A4","CH_B337_CIPP:A5","CH_B337_CIPP:A10","CH_B337_CIPP:A14","CH_B337_CIPP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:D3","text":"The simplicity of the representation “I” yields only logical unity and leaves the composition or non-composition of the represented subject unknown.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A5","CH_B337_CIPP:A11","CH_B337_CIPP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:D4","text":"Identity within the unity of consciousness cannot establish personal identity because it does not determine the intuited subject across time.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A6","CH_B337_CIPP:A12","CH_B337_CIPP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:D5","text":"Abstraction from external objects cannot establish separability because abstraction removes conditions from representation without proving existence apart from them.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A6","CH_B337_CIPP:A13","CH_B337_CIPP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:D6","text":"Immateriality, incorruptibility, personality, spirituality, and immortality lose their basis when the four primary categorical determinations of the soul fail.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A10","CH_B337_CIPP:A11","CH_B337_CIPP:A12","CH_B337_CIPP:A13","CH_B337_CIPP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:D7","text":"The category of substance cannot determine the self’s mode of existence or persistence in time because inner intuition supplies no permanent object corresponding to it.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A4","CH_B337_CIPP:A6","CH_B337_CIPP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:D8","text":"Even if the soul were conceded to be non-composite, the possible gradual diminution of its intensive reality would prevent simplicity from securing permanence.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A11","CH_B337_CIPP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:D9","text":"The logical unity of apperception resists a merely spatial definition of the thinking subject but cannot establish a spiritual substance.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A3","CH_B337_CIPP:A4","CH_B337_CIPP:A5","CH_B337_CIPP:A11","CH_B337_CIPP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:D10","text":"A rational psychology that makes temporal self-determination independent of external things renders external existence gratuitous and produces problematical idealism.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A6","CH_B337_CIPP:A13","CH_B337_CIPP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:D11","text":"Claims about all thinking beings arise by transferring the form of one’s own self-consciousness rather than by cognizing those beings as objects.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A1","CH_B337_CIPP:A2","CH_B337_CIPP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SD1","text":"Rational psychology is governed by an inverse relation between purity and objectivity: excluding empirical determination preserves its rational status by eliminating the material required for cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A2","CH_B337_CIPP:A3","CH_B337_CIPP:A5","CH_B337_CIPP:A9","CH_B337_CIPP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SD2","text":"The transcendental subject cannot cognize itself through the categories merely by being their bearer, because every such application already presupposes the self-consciousness it seeks to determine.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A1","CH_B337_CIPP:A2","CH_B337_CIPP:A3","CH_B337_CIPP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SD3","text":"The illusion is structurally recurrent because the universal condition of thought necessarily appears available for conversion into a universal property of thinking beings.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A1","CH_B337_CIPP:A2","CH_B337_CIPP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SD4","text":"Speculative indeterminacy produces a symmetrical law of renunciation under which spiritualist and materialist dogmatisms lose equal authority.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A3","CH_B337_CIPP:A4","CH_B337_CIPP:A10","CH_B337_CIPP:A11","CH_B337_CIPP:A12","CH_B337_CIPP:A13","CH_B337_CIPP:A14","CH_B337_CIPP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SD5","text":"Self-consciousness divides into spontaneous thought and receptive inner intuition, but neither pole supplies cognition of the self as a thing in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A5","CH_B337_CIPP:A6","CH_B337_CIPP:A7","CH_B337_CIPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0026","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SD6","text":"The subject that grounds temporal representation cannot determine its own substantial persistence in time from that grounding alone.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A5","CH_B337_CIPP:A6","CH_B337_CIPP:A7","CH_B337_CIPP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SD7","text":"Theoretical closure relocates transcendence into practical orientation without converting practical necessity into knowledge of a supersensible object.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A4","CH_B337_CIPP:A14","CH_B337_CIPP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:DNX1","text":"First-person grammatical invariance cannot function as evidence of ontological subsistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:DNX2","text":"Conditions under which a being must be represented cannot be identified with conditions under which that being exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:DNX3","text":"Immediate self-awareness does not entail transparency to the self’s noumenal constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:DNX4","text":"Analytic necessity within thought cannot generate a synthetic determination of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:DNX5","text":"The capacity to omit empirical conditions in thought cannot generate real independence from those conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:DNX6","text":"Logical conceivability cannot substitute for the intuition required to establish objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:DNX7","text":"The subject cannot assume a standpoint outside the conditions of its own cognition in order to inspect itself as a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:DNX8","text":"The failure of one metaphysical account cannot authorize its contrary as the default constitution of the self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:DNX9","text":"Intellectual determination through the moral law cannot supply the theoretical predicates withheld by the absence of supersensible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SNX1","text":"The system is organized around the absent intuition of the transcendental subject designated by x.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SNX2","text":"The absence of a permanent datum in inner sense blocks every speculative passage from temporal consciousness to substantial persistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SNX3","text":"No experiential standpoint remains from which personal continuity beyond death could be identified or verified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SNX4","text":"The foreclosed bridge from the self as a condition of cognition to the self as a constituted being is the terminal absence behind all four paralogisms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SNX5","text":"Future life is withheld from theoretical possession and retained only as a practically structured possibility or necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:SNX6","text":"The communion of soul and body remains unavailable as positive knowledge once intrinsic heterogeneity is denied without the underlying mode of interaction becoming cognizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0025","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:FGS1","text":"The input is restricted to “I think” after every determinate empirical predicate has been excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:FGS2","text":"The capture operation treats the unity of apperception as though it were an intuition of the thinking subject as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:FGS3","text":"Categorical projection converts logical subjecthood into substance, logical unity into simplicity, apperceptive identity into personality, and distinction from external objects into separability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:FGS4","text":"The validity test asks whether an intuition supplies the objective condition required for each categorical predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:FGS5","text":"The projected predicates fail because apperception supplies only the form of thought, while inner sense supplies only temporal phenomenal data.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:FGS6","text":"The permanence branch fails independently because non-composition does not exclude extinction through intensive diminution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:FGS7","text":"Failed projection produces speculative indeterminacy, symmetrical prohibition of opposed dogmatisms, and the conversion of rational psychology into a limiting discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:FGS8","text":"Speculative closure is followed by practical redirection toward freedom, moral vocation, and future existence without theoretical cognition of the soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:AF1","text":"The principal movement is: “I think” as condition of thought → purification into the sole text of psychology → reification as an intuited self → categorical projection → metaphysical soul-predicates → intuition test → paralogism → speculative ignorance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:AF2","text":"The terminal movement is: speculative ignorance → equal renunciation by materialism and spiritualism → rational psychology as discipline → practical redirection of supersensible claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ME1","text":"“I think” is reclassified from a minimal cognition of a soul-object into the formal condition accompanying cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ME2","text":"Substantiality, simplicity, personality, and separability are reclassified from discovered properties into ontologized logical functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ME3","text":"The self divides into the determining subject of thought and the determinable self given phenomenally in inner intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ME4","text":"Materialism and spiritualism cease to form an exhaustive metaphysical opposition and become symmetrical transgressions of the same cognitive boundary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ME5","text":"Rationalist proof of an independently self-determining soul is shown to generate problematical idealism rather than secure realism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ME6","text":"Rational psychology changes jurisdiction from positive doctrine to critical discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ME7","text":"Immortality moves from the terrain of speculative demonstration to that of practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ME8","text":"The soul-body problem is redrawn from an interaction between intrinsically heterogeneous substances into an unknowable relation beneath differently conditioned appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0025","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ALT1","text":"Surface: The explicit issue is whether “I think” proves the soul to be substantial, simple, personally identical, separable, and permanent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ALT2","text":"Structural: The operative mechanism is the substitution of logical apperception for object-giving intuition, followed by an illegitimate application of categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The critique replaces competition among metaphysical doctrines with a symmetrical limitation on speculative assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: The self’s mode of subsistence, composition, persistence, and noumenal identity remain indeterminate because temporal self-consciousness supplies no permanent object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:ALT5","text":"Practical: Supersensible existence remains available only through moral and practical orientation, not through constitutive knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the four paralogisms, the critique of permanence, the symmetrical limitation of materialism and spiritualism, and the practical redirection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:MRT2","text":"The system is internally coherent wherever it maintains the requirement that objective categorical predication must be grounded in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:MRT3","text":"Its strongest operation is the separation of logical subjecthood, unity, and identity from substantiality, simplicity, and personality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:MRT4","text":"The relation between the purely intellectual Ego and the empirical proposition “I exist thinking” remains delicately underdetermined despite the distinction between representation and application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0021","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0027","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:MRT5","text":"The argument depends on prior critical results concerning categories, noumena, inner sense, and permanence rather than independently re-establishing them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:MRT6","text":"The opening characterization of transcendental paralogism as formally correct sits in tension with the later diagnosis of a sophisma figurae dictionis grounded in equivocation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:MRT7","text":"The claim that apperception is real and simple supports an anti-materialist limit while the argument simultaneously denies that logical simplicity determines ontology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:MRT8","text":"The refutation of Mendelssohn establishes that simplicity is insufficient for permanence, not that intensive extinction actually occurs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:MRT9","text":"The practical retention of future life depends on a theoretical-practical distinction and teleological-moral premises that the paralogism critique itself does not generate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CIPP:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction preserves the chapter’s real movement only if its final product is understood as disciplined non-cognition rather than a positive theory of the soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CIPP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 59","title":"Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 59"}},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding","order":23,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"d759b18c3cf6896354add76e525048606066165a2380c240591ebe0b9ce6b43b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:AS1","text":"The problem is to exhibit systematically the pure a priori judgements actually produced by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:AS2","text":"The categories provide the natural and secure guide because their application to possible experience constitutes pure a priori cognition of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:AS3","text":"The relation between the categories and sensibility must therefore yield a complete catalogue of the transcendental principles governing the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:AS4","text":"A priori principles require proof from the subjective conditions that make cognition of objects possible, even when no higher objective principle can ground them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:AS5","text":"The inquiry is restricted to principles concerning the categories, while distinguishing them from principles of sensibility, mathematics, and analytical judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:AS6","text":"The argument moves from the category-table to a bounded system of principles, then clarifies synthetic a priori judgement through its opposition to analytical judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A1","text":"The categories are the pure concepts of the understanding whose application to possible experience constitutes pure a priori cognition of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A2","text":"The relation of the categories to sensibility determines the complete systematic catalogue of the transcendental principles of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A3","text":"A principle is a priori when it grounds other judgements and is not itself grounded in a higher or more general cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A4","text":"An a priori principle can require proof even when no higher cognition exists from which an objective proof could be derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A5","text":"The possibility of cognition of an object can be proved from the subjective sources that make such cognition possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A6","text":"Proof is necessary to prevent an a priori principle from appearing to be a gratuitous assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A7","text":"The present system concerns only principles related to the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A8","text":"Principles of transcendental aesthetic concern the conditions under which things can appear in space and time, not the categorical principles of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A9","text":"Mathematical principles are drawn from intuition rather than from pure concepts of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A10","text":"The possibility of mathematical synthetic a priori principles must nevertheless be rendered conceivable within the present inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A11","text":"Analytical judgement must be distinguished from synthetic judgement to determine the proper nature of the latter without ambiguity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:A12","text":"Synthetic a priori judgements are the proper subject of the systematic inquiry into principles of the pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:NX1","text":"The system is not restricted to principles that have an objective proof from a higher cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:NX2","text":"A priori status is not immunity from the need for proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:NX3","text":"The present inquiry is not a system of all principles of transcendental aesthetic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:NX4","text":"The principles of space and time are not applied to objects considered as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:NX5","text":"Mathematical principles are not derived from pure concepts of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:NX6","text":"The mathematical principles are not included here in order to prove their accuracy or apodeictic certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:NX7","text":"The inquiry is not confined to analytical judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:NX8","text":"Synthetic a priori principles are not treated as self-explanatory merely because they are evident.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:D1","text":"The category-table can function as the organizing principle for the entire catalogue of pure principles of the understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A1","CH_B337_CISA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:D2","text":"The systematic completeness of the principles depends on establishing how each category applies to sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A1","CH_B337_CISA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:D3","text":"The absence of a higher objective ground does not eliminate the possibility of a legitimate proof of an a priori principle.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A3","CH_B337_CISA:A4","CH_B337_CISA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:D4","text":"The proof of an a priori principle must be transcendental rather than deduced from a still higher empirical or conceptual law.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A4","CH_B337_CISA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:D5","text":"The subjective conditions of object-cognition can provide objective validity to principles governing possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A1","CH_B337_CISA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:D6","text":"The category-system requires a boundary between principles grounded in understanding and principles grounded in intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A7","CH_B337_CISA:A8","CH_B337_CISA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:D7","text":"Mathematical synthetic a priori cognition belongs to the field of possibility under examination without belonging to the core derivation of categorical principles.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A9","CH_B337_CISA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:D8","text":"The contrast between analytical and synthetic judgement functions as a clarification device for the theory of synthetic a priori judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A11","CH_B337_CISA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:SD1","text":"The pure understanding becomes a bounded system whose principles are generated by the interaction of categorical concepts with the conditions of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A1","CH_B337_CISA:A2","CH_B337_CISA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:SD2","text":"The validity of foundational principles is relocated from derivation out of higher propositions to justification through the conditions of possible object-cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A3","CH_B337_CISA:A4","CH_B337_CISA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:SD3","text":"The system separates the sources of cognition without isolating them, since understanding supplies pure concepts while sensibility supplies the conditions of their possible application.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A1","CH_B337_CISA:A2","CH_B337_CISA:A8","CH_B337_CISA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:SD4","text":"The distinction between analytical and synthetic judgement converts a classificatory opposition into a methodological condition for determining the proper domain of transcendental principles.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A11","CH_B337_CISA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:SD5","text":"Completeness is treated as a structural consequence of the category-system rather than as an accidental accumulation of separate principles.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A1","CH_B337_CISA:A2","CH_B337_CISA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:DNX1","text":"The legitimacy of a foundational principle is not exhausted by its being ungrounded in a higher proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:DNX2","text":"The proof of pure principles cannot depend on the ordinary model of proof as deduction from more general premises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:DNX3","text":"Cognition of objects cannot be explained by understanding alone, independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:DNX4","text":"Sensibility cannot by itself supply the categorical principles governing the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:DNX5","text":"The systematic unity of pure cognition cannot be secured by treating categories, spatial-temporal conditions, and mathematical intuitions as one undifferentiated source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:DNX6","text":"The possibility of synthetic a priori cognition cannot be explained solely through analysis of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:SNX1","text":"The system excludes the possibility that foundational cognition can rest on an arbitrary assertion lacking a transcendental justification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a cognition of objects wholly detached from the conditions under which objects can be given in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a complete account of pure cognition that bypasses the relation between understanding and sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:SNX4","text":"The system excludes the collapse of synthetic a priori judgement into either mere conceptual analysis or mere intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:SNX5","text":"The system leaves no place for a principle that is simultaneously categorical in source, independent of all conditions of sensibility, and applicable to objects as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:FGS1","text":"The category-table supplies the formal inventory of pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:FGS2","text":"The application of those concepts to possible experience identifies the domain of pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:FGS3","text":"The relation between categorical concepts and sensibility generates the candidate principles of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:FGS4","text":"The absence of a higher ground redirects proof toward the subjective conditions of possible object-cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:FGS5","text":"Transcendental proof converts those subjective conditions into the basis for objective validity within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:FGS6","text":"Boundary-setting removes principles belonging to transcendental aesthetic and intuition from the categorical system while retaining their possibility as an adjacent problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:FGS7","text":"The opposition between analytical and synthetic judgement disambiguates the target class of principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:FGS8","text":"Category → Relation to sensibility → Transcendental principle → Subjective proof of objective validity → Systematic catalogue of synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:AF1","text":"Unsystematized pure judgements → Category-table as guide → Relation of categories to sensibility → Complete catalogue of principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:AF2","text":"Ungrounded foundational status → Risk of gratuitous assertion → Transcendental proof from conditions of object-cognition → Justified a priori principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:AF3","text":"Mixed field of pure cognition → Source distinction between understanding and intuition → Restricted categorical system → Clarified domain of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:AF4","text":"Ambiguity concerning synthetic judgement → Opposition to analytical judgement → Determinate object of inquiry → System of synthetic a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:ME1","text":"The category-table is transformed from a classification of concepts into a generative map of principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:ME2","text":"Subjective conditions of cognition become legitimate grounds for establishing objective validity within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:ME3","text":"The field of pure a priori cognition is divided according to its sources: understanding, sensibility, and intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:ME4","text":"The boundary between phenomena and things in themselves becomes a governing limit on the applicability of spatial-temporal principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:ME5","text":"Mathematical cognition is repositioned as adjacent to, but not identical with, the categorical system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:ME6","text":"Analytical judgement is reclassified as a contrastive instrument for determining the structure of synthetic a priori judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:ALT1","text":"Surface — The chapter announces a systematic presentation of the a priori judgements produced by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:ALT2","text":"Structural — The system is organized by the interaction between categories and the conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument establishes criteria for inclusion, exclusion, proof, and systematic completeness within transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Cognition is restricted to objects as they can appear under the conditions of possible experience, while things in themselves remain outside categorical application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s central movement from the category-table to a systematic catalogue of transcendental principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:MRT2","text":"The axioms preserve the distinction between categorical principles, principles of sensibility, mathematical principles, and analytical judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:MRT3","text":"The proof-structure is reconstructible as a transcendental justification from subjective conditions of possible object-cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:MRT4","text":"The extracted system preserves the restriction of validity to objects of possible experience rather than things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:MRT5","text":"The detailed form of the individual principles remains underdetermined because the passage establishes the system’s method and boundaries rather than enumerating its subsequent contents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:MRT6","text":"The main unresolved tension is that subjective conditions are used to ground objective validity without the passage fully displaying the transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISA:MRT7","text":"The system is internally coherent at the level of architecture, but its completeness depends on the later derivation of particular principles from particular categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 44","title":"Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding","context":"Chapter 44"}},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding","order":24,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"c23df0725dff3aa80324b11e590b4d6b9b02f281342e14f419a1f4f89b4f44a5","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:AS1","text":"The application of a concept to an object requires homogeneity between concept and intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:AS2","text":"Categories and sensible intuitions are heterogeneous, so their subsumption cannot be immediate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:AS3","text":"A pure yet sensible-intellectual mediating representation is required to connect categories with phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:AS4","text":"Time-determinations generated by imagination provide this mediation as transcendental schemata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:AS5","text":"Schemata give categories objective significance only by making phenomena rule-governedly determinable in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:AS6","text":"The same sensible mediation that realizes categories restricts them to possible experience and excludes constitutive application to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A1","text":"Subsumption under a concept requires a representation homogeneous with both the concept and the object represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A2","text":"Pure concepts of understanding are heterogeneous with all sensible intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A3","text":"A valid application of a category requires a third representation mediating between category and phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A4","text":"The mediating representation must be pure, intellectual in relation to the category, and sensible in relation to the phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A5","text":"Time is the a priori form containing the manifold of inner sense and thus accompanies every empirical representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A6","text":"A transcendental time-determination is homogeneous with categories through a priori rule-governed universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A7","text":"A transcendental time-determination is homogeneous with phenomena because time belongs to every sensible representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A8","text":"A schema is a rule-governed synthesis of imagination for determining intuition, not an image of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A9","text":"The schema of a pure category is the category’s pure synthesis expressed as a rule for the temporal determination of inner sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A10","text":"Categories can determine objects only insofar as objects are given under the formal conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A11","text":"The unity of schematized intuition is indirectly the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A12","text":"Categories without schemata retain logical function but lack objective meaning and application to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:A13","text":"All objectively significant use of categories is restricted to phenomena within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:NX1","text":"Categories are not sensible predicates discoverable in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:NX2","text":"The application of categories to phenomena is not an immediate subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:NX3","text":"A schema is not an empirical image, a particular figure, or a surveyable presentation adequate to a general concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:NX4","text":"A schema is not an independently given object but a procedure of imagination for determining intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:NX5","text":"Pure categories are not objectively meaningful when abstracted from all conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:NX6","text":"Categories are not constitutively applicable to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:NX7","text":"Transcendental truth is not cognition of objects beyond the totality of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:NX8","text":"The realization of understanding through sensibility is not an enlargement of its legitimate objective domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:D1","text":"The problem of category-application is transformed from a question of conceptual inclusion into a question of mediation by temporal rule.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A1","CH_B337_CISP:A2","CH_B337_CISP:A3","CH_B337_CISP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:D2","text":"Imagination occupies a transcendental function because it produces the temporal synthesis through which understanding reaches sensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A4","CH_B337_CISP:A8","CH_B337_CISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:D3","text":"Number functions as the schema of quantity because successive synthesis generates time as homogeneous multiplicity.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A5","CH_B337_CISP:A8","CH_B337_CISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:D4","text":"Reality and negation become temporally determinable as degrees by the filling or emptying of time through sensation.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A5","CH_B337_CISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:D5","text":"Substance becomes objectively applicable only as permanence in time, through which temporal succession and coexistence can be determined.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A5","CH_B337_CISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:D6","text":"Causality becomes objectively applicable only as rule-governed succession in time.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A5","CH_B337_CISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:D7","text":"Reciprocity becomes objectively applicable only as rule-governed coexistence in time.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A5","CH_B337_CISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:D8","text":"Modal categories determine an object’s relation to time rather than supplying further sensible content.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A5","CH_B337_CISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:D9","text":"Objective experience is a unified temporal order of phenomena under categorial rules.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A9","CH_B337_CISP:A11","CH_B337_CISP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:SD1","text":"The objective validity of categories is neither derived from empirical induction nor secured by purely logical analysis, but is constituted through rule-governed temporal synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A5","CH_B337_CISP:A9","CH_B337_CISP:A12","CH_B337_CISP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:SD2","text":"Sensibility is simultaneously the enabling condition and limiting condition of understanding’s objective employment.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A7","CH_B337_CISP:A10","CH_B337_CISP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:SD3","text":"The unity of experience is generated through a layered coordination of receptivity, imagination, understanding, and apperception.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A5","CH_B337_CISP:A8","CH_B337_CISP:A9","CH_B337_CISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:SD4","text":"The distinction between logical significance and objective significance permits categories to remain thinkable outside experience while denying them determinate objects there.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A12","CH_B337_CISP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:SD5","text":"Transcendental truth is relocated from correspondence with supersensible objects to the a priori conditions under which empirical truth is possible.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A11","CH_B337_CISP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:DNX1","text":"No direct identity between intellectual form and sensible given can ground objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:DNX2","text":"No object can be known merely because it can be thought under a category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:DNX3","text":"No non-temporal employment of the categories can yield determinate cognition for finite knowers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:DNX4","text":"No image can furnish the universality required by a pure concept or category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:DNX5","text":"No objective validity belongs to understanding independently of receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:SNX1","text":"Cognition of things as they are independently of the conditions under which they can be given is structurally unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:SNX2","text":"An intellect whose concepts would immediately give or intuit their objects is excluded as the model of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:SNX3","text":"The ultimate ground of the imagination’s schematizing operation remains unavailable to discursive self-knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:FGS1","text":"Concept–intuition heterogeneity → failure of immediate subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:FGS2","text":"Failure of immediate subsumption → necessity of a third representation homogeneous with both terms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:FGS3","text":"Time as universal sensible form + category as rule of unity → transcendental time-determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:FGS4","text":"Transcendental time-determination + productive imagination → schema as rule for synthesizing and determining intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:FGS5","text":"Schema + category + phenomenon → objective application of the category within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:FGS6","text":"Objective application through sensibility → realization and simultaneous restriction of categories to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:FGS7","text":"Restriction to possible experience → logical residue without objective cognition beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:AF1","text":"Heterogeneity of category and intuition → necessity of mediation → temporal schema → imaginative synthesis → empirical significance of categories → restriction of categories to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:ME1","text":"The relation between concept and object is reclassified from direct representation to mediated rule-governed temporal synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:ME2","text":"Imagination is reclassified from a merely reproductive or pictorial faculty into the operator of transcendental object-application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:ME3","text":"Time is reclassified from a form of subjective receptivity into the universal medium through which categories acquire empirical meaning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:ME4","text":"The distinction between logical thinkability and objective validity becomes available as a boundary between empty categories and cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:ME5","text":"The apparent opposition between the a priori necessity of categories and their restriction to experience collapses, because their necessity concerns the form of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text explains how pure categories such as substance, causality, possibility, reality, and necessity apply to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text locates objective cognition in the coordination of sensibility, imagination, understanding, and apperception through temporal synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text resolves the threat of transcendent category-use by converting the question of metaphysical reach into a question of conditions of application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects of possible cognition are constituted as temporally determinable phenomena rather than as things independently available to pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central transition from category–intuition heterogeneity to schematized empirical application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:MRT2","text":"The system depends principally on the claim that time is universally present in sensible representation and can be determined according to categorial rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:MRT3","text":"The specific category-schemata are reconstructible as differentiated modes of temporal determination: series, filling, order, and totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent if imagination can genuinely synthesize a sensible manifold under intellectual rules without becoming either an additional faculty of understanding or an empirical psychological process.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:MRT5","text":"The account leaves the productive mechanism of schematizing underdetermined, since its “hidden art” is asserted as necessary while its operation remains unavailable to explicit analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:MRT6","text":"The system also retains a tension between the claim that categories have residual logical significance apart from sensibility and the claim that they lack any object-determining meaning there.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CISP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if realization and restriction are retained as one operation rather than treated as separate conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CISP:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 43","title":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding","context":"Chapter 43"}},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2","layer":"CH","title":"Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason","order":25,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"1c10c835288f8334cefd0e49453f6147315a5fc28ee6fe66a19f9d728679e083","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:AS1","text":"Cognition cannot attain scientific status while it remains a rhapsodic aggregate lacking necessary unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:AS2","text":"Reason converts cognition into science by supplying an idea that determines the whole, its end, its limits, and the position of its parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:AS3","text":"The argument is reconstructive, taxonomic, and critical because it establishes the conditions of systematic cognition, classifies rational knowledge, and restricts metaphysics to its legitimate form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:AS4","text":"The movement proceeds from aggregate cognition to architectonic unity, from received knowledge to rational self-production, and from scholastic organization to cosmical legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:AS5","text":"Philosophy is displaced from possession of a completed doctrine to approximation toward an uninstantiated rational archetype through the practice of philosophizing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:AS6","text":"The cosmical end of reason generates the division of philosophy into nature and freedom and the subordinate architecture of pure philosophy and metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:AS7","text":"The argument closes by converting metaphysics from an unsuccessful speculative foundation into an indispensable critical censor that orders cognition toward humanity’s highest end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A1","text":"Knowledge becomes science only through systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A2","text":"A system is the unity of diverse cognitions under one idea supplied by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A3","text":"The idea of a system determines a priori its end, form, limits, and the position of every part.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A4","text":"An architectonic schema arranges the contents of a science a priori according to its highest internal aim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A5","text":"Classification by empirical similarity or accidental use produces only technical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A6","text":"The rational idea of a science exists initially as an undeveloped germ and becomes explicit through the organization of accumulated cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A7","text":"Particular systems of cognition are members of one grand system of human knowledge grounded in reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A8","text":"Cognition is subjectively rational only when its possessor draws it independently from principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A9","text":"Philosophical cognition proceeds through concepts, whereas mathematical cognition proceeds through the construction of concepts in pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A10","text":"Objective philosophy is an uninstantiated archetype that serves as the standard for judging subjective attempts at philosophizing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A11","text":"Cosmical philosophy relates all cognition to the ultimate and essential aims of human reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A12","text":"The destination of humanity is the single ultimate end under which every other rational aim is subordinated as a means.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A13","text":"Philosophy legislates for reason across nature as what is and freedom as what ought to be, whose systems are ultimately to be united.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A14","text":"Pure philosophy is cognition grounded in pure reason and is organized as critical propædeutic and systematic metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A15","text":"Metaphysics divides according to the speculative and practical uses of pure reason into the metaphysics of nature and the metaphysics of ethics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A16","text":"Metaphysical cognition is delimited by its a priori origin and conceptual method rather than by its degree of generality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A17","text":"Speculative metaphysics comprises ontology, immanent rational physiology as rational physics and psychology, and transcendent physiology as rational cosmology and theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:A18","text":"Reason’s natural tendency toward dialectic makes critical metaphysics an indispensable discipline of rational self-knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX1","text":"An aggregate of cognitions is not a science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX2","text":"A system is not enlarged by arbitrary external additions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX3","text":"Empirical similarity and contingent utility do not establish architectonic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX4","text":"A science is not finally defined by its founder’s initial description or by inherited disciplinary usage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX5","text":"Memorized philosophical doctrine is not subjectively rational cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX6","text":"Imitation of another mind is not productive employment of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX7","text":"The philosopher is not merely an artist or specialist who arranges concepts for subordinate ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX8","text":"Completed philosophy is not an extant doctrine that can be learned as a finished possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX9","text":"Metaphysics is not distinguished from empirical cognition by greater generality alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX10","text":"Metaphysical cognition is not mathematical cognition, despite their common a priori origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX11","text":"Empirical psychology is not a constitutive part of pure metaphysics, even when retained provisionally as an appendix.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX12","text":"Metaphysics is neither the foundation of religion nor primarily an instrument for extending speculative knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:NX13","text":"The legitimacy of metaphysics is not determined by disappointed expectations or accidental historical effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:D1","text":"Unity of end makes the parts of a scientific system mutually determining, so omissions become detectable and arbitrary additions become inadmissible.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A2","CH_B337_CI_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:D2","text":"Scientific growth strengthens and articulates internally determined parts rather than appending unrelated material.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A3","CH_B337_CI_2:A4","CH_B337_CI_2:A5","CH_B337_CI_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:D3","text":"The rational idea disclosed by the accumulated parts of a science can correct the science’s founder and its inherited definition.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A2","CH_B337_CI_2:A3","CH_B337_CI_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:D4","text":"An architectonic of all human cognition is possible because particular sciences participate in one reason-governed whole.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A2","CH_B337_CI_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:D5","text":"Mathematical knowledge can remain subjectively rational when learned because its content is reproduced through necessary construction, whereas received philosophical knowledge remains historical.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A8","CH_B337_CI_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:D6","text":"Philosophy cannot be rationally possessed as an inherited doctrine; only the activity of philosophizing through the testing of principles can be learned.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A8","CH_B337_CI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:D7","text":"The cosmical philosopher functions as a lawgiver of reason, while mathematicians, natural philosophers, and logicians function as subordinate conceptual artists.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A11","CH_B337_CI_2:A12","CH_B337_CI_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:D8","text":"Moral philosophy occupies the superior position because it concerns the ultimate destination to which other rational enterprises are subordinated.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A12","CH_B337_CI_2:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:D9","text":"Empirical psychology belongs properly to applied philosophy alongside empirical physics because its principles do not possess the origin required for metaphysical inclusion.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A14","CH_B337_CI_2:A16","CH_B337_CI_2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:D10","text":"Critical metaphysics prevents lawless speculation from damaging morality and religion and therefore serves religion as a bulwark rather than as its foundation.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A15","CH_B337_CI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:D11","text":"Mathematics, natural science, and common experience require philosophical legislation if their operations are to be directed beyond accidental ends toward the happiness of humanity.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A11","CH_B337_CI_2:A12","CH_B337_CI_2:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SD1","text":"Scientific legitimacy is fundamentally teleological: coherence becomes architectonic only when an internal end determines the whole and its boundaries.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A1","CH_B337_CI_2:A2","CH_B337_CI_2:A3","CH_B337_CI_2:A4","CH_B337_CI_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SD2","text":"Reason is simultaneously the source of systematic order, the object subjected to critique, and the authority that legislates the ends of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A2","CH_B337_CI_2:A11","CH_B337_CI_2:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:A14","CH_B337_CI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SD3","text":"Rational ownership of knowledge depends less on the objective content possessed than on whether the subject can regenerate, test, and reject it from principles.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A8","CH_B337_CI_2:A9","CH_B337_CI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SD4","text":"Progress within a genuine system is intensive articulation of an immanent rational germ rather than indefinite accumulation of externally supplied material.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A3","CH_B337_CI_2:A4","CH_B337_CI_2:A5","CH_B337_CI_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SD5","text":"The autonomy of particular sciences is subordinated to a single philosophical teleology that assigns them instrumental roles within humanity’s rational destination.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A7","CH_B337_CI_2:A11","CH_B337_CI_2:A12","CH_B337_CI_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SD6","text":"Metaphysics retains supreme authority not by maximizing positive speculative discovery but by policing boundaries, preventing error, and coordinating the uses of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A14","CH_B337_CI_2:A16","CH_B337_CI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SD7","text":"Transcendent objects are incorporated into the architectonic of metaphysics so that their rational claims can be delimited critically rather than confirmed empirically.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A16","CH_B337_CI_2:A17","CH_B337_CI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SD8","text":"The absent completion of philosophy functions as a regulative authority over present philosophical practice despite not being available as an achieved science.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A6","CH_B337_CI_2:A10","CH_B337_CI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:DNX1","text":"No neutral accumulation of true propositions can become science independently of a unifying rational end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:DNX2","text":"No empirical taxonomy can establish the necessary completeness or limits of a science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:DNX3","text":"No authority, tradition, or mastery of received doctrine can substitute for the subject’s productive use of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:DNX4","text":"No plurality of independent ultimate ends can govern the system of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:DNX5","text":"No specialized science can determine its own final value without philosophical legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:DNX6","text":"No continuous ascent from empirical generality to metaphysical first principles can erase the difference in their origins.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:DNX7","text":"No unregulated speculative reason can remain harmless in the domains of morality and religion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:DNX8","text":"No currently existing subjective philosophy can identify itself without remainder with the objective archetype of philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SNX1","text":"The absent completed philosophy is the constitutive lack that permits every actual philosophy to be judged as an approximation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SNX2","text":"The absence of a transparent initial plan makes architectonic knowledge retrospective: reason’s germ becomes visible only after technical accumulation has exposed its latent unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SNX3","text":"A non-teleological conception of science is foreclosed because cognition without orientation toward rational ends is reduced to aggregate or technique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SNX4","text":"Reason cannot exit metaphysics or its own dialectical tendency; it can only submit that tendency to lawful self-criticism or suffer its destructive effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SNX5","text":"A wholly self-contained rational physiology is unavailable because experience must minimally provide the objects whose metaphysical conditions are then considered a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:SNX6","text":"The final unity of nature and freedom remains absent from the presented architecture even though that future union supplies its ultimate systematic horizon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:FGS1","text":"The primary production sequence is: rhapsodic cognition → rational idea of a whole → a priori schema → internal articulation and limitation → systematic unity → science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:FGS2","text":"The developmental sequence is: latent rational germ → technical collection of materials → retrospective disclosure of natural affinity → correction of inherited definitions → architectonic reconstruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:FGS3","text":"The subjective cognition filter is: reception from data → historical possession; derivation from principles → rational possession; concepts → philosophy; constructed concepts → mathematics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:FGS4","text":"The philosophical elevation sequence is: scholastic system → absent objective archetype → cosmical relation of cognition to final ends → philosopher as lawgiver → subordination of specialist sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:FGS5","text":"The taxonomic machine is: pure reason → critical propædeutic and metaphysical system → speculative nature and practical ethics → ontology and rational physiology → immanent physics and psychology or transcendent cosmology and theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:FGS6","text":"The critical closure is: natural dialectic → speculative excess → metaphysical self-knowledge → boundary enforcement → prevention of error → coordination of cognition toward humanity’s highest end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:AF1","text":"Aggregate cognition → reason’s demand for unity → idea-governed schema → organic science → distinction between historical and rational possession → philosophy as active philosophizing → cosmical legislation by ultimate ends → architectonic division of metaphysics → critical defense of metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:AF2","text":"Human cognition → historical or rational → philosophical or mathematical → scholastic or cosmical philosophy → nature and freedom → pure or empirical philosophy → critique and metaphysics → nature and ethics → immanent and transcendent speculative branches.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ME1","text":"Science is reclassified from an accumulation of cognitions into an organism whose end determines its parts and boundaries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ME2","text":"Disciplinary unity is relocated from observable similarity to derivability from a supreme internal aim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ME3","text":"A discipline’s rational identity becomes separable from its founder’s intentions, inherited definition, and historical arrangement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ME4","text":"The historical-rational distinction is relocated from the objective source of a proposition to the subject’s mode of possessing it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ME5","text":"Philosophy is reclassified from a transmissible doctrinal corpus into both a regulative archetype and an autonomous practice of testing principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ME6","text":"Specialist sciences are repositioned as instruments within a philosophical legislation of reason rather than as self-sufficient authorities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ME7","text":"Metaphysics is bounded by origin and method rather than by a scale running from particular to increasingly general concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ME8","text":"Empirical psychology is relocated from constitutive metaphysics to applied philosophy, with only provisional annexation preserved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ME9","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from a failed engine of speculative expansion into the critical institution that regulates reason’s unavoidable metaphysical activity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ME10","text":"Nature and freedom cease to be independent domains and become coordinated branches within one teleological legislation of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ALT1","text":"Surface: The chapter defines architectonic, distinguishes systems from aggregates, and presents the formal divisions of philosophy and metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ALT2","text":"Structural: Scientific wholes are generated by reason-given ends that determine internal articulation, disciplinary limits, and relations among fields of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Philosophy is reframed as legislation over the uses and ends of knowledge, while metaphysics is repositioned as critical boundary administration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Systems are treated as organisms developing from rational germs, completed philosophy is projected as a future archetype, and reality is divided between nature as what is and freedom as what ought to be.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from systematic unity through the classification of cognition to the critical rehabilitation of metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:MRT2","text":"The structure is strongest where the idea-schema-system relation explains scientific unity, limits, and internal articulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:MRT3","text":"The exact derivation of ontology, rational physics, psychology, cosmology, and theology from the highest aim of reason remains asserted more than demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:MRT4","text":"The claim that architectonic divisions are a priori and immutable remains in tension with their retrospective discovery through accumulated materials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:MRT5","text":"The absence of completed philosophy remains in tension with the present claim that its architectonic division possesses legislative authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:MRT6","text":"The exclusion of empirical elements from metaphysics is qualified by rational physiology’s dependence on minimally empirical object-givenness and by empirical psychology’s provisional annexation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:MRT7","text":"The privileged learnability and asserted certainty of mathematics depend on distinctions established outside this chapter rather than on the architectonic argument itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:MRT8","text":"The final union of nature and freedom and the identification of humanity’s highest aim with happiness function as teleological closures without being derived within the chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_CI_2:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if it retains the transition from formal system-building to epistemic autonomy, cosmical teleology, metaphysical taxonomy, and critical-censorial necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_CI_2:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 92","title":"Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 92"}},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI","layer":"CH","title":"Empicisim","order":26,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"657a4bb301a3603b0890fa6c83ceb356b70a6738987cfacd722b803f8450b762","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:AS1","text":"The argument seeks objective criteria for possibility, reality, and necessity while preventing their transcendental employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:AS2","text":"Modal predicates are reframed as relations between complete object-concepts and the faculties of cognition rather than as additional determinations of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:AS3","text":"Objective modal validity is grounded in formal intuition, perception, and the law-governed unity of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:AS4","text":"The argument proceeds by demonstrating the insufficiency of conceptual consistency and deriving the experiential conditions required for objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:AS5","text":"The refutation of idealism reverses the assumed priority of inner experience by making temporal self-determination dependent on external permanence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:AS6","text":"The movement is from modal criteria through experiential validation and externality to causal necessity and the restriction of understanding to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:AS7","text":"The argument is transcendental, critical, reconstructive, and polemically directed against conceptualist metaphysics and material idealism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A1","text":"Modal predicates express a complete object-concept’s relation to cognition rather than an added determination of the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A2","text":"A priori concepts and principles possess objective validity only as conditions of possible experience and its synthetic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A3","text":"Space and time are the a priori forms through which outer and inner appearances can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A4","text":"Objective possibility consists in agreement with the formal conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A5","text":"Logical noncontradiction is necessary but insufficient for objective possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A6","text":"A pure category acquires objective reality only through an intuition in which its synthesis can function as a condition of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A7","text":"An object is real when it is given in conscious sensation or lawfully connected with an actual perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A8","text":"Existence cannot be inferred from even a complete concept of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A9","text":"Every empirical determination of self-consciousness in time requires something permanent in perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A10","text":"Internal intuition presents no permanent item capable of determining temporal existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A11","text":"Matter in external spatial intuition supplies the permanent correlate required for temporal determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A12","text":"The reality of any particular external representation must be tested against the criteria of coherent experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A13","text":"Material necessity consists in the causal determination of states or effects under universal empirical laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A14","text":"Material necessity is valid only within possible experience and does not establish the necessary existence of substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A15","text":"Empirical synthesis is possible only as a continuous, law-governed unity under the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A16","text":"The understanding can determine only the synthesis of given phenomena, not absolute possibility or alternative totalities of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:A17","text":"External intuition is required to exhibit the objective reality of the categories of relation and quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX1","text":"Modal predicates do not enlarge or further determine the object-concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX2","text":"Freedom from contradiction does not establish the possibility of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX3","text":"Arbitrary recombinations of empirically borrowed concepts do not acquire objective possibility merely by remaining coherent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX4","text":"Existence is not a conceptual predicate and cannot be deduced from conceptual completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX5","text":"Immediate perception of the object itself is not the sole route to cognition of its reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX6","text":"Space is not treated as a property of things in themselves, so spatial objects cannot be dismissed as nonentities on that basis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX7","text":"Internal experience is not the uniquely immediate experience from which external existence must be uncertainly inferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX8","text":"The dependence of inner experience on external experience does not make every apparent external perception real.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX9","text":"Material necessity is not logical necessity in the connection of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX10","text":"Necessity does not establish the absolute or unconditioned existence of substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX11","text":"Blind chance, blind fate, leaps, and breaks cannot function as principles within empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX12","text":"Categories alone are not cognitions and cannot generate synthetic propositions about objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX13","text":"The conceivability of a thing’s nonexistence does not by itself establish its real contingency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX14","text":"Absolute possibility and multiple total spheres of experience are not objects decidable by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:NX15","text":"A postulate is not an immediately certain synthetic proposition exempt from deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D1","text":"Modal principles are subjectively synthetic because they determine the cognitive procedure through which an object-concept is assessed without augmenting the object.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A1","CH_B337_EMPI:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D2","text":"A synthetic concept is objectively possible only when its synthesis can occur within the formal structure of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A4","CH_B337_EMPI:A5","CH_B337_EMPI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D3","text":"Empirically borrowed concepts become chimerical when recombined without conformity to known experiential connections.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A2","CH_B337_EMPI:A4","CH_B337_EMPI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D4","text":"A priori mathematical construction establishes objective possibility because spatial construction employs the same formal synthesis required for external experience.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A3","CH_B337_EMPI:A4","CH_B337_EMPI:A6","CH_B337_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D5","text":"An unperceived object may count as real when empirical laws connect it with an actual perception, but existential cognition cannot outrun that perceptual network.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A7","CH_B337_EMPI:A8","CH_B337_EMPI:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D6","text":"Determinate internal experience is possible only through external experience because temporal determination requires an externally intuited permanent.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A9","CH_B337_EMPI:A10","CH_B337_EMPI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D7","text":"Problematic idealism’s epistemic priority is reversed without converting every external representation into a real object.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A9","CH_B337_EMPI:A10","CH_B337_EMPI:A11","CH_B337_EMPI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D8","text":"Dogmatic idealism loses its premise once space is treated as the form of external appearance rather than as a property of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A2","CH_B337_EMPI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D9","text":"Necessity is hypothetical and relational: given a cause under empirical law, its effect is necessary, but neither cause nor substance is thereby absolutely necessary.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A13","CH_B337_EMPI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D10","text":"The exclusions of chance, fate, leaps, and hiatus express the requirement that empirical change remain continuously intelligible under laws of the understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A13","CH_B337_EMPI:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D11","text":"Logical inclusion between the possible and the real does not prove that the sphere of possibility exceeds the sphere of reality.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A2","CH_B337_EMPI:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D12","text":"The modal principles are called postulates because each specifies the cognitive procedure by which a concept is presented as possible, real, or necessary.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A1","CH_B337_EMPI:A4","CH_B337_EMPI:A7","CH_B337_EMPI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D13","text":"Categories are forms for constructing cognition from given intuitions rather than independently sufficient cognitions.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A2","CH_B337_EMPI:A6","CH_B337_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:D14","text":"A purely conceptual proof that every contingent thing has a cause either fails to establish objective causality or becomes analytic by defining the contingent as an effect.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A2","CH_B337_EMPI:A6","CH_B337_EMPI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SD1","text":"The modal triad forms an ascending cognitive sequence: formal compatibility yields possibility, perceptual connection yields reality, and universal causal determination yields necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A4","CH_B337_EMPI:A7","CH_B337_EMPI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SD2","text":"The same conditions that make objective cognition possible also delimit it, so transcendental restriction is generated by the ground of objective validity itself.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A2","CH_B337_EMPI:A6","CH_B337_EMPI:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SD3","text":"Empirical realism and transcendental idealism become complementary: external objects are necessary as appearances while spatial predicates remain inapplicable to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A2","CH_B337_EMPI:A3","CH_B337_EMPI:A9","CH_B337_EMPI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SD4","text":"Empirical self-knowledge is structurally dependent on external spatial relations rather than grounded in autonomous inner self-presence.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A9","CH_B337_EMPI:A10","CH_B337_EMPI:A11","CH_B337_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SD5","text":"Necessity is displaced from beings to law-governed transitions between states, converting metaphysical necessity into conditioned natural intelligibility.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A13","CH_B337_EMPI:A14","CH_B337_EMPI:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SD6","text":"Reality is organized as a lawful perceptual network rather than as a binary distinction between immediately perceived objects and merely inferred objects.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A7","CH_B337_EMPI:A12","CH_B337_EMPI:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SD7","text":"Categories and experience form a reciprocal architecture in which categories unify experience while intuition and experiential synthesis supply categories with objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A2","CH_B337_EMPI:A6","CH_B337_EMPI:A15","CH_B337_EMPI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:DNX1","text":"The system requires objective validity independent of intuition to be impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:DNX2","text":"The system requires categorial knowledge of things as they are apart from possible experience to be unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:DNX3","text":"The system requires temporally determinate inner consciousness without external permanence to be impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:DNX4","text":"The intellectual representation of the “I” must not supply an intuition of a permanent self-substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:DNX5","text":"Possible worlds cannot be rendered objectively intelligible independently of every form of human intuition and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:DNX6","text":"Brute discontinuity, uncaused occurrence, and unconditional fate cannot enter experience without destroying its synthetic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:DNX7","text":"Principles valid as conditions of appearances must not become determinations of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:DNX8","text":"The extent of the possible cannot be inferred from logical relations among modal concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SNX1","text":"No external standpoint is available from which the complete domains of possibility, reality, and necessity can be surveyed and compared.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SNX2","text":"Pure self-presence as an internally intuited permanent subject is absent from the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SNX3","text":"Absolute possibility remains positively unavailable and is displaced from understanding to reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SNX4","text":"Things in themselves cannot receive determinate modal, spatial, temporal, or causal predicates within cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:SNX5","text":"The terminal excluded object is an alleged cognition grounded neither in intuition nor in the constitutive conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:FGS1","text":"Complete object-concept → relation to cognitive faculty → modal classification without augmentation of the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:FGS2","text":"Conceptual synthesis + formal conditions of intuition → experiential constructibility → objective possibility; noncontradiction without such constructibility → emptiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:FGS3","text":"Actual perception or law-governed connection with perception → empirical givenness → reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:FGS4","text":"Temporal self-determination → required permanence → absence of inner permanence → external spatial matter → external experience as condition of inner experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:FGS5","text":"Given cause or state + universal empirical law → determined effect or succeeding state → hypothetical material necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:FGS6","text":"Category + corresponding intuition → synthesis of possible experience → objective validity; category without intuition → mere logical form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:FGS7","text":"Transgression beyond possible experience → loss of experiential criterion → removal from understanding’s jurisdiction → reassignment to reason and dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:AF1","text":"Modal postulates → modality recoded as cognitive relation → possibility tied to formal experience → reality tied to perception → idealism reversed through external temporal conditions → necessity tied to causal states → trans-empirical modality reassigned to reason → all principles restricted to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ME1","text":"Modality is relocated from the intrinsic constitution of objects to the procedures and limits of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ME2","text":"Possibility is reclassified from logical consistency to constructibility within the formal conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ME3","text":"Reality is reclassified from immediate presence to membership in a lawfully connected perceptual field.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ME4","text":"The epistemic hierarchy of inner and outer experience is reversed: determinate inwardness depends on externality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ME5","text":"The opposition between realism and idealism is redrawn as empirical realism within transcendental idealism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ME6","text":"Necessity is transferred from substances to causal relations among phenomenal states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ME7","text":"Chance, fate, discontinuity, and void are reclassified as limits or disruptions of empirical synthesis rather than admissible explanatory principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ME8","text":"Categories are reclassified from autonomous cognitions to formal operations requiring intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ME9","text":"Absolute possibility and alternative experiential totalities are removed from the understanding’s field and assigned to reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ALT1","text":"Surface: The three postulates specify the experiential criteria for calling an object possible, real, or necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ALT2","text":"Structural: Formal intuition, perception, causal law, and synthetic unity constitute the mechanisms through which modal objectivity is produced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument converts metaphysical assertions into questions about conditions of cognition and uses those conditions to delimit conceptual speculation and reverse idealism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Phenomenal reality is lawfully constituted, temporal self-determination depends on spatial permanence, and necessity belongs to transitions among appearances rather than to substances in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the modal postulates, the experiential restriction of categories, the refutation of idealism, and the causal account of necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement from modal classification through experiential grounding to the final limitation of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:MRT3","text":"The structure is strongest in distinguishing logical consistency from objective possibility, conceptual completeness from existence, and external experience in general from particular external representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:MRT4","text":"The refutation of idealism depends on compressed claims that inner intuition contains no permanent correlate and that matter is the sole available permanent in perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:MRT5","text":"A fault line remains between describing external experience as immediate and establishing it through a transcendental argument from temporal self-determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:MRT6","text":"The reciprocal dependence of categories and possible experience functions as a transcendental validation loop but is not independently resolved against a charge of circularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0022","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:MRT7","text":"The continuity principles oscillate between rules governing empirical synthesis and claims about what nature itself can contain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:MRT8","text":"Absolute possibility, alternative forms of intuition, and the relative extension of modal domains remain intentionally underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EMPI:MRT9","text":"The system remains internally coherent only while its claims are restricted to appearances and are not converted into determinations of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EMPI:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 51","title":"Empicisim","context":"Chapter 51"}},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE","layer":"CH","title":"Experience","order":27,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"c53fdca2a446d68cc8bc2e81c69e94f7a53bc5682aeb1acf7f2db1ab68d37341","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:AS1","text":"Empirical apprehension presents perceptions contingently and successively, whereas experience claims cognition of objects in objectively determined time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:AS2","text":"The argument relocates objectivity from what perception immediately gives to the necessary rule-governed synthesis of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:AS3","text":"Because time itself cannot be perceived, phenomena must determine one another’s temporal positions through a priori rules unified in apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:AS4","text":"Permanence, succession, and coexistence generate the three analogies governing every possible temporal relation of phenomenal existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:AS5","text":"The three analogies establish substance, causality, and reciprocal community as conditions of duration, events, and simultaneity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:AS6","text":"These principles regulate relations among empirically given existences without constructing particular existences or their determinate content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:AS7","text":"The argument is transcendental-reconstructive and moves from contingent apprehension through the conditions of objective temporal determination to the unity and phenomenal restriction of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A1","text":"Experience is empirical cognition that determines objects through perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A2","text":"Apprehension presents a successive and contingent manifold without necessary objective connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A3","text":"Objective experience requires the synthetic unity of the perceptual manifold in one apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A4","text":"Time is the form of phenomenal existence but is not itself an object of perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A5","text":"Empirical determination in time requires a priori rules connecting phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A6","text":"Permanence, succession, and coexistence exhaust the temporal modes under which phenomenal existence is determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A7","text":"A priori concepts of the understanding possess empirical validity only through their temporal schemata and only for phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A8","text":"The analogies regulate relations among given existences rather than construct existence itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A9","text":"Temporal determination requires a permanent phenomenal substratum representing the abiding correlate of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A10","text":"Substance is the permanent substratum of phenomenal change, and its quantum neither increases nor diminishes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A11","text":"Change consists in the succession of determinations of an enduring substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A12","text":"An objective event requires an irreversible sequence determined by an antecedent according to a rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A13","text":"The relation of cause and effect supplies the necessary rule by which objective succession becomes possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A14","text":"Particular causes, powers, and contents of change are empirically knowable even though the causal form of change is a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A15","text":"Every change proceeds continuously through intermediate degrees because neither time nor phenomenal reality contains a smallest part.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A16","text":"Objective coexistence requires reciprocal influence through which phenomena determine one another’s positions in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A17","text":"All substances empirically representable as spatially coexistent belong, immediately or mediately, to one complete dynamical community.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0040","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:A18","text":"Synthetic a priori principles concerning existence can be justified only as conditions of possible experience, not by conceptual analysis alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0045","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX1","text":"Experience is not mere intuition, sensation, or the reception of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX2","text":"Necessary connection is not contained in perception or apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX3","text":"Temporal position is not determined by comparison with a perceptible absolute time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX4","text":"Philosophical analogy is not a mathematical proportion from which an unknown term can be constructed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX5","text":"The analogies do not anticipate which phenomena exist, what their qualities are, or how great they are.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX6","text":"Phenomena are not applied directly to unrestricted categories but to categories restricted through temporal schemata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX7","text":"The analogies do not establish synthetic knowledge of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX8","text":"The permanence of substance cannot be proved dogmatically by analysing the concept of substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX9","text":"Change is not the absolute origination or extinction of phenomenal substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX10","text":"Objective succession is not identical with the succession of representations in apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX11","text":"Causal necessity is not acquired by repeated observation of similar sequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX12","text":"Causal dependence does not require a measurable temporal interval between cause and effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX13","text":"Dynamical community is not mere spatial colocation or an aggregate lacking reciprocal influence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:NX14","text":"Empty space is not refuted, but where no perception is possible it cannot become an object of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0041","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D1","text":"Experience requires a source of necessary connection beyond perception because perception supplies only contingent succession while objective cognition requires unified determination.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A1","CH_B337_EXPE:A2","CH_B337_EXPE:A3","CH_B337_EXPE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D2","text":"Phenomena must determine their temporal positions relationally because time cannot provide a separately perceived standard.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A4","CH_B337_EXPE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D3","text":"Each fundamental temporal mode requires a corresponding a priori rule of synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A5","CH_B337_EXPE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D4","text":"Given one perception, an analogy determines how a connected perception must be sought without determining what that perception will be.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A7","CH_B337_EXPE:A8","CH_B337_EXPE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D5","text":"Duration, succession, and coexistence become empirically determinable only against a persistent phenomenal substratum.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A4","CH_B337_EXPE:A9","CH_B337_EXPE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D6","text":"Absolute phenomenal generation or annihilation would destroy the identity of the substratum through which one time is empirically represented.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A9","CH_B337_EXPE:A10","CH_B337_EXPE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D7","text":"Only the permanent can change, because change requires one enduring subject bearing successive determinations.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A9","CH_B337_EXPE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D8","text":"A perceived succession counts as an event only when its order cannot be reversed without violating a governing rule.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A12","CH_B337_EXPE:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D9","text":"The causal rule precedes experience as its condition even though explicit recognition of that rule arises through experience.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A2","CH_B337_EXPE:A13","CH_B337_EXPE:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D10","text":"Causal order can remain determinate when cause and effect are simultaneous because the order concerns asymmetric dependence rather than elapsed time.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A12","CH_B337_EXPE:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0029","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D11","text":"Action functions as an empirical criterion of substantiality because action produces change and therefore requires a permanent ultimate subject.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A9","CH_B337_EXPE:A11","CH_B337_EXPE:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D12","text":"The continuity of change can be anticipated formally, while the powers and contents producing a particular transition remain empirical.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A14","CH_B337_EXPE:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D13","text":"Reversible apprehension indicates objective simultaneity only when the reciprocal order of perception is grounded in reciprocal determination among the objects.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A2","CH_B337_EXPE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D14","text":"Wholly isolated substances could not be cognized as coexistent because no empirical synthesis could determine their positions within one time.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A4","CH_B337_EXPE:A16","CH_B337_EXPE:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0039","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:D15","text":"Nature is the totality of phenomena unified through permanence, causal succession, and reciprocal coexistence, and every empirical law presupposes this primitive unity.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A5","CH_B337_EXPE:A6","CH_B337_EXPE:A10","CH_B337_EXPE:A13","CH_B337_EXPE:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0043","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SD1","text":"Phenomenal objectivity consists in the necessary rule-governed organization of representations rather than in direct access to things as they are independently of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A2","CH_B337_EXPE:A3","CH_B337_EXPE:A5","CH_B337_EXPE:A7","CH_B337_EXPE:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SD2","text":"The phenomenal world acquires a threefold dynamical ontology: enduring subjects support determinations, causes order events, and reciprocal interaction composes contemporaneous wholes.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A6","CH_B337_EXPE:A9","CH_B337_EXPE:A11","CH_B337_EXPE:A13","CH_B337_EXPE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SD3","text":"A priori cognition legislates the formal discoverability of empirical relations without supplying the entities, forces, or determinate states to be discovered.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A7","CH_B337_EXPE:A8","CH_B337_EXPE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SD4","text":"The unity of individual consciousness expands through temporal synthesis into the necessary unity of one phenomenal nature.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A3","CH_B337_EXPE:A5","CH_B337_EXPE:A6","CH_B337_EXPE:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SD5","text":"Noumenal unknowability is compatible with empirical objectivity because objective validity is secured within the rule-bound field of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A3","CH_B337_EXPE:A7","CH_B337_EXPE:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SD6","text":"The analogies are nonconstructive regarding particular existence but constitutive of whether given perceptions can count as temporally determined objects.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A1","CH_B337_EXPE:A5","CH_B337_EXPE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SD7","text":"Phenomenal history excludes discontinuous substance replacement and unmediated state-transition while leaving the empirical causes and contents of every transition undetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A10","CH_B337_EXPE:A11","CH_B337_EXPE:A14","CH_B337_EXPE:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0035","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:DNX1","text":"Mere associative succession cannot be sufficient for objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:DNX2","text":"Objective time cannot exist for cognition as an independently given container against which phenomena are directly measured.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:DNX3","text":"Objects of experience cannot possess cognizable temporal relations independently of the rules that make their representation objective.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0026","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:DNX4","text":"Universality and necessity cannot emerge from the accumulation of contingent observations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0021","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:DNX5","text":"Change cannot remain intelligible if no numerically persistent subject spans its opposed states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:DNX6","text":"Empirically cognizable coexistence cannot obtain among entities lacking every possible route of reciprocal influence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:DNX7","text":"Multiple disconnected empirical times cannot compose one field of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:DNX8","text":"Relations of existence cannot be inferred from the analysis of concepts whose content contains no actual existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0045","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:DNX9","text":"Conditions of phenomenal objectivity cannot be converted into properties of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SNX1","text":"Direct perceptual access to objective temporal order is absent; lawful synthesis occupies the place of that unavailable access.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SNX2","text":"Radical beginning and absolute cessation of substance are excluded from the temporally unified phenomenal world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SNX3","text":"Uncaused events and wholly isolated contemporaneous existences are foreclosed because they could not enter one objective experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0039","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SNX4","text":"No noumenal standard is available to certify or overturn the temporal organization of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SNX5","text":"A complete a priori science of which things exist and how their real powers operate is structurally unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:SNX6","text":"Phenomenal plurality cannot remain ultimately unintegrated; whatever becomes an object of experience must enter one temporally and dynamically unified nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:FGS1","text":"Contingent perception combined with successive apprehension yields a subjective manifold without objective temporal determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:FGS2","text":"The demand for unified apperception converts the insufficiency of apprehension into a requirement for necessary temporal rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:FGS3","text":"The imperceptibility of time transfers temporal determination from direct comparison with time to lawful relations among phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:FGS4","text":"Temporal existence divides into permanence, succession, and coexistence, producing three distinct rule requirements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:FGS5","text":"Permanence requirement → enduring substratum → substance → duration and change become determinable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:FGS6","text":"Succession requirement → irreversible antecedent-consequent rule → causality → events become objectively determinable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:FGS7","text":"Coexistence requirement → reciprocal temporal determination → dynamical community → simultaneity becomes objectively determinable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:FGS8","text":"Substance, causality, and community converge into the unified lawful totality designated as nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0044","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:FGS9","text":"Removing any corresponding rule removes the objectivity of its temporal mode and thereby removes the possibility of experience in that mode.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0043","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:FGS10","text":"The resulting principles pass through a scope filter that restricts them to schematized categories, relations of phenomenal existence, and empirical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:AF1","text":"Contingent apprehension → absence of objective temporal necessity → transcendental question concerning possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:AF2","text":"Unity of apperception + imperceptibility of time → relational determination of phenomena → division into permanence, succession, and coexistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:AF3","text":"Permanence → substance; succession → causality; coexistence → reciprocity → one nature → restriction to regulative empirical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0037","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:AF4","text":"Failure of dogmatic and inductive proof → deduction from conditions of experience → universal validity for phenomena without noumenal extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ME1","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from perceptual givenness to necessary rule-governed temporal synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ME2","text":"The subjective-objective distinction is relocated from inner representation versus external thing to reversible apprehension versus necessary synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ME3","text":"Time is reclassified from a potentially observable medium to an unperceived form represented empirically through relations among phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0043","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ME4","text":"Substance is reclassified from an independently cognized metaphysical thing to the permanent phenomenal function required for temporal determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ME5","text":"Causality is reclassified from observed regularity to the condition under which a succession can count as an event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ME6","text":"Simultaneity is reclassified from mere same-time placement to reciprocal dynamical determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0038","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ME7","text":"Philosophical analogy is separated from mathematical construction and becomes a rule for finding relations among empirical terms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ME8","text":"Nature is reclassified from an aggregate of appearances to one lawfully connected temporal whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0044","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ME9","text":"The a priori-empirical boundary is redrawn so that temporal form and relational necessity are a priori while particular existences, forces, and states remain empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ALT1","text":"Surface: The argument establishes permanence of substance, causal succession, and reciprocal coexistence as the three analogies of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0037","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ALT2","text":"Structural: The understanding converts successive perceptions into objects by schematizing categories as necessary rules of temporal synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Dogmatic deduction from concepts and empirical induction from repetition are replaced by a transcendental proof from the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0045","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Phenomenal reality consists of enduring substrates, continuous causal transitions, and dynamically connected coexistences within one time, without licensing claims about things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0040","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:MRT1","text":"The extracted premise set is sufficient to reconstruct the transition from contingent perception to the three analogies and their unification as nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:MRT2","text":"The exhaustiveness of the three temporal modes and their correspondence with the categories remain dependent on architectonic material established outside this chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:MRT3","text":"The most determinate inference is the distinction between arbitrary subjective apprehension and objectively necessary temporal order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:MRT4","text":"The inference from a permanent substratum to an invariant quantum of substance compresses the problem of how phenomenal substance is quantitatively identified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:MRT5","text":"The admission of simultaneous cause and effect preserves causal asymmetry through dependence rather than temporal interval, but leaves the independent criterion of that asymmetry partially underformalized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:MRT6","text":"The third analogy compresses the transition from reciprocal interaction as a condition for cognizing coexistence to complete reciprocal interaction among all coexistent phenomenal substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0038","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0040","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:MRT7","text":"The designation of the analogies as merely regulative remains in tension with their role as necessary conditions for perceptions to constitute objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:MRT8","text":"The continuity theorem depends on treating generated phenomenal reality as quantitatively traversing every intermediate degree, an extension beyond the minimal proof of causal order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0034","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:MRT9","text":"The system remains internally coherent only while substance, causality, and community are restricted to phenomena and possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_EXPE:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction from this architecture preserves the real movement from perceptual insufficiency through transcendental deduction to dynamical unity and critical limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0044","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_EXPE:P0045","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 50","title":"Experience","context":"Chapter 50"}},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL","layer":"CH","title":"First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1","order":28,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"cfcd5ca0d7517631db9764269e69d35c1f9e32280ebce8626596a79418a52002","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:AS1","text":"The problem is to determine the elements and complete extent of pure understanding independently of empirical aggregation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:AS2","text":"Transcendental analytic reframes this problem as a systematic dissection of the whole of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:AS3","text":"The ground of the inquiry is the distinction between pure understanding and both empirical cognition and sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:AS4","text":"Completeness cannot be established by repeated observations but requires an idea of the totality that determines the division of elementary concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:AS5","text":"The resulting system supplies an internal test for the correctness and genuineness of its constituent cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:AS6","text":"The argument moves from conditions of conceptual purity to systematic completeness and then to the division of transcendental logic into concepts and principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:A1","text":"Transcendental analytic dissects a priori knowledge into the elements of pure cognition of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:A2","text":"The concepts belonging to transcendental analytic must be pure rather than empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:A3","text":"These concepts must belong to thought and understanding rather than to intuition and sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:A4","text":"The relevant concepts must be elementary rather than deduced or compound.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:A5","text":"The table of elementary concepts must be complete and encompass the whole sphere of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:A6","text":"Mere aggregation through repeated experiments and attempts cannot establish the completeness of a science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:A7","text":"Completeness requires an idea of the totality of a priori cognition and a division of concepts determined through their connection in a system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:A8","text":"Pure understanding is a self-subsistent and self-sufficient unity that is not enlarged by additions from outside.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:A9","text":"The completeness and articulation of the system can test the correctness and genuineness of the cognitions belonging to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:A10","text":"Transcendental logic divides into a book of the conceptions and a book of the principles of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:NX1","text":"Transcendental analytic is not an analysis of empirical concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:NX2","text":"Its objects are not derived from intuition or sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:NX3","text":"Its elementary concepts are not deduced or compound concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:NX4","text":"The completeness of pure understanding is not established by repeated experiments or attempts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:NX5","text":"Pure understanding is not dependent on additions from outside itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:NX6","text":"The system of pure understanding is not an unstructured aggregate of cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:D1","text":"The criteria of purity, non-sensibility, and elementarity jointly restrict transcendental analysis to a determinate class of concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A1","CH_B337_FDTL:A2","CH_B337_FDTL:A3","CH_B337_FDTL:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:D2","text":"A complete table of pure concepts cannot be obtained merely by collecting concepts that have appeared in experience.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A5","CH_B337_FDTL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:D3","text":"The idea of the totality of pure cognition must precede the determination of the complete division of its concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A5","CH_B337_FDTL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:D4","text":"The concepts of pure understanding acquire their scientific status through their place within a connected system rather than through isolated identification.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A7","CH_B337_FDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:D5","text":"Systematic completeness functions both as a criterion of inclusion and as a criterion of correctness.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A5","CH_B337_FDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:D6","text":"The distinction between concepts and principles is a structural division internal to transcendental logic rather than a division between empirical and pure cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A1","CH_B337_FDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:SD1","text":"The system of pure understanding is generated deductively from an anticipated totality rather than inductively from an accumulated inventory.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A6","CH_B337_FDTL:A7","CH_B337_FDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:SD2","text":"The system’s internal organization becomes the epistemic mechanism by which individual cognitions are authenticated.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A7","CH_B337_FDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:SD3","text":"The demand for completeness converts classification from a descriptive task into a constitutive operation that determines what counts as a genuine element of pure understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A4","CH_B337_FDTL:A5","CH_B337_FDTL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:DNX1","text":"The system cannot be grounded in an open-ended empirical search whose results remain externally accumulated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:DNX2","text":"The boundaries of pure understanding cannot be established by importing material from sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:DNX3","text":"Completeness cannot mean the indefinite addition of further concepts to an initially incomplete inventory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:DNX4","text":"The classification of pure concepts cannot be arbitrary, provisional, or disconnected from an idea of totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:DNX5","text":"The validity of a pure concept cannot be assessed independently of its systematic position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:SNX1","text":"The argument excludes the possibility that pure cognition is fundamentally an empirical residue assembled from repeated observations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:SNX2","text":"It excludes an understanding whose conceptual contents remain externally expandable without determinate systematic limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:SNX3","text":"It excludes the possibility that completeness can be recognized without a prior governing conception of the whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:SNX4","text":"It leaves no legitimate standpoint outside the system from which the pure understanding could be supplemented or completed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:FGS1","text":"Purity conditions → exclusion of empirical, sensible, and compound concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:FGS2","text":"Presupposed totality → determinate division of elementary concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:FGS3","text":"Systematic connection → completeness of the table of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:FGS4","text":"Completeness and articulation → internal test of correctness and genuineness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:FGS5","text":"Internal systematization → division of transcendental logic into conceptions and principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:AF1","text":"Indeterminate inventory of a priori cognition → criteria of purity and elementarity → rejection of empirical aggregation → idea of totality → systematic division → completeness → internal validation → division into conceptions and principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:ME1","text":"A priori cognition is reclassified from an aggregate of contents into a system governed by the idea of a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:ME2","text":"Completeness becomes a structural property of organization rather than a quantitative accumulation of instances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:ME3","text":"The boundary between pure understanding and sensibility becomes a constitutive boundary of the science itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:ME4","text":"The system’s architecture becomes a test of the legitimacy of its individual elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:ME5","text":"Transcendental logic is mapped as an internally articulated domain containing both concepts and principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text specifies the objects, criteria, and divisions of transcendental analytic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:ALT2","text":"Structural — Pure cognition is organized as a self-contained system whose totality determines the classification of its elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument replaces empirical enumeration with systematic reconstruction as the method for establishing completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Pure understanding is treated as a self-sufficient unity, while completeness is governed by a prior idea of totality rather than by temporal accumulation through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the definition of transcendental analytic to its systematic division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between pure understanding, empirical cognition, and sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:MRT3","text":"The central dependency is the claim that completeness requires a prior idea of totality and cannot arise from aggregation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent, but its validation mechanism creates a structural tension because the totality that organizes the system also tests the correctness of its parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:MRT5","text":"The argument remains underdetermined concerning how the idea of totality itself is established and how the complete table is concretely derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_FDTL:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement of the text, though the transition from the requirement of systematic completeness to the actual content of the concepts remains compressed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_FDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 22","title":"First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1","context":"Chapter 22"}},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA","layer":"CH","title":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.","order":29,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"ab770241ea0f5d577f7ec142c3ba600d59718015c92800c9c6f3d556477a2c9c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:AS1","text":"The text seeks to distinguish two fundamentally different ways in which a predicate can relate to a subject concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:AS2","text":"It reframes the distinction as identity-based containment versus nonidentity-based connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:AS3","text":"It establishes analytical judgments as conceptually extractive and a priori, while locating empirical judgments within synthetic augmentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:AS4","text":"It isolates synthetic a priori judgment as the unresolved case in which a noncontained predicate is connected universally and necessarily without appeal to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:AS5","text":"It concludes that analysis prepares conceptual clarity, whereas synthesis constitutes the real acquisition on which speculative a priori knowledge depends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:AS6","text":"The argument is classificatory and epistemological, moving from taxonomy through exclusion to the problem of grounding synthetic a priori connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A1","text":"A judgment cogitates a relation between a subject and a predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A2","text":"A predicate either belongs to the conception of its subject or lies outside that conception while remaining connected with it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A3","text":"An analytical judgment connects predicate and subject through identity or conceptual containment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A4","text":"An analytical judgment explicates what is already contained in the subject concept without augmenting it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A5","text":"A synthetical judgment connects a predicate with its subject without conceptual identity or containment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A6","text":"A synthetical judgment augments the subject concept with a predicate that analysis could not discover within it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A7","text":"Analytical judgments can be established a priori through conceptual analysis according to the principle of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A8","text":"Judgments of experience, as such, are synthetical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A9","text":"Experience can connect noncontained concepts as parts of one experiential whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A10","text":"Empirical connections between concepts are contingent rather than necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A11","text":"Synthetic a priori judgments connect noncontained predicates with their subjects universally and necessarily.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A12","text":"Experience cannot ground a connection that possesses both universality and necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A13","text":"The predicate of causality is not contained in the conception of something that happens.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A14","text":"Analytical judgments provide the conceptual clarity required for sure and extended synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:A15","text":"Synthesis, rather than analysis alone, constitutes the real acquisition in speculative a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:NX1","text":"An analytical judgment is not an augmentative judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:NX2","text":"An analytical predicate is not discovered by adding something external to the subject concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:NX3","text":"An analytical judgment is not grounded on testimony from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:NX4","text":"A synthetical judgment is not reducible to the analysis of a confused concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:NX5","text":"An empirical judgment is not secured by conceptual identity alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:NX6","text":"Experience is not sufficient to ground universality and necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:NX7","text":"The predicate of cause is not contained in the concept of an occurrence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:NX8","text":"Analysis is not the final acquisition of speculative a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:D1","text":"The analytical–synthetical distinction concerns the mode of connection between concepts rather than the subject matter of the judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A2","CH_B337_IDBA:A3","CH_B337_IDBA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:D2","text":"The necessity of an analytical judgment can be recognized without experience because the predicate is extracted from the subject concept itself.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A3","CH_B337_IDBA:A4","CH_B337_IDBA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:D3","text":"Empirical cognition extends knowledge when experience supplies a predicate not contained in the original concept.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A8","CH_B337_IDBA:A9","CH_B337_IDBA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:D4","text":"A concept may originate through experience while a judgment involving that concept remains analytically a priori.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A7","CH_B337_IDBA:A8","CH_B337_IDBA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:D5","text":"Synthetic a priori judgment requires an unknown nonempirical ground for connecting a noncontained predicate with its subject.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A5","CH_B337_IDBA:A11","CH_B337_IDBA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:D6","text":"The proposition that everything which happens has a cause exemplifies synthetic a priori judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A11","CH_B337_IDBA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:D7","text":"Empirical observation can establish that concepts are connected but cannot establish that their connection is necessary and universal.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A9","CH_B337_IDBA:A10","CH_B337_IDBA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:D8","text":"Conceptual analysis functions as a preliminary instrument for synthesis rather than as an independent source of augmented knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A14","CH_B337_IDBA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:SD1","text":"The system produces three epistemic regimes: analytical a priori judgment, empirical synthetical judgment, and synthetic a priori judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A7","CH_B337_IDBA:A8","CH_B337_IDBA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:SD2","text":"The origin of a concept and the mode by which a judgment concerning it is justified are distinct questions.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A7","CH_B337_IDBA:A8","CH_B337_IDBA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:SD3","text":"Experience can explain contingent belonging between concepts without explaining necessary belonging between them.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A9","CH_B337_IDBA:A10","CH_B337_IDBA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:SD4","text":"The central epistemological problem shifts from discovering predicates within concepts to explaining the ground of necessary connections between nonidentical concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A5","CH_B337_IDBA:A11","CH_B337_IDBA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:SD5","text":"The text establishes the category of synthetic a priori judgment more fully than it establishes the ground that makes such judgment possible.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A11","CH_B337_IDBA:A12","CH_B337_IDBA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:SD6","text":"The epistemological architecture is asymmetrical: analytical judgments receive an internal criterion of validity, while synthetic a priori judgments require a ground not supplied by either analysis or experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A7","CH_B337_IDBA:A11","CH_B337_IDBA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:DNX1","text":"Pure conceptual analysis cannot generate a genuinely new predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:DNX2","text":"Empirical regularity cannot by itself generate necessity or universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:DNX3","text":"The relation between subject and predicate is not exhausted by either conceptual identity or observed association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:DNX4","text":"The empirical origin of a concept does not explain the a priori status of every judgment involving that concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:DNX5","text":"Conceptual clarification is not equivalent to knowledge extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:DNX6","text":"Classifying synthetic a priori judgment does not by itself reveal the ground of its possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:SNX1","text":"No empirical source can serve as the ultimate ground of synthetic a priori necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:SNX2","text":"No purely analytical extraction can serve as the ground of a predicate explicitly defined as noncontained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:SNX3","text":"The necessary transition from contingent experience to universal connection remains without a fully specified mediator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:SNX4","text":"The unknown ground required by synthetic a priori judgment remains formally demanded but substantively unprovided in the chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:SNX5","text":"The possibility of speculative a priori knowledge cannot be secured by analysis alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:FGS1","text":"Subject–predicate relation → conceptual containment or noncontainment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:FGS2","text":"Conceptual containment → identity → analytical judgment → explication → a priori necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:FGS3","text":"Noncontainment plus experiential connection → synthetical judgment → contingent augmentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:FGS4","text":"Noncontainment plus universality and necessity → synthetic a priori judgment → exclusion of experience as ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:FGS5","text":"Exclusion of both analysis and experience as sufficient grounds → unknown X as required condition of synthetic a priori synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:FGS6","text":"Analytical clarification → preparation for synthesis → augmented knowledge as the decisive acquisition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:AF1","text":"The argument moves from the general problem of subject–predicate connection to a distinction between containment and noncontainment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:AF2","text":"It moves from containment to analytical explication and from noncontainment to synthetical augmentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:AF3","text":"It separates analytical a priori judgment from empirical synthetical judgment by contrasting conceptual necessity with experiential contingency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:AF4","text":"It introduces synthetic a priori judgment as the noncontained connection that is nevertheless universal and necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:AF5","text":"It ends by displacing the problem toward the unknown ground of synthesis and assigning analysis a preparatory role.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:ME1","text":"Predication is reclassified according to the structure of its connection rather than treated as a single homogeneous operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:ME2","text":"The distinction between concept formation and judgment justification becomes visible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:ME3","text":"The opposition between a priori and empirical knowledge is transformed by the introduction of synthetic a priori judgment as a third regime.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:ME4","text":"Experience is restricted to contingent synthesis and prevented from functioning as the ground of necessary universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:ME5","text":"Analysis is repositioned from final knowledge-producing activity to preparatory clarification for synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:ME6","text":"The central epistemological terrain shifts from predicate discovery to the grounding of necessary nonidentity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, the text classifies judgments according to whether the predicate is contained in the subject concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, it distinguishes conceptual identity, experiential association, and necessary noncontained connection as different mechanisms of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, it redirects inquiry from analyzing concepts to determining what makes synthesis possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:ALT4","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, an occurrence includes existence preceded by time, while causal necessity is represented as a relation not contained in the occurrence concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the analytical–synthetical taxonomy and its containment criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:MRT2","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from conceptual analysis through empirical synthesis to the problem of synthetic a priori judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:MRT3","text":"The extracted system does not determine the content of the unknown ground that makes synthetic a priori synthesis possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent as a classification, while its central grounding problem remains deliberately unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:MRT5","text":"Its strongest structure is the separation of conceptual identity, contingent experience, and necessary nonidentity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:MRT6","text":"Its principal fault line lies between the demonstrated insufficiency of analysis and experience and the unprovided ground required for synthetic a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBA:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement provided that the examples remain subordinate illustrations rather than independent premises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 04","title":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements.","context":"Chapter 04"}},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP","layer":"CH","title":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge","order":30,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"a4e41e5ebff36c005c382f4561c6abcd601f3f3ee370510f58306570251ec93e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AS1","text":"Human knowledge begins with experience because sensory objects awaken cognition and supply the occasion for representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AS2","text":"The temporal beginning of knowledge in experience does not establish that all knowledge originates from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AS3","text":"Experience consists of sensory impressions combined with contributions supplied by the faculty of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AS4","text":"The question of knowledge independent of all experience requires investigation because cognitive contributions are initially indistinguishable from sensory material.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AS5","text":"The distinction between empirical and a priori knowledge depends on the source-dependence of cognition, not merely on whether a proposition is inferred without immediate observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AS6","text":"A priori knowledge is absolutely independent of experience, while empirical knowledge is possible only through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AS7","text":"A priori knowledge divides into pure and impure according to whether empirical conceptual content is mixed into it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:A1","text":"Knowledge begins in time with experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:A2","text":"Sensory affection initiates the exercise of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:A3","text":"Cognition actively compares, connects, and separates sensory representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:A4","text":"Experience is a compound of received impressions and contributions supplied by cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:A5","text":"The temporal occasion of knowledge is distinct from the ultimate source of every element within knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:A6","text":"A priori knowledge is independent of all experience, not merely independent of a particular experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:A7","text":"Empirical knowledge is possible only through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:A8","text":"A proposition may be a priori in use while resting on a general rule previously acquired through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:A9","text":"Pure a priori knowledge contains no empirical element.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:A10","text":"Impure a priori knowledge contains an a priori form together with an empirical concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:NX1","text":"A temporal beginning in experience is not equivalent to an origin of every cognitive element in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:NX2","text":"A priori knowledge is not knowledge obtained merely without waiting for a particular observed event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:NX3","text":"A general rule borrowed from experience does not become absolutely a priori through indirect application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:NX4","text":"Empirical knowledge is not reducible to passive reception of sensory impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:NX5","text":"A priori knowledge is not defined by independence from one kind of experience while remaining dependent on another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:NX6","text":"Pure a priori knowledge is not identical with all a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:NX7","text":"The concept of change does not count as an empirically unmixed element merely because the proposition containing it is a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:D1","text":"Sensory impressions are occasions for cognition rather than the complete source of the resulting knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A2","CH_B337_IDBP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:D2","text":"Experience is constituted through an operation that transforms sensory material into knowledge of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A3","CH_B337_IDBP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:D3","text":"The contents of experience cannot initially be divided into received and supplied elements by immediate inspection.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A4","CH_B337_IDBP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:D4","text":"Determining whether a priori knowledge exists requires separating cognitive contribution from sensory reception.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A4","CH_B337_IDBP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:D5","text":"The statement that unsupported bodies fall is not absolutely a priori when its operative concepts and rule depend on prior experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A6","CH_B337_IDBP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:D6","text":"The distinction between pure and impure a priori knowledge is determined by the empirical status of the concepts involved, not solely by the logical status of the proposition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A9","CH_B337_IDBP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:D7","text":"“Every change has a cause” is a priori in form but impure because the concept of change is empirically derived.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A6","CH_B337_IDBP:A9","CH_B337_IDBP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:SD1","text":"The source of knowledge must be analyzed compositionally rather than assigned wholesale to either sensation or cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A3","CH_B337_IDBP:A4","CH_B337_IDBP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:SD2","text":"The possibility of a priori knowledge is concealed by the fact that cognitive forms are fused with sensory material in ordinary experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A4","CH_B337_IDBP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:SD3","text":"The criterion of apriority shifts from temporal priority or inferential immediacy to absolute independence from experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A6","CH_B337_IDBP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:SD4","text":"Knowledge can possess a non-empirical mode of validity while still containing empirically sourced conceptual content.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A9","CH_B337_IDBP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:SD5","text":"A complete theory of knowledge must distinguish the genesis of cognition, the source of its elements, and the purity of its composition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A1","CH_B337_IDBP:A5","CH_B337_IDBP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:DNX1","text":"The origin of the occasion for knowledge cannot determine the origin of every condition of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:DNX2","text":"Cognition cannot be treated as an unstructured receptacle for impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:DNX3","text":"The absence of direct observation cannot serve as the sufficient criterion of a priori status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:DNX4","text":"Generality, necessity, or inferential economy cannot by themselves establish absolute independence from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:DNX5","text":"The empirical and a priori domains cannot be separated solely by examining complete propositions without analyzing their concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:DNX6","text":"The system excludes an undifferentiated account in which all knowledge has a single homogeneous source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:SNX1","text":"The possibility that experience alone exhausts the conditions of knowledge is structurally foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:SNX2","text":"The possibility that cognition contributes nothing constitutive to experience is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:SNX3","text":"The possibility that “a priori” names merely what precedes an individual observation is rejected at the deepest definitional level.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:SNX4","text":"The possibility of treating empirical dependence and a priori validity as mutually exclusive is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:SNX5","text":"The text leaves no admissible account of knowledge that bypasses both sensory affection and cognitive synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:FGS1","text":"Sensory affection initiates cognition, but initiation does not exhaust constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:FGS2","text":"Cognitive comparison, connection, and separation transform impressions into representations of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:FGS3","text":"The resulting experience is analyzed into received material and supplied contribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:FGS4","text":"The separation of these elements generates the question of knowledge independent of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:FGS5","text":"The meaning of a priori is narrowed from non-immediacy to absolute independence from all experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:FGS6","text":"Absolute independence yields the division between a priori and empirical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:FGS7","text":"Inspection of the elements within a priori knowledge yields the further division between pure and impure a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:FGS8","text":"An a priori proposition containing an empirically derived concept is classified as impure rather than purely a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AF1","text":"Sensory affection → Cognitive activation → Synthesis of impressions → Experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AF2","text":"Experience as temporal beginning → Distinction between occasion and source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AF3","text":"Mixed constitution of experience → Problem of separating sensory reception from cognitive contribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AF4","text":"Ambiguous meaning of “a priori” → Restriction to absolute independence from all experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AF5","text":"Absolute independence → Empirical/a priori distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AF6","text":"Analysis of a priori content → Pure/impure distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:AF7","text":"Empirical concept within an a priori proposition → Classification as impure a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:ME1","text":"The map of knowledge is reorganized from a binary opposition between experience and non-experience into a layered relation between occasion, source, form, and content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:ME2","text":"Experience is reclassified from a passive datum into a composite product of receptivity and cognitive activity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:ME3","text":"“A priori” is reclassified from temporal or inferential priority to absolute experiential independence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:ME4","text":"The opposition between empirical and a priori knowledge is supplemented by an internal distinction between pure and impure a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:ME5","text":"Propositions are no longer classified solely as wholes; their constituent concepts become relevant to determining epistemic purity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:ALT1","text":"Surface — Knowledge begins with sensory experience, while a priori knowledge is independent of all experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Experience is generated by the interaction of sensory reception and cognitive operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text replaces immediate classification with an investigative procedure that decomposes knowledge into sources and components.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Temporal priority belongs to experience as the beginning of cognition, but epistemic independence can belong to elements not derived from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument from the dependence of cognition on experience to the distinction between empirical, a priori, pure, and impure knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the decisive distinction between the temporal beginning of knowledge and the experiential origin of its elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent because sensory affection and non-empirical cognitive contribution occupy different explanatory functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:MRT4","text":"The strongest point is the redefinition of a priori knowledge as absolute independence from all experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:MRT5","text":"The system remains underdetermined regarding the specific nature and mechanism of the cognitive contributions supplied independently of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:MRT6","text":"The distinction between pure and impure a priori knowledge depends on a prior account of which concepts are empirically derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDBP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement, but the proof of actual a priori knowledge is deferred rather than established within this chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDBP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 01","title":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge","context":"Chapter 01"}},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL","layer":"CH","title":"III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.","order":31,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"b5fde4bae5d680930d8f642bd57effd6228f11c09625d89fae5ae2f0da667865","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AS1","text":"The demand for a universal and sufficient criterion of truth conflicts with truth’s dependence on the particular object of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AS2","text":"General logic can supply only universal formal criteria because it abstracts from every object and every material content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AS3","text":"Formal conformity with the laws of thought is necessary for truth because self-contradiction is falsity, but it cannot establish objective truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AS4","text":"General logic is analytic when it decomposes the formal operations of understanding into principles for judging cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AS5","text":"Logic becomes dialectical illusion when its negative formal canon is used as an organon for producing objective knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AS6","text":"The argument moves from an impossible demand for universal material truth, through the delimitation of formal validity, to a critique of logic’s illegitimate cognitive expansion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A1","text":"Truth consists in the accordance of cognition with its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A2","text":"The truth or falsity of cognition depends on the object to which that cognition relates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A3","text":"A universal criterion of truth must abstract from the distinct objects and material contents of cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A4","text":"A criterion that abstracts from material content cannot determine the truth of cognition in relation to its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A5","text":"General logic exhibits universal and necessary laws of understanding and reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A6","text":"Cognition that contradicts the universal laws of thought is formally false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A7","text":"Formal logical correctness does not entail accordance between cognition and its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A8","text":"General logic analytically resolves formal cognition into elements and principles of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A9","text":"Objective cognition requires well-grounded information obtained independently of general logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A10","text":"General logic is a canon for judging cognition rather than an organon for extending knowledge of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A11","text":"General logic employed as an organon produces dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:A12","text":"Dialectic is the critique of the illusion produced by the organonic misuse of general logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:NX1","text":"No universal and sufficient criterion can determine the material truth of every cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:NX2","text":"Logical form is not identical with objective truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:NX3","text":"Non-contradiction is not sufficient evidence of accordance with an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:NX4","text":"General logic is not a source of information about objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:NX5","text":"Analytic logic is not a positive producer of material truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:NX6","text":"General logic is not a legitimate organon of objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:NX7","text":"Dialectic is not a science of objects but a diagnosis of formally persuasive illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:NX8","text":"Not every grammatically formulable question is rationally answerable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:D1","text":"Formal logic can exclude self-contradictory cognition without certifying objectively true cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A5","CH_B337_IDGL:A6","CH_B337_IDGL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:D2","text":"Logical examination must precede material investigation only as a negative screening of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A7","CH_B337_IDGL:A8","CH_B337_IDGL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:D3","text":"Material truth can be investigated only through object-related information not supplied by logic itself.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A4","CH_B337_IDGL:A7","CH_B337_IDGL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:D4","text":"The scope of logical authority ends where judgment passes from formal coherence to claims about objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A7","CH_B337_IDGL:A9","CH_B337_IDGL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:D5","text":"A formally disciplined procedure can simulate knowledge when it supplies the appearance of cognitive production without material warrant.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A8","CH_B337_IDGL:A10","CH_B337_IDGL:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:D6","text":"Dialectical critique identifies the conversion of a necessary condition of truth into a pretended sufficient condition of truth.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A6","CH_B337_IDGL:A7","CH_B337_IDGL:A11","CH_B337_IDGL:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:SD1","text":"Truth is structurally divided between a universal formal condition that can be tested and an object-dependent material relation that cannot be universally certified.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A1","CH_B337_IDGL:A3","CH_B337_IDGL:A4","CH_B337_IDGL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:SD2","text":"Error divides into formal contradiction, which logic can detect, and material non-correspondence, which logic alone cannot detect.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A4","CH_B337_IDGL:A6","CH_B337_IDGL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:SD3","text":"Logic acquires legitimate epistemic force only as a regulative constraint upon independently acquired cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A8","CH_B337_IDGL:A9","CH_B337_IDGL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:SD4","text":"Dialectical illusion is generated not by the absence of logical form but by logical form’s detachment from object-related content.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A7","CH_B337_IDGL:A9","CH_B337_IDGL:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:SD5","text":"The more universally formal a procedure becomes, the less it can by itself determine any particular object.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A2","CH_B337_IDGL:A3","CH_B337_IDGL:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:DNX1","text":"There is no object-independent route from universal laws of thought to determinate knowledge of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:DNX2","text":"There is no purely formal completion of the problem of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:DNX3","text":"There is no inference from coherence within cognition to correspondence outside cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:DNX4","text":"There is no neutral expansion of logic beyond its canonical function, because such expansion reclassifies formal constraint as cognitive production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:DNX5","text":"There is no universally valid material test because material truth is irreducibly indexed to differentiated objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses the fantasy of a standpoint from which one formal rule could certify every possible object-directed cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses knowledge generated from form alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a final universal criterion that would abolish the distinction between the conditions of thinking and the truth of what is thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:SNX4","text":"The system treats the promise that method can replace material cognition as the constitutive absence around which dialectical illusion forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:FGS1","text":"Correspondence truth → truth depends on the relation between cognition and a determinate object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:FGS2","text":"Universal criterion → abstraction from objects and contents → incapacity to judge material correspondence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:FGS3","text":"Formal laws of thought → exclusion of self-contradiction → necessary but insufficient condition of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:FGS4","text":"Formal decomposition of cognition → analytic canon for testing judgments → dependence on independently obtained material information.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:FGS5","text":"Canon refunctioned as organon → semblance of objective production → dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:FGS6","text":"Dialectical illusion → critical exposure of the illegitimate passage from formal validity to objective assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AF1","text":"The question of a universal criterion of truth → the distinction between material correspondence and formal validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AF2","text":"Material correspondence → object-dependence → impossibility of a universal material criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AF3","text":"Formal validity → universal logical laws → negative exclusion of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AF4","text":"Negative logical testing → analytic canon → prohibition on object-knowledge derived from logic alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AF5","text":"Canon treated as organon → formal semblance of knowledge → dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:AF6","text":"Dialectical illusion → critique of logical overreach → division of general logic into analytic and dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:ME1","text":"Truth is redistributed from a single universal property into formal validity and material correspondence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:ME2","text":"Logic is reclassified from a possible producer of knowledge into a negative condition governing the admissibility of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:ME3","text":"Dialectic is reclassified from an art of argument into the critical name for the misuse of formal logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:ME4","text":"The opposition between valid reasoning and true cognition becomes visible as a non-identity rather than a difference of degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:ME5","text":"The demand for a universal truth-test is reclassified from a difficult problem into a self-contradictory demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines whether logic can provide a universal criterion of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text separates formal laws of cognition from the material relation of cognition to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text restricts legitimate logical practice to critical testing and identifies the expansion of logic into an organon as illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text presupposes that objects are irreducibly differentiated and that cognition reaches them only through content not generated by universal form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central division between analytic logic and dialectical misuse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:MRT2","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the source, validation, and possible forms of the independently acquired material information required for objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent if formal necessity and material sufficiency remain strictly non-equivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:MRT4","text":"The system’s decisive leverage lies in converting the demand for universal material truth into a contradiction generated by abstraction from objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:MRT5","text":"The argument compresses the transition from formal insufficiency to dialectical illusion, leaving the mechanisms by which agents mistake a canon for an organon only minimally specified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:MRT6","text":"The designation of analytic logic as a negative test of truth risks ambiguity because the test excludes formal falsity without positively determining truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDGL:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the impossibility of universal material certification precedes the restricted legitimacy of formal logical testing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IDGL:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 20","title":"III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic.","context":"Chapter 20"}},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL","layer":"CH","title":"IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.","order":32,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"5291bed8a50ddb653b2c7b51770d45d34ed98fb4601e018f6d14bc2f874d74c2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:AS1","text":"Transcendental logic isolates the understanding and examines cognition originating from it alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:AS2","text":"Pure understanding requires intuition to supply objects, because cognition without an object is void.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:AS3","text":"Transcendental analytic identifies the elements and principles required for any object to be thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:AS4","text":"These principles constitute a logic of truth because their contradiction removes all objective content from cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:AS5","text":"The same principles generate dialectical illusion when employed beyond the boundaries of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:AS6","text":"Transcendental dialectic critiques this hyperphysical use and limits understanding and reason to testing judgements and preventing sophistical delusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A1","text":"Transcendental logic isolates the understanding from sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A2","text":"Pure cognition of the understanding originates in the understanding alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A3","text":"The application of pure understanding requires objects given in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A4","text":"Cognition without an object has no content and no truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A5","text":"Transcendental analytic contains the elements of pure understanding and the principles necessary for thinking any object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A6","text":"The principles of transcendental analytic are conditions of objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A7","text":"Experience is the only source from which the matter of cognition can be obtained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A8","text":"Pure principles of understanding are formally valid but cannot by themselves supply objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A9","text":"Employing pure understanding beyond possible experience converts its formal principles into groundless material claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A10","text":"Transcendental logic is properly a canon for judging the empirical use of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A11","text":"Treating transcendental logic as an unlimited organon produces dialectical exercise of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A12","text":"Transcendental dialectic critiques the hyperphysical use of understanding and reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:A13","text":"The proper employment of understanding and reason is to test judgements and guard against sophistical delusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:NX1","text":"Pure understanding is not an autonomous source of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:NX2","text":"Cognition without intuition is not objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:NX3","text":"Formal principles of understanding are not sufficient for determining objects in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:NX4","text":"Transcendental analytic is not an unlimited organon for synthetic judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:NX5","text":"Transcendental dialectic is not an art of producing metaphysical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:NX6","text":"Pure principles cannot legitimately enlarge cognition beyond the conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:NX7","text":"Claims concerning objects not given, or possibly not giveable, cannot acquire objective validity merely from pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:D1","text":"Because pure understanding requires intuition and cognition without objects is void, the validity of pure concepts is inseparable from their possible application to objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A3","CH_B337_IDTL:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:D2","text":"Because transcendental analytic states the conditions for thinking objects, contradiction of its principles destroys the object-directed content of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A5","CH_B337_IDTL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:D3","text":"Because experience supplies the matter of cognition while pure understanding supplies formal principles, objective cognition requires the conjunction of form and intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A3","CH_B337_IDTL:A7","CH_B337_IDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:D4","text":"Because pure principles lack the power to supply objects, their extension beyond experience produces the appearance of knowledge without objective content.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A7","CH_B337_IDTL:A8","CH_B337_IDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:D5","text":"Because transcendental logic is a canon rather than an unlimited organon, its legitimate function is regulative adjudication of understanding’s empirical use.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A10","CH_B337_IDTL:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:D6","text":"Because dialectical exercise results from applying pure understanding without supplied objects, critique must expose the groundlessness of the resulting claims.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A3","CH_B337_IDTL:A9","CH_B337_IDTL:A11","CH_B337_IDTL:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:D7","text":"Because reason and understanding can generate sophistical delusion through hyperphysical use, their proper role includes self-limitation and the testing of judgements.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A9","CH_B337_IDTL:A12","CH_B337_IDTL:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:SD1","text":"Objective cognition is produced by the coordination of pure conceptual form with intuitively given matter, not by either faculty in isolation.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A3","CH_B337_IDTL:A7","CH_B337_IDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:SD2","text":"The boundary between analytic truth and dialectical illusion is determined by whether pure principles remain tied to possible object-givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A4","CH_B337_IDTL:A6","CH_B337_IDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:SD3","text":"Transcendental critique converts the question of what understanding can assert into the question of the conditions under which its assertions have objective content.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A4","CH_B337_IDTL:A10","CH_B337_IDTL:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:SD4","text":"The division between analytic and dialectic is a division between valid constraint and illegitimate transcendence of that constraint.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A6","CH_B337_IDTL:A9","CH_B337_IDTL:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:SD5","text":"Reason’s positive claim to enlarge cognition by pure principles is replaced by a critical function that polices the use of understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A11","CH_B337_IDTL:A12","CH_B337_IDTL:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:DNX1","text":"No act of thinking can become objectively valid solely through formal coherence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:DNX2","text":"No pure concept can determine an object independently of possible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:DNX3","text":"No transcendental principle can generate the matter to which it is applied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:DNX4","text":"No extension of formal validity automatically yields material knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:DNX5","text":"No claim about objects in general is legitimate when the objects are unavailable to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:DNX6","text":"No faculty is entitled to unrestricted synthetic judgement merely because it possesses pure principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a self-sufficient metaphysics in which thought alone generates its objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:SNX2","text":"The system excludes cognition detached from the distinction between formal conditions and supplied content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:SNX3","text":"The system excludes the possibility that transcendental principles can enlarge knowledge without an experiential source of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:SNX4","text":"The system excludes an unbounded organon of reason and replaces it with a bounded critical tribunal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:SNX5","text":"The system leaves no legitimate positive science of hyperphysical objects accessible through pure understanding alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:FGS1","text":"Isolate understanding → distinguish pure cognition from sensibility → identify the need for intuitively given objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:FGS2","text":"Combine pure conceptual conditions with intuition → define objective cognition as contentful and truth-capable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:FGS3","text":"Abstract pure principles from their object-giving condition → convert formal validity into illegitimate material assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:FGS4","text":"Treat transcendental logic as an unlimited organon → produce synthetic judgements about objects not given in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:FGS5","text":"Diagnose this extension as dialectical exercise → expose its claims as groundless illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:FGS6","text":"Reassign transcendental logic to its proper critical function → use understanding and reason to test judgements and prevent sophistical delusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:AF1","text":"Isolation of pure understanding → dependence on intuition → analytic conditions of objective thought → misuse beyond experience → dialectical illusion → critique and restriction of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:ME1","text":"The field of transcendental logic is divided into a positive analytic of objective conditions and a negative dialectic of their misuse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:ME2","text":"The distinction between valid and invalid cognition is relocated from formal purity alone to the relation between concepts and possible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:ME3","text":"Metaphysical overreach is reclassified from successful cognition to an illegitimate transformation of formal rules into material claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:ME4","text":"Reason is reclassified from an unlimited producer of knowledge into a critical function that tests and limits judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:ME5","text":"The opposition between pure thought and experience is replaced by a dependency structure in which pure form requires experiential matter for objective use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:ALT1","text":"Surface — Transcendental logic is divided into transcendental analytic and transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:ALT2","text":"Structural — Objective cognition requires the interaction of formal principles of understanding with objects supplied in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critique must distinguish the legitimate canonical use of pure principles from their illegitimate organon-like extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects are cognitionally available only under conditions of possible intuition and experience; thought cannot independently establish a domain of hyperphysical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the division between transcendental analytic and transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the dependency of pure understanding on intuition and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction preserves the shift from formal validity to dialectical illusion when principles are extended beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as analytic principles govern object-directed cognition while dialectic diagnoses their objectless misuse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:MRT5","text":"The principal unresolved tension is that pure understanding is isolated as a distinct source of cognition while remaining unable to yield objective cognition without intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IDTL:MRT6","text":"The argument depends on maintaining a strict distinction between the formal origin of cognition and the experiential source of its objective matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IDTL:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 21","title":"IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic.","context":"Chapter 21"}},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE","layer":"CH","title":"II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.","order":33,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"1ef9b873628e7fd3b72163701161fcbb0198412186479e397e8e206eb762f7cd","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:AS1","text":"The argument seeks a secure criterion for distinguishing pure cognition from empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:AS2","text":"It reframes the distinction through necessity and strict universality rather than through observed regularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:AS3","text":"It establishes that necessity and exceptionless universality indicate an a priori source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:AS4","text":"It applies the criterion to mathematics, causality, and the rejection of Humean associationism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:AS5","text":"It grounds the possibility and certainty of experience in rules that cannot themselves be merely empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:AS6","text":"It extends the a priori analysis from judgments to concepts by identifying irreducible spatial and substantial contents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A1","text":"Experience establishes how an object is constituted in observed cases but not that it could not be otherwise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A2","text":"A proposition whose conception contains necessity is a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A3","text":"A proposition is absolutely a priori when it is not derived from another proposition except one equally involving necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A4","text":"Empirical judgments possess only assumed and comparative universality established through induction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A5","text":"A judgment possessing strict and absolute universality is not derived from experience and is valid a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A6","text":"Necessity and strict universality are inseparably connected and constitute infallible tests of pure cognition, even when used separately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A7","text":"Human cognition contains judgments that are necessary and strictly universal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A8","text":"The concept of a cause contains necessary connection with an effect and strict universality of the corresponding law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A9","text":"Rules that make experience possible cannot all be empirical, because empirical rules are contingent and cannot function as first principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A10","text":"Human beings possess and exercise a faculty of pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A11","text":"Removing sensory properties from the conception of a body leaves space as an element that cannot be annihilated in thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:A12","text":"Removing empirical properties from the conception of an object leaves predicates through which it is conceived as substance or as adhering to substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:NX1","text":"Cognition derived from experience is not cognition of impossibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:NX2","text":"Empirical universality is not strict and absolute universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:NX3","text":"Inductive frequency is not necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:NX4","text":"Causal necessity is not a subjective habit produced by repeated association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:NX5","text":"Experience cannot serve as the ultimate source of the rules on which the certainty of experience depends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:NX6","text":"Pure a priori cognition is not restricted to judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:NX7","text":"The conception of a body is not exhausted by sensory qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:NX8","text":"The conception of an object is not exhausted by empirically acquired properties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:D1","text":"Extending a proposition valid in most observed cases to all cases produces empirical universality rather than strict universality.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A1","CH_B337_IHIE:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:D2","text":"Necessity or strict universality identifies a source of cognition other than experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A2","CH_B337_IHIE:A5","CH_B337_IHIE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:D3","text":"The existence of necessary and strictly universal judgments establishes the exercise of pure a priori cognition in human understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A6","CH_B337_IHIE:A7","CH_B337_IHIE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:D4","text":"Frequent association cannot constitute the concept of causality because association supplies neither necessary connection nor strict universality.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A4","CH_B337_IHIE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:D5","text":"A priori principles are conditions of the certainty and possibility of experience rather than conclusions extracted from experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:D6","text":"Space is identified as an a priori element because it remains after sensory determinations of body are removed.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:D7","text":"Substance is identified as an a priori element because object-conception retains substance-related predicates after empirical properties are removed.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:D8","text":"The distinction between empirical and a priori cognition applies both to judgmental form and to conceptual content.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A10","CH_B337_IHIE:A11","CH_B337_IHIE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:SD1","text":"Experience is epistemically dependent on a priori rules even though empirical cognition is the immediate content of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A1","CH_B337_IHIE:A9","CH_B337_IHIE:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:SD2","text":"The text relocates necessity from repeated succession in objects to the a priori cognitive conditions under which objects can be experienced and judged.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A8","CH_B337_IHIE:NX4","CH_B337_IHIE:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:SD3","text":"Necessity and strict universality function as markers of cognitive origin rather than merely as degrees of evidential confidence.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A2","CH_B337_IHIE:A5","CH_B337_IHIE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:SD4","text":"The anti-Humean causal argument and the transcendental argument from the possibility of experience converge on the same a priori architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A8","CH_B337_IHIE:A9","CH_B337_IHIE:D4","CH_B337_IHIE:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:SD5","text":"The subtractive analysis of concepts identifies a priori content through what remains invariant when empirical determinations are removed.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A11","CH_B337_IHIE:A12","CH_B337_IHIE:D6","CH_B337_IHIE:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:SD6","text":"The a priori is presented as both the source of certain judgments and the condition for representing objects at all.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A7","CH_B337_IHIE:A10","CH_B337_IHIE:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:DNX1","text":"The system depends on the falsity of the claim that experience is the exhaustive source of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the conversion of contingency into necessity through accumulation of cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:DNX3","text":"The system excludes psychological compulsion as an adequate account of objective necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the possibility that experience can provide its own ultimate epistemic foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:DNX5","text":"The system excludes a conception of objects composed entirely of sensory predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:DNX6","text":"The system excludes the restriction of a priori cognition to explicit propositions while leaving concepts empirically assembled.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:SNX1","text":"A cognition wholly derived from contingent experience cannot possess absolute certainty within this system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:SNX2","text":"A law whose necessity is merely a record of repeated conjunction is inadmissible as genuine causal knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:SNX3","text":"A conception of experience without nonempirical governing rules is structurally unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:SNX4","text":"An object-conception with no irreducible spatial or substantial remainder is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:SNX5","text":"The system leaves no admissible position in which empirical regularity alone grounds strict universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:FGS1","text":"Observed regularity → contingency remains possible → experience cannot establish absolute necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:FGS2","text":"Necessity or strict universality → nonempirical origin → classification as a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:FGS3","text":"Necessary judgment → causal example → rejection of frequent association as the source of causal necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:FGS4","text":"Certainty of experience → dependence on governing rules → impossibility of treating all such rules as empirical → requirement of a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:FGS5","text":"Removal of sensory predicates from body → persistence of space → identification of spatial cognition as a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:FGS6","text":"Removal of empirical predicates from object → persistence of substance-related predicates → identification of substantial cognition as a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:FGS7","text":"Judgment-level necessity plus concept-level residue → unified faculty of pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:AF1","text":"Failure of experience to establish impossibility → distinction between comparative and strict universality → necessity and exceptionlessness as criteria of the a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:AF2","text":"Necessary and universal judgments → causal example → rejection of Humean association → demonstration that necessity cannot be empirical habit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:AF3","text":"Certainty of experience → dependence on nonempirical rules → establishment of pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:AF4","text":"Judgmental a priori cognition → subtractive analysis of concepts → residual space and substance → extension of the a priori beyond judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:ME1","text":"Universality is divided into empirical generalization and strict a priori validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:ME2","text":"Necessity becomes a diagnostic of cognitive origin rather than a product of accumulated observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:ME3","text":"Causality is reclassified from a sequence of associated events to a necessary rule of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:ME4","text":"Experience is reclassified from an independent foundation into a domain conditioned by a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:ME5","text":"The a priori field expands from propositions to the conceptual conditions under which objects can be represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:ME6","text":"The distinction between empirical and pure cognition becomes a distinction between removable content and irreducible cognitive form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:ALT1","text":"Surface: The text identifies necessity and strict universality as tests for distinguishing pure from empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:ALT2","text":"Structural: The text locates empirical judgment, causality, and object-conception within a dependence on nonempirical cognitive rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The text replaces inductive verification and associationist explanation with criteria based on modal force and transcendental dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: The text treats a priori cognition not as knowledge chronologically preceding experience but as a condition that makes certain experience and object-representation possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the distinction between empirical and pure cognition, its criteria, its causal application, its transcendental grounding, and its extension to concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:MRT2","text":"The extracted system underdetermines the detailed constitution of the faculty of a priori cognition and the mechanism by which necessity is represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if “a priori” denotes a nonempirical condition of validity rather than a temporally earlier acquisition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:MRT4","text":"Its strongest structure is the separation of empirical universality from strict universality and the dependence of experience on nonempirical rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:MRT5","text":"The claim that necessity and strict universality are inseparable while each remains independently infallible leaves their exact logical relation insufficiently formalized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:MRT6","text":"The causal example partly relies on the necessity already contained in the concept of cause, making the demonstration partly definitional.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:MRT7","text":"The subtractive concept argument depends on treating the inability to think away a representation as evidence of its a priori status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IHIE:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement from diagnostic criterion to transcendental ground and from judgmental form to conceptual residue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IHIE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 02","title":"II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”.","context":"Chapter 02"}},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT","layer":"CH","title":"§ I. Introductory.","order":34,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"09dcffe376aef823a5a5620a80b285f08c08974a6ab1001a63a14bd55a92835f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AS1","text":"The problem is to determine how human knowledge relates to objects and which faculties make that relation possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AS2","text":"Object-relation is reframed as a sequence in which intuition provides immediate givenness and thought subsequently determines what is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AS3","text":"Human intuition is grounded in receptivity: an object must be given by affecting the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AS4","text":"Sensibility and understanding are separated as the faculties that respectively furnish intuitions and concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AS5","text":"Empirical cognition is decomposed into sensory matter and a priori form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AS6","text":"A double isolation of sensibility and sensation reveals pure intuition as the formal condition of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AS7","text":"The argument concludes by identifying space and time as the two pure forms of sensuous intuition and by distinguishing transcendental æsthetic from transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A1","text":"Human knowledge relates immediately to objects only through intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A2","text":"Intuition occurs only when an object is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A3","text":"For human cognition, an object is given only insofar as it affects the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A4","text":"The capacity to receive representations through affection is sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A5","text":"Sensibility furnishes intuitions, while understanding thinks objects and produces conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A6","text":"Human thought must ultimately relate, directly or indirectly, to intuition and therefore to sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A7","text":"Sensation is the effect of an object upon the faculty of representation insofar as the mind is affected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A8","text":"An empirical intuition relates to an object through sensation, and its undetermined object is a phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A9","text":"The matter of a phenomenon corresponds to sensation, while its form orders its content under relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A10","text":"Phenomenal matter is given a posteriori, whereas phenomenal form lies a priori in the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A11","text":"Pure representation contains nothing belonging to sensation, and pure intuition is the a priori form of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A12","text":"When conceptual determinations and sensory qualities are removed from empirical intuition, extension and shape remain as formal content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A13","text":"Transcendental æsthetic is the science of the a priori principles of sensibility and is distinct from transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A14","text":"Inquiry into sensibility proceeds by first separating it from understanding and then separating empirical intuition from sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:A15","text":"Space and time are the two pure forms of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX1","text":"Human object-cognition is not immediately constituted by thought alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX2","text":"Intuition is not possible without an object being given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX3","text":"Human object-givenness is not independent of the mind’s being affected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX4","text":"Sensibility is not understanding, and intuition is not conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX5","text":"Sensation is not the form of a phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX6","text":"Phenomenal form is not posterior sensory content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX7","text":"Pure intuition is not sensation or empirical content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX8","text":"Pure intuition is not a conceptual determination supplied by understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX9","text":"Pure intuition is not itself a real sensed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX10","text":"Transcendental æsthetic is not a critique of taste.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX11","text":"Empirical rules of taste are not determinate a priori laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:NX12","text":"Transcendental æsthetic is not transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:D1","text":"Human cognition of objects requires a circuit of givenness, sensibility, intuition, and thought.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A1","CH_B337_IINT:A2","CH_B337_IINT:A4","CH_B337_IINT:A5","CH_B337_IINT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:D2","text":"Concepts cannot yield human object-cognition unless they ultimately connect with sensible intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A5","CH_B337_IINT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:D3","text":"An empirical phenomenon is a composite of sensory matter and a priori formal organization.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A7","CH_B337_IINT:A8","CH_B337_IINT:A9","CH_B337_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:D4","text":"The formal conditions of phenomenal organization can be investigated independently of particular sensations.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A9","CH_B337_IINT:A10","CH_B337_IINT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:D5","text":"The investigation of sensibility requires a double abstraction from conceptual determinations and sensory content.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A11","CH_B337_IINT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:D6","text":"Pure intuition is the residual formal structure that remains after empirical content and understanding’s determinations are removed.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A10","CH_B337_IINT:A11","CH_B337_IINT:A12","CH_B337_IINT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:D7","text":"A priori knowledge of sensibility concerns the form under which phenomena are arranged rather than their sensory matter.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A9","CH_B337_IINT:A10","CH_B337_IINT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:D8","text":"The principles governing pure sensibility require a distinct science from the principles governing pure thought.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A5","CH_B337_IINT:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:D9","text":"Space and time function as the fundamental formal coordinates of human sensuous intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A11","CH_B337_IINT:A14","CH_B337_IINT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SD1","text":"Human cognition is structurally heterogeneous: neither receptivity without form nor thought without intuition suffices for object-cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A4","CH_B337_IINT:A5","CH_B337_IINT:A6","CH_B337_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SD2","text":"The object as known by humans is accessible only through a relation between external affection and internal formal organization.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A3","CH_B337_IINT:A9","CH_B337_IINT:A10","CH_B337_IINT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SD3","text":"The transcendental method converts the analysis of experience into an extraction of conditions that are not themselves empirical contents.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A10","CH_B337_IINT:A11","CH_B337_IINT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SD4","text":"Phenomenal order possesses an a priori architecture even though its material content is supplied a posteriori.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A8","CH_B337_IINT:A9","CH_B337_IINT:A10","CH_B337_IINT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SD5","text":"The term æsthetic is reclassified from the domain of taste into the domain of the formal laws of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A13","CH_B337_IINT:NX10","CH_B337_IINT:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SD6","text":"The division between transcendental æsthetic and transcendental logic maps the conditions of cognition onto distinct receptive and conceptual operations.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A5","CH_B337_IINT:A13","CH_B337_IINT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:DNX1","text":"Human knowledge excludes access to objects through an intuition independent of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:DNX2","text":"The system excludes a purely empirical account of phenomenal order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:DNX3","text":"The system excludes a purely conceptual account of the immediate relation to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:DNX4","text":"Sensibility is not treated as unstructured reception; it already contains an a priori capacity for ordering representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:DNX5","text":"A priori conditions are not treated as material contents supplied before experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:DNX6","text":"Pure form is not collapsed into the activity of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:DNX7","text":"The formal conditions of appearance are not identified with the particular qualities delivered by sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:DNX8","text":"The analysis does not admit the object of human knowledge independently of the conditions under which it can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SNX1","text":"Unmediated human cognition of objects is unavailable within the operative system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SNX2","text":"No account of human object-knowledge is admissible outside the relation between givenness, affection, sensibility, and intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SNX3","text":"Phenomenal order cannot be reduced either to received sensation or to imposed concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SNX4","text":"The possibility of an object being known without sensible givenness is structurally foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SNX5","text":"The object apart from its mode of sensible givenness remains outside the developed account of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:SNX6","text":"Knowledge cannot be grounded in empirical content without presupposing a prior formal field in which that content can appear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:FGS1","text":"Object-cognition is first divided into immediate intuition and subsequent thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:FGS2","text":"Intuition is conditioned by object-givenness, object-affection, and the receptive faculty of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:FGS3","text":"Sensation supplies empirical matter, while a priori form organizes that matter into phenomenal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:FGS4","text":"The analysis removes understanding’s conceptual determinations from sensibility and then removes sensation from empirical intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:FGS5","text":"The residual formal structure is identified as pure intuition rather than as sensation, concept, or empirical object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:FGS6","text":"Pure intuition is specified as space and time, and the science of its principles is named transcendental æsthetic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:FGS7","text":"Transcendental æsthetic is placed beside, but not within, the science of the principles of pure thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AF1","text":"The question of object-knowledge moves from immediate intuition to the conditions under which an object can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AF2","text":"Object-givenness moves through affection into sensibility, and sensibility is distinguished from understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AF3","text":"Sensibility is decomposed into empirical sensation and a priori formal organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AF4","text":"Double abstraction isolates pure intuition from both conceptual determinations and sensory matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:AF5","text":"Pure intuition is specified as space and time, producing a distinct science of sensibility alongside transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:ME1","text":"Object-knowledge is reclassified from direct apprehension into a mediated relation among affection, receptivity, intuition, and thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:ME2","text":"The boundary between sensibility and understanding becomes a constitutive division within cognition rather than a distinction between different kinds of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:ME3","text":"Phenomena become analyzable as structured composites of a posteriori matter and a priori form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:ME4","text":"The a priori is relocated from conceptual thought alone to the formal operation of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:ME5","text":"Æsthetic is redefined as a science of sensibility rather than a theory of taste.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:ME6","text":"Space and time are elevated from ordinary features of experience to fundamental forms required for human sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:ME7","text":"The opening division of transcendental philosophy is redrawn around the difference between the conditions of receiving objects and the conditions of thinking them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, human representations are distinguished according to whether they arise through sensation, intuition, or conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, cognition is organized as a dependency system in which sensibility supplies the material of givenness and understanding supplies conceptual determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, the argument establishes a method of isolation and subtraction for identifying non-empirical conditions of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:ALT4","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, space and time are treated as a priori forms of sensuous appearance, while the status of objects apart from sensible givenness remains outside the developed analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s movement from object-relation through sensibility to space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:MRT2","text":"The system is strongest where it differentiates sensation, form, intuition, sensibility, and understanding without reducing one to another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction underdetermines the full argument for why space and time, rather than other forms, exhaust pure sensibility; that determination is deferred to the subsequent analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:MRT4","text":"The system retains an unresolved tension between the object’s affection of the mind and the mind’s a priori contribution to phenomenal form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:MRT5","text":"A second tension remains between intuition’s dependence on a given object and pure intuition’s independence from any actual sensory object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IINT:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the real argumentative sequence because the final identification of space and time follows from the prior double abstraction rather than appearing as an isolated assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IINT:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 08","title":"§ I. Introductory.","context":"Chapter 08"}},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0","layer":"CH","title":"I. Of Logic in General.","order":35,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"be27ef529335a4fa79d2c469b98044d46860ca5c618c4b61cb91494e0eec230b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:AS1","text":"Cognition must explain how an object is given to a finite mind and how that object is thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:AS2","text":"Receptive intuition gives objects, while spontaneous understanding thinks them through conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:AS3","text":"Knowledge arises only through the non-substitutable cooperation of sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:AS4","text":"The separation of cognitive faculties grounds the separation of æsthetic from logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:AS5","text":"Logic is internally divided by scope of use and by whether it abstracts from empirical conditions of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:AS6","text":"Pure general logic is established as an a priori formal canon, while applied logic is relegated to empirical regulation of actual understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:AS7","text":"The text proceeds by transcendental differentiation, taxonomic purification, and restriction of logical authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A1","text":"Objects are given through the mind’s receptivity for impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A2","text":"Objects are thought through the understanding’s spontaneous production of conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A3","text":"Neither conceptions without corresponding intuition nor intuition without conceptions affords cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A4","text":"Human intuition is necessarily sensuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A5","text":"Sensibility is the faculty of receptively receiving representations through affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A6","text":"Understanding is the faculty of spontaneously producing representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A7","text":"Sensibility cannot think, and understanding cannot intuite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A8","text":"Pure intuitions and pure conceptions alone are possible a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A9","text":"Empirical representations contain sensation and belong to a posteriori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A10","text":"Æsthetic concerns laws of sensibility, while logic concerns laws of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A11","text":"General logic supplies necessary laws of thought independently of differences among objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A12","text":"Particular logic supplies laws of correct thinking for a determinate class of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A13","text":"General logic abstracts from the content of cognition and from distinctions among objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A14","text":"Pure general logic abstracts from empirical conditions affecting the exercise of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A15","text":"Pure general logic contains no empirical or psychological principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A16","text":"Applied general logic concerns understanding under subjective empirical conditions of use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:A17","text":"Applied general logic is a cathartic of understanding rather than a canon or an organon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:NX1","text":"Cognition is not produced by concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:NX2","text":"Cognition is not produced by intuitions alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:NX3","text":"Sensibility is not a mode of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:NX4","text":"Understanding is not a faculty of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:NX5","text":"General logic is not concerned with the material content of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:NX6","text":"Pure general logic is not grounded in psychology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:NX7","text":"Pure general logic is not an organon for cognition of particular objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:NX8","text":"Applied logic is not a demonstrated a priori science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:NX9","text":"Applied logic is not merely a set of scholastic exercises derived from pure logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:D1","text":"Cognition requires that a sensuously given object be brought under a conception.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A1","CH_B337_ILG0:A2","CH_B337_ILG0:A3","CH_B337_ILG0:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:D2","text":"The distinction between pure and empirical cognition follows from whether sensation enters the representation.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A8","CH_B337_ILG0:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:D3","text":"Human cognition cannot receive objects except through sensuous affection.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A1","CH_B337_ILG0:A4","CH_B337_ILG0:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:D4","text":"The sciences of sensibility and understanding must remain distinct even though cognition requires both faculties.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A3","CH_B337_ILG0:A7","CH_B337_ILG0:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:D5","text":"General logic can legislate only for the form of thought, not for cognition of any particular object-domain.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A11","CH_B337_ILG0:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:D6","text":"A particular science can formulate its proper logical rules only after sufficient knowledge of its object-domain has been acquired.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A12","CH_B337_ILG0:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:D7","text":"Pure general logic claims demonstrative a priori certainty because it excludes empirical and psychological sources.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A14","CH_B337_ILG0:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:D8","text":"Applied logic can diagnose attention, error, doubt, hesitation, and conviction only as empirically conditioned states of understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A16","CH_B337_ILG0:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:SD1","text":"Logical validity is separated from the psychological causes, impediments, and habits through which thinkers actually reason.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A13","CH_B337_ILG0:A15","CH_B337_ILG0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:SD2","text":"The possibility of universally necessary logical law depends on the exclusion of every condition that individuates a cognizing subject or object.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A11","CH_B337_ILG0:A13","CH_B337_ILG0:A14","CH_B337_ILG0:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:SD3","text":"An organon of cognition is possible only locally, for determinate sciences and determinate object-domains, rather than universally for understanding as such.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A11","CH_B337_ILG0:A12","CH_B337_ILG0:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:SD4","text":"The pedagogical use of special logic is structurally posterior to scientific development, despite its institutional use as preparation for science.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A12","CH_B337_ILG0:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:SD5","text":"The critique of error belongs to empirical self-regulation of cognition rather than to the formal ground of logical necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A15","CH_B337_ILG0:A16","CH_B337_ILG0:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:DNX1","text":"Cognition is not passive reception of a self-present object, because givenness requires conceptual determination to become knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:DNX2","text":"Cognition is not spontaneous intellectual production of its own objects, because human thought requires sensuous givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:DNX3","text":"Formal logical necessity is not derived from the empirical constitution, history, or psychology of actual thinkers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:DNX4","text":"Universal logic cannot yield substantive knowledge of objects merely by applying formal laws of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:DNX5","text":"The contingent success or failure of human reasoning cannot determine the validity conditions of pure logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:SNX1","text":"Intellectual intuition is unavailable to the human cognitive condition presupposed by the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:SNX2","text":"A single faculty that both gives and thinks objects is foreclosed by the irreducible division between sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:SNX3","text":"A universal method for generating knowledge of every possible object is foreclosed by the restriction of general logic to form alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:SNX4","text":"Empirical anthropology cannot serve as the ultimate foundation of necessity, universality, or demonstrative certainty in logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:FGS1","text":"Affection of sensibility produces intuitive givenness of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:FGS2","text":"Spontaneous understanding brings the given intuition under conceptions and thereby produces cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:FGS3","text":"The non-exchangeability of givenness and thought requires a division between laws of sensibility and laws of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:FGS4","text":"Laws of understanding divide into universal formal laws and object-class-specific laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:FGS5","text":"Abstraction from all content and empirical conditions yields pure general logic as a formal canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:FGS6","text":"Retention of subjective empirical conditions yields applied logic as a non-demonstrative cathartic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:FGS7","text":"Restriction of pure logic to form prevents its conversion into an organon of objective knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:AF1","text":"Sensuous affection → intuition → conceptual determination → cognition → differentiation of sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:AF2","text":"Differentiated faculties → æsthetic and logic → general and particular logic → pure and applied general logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:AF3","text":"Abstraction from object-content and empirical conditions → a priori formal canon → exclusion of psychological grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:AF4","text":"Retention of subjective conditions → analysis of cognitive impediments and errors → cathartic rather than scientific logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:ME1","text":"Knowledge is redescribed as a composite achievement of two irreducible faculties rather than as the output of a single cognitive power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:ME2","text":"The distinction between validity and actual reasoning becomes visible as a distinction between pure and applied logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:ME3","text":"Logic is divided between universal formal legislation and domain-specific scientific method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:ME4","text":"Psychology is reclassified from a possible foundation of logic into an empirical account of obstacles and conditions in its exercise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:ME5","text":"Error is relocated from formal logic’s constitutive laws to the contingent empirical use of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text classifies intuition, conception, sensibility, understanding, general logic, particular logic, pure logic, and applied logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text grounds these classifications in the division between receptive object-givenness and spontaneous object-thinking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text secures logical necessity by purifying formal rules from object-content, psychology, and empirical variation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text presupposes finite human cognition, in which objects must affect sensibility before they can be thought, and in which special logical methods emerge only after sciences have matured.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the text’s central division of cognition into sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:MRT2","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the taxonomy of æsthetic, general logic, particular logic, pure logic, and applied logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning how intuition and conception are united in a determinate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:MRT4","text":"The system is most coherent where formal logical necessity is secured through abstraction from empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:MRT5","text":"The system depends on an unresolved tension between the indispensability of empirical cognition and the exclusion of empirical conditions from pure logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:MRT6","text":"The system contains a temporal tension because particular logic is institutionally preparatory yet rationally posterior to the mature science whose laws it formulates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ILG0:MRT7","text":"The reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement from the conditions of cognition to the restricted authority of pure formal logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ILG0:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 18","title":"I. Of Logic in General.","context":"Chapter 18"}},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3","layer":"CH","title":"Introductory § 3","order":36,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"69f35c9328203ba5860b9d64d8bb9eb1b233a3006b64c49d69e1a201288142c9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:AS1","text":"The exercise of a cognitive faculty produces conceptions under variable conditions of time, attention, and penetration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:AS2","text":"Conceptions discovered through unprincipled observation form contingent, non-systematic collections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:AS3","text":"The non-systematic character of empirical discovery prevents certainty about the completeness and proper order of the conceptions obtained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:AS4","text":"Transcendental philosophy must seek pure conceptions according to a principle because they arise from the understanding as an absolute unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:AS5","text":"A unifying principle assigns each pure conception its proper place and permits the completeness of the system to be determined a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:A1","text":"The activation of a cognitive faculty manifests different conceptions under different circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:A2","text":"The number and range of conceptions disclosed by observation vary with the time and penetration devoted to their consideration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:A3","text":"Mechanically discovered conceptions do not present themselves in systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:A4","text":"Haphazardly discovered conceptions are connected primarily through resemblance and ordered by increasing complexity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:A5","text":"Resemblance-based and complexity-based ordering cannot establish a complete system with certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:A6","text":"Pure conceptions arise unmixed from the understanding as an absolute unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:A7","text":"Conceptions arising from one absolute unity must be connected according to a single conception or idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:A8","text":"A principled connection supplies a rule for assigning each pure conception its proper place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:A9","text":"A principled rule makes the completeness of the system determinable a priori rather than by choice or chance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:NX1","text":"The discovery of conceptions is not sufficient when it proceeds mechanically or haphazardly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:NX2","text":"The discovered conceptions are not presented in an intrinsic systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:NX3","text":"Similarity does not constitute a sufficient principle of systematic connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:NX4","text":"Increasing complexity does not constitute a sufficient principle of systematic order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:NX5","text":"The completeness of the system cannot depend on mere choice or chance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:NX6","text":"Transcendental philosophy is not permitted to treat pure conceptions as an accidental collection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:D1","text":"Variable observational conditions make any mechanically assembled collection of conceptions epistemically incomplete.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A1","CH_B337_INT3:A2","CH_B337_INT3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:D2","text":"A resemblance-based classification can produce order without producing systematic necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A4","CH_B337_INT3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:D3","text":"The absence of a governing principle leaves the proper place of each conception undetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A3","CH_B337_INT3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:D4","text":"The unity of the understanding requires an internal rule of connection among its pure conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A6","CH_B337_INT3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:D5","text":"The system’s completeness becomes an a priori question only after the conceptions have been related through a unifying principle.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A7","CH_B337_INT3:A8","CH_B337_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:D6","text":"Transcendental philosophy converts the problem of collecting conceptions into the problem of determining their systematic order.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A3","CH_B337_INT3:A6","CH_B337_INT3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:SD1","text":"The passage shifts the criterion of philosophical adequacy from successful discovery to principled completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A3","CH_B337_INT3:A5","CH_B337_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:SD2","text":"The unity of the faculty becomes the generative ground for the architecture of its conceptual system.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A6","CH_B337_INT3:A7","CH_B337_INT3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:SD3","text":"A priori systematization functions as a control against the contingency, incompleteness, and arbitrary ordering of empirical classification.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A2","CH_B337_INT3:A5","CH_B337_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:SD4","text":"The text treats philosophical method as the reconstruction of an already presupposed unity rather than the indefinite accumulation of findings.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A3","CH_B337_INT3:A6","CH_B337_INT3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the possibility that exhaustive knowledge of pure conceptions can be secured by prolonged observation alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the possibility that resemblance reveals the necessary relations among conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the possibility that conceptual complexity mirrors the lawful structure of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the possibility that the understanding is merely an aggregate of independently discovered conceptual contents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the possibility that completeness is a retrospective judgment based on an arbitrarily selected collection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:DNX6","text":"The system excludes a plurality of unrelated principles as an adequate ground for the unity of pure conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:SNX1","text":"The argument cannot admit that the order of pure conceptions is fundamentally external to the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses the possibility that the system remains permanently open because no internal principle can determine its limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:SNX3","text":"The argument suppresses the possibility that conceptual unity is an effect of classification rather than a property of the faculty itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:SNX4","text":"The argument leaves no legitimate route from contingent discovery to completeness without a prior unity capable of governing discovery.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:FGS1","text":"Variable cognitive activation → manifestation of diverse conceptions → dependence on time and penetration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:FGS2","text":"Mechanical discovery → resemblance-based association and complexity-based sequencing → absence of systematic necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:FGS3","text":"Absence of systematic necessity → indeterminacy of proper placement and completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:FGS4","text":"Pure origin in the understanding → presupposition of absolute unity → requirement of connection under one conception or idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:FGS5","text":"Unifying conception or idea → rule of placement → a priori determination of systematic completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:FGS6","text":"The argument therefore replaces contingent collection with principled derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:AF1","text":"Variable cognition → haphazard conceptual discovery → non-systematic classification → epistemic indeterminacy → appeal to the unity of the understanding → principled connection → a priori completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:AF2","text":"Collection of concepts → insufficiency of resemblance and complexity → transcendental reorientation from discovery to system-generation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:ME1","text":"Conceptual plurality is reclassified from a collection to be surveyed into a system to be generated from a principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:ME2","text":"Similarity and complexity lose their status as primary ordering principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:ME3","text":"Completeness is relocated from empirical accumulation to a priori structural determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:ME4","text":"The understanding is reclassified from the source of scattered conceptual manifestations into the unified ground of their systematic relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:ME5","text":"Chance and arbitrary choice are displaced as possible foundations of philosophical classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:ALT1","text":"Surface — Conceptions appear under differing conditions and are collected with varying degrees of extent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:ALT2","text":"Structural — Mechanical collection produces resemblance-based and complexity-based orders without systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Transcendental philosophy must replace classificatory accumulation with principle-governed reconstruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Pure conceptions are grounded in the understanding’s absolute unity, while empirical discovery remains temporally conditioned and therefore unable to determine completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from mechanical discovery to transcendental systematization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:MRT2","text":"The central dependency is preserved: the demand for a system follows from the alleged absolute unity of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:MRT3","text":"The structure remains underdetermined regarding the content of the pure conceptions and the identity of the unifying conception or idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:MRT4","text":"The argument is internally coherent if the understanding’s absolute unity and the necessity of systematic connection are accepted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:MRT5","text":"Its strongest operation is the exclusion of empirical collection, resemblance, complexity, choice, and chance as grounds of completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_INT3:MRT6","text":"Its unresolved tension is that the required unity is asserted as the ground of the system before the specific rule generated from that unity is exhibited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_INT3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 24","title":"Introductory § 3","context":"Chapter 24"}},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS","layer":"CH","title":"II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","order":37,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"7888cf131663db18bd33dfc739f4ce5bb143299595b72c216761e77a52e634c6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:AS1","text":"Human cognition moves from sensibility through understanding to reason, which seeks the highest unity of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:AS2","text":"Reason is distinguished from understanding by producing unity among rules under principles rather than unity among appearances under rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:AS3","text":"The logical form of syllogism reveals reason’s operative demand to seek the condition of every conditioned cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:AS4","text":"Recursive pursuit of conditions generates the idea of the unconditioned as the completion of a conditioned series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:AS5","text":"The logical demand for completeness becomes transcendental illusion when it is treated as an objective principle concerning objects themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:AS6","text":"Transcendental dialectic must determine whether reason’s demand is regulative for cognition or constitutive of an unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A1","text":"All human cognition begins in sensibility, is ordered by understanding, and is brought toward higher unity by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A2","text":"Understanding produces unity of appearances by subsuming intuition under rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A3","text":"Reason produces unity of rules by subsuming the rules of understanding under principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A4","text":"Reason relates to objects only mediately through the concepts and judgments of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A5","text":"A principle, in the strict sense, is a synthetic cognition through concepts by which a particular is cognized in a general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A6","text":"The logical use of reason seeks to reduce the plurality of cognitions and rules to the smallest possible number of general conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A7","text":"Every syllogistic inference leads reason from a conditioned judgment toward the general condition under which that judgment follows.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A8","text":"The recursive search for conditions leads reason to seek the unconditioned as the completion of a series of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A9","text":"The inference from a given conditioned item to a given unconditioned totality is synthetic rather than analytically contained in the concept of the conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A10","text":"Principles of understanding are immanent because they concern only the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A11","text":"A principle that asserts completion of the conditioned series in objects would be transcendent and could not receive adequate empirical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:A12","text":"Transcendental dialectic arises from the possible confusion of a logical maxim of inquiry with an objectively valid principle of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:NX1","text":"Reason is not a faculty that directly applies concepts to sensuous intuitions or empirical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:NX2","text":"Rational unity is not the unity that makes experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:NX3","text":"A universal proposition is not thereby a principle in the strict transcendental sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:NX4","text":"Cognition from principles is not reducible to ordinary understanding-based generalization or induction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:NX5","text":"The logical need to seek higher conditions is not by itself an objective law of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:NX6","text":"The demand for systematic unity does not entitle cognition to presuppose that nature itself exhibits complete unity for the convenience of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:NX7","text":"The unconditioned is not analytically given whenever a conditioned cognition is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:NX8","text":"Transcendent principles are not legitimately usable as principles of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:D1","text":"Reason operates at a second order: it unifies the rules through which understanding already unifies appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A2","CH_B337_IPRS:A3","CH_B337_IPRS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:D2","text":"The logical form of syllogism supplies the model through which reason’s transcendental aspiration is identified.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A6","CH_B337_IPRS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:D3","text":"The search for an unconditioned is generated by the recursive structure of conditioning rather than by direct intuition of an unconditioned object.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A4","CH_B337_IPRS:A7","CH_B337_IPRS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:D4","text":"The claim that the whole series of conditions is given cannot be justified merely by analyzing the concept of a conditioned member of that series.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A8","CH_B337_IPRS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:D5","text":"Pure reason exceeds the domain of possible experience when its logical demand for completion is converted into a claim about objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A10","CH_B337_IPRS:A11","CH_B337_IPRS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:D6","text":"Transcendental illusion is structurally produced by reason’s own lawful drive toward systematic completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A6","CH_B337_IPRS:A8","CH_B337_IPRS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:SD1","text":"The same operation that makes systematic cognition possible also produces a tendency to posit an object exceeding all possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A6","CH_B337_IPRS:A8","CH_B337_IPRS:A10","CH_B337_IPRS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:SD2","text":"Reason’s highest unifying function is internally divided between a legitimate ordering of cognitions and an illegitimate determination of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A3","CH_B337_IPRS:A6","CH_B337_IPRS:A11","CH_B337_IPRS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:SD3","text":"Dialectic is not accidental error but the critical analysis of a necessary transition from logical conditioning to transcendent totalization.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A7","CH_B337_IPRS:A8","CH_B337_IPRS:A9","CH_B337_IPRS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:SD4","text":"The unconditioned functions as the limiting endpoint of rational inquiry before it can function as an object of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A4","CH_B337_IPRS:A8","CH_B337_IPRS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:DNX1","text":"No direct intuition or empirical cognition can secure the existence of the unconditioned required by reason’s logical movement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:DNX2","text":"No transition from the necessity of seeking conditions to the objective existence of a completed series is licensed without an additional synthetic claim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:DNX3","text":"No systematic unity demanded by cognition can be assumed to be an intrinsic total unity of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:DNX4","text":"No principle of reason can be constitutive of objects merely because it is unavoidable in the logical organization of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:SNX1","text":"The system excludes an available cognitive standpoint from which the totality of conditions could be encountered as a completed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the coincidence of thought’s demand for the unconditioned with experience’s capacity to supply it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:SNX3","text":"The system is organized around an unconditioned that reason must seek but cannot immediately cognize as given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:FGS1","text":"Intuition is ordered by understanding through rules, and rules are ordered by reason through principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:FGS2","text":"A conditioned judgment is referred to its general condition through syllogistic inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:FGS3","text":"The condition found for a conditioned judgment becomes itself conditioned and therefore subject to a further search for its condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:FGS4","text":"Recursive conditioning produces the logical maxim of seeking an unconditioned completion of the series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:FGS5","text":"Objectifying that maxim as a property of things converts rational unity into a transcendent synthetic claim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:FGS6","text":"Critical dialectic separates the legitimate logical demand for systematic unity from the illegitimate constitutive claim to cognize an unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:AF1","text":"Cognitive hierarchy → distinction between rules and principles → definition of reason as unity of rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:AF2","text":"Syllogistic mediation → search for general conditions → recursive ascent through conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:AF3","text":"Recursive ascent → idea of the unconditioned → synthetic postulation of a complete series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:AF4","text":"Synthetic postulation of totality → transcendence beyond possible experience → transcendental illusory appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:AF5","text":"Transcendental illusory appearance → critical distinction between logical maxim and objective principle → program of transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:ME1","text":"Reason is reclassified from a faculty of direct knowledge into a faculty that organizes the rules governing knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:ME2","text":"The distinction between immanent and transcendent use redraws the boundary between conditions of experience and claims about totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:ME3","text":"The unconditioned is reclassified from an apparently given metaphysical object into a problem generated by the logic of conditional inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:ME4","text":"Error is reclassified from contingent misuse into a structurally recurrent illusion rooted in reason’s own demand for completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:ME5","text":"Systematic unity is detached from any immediate entitlement to assert that objects themselves form a completed systematic whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:ALT1","text":"Surface — Reason is defined through its relation to syllogism, mediate inference, principles, and the search for higher conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:ALT2","text":"Structural — Understanding governs appearances through rules, while reason governs the plurality of those rules through systematic unification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critique must diagnose whether a demand intrinsic to rational inquiry regulates cognition or illegitimately legislates for objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The move from every conditioned member toward an unconditioned whole introduces a totality that cannot be supplied within the successive conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s movement from the hierarchy of faculties to the dialectical problem of the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:MRT2","text":"The system depends decisively on the distinction between a logical requirement to seek conditions and an objective claim that the complete series exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:MRT3","text":"The structure remains deliberately underdetermined regarding whether pure reason possesses valid synthetic principles, because that question is posed as the task of dialectic rather than settled here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:MRT4","text":"The strongest structural point is the derivation of transcendental illusion from the recursive form of syllogistic conditioning rather than from accidental irrationality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:MRT5","text":"The central fault line is that reason is presented as necessarily seeking the unconditioned while being denied any direct experiential route to establish it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:MRT6","text":"A second fault line remains between reason’s claim to supply principles and the critical restriction that its transcendent principles lack adequate empirical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPRS:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument if the chapter is treated as a diagnostic setup for dialectic rather than as a completed proof that the unconditioned is absent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPRS:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 53","title":"II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","context":"Chapter 53"}},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN","layer":"CH","title":"III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”","order":38,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"963622962cf201d7df0f17083649254bd76f210fce7a5ba5d884e9e38f2062bd","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:AS1","text":"Human reason forms cognitions that claim validity beyond possible experience and directs them toward supersensible problems.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:AS2","text":"The importance assigned to these problems drives metaphysics to pursue them before examining reason’s capacity for such knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:AS3","text":"The central difficulty is therefore not merely whether metaphysical propositions are true, but whether their mode of production is legitimate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:AS4","text":"Mathematics demonstrates the possibility of a priori cognition, but its dependence on intuition prevents it from authorizing unlimited speculative extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:AS5","text":"Reason mistakes the formal novelty produced by analyzing given concepts for a material enlargement of knowledge and thereby conceals foreign additions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:AS6","text":"The argument moves from supersensible ambition through diagnosis of its concealed mechanism to the demand that the modes, limits, and validity of a priori cognition be distinguished before metaphysical construction proceeds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A1","text":"Cognitions can claim to rise above the sphere of all possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A2","text":"Supersensible concepts lack corresponding objects within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A3","text":"Pure reason necessarily raises the problems of God, freedom, and immortality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A4","text":"Metaphysics undertakes to solve these problems before determining whether reason is capable of solving them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A5","text":"Experience provides the secure ground that supersensible speculation abandons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A6","text":"A priori cognition requires examination of its origin, extent, validity, and worth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A7","text":"Mathematical cognition is a priori while remaining connected to objects and cognitions representable through intuition, including pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A8","text":"The success of mathematics can generate the expectation that reason has no limit to the extension of a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A9","text":"Analysis of already possessed concepts produces formal clarification without adding new material content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A10","text":"Reason can add a priori concepts foreign to given concepts without recognizing how the addition has occurred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A11","text":"The absence of experiential opposition and evident contradiction permits speculative construction to continue without immediate correction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:A12","text":"The difference between analytic clarification and substantive a priori extension must be examined before metaphysical claims can be validated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:NX1","text":"Supersensible inquiry is not instructed or guided by experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:NX2","text":"Metaphysics is not critical at its outset because it assumes its task before examining reason’s ability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:NX3","text":"A priori cognition is not uniformly equivalent to mathematical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:NX4","text":"Formal novelty is not material enlargement of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:NX5","text":"Conceptual analysis is not the same as adding a new object or determination to knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:NX6","text":"The absence of contradiction is not a sufficient foundation for speculative cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:NX7","text":"Construction of an imposing system does not establish the solidity of its foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:NX8","text":"A fiction does not cease to be a fiction merely because it is constructed cautiously.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:D1","text":"Metaphysical dogmatism reverses the proper order of inquiry by attempting solutions before investigating the capacity that produces them.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A4","CH_B337_IPSN:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:D2","text":"The supersensible importance of a problem does not provide a warrant for the validity of its proposed solution.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A2","CH_B337_IPSN:A3","CH_B337_IPSN:A4","CH_B337_IPSN:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:D3","text":"Mathematical cognition functions simultaneously as evidence that a priori knowledge is possible and as a source of unjustified confidence in unlimited speculation.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A7","CH_B337_IPSN:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:D4","text":"A cognition can appear new in form while adding nothing new in content.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:D5","text":"Unrecognized foreign additions transform legitimate concept-analysis into unsupported a priori extension.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A9","CH_B337_IPSN:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:D6","text":"Freedom from experiential opposition removes a primary occasion for correction and thereby stabilizes speculative confidence.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A5","CH_B337_IPSN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:D7","text":"The preliminary problem of metaphysics is to distinguish valid a priori cognition from illegitimate extension rather than immediately to solve supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A6","CH_B337_IPSN:A9","CH_B337_IPSN:A10","CH_B337_IPSN:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:SD1","text":"A critical investigation of the possibility, principles, and extent of a priori cognition must precede any justified metaphysics.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A4","CH_B337_IPSN:A6","CH_B337_IPSN:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:SD2","text":"The principal speculative error occurs at the transition from analysis of given content to addition of foreign content, not merely at the endpoint of a false conclusion.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A9","CH_B337_IPSN:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:SD3","text":"Reason’s bounded successes generate a self-deceptive feedback loop in which valid a priori cognition authorizes invalid expansion beyond its conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A7","CH_B337_IPSN:A8","CH_B337_IPSN:A9","CH_B337_IPSN:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:SD4","text":"Reliable intellectual progress requires either resistance, support, or an equivalent warrant; the absence of such resistance cannot itself function as support.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A5","CH_B337_IPSN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:SD5","text":"Critique restricts dogmatic solutions without eliminating the supersensible problems that motivate metaphysics.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A2","CH_B337_IPSN:A3","CH_B337_IPSN:A4","CH_B337_IPSN:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:SD6","text":"The text diagnoses speculative error as an architectural failure in the order of construction: the edifice is completed before the legitimacy of its foundation is examined.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A4","CH_B337_IPSN:A8","CH_B337_IPSN:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:DNX1","text":"The domain of a priori cognition is not epistemically homogeneous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:DNX2","text":"Formal a priori status does not by itself establish objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:DNX3","text":"The decomposability of a concept does not entail the extension of knowledge beyond that concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:DNX4","text":"The power to analyze concepts does not authorize the production of foreign objects or determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:DNX5","text":"The absence of empirical resistance is not positive evidence for supersensible validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:DNX6","text":"The importance of a question is not a criterion of the truth of its answer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:DNX7","text":"Reason cannot determine its own unrestricted scope while remaining within the unexamined procedure that produced the claim of unrestricted scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:SNX1","text":"A self-grounding reason that could establish its supersensible reach without a prior criterion of validity is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:SNX2","text":"Formal novelty alone cannot yield substantive expansion of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:SNX3","text":"The absent experiential object, intuition, and resistance are not incidental omissions but the negative conditions that generate the need for critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:SNX4","text":"Neither the elevated importance of metaphysical questions nor the success of mathematics closes the gap between speculative aspiration and epistemic warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:SNX5","text":"The possibility that all a priori cognition can be extended by the same method is structurally foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:FGS1","text":"Supersensible aspiration + concepts without experiential objects → inquiry exceeds the jurisdiction of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:FGS2","text":"Loss of experiential guidance + elevated importance of the problems → metaphysics assumes its task dogmatically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:FGS3","text":"Successful mathematical a priori cognition → expectation of unrestricted rational extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:FGS4","text":"Analysis of given concepts → formal novelty without material addition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:FGS5","text":"Formal novelty mistaken for material increase → foreign a priori concepts enter unnoticed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:FGS6","text":"Absence of experiential opposition or evident contradiction → speculative construction proceeds without foundation test.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:FGS7","text":"Detection of the analytic/synthetic confusion → investigation of the possibility, principles, extent, and validity of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:AF1","text":"Supersensible aspiration → abandonment of experiential ground → dogmatic metaphysical construction → mathematical analogy → confusion of formal clarification with material extension → covert addition of foreign concepts → demand for critical examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:AF2","text":"Bounded a priori success → expectation of unlimited reason → absence of resistance → delayed foundation-check → exposure of speculative overreach.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:ME1","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from an immediate science of supersensible objects into an inquiry whose own possibility must first be established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:ME2","text":"A priori cognition is divided into analytic clarification and substantive extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:ME3","text":"Mathematical success is demoted from universal warrant for speculation to a bounded demonstration conditioned by intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:ME4","text":"The absence of contradiction is reclassified from implicit permission to continue into an insufficient basis requiring further examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:ME5","text":"Speculative error is relocated from defective conclusions to the concealed introduction of foreign content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:ME6","text":"The temporal order of philosophical construction is reversed: examination of the foundation must precede construction of the edifice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:ALT1","text":"Surface — Reason pursues God, freedom, and immortality beyond the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:ALT2","text":"Structural — The argument analyzes the relations among experience, intuition, concepts, a priori cognition, conceptual analysis, and speculative addition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Philosophical inquiry must suspend premature construction and first determine the jurisdiction and capacity of its cognitive instruments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text presupposes that cognition has conditions of objective reach and depicts speculative reason as temporally reversing the proper order by building before grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the passage’s movement from supersensible ambition to the need for critical examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:MRT2","text":"The strongest reconstruction point is the distinction between valid concept-analysis and unnoticed addition of foreign a priori content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent as a diagnosis of how legitimate a priori success produces illegitimate speculative confidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:MRT4","text":"The system remains underdetermined regarding the exact criteria by which valid synthetic a priori cognition will later be distinguished from invalid extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:MRT5","text":"The central unresolved tension is that metaphysical problems retain exceptional importance even though experience supplies neither objects nor guidance for their solution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:MRT6","text":"Mathematics operates ambivalently as both proof of a priori cognitive power and the model through which reason is deceived about its limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IPSN:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real argumentative movement, but the passage ends at the threshold of the promised distinction rather than supplying the completed critical doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IPSN:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 03","title":"III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”","context":"Chapter 03"}},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI","layer":"CH","title":"I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe","order":39,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"e684930906962631961d15640b0b62f60f7262229d977d65a61b62dbd73d03c1","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AS1","text":"The cosmological problem concerns whether the world, considered in space and past time, can be assigned a completed finite or infinite magnitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AS2","text":"The world is redefined from a whole allegedly given in intuition into the possible empirical regress through which its conditioned magnitude is represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AS3","text":"An absolute cosmical limit is excluded because it would require experience of a phenomenon bounded by empty time or empty space, and such experience has no content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AS4","text":"The absence of an empirically encounterable absolute boundary obliges reason to seek a further condition for every condition reached in regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AS5","text":"The regress cannot establish either a determinate finite whole or a determinate actually infinite whole, because the cosmical quantity is not given before regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AS6","text":"The argument is transcendental-regulative: it replaces an ontic verdict about the world’s completed quantity with a rule of indefinitely extendable empirical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:A1","text":"The world of sense is a field of phenomena and is not given as a total object in a single intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:A2","text":"The cosmical series has no content beyond the possible empirical regress through conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:A3","text":"Every condition reached in empirical regress must be regarded as itself empirically conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:A4","text":"No experience can present an absolutely unconditioned condition or an absolute limit of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:A5","text":"An absolute cosmical boundary would have to be represented as phenomena limited by mere void in time or space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:A6","text":"The quantity of the world can be determined only in and through empirical regress, not before it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:A7","text":"A determinate infinite regress would presuppose a completed infinite cosmical quantity and would anticipate unreached members of the series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:A8","text":"The regulative rule requires that no absolute limit be admitted and that every phenomenon be subordinated to a further condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:A9","text":"Regressus in indefinitum prescribes unbounded continuation without determining the quantity of the object as an actual infinity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:NX1","text":"The world is not an intuitively given totality from which the extent of regress can be inferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:NX2","text":"The world is not a thing in itself whose total spatial or temporal position can be dogmatically determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:NX3","text":"An absolute beginning in time is not empirically admissible as a boundary of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:NX4","text":"An absolute spatial limit is not empirically admissible as a boundary of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:NX5","text":"The regress is not regressus in infinitum if that expression means a determinate infinite series already given as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:NX6","text":"The world cannot be classified as either finite or infinite in itself as an object of possible sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:NX7","text":"The regulative rule does not prohibit local empirical beginnings, local limiting conditions, or finite subseries within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:D1","text":"Each attained condition generates the task of seeking a further condition, whether or not that further condition can actually be encountered.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A3","CH_B337_ISCI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:D2","text":"The magnitude of the empirical regress cannot be inferred from an independently known magnitude of the world.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A1","CH_B337_ISCI:A2","CH_B337_ISCI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:D3","text":"Neither finitude nor actual infinitude can be asserted of the world of sense as a completed cosmical quantity.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A1","CH_B337_ISCI:A6","CH_B337_ISCI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:D4","text":"The world has no empirically valid absolute beginning in time and no empirically valid absolute limit in space.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A4","CH_B337_ISCI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:D5","text":"The sole positive determination available to reason is an indefinite regress through phenomena rather than an infinite quantity of phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A6","CH_B337_ISCI:A7","CH_B337_ISCI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:D6","text":"The discovery of a local empirical origin or outermost accessible object does not terminate the universal rule of further conditioning.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A3","CH_B337_ISCI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:SD1","text":"Cosmological totality becomes a rule-governed task of synthesis rather than an object possessing a preassigned global magnitude.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A1","CH_B337_ISCI:A2","CH_B337_ISCI:A6","CH_B337_ISCI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:SD2","text":"Finitude and actual infinitude become parallel forms of illicit totalization because each converts the unfinished conditions of experience into a completed cosmical object.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A1","CH_B337_ISCI:A6","CH_B337_ISCI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:SD3","text":"Reason necessarily demands systematic continuation while being forbidden to convert that demand into constitutive knowledge of a completed world.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A3","CH_B337_ISCI:A7","CH_B337_ISCI:A8","CH_B337_ISCI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:SD4","text":"Cosmical magnitude exists for empirical cognition only as a progressive determination whose completion cannot be represented within experience.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A2","CH_B337_ISCI:A6","CH_B337_ISCI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:DNX1","text":"No standpoint outside empirical regress can disclose the world as a completed spatial-temporal whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:DNX2","text":"No phenomenal boundary can be given without a possible perception of the void that would bound phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:DNX3","text":"No empirically accessible member of the cosmical series can function as an absolutely unconditioned terminus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:DNX4","text":"No determinate cosmical quantity can precede the synthesis by which that quantity is cognized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:DNX5","text":"No transition is licensed from the necessity of continuing inquiry to the reality of an actually completed infinite series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:SNX1","text":"The system excludes any possible experience in which the universe appears together with its own final boundary or total measure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a final empirical synthesis that could satisfy reason’s demand for conditions by furnishing an unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:SNX3","text":"The system excludes cosmological knowledge of the world in itself while retaining the necessity of regulating inquiry into appearances as if further conditions must always be sought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:FGS1","text":"Phenomenal world without total intuition → cosmical totality is reduced to possible empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:FGS2","text":"Possible empirical regress + every reached condition is conditioned → reason must seek a further condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:FGS3","text":"Absolute limit → representation of phenomena bounded by void → impossible experience → exclusion of absolute beginning and spatial boundary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:FGS4","text":"Quantity determined only through regress + no completed total given → rejection of both finite totality and actual infinite totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:FGS5","text":"Rejection of completed totality + compulsory continuation of conditions → regressus in indefinitum as the sole legitimate cosmological determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AF1","text":"The demand to classify the world as finite or infinite confronts the absence of an intuition of the world as a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AF2","text":"The absence of total intuition redirects the inquiry from cosmical magnitude to the rule governing empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AF3","text":"The impossibility of experiencing a void-bounded phenomenon removes every absolute temporal or spatial terminus from possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AF4","text":"The removal of absolute termini requires continuation from each given condition to a further condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AF5","text":"The continuing rule cannot determine a completed quantity and therefore cannot establish actual infinitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:AF6","text":"The final position permits only an indefinite regress that regulates empirical synthesis without assigning the world a completed magnitude.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:ME1","text":"The world is reclassified from a possible object of global measurement into the horizon generated by conditioned empirical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:ME2","text":"The opposition between a finite universe and an actually infinite universe is displaced by the distinction between determinate infinity and regulative indefiniteness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:ME3","text":"A claim that the world has no absolute beginning or boundary is repositioned as a condition of possible empirical regress rather than as a constitutive description of a completed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:ME4","text":"Local empirical beginnings and limits cease to function as evidence for a globally bounded cosmos.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:ME5","text":"Reason’s demand for totality is preserved as an operational imperative while its fulfillment as an object of knowledge is blocked.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text resolves whether the world has a beginning in time, a limit in space, or an infinite extent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text grounds cosmological judgment in the relation between conditions, regress, possible experience, and the impossibility of a void-bounded phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text converts reason from a source of constitutive cosmological assertions into a regulator of empirically extendable inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text distinguishes phenomena in space and time from a world treated as a thing in itself, thereby withholding world-totality from phenomenal knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central transition from cosmological totality as object to cosmological regress as rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction depends principally on the claim that an absolute boundary would require an impossible experience of void.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:MRT3","text":"The system presupposes rather than independently establishes that every meaningful cosmological boundary must be phenomenally experienceable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent if “the world has no beginning or absolute limit” is read as a regulative exclusion of empirical termini rather than as a constitutive claim about a completed world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:MRT5","text":"A structural tension remains because the text first disallows assertions about the whole object of experience and then formulates a negative answer about the world’s temporal and spatial limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:MRT6","text":"The argument leaves the world in itself underdetermined because its conclusions concern only the conditions and limits of empirical representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ISCI:MRT7","text":"The extracted sequence preserves the real movement of the argument: unavailable totality, impossible boundary, compulsory continuation, and indefinite regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ISCI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 70","title":"I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe","context":"Chapter 70"}},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2","layer":"CH","title":"II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition","order":40,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"13651b66fe1125cb015ab17db604533331e1d264cd3aac7ed88ace6f93cca98a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:AS1","text":"The division of an intuitively given whole raises the question whether its conditioned parts can yield a completed unconditioned totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:AS2","text":"Infinite divisibility is reframed as an infinite regress of decomposition internal to a given continuum, not as an actually given infinite aggregate of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:AS3","text":"Phenomenal substance is stripped of the status of an absolute subject, so bodily divisibility does not threaten a thing-in-itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:AS4","text":"The distinction between continuous and discrete quantities blocks the transfer of infinite divisibility from spatial continua to organized wholes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:AS5","text":"Mathematical antinomies fail because they seek completed unconditioned totality within homogeneous phenomenal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:AS6","text":"Dynamical antinomies permit a non-phenomenal intelligible condition, allowing empirical regress and rational demand for the unconditioned to coexist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A1","text":"Dividing an intuitively given whole is a regress from a conditioned whole to its conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A2","text":"In a continuous whole, every attained part remains divisible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A3","text":"The parts attainable through division are co-given with the whole, but the completed series of division is not co-given with it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A4","text":"An infinite regress of division does not constitute an actually infinite number of parts assembled into a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A5","text":"The divisibility of a body follows from the infinite divisibility of the space in which it appears.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A6","text":"Phenomenal substance is a permanent sensuous intuition rather than an absolute subject or thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A7","text":"Infinite divisibility belongs to a quantum continuum, whereas a quantum discretum has a determinate numerical plurality of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A8","text":"An organized whole conceived as already containing infinitely many determinate organized parts is self-contradictory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A9","text":"Experience can establish only how far organization has been encountered, not the absolutely final extent of transcendental division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A10","text":"Mathematical cosmological ideas concern homogeneous sensuous conditions that must remain members of the phenomenal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A11","text":"Dynamical cosmological ideas can admit an intelligible condition that is not a member of the phenomenal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:A12","text":"A completed unconditioned phenomenal totality is impossible in mathematical series, while a non-sensuous unconditioned condition can satisfy dynamical reason without terminating empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:NX1","text":"Infinite divisibility is not a regressus in indefinitum, because the relevant conditions are co-given in the intuition of the whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:NX2","text":"A divisible continuum is not an infinite aggregate of already completed parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:NX3","text":"A body as phenomenon is not an absolutely subsisting substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:NX4","text":"The annihilation of spatial composition in thought does not disclose an unconditioned phenomenal substrate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:NX5","text":"An organized body is not necessarily organized through every possible level of division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:NX6","text":"A quantum discretum is not infinitely divisible in the same sense as a spatial continuum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:NX7","text":"Mathematical antinomies are not resolvable by affirming either contradictory assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:NX8","text":"An intelligible dynamical condition is not an interruption, replacement, or empirical member of the phenomenal causal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:D1","text":"The infinity of spatial division signifies the inexhaustibility of decomposition rather than the completed presence of infinitely many spatial units.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A2","CH_B337_IS_2:A3","CH_B337_IS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:D2","text":"A body can be divisible without end without being constituted by an actual infinite multitude of parts.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A4","CH_B337_IS_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:D3","text":"The persistence attributed to phenomenal substance cannot function as proof of a simple, indivisible, or unconditioned substrate.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A5","CH_B337_IS_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:D4","text":"Infinite division cannot demonstrate an infinitely pre-articulated organism, because the divided parts become determinate only through the regress that reaches them.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A3","CH_B337_IS_2:A7","CH_B337_IS_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:D5","text":"The empirical investigation of organization remains indefinitely extendable, while reason prohibits treating its regress as absolutely complete.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A2","CH_B337_IS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:D6","text":"The mathematical demand for an unconditioned member of a phenomenal series converts a conditioned member into a falsely supposed first member.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A10","CH_B337_IS_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:D7","text":"Dynamical reason can posit an intelligible ground without converting that ground into an empirical cause among appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A11","CH_B337_IS_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:SD1","text":"The system separates the givenness of a whole from the completion of the analysis of that whole, thereby preventing intuition from licensing an actual infinite totality.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A1","CH_B337_IS_2:A3","CH_B337_IS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:SD2","text":"The status of the unconditioned varies with the type of synthesis, so a single cosmological solution cannot govern both mathematical and dynamical antinomies.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A10","CH_B337_IS_2:A11","CH_B337_IS_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:SD3","text":"Phenomenal analysis is structurally open-ended because its objects are divisible appearances rather than self-grounding substances.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A2","CH_B337_IS_2:A5","CH_B337_IS_2:A6","CH_B337_IS_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:SD4","text":"Rational closure is denied within spatial magnitude but conditionally restored beyond phenomenal membership in dynamical relations.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A7","CH_B337_IS_2:A10","CH_B337_IS_2:A11","CH_B337_IS_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:DNX1","text":"No phenomenal whole can contain, as a completed object, the infinite series of operations required to determine all of its divisible parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:DNX2","text":"No category of substance can establish an unconditioned entity when applied only to phenomenal intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:DNX3","text":"No empirical regress can be transformed into a completed metaphysical inventory merely because its object is given as a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:DNX4","text":"No homogeneous phenomenal series can contain a condition that is both outside the series and still a phenomenal member of it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:DNX5","text":"No inference from the limits of observation can determine either an ultimate inorganic part or an infinitely organized totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses a mathematically unconditioned object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses a universal form of rational satisfaction that would resolve every antinomy by the same procedure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:SNX3","text":"The system withholds direct theoretical access to the intelligible condition that it permits as necessary for dynamical resolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:SNX4","text":"The system excludes the possibility that phenomenal reality could provide its own final ground while remaining wholly within empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:FGS1","text":"Given intuitive whole → division into conditioned parts → regress of decomposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:FGS2","text":"Continuous divisibility plus non-completion of regress → inexhaustible division without an actual infinite aggregate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:FGS3","text":"Spatial appearance plus phenomenal substance → bodily divisibility without disclosure of an absolute substrate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:FGS4","text":"Continuum/discretum distinction → rejection of infinitely pre-given organized parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:FGS5","text":"Homogeneous phenomenal series → impossibility of mathematical unconditioned totality → falsity of opposed mathematical theses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:FGS6","text":"Heterogeneous intelligible condition → intact empirical series plus admissible dynamical unconditioned → qualified truth of opposed dynamical positions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:AF1","text":"Given whole in intuition → divisional regress → distinction between infinite division and an infinite number of parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:AF2","text":"Spatial continuum → phenomenal body → denial of absolute substance → restriction on claims about organized divisibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:AF3","text":"Mathematical homogeneity → mutual falsity of antinomial claims → dynamical heterogeneity → possible reconciliation through an intelligible condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:ME1","text":"Infinity is reclassified from a completed numerical property of an object into the rule-governed non-termination of a regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:ME2","text":"Bodily substance is reclassified from an ontological remainder beneath composition into a permanent phenomenal determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:ME3","text":"Infinite divisibility and infinite organization are separated by the distinction between continuous extension and determinate discrete plurality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:ME4","text":"Antinomies are reclassified by synthesis-type rather than treated as instances of a single conflict between thesis and antithesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:ME5","text":"The unconditioned is redistributed from an impossible phenomenal totality to a conditionally admissible intelligible ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines what follows from dividing limited spaces, bodies, and organized wholes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text distinguishes a given aggregate of appearances from the regress that determines its parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text replaces direct adjudication between antinomial positions with diagnosis of the kind of synthesis each position presupposes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text treats phenomenal determination as successively inexhaustible while denying that succession yields an actually completed infinite whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:ALT5","text":"Transcendental — The text allows intelligible conditioning only where it can stand outside, rather than compete within, the empirical series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the division argument if the distinction between a given whole and a completed divisional series is retained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:MRT2","text":"The axioms reconstruct the transition from bodily divisibility to the denial of phenomenal substantiality only through the restriction of categories to appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:MRT3","text":"The system is coherent in treating mathematical infinity as a property of regress rather than a completed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it prevents the equivocation between indefinitely repeatable division and infinitely many determinate parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:MRT5","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the positive content and knowability of the intelligible condition admitted in dynamical cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_2:MRT6","text":"The sharp contrast between false mathematical oppositions and potentially true dynamical oppositions depends on preserving the disputed distinction between homogeneous and heterogeneous conditioning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_2:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 71","title":"II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition","context":"Chapter 71"}},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3","layer":"CH","title":"III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes","order":41,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"c85744e64259f9ac524a445b222ecad908a85fbc5fa3e033216f5574ef87cf99","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:AS1","text":"The central problem is whether universal natural necessity and transcendental freedom can determine the same action without contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:AS2","text":"Phenomenal causality produces an indefinitely conditioned series incapable of supplying the absolute beginning sought by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:AS3","text":"The apparent antinomy arises when phenomena are treated as things in themselves and both causalities are forced into one homogeneous series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:AS4","text":"The phenomenon–thing-in-itself distinction permits one causal subject to possess empirical and intelligible characters.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:AS5","text":"The same action can thereby be naturally necessary relative to its empirical conditions and free relative to its intelligible ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:AS6","text":"Practical obligation represents reason as causally efficacious even though every observable action remains empirically determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:AS7","text":"The argument moves by transcendental diagnosis and compatibility construction from causal regress to ontological reframing and finally to a delimited proof of non-opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A1","text":"Nature and freedom are the only two cogitable modes of causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A2","text":"Natural causality connects every phenomenal event, including causal action, to a preceding phenomenal state under temporal law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A3","text":"Universal natural causality is a condition of possible experience and admits no exception within the phenomenal domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A4","text":"No phenomenal cause can originate an absolutely first event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A5","text":"Reason demands an absolute totality of conditions for a causal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A6","text":"No empirical causal series can supply the absolute totality demanded by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A7","text":"Cosmological freedom is the spontaneous origination of a causal series without determination by a preceding cause in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A8","text":"Practical freedom is the independence of the will from necessitation by sensuous impulses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A9","text":"Practical freedom depends on the thinkability of transcendental freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A10","text":"If phenomena are things in themselves, natural causality exhausts every event and freedom is impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A11","text":"Phenomena are representations grounded in something nonphenomenal and intelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A12","text":"Every effective cause possesses a character, or rule governing its causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A13","text":"A single sensuous subject can be conceived under both an empirical character and an intelligible character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A14","text":"Empirical character places every action qua appearance under complete natural determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A15","text":"Intelligible character is not phenomenal, temporal, or subject to natural necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A16","text":"The practical ought expresses necessity from a pure rational ground and presupposes reason’s causality toward possible action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A17","text":"Only phenomenal effects and empirical character are knowable; intelligible character is merely thinkable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:A18","text":"The transcendental argument establishes only the non-contradiction of nature and freedom, not freedom’s actuality or real possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX1","text":"Cosmological freedom is not an empirical object or occurrence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX2","text":"Practical freedom is not freedom from all sensuous affection, but freedom from sensuous necessitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX3","text":"Free causality is not an exception to natural law within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX4","text":"A free effect does not originate itself within the phenomenal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX5","text":"Nature and freedom are not mutually exclusive when predicated of the same action under different relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX6","text":"Intelligible causality is not a temporally first event.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX7","text":"The ought is not a description of what occurs under natural law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX8","text":"The problem of freedom is not fundamentally physiological or psychological.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX9","text":"Intelligible character is not available to observation or direct cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX10","text":"The intelligible cause is not an additional empirical cause cooperating alongside natural causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX11","text":"Phenomena are not absolute things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:NX12","text":"The compatibility argument is not a proof that freedom exists or is positively possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:D1","text":"Every action considered as an appearance is necessarily connected with preceding appearances and remains fully available to physical explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A2","CH_B337_IS_3:A3","CH_B337_IS_3:A4","CH_B337_IS_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:D2","text":"Reason generates the idea of spontaneous causality because empirical causal regress cannot satisfy its demand for an unconditioned condition.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A5","CH_B337_IS_3:A6","CH_B337_IS_3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:D3","text":"If phenomenal causality were the only causality, sensuous conditions would necessitate the will and practical freedom would disappear.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A2","CH_B337_IS_3:A8","CH_B337_IS_3:A9","CH_B337_IS_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:D4","text":"The nature–freedom antinomy results from placing condition and conditioned within one series treated as absolutely real.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A3","CH_B337_IS_3:A7","CH_B337_IS_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:D5","text":"Treating appearances as representations permits an intelligible ground outside the empirical series while retaining its effects within that series.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A11","CH_B337_IS_3:A13","CH_B337_IS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:D6","text":"One action can be necessary relative to empirical character and free relative to intelligible character.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A13","CH_B337_IS_3:A14","CH_B337_IS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:D7","text":"Intelligible causality introduces no breach, gap, or absolute first event into the empirical sequence.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A3","CH_B337_IS_3:A4","CH_B337_IS_3:A14","CH_B337_IS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:D8","text":"Obligation requires reason to be represented as capable of determining conduct independently of the motives supplied by nature.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A8","CH_B337_IS_3:A9","CH_B337_IS_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:D9","text":"Complete empirical predictability of an action would establish natural necessity but would not disprove its intelligible freedom.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A13","CH_B337_IS_3:A14","CH_B337_IS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:D10","text":"Experience cannot prove freedom because experience discloses only the empirical character governed by natural law.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A3","CH_B337_IS_3:A14","CH_B337_IS_3:A17","CH_B337_IS_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:D11","text":"The respective contributions of freedom, natural constitution, and circumstance to moral conduct cannot be determined with certainty.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A14","CH_B337_IS_3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SD1","text":"The antinomy is dissolved not by weakening natural necessity or proving freedom, but by denying that both causal predicates apply under the same relation.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A3","CH_B337_IS_3:A10","CH_B337_IS_3:A11","CH_B337_IS_3:A13","CH_B337_IS_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SD2","text":"The system combines empirical causal closure with the thinkability of an empirically unconditioned ground.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A3","CH_B337_IS_3:A11","CH_B337_IS_3:A14","CH_B337_IS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SD3","text":"Freedom is relocated from a first event inside time to the atemporal grounding of a series whose every member remains temporally conditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A4","CH_B337_IS_3:A7","CH_B337_IS_3:A14","CH_B337_IS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SD4","text":"Practical judgment supplies a regulative representation of reason as causal without converting that representation into theoretical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A16","CH_B337_IS_3:A17","CH_B337_IS_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SD5","text":"The same action can be both fully explained and fully imputed without dividing causal responsibility into empirical and intelligible shares.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A13","CH_B337_IS_3:A14","CH_B337_IS_3:A15","CH_B337_IS_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SD6","text":"Empirical character can function as the sensible schema of intelligible character, but it cannot disclose why that intelligible character produces this particular conduct.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A13","CH_B337_IS_3:A14","CH_B337_IS_3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SD7","text":"Nature possesses constitutive validity for experience, whereas freedom retains only problematic and practical thinkability.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A3","CH_B337_IS_3:A9","CH_B337_IS_3:A16","CH_B337_IS_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:DNX1","text":"Empirical causal completeness must not entail metaphysical completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:DNX2","text":"Temporal succession must not govern things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:DNX3","text":"The empirical appearance of a subject must not exhaust the subject’s possible mode of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:DNX4","text":"Distinct causal descriptions of one action must not necessarily constitute competing causes within one explanatory order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:DNX5","text":"Normative necessity must not be reducible to sensuous impulse or empirical regularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:DNX6","text":"Legitimate thought must not be restricted to objects that can be empirically cognized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:DNX7","text":"Establishing non-contradiction must not require positive cognition of the mechanism connecting intelligible ground and empirical effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SNX1","text":"The intelligible character’s positive nature remains absent from the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SNX2","text":"No empirical criterion is available for identifying an action as actually produced by freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SNX3","text":"No absolute beginning is admissible anywhere within temporal experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SNX4","text":"Perfect knowledge of moral merit, demerit, and the free contribution to conduct is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SNX5","text":"The mediation by which an atemporal intelligible cause grounds a determinate temporal series remains unformalized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:SNX6","text":"A positive metaphysical settlement between determinism and actual freedom is withheld; only their necessary opposition is denied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:FGS1","text":"The natural-causal mechanism runs: phenomenal event → preceding phenomenal cause → further preceding cause → unbroken series without absolute beginning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:FGS2","text":"The rational-totalizing mechanism runs: indefinite conditioning → demand for an unconditioned condition → idea of spontaneous causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:FGS3","text":"The antinomy mechanism runs: phenomena treated as things in themselves → one homogeneous causal series → exclusion of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:FGS4","text":"The reframing mechanism runs: appearance as representation → nonphenomenal ground → empirical and intelligible characters of one subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:FGS5","text":"The compatibility mechanism runs: empirical character → naturally necessary effect; intelligible character → non-temporal origination of the same effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:FGS6","text":"The noninterference constraint runs: intelligible ground → no empirical exception → uninterrupted physical explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:FGS7","text":"The practical application runs: ought → represented causal power of reason → imputation despite empirical determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:FGS8","text":"The critical closure runs: unknowable intelligible cause → no proof of actuality or real possibility → proof only of non-opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:AF1","text":"The first movement is natural causal regress → absence of empirical totality → rational demand for spontaneity → apparent nature–freedom antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:AF2","text":"The decisive movement is absolute phenomenal reality → diagnosis of false homogenization → phenomenon–intelligible distinction → dual character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:AF3","text":"The concluding movement is empirical determination → practical ought and imputation → epistemic limitation → compatibility without existential proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ME1","text":"The exclusive opposition between nature and freedom is replaced by relation-indexed coexistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ME2","text":"Freedom is reclassified from a temporal first cause to an atemporal condition of temporal effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ME3","text":"Empirical causal closure is separated from metaphysical exhaustion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ME4","text":"A single action becomes legible under both explanatory necessity and practical imputation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ME5","text":"The ought is reclassified as evidence for the required representation of rational causality rather than as a natural predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ME6","text":"The cosmological antinomy is reclassified as an effect of confusing appearances with things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ME7","text":"The target of proof shifts from the existence or positive possibility of freedom to the removal of a contradiction blocking its thinkability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ALT1","text":"Surface: The argument asks whether natural necessity and freedom can coexist in one action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ALT2","text":"Structural: The empirical and intelligible characters distribute causality across distinct relations while preserving one subject and one effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The argument separates the jurisdiction of empirical explanation from that of practical imputation so neither must invalidate the other.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Time and causal succession govern appearances, while the intelligible ground is conceived as neither temporal nor changing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:ALT5","text":"Epistemic: Nature is knowable through experience, whereas freedom and intelligible character remain thinkable but uncognizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:MRT1","text":"The axiom set is sufficient to reconstruct the causal regress, the antinomy, the dual-character solution, the practical application, and the final limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:MRT2","text":"The nature of the transcendental ground, its determination of empirical character, and its production of particular actions remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent only if the appearance–thing-in-itself distinction and the restriction of time to appearances are granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:MRT4","text":"The argument is strongest where it preserves exceptionless empirical causality while showing that this law governs only the phenomenal relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:MRT5","text":"The claim that an atemporal cause originates temporal effects without itself beginning is declared thinkable but not positively explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:MRT6","text":"Relational indexing prevents formal causal competition, but it does not explain how intelligible grounding relates to an empirically sufficient causal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:MRT7","text":"The inference from obligation to represented rational causality supports practical imputation but cannot establish theoretical knowledge of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:MRT8","text":"The assertion of culpability coexists tensely with the admission that the actual free contribution and true moral worth of conduct are unknowable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:MRT9","text":"As transmitted, the sentence calling transcendental idealism an “illusion” conflicts with the chapter’s reliance on the idealist distinction and remains textually unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_3:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if its conclusion remains negative and critical: nature and freedom are not shown to exist together, but they are shown not to be necessarily incompatible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_3:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 72","title":"III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes","context":"Chapter 72"}},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4","layer":"CH","title":"IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences","order":42,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"11ac3359cf70bb65c923685b4a03236bd6f89c406c07217c57e84e6204ca5611","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AS1","text":"The problem is whether the universal contingency of phenomenal existence excludes an absolutely necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AS2","text":"The apparent contradiction is reframed by distinguishing an empirical series of conditions from a possible intelligible ground of that series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AS3","text":"A dynamical regress permits a condition that is not a member of the conditioned empirical series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AS4","text":"If an unconditioned existence is thinkable, it must be wholly intelligible and extramundane rather than a highest phenomenal member.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AS5","text":"The solution establishes compatibility between phenomenal contingency and an intelligible necessary ground without establishing that such a ground exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AS6","text":"The argument terminates by separating the regulative empirical use of reason from the transcendent attempt to constitute knowable objects beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:A1","text":"All objects of the sensuous world are phenomena, and phenomena are sensuously conditioned representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:A2","text":"Every phenomenal existence is contingent and empirically conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:A3","text":"Every condition sought within the sensuous world must itself be sought through further empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:A4","text":"A mathematical regress requires condition and conditioned to be homogeneous members of one series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:A5","text":"A dynamical regress does not require the condition to be a member of the empirical series containing the conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:A6","text":"A necessary being located within the phenomenal series would be subject to contingency and dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:A7","text":"An unconditioned condition, if conceived as possible, must be purely intelligible and entirely outside the world of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:A8","text":"The empirical use of reason is regulative: it requires continued search for empirical conditions without licensing a final empirical unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:A9","text":"No determinate cognition of an intelligible object is possible through predicates derived from empirical concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:A10","text":"The non-derivability of an intelligible necessary being from experience does not establish its impossibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:A11","text":"Cosmological ideas become transcendent when they treat the unconditioned beyond possible experience as an objectively determinable object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:NX1","text":"A necessary being is not a phenomenal object or the highest member of the empirical causal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:NX2","text":"Phenomenal contingency is not a proof that an intelligible necessary ground is impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:NX3","text":"The empirical regress is not authorized to terminate at an unconditioned empirical cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:NX4","text":"An intelligible condition is not available as an empirical explanation of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:NX5","text":"The intelligible ground is not knowable through determinate predicates concerning its internal nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:NX6","text":"The possibility of the intelligible is not equivalent to an assertion of its existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:NX7","text":"The transcendental object is not an object whose objective reality can be supplied by completion of empirical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:D1","text":"The contingency of every phenomenal member remains intact even if the whole phenomenal series has a non-empirical intelligible ground.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A2","CH_B337_IS_4:A5","CH_B337_IS_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:D2","text":"The fourth antinomy is dissolved as a conflict produced by treating an intelligible condition as though it had to occupy the same series as phenomenal conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A2","CH_B337_IS_4:A5","CH_B337_IS_4:A6","CH_B337_IS_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:D3","text":"Empirical reason must continue indefinitely through conditioned phenomena even when pure reason thinks an intelligible ground beyond that regress.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A3","CH_B337_IS_4:A7","CH_B337_IS_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:D4","text":"An absolutely necessary being cannot be inferred from empirical contingency, because contingency pertains only to the phenomenal mode of representation.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A1","CH_B337_IS_4:A2","CH_B337_IS_4:A9","CH_B337_IS_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:D5","text":"The necessary being can function only as an indeterminate intelligible condition, not as a positively known cause with empirical predicates.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A7","CH_B337_IS_4:A9","CH_B337_IS_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:D6","text":"The empirical and transcendental employments of reason can proceed without mutual contradiction because they govern different relations to the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A8","CH_B337_IS_4:A10","CH_B337_IS_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:SD1","text":"The text converts the question of necessary existence from an ontic question answerable by empirical regress into a modal question concerning the compatibility of heterogeneous orders.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A2","CH_B337_IS_4:A5","CH_B337_IS_4:A7","CH_B337_IS_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:SD2","text":"The unconditioned is preserved as a limiting demand of reason only by being removed from the field in which reason could either empirically discover or empirically refute it.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A3","CH_B337_IS_4:A7","CH_B337_IS_4:A8","CH_B337_IS_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:SD3","text":"The system preserves empirical determinism at the phenomenal level by locating any possible absolute necessity outside phenomenal causality.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A2","CH_B337_IS_4:A6","CH_B337_IS_4:A7","CH_B337_IS_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:SD4","text":"The transition toward a doctrine of intelligible objects begins from the necessity of thinking an unconditioned while simultaneously withholding knowledge of whatever is thereby thought.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A7","CH_B337_IS_4:A9","CH_B337_IS_4:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:DNX1","text":"No empirical totality can contain its own absolutely sufficient ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:DNX2","text":"No inference from the conditions of representation to the determinate constitution of things in themselves is valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:DNX3","text":"No positive metaphysical knowledge follows merely from the rational need to think the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:DNX4","text":"Neither empirical naturalism nor transcendent metaphysics is entitled to convert its own methodological limit into a verdict on the possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:DNX5","text":"The relation between an intelligible ground and phenomenal appearances cannot be represented as a relation internal to empirical causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a fully knowable absolute that could both ground phenomena and enter into phenomenal explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:SNX2","text":"The system excludes any final standpoint from which the totality of conditions could be completed as an experienced object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:SNX3","text":"The unconditioned is admissible only as what cannot be phenomenally present, empirically reached, or determinately possessed in cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:FGS1","text":"Phenomenal representation → universal empirical contingency → demand for conditions → indefinite empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:FGS2","text":"Demand for an unconditioned → distinction between mathematical and dynamical regress → possibility of a non-series condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:FGS3","text":"Non-series condition → exclusion from phenomenal existence → purely intelligible and extramundane status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:FGS4","text":"Pure intelligibility → absence of empirical predicates and proof → modal compatibility without ontological assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:FGS5","text":"Compatibility without cognition → preservation of empirical regulation and restriction of transcendent construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AF1","text":"Universal phenomenal contingency → apparent exclusion of necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AF2","text":"Apparent exclusion → distinction between dynamical and mathematical conditioning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AF3","text":"Dynamical distinction → admissibility of an intelligible condition outside the empirical series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AF4","text":"Intelligible condition → reconciliation of empirical contingency with possible absolute necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AF5","text":"Reconciliation → refusal both to prove the necessary being and to disprove it empirically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:AF6","text":"Epistemic refusal → transition from cosmological regulation to the problem of transcendent intelligible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:ME1","text":"The opposition between universal phenomenal contingency and possible necessary existence is reclassified from contradiction to difference of relation and domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:ME2","text":"The empirical regress is detached from the metaphysical demand for an unconditioned, so that infinite empirical conditioning no longer decides the status of intelligible necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:ME3","text":"The necessary being is reclassified from a candidate explanatory object within nature into an indeterminate limiting ground outside empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:ME4","text":"Empirical natural inquiry and pure rational speculation are assigned distinct jurisdictions, neither of which can conclusively abolish the other’s possible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:ME5","text":"The fourth antinomy becomes the threshold at which cosmological ideas cease to organize possible experience and become transcendent object-constructions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text addresses whether a necessary being can coexist with the contingency of all sensuous existences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:ALT2","text":"Structural — The resolution depends on separating conditions internal to an empirical series from a condition intelligible as external to that series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Reason is restricted from both illegitimately transcending empirical conditions and dogmatically denying what empirical conditions cannot present.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Phenomena form an indefinitely conditioned order, whereas absolute necessity can be thought only as non-temporal and non-phenomenal intelligibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — The possibility of an intelligible ground is retained as thinkable while its existence and determinate nature remain unavailable to knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central solution if the issue is limited to compatibility rather than proof of necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:MRT2","text":"The decisive hinge is the non-homology of dynamical conditioning, because without it an intelligible condition would be forced back into phenomenal dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent insofar as it distinguishes empirical regulation from transcendental possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:MRT4","text":"The structure remains underdetermined concerning how an intelligible ground can be called a ground without entering a knowable causal relation to phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:MRT5","text":"The system relies on a modal asymmetry: empirical contingency cannot prove intelligible impossibility, but intelligible possibility cannot prove existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:MRT6","text":"The strongest compression occurs in the shift from the rational demand for the unconditioned to permission to think an intelligible condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_IS_4:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if its final transition toward transcendent ideas is retained as a new problem rather than treated as a completed metaphysical result.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_IS_4:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 73","title":"IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences","context":"Chapter 73"}},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM","layer":"CH","title":"II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method","order":43,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"62f835c937d29538cfadbdf5fe38cf7ee4ce0c6fbcd64f192ccf6bbbff986108","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:AS1","text":"Pure speculative reason must be assessed as a possible edifice rather than assumed to possess unlimited constructive power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:AS2","text":"The Transcendental Doctrine of Elements determines the available materials, their proper use, and the possible height and stability of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:AS3","text":"The materials suffice for a secure habitation oriented toward terrestrial experience but not for a tower exceeding the limits of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:AS4","text":"The failure of the larger project results both from insufficient materials and from disputes that fragment the builders into incompatible individual constructions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:AS5","text":"The task therefore shifts from supplying materials to determining the formal plan and conditions of a complete system of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:AS6","text":"Discipline, canon, architectonic, and history replace undirected construction, while transcendental method corrects the insufficiency of general logic as a guide to substantive scientific cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:A1","text":"The cognition of pure speculative reason can be treated as an edifice requiring materials, design, limits, and stability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:A2","text":"The Transcendental Doctrine of Elements determines the materials available to pure reason and the range of construction they permit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:A3","text":"Human cognition possesses enough material for a secure system oriented to experience but not enough for unrestricted metaphysical extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:A4","text":"A rational system must proportion its design to the material actually available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:A5","text":"The transcendental doctrine of method determines the formal conditions of a complete system of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:A6","text":"A complete system of pure reason requires discipline, canon, architectonic, and history.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:A7","text":"General logic is not restricted to a determinate kind of cognition or object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:A8","text":"Because of its generality, logic alone provides only names, signs, and formal indications of possible methods.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:A9","text":"Substantive methods acquire their meaning and application from the particular sciences in which they operate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:A10","text":"A method that exceeds the limits of the available cognition cannot yield a secure system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:NX1","text":"Pure reason is not supplied with materials sufficient to construct an unrestricted tower reaching beyond human cognitive limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:NX2","text":"A secure system is not produced by designing independently of the available material.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:NX3","text":"The doctrine of method is not concerned primarily with acquiring further materials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:NX4","text":"General logic is not a substantive method for every determinate science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:NX5","text":"General logic does not provide the determinate meaning or application of the methods it names.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:NX6","text":"A plurality of individually designed systems does not constitute a unified system of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:NX7","text":"Practical logic, when detached from transcendental conditions, is not sufficient to organize pure cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:D1","text":"The limits established at the level of cognition-materials constrain the legitimate design of the entire system.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A2","CH_B337_ITDM:A3","CH_B337_ITDM:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:D2","text":"The failure of metaphysical construction is a methodological failure before it is merely a failure of ambition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A3","CH_B337_ITDM:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:D3","text":"The positive task of method begins only after the scope of possible cognition has been delimited.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A2","CH_B337_ITDM:A4","CH_B337_ITDM:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:D4","text":"A complete system requires an ordering principle in addition to a stock of valid cognitions.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A1","CH_B337_ITDM:A5","CH_B337_ITDM:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:D5","text":"Disagreement among constructors signals the absence of a shared systematic plan rather than merely individual error.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A1","CH_B337_ITDM:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:D6","text":"General logic can prepare formal terminology but cannot complete the transition from formal possibility to determinate method.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A7","CH_B337_ITDM:A8","CH_B337_ITDM:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:D7","text":"The learner’s possession of methodological names can precede and conceal possession of the concepts required to use them.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A8","CH_B337_ITDM:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:SD1","text":"The restriction of speculative reason does not abolish systematic reason; it converts system-building from unlimited expansion into formally ordered construction within determinate bounds.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A3","CH_B337_ITDM:A4","CH_B337_ITDM:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:SD2","text":"The doctrine of method transforms critique from a negative inventory of limits into a positive architecture for legitimate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A2","CH_B337_ITDM:A5","CH_B337_ITDM:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:SD3","text":"Systematic unity depends on coordination of cognition, not on the accumulation of independent constructions.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A1","CH_B337_ITDM:A6","CH_B337_ITDM:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:SD4","text":"Formal universality without determinate cognitive content produces the appearance of methodological mastery while deferring actual understanding to the sciences.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A7","CH_B337_ITDM:A8","CH_B337_ITDM:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:SD5","text":"The completed system must include both a synchronic plan of organization and a diachronic account of how pure reason has developed and fragmented.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A5","CH_B337_ITDM:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:DNX1","text":"The system cannot presuppose that human reason has unlimited access to materials adequate for every intelligible design.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:DNX2","text":"The system cannot treat formal possibility as evidence that the corresponding cognition is materially available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:DNX3","text":"The system cannot derive determinate scientific procedures from logic alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:DNX4","text":"The system cannot equate plurality of private constructions with rational unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:DNX5","text":"The system cannot separate the organization of cognition from the prior determination of its limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:DNX6","text":"The system cannot treat historical disagreement as external to the question of systematic form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:SNX1","text":"An absolutely unbounded metaphysical totality is unavailable as the material basis of a secure human system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:SNX2","text":"A universally self-sufficient method independent of determinate objects and sciences is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:SNX3","text":"Pure reason cannot secure unity through spontaneous agreement among isolated reasoners without a common architectonic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:SNX4","text":"Complete methodological transparency is unavailable when formal signs precede the cognition that gives them meaning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:SNX5","text":"The possibility of a system is preserved only through renunciation of the ambition to construct beyond the conditions of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:FGS1","text":"Available cognition → determination of materials → measurement of permissible scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:FGS2","text":"Insufficient materials → failure of unrestricted metaphysical construction → demand for proportional design.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:FGS3","text":"Proportional design → formal conditions of systematic unity → discipline, canon, architectonic, and history.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:FGS4","text":"General formal logic → abstract names and signs → dependence on determinate sciences for methodological content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:FGS5","text":"Cognitive limitation + formal organization + scientific determination → secure system of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:AF1","text":"Ambition of unlimited construction → recognition of insufficient materials → delimitation of legitimate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:AF2","text":"Delimitation of cognition → distinction between material and plan → transition from the Doctrine of Elements to the Doctrine of Method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:AF3","text":"Need for a plan → formal conditions of completeness → division into discipline, canon, architectonic, and history.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:AF4","text":"Failure of general logic → distinction between formal labels and substantive methods → transcendental reconstruction of systematic reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:ME1","text":"The boundary between legitimate and illegitimate metaphysical construction becomes a structural feature of the system rather than an external prohibition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:ME2","text":"Method is reclassified from a catalogue of procedures into the formal architecture governing a complete system of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:ME3","text":"The relation between logic and science is redrawn: logic supplies formal indications, while determinate sciences supply operative meaning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:ME4","text":"Disagreement is reclassified from a merely interpersonal dispute into evidence of missing systematic unity and shared methodological conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:ME5","text":"The object of critique shifts from isolated cognitions to the design, stability, and historical formation of the whole cognitive edifice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text replaces the failed project of an unlimited tower with a habitation proportioned to human cognitive materials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:ALT2","text":"Structural — A complete system requires formal organization through discipline, canon, architectonic, and history.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critique becomes a method for regulating construction rather than merely restricting claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human reason is finite, materially situated, historically divided, and capable of unity only through an ordered relation to its limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the transition from the analysis of cognitive materials to the formal organization of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:MRT2","text":"The system preserves the distinction between the material conditions of cognition and the architectural conditions of its unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:MRT3","text":"The role of discipline, canon, architectonic, and history is structurally recoverable from the definition of method as the formal condition of completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:MRT4","text":"The critique of general logic is recoverable because formal universality is distinguished from determinate methodological content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:MRT5","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined regarding the internal content of the four divisions, since the passage announces them without developing them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:MRT6","text":"The central tension lies between the demand for a complete system and the finite, experience-bounded material available to human reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:MRT7","text":"A secondary tension remains between the need for universally valid form and the dependence of methodological meaning on particular sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITDM:MRT8","text":"The extracted structure preserves the argument’s movement from failed expansion, through limitation, to architectonic system-building.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITDM:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 82","title":"II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method","context":"Chapter 82"}},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ","layer":"CH","title":"INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General","order":44,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"31e153b59d70368aaa9fde4a996491b997d9d5dd42246bb3c934f39953db9d42","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:AS1","text":"The problem is how a rule-governed understanding can determine whether a particular case falls under a rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:AS2","text":"Understanding can be instructed by rules, whereas judgement performs subsumption and cannot be governed by general logic through another rule without regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:AS3","text":"Judgement is therefore an irreducible capacity developed through exercise, while examples train application without constituting universal principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:AS4","text":"The argument is diagnostic in explaining failures of application, reconstructive in distinguishing general from transcendental logic, and architectonic in arranging the doctrine of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:AS5","text":"Transcendental logic differs from general logic because it can specify the a priori conditions under which pure concepts apply to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:AS6","text":"The ground of this possibility is the necessary a priori relation between pure concepts and the objects that can be given in accordance with them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:AS7","text":"The argument concludes by dividing transcendental doctrine into schematism, which supplies sensuous conditions of application, and principles, which formulate the resulting pure synthetic judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A1","text":"Understanding is the faculty of rules or laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A2","text":"Judgement is the faculty of subsuming particular cases under rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A3","text":"General logic abstracts from all content of cognition and treats only the formal relations of concepts, judgements, and conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A4","text":"Any general rule directing subsumption would itself require judgement for its application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A5","text":"Judgement cannot be produced by tuition in the manner of understanding; it requires exercise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A6","text":"The possession of general rules does not secure their correct application to particular cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A7","text":"Examples exercise judgement by sharpening discrimination between rules and cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A8","text":"Examples can weaken apprehension of rules in their universality when treated as substitutes for principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A9","text":"A deficiency in judgement cannot be remedied by scholastic learning alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A10","text":"Transcendental logic must secure and direct the use of pure concepts of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A11","text":"Philosophy considered as a doctrine for enlarging pure a priori cognition has yielded little or no gain, whereas philosophy considered as critique can guard against errors of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A12","text":"Pure concepts of the understanding must relate a priori to objects if they are to possess objective validity rather than remain empty logical forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A13","text":"Transcendental philosophy can indicate both the general condition of a rule and, a priori, the kind of case to which the rule applies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:A14","text":"The transcendental doctrine of judgement consists of a theory of schematism followed by a theory of the principles of the pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:NX1","text":"General logic is not a discipline that supplies directions for subsuming particulars under rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:NX2","text":"No further general rule can guarantee the correct use of rules when the defect lies in judgement itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:NX3","text":"Scholastic instruction cannot compensate for a deficient natural capacity of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:NX4","text":"Learned mastery of a science does not entail competence in applying its rules to concrete cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:NX5","text":"Examples are not adequate substitutes for universal principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:NX6","text":"Examples are not uniformly beneficial to cognition; they may impair the apprehension of rules independently of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:NX7","text":"Philosophy cannot successfully function here as an unrestricted positive doctrine that simply enlarges the stock of pure a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:NX8","text":"Transcendental critique is not primarily constructive metaphysics; its immediate use is negative, consisting in the prevention of errors.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:NX9","text":"Pure concepts cannot remain merely formal structures detached from the conditions under which objects are given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:D1","text":"The distinction between understanding and judgement is a distinction between rule-possession and rule-application.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A1","CH_B337_ITFJ:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:D2","text":"General logic cannot provide a non-circular account of subsumption because every proposed direction for subsumption would require a further act of subsumption.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A3","CH_B337_ITFJ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:D3","text":"A person may be scientifically learned while remaining practically incompetent in applying scientific rules.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A5","CH_B337_ITFJ:A6","CH_B337_ITFJ:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:D4","text":"The failure of application cannot be diagnosed solely as ignorance of rules; it may consist in a defect of judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A2","CH_B337_ITFJ:A6","CH_B337_ITFJ:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:D5","text":"Examples occupy a subordinate pedagogical role: they train application but cannot establish the universal validity of a rule.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A7","CH_B337_ITFJ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:D6","text":"The inability of general logic to direct judgement does not eliminate the possibility of a transcendental account of judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A3","CH_B337_ITFJ:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:D7","text":"Transcendental logic can direct the application of pure concepts because it concerns the conditions under which objects can be given in conformity with those concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A10","CH_B337_ITFJ:A12","CH_B337_ITFJ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:D8","text":"The schema functions as the mediating condition that allows a pure concept to be employed in relation to sensibly given objects.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A12","CH_B337_ITFJ:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:D9","text":"The principles of the pure understanding are a priori synthetic judgements generated by applying pure concepts under their sensuous conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A12","CH_B337_ITFJ:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:D10","text":"Critique acquires a determinate task by restricting and regulating the employment of a limited set of pure concepts rather than expanding cognition without limit.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A10","CH_B337_ITFJ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SD1","text":"The cognitive system is asymmetrical: understanding is teachable through rules, while judgement remains dependent on an irreducible capacity for application.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A1","CH_B337_ITFJ:A2","CH_B337_ITFJ:A5","CH_B337_ITFJ:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SD2","text":"The text replaces the ideal of complete rule-based instruction with a division between transmissible formal knowledge and non-transmissible competence in subsumption.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A4","CH_B337_ITFJ:A5","CH_B337_ITFJ:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SD3","text":"Critique becomes a protective technology of cognition: it does not primarily generate new pure knowledge but prevents misapplication of existing pure concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A10","CH_B337_ITFJ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SD4","text":"The distinction between empty form and valid cognition requires a mediating structure linking pure concepts to conditions of possible object-givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A3","CH_B337_ITFJ:A12","CH_B337_ITFJ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SD5","text":"Judgement is both irreducible to general rules and conditionally regulable within transcendental cognition, because pure concepts determine the conditions of their possible application.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A4","CH_B337_ITFJ:A10","CH_B337_ITFJ:A12","CH_B337_ITFJ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SD6","text":"The architecture of schematism followed by principles converts the problem of application into a staged relation between concept, condition, and synthetic cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A12","CH_B337_ITFJ:A13","CH_B337_ITFJ:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the reduction of judgement to mechanical rule-following.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the assumption that intellectual instruction automatically produces practical or concrete competence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the self-sufficiency of formal logic as a complete account of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the treatment of examples as evidential foundations for universal rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:DNX5","text":"The system excludes objective validity founded solely on empirical confirmation of pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:DNX6","text":"The system excludes a pure concept from counting as cognition without a condition governing the possible givenness of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:DNX7","text":"The system excludes the possibility that philosophical progress consists simply in accumulating additional pure doctrines.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:DNX8","text":"The system excludes a homogeneous model of cognition in which understanding and judgement perform the same operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SNX1","text":"The system cannot admit a final general meta-rule that abolishes the need for judgement in applying rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SNX2","text":"The system cannot admit a universal pedagogy that transfers correct subsumption from teacher to pupil as securely as it transfers rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SNX3","text":"The system cannot admit a form of cognition detached from the conditions under which objects can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SNX4","text":"The system cannot admit a purely formal order as sufficient for objective knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SNX5","text":"The system cannot admit philosophy as either unrestricted doctrinal expansion or mere formal analysis without critical regulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:SNX6","text":"The system leaves no position in which the gap between universal rule and particular case is simply erased; it can only be mediated under determinate transcendental conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS1","text":"Abstraction from cognitive content produces formal rules governing the relations among concepts, judgements, and conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS2","text":"The employment of a rule introduces the problem of determining whether a particular case falls under it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS3","text":"The attempt to solve this problem through another general rule generates regress because the second rule also requires application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS4","text":"The regress establishes judgement as a capacity irreducible to rule-transmission and dependent on exercise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS5","text":"Examples provide exercise for judgement but remain epistemically subordinate to universal rules and may undermine universal apprehension when overused.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS6","text":"The failure of general logic opens a distinction between formal logic, which abstracts from content, and transcendental logic, which specifies conditions of pure conceptual application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS7","text":"The a priori relation between pure concepts and possible objects allows transcendental philosophy to determine both the condition of application and the corresponding field of cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS8","text":"The schema mediates between pure concept and sensuous givenness, after which principles express the a priori synthetic judgements grounded in that mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:AF1","text":"Rule-governed understanding → subsumption of particulars → demand for a rule of application → regress → irreducibility of judgement → exercise through examples.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:AF2","text":"Failure of general logic → distinction between formal and transcendental logic → a priori concept-object relation → conditions of possible givenness → schematism → principles of the pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:ME1","text":"The boundary between cognition and education is redrawn: transmitting rules is separated from producing competence in their application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:ME2","text":"Intellectual error is reclassified from lack of knowledge to possible failure of subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:ME3","text":"Examples are reclassified from sources of theoretical confirmation to instruments of practical judgement-training.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:ME4","text":"The opposition between formal logic and useful philosophy is reorganized into a distinction between general logic and transcendental critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:ME5","text":"Pure concepts are no longer treated as self-sufficient formal entities; their validity depends on conditions of possible object-givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:ME6","text":"The problem of applying rules is transformed from a pedagogical problem into a transcendental problem of mediation between concepts and objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:ME7","text":"The architecture of pure cognition is redrawn as a sequence from concept to schema to principle rather than as a direct passage from rule to object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:ALT1","text":"Surface — The immediate object is the determination of whether a particular case stands under a given rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text separates formal rule-governed understanding from judgement as the capacity that connects rules with cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Philosophy is reassigned from unrestricted doctrinal construction to critical regulation of the employment of pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Cognition requires an a priori relation between concepts and objects, mediated by the conditions under which objects can be given; temporality is not separately developed in the passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central distinction between understanding as rule-governed and judgement as subsumptive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT2","text":"The axioms reconstruct the movement from the failure of general logic to the special task of transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT3","text":"The transition from pure concepts to applicable cognition is underdetermined at this stage because the detailed operation of schemata and principles is deferred to the following chapters.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent only if general logic and transcendental logic are treated as operating under different relations to content and object-givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT5","text":"Its strongest structural distinction is between possessing a rule and correctly subsuming a case under it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT6","text":"Its principal unresolved tension is that judgement is declared irreducible to rules while transcendental philosophy is said to direct it through determinate conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT7","text":"The tension is contained but not fully resolved by restricting transcendental direction to pure concepts and their conditions of possible application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement of the passage, but the practical status of natural judgement and the exact mechanics of transcendental application remain compressed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITFJ:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 42","title":"INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General","context":"Chapter 42"}},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA","layer":"CH","title":"I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","order":45,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"d8bf5b7089ba6058b852892bd5b16774136ca42e1dcf46f37b51aba328d251b8","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:AS1","text":"Dialectical appearance must be distinguished from probability, empirical illusion, and sensory phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:AS2","text":"Error is relocated from objects and senses to judgement, where subjective grounds can be mistaken for objective grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:AS3","text":"Transcendental reflection resolves erroneous judgement into the distinct contributions of sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:AS4","text":"The central danger is a transcendental appearance that induces reason to exceed possible experience under the semblance of objective principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:AS5","text":"Critique distinguishes immanent principles, limited to experience, from transcendent principles that authorize boundary-transgression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:AS6","text":"Transcendental illusion arises when subjective necessities of reason are represented as necessities belonging to things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:AS7","text":"Transcendental dialectic can expose and regulate this natural illusion but cannot abolish its recurrent appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A1","text":"Probability is imperfectly grounded truth and is not illusory appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A2","text":"Truth, error, and illusory appearance occur only in judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A3","text":"Sensibility, considered by itself, neither judges nor errs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A4","text":"Understanding, considered by itself and acting according to its own laws, does not err.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A5","text":"Error arises when sensibility covertly influences understanding and subjective grounds are treated as objective grounds of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A6","text":"Transcendental reflection can distinguish the respective operations of sensibility and understanding in pure a priori judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A7","text":"Immanent principles are valid only within the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A8","text":"Transcendent principles claim authority to extend cognition beyond the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A9","text":"Transcendental misuse of the categories is a failure of judgement, whereas transcendent principles positively authorize the crossing of experiential limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A10","text":"Logical illusion results from inattention to logical rules and disappears when the error is recognized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A11","text":"Transcendental illusion converts subjective maxims and necessities of reason into purported objective determinations of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:A12","text":"Transcendental illusion is natural, unavoidable, and recurrent even after criticism has disclosed its groundlessness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:NX1","text":"Dialectical appearance is not probability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:NX2","text":"Phenomenal intuition is not itself true, false, deceptive, or erroneous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:NX3","text":"Error is not produced by sensibility alone or by understanding alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:NX4","text":"The relevant illusion is not empirical illusion arising within ordinary experiential judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:NX5","text":"Transcendental use of the categories is not identical with transcendent principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:NX6","text":"Immanent use of pure understanding is not authorization to cognize objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:NX7","text":"Transcendental illusion is not a sophistical fabrication or a merely correctable logical lapse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:NX8","text":"Critique is not a procedure for extinguishing transcendental illusion altogether.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:D1","text":"Error requires a composite judgement in which sensibility supplies an unrecognized subjective determination to understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A2","CH_B337_ITIA:A3","CH_B337_ITIA:A4","CH_B337_ITIA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:D2","text":"The diagnosis of error requires faculty-differentiation rather than inspection of the represented object alone.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A2","CH_B337_ITIA:A5","CH_B337_ITIA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:D3","text":"Critical limitation of cognition distinguishes the legitimate empirical use of concepts from claims that purport to determine what exceeds experience.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A7","CH_B337_ITIA:A8","CH_B337_ITIA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:D4","text":"A subjective requirement of reason acquires deceptive force when its necessity is reclassified as a necessity inherent in objects themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:D5","text":"The practical function of transcendental dialectic is continual exposure and resistance rather than final correction.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A10","CH_B337_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:SD1","text":"Critique changes from an eliminative logic of error into a regulative discipline for recurring rational self-deception.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A10","CH_B337_ITIA:A11","CH_B337_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:SD2","text":"The boundary of legitimate cognition is determined by possible experience, not by the subjective force or apparent inevitability of a rational inference.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A7","CH_B337_ITIA:A8","CH_B337_ITIA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:SD3","text":"Dialectical appearance is generated internally by reason’s own lawful exercise when its subjective maxims are misrepresented as object-constituting principles.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A4","CH_B337_ITIA:A9","CH_B337_ITIA:A11","CH_B337_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:SD4","text":"The same rational capacity that supplies principles of cognition also generates the illusion of cognition beyond its own legitimate domain.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A7","CH_B337_ITIA:A8","CH_B337_ITIA:A11","CH_B337_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:DNX1","text":"Subjective necessity is not sufficient evidence of objective necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:DNX2","text":"Conceptual possession of categories is not sufficient warrant for cognition of objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:DNX3","text":"Disclosure of an illusion’s source is not equivalent to liberation from its sensible or rational appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:DNX4","text":"Cognitive error cannot be explained by locating falsity in objects, sensations, or the autonomous understanding considered separately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:DNX5","text":"Rational universality is not self-validating as knowledge of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:SNX1","text":"No final cognitive standpoint is available from which human reason can cease to generate the illusion rooted in its own subjective constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:SNX2","text":"No transition from the necessity of thinking to the determination of things in themselves is admissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:SNX3","text":"No complete immunity from dialectical error is available through mere recognition of critical limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:FGS1","text":"Sensibility affects understanding → subjective grounds enter judgement → subjective grounds are misidentified as objective.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:FGS2","text":"Reason employs subjective maxims → their necessity appears objective → claims arise concerning things beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:FGS3","text":"Possible experience supplies the boundary of immanent validity → boundary-crossing principles are classified as transcendent → their cognitive claim is disqualified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:FGS4","text":"Critical reflection exposes the source of transcendental appearance → exposure blocks its authority → natural illusion nevertheless recurs → vigilance replaces eradication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:AF1","text":"Appearance is distinguished from probability and phenomenon → error is located in judgement → error is analyzed as faculty-confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:AF2","text":"Empirical illusion is excluded → transcendental appearance is isolated → immanent and transcendent principles are separated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:AF3","text":"Subjective rational necessity is projected onto things in themselves → transcendental illusion is diagnosed as natural → critique is assigned a permanently regulative function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:ME1","text":"The opposition between reliable senses and deceptive senses is displaced by an analysis of judgement and interfaculty interference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:ME2","text":"The distinction between transcendental and transcendent separates a faulty use of cognition from principles that authorize an illegitimate new domain of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:ME3","text":"Critique is reclassified from a means of eliminating illusion into a practice of delimiting its authority and repeatedly counteracting its effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:ME4","text":"Rational compulsion is rendered ambiguous: it can indicate a subjective condition of thought without constituting an objective property of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text distinguishes logical, empirical, and transcendental forms of illusory appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:ALT2","text":"Structural — Error is produced by an unobserved mediation in which sensibility determines understanding’s judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Criticism operates by assigning faculties, concepts, and principles to their legitimate domains and refusing unauthorized extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Things in themselves cannot be determined from subjective necessities of reason, while transcendental illusion persists as a permanent feature of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the localization of error to the diagnosis of transcendental illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:MRT2","text":"The system depends principally on the distinction between subjective rational necessity and objective determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:MRT3","text":"The distinction between immanent and transcendent principles supplies the decisive criterion for limiting cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:MRT4","text":"The structure is coherent if critique is understood as limiting authority rather than abolishing appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:MRT5","text":"The system leaves underdetermined how continual critical vigilance can reliably resist an illusion declared natural and unavoidable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:MRT6","text":"The system contains a structural tension between reason’s lawful operation and reason’s recurrent production of deceptive objective claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITIA:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument only if the final result remains a regulated recurrence of illusion rather than a completed cure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITIA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 52","title":"I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance","context":"Chapter 52"}},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0","layer":"CH","title":"II. Of Transcendental Logic.","order":46,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"bb0b94e016987ae84a75a45b256e05eb9c415f5445a82dccbb02f8ef33055616","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AS1","text":"General logic abstracts from cognition’s content and from its relation to objects, retaining only the form of thought and the relations among cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AS2","text":"The distinction between pure and empirical intuition permits a corresponding distinction between pure and empirical thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AS3","text":"A logic of pure thought of objects must retain nonempirical object-directed content while excluding cognition with empirical content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AS4","text":"Transcendentality concerns cognition of the a priori possibility and use of representations, not merely the fact that they are a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AS5","text":"The transcendental/empirical distinction belongs to the critique of cognition rather than to the object-relation considered in isolation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AS6","text":"The possibility of pure concepts relating a priori to objects motivates the anticipatory construction of a science of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AS7","text":"The argument is a classificatory and reconstructive delimitation of transcendental logic by its origin, scope, and objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A1","text":"Cognition has both a logical form and a relation to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A2","text":"General logic abstracts from all content of cognition and from cognition’s relation to its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A3","text":"General logic considers the laws of understanding as applied to representations regardless of their origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A4","text":"Thought, like intuition, can be distinguished into pure and empirical forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A5","text":"A logic of pure thought of objects cannot abstract from all cognition-content while remaining object-directed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A6","text":"A logic of pure thought must exclude cognitions whose content is empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A7","text":"The origin of cognition may be investigated according to whether it is or is not ascribed to objects themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A8","text":"Transcendentality concerns the a priori possibility, application, or use of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A9","text":"Being a priori by itself is insufficient to make a cognition transcendental.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A10","text":"The transcendental/empirical distinction concerns the critique of cognition rather than the object-relation taken by itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A11","text":"Pure concepts may relate a priori to objects as acts of pure thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:A12","text":"A science of transcendental logic must determine the origin, extent, and objective validity of pure object-directed cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:NX1","text":"Transcendentality is not identical with every form of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:NX2","text":"Space is not itself a transcendental representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:NX3","text":"An a priori geometrical determination of space is not itself transcendental.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:NX4","text":"The a priori status of a representation does not by itself establish its transcendental status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:NX5","text":"General logic is not concerned with the origin of cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:NX6","text":"A logic restricted to pure thought of objects is not a logic that admits empirical-content cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:NX7","text":"Pure concepts under consideration are neither empirical nor aesthetic in origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:NX8","text":"The transcendental/empirical distinction is not a distinction between kinds of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:NX9","text":"Transcendental logic is not general logic applied indiscriminately to empirical and pure rational cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:NX10","text":"The relation of cognition to an object, considered without reference to its a priori mode of possibility or use, is not sufficient for transcendental classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:D1","text":"Because general logic abstracts from content and object-relation, it can apply its laws to representations regardless of their source.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A2","CH_B337_ITL0:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:D2","text":"Because pure thought remains object-directed while excluding empirical content, its logic retains a restricted form of content rather than becoming wholly formal.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A5","CH_B337_ITL0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:D3","text":"The origin of cognition becomes a distinguishing issue when logic is no longer limited to relations among representations.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A3","CH_B337_ITL0:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:D4","text":"A priori status becomes transcendental only when cognition of its possibility or use is included in the analysis.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A8","CH_B337_ITL0:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:D5","text":"The same representation can receive different classifications according to the mode in which it is related to objects.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A8","CH_B337_ITL0:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:D6","text":"Application to objects in general is transcendental, whereas restriction to objects of sense is empirical.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A8","CH_B337_ITL0:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:D7","text":"Transcendental logic investigates the non-object-derived origin of cognition without abandoning object-directed validity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A7","CH_B337_ITL0:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:D8","text":"The proposed science must investigate more than the existence of pure concepts; it must establish their scope and objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A11","CH_B337_ITL0:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:D9","text":"Transcendental logic is distinguished from general logic by its exclusive concern with the a priori relation of cognition to objects.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A3","CH_B337_ITL0:A8","CH_B337_ITL0:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SD1","text":"Formality alone cannot define transcendental logic, because transcendentality requires an a priori account of object-directed cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A2","CH_B337_ITL0:A8","CH_B337_ITL0:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SD2","text":"Content alone cannot define transcendental logic, because the relevant content is restricted to pure thought and excludes empirical determination.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A5","CH_B337_ITL0:A6","CH_B337_ITL0:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SD3","text":"Transcendental logic operates at a metacritical level by examining the conditions, scope, and validity of object-directed thought rather than merely applying laws of thought.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A3","CH_B337_ITL0:A7","CH_B337_ITL0:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SD4","text":"The objectivity of pure understanding is not presupposed by purity but becomes a problem requiring determination of origin, extent, and validity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A9","CH_B337_ITL0:A11","CH_B337_ITL0:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SD5","text":"Transcendental classification attaches to the critical mode of cognition’s use and possibility rather than to a representation considered in isolation.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A8","CH_B337_ITL0:A9","CH_B337_ITL0:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SD6","text":"The system establishes an intermediate domain between contentless formal logic and empirically conditioned cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A5","CH_B337_ITL0:A6","CH_B337_ITL0:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:DNX1","text":"The system excludes a contentless formalism as a sufficient account of cognition directed toward objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:DNX2","text":"The system excludes an empirical account of cognitive origin as sufficient for explaining a priori object-cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the object itself as the complete ground of the cognition’s a priori possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the mere possession of an a priori representation as proof of transcendentality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the reduction of transcendental logic to an inventory of pure representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:DNX6","text":"The system excludes a direct inference from object-relation alone to transcendental status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:DNX7","text":"The system excludes the identification of empirical and transcendental as two mutually exclusive classes of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:DNX8","text":"The system excludes a logic that treats form, origin, use, and objective validity as one undifferentiated problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SNX1","text":"The object itself cannot serve as the final explanatory ground of the a priori possibility of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SNX2","text":"Purity cannot by itself secure objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SNX3","text":"Empirical and transcendental cannot be treated as two independently given object-domains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SNX4","text":"No complete science of pure understanding exists until its origin, extent, and objective validity are determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SNX5","text":"The actual inventory of pure concepts remains unprovided and is introduced only as an anticipated possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SNX6","text":"A purely formal account of thought is unavailable as a complete theory of cognition because it cannot explain a priori applicability to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:SNX7","text":"An object-grounded account of cognition is unavailable as a complete theory because it cannot explain cognition whose origin is not ascribed to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:FGS1","text":"When cognition’s content and object-relation are abstracted, general logic is produced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:FGS2","text":"When pure and empirical intuition are distinguished, a corresponding distinction between pure and empirical thought becomes available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:FGS3","text":"When pure thought remains directed toward objects while empirical content is excluded, a restricted object-oriented logic is delimited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:FGS4","text":"When cognitive origin is divided between object-derived and non-object-derived sources, origin becomes a criterion of logical classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:FGS5","text":"When the a priori is defined through possibility, application, and use, transcendentality becomes a critical determination rather than a mere property of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:FGS6","text":"When pure concepts capable of a priori object-relation are posited, a science of pure understanding becomes conceptually possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:FGS7","text":"When that science is assigned the tasks of determining origin, extent, and objective validity, it becomes transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AF1","text":"Abstraction from content and object-relation → general logic as the study of formal relations among cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AF2","text":"Distinction between pure and empirical intuition → distinction between pure and empirical thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AF3","text":"Retention of pure object-directed content plus exclusion of empirical content → restricted logic of pure thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AF4","text":"Restriction of the a priori to possibility, application, and use → transcendental classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AF5","text":"Anticipation of pure concepts relating a priori to objects → science of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:AF6","text":"Determination of origin, extent, and objective validity → transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:ME1","text":"Logic is divided not simply into formal and material domains but according to whether it investigates an a priori relation to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:ME2","text":"The a priori is reclassified from a static property of representations into a criterion involving their possibility and use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:ME3","text":"Cognitive origin becomes a distinct analytical axis alongside logical form and object-directed content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:ME4","text":"Objective validity becomes an explicit problem of a science of pure understanding rather than an implicit consequence of formal purity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:ME5","text":"The apparent opposition between pure form and empirical content is replaced by a third domain: pure, object-directed cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:ME6","text":"The transcendental/empirical distinction is relocated from the object-field to the critique of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, the text distinguishes general logic from transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, the distinction is generated by four variables: content, logical form, cognitive origin, and relation to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, the text replaces direct classification of representations with a critique of their a priori possibility, application, and validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:ALT4","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, cognition is treated as capable of a priori relation to objects while its determining ground is not simply located in the objects themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the distinction between general logic and transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:MRT2","text":"The extracted sequence preserves the movement from abstraction, through differentiation and qualification, to the definition of a science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:MRT3","text":"The system is strongest in its distinction between mere a priori status and transcendental cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:MRT4","text":"The actual content of the pure concepts and the detailed determination of their validity remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:MRT5","text":"The system retains a productive tension between requiring content for object-directed logic and excluding empirical content from pure thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:MRT6","text":"A second tension remains between defining transcendentality through a priori object-relation and denying that the distinction concerns object-relation as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_ITL0:MRT7","text":"A third tension remains between denying objects as the ground of a priori cognition and requiring objective validity as the final task of transcendental logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_ITL0:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 19","title":"II. Of Transcendental Logic.","context":"Chapter 19"}},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ","layer":"CH","title":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15","order":47,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"33c03e40c7e7e29151222c88eef5b0feaa5f29475274c61078304b774121af2f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:AS1","text":"The text seeks an account of judgment that explains its objective validity rather than merely naming a relation between representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:AS2","text":"It rejects the conventional logical definition because it neither covers all judgment-forms nor determines the nature of their relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:AS3","text":"It distinguishes the objective relation produced by understanding from the subjective relation produced by reproductive imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:AS4","text":"It defines judgment as the mode by which given cognitions are brought under the objective unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:AS5","text":"It grounds objective unity in the necessary synthesis of representations according to principles derived from transcendental unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:AS6","text":"It contrasts objective predication with subjective association, showing why repeated perception cannot by itself yield an objectively valid judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:A1","text":"A judgment is not adequately defined as a relation between two conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:A2","text":"Hypothetical and disjunctive judgments contain relations between judgments rather than merely relations between conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:A3","text":"Identifying a relation formally does not determine what constitutes that relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:A4","text":"Judgment is the mode of bringing given cognitions under the objective unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:A5","text":"The relation belonging to understanding is distinct from the relation produced by the laws of reproductive imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:A6","text":"Objective unity relates representations to original apperception and gives them necessary unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:A7","text":"A judgment may be empirically contingent while its representations possess necessary unity in the synthesis of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:A8","text":"Necessary unity in synthesis is governed by principles of objective determination derived from transcendental unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:A9","text":"Objective validity requires representations to be conjoined in the object rather than merely co-present under a condition of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:A10","text":"Subjective association can yield a report of felt conjunction but cannot by itself yield objective predication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:NX1","text":"Judgment is not merely a relation between two conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:NX2","text":"Categorical judgment is not the universal form to which hypothetical and disjunctive judgments can be reduced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:NX3","text":"The logical naming of a relation is not an account of what makes the relation valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:NX4","text":"Objective unity is not empirical co-occurrence in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:NX5","text":"Objective validity is not produced by reproductive imagination or associative laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:NX6","text":"Repetition of a perceptive act does not establish that representations belong together in the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:D1","text":"A merely formal definition of judgment cannot explain the distinction between objective validity and subjective association.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A1","CH_B337_LFAJ:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:D2","text":"The diversity of judgment-forms requires an account of judgment that is not restricted to relations between concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A2","CH_B337_LFAJ:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:D3","text":"Objective judgment arises through the subsumption of given cognitions under a unity supplied by apperception rather than through associative succession.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A4","CH_B337_LFAJ:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:D4","text":"Empirical contingency does not eliminate the necessary unity required for a judgment to possess objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A6","CH_B337_LFAJ:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:D5","text":"The objective assertion that a body is heavy expresses more than the subjective report that carrying the body produces an impression of weight.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A7","CH_B337_LFAJ:A9","CH_B337_LFAJ:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:D6","text":"The transcendental unity of apperception functions as the condition through which given representations can become cognitions with objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A4","CH_B337_LFAJ:A6","CH_B337_LFAJ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:SD1","text":"The criterion of judgment shifts from the recurrence of associated representations to the mode of their synthesis under principles of objective determination.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A4","CH_B337_LFAJ:A5","CH_B337_LFAJ:A8","CH_B337_LFAJ:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:SD2","text":"Objectivity is constituted neither by empirical necessity nor by an externally given relation alone, but by the necessary unity through which representations are synthesized as cognitions.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A6","CH_B337_LFAJ:A7","CH_B337_LFAJ:A8","CH_B337_LFAJ:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:SD3","text":"The distinction between subject and object is operationalized as the distinction between representations conjoined in the object and representations merely conjoined in the subject’s perception.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A6","CH_B337_LFAJ:A9","CH_B337_LFAJ:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:SD4","text":"The exclusive elevation of categorical judgment produces a defective logical architecture because it suppresses relations that belong directly between judgments.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A2","CH_B337_LFAJ:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:DNX1","text":"Objective validity cannot be grounded in private empirical feeling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:DNX2","text":"Objective predication cannot be secured by induction from repeated perceptive conjunction alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:DNX3","text":"A purely formal logic cannot determine judgment without an account of the synthesis that gives representations objective unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:DNX4","text":"The necessity relevant to objective judgment is not necessity of empirical coexistence in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:DNX5","text":"Cognition cannot be treated as a mere aggregate of representations already given in isolation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:SNX1","text":"The system excludes an objective-valid judgment whose validity is secured solely by an object considered independently of the conditions under which representations are synthesized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:SNX2","text":"The system excludes empirical subjectivity as an adequate bearer of objectivity; objectivity requires transcendental apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a self-sufficient formal conception of logic that determines judgment without reference to the conditions of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:SNX4","text":"The system excludes any judgment that cannot distinguish objective conjunction from subjective co-presence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:FGS1","text":"Given cognitions enter judgment as representations requiring determination of their mode of relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:FGS2","text":"The relation of understanding is separated from the relation generated by reproductive imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:FGS3","text":"The understanding refers the given representations to original apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:FGS4","text":"Transcendental unity of apperception supplies the necessary unity of their synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:FGS5","text":"Principles derived from this unity determine whether the representations can form cognition with objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:FGS6","text":"If the representations are united in the object, judgment results; if they are united only by association in the subject, only a subjective report results.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:AF1","text":"Inadequate logical definition → plurality of judgment-forms → distinction between understanding and reproductive imagination → reference to original apperception → necessary synthesis → objective judgment versus subjective association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:AF2","text":"Subjective impression → associative conjunction → dependence on the subject’s condition; objective predication → synthesis under apperception → representation of conjunction in the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:ME1","text":"Judgment is reclassified from a static relation between concepts into an operation of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:ME2","text":"Objectivity is relocated from repeated perception to the necessary unity of representation under apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:ME3","text":"The distinction between objective and subjective unity becomes a criterion for distinguishing cognition from mere association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:ME4","text":"Categorical judgment loses its privileged status as the universal model of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:ME5","text":"The relation between subject and object is redrawn through the conditions of synthesis rather than through an appeal to unmediated object-givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:ALT1","text":"Surface: The text distinguishes an objective judgment about an object from a subjective report of an impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:ALT2","text":"Structural: Judgment is generated by the relation among given cognitions, understanding, reproductive imagination, apperception, and synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The text replaces a deficient logical taxonomy with a criterion based on the production of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Objectivity depends on transcendental conditions of synthesis, while empirical content remains contingent and temporally given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the insufficiency of the traditional definition to the transcendental account of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:MRT2","text":"The extracted structure preserves the decisive distinction between objective unity and subjective association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:MRT3","text":"The derivation of the principles from transcendental unity of apperception is assumed rather than demonstrated within the passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:MRT4","text":"The relation between empirical contingency and necessary unity is asserted through a distinction between intuition and synthesis but not fully formalized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:MRT5","text":"The system remains structurally tense because objectivity is grounded in apperception while being defined as independence from the empirical condition of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_LFAJ:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction would preserve the real argument: logical insufficiency → transcendental grounding → objective synthesis → separation of judgment from association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_LFAJ:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 34","title":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15","context":"Chapter 34"}},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1","layer":"CH","title":"Observation § 17","order":48,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"6019644bd7cc162ca6fb382f769edbb34383f031dc0a9125eea13f6a87066a94","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AS1","text":"The problem is how a manifold given in intuition becomes unified as belonging to one self-consciousness and as constituting an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AS2","text":"The category supplies the synthesis through which the manifold is related to the necessary unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AS3","text":"The deduction isolates this a priori unity by abstracting from the empirical mode in which the manifold is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AS4","text":"The deduction is completed when the unity of empirical intuition is shown to coincide with the unity imposed by the category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AS5","text":"This structure applies only to a finite understanding that receives its manifold from intuition and organizes it through thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AS6","text":"An intuitive understanding would produce its objects through representation and would therefore have no use for categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AS7","text":"The exact origin and number of the categories, judgmental functions, and forms of intuition remain irreducible facts about human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A1","text":"The manifold to be intuited is given prior to and independently of the understanding’s synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A2","text":"The understanding synthesizes the manifold presented in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A3","text":"The synthesis relates the manifold to the necessary unity of self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A4","text":"The category is the rule or means by which the understanding produces this synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A5","text":"Categories originate in the understanding independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A6","text":"Empirical consciousness of a manifold is subject to a pure a priori self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A7","text":"The represented unity through which an object is given includes both the synthesis of the manifold and its relation to the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A8","text":"The finite understanding is a discursive faculty whose power consists in thought rather than intuitive production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A9","text":"The finite understanding cognizes nothing through itself but connects and arranges material supplied by intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A10","text":"The unity belonging to empirical intuition is the same unity that the category imposes on a given intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A11","text":"The categories therefore possess a priori validity for all objects of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:A12","text":"The exact number of categories, the precise functions of judgment, and the restriction of intuition to space and time cannot be further explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:NX1","text":"The deduction is not an account of how the empirical manifold is originally given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:NX2","text":"The categories are not derived from sensibility or empirical content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:NX3","text":"The finite understanding is not an intuitive power that produces objects through representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:NX4","text":"Categories are not universally meaningful for every possible kind of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:NX5","text":"The understanding is not a self-sufficient source of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:NX6","text":"The object is not constituted by synthesis alone, since the manifold must already be given to synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:NX7","text":"The a priori validity of the categories is not established by explaining why these categories exist in precisely this number.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:NX8","text":"The unity of intuition is not reducible either to raw sensibility or to an isolated act of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:D1","text":"An object of cognition is available only when a given manifold is synthesized into a unity related to apperception.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A1","CH_B337_OBS1:A2","CH_B337_OBS1:A3","CH_B337_OBS1:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:D2","text":"The category functions as a condition of empirical consciousness rather than as an empirical feature discovered within consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A4","CH_B337_OBS1:A5","CH_B337_OBS1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:D3","text":"The deduction can isolate the category’s contribution only by bracketing the empirical mode in which intuition supplies its manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A1","CH_B337_OBS1:A4","CH_B337_OBS1:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:D4","text":"The category’s a priori validity is established by demonstrating that sensibility contributes the same unity that the understanding imposes.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A10","CH_B337_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:D5","text":"Finite cognition is structurally dependent on the cooperation of receptive intuition and spontaneous synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A1","CH_B337_OBS1:A2","CH_B337_OBS1:A8","CH_B337_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:D6","text":"Categories are rules for organizing presented material, not powers that generate material objects for a finite knower.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A4","CH_B337_OBS1:A8","CH_B337_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:D7","text":"The deduction establishes the function and scope of the categories without establishing their ultimate origin.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A5","CH_B337_OBS1:A11","CH_B337_OBS1:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SD1","text":"Objectivity is generated by the irreducible conjunction of independent givenness and a priori synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A1","CH_B337_OBS1:A2","CH_B337_OBS1:A7","CH_B337_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SD2","text":"The universality of the categories derives from the identity of the unity required by understanding and the unity present in sensible intuition, not from the categories’ empirical origin.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A5","CH_B337_OBS1:A10","CH_B337_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SD3","text":"Pure self-consciousness functions as the formal principle of unity without becoming the source of intuitive material.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A3","CH_B337_OBS1:A6","CH_B337_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SD4","text":"The validity of the categories is relative to the architecture of finite cognition rather than absolute across all possible intellects.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A8","CH_B337_OBS1:A9","CH_B337_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SD5","text":"The argument separates transcendental justification from metaphysical explanation: the categories can be shown necessary for experience without explaining why the human mind possesses them.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A5","CH_B337_OBS1:A10","CH_B337_OBS1:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SD6","text":"The explanatory system terminates in an irreducible fact about the structure of human cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A8","CH_B337_OBS1:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:DNX1","text":"No cognition occurs through pure understanding without a manifold supplied by intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:DNX2","text":"No empirical object is available as an object without synthesis under the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:DNX3","text":"No empirical investigation can explain the a priori source of the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:DNX4","text":"No single cognitive faculty is sufficient for finite object-knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:DNX5","text":"No explanation of categorical validity can eliminate the dependence of that validity on sensible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:DNX6","text":"No deduction can convert the contingent fact of possessing particular cognitive functions into a further rational derivation of those functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:DNX7","text":"No account of the object may treat givenness and synthesis as interchangeable operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SNX1","text":"The finite understanding cannot be an origin that produces both the material and the unity of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SNX2","text":"The ultimate source of the manifold’s givenness remains outside the deduction’s formal field.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SNX3","text":"The possibility of deriving the exact structure of human cognition from a deeper principle is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SNX4","text":"Neither a wholly preconstituted object nor a wholly self-generated object is admissible within the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SNX5","text":"The possibility of cognition without the asymmetry between receptivity and spontaneity is excluded for human understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:SNX6","text":"The system cannot make the conditions of cognition themselves fully transparent to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:FGS1","text":"An independently given manifold enters sensibility before the activity of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:FGS2","text":"The understanding synthesizes the manifold through categorical rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:FGS3","text":"Synthesis relates the manifold to the necessary unity of apperception and thereby presents it as belonging to one self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:FGS4","text":"The represented unity of intuition makes an object available for cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:FGS5","text":"The deduction abstracts from the empirical mode of givenness to isolate the a priori unity contributed by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:FGS6","text":"The structure of sensibility is then used to show that empirical intuition possesses the same unity imposed by the category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:FGS7","text":"The coincidence of these unities establishes categorical validity for objects of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:FGS8","text":"The contrast with an intuitive understanding limits the result to finite, discursive cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:FGS9","text":"The exact origin and enumeration of the categories remain as unexplained conditions of the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AF1","text":"Given manifold → synthesis by understanding → relation to apperception → object as unified representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AF2","text":"Category as a priori rule → empirical consciousness as subject to pure self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AF3","text":"Abstraction from empirical givenness → isolation of categorical unity → return to sensibility for confirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AF4","text":"Identity of sensible and categorical unity → a priori validity for objects of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AF5","text":"Finite discursive understanding → dependence on intuition → contrast with intuitive understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:AF6","text":"Functional deduction → explanatory limit concerning the origin and number of cognitive forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ME1","text":"The object is reclassified from a merely received manifold into a manifold constituted as an object through synthesis and apperceptive unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ME2","text":"The category is reclassified from a logical classification to a condition governing the unity of empirical consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ME3","text":"The deduction shifts from tracing empirical origins to isolating the formal contribution of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ME4","text":"The opposition between passive intuition and active understanding is replaced by a constitutive cooperation between givenness and synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ME5","text":"A priori validity is restricted to objects of sense and detached from claims about every possible intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ME6","text":"The boundary of explanation becomes part of the architecture: transcendental necessity is separated from the unexplained fact of possessing particular faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ME7","text":"The intuitive intellect functions as a limit-concept that reveals the specifically finite character of categorical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ALT1","text":"Surface — A manifold becomes “mine” and is apprehended as an object through synthesis under the unity of self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ALT2","text":"Structural — Sensibility supplies the manifold, understanding supplies categorical unity, and apperception supplies the principle of necessary connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument establishes validity by separating formal contribution from empirical givenness and then reconnecting them through a unity shared by both faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human cognition presupposes a prior givenness of material and an a priori ordering of that material; an intuitive intellect would operate without this temporal and structural asymmetry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — The system permits a deduction of conditions of experience while denying a final explanation of why those conditions have their exact human form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the passage’s central mechanism: given manifold, categorical synthesis, apperceptive unity, and object cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the movement from formal isolation of categorical unity to its validation through sensible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:MRT3","text":"The mode in which the manifold is initially given remains underdetermined by design.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent as a conditional account of finite cognition but does not provide an ultimate genesis of its own conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:MRT5","text":"A structural tension remains between the manifold’s independent givenness and the claim that an object is given through a represented unity produced by synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:MRT6","text":"The passage leaves unresolved whether represented object-unity should be read as constitution of the object for cognition or as formal articulation of an independently given object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction depends on the promised subsequent demonstration that sensible intuition and categorical synthesis possess the same unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OBS1:MRT8","text":"The strongest result is the limitation of categorical validity to finite discursive cognition; the weakest point is the unexplained facticity of the categories, judgmental functions, and forms of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OBS1:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 36","title":"Observation § 17","context":"Chapter 36"}},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA","layer":"CH","title":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12","order":49,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"3b7b9bfe599588711622216cec910f8af0d392b168d68f6043556b59d7e2c22c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AS1","text":"The argument addresses how a manifold given in intuition can belong to one subject of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AS2","text":"It proceeds by transcendental reconstruction rather than by psychological description.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AS3","text":"It identifies the “I think” as a necessary, spontaneous, and identical form of pure apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AS4","text":"It relocates the ground of self-identity from empirical accompaniment to the synthesis of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AS5","text":"It assigns this synthesis to the understanding rather than to objects, perception, or mere sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AS6","text":"It concludes that synthetical unity of apperception is the highest condition of concepts, logic, and a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A1","text":"A representation can exist for the subject only if the “I think” can accompany it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A2","text":"Intuition supplies a manifold of representations prior to determinate thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A3","text":"The “I think” is an act of spontaneity rather than a modification of mere sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A4","text":"Pure or primitive apperception is one and the same in all acts of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A5","text":"The manifold of intuition must stand in a necessary relation to one self-consciousness to count as the subject’s representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A6","text":"Empirical consciousness accompanying different representations is fragmentary and disunited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A7","text":"Identity across representations requires their conjunction in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A8","text":"Analytical unity of apperception presupposes synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A9","text":"Conjunction into a conception is an operation of the understanding rather than something supplied by objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A10","text":"The understanding is the faculty of conjoining representations a priori and bringing their manifold under the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A11","text":"Synthetical unity of apperception is the highest principle of human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A12","text":"The principle of the necessary unity of apperception is analytically identical while explaining the necessity of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A13","text":"The ego as a simple representation contains no manifold; manifold content is given only in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:A14","text":"Human understanding thinks but does not intuitively provide its own manifold and therefore depends on sense for intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:NX1","text":"A representation without possible relation to the “I think” is impossible or nothing for the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:NX2","text":"Pure apperception is not empirical consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:NX3","text":"The identity of consciousness does not result from consciously accompanying every representation individually.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:NX4","text":"Empirical consciousness does not by itself ground the identity of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:NX5","text":"The conjunction that produces a conception is not found ready-made in objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:NX6","text":"Conceptual unity cannot be borrowed from objects through perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:NX7","text":"The ego as a simple representation is not the source of manifold content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:NX8","text":"Human understanding is not an intuitive understanding that generates its own manifold through consciousness alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:D1","text":"A manifold counts as the subject’s manifold only insofar as it can be synthesized in one self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A1","CH_B337_OSUA:A5","CH_B337_OSUA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:D2","text":"The thought that representations belong to the subject is equivalent to the thought that they are united, or can be united, in one self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A5","CH_B337_OSUA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:D3","text":"Analytical self-identity is possible only under the presupposition of synthetic unity.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A7","CH_B337_OSUA:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:D4","text":"Representations can function as a general concept only after being synthetically unified with other actual or possible representations.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A8","CH_B337_OSUA:A9","CH_B337_OSUA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:D5","text":"The identity of consciousness can extend across representations of which the subject is not explicitly conscious as belonging to it, provided their synthesis is possible.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A1","CH_B337_OSUA:A5","CH_B337_OSUA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:D6","text":"Synthesis enables a simple ego representation to encompass a manifold without containing that manifold as intrinsic content.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A7","CH_B337_OSUA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:D7","text":"The operations of the understanding, including logic and transcendental philosophy, must be connected to synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A10","CH_B337_OSUA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:D8","text":"The a priori unity of self-consciousness is logically prior to determinate thought but materially dependent on intuition for its manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A2","CH_B337_OSUA:A8","CH_B337_OSUA:A13","CH_B337_OSUA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SD1","text":"The formal identity of the subject is established across a manifold through spontaneous synthesis rather than constituted by an inventory of empirical contents.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A3","CH_B337_OSUA:A6","CH_B337_OSUA:A7","CH_B337_OSUA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SD2","text":"Human cognition has a composite architecture in which intuition supplies heterogeneity, understanding supplies a priori conjunction, and apperception supplies unity.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A2","CH_B337_OSUA:A4","CH_B337_OSUA:A10","CH_B337_OSUA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SD3","text":"The explanatory priority of the system lies with the conditions that unify representations rather than with the contents that are unified.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A8","CH_B337_OSUA:A9","CH_B337_OSUA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SD4","text":"Synthetical unity becomes a totalizing condition for representation, concept-formation, logic, and transcendental cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A1","CH_B337_OSUA:A10","CH_B337_OSUA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SD5","text":"The “I” functions less as a content-bearing object than as the formal activity through which a manifold can belong to one consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A3","CH_B337_OSUA:A7","CH_B337_OSUA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SD6","text":"The system forms a reciprocal structure in which self-conscious identity requires synthesis while synthesis is organized under the unity of apperception.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A5","CH_B337_OSUA:A7","CH_B337_OSUA:A8","CH_B337_OSUA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:DNX1","text":"The subject cannot be grounded in the mere inventory of its conscious representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:DNX2","text":"Co-presence of representations does not constitute unity without an operation of conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:DNX3","text":"A common feature does not become a general concept without possible synthetic relation to other representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:DNX4","text":"Objects cannot serve as the ultimate source of the unity through which they are conceptually represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:DNX5","text":"A contentless ego cannot by itself explain the unity of the manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:DNX6","text":"Spontaneous thought without receptive intuition cannot constitute human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:DNX7","text":"The transcendental condition of unity cannot be reduced to the psychological fact of being conscious.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SNX1","text":"The subject cannot be modeled as the sum or succession of its representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SNX2","text":"Consciousness cannot be treated as a passive container in which relations among representations are already given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SNX3","text":"The manifold cannot be assumed to carry its own conceptual unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SNX4","text":"Pure self-consciousness cannot be treated as an intuitive presentation of the self’s manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SNX5","text":"The specific determination of spontaneous synthesis is not derived from objects and remains formally assigned to the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:SNX6","text":"The psychological or temporal genesis of apperceptive unity is excluded from the argument’s explanatory ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:FGS1","text":"Intuition gives a manifold prior to determinate thought, thereby supplying the material requiring unification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:FGS2","text":"The possible accompaniment of the “I think” makes the manifold available as representation for the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:FGS3","text":"Spontaneous pure apperception establishes one identical pole across the manifold without supplying manifold content itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:FGS4","text":"The understanding synthesizes and conjoins the manifold a priori under the unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:FGS5","text":"Synthetic conjunction makes it possible to regard the manifold as belonging to one self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:FGS6","text":"The possibility of synthetic unity grounds analytical unity, conceptuality, logic, and a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:FGS7","text":"Because human understanding is not intuitive, the synthetic operation remains dependent on intuition supplied through sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AF1","text":"The argument moves from the givenness of a manifold in intuition to the problem of its belonging to one subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AF2","text":"It moves from that problem to the necessary possible accompaniment of the “I think” and the distinction between pure and empirical apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AF3","text":"It moves from formal identity to synthesis as the condition for calling representations the subject’s own.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AF4","text":"It moves from synthesis to the understanding’s a priori activity and away from object-borrowed conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AF5","text":"It moves from this activity to the status of synthetical unity as the highest principle of concepts, logic, and cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:AF6","text":"It concludes by restricting human understanding to thought dependent on sensory intuition rather than intuitive self-givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:ME1","text":"Self-consciousness is reclassified from an empirical accompaniment of representations into a transcendental condition of their belonging to a subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:ME2","text":"Analytical unity is subordinated to synthetic unity rather than treated as an independently sufficient identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:ME3","text":"Unity is relocated from objects and perception to the spontaneous activity of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:ME4","text":"The subject is reclassified as a formal unifying function rather than as a manifold-bearing content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:ME5","text":"Concept-formation, logic, and transcendental philosophy are placed within one apperceptive architecture.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:ME6","text":"Human cognition is mapped as the interaction of receptivity and spontaneity rather than as either pure sensation or self-generating intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:ALT1","text":"Surface — Representations, intuition, the “I think,” and the distinction between empirical and pure apperception are the immediate objects of analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:ALT2","text":"Structural — Sensibility supplies the manifold, while the understanding synthesizes it under one self-consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument reverses the explanatory order by treating synthesis as the condition of identity rather than identity as a mere accompaniment of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — A priori priority denotes transcendental or logical dependence rather than temporal emergence; human cognition remains finite because thought requires an intuition supplied by sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument from the necessity of the “I think” to the primacy of synthetical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the decisive movement from empirical fragmentation to transcendental synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent if analytical unity and synthetic unity are treated as distinct levels of unity rather than as competing explanations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:MRT4","text":"The main unresolved fault line is that the necessary unity of apperception is called analytical while its explanatory force depends on synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:MRT5","text":"The argument names the understanding as the source of synthesis without deriving the specific rules or forms by which synthesis occurs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:MRT6","text":"The elevation of synthetical unity to the highest principle is functionally supported by its universal role but not independently demonstrated beyond that role.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:MRT7","text":"The reduction must preserve the distinction between actual consciousness of synthesis and the merely presupposed possibility of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_OSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_OSUA:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement of the text, but it leaves the transition from general synthetic unity to determinate concepts formally underdeveloped.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 31","title":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12","context":"Chapter 31"}},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR","layer":"CH","title":"Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.","order":50,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"19c36942cbef8fbc24c64e740fc95fe45044f78ab8a76a3db7bdc327c1de95c6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:AS1","text":"The passage addresses how a manifold supplied in sensuous intuition can count as conjoined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:AS2","text":"It separates receptive givenness from the spontaneous activity that produces conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:AS3","text":"It identifies conjunction as synthesis performed by the understanding and required for representing an object as conjoined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:AS4","text":"It makes analysis derivative by showing that decomposition presupposes a prior synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:AS5","text":"It distinguishes the synthetic unity within conjunction from the prior unity that makes conjunction possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:AS6","text":"It is a transcendental reconstruction that moves from sensory insufficiency to spontaneous synthesis and then to a higher qualitative ground of logical understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A1","text":"Sensuous intuition supplies the manifold content of representations through receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A2","text":"The form of sensuous intuition can exist a priori as the subject’s mode of being affected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A3","text":"Conjunction cannot be given by the senses or contained in the pure form of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A4","text":"Conjunction is a spontaneous act of the faculty of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A5","text":"Every conjunction, whether conscious or unconscious and whether involving intuitions or conceptions, is an act of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A6","text":"Synthesis is the general name for the understanding’s act of conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A7","text":"Representing an object as conjoined presupposes that the subject has previously conjoined its representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A8","text":"The possibility of conjunction is grounded in the nature of the understanding’s spontaneous act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A9","text":"Analysis presupposes a prior synthesis performed by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A10","text":"Conjunction is the representation of the synthetic unity of a manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A11","text":"A unity that precedes conjunction combines with the manifold and makes conjunction possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A12","text":"The prior unity that makes conjunction possible is not identical with the category of unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A13","text":"The category of unity presupposes conjunction because categories arise through logical functions of judgment in which conjunction is already operative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:A14","text":"A higher qualitative unity grounds the unity of diverse conceptions in judgment and therefore grounds the possibility of the understanding’s logical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:NX1","text":"Conjunction is not a product of sensory receptivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:NX2","text":"Conjunction is not contained in the pure form of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:NX3","text":"The representation of conjunction is not given through objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:NX4","text":"Conjunction is not restricted to conscious combinations of sensory intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:NX5","text":"Unity does not arise as a result of conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:NX6","text":"The prior unity enabling conjunction is not the category of unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:NX7","text":"The category of unity is not the ultimate ground of conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:NX8","text":"Analysis is not an operation independent of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:NX9","text":"The passage does not decide whether one representation can be thought analytically through another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:D1","text":"Sensibility supplies the manifold and the understanding supplies its conjunction, establishing a division between receptivity and spontaneity.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A1","CH_B337_PCMR:A3","CH_B337_PCMR:A4","CH_B337_PCMR:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:D2","text":"Synthesis is the universal form of conjunction across intuitions, conceptions, and levels of consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A5","CH_B337_PCMR:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:D3","text":"Objectual conjunction is intelligible only through a prior subjective synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A6","CH_B337_PCMR:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:D4","text":"The possibility of analysis depends on a previously constituted field of synthetic unity.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A8","CH_B337_PCMR:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:D5","text":"The unity represented in conjunction is a condition of conjunction rather than a mere outcome of it.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A10","CH_B337_PCMR:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:D6","text":"The category of unity cannot explain the original possibility of conjunction because its own operation already presupposes conjunction.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A12","CH_B337_PCMR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:D7","text":"The ground of logical judgment must be sought at a level more fundamental than the categories.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A13","CH_B337_PCMR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:SD1","text":"Cognition is structurally layered: sensibility provides the manifold and form of affection, while understanding produces the conjunction through which the manifold can be represented as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A1","CH_B337_PCMR:A2","CH_B337_PCMR:A4","CH_B337_PCMR:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:SD2","text":"Objectivity is not constituted by sensory givenness alone; it requires a subject-originated synthesis that organizes the given manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A3","CH_B337_PCMR:A6","CH_B337_PCMR:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:SD3","text":"Logical unity is derivative rather than foundational because judgment and the categories operate only within a unity already established by synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A10","CH_B337_PCMR:A12","CH_B337_PCMR:A13","CH_B337_PCMR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:SD4","text":"The understanding is functionally spontaneous but transcendentally dependent: it generates conjunction while depending on a prior unity for the possibility of that generation.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A4","CH_B337_PCMR:A8","CH_B337_PCMR:A11","CH_B337_PCMR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:SD5","text":"The system generalizes synthesis across the entire field of representation while preserving the distinction between the manifold received from sensibility and the unity produced by understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A1","CH_B337_PCMR:A5","CH_B337_PCMR:A6","CH_B337_PCMR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:DNX1","text":"A merely receptive account of cognition is structurally excluded because receptivity can provide a manifold but not its conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:DNX2","text":"An object-first account in which conjunction is simply delivered by objects is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:DNX3","text":"A purely logical account in which the category of unity produces the original unity of representation is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:DNX4","text":"An analytic or atomistic account in which decomposition precedes combination is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:DNX5","text":"A homogeneous account of unity is excluded because represented synthetic unity, categorical unity, and higher qualitative unity are not identified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:SNX1","text":"The ultimate nature of the higher qualitative unity that grounds conjunction and logical understanding remains unspecified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:SNX2","text":"The spontaneous activity of understanding is not absolutely self-grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:SNX3","text":"The passage does not fully formalize how the prior qualitative unity grounds the spontaneous act of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:SNX4","text":"The relation between distinguishable consciousness and possible analytic identity remains deliberately unintegrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:FGS1","text":"Sensuous affection yields a manifold of intuition, while the form of intuition can be a priori as the subject’s mode of being affected.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A1","CH_B337_PCMR:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:FGS2","text":"Because sensory receptivity does not supply conjunction, conjunction must originate through spontaneous representation.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A3","CH_B337_PCMR:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:FGS3","text":"Spontaneous conjunction is assigned to the understanding and named synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A5","CH_B337_PCMR:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:FGS4","text":"Synthesis makes it possible to represent the manifold as conjoined in an object.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A6","CH_B337_PCMR:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:FGS5","text":"Since conjunction represents synthetic unity, its possibility requires a unity that precedes the conjunction it enables.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A10","CH_B337_PCMR:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:FGS6","text":"Since categorical unity already operates within judgmental conjunction, the category of unity cannot provide the ultimate ground of conjunction.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A12","CH_B337_PCMR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:FGS7","text":"The explanatory sequence therefore ascends from sensuous manifold to synthesis, from synthesis to synthetic unity, and from synthetic unity to a higher qualitative unity grounding logical understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A10","CH_B337_PCMR:A11","CH_B337_PCMR:A13","CH_B337_PCMR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:AF1","text":"Manifold given in sensibility → conjunction denied to sensibility → spontaneous synthesis assigned to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:AF2","text":"Subjective synthesis required for objectual conjunction → synthesis generalized across representation → analysis subordinated to synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:AF3","text":"Synthetic unity identified as the condition of conjunction → categorical unity displaced as insufficient → higher qualitative unity posited as the ground of logical understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:ME1","text":"Conjunction is relocated from sensory or objectual givenness to the spontaneous activity of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:ME2","text":"The field of cognition is divided into received material and actively produced unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:ME3","text":"Objectual conjunction is reclassified as dependent on subjective synthesis without reducing the manifold itself to subjective production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:ME4","text":"Analysis is reclassified as a secondary operation within an already synthesized representational field.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:ME5","text":"The category of unity is displaced from ultimate foundation to derivative logical function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, the issue is the conjunction of a manifold of representations given in sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, the issue is the relation between receptivity, spontaneity, synthesis, and unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, the argument reverses the explanatory direction by deriving objectual and logical conjunction from prior subjective synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:ALT4","text":"At the transcendental level, the argument posits an a priori qualitative unity that grounds the possibility of conjunction and the logical use of understanding without identifying that unity with a category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the passage’s central movement from sensuous manifold to spontaneous synthesis and higher unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:MRT2","text":"The nature of the higher qualitative unity remains underdetermined because the passage identifies its function but does not characterize its internal constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent if unity is stratified into synthetic unity, categorical unity, and higher qualitative unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:MRT4","text":"The strongest link is the dependence of objectual conjunction and analysis on prior synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:MRT5","text":"The main compression concerns the relation between the subject’s spontaneous act and the prior unity that makes that act possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PCMR:MRT6","text":"The unresolved fault line is that conjunction depends on spontaneous understanding while the possibility of understanding depends on a unity prior to conjunction; the passage stratifies this dependence without fully resolving it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PCMR:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 30","title":"Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11.","context":"Chapter 30"}},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA","layer":"CH","title":"The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13","order":51,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"665de29b907d36e8f61ad96b829e13a93dae1d110a03d127097eef5f98694ff8","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AS1","text":"The problem is how a manifold merely given in intuition becomes thinkable, cognizable, and related to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AS2","text":"Sensibility supplies the formal conditions of givenness through space and time, while understanding supplies the condition of conjunction through original synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AS3","text":"Representations become connectable only through their conjunction in one consciousness under the formal identity of “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AS4","text":"Because an object is a unified manifold, unity of consciousness is the objective condition of object-relation, objective validity, cognition, and the possibility of understanding itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AS5","text":"The principle is analytically stated but synthetically operative: it expresses the condition through which representations become one cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AS6","text":"The principle governs finite human understanding, while an understanding that intuited its own manifold would not require the same act of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A1","text":"Sensibility gives a manifold of representations under the forms of space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A2","text":"The manifold in intuition must be subject to the original synthetical unity of apperception for understanding to operate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A3","text":"A representation can be thought or cognized only if it can be conjoined in one consciousness under “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A4","text":"An object is the concept in which the manifold of a given intuition is united.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A5","text":"Every union of representations requires unity of consciousness in their synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A6","text":"Unity of consciousness constitutes the possibility of representations relating to an object, possessing objective validity, becoming cognitions, and instantiating understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A7","text":"The principle of original synthetical unity of apperception is the first pure cognition of understanding and the ground of all its other exercise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A8","text":"Space and time provide the manifold of possible cognition but do not by themselves produce cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A9","text":"Every intuition must be subject to synthetic unity in order to become an object for human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A10","text":"Synthetic unity connects representations with an identical self through the formal expression “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A11","text":"Human understanding thinks but does not intuit, and its pure apperception contains no manifold content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A12","text":"An understanding that contained the manifold in intuition through its own self-conscious activity would not require a special act of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:A13","text":"Human understanding cannot positively conceive an understanding that is itself intuition or possesses sensuous forms other than space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:NX1","text":"Space and time are not sufficient for cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:NX2","text":"A given manifold is not yet an object or a cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:NX3","text":"Representations cannot become connected without their conjunction in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:NX4","text":"Unity of consciousness is not merely a subjective accompaniment of cognition; it is an objective condition of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:NX5","text":"Synthetic unity is not derived from the manifold it unifies; it is primitive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:NX6","text":"The principle of synthetical unity is not a principle for every possible understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:NX7","text":"Human understanding is not an intuiting understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:NX8","text":"An understanding that intuited its own manifold would not require the human act of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:NX9","text":"Space and time are not pure conceptions; they are intuitions containing a manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:D1","text":"The conditions of intuition and understanding are distinct but complementary: space and time organize givenness, while synthetical unity organizes thinkability.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A1","CH_B337_PSUA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:D2","text":"Object cognition arises through the unification of a manifold rather than through the manifold’s mere reception.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A3","CH_B337_PSUA:A4","CH_B337_PSUA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:D3","text":"Objective validity and the possibility of understanding depend on the same unity of consciousness that connects representations to objects.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A5","CH_B337_PSUA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:D4","text":"A spatial form becomes cognition only through a determinate synthesis that unifies the manifold and the act of consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A7","CH_B337_PSUA:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:D5","text":"The condition for cognizing an object is also the condition under which an intuition can become an object for the subject.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A8","CH_B337_PSUA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:D6","text":"“I think” functions as the formal identity through which representations are recognized as belonging to one consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A3","CH_B337_PSUA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:D7","text":"The need for synthesis marks human understanding as finite and discursive rather than intuitive.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A11","CH_B337_PSUA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:D8","text":"The foundational principle of understanding is independent in origin from sensuous intuition while governing the cognitive use of sensibly given intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A7","CH_B337_PSUA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:SD1","text":"Human cognition is generated by the conjunction of received manifold and active synthesis; neither sensibility nor understanding alone yields cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A1","CH_B337_PSUA:A2","CH_B337_PSUA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:SD2","text":"Objectivity is a structural effect of unification rather than a primitive property of individual representations.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A4","CH_B337_PSUA:A5","CH_B337_PSUA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:SD3","text":"Human cognition is doubly conditioned: space and time govern the form of givenness, while apperception governs the unity required for objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A1","CH_B337_PSUA:A2","CH_B337_PSUA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:SD4","text":"The identity of the knower functions as a formal condition of cognition rather than as an additional manifold content within cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A10","CH_B337_PSUA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:SD5","text":"The first principle of human understanding simultaneously grounds its operations and marks the boundary of its finitude.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A7","CH_B337_PSUA:A11","CH_B337_PSUA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:SD6","text":"The principle has universal force within human cognition without establishing a universal law for every conceivable intellect.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A6","CH_B337_PSUA:A12","CH_B337_PSUA:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:DNX1","text":"No bare givenness is admitted as cognition without an act of unification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:DNX2","text":"No isolated representation is admitted as objectively valid without its relation to one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:DNX3","text":"No purely sensuous condition can generate objectivity by itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:DNX4","text":"Unity is not reducible to an aggregate produced by the representations that it unifies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:DNX5","text":"The human condition of cognition cannot be silently universalized into a theory of every possible understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:DNX6","text":"Human thought cannot overcome its dependence on synthesis by becoming intuition through thought alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:SNX1","text":"Unmediated cognition of an object is excluded for the finite human knower.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:SNX2","text":"A human object is not available independently of the conditions under which its manifold can be unified in consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:SNX3","text":"A human consciousness whose unity contained its manifold directly is excluded as a positive cognitive possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:SNX4","text":"An intuitive understanding remains a limiting contrast that human cognition cannot positively reconstruct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:SNX5","text":"The possibility that objectivity could precede the unity relating representations to an object is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:FGS1","text":"Manifold given → space and time formalize intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:FGS2","text":"Intuition given → representations require conjunction in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:FGS3","text":"Conjunction required → “I think” supplies formal identity across representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:FGS4","text":"Unified manifold → representation relates to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:FGS5","text":"Object-relation established → objective validity and cognition become possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:FGS6","text":"Cognition possible → the operations of human understanding become possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:FGS7","text":"Human understanding distinguished from intuitive understanding → synthesis becomes a specifically finite condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AF1","text":"Manifold of intuition under space and time → distinction between givenness and cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AF2","text":"Need for conjunction → original synthetical unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AF3","text":"Unity under “I think” → relation of representations to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AF4","text":"Object-relation → objective validity, cognition, and the possibility of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AF5","text":"Universal application within human cognition → first principle of all human understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:AF6","text":"Contrast with intuitive understanding → restriction of the principle to finite, non-intuitive understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:ME1","text":"Unity of consciousness is reclassified from a subjective accompaniment into an objective condition of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:ME2","text":"The object is reclassified from a merely given manifold into a unified manifold constituted for cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:ME3","text":"“I think” is reclassified from an introspective utterance into the formal operator of representational unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:ME4","text":"Space and time are separated from cognition proper and assigned the role of supplying its necessary manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:ME5","text":"Human cognitive finitude is made visible as the gap between receiving intuition and synthesizing it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:ME6","text":"The scope of the principle is redrawn: absolute for human understanding, non-universal across all conceivable understandings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:ALT1","text":"Surface — The manifold of intuition must be united in one consciousness to become cognizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:ALT2","text":"Structural — Objectivity is generated by the synthesis that relates a manifold to an identical consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument distinguishes the conditions of human cognition from claims about every possible form of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Cognition concerns a manifold given through the forms of space and time, but objecthood requires an additional synthetic act that orders this manifold for consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the argument from sensuous givenness through synthesis to objectivity and cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:MRT2","text":"The strongest link is the derivation of objective validity from the unity required to connect representations in one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if primitive unity is distinguished from the synthetic act through which that unity is enacted in cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:MRT4","text":"It remains underdetermined whether the argument concerns only objects as cognizable for humans or also makes claims about objects independent of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:MRT5","text":"The claim that the principle is analytic while constituting synthetical unity is resolved only by distinguishing its mode of statement from its operative function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:MRT6","text":"The relation between primitive unity and performed synthesis is asserted as necessary but not fully explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:MRT7","text":"The principle is called supreme and first while being restricted to human understanding; its generality therefore depends on maintaining that scope distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_PSUA:MRT8","text":"The final limitation depends on the asserted inability of human understanding to form a positive conception of intuitive understanding, which functions as an epistemic closure rather than a demonstrated model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_PSUA:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 32","title":"The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13","context":"Chapter 32"}},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC","layer":"CH","title":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23","order":52,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"ec51df19673638659c6f4a80d2a5072507d3f04a9b0b35d3d5576a23ce6c0947","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:AS1","text":"The problem is to explain how the categories possess necessary objective validity without being derived from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:AS2","text":"Human object-cognition requires both categories and corresponding sensuous intuitions, so cognition of given objects is empirical experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:AS3","text":"Empirical cognition is not wholly produced by experience because pure intuitions and pure concepts contribute a priori elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:AS4","text":"The necessary harmony between experience and its concepts must arise either from experience or from the concepts’ making experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:AS5","text":"The categories’ a priori status excludes empirical derivation, while a preformation account reduces necessity to arbitrary subjective constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:AS6","text":"The deduction therefore grounds the possibility of experience in the categories, limits object-cognition to possible experience, and transfers the explanation of categorical application to transcendental judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A1","text":"An object can be thought only through the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A2","text":"A thought can be cognized only through a corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A3","text":"Human intuition is sensuous; therefore cognition insofar as its object is given is empirical cognition, or experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A4","text":"Human a priori cognition of objects is possible only with respect to objects of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A5","text":"Experience contains a priori elements, including pure intuitions and pure concepts of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A6","text":"Necessary harmony between experience and the concepts of its objects can arise either from experience or from the concepts’ making experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A7","text":"The categories are a priori concepts independent of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A8","text":"Necessity belongs essentially to the concept of a category.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A9","text":"A merely subjective aptitude for combining representations would make categorical necessity arbitrary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A10","text":"Objective validity requires necessity in the object, not merely an inability of a subject to think otherwise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A11","text":"The possibility of experience is connected to the original synthetical unity of apperception, understanding, time, and space as forms of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:A12","text":"The principles governing the application of the categories to phenomena belong to the transcendental exercise of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:NX1","text":"The categories alone do not yield cognition of an object without intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:NX2","text":"The categories are not restricted in their sphere of thought to the conditions of sensuous intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:NX3","text":"Human a priori object-cognition does not extend beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:NX4","text":"The categories are not empirical products derived from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:NX5","text":"Experience is not the complete source of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:NX6","text":"The categories are not merely implanted subjective aptitudes harmonized with nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:NX7","text":"Objective necessity is not reducible to the subject’s contingent constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:NX8","text":"The present deduction does not treat reason’s non-cognitive determination of the subject or its volition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:D1","text":"Since the categories are a priori and necessary harmony has only two possible grounds, the categories must contribute the conditions under which experience is possible.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A5","CH_B337_RTDC:A6","CH_B337_RTDC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:D2","text":"Empirical cognition can be experience without being derived entirely from experience.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A3","CH_B337_RTDC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:D3","text":"The categories possess objective validity only by functioning as conditions of possible experience rather than as private rules of association.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A8","CH_B337_RTDC:A9","CH_B337_RTDC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:D4","text":"Reducing causal necessity to subjective constitution produces skepticism about the objective validity of judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A8","CH_B337_RTDC:A9","CH_B337_RTDC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:D5","text":"The domain of valid a priori object-cognition is bounded by possible experience, even though categorical thought itself can exceed sensuous intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A1","CH_B337_RTDC:A2","CH_B337_RTDC:A3","CH_B337_RTDC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:D6","text":"The deduction establishes the ground of categorical validity without yet specifying the principles of categorical application to phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A11","CH_B337_RTDC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:D7","text":"Thought without intuition may have consequences for the subject’s exercise of reason, but it does not thereby become cognition of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A2","CH_B337_RTDC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:SD1","text":"A priori validity is relocated from empirical acquisition to the understanding’s contribution to the conditions of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A5","CH_B337_RTDC:A6","CH_B337_RTDC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:SD2","text":"Experience is constituted through the conjunction of sensuous givenness, a priori intuition, categorical synthesis, and apperceptive unity.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A3","CH_B337_RTDC:A5","CH_B337_RTDC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:SD3","text":"Objectivity is defined as necessary validity within the field of possible experience rather than as validity independent of the conditions of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A4","CH_B337_RTDC:A8","CH_B337_RTDC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:SD4","text":"The distinction between thought and cognition permits the categories an unbounded sphere of thought while denying them autonomous object-determining power without intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A1","CH_B337_RTDC:A2","CH_B337_RTDC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:SD5","text":"Skepticism is generated by severing necessity from the conditions that make objective experience possible.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A8","CH_B337_RTDC:A9","CH_B337_RTDC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:SD6","text":"The deduction terminates in a transition from establishing categorical validity to explaining categorical employment in judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A11","CH_B337_RTDC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:DNX1","text":"No brute empirical regularity can account for the necessity expressed by the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:DNX2","text":"No external guarantee, including a creator’s arrangement of the mind, can replace a transcendental ground of necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:DNX3","text":"No private constitution of the subject can secure intersubjective objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:DNX4","text":"No pure conceptual determination of an object is possible apart from the receptive conditions of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:DNX5","text":"No equivalence holds between the ability to think an object and the ability to cognize it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:DNX6","text":"No conclusion about objects beyond possible experience follows from the categories’ capacity for thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:DNX7","text":"No complete account of categorical application is contained in the elementary deduction alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a standpoint outside the conditions of possible experience from which objects could be cognized a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:SNX2","text":"The system excludes any source of necessity external to the relation among understanding, intuition, and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:SNX3","text":"The system excludes the collapse of thinkability into cognizability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:SNX4","text":"The system leaves unformalized, within this unit, the exact mechanism by which the categories generate the possibility of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:SNX5","text":"The system excludes a completed theory of the categories’ practical or volitional use from the present deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:FGS1","text":"Object-thought requires categories, while object-cognition requires corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:FGS2","text":"Sensuous intuition makes cognition of given objects empirical and therefore experiential.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:FGS3","text":"The presence of pure intuitions and pure concepts separates the conditions of cognition from its empirical content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:FGS4","text":"Necessary harmony between concepts and experience is reduced to two alternatives: empirical derivation or transcendental conditioning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:FGS5","text":"The categories’ a priori status eliminates empirical derivation and selects the thesis that the categories make experience possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:FGS6","text":"The preformation alternative is rejected because subjective aptitude converts necessity into contingent constitution and thereby produces skepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:FGS7","text":"Objective validity is grounded in the categories’ role as principles of possible experience, connected with apperceptive synthesis and the forms of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:FGS8","text":"The deduction then shifts from the ground of categorical validity to the determination of categorical employment in judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:AF1","text":"Required conditions of cognition → categories plus intuition → sensuous object-givenness → empirical experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:AF2","text":"Empirical cognition with a priori elements → dilemma of empirical origin versus transcendental conditioning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:AF3","text":"A priori status of categories → rejection of empirical derivation → selection of categories as conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:AF4","text":"Preformation hypothesis → loss of necessity → reduction to subjective constitution → skeptical consequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:AF5","text":"Categories as principles of possible experience → objective validity within experience → transition to transcendental judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:ME1","text":"The source of a priori cognition is reclassified from prior acquisition to conditions of possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:ME2","text":"Experience is reclassified from the origin of categorical form to the field constituted through categorical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:ME3","text":"Thought and cognition become distinct domains: thought can exceed intuition, while object-cognition cannot.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:ME4","text":"Objective validity is detached from both empirical induction and arbitrary subjective constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:ME5","text":"Skepticism is reclassified as the consequence of an inadequate account of necessity rather than as an unavoidable result of finite cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:ME6","text":"The deduction’s endpoint is displaced from elementary concepts to their systematic application through judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines the relations among categories, intuitions, objects, and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:ALT2","text":"Structural — The categories function as conditions of the possibility of experience, while sensibility supplies the conditions for object-givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument defeats empiricism, preformationism, and skepticism by converting the question of origin into a question of transcendental function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human cognition is bounded by possible experience, whose form involves space and time, while its unity is grounded in original synthesis and apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central deduction from the requirements of cognition to the categories as conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:MRT2","text":"The system is internally coherent if object-cognition is distinguished from thought without intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:MRT3","text":"The strongest inferential hinge is the conjunction of the categories’ a priori status with the exclusive two-way account of necessary harmony.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:MRT4","text":"The principal underdetermination is the exact account of how the categories make experience possible; the text explicitly defers this to the following treatment of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:MRT5","text":"The principal structural tension is that objective necessity must arise through the subject’s cognitive faculties without becoming merely subjective necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_RTDC:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the real movement from cognitive conditions, through alternative grounds and rejected models, to transcendental grounding and methodological transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_RTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 40","title":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23","context":"Chapter 40"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being","order":53,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"d567754a41a412fe04eca67710d3e62f1806251088322007ea2154fcf7e45694","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AS1","text":"Reason seeks an unconditioned ground because conditioned existence cannot satisfy its demand for complete explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AS2","text":"Human reason begins from empirical existence, ascends through causality, and posits a necessary being as the terminus of regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AS3","text":"Reason identifies the unconditioned with the conception of complete reality and converts complete reality into the idea of a supreme unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AS4","text":"The conversion from the need for a necessary being to the determination of that being as ens realissimum lacks objective warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AS5","text":"Practical necessity may give the deficient inference subjective authority without supplying speculative proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AS6","text":"All speculative proofs reduce to three paths—physico-theological, cosmological, and ontological—which are to be tested from their transcendental basis downward.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:A1","text":"Every contingent existence is thought as dependent upon a cause or condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:A2","text":"The regress from conditioned existence demands an unconditioned ground if it is to be complete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:A3","text":"The necessity of a ground for explanatory reason does not establish the objective existence of a being conceived by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:A4","text":"A conception can be associated with absolute necessity only if it contains no contradiction with unconditioned existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:A5","text":"The conception of all reality presents itself as the most adequate available conception of an unconditioned condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:A6","text":"Complete reality is represented as absolute unity and thereby as one supreme being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:A7","text":"Failure to cognize necessity in a limited being does not prove that the being is contingent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:A8","text":"No inference from the concept of a being to its objective existence is valid merely because that concept is required by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:A9","text":"Practical obligations conditioned by the supposition of a Supreme Being can rationally outweigh speculative doubt without removing theoretical insufficiency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:A10","text":"Every speculative proof of a Deity proceeds either from determinate experience, indeterminate empirical existence, or pure a priori concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:A11","text":"The empirical proofs are ultimately directed and governed by the transcendental idea of an unconditioned being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:NX1","text":"The required unconditioned ground is not established as an actual being merely because reason can conceive it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:NX2","text":"Absolute necessity is not demonstrably restricted to the being that contains all reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:NX3","text":"The absence of an intelligible mark of necessity in finite beings is not proof of their conditioned status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:NX4","text":"The ens realissimum is not known to satisfy every requirement of absolute necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:NX5","text":"The cosmological ascent is not a proof of the determinate properties of a necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:NX6","text":"Practical endorsement of a Supreme Being is not speculative cognition of its existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:NX7","text":"No fourth speculative route to divine existence is available beyond the empirical and a priori modes enumerated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:D1","text":"The causal regress can justify reason’s search for an unconditioned ground without entitling it to infer that any particular concept objectively exists.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A1","CH_B337_SIAE:A2","CH_B337_SIAE:A3","CH_B337_SIAE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:D2","text":"The identification of necessary being with complete reality is a selection under conceptual scarcity rather than a demonstrative determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A4","CH_B337_SIAE:A5","CH_B337_SIAE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:D3","text":"The transition from all reality to a single Supreme Being adds unity and supremacy to a concept initially selected only as an apparent condition of completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A5","CH_B337_SIAE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:D4","text":"The cosmological argument cannot establish the attributes of its alleged necessary being even if its initial ascent from contingent existence is conceded.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A1","CH_B337_SIAE:A2","CH_B337_SIAE:A7","CH_B337_SIAE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:D5","text":"Practical reason can convert speculative indecision into rational assent only by adding an interest external to theoretical proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A3","CH_B337_SIAE:A8","CH_B337_SIAE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:D6","text":"The physico-theological and cosmological proofs require examination through the transcendental idea that already determines their intended conclusion.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A10","CH_B337_SIAE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:SD1","text":"Speculative theology is generated by reason’s demand for explanatory closure, but that demand cannot validate the object posited as its closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:SD2","text":"The concept of a Supreme Being functions as a regulative terminus of causal explanation before it can function as a constitutively known object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:SD3","text":"The apparent plurality of proofs masks a dependency hierarchy in which empirical argument is guided by the same transcendental ideal that it cannot prove.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:SD4","text":"The argument separates the subjective inevitability of the idea of God from the objective validity of an existence claim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:SD5","text":"Practical postulation can preserve the operational authority of the Supreme Being after speculative reason has lost the right to assert its cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:DNX1","text":"Explanatory completeness is not identical with ontological completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:DNX2","text":"The logical demand that every wherefore have a therefore is not a warrant for asserting a being that contains all conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:DNX3","text":"Conceptual maximality is not a criterion of real existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:DNX4","text":"A necessary existence cannot be cognized through the mere comparative superiority of one possible concept over others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:DNX5","text":"Empirical ascent cannot escape transcendental dependence by beginning from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:SNX1","text":"The system disallows any speculative coincidence between the structure of reason’s need and the structure of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses a theoretical standpoint from which the unconditioned can be both required as an explanatory end and known as an existing object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a final speculative answer to the question of why there is a necessary being rather than only a rational demand to seek one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:FGS1","text":"Contingent existence → causal dependence → regress of conditions → demand for unconditioned ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:FGS2","text":"Demand for unconditioned ground → search for a concept compatible with necessity → selection of complete reality as the least inadequate candidate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:FGS3","text":"Complete reality → absolute unity → representation of a single Supreme Being → apparent determination of necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:FGS4","text":"Failure to find necessity in limited concepts → illicit exclusion of their possible necessity → collapse of the determination of the necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:FGS5","text":"Speculative insufficiency → practical need for effective moral law → conditional rational assent without theoretical proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:FGS6","text":"Three possible proof-sources → transcendental idea governing empirical ascent → reversal of exposition from ontological to empirical critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AF1","text":"Conditioned experience → demand for an unconditioned cause → necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AF2","text":"Necessary being → complete condition of possibility → ens realissimum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AF3","text":"Ens realissimum → absolute unity → Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AF4","text":"Conceptual preference → failure to exclude alternative necessary beings → indeterminacy of the alleged necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AF5","text":"Speculative indeterminacy → possible practical supplementation → non-theoretical assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:AF6","text":"Classification of proof-paths → exposure of their shared transcendental ground → ordered critique of speculative theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:ME1","text":"The question of divine existence is reorganized from a direct existence-claim into a problem of whether reason may convert explanatory necessity into objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:ME2","text":"The cosmological argument is reclassified as unable to determine its own necessary being, rather than as an independent proof proceeding securely from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:ME3","text":"The Supreme Being is divided into three non-equivalent statuses: a required explanatory idea, a selected concept of maximal reality, and a theoretically unproved existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:ME4","text":"Practical reason becomes visible as a distinct source of rational commitment rather than as supplementary evidence for speculative knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:ME5","text":"The three traditional proofs become a closed and reducible field whose empirical forms remain subordinate to transcendental presuppositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text tests arguments claiming to prove the existence of a Supreme Being through speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text exposes the inferential transition from contingent things, through necessary existence, to complete reality and supreme unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text reverses the apparent order of proof by treating empirical arguments as dependent upon the transcendental idea they appear to reach.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text distinguishes the ordered regress of conditioned causes from any cognizable possession of an unconditioned existent beyond that regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:ALT5","text":"Normative — The text allows practical rational obligation to require a supposition that speculative cognition cannot establish.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central movement from causal regress to the ideal of complete reality and the critique of that transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:MRT2","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning whether the initial inference from contingent existence to some necessary existence is accepted provisionally, strategically, or as independently valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent insofar as it separates reason’s demand for completion from its right to objective assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:MRT4","text":"The decisive pressure point is the unlicensed inference from the privileged conceivability of complete reality to the exclusion of all alternative necessary beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:MRT5","text":"The practical supplementation introduces a structural tension because rational assent is retained where speculative grounds are declared objectively insufficient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:MRT6","text":"The classification of all proofs as three exhaustive paths depends on the acceptance of the stated division between experience-based and purely a priori inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAE:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument only if the natural inevitability of the God-idea is not confused with its theoretical validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 76","title":"Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being","context":"Chapter 76"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason","order":54,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"0fba465e502ec8d0235396361fb36a656e4decdf5c7e954097a2fa4f6cb98475","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:AS1","text":"Pure reason necessarily generates empirically undecidable contradictions when it seeks an absolute world-totality beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:AS2","text":"Antithetic converts opposition between dogmas into a self-contradiction internal to reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:AS3","text":"The contradiction arises because reason requires absolute unity while understanding recognizes only rule-governed synthesis under empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:AS4","text":"Thesis and antithesis remain equally defensible because each expresses a necessary demand of reason without admitting experiential adjudication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:AS5","text":"The argument is transcendental, diagnostic, dialectical, and sceptical rather than dogmatically adjudicative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:AS6","text":"The movement proceeds from definition of antinomy through diagnosis of its cause and method of exposure to four paired cosmological conflicts and their standpoint-dependent consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:AS7","text":"The terminal aim is not victory for either side but disclosure of whether the disputed object is illusory and the resulting conflict harmless.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A1","text":"The objectively valid use of the principles of understanding is bounded by possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A2","text":"Pure reason necessarily demands the absolute unity and completeness of every conditioned synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A3","text":"Understanding admits synthesis only insofar as it proceeds according to determinate rules applicable to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A4","text":"A synthesis adequate to reason exceeds understanding, while a synthesis adequate to understanding fails to satisfy reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A5","text":"A transcendental antinomy consists of paired, self-consistent, rationally necessitated opposites between which neither experience nor reason can establish decided superiority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A6","text":"Transcendental illusion is natural, unavoidable, and persistent even after it ceases to deceive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A7","text":"Free conflict between opposed transcendental assertions is the available criterion for locating their misunderstanding and attaining critical certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A8","text":"Space and time are forms of sensible intuition rather than independent objects or self-subsisting determinants of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A9","text":"The cosmological world under examination is the phenomenal world governed by conditions of sensibility, whereas an intelligible world abstracted from those conditions supports no synthetic proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A10","text":"A regress from conditioned to condition must preserve the signification and domain governing the conditioned series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0031","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A11","text":"An infinite successive synthesis cannot be completed as a given totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A12","text":"A temporal beginning of the phenomenal world entails a preceding void time lacking any differentiating condition for origination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A13","text":"A spatial limit of the phenomenal world entails a relation to void space, which is a relation to no object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A14","text":"Substantial composition presupposes self-subsisting components that remain when composition is annihilated in thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A15","text":"Every spatial phenomenon contains an externally related manifold because every occupied spatial part is itself spatially divisible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A16","text":"Every event within nature is determined by a preceding condition according to causal law, and empirical unity depends on this uninterrupted lawful connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A17","text":"Transcendental freedom is causally unconditioned spontaneity initiating a phenomenal series, not necessarily a first event in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:A18","text":"Change establishes only empirical contingency within time, not intellectual contingency under pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0033","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX1","text":"Antithetic is not a dogmatic assertion of the opposite and is not an examination of one-sided propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX2","text":"A dialectical proposition does not answer an arbitrary question and does not produce a merely artificial illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX3","text":"Transcendental propositions are not empirically confirmable or empirically refutable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX4","text":"The sceptical method is not scepticism or the systematic destruction of confidence in knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX5","text":"The sceptical conflict is not instituted to decide immediately which combatant represents truth or right.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX6","text":"Transcendental conflict cannot be resolved by mathematical intuition, experimental testing, or exhibition of practical consequences in possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX7","text":"Infinity is not a greatest number or maximal quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX8","text":"Empty space and empty time are not independent objects capable of determining phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX9","text":"The sensible world cannot be replaced by an intelligible world in order to evade the conditions producing the antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX10","text":"The inference from composite to simple does not apply indiscriminately to space, time, states, accidents, or every possible composite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX11","text":"A pure concept of the simple cannot substitute for an intuition of a simple component within matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX12","text":"The bare representation of the thinking “I” does not prove that the represented subject is an objectively simple substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX13","text":"A causally primal action is not necessarily the first event in temporal succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX14","text":"Laws of freedom cannot replace laws of nature within the same empirical causal series, because law-determined freedom would become nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX15","text":"Temporal succession between contrary states does not prove the intellectual contingency of either state.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0033","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:NX16","text":"A cosmological regress cannot leap from empirical contingency to an extra-empirical necessary being through a change of categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D1","text":"The necessary pursuit of absolute completeness beyond possible experience produces questions that reason cannot avoid but experience cannot decide.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A1","CH_B337_SIAP:A2","CH_B337_SIAP:A4","CH_B337_SIAP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D2","text":"Equal rational support for contradictory claims converts their conflict into evidence of a misunderstanding concerning the disputed object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A5","CH_B337_SIAP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D3","text":"Under the standpoint of completed totality, an infinite elapsed past and an infinite coexistent world are impossible, so the world must have a temporal beginning and spatial limits.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A2","CH_B337_SIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D4","text":"Under the conditions of sensible intuition, a beginning or limit of the world requires void time or void space as a determining correlate; because neither is an object, the world can have neither beginning nor spatial limit.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A8","CH_B337_SIAP:A9","CH_B337_SIAP:A12","CH_B337_SIAP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D5","text":"If destruction of composition left no simple self-subsisting remainder, no substance would remain; therefore substantial composites require simple components.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D6","text":"A simple substantial part of a spatial phenomenon would have to occupy divisible space and would therefore contain a manifold, contradicting its simplicity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A8","CH_B337_SIAP:A9","CH_B337_SIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D7","text":"Monadological decomposition is defensible only if bodies are things in themselves; it fails for bodies considered as phenomena conditioned by space.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A8","CH_B337_SIAP:A9","CH_B337_SIAP:A14","CH_B337_SIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D8","text":"If every natural cause is itself temporally conditioned, the causal series never reaches completeness; reason therefore posits an unconditioned spontaneity capable of initiating a lawful series.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A2","CH_B337_SIAP:A16","CH_B337_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D9","text":"An empirically operative freedom would interrupt lawful causal continuity and destroy the unity by which experience is distinguished from visionary appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A3","CH_B337_SIAP:A16","CH_B337_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D10","text":"Transcendental freedom, where posited, begins a series with respect to causality while remaining temporally subsequent to earlier events.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D11","text":"The existence of change combined with reason’s demand for a complete series of conditions yields an absolutely necessary condition, which the thesis places within the sensible world because its causality must enter time.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A2","CH_B337_SIAP:A9","CH_B337_SIAP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0028","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D12","text":"No necessary being can terminate the phenomenal series from within without violating causal conditioning, or from outside without beginning to act in time and thereby entering the world.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A8","CH_B337_SIAP:A9","CH_B337_SIAP:A10","CH_B337_SIAP:A16","CH_B337_SIAP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D13","text":"A cosmological proof cannot determine an extra-worldly necessary being because such a conclusion changes an empirical condition into an intellectual condition without preserving the original relation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A10","CH_B337_SIAP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0031","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:D14","text":"The same temporal series yields a necessary unconditioned when viewed as an absolute totality and eliminates every unconditioned when viewed through the conditioning of each member.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A2","CH_B337_SIAP:A16","CH_B337_SIAP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:SD1","text":"Antinomy functions as a meta-criterion: reciprocal proof does not decide the object but reveals defects in the terms, standpoint, or domain of the question.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A5","CH_B337_SIAP:A7","CH_B337_SIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:SD2","text":"All four conflicts are generated by one recurrent structure: reason requires a completed unconditioned totality while empirical cognition supplies only conditioned synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A2","CH_B337_SIAP:A3","CH_B337_SIAP:A4","CH_B337_SIAP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:SD3","text":"Dogmatic victory is structurally positional rather than conclusive because whichever side makes the last attack appears victorious until the contrary proof is renewed.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A5","CH_B337_SIAP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:SD4","text":"The antitheses primarily restrict what can belong to nature or possible experience; they do not provide an unrestricted ontological disproof of every intelligible correlate.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A1","CH_B337_SIAP:A8","CH_B337_SIAP:A9","CH_B337_SIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0034","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:SD5","text":"Because transcendental illusion survives its diagnosis, critical certainty must consist in regulated non-deception rather than elimination of reason’s antinomial tendency.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A6","CH_B337_SIAP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:SD6","text":"The sequence of conflicts distributes the demand for the unconditioned across world-magnitude, substantial composition, causal origination, and existential necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A2","CH_B337_SIAP:A11","CH_B337_SIAP:A14","CH_B337_SIAP:A16","CH_B337_SIAP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:DNX1","text":"The world as an absolute completed whole must not be available as an object of possible experience; otherwise the opposed claims would admit empirical adjudication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:DNX2","text":"Logical consistency and rational necessity must not suffice for objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:DNX3","text":"Reason’s demand for an unconditioned condition must not itself prove the existence of a corresponding object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0028","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0029","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0030","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0035","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:DNX4","text":"No equivocation-free bridge may connect a sensible series to an intelligible ground while preserving the same causal or conditional relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0031","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:DNX5","text":"No phenomenal item can be absolutely first, absolutely simple, causally lawless, or absolutely necessary while remaining subject to sensibility and empirical law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:DNX6","text":"Critical certainty must not depend on the permanent disappearance of the illusion it diagnoses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:SNX1","text":"The absent center of the system is an experience of the world as absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:SNX2","text":"The foreclosed object is a phenomenal unconditioned: a sensible boundary, indivisible substance, law-independent cause, or necessary terminus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0029","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:SNX3","text":"The unavailable mediation is a valid synthetic passage from the conditioned sensible series to an intelligible ground beyond that series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:SNX4","text":"The terminal denial is that critique can abolish reason’s drive toward absolute completion; critique can only prevent that drive from determining illusory objects dogmatically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:FGS1","text":"Possible-experience boundary → reason’s demand for absolute completion → projection of a cosmological totality → equally necessary opposites → absence of empirical adjudication → sceptical conflict → diagnosis of misunderstanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:FGS2","text":"Totality standpoint → posit a completed series or unconditioned condition; member standpoint → condition every element and prohibit closure; identity of object plus divergence of standpoint → antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0035","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:FGS3","text":"Uncompletable infinite synthesis → temporal beginning and spatial limit; beginning or limit → void time or void space as non-object → no beginning and no limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:FGS4","text":"Annihilation of substantial composition → simple self-subsisting remainder; sensible spatial intuition → every part remains manifold and divisible → no simple phenomenal component.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:FGS5","text":"Demand for causal completeness → unconditioned spontaneity; demand for empirical unity → uninterrupted natural causation → freedom both required by reason and excluded from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:FGS6","text":"Complete series of conditions → necessary being; universal temporal conditioning → no necessary member; extra-worldly causal origin → beginning of action in time → collapse of externality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0028","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0029","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0030","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:FGS7","text":"Reciprocal proof → exhaustion of one-sided dogmatism → exposure of domain shifts and standpoint changes → harmless persistence of transcendental illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0031","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:AF1","text":"Definition of antithetic → necessary source in reason → reason-understanding mismatch → sceptical method → four paired conflicts → observation-level restrictions → standpoint diagnosis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:AF2","text":"Assumption of the contrary → regress, completion, or intuition test → contradiction → thesis assertion → reversed assumption → counter-contradiction → antithesis assertion → delimitation of both proofs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:ME1","text":"Contradiction is reclassified from accidental disagreement between doctrines into a necessary self-conflict of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:ME2","text":"Equal provability is reclassified from indecision about competing answers into evidence that the question’s object or framing may be defective.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:ME3","text":"Scepticism is divided into destructive suspension of knowledge and a sceptical method directed toward certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:ME4","text":"Infinity is reclassified from maximal quantity into the impossibility of completing successive synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:ME5","text":"Cosmological objects are redistributed between phenomena conditioned by space, time, and causal law and an intelligible domain incapable of synthetic determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:ME6","text":"Freedom is reclassified from temporal firstness into causal spontaneity, exposing its incompatibility with empirical causal continuity without simply identifying it with temporal origination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:ME7","text":"Necessary being is reclassified from an immediate conclusion of change into a disputed terminus whose derivation depends on an illicit shift from empirical to intellectual contingency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0033","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:ALT1","text":"Surface: The argument stages four contradictions concerning the world’s limits, simple parts, freedom, and necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0027","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:ALT2","text":"Structural: The contradictions arise from the mismatch between reason’s demand for absolute totality and understanding’s rule-bound synthesis under sensible conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Opposed proofs are deliberately permitted to conflict so that the conditions and equivocations generating them become visible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Space and time are forms of phenomena; temporal causality conditions every empirical event; causal firstness differs from temporal firstness; no absolute world-totality is given as an experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the definition of antithetic, the shared production mechanism, the sceptical method, and the four conflict structures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0021","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0028","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:MRT2","text":"The local rhetorical elaborations and several subsidiary distinctions remain underdetermined, but they are not required to regenerate the principal conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent at the meta-diagnostic level because empirical undecidability, opposed rational necessity, and methodological conflict mutually support one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:MRT4","text":"The strongest structure is the conversion of reciprocal contradiction into evidence about the limits and equivocations of reason rather than evidence for either dogmatic side.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:MRT5","text":"The final reconciliation remains underdetermined because this section arranges and diagnoses the conflicts but does not yet provide a complete transcendental resolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0035","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:MRT6","text":"The claim of equal superiority is strained by observations that asymmetrically restrict particular proofs through distinctions between phenomena, things in themselves, and category shifts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0017","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0031","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0032","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:MRT7","text":"Several proofs move from conditions for cogitating or experiencing a totality to conclusions about what can exist; that transition remains a load-bearing but unresolved fault line within this section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:MRT8","text":"The freedom thesis extends a requirement derived from cosmological origination to free action within the world, but the text presents this extension as authorization rather than an independent demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:MRT9","text":"The method’s aim at certainty remains compatible with ineradicable illusion only if certainty means diagnosis and non-deception rather than removal of the antinomial tendency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIAP:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if it terminates in diagnostic suspension of dogmatic victory rather than importing a later solution not supplied here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIAP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 62","title":"Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 62"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism","order":55,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"3bdbb2c768694aa9201a09633ced1a9130f568ebd717ae8cefa485b4a3b77318","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:AS1","text":"Mathematical success raises the problem of whether its demonstrative method can be transferred to transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:AS2","text":"Mathematical and philosophical certainty arise from formally different employments of reason and therefore cannot share one method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:AS3","text":"The argument is transcendental, diagnostic, and polemical: it explains mathematical success through construction in pure intuition and diagnoses its philosophical imitation as dogmatism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:AS4","text":"The distinction between a priori form and empirical matter restricts construction to space, time, and quantity while leaving transcendental cognition discursive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:AS5","text":"Definitions, axioms, and demonstrations are tested as the constitutive forms of mathematical evidence and denied equivalent status in philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:AS6","text":"The movement proceeds from mathematical success through formal differentiation and evidentiary restriction to the replacement of speculative dogmatism by systematic critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A1","text":"Possible intuitions are the only presentations through which objects are given to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A2","text":"Philosophical cognition proceeds through concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A3","text":"Mathematical cognition proceeds through the construction of concepts in a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A4","text":"Construction presents an individual intuition as a universally determined schema under general conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A5","text":"Space and time are the only pure a priori intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A6","text":"The material content of appearances is given only a posteriori in perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A7","text":"A synthetic judgment about a concept must pass beyond the concept to a corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A8","text":"Conceptual analysis yields only analytic clarification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A9","text":"Construction in pure intuition yields synthetic cognition possessing universality and necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A10","text":"Transcendental concepts represent rules for synthesizing possible empirical intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A11","text":"Transcendental synthetic propositions state conditions of possible experience without presenting objects in a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A12","text":"A mathematical definition constitutes an arbitrarily synthesized and a priori constructible concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A13","text":"A philosophical definition analyzes a given concept whose complete exposition cannot be demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A14","text":"Immediate synthetic certainty requires intuitive construction rather than conceptual mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A15","text":"Demonstration is apodeictic proof grounded in a priori intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A16","text":"Principles of pure understanding obtain objective validity only indirectly through their relation to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A17","text":"The proper transcendental task of philosophy is to determine the limits of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:A18","text":"Conceptual inquiry can be systematic without being dogmatical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX1","text":"Mathematical and philosophical methods are not identical merely because both produce universal a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX2","text":"Philosophy and mathematics are not distinguished by their subject matter alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX3","text":"Philosophy and mathematics are not distinguished by assigning quality exclusively to philosophy and quantity exclusively to mathematics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX4","text":"Conceptual analysis cannot discover synthetic properties absent from the analyzed concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX5","text":"Empirical intuition cannot ground strict universality or necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX6","text":"The sensible matter of appearances cannot be constructed a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX7","text":"Transcendental concepts do not present corresponding objects in pure intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX8","text":"Transcendental propositions are not products of conceptual construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX9","text":"Empirical and given a priori concepts do not admit strict mathematical definition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX10","text":"The arbitrary definition of a projected object does not establish that object’s existence or possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX11","text":"Philosophy possesses no immediately certain synthetic axioms in the mathematical sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX12","text":"Discursive philosophical proofs are not demonstrations in the mathematical sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX13","text":"Speculative pure reason contains no direct, objectively valid synthetic dogmata grounded in concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:NX14","text":"The rejection of dogmatical method does not entail the rejection of systematic philosophical method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D1","text":"A common object can receive mathematical and philosophical treatment because the decisive distinction lies in the mode of cognition rather than the object considered.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A2","CH_B337_SIDP:A3","CH_B337_SIDP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D2","text":"Mathematics concerns quantity primarily because quantity is constructible in pure intuition, not because quantity independently defines the mathematical method.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A3","CH_B337_SIDP:A5","CH_B337_SIDP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D3","text":"Algebra remains mathematical because its signs present possible quantitative constructions and operations in intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A3","CH_B337_SIDP:A4","CH_B337_SIDP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D4","text":"Cognition divides into analytic clarification through concepts alone, empirical synthesis through empirical intuition, and synthetic a priori cognition through pure construction.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A5","CH_B337_SIDP:A6","CH_B337_SIDP:A7","CH_B337_SIDP:A8","CH_B337_SIDP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D5","text":"Transcendental concepts yield principles governing the synthesis of possible experience rather than determinate knowledge of corresponding objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A10","CH_B337_SIDP:A11","CH_B337_SIDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D6","text":"Mathematical reasoning begins with definitions because the definition produces the concept, whereas philosophical inquiry can reach complete exposition only as a possible terminus of analysis.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A12","CH_B337_SIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D7","text":"Mathematical definitions cannot misstate the content they constitute, though they may be formally imprecise, while philosophical expositions remain vulnerable to omission and illicit addition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A12","CH_B337_SIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D8","text":"Philosophy has no axioms because every synthetic connection between concepts requires a mediating cognition and a deduction of validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A2","CH_B337_SIDP:A14","CH_B337_SIDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D9","text":"Philosophy has no demonstrations because its proofs remain discursive and cannot exhibit their objects in a priori intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A2","CH_B337_SIDP:A3","CH_B337_SIDP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D10","text":"A synthetic proposition grounded in construction is a mathema, whereas a direct synthetic proposition allegedly grounded in concepts is a dogma.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A2","CH_B337_SIDP:A3","CH_B337_SIDP:A7","CH_B337_SIDP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0021","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D11","text":"Speculative pure reason contains no dogmata because transcendental principles possess validity only through their relation to possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A10","CH_B337_SIDP:A11","CH_B337_SIDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D12","text":"Mathematical success cannot authorize transcendental method-transfer because its success depends on pure intuition unavailable to transcendental concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A3","CH_B337_SIDP:A5","CH_B337_SIDP:A9","CH_B337_SIDP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:D13","text":"Philosophy can organize conceptual investigation according to principles of unity while refusing dogmatical evidence and restricting its material to experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A6","CH_B337_SIDP:A17","CH_B337_SIDP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SD1","text":"Mathematical certainty is a local consequence of reason’s constructive access to the formal structure of appearances, not a general power of pure reason over every object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A3","CH_B337_SIDP:A5","CH_B337_SIDP:A6","CH_B337_SIDP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SD2","text":"Transcendental philosophy acquires second-order jurisdiction over mathematics by establishing its conditions and limits without acquiring its constructive method.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A5","CH_B337_SIDP:A11","CH_B337_SIDP:A16","CH_B337_SIDP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SD3","text":"Transcendental principles possess mediated apodeicticity because possible experience is both their field of validity and the ground through which their validity is proved.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A11","CH_B337_SIDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SD4","text":"Borrowing mathematical definitions, axioms, and demonstrations converts their external form into a simulacrum of evidence that conceals rather than corrects philosophical error.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A12","CH_B337_SIDP:A13","CH_B337_SIDP:A14","CH_B337_SIDP:A15","CH_B337_SIDP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SD5","text":"Mathematics and philosophy have opposed epistemic temporalities: mathematics begins with constituted concepts, while philosophy begins with given and partly obscure concepts whose analysis remains corrigible.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A12","CH_B337_SIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SD6","text":"Objective speculative cognition is bounded by possible experience because neither pure concepts nor sensible matter independently supply an a priori object beyond that field.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A1","CH_B337_SIDP:A6","CH_B337_SIDP:A10","CH_B337_SIDP:A11","CH_B337_SIDP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SD7","text":"The systematic unity of reason supplies an order of investigation but cannot supply the missing intuitions or experiential material required for objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A1","CH_B337_SIDP:A6","CH_B337_SIDP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:DNX1","text":"Human reason has no intellectual intuition capable of directly presenting transcendental realities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:DNX2","text":"Possession of a pure concept is not equivalent to the givenness of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:DNX3","text":"A priori determination of form cannot generate sensible matter or existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:DNX4","text":"Universality and a priori origin do not entail methodological uniformity across rational sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:DNX5","text":"The systematic coherence of reason does not extend its objective jurisdiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:DNX6","text":"Mathematical self-evidence cannot serve as the universal criterion of rational certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:DNX7","text":"Conditions governing possible experience cannot be converted into constructed properties of supersensible things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:DNX8","text":"Philosophical concepts cannot be assumed fully transparent before the inquiry that analyzes them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SNX1","text":"The absent object organizing the critique is an intuitable intellectual world beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SNX2","text":"No direct bridge runs from pure conceptual necessity to the existence or determinate character of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SNX3","text":"A single demonstrative method governing every possible object of thought is structurally foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SNX4","text":"Pure reason lacks an unrevisable speculative foundation from which it can proceed without monitoring and correcting its principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:SNX5","text":"Philosophy is denied the status of a constructive science whose concepts produce the objects needed to verify them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:FGS1","text":"Mathematical success → analogical projection into transcendental inquiry → demand for a method test.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:FGS2","text":"Appearance → a priori form in space and time plus a posteriori sensible matter → constructibility of form but not matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:FGS3","text":"Concept alone → analysis → analytic clarification; concept plus empirical intuition → empirical synthesis; concept plus pure intuition → construction → synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:FGS4","text":"Pure concept without corresponding pure intuition → rule for empirical synthesis → transcendental principle → deduction through possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:FGS5","text":"Mathematical evidence → constitutive definition plus intuitive axiom plus demonstration; philosophical counterpart → provisional exposition plus mediated principle plus discursive proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:FGS6","text":"Construction-based synthetic proposition → mathema; allegedly direct concept-based synthetic proposition → dogma; critical examination → absence of legitimate speculative dogmata.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:FGS7","text":"Failure of method-transfer → rejection of dogmatical philosophy → retention of systematic inquiry → critical delimitation of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:AF1","text":"Mathematical success → transcendental imitation → formal distinction between concepts and constructions → division of appearance into pure form and empirical matter → restriction of constructibility → failure of philosophical definitions, axioms, and demonstrations → elimination of speculative dogmata → systematic critical discipline.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:ME1","text":"The philosophy–mathematics boundary is redrawn from a division of subject matters to a division of cognitive procedures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:ME2","text":"Quantity is reclassified as a consequence of constructibility in pure intuition rather than the independent essence of mathematics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:ME3","text":"A priori synthetic cognition is divided into constructed mathemata and discursive transcendental principles rather than treated as a homogeneous domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:ME4","text":"Definitions, axioms, and demonstrations are reclassified from universal forms of rigorous reasoning into method-specific products of construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:ME5","text":"Possible experience becomes both the boundary of objective transcendental validity and the medium through which that validity is established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:ME6","text":"Philosophy is repositioned from a competitor for mathematical evidence to a discipline that assigns limits and exposes illegitimate transfers of method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:ME7","text":"Systematicity and dogmatism cease to be equivalent, allowing ordered inquiry without claims to immediate synthetic certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, the text decides whether mathematical definitions, axioms, and demonstrations can be reproduced in philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, the decision follows from the relation among concepts, intuitions, construction, and the formal–material composition of appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, philosophy polices the migration of evidentiary forms between sciences and converts speculative ambition into critical self-limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:ALT4","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, objects are cognitively available only through intuition, sensible matter arrives only in experience, and philosophical concepts become determinate through a revisable process rather than an originating definition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the distinction between mathematical construction, empirical synthesis, and transcendental discursivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:MRT2","text":"Reconstruction remains dependent on prior arguments establishing space and time as the only pure intuitions and possible experience as the limit of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if those prior transcendental results are granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where the formal difference between constructed evidence and mediated conceptual proof generates the separate analyses of definitions, axioms, and demonstrations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:MRT5","text":"The relation between transcendental principles and possible experience retains a circular appearance because experience proves principles that also constitute the possibility of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0022","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:MRT6","text":"The denial of strict philosophical definition sits uneasily beside the treatment of complete exposition as the possible conclusion of philosophical labor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0015","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:MRT7","text":"The claim that mathematical method offers philosophy no advantage exceeds the narrower proof that mathematical evidentiary forms cannot retain their original status when transferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0018","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:MRT8","text":"The description of constructed objects as created by reason leaves underdetermined the distinction between constructing a schema and producing an existent object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIDP:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the text’s real movement from analogical temptation through transcendental differentiation to critical and systematic closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIDP:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 84","title":"Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism","context":"Chapter 84"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU","layer":"CH","title":"Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas","order":56,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"3af8c404676e36933315b1a840219a1cd24fbba03439216921fc35b1abe361ef","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AS1","text":"The cosmological demand for absolute totality arises from treating phenomena as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AS2","text":"Once this presupposition is rejected, the question of whether the world-series is absolutely finite or infinite loses its required object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AS3","text":"The legitimate problem becomes the extent of empirical regress required to answer questions posed within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AS4","text":"The principle of reason is thereby restricted from constituting objects to regulating the extension of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AS5","text":"This restriction dissolves the antinomy by exposing the fallacy and redefining the ideas that generated the opposed claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AS6","text":"The formerly dialectical principle becomes doctrinal in its subjective, regulative employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AS7","text":"Its epistemic influence remains maximally expansive even without functioning as an a priori axiom determining objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:A1","text":"Neither the concepts of reason nor those of understanding admit transcendental use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:A2","text":"The demand for absolute totality in the series of sensible conditions presupposes that phenomena are things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:A3","text":"The absolute quantity of the series of conditions is not an object that empirical inquiry is required to determine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:A4","text":"Reason legitimately determines only how far empirical regress must proceed within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:A5","text":"The principle of reason is regulative for the extension of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:A6","text":"The principle of reason is not constitutive of phenomena considered as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:A7","text":"The antinomy results from a fallacious transcendental employment of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:A8","text":"The true meaning of cosmological ideas is clarified by restricting them to their legitimate subjective employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:A9","text":"The subjective principle of reason directs the unceasing extension of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:A10","text":"A regulative principle can have the same practical epistemic influence as an axiom without determining objects a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:NX1","text":"Reason and understanding are not instruments for transcendental cognition of objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:NX2","text":"Phenomena are not to be treated as things in themselves for the purpose of determining their absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:NX3","text":"Empirical inquiry is not required to establish whether the series of conditions is absolutely finite or absolutely infinite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:NX4","text":"The principle of reason is not a constitutive principle of phenomena in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:NX5","text":"The antinomial opposition is not a genuine conflict between two objectively established descriptions of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:NX6","text":"Cosmological ideas are not licenses for completing the totality of conditions beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:NX7","text":"The regulative use of reason is not epistemically impotent merely because it does not determine objects a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:D1","text":"The demand for an absolute answer concerning the world-series must be withdrawn once phenomena are distinguished from things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A2","CH_B337_SIEU:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:D2","text":"Cosmological investigation must be reformulated as a rule-governed empirical regress from condition to condition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A3","CH_B337_SIEU:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:D3","text":"The principle of reason remains valid by directing inquiry toward progressively wider empirical conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A4","CH_B337_SIEU:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:D4","text":"The invalidity of reason as a constitutive principle does not entail the invalidity of reason as a guide to inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A5","CH_B337_SIEU:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:D5","text":"The antinomy is resolved by changing the status of the cosmological problem rather than by selecting one opposed cosmological answer.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A7","CH_B337_SIEU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:D6","text":"The dialectical principle becomes doctrinal when its subjective meaning is established as the only legitimate meaning.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A7","CH_B337_SIEU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:D7","text":"The absence of an a priori determination of objects does not reduce the principle’s capacity to expand and correct empirical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A9","CH_B337_SIEU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:SD1","text":"The critical restriction of reason converts an apparently metaphysical demand for totality into an indefinitely extensible procedure of empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A2","CH_B337_SIEU:A4","CH_B337_SIEU:A5","CH_B337_SIEU:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:SD2","text":"Antinomy is generated by confusing a rule for the use of understanding with a description of reality in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A2","CH_B337_SIEU:A6","CH_B337_SIEU:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:SD3","text":"Critical limitation preserves the maximal operational force of reason while denying it metaphysical jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A5","CH_B337_SIEU:A6","CH_B337_SIEU:A9","CH_B337_SIEU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:SD4","text":"The system replaces completed total knowledge with an unceasing norm of expansion and rectification.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A4","CH_B337_SIEU:A9","CH_B337_SIEU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:SD5","text":"The resolution of the cosmological conflict depends on separating epistemic guidance from ontological constitution.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A5","CH_B337_SIEU:A6","CH_B337_SIEU:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:DNX1","text":"The world is not assumed to possess an independently determinable totality accessible to reason beyond the conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:DNX2","text":"The termination of empirical regress is not treated as a condition for meaningful knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:DNX3","text":"A contradiction between cosmological propositions is not evidence that reason has directly encountered the structure of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:DNX4","text":"The regulative use of an idea is not subordinated to the possibility of realizing that idea as a completed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:DNX5","text":"Epistemic progress is not dependent on possessing an ultimate ground or final absolute measure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:DNX6","text":"The distinction between reason and understanding is not allowed to collapse into either unrestricted metaphysical reason or merely passive empirical observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:SNX1","text":"No completed metaphysical totality is available as the final object that would close empirical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:SNX2","text":"No final answer to the absolute magnitude of the cosmological series can serve as the foundation of legitimate knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:SNX3","text":"No opposition between finite and infinite totality is admissible as an objectively decidable alternative under the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:SNX4","text":"No external metaphysical guarantee is required for reason to regulate the indefinite expansion of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:SNX5","text":"The system excludes the possibility that the value of reason depends on its possession of constitutive access to ultimate reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:FGS1","text":"Transcendental demand for totality → identification of phenomena with things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:FGS2","text":"Identification of phenomena with things in themselves → illegitimate demand for the absolute quantity of the condition-series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:FGS3","text":"Critical separation of phenomena from things in themselves → suspension of the finite-versus-infinite absolute question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:FGS4","text":"Suspension of the absolute question → redirection toward the required extent of empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:FGS5","text":"Redirection toward empirical regress → conversion of reason from constitutive principle into regulative rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:FGS6","text":"Conversion into a regulative rule → dissolution of the antinomy through diagnosis of its fallacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:FGS7","text":"Dissolution of the antinomy → doctrinal authorization of reason’s subjective employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:FGS8","text":"Doctrinal authorization of subjective reason → indefinite expansion and correction of empirical understanding without a priori object-determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AF1","text":"Absolute-totality demand → thing-in-itself presupposition → cosmological antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AF2","text":"Thing-in-itself presupposition → critical exposure of transcendental misuse → suspension of absolute quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AF3","text":"Suspension of absolute quantity → empirical-regress rule → regulative employment of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AF4","text":"Regulative employment → redefinition of cosmological ideas → antinomy resolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:AF5","text":"Antinomy resolution → doctrinal status of subjective reason → unceasing extension and rectification of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ME1","text":"The object of cosmological inquiry is reclassified from an absolutely completed world-totality to the rule-governed progression of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ME2","text":"The finite-versus-infinite opposition is removed from the domain of objectively answerable empirical questions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ME3","text":"The distinction between constitutive truth and regulative guidance becomes the decisive instrument for resolving antinomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ME4","text":"Limitation of reason’s metaphysical scope is converted into an expansion of its methodological scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ME5","text":"Knowledge is mapped as an indefinitely extensible process of empirical correction rather than as possession of an ultimate totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ME6","text":"The apparent conflict between reason and experience is reorganized as a conflict between illegitimate object-constitution and legitimate inquiry-regulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ALT1","text":"Surface: The text rejects the requirement to determine whether the cosmological series is absolutely finite or infinite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ALT2","text":"Structural: The conflict originates in the misapplication of a rule of empirical regress as a constitutive principle of phenomena in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Critique resolves the antinomy by changing the question’s legitimate function rather than answering its original metaphysical formulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ALT4","text":"Epistemic: Reason regulates the indefinite extension and correction of understanding without constituting the objects investigated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ALT5","text":"Ontological/Temporal: The system denies completed access to an absolute world-totality while affirming an unceasing temporal progression of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:ALT6","text":"Internal Tension: The principle is denied constitutive authority yet described as practically equivalent in influence to an a priori axiom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the passage’s central movement from metaphysical totality to empirical regulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the distinction between constitutive and regulative uses of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction explains the antinomy as a product of category-mistake and transcendental misemployment rather than as a genuine ontological conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:MRT4","text":"The reconstruction preserves the conversion of dialectical reason into a doctrinal rule for inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:MRT5","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the precise operational limit of “how far” empirical regress must proceed in particular cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:MRT6","text":"The principal unresolved tension lies between the denial of constitutive object-determination and the claim that regulative reason has equivalent epistemic influence to an object-determining axiom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:MRT7","text":"The argument is internally coherent only if epistemic efficacy is distinguished from ontological determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIEU:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement of the text: transcendental exposure → empirical restriction → antinomy dissolution → indefinite methodological expansion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIEU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 69","title":"Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas","context":"Chapter 69"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG","layer":"CH","title":"Section I—Of Ideas in General","order":57,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"db358af199bd7a6f66820fe2579b3bbf742bdbd8e1ceb49c86f4a0a0e3af206f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AS1","text":"Philosophical cognition requires a stable lexicon because conceptual distinctions collapse when their proper terms are used indiscriminately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AS2","text":"The term “idea” must be reserved for a conception originating in reason and transcending possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AS3","text":"Ideas lack adequate empirical instances without thereby becoming fictitious, because they function as rational archetypes and standards.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AS4","text":"Practical ideas are necessary conditions of moral judgment, legislation, and the progressive orientation of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AS5","text":"Experience supplies truth concerning nature but cannot ground laws of what ought to be done.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AS6","text":"Plato’s insight into ideas is retained in practical philosophy while his speculative extension and hypostatization of ideas are restricted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AS7","text":"The argument moves from terminological discipline through the distinction between experience and reason to a taxonomy that fixes the legitimate transcendental use of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:A1","text":"A distinct conception requires a distinct and consistently preserved expression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:A2","text":"An idea is a conception of reason formed from pure notions and transcending possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:A3","text":"No object of possible experience can perfectly correspond to an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:A4","text":"Ideas can be objectively necessary as standards even when they cannot be empirically instantiated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:A5","text":"Moral concepts require pure rational standards rather than derivation from empirical examples.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:A6","text":"Freedom makes practical ideas causally operative in actions, institutions, and legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:A7","text":"The impossibility of complete realization does not invalidate an idea as an archetype of progressive approximation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:A8","text":"Experience is authoritative for knowledge of nature but deceptive when treated as the source of ethical law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:A9","text":"The legitimate use of pure reason must be determined before its claims can be assigned their proper worth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:A10","text":"Representations differ hierarchically according to consciousness, relation to objects, singularity, mediation, origin, and transcendence of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:NX1","text":"An idea is not a sensation, an empirical perception, or a subjective modification of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:NX2","text":"An idea is not an intuition, because intuition is singular and immediately related to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:NX3","text":"An idea is not an ordinary conception or notion of the understanding, because those remain within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:NX4","text":"An idea is not an empirical generalization extracted from observed examples.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:NX5","text":"An idea is not invalidated by the absence of a perfectly corresponding empirical object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:NX6","text":"Moral law is not derivable from what human beings in fact do.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:NX7","text":"Political and ethical ideals are not dispensable because prevailing institutions render them difficult or apparently impracticable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:NX8","text":"Ideas are not independently existing speculative entities whose ontological status may be asserted by mystical deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:NX9","text":"Terminological variety is not harmless when it displaces the only expression adequate to a necessary distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:D1","text":"Virtue can be judged only by reference to an internally possessed rational standard rather than by conformity to exemplary conduct.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A2","CH_B337_SIIG:A4","CH_B337_SIIG:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:D2","text":"Imperfect moral conduct presupposes rather than refutes the idea of moral perfection by which its deficiency is assessed.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A3","CH_B337_SIIG:A4","CH_B337_SIIG:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:D3","text":"A constitution securing the reciprocal compatibility of freedom functions as a necessary regulative standard for legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A4","CH_B337_SIIG:A6","CH_B337_SIIG:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:D4","text":"Existing political failure cannot conclusively demonstrate that rational political ideals are impossible, because that failure may arise from prior legislation uninformed by ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A6","CH_B337_SIIG:A7","CH_B337_SIIG:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:D5","text":"The progressive reduction of punishment becomes intelligible as government approaches a constitution ordered by reciprocal freedom.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A4","CH_B337_SIIG:A6","CH_B337_SIIG:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:D6","text":"The transcendental examination of pure reason is preparatory to moral philosophy because practical ideas require their legitimate scope to be distinguished from speculative excess.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A2","CH_B337_SIIG:A5","CH_B337_SIIG:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:D7","text":"The term “idea” can be assigned a determinate philosophical use only through a graduated classification of representations.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A1","CH_B337_SIIG:A2","CH_B337_SIIG:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:SD1","text":"Rational normativity is structurally independent of empirical actuality while remaining indispensable for judging and transforming empirical actuality.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A3","CH_B337_SIIG:A4","CH_B337_SIIG:A5","CH_B337_SIIG:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:SD2","text":"Practical reason converts the gap between ideal and realization from an objection into the condition of continuous moral and political progress.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A4","CH_B337_SIIG:A6","CH_B337_SIIG:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:SD3","text":"The legitimacy of ideas depends neither on their empirical exemplification nor on their speculative reification, but on their rational necessity within practical judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A2","CH_B337_SIIG:A3","CH_B337_SIIG:A4","CH_B337_SIIG:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:SD4","text":"Semantic discipline is epistemically constitutive because loss of conceptual nomenclature erases the distinctions required for critique of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A1","CH_B337_SIIG:A9","CH_B337_SIIG:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:DNX1","text":"Rational validity is not equivalent to empirical availability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:DNX2","text":"Normative authority is not reducible to statistical regularity, historical custom, or successful institutional practice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:DNX3","text":"Human freedom is not bounded in advance by any determinable empirical measure of its distance from rational ideals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:DNX4","text":"Philosophy is not authorized to treat conceptual imprecision as stylistic freedom when theoretical distinctions depend upon exact names.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:DNX5","text":"The practical use of reason is not licensed by a metaphysical knowledge of supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:SNX1","text":"No final empirical state can close the normative demand generated by freedom, because every realization remains measurable against an unreached archetype.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:SNX2","text":"No appeal to reality as already constituted can decide what reason may require reality to become.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:SNX3","text":"No complete philosophical system is available if the boundary between sensible representation, understanding, and reason is allowed to dissolve.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:SNX4","text":"The text forecloses both empiricist reduction of ideals to facts and Platonic conversion of ideals into knowable transcendent substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:FGS1","text":"Conceptual ambiguity → loss of distinctions → classification of representations → preservation of the term “idea.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:FGS2","text":"Pure notions → transcendence of possible experience → idea → non-empirical archetype.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:FGS3","text":"Archetype without adequate instance → standard of judgment → moral evaluation and legislative orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:FGS4","text":"Freedom → practical causality of ideas → institutions capable of progressive approximation to rational standards.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:FGS5","text":"Empirical obstruction → rejection of impracticability as disproof → continuation of rational demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:FGS6","text":"Platonic insight → removal of mystical hypostatization and speculative overextension → bounded transcendental use of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AF1","text":"Linguistic indeterminacy → terminological preservation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AF2","text":"Terminological preservation → distinction of idea from lower kinds of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AF3","text":"Distinction of idea → separation of rational archetype from empirical object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AF4","text":"Separation from empirical object → defense of practical ideals against charges of chimericality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AF5","text":"Defense of practical ideals → critique of empirical morality and empirical political realism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:AF6","text":"Critique of empiricism and speculative Platonism → determination of the legitimate transcendental employment of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:ME1","text":"The absence of a perfect example is reclassified from evidence against an ideal into evidence that the ideal functions as a standard rather than an instance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:ME2","text":"The opposition between ideality and practical efficacy collapses because ideas become operative through freedom without becoming empirical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:ME3","text":"Political realism is reclassified as potentially derivative of defective institutions rather than as an independent tribunal against rational legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:ME4","text":"Moral judgment is relocated from comparison among observed actions to comparison between actions and a pure rational archetype.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:ME5","text":"Plato is divided into a recoverable practical insight and an inadmissible speculative ontology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:ME6","text":"Representation is reorganized as a hierarchy in which an idea occupies the determinate endpoint of conceptual transcendence beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text fixes the precise philosophical meaning of “idea” within a taxonomy of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:ALT2","text":"Structural — It distinguishes empirical cognition, understanding, and reason according to their relation to objects and possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — It blocks the use of empirical failure and practical difficulty as decisive objections to moral and political ideals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — It treats ideas as non-empirical standards that orient indefinite approximation without requiring complete realization in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:ALT5","text":"Political — It makes reciprocal freedom, rather than achieved happiness or prevailing practice, the rational measure of legitimate constitutional order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central distinction between ideas, empirical representations, and concepts of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:MRT2","text":"The axioms also reconstruct the transition from semantic precision to the practical necessity of moral and political archetypes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the exact deduction by which pure notions generate ideas and the criteria delimiting legitimate transcendence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:MRT4","text":"The system is coherent insofar as it separates practical validity from empirical instantiation and speculative existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:MRT5","text":"Its principal tension lies in affirming that ideas are real and practically causal while refusing their hypostatization as independently existing objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:MRT6","text":"A second tension lies in relying on an idea of freedom to orient political progress while declining to determine the empirically necessary limits of realization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIG:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if lexical taxonomy, critique of empiricism, partial recovery of Platonism, and transcendental restriction are treated as one integrated machine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIG:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 55","title":"Section I—Of Ideas in General","context":"Chapter 55"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO","layer":"CH","title":"Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God","order":58,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"8c29d7fdce557b1f131c99d2631722d2618a478db3faf2f5b1d1564618a6392d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:AS1","text":"Reason’s idea of an absolutely necessary being does not establish an object corresponding to that idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:AS2","text":"The apparent inference from existence in general to absolutely necessary existence depends on an unexamined conception of necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:AS3","text":"Logical necessity belongs to judgements under the condition that their subjects are posited; it does not establish the absolute necessity of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:AS4","text":"Contradiction follows from denying a necessary predicate while retaining its subject, but not from suppressing subject and predicates together.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:AS5","text":"The ontological proof converts the conceptual inclusion of existence into the existential positing of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:AS6","text":"Existence is not a real predicate that enlarges a concept, but the positing of an object in relation to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:AS7","text":"Since existence can be cognized only through possible experience, the existence of a being of pure thought cannot be established a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A1","text":"The absence of contradiction establishes only logical possibility, not the real possibility of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A2","text":"Absolute necessity would require that the non-existence of a thing be impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A3","text":"A necessary judgement makes a predicate necessary only on the condition that its subject is posited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A4","text":"The removal of both subject and predicate produces no contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A5","text":"Contradiction is the sole criterion of impossibility available to pure a priori thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A6","text":"Existence is not a real predicate that contributes content to the concept of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A7","text":"Existential propositions are synthetic rather than analytical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A8","text":"Positing an object as existent does not augment the real content already thought in its concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A9","text":"Cognition of the existence of sensuous objects requires their connection with actual or possible perception under empirical laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A10","text":"No a priori cognition can establish the existence of an object of pure thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A11","text":"The idea of a Supreme Being can regulate or limit reason without supplying cognition of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:A12","text":"Transcendent existence cannot be declared impossible merely because it exceeds experience, but its truth cannot thereby be ascertained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:NX1","text":"A rational need for the unconditioned is not objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:NX2","text":"The necessity of geometrical judgements is not the absolute necessity of geometrical things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:NX3","text":"The non-contradictoriness of the ens realissimum is not proof of the real possibility of such a being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:NX4","text":"The inclusion of existence within the concept of all reality is not an existential demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:NX5","text":"Being is not a determination added to the content of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:NX6","text":"Conceptual completeness is not actual existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:NX7","text":"Pure categories do not provide a criterion for distinguishing existence from mere possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:NX8","text":"The ontological proof is not a transition from concept to object but a tautological reassertion of an assumed positing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:D1","text":"Absolute necessity cannot be inferred from the conditional necessity of predicates within an already posited subject.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A2","CH_B337_SIIO:A3","CH_B337_SIIO:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:D2","text":"The denial of God’s existence contains no contradiction because all predicates attributed to God disappear when the subject is suppressed.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A4","CH_B337_SIIO:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:D3","text":"A concept containing every reality does not become existent by including existence among its contents.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A6","CH_B337_SIIO:A7","CH_B337_SIIO:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:D4","text":"The claim that an ens realissimum cannot be annihilated presupposes the existence of the absolutely necessary subject it is meant to prove.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A2","CH_B337_SIIO:A4","CH_B337_SIIO:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:D5","text":"The real possibility of a Supreme Being cannot be established solely by analysis of its concept.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A1","CH_B337_SIIO:A9","CH_B337_SIIO:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:D6","text":"The ontological proof fails because it treats a synthetic existential claim as though it were an analytical predicate relation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A6","CH_B337_SIIO:A7","CH_B337_SIIO:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:SD1","text":"The proof’s central operation is an illicit conversion of logical containment into ontological production.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A1","CH_B337_SIIO:A3","CH_B337_SIIO:A6","CH_B337_SIIO:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:SD2","text":"Reason can form an ideal of unconditioned completeness without gaining an object whose existence it can cognize.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A10","CH_B337_SIIO:A11","CH_B337_SIIO:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:SD3","text":"The concept of absolute necessity functions negatively as a limit on cognition rather than positively as knowledge of a necessary being.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A5","CH_B337_SIIO:A10","CH_B337_SIIO:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:SD4","text":"The impossibility of ontological proof does not entail the impossibility of God, but entails the theoretical undecidability of God’s existence by pure reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A10","CH_B337_SIIO:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:DNX1","text":"No concept can generate the existence of its object by attaining maximal internal determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:DNX2","text":"No logical criterion of conceivability can substitute for a real criterion of objective possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:DNX3","text":"No subject is shown to be unannihilable merely because predicates become contradictory when retained after its positing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:DNX4","text":"No purely intellectual access to existence is available independently of the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses any theoretical standpoint from which being as such could be known apart from the mode of its possible givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:SNX2","text":"The system denies that the demand for an unconditioned ground can itself supply the ground demanded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:SNX3","text":"The system leaves unavailable any proof that converts an ideal limit of reason into a constitutively given supersensible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:FGS1","text":"Rational demand for an unconditioned being → formation of the idea of absolute necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:FGS2","text":"Idea of absolute necessity → confusion of conditional logical necessity with unconditional necessity of a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:FGS3","text":"Confusion of predicate necessity with subject necessity → apparent contradiction in the denial of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:FGS4","text":"Suppression of subject together with predicates → disappearance of contradiction → collapse of the claim to absolute necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:FGS5","text":"Analysis of existence → existence identified as positing rather than content → ontological inference rendered tautological.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:FGS6","text":"Requirement of experiential conditions for existential cognition → supersensible existence left unprovable by pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:AF1","text":"The demand for an absolutely necessary being → the apparent necessity of an ontological inference → the distinction between judgement and thing → the cancellation of contradiction through suppression of the subject → the non-predicative status of existence → the restriction of existential cognition to experience → the failure of ontological proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:AF2","text":"Logical possibility → real possibility → objective reality → existential cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:AF3","text":"Conceptual ideal of total reality → attempted inclusion of existence → tautological positing → theoretical non-demonstrability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:ME1","text":"Necessity is divided into logical necessity within judgement and alleged absolute necessity of an object, preventing their mutual substitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:ME2","text":"Existence is reclassified from conceptual content to a relation between an object and the conditions under which it can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:ME3","text":"The ens realissimum is reclassified from a self-validating object to an idea whose logical consistency does not secure objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:ME4","text":"The question of God’s existence is displaced from conceptual analysis to the boundary between possible experience and transcendent hypothesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:ALT1","text":"Surface — The ontological argument fails to prove that God exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:ALT2","text":"Structural — The failure results from conflating analytical predicate-relations with synthetic existential positing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critique proceeds by identifying the conditions under which a concept can claim objective reality rather than by merely denying its content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Existence is knowable only as possible empirical givenness, while supersensible existence remains neither demonstrably actual nor demonstrably impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the rejection of the ontological proof through the distinction between logical necessity, real possibility, and existential positing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:MRT2","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning whether a Supreme Being exists, because it establishes non-demonstrability rather than non-existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:MRT3","text":"The argument is internally coherent if the distinction between a concept’s content and an object’s existence is maintained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it shows that contradiction can govern predicate attribution without establishing the existence of any subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:MRT5","text":"The system depends on the inherited restriction that cognition of existence requires possible experience, a condition not independently derived within this section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIO:MRT6","text":"The central structural tension is that transcendent existence is declared unknowable rather than impossible, leaving the idea of absolute necessity operative as a limit after its proof-function has been removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIO:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 77","title":"Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God","context":"Chapter 77"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions","order":59,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"c42c0edcafb5694adee3215191736d8d8f953f075c20c9c00d061b7ce81baae6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AS1","text":"Reason necessarily seeks an unconditioned totality for empirical series whose members are given only conditionally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AS2","text":"This necessary demand produces cosmological ideas that cannot be given as objects of possible experience or securely known through speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AS3","text":"The resulting contradictions concern practical hope, systematic cognition, and the ultimate orientation of reason, so they cannot be dismissed as idle disputes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AS4","text":"The inquiry therefore temporarily suspends truth-adjudication in order to diagnose why reason is drawn to each conflicting position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AS5","text":"The thesis is organized by dogmatic affirmation of unconditioned grounds, while the antithesis is organized by empirical restriction to conditioned natural explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AS6","text":"The conflict is redistributed through an analysis of practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interests rather than resolved by selecting a doctrinal victor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AS7","text":"The unresolved contradiction returns reason to a deferred radical inquiry into the source of its own disunion, while practical action can still force provisional alignment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A1","text":"Reason necessarily seeks the unconditioned totality of every empirical synthesis whose objects are determined only conditionally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A2","text":"No possible experience presents an object adequate to cosmological totality, and reason cannot cognize such totality as conforming to general empirical laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A3","text":"The four cosmological antinomies exhaust the a priori series of synthetic conditions that delimit empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A4","text":"Reason must investigate the source of its contradictions because its highest interests prohibit indifference or simple doctrinal retreat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A5","text":"An inquiry into rational interests can explain partisan adherence only by suspending the logical criterion of truth and comparing the principles of the opposing sides.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A6","text":"The thesis is dogmatism of pure reason because it supplements empirical explanation with intellectual propositions affirming unconditioned grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A7","text":"The antithesis is pure empiricism because it applies principles of possible experience uniformly to phenomena and cosmological ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A8","text":"The thesis treats beginning, simplicity, freedom, and a supreme ground as theoretical supports for morality and religion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A9","text":"The thesis promises a completed a priori derivation of conditioned existence from an unconditioned ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A10","text":"The antithesis leaves every condition conditioned and thereby prevents architectonic completion of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A11","text":"Experience alone provides speculative cognition with an objective basis, lawful procedure, and corresponding intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A12","text":"Empirical restraint is legitimate as a maxim of speculative inquiry but becomes dogmatic when it objectively denies what lies beyond phenomenal cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A13","text":"Neither party may transform a principle of inquiry or a practical interest into objective speculative knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:A14","text":"Practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interests shape adherence where doctrinal truth remains unadjudicated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:NX1","text":"Cosmological ideas are not arbitrary fictions, even though they lack objects adequate to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:NX2","text":"Cosmological totalities are not objects that can be exhibited, intuited, or lawfully cognized within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:NX3","text":"An analysis of rational interest is not a decision concerning the truth or right of either thesis or antithesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:NX4","text":"Pure empiricism excludes a first epoch, final spatial boundary, free causal faculty, indivisible ultimate substance, and cause beyond nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:NX5","text":"Speculative cognition cannot acquire an objective basis from intellectual entities that cannot be presented in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:NX6","text":"Proper empiricism is not entitled to deny objectively what it cannot cognize phenomenally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:NX7","text":"The antithesis cannot supply the first foundation required for a completed architectonic system of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:D1","text":"The cosmological antinomies are necessary and delimited products of reason’s demand for unconditioned totality, not optional metaphysical inventions.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A1","CH_B337_SIIR:A2","CH_B337_SIIR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:D2","text":"The thesis and antithesis differ primarily in their principles of explanatory legitimacy rather than merely in their answers to four separate questions.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A5","CH_B337_SIIR:A6","CH_B337_SIIR:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:D3","text":"The thesis gains a practical and architectonic advantage by promising moral support and systematic closure, but this advantage cannot establish its truth.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A8","CH_B337_SIIR:A9","CH_B337_SIIR:A10","CH_B337_SIIR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:D4","text":"Appeal to experience yields either a legitimate discipline of speculative modesty or an illegitimate metaphysical denial, depending on whether it remains methodological.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A11","CH_B337_SIIR:A12","CH_B337_SIIR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:D5","text":"The antithesis secures indefinitely extendible natural inquiry only by surrendering unconditioned grounding and completed systematic unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A7","CH_B337_SIIR:A10","CH_B337_SIIR:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:D6","text":"Interest-analysis is necessarily preliminary because only an inquiry into the source of reason’s self-division can address the conflict as a conflict of right.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A4","CH_B337_SIIR:A5","CH_B337_SIIR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:SD1","text":"Antinomy is a systematic self-conflict in which reason applies its demand for totality to an object-domain whose conditions prohibit theoretical completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A1","CH_B337_SIIR:A2","CH_B337_SIIR:A4","CH_B337_SIIR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:SD2","text":"The authority of reason is partitioned: experience governs theoretical objectivity, while unconditioned ideas may retain practical significance without becoming speculative knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A8","CH_B337_SIIR:A11","CH_B337_SIIR:A12","CH_B337_SIIR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:SD3","text":"Dogmatism and empiricism are asymmetrically deficient: dogmatism obtains closure by exceeding knowledge, while empiricism preserves inquiry by refusing closure and becomes destructive when converted into denial.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A9","CH_B337_SIIR:A10","CH_B337_SIIR:A11","CH_B337_SIIR:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:SD4","text":"Reason can secure its authority only through reflexive self-limitation rather than through expansion of speculative claims beyond experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A2","CH_B337_SIIR:A4","CH_B337_SIIR:A11","CH_B337_SIIR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:DNX1","text":"The necessity of reason’s demand for unconditioned totality does not establish the reality of an unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:DNX2","text":"Architectonic unity cannot serve as theoretical warrant for a first ground or completed chain of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:DNX3","text":"Practical indispensability cannot supply the intuition required for speculative knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:DNX4","text":"The absence of phenomenal cognition cannot license objective denial of what transcends phenomenal cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:DNX5","text":"Popularity, psychological convenience, and practical attraction cannot function as criteria of doctrinal truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:SNX1","text":"A theoretically possessable unconditioned totality is unavailable even though reason cannot cease demanding it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:SNX2","text":"Neither thesis nor antithesis can reconcile moral-religious support, lawful natural explanation, and completed systematic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:SNX3","text":"A partisan verdict is inadmissible as resolution until the source of reason’s own division has been exposed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:FGS1","text":"Conditioned empirical synthesis → rational demand for unconditioned totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:FGS2","text":"No adequate experience or intuition → no objective speculative construction of that totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:FGS3","text":"Unconditioned completion affirmed / unconditioned completion refused → dogmatic thesis / empirical antithesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:FGS4","text":"Contradictory doctrines + suspended truth-verdict → comparison of source-principles and interest-profiles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:FGS5","text":"Thesis: practical support + systematic closure; antithesis: lawful inquiry + infinite conditioning; architectonic and popular interests redistribute adherence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:FGS6","text":"Empirical maxim distinguished from empirical denial → reciprocal exposure of dogmatic excess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:FGS7","text":"Unresolved self-disunion → deferred radical critique; practical action → provisional alignment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AF1","text":"Conditioned experience → demand for unconditioned totality → cosmological ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AF2","text":"Cosmological ideas beyond possible experience → contradictory thesis and antithesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AF3","text":"Contradiction involving highest rational interests → refusal of indifference → search for the source of disunion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AF4","text":"Deferred radical resolution → suspension of truth-adjudication → comparison of dogmatism and empiricism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AF5","text":"Principle-comparison → practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interest-profiles → explanation of partisan adherence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AF6","text":"Distinction between empirical discipline and empirical denial → critique of both dogmatic overreach and dogmatic empiricism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:AF7","text":"Persistent theoretical stalemate → reflective suspension of allegiance and practical provisional choice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:ME1","text":"The four cosmological questions are reclassified as formal outputs of the relation between conditioned experience and reason’s demand for the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:ME2","text":"Thesis and antithesis are reclassified from isolated metaphysical answers into competing principles of rational employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:ME3","text":"Grounds of doctrinal truth are separated from motives of philosophical adhesion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:ME4","text":"Proper empirical method is separated from metaphysical naturalism or objective denial of the supersensible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:ME5","text":"Architectonic systematic unity becomes visible as an interest of reason rather than as evidence of a first ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:ME6","text":"Infinite regress becomes visible both as the condition of open-ended scientific inquiry and as the obstacle to completed rational system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:ALT1","text":"Surface — The explicit conflict concerns the world’s beginning and limits, the simple self, freedom, and a supreme world-ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:ALT2","text":"Structural — Antinomy is generated by the collision between empirical conditioning and reason’s demand for unconditioned completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text shifts from deciding metaphysical doctrines to diagnosing the principles and interests that make adherence to them intelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:ALT4","text":"Epistemic — Possible experience is established as the sole objective basis of speculative cognition, while practical interest is denied the status of theoretical proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:ALT5","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The knowable world appears as an indefinitely conditioned natural order, while beginning, unconditioned cause, and completed totality lack theoretical objecthood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the movement from necessary cosmological ideas through antinomy to a non-adjudicative analysis of rational interests.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:MRT2","text":"The system remains intentionally underdetermined at its decisive point because the origin of reason’s disunion and its final resolution are deferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:MRT3","text":"The preliminary architecture is internally coherent because it explicitly limits itself to explaining attraction to doctrines rather than deciding their truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:MRT4","text":"The most determinate distinction is between empirical methodology and empirical metaphysical denial, which preserves speculative restraint while blocking dogmatic negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:MRT5","text":"A central tension remains between treating unconditioned claims as supports of practical interest and denying them an objective speculative basis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:MRT6","text":"A second tension remains between reason’s architectonic demand for completed unity and the open-ended empirical inquiry declared uniquely objective.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:MRT7","text":"The account of popular preference depends on asserted attributions of indolence, vanity, and practical need rather than on deductions from the governing principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIR:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the section’s actual movement only if interest-analysis remains preparatory and neither party is presented as the final conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIR:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 63","title":"Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions","context":"Chapter 63"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason","order":60,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"e87664969cf1367d9ae6bb5948a890bb632992a53d93768aad5943f959573655","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:AS1","text":"The argument reconstructs reason’s ultimate interest practically after speculative cognition reaches its limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:AS2","text":"The questions of duty and hope become decisive because knowledge cannot settle the objects implicated in reason’s highest ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:AS3","text":"Pure moral law supplies an a priori criterion of worthiness for happiness independently of inclination and empirical reward.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:AS4","text":"Rational hope demands a correspondence between happiness and moral worth that nature alone cannot guarantee.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:AS5","text":"The ideal of the supreme good therefore makes a supreme moral cause and a future moral order practically necessary hypotheses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:AS6","text":"Moral theology extends this practical postulate into teleological unity while remaining subordinate to autonomous moral legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A1","text":"The whole interest of reason is ordered by the questions of knowledge, duty, and hope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A2","text":"Speculative reason cannot cognize the objects required to fulfill reason’s ultimate interests.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A3","text":"Moral laws are a priori, necessary, and unconditional laws governing the freedom of rational beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A4","text":"Prudential rules derive from empirical desires for happiness, whereas moral laws determine worthiness for happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A5","text":"A moral world has practical objective reality as the intelligible systematic unity that rational freedom ought to realize in the world of sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A6","text":"The complete good requires happiness to be distributed in exact proportion to moral worth, with worthiness as the condition of participation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A7","text":"Practical reason necessarily connects the endeavor to become worthy of happiness with hope for proportionate happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A8","text":"Nature alone provides neither a necessary causal connection nor theoretical knowledge of a connection between morality and happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A9","text":"A self-rewarding moral order is conceivable only under a supreme will encompassing and harmonizing particular rational wills.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A10","text":"The ideal of the supreme good is a morally perfect intelligence united with supreme blessedness and causing happiness according to moral worthiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A11","text":"God and a future life are practically necessary hypotheses inseparable from moral obligation but unavailable as speculative cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A12","text":"The practical task of securing universal moral teleology requires the supreme cause to be conceived as single, omnipotent, omniscient, omnipresent, and eternal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A13","text":"Practical moral ends impose a teleological unity upon nature and thereby direct theological reflection on nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A14","text":"Moral law binds rational beings prior to and independently of divine command, and divine will cannot legislate morality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:A15","text":"Moral theology has legitimate use only when it serves immanent moral action rather than displacing practical reason’s legislative authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:NX1","text":"The supreme good is not an object available to speculative knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:NX2","text":"Morality is not prudence, hypothetical calculation, or a technique for satisfying inclinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:NX3","text":"Happiness alone is not the complete good, and moral desert alone is not the complete good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:NX4","text":"The empirical course of nature is not evidence that virtue receives happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:NX5","text":"The moral world is not an empirically given realm or an object of intelligible intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:NX6","text":"God and future life are not demonstrated dogmas or newly cognized objects of practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:NX7","text":"Moral obligation is not binding because it has been externally commanded by God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:NX8","text":"Moral theology is not licensed to replace autonomous moral legislation with transcendent religious authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:D1","text":"Moral necessity gives practical objective reality to free action even though speculative reason cannot establish a systematic moral order within nature.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A3","CH_B337_SIIS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:D2","text":"The duty to become worthy of happiness cannot yield theoretical knowledge that happiness will actually occur.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A4","CH_B337_SIIS:A7","CH_B337_SIIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:D3","text":"Rational hope for proportionate happiness requires postulating a moral cause of nature rather than inferring reward from empirical causality.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A7","CH_B337_SIIS:A8","CH_B337_SIIS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:D4","text":"The postulated moral cause must be conceived as one will with capacities adequate to know moral worth and distribute happiness universally.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A9","CH_B337_SIIS:A10","CH_B337_SIIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:D5","text":"The future intelligible world is represented as a consequence of moral conduct because the sensible world contains no indication of a completed moral order.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A5","CH_B337_SIIS:A8","CH_B337_SIIS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:D6","text":"Teleological interpretation of nature is downstream from moral reason and cannot independently establish the theological ground that morality requires.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A2","CH_B337_SIIS:A13","CH_B337_SIIS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:D7","text":"Divine commands are morally valid only insofar as they express laws already recognized as internally binding by reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A14","CH_B337_SIIS:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:SD1","text":"The system converts speculative incapacity into practical legitimacy by treating what cannot be known as what must be postulated for moral reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:SD2","text":"The supreme good coordinates individual moral autonomy with a total order in which happiness is distributively regulated by desert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:SD3","text":"Moral theology functions as a unification mechanism joining freedom, natural causality, happiness, divine attributes, and teleological order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:SD4","text":"The system requires happiness to remain morally non-motivating as a condition of virtue while also requiring its possibility to sustain morality as purposive action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:SD5","text":"Theological claims are admissible only when generated from moral law and are invalid when used to generate, revise, or suspend moral law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:DNX1","text":"The empirical causal order must not be presumed to be morally self-correcting or self-rewarding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:DNX2","text":"Individual moral compliance must not be treated as sufficient to produce the collective harmony required by the supreme good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:DNX3","text":"An arbitrary or externally legislating divine will must not precede the moral criterion by which any divine will is conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:DNX4","text":"Practical postulation must not become theoretical possession of its own postulated objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:DNX5","text":"A plurality of independent supreme causal wills must not be allowed if complete unity of moral ends is to be conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:SNX1","text":"The system is organized around the absence of present sensible-world closure between virtue and happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses both speculative proof of moral teleology and natural self-rewarding morality as alternatives to practical postulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a morality grounded in reward while denying that morality can become a complete practical world without a possible order of reward.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:FGS1","text":"Speculative limitation → unresolved ultimate interest → practical reason becomes the remaining route to reason’s highest ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:FGS2","text":"A priori moral law → rational freedom → worthiness for happiness rather than prudential acquisition of happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:FGS3","text":"Worthiness + demand for the complete good → happiness must correspond proportionately to morality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:FGS4","text":"Natural causal indifference to moral worth → empirical nature cannot secure rational hope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:FGS5","text":"Practical demand for proportionate happiness + unavailable natural guarantee → ideal of the supreme good → postulation of God and future life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:FGS6","text":"Supreme moral cause → unity of ends → functional attribution of divine perfection → teleological unity of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:FGS7","text":"Moral-law priority → immanent moral theology → prohibition on deriving duty from divine will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:AF1","text":"Speculative boundary → practical reframing of reason’s interest → pure moral law → moral worthiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:AF2","text":"Moral worthiness → demand for proportionate happiness → natural insufficiency → postulation of the supreme good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:AF3","text":"Supreme-good postulate → moral theology → teleological unity of nature → return to autonomous and immanent moral action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:ME1","text":"God and future life are reclassified from possible theoretical objects into practically necessary hypotheses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:ME2","text":"Happiness and morality are separated at the level of motivation but recombined at the level of the complete good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:ME3","text":"The moral world is reclassified as a practical intelligible order oriented toward transforming the sensible world rather than escaping it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:ME4","text":"Natural teleology is reclassified as an effect of practical moral reason rather than a speculative foundation of morality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:ME5","text":"Divine command is reclassified as morally intelligible only through prior autonomous recognition of obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:ALT1","text":"The surface level distinguishes what can be known, what ought to be done, and what may be hoped.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:ALT2","text":"The structural level identifies the missing causal link between moral worth and happiness within nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:ALT3","text":"The metastrategic level subordinates theology and teleology to moral autonomy while using them to complete practical reason’s system of ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:ALT4","text":"The ontological-temporal level contrasts the sensible world’s moral opacity with an intelligible moral order represented as future relative to finite agents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from speculative limitation through moral obligation to practical theological postulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:MRT2","text":"The system depends decisively on the distinction between theoretical cognition and practically necessary assumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:MRT3","text":"The inferential transition from proportional happiness to a single supreme moral cause remains underdetermined unless complete unity of ends is accepted as a necessary demand of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:MRT4","text":"The claim that morality requires hope for effective purposive action strains against the claim that moral worth must remain independent of the prospect of happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:MRT5","text":"The derivation of determinate divine attributes from moral teleology approaches the cognitive expansion that the system simultaneously prohibits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:MRT6","text":"The system is most determinate where natural moral indifference generates the need for a practical postulate rather than a speculative proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIIS:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s governing reversal: moral law produces theology, and theology may not produce moral law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIIS:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 90","title":"Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 90"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5","order":61,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"aecbb325cb9fcc2f1862c564b22a1b51e0821601305f2054743f4f7448a74bc7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:AS1","text":"Judgement becomes analysable when its material content is abstracted and its intellectual form is isolated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:AS2","text":"The pure function of judgement is organized into four irreducible headings: quantity, quality, relation, and modality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:AS3","text":"General logic’s classifications are insufficient wherever forms equivalent for syllogistic use differ in their contribution to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:AS4","text":"The relational forms of judgement articulate predication, consequence, and exhaustive reciprocal exclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:AS5","text":"Modality orders judgements by their logical standing before thought rather than by any addition to their content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:AS6","text":"The argument moves from formal classification to transcendental differentiation by showing that logical forms have unequal cognitive functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A1","text":"The intellectual form of a judgement can be considered independently of its content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A2","text":"Every judgement-function belongs under quantity, quality, relation, or modality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A3","text":"Each heading contains three distinct momenta of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A4","text":"Forms that are equivalent for syllogistic application may differ essentially when considered as cognitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A5","text":"Transcendental logic evaluates the cognitive import of logical affirmation, whereas general logic abstracts from predicate-content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A6","text":"An infinite judgement affirmatively places a subject under a negative predicate while only limitatively determining the subject’s cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A7","text":"Judgement-relations are exhausted by subject–predicate relation, ground–consequence relation, and reciprocal relation among members of a division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A8","text":"A hypothetical judgement thinks a consequence between propositions without deciding the truth of either proposition considered separately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A9","text":"A disjunctive judgement combines reciprocal exclusion with collective completion of a determinate sphere of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A10","text":"Modality concerns the value of the copula in relation to thought and contributes no content to judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A11","text":"Problematic, assertoric, and apodeictic judgement respectively express logical possibility, logical actuality, and logical necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:A12","text":"Logical necessity arises when an assertoric judgement is determined by laws of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:NX1","text":"Logical classification is not exhausted by the requirements of syllogistic use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:NX2","text":"A singular judgement is not simply a universal judgement, despite their common treatment in syllogistic inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:NX3","text":"An infinite judgement is not a positive determination of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:NX4","text":"A negative predicate does not yield affirmative cognition merely because it is logically affirmed of a subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:NX5","text":"A hypothetical judgement is not an assertion that either its antecedent or consequent is true.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:NX6","text":"Disjunction is not mere alternation between propositions; it is a structured partition of a cognitive whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:NX7","text":"Modality is not a further determination of the object represented in judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:NX8","text":"Problematic judgement does not express objective possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:D1","text":"A complete transcendental table must distinguish singular from universal judgement because their cognitive quantities differ despite their shared inferential treatment.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A3","CH_B337_SILF:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:D2","text":"Infinite judgement has a transcendental role because limitation of a conceptual sphere differs from positive specification within that sphere.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A5","CH_B337_SILF:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:D3","text":"Hypothetical form permits propositions that may be false to function provisionally as conditions for discovering truth.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A8","CH_B337_SILF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:D4","text":"Disjunctive form determines a cognition through the jointly exhaustive exclusion of alternative spheres.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:D5","text":"The modal sequence organizes thought as a progression from optional admission, through accepted truth, to necessity under understanding’s laws.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A11","CH_B337_SILF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:D6","text":"Logical form must be assessed both by its formal validity and by the kind and degree of cognition it makes possible.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A4","CH_B337_SILF:A5","CH_B337_SILF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:SD1","text":"The table of judgement functions is generated by a shift from inter-judgement operations to the contribution each form makes to cognition as such.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A1","CH_B337_SILF:A4","CH_B337_SILF:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:SD2","text":"Determination proceeds through both positive placement and negative delimitation, but these operations have unequal cognitive yield.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A5","CH_B337_SILF:A6","CH_B337_SILF:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:SD3","text":"Logical relations can organize the search for truth without themselves supplying truth about objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A8","CH_B337_SILF:A10","CH_B337_SILF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:SD4","text":"The understanding’s formal activity culminates in necessity only when accepted judgement is incorporated under its own laws.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A10","CH_B337_SILF:A11","CH_B337_SILF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the assumption that formal equivalence in inference establishes sameness of cognitive function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the assumption that every logical affirmation increases positive knowledge of its subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the assumption that relational form intrinsically guarantees the truth of related propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the identification of logical possibility with objective possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the assumption that necessity belongs to a judgement merely because it is asserted as true.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:SNX1","text":"The argument is organized around the absence of immediate access from judgement-form to object-determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses the possibility that logical form alone can supply materially affirmative cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:SNX3","text":"The argument treats truth as unavailable at the level of merely problematic alternatives, even when those alternatives are indispensable to its search.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:SNX4","text":"The argument leaves no place for unconditioned logical necessity outside determination by laws of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:FGS1","text":"Abstraction from content → isolation of intellectual form → classification of all judgement-functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:FGS2","text":"Formal classification → comparison with general logic → differentiation of inferential equivalence from cognitive distinctness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:FGS3","text":"Negative predicate affirmed of subject → exclusion from a sphere → merely limitative cognition rather than positive determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:FGS4","text":"Propositions related conditionally or disjunctively → truth suspended or alternatives reciprocally excluded → cognition structurally ordered without immediate object-truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:FGS5","text":"Possible admission → accepted truth → determination under laws of understanding → logical necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:AF1","text":"Material content abstracted → intellectual form isolated → four headings of judgement established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:AF2","text":"Standard logical taxonomy questioned → singular and infinite forms differentiated by cognitive function → transcendental table secured.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:AF3","text":"Relations distinguished as predication, consequence, and reciprocal division → hypothetical and disjunctive judgement separated from truth-assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:AF4","text":"Content exhausted by quantity, quality, and relation → modality relocated to the copula’s logical value → possibility, actuality, and necessity sequenced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:ME1","text":"Singular judgement becomes visible as cognitively distinct from universal judgement despite its inferential substitutability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:ME2","text":"Negation becomes divisible into denial, logical affirmation through a negative predicate, and cognitive limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:ME3","text":"Disjunction becomes an architecture of mutually excluding yet jointly exhaustive cognitive regions rather than a bare choice-form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:ME4","text":"Modal distinctions become distinctions in the status of thinking rather than predicates of represented objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:ME5","text":"General logic and transcendental logic become differentiated by their objects of assessment: formal use versus cognitive contribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:ALT1","text":"Surface — Judgements are classified by quantity, quality, relation, and modality, each with three momenta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:ALT2","text":"Structural — Logical forms are differentiated according to whether they determine a subject, relate propositions, partition a cognitive sphere, or qualify a copula.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The analysis displaces standard logical taxonomy whenever its operational classifications conceal distinctions necessary for cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Thought advances from optional logical positing to truth-acceptance and finally to necessity under laws internal to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the fourfold table and the reason for separating its twelve momenta.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction requires the distinction between general logical use and transcendental cognitive assessment.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A4","CH_B337_SILF:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent insofar as content, relation, and modal status are kept analytically distinct.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A7","CH_B337_SILF:A9","CH_B337_SILF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it demonstrates that logical affirmation, truth, and cognitive determination are non-identical operations.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A5","CH_B337_SILF:A6","CH_B337_SILF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:MRT5","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the ground of the completeness and necessity of the four headings themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILF:MRT6","text":"The treatment of modal progression depends on the asserted incorporation of judgement into understanding without fully deriving that incorporation within this unit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILF:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 26","title":"Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5","context":"Chapter 26"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4","order":62,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"53298e57baf97d16076f412aca769a4b571469fa67e56d601e7c8bcddb2fb86f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AS1","text":"The understanding is first delimited as a non-sensuous faculty and thereby excluded from intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AS2","text":"Since human cognition occurs either through intuition or through conception, the understanding is identified with discursive cognition through conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AS3","text":"Sensuous intuition is grounded in receptivity, whereas conception is grounded in the spontaneous function of ordering representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AS4","text":"Because conceptions relate to objects only through other representations, cognition by conception is necessarily mediate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AS5","text":"Judgement is the act through which conceptions unify representations and mediately determine objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AS6","text":"The understanding is therefore defined as the faculty of judging.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AS7","text":"The functions of the understanding can be systematically exhibited by classifying the forms of unity operative in judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A1","text":"The understanding is a non-sensuous faculty of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A2","text":"Intuition is the only immediate mode of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A3","text":"Human cognition that is not intuitive is discursive cognition through conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A4","text":"Sensuous intuitions depend on the receptivity of impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A5","text":"Conceptions depend on the spontaneity of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A6","text":"A function is the unity of the act of arranging diverse representations under one common representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A7","text":"The understanding uses conceptions only in judging.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A8","text":"A conception relates to an object only through another representation of that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A9","text":"A judgement is the mediate cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A10","text":"Every judgement contains a conception valid for multiple other conceptions and applicable to a given representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A11","text":"Judgement unifies many possible cognitions under a higher representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A12","text":"Thought is cognition by means of conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A13","text":"A conception is a predicate of possible judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A14","text":"A conception can relate to objects because other representations are contained under it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A15","text":"All acts of the understanding can be reduced to judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:A16","text":"The complete exhibition of the functions of unity in judgements reveals all functions of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:NX1","text":"The understanding is not a sensuous faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:NX2","text":"The understanding is not a faculty of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:NX3","text":"Human cognition through the understanding is not intuitive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:NX4","text":"Conceptions are not grounded in receptivity to impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:NX5","text":"A conception does not relate immediately to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:NX6","text":"A judgement is not an immediate presentation of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:NX7","text":"Thought is not cognition independent of conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:NX8","text":"The understanding is not a plurality of irreducible cognitive acts outside judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:NX9","text":"A conception is not defined by isolated reference to a single object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:D1","text":"The exclusion of intuition from the understanding makes conceptual mediation necessary for its cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A1","CH_B337_SILU:A2","CH_B337_SILU:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:D2","text":"The distinction between receptivity and spontaneity establishes the basic division between intuition and conception.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A4","CH_B337_SILU:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:D3","text":"A conception functions cognitively by subsuming diverse representations under a common representation.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A6","CH_B337_SILU:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:D4","text":"Since conceptions cannot connect directly with objects, object-cognition through the understanding requires an intervening representation.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A8","CH_B337_SILU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:D5","text":"Judgement is the operative form of conceptual mediation because it applies a general conception to a representation connected with an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A7","CH_B337_SILU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:D6","text":"The unity produced by judgement converts multiple possible cognitions into one mediate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A9","CH_B337_SILU:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:D7","text":"The understanding can be represented as the faculty of judging because thought, conception, and judgement form a continuous functional sequence.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A7","CH_B337_SILU:A12","CH_B337_SILU:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:D8","text":"The logical forms of judgement provide the classificatory access-point to the functions of the understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A15","CH_B337_SILU:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:SD1","text":"The understanding is constituted not by immediate access to objects but by the formal organization of representations.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A6","CH_B337_SILU:A8","CH_B337_SILU:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:SD2","text":"Object-cognition by the understanding is structurally relational: an object becomes cognitively available through representations ordered under further representations.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A8","CH_B337_SILU:A10","CH_B337_SILU:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:SD3","text":"The unity of judgement is the generative mechanism linking conceptual spontaneity to determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A5","CH_B337_SILU:A6","CH_B337_SILU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:SD4","text":"The reduction of understanding to judgement converts a faculty-description into a formal program for enumerating its functions.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A12","CH_B337_SILU:A15","CH_B337_SILU:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:SD5","text":"The distinction between intuition and conception establishes a division between immediate givenness and mediated unity without making either sufficient in isolation for the full account of human cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A2","CH_B337_SILU:A3","CH_B337_SILU:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:DNX1","text":"The understanding cannot generate an immediate presentation of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:DNX2","text":"Conceptual cognition cannot operate without material supplied by another representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:DNX3","text":"Spontaneity does not amount to independent object-givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:DNX4","text":"The unity of cognition cannot be identified with the mere aggregation of impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:DNX5","text":"The understanding cannot be fully characterized by its contents apart from the acts that order them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:DNX6","text":"A conception cannot function cognitively without a field of possible subsumption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:DNX7","text":"The logical analysis of judgement cannot be bypassed if the functions of the understanding are to be completely exhibited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:SNX1","text":"Immediate intellectual intuition is excluded as a possible model of human understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:SNX2","text":"A cognition of objects without mediation by representations is unavailable to the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:SNX3","text":"Pure receptivity without spontaneous unification cannot constitute conceptual cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:SNX4","text":"Pure spontaneity without an object-related representation cannot constitute human object-cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:SNX5","text":"The understanding cannot be grounded in an unstructured multiplicity of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:SNX6","text":"The functions of the understanding cannot remain inaccessible or purely empirical once they are identified with functions of unity in judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:FGS1","text":"Non-sensuous faculty → exclusion of intuition → restriction of the understanding to conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:FGS2","text":"Conception → spontaneous function → arrangement of diverse representations under unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:FGS3","text":"Conceptual mediation → relation through another representation → mediate cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:FGS4","text":"Mediate cognition → application of a general conception to a given representation → judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:FGS5","text":"Judgement → unity of representations → collection of multiple possible cognitions into one.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:FGS6","text":"Thought through conceptions → reduction of understanding to judgement → identification of the understanding as the faculty of judging.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:FGS7","text":"Forms of unity in judgement → complete logical exhibition → systematic classification of the functions of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AF1","text":"Negative definition of the understanding → exclusion of intuition → identification of discursive cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AF2","text":"Discursive cognition → distinction between receptivity and spontaneity → account of conceptual function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AF3","text":"Conceptual function → indirect relation to objects → definition of judgement as mediate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AF4","text":"Mediate cognition → unification of representations → reduction of understanding to judging.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:AF5","text":"Reduction to judging → analysis of judgemental unity → systematic disclosure of the functions of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:ME1","text":"Cognition is reclassified from a direct relation between faculty and object into an ordered relation among representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:ME2","text":"The understanding is shifted from a general power of thought to a determinate faculty defined by judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:ME3","text":"Conceptions are reclassified from static contents into functions of subsumption and unification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:ME4","text":"Judgement becomes the bridge between the spontaneous activity of thought and the object-directedness of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:ME5","text":"The logical forms of judgement become the map through which the plurality of intellectual functions can be made explicit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:ME6","text":"The opposition between intuition and conception is transformed into a functional division between immediate givenness and mediated unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:ALT1","text":"Surface — The understanding is defined as cognition through conceptions rather than intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:ALT2","text":"Structural — Conceptions order diverse representations through functions of unity and thereby mediate reference to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The analysis replaces an inventory of mental powers with a reduction of intellectual activity to the forms of judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human object-cognition presupposes a non-immediate structure in which objects become cognizable through successive representational mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the negative definition of understanding to its identification as the faculty of judging.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:MRT2","text":"The distinction between receptivity and spontaneity, together with the principle of mediate conceptual reference, supplies the argument’s essential internal transitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:MRT3","text":"The reduction of all acts of understanding to judgement remains dependent on the asserted exhaustiveness of the intuition/conception division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent at the level of functional analysis, but the relation between representation, conception, and object remains formally mediated rather than fully explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:MRT5","text":"The strongest point is the derivation of judgement from the conditions of conceptual cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:MRT6","text":"The principal under-resolution is whether the complete classification of judgemental unities exhausts every operation of the understanding or only every operation relevant to discursive cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_SILU:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement if it treats judgement as the generative hinge between conceptual spontaneity and object-directed cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SILU:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 25","title":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4","context":"Chapter 25"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI","layer":"CH","title":"Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems","order":63,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"1770ab8dfa2434738a5f4e5e21512aaa426243fb561e3f6ed81c36b97dc8ce76","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AS1","text":"Pure reason appears compelled to answer questions concerning the unconditioned, although their objects cannot be given in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AS2","text":"The apparent uncertainty of transcendental problems is reframed as a defect in the ideas that generate them, not as ignorance of a hidden object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AS3","text":"Cosmological questions are distinctive because they concern the relation between an idea of totality and the empirical synthesis of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AS4","text":"Reason must provide a critical resolution of cosmological problems because those problems originate in reason’s own demand for absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AS5","text":"Dogmatic solutions fail by treating an idea of the unconditioned as cognition of an object; skeptical abstention fails by treating an internally generated dialectic as unavoidable ignorance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AS6","text":"The argument moves from the demand for answers, through the delimitation of legitimate transcendental questions, to critical diagnosis of the dialectical illusion producing them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:A1","text":"A question is necessarily answerable when the cognition that generates it also supplies the conditions under which its object is thinkable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:A2","text":"Questions concerning objects dependent on experience may remain unresolved when their answers require information not contained in the concepts that raise them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:A3","text":"A question concerning a transcendental object as a thing in itself lacks an object available for determinate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:A4","text":"Cosmological ideas presuppose phenomena and the empirical synthesis through which their objects are conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:A5","text":"Cosmological questions concern the demanded absolute totality of empirical synthesis rather than an independently given thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:A6","text":"Absolute totality cannot be given in any possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:A7","text":"Ideas of the unconditioned originate from reason’s own demand for completion and are not required for the explanation of particular phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:A8","text":"Empirical explanation extends only as far as explanatory conditions can be given in perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:A9","text":"The categories cannot determine a transcendental subject or object that is not given as a phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:A10","text":"A dogmatic solution falsely assumes that an idea of reason corresponds to an object cognizable under the laws of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:A11","text":"A critical solution determines the basis and validity of the cognition that generates a question rather than the alleged object of that question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:NX1","text":"Transcendental philosophy is not entitled to evade cosmological problems by invoking the finitude of human faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:NX2","text":"The unconditioned is not an object that can be encountered, completed, or measured in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:NX3","text":"A transcendental object is not thereby impossible because it is unknowable; its constitution is simply not determinable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:NX4","text":"A question about the determinate constitution of what cannot be presented under any predicate is not a genuine cognitive question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:NX5","text":"Cosmological ideas are not empirical hypotheses introduced to explain phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:NX6","text":"The absolute whole is not an empirical whole enlarged without limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:NX7","text":"Dogmatic metaphysics is not a satisfactory solution because it converts a rational demand into purported knowledge of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:NX8","text":"Critical resolution is not skeptical suspension of judgment concerning an unknown object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:D1","text":"Cosmological antinomies require resolution even though no empirical observation can decide their competing claims.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A4","CH_B337_SINI:A5","CH_B337_SINI:A6","CH_B337_SINI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:D2","text":"The questions of whether the world has a beginning, limits, simples, freedom, or an unconditioned being must be treated as problems of reason’s idea of totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A5","CH_B337_SINI:A6","CH_B337_SINI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:D3","text":"The inability to produce an empirical instance of the unconditioned cannot establish uncertainty about the transcendental problem.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A6","CH_B337_SINI:A7","CH_B337_SINI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:D4","text":"Questions about the soul or a necessary being, when posed as questions about things in themselves, terminate in the nullity of their object-determining demand.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A3","CH_B337_SINI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:D5","text":"The explanatory irrelevance of absolute totality to particular phenomena shows that cosmological ideas are generated independently of empirical explanatory need.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A7","CH_B337_SINI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:D6","text":"A valid transcendental resolution replaces asserted knowledge of the unconditioned with an account of why reason necessarily seeks it.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A7","CH_B337_SINI:A10","CH_B337_SINI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:SD1","text":"Transcendental dialectic becomes an internal critique of reason: reason both generates the demand for the unconditioned and supplies the means to expose the invalid objectification of that demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:SD2","text":"The antinomial conflict is reclassified from a dispute over inaccessible reality into a structural collision between reason’s demand for completeness and the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:SD3","text":"Critical certainty is possible without metaphysical object-knowledge because certainty attaches to the diagnosis of the cognitive procedure that produces the question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:SD4","text":"The distinction between cosmology and transcendental psychology depends on whether an empirical synthesis can be presupposed as the field to which an idea of totality is applied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:SD5","text":"Reason’s obligation to answer its own cosmological questions is fulfilled by dissolving their dogmatic form rather than by selecting one empirical or metaphysical alternative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the model in which every meaningful metaphysical question concerns a pre-existing object whose properties are merely hidden from inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the inference that non-empirical origin confers non-empirical objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the assumption that an infinite extension of empirical knowledge could yield intuition of absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the application of categories wherever no phenomenal object supplies their conditions of use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:DNX5","text":"The system excludes an external explanatory ground for the cosmological ideas, locating their source in reason’s internal drive toward the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:DNX6","text":"The system excludes a choice between dogmatic affirmation and confessed ignorance as the only possible responses to transcendental problems.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:SNX1","text":"No completed standpoint from which the totality of conditions can be intuited is available to finite empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:SNX2","text":"No correspondence between the idea of the unconditioned and a determinately cognizable object may be presupposed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:SNX3","text":"No empirical fact can finally validate or invalidate the absolute alternatives generated by cosmological reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:SNX4","text":"No transition from the necessity of thinking the unconditioned to knowledge of an unconditioned entity is admissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:FGS1","text":"Reason demands completeness of conditions → reason forms an idea of absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:FGS2","text":"Idea of absolute totality + empirical series of conditions → cosmological question concerning the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:FGS3","text":"Absence of an experiential presentation of totality → impossibility of empirical determination of the alleged object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:FGS4","text":"Mistaking the idea for object-cognition → dogmatic assertion, skeptical uncertainty, or antinomial conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:FGS5","text":"Critical inquiry into the origin and conditions of the idea → diagnosis of dialectical objectification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:FGS6","text":"Diagnosis of dialectic → determinate critical resolution without cognition of a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AF1","text":"Rational demand for answers → distinction between concept-internal and experience-dependent questions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AF2","text":"Experience-dependent uncertainty → exclusion from the model of transcendental necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AF3","text":"Cosmological idea of totality → recognition that its object concerns possible experience but exceeds all actual experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AF4","text":"Failure of empirical completion → rejection of ignorance as the proper diagnosis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AF5","text":"Exposure of the idea’s dialectic → critical dissolution of dogmatic object-claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:AF6","text":"Critical diagnosis of reason’s demand → resolution of transcendental problems without speculative object-knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:ME1","text":"The division between answerable and unanswerable questions is redrawn according to the source and conditions of a question, not according to its apparent metaphysical grandeur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:ME2","text":"Cosmological questions are reclassified from inquiries into a supersensible world-whole into inquiries into reason’s demand that empirical synthesis be absolutely complete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:ME3","text":"Ignorance and critical certainty cease to be opposites: ignorance concerns inaccessible objects, while certainty can concern the invalidity of objectifying ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:ME4","text":"Dogmatism and skepticism become parallel errors insofar as both retain the fiction of an unknown object corresponding to the cosmological idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:ME5","text":"The unconditioned becomes visible as a regulative demand internal to rational synthesis rather than as an empirically discoverable constituent of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text addresses whether pure reason must answer questions concerning the world, freedom, simplicity, necessity, and the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text distinguishes questions whose objects are conditioned by possible experience from questions that illegitimately seek determination of a transcendental object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text replaces the alternatives of metaphysical assertion and professed ignorance with a critical examination of the cognition that constructs the problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text denies possible experiential access to an absolutely completed series of conditions, whether conceived as temporal beginning, spatial limit, infinite divisibility, causal freedom, or necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central transition from apparent metaphysical ignorance to critical responsibility for resolving cosmological problems.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction depends centrally on the distinction between objects given in possible experience and alleged transcendental objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent insofar as cosmological ideas are simultaneously rooted in empirical synthesis and incapable of empirical completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it separates the source of a question from the availability of an object corresponding to that question.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:MRT5","text":"The system leaves underdetermined the precise positive status of the unconditioned once dogmatic objectification has been excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:MRT6","text":"The argument compresses a tension between reason’s asserted obligation to answer its questions and the claim that certain questions are answered only through the nullity of their object-demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SINI:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if critical solution is understood as diagnosis and dissolution of transcendental illusion rather than as a substantive theory of supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SINI:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 64","title":"Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems","context":"Chapter 64"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief","order":64,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"53b68438ca4cfca471deb5df29b8ad33dbebb87aff7d2354a4b452cf76423930","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:AS1","text":"The problem is to distinguish valid holding-for-true from merely private assent without reducing validity to subjective confidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:AS2","text":"The text reframes assent through the relation between subjective sufficiency, objective sufficiency, communicability, and agreement with an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:AS3","text":"It differentiates opinion, belief, and knowledge by their respective combinations of subjective and objective sufficiency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:AS4","text":"It restricts theoretical reason beyond experience from knowledge while preserving doctrinal belief as a regulative guide for reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:AS5","text":"It grounds moral belief in the necessity of acting under the moral law and in the practical need for unity between moral ends and reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:AS6","text":"The movement proceeds from private illusion → intersubjective test → graded assent → theoretical limitation → practical authorization of belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A1","text":"Holding a proposition to be true requires subjective causes in the judging subject even when it rests on objective grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A2","text":"A judgement has objective sufficiency when it is valid for every rational being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A3","text":"A judgement grounded only in the subject’s particular constitution is persuasion rather than conviction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A4","text":"Truth requires agreement with the object, and true judgements must therefore be mutually concordant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A5","text":"Communicability to other rational agents is an external test for the presumptive objectivity of a judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A6","text":"Subjective introspection alone cannot distinguish conviction from persuasion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A7","text":"Opinion is insufficient both subjectively and objectively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A8","text":"Belief is subjectively sufficient while recognized as objectively insufficient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A9","text":"Knowledge is sufficient both subjectively and objectively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A10","text":"Pure reason admits only universally necessary cognition and therefore excludes mere opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A11","text":"A theoretically insufficient judgement may count as belief only when it supplies necessary guidance for practical or rational activity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A12","text":"Moral law necessitates action independently of speculative knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A13","text":"The unity of moral ends requires belief in conditions that cannot be known theoretically, namely God and a future life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A14","text":"Moral certainty is subjective practical conviction rather than logical or communicable knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:A15","text":"Critical philosophy secures practical orientation chiefly by limiting speculative claims rather than by extending theoretical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:NX1","text":"Persuasion is not conviction, because its apparent objectivity is produced by a merely subjective ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:NX2","text":"Private certainty is not truth, because truth requires possible agreement among rational judgements through their common object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:NX3","text":"Strong feeling of assurance is not sufficient evidence of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:NX4","text":"Opinion is not permissible in pure mathematics, morality, or any domain governed by universal necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:NX5","text":"Speculative reason cannot know supersensible objects such as God and immortality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:NX6","text":"Doctrinal belief is not a hypothesis, because a hypothesis claims a determinate speculative conception of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:NX7","text":"Moral belief is not theoretical proof, logical certainty, or universally communicable cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:NX8","text":"Critical limitation is not an abandonment of practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:D1","text":"The stability of assent cannot be measured solely by introspective certainty, because subjective conviction may conceal private persuasion.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A1","CH_B337_SIOK:A3","CH_B337_SIOK:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:D2","text":"Testing whether one’s reasons can affect other rational agents functions as a fallible diagnostic for detecting private validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A2","CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_SIOK:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:D3","text":"The distinction among opinion, belief, and knowledge is a classification of sufficiency rather than a classification of psychological intensity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A7","CH_B337_SIOK:A8","CH_B337_SIOK:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:D4","text":"A judgement may guide action without becoming objective knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A8","CH_B337_SIOK:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:D5","text":"Pragmatical belief is contingent because it depends upon incomplete knowledge of means toward a chosen end.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A8","CH_B337_SIOK:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:D6","text":"Doctrinal belief can rationally guide inquiry into nature while remaining incapable of a speculative account of its object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A8","CH_B337_SIOK:A11","CH_B337_SIOK:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:D7","text":"Moral belief is practically necessary because rejection of its postulates would dissolve the agent’s own moral maxim.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A12","CH_B337_SIOK:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:D8","text":"Theoretical ignorance concerning God and immortality is compatible with moral certainty concerning their practical necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:D9","text":"Critical philosophy equalizes access to essential practical orientation by denying that such orientation depends on exceptional philosophical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A12","CH_B337_SIOK:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:SD1","text":"The system divides rational authority into objective cognition, regulative orientation, and moral commitment rather than allowing one mode of assent to govern all domains.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A9","CH_B337_SIOK:A11","CH_B337_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:SD2","text":"The inability of speculative reason to establish supersensible reality becomes the condition under which practical reason can legitimately require belief without counterfeiting knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A10","CH_B337_SIOK:A12","CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_SIOK:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:SD3","text":"Rational modesty is structurally required because claims exceeding communicable cognition must be held as belief, even where practical commitment is unwavering.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_SIOK:A8","CH_B337_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:SD4","text":"The critique of illusion is transformed from the elimination of subjective influence into the disciplined allocation of subjective grounds to their proper practical role.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A1","CH_B337_SIOK:A3","CH_B337_SIOK:A11","CH_B337_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:SD5","text":"The system preserves moral obligation against scepticism by making practical commitment depend on the agent’s relation to the moral law rather than on demonstrative metaphysical evidence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A12","CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:DNX1","text":"Objective validity cannot be grounded by sincerity, confidence, emotional intensity, or the felt necessity of a judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:DNX2","text":"Theoretical reason cannot legitimately convert a practical need for orientation into cognition of supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:DNX3","text":"Moral agency cannot wait for complete metaphysical knowledge before acting.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:DNX4","text":"Practical reason cannot sustain its highest moral end under the assumption that reality is wholly indifferent to the unity of moral ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:DNX5","text":"Philosophical authority cannot monopolize the practical conditions common to all rational human beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses a unified epistemic status in which metaphysical postulates could be simultaneously known, morally necessary, and universally demonstrable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the possibility that reason can possess an unmediated standpoint outside the conditions of human cognition and practical agency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:SNX3","text":"The system cannot admit that moral law is wholly motivationally inert, because moral belief derives its necessity from practical constraint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:SNX4","text":"The system denies that the final practical orientation of reason can be supplied by empirical nature alone without supersensible postulates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:FGS1","text":"Subjective assent + claimed objectivity → distinction between conviction and persuasion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:FGS2","text":"Object-agreement + possible intersubjective concordance → external diagnostic of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:FGS3","text":"Subjective sufficiency / objective sufficiency → opinion / belief / knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:FGS4","text":"Necessary theoretical domain → exclusion of opinion and restriction of speculative belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:FGS5","text":"Theoretical insufficiency + practical necessity → legitimate practical belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:FGS6","text":"Moral law + required unity of ends → moral postulates of God and immortality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:FGS7","text":"Critical denial of speculative knowledge → preservation of universally accessible practical orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:AF1","text":"Private assent → distinction between subjective and objective grounds → communicability test → taxonomy of assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:AF2","text":"Taxonomy of assent → exclusion of opinion from necessary cognition → limitation of speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:AF3","text":"Speculative limitation → doctrinal belief as rational guidance → moral necessity → moral belief in God and a future life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:AF4","text":"Moral belief without knowledge → negative achievement of critique → common accessibility of practical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:ME1","text":"Certainty is split into subjective conviction and objective certainty, preventing confidence from functioning as a direct index of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:ME2","text":"Belief is reclassified from irrational deficiency into a legitimate but bounded mode of rational orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:ME3","text":"Communicability becomes an epistemic test without becoming a final criterion identical to truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:ME4","text":"Metaphysical claims concerning God and immortality are relocated from theoretical explanation to practical necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:ME5","text":"Critique is reclassified as productive through restriction: it removes illegitimate knowledge claims while retaining moral orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:ME6","text":"The practical destination of reason becomes available to common understanding without requiring philosophical specialization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text classifies holding-for-true as opinion, belief, or knowledge according to subjective and objective sufficiency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text organizes validity through the relation among subjective grounds, objective grounds, objects, rational plurality, and communicability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text protects practical commitment by prohibiting speculative reason from presenting moral postulates as theoretical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human reason is finite, bounded by experience in cognition, yet required to act toward ends whose final unity exceeds temporal empirical fulfillment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central distinction between persuasion, conviction, opinion, belief, and knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:MRT2","text":"The structure is strongest where it separates subjective firmness from objective validity while preserving a non-cognitive role for rational belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:MRT3","text":"The system depends on a compressed transition from communicability as a presumptive test to objectivity as agreement with the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:MRT4","text":"The system leaves underdetermined how subjective moral grounds can compel belief while remaining categorically distinct from objective grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:MRT5","text":"The system contains a tension between excluding subjective grounds from speculative validity and permitting subjective practical necessity to authorize belief in supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:MRT6","text":"The system contains a further tension between the universality claimed for moral law and the admission that moral belief may weaken where moral disposition is absent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIOK:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement only if critical limitation is treated as the enabling condition of practical belief rather than as a merely negative conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIOK:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 91","title":"Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief","context":"Chapter 91"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6","order":65,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"78129339c9f915942e31a43d43842c57631829d15e88317fafc877be70981b4c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:AS1","text":"Transcendental logic must explain how a priori sensible manifold becomes cognition of objects and how the pure concepts enabling this cognition can be completely determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:AS2","text":"The argument proceeds transcendentally from conditions of possible cognition to the finite inventory of pure concepts required by those conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:AS3","text":"Cognition originates not in analysis but in synthesis of a manifold, which imagination performs and understanding renders conceptual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:AS4","text":"Categories arise because the function that unifies representations in judgment also unifies sensible manifold into an object of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:AS5","text":"The logical functions of judgment provide an exhaustive and systematic principle for deriving the table of categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:AS6","text":"The system secures its boundaries by excluding empirical, sensible, derivative, and merely logical items from the inventory of original categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A1","text":"Transcendental logic receives a priori sensible content, whereas general logic abstracts from cognitive content and awaits representations from elsewhere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A2","text":"Space and time contain an a priori manifold and condition the mind’s receptivity to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A3","text":"Synthesis joins manifold representations and is prior to analysis as the first condition for producing cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A4","text":"Imagination performs synthesis blindly and indispensably, while understanding reduces synthesis to concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A5","text":"Pure synthesis rests upon an a priori synthetic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A6","text":"Categories represent the necessary synthetic unity through which manifold intuition can be thought as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A7","text":"The same understanding-function produces analytical unity in judgment and synthetic unity in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A8","text":"There are exactly as many original categories as there are logical functions in all possible judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A9","text":"The category-table is systematic because it derives from the faculty of judgment rather than from inductive collection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A10","text":"Predicables are pure but derivative concepts generated from original categories and their combinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A11","text":"The four category-classes divide into mathematical categories concerning objects of intuition and dynamical categories concerning existence and relations of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A12","text":"In every categorical triad, the third member combines the first two but requires a distinct act of understanding and remains primitive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A13","text":"Community consists in reciprocal coordination among substances and corresponds to the form of disjunctive judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:A14","text":"Unity, truth, and perfection are logical criteria of cognition derived from transformed categories of quantity, not additional categories or properties of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:NX1","text":"General logic does not supply the content through which objects can be cognized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:NX2","text":"Analysis cannot generate the content of concepts because representations must precede their analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:NX3","text":"Synthesis without categorical unity is not yet cognition proper.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:NX4","text":"An inductive or rhapsodic inventory cannot establish either the number or the necessity of pure categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:NX5","text":"Modes of sensibility, empirical concepts, and derivative concepts are not original categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:NX6","text":"The third member of a category-triad is not merely a deduced result of its first two members.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:NX7","text":"Community is not unilateral causal dependence or a linear relation of principle to consequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:NX8","text":"Logical conditions of coherent cognition cannot be converted into predicates of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:NX9","text":"The critical exposition does not undertake a complete vocabulary, full definition, or exhaustive derivation of predicables.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:D1","text":"Cognition requires a sequence of sensible manifold, imaginative synthesis, and conceptual unity supplied by understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A2","CH_B337_SIPC:A3","CH_B337_SIPC:A4","CH_B337_SIPC:A5","CH_B337_SIPC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:D2","text":"Transcendental logic concerns the conceptual reduction of pure synthesis, whereas general logic concerns the analytical ordering of already given representations.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A1","CH_B337_SIPC:A3","CH_B337_SIPC:A4","CH_B337_SIPC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:D3","text":"The exhaustive category-table contains quantity as unity, plurality, totality; quality as reality, negation, limitation; relation as inherence and subsistence, causality and dependence, community; and modality as possibility and impossibility, existence and non-existence, necessity and contingency.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A8","CH_B337_SIPC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:D4","text":"The category-table supplies a topology for locating original concepts, deriving predicables, detecting omissions, and planning speculative a priori science.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A8","CH_B337_SIPC:A9","CH_B337_SIPC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:D5","text":"Mathematical categories concern objects of intuition without correlates, while dynamical categories concern existence and contain correlates.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:D6","text":"Totality, limitation, community, and necessity are each structurally composite but irreducible to simple addition of their preceding terms.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:D7","text":"Community represents contemporaneous reciprocal determination among independently existing substances rather than sequential cause-effect production.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:D8","text":"The predicates unity, truth, and perfection test the self-consistency of cognition, definition, and hypothesis without enlarging the transcendental category-table.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:SD1","text":"An object becomes cognitively available only through the convergence of receptivity, imaginative synthesis, and categorical unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A2","CH_B337_SIPC:A4","CH_B337_SIPC:A5","CH_B337_SIPC:A6","CH_B337_SIPC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:SD2","text":"The legitimacy and completeness of pure concepts become genealogical questions of origin in the functions of judgment rather than historical questions of conceptual collection.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A7","CH_B337_SIPC:A8","CH_B337_SIPC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:SD3","text":"A finite inventory of primitive categories can generate a large secondary field of a priori concepts without increasing the number of primitive concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A8","CH_B337_SIPC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:SD4","text":"The category-table functions simultaneously as a productive system for a priori concepts and as a policing mechanism against category inflation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A8","CH_B337_SIPC:A9","CH_B337_SIPC:A10","CH_B337_SIPC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:SD5","text":"The formal plan of speculative science is made dependent on the internal architecture of judgment rather than on the empirical organization of disciplines.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A7","CH_B337_SIPC:A8","CH_B337_SIPC:A9","CH_B337_SIPC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:DNX1","text":"Neither unsynthesized receptivity nor analytical manipulation of concepts can by itself yield cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:DNX2","text":"No original pure concept may stand outside the finite system generated by logical functions of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:DNX3","text":"No historical catalogue, however extensive, can substitute for a principle that guarantees the completeness of a priori concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:DNX4","text":"Formal conditions for the validity of cognition cannot establish ontological predicates of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:DNX5","text":"A relation among coexisting substances cannot be adequately represented by the unilateral structure of efficient causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:SNX1","text":"No object of possible cognition is available independently of a priori intuition, synthesis, and categorical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:SNX2","text":"No route leads from the logical coherence of cognition directly to determinations of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:SNX3","text":"No metaphysical expansion may alter the primitive category-table; legitimate expansion must remain derivative from its fixed elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:FGS1","text":"A priori intuition supplies a manifold that is given but not yet cognized as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:FGS2","text":"Imagination synthesizes the manifold into an ordered unity without yet producing conceptual cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:FGS3","text":"Understanding represents the necessary unity of synthesis through categories and thereby makes an object thinkable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:FGS4","text":"The logical functions of judgment determine the number and form of the original categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:FGS5","text":"The systematic table distinguishes primitive categories from predicables, sensible forms, empirical concepts, and logical criteria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:FGS6","text":"The resulting architecture supplies a formal plan for a priori science while preventing logical predicates from becoming ontological additions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:AF1","text":"A priori sensible manifold → imaginative synthesis → necessary categorical unity → cognition of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:AF2","text":"Logical judgment-functions → exhaustive category-table → systematic division of pure concepts → formal plan of speculative science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:AF3","text":"Candidate concept or predicate → test of origin and function → category, predicable, sensible or empirical form, or merely logical criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:AF4","text":"Disjunctive division → coordination of mutually excluding parts within a whole → reciprocal community among substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:ME1","text":"The origin of cognition is relocated from analysis of concepts to synthesis of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:ME2","text":"Judgment is reclassified from a merely logical form into the source-pattern of transcendental unity in objects of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:ME3","text":"The category-table replaces empirical accumulation with a closed derivational system capable of certifying completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:ME4","text":"Pure concepts are stratified into original categories, derivative predicables, sensible forms, empirical concepts, and logical criteria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:ME5","text":"Unity, truth, and perfection are moved from ontology to the formal self-consistency of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:ALT1","text":"Surface: The understanding contains a determinate table of pure categories corresponding to forms of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:ALT2","text":"Structural: Object-cognition requires the coordination of receptive intuition, imaginative synthesis, and categorical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: A systematic derivation from judgment displaces induction, historical enumeration, and arbitrary metaphysical catalogues.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Epistemic: Conditions for the possibility and coherence of cognition must not be mistaken for predicates of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the movement from sensible manifold through synthesis to the category-table and its exclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:MRT2","text":"The system is most determinate where the category count is tied to the fixed functions of judgment rather than to historical collection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:MRT3","text":"The asserted identity between the function of judgment and the function of synthesis is indispensable but not demonstrated within the supplied passage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:MRT4","text":"The third categories are structurally tense because they are both combinations of prior terms and irreducible products of a distinct intellectual act.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:MRT5","text":"The relation between imagination’s blind synthesis and understanding’s conceptual reduction is stipulated as necessary but remains mechanically under-specified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:MRT6","text":"The claim that categories apply a priori to objects of intuition is stated before its full warrant or limitation is supplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPC:MRT7","text":"The omission of definitions and a full predicable-tree preserves the generative architecture but leaves the semantic content of individual categories underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPC:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 27","title":"Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6","context":"Chapter 27"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9","order":66,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"0f7b488d2d534c75be9884e13f482dfc6e5795a430bedaaaeadd15d73a214429","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:AS1","text":"The legitimacy of a cognition’s use must be distinguished from the historical fact of its acquisition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:AS2","text":"Pure a priori conceptions require a proof of their objective right because their application cannot be validated by experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:AS3","text":"Space and time possess such validation insofar as they condition the possibility of objects as appearances in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:AS4","text":"Categories present a distinct problem because they are conditions of thought rather than conditions under which objects are intuitively given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:AS5","text":"The conception of cause exposes the problem because necessary and universal connection cannot be extracted from observed regularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:AS6","text":"Critical philosophy must either establish the a priori objective validity of pure understanding or renounce claims to cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:A1","text":"A deduction establishes the right to employ a conception, not merely the fact of possessing it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:A2","text":"Empirical conceptions can ordinarily claim objective reality through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:A3","text":"Pure a priori conceptions claim application to objects independently of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:A4","text":"An empirical account of a conception’s origin cannot establish its a priori objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:A5","text":"Space and time are pure forms of sensibility that condition the possibility of appearances as objects of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:A6","text":"The categories are pure conceptions of understanding that concern synthetic unity in thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:A7","text":"Objects can be given in sensuous intuition without thereby being shown to conform to the conditions required by understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:A8","text":"A conception of cause requires necessary and strictly universal connection between distinct states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:A9","text":"Experience yields comparative regularity but cannot yield necessity or strict universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:A10","text":"Pure conceptions lacking an a priori ground of objective application are void claims without warranted objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:NX1","text":"Pure a priori cognition is not justified by its psychological, physiological, or historical derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:NX2","text":"A transcendental deduction is not an empirical deduction of the origin of conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:NX3","text":"The frequent and socially accepted use of a conception is not proof of its legitimate employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:NX4","text":"Geometrical certainty does not supply a model sufficient for validating the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:NX5","text":"The categories are not forms through which objects are immediately given in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:NX6","text":"Causal necessity is not an additive association of repeatedly conjoined appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:NX7","text":"Empirical induction is not strict universality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:NX8","text":"A pure conception is not an empirical production without forfeiting its distinctive character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:D1","text":"The inquiry into pure cognition must shift from the genesis of representations to the conditions under which they can rightfully refer to objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A1","CH_B337_SIPT:A3","CH_B337_SIPT:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:D2","text":"Space and time can be validated through their constitutive role in the givenness of appearances, whereas categories cannot be validated by that route.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A5","CH_B337_SIPT:A6","CH_B337_SIPT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:D3","text":"The objective validity of categories is more problematic than that of pure intuition because their conformity is not secured by the mere appearance of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A5","CH_B337_SIPT:A6","CH_B337_SIPT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:D4","text":"The conception of cause must be grounded a priori or abandoned as objectively empty.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A8","CH_B337_SIPT:A9","CH_B337_SIPT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:D5","text":"The extension of spatial cognition beyond sensuous intuition becomes equivocal when categories are used without a prior determination of their limits.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A5","CH_B337_SIPT:A6","CH_B337_SIPT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:D6","text":"A transcendental deduction is necessary wherever pure thought claims to determine objects rather than merely order empirically acquired representations.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A1","CH_B337_SIPT:A3","CH_B337_SIPT:A6","CH_B337_SIPT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:SD1","text":"The text converts the problem of metaphysical knowledge into a juridical problem of entitlement: possession of a concept does not authorize its objective use.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A1","CH_B337_SIPT:A3","CH_B337_SIPT:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:SD2","text":"The division between sensibility and understanding creates an asymmetry of proof in which intuitive forms possess immediate evidential purchase while pure concepts require a distinct legitimating procedure.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A5","CH_B337_SIPT:A6","CH_B337_SIPT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:SD3","text":"Necessity becomes the discriminating mark that separates transcendental cognition from empirical generalization.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A8","CH_B337_SIPT:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:SD4","text":"The possibility of objective experience is made dependent on establishing that subjective functions of synthesis have valid application to objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A6","CH_B337_SIPT:A7","CH_B337_SIPT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:DNX1","text":"Subjective conditions of thinking are not self-validating conditions of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:DNX2","text":"The appearance of objects in intuition is not sufficient to prove that they are lawfully unified under pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:DNX3","text":"The empirical availability of causal sequences is not sufficient to establish causal necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:DNX4","text":"Pure reason cannot claim objective cognition merely because its concepts are indispensable to its own activity of thinking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:DNX5","text":"Knowledge beyond possible experience cannot remain legitimate if the objective scope of pure concepts is left undetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:SNX1","text":"The text forecloses an unexamined transition from a concept’s subjective possession to its objective authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:SNX2","text":"The text forecloses a metaphysics secured by inherited concepts, common usage, or inductive success.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:SNX3","text":"The text treats the absence of a transcendental ground as the collapse of the claim to pure cognition rather than as a merely local explanatory gap.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:FGS1","text":"Possession of a conception → distinction between factual origin and juridical right.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:FGS2","text":"Claim of a priori objective application → insufficiency of empirical genesis and empirical confirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:FGS3","text":"Pure intuition as condition of givenness → immediate validation of space and time for appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:FGS4","text":"Pure understanding as condition of synthesis without intuitive givenness → unresolved possibility of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:FGS5","text":"Causal necessity exceeding empirical regularity → demand for transcendental deduction or classification as chimera.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:FGS6","text":"Failure to establish the scope of categories → renunciation of warranted cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:AF1","text":"Juridical distinction of right and fact → identification of pure a priori claims → rejection of empirical derivation as validation → validation of sensibility through intuitive givenness → exposure of the categorical gap → causal necessity as test case → necessity of transcendental deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:ME1","text":"Concepts are reclassified by the difference between their origin, their possession, and their right to objective employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:ME2","text":"Space and time are separated from categories not by their a priority alone, but by their different relation to the givenness of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:ME3","text":"Causality is reclassified from an observed pattern into a claim of necessary synthesis requiring independent authorization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:ME4","text":"The boundary of possible experience becomes a jurisdictional limit determined by the successful deduction of pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:ME5","text":"The apparent opposition between empiricism and pure reason is displaced by a prior question concerning the conditions of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:ALT1","text":"Surface — Pure conceptions such as cause require proof that they can apply to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:ALT2","text":"Structural — Sensibility supplies conditions of intuitive givenness, while understanding supplies functions of synthetic unity whose objectivity is not immediately secured.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Philosophical inquiry is redirected from explaining how concepts arise to adjudicating their right to claim objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Necessity and strict universality cannot be derived from successively observed events and therefore require an a priori ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:ALT5","text":"Critical-Jurisdictional — Claims to cognition beyond possible experience remain inadmissible until the scope of pure concepts has been established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the distinction between factual genesis and juridical validity to the necessity of transcendental deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:MRT2","text":"The extracted system preserves the decisive asymmetry between pure intuition and pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined with respect to the positive proof by which categories acquire objective validity, because this section establishes the demand for deduction rather than supplying its completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:MRT4","text":"The structure is most determinate where causal necessity is opposed to empirical regularity and induction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:MRT5","text":"The system depends on the asserted distinction between objects being given in intuition and objects being cognized through synthetic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:MRT6","text":"A structural tension remains between the claim that phenomena can be given without functions of thought and the claim that cognition of objects requires the conditions of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIPT:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement only if transcendental deduction is treated as a condition of legitimacy rather than as a genealogy of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIPT:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 28","title":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9","context":"Chapter 28"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas","order":67,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"cba97ca5517cfa9d4d0079fca6cc3d67602b787422e5c270449edcd8c9d2cb6e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:AS1","text":"Reason must systematize its ideas by deriving them from categories while extending those categories beyond every possible empirical completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:AS2","text":"A given conditioned compels reason to seek the complete antecedent series of conditions and, through that series, the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:AS3","text":"Only subordinated serial syntheses support this demand; co-ordinated aggregates and descending consequences do not.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:AS4","text":"Time, spatial limitation, material division, causation, and contingent existence supply the serial forms through which cosmological ideas are constructed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:AS5","text":"The resulting ideas classify the possible forms of absolute completeness without establishing that such completeness can be given in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:AS6","text":"Cosmological inquiry therefore concerns the problematic relation between phenomenal regress and an unconditioned that reason necessarily thinks but cannot preliminarily exhibit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A1","text":"Pure transcendental conceptions originate in the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A2","text":"Reason does not create concepts but frees concepts of understanding from the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A3","text":"Given a conditioned, reason demands the complete series of its conditions and thereby the absolutely unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A4","text":"A category becomes a transcendental idea when reason extends its synthesis toward the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A5","text":"Reason demands absolute totality only where conditions form a subordinated series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A6","text":"The necessary direction of cosmological synthesis is regressive, from the given conditioned toward its antecedent conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A7","text":"A progressive series of consequences is not required for the possibility or complete understanding of a given conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A8","text":"Cosmological ideas concern only the absolute completeness of phenomenal synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A9","text":"Time furnishes an intrinsic series of antecedent conditions, whereas space furnishes regress only through the successive synthesis of limitation and measurement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A10","text":"Matter furnishes a regressive series because every material whole is conditioned by its parts and every part by further parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A11","text":"Causality and contingent existence furnish regressive series because effects require causes and contingency requires a conditioning ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A12","text":"Substance, accidents, and reciprocal community do not furnish the required series because their relations are co-ordinated rather than subordinated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A13","text":"The complete regressive synthesis is an idea whose sensible possibility remains unestablished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A14","text":"The unconditioned can be represented either as the complete infinite series of conditioned members or as a first member not conditioned by another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A15","text":"The four cosmological ideas are completeness of composition, division, origination, and dependence of changeable existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:A16","text":"“World” denotes the totality of phenomena mathematically, while “nature” denotes their dynamical unity through internal causal connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:NX1","text":"Reason is not an independent source of pure concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:NX2","text":"A transcendental idea is not a category left within the limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:NX3","text":"Absolute totality is not demanded of a descending sequence of consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:NX4","text":"Absolute totality is not demanded of a merely co-ordinated aggregate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:NX5","text":"Cosmological totality is not a totality of things considered apart from phenomenal exposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:NX6","text":"Spatial parts are not intrinsically antecedent and consequent in the manner of temporal moments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:NX7","text":"Substance and accident are not a serial conditioning relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:NX8","text":"The complete synthesis required by reason is not thereby established as sensuously possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:NX9","text":"Infinite regress is not a regress that can be completed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:NX10","text":"Cosmological ideas do not concern noumena, despite exceeding every possible empirical completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:D1","text":"The table of cosmological ideas must be selected from categories capable of generating regressive serial conditioning rather than from all categories without distinction.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A4","CH_B337_SISC:A5","CH_B337_SISC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:D2","text":"The distinction between regress and progress determines whether a totality is rationally necessary or merely arbitrarily investigable.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A6","CH_B337_SISC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:D3","text":"Past time must be thought as conditionally complete relative to a present moment, while future time need not be thought as complete for that moment to be given.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A6","CH_B337_SISC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:D4","text":"Spatial magnitude becomes cosmologically relevant only when limitation is construed as dependence upon further space.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A5","CH_B337_SISC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:D5","text":"Material divisibility becomes a cosmological problem because its completion would require arrival at either nothing or the simple.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A10","CH_B337_SISC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:D6","text":"The dynamical cosmological problems arise only from causal origination and the dependence of contingent existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A11","CH_B337_SISC:A12","CH_B337_SISC:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:D7","text":"The unconditioned is reason’s final object of inquiry, although reason initially represents it through the idea of absolute totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A3","CH_B337_SISC:A13","CH_B337_SISC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:D8","text":"The same formal demand for completeness produces alternative representations of the unconditioned as either an infinite totality or an unconditioned first member.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A3","CH_B337_SISC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:SD1","text":"Cosmological inquiry converts categories from rules for organizing experience into problems concerning the ultimate closure of phenomenal conditioning.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A1","CH_B337_SISC:A2","CH_B337_SISC:A4","CH_B337_SISC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:SD2","text":"The world is reconstituted as a field of regress rather than as an immediately available total object.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A6","CH_B337_SISC:A8","CH_B337_SISC:A13","CH_B337_SISC:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:SD3","text":"Mathematical and dynamical cosmological questions differ because the former concern composition and division of magnitude, while the latter concern origination and existential dependence.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A9","CH_B337_SISC:A10","CH_B337_SISC:A11","CH_B337_SISC:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:SD4","text":"The necessity of seeking completion exceeds the capacity to determine whether completion belongs to phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A3","CH_B337_SISC:A8","CH_B337_SISC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:SD5","text":"Cosmological ideas are structurally transcendent without becoming inquiries into objects outside sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A2","CH_B337_SISC:A8","CH_B337_SISC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the possibility that a conditioned phenomenon could be intelligible without a regress toward conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the possibility that all relations of dependence are merely lateral, reciprocal, or aggregate relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the treatment of the world as a completed empirical object available to intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the inference from the necessity of an idea to the empirical reality of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:DNX5","text":"The system excludes noumenal explanation as a solution to the demand for cosmological completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:SNX1","text":"The argument is organized around an unavailable completion: reason cannot cease demanding the unconditioned, yet no completed phenomenal synthesis is given in advance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses any final standpoint from within experience at which the totality of the world could be surveyed as present.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:SNX3","text":"The unconditioned functions as the absent limit of serial explanation rather than as an empirically secured member of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:FGS1","text":"Given conditioned phenomenon → reason demands antecedent conditions → category is extended beyond empirical restriction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:FGS2","text":"Serial subordination → regressive synthesis → representation of absolute totality → orientation toward the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:FGS3","text":"Non-serial relation → absence of required regress → exclusion from cosmological-idea formation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:FGS4","text":"Temporal, spatial, material, causal, and existential conditioning → four admissible forms of cosmological totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:FGS5","text":"Necessary idea of completion → unresolved possibility of phenomenal completion → transcendental cosmological problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:FGS6","text":"Unconditioned as entire infinite series or first unconditioned member → alternative forms of cosmological regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:AF1","text":"Conditioned phenomenon → demand for conditions → regress to antecedents → absolute totality → unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:AF2","text":"Categories → test for subordinated serial synthesis → exclusion of nonserial categories → four cosmological ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:AF3","text":"Time and spatial limitation → mathematical completeness; division, causality, and contingency → dynamical completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:AF4","text":"Necessary representation of totality → distinction between infinite regress and first condition → unresolved question of empirical realizability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:AF5","text":"Phenomenal world as totality → mathematical composition and division / dynamical causal unity → cosmological classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:ME1","text":"The relevant distinction is shifted from finite versus infinite objects to regressive versus progressive syntheses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:ME2","text":"Space is reclassified from a mere aggregate into a cosmologically relevant field insofar as limitation requires successive regression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:ME3","text":"Categories are sorted by their capacity to encode asymmetric conditioning rather than by their ordinary logical classification alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:ME4","text":"“World” becomes the name for the totality-problem of phenomena, while “nature” becomes the name for their causal unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:ME5","text":"Infinity is reclassified as potential incompletability of regress rather than as an empirically completed whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:ME6","text":"The unconditioned is relocated from a possible empirical item to the limiting aim implicit in rational explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text constructs a table of four cosmological ideas corresponding to admissible serial applications of the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text distinguishes subordinated conditions from co-ordinated relations and makes that distinction the criterion of cosmological relevance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text redirects inquiry from objects and consequences toward the regressive conditions that make given phenomena possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text treats temporality as intrinsically ordered by antecedence, spatiality as conditionally regressive through limitation, and the world as a phenomenal totality never simply given as a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — The text establishes a necessary rational demand for totality while withholding any prior warrant that the demanded totality can be sensibly realized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the transition from categories to cosmological ideas because they specify the originating faculty, the demand for totality, and the criterion of serial regression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:MRT2","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning whether any complete phenomenal regress is possible, because this possibility is explicitly deferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent insofar as it consistently restricts cosmological inquiry to conditioned phenomenal series and excludes nonserial relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where the regress/progress distinction selects the legitimate field of rational totalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:MRT5","text":"The structure depends on an unresolved transition from the necessity of thinking the unconditioned to the possibility of representing its corresponding totality in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISC:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the four ideas are treated as products of a prior logic of conditioning rather than as a merely enumerated taxonomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 61","title":"Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas","context":"Chapter 61"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements","order":68,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"81bd4c34c8a5afa170beb8c3cefdab343d9c4a36e07f8456c84958b99ffb94f2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:AS1","text":"The argument determines the universal condition under which cognition can constitute a judgment and distinguishes that condition from grounds of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:AS2","text":"It establishes noncontradiction as the necessary negative condition of every judgment independently of content and object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:AS3","text":"It separates logical consistency from object-correspondence, warrant, and truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:AS4","text":"It restricts the positive truth-yielding use of noncontradiction to analytical judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:AS5","text":"It diagnoses the familiar temporal formula as a synthetic determination improperly inserted into a purely formal principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:AS6","text":"It relocates contradiction from simultaneous predicate opposition to incompatibility between a predicate and the concept of its subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:AS7","text":"It preserves the principle as an inviolable condition of thought while denying it authority to establish synthetic truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:A1","text":"A judgment that contradicts itself is null as cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:A2","text":"Noncontradiction is the universal negative condition of judgment independently of content and relation to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:A3","text":"A noncontradictory judgment may fail to correspond to its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:A4","text":"A noncontradictory judgment may lack both a priori and a posteriori grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:A5","text":"In an analytical judgment, truth is recognizable through the principle of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:A6","text":"The principle of contradiction is fully sufficient for analytical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:A7","text":"The principle of contradiction is a necessary condition of truth but not its determining ground in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:A8","text":"The principle of contradiction provides no direct establishment of the truth of synthetic propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:A9","text":"The formula that a thing cannot be and not be at the same time introduces temporal qualification and synthetic combination into the formal principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:A10","text":"Contrary predicates may apply to the same thing successively without contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:A11","text":"In an analytical judgment, the predicate is contained in the concept of the subject, so its contrary conflicts with the subject without requiring a temporal condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:NX1","text":"The principle of contradiction is not a sufficient guarantee that a judgment corresponds to its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:NX2","text":"Logical consistency is not equivalent to either truth or justification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:NX3","text":"The principle of contradiction is not an objective or content-bearing criterion of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:NX4","text":"The principle of contradiction does not positively establish synthetic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:NX5","text":"Contradiction is not fundamentally a rule about the temporal coexistence of predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:NX6","text":"Opposition between two predicates is not by itself contradiction in the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:NX7","text":"Analytical and synthetic judgments do not possess the same mode of truth-recognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:D1","text":"Contradiction nullifies a judgment before any question of object-correspondence arises.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A1","CH_B337_SISP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:D2","text":"Noncontradiction can exclude contradiction-based error without confirming the judgment’s truth.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A2","CH_B337_SISP:A3","CH_B337_SISP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:D3","text":"In analytical cognition, negating the contrary of a contained predicate permits recognition of the predicate’s truth.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A5","CH_B337_SISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:D4","text":"The principle is universally necessary as a logical condition but positively sufficient only within analytical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A6","CH_B337_SISP:A7","CH_B337_SISP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:D5","text":"A synthetic judgment may satisfy the principle of contradiction while remaining false or groundless.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A3","CH_B337_SISP:A4","CH_B337_SISP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:D6","text":"The temporal formula fails to express pure contradiction because contrary predicates can belong to one thing at different times.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A9","CH_B337_SISP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:D7","text":"The apparent contradiction in successive predicate attribution results from confusing predicate opposition with contradiction between predicate and subject concept.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A10","CH_B337_SISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:D8","text":"The same-time condition is required for synthetic predicate opposition but not for analytical contradiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A9","CH_B337_SISP:A10","CH_B337_SISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:SD1","text":"The argument separates formal validity, object-correspondence, and epistemic grounding into distinct levels of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A2","CH_B337_SISP:A3","CH_B337_SISP:A4","CH_B337_SISP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:SD2","text":"Analytical cognition converts a purely negative logical condition into a positive recognition of truth because conceptual containment supplies the relevant content.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A5","CH_B337_SISP:A6","CH_B337_SISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:SD3","text":"The temporal formula conflates logical contradiction with the changing states of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A9","CH_B337_SISP:A10","CH_B337_SISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:SD4","text":"Critical reasoning must apply the principle of contradiction universally as a safeguard while withholding from it the power to prove synthetic truth.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A2","CH_B337_SISP:A7","CH_B337_SISP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:SD5","text":"The formal principle cannot close the gap between conceptual consistency and the determining grounds of cognition about objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A3","CH_B337_SISP:A4","CH_B337_SISP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:SD6","text":"The principle is formally without content yet yields analytical truth only through the determinate content of subject concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A5","CH_B337_SISP:A6","CH_B337_SISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:DNX1","text":"The system excludes consistency as sufficient epistemic warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:DNX2","text":"The system excludes a purely logical passage from formal validity to object-correspondence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:DNX3","text":"The system excludes temporal simultaneity as the foundation of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:DNX4","text":"The system excludes predicate-level opposition as sufficient to constitute contradiction in the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:DNX5","text":"The system excludes a single undifferentiated truth-test for analytical and synthetic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:DNX6","text":"The system excludes logical self-sufficiency as a basis for synthetic knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:SNX1","text":"No purely formal principle can guarantee the relation between cognition and its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:SNX2","text":"The determining ground of synthetic truth remains outside the principle of contradiction and is not supplied by the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:SNX3","text":"Becoming and temporal change cannot be absorbed into pure contradiction without transforming the principle into a synthetic proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:SNX4","text":"The principle cannot function as a total ontology of being and non-being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:SNX5","text":"The text forecloses the possibility that logical noncontradiction alone could complete the theory of truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:FGS1","text":"A candidate judgment is first subjected to the condition of noncontradiction, and contradiction nullifies it.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A1","CH_B337_SISP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:FGS2","text":"Passing the contradiction test establishes only formal admissibility because correspondence and grounds remain independently unresolved.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A3","CH_B337_SISP:A4","CH_B337_SISP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:FGS3","text":"When the judgment is analytical, containment of the predicate in the subject concept allows noncontradiction to recognize truth positively.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A5","CH_B337_SISP:A6","CH_B337_SISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:FGS4","text":"When the judgment is synthetic, noncontradiction remains a necessary guard but cannot establish truth.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A7","CH_B337_SISP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:FGS5","text":"The familiar formula is decomposed into a subject, a predicate, its contrary, and a same-time condition.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:FGS6","text":"Successive applicability of contrary predicates demonstrates that temporal opposition is not logical contradiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:FGS7","text":"Recasting contradiction as incompatibility between a predicate and the subject concept restores the analytical principle and preserves the boundary around synthetic cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A8","CH_B337_SISP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:AF1","text":"Universal logical condition → distinction between consistency and truth → restriction of positive validity to analytical cognition → diagnosis of temporal contamination → distinction between predicate opposition and subject-concept contradiction → bounded jurisdiction over synthetic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:AF2","text":"Contradiction → nullification; noncontradiction → negative validity; conceptual containment → analytical truth-recognition; synthetic content → unresolved truth determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:ME1","text":"Truth is divided into formal noncontradiction, object-correspondence, and epistemic grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:ME2","text":"The principle of contradiction is reclassified from a complete truth criterion into a universal condition with domain-limited positive authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:ME3","text":"The analytical–synthetic distinction becomes a distinction of logical jurisdiction rather than merely a distinction of proposition types.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:ME4","text":"Contradiction is relocated from temporal co-occurrence to incompatibility between a predicate and a subject concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:ME5","text":"Change over time is prevented from being misclassified as logical contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:ME6","text":"The familiar formula’s apparent ontological reach is reduced to a restricted logical function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:ALT1","text":"Surface — Judgments containing contradiction are null, while noncontradictory judgments may still be false or groundless.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Logical form, object-relation, epistemic grounds, and analytical–synthetic classification are separated into distinct functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument establishes a method of preserving a formal rule while refusing to let it exceed its jurisdiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Conceptual incompatibility is distinguished from the successive states of an object, and logical form is denied the power to generate object-truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from noncontradiction as negative condition to its restricted positive use in analytical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the decisive distinction between logical admissibility and truth grounded in an object or justification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if the principle is read as universally necessary but positively sufficient only for analytical judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:MRT4","text":"The determining grounds of synthetic truth remain underdetermined because the passage excludes logical sufficiency without specifying their positive source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:MRT5","text":"The strongest structural point is the separation of logical jurisdiction from objective truth; the main fault line is the relation between contentless form and analytically content-dependent truth-recognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISP:MRT6","text":"The reconstruction preserves the real argumentative movement while compressing the illustrative cases and the detailed diagnosis of the temporal formula.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 45","title":"Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements","context":"Chapter 45"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding","order":69,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"60adf791fddf265464c4130fbc6c0a31bd13ebf07d1144b93c6292ec7388fc03","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:AS1","text":"Synthetic a priori principles must be shown capable of governing every possible object without being confused with empirical laws or mathematical propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:AS2","text":"Pure understanding supplies the necessary rule-structure under which alone objects of experience become cognizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:AS3","text":"The categories systematically determine four classes of principles whose modes of evidence differ according to whether synthesis concerns intuition or existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:AS4","text":"The synthesis required to apprehend space and time establishes every phenomenal intuition as an extensive quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:AS5","text":"The identity of mathematical construction and phenomenal apprehension grounds the objective validity of mathematics for appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:AS6","text":"The transition of sensation from zero to a determinate amount establishes the real in perception as an intensive quantity or degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:AS7","text":"The continuity of extensive and intensive quantity constrains empirical inference without determining particular physical realities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:AS8","text":"The argument moves transcendentally and systematically from conditions of object-cognition to quantitative principles, their applications, and their limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A1","text":"Pure understanding supplies the necessary rules without which no presented object can be cognized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A2","text":"Empirical laws of nature are particular applications of higher principles of pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A3","text":"Necessity grounded in concepts distinguishes principles of pure understanding from empirical propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A4","text":"Principles of pure understanding proceed from pure concepts, whereas mathematical principles derive from pure intuitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A5","text":"The possibility and objective validity of a priori mathematical synthesis depend on pure understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A6","text":"The table of categories determines the table of principles as rules for the categories’ objective employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A7","text":"Synthesis in the application of categories to possible experience is directed either to intuition or to existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A8","text":"A priori conditions of intuition are absolutely necessary, whereas conditions of existence are necessary only under the condition of empirical thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A9","text":"Every phenomenon has its form in the a priori intuitions of space or time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A10","text":"Apprehension of determinate space or time requires the productive synthesis of a homogeneous manifold under synthetic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A11","text":"An extensive quantity is one whose represented parts precede and make possible the representation of the whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A12","text":"Perception is empirical consciousness containing sensation as its material element.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A13","text":"The determinate quality of sensation is given only a posteriori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A14","text":"Every sense has a determinate degree of receptivity below which sensation is not perceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A15","text":"The magnitude of sensation can be synthesized as an ascent from zero to a given consciousness, although sensation is apprehended in one moment rather than successively through parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A16","text":"A quantity apprehended as unity and varied through approximation to zero is an intensive quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A17","text":"Space, time, and phenomenal reality are continuous because none contains a smallest possible constituent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:A18","text":"Phenomena are not things in themselves and are necessarily subject to the formal conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:NX1","text":"No empirical proposition becomes a principle of pure understanding merely by attaining extensive generality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:NX2","text":"Mathematical principles themselves are not principles of pure understanding; only the grounds of their possibility and objective validity belong to the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:NX3","text":"“Mathematical” and “dynamical” designate modes of application, not principles belonging respectively to mathematics and physical dynamics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:NX4","text":"Phenomena are not objects given independently of the formal conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:NX5","text":"Sensation does not possess extensive quantity because its apprehension does not proceed successively from parts to a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:NX6","text":"Rules concerning the addition or subtraction of equals are analytical rather than axiomatic, while determinate numerical equations are singular synthetic formulae rather than universal axioms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:NX7","text":"The empirical quality of a sensation cannot be anticipated a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:NX8","text":"The absence of perceptible reality does not constitute experiential proof of empty space or empty time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:NX9","text":"Differences in phenomenal reality cannot be reduced necessarily to differences of aggregation or extensive quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:NX10","text":"The continuity of change is not established within this argument because causal determination requires empirical principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:NX11","text":"The possibility of explaining material differences through intensive degrees is not an assertion that matter is actually constituted in that manner.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:D1","text":"Quantity yields Axioms of Intuition, quality yields Anticipations of Perception, relation yields Analogies of Experience, and modality yields Postulates of Empirical Thought.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:D2","text":"The principles of quantity and quality possess intuitive and apodeictic evidence, whereas those of relation and modality possess discursive but complete certainty conditioned by empirical thought.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A6","CH_B337_SISR:A7","CH_B337_SISR:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:D3","text":"Every phenomenon considered as an intuition is an extensive quantity whose apprehension proceeds successively from part to part.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A9","CH_B337_SISR:A10","CH_B337_SISR:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:D4","text":"Geometry applies necessarily to phenomena because the same synthesis determines pure space and makes empirical spatial apprehension possible.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A4","CH_B337_SISR:A5","CH_B337_SISR:A9","CH_B337_SISR:A10","CH_B337_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:D5","text":"The real corresponding to sensation possesses intensive quantity because its magnitude is determined through a scale from zero without successive composition from parts.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A12","CH_B337_SISR:A15","CH_B337_SISR:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:D6","text":"Phenomena are continuous both in their extensive form and in the intensive degree of their perceptual reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A9","CH_B337_SISR:A10","CH_B337_SISR:A11","CH_B337_SISR:A15","CH_B337_SISR:A16","CH_B337_SISR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:D7","text":"Neither perception nor experience can prove the complete absence of phenomenal reality and therefore cannot establish the existence of empty space or empty time.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A12","CH_B337_SISR:A14","CH_B337_SISR:A16","CH_B337_SISR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:D8","text":"Equal extensive spaces may be completely filled while differing in intensive degree, so differences of material quantity do not require intervening voids.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A11","CH_B337_SISR:A15","CH_B337_SISR:A16","CH_B337_SISR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:D9","text":"The property that sensation has a degree can be anticipated a priori even though the particular quality and amount of any sensation remain empirical.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A13","CH_B337_SISR:A15","CH_B337_SISR:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SD1","text":"Objectivity within possible experience consists in conformity to the a priori syntheses that make an object presentable and cognizable.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A1","CH_B337_SISR:A6","CH_B337_SISR:A9","CH_B337_SISR:A10","CH_B337_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SD2","text":"The applicability of mathematics to experience is not an external correspondence but an identity between the synthesis constructing pure intuition and the synthesis apprehending appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A4","CH_B337_SISR:A5","CH_B337_SISR:A9","CH_B337_SISR:A10","CH_B337_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SD3","text":"Appearance is doubly quantified: its spatiotemporal form is extensive, while its sensible reality is intensive.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A11","CH_B337_SISR:A12","CH_B337_SISR:A15","CH_B337_SISR:A16","CH_B337_SISR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SD4","text":"A priori cognition reaches the material dimension of experience only by determining its invariant quantitative form, never its specific sensible content.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A12","CH_B337_SISR:A13","CH_B337_SISR:A15","CH_B337_SISR:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SD5","text":"Nonperception cannot be converted into an ontology of absence because continuous degrees may fall below the receptivity required for perception.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A14","CH_B337_SISR:A16","CH_B337_SISR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SD6","text":"Transcendental principles delimit the field of admissible empirical explanation without selecting which admissible explanation is actual.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A2","CH_B337_SISR:A13","CH_B337_SISR:A17","CH_B337_SISR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:DNX1","text":"Objects cannot first be cognized as ruleless and only subsequently brought under principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:DNX2","text":"Repeated experience cannot generate the necessity claimed by pure principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:DNX3","text":"Sensible objects cannot retain phenomenal objectivity while being exempted from space, time, and synthetic apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:DNX4","text":"A continuum cannot be constituted from indivisible elements given prior to the continuum itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:DNX5","text":"Failure to detect a sensation cannot license the inference that no phenomenal reality occupies the relevant space or time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:DNX6","text":"Quantitative difference cannot be exhausted by multiplicity, aggregation, or spatial extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:DNX7","text":"A transcendental demonstration of possibility cannot determine which empirical constitution actually obtains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:DNX8","text":"Complete a priori certainty need not always possess immediate or intuitive evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SNX1","text":"Noumenal structure is unavailable as a criterion for either mathematical truth or its application to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SNX2","text":"Zero functions as the limiting negation of phenomenal reality rather than as a positively perceived state.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SNX3","text":"The origin and specific quality of sensation remain structurally inaccessible to a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SNX4","text":"The actual constitution of matter is absent from the transcendental system even where the system excludes allegedly necessary physical hypotheses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SNX5","text":"The productive synthesis that generates determinate intuition is treated as an enabling operation rather than itself being generated by the principles proved here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:SNX6","text":"Objects exempt from spatiotemporal conditions are excluded from possible experience without being positively described or demonstrated impossible in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:FGS1","text":"Presented object → necessary rule-subjection → pure understanding → a priori principles → empirical laws as particular applications.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:FGS2","text":"Category table → objective-use rules → quantity/Axioms → quality/Anticipations → relation/Analogies → modality/Postulates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:FGS3","text":"Objective synthesis → composition of homogeneous manifolds through aggregation or coalition → mathematical use; necessary nexus of heterogeneous existences → dynamical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:FGS4","text":"Space or time → homogeneous manifold → successive productive synthesis → parts-before-whole → extensive quantity → objective validity of geometry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:FGS5","text":"Sensation → empirical consciousness → abstraction from empirical quality → ascent from zero → instantaneous quantitative unity → intensive degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:FGS6","text":"Intensive continuity + limited sensory receptivity → possible imperceptible reality → failure of experiential proofs of void → admissibility of degree-based explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:FGS7","text":"A priori determination closes at form, quantity, and degree; specific qualities, causal changes, and actual physical constitutions remain empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:AF1","text":"Necessity of principles → source in pure understanding → exclusion of empirical and mathematical conflation → category-guided classification → mathematical/dynamical bifurcation → extensive synthesis of intuition → validation of mathematics for phenomena → intensive synthesis of sensation → continuity → restriction of void-inferences → empirical boundary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ME1","text":"Cognizable objecthood is reclassified as an effect of necessary rule-governed synthesis rather than as independently given material later subjected to rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ME2","text":"Laws of nature are repositioned from autonomous empirical regularities to applications of higher a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ME3","text":"Mathematical applicability is relocated from correspondence with things in themselves to the constitutive spatiotemporal form of appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ME4","text":"Quantity is divided into extensive composition and intensive degree, preventing magnitude from being identified solely with multiplicity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ME5","text":"The empirical matter of perception becomes partially anticipable without its empirical quality becoming knowable a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ME6","text":"Empty space and empty time are reclassified from possible experiential findings to conclusions that perception cannot establish.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ME7","text":"Axiomatic status is narrowed to universal synthetic conditions, separating geometrical axioms from analytical equality-rules and singular numerical formulae.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ME8","text":"A priori certainty is differentiated into intuitive immediacy and discursive conditionality without reducing the latter to uncertainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ALT1","text":"Surface: The text classifies the principles of pure understanding and proves the principles governing phenomenal quantity and perceptual degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ALT2","text":"Structural: Categories, pure intuition, productive synthesis, and sensory receptivity jointly constitute the conditions under which phenomena become determinate objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Transcendental argument validates mathematical application, blocks illicit empirical inferences, and separates conditions of possibility from physical explanations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Phenomenal reality is spatiotemporal, continuously generated through synthesis, divisible without simples, and differentiated through degrees approaching zero.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:MRT1","text":"The extracted premises are sufficient to reconstruct the systematic classification and the arguments for extensive and intensive quantity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:MRT2","text":"The Analogies of Experience and Postulates of Empirical Thought remain underdetermined because their proofs are not developed in the supplied text.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent if transcendental idealism and the constitutive role of synthetic apprehension are granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:MRT4","text":"The strongest reconstructive dependency is the shared synthesis through which pure spatiotemporal construction and empirical apprehension receive the same formal law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:MRT5","text":"The transition from the diminishability of sensation to universal intensive continuity depends on a compressed claim that every sensation admits indefinitely smaller degrees.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0015","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:MRT6","text":"The designation of pure understanding as the sole source of principles remains in tension with the indispensable contribution of pure intuition to mathematical principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:MRT7","text":"The a priori determination of degree remains in tension with the wholly empirical status of sensation and depends on maintaining a strict separation between quantitative form and qualitative content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0019","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:MRT8","text":"The anti-void argument establishes explanatory possibility and inferential restraint, not an actual ontology of matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SISR:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the movement from transcendental ground through quantitative proof to epistemic and empirical limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SISR:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 47","title":"Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding","context":"Chapter 47"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas","order":70,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"532fa68557276ad32f1f437a1027dda0df0dea7652cd2e56c50430959d7a75b6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AS1","text":"Transcendental dialectic must explain the a priori origin of reason’s ideas where no empirical object can correspond to them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AS2","text":"Reason differs from understanding by necessarily ascending from conditioned syntheses toward an unconditioned unity inaccessible to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AS3","text":"The possible relations of representation yield three exhaustive domains for transcendental ideas: subject, phenomenal series, and objects of thought in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AS4","text":"These domains generate rational psychology, rational cosmology, and transcendental theology as products of pure reason rather than of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AS5","text":"The ideas admit a subjective deduction from the necessary functions of reason, while objective deduction is barred by their lack of empirical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AS6","text":"The system delimits pure reason by distinguishing its legitimate demand for totality of conditions from fictitious totalities of consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A1","text":"Transcendental dialectic concerns a priori cognitions and ideas produced by pure reason beyond possible empirical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A2","text":"Reason necessarily seeks the unconditioned for every conditioned synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A3","text":"Understanding proceeds only within conditioned synthesis and cannot reach the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A4","text":"Pure concepts of understanding concern synthetic unity of representations, whereas transcendental ideas concern unconditional synthetic unity of all conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A5","text":"All possible transcendental ideas derive from three relations of representation: relation to the subject, to the phenomenal object, and to objects of thought in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A6","text":"The three relations generate the ideas of the absolutely unified thinking subject, the complete series of phenomenal conditions, and the highest condition of all thinkable objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A7","text":"Rational psychology, cosmology, and theology are pure products or problems of reason and cannot originate from understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A8","text":"Reason relates immediately to concepts held in understanding rather than immediately to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A9","text":"The forms of categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive syllogism respectively generate the ideas of soul, unconditioned cosmological series, and highest being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A10","text":"Transcendental ideas lack objective deduction because no object in experience can be given for them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A11","text":"Transcendental ideas admit subjective deduction from the constitutive nature and logical functions of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A12","text":"Reason requires totality on the side of conditions, not total completeness on the side of conditioned consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A13","text":"A completely given unconditioned condition removes any rational need to posit a completed descending series of consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:A14","text":"The three transcendental ideas form a unified system progressing from self through world to supreme being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:NX1","text":"Transcendental dialectic is not logical dialectic abstracted from all cognitive content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:NX2","text":"Transcendental ideas are not concepts of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:NX3","text":"Transcendental ideas are not cognitions of empirically given objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:NX4","text":"Rational psychology, cosmology, and theology are not extensions of empirical synthesis performed by understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:NX5","text":"The complete totality of future conditioned events is not a necessary transcendental idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:NX6","text":"The descending series from condition to conditioned does not require transcendental use of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:NX7","text":"An idea of exhaustive consequences without a necessity grounded in conditions is an ens rationis or arbitrary fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:NX8","text":"The systematic unity of the ideas is not yet warranted by a demonstrated analogy between logical and transcendental procedure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:D1","text":"Reason’s dialectical production is structurally determinate rather than an indeterminate multiplication of metaphysical objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A2","CH_B337_SIST:A5","CH_B337_SIST:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:D2","text":"The number and order of transcendental ideas can be derived from reason’s logical functions rather than collected from inherited metaphysical doctrines.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A5","CH_B337_SIST:A8","CH_B337_SIST:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:D3","text":"The rational sciences possess subjective necessity without constituting objective knowledge of supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A7","CH_B337_SIST:A10","CH_B337_SIST:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:D4","text":"The unconditioned functions as a principle terminating ascent through conditions rather than as an object completing every possible empirical sequence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A2","CH_B337_SIST:A12","CH_B337_SIST:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:D5","text":"Cosmological totality is rationally required only as totality of grounds or conditions, not as the completed aggregate of conditioned effects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A12","CH_B337_SIST:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:D6","text":"Pure reason is bounded internally by the distinction between its regulative ascent toward principles and its illegitimate reification of merely imagined totalities.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A1","CH_B337_SIST:A10","CH_B337_SIST:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:D7","text":"The sequence self–world–supreme being expresses reason’s progressive unification of cognition into one systematic horizon.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A6","CH_B337_SIST:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:SD1","text":"Metaphysical objects are recoded as necessary outputs of reason’s demand for unconditional grounding rather than as independently encountered entities.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A2","CH_B337_SIST:A7","CH_B337_SIST:A10","CH_B337_SIST:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:SD2","text":"The system converts traditional metaphysics from a doctrine of supersensible objects into a critique of the architecture that necessarily generates supersensible ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A1","CH_B337_SIST:A7","CH_B337_SIST:A10","CH_B337_SIST:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:SD3","text":"Reason’s systematic unity is achieved through a hierarchy of conditions, so the movement toward God is structurally continuous with reflection on self and world rather than externally appended to them.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A6","CH_B337_SIST:A9","CH_B337_SIST:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:SD4","text":"The distinction between conditions and consequences establishes an asymmetry in rational totalization: ascent has transcendental necessity, whereas descent has only logical extensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A3","CH_B337_SIST:A12","CH_B337_SIST:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:SD5","text":"The delimitation of pure reason depends on preserving ideas as subjective necessities while withholding the objective validity that would convert them into knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A10","CH_B337_SIST:A11","CH_B337_SIST:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:DNX1","text":"The system excludes any account in which reason can be satisfied by indefinitely conditioned cognition without demanding an unconditioned ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:DNX2","text":"The system excludes empirical intuition as a source of rational psychology, cosmology, or theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the inference that formal logical validity alone establishes transcendental objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:DNX4","text":"The system excludes a symmetrical treatment of grounds and consequences, because only the regress toward conditions is held to require completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:DNX5","text":"The system excludes an unbounded metaphysical inventory by deriving a determinate triad from the exhaustive forms of representation and syllogistic inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:DNX6","text":"The system excludes objective metaphysical science while retaining the unavoidable subjective generation of metaphysical ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:SNX1","text":"The argument is organized around the unavailable object of the unconditioned: reason must posit it as the terminus of grounding while no experience can provide it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses any final conversion of rational necessity into object-knowledge, since the very ideas required for systematic completion lack empirical presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:SNX3","text":"The system withholds a completed proof that the logical forms of syllogism and the transcendental system of ideas are genuinely analogous, even though that correspondence bears its classificatory force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:SNX4","text":"The system disallows a metaphysics of completed effects, futures, or consequences, preserving totality only where it serves the demand for sufficient conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:FGS1","text":"Conditioned synthesis → reason’s demand for unconditioned conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:FGS2","text":"Demand for unconditioned conditions → classification by the three possible relations of representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:FGS3","text":"Three relations of representation → absolute subject-unity / absolute unity of phenomenal conditions / absolute condition of objects of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:FGS4","text":"Logical forms of categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive inference → subjective deduction of the corresponding transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:FGS5","text":"Absence of empirical objects → prohibition of objective deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:FGS6","text":"Ascent toward total conditions + exclusion of total consequences → bounded transcendental use of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:FGS7","text":"Ordered ideas of self, world, and supreme being → systematic enclosure of pure reason’s proper sphere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AF1","text":"Dialectical illusion beyond empirical objects → distinction between logical and transcendental dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AF2","text":"Transcendental dialectic → reason’s ascent from conditioned syntheses to the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AF3","text":"Demand for unconditioned unity → triadic classification of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AF4","text":"Triadic classification → rational psychology, cosmology, and theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AF5","text":"Logical functions of syllogism → subjective derivation of the three ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AF6","text":"Lack of empirical objects → denial of objective deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AF7","text":"Priority of conditions over consequences → restriction of transcendental totality to ascending regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:AF8","text":"Restricted triad → systematic delimitation of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:ME1","text":"Metaphysical disciplines are reclassified from putative sciences of independently known objects into necessary problem-domains generated by pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:ME2","text":"The distinction between understanding and reason becomes a distinction between cognition of conditioned objects and demand for unconditional unity of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:ME3","text":"Totality is divided into a legitimate totality of grounds and an illegitimate or merely fictive totality of consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:ME4","text":"Soul, world, and God become structurally linked moments in one system rather than separate topics inherited from metaphysical tradition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:ME5","text":"The validity question is bifurcated: rational ideas can be subjectively necessary while objectively undemonstrable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:ME6","text":"The apparent reach of reason beyond experience is redescribed as an internally generated movement of concepts rather than direct access to supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text classifies the transcendental ideas as soul, world, and highest being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:ALT2","text":"Structural — The classification is generated by the three relations of representation and by the corresponding forms of syllogistic inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text replaces dogmatic derivation of metaphysical objects with a subjective deduction that both explains and limits reason’s claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Reality is treated as conditioned synthesis requiring rational regress toward grounds, while a completed totality of future or descending consequences is denied transcendental necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:ALT5","text":"Systematic — The progression from self through world to supreme being organizes all rational cognition into a single hierarchy of unconditionality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the text’s central derivation: reason seeks the unconditioned, its possible objects are triadically determined, and the resulting ideas lack objective deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:MRT2","text":"The extraction preserves the decisive asymmetry between regress toward conditions and progress toward consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:MRT3","text":"The system is coherent if subjective deduction is allowed to establish necessary generation without establishing objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:MRT4","text":"The structure depends on an asserted correspondence between forms of syllogism and forms of transcendental ideas that is announced rather than demonstrated here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:MRT5","text":"The systematic sequence of soul, world, and God is stronger as an architectonic ordering than as evidence for supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:MRT6","text":"The footnoted metaphysical triad of God, freedom, and immortality remains not fully integrated with the chapter’s triad of soul, world, and God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIST:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction retains the text’s movement only if the unconditioned is treated simultaneously as rationally necessary for ascent and unavailable as an empirically given object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIST:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 57","title":"Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas","context":"Chapter 57"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas","order":71,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"3524468c2cabfd2c63c8664bf521670a764183b925299f49ded33888a9f79793","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AS1","text":"The text seeks the a priori source, function, and limits of pure conceptions of reason by extending the analytic derivation from judgement to syllogism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AS2","text":"Reason transforms conditioned cognition into a demand for the complete series of its conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AS3","text":"The totality of conditions entails an unconditioned, which constitutes the content of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AS4","text":"The three logical relations of syllogistic inference generate three corresponding forms of rational unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AS5","text":"Transcendental ideas exceed possible experience because no sensible object can adequately correspond to absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AS6","text":"Their legitimate speculative role is regulative: they direct understanding toward systematic unity without constituting additional objects of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AS7","text":"The requirement for totality is grounded in the asymmetry between the regress from a conditioned cognition to its grounds and the progress from grounds to further consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A1","text":"The logical forms of judgement yield the categories when converted into conceptions of synthetic unity in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A2","text":"The logical forms of syllogism yield pure conceptions of reason when applied to the synthetic unity of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A3","text":"Reason is the faculty of mediate cognition through the subsumption of a condition under a universal rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A4","text":"A conditioned cognition requires a series of conditions under which it is determined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A5","text":"The totality of conditions is possible only through the unconditioned, and totality itself is unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A6","text":"Categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive relations generate corresponding demands for an unconditioned subject, an unconditioned ground, and a complete system of members.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A7","text":"A transcendental idea is a necessary conception of reason for which no adequate object can be presented in sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A8","text":"Pure concepts of understanding are immanent because they are restricted to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A9","text":"The objective employment of pure concepts of reason is transcendent because it seeks absolute totality beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A10","text":"Transcendental ideas regulate the extended, consistent, and systematic exercise of understanding without enabling cognition of additional objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A11","text":"Absolute validity in all relations is not identical with intrinsic validity in a thing considered in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A12","text":"A conditioned cognition requires totality on the side of its grounds, whereas its series of consequences may remain indefinitely incomplete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:A13","text":"Speculative ideas cannot be adequately exhibited in concreto, while practical ideas can be partially realized as rules of action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:NX1","text":"Transcendental ideas are not empirical concepts because no experience presents an unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:NX2","text":"Transcendental ideas are not arbitrary fictions because they arise necessarily from the form of reason itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:NX3","text":"Transcendental ideas are not constitutive concepts that determine further objects within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:NX4","text":"Rational unity is not the unity through which objects of possible experience are first given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:NX5","text":"The absolute is not merely what is intrinsically possible, necessary, or valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:NX6","text":"The necessity of a condition-series is not mirrored by a necessity for a completed series of consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:NX7","text":"The speculative impossibility of adequate exhibition does not render an idea practically idle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:D1","text":"The system of transcendental ideas is formally exhaustive only insofar as categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive inference exhaust the relevant relations of understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A2","CH_B337_SITI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:D2","text":"The necessity of transcendental ideas belongs to the activity of reason rather than to the empirical availability of their objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A2","CH_B337_SITI:A5","CH_B337_SITI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:D3","text":"Reason cannot enlarge experiential cognition by its ideas, but it can impose greater systematic coherence upon the use of understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A8","CH_B337_SITI:A9","CH_B337_SITI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:D4","text":"Confusing absolute necessity with internal necessity converts a relational determination into an empty or unjustified intrinsic predicate.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:D5","text":"A given conditioned cognition rationally demands a completed regress toward grounds even when no highest condition can be empirically apprehended.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A3","CH_B337_SITI:A4","CH_B337_SITI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:D6","text":"The same idea can be theoretically non-exhibitable and practically indispensable because speculative cognition and practical causality have different conditions of realization.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A7","CH_B337_SITI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:SD1","text":"Reason necessarily produces a transcendent surplus over experience, while its legitimate speculative effect remains indirect and regulative.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A5","CH_B337_SITI:A7","CH_B337_SITI:A9","CH_B337_SITI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:SD2","text":"The architecture of rational ideas is derived from logical form rather than discovered through an empirical inventory of objects or causes.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A1","CH_B337_SITI:A2","CH_B337_SITI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:SD3","text":"Rational inquiry is structurally asymmetric: it must seek ground-completion for a conditioned claim while it need not complete the open field of possible consequences.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A3","CH_B337_SITI:A4","CH_B337_SITI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:SD4","text":"Absolute totality functions as a necessary horizon of cognition rather than as an object that cognition can possess.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A5","CH_B337_SITI:A7","CH_B337_SITI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:SD5","text":"The practical domain preserves the productive force of ideas that speculative reason cannot adequately objectify.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A10","CH_B337_SITI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:DNX1","text":"The validity of a rational concept does not depend wholly on the sensible givenness of a corresponding object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:DNX2","text":"Empirical experience cannot supply an absolute totality of conditions because every experience is itself conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:DNX3","text":"The formal derivation of an idea from syllogistic reason does not authorize its constitutive objective use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:DNX4","text":"The regress toward conditions cannot be treated as structurally equivalent to the progress toward consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:DNX5","text":"The systematic unity required by reason cannot be identified with a completed empirical system actually available to cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:SNX1","text":"The unconditioned must be required for reason without becoming a knowable object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:SNX2","text":"Rational completion must remain a rule of orientation rather than an empirical achievement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:SNX3","text":"The system excludes any final conversion of the demand for totality into a constituting representation of totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:FGS1","text":"Logical form of judgement → synthetic unity of intuition → categories of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:FGS2","text":"Logical form of syllogism → universal condition of a rule → mediate cognition through a series of premises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:FGS3","text":"Given conditioned cognition → demand for its complete grounds → totality of conditions → unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:FGS4","text":"Categorical relation → unconditioned subject; hypothetical relation → unconditioned ground; disjunctive relation → complete systematic division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:FGS5","text":"Unconditioned as transcendental idea → absence of adequate sensible object → exclusion of constitutive empirical employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:FGS6","text":"Absence of constitutive employment → regulative direction of understanding toward maximum systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:FGS7","text":"Condition-side regress → required totality; consequence-side progress → indefinite possible continuation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:FGS8","text":"Speculative non-exhibition → practical partial realization under the rule of an absolute ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AF1","text":"Analytic derivation of categories → analogous derivation of ideas from syllogisms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AF2","text":"Syllogistic universality → search for conditions of conditioned cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AF3","text":"Search for conditions → demand for their totality → conception of the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AF4","text":"Table of relations and syllogisms → triadic classification of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AF5","text":"Absolute totality without sensible correspondent → transcendent status of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AF6","text":"Transcendent status → regulative rather than constitutive employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:AF7","text":"Asymmetry of regress and progress → rational requirement for total grounds without a requirement for completed consequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:ME1","text":"Logical form is reclassified from a merely formal feature of inference into a generative source of transcendental concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:ME2","text":"The unconditioned is reclassified from a possible empirical object into the necessary horizon of the demand for systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:ME3","text":"The distinction between understanding and reason becomes a distinction between experiential determination and regulative totalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:ME4","text":"The distinction between intrinsic and unrestricted validity blocks the reduction of absolute necessity to an internal property.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:ME5","text":"The series of grounds and the series of consequences become non-symmetrical epistemic terrains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:ME6","text":"The opposition between speculative emptiness and practical efficacy is displaced by a distinction between inadequate exhibition and rule-governed realization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:ALT1","text":"Surface — Transcendental ideas are defined as necessary conceptions of reason lacking adequate sensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:ALT2","text":"Structural — Syllogistic inference generates a demand for totality that exceeds the experiential jurisdiction of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text disciplines reason by preserving its systematic necessity while denying it constitutive authority over supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Conditioned cognition is oriented toward a total regress of grounds, while consequences remain open to indefinite continuation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:ALT5","text":"Practical — Ideas excluded from adequate speculative exhibition remain operative as partial yet necessary rules of action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the derivation from logical form through syllogistic totalization to the regulative status of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:MRT2","text":"The system depends on the assumed completeness of the correspondence among categories, relations, syllogisms, and forms of the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:MRT3","text":"The central tension is that reason must presuppose totality for conditioned cognition while cognition cannot present that totality as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:MRT4","text":"The distinction between regulative necessity and constitutive knowledge bears the principal burden of preventing transcendence from becoming dogmatic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:MRT5","text":"The claim that the regress of conditions must be total remains compressed where no first condition is available and no completed series can be apprehended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:MRT6","text":"The practical efficacy of ideas is asserted as a distinct branch of their use, but its connection to the speculative deduction is deferred rather than demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SITI:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement if it retains the sequence logical form → unconditioned totality → transcendent idea → regulative unity → asymmetrical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SITI:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 56","title":"Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas","context":"Chapter 56"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason","order":72,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"7243d2d97519ec0936494d61ebf8a12fa4004db8f1f4304a9bf0c054bb7dae58","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:AS1","text":"Reason naturally seeks cognition beyond experience and strives to complete cognition as a self-subsistent systematic whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:AS2","text":"The text tests whether this rational striving is ultimately speculative or practical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:AS3","text":"The three transcendental ideas fail to yield immanent principles for experience and therefore lack speculative utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:AS4","text":"The importance of transcendental ideas is transferred from theoretical cognition to practical interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:AS5","text":"Practical reason is divided between empirically conditioned prudence and pure moral legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:AS6","text":"The practical canon excludes transcendental freedom as speculative and orders the remaining ultimate questions under the moral end of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A1","text":"Reason has a natural interest in extending cognition beyond experience into systematic completeness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A2","text":"The highest ends of reason must possess complete unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A3","text":"Transcendental speculation concerns freedom of the will, immortality of the soul, and the existence of God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A4","text":"These three ideas are transcendent for speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A5","text":"Transcendent ideas cannot function as immanent principles for objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A6","text":"Speculative cognition of freedom, immortality, and God has no decisive use in empirical investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A7","text":"The value of the three transcendental ideas belongs to practical rather than speculative interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A8","text":"The practical concerns whatever is possible through free will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A9","text":"Empirically conditioned practical reasoning yields only regulative and pragmatical laws directed toward happiness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A10","text":"Pure practical laws arise from ends given completely a priori and command absolutely.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A11","text":"Moral laws alone belong to pure practical reason and admit of a canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A12","text":"Practical freedom consists in the capacity of the will to be determined by motives presented by reason independently of sensuous impulses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A13","text":"Experience establishes practical freedom by showing reason to be causally effective in determining the will.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A14","text":"Laws of freedom prescribe what ought to occur, whereas laws of nature describe what does occur.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A15","text":"Transcendental freedom remains a speculative problem and is excluded from the practical canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:A16","text":"The ultimate intention of reason is moral because its highest questions converge on what ought to be done.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:NX1","text":"Speculative interest alone does not justify the labor of transcendental inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:NX2","text":"Freedom of the will does not authorize exceptions to natural explanation of human actions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:NX3","text":"The immortality or incorporeality of the soul does not provide positive cognition of present or future life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:NX4","text":"The existence of God does not authorize deduction of particular arrangements in the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:NX5","text":"Transcendent ideas are not explanatory substitutes for natural causes or experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:NX6","text":"Prudence directed toward happiness is not pure practical legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:NX7","text":"Feeling, pleasure, pain, and empirical psychology are excluded from transcendental philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:NX8","text":"Transcendental freedom is not an explanatory ground for phenomenal events.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:NX9","text":"The practical use of reason does not require resolution of whether rational willing is itself determined by higher natural causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:D1","text":"Empirical inquiry remains governed by natural causality even when freedom, immortality, or God are entertained as transcendental ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A4","CH_B337_SIUE:A5","CH_B337_SIUE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:D2","text":"The ultimate relevance of transcendental ideas is practical orientation rather than theoretical enlargement of knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A6","CH_B337_SIUE:A7","CH_B337_SIUE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:D3","text":"A practical canon contains moral laws but excludes prudential rules for attaining happiness.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A9","CH_B337_SIUE:A10","CH_B337_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:D4","text":"Practical freedom can ground laws of obligation without settling transcendental freedom.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A12","CH_B337_SIUE:A13","CH_B337_SIUE:A14","CH_B337_SIUE:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:D5","text":"The practical canon retains God and future life as ultimate practical questions while setting aside transcendental freedom.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A3","CH_B337_SIUE:A7","CH_B337_SIUE:A15","CH_B337_SIUE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:D6","text":"Moral obligation requires a source independent of sensuous inclination and empirical ends.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A9","CH_B337_SIUE:A10","CH_B337_SIUE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:D7","text":"Transcendental philosophy preserves its purity by treating practical reason without importing empirical psychology.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A10","CH_B337_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:SD1","text":"Metaphysical ideas are reclassified from candidates for theoretical knowledge into conditions of practical significance.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A3","CH_B337_SIUE:A5","CH_B337_SIUE:A7","CH_B337_SIUE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:SD2","text":"Reason operates in two irreducible registers: natural explanation of what occurs and moral legislation of what ought to occur.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A5","CH_B337_SIUE:A13","CH_B337_SIUE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:SD3","text":"Rational systematic unity is completed ethically rather than by a speculative totality of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A1","CH_B337_SIUE:A2","CH_B337_SIUE:A6","CH_B337_SIUE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:SD4","text":"The admissibility of transcendence is limited by its incapacity to alter the causal method of empirical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A4","CH_B337_SIUE:A5","CH_B337_SIUE:A6","CH_B337_SIUE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:SD5","text":"The practical necessity of ultimate questions does not convert them into theoretical knowledge of supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A4","CH_B337_SIUE:A7","CH_B337_SIUE:A11","CH_B337_SIUE:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:DNX1","text":"Positive theoretical cognition of supersensible causes is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:DNX2","text":"Unconditional moral law cannot be grounded in happiness, inclination, or empirical utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:DNX3","text":"Practical obligation does not depend on a completed metaphysical solution to the causality of freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:DNX4","text":"Systematic unity cannot be achieved by mixing transcendental principles with psychological or affective explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:DNX5","text":"No inference from a supreme intelligence licenses knowledge of unperceived particular purposes in nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:SNX1","text":"The unconditioned cannot become an object of completed speculative cognition, although reason cannot cease to seek systematic completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:SNX2","text":"A theoretical reconciliation of natural necessity and transcendental freedom is unavailable to the practical canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:SNX3","text":"A merely speculative system of knowledge cannot constitute reason’s ultimate end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:FGS1","text":"Rational demand for systematic completion generates transcendental questions concerning freedom, immortality, and God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:FGS2","text":"Transcendence prevents these questions from yielding immanent explanatory principles for experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:FGS3","text":"The failure of empirical application deprives speculative answers of their decisive value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:FGS4","text":"The loss of speculative value redirects the questions toward practical interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:FGS5","text":"Practical reason divides into empirical prudence and pure moral legislation according to whether its ends are empirically conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:FGS6","text":"The canon retains only pure moral law and practical freedom while excluding transcendental freedom as unresolved speculation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:FGS7","text":"The final practical horizon is the moral question of what ought to be done under the ideas of God and a future life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:AF1","text":"Rational demand for totality → transcendental ideas → failure of immanent speculative use → practical revaluation → division of prudence and morality → practical freedom and moral law → exclusion of transcendental freedom → moral ultimate end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:AF2","text":"Natural causality for appearances → preservation of empirical explanation → non-empirical normativity of reason → practical canon without metaphysical causal resolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:ME1","text":"Freedom, immortality, and God are reclassified from theoretical objects into practically significant limits of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:ME2","text":"The distinction between what occurs and what ought to occur separates natural explanation from moral legislation without allowing either to absorb the other.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:ME3","text":"Practical reason is divided into prudential coordination of empirical ends and pure legislation of a priori ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:ME4","text":"The question of freedom is split into an empirically attestable practical capacity and an unresolved transcendental causality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines whether freedom, immortality, and God have speculative or practical importance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text limits legitimate cognition by separating transcendent ideas from immanent principles of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text redirects reason from metaphysical acquisition of objects toward construction of a practical canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text separates phenomenal causal order from rational obligation while leaving the ultimate causal status of freedom unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the transition from speculative impotence to practical primacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:MRT2","text":"The system is internally organized by the distinction between transcendent cognition and immanent empirical use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:MRT3","text":"The content and derivation of pure moral law remain underdetermined because the section asserts its canonical status without supplying its full ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:MRT4","text":"The transition from practical relevance to nature’s ultimate moral intention is asserted more strongly than it is demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:MRT5","text":"Practical freedom is empirically affirmed as rational causality while its relation to exhaustive natural determination is suspended rather than resolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:MRT6","text":"The initial triad of transcendental questions contracts into a practical dyad only by excluding transcendental freedom from the canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SIUE:MRT7","text":"The exclusion of empirical psychology secures transcendental purity while leaving practical reason’s indirect relation to feeling structurally bracketed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SIUE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 89","title":"Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason","context":"Chapter 89"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements","order":73,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"4d8ed3a12f44c88e614b3248f115990ef503d88487c24922980b41e0d76855bf","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:AS1","text":"The problem is to establish how synthetical judgements à priori can possess objective validity rather than merely formal coherence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:AS2","text":"The argument distinguishes analytical judgement, which explicates a concept, from synthetical judgement, which connects a concept with what is not contained within it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:AS3","text":"Because synthetic connection exceeds identity and contradiction, it requires a mediating representational ground unavailable to general logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:AS4","text":"Imagination, time, and transcendental apperception supply the conditions through which representations can be synthetically unified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:AS5","text":"Objective reality belongs to a priori cognition only insofar as it determines the necessary conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:AS6","text":"The argument culminates in the principle that objects of possible experience are subject to the necessary synthetic unity of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:A1","text":"An analytical judgement does not extend beyond the content already thought in its concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:A2","text":"A synthetical judgement connects a given concept with something not already thought within it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:A3","text":"The truth of a synthetical judgement cannot be determined from the relation of its concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:A4","text":"Every synthetic connection requires a third term in which the connected representations can be united.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:A5","text":"The internal sense and its a priori form, time, provide the representational field in which synthetic connection can arise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:A6","text":"Imagination performs the synthesis of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:A7","text":"Transcendental apperception provides the necessary unity required for synthesized representations to constitute judgement and experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:A8","text":"Cognition has objective reality only when its representation can be given an object in intuition through real or possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:A9","text":"Experience is possible only as a rule-governed synthetic unity of phenomena within one consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:A10","text":"A priori cognition has objective validity only insofar as it expresses conditions necessary for possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:A11","text":"The conditions of possible experience are simultaneously conditions of possible objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:NX1","text":"The possibility of synthetical judgement is not a problem for general logic, because general logic abstracts from the conditions under which objects can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:NX2","text":"Synthetical judgement is not reducible to identity, contradiction, or conceptual containment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:NX3","text":"A concept without possible intuitive presentation does not cognize an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:NX4","text":"Space and time are not objectively valid merely because they are represented a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:NX5","text":"Experience is not an aggregate or rhapsody of perceptions lacking necessary synthetic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:NX6","text":"A priori synthetic propositions detached from possible experience have no objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:NX7","text":"Pure synthesis is not true through independent correspondence with an object outside the conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:D1","text":"Synthetic judgement requires transcendental rather than merely formal logical investigation because its validity depends on the conditions under which an object can be given.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A2","CH_B337_SI_2:A3","CH_B337_SI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:D2","text":"The possibility of pure synthetic judgement depends on the cooperation of temporal intuition, imaginative synthesis, and apperceptive unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A4","CH_B337_SI_2:A5","CH_B337_SI_2:A6","CH_B337_SI_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:D3","text":"Objective cognition requires more than thought, because thought becomes cognition only through possible experiential presentation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A8","CH_B337_SI_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:D4","text":"The objective validity of space, time, and every pure concept is conditioned by their necessary applicability to possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A8","CH_B337_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:D5","text":"Experience constitutes knowledge only when perceptions are synthesized according to rules under a unified consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A6","CH_B337_SI_2:A7","CH_B337_SI_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:D6","text":"Pure synthetic knowledge is valid not because it originates in experience, but because it legislates conditions without which experience could not occur.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A10","CH_B337_SI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:D7","text":"Every object claim within synthetic a priori cognition is subordinated to the formal conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A8","CH_B337_SI_2:A10","CH_B337_SI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:SD1","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as necessary experiential applicability rather than as a relation established independently between concepts and objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A3","CH_B337_SI_2:A8","CH_B337_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:SD2","text":"The object is not the external terminus of cognition but the correlate of a manifold unified under the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A7","CH_B337_SI_2:A9","CH_B337_SI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:SD3","text":"The authority of pure understanding is limited and enabled by the same condition: it can determine objects only as objects of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A8","CH_B337_SI_2:A10","CH_B337_SI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:SD4","text":"The supreme principle converts the problem of synthetic truth into a problem of the necessary constitution of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A4","CH_B337_SI_2:A9","CH_B337_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:SD5","text":"A priori necessity is grounded neither in empirical accumulation nor in autonomous conceptual self-evidence, but in transcendental conditions of experiential unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A3","CH_B337_SI_2:A7","CH_B337_SI_2:A9","CH_B337_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:DNX1","text":"No cognition can claim objective status solely by possessing internally coherent concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:DNX2","text":"No synthetic connection can be objectively valid without an intuitively grounded field in which its terms are synthesizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:DNX3","text":"No object is cognizable as an object independently of the conditions through which experience unifies appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:DNX4","text":"No pure form, schema, or concept retains determinate meaning when severed from possible experiential use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:DNX5","text":"No empirical multiplicity becomes experience without transcendental unity of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses an intelligible route from pure thought to objects that bypasses intuition, synthesis, and possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:SNX2","text":"The system excludes an account of truth in which synthetic a priori judgement is validated by access to objects as they might be apart from conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:SNX3","text":"The system renders unavailable an object whose objecthood is wholly independent of the unifying activity required for experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:FGS1","text":"Conceptual non-containment → synthetic relation exceeds analytical logic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:FGS2","text":"Synthetic excess → requirement for a mediating representational field.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:FGS3","text":"Temporal intuition + imaginative synthesis + apperceptive unity → possible synthesis of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:FGS4","text":"Rule-governed synthesis under unified consciousness → possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:FGS5","text":"Necessary conditions of possible experience → objective reality of a priori synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:FGS6","text":"Conditions of possible experience = conditions of possible objects → supreme principle of synthetical judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:AF1","text":"The insufficiency of analytical logic for synthetic judgement leads to the search for a mediating condition of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:AF2","text":"The mediating condition is located in temporal intuition, imagination, and transcendental apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:AF3","text":"Their unity makes experience possible as connected cognition rather than dispersed perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:AF4","text":"The possibility of experience supplies the criterion of objective reality for a priori representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:AF5","text":"The criterion is formalized as the subjection of every possible object to the necessary synthetic unity of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:ME1","text":"The distinction between thinking and cognizing becomes decisive: concepts can be thinkable without thereby yielding cognition of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:ME2","text":"Objectivity is relocated from a presumed direct relation between concept and object to necessary validity within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:ME3","text":"Space, time, and pure concepts are reclassified from self-validating representations into conditions requiring experiential authorization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:ME4","text":"Experience is reclassified from passive reception of perceptions into a transcendental achievement of unified synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:ME5","text":"The apparent opposition between a priori necessity and experiential relevance collapses because a priori principles are valid precisely as conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines the supreme principle governing the validity of synthetical judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text grounds object-directed cognition in the coordinated functions of intuition, imagination, conceptual synthesis, and apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text displaces the question of truth from conceptual comparison to the transcendental conditions under which experience can be constituted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects are determinable only within temporal forms of intuition and the unifying conditions of a possible consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the transition from the insufficiency of analytical logic to the transcendental grounding of synthetic judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:MRT2","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning how the specific pure concepts governing experience are derived from the general requirement of synthetic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent if objective reality is defined as necessary applicability to possible experience rather than as access to objects independently given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:MRT4","text":"The argument is strongest where it derives the validity of synthetic a priori cognition from conditions that are simultaneously conditions of possible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:MRT5","text":"The argument compresses the transition from the fact of unified experience to the necessity and universality of its governing principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:MRT6","text":"A residual tension remains between the claim that objects must be given and the claim that the formal conditions of experience determine what can count as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_2:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the dependence of objectivity on possible experience remains the terminal constraint on every synthetic claim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_2:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 46","title":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements","context":"Chapter 46"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3","layer":"CH","title":"Section I. Of the Ideal in General","order":74,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"962b74addf2158f04060a2c5108ac6481d97a55fe31b79fefd07925787dbf1fa","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:AS1","text":"The problem is how reason can employ concepts more remote from empirical objectivity without treating them as objects of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:AS2","text":"The argument is reconstructive and boundary-setting, distinguishing pure concepts, ideas, ideals, and imaginative images by their modes of determination and validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:AS3","text":"Pure concepts require sensuous conditions for objective presentation, whereas ideas exceed every possible phenomenal presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:AS4","text":"An ideal individualizes an idea as a completely determined bearer of perfection rather than as a merely abstract rule or vague image.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:AS5","text":"The ideal acquires practical force as a regulative standard for comparison, criticism, limitation, and self-reform despite lacking objective reality and empirical attainability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:AS6","text":"Empirical embodiment and imaginative indeterminacy are excluded as adequate forms of the ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:AS7","text":"Reason therefore cogitates a transcendent object completely determinable by a priori principles in the absence of empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A1","text":"Objective reality for cognition requires conditions of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A2","text":"Pure concepts contain the mere form of thought when detached from sensuous conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A3","text":"Phenomena supply the material through which concepts can acquire empirical concreteness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A4","text":"No phenomenon can present an idea in concreto to the human mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A5","text":"Ideas contain a perfection unattainable by possible empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A6","text":"Ideas provide reason with a systematic unity toward which experience can approximate without completely attaining it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A7","text":"An ideal is an idea considered in individuo as an individual thing determined or determinable by the idea alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A8","text":"Complete ideal perfection requires the full attainment of the powers and faculties constitutive of the relevant nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A9","text":"Complete determination requires that only one predicate conform to the idea among contradictory predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A10","text":"Moral concepts contain empirical elements such as pleasure and pain, but their formal principle can be treated as a pure conception of reason when reason limits otherwise lawless freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A11","text":"Human ideals possess practical and regulative power rather than the creative power attributed to Platonic ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A12","text":"The ideal functions as an archetype and standard for determining, comparing, judging, and reforming empirical copies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A13","text":"Lack of objective reality does not reduce the ideal to a chimera.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A14","text":"Attempted empirical embodiment of the ideal is impracticable because natural limitations expose the representation as incomplete and fictitious.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:A15","text":"A rational ideal is based on determinate conceptions and a priori rules, whereas an imaginative ideal is an indeterminate composite lacking rule-governed standards.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:NX1","text":"The ideal is not an object that any phenomenon can present in concreto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:NX2","text":"The ideal is not identical with a pure concept or category whose content is merely formal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:NX3","text":"The ideal is not an empirically attainable perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:NX4","text":"The ideal is not objectively real in the sense of an object available to empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:NX5","text":"The ideal is not a chimera merely because objective reality cannot be conceded to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:NX6","text":"The rational ideal is not a creative Platonic principle governing phenomenal production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:NX7","text":"The ideal is not adequately represented by an empirical character, literary portrait, or worldly example.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:NX8","text":"The rational ideal is not a vague image assembled from diverse experiences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:NX9","text":"An imaginative ideal is not a rule for production or a standard for appreciation, explanation, or examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:D1","text":"Pure concepts can become concrete only through phenomenal material, while ideas cannot acquire the same phenomenal concreteness.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A1","CH_B337_SI_3:A2","CH_B337_SI_3:A3","CH_B337_SI_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:D2","text":"Experience is structurally oriented toward systematic unity but remains incapable of completing it.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A5","CH_B337_SI_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:D3","text":"The ideal is more remote from empirical reality than the idea while being more specifically determined as an individual bearer.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A4","CH_B337_SI_3:A5","CH_B337_SI_3:A7","CH_B337_SI_3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:D4","text":"The ideal’s practical validity does not depend on its empirical existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A11","CH_B337_SI_3:A12","CH_B337_SI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:D5","text":"The proper use of the ideal is comparison, criticism, limitation, and reform rather than imitation or empirical duplication.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A12","CH_B337_SI_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:D6","text":"Representing the ideal as an empirical character undermines the very perfection that makes it normative.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A12","CH_B337_SI_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:D7","text":"Determinate rule-governedness distinguishes a rational ideal from an imaginative image.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A12","CH_B337_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:D8","text":"An object can be cogitated as completely determinable by principles while remaining unavailable under empirical conditions, and is therefore transcendent.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A1","CH_B337_SI_3:A4","CH_B337_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:D9","text":"Moral ideals can function within pure reason only through the formal limitation of freedom, not through the elimination of their empirical content.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A10","CH_B337_SI_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SD1","text":"The ideal becomes more normatively precise as it becomes more distant from empirical presentation; transcendence therefore does not entail indeterminacy.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A4","CH_B337_SI_3:A7","CH_B337_SI_3:A9","CH_B337_SI_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SD2","text":"The ideal’s regulative force is generated by the structured gap between complete determination and the incompleteness of empirical copies.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A8","CH_B337_SI_3:A12","CH_B337_SI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SD3","text":"The system separates practical efficacy from objective reality without reducing practical efficacy to illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A11","CH_B337_SI_3:A12","CH_B337_SI_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SD4","text":"The rational ideal must remain non-empirical in order to function as a critical standard against empirical limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A12","CH_B337_SI_3:A13","CH_B337_SI_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SD5","text":"Experience is assigned a double but unequal role: it supplies material for empirical cognition and supplies deficient copies for comparison with the ideal, but it cannot generate the ideal itself.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A3","CH_B337_SI_3:A4","CH_B337_SI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SD6","text":"Reason converts an a priori form of perfection into a practical discipline of limitation, judgment, and self-reform.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A10","CH_B337_SI_3:A11","CH_B337_SI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:DNX1","text":"The system excludes the identification of objective reality with practical validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the assumption that perfection can be certified by an empirical exemplar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the reduction of a standard to a sensuous representation or image.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the derivation of a rational norm from the mere accumulation of experiences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:DNX5","text":"The system excludes a constitutive or creative role for the practical ideal in phenomenal production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:DNX6","text":"The system excludes the possibility that experience can close itself into complete systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:DNX7","text":"The system excludes the coincidence of complete determination with empirical presence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SNX1","text":"The possibility that finite experience could contain its own complete criterion of perfection is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SNX2","text":"The possibility of a perfectly realized human exemplar existing within empirical conditions is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SNX3","text":"The possibility that the normative and empirical orders could coincide without remainder is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SNX4","text":"The ideal’s positive empirical bearer is withheld so that its critical function can remain intact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SNX5","text":"The text leaves unavailable any empirical procedure for producing the ideal, retaining only a priori determinability as its condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:SNX6","text":"The mechanism by which a non-objective ideal acquires practical force is presupposed rather than fully formalized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:FGS1","text":"Sensuous conditions gate objective presentation, leaving pure concepts as mere forms when those conditions are absent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:FGS2","text":"Phenomena supply material that makes concepts empirically concrete but cannot present ideas in concreto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:FGS3","text":"The absence of phenomenal presentation elevates the idea into an unattainable principle of systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:FGS4","text":"Individualizing the idea and determining all its predicates produces the ideal as a complete bearer of perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:FGS5","text":"The ideal is converted into an archetype and standard against which empirical objects and actions are judged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:FGS6","text":"Empirical embodiment is rejected because limitation would expose the copy as inadequate, while imaginative indeterminacy is rejected because it lacks rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:FGS7","text":"Reason thereby cogitates a transcendent object that is completely determinable by a priori principles despite the absence of empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:AF1","text":"Sensuous conditions required for objective reality → pure concepts remain formal without them → phenomena concretize concepts → ideas exceed phenomenal presentation → ideas individualize into ideals → ideals regulate comparison and reform → complete a priori determinability yields transcendence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:AF2","text":"Determinate conception and a priori rule → rational ideal → standard for criticism; vague composite image and absence of rule → imaginative ideal → exclusion from production, appreciation, explanation, and examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:ME1","text":"The ideal is reclassified from a failed empirical object into a regulative standard.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:ME2","text":"Objective reality and practical efficacy are separated as distinct dimensions of validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:ME3","text":"Empirical incompleteness becomes measurable deviation through comparison with the ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:ME4","text":"The distinction between idea and ideal is redrawn around individual determination rather than mere abstraction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:ME5","text":"The distinction between rational and imaginative ideals is redrawn around rule-governed determinacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:ME6","text":"Experience is repositioned as material and approximation rather than as the source or completion of perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:ME7","text":"Empirical representation of perfection is reclassified as a category error rather than a successful realization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:ALT1","text":"Surface: The ideal is an individual object conceived as completely conforming to an idea and used as a standard for action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:ALT2","text":"Structural: The system organizes cognition through a hierarchy of sensuous condition, pure concept, idea, ideal, and empirical copy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Reason transforms unattainable perfection into a procedure for comparison, criticism, limitation, and self-reform.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: The ideal is transcendent because it is completely determinable by principles without being present within empirical conditions, while experience remains indefinitely approximate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the conditions of objective cognition to the regulative function of the ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction preserves the hierarchy from pure concept to idea to ideal and the parallel exclusion of imaginative images.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:MRT3","text":"Internal coherence depends on separating objective reality from practical and critical validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:MRT4","text":"The system retains an unresolved tension between the ideal’s status as an individual object and its lack of objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:MRT5","text":"The ideal’s unattainability is required for critical distance but leaves its practical efficacy only partially explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:MRT6","text":"The relation between a priori principles and the ideal’s individual content is asserted more strongly than it is derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:MRT7","text":"The architecture is strongest in its negative boundaries separating rational ideals from empirical objects and imaginative images.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_3:MRT8","text":"The extracted system preserves the argument’s real movement while leaving its transition from formal determination to practical motivation underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_3:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 74","title":"Section I. Of the Ideal in General","context":"Chapter 74"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)","order":75,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"0699a86f3cb224e5162b75f7bab2b78f661e55a3a1438a347bd10198a35ac7dc","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:AS1","text":"Complete determination requires more than the logical exclusion of contradictory predicates; it requires a material relation to the total field of possible predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:AS2","text":"The material condition of complete determination is represented as a totality of reality from which determinate negations and limitations are derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:AS3","text":"Reason converts the totality of reality into an individually determined ideal, the ens realissimum, as the prototype of all possible determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:AS4","text":"The ideal functions legitimately as a regulative condition for determining possible objects but does not establish the existence of an object corresponding to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:AS5","text":"Transcendental theology arises when the empirical condition of possible experience is illicitly extended to things in general and hypostatized as a primal being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:AS6","text":"The movement proceeds from logical determinability through transcendental totality and idealization to diagnosis of the dialectical objectification of reason’s own regulative idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A1","text":"Logical determinability requires that only one predicate of a contradictory pair belong to a concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A2","text":"Complete determination requires that every possible contradictory predicate be positively or negatively attributable to a thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A3","text":"Complete determination concerns the material content of possibility rather than the formal consistency of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A4","text":"Complete determination presupposes a totality containing the material for every possible predicate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A5","text":"Transcendental negation signifies privation or absence, whereas transcendental affirmation signifies reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A6","text":"Every determinate negation is cognizable only through the corresponding affirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A7","text":"The totality of all reality is the transcendental substratum required for the complete determination of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A8","text":"The totality of reality is transformed by reason into an individual, completely determined ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A9","text":"The ens realissimum is the ideal representation of a being possessing every affirmative reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A10","text":"Finite determinate things are represented as possible through limitation relative to the totality of reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A11","text":"The primal being must be thought as simple because derivative beings cannot constitute the ground that their own possibility presupposes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A12","text":"The ideal of highest reality is a ground of possible determinations, not an aggregate composed of derivative realities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A13","text":"The possibility of sensuous objects is conditioned by the unity of possible experience and the given matter of sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A14","text":"Reason commits transcendental subreption when it treats a principle valid for empirical objects as valid for things in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:A15","text":"Hypostatization converts the distributive unity governing empirical cognition into the collective unity of an allegedly existing individual being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:NX1","text":"Complete determination is not reducible to the law of contradiction or the principle of excluded middle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:NX2","text":"Logical negation is not itself a representation of non-being in an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:NX3","text":"Negation is not original material of possibility; it is limitation of antecedent reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:NX4","text":"The totality of reality is not a highest genus under which particular realities are species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:NX5","text":"The primal being is not an aggregate of derivative beings or partial realities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:NX6","text":"The transcendental ideal is not evidence for the actual existence of a supreme being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:NX7","text":"The title ens originarium, ens summum, or ens entium does not establish an objective relation among existing beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:NX8","text":"The empirical unity of experience is not an objectively existing individual totality containing all reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:NX9","text":"The regulative unity of cognition is not constitutive knowledge of a supreme understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:D1","text":"A thing can be completely determined only by reference to a whole of possibilities that no finite intuition can present in concreto.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A2","CH_B337_SI_4:A3","CH_B337_SI_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:D2","text":"The material asymmetry between affirmation and negation makes reality primary and limitation derivative within the representation of possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A5","CH_B337_SI_4:A6","CH_B337_SI_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:D3","text":"Reason can represent complete determination only by forming an ideal individual from the totality of all realities.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A4","CH_B337_SI_4:A7","CH_B337_SI_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:D4","text":"The determination of finite things proceeds through exclusion from total reality in an analogue of disjunctive syllogistic division.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A1","CH_B337_SI_4:A2","CH_B337_SI_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:D5","text":"The representation of an ens realissimum permits the conceptual derivation of predicates such as unity, simplicity, sufficiency, and eternity without entitling their objective application.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A9","CH_B337_SI_4:A11","CH_B337_SI_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:D6","text":"The transcendental ideal becomes the object of theology only after its regulative function is mistaken for a constitutive representation of existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A8","CH_B337_SI_4:A14","CH_B337_SI_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:D7","text":"The illusion of a supreme being originates in the extension of conditions of empirical objecthood beyond the domain of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A13","CH_B337_SI_4:A14","CH_B337_SI_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:SD1","text":"Ontological hierarchy is generated as a projection of the logical-transcendental requirement for complete determination rather than discovered as a hierarchy of independently known beings.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A2","CH_B337_SI_4:A7","CH_B337_SI_4:A8","CH_B337_SI_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:SD2","text":"The system converts diversity into derivative limitation while denying that the ground of diversity is itself divided, composite, or numerically manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A6","CH_B337_SI_4:A10","CH_B337_SI_4:A11","CH_B337_SI_4:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:SD3","text":"The ideal simultaneously enables systematic cognition and generates unavoidable metaphysical illusion because the same demand for unity that regulates experience invites objectification beyond experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A8","CH_B337_SI_4:A13","CH_B337_SI_4:A14","CH_B337_SI_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:SD4","text":"God in the transcendental sense is produced as the personified terminus of reason’s movement from empirical completeness to unconditioned unity, not as an object supplied by intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A8","CH_B337_SI_4:A9","CH_B337_SI_4:A13","CH_B337_SI_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:DNX1","text":"No finite object can supply its own complete material determination independently of a total horizon of possible predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:DNX2","text":"No negative determination can be original if privation is intelligible only against a prior affirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:DNX3","text":"No transition from an ideal condition of thought to an existing supersensible object is licensed by complete determination alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:DNX4","text":"No empirical totality can become a transcendent object merely by being represented under the demand for systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:DNX5","text":"No knowledge of things in general follows from conditions under which sensuous objects alone can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:SNX1","text":"The unconditioned ground sought by reason remains unavailable as an object of cognition even while its idea is indispensable for reason’s representation of complete determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses a positive cognition of God while preserving the structural necessity of the idea from which theological objectification arises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:SNX3","text":"The argument is organized around the absence of a possible intuition adequate to total reality, an absence concealed when reason personifies its own ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:FGS1","text":"Contradictory-predicate exclusion → formal determinability of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:FGS2","text":"Demand for complete determination → presupposition of material for all possible predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:FGS3","text":"Primacy of affirmation over negation → totality of reality as the ground of all limitations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:FGS4","text":"Totality of reality → individual ideal of an ens realissimum → prototype for finite determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:FGS5","text":"Empirical unity of possible experience → illicit universalization to things in general → transcendental subreption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:FGS6","text":"Distributive empirical unity → collective totality → objectification → hypostatization → personification as supreme being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:FGS7","text":"Diagnosis of subreption → retention of the ideal as regulative condition → denial of constitutive theological knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:AF1","text":"Logical determinability → complete material determination → totality of reality → individual transcendental ideal → ens realissimum → empirical origin of the ideal → transcendental subreption → hypostatized supreme being → critical restriction to regulative use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:AF2","text":"The argument moves from a necessary condition of conceptual completeness to an explanation of why reason falsely treats that condition as an existent unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:ME1","text":"Negation is reclassified from an equal logical opposite of affirmation into a derivative limitation within a materially affirmative field of reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:ME2","text":"The ens realissimum is reclassified from a candidate object of theology into a necessary ideal-form produced by reason’s demand for complete determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:ME3","text":"The apparent opposition between empirical completeness and metaphysical theology is recast as a single process in which legitimate empirical regulation becomes illegitimate transcendental constitution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:ME4","text":"Divine predicates are rendered conceptually derivable from an ideal of total reality while being epistemically unavailable as predicates of a known object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text distinguishes logical determinability from the complete determination required for a fully determined thing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:ALT2","text":"Structural — Complete determination depends on a totality of reality in relation to which finite predicates function as affirmative shares or negative limitations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critical philosophy preserves reason’s ideal as a regulative instrument while blocking its conversion into constitutive metaphysical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The primal being is represented as simple, original, and underived, but these predicates express the internal architecture of an idea rather than knowledge of an existent eternal object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:ALT5","text":"Genealogical — The illusion of God as an objectively existing highest being is traced to the mistaken absolutization and personification of the unity required for possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the progression from logical exclusion to complete determination, total reality, idealization, and dialectical hypostatization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:MRT2","text":"The structure depends decisively on the distinction between formal contradiction and material possibility; without it, the totality of reality loses its required function.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as it treats the ideal as regulative, but it retains a tension between describing total reality as a condition of thought and assigning it the form of an individual being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:MRT4","text":"The derivation of finite diversity is underdetermined because limitation initially explains difference, while the primal ground must later be protected from being conceived as a divisible aggregate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:MRT5","text":"The critical diagnosis is strongest where it identifies the precise conversion of distributive empirical unity into collective ontological unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_4:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if the theological ideal is retained as a necessary product of reason and denied as a valid object of transcendent cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_4:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 75","title":"Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)","context":"Chapter 75"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5","layer":"CH","title":"Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics","order":76,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"6c21b232f73b671b56cdb977d34685ff59760094c8d64bcb6a63000069faca4b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:AS1","text":"The problem is how reason can permit unrestricted speculative conflict without surrendering truth, practical commitment, or intellectual order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:AS2","text":"Only critique can secure rational freedom because it determines the lawful powers and necessary limits of reason itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:AS3","text":"The argument is diagnostic, juridical, reconstructive, and pedagogical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:AS4","text":"Dogmatic conflict is reframed from a contest between supersensible object-claims into an inquiry concerning the jurisdiction of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:AS5","text":"The absence of any experiential criterion renders opposed supersensible assertions mutually non-demonstrable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:AS6","text":"Scepticism destabilizes dogmatism but cannot establish the necessary limits that would settle reason’s jurisdiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:AS7","text":"The argument moves from free polemic through sceptical disruption to systematic self-critique, bounded cognition, critical education, and lawful peace.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A1","text":"The legitimate operation of reason requires unrestricted permission to criticize every claim, including its own.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A2","text":"Critique determines the rights and limits of reason by examining cognitive powers rather than supersensible objects directly.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A3","text":"Universal human reason is reason’s sole legitimate judge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A4","text":"Possible experience supplies the only objective criterion of truth available to human cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A5","text":"The understanding possesses a priori synthetic principles that are valid as conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A6","text":"Pure reason necessarily generates ideas of unconditioned totality extending beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0019","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A7","text":"Speculative reason cannot establish affirmative claims about supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A8","text":"Speculative reason cannot establish negative claims about supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A9","text":"The laws of the understanding become dialectical when applied beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A10","text":"The cosmological antinomy arises from treating phenomena as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A11","text":"Polemical success establishes only the insufficiency of an opponent’s proof, not the truth of one’s own proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A12","text":"Practical rational interest can license assent to supersensible propositions after speculative knowledge has been renounced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A13","text":"Rational correction requires the free disclosure of arguments, doubts, and unresolved difficulties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A14","text":"Speculative argument remains subject to strict honesty even when practically important doctrines are being defended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A15","text":"Scepticism tests the contingent performances of dogmatic reason and produces doubt concerning its claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A16","text":"Contingent failures of dogmatic reasoning cannot establish necessary limits on cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A17","text":"Necessary limits on cognition can be established only through a systematic a priori investigation of reason’s powers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:A18","text":"Lasting intellectual peace results from critical demarcation rather than victory between dogmatic parties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX1","text":"No claim is exempt from criticism because it is sacred, useful, consoling, or socially established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX2","text":"Reason is not a dictatorial authority whose claims can replace universal examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX3","text":"The failure to prove an affirmative supersensible proposition does not prove its negation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX4","text":"Polemical refutation is not speculative demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX5","text":"Practical faith is not cognition of a supersensible object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX6","text":"Critique is not another dogmatic party within the dispute it adjudicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX7","text":"Purely speculative opposition does not by itself overthrow the practical interests attached to God, freedom, or immortality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX8","text":"A good cause cannot justify deceitful, insufficient, or knowingly illusory arguments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX9","text":"External coercion, denunciation, and appeals to public fear are not legitimate instruments of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX10","text":"Intellectual guardianship is not an adequate substitute for critical education.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX11","text":"Scepticism is not a permanent settlement of reason’s disputes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX12","text":"Empirical observation of reason’s failures is not knowledge of its necessary limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX13","text":"The necessity of causality is not reducible to association, habit, or repeated observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:NX14","text":"The empirical contingency of a determinate cause-effect relation does not entail the contingency of the causal law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D1","text":"Opposed supersensible assertions remain theoretically undecidable because neither side possesses an experiential criterion or demonstrative proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A4","CH_B337_SI_5:A7","CH_B337_SI_5:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D2","text":"Polemic can defend a proposition against dogmatic defeat without converting that proposition into knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A7","CH_B337_SI_5:A8","CH_B337_SI_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D3","text":"Affirmative supersensible propositions can retain practical standing under speculative suspension.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A7","CH_B337_SI_5:A8","CH_B337_SI_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D4","text":"Cosmological contradiction dissolves when the demand for absolute completion is withdrawn from phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A4","CH_B337_SI_5:A9","CH_B337_SI_5:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D5","text":"Unrestricted speculative controversy cannot by itself eliminate practical commitment because negative transcendental claims are no more demonstrable than affirmative ones.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A1","CH_B337_SI_5:A7","CH_B337_SI_5:A8","CH_B337_SI_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D6","text":"Dishonest defence weakens a doctrine by obstructing the exposure and correction of its actual grounds.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A13","CH_B337_SI_5:A14","CH_B337_SI_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D7","text":"Exposure to opposed arguments under critical rules produces more durable judgement than insulation within inherited dogma.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A1","CH_B337_SI_5:A9","CH_B337_SI_5:A13","CH_B337_SI_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D8","text":"Scepticism is a necessary resting place between dogmatic confidence and mature critique, but it cannot serve as reason’s final position.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A15","CH_B337_SI_5:A16","CH_B337_SI_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D9","text":"Hume mistakes the inability to infer a determinate effect a priori for the inability to know a priori the lawful form connecting events within possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A5","CH_B337_SI_5:A9","CH_B337_SI_5:A16","CH_B337_SI_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D10","text":"A systematic inventory of a priori synthesis simultaneously validates the understanding’s limited domain and excludes transcendent extension.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A5","CH_B337_SI_5:A6","CH_B337_SI_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0022","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D11","text":"Critique converts speculative conflict from competitive force into adjudication under universally valid laws.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A2","CH_B337_SI_5:A3","CH_B337_SI_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:D12","text":"There is no proper object-level polemic of pure reason once tactical refutation is distinguished from cognitive determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A4","CH_B337_SI_5:A7","CH_B337_SI_5:A8","CH_B337_SI_5:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SD1","text":"Rational freedom is constituted as public self-legislation under universal critical law rather than as exemption from law.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A1","CH_B337_SI_5:A2","CH_B337_SI_5:A3","CH_B337_SI_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SD2","text":"Critique transforms ignorance from an indefinite empirical lack into determinate knowledge of necessary boundaries.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A16","CH_B337_SI_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SD3","text":"Stable metaphysical peace is obtainable only by moving from first-order object-claims to second-order jurisdictional determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A2","CH_B337_SI_5:A4","CH_B337_SI_5:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SD4","text":"Supersensible ideas are protected from dogmatic negation precisely by being withdrawn from dogmatic affirmation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A7","CH_B337_SI_5:A8","CH_B337_SI_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SD5","text":"Dialectical opposition becomes functionally productive when it is treated as evidence requiring critique rather than as a contest requiring victory.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A13","CH_B337_SI_5:A15","CH_B337_SI_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SD6","text":"Critical pedagogy replaces protection from error with the capacity to diagnose error symmetrically.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A1","CH_B337_SI_5:A9","CH_B337_SI_5:A13","CH_B337_SI_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SD7","text":"The system institutes theoretical symmetry between affirmation and denial while retaining a practical asymmetry in favour of affirmative rational maxims.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A7","CH_B337_SI_5:A8","CH_B337_SI_5:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SD8","text":"Dogmatism and unrestricted scepticism share the defect of totalizing conclusions without a complete examination of cognitive powers.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A9","CH_B337_SI_5:A15","CH_B337_SI_5:A16","CH_B337_SI_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0023","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0025","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SD9","text":"Human cognition is reconstituted as a finite but internally determinable sphere rather than an indefinitely extended field bounded only by accumulated failure.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A4","CH_B337_SI_5:A5","CH_B337_SI_5:A6","CH_B337_SI_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:DNX1","text":"Objective cognition cannot possess an independent criterion beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:DNX2","text":"Things in themselves cannot become directly present as objects of speculative verification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:DNX3","text":"Affirmative and negative transcendental claims cannot receive unequal evidential privileges merely from their logical form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:DNX4","text":"Practical usefulness, social consensus, and moral consolation cannot function as speculative evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:DNX5","text":"External authority cannot settle a dispute generated by reason’s own structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0022","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:DNX6","text":"A history of failed demonstrations cannot prove the necessary impossibility of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:DNX7","text":"The empirical origin of determinate cognition cannot entail that every synthetic principle is empirical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:DNX8","text":"Reason cannot require guardians empowered to decide which conclusions it may examine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:DNX9","text":"No object-level metaphysical victory can produce the permanent peace promised by critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SNX1","text":"The decisive absent object is any possible presentation capable of verifying God, the soul as pure intelligence, or unconditioned totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0019","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SNX2","text":"The foreclosed possibility is an apodeictic speculative metaphysics of supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SNX3","text":"Reason has no permanent dwelling outside both dogmatic assertion and critical adjudication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SNX4","text":"No religious, political, pedagogical, or social sovereign can legitimately occupy the place of universal reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SNX5","text":"There is no final object-level truce between transcendent assertions; closure is available only through jurisdictional demarcation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SNX6","text":"The system contains no cognitive bridge from speculative suspension to practical affirmation; rational interest occupies the gap.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:SNX7","text":"The system excludes the possibility that critique is merely another contestant, because its authority depends on standing at the level of conditions and limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:FGS1","text":"Unrestricted claim and counterclaim → exposure of opposed dogmatic pretensions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:FGS2","text":"Domain classification → distinction between possible experience and transcendent projection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:FGS3","text":"Phenomenon treated as thing in itself → apparent antinomy → correction of the category mistake → dissolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:FGS4","text":"Supersensible object-claim → absence of experiential criterion → bilateral non-demonstrability → speculative suspension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:FGS5","text":"Mutual dogmatic attack → disclosure of weakness → sceptical destabilization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:FGS6","text":"Sceptical censure of contingent performances → inability to establish necessary limits → demand for deeper inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0023","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:FGS7","text":"Systematic investigation of a priori synthesis → determination of legitimate powers → determination of necessary boundaries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0021","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:FGS8","text":"Critical boundary sentence → termination of jurisdictionally invalid conflict → lawful intellectual peace.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:FGS9","text":"Speculative limitation → relocation of supersensible assent to practical reason → institutionalization through free inquiry, honesty, and critical education.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:AF1","text":"Rational freedom → dogmatic conflict → free polemic → sceptical destabilization → critical self-investigation → a priori demarcation → lawful peace.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:AF2","text":"Supersensible assertion → absence of possible experience → mutual non-demonstrability → tactical refutation without theoretical victory → practical rather than cognitive standing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:AF3","text":"Intellectual guardianship → fragile inherited conviction → exposure to novel dogmatism → critical education → durable diagnostic autonomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ME1","text":"Metaphysical disagreement is relocated from disputed supersensible objects to the jurisdiction and limits of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ME2","text":"Affirmation and denial are reclassified as epistemically symmetrical when both transcend possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ME3","text":"Ignorance is divided into remediable lack of information and necessary limitation demonstrable by critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ME4","text":"Faith and knowledge cease to be rival grades of the same speculative cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ME5","text":"Scepticism is reclassified from a final philosophy into a transitional instrument of intellectual maturation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ME6","text":"Freedom and discipline cease to be opposites because criticism disciplines reason through self-legislation rather than external coercion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ME7","text":"Intellectual opponents are reclassified from threats into diagnostic instruments for testing critical principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ME8","text":"Education is redrawn from doctrinal insulation to supervised confrontation with opposed arguments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ME9","text":"Causality is divided into an a priori law of possible experience and empirically determined causal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ME10","text":"Permanent philosophical peace becomes a boundary-effect rather than the result of a victorious metaphysical system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ALT1","text":"At the surface level, the argument determines the legitimate status of polemic concerning God, freedom, immortality, and the limits of speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ALT2","text":"At the structural level, criticism functions as a juridical institution that converts intellectual war into adjudication under universal laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ALT3","text":"At the metastrategic level, dogmatic opposition and sceptical disruption are used as transitional mechanisms for producing critical self-knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0017","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0027","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ALT4","text":"At the ontological and epistemic level, the distinction between phenomena and things in themselves fixes possible experience as the domain of objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:ALT5","text":"At the temporal level, reason develops from dogmatic infancy through sceptical interruption to critical maturity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from unrestricted polemic to critical demarcation and rational peace.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:MRT2","text":"The deduction and complete inventory of a priori synthetic principles remain underdetermined because the section imports them from the larger critical system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent where it distinguishes immanent understanding, transcendent reason, tactical refutation, and critical adjudication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:MRT4","text":"The argument is strongest in its symmetrical treatment of affirmative and negative supersensible claims and its distinction between scepticism and knowledge of limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0018","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0023","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:MRT5","text":"The initial authorization of polemic and the final denial of any proper polemic remain consistent only if tactical defence and cognitive settlement are kept at different levels.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:MRT6","text":"Theoretical symmetry and practical preference remain joined by rational interest rather than by a demonstrated cognitive transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:MRT7","text":"Critique’s claim to establish reason’s limits a priori depends on reason successfully validating a tribunal constituted from its own principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:MRT8","text":"Unrestricted freedom and strict discipline are reconciled only by distinguishing external coercion from reason’s internal self-legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:MRT9","text":"Providential and teleological claims concerning the usefulness of conflict supply rhetorical reinforcement but are not required by the critical mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_5:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the argument’s actual progression while leaving its imported transcendental proofs unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0020","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0024","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_5:P0025","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 85","title":"Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics","context":"Chapter 85"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6","layer":"CH","title":"Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis","order":77,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"4b723d3e50adfd93cf1f5c1541f66339cd5903c5414c59a87f9107af448b3910","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AS1","text":"Pure speculative reason cannot enlarge cognition beyond the conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AS2","text":"Hypothesis is legitimate only where the possibility of its object is secured and its explanatory use remains connected to what is empirically given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AS3","text":"Transcendental hypotheses are excluded from explanatory cognition because they invoke grounds neither given nor determinable in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AS4","text":"Hypotheses must be sufficient to derive the experienced consequences they explain without requiring ad hoc auxiliary suppositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AS5","text":"Speculative hypotheses retain a restricted legitimacy as relative defensive instruments against equally transcendent dogmatic objections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AS6","text":"Reason secures itself not by suppressing objections but by eliciting, strengthening, and resolving its own dialectical opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AS7","text":"The argument moves from the restriction of explanatory hypothesis, through its polemical preservation, to the prohibition against converting defensive possibility into dogmatic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A1","text":"Pure speculation cannot produce knowledge of objects beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A2","text":"A legitimate hypothesis presupposes the established possibility of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A3","text":"A legitimate explanatory hypothesis must connect its posited ground with what is given and certain in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A4","text":"The conditions of possible experience are the only conditions under which reason can determine the possibility of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A5","text":"Ideas of reason are regulative principles for the systematic use of understanding, not representations of possible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A6","text":"Explanations of natural phenomena may employ only grounds connected to those phenomena by known empirical laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A7","text":"A hypothesis is sufficient only if it determines in advance the experienced consequences attributed to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A8","text":"Auxiliary hypotheses weaken a hypothesis because each requires independent justification that the original hypothesis cannot supply.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A9","text":"In pure speculative cognition, reason may assert only necessity or the impossibility of knowledge; it cannot assert mere opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A10","text":"Hypotheses may defend a speculative position against transcendent objections but may not support a dogmatic speculative assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A11","text":"Speculative reason is intrinsically dialectical and must expose its own objections in order to neutralize them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:A12","text":"A merely defensive hypothesis must be abandoned when the opposing dogmatic claim is withdrawn.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:NX1","text":"Hypothesis is not unrestricted imaginative invention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:NX2","text":"Logical non-contradiction is not sufficient to establish an object’s possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:NX3","text":"Ideas of reason are not images of intelligible, supersensible, or merely possible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:NX4","text":"A transcendental ground is not an admissible explanation of a natural phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:NX5","text":"A divine, intelligible, or hyperphysical cause is not entitled to replace empirical causal investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:NX6","text":"The absence of empirical conditions is not proof of the absolute impossibility of what lies beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:NX7","text":"Speculative probability is not a valid status for propositions concerning supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:NX8","text":"Polemical counter-hypothesis is not a positive doctrine or personal opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:NX9","text":"External tranquility is not genuine philosophical settlement while dialectical contradiction remains internal to reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:D1","text":"Reason cannot posit new powers, substances, modes of presence, kinds of community, or forms of duration for explanatory use unless they are given or could be given in experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A2","CH_B337_SI_6:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:D2","text":"A hypothesis concerning the soul may regulate inquiry into mental phenomena without establishing that the soul is a simple substance.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A5","CH_B337_SI_6:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:D3","text":"Hyperphysical explanation produces explanatory arrest because it substitutes an incomprehensible ultimate ground for discoverable empirical causes.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A3","CH_B337_SI_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:D4","text":"The multiplication of auxiliary assumptions marks a hypothesis as insufficiently grounded even when those assumptions are not demonstrably false.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A7","CH_B337_SI_6:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:D5","text":"Neither affirmation nor denial of supersensible objects gains speculative superiority when both exceed possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A1","CH_B337_SI_6:A9","CH_B337_SI_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:D6","text":"A counter-hypothesis may show that an opponent has not exhausted the sphere of possibility without claiming knowledge of an alternative reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A4","CH_B337_SI_6:A10","CH_B337_SI_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:D7","text":"Philosophical security requires the deliberate production of the strongest possible objections against one’s own position.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:SD1","text":"The text divides rational discourse into empirical explanation, regulative orientation, practical supposition, and polemical defence, assigning each a different warrant.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A1","CH_B337_SI_6:A5","CH_B337_SI_6:A6","CH_B337_SI_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:SD2","text":"The restriction on transcendental explanation preserves empirical inquiry by making explanatory incompleteness preferable to fictitious completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A1","CH_B337_SI_6:A4","CH_B337_SI_6:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:SD3","text":"Speculative equality between competing transcendent positions converts controversy from a contest of proofs into a discipline of reciprocal limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A9","CH_B337_SI_6:A10","CH_B337_SI_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:SD4","text":"The system authorizes fictive supersensible constructions only when they expose the opponent’s illicit conversion of empirical limits into absolute impossibility.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A4","CH_B337_SI_6:A10","CH_B337_SI_6:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:SD5","text":"The system’s polemical permission strains against its explanatory prohibition because it permits conceptions declared objectively indeterminable to perform strategically necessary work.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A1","CH_B337_SI_6:A5","CH_B337_SI_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:DNX1","text":"No supersensible object is available as a positive theoretical ground of explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:DNX2","text":"No completed causal totality is required for the legitimate investigation of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:DNX3","text":"No speculative position beyond experience can secure a final evidential advantage over its contrary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:DNX4","text":"No empirical limitation may be converted into a proof that all non-empirical possibility is impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:DNX5","text":"No permanent rational peace can rest on the unexamined suppression of internal contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:SNX1","text":"The forbidden speculative posture is possession of transcendent reality as an object of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:SNX2","text":"The argument is organized around the unavailability of a theoretical standpoint from which reason could both exceed experience and certify its excess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:SNX3","text":"The final exclusion is the conversion of provisional defensive possibility into absolute metaphysical validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:FGS1","text":"Critique of speculative extension → denial of transcendent cognition → apparent enlargement of hypothetical freedom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:FGS2","text":"Possible object → experiential connection → lawful explanatory relation → admissible physical hypothesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:FGS3","text":"Mere idea of reason → departure from experience → objectless fiction → inadmissible transcendental explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:FGS4","text":"Hypothesis → derivation of experienced consequences → adequacy; auxiliary supplementation → insufficiency and weakened warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:FGS5","text":"Transcendent assertion versus transcendent denial → equal speculative incapacity → defensive counter-hypothesis without positive commitment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:FGS6","text":"Internal dialectical objection → maximal development of opposition → exposure of its limits → conditional philosophical security.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AF1","text":"The failure of pure speculation to extend knowledge leads to the problem of whether hypothesis can occupy the abandoned space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AF2","text":"The possibility and experiential applicability of the hypothesized object become the criterion separating lawful hypothesis from chimerical invention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AF3","text":"The exclusion of transcendental explanation is reinforced by the requirements of sufficiency, empirical consequence, and non-auxiliary warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AF4","text":"The failure of speculative proof on both sides permits hypotheses only as relative weapons against dogmatic opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:AF5","text":"The internal dialectic of reason requires self-opposition to be cultivated, while every defensive fiction remains barred from doctrinal status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:ME1","text":"Hypothesis is reclassified from free conjecture into a regulated operation whose legitimacy depends on experiential possibility, connection, and sufficiency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:ME2","text":"The distinction between physical and transcendental explanation becomes a distinction between inquiry that remains open and explanation that prematurely closes inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:ME3","text":"The opposition between affirmation and denial of supersensible objects collapses into a shared condition of speculative non-knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:ME4","text":"Fictions concerning supersensible reality are reclassified from possible knowledge claims into temporary polemical instruments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:ME5","text":"Objection is reclassified from an external threat into material through which reason discovers and disciplines its own dialectical structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines when hypotheses concerning nature, soul, divine causation, and supersensible life may be used.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:ALT2","text":"Structural — Possible experience supplies the boundary that governs objecthood, causal explanation, evidential warrant, and theoretical admissibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text separates explanation, regulative use, dogmatic assertion, practical supposition, and polemical defence into distinct rational jurisdictions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Phenomenal objects are treated as knowable only under spatial, temporal, and causal conditions, while intelligible existence remains neither cognizable nor dogmatically deniable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:ALT5","text":"Dialectical — Reason attains stability only by staging its own strongest counterclaims and withholding final speculative possession from either side.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the argument if the central distinction between empirical hypothesis and transcendental hypothesis is retained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:MRT2","text":"The system depends most heavily on the claim that possible experience both limits cognition and determines the admissible possibility of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:MRT3","text":"The system remains coherent by differentiating explanatory, regulative, practical, and polemical uses of reason rather than granting them a single standard of warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:MRT4","text":"The principal underdetermination concerns the status of defensive fictions, which are denied objective warrant yet permitted strategic rational use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:MRT5","text":"The principal structural tension is that reason may invoke possibilities beyond experience to resist dogmatic denial while being forbidden to treat those possibilities as objects or explanations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_6:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the terminal restriction is retained: relative polemical admissibility never becomes positive speculative validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_6:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 86","title":"Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis","context":"Chapter 86"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7","layer":"CH","title":"Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs","order":78,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"ddbd472e094b07f74262e4d6e6380d820edab45ac463a9c25082a3deb5668852","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AS1","text":"Transcendental synthetic proof requires a prior justification of the concepts and synthetic extension through which it claims objective knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AS2","text":"The argument relocates proof from inferential connection between concepts to the conditions under which experience or an object of experience is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AS3","text":"Pure reason cannot validate its ideas as objective principles, because their legitimate use is only regulative within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AS4","text":"A transcendental proposition has one possible ground of proof because its object is determined only through the concept from which the proposition proceeds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AS5","text":"Indirect proof is disallowed in transcendental cognition because dialectical illusion permits subjective conditions of thought to masquerade as objective conditions of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AS6","text":"Critical discipline replaces competitive dogmatic proof with transcendental deduction and confines speculative reason to limits that redirect it toward practical principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A1","text":"A transcendental synthetic proposition cannot be proved unless the objective validity of its concepts is established a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A2","text":"A synthetic extension of cognition requires a guide not contained in the concept from which the extension begins.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A3","text":"Pure intuition guides mathematical synthesis, while possible experience guides transcendental cognition concerning concepts of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A4","text":"A concept of the understanding has objective validity only insofar as it determines an object of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A5","text":"A proposition employing ideas of pure reason must establish the possibility of its synthetic extension before it can establish its truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A6","text":"The subjective simplicity of a representation does not establish the objective simplicity of its supposed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A7","text":"Principles of the understanding cannot establish ideas of pure reason because their validity is restricted to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A8","text":"Principles of pure reason, when used objectively, are dialectical and lack objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A9","text":"A transcendental proposition derives from a concept the synthetic condition under which an object corresponding to that concept is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A10","text":"Because the determining concept supplies the sole condition of the object’s possibility, a transcendental proposition permits only one valid ground of proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A11","text":"An ostensive proof establishes both the truth of a proposition and the ground of its possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A12","text":"An apagogic proof is valid only where subjective representation cannot be mistaken for objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A13","text":"Transcendental reasoning operates in a subjective medium in which dialectical illusion systematically produces such mistaking.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:A14","text":"A critical deduction of the principles used in a proof is required before claims made through those principles can be admitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:NX1","text":"Transcendental proof is not a direct transition from one concept to another concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:NX2","text":"Subjective association is not objective affinity or necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:NX3","text":"The unity or simplicity of consciousness is not proof that the soul is an objectively simple substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:NX4","text":"A pure idea is not an objectively constitutive principle of speculative cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:NX5","text":"A principle valid for possible experience is not a basis for cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:NX6","text":"Multiple putative proofs do not increase the validity of a transcendental thesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:NX7","text":"Indirect disproof of a counter-proposition is not transcendental proof of the original proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:NX8","text":"Contradiction with subjective conditions of thought is not contradiction in the object itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:NX9","text":"Opposed predicates cannot determine an object founded on an impossible or self-contradictory conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:NX10","text":"Dogmatic victory over an opponent is not a deduction of the right to assert a speculative proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:D1","text":"Causality is established transcendentally only as a condition without which temporal events could not constitute experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A3","CH_B337_SI_7:A4","CH_B337_SI_7:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:D2","text":"The principle of sufficient reason cannot receive dogmatic proof if its alleged necessity is not derived from conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A1","CH_B337_SI_7:A4","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:D3","text":"Claims about the soul’s substantial simplicity fail when they infer an object-property from the formal simplicity of self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A5","CH_B337_SI_7:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:D4","text":"A proof based on principles of understanding cannot establish a supersensible object such as an unconditioned being.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A7","CH_B337_SI_7:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:D5","text":"A dogmatic proof lacking a deduction of its principles may be suspended without identifying its particular sophism.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A8","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:D6","text":"The existence of several arguments for a transcendental thesis indicates that none has located the singular determining ground required for proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A9","CH_B337_SI_7:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:D7","text":"Indirect proof cannot establish transcendental synthetic propositions because the falsity of a counterstatement may arise from subjective illusion or from a false shared presupposition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A12","CH_B337_SI_7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:D8","text":"Opposed cosmological claims can both be false when they predicate finitude or infinitude of a world falsely assumed to be given as a totality in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A8","CH_B337_SI_7:A12","CH_B337_SI_7:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:D9","text":"Speculative disputes become decidable only when each party supplies the deduction of the grounds through which it claims objective authority.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A11","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:SD1","text":"The criterion of proof is converted from persuasive inferential force into the legitimating source and domain of the principles used.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A1","CH_B337_SI_7:A4","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:SD2","text":"Transcendental proof is structurally self-limiting, because the same inquiry that validates a concept determines the boundary beyond which that concept cannot generate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A1","CH_B337_SI_7:A4","CH_B337_SI_7:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:SD3","text":"The unity of valid proof and the exclusion of indirect proof jointly make transcendental demonstration a deduction of possibility rather than an accumulation of arguments.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A9","CH_B337_SI_7:A10","CH_B337_SI_7:A11","CH_B337_SI_7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:SD4","text":"Critical reason neutralizes dialectical proliferation by judging the source of claims rather than pursuing every conclusion generated from them.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A8","CH_B337_SI_7:A13","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:SD5","text":"The restriction of speculative reason is not mere sceptical abstention, because it preserves a distinct practical sphere for reason after invalid speculative extensions are withdrawn.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A8","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:DNX1","text":"Objective necessity is not recoverable from the psychological fact that one representation habitually succeeds another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:DNX2","text":"Conceivability is not evidence of the objective possibility of what is conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:DNX3","text":"Formal features of cognition are not transferable without deduction into ontological predicates of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:DNX4","text":"Logical consistency of a proposition and falsity of its negation are not sufficient conditions of transcendental truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:DNX5","text":"Reason has no speculative access to objects independently of the conditions under which an object can be given in possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:DNX6","text":"A shared conceptual premise is not vindicated because rival parties derive opposed consequences from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:SNX1","text":"No unrestricted standpoint exists from which pure reason can legislate the constitution of supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:SNX2","text":"No speculative proof can convert the unconditioned, merely demanded by reason, into an object positively known by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:SNX3","text":"No final dogmatic settlement can arise from adversarial refutation when both parties may depend on the same unexamined subjective condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:SNX4","text":"The object sought by metaphysical proof is unavailable whenever its possibility cannot be connected to conditions of givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:FGS1","text":"Concept claiming synthetic extension → demand for a non-conceptual guide → inquiry into the source of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:FGS2","text":"Concept of understanding → condition of possible experience → objective determination of an experiential object → valid transcendental proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:FGS3","text":"Idea of pure reason → required proof of possible synthetic extension → absence of objective deduction → suspension of speculative claim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:FGS4","text":"Single concept determining a possible object → single condition of possibility → single legitimate transcendental ground of proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:FGS5","text":"Subjective conditions treated as objective → dialectical illusion → invalidity of apagogic inference → requirement of ostensive deduction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:FGS6","text":"Deduction of grounds of proof → exposure of dogmatic overreach → renunciation of speculative pretension → confinement of reason to practical principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AF1","text":"Unjustified transcendental inference → requirement of objective validity for the concepts employed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AF2","text":"Objective-validity requirement → possible experience as the criterion for concepts of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AF3","text":"Attempted extension through pure ideas → prior demand for proof that such extension is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AF4","text":"Restriction of objective validity → rejection of dogmatic proofs concerning supersensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AF5","text":"Conceptual determination of a possible object → singularity of the valid transcendental proof-ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AF6","text":"Subjective medium of transcendental illusion → prohibition of indirect proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:AF7","text":"Transcendental deduction of principles → critical limitation of speculative reason → practical destination of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:ME1","text":"Proof is reclassified from a relation among propositions into a justification of the objective right to use the concepts composing those propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:ME2","text":"The distinction between mathematics and transcendental philosophy is redrawn by their distinct guides of synthesis: intuition in the former and possible experience in the latter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:ME3","text":"Metaphysical disputes are reclassified as disputes over the validity of their conditions of assertion rather than contests between independently established theses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:ME4","text":"The apparent opposition between a proposition and its negation no longer guarantees that one is true, because both may depend on an impossible object-conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:ME5","text":"Critical procedure gains economy by invalidating a source of dialectical claims rather than refuting each of its indefinitely proliferating conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text regulates acceptable proofs for transcendental synthetic propositions and rejects defective arguments about causality, the soul, the world, and God.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:ALT2","text":"Structural — Proof is governed by the relation among concepts, conditions of possible experience, and the possibility of objects corresponding to those concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Critical reason substitutes deduction of entitlement for dogmatic assertion, rhetorical accumulation, and adversarial refutation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects can be determinately cognized only under conditions of possible experience, including temporal determination and causal connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:ALT5","text":"Normative — The limitation of speculative cognition preserves reason’s proper orientation toward practical principles rather than eliminating reason’s authority altogether.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:MRT1","text":"The axioms reconstruct the argument if transcendental proof is understood as proof of objective possibility rather than merely logical derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:MRT2","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as possible experience supplies both the criterion validating concepts of understanding and the boundary restricting their use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:MRT3","text":"The system depends on an underdeveloped transition from the invalidity of objective speculative use to the retained legitimacy of practical principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:MRT4","text":"The singular-proof thesis is strongest where one concept demonstrably supplies one necessary condition for the possibility of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:MRT5","text":"The exclusion of apagogic proof depends on the claim that transcendental cognition is uniquely exposed to confusion between subjective and objective conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:MRT6","text":"The critique of dogmatic plurality compresses a tension between the formal uniqueness of a proof-ground and the possible plurality of expository routes to that ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SI_7:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if limitation is treated as productive discipline rather than as a mere denial of metaphysical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SI_7:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 87","title":"Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs","context":"Chapter 87"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA","layer":"CH","title":"Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason","order":79,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"2d30109046700c4e3bc70fa5c13d356ae17e856dda8b54b535fea8c183f59137","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:AS1","text":"Theology is divided by its alleged source of cognition, but the decisive division is between speculative claims about what is and practical postulation concerning what ought to be.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:AS2","text":"Speculative theology attempts to infer a Supreme Being either from experience, the world, causality, or pure conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:AS3","text":"The principles required by these inferences possess objective validity only within possible experience and cannot warrant an object beyond it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:AS4","text":"Every speculative proof of a Supreme Being therefore fails because it attempts to convert concepts, empirical conditions, or causal relations into cognition of a supersensible existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:AS5","text":"Moral necessity may practically postulate what speculative reason cannot theoretically establish.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:AS6","text":"Transcendental theology survives not as positive cognition of God but as negative criticism and conceptual purification of the idea of a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A1","text":"The existence of an object cannot be extracted from the analysis of its concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A2","text":"Synthetic a priori principles have objective validity only as conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A3","text":"Speculative cognition concerns objects that cannot be given or discovered in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A4","text":"The causal principle applies to empirically contingent events and states, not to the existence of substances or the world as a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A5","text":"Outside experience, the concepts of cause and contingency lose their determinate objective meaning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A6","text":"An alleged cause wholly distinct from the world cannot become an object of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A7","text":"Theoretical cognition of absolute necessity would have to be a priori, but a priori concepts cannot thereby establish the existence of their objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A8","text":"Moral laws are absolutely necessary practical laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A9","text":"A condition necessarily presupposed by an absolutely necessary practical law may be postulated from a practical point of view.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A10","text":"Pure speculative reason can neither prove nor disprove the existence of a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A11","text":"The idea of a Supreme Being can be determined through pure transcendental predicates while its objective reality remains unestablished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:A12","text":"A valid conception of a Supreme Being must exclude empirical limitations and contradictory predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:NX1","text":"Theology based on revelation is not adjudicated by the critique of speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:NX2","text":"The existence of the universe is not given as empirically contingent merely because events within it are contingent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:NX3","text":"The causal principle does not license an inference from the world to a cause outside the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:NX4","text":"No analysis of the concept of a necessary or perfect being establishes that such a being exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:NX5","text":"Physico-theology is not an independent demonstration of a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:NX6","text":"Practical postulation is not theoretical knowledge of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:NX7","text":"The speculative impossibility of proving God is not a speculative proof that God does not exist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:NX8","text":"The Supreme Being is not legitimately conceived through empirical anthropomorphic predicates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:NX9","text":"A deistic primal cause does not by itself yield cognition of a living, intelligent, free author of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:D1","text":"Cosmological inference cannot establish a Supreme Being, because it treats the existence of the world as contingent and extends causality beyond its experiential validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A2","CH_B337_SVCA:A4","CH_B337_SVCA:A5","CH_B337_SVCA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:D2","text":"Ontological inference cannot establish a Supreme Being, because a concept of necessity or perfection does not posit its object as existing.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A1","CH_B337_SVCA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:D3","text":"Physico-theological inference can orient reflection toward theology but cannot supply the required proportion between the experienced world and an absolutely supreme cause.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A2","CH_B337_SVCA:A5","CH_B337_SVCA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:D4","text":"All speculative proofs ultimately require a transition from an idea of absolute necessity to the existence of its object, and this transition is unavailable.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A1","CH_B337_SVCA:A2","CH_B337_SVCA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:D5","text":"Moral theology has a distinct logical status because it proceeds from practical necessity rather than from speculative explanation of what exists.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A8","CH_B337_SVCA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:D6","text":"The speculative idea of a Supreme Being functions as a faultless but merely ideal completion of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A10","CH_B337_SVCA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:D7","text":"Speculative critique has equal force against dogmatic atheism, deism, and anthropomorphism insofar as each makes transcendent assertions without corresponding experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A2","CH_B337_SVCA:A3","CH_B337_SVCA:A10","CH_B337_SVCA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:SD1","text":"Theology is redistributed into two nonconvertible functions: practical postulation may concern existence, while speculative reason may only regulate and purify the concept.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A8","CH_B337_SVCA:A9","CH_B337_SVCA:A10","CH_B337_SVCA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:SD2","text":"The unconditioned cannot be reached by extending the explanatory demands generated within conditioned experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A2","CH_B337_SVCA:A4","CH_B337_SVCA:A5","CH_B337_SVCA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:SD3","text":"Theoretical reason is converted from a faculty that establishes transcendent beings into a tribunal that limits admissible predicates of such beings.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A10","CH_B337_SVCA:A11","CH_B337_SVCA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:SD4","text":"The perfection of the idea of God is formal and regulative rather than existentially demonstrative.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A1","CH_B337_SVCA:A7","CH_B337_SVCA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:DNX1","text":"Possible experience cannot be exceeded by treating its formal conditions as knowledge of what grounds experience absolutely.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A2","CH_B337_SVCA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:DNX2","text":"An explanatory regress within empirical nature cannot be converted into cognition of an unconditioned external first cause.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A4","CH_B337_SVCA:A5","CH_B337_SVCA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:DNX3","text":"Theoretical necessity and practical necessity cannot be treated as the same mode of warrant.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A7","CH_B337_SVCA:A8","CH_B337_SVCA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:DNX4","text":"The human model of intelligence and freedom cannot be projected directly onto a supersensible being.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A3","CH_B337_SVCA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:SNX1","text":"No theoretical standpoint outside the conditions of human experience is available from which the absolute ground of all existence can be known.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A2","CH_B337_SVCA:A3","CH_B337_SVCA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:SNX2","text":"The desired transition from the formal idea of a highest being to a living author of the world is structurally unavailable to speculative reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A1","CH_B337_SVCA:A6","CH_B337_SVCA:A11","CH_B337_SVCA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:SNX3","text":"The ultimate object sought by speculative theology remains neither affirmable nor deniable as an object of theoretical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A3","CH_B337_SVCA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:FGS1","text":"Theology is sorted by source of cognition, and speculative theology is separated from practical moral postulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:FGS2","text":"Speculative theology invokes concept-analysis, causal inference, or worldly order to posit a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:FGS3","text":"Immanence of synthetic principles within possible experience blocks every attempted transition to a supersensible cause or necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:FGS4","text":"The failure of positive proof symmetrically blocks speculative disproof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:FGS5","text":"Failed constitution of an object yields a negative critical function: the idea is retained, purified of anthropomorphism and contradiction, and reserved for possible practical employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:AF1","text":"Taxonomy of theology → distinction between theoretical and practical cognition → isolation of speculative theology as the object of critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:AF2","text":"Causal inference from empirical contingency → illicit extension to world-totality or extramundane cause → collapse of cosmological and physico-theological proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:AF3","text":"Pure concept of necessity or perfection → inability of analysis to posit existence → collapse of ontological proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:AF4","text":"Failure of speculative affirmation and denial → Supreme Being as a merely ideal object → negative transcendental regulation of theological concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:AF5","text":"Moral necessity → practical postulation of a necessary condition → possible moral theology without speculative demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:ME1","text":"The classical proofs are reclassified from rival demonstrations into variants of one invalid transcendent transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:ME2","text":"Causality is reclassified from a universal metaphysical bridge to a rule governing empirical events and states.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:ME3","text":"The question of divine existence is split into theoretical demonstrability, practical postulation, and conceptual determinacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:ME4","text":"Theological critique is reclassified as negative conceptual discipline rather than positive supersensible knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:ME5","text":"Atheistic denial, deistic abstraction, and anthropomorphic depiction become equally subject to critique when they claim speculative cognition beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text examines whether reason can establish the existence and attributes of a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text restricts valid use of causality, contingency, and synthetic principles to possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text displaces theology from speculative proof toward critical limitation and possible practical grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text denies theoretical access to an unconditioned being existing independently of worldly, spatial, and temporal conditions while retaining the formal idea of such independence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central conclusion that speculative theology cannot establish or refute a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:MRT2","text":"The reconstruction requires the distinction between conceptual content and existential positing, because its removal restores the ontological proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction requires the experiential restriction on causality, because its removal restores cosmological and physico-theological inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:MRT4","text":"Moral theology remains underdetermined because its promised derivation from moral law is deferred rather than supplied within this section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:MRT5","text":"The system is strongest where it blocks illicit transitions from experience or concepts to supersensible existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:MRT6","text":"The system retains a tension between declaring the Supreme Being theoretically unknowable and assigning a determinate transcendental ideal to that being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCA:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if negative critique is treated as a productive operation that preserves an idea while withdrawing theoretical objecthood from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCA:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 80","title":"Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason","context":"Chapter 80"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS","layer":"CH","title":"Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem","order":80,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"fb84dfda2ea72d4ebe40adccd7a77eb3c6d01135f96185121fd45c4281a1d466","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AS1","text":"The cosmological antinomy arises from inferring that the givenness of a conditioned phenomenon entails the simultaneous givenness of the complete series of its conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AS2","text":"The inference equivocates between conditioned things in themselves and conditioned appearances given only in empirical apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AS3","text":"Reason legitimately requires regress toward conditions, but cannot legitimately convert this requirement into a completed totality of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AS4","text":"The opposing cosmological assertions become undecidable because both presuppose that the world is a self-subsistent totality available for determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AS5","text":"Once the world is understood as existing only in the successive empirical regress of appearances, it is neither an actually finite nor an actually infinite whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AS6","text":"The antinomy is thereby transformed from a conflict over objects into an exposure of transcendental illusion and an indirect proof of transcendental ideality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A1","text":"Every conditioned object requires a regress toward its conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A2","text":"A logical requirement to seek conditions does not establish the actual givenness of those conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A3","text":"If conditioned and conditioning terms were things in themselves, the givenness of the conditioned would entail the givenness of the complete series of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A4","text":"Phenomena are representations given only through empirical synthesis in apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A5","text":"Empirical synthesis is necessarily successive and exists only in time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A6","text":"The complete series of empirical conditions can be given only through an actually executed regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A7","text":"The cosmological syllogism applies a transcendental meaning of “conditioned” in its major premise and an empirical meaning in its minor premise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A8","text":"Absolute totality is admissible as an idea of things in themselves but not as a determinate property of appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A9","text":"Opposed judgments may both be false when they share an ungrounded condition rather than stand in strict contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A10","text":"The world as a series of appearances exists only in empirical regress and not as a self-subsistent whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A11","text":"A series that is always conditioned cannot be given as an unconditioned completed whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:A12","text":"The collapse of cosmological antinomy supports the transcendental ideality of appearances without supplying positive dogmatic knowledge of metaphysical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:NX1","text":"The regress required by reason is not an already completed series of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:NX2","text":"A phenomenon is not a thing in itself available independently of empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:NX3","text":"The world is not an object given prior to the regressive synthesis of appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:NX4","text":"“Not infinite” is not identical with “finite” when the alleged object is not given as a quantity in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:NX5","text":"Dialectical opposition is not necessarily contradictory opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:NX6","text":"The critical dissolution of antinomy is not sceptical suspension between two equally valid world-theories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:NX7","text":"The antinomy is not a source of positive dogmatic knowledge concerning the world as a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:D1","text":"The demand for explanation extends regressively without authorizing an assertion that every explanatory condition is already present.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A1","CH_B337_SVCS:A2","CH_B337_SVCS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:D2","text":"The cosmological argument commits a fallacy of equivocation by transferring a relation valid for things in themselves to appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A3","CH_B337_SVCS:A4","CH_B337_SVCS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:D3","text":"Neither a finite nor an infinite world-whole follows from the empirical series of appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A5","CH_B337_SVCS:A8","CH_B337_SVCS:A10","CH_B337_SVCS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:D4","text":"The thesis and antithesis of each cosmological idea lose their apparent contradiction when their common assumption of a world in itself is withdrawn.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A8","CH_B337_SVCS:A9","CH_B337_SVCS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:D5","text":"The same restriction applies to totalities of parts, subordinated causes, and conditioned existence as to totality in spatial or temporal magnitude.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A4","CH_B337_SVCS:A5","CH_B337_SVCS:A6","CH_B337_SVCS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:D6","text":"Antinomial conflict diagnoses a false ontological presupposition rather than establishes either conflicting cosmological proposition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A7","CH_B337_SVCS:A9","CH_B337_SVCS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:SD1","text":"Reason’s demand for completeness is regulative within empirical inquiry but becomes dialectical when represented as constitutive knowledge of an unconditioned object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A1","CH_B337_SVCS:A2","CH_B337_SVCS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:SD2","text":"Cosmological totality is reclassified from a property of the world to a limit-idea generated by the procedure of regression.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A5","CH_B337_SVCS:A6","CH_B337_SVCS:A10","CH_B337_SVCS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:SD3","text":"The mutual strength of opposed antinomial proofs becomes evidence against their shared object-assumption rather than evidence for global scepticism.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A7","CH_B337_SVCS:A9","CH_B337_SVCS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:SD4","text":"The conditions for representing a world as an object forbid representing it as an absolutely completed object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A4","CH_B337_SVCS:A5","CH_B337_SVCS:A10","CH_B337_SVCS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:DNX1","text":"No inference may pass from the necessity of seeking conditions to the existence of an unconditioned totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:DNX2","text":"No empirical object may be detached from the temporal procedure through which it is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:DNX3","text":"No exhaustive disjunction between finitude and infinitude is valid unless the world is first established as a determinate thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:DNX4","text":"No cosmological predicate can be assigned to “the world as a whole” where the supposed whole has no presentation independent of regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:DNX5","text":"No resolution of antinomy may retain both the empirical reality of appearances and the transcendental reality of their completed totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:SNX1","text":"The system excludes any standpoint from which the totality of possible experience could be cognized as a completed object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a final empirical arrival at the unconditioned, because empirical conditions remain conditions only within an open regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:SNX3","text":"The system excludes metaphysical knowledge that would convert the formal demand of reason into intuition of an absolute whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:SNX4","text":"The system excludes the presupposition that the alternatives generated by reason must correspond to jointly available objects of decision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:FGS1","text":"Conditioned appearance → rational demand for conditions → empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:FGS2","text":"Empirical regress → temporally successive synthesis → absence of a pre-given completed series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:FGS3","text":"Transcendental concept of the conditioned + empirical appearance of the conditioned → equivocal cosmological syllogism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:FGS4","text":"Equivocal syllogism → projection of absolute totality onto appearances → thesis–antithesis conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:FGS5","text":"Withdrawal of world-in-itself assumption → conversion of contradiction into dialectical opposition → simultaneous invalidation of thesis and antithesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:FGS6","text":"Dissolved antinomy → indirect confirmation of transcendental ideality → critical rather than dogmatic metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AF1","text":"The apparent givenness of a conditioned object leads reason to posit the complete series of conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AF2","text":"The posited totality requires treating appearances as things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AF3","text":"Treating appearances as things in themselves permits opposed determinations of the world as finite or infinite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AF4","text":"The temporal and regressive character of empirical synthesis removes the warrant for either determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AF5","text":"The removal exposes both determinations as dialectically opposed claims resting on a shared illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:AF6","text":"The exposure converts antinomy into evidence that appearances lack self-subsistent totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:ME1","text":"The distinction between a required regress and a given totality separates methodological necessity from ontological assertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:ME2","text":"Finite and infinite cease to function as exhaustive predicates of the phenomenal world considered as a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:ME3","text":"Antinomy is reclassified from a failure of reason’s formal logic into a diagnostic instrument for uncovering transcendental illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:ME4","text":"The world is reclassified from a completed object of cosmology into an empirical series constituted in successive regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:ME5","text":"The confrontation of equally forceful proofs becomes critical evidence against their common premise rather than a warrant for equal assent or sceptical indecision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text denies that the phenomenal world can be established as either finite or infinite in its totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text locates antinomy in the illicit transfer of predicates from things in themselves to temporally synthesized appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text resolves a seemingly undecidable dispute by contesting the object-condition shared by both antagonists rather than adjudicating their conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text treats appearances as dependent on successive empirical synthesis and therefore incapable of existing as a completed unconditioned series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — The text preserves rational regress as a legitimate demand while denying speculative cognition of absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central transition from the legitimate regress to conditions to the invalid claim that the entire series is given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:MRT2","text":"The axioms also reconstruct the dissolution of finite–infinite antinomy by removing the presupposition of a world existing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent insofar as temporal succession blocks completed empirical totality while preserving the demand for continued regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it distinguishes logical requirement, empirical givenness, and transcendental objecthood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:MRT5","text":"The system depends on the asserted asymmetry between things in themselves and appearances, since the entire diagnosis fails if appearances may be cognized as self-subsistent totalities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:MRT6","text":"The system leaves underdetermined how an indefinitely required regress can be positively delimited without reintroducing some form of totality as a regulative horizon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVCS:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement only if the refutation of cosmological claims is retained as a transformation of their common presupposition rather than as a choice between them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVCS:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 67","title":"Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem","context":"Chapter 67"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC","layer":"CH","title":"Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God","order":81,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"63120741edb47e3afff5ec33ff5beb76784adc1f238bceb258532d2f07b5499a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:AS1","text":"The problem is how a proof beginning from empirical existence claims apodeictic knowledge of an absolutely necessary Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:AS2","text":"The argument is a transcendental-diagnostic critique that reconstructs the cosmological proof before identifying the conditions of its illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:AS3","text":"The cosmological proof moves from given existence to a necessary being and then from necessary being to the ens realissimum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:AS4","text":"Formal conversion reveals that the second transition presupposes the ontological inference from the ens realissimum to necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:AS5","text":"The proof further depends on transcendent causality, an illicit termination of regress, a merely negative conception of necessity, and a conflation of logical with transcendental possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:AS6","text":"The collapse of the proof leaves reason compelled to seek a necessary ground while unable to identify any determinate being as necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:AS7","text":"The contradiction is resolved by reclassifying necessity as a regulative demand for systematic unity whose constitutive hypostatization produces transcendental illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A1","text":"Reason demands an unconditioned ground for the completion of the regress through conditions of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A2","text":"Empirical consciousness warrants that something exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A3","text":"Experience cannot establish absolute necessity in any determinate being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A4","text":"The cosmological proof asserts that any given existence requires an absolutely necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A5","text":"The cosmological proof asserts that every absolutely necessary being is an ens realissimum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A6","text":"The ens realissimum is the unique ideal of complete a priori determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A7","text":"Complete conceptual determination of internal predicates does not determine existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A8","text":"An inference from the concept of the ens realissimum to necessary existence is an ontological inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A9","text":"An ontological inference cannot establish actual existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A10","text":"The category of causality has objective significance only within the phenomenal world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A11","text":"The removal of all conditions does not produce a positive conception of absolute necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A12","text":"Logical consistency does not establish transcendental possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A13","text":"No determinate being can be conceived as absolutely necessary when its nonexistence remains thinkable without contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A14","text":"Necessity and contingency function here as subjective principles of reason rather than objective properties of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A15","text":"The positive regulative rule requires inquiry to seek a necessary ground for every conditioned existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A16","text":"The negative regulative rule prohibits treating any empirical member of a regress as unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A17","text":"The ideal of a Supreme Being represents systematic unity as if grounded in an all-sufficient necessary cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:A18","text":"Transcendental subreption converts a regulative principle into a constitutive object and thereby generates dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX1","text":"The ontological argument is not a valid derivation of existence from a concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX2","text":"The cosmological proof is not an independent empirical alternative to the ontological argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX3","text":"The causal principle governing contingent phenomena is not valid beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX4","text":"The alleged impossibility of an infinite regress does not establish a first cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX5","text":"The cessation of conditioning is not a positive representation of necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX6","text":"Freedom from contradiction is not sufficient evidence of transcendental possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX7","text":"The complete internal determination supplied by the ens realissimum does not answer whether it exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX8","text":"An admissible hypothesis for organizing explanation is not apodeictic cognition of necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX9","text":"Nothing empirically given can be treated as an absolutely necessary primal being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX10","text":"Matter cannot serve as the unconditioned ground of empirical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX11","text":"The transcendental ideal is not a mysterious object whose nature is hidden from observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:NX12","text":"A principle required for systematic inquiry is not thereby a self-subsisting being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D1","text":"Experience supplies only the initial datum of existence and cannot identify either absolute necessity or its bearer.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A2","CH_B337_SVIC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D2","text":"The cosmological proof overextends phenomenal causality when it converts conditioned existence into a transcendent necessary cause.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A4","CH_B337_SVIC:A10","CH_B337_SVIC:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D3","text":"The noncompletion of an empirical causal regress does not license the introduction of a first unconditioned member.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A10","CH_B337_SVIC:A15","CH_B337_SVIC:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D4","text":"Conversion of the claim that every necessary being is an ens realissimum yields that some ens realissimum is necessary, while the ideal’s uniqueness extends necessity to the ens realissimum as such.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A5","CH_B337_SVIC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D5","text":"The decisive determination of the necessary being therefore reproduces the ontological inference that the ens realissimum necessarily exists.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A5","CH_B337_SVIC:A6","CH_B337_SVIC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D6","text":"The cosmological proof derives its apparent force from an ontological inference that cannot establish existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A7","CH_B337_SVIC:A8","CH_B337_SVIC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D7","text":"A merely conditionless remainder cannot satisfy the demand for a positively intelligible necessary being.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A1","CH_B337_SVIC:A11","CH_B337_SVIC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D8","text":"The logically coherent concept of total reality cannot by itself establish either its transcendental possibility or its actuality.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A7","CH_B337_SVIC:A9","CH_B337_SVIC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D9","text":"Reason must pursue completeness as if a necessary ground existed while refusing to terminate inquiry at any empirical condition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A14","CH_B337_SVIC:A15","CH_B337_SVIC:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D10","text":"The absolutely necessary is placed beyond the empirical world only as a principle of unity, not as a discovered transcendent object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A3","CH_B337_SVIC:A16","CH_B337_SVIC:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D11","text":"Phenomena may be explained through further phenomena as if no necessary being existed while completeness is pursued as if such a ground were presupposed.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A15","CH_B337_SVIC:A16","CH_B337_SVIC:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:D12","text":"The ideal’s reality is explainable through reason’s formal need for systematic unity rather than through evidence of a corresponding object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A14","CH_B337_SVIC:A17","CH_B337_SVIC:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SD1","text":"The cosmological and ontological proofs are not two independent proofs but two presentations of one concept-to-existence inference.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A2","CH_B337_SVIC:A4","CH_B337_SVIC:A5","CH_B337_SVIC:A6","CH_B337_SVIC:A8","CH_B337_SVIC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SD2","text":"The impossibility established is an impossibility of speculative demonstration, not a demonstration that no Supreme Being exists.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A3","CH_B337_SVIC:A7","CH_B337_SVIC:A9","CH_B337_SVIC:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SD3","text":"Reason is structurally divided between the unavoidable demand for an unconditioned ground and the impossibility of identifying any object as that ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A1","CH_B337_SVIC:A3","CH_B337_SVIC:A13","CH_B337_SVIC:A15","CH_B337_SVIC:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SD4","text":"The regulative reinterpretation converts speculative theology into a rule for maximizing systematic unity in empirical explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A14","CH_B337_SVIC:A15","CH_B337_SVIC:A16","CH_B337_SVIC:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SD5","text":"Rational inquiry remains indefinitely progressive because its projected completion guides the regress without becoming one of its members.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A15","CH_B337_SVIC:A16","CH_B337_SVIC:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SD6","text":"Transcendental illusion is natural and recurrent because reason mistakes the necessary form of its explanatory activity for a necessary condition of existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A14","CH_B337_SVIC:A17","CH_B337_SVIC:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SD7","text":"Critical resolution preserves the demand for total explanation while denying that this demand authorizes ontological closure.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A1","CH_B337_SVIC:A9","CH_B337_SVIC:A15","CH_B337_SVIC:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:DNX1","text":"A formal requirement of reason need not correspond to an independently existing object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:DNX2","text":"The incompleteness of a series does not imply the existence of a terminating member.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:DNX3","text":"Explanatory completeness is not equivalent to ontological completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:DNX4","text":"Conceptual maximality does not confer existential or modal privilege.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:DNX5","text":"The uniqueness of an idea does not entail the existence of a unique object corresponding to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:DNX6","text":"The subjective inevitability of an inference does not establish its objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:DNX7","text":"Placement beyond the world does not open a positively cognizable noumenal domain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:DNX8","text":"Reason’s inability to rest without an ultimate ground does not supply evidence for such a ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:DNX9","text":"Systematic unity in explanation need not be produced by a real all-sufficient cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SNX1","text":"The system lacks any legitimate bridge from conditioned existence to an unconditioned existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SNX2","text":"Absolute necessity lacks a positive concept through which it could be attributed to an individual being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SNX3","text":"The empirical regress lacks an admissible final term.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SNX4","text":"The transcendental ideal lacks object-givenness and therefore cannot become an inscrutable object of possible cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SNX5","text":"Speculative reason lacks a standpoint from which the formal conditions of thought can be identified with material conditions of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SNX6","text":"The argument is organized around a necessary ground that reason can neither eliminate from inquiry nor establish as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:SNX7","text":"Speculative reason is denied both apodeictic affirmation and apodeictic negation of the Supreme Being’s existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:FGS1","text":"The initial proof-chain is: empirical existence → demand for a sufficient ground → alleged absolutely necessary being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:FGS2","text":"The determination-chain is: necessary being → complete a priori determination → ens realissimum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:FGS3","text":"The conversion-chain is: necessary being implies ens realissimum → some ens realissimum is necessary → the unique ens realissimum is necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:FGS4","text":"The dependency diagnosis is: converted proposition → concept entails necessary existence → ontological argument reappears → cosmological independence collapses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:FGS5","text":"The fallacy diagnosis is: phenomenal causality universalized → regress forcibly terminated → absence of conditions treated as necessity → logical possibility treated as transcendental possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:FGS6","text":"The residual contradiction is: reason must seek a necessary basis → no individual being is conceivable as necessary → objective determination fails.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:FGS7","text":"The critical resolution is: objective necessity displaced → subjective regulative rules distinguished → systematic inquiry preserved without existential proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:FGS8","text":"The illusion-loop is: demand for unity → ideal all-sufficient cause → regulative principle treated constitutively → ideal hypostatized → critical exposure → regulative function restored.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:AF1","text":"Given existence → necessary being → ens realissimum → alleged necessary existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:AF2","text":"Formal conversion → ontological dependency exposed → transcendental fallacies isolated → cosmological proof invalidated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:AF3","text":"Unavoidable demand for a ground → inability to identify a necessary object → modality reclassified as regulative → Supreme Being retained as an as-if principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:AF4","text":"Regulative ideal → constitutive misreading → hypostatized object → natural dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ME1","text":"A proof is reclassified as nonempirical when experience supplies only its starting datum and pure concepts perform its decisive inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ME2","text":"The cosmological proof is reclassified from an independent argument to a disguised ontological argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ME3","text":"Absolute necessity is relocated from the properties of objects to the regulative economy of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ME4","text":"The Supreme Being is reclassified from a demonstrated existent to an ideal governing systematic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ME5","text":"The opposition between completed explanation and endless regress is replaced by complementary regulative rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ME6","text":"A transcendent first cause ceases to be a discoverable endpoint and becomes an as-if orientation external to the empirical series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ME7","text":"Theological proof is redrawn as a case of transcendental subreption in which a formal principle is mistaken for an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ME8","text":"The naturalness of an inference is separated from its validity, making unavoidable illusion compatible with critical correction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ALT1","text":"Altitude 1 — Surface: The cosmological proof fails to establish the necessary existence of a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ALT2","text":"Altitude 2 — Structural: Experience initiates the proof, but conceptual determination and ontological conversion perform its decisive work.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ALT3","text":"Altitude 3 — Transcendental: Categories valid for phenomena are illegitimately extended to an unconditioned object beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ALT4","text":"Altitude 4 — Metastrategic: Critique preserves reason’s search for unity by replacing constitutive assertion with regulative employment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ALT5","text":"Altitude 5 — Ontological: Necessity and contingency cease to function as cognizable properties of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:ALT6","text":"Altitude 6 — Temporal/Regressive: Inquiry is directed toward completeness while every empirical stopping point remains provisional.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the proof, its ontological dependency, its fallacies, and its regulative replacement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:MRT2","text":"Reconstruction depends on the prior invalidation of the ontological argument, which this section invokes rather than fully reproduces.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:MRT3","text":"The restriction of causality to phenomena depends on the broader critical account of categories and possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where formal conversion demonstrates that the cosmological proof cannot determine its necessary being without reviving the ontological inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:MRT5","text":"The uniqueness and complete determinacy of the ens realissimum are compressed premises whose full derivation lies outside the local argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:MRT6","text":"The placement of the necessary ground beyond the world risks constitutive reification unless read strictly as a regulative assignment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0015","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:MRT7","text":"The use of thinkable nonexistence to reject absolute necessity relies on a modal asymmetry not fully defended within this section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:MRT8","text":"The argument remains coherent if its conclusion is restricted to the impossibility of speculative proof rather than expanded into a denial of divine existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0009","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIC:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement: proof reconstruction → dependency exposure → fallacy inventory → rational antinomy → regulative resolution → diagnosis of subreption.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0007","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0013","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0014","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIC:P0016","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 78","title":"Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God","context":"Chapter 78"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP","layer":"CH","title":"Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof","order":82,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"a4ae97be4006ffc000c43df7d7cd80e18adf1eaefc7aaeeb903f1dc860362cf2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AS1","text":"The argument tests whether determinate experience of natural order can establish the existence of a Supreme Being after pure concepts and undetermined experience have failed to do so.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AS2","text":"Empirical order can warrant ascent only to a cause proportionate to observed arrangement, not to an unconditioned and all-sufficient being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AS3","text":"The physico-theological proof covertly exceeds experience when it converts a worldly designer into an absolutely necessary and completely determined reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AS4","text":"This excess transfers the proof into the cosmological argument, which in turn depends upon the ontological argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AS5","text":"The argument is diagnostic and reductive: it preserves the practical and heuristic force of natural teleology while denying its independent demonstrative force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AS6","text":"The movement runs from empirical admiration and purposiveness through causal inference and conceptual overreach to the exposure of transcendental dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A1","text":"No possible experience is adequate to a transcendental idea of the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A2","text":"Empirical causal inference has significance only within the domain of possible experience and sensible objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A3","text":"A cause placed within the empirical series is conditioned by that series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A4","text":"A cause placed outside the empirical series cannot be reached by empirical laws of causal regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A5","text":"Order, purposiveness, and harmony in nature indicate contingency of worldly form, not contingency of worldly matter or substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A6","text":"An inference from designed order can establish at most an intelligent architect constrained by given material.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A7","text":"A creator absolutely subjecting all things to itself requires proof of the contingency of matter itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A8","text":"Comparative predicates of greatness, wisdom, and power do not determine the intrinsic nature or absolute magnitude of their object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A9","text":"Only the conception of total reality yields a completely determined conception of all perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A10","text":"Absolute totality cannot be attained through empirical ascent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A11","text":"The transition from a contingent world to an absolutely necessary being employs transcendental concepts rather than empirical evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A12","text":"The transition from absolute necessity to an all-real being is ontological in structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:A13","text":"Speculative reason has no path to a Supreme Being other than the physico-theological, cosmological, and ontological routes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:NX1","text":"The Supreme Being is not an object whose transcendental reality can be matched by empirical evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:NX2","text":"The unconditioned is not discoverable as one conditioned item among other items in the causal series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:NX3","text":"An intelligible first cause is not inferentially connected to nature by empirical causal laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:NX4","text":"Natural purposiveness is not proof that matter itself originates from supreme intelligence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:NX5","text":"The inferred world-cause is not thereby a creator rather than an architect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:NX6","text":"Extraordinary magnitude is not equivalent to absolute perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:NX7","text":"Empirical teleology is not an independent demonstrative proof of theology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:NX8","text":"The physico-theological argument is not entitled to conceal its reliance on pure reason under an empirical appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:NX9","text":"The argument’s practical reasonableness and usefulness are not demonstrative certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:D1","text":"A first cause inferred wholly from empirical order must remain proportionate to empirical order rather than exceed it into absolute reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A1","CH_B337_SVIP:A2","CH_B337_SVIP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:D2","text":"The inference from natural design establishes a world-artificer only under the analogy with human art.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A5","CH_B337_SVIP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:D3","text":"The inference from a world-artificer to a world-creator requires a further proof that the material of the world is contingent.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A5","CH_B337_SVIP:A6","CH_B337_SVIP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:D4","text":"An empirically inferred cause cannot acquire the determinate predicates of omnipotence, highest wisdom, and all-sufficiency through comparative admiration alone.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A8","CH_B337_SVIP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:D5","text":"The proof abandons its empirical basis when it infers absolute necessity from worldly contingency.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A10","CH_B337_SVIP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:D6","text":"The proof becomes ontological when it identifies an absolutely necessary being with all-encompassing reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A9","CH_B337_SVIP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:D7","text":"The physico-theological proof can function as an introduction to theology only by borrowing the decisive inferential work from cosmological and ontological argument.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A11","CH_B337_SVIP:A12","CH_B337_SVIP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:SD1","text":"Empirical natural theology is structurally parasitic upon non-empirical premises precisely where it claims to derive the unconditioned from experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:D5","CH_B337_SVIP:D6","CH_B337_SVIP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:SD2","text":"The proof’s apparent continuity from observed order to divine perfection is interrupted by an unlicensed discontinuity between conditioned comparative predicates and absolute determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:D1","CH_B337_SVIP:D4","CH_B337_SVIP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:SD3","text":"Natural teleology retains legitimacy as a regulative orientation of inquiry while losing status as a constitutive cognition of a Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:NX7","CH_B337_SVIP:NX9","CH_B337_SVIP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:SD4","text":"The more the proof seeks a cause adequate to the whole world, the more it is forced away from experience and into pure conceptual construction.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A1","CH_B337_SVIP:A10","CH_B337_SVIP:A11","CH_B337_SVIP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:DNX1","text":"No finite empirical totality can supply a measure for absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:DNX2","text":"No causal relation valid for appearances can establish a relation between appearances and a transcendent cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:DNX3","text":"No analogy with finite craftsmanship can yield cognition of an unconditioned creative source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:DNX4","text":"No determinate theology can arise from predicates whose content is only relative to human comparison and reverence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:DNX5","text":"No speculative proof can be both wholly empirical in origin and sufficient for an object defined as transcending all possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:SNX1","text":"The unavailable object is a Supreme Being knowable as such through theoretical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:SNX2","text":"The foreclosed synthesis is a continuous passage from sensible nature to supersensible absolute reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:SNX3","text":"The argument disallows any final theological cognition whose certainty is greater than the conditions under which its concepts can be objectively applied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:FGS1","text":"Natural order → analogy with purposive art → inference to intelligent formative cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:FGS2","text":"Intelligent formative cause → demand for absolute sufficiency → failure of empirical predicates to determine absolute perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:FGS3","text":"Failure of empirical determination → inference from contingency to necessity → cosmological displacement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:FGS4","text":"Absolute necessity → complete determination through all reality → ontological dependency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:FGS5","text":"Ontological dependency → collapse of physico-theology’s claim to independent empirical proof.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:FGS6","text":"Collapse of demonstrative independence → retention of natural teleology as psychologically powerful and heuristically useful orientation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AF1","text":"Available proof-paths exhausted → determinate natural experience tested as final empirical basis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AF2","text":"Natural order and purposiveness → intelligent cause proportionate to observed form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AF3","text":"Proportionate intelligent cause → architect rather than creator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AF4","text":"Architect → insufficiency for all-sufficient Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AF5","text":"Empirical insufficiency → transcendental inference to necessity and total reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0010","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AF6","text":"Transcendental inference → cosmological argument → ontological argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:AF7","text":"Concealed ontological dependence → denial of independent physico-theological demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:ME1","text":"Natural order is reclassified from evidence of divine creation to evidence, at most, of formative intelligence operating upon given material.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:ME2","text":"The distinction between architect and creator becomes the decisive boundary between empirical teleology and theology of an all-sufficient being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:ME3","text":"Admiration of natural magnitude is separated from determination of divine attributes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0009","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:ME4","text":"The alleged opposition between empirical physico-theology and transcendental metaphysics collapses because the former depends on the latter at its decisive step.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:ME5","text":"The three traditional proofs are reorganized as a dependency chain rather than as independent routes to the same conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The argument assesses whether order and purposiveness in nature prove a Supreme Being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The proof is constrained by the difference between contingency of form and contingency of matter, and by the limits of causal inference within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument distinguishes legitimate heuristic employment of teleology from illegitimate claims of speculative demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Conditioned empirical sequences cannot yield cognition of an unconditioned, necessary, completely determined ground outside those sequences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the central reduction from natural design to a limited world-architect and the subsequent exposure of ontological dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:MRT2","text":"The system depends decisively on the prior invalidation or instability of the ontological proof, although that invalidation is not re-established within this section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent in denying that empirical evidence can determine a transcendent all-sufficient being while allowing empirical order to motivate regulative teleology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:MRT4","text":"The principal tension lies in preserving the powerful rational and practical authority of natural teleology after denying its capacity to yield theoretical cognition of its intended object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:MRT5","text":"The architect-creator distinction bears the argumentative burden and depends on accepting that observed order establishes only formal, not material, contingency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVIP:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the empirical ascent, the transcendental leap, and the concealed ontological completion remain analytically distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0008","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0011","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVIP:P0012","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 79","title":"Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof","context":"Chapter 79"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP","layer":"CH","title":"Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas","order":83,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"e16638753faab8e6ca07a5ca8c47405766727ca270f49f045197d948308fa29c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:AS1","text":"Cosmological totality cannot yield knowledge of the maximum series of conditions as an object existing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:AS2","text":"The demand for the unconditioned must be recast from a constitutive claim about objects into a regulative rule for empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:AS3","text":"Because every phenomenal condition is itself conditioned, empirical inquiry may not terminate at an allegedly absolute condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:AS4","text":"The rule determines the procedure of regression without determining whether the conditioned series is objectively finite or infinite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:AS5","text":"The form of legitimate regress depends on whether a whole is empirically given prior to regression or only constructed through regression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:AS6","text":"Cosmological ideas are thereby retained as directives for extending experience while denied objective reality as descriptions of a completed world-totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:A1","text":"Objects of sensuous experience are appearances rather than things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:A2","text":"Every condition encountered within appearance is itself conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:A3","text":"The absolutely unconditioned cannot be given in possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:A4","text":"Pure reason necessarily seeks totality in the series of conditions for a given conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:A5","text":"A principle that determines an object independently of empirical synthesis is constitutive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:A6","text":"A principle that prescribes the continuation of empirical synthesis without determining its object is regulative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:A7","text":"The cosmological idea of totality has validity only as a rule for regressive synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:A8","text":"No empirical limit may be treated as an absolutely unconditioned termination of regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:A9","text":"A regress is in infinitum when the whole whose internal conditions are sought is empirically given as a whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:A10","text":"A regress is in indefinitum when only a member is given and higher conditions must be sought without a given whole.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:A11","text":"Neither infinite nor indefinite regress licenses the claim that a phenomenal series is actually infinite in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:NX1","text":"The cosmological principle is not an axiom that presents totality as actual in the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:NX2","text":"The principle of regress is not a principle of the possibility of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:NX3","text":"The principle of regress is not a principle of the understanding that fixes empirical objects within determinate limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:NX4","text":"The cosmological idea is not a warrant for extending knowledge beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:NX5","text":"The unconditioned is not an empirically discoverable member of a series of appearances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:NX6","text":"A phenomenal series is not an object whose intrinsic quantity can be known as finite or infinite.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:NX7","text":"An empirical stopping point is not an absolute termination of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:NX8","text":"Progressus in infinitum is not equivalent to an actually infinite object-series.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:D1","text":"Empirical inquiry must continue from each discovered condition to further conditions without claiming possession of the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A2","CH_B337_SVRP:A3","CH_B337_SVRP:A7","CH_B337_SVRP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:D2","text":"Cosmological antinomies lose their object-level form because finitude and infinitude cannot be predicates of appearances considered apart from regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A1","CH_B337_SVRP:A5","CH_B337_SVRP:A7","CH_B337_SVRP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:D3","text":"The idea of totality functions as an unattainable task-condition for cognition rather than as a datum available to cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A3","CH_B337_SVRP:A4","CH_B337_SVRP:A6","CH_B337_SVRP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:D4","text":"A given whole permits assertion that further internal conditions are empirically present beyond those already reached by analysis.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:D5","text":"A merely given member permits only the requirement to search for higher conditions, not assertion that all such conditions are already given as a whole.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:D6","text":"The distinction between infinite and indefinite regress concerns the epistemic situation of empirical synthesis rather than the metaphysical size of the world.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A6","CH_B337_SVRP:A9","CH_B337_SVRP:A10","CH_B337_SVRP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:SD1","text":"The cosmological demand for completeness is converted from an ontological thesis into a permanently self-exceeding procedure of investigation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A3","CH_B337_SVRP:A4","CH_B337_SVRP:A6","CH_B337_SVRP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:SD2","text":"Reason expands empirical knowledge precisely by refusing to convert the provisional limits of experience into final limits of reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A2","CH_B337_SVRP:A7","CH_B337_SVRP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:SD3","text":"The same totality-idea produces two non-equivalent modes of inquiry because the mode of givenness determines what regress may assert.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A9","CH_B337_SVRP:A10","CH_B337_SVRP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:SD4","text":"The apparent opposition between a finite world and an infinite world is displaced by the distinction between completed objecthood and rule-governed regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A5","CH_B337_SVRP:A6","CH_B337_SVRP:A7","CH_B337_SVRP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:DNX1","text":"Phenomenal reality cannot be treated as self-subsistent independently of the conditions under which it is given in intuition and synthesized in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:DNX2","text":"Reason cannot occupy a standpoint from which the total series of worldly conditions is available at once.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:DNX3","text":"The absence of a presently known limit cannot be converted into knowledge that no limit exists.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:DNX4","text":"The absence of a completed regress cannot be converted into knowledge that the series is incomplete in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:DNX5","text":"Completion cannot be represented as an empirical achievement where the rule of inquiry excludes an empirically given unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a final empirical position from which reason could certify its own regress as complete.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the transformation of methodological necessity into ontological disclosure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:SNX3","text":"The system excludes an object-level world-totality proportionate to reason’s demand for absolute completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:SNX4","text":"The system forecloses a definitive cosmological answer while preserving the rational necessity of continuing to seek one condition beyond every conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:FGS1","text":"Given conditioned appearance → every found condition is conditioned → no empirical unconditioned is available.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:FGS2","text":"Rational demand for totality + empirical unavailability of the unconditioned → totality is converted from object-claim into rule of regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:FGS3","text":"Regulative rule → continued search for conditions → prohibition against treating empirical limits as absolute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:FGS4","text":"Whole empirically given → internal analysis of already given conditions → regressus in infinitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:FGS5","text":"Only a member empirically given → search for unprovided higher conditions → regressus in indefinitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:FGS6","text":"Distinction of regress-modes → suspension of claims that phenomenal series are finite or infinite in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:AF1","text":"Cosmological totality as alleged knowledge → denial of its constitutive validity → recoding as a regulative rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:AF2","text":"Conditioned appearances → impossibility of empirical access to the unconditioned → compulsory continuation of regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:AF3","text":"Continuation of regress → distinction between given-whole and ungiven-whole cases → distinction between infinitum and indefinitum.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:AF4","text":"Distinction between regress-modes → rejection of finite/infinite predicates for phenomenal series in themselves → methodological cosmology.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:ME1","text":"Cosmological ideas are reclassified from failed descriptions of the world into valid rules for enlarging empirical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:ME2","text":"Infinity is reclassified from a property of a world-object into a mode of permissible empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:ME3","text":"The opposition between finite and infinite cosmology is displaced by the distinction between empirical givenness and objective totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:ME4","text":"Empirical boundaries become provisional nodes in inquiry rather than final boundaries of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:ME5","text":"The epistemic status of a series depends on how it is given, not on a presumed intrinsic magnitude belonging to the series itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text determines how inquiry should proceed when seeking conditions for a given conditioned phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The distinction between appearance and thing in itself prevents the series of appearances from bearing predicates of absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Reason is disciplined by retaining its demand for systematic completion while forbidding it to present that demand as knowledge of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The world of appearance has no available completed totality independent of the temporal and iterative process of empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central conversion of cosmological totality from constitutive doctrine into regulative procedure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:MRT2","text":"The distinction between appearances and things in themselves is indispensable because it blocks the inference from rational totality to worldly totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:MRT3","text":"The distinction between infinitum and indefinitum is indispensable because it specifies two different relations between inquiry and empirical givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:MRT4","text":"The system is coherent insofar as it consistently separates the rule governing regress from an object allegedly given independently of regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:MRT5","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning how an investigator can identify a genuinely given whole without importing a prior totalizing judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:MRT6","text":"The claim that a whole may be empirically given while its internal regress proceeds without end creates pressure between finite intuition and inexhaustible decomposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVRP:MRT7","text":"The system relies on an asymptotic conception of complete cognition while denying that the completion toward which inquiry is directed can ever be attained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVRP:P0007","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 68","title":"Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas","context":"Chapter 68"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE","layer":"CH","title":"Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas","order":84,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"5e2e6139056bcc0013a04a0102e61917dffaf3e4aa601ef14de7d0a590860936","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:AS1","text":"Dogmatic answers to cosmological questions are to be suspended when either answer increases obscurity and generates contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:AS2","text":"The sceptical comparison of opposed answers tests whether the question rests on a groundless presupposition rather than deciding either side.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:AS3","text":"The argument is diagnostic: antinomy is treated as evidence that a cosmological idea falsely represents its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:AS4","text":"Every cosmological idea of an unconditioned totality is measured against the conditions of possible empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:AS5","text":"Infinite regress makes the cosmological object too great for empirical synthesis, while a terminating condition makes it too small for the demand for further conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:AS6","text":"The reciprocal failure of both sides redirects inquiry from cosmological objects to the mode in which those objects are supposed to be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A1","text":"Possible experience alone gives objective reality to a conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A2","text":"A conception without possible experience is an idea without truth or relation to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A3","text":"A cosmological idea concerns the unconditioned totality of a regressive synthesis of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A4","text":"An object represented through a cosmological idea must be adequate to a possible conception of the understanding if it is to have significance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A5","text":"A successive empirical regress cannot overtake or complete an infinite series of elapsed conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A6","text":"Every empirically given condition requires the search for a higher condition under the laws of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A7","text":"A finite termination of empirical regress cannot be unconditioned when further conditions remain thinkable or empirically required.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A8","text":"Absolute void is neither a self-subsistent correlate of things nor an empirical condition available in possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A9","text":"Empirical causality requires each given event-cause to be sought as conditioned by a prior cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A10","text":"Contingent existence requires the search for another existence on which it depends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A11","text":"An absolutely necessary existence cannot be reached through a finite empirical regress from a given moment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:A12","text":"When opposed dogmatic answers are equally nonsensical, the question itself warrants critical investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:NX1","text":"Dogmatic affirmation and dogmatic negation are not alternatives that necessarily yield cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:NX2","text":"An infinite temporal, spatial, causal, or divisional totality is not an object completable by empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:NX3","text":"A temporal beginning, spatial limit, simple part, spontaneous event, or necessary being is not an unconditioned stopping point if it remains subject to further conditioning inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:NX4","text":"Empty space is not an experiential object that can determine the boundary of a finite world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:NX5","text":"A bare idea is not objectively valid merely because it can be represented in thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:NX6","text":"Cosmological contradiction is not resolved by selecting the thesis or antithesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:NX7","text":"Universal science is not secured by presumptuous extension of pure reason beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:D1","text":"The opposed claims that the world has a beginning and that it has no beginning both fail to yield an empirically adequate unconditioned totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A3","CH_B337_SVSE:A5","CH_B337_SVSE:A6","CH_B337_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:D2","text":"The opposed claims that the world is spatially infinite and that it is spatially finite both fail because infinity exceeds empirical synthesis while a limit requires a nonempirical determinant.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A5","CH_B337_SVSE:A7","CH_B337_SVSE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:D3","text":"The opposed claims that matter is infinitely divisible and that division terminates in simples both fail to supply an unconditioned object of division.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A5","CH_B337_SVSE:A6","CH_B337_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:D4","text":"The opposed claims of universal natural causality and spontaneous freedom both fail to close the causal regress required by empirical explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A6","CH_B337_SVSE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:D5","text":"The opposed claims of an absolutely necessary being and universal contingency both fail to provide an empirically attainable unconditioned ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A10","CH_B337_SVSE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:D6","text":"Cosmological ideas are void of objective significance insofar as every proposed object is either excessive or deficient relative to possible empirical regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A1","CH_B337_SVSE:A2","CH_B337_SVSE:A3","CH_B337_SVSE:A4","CH_B337_SVSE:A5","CH_B337_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:D7","text":"The sceptical procedure functions as a cathartic because it converts irresolvable dogmatic conflict into a critique of the question’s presuppositions.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:SD1","text":"The four cosmological antinomies are structurally homologous because each converts the rule of regressive conditioning into a supposed object of completed totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A3","CH_B337_SVSE:A5","CH_B337_SVSE:A6","CH_B337_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:SD2","text":"Antinomy is reclassified from a contest between doctrines into a diagnostic symptom of transcendental illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A4","CH_B337_SVSE:A12","CH_B337_SVSE:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:SD3","text":"The empirical regress becomes the normative measure of cosmological validity because possible experience, rather than pure ideation, determines objective reference.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A1","CH_B337_SVSE:A2","CH_B337_SVSE:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:SD4","text":"The apparent opposition between infinite and finite cosmological totality collapses because each side fails under a different requirement of the same empirical standard.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A5","CH_B337_SVSE:A6","CH_B337_SVSE:A7","CH_B337_SVSE:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:SD5","text":"Critical reason does not answer the cosmological demand for an unconditioned object; it invalidates the object-form assumed by that demand.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A2","CH_B337_SVSE:A3","CH_B337_SVSE:A12","CH_B337_SVSE:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:DNX1","text":"The conditioned series is not given in experience as a completed totality merely because its regress can be represented.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A1","CH_B337_SVSE:A3","CH_B337_SVSE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:DNX2","text":"The unconditioned is not available as an empirical object within the very series whose conditions empirical inquiry must continue to seek.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A6","CH_B337_SVSE:A7","CH_B337_SVSE:A10","CH_B337_SVSE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:DNX3","text":"The demand for an ultimate cosmological condition is not licensed by the empirical law that requires conditioned explanations.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A3","CH_B337_SVSE:A6","CH_B337_SVSE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:DNX4","text":"The representation of the world, matter, causality, or existence as a whole is not equivalent to an encounter with an object corresponding to that whole.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A1","CH_B337_SVSE:A2","CH_B337_SVSE:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:SNX1","text":"No cosmological object can be both wholly given to experience and absolutely unconditioned within the regressive order of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A1","CH_B337_SVSE:A3","CH_B337_SVSE:A6","CH_B337_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:SNX2","text":"The conversion of a necessary rule for seeking conditions into knowledge of a completed unconditioned reality is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A2","CH_B337_SVSE:A6","CH_B337_SVSE:A9","CH_B337_SVSE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:SNX3","text":"Neither side of a cosmological antinomy is available as a final doctrinal position when the question’s object lacks possible experiential presentation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A1","CH_B337_SVSE:A4","CH_B337_SVSE:A12","CH_B337_SVSE:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:FGS1","text":"A demand for an unconditioned cosmological object enters a domain in which objective reference requires possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:FGS2","text":"The demand is expressed as a binary assertion of infinite continuation or finite termination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:FGS3","text":"Infinite continuation exceeds the capacity of successive empirical synthesis to reach totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:FGS4","text":"Finite termination violates the requirement to seek a further condition for what has been given as conditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:FGS5","text":"Bilateral failure deprives the cosmological idea of an adequate object and transforms antinomy into evidence of a false object-presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:FGS6","text":"Critical investigation replaces doctrinal adjudication by analysis of the presupposition generating the contradictory alternatives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:AF1","text":"Dogmatic demand for an answer → comparison of opposed answers → detection of bilateral nonsense → testing by possible empirical regress → identification of excess or deficiency → suspicion of fictitious object-presentation → critical exposure of transcendental illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:AF2","text":"Temporal totality, spatial totality, divisional totality, causal totality, and existential totality are processed through the same excess-or-deficiency test.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:AF3","text":"The conclusion preserves the demand for empirical conditioning while withdrawing objective validity from the cosmological totality demanded by pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:ME1","text":"Antinomies become indicators of a defective question-form rather than evidence that reality contains contradictory determinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:ME2","text":"Infinite and finite answers cease to be rival cosmological doctrines and become reciprocal modes of failure relative to empirical regress.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:ME3","text":"Possible experience becomes the criterion separating objectively valid concepts from fictitious totalizing ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:ME4","text":"The four transcendental ideas become instances of one structural error: treating an unconditioned totality as an empirically given object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:ME5","text":"Scepticism is repositioned as an instrument of critical purification rather than as a refusal of rational inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text examines opposed answers concerning the beginning, extent, divisibility, causality, and necessary ground of the world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:ALT2","text":"Structural — Each cosmological problem is governed by the incompatibility between complete totality and the open regress of empirical conditioning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text substitutes critique of the question’s presupposition for dogmatic selection between its answers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:ALT4","text":"Epistemic — Objective reference belongs only to what can be related to possible experience through concepts of the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:ALT5","text":"Temporal — The temporal regress exposes the general structure most directly: neither an elapsed infinity nor a first moment can function as an empirically adequate unconditioned totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central inference that all four antinomies fail through the same relation between empirical regress and unconditioned totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:MRT2","text":"The system remains underdetermined regarding the positive origin of the false conception of object-presentation, because the text concludes with a suspicion and a prospective critical exposure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if possible experience is accepted as the exclusive source of objective reality for cosmological concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it demonstrates that infinite continuation and finite closure fail symmetrically under one empirical criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:MRT5","text":"The system compresses a substantive premise into its standard of judgment: the priority of possible experience over any purported object of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:MRT6","text":"A structural tension remains because the same demand for conditions that governs empirical inquiry is shown to become illegitimate when converted into a demand for a completed unconditioned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVSE:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if sceptical suspension is treated as the transition to critique rather than as the final position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVSE:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 65","title":"Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas","context":"Chapter 65"}},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI","layer":"CH","title":"Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic","order":85,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"92a0da8e1a56f979c9602bc0372d775eb762bced3bda5c6c90e5fcf07ead8a48","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:AS1","text":"Pure cosmological dialectic arises when objects of possible experience are treated as things possessing determinate existence independently of the conditions under which they can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:AS2","text":"Transcendental idealism reframes spatial and temporal objects as phenomena constituted within human sensibility rather than as self-subsistent things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:AS3","text":"The argument preserves empirical realism by locating reality in lawful connection among actual and possible perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:AS4","text":"Claims about unperceived, past, distant, or future objects are valid only as claims concerning the possible continuation or regression of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:AS5","text":"The non-sensuous ground of appearances can be thought only as an unknown transcendental correlate and cannot supply determinate cosmological knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:AS6","text":"Cosmological illusion is dissolved when the demand for an absolute world-whole is returned to the immanent rules and limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:A1","text":"Everything intuited in space and time is phenomenon rather than thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:A2","text":"Space and time are forms of human sensibility rather than properties of things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:A3","text":"Phenomena possess no self-subsistent existence apart from the conditions of their representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:A4","text":"Extended bodies and temporal changes are empirically real within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:A5","text":"Empirical reality consists in lawful connection between perception and possible perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:A6","text":"No phenomenon is actually given as real except through perception or its lawful empirical continuation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:A7","text":"Assertions about unperceived objects signify the possibility of finding them through empirical progression or regression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:A8","text":"The temporal determination of the empirical self is phenomenon rather than the transcendental subject itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:A9","text":"The non-sensuous cause of appearances is unknowable because it cannot be intuited under space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:A10","text":"The transcendental object is only a mental correlate of sensibility and not an object of determinate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:A11","text":"Cosmological claims become illusory when empirical objects are posited as an absolute totality independent of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:NX1","text":"Transcendental idealism is not empirical idealism, because it does not deny the reality of external bodies in space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:NX2","text":"Empirical reality is not existence as a thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:NX3","text":"Space and time are not containers or conditions belonging to things independently of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:NX4","text":"Phenomena are not self-subsistent entities existing outside experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:NX5","text":"The empirical self appearing in time is not the real transcendental self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:NX6","text":"The transcendental object is not a knowable non-sensuous object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:NX7","text":"Past, distant, or unobserved objects are not objects for cognition merely by being imagined as independently existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:NX8","text":"The absolute completeness of all objects in space and time is not an object given prior to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:D1","text":"External objects can be affirmed as real without being affirmed as things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A1","CH_B337_SVTI:A2","CH_B337_SVTI:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:D2","text":"The distinction between reality and illusion is empirical rather than metaphysical, because both are representations but only reality belongs to a lawful experiential order.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A3","CH_B337_SVTI:A5","CH_B337_SVTI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:D3","text":"An unobserved object is real only insofar as it is locatable within a possible empirical series connected to actual perception.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A5","CH_B337_SVTI:A6","CH_B337_SVTI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:D4","text":"Assertions about historical events mean that present experience can be regressed according to empirical laws toward conditions determining it.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A5","CH_B337_SVTI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:D5","text":"Assertions about indefinitely distant objects mean that experience could be extended toward them, not that they are cognized as independently given entities.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A1","CH_B337_SVTI:A6","CH_B337_SVTI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:D6","text":"The empirical subject cannot ground knowledge of the subject as it exists in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A2","CH_B337_SVTI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:D7","text":"The transcendental object can regulate reference to an unknown ground of appearances without yielding knowledge of that ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A9","CH_B337_SVTI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:D8","text":"Cosmological reasoning exceeds its warrant when it converts the open series of possible experience into a completed world-totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A5","CH_B337_SVTI:A7","CH_B337_SVTI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:SD1","text":"Empirical realism and transcendental idealism are complementary positions: the first affirms objects within experience, while the second denies their independent noumenal status.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A1","CH_B337_SVTI:A3","CH_B337_SVTI:A4","CH_B337_SVTI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:SD2","text":"Objectivity is transformed from independent subsistence into lawful availability for a unified possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A3","CH_B337_SVTI:A5","CH_B337_SVTI:A6","CH_B337_SVTI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:SD3","text":"Temporal and spatial totalization is structurally illegitimate because every empirical object is given only within an indefinitely extendible, never pre-given experiential series.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A2","CH_B337_SVTI:A5","CH_B337_SVTI:A7","CH_B337_SVTI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:SD4","text":"The cosmological antinomy is displaced from a conflict among facts about the world to a misuse of the conditions under which worldly objects can be cognized.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A1","CH_B337_SVTI:A9","CH_B337_SVTI:A10","CH_B337_SVTI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:SD5","text":"The argument restricts metaphysical assertion while preserving the full operational authority of empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A4","CH_B337_SVTI:A5","CH_B337_SVTI:A9","CH_B337_SVTI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:DNX1","text":"No cognition can compare appearances with things as they are independently of the forms of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:DNX2","text":"No object can be determinately real for cognition outside a possible order of perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:DNX3","text":"No empirical regress can disclose the unconditioned ground determining where that regress must ultimately terminate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:DNX4","text":"No temporal self-description can yield access to the subject that undergoes temporal appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:DNX5","text":"No cosmological whole can be given as complete if the conditions of objectivity require possible experiential continuation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a standpoint external to human sensibility from which the world could be known as it exists in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:SNX2","text":"The system excludes the possibility that absolute totality could become an empirical object without ceasing to be absolute totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:SNX3","text":"The system excludes a final empirical conversion of the transcendental object into a positively cognized cause or substrate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:SNX4","text":"The system excludes metaphysical completion as a condition of empirical objectivity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:FGS1","text":"Spatial-temporal intuition → appearance rather than thing in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:FGS2","text":"Appearance + lawful connection among perceptions → empirical objectivity and empirical reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:FGS3","text":"Actual perception + possible progression or regression → warranted claims concerning absent, past, distant, or future objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:FGS4","text":"Appearance treated as independently subsistent → transcendental realism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:FGS5","text":"Transcendental realism + demand for absolute completeness → pure cosmological illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:FGS6","text":"Return of the object to possible experience + restriction of the transcendental ground to unknowability → dissolution of cosmological dialectic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:AF1","text":"Cosmological demand for an independently real world-whole → distinction between phenomenon and thing in itself → affirmation of empirical reality through lawful possible experience → restriction of unperceived existence to experiential progression → exclusion of knowledge of the transcendental ground → neutralization of cosmological totalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:ME1","text":"External reality is reclassified from noumenal subsistence into empirical validity within space, time, and lawful experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:ME2","text":"The opposition between realism and idealism is redrawn so that empirical realism can coexist with transcendental idealism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:ME3","text":"Past, future, distant, and unobserved entities are reclassified as positions in possible empirical continuation rather than independently available objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:ME4","text":"The self is divided into a temporally appearing empirical subject and an unknowable transcendental subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:ME5","text":"Cosmological questions are reclassified from inquiries into a world-existing-in-itself to inquiries into the legitimate scope of experiential regress and progression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:ALT1","text":"Surface — Bodies in space, representations in time, historical events, and unobserved objects are affirmed as empirically real.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:ALT2","text":"Structural — Space and time condition all empirical givenness, while lawful unity of experience conditions the reality of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text resolves dialectic by converting metaphysical assertions into claims about the conditions and limits of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Temporal succession belongs to appearance, and temporal regress can extend indefinitely without yielding an unconditioned world or subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central transition from transcendental ideality to empirical realism and then to the restriction of cosmological claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:MRT2","text":"The system depends principally on the distinction between empirical existence and existence in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if possible experience is accepted as the sole condition under which phenomenal objectivity can be asserted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:MRT4","text":"The strongest inferential hinge is the conversion of claims about absent objects into claims about lawful possible perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:MRT5","text":"The relation between the transcendental object’s asserted existence and its status as a merely mental correlate remains structurally compressed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:MRT6","text":"The claim that empirical reality is guaranteed beyond doubt depends on the assumed sufficiency of lawful experiential connection to distinguish reality from illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:MRT7","text":"The system leaves underdetermined how an unknowable transcendental ground can be invoked as a correlate without acquiring illicit positive determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_SVTI:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement only if the final restriction on cosmological totality is treated as a consequence of the theory of experience rather than as an independently imposed prohibition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_SVTI:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 66","title":"Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic","context":"Chapter 66"}},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI","layer":"CH","title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason.","order":86,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"ebf58517a9d4ff4bcc6b39da8175995e9767ac7bdf8a3368309d1ed9f2ce47a8","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:AS1","text":"The text distinguishes conceptions of pure reason from conceptions of understanding by their mode of derivation and scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:AS2","text":"Conceptions of understanding organize possible experience, whereas conceptions of reason are inferred from cognition and reach toward what exceeds experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:AS3","text":"The understanding determines objects through empirical cognition, while reason seeks the comprehension of cognition under an unconditioned ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:AS4","text":"Rational conceptions can possess objective validity only if their transcendence of experience remains connected to the totality and ordering of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:AS5","text":"The text distinguishes legitimately concluded rational conceptions from sophistical conceptions whose validity rests only on the appearance of correct inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:AS6","text":"The argument moves from distinction of faculties, through restriction of objective validity, to the naming and future justification of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A1","text":"Conceptions of pure reason are obtained through inference or conclusion rather than through mere reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A2","text":"Conceptions of understanding are conceived a priori and make experience possible by unifying phenomena within possible empirical consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A3","text":"Cognition and the determination of an object require the conceptions of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A4","text":"The objective reality of conceptions of understanding depends on restricting their application to the sphere of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A5","text":"Conceptions of reason do not confine themselves within the limits of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A6","text":"The object-matter of reason is a cognition of which empirical cognition and even the whole of possible experience may be only a part.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A7","text":"The aim of reason is comprehension, whereas the aim of understanding is the understanding of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A8","text":"The unconditioned sought by reason is that to which all experience is subordinate but which is never itself an object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A9","text":"Reason uses the unconditioned as a standard for estimating the degree of empirical cognition without making it an element of empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A10","text":"Rational conceptions possess objective validity only when their inference is legitimate rather than merely apparently correct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A11","text":"Legitimately concluded conceptions of reason are conceptus ratiocinati.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A12","text":"Sophistical conceptions of reason are conceptus ratiocinantes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:A13","text":"The pure conceptions of reason are designated transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:NX1","text":"Conceptions of pure reason are not obtained by reflection alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:NX2","text":"Conceptions of reason are not derived from prior a priori conceptions of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:NX3","text":"Conceptions of understanding do not extend legitimately beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:NX4","text":"The unconditioned is not an object of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:NX5","text":"The unconditioned is not an element in an empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:NX6","text":"Empirical cognition does not fully attain the cognition toward which reason tends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:NX7","text":"A conception is not objectively valid merely because it appears to have been correctly concluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:NX8","text":"The text does not yet establish the objective validity of rational conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:NX9","text":"Transcendental ideas are not categories of understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:D1","text":"The understanding supplies the material from which reason conducts its inferences.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A2","CH_B337_TDBI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:D2","text":"Reason cannot generate its objects from independent a priori object-conceptions because no such prior conceptions are available antecedently to the understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A2","CH_B337_TDBI:A3","CH_B337_TDBI:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:D3","text":"The understanding has objective applicability through limitation, while reason has its defining tendency through transcendence of that limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A4","CH_B337_TDBI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:D4","text":"Reason’s transcendence does not sever it from experience, because the object sought by reason includes experience as a subordinate part.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A5","CH_B337_TDBI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:D5","text":"Reason’s relation to empirical cognition is regulative and comparative rather than constitutive of an empirical object.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A8","CH_B337_TDBI:A9","CH_B337_TDBI:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:D6","text":"The same rational demand for the unconditioned can yield either legitimate conceptions or sophistical pseudo-conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A8","CH_B337_TDBI:A10","CH_B337_TDBI:A11","CH_B337_TDBI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:D7","text":"The validity of rational conceptions requires a dialectical examination of the inferences by which they are produced.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A1","CH_B337_TDBI:A10","CH_B337_TDBI:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:D8","text":"Transcendental ideas are defined as the rational counterparts of the categories of understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A7","CH_B337_TDBI:A13","CH_B337_TDBI:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:SD1","text":"The architecture of cognition is stratified: understanding constitutes determinate experience, while reason seeks the totality or ground under which determinate cognition can be comprehended.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A2","CH_B337_TDBI:A3","CH_B337_TDBI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:SD2","text":"The unconditioned functions as a boundary-concept: it cannot constitute an experienced object, yet it organizes the direction and evaluation of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A8","CH_B337_TDBI:A9","CH_B337_TDBI:NX4","CH_B337_TDBI:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:SD3","text":"Reason’s legitimate use depends on maintaining a tension between transcendence of experience and dependence upon cognition supplied by the understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A2","CH_B337_TDBI:A5","CH_B337_TDBI:A6","CH_B337_TDBI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:SD4","text":"The possibility of transcendental illusion arises because a necessary demand for completion can be mistaken for an already validated object-conception.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A8","CH_B337_TDBI:A10","CH_B337_TDBI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:SD5","text":"The transition from categories to transcendental ideas converts the analysis of cognition into an analysis of reason’s tendency toward totality and the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A2","CH_B337_TDBI:A7","CH_B337_TDBI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:DNX1","text":"The system excludes a self-sufficient rational ontology whose objects are known independently of the conditions of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:DNX2","text":"The system excludes the reduction of reason to the mere organization of empirical perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:DNX3","text":"The system excludes the identification of the unconditioned with any determinate experienced object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the inference that cognitive necessity automatically yields objective existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:DNX5","text":"The system excludes the assumption that the totality of experience can itself be completed as an empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:DNX6","text":"The system excludes the conflation of the function of guiding cognition with the production of constitutive knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:DNX7","text":"The system excludes the equivalence of inferential appearance and inferential validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:SNX1","text":"The system cannot admit an unconditioned that is both fully available to experience and genuinely unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses complete empirical closure: experience can be organized and evaluated, but cannot contain the ultimate condition of its own totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:SNX3","text":"The system withholds any final object corresponding directly to reason’s demand for comprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:SNX4","text":"The argument is organized around the absence of a cognition that would exhaust the relation between conditioned experience and its unconditioned ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:SNX5","text":"The system cannot resolve, at this stage, whether the unconditioned has objective validity or is only the product of reason’s inferential demand.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:FGS1","text":"A priori synthesis of phenomena → unity of empirical consciousness → determinate cognition and objectification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:FGS2","text":"Determinate cognition supplied by understanding → inferential movement of reason → demand for cognition beyond the empirical part.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:FGS3","text":"Demand for the unconditioned → relation of all experience to a superior ground → non-empirical standard for evaluating empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:FGS4","text":"Rational inference → distinction between legitimate conclusion and merely apparent conclusion → conceptus ratiocinati / conceptus ratiocinantes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:FGS5","text":"Differentiation from categories of understanding → designation of reason’s pure conceptions as transcendental ideas → requirement of later dialectical validation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:AF1","text":"A priori understanding → constitution of possible experience → provision of cognitive material → inferential activity of reason → pursuit of the unconditioned → distinction between valid and sophistical rational conceptions → designation of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:ME1","text":"The field of a priori cognition is divided into constitutive concepts of understanding and transcendent concepts of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:ME2","text":"Objective validity is reclassified from a general feature of a priori cognition into a function of restricted applicability and legitimate inference.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:ME3","text":"The unconditioned is repositioned from a possible object of knowledge to a non-empirical standard governing the comprehension and assessment of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:ME4","text":"The boundary between legitimate metaphysical cognition and sophistical appearance becomes an inferential problem rather than a merely doctrinal opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:ME5","text":"Reason’s excess beyond experience is treated as structurally generated by cognition itself rather than as an arbitrary departure from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:ME6","text":"The transition from categories to ideas redraws the conceptual map from object-determination toward totality, completion, and ultimate conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text distinguishes conceptions of understanding from conceptions of pure reason and introduces the term “transcendental ideas.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:ALT2","text":"Structural — Understanding constitutes possible experience through unity of reflection, while reason operates upon that cognition by seeking its unconditioned ground and total comprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text establishes a test for separating legitimate rational extension from sophistical inference: the form and validity of the conclusion must be examined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Reality is divided between conditioned objects available within experience and an unconditioned sought by reason but unavailable as an empirical object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the distinction between understanding and reason, their different objects, and their different modes of operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:MRT2","text":"The axioms are sufficient to derive the restricted objective validity of understanding and the transcendent orientation of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:MRT3","text":"The structure preserves the distinction between legitimate and sophistical rational conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:MRT4","text":"The status of the unconditioned remains intentionally underdetermined because its objective validity is deferred to the later dialectical analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:MRT5","text":"The system is internally coherent insofar as reason’s transcendence remains dependent upon cognition first constituted by the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:MRT6","text":"The principal unresolved tension is that reason seeks cognition beyond experience while its only available material originates within experience-bound understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDBI:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement from cognitive constitution, through inferential excess, to the naming and prospective validation of transcendental ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDBI:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 54","title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason.","context":"Chapter 54"}},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP","layer":"CH","title":"Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22","order":87,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"4aa78916e926a4e78a4606fa0682e0489f6a52ee9bd986caf440f68e832b515b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:AS1","text":"The problem is to establish why pure categories necessarily govern every object that can enter possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:AS2","text":"The argument shifts from the categories’ logical origin to their necessary employment in empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:AS3","text":"Perception is grounded in a synthesis of apprehension that must conform to the a priori unity of space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:AS4","text":"This required unity of sensible synthesis is identified with the intellectual unity expressed in the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:AS5","text":"Because experience consists in conjoined perceptions, categories become conditions of possible experience and laws of nature in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:AS6","text":"The deduction closes by restricting categorical legislation to universal conditions of nature, while reserving particular natural laws for empirical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A1","text":"Categories are a priori concepts originating in the understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A2","text":"Perception is possible only through a synthesis of apprehension of an empirical manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A3","text":"Every synthesis of apprehension must conform to the forms of space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A4","text":"Space and time are formal intuitions containing an a priori unity of their manifolds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A5","text":"The unity required for sensible apprehension is a synthetic unity of a given manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A6","text":"The synthetic unity of sensible apprehension is the intellectual unity represented by the categories when applied to sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A7","text":"Experience is cognition through conjoined perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A8","text":"Phenomena are representations existing only in relation to a subject possessing sensibility and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A9","text":"Laws governing the conjunction of phenomena exist only in relation to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A10","text":"Categories prescribe the universal conditions of lawful nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A11","text":"Particular empirical laws cannot be derived wholly from pure categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:A12","text":"Experience must supplement pure principles in the cognition of particular laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:NX1","text":"Categories are not empirical concepts abstracted from nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:NX2","text":"Categories do not regulate themselves by taking nature as a prior model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:NX3","text":"The deduction does not concern the sensible form of intuition as such, but the lawful conjunction of its manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:NX4","text":"Laws of phenomenal conjunction do not exist in phenomena independently of their relation to understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:NX5","text":"Phenomena are not things in themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:NX6","text":"Perception is not a mere reception of an already unified sensible given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:NX7","text":"Pure categories do not yield determinate empirical laws of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:D1","text":"Every possible empirical perception is subject to the categories because apprehension requires categorical synthetic unity.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A2","CH_B337_TDUP:A3","CH_B337_TDUP:A5","CH_B337_TDUP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:D2","text":"Categories are valid a priori for all objects of possible experience because every such object is cognized only through conjoined perceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A6","CH_B337_TDUP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:D3","text":"Spatial perception presupposes categorical quantity because the apprehended manifold must be synthesized as a unified magnitude.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A3","CH_B337_TDUP:A4","CH_B337_TDUP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:D4","text":"The perception of temporal succession presupposes causality because an event must be apprehended under a determined temporal relation.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A3","CH_B337_TDUP:A5","CH_B337_TDUP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:D5","text":"Nature in general is necessarily lawful because all phenomenal conjunction depends upon the categories.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A8","CH_B337_TDUP:A9","CH_B337_TDUP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:D6","text":"The objective scope of categories is limited to phenomena because their necessity is established only as a condition of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A7","CH_B337_TDUP:A8","CH_B337_TDUP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:D7","text":"Empirical science requires both pure principles and empirical supplementation because universal lawfulness does not determine particular laws.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A11","CH_B337_TDUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:SD1","text":"The necessity of natural law is relocated from independently existing phenomena to the conditions under which phenomena can be experienced as objects.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A7","CH_B337_TDUP:A8","CH_B337_TDUP:A9","CH_B337_TDUP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:SD2","text":"Nature in the formal sense is constituted as an ordered field of possible experience rather than discovered as a self-legislating empirical object.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A6","CH_B337_TDUP:A8","CH_B337_TDUP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:SD3","text":"The same spontaneity grounds both empirical perception and intellectual lawfulness by synthesizing sensible multiplicity into objective unity.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A2","CH_B337_TDUP:A5","CH_B337_TDUP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:SD4","text":"Categorical necessity is universally binding but materially indeterminate, because it orders all possible experience without producing its particular empirical content.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A10","CH_B337_TDUP:A11","CH_B337_TDUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:DNX1","text":"A subject-independent phenomenal order cannot ground the necessity of experience, because phenomenal lawfulness is conditioned by synthesis and understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:DNX2","text":"An intuition that unifies itself without synthesis cannot serve as the basis of perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:DNX3","text":"Objective validity cannot consist in the categories copying laws already present in phenomenal nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:DNX4","text":"Empirical determinacy cannot be exhausted by pure conceptual legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:SNX1","text":"Things in themselves remain unavailable as objects to which categories can be validly applied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:SNX2","text":"No uncategorized sensible manifold can count as a perceived or experienced object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:SNX3","text":"No complete a priori science of determinate natural laws is available from the categories alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:FGS1","text":"Sensibility supplies a manifold under space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:FGS2","text":"Formal intuition supplies the a priori unity required for apprehending that manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:FGS3","text":"Imaginative apprehension synthesizes the manifold into perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:FGS4","text":"Intellectual spontaneity gives this synthesis categorical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:FGS5","text":"Categorically unified perceptions compose possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:FGS6","text":"Possible experience constitutes nature in general as necessarily lawful.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:FGS7","text":"Empirical investigation supplies the determinations excluded from pure categorical legislation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:AF1","text":"Nonempirical origin of categories → need to establish their objective use → analysis of perception as synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:AF2","text":"Sensible manifold in space and time → required synthetic unity → categorical determination of apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:AF3","text":"Categorically determined perception → conjoined perceptions → possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:AF4","text":"Possible experience → lawful phenomenal nature → a priori validity of categories for all experiential objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:AF5","text":"Universal conditions of nature → exclusion of particular laws from pure deduction → empirical supplementation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:ME1","text":"The categories are reclassified from logical forms of thought into necessary conditions of objective empirical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:ME2","text":"Nature is divided into formal lawfulness, governed a priori, and particular empirical law, cognized only through experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:ME3","text":"The opposition between subjective synthesis and objective natural order is recast as a relation of constitutive condition and conditioned phenomenon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:ME4","text":"Space and time are reclassified from passive forms of receptivity into formal intuitions requiring synthetic unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:ME5","text":"Imagination is positioned as the mediating operation through which sensible apprehension and intellectual unity coincide.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text establishes the validity of categories for every object that can be given in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:ALT2","text":"Structural — Perception depends on a synthesis whose sensible manifold, formal unity, and intellectual determination are inseparable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The question of whether nature conforms to categories is resolved by redefining nature as phenomenal lawfulness constituted under conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Phenomenal objects exist only within subject-relative forms of sensibility and understanding, while temporal succession becomes cognizable only under categorical relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:ALT5","text":"Epistemic — Pure understanding supplies universal conditions of possible experience but cannot replace empirical inquiry into determinate natural laws.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the deduction from sensible manifold through synthesis, perception, experience, and formal nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:MRT2","text":"The system depends on the prior establishment that categories originate a priori in the understanding and possess transcendental validity for objects in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:MRT3","text":"The central inferential hinge is the identification of sensible synthetic unity with categorical intellectual unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:MRT4","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning how the unity belonging to formal intuition can belong to sensibility while presupposing a synthesis not belonging to sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:MRT5","text":"The system is internally coherent only if phenomenal nature is restricted to nature as possible experience rather than treated as nature in itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:MRT6","text":"The deduction’s strongest closure is the transition from the conditions of perception to the universal lawfulness of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:MRT7","text":"The system cannot derive empirical natural science in its determinate content, because particular laws remain dependent on experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TDUP:MRT8","text":"The extracted structure preserves the argument’s movement from the possibility of perception to the lawful constitution and empirical limitation of nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TDUP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 39","title":"Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22","context":"Chapter 39"}},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2","layer":"CH","title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason","order":88,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"2779cb6434707c1148d54c21212f278194769aeca30d7cab3eb20cc55d06b792","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:AS1","text":"Reason necessarily generates ideas whose objects cannot be adequately conceived or known within possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:AS2","text":"The argument diagnoses dialectical error as a necessary procedure of pure reason rather than as a contingent human mistake.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:AS3","text":"Pure reason proceeds without empirical premises from something known to something neither known nor conceptually possessed, then ascribes objective reality to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:AS4","text":"This attribution is unavoidable illusion: the arguments are syllogistic in origin but sophistical in result.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:AS5","text":"The dialectical procedure divides into three forms corresponding to the subject, the series of conditions, and the totality of conditions for thinking objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:AS6","text":"The movement proceeds from absolute unity of the subject through the unconditioned unity of conditioned series to the being and necessity of all beings, while each conclusion exceeds possible conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A1","text":"An object adequate to a transcendental idea would have to be presented and intuited in possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A2","text":"No object perfectly corresponding to a transcendental idea can therefore be known, although a problematical conception of it may be held.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A3","text":"Transcendental ideas are necessary products of the original laws of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A4","text":"Pure concepts of reason possess subjective transcendental reality because a necessary procedure of reason produces them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A5","text":"Pure reason forms syllogisms that contain no empirical premises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A6","text":"These syllogisms infer from something known to something of which no knowledge or adequate conception is possessed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A7","text":"The inference ascribes objective reality to its unconceived conclusion through unavoidable illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A8","text":"The dialectical arguments are syllogisms by origin but sophisms by result.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A9","text":"Their necessity derives from the nature of pure reason rather than from fiction, accident, or individual error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A10","text":"The illusion generated by pure reason cannot be eradicated, although the resulting error can be guarded against.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A11","text":"The dialectical arguments divide into three kinds corresponding to three ideas: the subject, the series of conditions for a phenomenon, and the totality of conditions for thinking objects in general.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A12","text":"The transcendental conception of the subject contains no manifold and is converted into the absolute unity of the subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A13","text":"The self-contradictory conception of the unconditioned synthetic unity of a conditioned series is converted into the truth of the opposite unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:A14","text":"The totality of conditions for thinking objects is converted into the absolute synthetic unity of all conditions and into a being of all beings possessing unconditioned necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:NX1","text":"A transcendental idea is not accompanied by an object that can be adequately conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:NX2","text":"A transcendental idea does not yield knowledge of an object perfectly corresponding to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:NX3","text":"The dialectical arguments are not fictions or accidental products of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:NX4","text":"The dialectical arguments are not merely sophisms produced by individual human beings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:NX5","text":"A conclusion inferred by pure reason does not thereby possess legitimate objective reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:NX6","text":"Critical awareness does not abolish the illusion generated by pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:NX7","text":"The absolute unity of the subject is not attained as an adequate conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:NX8","text":"The unconditioned synthetic unity of the series of conditions is not attained as an adequate conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:NX9","text":"The being of all beings and its unconditioned necessity are not attained through a transcendental conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:NX10","text":"The dialectical procedure is not grounded in empirical premises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:D1","text":"The necessary generation of a transcendental idea does not establish the knowability of its object.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A1","CH_B337_TD_2:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:D2","text":"Subjective transcendental reality can exist without objective cognition of the corresponding object.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A3","CH_B337_TD_2:A4","CH_B337_TD_2:A5","CH_B337_TD_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:D3","text":"Pure reason produces objective seeming by extending a formally necessary inference beyond the limits of knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A6","CH_B337_TD_2:A7","CH_B337_TD_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:D4","text":"The dialectical error is structurally unavoidable because its source is the necessary operation of reason itself.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A8","CH_B337_TD_2:A9","CH_B337_TD_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:D5","text":"The paralogism arises when the empty unity of the subject-concept is treated as the absolute unity of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A11","CH_B337_TD_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:D6","text":"The antinomy arises when the contradiction of the unconditioned series is resolved by asserting the opposite unity without possessing its conception.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A11","CH_B337_TD_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:D7","text":"The ideal of pure reason arises when the totality of conditions is elevated into a being of all beings whose necessity remains inconceivable.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A11","CH_B337_TD_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:D8","text":"The three dialectical forms share a procedure that converts a formal demand for unity into an asserted unconditioned object.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A12","CH_B337_TD_2:A13","CH_B337_TD_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:SD1","text":"Pure reason is simultaneously the source of transcendental ideas, the generator of their objective seeming, and the ground of the illusion that misrepresents them as knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A2","CH_B337_TD_2:A3","CH_B337_TD_2:A4","CH_B337_TD_2:A5","CH_B337_TD_2:A6","CH_B337_TD_2:A7","CH_B337_TD_2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:SD2","text":"Dialectic is produced by the conflict between reason’s necessary demand for unconditioned unity and the conditions under which objects can be known.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A1","CH_B337_TD_2:A2","CH_B337_TD_2:A6","CH_B337_TD_2:A7","CH_B337_TD_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:SD3","text":"The three dialectical forms expand the scope of totalization from the subject to conditioned series and finally to the totality of things, while the object becomes progressively less conceptually available.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A11","CH_B337_TD_2:A12","CH_B337_TD_2:A13","CH_B337_TD_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:SD4","text":"Formal necessity and epistemic illegitimacy coexist within the same argument, making dialectic neither arbitrary error nor valid cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A8","CH_B337_TD_2:A9","CH_B337_TD_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:SD5","text":"The proper critical response is not the elimination of illusion but the separation of reason’s necessary procedure from the erroneous objective conclusion attached to it.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A8","CH_B337_TD_2:A9","CH_B337_TD_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:DNX1","text":"Rational necessity does not entail the knowability of the object generated by that necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:DNX2","text":"Subjective transcendental reality does not entail objective transcendental reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:DNX3","text":"Formal inference from the known does not provide a conception of the unconditioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:DNX4","text":"No valid correction can remove the reason-generated procedure that produces dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:DNX5","text":"The origin of dialectic cannot be displaced onto individual psychology or contingent misuse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:DNX6","text":"No unconditioned unity is available as an object independently of the totalizing operation that projects it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:SNX1","text":"No possible experience closes the gap between reason’s demand for unconditioned unity and the conditions of objective cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:SNX2","text":"No standpoint external to pure reason is available from which its dialectical illusion could be wholly abolished.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:SNX3","text":"The system is organized around the permanent non-coincidence between reason’s necessary totalizing procedure and the object it claims to disclose.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:FGS1","text":"Necessary law of reason → transcendental idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:FGS2","text":"Transcendental idea → demand for an adequate object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:FGS3","text":"Possible-experience criterion → impossibility of adequate object and persistence of a problematical conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:FGS4","text":"Nonempirical syllogism → inference from known premise to unconceived conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:FGS5","text":"Unconceived conclusion → attribution of objective reality → unavoidable illusion → sophism in result.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:FGS6","text":"Subject without manifold → absolute unity of subject → transcendental paralogism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:FGS7","text":"Series of conditions → self-contradictory unconditioned unity → assertion of opposite unity → antinomy of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:FGS8","text":"Totality of conditions for thinking objects → absolute synthetic unity → being of all beings → ideal of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:FGS9","text":"Persistent illusion → distinction between necessary origin and erroneous result → guarding against error without abolishing illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:AF1","text":"Necessary idea → impossible adequate object → problematical conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:AF2","text":"Nonempirical inference → movement from known to unconceived → objective ascription → unavoidable illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:AF3","text":"Subjective unity → series-totality → totality of all conditions → paralogism, antinomy, ideal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:AF4","text":"Syllogistic origin → sophistical result → error-control rather than abolition of illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:ME1","text":"Transcendental ideas are reclassified from candidate objects of knowledge into necessary but problematical products of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:ME2","text":"Dialectical error is relocated from contingent human reasoning to the constitutive procedure of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:ME3","text":"The distinction between origin and result permits critique without treating reason as arbitrary or accepting its conclusions as knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:ME4","text":"The field of unconditioned claims is stratified into psychological unity, cosmological series, and theological totality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:ME5","text":"Contradiction in the conception of the unconditioned series is reclassified as a structural antinomy rather than as a simple failure of reasoning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:ME6","text":"The critical task is redirected from eliminating metaphysical illusion to identifying its necessary production and limiting its epistemic effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:ALT1","text":"Surface: Pure reason generates three kinds of dialectical arguments whose conclusions concern the subject, conditioned series, and totality of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:ALT2","text":"Structural: A nonempirical syllogistic procedure converts formal concepts of unity and totality into claims about unconditioned objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Critique must distinguish necessary rational procedure from illegitimate objective interpretation and can regulate error without abolishing illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological: Reality is divided between the subjective transcendental necessity of reason’s ideas and the absence of an objectively cognizable object corresponding to them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from necessary idea to dialectical illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:MRT2","text":"The extraction preserves the distinction between subjective transcendental reality and objective knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:MRT3","text":"The extraction preserves the dual status of the arguments as syllogisms by origin and sophisms by result.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:MRT4","text":"The three branches can be reconstructed as successive forms of totalization: subject, conditioned series, and all conditions of things.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:MRT5","text":"The exact formal premises of the three arguments remain underdetermined because the passage names their structures without developing their full demonstrations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:MRT6","text":"The system is internally coherent only if necessary generation is kept distinct from objective validity; that distinction is also its central unresolved fault line.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:MRT7","text":"The argument is strongest in explaining the unavoidable origin of dialectical illusion and depends most on compressed claims about how each formal inference produces its conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TD_2:MRT8","text":"The reconstruction preserves the real movement from reason’s necessary demand for unity to the permanent impossibility of converting that demand into knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TD_2:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 58","title":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason","context":"Chapter 58"}},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC","layer":"CH","title":"Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10","order":89,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"a8aff054453dd2620282469f5b0c0e03c9fd2504aa839d2a232d3bfe8fcc3376","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AS1","text":"Synthetic representation can necessarily correspond to an object only if either the object grounds the representation or the representation formally determines the object as cognizable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AS2","text":"Empirical affection can explain representations only as contingent sensations and cannot explain objectively valid a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AS3","text":"Intuition gives an object as phenomenon, while conception thinks what is given as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AS4","text":"The transcendental deduction must establish categories as a priori conditions under which experience can contain objects rather than merely find categories within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AS5","text":"Locke’s empirical derivation and Hume’s habitual association fail because neither can ground the necessity exhibited by mathematics and general physics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AS6","text":"The argument is a transcendental diagnostic and reconstructive argument moving from the possibility of objective experience to the necessary validity and limits of the categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A1","text":"A synthetic representation and its object can coincide necessarily only through the object’s production of representation or the representation’s formal determination of the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A2","text":"When an object produces a representation, the relation is empirical and cannot yield an a priori representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A3","text":"Representation can make an object possible for cognition without producing the object’s existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A4","text":"Cognition of an object requires intuition through which an object is given and conception through which it is thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A5","text":"The formal condition of sensibility exists a priori in the mind and necessarily conditions every phenomenon as intuitable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A6","text":"Experience contains both sensible intuition and a conception of the object given in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A7","text":"If a conception is a condition under which anything can be thought as an object, it necessarily applies a priori to every object of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A8","text":"The objective validity of an a priori conception is established only by showing that it is a condition of the possibility of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A9","text":"The discovery of a conception in experience cannot deduct its necessity or objective validity from experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A10","text":"Categories are conceptions of an object in general that determine an intuition in relation to logical functions of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A11","text":"Logical functions of judgment alone do not determine the objective roles of concepts in intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:A12","text":"Scientific a priori cognition in mathematics and general physics cannot be reconciled with an empirical derivation of its constitutive conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:NX1","text":"A priori representation is not produced by objects insofar as they affect sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:NX2","text":"The category is not a cause that brings an object into existence through the will or through conceptual production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:NX3","text":"The transcendental deduction is not an empirical inventory or analysis of concepts encountered in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:NX4","text":"Objective necessity is not subjective expectation generated by repeated association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:NX5","text":"Categories are not empirically derived abstractions from experienced objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:NX6","text":"Logical judgment is not by itself sufficient to determine the objective status of subject and predicate in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:NX7","text":"The legitimate use of a priori conceptions is not unrestricted cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:NX8","text":"The required position is neither Lockean empiricism opening dogmatic excess nor Humean skepticism closing a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:D1","text":"Every phenomenon necessarily conforms to the a priori forms of sensibility because it can be given only through those forms.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A4","CH_B337_TTDC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:D2","text":"If categories are conditions of thinking an object, all empirical cognition must conform to them because experience contains objects only as thought through conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A6","CH_B337_TTDC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:D3","text":"The deduction of categories must proceed from the possibility of experience rather than from the psychological origin of concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A8","CH_B337_TTDC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:D4","text":"Categories possess objective validity only for objects insofar as those objects belong to possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A7","CH_B337_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:D5","text":"Empiricism cannot account for necessary scientific cognition because empirical origin supplies neither necessity nor universal objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A2","CH_B337_TTDC:A9","CH_B337_TTDC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:D6","text":"The category of substance determines the empirical intuition of body as necessarily thinkable as subject rather than merely as predicate.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A10","CH_B337_TTDC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:SD1","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as lawful availability for experience under a priori conditions rather than as a relation secured by the independent object’s direct imprint on the mind.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A1","CH_B337_TTDC:A3","CH_B337_TTDC:A7","CH_B337_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:SD2","text":"The categories mediate between logical form and empirical objectivity by converting functions of judgment into determinations under which intuition can count as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A4","CH_B337_TTDC:A6","CH_B337_TTDC:A10","CH_B337_TTDC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:SD3","text":"The argument establishes a bounded rationalism in which a priori necessity is preserved precisely by restricting its validity to possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A7","CH_B337_TTDC:A8","CH_B337_TTDC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:SD4","text":"The debate over the origin of concepts is displaced by a functional question: what must already be valid for experience to present objects at all.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A7","CH_B337_TTDC:A8","CH_B337_TTDC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:DNX1","text":"No object can be cognized as an object of experience independently of the a priori forms through which it is given and thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:DNX2","text":"No transition from subjective psychological regularity to objective necessity is available without a priori conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:DNX3","text":"No category has demonstrable objective validity merely by being logically conceivable or empirically encountered.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:DNX4","text":"No legitimate metaphysical cognition can claim validity by extending categories beyond the field in which intuition can be given.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses an account in which objectivity is grounded in access to objects wholly independent of the conditions of their possible presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses an account in which necessity is either extracted from experience or imposed arbitrarily upon it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:SNX3","text":"The system leaves unavailable any standpoint from which the correspondence of categories and objects could be established outside the possibility of experience itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:FGS1","text":"Necessary object-representation correspondence → exclusion of empirical affection as a source of a priori necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:FGS2","text":"Conditions of cognition → intuition gives phenomena + conception thinks objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:FGS3","text":"A priori form of sensibility → necessary conformity of all phenomena to conditions of intuition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:FGS4","text":"Possible a priori concepts of objects → categories become necessary conditions of thinking objects in experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:FGS5","text":"Conditions of possible experience → objective validity of categories within experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:FGS6","text":"Rejection of empirical genesis → rejection of Lockean dogmatism and Humean skepticism → bounded legitimate reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:FGS7","text":"Logical functions of judgment + empirical intuition → categorial determination of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AF1","text":"Necessary correspondence between representation and object → distinction between empirical production and formal determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AF2","text":"Formal determination of cognizable objects → intuition and conception as irreducible conditions of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AF3","text":"A priori forms of intuition → question whether a priori concepts likewise condition objecthood.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AF4","text":"Categories as conditions of possible experience → objective validity of categories for experienced objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AF5","text":"Failure of empirical derivations → rejection of dogmatic extension and skeptical restriction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:AF6","text":"Categories as determinations linked to judgment-functions → transcendental deduction as the proof of their legitimate use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:ME1","text":"The origin-question concerning categories is reclassified as a validity-question concerning the conditions under which experience can contain objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:ME2","text":"The opposition between empirical dependence and a priori objectivity is reorganized by distinguishing causal production of representations from formal conditions of cognizability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:ME3","text":"Objectivity is relocated from resemblance or association to the necessary structure of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:ME4","text":"Categories are reclassified from abstract general concepts into rules that determine how intuition is thinkable as an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:ME5","text":"Rational legitimacy is made coextensive with the domain of possible experience, dissolving the apparent choice between unlimited metaphysics and total skepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text asks whether categories possess objective validity and defines them as concepts determining objects through judgment-functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:ALT2","text":"Structural — Experience is constituted as the unity of sensible givenness and conceptual object-determination under a priori conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument replaces empirical genealogy with transcendental deduction and positions itself between dogmatic rational extension and skeptical empiricism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects are available for cognition only as phenomena ordered under conditions of possible experience; the argument does not establish categories as valid for objects apart from such availability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:MRT1","text":"The axioms suffice to reconstruct the transition from the problem of necessary representation-object correspondence to the need for a transcendental deduction of categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:MRT2","text":"The system depends decisively on the distinction between an object’s existence and its possibility as an object of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:MRT3","text":"The reconstruction preserves the central proof-form only if intuition and conception remain jointly necessary and irreducible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:MRT4","text":"The system is strongest where it shows why empirical occurrence cannot establish necessity or objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:MRT5","text":"The relation between logical functions of judgment and the complete system of categories remains compressed here and requires subsequent deduction for full specification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:MRT6","text":"A persistent tension remains between the claim that representation makes objects possible for cognition and the restriction that representation does not produce their existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_TTDC:MRT7","text":"Another unresolved boundary concerns how the deduction can secure objective validity for experience without claiming cognition of objects independently of the conditions of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_TTDC:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 29","title":"Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10","context":"Chapter 29"}},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS","layer":"CH","title":"V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.","order":90,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"41af2cc982047d292783fd85fea8394cff98265f8c6691a5e64804f51046d74d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:AS1","text":"The argument distinguishes necessary judgement from analytic judgement in order to identify the principles proper to theoretical science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:AS2","text":"The principle of contradiction can govern deduction from a synthetic premise without generating synthetic content by itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:AS3","text":"Mathematics demonstrates that necessary and universally valid propositions require intuition added to concepts and are therefore synthetic a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:AS4","text":"Pure physics demonstrates that necessary laws add predicates not contained in the concepts of matter and motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:AS5","text":"Metaphysics, insofar as it seeks to enlarge a priori cognition beyond conceptual analysis and experience, necessarily consists of synthetic a priori propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:AS6","text":"The argument moves from the failure of analyticity to account for necessary knowledge toward the classification of all theoretical sciences by synthetic a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:A1","text":"Experience cannot supply the necessity characteristic of proper a priori judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:A2","text":"Analytic judgement explicates only what is already thought in a given concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:A3","text":"Synthetic judgement adds to a concept a predicate not contained within that concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:A4","text":"The principle of contradiction can establish the consistency or deduction of a proposition but cannot independently establish synthetic content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:A5","text":"Mathematical cognition requires intuition corresponding to concepts in order to produce determinate quantitative or spatial content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:A6","text":"Mathematical propositions are necessary, non-empirical, and synthetic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:A7","text":"The fundamental propositions of pure natural philosophy are necessary, a priori, and synthetic.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:A8","text":"Metaphysics is constituted by the attempt to extend a priori cognition beyond the analysis of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:A9","text":"Extension of a priori cognition requires synthetic a priori judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:A10","text":"Mathematics, pure natural philosophy, and metaphysics belong to the domain of theoretical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:NX1","text":"Mathematical judgement is not analytic merely because its conclusions can be derived in accordance with the principle of contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:NX2","text":"A determinate sum or geometrical magnitude is not obtainable through the analysis of its component concepts alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:NX3","text":"The necessity of a judgement is not evidence that its predicate is conceptually contained in its subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:NX4","text":"Pure natural laws are not empirical generalizations merely because they concern material nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:NX5","text":"Metaphysics is not merely the analytic clarification of concepts formed a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:NX6","text":"Analytic mathematical propositions are not the constitutive principles of mathematics, even where they serve its method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:D1","text":"Apodeictic certainty does not reduce to analyticity, because a synthetic proposition may be necessarily deduced only after a prior synthetic ground has supplied its content.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A3","CH_B337_VATS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:D2","text":"Arithmetic requires constructive addition in intuition because the concept of combining numbers does not contain the resulting number.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A2","CH_B337_VATS:A3","CH_B337_VATS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:D3","text":"Geometry requires intuition because qualitative straightness does not analytically contain comparative magnitude.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A2","CH_B337_VATS:A3","CH_B337_VATS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:D4","text":"The law of conservation of matter is synthetic because permanence is added to the concept of matter rather than extracted from it.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A3","CH_B337_VATS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:D5","text":"The equality of action and reaction is synthetic a priori because it supplies a necessary relation not contained in the bare conception of communicated motion.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A3","CH_B337_VATS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:D6","text":"Metaphysical propositions concerning the world’s beginning exemplify an a priori extension that exceeds both conceptual identity and experiential limits.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A8","CH_B337_VATS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:SD1","text":"The formal validity of proof and the material source of cognition must be distinguished, because contradiction can order a science without furnishing its substantive principles.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A3","CH_B337_VATS:A4","CH_B337_VATS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:SD2","text":"Theoretical science is redefined as a field whose necessary principles expand cognition rather than merely unpack conceptual content.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A1","CH_B337_VATS:A3","CH_B337_VATS:A7","CH_B337_VATS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:SD3","text":"Intuition functions as the positive condition of mathematical synthesis while conceptual analysis functions only as a negative test and methodological link.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A2","CH_B337_VATS:A4","CH_B337_VATS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:SD4","text":"Metaphysics is structurally compelled to claim more than analytic clarification, because its defining aim is the expansion of a priori knowledge beyond experience.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A8","CH_B337_VATS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:DNX1","text":"Necessary cognition is not derivable from accumulated experience, however universal its observed regularities may appear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:DNX2","text":"Conceptual containment is not the universal model of intelligibility for theoretical judgement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:DNX3","text":"Logical non-contradiction is not a sufficient source of objective or determinate cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:DNX4","text":"Theoretical reason is not confined to the representation of what is already given in experience or already included in concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:SNX1","text":"There is no purely discursive route from concepts alone to the substantive necessities claimed by mathematics and theoretical science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:SNX2","text":"There is no reduction of metaphysics to either empirical science or formal logic if metaphysics is to retain its stated function of a priori extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:SNX3","text":"The system excludes the possibility that demonstrative certainty and cognitive novelty are mutually incompatible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:FGS1","text":"Necessity excludes empirical origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:FGS2","text":"Conceptual analysis excludes predicates not already contained in the concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:FGS3","text":"A predicate not contained in a concept requires synthetic supplementation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:FGS4","text":"Mathematical supplementation proceeds through corresponding intuition, whereas physical and metaphysical supplementation proceeds through necessary a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:FGS5","text":"Necessary synthetic supplementation across mathematics, physics, and metaphysics establishes synthetic a priori judgement as the principle-form of theoretical reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:AF1","text":"The apparent analyticity of apodeictic judgement is displaced by the distinction between logical deduction and synthetic grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:AF2","text":"The distinction between analysis and synthesis is tested through arithmetic and geometry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:AF3","text":"Intuition is introduced as the condition enabling mathematical synthetic cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:AF4","text":"The same synthetic a priori structure is extended to the necessary laws of pure physics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:AF5","text":"Metaphysics is then classified as necessarily synthetic a priori because its aim is to expand cognition beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:ME1","text":"The opposition between necessary and synthetic judgement collapses, since necessity no longer marks analytic containment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:ME2","text":"Logic is reclassified from a source of substantive scientific principles to a condition for testing and linking propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:ME3","text":"Intuition is reclassified from an optional illustration of mathematical reasoning to a constitutive condition of mathematical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:ME4","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from conceptual dissection to a discipline defined by the attempted enlargement of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text classifies mathematical, physical, and metaphysical principles as synthetic a priori judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text separates conceptual containment, logical deduction, intuition, and a priori extension as distinct operations of cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text defeats the inference from demonstrative certainty to analyticity and thereby reopens theoretical science to substantive a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The text presupposes that necessity cannot arise from temporal experience and that some cognition exceeds the limits of what experience can supply.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the classification of mathematics, pure physics, and metaphysics as domains containing synthetic a priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:MRT2","text":"The system is strongest where it distinguishes analytic containment from necessary synthesis through the mathematical cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:MRT3","text":"The transition from mathematical intuition to the synthetic a priori principles of physics is asserted by examples rather than derived through an equivalent account of their conditions of possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:MRT4","text":"The claim that metaphysics is indispensable is not itself established by the argument that metaphysics must be synthetic if it seeks a priori enlargement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:MRT5","text":"The objective validity of metaphysical extension beyond experience remains underdetermined, since the text establishes its required judgement-form without validating particular metaphysical propositions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:MRT6","text":"The system depends on a tension between limiting metaphysics beyond experience and treating its synthetic a priori expansion as the proper aim of reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VATS:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement if logical deduction, synthetic grounding, intuition, and a priori enlargement remain non-interchangeable functions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VATS:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 05","title":"V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles.","context":"Chapter 05"}},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP","layer":"CH","title":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.","order":91,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"3e4b4709292cdd6857a4b4e0e93b5866a22e5492728bb82a69050bfff0802770","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:AS1","text":"The possibility of a systematic pure reason must first be tested because its scope and legitimacy remain undetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:AS2","text":"This test requires a critique of reason’s own a priori sources, conditions, limits, and validity rather than a doctrine extending knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:AS3","text":"Transcendental inquiry shifts attention from objects to the a priori conditions under which objects can be given and thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:AS4","text":"The critique is restricted to what is necessary for judging synthetic a priori cognition, although it must enumerate its radical concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:AS5","text":"The inquiry is architectonically ordered because a future system requires principled completeness and secure division.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:AS6","text":"Pure speculative reason divides into the conditions of object-givenness and object-thinking, with sensibility preceding understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A1","text":"Reason supplies principles of a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A2","text":"Pure reason contains principles for cognizing objects absolutely a priori.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A3","text":"An organon would provide the principles by which all pure a priori cognitions are obtained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A4","text":"The possibility and extent of a pure-reason organon are not established in advance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A5","text":"A critique can determine the sources, limits, and validity of pure reason without enlarging its knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A6","text":"Transcendental knowledge concerns the a priori mode of cognition of objects rather than objects considered independently of that mode.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A7","text":"Transcendental philosophy would be the complete system of principles of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A8","text":"The critique need analyze only as far as is necessary to assess synthetic a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A9","text":"The a priori cognitive powers under investigation are internally accessible and therefore finitely surveyable in principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A10","text":"Transcendental philosophy admits no empirical element into its constitutive concepts or principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A11","text":"Practical morality cannot belong to transcendental philosophy because its construction necessarily incorporates empirical motives and feelings.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A12","text":"Human knowledge has two irreducible operative sources: sensibility gives objects, and understanding thinks them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A13","text":"A priori conditions of sensibility must be examined before a priori conditions of understanding because givenness precedes thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:A14","text":"The ultimate common root of sensibility and understanding is unknown.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:NX1","text":"Critique is not a doctrine that directly produces an enlarged body of pure knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:NX2","text":"Critique is not yet an organon or the completed system of pure reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:NX3","text":"Transcendental inquiry is not empirical psychology, natural science, or inquiry into external objects as such.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:NX4","text":"Transcendental philosophy is not identical with the Critique because it requires a complete analysis of all a priori knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:NX5","text":"The critique does not undertake a complete analysis and deduction of every a priori concept.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:NX6","text":"The critique is not a critique of philosophical books, traditions, or rival systems.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:NX7","text":"Pure speculative philosophy excludes practical principles insofar as they require empirically grounded motives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:NX8","text":"Sensibility is not reducible to understanding, and understanding is not reducible to sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:D1","text":"The primary achievement of critique is purification and error-prevention rather than doctrinal expansion.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A4","CH_B337_VIDP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:D2","text":"Critique functions as a touchstone for distinguishing valid from invalid claims to a priori knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A5","CH_B337_VIDP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:D3","text":"The critique can serve as a propaedeutic to a future organon and, if no organon is possible, as a canon regulating reason’s legitimate use.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A3","CH_B337_VIDP:A4","CH_B337_VIDP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:D4","text":"A complete inventory of radical a priori concepts is required even where their exhaustive analytic derivation is deferred.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A7","CH_B337_VIDP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:D5","text":"The division of transcendental philosophy must be governed by purity conditions rather than by the general division of human interests.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A10","CH_B337_VIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:D6","text":"The doctrine of elements begins with sensibility because cognition cannot think an object before an object is given.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A12","CH_B337_VIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:SD1","text":"The project converts metaphysical construction into prior jurisdictional inquiry: reason must establish its authority before it legislates any system of knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A3","CH_B337_VIDP:A4","CH_B337_VIDP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:SD2","text":"The finitude of the field belongs not to objects but to the self-investigation of a priori cognitive faculties.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A6","CH_B337_VIDP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:SD3","text":"The system’s architectonic unity depends on selective incompleteness: it must enumerate all fundamental materials while refusing every analysis not required for synthetic a priori judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A7","CH_B337_VIDP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:SD4","text":"The separation of speculative purity from practical motivation makes transcendental philosophy a restricted science of conditions of cognition rather than a total philosophy of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A10","CH_B337_VIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:SD5","text":"The order of the science is generated by the dependency of thought upon givenness, not merely by a classificatory preference.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A12","CH_B337_VIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:DNX1","text":"No claim to a priori knowledge may be accepted merely because it appears in an inherited metaphysical doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:DNX2","text":"No completed science of pure reason may presume that reason has an unrestricted positive capacity for a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:DNX3","text":"No transcendental explanation may derive a priori validity from empirical motives, feelings, desires, or inclinations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:DNX4","text":"No account of cognition may begin from thought alone, because object-reference requires antecedent conditions of givenness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:DNX5","text":"No final grounding of the two cognitive sources is available within the inquiry, since their common root remains unknown.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:SNX1","text":"The system is organized around the unavailable assurance that pure reason can become a completed positive science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:SNX2","text":"The critique gains determinate authority only by withholding the doctrinal authority it is designed to prepare.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:SNX3","text":"The ultimate unity of cognition is presupposed as possible while remaining inaccessible as knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:SNX4","text":"The possibility of pure practical reason is displaced from the transcendental field because the system admits only cognition purified of empirical incentive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:FGS1","text":"Claim of a priori reason → uncertainty concerning its legitimate extension → critical examination of sources and limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:FGS2","text":"Critical examination → negative purification and validation → conditional preparation for organon or canon.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:FGS3","text":"Transcendental reframing of cognition → exclusion of empirical constituents → delimitation of pure speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:FGS4","text":"Requirement of architectonic system → enumeration of radical concepts → restricted analysis of synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:FGS5","text":"Dual-source cognition → object-givenness before object-thinking → sensibility as the first doctrine of elements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:AF1","text":"Undetermined scope of pure reason → critique rather than organon → negative validation of a priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:AF2","text":"Cognition of objects → inquiry into its a priori mode → transcendental standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:AF3","text":"Idea of complete transcendental philosophy → deliberate incompleteness of critique → concentration on synthetic a priori judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:AF4","text":"Purity requirement → exclusion of practical motivation → restriction to speculative reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:AF5","text":"Sense gives objects and understanding thinks them → priority of givenness → transcendental doctrine of sensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:ME1","text":"Metaphysical inquiry is reclassified from the production of doctrines about objects into the assessment of cognition’s conditions of legitimacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:ME2","text":"The distinction between critique, organon, canon, and system separates preparatory validation from positive scientific completion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:ME3","text":"The distinction between transcendental and empirical inquiry separates a priori conditions of cognition from psychological or material contents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:ME4","text":"Morality is reclassified as a priori yet non-transcendental for this science because practical construction cannot eliminate empirical incentive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:ME5","text":"The division between sensibility and understanding becomes a dependency structure in which givenness conditions thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text defines the proper scope, name, and division of a Critique of Pure Reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The text establishes a bounded science that tests a priori cognition by isolating its sources, purity conditions, and limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The text suspends metaphysical construction until the faculty that constructs metaphysics has been critically authorized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Objects enter human cognition only through a sequence in which sensibility gives what understanding subsequently thinks, while their ultimate common ground remains unknown.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the transition from the uncertain possibility of pure metaphysics to the necessity of critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:MRT2","text":"The system depends centrally on the distinction between negative critical validation and positive doctrinal construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:MRT3","text":"The account remains underdetermined concerning the criteria by which critique itself validates or invalidates particular a priori claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:MRT4","text":"The strongest structural nexus is the connection between transcendental purity, restricted scope, and architectonic preparation for a future system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:MRT5","text":"The system compresses a tension between claiming a complete enumeration of radical concepts and refusing the complete analysis that would secure that enumeration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:MRT6","text":"The system also leaves unresolved how sensibility and understanding can belong to one cognition when their common root is declared unknown.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VIDP:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument only if critique remains preliminary, negative, and internally directed rather than being converted into a completed transcendental doctrine.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VIDP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 07","title":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason.","context":"Chapter 07"}},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP","layer":"CH","title":"VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.","order":92,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"d1abfa849388ed395dc4f943fadbb9d1f2c65626bc7a6595df66063438943d19","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:AS1","text":"Metaphysics remains unstable because it has not isolated the condition under which synthetic à priori cognition is possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:AS2","text":"The universal question of pure reason is whether synthetic judgements à priori are possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:AS3","text":"Mathematics and pure natural science establish that some synthetic à priori cognition exists and therefore require transcendental explanation rather than sceptical dismissal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:AS4","text":"Metaphysics is unavoidable as a natural disposition of reason but unestablished as a science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:AS5","text":"Reason’s unavoidable contradictions concerning supersensible questions require critique to determine its legitimate powers and limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:AS6","text":"The argument moves from the fact of synthetic à priori cognition and metaphysical impulse to a critique that conditions any scientific metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:A1","text":"Synthetic judgements à priori require a distinct account of their possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:A2","text":"The possibility of metaphysics as a science depends on resolving the possibility of synthetic à priori cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:A3","text":"Pure mathematics contains synthetic à priori judgements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:A4","text":"Pure natural science contains synthetic à priori principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:A5","text":"The actual existence of mathematics and pure natural science proves that their corresponding forms of synthetic à priori cognition are possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:A6","text":"Human reason necessarily generates metaphysical questions independently of empirical interests.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:A7","text":"Reason encounters unavoidable contradictions when it dogmatically answers metaphysical questions beyond possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:A8","text":"Critique must determine whether reason can know its metaphysical objects and, if not, must establish determinate limits for its use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:A9","text":"Metaphysics proper aims at synthetic extension of à priori knowledge rather than mere analysis of concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:A10","text":"Reason can determine its transcendent employment only after understanding its power in relation to experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:NX1","text":"The universal problem is not exhausted by the causal principle examined by Hume.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:NX2","text":"The impossibility of one proposed synthetic à priori principle does not entail the impossibility of all pure science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:NX3","text":"Metaphysics as a natural disposition is not thereby metaphysics as an established science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:NX4","text":"Dogmatic metaphysical systems are not valid merely because they contain conceptual analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:NX5","text":"Analysis of concepts is not the synthetic justification required for metaphysics proper.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:NX6","text":"Metaphysical questions are not supplied by external objects or empirical experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:NX7","text":"Contradictory dogmatic assertion is not knowledge and terminates structurally in scepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:NX8","text":"The extension of reason beyond experience is not legitimate without a prior determination of its limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:D1","text":"Hume’s restriction of the problem to causality converts a universal transcendental question into a local sceptical conclusion and therefore cannot account for mathematics.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A1","CH_B337_VUPP:A3","CH_B337_VUPP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:D2","text":"Mathematics and pure natural science function as fixed facts from which critique must regress to their conditions of possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A3","CH_B337_VUPP:A4","CH_B337_VUPP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:D3","text":"Metaphysics requires a double investigation: the origin of its unavoidable questions and the legitimacy of their possible answers.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A6","CH_B337_VUPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:D4","text":"The natural inevitability of metaphysical inquiry makes its critical regulation necessary rather than optional.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A6","CH_B337_VUPP:A7","CH_B337_VUPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:D5","text":"Critique replaces the question of which dogmatic metaphysical doctrine is true with the prior question of whether metaphysical judgement is possible.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A2","CH_B337_VUPP:A7","CH_B337_VUPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:D6","text":"The distinction between analytic and synthetic judgement becomes architectonic because only synthetic cognition can extend knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A1","CH_B337_VUPP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:D7","text":"A science of reason can be finite in scope because its object is reason’s own powers and problems rather than an inexhaustible inventory of external objects.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A6","CH_B337_VUPP:A8","CH_B337_VUPP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:SD1","text":"Metaphysics is reconstituted from a doctrine of supersensible objects into a self-limiting investigation of the conditions and bounds of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A2","CH_B337_VUPP:A8","CH_B337_VUPP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:SD2","text":"The same reason that necessarily exceeds experience must become the tribunal that restricts its own transcendent claims.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A6","CH_B337_VUPP:A7","CH_B337_VUPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:SD3","text":"Scientific metaphysics can arise only through a conversion of contradiction from a failure of isolated systems into evidence concerning reason’s structure.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A2","CH_B337_VUPP:A7","CH_B337_VUPP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:SD4","text":"Established sciences supply positive evidence for the possibility of à priori synthesis, while metaphysical failure supplies negative evidence for the limits of its application.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A3","CH_B337_VUPP:A4","CH_B337_VUPP:A5","CH_B337_VUPP:A7","CH_B337_VUPP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:DNX1","text":"The necessity with which reason asks metaphysical questions is not evidence that their objects are knowable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:DNX2","text":"The existence of valid à priori cognition within mathematics and natural science does not authorize unrestricted à priori cognition beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:DNX3","text":"Conceptual containment is not equivalent to objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:DNX4","text":"Reason is not entitled to treat the absence of empirical contradiction as proof of transcendent knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:DNX5","text":"Metaphysics cannot retain scientific status by accumulating rival doctrines whose contradictions remain undecided.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:SNX1","text":"Reason cannot escape metaphysical questioning by sceptically abolishing metaphysics, because the demand for such questions arises from reason’s own nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:SNX2","text":"No direct passage is available from the subjective necessity of thinking beyond experience to objective knowledge of what lies beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:SNX3","text":"A final metaphysical doctrine is foreclosed until the conditions of possible judgement have been established prior to doctrinal construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Negative","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:FGS1","text":"Synthetic à priori cognition is isolated as the universal problem that unifies mathematics, natural science, and metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:FGS2","text":"The actuality of mathematics and pure natural science establishes the positive case requiring transcendental explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:FGS3","text":"The natural inevitability and contradictory history of metaphysics establish the negative case requiring critical limitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:FGS4","text":"Positive possibility plus negative contradiction yields a critique of reason’s powers, sources, scope, and limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:FGS5","text":"Critique of reason yields the condition under which metaphysics may either become a science or be conclusively delimited.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:AF1","text":"Metaphysical uncertainty → isolation of synthetic à priori judgement → correction of Hume’s restricted scepticism → confirmation through mathematics and pure natural science → recognition of metaphysics as natural disposition → contradiction of dogmatic reason → critique of powers and limits → conditional possibility of metaphysics as science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:AF2","text":"Inevitable metaphysical demand → unsuccessful dogmatic construction → sceptical conflict → self-examination of reason → regulated synthetic use or securely determined restriction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:ME1","text":"Metaphysics is divided into an unavoidable human disposition and a scientifically valid body of knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:ME2","text":"Mathematics and pure natural science are reclassified from merely successful sciences into evidentiary cases for transcendental conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:ME3","text":"Contradictions in metaphysics are reclassified from accidental disputes into diagnostic signs of uncriticized reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:ME4","text":"The central opposition becomes not empiricism versus rationalism, but legitimate synthetic à priori cognition versus illegitimate transcendent extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:ME5","text":"Limitation is reclassified from the defeat of reason into the enabling condition of its secure scientific use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:ALT1","text":"Surface — The chapter asks how synthetic judgements à priori are possible and whether metaphysics can become a science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Direct","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:ALT2","text":"Structural — The chapter distinguishes the proven possibility of pure cognition in mathematics and physics from the unresolved legitimacy of metaphysical cognition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The chapter displaces doctrinal metaphysics with critique as the necessary preliminary method for deciding all metaphysical claims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Human reason is presented as structurally compelled to pose questions exceeding experience, while its historical dogmatic answers repeatedly generate contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s transition from the universal problem of synthetic à priori judgement to critique as the condition of scientific metaphysics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:MRT2","text":"The system depends most strongly on the distinction between the fact of metaphysical questioning and the validity of metaphysical knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:MRT3","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the positive mechanism by which synthetic à priori judgement is possible, because that mechanism is deferred to critique.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"medium","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:MRT4","text":"The central structural tension is that reason must be authoritative enough to determine its own limits while being the source of the contradictions that make such determination necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0005","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:MRT5","text":"A second tension lies between the confirmed existence of synthetic à priori science and the unconfirmed extension of such cognition to metaphysical objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0002","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Tension","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_VUPP:MRT6","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if dogmatic metaphysics, natural metaphysical disposition, and critical metaphysics remain non-equivalent terms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0003","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0004","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"},{"id":"RAW_B337_VUPP:P0006","trace_type":"Composite","confidence":"high","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 06","title":"VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason.","context":"Chapter 06"}},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS","layer":"CH","title":"What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14","order":93,"parents":[],"revision":"v1","sha256":"882a54b38f053ecad5bce11e2baadbc9598aefb156b2996db9ef9dd0a6df72bc","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:AS1","text":"The problem is to distinguish the objective unity of consciousness from the merely subjective unity produced within empirical inner sense.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:AS2","text":"Objective unity is grounded in the transcendental unity of apperception, not in the empirical association of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:AS3","text":"The manifold of intuition becomes related to an object through its necessary relation to the “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:AS4","text":"Empirical consciousness registers coexistence or succession only under contingent empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:AS5","text":"Pure synthesis of the understanding is established as the a priori ground of all empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:AS6","text":"The argument moves from the distinction between subjective and objective unity to the restriction of objective validity to transcendental apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:A1","text":"The manifold given in intuition is united into a conception of the object through the transcendental unity of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:A2","text":"The unity produced by transcendental apperception is objective because it determines the manifold as belonging to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:A3","text":"Subjective unity of consciousness is a determination of internal sense in which the manifold is empirically given as united.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:A4","text":"Empirical consciousness of coexistence or succession depends on contingent empirical conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:A5","text":"Empirical unity generated through association relates only to a phenomenal world.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:A6","text":"The manifold in pure intuition is necessarily related to the “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:A7","text":"Pure synthesis of the understanding is a priori presupposed by every empirical synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:A8","text":"Transcendental unity of apperception possesses objective validity, whereas empirical unity possesses only subjective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:A9","text":"Empirical unity is derived from transcendental unity under determinate conditions in concreto.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:A10","text":"Individual associations between words and things lack necessary and universal validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:NX1","text":"Objective unity is not identical with the empirical unity of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:NX2","text":"Objective unity is not founded on the association of representations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:NX3","text":"Empirical consciousness is not necessary or universally valid.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:NX4","text":"The phenomenal world disclosed through empirical association is not the ground of objective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:NX5","text":"Pure intuition is not merely an empirical presentation of a manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:NX6","text":"The “I think” is not an optional addition to an already unified manifold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:NX7","text":"Individual connections between words and things are not objectively binding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:D1","text":"The unity of an object requires a synthesis that precedes empirical association.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A1","CH_B337_WOUS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:D2","text":"Since empirical coexistence and succession depend on circumstances, they cannot ground necessary unity.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A3","CH_B337_WOUS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:D3","text":"Empirical consciousness has only subjective validity because its unity is produced under variable conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A4","CH_B337_WOUS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:D4","text":"Pure intuition becomes objectively ordered only through its necessary relation to transcendental apperception.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A6","CH_B337_WOUS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:D5","text":"Every empirical synthesis presupposes a non-empirical synthesis of the understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A7","CH_B337_WOUS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:D6","text":"Divergent individual associations demonstrate the absence of universal objectivity at the empirical level.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A8","CH_B337_WOUS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:D7","text":"The distinction between objective and subjective unity is a distinction between necessary validity and contingent validity.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A2","CH_B337_WOUS:A4","CH_B337_WOUS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:SD1","text":"Objectivity is generated by the formal condition of synthesis rather than extracted from the empirical contents synthesized.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A1","CH_B337_WOUS:A5","CH_B337_WOUS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:SD2","text":"Empirical consciousness can count as a unified field only because a transcendental unity already structures the possibility of its associations.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A6","CH_B337_WOUS:A7","CH_B337_WOUS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:SD3","text":"The universal validity of object-relation is secured at the level of the subject’s synthesizing activity, not at the level of private experience.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A2","CH_B337_WOUS:A8","CH_B337_WOUS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:SD4","text":"The phenomenal world is intelligible as an ordered field only through a distinction between its contingent empirical presentation and its necessary formal synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A5","CH_B337_WOUS:A6","CH_B337_WOUS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:DNX1","text":"No empirical sequence or coexistence can by itself yield the necessity of objective unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:DNX2","text":"No accumulation of associations can convert subjective validity into universal validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:DNX3","text":"No object is available to consciousness independently of the synthesizing relation to the “I think.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:DNX4","text":"No empirical condition can serve as the ultimate ground of the synthesis through which empirical conditions become intelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:DNX5","text":"No private act of meaning-association can determine the universally valid conception of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:SNX1","text":"The possibility of an objectively unified world excludes a world constituted solely by contingent streams of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:SNX2","text":"The argument forecloses any account of objectivity that begins from already constituted objects rather than from the synthesis that constitutes their unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:SNX3","text":"The text leaves unavailable an unmediated passage from empirical givenness to necessary knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:SNX4","text":"The ultimate ground of objective unity cannot itself appear as an empirical item within the unity it makes possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:FGS1","text":"Manifold given in intuition → relation to the “I think” → pure synthesis of the understanding → conception of an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:FGS2","text":"Pure synthesis → a priori ground of empirical synthesis → association of representations → empirical unity of consciousness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:FGS3","text":"Empirical conditions → variable coexistence or succession → contingent phenomenal unity → subjective validity only.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:FGS4","text":"Transcendental unity → necessary synthesis → objective unity → necessary and universal validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:FGS5","text":"Divergent individual associations → failure of universal agreement at the empirical level → dependence of objectivity on transcendental rather than subjective unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:AF1","text":"Empirical manifold → distinction between subjective and objective unity → transcendental apperception → necessary synthesis → objective validity → limitation of empirical consciousness to subjective validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:ME1","text":"Unity is reclassified from an observed feature of consciousness into an activity of synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:ME2","text":"The phenomenal world is divided into an empirical layer of contingent presentation and a transcendental layer of necessary unification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:ME3","text":"Objectivity is detached from interpersonal or individual association and relocated in the universal form of apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:ME4","text":"The relation between pure and empirical cognition is organized as ground and derivation rather than as two independent kinds of unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:ME5","text":"The contrast between necessary validity and subjective contingency replaces the contrast between different empirical contents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:ALT1","text":"Surface — Consciousness contains a manifold that may be experienced as unified, successive, or coexistent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:ALT2","text":"Structural — Empirical unity arises through association under conditions of inner sense, while objective unity arises through necessary synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The criterion of objectivity is shifted from empirical agreement or association to the transcendental conditions of possible experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Time supplies the pure form in which the manifold is given, but temporal ordering becomes objectively valid only through its relation to apperceptive synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the distinction between transcendental and empirical unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:MRT2","text":"The system preserves the derivation of empirical synthesis from pure synthesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:MRT3","text":"The system preserves the restriction of objective validity to transcendental apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:MRT4","text":"The argument is internally coherent if objective validity means necessary validity of the synthesis relating representations to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:MRT5","text":"The relation between the “I think” and the object remains formally specified rather than empirically illustrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:MRT6","text":"The argument depends on the unelaborated transition from pure intuition to the necessary relation of its manifold to apperception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]},{"id":"CH_B337_WOUS:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement from contingent empirical unity to necessary transcendental unity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_B337_WOUS:P0001","trace_type":"Structural","confidence":"low","alignment_provenance":"frozen-grounding"}]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 33","title":"What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14","context":"Chapter 33"}},{"id":"CL_B337_01","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 01–03","order":94,"parents":["CH_B337_IDBP","CH_B337_IHIE","CH_B337_IPSN"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"5dd1fe33e109312e00dcd559d87634deecdc38f2cce9d5ebf308546172b63e9b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:AS-01","text":"Knowledge begins through sensory affection, but this temporal initiation cannot determine the source or validity of every element subsequently contained in cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AS-02","text":"Experience is reconstructed as a compound whose received material is organized by cognitive contributions that cannot be read directly from sensation.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AS-03","text":"Absolute independence from experience, rather than mere non-observation or chronological priority, becomes the governing criterion of a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AS-04","text":"Necessity and strict universality operationalize that criterion by distinguishing cognitive validity from empirical generalization.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AS-05","text":"The a priori expands from necessary judgments to irreducible conceptual conditions for representing objects, including space and substance.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AS-06","text":"The demonstrated reality of bounded a priori cognition becomes dangerous when mathematical success is generalized into a license for supersensible speculation.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AS-07","text":"Metaphysical error is located less in isolated false conclusions than in the unnoticed transition from analysis of given concepts to foreign substantive addition.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AS-08","text":"The cluster therefore converts the question of metaphysical truth into a prior critique of the origin, conditions, extent, and legitimacy of a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Sensory affection initiates cognition without exhausting the conditions that constitute experience or knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Experience is neither passive reception nor an autonomous ground; it is a mixed product of receptivity and cognitive synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] A priori status means absolute independence from experience, then acquires diagnostic expression through necessity and strict universality.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:SD3","CH_B337_IHIE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Empirical regularity yields comparative generality but cannot establish necessity, impossibility, or strict universality.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] The conditions that make experience certain and object-directed cannot themselves be derived wholly from contingent experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] A priori cognition includes both judgmental validity and irreducible conceptual or intuitional content.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] The purity of a priori cognition depends on the empirical status of its constituent concepts, not only on the modal status of its proposition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Legitimate cognition requires a distinction between formal clarification of what is already given and substantive extension beyond it.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] The scope of a priori cognition must be critically determined before it can authorize metaphysical construction beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:RMD-01","text":"Experience recurs first as the temporal occasion of cognition, then as a composite achievement, and finally as a conditioned domain whose certainty presupposes nonempirical rules.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A1","CH_B337_IDBP:ME2","CH_B337_IHIE:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:RMD-02","text":"The a priori recurs as independence from experience, mutates into a modal criterion of necessity and universality, and culminates as an object of critique concerning its legitimate extent.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:AS6","CH_B337_IHIE:AS3","CH_B337_IPSN:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:RMD-03","text":"Cognitive contribution begins as an initially concealed component of experience and expands into the condition of causal judgment, object-representation, and experiential certainty.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:D3","CH_B337_IHIE:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:RMD-04","text":"Purity is displaced from a simple opposition between empirical and nonempirical knowledge into a compositional test internal to a priori cognition itself.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:SD4","CH_B337_IDBP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:RMD-05","text":"Mathematics reverses valence: it first supplies evidence that a priori cognition is possible, then becomes the mechanism through which reason overextends that possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:D3","CH_B337_IPSN:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:CGD-01","text":"The examples of unsupported bodies, causal connection, space, and substance remain local demonstrations; their current-level function is to establish that a priori conditions operate across distinct cognitive registers.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:D5","CH_B337_IHIE:D6","CH_B337_IHIE:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:CGD-02","text":"The empirical/a priori distinction scales upward because it organizes the relation among genesis, validity, conceptual composition, and metaphysical jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:SD5","CH_B337_IPSN:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:CGD-03","text":"Necessity and strict universality bridge the initial source-analysis of knowledge and the later critique of speculative extension by functioning as both tests and limits.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:D2","CH_B337_IPSN:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:CGD-04","text":"The pure/impure distinction is locally classificatory but globally catalytic because it blocks the inference from a priori form to unrestricted epistemic purity.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:D7","CH_B337_IPSN:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:CGD-05","text":"The analytic/synthetic distinction supersedes mere source classification by identifying the transition at which valid a priori activity becomes illegitimate speculative enlargement.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:D5","CH_B337_IPSN:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:GNX-01","text":"The system rejects the reduction of knowledge to sensory reception, because reception alone cannot account for the synthesis and necessity present in experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:NX4","CH_B337_IDBP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GNX-02","text":"It rejects the conversion of repeated observation into necessity, since accumulation of cases remains compatible with contingency.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GNX-03","text":"It rejects the identification of absence of observation, absence of contradiction, or conceptual caution with genuine a priori warrant.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:DNX3","CH_B337_IPSN:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GNX-04","text":"It rejects any homogeneous account of the a priori that ignores the distinction among pure form, empirically mixed content, intuitive construction, and speculative extension.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:DNX6","CH_B337_IPSN:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:GNX-05","text":"It rejects metaphysical construction that treats the importance of supersensible questions as evidence that reason can legitimately solve them.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:D2","CH_B337_IPSN:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:DBN-01","text":"The system cannot admit that experience is the exhaustive source of cognition, because experience itself depends on rules not established by empirical induction.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:DNX1","CH_B337_IHIE:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:DBN-02","text":"The system cannot admit that objective necessity is psychological habituation, because association cannot generate necessary connection or strict universality.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:NX4","CH_B337_IHIE:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:DBN-03","text":"The system cannot admit that a priori validity automatically establishes objective reach, because formal clarification can simulate substantive discovery.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:DNX2","CH_B337_IPSN:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:DBN-04","text":"The system cannot admit a self-authorizing reason that determines its own unrestricted jurisdiction through the same unexamined procedures that generate speculative overreach.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:DNX7","CH_B337_IPSN:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:BSA-01","text":"The unavailable possibility structuring the cluster is a cognition that is wholly empirical yet necessary, objective, and self-grounding.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:SNX1","CH_B337_IHIE:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:BSA-02","text":"The system circles an impossible fusion in which metaphysical aspiration has substantive reach without intuition, experiential object, resistance, or prior validation.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:BSA-03","text":"The deepest exclusion is not metaphysics itself but uncritical metaphysics: construction before the critique of the cognitive powers that construct.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:SD6","CH_B337_IPSN:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:XD-01","text":"The distinction between temporal origin and epistemic source becomes the condition for explaining how experience can begin cognition while a priori rules condition its certainty.","refs":["CL_B337_01:GA-01","CL_B337_01:GA-05","CH_B337_IDBP:A5","CH_B337_IHIE:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:XD-02","text":"Necessity functions as a criterion of nonempirical origin only when it is separated from both empirical frequency and the merely formal coherence of conceptual analysis.","refs":["CL_B337_01:GA-03","CL_B337_01:GA-08","CH_B337_IHIE:NX3","CH_B337_IPSN:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:XD-03","text":"The pure/impure distinction anticipates the later critique of speculative reason by showing that a priori form can coexist with empirically derived content and therefore cannot alone guarantee unrestricted validity.","refs":["CL_B337_01:GA-07","CL_B337_01:GA-08","CH_B337_IDBP:SD4","CH_B337_IPSN:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:XD-04","text":"The same cognitive contribution that makes experience possible also creates the risk of concealed addition when reason operates without experiential resistance.","refs":["CL_B337_01:GA-02","CL_B337_01:GA-09","CH_B337_IDBP:SD2","CH_B337_IPSN:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:XD-05","text":"Critique emerges as a second-order discipline because neither sensory origin nor modal force alone determines whether a priori cognition has objective jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_01:GA-03","CL_B337_01:GA-09","CH_B337_IDBP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:BSD-01","text":"Experience is demoted from ultimate epistemic foundation to a field whose objectivity is enabled by conditions that cannot be read off from its empirical contents.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:ME4","CH_B337_IHIE:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:BSD-02","text":"The a priori is divided into a legitimate enabling function and an illegitimate expansive temptation; its power to condition experience becomes the source of its speculative misuse.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:BSD-03","text":"Metaphysical critique is structurally preventive rather than merely corrective: it intervenes at the production of claims before their conclusions harden into systems.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:SD2","CH_B337_IPSN:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:BSD-04","text":"Objectivity is recast as conditioned reach rather than unrestricted correspondence, because cognition must establish the limits under which its concepts can relate to objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:BSD-05","text":"The system produces a recursive epistemology in which reason must investigate the legitimacy of the powers through which it conducts that investigation.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:DNX7","CH_B337_IPSN:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:RLE-01","text":"Sensory affection initiates cognition; cognition synthesizes impressions; synthesized experience conceals the contribution that made it possible; analysis then returns to recover that concealed contribution.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:FGS1","CH_B337_IDBP:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:RLE-02","text":"Empirical regularity produces expectation; expectation is mistaken for necessity; necessity is reclassified as a priori; the classification exposes the insufficiency of empirical regularity.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:FGS1","CH_B337_IHIE:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:RLE-03","text":"Valid a priori success generates confidence; confidence removes the perceived need for limits; lack of resistance permits foreign additions; critique returns to test the initial authorization.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:FGS3","CH_B337_IPSN:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:RLE-04","text":"The conceptual attractor is conditioned legitimacy: every cognitive achievement is redirected toward the question of the conditions under which its validity holds.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:A6","CH_B337_IPSN:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:RLE-05","text":"The decisive escalation runs from classification of knowledge, through conditions of experience, to limitation of metaphysical construction.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:ME1","CH_B337_IHIE:ME4","CH_B337_IPSN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:FGS-B01","text":"Sensory affection activates cognition without supplying a complete account of the knowledge thereby initiated.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:FGS-B02","text":"Cognitive synthesis produces experience as a composite whose received and contributed elements must be analytically separated.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:D2","CH_B337_IDBP:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:FGS-B03","text":"Separation of sources yields the criterion of absolute experiential independence and the internal distinction between pure and impure a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:FGS5","CH_B337_IDBP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:FGS-B04","text":"Necessity, strict universality, and irreducible conceptual residue establish that a priori conditions govern both judgment and object-representation.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:FGS-B05","text":"Bounded a priori success is generalized beyond its conditions, allowing formal novelty and foreign conceptual additions to appear as material knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:FGS4","CH_B337_IPSN:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:FGS-B06","text":"Critical inquiry interrupts this escalation by determining the origin, principles, extent, and validity of a priori cognition before speculative construction proceeds.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:AFM-B01","text":"Sensory affection → cognitive synthesis → composite experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AFM-B02","text":"Composite experience → distinction between temporal beginning and source of cognitive elements.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AFM-B03","text":"Source distinction → absolute experiential independence → empirical/a priori differentiation.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AFM-B04","text":"A priori differentiation → necessity and strict universality → conditions of possible experience and object-representation.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:AF1","CH_B337_IHIE:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AFM-B05","text":"Bounded a priori cognition → mathematical analogy → unnoticed foreign additions in supersensible speculation.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:AFM-B06","text":"Speculative overreach → critique of cognitive jurisdiction → suspension of premature metaphysical construction.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:AF2","CH_B337_IPSN:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:MME-01","text":"Knowledge is remapped from a binary between experience and nonexperience into a layered structure of occasion, source, validity, composition, and jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:MME-02","text":"Universality is divided into empirical extensibility and strict necessity, eliminating the equation of induction with objective law.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:MME-03","text":"Causality is reclassified from associated succession to a necessary condition of judgment and experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:MME-04","text":"Object-conception is remapped from empirical aggregation to a structure containing irreducible spatial and substantial conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:MME-05","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from direct doctrine about supersensible objects into a prior inquiry into the possibility of its own cognitive claims.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The system distinguishes empirical cognition, a priori cognition, pure a priori cognition, and speculative metaphysical claims.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Experience depends on the interaction of receptivity, synthesis, necessary rules, and conceptual conditions of object-representation.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:ALT2","CH_B337_IHIE:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — Inquiry must decompose claims into sources and conditions before granting them epistemic authority or metaphysical reach.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:ALT3","CH_B337_IPSN:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Experience is temporally first for finite cognition, while its possibility depends on nonempirical conditions that are not temporally prior acquisitions.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:ALT4","CH_B337_IHIE:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The text performs critique by repeating the same operation at increasing scales: separate what is given from what is contributed, then test the warrant for extension.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:SD5","CH_B337_IPSN:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:FL-01","text":"Necessity is treated as an infallible diagnostic of a priori origin, yet the account does not fully formalize the relation between necessity, strict universality, and objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:FL-02","text":"The causal example risks circularity because the necessity used to establish causality may already be contained in the concept being analyzed.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:FL-03","text":"The system requires supersensible problems to remain significant while withholding the experiential objects and resistance that would validate answers to them.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:MP-01","text":"“A priori” mutates from a source-term into a modal test, then into a problem of jurisdiction whose legitimacy cannot be established by the original test alone.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:MP-02","text":"Mathematical cognition mutates from proof of cognitive power into evidence of reason’s propensity to mistake conditioned success for unrestricted authority.","refs":["CH_B337_IPSN:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:MP-03","text":"Subtractive analysis shifts from a method for isolating irreducible cognitive content into an unstable inference from inconceivability of removal to a priori status.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a field in which objects are accessible only through a joint machine of affection, synthesis, rules, and representational conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:SD1","CH_B337_IHIE:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject is neither passive recipient nor sovereign creator, but a finite operator whose cognitive powers condition experience while requiring limits on their use.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:DNX2","CH_B337_IPSN:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is divided between the chronological arrival of experience and the nonchronological enabling conditions that make experience determinately knowable.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_01:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s movement from experiential initiation to critical limitation of metaphysical extension.","refs":["CH_B337_IHIE:MRT1","CH_B337_IPSN:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where it links the composite constitution of experience to the claim that experience depends on nonempirical conditions of certainty.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:MRT3","CH_B337_IHIE:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_01:MRT-B03","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the positive mechanism of a priori cognition and the criteria separating valid synthetic extension from invalid speculative addition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 01–03","context":"I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge · II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”. · III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori”"}},{"id":"CL_B337_02","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 04–06","order":95,"parents":["CH_B337_IDBA","CH_B337_VATS","CH_B337_VUPP"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"5265f0fbba88d96672533b2304f041350c386e70b6889eef38cc3f94cad28648","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:AS-01","text":"The cluster converts the distinction between contained and noncontained predicates into the universal problem of how necessary nonidentity can be known.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:AS2","CH_B337_VUPP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AS-02","text":"Analytic explication and empirical association are separated as insufficient routes to universally necessary cognitive extension.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:DNX2","CH_B337_IDBA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AS-03","text":"Synthetic a priori judgment shifts from an ungrounded classificatory remainder into the principle-form required by theoretical science.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:AS4","CH_B337_VATS:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AS-04","text":"Mathematics and pure natural science function as actual cases that compel regression from established cognition to its conditions of possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A5","CH_B337_VUPP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AS-05","text":"Theoretical science is redefined as necessary cognition that enlarges knowledge, while logic is restricted to testing and deductive ordering.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:SD2","CH_B337_VATS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AS-06","text":"Metaphysics is split between an unavoidable demand of reason and an unverified claim to scientific knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:AS4","CH_B337_VUPP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AS-07","text":"Contradictory metaphysical construction becomes diagnostic evidence that reason requires critique before it may extend itself beyond experience.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:AS5","CH_B337_VUPP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AS-08","text":"The cluster closes conditionally: critique must either authorize regulated synthetic extension or establish the limits that foreclose it, while the positive mechanism remains deferred.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:FGS5","CH_B337_VUPP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Cognitive extension requires a predicate not contained in the subject concept; analysis can clarify such a concept but cannot supply its new predicate.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A4","CH_B337_IDBA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Experience can establish contingent conceptual connection but cannot supply the universality and necessity proper to a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A10","CH_B337_IDBA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Synthetic a priori judgment names necessary, universal, noncontained connection and becomes the common principle-form of theoretical reason.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A11","CH_B337_VATS:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Logical contradiction can test consistency and govern deduction but cannot generate the substantive content of synthetic cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A7","CH_B337_VATS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Mathematical synthesis requires intuition corresponding to concepts, establishing that discursive analysis alone cannot yield determinate necessary cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:AS3","CH_B337_VATS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] The actuality of mathematics and pure natural science is positive evidence that some synthetic a priori cognition is possible.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A3","CH_B337_VUPP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Metaphysics aims at a priori extension but has scientific standing only if its synthetic claims can be critically legitimized.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A8","CH_B337_VUPP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The possibility of synthetic a priori cognition must be determined before any metaphysical doctrine can claim validity.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A1","CH_B337_VUPP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Transcendent employment of reason is legitimate only after reason’s power has been determined in relation to possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A10","CH_B337_VUPP:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Reason must become the tribunal of its own powers because its uncriticized extension generates unavoidable contradiction.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:AS5","CH_B337_VUPP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:RMD-01","text":"Conceptual containment retains its identity as the criterion of analytic judgment but is displaced from a general model of knowledge into a boundary marking non-augmentative cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A3","CH_B337_VATS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RMD-02","text":"Synthesis recurs first as generic augmentation, then expands into the necessary principle-form organizing mathematics, pure natural science, and the problem of metaphysics.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A6","CH_B337_VUPP:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RMD-03","text":"Necessity reverses its presumed attachment to analyticity and becomes evidence that cognition may be synthetic rather than conceptually contained.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A7","CH_B337_VATS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RMD-04","text":"Contradiction mutates from an internal logical test of analytic validity into a negative diagnostic of dogmatic metaphysical overreach.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A4","CH_B337_VUPP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RMD-05","text":"Intuition emerges as a positive condition of mathematical synthesis but does not become a universal explanatory substitute for all a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A5","CH_B337_VATS:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RMD-06","text":"Experience shifts from a source of contingent synthesis to the boundary relative to which reason’s legitimate transcendent employment must be determined.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A9","CH_B337_VUPP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RMD-07","text":"Metaphysics changes function from an attempted enlargement of a priori cognition into an object of critical self-investigation.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A8","CH_B337_VUPP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RMD-08","text":"The unknown ground of synthetic a priori connection is absorbed into the critical program: it remains unprovided as doctrine but becomes the organizing demand for critique.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:SNX4","CH_B337_VUPP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:CGD-01","text":"Causal predication, arithmetic construction, geometrical magnitude, and physical laws remain local demonstrations rather than independent foundations of the cluster architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:D6","CH_B337_IDBA:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:CGD-02","text":"The mathematical requirement of intuition remains domain-specific, but its higher-order function scales upward as proof that formal analysis cannot be the sufficient source of substantive necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:D2","CH_B337_VATS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:CGD-03","text":"The relation between noncontainment, necessity, and universality scales upward as the shared problem connecting judgment theory, theoretical science, and critique.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A11","CH_B337_VUPP:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:CGD-04","text":"The separation of logical deduction from synthetic grounding bridges the classificatory distinction of judgments to the methodological reclassification of theoretical science.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:D1","CH_B337_VATS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:CGD-05","text":"Established mathematics and pure natural science catalyze the transition from merely classifying synthetic a priori cognition to regressing toward its transcendental conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A5","CH_B337_VUPP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:CGD-06","text":"The priority of doctrinal metaphysics is superseded by the prior investigation of whether metaphysical judgment is possible at all.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:D5","CH_B337_VUPP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:CGD-07","text":"Metaphysical contradiction is recoded from an accidental dispute between systems into structural evidence concerning reason’s unregulated powers.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:NX7","CH_B337_VUPP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:GNX-01","text":"The cluster excludes the reduction of cognitive extension to the clarification of what is already contained in a concept.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:NX1","CH_B337_IDBA:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GNX-02","text":"The cluster excludes empirical association as a ground of necessary and universal connection.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:NX6","CH_B337_VATS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GNX-03","text":"The cluster excludes the inference from logical noncontradiction or deductive derivability to substantive synthetic cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:NX1","CH_B337_VATS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GNX-04","text":"The cluster excludes the identification of necessity with analytic containment.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:NX3","CH_B337_VATS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GNX-05","text":"The cluster excludes the conversion of successful pure science into a blanket authorization for metaphysical claims beyond experience.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:DNX2","CH_B337_VUPP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:GNX-06","text":"The cluster excludes the equivalence of natural metaphysical demand, conceptual coherence, and scientifically valid metaphysical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:NX3","CH_B337_VUPP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:DBN-01","text":"The architecture fails if predicates not contained in concepts can nevertheless be generated by conceptual analysis alone.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:DNX1","CH_B337_VATS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:DBN-02","text":"The architecture fails if accumulated empirical regularity can become necessity merely by repetition or breadth of observation.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:DNX2","CH_B337_VATS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:DBN-03","text":"The architecture fails if formal consistency is treated as sufficient proof of objective or determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A4","CH_B337_VATS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:DBN-04","text":"The architecture fails if the actuality of valid scientific synthesis is treated as direct knowledge of supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:SD4","CH_B337_VUPP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:DBN-05","text":"The architecture fails if reason’s inevitable metaphysical questioning is taken as evidence that its objects are knowable.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A6","CH_B337_VUPP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:BSA-01","text":"No direct analytic or empirical passage is available to the objective ground of necessary noncontained connection.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:SNX1","CH_B337_IDBA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:BSA-02","text":"No direct passage is available from the subjective necessity of transcendent thought to objective knowledge beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:SNX1","CH_B337_VUPP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:BSA-03","text":"No final metaphysical doctrine can be admitted before the conditions and limits of possible judgment have been determined.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:D5","CH_B337_VUPP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:BSA-04","text":"The architecture circles an absent positive mediator of synthetic a priori cognition: this absence is not resolved within the cluster but organizes its critical trajectory.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:SNX3","CH_B337_VUPP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:XD-01","text":"The distinction between logical validity and cognitive source follows because synthetic extension cannot be converted into analytic content by a valid deduction.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GA-01","CL_B337_02:GA-04","CH_B337_IDBA:A6","CH_B337_VATS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:XD-02","text":"The actuality of mathematics and pure natural science converts the question of synthetic a priori cognition from a speculative possibility into a transcendental regression problem.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GA-02","CL_B337_02:GA-06","CL_B337_02:GA-08","CH_B337_IDBA:A12","CH_B337_VUPP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:XD-03","text":"Mathematical intuition blocks any attempt to treat logical noncontradiction as the universal productive mechanism of necessary cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GA-04","CL_B337_02:GA-05","CH_B337_VATS:AS3","CH_B337_VATS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:XD-04","text":"Scientific confirmation of synthetic a priori cognition does not validate metaphysical objects, because the form of a judgment and the scope of its legitimate application are distinct questions.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GA-06","CL_B337_02:GA-07","CL_B337_02:GA-09","CH_B337_VUPP:DNX2","CH_B337_VUPP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:XD-05","text":"Metaphysical contradiction becomes methodologically productive when it is treated as evidence for the need to examine reason rather than as evidence that reason has no legitimate a priori use.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GA-06","CL_B337_02:GA-10","CH_B337_VUPP:SD3","CH_B337_VUPP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:XD-06","text":"Limitation becomes a positive condition of secure theoretical use because exclusion of illegitimate transcendence protects rather than abolishes the possibility of a priori cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GA-02","CL_B337_02:GA-09","CH_B337_VUPP:NX8","CH_B337_VUPP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:XD-07","text":"The unknown ground of synthetic a priori connection becomes a regulated research demand rather than a license for inventing metaphysical doctrine.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GA-03","CL_B337_02:GA-08","CH_B337_IDBA:SNX4","CH_B337_VUPP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:XD-08","text":"Reason’s relation to experience must be determined internally by critique, since neither empirical accumulation nor uncriticized reason can establish the limits of transcendent judgment.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GA-02","CL_B337_02:GA-09","CL_B337_02:GA-10","CH_B337_VATS:DNX1","CH_B337_VUPP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:BSD-01","text":"The cluster transforms epistemology from a taxonomy of judgments into an inquiry into the conditions under which cognitive extension can have objective necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:ME1","CH_B337_VUPP:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:BSD-02","text":"Theoretical reason becomes a divided architecture in which productive synthetic capacity and critical self-limitation are mutually necessary functions.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A9","CH_B337_VUPP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:BSD-03","text":"Theoretical disciplines are differentiated by the mode in which necessary synthesis is established, rather than by the mere presence of demonstrative certainty.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:SD3","CH_B337_VATS:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:BSD-04","text":"The status of metaphysics becomes conditional rather than simply affirmative or sceptical: it is unavoidable as demand but undecided as knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:AS4","CH_B337_VUPP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:BSD-05","text":"Historical conflict among metaphysical systems becomes data about the structure and misuse of reason rather than a merely external failure of doctrinal agreement.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A7","CH_B337_VUPP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:BSD-06","text":"Necessary cognition is detached from temporal empirical origin while its legitimate scope is tethered to the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:FGS1","CH_B337_VUPP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:RLE-01","text":"Subject–predicate relation → noncontainment → demand for necessary connection → unknown condition → critique of the powers that could supply or limit that condition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:FGS1","CH_B337_VUPP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RLE-02","text":"Established science → regression to conditions of possibility → determination of legitimate synthesis → reclassification of established science as transcendental evidence.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:D2","CH_B337_VUPP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RLE-03","text":"Natural metaphysical demand → dogmatic answer → contradiction → sceptical conflict → critical self-examination → regulated extension or restriction.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:AF2","CH_B337_VUPP:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RLE-04","text":"Contradiction operates as a phase-change mechanism: it first marks logical exclusion, then exposes the insufficiency of logic, and finally diagnoses uncriticized metaphysical reason.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A7","CH_B337_VUPP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RLE-05","text":"Synthetic a priori cognition functions as the cluster’s conceptual attractor because it concentrates the problems of science, metaphysics, necessity, and critical limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:FGS1","CH_B337_VUPP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:RLE-06","text":"Metaphysics functions as an attractor of reason’s extension and a limit-case that repeatedly returns cognition to the prior examination of its own authority.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A6","CH_B337_VUPP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:FGS-B01","text":"Subject–predicate relation → conceptual containment or noncontainment → analytic explication or synthetic augmentation.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:FGS1","CH_B337_IDBA:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:FGS-B02","text":"Analytic containment plus contradiction yields clarified necessity, while experiential synthesis yields contingent extension and cannot ground universality.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:FGS2","CH_B337_IDBA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:FGS-B03","text":"Necessary noncontained connection produces the problem of synthetic a priori cognition because neither analysis nor experience can supply its sufficient ground.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:FGS5","CH_B337_IDBA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:FGS-B04","text":"Mathematics and pure natural science supply positive instances of synthetic a priori cognition, while their different conditions prevent a reduction to one merely logical mechanism.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A3","CH_B337_VATS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:FGS-B05","text":"Metaphysics supplies the negative instance: reason necessarily seeks a priori extension, but dogmatic extension beyond experience generates contradiction rather than science.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A9","CH_B337_VUPP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:FGS-B06","text":"Positive scientific possibility plus negative metaphysical contradiction yields critique as the machine that regulates synthetic use and determines the conditional future of metaphysics.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:FGS2","CH_B337_VUPP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:AFM-B01","text":"Predicate relation → containment / noncontainment → analytic explication / synthetic augmentation.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:AF1","CH_B337_IDBA:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AFM-B02","text":"Synthetic augmentation → contingency when experiential → unresolved necessity when a priori.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:FGS3","CH_B337_IDBA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AFM-B03","text":"Necessary synthesis → separation of logical deduction from synthetic grounding → refusal of analyticity as the model of all certainty.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:AF1","CH_B337_VATS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AFM-B04","text":"Mathematical intuition and pure physical law → actual domains of synthetic a priori cognition → evidence requiring transcendental explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:AF3","CH_B337_VUPP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AFM-B05","text":"Actual science → universal possibility-question → regression toward conditions of possible synthetic cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:AS2","CH_B337_VUPP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AFM-B06","text":"Inevitable metaphysical demand → dogmatic contradiction → critique of reason’s powers, sources, scope, and limits.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:FGS3","CH_B337_VUPP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:AFM-B07","text":"Critique → regulated synthetic extension or determinate restriction → conditional possibility of metaphysics as science.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:FGS5","CH_B337_VUPP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:MME-01","text":"The opposition between analytic and a priori cognition is dissolved, since a priori necessity may belong to synthetic judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:ME1","CH_B337_IDBA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MME-02","text":"Logic is repositioned from a productive source of scientific principles to a negative and connective condition for their proof.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A4","CH_B337_VATS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MME-03","text":"Mathematics and pure natural science are reclassified from successful bodies of knowledge into evidentiary cases for transcendental investigation.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:D2","CH_B337_VUPP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MME-04","text":"Metaphysics is divided into a natural disposition of reason and a body of knowledge whose scientific validity remains conditional.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:AS4","CH_B337_VUPP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MME-05","text":"Limitation is transformed from the defeat of reason into the enabling condition of its secure scientific employment.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:NX8","CH_B337_VUPP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MME-06","text":"The decisive opposition becomes legitimate synthetic a priori cognition versus illegitimate transcendent extension, not empiricism versus rationalism.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:ME4","CH_B337_VUPP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the cluster classifies judgments, identifies synthetic a priori principles in theoretical sciences, and asks whether metaphysics can become a science.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:ALT1","CH_B337_VUPP:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, the cluster differentiates conceptual containment, experiential association, logical deduction, intuition, and necessary a priori extension as non-interchangeable cognitive operations.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:ALT2","CH_B337_VATS:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the cluster replaces doctrinal proof with transcendental regression from valid cognition and critical determination of its scope.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:D2","CH_B337_VUPP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, necessary cognition cannot arise from temporal experience, while reason repeatedly produces questions that exceed possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:FGS1","CH_B337_VUPP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the cluster enacts critique by withholding precritical metaphysical doctrine and making the conditions of judgment its primary object.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:SNX4","CH_B337_VUPP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:FL-01","text":"The cluster requires a positive ground of synthetic a priori cognition while deliberately deferring a substantive account of that ground.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:SNX4","CH_B337_VUPP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:FL-02","text":"The cluster treats mathematics and pure natural science as confirmation of synthetic a priori cognition while denying that their success validates metaphysical cognition beyond experience.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A5","CH_B337_VUPP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:FL-03","text":"Mathematical intuition is given a positive condition of possibility, whereas the transition to pure physical and metaphysical synthesis lacks an equivalent account within the cluster.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:SD3","CH_B337_VATS:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:FL-04","text":"Reason must be authoritative enough to determine its own limits even though it is also the source of the contradictions requiring those limits.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:MRT4","CH_B337_VUPP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:FL-05","text":"Metaphysics is structurally unavoidable for reason but illegitimate when it converts that unavoidable demand directly into transcendent knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:SNX1","CH_B337_VUPP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MP-01","text":"Analysis mutates from the explication of contained predicates into a merely negative test and preparatory instrument for synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A4","CH_B337_VATS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MP-02","text":"Necessity mutates from a mark associated with analytic identity into the defining demand placed on synthetic a priori judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A7","CH_B337_IDBA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MP-03","text":"Contradiction mutates from a criterion of analytic validity into a sign that metaphysical reason has exceeded its legitimate employment.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:A4","CH_B337_VUPP:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MP-04","text":"Experience mutates from the medium of contingent conceptual connection into the limiting relation through which reason’s transcendent claims are judged.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:A9","CH_B337_VUPP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly figured as a field of relations whose necessity is neither visibly contained in concepts nor recoverable from observed association alone.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:DNX3","CH_B337_IDBA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is figured as reason: a self-extending power that produces metaphysical demands and must also determine the bounds of its own authority.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:A6","CH_B337_VUPP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is figured as the order of empirical occurrence from which necessity cannot arise, although possible experience remains the measure of legitimate cognitive scope.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:ALT4","CH_B337_VUPP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:HSI-04","text":"Intellectual struggle is figured as navigation between sterile analysis, contingent experience, productive synthesis, and illegitimate transcendence.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:NX8","CH_B337_VUPP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:HSI-05","text":"The complete machine is a feedback system in which successful sciences provide positive input, metaphysical contradiction provides negative feedback, and critique regulates future extension.","refs":["CH_B337_VUPP:SD4","CH_B337_VUPP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_02:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s movement from the analytic–synthetic distinction through theoretical science to critique as the condition of scientific metaphysics.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:MRT2","CH_B337_VUPP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the positive mechanism that makes synthetic a priori cognition possible and concerning the validity of particular metaphysical propositions.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:MRT5","CH_B337_VUPP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent as a conditional and critical system, not as a completed doctrine of metaphysical objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:MRT4","CH_B337_VUPP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MRT-B04","text":"Its strongest compression separates logical form, cognitive source, and legitimate scope without allowing any one of them to substitute for the others.","refs":["CH_B337_VATS:MRT7","CH_B337_VATS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_02:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction requires the sequence in which classification produces an insufficiency, actual science supplies a positive case, metaphysical contradiction supplies a negative case, and critique provides conditional closure.","refs":["CH_B337_IDBA:FGS5","CH_B337_VUPP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 04–06","context":"IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements. · V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles. · VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason."}},{"id":"CL_B337_03","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 07–09","order":96,"parents":["CH_B337_VIDP","CH_B337_IINT","CH_B337_2MET"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"787e1ef65e37327f5c9ca87a16008f8b7a9f31962739080cd1174b4d0bf42294","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:AS-B01","text":"The system begins from the incapacity to assume that pure reason already possesses a legitimate positive science, making critique a prior jurisdictional requirement.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AS-B02","text":"The inquiry redirects metaphysical attention from putative objects toward the a priori conditions under which objects can be given and thought.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AS-B03","text":"Human object-cognition is reconstructed as a mediated circuit in which intuition supplies givenness and thought can operate only through that sensible relation.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AS-B04","text":"The critical method becomes operational by isolating sensibility from understanding and pure intuition from empirical sensation.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AS-B05","text":"The analysis of external cognition establishes that spatial representation must precede the ordering of sensations as external and separate.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AS-B06","text":"Space is thereby determined as a pure intuition rather than as an empirical relation, a general concept, or a thing-like container.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AS-B07","text":"Geometry receives apodeictic force because its principles articulate the a priori form required for possible external appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AS-B08","text":"The system closes conditionally: critique gains authority by refusing the completed doctrinal authority whose possibility it prepares, while leaving cognition’s ultimate root ungrounded.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:SNX2","CH_B337_VIDP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Reason must establish the legitimacy and limits of its a priori claims before it can authorize a positive metaphysical science.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Human thought has objective significance only insofar as it ultimately relates to sensible intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The a priori is not confined to discursive thought but includes the formal organization through which sensibility receives phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The priority of object-givenness over object-thinking fixes sensibility as the first necessary domain of transcendental investigation.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Transcendental inquiry produces its objects of analysis through subtractive isolation rather than by collecting empirical materials or extending doctrine.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Space is the necessary a priori form through which external phenomena can be represented at all.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] The unity, infinity, and prior givenness of space make determinate spatial plurality derivative rather than fundamental.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A7","CH_B337_2MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Geometrical necessity is valid for possible appearances because it derives from pure spatial intuition, not from empirical induction or things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Architectonic completeness is pursued through a selective inventory of radical conditions rather than through exhaustive doctrinal completion.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The science can map the functional articulation of cognition without supplying the genetic ground of the faculties or their common root.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A14","CH_B337_2MET:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:RMD-01","text":"[Functional Recurrence] Critique recurs from a general examination of pure reason into the operative rule that positive a priori claims must be validated before use.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A5","CH_B337_VIDP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:RMD-02","text":"[Displacement] The a priori migrates from a general capacity of pure reason into the receptive form of sensibility and culminates in spatial intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A2","CH_B337_IINT:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Sensibility changes from a merely receptive source of givenness into a formally ordered field capable of structuring appearances a priori.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A12","CH_B337_IINT:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:RMD-04","text":"[Escalation] Form changes from one component of phenomenal composition into the necessary condition for external experience and geometrical necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:A9","CH_B337_2MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Space shifts from an ontological candidate among competing accounts into the condition that makes externality and spatial relations representable.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:AS1","CH_B337_2MET:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:RMD-06","text":"[Valence Reversal] Subjective origin ceases to imply private arbitrariness and becomes the ground of universal validity for appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:SD5","CH_B337_2MET:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:RMD-07","text":"[Absorption] The completed system of transcendental philosophy remains a regulative horizon, while critique is absorbed as its deliberately incomplete preliminary condition.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:NX4","CH_B337_VIDP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:RMD-08","text":"[Terminal Disappearance] Practical motivation is excluded after it has secured the purity boundary of speculative transcendental inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A11","CH_B337_VIDP:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:CGD-01","text":"The exclusion of practical motives remains locally necessary for defining speculative purity but does not organize the cluster’s positive account of spatial cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:CGD-02","text":"The redefinition of æsthetic from taste to sensibility is locally terminological, whereas the division between receptive and conceptual conditions scales into the cluster’s architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:SD5","CH_B337_IINT:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between critique, organon, canon, and completed system scales upward as the cluster’s governing separation between validation and doctrine.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:NX2","CH_B337_VIDP:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:CGD-04","text":"Double isolation bridges the jurisdictional demand for purity and the determination of space as a pure form of intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:FGS4","CH_B337_2MET:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:CGD-05","text":"The priority of sensibility is the catalytic transition through which the critique of pure reason becomes an analysis of the conditions of external objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:A13","CH_B337_IINT:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:CGD-06","text":"Competing ontological accounts of space are superseded by a transcendental account that treats spatiality as a condition of representation rather than an independently settled entity.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:FGS1","CH_B337_2MET:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:CGD-07","text":"The spatial proof recodes metaphysical exposition from a dispute over what space is in itself into a demonstration of what external experience presupposes.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:AS2","CH_B337_2MET:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:GNX-01","text":"The system rejects the assumption that critique is itself a completed doctrine capable of directly enlarging metaphysical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GNX-02","text":"The system rejects both empirical psychology and inquiry into objects independently of the conditions under which they can be given and thought.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GNX-03","text":"The system excludes a purely conceptual account of human object-cognition because thought alone cannot furnish givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GNX-04","text":"The system excludes the empirical derivation of space because external experience already presupposes spatial representation.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GNX-05","text":"The system excludes the attribution of spatiality to things considered independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:GNX-06","text":"The system excludes the grounding of geometrical necessity in induction, abstraction, or accumulated comparison.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:DBN-01","text":"No completed science of pure reason may assume unrestricted positive a priori power before that power has been critically delimited.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:DBN-02","text":"No account of human knowledge is admissible if it bypasses the circuit of affection, sensibility, intuition, and thought.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:DBN-03","text":"No pre-spatially articulated external world can function as the source of spatial representation.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:DBN-04","text":"No final genetic account can be given of the unity between sensibility and understanding from within the analysis that distinguishes them.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:DBN-05","text":"No architectonic completion can eliminate the unavailable assurance that pure reason can become a fully positive science.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:BSA-01","text":"No standpoint outside the conditions of sensibility is available from which appearances can be compared with things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:BSA-02","text":"The object independently of its sensible mode of givenness is unavailable as a foundation for human knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:BSA-03","text":"A priori necessity cannot be grounded either in empirical recurrence or in unrestricted predicates of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:DNX5","CH_B337_2MET:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:BSA-04","text":"The system circles an inaccessible unity: cognition must function as one system even though the common source of its heterogeneous faculties remains unknown.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:SNX3","CH_B337_VIDP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:XD-01","text":"The critical restriction of inquiry becomes a productive method only when it is enacted as the double isolation that extracts pure intuition from conceptual and sensory admixture.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-01","CL_B337_03:GA-05","CH_B337_VIDP:AS4","CH_B337_IINT:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:XD-02","text":"Because object-givenness precedes thought and spatial representation precedes external ordering, space is a condition of objective externality rather than an attribute inferred from objects.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-02","CL_B337_03:GA-04","CL_B337_03:GA-06","CH_B337_VIDP:A13","CH_B337_2MET:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:XD-03","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as universally valid phenomenal form rather than as access to spatial things independently of the subject’s sensibility.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-03","CL_B337_03:GA-06","CL_B337_03:GA-08","CH_B337_IINT:SD2","CH_B337_2MET:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:XD-04","text":"Geometry becomes possible as a priori cognition because the form isolated from empirical intuition is identified with a pure spatial intuition rather than a concept.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-05","CL_B337_03:GA-06","CL_B337_03:GA-08","CH_B337_IINT:D6","CH_B337_2MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:XD-05","text":"Spatial multiplicity is reclassified as a derivative distribution of limits within one prior field, so external plurality never supplies the original basis of space.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-07","CH_B337_2MET:A7","CH_B337_2MET:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:XD-06","text":"The faculty-architecture supplies conditions of validity without supplying their ultimate origin, preventing transcendental explanation from becoming a genetic ontology of the subject.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-10","CH_B337_VIDP:A14","CH_B337_2MET:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:BSD-01","text":"A priori validity becomes domain-relative necessity: it governs possible appearances without licensing claims about things as they are independently of appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:BSD-02","text":"Universal objectivity is generated by universally shared conditions of sensibility, not by a bypass of subjective constitution.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:SD4","CH_B337_2MET:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:BSD-03","text":"Geometry becomes the model case of legitimate pure cognition because it exhibits necessity produced by form while remaining bounded to phenomenal application.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:SD2","CH_B337_VIDP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:BSD-04","text":"Critical negativity is converted into positive epistemic productivity because exclusion of illegitimate origins supplies the proof of a legitimate formal source.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:D1","CH_B337_VIDP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:BSD-05","text":"Architectonic aspiration remains valid only as a regulated process of partition, enumeration, and limitation rather than as possession of a final metaphysical totality.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:SD3","CH_B337_VIDP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:RLE-01","text":"The jurisdictional loop runs from the uncertain authority of pure reason through critique to a limited validation that authorizes only regulated further inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:FGS1","CH_B337_VIDP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:RLE-02","text":"The abstraction loop begins with empirical object-cognition, removes sensory and conceptual determinations, isolates formal conditions, and returns to explain the possibility of the empirical cognition from which it began.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:FGS2","CH_B337_IINT:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:RLE-03","text":"The spatial loop converts one prior space into determinate spatial plurality through limitation, then treats that plurality as evidence of the unity from which it derives.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A7","CH_B337_2MET:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:RLE-04","text":"The ontological escalation path transforms alternatives concerning the being of space into a transcendental proof of the conditions under which geometry can possess necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:FGS1","CH_B337_2MET:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:RLE-05","text":"The inaccessible common root functions as a terminal attractor: each successful articulation of cognitive structure returns to the limit against which further genetic explanation is barred.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:DNX5","CH_B337_2MET:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:FGS-B01","text":"An unverified claim to pure a priori cognition generates the need for critique as a prior examination of authority and scope.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:FGS-B02","text":"Critique redirects inquiry from objects independently considered to the conditions under which objects can be given and thought.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:FGS-B03","text":"The heterogeneous structure of cognition divides the field into receptive givenness and conceptual determination, while subordinating thought to intuition for object-reference.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:AS4","CH_B337_IINT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:FGS-B04","text":"Subtractive isolation removes empirical sensation and conceptual determination to identify the pure formal residue of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:FGS4","CH_B337_IINT:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:FGS-B05","text":"The antecedence of spatial representation identifies space as the necessary, singular, and infinite form of external appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A3","CH_B337_2MET:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:FGS-B06","text":"Spatial intuition grounds apodeictic geometry while the restriction to appearances prevents that certainty from expanding into knowledge of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:SD2","CH_B337_2MET:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:AFM-B01","text":"Undetermined authority of pure reason → critique as a test of sources, limits, and validity.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AFM-B02","text":"Critical jurisdiction → transcendental reframing of cognition as the a priori mode of object-relation.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AFM-B03","text":"Object-relation → affection, sensibility, intuition, and the distinction between receptive givenness and conceptual thought.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AFM-B04","text":"Empirical intuition → decomposition into sensory matter and a priori form → double abstraction of pure intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:AF3","CH_B337_IINT:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AFM-B05","text":"Pure intuition → proof that external ordering presupposes spatial representation rather than producing it.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:AFM-B06","text":"One infinite spatial intuition → derivative determinate spaces → apodeictic geometry valid for appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:FGS5","CH_B337_2MET:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:MME-01","text":"Metaphysics is reclassified from doctrinal construction about objects into jurisdictional assessment of the conditions of legitimate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MME-02","text":"The a priori is relocated from conceptual thought alone into the formal operation of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MME-03","text":"Objectivity is reorganized as universal phenomenal validity grounded in subject-dependent form rather than as independence from cognitive conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MME-04","text":"Spatial plurality is redrawn as regional limitation within one all-encompassing intuitive field.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MME-05","text":"Geometry is reclassified from empirical generalization or conceptual deduction into the articulation of pure intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MME-06","text":"Spatial predicates are restricted to phenomena, dissolving the inference from the necessity of spatial experience to the spatiality of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The system defines critique, sensibility, intuition, space, and geometry as distinct but ordered objects of inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:ALT1","CH_B337_2MET:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Cognition operates as a dependency system in which affection supplies material, sensibility gives form, intuition presents, and understanding determines.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — The method suspends direct metaphysical assertion and uses isolation, subtraction, and boundary-setting to derive conditions of validity.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Space structures external appearance, time remains its paired form for internal determination, and neither license access to things independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The system performs critique by making its own architecture depend on selective incompleteness, ordered division, and withheld final grounding.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:FL-01","text":"The demand for architectonic completeness conflicts with the critique’s deliberate restriction to what is necessary for assessing synthetic a priori cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:FL-02","text":"The object is said to affect the mind, yet phenomenal form is supplied a priori by the mind, leaving the coordination of affection and form functionally rather than genetically explained.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:FL-03","text":"Intuition requires an object to be given, while pure intuition is identified through abstraction from any actual sensory object.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:FL-04","text":"Space is subjective in origin but universal in geometrical validity, requiring a distinction between transcendental subjectivity and private arbitrariness that is asserted functionally rather than derived genetically.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MP-01","text":"Critique mutates from a negative restriction on metaphysical construction into a propaedeutic capable of preparing an organon or regulating reason through a canon.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MP-02","text":"The a priori mutates from a property attributed generally to pure reason into the formal structure of sensibility that orders empirical appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MP-03","text":"Space mutates from an ontological problem concerning independent reality or relations among things into the transcendental form of external intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:AS1","CH_B337_2MET:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MP-04","text":"Unity mutates from the inaccessible common root of cognition into the demonstrable unity of one all-encompassing spatial intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_VIDP:SNX3","CH_B337_2MET:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears for human cognition as a conditioned interface in which received affection becomes objective appearance only through a priori formal organization.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:SD2","CH_B337_IINT:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite two-source site: receptive enough to be affected, formally structured enough to order appearance, and unable to cognize objects through thought alone.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:AS4","CH_B337_IINT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:HSI-03","text":"Externality is imaged as a single infinite field in which determinate places and objects emerge only as bounded occupancies or limitations.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:A7","CH_B337_2MET:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:HSI-04","text":"Critical navigation remains possible as the disciplined identification of conditions and limits, but metaphysical escape from those conditions remains unavailable.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:MRT5","CH_B337_2MET:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_03:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the uncertain authority of pure reason to the bounded validity of spatial and geometrical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MRT-B02","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the genetic basis of the common root of sensibility and understanding and concerning why space exhausts the relevant form of external sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:MRT3","CH_B337_VIDP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where the antecedence of givenness, the isolation of pure form, and the proof of spatial necessity are connected as one dependency chain.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:MRT3","CH_B337_2MET:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if transcendental validity for appearances is not converted into knowledge of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_2MET:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_03:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when critique remains preliminary and restrictive while its exclusions are allowed to generate the positive determination of pure spatial intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_IINT:MRT6","CH_B337_VIDP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 07–09","context":"VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason. · § I. Introductory. · § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception."}},{"id":"CL_B337_04","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 10–12","order":97,"parents":["CH_B337_3TEC","CH_B337_4CFC","CH_B337_5MET"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"5332e18cc7e0701d1e4e20106a289ab0c0250b1dc1f6176da501bed26904715d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:BAS-01","text":"The cluster determines how synthetic a priori cognition of spatial and temporal order is possible without deriving its necessity from empirical objects or events.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:AS1","CH_B337_5MET:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BAS-02","text":"Geometry and temporal judgment jointly require forms of intuition because conceptual analysis cannot generate synthetic extension or immediate temporal order.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:AS4","CH_B337_5MET:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BAS-03","text":"Space is reconstructed as the formal condition of external receptivity, while time is reconstructed as the universal condition of sensuous appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A12","CH_B337_5MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BAS-04","text":"The subjective origin of spatial and temporal form does not abolish objectivity; it relocates objectivity within the universally shared conditions of possible human experience.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SD1","CH_B337_5MET:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BAS-05","text":"Spatial cognition is restricted to externally intuited phenomena, and temporal cognition is restricted to phenomenal reality, thereby preventing either form from becoming knowledge of things independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A10","CH_B337_5MET:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BAS-06","text":"The system proceeds through recursive exclusion: empirical derivation fails to explain necessity, conceptual derivation fails to explain synthesis, and transcendental realism fails to explain the subject’s constitutive role.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:DNX2","CH_B337_5MET:NX4","CH_B337_4CFC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BAS-07","text":"Space and time become distinct but coordinated modes through which phenomena are externally ordered and temporally related before empirical cognition can begin.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:D5","CH_B337_5MET:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BAS-08","text":"The cluster closes by preserving empirical validity while suspending positive cognition of the thing in itself and of any non-human form of intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SD5","CH_B337_4CFC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Synthetic a priori cognition requires an intuitive ground whenever it extends beyond conceptual containment.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A6","CH_B337_5MET:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Apodeictic necessity cannot originate in experience because experience presupposes the forms that make its ordered contents representable.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:D2","CH_B337_5MET:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Pure intuition functions as spatial externality in one register and as temporal ordering in another, without becoming an empirical object in either case.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A12","CH_B337_5MET:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] A form that precedes particular appearances belongs to the subject’s receptivity rather than to objects considered independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A10","CH_B337_4CFC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Objective validity is conditional universality: forms supplied by sensibility are universally binding for the domain of possible human experience.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A8","CH_B337_4CFC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Phenomenal objectivity is produced through transcendental ideality rather than opposed to it, because the same forms that limit knowledge make empirical knowledge possible.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:D4","CH_B337_5MET:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Spatial and temporal plurality are derivative determinations: external objects are spatially ordered through one form, while distinct times are limitations within one temporal intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A6","CH_B337_5MET:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] The thing in itself remains a necessary limiting correlate of representation but cannot be positively known through spatial or temporal form.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A13","CH_B337_4CFC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Transcendental explanation is adequate only when it generates the possibility and necessity of the cognition it claims to ground.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A13","CH_B337_5MET:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:RMD-01","text":"Intuition persists as the non-conceptual ground of synthetic cognition, but its function mutates from grounding geometry to grounding the universal conditions of sensuous appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:D1","CH_B337_5MET:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:RMD-02","text":"A priority recurs without reversal: in spatial cognition it precedes external objects, while in temporal cognition it precedes coexistence and succession.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:D3","CH_B337_5MET:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:RMD-03","text":"Subjectivity changes function from the location of external receptivity to the limiting condition that distinguishes phenomenal validity from noumenal claims.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:D4","CH_B337_4CFC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:RMD-04","text":"Necessity migrates from geometrical proof to temporal axioms, becoming the shared sign that a cognition arises from form rather than empirical regularity.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A5","CH_B337_5MET:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:RMD-05","text":"Space is not displaced by time; their difference is preserved as the distinction between the form of externality and the condition of all sensuous ordering.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A12","CH_B337_5MET:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:RMD-06","text":"The concept of objectivity reverses its realist valence by becoming validity for appearances rather than independence from the conditions of representation.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:NX10","CH_B337_4CFC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:RMD-07","text":"The thing in itself enters only after spatial form has secured empirical reality, where it becomes an exclusionary boundary rather than a positive object of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SD5","CH_B337_4CFC:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:CGD-01","text":"The detailed derivation of geometry from spatial intuition remains local machinery; the current-level structure retains only the general rule that synthetic necessity requires pure intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:D6","CH_B337_3TEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:CGD-02","text":"The singularity, unlimitedness, and part-whole structure of time remain temporally specific machinery, but they scale upward as evidence that intuition cannot be reduced to conceptual universality.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A12","CH_B337_5MET:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between sensations and formal intuition is locally directed against false analogies, but globally it protects the difference between subjective affection and transcendental form.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A12","CH_B337_4CFC:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:CGD-04","text":"The space–time relation is a current-level bridge because each form independently blocks empirical derivation while together establishing the architecture of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:D2","CH_B337_5MET:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:CGD-05","text":"Empirical reality and transcendental ideality become cluster-level coordinators because they resolve the apparent conflict between subjective origin and objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:AS5","CH_B337_4CFC:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:CGD-06","text":"Claims about the forms of intuition possessed by other thinking beings remain deliberately underdetermined and cannot be elevated into a universal anthropology of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SNX3","CH_B337_4CFC:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:GNX-01","text":"Neither space nor time is empirically abstracted from the relations it makes possible to represent.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:NX5","CH_B337_5MET:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GNX-02","text":"Neither space nor time is a merely discursive concept capable of generating its own synthetic content without intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:NX1","CH_B337_5MET:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GNX-03","text":"Spatial form cannot be attributed to things in themselves, and temporal necessity cannot be construed as knowledge of a self-subsisting temporal substance.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:NX1","CH_B337_5MET:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GNX-04","text":"External objectivity cannot be equated with a perspective-free spatial ontology.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:NX10","CH_B337_4CFC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GNX-05","text":"Phenomenal forms cannot be removed in order to obtain a more adequate cognition of the objects that appear through them.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:DNX4","CH_B337_5MET:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:GNX-06","text":"Complete conceptual capture is excluded because concepts require immediate intuitive givenness as their basis.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A14","CH_B337_5MET:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:DBN-01","text":"The cluster disqualifies any model in which experience is self-grounding, because both externality and temporal order are conditions rather than products of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:DNX2","CH_B337_5MET:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:DBN-02","text":"The cluster disqualifies an ontology in which forms of representation are simply properties copied from independently constituted objects.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:DNX1","CH_B337_4CFC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:DBN-03","text":"The cluster disqualifies an unqualified universalism that projects the forms of human sensibility onto every possible knower.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:NX5","CH_B337_4CFC:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:DBN-04","text":"The cluster disqualifies the conversion of a limiting correlate into a knowable object by spatialization, temporalization, or conceptual abstraction.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:DNX8","CH_B337_4CFC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:DBN-05","text":"The cluster disqualifies the separation of formal conditions from the possibility of object-representation, since such separation would erase the ground of synthetic necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:DNX6","CH_B337_5MET:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:BSA-01","text":"A cognition of reality from outside the conditions of sensibility is unavailable, because any object of possible cognition must already appear through those conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SNX6","CH_B337_5MET:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BSA-02","text":"The system is organized around an inaccessible remainder: the thing in itself must be thought as the correlate of appearance while remaining unavailable to positive determination.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SNX2","CH_B337_4CFC:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BSA-03","text":"A complete conceptual system of spatial or temporal reality is inadmissible because intuition remains irreducible to conceptual construction.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:SNX2","CH_B337_5MET:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BSA-04","text":"The possibility of a non-human form of intuition is neither affirmed nor refuted, but remains structurally unavailable to human transcendental cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:D8","CH_B337_4CFC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:XD-01","text":"The forms of space and time jointly establish that empirical order is not the source of necessity but the field in which prior formal conditions become objectively valid.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:SD3","CH_B337_5MET:SD3","CL_B337_04:GA-02","CL_B337_04:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:XD-02","text":"Externality and succession are not independent empirical relations; each presupposes a form through which objects can appear as outside one another and events can appear as ordered.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:D5","CH_B337_5MET:D1","CL_B337_04:GA-03","CL_B337_04:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:XD-03","text":"The subjective source of form produces a twofold restriction: forms are universally valid for human appearances but cannot determine the constitution of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SD3","CH_B337_5MET:DNX6","CL_B337_04:GA-05","CL_B337_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:XD-04","text":"The unity of temporal intuition generalizes the spatial argument’s anti-realist force by showing that even temporal plurality is derivative rather than independently given.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:DNX3","CH_B337_5MET:SD4","CL_B337_04:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:XD-05","text":"The failure of empiricism and conceptualism is not merely negative: their joint failure positively identifies intuition as the irreducible medium of synthetic cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:SNX3","CH_B337_5MET:SD5","CL_B337_04:GA-01","CL_B337_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:XD-06","text":"The distinction between phenomenon and thing in itself becomes a rule of method rather than a second ontology, prohibiting the conversion of representational conditions into properties of independent reality.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SD4","CH_B337_5MET:DNX6","CL_B337_04:GA-06","CL_B337_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:BSD-01","text":"The cluster transforms objectivity from independence from subjects into invariance across the formal conditions under which a human subject can encounter objects.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SD1","CH_B337_3TEC:SD5","CL_B337_04:GA-04","CL_B337_04:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BSD-02","text":"The cluster establishes a one-way dependency in which phenomena depend on spatial and temporal forms, while those forms do not depend on the existence of particular phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:D3","CH_B337_5MET:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BSD-03","text":"The cluster produces a critical realism of appearances: empirical science remains valid precisely because it is limited to the order of appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SD2","CH_B337_4CFC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BSD-04","text":"The cluster converts the limits of cognition into conditions of scientific necessity, since what cannot be known independently of sensibility is what enables universal knowledge within sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SNX6","CH_B337_5MET:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BSD-05","text":"The cluster makes form prior to content without treating form as a separable object, thereby defining transcendental conditions as enabling media rather than hidden entities.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:FGS3","CH_B337_5MET:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:BSD-06","text":"The cluster renders metaphysical claims legitimate only when they are reconstructed as claims about the conditions of possible representation.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:SD4","CH_B337_5MET:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:RLE-01","text":"Synthetic necessity generates a recursive loop: necessity requires pure intuition, pure intuition requires subjective form, and subjective form returns as the condition of objectively valid experience.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:RLE-02","text":"The principal escalation path runs from the inadequacy of empirical derivation to the reclassification of experience as an effect of prior formal conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:RLE-03","text":"The distinction between intuition and concept becomes a conceptual attractor because every failed attempt to derive necessity returns to the requirement for immediate givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A7","CH_B337_5MET:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:RLE-04","text":"Empirical reality and transcendental ideality form a stabilizing loop: the restriction of cognition to appearances preserves, rather than weakens, the validity of spatial and temporal judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:D4","CH_B337_4CFC:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:RLE-05","text":"The thing in itself is a negative attractor: it repeatedly prevents phenomenal conditions from being converted into ontological predicates of independent reality.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SD4","CH_B337_4CFC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:RLE-06","text":"Temporal unity intensifies the formal architecture by converting apparent plurality into limitation within an underlying intuition rather than multiplicity of independent times.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:D6","CH_B337_5MET:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:FGS-B01","text":"Synthetic extension and apodeictic necessity expose the insufficiency of both conceptual containment and empirical abstraction.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:FGS1","CH_B337_3TEC:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:FGS-B02","text":"The failure of those derivations requires pure intuition as the immediate ground of spatial and temporal determination.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:A7","CH_B337_5MET:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:FGS-B03","text":"Pure intuition is localized in sensibility because it must precede particular phenomena while remaining available for their representation.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:FGS3","CH_B337_5MET:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:FGS-B04","text":"Sensibility differentiates into external form and universal temporal form, producing spatial externality and temporal succession as conditions rather than empirical discoveries.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:FGS4","CH_B337_5MET:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:FGS-B05","text":"Shared formal conditions generate universally valid synthetic judgments for phenomena without licensing claims about things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:FGS5","CH_B337_4CFC:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:FGS-B06","text":"The restriction of validity to phenomena yields empirical reality, while the withdrawal of sensibility yields transcendental ideality and epistemic limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:FGS6","CH_B337_4CFC:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:FGS-B07","text":"The system closes by treating explanatory adequacy as generative success: an account is valid only if it renders necessary cognition possible.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:FGS6","CH_B337_3TEC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:AFM-B01","text":"Synthetic and necessary cognition of spatial and temporal relations reveals the failure of empirical and conceptual derivation.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:AF1","CH_B337_5MET:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:AFM-B02","text":"Failed derivation → demand for pure intuition as the source of synthetic extension and apodeictic order.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:AF2","CH_B337_5MET:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:AFM-B03","text":"Pure intuition → localization in the subject’s receptive form rather than in independently constituted objects.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:AFM-B04","text":"Receptive form → space as external form and time as the universal condition of sensuous appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:AF4","CH_B337_5MET:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:AFM-B05","text":"Formal conditioning → universal and objective validity for all objects that can appear within human experience.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:D2","CH_B337_4CFC:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:AFM-B06","text":"Phenomenal validity → separation of empirical reality from transcendental ideality.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:AF5","CH_B337_4CFC:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:AFM-B07","text":"Transcendental ideality → prohibition against treating spatial or temporal representation as cognition of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:AF6","CH_B337_5MET:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:MME-01","text":"Space and time are reclassified from encountered contents to formal conditions under which externality and succession become representable.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:ME1","CH_B337_5MET:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MME-02","text":"The opposition between realism and subjectivism collapses into a differentiated account of empirical reality and transcendental ideality.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:ME2","CH_B337_4CFC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MME-03","text":"Scientific necessity is relocated from observed regularity to the formal conditions that order all possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:ME4","CH_B337_5MET:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MME-04","text":"The object is redrawn as phenomenal presentation plus unknowable correlate rather than as a directly known independently spatial entity.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:ME4","CH_B337_4CFC:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MME-05","text":"Temporal multiplicity becomes intelligible as internal differentiation of one intuition rather than a collection of self-subsisting temporal units.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:ME4","CH_B337_5MET:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MME-06","text":"Transcendental inquiry becomes a method of testing explanatory systems by whether they generate the conditions of their own claimed cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:ME5","CH_B337_3TEC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — Geometry concerns space, temporal judgment concerns coexistence and succession, and both claim necessity beyond empirical observation.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:ALT1","CH_B337_5MET:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Spatial and temporal forms organize external and sensuous appearances before particular objects or events are empirically given.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:ALT2","CH_B337_5MET:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — The cluster shifts inquiry from the empirical origin of representations to the conditions that make experience and its necessary judgments possible.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:ALT3","CH_B337_5MET:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Phenomenal reality is constituted within spatial and temporal form, while things in themselves remain outside positive spatial and temporal cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:ALT4","CH_B337_5MET:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The argument performs its critical method by deriving the status of forms from the failure of competing derivations to explain necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:MRT6","CH_B337_5MET:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:FL-01","text":"Objective validity is attributed to forms whose origin lies wholly in subjective sensibility, requiring a distinction between subjective source and public validity that the cluster can state more fully than it can independently prove.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:MRT6","CH_B337_3TEC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:FL-02","text":"Time is both the condition of all phenomenal reality and representable without phenomena, leaving its relation to non-phenomenal reality structurally unelaborated.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:MRT6","CH_B337_5MET:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:FL-03","text":"The system requires the thing in itself as a limiting correlate while denying every positive route to determine that correlate.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SNX2","CH_B337_4CFC:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:FL-04","text":"The forms of human sensibility are universal within human experience, but the system cannot determine whether that universality extends to other possible knowers.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:MRT7","CH_B337_4CFC:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MP-01","text":"The concept of objectivity mutates from mind-independent existence into universally valid phenomenal determination.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:NX10","CH_B337_4CFC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MP-02","text":"The concept of reality mutates from a possible attribute of things in themselves into empirical validity under the conditions of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:A8","CH_B337_5MET:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MP-03","text":"The concept of unity mutates from conceptual generality into immediate intuitive wholeness, most explicitly in the account of one unlimited time.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:A9","CH_B337_5MET:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a conditioned field rather than a self-disclosing inventory of independent objects: appearance is generated through forms that do not themselves appear as empirical contents.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:SD2","CH_B337_5MET:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:HSI-02","text":"The subject is neither an arbitrary inventor nor a passive receiver, but the site of formal receptivity through which objects can become externally and temporally determinate.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:DNX4","CH_B337_4CFC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:HSI-03","text":"Time is the singular medium in which phenomenal reality can differentiate into determinate succession without becoming a plurality of independent temporal entities.","refs":["CH_B337_5MET:D7","CH_B337_5MET:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:HSI-04","text":"Space is the formal architecture of externality through which objects can stand outside one another without space becoming a property of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:D5","CH_B337_4CFC:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:HSI-05","text":"Critical struggle consists in maintaining the boundary where conditions of representation enable empirical knowledge but forbid ontological overreach.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:SD4","CH_B337_5MET:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_04:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s central movement from synthetic necessity through pure intuition to phenomenally valid but transcendentally limited cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the positive nature of the thing in itself and the possible forms of intuition of non-human knowers.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:MRT5","CH_B337_4CFC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent if subjective origin, empirical objectivity, and transcendental limitation are maintained as distinct but mutually necessary registers.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:MRT4","CH_B337_4CFC:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MRT-B04","text":"The strongest compression point is the transition from the failure of empirical and conceptual derivation to the localization of pure intuition in sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_3TEC:MRT6","CH_B337_5MET:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_04:MRT-B05","text":"The system cannot be fully reduced to isolated axioms because its force depends on recursive return: each restriction on knowledge simultaneously functions as a condition of possible empirical validity.","refs":["CH_B337_4CFC:D5","CH_B337_5MET:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 10–12","context":"§ 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space. · § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions. · § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception."}},{"id":"CL_B337_05","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 13–15","order":98,"parents":["CH_B337_6TEC","CH_B337_7CAC","CH_B337_8ELU"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"5bf647490184eb26efe5db53e96d3edb97f327efe82d4bae021c47c3d24cba9b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:AS-B01","text":"The system resolves the problem of alteration by showing that opposed determinations can belong to one object only through successive temporal ordering rather than simultaneous predication.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:AS1","CH_B337_8ELU:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AS-B02","text":"Time is relocated from an alleged object, property, or empirical abstraction into the formal condition through which change can be represented at all.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:AS2","CH_B337_8ELU:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AS-B03","text":"The ground of temporal ordering is an a priori intuition functioning as the form of internal sense.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A4","CH_B337_7CAC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AS-B04","text":"Internal sense distributes temporal order across all appearances, immediately for inner states and mediately for outer appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AS-B05","text":"The system secures time’s empirical reality within experience while denying its absolute reality independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SD1","CH_B337_8ELU:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AS-B06","text":"Pure intuition grounds synthetic a priori cognition and mathematics, but its objective validity is restricted to sensuous phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A8","CH_B337_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AS-B07","text":"Motion is intelligible through time as a condition of possible cognition, yet motion and change remain empirical determinations requiring perceptually given objects.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AS-B08","text":"The closure is transcendental-diagnostic: valid knowledge is preserved by delimiting the phenomenal field rather than by supplying an ontology of time or things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:AS4","CH_B337_7CAC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Time is an a priori intuitive form of internal sense rather than an independently subsisting entity or property of things.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A4","CH_B337_7CAC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Change is intelligible only when opposed determinations are successively ordered so that one object is not assigned contradictions at one time.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A1","CH_B337_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Temporal form conditions all phenomena because every representation belongs to an internal state, though external phenomena receive this condition mediately.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A5","CH_B337_7CAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Time is empirically real for objects of possible experience while transcendentally ideal relative to things considered independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A8","CH_B337_8ELU:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Subjectivity is not opposed to objectivity but supplies the necessary form through which phenomenal objectivity becomes possible.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SD2","CH_B337_7CAC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Space and time as pure forms of intuition ground synthetic a priori cognition without extending that cognition beyond sensuous appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Transcendental ideality avoids both the reification of spatial-temporal forms as self-subsisting containers and their reduction to empirical abstractions.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A11","CH_B337_8ELU:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Temporal form is a necessary a priori condition for cognizing motion, while motion and change themselves are empirical determinations rather than pure data of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Identity through alteration is secured by temporal distribution of determinations, not by eliminating their opposition.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:D2","CH_B337_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Objectivity means necessary validity within the conditions of possible experience rather than access to absolute ontology.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:ALT3","CH_B337_7CAC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Things in themselves operate as a limiting boundary on temporal predication rather than as temporally knowable objects.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SNX2","CH_B337_8ELU:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] The inquiry shifts from asking what time is independently to identifying the conditions under which change, knowledge, and objectivity can appear.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A5","CH_B337_8ELU:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:RMD-01","text":"Time retains stable identity as an a priori intuition, but its function expands from ordering change to structuring internal sense and all phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A4","CH_B337_8ELU:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:RMD-02","text":"Change recurs first as the problem of compatible opposition and later as evidence of empirical temporal reality, while remaining unable to establish absolute temporal reality.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A1","CH_B337_7CAC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:RMD-03","text":"Motion reverses function from an instance whose intelligibility depends on time into a boundary case demonstrating that empirical movement is not itself a pure form.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A2","CH_B337_8ELU:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:RMD-04","text":"Subjectivity mutates from the internal location of temporal representation into the positive condition of phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:AS3","CH_B337_7CAC:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:RMD-05","text":"Objectivity is displaced from a supposed property of independently existing things into necessary validity for objects of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SD2","CH_B337_7CAC:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:RMD-06","text":"Pure intuition expands from temporal succession to the joint formal ground of spatial-temporal mathematics, while its validity remains phenomenally bounded.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A8","CH_B337_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:RMD-07","text":"The line analogy recurs only as a representational aid for succession and is prevented from converting time into spatial coexistence.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A12","CH_B337_7CAC:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:RMD-08","text":"Things in themselves persist as an exclusionary limit, but their role mutates from the denial of temporal predicates to an explicitly problematic aspect beyond determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A11","CH_B337_8ELU:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:CGD-01","text":"The logical problem of opposed determinations in one object remains local machinery until it is integrated into the wider distinction between phenomenal validity and absolute ontology.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A1","CH_B337_7CAC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:CGD-02","text":"The one-dimensional line remains a local representational schema and does not carry the cluster’s load-bearing account of time.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A12","CH_B337_7CAC:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:CGD-03","text":"Time’s status as the formal condition of all phenomena scales upward because it joins the analysis of change to the general conditions of experience and mathematics.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A5","CH_B337_8ELU:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:CGD-04","text":"Internal sense is the bridge mechanism through which an apparently subjective temporal form reaches outer phenomena without becoming an external property.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A6","CH_B337_7CAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:CGD-05","text":"The distinction between empirical reality and transcendental ideality catalyzes the transition from a theory of succession to a general account of phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:D6","CH_B337_7CAC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:CGD-06","text":"Motion is recoded from a candidate for synthetic a priori cognition into a test of the difference between formal conditions and empirical content.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:CGD-07","text":"The lower-level opposition between subjective and objective validity is superseded by the distinction between phenomenal objectivity and absolute reality.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:ME2","CH_B337_7CAC:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:GNX-01","text":"Time is excluded as an independently existing object, substance, or container.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GNX-02","text":"Temporal predicates are excluded from things in themselves because their validity originates only within sensuous experience.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:NX6","CH_B337_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GNX-03","text":"Space and time are excluded as abstractions derived from empirical relations because such derivation would destroy apodeictic necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:NX8","CH_B337_8ELU:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GNX-04","text":"The proposition that all things are in time is excluded unless “things” is restricted to objects of sensuous intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:NX5","CH_B337_7CAC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GNX-05","text":"Inner consciousness is excluded as privileged access to the self considered as a thing in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:NX1","CH_B337_7CAC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GNX-06","text":"Mathematical necessity is excluded from both absolute spatial-temporal realism and empiricist reduction.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A11","CH_B337_8ELU:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:GNX-07","text":"No transfer of the forms of sensibility beyond phenomenal conditions is admissible.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SNX2","CH_B337_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:DBN-01","text":"Experience cannot establish its own transcendental conditions as independently existing realities.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:NX1","CH_B337_8ELU:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:DBN-02","text":"A conception of objectivity purified of every condition of subjective access is disqualified because phenomenal objectivity depends on those conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SD2","CH_B337_7CAC:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:DBN-03","text":"Mathematical necessity cannot be preserved either by hypostatizing pure forms or by deriving them from empirical relations.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A11","CH_B337_8ELU:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:DBN-04","text":"An unconditioned standpoint outside the relation between intuition and appearance is unavailable to human knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SNX3","CH_B337_7CAC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:DBN-05","text":"The system collapses if formal temporal conditions and empirically perceived changes are treated as interchangeable.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:AS7","CH_B337_8ELU:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:BSA-01","text":"A subject-independent temporal order governing things in themselves is unavailable as an object of determinate human cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SNX1","CH_B337_7CAC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:BSA-02","text":"The absolute outside of temporal intuition cannot be positively represented as another temporal domain without reinstating the excluded form.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SNX4","CH_B337_7CAC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:BSA-03","text":"A non-sensible mode of intuition functions only as a limiting possibility and cannot provide an alternative human standpoint.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:SNX2","CH_B337_7CAC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:BSA-04","text":"The system cannot provide an unconditioned proof that subjective form yields universal objectivity; it can only establish their necessary linkage within experience.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:MRT5","CH_B337_7CAC:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:XD-01","text":"Identity through alteration is objectively valid only for objects temporally constituted in experience, not as a determination of things independently of sensibility.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-02","CL_B337_05:GA-04","CL_B337_05:GA-09","CH_B337_6TEC:A1","CH_B337_7CAC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:XD-02","text":"Temporal form enables a priori cognition concerning motion without converting actual motion into a pure a priori datum.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-01","CL_B337_05:GA-08","CH_B337_6TEC:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:XD-03","text":"The universality of time arises from its unavoidable role in representation, so its scope is phenomenal rather than ontological.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-03","CL_B337_05:GA-04","CL_B337_05:GA-05","CH_B337_7CAC:A5","CH_B337_8ELU:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:XD-04","text":"The phenomenal restriction of space and time is the mechanism that secures, rather than weakens, the necessity and objective applicability of mathematics.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-04","CL_B337_05:GA-06","CH_B337_8ELU:A8","CH_B337_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:XD-05","text":"Transcendental ideality forms a third position that rejects both absolute containers and empirical abstractions while preserving formal necessity.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-06","CL_B337_05:GA-07","CL_B337_05:GA-10","CH_B337_8ELU:A11","CH_B337_8ELU:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:XD-06","text":"Inner sense supplies temporal order without granting introspective access to a self as it is in itself.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-01","CL_B337_05:GA-05","CL_B337_05:GA-11","CH_B337_7CAC:A3","CH_B337_8ELU:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:BSD-01","text":"Reality is stratified into empirical objectivity and unavailable absolute ontology, so ideality concerns the status of forms rather than the unreality of appearances.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-04","CL_B337_05:GA-10","CL_B337_05:GA-11","CH_B337_7CAC:A8","CH_B337_8ELU:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:BSD-02","text":"Finite sensibility becomes the condition of universal phenomenal validity rather than a merely private psychological limitation.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-03","CL_B337_05:GA-05","CL_B337_05:GA-10","CH_B337_7CAC:SD2","CH_B337_7CAC:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:BSD-03","text":"Mathematical necessity and empirical certainty acquire their force from the same formal conditions that prohibit metaphysical extension beyond appearances.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-04","CL_B337_05:GA-06","CL_B337_05:GA-11","CH_B337_8ELU:A8","CH_B337_8ELU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:BSD-04","text":"No subject can occupy a standpoint outside the forms through which objects and the self are presented, so the limit of knowledge is internal to every act of knowing.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-05","CL_B337_05:GA-11","CH_B337_7CAC:SNX3","CH_B337_8ELU:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:BSD-05","text":"Alteration is neither a property read directly from independent being nor a mere conceptual relation; it is a formally conditioned phenomenal event requiring empirical instantiation.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-02","CL_B337_05:GA-03","CL_B337_05:GA-08","CH_B337_6TEC:A6","CH_B337_8ELU:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:RLE-01","text":"Alteration generates a recursive loop: opposed determinations require succession, succession requires time, and time in turn makes alteration intelligible.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:AS1","CH_B337_6TEC:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:RLE-02","text":"Internal sense generates a scope-expansion loop: temporal form orders representations, every representation is internal, and temporal order consequently extends across all phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A3","CH_B337_7CAC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:RLE-03","text":"Empirical reality and transcendental ideality form a stabilizing loop in which phenomenal validity is preserved precisely by refusing absolute ontological extension.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:D6","CH_B337_7CAC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:RLE-04","text":"Motion produces a boundary loop: it presupposes time for intelligibility, but its empirical givenness returns it to the domain that pure intuition conditions without exhausting.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:RLE-05","text":"The form of intuition is the dominant conceptual attractor because it transforms contradiction, subjectivity, mathematical necessity, and phenomenal objectivity into effects of one formal condition.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A4","CH_B337_8ELU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:FGS-B01","text":"Opposed determinations are attributed to one object and become incompatible if treated as simultaneous.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:AS1","CH_B337_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:FGS-B02","text":"Successive ordering is introduced as the condition under which opposition becomes compatible with identity through change.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:D2","CH_B337_6TEC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:FGS-B03","text":"The ordering condition is identified as time, understood as an a priori form of internal intuition rather than an object or property.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A4","CH_B337_8ELU:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:FGS-B04","text":"Internal sense distributes temporal form over all representations and thereby over all possible phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A6","CH_B337_7CAC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:FGS-B05","text":"Phenomenal universality is separated from absolute ontology through the distinction between empirical reality and transcendental ideality.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SD1","CH_B337_8ELU:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:FGS-B06","text":"Pure forms of intuition ground mathematics and empirical certainty while their extension beyond appearances is prohibited.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A8","CH_B337_8ELU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:FGS-B07","text":"Motion and change return as empirical determinations, fixing the boundary between the formal conditions of appearance and the given content of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:AS7","CH_B337_8ELU:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:AFM-B01","text":"The movement begins with opposed determinations attributed to one object and the demand for their non-contradictory compatibility.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:AS1","CH_B337_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AFM-B02","text":"The demand is redirected from conceptual analysis to temporal succession as the condition of intelligible change.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A5","CH_B337_6TEC:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AFM-B03","text":"Temporal succession is grounded in the a priori form of internal sense.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A4","CH_B337_7CAC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AFM-B04","text":"Internal form becomes universally phenomenal because outer appearances are represented through internal states.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A6","CH_B337_7CAC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AFM-B05","text":"Universal phenomenal validity is divided from absolute temporal reality through transcendental ideality.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:D6","CH_B337_7CAC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:AFM-B06","text":"The resulting formal field grounds mathematical necessity and empirical certainty while excluding transcendent application.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A8","CH_B337_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:MME-01","text":"The opposition between the reality and ideality of time is replaced by the distinction between empirical reality for appearances and ideality relative to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SD1","CH_B337_8ELU:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MME-02","text":"The opposition between subjective and objective validity is replaced by a model in which subjective form conditions phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SD2","CH_B337_7CAC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MME-03","text":"Inner and outer experience are reorganized into an asymmetric topology in which time is immediate for inner representation and mediate for external appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A6","CH_B337_7CAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MME-04","text":"Mathematics is detached from both metaphysical container-realism and empirical abstractionism without being deprived of necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A11","CH_B337_8ELU:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MME-05","text":"Motion and change are reclassified as empirical content whose possibility is formally conditioned but whose actuality is not supplied by pure sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system explains how change and motion can be represented without assigning simultaneous contradictions to one object.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A1","CH_B337_6TEC:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, internal sense mediates temporal order across representations and thereby across all phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A3","CH_B337_7CAC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, questions about time are reframed as questions about the conditions of possible experience rather than as direct ontological inventories.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:AS4","CH_B337_7CAC:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological-temporal level, time is necessary for appearances while remaining unavailable as a determination of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A4","CH_B337_7CAC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological level, the system performs its own thesis by securing validity through restriction, exclusion, and scope-control rather than metaphysical assertion.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SNX2","CH_B337_8ELU:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:FL-01","text":"Time must be subjective as a form of intuition and objectively necessary for all experience, and the system contains this tension by separating phenomenal objectivity from absolute reality.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:MRT6","CH_B337_7CAC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:FL-02","text":"The a priori formal structure of time is asserted as necessary, but its derivation is compressed rather than independently demonstrated within the system.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:MRT6","CH_B337_7CAC:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:FL-03","text":"The spatial line represents temporal succession while the system denies that succession is reducible to spatial coexistence.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:MRT7","CH_B337_7CAC:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:FL-04","text":"Things in themselves are required as a limit on phenomenal claims, but their positive relation to appearance remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:MRT3","CH_B337_8ELU:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:FL-05","text":"Motion is said to admit synthetic a priori cognition through temporal form while also being excluded from pure a priori sensible content.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MP-01","text":"Time mutates from the ordering condition of succession into the universal form of internal sense and then into a member of the dual formal basis of mathematics.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A4","CH_B337_8ELU:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MP-02","text":"Subjectivity mutates from an apparent restriction on knowledge into the enabling condition of phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:AS3","CH_B337_7CAC:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MP-03","text":"Motion mutates from a paradigmatic form of change into a boundary marker separating formal conditions from empirical perception.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A2","CH_B337_8ELU:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MP-04","text":"Mathematical necessity mutates from an implication of pure intuition into the decisive criterion separating transcendental ideality from both reification and empiricism.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:A8","CH_B337_8ELU:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a phenomenal field whose objects are available only through forms that simultaneously constitute their experienced order and limit claims about their independent being.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:A5","CH_B337_7CAC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not a private inventor of temporal order but a finite locus in which a non-arbitrary form necessarily structures possible intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:DNX4","CH_B337_7CAC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:HSI-03","text":"Time functions as the sequencing machine through which one object can remain identifiable while undergoing incompatible determinations.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:D2","CH_B337_6TEC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:HSI-04","text":"The outside of appearance functions as a limit-image rather than an accessible second realm, because representing it temporally would reintroduce the form excluded from it.","refs":["CH_B337_7CAC:SNX4","CH_B337_7CAC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:HSI-05","text":"Critical navigation consists in distinguishing the conditions of valid experience from illicit metaphysical transfer rather than escaping the conditions of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:AS4","CH_B337_7CAC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_05:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s movement from change through temporal form to phenomenal objectivity, mathematical necessity, and empirical restriction.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:MRT1","CH_B337_8ELU:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction is strongest where temporal succession resolves the contradiction of opposed determinations without converting time into an object.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:MRT4","CH_B337_7CAC:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MRT-B03","text":"Coherence depends on retaining empirical reality, transcendental ideality, phenomenal validity, and mathematical necessity as a single articulated structure.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:MRT7","CH_B337_8ELU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MRT-B04","text":"The relation between appearances and things in themselves remains underdetermined and cannot be reconstructed as positive knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_8ELU:MRT3","CH_B337_7CAC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_05:MRT-B05","text":"The indispensable irreducible tension is the distinction between temporal form as a condition of motion and motion as empirically perceived content.","refs":["CH_B337_6TEC:A7","CH_B337_8ELU:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 13–15","context":"§ 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time. · § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions. · § 8. Elucidation."}},{"id":"CL_B337_06","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 16–18","order":99,"parents":["CH_B337_9GRT","CH_B337_1CTS","CH_B337_ILG0"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"d86ad1171ed6f9eb3138ce2f9105b2968b40b998f032eea043ddabda72d129f9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:AS-01","text":"The system must secure necessary synthetic cognition while explaining how objects can be given to a finite mind without becoming knowable as they are independently of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:AS1","CH_B337_ILG0:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:AS-02","text":"Objectivity is reframed as the result of phenomenal givenness structured by sensibility and conceptual determination supplied by understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:AS2","CH_B337_ILG0:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:AS-03","text":"Pure intuition supplies the nonconceptual increment required for synthetic judgment, while understanding unites that increment with conceptual content.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:D3","CH_B337_1CTS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:AS-04","text":"Sensibility and understanding are irreducible but jointly necessary, so neither receptive givenness nor spontaneous thought can independently constitute cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:AS3","CH_B337_ILG0:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:AS-05","text":"The distinction between the faculties grounds the separation of æsthetic from logic without converting that disciplinary separation into cognitive independence.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:D4","CH_B337_ILG0:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:AS-06","text":"Every condition that enables validity also fixes its jurisdiction: pure intuition restricts cognition to possible experience, and pure logic restricts itself to thought’s form.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SD2","CH_B337_1CTS:D4","CH_B337_ILG0:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:AS-07","text":"The movement is recursively differentiating and recompositive: cognition is divided into faculties, each faculty is purified, and their restricted cooperation reconstructs bounded objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:AS7","CH_B337_9GRT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:AS-08","text":"Closure remains transitional rather than terminal because the Æsthetic resolves only the sensible part of the problem, while the mechanism joining intuition and conception remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:D5","CH_B337_ILG0:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Finite cognition is divided between receptive sensibility and spontaneous understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A1","CH_B337_ILG0:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Cognition requires the non-substitutable cooperation of intuition and conception.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:AS3","CH_B337_ILG0:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Space and time function first as necessary forms of sensible givenness and then as sources of a priori content exceeding concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A3","CH_B337_1CTS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] The conditions making cognition possible are simultaneously the principles limiting its legitimate scope.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SD2","CH_B337_1CTS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Subjective conditions can ground bounded objectivity because empirical reality concerns objects as given under those conditions rather than as things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SD1","CH_B337_1CTS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Faculty distinctions preserve functional non-exchangeability while requiring operational cooperation.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:D4","CH_B337_ILG0:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] A priori necessity requires exclusion of empirical derivation, but formal thought alone cannot supply objective synthetic content.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A7","CH_B337_ILG0:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] The legitimate domain of a cognition is indexed to the representational condition enabling it.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A7","CH_B337_ILG0:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] The subject is spontaneous in thought but can intuit itself only through temporal self-affection.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A10","CH_B337_ILG0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Transcendental inquiry replaces unrestricted claims about objects with an explanatory and restrictive mapping of cognitive conditions and jurisdictions.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SD5","CH_B337_1CTS:ME4","CH_B337_ILG0:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:RMD-01","text":"Sensibility retains its identity as receptivity but changes from the complete field of the Æsthetic into one indispensable component of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A1","CH_B337_ILG0:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:RMD-02","text":"Pure intuition migrates from conditioning phenomenal objects to supplying the synthetic increment that conceptual analysis cannot produce.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A8","CH_B337_1CTS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:RMD-03","text":"Limitation recurs with altered objects: it first bounds spatial-temporal cognition, then synthetic judgment, and finally the authority of pure logic.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SD2","CH_B337_1CTS:A6","CH_B337_ILG0:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:RMD-04","text":"Subjectivity reverses valence from an apparent threat to realism into the condition under which empirical objectivity becomes valid.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SD4","CH_B337_1CTS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:RMD-05","text":"Faculty separation is absorbed into a higher-order structure in which distinct cognitive functions cooperate without exchanging roles.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:AS4","CH_B337_ILG0:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:RMD-06","text":"Intellectual intuition shifts from a contrastive theological limit to the generalized exclusion of any human faculty that both gives and thinks its object.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:AS7","CH_B337_ILG0:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:RMD-07","text":"The empirical changes function from sensible matter within intuition to the subjective conditions governing the actual exercise of understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A3","CH_B337_ILG0:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:CGD-01","text":"Geometry and natural theology remain local proof-machines, supporting the ideality of sensible forms without becoming the cluster’s general cognitive mechanism.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:ME3","CH_B337_9GRT:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:CGD-02","text":"The a priori status of space and time scales upward because their formal role becomes the source of intuitive content required by synthetic judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:D2","CH_B337_1CTS:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:CGD-03","text":"The conclusion of the Æsthetic is catalytic rather than final: it converts a local account of pure intuition into the problem of interaction between intuition and conception.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:D5","CH_B337_1CTS:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:CGD-04","text":"The detailed taxonomy of general, particular, pure, and applied logic remains local, while its restriction of logical authority scales into a general jurisdictional principle.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:AF2","CH_B337_ILG0:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:CGD-05","text":"The non-substitutable cooperation of faculties bridges the Æsthetic and logic by specifying how intuitive surplus can enter synthetic judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:AS3","CH_B337_1CTS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:CGD-06","text":"Temporal self-affection remains a local account of inner sense but scales upward by revealing the subject as divided between spontaneous activity and receptive self-appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:D6","CH_B337_ILG0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:GNX-01","text":"Synthetic cognition cannot be produced by concepts alone.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:NX5","CH_B337_ILG0:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GNX-02","text":"Necessary cognition cannot be derived from accumulated empirical content.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:NX4","CH_B337_1CTS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GNX-03","text":"A priori validity neither converts space and time into properties of things nor licenses judgments about every possible object.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:NX5","CH_B337_9GRT:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GNX-04","text":"Phenomenality is not illusion, because transcendental ideality remains compatible with empirical reality.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:NX7","CH_B337_9GRT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GNX-05","text":"Pure general logic cannot become an organon that generates substantive knowledge of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:NX7","CH_B337_ILG0:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GNX-06","text":"Human cognition cannot collapse sensibility and understanding into a single intellectually intuitive faculty.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:SNX1","CH_B337_ILG0:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:GNX-07","text":"Empirical psychology cannot ground the necessity and universality of pure logic.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:NX6","CH_B337_ILG0:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:DBN-01","text":"The system requires that no independent cognition be available for comparing appearances with things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:DNX1","CH_B337_1CTS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:DBN-02","text":"Finite cognition cannot simultaneously receive an object and originate that object through the same faculty.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:DNX4","CH_B337_ILG0:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:DBN-03","text":"Validity cannot be established from a standpoint outside every possible form of intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SNX2","CH_B337_1CTS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:DBN-04","text":"A priori necessity cannot be identified with unrestricted validity for objects independently of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:DNX4","CH_B337_1CTS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:DBN-05","text":"Neither passive givenness nor spontaneous thought can constitute knowledge without mediation by the other.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:DNX1","CH_B337_ILG0:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:BSA-01","text":"The deepest excluded possibility is finite intellectual intuition: immediate cognition in which giving, thinking, and self-knowledge coincide.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SNX3","CH_B337_ILG0:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:BSA-02","text":"The architecture circles an unpresentable affecting ground whose object-dependence must be retained although the relation of affection remains unexplained.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SNX1","CH_B337_9GRT:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:BSA-03","text":"No transparent metacognitive standpoint can simultaneously condition experience, compare appearance with noumenal reality, and display the complete union of intuition and conception.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SNX2","CH_B337_ILG0:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:XD-01","text":"The subjectivity of pure intuition explains both its synthetic contribution and the restriction of that contribution to objects given under sensible conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-03","CL_B337_06:GA-04","CL_B337_06:GA-08","CH_B337_9GRT:A8","CH_B337_1CTS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:XD-02","text":"Synthetic judgment is the operational form taken by the cooperation of receptive intuition and spontaneous understanding.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-01","CL_B337_06:GA-02","CL_B337_06:GA-03","CH_B337_1CTS:A4","CH_B337_ILG0:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:XD-03","text":"Empirical objectivity arises when sensible givenness under pure forms is conceptually determined without being converted into knowledge of things in themselves.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-02","CL_B337_06:GA-05","CL_B337_06:GA-08","CH_B337_9GRT:SD1","CH_B337_1CTS:SD4","CH_B337_ILG0:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:XD-04","text":"The restriction of pure intuition to possible experience and the restriction of pure logic to form instantiate one common principle of bounded authority.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-04","CL_B337_06:GA-08","CL_B337_06:GA-10","CH_B337_1CTS:A6","CH_B337_ILG0:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:XD-05","text":"The subject is structurally asymmetric: it spontaneously produces representations yet encounters itself only through temporal receptivity.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-01","CL_B337_06:GA-09","CH_B337_9GRT:A10","CH_B337_ILG0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:XD-06","text":"Geometrical necessity requires pure intuition because empirical induction cannot ground it and formal logic cannot supply its objective synthetic content.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-03","CL_B337_06:GA-07","CL_B337_06:GA-08","CH_B337_9GRT:A7","CH_B337_1CTS:D2","CH_B337_ILG0:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity becomes a rule-governed relation among givenness, sensible form, and conceptual determination rather than disclosure of intrinsic object-nature.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-02","CL_B337_06:GA-05","CH_B337_9GRT:A11","CH_B337_ILG0:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:BSD-02","text":"Necessity is generated by the architecture of finite cognition and therefore carries the jurisdictional limits of that architecture.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-04","CL_B337_06:GA-07","CL_B337_06:GA-08","CH_B337_1CTS:SD1","CH_B337_ILG0:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:BSD-03","text":"Transcendental critique becomes a method for mapping the enabling conditions and restricted authority of each cognitive power.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-04","CL_B337_06:GA-06","CL_B337_06:GA-10","CH_B337_1CTS:ME4","CH_B337_ILG0:D5","CH_B337_9GRT:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:BSD-04","text":"The finite subject is neither passive substance nor sovereign spontaneity but a divided operation that thinks actively and appears to itself receptively.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-01","CL_B337_06:GA-09","CH_B337_9GRT:D6","CH_B337_ILG0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:BSD-05","text":"Transcendental error arises when subjective forms are treated as properties of all beings or when formal logic is treated as an organon of objects.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-04","CL_B337_06:GA-08","CL_B337_06:GA-10","CH_B337_9GRT:D8","CH_B337_ILG0:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:RLE-01","text":"Affection generates receptive givenness, space and time order that givenness, and understanding brings the resulting intuition under conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:FGS1","CH_B337_9GRT:A3","CH_B337_ILG0:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:RLE-02","text":"The validity–limitation loop repeatedly converts an enabling condition into the principle restricting the scope of what it enables.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:SD1","CH_B337_9GRT:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:RLE-03","text":"The differentiation–cooperation loop separates faculties to preserve their functions and reunites them to produce cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:D4","CH_B337_ILG0:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:RLE-04","text":"Purification escalates from removing empirical grounds to securing necessity, but that necessity is recursively restricted before it can become an unrestricted organon.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:SD2","CH_B337_ILG0:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:RLE-05","text":"Possible experience is the dominant attractor toward which pure intuition, synthetic judgment, empirical objectivity, and logical restriction converge.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A6","CH_B337_1CTS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:RLE-06","text":"Spontaneous activity returns through self-affection as temporally ordered appearance, producing a recursive subject that cannot become immediately self-present.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A10","CH_B337_ILG0:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:FGS-B01","text":"Object-affection establishes receptive givenness without yet producing cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:FGS1","CH_B337_9GRT:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:FGS-B02","text":"Space and time formally order sensible matter and contribute a priori content unavailable through conceptual analysis.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A3","CH_B337_1CTS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:FGS-B03","text":"Understanding unites conceptual content with given intuition and thereby produces determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A4","CH_B337_ILG0:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:FGS-B04","text":"Cooperative synthesis generates empirically real phenomena without disclosing objects as things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SD1","CH_B337_ILG0:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:FGS-B05","text":"The enabling representational condition fixes the domain within which the resulting cognition is valid.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A7","CH_B337_9GRT:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:FGS-B06","text":"Faculty differentiation yields æsthetic and logic, while purification of logic into formal law blocks its conversion into an objective organon.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:AS4","CH_B337_ILG0:A14","CH_B337_ILG0:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:AFM-B01","text":"Finite affection → receptive intuition → spatial-temporal ordering establishes the field of possible givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A1","CH_B337_9GRT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:AFM-B02","text":"Pure intuitive surplus → union with conceptual content → synthetic a priori judgment explains non-analytic necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:D3","CH_B337_1CTS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:AFM-B03","text":"Non-substitutable faculty cooperation → determinate cognition → empirical reality produces bounded objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:AS3","CH_B337_9GRT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:AFM-B04","text":"Enabling condition → restricted object-domain → possible experience displaces unrestricted metaphysical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:A6","CH_B337_9GRT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:AFM-B05","text":"Faculty differentiation → æsthetic and logic → pure formal canon → exclusion of logic as a universal organon.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:AS4","CH_B337_ILG0:FGS5","CH_B337_ILG0:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:MME-01","text":"Reality is redistributed between empirically real phenomena and unknowable things in themselves rather than between reality and mere illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:ME1","CH_B337_9GRT:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MME-02","text":"The source of a cognition and the scope of its application become independently assessable, dissolving the equation of a priori origin with unlimited reach.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:ME2","CH_B337_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MME-03","text":"The subject is reclassified as a composite cognitive operation whose spontaneity does not abolish receptive dependence or phenomenal self-knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:ME1","CH_B337_9GRT:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MME-04","text":"Logic is redrawn by separating formal validity from actual reasoning and by relocating psychology outside its pure foundation.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:ME2","CH_B337_ILG0:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MME-05","text":"Transcendental method becomes simultaneously explanatory and jurisdictional: it identifies enabling conditions in order to delimit legitimate claims.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SD5","CH_B337_1CTS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, space, time, synthetic judgments, sensibility, understanding, and divisions of logic constitute the explicit object-field.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:ALT1","CH_B337_ILG0:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, cognition arises from the interaction of pure sensible forms with conceptual spontaneity.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:ALT2","CH_B337_ILG0:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, possibility-questions are answered by isolating enabling conditions and purifying their legitimate authority.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:ALT3","CH_B337_ILG0:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, finite cognition knows objects only through sensible conditions and knows itself only through temporal self-affection.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A10","CH_B337_ILG0:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, successive differentiations, exclusions, and restrictions enact the purification that the system attributes to critique.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:AS7","CH_B337_ILG0:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:FL-01","text":"The relation through which an unknowable object affects sensibility is indispensable but remains structurally underdetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:FL-02","text":"The union of intuition and conception is required for cognition, but the mechanism accomplishing that union is not yet supplied.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:MRT3","CH_B337_1CTS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:FL-03","text":"Transcendental ideality and empirical reality remain productively tense because objectivity must be secured through conditions that deny noumenal disclosure.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SD1","CH_B337_9GRT:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:FL-04","text":"Pure logic is necessarily formal and therefore insufficient for cognition, even though every cognition requires conceptual determination governed by understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:D5","CH_B337_ILG0:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MP-01","text":"Pure intuition mutates from the form of sensible objects into the synthetic increment contributed to judgment and then into one term of faculty cooperation.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A8","CH_B337_1CTS:D3","CH_B337_1CTS:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MP-02","text":"Subjectivity mutates from a limitation on metaphysical realism into the positive basis of bounded validity.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:SD4","CH_B337_1CTS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MP-03","text":"Faculty separation mutates from disciplinary division into a relational architecture whose parts remain distinct precisely in order to cooperate.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:AS4","CH_B337_ILG0:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a finite transformation machine in which affection becomes ordered intuition, intuition becomes conceptually determined cognition, and validity remains domain-bound.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:FGS1","CH_B337_ILG0:FGS2","CH_B337_1CTS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a divided interface: receptive enough to be given objects and spontaneous enough to produce conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:A1","CH_B337_ILG0:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:HSI-03","text":"Time is the internal medium through which spontaneous activity returns to the subject as phenomenal self-appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:A10","CH_B337_9GRT:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:HSI-04","text":"Cognitive navigation proceeds by mapping jurisdictions rather than escaping finitude, because intellectual intuition is unavailable and critique remains explanatory through restriction.","refs":["CH_B337_ILG0:SNX1","CH_B337_1CTS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_06:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s movement from receptive givenness through pure intuition to conceptual cognition and restricted logical authority.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:MRT1","CH_B337_ILG0:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined at two indispensable interfaces: affection by an unknowable object and the union of intuition with conception.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:MRT4","CH_B337_ILG0:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale because positive explanation and negative limitation arise from the same purified cognitive conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:MRT5","CH_B337_ILG0:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where the partial solution of the Æsthetic becomes the restricted authority of logic rather than an unrestricted metaphysics.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:D5","CH_B337_ILG0:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if sensible limitation precedes logical supplementation and faculty separation is retained within cooperation.","refs":["CH_B337_1CTS:D5","CH_B337_ILG0:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_06:MRT-B06","text":"The evidential force of geometrical necessity and the precise mechanism of synthesis resist complete axiomatic reduction and remain dependent on local proof and subsequent development.","refs":["CH_B337_9GRT:MRT6","CH_B337_ILG0:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 16–18","context":"§ 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic. · § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic. · I. Of Logic in General."}},{"id":"CL_B337_07","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 19–21","order":100,"parents":["CH_B337_ITL0","CH_B337_IDGL","CH_B337_IDTL"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"1811518731416f144acddbcb63e5c106d55f75ea0d6ac32d454f785986a6d5b1","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:ASB-01","text":"The system constructs a restricted science of pure object-directed thought by separating it from general logic’s abstraction from content and object-relation.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:AS1","CH_B337_ITL0:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:ASB-02","text":"Transcendentality is recoded from a property of a priori representations into a critique of their possibility, application, use, and objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:AS4","CH_B337_ITL0:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:ASB-03","text":"Truth divides between formal non-contradiction and object-related correspondence, so universal logical form can constrain cognition without certifying its objective truth.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A1","CH_B337_IDGL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:ASB-04","text":"Pure understanding supplies conditions for thinking objects but cannot supply the objects or material content on which objective cognition depends.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A3","CH_B337_IDTL:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:ASB-05","text":"Analytic names the legitimate examination of conditions and judgments, whereas logic becomes dialectical when its canonical authority is converted into a productive organon.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A10","CH_B337_IDTL:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:ASB-06","text":"Dialectical illusion is generated when valid formal conditions are detached from possible object-givenness and asserted as material knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:SD4","CH_B337_IDTL:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:ASB-07","text":"The argument moves recursively from abstraction, through the specification of objective conditions, into the diagnosis of their misuse, and back to a restricted critical use.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:FGS1","CH_B337_IDTL:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:ASB-08","text":"The system closes not with a positive science of hyperphysical objects but with a bounded tribunal of judgment that limits claims to conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:SNX4","CH_B337_IDTL:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition is relational: logical form must be assessed in relation to objects rather than treated as a self-sufficient property of representations.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A1","CH_B337_IDGL:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] A priori status becomes transcendental only when cognition is examined according to its possibility, application, use, and claim to objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A8","CH_B337_ITL0:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Formal conformity is a necessary negative condition of truth but cannot establish objective correspondence or material cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:AS3","CH_B337_IDTL:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Transcendental analytic supplies conditions for thinking any object, yet those conditions become objectively valid only in possible application to intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A5","CH_B337_IDTL:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Experience or intuition supplies the matter of cognition that neither general logical form nor pure understanding can generate.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A7","CH_B337_IDTL:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] The canon–organon distinction governs both general and transcendental logic by separating critical adjudication from illegitimate cognitive production.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A10","CH_B337_IDTL:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Dialectic is not mere error but the structurally recurrent illusion produced when conditions of valid thought are extended into objectless material assertion.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A11","CH_B337_IDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Critique culminates in self-limitation: understanding and reason legitimately test judgments and prevent delusion rather than establish unrestricted synthetic knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A13","CH_B337_IDTL:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:RMD-01","text":"Abstraction recurs first as the constitutive procedure of general logic and then as the diagnosis of why general logic cannot determine material truth.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:AS1","CH_B337_IDGL:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:RMD-02","text":"Object-relation shifts from the retained target of pure thought to the condition of possible intuition that decides whether pure concepts have objective use.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:AS3","CH_B337_IDTL:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:RMD-03","text":"The a priori mutates from a classificatory mark into a rule of bounded applicability: its validity depends upon possible experience rather than purity alone.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:D4","CH_B337_IDTL:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:RMD-04","text":"Analytic changes function from formal decomposition of cognition into a determination of the universal conditions under which objects can be thought.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:AS4","CH_B337_IDTL:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:RMD-05","text":"Dialectic expands from the misuse of general logic into the critique of hyperphysical employment of pure understanding and reason.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A11","CH_B337_IDTL:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:CGD-01","text":"The distinction between pure and empirical thought remains local taxonomy until it is integrated into the higher-order distinction between cognition’s formal origin and its experiential matter.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:AS2","CH_B337_IDTL:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:CGD-02","text":"Formal decomposition scales upward only as a negative screening procedure; it does not become a producer or sufficient validator of objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:AS4","CH_B337_IDGL:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:CGD-03","text":"The investigation of origin, extent, and validity becomes a cluster-level framework because it coordinates the limits of general logic with the conditions of transcendental logic.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A12","CH_B337_IDTL:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:CGD-04","text":"The canon–organon contrast bridges all three units by converting local logical delimitations into a general principle of critical jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A10","CH_B337_IDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:CGD-05","text":"The anticipated inventory of pure concepts remains underdetermined and is superseded by the operative question of how any pure concept can claim objective use.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:SNX5","CH_B337_IDTL:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:GNX-01","text":"The system repeatedly rejects the inference from coherence within cognition to correspondence with an object.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:DNX3","CH_B337_IDTL:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:GNX-02","text":"The system rejects purity and a priori possession as self-certifying grounds of objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:DNX4","CH_B337_IDTL:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:GNX-03","text":"The system rejects every unlimited organon that would produce knowledge of objects from logical or transcendental form alone.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:NX6","CH_B337_IDTL:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:GNX-04","text":"The system rejects the treatment of transcendental and empirical as independently given classes of objects rather than modes of cognitive use and possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:NX8","CH_B337_IDTL:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:GNX-05","text":"The system rejects a metaphysics in which thought autonomously generates, determines, or gains knowledge of hyperphysical objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:SNX1","CH_B337_IDTL:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:DBN-01","text":"No universal formal procedure can certify the material truth of every object-directed cognition, because universality is purchased through abstraction from differentiated objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:NX1","CH_B337_IDGL:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:DBN-02","text":"No completion of truth is available through form alone, because formal necessity cannot generate either object-givenness or material correspondence.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:DNX2","CH_B337_IDTL:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:DBN-03","text":"No pure faculty can become an autonomous source of objective cognition, because its formal origin remains dependent on intuition and experience for objective matter.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:NX1","CH_B337_IDTL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:DBN-04","text":"No direct object-relation can establish transcendental status without an account of the a priori conditions, possibility, and use of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:NX10","CH_B337_ITL0:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:BSA-01","text":"The system forecloses the fantasy of a universal standpoint from which a single formal criterion could certify every possible object-directed cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:SNX1","CH_B337_IDGL:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable possibility around which the system is organized is self-generating knowledge: thought cannot create its own objects, matter, or material truth.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:SNX2","CH_B337_IDTL:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:BSA-03","text":"The system disallows an intelligible domain of objectively knowable hyperphysical objects detached from the horizon of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:DNX5","CH_B337_IDTL:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:XD-01","text":"The interaction of general and transcendental logic produces an intermediate domain: pure object-directed conditions that are neither contentless form nor self-supplying empirical knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GA-03","CL_B337_07:GA-04","CL_B337_07:GA-05","CH_B337_ITL0:SD6","CH_B337_IDTL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:XD-02","text":"Analytic divides into two non-equivalent critical functions: formal analysis excludes contradiction, while transcendental analysis identifies conditions without which object-directed content collapses.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GA-03","CL_B337_07:GA-04","CH_B337_IDGL:D1","CH_B337_IDTL:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:XD-03","text":"Objective legitimacy requires a double constraint: cognition must conform to formal laws and remain tied to possible object-givenness.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GA-03","CL_B337_07:GA-04","CH_B337_IDGL:A7","CH_B337_IDTL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:XD-04","text":"Dialectical illusion is generated by a repeatable category-transition in which a necessary condition of objective thought is recoded as a sufficient source of material knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GA-06","CL_B337_07:GA-07","CH_B337_IDGL:D6","CH_B337_IDTL:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:XD-05","text":"Transcendental classification concerns the mode of cognition’s possible use under experiential conditions, not a separate object-domain or an intrinsic status of isolated representations.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GA-02","CL_B337_07:GA-05","CH_B337_ITL0:SD5","CH_B337_IDTL:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:BSD-01","text":"Objective validity becomes an achieved relation between pure conceptual conditions and intuitively given matter, not a property possessed by either form or content in isolation.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:SD1","CH_B337_IDGL:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:BSD-02","text":"Logic is transformed from a potential producer of knowledge into a differentiated jurisdiction in which analytic establishes admissibility and dialectic blocks illegitimate surplus.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:ME2","CH_B337_IDTL:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:BSD-03","text":"Error becomes structural rather than merely accidental, because valid formal procedures systematically produce illusion when detached from object-related content.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:SD4","CH_B337_IDTL:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:BSD-04","text":"Self-limitation is not external restraint upon cognition but an internal condition for preserving the objective content of its assertions.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:D7","CH_B337_IDTL:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:BSD-05","text":"The cluster replaces the hope for final formal certification with a permanently bounded critical practice whose claims remain indexed to possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:SNX3","CH_B337_IDTL:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:RLE-01","text":"Abstraction produces formal law; formal law treated as productive knowledge produces illusion; critique returns formal law to its canonical function.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:FGS1","CH_B337_IDGL:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:RLE-02","text":"Pure understanding is isolated, discovers its dependence on intuition, and becomes dialectical when that dependence is abstracted away.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:FGS1","CH_B337_IDTL:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:RLE-03","text":"The canon–organon distinction functions as the principal attractor because every escalation of logical authority is measured by whether it adjudicates or purports to produce objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A10","CH_B337_IDTL:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:RLE-04","text":"Objective validity is the system’s recurrent attractor: origin, purity, form, and use are repeatedly subordinated to the question of whether cognition can relate to an object.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A12","CH_B337_IDTL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:RLE-05","text":"Dialectic operates as a feedback mechanism rather than a terminal failure by exposing the illegitimate transition and restoring critical restrictions on judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:FGS6","CH_B337_IDTL:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:FGS-B01","text":"Differentiating content, form, cognitive origin, and object-relation establishes the variable field within which kinds of logic can be classified.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:ALT2","CH_B337_IDTL:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:FGS-B02","text":"Abstracting from content and object-relation generates general logic as a universally necessary but materially insufficient formal canon.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:FGS1","CH_B337_IDGL:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:FGS-B03","text":"Retaining pure object-directed conditions while requiring intuition generates transcendental analytic as the conditional logic of possible objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:FGS3","CH_B337_IDTL:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:FGS-B04","text":"Detaching formal conditions from supplied objects converts valid constraints into groundless material assertions and produces dialectical illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:FGS3","CH_B337_IDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:FGS-B05","text":"Critiquing this detachment reassigns understanding and reason to the testing of judgments and the prevention of sophistical delusion.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:FGS5","CH_B337_IDTL:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:AFM-B01","text":"Differentiation of cognitive form, content, origin, and object-relation creates the field in which logical domains can be sorted.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:AFM-B02","text":"Abstraction from content and object-relation yields general logic, whose laws are necessary for thought but insufficient for material truth.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:AS1","CH_B337_IDGL:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:AFM-B03","text":"Retention of pure object-directed thought yields transcendental logic, which investigates the conditions of a priori applicability rather than merely the possession of a priori representations.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:AS3","CH_B337_ITL0:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:AFM-B04","text":"Connection of pure concepts to possible intuition determines the conditions under which objective cognition acquires content and truth-capacity.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:D1","CH_B337_IDTL:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:AFM-B05","text":"Conversion of a canon into an organon transforms conditions of valid judgment into semblances of material knowledge and dialectical illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A11","CH_B337_IDGL:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:AFM-B06","text":"Dialectical critique returns cognition to bounded empirical use by treating judgment as testable rather than as a source of unrestricted metaphysical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A10","CH_B337_IDTL:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:MME-01","text":"Logic is reorganized into a differentiated field of general formal constraint, transcendental conditions of objectivity, and dialectical critique of illegitimate extension.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:SD6","CH_B337_IDTL:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:MME-02","text":"Truth is redistributed into formal validity and material correspondence, while transcendental analysis specifies the conditions under which that correspondence can have objective standing.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:ME1","CH_B337_IDTL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:MME-03","text":"Cognitive authority is reclassified from the capacity to generate knowledge into the capacity to regulate, test, and delimit claims.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:ME2","CH_B337_IDTL:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:MME-04","text":"Metaphysical overreach is reclassified as a category error in which formal rules are illicitly converted into material claims about unavailable objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:ME3","CH_B337_IDTL:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: the system divides general logic and transcendental logic, then divides each into analytic and dialectical functions.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:ALT1","CH_B337_IDTL:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: objective cognition requires coordination among formal laws, pure conceptual conditions, intuition, experience, and relation to objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:ALT2","CH_B337_IDTL:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: critique replaces the expansion of cognition with a jurisdictional practice that tests claims and blocks illegitimate organonic use.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:ALT3","CH_B337_IDTL:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological level: objects are cognitionally available only under conditions of possible intuition and experience, not through pure thought’s autonomous production.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:ALT4","CH_B337_IDTL:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological and performative level: the system enacts its thesis by repeatedly producing a logical distinction, testing its extension, and converting its excess into dialectical diagnosis.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:FGS4","CH_B337_IDTL:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:FL-01","text":"Pure object-directed thought must retain restricted content in order to concern objects, yet it cannot obtain objective content without empirically supplied intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:D2","CH_B337_IDTL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:FL-02","text":"The designation of transcendental analytic as a logic of truth risks conflation with the general logical truth that remains merely necessary and negative.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:AS4","CH_B337_IDGL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:FL-03","text":"Understanding is isolated as a distinct origin of pure cognition while being denied autonomy as a source of objects and objective knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A2","CH_B337_IDTL:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:MP-01","text":"A priori cognition changes function at the point where its status as nonempirical representation is replaced by an inquiry into its possibility, application, and use.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:A9","CH_B337_ITL0:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:MP-02","text":"Dialectic changes scope at the point where the misuse of general formal logic becomes the hyperphysical extension of pure understanding and reason.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:A11","CH_B337_IDTL:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a conditional field of object-givenness in which pure concepts can order experience but cannot manufacture the matter to which they apply.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:SD1","CH_B337_IDTL:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes a bounded adjudicator of claims rather than a sovereign producer of objects, truth, or metaphysical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:A13","CH_B337_IDTL:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:HSI-03","text":"Possible experience functions as the horizon of cognitive availability, beyond which formal thought can continue but objective cognition cannot.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:ALT4","CH_B337_IDTL:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:HSI-04","text":"Struggle within cognition takes the form of maintaining the boundary at which valid conditions cease to authorize material assertions.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:SD4","CH_B337_IDTL:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_07:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s central architecture: objective cognition requires conditions, supplied matter, and a critique of their illegitimate separation.","refs":["CH_B337_IDTL:MRT1","CH_B337_IDTL:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:MRT-B02","text":"The system remains underdetermined regarding the inventory of pure concepts, the validation of independently acquired material information, and the mechanism through which agents mistake canons for organons.","refs":["CH_B337_ITL0:SNX5","CH_B337_IDGL:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if formal necessity, transcendental condition, and material sufficiency remain distinct and non-substitutable functions.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:MRT3","CH_B337_IDTL:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_07:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when the insufficiency of form precedes the delimitation of analytic legitimacy and the diagnosis of dialectical excess.","refs":["CH_B337_IDGL:MRT7","CH_B337_IDTL:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 19–21","context":"II. Of Transcendental Logic. · III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic. · IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic."}},{"id":"CL_B337_08","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 22–24","order":101,"parents":["CH_B337_FDTL","CH_B337_BIAC","CH_B337_INT3"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"9fc806c84b78e7a9c71255b88fba254293606a368de159d8d6fb4ec9621a209d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:AS-01","text":"The problem is to establish the complete domain of pure understanding without treating it as an empirical inventory.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:AS1","CH_B337_INT3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:AS-02","text":"The inquiry shifts from collecting concepts to reconstructing the faculty and principle that generate and order them.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:AS2","CH_B337_INT3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:AS-03","text":"Pure cognition is grounded in the understanding’s endogenous unity while remaining sharply distinguished from sensibility and empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:AS3","CH_B337_BIAC:A2","CH_B337_INT3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:AS-04","text":"Completeness becomes possible only when an anticipated totality determines the systematic placement of elementary conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A7","CH_B337_INT3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:AS-05","text":"Experience is retained as an occasion for manifestation but rejected as the source or ordering principle of pure conception.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:D4","CH_B337_BIAC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:AS-06","text":"The system moves through exclusion, genetic relocation, principled ordering, and internal validation rather than through cumulative discovery.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:NX4","CH_B337_INT3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:AS-07","text":"Its closure is architectural but incomplete: transcendental logic reaches the division of concepts and principles while leaving the concrete derivation of the unifying rule compressed.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A10","CH_B337_INT3:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Pure understanding is a distinct, self-contained source of cognition rather than an empirical residue.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:AS3","CH_B337_FDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Pure conceptions belong to the understanding and must be separated from intuition, sensibility, and empirical content.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A3","CH_B337_FDTL:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Completeness changes from a requirement concerning the extent of a table into a requirement concerning the principled placement of every conception.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A5","CH_B337_INT3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Experience occasions the development and manifestation of pure conceptions without constituting their ultimate origin.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A8","CH_B337_BIAC:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] The understanding’s unity functions as a generative rule for organizing its conceptual plurality.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A6","CH_B337_INT3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Empirical aggregation, repeated observation, resemblance, and complexity cannot establish systematic completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:NX4","CH_B337_INT3:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] A complete system requires an internal principle that assigns each pure conception its necessary place.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A7","CH_B337_INT3:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Purity is not merely an original condition but an operation by which empirical conditions are removed from developing conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A9","CH_B337_BIAC:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Transcendental logic becomes internally divided into the domains of conceptions and principles.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A10","CH_B337_FDTL:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] The idea of totality or unified understanding governs classification before the complete content of the system is exhibited.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A7","CH_B337_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:RMD-01","text":"“Pure understanding” recurs first as a boundary against sensibility, then as the endogenous faculty that generates and orders conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A3","CH_B337_BIAC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RMD-02","text":"“Completeness” mutates from total coverage of a conceptual sphere into a priori determinability through principled connection.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A5","CH_B337_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RMD-03","text":"“Experience” reverses function: it is excluded as the ground of purity but retained as the occasion through which latent conceptions develop.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:NX3","CH_B337_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RMD-04","text":"“Analysis” shifts from the decomposition of conceptual contents to the reconstruction of the productive faculty that makes those contents possible.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:AS1","CH_B337_BIAC:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RMD-05","text":"“Unity” moves from a self-subsistent ontological description to an operative rule of connection and placement.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A8","CH_B337_INT3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RMD-06","text":"“Classification” is absorbed into a constitutive operation: it no longer records concepts but determines what counts as a genuine element.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:SD3","CH_B337_FDTL:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RMD-07","text":"“Division” persists as systematic articulation but changes from a taxonomic endpoint into the internal architecture of transcendental logic.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A10","CH_B337_FDTL:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:CGD-01","text":"Variable attention, time, and observational penetration remain local mechanisms of empirical discovery; they scale upward only as evidence of why collection cannot secure completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A1","CH_B337_INT3:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:CGD-02","text":"The germinal development of individual conceptions remains lower-layer machinery, while the origin–occasion distinction becomes a cluster-level principle.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A7","CH_B337_BIAC:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:CGD-03","text":"The local contrast between content-analysis and faculty-analysis scales upward into a general displacement from conceptual inventory to generative architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:AS1","CH_B337_BIAC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:CGD-04","text":"The demand for a complete table bridges all three units by converting totality, faculty unity, and principled placement into one problem.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A7","CH_B337_INT3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:CGD-05","text":"The rejection of resemblance and complexity is local as a critique of classification but becomes globally significant as the rejection of external ordering principles.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A4","CH_B337_INT3:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:CGD-06","text":"The division between conceptions and principles is a terminal lower-layer result that becomes the cluster’s formal closure.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A10","CH_B337_FDTL:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:CGD-07","text":"The concrete content of the pure conceptions remains underdetermined, while the conditions for their systematic legitimacy become the cluster’s higher-order object.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:MRT3","CH_B337_FDTL:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:GNX-01","text":"Pure understanding is not an empirical aggregate assembled through repeated observation.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:SNX1","CH_B337_FDTL:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GNX-02","text":"Transcendental analysis is not exhausted by clarifying conceptual contents or sorting concepts by resemblance.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:NX1","CH_B337_INT3:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GNX-03","text":"Experience cannot serve simultaneously as the origin, validating ground, and ordering principle of pure conception.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:NX3","CH_B337_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GNX-04","text":"Completeness is not an indefinite accumulation of further concepts added to an initially incomplete inventory.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:DNX3","CH_B337_FDTL:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GNX-05","text":"Systematic order is not produced by arbitrary choice, chance, complexity, or external resemblance.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:NX5","CH_B337_INT3:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:GNX-06","text":"The system rejects any understanding whose contents remain externally expandable without determinate internal limits.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:SNX2","CH_B337_INT3:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:DBN-01","text":"No external accumulation can determine the boundary of a self-sufficient conceptual faculty.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:NX5","CH_B337_FDTL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:DBN-02","text":"No empirical manifestation can be accepted as identical with the transcendental constitution of a conception.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:DNX3","CH_B337_BIAC:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:DBN-03","text":"No classification based only on similarity or complexity can establish necessary conceptual relations.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:DNX2","CH_B337_INT3:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:DBN-04","text":"No complete system can be recognized without a prior conception of the whole that governs its divisions.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:SNX3","CH_B337_FDTL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:DBN-05","text":"No conception is validated independently of the systematic position through which its legitimacy is determined.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:DNX5","CH_B337_FDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:DBN-06","text":"No plurality of unrelated principles can adequately ground the unity attributed to pure conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:DNX6","CH_B337_INT3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:BSA-01","text":"The system cannot admit that pure cognition is fundamentally an empirical remainder assembled after the fact.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:BSA-02","text":"It cannot admit a standpoint outside the understanding from which the system could be supplemented or completed.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:SNX4","CH_B337_FDTL:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:BSA-03","text":"It cannot admit that conceptual unity is merely an effect imposed by classification rather than a property of the faculty.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:SNX3","CH_B337_INT3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:BSA-04","text":"It cannot admit a permanently open system whose limits remain undecidable because no internal principle governs them.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:SNX2","CH_B337_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:BSA-05","text":"The unavailable possibility organizing the argument is a complete conceptual order that would arise without a prior unity or totality.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:SNX4","CH_B337_FDTL:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:XD-01","text":"The demand for a complete table becomes inseparable from the demand to identify the faculty and principle that make completeness possible.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-03","CL_B337_08:GA-05","CL_B337_08:GA-07","CH_B337_FDTL:A5","CH_B337_BIAC:A2","CH_B337_INT3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:XD-02","text":"The distinction between origin and occasion converts experience from a rival ground of pure cognition into the temporal medium of its manifestation.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-04","CL_B337_08:GA-08","CH_B337_BIAC:D4","CH_B337_BIAC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:XD-03","text":"The rejection of empirical collection and the rejection of resemblance-based order jointly produce the requirement for an internally generated rule of placement.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-06","CL_B337_08:GA-07","CH_B337_INT3:D2","CH_B337_INT3:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:XD-04","text":"Purity becomes both a boundary condition and an active operation: concepts originate in the understanding but become unalloyed through the removal of empirical conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-02","CL_B337_08:GA-08","CH_B337_BIAC:A2","CH_B337_BIAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:XD-05","text":"The totality that validates individual cognitions is itself presupposed through the unity of the faculty, producing a productive circularity between whole and part.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-05","CL_B337_08:GA-10","CH_B337_FDTL:A9","CH_B337_INT3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:XD-06","text":"The same system that excludes temporal accumulation must nevertheless accommodate temporal development from latent conception to explicit purity.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-03","CL_B337_08:GA-04","CH_B337_FDTL:ALT4","CH_B337_BIAC:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:XD-07","text":"The division between concepts and principles emerges as the formal result of converting conceptual origin into systematic architecture.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-05","CL_B337_08:GA-09","CH_B337_FDTL:A10","CH_B337_FDTL:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:BSD-01","text":"Knowledge becomes a bounded self-authenticating architecture in which the organization of the system tests the legitimacy of its elements.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-05","CL_B337_08:GA-10","CH_B337_FDTL:SD2","CH_B337_FDTL:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:BSD-02","text":"The system produces a stratified temporality: pure structures originate in the understanding, emerge through experiential activation, and are completed outside empirical accumulation.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-01","CL_B337_08:GA-04","CL_B337_08:GA-06","CH_B337_BIAC:SD3","CH_B337_BIAC:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:BSD-03","text":"The subject is recoded from a passive recipient or collector into the operational unity that generates, purifies, and orders conceptual material.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-01","CL_B337_08:GA-05","CL_B337_08:GA-08","CH_B337_BIAC:NX6","CH_B337_BIAC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:BSD-04","text":"Philosophical method becomes a negative-positive machine: it excludes empirical and arbitrary models in order to construct a principled system.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-06","CL_B337_08:GA-07","CH_B337_INT3:FGS6","CH_B337_INT3:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:BSD-05","text":"The opposition between the a priori and the empirical is reorganized into a distinction between endogenous origin and developmental occasion.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-02","CL_B337_08:GA-04","CH_B337_BIAC:ME2","CH_B337_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:BSD-06","text":"Closure is achieved as a demand for determinate systematicity before it is achieved as an exhibited derivation of every conceptual element.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-07","CL_B337_08:GA-10","CH_B337_INT3:MRT3","CH_B337_INT3:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:RLE-01","text":"Empirical collection generates incompleteness; incompleteness demands a principle; the principle generates a system; the system intensifies the exclusion of collection.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:D1","CH_B337_INT3:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RLE-02","text":"Experience is excluded as origin, retained as occasion, and thereby reintroduced as the necessary temporal field in which purity becomes manifest.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:NX3","CH_B337_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RLE-03","text":"The idea of totality determines the division of concepts, while the completed division retroactively functions as evidence for the totality that governed it.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A7","CH_B337_FDTL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RLE-04","text":"The understanding’s unity first appears as a premise, then becomes a generative rule, and finally becomes the architecture of the system.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A6","CH_B337_INT3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RLE-05","text":"Purification operates recursively: a conception develops through experience, is stripped of empirical conditions, and is returned to the understanding as pure.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A8","CH_B337_BIAC:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RLE-06","text":"The conceptual attractor is not an individual conception but the principle-governed completeness of the whole.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A9","CH_B337_FDTL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:RLE-07","text":"Apparent repetition of completeness marks a phase change from quantitative inclusion, to systematic order, to internal validation.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:ME2","CH_B337_FDTL:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:FGS-B01","text":"Contingent manifestation produces a variable and incomplete collection of conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A1","CH_B337_INT3:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:FGS-B02","text":"The insufficiency of collection disqualifies observation, resemblance, complexity, choice, and chance as grounds of systematic completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:NX3","CH_B337_INT3:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:FGS-B03","text":"The understanding is posited as an absolute unity whose pure conceptions require connection under one governing conception or idea.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A6","CH_B337_INT3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:FGS-B04","text":"The governing unity becomes a rule that assigns each conception its proper place and makes completeness determinable a priori.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:AS5","CH_B337_INT3:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:FGS-B05","text":"Experience activates and develops germinal conceptions, after which the understanding removes empirical conditions and produces unalloyed forms.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A7","CH_B337_BIAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:FGS-B06","text":"Systematic articulation converts the organized field into the terminal division between conceptions and principles.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A10","CH_B337_FDTL:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:AFM-B01","text":"Variable cognition → haphazard discovery → non-systematic collection.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A2","CH_B337_INT3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:AFM-B02","text":"Non-systematic collection → failure of resemblance and complexity → rejection of empirical completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:A5","CH_B337_INT3:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:AFM-B03","text":"Failure of collection → appeal to the understanding’s unity → requirement for a single governing principle.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:SD2","CH_B337_INT3:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:AFM-B04","text":"Governing principle → proper placement of conceptions → a priori determinability of the complete system.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:AS5","CH_B337_INT3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:AFM-B05","text":"Endogenous conception → experiential development → removal of empirical conditions → purified conceptual form.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A7","CH_B337_BIAC:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:AFM-B06","text":"Purified system → internal validation → division of transcendental logic into conceptions and principles.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:AS5","CH_B337_FDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:MME-01","text":"Conceptual plurality is reclassified from an inventory to a system generated by an internal principle.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:ME1","CH_B337_INT3:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MME-02","text":"Completeness is reclassified from the quantity of discovered items to the necessity of their systematic relations.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:ME2","CH_B337_INT3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MME-03","text":"The boundary between pure understanding and sensibility becomes a constitutive boundary of the science rather than a preliminary distinction.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:ME3","CH_B337_FDTL:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MME-04","text":"Experience is relocated from foundational source to developmental trigger, allowing empirical manifestation without empirical constitution.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:ME2","CH_B337_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MME-05","text":"The understanding is reclassified from a repository of concepts into the generative ground of their order.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:ME1","CH_B337_INT3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MME-06","text":"The system’s architecture becomes an epistemic instrument capable of testing the legitimacy of its own elements.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:ME4","CH_B337_FDTL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: the system specifies pure concepts, their exclusions, their developmental conditions, and their division into concepts and principles.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:ALT1","CH_B337_FDTL:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: conceptual plurality is organized through the relation among faculty, totality, principle, placement, and completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:ALT2","CH_B337_INT3:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: philosophical method shifts from collecting and comparing concepts to reconstructing the conditions of their systematic legitimacy.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:ALT3","CH_B337_INT3:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological and temporal level: pure understanding is self-sufficient in origin but requires temporal experience for development and manifestation.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:ALT4","CH_B337_BIAC:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological and performative level: the text performs its claim by replacing an open inventory with a recursively bounded architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:SNX2","CH_B337_FDTL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:FL-01","text":"The system’s totality is required to determine the parts, yet the articulated parts are also what render the totality testable.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:D3","CH_B337_FDTL:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:FL-02","text":"Pure conceptions are independent of experience in origin but dependent on experience for development and explicit manifestation.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A8","CH_B337_BIAC:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:FL-03","text":"The understanding’s unity is asserted as the ground of systematic order before the specific rule generated by that unity is exhibited.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:MRT6","CH_B337_INT3:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:FL-04","text":"The system risks converting internal coherence into self-certification because its architecture both defines and validates legitimate elements.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:D5","CH_B337_FDTL:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MP-01","text":"Experience mutates from excluded source into necessary developmental occasion without losing its subordinate status.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:SD2","CH_B337_BIAC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MP-02","text":"Completeness mutates from the possession of all elements into the principled determination of their relations and places.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:ME2","CH_B337_INT3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MP-03","text":"Analysis mutates from decomposition of conceptual content into reconstruction of the faculty that produces conceptual possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:AS1","CH_B337_BIAC:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MP-04","text":"Unity mutates from a description of self-sufficiency into an operative rule that structures the entire conceptual field.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:A8","CH_B337_INT3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly modeled as a bounded generative system whose elements become intelligible through their position within a prior whole.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:ALT2","CH_B337_FDTL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not primarily an observer but a self-organizing faculty that activates, purifies, and orders conceptual potentials.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:A1","CH_B337_BIAC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:HSI-03","text":"Time is developmental rather than foundational: it reveals and occasions structures whose origin is not temporal accumulation.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:ALT4","CH_B337_BIAC:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:HSI-04","text":"The remaining mode of inquiry is recursive reconstruction: move from failed collection to internal principle, then from principle to systematic validation.","refs":["CH_B337_INT3:AF1","CH_B337_INT3:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_08:MRT-B01","text":"The current-level axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from empirical insufficiency through faculty, unity, principle, purification, and systematic closure.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:MRT1","CH_B337_INT3:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction preserves the decisive distinctions among pure origin, empirical occasion, systematic placement, and internal validation.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAC:MRT2","CH_B337_FDTL:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MRT-B03","text":"The strongest architecture lies in the interaction of totality, faculty unity, and principled completeness; the weakest point is the unexhibited derivation of the governing rule.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:MRT3","CH_B337_INT3:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_08:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent at the cluster level, but its closure depends on recursive return, compression of the derivation, and acceptance of the understanding’s unity as a governing premise.","refs":["CH_B337_FDTL:MRT5","CH_B337_INT3:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 22–24","context":"First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1 · BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2 · Introductory § 3"}},{"id":"CL_B337_09","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 25–27","order":102,"parents":["CH_B337_SILU","CH_B337_SILF","CH_B337_SIPC"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"cdba6fcce7684f6e211e320637885f6dfa3a148407b79b594b852ea4d12a9496","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:AS-B01","text":"The system seeks a non-empirical derivation of the complete pure conceptual structure required for cognition of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:AS1","CH_B337_SIPC:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AS-B02","text":"Human understanding is first restricted to discursive and mediate cognition because it lacks immediate intuition of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AS1","CH_B337_SILU:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AS-B03","text":"Judgement becomes the hinge through which the acts of understanding are reduced to a classifiable system of unifying functions.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AS6","CH_B337_SILU:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AS-B04","text":"Abstraction from material content isolates a fourfold logical table that purports to exhaust the forms of judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:AS1","CH_B337_SILF:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AS-B05","text":"The logical table becomes transcendentally significant when inferentially equivalent forms are differentiated by their unequal contributions to cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:AS3","CH_B337_SILF:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AS-B06","text":"The function of judgement is transferred to sensible synthesis so that the same unity organizing concepts also makes an intuited manifold thinkable as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:AS3","CH_B337_SIPC:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AS-B07","text":"The movement is recursively escalatory: exclusion of intuition produces conceptual mediation, mediation produces judgement, judgement produces its table, and the table returns as the source-pattern of object-cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AF1","CH_B337_SIPC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AS-B08","text":"The architecture closes systematically but only conditionally, because neither the completeness of the logical headings nor the identity of judgemental and synthetic unity is demonstrated within the supplied system.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:MRT3","CH_B337_SILF:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Human understanding is non-intuitive and therefore cognizes objects discursively through mediated relations among representations.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A1","CH_B337_SILU:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Receptivity supplies intuitive material while spontaneity orders it, and neither pole alone constitutes full object-cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AS3","CH_B337_SIPC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Judgement is the comprehensive operation of understanding because it unifies representations under higher representations and reduces intellectual acts to functions of unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A15","CH_B337_SILU:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The intellectual form of judgement can be isolated from its content and exhaustively classified under quantity, quality, relation, and modality.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A1","CH_B337_SILF:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Logical forms must be distinguished by cognitive function even where their inferential employment treats them as equivalent.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A4","CH_B337_SILF:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Synthesis precedes analysis because a manifold must first be joined before its representations can be analytically decomposed.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A3","CH_B337_SIPC:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Imagination performs the indispensable synthesis of the manifold, while understanding renders that synthesis conceptually unified.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A4","CH_B337_SIPC:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The same function of understanding produces analytical unity in judgement and synthetic unity in intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A7","CH_B337_SIPC:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Categories are the logical functions of judgement transformed into necessary forms for thinking a synthesized manifold as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A6","CH_B337_SIPC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The number and organization of original categories derive systematically from the complete logical table rather than from historical or inductive collection.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A8","CH_B337_SIPC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Logical form can organize cognition without independently supplying material truth, objective possibility, or predicates of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:SD3","CH_B337_SIPC:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] A finite inventory of original categories permits derivative conceptual expansion while prohibiting additions to the primitive table.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A10","CH_B337_SIPC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:RMD-01","text":"Unity retains functional identity as the ordering of multiplicity under one act, but expands from conceptual subsumption to the a priori unity of synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A6","CH_B337_SIPC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RMD-02","text":"Judgement mutates from an act mediating concept and object into a table of logical functions and finally into the source-pattern of transcendental object-unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AS5","CH_B337_SIPC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RMD-03","text":"Form is first isolated by abstraction from content and then reinserted into a transcendental logic that receives a priori sensible material.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A1","CH_B337_SIPC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RMD-04","text":"Spontaneity moves from the production and ordering of conceptions to the conceptual reduction of synthesis performed by imagination.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A5","CH_B337_SIPC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RMD-05","text":"Mediation expands from a relation among representations into a distributed sequence comprising sensible manifold, imaginative synthesis, and categorical unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:SD2","CH_B337_SIPC:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RMD-06","text":"Classification progresses from a promised exhibition of understanding’s functions to a topology that derives categories, locates derivatives, and detects illegitimate additions.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AS7","CH_B337_SIPC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RMD-07","text":"Negation changes from exclusion of a predicate into limitation of a conceptual sphere and is then absorbed as a structured moment within the category-table.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:ME2","CH_B337_SIPC:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RMD-08","text":"Modality migrates from the logical standing of the copula to a category-class concerning possibility, existence, and necessity, although the warrant for this object-directed migration remains deferred.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A10","CH_B337_SIPC:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RMD-09","text":"Disjunction reverses from reciprocal exclusion among cognitive alternatives into reciprocal coordination among coexisting substances under the category of community.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A9","CH_B337_SIPC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RMD-10","text":"General logic is retained as the source of judgemental forms but subordinated because it cannot provide the content or object-directed significance required by transcendental logic.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:AS3","CH_B337_SIPC:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RMD-11","text":"Unity, truth, and perfection lose their apparent ontological status and are displaced into criteria for the formal coherence of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A14","CH_B337_SIPC:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:CGD-01","text":"The local definition of a conception as a possible predicate scales upward only through the stronger reduction of all intellectual activity to judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A13","CH_B337_SILU:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:CGD-02","text":"The distinction between singular and universal judgement remains locally taxonomic, while its governing principle—that inferential equivalence can conceal cognitive difference—scales across the cluster.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:NX2","CH_B337_SILF:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:CGD-03","text":"The suspension of truth in hypothetical judgement remains a local relational mechanism, but it supports the wider separation of logical organization from object-truth.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A8","CH_B337_SILF:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:CGD-04","text":"The twelve individual moments remain lower-layer determinations, whereas the asserted correspondence between the complete judgement-table and the complete category-table becomes globally load-bearing.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A3","CH_B337_SIPC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:CGD-05","text":"The definition of understanding as the faculty of judging is the principal bridge from faculty analysis to transcendental derivation.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AS6","CH_B337_SIPC:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:CGD-06","text":"The identity of judgemental and synthetic functions catalyzes the decisive transition from logical classification to categories.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A7","CH_B337_SIPC:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:CGD-07","text":"Predicables remain a local taxonomic distinction but become current-level evidence that finite primitive closure is compatible with extensive derivative production.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A10","CH_B337_SIPC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:CGD-08","text":"The correction of unity, truth, and perfection is locally specific but scales into the general prohibition against converting cognitive conditions into predicates of things.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A14","CH_B337_SIPC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:CGD-09","text":"The composite yet primitive status of third categories does not organize the whole derivation and therefore persists chiefly as a structural fault line.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A12","CH_B337_SIPC:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:CGD-10","text":"Empirical inventories and rhapsodic catalogues become obsolete once completeness is relocated to derivation from a fixed faculty-structure.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:NX4","CH_B337_SIPC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:GNX-01","text":"Human understanding is defined against intellectual intuition and cannot obtain objects through immediate intellectual presentation.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:SNX1","CH_B337_SIPC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GNX-02","text":"Neither pure receptivity nor pure spontaneity is sufficient for cognition of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:SNX3","CH_B337_SILU:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GNX-03","text":"Logical form alone cannot furnish material cognition or directly determine an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:SNX2","CH_B337_SIPC:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GNX-04","text":"Equivalence in syllogistic use does not establish identity of cognitive function.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:DNX1","CH_B337_SILF:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GNX-05","text":"Analysis cannot originate cognition because it presupposes representations already joined by synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:NX2","CH_B337_SIPC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GNX-06","text":"No inductive or historical catalogue can guarantee the completeness or necessity of pure concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:NX4","CH_B337_SIPC:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GNX-07","text":"Conditions governing possible cognition cannot be converted directly into determinations of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:NX8","CH_B337_SIPC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:GNX-08","text":"No candidate may enter the primitive conceptual inventory unless it derives from the fixed logical functions of judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:DNX2","CH_B337_SIPC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:DBN-01","text":"The architecture excludes a faculty psychology in which understanding consists of irreducible acts lacking a common judgemental form.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:NX8","CH_B337_SILU:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:DBN-02","text":"The architecture cannot admit cognition as an unmediated relation between an intellectual subject and an independently available object.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:SNX2","CH_B337_SIPC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:DBN-03","text":"A purely formalist model that identifies logical validity with cognitive determination must be false for transcendental differentiation to operate.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:DNX1","CH_B337_SILF:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:DBN-04","text":"An empiricist or historical genealogy of categories is disqualified because occurrence cannot establish necessity or completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:NX4","CH_B337_SIPC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:DBN-05","text":"Ontological realism about logical criteria is excluded because unity, truth, and perfection regulate cognition rather than describe things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:NX8","CH_B337_SIPC:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:DBN-06","text":"Spontaneity cannot function as autonomous object-production because it remains dependent on receptive material and imaginative synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:DNX3","CH_B337_SIPC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSA-01","text":"The deepest unavailable possibility is an understanding that would know objects immediately without receptivity, representation, synthesis, or judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:SNX1","CH_B337_SIPC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSA-02","text":"The system circles an inaccessible ontological remainder: the thing in itself cannot be reached by extending the formal conditions of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:SNX2","CH_B337_SIPC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSA-03","text":"The completeness of the four logical headings functions as an unavailable proof-ground whose absence is concealed by the determinacy of the resulting table.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:MRT5","CH_B337_SIPC:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSA-04","text":"The system depends on, but does not demonstrate, the identity between the function unifying judgement and the function unifying intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:MRT3","CH_B337_SIPC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSA-05","text":"A complete semantic articulation of the categories remains absent because structural derivation substitutes for exhaustive definition and derivation of predicables.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:NX9","CH_B337_SIPC:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:XD-01","text":"Once all acts of understanding are reduced to judgement, an exhaustive table of judgement-functions also claims to exhaust the forms of understanding.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-03","CL_B337_09:GA-04","CH_B337_SILU:A16","CH_B337_SILF:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:XD-02","text":"Mediate conceptual reference becomes full object-cognition only when receptive material is synthesized by imagination and unified categorically by understanding.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-01","CL_B337_09:GA-02","CL_B337_09:GA-06","CL_B337_09:GA-07","CH_B337_SILU:SD2","CH_B337_SIPC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:XD-03","text":"The category-table is generated not by analogy with the judgement-table but by the claimed reapplication of one identical unifying function across logical and sensible domains.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-04","CL_B337_09:GA-08","CL_B337_09:GA-09","CL_B337_09:GA-10","CH_B337_SIPC:AS4","CH_B337_SIPC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:XD-04","text":"General logic is simultaneously indispensable as the source of forms and insufficient as an account of their cognitive and object-directed use.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-05","CL_B337_09:GA-08","CL_B337_09:GA-11","CH_B337_SILF:AS3","CH_B337_SIPC:NX1","CH_B337_SIPC:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:XD-05","text":"The transition from logical form to category cannot mean that form supplies object-content; it must instead mean that form specifies a necessary condition for unifying independently supplied content.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-09","CL_B337_09:GA-11","CH_B337_SILF:SNX1","CH_B337_SIPC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:XD-06","text":"Negation becomes cognitively productive only as limitation and structured determination, not as an automatic increase in positive knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-05","CL_B337_09:GA-09","CL_B337_09:GA-11","CH_B337_SILF:A6","CH_B337_SIPC:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:XD-07","text":"Disjunctive exclusion supplies the logical schema for community by transforming an exhaustive partition of alternatives into reciprocal coordination within a whole.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-08","CL_B337_09:GA-09","CH_B337_SILF:A9","CH_B337_SIPC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:XD-08","text":"The migration of modality from a judgement’s standing before thought to categories of possibility, existence, and necessity introduces an object-directed claim whose full warrant is not supplied here.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-04","CL_B337_09:GA-09","CL_B337_09:GA-11","CH_B337_SILF:A10","CH_B337_SIPC:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:XD-09","text":"Primitive closure and derivative productivity are mutually enabling: fixing the original categories makes systematic expansion through predicables possible without category inflation.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-10","CL_B337_09:GA-12","CH_B337_SIPC:SD3","CH_B337_SIPC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as rule-governed unity across receptive, synthetic, and conceptual operations rather than as immediate givenness to a faculty.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-01","CL_B337_09:GA-02","CL_B337_09:GA-07","CL_B337_09:GA-09","CH_B337_SIPC:SD1","CH_B337_SIPC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSD-02","text":"The cognitive subject becomes a distributed functional circuit comprising receptivity, imagination, understanding, and judgement rather than a single undifferentiated power.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-02","CL_B337_09:GA-03","CL_B337_09:GA-07","CH_B337_SILU:A4","CH_B337_SIPC:A4","CH_B337_SIPC:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSD-03","text":"Completeness becomes genealogical rather than enumerative: a concept belongs to the primitive system only if its origin can be traced to the exhaustive functions of judgement.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-03","CL_B337_09:GA-04","CL_B337_09:GA-10","CH_B337_SILU:SD4","CH_B337_SIPC:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSD-04","text":"Abstraction from content is methodologically necessary for isolating form, but transcendental cognition requires that isolated form return to organize a priori sensible content.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-04","CL_B337_09:GA-06","CL_B337_09:GA-09","CL_B337_09:GA-11","CH_B337_SILF:A1","CH_B337_SIPC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSD-05","text":"Repeated exclusion becomes productive: denying immediate intuition, formal self-sufficiency, and arbitrary conceptual accumulation generates the positive architecture of mediated synthesis and categorical closure.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-01","CL_B337_09:GA-05","CL_B337_09:GA-11","CL_B337_09:GA-12","CH_B337_SILU:D1","CH_B337_SIPC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSD-06","text":"A finite transcendental kernel can generate an extensive a priori conceptual field while retaining a strict distinction between primitive and derivative concepts.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-10","CL_B337_09:GA-12","CH_B337_SIPC:SD3","CH_B337_SIPC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSD-07","text":"The logical table becomes a formal blueprint for speculative science while simultaneously restricting that science from treating cognitive forms as properties of things in themselves.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-08","CL_B337_09:GA-09","CL_B337_09:GA-10","CL_B337_09:GA-11","CH_B337_SIPC:SD5","CH_B337_SIPC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:BSD-08","text":"The system’s necessity is conditional rather than absolute because its terminal category-architecture inherits the unproved completeness and identity claims of its logical premises.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-08","CL_B337_09:GA-10","CH_B337_SIPC:MRT3","CH_B337_SILF:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:RLE-01","text":"Understanding is reduced to judgement, judgement is classified by understanding’s functions, and those functions return as the categories through which understanding thinks objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A15","CH_B337_SIPC:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RLE-02","text":"The principal escalation runs from denied intuition through conceptual mediation and logical classification to the synthetic unity of an a priori sensible manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AF1","CH_B337_SIPC:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RLE-03","text":"Unity is the dominant attractor: every transition converts a multiplicity of representations, judgements, or intuitions into a determinate whole under one function.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A6","CH_B337_SIPC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RLE-04","text":"The form-content loop abstracts content to expose pure form and then returns that form to content as its necessary condition of conceptual organization.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:AS1","CH_B337_SIPC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RLE-05","text":"Negation escalates from excluding intuition, to differentiating cognitive functions, to policing the boundary of the primitive category-system.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:NX1","CH_B337_SILF:DNX2","CH_B337_SIPC:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RLE-06","text":"Disjunction marks a phase change when exclusion among alternatives becomes the positive architecture of reciprocal inclusion within a completed whole.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A9","CH_B337_SIPC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:RLE-07","text":"Closure recursively produces expansion because the fixed category-table generates predicables while using their derivative status to preserve its own primitivity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A10","CH_B337_SIPC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:FGS-B01","text":"Non-sensuous understanding → exclusion of intellectual intuition → restriction to discursive cognition through conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AS1","CH_B337_SILU:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FGS-B02","text":"Receptive intuition → a priori manifold in space and time → material available for but not yet constituting object-cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A4","CH_B337_SIPC:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FGS-B03","text":"Conceptual mediation → unification under higher representations → judgement as the comprehensive operation of understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AS5","CH_B337_SILU:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FGS-B04","text":"Abstraction from judgement-content → isolation of intellectual form → fourfold classification of judgement-functions.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:AS1","CH_B337_SILF:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FGS-B05","text":"Comparison by cognitive contribution → differentiation beyond syllogistic equivalence → transcendental specification of logical forms.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A4","CH_B337_SILF:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FGS-B06","text":"Imaginative synthesis of manifold → conceptual representation of synthetic unity → identity of synthetic and judgemental function.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A4","CH_B337_SIPC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FGS-B07","text":"Judgement-table → finite category-table → distinction of primitive categories from derivative, sensible, empirical, and merely logical forms.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A8","CH_B337_SIPC:A10","CH_B337_SIPC:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:AFM-B01","text":"Intellectual intuition denied → understanding restricted to conceptual mediation.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:NX1","CH_B337_SILU:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AFM-B02","text":"Conceptual mediation → representations unified under higher representations → judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AS4","CH_B337_SILU:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AFM-B03","text":"Judgement as comprehensive act → abstraction from content → formal table of functions.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AS7","CH_B337_SILF:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AFM-B04","text":"Formal table → cognitive differentiation of its moments → transcendental reinterpretation of logical form.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:AS2","CH_B337_SILF:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AFM-B05","text":"Synthesis prior to analysis → identity of judgemental and synthetic unity → categories as conditions for thinking objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:AS3","CH_B337_SIPC:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:AFM-B06","text":"Category derivation → finite primitive closure → derivative expansion under ontological restraint.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A6","CH_B337_SIPC:SD4","CH_B337_SIPC:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:MME-01","text":"Cognition is remapped from a direct faculty-object relation into a coordinated architecture of representation, synthesis, and categorical unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:ME1","CH_B337_SIPC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MME-02","text":"Understanding is reclassified from a generic power of thought into the faculty whose judgemental functions supply the source-patterns of object-cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:ME2","CH_B337_SIPC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MME-03","text":"Concepts cease to appear as static contents and become functions for subsumption, synthesis, and determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:ME3","CH_B337_SIPC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MME-04","text":"Logic divides into formal analysis of already given representations and transcendental analysis of the conditions under which representations can constitute cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:ME5","CH_B337_SIPC:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MME-05","text":"Negation is differentiated into denial, limitative determination, and an internal categorical moment rather than treated as a single operation.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:ME2","CH_B337_SIPC:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MME-06","text":"The category-table becomes a topology that stratifies original categories, predicables, sensible forms, empirical concepts, and logical criteria.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:ME4","CH_B337_SIPC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MME-07","text":"Metaphysical inquiry is redirected from accumulation of concepts toward genealogical derivation, systematic placement, and control of category inflation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:ME3","CH_B337_SIPC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system classifies understanding, judgement, and the pure categories corresponding to judgemental forms.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:ALT1","CH_B337_SILF:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, object-cognition is produced by coordinating receptive intuition, imaginative synthesis, judgemental unity, and categorical determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:ALT2","CH_B337_SIPC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, systematic derivation displaces standard taxonomy wherever inferential convenience conceals differences in cognitive function.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:ALT3","CH_B337_SIPC:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological level, objects of possible cognition are inseparable from a priori conditions of intuition and unity, while things in themselves remain outside legitimate derivation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:ALT4","CH_B337_SIPC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system enacts its thesis by abstracting, tabulating, differentiating, and policing the very functions through which it claims cognition becomes systematic.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:AS1","CH_B337_SIPC:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:FL-01","text":"The completeness of the four headings is asserted as the ground of categorical completeness without an internal derivation of why those headings exhaust judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:MRT5","CH_B337_SIPC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FL-02","text":"The identity of the function unifying judgement and the function unifying intuition is indispensable but remains undemonstrated.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:MRT3","CH_B337_SIPC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FL-03","text":"Categories are claimed to apply a priori to objects before the full warrant and limits of that application are supplied.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FL-04","text":"The division of labour between imagination’s blind synthesis and understanding’s conceptual reduction is necessary to the system but mechanically under-specified.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:MRT5","CH_B337_SIPC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FL-05","text":"Form must first be abstracted from content yet later acquire transcendental relevance to a priori content, making separation and reintegration mutually necessary.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A1","CH_B337_SIPC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FL-06","text":"Third categories are simultaneously combinations of prior terms and primitive products of distinct intellectual acts, preventing simple derivation from explaining their status.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:MRT4","CH_B337_SIPC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:FL-07","text":"Modality changes from the copula’s standing before thought to an object-directed category-class without the transition being fully secured at this stage.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A10","CH_B337_SIPC:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MP-01","text":"Judgement mutates from the act mediating conceptual reference into a classified source of the forms governing possible object-cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:AS5","CH_B337_SIPC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MP-02","text":"Synthesis mutates from a blind imaginative operation into a unity representable through categories without ceasing to depend on imagination.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A4","CH_B337_SIPC:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MP-03","text":"Disjunction mutates from logical partition into reciprocal community, shifting from a relation among alternatives to a relation among substances.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:A9","CH_B337_SIPC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:HSI-01","text":"Reality as cognizable is implicitly modeled as a synthesis engine in which receptive multiplicity becomes an object only through recursively imposed functions of unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:SD1","CH_B337_SIPC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes a distributed relay rather than a sovereign spectator: sensibility receives, imagination joins, understanding conceptualizes, and judgement unifies.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:A4","CH_B337_SIPC:A4","CH_B337_SIPC:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:HSI-03","text":"Time is both an a priori condition of receptive manifold and distinct from the logical progression by which thought moves from possibility through actuality to necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A2","CH_B337_SILF:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:HSI-04","text":"Navigation within the system consists in tracing each candidate concept to its functional origin and assigning it to a primitive, derivative, sensible, empirical, or merely logical stratum.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:SD2","CH_B337_SIPC:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:HSI-05","text":"Escape from arbitrary metaphysical accumulation remains possible only through systematic self-limitation by a fixed but generative category-table.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:A9","CH_B337_SIPC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_09:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from the delimitation of understanding through judgemental classification to the category-system and its exclusions.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:MRT1","CH_B337_SILU:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined at the point where the fourfold judgement-table is treated as complete rather than merely fixed.","refs":["CH_B337_SILF:MRT5","CH_B337_SIPC:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale if content, logical relation, modal standing, and transcendental application remain analytically distinct.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:MRT4","CH_B337_SILF:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where judgement mediates conceptual cognition and then supplies the formal pattern for categorical synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:MRT5","CH_B337_SIPC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if it retains the recursive return from judgemental unity to the synthesis of the sensible manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_SILU:MRT7","CH_B337_SIPC:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_09:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable elements most resistant to axiomatic reduction are the unproved identity of judgement and synthesis and the underdetermined semantic content of the individual categories.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPC:MRT3","CH_B337_SIPC:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 25–27","context":"Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4 · Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5 · Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6"}},{"id":"CL_B337_10","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 28–30","order":103,"parents":["CH_B337_SIPT","CH_B337_TTDC","CH_B337_PCMR"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"b08b9b99bb90c9fab4e34198cd5eca26567532c594c5b40894a69ebd610b13a0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:AS-B01","text":"Possession of a pure conception creates no entitlement to employ it objectively; its right must be established independently of its historical or psychological acquisition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AS-B02","text":"Necessary correspondence between representation and object can be established only by showing how representation formally conditions an object’s cognizability, not by treating objects as empirical causes of a priori representation.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AS-B03","text":"Sensibility supplies the manifold and the forms of givenness, but cannot supply the conjunction through which a manifold is represented as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AS-B04","text":"Understanding supplies synthesis as the spontaneous operation that converts given multiplicity into representable objectual unity.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AS-B05","text":"Categories acquire objective validity only by functioning as necessary conditions under which experience can contain objects.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AS-B06","text":"Necessity remains irreducible to observed regularity, making causal connection a test case for the general demand that pure concepts be transcendentally warranted.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AS-B07","text":"The deduction’s explanatory movement does not terminate with categories, because judgmental and categorical unity presuppose a more fundamental unity enabling conjunction itself.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AS-B08","text":"The resulting closure is bounded and incomplete: reason is legitimate only within possible experience, while the higher ground of synthesis is functionally required but not yet characterized.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:SD3","CH_B337_PCMR:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] The factual possession or empirical origin of a concept never establishes its objective right of use.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] A pure conception is objectively valid only insofar as it is demonstrably necessary for the possibility of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Objectivity is lawful availability for possible experience rather than resemblance to, imprint by, or independent access to objects.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Cognition requires irreducible cooperation between intuition, through which an object is given, and conception, through which it is thought.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Sensibility validates the forms of givenness but remains insufficient for objecthood because it supplies no conjunction of the manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A5","CH_B337_PCMR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Synthesis is the understanding’s spontaneous and general operation through which conjunction and objectual representation become possible.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Categories are not abstract generalities or self-sufficient logical forms, but rules by which intuition is thinkable as an object within experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The unity represented in conjunction presupposes a prior unity that enables synthesis and cannot be reduced to the category of unity.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Empirical association, repetition, and induction cannot generate the necessity or strict universality claimed by pure cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The authority of categories is inseparable from their restriction to objects insofar as those objects belong to possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] The deduction is driven toward a higher qualitative unity that grounds logical understanding while remaining materially unspecified at this level.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A14","CH_B337_PCMR:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] The distinction between possessing a conception and possessing a right to use it remains the stable juridical operator across the cluster.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] The object shifts from what intuition merely gives as phenomenon to what synthesis represents as a conjoined object of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:AS3","CH_B337_PCMR:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RMD-03","text":"[Functional recurrence] Sensibility persists as the condition of givenness, but its role changes from an apparent model of validation to an explicitly insufficient source of conjunction.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A5","CH_B337_PCMR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RMD-04","text":"[Expansion] Synthesis recurs as spontaneous conjunction and expands from a local account of combination into the universal condition of objectual representation.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A4","CH_B337_PCMR:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Categories first appear as conditions for thinking objects, then become derivative relative to the conjunction their own judgmental operation presupposes.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A7","CH_B337_PCMR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RMD-06","text":"[Escalation] Necessity migrates from the causal example into the general criterion separating transcendental validity from empirical explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:AS5","CH_B337_TTDC:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RMD-07","text":"[Absorption] Analysis survives only as a derivative operation within a field already constituted by synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RMD-08","text":"[Terminal transformation] Categorical unity loses foundational status and is absorbed into a stratified architecture culminating in prior qualitative unity.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:D6","CH_B337_PCMR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:CGD-01","text":"The special validation of space and time through intuitive givenness remains a local contrast whose current-level function is to expose why categories require another proof-form.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:CGD-02","text":"The disputes named through Locke and Hume remain local historical formulations of a more general structural exclusion of empirical genesis as transcendental warrant.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:CGD-03","text":"The condition-of-possible-experience test scales upward as the cluster’s primary bridge between pure thought and objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:CGD-04","text":"The division between intuition and conception becomes current-level machinery only when joined to the division between received manifold and spontaneous synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A4","CH_B337_PCMR:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:CGD-05","text":"Prior unity functions as the principal catalytic transition because it converts the deduction from a proof of categories into a regression beneath categorical unity.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:CGD-06","text":"The category of unity becomes superseded as an ultimate foundation and recoded as a derivative logical function.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:CGD-07","text":"Causal necessity ceases to be merely an exemplary local problem and becomes the cluster-wide discriminating criterion of nonempirical validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:GNX-01","text":"Objective validity is not established by psychological, physiological, historical, or empirical derivation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GNX-02","text":"Necessary objectivity is not subjective expectation produced through repetition or habitual association.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GNX-03","text":"Conjunction is not delivered by sensory receptivity, the pure form of intuition, or objects themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GNX-04","text":"Logical judgment and the category of unity are not self-sufficient grounds of the original unity required for cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:NX6","CH_B337_PCMR:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GNX-05","text":"Categories do not create the existence of objects and therefore cannot be treated as acts of ontological production.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:GNX-06","text":"Legitimate a priori cognition is not unrestricted cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:DBN-01","text":"Subjective conditions of thinking cannot validate themselves as conditions that govern objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:DBN-02","text":"No object can be cognized as an object of experience independently of the a priori forms through which it is given and thought.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:DBN-03","text":"No transition from psychological regularity to objective necessity is available without a priori conditions of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:DBN-04","text":"No category acquires validity merely by being logically conceivable or empirically encountered.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:DBN-05","text":"The spontaneity of understanding cannot serve as an absolutely self-grounding source of its own possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:BSA-01","text":"No standpoint outside possible experience is available from which correspondence between categories and objects could be independently inspected.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:BSA-02","text":"No account of objectivity can rely on access to objects wholly independent of the conditions of their possible presentation.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:BSA-03","text":"No homogeneous or fully transparent foundation of unity is available, because synthetic, categorical, and higher qualitative unity remain distinct.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:DNX5","CH_B337_PCMR:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:BSA-04","text":"Neither dogmatic empirical extension nor skeptical restriction supplies the unavailable external guarantee of pure cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:XD-01","text":"Subject-originated synthesis can count as objective only when it is established as indispensable to the possibility of experience rather than merely described as an internal activity.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GA-02","CL_B337_10:GA-03","CL_B337_10:GA-06","CH_B337_TTDC:A8","CH_B337_PCMR:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:XD-02","text":"Givenness and synthesis form a noncompetitive division: sensibility supplies the material of experience, while understanding supplies the unity in which that material can count as an object.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GA-04","CL_B337_10:GA-05","CL_B337_10:GA-06","CH_B337_SIPT:A5","CH_B337_PCMR:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:XD-03","text":"Categories are necessary rules within object-cognition but cannot be the original source of the unity their own judgmental use presupposes.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GA-07","CL_B337_10:GA-08","CH_B337_TTDC:ME4","CH_B337_PCMR:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:XD-04","text":"Restriction to possible experience is the positive mechanism that grants categorial authority rather than a merely external prohibition imposed upon otherwise unlimited reason.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GA-02","CL_B337_10:GA-10","CH_B337_TTDC:D4","CH_B337_TTDC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:XD-05","text":"Empirical association and bare logical form fail for the same structural reason: neither establishes the necessary relation by which intuition becomes objectively determined as an object.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GA-03","CL_B337_10:GA-07","CL_B337_10:GA-09","CH_B337_TTDC:A9","CH_B337_TTDC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:XD-06","text":"The transcendental deduction becomes regressive because the attempt to ground categories forces ascent from categories to synthesis and from synthesis to a prior qualitative unity.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GA-06","CL_B337_10:GA-08","CL_B337_10:GA-11","CH_B337_PCMR:D7","CH_B337_PCMR:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:XD-07","text":"Causal necessity is converted from a local challenge to a diagnostic case for the general claim that objective necessity must arise from conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GA-02","CL_B337_10:GA-09","CH_B337_SIPT:AS5","CH_B337_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity becomes neither an imprint of independently known objects nor an arbitrary subjective projection, but lawful synthesis of what can be given in experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:SD1","CH_B337_PCMR:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:BSD-02","text":"The cognizing subject becomes receptive and spontaneous at once: it does not create object-existence, but it actively produces the unity under which givenness is cognizable as objectual.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:SD4","CH_B337_TTDC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:BSD-03","text":"Transcendental necessity has a constitutive-restrictive form: it is necessary within possible experience precisely because it has no licensed use outside that domain.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:SD3","CH_B337_TTDC:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:BSD-04","text":"Concepts are transformed from inherited possessions into jurisdictionally regulated rules whose legitimacy depends on their role in structuring possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:ME1","CH_B337_TTDC:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:BSD-05","text":"Critical method becomes an ascending regression from objectual experience to the synthesis and prior unity that make any objectual experience possible.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:BSD-06","text":"The architecture produces an open closure: it establishes the limits of categorial validity while leaving the internal constitution of its deepest unifying ground unresolved.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:RLE-01","text":"The juridical loop runs from possession of a pure conception through demand for objective right to the possible-experience test that limits its authorized use.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A1","CH_B337_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RLE-02","text":"The constitutive loop runs from manifold givenness through spontaneous synthesis to represented objecthood, which then supplies the field in which categories can be validated.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A1","CH_B337_PCMR:FGS4","CH_B337_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RLE-03","text":"The regressive loop runs from categorical judgment through conjunction to prior unity, then returns by making that unity the condition of logical understanding itself.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A13","CH_B337_PCMR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RLE-04","text":"Analysis returns to synthesis as a derivative operation, thereby confirming that decomposition cannot supply the origin of the unity it presupposes.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RLE-05","text":"The necessity-path escalates from the failure of empirical regularity to the demand that objective necessity be grounded in conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A9","CH_B337_TTDC:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:RLE-06","text":"Higher qualitative unity operates as the central attractor because every attempt to terminate explanation in categories or spontaneous understanding reopens the question of their enabling unity.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:FGS-B01","text":"A claimed pure conception is separated from its mere possession, making objective right rather than factual origin the problem to be solved.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:FGS-B02","text":"Empirical genesis and empirical affection are excluded because neither can yield the a priori necessity claimed by pure cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A4","CH_B337_TTDC:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:FGS-B03","text":"Necessary representation-object correspondence requires formal conditions through which an object can be possible for cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:FGS-B04","text":"Sensibility provides the manifold of intuition but cannot provide its conjunction.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A1","CH_B337_PCMR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:FGS-B05","text":"Understanding performs conjunction as synthesis, enabling the manifold to be represented as conjoined in an object.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A5","CH_B337_PCMR:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:FGS-B06","text":"Categories become objectively valid when their role as a priori conditions for thinking objects in possible experience is demonstrated.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A7","CH_B337_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:FGS-B07","text":"Categorical unity is displaced by the prior unity required for conjunction, causing the deduction to regress beneath the categories it seeks to legitimate.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:D6","CH_B337_PCMR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:FGS-B08","text":"The machine closes by limiting valid cognition to possible experience while preserving the higher ground of unity as an unresolved condition of its own operation.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:NX7","CH_B337_PCMR:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:AFM-B01","text":"Possession of pure concepts → demand for juridical right of objective employment.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AFM-B02","text":"Demand for necessary correspondence → rejection of empirical affection as the source of a priori validity.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AFM-B03","text":"Intuitive givenness → exposure of the manifold’s inability to provide conjunction.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AFM-B04","text":"Missing conjunction → spontaneous synthesis by understanding → representation of an object as conjoined.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AFM-B05","text":"Synthesis of objecthood → categories as conditions under which possible experience contains objects.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:AFM-B06","text":"Categorical validity → regression to prior unity → bounded but unfinished critical closure.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:D6","CH_B337_TTDC:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:MME-01","text":"Concepts are reclassified by the difference between their origin, possession, and authorized objective use.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MME-02","text":"Objectivity is relocated from resemblance and association to the necessary structure of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MME-03","text":"Objectual unity is reclassified as dependent on subjective synthesis without reducing the received manifold to subjective production.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MME-04","text":"Analysis is recoded as a secondary operation that works within, rather than grounds, a previously synthesized field.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MME-05","text":"The category of unity is displaced from ultimate foundation to derivative logical function.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MME-06","text":"The apparent choice between unrestricted metaphysics and total skepticism is dissolved by making legitimacy coextensive with possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the cluster asks whether categories can possess objective validity as conceptions determining objects through judgment-functions.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, experience is constituted through the unity of sensible givenness and conceptual object-determination under a priori conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, empirical genealogy is replaced by transcendental deduction, which avoids both dogmatic extension and skeptical reduction.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, objects are cognizable only as phenomena ordered under conditions of possible experience, while necessity cannot be extracted from successive observation.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the argument enacts its thesis by reversing explanation from given objects and analysis toward the conditions of synthesis and unity.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:FL-01","text":"Representation can make an object possible for cognition without producing its existence, preserving a tension between constitutive cognition and nonproductive ontology.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:FL-02","text":"Categories must apply wherever objects are thought, yet the category of unity cannot ground the original conjunction required for their own operation.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A7","CH_B337_PCMR:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:FL-03","text":"Understanding is spontaneous in producing conjunction but dependent on a unity prior to the activity whose possibility it enables.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:FL-04","text":"Intuition can give phenomena without proving their conformity to understanding, while cognition nevertheless requires the conditions of understanding for objective unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:A7","CH_B337_SIPT:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:FL-05","text":"The cluster demands a deduction of categories while leaving both the relation of judgment-functions to the full categorial system and the nature of higher unity compressed.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:MRT5","CH_B337_PCMR:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MP-01","text":"The inquiry mutates from a question concerning the genesis of representations into a question concerning their rightful objective reference.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:D1","CH_B337_TTDC:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MP-02","text":"Conjunction mutates from an apparent feature of sensory or objectual givenness into a spontaneous act attributable to understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:ME1","CH_B337_PCMR:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MP-03","text":"Unity mutates into a stratified architecture of represented synthetic unity, categorical unity, and prior qualitative unity.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MP-04","text":"Categories mutate from apparent foundations of objectivity into derivative rules whose legitimacy depends on a more primitive synthetic order.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:ME4","CH_B337_PCMR:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a layered field in which sensibility receives a manifold while understanding produces the conjunction under which the manifold can count as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:HSI-02","text":"The subject is neither passive receiver nor sovereign creator, but a spontaneous yet transcendentally dependent site of synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:HSI-03","text":"Categories function as jurisdictional rules for cognizability rather than as powers that generate the existence of the objects they order.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:HSI-04","text":"Time and succession do not disclose necessity by themselves; necessity belongs to the conditions under which temporal appearances can be objectively unified.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:HSI-05","text":"Critical navigation consists in determining the lawful scope of cognition rather than seeking an unrestricted exit from the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:ME4","CH_B337_TTDC:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_10:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to reconstruct the movement from the problem of objective validity through synthesis to the demand for a prior unity of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:MRT1","CH_B337_PCMR:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where it shows that empirical occurrence cannot establish necessity and that objectual conjunction depends on prior synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:MRT4","CH_B337_PCMR:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined regarding the full relation between judgment-functions and categories and the internal constitution of higher qualitative unity.","refs":["CH_B337_TTDC:MRT5","CH_B337_PCMR:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence is preserved only if unity is stratified and the distinction between an object’s existence and its possibility for cognition is retained.","refs":["CH_B337_PCMR:MRT3","CH_B337_TTDC:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_10:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining the recursive transition from juridical demand to transcendental condition and the deferred completion of the deduction.","refs":["CH_B337_SIPT:MRT3","CH_B337_SIPT:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 28–30","context":"Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9 · Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10 · Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11."}},{"id":"CL_B337_11","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 31–33","order":104,"parents":["CH_B337_OSUA","CH_B337_PSUA","CH_B337_WOUS"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"e51090d164c0585f5f14f60da4c2d6698abcadfb02158729ff2e054d31cd64ce","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:AS-B01","text":"A manifold given through intuition poses the problem of how heterogeneous representations can belong to one subject and become cognizable.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:AS1","CH_B337_PSUA:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AS-B02","text":"The “I think” is reconstructed as the formal possibility of representational belonging rather than as an empirical report of accompanying consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A1","CH_B337_OSUA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AS-B03","text":"Synthesis is the operation that converts a merely given manifold into a unity available to thought, conception, and object-relation.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A9","CH_B337_PSUA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AS-B04","text":"The understanding supplies the spontaneous conjunction that neither sensibility, empirical association, nor objects can supply from themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A10","CH_B337_WOUS:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AS-B05","text":"Objectivity is generated where the unified manifold is necessarily related to one consciousness, not where representations happen to coexist empirically.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A6","CH_B337_WOUS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AS-B06","text":"Empirical unity is derivative, contingent, and phenomenally restricted because it presupposes the pure synthesis that makes association intelligible.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A7","CH_B337_WOUS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AS-B07","text":"The system makes synthetical unity the highest principle of human understanding while restricting its necessity to finite cognition dependent on received intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A11","CH_B337_PSUA:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AS-B08","text":"Its movement is recursive: receptively given manifold requires synthesis; synthesis presupposes apperceptive unity; apperceptive unity becomes effective only in synthesizing a manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:SD6","CH_B337_OSUA:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Representation belongs to a subject only insofar as it can stand under the possible accompaniment of one identical self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A1","CH_B337_PSUA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Intuition supplies a manifold, but manifold givenness is not yet cognition, objecthood, or conceptual unity.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A2","CH_B337_PSUA:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Synthetic conjunction is an a priori activity of understanding rather than a relation precontained in objects, perception, or empirical consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A9","CH_B337_OSUA:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The “I think” functions first as the formal condition of subjective belonging and then as the condition through which a manifold can relate to an object.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:SD5","CH_B337_WOUS:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Objectivity is the necessary validity generated by synthesis under transcendental apperception, not an intrinsic feature of independently given representations.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:SD2","CH_B337_WOUS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Analytical identity presupposes synthetical unity because consciousness can be identical across representations only where they are conjoinable.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A8","CH_B337_OSUA:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Empirical consciousness is a derivative field of contingent association whose subjective unity presupposes transcendental synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A5","CH_B337_WOUS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The unity of apperception organizes concept-formation, object-relation, objective validity, logic, and all exercise of human understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:AS6","CH_B337_PSUA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Human cognition is finite because thought synthesizes a manifold received through sensibility rather than intuiting or generating that manifold itself.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A14","CH_B337_PSUA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The principle is universally binding for human cognition without being a law of every conceivable understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:SD6","CH_B337_PSUA:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:RMD-01","text":"The manifold recurs with stable identity as the material of cognition, but its function changes from a problem of subjective ownership to the material condition of objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A2","CH_B337_PSUA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:RMD-02","text":"The “I think” mutates from a condition of representational accompaniment into a formal operator of object-relation and objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A1","CH_B337_PSUA:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:RMD-03","text":"Synthesis expands from the conjunction required for self-identity into the universal condition of cognition and then into the ground of empirical association.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:SD1","CH_B337_WOUS:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:RMD-04","text":"Unity reverses valence from an apparently subjective feature of consciousness into the objective condition under which objects and universal validity are possible.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:ME1","CH_B337_WOUS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:RMD-05","text":"Empirical consciousness persists only after being subordinated: it is retained as phenomenal association but stripped of foundational authority.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A6","CH_B337_WOUS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:CGD-01","text":"The distinction between pure and empirical apperception is local when it merely separates two kinds of consciousness, but becomes global when it grounds the hierarchy of necessary and contingent validity.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:NX2","CH_B337_WOUS:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:CGD-02","text":"The forms of space and time remain local conditions of sensuous givenness; they scale upward only as evidence that objectivity requires a condition beyond intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A1","CH_B337_PSUA:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:CGD-03","text":"The understanding’s spontaneous conjunction bridges all units because it connects subject-identity, concept-formation, object-relation, and empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A10","CH_B337_WOUS:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:CGD-04","text":"The contrast with intuitive understanding functions as a terminal boundary rather than a positive model of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A12","CH_B337_PSUA:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:CGD-05","text":"Contingent associations between words and things are locally illustrative, but their current-level function is to demonstrate that empirical linkage cannot yield universal objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A10","CH_B337_WOUS:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:GNX-01","text":"No representation counts as the subject’s representation if it cannot be related to the “I think.”","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:NX1","CH_B337_PSUA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GNX-02","text":"No merely given manifold, spatial form, or temporal sequence is sufficient to constitute cognition or an object.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A8","CH_B337_WOUS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GNX-03","text":"No object can supply the unity through which it is represented as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:NX6","CH_B337_WOUS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GNX-04","text":"No accumulation of empirical associations can transform subjective contingency into necessary and universal validity.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:DNX2","CH_B337_WOUS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:GNX-05","text":"No human understanding can abolish its finitude by treating thought as self-intuiting or self-manifolding.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:NX8","CH_B337_PSUA:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:DBN-01","text":"The system excludes any account in which the subject is the aggregate, sequence, or empirical inventory of its representations.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:SNX1","CH_B337_OSUA:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:DBN-02","text":"The system excludes any account in which objectivity precedes the synthesizing relation that makes a manifold objectively available.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:SNX5","CH_B337_WOUS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:DBN-03","text":"The system excludes any reduction of necessary unity to psychological occurrence, private association, or temporal coexistence.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:SNX6","CH_B337_WOUS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:BSA-01","text":"The unavailable possibility structuring the system is unmediated cognition: for finite knowers, neither self nor object can be present without synthetic mediation.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:SNX1","CH_B337_WOUS:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:BSA-02","text":"The system cannot admit a world composed solely of contingent streams of consciousness without collapsing the distinction between subjective association and objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:SNX1","CH_B337_WOUS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:BSA-03","text":"The ultimate condition of objective unity cannot appear as one empirical item among the objects it makes possible.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:SNX4","CH_B337_WOUS:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:XD-01","text":"The relation of representations to one consciousness is not merely a condition of self-ascription; it is the mechanism by which a manifold acquires relation to an object.","refs":["CL_B337_11:GA-01","CL_B337_11:GA-04","CH_B337_OSUA:D2","CH_B337_WOUS:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:XD-02","text":"Objective validity arises neither from intuition alone nor from self-consciousness considered apart from intuition, but from their ordered conjunction through understanding.","refs":["CL_B337_11:GA-02","CL_B337_11:GA-03","CL_B337_11:GA-05","CH_B337_OSUA:SD2","CH_B337_PSUA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:XD-03","text":"The distinction between transcendental and empirical unity converts the earlier claim that synthesis grounds identity into a criterion distinguishing necessity from contingency.","refs":["CL_B337_11:GA-06","CL_B337_11:GA-07","CH_B337_OSUA:D3","CH_B337_WOUS:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:XD-04","text":"The dependency of empirical synthesis on pure synthesis prevents empirical association from serving as either the origin or proof of objectivity.","refs":["CL_B337_11:GA-05","CL_B337_11:GA-07","CH_B337_WOUS:D5","CH_B337_WOUS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:XD-05","text":"The highest principle of understanding is at once architectonic and restrictive: it enables cognition precisely by marking cognition as finite and non-intuitive.","refs":["CL_B337_11:GA-08","CL_B337_11:GA-09","CL_B337_11:GA-10","CH_B337_PSUA:A7","CH_B337_PSUA:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:BSD-01","text":"The subject becomes a formally identical site of synthesis rather than a substance whose contents independently guarantee its identity.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:SD5","CH_B337_OSUA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:BSD-02","text":"Objectivity becomes a transcendental achievement of necessary unification rather than a property extracted from empirical representations or their agreement.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:SD3","CH_B337_WOUS:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:BSD-03","text":"The phenomenal world becomes intelligible as a derivative order in which contingent empirical syntheses enact, without grounding, the formal conditions of objective unity.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:SD4","CH_B337_WOUS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:BSD-04","text":"Human finitude is not an accidental limitation added to cognition; it is the structural difference between receptive givenness and spontaneous synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:ME6","CH_B337_PSUA:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:BSD-05","text":"The system converts the question of what objects are into the prior question of what makes an object-relation possible for a finite consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:ME2","CH_B337_PSUA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:RLE-01","text":"The primary recursive loop runs from manifold to synthesis, from synthesis to unity of consciousness, and from that unity back to the manifold as the subject’s or object’s manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:FGS1","CH_B337_OSUA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:RLE-02","text":"The system escalates from subjective belonging to conceptual unity, from conceptual unity to object-relation, and from object-relation to objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:D4","CH_B337_PSUA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:RLE-03","text":"The distinction between pure and empirical unity forms a derivational loop in which empirical association depends on pure synthesis while furnishing the phenomenal field in which that dependence is enacted.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:FGS2","CH_B337_WOUS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:RLE-04","text":"Synthetical unity is the dominant attractor because each apparent alternative—empirical consciousness, object-givenness, association, and formal identity—returns to it as its condition.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:SD4","CH_B337_WOUS:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:RLE-05","text":"The intuitive-understanding contrast interrupts rather than completes the recursive machine by fixing a limit where synthesis would no longer be required.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A12","CH_B337_PSUA:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:FGS-B01","text":"Receptive intuition provides a manifold under space and time without thereby producing cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A1","CH_B337_PSUA:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:FGS-B02","text":"Possible relation to the “I think” makes the manifold eligible to count as representation for one subject.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A1","CH_B337_OSUA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:FGS-B03","text":"Understanding performs spontaneous synthesis, conjoining the manifold under one formal self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A3","CH_B337_OSUA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:FGS-B04","text":"Synthetic unity makes analytical identity, concept-formation, and the belonging of representations to one consciousness possible.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A8","CH_B337_OSUA:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:FGS-B05","text":"The unified manifold becomes related to an object and thereby gains objective validity as cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:A4","CH_B337_PSUA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:FGS-B06","text":"Empirical association derives from this pure synthesis but retains only contingent and subjective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A7","CH_B337_WOUS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:AFM-B01","text":"Manifold givenness under sensibility exposes the insufficiency of mere reception for cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:AS2","CH_B337_PSUA:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AFM-B02","text":"The insufficiency of reception requires a condition under which representations can belong to one consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:AS1","CH_B337_OSUA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AFM-B03","text":"Formal self-consciousness requires synthesis because identity cannot arise from a succession or inventory of representations.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A7","CH_B337_OSUA:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AFM-B04","text":"Synthesis is assigned to understanding and detached from both object-givenness and empirical association.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A9","CH_B337_WOUS:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AFM-B05","text":"Synthesis under apperception transforms a manifold into an object-related unity with objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A1","CH_B337_WOUS:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AFM-B06","text":"Objective unity grounds empirical unity while restricting the latter to contingent phenomenal association.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:A9","CH_B337_WOUS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:AFM-B07","text":"The resulting architecture defines human cognition as universally structured yet irreducibly finite.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:SD6","CH_B337_PSUA:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:MME-01","text":"The subject is reclassified from an empirical bearer of experiences into the formal unity through which experiences can count as belonging together.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:ME4","CH_B337_OSUA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:MME-02","text":"The object is reclassified from an independently received manifold into a manifold unified for consciousness under necessary synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:ME2","CH_B337_WOUS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:MME-03","text":"The opposition between subjective consciousness and objective cognition is redrawn: objectivity is not outside consciousness but depends on its transcendental unity.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:ME1","CH_B337_WOUS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:MME-04","text":"The opposition between empiricism and formalism is displaced by a composite architecture in which sensible reception and spontaneous synthesis are jointly necessary but non-interchangeable.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:SD2","CH_B337_PSUA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The explicit field consists of intuitions, representations, the “I think,” objects, and the distinction between empirical and pure consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:ALT1","CH_B337_WOUS:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Sensibility gives a manifold, understanding synthesizes it, and apperception supplies the unity under which the synthesis can count as cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:ALT2","CH_B337_OSUA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — The explanatory order is reversed so that identity, objects, and empirical unity are treated as effects of synthesis rather than as its sources.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:ALT3","CH_B337_WOUS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Time and space structure givenness, but temporal succession and coexistence become objectively valid only through apperceptive synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:ALT4","CH_B337_WOUS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The argument performs transcendental reconstruction by repeatedly withdrawing explanatory authority from empirical facts and relocating it in conditions of possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:AS2","CH_B337_OSUA:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:FL-01","text":"The principle is stated as analytical although its explanatory force depends on synthetical activity, requiring a distinction between its formal expression and operative function.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:AS5","CH_B337_PSUA:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:FL-02","text":"Primitive unity and the synthetic act are mutually required, but the transition from the unity that grounds synthesis to the synthesis that enacts unity remains formally compressed.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:MRT6","CH_B337_OSUA:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:FL-03","text":"Objectivity is grounded in the subject’s transcendental activity, yet the relation between that activity and objects is specified formally rather than demonstrated through determinate rules.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:MRT5","CH_B337_OSUA:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:MP-01","text":"The “I think” mutates from a condition of possible accompaniment into an operator of objectivity without becoming a content-bearing representation.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A1","CH_B337_OSUA:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:MP-02","text":"The term unity changes level from formal self-identity, to synthetic conjunction, to objective validity, and the architecture depends on preserving these non-identical functions.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:D3","CH_B337_WOUS:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a field of manifold givenness that becomes an ordered world only through necessary operations of unification.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:SD4","CH_B337_WOUS:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:HSI-02","text":"The subject appears as a non-substantial formal relay that receives no manifold from itself but binds received multiplicity into accountable unity.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:A13","CH_B337_OSUA:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:HSI-03","text":"Time appears as a form of empirical succession whose objective order cannot be read directly from succession itself.","refs":["CH_B337_WOUS:AS4","CH_B337_WOUS:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:HSI-04","text":"Cognitive struggle appears as navigation of a boundary: synthesis makes objectivity possible while preventing finite understanding from claiming intuitive self-possession.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:SD5","CH_B337_PSUA:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_11:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from manifold givenness through synthesis to objective unity and the derivation of empirical unity.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:MRT1","CH_B337_WOUS:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where it establishes that the same unity required for self-consciousness is also required for object-relation and objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_PSUA:AS4","CH_B337_WOUS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:MRT-B03","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined at the point where pure synthesis must become determinate conceptual rule without a derivation of the specific forms of conjunction.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:MRT8","CH_B337_OSUA:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_11:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if formal identity, performed synthesis, and objective unity are held apart as recursively connected levels rather than collapsed into one meaning of unity.","refs":["CH_B337_OSUA:MRT3","CH_B337_PSUA:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 31–33","context":"Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12 · The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13 · What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14"}},{"id":"CL_B337_12","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 34–36","order":105,"parents":["CH_B337_LFAJ","CH_B337_ASIA","CH_B337_OBS1"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"a7bfb22a06c96e31e56ec4de3179176d06d12134846936eeba12a1f5441e66a4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:AS-B01","text":"Judgment is reconstructed as the operation through which representations acquire objective unity rather than as a static relation among conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AS-B02","text":"The unity required for judgment, empirical intuition, and object-cognition is traced to original synthetical apperception.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:AS2","CH_B337_OBS1:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AS-B03","text":"Categories emerge as judgment-functions insofar as they determine sensible manifold for unified consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A5","CH_B337_ASIA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AS-B04","text":"Objectivity requires neither mere receptive givenness nor free intellectual production, but synthesis of independently given manifold under apperceptive unity.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A1","CH_B337_OBS1:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AS-B05","text":"The argument moves recursively from logical form to intuitive unity and returns from intuitive unity to the validity of logical categories.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:D2","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AS-B06","text":"The deduction establishes the necessity and scope of categorical synthesis without deriving the ultimate origin, exact number, or human specificity of the cognitive forms involved.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:D7","CH_B337_OBS1:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AS-B07","text":"Its closure is conditional: categories are valid for objects of sense because finite cognition can encounter objects only through the conjunction of receptivity and spontaneous synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:D5","CH_B337_OBS1:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Judgment is the act by which given cognitions are placed under objective unity of apperception.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The categories are logical functions of judgment transformed into determinations of intuitive manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Objective cognition requires the irreducible conjunction of independently given manifold and a priori synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] The unity of judgment, the unity of empirical intuition, and the unity imposed by categories are different articulations of one apperceptive synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:SD3","CH_B337_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Objective validity depends on conjunction in the object rather than associative co-presence conditioned by the empirical subject.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A9","CH_B337_LFAJ:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Logical form ceases to be merely classificatory when it becomes the rule through which intuition is unified as experience.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:ME2","CH_B337_OBS1:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] Categorical validity is valid only for finite discursive cognition whose understanding orders material received through sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A8","CH_B337_OBS1:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Transcendental deduction justifies the necessary role of cognitive forms without explaining why those forms exist in their exact human configuration.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:SD5","CH_B337_OBS1:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Apperception is neither an empirical observer nor a producer of material content, but the formal principle through which synthesis can count as objectively unified cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:SD3","CH_B337_OBS1:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:RMD-01","text":"Apperception retains stable identity as necessary unity, but its function expands from grounding objective judgment to governing the unity of all empirical intuition and object-cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A6","CH_B337_ASIA:A2","CH_B337_OBS1:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:RMD-02","text":"Judgment mutates from the site of objective predication into the operative bridge by which categories determine sensible manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A4","CH_B337_ASIA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:RMD-03","text":"Categories migrate from logical functions to constitutive rules of empirical consciousness, thereby absorbing the problem of their applicability to intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A5","CH_B337_OBS1:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:RMD-04","text":"The object reverses from a presumed external ground of valid predication into a cognition-available unity constituted through givenness plus synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:SNX1","CH_B337_OBS1:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:RMD-05","text":"Sensibility loses the status of a potentially self-unifying domain and becomes the irreducible source of manifold that synthesis must organize but cannot generate.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:NX2","CH_B337_OBS1:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:RMD-06","text":"Associative imagination performs a terminal discriminative function: it remains necessary as the excluded alternative through which objective synthesis becomes legible.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:NX5","CH_B337_LFAJ:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:CGD-01","text":"The insufficiency of defining judgment as a relation between conceptions remains local until it is recoded as evidence that logical form requires transcendental realization in intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A1","CH_B337_ASIA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:CGD-02","text":"The plurality of categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive judgment-forms remains a local logical correction rather than a current-level generative principle.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A2","CH_B337_LFAJ:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:CGD-03","text":"The identity between apperceptive synthesis and judgment-function scales upward because it connects the logical, sensible, and objective registers of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:MRT2","CH_B337_ASIA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between independently given manifold and synthesized object-unity becomes a transition mechanism linking the theory of intuition to the theory of objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A1","CH_B337_OBS1:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:CGD-05","text":"The unexplained origin and number of categories become current-level boundary conditions rather than defects in the deduction’s functional argument.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:FGS9","CH_B337_OBS1:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:GNX-01","text":"Objective unity is not empirical co-occurrence, subjective association, or repetition of perceptual conjunction.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:NX4","CH_B337_LFAJ:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GNX-02","text":"Sensuous manifold is not self-unifying; mere givenness cannot produce one consciousness or one empirical intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:NX1","CH_B337_ASIA:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GNX-03","text":"Categories are not externally appended concepts, empirical classifications, or merely abstract forms detached from intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:NX3","CH_B337_ASIA:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GNX-04","text":"Finite understanding is not an intuitive intellect and cannot generate either its objects or the manifold from which cognition begins.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:NX3","CH_B337_OBS1:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:GNX-05","text":"Transcendental validity is not an explanation of the genesis, number, or ultimate ground of the categories.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:NX7","CH_B337_OBS1:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:DBN-01","text":"The system excludes a model of cognition in which independently given content and objective unity are interchangeable or reducible to one faculty.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:DNX7","CH_B337_OBS1:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:DBN-02","text":"The system excludes an object wholly constituted before cognition and an object wholly created by cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:DBN-03","text":"The system excludes a self-sufficient formal logic that determines judgment without accounting for the synthesis of representations in intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:DNX3","CH_B337_LFAJ:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:DBN-04","text":"The system excludes a pure understanding that knows without receptivity, as well as a sensibility that unifies without spontaneous determination.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:DNX1","CH_B337_ASIA:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:BSA-01","text":"What remains unavailable is a standpoint from which cognition could completely explain the source of the conditions that make cognition possible.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:SNX6","CH_B337_OBS1:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:BSA-02","text":"The system is organized around the impossibility of immediate objectivity: no object can be cognitively present without mediation by synthesis, yet synthesis cannot supply its own material ground.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:DNX2","CH_B337_OBS1:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:BSA-03","text":"An intuitive understanding functions as the excluded possibility that defines finite cognition by its enduring asymmetry between receptivity and spontaneity.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:AS6","CH_B337_OBS1:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:XD-01","text":"The criterion of objective judgment and the condition for unified intuition are the same operation viewed at different scales: categorical synthesis under apperception.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-01","CL_B337_12:GA-02","CL_B337_12:GA-04","CH_B337_LFAJ:A4","CH_B337_ASIA:A5","CH_B337_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:XD-02","text":"The applicability of categories cannot be a secondary correspondence between thought and intuition because categorical determination is already constitutive of there being one empirical intuition.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-02","CL_B337_12:GA-04","CH_B337_ASIA:SD4","CH_B337_ASIA:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:XD-03","text":"Objectivity is produced neither by an object independent of cognitive conditions nor by an empirical subject’s associative experience, but by a relation that preserves both givenness and necessary synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-03","CL_B337_12:GA-05","CH_B337_LFAJ:SNX1","CH_B337_LFAJ:A10","CH_B337_OBS1:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:XD-04","text":"The transcendental deduction converts the formal problem of judgment into a restricted ontology of objects of sense: objects are knowable only as apperceptively unified manifolds.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-01","CL_B337_12:GA-06","CL_B337_12:GA-07","CH_B337_OBS1:ME1","CH_B337_OBS1:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:XD-05","text":"The system gains necessity by bracketing empirical genesis, but this same abstraction installs an irreducible explanatory remainder at its limit.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-08","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS5","CH_B337_OBS1:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:BSD-01","text":"The cluster converts objectivity from a property discovered in representations into a rule-governed achievement of their synthetic unification.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-01","CL_B337_12:GA-03","CL_B337_12:GA-05","CH_B337_LFAJ:ME2","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:BSD-02","text":"Logical form has transcendental force only when it is read as an operative rule of synthesis rather than as an inventory of propositional types.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-02","CL_B337_12:GA-06","CH_B337_LFAJ:ME4","CH_B337_ASIA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:BSD-03","text":"Human experience is structurally co-constituted: receptivity supplies what thought cannot produce, while thought supplies unity that receptivity cannot furnish.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-03","CL_B337_12:GA-07","CH_B337_OBS1:ME4","CH_B337_OBS1:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:BSD-04","text":"The system establishes a transcendental necessity that is epistemically finite, because its validity is inseparable from the architecture of human sensibility and discursive understanding.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-07","CL_B337_12:GA-08","CH_B337_OBS1:SD4","CH_B337_OBS1:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:BSD-05","text":"The formal condition of objective knowledge includes its own opacity: the categories are necessary for experience while their exact source remains non-deducible.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-08","CL_B337_12:GA-09","CH_B337_OBS1:A12","CH_B337_OBS1:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:RLE-01","text":"Given manifold → synthesis → relation to apperception → unity in consciousness → objectivity → renewed validation of the categories forms the principal recursive loop.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:FGS1","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS3","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:RLE-02","text":"Judgment becomes a conceptual attractor because it begins as a theory of objective predication and ends as the functional form of categorical determination.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A4","CH_B337_ASIA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:RLE-03","text":"The argument escalates from rejecting subjective association to reconstructing the constitution of empirical objects.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A10","CH_B337_OBS1:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:RLE-04","text":"The contrast between finite and intuitive understanding operates as a limit-loop: a nonhuman alternative clarifies the finite conditions while confirming their non-derivability.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:AS6","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:RLE-05","text":"Explanation repeatedly terminates in facticity: the system can derive functional necessity from unity but cannot derive the original possession of the unifying forms themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:D7","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:FGS-B01","text":"A manifold is given independently of the understanding and therefore cannot yet count as an object or unified cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:FGS-B02","text":"Reproductive association is excluded because it can report subjective conjunction without determining objective unity.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:FGS-B03","text":"Understanding synthesizes the given manifold by referring it to original apperception.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:FGS-B04","text":"This syntheses’ logical form is judgment, and its sensible determination is categorical.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:AS3","CH_B337_ASIA:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:FGS-B05","text":"The coincidence of categorical unity with the unity required by empirical intuition yields an object available for finite cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A10","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:FGS-B06","text":"The deduction validates this structure for objects of sense while preserving the non-deducible facticity of the forms through which it operates.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A11","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:AFM-B01","text":"Inadequate formal definition of judgment → demand for a criterion of objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AFM-B02","text":"Objective validity → necessary unity under original apperception rather than subjective association.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A6","CH_B337_LFAJ:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AFM-B03","text":"Necessary apperceptive unity → judgment as the act of unifying manifold representations.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A3","CH_B337_ASIA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AFM-B04","text":"Judgment-function applied to intuition → category as rule of sensible determination.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A5","CH_B337_OBS1:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AFM-B05","text":"Categorical determination → unity in consciousness and object-availability.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A7","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AFM-B06","text":"Identity of sensible and categorical unity → a priori validity for all objects of sense.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:FGS6","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:AFM-B07","text":"Restriction to finite discursive cognition → explanatory termination in the irreducible facticity of its forms.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:ME5","CH_B337_OBS1:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:MME-01","text":"Judgment is reclassified as a productive operation of synthesis rather than a relation already available among fixed conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:MME-02","text":"The categories are reclassified as conditions of possible unified experience rather than optional conceptual interpretations of independently unified intuitions.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:SD1","CH_B337_ASIA:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:MME-03","text":"The subject-object distinction is redrawn as the difference between subjective association and objective unity constituted through apperceptive synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:SD3","CH_B337_LFAJ:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:MME-04","text":"The boundary between sensibility and understanding becomes functional dependence rather than absolute separation.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:MME-05","text":"The limit of explanation becomes internal to the map: transcendental necessity is demonstrable even where the origin of the relevant forms remains unexplained.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:ME6","CH_B337_OBS1:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: a manifold becomes an objectively cognized object only through synthesis under self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: sensibility supplies manifold, understanding synthesizes it categorically, and apperception supplies the principle of necessary unity.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: validity is established by isolating the formal contribution of understanding and reconnecting it to the unity required by sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological and temporal level: experience is an ordered unity of prior givenness and a priori determination rather than a pre-unified empirical field.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:ALT4","CH_B337_ASIA:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological level: the deduction proceeds through abstraction from empirical genesis, functional reconstruction of unity, and restriction of the result to finite cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:AS3","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:FL-01","text":"Objectivity is defined through independence from the empirical subject while being grounded in the unity of self-consciousness, producing a constitutive tension between transcendental subjectivity and object-validity.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:MRT5","CH_B337_OBS1:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:FL-02","text":"The manifold is said to be independently given, yet the object is available only through a unity produced by synthesis, leaving unsettled whether synthesis constitutes objecthood or only cognition of an antecedent object.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:MRT5","CH_B337_OBS1:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:FL-03","text":"The argument depends on the identity of intuitive and categorical unity, but the transition by which logical judgment becomes sensible determination remains compressed.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:MRT6","CH_B337_OBS1:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:FL-04","text":"The system claims a priori necessity while terminating in unexplainable facts about the exact forms that carry that necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A12","CH_B337_OBS1:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:MP-01","text":"Judgment mutates from logical predication into the operative synthesis through which sensible manifold is unified.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A4","CH_B337_ASIA:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:MP-02","text":"The category mutates from a logical form into a condition of empirical consciousness and object-cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ASIA:A5","CH_B337_OBS1:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:MP-03","text":"The intuitive intellect mutates from a contrast case into a limit-concept delimiting the validity, dependency, and finitude of categories.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:AS6","CH_B337_OBS1:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:HSI-01","text":"Reality for finite cognition is a two-input machine in which receptive manifold and spontaneous rules must be coupled before an object can appear.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:D5","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not an empirical spectator but the site in which representations become objectively unified through apperception.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:A6","CH_B337_OBS1:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:HSI-03","text":"Time is structurally asymmetric: material is given prior to synthesis, while objecthood emerges only through subsequent unification.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:FGS1","CH_B337_OBS1:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:HSI-04","text":"Cognitive struggle consists in distinguishing rule-governed objective connection from merely contingent subjective association.","refs":["CH_B337_LFAJ:SD1","CH_B337_LFAJ:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:HSI-05","text":"The system’s horizon is finite navigation rather than absolute cognitive self-grounding, because the constitutive forms remain necessary but not finally derivable.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:SD4","CH_B337_OBS1:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_12:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central machine: given manifold is synthesized through judgmental categories under apperception and thereby becomes objectively cognizable.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction is strongest where it identifies one unity across objective judgment, empirical intuition, and categorical validity.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:A10","CH_B337_OBS1:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:MRT-B03","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the initial mode of givenness, the derivation of individual categories, and the ultimate origin of human cognitive forms.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:MRT3","CH_B337_ASIA:MRT3","CH_B337_OBS1:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_12:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is coherent as a conditional account of finite cognition, but it requires unresolved tension between independent givenness and synthesis-constituted objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_OBS1:MRT4","CH_B337_OBS1:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 34–36","context":"The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15 · All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16 · Observation § 17"}},{"id":"CL_B337_13","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 37–39","order":106,"parents":["CH_B337_CAOE","CH_B337_ACOS","CH_B337_TDUP"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"3df43839e47ef5450d16ddc729b6df2ea94ec15d9a249ff986316b6b7edb5f2d","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:AS-01","text":"Objective cognition begins only where a concept is joined to an intuition through which an object is given.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AS-02","text":"Because human intuition is sensuous, the field of possible cognition is bounded by the conditions of sensuous givenness rather than by conceptual coherence alone.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AS-03","text":"Sensible apprehension requires a synthetic unity whose intellectual form is expressed through the categories.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AS-04","text":"Productive imagination converts the spontaneous unity of understanding into a determination of receptive sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AS-05","text":"Categories acquire objective reality only through their role in the synthesis of phenomenal intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AS-06","text":"Conjoined perceptions constitute experience, and experience constitutes nature in its universally lawful form.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AS-07","text":"Pure categorical legislation governs universal lawfulness but cannot produce determinate empirical laws.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AS-08","text":"The thought of existence and the possibility of extra-sensuous being remain thinkable without becoming determinately knowable.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:SNX1","CH_B337_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires the conjunction of conceptual determination and corresponding intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] All human cognition is conditioned by sensuous intuition and therefore cannot acquire objective content independently of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Synthetic unity originates in apperception and must be enacted upon a sensible manifold rather than found ready-made in intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A3","CH_B337_ACOS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Productive imagination mediates between intellectual spontaneity and receptive sensibility by producing figurative synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A7","CH_B337_ACOS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Apperception grounds necessary conjunction while remaining distinct from the inner sense that receives its determination.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A10","CH_B337_ACOS:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] The categories are conditions of possible empirical cognition and possess legitimate use only within experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A9","CH_B337_CAOE:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Nature in general is necessarily lawful because phenomenal conjunction is constituted under categorical unity.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A10","CH_B337_TDUP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Pure principles legislate only universal conditions, while determinate natural content requires empirical supplementation.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A12","CH_B337_TDUP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] The subject can think itself as existing, yet can cognize itself only through temporal intuition as appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A11","CH_B337_ACOS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Formal applicability and objectively valid cognition must remain distinct if categorial universality is not to become metaphysical overreach.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:RMD-01","text":"The concept–intuition relation retains its identity as the criterion of cognition but expands from a boundary condition into the mechanism governing every possible synthesis of an experiential manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A1","CH_B337_ACOS:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:RMD-02","text":"Categories mutate from mere forms of thought into conditions of phenomenal objectivity and universal natural lawfulness.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A1","CH_B337_TDUP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:RMD-03","text":"Intuition shifts from passive sensuous receptivity into formal intuition whose unity requires synthesis for perceptual use.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A4","CH_B337_TDUP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:RMD-04","text":"Productive imagination emerges from an implicit requirement for mediation into the operative bridge between understanding and sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A7","CH_B337_TDUP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:RMD-05","text":"Apperception expands from the source of objective unity into the site of a divided self-relation between active thought and passive self-appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A10","CH_B337_ACOS:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:RMD-06","text":"Space and time mutate from forms delimiting sensibility into synthesized conditions under which temporal order and self-cognition become possible.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A11","CH_B337_ACOS:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:RMD-07","text":"The extra-sensuous is displaced from a possible target of negative delimitation into an undecidable boundary where thought persists but cognition is suspended.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:D7","CH_B337_TDUP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:CGD-01","text":"The taxonomy distinguishing pure from empirical intuition remains locally necessary but scales upward only as the more general division between formal condition and object-giving content.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A4","CH_B337_CAOE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:CGD-02","text":"The restriction of categories to experience scales upward into a universal account of how every possible experiential object can be objectively determined.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A10","CH_B337_TDUP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:CGD-03","text":"Productive imagination functions as the cross-unit bridge that turns the limitation imposed by sensibility into a positive account of categorical application.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A7","CH_B337_ACOS:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:CGD-04","text":"Self-affection becomes a catalytic transition because it internalizes the relation between spontaneous determination and receptive appearance within the subject itself.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:CGD-05","text":"The distinction between universal and particular laws recodes the general restriction on categories as a positive division of labor between transcendental principles and empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A10","CH_B337_TDUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:CGD-06","text":"Negative predicates remain boundary-markers rather than sources of positive cognition, and are absorbed into the general distinction between exclusion and determination.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:NX9","CH_B337_CAOE:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:GNX-01","text":"Thought is not cognition, because formal conceptual determinacy alone cannot supply an object.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GNX-02","text":"Categories are not independently object-cognizing contents and cannot acquire objective validity apart from sensible synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GNX-03","text":"Intellectual intuition is excluded as an available human route by which determining activity could directly intuit its own object.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GNX-04","text":"Phenomena are not things in themselves, so categorical validity cannot be transferred from appearances to independently existing being.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GNX-05","text":"Perception is not passive reception of an already unified given but the achieved result of synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:GNX-06","text":"Pure categories do not yield determinate empirical laws and therefore cannot complete natural science without experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:DBN-01","text":"The system depends upon the rejection of conceptual self-sufficiency as a source of object-directed knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:DBN-02","text":"A subject-independent phenomenal order cannot ground the necessity of experience, because phenomenal lawfulness is conditioned by synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:DBN-03","text":"Immediate self-presence cannot yield intuition of the spontaneous subject as it is in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:DBN-04","text":"Objective validity cannot consist in categories copying laws presumed to be already present in nature.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:DBN-05","text":"Empirical determinacy cannot be exhausted by pure conceptual legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:DBN-06","text":"The possibility of an object cannot be established merely through its concept when no corresponding intuition can be supplied.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:BSA-01","text":"Positive knowledge of reality beyond sensibility remains structurally unavailable rather than refuted as impossible.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:BSA-02","text":"The absent positive intuition of an extra-sensuous object is the constitutive lack around which the system’s boundary-work is organized.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:BSA-03","text":"The determining subject remains unavailable precisely at the point where self-consciousness seems most immediate.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:BSA-04","text":"A complete a priori science of determinate natural laws is unavailable because universal conditions cannot generate empirical content.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:BSA-05","text":"Things in themselves remain unavailable as objects of legitimate categorial cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:XD-01","text":"The objective validity of categories is produced through figurative synthesis, which makes every possible experiential object subject to categorical unity without converting categories into predicates of being in itself.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-03","CL_B337_13:GA-04","CL_B337_13:GA-06","CL_B337_13:GA-10","CH_B337_ACOS:A9","CH_B337_TDUP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:XD-02","text":"Sensibility functions not merely as a limit upon cognition but as the indispensable material whose determination allows intellectual spontaneity to become objectively effective.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-02","CL_B337_13:GA-03","CL_B337_13:GA-04","CH_B337_ACOS:A7","CH_B337_ACOS:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:XD-03","text":"The distinction between formal reach and objective application permits the categories to be universally necessary for experience while blocking their use as direct ontology.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-06","CL_B337_13:GA-07","CL_B337_13:GA-10","CH_B337_CAOE:SD7","CH_B337_TDUP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:XD-04","text":"The subject’s self-relation reproduces the general architecture of cognition: the same unity that determines appearances can know itself only through an appearing self.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-03","CL_B337_13:GA-05","CL_B337_13:GA-09","CH_B337_ACOS:D5","CH_B337_ACOS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:XD-05","text":"Natural necessity is universally binding yet materially indeterminate, so the lawfulness of nature requires empirical inquiry for all determinate content.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-07","CL_B337_13:GA-08","CH_B337_TDUP:SD4","CH_B337_TDUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:XD-06","text":"The inability to intuit either an extra-sensuous object or the self as it is in itself prevents negative boundaries from becoming positive metaphysical descriptions.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-02","CL_B337_13:GA-09","CL_B337_13:GA-10","CH_B337_CAOE:SNX1","CH_B337_ACOS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:XD-07","text":"Temporal self-cognition is an achieved effect of self-affection and figurative synthesis rather than an immediate inner disclosure.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-03","CL_B337_13:GA-04","CL_B337_13:GA-09","CH_B337_ACOS:D4","CH_B337_ACOS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:BSD-01","text":"Objective reality is reconstructed as the constitution of possible experience rather than as correspondence between concepts and independently accessible objects.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-04","CL_B337_13:GA-06","CL_B337_13:GA-07","CH_B337_ACOS:SD1","CH_B337_TDUP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:BSD-02","text":"World and subject become coupled phenomenal structures: nature is lawful only through understanding, while the subject knows itself only through temporal appearance.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-07","CL_B337_13:GA-09","CH_B337_ACOS:SD2","CH_B337_TDUP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:BSD-03","text":"Necessity no longer supplies substantive metaphysical content; it supplies the universal form within which empirical content can be encountered and ordered.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-07","CL_B337_13:GA-08","CH_B337_TDUP:SD4","CH_B337_TDUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:BSD-04","text":"Natural science is structurally bifurcated into transcendental conditions of lawfulness and empirical determination of particular laws.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-08","CH_B337_TDUP:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:BSD-05","text":"Metaphysical extension is transformed into a discipline of legitimate application, where the question of what exists is subordinated to the conditions under which objects can be given.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-02","CL_B337_13:GA-06","CL_B337_13:GA-10","CH_B337_CAOE:ALT3","CH_B337_ACOS:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:BSD-06","text":"The subject’s apparent self-certainty is structurally delayed, because its existence can be thought immediately while its mode of being can be known only mediately.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-09","CH_B337_ACOS:SD3","CH_B337_ACOS:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:RLE-01","text":"Conceptual determination repeatedly returns to intuition because every claim to objective validity is annulled when corresponding givenness is absent.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A1","CH_B337_CAOE:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:RLE-02","text":"Apperceptive unity escalates into figurative synthesis, which returns categorical form to phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:FGS2","CH_B337_ACOS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:RLE-03","text":"Categorically unified perception generates experience, and experience reappears as the lawful field that confirms the necessity of categorical unity.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:FGS5","CH_B337_TDUP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:RLE-04","text":"Self-affection forms a recursive loop in which the active subject determines the receptive self that then appears back to that subject in time.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:D5","CH_B337_ACOS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:RLE-05","text":"Formal categorial scope repeatedly encounters its experiential limit, where attempted extension becomes negative boundary-work rather than cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:SD7","CH_B337_CAOE:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:RLE-06","text":"The limitation of pure legislation reopens empirical inquiry, preventing the constitution of formal nature from closing into a complete deductive science.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:AS6","CH_B337_TDUP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:FGS-B01","text":"A sensuous manifold given under space and time requires synthesis before it can count as perceptually apprehended.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:FGS1","CH_B337_TDUP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:FGS-B02","text":"Apperception supplies the rule of necessary conjunction that prevents apprehension from remaining a merely dispersed manifold.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:FGS-B03","text":"Productive imagination translates intellectual spontaneity into the determination of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:FGS-B04","text":"Figurative synthesis yields determinate appearance and grants categories objective reality within possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:FGS-B05","text":"Categorically unified perceptions compose experience, and experience constitutes nature in its formal lawfulness.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A7","CH_B337_TDUP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:FGS-B06","text":"Pure categorical lawfulness stops at universal conditions and transfers determinate natural inquiry to experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:A11","CH_B337_TDUP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:FGS-B07","text":"The same generative sequence folds back upon the subject, yielding temporal self-appearance rather than self-knowledge in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:AFM-B01","text":"The distinction between merely thinking an object and cognizing one initiates the demand for a criterion of objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AFM-B02","text":"The criterion of concept plus intuition leads to the restriction of human cognition to sensuous givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AFM-B03","text":"The insufficiency of pure categories leads to the requirement that sensuous intuition be synthetically determined.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AFM-B04","text":"The need for sensible determination leads to productive imagination as the mediation of understanding and sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AFM-B05","text":"Figurative synthesis leads from categorical form to objective validity for phenomenal objects.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AFM-B06","text":"Objectively unified perception leads to experience, formal nature, and the residual necessity of empirical inquiry into particular laws.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:AS5","CH_B337_TDUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:AFM-B07","text":"The deduction terminates in a doubled restriction: the self and the extra-sensuous can be thought, but neither can be positively cognized beyond conditions of intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A12","CH_B337_CAOE:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:MME-01","text":"Categories are reclassified from general determinations of being into conditions governing phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MME-02","text":"The boundary of knowledge is relocated from conceptual completeness to possible application within intuition and synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MME-03","text":"Imagination is reclassified from an auxiliary image-making power into the transcendental mediator of objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MME-04","text":"Nature is divided into universal formal lawfulness and particular empirical law, dissolving the false alternative between pure rational derivation and merely contingent observation.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MME-05","text":"The opposition between active subject and passive object is redrawn as an internal division within the cognizing subject.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MME-06","text":"Inner temporal cognition is shown to depend upon figuration and alteration rather than upon direct inspection of an inward substance.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system establishes the conditions under which categories can apply to objects of sense.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, perception requires the inseparable coordination of sensible manifold, synthetic unity, imagination, and categorical determination.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the question whether nature conforms to categories is displaced by the claim that phenomenal nature is constituted under conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, phenomenal objects and temporal selves exist only within the reciprocal conditions of sensibility and understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, transcendental explanation separates productive synthesis from empirical association and reproduces its own restriction of explanatory scope.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:FL-01","text":"Productive imagination is required to belong to sensibility while exercising the spontaneity of understanding, leaving its ontological status structurally unstable.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:FL-02","text":"The account requires formal intuition to contain unity while also requiring synthesis for the unity of apprehension, leaving the source of sensible unity incompletely determined.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:FL-03","text":"The claim that “I am” asserts existence without determinate self-knowledge preserves a distinction whose positive warrant remains compressed.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:FL-04","text":"Inner temporal cognition depends upon outer figuration and alteration, creating a residual dependence of self-knowledge on external representation.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MP-01","text":"Categories mutate from forms of thought into conditions of universal natural lawfulness, and this change is necessary for their objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A1","CH_B337_TDUP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MP-02","text":"Intuition mutates from formal phenomenal condition into formally unified material requiring synthesis for perception.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:A5","CH_B337_TDUP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MP-03","text":"The subject mutates from spontaneous determining unity into a divided structure that is both active thought and passively intuited appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A6","CH_B337_ACOS:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a generative machine in which sensible manifold is processed through synthesis into categorically unified objects.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:FGS1","CH_B337_TDUP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:HSI-02","text":"The subject appears as a bifurcated operator that determines phenomenal order while receiving its own determinations as temporal appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:HSI-03","text":"Time appears not as a self-given inner medium but as an order produced through successive figurative synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:A13","CH_B337_ACOS:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:HSI-04","text":"Nature appears as necessarily lawful in form but empirically unfinished in content.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:SD4","CH_B337_TDUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:HSI-05","text":"The extra-sensuous appears as a horizon of epistemic indeterminacy whose possibility remains undecided because no positive intuition can fill it.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:ME6","CH_B337_CAOE:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_13:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s central conclusion that categories have legitimate cognitive use only within possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where objective determination is shown to depend upon corresponding intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MRT-B03","text":"The system remains coherent only if formal extension of categories is held apart from their objectively valid application.","refs":["CH_B337_CAOE:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MRT-B04","text":"Productive imagination and the unity of formal intuition remain resistant to complete axiomatic reduction because their mediating status is asserted more fully than derived.","refs":["CH_B337_ACOS:MRT3","CH_B337_TDUP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_13:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when it retains the recursive transition from perception to lawful nature and back to the limits of self-knowledge and extra-sensuous cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TDUP:MRT8","CH_B337_ACOS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 37–39","context":"In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18 · Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20 · Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22"}},{"id":"CL_B337_14","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 40–42","order":107,"parents":["CH_B337_RTDC","CH_B337_BIAP","CH_B337_ITFJ"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"ab43ac2820cbd76f80ff2d90fc0cd99c9d3547ab9eea6adbc18a1eca2dbd2cd4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:AS-B01","text":"The cluster establishes that pure concepts attain objective validity only as necessary conditions of possible experience rather than as independent representations of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AS-B02","text":"It converts the origin-question concerning categories into an application-question concerning the lawful subsumption of appearances under pure concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:ME1","CH_B337_ITFJ:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AS-B03","text":"The decisive mediation is judgement: categories require sensuous conditions, and rule-possession requires an irreducible act of subsumption.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:D5","CH_B337_ITFJ:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AS-B04","text":"General logic cannot resolve this mediation because every rule for applying a rule renews the problem of application.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AS-B05","text":"Transcendental logic resolves the regress only partially by determining the a priori conditions under which pure concepts can apply to given objects.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AS-B06","text":"The resulting architecture is asymmetrical: understanding supplies concepts, judgement mediates their valid use, and reason becomes dialectical when it seeks objects beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:SD1","CH_B337_BIAP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AS-B07","text":"The movement is recursive rather than linear: categorical validity requires application, application requires judgement, and judgement requires conditions that prevent pure concepts from becoming empty or transcendent.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:D6","CH_B337_ITFJ:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AS-B08","text":"Closure occurs not in an unrestricted doctrine of objects but in a critical canon governing the valid employment of pure concepts in phenomenal experience.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A9","CH_B337_ITFJ:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires both categorical thought and corresponding sensuous intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Categories possess objective validity only insofar as they condition the possibility of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] The problem of pure concepts changes from their deduction as valid concepts to their judged application to phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SD6","CH_B337_BIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] Judgement is the indispensable mediating faculty between universal rules and particular appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A2","CH_B337_BIAP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Formal coherence cannot secure objective validity because logic abstracted from content cannot determine object-reference.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:D1","CH_B337_BIAP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Transcendental direction of judgement is possible only because pure concepts bear an a priori relation to possible objects and their conditions of givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:AS6","CH_B337_ITFJ:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GA-07","text":"[Terminal] Reason loses analytic authority when it converts the boundary of possible experience into an object of speculative cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is the shared criterion that both grounds categorical necessity and limits legitimate cognitive employment.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SD3","CH_B337_BIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Critique functions less as the expansion of pure doctrine than as the regulation and prevention of misapplied pure concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:D10","CH_B337_ITFJ:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Schematism and principles form a staged apparatus through which pure concepts become synthetic cognition without becoming empirical products.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:SD6","CH_B337_RTDC:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:RMD-01","text":"Categories retain their identity as a priori concepts, but their operative role mutates from grounding experience in the deduction to governing phenomenal application through judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A7","CH_B337_BIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:RMD-02","text":"Objective validity recurs as necessity rather than private association, then expands into the criterion separating analytic cognition from dialectical illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A10","CH_B337_BIAP:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:RMD-03","text":"Possible experience migrates from the limit of human object-cognition to the jurisdictional boundary distributing authority among the faculties.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:NX3","CH_B337_BIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:RMD-04","text":"Judgement changes from the deferred site of categorical employment into the central mechanism that renders categories applicable without reducing application to formal rule-following.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:D6","CH_B337_ITFJ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:RMD-05","text":"Formal logic reverses valence: it remains valid as a formal canon while becoming insufficient wherever cognition requires determinate content and object-reference.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:AS2","CH_B337_BIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:RMD-06","text":"The rule becomes a recursive problem-machine when its possession is distinguished from its correct use in a particular case.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A6","CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:RMD-07","text":"Examples persist only as subordinate instruments of exercise; they lose any claim to ground universal validity or replace judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:D5","CH_B337_ITFJ:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:CGD-01","text":"The deduction’s two-alternative proof of categorical validity remains local machinery, while its cluster-level consequence is that validity must culminate in a doctrine of application.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:FGS4","CH_B337_BIAP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:CGD-02","text":"The faculty taxonomy of understanding, judgement, and reason scales upward because it becomes a distribution of unequal cognitive jurisdictions.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:A1","CH_B337_BIAP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:CGD-03","text":"The regress of subsumption is locally a diagnosis of judgement, but globally it blocks every attempt to derive objective cognition from formal procedure alone.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:D2","CH_B337_BIAP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:CGD-04","text":"Schematism is the transition-catalyst that absorbs the gap between category and appearance into a determinate architecture of mediating conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:D8","CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:CGD-05","text":"Preformationism and empiricist derivation are superseded as rival origins of necessity; their lasting cluster-level function is to mark what objective validity cannot be.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:NX6","CH_B337_RTDC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:CGD-06","text":"Reason’s speculative employment is not merely a local error but a terminal recoding of cognitive excess as dialectical illusion rather than analytic knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:ME2","CH_B337_BIAP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:GNX-01","text":"The system rejects empirical derivation as a source of the necessity expressed in categories and principles.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:NX4","CH_B337_RTDC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GNX-02","text":"The system rejects subjective constitution, preformed aptitude, and private association as grounds of objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:NX7","CH_B337_RTDC:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GNX-03","text":"The system rejects the self-sufficiency of pure concepts, formal logic, and bare thought without conditions of object-givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:NX1","CH_B337_ITFJ:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GNX-04","text":"The system rejects a final meta-rule of correct application because every such rule requires the judgement it claims to replace.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:NX2","CH_B337_ITFJ:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GNX-05","text":"The system rejects speculative reason as a legitimate extension of cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:NX4","CH_B337_BIAP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:GNX-06","text":"The system rejects philosophy as unrestricted doctrinal accumulation and recodes its critical work as error-prevention through limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A11","CH_B337_ITFJ:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:DBN-01","text":"No account of objectivity can survive if necessity is either induced from empirical regularity or reduced to subjective inability to think otherwise.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:D4","CH_B337_RTDC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:DBN-02","text":"No cognition can be objectively valid if the relation between concept and object remains merely formal, inferential, or privately psychological.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:SD3","CH_B337_ITFJ:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:DBN-03","text":"No complete logic of cognition can treat understanding, judgement, and reason as symmetrically authorized faculties.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:SNX3","CH_B337_BIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:DBN-04","text":"No rule-based system can erase the irreducible interval between universal rule and particular case.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:SNX6","CH_B337_ITFJ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:DBN-05","text":"No pure cognition of supersensible objects is admitted as an exception to the boundary of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SNX1","CH_B337_BIAP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:BSA-01","text":"The cluster excludes an epistemic standpoint external to the conditions that make objects available to cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SNX1","CH_B337_ITFJ:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable possibility organizing the system is a fully mechanical application of rules that would abolish judgement without regress.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:DNX1","CH_B337_ITFJ:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:BSA-03","text":"The system cannot admit an unmediated passage from pure concept to object, because such a passage would collapse thought, cognition, and phenomenal givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:DNX5","CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:BSA-04","text":"The system cannot admit a positive metaphysics that expands knowledge without simultaneously supplying a criterion for its legitimate application.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:NX7","CH_B337_ITFJ:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:XD-01","text":"The objective validity established by the deduction becomes operational only when judgement mediates categories and sensuous appearances under conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GA-02","CL_B337_14:GA-04","CL_B337_14:GA-06","CH_B337_RTDC:D3","CH_B337_ITFJ:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:XD-02","text":"The limitation of cognition to possible experience is simultaneously a positive condition of objectivity and a negative diagnosis of speculative reason.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GA-07","CL_B337_14:GA-08","CH_B337_RTDC:D5","CH_B337_BIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:XD-03","text":"The irreducibility of judgement does not abolish transcendental regulation; it restricts regulation to determining conditions of applicability rather than prescribing a universal act of subsumption.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GA-04","CL_B337_14:GA-06","CH_B337_ITFJ:SD5","CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:XD-04","text":"The distinction between formal and transcendental logic converts the failure of formal rule-following into the need for a content-bound theory of mediation.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GA-05","CL_B337_14:GA-10","CH_B337_BIAP:D2","CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:XD-05","text":"Critique acquires its force by excluding both empirical and speculative inflation while preserving a lawful domain of a priori cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GA-08","CL_B337_14:GA-09","CH_B337_RTDC:SD1","CH_B337_ITFJ:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as a mediated relation among categorical necessity, sensuous givenness, and judged application rather than as a property of concepts considered alone.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GA-01","CL_B337_14:GA-02","CL_B337_14:GA-04","CH_B337_RTDC:SD2","CH_B337_BIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:BSD-02","text":"The cluster produces an asymmetrical theory of cognitive agency in which the subject supplies necessary form but cannot convert that contribution into private arbitrariness.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GA-02","CL_B337_14:GA-05","CH_B337_RTDC:MRT5","CH_B337_RTDC:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:BSD-03","text":"The boundary of experience becomes a productive operator: it licenses laws for appearances precisely by disqualifying transcendent object-claims.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GA-07","CL_B337_14:GA-08","CH_B337_BIAP:A10","CH_B337_BIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:BSD-04","text":"The system transforms philosophical method from doctrinal possession into controlled employment, where the possession of a concept is never equivalent to its legitimate use.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GA-03","CL_B337_14:GA-09","CH_B337_ITFJ:A6","CH_B337_ITFJ:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:BSD-05","text":"Error is reclassified as a structural failure of mediation rather than merely a lack of rules, data, or inferential form.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GA-04","CL_B337_14:GA-05","CH_B337_ITFJ:D4","CH_B337_ITFJ:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:RLE-01","text":"Categories require intuition for cognition, intuition requires synthesis for objective unity, and synthesis returns categorical necessity as a condition of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:FGS1","CH_B337_RTDC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:RLE-02","text":"Rule-possession generates the demand for application; application generates regress; regress returns judgement as an irreducible capacity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS2","CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:RLE-03","text":"The inability of general logic to direct subsumption escalates into the positive task of transcendental logic: specifying the content-bound conditions of valid application.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:D6","CH_B337_ITFJ:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:RLE-04","text":"Possible experience is the principal attractor because it repeatedly converts apparent alternatives—empiricism or innatism, form or content, concept or object—into conditions of coordinated cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SD2","CH_B337_BIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:RLE-05","text":"Reason’s escalation from formal law to transcendent object-claim reverses its status from logical faculty to source of dialectical illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:D3","CH_B337_BIAP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:RLE-06","text":"Schematism converts the recurring interval between universal concept and singular appearance into a machine that produces principles.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:SD6","CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:FGS-B01","text":"Categories supply the necessary form of object-thought, while sensuous intuition supplies the givenness without which thought is not cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A1","CH_B337_RTDC:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:FGS-B02","text":"The a priori status of categories excludes their empirical derivation and relocates their validity in the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A7","CH_B337_RTDC:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:FGS-B03","text":"Possible experience differentiates legitimate categorical employment from subjective association and speculative transcendent use.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:D3","CH_B337_BIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:FGS-B04","text":"The need to apply categories introduces judgement, whose subsumptive act cannot be generated by another general rule without regress.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A2","CH_B337_ITFJ:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:FGS-B05","text":"Transcendental logic specifies the a priori relation between pure concepts and possible objects, thereby directing application without mechanically replacing judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:A12","CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:FGS-B06","text":"Schematism supplies sensuous conditions of application, and principles articulate the synthetic laws produced by that mediation.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:AS7","CH_B337_ITFJ:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:AFM-B01","text":"Necessary object-thought → categories plus intuition → empirical experience as the field of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AFM-B02","text":"A priori categories → rejection of empirical derivation and subjective preformation → transcendental conditioning of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AFM-B03","text":"Categorical validity → unresolved question of phenomenal employment → transfer to transcendental judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SD6","CH_B337_RTDC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AFM-B04","text":"Rule-governed understanding → subsumption problem → regress of further rules → irreducible judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AFM-B05","text":"Failure of general logic → content-bound transcendental logic → a priori conditions of concept-object relation.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:AF2","CH_B337_ITFJ:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AFM-B06","text":"Schema → sensuous applicability → principles of pure understanding → lawful phenomenal cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS8","CH_B337_BIAP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:AFM-B07","text":"Reason beyond possible experience → loss of objective validity → dialectical diagnosis rather than analytic canon.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:MME-01","text":"Experience is reclassified from the empirical source of concepts into the constituted field within which categories can possess objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:ME2","CH_B337_RTDC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MME-02","text":"The concept-object relation is redrawn as a three-term structure of category, schema, and phenomenal object rather than a direct conceptual grasp.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:ME7","CH_B337_ITFJ:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MME-03","text":"The cognitive faculties are reorganized by jurisdiction: understanding supplies rules, judgement authorizes application, and reason is restricted when transcendent.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:D1","CH_B337_BIAP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MME-04","text":"Skepticism is relocated from an unavoidable condition of finite cognition to the result of separating necessity from the conditions of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SD5","CH_B337_RTDC:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MME-05","text":"Error is no longer reducible to false propositions; it includes failures in the application of valid rules to cases.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:D4","CH_B337_ITFJ:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MME-06","text":"Critique becomes a method of delimiting valid cognition rather than a project of expanding metaphysical inventory.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:SD3","CH_B337_ITFJ:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The cluster concerns categories, intuitions, rules, cases, phenomena, schemata, and principles.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:ALT1","CH_B337_ITFJ:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Cognition is organized by the mediation of categorical synthesis, sensuous givenness, apperceptive unity, and judgement.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SD2","CH_B337_RTDC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — Critique defeats empiricism, formalism, preformationism, and speculative overreach by recoding validity as lawful application.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:ALT3","CH_B337_BIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Objects are cognitively available only within possible experience, whose appearing is structured by sensibility and whose unity is synthesized under categories.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:ALT4","CH_B337_RTDC:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The cluster performs its thesis by repeatedly deferring pure conceptual authority to the mediating operations it must itself reconstruct.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:D6","CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:FL-01","text":"Objective necessity must arise through subjective cognitive faculties without becoming merely subjective necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:FL-02","text":"Judgement is irreducible to general rules, yet transcendental philosophy claims to direct its use through determinate conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:FL-03","text":"Understanding supplies principles, but judgement is named as the doctrine’s operative faculty, leaving faculty ownership functionally distributed rather than fully resolved.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:FL-04","text":"Categories can think beyond sensibility while cognition remains bounded by possible experience, producing a stable excess that cannot become knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MP-01","text":"The category mutates from a condition of objective validity into a rule requiring schematized employment.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SD6","CH_B337_ITFJ:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MP-02","text":"Judgement mutates from natural capacity developed through exercise into a transcendentally regulable site of pure conceptual application.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:AS3","CH_B337_ITFJ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MP-03","text":"Logic mutates from formal analysis of discursive operations into a validity-filter that distinguishes analytic cognition from dialectical illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_BIAP:AS2","CH_B337_BIAP:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MP-04","text":"Critique mutates from negative restriction into a positive architecture for generating lawful synthetic principles.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:NX8","CH_B337_ITFJ:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a conditioned field in which objects become cognitively available only through the coordination of receptivity, synthesis, and rules.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:HSI-02","text":"The subject is neither an empirical recipient nor an arbitrary legislator but the site where necessary form meets irreducible acts of application.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:DNX3","CH_B337_ITFJ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:HSI-03","text":"Time is not an independent object of cognition but a form of sensibility within the horizon that makes experience and categorical application possible.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:A11","CH_B337_RTDC:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:HSI-04","text":"Struggle within cognition takes the form of boundary maintenance: preventing concepts from becoming empty, rules from becoming mechanical, and reason from becoming transcendent.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:NX9","CH_B337_BIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:HSI-05","text":"The governing image is a mediation machine in which concept, schema, judgement, and appearance must continually pass through one another to yield objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:FGS8","CH_B337_BIAP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_14:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s movement from categorical validity to the problem of its phenomenal application.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:MRT1","CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction is coherent only if possible experience is retained as both the source-condition of objectivity and the limit on cognitive extension.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:SD3","CH_B337_BIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where it connects necessity, objectivity, and application through judgement rather than treating them as separate problems.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:D3","CH_B337_BIAP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MRT-B04","text":"The principal underdetermination remains the exact mechanism by which schemata direct judgement without converting judgement into rule-following.","refs":["CH_B337_RTDC:MRT4","CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_14:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual recursive movement only if the unresolved tension between irreducible judgement and transcendental direction remains active rather than harmonized away.","refs":["CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT6","CH_B337_ITFJ:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 40–42","context":"Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23 · BOOK II. Analytic of Principles · INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General"}},{"id":"CL_B337_15","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 43–45","order":108,"parents":["CH_B337_CISP","CH_B337_CISA","CH_B337_SISP"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"198c02e7f1c132dfec27cece0315ad53be135a47a657f8b04318a234726e963f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:AS-B01","text":"The cluster addresses how pure thought can attain objective force without allowing either conceptual form or logical consistency to substitute for cognition of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:AS2","CH_B337_SISP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AS-B02","text":"Heterogeneity between categories and intuitions makes temporal mediation by imagination the necessary route from pure concepts to phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:AS3","CH_B337_CISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AS-B03","text":"The operation that realizes categories in experience simultaneously confines their objective employment to phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:AS6","CH_B337_CISP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AS-B04","text":"The relation of categories to sensibility converts the category-table from a formal inventory into a systematic generator of transcendental principles.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A2","CH_B337_CISA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AS-B05","text":"The principle of contradiction universally screens judgments for nullity but supplies positive truth-recognition only where conceptual containment makes a judgment analytical.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A2","CH_B337_SISP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AS-B06","text":"Transcendental proof grounds the objective validity of a priori principles by reconstructing the subjective conditions under which objects can be cognized.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:D5","CH_B337_CISA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AS-B07","text":"The movement is recursive rather than cumulative: formal categories require schemata, schemata require conditions of experience, and those conditions become the proof of bounded objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:ME1","CH_B337_SISP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AS-B08","text":"The closure is a differentiated jurisdiction of cognition in which categories retain logical thinkability beyond experience while losing all object-determining authority there.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:SD4","CH_B337_CISA:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Categories acquire objective significance only through determinations of objects under the sensible form of time.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A10","CH_B337_CISP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Heterogeneous intellectual and sensible elements require a mediating representation rather than direct subsumption.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:AS2","CH_B337_CISP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Imagination is the productive operator that translates categorial unity into rules for temporal synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A8","CH_B337_CISP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Sensibility is inseparably the enabling condition and the limiting condition of the understanding’s objective employment.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] The systematic completeness of transcendental principles derives from the ordered application of categories to sensibility rather than from an aggregate of independent claims.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A2","CH_B337_CISA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Subjective conditions of possible cognition can establish objective validity when they are shown to be necessary for objects of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A5","CH_B337_CISA:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Logical noncontradiction is necessary for judgment but cannot establish object-correspondence or epistemic grounding.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A3","CH_B337_SISP:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] The principle of contradiction yields positive truth-recognition only when the predicate is already contained in the subject concept.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A5","CH_B337_SISP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Synthetic a priori cognition cannot be grounded by formal logic alone and requires schematized conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A12","CH_B337_SISP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Objective cognition is generated through a differentiated coordination of sensibility, imagination, understanding, and apperception rather than through any self-sufficient faculty.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:SD3","CH_B337_CISP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:RMD-01","text":"The category persists as a rule of unity but mutates from a bare pure concept into a temporal procedure and then into a principle-generating systematic function.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A9","CH_B337_CISA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:RMD-02","text":"Sensibility shifts from a receptive condition of appearance into the positive medium through which objective validity is both enabled and bounded.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:SD2","CH_B337_CISA:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:RMD-03","text":"Time expands from the universal medium of objective application into a jurisdictionally split structure: indispensable for synthetic cognition but excluded from the pure formulation of contradiction.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:ME3","CH_B337_SISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:RMD-04","text":"Imagination recurs first as the productive maker of schemata and is then absorbed into a distributed architecture of receptivity, understanding, and apperception.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:ME2","CH_B337_CISP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:RMD-05","text":"Noncontradiction retains its universal negative function while its apparent authority as a complete criterion of truth is curtailed.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:A2","CH_B337_SISP:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:RMD-06","text":"The analytical–synthetic distinction mutates from a classificatory contrast into a division of logical jurisdiction and proof requirements.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:A11","CH_B337_SISP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:RMD-07","text":"Mathematical cognition is retained as an adjacent possibility of synthetic a priori knowledge while being excluded from the categorical system’s operative core.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:ME5","CH_B337_CISA:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:CGD-01","text":"The specific schemata of quantity, quality, relation, and modality remain local differentiations; the cluster retains only their common function as temporal rules of object-determination.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:D3","CH_B337_CISP:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:CGD-02","text":"The individual derivation of particular transcendental principles remains deferred, while the category-table scales upward as the principle of systematic completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:D1","CH_B337_CISA:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:CGD-03","text":"The category–sensibility relation is the principal bridge between local schematism and the current-level architecture of principles.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:D2","CH_B337_CISA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:CGD-04","text":"The principle of contradiction remains locally decisive for analytical judgments but scales upward only as a boundary against illegitimate logical proof of synthetic truth.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:SD4","CH_B337_SISP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:CGD-05","text":"The distinction between mathematical and categorical principles functions at this level as a boundary that preserves source-differentiation rather than as an independent argument.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:D7","CH_B337_CISA:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:CGD-06","text":"The demand for transcendental proof catalyzes the cluster’s transition from a system of concepts to a justified system of objectively valid principles.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:D3","CH_B337_CISA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:GNX-01","text":"Objective category-application excludes immediate subsumption of phenomena under pure concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GNX-02","text":"Logical consistency is excluded as a content-bearing criterion capable of establishing truth about objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GNX-03","text":"Categories are excluded from constitutive application to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:NX6","CH_B337_CISA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GNX-04","text":"Neither sensibility nor understanding can supply objective cognition in isolation from the other.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:DNX3","CH_B337_CISA:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GNX-05","text":"Images and particular figures are excluded as adequate vehicles of the universality required for transcendental schemata.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GNX-06","text":"Temporal coexistence is excluded as the foundation of logical contradiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:GNX-07","text":"The unity of pure cognition excludes the reduction of categories, sensible forms, and mathematical intuitions to one undifferentiated source.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:DBN-01","text":"The cluster excludes an intellectual-intuitive model in which concepts could directly disclose or give their own objects.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:DNX1","CH_B337_CISP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:DBN-02","text":"The cluster excludes any passage from formal validity to objective cognition that bypasses receptivity and temporal determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:DNX2","CH_B337_CISP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:DBN-03","text":"Foundational principles cannot be admitted as arbitrary axioms and require transcendental justification despite lacking higher premises.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:SNX1","CH_B337_CISA:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:DBN-04","text":"Synthetic a priori cognition cannot be reduced either to conceptual analysis or to intuition detached from categories.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:SNX4","CH_B337_CISA:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:BSA-01","text":"Cognition of objects independently of the conditions under which they can be given remains structurally unavailable.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:BSA-02","text":"The productive ground of schematizing imagination remains necessary to the system but unavailable to discursive self-knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:BSA-03","text":"The principle of contradiction cannot be elevated into a total ontology of being, non-being, and temporal change.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:BSA-04","text":"No principle can be simultaneously categorical in source, independent of sensibility, and objectively applicable to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:XD-01","text":"Temporal schematism converts the category-table into a generator of principles by supplying the determinate sensible relation required for each category’s objective employment.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-03","CL_B337_15:GA-05","CH_B337_CISP:A9","CH_B337_CISA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:XD-02","text":"The principle of contradiction performs a preliminary exclusion of null judgments, while schematism and transcendental proof supply the positive conditions required for synthetic validity.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-03","CL_B337_15:GA-07","CL_B337_15:GA-09","CH_B337_SISP:A8","CH_B337_CISP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:XD-03","text":"The logical residue of categories outside experience and the negative validity of noncontradiction jointly establish a domain of thinkability that is not yet a domain of cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-04","CL_B337_15:GA-07","CH_B337_CISP:A12","CH_B337_SISP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:XD-04","text":"Subjective temporal synthesis can ground objective order only when it is coordinated with the unity of apperception and reconstructed as a condition of possible object-cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-03","CL_B337_15:GA-06","CL_B337_15:GA-10","CH_B337_CISP:A11","CH_B337_CISA:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:XD-05","text":"Time has a dual function: it is the medium through which categories gain empirical meaning and the element whose insertion would transform a formal principle of contradiction into a synthetic claim.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-01","CL_B337_15:GA-08","CH_B337_CISP:ME3","CH_B337_SISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:XD-06","text":"Boundary-setting produces systematic unity by assigning categorical principles, mathematical cognition, and analytical judgment distinct but coordinated jurisdictions.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-05","CL_B337_15:GA-08","CL_B337_15:GA-10","CH_B337_CISA:FGS6","CH_B337_SISP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:BSD-01","text":"Objective validity becomes a relational accomplishment produced by the coordination of formal concepts, temporal synthesis, and conditions of possible cognition rather than a property of concepts considered alone.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-01","CL_B337_15:GA-03","CL_B337_15:GA-06","CH_B337_CISP:SD1","CH_B337_SISP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:BSD-02","text":"The cluster produces a stratified theory of truth consisting of formal admissibility, analytical truth-recognition, and synthetic objective validity.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-06","CL_B337_15:GA-07","CL_B337_15:GA-08","CL_B337_15:GA-09","CH_B337_SISP:ME1","CH_B337_CISA:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:BSD-03","text":"Objects of possible cognition are reconstituted as temporally ordered phenomena governed by categorial rules rather than as entities accessible to pure understanding independently of appearance.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-01","CL_B337_15:GA-04","CH_B337_CISP:D9","CH_B337_CISA:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:BSD-04","text":"Foundational proof is transformed from deduction out of higher propositions into a reconstruction of enabling conditions whose necessity licenses their objective use.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-05","CL_B337_15:GA-06","CH_B337_CISA:SD2","CH_B337_CISA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:BSD-05","text":"Critical restriction is productive rather than merely prohibitive because it allocates distinct roles to sensibility, imagination, understanding, logic, and mathematical intuition.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-04","CL_B337_15:GA-05","CL_B337_15:GA-10","CH_B337_CISP:AS6","CH_B337_CISA:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:BSD-06","text":"The system prevents synthetic cognition from being either arbitrary content or falsely deduced logical form by binding it to necessary temporal conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-01","CL_B337_15:GA-06","CL_B337_15:GA-09","CH_B337_SISP:SD4","CH_B337_CISA:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:RLE-01","text":"Heterogeneity → schema → objective application → restriction to experience forms the primary recursive loop, because the condition that enables application also produces its limit.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:AF1","CH_B337_CISP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:RLE-02","text":"Category-table → relation to sensibility → transcendental principle → proof of objective validity forms the system-building escalation path.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:FGS1","CH_B337_CISA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:RLE-03","text":"Judgment → contradiction test → analytical recognition or synthetic deferral forms the logical-jurisdiction loop.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:FGS2","CH_B337_SISP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:RLE-04","text":"Time is the central attractor because every objective category is routed through it, while pure logic must expel temporal qualification to preserve its own formal status.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:ME3","CH_B337_SISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:RLE-05","text":"Boundary-setting recurs as a productive operation: every exclusion of overreach simultaneously specifies the proper source, domain, and proof-form of a legitimate principle.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:FGS6","CH_B337_CISA:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:RLE-06","text":"The hidden operation of imagination is a recursive pressure point because the system requires it to mediate faculties while withholding a fully explicit account of its productive ground.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:MRT5","CH_B337_CISP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:FGS-B01","text":"Heterogeneous pure concepts and sensible givenness establish mediation, rather than direct subsumption, as the initial problem of objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:AS2","CH_B337_CISP:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:FGS-B02","text":"Time as the universal sensible form and imagination as productive synthesis generate schemata for determining intuition under categorial rules.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A5","CH_B337_CISP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:FGS-B03","text":"Schemata connect categories to phenomena and constitute objects of experience as rule-governed temporal orders.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:AS5","CH_B337_CISP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:FGS-B04","text":"The category-table, once schematized, yields the structural inventory from which a complete system of transcendental principles can be generated.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:D1","CH_B337_CISA:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:FGS-B05","text":"Transcendental proof converts subjective conditions of possible cognition into objectively valid conditions for objects of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:D5","CH_B337_CISA:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:FGS-B06","text":"The principle of contradiction separates formal admissibility from synthetic truth and preserves analytical truth-recognition within its restricted jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:D4","CH_B337_SISP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:FGS-B07","text":"The resulting system closes by restricting all objective cognition to possible experience while preserving a merely logical remainder beyond that domain.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A13","CH_B337_CISP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:AFM-B01","text":"Pure categories + sensible heterogeneity → failure of direct application → necessity of mediation.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:AS2","CH_B337_CISP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AFM-B02","text":"Mediation → temporal schema → imaginative synthesis of intuitions under categorial rules.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:FGS3","CH_B337_CISP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AFM-B03","text":"Temporal schema → objective determination of phenomena → empirical significance of categories.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:FGS5","CH_B337_CISP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AFM-B04","text":"Schematized categories → relation to sensibility → systematic catalogue of transcendental principles.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:FGS2","CH_B337_CISA:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AFM-B05","text":"Lack of higher premises → transcendental proof from subjective conditions → objective validity within experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:FGS4","CH_B337_CISA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:AFM-B06","text":"Universal noncontradiction → analytical truth-recognition or synthetic deferral → bounded logical jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:AF2","CH_B337_SISP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:MME-01","text":"The concept–object relation is reclassified from direct representation into mediated rule-governed temporal synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MME-02","text":"The category-table becomes a generative map of principles rather than a static classificatory table.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MME-03","text":"Truth is differentiated into formal noncontradiction, object-correspondence, and epistemic grounding.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MME-04","text":"The apparent conflict between a priori necessity and experiential restriction collapses because necessity concerns the form of possible experience rather than supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MME-05","text":"Subjective conditions cease to be merely private psychological facts and become transcendental grounds of objective validity within possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:ME2","CH_B337_CISA:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MME-06","text":"Logical rules, categorial principles, and mathematical cognition become differentiated domains with distinct sources and standards of warrant.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:ME3","CH_B337_SISP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the cluster determines how categories, transcendental principles, and judgments can legitimately apply to phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:ALT1","CH_B337_CISA:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, objective cognition is generated by the coordinated distribution of sensibility, imagination, understanding, apperception, and logical judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:ALT2","CH_B337_SISP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the cluster converts claims of metaphysical reach into questions of jurisdiction, conditions of application, and proof.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:ALT3","CH_B337_SISP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological-temporal level, objects of cognition are phenomena constituted as temporally determinable under categorial rules.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:ALT4","CH_B337_CISA:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological level, the system performs its own claim by repeatedly restricting a rule’s use to the conditions that make its valid operation possible.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:AS7","CH_B337_CISP:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:FL-01","text":"Subjective conditions are made to ground objective validity, but the conversion from conditions of cognition to conditions of objects remains architecturally asserted more fully than displayed.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:MRT6","CH_B337_CISA:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:FL-02","text":"Imagination must be simultaneously sensible enough to determine intuition and intellectual enough to enact categorial rules without collapsing into either faculty.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A4","CH_B337_CISP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:FL-03","text":"The principle of contradiction is formally contentless, yet its positive analytical employment depends upon determinate conceptual content.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:SD6","CH_B337_SISP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MP-01","text":"Time mutates from the universal medium of synthetic objectivity into an inadmissible addition when inserted into the formal principle of contradiction.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:ME3","CH_B337_SISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MP-02","text":"Categories mutate from objectively meaningful rules within experience into merely logically significant forms beyond experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:SD4","CH_B337_CISP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MP-03","text":"Systematic completeness is claimed at the level of generative architecture while the individual derivation of the particular principles remains deferred.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:SD5","CH_B337_CISA:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:HSI-01","text":"The system imagines cognition as a translation machine that converts formal rules into phenomenal order through temporal synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:A9","CH_B337_CISP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not a self-sufficient knower but a functional nexus coordinating receptivity, imagination, understanding, and apperception.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:HSI-03","text":"Time is both the universal channel through which objects become cognizable and the quarantine line separating synthetic object-determination from pure logical form.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:ME3","CH_B337_SISP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:HSI-04","text":"Critical struggle consists in assigning every cognitive power and principle its proper jurisdiction while preventing its illegitimate expansion.","refs":["CH_B337_SISP:SD4","CH_B337_CISA:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:HSI-05","text":"Beyond possible experience lies not simple nonsense but a residual field of thinkability without determinable objects.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:SD4","CH_B337_CISP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_15:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s central movement from heterogeneity through schematized application to a bounded system of justified principles.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:MRT1","CH_B337_CISA:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MRT-B02","text":"The detailed derivation of individual principles and the productive mechanism of imagination remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_CISA:MRT5","CH_B337_CISP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MRT-B03","text":"Coherence depends on preserving both the mediation of intellectual rules with sensible intuition and the conversion of subjective conditions into objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:MRT4","CH_B337_CISA:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where realization and restriction are treated as one operation and logical jurisdiction is separated from objective truth.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:MRT7","CH_B337_SISP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_15:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if it retains recursive return between mediation, proof, restriction, and the unresolved limits of formal logic.","refs":["CH_B337_CISP:MRT7","CH_B337_SISP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 43–45","context":"Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding · Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding · Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements"}},{"id":"CL_B337_16","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 46–48","order":109,"parents":["CH_B337_SI_2","CH_B337_SISR","CH_B337_CIGD"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"1a52f48d802d9362dc9811aeb6991dcdf076e079ce45e907e8984c2f2e1286b2","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:AS-01","text":"The system must establish the objective validity of synthetic a priori judgment while determining the immutable boundary beyond which that validity cannot extend.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:AS1","CH_B337_CIGD:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AS-02","text":"Synthetic a priori cognition is objectively valid only by articulating conditions necessary for possible experience, and those same conditions restrict pure concepts to empirical use.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A10","CH_B337_CIGD:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AS-03","text":"The argument therefore becomes a transcendental determination of the constitution and jurisdiction of experience rather than a comparison of concepts with independently available objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:SD4","CH_B337_CIGD:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AS-04","text":"Sensible intuition, imagination, temporal determination, categorial synthesis, and apperceptive unity form the distributed mediation through which an object becomes cognizable.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:AS4","CH_B337_CIGD:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AS-05","text":"The general condition of objective synthesis becomes operational through category-guided classes of principles that determine appearances extensively and intensively without determining their empirical qualities.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:AS3","CH_B337_SISR:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AS-06","text":"Mathematics applies to appearances because pure construction and phenomenal apprehension enact the same synthesis, not because mathematics corresponds to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:SD2","CH_B337_SISR:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AS-07","text":"When intuition and schema are removed, categories retain logical form but lose objective employment; the noumenon can consequently survive only as a negative limit-concept.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:D2","CH_B337_CIGD:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AS-08","text":"The movement is a recursive tightening from conditions of object-cognition, through systematic phenomenal determination, to semantic deprivation and negative closure at the boundary of human cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:AS8","CH_B337_CIGD:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] A priori cognition has objective validity only insofar as it expresses conditions constitutive of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A10","CH_B337_CIGD:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Every objectively valid synthetic connection requires a mediating third term, which human cognition can obtain only through possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A4","CH_B337_CIGD:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Cognizable objecthood arises through the coordinated operation of sensible givenness, imaginative synthesis, temporal form, categorial determination, and apperceptive unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:AS4","CH_B337_CIGD:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Pure understanding supplies necessary rules for objects but acquires objective employment only through intuition and schema.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A1","CH_B337_CIGD:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] The condition that enables pure understanding to determine objects is also the condition that limits its authority to appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:SD3","CH_B337_CIGD:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] A priori cognition determines invariant forms of empirical content but cannot determine the specific quality or actuality of that content.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:SD4","CH_B337_SISR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Appearance is doubly quantified and continuous: its spatiotemporal form is extensive, while its sensible reality possesses intensive degree.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:SD3","CH_B337_SISR:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Mathematics applies objectively to appearances because pure construction and empirical apprehension share a constitutive spatiotemporal synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:SD2","CH_B337_SISR:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is simultaneously the criterion of objective reality, the domain of valid cognition, and the terminal jurisdiction of pure principles.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:AF4","CH_B337_CIGD:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The noumenon is admissible only as an empty negative boundary and never as a positively cognizable field of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A17","CH_B337_CIGD:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] The object ceases to function as an independently available terminus and becomes the correlate of a manifold constituted by necessary rule-governed synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:SD2","CH_B337_SISR:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:RMD-01","text":"Possible experience retains its identity as the ground of validity but expands from a criterion of objectivity into the total jurisdiction and boundary of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:AF4","CH_B337_CIGD:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RMD-02","text":"The third term changes function from a general mediator of synthetic connection, to the schema enabling subsumption, and finally to the missing mediation that makes noumenal synthesis impossible.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A4","CH_B337_CIGD:A6","CH_B337_CIGD:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RMD-03","text":"Synthesis migrates from the general unification of representations into the productive construction of determinate intuition and then becomes the condition whose removal deprives categories of objective use.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A6","CH_B337_SISR:A10","CH_B337_CIGD:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RMD-04","text":"Objectivity mutates from experiential applicability into conformity with the a priori syntheses that make an object presentable and cognizable.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:SD1","CH_B337_SISR:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RMD-05","text":"Pure understanding first appears as the source of necessary rule-structure, but its legislative role is absorbed into a larger sensibility–understanding relation that blocks pure ontology.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:AS2","CH_B337_CIGD:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RMD-06","text":"Intuition expands from the temporal field of mediation into the common basis of mathematical construction, phenomenal apprehension, and schematic application.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A5","CH_B337_SISR:D4","CH_B337_CIGD:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RMD-07","text":"The opposition between a priori necessity and empirical relevance is absorbed into a constitutive relation in which the a priori supplies the form of experience rather than cognition beyond it.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:ME5","CH_B337_CIGD:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RMD-08","text":"Noumenon reverses valence from an apparent supersensible object-class into a limit-operator that prevents the determinate field of cognition from claiming unrestricted scope.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:NX5","CH_B337_CIGD:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RMD-09","text":"Zero performs local work as the limiting negation of phenomenal degree and then disappears, while its anti-ontological function persists as the prohibition against converting nonperception into nonbeing.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:SNX2","CH_B337_SISR:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:CGD-01","text":"The analytical–synthetic distinction remains local as an initial diagnostic, while its demand for a third term scales upward into the general architecture of mediation and jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:AS2","CH_B337_SI_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:CGD-02","text":"The detailed category table remains lower-layer classificatory machinery, while the requirement that categories receive rules of objective employment scales into a global account of legitimate use.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:D1","CH_B337_SISR:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:CGD-03","text":"The proofs of extensive and intensive quantity remain local demonstrations, but their conjunction scales upward as the double quantitative constitution of appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:SD3","CH_B337_SISR:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:CGD-04","text":"The anti-void argument remains a restricted inferential intervention and scales only as a prohibition against turning perceptual absence into an ontology of absence.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:MRT8","CH_B337_SISR:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:CGD-05","text":"The identity of pure construction and empirical apprehension bridges the supreme principle of synthetic judgment with the objective applicability of mathematics.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:SD2","CH_B337_SI_2:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:CGD-06","text":"Schema and third-term mediation bridge the positive account of objective synthesis with the negative demonstration that no synthetic noumenal judgment is possible.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:D8","CH_B337_SI_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:CGD-07","text":"A positive division into sensible and intelligible worlds becomes obsolete and is superseded by an asymmetry between a determinate cognitive field and an indeterminate boundary.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:NX5","CH_B337_CIGD:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:CGD-08","text":"The independence of categories from sensibility in origin is recoded as dependence on sensibility in application, preventing origin from determining legitimate scope.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:D4","CH_B337_CIGD:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:GNX-01","text":"Internally coherent concepts and logical consistency cannot by themselves establish objective reference or the real possibility of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:DNX1","CH_B337_CIGD:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GNX-02","text":"Synthetic judgment cannot be reduced to identity, contradiction, or conceptual containment and cannot become objectively valid without an intuitively grounded mediation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:NX2","CH_B337_SI_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GNX-03","text":"Objects cannot first be given as ruleless realities and only afterward subjected to the forms that make them appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:DNX1","CH_B337_SISR:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GNX-04","text":"Categories cannot acquire objective use beyond sensible intuition and schema, because abstraction from those conditions leaves no determinable object.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:NX1","CH_B337_CIGD:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GNX-05","text":"A priori cognition cannot anticipate the particular quality, origin, or empirical actuality of sensation.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:NX7","CH_B337_SISR:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GNX-06","text":"Failure to perceive a sensible degree cannot establish empty space, empty time, or the complete absence of phenomenal reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:DNX5","CH_B337_SISR:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:GNX-07","text":"Phenomena and noumena cannot form two positively known worlds, because the intellectual intuition required for positive noumenal cognition is unavailable.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:NX5","CH_B337_CIGD:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires pure thought to be incapable of giving itself an object independently of sensible presentation.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:DBN-02","text":"The architecture requires necessity to be irreducible to repeated experience or accumulated empirical generality.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:DBN-03","text":"Objective validity cannot consist in correspondence with a noumenal structure available independently of the conditions of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:SNX2","CH_B337_SISR:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:DBN-04","text":"Continuity cannot be constituted by assembling indivisible elements that exist prior to the continuum.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:DBN-05","text":"Categories cannot generate their own manifold, intuition, schema, or object of application.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:DBN-06","text":"The legitimate scope of a concept cannot be inferred from its a priori or non-sensible origin.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:DBN-07","text":"A cognitive boundary cannot disclose the positive constitution of what lies beyond it or support an absolute survey of sensible and intelligible domains.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:DNX6","CH_B337_CIGD:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:BSA-01","text":"Intellectual intuition is the missing faculty around which the terminal boundary is organized: it alone could enable positive noumenal cognition, yet it is unavailable to human cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:SNX1","CH_B337_CIGD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:BSA-02","text":"The deepest missing mediation is a non-empirical third term capable of grounding synthetic judgments about things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:SNX2","CH_B337_CIGD:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:BSA-03","text":"No absolute standpoint is available from which appearances and a positive intelligible world could be surveyed as two completed domains.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:SNX5","CH_B337_CIGD:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:BSA-04","text":"The system is organized around a remainder that cannot be identified with appearance, positively predicated, or converted into proof that sensible appearance exhausts reality.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:SNX4","CH_B337_CIGD:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:XD-01","text":"Because possible experience both validates and restricts pure understanding, transcendental objectivity is intrinsically jurisdictional rather than indefinitely extensible.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-01","CL_B337_16:GA-05","CL_B337_16:GA-09","CH_B337_SI_2:SD3","CH_B337_CIGD:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:XD-02","text":"The same mediating requirement that establishes synthetic judgment within experience invalidates synthetic judgment about things in themselves when the required third term is absent.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-02","CL_B337_16:GA-10","CH_B337_CIGD:A12","CH_B337_CIGD:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:XD-03","text":"Because appearance is constituted through rule-governed synthesis, extensive and intensive quantity are not external measurements imposed on finished objects but structural modes of phenomenal objecthood.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-07","CL_B337_16:GA-11","CH_B337_SISR:SD3","CH_B337_SISR:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:XD-04","text":"A priori cognition can anticipate that sensation has degree while remaining unable to determine specific sensible quality or the actual constitution of matter.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-06","CL_B337_16:GA-07","CH_B337_SISR:D9","CH_B337_SISR:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:XD-05","text":"Mathematical objectivity follows from shared synthesis rather than external correspondence: the operation determining pure space also governs empirical spatial apprehension.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-03","CL_B337_16:GA-08","CH_B337_SISR:SD2","CH_B337_SISR:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:XD-06","text":"A limit in sensible givenness never directly yields an ontology: nonperception does not prove absence, and unavailable intuition does not disclose a noumenal object.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-02","CL_B337_16:GA-10","CH_B337_SISR:SD5","CH_B337_CIGD:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:XD-07","text":"Pure understanding is legislative only relationally, because its rule-giving authority cannot supply the sensible manifold or object that its rules determine.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-04","CL_B337_16:GA-05","CH_B337_SISR:MRT6","CH_B337_CIGD:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:XD-08","text":"Thought can exceed cognition only by retaining an indeterminate boundary-place; it cannot transform that excess into objective knowledge of a wider domain.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-09","CL_B337_16:GA-10","CH_B337_CIGD:SD4","CH_B337_CIGD:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:BSD-01","text":"Transcendental necessity is self-limiting constitution: pure understanding possesses authority precisely where its principles make experience possible and loses authority when detached from that function.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:SD3","CH_B337_CIGD:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:BSD-02","text":"The cognizable object is a rule-governed appearance constituted through unified synthesis, while the status of that object apart from human intuition remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:SD2","CH_B337_CIGD:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:BSD-03","text":"Time functions as the hidden universal medium connecting synthetic judgment, schema, quantitative apprehension, and objective categorial application.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A5","CH_B337_CIGD:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:BSD-04","text":"The subject is not an intellectual creator of objects but the formal unity under which a sensibly given manifold can become one experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A7","CH_B337_CIGD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:BSD-05","text":"Metaphysics is transformed from a putative ontology of unrestricted being into boundary-knowledge concerning the conditions and jurisdiction of understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:SD1","CH_B337_CIGD:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:BSD-06","text":"Across both physical and metaphysical contexts, nonpresentation licenses inferential restraint rather than positive claims about absence or supersensible reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:SD5","CH_B337_CIGD:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:BSD-07","text":"The characteristic proof procedure is deprivation-testing: a concept’s objective status is established by identifying its experiential conditions and then showing the loss of meaning or application when those conditions are removed.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:AS4","CH_B337_SISR:AS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:RLE-01","text":"Possible experience is the principal attractor: its conditions determine possible objects, those objects are governed by principles, and the resulting lawful unity is what counts as experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A11","CH_B337_SI_2:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RLE-02","text":"Synthetic excess escalates into a demand for mediation, mediation into unified experience, and unified experience into the criterion of objective reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:FGS2","CH_B337_SI_2:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RLE-03","text":"The category table generates objective-use rules, those rules delimit classes of principles, and empirical laws return as particular applications of the resulting a priori structure.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A6","CH_B337_SISR:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RLE-04","text":"A priori origin repeatedly produces the temptation of transcendental reach, which critique recursively corrects by restricting application to possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:D4","CH_B337_CIGD:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RLE-05","text":"Synthesis becomes a machine rather than a single operation: it successively organizes representation, determinate intuition, phenomenal quantity, apperceptive unity, and objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A10","CH_B337_CIGD:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RLE-06","text":"Repeated prohibitions against inferring ontology from perceptual failure undergo a phase change when generalized into the prohibition against treating a cognitive boundary as disclosure of the beyond.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:DNX5","CH_B337_CIGD:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:RLE-07","text":"Calling an object a phenomenon generates the thought of what it might be apart from human intuition, but that thought recursively returns as a negative restriction on cognition rather than an escape from it.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A13","CH_B337_CIGD:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:FGS-B01","text":"Conceptual non-containment → synthetic excess beyond analytical logic → requirement for a mediating ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:FGS-B02","text":"Temporal intuition + imaginative synthesis + apperceptive unity → unified manifold capable of entering judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:FGS-B03","text":"Rule-governed unification → possible experience → object as the correlate of synthesized appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:FGS4","CH_B337_SI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:FGS-B04","text":"Categories → rules of objective employment → differentiated principles of quantity, quality, relation, and modality.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:A6","CH_B337_SISR:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:FGS-B05","text":"Shared pure and empirical synthesis → objective mathematical applicability → a priori determination of form and degree without empirical quality.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:SD2","CH_B337_SISR:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:FGS-B06","text":"Removal of sensible intuition and schema → category reduced to logical form → loss of determinate object and objective inference.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:D2","CH_B337_CIGD:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:FGS-B07","text":"Attempted noumenal synthesis → missing intuitive third term → rejection of positive noumena → retention of an empty negative boundary.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:FGS8","CH_B337_CIGD:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:AFM-B01","text":"Analytical insufficiency → synthetic excess → search for a mediating third term.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:AF1","CH_B337_SI_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AFM-B02","text":"Mediating third term → temporal intuition, imagination, and apperception → connected cognition rather than dispersed perception.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AFM-B03","text":"Unified experience → criterion of objective reality → supreme principle governing every possible object.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:AF4","CH_B337_SI_2:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AFM-B04","text":"Supreme principle → category-guided classification → extensive form and intensive sensible reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:AS3","CH_B337_SISR:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AFM-B05","text":"Systematic phenomenal determination → boundary between a priori form and empirical actuality → diagnosis of illusory transcendental reach.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:SD6","CH_B337_CIGD:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:AFM-B06","text":"Removal of intuition and schema → loss of objective inference → rejection of positive noumena → retention of the negative limit.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:D3","CH_B337_CIGD:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from direct concept–object correspondence into necessary validity within a field constituted by rule-governed synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:ME2","CH_B337_SISR:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MME-02","text":"The opposition between a priori and empirical cognition collapses into a division of labor: the a priori supplies experiential form while empirical cognition supplies determinate content.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:ME5","CH_B337_CIGD:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MME-03","text":"Mathematical applicability is relocated from correspondence with things in themselves to the constitutive spatiotemporal synthesis shared by construction and apprehension.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:ME3","CH_B337_SISR:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MME-04","text":"Quantity is divided into extensive composition and intensive degree, while perceptual absence is severed from conclusions about ontological absence.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:ME4","CH_B337_SISR:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MME-05","text":"Thought and cognition become separable, and noumenon is reclassified from a supersensible object into a limit-operator governing claims made by sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:ME4","CH_B337_CIGD:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MME-06","text":"Metaphysics becomes a jurisdictional map that differentiates valid use, invalid extension, unavoidable illusion, and boundary-knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:ME7","CH_B337_CIGD:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The explicit field comprises the supreme principle of synthetic judgment, the systematic classification of pure principles, and the question whether categories can apply to noumena.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:ALT1","CH_B337_SISR:ALT1","CH_B337_CIGD:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Sensible intuition, productive synthesis, categories, schema, sensory receptivity, and apperception jointly constitute determinate phenomenal objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:ALT2","CH_B337_SISR:ALT2","CH_B337_CIGD:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] The system relocates truth into transcendental conditions, separates possibility-conditions from empirical explanations, and determines legitimacy before speculative extension.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:ALT3","CH_B337_SISR:ALT3","CH_B337_CIGD:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Phenomenal reality is spatiotemporal, continuously divisible, intensively graduated, and objectively categorizable only through temporal determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:ALT4","CH_B337_SISR:ALT4","CH_B337_CIGD:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The system performs its jurisdictional thesis by moving from transcendental ground through systematic determination and then testing concepts through semantic deprivation at the boundary.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:AS8","CH_B337_CIGD:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:FL-01","text":"Pure understanding is declared the source of necessary principles, yet mathematical objectivity remains inseparable from pure intuition and its distinctive synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:MRT6","CH_B337_SISR:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:FL-02","text":"Objects must be given for cognition, yet the formal conditions of synthesis determine what can count as an object in the first place.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:MRT6","CH_B337_SISR:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:FL-03","text":"The negative noumenon is declared necessary as a limit even though its objective possibility cannot be comprehended or positively established.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:MRT6","CH_B337_CIGD:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:FL-04","text":"The thing in itself remains unstable between the same object considered apart from human intuition and a merely possible non-sensible object.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:MRT2","CH_B337_CIGD:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:FL-05","text":"The derivation of universal intensive continuity depends on a compressed transition from the diminishability of sensation to the claim that every sensation admits indefinitely smaller degrees.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:MRT5","CH_B337_SISR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:FL-06","text":"The terminal closure governs human cognition and its sensible intuition, not every possible intellect, so the boundary is immutable only within the declared cognitive jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:MRT8","CH_B337_CIGD:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MP-01","text":"The third term mutates from a positive mediator of synthetic connection, to a temporal schema of subsumption, to the absent mediation that closes noumenal synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A4","CH_B337_CIGD:A6","CH_B337_CIGD:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MP-02","text":"Pure understanding mutates from constitutive legislator into a faculty whose highest critical achievement is the simultaneous limitation of sensibility and its own claims.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:AS2","CH_B337_CIGD:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MP-03","text":"Noumenon mutates from the apparent object of non-sensible intuition into a negative operator that regulates the scope of sensible cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A15","CH_B337_CIGD:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MP-04","text":"A priori necessity reverses its apparent direction: instead of authorizing cognition beyond experience, it becomes the form that makes experience possible.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:ME5","CH_B337_CIGD:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:HSI-01","text":"Reality-for-cognition is a conversion machine in which a sensible manifold passes through temporal and rule-governed synthesis to emerge as a determinate appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:A4","CH_B337_SISR:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:HSI-02","text":"The subject is the formal unity of synthesis within a receptive field, not an intellectual intuition capable of generating its own objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A7","CH_B337_CIGD:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:HSI-03","text":"Time is the continuous medium of synthesis, schema, quantity, and categorial application rather than a neutral container external to cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:A5","CH_B337_SISR:A17","CH_B337_CIGD:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:HSI-04","text":"The remaining strategic action is jurisdictional navigation: determine valid use within experience, resist illicit extension, and preserve the noumenal beyond only as an empty boundary.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:ME7","CH_B337_CIGD:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_16:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted architecture is sufficient to reconstruct the movement from analytical insufficiency through transcendental grounding and systematic phenomenal determination to negative noumenal closure.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:MRT1","CH_B337_CIGD:MRT1","CH_B337_SISR:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the derivation of specific pure concepts and the undeveloped proofs of the Analogies and Postulates.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:MRT2","CH_B337_SISR:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if objective validity is defined through possible experience and thought is strictly distinguished from cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:MRT3","CH_B337_CIGD:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where shared synthesis simultaneously explains objective mathematical application, phenomenal constitution, and the restriction of categories.","refs":["CH_B337_SISR:MRT4","CH_B337_CIGD:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on compressed transitions from unified experience to universal necessity and from diminishable sensation to continuous intensive degree.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_2:MRT5","CH_B337_SISR:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_16:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable element resistant to complete axiomatic reduction is the thing-in-itself remainder: it must prevent appearance from becoming absolute, yet cannot receive positive predicates without destroying the critical boundary.","refs":["CH_B337_CIGD:MRT2","CH_B337_CIGD:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 46–48","context":"Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements · Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding · Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena"}},{"id":"CL_B337_17","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 49–51","order":110,"parents":["CH_B337_APPX","CH_B337_EXPE","CH_B337_EMPI"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"6f3d20f7fd553fc7adf6371718671d125602759903f248a82824841a6183093b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:AS-B01","text":"The system asks how representations can acquire objective validity when neither conceptual comparison, contingent perception, nor unrestricted modal judgment can establish an object by itself.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:AS1","CH_B337_EXPE:AS1","CH_B337_EMPI:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AS-B02","text":"Objective cognition arises when representations are assigned to their proper faculty, joined through rule-governed temporal synthesis, and assessed within the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:AS2","CH_B337_EXPE:AS3","CH_B337_EMPI:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AS-B03","text":"Sensibility and understanding remain irreducible yet reciprocally dependent, because intuition supplies givenness while categorial synthesis supplies objective unity.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A4","CH_B337_APPX:A5","CH_B337_EMPI:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AS-B04","text":"The movement proceeds from diagnosing logical comparison as metaphysically insufficient, through the lawful determination of temporal relations, to the experiential certification of possibility, reality, and necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:AS3","CH_B337_EXPE:AS4","CH_B337_EMPI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AS-B05","text":"Externality progressively becomes an individuating, integrating, and temporally grounding condition through spatial position, reciprocal community, and enduring matter.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A9","CH_B337_EXPE:A16","CH_B337_EMPI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AS-B06","text":"The argument becomes a transcendental jurisdiction that derives objective validity and cognitive restriction from the same enabling conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:ALT3","CH_B337_EMPI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AS-B07","text":"Its movement is recursive: each failure of immediate access generates a mediating rule, and every successful mediation passes through a scope restriction that returns cognition to phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:SNX1","CH_B337_EXPE:FGS10","CH_B337_EMPI:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AS-B08","text":"Closure is asymmetrical: the phenomenal field becomes objectively unified and empirically extensible, while noumenal cognition and absolute modality remain unavailable.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SD4","CH_B337_EXPE:D15","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective validity is jurisdictional rather than intrinsic: it depends on the relation among cognitive source, conditions of givenness, and rule-governed synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SD1","CH_B337_EXPE:SD1","CH_B337_EMPI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Sensibility and understanding are irreducible sources whose conjunction is required for objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A4","CH_B337_APPX:A5","CH_B337_EMPI:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Categories acquire objective validity only through intuition, temporal schematization, and possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A12","CH_B337_EXPE:A7","CH_B337_EMPI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] The conditions constituting empirical objectivity simultaneously delimit cognition, so validation and restriction are one operation rather than successive corrections.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SD4","CH_B337_EXPE:SD6","CH_B337_EMPI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Objectivity progresses from correct faculty-assignment, through necessary temporal organization, to modal assessment relative to cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:AS2","CH_B337_EXPE:AS2","CH_B337_EMPI:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Logical comparison, associative succession, and conceptual noncontradiction are necessary but insufficient for real identity, temporal connection, or objective possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:NX1","CH_B337_EXPE:NX2","CH_B337_EMPI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Because time is not perceived directly, temporal objectivity must be produced relationally through phenomenal connections and external permanence.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A4","CH_B337_EXPE:D2","CH_B337_EMPI:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The phenomenal world converges upon one lawful dynamical network of enduring subjects, ordered events, reciprocal coexistences, and perceptual connections.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:SD2","CH_B337_EXPE:SD4","CH_B337_EMPI:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Externality changes from a form of numerical individuation into the medium of dynamical community and finally into a condition of determinate self-consciousness.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A9","CH_B337_EXPE:A17","CH_B337_EMPI:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Necessity belongs to causally governed phenomenal relations and never establishes the absolute necessity of substances or things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A13","CH_B337_EMPI:A14","CH_B337_EMPI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Robust empirical objectivity is compatible with noumenal unknowability because the noumenon functions only as a limiting marker.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:AS6","CH_B337_EXPE:SD5","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-12","text":"[Persistent] A priori principles legislate forms of connection and discoverability without constructing particular existences, forces, or determinate contents.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:AS6","CH_B337_EXPE:SD3","CH_B337_EMPI:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] The empirical subject is an apperceptive unity temporally dependent on external relations rather than an internally intuited permanent substance.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A3","CH_B337_EMPI:SD4","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:RMD-01","text":"Objectivity retains its identity as warranted cognition but changes function from faculty-sensitive comparison to temporal synthesis and then to modal validation.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SD1","CH_B337_EXPE:ME1","CH_B337_EMPI:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RMD-02","text":"Relation migrates from reflective comparison among concepts into lawful relations among existences and finally into relations between object-concepts and cognitive faculties.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A6","CH_B337_EXPE:AS6","CH_B337_EMPI:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RMD-03","text":"Time shifts from an a priori form distinguishing sensibility from understanding into an imperceptible relational order whose empirical determination requires external permanence.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A8","CH_B337_EXPE:AS3","CH_B337_EMPI:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RMD-04","text":"Permanence changes from the phenomenal substratum of temporal change into spatial matter’s grounding role and then becomes the absent property of pure inner self-presence.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A9","CH_B337_EMPI:A11","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RMD-05","text":"Causality expands from the rule that determines objective succession into the form of material necessity governing transitions between phenomenal states.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A13","CH_B337_EMPI:A13","CH_B337_EMPI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RMD-06","text":"Externality moves from spatial individuation through reciprocal influence to the transcendental grounding of inner temporal experience.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:D2","CH_B337_EXPE:A16","CH_B337_EMPI:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RMD-07","text":"Emptiness is displaced from a categorical taxonomy of failed object-reference into the diagnosis of categories lacking intuition and concepts lacking experiential constructibility.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:D11","CH_B337_EMPI:A6","CH_B337_EMPI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RMD-08","text":"Nature absorbs the analogies and modal criteria into one temporally unified, lawfully connected perceptual field.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:D15","CH_B337_EXPE:ME8","CH_B337_EMPI:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RMD-09","text":"The noumenon retains a stable negative function across the system: it marks limitation without becoming a determinately possible object.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:NX10","CH_B337_EXPE:SD5","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RMD-10","text":"Polemics against rationalism, empiricism, and idealism disappear after exposing a common structural error: the attempt to privilege one cognitive source or one side of the inner–outer relation.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:ME6","CH_B337_EMPI:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:CGD-01","text":"The four titles of reflection remain local diagnostic instruments, while their scalable result is the principle that conceptual relations change when transferred into sensuous cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A6","CH_B337_APPX:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:CGD-02","text":"Indiscernibility, monadology, pre-established harmony, and intellectualized space remain local doctrinal targets whose current-level significance is their derivation from one-source cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SD2","CH_B337_APPX:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:CGD-03","text":"The categorical table of nothing remains weakly integrated locally, but it bridges upward by distinguishing contradiction from other failures of objective instantiation.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:MRT8","CH_B337_APPX:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:CGD-04","text":"The three analogies remain specific rules for permanence, succession, and coexistence, while their scalable function is to generate one dynamically unified nature.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:AS4","CH_B337_EXPE:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:CGD-05","text":"Conservation of substantial quantum and the continuity theorem remain lower-layer extensions because they exceed the minimally necessary proof of temporal order.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:MRT4","CH_B337_EXPE:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:CGD-06","text":"The three modal postulates scale upward because they convert possibility, reality, and necessity into successive degrees of experiential integration.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:SD1","CH_B337_EMPI:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:CGD-07","text":"The refutation of idealism is a transition catalyst whose current-level effect is to make externality constitutive of empirical subjectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:AS5","CH_B337_EMPI:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:CGD-08","text":"Mathematical construction and the terminology of postulation remain local validation cases rather than independent global axioms.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:D4","CH_B337_EMPI:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:CGD-09","text":"Complete dynamical community scales into the unity of nature, although the transition from cognizable coexistence to universal interaction remains compressed.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:MRT6","CH_B337_EXPE:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:GNX-01","text":"Neither logical comparison nor noncontradiction suffices to establish real identity, opposition, or objective possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:NX1","CH_B337_EMPI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GNX-02","text":"Neither sensation nor contingent apprehension contains the necessary connection required for objective experience.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:NX1","CH_B337_EXPE:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GNX-03","text":"Categories without sensuous intuition are logical forms rather than cognitions of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:NX9","CH_B337_EMPI:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GNX-04","text":"Critical restriction cannot authorize positive knowledge of an intelligible world or synthetic knowledge of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:NX12","CH_B337_EXPE:NX7","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GNX-05","text":"Existence cannot be extracted from conceptual completeness or from analysis of relations among concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A8","CH_B337_EXPE:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GNX-06","text":"Inner experience is neither autonomously immediate nor grounded in an intuited permanent self-substance.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:NX7","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GNX-07","text":"Objective temporal order cannot be measured against a separately perceived absolute time.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:NX3","CH_B337_EXPE:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GNX-08","text":"Uncaused events, disconnected empirical times, blind chance, and wholly isolated coexistences are excluded from one possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:SNX3","CH_B337_EXPE:DNX7","CH_B337_EMPI:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:GNX-09","text":"Space and time cannot be transferred from forms of appearance into predicates of things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:NX8","CH_B337_EMPI:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:DBN-01","text":"A one-source model of cognition must be false, because eliminating either sensibility or understanding destroys objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SD3","CH_B337_APPX:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:DBN-02","text":"Objects cannot possess cognizable objectivity or modality independently of their conditions of givenness and validation.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:ME1","CH_B337_EMPI:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:DBN-03","text":"Time cannot function as a directly perceived container, because objective temporal determination is possible only through relations among phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:SNX1","CH_B337_EXPE:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:DBN-04","text":"A self-sufficient inner subject cannot ground temporal cognition without external permanence or an intuition of itself as substance.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:DNX3","CH_B337_EMPI:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:DBN-05","text":"Necessity cannot belong absolutely to substances, because its valid phenomenal form is conditioned causal determination.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A14","CH_B337_EMPI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:DBN-06","text":"Empirically cognizable plurality cannot remain dynamically disconnected while still composing one temporal field.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:DNX6","CH_B337_EXPE:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:DBN-07","text":"Abstraction from conditions of intuition cannot reveal the object as it is independently of those conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A15","CH_B337_APPX:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:DBN-08","text":"No complete a priori science can determine which entities, forces, or states actually exist.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:SNX5","CH_B337_EXPE:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSA-01","text":"Intellectual intuition and direct perceptual access to objective order are absent, so cognition must operate through mediated synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SNX1","CH_B337_EXPE:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSA-02","text":"No standpoint is available from which appearance can be compared with its in-itself ground or the complete modal domains surveyed.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SNX5","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSA-03","text":"The ground coordinating subjective forms with objective appearance remains inaccessible even though that coordination is presupposed by experience.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSA-04","text":"Pure self-presence is structurally absent; the subject encounters itself only through temporally ordered appearances dependent on external permanence.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:SNX2","CH_B337_EMPI:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSA-05","text":"Absolute possibility and alternative totalities of experience remain unavailable to the understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:SNX3","CH_B337_EMPI:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSA-06","text":"Positive noumenal cognition is the terminal excluded object, while the intelligible remains only as an empty boundary-space.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SNX4","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:XD-01","text":"Correct faculty-assignment is necessary but insufficient for objectivity; representations must also be integrated through rules determining their temporal relations.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-01","CL_B337_17:GA-05","CL_B337_17:GA-07","CH_B337_APPX:AS2","CH_B337_EXPE:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:XD-02","text":"The permanent substratum required for temporal determination becomes empirically available as externally intuited matter, making outer experience a condition of determinate inner experience.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-07","CL_B337_17:GA-09","CL_B337_17:GA-13","CH_B337_EXPE:A9","CH_B337_EMPI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:XD-03","text":"Externality is not merely one class of represented objects; it individuates appearances, integrates coexistence, and anchors temporal self-consciousness.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-08","CL_B337_17:GA-09","CL_B337_17:GA-13","CH_B337_APPX:A9","CH_B337_EXPE:A17","CH_B337_EMPI:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:XD-04","text":"Logical consistency cannot establish a real event or objective possibility, because both require synthesis within determinate experiential relations.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-06","CL_B337_17:GA-07","CH_B337_APPX:D3","CH_B337_EXPE:D8","CH_B337_EMPI:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:XD-05","text":"The objective meaning of a category is exhausted by the operations it can perform within schematized possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-03","CL_B337_17:GA-04","CH_B337_APPX:A12","CH_B337_EXPE:A7","CH_B337_EMPI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:XD-06","text":"Nature and empirical reality become two descriptions of the same lawful perceptual network: one emphasizes temporal-dynamical unity, the other modal membership.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-08","CL_B337_17:GA-10","CH_B337_EXPE:D15","CH_B337_EMPI:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:XD-07","text":"The absence of immediate intellectual or temporal access does not weaken empirical objectivity; it makes lawful mediation constitutive of objectivity.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-01","CL_B337_17:GA-04","CL_B337_17:GA-07","CH_B337_APPX:SNX1","CH_B337_EXPE:SNX1","CH_B337_EXPE:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:XD-08","text":"One-source reductions and the priority of inner experience fail for the same reason: each suppresses a relation whose terms become objective only through mutual conditioning.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-02","CL_B337_17:GA-09","CL_B337_17:GA-13","CH_B337_APPX:SD3","CH_B337_EMPI:NX7","CH_B337_EMPI:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:XD-09","text":"Cognition is horizontally open to indefinite empirical investigation but vertically closed against transition from appearances to their non-sensuous ground.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-04","CL_B337_17:GA-11","CL_B337_17:GA-12","CH_B337_APPX:SD7","CH_B337_EXPE:SD5","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:XD-10","text":"Failed object-reference is graded by failures of intuition, synthesis, perception, or law rather than reduced to contradiction alone.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-03","CL_B337_17:GA-06","CH_B337_APPX:SD6","CH_B337_EMPI:A5","CH_B337_EMPI:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSD-01","text":"Phenomenal objects are stabilized nodes within a lawful network rather than independently cognized substances carrying their objectivity intrinsically.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-01","CL_B337_17:GA-08","CH_B337_EXPE:SD2","CH_B337_EMPI:SD6","CH_B337_APPX:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSD-02","text":"One phenomenal time and one empirical subject are co-generated through temporal synthesis, external permanence, and apperceptive unity.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-07","CL_B337_17:GA-09","CL_B337_17:GA-13","CH_B337_EXPE:SD4","CH_B337_EMPI:D6","CH_B337_EMPI:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSD-03","text":"Epistemic validity becomes a matter of lawful jurisdiction rather than correspondence verified from a standpoint outside appearance.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-01","CL_B337_17:GA-11","CH_B337_APPX:SD1","CH_B337_EXPE:SD1","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSD-04","text":"Possibility, reality, and necessity are successive intensities of integration into one experiential order rather than intrinsic modal properties added to objects.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-04","CL_B337_17:GA-08","CL_B337_17:GA-10","CH_B337_EMPI:SD1","CH_B337_EMPI:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSD-05","text":"Externality becomes the distributed infrastructure of phenomenal cognition by individuating objects, connecting coexistences, and stabilizing self-determination.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-09","CL_B337_17:GA-13","CH_B337_APPX:A9","CH_B337_EXPE:A17","CH_B337_EMPI:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSD-06","text":"Causality simultaneously distinguishes events from subjective succession, converts real transitions into necessities, and integrates them into nature.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-08","CL_B337_17:GA-10","CH_B337_EXPE:A12","CH_B337_EMPI:A13","CH_B337_EMPI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSD-07","text":"Critique operates methodologically by translating metaphysical assertions into questions about cognitive conditions, objective employment, and scope.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-01","CL_B337_17:GA-04","CH_B337_APPX:AS3","CH_B337_EXPE:ALT3","CH_B337_EMPI:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:BSD-08","text":"The system replaces vertical progress toward supersensible cognition with horizontal empirical extensibility inside a permanently bounded phenomenal field.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-11","CL_B337_17:GA-12","CH_B337_APPX:SD7","CH_B337_EXPE:SNX5","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:RLE-01","text":"Representation enters a recursive validation loop: faculty-assignment enables schematization, schematization enables synthesis, synthesis yields objectivity, and objectivity reimposes phenomenal restriction.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:AS2","CH_B337_EXPE:A7","CH_B337_EMPI:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RLE-02","text":"Perceptual insufficiency generates necessary rules, those rules produce one nature, and empirical discoveries repeatedly return to the rules for their objective integration.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:D1","CH_B337_EXPE:SD3","CH_B337_EXPE:D15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RLE-03","text":"The imperceptibility of time drives an escalation from relational temporal determination to substance, causality, community, and the unity of nature.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:AS3","CH_B337_EXPE:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RLE-04","text":"Externality forms a reciprocal loop in which outer permanence makes inner temporal determination possible while apperceptive synthesis makes outer objects cognizable.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:D6","CH_B337_EMPI:SD4","CH_B337_EXPE:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RLE-05","text":"Modal escalation proceeds from formal experiential compatibility through perceptual connection to causal determination, without adding predicates to the object-concept.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:SD1","CH_B337_EMPI:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RLE-06","text":"Repeated negation functions productively: the rejection of logical sufficiency, direct temporal access, and autonomous categories generates positive procedures of reflection, synthesis, and validation.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:NX1","CH_B337_EXPE:SNX1","CH_B337_EMPI:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RLE-07","text":"Possible experience is the dominant attractor because faculty distinctions, temporal rules, categories, modalities, and scope restrictions all converge upon it.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A2","CH_B337_EMPI:SD7","CH_B337_EMPI:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:RLE-08","text":"The noumenon maintains a boundary loop: every attempted transgression produces loss of objective criteria and redirects cognition toward phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SNX4","CH_B337_APPX:SD7","CH_B337_EMPI:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:FGS-B01","text":"Unassigned representations + contingent perceptions + coherent concepts → insufficient object-reference.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:AS1","CH_B337_EXPE:A2","CH_B337_EMPI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FGS-B02","text":"Cognitive source-assignment → distinction between sensuous givenness and conceptual form → prevention of amphibolous comparison.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:AS2","CH_B337_APPX:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FGS-B03","text":"Sensuous givenness + categorial form + temporal schema → objectively employable synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A12","CH_B337_EXPE:A7","CH_B337_EMPI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FGS-B04","text":"Permanence + irreversible succession + reciprocal coexistence → substance + causality + community → one nature.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:AS4","CH_B337_EXPE:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FGS-B05","text":"Formal compatibility + lawful perceptual connection + causal determination → possibility + reality + necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FGS-B06","text":"Need for temporal permanence + absence of inner permanence → external matter → outer experience as a condition of inner experience.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:A10","CH_B337_EMPI:A11","CH_B337_EMPI:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FGS-B07","text":"Successful empirical synthesis → objective validity → scope filter → prohibition of noumenal extension.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:FGS10","CH_B337_EMPI:SD2","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FGS-B08","text":"Final output → robust empirical realism + unified temporal nature + indefinite inquiry + negative noumenal boundary.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:SD3","CH_B337_EXPE:SD4","CH_B337_APPX:SD7","CH_B337_APPX:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:AFM-B01","text":"Unreflected comparison → confusion of cognitive sources → transcendental localization.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:AF1","CH_B337_APPX:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AFM-B02","text":"Contingent apprehension → absence of objective temporal order → requirement of rule-governed synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:AF1","CH_B337_EXPE:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AFM-B03","text":"Imperceptible time → permanence, succession, and coexistence → substance, causality, and community → one nature.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:AS3","CH_B337_EXPE:AS4","CH_B337_EXPE:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AFM-B04","text":"Complete object-concept → experiential possibility test → perceptual reality test → causal necessity test.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:FGS1","CH_B337_EMPI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AFM-B05","text":"Temporal self-determination → demand for permanence → absence of inner permanence → dependence on external experience.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:FGS4","CH_B337_EMPI:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:AFM-B06","text":"Objective phenomenal unity → empirical realism → loss of criteria beyond experience → noumenal restriction.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:SD4","CH_B337_EMPI:SD3","CH_B337_EMPI:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is relocated from conceptual form or immediate perceptual presence to the lawful relation between representation and conditions of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:ME1","CH_B337_EXPE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MME-02","text":"The opposition between logical and real determination becomes a global distinction governing identity, opposition, succession, existence, and possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:ME2","CH_B337_APPX:ME3","CH_B337_EMPI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MME-03","text":"Time is reclassified from an observable medium into an unperceived form represented through lawful phenomenal relations.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:ME3","CH_B337_EXPE:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MME-04","text":"The subject is reclassified from a self-sufficient inner substance into an apperceptive unity dependent on external temporal conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:SD4","CH_B337_EMPI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MME-05","text":"Externality is redrawn from a doubtful inferred realm into a constitutive condition of individuation, coexistence, and self-knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:A9","CH_B337_EXPE:A16","CH_B337_EMPI:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MME-06","text":"Nature is reclassified from an aggregate of appearances into a single temporally and dynamically connected perceptual whole.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:ME8","CH_B337_EMPI:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MME-07","text":"Modality is reclassified from intrinsic object-determination into a sequence of cognitive procedures for experiential validation.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:ME1","CH_B337_EMPI:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MME-08","text":"Metaphysics is reorganized from supersensible object-construction into diagnosis of jurisdiction, conditions, and limits.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:ME8","CH_B337_EMPI:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, reflective concepts, the three analogies, and the three postulates supply the explicit objects through which objective validity is tested.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:ALT1","CH_B337_EXPE:ALT1","CH_B337_EMPI:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, two cognitive sources, temporal rules, formal intuition, perception, and causal synthesis jointly produce empirical objects.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:ALT2","CH_B337_EXPE:ALT2","CH_B337_EMPI:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, transcendental proof replaces dogmatic inference and empirical induction with jurisdictional analysis of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:ALT3","CH_B337_EXPE:ALT3","CH_B337_EMPI:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is one field of enduring substrates, causal transitions, reciprocal coexistences, and externally grounded self-determination.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:ALT4","CH_B337_EXPE:ALT4","CH_B337_EMPI:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system repeatedly stages an overextension, reconstructs its enabling condition, and forces the resulting principle back through a scope restriction.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:AS3","CH_B337_EXPE:FGS10","CH_B337_EMPI:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:FL-01","text":"The reciprocal validation of categories and possible experience risks circularity because each supplies the objective credentials of the other.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:MRT6","CH_B337_EMPI:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FL-02","text":"The analogies are called regulative regarding particular existence while functioning constitutively as conditions under which perceptions count as objects.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:MRT7","CH_B337_EXPE:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FL-03","text":"Conservation and continuity shift from conditions of intelligible synthesis toward claims about what phenomenal nature itself must contain.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:MRT4","CH_B337_EXPE:MRT8","CH_B337_EMPI:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FL-04","text":"The third analogy moves uncertainly from reciprocal influence as a condition for cognizing coexistence to universal interaction among all coexistent substances.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:MRT6","CH_B337_EXPE:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FL-05","text":"External experience is described as immediate even though its priority is established through a transcendental argument from inner temporal determination.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:FL-06","text":"The transcendental object is sometimes designated through causal language even though categorial determination of its non-sensuous ground is prohibited.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:MRT6","CH_B337_APPX:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MP-01","text":"Objectivity mutates from a problem of reflective comparison into a problem of temporal constitution and finally into modal certification.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SD1","CH_B337_EXPE:ME1","CH_B337_EMPI:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MP-02","text":"Permanence mutates from a general phenomenal substratum into external matter and thereby reverses the assumed autonomy of inner experience.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A9","CH_B337_EMPI:A11","CH_B337_EMPI:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MP-03","text":"Necessity mutates from the rule of irreversible event-order into conditioned material necessity while losing every claim to absolute substantial necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A12","CH_B337_EMPI:SD5","CH_B337_EMPI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MP-04","text":"Modality reverses valence from an apparent feature of objects into a classification of their relation to cognitive procedures.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:AS2","CH_B337_EMPI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MP-05","text":"The inner–outer hierarchy reverses when determinate inwardness becomes dependent on external permanence rather than external reality being inferred from autonomous inwardness.","refs":["CH_B337_EMPI:NX7","CH_B337_EMPI:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly modeled as a jurisdictional-temporal machine that converts given manifolds into objects through localization, synthesis, and lawful connection.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SD1","CH_B337_EXPE:ALT2","CH_B337_EMPI:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:HSI-02","text":"The subject is an apperceptive relay within this machine rather than an independently permanent substance.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A3","CH_B337_EMPI:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:HSI-03","text":"Time is an invisible formal medium that becomes empirically legible only through permanence, causal order, and reciprocal coexistence.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:A4","CH_B337_EXPE:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:HSI-04","text":"Objects are relational condensations whose identities, events, coexistences, and modal statuses depend on their place in one phenomenal network.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:DNX6","CH_B337_EXPE:SD2","CH_B337_EMPI:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:HSI-05","text":"Navigation remains possible through critical scope-checking and indefinitely extensible empirical inquiry, but no vertical escape to the in-itself is available.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:ALT3","CH_B337_APPX:SD7","CH_B337_APPX:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_17:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from faculty-confusion through temporal synthesis to modal validation and phenomenal restriction.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:MRT1","CH_B337_EXPE:MRT1","CH_B337_EMPI:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where it distinguishes logical from objective validity, subjective apprehension from necessary temporal order, and conceptual completeness from existence.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:MRT4","CH_B337_EXPE:MRT3","CH_B337_EMPI:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MRT-B03","text":"The exhaustiveness of the reflective titles, temporal modes, and modal sequence remains underdetermined because their architectonic correspondences are not independently derived here.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:MRT2","CH_B337_EXPE:MRT2","CH_B337_EMPI:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MRT-B04","text":"The system remains coherent only if sensibility and understanding remain irreducible, categories remain restricted to appearances, and modal predicates remain confined to possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:MRT3","CH_B337_EXPE:MRT9","CH_B337_EMPI:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when recursive return and scope restriction replace a merely sequential reproduction of the source units.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:MRT9","CH_B337_EXPE:MRT10","CH_B337_EMPI:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MRT-B06","text":"Conservation, continuity, universal dynamical community, and the exclusive grounding role of external matter remain resistant compression points rather than indispensable global axioms.","refs":["CH_B337_EXPE:MRT4","CH_B337_EXPE:MRT6","CH_B337_EMPI:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_17:MRT-B07","text":"No indispensable positive component remains outside axiomatic reconstruction, but the inaccessible ground coordinating sensibility, understanding, and appearance remains constitutively unreconstructible.","refs":["CH_B337_APPX:SNX2","CH_B337_EMPI:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 49–51","context":"Appendix · Experience · Empicisim"}},{"id":"CL_B337_18","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 52–54","order":111,"parents":["CH_B337_ITIA","CH_B337_IPRS","CH_B337_TDBI"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"ebc372a4fc95b1a562059f005fde23398df7c741de2865e44f4e4cb8e00c149f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:AS-B01","text":"Illusory appearance is relocated from objects or sensations to judgement, where subjective grounds can acquire the falsely objective status required for error.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AS-B02","text":"Cognition is stratified such that sensibility supplies intuitions, understanding constitutes empirical unity, and reason unifies understanding’s rules under principles.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A1","CH_B337_IPRS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AS-B03","text":"Reason’s syllogistic movement recursively seeks conditions for the conditioned and thereby generates the unconditioned as the projected completion of a series.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A7","CH_B337_IPRS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AS-B04","text":"The unconditioned is retained as the orienting limit and evaluative standard of cognition while excluded from empirical objecthood and empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A8","CH_B337_TDBI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AS-B05","text":"Transcendental illusion begins when the logical demand for complete unity is converted into an objective determination of things.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:AS5","CH_B337_ITIA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AS-B06","text":"Objective validity is divided between restricted applicability within possible experience and the legitimacy of the inferences through which rational conceptions are produced.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A4","CH_B337_TDBI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AS-B07","text":"Dialectic becomes the procedure that separates a logical maxim of inquiry from a constitutive claim about objects and distinguishes valid rational conclusions from sophistical appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A12","CH_B337_TDBI:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AS-B08","text":"The system closes not through possession of the unconditioned or abolition of illusion, but through recurrent critical regulation that withdraws illusion’s authority.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:AS7","CH_B337_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Human cognition is architecturally stratified: sensibility supplies, understanding constitutes objects of experience, and reason unifies rules under principles.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A1","CH_B337_IPRS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Object-determination has objective validity only through concepts whose application is restricted to possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A3","CH_B337_TDBI:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Every conditioned cognition provokes a recursive search for its condition, making the unconditioned the inevitable attractor of rational completion.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A7","CH_B337_IPRS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The unconditioned changes from the projected completion of a conditioned series into a boundary-standard for ordering and evaluating empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A9","CH_B337_TDBI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Transcendental illusion is generated when reason’s subjective demand for systematic completion is objectified as a determination of things themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:D6","CH_B337_ITIA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] The distinction between immanent and transcendent principles functions as a jurisdictional boundary between cognition of experience and unauthorized claims of totality.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A7","CH_B337_IPRS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Dialectic changes critique from the correction of isolated mistakes into an inferential audit that limits the authority of rational excess.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:FGS6","CH_B337_TDBI:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Rational conceptions divide into legitimately inferred conceptions and sophistical pseudo-conceptions, so transcendence is not identical with inferential invalidity.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A11","CH_B337_TDBI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Critical closure consists in preserving rational inquiry while preventing its recurrent appearances from acquiring constitutive authority.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:FGS4","CH_B337_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:RMD-01","text":"[Stable Identity] Judgement remains the sole site of truth, error, and illusory appearance, but becomes the mediation through which reason can indirectly acquire apparent objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A2","CH_B337_IPRS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:RMD-02","text":"[Functional Recurrence] Possible experience remains the stable criterion of object-valid cognition, but its function expands from delimiting categories to judging rational claims of totality.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A7","CH_B337_TDBI:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] A principle shifts from a synthetic cognition relating particulars to generals into a possible vehicle for asserting the completed series of conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A5","CH_B337_IPRS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:RMD-04","text":"[Displacement] The unconditioned migrates from the endpoint projected by recursive conditioning into a boundary-concept that regulates cognition without supplying an empirical object.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A8","CH_B337_TDBI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Reason’s transcendence is not simply an illegitimate escape from experience; it becomes a necessary orientation beyond experience whose objectifying use remains illegitimate.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:D3","CH_B337_TDBI:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:RMD-06","text":"[Absorption] The transition from categories to transcendental ideas absorbs the analysis of object-determination into a higher analysis of totality, completion, and inferential validity.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:NX9","CH_B337_TDBI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:RMD-07","text":"[Terminal Transformation] Illusion ceases to be a merely classifiable aberration and becomes the recurrent structural by-product of reason’s lawful drive toward systematic unity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:AS1","CH_B337_IPRS:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:CGD-01","text":"The specific account of sensibility’s covert influence on understanding remains a local diagnostic mechanism rather than the cluster’s final generative architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A5","CH_B337_ITIA:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:CGD-02","text":"The hierarchy of faculties and the recursive pursuit of the unconditioned scale upward because together they explain both the necessity and the danger of rational totalization.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A1","CH_B337_IPRS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:CGD-03","text":"Possible experience becomes the bridge-concept linking restricted object-determination to the validation or disqualification of rational inference.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A4","CH_B337_TDBI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between logical maxim and objective principle catalyzes the transition from a faculty theory into a dialectical critique of metaphysical inference.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A12","CH_B337_IPRS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:CGD-05","text":"Categories are recoded as concepts of possible experience, while transcendental ideas mark the new problem-space of reason’s demand for unconditioned totality.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:NX9","CH_B337_TDBI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:GNX-01","text":"Phenomenal intuition is neither true nor false in itself and cannot bear the burden of explaining transcendental error.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GNX-02","text":"Reason is not a faculty of direct intuition or immediate empirical object-determination.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GNX-03","text":"The necessity of seeking conditions does not license the assertion that a completed unconditioned series objectively exists.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GNX-04","text":"Systematic unity demanded by cognition cannot be assumed to mirror a completed unity intrinsic to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:GNX-05","text":"Critical disclosure of illusion’s ground cannot produce complete immunity from its recurrent appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:DBN-01","text":"The system excludes a self-sufficient rational ontology in which reason independently knows objects beyond the conditions of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:DBN-02","text":"The system excludes the identification of thought’s demand for systematic unity with an ontological totality already possessed by things.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:DBN-03","text":"The system excludes the equivalence of apparently correct inference, formal necessity, and objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:DBN-04","text":"The system excludes the conflation of regulative orientation with constitutive production of knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:BSA-01","text":"No available cognitive standpoint can encounter the totality of conditions as a completed object.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:BSA-02","text":"The unconditioned cannot be admitted as both genuinely unconditioned and fully available within experience.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:BSA-03","text":"The deepest exclusion is the coincidence of reason’s demand for completion with experience’s capacity to provide a final corresponding object.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:XD-01","text":"Reason’s claims remain dependent on cognitive material supplied through understanding even when reason seeks a ground exceeding every determinate empirical cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-01","CL_B337_18:GA-02","CL_B337_18:GA-03","CH_B337_IPRS:A4","CH_B337_TDBI:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:XD-02","text":"The judgement-level misclassification of subjective grounds scales into metaphysical illusion when reason’s recursive demand for the unconditioned is reclassified as an objective property of things.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-03","CL_B337_18:GA-05","CH_B337_ITIA:A5","CH_B337_IPRS:A8","CH_B337_ITIA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:XD-03","text":"Objective validity becomes a differentiated test: concepts of understanding require experiential restriction, while rational conceptions require dialectical examination of their inferential production.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-02","CL_B337_18:GA-07","CL_B337_18:GA-08","CH_B337_TDBI:A4","CH_B337_TDBI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:XD-04","text":"The unconditioned is operationally necessary as a guide to systematic inquiry but ontologically unlicensed as an empirical or constitutive object.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-03","CL_B337_18:GA-04","CL_B337_18:GA-06","CH_B337_TDBI:A9","CH_B337_TDBI:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:XD-05","text":"Critique must distinguish correctable logical error from natural transcendental illusion, because only the latter persists after its ground has been exposed.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-05","CL_B337_18:GA-07","CL_B337_18:GA-09","CH_B337_ITIA:A10","CH_B337_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:XD-06","text":"The distinction between legitimate and sophistical rational conceptions prevents the blanket rejection of reason while withholding immediate validation of its transcendent objects.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-07","CL_B337_18:GA-08","CH_B337_TDBI:A11","CH_B337_TDBI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:BSD-01","text":"The cluster produces a two-register epistemology in which understanding constitutes determinate experience while reason regulates the horizon under which experience is comprehended.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-01","CL_B337_18:GA-02","CL_B337_18:GA-04","CH_B337_TDBI:SD1","CH_B337_TDBI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:BSD-02","text":"Metaphysical claims are recoded as claims requiring an audit of inferential genesis rather than as self-certifying reports about supersensible objects.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-07","CL_B337_18:GA-08","CH_B337_TDBI:ME4","CH_B337_TDBI:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:BSD-03","text":"Reason is structurally self-dividing because the same operation that enables systematic cognition also produces the impulse to totalize cognition into transcendent object-knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-03","CL_B337_18:GA-05","CH_B337_IPRS:SD1","CH_B337_IPRS:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:BSD-04","text":"Objectivity is no longer a unitary predicate; it is distributed across experiential applicability, inferential legitimacy, and the strictly regulative use of ideas.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-02","CL_B337_18:GA-04","CL_B337_18:GA-08","CH_B337_TDBI:ME2","CH_B337_TDBI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:BSD-05","text":"Cognition is temporally nonclosing because every achieved condition can renew the search for a higher condition while the totality that would terminate the series remains unavailable.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-03","CL_B337_18:GA-04","CL_B337_18:GA-09","CH_B337_IPRS:ALT4","CH_B337_TDBI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:RLE-01","text":"A conditioned judgement is referred to its condition, the condition becomes conditioned in turn, and the resulting recursion drives reason toward the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:FGS2","CH_B337_IPRS:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:RLE-02","text":"Recursive conditioning produces the maxim of seeking completion, making the unconditioned a formal attractor before it becomes any asserted object.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:FGS4","CH_B337_IPRS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:RLE-03","text":"The escalation from logical demand to objective determination is the decisive phase transition through which rational unity becomes transcendental illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:FGS5","CH_B337_ITIA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:RLE-04","text":"Critique exposes the ground of illusion, blocks its authority, and returns to a renewed vigilance because natural illusion necessarily reappears.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:FGS4","CH_B337_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:RLE-05","text":"Rational inference repeatedly divides into legitimate conclusion and sophistical appearance, making dialectic a recurrent sorting operation rather than a completed doctrine.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:AS5","CH_B337_TDBI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:RLE-06","text":"The unconditioned organizes the system as an absent centre: it attracts inquiry, evaluates partial cognition, and cannot be stabilized as a final object.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:SD2","CH_B337_TDBI:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:FGS-B01","text":"A priori synthesis constitutes phenomena as determinate experience, while reason receives the resulting rules as material for higher-order unification.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:FGS1","CH_B337_IPRS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:FGS-B02","text":"A conditioned judgement is referred to a condition, and each condition renews the demand for a further condition.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:FGS2","CH_B337_IPRS:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:FGS-B03","text":"The recursive sequence generates the unconditioned as a projected completion and uses it as an orienting standard for cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:FGS4","CH_B337_TDBI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:FGS-B04","text":"Objectification of this subjective rational demand converts regulative orientation into a transcendent claim about things.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:FGS5","CH_B337_ITIA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:FGS-B05","text":"Dialectic examines the inference producing the claim and separates legitimate rational orientation from sophistical pseudo-objectivity.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:FGS6","CH_B337_TDBI:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:FGS-B06","text":"Critical exposure suspends transcendent authority without terminating the demand for completion, so the system regenerates its own need for critique.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:FGS4","CH_B337_ITIA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:AFM-B01","text":"The relocation of error into judgement → the differentiation of faculties → the rejection of object-based accounts of transcendental error.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:AS2","CH_B337_ITIA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AFM-B02","text":"The hierarchy of sensibility, understanding, and reason → the constitution of possible experience → the emergence of reason as unity of rules.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A1","CH_B337_TDBI:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AFM-B03","text":"The unification of rules under principles → syllogistic referral to conditions → recursive escalation toward the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A3","CH_B337_IPRS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AFM-B04","text":"Recursive conditioning → the unconditioned as rational completion → the unconditioned as a non-empirical standard of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:A8","CH_B337_TDBI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AFM-B05","text":"The projection of subjective necessity onto things → transcendent objectification → transcendental illusory appearance.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:A11","CH_B337_IPRS:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:AFM-B06","text":"Transcendental appearance → dialectical examination of inference → recurrent regulation rather than final cognitive cure.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:FGS6","CH_B337_ITIA:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:MME-01","text":"The field of error is displaced from the opposition between deceptive and reliable senses into the architecture of judgement, faculty-interaction, and rational objectification.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:ME1","CH_B337_ITIA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:MME-02","text":"A priori cognition is reorganized into a field divided between constitutive concepts of understanding and transcendental ideas of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:ME1","CH_B337_TDBI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:MME-03","text":"The unconditioned is reclassified from a presumptive metaphysical object into an absent horizon that directs the assessment of empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:ME3","CH_B337_TDBI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:MME-04","text":"Objective validity becomes a problem of jurisdiction and inferential legitimacy rather than a generic possession of all a priori conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:ME2","CH_B337_TDBI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:MME-05","text":"Critique becomes a permanent practice of delimitation and counteraction rather than an instrument for eliminating the source of illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:ME3","CH_B337_ITIA:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The system distinguishes conceptions of understanding from conceptions of pure reason and identifies the latter with transcendental ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:AS1","CH_B337_TDBI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Understanding constitutes possible experience while reason unifies the rules of that experience through principles and the search for conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:ALT2","CH_B337_IPRS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — Critique intervenes by determining whether reason’s intrinsic demand regulates inquiry or illegitimately legislates for objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:ALT3","CH_B337_TDBI:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological and temporal level — Reality is divided between conditioned empirical objects and an unconditioned sought through an indefinitely renewable sequence of conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:ALT4","CH_B337_IPRS:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological and performative level — The system performs its thesis by repeatedly converting apparent object-claims into questions of critical delimitation and inferential authority.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:ME3","CH_B337_ITIA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:FL-01","text":"Reason is necessarily driven to seek the unconditioned while being denied any direct intuition or empirical cognition capable of securing it.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:MRT5","CH_B337_IPRS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:FL-02","text":"Reason seeks cognition beyond experience even though the material of its inferential activity originates in understanding’s experience-bound determinations.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:MRT6","CH_B337_TDBI:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:FL-03","text":"The possibility of legitimate rational conceptions remains tense with the restriction that transcendent principles lack adequate empirical use.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:MRT6","CH_B337_TDBI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:MP-01","text":"The unconditioned mutates from the limiting endpoint of rational inquiry into a boundary-concept that guides cognition without being cognized as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:SD4","CH_B337_TDBI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:MP-02","text":"Objective validity mutates from restricted applicability within possible experience into a differentiated question of inferential legitimacy for rational conceptions.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:A4","CH_B337_TDBI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:MP-03","text":"Critique mutates from an eliminative logic of error into a permanent dialectical discipline of separating regulative demand from constitutive claim.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:SD1","CH_B337_IPRS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a two-level machine in which empirical objects are constituted through synthesis while rational totality remains an absent horizon of comprehension.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:SD1","CH_B337_TDBI:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a divided cognitive arrangement in which reason internally generates the very excess that critique must regulate.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:SD3","CH_B337_IPRS:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:HSI-03","text":"Time is structurally open because every condition can become conditioned again, while the complete series that would terminate the movement is unavailable.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:FGS3","CH_B337_TDBI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:HSI-04","text":"Struggle takes the form of jurisdictional navigation: preserving the regulative orientation of ideas while refusing their conversion into constitutive knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_ITIA:ME3","CH_B337_IPRS:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_18:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from empirical constitution through recursive conditioning to dialectical limitation of rational totalization.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:MRT1","CH_B337_IPRS:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where it derives transcendental illusion from reason’s lawful recursive demand rather than from accidental irrationality.","refs":["CH_B337_IPRS:MRT4","CH_B337_ITIA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:MRT-B03","text":"The objective status of the unconditioned and of rational conceptions remains intentionally underdetermined because dialectic has posed, but not yet completed, their validation.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:MRT4","CH_B337_TDBI:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_18:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction remains coherent only if critique is treated as the recurrent limitation of authority rather than the final cure of natural illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_TDBI:MRT5","CH_B337_ITIA:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 52–54","context":"I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason."}},{"id":"CL_B337_19","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 55–57","order":112,"parents":["CH_B337_SIIG","CH_B337_SITI","CH_B337_SIST"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"313b922be274294ec9866ef98689695092727aa12c7a88c051a4b144b47f880e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:AS-B01","text":"The cluster resolves how reason can necessarily exceed experience without converting its necessary products into objects of knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A7","CH_B337_SIST:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AS-B02","text":"Ideas are reframed from proposed supersensible entities into architectonically generated demands produced when conditioned cognition seeks the totality of its grounds.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:AS2","CH_B337_SIST:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AS-B03","text":"The proof joins semantic differentiation, logical derivation, and functional delimitation: representations must be named distinctly, reason must generate its ideas formally, and their valid use must be separated from object-knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A1","CH_B337_SITI:A2","CH_B337_SITI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AS-B04","text":"The movement is recursive rather than linear because every conditioned cognition renews the ascent toward the unconditioned while the unconditioned remains unavailable as an empirical completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A2","CH_B337_SITI:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AS-B05","text":"Reason’s excess splits into speculative regulation, which orders understanding systematically, and practical orientation, which guides action and institutions through freedom.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:AS6","CH_B337_SIIG:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AS-B06","text":"Closure occurs as a bounded architectonic of subject, phenomenal world, and highest condition rather than as completion of experience, history, or metaphysical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A6","CH_B337_SIST:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AS-B07","text":"Legitimate totalization is confined to the regress toward grounds, whereas consequences remain open and cannot be assembled into an equally necessary completed whole.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A12","CH_B337_SIST:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AS-B08","text":"Dialectical critique therefore preserves rational normativity and systematicity by converting metaphysical assertion into an account of reason’s necessary but bounded production of ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:SD2","CH_B337_SIIG:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Reason necessarily generates ideas whenever conditioned cognition demands the complete conditions under which it is determined.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A2","CH_B337_SITI:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] The absence of an adequate empirical object limits ideas as knowledge but does not cancel their rational necessity or legitimate function.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A3","CH_B337_SITI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Transcendence changes from a practical archetype of action into a formal demand for unconditioned totality and then into a triadic architectonic, without becoming an independently existing thing.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:AS3","CH_B337_SITI:AS3","CH_B337_SIST:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] The unconditioned functions as the horizon that organizes reason’s demand for systematic unity while remaining unavailable as an empirical object.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:AS3","CH_B337_SITI:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The legitimacy of ideas depends on their use as regulative principles or practical standards, not on empirical instantiation or speculative existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:SD3","CH_B337_SITI:A10","CH_B337_SIIG:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Formal differentiation becomes architectonic generation when the classification of representations and syllogistic forms yields a determinate system of rational ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:D7","CH_B337_SITI:A2","CH_B337_SIST:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Understanding determines conditioned objects within possible experience, whereas reason demands unconditional unity of the conditions under which such determination occurs.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A8","CH_B337_SIST:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] The asymmetry between grounds and consequences makes regressive totality rationally required while preserving the indefinite openness of consequences.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A12","CH_B337_SIST:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Subjective deduction replaces objective deduction as the valid mode for explaining why metaphysical ideas are necessary without treating them as knowledge of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A11","CH_B337_SIST:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Practical realization preserves the efficacy of ideas under conditions in which speculative reason cannot exhibit or cognize their objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A13","CH_B337_SIIG:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:RMD-01","text":"“Idea” retains its identity as a conception of reason beyond possible experience but shifts from a general classificatory term into the formal output of reason’s demand for totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A2","CH_B337_SITI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:RMD-02","text":"Terminological discipline begins as local protection against conceptual confusion and is absorbed into the formal derivation that prevents rational ideas from being confused with empirical concepts or objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A1","CH_B337_SITI:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:RMD-03","text":"The archetype mutates from a moral standard for judging conduct into the systematic horizon by which understanding is directed toward greater unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A7","CH_B337_SITI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:RMD-04","text":"The qualified recovery of Plato performs local boundary work and then disappears, leaving behind the permanent prohibition on speculative hypostatization.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:AS6","CH_B337_SIIG:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:RMD-05","text":"The unconditioned expands from an implicit mark of transcendence into the explicit horizon of systematic unity and the principle of the threefold metaphysical system.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:AS3","CH_B337_SITI:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:RMD-06","text":"Objectivity reverses function: ideas can be objectively necessary as standards while lacking the objective deduction that would establish them as objects of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A4","CH_B337_SIST:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:RMD-07","text":"The logical triad of categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive inference is displaced into the architectonic sequence of self, world, and highest being.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A9","CH_B337_SIST:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:RMD-08","text":"Freedom remains the practical carrier of rational efficacy but becomes a residual bridge because its connection to the speculative derivation of ideas is asserted rather than integrated.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A6","CH_B337_SITI:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:CGD-01","text":"The taxonomy of representations remains local as a classificatory apparatus, but it scales upward by supplying the distinction required for the system’s subsequent derivation and delimitation of ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A10","CH_B337_SIIG:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:CGD-02","text":"The treatment of Plato remains a local historical and polemical mechanism whose current-level function is exhausted once speculative reification has been excluded.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:AS6","CH_B337_SIIG:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:CGD-03","text":"Moral and political examples scale upward only insofar as they establish that non-exhibition does not entail practical inactivity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A6","CH_B337_SITI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:CGD-04","text":"Syllogistic form bridges the local account of ideas and the current-level architectonic by converting rational transcendence from a generic claim into a determinate system.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A2","CH_B337_SIST:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:CGD-05","text":"Subjective deduction is a current-level transition mechanism because it explains the necessary generation of ideas while withholding the validity required for object-knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A11","CH_B337_SIST:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:CGD-06","text":"Rational psychology, cosmology, and theology cease to be candidate sciences of supersensible objects and become terminally recoded as internally necessary problem-domains of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A7","CH_B337_SIST:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:GNX-01","text":"Ideas are excluded from the entire register of sensation, intuition, and understanding-concepts that derive their validity from immediate or possible empirical objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:NX1","CH_B337_SIIG:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GNX-02","text":"Ideas are neither empirical generalizations nor arbitrary fictions, because their necessity arises from reason rather than from observed recurrence or invention.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:NX4","CH_B337_SITI:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GNX-03","text":"Ideas cannot be hypostatized into independently existing speculative entities or constitutive concepts that determine additional objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:NX8","CH_B337_SITI:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GNX-04","text":"A completed totality of consequences is excluded because only the regress toward conditions carries the necessity of rational completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:NX6","CH_B337_SIST:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:GNX-05","text":"Formal derivation and logical validity are insufficient to establish transcendent objects, so logical structure must not be mistaken for metaphysical proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:DNX3","CH_B337_SIST:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:DBN-01","text":"The system cannot admit an empirically available unconditioned, because the unconditioned must orient rational ascent precisely without entering the field of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:SNX1","CH_B337_SIST:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:DBN-02","text":"The system disqualifies both empiricist reduction of ideas to facts and dogmatic conversion of rational necessity into knowledge of supersensible entities.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:SNX4","CH_B337_SIST:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:DBN-03","text":"Rational validity cannot be identified with sensible givenness, yet its absence of sensible givenness cannot be taken as a license for objective metaphysical assertion.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:DNX1","CH_B337_SIST:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:DBN-04","text":"Normative authority cannot be derived from prevailing practice, and systematic unity cannot be identified with an empirically completed system.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:DNX2","CH_B337_SITI:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:BSA-01","text":"No final empirical or historical state can close rational demand, because realization remains answerable to standards and horizons that it cannot finally instantiate.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:SNX1","CH_B337_SITI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable object of the unconditioned gives the system its force: reason must require it while being forbidden to possess it as knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:SNX1","CH_B337_SIST:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:BSA-03","text":"Reality as already constituted cannot determine what reason must demand, nor can a completed future sequence be admitted as the proper object of transcendental totalization.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:SNX2","CH_B337_SIST:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:XD-01","text":"A rational idea is legitimate only when its name identifies a formally generated function of reason rather than a purportedly encountered metaphysical referent.","refs":["CL_B337_19:GA-01","CL_B337_19:GA-06","CL_B337_19:GA-09","CH_B337_SIIG:A1","CH_B337_SITI:A2","CH_B337_SIST:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:XD-02","text":"The lack of an adequate object differentiates valid uses instead of producing uniform invalidity: speculative ideas regulate cognition while practical ideas guide realization.","refs":["CL_B337_19:GA-02","CL_B337_19:GA-05","CL_B337_19:GA-10","CH_B337_SIIG:A4","CH_B337_SITI:A13","CH_B337_SIIG:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:XD-03","text":"Rational progress can be normatively directed without being empirically predictable or historically complete, because ideals govern approximation while consequences remain non-totalizable.","refs":["CL_B337_19:GA-08","CL_B337_19:GA-10","CH_B337_SIIG:A7","CH_B337_SITI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:XD-04","text":"Metaphysical disciplines become maps of reason’s own necessary production once their triadic derivation is retained and their claim to objective deduction is withdrawn.","refs":["CL_B337_19:GA-06","CL_B337_19:GA-09","CH_B337_SIST:A9","CH_B337_SIST:A10","CH_B337_SIST:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:XD-05","text":"Critique must explain why transcendent ideas are unavoidable rather than merely reject them as errors, because their necessity and their cognitive invalidity coexist.","refs":["CL_B337_19:GA-01","CL_B337_19:GA-02","CL_B337_19:GA-09","CH_B337_SITI:NX2","CH_B337_SIST:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:BSD-01","text":"Reason’s excess becomes a two-channel ordering mechanism: it systematizes cognition regulatively and directs freedom practically without acquiring speculative objects.","refs":["CL_B337_19:GA-05","CL_B337_19:GA-10","CH_B337_SITI:A10","CH_B337_SIIG:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:BSD-02","text":"Metaphysics is transformed from an inventory of supersensible beings into a diagnostic account of the rational operations that necessarily produce ideas of such beings.","refs":["CL_B337_19:GA-01","CL_B337_19:GA-09","CH_B337_SIST:AS1","CH_B337_SIST:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:BSD-03","text":"The system distributes legitimacy across distinct registers—subjective necessity, regulative orientation, and practical efficacy—while withholding the register of constitutive knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_19:GA-02","CL_B337_19:GA-05","CL_B337_19:GA-09","CL_B337_19:GA-10","CH_B337_SIST:D3","CH_B337_SITI:A10","CH_B337_SIIG:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:BSD-04","text":"The asymmetry of grounds and consequences produces a non-predictive model of rationality in which sufficient grounding is demanded but exhaustive future completion is not.","refs":["CL_B337_19:GA-08","CH_B337_SITI:SD3","CH_B337_SIST:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:BSD-05","text":"The ordered sequence of self, world, and highest condition functions as an architectonic closure of inquiry while remaining evidentially insufficient to establish any supersensible object.","refs":["CL_B337_19:GA-06","CL_B337_19:GA-09","CH_B337_SIST:SD3","CH_B337_SIST:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned cognition generates a demand for total grounds; the absence of an object blocks constitutive closure; the idea returns as a regulative rule for further cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A4","CH_B337_SITI:FGS5","CH_B337_SITI:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:RLE-02","text":"Practical deficiency is judged against an archetype, and the unfulfilled archetype renews the demand for progressive approximation rather than cancelling the standard.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:D2","CH_B337_SIIG:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:RLE-03","text":"Syllogistic form generates a triadic system of ideas; the system generates dialectical disciplines; critique then returns to delimit the very rational operations that generated them.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A2","CH_B337_SIST:A7","CH_B337_SIST:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:RLE-04","text":"Semantic clarification becomes a recursive safeguard because each newly generated distinction requires the preservation of a term adequate to its specific function.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:AS1","CH_B337_SIIG:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:RLE-05","text":"The unconditioned is the dominant attractor: it draws reason upward through conditions while the excluded totality of consequences prevents that ascent from becoming a completed cosmological inventory.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A2","CH_B337_SIST:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:FGS-B01","text":"Differentiated representation → determinate designation of ideas as rational conceptions beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A1","CH_B337_SIIG:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:FGS-B02","text":"Conditioned cognition → demand for a complete regress of grounds → conception of the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A4","CH_B337_SITI:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:FGS-B03","text":"Unconditioned conception without adequate sensible object → prohibition of constitutive speculative knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:A7","CH_B337_SITI:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:FGS-B04","text":"Syllogistic relations and representation-relations → system of subject-unity, world-totality, and highest condition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A5","CH_B337_SIST:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:FGS-B05","text":"Prohibition of object-knowledge → subjective deduction and regulative use in speculation, together with practical realization under freedom.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A11","CH_B337_SITI:A10","CH_B337_SIIG:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:FGS-B06","text":"Total regress of grounds plus open consequences → bounded systematic orientation without empirical, historical, or metaphysical completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A12","CH_B337_SITI:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:AFM-B01","text":"Semantic differentiation → exclusion of ideas from empirical representation and ordinary understanding-concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:AF2","CH_B337_SIIG:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AFM-B02","text":"Non-empirical rational conception → conditioned cognition’s demand for totality of conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A2","CH_B337_SITI:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AFM-B03","text":"Demand for totality → conception of the unconditioned → triadic derivation of rational ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:FGS3","CH_B337_SITI:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AFM-B04","text":"Triadic derivation → rational psychology, cosmology, and theology as necessary products of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A6","CH_B337_SIST:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AFM-B05","text":"Lack of empirical objects → denial of objective deduction → regulative and subjective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:FGS5","CH_B337_SIST:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:AFM-B06","text":"Asymmetrical totalization and practical realization → bounded but continuously operative rational orientation.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:FGS7","CH_B337_SITI:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:MME-01","text":"Validity is reorganized from correspondence with an available object into a differentiated field of subjective necessity, regulative direction, and practical efficacy.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A4","CH_B337_SIST:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:MME-02","text":"The opposition between ideality and efficacy collapses because an idea can orient action and institutions without becoming an empirical object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:ME2","CH_B337_SIIG:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:MME-03","text":"Metaphysical disciplines are reclassified as necessary outputs of reason’s internal architecture rather than as sciences founded upon independently known supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:SD1","CH_B337_SIST:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:MME-04","text":"Totality is divided into legitimate totality of grounds and illegitimate totality of effects, futures, or consequences.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:ME3","CH_B337_SIST:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:MME-05","text":"Rational error is reclassified from accidental excess into a necessary and structurally intelligible effect of reason’s own demand for unconditional unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:AS1","CH_B337_SIST:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system defines transcendental ideas and classifies their principal forms as soul, world, and highest being.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:ALT1","CH_B337_SIST:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, syllogistic mediation and the relations of representation generate a demand for unconditional unity that exceeds understanding’s empirical jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:ALT2","CH_B337_SIST:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, critique preserves the necessity of reason’s ideas while preventing them from functioning as constitutive claims to supersensible knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:ALT3","CH_B337_SITI:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality appears as conditioned synthesis requiring regressive grounding, while future consequences and practical realization remain open.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:ALT4","CH_B337_SIST:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system enacts its own thesis by generating, classifying, restricting, and redirecting the ideas whose production it explains.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:AF1","CH_B337_SIST:AF6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:FL-01","text":"The system requires the unconditioned as the terminus of rational grounding while refusing it as an object of cognition; this is its constitutive limitation rather than a removable defect.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:MRT3","CH_B337_SIST:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:FL-02","text":"The architectonic exhaustiveness of the system depends on a correspondence between syllogistic forms and transcendental ideas that is asserted as classificatory ground but not demonstrated.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:NX8","CH_B337_SIST:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:FL-03","text":"Practical efficacy is retained as a distinct use of ideas, but the deduction connecting speculative transcendence to practical freedom remains deferred.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:MRT6","CH_B337_SIIG:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:FL-04","text":"Regressive totality is demanded even though no first condition or completed series can be empirically apprehended, leaving the status of completion structurally compressed.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:MRT5","CH_B337_SITI:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:MP-01","text":"“Idea” mutates from a practical archetype to a transcendental horizon, preserving non-empirical authority while changing its operational domain.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:AS3","CH_B337_SITI:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:MP-02","text":"Objective necessity mutates into subjective deduction when rational ideas are retained as necessary products of reason but denied objective validity as cognitions.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:A4","CH_B337_SIST:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:MP-03","text":"Totality mutates from a presumed possible completion into a regulative horizon once the distinction between total grounds and open consequences is enforced.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:ME2","CH_B337_SITI:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:HSI-01","text":"Reality is figured as a stratified field of conditioned syntheses whose intelligibility continually drives reason toward unavailable unconditioned grounds.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A2","CH_B337_SIST:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not a self-subsistent metaphysical object but a site within which reason necessarily produces the demand for absolute unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIST:A6","CH_B337_SIST:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:HSI-03","text":"Time is open because consequences remain indefinitely extensible, while practical history is normatively oriented by ideals that no final state can exhaust.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:SD3","CH_B337_SIIG:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:HSI-04","text":"Rational struggle consists in navigating between empirical reduction and speculative reification while retaining ideas as guides for cognition and action.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:SNX4","CH_B337_SITI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_19:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s central machine: reason generates ideas from conditioned cognition, ideas lack objective deduction, and their valid functions remain regulative or practical.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:MRT1","CH_B337_SIST:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:MRT-B02","text":"The system remains underdetermined where it depends on the completeness of the syllogistic-transcendental correspondence and on the transition from speculative deduction to practical efficacy.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:MRT2","CH_B337_SITI:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent at this scale only if subjective necessity, non-objective status, regulative use, and practical realization are held apart as distinct forms of validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SITI:MRT4","CH_B337_SIST:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_19:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only when lexical differentiation, formal generation, apophatic restriction, and functional redirection are treated as one recursive machine rather than as separate doctrines.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIG:MRT7","CH_B337_SITI:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 55–57","context":"Section I—Of Ideas in General · Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas · Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas"}},{"id":"CL_B337_20","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 58–60","order":113,"parents":["CH_B337_TD_2","CH_B337_CIPP","CH_B337_CIAP"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"945dd59d7c3cb99e72b447d0a861b6fef590e6d2baf9dfe652c4f5a557f0cca4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:AS-B01","text":"The system confronts reason’s necessary demand for unconditioned unity with the absence of the intuition required to convert that demand into cognition of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SD2","CH_B337_CIPP:AS5","CH_B337_CIAP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:AS-B02","text":"Dialectical error is generated internally by reason’s lawful operation, not externally by accident, empirical confusion, or merely individual incompetence.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A9","CH_B337_CIPP:AS2","CH_B337_CIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:AS-B03","text":"Possible experience and object-giving intuition form the validity threshold separating the subjective necessity of rational ideas from legitimate objective determination.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A1","CH_B337_CIPP:A4","CH_B337_CIAP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:AS-B04","text":"The movement expands from the unity of the thinking subject through the totality of phenomenal conditions toward the conditions of objects in general, while objective availability decreases as scope increases.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:AS6","CH_B337_CIAP:AS2","CH_B337_CIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:AS-B05","text":"The subjective branch reifies logical apperception as a soul-object, whereas the objective branch totalizes phenomenal conditions until reason produces opposed cosmological claims.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:D5","CH_B337_CIAP:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:AS-B06","text":"Dialectical illusion changes morphology as it scales: the paralogism is asymmetrical and equivocal, while the antinomy gives both opposed positions apparent rational support.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A5","CH_B337_CIAP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:AS-B07","text":"Critique cannot abolish illusion; it instead separates necessary rational procedure from illegitimate objectification and recodes metaphysical doctrine as a discipline of limits.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SD5","CH_B337_CIPP:AS7","CH_B337_CIAP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:AS-B08","text":"Closure remains critical rather than constitutive: the soul is left speculatively indeterminate, rational cosmology is suspended, practical redirection remains local, and the third dialectical domain remains only anticipated.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:SD7","CH_B337_CIAP:SD6","CH_B337_CIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Reason necessarily generates transcendental demands for unconditioned unity even though their corresponding objects cannot thereby be known.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A3","CH_B337_CIPP:A16","CH_B337_CIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires intuition within possible experience; logical necessity, conceptual purity, and rational plausibility cannot replace this condition.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A1","CH_B337_CIPP:A4","CH_B337_CIAP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] A formal condition for thinking becomes dialectical when it is transferred into a synthetic determination of an object or totality.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SD4","CH_B337_CIPP:A14","CH_B337_CIAP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] Transcendental illusion is structurally recurrent because reason cannot cease demanding the unconditioned, although critique can prevent assent to its objective seeming.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A10","CH_B337_CIPP:SD3","CH_B337_CIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The formal architecture of syllogism distributes dialectic across the subject, the series of phenomenal conditions, and the conditions of objects in general.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A11","CH_B337_CIAP:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] In the subjective branch, logical subjecthood, unity, identity, and distinction are projected as substantiality, simplicity, personality, and separability.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:AS4","CH_B337_CIPP:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] In the objective branch, reason’s demand for the complete series of phenomenal conditions generates an internally supported contradiction rather than a single unsupported conclusion.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:AS3","CH_B337_CIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] The passage from paralogism to antinomy transforms dialectic from one-sided reification into symmetrical conflict without changing its origin in unconditioned totalization.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:AS4","CH_B337_CIAP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Rational psychology and rational cosmology lose the status of positive sciences and become objects of critical limitation and diagnosis.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:AS7","CH_B337_CIAP:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Opposed metaphysical positions become symmetrical transgressions whenever neither side possesses the intuition required for objective determination.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:NX11","CH_B337_CIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Dialectical totalization escalates from self to world and toward all possible objects while converging on an increasingly unavailable unconditioned object.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SD3","CH_B337_CIAP:AS2","CH_B337_CIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] The critical endpoint is disciplined non-cognition: necessary rational operations are retained, their dogmatic conclusions are suspended, and practical orientation is not converted into theoretical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SD5","CH_B337_CIPP:SD4","CH_B337_CIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:RMD-01","text":"Unconditioned unity retains its identity as reason’s governing demand but changes its correlate from the thinking subject to phenomenal series and finally to objects in general.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:D8","CH_B337_CIAP:A2","CH_B337_CIAP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RMD-02","text":"Syllogistic form recurs functionally: it begins as the origin of dialectical arguments and expands into a generative architecture distributing distinct kinds of illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A8","CH_B337_CIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RMD-03","text":"Unity mutates from the logical unity of apperception into the synthetic unity of phenomenal conditions and then into the anticipated totality of conditions for possible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:SD5","CH_B337_CIAP:A6","CH_B337_CIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RMD-04","text":"The requirement of object-giving intuition remains stable across every domain and supplies the common test by which subjective necessity is denied objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A1","CH_B337_CIPP:A3","CH_B337_CIAP:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RMD-05","text":"Illusion changes function from the unsupported ontologization of a logical subject to a conflict in which opposed cosmological positions acquire comparable rational force.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A5","CH_B337_CIAP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RMD-06","text":"The absent adequate object is displaced from the transcendental subject designated only problematically to the empirically unavailable totality of the world-series.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:SNX1","CH_B337_CIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RMD-07","text":"Contradiction reverses valence: instead of serving as a merely local failure, it becomes evidence that reason has exceeded the conditions governing phenomenal synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A13","CH_B337_CIAP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RMD-08","text":"Positive metaphysical doctrines disappear after furnishing the material of critique and are absorbed into rational psychology as discipline and rational cosmology as critical problem.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:ME6","CH_B337_CIAP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:CGD-01","text":"The detailed predicates of substantiality, simplicity, personality, separability, and permanence remain local specifications of the subjective dialectical branch.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:CGD-02","text":"Intensive diminution is a local counterargument against deriving permanence from simplicity and does not become a general mechanism of transcendental dialectic.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A17","CH_B337_CIPP:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:CGD-03","text":"The practical retention of future existence remains a terminal redirection within rational psychology rather than the general resolution of dialectical illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A18","CH_B337_CIPP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between cosmical totality and the conditions of all possible things scales upward because it prevents the second dialectical domain from absorbing the third.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:D7","CH_B337_CIAP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:CGD-05","text":"The intuition and possible-experience criterion scales from local objections to the common jurisdictional rule governing every objective claim of pure reason.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A1","CH_B337_CIPP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:CGD-06","text":"The classification of syllogistic forms bridges the units by converting separate metaphysical errors into coordinated outputs of one rational architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:AS5","CH_B337_CIAP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:CGD-07","text":"The shift from subjective unity to objective phenomenal conditions catalyzes the transition from equivocal predication to contradiction between opposed totalizations.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:AS2","CH_B337_CIAP:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:CGD-08","text":"The ideal of pure reason is structurally announced but not developed sufficiently to be absorbed into the cluster’s demonstrated machinery.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:D7","CH_B337_CIAP:A4","CH_B337_CIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:GNX-01","text":"Dialectic cannot be explained as accidental sophistry, empirical carelessness, or an idiosyncratic mistake by individual reasoners.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:NX3","CH_B337_CIAP:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GNX-02","text":"Necessary generation does not establish either the knowability or the objective reality of the object demanded by reason.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:DNX1","CH_B337_TD_2:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GNX-03","text":"Logical unity, grammatical subjecthood, and analytic necessity cannot supply synthetic metaphysical determinations of the self.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:DNX1","CH_B337_CIPP:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GNX-04","text":"The failure of spiritualism does not validate materialism, just as the failure of one cosmological position does not validate its contrary by default.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:DNX8","CH_B337_CIAP:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GNX-05","text":"Neither sceptical withdrawal nor dogmatic persistence can resolve a contradiction generated by reason’s own totalizing procedure.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:NX5","CH_B337_CIAP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:GNX-06","text":"Critique does not abolish transcendental illusion but excludes its conversion into authorized metaphysical doctrine.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:NX6","CH_B337_TD_2:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:DBN-01","text":"The system requires the falsity of any model in which the necessary forms of thought directly disclose the constitution of supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:DNX2","CH_B337_CIPP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:DBN-02","text":"The thinking subject cannot be transparent to itself as a thing in itself merely because it accompanies and unifies its representations.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:DNX3","CH_B337_CIPP:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:DBN-03","text":"The world-whole cannot be admitted as an ordinary empirical object whose complete series of conditions could be given within experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:DNX1","CH_B337_CIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:DBN-04","text":"Contradiction cannot authorize the selection of one opposed claim while leaving the shared totalizing premise unexamined.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:NX4","CH_B337_CIAP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:DBN-05","text":"No standpoint outside reason’s own cognitive conditions is available from which the illusion could be inspected, escaped, or finally extinguished.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SNX2","CH_B337_CIPP:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:BSA-01","text":"The deepest absence is an intuition of the unconditioned adequate to reason’s demand, whether that demand concerns self, world, or objects in general.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SNX1","CH_B337_CIPP:SNX1","CH_B337_CIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:BSA-02","text":"The system is organized around a permanent non-coincidence between the operation that demands totality and the cognition that would be required to possess it objectively.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SNX3","CH_B337_CIPP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:BSA-03","text":"Complete self-possession and complete world-possession are structurally unavailable because the condition of cognition cannot become its own adequately intuited object or completed phenomenal totality.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:SNX4","CH_B337_CIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:BSA-04","text":"Positive resolution remains absent at the cluster boundary: the individual antinomies are not resolved and the third dialectical domain is only projected.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:SNX5","CH_B337_CIAP:MRT4","CH_B337_CIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:XD-01","text":"A single conversion mechanism underlies both developed branches: reason transforms a formal demand for unity into an asserted determination of an unconditioned object.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:D8","CH_B337_CIPP:FGS3","CH_B337_CIAP:SD2","CL_B337_20:GA-03","CL_B337_20:GA-05","CL_B337_20:GA-06","CL_B337_20:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:XD-02","text":"Rational psychology verifies at the level of the self the broader distinction between subjective transcendental necessity and objective cognition announced by the general dialectical architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:D2","CH_B337_CIPP:SD1","CL_B337_20:GA-01","CL_B337_20:GA-02","CL_B337_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:XD-03","text":"The movement from paralogism to antinomy increases dialectical force because the expansion from subjective to objective synthesis replaces unsupported reification with opposed claims of comparable plausibility.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:SD3","CH_B337_CIAP:D3","CL_B337_20:GA-07","CL_B337_20:GA-08","CL_B337_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:XD-04","text":"Because illusion persists across both subjective and objective domains, critique must govern assent rather than attempt to suppress the rational operation producing the illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A10","CH_B337_CIPP:SD3","CH_B337_CIAP:A8","CL_B337_20:GA-04","CL_B337_20:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:XD-05","text":"The collapse of positive rational psychology and pure rational cosmology establishes a common institutional transformation from metaphysical science into critical discipline.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:ME6","CH_B337_CIAP:ME5","CL_B337_20:GA-09","CL_B337_20:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:XD-06","text":"Expansion in totalizing scope produces an inverse decline in object availability: the self is designated without intuition, the world-whole is unavailable in experience, and the third totality remains only anticipated.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SD3","CH_B337_CIPP:SNX1","CH_B337_CIAP:SNX1","CL_B337_20:GA-02","CL_B337_20:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:XD-07","text":"The prohibition on inferring a contrary from the failure of one doctrine generalizes from the soul dispute to cosmological conflict and becomes a rule against one-sided metaphysical closure.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:DNX8","CH_B337_CIAP:DNX4","CL_B337_20:GA-10","CL_B337_20:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:XD-08","text":"Practical orientation after speculative failure is an asymmetrical remainder of the subjective branch and cannot be generalized into the still-absent critical resolution of cosmological antinomy.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:SD7","CH_B337_CIAP:SD6","CL_B337_20:GA-09","CL_B337_20:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:BSD-01","text":"Rational necessity and objective validity become orthogonal: an idea or inference may be unavoidable as an operation of reason while remaining illegitimate as cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SD4","CH_B337_CIPP:SD1","CL_B337_20:GA-01","CL_B337_20:GA-02","CL_B337_20:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:BSD-02","text":"Reason is both the generator of transcendental error and the only available site of its critical containment, making critique immanent rather than externally corrective.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SD1","CH_B337_TD_2:SNX2","CL_B337_20:GA-04","CL_B337_20:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:BSD-03","text":"The transcendental subject is demoted from a knowable metaphysical entity to a necessary but ontologically indeterminate function within cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:SD2","CH_B337_CIPP:ME3","CL_B337_20:GA-02","CL_B337_20:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:BSD-04","text":"Contradiction becomes a scale-sensitive boundary signal: it appears when reason applies its demand for unconditioned unity to the objective synthesis of phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:SD2","CH_B337_CIAP:ME6","CL_B337_20:GA-07","CL_B337_20:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:BSD-05","text":"Temporal persistence and completed historical-cosmological totality are jointly withheld because inner time supplies no permanent self-object and phenomenal succession supplies no given absolute whole.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:SNX2","CH_B337_CIAP:A11","CL_B337_20:GA-02","CL_B337_20:GA-06","CL_B337_20:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:BSD-06","text":"Critical non-cognition is productive rather than empty because it reclassifies objects, neutralizes opposed dogmatisms, and establishes rules for legitimate metaphysical judgment.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:ME3","CH_B337_CIPP:ME4","CH_B337_CIAP:ME3","CL_B337_20:GA-09","CL_B337_20:GA-10","CL_B337_20:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:RLE-01","text":"Reason demands unconditioned unity, projects an adequate object, fails the intuition test, limits the resulting claim, and then reproduces the demand in a broader domain.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:FGS2","CH_B337_CIPP:FGS4","CH_B337_CIAP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RLE-02","text":"Rational psychology enters an inverse loop in which increasing conceptual purity removes the empirical content required for objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:D1","CH_B337_CIPP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RLE-03","text":"In the subjective branch, apperception is captured as an object, projected through the categories, rejected for lack of intuition, and returned as disciplined indeterminacy.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:FGS2","CH_B337_CIPP:FGS5","CH_B337_CIPP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RLE-04","text":"In the objective branch, the demand for complete phenomenal conditions produces opposed totalizations, which intensify conflict until both dogmatic choice and sceptical retreat are blocked.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A7","CH_B337_CIAP:D5","CH_B337_CIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RLE-05","text":"The escalation path runs from equivocation about the self to contradiction about the world and toward an anticipated absolute totality of conditions for all possible things.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:AS2","CH_B337_CIAP:A4","CH_B337_CIAP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:RLE-06","text":"The governing attractor is the unavailable adequate object: every domain seeks it, every constitutive claim fails without it, and critique survives by preserving its absence.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:NX1","CH_B337_CIPP:SNX1","CH_B337_CIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:FGS-B01","text":"Necessary laws of reason generate transcendental ideas and demands for unconditioned unity.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A3","CH_B337_TD_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:FGS-B02","text":"Formal syllogistic differences distribute those demands across subjective unity, phenomenal conditions, and conditions of objects in general.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A11","CH_B337_CIAP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:FGS-B03","text":"Formal demand for unity → projection of an adequate unconditioned object → attribution of objective reality.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A7","CH_B337_TD_2:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:FGS-B04","text":"Projected object → intuition and possible-experience test → failure of constitutive cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A1","CH_B337_CIPP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:FGS-B05","text":"Subjective branch → logical apperception treated as intuition → categorical soul-predicates → paralogism → speculative indeterminacy.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:FGS2","CH_B337_CIPP:FGS3","CH_B337_CIPP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:FGS-B06","text":"Objective branch → totalized phenomenal conditions → opposed rational claims → antinomy → suspension of doctrinal cosmology.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A7","CH_B337_CIAP:SD2","CH_B337_CIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:FGS-B07","text":"Persistent illusion → separation of necessary origin from invalid result → transformation of metaphysical doctrine into critical discipline → open horizon toward the undeveloped third form.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SD5","CH_B337_CIPP:ME6","CH_B337_CIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:AFM-B01","text":"Necessary rational idea → demand for unconditioned unity → objectification without intuition → objective seeming → dialectical failure → critical limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:D3","CH_B337_TD_2:SD2","CH_B337_TD_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:AFM-B02","text":"Logical self-unity → projected soul-properties → paralogistic indeterminacy → phenomenal world-series → opposed totalizations → cosmological antinomy.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:D5","CH_B337_CIPP:FGS3","CH_B337_CIAP:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:AFM-B03","text":"Metaphysical doctrine → intuition test → symmetrical prohibition of dogmatic contraries → critical discipline → anticipated but undeveloped ideal of pure reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:AS7","CH_B337_CIAP:DNX5","CH_B337_CIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:MME-01","text":"Metaphysics is redrawn from competition among supersensible doctrines into an immanent analysis of how reason generates illegitimate object claims.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:ME2","CH_B337_CIAP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MME-02","text":"The self is reclassified from a cognized soul-substance into a logical condition of thought coupled with a temporally given phenomenal self.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:ME1","CH_B337_CIPP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MME-03","text":"The world-whole of empirical synthesis is separated from the absolute totality of conditions for all possible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:A11","CH_B337_CIAP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MME-04","text":"Contradiction is reclassified from simple logical breakdown into a diagnostic effect of applying unconditioned unity to conditioned phenomenal synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:SD2","CH_B337_CIAP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MME-05","text":"Materialism and spiritualism, like dogmatism and scepticism, cease to constitute exhaustive alternatives and become paired failures at a shared cognitive boundary.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:ME4","CH_B337_CIAP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MME-06","text":"Critical reading must distinguish an argument’s necessary origin, formal operation, objective interpretation, and terminal epistemic status rather than classifying it simply as valid or invalid.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A8","CH_B337_TD_2:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system examines claims about the soul, the totality of phenomenal conditions, and the projected totality grounding objects in general.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A11","CH_B337_CIPP:AS1","CH_B337_CIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, syllogistic form and the demand for unconditioned unity repeatedly convert conditions of thought into alleged determinations of objects.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:D8","CH_B337_CIPP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, critique replaces doctrinal victory and sceptical retreat with analysis of the shared mechanism generating opposed metaphysical claims.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:AS5","CH_B337_CIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, the subject’s noumenal constitution, its persistence through time, and the completed world-series remain unavailable to cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:DNX3","CH_B337_CIPP:SNX2","CH_B337_CIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system reproduces reason’s escalation across domains and then interrupts each expansion through the recurring demand for intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:AS6","CH_B337_CIPP:FGS4","CH_B337_CIAP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:FL-01","text":"The characterization of dialectical arguments as syllogistic in origin but sophistical in result remains unstable beside the later treatment of paralogism as formally correct yet dependent on equivocation.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A8","CH_B337_CIPP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:FL-02","text":"The logical reality and simplicity of apperception support an anti-materialist limit while the system denies that the same simplicity determines the subject’s ontology.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:MRT7","CH_B337_CIPP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:FL-03","text":"The overview’s conversion of the contradictory unconditioned series into the opposite unity sits uneasily with the antinomy’s insistence that both sides possess apparent support and that positive resolution remains unstated.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A13","CH_B337_CIAP:AS4","CH_B337_CIAP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:FL-04","text":"Practical retention of future existence depends on moral and teleological premises not generated by the critique of speculative psychology itself.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:MRT9","CH_B337_CIPP:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MP-01","text":"Logical functions mutate into metaphysical predicates at the precise point where apperception is treated as an intuition of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:FGS2","CH_B337_CIPP:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MP-02","text":"One-sided illusion mutates into symmetrical antinomy when reason moves from subjective conditions of representation to objective conditions in phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:AS2","CH_B337_CIAP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MP-03","text":"Contradiction mutates from an apparent defeat of reason into a diagnostic instrument revealing the boundary of unconditioned phenomenal totalization.","refs":["CH_B337_CIAP:SD2","CH_B337_CIAP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MP-04","text":"Metaphysical doctrine mutates into critical discipline when failed objective predication is retained as knowledge of reason’s limits rather than discarded as meaningless.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:AS7","CH_B337_CIAP:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly modeled as a conditioned field over which reason repeatedly projects horizons of completion that cannot be given as objects within that field.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:SD2","CH_B337_CIAP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes an empty organizing operator coupled to a temporal appearance rather than a self-transparent substance standing outside cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A2","CH_B337_CIPP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:HSI-03","text":"Time is a conditioned succession that supplies neither a permanent soul-object nor a completed world-series, while reason persistently demands both persistence and totality.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:A6","CH_B337_CIPP:SNX2","CH_B337_CIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:HSI-04","text":"Critical navigation is possible only as recursive boundary work: reason cannot escape its illusion but can prevent its projected horizons from hardening into possessed objects.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:A10","CH_B337_TD_2:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_20:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the common mechanism linking necessary transcendental ideas, illicit objectification, paralogistic projection, cosmological contradiction, and critical limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:MRT1","CH_B337_CIPP:MRT1","CH_B337_CIAP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined regarding the exact premises of the three dialectical arguments, the individual cosmological contradictions, and the developed operation of the ideal of pure reason.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:MRT5","CH_B337_CIAP:MRT4","CH_B337_CIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only while necessary generation, subjective transcendental reality, objective validity, and constitutive cognition remain sharply distinguished.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:MRT6","CH_B337_CIPP:MRT2","CH_B337_CIAP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest in its shared intuition criterion, its differentiation of asymmetrical and symmetrical illusion, and its recoding of metaphysical doctrine as critical discipline.","refs":["CH_B337_TD_2:MRT7","CH_B337_CIPP:MRT3","CH_B337_CIAP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_20:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if practical redirection remains local, positive antinomy resolution remains absent, and the undeveloped third form remains an open structural horizon rather than a completed synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_CIPP:MRT9","CH_B337_CIAP:SD6","CH_B337_CIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 58–60","context":"Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason · Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason · Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason"}},{"id":"CL_B337_21","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 61–63","order":114,"parents":["CH_B337_SISC","CH_B337_SIAP","CH_B337_SIIR"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"089472698580dea9c6d4e296ea2ba4802f1ea543791be92a072b62c28a7569ac","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:AS-B01","text":"The system confronts a rational demand that moves from every conditioned phenomenon toward an unconditioned totality that no possible experience can present as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:AS2","CH_B337_SIIR:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AS-B02","text":"Reason first generates cosmological ideas by extending categories into four forms of absolute phenomenal completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A4","CH_B337_SISC:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AS-B03","text":"When the demanded completeness is imposed upon synthesis governed by empirical conditions, the four ideas become four necessary self-conflicts of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:AS3","CH_B337_SIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AS-B04","text":"Reciprocal proof does not establish a doctrinal victor but converts antinomy into a diagnostic of defective framing, domain shifts, and incompatible standpoints.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:AS7","CH_B337_SIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AS-B05","text":"Suspension of doctrinal adjudication permits a second diagnosis that separates grounds of truth from the practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic motives of adherence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A5","CH_B337_SIIR:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AS-B06","text":"The thesis secures unconditioned grounds, practical support, and systematic closure, whereas the antithesis secures lawful and indefinitely extensible natural inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A8","CH_B337_SIIR:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AS-B07","text":"The movement is recursive rather than linear: constructed totality produces contradiction, contradiction returns reason to its own procedures, and unresolved disunion reopens the demand for critique.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:AS6","CH_B337_SIIR:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AS-B08","text":"Closure consists neither in possession of the unconditioned nor in abolition of its demand, but in regulated non-deception, refusal of partisan victory, and deferred inquiry into reason’s self-division.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:SD5","CH_B337_SIIR:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every given conditioned phenomenon activates reason’s necessary demand for the complete series of its conditions and ultimately for the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A3","CH_B337_SIIR:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-02","text":"[Mutating] Cosmological ideas arise when reason transforms categories from empirically bounded rules into representations directed toward absolute completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A2","CH_B337_SISC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Only subordinated serial conditioning supports the rational demand for absolute totality; co-ordinated relations and descending consequences do not.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:AS3","CH_B337_SISC:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Cosmological inquiry remains concerned with phenomenal synthesis even when its demand exceeds every possible empirical completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A8","CH_B337_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Complete phenomenal totality is necessarily represented but cannot be exhibited as an object of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A13","CH_B337_SIIR:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Antinomy is generated by the structural mismatch between reason’s demand for completed unity and understanding’s rule-governed empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A4","CH_B337_SIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] The four forms of cosmological completeness become four conflicts concerning magnitude, composition, causal origination, and existential dependence.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A15","CH_B337_SIIR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Equal rational support for opposed cosmological propositions functions as evidence of a misunderstanding in the object, domain, or standpoint of the dispute.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:D2","CH_B337_SIAP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] The standpoint of absolute totality demands closure, while the standpoint of each conditioned member perpetually reopens the series and prohibits an empirical terminus.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:D3","CH_B337_SIIR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Experience supplies the sole objective basis of speculative cognition, but empirical restriction is legitimate only as a method and not as an exhaustive denial of the supersensible.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A11","CH_B337_SIIR:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Where truth remains undecided, rational interests explain adherence to doctrines without providing evidence for their objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A5","CH_B337_SIIR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] The authority of reason is partitioned between empirical objectivity in speculative cognition and non-probative practical significance for unconditioned ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:SD2","CH_B337_SIIR:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Transcendental illusion persists after diagnosis because the rational drive toward absolute completion is natural and cannot be extinguished.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A6","CH_B337_SIAP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Critical authority is secured through reflexive self-limitation and diagnostic suspension rather than through victory for either dogmatism or empiricism.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:SD4","CH_B337_SIAP:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:RMD-01","text":"The conditioned-to-unconditioned movement retains stable identity across the construction of cosmological ideas, their antinomial conflict, and the analysis of rational interest.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A3","CH_B337_SIIR:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RMD-02","text":"The category changes function from a rule of empirical synthesis into a transcendental idea and is then absorbed into the common production mechanism of the four antinomies.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A4","CH_B337_SIAP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RMD-03","text":"Regress first selects cosmologically relevant categories, then becomes the antithetical principle of indefinitely continued empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:D2","CH_B337_SIIR:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RMD-04","text":"The world mutates from the intended totality of phenomena into an unavailable object whose absence organizes the entire conflict.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:SD2","CH_B337_SIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RMD-05","text":"The unconditioned shifts from an alternative between a complete series and a first member into a doctrinal, practical, and architectonic object of partisan investment.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A14","CH_B337_SIIR:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RMD-06","text":"The four cosmological ideas become four conflicts and are finally recoded as expressions of competing principles for employing reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A15","CH_B337_SIAP:SD6","CH_B337_SIIR:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RMD-07","text":"Contradiction reverses valence from an apparent obstruction to knowledge into a method for exposing domain confusion and defective legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:ME1","CH_B337_SIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RMD-08","text":"Empiricism changes from the restrictive pole of antinomy into either a legitimate maxim of inquiry or, when absolutized, a dogmatic metaphysical denial.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A12","CH_B337_SIIR:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RMD-09","text":"The individual objects of conflict disappear as independent centers once they have exposed the higher-order impossibility of a phenomenal unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:DNX5","CH_B337_SIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:CGD-01","text":"Arguments involving void time, void space, spatial divisibility, causal interruption, and necessary existence remain local proof mechanisms rather than independent cluster-level conclusions.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A12","CH_B337_SIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:CGD-02","text":"The logic of subordinated serial conditioning scales upward because it generates both the cosmological table and the recurrent form of all four antinomies.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:AS3","CH_B337_SIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:CGD-03","text":"The fourfold classification bridges construction and conflict by predetermining the domains in which reason’s demand for completeness will divide against itself.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A15","CH_B337_SIIR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:CGD-04","text":"Equal rational support for opposed claims catalyzes the transition from cosmological doctrine to transcendental diagnosis.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:D2","CH_B337_SIAP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:CGD-05","text":"Interest-analysis bridges epistemic stalemate and reflexive critique by explaining allegiance without converting motives into truth conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:AS6","CH_B337_SIIR:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:CGD-06","text":"The original selection of categories is absorbed into the higher-order claim that the four cosmological questions are necessary formal outputs of conditioned synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:D1","CH_B337_SIIR:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:CGD-07","text":"Psychological attributions concerning popular preference remain local and dispensable, whereas the distinction among practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interests scales upward.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:MRT7","CH_B337_SIIR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:GNX-01","text":"The world cannot be treated as a completed empirical object available to a surveying intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GNX-02","text":"The necessity of representing complete conditions does not establish the reality of a corresponding unconditioned object.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GNX-03","text":"Co-ordinated aggregates and descending consequences cannot generate cosmological totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GNX-04","text":"No phenomenal item can function as an absolutely first, simple, causally unconditioned, or necessary terminus while remaining subject to sensible conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GNX-05","text":"No synthetic passage may transfer the conditioning relation from a sensible series to an intelligible ground without equivocation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GNX-06","text":"Practical indispensability and architectonic desirability cannot provide the intuition required for speculative knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:DNX3","CH_B337_SIIR:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:GNX-07","text":"Empirical restraint cannot be converted into objective denial of whatever lies beyond phenomenal cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any model in which the world is given as an absolute totality within possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:DNX1","CH_B337_SIIR:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:DBN-02","text":"Logical consistency, rational necessity, and reciprocal provability must remain insufficient for objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:DNX2","CH_B337_SIIR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:DBN-03","text":"A univocal bridge between sensible conditions and intelligible grounds must be unavailable, or cosmological overreach would regain dogmatic legitimacy.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:DNX4","CH_B337_SIAP:NX16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:DBN-04","text":"The jurisdiction of experience over speculative objectivity must not be confused with the ontological exhaustiveness of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A1","CH_B337_SIIR:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:DBN-05","text":"Critical success cannot mean elimination of transcendental illusion, because the demand producing the illusion is constitutive of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:DNX6","CH_B337_SIAP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:DBN-06","text":"No partisan preference grounded in usefulness, popularity, or systematic convenience may function as a criterion of doctrinal truth.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:NX3","CH_B337_SIIR:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absence of an experience in which the world could be encountered as an absolute whole.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:BSA-02","text":"Its foreclosed object is a phenomenal unconditioned that would remain sensible while escaping every condition of sensibility.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:BSA-03","text":"The unavailable synthesis is a single theoretical position reconciling moral support, lawful natural explanation, and completed systematic cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:BSA-04","text":"No final experiential standpoint can terminate reason’s demand, possess the totality it seeks, or dissolve the division by a partisan verdict.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:SNX2","CH_B337_SIIR:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:XD-01","text":"Extending categories toward absolute completeness necessarily produces antinomy when the resulting ideas are treated as objects despite the objective boundary of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-02","CL_B337_21:GA-04","CL_B337_21:GA-06","CH_B337_SISC:A4","CH_B337_SIAP:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:XD-02","text":"The fourfold table of cosmological ideas predicts the exhaustiveness and ordering of the four antinomies.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-03","CL_B337_21:GA-07","CH_B337_SISC:A15","CH_B337_SIIR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:XD-03","text":"The alternative representations of the unconditioned as complete series or first condition become opposed standpoints once the same temporal or causal series is viewed globally and member by member.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-05","CL_B337_21:GA-09","CH_B337_SISC:A14","CH_B337_SIAP:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:XD-04","text":"The necessity of seeking completion combined with the equal defensibility of incompatible conclusions converts reciprocal contradiction into a criterion for transcendental misunderstanding.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-05","CL_B337_21:GA-08","CH_B337_SISC:SD4","CH_B337_SIAP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:XD-05","text":"Because transcendental illusion persists and interests shape allegiance, critique must regulate both recurrent inference and recurrent partisan attachment.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-11","CL_B337_21:GA-13","CL_B337_21:GA-14","CH_B337_SIAP:A6","CH_B337_SIIR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:XD-06","text":"The distinction between world-totality and causal nature allows indefinite empirical explanation to remain valid without becoming an ontological proof that no unconditioned ground is possible.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-04","CL_B337_21:GA-10","CH_B337_SISC:A16","CH_B337_SIIR:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:XD-07","text":"Exclusion of noumenal cosmology does not eliminate practical significance; it partitions practical orientation from speculative object-cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-04","CL_B337_21:GA-12","CH_B337_SISC:NX10","CH_B337_SIIR:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:XD-08","text":"The unavailability of theoretical completion explains why architectonic and practical interests attach to dogmatic closure without making that closure true.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-05","CL_B337_21:GA-11","CL_B337_21:GA-12","CH_B337_SISC:SNX1","CH_B337_SIIR:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes cognizable as an indefinitely conditioned field of phenomenal regress rather than as a completed world-object.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-03","CL_B337_21:GA-04","CL_B337_21:GA-05","CH_B337_SISC:SD2","CH_B337_SIIR:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:BSD-02","text":"Objective validity is restricted to possible experience, while cosmological ideas retain diagnostic and practical functions without acquiring speculative objecthood.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-10","CL_B337_21:GA-12","CH_B337_SIAP:A1","CH_B337_SIIR:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:BSD-03","text":"The rational subject becomes an internally divided tribunal in which totalizing reason, empirical understanding, and competing interests exercise non-identical forms of authority.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-06","CL_B337_21:GA-11","CL_B337_21:GA-12","CH_B337_SIAP:AS3","CH_B337_SIIR:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes an open regressive and causal order in which an empirical first event is unavailable, while causal firstness remains conceptually distinguishable from temporal firstness.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-03","CL_B337_21:GA-09","CH_B337_SISC:A6","CH_B337_SIAP:A17","CH_B337_SIIR:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:BSD-05","text":"Contradiction becomes a methodological instrument that tests whether a question preserves its domain, its conditioning relation, and the distinction between motivation and proof.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-08","CL_B337_21:GA-11","CH_B337_SIAP:SD1","CH_B337_SIIR:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:BSD-06","text":"Philosophical victory becomes strategically suspect because alternating proofs and interests can reproduce allegiance without improving objective warrant.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-08","CL_B337_21:GA-11","CL_B337_21:GA-14","CH_B337_SIAP:SD3","CH_B337_SIIR:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:BSD-07","text":"Systematic closure survives only as an architectonic attractor whose force organizes inquiry even though it cannot legitimate a first ground.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-05","CL_B337_21:GA-11","CL_B337_21:GA-13","CH_B337_SISC:SNX1","CH_B337_SIIR:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned experience generates a demand for completion; projected completion generates antinomy; diagnosed antinomy returns reason to the same unavoidable demand under stricter regulation.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:AS2","CH_B337_SIAP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RLE-02","text":"The principal escalation path runs from empirical category to transcendental idea, from idea to world-totality, and from world-totality to necessary self-conflict.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A4","CH_B337_SIAP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RLE-03","text":"The unconditioned is the dominant conceptual attractor because every serial regress points toward it although no speculative cognition can secure it.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:D7","CH_B337_SIIR:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RLE-04","text":"Possible experience forms a counter-attractor that continually redirects speculative cognition from absolute closure toward lawful and indefinitely extensible inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A11","CH_B337_SIIR:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RLE-05","text":"Thesis and antithesis recursively reinforce one another because closure exposes violations of empirical conditioning, while infinite conditioning renews reason’s demand for closure.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:D14","CH_B337_SIIR:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RLE-06","text":"Repetition across magnitude, composition, causality, and existence marks a phase change from separate metaphysical problems to a single distributed antinomial machine.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:SD6","CH_B337_SIIR:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:RLE-07","text":"Persistent illusion creates a regulatory loop in which critique must be repeatedly reapplied rather than completed once and for all.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A6","CH_B337_SIIR:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:FGS-B01","text":"Given conditioned phenomenon → demand for complete antecedent conditions → orientation toward the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:AS2","CH_B337_SIIR:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FGS-B02","text":"Test for subordinated serial conditioning → exclusion of co-ordinated and merely progressive relations → delimitation of cosmological relevance.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:AS3","CH_B337_SISC:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FGS-B03","text":"Extension of admissible categories → four ideas of absolute phenomenal completeness → projected world-totalities.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A4","CH_B337_SISC:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FGS-B04","text":"Necessary representation of completion + absence of adequate experience → unstable objectification of cosmological totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A13","CH_B337_SIIR:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FGS-B05","text":"Totality standpoint / conditioned-member standpoint → opposed but rationally necessitated conclusions → antinomy.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A4","CH_B337_SIAP:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FGS-B06","text":"Free reciprocal conflict → exposure of equivocation, domain change, and standpoint confusion → diagnostic certainty without doctrinal victory.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A7","CH_B337_SIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FGS-B07","text":"Suspended truth-verdict → comparison of practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interests → explanation of allegiance without proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A5","CH_B337_SIIR:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FGS-B08","text":"Persistent totalizing drive + empirical restriction → reflexive self-limitation → regulated non-deception and deferred radical critique.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:SD5","CH_B337_SIIR:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned phenomenon → regressive demand for conditions → unconditioned as rational terminus.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AFM-B02","text":"Serial-conditioning filter → four admissible forms of cosmological completeness → systematic cosmological table.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:D1","CH_B337_SISC:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AFM-B03","text":"Projected absolute totality → collision with sensible and empirical conditions → four antinomies.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:AS1","CH_B337_SIIR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AFM-B04","text":"Reciprocal proof → failure of dogmatic adjudication → diagnosis of misunderstanding.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:D2","CH_B337_SIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AFM-B05","text":"Suspension of truth-adjudication → comparison of dogmatism and empiricism → mapping of rational interests.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:AS4","CH_B337_SIIR:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AFM-B06","text":"Empirical discipline separated from empirical denial → simultaneous restriction of dogmatism and naturalistic overreach.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:D4","CH_B337_SIIR:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:AFM-B07","text":"Persistent illusion → reflexive self-limitation → provisional practical orientation and deferred resolution of reason’s disunion.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:SNX4","CH_B337_SIIR:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:MME-01","text":"The decisive distinction shifts from finite versus infinite objects to regress versus progress and completed versus uncompletable synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:ME1","CH_B337_SIAP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MME-02","text":"The world is reclassified from a possible total object into a problem generated by the absent completion of phenomenal synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:SD2","CH_B337_SIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MME-03","text":"Contradiction is reclassified from accidental doctrinal disagreement into evidence concerning the defective constitution of a transcendental question.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:ME1","CH_B337_SIAP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MME-04","text":"The four cosmological disputes are reclassified from separate metaphysical topics into expressions of competing principles for employing reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:ME1","CH_B337_SIIR:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MME-05","text":"Grounds of objective truth are separated from motives of philosophical adhesion, making interest-analysis possible without psychologizing validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:ME3","CH_B337_SIIR:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MME-06","text":"Empirical method is separated from metaphysical naturalism, while architectonic unity is separated from evidence for a first ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:ME4","CH_B337_SIIR:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MME-07","text":"Reciprocal proof becomes a critical reading method that tracks domain changes, standpoint shifts, and equivocations instead of extracting a doctrinal winner.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:SD1","CH_B337_SIAP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system stages conflicts concerning world-limits, simple parts, freedom, and necessary existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:ALT1","CH_B337_SIIR:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, every conflict is produced by the collision between rational completion and empirically conditioned synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:ALT2","CH_B337_SIIR:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, opposed proofs and interest-profiles displace direct doctrinal adjudication with diagnosis of how reason employs and divides itself.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:ALT3","CH_B337_SIIR:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is a phenomenal order governed by spatial, temporal, and causal conditions, without a given absolute whole or empirically unconditioned beginning.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:ALT4","CH_B337_SIIR:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the staging of symmetrical conflicts followed by suspended judgment enacts the reflexive self-limitation the system prescribes.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:AS6","CH_B337_SIIR:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:FL-01","text":"Reason necessarily demands completion even though no corresponding totality can become an object of speculative possession.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:SD4","CH_B337_SIIR:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FL-02","text":"The declared equal defensibility of thesis and antithesis is strained by later restrictions that asymmetrically expose domain errors in particular proofs.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:AS4","CH_B337_SIAP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FL-03","text":"Experience is the sole objective basis of speculative cognition, yet the absence of phenomenal cognition cannot warrant denial beyond experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A11","CH_B337_SIIR:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FL-04","text":"Unconditioned ideas are presented as supports for morality and religion while being denied the evidential status required for speculative knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A8","CH_B337_SIIR:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FL-05","text":"Reason’s architectonic demand for completed derivation conflicts with the indefinitely extensible inquiry that alone possesses objective empirical warrant.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A9","CH_B337_SIIR:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:FL-06","text":"Several arguments risk moving from conditions for thinking or experiencing totality to conclusions about existence, despite the system’s prohibition on inferring objects from necessary ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:MRT7","CH_B337_SISC:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MP-01","text":"The first mutation occurs when an empirical category becomes a transcendental idea directed toward ultimate closure.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A4","CH_B337_SISC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MP-02","text":"The unconditioned mutates from a formal limit of regress into the affirmative or negative object of dogmatic and empirical allegiance.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A14","CH_B337_SIIR:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MP-03","text":"Contradiction mutates from apparent metaphysical deadlock into a meta-criterion for detecting defective questions.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:ME2","CH_B337_SIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MP-04","text":"Empiricism mutates from a valid speculative maxim into dogmatism when methodological restriction becomes objective denial.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIR:A12","CH_B337_SIIR:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MP-05","text":"Freedom mutates from apparent temporal firstness into causal spontaneity, preserving conceptual possibility only by separating causal origin from empirical temporal interruption.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A17","CH_B337_SIAP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as an indefinitely extensible machine of conditioned series coupled to a rational engine that repeatedly projects unavailable completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:SD2","CH_B337_SIIR:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:HSI-02","text":"The subject appears as a divided tribunal whose internal conflict distributes authority among totalizing reason, empirical understanding, and practical or architectonic interests.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:AS2","CH_B337_SIIR:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:HSI-03","text":"Time appears as an irreversible regress through antecedent conditions in which causal firstness may be thought but cannot be identified with an empirically first moment.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:A6","CH_B337_SIAP:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:HSI-04","text":"Navigation remains possible through free conflict, preservation of domain and signification, exposure of interests, and repeated refusal to convert orientation into knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:A7","CH_B337_SIAP:A10","CH_B337_SIIR:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_21:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the passage from serial conditioning through cosmological ideas and antinomy to the analysis of rational interests.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:MRT1","CH_B337_SIAP:MRT1","CH_B337_SIIR:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MRT-B02","text":"The final transcendental resolution remains underdetermined because the supplied architecture diagnoses reason’s disunion but deliberately defers its complete reconciliation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:MRT5","CH_B337_SIIR:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale because serial generation, empirical undecidability, reciprocal proof, and non-adjudicative interest-analysis constrain one another.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:MRT3","CH_B337_SIIR:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where the regress criterion generates the fourfold field and reciprocal contradiction becomes evidence about reason’s limits rather than either doctrine’s truth.","refs":["CH_B337_SISC:MRT4","CH_B337_SIAP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on sequencing and recursive return because construction must precede conflict, conflict must precede interest-analysis, and unresolved analysis must reopen critique.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:AS6","CH_B337_SIIR:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MRT-B06","text":"The actual movement is preserved only if synthesis ends in diagnostic suspension and provisional orientation rather than importing a doctrinal victor.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:MRT10","CH_B337_SIIR:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_21:MRT-B07","text":"Particular antinomial proofs, rhetorical battlefield effects, and practical attractions resist complete axiomatic reduction, but they remain local intensifiers rather than missing components of the cluster-level engine.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAP:MRT2","CH_B337_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 61–63","context":"Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas · Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason · Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions"}},{"id":"CL_B337_22","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 64–66","order":115,"parents":["CH_B337_SINI","CH_B337_SVSE","CH_B337_SVTI"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"dbe6d97e782bdac231154e942dbd74063e734cd83c50e551de55f97105bfd289","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:AS-B01","text":"Pure reason is compelled to seek an unconditioned totality, although no completed totality can be presented within experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:AS1","CH_B337_SINI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AS-B02","text":"The cosmological problem is recast from a question about a hidden world-object into a question about the cognitive operation that produces absolute-totality demands.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A11","CH_B337_SINI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AS-B03","text":"Sceptical comparison establishes that both infinite continuation and finite termination fail when treated as objects of empirical regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:AS5","CH_B337_SVSE:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AS-B04","text":"The failure of opposed cosmological answers does not suspend inquiry; it transfers inquiry from the alleged object to its mode of presentation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:AS2","CH_B337_SVSE:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AS-B05","text":"Transcendental idealism supplies the positive key by locating objects in lawful possible experience rather than in self-subsistent existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:AS2","CH_B337_SVTI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AS-B06","text":"Empirical realism is retained because bodies, events, and absent objects remain objectively valid as positions within possible progression and regression of perception.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:AS3","CH_B337_SVTI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AS-B07","text":"Cosmological dialectic arises when the open rule of seeking conditions is converted into a completed object that supposedly exists independently of experiential conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A6","CH_B337_SVTI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AS-B08","text":"The cluster closes through a critical dissolution: reason retains its demand for systematic completion while losing entitlement to cognize an unconditioned world-whole.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:SD5","CH_B337_SVTI:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective validity belongs only to what can be related to possible experience under lawful conditions of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A1","CH_B337_SVTI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Cosmological ideas arise from reason’s internally generated demand to complete the series of empirical conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A7","CH_B337_SVSE:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Every empirical condition opens a further regress, while every attempted final condition either exceeds empirical synthesis or remains conditionable.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A5","CH_B337_SVSE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Antinomy is not conflict between determinate cosmological facts but the diagnostic symptom of objectifying an idea of totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:AS3","CH_B337_SVTI:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Critical resolution examines the conditions generating a transcendental question rather than deciding a purported object beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A11","CH_B337_SVSE:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Transcendental idealism and empirical realism are jointly required: phenomenal objects are real within experience precisely because they are not things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:SD1","CH_B337_SVTI:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Empirical regress validates claims about absent objects as possible continuations of experience while invalidating claims to a completed empirical whole.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:D3","CH_B337_SVTI:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] The transcendental object may be thought as an indeterminate correlate of appearances but cannot supply determinate cosmological cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A10","CH_B337_SVTI:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] The unconditioned persists as a rational demand for completion but loses all warrant as an empirically or metaphysically cognizable entity.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:ME5","CH_B337_SVSE:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:RMD-01","text":"The unconditioned persists as reason’s demand for completion, mutates into the fictitious cosmological object tested by scepticism, and is terminally recoded as unavailable to determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A7","CH_B337_SVSE:DNX2","CH_B337_SVTI:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:RMD-02","text":"Possible experience recurs first as a boundary on object-knowledge, then as the test of antinomial claims, and finally as the constitutive field of empirical reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A6","CH_B337_SVSE:SD3","CH_B337_SVTI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:RMD-03","text":"Scepticism reverses function: rejected as a final confession of ignorance, it becomes a diagnostic procedure for exposing groundless cosmological presuppositions.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:NX8","CH_B337_SVSE:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:RMD-04","text":"The cosmological object migrates from alleged supersensible totality to a defective presentation of empirical series and is thereby absorbed into the critique of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:ME2","CH_B337_SVSE:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:RMD-05","text":"Empirical regress changes from a merely explanatory movement into the normative measure that distinguishes lawful object-reference from totalizing illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A8","CH_B337_SVSE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:RMD-06","text":"Idealism reverses valence from apparent denial of reality to the condition under which empirical reality can be affirmed without noumenal inflation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:NX1","CH_B337_SVTI:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:CGD-01","text":"The particular disputes concerning temporal beginning, spatial limits, simples, freedom, and necessity remain local instances; their shared excess-or-deficiency structure scales to the cluster level.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:AF2","CH_B337_SVSE:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:CGD-02","text":"The distinction between cosmological ideas and questions concerning a transcendental subject remains locally necessary, but scales upward only as a rule governing where empirical synthesis can be presupposed.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:SD4","CH_B337_SINI:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:CGD-03","text":"The bilateral refutation of thesis and antithesis is lower-layer machinery; the cluster-level proposition is that contradiction indexes defective object-presentation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:NX6","CH_B337_SVSE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:CGD-04","text":"The transition from sceptical suspension to transcendental idealism is a cluster-level catalytic bridge because diagnosis alone cannot explain why empirical objects are real without being self-subsistent.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:MRT7","CH_B337_SVTI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:CGD-05","text":"Assertions concerning absent, historical, distant, and future objects scale upward because they specify how open experiential continuation preserves realism while blocking totalization.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:D4","CH_B337_SVTI:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:GNX-01","text":"The cluster rejects the inference from a necessary thought of totality to a correspondingly determinate object.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:SNX4","CH_B337_SVSE:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GNX-02","text":"The cluster rejects both infinite completion and finite stopping points as empirically adequate forms of the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:FGS3","CH_B337_SVSE:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GNX-03","text":"The cluster rejects dogmatic affirmation and skeptical ignorance when both retain the fiction of an unknown object behind the cosmological question.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:ME4","CH_B337_SVSE:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GNX-04","text":"The cluster rejects transcendental realism, according to which spatial-temporal appearances possess determinate existence independently of sensibility and possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:AS1","CH_B337_SVTI:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:GNX-05","text":"The cluster rejects the conversion of the transcendental correlate into a knowable substrate, cause, or final ground of appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A9","CH_B337_SVTI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:DBN-01","text":"The cluster excludes the model in which every meaningful metaphysical question concerns a pre-existing object whose properties are merely hidden from inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:DBN-02","text":"The cluster excludes the assumption that an indefinitely extended empirical inquiry could become intuition of absolute totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:DNX3","CH_B337_SVTI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:DBN-03","text":"The cluster excludes a standpoint outside sensibility from which appearances could be compared with things as they exist independently.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:DNX1","CH_B337_SVTI:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:DBN-04","text":"The cluster excludes metaphysical completion as a prerequisite for empirical objectivity, making unfinished regress the condition rather than the defect of empirical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:SNX4","CH_B337_SVTI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:BSA-01","text":"No completed cognitive standpoint can contain the totality of conditions while remaining a standpoint within the empirical order whose conditions it totalizes.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:SNX1","CH_B337_SVSE:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:BSA-02","text":"No object can be both absolutely unconditioned and objectively available under the conditions that make empirical objects cognizable.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:SNX1","CH_B337_SVTI:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:BSA-03","text":"No final conversion of rational necessity into metaphysical object-knowledge is admissible; the absence of such conversion structures critical certainty itself.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:SNX2","CH_B337_SINI:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:XD-01","text":"Reason’s obligation to resolve cosmological problems is fulfilled only when sceptical bilateral failure is completed by an account of why the alleged object could never be given.","refs":["CL_B337_22:GA-04","CL_B337_22:GA-05","CL_B337_22:GA-06","CH_B337_SINI:AS4","CH_B337_SVSE:FGS5","CH_B337_SVTI:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:XD-02","text":"The same open regress that authorizes empirical claims about absent objects prohibits treating the regress as a completed world-object.","refs":["CL_B337_22:GA-01","CL_B337_22:GA-03","CL_B337_22:GA-07","CH_B337_SVTI:D3","CH_B337_SVTI:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:XD-03","text":"Antinomy becomes soluble without adjudicating its sides because transcendental idealism removes the shared realist presupposition that makes the opposed assertions look factual.","refs":["CL_B337_22:GA-04","CL_B337_22:GA-06","CH_B337_SVSE:SD2","CH_B337_SVTI:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:XD-04","text":"Empirical realism is not a residual concession after metaphysical restriction; it is the positive epistemic regime produced by restricting objects to lawful possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_22:GA-01","CL_B337_22:GA-06","CH_B337_SVTI:SD5","CH_B337_SVTI:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:XD-05","text":"The indeterminate transcendental correlate prevents critical idealism from collapsing into empirical idealism while simultaneously preventing it from becoming positive noumenal doctrine.","refs":["CL_B337_22:GA-06","CL_B337_22:GA-08","CH_B337_SVTI:A10","CH_B337_SVTI:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:BSD-01","text":"The conditions that make empirical objectivity possible also make cosmological completion impossible: objectivity requires an extendible field of possible experience rather than a completed field.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:ME3","CH_B337_SVTI:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:BSD-02","text":"Critical certainty concerns the genesis and invalid objectification of a question, not a metaphysical object concealed behind it.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:SD3","CH_B337_SVSE:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:BSD-03","text":"Historical, distant, and future reality acquire a modal status: they are real through lawful regress and progression, not through a view from beyond temporal experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:D4","CH_B337_SVTI:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:BSD-04","text":"The subject is divided by the same operation that divides the world: temporal self-appearance is empirically real, while the transcendental subject remains inaccessible to determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A8","CH_B337_SVTI:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:BSD-05","text":"The antinomies form a general algorithm of critical diagnosis: identify a totalizing demand, test both closures against empirical regress, and relocate the contradiction in the mode of representation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:AF1","CH_B337_SVTI:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:RLE-01","text":"Reason’s demand for conditions recursively generates an idea of totality, objectifies that idea, encounters antinomy, and returns to the demand as a merely regulative requirement.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:FGS1","CH_B337_SINI:FGS4","CH_B337_SINI:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:RLE-02","text":"Empirical regress functions as a double loop: every conditioned object demands further inquiry, while every attempt to complete the inquiry cancels its own empirical validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:A6","CH_B337_SVSE:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:RLE-03","text":"The central attractor is the world-whole, which repeatedly draws temporal, spatial, divisional, causal, and existential questions into one totalizing form.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:AF2","CH_B337_SVSE:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:RLE-04","text":"Scepticism is an escalation path rather than a terminus: bilateral contradiction intensifies until it forces investigation of the presupposition shared by both sides.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:AS2","CH_B337_SVSE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:RLE-05","text":"Transcendental idealism closes the loop by returning all world-claims to possible experience, thereby preserving empirical use while disabling absolute totalization.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:AF1","CH_B337_SVTI:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:FGS-B01","text":"Reason demands the completion of conditions and therefore forms an idea of absolute totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:FGS-B02","text":"The idea is attached to empirical series and converted into a demand for an unconditioned cosmological object.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:FGS2","CH_B337_SVSE:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:FGS-B03","text":"The demand bifurcates into infinite continuation and finite termination, each claiming to specify the absolute condition of the series.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:FGS-B04","text":"Infinite continuation exceeds possible synthesis, while finite termination remains exposed to the demand for further conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:FGS3","CH_B337_SVSE:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:FGS-B05","text":"Bilateral failure reveals that the alleged object is generated by a defective presentation of the empirical series rather than discovered within it.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:FGS5","CH_B337_SVSE:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:FGS-B06","text":"Transcendental idealism redefines objects as lawfully available appearances, preserving empirical reality while withdrawing totality and noumenal ground from determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:FGS2","CH_B337_SVTI:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:AFM-B01","text":"Rational demand for completion generates the necessity of a cosmological answer.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AFM-B02","text":"Cosmological totality is attached to empirical series but exceeds every possible empirical presentation.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A5","CH_B337_SINI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AFM-B03","text":"Opposed doctrines of infinite continuation and finite closure are tested through the same rule of empirical regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:AF1","CH_B337_SVSE:AF2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AFM-B04","text":"Their reciprocal failure displaces contradiction from reality to the objectifying form of the cosmological idea.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:AS6","CH_B337_SVSE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AFM-B05","text":"Transcendental idealism recodes spatial-temporal objects as appearances constituted under sensibility and lawful possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A1","CH_B337_SVTI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:AFM-B06","text":"Critical resolution preserves empirical inquiry and dissolves the demand for a completed world-whole or cognizable unconditioned ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:SD5","CH_B337_SVTI:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:MME-01","text":"The opposition between realism and idealism is redrawn: realism names lawful empirical validity, while idealism denies only independent noumenal subsistence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:ME2","CH_B337_SVTI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:MME-02","text":"The opposition between dogmatism and scepticism collapses because both positions can preserve the same false assumption of an unknown cosmological object.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:MME-03","text":"Antinomies become instruments for detecting errors in question-formation rather than evidence that reality itself contains contradictory determinations.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:MME-04","text":"Absent, past, distant, and future objects become intelligible as positions within lawful possible experience rather than as independently grasped entities.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:MME-05","text":"The unconditioned is reclassified from a possible constituent of the world into a regulative pressure internal to rational synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The cluster concerns rival claims about beginning, extent, divisibility, causality, freedom, necessity, and the reality of external objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:ALT1","CH_B337_SVSE:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — The operative mechanism is the incompatibility between open empirical regress and the demand for an unconditioned totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — Critical method replaces selection between doctrines with examination of the cognitive conditions that generate their shared object-demand.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:ALT3","CH_B337_SVTI:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological and temporal level — Space and time belong to appearance, and temporal succession can be indefinitely regressed without yielding a first or final world-condition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A2","CH_B337_SVTI:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological and performative level — The system performs its own thesis by converting every apparent object-level contradiction into a rule-governed diagnosis of representation.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:FGS5","CH_B337_SVSE:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:FL-01","text":"Reason is declared obligated to answer its cosmological questions, yet the answer often consists in cancelling the object-demand through which the question was posed.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:AS4","CH_B337_SINI:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:FL-02","text":"Empirical reality is affirmed through lawful possible perception, yet the adequacy of lawful connection to distinguish reality from illusion remains an assumed hinge.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:D2","CH_B337_SVTI:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:FL-03","text":"The transcendental object is invoked as a correlate of appearances while denied positive cognition, producing a necessary but compressed limit-concept.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:D7","CH_B337_SVTI:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:MP-01","text":"The unconditioned mutates from a necessary demand for completion into a fictitious object and then into a regulative boundary on cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:A7","CH_B337_SVSE:D6","CH_B337_SINI:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:MP-02","text":"Scepticism mutates from an inadequate final posture into the cathartic mechanism that prepares critical resolution.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:NX8","CH_B337_SVSE:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:MP-03","text":"Objectivity mutates from supposed independent subsistence into lawful availability for unified possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:HSI-01","text":"Reality is figured as an open, lawfully connected field of appearances rather than a completed container of self-subsistent things.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A3","CH_B337_SVTI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:HSI-02","text":"The subject is an empirically appearing knower whose temporal self-representation cannot yield access to the transcendental subject.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:A8","CH_B337_SVTI:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:HSI-03","text":"Time is not a totalizable line possessing a first or final point but an indefinitely extendible order of regression and progression.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:ALT5","CH_B337_SVTI:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:HSI-04","text":"Critical struggle consists in navigating the recurrent conversion of necessary regulative demands into illegitimate claims about independently existing wholes.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:FGS4","CH_B337_SVTI:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_22:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the transition from rational demand, through antinomial diagnosis, to transcendental-idealist dissolution.","refs":["CH_B337_SINI:MRT1","CH_B337_SVTI:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is coherent at this scale if possible experience is accepted as both the condition of phenomenal objectivity and the limit on cosmological totalization.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:MRT3","CH_B337_SVTI:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where it shows that infinite continuation and finite closure fail under the same empirical criterion while empirical inquiry itself remains intact.","refs":["CH_B337_SVSE:MRT4","CH_B337_SVTI:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_22:MRT-B04","text":"The irreducible remainder is the status of the transcendental correlate, whose invocation is necessary to mark the limit of appearance but resistant to positive axiomatic determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SVTI:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 64–66","context":"Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems · Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas · Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic"}},{"id":"CL_B337_23","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 67–69","order":116,"parents":["CH_B337_SVCS","CH_B337_SVRP","CH_B337_SIEU"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"1b57dce6dc2fcae1a83956fbdc33e22ad0f0fce7da935d0855a7ba1a05f207bf","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:AS-B01","text":"The system solves the problem of preserving reason’s necessary demand for conditions without converting that demand into knowledge that a completed series of conditions is given.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AS-B02","text":"The phenomenal world is reframed as a temporally enacted empirical regress rather than an independently available total object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AS-B03","text":"Cosmological antinomy arises when the conditioned is used in a transcendental sense to infer conclusions about empirically apprehended appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AS-B04","text":"The opposed cosmological conclusions are jointly dissolved because their apparent contradiction depends on a shared and ungrounded world-totality assumption.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AS-B05","text":"Totality is retained only by being recoded from a constitutive property of objects into a regulative rule for continuing empirical regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AS-B06","text":"The distinction between infinite and indefinite regress relocates cosmological difference from the alleged size of the world to the mode in which empirical material is given.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AS-B07","text":"Critical restriction does not diminish reason’s epistemic force but converts that force into the indefinite expansion and correction of empirical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AS-B08","text":"The system closes critically rather than cosmologically: no completed metaphysical totality is attained, while empirical inquiry remains normatively compelled to continue.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every conditioned appearance requires regress toward further conditions, but that requirement never establishes that the complete series is already given.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A1","CH_B337_SVCS:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Appearances exist only through temporally successive empirical synthesis and therefore cannot be treated as self-subsistent completed totalities.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Totality changes from an illicit object-predicate of appearances into a legitimate directive for regressive empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] The distinction between constitutive object-determination and regulative procedure governs both the dissolution of antinomy and the authorization of inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Antinomy is generated by mistaking a rule for extending understanding for a description of reality in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Finitude and infinitude are not determinable predicates of the phenomenal series considered independently of the regress through which it is given.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The permissible form of regress depends on the mode of empirical givenness rather than on an intrinsic metaphysical magnitude of the series.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] The absolutely unconditioned cannot appear as an empirical member or final termination within a series of appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Reason acquires practical epistemic influence precisely by losing authority to determine objects a priori.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Critique resolves cosmological conflict by transforming the status and function of the question rather than selecting one cosmological answer.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:RMD-01","text":"The appearance–thing-in-itself distinction retains stable identity as the system’s permanent boundary against transcendental object-claims.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RMD-02","text":"The conditioned recurs with altered function: it begins as the trigger of rational regress and becomes the endlessly renewed empirical condition that prevents regress from closing.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RMD-03","text":"Totality reverses valence from the false completion of a world-object into an unattainable task-condition governing inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RMD-04","text":"The world migrates from a presumed self-subsistent whole into a series that exists only in empirical regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RMD-05","text":"Infinity is displaced from an ontological quantity belonging to the world into a mode of permissible regression.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RMD-06","text":"Antinomy changes from an apparent conflict between world-descriptions into a diagnostic mechanism for exposing transcendental illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RMD-07","text":"The unconditioned persists only as a negative limit: it directs inquiry while being excluded as an empirically discoverable condition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RMD-08","text":"Subjective employment of reason ceases to signify mere insufficiency and becomes the doctrinally authorized form of cosmological cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:CGD-01","text":"The finite–infinite opposition remains a local form of cosmological predication, but at cluster scale it is absorbed into the broader prohibition against treating appearances as completed totalities.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:CGD-02","text":"The distinction between regressus in infinitum and regressus in indefinitum remains a local differentiation of inquiry, while bridging empirical givenness to the cluster-wide restriction on ontological totalization.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:CGD-03","text":"The equivocal cosmological syllogism is a lower-layer logical diagnosis that scales upward into a general account of antinomy as category-mistaken object-constitution.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A7","CH_B337_SIEU:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:CGD-04","text":"The appearance–thing-in-itself distinction scales from a local epistemic distinction into the global condition preventing phenomenal series from bearing absolute predicates.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:CGD-05","text":"Restrictions on spatial magnitude, temporal duration, causal subordination, and composition are absorbed into one higher-order rule against assigning completed totality to appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:CGD-06","text":"The possibility of an empirically given whole remains a local operational caveat rather than a global authorization to posit a completed cosmological whole.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:CGD-07","text":"The question of how far regress must proceed becomes a transition catalyst from metaphysical totality toward empirical procedure, but remains operationally underdetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A4","CH_B337_SIEU:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:CGD-08","text":"The conversion of dialectical reason into doctrinal subjective employment is the terminal recoding that gathers the preceding restrictions into an authorized method.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:GNX-01","text":"The required regress toward conditions is not an already completed series of conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GNX-02","text":"A phenomenon is not a thing in itself available independently of empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GNX-03","text":"The phenomenal series is not an object with an intrinsic quantity determinable as finite or infinite.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GNX-04","text":"No provisional empirical limit may be treated as the absolute termination of regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GNX-05","text":"The dissolution of antinomy is not sceptical suspension between equally valid cosmological theories.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GNX-06","text":"Reason and understanding are not instruments for transcendental cognition beyond possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:GNX-07","text":"Regulative reason is not epistemically impotent because it lacks constitutive authority over objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:DBN-01","text":"The system requires that the necessity of seeking conditions never count as proof that their complete totality is given.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:DBN-02","text":"The system disqualifies a world-object that exists prior to and independently of the regressive synthesis through which appearances are given.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:DBN-03","text":"The system rejects every supposedly exhaustive disjunction between finitude and infinitude that presupposes the world as a determinate thing in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:DBN-04","text":"The system cannot admit empirical completion as an achievement because its own rule of inquiry excludes an empirically given unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:DBN-05","text":"The epistemic value of reason cannot depend on possession of constitutive access to ultimate reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:BSA-01","text":"The system excludes any standpoint from which the totality of possible experience can be cognized as a completed object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable coincidence between reason’s formal demand and an intuition of an absolute whole structures the system’s critical restraint.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:BSA-03","text":"Final empirical arrival at the unconditioned is structurally unavailable because empirical conditions remain conditions only within open regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:BSA-04","text":"No external metaphysical guarantee may close inquiry or validate regulation from outside possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:XD-01","text":"The absence of a pre-given condition-series becomes productive only when totality is recoded as the rule governing continued regress rather than as a missing object.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-01","CL_B337_23:GA-03","CL_B337_23:GA-04","CH_B337_SVCS:A6","CH_B337_SVRP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:XD-02","text":"The distinction between infinite and indefinite regress establishes that cosmological claims are conditioned by the epistemic mode of givenness rather than by a world-series’ presumed intrinsic magnitude.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-06","CL_B337_23:GA-07","CH_B337_SVRP:AS5","CH_B337_SVRP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:XD-03","text":"The simultaneous invalidation of opposed cosmological claims licenses continuing inquiry without converting critical dissolution into sceptical paralysis.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-05","CL_B337_23:GA-09","CL_B337_23:GA-10","CH_B337_SVCS:A9","CH_B337_SIEU:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:XD-04","text":"Temporal succession and the empirical unavailability of the unconditioned turn each attained condition into a new occasion for further inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-02","CL_B337_23:GA-08","CH_B337_SVCS:A5","CH_B337_SVRP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:XD-05","text":"The rejection of world-totality recodes cosmology as an inquiry into the required extent of regress rather than a quest for the absolute quantity of reality.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-03","CL_B337_23:GA-10","CH_B337_SVCS:NX6","CH_B337_SIEU:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:XD-06","text":"Restricting reason’s object-claims becomes the condition of its maximal epistemic influence, producing a productive tension between limitation and expansion.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-04","CL_B337_23:GA-09","CH_B337_SIEU:A6","CH_B337_SIEU:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:XD-07","text":"The absence of a known limit cannot establish the absence of all limits, so every empirical boundary remains revisable rather than final.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-07","CL_B337_23:GA-08","CH_B337_SVRP:DNX3","CH_B337_SVRP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:BSD-01","text":"Knowledge becomes non-terminating but normatively compelled: its expansion is governed by the demand for completeness while completion remains unavailable.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-01","CL_B337_23:GA-08","CL_B337_23:GA-09","CH_B337_SIEU:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:BSD-02","text":"The world ceases to ground inquiry as a completed object and instead functions as the horizon generated through iterative empirical synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-02","CL_B337_23:GA-03","CH_B337_SVCS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:BSD-03","text":"Infinity becomes an epistemic-temporal vector of possible regress rather than an ontological measure of a world-whole.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-06","CL_B337_23:GA-07","CH_B337_SVRP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:BSD-04","text":"Antinomy supplies a general critical method: apparently irresolvable oppositions are tested by examining whether both claims rely on the same illicit object-condition.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-05","CL_B337_23:GA-10","CH_B337_SVCS:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:BSD-05","text":"Rational authority divides into procedural necessity and ontological restraint, preserving efficacy only if the two forms of authority remain distinct.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-04","CL_B337_23:GA-09","CH_B337_SIEU:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:BSD-06","text":"Objective incompletion becomes the condition of empirical correction because provisional limits are reclassified as nodes for further investigation.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-07","CL_B337_23:GA-08","CH_B337_SVRP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:BSD-07","text":"The absence of a total standpoint is not merely an epistemic deficiency but the boundary that makes critical cosmology possible.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-03","CL_B337_23:GA-10","CH_B337_SVCS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned appearance → discovered condition → renewed conditionedness → further regress; each empirical advance regenerates the demand it partially satisfies.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RLE-02","text":"The demand for totality recursively generates dialectic when objectified, then returns as legitimate regulation when critically restricted.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RLE-03","text":"Antinomy → exposure of equivocation → withdrawal of world-totality → reauthorization of regress; conflict is transformed into the mechanism that disciplines reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RLE-04","text":"Empirical givenness forms a branching point: a given whole directs internal analysis, while a given member directs ascent toward unprovided higher conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RLE-05","text":"Every apparent stopping point becomes a provisional node whose critical function is to restart, not conclude, inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RLE-06","text":"The mode of givenness is the central attractor because it decides whether a claim concerns an object, a task, an internal analysis, or an open regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:RLE-07","text":"The unconditioned functions as a negative attractor: it cannot be encountered empirically, yet its demanded totality continuously propels inquiry beyond every attained condition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:FGS-B01","text":"Conditioned appearance → rational demand for conditions → initiation of empirical regress without a given completed series.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:FGS-B02","text":"Transcendental sense of the conditioned + empirical sense of the conditioned → equivocal cosmological inference.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:FGS-B03","text":"Equivocal inference → projection of absolute totality onto appearances → finite–infinite antinomial conflict.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:FGS-B04","text":"Withdrawal of the world-in-itself assumption → conversion of apparent contradiction into jointly invalid dialectical opposition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:FGS-B05","text":"Rational demand for totality + empirical unavailability of the unconditioned → totality converted from object-claim into regulative rule.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:FGS-B06","text":"Regulative regress + differential empirical givenness → distinction between internal analysis of a given whole and ascent from a merely given member.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:FGS4","CH_B337_SVRP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:FGS-B07","text":"Regulative inquiry without empirical closure → indefinite expansion and correction of understanding without a priori object-determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned appearance → demand for further conditions → the initial pressure toward cosmological completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AFM-B02","text":"Demand for completion → conflation of appearances with things in themselves → illicit objectification of the world-series.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AFM-B03","text":"Illicit world-objectification → absolute finite–infinite alternatives → cosmological antinomy.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AFM-B04","text":"Antinomy → examination of the shared object-condition → simultaneous invalidation of opposed conclusions.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AFM-B05","text":"Invalidation of totality as object → totality recoded as regulative procedure → continuation of regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AFM-B06","text":"Continued regress → distinction of given-whole and merely-given-member cases → differentiated modes of inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:AF3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AFM-B07","text":"Differentiated inquiry → suspension of absolute cosmological predicates → methodological cosmology.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:AFM-B08","text":"Methodological cosmology → indefinite extension and rectification of understanding → critical closure without empirical completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:MME-01","text":"The finite–infinite opposition is displaced by the distinction between empirical givenness and objective totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MME-02","text":"Cosmological inquiry is reclassified from investigation of an absolutely completed world-totality into rule-governed progression within possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MME-03","text":"The distinction between constitutive truth and regulative guidance becomes the decisive instrument for sorting legitimate from illegitimate cosmological claims.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MME-04","text":"Empirical boundaries become provisional nodes in inquiry rather than final boundaries of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MME-05","text":"Antinomy becomes an instrument for diagnosing transcendental illusion rather than a failure of reason or a warrant for scepticism.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MME-06","text":"Critical method redraws the terrain of dispute by changing the legitimate function of a question rather than merely adjudicating its inherited answers.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The system rejects the requirement to determine whether the cosmological series is absolutely finite or absolutely infinite.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Antinomy is produced by the misapplication of a rule of empirical regress as a constitutive principle of appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — Critical resolution proceeds by contesting the shared object-condition of antagonistic claims rather than deciding between their conclusions.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Appearances lack a completed totality independent of the successive and iterative process of empirical regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The system performs its own restriction by converting dialectical overreach into a doctrinal rule for the extension and correction of understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:FL-01","text":"The claim that a whole may be empirically given while its internal regress proceeds without end produces pressure between finite intuition and inexhaustible decomposition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:FL-02","text":"The principle is denied constitutive authority while being described as practically equivalent in influence to an a priori axiom.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:ALT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:FL-03","text":"The system directs cognition toward systematic completion while denying that the completion toward which inquiry is directed can ever be attained.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:FL-04","text":"The rule requiring inquiry to determine how far regress must proceed lacks a fully specified operational criterion for particular empirical cases.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MP-01","text":"“Conditioned” mutates at the decisive fault line where a transcendental sense in one premise is substituted for an empirical sense in another.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MP-02","text":"Totality mutates from a constitutive claim concerning objects into a regulative rule governing regression.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MP-03","text":"Dialectical reason mutates into doctrinal reason when its subjective regulative meaning is established as its only legitimate employment.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MP-04","text":"Infinity mutates from a presumed property of a world-object into a permissible mode of empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:HSI-01","text":"Reality is figured as an empirically constituted series rather than a completed world-object standing outside the procedures that disclose it.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes a finite investigator compelled to pass from each discovered condition toward further conditions without assuming possession of the whole.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:HSI-03","text":"Time functions as the medium that prevents empirical completion because synthesis is necessarily successive.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:HSI-04","text":"Struggle is represented as critical reclassification: conflict is navigated by exposing the illicit object-condition that makes opposed claims appear compulsory.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:HSI-05","text":"The available form of orientation is indefinite correction and expansion, not exit into a final metaphysical standpoint.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_23:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the central conversion of cosmological totality from constitutive doctrine into regulative procedure.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where it separates the rule governing regress from an object allegedly given independently of regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined concerning the criteria by which an investigator identifies a genuinely given whole without importing prior totalization.","refs":["CH_B337_SVRP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent only if epistemic efficacy remains distinct from ontological determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the system’s movement only if antinomial refutation is retained as transformation of a shared presupposition rather than selection between opposed answers.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_23:MRT-B06","text":"The claim of practically axiom-like regulative influence remains resistant to complete reduction because it must sustain methodological force while refusing constitutive objecthood.","refs":["CH_B337_SIEU:ALT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 67–69","context":"Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem · Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas · Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas"}},{"id":"CL_B337_24","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 70–72","order":117,"parents":["CH_B337_ISCI","CH_B337_IS_2","CH_B337_IS_3"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"c9630bbfb313327b491bbf69cea197f11f26a7e93a1899a70cbfa695dc877946","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:AS-01","text":"The cluster addresses whether cosmological reason can obtain an unconditioned totality through world-composition, division of a given whole, or causal determination of an action.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:AS1","CH_B337_IS_2:AS1","CH_B337_IS_3:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AS-02","text":"It reframes each alleged totality as a regress through conditioned appearances rather than as a completed object antecedently available to cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:AS2","CH_B337_IS_2:AS2","CH_B337_IS_3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AS-03","text":"No empirical series supplies its own completion, because neither the magnitude, divisional series, nor causal totality is given before the synthesis that seeks it.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:AS5","CH_B337_IS_2:A3","CH_B337_IS_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AS-04","text":"Mathematical infinity divides into indefinite composition and inexhaustible decomposition, but neither form licenses an actually completed infinite aggregate.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A9","CH_B337_IS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AS-05","text":"The system therefore replaces a uniform solution to antinomy with a classification by synthesis-type: homogeneous mathematical series exclude phenomenal completion, whereas dynamical series can admit heterogeneous conditioning.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:AS5","CH_B337_IS_2:A12","CH_B337_IS_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AS-06","text":"The dynamical branch relocates the unconditioned outside empirical membership and distributes one subject’s causality across empirical and intelligible characters.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A11","CH_B337_IS_3:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AS-07","text":"This relocation preserves uninterrupted empirical determination while establishing only the thinkability and non-contradiction, not the actuality, of intelligible freedom.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:NX8","CH_B337_IS_3:A17","CH_B337_IS_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AS-08","text":"The movement is recursively critical: reason’s demand for completion produces antinomy, analysis differentiates the regress involved, and closure becomes regulative continuation or delimited non-opposition.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:AS6","CH_B337_IS_2:SD2","CH_B337_IS_3:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Phenomena form the only field of empirical cognition, but that field is neither given as a completed world-totality nor metaphysically exhaustive of its nonphenomenal ground.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A1","CH_B337_IS_3:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Cosmological totality has empirical significance only through the regress that composes, decomposes, or causally conditions appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A2","CH_B337_IS_2:AS2","CH_B337_IS_3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Every member reached within an empirical series remains conditioned, so no phenomenal member can become an absolutely first or unconditioned terminus.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A3","CH_B337_IS_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The non-termination of an operation does not establish a completed infinite object, because such an inference anticipates members not determined through synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A7","CH_B337_IS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] The admissible form of cosmological resolution depends on the synthesis-type generating the series rather than on a universal opposition between thesis and antithesis.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:SD2","CH_B337_IS_2:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-06","text":"[Terminal] A homogeneous mathematical series cannot contain a completed phenomenal unconditioned, because anything admitted within it remains another conditioned member.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A10","CH_B337_IS_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Composition and division instantiate different forms of non-completion: composition proceeds indefinitely toward unencountered conditions, while division is inexhaustible within a co-given continuum.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A9","CH_B337_IS_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Categories such as substance and causality determine appearances without disclosing absolute subjects, substrates, or metaphysically exhaustive causes.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A6","CH_B337_IS_3:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] A dynamical series can admit a heterogeneous intelligible condition only if that condition remains outside empirical membership and introduces no competing empirical cause.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A11","CH_B337_IS_3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] One subject and one action can receive complete empirical determination and intelligible attribution under distinct relations without dividing the effect into causal shares.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A13","CH_B337_IS_3:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] The intelligible remains thinkable but uncognizable, and critical proof terminates at non-contradiction rather than establishing actuality or mechanism.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A17","CH_B337_IS_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Reason’s demand for the unconditioned cannot be fulfilled within experience but continues to organize empirical inquiry, synthesis-classification, and the search for compatible intelligible grounding.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A5","CH_B337_ISCI:SD3","CH_B337_IS_3:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:RMD-01","text":"Totality retains its identity as demanded completion but migrates from global magnitude, through the complete analysis of parts, to the absolute condition of a causal series.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:AS1","CH_B337_IS_2:AS1","CH_B337_IS_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RMD-02","text":"Regress recurs functionally across all three domains, but its operation mutates from outward empirical extension to inward decomposition and then to temporal causal conditioning.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A9","CH_B337_IS_2:AS2","CH_B337_IS_3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RMD-03","text":"Infinity reverses from a supposed property of a completed object into procedural non-completion, expressed either as regulative indefiniteness or inexhaustible decomposition.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:SD2","CH_B337_IS_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RMD-04","text":"The absolute boundary is displaced from a temporal or spatial edge to the impossible first phenomenal cause, preserving the exclusion of any empirical terminus that could end conditioning.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A4","CH_B337_IS_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RMD-05","text":"The unconditioned changes valence: it is impossible as a completed member or totality of a mathematical series but conditionally admissible as a non-sensuous ground of a dynamical series.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:SNX1","CH_B337_IS_2:A12","CH_B337_IS_2:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RMD-06","text":"The whole expands from the world as unavailable total intuition, through a whole given for division, to one action jointly indexed to natural and intelligible relations.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A1","CH_B337_IS_2:A3","CH_B337_IS_3:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RMD-07","text":"Substance loses its function as an absolute substrate and is absorbed into the broader distinction between phenomenal character and intelligible grounding.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A6","CH_B337_IS_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RMD-08","text":"Reason’s demand persists unchanged, but its product mutates from compulsory continuation to synthesis-classification and finally to the idea of spontaneous causality.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:SD3","CH_B337_IS_3:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:CGD-01","text":"The impossibility of experiencing phenomena bounded by empty time or space remains local machinery for excluding an absolute cosmical edge.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:CGD-02","text":"The continuum–discretum distinction and the rejection of infinitely pre-articulated organisms remain local controls on claims about bodily division.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:AS4","CH_B337_IS_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:CGD-03","text":"The practical ought and moral imputation remain local applications of the dynamical solution rather than universal premises of mathematical cosmology.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:CGD-04","text":"The distinction between a given object and the completed synthesis of its conditions scales upward into the cluster’s general prohibition on antecedently completed totality.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:SD1","CH_B337_ISCI:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:CGD-05","text":"The mathematical–dynamical distinction bridges the units by explaining why composition and division end in procedural openness while causality permits qualified intelligible closure.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:SD2","CH_B337_IS_2:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:CGD-06","text":"The appearance–thing-in-itself distinction catalyzes the major transition from merely blocking totalization to constructing compatibility between empirical closure and nonphenomenal grounding.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:NX11","CH_B337_IS_3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:CGD-07","text":"The exclusion of cosmical beginnings and limits is recoded at cluster scale as the more general denial that any temporal or spatial phenomenon can supply an absolutely first condition.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:D4","CH_B337_IS_3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:GNX-01","text":"The system rejects both the world as an intuitively completed object and the constitutive classification of that object as finite or actually infinite.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:NX1","CH_B337_ISCI:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GNX-02","text":"It rejects the conversion of inexhaustible division into an infinite aggregate already contained as a completed inventory within a whole.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:NX2","CH_B337_IS_2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GNX-03","text":"It rejects every phenomenal condition proposed as an absolutely unconditioned terminus, including any temporally first empirical cause.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:DNX3","CH_B337_IS_3:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GNX-04","text":"It rejects the metaphysical use of empirical categories to infer an absolute substance or to equate empirical causal completeness with metaphysical completeness.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:DNX2","CH_B337_IS_3:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GNX-05","text":"It rejects a universal antinomy-resolution procedure because different forms of synthesis impose different conditions on admissible closure.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:SNX2","CH_B337_IS_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GNX-06","text":"It rejects any intelligible condition construed as an interruption, additional empirical cause, or member of the phenomenal series.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:NX8","CH_B337_IS_3:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:GNX-07","text":"It rejects the conversion of compatibility into existential proof: removal of contradiction does not establish actual freedom or a positive metaphysical settlement.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:NX12","CH_B337_IS_3:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires that appearances not be things in themselves; otherwise natural causality exhausts being and every intelligible solution collapses.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:NX11","CH_B337_IS_3:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:DBN-02","text":"It requires empirical completeness not to entail metaphysical self-grounding, because phenomenal reality cannot both remain conditioned and provide its own final ground.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:DNX1","CH_B337_IS_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:DBN-03","text":"It requires temporal succession not to govern things in themselves, since intelligible causality would otherwise become another temporally conditioned event.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:DNX2","CH_B337_IS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:DBN-04","text":"It requires that no final empirical synthesis satisfy reason’s demand by converting an operationally generated series into a completed unconditioned object.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:SNX2","CH_B337_IS_2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:DBN-05","text":"It requires distinct causal descriptions of one action not to be competing causes within the same explanatory order.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:DNX4","CH_B337_IS_3:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:DBN-06","text":"It requires normative necessity not to reduce to natural description, sensuous impulse, or empirical regularity.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:DNX5","CH_B337_IS_3:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:BSA-01","text":"The absent standpoint organizing the cluster is an intuition outside regress from which the world, its complete articulation, and its total causal order could be surveyed together.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:DNX1","CH_B337_ISCI:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable object is an empirical unconditioned capable of satisfying reason while remaining a legitimate member or completion of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:SNX2","CH_B337_IS_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:BSA-03","text":"The positive nature of intelligible grounding remains absent even where direct theoretical access to such a ground is structurally permitted.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:SNX1","CH_B337_IS_2:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:BSA-04","text":"The deepest unformalized absence is the mediation by which an atemporal intelligible ground would determine a particular temporal series already sufficient under empirical causality.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:SNX5","CH_B337_IS_3:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:XD-01","text":"Cosmological completion cannot be transferred from the givenness of an object to the completed synthesis of its magnitude or parts; completion belongs to the operation and remains unavailable as a pre-given totality.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-02","CL_B337_24:GA-04","CL_B337_24:GA-07","CH_B337_ISCI:SD1","CH_B337_IS_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:XD-02","text":"The location of the sought condition determines the outcome of antinomy: an unconditioned inside a homogeneous phenomenal series is impossible, whereas a heterogeneous condition outside that series is conditionally thinkable.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-05","CL_B337_24:GA-06","CL_B337_24:GA-09","CH_B337_IS_2:A10","CH_B337_IS_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:XD-03","text":"The structural openness of phenomenal analysis and the closure of empirical causality jointly show that empirical inexhaustibility and empirical completeness are compatible when applied to different questions, neither yielding metaphysical completion.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-03","CL_B337_24:GA-08","CL_B337_24:GA-09","CH_B337_IS_2:SD3","CH_B337_IS_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:XD-04","text":"Indefinite composition and infinite division are distinct procedures but share one prohibition: operational non-termination cannot be converted into an actually infinite inventory.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-04","CL_B337_24:GA-07","CH_B337_ISCI:A9","CH_B337_IS_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:XD-05","text":"The restriction of substance to phenomenal intuition and the dual-character account of agency are successive effects of one critical operation: empirical permanence does not exhaust possible being.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-08","CL_B337_24:GA-10","CH_B337_IS_2:A6","CH_B337_IS_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:XD-06","text":"Indefinitely extensible empirical investigation and complete natural explanation leave practical imputation thinkable because neither observation nor causal prediction can determine the intelligible contribution.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-03","CL_B337_24:GA-10","CL_B337_24:GA-11","CH_B337_IS_2:D5","CH_B337_IS_3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:XD-07","text":"Reason’s failure to obtain constitutive closure becomes productive: it generates regulative continuation in mathematical series and a negative proof of compatibility in the dynamical series.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-11","CL_B337_24:GA-12","CH_B337_ISCI:SD3","CH_B337_IS_3:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:BSD-01","text":"The cluster produces a relation-indexed ontology in which phenomenal and intelligible determinations are not two interacting empirical realities but distinct conditions under which one subject or effect is considered.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-08","CL_B337_24:GA-09","CL_B337_24:GA-10","CH_B337_IS_3:NX5","CH_B337_IS_2:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:BSD-02","text":"Time becomes an exclusively phenomenal ordering without an absolute beginning, while intelligible origination is relocated from a first event to the atemporal ground of a temporal series.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-03","CL_B337_24:GA-09","CL_B337_24:GA-10","CH_B337_IS_3:SNX3","CH_B337_IS_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:BSD-03","text":"Maximum empirical determinability can coexist with maximum metaphysical underdetermination: prediction secures natural necessity but cannot disclose or disprove intelligible character.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-08","CL_B337_24:GA-10","CL_B337_24:GA-11","CH_B337_IS_3:D9","CH_B337_IS_3:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:BSD-04","text":"Cosmological method must diagnose the synthesis generating a conflict before adjudicating its opposed claims, and its legitimate proof-target may be only the removal of contradiction.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-05","CL_B337_24:GA-11","CH_B337_IS_2:ALT3","CH_B337_IS_3:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:BSD-05","text":"Practical imputation no longer competes with physical explanation, yet the system cannot calculate how much of any conduct belongs to freedom rather than natural constitution and circumstance.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-10","CL_B337_24:GA-11","CH_B337_IS_3:SD5","CH_B337_IS_3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:BSD-06","text":"Closure becomes jurisdictional rather than total: experience retains constitutive natural validity, reason retains regulative or practical demands, and neither domain supplies a universal form of satisfaction.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-05","CL_B337_24:GA-08","CL_B337_24:GA-12","CH_B337_IS_3:SD7","CH_B337_IS_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:RLE-01","text":"The principal loop runs from a conditioned member to reason’s demand for totality, from that demand to illicit completion, from completion to antinomy, and from critical restriction back to continued conditioning.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A3","CH_B337_IS_3:A5","CH_B337_ISCI:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RLE-02","text":"The compositional path converts unavailable world-totality into possible empirical regress and terminates in indefinite continuation as the sole legitimate cosmological determination.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:FGS1","CH_B337_ISCI:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RLE-03","text":"The divisional path converts a given whole into a regress through conditioned parts and then into inexhaustible decomposition without an actual infinite aggregate.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:FGS1","CH_B337_IS_2:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RLE-04","text":"The dynamical escalation runs from causal regress to spontaneous causality, from false homogenization to dual character, and from dual character to a proof confined to non-opposition.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:FGS2","CH_B337_IS_3:FGS4","CH_B337_IS_3:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RLE-05","text":"The unconditioned is the cluster’s attractor: excluded as mathematical completion, it reappears as a conditionally admissible intelligible ground and finally as the atemporal grounding of freedom.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:ME5","CH_B337_IS_3:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:RLE-06","text":"Regress becomes a conceptual machine whose repeated appearance marks phase change: from regulative indefiniteness, to rule-governed divisional non-termination, to causal pressure for spontaneity.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:ME2","CH_B337_IS_2:ME1","CH_B337_IS_3:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:FGS-B01","text":"Projected totality of world, parts, or causes → demand for an unconditioned completion.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:AS1","CH_B337_IS_2:AS1","CH_B337_IS_3:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:FGS-B02","text":"Restriction to appearances → no given world-totality, no absolute phenomenal substance, and no phenomenal thing in itself.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A1","CH_B337_IS_2:A6","CH_B337_IS_3:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:FGS-B03","text":"Conditioned phenomenal field → differentiation of outward indefinite regress, inward infinite division, and temporal causal regress.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A9","CH_B337_IS_2:NX1","CH_B337_IS_3:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:FGS-B04","text":"Homogeneous mathematical synthesis → every condition remains phenomenal → completed unconditioned totality is impossible.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:AS5","CH_B337_IS_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:FGS-B05","text":"Heterogeneous dynamical synthesis → intelligible condition outside empirical membership → empirical series remains uninterrupted.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A11","CH_B337_IS_3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:FGS-B06","text":"Dual character plus practical obligation → reason represented as causally efficacious → imputation without empirical exemption.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A16","CH_B337_IS_3:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:FGS-B07","text":"Epistemic restriction → intelligible causality remains thinkable but uncognizable → terminal proof of non-incompatibility rather than existence.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A17","CH_B337_IS_3:A18","CH_B337_IS_3:MRT10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:AFM-B01","text":"Demand for completed cosmological totality → confrontation with conditioned magnitude, parts, and causes.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:AS1","CH_B337_IS_2:AS1","CH_B337_IS_3:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AFM-B02","text":"Unavailable world-intuition → displacement of global magnitude into possible empirical regress.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AFM-B03","text":"Composition through successive conditions → exclusion of absolute limits → regressus in indefinitum.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A8","CH_B337_ISCI:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AFM-B04","text":"Given continuous whole → divisional regress → inexhaustible parts without a completed infinite aggregate.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:AS2","CH_B337_IS_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AFM-B05","text":"Comparison of regress-types → mathematical–dynamical classification → redistribution of the unconditioned beyond phenomenal membership.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:ME4","CH_B337_IS_2:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AFM-B06","text":"Causal regress → false homogenization diagnosed → empirical and intelligible characters applied to one action.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:AS3","CH_B337_IS_3:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:AFM-B07","text":"Complete natural determination → practical imputation → epistemic restriction → compatibility without existential proof.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:SD7","CH_B337_IS_3:MRT10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:MME-01","text":"The world is reclassified from a globally measurable object into the horizon and task generated by conditioned empirical synthesis.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:ME1","CH_B337_ISCI:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MME-02","text":"The finite–infinite opposition is displaced by distinctions among actual infinity, regulative indefiniteness, and rule-governed divisional non-termination.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:ME2","CH_B337_IS_2:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MME-03","text":"Givenness of a whole is separated from completed determination of its parts, blocking the inference from intuition to an infinitely pre-articulated inventory.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:SD1","CH_B337_IS_2:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MME-04","text":"Antinomies are reorganized by synthesis-type, replacing direct thesis–antithesis adjudication with diagnosis of homogeneous and heterogeneous conditioning.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:ME4","CH_B337_IS_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MME-05","text":"Empirical causal closure is separated from metaphysical exhaustion, permitting complete natural explanation without treating appearance as the whole of being.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:ME3","CH_B337_IS_3:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MME-06","text":"Freedom is reclassified from a temporal exception to nature into an atemporal condition under which the same naturally determined action remains imputable.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:ME1","CH_B337_IS_3:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MME-07","text":"The standard of successful proof shifts from positive knowledge of the unconditioned to the regulated continuation of inquiry or the removal of a contradiction blocking thinkability.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:ME7","CH_B337_IS_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the cluster treats the world’s beginning and extent, the divisibility of spaces and bodies, and the coexistence of natural necessity with freedom.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:ALT1","CH_B337_IS_2:ALT1","CH_B337_IS_3:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, each problem is governed by the relation among conditioned members, the regress determining them, and the distinction between the series and its alleged completion.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:ALT2","CH_B337_IS_2:ALT2","CH_B337_IS_3:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, reason is converted from a source of dogmatic totalities into a regulator that diagnoses synthesis-types and separates empirical explanation from practical imputation.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:ALT3","CH_B337_IS_2:ALT3","CH_B337_IS_3:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, space, time, succession, and causal necessity govern appearances, while no completed infinity or intelligible ground becomes an object within that order.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:ALT4","CH_B337_IS_2:ALT4","CH_B337_IS_3:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the cluster repeatedly stages reason’s overreach, introduces a critical distinction, and narrows the result from constitutive knowledge to regulation or non-opposition.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:AS6","CH_B337_IS_3:AS7","CH_B337_IS_3:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:FL-01","text":"The denial of constitutive knowledge of the world-totality sits tensely beside negative formulations asserting that the world has no absolute beginning or limit.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:MRT5","CH_B337_ISCI:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:FL-02","text":"Divisional conditions are said to be co-given with the whole although the completed divisional series is not co-given, leaving the exact status of this incomplete co-givenness unstable.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A3","CH_B337_IS_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:FL-03","text":"The intelligible ground is required to avoid causal competition, yet the mediation by which it determines an empirically sufficient temporal series remains unformalized.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:SNX5","CH_B337_IS_3:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:FL-04","text":"Full empirical explanation and full imputation are jointly asserted even though no criterion can determine the free contribution to particular conduct.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:SD5","CH_B337_IS_3:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:FL-05","text":"The asymmetry between false mathematical oppositions and reconcilable dynamical oppositions depends on preserving the disputed distinction between homogeneous and heterogeneous conditioning.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:MRT6","CH_B337_IS_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:FL-06","text":"The transmitted description of transcendental idealism as an “illusion” conflicts with the idealist distinction required by the causal solution and remains textually unresolved.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MP-01","text":"Infinity mutates from indefinite outward continuation to infinite inward decomposition, requiring procedural unity without identity of regress-form.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A9","CH_B337_IS_2:NX1","CH_B337_IS_2:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MP-02","text":"The unconditioned mutates from an impossible mathematical object into a conditionally admissible intelligible ground, marking the decisive transition between antinomy-types.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:SNX1","CH_B337_IS_2:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MP-03","text":"The subject mutates from phenomenal substance denied absolute status into a dual-character bearer of empirical necessity and intelligible attribution.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:A6","CH_B337_IS_3:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as nested fields of conditioned phenomenal series whose empirical operation is autonomous, while any admissible intelligible ground remains outside membership in those series.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:A2","CH_B337_IS_2:A11","CH_B337_IS_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes a phenomenal node completely determined through empirical character and simultaneously thinkable as the intelligible ground of the same effects.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:A13","CH_B337_IS_3:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:HSI-03","text":"Time becomes an indefinitely extensible phenomenal horizon without an absolute beginning, while the intelligible is conceived as neither temporal nor changing.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:NX3","CH_B337_IS_3:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:HSI-04","text":"Navigation consists neither in escaping empirical law nor reaching a final totality, but in continuing inquiry, preserving practical imputation, and refusing to convert thinkability into knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:SD3","CH_B337_IS_3:SD5","CH_B337_IS_3:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_24:MRT-B01","text":"The axiom set is sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s three indispensable movements: totality becomes regress, regress differentiates by synthesis-type, and dynamical heterogeneity permits compatibility without positive cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:MRT1","CH_B337_IS_2:MRT1","CH_B337_IS_3:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MRT-B02","text":"The positive content of intelligible conditioning, its production of particular actions, and the exact relation between a given continuum and its incompletely determined parts remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_2:MRT5","CH_B337_IS_3:MRT2","CH_B337_IS_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if cosmological negations remain regulative, synthesis-types remain distinct, and time is restricted to appearances.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:MRT4","CH_B337_IS_2:MRT6","CH_B337_IS_3:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where it blocks illicit actual infinity, separates repeatable operation from completed inventory, and preserves exceptionless empirical causality without metaphysical exhaustion.","refs":["CH_B337_ISCI:SD2","CH_B337_IS_2:MRT4","CH_B337_IS_3:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_24:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining negative closure and recursive mutation; the transition from obligation to rational causality, the intelligible mediation, and the unresolved textual transmission resist full axiomatic reduction.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_3:MRT7","CH_B337_IS_3:SNX5","CH_B337_ISCI:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 70–72","context":"I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe · II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition · III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes"}},{"id":"CL_B337_25","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 73–75","order":118,"parents":["CH_B337_IS_4","CH_B337_SI_3","CH_B337_SI_4"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"b2bf6fcd3598b62371baa2dc2eeb30af5c37ac006f5d4358d4690229e4e4d127","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:AS-B01","text":"Reason confronts a demand for unconditioned completeness that exceeds empirical objectivity yet cannot simply be discarded as meaningless.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:AS6","CH_B337_SI_3:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AS-B02","text":"Phenomenal contingency entails an indefinitely continued search for empirical conditions, not the empirical cancellation of a possible intelligible condition.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A3","CH_B337_IS_4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AS-B03","text":"The unconditioned is progressively recoded as an ideal: an individually determined principle that directs judgment without appearing as an empirical object.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:AS4","CH_B337_SI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AS-B04","text":"Complete determination requires a totality of affirmative reality from which finite limitations can be derived, thereby generating the transcendental ideal.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A4","CH_B337_SI_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AS-B05","text":"The same ideal that regulates possible determination becomes dialectically unstable when its regulative unity is converted into an objectively existing individual.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:AS4","CH_B337_SI_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AS-B06","text":"Critical restriction preserves the thinkability and practical force of ideals while withholding determinate cognition, empirical proof, and theological constitution.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A9","CH_B337_SI_4:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AS-B07","text":"The movement is recursive rather than linear: empirical incompleteness produces rational totalization, totalization produces ideal individuation, and individuation produces the risk of hypostatization requiring renewed critique.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:AS6","CH_B337_SI_4:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AS-B08","text":"The cluster closes by retaining the unconditioned as a necessary but unoccupiable limit of reason rather than as a knowable object or a completed empirical totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:SNX1","CH_B337_IS_4:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition is gated by sensibility; what lacks conditions of possible presentation cannot be known as an empirical object.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Phenomenal existence remains contingent and indefinitely conditioned, so empirical reason cannot legitimately terminate its regress in a final empirical ground.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A2","CH_B337_IS_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] A dynamical condition may be thinkable outside the empirical series, but its non-series status simultaneously prevents determinate cognition of it.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A5","CH_B337_IS_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] Complete determination requires a total horizon of possible predicates rather than mere formal consistency among concepts.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A3","CH_B337_SI_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Affirmation is structurally primary and negation derivative, because determinate limitation is intelligible only against an antecedent field of reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A6","CH_B337_SI_4:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Reason individualizes the horizon of complete determination as an ideal whose determinacy makes it regulative without making it empirically real.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A7","CH_B337_SI_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Practical, critical, and regulative validity do not entail objective existence or constitutive knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:D4","CH_B337_SI_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Every rational ideal bears a constitutive danger: distributive conditions governing possible cognition can be collectivized and hypostatized as an existent supreme being.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A14","CH_B337_SI_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:RMD-01","text":"The unconditioned retains its nonphenomenal status but mutates from a possible intelligible condition of the world-series into a totality represented as an ideal individual.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A7","CH_B337_SI_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:RMD-02","text":"Regulativity recurs with altered function: it first sustains endless empirical inquiry, then governs comparison and reform, and finally conditions the determination of possible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A8","CH_B337_SI_3:A12","CH_B337_SI_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:RMD-03","text":"Complete determination shifts from the ideal’s exclusion of incompatible predicates to the material presupposition of a total field containing every possible determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A9","CH_B337_SI_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:RMD-04","text":"Negation reverses function from an apophatic restriction on what can be known to a derivative limitation through which finite determinacy becomes intelligible.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:NX5","CH_B337_SI_4:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:RMD-05","text":"The necessary or supreme being is displaced from a merely possible intelligible ground into a genealogically diagnosed personification of reason’s demand for totality.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A10","CH_B337_SI_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:RMD-06","text":"Experience mutates from the material of empirical cognition into the field of deficient copies and finally into the restricted domain whose unity is falsely universalized into theology.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A3","CH_B337_SI_3:SD5","CH_B337_SI_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:CGD-01","text":"The distinction between mathematical and dynamical regress remains local machinery, but its scalable function is to establish that rational conditioning need not share the ontological form of empirical conditioning.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A4","CH_B337_IS_4:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:CGD-02","text":"The analysis of moral ideals remains locally practical, while its current-level contribution is the separation of critical force from empirical realization.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A10","CH_B337_SI_3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:CGD-03","text":"The distinction between objective reality and regulative validity scales upward because it governs intelligible grounds, human ideals, and the transcendental ideal alike.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A13","CH_B337_SI_4:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:CGD-04","text":"The individualization of an idea becomes a bridge proposition: it converts an abstract demand for totality into a determinate ideal capable of regulating comparison and possible-object determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:AS4","CH_B337_SI_4:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:CGD-05","text":"Transcendental subreption is the major transition mechanism because it explains how a legitimate condition of cognition becomes an illegitimate claim about things in general.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A14","CH_B337_SI_4:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:CGD-06","text":"Positive divine predicates are superseded as knowledge claims and recoded as internally derivable features of an ideal whose objective application remains unavailable.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:D5","CH_B337_SI_4:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:GNX-01","text":"The system rejects every attempt to locate an unconditioned ground as the highest member or terminal cause within phenomenal existence.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:NX1","CH_B337_IS_4:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:GNX-02","text":"The system rejects the identification of nonempirical thinkability with either objective reality or ontological existence.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:NX6","CH_B337_SI_4:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:GNX-03","text":"The system rejects the reduction of complete determination to formal contradiction, because material possibility requires a horizon no finite intuition can present.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:NX1","CH_B337_SI_4:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:GNX-04","text":"The system rejects empirical exemplars, imaginative composites, and phenomenal embodiments as adequate bearers of rational perfection.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:NX3","CH_B337_SI_3:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:DBN-01","text":"No empirical series contains the sufficient ground of its own totality, because every phenomenal condition remains itself conditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:DNX1","CH_B337_IS_4:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:DBN-02","text":"No finite experience contains its own criterion of complete determination, perfection, or systematic closure.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:DNX6","CH_B337_SI_4:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:DBN-03","text":"No relation between intelligible ground and appearance may be represented as an internal empirical causal relation without collapsing the critical distinction of domains.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:DNX5","CH_B337_IS_4:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:DBN-04","text":"No theological object may be constituted by converting a condition of possible experience or a regulative ideal into a collectively existing being.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:NX8","CH_B337_SI_4:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:BSA-01","text":"The system excludes a fully knowable absolute that could both ground finite appearances and function as an empirical explanation of them.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:SNX1","CH_B337_IS_4:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:BSA-02","text":"The system excludes the coincidence of complete determination, normative perfection, and empirical presence.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:DNX7","CH_B337_SI_3:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:BSA-03","text":"The system is organized around the absence of an intuition adequate to total reality, an absence that reason obscures when it personifies its own ideal.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:SNX3","CH_B337_SI_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:XD-01","text":"A nonempirical condition can be indispensable to rational orientation without becoming an object of empirical cognition or a proof of existence.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-03","CL_B337_25:GA-07","CH_B337_IS_4:A5","CH_B337_SI_3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:XD-02","text":"Infinite empirical regress and complete ideal determination perform complementary functions: the first prohibits empirical closure, while the second supplies a nonempirical horizon for judging incompleteness.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-02","CL_B337_25:GA-04","CL_B337_25:GA-06","CH_B337_IS_4:A8","CH_B337_SI_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:XD-03","text":"Individualization is the productive danger-point of reason: it gives ideals their determinate regulative force while making totality appear eligible for personification.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-06","CL_B337_25:GA-08","CH_B337_SI_3:A7","CH_B337_SI_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:XD-04","text":"Finite determinacy is redescribed as limitation relative to affirmative totality, while empirical imperfection is redescribed as deviation relative to an ideal standard.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-05","CL_B337_25:GA-06","CH_B337_SI_4:A10","CH_B337_SI_3:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:XD-05","text":"The critical task shifts from merely permitting an intelligible ground to diagnosing why reason turns its necessary representation of totality into theological object-knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-03","CL_B337_25:GA-08","CH_B337_IS_4:AS5","CH_B337_SI_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:BSD-01","text":"Rational necessity is reclassified as structural indispensability rather than as evidence that an unconditioned object exists.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:SNX1","CH_B337_SI_3:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:BSD-02","text":"Metaphysical theology is generated as a recurrent rational illusion because the cognitive demand for complete unity cannot cease merely because its objective use is prohibited.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:SD3","CH_B337_SI_4:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:BSD-03","text":"Finitude becomes doubly relational: empirical objects are conditioned within an open series, and determinate predicates are limitations relative to an ideal horizon of reality.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A2","CH_B337_SI_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:BSD-04","text":"Critique, practical judgment, and transcendental dialectic share one architecture: nonempirical ideals govern assessment precisely because they cannot be occupied as empirical objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:D5","CH_B337_SI_4:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:BSD-05","text":"The system establishes an asymmetrical modal order in which empirical contingency cannot refute intelligible possibility, while intelligible possibility cannot establish existence.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:MRT5","CH_B337_IS_4:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:RLE-01","text":"Empirical conditioning produces a demand for the unconditioned; the demand generates an ideal; the ideal risks objectification; critique then returns the ideal to regulative use without extinguishing the demand.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:FGS1","CH_B337_SI_4:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:RLE-02","text":"Empirical inadequacy generates comparison with an ideal, and comparison continuously reproduces the inadequacy that makes critical reform necessary.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A12","CH_B337_SI_3:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:RLE-03","text":"Affirmation generates limitation, limitation generates determinate finitude, and finitude reactivates the demand for the totality relative to which it is limited.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:D2","CH_B337_SI_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:RLE-04","text":"The conceptual attractor is totality: totality of conditions, totality of perfection, and totality of reality converge as distinct forms of reason’s drive toward complete determination.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:SNX2","CH_B337_SI_3:A8","CH_B337_SI_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:RLE-05","text":"The decisive phase change occurs when a condition of thought becomes represented as an individual being, since the transition from idealization to hypostatization produces the theological illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A8","CH_B337_SI_4:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:FGS-B01","text":"Sensibility conditions objectivity, while phenomenal contingency prevents experience from presenting an unconditioned completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A1","CH_B337_IS_4:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:FGS-B02","text":"The failure of empirical closure generates a rational demand for conditions that may be thought beyond, but never inserted into, the empirical series.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A8","CH_B337_IS_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:FGS-B03","text":"The demand for complete determination presupposes a totality of affirmative reality from which negations and finite determinations are derived.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A4","CH_B337_SI_4:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:FGS-B04","text":"Reason individualizes this totality as an ideal that regulates possible determinations, critical comparisons, and practical reform without acquiring objective reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A8","CH_B337_SI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:FGS-B05","text":"Individualized totality is liable to be misrecognized as an existing supreme being when regulative unity is converted into collective ontological unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A15","CH_B337_SI_4:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:AFM-B01","text":"Phenomenal contingency → indefinite empirical regress → prohibition on empirical closure.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A2","CH_B337_IS_4:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AFM-B02","text":"Prohibition on empirical closure → admissibility of a non-series intelligible condition → withholding of determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A5","CH_B337_IS_4:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AFM-B03","text":"Withholding of cognition → transformation of the unconditioned into an idea and ideal → regulative comparison with empirical insufficiency.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A6","CH_B337_SI_3:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AFM-B04","text":"Regulative idealization → demand for complete material determination → totality of affirmative reality.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A3","CH_B337_SI_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AFM-B05","text":"Totality of reality → individual ens realissimum → prototype for finite determinations.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A9","CH_B337_SI_4:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:AFM-B06","text":"Individual ideal → transcendental subreption → critical retention of regulativity and denial of constitutive theology.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:A14","CH_B337_SI_4:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:MME-01","text":"The opposition between empirical contingency and necessary existence is redrawn as a difference between conditioned phenomenal existence and nonempirical intelligible thinkability.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:ME1","CH_B337_IS_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:MME-02","text":"The distinction between ideal and object replaces the crude alternative between empirical existence and arbitrary fiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A13","CH_B337_SI_3:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:MME-03","text":"Negation ceases to designate an equal ontological opposite and becomes a derivative operation of finite limitation within an affirmative horizon.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:ME1","CH_B337_SI_4:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:MME-04","text":"Theology is reclassified from a direct discovery of a supreme object into the dialectical aftermath of reason’s legitimate demand for systematic unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:ME2","CH_B337_SI_4:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The system addresses whether a necessary ground, a rational ideal, and a highest reality can be thought without being given as objects of experience.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:ALT1","CH_B337_SI_3:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — The system distributes cognition across phenomenal conditioning, intelligible limitation, ideal determination, and the total horizon of reality.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:ALT2","CH_B337_SI_4:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — Critique preserves ideals as instruments of comparison and systematic unity while blocking their conversion into constitutive metaphysical objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:ALT3","CH_B337_SI_4:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level — Phenomena occupy an indefinitely conditioned order, whereas the unconditioned is thinkable only as nonphenomenal and not temporally situated within the series.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:ALT4","CH_B337_IS_4:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level — The system repeatedly produces the ideal it must prohibit as an object, thereby performing the dialectic of reason rather than merely describing it.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:SD3","CH_B337_SI_4:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:FL-01","text":"The cluster permits an intelligible necessary ground as thinkable while diagnosing the supreme being as a hypostatized product of reason, leaving the boundary between legitimate intelligible thought and theological illusion unstable.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A10","CH_B337_SI_4:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:FL-02","text":"The ideal must be an individual in order to be completely determined, yet it must lack objective reality in order to retain its critical status.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A7","CH_B337_SI_3:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:FL-03","text":"Total reality must ground finite diversity without becoming a divisible aggregate, but limitation explains difference only by presupposing the affirmative field whose unity remains unpresentable.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:SD2","CH_B337_SI_4:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:MP-01","text":"The condition changes function when it passes from a possible external ground of the phenomenal series to an ideal material condition for complete determination.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A5","CH_B337_SI_4:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:MP-02","text":"Negation changes function when epistemic prohibition is transformed into ontological limitation relative to antecedent affirmation.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:NX5","CH_B337_SI_4:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:MP-03","text":"Regulativity changes valence when the same unity that disciplines empirical and practical judgment becomes the source of transcendental subreption.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:AS5","CH_B337_SI_4:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as an open field of conditioned phenomena whose finite determinacy is intelligible only against a nonempirical horizon of total reality.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:A2","CH_B337_SI_4:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:HSI-02","text":"The subject appears as a finite rational operator that cannot possess totality but necessarily constructs ideals through which it judges its empirical limits.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:A6","CH_B337_SI_3:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:HSI-03","text":"Time appears as indefinite empirical continuation rather than a completed sequence, while absolute necessity is displaced outside temporal phenomenal succession.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:D3","CH_B337_IS_4:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:HSI-04","text":"Struggle consists neither in attaining the ideal nor in abolishing it, but in retaining its regulative force while resisting its conversion into an object of possession.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:D5","CH_B337_SI_4:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_25:MRT-B01","text":"The current-level axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if reconstruction preserves the distinction between empirical regulation, ideal determination, and transcendent objectification.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:MRT3","CH_B337_SI_4:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where it explains why rational ideals are simultaneously necessary for judgment and unavailable as objects of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_3:SD3","CH_B337_SI_4:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:MRT-B03","text":"The principal underdetermination concerns how an intelligible ground can retain the status of ground without entering a representable causal relation to phenomena.","refs":["CH_B337_IS_4:MRT4","CH_B337_IS_4:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_25:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction fails if the sequence from legitimate rational demand to theological hypostatization is flattened into either dogmatic metaphysics or simple skeptical dismissal.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_4:SD3","CH_B337_SI_4:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 73–75","context":"IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences · Section I. Of the Ideal in General · Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale)"}},{"id":"CL_B337_26","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 76–78","order":119,"parents":["CH_B337_SIAE","CH_B337_SIIO","CH_B337_SVIC"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"94521f74e4e227183a25701d36ff84a7409f7ca20743efe865b09e85736a3512","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:AS-01","text":"Speculative theology originates when reason converts its unavoidable demand for an unconditioned explanation into a claim concerning an objectively existing Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:AS1","CH_B337_SVIC:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AS-02","text":"The apparent plurality of speculative proofs forms a closed field whose empirical routes remain governed by the transcendental conception they purport to establish.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:AS6","CH_B337_SIAE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AS-03","text":"The generative ascent proceeds from given existence through a necessary ground to complete reality and then to the unity of a Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:AS3","CH_B337_SIAE:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AS-04","text":"Critical analysis separates reason’s need for explanatory necessity from its entitlement to posit a corresponding object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A3","CH_B337_SIIO:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AS-05","text":"The ontological route collapses because predicate necessity presupposes a posited subject, while existence adds no conceptual determination capable of making that subject necessary.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:AS4","CH_B337_SIIO:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AS-06","text":"Formal conversion exposes the cosmological proof as dependent on the ontological inference and therefore unable to function as an independent empirical demonstration.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:AS4","CH_B337_SVIC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AS-07","text":"The cosmological route additionally fails by extending causality beyond experience, forcing termination of the regress, and treating the removal of conditions as positive necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:AS5","CH_B337_SVIC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AS-08","text":"Speculative failure bifurcates rather than eliminates the God-idea: theoretical reason retains it regulatively, while practical interest may confer non-theoretical assent.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:AS5","CH_B337_SVIC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AS-09","text":"The movement is recursive: reason projects unity, hypostatizes the projection, exposes the resulting subreption, and restores the projected unity as a rule of inquiry rather than an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A18","CH_B337_SVIC:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Conditioned existence activates reason’s demand for an unconditioned ground capable of completing explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A2","CH_B337_SVIC:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] A formal requirement for explanatory completion does not establish the objective existence of anything corresponding to that requirement.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A3","CH_B337_SVIC:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Necessity begins as the alleged terminus of causal ascent but is progressively relocated from an objective property to a subjective principle governing reason’s regress.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:AS2","CH_B337_SVIC:A14","CH_B337_SVIC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] The ens realissimum supplies an ideal of complete conceptual determination, but complete determination does not decide existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A5","CH_B337_SVIC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-05","text":"[Terminal] Existence is not a real predicate, existential claims are synthetic, and no purely conceptual analysis can establish an object’s existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A6","CH_B337_SIIO:A7","CH_B337_SIIO:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Logical consistency establishes neither the real possibility nor the transcendental possibility of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A1","CH_B337_SVIC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] An argument does not remain empirical merely because experience supplies its first datum when pure concepts perform its decisive existential inference.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:DNX5","CH_B337_SVIC:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] The cosmological proof is not independent of the ontological proof but reproduces its illicit concept-to-existence transition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:NX2","CH_B337_SVIC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Existential cognition and objective category-use are bounded by possible experience, so phenomenal causality cannot determine a supersensible ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A9","CH_B337_SVIC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] The Supreme Being persists as an ideal representation of systematic unity after losing its status as a demonstrated existent.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A17","CH_B337_SVIC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] The subjective inevitability of the unconditioned generates a natural and recurrent illusion when the form of inquiry is mistaken for an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:DNX6","CH_B337_SVIC:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Speculative critique establishes non-demonstrability rather than non-existence, denying reason both apodeictic affirmation and apodeictic negation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:SD4","CH_B337_SVIC:SNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:RMD-01","text":"[Functional recurrence] The unconditioned retains its identity as the demanded completion of explanation but changes from an alleged terminating being into a horizon directing indefinite inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A2","CH_B337_SVIC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] Necessity changes from an alleged property of a thing, through conditional necessity within judgement, into a subjective rule of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:AS3","CH_B337_SVIC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RMD-03","text":"[Migration and absorption] The ens realissimum migrates from the preferred conception of the necessary being, into the hidden premise of the cosmological proof, and finally into the regulative ideal of systematic unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A5","CH_B337_SVIC:D5","CH_B337_SVIC:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Empirical existence changes from the apparent ground of an independent proof into a mere trigger for an inference whose decisive work is nonempirical.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A11","CH_B337_SVIC:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RMD-05","text":"[Mutation] Existence changes from something treated as conceptual content into the positing of an object under conditions of possible givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:AS5","CH_B337_SIIO:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RMD-06","text":"[Absorption] The apparent plurality of proofs is absorbed into a dependency hierarchy organized by the ontological conversion of concept into existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:SD3","CH_B337_SVIC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RMD-07","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] Practical supplementation marks the boundary between rational assent and speculative cognition, then drops out of the theoretical resolution, which proceeds through regulative reinterpretation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:D5","CH_B337_SVIC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RMD-08","text":"[Stable identity with altered function] Contradiction remains the criterion of logical impossibility, but its operative role shifts from supporting necessary existence to showing that suppression of the subject produces no contradiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A5","CH_B337_SIIO:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:CGD-01","text":"[Lower-layer local] The exhaustive three-path taxonomy and the announced physico-theological route delimit the field but do not themselves execute the latter proof’s critique within this cluster.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:AS6","CH_B337_SIAE:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:CGD-02","text":"[Lower-layer local] Practical obligation supplies a distinct possible basis for assent, but it neither repairs nor participates in the speculative demonstrations under examination.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A9","CH_B337_SIAE:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:CGD-03","text":"[Scaled upward] The distinction between a concept required by reason and an objectively existing object governs both the ontological and cosmological critiques.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A8","CH_B337_SIIO:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:CGD-04","text":"[Bridge] The invalidation of the ontological inference becomes the indispensable premise for exposing the cosmological proof’s concealed dependency.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A8","CH_B337_SVIC:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:CGD-05","text":"[Transition catalyst] Formal conversion changes a sequence presented as empirical ascent into a concept-to-existence inference and thereby collapses the cosmological proof’s claimed independence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:AS4","CH_B337_SVIC:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:CGD-06","text":"[Recoded] The necessary being ceases to function as a discovered terminus and becomes a regulative demand whose bearer remains objectively indeterminate.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:AS6","CH_B337_SVIC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:CGD-07","text":"[Inherited compression] The uniqueness and complete determination of the ens realissimum remain compressed premises rather than conclusions fully generated within these units.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:MRT5","CH_B337_SVIC:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:GNX-01","text":"A rational need for an unconditioned ground is not the objective reality of such a ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:NX1","CH_B337_SIAE:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GNX-02","text":"No increase in conceptual completeness can generate the existence of the concept’s object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:DNX1","CH_B337_SIAE:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GNX-03","text":"Freedom from contradiction is not a real or transcendental criterion of objective possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:DNX2","CH_B337_SVIC:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GNX-04","text":"Phenomenal causality cannot be extended beyond possible experience, and an unfinished regress cannot establish a first cause.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:NX3","CH_B337_SVIC:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GNX-05","text":"An empirical starting point does not make a proof empirically sufficient when experience identifies neither absolute necessity nor its bearer.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:NX2","CH_B337_SVIC:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GNX-06","text":"The impossibility of speculative proof is not a proof of divine non-existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SD2","CH_B337_SIIO:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:GNX-07","text":"A principle required for systematic inquiry is not thereby a constitutive, self-subsisting object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:NX12","CH_B337_SVIC:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any speculative isomorphism between the structure of reason’s need and the structure of being.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:SNX1","CH_B337_SVIC:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:DBN-02","text":"Explanatory completion must not be identical with ontological completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:DNX1","CH_B337_SVIC:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:DBN-03","text":"Conditional logical necessity within a judgement must not be convertible into absolute necessity of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:ME1","CH_B337_SIIO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:DBN-04","text":"The incompleteness of a regress must not entail the existence of a terminating member.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:DNX2","CH_B337_SVIC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:DBN-05","text":"Systematic unity must remain possible without being produced by a real all-sufficient cause corresponding to reason’s ideal.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:DNX9","CH_B337_SVIC:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:BSA-01","text":"The deepest unavailable element is a legitimate bridge from conditioned existence to an unconditioned existent.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:BSA-02","text":"Absolute necessity lacks the positive conceptual content required for attribution to any determinate being.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SNX2","CH_B337_SVIC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:BSA-03","text":"No theoretical standpoint is available from which being could be known independently of the conditions of its possible givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:SNX1","CH_B337_SVIC:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:BSA-04","text":"The cluster is organized around a necessary ground that reason can neither eliminate from inquiry nor establish as an object of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SNX6","CH_B337_SIAE:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:XD-01","text":"The plurality of speculative proofs collapses into variants of one illicit operation: the conversion of conceptual necessity into objective existence.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-07","CL_B337_26:GA-08","CH_B337_SIAE:AS6","CH_B337_SIIO:SD1","CH_B337_SVIC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:XD-02","text":"Selecting complete reality as the least inadequate conception of necessity cannot determine that complete reality is the bearer of necessary existence.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-03","CL_B337_26:GA-04","CL_B337_26:GA-06","CH_B337_SIAE:D2","CH_B337_SIIO:D5","CH_B337_SVIC:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:XD-03","text":"The empirical datum that something exists cannot convert a subsequent pure conceptual determination into empirical cognition of a necessary being.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-05","CL_B337_26:GA-07","CL_B337_26:GA-08","CL_B337_26:GA-09","CH_B337_SVIC:D1","CH_B337_SVIC:ME1","CH_B337_SIIO:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:XD-04","text":"Once causality is restricted to possible experience, the demand for an unconditioned ground can govern only the continuation of inquiry, not the positing of a first cause.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-01","CL_B337_26:GA-09","CL_B337_26:GA-10","CH_B337_SVIC:A1","CH_B337_SVIC:A10","CH_B337_SVIC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:XD-05","text":"Practical assent and speculative non-demonstrability can coexist only if practical commitment is denied the status of theoretical cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-02","CL_B337_26:GA-12","CH_B337_SIAE:NX6","CH_B337_SIIO:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:XD-06","text":"Because the demand for unity is unavoidable, critical exposure cannot abolish transcendental illusion; it can only interrupt hypostatization and restore regulative use.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-01","CL_B337_26:GA-10","CL_B337_26:GA-11","CH_B337_SVIC:A18","CH_B337_SVIC:SD6","CH_B337_SVIC:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:BSD-01","text":"Speculative theology becomes a diagnosis of reason’s self-objectification: a demand generated by inquiry is misrecognized as evidence of an independently existing object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:SD1","CH_B337_SVIC:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:BSD-02","text":"The Supreme Being acquires irreducibly different statuses as explanatory requirement, maximal concept, hypostatized object, regulative ideal, and possible practical supposition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:ME3","CH_B337_SVIC:ME4","CH_B337_SIAE:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:BSD-03","text":"Inquiry assumes an indefinitely progressive temporality because projected completion directs the regress while no empirical condition may become its final term.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SD5","CH_B337_SVIC:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:BSD-04","text":"The result is an epistemic asymmetry in which supersensible existence is neither demonstrable nor demonstrably impossible.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A12","CH_B337_SVIC:SNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:BSD-05","text":"Critical reason preserves the maximal search for explanation while prohibiting explanatory completeness from becoming ontological closure.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SD7","CH_B337_SVIC:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:BSD-06","text":"Proof-validity is relocated from conceptual adequacy to the conditions under which an object could possess objective reality and be given to cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:ALT3","CH_B337_SVIC:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:RLE-01","text":"The primary loop runs from demand for unity to hypostatized ideal, from hypostatization to critical exposure, and from exposure back to regulative unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SD6","CH_B337_SVIC:A18","CH_B337_SVIC:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RLE-02","text":"The central escalation path moves from empirical existence to necessary ground, from necessary ground to complete reality, and from complete reality to alleged necessary existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:AS3","CH_B337_SVIC:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RLE-03","text":"The dependency loop occurs when the cosmological proof requires an ontological inference that cannot establish existence, thereby returning the empirical proof to the failure it was meant to avoid.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:D6","CH_B337_SVIC:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RLE-04","text":"The ens realissimum functions as the conceptual attractor that absorbs necessity, complete determination, unity, and supremacy before surviving their failed proof as a regulative ideal.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A6","CH_B337_SVIC:A6","CH_B337_SVIC:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RLE-05","text":"Inquiry spirals because it must proceed as if a complete ground were available while refusing every empirical stopping point as unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:D9","CH_B337_SVIC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:RLE-06","text":"Speculative failure produces a bifurcation rather than a terminus: regulative employment preserves theoretical inquiry, while practical postulation preserves a distinct mode of assent.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:SD5","CH_B337_SVIC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:FGS-B01","text":"Conditioned or empirically given existence activates the demand for a sufficient and unconditioned ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:AS2","CH_B337_SVIC:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:FGS-B02","text":"The demand for completion generates the provisional posit of an absolutely necessary being.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A2","CH_B337_SVIC:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:FGS-B03","text":"Conceptual scarcity selects complete reality as the candidate for necessity and adds unity and supremacy to produce the Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:D2","CH_B337_SIAE:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:FGS-B04","text":"Ontological audit distinguishes conditional predicate-necessity from subject-necessity and existence from conceptual content, collapsing the concept-to-object transition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:D1","CH_B337_SIIO:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:FGS-B05","text":"Formal conversion then exposes the cosmological proof’s ontological dependency, while category critique isolates its transcendent causal and modal fallacies.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:AS4","CH_B337_SVIC:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:FGS-B06","text":"The residual contradiction between reason’s unavoidable demand and its inability to identify a necessary object is resolved through regulative reclassification.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:AS6","CH_B337_SVIC:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:FGS-B07","text":"Recurrent subreption converts the regulative ideal into an object, while critique reverses the conversion without abolishing the ideal’s function.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:A18","CH_B337_SVIC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:FGS-B08","text":"A separate practical branch can supplement speculative indecision with rational assent, but only through an interest external to theoretical proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:D5","CH_B337_SIAE:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned existence → explanatory regress → demand for an unconditioned ground → provisional necessary being.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:AS2","CH_B337_SIAE:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AFM-B02","text":"Necessary being → complete reality → absolute unity → representation of a Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:AS3","CH_B337_SIAE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AFM-B03","text":"Maximal concept → audit of necessity → existence reclassified as non-predicative positing → ontological proof invalidated.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:AS6","CH_B337_SIIO:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AFM-B04","text":"Empirical restart → transcendent causal inference → formal conversion → ontological dependency exposed → cosmological proof invalidated.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:D2","CH_B337_SVIC:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AFM-B05","text":"Failed constitutive proof → regulative ideal → indefinite empirical inquiry → distinct possibility of practical assent.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SD4","CH_B337_SIAE:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:AFM-B06","text":"Natural subreption → renewed hypostatization → critical correction → restoration of the unconditioned as orientation rather than object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SD6","CH_B337_SVIC:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:MME-01","text":"The traditional plurality of proofs is reorganized into a hierarchy in which ostensibly empirical demonstrations depend on the ontological inference.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:SD3","CH_B337_SVIC:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MME-02","text":"Necessity is divided into logical, alleged objective, and regulative forms, preventing their mutual substitution.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:ME1","CH_B337_SVIC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MME-03","text":"Existence is reclassified from conceptual content to objective positing under conditions of possible givenness.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:ME2","CH_B337_SIIO:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MME-04","text":"The opposition between completed explanation and endless regress is replaced by complementary rules: seek completeness while treating every empirical stopping point as provisional.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:ME5","CH_B337_SVIC:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MME-05","text":"The Supreme Being is redistributed across non-equivalent functions rather than retained as a single object with a single epistemic status.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:ME3","CH_B337_SVIC:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MME-06","text":"The naturalness of an inference is separated from its validity, making unavoidable illusion compatible with critical correction.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:ME8","CH_B337_SVIC:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface level] Speculative reason’s ontological and cosmological arguments fail to prove the existence of a Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:ALT1","CH_B337_SVIC:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural level] Both failures arise from converting conceptual or explanatory necessity into the objective existence of a determinate being.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:ALT2","CH_B337_SVIC:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic level] Critique preserves reason’s search for unity by replacing constitutive assertion with regulative employment.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:ALT4","CH_B337_SVIC:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal level] Necessity and contingency cease to be cognizable properties of things in themselves, while empirical inquiry becomes indefinitely progressive.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:ALT5","CH_B337_SVIC:ALT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative level] The analysis performs its thesis by reconstructing each proof, reversing its apparent direction, exposing its hidden premise, and restoring only the function that survives critique.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:AS2","CH_B337_SVIC:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:ALT-B06","text":"[Normative level] Practical obligation can sustain conditional assent only by remaining categorically distinct from speculative evidence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A9","CH_B337_SIAE:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:FL-01","text":"The initial inference from contingent existence to some necessary existence remains locally ambiguous even though the developed critique denies any legitimate bridge to an unconditioned existent.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:MRT2","CH_B337_SVIC:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:FL-02","text":"The restriction of existential cognition to possible experience leaves transcendent existence neither cognizable nor impossible, producing a deliberately suspended ontological status.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:MRT5","CH_B337_SIIO:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:FL-03","text":"Placement of the necessary ground beyond the world remains vulnerable to constitutive reification whenever its strictly regulative status is relaxed.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:MRT6","CH_B337_SVIC:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:FL-04","text":"Practical assent retains rational authority after speculative grounds are declared insufficient, creating a controlled but unresolved tension between modes of rational commitment.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:MRT5","CH_B337_SIAE:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:FL-05","text":"The conversion exposing cosmological dependence requires the uniqueness and complete determinacy of the ens realissimum, premises whose full derivation remains external to the local proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:MRT5","CH_B337_SVIC:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MP-01","text":"Necessity mutates from an object-property into conditional logical necessity and finally into a regulative principle of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:ME1","CH_B337_SVIC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MP-02","text":"The ens realissimum mutates from the preferred candidate for necessary being into the hidden engine of failed proof and then into an ideal of systematic unity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:A5","CH_B337_SVIC:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MP-03","text":"Empirical ascent reverses into transcendental dependence once experience is shown to supply only a datum rather than the proof’s decisive inference.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:ME1","CH_B337_SIAE:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MP-04","text":"Contradiction changes from a proposed test of necessary being into a limit on logical analysis, yet the appeal to thinkable non-existence leaves a modal asymmetry insufficiently defended.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIO:A5","CH_B337_SVIC:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as an open phenomenal regress organized by a projected unconditioned horizon that can guide the series without entering it as a member.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SD5","CH_B337_SVIC:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes a recursive engine of inquiry that generates ideals of completion and repeatedly mistakes those ideals for objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SD3","CH_B337_SVIC:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:HSI-03","text":"Time becomes indefinitely progressive because explanation is directed toward completion while the empirical regress remains without an admissible final term.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SD5","CH_B337_SVIC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:HSI-04","text":"Critical navigation proceeds by explaining phenomena through further phenomena while orienting inquiry as if an ultimate ground existed, without converting that orientation into cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:D11","CH_B337_SIAE:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:HSI-05","text":"Capture occurs when a regulative principle is hypostatized; escape consists not in eliminating the ideal but in restoring its non-objective function.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:ME7","CH_B337_SVIC:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_26:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the proof taxonomy, the ontological failure, the cosmological dependency, the fallacy inventory, and the regulative resolution.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:MRT1","CH_B337_SIIO:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined where the physico-theological path is only classified, the restriction of categories to experience is inherited, and practical authority is not integrated into the regulative resolution.","refs":["CH_B337_SIAE:AS6","CH_B337_SVIC:MRT3","CH_B337_SIAE:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if its terminal conclusion remains the impossibility of speculative proof rather than a denial of divine existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:MRT8","CH_B337_SIIO:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where the non-predicative analysis of existence combines with formal conversion to expose the cosmological proof’s ontological dependency.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:MRT4","CH_B337_SIIO:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining the recursive sequence of projection, proof, exposure, reclassification, and renewed susceptibility to illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:MRT9","CH_B337_SIAE:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_26:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable residue resistant to axiomatic closure is the gap between reason’s unavoidable need for a necessary ground and its inability to cognize that ground as an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIC:SNX6","CH_B337_SIIO:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 76–78","context":"Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being · Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God · Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God"}},{"id":"CL_B337_27","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 79–81","order":120,"parents":["CH_B337_SVIP","CH_B337_SVCA","CH_B337_APP2"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"2e5a609ef6d7c39dc8c9e9d8316159e0d9127b66a9e90617f93409cc20845cb6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:AS-01","text":"The governing problem is how reason can legitimately employ transcendental ideas after every speculative attempt to cognize their supersensible objects has failed.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:AS1","CH_B337_SVCA:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:AS-02","text":"A Supreme Being cannot become an object of theoretical knowledge, yet the idea survives as a critically purified concept, a possible practical postulate, and a regulative rule.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A10","CH_B337_SVCA:AS6","CH_B337_SVCA:AS5","CH_B337_APP2:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:AS-03","text":"At this scale, the argument becomes a conversion machine that transforms failed metaphysical object-claims into methodological governance of empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:SD1","CH_B337_APP2:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:AS-04","text":"Its reductive movement descends from observed order to a proportionate architect, exposes the leap to necessity and total reality, and reorganizes the traditional proofs as a dependency chain.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:AS2","CH_B337_SVIP:FGS3","CH_B337_SVIP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:AS-05","text":"Its recursive movement begins because reason necessarily projects unconditioned totality, mistakes the projection for an object, and therefore requires permanent critical correction.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A2","CH_B337_APP2:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:AS-06","text":"The boundary of possible experience is reconstructed from a prohibition against transcendence into the enabling condition of indefinitely intensified empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:SD2","CH_B337_APP2:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:AS-07","text":"Practical postulation and regulative employment remain nonconvertible survivals of theology: one concerns practical warrant, while the other directs systematic cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:SD1","CH_B337_APP2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:AS-08","text":"Closure is asymptotic rather than doctrinal: systematic unity governs inquiry as an unattainable maximum, while final theoretical completion remains excluded.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A15","CH_B337_APP2:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Possible experience is the terminal domain of determinate speculative cognition; no inference may acquire objective validity by crossing that boundary.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A1","CH_B337_APP2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Conceptual analysis, necessity, perfection, and noncontradiction cannot posit the existence of their objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A1","CH_B337_APP2:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Causality has determinate meaning only within experience and cannot connect the empirical world to an extramundane ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A2","CH_B337_SVCA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Transcendental ideas arise necessarily from reason but do not thereby constitute supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A2","CH_B337_APP2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Dialectic belongs to the constitutive misemployment of an idea rather than to the idea’s mere presence in reason.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A3","CH_B337_APP2:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] The traditional proofs are not independent demonstrations but dependent variants of one invalid transition from conditioned cognition to unconditioned existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:ME5","CH_B337_SVCA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Reason does not directly cognize higher objects; it mediates and systematizes the understanding’s empirical cognitions.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A4","CH_B337_APP2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] An idea may possess indirect and indeterminate validity by functioning analogically as a projected focus of systematic unity.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A8","CH_B337_APP2:A9","CH_B337_APP2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Natural purposiveness establishes at most an intelligent formative cause proportionate to worldly form, not an all-sufficient creator of matter.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A5","CH_B337_SVIP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Teleology remains legitimate only as a heuristic intensification of inquiry under universal natural laws and cannot replace mechanical explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A17","CH_B337_APP2:SD9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Speculative reason can neither prove nor disprove a Supreme Being.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A10","CH_B337_SVCA:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Practical necessity may warrant postulation, but such postulation cannot be converted into theoretical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A9","CH_B337_SVCA:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Complete systematic unity is an asymptotic maximum that regulates every stage of inquiry while remaining empirically unattainable.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A15","CH_B337_APP2:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GA-14","text":"[Emergent] Critical limitation is productive because it blocks transcendent cognition, expands immanent inquiry, and must remain permanently active against recurring illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:SD2","CH_B337_APP2:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:RMD-01","text":"[Mutation] Natural purposiveness moves from an alleged independent proof to a non-demonstrative orientation and finally to an as-if schema for systematic inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:NX7","CH_B337_SVIP:SD3","CH_B337_APP2:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RMD-02","text":"[Displacement] The Supreme Being shifts from the intended object of proof to a merely ideal completion and then to a relative analogical schema.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:SNX1","CH_B337_SVCA:D6","CH_B337_APP2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RMD-03","text":"[Reversal] Necessity first operates as an illicit transcendental bridge to existence and then reappears as a practical mode of warrant that renounces theoretical demonstration.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A11","CH_B337_SVCA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RMD-04","text":"[Stable identity] Causality remains restricted to experience throughout, but its function expands from defeating one proof to defining the global jurisdiction of explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A2","CH_B337_SVCA:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RMD-05","text":"[Terminal transformation] The failure of demonstration ceases to be a merely negative result when failed object-claims become rules for empirical method.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:AS5","CH_B337_APP2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RMD-06","text":"[Absorption] The local dependency of physico-theology on cosmological and ontological reasoning is absorbed into the general diagnosis of an unavailable transcendent transition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:AS4","CH_B337_SVCA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RMD-07","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] The architect–creator distinction disappears as an independent result after exposing the mismatch between empirical causation and constitutive theology; its function is absorbed into the constitutive–regulative distinction.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:ME2","CH_B337_APP2:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RMD-08","text":"[Escalation] The concealed use of pure reason in a single proof becomes the general doctrine that transcendental illusion is a permanent structural effect.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:NX8","CH_B337_APP2:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RMD-09","text":"[Functional recurrence] Absolute totality remains impossible as an attained object but returns as the asymptotic horizon organizing empirical approximation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A10","CH_B337_APP2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:CGD-01","text":"[Local] The distinction between architect and creator remains a specific diagnostic of physico-theology and depends on separating formal order from material contingency.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:MRT5","CH_B337_SVIP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:CGD-02","text":"[Scales upward] Particular taxonomies of theology remain local, but the nonconvertibility of speculative and practical warrant becomes globally load-bearing.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:AS1","CH_B337_SVCA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:CGD-03","text":"[Local machinery] Homogeneity, specification, and affinity are domain-specific procedures implementing the higher-order demand for systematic unity.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A12","CH_B337_APP2:A13","CH_B337_APP2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:CGD-04","text":"[Scales upward] The experiential restriction on speculative principles expands from a constraint on theological proof into the jurisdictional axiom governing all determinate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:AS3","CH_B337_APP2:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] Natural teleology links the demolition of proof to the recovery of theology as a regulative organization of nature.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:SD3","CH_B337_APP2:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:CGD-06","text":"[Catalyst] Symmetrical inability to prove or disprove a Supreme Being permits critical retention of the idea without restoring theoretical objecthood.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A10","CH_B337_SVCA:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:CGD-07","text":"[Underdetermined bridge] Moral postulation opens a route beyond speculative theology, but its positive derivation remains deferred and cannot complete this cluster’s theoretical architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:MRT4","CH_B337_SVCA:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:CGD-08","text":"[Superseded] The world-artificer is not retained as a positive theological object; it is recoded into an analogical, functionally object-like schema.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:D2","CH_B337_APP2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:GNX-01","text":"No continuous passage from sensible nature to a living supersensible author is available to speculative reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:SNX2","CH_B337_SVCA:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GNX-02","text":"No causal law valid for appearances licenses an inference to a cause outside the world.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:DNX2","CH_B337_SVCA:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GNX-03","text":"No analysis of necessity or perfection can extract the existence of a corresponding being.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:NX4","CH_B337_SVCA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GNX-04","text":"Comparative greatness, wisdom, power, and observed magnitude do not determine absolute perfection.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A8","CH_B337_SVIP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GNX-05","text":"Practical postulation is not theoretical knowledge, and practical necessity cannot substitute for speculative necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:NX6","CH_B337_SVCA:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GNX-06","text":"An ideal or analogical object is not a supersensible entity, and its conceptual consistency does not establish existence.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:NX1","CH_B337_APP2:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GNX-07","text":"Teleology cannot terminate investigation, exempt events from natural law, or replace physico-mechanical explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:NX12","CH_B337_APP2:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:GNX-08","text":"The failure of speculative affirmation is not atheistic disproof; the same boundary blocks both operations.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:NX7","CH_B337_SVCA:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:DBN-01","text":"The formal conditions enabling experience cannot also function as knowledge of experience’s absolute supersensible ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:DNX1","CH_B337_SVCA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:DBN-02","text":"The world as a whole cannot be treated as empirically contingent merely because events within it exhibit contingency.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:NX2","CH_B337_SVCA:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:DBN-03","text":"No external theoretical standpoint exists from which conditioned cognition could inspect or determine the unconditioned ground of existence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:SNX1","CH_B337_APP2:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:DBN-04","text":"Nature can be neither radically heterogeneous nor wholly undifferentiated without destroying classification, specification, and empirical determination.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:DNX2","CH_B337_APP2:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:DBN-05","text":"The regulative recovery of reason silently excludes the possibility that reason is naturally ordered toward unavoidable constitutive falsehood.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:DNX1","CH_B337_APP2:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:DBN-06","text":"No ideal explanation may close empirical investigation, because every apparent completion must remain open to further determination.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:NX12","CH_B337_APP2:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSA-01","text":"The architecture is organized around an indispensable but unpresentable center: the adequate object of complete unity and the Supreme Being knowable as such.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:SNX1","CH_B337_SVIP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSA-02","text":"The ground of correspondence between reason’s demand for systematicity and nature’s presumed unifiability remains unavailable.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:SNX2","CH_B337_APP2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSA-03","text":"The impossible standpoint is theoretical cognition of the unconditioned from beyond the conditions of human experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:SNX1","CH_B337_APP2:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSA-04","text":"Completed totality is structurally absent: no empirical series can present it, and no final theoretical closure may replace its regulative force.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:DNX7","CH_B337_APP2:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSA-05","text":"The ultimate theological object occupies a suspended position in which it is neither theoretically affirmable nor theoretically deniable.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:SNX3","CH_B337_SVCA:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:XD-01","text":"Because speculative proof fails while transcendental ideas remain necessary, critique must transform theological object-claims into methodological functions rather than abolish them.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-06","CL_B337_27:GA-14","CH_B337_SVCA:AS4","CH_B337_APP2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:XD-02","text":"The limited architect inferred from order and the supreme intelligence used as an as-if schema mark two nonconvertible functions of the same theological projection.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-09","CL_B337_27:GA-10","CH_B337_SVIP:A6","CH_B337_APP2:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:XD-03","text":"The dependency of physico-theology on cosmological and ontological reasoning demonstrates that every speculative proof ultimately requires the same unavailable transcendent transition.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-03","CL_B337_27:GA-06","CH_B337_SVIP:AS4","CH_B337_SVCA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:XD-04","text":"Symmetrical agnosticism preserves the theological idea precisely by withdrawing the theoretical objecthood required by both dogmatic affirmation and dogmatic denial.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-11","CL_B337_27:GA-14","CH_B337_SVCA:A10","CH_B337_SVCA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:XD-05","text":"The impossibility of an empirical proof of a transcendent object becomes the condition for expanding and coordinating inquiry within experience.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-01","CL_B337_27:GA-14","CH_B337_SVIP:DNX5","CH_B337_APP2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:XD-06","text":"Practical postulation and regulative employment constitute parallel but nonconvertible survivals of transcendental theology because they invoke different modes of necessity and warrant.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-08","CL_B337_27:GA-12","CH_B337_SVCA:A9","CH_B337_APP2:A7","CH_B337_SVCA:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:XD-07","text":"Absolute theological predicates cease to function as positive descriptions and become limits excluding empirical anthropomorphism while permitting only analogical representation.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-08","CL_B337_27:GA-09","CH_B337_SVIP:A8","CH_B337_SVCA:A12","CH_B337_APP2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:XD-08","text":"The hidden ontological completion of physico-theology exemplifies the circular pathology in which projected unity is hypostatized as a cause and then used to explain that same unity.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-05","CL_B337_27:GA-06","CH_B337_SVIP:A12","CH_B337_APP2:D14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:XD-09","text":"Concealed reliance on pure reason in individual proofs generalizes into the need for a permanent discipline against the recurrent objectification of regulative projections.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-05","CL_B337_27:GA-14","CH_B337_SVIP:NX8","CH_B337_APP2:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSD-01","text":"A transcendental idea can acquire objective significance as an operation organizing experience without acquiring a corresponding supersensible object.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-04","CL_B337_27:GA-08","CH_B337_APP2:D12","CH_B337_APP2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSD-02","text":"Objective validity divides into determinate constitutive validity for experienced objects and indirect regulative validity for projected systematic unity.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-01","CL_B337_27:GA-08","CH_B337_APP2:ME3","CH_B337_APP2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSD-03","text":"Rational time becomes indefinite convergence: completeness governs every present inquiry but can never become a completed empirical present.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-13","CL_B337_27:GA-14","CH_B337_APP2:SD8","CH_B337_APP2:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSD-04","text":"The rational subject becomes both systematizer and tribunal, projecting totality while continuously policing its own tendency to hypostatize that projection.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-05","CL_B337_27:GA-07","CL_B337_27:GA-14","CH_B337_SVCA:SD3","CH_B337_APP2:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSD-05","text":"Theology is redistributed across three irreducible statuses: suspended theoretical existence, practical postulation, and purified regulative conceptuality.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-08","CL_B337_27:GA-11","CL_B337_27:GA-12","CH_B337_SVCA:ME3","CH_B337_SVCA:SD1","CH_B337_SVCA:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSD-06","text":"Causal explanation becomes wholly immanent and indefinitely extensible because no transcendent ground may terminate the empirical regress.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-03","CL_B337_27:GA-14","CH_B337_SVCA:ME2","CH_B337_APP2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSD-07","text":"Mechanism and teleology cease to be rival causal orders: mechanism explains events, while teleology regulates the search for systematic connection.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-09","CL_B337_27:GA-10","CH_B337_APP2:ME8","CH_B337_APP2:SD9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:BSD-08","text":"Negative critique becomes generative rather than merely destructive because conceptual purification supplies the conditions for legitimate regulative reuse.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-14","CH_B337_SVCA:AS6","CH_B337_APP2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:RLE-01","text":"Reason demands unconditioned totality, objectifies its projection, encounters contradiction, relocates the error to employment, and redirects the same demand into indefinite empirical expansion.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:FGS1","CH_B337_APP2:FGS3","CH_B337_APP2:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RLE-02","text":"The principal escalation path runs from purposive order through a formative intelligence and absolute necessity to complete determination, where physico-theology collapses into ontological dependence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:FGS1","CH_B337_SVIP:FGS4","CH_B337_SVIP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RLE-03","text":"Complete systematic unity is the dominant attractor: it coordinates cognition from beyond every attainable standpoint without becoming an attained whole.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A8","CH_B337_APP2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RLE-04","text":"Transcendental illusion forms a permanent loop because projected totality repeatedly appears object-like even after criticism has blocked its authority.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:D2","CH_B337_APP2:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RLE-05","text":"Constitutive teleology forms a pathological explanatory loop when unity is first projected, then hypostatized as a cause, and finally invoked to terminate investigation.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:D14","CH_B337_APP2:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RLE-06","text":"Asymptotic inquiry spirals through repeated unification, differentiation, and mediation; each gain in systematicity renews rather than satisfies the demand for completion.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:SD8","CH_B337_APP2:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:RLE-07","text":"The decisive phase change occurs when the prohibition on transcendent proof is reversed into the productive methodological boundary of empirical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:SD1","CH_B337_APP2:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:FGS-B01","text":"The understanding supplies distributive empirical connections, while reason relates to objects only mediately by organizing those connections under projected wholes.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:AS4","CH_B337_APP2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:FGS-B02","text":"Reason’s demand for unconditioned completion projects a maximum of unity beyond every attainable empirical standpoint.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:FGS1","CH_B337_APP2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:FGS-B03","text":"Constitutive treatment of that projection generates transcendent proofs despite the absence of a sensible schema capable of determining their object.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:AS3","CH_B337_APP2:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:FGS-B04","text":"Critical reduction limits the empirical result to a proportionate intelligence and exposes the subsequent ascent as borrowed cosmological and ontological machinery.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:A6","CH_B337_SVIP:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:FGS-B05","text":"Jurisdictional critique blocks both speculative affirmation and speculative denial because every decisive transition exceeds possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A10","CH_B337_SVCA:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:FGS-B06","text":"The error is relocated from the idea to its employment, allowing the purified idea to be reserved for practical or regulative use.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:FGS3","CH_B337_SVCA:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:FGS-B07","text":"Regulative redirection generates systematic maxims and teleological orientation while preserving universal mechanical explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:FGS5","CH_B337_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:FGS-B08","text":"The resulting machine produces indefinite empirical expansion under a permanent critical discipline that prevents renewed hypostatization.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:FGS8","CH_B337_APP2:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:AFM-B01","text":"Observed order → proportionate architect → demand for creator → contingency-to-necessity leap → complete determination → ontological dependence → collapse of independent physico-theology.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:AS2","CH_B337_SVIP:FGS3","CH_B337_SVIP:FGS4","CH_B337_SVIP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:AFM-B02","text":"Collapse of speculative proof → symmetrical agnosticism → separation of idea from employment → analogical as-if schema → lawful systematic inquiry → asymptotic openness.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:FGS4","CH_B337_APP2:FGS3","CH_B337_APP2:A16","CH_B337_APP2:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:MME-01","text":"The classical proofs are redrawn from independent routes into a dependency structure generated by one recurrent transcendent overreach.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:ME5","CH_B337_SVCA:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MME-02","text":"God is reclassified from a knowable entity into a divided object of suspended theory, practical postulation, and regulative representation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:ME3","CH_B337_APP2:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MME-03","text":"Reason is repositioned from a faculty of higher-object cognition to a faculty that systematizes the understanding.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:ME4","CH_B337_APP2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MME-04","text":"Objective validity is no longer univocal but divides between determinate object-cognition and indirect, indeterminate regulation.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:ME3","CH_B337_APP2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MME-05","text":"The experiential boundary changes from a merely prohibitive limit into the condition of empirical productivity and coherence.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:ME2","CH_B337_APP2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MME-06","text":"Teleology and mechanism cease to designate competing explanations and become hierarchically coordinated as regulative orientation and constitutive explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:ME8","CH_B337_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MME-07","text":"Rational completion is temporalized as an indefinitely receding horizon rather than represented as an available totality.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:ALT4","CH_B337_APP2:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MME-08","text":"Illusion is reclassified from an eliminable mistake into a permanent structural effect requiring continuous critical governance.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:ME9","CH_B337_APP2:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system tests proofs of a Supreme Being and then asks what legitimate employment remains for the defeated transcendental ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:ALT1","CH_B337_APP2:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, sensibility supplies schemata, understanding determines experienced objects, and reason organizes these determinations without directly reaching objects.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:ALT2","CH_B337_APP2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, theology is displaced from speculative proof toward critical limitation, methodological regulation, and the prevention of inquiry-terminating explanations.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:ALT3","CH_B337_APP2:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, cognition remains conditioned and phenomenal, while unconditioned completion recedes indefinitely beyond every empirical sequence.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:ALT4","CH_B337_APP2:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system reenacts transcendental overreach, diagnoses its illusion, and reconstructs its failed terms as heuristic operators.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:AS5","CH_B337_APP2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:FL-01","text":"Systematic unity remains unstable between subjective maxim, objective but indeterminate principle, and transcendental presupposition of nature.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:MRT2","CH_B337_APP2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:FL-02","text":"The Supreme Being is declared theoretically unknowable while still receiving a determinate ideal structure and a relative analogical role.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:MRT6","CH_B337_APP2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:FL-03","text":"Natural teleology retains powerful rational and practical authority after losing the cognition of the object toward which it appears to point.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:MRT4","CH_B337_SVIP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:FL-04","text":"Moral necessity is assigned a distinct theological route, but the derivation that would justify its postulates remains deferred.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:MRT4","CH_B337_SVCA:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:FL-05","text":"The recovery of transcendental ideas depends on an unproved purposive assumption that reason cannot be naturally ordered toward constitutive deception.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:MRT8","CH_B337_APP2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MP-01","text":"The transition from architect to creator is the mutation point where empirical analogy ceases to suffice and a new transcendental proof becomes necessary.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:ME2","CH_B337_SVIP:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MP-02","text":"Necessity changes function when theoretical necessity fails to establish existence and practical necessity becomes a distinct warrant for postulation.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:A7","CH_B337_SVCA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MP-03","text":"Dialectic is relocated from the transcendental idea itself to its constitutive employment, making critical reuse possible.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:AS2","CH_B337_APP2:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MP-04","text":"The theological object mutates into a domain-specific projection once supersensible objecthood is denied but systematic function is retained.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:NX1","CH_B337_APP2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MP-05","text":"Validity changes from constitutive determination to indirect regulation when the absence of sensible schemata blocks object-cognition without eliminating functional necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:A6","CH_B337_APP2:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a layered cognitive machine in which sensibility supplies material, understanding determines empirical objects, and reason projects organizing horizons.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes a finite systematizer and internal tribunal that must govern the unavoidable projections generated by its own rational structure.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:SD3","CH_B337_APP2:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:HSI-03","text":"Time becomes indefinite convergence toward a completeness that regulates movement without ever becoming present as a completed whole.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:SD8","CH_B337_APP2:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:HSI-04","text":"Navigation remains possible by treating mechanism as the law of explanation, teleology as an orienting horizon, and critique as protection against their conflation.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:ME8","CH_B337_APP2:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_27:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the reduction of physico-theology, the general suspension of speculative theology, and the regulative recovery of transcendental ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:MRT1","CH_B337_SVCA:MRT1","CH_B337_APP2:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where experiential limits block transitions to supersensible existence and the constitutive–regulative distinction governs the recovered ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:MRT5","CH_B337_APP2:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MRT-B03","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the exact objective status of systematic unity and the positive derivation of moral theology.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:MRT2","CH_B337_SVCA:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MRT-B04","text":"Reconstruction depends on preserving the sequence from empirical ascent through transcendental exposure to regulative recovery; collapsing these phases destroys the argument’s movement.","refs":["CH_B337_SVIP:MRT6","CH_B337_APP2:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MRT-B05","text":"The principal elements resistant to axiomatic reduction are the ground of fit between reason and nature and the purposive assumption that reason must possess a truthful proper employment.","refs":["CH_B337_APP2:SNX2","CH_B337_APP2:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_27:MRT-B06","text":"The cluster is coherent only if negative critique is treated as a productive transformation rather than as the elimination of transcendental ideas.","refs":["CH_B337_SVCA:MRT7","CH_B337_APP2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 79–81","context":"Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof · Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason · Appendix"}},{"id":"CL_B337_28","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 82–84","order":121,"parents":["CH_B337_ITDM","CH_B337_CIDP","CH_B337_SIDP"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"f1e8511d8e547fc31fb4ca75c83d95324c801e1c2f609048bf84e58a2670e867","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:AS-01","text":"The cluster confronts the problem of constructing a secure system of reason from cognition bounded by possible experience without converting rational ambition into unsupported metaphysical extension.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:AS3","CH_B337_SIDP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AS-02","text":"Its central claim is that cognitive limitation must become a positive regulatory architecture rather than remain a merely external prohibition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:SD2","CH_B337_CIDP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AS-03","text":"The argument becomes architectonic and transcendental by deriving legitimate systematic form from the available material and from the task of determining reason’s limits.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A4","CH_B337_SIDP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AS-04","text":"The operative ground is the unequal constraint imposed on empirical, mathematical, and transcendental cognition, which prevents methods from remaining valid across domains without examination.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:AS5","CH_B337_CIDP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AS-05","text":"Mathematical method supplies the decisive test because its success depends on pure intuition, while its philosophical imitation produces only a simulacrum of evidence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:D12","CH_B337_SIDP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AS-06","text":"The movement is recursive: failed expansion produces delimitation, delimitation produces discipline, and discipline returns to the original ambition in order to retain systematic inquiry under transformed conditions.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:AF1","CH_B337_CIDP:AF4","CH_B337_SIDP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AS-07","text":"Closure is achieved not by completing speculative knowledge but by preserving reason’s authority through self-limitation and separating systematicity from dogmatism.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:SD6","CH_B337_SIDP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AS-08","text":"The resulting closure remains formally complete but materially open because the architecture establishes the necessity and scope of regulation without exhausting its divisions or procedures.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:MRT5","CH_B337_CIDP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective speculative cognition is finite and remains bounded by possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A3","CH_B337_SIDP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] A rational system must proportion its form and claims to the cognitive material actually available.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A4","CH_B337_SIDP:SD7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Method begins as the formal plan of systematic unity and becomes discipline directed toward the valid employment of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A5","CH_B337_CIDP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] A cognitive boundary governs the validity of methods as well as the permissible scope of conclusions.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:SD3","CH_B337_SIDP:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Reason generates interconnected illusion when its general principles operate without empirical, intuitive, or critical constraint.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A10","CH_B337_CIDP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Negative discipline is a constitutive condition of positive systematic unity.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:SD2","CH_B337_ITDM:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Mathematical certainty is a local consequence of construction in pure intuition rather than a universal power of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:SD1","CH_B337_SIDP:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Philosophy remains discursive because concepts do not provide the intellectual intuition required to present transcendental objects directly.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A2","CH_B337_SIDP:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Systematic inquiry survives the rejection of dogmatism because order and unity do not entail immediate synthetic certainty.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A18","CH_B337_SIDP:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] Philosophy’s legitimate authority is second-order jurisdiction over the conditions, limits, and migration of methods.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:SD2","CH_B337_SIDP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience functions simultaneously as the boundary, field, and validating medium of transcendental principles.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:ME5","CH_B337_SIDP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Pure reason preserves systematic authority only by renouncing unrestricted speculative construction.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:SD6","CH_B337_ITDM:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] The limit of possible cognition persists from the restriction of available materials to the methodological boundary of transcendental validity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:ME1","CH_B337_CIDP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] Critique changes from a negative inventory of limits into a complete architecture regulating reason’s employment.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:SD2","CH_B337_CIDP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RMD-03","text":"[Functional recurrence] Method recurs first as formal system-design, then as regulation of method, and finally as policing the migration of evidentiary forms.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:ME2","CH_B337_CIDP:FGS7","CH_B337_SIDP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RMD-04","text":"[Displacement] The insufficiency of general logic is displaced into the stronger rejection of any domain-invariant rational method.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:ME3","CH_B337_CIDP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Mathematical success changes from an apparent model for philosophy into evidence that certainty depends on local conditions and becomes deceptive when imitated elsewhere.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:SD1","CH_B337_SIDP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RMD-06","text":"[Terminal transformation] Systematicity is detached from dogmatism and retained as ordered, self-limiting inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A18","CH_B337_SIDP:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RMD-07","text":"[Absorption] Definitions, axioms, and demonstrations cease to function as universal marks of rigor and are absorbed into a higher-order account of method-specific evidence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:ME4","CH_B337_SIDP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RMD-08","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] Canon, architectonic, and history establish the promised completeness of the method but recede when discipline becomes the operative focus of the cluster.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A6","CH_B337_ITDM:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:CGD-01","text":"[Merely local] General logic’s provision of methodological names and signs remains introductory machinery rather than the cluster’s final account of method.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:CGD-02","text":"[Merely local] The unelaborated divisions of canon, architectonic, and history mark the larger system but do not become operative within this cluster.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A6","CH_B337_ITDM:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:CGD-03","text":"[Scales upward] The finite stock of cognition becomes the global requirement that every method justify its domain of validity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:D1","CH_B337_CIDP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:CGD-04","text":"[Bridge] The general negative code of discipline connects architectonic system-building to the concrete examination of dogmatical method-transfer.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A11","CH_B337_SIDP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:CGD-05","text":"[Catalyst] Definitions, axioms, and demonstrations become transition mechanisms by exposing the formal dependence of evidence on construction.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:AS5","CH_B337_SIDP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:CGD-06","text":"[Merely local] Algebraic signs and the constructibility of quantity support the mathematical case without remaining indispensable to the cluster-level architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:D3","CH_B337_SIDP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:CGD-07","text":"[Superseded] Isolated correction of errors is replaced by examination of the general structures through which errors recur across disguises.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:NX7","CH_B337_CIDP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:CGD-08","text":"[Recoded] Mathematical rigor is retained as legitimate within construction while being denied authority as a universal philosophical model.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:SD1","CH_B337_SIDP:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:GNX-01","text":"A secure system cannot be grounded in unrestricted metaphysical construction beyond the material conditions of human cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:SNX1","CH_B337_CIDP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GNX-02","text":"No method remains valid independently of differences among empirical observation, intuitive construction, and transcendental discursivity.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:SNX2","CH_B337_SIDP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GNX-03","text":"Positive instruction cannot substitute for negative regulation when reason’s methods generate systematic illusion.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:DNX7","CH_B337_CIDP:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GNX-04","text":"Reason cannot authenticate every employment merely by virtue of being reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:DNX2","CH_B337_CIDP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GNX-05","text":"Mathematical success cannot legitimate the transfer of construction, axioms, or demonstrations into transcendental philosophy.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:D12","CH_B337_SIDP:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GNX-06","text":"The rejection of dogmatical evidence does not require the rejection of systematic philosophical inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:NX14","CH_B337_SIDP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:GNX-07","text":"Pure conceptual necessity provides no direct transition to the existence or determinate character of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:SNX2","CH_B337_SIDP:NX13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:DBN-01","text":"Formal coherence must not be treated as the production or presentation of an object.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:DNX2","CH_B337_SIDP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:DBN-02","text":"The prestige of reason must not be treated as proof that its unexamined methods are valid.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:AS4","CH_B337_CIDP:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:DBN-03","text":"Universality and a priori origin must not be treated as evidence of methodological uniformity among rational sciences.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:DNX4","CH_B337_SIDP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:DBN-04","text":"A priori form must not be treated as capable of generating sensible matter, existence, or supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:DNX3","CH_B337_SIDP:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:DBN-05","text":"Systematic error must not be reduced to local accidents removable by isolated refutation.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:DNX5","CH_B337_CIDP:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:DBN-06","text":"Rational unity must not be identified with the accumulation or spontaneous agreement of private constructions.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:DNX4","CH_B337_ITDM:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSA-01","text":"The deepest absent capacity is an intellectual intuition able to present transcendental realities directly.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:DNX1","CH_B337_SIDP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSA-02","text":"The system is organized around the unavailability of an unrestricted metaphysical totality adequate to every rational design.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:SNX1","CH_B337_ITDM:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSA-03","text":"An unrevisable speculative foundation from which reason could proceed without self-correction is inadmissible.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:SNX4","CH_B337_CIDP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSA-04","text":"Complete transparency between formal signs, philosophical concepts, and their objects remains unavailable.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:SNX4","CH_B337_SIDP:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSA-05","text":"The unavailable possibility that gives the cluster its force is a reason capable of retaining authority without renouncing speculative overreach.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:SNX5","CH_B337_CIDP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:XD-01","text":"Finite cognitive materials and systematically generated error jointly require an architecture whose first operative function is negative discipline.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-01","CL_B337_28:GA-05","CL_B337_28:GA-06","CH_B337_ITDM:D1","CH_B337_CIDP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:XD-02","text":"The insufficiency of general logic and the formal difference among rational employments jointly establish that method is indexed to a mode of cognition rather than universally transferable.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-03","CL_B337_28:GA-04","CH_B337_ITDM:D6","CH_B337_CIDP:SNX2","CH_B337_SIDP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:XD-03","text":"Because transcendental reasoning lacks the constraints supporting mathematical certainty, imitation of mathematical evidence intensifies rather than corrects philosophical illusion.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-05","CL_B337_28:GA-07","CL_B337_28:GA-08","CH_B337_CIDP:D5","CH_B337_SIDP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:XD-04","text":"Once method is treated as system-design and method-employment together, philosophical rigor becomes mediated justification and domain-sensitive regulation rather than formal imitation.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-03","CL_B337_28:GA-04","CL_B337_28:GA-10","CH_B337_ITDM:AS5","CH_B337_CIDP:A12","CH_B337_SIDP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:XD-05","text":"Self-limitation becomes constructive because it eliminates dogmatical evidence while preserving systematic inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-06","CL_B337_28:GA-09","CL_B337_28:GA-12","CH_B337_CIDP:SD6","CH_B337_SIDP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:XD-06","text":"Possible experience is not merely an external frontier but the productive medium through which transcendental principles receive objective validity.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-01","CL_B337_28:GA-11","CH_B337_ITDM:A3","CH_B337_SIDP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:XD-07","text":"Architectonic unity cannot rely on transparent concepts or spontaneous agreement because philosophical concepts remain corrigible and private constructions remain mutually fragmentary.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-02","CL_B337_28:GA-08","CL_B337_28:GA-09","CH_B337_ITDM:D5","CH_B337_SIDP:DNX8","CH_B337_SIDP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:XD-08","text":"The conjunction of historical architectonic and corrigible conceptual analysis gives philosophical system-building an intrinsically diachronic form.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-03","CL_B337_28:GA-09","CH_B337_ITDM:SD5","CH_B337_SIDP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSD-01","text":"The rational subject becomes simultaneously legislator, source of error, object of discipline, and judge of its own jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-05","CL_B337_28:GA-06","CL_B337_28:GA-12","CH_B337_CIDP:SD1","CH_B337_CIDP:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSD-02","text":"Objectivity is redistributed from conceptual possession to the conditions under which objects can be given through intuition and experience.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-01","CL_B337_28:GA-08","CL_B337_28:GA-11","CH_B337_SIDP:A1","CH_B337_SIDP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSD-03","text":"Philosophical temporality becomes recursive and corrigible because inquiry begins with obscure given concepts and approaches exposition only through revisable analysis.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-08","CL_B337_28:GA-09","CH_B337_SIDP:SD5","CH_B337_SIDP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSD-04","text":"Error is reclassified as an effect of method-transfer and generative principles rather than an accidental defect in isolated propositions.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-04","CL_B337_28:GA-05","CH_B337_CIDP:SD4","CH_B337_CIDP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSD-05","text":"Philosophy acquires strategic authority by assigning jurisdictions to methods rather than competing with mathematics for constructive certainty.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-07","CL_B337_28:GA-10","CH_B337_SIDP:SD2","CH_B337_SIDP:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSD-06","text":"Rigor is transformed from immediate evidence into mediated validity, deduction, and fidelity to the conditions of presentation.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-04","CL_B337_28:GA-08","CL_B337_28:GA-11","CH_B337_SIDP:ME3","CH_B337_SIDP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:BSD-07","text":"The negative system becomes positively generative because every exclusion of illegitimate method further specifies the form of legitimate systematic reason.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-06","CL_B337_28:GA-09","CL_B337_28:GA-12","CH_B337_ITDM:SD2","CH_B337_CIDP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:RLE-01","text":"Speculative ambition exceeds finite materials, generates unstable construction, encounters critique, and returns as design proportioned to cognitive limits.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:AS1","CH_B337_ITDM:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RLE-02","text":"Reason legislates cognition, misuses its own rules, becomes subject to discipline, and thereby restores rather than abandons its legislative authority.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:SD1","CH_B337_CIDP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RLE-03","text":"Mathematical success invites analogical transfer, transfer produces dogmatism, and critique returns that success to the local conditions of pure intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:FGS1","CH_B337_SIDP:FGS7","CH_B337_SIDP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RLE-04","text":"Possible experience grounds transcendental principles while those principles articulate the conditions of possible experience, producing a productive but unresolved justificatory loop.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:MRT5","CH_B337_SIDP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RLE-05","text":"Given philosophical concepts initiate analysis, analysis approaches fuller exposition, and the impossibility of guaranteed completeness returns inquiry to correction.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:SD5","CH_B337_SIDP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RLE-06","text":"Repeated negation undergoes phase change when error-prevention becomes a complete regulatory architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:AS1","CH_B337_CIDP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:RLE-07","text":"The dominant attractor is systematic but non-dogmatical reason: ordered enough to unify cognition and limited enough to refuse immediate speculative certainty.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:A18","CH_B337_SIDP:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:FGS-B01","text":"Finite cognitive materials plus unrestricted speculative design generate unstable construction and the demand for proportional architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:AS3","CH_B337_ITDM:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:FGS-B02","text":"Unequal empirical, intuitive, and transcendental constraints generate unequal conditions of methodological validity.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:AS5","CH_B337_CIDP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:FGS-B03","text":"Unconstrained transcendental employment generates interconnected illusion, which requires a general negative code rather than isolated correction.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A10","CH_B337_CIDP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:FGS-B04","text":"The general negative code converts formal method into reflexive regulation of reason’s own employment.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:A12","CH_B337_CIDP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:FGS-B05","text":"Mathematical success generates a transfer temptation whose validity must be tested against the conditions that make construction possible.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:AS1","CH_B337_SIDP:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:FGS-B06","text":"Construction in pure intuition yields mathematical evidence, whereas discursive cognition yields mediated philosophical proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:AS3","CH_B337_SIDP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:FGS-B07","text":"Failure of method-transfer eliminates speculative dogmata while retaining systematic conceptual inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:FGS-B08","text":"Negative discipline plus domain-specific validation produces a self-limiting architecture in which reason preserves authority without unrestricted expansion.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:SD6","CH_B337_SIDP:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:AFM-B01","text":"Unlimited speculative construction → recognition of insufficient materials → delimitation of legitimate cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AFM-B02","text":"Delimitation of cognition → distinction between materials and plan → formal division of transcendental method.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:AF2","CH_B337_ITDM:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AFM-B03","text":"Devaluation of negation → recognition of systematic epistemic risk → method-focused discipline.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:AF1","CH_B337_CIDP:AF4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AFM-B04","text":"Mathematical success → analogical transfer → transcendental differentiation → elimination of speculative dogmatism.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:FGS1","CH_B337_SIDP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AFM-B05","text":"Constitutive mathematical evidence → failed philosophical imitation → discursive and mediated validation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:FGS5","CH_B337_SIDP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:AFM-B06","text":"Rejection of dogmatical method → retention of systematic inquiry → self-limiting critical closure.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:FGS7","CH_B337_SIDP:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:MME-01","text":"Method is reclassified from a catalogue of procedures into an architecture governing the jurisdiction and employment of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:ME2","CH_B337_CIDP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MME-02","text":"Cognitive boundaries become internal validity conditions rather than external prohibitions imposed on otherwise complete methods.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:ME1","CH_B337_CIDP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MME-03","text":"The opposition between negative and positive cognition collapses because error-prevention becomes the condition of legitimate construction.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:ME1","CH_B337_ITDM:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MME-04","text":"The mathematics–philosophy boundary is redrawn as a difference of cognitive procedures rather than a division of subject matter.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:ME1","CH_B337_SIDP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MME-05","text":"Error becomes a generative system produced by principles and method-transfer rather than a collection of detachable mistakes.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:ME6","CH_B337_CIDP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MME-06","text":"Systematicity and dogmatism become separable, making rigorous philosophical order possible without mathematical-style evidence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:ME7","CH_B337_SIDP:NX12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MME-07","text":"Philosophy is repositioned as a second-order practice that assigns limits and regulates evidentiary migration among sciences.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:SD2","CH_B337_SIDP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the cluster moves among the bounded cognitive edifice, the value of negative discipline, and the attempted transfer of mathematical definitions, axioms, and demonstrations.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:ALT1","CH_B337_CIDP:ALT1","CH_B337_SIDP:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, valid cognition depends on the relation among concepts, intuitions, constraints, and the mode in which a method is employed.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:ALT2","CH_B337_SIDP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, critique reframes philosophical struggle as the regulation of construction and the policing of method-transfer.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:ALT3","CH_B337_SIDP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, objects require intuition, sensible matter arrives through experience, and philosophical concepts become determinate through revisable inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:ALT4","CH_B337_SIDP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the cluster enacts its thesis by moving from an announced architecture through negative discipline to a domain-specific test of method.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:AF2","CH_B337_SIDP:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:FL-01","text":"The demand for a complete rational system remains in tension with the finite and experience-bounded material from which it must be built.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:MRT6","CH_B337_ITDM:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:FL-02","text":"Reason must legislate its own employment even though its need for discipline shows that it cannot simply authenticate that legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:MRT5","CH_B337_CIDP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:FL-03","text":"Possible experience appears both as the ground proved possible by transcendental principles and as the medium through which those principles are validated.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:MRT5","CH_B337_SIDP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:FL-04","text":"Discipline is distinguished from positive instruction, yet its general code gives negation an implicitly constructive and legislative form.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:MRT6","CH_B337_CIDP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:FL-05","text":"The categorical denial that mathematical method benefits philosophy exceeds the narrower demonstration that mathematical evidence changes status when transferred.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:MRT7","CH_B337_SIDP:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MP-01","text":"Method mutates from the formal plan of a complete system into regulation of the employment of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:ME2","CH_B337_CIDP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MP-02","text":"Limit mutates from a restriction on cognitive scope into a criterion governing methodological validity.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:ME1","CH_B337_CIDP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MP-03","text":"Mathematical certainty mutates from a model of rational success into a locally valid power whose imitation generates false evidence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:SD1","CH_B337_SIDP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MP-04","text":"Negative judgment mutates from apparent epistemic deficiency into the architecture required for legitimate positive cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:ME1","CH_B337_CIDP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MP-05","text":"Definition reverses temporal function: it constitutes the mathematical concept at the beginning but remains a possible terminus of philosophical analysis.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:D6","CH_B337_SIDP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly modeled as a finite architectonic machine whose passages are gated by the specific conditions under which cognition can present and validate objects.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:A1","CH_B337_SIDP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes a divided reason that builds, legislates, generates illusion, and installs a negative code against its own overreach.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:AS4","CH_B337_CIDP:SD1","CH_B337_CIDP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:HSI-03","text":"Time divides between mathematics, which begins from constituted concepts, and philosophy, which advances through revisable clarification of concepts already given.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:SD5","CH_B337_SIDP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:HSI-04","text":"Escape from dogmatism occurs not by destroying reason but by restraining method-transfer and converting expansion into self-limiting navigation.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:DNX6","CH_B337_CIDP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_28:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s movement from finite cognitive materials through disciplinary regulation to the rejection of dogmatical method-transfer.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:MRT1","CH_B337_CIDP:MRT1","CH_B337_SIDP:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning canon, architectonic, history, and the detailed procedures of the negative code.","refs":["CH_B337_ITDM:MRT5","CH_B337_CIDP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale if the prior claims that space and time are the only pure intuitions and that objective validity is bounded by possible experience are granted.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:MRT2","CH_B337_SIDP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where domain-specific conditions of presentation explain both legitimate certainty and the generative structure of error.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:MRT4","CH_B337_CIDP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MRT-B05","text":"Its principal unresolved dependencies are the reflexivity of reason’s self-legislation and the circular relation between experience and transcendental principles.","refs":["CH_B337_CIDP:MRT5","CH_B337_SIDP:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_28:MRT-B06","text":"Construction in pure intuition remains the indispensable technical hinge resistant to further axiomatic compression because the distinction between constructing a schema and producing an existent object is not fully determined.","refs":["CH_B337_SIDP:AS3","CH_B337_SIDP:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 82–84","context":"II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method · Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason · Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism"}},{"id":"CL_B337_29","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 85–87","order":122,"parents":["CH_B337_SI_5","CH_B337_SI_6","CH_B337_SI_7"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"f08d8de65437b26d3a4ed96d8afe6324716ce3f2d3478ede0c98958aa087b002","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:AS-B01","text":"The system asks how finite reason can preserve unrestricted inquiry while preventing speculative conflict, conjecture, and proof from becoming unauthorized cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:AS1","CH_B337_SI_6:AS1","CH_B337_SI_7:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:AS-B02","text":"Dogmatic disputes are displaced from alleged supersensible objects to the prior question of what entitles reason to assert, hypothesize, explain, or prove anything.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:AS4","CH_B337_SI_7:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:AS-B03","text":"Possible experience becomes the common jurisdictional ground that validates empirical cognition, determines objective possibility, and blocks transcendent extension.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A4","CH_B337_SI_6:A4","CH_B337_SI_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:AS-B04","text":"Ideas exceeding experience are redistributed among regulative orientation, practical commitment, and temporary defence while being denied constitutive, explanatory, and demonstrative authority.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A12","CH_B337_SI_6:A5","CH_B337_SI_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:AS-B05","text":"Opposition is internalized as reason’s diagnostic medium: objections must be freely developed, but their force must be judged through deductions of entitlement rather than victory.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:AS6","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:AS-B06","text":"The architecture moves from unrestricted controversy through bilateral speculative suspension to regulated hypothesis, transcendental deduction, and productive limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:AS7","CH_B337_SI_6:AS7","CH_B337_SI_7:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:AS-B07","text":"Its movement is recursive and cumulative because each failed extension returns reason to the conditions of its own operation at a more determinate level.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD5","CH_B337_SI_6:AF5","CH_B337_SI_7:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:AS-B08","text":"Closure takes the form of lawful but revisable peace: speculative possession is renounced, critical testing remains active, and practical orientation survives without becoming knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A18","CH_B337_SI_7:SD5","CH_B337_SI_5:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience supplies both the positive condition of objective cognition and the negative boundary against transcendent use.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A4","CH_B337_SI_6:A4","CH_B337_SI_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Critique determines rational jurisdiction by examining the powers, principles, and limits of cognition before adjudicating object-level claims.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A2","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Assertion, hypothesis, explanation, regulation, defence, practical commitment, and proof constitute distinct rational operations with non-interchangeable warrants.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:SD1","CH_B337_SI_7:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Ideas of unconditioned totality remain structurally necessary while losing constitutive objecthood and acquiring regulative, practical, and defensive functions.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A6","CH_B337_SI_6:A5","CH_B337_SI_7:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Affirmation and denial concerning supersensible objects possess equal speculative incapacity when neither can connect its claim to possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A7","CH_B337_SI_5:A8","CH_B337_SI_6:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Defeating an opponent’s argument can suspend that argument but cannot establish the objective truth of the contrary proposition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A11","CH_B337_SI_7:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] A legitimate explanatory hypothesis requires an independently secured possible object, an experiential connection, and sufficient derivation of its consequences.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A2","CH_B337_SI_6:A3","CH_B337_SI_6:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Transcendental proof is a deduction of objective entitlement and object-possibility rather than a merely valid inferential sequence.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A1","CH_B337_SI_7:A11","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Dialectical opposition changes from external combat into a recursively produced test through which reason exposes and limits its own illusions.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD5","CH_B337_SI_6:AS6","CH_B337_SI_6:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Scepticism can interrupt dogmatic confidence but cannot replace an a priori determination of necessary cognitive limits.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:D8","CH_B337_SI_5:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Speculative restriction redirects supersensible commitment toward practical reason without converting practical warrant into theoretical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A12","CH_B337_SI_7:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Rational freedom consists in public self-legislation through unrestricted criticism governed by universally accountable rules.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD1","CH_B337_SI_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Intellectual peace is produced by jurisdictional demarcation and recurring self-examination, not by object-level victory or suppressed contradiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD3","CH_B337_SI_6:DNX5","CH_B337_SI_7:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:RMD-01","text":"[Stable Identity / Scope Expansion] Possible experience persists as the cognitive boundary but expands from a truth criterion into the condition of object-possibility, explanatory admissibility, and proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A4","CH_B337_SI_6:A4","CH_B337_SI_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RMD-02","text":"[Functional Recurrence / Absorption] Critique first adjudicates public conflict, then regulates conjectural explanation, and finally becomes deduction of the right to employ principles.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:D11","CH_B337_SI_6:ME1","CH_B337_SI_7:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Supersensible ideas move from contested objects to practically significant propositions, regulative orientations, defensive fictions, and finally non-objective demands of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:D3","CH_B337_SI_6:ME4","CH_B337_SI_7:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Opposition changes from a threat to rational order into the indispensable mechanism by which reason discovers illicit extension.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ME7","CH_B337_SI_6:D7","CH_B337_SI_6:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RMD-05","text":"[Displacement] Possibility shifts from mere openness against dogmatic denial to experiential object-possibility and then to the condition that transcendental proof must establish.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:D1","CH_B337_SI_6:A2","CH_B337_SI_7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RMD-06","text":"[Domain Migration] Causality moves from a dispute with empirical scepticism into a constraint on physical explanation and finally into a transcendental condition of temporal experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ME9","CH_B337_SI_6:A6","CH_B337_SI_7:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RMD-07","text":"[Terminal Transformation] Scepticism disappears as a final position after provoking the transition from observed failures to systematic knowledge of necessary limits.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A15","CH_B337_SI_5:SD2","CH_B337_SI_5:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RMD-08","text":"[Absorption] Tactical refutation survives as temporary defence, narrows into counter-hypothesis, and is barred from becoming indirect proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:D2","CH_B337_SI_6:D6","CH_B337_SI_7:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RMD-09","text":"[Escalation] Peace develops from termination of dogmatic conflict into an institutional condition maintained by the deliberate renewal and adjudication of objections.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A18","CH_B337_SI_6:D7","CH_B337_SI_6:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Public controversy, intellectual guardianship, and critical education remain the specific institutional machinery of polemic rather than the complete architecture of discipline.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ME7","CH_B337_SI_5:ME8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:CGD-02","text":"[Local] Prediction of consequences and the prohibition on auxiliary assumptions remain the distinctive adequacy tests for empirical hypotheses.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A7","CH_B337_SI_6:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:CGD-03","text":"[Local] The singular proof-ground and prohibition of apagogic demonstration remain specialized constraints on transcendental proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A10","CH_B337_SI_7:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:CGD-04","text":"[Scales Upward] Possible experience scales across all three disciplines as their common criterion of objective entitlement and common boundary of misuse.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A4","CH_B337_SI_6:A4","CH_B337_SI_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] The requirement to establish an object’s possibility connects admissible hypothesis to valid transcendental demonstration.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A2","CH_B337_SI_7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:CGD-06","text":"[Catalyst] Deliberately strengthened opposition catalyzes the transition from first-order dispute to second-order examination of principles.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:AS6","CH_B337_SI_6:ME5","CH_B337_SI_7:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:CGD-07","text":"[Obsolete] Scepticism becomes structurally obsolete once critique can distinguish contingent argumentative failure from necessary cognitive limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:D8","CH_B337_SI_5:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:CGD-08","text":"[Superseded] Object-level metaphysical victory is recoded as insufficient because neither refutation nor rhetorical superiority supplies a deduction of objective right.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:DNX9","CH_B337_SI_7:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:GNX-01","text":"No proposition acquires immunity from criticism through sanctity, utility, consolation, or inherited authority.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GNX-02","text":"Logical consistency and conceivability do not establish the objective possibility of an alleged object.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:NX2","CH_B337_SI_7:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GNX-03","text":"An idea of reason is neither a supersensible object nor an admissible theoretical explanation merely because reason necessarily generates it.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:NX3","CH_B337_SI_6:DNX1","CH_B337_SI_7:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GNX-04","text":"Empirical failure, habitual succession, and psychological association cannot establish either necessary limits or objective necessity.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:NX12","CH_B337_SI_7:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GNX-05","text":"Refutation, argument accumulation, and disproof of a contrary claim are not substitutes for positive transcendental deduction.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:NX4","CH_B337_SI_7:NX6","CH_B337_SI_7:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GNX-06","text":"External coercion and social authority cannot settle disputes generated by reason’s own internal structure.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:NX9","CH_B337_SI_5:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:GNX-07","text":"Practical usefulness and practical faith cannot function as speculative evidence for supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:DNX4","CH_B337_SI_5:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:DBN-01","text":"The system requires the falsity of any realism that treats appearances, the world-totality, or objects of thought as things independently given in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A10","CH_B337_SI_7:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:DBN-02","text":"The system excludes a univocal standard of warrant under which explanation, regulation, defence, practical assent, and proof would be interchangeable.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:SD1","CH_B337_SI_6:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:DBN-03","text":"The system rejects exhaustive speculative bivalence whenever opposed predicates presuppose an impossible or undetermined object.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:NX9","CH_B337_SI_7:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:DBN-04","text":"Explanatory completeness cannot be purchased by positing an unknowable ultimate ground or completed causal totality.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:D3","CH_B337_SI_6:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:DBN-05","text":"Neither dogmatism nor unrestricted scepticism may infer total conclusions without a complete investigation of cognitive powers.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD8","CH_B337_SI_5:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:DBN-06","text":"Critique cannot depend on an external sovereign; its tribunal must be constituted and validated through reason’s own universally examinable principles.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:MRT7","CH_B337_SI_5:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSA-01","text":"The constitutive absence is any givenness through which God, soul, world-totality, or the unconditioned could become a verifiable speculative object.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SNX1","CH_B337_SI_7:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSA-02","text":"No standpoint is available from which reason could exceed experience and simultaneously certify the objective validity of that excess.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:SNX2","CH_B337_SI_7:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSA-03","text":"The transition from speculative suspension to practical affirmation lacks a cognitive bridge and therefore remains governed by rational interest.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SNX6","CH_B337_SI_7:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSA-04","text":"No final object-level truce is possible because closure belongs to jurisdictional demarcation rather than agreement about transcendent objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SNX5","CH_B337_SI_7:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSA-05","text":"Frictionless reason is inadmissible: philosophical security requires internally generated contradiction to remain available for critical processing.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:DNX5","CH_B337_SI_6:AF5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:XD-01","text":"Unrestricted criticism and strict deduction jointly make rational freedom a condition of answerability rather than exemption from law.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-02","CL_B337_29:GA-08","CL_B337_29:GA-12","CH_B337_SI_5:A1","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:XD-02","text":"Bilateral speculative incapacity permits a counter-possibility to block dogmatic denial while withholding every positive claim to alternative reality.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-05","CL_B337_29:GA-06","CL_B337_29:GA-07","CH_B337_SI_6:D5","CH_B337_SI_6:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:XD-03","text":"The same experiential boundary that admits an explanatory hypothesis also fixes the domain within which its concepts could receive objective proof.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-01","CL_B337_29:GA-07","CL_B337_29:GA-08","CH_B337_SI_6:A3","CH_B337_SI_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:XD-04","text":"Because transcendental opposition may arise from shared subjective illusion, an objection can diagnose overreach but cannot operate as indirect proof.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-06","CL_B337_29:GA-09","CH_B337_SI_6:D7","CH_B337_SI_7:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:XD-05","text":"Critique surpasses scepticism by replacing accumulated failures with a deduction capable of distinguishing contingent error from necessary limitation.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-02","CL_B337_29:GA-08","CL_B337_29:GA-10","CH_B337_SI_5:A16","CH_B337_SI_5:A17","CH_B337_SI_7:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:XD-06","text":"Hyperphysical explanation and supersensible proof fail for the same jurisdictional reason: neither establishes an object under conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-01","CL_B337_29:GA-07","CL_B337_29:GA-08","CH_B337_SI_6:NX4","CH_B337_SI_7:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:XD-07","text":"Speculative limitation can preserve practical commitment only by maintaining a categorical difference between practical warrant and objective cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-03","CL_B337_29:GA-05","CL_B337_29:GA-11","CH_B337_SI_5:D3","CH_B337_SI_7:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:XD-08","text":"Defensive fictions occupy an unstable but necessary position because they resist dogmatic impossibility without satisfying the object-possibility required for explanation or proof.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-04","CL_B337_29:GA-07","CL_B337_29:GA-08","CH_B337_SI_6:SD5","CH_B337_SI_6:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSD-01","text":"Objecthood becomes relational to conditions of givenness, so finite cognition is a determinable domain rather than an indefinitely incomplete approach to things in themselves.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD9","CH_B337_SI_7:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSD-02","text":"Rational discourse becomes a differentiated economy of warrants in which each operation is judged by its source, domain, and authorized use.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:SD1","CH_B337_SI_7:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSD-03","text":"The rational subject becomes a recursively divided office that must act as claimant, opponent, examiner, and legislator of its own cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD1","CH_B337_SI_6:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSD-04","text":"Causality is secured neither as a supersensible relation nor as empirical habit, but as a law governing temporally determinable experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ME9","CH_B337_SI_7:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSD-05","text":"Reason’s temporality is developmental and recursive: dogmatic assertion produces sceptical interruption, which returns as critical maturity rather than terminal doubt.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ALT5","CH_B337_SI_5:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSD-06","text":"Public rationality becomes a constitutional order in which free disclosure is preserved by internally justified discipline rather than external control.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD1","CH_B337_SI_5:A13","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:BSD-07","text":"Theoretical symmetry between affirmation and denial coexists with practical asymmetry, producing commitment without speculative privilege.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD7","CH_B337_SI_7:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:RLE-01","text":"Claim and counterclaim expose reciprocal insufficiency, which generates critique, boundary determination, and a peace that remains dependent on renewed examination.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:FGS1","CH_B337_SI_5:SD5","CH_B337_SI_5:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RLE-02","text":"Hypothesis enters a tightening loop in which experiential consequences test sufficiency, failure invites auxiliary assumptions, and supplementation further weakens warrant.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A7","CH_B337_SI_6:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RLE-03","text":"The proliferation of proofs becomes evidence of failure, redirecting inquiry from accumulated arguments toward the singular condition making an object possible.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:D6","CH_B337_SI_7:A10","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RLE-04","text":"The idea of unconditioned totality functions as an escalation machine: it demands completion, drives categories beyond experience, produces dialectic, and returns as regulation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A6","CH_B337_SI_5:A9","CH_B337_SI_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RLE-05","text":"Possible experience is the dominant conceptual attractor because every valid claim about objects, causes, hypotheses, or proofs is redirected toward it.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A4","CH_B337_SI_6:A4","CH_B337_SI_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RLE-06","text":"Freedom and discipline form a reinforcing loop: freedom exposes every claim, while self-legislated discipline prevents exposure from collapsing into arbitrary scepticism.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:MRT8","CH_B337_SI_5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:RLE-07","text":"Local disputes escalate into jurisdictional questions, and jurisdictional questions converge on the source and limits of objective authority.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ME1","CH_B337_SI_7:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:FGS-B01","text":"Every claim enters an unrestricted field of criticism in which concealment and external protection are disallowed.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A1","CH_B337_SI_5:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:FGS-B02","text":"Criticism activates the domain gate: connection to possible experience permits objective determination, while its absence suspends constitutive use.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A4","CH_B337_SI_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:FGS-B03","text":"Ideas failing the domain gate are redistributed into regulative, practical, or defensive functions rather than discarded or converted into objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A5","CH_B337_SI_7:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:FGS-B04","text":"Reason recursively produces the strongest opposition to each position so that concealed dialectical assumptions become explicit.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:AS6","CH_B337_SI_6:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:FGS-B05","text":"Critical deduction examines the source, objective validity, and legitimate range of every principle used in explanation or proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:A14","CH_B337_SI_7:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:FGS-B06","text":"The result is assigned to a differentiated status: knowledge, empirical hypothesis, regulative orientation, practical commitment, temporary defence, or invalid pretension.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:SD1","CH_B337_SI_6:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:FGS-B07","text":"The machine terminates invalid speculative conflict while preserving free inquiry, practical orientation, and recursive self-testing.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A18","CH_B337_SI_7:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:AFM-B01","text":"Unrestricted assertion → opposed assertion → bilateral insufficiency → jurisdictional inquiry → critical discipline → lawful limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:AS7","CH_B337_SI_7:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:AFM-B02","text":"Demand for unconditioned totality → transcendent category use → dialectical contradiction → withdrawal of objecthood → regulative redirection.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A6","CH_B337_SI_5:A9","CH_B337_SI_6:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:AFM-B03","text":"Conjectural possibility → experiential connection → explanatory testing → demand for objective validity → transcendental deduction → bounded cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:AS2","CH_B337_SI_7:AS1","CH_B337_SI_7:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:AFM-B04","text":"Free criticism → internalized opposition → self-legislated discipline → differentiated warrants → boundary-produced peace → practical orientation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD1","CH_B337_SI_5:ME10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:MME-01","text":"Metaphysical disagreement becomes a dispute over conditions of assertion rather than a contest between independently available supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ME1","CH_B337_SI_7:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MME-02","text":"Possibility divides into logical non-contradiction, polemical openness, experiential object-possibility, and demonstrable transcendental possibility.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:NX2","CH_B337_SI_7:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MME-03","text":"Proof becomes a question of objective right and domain rather than persuasive force, formal consequence, or argumentative accumulation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:ME1","CH_B337_SI_7:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MME-04","text":"Explanatory incompleteness is revalued as rational openness, while transcendent completion is exposed as premature arrest of inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:ME2","CH_B337_SI_6:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MME-05","text":"Opponents and objections become diagnostic resources rather than threats to be silenced or defeated.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ME7","CH_B337_SI_6:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MME-06","text":"Freedom and discipline cease to be opposites once discipline is reconstructed as reason’s public self-legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ME6","CH_B337_SI_5:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MME-07","text":"Faith, regulation, hypothesis, and knowledge cease to occupy a single speculative scale and acquire distinct modes of legitimacy.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ME4","CH_B337_SI_6:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] Polemics, hypotheses, and proofs concerning God, freedom, soul, world, causality, and nature are regulated as distinct explicit problems.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ALT1","CH_B337_SI_6:AS2","CH_B337_SI_7:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Possible experience operates as the domain gate coordinating objecthood, causality, explanation, evidential warrant, and conceptual validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:A4","CH_B337_SI_7:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Intervention shifts from answering every claim to auditing the source, domain, and permitted use of the principles generating claims.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ALT3","CH_B337_SI_7:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Reality is determinately cognizable only as temporally and causally ordered experience, while the supersensible remains neither known nor eliminated.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:DNX5","CH_B337_SI_7:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The architecture performs its thesis by staging opposition, withholding premature closure, differentiating warrants, and returning recursively to conditions of validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:AS3","CH_B337_SI_6:AF5","CH_B337_SI_7:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:FL-01","text":"Unrestricted criticism and strict discipline cohere only if discipline remains internal self-legislation rather than concealed external authority.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:MRT8","CH_B337_SI_5:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:FL-02","text":"Possible experience validates objective concepts while critique must still justify why this criterion can authoritatively delimit every speculative extension.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:MRT7","CH_B337_SI_7:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:FL-03","text":"Defensive fictions perform strategically necessary work despite lacking the objective possibility demanded from explanatory hypotheses and proofs.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:SD5","CH_B337_SI_6:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:FL-04","text":"Theoretical symmetry and practical preference remain connected by rational interest rather than by a demonstrated transition between cognition and commitment.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD7","CH_B337_SI_5:MRT6","CH_B337_SI_7:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:FL-05","text":"The singularity of a transcendental proof-ground does not fully settle whether several valid expository routes may disclose that same ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_7:MRT6","CH_B337_SI_7:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MP-01","text":"Possibility mutates from mere non-contradiction or polemical openness into experiential object-possibility and finally into a required ground of proof.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_6:NX2","CH_B337_SI_6:A2","CH_B337_SI_7:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MP-02","text":"Ideas mutate from apparent supersensible objects into regulative principles, practical orientations, and temporary defensive constructions.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A6","CH_B337_SI_6:A5","CH_B337_SI_6:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MP-03","text":"Opposition mutates from external adversarial conflict into internal self-testing, but it must cease functioning as proof when subjective illusion remains possible.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ME7","CH_B337_SI_6:ME5","CH_B337_SI_7:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly processed by a tribunal-laboratory that admits claims publicly, generates objections, tests conditions, and assigns bounded jurisdictions.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ALT2","CH_B337_SI_6:ME5","CH_B337_SI_7:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not a sovereign spectator but a finite institution divided among claimant, litigant, examiner, and self-legislating judge.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD1","CH_B337_SI_6:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:HSI-03","text":"Time is spiral maturation: conflict recurs, but each recurrence can return reason to its limits with greater jurisdictional determination.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:ALT5","CH_B337_SI_5:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:HSI-04","text":"Cognizable reality forms a finite, internally articulable field surrounded by a supersensible horizon that can orient action without becoming territory.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:SD9","CH_B337_SI_6:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:HSI-05","text":"The remaining struggle is disciplined navigation between dogmatic possession and sceptical abandonment through free disclosure, recursive opposition, and boundary maintenance.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:A13","CH_B337_SI_6:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_29:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from free speculative conflict through regulated hypothesis and transcendental deduction to productive limitation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:MRT1","CH_B337_SI_6:MRT1","CH_B337_SI_7:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MRT-B02","text":"The deduction of the complete system of a priori principles remains underdetermined because the architecture presupposes rather than reproduces that larger validation.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:MRT2","CH_B337_SI_6:MRT2","CH_B337_SI_7:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MRT-B03","text":"The cluster is coherent if explanatory, regulative, polemical, practical, and demonstrative warrants remain differentiated under the common boundary of possible experience.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:MRT3","CH_B337_SI_6:MRT3","CH_B337_SI_7:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where possible experience simultaneously validates finite cognition, excludes transcendent extension, and converts opposition into diagnostic evidence.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:MRT4","CH_B337_SI_6:MRT2","CH_B337_SI_7:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining recursive return, progressive differentiation of warrants, and limitation as a productive result.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:MRT10","CH_B337_SI_6:MRT6","CH_B337_SI_7:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_29:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable residue resistant to axiomatic closure is the status of defensive fictions and the non-cognitive transition from speculative suspension to practical commitment.","refs":["CH_B337_SI_5:MRT6","CH_B337_SI_6:MRT4","CH_B337_SI_7:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 85–87","context":"Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics · Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis · Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs"}},{"id":"CL_B337_30","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 88–90","order":123,"parents":["CH_B337_CICP","CH_B337_SIUE","CH_B337_SIIS"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"fb96cb285fbe14546682fa4771a92389baa31cbbd921f320346867d246a467e1","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:AS-01","text":"Reason seeks systematic completion beyond experience, but speculative cognition cannot possess the supersensible objects implicated in that demand.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:AS1","CH_B337_SIIS:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AS-02","text":"Internal critique converts speculative incapacity into a self-imposed jurisdiction that limits reason without surrendering its legitimate authority.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A2","CH_B337_CICP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AS-03","text":"Transcendental ideas cannot govern empirical explanation, which remains methodologically subject to natural causality.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A5","CH_B337_SIUE:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AS-04","text":"Speculative failure does not abolish reason’s ultimate interests but reallocates their legitimate fulfillment to practical significance.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:SD2","CH_B337_SIUE:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AS-05","text":"The practical canon contracts legitimate pure reason to unconditional moral law, excluding prudence and unresolved transcendental freedom.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A11","CH_B337_SIUE:D3","CH_B337_SIUE:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AS-06","text":"Moral law establishes worthiness for happiness and gives practical reality to a moral world that rational freedom ought to realize.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A4","CH_B337_SIIS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AS-07","text":"Rational hope arises because moral striving demands proportionate happiness while nature supplies no necessary connection between virtue and reward.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A7","CH_B337_SIIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AS-08","text":"God and future life become practically necessary hypotheses inseparable from obligation, but they remain unavailable for theoretical possession.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A11","CH_B337_SIIS:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AS-09","text":"Moral theology and teleology are legitimate only as consequences of autonomous morality and may never become its external ground.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:D6","CH_B337_SIIS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AS-10","text":"The movement closes recursively: moral law generates theology, theology returns reason to immanent action, and critique blocks the resulting postulates from becoming speculative doctrine.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:FGS7","CH_B337_SIIS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Pure reason secures legitimacy through internal discipline and error prevention rather than unrestricted speculative expansion.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A1","CH_B337_CICP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Reason remains constitutively directed beyond experience toward systematic completion even after speculative access is denied.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A6","CH_B337_SIUE:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-03","text":"[Terminal] Pure speculative reason has no legitimate synthetic employment and therefore admits neither a speculative canon nor genuine supersensible cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A10","CH_B337_CICP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Limitation changes from a merely negative prohibition into a protective jurisdiction that reallocates reason’s legitimate use.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:D2","CH_B337_CICP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Transcendental ideas cannot serve as immanent principles or substitutes for empirical causal explanation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A5","CH_B337_SIUE:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] A canon is possible only for a proper employment of reason, and at this scale that employment is identified with pure moral legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A8","CH_B337_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Freedom divides into an actionable capacity of rational self-determination and an unresolved transcendental causality excluded from the practical canon.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A12","CH_B337_SIUE:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-08","text":"[Attractor] Natural explanation and moral legislation operate as irreducible registers of what occurs and what ought to occur.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A14","CH_B337_SIUE:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Moral law governs freedom unconditionally and precedes every theological command, empirical end, or promised reward.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A3","CH_B337_SIIS:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Happiness is excluded as the ground of moral legislation but returns as a component of the complete good when subordinated to moral worthiness.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:NX6","CH_B337_SIIS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Nature’s inability to connect morality and happiness generates the structural need for rational hope.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A8","CH_B337_SIIS:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] God and future moral order are practically necessary hypotheses whose necessity never converts them into demonstrated objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:AS5","CH_B337_SIIS:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] The supreme good unifies autonomy, happiness, divine causality, future order, and natural teleology under a moral rather than speculative demand.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:SD3","CH_B337_SIIS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Moral law may generate theology, but theology may neither replace nor legislate moral law.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:SD5","CH_B337_SIIS:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:RMD-01","text":"The canon persists as a criterion of legitimate employment but mutates from an abstract faculty-rule into the exclusive jurisdiction of pure moral law.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:SD4","CH_B337_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RMD-02","text":"Transcendental ideas migrate from failed candidates for theoretical cognition to practically necessary hypotheses.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:SD1","CH_B337_SIIS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RMD-03","text":"Freedom splits functionally: transcendental freedom remains problematic, while practical freedom becomes the operative capacity of rational willing.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A3","CH_B337_SIUE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RMD-04","text":"Happiness reverses valence by moving from excluded prudential motivation to a distributive consequence conditioned by moral worth.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:NX6","CH_B337_SIIS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RMD-05","text":"Limitation changes function from the negative discipline of philosophy into the positive jurisdiction through which incapacity protects legitimate reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:AS2","CH_B337_CICP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RMD-06","text":"Nature remains closed under causal explanation, becomes morally insufficient as a distributor of happiness, and is subsequently reread through moral teleology.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:D1","CH_B337_SIIS:FGS4","CH_B337_SIIS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RMD-07","text":"God migrates from the speculative triad into the practical figure of a morally perfect cause, while future life becomes the temporal horizon of moral completion.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A3","CH_B337_SIIS:A10","CH_B337_SIIS:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RMD-08","text":"Systematic unity is displaced from a completed totality of cognition into an ethically completed unity of ends.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:AS1","CH_B337_SIUE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RMD-09","text":"The rejection of external authority recurs first as rational self-discipline and then as the denial that divine command can ground obligation.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:NX4","CH_B337_SIIS:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RMD-10","text":"Transcendental freedom does not vanish after exclusion; it persists as the suspended boundary between natural causality and practical obligation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:SNX2","CH_B337_SIUE:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:CGD-01","text":"The classification of speculative transcendental logic as discipline rather than canon remains a local juridical formulation of the broader limit on speculative reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:CGD-02","text":"The exclusion of empirical psychology remains a local methodological safeguard whose current-level function is to preserve the purity of practical legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:CGD-03","text":"The determinate attributes assigned to the supreme cause remain downstream theological specifications and are not required for the initial transfer from speculation to practice.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A12","CH_B337_SIIS:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:CGD-04","text":"The discipline of cognitive delimitation scales upward because it becomes the protective jurisdiction governing every later practical claim.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:ME1","CH_B337_CICP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:CGD-05","text":"The persistence of supersensible orientation bridges speculative failure and practical redirection without licensing speculative recovery.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:D5","CH_B337_CICP:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:CGD-06","text":"Nature’s moral indifference catalyzes the transition from unconditional obligation to hope, postulation, and moral theology.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:CGD-07","text":"The conditional claim that reason’s proper employment must be practical is superseded by the determinate identification of that employment with moral law.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A11","CH_B337_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:CGD-08","text":"The initial triad of freedom, immortality, and God is recoded into a practical dyad of God and future life after transcendental freedom is excluded and practical freedom internalized.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:MRT6","CH_B337_SIUE:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:GNX-01","text":"Pure reason is not an unrestricted instrument for extending knowledge beyond experience.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GNX-02","text":"Synthetic cognition of supersensible objects is unavailable to pure speculative reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GNX-03","text":"Transcendental ideas cannot replace natural causes or empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GNX-04","text":"Prudential pursuit of happiness does not constitute pure practical legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GNX-05","text":"Transcendental freedom cannot explain phenomenal events or enter the practical canon as resolved cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GNX-06","text":"Nature cannot be presumed morally self-correcting or capable of distributing happiness according to virtue.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GNX-07","text":"Practical necessity does not transform God and future life into demonstrated dogmas.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:GNX-08","text":"Moral theology cannot replace autonomous legislation with transcendent authority.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:DBN-01","text":"The collapse of speculative metaphysics cannot entail the abolition of reason’s supersensible orientation.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:DBN-02","text":"Failure within the speculative mode cannot be repaired by expanding that same mode or by converting practical relevance into theoretical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:DNX5","CH_B337_SIUE:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:DBN-03","text":"Moral obligation cannot be reduced to natural description, because the normative register cannot be absorbed into the causal register.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:ME2","CH_B337_SIUE:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:DBN-04","text":"Unconditional morality cannot be grounded in happiness, inclination, or empirical utility.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:DBN-05","text":"The empirical world cannot be admitted as a self-rewarding moral order without eliminating the structural basis of hope and postulation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:DBN-06","text":"Neither critique nor morality can accept an external authority that precedes reason’s own standards of validity.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:DNX4","CH_B337_SIIS:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:DBN-07","text":"Practical postulation cannot become theoretical possession without collapsing the distinction that legitimates it.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absence of speculative access to the very domain toward which reason remains directed.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSA-02","text":"The absent present-world closure between virtue and happiness supplies the negative center of rational hope.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSA-03","text":"No theoretical reconciliation of natural necessity and transcendental freedom is available to close the causal architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSA-04","text":"No final external authority can ground either rational discipline or moral obligation.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:SNX2","CH_B337_SIIS:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSA-05","text":"Complete unity remains unavailable as a cognized object and persists instead as the demand organizing reason’s speculative and practical transformations.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:SNX1","CH_B337_SIUE:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:XD-01","text":"Speculative restriction preserves reason because its unsatisfied supersensible orientation can be reassigned without being abolished.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-01","CL_B337_30:GA-02","CL_B337_30:GA-04","CH_B337_CICP:SD1","CH_B337_CICP:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:XD-02","text":"The absence of empirical or explanatory utility forces transcendental ideas to derive their significance from practical orientation.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-02","CL_B337_30:GA-05","CH_B337_SIUE:A6","CH_B337_SIUE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:XD-03","text":"The practical canon is a legitimacy filter rather than an expansion of cognition because only pure moral law satisfies the condition of proper employment.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-03","CL_B337_30:GA-06","CH_B337_CICP:A9","CH_B337_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:XD-04","text":"Obligation can operate through practical freedom without settling the transcendental causality of rational willing.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-07","CL_B337_30:GA-08","CH_B337_SIUE:D4","CH_B337_SIUE:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:XD-05","text":"Happiness becomes morally admissible only after it ceases to be the motive of action and becomes a consequence proportioned to worthiness.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-09","CL_B337_30:GA-10","CH_B337_SIUE:D3","CH_B337_SIIS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:XD-06","text":"The practical postulate of a moral cause arises from the mismatch between rational hope and nature’s inability to secure it.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-11","CL_B337_30:GA-12","CH_B337_SIIS:FGS4","CH_B337_SIIS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:XD-07","text":"Divine attributes function as requirements of universal moral distribution rather than as speculative discoveries about a supersensible object.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-12","CL_B337_30:GA-13","CH_B337_SIIS:D4","CH_B337_SIIS:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:XD-08","text":"Moral theology is legitimate only under a directional asymmetry in which moral law authorizes theology and theology cannot authorize moral law.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-09","CL_B337_30:GA-14","CH_B337_SIIS:D6","CH_B337_SIIS:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:XD-09","text":"Systematic completion mutates from possession of a total object-domain into a future-oriented ethical unity sustained by hope.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-02","CL_B337_30:GA-13","CH_B337_SIUE:SD3","CH_B337_SIIS:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:XD-10","text":"Rational self-critique and moral autonomy instantiate the same anti-externality principle at epistemic and practical levels.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-01","CL_B337_30:GA-09","CL_B337_30:GA-14","CH_B337_CICP:DNX4","CH_B337_SIIS:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSD-01","text":"Reason’s total interest differentiates into knowledge, obligation, and hope, each governed by a distinct modality of legitimacy.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-03","CL_B337_30:GA-09","CL_B337_30:GA-12","CH_B337_SIIS:A1","CH_B337_SIIS:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSD-02","text":"The status of an idea depends on its employment: the same supersensible content can be illegitimate as cognition and necessary as practical hypothesis.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-05","CL_B337_30:GA-12","CH_B337_SIUE:SD1","CH_B337_SIIS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSD-03","text":"Epistemic absence becomes generative because acknowledged incapacity creates the jurisdiction within which practical legitimacy can arise.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-03","CL_B337_30:GA-04","CL_B337_30:GA-12","CH_B337_CICP:SD1","CH_B337_SIIS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSD-04","text":"The finite subject becomes simultaneously an appearance within nature and an autonomous agent whose will can be determined by rational law.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-07","CL_B337_30:GA-08","CL_B337_30:GA-09","CH_B337_SIUE:A12","CH_B337_SIIS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSD-05","text":"Time divides between a present sensible order lacking moral closure and a future horizon in which moral completion is represented as possible.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-11","CL_B337_30:GA-13","CH_B337_SIIS:SNX1","CH_B337_SIIS:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSD-06","text":"Causality acquires a dual grammar: natural causality explains appearances, while rational determination grounds obligation without theoretically reconciling the two.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-07","CL_B337_30:GA-08","CH_B337_SIUE:SD2","CH_B337_SIUE:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:BSD-07","text":"Individual moral compliance cannot generate the universal harmony demanded by the supreme good, so practical reason projects a coordinating moral order beyond individual agency.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-11","CL_B337_30:GA-13","CH_B337_SIIS:DNX2","CH_B337_SIIS:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:RLE-01","text":"Speculative desire produces overreach, critique redirects it toward practice, and practical postulation returns to critique whenever it threatens to become theory.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A1","CH_B337_CICP:D8","CH_B337_SIIS:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RLE-02","text":"Limitation recursively protects the authority that accepts limitation, turning incapacity into secure jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:D2","CH_B337_CICP:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RLE-03","text":"Moral striving generates worthiness, worthiness generates hope, hope generates postulation, and postulation returns to the priority of moral law.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A7","CH_B337_SIIS:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RLE-04","text":"Happiness marks a phase change: it is first expelled as prudential motivation and later restored within the complete good.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:NX6","CH_B337_SIIS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RLE-05","text":"Freedom spirals through three functions: unresolved transcendental causality, practical self-determination, and the collective moral world freedom ought to realize.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A15","CH_B337_SIUE:A12","CH_B337_SIIS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RLE-06","text":"Complete unity functions as the principal attractor, drawing speculative totality, moral legislation, happiness, theology, and teleology into one practical architecture.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A2","CH_B337_SIIS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:RLE-07","text":"The cognition–postulation boundary repeatedly reasserts itself, preventing practical theology from escaping its subordination to immanent moral action.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:DNX4","CH_B337_SIIS:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:FGS-B01","text":"Rational demand for systematic completion generates transcendental ideas concerning the unconditioned.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:FGS1","CH_B337_SIUE:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FGS-B02","text":"The speculative uselessness of those ideas activates self-critique and determines the limits of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:FGS3","CH_B337_CICP:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FGS-B03","text":"Determinate limits protect empirical cognition while separating reason’s positive orientation from its speculative misuse.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:FGS3","CH_B337_CICP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FGS-B04","text":"The remaining rational interest transfers to practice and divides empirical prudence from pure moral legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:FGS4","CH_B337_SIUE:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FGS-B05","text":"Pure moral law produces worthiness for happiness and gives practical reality to a moral world.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:AS3","CH_B337_SIIS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FGS-B06","text":"Nature’s inability to secure proportionate happiness converts rational hope into the postulate of a supreme moral cause.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:FGS4","CH_B337_SIIS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FGS-B07","text":"The supreme moral cause generates theological and teleological unity, which is then subordinated again to autonomous moral legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:FGS6","CH_B337_SIIS:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FGS-B08","text":"Renewed critique blocks the practical postulate from becoming theoretical possession and confines theology to immanent moral service.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:DNX4","CH_B337_SIIS:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:AFM-B01","text":"Demand for systematic completion → speculative overreach → self-imposed discipline → determination of cognitive limits → protection of empirical jurisdiction.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:AS1","CH_B337_CICP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AFM-B02","text":"Preserved supersensible orientation → practical redirection → pure moral canon → practical freedom → unconditional obligation.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:SD5","CH_B337_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AFM-B03","text":"Moral worthiness → demand for proportionate happiness → natural insufficiency → rational hope → supreme-good postulation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A6","CH_B337_SIIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:AFM-B04","text":"Supreme-good postulate → moral theology → teleological unity → return to autonomous action → prohibition on speculative promotion.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:AS5","CH_B337_SIIS:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:MME-01","text":"Philosophy is redrawn from an organon of metaphysical acquisition into a discipline that governs the legitimacy of cognitive and practical employments.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MME-02","text":"Supersensible ideas are reclassified by use rather than content, becoming practical limits where they fail as theoretical objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MME-03","text":"Knowledge, duty, and hope become distinct but coordinated rational modalities rather than competing answers to one theoretical question.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A1","CH_B337_SIIS:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MME-04","text":"The opposition between causality and normativity is replaced by a non-reductive distinction between natural explanation and moral legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MME-05","text":"Freedom becomes thinkable as practical agency without requiring a solved metaphysics of transcendental causality.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MME-06","text":"Happiness and morality are separated at the level of motivation but recombined at the level of the complete good.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MME-07","text":"Theology and natural teleology become downstream interpretations licensed by autonomous moral reason rather than foundations of obligation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:ME4","CH_B337_SIIS:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MME-08","text":"The moral world becomes a practical order directed toward transforming the sensible world rather than an independently cognized supersensible realm.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system adjudicates freedom, immortality, God, moral duty, and rational hope after the failure of speculative cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A3","CH_B337_SIIS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, a criterion of legitimate employment governs the missing connection among cognition, morality, and happiness.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:ALT2","CH_B337_CICP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, reason redirects metaphysical acquisition into a practical canon and subordinates theology and teleology to autonomous action.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:ALT3","CH_B337_SIIS:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, phenomenal causality remains unresolved against freedom while moral completion is projected beyond the present sensible order.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:ALT4","CH_B337_SIIS:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system enacts self-critique by generating theology from moral law and then denying theology legislative priority.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:D1","CH_B337_SIIS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:FL-01","text":"The transition from speculative incapacity to practical possibility is structurally necessary but not fully demonstrated by the failure of speculation alone.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:MRT6","CH_B337_SIUE:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FL-02","text":"Practical freedom is treated as experientially effective while its relation to exhaustive natural causality remains suspended.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:MRT5","CH_B337_SIUE:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FL-03","text":"Moral worth must remain independent of happiness, yet effective purposive morality is claimed to require hope for happiness proportionate to worth.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:MRT4","CH_B337_SIIS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FL-04","text":"The derivation of determinate divine attributes from moral requirements approaches the speculative expansion that practical postulation forbids.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:MRT5","CH_B337_SIIS:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FL-05","text":"The inference from unity of moral ends to a single supreme causal will remains underdetermined unless unity is accepted as an unconditional rational demand.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:MRT3","CH_B337_SIIS:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:FL-06","text":"The moral world receives practical objective reality while remaining unavailable as an empirically given realm or intelligible intuition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:A5","CH_B337_SIIS:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MP-01","text":"The canon mutates from a general criterion of proper employment into the specific jurisdiction of pure moral legislation.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:SD4","CH_B337_SIUE:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MP-02","text":"Freedom mutates from an unresolved speculative object into an operative capacity for rational determination of the will.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A15","CH_B337_SIUE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MP-03","text":"Happiness mutates from an excluded prudential end into a conditionally admissible component of the supreme good.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:NX6","CH_B337_SIIS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MP-04","text":"God mutates from a member of the speculative triad into a practically postulated moral cause inseparable from obligation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:A3","CH_B337_SIIS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MP-05","text":"Systematic unity mutates from the projected completion of cognition into the ethical completion of rational ends.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:AS1","CH_B337_SIUE:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a two-register machine in which natural causality explains events while rational law legislates conduct without absorbing either register into the other.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:SD2","CH_B337_SIUE:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:HSI-02","text":"The subject becomes a self-disciplining finite agent situated within nature yet capable of having its will determined by reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:A2","CH_B337_SIUE:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:HSI-03","text":"Time becomes the interval between a present world lacking moral closure and a future horizon in which practical completion can be represented.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:SNX1","CH_B337_SIIS:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:HSI-04","text":"Rational navigation consists in changing the mode of employment when legitimate content threatens to become speculative error or practical postulation threatens to become theory.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:DNX3","CH_B337_SIIS:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:HSI-05","text":"The remaining struggle is immanent transformation of the sensible world under moral law rather than speculative escape into a supersensible domain.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:ME3","CH_B337_SIIS:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_30:MRT-B01","text":"The architecture is sufficient to reconstruct the movement from speculative limitation through practical legislation to moral-theological postulation.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:MRT1","CH_B337_SIIS:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MRT-B02","text":"The strongest structure is the conversion of limitation into protection followed by the conversion of natural moral indifference into practical postulation.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:MRT4","CH_B337_SIIS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MRT-B03","text":"The derivation of pure moral law and the initial inference from speculative failure to practical possibility remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_SIUE:MRT3","CH_B337_CICP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent if theoretical cognition and practical necessity remain distinct modalities rather than rival claims to the same knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_CICP:MRT3","CH_B337_SIIS:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction must preserve the recursive return from theology to moral autonomy and the repeated prohibition against promoting postulates into cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:MRT7","CH_B337_SIIS:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_30:MRT-B06","text":"Rational hope remains the element most resistant to axiomatic reduction because it must sustain purposive morality without becoming a reward-based motive.","refs":["CH_B337_SIIS:MRT4","CH_B337_SIIS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 88–90","context":"Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason · Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason · Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason"}},{"id":"CL_B337_31","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 91–93","order":124,"parents":["CH_B337_SIOK","CH_B337_CI_2","CH_B337_CIHP"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"6eaa96bfac175c5a5be83a103e9167915164fce0b79c713e8d93e1d992759239","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:AS-B01","text":"The system confronts three forms of ungoverned reason: private assent mistaken for truth, accumulated cognition mistaken for science, and inherited dispute mistaken for philosophical progress.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:AS1","CH_B337_CI_2:AS1","CH_B337_CIHP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AS-B02","text":"Rational authority is reconstructed through coordinated distinctions among subjective and objective sufficiency, systemic position and end, and the object, origin, and method of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:AS2","CH_B337_CI_2:A3","CH_B337_CIHP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AS-B03","text":"Critique becomes productive by limiting speculative claims, policing architectonic boundaries, and replacing competition among doctrines with inquiry into admissible claims.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A15","CH_B337_CI_2:SD6","CH_B337_CIHP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AS-B04","text":"At this scale, the argument is simultaneously epistemic, architectonic, and historical: it differentiates rational authority, organizes its legitimate forms, and diagnoses their recurrent corruption.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:SD1","CH_B337_CI_2:AS3","CH_B337_CIHP:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AS-B05","text":"The movement passes from the correction of private assent through active rational possession to the collective construction of a critical route.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:AF1","CH_B337_CI_2:AF1","CH_B337_CIHP:AF6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AS-B06","text":"Repeated exclusions do not terminate reason but reassign its powers: theoretical denial enables practical belief, critical censorship preserves metaphysics, and failed alternatives disclose criticism.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:SD2","CH_B337_CI_2:SD6","CH_B337_CIHP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AS-B07","text":"The system moves recursively because reason cannot escape its metaphysical tendency; recurrent overreach requires renewed limitation and disciplined redistribution rather than a final elimination of error.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SNX4","CH_B337_CIHP:SD4","CH_B337_SIOK:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AS-B08","text":"Its dominant attractor is a practically required but theoretically unavailable unity in which moral ends, the ultimate end of reason, and nature and freedom would converge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:A12","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AS-B09","text":"Closure remains regulative and infrastructural rather than doctrinal: completed philosophy is absent, the critical route is only opened, and its continuation is collective.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SD8","CH_B337_CIHP:SNX4","CH_B337_CIHP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Reason acquires authority through disciplined self-limitation rather than through unrestricted speculative reach.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A15","CH_B337_CI_2:SD6","CH_B337_CIHP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] A cognition is objectively authoritative only when its grounds answer both to an object and to the legitimate source and scope of cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A4","CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_CIHP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Subjective sufficiency cannot constitute knowledge, but it can acquire bounded rational authority when practical action requires orientation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A8","CH_B337_SIOK:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Systematic unity is a condition both of scientific cognition and of any legitimate inquiry into pure reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A1","CH_B337_CIHP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Every cognition, discipline, and method receives its legitimate limits from its position within a reason-governed whole.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A3","CH_B337_CI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] The final practical end of reason subordinates other cognitive ends without converting that teleological priority into theoretical knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:A12","CH_B337_SIOK:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Restriction is the system’s principal productive operation: it preserves practical orientation, reorganizes metaphysics, and generates criticism as a method.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:SD2","CH_B337_CI_2:ME9","CH_B337_CIHP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Persistent metaphysical conflict is evidence of defective rational self-governance rather than of cumulative doctrinal progress.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:SD1","CH_B337_CI_2:SD2","CH_B337_CIHP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Metaphysics changes from an attempted expansion of supersensible knowledge into a critical institution that delimits and coordinates reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SD6","CH_B337_CI_2:ME9","CH_B337_CIHP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Rational autonomy requires the regeneration and testing of claims from principles; neither inward certainty nor inherited authority is sufficient.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A6","CH_B337_CI_2:SD3","CH_B337_CI_2:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Public reason consists in communicable and collectively reproducible procedures, not in private certainty or mere social agreement.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_CIHP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Nature and freedom remain distinct domains whose final unity is demanded by reason but withheld from theoretical completion.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX6","CH_B337_SIOK:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Supersensible objects cannot become theoretical knowledge; their surviving rational status is critical delimitation or practically necessary belief.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:NX7","CH_B337_SIOK:ME4","CH_B337_CIHP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] The absence of completed philosophy functions as a regulative authority that directs present inquiry toward collective continuation.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SD8","CH_B337_CIHP:SNX4","CH_B337_CIHP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:RMD-01","text":"Communicability first functions as a fallible test of objective assent and then expands into the collective reproducibility required of critical method.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_CIHP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RMD-02","text":"The subjective–objective distinction migrates from degrees of assent to modes of possessing knowledge and then to coherence between cognitive source and authorized scope.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:AS2","CH_B337_CI_2:ME4","CH_B337_CIHP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RMD-03","text":"Belief reverses from apparent epistemic deficiency into a legitimate but bounded orientation and is subsequently subordinated to the ultimate ends of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:ME2","CH_B337_CI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RMD-04","text":"Systematicity retains its identity while expanding from the internal condition of science to the compulsory form of inquiry into pure reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A1","CH_B337_CIHP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RMD-05","text":"Metaphysics reverses valence from an attempted foundation or speculative enlargement into a boundary-regulating institution.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:NX12","CH_B337_CI_2:ME9","CH_B337_CI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RMD-06","text":"History is displaced from chronological narration into a transcendental diagnostic that maps recurrent structures of rational failure.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:AS1","CH_B337_CIHP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RMD-07","text":"Philosophy mutates from a supposedly transmissible doctrine into autonomous activity, regulative archetype, and collective infrastructure.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:NX8","CH_B337_CI_2:D6","CH_B337_CIHP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RMD-08","text":"The unity of moral ends expands into a cosmical teleology under which all subordinate rational aims receive their position.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RMD-09","text":"Common reason divides into two contrary functions: universal access to practical orientation and illegitimate substitution for scientific method.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:D9","CH_B337_CIHP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:CGD-01","text":"The taxonomy of opinion, belief, and knowledge remains local machinery, while its scalable contribution is the principle that rational authority must be differentiated by kind and sufficiency.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:D3","CH_B337_SIOK:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:CGD-02","text":"The object–origin–method classification remains a historical diagnostic locally but scales upward when joined to the architectonic requirement that every cognition have a delimited position.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A3","CH_B337_CI_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:CGD-03","text":"The detailed division into ontology, rational physics, psychology, cosmology, and theology remains lower-layer taxonomy because its derivation from the ultimate end is not fully demonstrated.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A17","CH_B337_CI_2:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:CGD-04","text":"God and immortality remain local objects of moral belief, while the current-level structure retains them only as instances of practically authorized but noncognitive postulates.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_SIOK:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:CGD-05","text":"The contrast between technical collection and architectonic science scales upward into the distinction between accumulated doctrines and criticism as transformed method.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A5","CH_B337_CI_2:SD1","CH_B337_CIHP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:CGD-06","text":"Sensualism, intellectualism, empiricism, and noology become obsolete as candidate closures and survive only as classified failure modes.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A4","CH_B337_CIHP:A5","CH_B337_CIHP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:CGD-07","text":"Communicability, autonomous regeneration from principles, and collective continuation are transition catalysts that combine into the current-level structure of public criticism.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_CI_2:SD3","CH_B337_CIHP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:GNX-01","text":"Felt assurance is not an index of objective validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GNX-02","text":"An aggregate of cognitions is not a scientific system.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GNX-03","text":"Tradition, doctrinal mastery, and intellectual authority cannot substitute for autonomous rational possession.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:DNX3","CH_B337_CI_2:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GNX-04","text":"Speculative reason cannot establish knowledge of supersensible objects.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:NX5","CH_B337_CIHP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GNX-05","text":"Practical need cannot be converted into theoretical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:DNX2","CH_B337_SIOK:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GNX-06","text":"Common sense and the abandonment of scientific procedure cannot function as methods for extending metaphysical cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:NX6","CH_B337_CIHP:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:GNX-07","text":"Neither dogmatism nor scepticism can constitute the final methodological destiny of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:NX9","CH_B337_CIHP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:DBN-01","text":"The system requires that no neutral accumulation of true propositions can generate science without a rationally determining end.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:DNX1","CH_B337_CI_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:DBN-02","text":"It cannot admit an unexamined authority of reason or a standpoint outside the conditions of finite cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:SNX3","CH_B337_SIOK:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:DBN-03","text":"It excludes a unified epistemic status in which supersensible postulates are simultaneously known, practically necessary, and demonstrable.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:SNX1","CH_B337_SIOK:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:DBN-04","text":"Historical duration and accumulated labor cannot count as rational progress unless method itself is transformed.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:DNX3","CH_B337_CIHP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:DBN-05","text":"Specialized sciences cannot determine their final value independently of the ultimate end that orders the system of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:DNX5","CH_B337_CI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:DBN-06","text":"Moral agency cannot suspend action until metaphysical knowledge is complete.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:DNX3","CH_B337_SIOK:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSA-01","text":"The deepest excluded possibility is immediate access to ultimate reality from a standpoint unconstrained by sensibility, experience, or practical finitude.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:SNX1","CH_B337_SIOK:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable unity of nature and freedom structures the system as a horizon that must orient reason without becoming present knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SNX6","CH_B337_SIOK:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSA-03","text":"Completed philosophy is constitutively absent: it judges actual systems while remaining unavailable as a finished possession.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SNX1","CH_B337_CIHP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSA-04","text":"Permanent rational contentment is withheld and replaced by the construction and maintenance of a critical road.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:SNX2","CH_B337_CIHP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSA-05","text":"Reason cannot escape metaphysics or abolish its own dialectic; only recurrent critical government of that tendency remains possible.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SNX4","CH_B337_CIHP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:XD-01","text":"Rational authority becomes a differentiated architecture of functions rather than a single ascending scale of certainty.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-03","CL_B337_31:GA-05","CH_B337_SIOK:SD1","CH_B337_CI_2:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:XD-02","text":"Public reason is constituted by communicability, autonomous regeneration from principles, and collective reproducibility rather than by consensus alone.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-10","CL_B337_31:GA-11","CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_CI_2:SD3","CH_B337_CIHP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:XD-03","text":"Speculative failure is converted into an institutional function when limitation becomes boundary administration and the criterion of admissible answers.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-01","CL_B337_31:GA-07","CL_B337_31:GA-09","CH_B337_SIOK:SD2","CH_B337_CI_2:SD6","CH_B337_CIHP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:XD-04","text":"Practical belief can orient the architectonic whole toward its ultimate end without becoming a theoretical premise of that whole.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-03","CL_B337_31:GA-06","CL_B337_31:GA-13","CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:A11","CH_B337_SIOK:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:XD-05","text":"Because reason’s dialectical tendency recurs historically, critique must operate as permanent recursive maintenance rather than as a single completed correction.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-07","CL_B337_31:GA-08","CH_B337_CI_2:A18","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX4","CH_B337_CIHP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:XD-06","text":"The absence of completed philosophy transforms completion from individual possession into a collectively continued regulative project.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-11","CL_B337_31:GA-14","CH_B337_CI_2:SD8","CH_B337_CIHP:A12","CH_B337_CIHP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:XD-07","text":"Every metaphysical assertion must pass two distinct filters: the status of its sufficiency and the coherence between its cognitive source and claimed range.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-02","CL_B337_31:GA-03","CH_B337_SIOK:D3","CH_B337_CIHP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:XD-08","text":"Accumulation cannot produce progress when cognition lacks an immanent system and history reproduces an unresolved problem of self-government.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-04","CL_B337_31:GA-08","CH_B337_CI_2:SD4","CH_B337_CIHP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:XD-09","text":"Universal access to practical reason does not authorize common sense to function as a scientific metaphysical method.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-10","CL_B337_31:GA-11","CH_B337_SIOK:D9","CH_B337_CIHP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSD-01","text":"The rational subject becomes a finite self-governing agent who must test assent, regenerate principles, act without complete knowledge, and participate in a collective critical project.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-03","CL_B337_31:GA-10","CL_B337_31:GA-11","CH_B337_SIOK:A6","CH_B337_CI_2:SD3","CH_B337_CIHP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSD-02","text":"Validity becomes topological as well as epistemic: a claim must agree with its object and occupy a legitimate place within the system of reason.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-02","CL_B337_31:GA-05","CH_B337_SIOK:A4","CH_B337_CI_2:A3","CH_B337_CIHP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSD-03","text":"Rational progress is intensive, methodological, and collective rather than merely chronological or accumulative.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-04","CL_B337_31:GA-08","CL_B337_31:GA-11","CH_B337_CI_2:SD4","CH_B337_CIHP:SD4","CH_B337_CIHP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSD-04","text":"Metaphysics survives as a protocol for regulating transitions among cognition, belief, practical commitment, and historical method.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-03","CL_B337_31:GA-09","CH_B337_SIOK:SD1","CH_B337_CI_2:SD6","CH_B337_CIHP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSD-05","text":"Teleology supplies systemic orientation without supplying total theoretical closure: the final end coordinates the whole while its completed unity remains absent.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-06","CL_B337_31:GA-12","CL_B337_31:GA-14","CH_B337_CI_2:A12","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX6","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSD-06","text":"Epistemic authority is neither privately possessed nor socially inherited; it is publicly reproducible only through procedures constrained by rational limits.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-10","CL_B337_31:GA-11","CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_CI_2:DNX3","CH_B337_CIHP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:BSD-07","text":"The system produces a divided ontology in which empirical objectivity governs knowledge while the unity of nature and freedom governs practical orientation as an unavailable horizon.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-02","CL_B337_31:GA-12","CL_B337_31:GA-13","CH_B337_SIOK:A4","CH_B337_CI_2:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:RLE-01","text":"Private assurance generates a demand for communicability; communicability exposes persuasion; the resulting correction returns the subject to renewed examination of assent.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A6","CH_B337_SIOK:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RLE-02","text":"Reason’s demand for unity generates metaphysical excess; excess produces conflict; conflict requires critique; critique restores only a regulated form of unity.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SNX4","CH_B337_CIHP:SD1","CH_B337_CI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RLE-03","text":"Accumulated materials disclose a latent rational germ; the germ corrects inherited definitions; the reconstructed schema then exposes new omissions and arbitrary additions.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A6","CH_B337_CI_2:D3","CH_B337_CI_2:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RLE-04","text":"Historical oppositions escalate from disagreement about objects to incoherence between cognitive origin and scope, forcing a transformation of method.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:AS3","CH_B337_CIHP:D3","CH_B337_CIHP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RLE-05","text":"The denial of speculative knowledge becomes practical authorization; practical authorization becomes moral orientation; moral orientation becomes architectonic teleology.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:SD2","CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RLE-06","text":"Communicability, philosophizing from principles, and collective continuation converge on the attractor of a public critical infrastructure.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_CI_2:D6","CH_B337_CIHP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:RLE-07","text":"The absent completion of philosophy, the withheld unity of nature and freedom, and deferred rational contentment form a single regulative attractor.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SD8","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX6","CH_B337_CIHP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:FGS-B01","text":"Private assent + rhapsodic cognition + inherited doctrinal conflict → registration of ungoverned rational materials.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:AS1","CH_B337_CI_2:AS1","CH_B337_CIHP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:FGS-B02","text":"Subjective sufficiency / objective sufficiency + object-agreement + communicability → differentiated statuses of assent.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:AS2","CH_B337_SIOK:A4","CH_B337_SIOK:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:FGS-B03","text":"Rational idea + highest end + a priori position of parts → architectonic unity and scientific form.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A2","CH_B337_CI_2:A3","CH_B337_CI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:FGS-B04","text":"Object + origin + method + source–scope coherence → transcendental diagnosis of metaphysical systems.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:A3","CH_B337_CIHP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:FGS-B05","text":"Speculative excess + historical recurrence + failed alternatives → critical boundary administration.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SNX4","CH_B337_CIHP:SD1","CH_B337_CIHP:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:FGS-B06","text":"Theoretical insufficiency + practical necessity + ultimate end → bounded belief and moral orientation without knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A11","CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:FGS-B07","text":"Autonomous testing + communicable procedure + collective continuation + absent completion → recursive public criticism.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SD3","CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_CIHP:A12","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:AFM-B01","text":"Private certainty, accumulated cognition, and ruined philosophical inheritance → common diagnosis of reason lacking self-government.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:NX3","CH_B337_CI_2:DNX1","CH_B337_CIHP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AFM-B02","text":"Ungoverned reason → differentiation of subjective and objective sufficiency, aggregate and system, and object, origin, and method.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:AS2","CH_B337_CI_2:NX1","CH_B337_CIHP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AFM-B03","text":"Differentiation → tests of object-agreement, systemic position, and source–scope coherence.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A4","CH_B337_CI_2:A3","CH_B337_CIHP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AFM-B04","text":"Boundary tests → restriction of speculative cognition and reconstruction of metaphysics as critical regulation.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A15","CH_B337_CI_2:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AFM-B05","text":"Speculative restriction → practical belief → cosmical ordering by the ultimate end of reason.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:SD2","CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:AFM-B06","text":"Autonomous rational possession → communicable criticism → collectively maintained route under an absent completion.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SD3","CH_B337_CIHP:SD5","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:MME-01","text":"Certainty is redrawn as a relation among subjective sufficiency, objective validity, and practical necessity rather than as psychological intensity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:ME1","CH_B337_SIOK:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MME-02","text":"Science is reclassified as a teleologically organized system whose end determines its internal parts and limits.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:ME1","CH_B337_CI_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MME-03","text":"The history of metaphysics becomes a structured field of recurrent rational errors rather than a chronological accumulation of doctrines.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:ME1","CH_B337_CIHP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MME-04","text":"Metaphysics is relocated from speculative expansion to critical coordination of reason’s legitimate uses.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SD6","CH_B337_CI_2:ME9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MME-05","text":"Belief becomes a bounded rational mode that can guide action without entering the domain of knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:ME2","CH_B337_SIOK:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MME-06","text":"Publicness is redefined as reproducible critical procedure rather than unanimity, inherited authority, or common-sense immediacy.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A5","CH_B337_CI_2:DNX3","CH_B337_CIHP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MME-07","text":"Progress is redrawn as a change in rational method and systemic articulation rather than as duration, accumulation, or victory by one school.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:SD4","CH_B337_CI_2:SD4","CH_B337_CIHP:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system classifies assent, defines scientific architecture, and diagnoses the principal divisions of metaphysical history.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:AS3","CH_B337_CI_2:ALT1","CH_B337_CIHP:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, cognition is governed by linked filters of sufficiency, object-agreement, systemic position, cognitive origin, authorized scope, and method.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:AS2","CH_B337_CI_2:A3","CH_B337_CIHP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, critique redirects struggle from competition among doctrines toward governance of the rules that make claims admissible.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:SD3","CH_B337_CIHP:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, finite reason knows nature through bounded cognition, acts toward freedom, and projects a future unity that historical duration alone cannot realize.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:ALT4","CH_B337_CI_2:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX6","CH_B337_CIHP:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system enacts its claims by classifying, excluding, architectonically repositioning, and reopening its own completion as collective work.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:AS3","CH_B337_CIHP:D6","CH_B337_CIHP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:FL-01","text":"Communicability is only a presumptive test of objectivity, yet collective reproducibility bears much of the burden of stabilizing the critical route.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:ME3","CH_B337_CIHP:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:FL-02","text":"Architectonic divisions are presented as a priori and immutable even though their rational form emerges retrospectively from accumulated materials.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:MRT4","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:FL-03","text":"The absent completion of philosophy regulates actual inquiry despite the lack of a completed system capable of fully grounding that authority.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:MRT5","CH_B337_CI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:FL-04","text":"Subjective practical necessity authorizes belief in supersensible objects although subjective grounds are categorically insufficient for speculative validity.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:MRT5","CH_B337_SIOK:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:FL-05","text":"Criticism is established partly through the exhaustion of alternatives while its positive procedures and transition to collective completion remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:MRT5","CH_B337_CIHP:MRT3","CH_B337_CIHP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MP-01","text":"Certainty mutates from objective validity into moral certainty when practical conviction remains firm without becoming communicable knowledge.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:ME1","CH_B337_SIOK:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MP-02","text":"Metaphysics mutates from a speculative engine into a critical institution whose authority consists in regulation rather than discovery.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:ME9","CH_B337_CI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MP-03","text":"Common reason mutates from universally accessible practical orientation into misology when it claims methodological authority over metaphysics.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:D9","CH_B337_CIHP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MP-04","text":"History mutates from narration of past doctrines into a transcendental map of recurrent errors in reason’s self-government.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:AS1","CH_B337_CIHP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a divided but teleologically coordinated field in which empirical nature is knowable, freedom is practically legislating, and their final unity remains absent.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A13","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:HSI-02","text":"Reason implicitly functions as an architectonic tribunal that classifies claims, assigns their positions, and converts its own recurrent excesses into boundary rules.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:A3","CH_B337_CI_2:SD6","CH_B337_CIHP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:HSI-03","text":"The subject becomes a finite judge-builder who must distinguish persuasion from conviction, reconstruct cognition from principles, and help maintain a public critical road.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:NX1","CH_B337_CI_2:SD3","CH_B337_CIHP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:HSI-04","text":"Time is recursive rather than automatically progressive: unresolved divisions recur until methodological transformation interrupts their reproduction.","refs":["CH_B337_CIHP:SD4","CH_B337_CIHP:ALT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:HSI-05","text":"No escape from dialectic is available; navigation consists in repeated limitation, practical reorientation, and collective repair.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SNX4","CH_B337_SIOK:SD2","CH_B337_CIHP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_B337_31:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if the architecture preserves the distinction among epistemic status, systemic position, and historical-methodological admissibility.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:MRT1","CH_B337_CI_2:MRT1","CH_B337_CIHP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where limitation simultaneously disciplines assent, organizes science, and converts metaphysical failure into critical method.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:MRT2","CH_B337_CI_2:MRT2","CH_B337_CIHP:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MRT-B03","text":"The derivation of particular metaphysical divisions, moral postulates, and the positive procedures of criticism remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:MRT3","CH_B337_SIOK:MRT4","CH_B337_CIHP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent at this scale only if critical restriction is understood as productive redistribution rather than as sceptical termination.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:MRT7","CH_B337_CI_2:ME9","CH_B337_CIHP:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction must preserve recursive return: reason repeatedly generates excess, receives limits, reallocates authority, and reopens completion as future collective labor.","refs":["CH_B337_CI_2:SNX4","CH_B337_SIOK:SD4","CH_B337_CIHP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_B337_31:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable residue resistant to complete axiomatic reduction is the practical force by which moral necessity and an absent final unity orient reason without becoming objective cognition.","refs":["CH_B337_SIOK:A13","CH_B337_SIOK:SNX1","CH_B337_CI_2:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"Chapters 91–93","context":"Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief · Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason · Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason"}},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01","layer":"MC","title":"B337 L1 01","order":125,"parents":["CL_B337_01","CL_B337_02","CL_B337_03","CL_B337_04"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"3a71ed9fa748c8eececa76bb29146cdccccdd04cd17928ed104133d4bc4ee2d6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AS-01","text":"The governing problem is how cognition can begin with sensory affection yet contain necessary extensions that neither sensation nor conceptual analysis can supply.","refs":["CL_B337_01:AS-01","CL_B337_02:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AS-02","text":"Experience is recast as a constituted product whose object-directed order depends on cognitive contributions not derivable from its received materials.","refs":["CL_B337_01:AS-02","CL_B337_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AS-03","text":"Mathematics and theoretical science force a regression from actual necessary cognition to the conditions that make synthetic a priori cognition possible.","refs":["CL_B337_02:AS-04","CL_B337_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AS-04","text":"This regression redirects inquiry from independently considered objects to the sensible and conceptual conditions under which objects can be given and thought.","refs":["CL_B337_03:AS-B02","CL_B337_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AS-05","text":"The a priori consequently migrates into sensibility, where pure intuition supplies spatial and temporal forms antecedent to empirical ordering.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-06","CL_B337_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AS-06","text":"Subjective form becomes the ground of phenomenal objectivity because universally shared receptive conditions bind every possible human appearance.","refs":["CL_B337_04:BAS-04","CL_B337_04:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AS-07","text":"The same a priori power that secures necessary science becomes illegitimate when its bounded success is generalized into supersensible cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_01:AS-06","CL_B337_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AS-08","text":"The argument moves by recursive transcendental regression: unexplained necessity leads to enabling conditions, and those conditions return as limits on the cognition they enable.","refs":["CL_B337_01:RLE-05","CL_B337_04:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AS-09","text":"Closure is therefore conditional rather than doctrinal: empirical objectivity is secured, while positive metaphysical cognition beyond possible experience remains suspended.","refs":["CL_B337_02:AS-08","CL_B337_04:BAS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Sensory affection initiates cognition but does not exhaust the conditions through which experience becomes objective knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_01:GA-01","CL_B337_01:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Necessary cognitive extension cannot be generated either by analysis of contained concepts or by contingent empirical association.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GA-01","CL_B337_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] The possibility of synthetic a priori cognition organizes the relations among science, experience, metaphysics, and critique.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GA-03","CL_B337_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Human cognition acquires objective reference only through sensible intuition capable of giving an object to thought.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-02","CL_B337_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The a priori expands from experiential independence and modal necessity into the formal organization of sensibility itself.","refs":["CL_B337_01:RMD-02","CL_B337_03:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Space and time are irreducible forms of pure intuition through which externality and sensuous order become representable.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-06","CL_B337_04:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Experience is a conditioned domain whose objective order presupposes forms not derived from the empirical contents they organize.","refs":["CL_B337_01:GA-05","CL_B337_04:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Objectivity becomes conditional universality for appearances rather than cognition independent of every subjective condition.","refs":["CL_B337_03:XD-03","CL_B337_04:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] A priori necessity has legitimate objective reach only within the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_03:BSD-01","CL_B337_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Critique must determine reason’s authority, sources, and limits before positive metaphysical doctrine can be authorized.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-01","CL_B337_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Critical restriction is productive because exclusion of illegitimate derivations identifies the formal conditions that make valid cognition possible.","refs":["CL_B337_03:BSD-04","CL_B337_04:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] The thing in itself functions as a necessary limiting correlate but remains unavailable to positive spatial, temporal, or conceptual determination.","refs":["CL_B337_04:GA-08","CL_B337_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Transcendental analysis can articulate the functional structure of cognition without supplying a genetic account of its faculties or their common root.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-10","CL_B337_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-01","text":"[Mutation] Experience changes from the temporal occasion of cognition into a synthesized product, then into the bounded field whose possibility requires prior formal conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_01:RMD-01","CL_B337_02:RMD-06","CL_B337_04:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-02","text":"[Escalation] The a priori moves from independence from experience through necessity and universality to the receptive forms that determine its legitimate phenomenal scope.","refs":["CL_B337_01:RMD-02","CL_B337_03:RMD-02","CL_B337_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-03","text":"[Absorption] Synthesis begins as an unobserved cognitive contribution, becomes necessary noncontained connection, and is absorbed into the formal constitution of objective experience.","refs":["CL_B337_01:RMD-03","CL_B337_02:RMD-02","CL_B337_04:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-04","text":"[Mutation] Necessity changes from a diagnostic mark of nonempirical origin into the demand posed by synthetic judgment and finally into validity restricted to appearances.","refs":["CL_B337_01:AS-04","CL_B337_02:RMD-03","CL_B337_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-05","text":"[Domain Expansion] Intuition first grounds mathematical construction, then becomes the indispensable medium of object-givenness, space, time, and phenomenal order.","refs":["CL_B337_02:RMD-05","CL_B337_04:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-06","text":"[Valence Reversal] Subjective origin ceases to imply private arbitrariness and becomes the source of universally valid phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CL_B337_03:RMD-06","CL_B337_04:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-07","text":"[Functional Reversal] Metaphysics changes from attempted supersensible enlargement into the object whose possibility and limits critique must first determine.","refs":["CL_B337_02:RMD-07","CL_B337_03:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-08","text":"[Phase Change] Contradiction and exclusion cease to be merely negative tests and become productive diagnostics of cognitive overreach and legitimate conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_02:RMD-04","CL_B337_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-09","text":"[Terminal Emergence] The thing in itself appears only after phenomenal validity is secured and persists as a negative boundary preventing transcendental conditions from becoming ontological predicates.","refs":["CL_B337_04:RMD-07","CL_B337_04:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-10","text":"[Stable Identity / Absorption] Conceptual containment retains its identity as the test of analytic judgment but loses its status as a productive model of necessary cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_02:RMD-01","CL_B337_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Bodies, causal predicates, arithmetic constructions, and geometrical magnitudes remain demonstrations; their higher-order function is to expose heterogeneous forms of nonempirical conditioning.","refs":["CL_B337_01:CGD-01","CL_B337_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:CGD-02","text":"[Bridge] Mathematical intuition remains domain-specific, but it scales upward by proving that conceptual analysis cannot produce substantive necessity.","refs":["CL_B337_02:CGD-02","CL_B337_03:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:CGD-03","text":"[Superseded] The pure–impure classification of a priori cognition becomes subordinate to the stronger distinction between phenomenal validity and illegitimate transcendent application.","refs":["CL_B337_01:CGD-04","CL_B337_04:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:CGD-04","text":"[Scaling] The isolated spatial proof becomes current-level architecture only when coordinated with time as a second irreducible form of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_04:CGD-01","CL_B337_04:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:CGD-05","text":"[Catalyst] Contradictory metaphysical systems do not supply doctrine; they catalyze the transition from dogmatic construction to critical jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_02:CGD-07","CL_B337_02:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:CGD-06","text":"[Underdetermined] Possible forms of intuition belonging to non-human knowers remain outside the architecture and cannot be generalized from human sensibility.","refs":["CL_B337_04:CGD-06","CL_B337_04:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:CGD-07","text":"[Terminal Limit] The unknown common root of sensibility and understanding does not bridge into a genetic theory; it remains the boundary of functional reconstruction.","refs":["CL_B337_03:XD-06","CL_B337_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GNX-01","text":"Cognition cannot be reduced to sensory reception or experience treated as the exhaustive source of its objective form.","refs":["CL_B337_01:GNX-01","CL_B337_01:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GNX-02","text":"Repeated observation cannot become necessity merely through accumulation, frequency, or unrestricted empirical generalization.","refs":["CL_B337_01:GNX-02","CL_B337_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GNX-03","text":"Conceptual explication, logical consistency, and deductive validity cannot generate the content of synthetic cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GNX-01","CL_B337_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GNX-04","text":"Spatial and temporal forms cannot be copied from or attributed to independently constituted things.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GNX-05","CL_B337_04:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GNX-05","text":"Subjective origin cannot be equated with private arbitrariness, and objectivity cannot be equated with perspective-free ontology.","refs":["CL_B337_03:RMD-06","CL_B337_04:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:GNX-06","text":"Successful a priori science cannot provide blanket authorization for cognition of supersensible objects.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GNX-05","CL_B337_01:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"The architecture collapses if experience is self-grounding or exhaustively determines the forms through which it becomes intelligible.","refs":["CL_B337_04:DBN-01","CL_B337_01:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"Necessary noncontained predicates cannot arise from conceptual analysis or become necessary through empirical repetition.","refs":["CL_B337_02:DBN-01","CL_B337_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"Formal consistency cannot establish objective reach, and a priori validity cannot automatically authorize unrestricted application.","refs":["CL_B337_02:DBN-03","CL_B337_01:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"Reason cannot be treated as self-authorizing, because the power judging its limits is also the power producing the overreach under judgment.","refs":["CL_B337_01:DBN-04","CL_B337_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:DBN-05","text":"A functional account of cognitive conditions cannot be converted into a genetic ontology of the faculties that enact them.","refs":["CL_B337_03:DBN-04","CL_B337_03:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:DBN-06","text":"Human forms of sensibility cannot be projected onto every possible knower without violating the critical restriction that establishes them.","refs":["CL_B337_04:DBN-03","CL_B337_04:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"No standpoint outside sensibility is available from which appearances could be compared with objects as they are independently of appearance.","refs":["CL_B337_03:BSA-01","CL_B337_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"The system excludes the unavailable possibility of cognition that is wholly empirical yet necessary, universal, and self-grounding.","refs":["CL_B337_01:BSA-01","CL_B337_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"The independently existing object remains indispensable as a limiting correlate while being unavailable as a positive foundation for human cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_03:BSA-02","CL_B337_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSA-04","text":"The architecture circles an unavailable self-grounding unity in which reason would know both its complete system and the common origin of its heterogeneous powers.","refs":["CL_B337_03:BSA-04","CL_B337_03:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSA-05","text":"Non-human intuition marks an inaccessible outside that prevents human transcendental conditions from becoming an ontology of cognition as such.","refs":["CL_B337_04:BSA-04","CL_B337_04:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:XD-01","text":"Distinguishing temporal origin from epistemic source converts the actuality of necessary cognition into a regression toward nonempirical conditions rather than a denial that cognition begins in experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-03","CL_B337_01:XD-01","CL_B337_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:XD-02","text":"Established sciences warrant transcendental investigation of their possibility but do not authorize the extension of their validity to supersensible objects.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-09","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-10","CL_B337_02:AS-04","CL_B337_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:XD-03","text":"The joint failure of conceptual analysis and empirical association positively identifies intuition as an irreducible condition of synthetic necessity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-04","CL_B337_02:AS-02","CL_B337_04:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:XD-04","text":"Once object-givenness and synthetic necessity are combined, pure intuition must function not merely as a mathematical aid but as a condition of possible experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-07","CL_B337_03:XD-04","CL_B337_04:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:XD-05","text":"Subjective form can ground public objectivity only by binding every possible human appearance while remaining inapplicable to things independently of sensibility.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-08","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-11","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-12","CL_B337_03:RMD-06","CL_B337_04:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:XD-06","text":"The a priori contribution that makes experience possible also generates speculative danger when its enabling function is mistaken for unrestricted cognitive authority.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-09","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-10","CL_B337_01:XD-04","CL_B337_01:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:XD-07","text":"Critical exclusion becomes productive when the rejection of empirical, conceptual, and realist derivations isolates the formal source of valid cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-11","CL_B337_03:XD-01","CL_B337_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:XD-08","text":"Reason’s tribunal remains structurally circular because reason must authorize the critical procedure necessitated by its own tendency toward contradiction.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-13","CL_B337_02:FL-04","CL_B337_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:XD-09","text":"Coordinating space and time converts the phenomenon–thing distinction into a methodological boundary governing the whole domain of possible experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-08","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-12","CL_B337_04:FGS-B04","CL_B337_04:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"Epistemology becomes transcendental jurisdiction: its primary task is to determine the conditions and scope under which cognitive extension can count as objectively necessary.","refs":["CL_B337_02:BSD-01","CL_B337_03:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"Objectivity becomes invariance across the formal conditions of human experience rather than independence from every contribution of the subject.","refs":["CL_B337_04:BSD-01","CL_B337_03:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"The subject becomes a finite constituting interface that receives affection, supplies formal order, and cannot create objects through thought alone.","refs":["CL_B337_03:HSI-02","CL_B337_04:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"Time is divided between the empirical succession in which cognition occurs and the a priori medium that makes succession phenomenally representable.","refs":["CL_B337_01:HSI-03","CL_B337_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"Cognitive limitation becomes a positive epistemic condition because restricting knowledge to appearances secures the necessity and validity of empirical science.","refs":["CL_B337_02:MME-05","CL_B337_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSD-06","text":"Metaphysics becomes unavoidable as a demand of reason but conditional as knowledge, legitimate only when reconstructed as inquiry into possible representation.","refs":["CL_B337_02:BSD-04","CL_B337_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSD-07","text":"Explanatory adequacy is redefined generatively: an account succeeds by showing how the cognition it analyzes becomes possible and necessary.","refs":["CL_B337_04:GA-09","CL_B337_04:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:BSD-08","text":"Rational agency becomes internally divided between productive synthesis and self-limitation, with neither function sufficient independently of the other.","refs":["CL_B337_02:BSD-02","CL_B337_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"Sensory affection produces synthesized experience; analysis then returns through that experience to isolate the contribution that made it possible.","refs":["CL_B337_01:RLE-01","CL_B337_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"Successful science initiates a loop from actual necessity to its conditions of possibility and back to a restricted validation of scientific cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_02:XD-02","CL_B337_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"Synthetic necessity leads to pure intuition, pure intuition to subjective form, and subjective form back to objectively binding experience.","refs":["CL_B337_04:RLE-01","CL_B337_04:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"Dogmatic metaphysical extension produces contradiction, and contradiction returns reason to critique rather than terminating a priori cognition altogether.","refs":["CL_B337_02:AS-07","CL_B337_02:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"Reason recursively investigates the legitimacy of the same powers through which that investigation is conducted.","refs":["CL_B337_01:BSD-05","CL_B337_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RLE-06","text":"The thing in itself operates as a negative attractor that repeatedly interrupts the conversion of phenomenal conditions into claims about independent reality.","refs":["CL_B337_04:RLE-05","CL_B337_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:RLE-07","text":"Conditioned legitimacy and synthetic a priori cognition form the coupled attractor toward which analyses of experience, science, sensibility, and metaphysics converge.","refs":["CL_B337_01:RLE-04","CL_B337_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"Sensory affection activates cognition, while synthesis converts received material into composite experience.","refs":["CL_B337_01:AS-01","CL_B337_01:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Composite experience is decomposed into empirical occasion, cognitive contribution, modal validity, and legitimate scope.","refs":["CL_B337_01:AS-03","CL_B337_01:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"The analytic–synthetic distinction isolates necessary noncontained connection as the unresolved form of genuine cognitive extension.","refs":["CL_B337_01:CGD-05","CL_B337_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Mathematics and pure natural science establish that such extension is actual, while their restricted success blocks immediate metaphysical generalization.","refs":["CL_B337_02:GA-06","CL_B337_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Transcendental regression redirects inquiry from objects to their conditions and isolates pure form from empirical sensation and discursive determination.","refs":["CL_B337_03:AS-B02","CL_B337_03:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Pure form differentiates into space and time as the receptive conditions of externality and sensuous order.","refs":["CL_B337_03:GA-06","CL_B337_04:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FGS-B07","text":"Shared formal conditions generate phenomenal objectivity and synthetic necessity for every object of possible human experience.","refs":["CL_B337_04:GA-06","CL_B337_04:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FGS-B08","text":"The machine closes by preserving empirical validity while withholding positive spatial, temporal, or metaphysical cognition of the thing in itself.","refs":["CL_B337_04:GA-08","CL_B337_04:BAS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Sensory initiation → composite experience → discrepancy between temporal beginning and epistemic source.","refs":["CL_B337_01:AS-01","CL_B337_01:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Composite experience → experiential independence → necessity and strict universality as marks of a priori cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_01:AS-03","CL_B337_01:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"A priori necessity → synthetic noncontainment → the problem of necessary cognitive extension.","refs":["CL_B337_02:AS-03","CL_B337_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AFM-B04","text":"Actual mathematics and natural science → transcendental regression toward conditions of possibility.","refs":["CL_B337_02:CGD-05","CL_B337_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AFM-B05","text":"Transcendental regression → priority of object-givenness → isolation of sensible form.","refs":["CL_B337_03:AS-B02","CL_B337_03:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AFM-B06","text":"Pure intuition → space and time → formal constitution of externality and succession.","refs":["CL_B337_04:BAS-03","CL_B337_04:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AFM-B07","text":"Formal constitution → phenomenal objectivity → empirical reality joined to transcendental ideality.","refs":["CL_B337_04:GA-06","CL_B337_04:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:AFM-B08","text":"Transcendental ideality → noumenal boundary → critical suspension of unrestricted metaphysical construction.","refs":["CL_B337_01:FGS-B06","CL_B337_04:BAS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MME-01","text":"Knowledge is remapped across distinct axes of temporal origin, constitutive source, modal validity, intuitive form, and legitimate jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_01:MME-01","CL_B337_02:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MME-02","text":"The empiricism–rationalism opposition is displaced by the distinction between legitimate synthetic conditioning and illegitimate transcendent extension.","refs":["CL_B337_02:MME-06","CL_B337_04:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MME-03","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from mind-independence into universally valid determination within the conditions of possible appearance.","refs":["CL_B337_01:BSD-04","CL_B337_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MME-04","text":"The subject–object opposition becomes a dependency circuit in which affection supplies material while formal receptivity makes objective appearance possible.","refs":["CL_B337_03:HSI-01","CL_B337_04:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MME-05","text":"Space and time are reclassified from encountered structures or independent entities into forms that make externality and succession representable.","refs":["CL_B337_04:MME-01","CL_B337_04:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MME-06","text":"Science becomes evidence for transcendental conditions, while metaphysics becomes a conditional inquiry into the possibility and limits of cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_02:MME-03","CL_B337_03:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MME-07","text":"Explanation is transformed from causal or genetic derivation into reconstruction of the conditions required for a cognition’s possibility and necessity.","refs":["CL_B337_04:GA-09","CL_B337_04:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MME-08","text":"Limitation is redrawn as an enabling operation: the boundary that blocks noumenal knowledge secures empirical validity.","refs":["CL_B337_02:XD-06","CL_B337_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface level] The explicit field contains empirical cognition, analytic and synthetic judgments, mathematics, natural science, sensibility, space, time, and the disputed scientific status of metaphysics.","refs":["CL_B337_04:BAS-02","CL_B337_02:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural level] Cognition operates as a dependency system joining affection, receptivity, intuition, synthesis, conceptual determination, and bounded object-reference.","refs":["CL_B337_01:GA-02","CL_B337_04:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic level] Inquiry replaces doctrinal assertion with regression, decomposition, exclusion, and jurisdictional testing of cognitive claims.","refs":["CL_B337_02:ALT-B03","CL_B337_03:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal level] Reality for human cognition is phenomenal order within space and time, while the thing in itself remains outside positive determination.","refs":["CL_B337_04:BSD-03","CL_B337_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative level] The architecture performs its thesis by repeatedly converting negation into positive condition and by withholding the final grounding it prohibits itself from claiming.","refs":["CL_B337_01:ALT-B05","CL_B337_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FL-01","text":"Reason must possess enough authority to judge its powers even though its unregulated use is the source of the contradictions requiring judgment.","refs":["CL_B337_02:FL-04","CL_B337_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FL-02","text":"Synthetic a priori cognition is established as actual before its complete positive mechanism has been supplied.","refs":["CL_B337_02:FL-01","CL_B337_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FL-03","text":"Pure intuition is claimed to precede empirical objects even though the method identifies it through abstraction from empirical cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_03:FL-03","CL_B337_03:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FL-04","text":"Universal phenomenal validity is grounded in subjective sensibility, but the transition from subjective source to public necessity remains functionally stronger than genetically explained.","refs":["CL_B337_04:FL-01","CL_B337_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FL-05","text":"The thing in itself is required to delimit appearance while every positive route to its determination is prohibited.","refs":["CL_B337_04:FL-03","CL_B337_04:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FL-06","text":"Architectonic aspiration demands systematic completeness while the inaccessible common root prevents complete genetic closure.","refs":["CL_B337_03:FL-01","CL_B337_03:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:FL-07","text":"Affection and a priori form must cooperate in one cognition, yet their coordination is specified functionally rather than derived from a common source.","refs":["CL_B337_03:FL-02","CL_B337_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MP-01","text":"The a priori mutates from a source classification into a modal test, a receptive form, and finally a rule governing legitimate jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_01:RMD-02","CL_B337_03:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MP-02","text":"Necessity mutates from evidence of nonempirical origin into a demand for synthetic grounding and then into domain-relative phenomenal validity.","refs":["CL_B337_01:FL-01","CL_B337_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Objectivity mutates from presumed mind-independence into universally binding determination under subjective formal conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_04:MP-01","CL_B337_04:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MP-04","text":"Critique mutates from negative restriction into the productive procedure that isolates conditions, regulates reason, and preserves empirical knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_03:MP-01","CL_B337_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a conditioned interface in which received affection becomes objective appearance through nonempirical forms that never appear as ordinary contents.","refs":["CL_B337_03:HSI-01","CL_B337_04:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite two-source operator: receptive enough to be affected, formative enough to order appearance, and reflexive enough to police its own overreach.","refs":["CL_B337_01:HSI-02","CL_B337_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is both the order of empirical occurrence and the singular formal medium within which phenomenal succession can differentiate.","refs":["CL_B337_01:HSI-03","CL_B337_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"Intellectual struggle is navigation among sterile analysis, contingent association, productive intuition, phenomenal validity, and transcendent overreach.","refs":["CL_B337_02:HSI-04","CL_B337_04:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:HSI-05","text":"The thing in itself is the machine’s dark exterior: required to prevent appearance from becoming absolute, but unavailable as a destination of cognitive escape.","refs":["CL_B337_04:BSA-02","CL_B337_04:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from experiential initiation through synthetic necessity and pure intuition to bounded phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CL_B337_03:MRT-B01","CL_B337_04:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where it separates logical form, cognitive source, intuitive condition, and legitimate scope without permitting substitution among them.","refs":["CL_B337_02:MRT-B04","CL_B337_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the full mechanism of synthetic a priori cognition and the genetic unity of sensibility and understanding.","refs":["CL_B337_02:MRT-B02","CL_B337_03:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"The system is coherent only if subjective form, empirical objectivity, and transcendental limitation remain distinct but mutually dependent registers.","refs":["CL_B337_03:MRT-B04","CL_B337_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction requires recursive sequencing because each limitation must return as an enabling condition rather than remain a merely negative prohibition.","refs":["CL_B337_02:MRT-B05","CL_B337_04:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_01:MRT-B06","text":"The thing in itself remains indispensable yet resistant to axiomatic reduction because its structural function depends precisely on the absence of positive content.","refs":["CL_B337_04:FL-03","CL_B337_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"B337 L1 01","context":"Chapters 01–12 · I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge · II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”. · III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori” · IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements. · V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles. · VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason. · VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason. · § I. Introductory. · § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception. · § 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space. · § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions. · § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception."}},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02","layer":"MC","title":"B337 L1 02","order":126,"parents":["CL_B337_05","CL_B337_06","CL_B337_07","CL_B337_08"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"006a8ad791b57f0da7eaf7272a3345bd645f0ef8394b0a88be75340777f900cb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AS-01","text":"The system seeks to secure necessary objective cognition for a finite subject without converting appearances into things as they are independently or thought into a self-sufficient source of objects.","refs":["CL_B337_06:AS-01","CL_B337_08:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AS-02","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as the bounded result of sensible givenness, spatial-temporal ordering, conceptual determination, and systematic unity rather than direct access to independent being.","refs":["CL_B337_05:AS-B02","CL_B337_06:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AS-03","text":"Sensibility and understanding must remain functionally irreducible because one gives ordered appearances while the other supplies rules for thinking them.","refs":["CL_B337_06:AS-04","CL_B337_07:ASB-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AS-04","text":"Every cognitive condition acquires legitimate authority only by simultaneously establishing the boundary beyond which its use becomes empty, dialectical, or metaphysically illicit.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-04","CL_B337_07:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AS-05","text":"Formal consistency and pure conceptual order are necessary for cognition but cannot produce material truth without possible intuitive givenness.","refs":["CL_B337_07:ASB-03","CL_B337_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AS-06","text":"The inquiry therefore moves from collecting representations to reconstructing the faculties, rules, and anticipated totality that make their systematic placement possible.","refs":["CL_B337_08:AS-02","CL_B337_08:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AS-07","text":"Its movement is recursively differentiating and recompositive: it separates forms, faculties, domains, and uses, then reunites them under restricted conditions of objective validity.","refs":["CL_B337_06:AS-07","CL_B337_07:ASB-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AS-08","text":"Dialectical illusion is structurally productive because the failure of detached formal conditions feeds back into a stricter account of their legitimate employment.","refs":["CL_B337_07:ASB-06","CL_B337_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AS-09","text":"Closure remains architectural and transitional: the field of valid cognition is bounded and systematically divided, while affection, synthesis, and the governing rule of conceptual completeness remain compressed.","refs":["CL_B337_06:AS-08","CL_B337_08:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Finite cognition requires the non-substitutable cooperation of receptive sensibility and spontaneous understanding.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-01","CL_B337_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Space and time are a priori intuitive forms that order sensible givenness without becoming properties of independently existing things.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GA-01","CL_B337_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Pure understanding is an endogenous source of conceptual form and order but cannot independently supply objects, intuitive matter, or material truth.","refs":["CL_B337_07:ASB-04","CL_B337_08:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Objective validity is an achieved relation among sensible form, intuitive matter, conceptual determination, and possible application rather than an intrinsic possession of any isolated component.","refs":["CL_B337_06:AS-02","CL_B337_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Every condition enabling cognition also determines the jurisdiction within which that cognition is legitimate.","refs":["CL_B337_06:AS-06","CL_B337_06:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Formal conformity is necessary for truth but insufficient for objective correspondence and synthetic material cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_07:ASB-03","CL_B337_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Critical cognition functions canonically by testing claims, while dialectic arises whenever those conditions of judgment are converted into an organon that purports to produce objects.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GA-06","CL_B337_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] A priori origin establishes neither unrestricted scope nor objective use; legitimacy remains indexed to the representational conditions under which application is possible.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-08","CL_B337_07:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Systematic completeness requires an antecedent unity or totality that assigns every pure conception its necessary place.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-05","CL_B337_08:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Experience must be distinguished as the matter and horizon of objective application from its subordinate role as the occasion through which pure structures become manifest.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-04","CL_B337_08:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] The finite subject is divided between spontaneous conceptual activity and receptive, temporally ordered self-appearance.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-09","CL_B337_06:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Transcendental inquiry becomes a recursive jurisdictional method that differentiates cognitive powers, reconstructs their cooperation, and restricts every resulting claim to possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GA-10","CL_B337_07:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Systematic closure can specify the architecture and limits of pure cognition before it has fully exhibited the mechanisms joining faculties or deriving every conceptual element.","refs":["CL_B337_06:AS-08","CL_B337_08:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"Time retains its identity as an a priori form, expands into the internal medium of self-affection, and is finally displaced into the developmental field where nonempirical structures become manifest.","refs":["CL_B337_05:RMD-01","CL_B337_06:HSI-03","CL_B337_08:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"Subjectivity reverses from an apparent restriction on objectivity into its enabling condition and then expands into the self-organizing unity of conceptual order.","refs":["CL_B337_05:RMD-04","CL_B337_06:RMD-04","CL_B337_08:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"Pure intuition migrates from the condition of phenomenal presentation into the synthetic increment required by judgment and is then absorbed as one non-substitutable term of cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_06:RMD-02","CL_B337_06:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"Pure understanding changes from an isolated conceptual source into a restricted object-directed power and finally into the unity governing systematic placement.","refs":["CL_B337_07:RLE-02","CL_B337_08:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"Objectivity is displaced from independent object-nature into a relation whose validity depends on formal ordering, conceptual determination, and possible givenness.","refs":["CL_B337_05:RMD-05","CL_B337_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"The a priori mutates from a mark of nonempirical origin into a question of bounded application and then into a principle of systematic determinability.","refs":["CL_B337_07:RMD-03","CL_B337_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-07","text":"Experience changes function across the architecture: it supplies objective matter, is excluded as the origin of pure structures, and returns as their developmental occasion.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GA-05","CL_B337_08:RMD-03","CL_B337_08:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-08","text":"Purity changes from a boundary excluding empirical derivation into a recursive operation that removes empirical conditions from conceptions activated in experience.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GA-08","CL_B337_08:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-09","text":"Completeness undergoes a phase change from quantitative inclusion to principled placement and finally to internal systemic validation.","refs":["CL_B337_08:RMD-02","CL_B337_08:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-10","text":"Dialectic reverses from the appearance of mere error into a feedback mechanism that exposes detached formal authority and restores jurisdictional limits.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GA-07","CL_B337_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"The line analogy, geometry, and natural-theological contrasts remain local proof-machines; they support but do not generate the meta-cluster’s architecture of bounded cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_05:RMD-07","CL_B337_06:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"The logical compatibility of alteration scales upward only when temporal succession is integrated into the distinction between phenomenal validity and absolute ontology.","refs":["CL_B337_05:CGD-01","CL_B337_05:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"Inner sense bridges temporal form and the wider cognitive architecture by showing how spontaneous activity becomes receptively available without yielding noumenal self-knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_05:CGD-04","CL_B337_06:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"Detailed faculty and logical taxonomies remain local until their distinctions are recoded as non-substitutable functions within cooperative cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_06:CGD-04","CL_B337_06:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"The canon–organon distinction scales upward because it converts local logical restrictions into a general test for every claim to cognitive production.","refs":["CL_B337_07:CGD-04","CL_B337_07:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:CGD-06","text":"The anticipated inventory of pure concepts remains underdetermined, while the requirement that every legitimate concept possess a systematic position becomes globally load-bearing.","refs":["CL_B337_07:CGD-05","CL_B337_08:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:CGD-07","text":"Germinal conceptual development remains local machinery, but the distinction between pure origin and experiential occasion becomes a general account of manifestation without empirical constitution.","refs":["CL_B337_08:CGD-02","CL_B337_08:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:CGD-08","text":"The division between conceptions and principles is not the system’s demonstrated completion but its terminal architectural partition.","refs":["CL_B337_08:CGD-06","CL_B337_08:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"Time cannot be admitted as an independent object, substance, container, or property of things in themselves.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GNX-01","CL_B337_05:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"Inner consciousness cannot provide immediate access to the subject as it is independently of temporal self-appearance.","refs":["CL_B337_05:GNX-05","CL_B337_06:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"Neither concepts alone nor pure general logic can generate synthetic objective cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GNX-01","CL_B337_06:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"Necessity and systematic completeness cannot be derived from empirical accumulation, repeated observation, or resemblance.","refs":["CL_B337_06:GNX-02","CL_B337_08:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"Purity and a priori origin cannot certify unrestricted objective validity.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GNX-02","CL_B337_06:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GNX-06","text":"No formal or transcendental canon may be converted into an organon that manufactures objects, matter, or metaphysical knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GNX-03","CL_B337_07:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GNX-07","text":"Hyperphysical objects and things in themselves cannot become objectively knowable by transferring sensible or conceptual conditions beyond possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_07:BSA-03","CL_B337_05:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:GNX-08","text":"Systematic order cannot rest on arbitrary classification, indefinite expandability, chance, or external similarity.","refs":["CL_B337_08:GNX-05","CL_B337_08:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"No unconditioned standpoint outside every possible form of intuition can validate cognition while remaining available to finite knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_05:DBN-04","CL_B337_06:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"No finite faculty can simultaneously receive an object, originate it, and think it as objectively known.","refs":["CL_B337_06:DBN-02","CL_B337_06:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"No universal formal criterion can certify material truth because its universality is achieved by abstracting from differentiated object-content.","refs":["CL_B337_07:DBN-01","CL_B337_07:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:DBN-04","text":"No independent comparison between appearances and things in themselves can ground the system’s distinction between phenomenal validity and unavailable ontology.","refs":["CL_B337_06:DBN-01","CL_B337_06:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:DBN-05","text":"No external accumulation or standpoint can complete a faculty whose boundary and internal relations must be generated from its own unity.","refs":["CL_B337_08:DBN-01","CL_B337_08:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:DBN-06","text":"The architecture collapses if formal conditions are treated as empirical content or if pure faculties are treated as autonomous sources of objective objects.","refs":["CL_B337_05:DBN-05","CL_B337_07:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"The deepest excluded possibility is finite intellectual intuition in which giving, thinking, creating, and knowing an object coincide.","refs":["CL_B337_06:BSA-01","CL_B337_07:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"The system circles an unavailable absolute outside from which all object-directed cognitions could be compared and universally certified.","refs":["CL_B337_05:BSA-02","CL_B337_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"Transparent metacognition is inadmissible because the subject cannot simultaneously condition experience, know itself noumenally, and display the completed union of intuition and conception.","refs":["CL_B337_06:BSA-03","CL_B337_06:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSA-04","text":"A complete conceptual order arising without prior unity, totality, or governing principle is treated as impossible.","refs":["CL_B337_08:BSA-05","CL_B337_08:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSA-05","text":"Immediate self-presence remains absent: spontaneous activity returns only as temporally ordered appearance, while the outside of appearance remains a limit-image.","refs":["CL_B337_05:HSI-04","CL_B337_06:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:XD-01","text":"Temporal form is the sensible infrastructure of conceptual objectivity because every representation must be internally ordered before understanding can determine it as an object.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-04","CL_B337_05:AS-B04","CL_B337_06:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:XD-02","text":"Synthetic necessity is possible only because pure intuition contributes nonconceptual form while restriction to possible experience prevents that contribution from becoming unrestricted ontology.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-05","CL_B337_05:AS-B06","CL_B337_06:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:XD-03","text":"The understanding can systematically order pure concepts only by remaining dependent on an intuitive domain it neither generates nor replaces.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-09","CL_B337_07:RLE-02","CL_B337_08:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:XD-04","text":"Experience has a double but noncontradictory role: it supplies the matter of objective cognition while merely occasioning the manifestation of pure conceptual structures.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-10","CL_B337_07:GA-05","CL_B337_08:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Dialectical error occurs when a necessary condition of thought is elevated into a sufficient ground of objects, and critique repairs the transition by restoring the condition’s limited jurisdiction.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-07","CL_B337_07:XD-04","CL_B337_07:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:XD-06","text":"The subject is self-organizing without being self-originating: it supplies conceptual unity but receives both objects and its own appearance under sensible conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-11","CL_B337_06:XD-05","CL_B337_08:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:XD-07","text":"Systematic placement can establish the internal legitimacy of pure concepts but cannot by itself establish their objective applicability without possible intuition.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-09","CL_B337_08:DBN-05","CL_B337_07:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:XD-08","text":"The architecture produces a stratified temporality in which pure forms are nonempirical in origin yet temporally manifested through affection, self-appearance, and experiential development.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-11","CL_B337_08:BSD-02","CL_B337_08:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes a conditional phenomenal field in which sensible forms and pure concepts jointly organize objects without disclosing their independent constitution.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-04","CL_B337_05:HSI-01","CL_B337_07:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"Necessity is jurisdictional rather than absolute because the architecture that generates universal validity also restricts that validity to its enabling conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-08","CL_B337_06:BSD-02","CL_B337_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"Time acquires three coordinated functions: sequencing alteration, mediating receptive self-appearance, and providing the developmental field for nonempirical conceptual manifestation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-11","CL_B337_05:RMD-01","CL_B337_08:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"The finite subject becomes a divided interface and organizing system rather than either a passive substance or a sovereign creator of objects.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-11","CL_B337_06:BSD-04","CL_B337_08:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Error becomes structurally recurrent because valid formal powers can detach from the intuitive conditions that alone give them objective employment.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-07","CL_B337_07:BSD-03","CL_B337_06:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-06","text":"Transcendental method becomes a negative-positive machine that constructs bounded objectivity by excluding empirical derivation, faculty substitution, and organonic overreach.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-12","CL_B337_08:BSD-04","CL_B337_06:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-07","text":"Systematic architecture can test the legitimacy of its elements, but this self-validation remains provisional wherever its governing derivation or connection to intuition is compressed.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-09","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-13","CL_B337_08:BSD-01","CL_B337_08:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"Alteration requires succession, succession requires temporal form, and temporal form acquires empirical validity through the alterations it makes representable.","refs":["CL_B337_05:RLE-01","CL_B337_05:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"Faculty differentiation preserves distinct cognitive functions, while their recombination generates cognition and retrospectively justifies the differentiation.","refs":["CL_B337_06:RLE-03","CL_B337_06:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"The validity–limitation loop converts every enabling condition into a restriction and every restriction into a safeguard of objective validity.","refs":["CL_B337_06:RLE-02","CL_B337_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Formal abstraction produces law, law treated as productive knowledge generates illusion, and dialectical critique returns law to canonical use.","refs":["CL_B337_07:RLE-01","CL_B337_07:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"Empirical collection produces incompleteness, incompleteness demands a governing principle, and the resulting system intensifies the exclusion of collection as its ground.","refs":["CL_B337_08:RLE-01","CL_B337_08:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-06","text":"Experience is excluded as origin, reintroduced as occasion, and recursively purified from the structures whose manifestation it enables.","refs":["CL_B337_08:RLE-02","CL_B337_08:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-07","text":"The whole determines the placement of its parts, while the articulated parts retroactively test the totality that anticipated them.","refs":["CL_B337_08:RLE-03","CL_B337_08:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-08","text":"Possible experience and principle-governed completeness form the joint attractor: one bounds objective application, while the other organizes the pure conditions operating within that boundary.","refs":["CL_B337_06:RLE-05","CL_B337_08:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Affection generates receptive givenness without yet producing objective cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_06:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Space and time order sensible matter and provide the nonconceptual forms required for synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_06:FGS-B02","CL_B337_05:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Understanding contributes conceptual rules that determine given intuition without generating its matter.","refs":["CL_B337_06:FGS-B03","CL_B337_07:ASB-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Cooperative synthesis produces empirically objective phenomena through the non-substitutable union of intuitive and conceptual conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_06:FGS-B04","CL_B337_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"The representational condition enabling each cognition fixes the domain within which that cognition can claim validity.","refs":["CL_B337_06:FGS-B05","CL_B337_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FGS-B06","text":"Abstraction from content generates formal logic, while retention of restricted object-relation generates transcendental analysis.","refs":["CL_B337_07:FGS-B02","CL_B337_07:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FGS-B07","text":"Detachment of formal conditions from possible givenness converts valid constraints into dialectical assertions about unavailable objects.","refs":["CL_B337_07:FGS-B04","CL_B337_07:ASB-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FGS-B08","text":"A governing unity assigns pure concepts their places and terminates the architecture in the systematic division between conceptions and principles.","refs":["CL_B337_08:FGS-B04","CL_B337_08:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Opposed determinations → temporal succession → pure temporal form converts alteration into representable phenomenal order.","refs":["CL_B337_05:FGS-B01","CL_B337_05:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Affection → receptive intuition → spatial-temporal ordering → conceptual determination generates finite objective cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_06:RLE-01","CL_B337_06:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Formal conformity + possible intuitive givenness → objective legitimacy establishes the double constraint on truth-capable cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_07:XD-03","CL_B337_06:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AFM-B04","text":"A priori condition → bounded application → canon–organon distinction → dialectical diagnosis transforms cognitive power into critical jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GA-02","CL_B337_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AFM-B05","text":"Failed empirical collection → faculty unity → governing principle → necessary placement reconstructs conceptual completeness as architecture.","refs":["CL_B337_08:FGS-B02","CL_B337_08:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:AFM-B06","text":"Systematic articulation → division into conceptions and principles → compressed derivation yields provisional rather than exhaustive closure.","refs":["CL_B337_08:FGS-B06","CL_B337_08:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MME-01","text":"The opposition between reality and ideality is replaced by the distinction between empirically valid appearances and unavailable independent being.","refs":["CL_B337_05:MME-01","CL_B337_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MME-02","text":"The opposition between subjectivity and objectivity collapses because subjective cognitive conditions become the basis of non-private phenomenal validity.","refs":["CL_B337_05:MME-02","CL_B337_06:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MME-03","text":"The source of cognition and the scope of its legitimate application become separable, dissolving the inference from a priori origin to unlimited reach.","refs":["CL_B337_06:MME-02","CL_B337_07:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MME-04","text":"Logic is redrawn as a differentiated jurisdiction comprising formal constraint, transcendental conditions of objectivity, and dialectical critique.","refs":["CL_B337_07:MME-01","CL_B337_07:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MME-05","text":"Truth is redistributed between formal admissibility and material correspondence, with transcendental analysis specifying the conditions under which correspondence can claim objective standing.","refs":["CL_B337_07:MME-02","CL_B337_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MME-06","text":"Metaphysical overreach becomes a structural category error rather than a mere false belief: a valid condition is mistaken for a sufficient source of objects.","refs":["CL_B337_07:MME-04","CL_B337_07:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MME-07","text":"Conceptual plurality and completeness are reclassified from inventory and quantity into internally generated placement and necessary relation.","refs":["CL_B337_08:MME-01","CL_B337_08:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, time, intuition, understanding, truth, logical divisions, pure concepts, and systematic completeness form the explicit object-field.","refs":["CL_B337_06:ALT-B01","CL_B337_08:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, receptive givenness, pure sensible form, conceptual spontaneity, objective application, and systematic unity operate as mutually restricted components.","refs":["CL_B337_06:ALT-B02","CL_B337_08:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, interpretation shifts from inventorying objects and concepts to reconstructing enabling conditions, jurisdictions, and failures of legitimate application.","refs":["CL_B337_06:ALT-B03","CL_B337_07:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is phenomenally ordered under sensible conditions, while pure conceptual origin remains distinct from temporal manifestation.","refs":["CL_B337_06:ALT-B04","CL_B337_08:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, recursive differentiation, exclusion, purification, recombination, and delimitation enact the critical method being articulated.","refs":["CL_B337_06:ALT-B05","CL_B337_08:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FL-01","text":"An unknowable affecting ground is indispensable to receptive givenness, yet the relation through which it affects sensibility remains unexplained.","refs":["CL_B337_06:FL-01","CL_B337_06:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Intuition and conception must unite for cognition, but the architecture does not yet supply the mechanism accomplishing their union.","refs":["CL_B337_06:FL-02","CL_B337_06:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FL-03","text":"Pure understanding must be self-contained in origin while remaining unable to obtain objective content without intuition and experience.","refs":["CL_B337_07:FL-01","CL_B337_08:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FL-04","text":"The system risks circular self-certification because anticipated totality determines legitimate parts while those parts provide the only available test of totality.","refs":["CL_B337_08:FL-04","CL_B337_08:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:FL-05","text":"Time must be a subjective condition universally necessary for appearances while also functioning as the developmental medium of structures whose origin is declared nonempirical.","refs":["CL_B337_05:FL-01","CL_B337_08:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Subjectivity mutates from a restriction on metaphysical access into the positive condition of phenomenal objectivity.","refs":["CL_B337_06:RMD-04","CL_B337_06:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MP-02","text":"Transcendentality mutates from a classification by a priori origin into an inquiry concerning possibility, application, use, and objective jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GA-02","CL_B337_07:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MP-03","text":"Experience mutates from excluded source into necessary objective matter and developmental occasion without becoming the constitutive origin of pure forms.","refs":["CL_B337_08:RMD-03","CL_B337_08:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MP-04","text":"Completeness mutates from possessing all elements into determining their necessary relations and places through a prior whole.","refs":["CL_B337_08:RMD-02","CL_B337_08:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MP-05","text":"Dialectic mutates from the misuse of formal logic into a general diagnosis of hyperphysical extension and a recursive mechanism of critical correction.","refs":["CL_B337_07:GA-07","CL_B337_07:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly modeled as a bounded transformation machine in which affection becomes ordered intuition, intuition becomes conceptual cognition, and cognition becomes systematically regulated validity.","refs":["CL_B337_06:HSI-01","CL_B337_08:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a divided interface that receives appearances, generates conceptual order, judges claims, and organizes pure structures without creating its objects.","refs":["CL_B337_06:HSI-02","CL_B337_07:HSI-02","CL_B337_08:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is the sequencing and developmental medium through which alteration, self-affection, and conceptual manifestation become available without time becoming absolute being.","refs":["CL_B337_05:HSI-03","CL_B337_06:HSI-03","CL_B337_08:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"Knowledge appears as a gated architecture whose elements become legitimate only when their systematic position and possible objective use coincide.","refs":["CL_B337_08:BSD-01","CL_B337_08:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:HSI-05","text":"Critical struggle consists in navigating jurisdictions and preventing the recurrent conversion of valid conditions into claims about unavailable objects.","refs":["CL_B337_05:HSI-05","CL_B337_07:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from temporal form and sensible givenness through faculty cooperation, logical restriction, and systematic conceptual closure.","refs":["CL_B337_06:MRT-B01","CL_B337_08:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"The system is coherent at this scale if sensible form, conceptual determination, objective application, and systematic unity remain distinct but operationally coordinated.","refs":["CL_B337_06:MRT-B03","CL_B337_07:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined at the interfaces of affection, intuition–conception synthesis, and derivation of the governing conceptual rule.","refs":["CL_B337_06:MRT-B02","CL_B337_08:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where enabling conditions become jurisdictional limits and where totality, faculty unity, and principled completeness converge.","refs":["CL_B337_06:MRT-B04","CL_B337_08:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if differentiation precedes cooperation, restriction accompanies validity, and systematic closure emerges through recursive return rather than linear accumulation.","refs":["CL_B337_06:MRT-B05","CL_B337_07:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_02:MRT-B06","text":"Local demonstrations of synthetic necessity and the concrete derivation of the conceptual system remain resistant to complete axiomatic reduction, so closure depends on compression and anticipatory architecture.","refs":["CL_B337_06:MRT-B06","CL_B337_08:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"B337 L1 02","context":"Chapters 13–24 · § 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time. · § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions. · § 8. Elucidation. · § 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic. · § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic. · I. Of Logic in General. · II. Of Transcendental Logic. · III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic. · IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic. · First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1 · BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2 · Introductory § 3"}},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03","layer":"MC","title":"B337 L1 03","order":127,"parents":["CL_B337_09","CL_B337_10","CL_B337_11","CL_B337_12"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"10c3c619e22b8455cd137d23a06b818534aef1ca7a0bd2051aa80ad1b3cc62ff","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AS-01","text":"The system must both derive the pure conceptual structure of cognition and establish its entitlement to objective employment.","refs":["CL_B337_09:AS-B01","CL_B337_10:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AS-02","text":"Categories become necessary forms of object-thought only when they are shown to condition the possibility of experience.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-09","CL_B337_10:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AS-03","text":"Finite cognition arises from the non-interchangeable cooperation of receptive material and spontaneous synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-02","CL_B337_11:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AS-04","text":"The derivation regresses beneath judgment and categories toward the prior unity that makes conjunction and understanding possible.","refs":["CL_B337_10:AS-B07","CL_B337_11:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AS-05","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as the necessary conjunction of a manifold for one consciousness rather than as immediate object-presence.","refs":["CL_B337_11:AS-B05","CL_B337_12:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AS-06","text":"The movement is recursive: logical form becomes categorical synthesis, and synthesized intuition returns as the ground of logical form’s objective validity.","refs":["CL_B337_12:AS-B05","CL_B337_12:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AS-07","text":"Restriction to possible experience is the positive condition of categorical authority, not an external curtailment of otherwise unlimited cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_10:BSD-03","CL_B337_12:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AS-08","text":"Closure remains conditional because the necessity of the architecture terminates in unexplained completeness claims and factical cognitive forms.","refs":["CL_B337_09:BSD-08","CL_B337_12:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AS-09","text":"The argument becomes a juridical-transcendental regression that withdraws explanatory authority from objects, empirical genesis, and analysis in order to reconstruct their enabling conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_10:BSD-05","CL_B337_11:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Finite discursive cognition requires an independently received manifold and cannot replace reception with intellectual intuition.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-01","CL_B337_12:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Judgment is the comprehensive unifying operation whose logical functions furnish the formal patterns of categories.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-03","CL_B337_12:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Categories change from a systematically derived inventory into rules by which intuition becomes thinkable as an object.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-09","CL_B337_10:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] Synthesis precedes analysis and supplies the conjunction that neither receptivity, objects, nor empirical association can furnish.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-06","CL_B337_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Original apperceptive unity enables synthesis, representational belonging, concept-formation, and object-relation without itself being a category or empirical representation.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GA-08","CL_B337_11:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Objective validity is the necessary unification of a given manifold under apperception within possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_11:GA-05","CL_B337_12:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Empirical repetition, association, and temporal succession cannot generate objective necessity or strict universality.","refs":["CL_B337_10:AS-B06","CL_B337_10:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-08","text":"[Terminal] Categorical authority is coextensive with its restriction to objects of possible finite experience.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GA-10","CL_B337_12:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] The cognitive subject changes from a distributed faculty-circuit into the non-substantial formal relay through which manifold representations become one cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_09:BSD-02","CL_B337_11:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The cognizable object is neither antecedently self-unified nor freely produced, but is the unity attained when given material is synthesized lawfully.","refs":["CL_B337_10:BSD-01","CL_B337_12:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Functional necessity does not yield a genetic explanation of the exact forms, number, or human configuration of cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-08","CL_B337_12:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Judgmental unity, synthetic unity, and apperceptive unity recur as non-identical articulations of one recursive architecture.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-08","CL_B337_11:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Systematic derivation establishes a concept’s place in the primitive inventory, whereas transcendental deduction establishes its objective right of use.","refs":["CL_B337_09:BSD-03","CL_B337_10:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-01","text":"[Mutation] Judgment changes from conceptual mediation into the operative synthesis through which sensible manifold acquires objective unity.","refs":["CL_B337_09:RMD-02","CL_B337_12:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-02","text":"[Expansion] Unity expands from conceptual subsumption into a stratified architecture of conjunction, self-consciousness, and object-validity.","refs":["CL_B337_09:RMD-01","CL_B337_10:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-03","text":"[Functional recurrence] Synthesis moves from imaginative conjunction to the universal condition that also grounds empirical association.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-07","CL_B337_11:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] The object changes from an apparently antecedent given into the cognition-available unity of a manifold, while its existence remains unproduced by synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_10:RMD-02","CL_B337_12:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-05","text":"[Displacement] Categories are displaced beneath their own judgmental operation as derivative from prior unity, then return as constitutive rules of empirical cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_10:RMD-05","CL_B337_12:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-06","text":"[Terminal transformation] The subject ceases to function as a sovereign faculty-holder and becomes a non-substantial relay distributed across reception, synthesis, and unity.","refs":["CL_B337_09:BSD-02","CL_B337_11:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-07","text":"[Reinsertion] Logical form is first abstracted from content and then reinserted as an operative rule for synthesizing sensible material.","refs":["CL_B337_09:RMD-03","CL_B337_12:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-08","text":"[Escalation] Necessity migrates from the local problem of causality into the constitutive-restrictive criterion governing all transcendental validity.","refs":["CL_B337_10:RMD-06","CL_B337_10:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-09","text":"[Terminal boundary] Intuitive understanding disappears as a positive cognitive model after defining finite cognition through contrast.","refs":["CL_B337_11:CGD-04","CL_B337_12:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-10","text":"[Absorption] Empirical association remains only as a subordinated phenomenal field and an excluded alternative that renders objective synthesis discriminable.","refs":["CL_B337_11:RMD-05","CL_B337_12:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:CGD-01","text":"The individual judgment-forms remain lower-layer determinations; what scales upward is the claimed correspondence between judgmental completeness and categorical completeness.","refs":["CL_B337_09:CGD-04","CL_B337_12:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:CGD-02","text":"Space and time remain local forms of givenness and scale upward only by demonstrating that intuition supplies manifold without objective conjunction.","refs":["CL_B337_10:CGD-01","CL_B337_11:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:CGD-03","text":"Historical disputes over empirical concept-genesis remain local formulations of the wider distinction between factual acquisition and objective warrant.","refs":["CL_B337_10:CGD-02","CL_B337_10:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:CGD-04","text":"The identity linking judgmental function, synthesis, and apperception is the principal bridge across logical, sensible, and objective registers.","refs":["CL_B337_09:CGD-06","CL_B337_12:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:CGD-05","text":"Prior unity is the catalytic transition that converts a derivation of categories into a regression beneath categorical operations.","refs":["CL_B337_10:CGD-05","CL_B337_10:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:CGD-06","text":"Empirical catalogues and associative accounts become obsolete once completeness and validity are relocated to systematic derivation and necessary synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_09:CGD-10","CL_B337_11:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:CGD-07","text":"The unexplained number and origin of the forms scale upward only as boundary conditions on the deduction, not as positive components of its proof.","refs":["CL_B337_12:CGD-05","CL_B337_12:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:CGD-08","text":"Predicable expansion remains peripheral at this level; its surviving function is to show that finite primitive closure can support derivative productivity.","refs":["CL_B337_09:CGD-07","CL_B337_09:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GNX-01","text":"Finite understanding is defined against intellectual intuition and cannot secure immediate cognition of objects.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GNX-01","CL_B337_11:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GNX-02","text":"Neither pure receptivity nor unconditioned spontaneity can independently constitute cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GNX-02","CL_B337_12:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GNX-03","text":"Logical form and categorical unity cannot validate themselves without accounting for sensible conjunction.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GNX-03","CL_B337_10:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GNX-04","text":"Objective validity is not empirical origin, associative regularity, or subjective expectation intensified into necessity.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GNX-01","CL_B337_11:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GNX-05","text":"Subjective synthesis does not create object-existence, even though it is necessary for objecthood within cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GNX-05","CL_B337_12:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GNX-06","text":"Categories possess no legitimate unrestricted employment beyond possible finite experience.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GNX-06","CL_B337_12:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GNX-07","text":"Apperception is neither an empirical observer nor an aggregate of representations.","refs":["CL_B337_11:DBN-01","CL_B337_12:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:GNX-08","text":"Conditions of cognition cannot be converted into determinations of things in themselves.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GNX-07","CL_B337_09:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:DBN-01","text":"The architecture cannot admit a direct correspondence model in which an independently available object validates cognition from outside cognitive conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_09:DBN-02","CL_B337_10:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:DBN-02","text":"Psychological occurrence cannot be transformed into objective necessity by accumulation, regularity, or genealogy.","refs":["CL_B337_09:DBN-04","CL_B337_10:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:DBN-03","text":"A self-sufficient formalism identifying logical validity with object-determination must be false.","refs":["CL_B337_09:DBN-03","CL_B337_12:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:DBN-04","text":"Both a wholly preconstituted object and a wholly cognition-created object are excluded.","refs":["CL_B337_11:DBN-02","CL_B337_12:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:DBN-05","text":"The subject cannot be an absolutely self-grounding source because spontaneity remains dependent on receptive material and prior unity.","refs":["CL_B337_09:DBN-06","CL_B337_10:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:DBN-06","text":"Unity cannot be homogeneous without collapsing the differences among synthetic, categorical, apperceptive, and objective functions.","refs":["CL_B337_10:BSA-03","CL_B337_11:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:DBN-07","text":"The system rejects the false alternative between unrestricted metaphysics and skepticism because neither supplies an external guarantee of cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_10:MME-06","CL_B337_10:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSA-01","text":"The deepest unavailable possibility is an intuitive understanding for which receptivity, synthesis, judgment, and deduction would be unnecessary.","refs":["CL_B337_09:BSA-01","CL_B337_12:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSA-02","text":"The cognition-independent thing in itself remains unavailable because no standpoint outside possible experience can inspect correspondence directly.","refs":["CL_B337_09:BSA-02","CL_B337_10:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSA-03","text":"The exact ground of the category-table and human cognitive forms remains absent beneath their asserted necessity.","refs":["CL_B337_09:BSA-03","CL_B337_12:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSA-04","text":"No independently prior term resolves the mutual dependence between unity and synthesis without reopening the architecture’s regress.","refs":["CL_B337_11:FL-02","CL_B337_10:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSA-05","text":"Absolute cognitive self-grounding is ontologically inadmissible because cognition cannot occupy a standpoint from which its own conditions are exhaustively derived.","refs":["CL_B337_12:BSA-01","CL_B337_12:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:XD-01","text":"A systematic derivation can identify the primitive categories without yet establishing their objective right, so metaphysical deduction and transcendental deduction perform non-substitutable tasks.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-13","CL_B337_09:GA-10","CL_B337_10:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:XD-02","text":"Logical functions acquire objective force only when their unity is reconstructed as categorical synthesis under apperception.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-12","CL_B337_09:GA-08","CL_B337_11:GA-08","CL_B337_12:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:XD-03","text":"Possible experience does not merely delimit an independently valid category-system; it is the synthesized field through which categorical validity is generated.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-08","CL_B337_10:GA-02","CL_B337_11:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:XD-04","text":"Categories are simultaneously derivative from prior unity and constitutive of empirical cognition, making foundation and operation asymmetrical rather than contradictory.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-12","CL_B337_10:RMD-05","CL_B337_12:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:XD-05","text":"Restriction to finite experience grants categorial authority by specifying the only domain in which necessary object-determination is possible.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-08","CL_B337_10:XD-04","CL_B337_11:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:XD-06","text":"Completeness divides into two burdens: exhaustion of the logical functions and justification of their necessary application to sensible objects.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-13","CL_B337_09:FL-01","CL_B337_12:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:XD-07","text":"The architecture avoids both direct realism and unrestricted construction because the manifold is independently given while its objectual unity is synthesized.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-10","CL_B337_10:GNX-05","CL_B337_12:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:XD-08","text":"Analysis can isolate logical form only within a field whose representations have already been synthetically joined.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-04","CL_B337_09:GA-06","CL_B337_09:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:XD-09","text":"Empirical association becomes the derivative enactment and contrast-case of transcendental synthesis rather than its origin or proof.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-07","CL_B337_11:BSD-03","CL_B337_12:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity becomes a jurisdictionally bounded achievement of rule-governed synthesis rather than an intrinsic property of representations.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-08","CL_B337_10:BSD-01","CL_B337_12:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes a formally identical but non-substantial site through which received multiplicity is rendered accountable as one cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-09","CL_B337_11:BSD-01","CL_B337_11:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-03","text":"The experiential world is neither simply given nor freely made; it is a lawful order produced by coupling manifold givenness to necessary synthesis.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-10","CL_B337_11:HSI-01","CL_B337_12:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-04","text":"Transcendental necessity has a constitutive-restrictive form in which lawful scope and objective authority are the same structure.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-08","CL_B337_10:BSD-03","CL_B337_10:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-05","text":"Logic becomes recursively dependent on the apperceptive unity that its own judgment-functions operationalize as categories.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-12","CL_B337_09:RLE-01","CL_B337_10:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-06","text":"The proof-architecture is productively regressive: categories lead back to synthesis and apperception, which then return as the grounds of categorial validity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-06","CL_B337_10:BSD-05","CL_B337_12:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-07","text":"The conditions of knowledge include their own opacity because functional necessity does not eliminate the limit of genetic explanation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-11","CL_B337_12:BSD-05","CL_B337_12:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-08","text":"Abstraction from empirical genesis secures transcendental necessity only by preserving the facticity of the forms through which that necessity operates.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-11","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-13","CL_B337_12:XD-05","CL_B337_12:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RLE-01","text":"The principal loop runs from understanding to judgment, from judgment to categories, from categories to synthesis, from synthesis to apperception, and from apperception back to objective judgment.","refs":["CL_B337_09:RLE-01","CL_B337_12:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RLE-02","text":"The juridical loop moves from possession through demand for objective right to restriction of authorized use.","refs":["CL_B337_10:RLE-01","CL_B337_10:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RLE-03","text":"Unity is the dominant attractor because every multiplicity—conceptual, intuitive, subjective, or objective—is returned to a condition of necessary conjunction.","refs":["CL_B337_09:RLE-03","CL_B337_11:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RLE-04","text":"The regressive escalation from categories to synthesis and prior unity repeatedly prevents explanation from terminating in logical understanding alone.","refs":["CL_B337_10:XD-06","CL_B337_10:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RLE-05","text":"The form-content loop abstracts logical form from material content and then returns form to content as its rule of objective organization.","refs":["CL_B337_09:RLE-04","CL_B337_12:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RLE-06","text":"Negative escalation removes immediate intuition, empirical necessity, subjective association, and unrestricted use until a positive architecture of synthesis appears.","refs":["CL_B337_09:BSD-05","CL_B337_12:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RLE-07","text":"Pure synthesis grounds empirical association, while empirical association supplies the phenomenal field in which transcendental dependence becomes visible.","refs":["CL_B337_11:RLE-03","CL_B337_11:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:RLE-08","text":"Closure remains generative because a finite primitive system produces derivative expansion while reopening the question of its own ultimate ground.","refs":["CL_B337_09:RLE-07","CL_B337_10:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B01","text":"Finite non-intuitive understanding → dependence on a manifold received through sensibility.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-01","CL_B337_11:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B02","text":"Abstraction from judgment-content → systematic logical table → candidate inventory of pure categories.","refs":["CL_B337_09:AS-B04","CL_B337_09:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B03","text":"Possession of pure categories → separation of systematic title from objective right.","refs":["CL_B337_10:AS-B01","CL_B337_10:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B04","text":"Rejection of empirical affection and psychological regularity → demand for a priori conditions of object-cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_10:AS-B02","CL_B337_10:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B05","text":"Sensible manifold without conjunction → spontaneous synthesis as the missing operation.","refs":["CL_B337_10:AS-B03","CL_B337_10:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B06","text":"Synthesis of manifold → presupposition of one possible self-consciousness → original apperceptive unity.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GA-08","CL_B337_11:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B07","text":"Apperceptive synthesis → judgment-functions transformed into categorical determinations of intuition.","refs":["CL_B337_12:GA-02","CL_B337_12:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B08","text":"Categorically unified manifold → object-related cognition → validation through possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_10:AS-B05","CL_B337_11:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B09","text":"Restriction to possible experience → bounded closure → unresolved facticity of the cognitive forms.","refs":["CL_B337_10:AS-B08","CL_B337_12:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AFM-B01","text":"Intellectual intuition denied → understanding restricted to discursive reception and mediation.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GNX-01","CL_B337_09:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AFM-B02","text":"Conceptual mediation → judgment as the comprehensive operation of understanding.","refs":["CL_B337_09:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AFM-B03","text":"Judgmental classification → logical table → derivation of candidate categories.","refs":["CL_B337_09:GA-09","CL_B337_09:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AFM-B04","text":"Possession of categories → juridical demand for objective entitlement.","refs":["CL_B337_10:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AFM-B05","text":"Manifold givenness without conjunction → spontaneous synthesis by understanding.","refs":["CL_B337_10:AS-B03","CL_B337_11:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AFM-B06","text":"Synthesis → presupposed apperceptive unity → one consciousness of the manifold.","refs":["CL_B337_11:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AFM-B07","text":"Apperceptive unity → categorical determination → objectivity within experience.","refs":["CL_B337_11:AS-B05","CL_B337_12:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AFM-B08","text":"Objective validity → restriction to possible experience → positive authorization of categories.","refs":["CL_B337_10:GA-10","CL_B337_10:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:AFM-B09","text":"Bounded validation → explanatory termination in the non-deducible facticity of cognitive forms.","refs":["CL_B337_12:AS-B06","CL_B337_12:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MME-01","text":"Cognition is remapped from a direct subject-object relation into the necessary structure of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_09:MME-01","CL_B337_10:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MME-02","text":"The subject is reclassified from an empirical bearer or sovereign creator into the formal unity of representational belonging.","refs":["CL_B337_11:MME-01","CL_B337_11:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MME-03","text":"The object is reclassified from an independently received unity into a cognition-available manifold unified under necessary synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_11:MME-02","CL_B337_12:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MME-04","text":"Logic divides into formal classification and transcendental reconstruction, while operative synthesis supplies the bridge between them.","refs":["CL_B337_09:MME-04","CL_B337_12:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MME-05","text":"Categories become jurisdictional rules for cognizability rather than inherited concepts or ontological powers.","refs":["CL_B337_10:BSD-04","CL_B337_10:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MME-06","text":"Cognitive limitation is reclassified as the internal condition of legitimate necessity, dissolving the opposition between dogmatism and total skepticism.","refs":["CL_B337_10:BSD-03","CL_B337_10:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MME-07","text":"Completeness becomes dual: genealogical derivation fixes primitive membership, while juridical deduction fixes authorized employment.","refs":["CL_B337_09:BSD-03","CL_B337_10:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MME-08","text":"Metaphysical reading must trace recursive conditions and systematic placement rather than accept empirical genesis or linear enumeration as proof.","refs":["CL_B337_09:MME-07","CL_B337_11:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface level] The explicit problem concerns whether categories can possess objective validity for a manifold that becomes an object only through synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_10:ALT-B01","CL_B337_12:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural level] Sensibility supplies manifold, understanding synthesizes it categorically, and apperception provides the necessary unity of the result.","refs":["CL_B337_10:ALT-B02","CL_B337_12:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic level] Transcendental deduction replaces empirical genealogy with determination of the lawful scope of cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_10:ALT-B03","CL_B337_10:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal level] Objects are available only within synthesized experience, and neither temporal succession nor coexistence yields objective necessity by itself.","refs":["CL_B337_10:ALT-B04","CL_B337_11:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative level] The system performs its thesis by repeatedly withdrawing authority from empirical facts and reconstructing the conditions that organize them.","refs":["CL_B337_09:ALT-B05","CL_B337_11:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FL-01","text":"The category-system inherits an unproved claim that the four logical headings exhaust all judgmental functions.","refs":["CL_B337_09:FL-01","CL_B337_09:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FL-02","text":"The identity of logical and intuitive unity is indispensable, but the transition from judgment-form to sensible determination remains compressed.","refs":["CL_B337_09:FL-02","CL_B337_12:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FL-03","text":"Prior unity and synthetic activity mutually require one another, preventing either from functioning as a transparent first ground.","refs":["CL_B337_11:FL-02","CL_B337_10:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FL-04","text":"Synthesis is constitutive of cognition without producing existence, leaving unsettled whether it constitutes objecthood or only access to an antecedent object.","refs":["CL_B337_10:FL-01","CL_B337_12:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FL-05","text":"Objectivity is independent of the empirical subject while remaining grounded in transcendental self-consciousness.","refs":["CL_B337_12:FL-01","CL_B337_11:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FL-06","text":"The forms are claimed as a priori necessary even though their exact number and configuration terminate in facticity.","refs":["CL_B337_12:FL-04","CL_B337_12:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:FL-07","text":"The division between imagination’s synthesis and understanding’s determination is necessary but mechanically under-specified.","refs":["CL_B337_09:FL-04","CL_B337_11:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MP-01","text":"Judgment mutates from conceptual predication into the operative synthesis that unifies sensible manifold.","refs":["CL_B337_09:MP-01","CL_B337_12:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MP-02","text":"Unity mutates from a logical category into a stratified relation among formal identity, synthetic conjunction, apperception, and objective validity.","refs":["CL_B337_10:MP-03","CL_B337_11:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MP-03","text":"The category mutates from a derivative logical form into a constitutive rule while remaining dependent on a prior unity.","refs":["CL_B337_10:MP-04","CL_B337_12:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MP-04","text":"Restriction reverses from apparent deprivation into the positive mechanism authorizing finite categorial cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_10:XD-04","CL_B337_12:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:HSI-01","text":"Reality for finite cognition is a recursive synthesis engine coupling manifold reception to spontaneous rules before an object can appear.","refs":["CL_B337_09:HSI-01","CL_B337_12:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a distributed, non-substantial relay that receives multiplicity and returns it as accountable unity.","refs":["CL_B337_09:HSI-02","CL_B337_11:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:HSI-03","text":"Time supplies succession but not its objective order or necessity, which emerge only through synthetic unification.","refs":["CL_B337_10:HSI-04","CL_B337_11:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:HSI-04","text":"Cognitive navigation is juridical: every claimed concept must be assigned a lawful scope and tested as a rule of cognizability.","refs":["CL_B337_10:HSI-03","CL_B337_10:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:HSI-05","text":"Escape does not mean reaching an external standpoint; it means systematic self-limitation within a finite architecture that cannot ground itself absolutely.","refs":["CL_B337_09:HSI-05","CL_B337_12:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms suffice to reconstruct the passage from logical classification through transcendental warrant to apperceptively unified object-cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_10:MRT-B01","CL_B337_12:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined concerning the completeness of the judgment-table, the individual categories, and the ultimate origin of the forms.","refs":["CL_B337_09:MRT-B02","CL_B337_12:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if logical identity, synthetic activity, apperceptive unity, objective validity, and object-existence remain distinct but recursively connected.","refs":["CL_B337_11:MRT-B04","CL_B337_10:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where one necessary unity links self-consciousness, object-relation, judgment, and categorical validity.","refs":["CL_B337_11:MRT-B02","CL_B337_12:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining the recursive return from judgmental form through synthesis and apperception to objective validity.","refs":["CL_B337_09:MRT-B05","CL_B337_10:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MRT-B06","text":"The element most resistant to axiomatic reduction is the compressed identity through which logical judgment becomes sensible determination.","refs":["CL_B337_09:MRT-B06","CL_B337_12:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_03:MRT-B07","text":"The system depends on sequencing and compression where tabular determinacy masks the unresolved higher ground of unity.","refs":["CL_B337_09:BSA-03","CL_B337_10:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"B337 L1 03","context":"Chapters 25–36 · Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4 · Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5 · Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6 · Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9 · Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10 · Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11. · Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12 · The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13 · What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14 · The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15 · All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16 · Observation § 17"}},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04","layer":"MC","title":"B337 L1 04","order":128,"parents":["CL_B337_13","CL_B337_14","CL_B337_15","CL_B337_16"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"632948dacce58027cecab6cace948519439baccb527c1b2b63ac74a97ec955ef","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AS-01","text":"The governing problem is how pure thought can acquire objective force while remaining incapable of extending cognition beyond its sensible jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_15:AS-B01","CL_B337_16:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AS-02","text":"Possible experience solves this problem by functioning simultaneously as the ground of objective validity and the restriction on legitimate conceptual use.","refs":["CL_B337_16:AS-02","CL_B337_14:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AS-03","text":"Cognizable objecthood is generated through distributed mediation among sensible givenness, imagination, temporal synthesis, categories, apperception, and judgment.","refs":["CL_B337_16:AS-04","CL_B337_15:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AS-04","text":"Categories consequently mutate from bare conceptual forms into schematized procedures and systematic principles that legislate universal form without generating determinate empirical content.","refs":["CL_B337_15:RMD-01","CL_B337_13:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AS-05","text":"Judgment occupies the irreducible interval between universal rule and particular appearance, preventing valid application from becoming a mechanically derivable operation.","refs":["CL_B337_14:AS-B03","CL_B337_14:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AS-06","text":"The argument becomes a transcendental and jurisdictional proof procedure that validates concepts by reconstructing their enabling conditions and tests them by removing those conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_15:AS-B06","CL_B337_16:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AS-07","text":"Its movement is recursive tightening: each successful mediation produces objective application, and each application returns as a limitation on the scope of the mediating concept.","refs":["CL_B337_16:AS-08","CL_B337_15:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AS-08","text":"Closure occurs through semantic deprivation rather than positive ontology: categories retain logical form beyond experience, but the supersensible survives only as an indeterminate boundary.","refs":["CL_B337_16:AS-07","CL_B337_16:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires conceptual determination joined to sensible intuition; logical admissibility alone cannot establish object-reference.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-01","CL_B337_15:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is at once the criterion of objective reality, the domain of valid cognition, and the boundary of pure conceptual authority.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-09","CL_B337_14:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Synthetic unity becomes objectively effective only when intellectual spontaneity is translated by imagination into temporally determined sensible synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-04","CL_B337_15:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] The conditions that realize categories as objectively valid are identical with the conditions that restrict their use to appearances.","refs":["CL_B337_15:MRT-B04","CL_B337_16:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Judgment and schema mediate the heterogeneous relation between universal categories and particular appearances without reducing application to another universal rule.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GA-04","CL_B337_15:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Subjective conditions ground objective validity only when they are necessary conditions under which any object of possible experience can be cognized.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-06","CL_B337_15:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Schematized categories generate a systematic architecture of transcendental principles while remaining unable to determine particular empirical qualities or actualities.","refs":["CL_B337_15:GA-05","CL_B337_16:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] The object ceases to be an independently unified terminus and becomes the correlate of rule-governed synthesis of a sensible manifold.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-11","CL_B337_13:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Thought can exceed cognition only as indeterminate thinkability, while the noumenon remains an empty negative boundary rather than a knowable object.","refs":["CL_B337_16:MME-05","CL_B337_16:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Formal logic retains a universal negative jurisdiction, but reason becomes dialectical whenever formal possibility is converted into transcendent object-knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_15:RMD-05","CL_B337_14:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Critique is a productive method of jurisdiction: limitation allocates valid functions to faculties, principles, proofs, and domains rather than merely prohibiting claims.","refs":["CL_B337_14:MME-06","CL_B337_15:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-12","text":"[Persistent] The finite subject is the formal unity of synthesis within receptivity, not an intellectual creator capable of supplying its own sensible manifold.","refs":["CL_B337_16:BSD-04","CL_B337_16:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] A priori necessity determines the universal form in which empirical content can appear but cannot determine that content’s specific quality, existence, or laws.","refs":["CL_B337_13:BSD-03","CL_B337_16:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-01","text":"The category retains its identity as a rule of unity but mutates from pure form into temporal procedure, principle-generating function, and finally a merely logical residue outside experience.","refs":["CL_B337_15:RMD-01","CL_B337_15:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-02","text":"Possible experience expands from a field of cognition into the total jurisdiction that grounds, organizes, and limits every claim to objective validity.","refs":["CL_B337_16:RMD-01","CL_B337_14:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-03","text":"Imagination moves from a bridge between faculties into a distributed temporal synthesis and is finally absorbed into synthesis as the general machine of phenomenal constitution.","refs":["CL_B337_15:RMD-04","CL_B337_16:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-04","text":"Judgment changes from an exercised natural capacity into the central, transcendentally directed, yet irreducible site of concept-application.","refs":["CL_B337_14:RMD-04","CL_B337_14:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-05","text":"Time expands from a sensible form into the universal medium of schema, categorical meaning, quantitative determination, mathematical applicability, and phenomenal order.","refs":["CL_B337_15:XD-05","CL_B337_15:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-06","text":"The object reverses from an already available entity into a synthesized correlate whose objectivity consists in necessary validity within experience.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-11","CL_B337_16:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-07","text":"Noumenon reverses from an apparent supersensible object-class into a negative operator regulating the scope of sensible cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_16:RMD-08","CL_B337_16:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-08","text":"A priori necessity reverses its apparent direction: instead of licensing transcendence, it becomes the form that makes experience possible.","refs":["CL_B337_16:MP-04","CL_B337_15:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-09","text":"Noncontradiction retains its negative identity while losing any claim to provide a complete criterion of synthetic truth or objective existence.","refs":["CL_B337_15:RMD-05","CL_B337_15:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:CGD-01","text":"Particular schemata and category classifications remain lower-layer differentiations; their current-level significance is the common architecture by which temporal mediation converts categories into valid rules of use.","refs":["CL_B337_15:CGD-01","CL_B337_16:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:CGD-02","text":"Self-affection remains a local account of inner cognition, but it scales upward as a model of the finite subject’s division between determining unity and receptive appearance.","refs":["CL_B337_13:CGD-04","CL_B337_16:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:CGD-03","text":"The regress of rule-application scales upward from a local analysis of judgment into a general prohibition against deriving objective cognition from formal procedure alone.","refs":["CL_B337_14:CGD-03","CL_B337_14:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:CGD-04","text":"Imagination, time, and schema bridge lower-layer faculty analysis with the current-level architecture of mediated application and principle-generation.","refs":["CL_B337_14:CGD-04","CL_B337_15:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:CGD-05","text":"The principle of contradiction remains locally decisive for analytical judgment but scales upward only as one stratum within a differentiated theory of truth.","refs":["CL_B337_15:CGD-04","CL_B337_15:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:CGD-06","text":"Extensive quantity, intensive degree, and mathematical construction remain local demonstrations, while their interaction scales into the division of labor between a priori form and empirical content.","refs":["CL_B337_16:CGD-03","CL_B337_16:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:CGD-07","text":"The anti-void argument remains a restricted intervention, but its prohibition against inferring ontology from perceptual absence becomes a general rule of inferential restraint.","refs":["CL_B337_16:CGD-04","CL_B337_16:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:CGD-08","text":"The positive division into sensible and intelligible worlds becomes obsolete and is superseded by the asymmetry between determinate experience and an indeterminate limit.","refs":["CL_B337_16:CGD-07","CL_B337_16:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GNX-01","text":"Formal conceptual determinacy is not cognition and cannot independently establish objective reference.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GNX-01","CL_B337_16:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GNX-02","text":"Categories detached from intuition and schema cannot acquire objective validity because no determinable object remains.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GNX-02","CL_B337_16:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GNX-03","text":"Human cognition excludes intellectual intuition and therefore excludes any direct conceptual disclosure of objects.","refs":["CL_B337_15:DBN-01","CL_B337_16:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GNX-04","text":"Empirical derivation cannot ground a priori necessity, but a priori origin likewise cannot license transcendent application.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GNX-01","CL_B337_16:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GNX-05","text":"Neither formal noncontradiction nor a final meta-rule can supply the content-bearing mediation required for synthetic objective judgment.","refs":["CL_B337_14:GNX-04","CL_B337_15:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GNX-06","text":"Phenomena and noumena cannot constitute two positively cognized worlds because positive noumenal cognition would require unavailable intellectual intuition.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GNX-07","CL_B337_16:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GNX-07","text":"Pure principles cannot complete natural science because they determine universal form without anticipating empirical quality, origin, or actuality.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GNX-06","CL_B337_16:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:GNX-08","text":"Nonpresentation cannot be converted into ontology: perceptual absence does not prove nonbeing, and unavailable intuition does not disclose a supersensible object.","refs":["CL_B337_16:CGD-04","CL_B337_16:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-01","text":"Objectivity cannot be correspondence with a structure available independently of cognition’s conditions; it must be constituted validity within possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_16:DBN-03","CL_B337_13:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-02","text":"Objects cannot be admitted as already unified, ruleless realities to which categories are subsequently added from outside.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GNX-05","CL_B337_16:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-03","text":"Necessary form cannot collapse into subjective association, psychological compulsion, or private arbitrariness merely because it operates through subjective faculties.","refs":["CL_B337_14:DBN-01","CL_B337_14:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-04","text":"No mechanical application system can replace judgment, even though transcendental conditions can regulate its legitimate employment.","refs":["CL_B337_14:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-05","text":"Legitimate scope cannot be inferred from a concept’s a priori origin, because that inference is the recurrent temptation of transcendental overreach.","refs":["CL_B337_16:DBN-06","CL_B337_16:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-06","text":"No absolute standpoint can survey appearances and things in themselves as completed domains or disclose positively what lies beyond the cognitive boundary.","refs":["CL_B337_16:BSA-03","CL_B337_16:DBN-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-07","text":"Pure understanding cannot generate its own manifold, intuition, schema, or object without collapsing finite cognition into intellectual creation.","refs":["CL_B337_16:DBN-05","CL_B337_16:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSA-01","text":"The unavailable intellectual intuition that could directly unite thought and object is the deepest missing faculty organizing the system’s limit.","refs":["CL_B337_16:BSA-01","CL_B337_13:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSA-02","text":"Positive noumenal judgment is organized around the absence of a non-empirical third term capable of mediating synthetic connection beyond sensibility.","refs":["CL_B337_16:BSA-02","CL_B337_16:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSA-03","text":"The spontaneous subject and the thing in itself remain necessary remainders that prevent appearance from becoming absolute while remaining unavailable for positive determination.","refs":["CL_B337_13:BSA-03","CL_B337_16:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSA-04","text":"A complete a priori derivation of empirical nature remains unavailable because universal conditions cannot generate specific qualities, actualities, or laws.","refs":["CL_B337_13:BSA-04","CL_B337_16:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSA-05","text":"The system lacks any absolute closure in which appearance exhausts reality or the supersensible becomes positively surveyable.","refs":["CL_B337_16:BSA-03","CL_B337_16:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:XD-01","text":"Categorical validity becomes objective only when figurative synthesis constitutes a unified appearance and judgment authorizes its subsumption under a rule.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-05","CL_B337_13:XD-01","CL_B337_14:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:XD-02","text":"Schema directs judgment by furnishing sensible applicability without eliminating the irreducible act that connects a universal rule to a particular case.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-05","CL_B337_14:XD-03","CL_B337_15:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:XD-03","text":"The system of principles determines universal structures of phenomenal nature while leaving specific sensation, empirical quality, and determinate natural law to experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-13","CL_B337_13:GA-08","CL_B337_16:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:XD-04","text":"Temporal-sensible mediation both realizes categories within experience and invalidates their objective use beyond experience when the required mediation is absent.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-09","CL_B337_15:AS-B03","CL_B337_16:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:XD-05","text":"Subject and object become coupled but asymmetrical phenomenal structures: the object is constituted through synthesis, while the subject knows itself only as temporally appearing.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-08","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-12","CL_B337_13:BSD-02","CL_B337_16:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:XD-06","text":"Truth becomes stratified into formal admissibility, analytical recognition, and synthetic objective validity, with each stratum requiring a distinct warrant.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-10","CL_B337_15:XD-02","CL_B337_15:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:XD-07","text":"Critique becomes controlled employment through deprivation-testing: concepts are licensed by their enabling conditions and deprived of objective authority when those conditions are removed.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-11","CL_B337_14:BSD-04","CL_B337_16:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:XD-08","text":"Because possible experience both constitutes objectivity and delimits application, transcendental objectivity is intrinsically jurisdictional rather than indefinitely extensible.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-06","CL_B337_14:RLE-04","CL_B337_16:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-01","text":"Objectivity is a relational accomplishment constituted through possible experience rather than a direct correspondence between concepts and independently accessible objects.","refs":["CL_B337_13:BSD-01","CL_B337_15:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-02","text":"Transcendental necessity is self-limiting authority: it is valid precisely where its principles make experience possible and invalid when detached from that function.","refs":["CL_B337_16:BSD-01","CL_B337_15:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-03","text":"The subject becomes a distributed functional nexus whose necessary unity coordinates receptivity and synthesis without creating the manifold it orders.","refs":["CL_B337_14:HSI-02","CL_B337_16:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes the universal operational medium of finite cognition rather than a neutral container independently available to thought.","refs":["CL_B337_16:BSD-03","CL_B337_16:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-05","text":"Nature is necessarily lawful in universal form yet empirically unfinished, producing a structural bifurcation between transcendental legislation and empirical science.","refs":["CL_B337_13:BSD-04","CL_B337_13:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-06","text":"Error becomes a structural failure of mediation and jurisdiction rather than merely a false proposition or deficiency of formal logic.","refs":["CL_B337_14:BSD-05","CL_B337_16:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-07","text":"Metaphysics is transformed from unrestricted ontology into boundary-knowledge that preserves excess as thinkability without converting it into cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_16:BSD-05","CL_B337_16:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-01","text":"Categories require intuition, intuition requires synthesis, synthesis yields unified experience, and experience returns as the criterion validating categorical necessity.","refs":["CL_B337_14:RLE-01","CL_B337_16:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-02","text":"Heterogeneity produces schema, schema enables application, and the enabling condition returns as the limit confining objective employment to phenomena.","refs":["CL_B337_15:RLE-01","CL_B337_15:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-03","text":"Rule-possession generates the application problem, the application problem generates regress, and regress returns judgment as irreducible but transcendentally directable.","refs":["CL_B337_14:RLE-02","CL_B337_14:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-04","text":"The category-table generates schemata and principles, principles constitute lawful phenomenal form, and their material incompleteness reopens empirical inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_16:RLE-03","CL_B337_13:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-05","text":"A priori origin repeatedly tempts transcendent extension, while critique repeatedly converts that temptation into a positive allocation of source, domain, and warrant.","refs":["CL_B337_16:RLE-04","CL_B337_15:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-06","text":"Calling an object an appearance generates thought of what exceeds appearance, but that thought returns as a restriction on cognition through the negative noumenon.","refs":["CL_B337_16:RLE-07","CL_B337_16:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-07","text":"Deprivation-testing loops from valid application to removal of its sensible conditions and then to the loss of determinate object-reference.","refs":["CL_B337_16:BSD-07","CL_B337_16:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-08","text":"Time is the central conceptual attractor because every objectively significant category is routed through temporal synthesis, schema, quantity, and phenomenal application.","refs":["CL_B337_15:RLE-04","CL_B337_16:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B01","text":"Formal conceptual coherence is deprived of object-giving authority, generating the need for sensible presentation and synthetic mediation.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GNX-01","CL_B337_15:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B02","text":"Sensible givenness, temporal form, and apperceptive unity establish a manifold capable of entering connected cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-03","CL_B337_16:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B03","text":"Productive imagination translates intellectual spontaneity into temporal rules for determining intuition.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-04","CL_B337_15:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B04","text":"Schema furnishes the sensible condition under which judgment can apply categories to particular appearances.","refs":["CL_B337_14:AS-B03","CL_B337_15:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B05","text":"Rule-governed synthesis produces possible experience, within which the object emerges as the correlate of a unified manifold.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-01","CL_B337_16:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B06","text":"Schematized categories generate a systematic field of principles that determines appearances through extensive form, intensive degree, relation, and modality.","refs":["CL_B337_15:FGS-B04","CL_B337_16:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B07","text":"Pure principles and mathematical construction determine invariant phenomenal form while empirical inquiry supplies specific natural content.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-08","CL_B337_16:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B08","text":"Removing sensible intuition and schema deprives categories of determinate objects, leaving only logical form and an empty negative boundary.","refs":["CL_B337_16:FGS-B06","CL_B337_16:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AFM-B01","text":"Formal concept and logical admissibility → failure of object-reference → demand for sensible intuition.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GNX-01","CL_B337_15:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AFM-B02","text":"Sensible heterogeneity → productive imagination and time → synthesized intuition.","refs":["CL_B337_15:AS-B02","CL_B337_15:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AFM-B03","text":"Synthesized intuition → schema and judgment → legitimate categorical application.","refs":["CL_B337_14:AS-B03","CL_B337_15:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AFM-B04","text":"Categorical application → unified experience → objectivity as necessary experiential validity.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-01","CL_B337_16:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AFM-B05","text":"Unified experience → schematized category-table → systematic principles and quantified phenomenal nature.","refs":["CL_B337_15:FGS-B04","CL_B337_16:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AFM-B06","text":"Universal phenomenal form → empirical indeterminacy → division of labor between transcendental legislation and inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_13:GA-08","CL_B337_16:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:AFM-B07","text":"Removal of intuition and schema → semantic deprivation → rejection of positive noumena → retention of a negative limit.","refs":["CL_B337_16:FGS-B06","CL_B337_16:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from correspondence with independent objects into constituted validity within possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_16:MME-01","CL_B337_13:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MME-02","text":"The object is reclassified from a pre-unified terminus into the rule-governed correlate of synthesized appearances.","refs":["CL_B337_16:GA-11","CL_B337_16:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MME-03","text":"The opposition between a priori and empirical cognition collapses into a division of labor between universal experiential form and determinate content.","refs":["CL_B337_16:MME-02","CL_B337_15:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MME-04","text":"Truth is differentiated into formal admissibility, analytical recognition, synthetic grounding, and objective applicability.","refs":["CL_B337_15:BSD-02","CL_B337_15:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MME-05","text":"Cognitive faculties are reorganized by jurisdiction rather than ranked as interchangeable sources of knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_14:MME-03","CL_B337_15:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MME-06","text":"Nature is redrawn as universally lawful in form yet empirically open in content.","refs":["CL_B337_13:MME-04","CL_B337_16:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MME-07","text":"Metaphysics becomes a jurisdictional map distinguishing valid use, dialectical extension, unavoidable remainder, and boundary-knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_16:MME-06","CL_B337_14:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MME-08","text":"Noumenon is reclassified from a supersensible object into a limit-operator that preserves the distinction between thought and cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_16:RMD-08","CL_B337_16:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system coordinates categories, judgments, schemata, principles, mathematical construction, phenomenal quantity, and the noumenal boundary.","refs":["CL_B337_16:ALT-B01","CL_B337_15:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, sensible intuition, imagination, time, apperception, categories, and judgment jointly constitute determinate phenomenal objects.","refs":["CL_B337_16:AS-04","CL_B337_16:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, metaphysical claims are converted into questions of application, jurisdiction, proof, and legitimacy before speculative extension.","refs":["CL_B337_15:ALT-B03","CL_B337_16:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, phenomenal reality is spatiotemporal, synthesized, continuously determinable, and unavailable apart from the forms of finite cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_16:ALT-B04","CL_B337_16:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system enacts its own thesis by repeatedly specifying valid conditions and testing concepts through semantic deprivation.","refs":["CL_B337_15:ALT-B05","CL_B337_16:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FL-01","text":"The conversion of subjective conditions into objective conditions remains necessary but is asserted more fully than its decisive transition is displayed.","refs":["CL_B337_15:FL-01","CL_B337_15:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FL-02","text":"Judgment must remain irreducible to rules while being directed through transcendental conditions, leaving the boundary between guidance and mechanical determination unstable.","refs":["CL_B337_14:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FL-03","text":"Imagination must exercise intellectual spontaneity while belonging to sensible determination, making its mediating status structurally hybrid.","refs":["CL_B337_13:FL-01","CL_B337_15:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FL-04","text":"Objects must be given to cognition, yet the formal conditions of synthesis determine what can count as an object, leaving givenness and constitution mutually dependent.","refs":["CL_B337_16:FL-02","CL_B337_16:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FL-05","text":"The negative noumenon is required to limit sensible cognition even though its objective possibility cannot be positively established.","refs":["CL_B337_16:FL-03","CL_B337_16:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FL-06","text":"The terminal boundary is immutable for human sensible cognition but cannot be universalized to every possible intellect.","refs":["CL_B337_16:FL-06","CL_B337_16:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:FL-07","text":"Pure understanding is assigned legislative priority even though mathematical objectivity remains inseparable from pure intuition and its distinctive synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_16:FL-01","CL_B337_16:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MP-01","text":"The category mutates from pure conceptual form into temporal rule, systematic principle, and finally empty logical significance outside experience.","refs":["CL_B337_15:RMD-01","CL_B337_15:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MP-02","text":"Time mutates from sensible form into the medium of synthetic objectivity while becoming inadmissible when inserted into purely formal contradiction.","refs":["CL_B337_15:RMD-03","CL_B337_15:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MP-03","text":"Critique mutates from negative restriction into a positive architecture for assigning valid cognitive functions and producing lawful principles.","refs":["CL_B337_14:MP-04","CL_B337_15:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MP-04","text":"Noumenon mutates from a presumed object of non-sensible intuition into a negative regulator of sensible cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_16:RMD-08","CL_B337_16:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MP-05","text":"A priori necessity mutates from apparent authorization of transcendence into the constitutive form of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_16:MP-04","CL_B337_15:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:HSI-01","text":"Reality-for-cognition is a conversion machine in which sensible manifolds pass through temporal and rule-governed synthesis to emerge as determinate appearances.","refs":["CL_B337_16:HSI-01","CL_B337_15:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a distributed switching-point where receptivity, synthesis, necessary form, and application converge without becoming intellectual creation.","refs":["CL_B337_16:HSI-02","CL_B337_14:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:HSI-03","text":"Time is the continuous transmission medium through which schemas, quantities, categories, and objects become operationally connected.","refs":["CL_B337_16:HSI-03","CL_B337_15:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:HSI-04","text":"Nature is a formally closed but materially unfinished field: necessarily lawful in form and indefinitely open to empirical determination.","refs":["CL_B337_13:HSI-04","CL_B337_13:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:HSI-05","text":"The remaining struggle is jurisdictional navigation: preserve valid use within experience, resist conceptual overreach, and retain the noumenal beyond only as a boundary.","refs":["CL_B337_16:HSI-04","CL_B337_14:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted architecture is sufficient to reconstruct the meta-cluster’s movement from conceptual insufficiency through mediated phenomenal constitution to negative noumenal closure.","refs":["CL_B337_16:MRT-B01","CL_B337_15:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B02","text":"Coherence requires possible experience to remain both the ground of objectivity and the limit separating thought from cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_16:MRT-B03","CL_B337_14:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where one shared synthesis explains objective application, phenomenal constitution, mathematical validity, and categorical restriction.","refs":["CL_B337_16:MRT-B04","CL_B337_15:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B04","text":"The productive mechanism of imagination and the exact manner in which schema directs irreducible judgment remain underdetermined.","refs":["CL_B337_14:MRT-B04","CL_B337_15:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining recursive return, compressed transitions, and unresolved mediation rather than harmonizing them into linear deduction.","refs":["CL_B337_16:MRT-B05","CL_B337_14:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B06","text":"The thing-in-itself remainder resists complete axiomatic reduction because it must prevent phenomenal absolutization while remaining unstable and positively indeterminate.","refs":["CL_B337_16:MRT-B06","CL_B337_16:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"B337 L1 04","context":"Chapters 37–48 · In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18 · Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20 · Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22 · Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23 · BOOK II. Analytic of Principles · INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General · Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding · Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding · Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements · Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements · Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding · Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena"}},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05","layer":"MC","title":"B337 L1 05","order":129,"parents":["CL_B337_17","CL_B337_18","CL_B337_19","CL_B337_20"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"fba6a4bdd4d6c6e72b71f220df4184ef4834b17cf18754b63825e74d80b58261","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AS-01","text":"The system asks how objective validity can be secured within experience while reason necessarily demands an unconditioned completion that experience cannot supply.","refs":["CL_B337_17:AS-B01","CL_B337_20:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AS-02","text":"Cognition forms a stratified architecture in which sensibility gives, understanding constitutes empirical objects, and reason seeks systematic unity among understanding’s rules.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-02","CL_B337_18:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AS-03","text":"The same conditions that validate empirical objects restrict cognition to possible experience, making authorization and limitation one jurisdictional operation.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-04","CL_B337_20:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AS-04","text":"Reason recursively ascends from conditioned cognition toward the unconditioned, which remains necessary as an orienting horizon but unavailable as an object.","refs":["CL_B337_18:AS-B03","CL_B337_19:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AS-05","text":"Transcendental illusion arises when a formal or subjective demand for unity is converted into a synthetic determination of things.","refs":["CL_B337_18:AS-B05","CL_B337_20:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AS-06","text":"As reason expands from self to world and toward the conditions of objects in general, objective availability declines and illusion changes from reification into contradiction.","refs":["CL_B337_20:AS-B04","CL_B337_20:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AS-07","text":"Critique cannot eliminate reason’s recurrent illusion; it recurrently withdraws constitutive authority while preserving systematic inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_18:AS-B08","CL_B337_20:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AS-08","text":"The movement is spiral: failures of immediacy generate mediation, rational mediation generates excess, and critical return closes in disciplined non-cognition rather than supersensible possession.","refs":["CL_B337_17:AS-B07","CL_B337_20:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Cognition is stratified across irreducible faculties whose distinct operations must cooperate without being conflated.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-01","CL_B337_17:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Objective cognition requires intuition, categorial synthesis, and applicability within possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-03","CL_B337_20:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Conditions of validity are intrinsically restrictive: what authorizes cognition also determines the boundary beyond which necessity no longer establishes an object.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-04","CL_B337_20:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is the positive jurisdiction toward which empirical synthesis, category-use, and every test of objective cognition converge.","refs":["CL_B337_17:RLE-07","CL_B337_20:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] The unconditioned is the absent counter-attractor generated by every renewed search for complete grounds.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-03","CL_B337_19:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Validity differentiates into empirical objectivity, inferential legitimacy, regulative necessity, practical efficacy, and disciplined non-cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_18:AS-B06","CL_B337_19:MME-01","CL_B337_20:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Logical consistency, formal derivation, and rational necessity cannot substitute for object-giving intuition.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-06","CL_B337_19:GNX-05","CL_B337_20:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Transcendental illusion is the conversion of a necessary formal demand into a synthetic determination of an unconditioned object.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-05","CL_B337_20:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Critique changes from delimiting empirical employment into an immanent and recurrent regulation of reason’s own excess.","refs":["CL_B337_18:RLE-04","CL_B337_20:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Ideas retain legitimate force as regulative principles and practical standards while remaining unavailable as constitutive knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_19:GA-05","CL_B337_19:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] The subject is progressively deflated from an externally conditioned empirical unity into an ontologically indeterminate transcendental function rather than a self-known substance.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-13","CL_B337_20:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Metaphysics terminates as critical discipline: empirical inquiry remains extensible while rational psychology, cosmology, and supersensible object-knowledge lose scientific authority.","refs":["CL_B337_17:BSD-08","CL_B337_20:GA-09","CL_B337_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-01","text":"[Stable Identity / Functional Mutation] Objectivity remains warranted cognition but shifts from empirical constitution to differentiated judgments about inference, regulation, and practice.","refs":["CL_B337_17:RMD-01","CL_B337_18:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-02","text":"[Functional Recurrence] Possible experience remains the stable criterion of object-cognition while expanding into the jurisdictional test applied to rational totalization.","refs":["CL_B337_18:RMD-02","CL_B337_20:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation / Escalation] The unconditioned changes from projected completion into boundary-horizon, architectonic generator, and unavailable adequate object.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GA-04","CL_B337_19:RMD-05","CL_B337_20:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-04","text":"[Absorption / Disappearance] Externality grounds individuation and empirical time, then recedes as an independent theme after being absorbed into the established field of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_17:RMD-06","CL_B337_20:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-05","text":"[Terminal Transformation] The subject moves from relational empirical unity through logical subjecthood to disciplined ontological indeterminacy after its reification as soul fails.","refs":["CL_B337_17:MME-04","CL_B337_20:GA-06","CL_B337_20:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-06","text":"[Displacement] Time shifts from the relational order of one empirical nature into the nonclosing sequence through which reason repeatedly seeks total conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_17:RMD-03","CL_B337_18:BSD-05","CL_B337_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-07","text":"[Reversal] Contradiction changes from a local mark of failure into a scale-sensitive diagnostic of illegitimate totalization.","refs":["CL_B337_20:RMD-07","CL_B337_20:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-08","text":"[Mutation] Critique shifts from translating metaphysical claims into jurisdictional questions to permanent regulation of recurrent rational illusion.","refs":["CL_B337_17:BSD-07","CL_B337_18:MME-05","CL_B337_20:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:CGD-01","text":"The analogies and modal postulates remain lower-layer rules, while their scalable effect is the constitution and validation of one field of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_17:CGD-04","CL_B337_17:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:CGD-02","text":"The refutation of idealism remains a local transition, but its result supplies the empirically objective field presupposed by the later critique of reason.","refs":["CL_B337_17:CGD-07","CL_B337_17:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:CGD-03","text":"Faculty hierarchy and the distinction between conditioned understanding and unconditioned reason scale upward as the architecture explaining both knowledge and dialectic.","refs":["CL_B337_18:CGD-02","CL_B337_19:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:CGD-04","text":"Terminological distinctions and syllogistic forms bridge local classification to the architectonic generation of ideas and dialectical domains.","refs":["CL_B337_19:RMD-02","CL_B337_19:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:CGD-05","text":"Individual soul-predicates and cosmological disputes remain local, while the conversion of formal unity into object-determination becomes the scalable mechanism.","refs":["CL_B337_20:CGD-01","CL_B337_20:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:CGD-06","text":"Moral and political examples remain local demonstrations of efficacy, while the transition from speculative ideas to practical realization remains a partially integrated bridge.","refs":["CL_B337_19:CGD-03","CL_B337_19:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:CGD-07","text":"The ideal of pure reason and positive antinomy resolution remain undeveloped horizons and cannot be promoted into demonstrated current-level closure.","refs":["CL_B337_20:CGD-08","CL_B337_20:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GNX-01","text":"Neither a one-source cognition nor intellectual intuition can replace the mediated cooperation of sensibility, understanding, and reason.","refs":["CL_B337_17:DBN-01","CL_B337_17:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GNX-02","text":"Logical comparison and formal derivation cannot establish real objects, existence, or supersensible totality.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GNX-01","CL_B337_19:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GNX-03","text":"Categories without intuition and ideas without adequate objects cannot function as constitutive cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GNX-03","CL_B337_19:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GNX-04","text":"Neither the thinking subject nor the world-whole is available as a complete object under the conditions of cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_20:DBN-02","CL_B337_20:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GNX-05","text":"The failure of one metaphysical doctrine cannot validate its contrary, because opposed claims may share the same illegitimate totalizing premise.","refs":["CL_B337_20:GNX-04","CL_B337_20:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:GNX-06","text":"Critique cannot abolish natural illusion or close rational demand in a final empirical state.","refs":["CL_B337_18:GNX-05","CL_B337_19:BSA-01","CL_B337_20:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:DBN-01","text":"The forms necessary for thought cannot directly disclose the constitution of things independently of cognitive conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_20:DBN-01","CL_B337_17:DBN-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:DBN-02","text":"Subjective necessity, regulative legitimacy, practical efficacy, and objective cognition cannot be collapsed into a single undifferentiated validity.","refs":["CL_B337_18:BSD-04","CL_B337_19:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:DBN-03","text":"No standpoint outside appearance or reason’s own conditions is available to compare cognition with its supersensible ground or extinguish illusion externally.","refs":["CL_B337_17:BSA-02","CL_B337_20:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:DBN-04","text":"Neither alternative totalities nor a completed series of consequences can be surveyed as an empirical whole.","refs":["CL_B337_17:BSA-05","CL_B337_19:GNX-04","CL_B337_20:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:DBN-05","text":"Metaphysics cannot remain an inventory of supersensible beings; admitting that model would collapse the distinction between rational production and objective cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_19:BSD-02","CL_B337_20:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:BSA-01","text":"The deepest absent object is an intuition adequate to the unconditioned that reason must demand but cannot possess.","refs":["CL_B337_20:BSA-01","CL_B337_19:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:BSA-02","text":"The architecture is organized by the non-coincidence between reason’s demand for completion and cognition’s capacity to provide a corresponding object.","refs":["CL_B337_18:BSA-03","CL_B337_20:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:BSA-03","text":"Pure self-presence and complete world-possession are structurally unavailable forms of closure.","refs":["CL_B337_17:BSA-04","CL_B337_20:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:BSA-04","text":"The architectonic formally closes around subject, world, and highest condition while withholding positive completion of its terminal domains.","refs":["CL_B337_19:AS-B06","CL_B337_20:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:XD-01","text":"Reason depends on the lawfully constituted empirical field for its material even when it seeks a unity exceeding every determinate empirical cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-02","CL_B337_17:AS-B02","CL_B337_18:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:XD-02","text":"The enabling-restrictive structure governing categories recurs at the level of ideas: necessary operations are retained while their constitutive extension is blocked.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-08","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-09","CL_B337_17:GA-04","CL_B337_20:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:XD-03","text":"Objective validity becomes a layered jurisdiction in which empirical applicability, inferential legitimacy, regulation, and practical use require different warrants.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-10","CL_B337_18:XD-03","CL_B337_19:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:XD-04","text":"Robust empirical objectivity and productive transcendental non-cognition are complementary results of the same critical boundary rather than sceptical alternatives.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-12","CL_B337_17:GA-11","CL_B337_20:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:XD-05","text":"The empirical subject’s dependence on temporal and external relations combines with the transcendental subject’s functional emptiness to block rational psychology’s soul-substance.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-11","CL_B337_17:GA-13","CL_B337_20:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:XD-06","text":"One objectively ordered phenomenal time coexists with an indefinitely open sequence of rational conditions whose totality cannot be given.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-05","CL_B337_17:BSD-02","CL_B337_19:GA-08","CL_B337_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:XD-07","text":"Because transcendental illusion is a recurrent by-product of rational systematicity, critique must regulate assent rather than suppress reason’s operation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-08","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-09","CL_B337_18:RMD-07","CL_B337_20:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:XD-08","text":"Increasing totalizing scope produces decreasing object availability and changes dialectic from reification of the self into conflict over the world and anticipation of an absolute condition.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-08","CL_B337_20:AS-B04","CL_B337_20:AS-B06","CL_B337_20:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:XD-09","text":"Practical efficacy can survive the loss of speculative object-knowledge, but the deduction connecting those registers remains incomplete.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_05:GA-12","CL_B337_19:GA-10","CL_B337_19:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:BSD-01","text":"Cognition becomes horizontally extensible through empirical inquiry and vertically regulative through reason without acquiring supersensible objects.","refs":["CL_B337_17:XD-09","CL_B337_18:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:BSD-02","text":"Rational error is not external to systematic cognition but the recurrent excess generated by the same operation that organizes knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_18:BSD-03","CL_B337_20:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:BSD-03","text":"Metaphysics becomes a cartography of the operations by which formal functions are transformed into apparent supersensible objects.","refs":["CL_B337_19:BSD-02","CL_B337_20:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:BSD-04","text":"Agency becomes critical self-government of assent combined with practical approximation toward standards that cannot be exhaustively realized.","refs":["CL_B337_18:HSI-04","CL_B337_19:RLE-02","CL_B337_20:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:BSD-05","text":"Totality divides into the demanded regress of grounds and the prohibited completion of consequences, while antinomy exposes the instability of treating either as a given object.","refs":["CL_B337_19:MME-04","CL_B337_20:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:BSD-06","text":"Reality is reconstructed as a conditioned empirical network inhabited by a functional subject and bordered by temporally open but objectively unavailable horizons of completion.","refs":["CL_B337_17:HSI-01","CL_B337_18:HSI-03","CL_B337_20:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-01","text":"Empirical validation forms a loop in which synthesis produces objectivity and successful objectivity reimposes the phenomenal restriction that made it possible.","refs":["CL_B337_17:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-02","text":"Recursive conditioning turns each condition into another conditioned term, projects the unconditioned, fails to objectify it, and returns the idea as regulation.","refs":["CL_B337_18:RLE-01","CL_B337_19:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-03","text":"Illusion generates critique, critique suspends authority, and the natural return of illusion regenerates the need for critique.","refs":["CL_B337_18:RLE-04","CL_B337_20:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-04","text":"Dialectical escalation runs from self through world toward highest condition, converting a logical sequence into an expanding architecture of unavailable objects.","refs":["CL_B337_20:RLE-05","CL_B337_19:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-05","text":"Rational psychology follows an inverse loop in which increasing conceptual purity removes the empirical content required for objective cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_20:RLE-02","CL_B337_20:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-06","text":"Possible experience and the unconditioned operate as opposed attractors: one stabilizes objective cognition, while the other perpetually draws cognition beyond its stable jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_17:RLE-07","CL_B337_19:RLE-05","CL_B337_20:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FGS-B01","text":"Sensuous givenness + categorial form + temporal mediation → empirically objective synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_17:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FGS-B02","text":"Empirical synthesis + lawful temporal connection → one dynamical nature governed by the threshold of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_17:GA-08","CL_B337_20:AS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FGS-B03","text":"Constituted empirical rules → material for reason → demand for higher systematic unity.","refs":["CL_B337_18:AS-B02","CL_B337_18:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FGS-B04","text":"Conditioned cognition → renewed search for conditions → formal generation of the unconditioned.","refs":["CL_B337_18:AS-B03","CL_B337_19:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FGS-B05","text":"Subjective demand for completion + transfer into object-determination → transcendental illusion.","refs":["CL_B337_18:AS-B05","CL_B337_20:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FGS-B06","text":"Subjective unity → paralogistic reification; phenomenal totality → symmetrical antinomy; absolute condition → projected terminal horizon.","refs":["CL_B337_20:AS-B06","CL_B337_20:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FGS-B07","text":"Critical exposure → suspension of transcendent authority → retention of regulative and practical use → recurrent return to empirical jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_18:FGS-B06","CL_B337_19:GA-05","CL_B337_20:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AFM-B01","text":"Faculty differentiation → temporal synthesis → objective empirical cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_17:AS-B04","CL_B337_17:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AFM-B02","text":"Constituted experience → understanding’s rules → reason’s demand for higher unity.","refs":["CL_B337_18:FGS-B01","CL_B337_18:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AFM-B03","text":"Conditioned cognition → renewed conditions → projected unconditioned completion.","refs":["CL_B337_18:FGS-B02","CL_B337_19:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AFM-B04","text":"Projected completion → objectification of subjective necessity → structurally recurrent illusion.","refs":["CL_B337_18:FGS-B04","CL_B337_20:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AFM-B05","text":"Logical self-unity → soul-reification → world-totalization → antinomy → anticipated absolute condition.","refs":["CL_B337_20:AS-B05","CL_B337_20:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:AFM-B06","text":"Intuition test → suspension of doctrine → regulative or practical retention → recursive critical discipline.","refs":["CL_B337_20:CGD-05","CL_B337_20:AS-B07","CL_B337_19:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is relocated from intrinsic possession to a differentiated jurisdiction distributed across faculties, uses, and validation procedures.","refs":["CL_B337_17:MME-01","CL_B337_18:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MME-02","text":"Metaphysics is redrawn from a contest among supersensible doctrines into an immanent analysis of reason’s object-producing operations.","refs":["CL_B337_20:MME-01","CL_B337_19:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MME-03","text":"The subject is reclassified from inner substance into externally conditioned empirical unity and ontologically indeterminate transcendental function.","refs":["CL_B337_17:MME-04","CL_B337_20:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MME-04","text":"Totality divides into lawful empirical unity, demanded regressive grounding, prohibited completion of consequences, and an unavailable absolute condition.","refs":["CL_B337_19:MME-04","CL_B337_20:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MME-05","text":"Contradiction becomes a diagnostic boundary signal rather than a simple logical collapse or warrant for choosing one dogmatic side.","refs":["CL_B337_20:MME-04","CL_B337_20:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MME-06","text":"Critical reading must separate an argument’s origin, formal operation, objective interpretation, use, and terminal epistemic status.","refs":["CL_B337_20:MME-06","CL_B337_19:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MME-07","text":"Time is divided between one lawfully determinable empirical order and an open sequence of consequences that no final state can totalize.","refs":["CL_B337_17:MME-03","CL_B337_19:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MME-08","text":"The opposition between ideality and efficacy collapses because non-objective ideas can still organize cognition and practical realization.","refs":["CL_B337_19:MME-02","CL_B337_19:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system examines empirical conditions, transcendental ideas, the soul, the world-totality, and the projected highest condition.","refs":["CL_B337_19:ALT-B01","CL_B337_20:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, differentiated faculties transform given manifolds into objects and conditioned rules into demands for unconditioned unity.","refs":["CL_B337_17:ALT-B02","CL_B337_20:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, jurisdictional proof and immanent critique replace dogmatic inference, doctrinal victory, and sceptical withdrawal.","refs":["CL_B337_17:ALT-B03","CL_B337_20:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is a connected phenomenal field whose subject, future consequences, and complete world-series remain non-totalizable.","refs":["CL_B337_17:ALT-B04","CL_B337_19:ALT-B04","CL_B337_20:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system repeatedly stages rational overextension and interrupts it through renewed tests of intuition, use, and scope.","refs":["CL_B337_17:ALT-B05","CL_B337_20:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FL-01","text":"Categories and possible experience risk reciprocal validation because each appears to supply the objective credentials of the other.","refs":["CL_B337_17:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FL-02","text":"Reason requires the unconditioned as the terminus of grounding while lacking the intuition that could secure it as an object.","refs":["CL_B337_19:FL-01","CL_B337_20:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FL-03","text":"The claimed exhaustiveness of the architectonic depends on a syllogistic correspondence whose derivation remains compressed and whose relation to sophistical results is unstable.","refs":["CL_B337_19:FL-02","CL_B337_20:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FL-04","text":"Practical efficacy is preserved after speculative failure, but its dependence on moral and teleological premises prevents seamless derivation from theoretical critique.","refs":["CL_B337_19:FL-03","CL_B337_20:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FL-05","text":"Positive antinomy resolution and the developed third dialectical form remain absent, leaving the architectonic formally announced but materially incomplete.","refs":["CL_B337_20:BSA-04","CL_B337_20:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:FL-06","text":"Critique must arbitrate an illusion generated by reason without access to any standpoint outside reason, making critical containment necessarily self-referential.","refs":["CL_B337_20:DBN-05","CL_B337_20:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MP-01","text":"Objective validity mutates from experiential applicability into a differentiated distribution of inferential, regulative, practical, and critical statuses.","refs":["CL_B337_18:MP-02","CL_B337_19:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MP-02","text":"The unconditioned mutates from projected endpoint into boundary-concept, architectonic generator, and unavailable object.","refs":["CL_B337_18:MP-01","CL_B337_19:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MP-03","text":"The subject mutates when logical functions are converted into metaphysical predicates and then returned, after critique, as an indeterminate cognitive operator.","refs":["CL_B337_20:MP-01","CL_B337_20:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MP-04","text":"Contradiction mutates from apparent defeat into evidence that reason has applied unconditioned unity beyond the conditions of phenomenal synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_20:MP-03","CL_B337_20:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a jurisdictional-temporal network of conditioned objects over which reason projects horizons of completion that cannot enter the network as objects.","refs":["CL_B337_17:HSI-01","CL_B337_20:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:HSI-02","text":"The subject is an apperceptive relay and divided operator that generates rational excess without becoming a self-transparent substance.","refs":["CL_B337_17:HSI-02","CL_B337_18:HSI-02","CL_B337_20:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:HSI-03","text":"Time is an invisible but lawfully articulated empirical order nested within an open rational recursion that supplies neither permanent soul nor completed world.","refs":["CL_B337_17:HSI-03","CL_B337_18:HSI-03","CL_B337_20:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:HSI-04","text":"Navigation consists in recursive boundary work between possible experience and the unconditioned; regulative and practical movement remains possible, but vertical escape does not.","refs":["CL_B337_20:HSI-04","CL_B337_17:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from empirical constitution through rational totalization to differentiated dialectical limitation.","refs":["CL_B337_17:MRT-B01","CL_B337_20:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction remains underdetermined at the syllogistic derivation of the architectonic, the speculative-practical transition, the individual antinomies, and the undeveloped ideal of pure reason.","refs":["CL_B337_19:MRT-B02","CL_B337_20:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MRT-B03","text":"Coherence depends on keeping empirical objectivity, subjective necessity, regulative legitimacy, practical efficacy, and constitutive cognition distinct.","refs":["CL_B337_17:MRT-B04","CL_B337_19:MRT-B03","CL_B337_20:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where one jurisdictional criterion explains both empirical objectivity and the changing morphologies of transcendental illusion.","refs":["CL_B337_18:MRT-B02","CL_B337_20:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_05:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only through recursive return, scope restriction, and retention of the absent intuition and undeveloped terminal domain as irreducible limits.","refs":["CL_B337_17:MRT-B05","CL_B337_20:BSA-01","CL_B337_20:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"B337 L1 05","context":"Chapters 49–60 · Appendix · Experience · Empicisim · I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason. · Section I—Of Ideas in General · Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas · Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas · Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason · Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason · Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason"}},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06","layer":"MC","title":"B337 L1 06","order":130,"parents":["CL_B337_21","CL_B337_22","CL_B337_23","CL_B337_24"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"2cbaa14b1569b1fa1a7125a2b964a9dfdf7c95ccfb707dbf711ac717da2003c5","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AS-01","text":"The system must preserve reason’s necessary movement from conditioned appearances toward complete conditions without converting that necessity into possession of an unconditioned object.","refs":["CL_B337_21:AS-B01","CL_B337_23:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AS-02","text":"The world is therefore reframed from a hidden completed object into the open, temporally enacted operation through which cognition seeks conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_22:AS-B02","CL_B337_23:AS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AS-03","text":"Antinomy arises when an open rule of inquiry is mistaken for a constitutive description of an independently completed totality.","refs":["CL_B337_22:AS-B07","CL_B337_23:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AS-04","text":"Reciprocal contradiction displaces adjudication between cosmological doctrines by exposing the shared realist presupposition that generated their apparent object.","refs":["CL_B337_21:AS-B04","CL_B337_22:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AS-05","text":"Transcendental idealism and empirical realism jointly provide the positive ground for dissolving totalization while preserving empirical objectivity.","refs":["CL_B337_22:GA-06","CL_B337_22:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AS-06","text":"Totality then mutates from an object-predicate into a regulative directive whose legitimate operation depends on the mode of empirical givenness.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-03","CL_B337_23:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AS-07","text":"Differentiation by synthesis-type excludes unconditioned completion from homogeneous mathematical series while permitting a heterogeneous intelligible condition to remain thinkable outside empirical membership.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-05","CL_B337_24:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AS-08","text":"The movement is recursive and mutation-driven: projected completion generates contradiction, critique redistributes jurisdiction, and closure becomes regulative, negative, and non-total.","refs":["CL_B337_21:AS-B07","CL_B337_24:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every conditioned appearance requires further conditions, but this requirement never establishes that a complete series is already given.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-01","CL_B337_23:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Possible experience is the condition of objective validity, and appearances are empirically real precisely because they are not things in themselves.","refs":["CL_B337_22:GA-01","CL_B337_22:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Categories and rules of empirical synthesis become ideas of absolute totality and generate antinomy when those ideas are treated as constitutive descriptions of objects.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-02","CL_B337_23:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] The unconditioned is necessarily demanded as completion but cannot be exhibited as an empirical or metaphysically determinate object.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-05","CL_B337_22:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Antinomy is a diagnostic of objectified totality and shared transcendental realism rather than a conflict between independently determinate cosmological facts.","refs":["CL_B337_22:GA-04","CL_B337_22:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] The phenomenal world is an open and lawfully connected field constituted through temporally successive synthesis, not a completed object antecedent to cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-02","CL_B337_22:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Totality becomes an unattainable task-condition governing regress rather than a constitutive property possessed by the world.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-03","CL_B337_23:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Critical resolution transforms the status and legitimate function of a cosmological question instead of selecting one inherited answer.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-10","CL_B337_23:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Empirical regress both validates reference to absent or unperceived objects through possible continuation and prohibits conversion of that continuation into a completed world-whole.","refs":["CL_B337_22:GA-07","CL_B337_22:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] The legitimate resolution of a cosmological conflict depends on its mode of givenness and the synthesis-type generating its series.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-07","CL_B337_24:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] A homogeneous mathematical series cannot contain a completed phenomenal unconditioned, because operational non-termination never establishes an actually completed infinite object.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-04","CL_B337_24:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] A dynamical series can admit a heterogeneous intelligible condition outside empirical membership, but only as a relation-indexed possibility that remains uncognizable.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-09","CL_B337_24:GA-10","CL_B337_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Rational authority is jurisdictionally partitioned among empirical objectivity, regulative completion, and non-probative practical significance.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-12","CL_B337_24:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Transcendental illusion persists after diagnosis, so critical closure consists in recurrent reflexive self-limitation rather than final immunity from totalization.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-13","CL_B337_21:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-01","text":"The unconditioned retains identity as reason’s demand, mutates into a fictitious cosmological object, returns as a regulative boundary, and becomes conditionally admissible only as an intelligible ground outside empirical series.","refs":["CL_B337_22:RMD-01","CL_B337_24:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-02","text":"Totality reverses from a completed object-predicate into an unattainable task and finally into the horizon generated by iterative empirical synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_23:RMD-03","CL_B337_23:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-03","text":"Regress changes from a criterion selecting cosmological categories into a norm of empirical objectivity and then differentiates into outward composition, inward decomposition, and causal conditioning.","refs":["CL_B337_21:RMD-03","CL_B337_22:RMD-05","CL_B337_24:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-04","text":"Contradiction reverses from an obstruction to doctrinal knowledge into a diagnostic mechanism for exposing defective questions, equivocations, and illicit object-conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_21:RMD-07","CL_B337_23:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-05","text":"Idealism reverses from an apparent denial of reality into the condition under which empirical realism becomes sustainable without noumenal inflation.","refs":["CL_B337_22:RMD-06","CL_B337_22:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-06","text":"Infinity migrates from a supposed property of the world to a mode of permissible regression and then divides into indefinite composition and inexhaustible decomposition.","refs":["CL_B337_23:RMD-05","CL_B337_24:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-07","text":"Empirical incompletion first becomes the condition of objectivity and later coexists with complete empirical causal explanation because neither form entails metaphysical completion.","refs":["CL_B337_22:DBN-04","CL_B337_24:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-08","text":"The subject expands from an internally divided tribunal into a finite investigator and finally into a dual-character bearer of empirical necessity and intelligible attribution.","refs":["CL_B337_21:BSD-03","CL_B337_24:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-09","text":"Practical significance shifts from explaining doctrinal allegiance to supporting imputation, but it never acquires the intuition or cognition required for speculative proof.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GNX-06","CL_B337_24:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-10","text":"The world disappears as an independently available object and returns as the horizon and task generated by conditioned synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_21:RMD-04","CL_B337_24:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:CGD-01","text":"Arguments involving empty space, temporal beginnings, divisibility, and necessary existence remain local proof mechanisms; their scalable content is the recurrent failure of phenomenal completion.","refs":["CL_B337_21:CGD-01","CL_B337_24:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:CGD-02","text":"The fourfold cosmological table scales upward not as four independent doctrines but as a distributed antinomial machine later differentiated by synthesis-type.","refs":["CL_B337_21:CGD-03","CL_B337_21:RLE-06","CL_B337_24:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:CGD-03","text":"The taxonomy of practical, speculative, popular, and architectonic interests remains locally explanatory, while the non-probative partition between motivation and objective validity scales upward.","refs":["CL_B337_21:CGD-05","CL_B337_21:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:CGD-04","text":"Bilateral sceptical testing is transitional machinery whose current-level function is to force examination of the shared presupposition behind opposed doctrines.","refs":["CL_B337_22:CGD-03","CL_B337_22:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:CGD-05","text":"Examples involving absent, historical, distant, and future objects remain local demonstrations, but their model of open experiential continuation scales into empirical realism.","refs":["CL_B337_22:CGD-05","CL_B337_22:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:CGD-06","text":"The distinction between infinite and indefinite regress remains a local classification until it joins the mathematical–dynamical distinction as a general rule for sorting modes of synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_23:AS-B06","CL_B337_24:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:CGD-07","text":"Freedom and moral imputation remain dynamical applications, but they catalyze the higher-order distinction between empirical causal completeness and metaphysical exhaustion.","refs":["CL_B337_24:CGD-03","CL_B337_24:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:CGD-08","text":"The transcendental correlate remains a compressed local limit-concept rather than a global explanatory object, although it bridges appearances to the possibility of nonphenomenal grounding.","refs":["CL_B337_22:XD-05","CL_B337_22:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GNX-01","text":"The world cannot be treated as a completed empirical object or as a totality available to a surveying intuition.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GNX-01","CL_B337_24:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GNX-02","text":"A necessary thought of totality cannot establish a correspondingly determinate object.","refs":["CL_B337_22:GNX-01","CL_B337_21:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GNX-03","text":"No phenomenal item can serve as an absolutely first, simple, causally unconditioned, or necessary terminus.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GNX-04","CL_B337_24:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GNX-04","text":"Neither dogmatic affirmation nor sceptical ignorance is adequate when both preserve the fiction of a hidden cosmological object.","refs":["CL_B337_22:GNX-03","CL_B337_22:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GNX-05","text":"Empirical restriction cannot be converted into naturalistic denial of whatever may exceed phenomenal cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GNX-07","CL_B337_24:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GNX-06","text":"Operational non-termination cannot be converted into an already completed infinite aggregate.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-04","CL_B337_24:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GNX-07","text":"An intelligible condition cannot be a member, interruption, or additional cause within the phenomenal series, and its compatibility cannot establish its actuality.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GNX-06","CL_B337_24:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:GNX-08","text":"Practical indispensability, architectonic desirability, and moral imputation cannot provide speculative intuition or object-knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GNX-06","CL_B337_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires that appearances not be things in themselves, because independent phenomenal subsistence would restore the completed world-object and collapse intelligible compatibility.","refs":["CL_B337_24:DBN-01","CL_B337_22:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:DBN-02","text":"No standpoint outside sensibility may survey and compare the totality of appearances with things as they exist independently.","refs":["CL_B337_22:DBN-03","CL_B337_23:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:DBN-03","text":"Metaphysical completion cannot be a prerequisite of empirical objectivity, because unfinished regress is constitutive rather than defective.","refs":["CL_B337_22:DBN-04","CL_B337_22:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:DBN-04","text":"The jurisdiction of experience over objective cognition cannot entail that experience metaphysically exhausts possible being.","refs":["CL_B337_21:FL-03","CL_B337_24:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:DBN-05","text":"Critical success cannot mean elimination of illusion, partisan victory, or final immunity from renewed totalization.","refs":["CL_B337_21:DBN-05","CL_B337_21:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:DBN-06","text":"Empirical causal completeness cannot exhaust causality as such, and temporal succession cannot legislate over things in themselves.","refs":["CL_B337_24:MME-05","CL_B337_24:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absence of an intuition outside regress from which the world and its complete conditions could be surveyed together.","refs":["CL_B337_24:BSA-01","CL_B337_21:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSA-02","text":"Its unavailable coincidence is the union of reason’s formal demand for completion with an intuition of the absolute whole demanded.","refs":["CL_B337_23:BSA-02","CL_B337_21:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSA-03","text":"The foreclosed theoretical position would reconcile lawful natural explanation, completed systematic cognition, and practical agency within one total standpoint.","refs":["CL_B337_21:BSA-03","CL_B337_24:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSA-04","text":"The positive nature of intelligible grounding and the mediation by which it determines a particular temporal series remain structurally absent.","refs":["CL_B337_24:BSA-03","CL_B337_24:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSA-05","text":"No final experiential standpoint can terminate regress, extinguish totalizing demand, and convert provisional orientation into possession of the unconditioned.","refs":["CL_B337_21:BSA-04","CL_B337_23:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:XD-01","text":"Reciprocal support for opposed doctrines becomes critically resolvable only when transcendental idealism removes the realist object-presupposition common to both sides.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-08","CL_B337_21:GA-08","CL_B337_22:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:XD-02","text":"The same open regress that grounds empirical realism also makes knowledge non-terminating, revisable, and incapable of becoming intuition of a completed world.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-09","CL_B337_22:GA-07","CL_B337_23:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:XD-03","text":"Cosmological diagnosis must determine the mode of givenness and synthesis-type before deciding whether regress is indefinite, inexhaustible, or open to heterogeneous conditioning.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-11","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-12","CL_B337_23:GA-07","CL_B337_24:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:XD-04","text":"Practical significance can support the thinkability and attribution of freedom only because its authority remains partitioned from speculative proof.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-12","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-13","CL_B337_21:GA-12","CL_B337_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:XD-05","text":"Empirical realism and complete natural explanation are compatible with metaphysical underdetermination because empirical causal closure does not establish noumenal exhaustion.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-12","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-13","CL_B337_22:GA-06","CL_B337_24:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:XD-06","text":"Because transcendental illusion persists while totality returns as legitimate regulation, critique must operate as a permanent recursive discipline rather than a completed cure.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-14","CL_B337_21:GA-13","CL_B337_23:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:XD-07","text":"The unconditioned becomes conditionally thinkable only by leaving homogeneous empirical membership; it cannot re-enter experience as an exception or terminal event.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-11","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-12","CL_B337_23:GA-08","CL_B337_24:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:XD-08","text":"The indeterminate transcendental correlate expands into relation-indexed intelligible attribution without acquiring positive content or a determinate mechanism of efficacy.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-12","CL_B337_22:XD-05","CL_B337_24:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:XD-09","text":"The distinction between infinite and indefinite regress becomes operationally complete only when composition and division are recognized as non-identical forms of non-completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-11","CL_B337_23:AS-B06","CL_B337_24:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes a relation-indexed, lawfully connected field of appearances whose empirical autonomy does not entail metaphysical self-sufficiency.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-12","CL_B337_22:HSI-01","CL_B337_24:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-02","text":"Time becomes the successive phenomenal medium that prevents completed empirical totality, while intelligible grounding is displaced outside temporal change.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-12","CL_B337_23:HSI-03","CL_B337_24:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-03","text":"The subject becomes a finite investigator whose empirical character is fully conditionable while intelligible attribution remains thinkable under a distinct relation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-12","CL_B337_23:HSI-02","CL_B337_24:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-04","text":"Maximum empirical determinability can coexist with maximum metaphysical underdetermination because causal completeness and ontological exhaustion belong to different jurisdictions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-12","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-13","CL_B337_24:BSD-03","CL_B337_24:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-05","text":"Knowledge becomes non-terminating but normatively compelled: the demand for completion governs expansion and correction while forbidding completed possession.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-09","CL_B337_23:BSD-01","CL_B337_23:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-06","text":"Contradiction becomes a general critical algorithm for locating shared illicit object-conditions, classifying synthesis, and resetting the permissible proof-target.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-08","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-10","CL_B337_22:BSD-05","CL_B337_24:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-07","text":"Practical imputation can coexist with complete natural explanation, but no criterion can quantify the intelligible contribution to particular conduct.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-12","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-13","CL_B337_24:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-08","text":"Closure becomes jurisdictional rather than total: experience retains constitutive validity, reason retains regulative and practical force, and the unconditioned remains unpossessed.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-13","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-14","CL_B337_24:BSD-06","CL_B337_21:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-01","text":"Conditioned experience generates a demand for completion; projected completion generates antinomy; diagnosis returns completion as regulation; regulation renews inquiry into conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_21:RLE-01","CL_B337_23:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-02","text":"Every empirical advance partially satisfies reason while regenerating the demand for further conditions, producing expansion without terminal accumulation.","refs":["CL_B337_23:RLE-01","CL_B337_22:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-03","text":"The principal escalation proceeds from empirical category to transcendental idea, from idea to world-totality, and from world-totality to distributed antinomial conflict.","refs":["CL_B337_21:RLE-02","CL_B337_21:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-04","text":"Totality marks a phase change when it ceases to name a purported object and becomes the task-condition regulating the continued regress that displaced it.","refs":["CL_B337_23:AS-B05","CL_B337_23:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-05","text":"Regress becomes a conceptual machine by differentiating into indefinite composition, inexhaustible decomposition, and causal pressure toward spontaneous grounding.","refs":["CL_B337_24:RLE-06","CL_B337_24:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-06","text":"The unconditioned and possible experience form opposed attractors: one perpetually demands completion, while the other redirects cognition toward lawful and extensible inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_21:RLE-03","CL_B337_21:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-07","text":"Empirical causal closure and metaphysical openness form a productive recursive tension in which maximal explanation repeatedly fails to become ontological exhaustion.","refs":["CL_B337_24:XD-03","CL_B337_24:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-08","text":"Persistent illusion requires critique to be reapplied whenever regulation is converted back into constitution, thinkability into knowledge, or compatibility into proof.","refs":["CL_B337_21:RLE-07","CL_B337_24:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FGS-B01","text":"Conditioned appearance → demand for antecedent conditions → orientation toward the unconditioned.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FGS-B02","text":"Empirical category or rule → extension toward absolute completeness → projected world-totality.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FGS-B03","text":"Projected totality + empirical conditioning → opposed closures → distributed antinomial conflict.","refs":["CL_B337_21:AS-B07","CL_B337_21:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FGS-B04","text":"Reciprocal failure → exposure of shared transcendental realism → displacement from object-adjudication to cognitive diagnosis.","refs":["CL_B337_22:XD-03","CL_B337_22:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FGS-B05","text":"Transcendental idealism + empirical realism → appearances as objectively valid within possible experience → withdrawal of completed world-objecthood.","refs":["CL_B337_22:AS-B05","CL_B337_22:AS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FGS-B06","text":"Withdrawn object-totality → totality recoded as regulative task → open regress and indefinite empirical correction.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-03","CL_B337_23:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FGS-B07","text":"Open regress → classification by givenness and synthesis-type → differentiation of composition, division, and causal conditioning.","refs":["CL_B337_23:GA-07","CL_B337_24:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FGS-B08","text":"Homogeneous mathematical series → exclusion of completed unconditioned; heterogeneous dynamical series → intelligible compatibility without cognition or existential proof.","refs":["CL_B337_24:GA-06","CL_B337_24:GA-09","CL_B337_24:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned phenomenon → rational demand for complete conditions → unconditioned as unavoidable terminus of orientation.","refs":["CL_B337_21:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AFM-B02","text":"Extension of empirical synthesis → objectification of totality → world represented as completed independently of experience.","refs":["CL_B337_22:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AFM-B03","text":"Completed world-representation → finite and infinite closures → reciprocal contradiction and failure of doctrinal adjudication.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-08","CL_B337_23:AS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AFM-B04","text":"Reciprocal contradiction → diagnosis of shared realism → transcendental-idealist dissolution of the alleged object.","refs":["CL_B337_22:XD-03","CL_B337_22:AS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AFM-B05","text":"Dissolved world-object → empirical realism → totality retained as regulative procedure within possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_22:XD-04","CL_B337_23:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AFM-B06","text":"Regulative procedure → analysis of empirical givenness → classification by regress-form and synthesis-type.","refs":["CL_B337_23:RLE-06","CL_B337_24:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AFM-B07","text":"Homogeneous synthesis → indefinite or inexhaustible continuation; heterogeneous synthesis → possible intelligible grounding outside empirical membership.","refs":["CL_B337_24:AS-05","CL_B337_24:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:AFM-B08","text":"Empirical closure + metaphysical underdetermination → practical compatibility without speculative possession → recurrent critical self-limitation.","refs":["CL_B337_24:BSD-03","CL_B337_21:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MME-01","text":"The world is reclassified from a totalizable object into the horizon and task generated through conditioned empirical synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_21:MME-02","CL_B337_24:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MME-02","text":"The finite–infinite opposition is displaced by distinctions among empirical givenness, indefinite regress, inexhaustible division, and synthesis-type.","refs":["CL_B337_23:MME-01","CL_B337_24:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MME-03","text":"Realism and idealism cease to be exclusive alternatives: realism names lawful empirical validity, while idealism denies independent noumenal subsistence.","refs":["CL_B337_22:MME-01","CL_B337_22:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MME-04","text":"Dogmatism and scepticism collapse as exhaustive alternatives because both can preserve the same fictitious cosmological object.","refs":["CL_B337_22:MME-02","CL_B337_22:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MME-05","text":"Contradiction is reclassified from evidence of contradictory reality into evidence that a question has illicitly constituted its object.","refs":["CL_B337_21:MME-03","CL_B337_22:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MME-06","text":"Objective incompletion becomes a condition of empirical objectivity, correction, and continuing inquiry rather than a deficiency awaiting metaphysical closure.","refs":["CL_B337_23:BSD-06","CL_B337_22:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MME-07","text":"Empirical causal closure is separated from metaphysical exhaustion, allowing complete natural explanation without total naturalism.","refs":["CL_B337_24:MME-05","CL_B337_24:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MME-08","text":"Successful proof is redefined from positive cognition of the unconditioned to regulation of inquiry or removal of contradiction blocking legitimate thinkability.","refs":["CL_B337_24:MME-07","CL_B337_24:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system treats world-composition, division, causal origination, necessary existence, freedom, and the status of absent empirical objects.","refs":["CL_B337_24:AS-01","CL_B337_22:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, these problems are generated by the mismatch between reason’s demand for completion and empirically conditioned synthesis.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-06","CL_B337_23:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, the system replaces doctrinal selection with an algorithm that diagnoses shared object-conditions and reorganizes antinomies by synthesis-type.","refs":["CL_B337_22:BSD-05","CL_B337_24:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is an open phenomenal field ordered through succession, while intelligible grounding remains outside temporal change and empirical membership.","refs":["CL_B337_22:HSI-01","CL_B337_24:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, symmetrical conflict is repeatedly narrowed into regulation, jurisdiction, or non-opposition, thereby enacting the self-limitation asserted by the system.","refs":["CL_B337_21:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FL-01","text":"Equal rational support for opposed doctrines is strained by later resolutions that asymmetrically diagnose particular proofs and presuppositions.","refs":["CL_B337_21:GA-08","CL_B337_21:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FL-02","text":"Reason is obligated to answer cosmological questions, yet its answer often consists in cancelling the object-demand through which those questions were posed.","refs":["CL_B337_22:AS-B01","CL_B337_22:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FL-03","text":"Empirical objectivity depends on lawful possible experience, but the adequacy of lawful connection and the status of the transcendental correlate remain compressed assumptions.","refs":["CL_B337_22:FL-02","CL_B337_22:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FL-04","text":"Totality must exert practically axiom-like influence while remaining unattainable and deprived of constitutive authority.","refs":["CL_B337_23:FL-02","CL_B337_23:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FL-05","text":"The denial of constitutive world-knowledge sits uneasily beside negative formulations that appear to determine the world as lacking absolute beginnings or limits.","refs":["CL_B337_24:FL-01","CL_B337_24:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FL-06","text":"The asymmetry between false mathematical oppositions and reconcilable dynamical oppositions depends on preserving the contested distinction between homogeneous and heterogeneous conditioning.","refs":["CL_B337_24:FL-05","CL_B337_24:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FL-07","text":"Intelligible causality must ground an empirically sufficient temporal series without competing within it, but the mediation accomplishing this remains unformalized.","refs":["CL_B337_24:FL-03","CL_B337_24:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:FL-08","text":"Full empirical explanation and full imputation are jointly maintained even though no criterion can determine the intelligible contribution to particular conduct.","refs":["CL_B337_24:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MP-01","text":"The first mutation occurs when an empirical category changes function and becomes an idea directed toward absolute completion.","refs":["CL_B337_21:RMD-02","CL_B337_21:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MP-02","text":"Totality mutates from a constitutive claim concerning objects into a regulative rule governing inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_23:MP-02","CL_B337_23:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MP-03","text":"Infinity mutates from an ontological magnitude into procedurally distinct forms whose unity does not erase their different regress-structures.","refs":["CL_B337_24:MP-01","CL_B337_24:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MP-04","text":"The unconditioned mutates from an impossible mathematical completion into a conditionally admissible intelligible ground.","refs":["CL_B337_24:MP-02","CL_B337_24:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MP-05","text":"The subject mutates from a phenomenal node of conditioned cognition into a dual-character bearer of empirical necessity and intelligible attribution.","refs":["CL_B337_24:MP-03","CL_B337_24:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MP-06","text":"Scepticism mutates from an inadequate final posture into the cathartic mechanism that prepares transcendental diagnosis.","refs":["CL_B337_22:MP-02","CL_B337_22:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as an open, lawfully connected field of conditioned series whose empirical operation is autonomous but not metaphysically exhaustive.","refs":["CL_B337_22:HSI-01","CL_B337_24:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:HSI-02","text":"The subject appears as a finite investigator and divided tribunal, completely determined through empirical character yet thinkable under an intelligible relation.","refs":["CL_B337_23:HSI-02","CL_B337_24:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:HSI-03","text":"Time appears as the successive medium of indefinitely extensible synthesis, while intelligible grounding is represented as neither temporal nor changing.","refs":["CL_B337_22:HSI-03","CL_B337_24:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:HSI-04","text":"Navigation consists in critical reclassification, continued empirical inquiry, preservation of practical imputation, and refusal to convert thinkability into knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_23:HSI-04","CL_B337_24:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B01","text":"The axiom set is sufficient to reconstruct the movement from conditioned synthesis through totalizing objectification and antinomy to transcendental-idealist dissolution, regulative regress, and synthesis-specific closure.","refs":["CL_B337_22:MRT-B01","CL_B337_24:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B02","text":"The status of the transcendental correlate, the operational criteria for determining modes of givenness, and the mediation of intelligible causality remain underdetermined.","refs":["CL_B337_22:MRT-B04","CL_B337_23:FL-04","CL_B337_24:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if possible experience grounds objectivity without exhausting being, epistemic efficacy remains distinct from ontological determination, and time is restricted to appearances.","refs":["CL_B337_23:MRT-B04","CL_B337_24:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where it converts contradiction into diagnosis, operational non-termination into regulated inquiry, and causal completeness into non-exhaustive empirical validity.","refs":["CL_B337_21:MRT-B04","CL_B337_24:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if sequencing and recursive return are retained: construction precedes conflict, diagnosis precedes recoding, and regulation repeatedly reopens inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_21:MRT-B05","CL_B337_23:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B06","text":"Practical force, intelligible grounding, and negative closure remain resistant to complete axiomatic reduction because their efficacy depends on preserving what cannot become constitutive knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_23:MRT-B06","CL_B337_24:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"B337 L1 06","context":"Chapters 61–72 · Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas · Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason · Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions · Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems · Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas · Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic · Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem · Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas · Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas · I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe · II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition · III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes"}},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07","layer":"MC","title":"B337 L1 07","order":131,"parents":["CL_B337_25","CL_B337_26","CL_B337_27","CL_B337_28"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"5bf31a5cf868660547e3c525bb6329f3784667f011c9b4dabcc19b514073ea0e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AS-01","text":"Reason must answer its demand for unconditioned completeness while constructing a secure system from cognition that cannot exceed possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_25:AS-B01","CL_B337_28:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AS-02","text":"The governing thesis is that the limit conditioning objective cognition must be converted into a positive regulatory architecture rather than treated as an external deprivation.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-01","CL_B337_28:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AS-03","text":"At this scale, critique becomes an architectonic conversion machine that transforms failed metaphysical claims into rules governing cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_27:AS-03","CL_B337_28:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AS-04","text":"Reason individualizes its demand for totality as an ideal, then converts the unavoidable demand into apparent evidence for a transcendent object.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-06","CL_B337_26:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AS-05","text":"The plurality of speculative proofs is therefore reorganized as dependent variants of one illicit passage from rational necessity to existence.","refs":["CL_B337_26:AS-02","CL_B337_27:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AS-06","text":"The decisive audit denies that existence belongs to conceptual determination and restricts determinate cognition to possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-05","CL_B337_27:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AS-07","text":"Speculative failure bifurcates rather than terminates reason, transforming defeated theological object-claims into regulative method and categorically distinct practical warrant.","refs":["CL_B337_26:AS-08","CL_B337_27:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AS-08","text":"Critical limitation becomes productive by governing the validity of methods and intensifying inquiry within the domain it bounds.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-14","CL_B337_28:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AS-09","text":"The movement is recursive: speculative ambition exceeds finite cognition, critique interrupts the excess, and the same ambition returns as disciplined systematicity.","refs":["CL_B337_25:AS-B07","CL_B337_28:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AS-10","text":"Closure is asymptotic and architectonic: the system becomes formally complete but materially open, preserving orientation toward unity while excluding attained speculative totality.","refs":["CL_B337_27:AS-08","CL_B337_28:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Determinate objective cognition requires sensible presentation and remains bounded by possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-01","CL_B337_26:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Conditioned existence necessarily activates a demand for unconditioned completion, whose dominant form is complete systematic unity.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-01","CL_B337_27:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Complete reality supplies the ideal horizon of determination, but complete determination does not establish the existence of an object corresponding to it.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-04","CL_B337_26:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-04","text":"[Terminal] Existence is not conceptual content, so pure concepts provide no direct transition to objective existence.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-05","CL_B337_28:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Categories such as causality have determinate meaning only within possible experience and cannot identify a supersensible ground.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-03","CL_B337_26:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] The speculative proofs form a dependency system in which allegedly empirical routes are absorbed into the same concept-to-existence inference.","refs":["CL_B337_26:RMD-06","CL_B337_27:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Transcendental ideas arise necessarily without constituting supersensible objects, and dialectic begins only when their employment becomes constitutive.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-04","CL_B337_27:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Theoretical regulation, practical postulation, and objective cognition are nonconvertible modes of rational validity.","refs":["CL_B337_27:AS-07","CL_B337_27:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] An idea can acquire indirect and indeterminate validity by systematizing empirical cognition without determining a transcendent object.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-08","CL_B337_27:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Negative discipline is a constitutive condition of productive inquiry because critical limitation regulates reason’s legitimate employment.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-06","CL_B337_27:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Method is indexed to cognitive conditions; mathematical construction has local validity and cannot become a universal model for philosophy.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-04","CL_B337_28:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Transcendental illusion is a natural and recurrent structural effect that requires permanent critical governance.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-11","CL_B337_27:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Reason preserves systematic authority only by renouncing unrestricted speculative construction and becoming systematically non-dogmatical.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-12","CL_B337_28:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-01","text":"[Mutation] The unconditioned moves from a possible intelligible ground, through an individualized ideal, into an asymptotic horizon organizing empirical inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_25:RMD-01","CL_B337_27:RMD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-02","text":"[Functional recurrence] Complete determination persists as the material horizon of predicates but loses every power to decide existence.","refs":["CL_B337_25:RMD-03","CL_B337_26:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Necessity changes from an alleged property of a transcendent object into a regulative requirement and then reappears as a distinct practical warrant.","refs":["CL_B337_26:RMD-02","CL_B337_27:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-04","text":"[Displacement] The ens realissimum migrates from candidate necessary being to hidden proof-premise, regulative ideal, and suspended theological representation.","refs":["CL_B337_26:RMD-03","CL_B337_27:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] The boundary of possible experience changes from a prohibition on transcendence into a condition of empirical productivity.","refs":["CL_B337_27:AS-06","CL_B337_27:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-06","text":"[Terminal transformation] Natural purposiveness moves from attempted proof to heuristic orientation, while mechanism and teleology become differentiated functions rather than rival causal orders.","refs":["CL_B337_27:RMD-01","CL_B337_27:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-07","text":"[Escalation] Critique changes from a negative inventory of limits into a phase-changing architecture of method and jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_28:RMD-02","CL_B337_28:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-08","text":"[Reversal] Mathematical success changes from an apparent model for philosophy into evidence that constructive certainty depends on local cognitive conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_28:RMD-05","CL_B337_28:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-09","text":"[Absorption] Method moves from the formal plan of systematic unity into an architecture governing the jurisdiction of heterogeneous cognitive procedures.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-03","CL_B337_28:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-10","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] Metaphysical object-claims disappear as knowledge claims after being converted into rules through which systematic inquiry survives their defeat.","refs":["CL_B337_27:RMD-05","CL_B337_28:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:CGD-01","text":"[Local machinery] The distinction between mathematical and dynamical regress remains local, while its scalable result is the nonidentity of empirical and intelligible conditioning.","refs":["CL_B337_25:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:CGD-02","text":"[Local machinery] The architect–creator distinction performs a specific demolition of physico-theology and then disappears into the broader constitutive–regulative distinction.","refs":["CL_B337_27:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:CGD-03","text":"[Local machinery] Algebraic signs, definitions, axioms, and demonstrations supply the mathematical test case but do not remain universal marks of rigor.","refs":["CL_B337_28:CGD-06","CL_B337_28:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:CGD-04","text":"[Scaled upward] The distinction between objective reality and regulative validity becomes a general partition among determinate cognition, indirect orientation, and practical postulation.","refs":["CL_B337_25:CGD-03","CL_B337_27:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] The individualization of an idea connects rational totalization to transcendental subreption by making functional unity appear object-eligible.","refs":["CL_B337_25:CGD-04","CL_B337_25:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:CGD-06","text":"[Transition catalyst] Formal conversion and the dependency of physico-theology expose the apparent plurality of proofs as one recurrent transcendent transition.","refs":["CL_B337_26:CGD-05","CL_B337_27:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:CGD-07","text":"[Scaled upward] The general negative code of discipline converts proof-critique into a permanent architecture for governing cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_28:CGD-04","CL_B337_28:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:CGD-08","text":"[Recoded] Mathematical rigor remains valid where construction is available but becomes a difference of cognitive procedure rather than a universal philosophical standard.","refs":["CL_B337_28:CGD-08","CL_B337_28:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:CGD-09","text":"[Superseded] The necessary being ceases to function as a discovered explanatory terminus and survives only as ideal completion, regulative orientation, or practical postulate.","refs":["CL_B337_26:CGD-06","CL_B337_27:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GNX-01","text":"No continuous passage from sensible cognition to determinate knowledge of a supersensible being is available.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GNX-01","CL_B337_27:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GNX-02","text":"No empirical regress may terminate in a highest member merely because its incompleteness demands further explanation.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GNX-01","CL_B337_26:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GNX-03","text":"No increase in conceptual completeness can generate the existence of the concept’s object.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GNX-02","CL_B337_26:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GNX-04","text":"Phenomenal causality cannot determine an extramundane ground because no causal law valid within appearances crosses the experiential boundary.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GNX-04","CL_B337_27:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GNX-05","text":"Teleology cannot terminate investigation or replace mechanical explanation with a second constitutive causal order.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GNX-07","CL_B337_27:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GNX-06","text":"Mathematical success cannot legitimate the transfer of construction or demonstration into domains where no corresponding intuition is available.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GNX-05","CL_B337_28:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GNX-07","text":"The impossibility of speculative proof entails neither divine non-existence nor the abolition of transcendental ideas.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GNX-06","CL_B337_27:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:GNX-08","text":"Rejecting dogmatism does not require rejecting systematic philosophy, because systematicity can be detached from unrestricted construction.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GNX-06","CL_B337_28:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:DBN-01","text":"The structure of reason’s need must not be treated as isomorphic with the structure of being.","refs":["CL_B337_26:DBN-01","CL_B337_26:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:DBN-02","text":"Explanatory completion must not become ontological completion or authorize a final existent.","refs":["CL_B337_26:DBN-02","CL_B337_26:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:DBN-03","text":"Formal coherence must not be confused with the production or presentation of an object.","refs":["CL_B337_28:DBN-01","CL_B337_26:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:DBN-04","text":"Systematic unity must remain possible without being attributed to a projected cause hypostatized as a Supreme Being.","refs":["CL_B337_26:DBN-05","CL_B337_27:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:DBN-05","text":"No theoretical standpoint outside possible experience can inspect conditioned reality and its unconditioned ground together.","refs":["CL_B337_26:BSA-03","CL_B337_27:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:DBN-06","text":"Reason cannot authenticate every employment merely by legislating for itself, because the need for discipline invalidates unrestricted self-certification.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GNX-04","CL_B337_28:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:DBN-07","text":"Cognitive finitude cannot be treated as an external defect, because experiential limits are internal validity conditions and the productive medium of objective cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_28:MME-02","CL_B337_28:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absence of an intellectual intuition capable of presenting total or supersensible reality directly.","refs":["CL_B337_25:BSA-03","CL_B337_28:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSA-02","text":"Its deepest missing operation is a legitimate bridge from conditioned existence to an unconditioned existent.","refs":["CL_B337_26:BSA-01","CL_B337_27:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSA-03","text":"Complete unity functions as an indispensable but unpresentable center because completed totality is structurally absent from experience.","refs":["CL_B337_27:BSA-01","CL_B337_27:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSA-04","text":"The correspondence between reason’s demand for systematicity and nature’s presumed unifiability lacks an available ground.","refs":["CL_B337_27:BSA-02","CL_B337_27:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSA-05","text":"The unavailable fantasy is a reason capable of retaining authority without renouncing overreach; actual authority arises only through self-limitation.","refs":["CL_B337_28:BSA-05","CL_B337_28:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSA-06","text":"The theological object remains structurally suspended because reason cannot eliminate its demand for a ground or make that ground theoretically affirmable.","refs":["CL_B337_27:BSA-05","CL_B337_26:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:XD-01","text":"The ideal of complete determination can orient every determination only because it is denied the power to produce the existence of its object.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-06","CL_B337_26:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:XD-02","text":"Once the speculative proofs collapse into a dependency system, preserving theology requires transforming its object-claims into nonconstitutive functions.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-08","CL_B337_27:XD-01","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:XD-03","text":"Because the transition from ideal to object is recurrent rather than accidental, negative critique must become a permanent institution of reason rather than a completed refutation.","refs":["CL_B337_25:RLE-05","CL_B337_28:RLE-06","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-12","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:XD-04","text":"Open empirical conditioning and complete systematic unity jointly require inquiry to seek completeness while refusing every attained closure.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-02","CL_B337_27:GA-13","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:XD-05","text":"The experiential boundary validates methods precisely by preventing transcendence and redirecting rational ambition into expanding empirical inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_27:XD-05","CL_B337_28:XD-06","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:XD-06","text":"Since existence is not conceptual content and mathematical certainty depends on construction, constructive evidence cannot serve as a model for metaphysical existence claims.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-05","CL_B337_28:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:XD-07","text":"Speculative failure produces distinct practical and regulative survivals whose rational force depends on their remaining nonconvertible.","refs":["CL_B337_26:RLE-06","CL_B337_27:XD-06","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-08","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:XD-08","text":"Mechanism and teleology become compatible once each method is indexed to a different cognitive function rather than treated as a competing ontology.","refs":["CL_B337_27:BSD-07","CL_B337_28:XD-02","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-09","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:XD-09","text":"Repeated negative critique becomes positively generative by converting every excluded overreach into a further determination of legitimate reason.","refs":["CL_B337_27:BSD-08","CL_B337_28:BSD-07","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-01","text":"Metaphysics becomes the diagnosis and governance of reason’s self-objectification rather than cognition of a supersensible inventory.","refs":["CL_B337_26:BSD-01","CL_B337_28:BSD-01","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-02","text":"Objectivity becomes stratified among determinate empirical cognition, indirect systematic validity, and practical assent.","refs":["CL_B337_27:BSD-02","CL_B337_27:BSD-05","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-08","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-03","text":"The rational subject becomes a systematizing tribunal that generates projections and installs a negative code against its own recurrent overreach.","refs":["CL_B337_27:BSD-04","CL_B337_28:HSI-02","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-12","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-04","text":"Rational time becomes indefinitely progressive convergence toward a completeness that can regulate every present inquiry but never become present as a whole.","refs":["CL_B337_26:BSD-03","CL_B337_27:BSD-03","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-05","text":"Reality becomes an open field of conditioned phenomena organized by a projected unconditioned horizon that cannot enter the field as an object.","refs":["CL_B337_25:HSI-01","CL_B337_26:HSI-01","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-06","text":"Theology no longer names one object with one status but a distribution among ideal determination, failed proof, regulative orientation, and practical postulation.","refs":["CL_B337_26:BSD-02","CL_B337_27:BSD-05","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-08","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-07","text":"Causal explanation becomes wholly immanent, while teleology survives as a regulative orientation toward systematic connection.","refs":["CL_B337_27:BSD-06","CL_B337_27:BSD-07","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-09","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-08","text":"Philosophy acquires second-order authority by assigning jurisdictions to methods and making the negative system positively generative.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-10","CL_B337_28:BSD-07","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-11","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-09","text":"Rational authority now consists in sustaining the maximal search for explanation within a formally complete but materially open architecture.","refs":["CL_B337_26:BSD-05","CL_B337_28:AS-08","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RLE-01","text":"The primary loop runs from the demand for unity to a hypostatized ideal, through critical exposure, and back to the ideal’s non-objective function.","refs":["CL_B337_26:RLE-01","CL_B337_26:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RLE-02","text":"Totality is the persistent attractor that mutates from total conditions and complete reality into systematic unity beyond attainment.","refs":["CL_B337_25:RLE-04","CL_B337_27:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RLE-03","text":"The main escalation path moves from empirical existence to necessary ground, then through formative intelligence and absolute necessity to complete reality.","refs":["CL_B337_26:RLE-02","CL_B337_27:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RLE-04","text":"The proof-system closes recursively when the cosmological argument requires the ontological inference and physico-theology depends on both.","refs":["CL_B337_26:RLE-03","CL_B337_27:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RLE-05","text":"Inquiry spirals because each gain in systematicity renews the demand for completion and returns cognition to correction rather than closure.","refs":["CL_B337_27:RLE-06","CL_B337_28:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RLE-06","text":"A decisive phase change occurs when the experiential boundary becomes a productive methodological condition rather than a merely prohibitive edge.","refs":["CL_B337_27:RLE-07","CL_B337_28:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RLE-07","text":"Mathematical success invites transfer, transfer produces dogmatism, and critique returns that success to its local conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_28:RLE-03","CL_B337_28:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RLE-08","text":"Reason’s self-legislation loops through misuse and discipline, thereby restoring authority while preventing that authority from authenticating itself immediately.","refs":["CL_B337_28:RLE-02","CL_B337_28:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:RLE-09","text":"The terminal attractor is systematic but non-dogmatical reason whose closure remains asymptotic rather than doctrinal.","refs":["CL_B337_28:RLE-07","CL_B337_27:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FGS-B01","text":"Sensibly gated objectivity plus contingent phenomenal existence generates an open empirical field without a final presented condition.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-01","CL_B337_25:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FGS-B02","text":"Empirical incompleteness generates rational totalization and the demand for an unconditioned ground.","refs":["CL_B337_25:AS-B07","CL_B337_26:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FGS-B03","text":"The demand for determination presupposes a totality of affirmative reality that reason individualizes as an ideal.","refs":["CL_B337_25:AS-B04","CL_B337_25:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FGS-B04","text":"Individualized regulative unity is converted into an apparent object, and speculative theology originates from that conversion.","refs":["CL_B337_25:AS-B05","CL_B337_26:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FGS-B05","text":"Ontological, dependency, and jurisdictional audits show that existence adds no conceptual determination, empirical proofs depend on conceptual conversion, and causality remains experiential.","refs":["CL_B337_26:AS-05","CL_B337_26:AS-06","CL_B337_27:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FGS-B06","text":"Speculative failure bifurcates the defeated theological object into suspended theory, practical postulation, and regulative orientation.","refs":["CL_B337_26:AS-08","CL_B337_27:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FGS-B07","text":"Regulative ideas systematize the understanding and direct teleological inquiry under universal natural laws.","refs":["CL_B337_27:MME-03","CL_B337_27:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FGS-B08","text":"Negative discipline regulates the employment of cognition and prevents the return of constitutive illusion through method-transfer.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-06","CL_B337_28:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FGS-B09","text":"The resulting architecture preserves systematic authority by becoming formally complete but materially open.","refs":["CL_B337_28:GA-12","CL_B337_28:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AFM-B01","text":"Conditioned phenomenal existence → indefinite empirical regress → demand for unconditioned completeness.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-02","CL_B337_25:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AFM-B02","text":"Demand for completion → total horizon of determination → individualized ideal → risk of hypostatization.","refs":["CL_B337_25:GA-04","CL_B337_25:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AFM-B03","text":"Hypostatized unity → plurality of speculative proofs → dependency hierarchy → collapse into one invalid transition.","refs":["CL_B337_25:AS-B05","CL_B337_26:AS-02","CL_B337_26:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AFM-B04","text":"Audit of existence and categories → restriction to possible experience → non-demonstrability rather than non-existence.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-05","CL_B337_26:GA-09","CL_B337_26:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AFM-B05","text":"Failed theological object → differentiated practical and regulative survivals → complete systematic unity as an unattainable maximum.","refs":["CL_B337_27:BSD-05","CL_B337_27:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:AFM-B06","text":"Asymptotic inquiry → recurrent illusion → negative discipline → self-limiting architectonic closure.","refs":["CL_B337_26:GA-11","CL_B337_28:GA-06","CL_B337_28:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MME-01","text":"The distinction between ideal and object replaces the opposition between existent reality and arbitrary fiction with a plurality of non-equivalent rational functions.","refs":["CL_B337_25:MME-02","CL_B337_26:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MME-02","text":"The traditional proofs are reorganized into a hierarchy governed by one recurrent conversion of conceptual or explanatory necessity into existence.","refs":["CL_B337_26:MME-01","CL_B337_27:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MME-03","text":"Objective validity divides into determinate cognition and indirect regulation rather than remaining a univocal relation between representation and object.","refs":["CL_B337_27:BSD-02","CL_B337_27:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MME-04","text":"Cognitive boundaries become conditions of empirical productivity and internal validity rather than external restraints on an otherwise complete cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_27:MME-05","CL_B337_28:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MME-05","text":"Mechanism and teleology cease to be competing ontologies and become constitutive explanation and regulative orientation.","refs":["CL_B337_27:BSD-07","CL_B337_27:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MME-06","text":"Method becomes an architecture governing jurisdiction, while the mathematics–philosophy boundary becomes a difference of cognitive procedures.","refs":["CL_B337_28:MME-01","CL_B337_28:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MME-07","text":"Error is reclassified as a permanent structural effect and a generative system produced by principles, projections, and method-transfer.","refs":["CL_B337_27:MME-08","CL_B337_28:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MME-08","text":"The opposition between negative and positive cognition collapses because exclusion becomes the condition of legitimate construction.","refs":["CL_B337_28:MME-03","CL_B337_28:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system tests proofs of a Supreme Being, determines the surviving uses of transcendental ideas, and constructs a secure system of bounded reason.","refs":["CL_B337_27:ALT-B01","CL_B337_28:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, sensibility conditions presentation, the understanding determines experienced objects, and reason assigns each method the mode in which it may operate.","refs":["CL_B337_27:ALT-B02","CL_B337_28:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, theology is displaced from object-proof toward the regulation of construction, systematic inquiry, and method-transfer.","refs":["CL_B337_27:ALT-B03","CL_B337_28:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, unconditioned completion recedes indefinitely while empirical explanation becomes progressively extensible.","refs":["CL_B337_27:ALT-B04","CL_B337_26:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the system reenacts transcendental overreach, reverses each proof’s apparent direction, and reconstructs its residue as method.","refs":["CL_B337_27:ALT-B05","CL_B337_26:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:ALT-B06","text":"At the normative level, practical assent remains rationally available only by remaining categorically distinct from speculative cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_26:ALT-B06","CL_B337_27:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FL-01","text":"Legitimate thought of an intelligible ground remains unstable because any relaxation of its critical status makes it vulnerable to constitutive reification.","refs":["CL_B337_25:FL-01","CL_B337_26:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FL-02","text":"Systematic unity oscillates between subjective maxim, objective but indeterminate principle, and an unexplained ground of fit between reason and nature.","refs":["CL_B337_27:FL-01","CL_B337_27:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FL-03","text":"Reason must legislate and discipline itself even though its role as legislator and source of error prevents immediate self-authentication.","refs":["CL_B337_28:FL-02","CL_B337_28:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FL-04","text":"Possible experience functions both as the medium validating transcendental principles and as what those principles claim to make possible, producing an unresolved justificatory loop.","refs":["CL_B337_28:RLE-04","CL_B337_28:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FL-05","text":"Practical theology retains rational authority after speculative failure, but its positive derivation remains deferred.","refs":["CL_B337_26:FL-04","CL_B337_27:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FL-06","text":"The demand for a complete rational system remains in tension with closure that is formally complete but materially open.","refs":["CL_B337_28:FL-01","CL_B337_28:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:FL-07","text":"The regulative recovery of reason depends on an unproved purposive assumption that reason is not naturally ordered toward unavoidable constitutive deception.","refs":["CL_B337_27:DBN-05","CL_B337_27:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MP-01","text":"The unconditioned changes from a possible external ground of appearances into an ideal horizon governing determination and inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_25:MP-01","CL_B337_25:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MP-02","text":"Necessity mutates from an alleged object-property into regulative demand and then into practical warrant.","refs":["CL_B337_26:MP-01","CL_B337_27:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MP-03","text":"Dialectic is relocated from the transcendental idea itself to the constitutive misemployment of that idea.","refs":["CL_B337_27:GA-05","CL_B337_27:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MP-04","text":"Limit mutates from a restriction on scope into a condition of empirical productivity and methodological validity.","refs":["CL_B337_28:MP-02","CL_B337_27:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MP-05","text":"Negative judgment mutates from epistemic deficiency into the phase-changing architecture of legitimate cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_28:MP-04","CL_B337_28:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MP-06","text":"The theological object mutates into a distributed set of theoretical, practical, and regulative functions.","refs":["CL_B337_27:MP-04","CL_B337_27:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MP-07","text":"Mathematical certainty mutates from a model of universal rigor into a locally valid procedure whose imitation generates false philosophical evidence.","refs":["CL_B337_28:MP-03","CL_B337_28:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a layered cognitive machine and finite architectonic whose passages are gated by conditions of presentation and validity.","refs":["CL_B337_27:HSI-01","CL_B337_28:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite systematizer and internal tribunal that generates ideals, legislates cognition, and installs a negative code against itself.","refs":["CL_B337_27:HSI-02","CL_B337_28:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:HSI-03","text":"Time is indefinite convergence governed by recursive and corrigible inquiry rather than the completed presence of totality.","refs":["CL_B337_27:HSI-03","CL_B337_28:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:HSI-04","text":"Navigation remains possible by retaining regulative force, using critique against conflation, and converting speculative expansion into self-limiting movement.","refs":["CL_B337_25:HSI-04","CL_B337_27:HSI-04","CL_B337_28:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from rational totalization through failed objectification to regulative and methodological recovery.","refs":["CL_B337_27:MRT-B01","CL_B337_28:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is strongest where conditions of presentation explain both the invalidity of transcendent proof and the legitimacy of domain-specific cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_27:MRT-B02","CL_B337_28:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MRT-B03","text":"Coherence requires speculative non-demonstrability to remain distinct from non-existence and negative critique to remain a productive transformation.","refs":["CL_B337_26:MRT-B03","CL_B337_27:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MRT-B04","text":"The principal underdetermination concerns the exact objective status of systematic unity and the unavailable ground of fit between reason and nature.","refs":["CL_B337_27:MRT-B03","CL_B337_27:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining the recursive sequence of projection, exposure, reclassification, and renewed critical discipline.","refs":["CL_B337_26:MRT-B05","CL_B337_27:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MRT-B06","text":"The architecture remains weakest at the circular relation between experience and transcendental principles and at reason’s inability to authenticate its own legislation directly.","refs":["CL_B337_28:MRT-B05","CL_B337_28:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MRT-B07","text":"Practical theology and direct supersensible presentation remain resistant to axiomatic closure because the former’s derivation is deferred and the latter would require intellectual intuition.","refs":["CL_B337_27:CGD-07","CL_B337_28:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_07:MRT-B08","text":"Reconstruction is adequate only if closure remains asymptotic, formally complete, and materially open rather than becoming a recovered metaphysical totality.","refs":["CL_B337_27:AS-08","CL_B337_28:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"B337 L1 07","context":"Chapters 73–84 · IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences · Section I. Of the Ideal in General · Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale) · Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being · Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God · Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God · Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof · Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason · Appendix · II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method · Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason · Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism"}},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08","layer":"MC","title":"B337 L1 08","order":132,"parents":["CL_B337_29","CL_B337_30","CL_B337_31"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"826a9e9f09a478050facffa8da20cae058f409ad80a9dc32b9d1de46575ec863","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AS-01","text":"Finite reason must preserve unrestricted inquiry, systematic aspiration, and practical orientation without allowing private conviction, speculative totalization, or inherited doctrine to masquerade as objective knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_29:AS-B01","CL_B337_30:AS-01","CL_B337_31:AS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AS-02","text":"Critique solves this problem by converting limitation into a self-imposed jurisdiction that authorizes cognition, redistributes noncognitive claims, and regulates reason’s recurrent excesses.","refs":["CL_B337_29:AS-B03","CL_B337_30:AS-02","CL_B337_31:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AS-03","text":"At this scale, the argument becomes a differentiated constitutional theory of reason in which every operation must answer to its object, source, scope, systemic position, and mode of warrant.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-03","CL_B337_31:GA-02","CL_B337_31:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AS-04","text":"Supersensible ideas are denied theoretical possession but retained as regulative orientations, practical hypotheses, defensive limits, and horizons of systematic completion.","refs":["CL_B337_30:RMD-02","CL_B337_31:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AS-05","text":"Moral law converts speculative absence into practical agency, while nature’s failure to unite virtue and happiness generates hope and a morally ordered horizon without yielding cognition of that horizon.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-09","CL_B337_30:GA-11","CL_B337_30:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AS-06","text":"Rational autonomy expands from individual self-legislation into communicable, collectively reproducible, and architectonically organized critical practice.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-12","CL_B337_31:GA-11","CL_B337_31:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AS-07","text":"The movement is recursive and spiral: overreach produces conflict, conflict produces jurisdiction, jurisdiction redirects reason toward practice, and every practical postulate returns to critique when it threatens to become theory.","refs":["CL_B337_29:AS-B07","CL_B337_30:RLE-01","CL_B337_31:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AS-08","text":"Closure is therefore lawful but unfinished: cognitive peace, moral orientation, and public critical infrastructure are established, while completed philosophy and final unity remain absent regulative ends.","refs":["CL_B337_29:AS-B08","CL_B337_31:AS-B09","CL_B337_31:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-01","text":"[Emergent] Rational authority is jurisdictional: a claim is valid only when its object, cognitive source, authorized scope, mode of use, and systemic position agree.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-01","CL_B337_31:GA-02","CL_B337_31:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Possible experience remains the positive condition of theoretical objectivity and the negative boundary against constitutive supersensible use.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-01","CL_B337_29:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Reason remains directed beyond experience toward completion even though the completion it demands cannot become a theoretically possessed object.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-02","CL_B337_31:AS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Knowledge, hypothesis, regulation, defence, practical belief, obligation, and hope are irreducible rational operations with distinct warrants.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-03","CL_B337_30:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Limitation changes from prohibition into a productive jurisdiction that protects empirical cognition, reallocates supersensible orientation, and organizes critical method.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-04","CL_B337_31:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Autonomous moral law has unconditional practical priority and cannot derive its authority from theology, happiness, utility, or promised reward.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-09","CL_B337_30:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Natural explanation and moral legislation are irreducible registers; neither causal description nor normative prescription can absorb the other.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-08","CL_B337_30:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] The failure of nature to connect moral worth and happiness generates rational hope and practically necessary postulation without theoretical demonstration.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-11","CL_B337_30:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Supersensible commitments survive only as regulated, defensive, or practical uses and may never be promoted into theoretical possession.","refs":["CL_B337_30:BSD-02","CL_B337_31:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Systematic unity migrates from speculative totality into the ethical coordination of ends and the demanded but unavailable unity of nature and freedom.","refs":["CL_B337_30:RMD-08","CL_B337_31:GA-06","CL_B337_31:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Rational autonomy requires unrestricted criticism, regeneration from principles, communicability, and collective reproducibility under self-legislated rules.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-12","CL_B337_31:GA-10","CL_B337_31:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Metaphysics changes from attempted supersensible acquisition into a critical institution coordinating the legitimate employments of reason.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-09","CL_B337_31:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-13","text":"[Persistent] Dialectical excess is recurrent and must be converted into an internal diagnostic and permanently maintained critical practice.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-09","CL_B337_31:AS-B07","CL_B337_31:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] The absence of completed philosophy regulates inquiry by turning completion from a possession into a collectively maintained orientation.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-14","CL_B337_31:BSA-03","CL_B337_31:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-01","text":"[Stable Identity / Scope Expansion] Possible experience persists as the boundary of cognition but expands into one component of a larger jurisdiction involving source, scope, use, and systemic position.","refs":["CL_B337_29:RMD-01","CL_B337_29:CGD-04","CL_B337_31:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] Limitation moves from negative restraint to protective jurisdiction and finally to the productive operation that organizes reason’s critical infrastructure.","refs":["CL_B337_30:RMD-05","CL_B337_31:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-03","text":"[Domain Migration] Supersensible ideas migrate from disputed objects to regulative functions, practical hypotheses, and bounded noncognitive orientations.","refs":["CL_B337_29:RMD-03","CL_B337_30:RMD-02","CL_B337_31:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-04","text":"[Functional Differentiation] Freedom divides into unresolved transcendental causality, operative rational self-determination, and the collective moral order practical agency ought to realize.","refs":["CL_B337_30:RMD-03","CL_B337_30:RLE-05","CL_B337_31:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Happiness changes from an inadmissible motive of morality into a consequence conditionally proportioned to moral worth.","refs":["CL_B337_30:RMD-04","CL_B337_30:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-06","text":"[Escalation / Absorption] Systematic unity shifts from cognitive totalization through ethical completion into the regulative attractor coordinating nature, freedom, and the unfinished system of philosophy.","refs":["CL_B337_30:RMD-08","CL_B337_30:MP-05","CL_B337_31:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-07","text":"[Displacement] Opposition moves from external combat into internal diagnosis and then into a historical map of recurrent failures in rational self-government.","refs":["CL_B337_29:RMD-04","CL_B337_31:RMD-06","CL_B337_31:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-08","text":"[Reversal] Metaphysics reverses from speculative foundation and enlargement into a boundary-regulating institution whose authority consists in coordination rather than discovery.","refs":["CL_B337_31:RMD-05","CL_B337_31:GA-09","CL_B337_31:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-09","text":"[Scope Expansion] Communicability develops from a fallible test of assent into the collective reproducibility required by public critical method.","refs":["CL_B337_31:RMD-01","CL_B337_31:GA-11","CL_B337_31:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-10","text":"[Terminal Transformation] Scepticism disappears as a final position but survives as a recurrent interruption absorbed into permanent critical government.","refs":["CL_B337_29:RMD-07","CL_B337_31:GNX-07","CL_B337_31:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Consequence-prediction for empirical hypotheses and singular proof-ground requirements remain specialized tests rather than the complete current-level jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_29:CGD-02","CL_B337_29:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:CGD-02","text":"[Local] Determinate divine attributes and named moral postulates remain downstream specifications; the scalable structure is their noncognitive practical authorization.","refs":["CL_B337_30:CGD-03","CL_B337_31:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:CGD-03","text":"[Local] Taxonomies of assent and detailed divisions of metaphysical disciplines remain lower-layer classification machinery.","refs":["CL_B337_31:CGD-01","CL_B337_31:CGD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:CGD-04","text":"[Scales Upward] Possible experience scales upward as the common theoretical boundary nested within a broader protective jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_29:CGD-04","CL_B337_30:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:CGD-05","text":"[Scales Upward] The differentiation of rational operations by employment becomes the general rule for distributing authority across knowledge, belief, obligation, and hope.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GA-03","CL_B337_30:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] The supreme good bridges autonomous moral legislation and architectonic teleology by ordering cognition toward a moral unity without making that unity knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_30:GA-13","CL_B337_31:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:CGD-07","text":"[Catalyst] Strengthened opposition and nature’s moral indifference catalyze the two principal transitions: from dispute to jurisdiction and from obligation to hope.","refs":["CL_B337_29:CGD-06","CL_B337_30:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:CGD-08","text":"[Superseded] Rhetorical victory and doctrinal accumulation are recoded as failures because neither supplies objective entitlement nor methodological progress.","refs":["CL_B337_29:CGD-08","CL_B337_31:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GNX-01","text":"Supersensible objects cannot be given, synthesized, or established as theoretical knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_29:BSA-01","CL_B337_30:GNX-02","CL_B337_31:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GNX-02","text":"Logical consistency, subjective assurance, and source-insensitive assertion cannot establish objective validity.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GNX-02","CL_B337_31:GNX-01","CL_B337_31:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GNX-03","text":"Ideas of reason cannot substitute for natural causes or become empirical explanations merely because reason necessarily generates them.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GNX-03","CL_B337_30:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GNX-04","text":"Practical utility, need, or conviction cannot function as speculative evidence or be converted into theoretical cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_29:GNX-07","CL_B337_30:GNX-07","CL_B337_31:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GNX-05","text":"Moral obligation cannot be reduced to causal description, and nature cannot be presumed to contain a self-executing moral order.","refs":["CL_B337_30:DBN-03","CL_B337_30:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GNX-06","text":"Neither cognitive discipline nor moral obligation can derive final authority from theology, tradition, guardianship, or any external sovereign.","refs":["CL_B337_29:DBN-06","CL_B337_30:DBN-06","CL_B337_31:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:GNX-07","text":"Dogmatism, scepticism, argumentative accumulation, and doctrinal collection cannot constitute rational closure or progress.","refs":["CL_B337_29:DBN-05","CL_B337_31:GNX-02","CL_B337_31:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any realism or transcendent standpoint that treats thought’s totalities as independently given objects available beyond finite conditions.","refs":["CL_B337_29:DBN-01","CL_B337_31:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:DBN-02","text":"No univocal scale of warrant may collapse knowledge, hypothesis, regulation, belief, and practical necessity into interchangeable degrees of certainty.","refs":["CL_B337_29:DBN-02","CL_B337_31:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:DBN-03","text":"Natural causality and moral legislation cannot be theoretically reconciled or reductively unified without destroying the structure of practical freedom.","refs":["CL_B337_30:DBN-03","CL_B337_31:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:DBN-04","text":"Theological command cannot precede rational autonomy without invalidating both critical self-government and unconditional morality.","refs":["CL_B337_30:DBN-06","CL_B337_30:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:DBN-05","text":"Neither accumulated truth nor historical duration can generate science or progress without transformation of method and architectonic position.","refs":["CL_B337_31:DBN-01","CL_B337_31:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:DBN-06","text":"A frictionless reason permanently cured of dialectic is inadmissible; recurrent excess must remain available for critical processing.","refs":["CL_B337_29:BSA-05","CL_B337_31:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absent givenness of the supersensible domain toward which reason nevertheless remains directed.","refs":["CL_B337_29:BSA-01","CL_B337_30:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable unity of nature and freedom supplies the horizon that coordinates cognition and action without becoming an object of knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_31:BSA-02","CL_B337_30:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSA-03","text":"The absent present-world union of virtue and happiness is the negative center from which rational hope and future moral order are generated.","refs":["CL_B337_30:BSA-02","CL_B337_30:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSA-04","text":"Completed philosophy is constitutively unavailable and exerts authority only as a regulative end of collective inquiry.","refs":["CL_B337_31:BSA-03","CL_B337_31:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSA-05","text":"Final rational contentment is denied; lawful peace can only be maintained through renewed criticism and infrastructural repair.","refs":["CL_B337_29:BSA-05","CL_B337_31:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:XD-01","text":"Objective validity is simultaneously experiential and topological: a claim must concern a possible object and occupy a legitimate position within reason’s differentiated system.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-02","CL_B337_29:GA-01","CL_B337_31:GA-02","CL_B337_31:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:XD-02","text":"Because warrant follows employment rather than content alone, one supersensible idea can be invalid as cognition yet legitimate as regulation, defence, or practical orientation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-09","CL_B337_29:GA-03","CL_B337_30:BSD-02","CL_B337_30:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:XD-03","text":"Speculative restriction preserves rather than abolishes reason because enduring supersensible orientation can be reassigned under a protective jurisdiction.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-05","CL_B337_30:GA-02","CL_B337_30:GA-04","CL_B337_30:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:XD-04","text":"The unresolved divide between natural causality and moral legislation makes present moral incompletion the generative condition of hope rather than evidence of theoretical reconciliation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-08","CL_B337_30:GA-08","CL_B337_30:GA-11","CL_B337_30:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:XD-05","text":"Epistemic self-legislation, moral autonomy, and public criticism instantiate one anti-externality principle across cognition, action, and collective method.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-11","CL_B337_29:GA-12","CL_B337_30:GA-09","CL_B337_31:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:XD-06","text":"Systematic unity can coordinate the whole only by remaining an absent teleological end rather than a completed theoretical totality.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-14","CL_B337_30:RMD-08","CL_B337_31:GA-12","CL_B337_31:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:XD-07","text":"Since metaphysical excess recurs, rational progress consists in institutionalized methodological correction rather than victory, accumulation, or chronological succession.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:GA-12","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-13","CL_B337_31:AS-B07","CL_B337_31:GA-09","CL_B337_31:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:XD-08","text":"Practical certainty may rationally orient action while remaining incapable of the communicable objectivity required for theoretical knowledge.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:GA-09","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-11","CL_B337_31:GA-03","CL_B337_31:MP-01","CL_B337_31:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:XD-09","text":"Defensive possibility, practical postulation, and bounded belief form a layered noncognitive authorization that blocks dogmatic denial without establishing alternative objects.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-09","CL_B337_29:XD-08","CL_B337_30:GA-12","CL_B337_31:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-01","text":"Rational validity becomes a differentiated topology of warrants rather than a single scale running from uncertainty to certainty.","refs":["CL_B337_29:BSD-02","CL_B337_31:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-02","text":"The rational subject becomes a divided institutional agent who must claim, oppose, judge, legislate, act, and collectively maintain the conditions of criticism.","refs":["CL_B337_29:BSD-03","CL_B337_30:HSI-02","CL_B337_31:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-03","text":"Reality becomes a divided but coordinated field: empirical nature is theoretically cognizable, freedom is practically legislating, and their unity remains a horizon.","refs":["CL_B337_30:HSI-01","CL_B337_31:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-04","text":"Time becomes spiral and bifurcated: critical conflicts recur, while moral and philosophical completion are projected into future horizons of practical and collective continuation.","refs":["CL_B337_29:HSI-03","CL_B337_30:BSD-05","CL_B337_31:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-05","text":"Causality acquires a dual grammar in which natural explanation and rational determination remain operationally distinct and theoretically unreconciled.","refs":["CL_B337_30:BSD-06","CL_B337_30:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-06","text":"Progress becomes intensive and methodological: it occurs when reason reorganizes its jurisdictions, warrants, and procedures rather than merely adding doctrines.","refs":["CL_B337_31:BSD-03","CL_B337_31:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-07","text":"Metaphysics survives as a constitutional protocol regulating transitions among cognition, belief, obligation, hope, and historical method.","refs":["CL_B337_31:BSD-04","CL_B337_31:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-08","text":"Finite agency is authorized to transform the sensible world without complete metaphysical knowledge, provided action remains governed by autonomous law and public criticism.","refs":["CL_B337_30:MME-08","CL_B337_31:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RLE-01","text":"The demand for unconditioned unity drives categories beyond experience, generates conflict, and returns as regulated systematic orientation.","refs":["CL_B337_29:RLE-04","CL_B337_31:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RLE-02","text":"Claim and counterclaim expose reciprocal insufficiency, which internalizes opposition as a recursively produced diagnostic of illicit extension.","refs":["CL_B337_29:RLE-01","CL_B337_29:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RLE-03","text":"Limitation recursively protects reason by redirecting speculative desire toward practice and returning every practical postulate to critique.","refs":["CL_B337_30:RLE-01","CL_B337_30:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RLE-04","text":"Moral striving produces worthiness, worthiness produces hope, hope produces postulation, and postulation returns to the priority of moral law.","refs":["CL_B337_30:RLE-03","CL_B337_30:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RLE-05","text":"Ethical completion, nature–freedom unity, and absent philosophy converge on a single attractor: systematic unity that governs without becoming possessed.","refs":["CL_B337_30:RLE-06","CL_B337_31:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RLE-06","text":"Private assurance generates communicability tests, which generate public reproducibility and finally a critical infrastructure maintained beyond individual possession.","refs":["CL_B337_31:RLE-01","CL_B337_31:RLE-06","CL_B337_31:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:RLE-07","text":"Historical recurrence becomes a spiral when repeated source–scope incoherence forces methodological transformation rather than another doctrinal succession.","refs":["CL_B337_31:RLE-04","CL_B337_31:HSI-04","CL_B337_31:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B01","text":"Reason’s demand for systematic completion generates ideas that exceed the conditions of possible experience.","refs":["CL_B337_30:FGS-B01","CL_B337_30:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B02","text":"Transcendent use generates opposed claims, dialectical conflict, and recurrent evidence of defective self-government.","refs":["CL_B337_29:RLE-04","CL_B337_31:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B03","text":"Critique activates an experiential domain gate and audits every claim’s source, scope, object, and entitlement.","refs":["CL_B337_29:FGS-B02","CL_B337_29:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B04","text":"The audit distributes claims among knowledge, hypothesis, regulation, defence, belief, obligation, hope, and invalid pretension.","refs":["CL_B337_29:FGS-B06","CL_B337_31:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B05","text":"Supersensible orientation excluded from cognition transfers to practice, where autonomous moral law determines the will.","refs":["CL_B337_30:FGS-B04","CL_B337_30:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B06","text":"Nature’s failure to secure morally proportionate happiness generates hope and practically necessary postulation.","refs":["CL_B337_30:FGS-B06","CL_B337_30:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B07","text":"Architectonic position, communicable procedure, and absent completion organize the redistributed powers into a public critical infrastructure.","refs":["CL_B337_31:GA-05","CL_B337_31:GA-11","CL_B337_31:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B08","text":"Recursive critique prevents theology, teleology, practical belief, or systematic aspiration from reverting to speculative possession.","refs":["CL_B337_30:FGS-B08","CL_B337_31:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AFM-B01","text":"Demand for completion → transcendent extension → opposed claims → speculative conflict.","refs":["CL_B337_30:AFM-B01","CL_B337_29:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AFM-B02","text":"Speculative conflict → bilateral suspension → experiential domain gate → jurisdictional critique.","refs":["CL_B337_29:AS-B06","CL_B337_29:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AFM-B03","text":"Jurisdictional critique → differentiated warrants → speculative restriction → practical redirection.","refs":["CL_B337_29:FGS-B06","CL_B337_30:AS-04","CL_B337_30:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AFM-B04","text":"Moral law → worthiness → natural insufficiency → rational hope → practical postulation.","refs":["CL_B337_30:AS-06","CL_B337_30:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AFM-B05","text":"Practical postulation → moral theology and teleology → renewed subordination to autonomous legislation.","refs":["CL_B337_30:FGS-B07","CL_B337_30:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:AFM-B06","text":"Architectonic ordering → public critical infrastructure → collective continuation → absent completion.","refs":["CL_B337_31:RLE-06","CL_B337_31:AS-B09","CL_B337_31:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MME-01","text":"Metaphysical disagreement is reclassified from competition over transcendent objects into governance of the conditions under which claims become admissible.","refs":["CL_B337_29:MME-01","CL_B337_31:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MME-02","text":"Validity becomes both epistemic and architectonic: proof must establish objective right and legitimate systemic position.","refs":["CL_B337_29:MME-03","CL_B337_31:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MME-03","text":"Knowledge, faith, hypothesis, duty, and hope cease to form one speculative continuum and acquire coordinated but non-interchangeable modes of legitimacy.","refs":["CL_B337_29:MME-07","CL_B337_30:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MME-04","text":"Restriction ceases to oppose freedom once limitation is reconstructed as the productive self-government of reason.","refs":["CL_B337_29:MME-06","CL_B337_31:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MME-05","text":"The causality–freedom opposition is redrawn as a non-reductive difference between explanatory and legislative registers organized around an unavailable unity.","refs":["CL_B337_30:MME-04","CL_B337_31:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MME-06","text":"Publicness becomes reproducible critical procedure rather than consensus, psychological certainty, inherited authority, or common-sense immediacy.","refs":["CL_B337_29:BSD-06","CL_B337_31:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MME-07","text":"Rational progress becomes methodological and collective rather than chronological, accumulative, or school-based.","refs":["CL_B337_31:BSD-03","CL_B337_31:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MME-08","text":"Philosophy is reclassified from completed doctrine into a disciplinary and infrastructural project governed by an absent regulative completion.","refs":["CL_B337_30:MME-01","CL_B337_31:GA-14","CL_B337_31:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The system adjudicates polemics, hypotheses, proofs, freedom, God, immortality, moral duty, assent, science, and metaphysical history.","refs":["CL_B337_29:ALT-B01","CL_B337_30:ALT-B01","CL_B337_31:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Possible experience and linked filters of sufficiency, source, scope, employment, and systemic position distribute rational authority.","refs":["CL_B337_29:ALT-B02","CL_B337_30:ALT-B02","CL_B337_31:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Intervention shifts from defeating doctrines to auditing their generating principles, redirecting metaphysics toward autonomous action and governance of admissibility.","refs":["CL_B337_29:ALT-B03","CL_B337_30:ALT-B03","CL_B337_31:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Nature remains the field of bounded cognition, freedom the register of legislation, and their deferred unity the horizon of moral and philosophical futurity.","refs":["CL_B337_29:ALT-B04","CL_B337_30:ALT-B04","CL_B337_31:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The architecture performs its claims through recursive return, classificatory exclusion, architectonic repositioning, and the renewed subordination of theology to autonomy.","refs":["CL_B337_29:ALT-B05","CL_B337_30:ALT-B05","CL_B337_31:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FL-01","text":"Possible experience authorizes theoretical objectivity, yet the universal authority of this criterion still requires a critical justification that cannot merely presuppose its jurisdiction.","refs":["CL_B337_29:FL-02","CL_B337_31:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FL-02","text":"Practical freedom is treated as effective while its relation to exhaustive natural causality remains theoretically unreconciled.","refs":["CL_B337_30:FL-02","CL_B337_30:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FL-03","text":"Moral worth must remain independent of happiness, yet rational hope must sustain purposive action without becoming reward-based motivation.","refs":["CL_B337_30:FL-03","CL_B337_30:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FL-04","text":"The derivation of determinate theological content from practical necessity approaches the speculative expansion that the cognition–postulation boundary forbids.","refs":["CL_B337_30:FL-04","CL_B337_30:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FL-05","text":"Communicability bears the burden of public stabilization even though practical moral certainty can remain firm without becoming communicable knowledge.","refs":["CL_B337_31:FL-01","CL_B337_31:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:FL-06","text":"Architectonic divisions claim a priori authority while their form emerges retrospectively and remains regulated by a system that is not yet complete.","refs":["CL_B337_31:FL-02","CL_B337_31:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MP-01","text":"Possibility mutates from logical openness into experiential object-possibility, practical admissibility, and architectonic legitimacy.","refs":["CL_B337_29:MP-01","CL_B337_31:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MP-02","text":"Limitation mutates from negative incapacity into protection, redistribution, and institutional critical authority.","refs":["CL_B337_30:RMD-05","CL_B337_31:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MP-03","text":"Belief mutates from subjective insufficiency into bounded practical authority and finally into moral certainty without theoretical validity.","refs":["CL_B337_31:RMD-03","CL_B337_31:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MP-04","text":"Metaphysics mutates from an engine of speculative acquisition into an institution whose authority consists in regulation.","refs":["CL_B337_31:MP-02","CL_B337_31:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MP-05","text":"Systematic unity mutates from cognitive completion into ethical totality and then into an absent regulative attractor.","refs":["CL_B337_30:RMD-08","CL_B337_30:MP-05","CL_B337_31:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:HSI-01","text":"Reality is processed by a tribunal-laboratory that becomes an architectonic road system: it admits claims, tests jurisdictions, assigns positions, and maintains routes for collective criticism.","refs":["CL_B337_29:HSI-01","CL_B337_31:HSI-02","CL_B337_31:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a finite judge-builder divided among claimant, opponent, examiner, legislator, moral agent, and maintainer of public reason.","refs":["CL_B337_29:HSI-02","CL_B337_30:HSI-02","CL_B337_31:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:HSI-03","text":"Reality is a divided but teleologically coordinated field in which empirical nature is knowable, freedom legislates, and final unity remains absent.","refs":["CL_B337_30:HSI-01","CL_B337_31:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:HSI-04","text":"Time is spiral maturation stretched between recurrent dialectical failure, present moral incompletion, and deferred collective completion.","refs":["CL_B337_29:HSI-03","CL_B337_30:HSI-03","CL_B337_31:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:HSI-05","text":"Navigation consists in disciplined movement between dogmatic possession and sceptical abandonment through immanent action, repeated limitation, and collective repair.","refs":["CL_B337_29:HSI-05","CL_B337_30:HSI-05","CL_B337_31:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the meta-cluster’s movement from speculative conflict through jurisdictional differentiation to practical redirection and public critical infrastructure.","refs":["CL_B337_29:MRT-B01","CL_B337_30:MRT-B01","CL_B337_31:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B02","text":"The deductions of the complete a priori system, pure moral law, particular metaphysical divisions, and positive procedures of criticism remain underdetermined.","refs":["CL_B337_29:MRT-B02","CL_B337_30:MRT-B03","CL_B337_31:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent only if cognitive, practical, regulative, and historical warrants remain distinct while limitation operates as productive redistribution rather than sceptical termination.","refs":["CL_B337_29:MRT-B03","CL_B337_30:MRT-B04","CL_B337_31:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where limitation simultaneously validates finite cognition, protects practical authority, and reorganizes reason’s methods and institutions.","refs":["CL_B337_29:MRT-B04","CL_B337_30:MRT-B02","CL_B337_31:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining recursive return, the re-subordination of theology to autonomy, and the reopening of completion as future collective work.","refs":["CL_B337_29:MRT-B05","CL_B337_30:MRT-B05","CL_B337_31:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B06","text":"The irreducible residue is the practical force by which defensive possibility, rational hope, and absent final unity orient action without becoming objective cognition.","refs":["CL_B337_29:MRT-B06","CL_B337_30:MRT-B06","CL_B337_31:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"B337 L1 08","context":"Chapters 85–93 · Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics · Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis · Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs · Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason · Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason · Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason · Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief · Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason · Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason"}},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01","layer":"MC","title":"B337 L2 01","order":133,"parents":["MC_B337_L1_01","MC_B337_L1_02","MC_B337_L1_03","MC_B337_L1_04"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"5769c9893804c2797ad63710c65c433ea00a518bc0c7d6be32626cb7a216dd34","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B01","text":"The governing problem is how finite cognition can begin in receptivity yet attain necessary objective force without converting thought into intellectual creation or appearance into independently known being.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:AS-01","MC_B337_L1_04:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B02","text":"Objectivity is reconstructed as the lawfully unified result of sensible givenness, formal intuition, synthesis, apperception, conceptual determination, and mediated application.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:AS-02","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B03","text":"Finite knowledge depends on irreducible but coordinated operations whose distributed mediation produces cognizable objecthood without allowing any faculty to become self-sufficient.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_04:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B04","text":"Possible experience simultaneously generates categorical authority and confines that authority to the conditions under which objects can be given, unified, and judged.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:AS-07","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B05","text":"The proof proceeds by transcendental regression from actual cognition to its enabling operations and then returns from those operations to determine their objective entitlement and jurisdiction.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:AS-08","MC_B337_L1_03:AS-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B06","text":"The system moves through recursive differentiation and recomposition: each mediation enables objective use, while deprivation of that mediation reveals the limit of the resulting claim.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:AS-07","MC_B337_L1_04:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B07","text":"Its positive result is a nature necessarily lawful in universal phenomenal form but irreducibly dependent on experience for particular qualities, actualities, and laws.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-13","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B08","text":"Closure is achieved through bounded empirical objectivity and semantic deprivation beyond experience, while the genetic origin and ultimate unity of the cognitive architecture remain opaque.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:AS-08","MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Finite cognition requires independently received material and spontaneous ordering operations that cooperate without becoming interchangeable.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Space and time are nonempirical forms that order sensible givenness without becoming properties of things independently of human sensibility.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_02:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Synthesis precedes analysis because neither receptive multiplicity nor conceptual decomposition supplies the conjunction required for cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] Original apperceptive unity makes synthesis attributable to one consciousness and thereby enables representational belonging, judgment, and object-relation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Categories become objectively effective only by changing from logical functions into rules for unifying intuition and then into temporally mediated procedures and principles.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Objective validity is an achieved relation in which a given manifold is necessarily unified under shared conditions of possible experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience is simultaneously the field that realizes objective cognition, the criterion that validates it, and the boundary that limits its legitimate use.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Time expands from a form of inner sense into the universal operational medium through which synthesis, schema, self-appearance, quantity, and categorial application become possible.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-05","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Schema and judgment mediate universal rules and particular appearances, but no further universal rule can mechanically eliminate the act of application.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_04:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Systematic completeness requires both principled placement within a prior whole and an independent demonstration of each concept’s objective right of use.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-09","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] Critique is productive jurisdiction: it derives valid functions by identifying their enabling conditions and blocks overreach by withholding authority when those conditions are absent.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-11","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] A priori principles determine universal phenomenal form but cannot generate particular empirical qualities, actualities, or laws.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-13","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Beyond possible experience, categories retain only indeterminate logical significance, while the noumenon functions as an empty negative limit rather than an object of cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GA-09","MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Functional reconstruction can establish necessary cognitive relations without genetically deriving the faculties, their exact forms, or their common ground.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-13","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-01","text":"[Mutation / Escalation] Experience changes from the occasion of cognition into a constituted product and finally into the validating jurisdiction of every claim to objective use.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-01","MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-07","MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation / Valence Reversal] The a priori changes from a mark of nonempirical origin into sensible form, constitutive rule, and self-limiting authority rather than authorization for transcendence.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-06","MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-03","text":"[Reversal / Terminal Transformation] Subjectivity changes from an apparent obstacle to objectivity into its universal condition and then into a distributed, non-substantial relay of reception, synthesis, and self-limitation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:RMD-06","MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-02","MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] The object changes from an antecedently unified terminus into the cognition-available correlate of lawful synthesis, while object-existence remains unproduced by that synthesis.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-04","MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-05","text":"[Expansion / Absorption] Unity expands from conceptual subsumption into synthetic conjunction, apperceptive belonging, judgmental form, and objective validity, absorbing synthesis without becoming identical to any single operation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-02","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-06","text":"[Domain Expansion] Time retains its sensible identity while becoming the medium of self-affection, schema, categorial meaning, mathematical determination, and phenomenal application.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-01","MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-07","text":"[Serial Mutation] Category and judgment move from logical classification through synthetic object-determination to temporally mediated application and principle-generation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-01","MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-08","text":"[Valence Reversal] Restriction changes from apparent deprivation into the positive structure that authorizes necessity by fixing its lawful domain.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:MP-04","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-09","text":"[Disappearance] Intellectual intuition disappears as a positive model after establishing by contrast why finite cognition requires reception, mediation, synthesis, and judgment.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-09","MC_B337_L1_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-10","text":"[Terminal Transformation] Noumenon changes from a presumed supersensible object into a negative regulator that prevents phenomenal conditions from becoming unrestricted ontology.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-07","MC_B337_L1_04:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-01","text":"[Scaling] Mathematical constructions and scientific examples remain local proofs, but their higher-order role is to establish that conceptual analysis cannot produce necessary cognitive extension.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:CGD-02","MC_B337_L1_04:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-02","text":"[Bridge] Space and time scale beyond local forms of intuition when their manifold is shown to require imagination, synthesis, and categorial determination for objective order.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:CGD-02","MC_B337_L1_04:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-03","text":"[Bridge] Logical and faculty taxonomies become current-level architecture only when recoded as non-substitutable functions and linked to the claimed correspondence between judgment and category.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:CGD-04","MC_B337_L1_03:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-04","text":"[Superseded] Empirical association remains a local phenomenal mechanism but loses explanatory priority once synthesis is established as the condition of associative order.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-10","MC_B337_L1_03:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-05","text":"[Scaling] Particular schemata, categories, and principles remain lower-layer differentiations; their shared higher-order function is to mediate universal phenomenal form without producing empirical content.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:CGD-01","MC_B337_L1_04:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-06","text":"[Terminal Boundary] Non-human forms of intuition remain outside the architecture and prevent conditions of human cognition from becoming an ontology of every possible intellect.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:CGD-06","MC_B337_L1_04:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-07","text":"[Underdetermined] Affection, intuition–conception union, imagination, and schema remain compressed interfaces rather than fully derived components of the current-level machine.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:MRT-B03","MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-01","text":"Objective necessity cannot be reduced to sensory reception, empirical repetition, or accumulated regularity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GNX-01","MC_B337_L1_02:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-02","text":"Concepts, logical coherence, and formal determinacy cannot independently give objects or establish synthetic objective reference.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GNX-03","MC_B337_L1_04:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-03","text":"Subjective origin cannot be identified with private arbitrariness when the relevant conditions necessarily govern every possible object of human experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GNX-05","MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-04","text":"A priori origin cannot certify unrestricted scope, because objective authority depends on the conditions of possible application.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-08","MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-05","text":"Cognizable objects are neither wholly preconstituted unities nor products whose existence is created by subjective synthesis.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:DBN-04","MC_B337_L1_03:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-06","text":"Categories detached from sensible intuition, temporal schema, and judgment possess no legitimate unrestricted objective employment.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GNX-06","MC_B337_L1_04:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-07","text":"Phenomena and noumena cannot form two positively cognized worlds, because the supersensible boundary lacks determinate object-giving conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:GNX-06","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-08","text":"No mechanical hierarchy of rules can replace judgment at the point where universal concepts must be applied to particular appearances.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-04","MC_B337_L1_04:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-01","text":"No standpoint outside sensibility is available from which appearances and things in themselves could be compared to certify correspondence.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:BSA-01","MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-02","text":"No finite faculty can receive, originate, unify, and know an object through a single self-sufficient operation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:DBN-02","MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-03","text":"A direct correspondence model must be excluded because the independently constituted object needed to validate cognition is unavailable outside cognition’s own conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:DBN-01","MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-04","text":"Psychological occurrence, habitual association, and empirical succession cannot be transformed into strict objective necessity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:DBN-02","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-05","text":"Systematic completeness cannot arise from external accumulation because the placement of parts must be generated and tested through an antecedent unity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:DBN-05","MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-06","text":"Functional necessity cannot be converted into a genetic ontology capable of explaining the origin, number, or common root of the cognitive powers.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:DBN-05","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSA-01","text":"The deepest excluded faculty is finite intellectual intuition, in which giving, creating, thinking, and knowing an object would coincide without mediation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:BSA-01","MC_B337_L1_04:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSA-02","text":"The architecture is organized around the absence of an absolute outside from which all cognition could be compared with independently existing reality.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:BSA-02","MC_B337_L1_04:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSA-03","text":"A self-grounding unity of sensibility, understanding, synthesis, and apperception remains unavailable because no independently prior term terminates their reciprocal dependence.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:BSA-04","MC_B337_L1_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSA-04","text":"Positive supersensible cognition is structured by the absence of a nonempirical third term capable of mediating synthetic connection beyond sensibility.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:BSA-02","MC_B337_L1_03:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSA-05","text":"Absolute closure is inadmissible: appearance cannot exhaust reality, the supersensible cannot become positively surveyable, and cognition cannot derive all its own conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:BSA-05","MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:XD-01","text":"Received material becomes an object for cognition only through lawful synthesis, so objectivity is neither passive copying nor free construction.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-01","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-01","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-03","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:XD-02","text":"Synthetic a priori cognition requires pure intuitive form, categorical unity, temporal translation, and judgmental application as non-substitutable operations.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-05","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-02","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-05","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-08","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:XD-03","text":"Possible experience does not externally curtail categorical authority; it produces that authority by supplying the only domain in which categories can determine objects.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:AS-07","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-04","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-05","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:XD-04","text":"Subjective form grounds public objectivity when it functions as a necessary condition shared by every possible human appearance rather than as a private psychological state.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:AS-06","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-06","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-02","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:XD-05","text":"Conceptual completeness divides into systematic membership and objective entitlement, so a concept’s necessary place cannot by itself establish its valid application.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:GA-09","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-13","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-10","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:XD-06","text":"Time becomes the operational relay that converts intellectual spontaneity into sensible procedures while preserving the distinction between category and appearance.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-05","MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B03","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-05","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:XD-07","text":"Schema can direct judgment without replacing it, making objective application transcendentally conditioned yet irreducible to mechanical rule-following.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:XD-02","MC_B337_L1_04:FL-02","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:XD-08","text":"Transcendental principles secure the universal lawfulness of phenomenal nature while necessarily reopening empirical inquiry into its specific content.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:XD-03","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-05","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-12","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:XD-09","text":"The negative noumenon prevents phenomenal objectivity from becoming absolute precisely by withholding every positive determination that could convert it into another object-domain.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-07","MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B06","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-13","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-01","text":"Reality for finite cognition becomes a conditional phenomenal order that is neither simply given nor freely made but constituted through the lawful organization of received material.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-01","MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes a distributed functional nexus that receives objects, synthesizes manifolds, unifies representations, applies rules, and restricts its own authority without creating existence.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-04","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-03","text":"Time becomes a stratified operational medium joining succession, receptive self-appearance, schema, conceptual manifestation, and objective application.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-03","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-04","text":"Necessity becomes jurisdictional authority because the same architecture that establishes universal validity restricts that validity to possible experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-02","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-05","text":"Nature becomes formally closed and materially unfinished: universal structure is legislated a priori, while determinate content remains empirically open.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-05","MC_B337_L1_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-06","text":"Error becomes a recurrent structural failure in which valid formal powers detach from the sensible mediations and jurisdictions that alone give them objective employment.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-05","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-07","text":"Metaphysics becomes boundary-knowledge that maps valid use, inevitable overreach, indeterminate thinkability, and the limits of positive ontology.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-07","MC_B337_L1_04:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-08","text":"Epistemic proof becomes jurisdictional rather than merely deductive: possession of a concept must be supplemented by reconstruction of its objective entitlement and lawful scope.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:BSD-01","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-01","text":"Affection supplies material, sensible form orders it, synthesis unifies it, apperception makes the unity attributable, and possible experience returns as the criterion validating the entire sequence.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:RLE-01","MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-02","text":"The logical loop moves from judgment to categories, from categories to synthesis, from synthesis to apperception, and from apperception back to objectively valid judgment.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:RLE-01","MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-03","text":"The validity–limitation loop converts each enabling condition into a jurisdictional restriction and each restriction into a safeguard of objective necessity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-03","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-04","text":"Rule-possession generates the problem of application, application generates regress, and regress returns judgment as irreducible but conditionally directable through schema.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-03","MC_B337_L1_04:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-05","text":"The whole assigns systematic places to its parts, while the articulated parts retroactively test the completeness and legitimacy of the anticipated whole.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-07","MC_B337_L1_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-06","text":"Experience is excluded as the origin of pure forms, reintroduced as their occasion and objective horizon, and then analyzed through the structures that make it possible.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:RLE-06","MC_B337_L1_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-07","text":"A priori origin repeatedly tempts transcendent extension, and critique repeatedly converts that overreach into a more exact allocation of source, warrant, and domain.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-05","MC_B337_L1_04:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-08","text":"Noumenon is a negative attractor: thought of what exceeds appearance recursively returns as a prohibition against positive supersensible cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-06","MC_B337_L1_01:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-09","text":"Time is an operational attractor because every objectively significant category must pass through temporal synthesis, schema, quantity, or phenomenal determination.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-08","MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FGS-B01","text":"Affection → received sensible material without objective unity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:FGS-B01","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FGS-B02","text":"Received material → spatial-temporal ordering → manifold available for synthesis.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:FGS-B02","MC_B337_L1_03:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FGS-B03","text":"Unconjoined manifold → spontaneous synthesis → apperceptive belonging to one consciousness.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B05","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FGS-B04","text":"Judgmental functions → categories → rules for determining synthesized intuition as an object.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FGS-B05","text":"Intellectual rules → productive imagination → temporal schema → directed but irreducible judgment.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B03","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FGS-B06","text":"Schematized application → unified possible experience → objective validity and bounded categorical authority.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B05","MC_B337_L1_04:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FGS-B07","text":"Schematized categories → transcendental principles → universally lawful phenomenal nature plus empirically undetermined content.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B06","MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FGS-B08","text":"Removal of intuition and schema → loss of determinate reference → merely logical significance → negative noumenal boundary.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-07","MC_B337_L1_04:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AFM-B01","text":"Actual necessary cognition → inadequacy of empirical and analytic derivations → transcendental regression toward enabling conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:AS-03","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AFM-B02","text":"Finite receptivity → pure spatial-temporal form → a manifold given without objective conjunction.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:GA-01","MC_B337_L1_01:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AFM-B03","text":"Judgmental classification → candidate categories → separation of systematic possession from objective entitlement.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B03","MC_B337_L1_03:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AFM-B04","text":"Unconjoined manifold → spontaneous synthesis → apperceptive unity → cognition-available objecthood.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B05","MC_B337_L1_03:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AFM-B05","text":"Categorical heterogeneity → imagination and time → schema and judgment → legitimate application to appearances.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:RLE-02","MC_B337_L1_04:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AFM-B06","text":"Objective application → possible experience → systematic principles → formally lawful but empirically open nature.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:AFM-B04","MC_B337_L1_04:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:AFM-B07","text":"Withdrawal of sensible mediation → semantic deprivation → rejection of positive noumena → retention of an indeterminate limit.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:AFM-B07","MC_B337_L1_04:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is reclassified from access to independently available being into necessary validity constituted within possible experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:MME-01","MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MME-02","text":"The subject–object opposition becomes an asymmetric circuit in which material is received, objectual unity is synthesized, and neither pole is cognitively available outside the circuit.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:MME-04","MC_B337_L1_04:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MME-03","text":"The opposition between a priori and empirical cognition becomes a division of labor between universal experiential form and determinate empirical content.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:MME-03","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MME-04","text":"Logic is redistributed among formal admissibility, transcendental conditions of objectivity, systematic derivation, and dialectical diagnosis.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:MME-04","MC_B337_L1_04:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MME-05","text":"Necessity is reclassified from unrestricted modal force into constitutive validity indexed to the jurisdiction that generates it.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:BSD-02","MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MME-06","text":"Nature is redrawn as universally lawful in form but indefinitely open to empirical specification, preventing transcendental philosophy from replacing science.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:MME-06","MC_B337_L1_04:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MME-07","text":"Metaphysics is reorganized from positive supersensible ontology into a jurisdictional map of valid use, inevitable illusion, thinkable excess, and negative boundary.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:MME-07","MC_B337_L1_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MME-08","text":"Explanation is transformed from empirical genealogy or direct correspondence into reconstruction of possibility, entitlement, mediation, and lawful scope.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:MME-07","MC_B337_L1_03:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface level] The explicit field comprises sensation, space, time, judgment, categories, imagination, schema, apperception, principles, nature, and the noumenal boundary.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:ALT-B01","MC_B337_L1_01:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural level] Cognition is a distributed dependency system in which receptive givenness, formal intuition, synthesis, unity, conceptual determination, and application remain distinct but coordinated.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:ALT-B02","MC_B337_L1_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic level] Interpretation shifts from inventorying objects and concepts to testing enabling conditions, objective entitlement, mediation, jurisdiction, and failure of application.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:ALT-B03","MC_B337_L1_04:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal level] Reality for finite cognition is a spatiotemporal phenomenal order whose objectivity depends on synthesis, while independent being remains unavailable to positive determination.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:ALT-B04","MC_B337_L1_01:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative level] The system performs its thesis through regression, differentiation, recombination, deprivation-testing, and recursive conversion of limits into positive conditions of validity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:ALT-B05","MC_B337_L1_04:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FL-01","text":"An affecting ground is required for receptivity, yet the relation between that ground and sensible affection cannot be positively explained.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:FL-01","MC_B337_L1_01:FL-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FL-02","text":"Intuition and conception must unite in one cognition, but the division among imagination, understanding, and synthesis leaves their operative union mechanically underdetermined.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:FL-02","MC_B337_L1_03:FL-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FL-03","text":"The category-system depends on the completeness of logical judgmental forms even though their exact number and configuration terminate in asserted facticity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:FL-01","MC_B337_L1_03:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FL-04","text":"The transition from subjective source to objective necessity is indispensable, but the conversion is functionally asserted more strongly than genetically demonstrated.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:FL-04","MC_B337_L1_04:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FL-05","text":"Apperceptive unity and synthetic activity mutually require one another, leaving no transparent first ground that can terminate the transcendental regress.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:FL-03","MC_B337_L1_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FL-06","text":"Judgment must remain irreducible to rules while receiving transcendental direction from schema, leaving the boundary between guidance and mechanical determination unstable.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:FL-02","MC_B337_L1_04:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:FL-07","text":"The noumenal remainder is required to prevent phenomenal absolutization, although its positive possibility and relation to appearance cannot be established.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:FL-05","MC_B337_L1_04:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MP-01","text":"The a priori mutates from a source-classification into sensible form, constitutive rule, modal authority, and finally the bounded form of possible experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:MP-01","MC_B337_L1_04:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MP-02","text":"The object mutates from an apparently antecedent unity into the rule-governed correlate of synthesized givenness without becoming a product of existential creation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:RMD-04","MC_B337_L1_04:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MP-03","text":"Time mutates from sensible form into self-affective, schematic, mathematical, and categorial mediation while remaining inapplicable to things in themselves.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:RMD-01","MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MP-04","text":"Judgment mutates from conceptual predication into synthesis and then into the irreducible site where universal rules acquire particular objective application.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:MP-01","MC_B337_L1_04:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MP-05","text":"Critique mutates from negative restriction into a positive architecture that allocates cognitive functions, constructs lawful principles, and diagnoses overreach.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_01:MP-04","MC_B337_L1_04:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality-for-cognition is a bounded conversion machine in which affection becomes ordered manifold, manifold becomes synthesized unity, and unity becomes a determinate appearance.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:HSI-01","MC_B337_L1_04:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a distributed switching-point that receives multiplicity, synthesizes it, returns it as accountable unity, and polices the limits of that operation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:HSI-02","MC_B337_L1_04:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is the continuous transmission medium connecting affection, self-appearance, schema, quantity, category, and phenomenal objecthood.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:HSI-03","MC_B337_L1_04:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:HSI-04","text":"Knowledge is a gated architecture in which systematic position, sensible mediation, objective entitlement, and lawful jurisdiction must coincide.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:HSI-04","MC_B337_L1_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:HSI-05","text":"Cognitive struggle is jurisdictional navigation: valid conditions must be preserved within experience, recurrent overreach must be reversed, and the exterior must remain a boundary rather than an escape route.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:HSI-05","MC_B337_L1_04:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from receptive affection through formal intuition, synthesis, apperception, categorization, schema, judgment, and bounded objective experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B01","MC_B337_L1_03:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture is coherent only if sensible form, received matter, synthetic unity, conceptual determination, and objective application remain distinct but recursively coordinated.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:MRT-B02","MC_B337_L1_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B03","text":"The reconstruction is strongest where one shared synthesis connects object-constitution, apperceptive unity, categorical authority, mathematical form, and jurisdictional restriction.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_03:MRT-B04","MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B04","text":"It remains underdetermined at affection, faculty-union, logical completeness, imagination, schema, judgmental application, and the genetic origin of the cognitive forms.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:MRT-B03","MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining recursive return, deprivation, compression, and unresolved mediation rather than recasting the architecture as a linear deduction.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_02:MRT-B05","MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B06","text":"The noumenal remainder and the facticity of cognitive form resist complete axiomatic reduction because closure requires both an unavailable exterior and conditions the system cannot genetically derive.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_04:MRT-B06","MC_B337_L1_03:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"B337 L2 01","context":"Chapters 01–48 · I. Of the difference between Pure and Empirical Knowledge · II. The Human Intellect, even in an Unphilosophical State, is in Possession of Certain Cognitions “à priori”. · III. Philosophy stands in need of a Science which shall Determine the Possibility, Principles, and Extent of Human Knowledge “à priori” · IV. Of the Difference Between Analytical and Synthetical Judgements. · V. In all Theoretical Sciences of Reason, Synthetical Judgements “à priori” are contained as Principles. · VI. The Universal Problem of Pure Reason. · VII. Idea and Division of a Particular Science, under the Name of a Critique of Pure Reason. · § I. Introductory. · § 2. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception. · § 3. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Space. · § 4. Conclusions from the foregoing Conceptions. · § 5. Metaphysical Exposition of this Conception. · § 6. Transcendental Exposition of the Conception of Time. · § 7. Conclusions from the above Conceptions. · § 8. Elucidation. · § 9. General Remarks on Transcendental Æsthetic. · § 10. Conclusion of the Transcendental Æsthetic. · I. Of Logic in General. · II. Of Transcendental Logic. · III. Of the Division of General Logic into Analytic and Dialectic. · IV. Of the Division of Transcendental Logic into Transcendental Analytic and Dialectic. · First Division. Transcendental Logic. Transcendental Analytic. § 1 · BOOK I. Analytic of Conceptions. § 2 · Introductory § 3 · Section I. Of the Logical Use of the Understanding in General § 4 · Section II. Of the Logical Function of the Understanding in Judgements § 5 · Section III. Of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding, or Categories § 6 · Section I. Of the Principles of a Transcendental Deduction in general § 9 · Transition to the Transcendental Deduction of the Categories § 10 · Of the Possibility of a Conjunction of the manifold representations given by Sense § 11. · Of the Originally Synthetical Unity of Apperception § 12 · The Principle of the Synthetical Unity of Apperception is the highest Principle of all exercise of the Understanding § 13 · What Objective Unity of Self-consciousness is § 14 · The Logical Form of all Judgements consists in the Objective Unity of Apperception of the Conceptions contained therein § 15 · All Sensuous Intuitions are subject to the Categories, as Conditions under which alone the manifold Content of them can be united in one Consciousness § 16 · Observation § 17 · In Cognition, its Application to Objects of Experience is the only legitimate use of the Category § 18 · Of the Application of the Categories to Objects of the Senses in general § 20 · Transcendental Deduction of the universally possible employment in experience of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding § 22 · Result of this Deduction of the Conceptions of the Understanding § 23 · BOOK II. Analytic of Principles · INTRODUCTION. Of the Transcendental Faculty of judgement in General · Chapter I. Of the Schematism at of the Pure Conceptions of the Understanding · Chapter II. System of all Principles of the Pure Understanding · Section I. Of the Supreme Principle of all Analytical Judgements · Section II. Of the Supreme Principle of all Synthetical Judgements · Section III. Systematic Representation of all Synthetical Principles of the Pure Understanding · Chapter III Of the Ground of the Division of all Objects into Phenomena and Noumena"}},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02","layer":"MC","title":"B337 L2 02","order":134,"parents":["MC_B337_L1_05","MC_B337_L1_06","MC_B337_L1_07","MC_B337_L1_08"],"revision":"v1","sha256":"d0c8fd8f5a7114e2eb8c1bdd620f5cbb099b785df51ca62d872755bb35074ff4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AS-01","text":"Finite cognition must secure objective validity while answering reason’s unavoidable demand for unconditioned completeness without pretending to possess its object.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:AS-01","MC_B337_L1_07:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AS-02","text":"The architecture becomes a constitutional ordering of sensibility, understanding, reason, practical legislation, and public criticism rather than a hierarchy reducible to one cognitive power.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:AS-02","MC_B337_L1_08:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AS-03","text":"Possible experience authorizes empirical objectivity and simultaneously withdraws constitutive authority from every attempt to convert rational necessity into supersensible cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:AS-03","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AS-04","text":"Reason objectifies its formal demand for unity across the subject, world, and highest condition, thereby generating the recurrent metaphysical objects that critique must dismantle.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:AS-05","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_07:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AS-05","text":"Contradiction changes from doctrinal failure into a scale-sensitive diagnostic that exposes illicit objectification, shared realism, and unauthorized transfer between cognitive domains.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-07","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AS-06","text":"Speculative defeat does not abolish reason’s ideas but differentiates their surviving force into regulation, practical orientation, defensive possibility, and methodological discipline.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_07:AS-07","MC_B337_L1_08:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AS-07","text":"The world becomes an open task, theological proof becomes an audited conversion of necessity into existence, and their residues are reorganized within a public critical infrastructure.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-10","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AS-08","text":"The movement is spiral and mutation-driven: rational excess generates contradiction, critique redistributes authority, practical claims return for renewed audit, and closure remains formally complete but materially open.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:AS-08","MC_B337_L1_08:AS-07","MC_B337_L1_07:AS-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Determinate objective cognition requires sensible presentation, categorial synthesis, and applicability within possible experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-02","text":"[Emergent] The conditions authorizing cognition are intrinsically restrictive, so validation and limitation constitute one jurisdictional operation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-03","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] The unconditioned is necessarily generated as the completion of conditioned cognition but cannot be presented as a theoretically determinate object.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-05","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Transcendental ideas arise necessarily, while illusion begins when their formal or regulative necessity is converted into constitutive object-determination.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-08","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Totality changes from a purported completed object into an unattainable task, an asymptotic rule of inquiry, and an absent coordinator of nature and freedom.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-02","MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Contradiction is a critical algorithm for detecting objectified totality, shared illicit premises, and mistaken jurisdiction rather than a warrant for choosing one dogmatic side.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-04","MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Empirical reality is an open, lawfully connected field whose incompletion enables continuing objectivity rather than awaiting metaphysical completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_06:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Rational validity is distributed among empirical cognition, inferential legitimacy, regulation, practical efficacy, obligation, hope, and disciplined non-cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Limitation is positively generative because the experiential boundary protects objectivity, redirects rational ambition, and determines valid methods.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-05","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Critique must become a permanent institution because transcendental illusion is recurrent and cannot be eliminated by a single exposure.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-09","MC_B337_L1_07:XD-03","MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Failed metaphysical objects survive only after conversion into regulative rules, practical commitments, defensive possibilities, or suspended representations.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-10","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-12","text":"[Persistent] Complete empirical explanation and autonomous practical legislation belong to irreducible registers whose coexistence does not yield theoretical reconciliation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-04","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-13","text":"[Mutating] The subject develops from an apperceptive function into a divided empirical–intelligible tribunal and finally into an institutional agent maintaining public criticism.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:HSI-02","MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-08","MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Metaphysics terminates as a critical institution coordinating heterogeneous employments of reason rather than inventorying supersensible beings.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-12","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] Systematic unity governs cognition, action, and collective inquiry only by remaining an absent completion within a formally complete but materially open architecture.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:AS-10","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-01","text":"[Stable Identity / Scope Expansion] Possible experience remains the criterion of object-cognition but expands into one component of a wider jurisdiction governing source, scope, use, and systemic position.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-02","MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation / Escalation] The unconditioned moves from projected completion through cosmological fiction and intelligible possibility into an asymptotic horizon of systematic inquiry.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-01","MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-03","text":"[Reversal / Absorption] Totality ceases to name a completed world and becomes first an unattainable task, then the coordinating attractor of cognitive, ethical, and architectonic completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-02","MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Contradiction changes from evidence of doctrinal defeat into a diagnostic mechanism for identifying illegitimate totalization and defective object-conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-07","MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-05","text":"[Functional Mutation] Limit changes from a prohibitive edge into the productive jurisdiction that validates inquiry and organizes permanent critical infrastructure.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-05","MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-06","text":"[Terminal Transformation] The subject passes through logical subjecthood and ontological indeterminacy before reappearing as a finite judge-builder distributed across cognitive, moral, and public roles.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-05","MC_B337_L1_08:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-07","text":"[Displacement] Time moves from the lawfully ordered sequence of appearances into a nonclosing rational recursion and finally into spiral critical and moral futurity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:RMD-06","MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-08","text":"[Absorption / Disappearance] Metaphysical object-claims disappear as cognition after being redistributed into theoretical suspension, practical postulation, regulation, and method.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:RMD-10","MC_B337_L1_07:MP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-09","text":"[Domain Migration] Freedom moves from an unresolved intelligible condition to dual-character attribution, operative self-determination, and collective moral ordering without becoming theoretical knowledge.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-08","MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-01","text":"[Local / Scaled Upward] Analogies, modal postulates, and particular rules of synthesis remain local machinery, while their scalable effect is the constitution of one field of possible experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-02","text":"[Local / Absorbed] Particular disputes over beginnings, limits, divisibility, and causality remain local proofs whose current-level function is absorbed into a distributed antinomial machine.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:CGD-01","MC_B337_L1_06:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-03","text":"[Transition Catalyst] The individual theological proofs remain local demolitions, while their dependency exposes one recurrent conversion of conceptual or explanatory necessity into existence.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:CGD-06","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-04","text":"[Bridge] Distinctions among regress-forms and synthesis-types scale upward only when joined to the mode of givenness as a general jurisdictional test.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:CGD-06","MC_B337_L1_06:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-05","text":"[Local / Scaled Upward] Moral examples and named postulates remain downstream specifications, while the nonconversion of speculative and practical authority becomes globally load-bearing.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:CGD-06","MC_B337_L1_08:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Transcendental idealism and empirical realism connect cosmological dissolution to the broader principle that empirical completeness does not entail metaphysical exhaustion.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:AS-05","MC_B337_L1_06:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-07","text":"[Superseded / Recoded] Rhetorical victory, doctrinal accumulation, and scepticism as a terminal posture are superseded by recurrent diagnostic interruption and methodological correction.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:CGD-08","MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-08","text":"[Scaled Upward] Architectonic ordering scales into a collectively reproducible critical infrastructure, making philosophy an institutional project rather than an individually possessed doctrine.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:GA-11","MC_B337_L1_08:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-01","text":"Formal derivation, conceptual completeness, and rational necessity cannot establish existence or generate a real object.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GNX-02","MC_B337_L1_07:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-02","text":"Neither the thinking subject nor the completed world can be given as a total object under finite cognitive conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GNX-04","MC_B337_L1_06:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-03","text":"Categories and transcendental ideas cannot become constitutive supersensible cognition without adequate sensible presentation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GNX-03","MC_B337_L1_08:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-04","text":"The defeat of one dogmatic position cannot validate its contrary when both preserve the same fictitious object and illicit conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GNX-05","MC_B337_L1_06:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-05","text":"Temporal and natural causality cannot legislate over things in themselves or reductively absorb autonomous moral legislation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:DBN-06","MC_B337_L1_08:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-06","text":"Practical need, architectonic desirability, and subjective conviction cannot provide speculative intuition or theoretical evidence.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:GNX-08","MC_B337_L1_08:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-07","text":"Neither external sovereignty nor a frictionless immunity from dialectic can ground rational authority; criticism must remain autonomous and recurrent.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:GNX-06","MC_B337_L1_08:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires that the structures demanded by thought not be treated as direct disclosures of independently constituted being.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:DBN-01","MC_B337_L1_07:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-02","text":"Appearances cannot be things in themselves, because their independent completion would restore the totalized world-object that critique dissolves.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-03","text":"No standpoint outside finite cognition can compare appearances with their supersensible ground or survey conditioned reality and complete conditions together.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:DBN-03","MC_B337_L1_07:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-04","text":"Rational validity cannot be arranged on one univocal scale that collapses objective cognition, regulation, practical necessity, belief, and hope into degrees of certainty.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:DBN-02","MC_B337_L1_08:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-05","text":"Metaphysical completion cannot be a prerequisite of cognition, because unfinished regress and finite conditions are productive constituents of objectivity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:DBN-03","MC_B337_L1_07:DBN-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-06","text":"Reason cannot authenticate unrestricted self-legislation immediately, because the faculty that legislates is also the source of recurrent overreach requiring discipline.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:DBN-06","MC_B337_L1_07:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-01","text":"The deepest absent operation is an intellectual intuition capable of presenting the unconditioned and supersensible reality directly.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:BSA-01","MC_B337_L1_07:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-02","text":"The system is organized by the unavailable coincidence between reason’s formal demand for completion and an intuition of the completed whole demanded.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:BSA-02","MC_B337_L1_06:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-03","text":"The unavailable unity of nature and freedom coordinates theoretical cognition and practical action without becoming an object of either.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-04","text":"The correspondence between reason’s drive toward unity and nature’s presumed systematic unifiability lacks a cognizable ground.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-05","text":"The absent present union of virtue and happiness is the negative center from which moral hope and future-oriented postulation arise.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-06","text":"Completed philosophy is constitutively unavailable and can govern collective inquiry only as an absent regulative completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:XD-01","text":"The enabling–restrictive structure first governing categories recurs across ideas, practical commitments, and method: necessary operations are retained only after constitutive overreach is blocked.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-02","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-08","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-11","MC_B337_L1_05:XD-02","MC_B337_L1_08:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:XD-02","text":"Open regress simultaneously grounds empirical extensibility and prevents empirical inquiry from becoming intuition of a completed world.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-01","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-07","MC_B337_L1_06:XD-02","MC_B337_L1_05:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:XD-03","text":"Soul, world, and highest being are scale-differentiated products of one operation in which reason converts functional unity into an apparent object.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-04","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-06","MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-04","MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:XD-04","text":"Antinomial contradiction does not destroy the demand for totality; it exposes the common realist presupposition and returns completion as a regulated task.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-05","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-06","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-10","MC_B337_L1_06:AS-04","MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:XD-05","text":"Because existence is not conceptual content and objective cognition requires presentation, neither complete determination nor constructive form can establish a supersensible existent.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-01","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-03","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-11","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-04","MC_B337_L1_07:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:XD-06","text":"Practical agency survives speculative failure only because practical orientation and theoretical cognition remain nonconvertible modes of warrant.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-08","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-12","MC_B337_L1_07:XD-07","MC_B337_L1_08:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:XD-07","text":"Systematic unity can coordinate empirical inquiry, moral hope, and public philosophy only by remaining an absent end rather than a possessed totality.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-14","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-15","MC_B337_L1_08:XD-06","MC_B337_L1_08:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:XD-08","text":"The recurrence of illusion converts rational progress from doctrinal accumulation into institutionalized methodological correction and collective critical maintenance.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-09","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-10","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-14","MC_B337_L1_08:XD-07","MC_B337_L1_08:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes an open, lawfully connected network of conditioned appearances whose empirical autonomy coexists with metaphysical underdetermination and practical differentiation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:BSD-06","MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-01","MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-02","text":"Knowledge becomes non-terminating and normatively compelled within a differentiated topology of empirical, regulative, practical, and critical warrants.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-05","MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-02","MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-03","text":"The rational subject becomes a divided institutional tribunal that produces projections, disciplines its own excess, legislates action, and maintains public criticism.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:HSI-02","MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-03","MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-04","text":"Time divides into lawful empirical succession, indefinite rational convergence, recurrent critical spirals, and deferred moral–philosophical completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MME-07","MC_B337_L1_07:BSD-04","MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-05","text":"Causality acquires a dual grammar in which maximal empirical determination does not exhaust intelligible attribution or autonomous practical legislation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-04","MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-06","text":"Metaphysics becomes a recursive cartography and constitutional protocol for governing transitions among cognition, regulation, belief, obligation, and hope.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:BSD-03","MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-07","text":"Finite agency can transform the empirical world without complete metaphysical knowledge by combining critical self-government, autonomous law, and reproducible public procedure.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:BSD-04","MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-08","MC_B337_L1_08:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-01","text":"Empirical synthesis generates objectivity, objectivity reinstates the phenomenal boundary, and that boundary redirects rational ambition toward further empirical inquiry.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-01","MC_B337_L1_07:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-02","text":"Conditioned cognition generates the unconditioned, objectification generates conflict, diagnosis recodes completion as regulation, and regulation renews the search for conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-02","MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-03","text":"Possible experience and the unconditioned function as opposed attractors: the first stabilizes objective cognition, while the second perpetually draws reason beyond stable jurisdiction.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-04","text":"Totality marks a phase change when it ceases to name an object and becomes the task coordinating inquiry, ethical completion, and the absent unity of the system.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-04","MC_B337_L1_08:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-05","text":"Illusion generates critique, critique suspends constitutive authority, and illusion’s return repeatedly reactivates the critical institution.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-03","MC_B337_L1_06:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-06","text":"Moral striving generates worthiness, worthiness generates hope, hope generates postulation, and postulation returns to the unconditional priority of moral law.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-07","text":"Private assurance is escalated into communicability, reproducibility, and a public critical infrastructure whose maintenance exceeds individual possession.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:RLE-06","MC_B337_L1_08:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-08","text":"The principal escalation runs from self through world and highest condition, then from failed speculative possession into regulated method, practical agency, and collective architecture.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-04","MC_B337_L1_07:RLE-03","MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FGS-B01","text":"Sensible presentation + categorial synthesis + temporal mediation → empirically objective cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FGS-B02","text":"Constituted empirical rules + conditioned objects → material for reason → demand for systematic unity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FGS-B03","text":"Renewed search for conditions + demand for absolute completeness → formal generation of the unconditioned and ideas of totality.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:FGS-B04","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FGS-B04","text":"Functional unity + constitutive objectification → soul-reification, completed world-representation, and individualized theological ideal.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:RLE-04","MC_B337_L1_07:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FGS-B05","text":"Reciprocal contradiction + proof dependency → exposure of shared realism and one repeated conversion of rational necessity into existence.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:XD-01","MC_B337_L1_07:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FGS-B06","text":"Possible experience + transcendental idealism → empirical realism + withdrawal of constitutive authority from completed supersensible objects.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:AS-07","MC_B337_L1_06:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FGS-B07","text":"Speculative defeat → theoretical suspension + regulative orientation + practical postulation + hope.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:FGS-B06","MC_B337_L1_08:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FGS-B08","text":"Architectonic ordering + communicable procedure + absent completion → public critical infrastructure → recursive prevention of renewed speculative possession.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B07","MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AFM-B01","text":"Sensible givenness → categorial synthesis → temporal connection → one lawfully ordered field of empirical objects.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:GA-02","MC_B337_L1_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AFM-B02","text":"Conditioned cognition → recursive ascent through conditions → unavoidable demand for unconditioned completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:AS-04","MC_B337_L1_06:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AFM-B03","text":"Logical self-unity → soul-reification → world-totalization → antinomy → individualized ideal of complete determination.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:AFM-B05","MC_B337_L1_07:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AFM-B04","text":"Reciprocal contradiction + dependent speculative proofs → exposure of shared illicit object-conditions → jurisdictional diagnosis.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:AS-04","MC_B337_L1_07:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AFM-B05","text":"Experiential restriction → withdrawal of speculative possession → totality recoded as regulation → differentiated practical survival.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:BSD-08","MC_B337_L1_07:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:AFM-B06","text":"Autonomous moral law → natural insufficiency → rational hope → public critical architecture → absent collective completion → recurrent critique.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:AS-05","MC_B337_L1_08:FGS-B07","MC_B337_L1_08:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is relocated from possession of intrinsic objects to an experiential and topological jurisdiction distributed across faculties, warrants, and systemic positions.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MME-01","MC_B337_L1_08:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MME-02","text":"The world is reclassified from a totalizable object into the horizon and task generated by open empirical synthesis.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:MME-01","MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MME-03","text":"The subject is reclassified from inner substance into externally conditioned unity, functional tribunal, dual-character agent, and maintainer of public reason.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MME-03","MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MME-04","text":"Contradiction becomes evidence that a question has illicitly constituted its object rather than evidence of contradictory reality or simple logical collapse.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MME-05","MC_B337_L1_06:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MME-05","text":"Totality and infinity are redistributed among lawful empirical unity, indefinite regress, inexhaustible division, demanded grounding, and unavailable completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MME-04","MC_B337_L1_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MME-06","text":"Natural explanation and freedom cease to be exhaustive rivals and become non-reductive explanatory and legislative registers organized around an unavailable unity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:MME-07","MC_B337_L1_08:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MME-07","text":"Cognitive boundaries become conditions of productivity, while progress becomes methodological and collective rather than accumulative or chronological.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:MME-04","MC_B337_L1_08:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MME-08","text":"Philosophy is reclassified from completed supersensible doctrine into an immanent, disciplinary, and infrastructural project of governing reason’s operations.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MME-02","MC_B337_L1_08:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:ALT-B01","text":"At the surface level, the system treats the subject, world-totality, highest being, freedom, morality, proof, hope, and the public organization of philosophy.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:ALT-B01","MC_B337_L1_08:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:ALT-B02","text":"At the structural level, differentiated faculties and linked jurisdictional filters distribute authority according to presentation, source, scope, employment, and systemic position.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:ALT-B02","MC_B337_L1_08:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:ALT-B03","text":"At the metastrategic level, doctrinal selection is replaced by immanent diagnosis, jurisdictional audit, and redirection of metaphysical energies toward method and autonomous action.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:ALT-B03","MC_B337_L1_08:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:ALT-B04","text":"At the ontological and temporal level, reality is an open phenomenal field ordered through succession, while nature–freedom unity and complete systematicity remain deferred horizons.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:ALT-B04","MC_B337_L1_08:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:ALT-B05","text":"At the methodological and performative level, the architecture repeatedly stages overextension, interrupts it through jurisdictional tests, repositions the remainder, and subordinates theology to autonomy.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:ALT-B05","MC_B337_L1_08:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FL-01","text":"Possible experience both validates transcendental principles and appears constituted by them, leaving the universal authority of the jurisdiction exposed to reciprocal validation.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:FL-01","MC_B337_L1_08:FL-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FL-02","text":"Reason requires the unconditioned to orient grounding while lacking the intuition that could secure it, yet the unavailable terminus must still exert axiom-like force.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:FL-02","MC_B337_L1_06:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FL-03","text":"Systematic unity oscillates among subjective maxim, objectively indeterminate principle, and an unexplained ground of fit between reason and nature.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:FL-02","MC_B337_L1_07:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FL-04","text":"Intelligible agency must ground conduct without competing inside the empirical causal series, but the mediation connecting these relations remains unformalized.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:FL-07","MC_B337_L1_08:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FL-05","text":"The architectonic claims formal completeness and a priori authority while remaining materially open, retrospectively organized, and dependent on a system not yet completed.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:FL-06","MC_B337_L1_08:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:FL-06","text":"Practical theology retains rational authority after speculative failure, but the derivation of determinate content risks reproducing the speculative expansion it excludes.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:FL-05","MC_B337_L1_08:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MP-01","text":"An empirical category mutates into a transcendental idea when its rule is redirected from conditioned synthesis toward absolute completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:MP-01","MC_B337_L1_07:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MP-02","text":"Totality mutates from a constitutive claim about a completed object into an unattainable task governing inquiry.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:MP-02","MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MP-03","text":"Contradiction mutates from apparent defeat into evidence that reason has illicitly objectified unconditioned unity.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MP-04","MC_B337_L1_06:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MP-04","text":"Limit mutates from restriction on scope into empirical productivity, protective redistribution, and institutional authority.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_07:MP-04","MC_B337_L1_08:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MP-05","text":"Metaphysics mutates from speculative acquisition into an institution whose authority consists in coordinating legitimate employments of reason.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_08:MP-04","MC_B337_L1_08:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MP-06","text":"The subject mutates from an indeterminate cognitive operator into a divided institutional agent responsible for cognition, legislation, action, and critical maintenance.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MP-03","MC_B337_L1_08:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality is an open jurisdictional network processed by a tribunal-laboratory that becomes a road system for testing claims, assigning positions, and maintaining legitimate routes.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:HSI-01","MC_B337_L1_06:HSI-01","MC_B337_L1_08:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is an apperceptive relay transformed into a finite judge-builder divided among claimant, opponent, examiner, legislator, moral agent, and public maintainer.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:HSI-02","MC_B337_L1_08:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is a lawfully articulated empirical order nested within open rational recursion and spiral maturation toward deferred moral and philosophical completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:HSI-03","MC_B337_L1_08:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:HSI-04","text":"Navigation consists in recursive boundary work between possible experience and the unconditioned, avoiding both dogmatic possession and sceptical abandonment through action and collective repair.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:HSI-04","MC_B337_L1_08:HSI-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from empirical constitution through rational objectification and dialectical failure to regulative, practical, and institutional recovery.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MRT-B01","MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B01","MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B02","text":"Coherence requires empirical objectivity, regulative legitimacy, practical authority, and historical-critical maintenance to remain distinct while operating within one jurisdictional architecture.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MRT-B03","MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B03","MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B03","text":"The main underdeterminations concern the derivation of the architectonic, the mediation of intelligible agency, the objective status of systematic unity, and positive procedures of criticism.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MRT-B02","MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B02","MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where one jurisdictional logic converts contradiction into diagnosis, finite limitation into empirical validity, and practical restriction into institutional coordination.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MRT-B04","MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B04","MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining sequencing, recursive return, recurrent reclassification, and the reopening of completion as collective work.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_05:MRT-B05","MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B05","MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B06","text":"The irreducible residue is the force by which intelligible possibility, rational hope, and absent unity orient action without becoming objective cognition or admitting complete axiomatic closure.","refs":["MC_B337_L1_06:MRT-B06","MC_B337_L1_08:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · The Critique of Pure Reason","title":"B337 L2 02","context":"Chapters 49–93 · Appendix · Experience · Empicisim · I. Of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · II. Of Pure Reason as the Seat of Transcendental Illusory Appearance · Transcendental Dialectic—Book I—Of The Conceptions Of Pure Reason. · Section I—Of Ideas in General · Section II. Of Transcendental Ideas · Section III. System of Transcendental Ideas · Transcendental Dialectic—Book Ii—Of The Dialectical Procedure Of Pure Reason · Chapter I. Of the Paralogisms of Pure Reason · Chapter II. The Antinomy of Pure Reason · Section I. System of Cosmological Ideas · Section II. Antithetic of Pure Reason · Section III. Of the Interest of Reason in these Self-contradictions · Section IV. Of the necessity imposed upon Pure Reason of presenting a Solution of its Transcendental Problems · Section V. Sceptical Exposition of the Cosmological Problems presented in the four Transcendental Ideas · Section VI. Transcendental Idealism as the Key to the Solution of Pure Cosmological Dialectic · Section VII. Critical Solution of the Cosmological Problem · Section VIII. Regulative Principle of Pure Reason in relation to the Cosmological Ideas · Section IX. Of the Empirical Use of the Regulative Principle of Reason with regard to the Cosmological Ideas · I. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Composition of Phenomena in the Universe · II. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Division of a Whole given in Intuition · III. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Deduction of Cosmical Events from their Causes · IV. Solution of the Cosmological Idea of the Totality of the Dependence of Phenomenal Existences · Section I. Of the Ideal in General · Section II. Of the Transcendental Ideal (Prototypon Trancendentale) · Section III. Of the Arguments employed by Speculative Reason in Proof of the Existence of a Supreme Being · Section IV. Of the Impossibility of an Ontological Proof of the Existence of God · Section V. Of the Impossibility of a Cosmological Proof of the Existence of God · Section VI. Of the Impossibility of a Physico-Theological Proof · Section VII. Critique of all Theology based upon Speculative Principles of Reason · Appendix · II. Transcendental Doctrine of Method · Chapter I. The Discipline of Pure Reason · Section I. The Discipline of Pure Reason in the Sphere of Dogmatism · Section II. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Polemics · Section III. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Hypothesis · Section IV. The Discipline of Pure Reason in Relation to Proofs · Chapter II. The Canon of Pure Reason · Section I. Of the Ultimate End of the Pure Use of Reason · Section II. Of the Ideal of the Summum Bonum as a Determining Ground of the Ultimate End of Pure Reason · Section III. Of Opinion, Knowledge, and Belief · Chapter III. The Architectonic of Pure Reason · Chapter IV. The History of Pure Reason"}},{"id":"WB_B337","layer":"WB","title":"The Critique of Pure Reason","order":135,"parents":["MC_B337_L2_01","MC_B337_L2_02"],"revision":"WB_B337@R002","sha256":"9972d80fbbfe6f74f40be6455996c29896325f057bcd46afeb01cd8a97c1a3fe","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:BAS-01","text":"Finite cognition must secure objective necessity from receptive conditions while answering reason’s demand for completeness without claiming intellectual creation or possession of the unconditioned.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B01","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BAS-02","text":"Objectivity is generated by the coordinated constitution of appearances, while the resulting field supplies conditioned material that necessarily provokes reason’s search for total unity.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B02","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BAS-03","text":"Possible experience is the book’s primary jurisdiction: it enables determinate cognition, restricts categorical authority, and denies constitutive force to supersensible projections.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B04","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BAS-04","text":"The argument becomes a transcendental-constitutional procedure that regresses to the conditions of cognition, stress-tests their illicit extension through contradiction, and redistributes surviving claims among distinct warrants.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B05","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BAS-05","text":"Its movement is recursive and spiral: mediation produces objective validity, conditioned validity generates demands for completion, excess generates conflict, and critique returns each claim to a revised jurisdiction.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B06","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BAS-06","text":"No faculty governs alone; receptive, synthetic, rational, practical, and public functions form a distributed architecture whose unity depends on maintained differentiation.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B03","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BAS-07","text":"Contradiction becomes the decisive transition mechanism because it exposes reason’s conversion of formal demands into objects rather than merely refuting isolated doctrines.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:AS-04","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BAS-08","text":"Closure is jurisdictional rather than total: empirical objectivity is secured, speculative possession is blocked, practical and regulative functions remain active, and completion persists only as an absent task.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B08","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Finite cognition requires received material and spontaneous ordering functions whose cooperation produces objects without erasing their functional differences.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-01","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Synthesis is prior to every analyzed unity and constitutes an empirically open field of conditioned objects.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-03","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Apperceptive unity begins as the condition of representational belonging and develops into a divided subject that must judge, legislate, and criticize its own operations.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-04","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Categories acquire objective force only through sensible and temporal mediation, and lose determinate authority when redirected toward absolute completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-05","MC_B337_L2_01:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Possible experience simultaneously realizes objective cognition, validates its constitutive principles, and restricts every claim to determinate theoretical knowledge.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-07","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-06","text":"[Terminal] A priori principles legislate universal phenomenal form, but the lawful field they constitute remains empirically open and cannot yield its particular content from pure cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-12","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Conditioned cognition necessarily generates the demand for an unconditioned completion that thought can require but theoretical cognition cannot present.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-03","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Transcendental ideas are structurally unavoidable, but illusion begins when their formal necessity is mistaken for constitutive knowledge of supersensible objects.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-04","MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Contradiction functions as a diagnostic algorithm that detects illicit objectification, totalization, and transfers of authority across jurisdictions.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-06","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Limitation is productive because the same restriction that blocks transcendence establishes objective necessity, protects empirical inquiry, and reallocates rational claims.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-08","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Totality changes from an allegedly completed object into an asymptotic task and absent coordinator of inquiry, morality, and systematic form.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-05","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Rational validity is topologically differentiated among cognition, regulation, practical legislation, belief, hope, and critical suspension rather than arranged on one scale.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-08","MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-13","text":"[Persistent] Complete empirical determination and autonomous practical legislation are irreducible registers whose coexistence permits agency without yielding theoretical reconciliation.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-12","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Critique must be both a productive jurisdiction and a permanent institution because the rational operations it validates also regenerate overreach.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-11","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] Metaphysics terminates as a constitutional practice that maps boundaries, coordinates heterogeneous employments of reason, and prevents supersensible doctrine from reclaiming cognitive authority.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-14","MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-01","text":"[Mutation / Scope Expansion] Experience changes from the occasion and constituted product of cognition into the criterion and wider jurisdiction governing every claim to objective use.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-01","MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-02","text":"[Reversal / Terminal Transformation] Subjectivity changes from an apparent obstacle to objectivity into its distributed condition and finally into a divided tribunal responsible for self-discipline.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-03","MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation / Domain Expansion] The object changes from an antecedently unified terminus into the correlate of lawful synthesis, while the world changes from a completed object into an open task.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-04","MC_B337_L2_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-04","text":"[Displacement] Time retains its sensible identity while expanding into the medium of categorial application, rational recursion, critical return, and deferred completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-06","MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-05","text":"[Serial Mutation] Category and judgment move from logical functions to objective determination, while a category redirected toward absolute completion mutates into a transcendental idea.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-07","MC_B337_L2_02:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-06","text":"[Valence Reversal] Restriction changes from deprivation into the enabling boundary of cognition and then into the institutional authority that protects differentiated rational uses.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-08","MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-07","text":"[Reversal / Absorption] Totality ceases to designate a completed world and becomes the absent attractor coordinating empirical inquiry, moral striving, and architectonic completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-03","MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-08","text":"[Reversal] Contradiction changes from evidence of doctrinal failure into evidence that reason has illicitly constituted its question as an object.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-04","MC_B337_L2_02:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-09","text":"[Terminal Transformation] Noumenal and metaphysical objects disappear as possible cognition but survive as negative limits, regulative orientations, practical commitments, or suspended representations.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-10","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-10","text":"[Functional Expansion] Judgment develops from predication and particular application into the distributed tribunal through which the subject assigns cognitive, practical, and critical authority.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MP-04","MC_B337_L2_02:MP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-11","text":"[Scope Expansion] Nature changes from a formally lawful but materially unfinished field into an open network whose incompletion generates indefinite inquiry.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-05","MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RMD-12","text":"[Disappearance / Negative Persistence] Intellectual intuition disappears as a positive model after establishing finite mediation, but persists as the deepest absent operation organizing the system’s limits.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RMD-09","MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:CGD-01","text":"Mathematical construction and scientific examples remain local demonstrations; at whole-book scale they establish the irreducibility of synthetic extension to conceptual analysis.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:CGD-02","text":"Faculty and logical taxonomies scale upward only when their differentiated functions become a constitutional distribution of cognitive authority.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-03","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:CGD-03","text":"Particular schemata, categories, and principles remain local mechanisms, while their higher-order effect is the constitution of one bounded field of possible experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-05","MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:CGD-04","text":"Particular cosmological disputes remain local proofs, but their whole-book function is to instantiate one antinomial machine that diagnoses objectified totality.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-02","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:CGD-05","text":"Individual theological proofs remain local demolitions whose scalable effect is to expose the recurrent conversion of rational necessity into existence.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-03","MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:CGD-06","text":"Moral examples and postulates remain downstream specifications; what scales upward is the nonconversion between speculative cognition and practical authority.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-05","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:CGD-07","text":"Affection, imagination, schema, judgmental application, and intelligible agency remain compressed interfaces rather than fully derived operations of the whole-book machine.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-07","MC_B337_L2_02:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:CGD-08","text":"Non-human intuition remains outside the constituted architecture and blocks its jurisdiction from becoming an ontology of every possible intellect.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-06","MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:CGD-09","text":"Architectonic ordering scales into a collectively reproducible critical infrastructure, transforming individual philosophy into permanent public maintenance.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-08","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:CGD-10","text":"Doctrinal accumulation and terminal scepticism become obsolete once metaphysical conflict is recoded as recurrent diagnostic interruption and jurisdictional repair.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-07","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:GNX-01","text":"Objective necessity cannot be reduced to sensory reception, empirical repetition, habitual succession, or accumulated regularity.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-01","MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GNX-02","text":"Conceptual completeness and rational necessity cannot independently give an object or establish existence.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-02","MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GNX-03","text":"The subject can be neither private arbitrariness nor a positively cognized inner substance.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-03","MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GNX-04","text":"A priori origin cannot authorize unrestricted objective scope, and transcendental ideas cannot become constitutive supersensible cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-04","MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GNX-05","text":"Cognizable objects are neither wholly preconstituted unities nor existents created by subjective synthesis.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-05","MC_B337_L2_01:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GNX-06","text":"Phenomena and noumena cannot form two positively cognized worlds, because no adequate presentation establishes the supersensible domain.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-07","MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GNX-07","text":"Possession of universal rules cannot eliminate judgment or mechanically determine every particular application.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GNX-08","MC_B337_L2_01:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GNX-08","text":"Refuting one dogmatic position cannot validate its contrary when both positions preserve the same illicitly constituted object.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-04","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GNX-09","text":"Natural causality cannot absorb autonomous moral legislation, and practical need cannot be converted into speculative evidence.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-05","MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:GNX-10","text":"Rational authority cannot be secured through external sovereignty or immunity from criticism; critique must remain autonomous and recurrent.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-07","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:DBN-01","text":"No standpoint outside finite sensibility is available from which appearance, supersensible ground, and completed conditions could be compared.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-01","MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:DBN-02","text":"No single finite faculty can receive, originate, unify, totalize, and know its object through one self-sufficient operation.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-02","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:DBN-03","text":"Direct correspondence must be excluded because the structures demanded by thought cannot be independently compared with being outside cognition’s conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-03","MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:DBN-04","text":"Psychological occurrence and habitual succession cannot become strict necessity, just as subjective conviction cannot become speculative evidence.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-04","MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:DBN-05","text":"Systematic completeness cannot arise through accumulation or terminate in completed philosophy, because the whole governs only as an unavailable completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-05","MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:DBN-06","text":"Functional necessity cannot be converted into a genetic ontology explaining the origin, number, or common root of reason’s powers.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-06","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:DBN-07","text":"Rational validity cannot be arranged on a univocal scale without collapsing cognition, regulation, practical authority, belief, and hope into interchangeable warrants.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-04","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:DBN-08","text":"Metaphysical completion cannot be a prerequisite for cognition, because open regress and empirical incompletion are productive conditions of inquiry.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-05","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:DBN-09","text":"Reason cannot claim immediate immunity for its self-legislation because the faculty assigning unity is also the source of recurrent overreach.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-06","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:BSA-01","text":"The system’s deepest excluded faculty is intellectual intuition, in which presentation, creation, thought, and knowledge of the unconditioned would coincide.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSA-01","MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSA-02","text":"The architecture is organized around the absence of an absolute outside capable of surveying finite cognition and completed reality together.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSA-02","MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSA-03","text":"A self-grounding unity of the cognitive faculties remains unavailable because no independently prior term terminates their reciprocal dependence.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSA-04","text":"Reason is driven by the unavailable coincidence between its formal demand for completion and an intuition of the completed whole.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-02","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSA-05","text":"Positive supersensible cognition is barred by the absence of a nonempirical mediator capable of presenting unconditioned objects.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSA-04","MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSA-06","text":"The unavailable unity of nature and freedom coordinates theoretical and practical reason without becoming an object of either register.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-03","MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSA-07","text":"The absent union of virtue and happiness generates moral hope, while the constitutive unavailability of completed philosophy generates collective critical futurity.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-05","MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSA-08","text":"Absolute closure is inadmissible because reality exceeds appearance, the supersensible remains unpresented, and reason cannot derive or complete all its own conditions.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSA-05","MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}]},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:XD-01","text":"The constitution of objective experience necessarily generates conditioned series whose intelligibility provokes reason’s demand for an unconditioned terminus.","refs":["WB_B337:GA-02","WB_B337:GA-05","WB_B337:GA-07","MC_B337_L2_01:XD-03","MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:XD-02","text":"The sensible restriction that grants categories objective authority also explains why transcendental ideas cannot acquire constitutive objectivity.","refs":["WB_B337:GA-04","WB_B337:GA-05","WB_B337:GA-08","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-13","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:XD-03","text":"Time shifts from the operational relay of categorial application into the open recursion through which reason, criticism, and moral completion fail to close.","refs":["WB_B337:GA-04","WB_B337:GA-07","WB_B337:GA-11","MC_B337_L2_01:XD-06","MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:XD-04","text":"Apperceptive unity must become a self-critical tribunal because the subject that unifies experience also generates and objectifies rational illusion.","refs":["WB_B337:GA-03","WB_B337:GA-08","WB_B337:GA-14","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-04","MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:XD-05","text":"The formal lawfulness and material incompletion of nature convert empirical openness into the legitimate continuation of inquiry and prevent the world from becoming a completed object.","refs":["WB_B337:GA-06","WB_B337:GA-11","MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-05","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:XD-06","text":"Categorial error and metaphysical illusion instantiate one structure: a valid formal function is detached from its conditions and converted into an independently determinate object.","refs":["WB_B337:GA-04","WB_B337:GA-08","WB_B337:GA-10","MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-06","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:XD-07","text":"Contradiction diagnoses not merely incompatible conclusions but the shared realist premise through which reason objectifies an unavailable totality.","refs":["WB_B337:GA-09","WB_B337:GA-11","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-06","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:XD-08","text":"Failed metaphysical objects can survive only if positive cognition is withdrawn and their remainder is redistributed into negative, regulative, practical, or suspensive functions.","refs":["WB_B337:GA-08","WB_B337:GA-10","WB_B337:GA-12","MC_B337_L2_01:XD-09","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:XD-09","text":"Practical freedom becomes operable because universal phenomenal determination does not exhaust intelligible attribution, although this non-exhaustion cannot become theoretical knowledge.","refs":["WB_B337:GA-06","WB_B337:GA-12","WB_B337:GA-13","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-12","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:XD-10","text":"The combination of recurrent illusion and absent systematic completion makes public critical maintenance, rather than doctrinal possession, the only viable form of architectonic closure.","refs":["WB_B337:GA-11","WB_B337:GA-14","WB_B337:GA-15","MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-05","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:BSD-01","text":"Reality for finite cognition becomes a conditionally constituted and lawfully connected field that is objectively valid, empirically open, and metaphysically underdetermined.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-01","MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes a distributed tribunal that constitutes experience, projects totality, disciplines its projections, legislates action, and maintains public criticism.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-02","MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSD-03","text":"Time becomes a stratified medium comprising empirical succession, categorial application, rational recursion, critical return, and deferred moral completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-03","MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSD-04","text":"Necessity becomes jurisdictional and plural: constitutive necessity governs possible experience, while regulative and practical necessities exert force without becoming object-cognition.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-04","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSD-05","text":"Nature becomes formally lawful but materially unfinished, and the world therefore becomes an open task rather than a metaphysically completed object.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-05","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSD-06","text":"Error becomes methodologically productive when recurrent overreach is converted into a diagnostic of invalid jurisdiction and a motive for critical repair.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-06","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSD-07","text":"Causality acquires a dual grammar in which complete empirical determination coexists with irreducible practical attribution without theoretical synthesis of the two.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-05","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSD-08","text":"Metaphysics becomes a recursive constitutional protocol that assigns claims to cognition, regulation, practical legislation, belief, hope, or suspension.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:BSD-07","MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSD-09","text":"Finite agency becomes possible without completed metaphysical knowledge because critical self-government can combine empirical intervention, autonomous legislation, and reproducible public procedure.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-07","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:BSD-10","text":"Whole-book closure consists in a formally ordered but materially open system whose unavailable completion continues to orient cognition and action.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B08","MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:RLE-01","text":"Affection generates ordered material, synthesis generates objectivity, objectivity reinstates the phenomenal boundary, and that boundary redirects inquiry toward further conditioned objects.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-01","MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RLE-02","text":"Objective synthesis generates conditioned cognition, conditioned cognition generates the unconditioned, and attempted objectification converts rational ascent into dialectical conflict.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-02","MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RLE-03","text":"Every enabling condition becomes a restriction, and every restriction returns as the safeguard of a legitimate but bounded authority.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-03","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RLE-04","text":"Rule-possession generates problems of application, while the search for complete conditions generates a parallel regress that no additional universal rule can terminate.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-04","MC_B337_L2_02:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RLE-05","text":"Possible experience and the unconditioned operate as opposed attractors: one stabilizes objectivity, while the other perpetually destabilizes every claim to final completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-03","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RLE-06","text":"Totality marks a phase change when it ceases to name an object and becomes the absent task coordinating inquiry, morality, and architectonic form.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-04","MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RLE-07","text":"Illusion generates critique, critique suspends constitutive authority, and the inevitable return of illusion reactivates the critical institution.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-05","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RLE-08","text":"The subject’s progression from apperceptive relay to public judge-builder is driven by the need to govern the excess produced by its own rational operations.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:HSI-02","MC_B337_L2_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RLE-09","text":"Moral striving generates worthiness, worthiness generates hope, and hope generates postulates that return authority to the unconditional priority of moral law.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-06","MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:RLE-10","text":"Architectonic unity assigns positions to cognitive functions, while public criticism repeatedly tests and repairs the system whose completion remains unavailable.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-05","MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:FGS-B01","text":"Affection + spatiotemporal form → an ordered manifold lacking objective unity.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:RLE-01","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FGS-B02","text":"Ordered manifold + synthesis + apperceptive unity → representations attributable to one consciousness.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-03","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FGS-B03","text":"Apperceptive synthesis + categories + temporal mediation + judgment → determinate object-relation.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-05","MC_B337_L2_01:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FGS-B04","text":"Determinate object-relation + possible experience → universal phenomenal lawfulness with empirically open content.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-07","MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FGS-B05","text":"Conditioned empirical cognition + recursive search for grounds → necessary demand for the unconditioned.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-03","MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FGS-B06","text":"Formal demand for unity + constitutive objectification → soul-reification, world-totalization, and the ideal of a highest being.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:AS-04","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FGS-B07","text":"Reciprocal contradiction + shared realist premises → diagnosis of illicit object-conditions rather than selection of a dogmatic victor.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-06","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FGS-B08","text":"Withdrawal of speculative authority + preservation of rational function → regulation, suspension, defensive possibility, and practical orientation.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-11","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FGS-B09","text":"Empirical determination + irreducible practical legislation → finite agency without theoretical reconciliation of nature and freedom.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-12","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FGS-B10","text":"Recurrent illusion + architectonic ordering + communicable procedure → permanent public critique organized around absent completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:GA-10","MC_B337_L2_02:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:AFM-B01","text":"Receptive affection → spatiotemporal ordering → a manifold available but not yet unified as an object.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-01","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:AFM-B02","text":"Manifold → synthesis → apperceptive belonging → categorial and judgmental determination.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:GA-03","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:AFM-B03","text":"Schematized determination → possible experience → universally lawful but empirically unfinished nature.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:XD-07","MC_B337_L2_01:AS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:AFM-B04","text":"Conditioned cognition → recursive ascent through conditions → necessary projection of unconditioned completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:RLE-02","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:AFM-B05","text":"Projected completion → soul, world, and highest being → constitutive objectification.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:XD-03","MC_B337_L2_02:AS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:AFM-B06","text":"Objectified totality → antinomial contradiction and proof dependency → exposure of shared illicit premises.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:XD-04","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:AFM-B07","text":"Jurisdictional diagnosis → withdrawal of speculative possession → regulative, practical, and suspensive redistribution.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:AS-06","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:AFM-B08","text":"Autonomous action and rational hope → architectonic ordering → public critical infrastructure → recurrent correction without final completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-07","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:MME-01","text":"Objectivity is relocated from correspondence with independently available being to a jurisdiction constituted through synthesis, mediation, and valid application.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MME-01","MC_B337_L2_02:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MME-02","text":"The subject–object opposition becomes an asymmetric circuit whose subjective pole later differentiates into a tribunal governing the circuit’s legitimate uses.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MME-02","MC_B337_L2_02:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MME-03","text":"The world is reclassified from a totalizable object into the indefinitely extensible horizon generated by empirical synthesis.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:MME-02","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MME-04","text":"Necessity is reclassified from unrestricted modal force into authority indexed to constitutive, regulative, practical, or critical jurisdiction.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MME-05","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MME-05","text":"Contradiction becomes evidence of illicit object-constitution and shared premises rather than direct evidence of contradictory reality.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:MME-04","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MME-06","text":"The a priori–empirical opposition becomes a division of labor between universal phenomenal form and indefinitely discoverable empirical content.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MME-03","MC_B337_L2_01:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MME-07","text":"Natural explanation and freedom cease to be exhaustive rivals and become irreducible registers whose unavailable unity prevents reduction in either direction.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:MME-06","MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MME-08","text":"Metaphysics is reorganized from supersensible inventory into recursive cartography, jurisdictional audit, and constitutional coordination.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MME-07","MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MME-09","text":"Progress is reclassified from doctrinal accumulation into collective methodological correction sustained by recurrent criticism.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:MME-07","MC_B337_L2_02:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MME-10","text":"Logic is redistributed among formal admissibility, transcendental object-constitution, dialectical diagnosis, practical legislation, and architectonic organization.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MME-04","MC_B337_L2_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface level] The explicit field runs from sensation, space, time, judgment, and categories to soul, world, highest being, freedom, morality, hope, and public philosophy.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:ALT-B01","MC_B337_L2_02:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural level] Cognition and reason form a distributed dependency system in which authority is assigned according to presentation, source, scope, mediation, use, and systemic position.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:ALT-B02","MC_B337_L2_02:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic level] Interpretation shifts from inventorying doctrines to reconstructing enabling conditions, auditing jurisdiction, diagnosing objectification, and redirecting failed claims.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:ALT-B03","MC_B337_L2_02:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal level] Reality is an open phenomenal order governed by empirical succession, while supersensible completion, nature–freedom unity, and final systematicity remain deferred.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:ALT-B04","MC_B337_L2_02:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative level] The system performs its thesis by staging overextension, interrupting it through deprivation and contradiction, redistributing the remainder, and recursively rebuilding jurisdiction.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:ALT-B05","MC_B337_L2_02:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:FL-01","text":"Possible experience validates transcendental principles while appearing constituted by those same principles, leaving the ultimate jurisdiction reciprocally grounded.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:FL-01","MC_B337_L2_01:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FL-02","text":"Receptivity requires an affecting ground, yet the relation between that ground and sensible affection cannot be positively cognized.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:FL-01","MC_B337_L2_01:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FL-03","text":"Intuition and conception must unite, but imagination, schema, synthesis, and judgment remain compressed interfaces rather than mechanically derived connections.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:FL-02","MC_B337_L2_01:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FL-04","text":"The completeness of the category-system and the architectonic is claimed a priori even though their exact configuration remains factical, retrospective, and materially open.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:FL-03","MC_B337_L2_02:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FL-05","text":"Reason requires the unconditioned to orient grounding, although the absent intuition needed to secure that terminus leaves its force underdetermined.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:FL-02","MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FL-06","text":"Systematic unity oscillates among subjective maxim, objectively indeterminate principle, and unexplained presupposition of fit between reason and nature.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:FL-03","MC_B337_L2_02:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FL-07","text":"Intelligible agency must ground practical attribution without entering the empirical causal series, but the mediation connecting these registers remains unformalized.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:FL-04","MC_B337_L2_02:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FL-08","text":"Practical theology preserves rational authority after speculative defeat, but determinate postulation risks reproducing the supersensible expansion that critique forbids.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:FL-06","MC_B337_L2_02:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:FL-09","text":"The noumenal remainder is required to prevent phenomenal absolutization, yet its positive possibility and relation to appearances cannot be established.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:FL-07","MC_B337_L2_01:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MP-01","text":"The a priori mutates from a classification of origin into sensible form, constitutive authority, jurisdictional restriction, and the bounded structure of experience.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MP-01","MC_B337_L2_02:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MP-02","text":"Time mutates from sensible form into schematic and categorial mediation, then into rational recursion and deferred critical-moral futurity.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MP-03","MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MP-03","text":"The subject mutates from a cognitive operator into a distributed institutional agent responsible for objectivity, legislation, action, and critical maintenance.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:MP-06","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MP-04","text":"The object mutates from an antecedent unity into the synthesized correlate of givenness, while the world-object mutates into an open horizon.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MP-02","MC_B337_L2_02:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MP-05","text":"An empirical category mutates into a transcendental idea when its rule is redirected from conditioned synthesis toward absolute completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:MP-01","MC_B337_L2_02:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MP-06","text":"Totality mutates from a constitutive claim about a completed object into the unattainable task governing inquiry and systematicity.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:MP-02","MC_B337_L2_02:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MP-07","text":"Contradiction mutates from apparent doctrinal defeat into evidence that reason has illicitly objectified unconditioned unity.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_02:MP-03","MC_B337_L2_02:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MP-08","text":"Critique and metaphysics mutate from negative restriction and speculative acquisition into productive jurisdiction and permanent institutional coordination.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MP-05","MC_B337_L2_02:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:HSI-01","text":"Reality is a bounded conversion network in which received multiplicity becomes appearance, appearance becomes conditioned knowledge, and conditioned knowledge opens onto unpossessable completion.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:HSI-01","MC_B337_L2_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a distributed switching-point transformed into a judge-builder who alternates among claimant, synthesizer, examiner, legislator, and public maintainer.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:HSI-02","MC_B337_L2_02:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:HSI-03","text":"Time is a nested transmission system joining sensible succession and categorial application to open rational recursion and deferred collective maturation.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:HSI-03","MC_B337_L2_02:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:HSI-04","text":"Knowledge is a gated road system in which claims advance only through sensible presentation, objective entitlement, appropriate warrant, and lawful jurisdiction.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:HSI-04","MC_B337_L2_02:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:HSI-05","text":"Rational struggle is recursive boundary navigation: dogmatic possession and sceptical abandonment are both refused in favor of empirical inquiry, autonomous action, and collective repair.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:HSI-05","MC_B337_L2_02:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}]},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"WB_B337:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from receptive object-constitution through rational objectification and dialectical failure to regulative, practical, and institutional recovery.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B01","MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MRT-B02","text":"Coherence requires receptive givenness, empirical objectivity, regulative legitimacy, practical authority, and critical maintenance to remain distinct while participating in one jurisdictional architecture.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B02","MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MRT-B03","text":"The reconstruction is strongest where one enabling–restrictive logic connects synthesis, objective validity, contradiction, practical survival, and institutional coordination.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B03","MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined at affection, faculty-union, logical completeness, schema, judgmental application, intelligible agency, systematic unity, and positive critical procedure.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B04","MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining recursive return, deprivation, contradiction, reclassification, and the reopening of completion as collective work.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B05","MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"WB_B337:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable residue is the unavailable force by which noumenal excess, rational hope, intelligible possibility, and absent unity orient the system without admitting complete axiomatic closure.","refs":["MC_B337_L2_01:MRT-B06","MC_B337_L2_02:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}]}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"WB","kicker":"Whole book","title":"The Critique of Pure Reason","context":""}}],"seal":"SAVED_0C313586826C58D7566B4ACB","version_work_id":"WORK_TRIAL_65502ADE3E1241659B2681AB7329479B","version_label":"Version 5 · Build · Added 9 Nov 2026 · Run 9 Nov 2026 · gpt-5.6-sol / gpt-5.6-terra","version_revision":"2d9a104e0581957bae91967a2ef152810c2aabdaf4215e3bcfd947c7dc623a5d"},{"slug":"hume","title":"A Treatise of Human Nature","author":"David Hume","edition":"Original English · Gutenberg eText #4705 · retained 2012 transcription","source_url":"https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/4705","year":"1739–1740","description":"How do perceptions become beliefs, a self, and a world held in common?","language_code":"en","language_name":"English","cover_url":"./assets/covers/beta-d0138d38bb5ad732-8308e15450bed635.png","id":"WORK_A_TREATISE_OF_HUMAN_NATURE_DAVID_HUME","seal":"SEAL_THN_B3917927880001FF07EB","units":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR","layer":"CH","title":"Introduction","order":1,"parents":["RAW_THN_INTR"],"revision":"CH_THN_INTR@R001","sha256":"dd8ac7fb96e4845069ae1d62bb4f83c69ee978f2a08d3932b23d8bab63f6e4d5","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS1","text":"The governing problem is the absence of evidentially secure and internally coherent foundations across the sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS2","text":"Scientific disorder displaces rational adjudication with controversy and rhetorical persuasion, thereby discrediting philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS3","text":"The resulting rejection of abstruse reasoning mistakes previous failure for proof that difficult inquiry is illegitimate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS4","text":"Human nature is the common epistemic center of the sciences because every science is cognized and judged through human faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS5","text":"The science of human nature must itself be reconstructed through experience and observation rather than speculation about mental essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS6","text":"Moral experimentation must use naturally occurring conduct because deliberate self-experiment alters the operations under investigation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AS7","text":"The argument is diagnostic, polemical, and reconstructive: foundational crisis → anti-metaphysical prejudice → anthropological recentering → experimental method → epistemic limit → observational protocol.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A1","text":"Inherited philosophical systems rest on inadequately warranted and incoherently connected principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A2","text":"Where decisive evidence is absent, eloquence can govern assent more effectively than reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A3","text":"Repeated failure in abstruse inquiry produces indiscriminate aversion to metaphysical reasoning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A4","text":"If truth is within human capacity, its depth makes sustained abstruse inquiry necessary rather than dispensable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A5","text":"Every science is cognized and judged through human powers and faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A6","text":"The most humanly important sciences directly concern reasoning, ideas, sentiments, tastes, and social dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A7","text":"Every important scientific decision depends on prior acquaintance with human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A8","text":"The science of human nature can be securely founded only on experience and observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A9","text":"The essence of mind is no more cognitively available than the essence of external bodies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A10","text":"Mental powers and qualities are knowable only through their effects under differing circumstances and situations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A11","text":"Observed effects should be reduced to maximally universal principles and the simplest, fewest causes compatible with experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A12","text":"Inquiry into human nature cannot legitimately pass beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A13","text":"Hypotheses claiming access to the ultimate original qualities of human nature are presumptuous and chimerical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A14","text":"The experiential limit is common to every science and art.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A15","text":"Recognition that reason has reached its utmost extent dissolves the demand for further explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A16","text":"Premeditated production of moral phenomena alters the natural principles being observed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A17","text":"Ordinary human conduct is the proper evidential field for moral experiments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:A18","text":"Judicious collection and comparison of ordinary-life observations can establish a science of human nature with non-inferior certainty and superior utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX1","text":"Established credit does not supply secure warrant for philosophical principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX2","text":"The proliferation and intensity of disputes do not constitute certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX3","text":"Rhetorical attractiveness is not rational evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX4","text":"Metaphysical reasoning is not confined to a particular subject matter; the prejudice targets abstruse argument generally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX5","text":"Previous failures in difficult inquiry do not justify abandoning it without embracing comprehensive scepticism and indolence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX6","text":"Ease and obviousness are not presumptions in favor of philosophical truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX7","text":"Mental powers cannot be deduced from speculative knowledge of mental essence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX8","text":"The pursuit of universal principles does not license passage beyond experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX9","text":"Ultimate qualities of human nature are not legitimate objects of explanatory hypothesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX10","text":"Moral experiments cannot be validly produced by deliberately placing oneself in the condition under examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:NX11","text":"Ignorance of ultimate principles is not a defect peculiar to the science of human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D1","text":"Systemic evidential disorder both discredits philosophy and generates overbroad rejection of difficult reasoning.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A1","CH_THN_INTR:A2","CH_THN_INTR:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D2","text":"Past failure supplies no warrant for methodological indolence if truth remains humanly attainable.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A3","CH_THN_INTR:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D3","text":"Reform of the science of human nature becomes the precondition for reforming the sciences dependent upon human reasoning, sentiment, and sociality.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A5","CH_THN_INTR:A6","CH_THN_INTR:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D4","text":"Investigation of mind must proceed from circumstance and effect to powers and operations, not from essence to deduced consequences.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A8","CH_THN_INTR:A9","CH_THN_INTR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D5","text":"Empirical generalization may compress effects into simple causes but may not convert causal economy into an account of ultimate origins.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_INTR:A12","CH_THN_INTR:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D6","text":"Confessed ignorance at the experiential boundary constitutes legitimate closure rather than a unique failure of moral philosophy.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A12","CH_THN_INTR:A14","CH_THN_INTR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D7","text":"Moral experimentalism must be naturalistic and observational rather than deliberately interventionist.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A16","CH_THN_INTR:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D8","text":"Comparison across ordinary circumstances supplies the variation from which general principles of human nature may be inferred.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_INTR:A17","CH_THN_INTR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:D9","text":"A successful science of human nature acquires exceptional utility because its closest dependent sciences govern reasoning, evaluation, and social life.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A6","CH_THN_INTR:A7","CH_THN_INTR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD1","text":"The science of human nature becomes a metascience governing the possible security of other sciences while remaining empirically constrained itself.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A5","CH_THN_INTR:A7","CH_THN_INTR:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD2","text":"Foundational security is redefined from access to essence into disciplined knowledge of recurrent effects within experiential limits.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A8","CH_THN_INTR:A9","CH_THN_INTR:A10","CH_THN_INTR:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD3","text":"Reflexivity does not defeat moral science; it relocates experimentation from constructed situations to naturally occurring social life.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A16","CH_THN_INTR:A17","CH_THN_INTR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD4","text":"Scepticism receives a selective function: it destroys inherited pretensions and ultimate hypotheses but cannot license abandonment of abstruse inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A1","CH_THN_INTR:A4","CH_THN_INTR:A13","CH_THN_INTR:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD5","text":"Scientific certainty and ultimate ignorance can coexist because certainty concerns observed regularities while ignorance concerns their final origin.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_INTR:A12","CH_THN_INTR:A14","CH_THN_INTR:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SD6","text":"Humanity becomes both the medium through which science operates and the object whose investigation is required to secure that operation.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A5","CH_THN_INTR:A7","CH_THN_INTR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX1","text":"Scientific objects and standards are not intelligible independently of the faculties by which humans apprehend and judge them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX2","text":"Knowledge of mental operations does not require prior access to an underlying mental substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX3","text":"Human conduct cannot be irreducibly singular or unstable; recurrent relations across circumstances must be available to comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX4","text":"Controlled intervention cannot be a necessary condition of all scientific warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX5","text":"Premeditated introspection cannot be assumed to leave the operations under inspection unchanged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:DNX6","text":"A science need not justify its most general experiential principles through a deeper non-experiential ground.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SNX1","text":"Ultimate original qualities remain unavailable, and their absence functions as the terminal boundary of legitimate explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SNX2","text":"No science can occupy an unmediated standpoint outside human nature from which human cognition could be judged independently.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SNX3","text":"A pure moral experiment separating observer, intervention, and observed operation is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:SNX4","text":"The proposed foundation of the sciences has no further foundation beyond experienced reality and the regularities abstracted from it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS1","text":"Insecure principles → defective deductions → unresolved controversy → rhetorical assent → philosophical discredit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS2","text":"Philosophical discredit → indiscriminate anti-metaphysical prejudice → conditional attainability of deep truth → reinstatement of abstruse inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS3","text":"Mediation of every science by human faculties → common dependence on human nature → strategic redirection toward the science of man.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS4","text":"Unavailable mental essence → observation of effects across circumstances → inference to powers and operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS5","text":"Comparative observations → causal simplification → maximally general empirical principles → bounded scientific system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS6","text":"Claim to ultimate qualities → transgression of experiential limits → rejection of hypothesis → confessed ignorance as closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:FGS7","text":"Deliberate moral intervention → reflexive disturbance → ordinary conduct as experimental field → judicious comparison → moral science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:AF1","text":"Foundational disorder → rhetorical controversy → anti-metaphysical prejudice → defense of difficult inquiry → human nature as common center → experience as foundation → ultimate explanation excluded → ordinary life converted into the field of moral experiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME1","text":"Disciplinary disorder is reclassified from a plurality of local failures into a common foundational deficit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME2","text":"Human nature is repositioned from one scientific object among others to the epistemic center of the sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME3","text":"Metaphysics is divided into legitimate abstruse empirical reasoning and illegitimate speculation about ultimate qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME4","text":"Philosophical foundation is reclassified from knowledge of essences into observed regularities and constrained causal generalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME5","text":"Ordinary social life becomes an experimental archive rather than merely the domain to which theory is applied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME6","text":"The difference between natural and moral science becomes a difference in experimental access rather than a difference in possible scientific status.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ME7","text":"The opposition between certainty and confessed ignorance collapses once certainty is assigned to experienced relations and ignorance to ultimate origins.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ALT1","text":"Surface — Existing sciences lack secure principles, coherent deductions, and rationally decisive outcomes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ALT2","text":"Structural — Human faculties mediate every scientific judgment, making human nature the common dependency beneath disciplinary plurality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Inquiry is redirected from piecemeal disciplinary conquest toward the central investigation of human nature and its downstream sciences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Essences and ultimate origins remain inaccessible; reality enters inquiry as effects under circumstances, while experimental philosophy extends historically from nature to humanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from disciplinary crisis through anthropological recentering to observational moral science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT2","text":"The specific taxonomy of human faculties, the rules of observational comparison, and the criterion of adequate certainty remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent insofar as inaccessible essence, empirical method, causal compression, and explanatory limitation mutually constrain one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT4","text":"The strongest mechanism is the conversion of reflexive interference from an objection against moral science into a reason for naturalistic observation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT5","text":"The transition from human mediation of science to the exclusive foundational priority of the science of man is asserted more strongly than it is demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT6","text":"The promise of non-inferior certainty depends on ordinary-life observations supplying sufficient control, representativeness, and comparability despite the prohibition on constructed experiments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT7","text":"The universal prohibition on passing beyond experience risks functioning as a methodological principle whose own experiential warrant is not supplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT8","text":"Foundational recursion remains unresolved because the science intended to secure human cognition must already employ cognition, observation, and comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_INTR:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real argumentative movement without the martial, patriotic, and honorific rhetoric, none of which generates the methodological conclusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_INTR:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 01","title":"Introduction","context":"Chapter 01","original_title":"Introduction","original_context":"Chapter 01"},"original_title":"Introduction","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas","order":2,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIOO"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIOO@R001","sha256":"bcf40194b70e4074c589b58c2958589f8b807bb364d3b8645afd1092931c051a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS1","text":"Problem: determine the kinds of mental perception and the direction of origin between them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS2","text":"Core thesis: simple ideas, on first appearance, derive from prior corresponding simple impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS3","text":"Argument type: introspective taxonomy → empirical genealogy → anti-innatist reconstruction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS4","text":"Initial impression–idea resemblance is restricted through the simple–complex distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS5","text":"Correspondence plus constant conjunction establishes dependence; temporal priority fixes impression-to-idea causation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AS6","text":"Deprivation and recursive copying qualify the maxim; the missing-shade anomaly is contained rather than permitted to revise it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A1","text":"Every mental perception is either an impression or an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A2","text":"Impressions are perceptions at first appearance marked generally by greater force and liveliness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A3","text":"Ideas are fainter perceptions operating in thinking and reasoning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A4","text":"Every impression and idea is either simple or complex.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A5","text":"Simple perceptions admit no distinction or separation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A6","text":"Complex perceptions are formed from distinguishable simple parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A7","text":"Every simple perception has a correspondent in the other perception class that matches its content and differs only in force and liveliness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A8","text":"Complex ideas and complex impressions need not correspond as exact wholes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A9","text":"Constant conjunction between corresponding perceptions establishes dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A10","text":"Within a dependent pair, temporal priority identifies causal direction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A11","text":"A simple impression precedes its corresponding idea on first appearance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A12","text":"Unavailable operation of an impression-producing faculty entails absence of the corresponding idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A13","text":"Every simple idea on first appearance derives from its corresponding simple impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A14","text":"Secondary ideas may copy prior ideas while remaining mediately derived from impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A15","text":"A missing shade can be imaginatively supplied as a simple idea without its corresponding prior impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:A16","text":"A singular counterinstance is insufficient to alter the generally established derivation maxim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX1","text":"Impression does not name the mechanism by which a lively perception is produced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX2","text":"Idea does not name every mental perception indiscriminately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX3","text":"The impression–idea distinction is not infallibly discriminable by vivacity in every exceptional state.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX4","text":"Exact whole-to-whole correspondence is not universal among complex perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX5","text":"Merely thinking an idea does not ordinarily produce its corresponding sensation, passion, or emotion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX6","text":"The conjunction of impressions and ideas is not treated as accidental or causally neutral.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX7","text":"Secondary ideation is not a reversal of the impression-to-idea genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX8","text":"The missing-shade case is not allowed to repeal the general maxim.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:NX9","text":"Ideas preceded by corresponding lively perceptions are not innate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D1","text":"The perception field is organized by two intersecting distinctions: impression versus idea and simple versus complex. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A1 + CH_THN_SIOO:A4 + CH_THN_SIOO:A5 + CH_THN_SIOO:A6)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D2","text":"Failure of exact complex correspondence does not defeat exact correspondence at the simple level. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A6 + CH_THN_SIOO:A7 + CH_THN_SIOO:A8)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D3","text":"Corresponding simple impressions and ideas form dependent rather than accidental pairs. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A7 + CH_THN_SIOO:A9)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D4","text":"Impressions are causes and first ideas are effects. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A9 + CH_THN_SIOO:A10 + CH_THN_SIOO:A11)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D5","text":"Normal acquisition of a simple idea requires operative access to its corresponding impression. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A12 + CH_THN_SIOO:A13)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D6","text":"Complex imagination may exceed experienced wholes without exceeding impression-derived simple content. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A6 + CH_THN_SIOO:A7 + CH_THN_SIOO:A8 + CH_THN_SIOO:A13)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D7","text":"Ideas may generate further ideas without becoming the ultimate source of their simple content. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A13 + CH_THN_SIOO:A14)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D8","text":"The admitted missing-shade case converts the derivation principle logically into an exception-tolerant general maxim. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A13 + CH_THN_SIOO:A15 + CH_THN_SIOO:A16)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:D9","text":"The dispute over innate ideas is reconstructed as a question of whether ideas are preceded by livelier perceptions. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIOO:A2 + CH_THN_SIOO:A3 + CH_THN_SIOO:A11 + CH_THN_SIOO:A13)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","text":"Empirical restriction at the level of simple content coexists with recombinatorial freedom at the level of complex ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SD2","text":"The origin of an idea becomes a temporal-causal question rather than a question settled by its apparent content alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SD3","text":"The missing-shade case reveals that relations among acquired ideas may generate an unexperienced simple candidate, but the system quarantines this generativity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SD4","text":"The proof of the copy principle employs constant conjunction and succession as causal criteria before the promised full examination of causation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SD5","text":"Where force and liveliness cease to discriminate impressions from ideas, genealogical order must carry the classificatory burden.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:DNX1","text":"Simple ideational content cannot generally originate from an autonomous productive intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:DNX2","text":"Complex wholes cannot function as irreducible primitive contents independent of simple perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:DNX3","text":"Causal symmetry between impressions and ideas cannot hold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:DNX4","text":"Ideas cannot normally compensate for the total absence of the faculties required to produce their corresponding impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:DNX5","text":"A singular counterexample cannot be permitted the logical force ordinarily sufficient to defeat an unrestricted universal proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SNX1","text":"Foreclosed possibility: the mind as an original source of primitive simple representational content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SNX2","text":"Quarantined possibility: relations among acquired perceptions constitute a general power for synthesizing genuinely new simple qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:SNX3","text":"Unavailable foundation: an exceptionless demonstration of the copy principle independent of introspective induction and challenge to produce a counterexample.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS1","text":"Mental perception → force-and-liveliness partition → impression / idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS2","text":"Each perception class → separability test → simple / complex.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS3","text":"Initial universal resemblance → complex counterinstances → restriction of exact correspondence to simple perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS4","text":"Simple correspondence + constant conjunction → dependence; first-appearance priority → impressions cause ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS5","text":"Obstructed impression-faculty → absent impression + absent corresponding idea → confirmation of derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS6","text":"Idea → secondary idea → recursive copying without reversal of ultimate impression-origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:FGS7","text":"Missing-shade counterinstance → concession → singularity filter → maxim retained → innateness dispute translated into perceptual genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:AF1","text":"Undifferentiated perceptions → impression–idea distinction → simple–complex distinction → failure of universal complex copying → exact simple correspondence → causal dependence → impression priority → derivation maxim → anomaly containment → anti-innatist reframing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ME1","text":"Mental contents become classifiable along two independent axes: vivacity and compositional separability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ME2","text":"Feeling and thinking become different intensities of corresponding perceptual content rather than wholly different kinds of content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ME3","text":"Novel complex imagination is separated from the production of novel primitive content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ME4","text":"Sensory or affective deprivation becomes a genealogical test for the availability of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ME5","text":"Innateness is reclassified from an obscure metaphysical status into a question of perceptual precedence and derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ALT1","text":"Surface: perceptions are classified as impressions or ideas and as simple or complex.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ALT2","text":"Structural: simple correspondence, conjunction, and temporal priority produce a causal-copy model of mental content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: the vocabulary of innate ideas is displaced by an empirically testable genealogy of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: mental content is modeled as a succession of perceptions whose causal status depends on order of appearance, while corresponding thought and feeling differ primarily by degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the taxonomy, causal derivation, qualification, and anti-innatist reframing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT2","text":"The criteria for simplicity, exact correspondence, and measurable force or liveliness remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent as a general empirical genealogy but not as an exceptionless universal system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT4","text":"The argument is strongest where temporal priority and deprivation converge on the same direction of dependence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT5","text":"The causal inference compresses dependence into constant conjunction and succession before causation itself has been examined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT6","text":"The missing-shade case directly conflicts with the unrestricted wording of the first principle and is managed by rarity rather than resolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOO:MRT7","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s real movement only if the missing-shade anomaly and the deferred theory of causation remain explicit structural fault lines.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOO:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 02","title":"Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas","context":"Chapter 02","original_title":"Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas","original_context":"Chapter 02"},"original_title":"Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. II. Division Of The Subject","order":3,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIDS"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIDS@R001","sha256":"5ca2887122328300e80be704688f596ced4d0676c1c4c90d6cfea047dcab0d9f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS1","text":"The argument is classificatory, genealogical, and methodological.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS2","text":"The problem is to determine the proper order for examining impressions and ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS3","text":"The general priority of simple impressions over correspondent ideas initially requires impressions-first inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS4","text":"The division of impressions into sensation and reflection replaces undifferentiated priority with a stratified genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS5","text":"The operative sequence is sensation → idea-copy → reflective impression → reflective idea → possible recurrence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AS6","text":"Because sensations are disciplinarily deferred and reflective impressions mostly arise from ideas, the inquiry must begin with ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A1","text":"Simple impressions generally precede their correspondent ideas, notwithstanding rare exceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A2","text":"Impressions divide into impressions of sensation and impressions of reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A3","text":"Impressions of sensation arise originally in the soul from unknown causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A4","text":"An idea is a mental copy that remains after its originating impression ceases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A5","text":"Returned ideas of pleasure and pain produce desire, aversion, hope, and fear as impressions of reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A6","text":"Memory and imagination copy impressions of reflection into correspondent ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A7","text":"Resulting ideas may generate further impressions and ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A8","text":"An impression of reflection is posterior to and derived from sensation while antecedent to its own correspondent idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A9","text":"Passions, desires, and emotions are the principal objects of the present inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A10","text":"Impressions of reflection arise mostly from ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A11","text":"Examination of sensations belongs to anatomy and natural philosophy rather than the present moral inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:A12","text":"Method requires the operative antecedents of the focal object to be examined before that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX1","text":"Ideas are not ordinarily prior to their correspondent simple impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX2","text":"The original causes of sensory impressions are not known.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX3","text":"Impressions of reflection are not original sensory occurrences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX4","text":"Reflective impressions are not prior to the sensations from which they derive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX5","text":"Ideas are not causally inert copies; returned ideas can produce new impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX6","text":"The present inquiry is not an anatomical or natural-philosophical investigation of sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:NX7","text":"General impression-priority does not require every kind of impression to precede ideas in the order of exposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D1","text":"Impressions-first inquiry is the default method when simple impressions and their correspondent ideas are considered without further division.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A1","CH_THN_SIDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D2","text":"Priority cannot be assigned uniformly to impressions as an undifferentiated class.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A2","CH_THN_SIDS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D3","text":"Ideas mediate the derivation of reflective impressions from sensory impressions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A4","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D4","text":"The copy relation can enter a recursive production sequence rather than terminate in a single idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A6","CH_THN_SIDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D5","text":"Sensations can be deferred without abandoning the principal objects of the inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A9","CH_THN_SIDS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:D6","text":"Ideas must be examined before the reflective impressions principally under investigation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A9","CH_THN_SIDS:A10","CH_THN_SIDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SD1","text":"Ideas are derivative relative to sensation but productive relative to reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SD2","text":"Temporal priority is relational: each item may be posterior to one stratum while anterior to another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SD3","text":"The methodological reversal changes the relevant reference class rather than contradicting the priority of simple impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SD4","text":"The perception-chain is recursively extensible but asymmetrically grounded in sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SD5","text":"Moral inquiry begins from an intermediate representational mechanism while leaving the ultimate sensory origin unexplained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:DNX1","text":"Explanation of reflective life must not require prior resolution of the unknown causes of sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:DNX2","text":"The principal reflective impressions cannot be predominantly independent of antecedent ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:DNX3","text":"Chronological firstness alone cannot determine method once the object and disciplinary scope are restricted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:DNX4","text":"Rare exceptions must not invalidate the general precedence of simple impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:DNX5","text":"Recursive production must not be mistaken for a self-originating circle detached from sensation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SNX1","text":"A complete account of the first cause of perception is unavailable within the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SNX2","text":"A purely immediate and representation-free account of passion is foreclosed as the primary moral model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SNX3","text":"A single invariant order governing every relation between impressions and ideas is inadmissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:SNX4","text":"Exceptionless closure is withheld by the qualifiers governing priority, derivation, and recurrence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS1","text":"General precedence: simple impression → correspondent idea → default impressions-first method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS2","text":"Class division: impression → sensation or reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS3","text":"Genetic sequence: unknown cause → sensory impression → idea-copy → returned pleasure-or-pain idea → reflective impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS4","text":"Recursive extension: reflective impression → memory-or-imagination copy → reflective idea → possible further impression or idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS5","text":"Scope filter: sensation → anatomy and natural philosophy; reflection → present moral inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:FGS6","text":"Local methodological output: ideas as antecedents of reflection → ideas-first exposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:AF1","text":"General impression-priority → division of impressions → stratified genealogy → relative-priority correction → disciplinary exclusion of sensation → concentration on reflection → ideas-first method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ME1","text":"“Impression” is reclassified from a uniform category into kinds distinguished by origin and dependency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ME2","text":"“Idea” is reclassified as both a copy of an antecedent impression and a cause of subsequent reflective impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ME3","text":"Priority becomes indexed to a specific correspondence and genealogical stratum rather than assigned absolutely to a whole class.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ME4","text":"Order of exposition is detached from absolute origin and governed by the dependencies of the selected object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ALT1","text":"Surface: impressions are divided, and ideas are selected as the starting point of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ALT2","text":"Structural: copying and return mediate a recursive sequence between sensation, idea, reflection, and further ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: disciplinary jurisdiction converts the natural impressions-first order into an ideas-first method.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: perception has an unknown sensory origin, layered temporal precedence, and recursively productive mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the classification, genealogy, and methodological reversal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT2","text":"The causes of sensation and the rare exceptions to impression-priority remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT3","text":"The mechanism by which an idea “returns” and produces reflection is asserted rather than explained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT4","text":"The structure is coherent if genetic order, correspondent order, and expository order remain distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT5","text":"The strongest inferential joint is the derivation of ideas-first inquiry from the idea-dependence of the focal reflective impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT6","text":"The principal fault line is that sensation supplies the indispensable foundation while being excluded from present analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT7","text":"The argument depends on compressed qualifiers—rarely, mostly, and perhaps—that prevent universal closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIDS:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if the final reversal is treated as local and methodological rather than as a denial of sensory precedence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIDS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 03","title":"Sect. II. Division Of The Subject","context":"Chapter 03","original_title":"Sect. II. Division Of The Subject","original_context":"Chapter 03"},"original_title":"Sect. II. Division Of The Subject","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination","order":4,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIIM"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIIM@R001","sha256":"c77c75966dc93d81fc13f9073b99a4625607eabe71fdfa1d7914927bd14c330f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS1","text":"The problem is to distinguish memory from imagination when both consist of impressions returning as ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS2","text":"The argument is empirical-taxonomic and reconstructive: experienced differences among ideas are assigned to distinct faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS3","text":"Both faculties remain subject to a common antecedent-impression constraint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS4","text":"The first differential is vivacity: memory retains considerable force, whereas imagination loses it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS5","text":"The second differential is relational fidelity: memory conserves original form and order, whereas imagination can transpose them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS6","text":"Imaginative liberty is generated from copy-dependence combined with distinction, separability, and simple-complex composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AS7","text":"The movement is recurrence → common origin → force distinction → order distinction → combinatorial explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A1","text":"A previously present impression can later reappear in the mind as an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A2","text":"Every idea is copied from a corresponding impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A3","text":"Memory repeats an impression as an idea retaining a considerable degree of its original vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A4","text":"Imagination repeats an impression as an idea after its original vivacity has been lost.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A5","text":"Proper memory preserves the original form, order, and position of its objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A6","text":"A departure in recollection from the original arrangement arises from defect or imperfection in memory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A7","text":"Imagination is free to transpose and change copied ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A8","text":"No two impressions are perfectly inseparable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A9","text":"Any difference perceived by imagination among ideas permits their separation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:A10","text":"Ideas are divided into simple and complex forms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:NX1","text":"Neither memory nor imagination can supply an idea without a prior corresponding impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:NX2","text":"Memory is not free to alter the original relations among remembered objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:NX3","text":"Imagination is not bound to reproduce the original conjunction or order of impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:NX4","text":"Imaginative freedom is not the creation of unprecedented simple ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:NX5","text":"An altered order of exposition is not an altered remembered order when the original positions remain acknowledged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D1","text":"Memory and imagination differ by mode of recurrence rather than by the origin of their contents. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A1 + CH_THN_SIIM:A2 + CH_THN_SIIM:A3 + CH_THN_SIIM:A4)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D2","text":"Memory ideas are more forceful, lively, distinct, and stable than imagination ideas. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A3 + CH_THN_SIIM:A4)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D3","text":"Memory’s characteristic work is preservation of relational order and position rather than preservation of simple contents as such. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A2 + CH_THN_SIIM:A5 + CH_THN_SIIM:A10)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D4","text":"Deliberate narrative reordering remains compatible with memory when due positions are retained. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A5 + CH_THN_SIIM:A6)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D5","text":"Imagination can form unexperienced complexes by separating and rearranging impression-derived components. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A2 + CH_THN_SIIM:A7 + CH_THN_SIIM:A9 + CH_THN_SIIM:A10)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:D6","text":"Perceived difference becomes compositional liberty because distinguishable components are separable rather than necessarily conjoined. (from Axioms CH_THN_SIIM:A7 + CH_THN_SIIM:A8 + CH_THN_SIIM:A9)","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SD1","text":"Mental recurrence is organized along two axes—vivacity and relational fidelity—rather than by differences in elemental source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","text":"Memory conserves experienced temporal and positional order, while imagination releases copied contents from that order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","text":"Imagination is dependent at the level of elements but free at the level of arrangement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SD4","text":"The faculty boundary is maintained asymmetrically: mnemonic disorder counts as failure, while imaginative disorder counts as successful liberty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:DNX1","text":"Copy-dependence is not relational determinism; derivation from impressions does not require preservation of their conjunctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:DNX2","text":"A component idea’s identity is not constituted by the complex whole in which its impression originally appeared.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:DNX3","text":"Distinguishability cannot coexist with an unbreakable necessary union among ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:DNX4","text":"Mnemonic variation cannot count as an equally proper mode of memory without dissolving the fidelity criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","text":"Absolute mental spontaneity is unavailable: productivity begins only after impression-given materials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SNX2","text":"Necessary continuity between experienced world-order and thinkable world-order is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SNX3","text":"Mixed cases in which vivacity and order-fidelity point toward different faculties remain unformalized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:SNX4","text":"Independent access to the original arrangement by which mnemonic fidelity is judged is absent from the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS1","text":"Present impression → later recurrence as corresponding idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS2","text":"Recurrence with considerable retained vivacity → memory → force, distinctness, and relative stability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS3","text":"Recurrence after loss of original vivacity → imagination → faintness and instability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS4","text":"Preservation of original form, order, and position → proper memory; altered remembered order → mnemonic defect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS5","text":"Perceived difference + absence of perfect inseparability → separable idea-components.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS6","text":"Separable components + simple-complex composition → transposition and changed conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:FGS7","text":"Copy-fixed elements + variable arrangement → imaginative fiction without novel elemental content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:AF1","text":"Experienced return of impressions as ideas → bifurcation by vivacity → common copy constraint → bifurcation by fidelity to original order → memory as positional conservation → imagination as transposition → separability as the ground of fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ME1","text":"Ideas are reclassified from a homogeneous derivative class into faculties differentiated by force and operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ME2","text":"Common provenance is separated from divergent treatment: identical source materials can support conservation or recombination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ME3","text":"Memory is reclassified as an archive of relations and positions rather than merely a repository of simple ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ME4","text":"Imaginative liberty is localized in arrangement rather than in the origination of content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ME5","text":"Unnatural fictional objects become evidence of recombination rather than exceptions to impression-origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ALT1","text":"Surface: Memory and imagination are distinguished by vivacity and fidelity to original arrangement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ALT2","text":"Structural: The mind applies conservation or separation-recombination to common impression-derived materials.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The faculty distinction is secured by coding mnemonic variation as defect and fictional anomaly as lawful recombination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Ideas are posterior copies; memory preserves experienced succession and position, while imagination detaches contents from that succession without escaping derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the two faculties, their dual criteria, and the bounded liberty of imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT2","text":"The vivacity threshold remains underdetermined; memory is both an idea and intermediate between impression and fully diminished idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT3","text":"The relation between vivacity and order-fidelity is unspecified, leaving mixed cases unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT4","text":"The structure is coherent if copy-dependence governs elements while separability governs arrangements.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT5","text":"The tightest inference is the derivation of novel complexes from copied but rearrangeable components.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT6","text":"The memory principle depends on compressed appeal to common phenomena rather than an independent derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT7","text":"The move from perceived difference and non-inseparability to easy separation is asserted without specifying limits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT8","text":"Mnemonic fidelity lacks an independent standard for recovering original order, while the classification of deviations as defects partially insulates the principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIIM:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the text’s movement from experiential recurrence through dual differentiation to combinatorial closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIIM:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 04","title":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination","context":"Chapter 04","original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination","original_context":"Chapter 04"},"original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas","order":5,"parents":["RAW_THN_SICA"],"revision":"CH_THN_SICA@R001","sha256":"d15603460219e0b659086e422a41830bed5d13c1445bdcf3cabe89df7eea21e9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS1","text":"The central problem is how freely separable and recombinable ideas nevertheless form regular sequences and complexes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS2","text":"Uniformity requires universal associative principles that guide imagination without determining it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS3","text":"The argument is empirical, reconstructive, taxonomic, and methodologically anti-speculative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS4","text":"Free combination → inadequacy of chance → non-necessary associative force → three relational operators.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS5","text":"Direct association expands into attenuated relational chains, with causation extending from production to power and social influence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AS6","text":"Inquiry terminates at original qualities of human nature and redirects itself from unknown causes to complex ideational effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A1","text":"Every simple idea is separable and recombinable by imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A2","text":"The regular recurrence of simple ideas in complex formations requires a universal bond rather than chance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A3","text":"Association is a gentle prevailing tendency that introduces one idea through another without compelling the transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A4","text":"Resemblance, contiguity in time or place, and cause and effect are the three associative qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A5","text":"Resemblance alone is sufficient to facilitate an imaginative transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A6","text":"Custom transfers the regular contiguity of sensory succession into the imagination’s method of transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A7","text":"Cause and effect produce the strongest and most extensive associative relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A8","text":"Association propagates through relational intermediaries while weakening at each remove.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A9","text":"Causal association includes the production of an object’s existence, action, or motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A10","text":"A power to produce action establishes causal relation because its exertion through will remains possible and often probable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A11","text":"Relations of interest, duty, government, and subordination arise from powers by which persons influence one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A12","text":"Associative cohesion supplies within imagination the role played by inseparable connexion within memory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A13","text":"The causes of associative attraction are mostly unknown and terminate here in original qualities of human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A14","text":"Once a principle is experimentally established, inquiry should examine its effects rather than pursue obscure ultimate causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A15","text":"Complex ideas, as the common objects of thought and reasoning, generally arise from associative union among simple ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:A16","text":"Complex ideas divide into relations, modes, and substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX1","text":"Association is not an inseparable or necessary connexion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX2","text":"Association is not a condition without which imagination becomes unable to join ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX3","text":"Regular ideational composition is not attributable to chance alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX4","text":"Associative connexion is not confined to objects standing in an immediate relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX5","text":"Causation is not restricted to actual production or to the production of existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX6","text":"The theory does not claim to explain the ultimate causes of associative principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:NX7","text":"Philosophical inquiry should not continue into obscure and uncertain speculation after experimental sufficiency is reached.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D1","text":"Mental order is compatible with combinatorial freedom because association governs probabilistically rather than necessarily.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D2","text":"Correspondence among languages follows from shared associative tendencies toward similar complex formations.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D3","text":"Relational proximity is inversely proportional to the number of intermediaries, making kinship distance representable as causal-chain length.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SICA:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D4","text":"Social offices and hierarchies become associatively causal wherever positional power can be converted into another person’s action.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D5","text":"Association is admissible as an experimentally supported original principle even when its underlying mechanism remains unknown.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:D6","text":"The investigation must proceed from the associative mechanism to the classes of complex ideas it generates.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SICA:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SD1","text":"The imagination is neither mechanically determined nor randomly discontinuous; it is free under recurrent natural tendencies.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SD2","text":"The ideational field forms an attenuating network in which relation type and relational distance jointly regulate recall.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A4","CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SD3","text":"Causation becomes a bridge between natural production, counterfactual capacity, and institutional authority.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SD4","text":"A science of mind can map the production of complex thought through observable associative effects without possessing an ultimate metaphysics of mental connexion.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A12","CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SICA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX1","text":"Radical cross-contextual idiosyncrasy in the succession of ideas must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX2","text":"The opposition between necessary determination and pure chance must be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX3","text":"No independent fourth principle may be required to explain ordinary associative transitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX4","text":"Relational force cannot remain unchanged across indefinitely extended mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX5","text":"Social interest and duty cannot require an irreducibly normative bond wholly outside causal power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:DNX6","text":"Adequate empirical explanation cannot depend upon access to the ultimate mechanism of every observed principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SNX1","text":"The system is organized around the absence of necessary mental connexion, whose functional place is occupied by gentle attraction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SNX2","text":"The origin of associative attraction remains unavailable; “original qualities of human nature” names the terminal explanatory absence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SNX3","text":"The possibility of further primitive associative sources is excluded by the triadic taxonomy without being independently refuted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:SNX4","text":"The nature of causation remains deferred even while causation carries the greatest explanatory scope within the chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS1","text":"Free separability and recombination + recurrent complex formations → demand for a non-random ordering principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS2","text":"Ordering without compulsion → gentle associative force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS3","text":"Associative force → resemblance / contiguity / cause and effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS4","text":"Immediate relation + intermediary relations → extended but progressively weakened associative chains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS5","text":"Causal production → action and existence → power to produce → social influence and hierarchy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS6","text":"Observable effects + unknown ultimate causes → methodological arrest at original human qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:FGS7","text":"Associative cohesion → complex ideas → relations / modes / substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:AF1","text":"Freedom of combination → recurrent uniformity → insufficiency of chance → gentle universal association → triadic operators → mediated attenuation → causal expansion into power → methodological limit → taxonomy of complex effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME1","text":"Imagination is reclassified from an arbitrary combinatorial faculty into a free faculty regulated by natural tendencies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME2","text":"Necessary connexion and random succession cease to exhaust the possible models of mental order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME3","text":"Ideational connexion becomes graded and transmissible rather than merely present or absent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME4","text":"Causation expands from actual event-production to latent power and institutional command.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME5","text":"Complex ideas become generated effects of associative operations rather than primitive mental units.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ME6","text":"Philosophical explanation is redirected from inaccessible origins toward experimentally observable effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ALT1","text":"Surface: One idea regularly recalls another, and simple ideas regularly form complexes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ALT2","text":"Structural: Three relational operators organize transitions through direct and attenuated chains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: The inquiry expands causation into social power while restricting philosophy to experimentally warranted effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Human nature supplies universal but non-necessary tendencies, while sensory succession and custom sediment spatial-temporal order into imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s central account of regularity within a freely combinatorial imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT2","text":"The core structure is coherent insofar as gentle tendency mediates between necessity and chance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT3","text":"The exhaustiveness and universality of the three associative relations remain underdetermined rather than demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT4","text":"Causation is assigned maximal explanatory force before its own nature is examined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT5","text":"Contiguity is explained through acquired custom, while associative causes are classified as original qualities; the relation between acquisition and originality remains unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT6","text":"The argument is strongest in its graded model of mediated association and its separation of regularity from necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT7","text":"Its extension into social relations depends on compressing interest, duty, authority, and obedience into causal power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SICA:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the movement from combinatorial freedom through associative regulation to complex ideational effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SICA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 05","title":"Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas","context":"Chapter 05","original_title":"Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas","original_context":"Chapter 05"},"original_title":"Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time","order":6,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIOQ"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIOQ@R001","sha256":"e4f6d92f6c618661d4df2f9202bcdcbf3378912b38990881cee3e7ed30dae00e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS1","text":"Problem — Determine the impression-origin and internal composition of the ideas of space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS2","text":"Thesis — Space and time are not distinct impressions but ordered manners in which sensible or perceptual contents appear.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3","text":"Argument type — Empiricist genealogy joined to eliminative, separability, and compositional tests.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS4","text":"Spatial movement — Sensible points → disposition → extension → abstraction across variable sensible qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS5","text":"Temporal movement — Successive perceptions → noncoexistent order → duration → exclusion of time without change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AS6","text":"Closure — Indivisible spatial and temporal components remain conceivable only when filled by sensible quality or real existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","text":"Every idea is preceded by a correspondent impression that it copies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A2","text":"Only sight and touch supply impressions from which extension can be derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","text":"An extended sensible impression consists of indivisible colored or tangible points disposed in a determinate order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","text":"Every abstract idea is a particular idea considered under a shared aspect and attached to a general term.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","text":"The idea of time originates in the succession of perceptions of every kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","text":"Time is the manner in which successive perceptions appear, not an additional impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","text":"The parts of duration are noncoexistent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A8","text":"The parts of extension are coexistent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A9","text":"Whatever is different is distinguishable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A10","text":"Whatever is distinguishable is separable in idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A11","text":"What is not distinguishable cannot be separated in idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","text":"An idea can properly represent only the objects or impressions from which it derives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A13","text":"The compound ideas of extension and duration resolve into simple indivisible component ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","text":"A real compound idea cannot be composed of nonentities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:A15","text":"An indivisible spatial point is conceivable only through retained color or tangibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX1","text":"The idea of extension is not derived from passions, emotions, desires, or aversions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX2","text":"Extension is not an impression additional to colored or tangible points and their disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX3","text":"An abstract idea is not an indeterminate or contentless universal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX4","text":"Time cannot appear to the mind alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX5","text":"A steady and unchangeable object cannot originate the idea of time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX6","text":"Time is neither an additional sensation nor an additional impression of reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX7","text":"Duration cannot properly be predicated of an unchangeable object without fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX8","text":"A simple spatial point is neither itself extended nor a nonentity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:NX9","text":"A point deprived of every visible or tangible quality is unavailable to thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D1","text":"The idea of extension copies sensible points together with their relative disposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D2","text":"Abstract extension suppresses variable sensible peculiarities only while retaining their shared disposition in a particular sensible representation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D3","text":"One abstract idea of extension can represent both visible and tangible configurations because their parts share a disposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D4","text":"Abstract time ranges over sensations, reflections, impressions, and ideas while remaining represented by a determinate succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D5","text":"Without successive perceptions there is no notion of time, even where objects undergo real succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D6","text":"Apparent duration varies with the rate and discriminability of successive perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D7","text":"Coexistence and noncoexistence provide the constitutive distinction between extension and duration.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D8","text":"Time may be abstracted from particular successive objects but cannot be separated from some succession of objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","text":"Applying duration to an unchangeable object exceeds the impression from which duration derives and is therefore fictional.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D10","text":"The indivisible component of extension must be qualitatively sensible rather than extended or nonexistent.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A8","CH_THN_SIOQ:A13","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D11","text":"No idea of space or extension remains when all visible and tangible content is removed.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:D12","text":"An indivisible moment must be filled by a real object or existence whose succession makes duration conceivable.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A13","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1","text":"Space and time are higher-order arrangements of content rather than additional contents: space orders coexistent sensible points, while time orders successive existences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SD2","text":"Abstract universality is representational rather than imagistic: a determinate particular represents heterogeneous cases through selective regard and general denomination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SD3","text":"Experienced temporality is constrained by the mind’s resolution and rate of perception rather than determined immediately by objective succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SD4","text":"Pure spatial and temporal minima are conceptually unavailable because every minimum must retain sensible quality or existential filling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5","text":"The genealogy of an idea functions as a limit on its legitimate application: predication cannot properly outrun the originating impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX1","text":"Space and time cannot be innate or independently given ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX2","text":"Repeated contemplation cannot generate a new simple idea unless a new original impression occurs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX3","text":"Abstraction cannot remove every particular determination while leaving a positive idea intact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX4","text":"A manner of appearance cannot be reified as an additional constituent of what appears.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX5","text":"Common linguistic application cannot establish conceptual legitimacy independently of impression-origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX6","text":"Objective occurrence alone cannot furnish an idea when the corresponding order is not perceivable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX1","text":"Pure empty spatiality is the absent object around which the spatial argument is organized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0042","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2","text":"Bare duration without successive existence is excluded from possible conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX3","text":"Absolute time capable of measuring changeless being is foreclosed except as fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX4","text":"Direct cognitive access to space or time independently of perceptual content is unavailable to the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS1","text":"Copy principle → impression audit → elimination of reflective sources → restriction to sight and touch.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS2","text":"Sensible minima → relative disposition → compound extension → spatial idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS3","text":"Recurrent disposition across variable qualities → selective omission → particular representative → abstract space.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS4","text":"Heterogeneous perceptions → perceivable succession → noncoexistent order → temporal idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS5","text":"Absence of distinct temporal impression → non-distinguishability → non-separability from succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS6","text":"Genetic restriction → absence of temporal source in changeless objects → fictional application of duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS7","text":"Compound decomposition → indivisible component → exclusion of nonentity → sensible or existential filling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:AF1","text":"Copy principle → source elimination → spatial derivation → abstraction mechanism → temporal derivation → separability test → exclusion of changeless duration → indivisible-component test → content-filled space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME1","text":"Space is reclassified from an independent receptacle into the disposition of visible or tangible minima.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME2","text":"Time is reclassified from an independent medium into the perceivable succession of changing contents.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME3","text":"Abstraction is reclassified from production of a contentless universal into selective use of a particular idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME4","text":"Coexistence and noncoexistence replace independent substances as the primary distinction between space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME5","text":"Changeless duration and qualityless spatial points are relocated from proper ideas to fiction or nonconception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ME6","text":"Questions concerning space and time are made answerable through impression-tracing rather than through unrestricted metaphysical postulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0045","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT1","text":"Surface — The immediate inquiry identifies the impressions and component qualities represented by ideas of extension and duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT2","text":"Structural — Copying, abstraction, separability, and decomposition govern the formation and limits of both ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Conceptual disputes are adjudicated by tracing ideas to impressions and rejecting applications that exceed that genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Spatiality and temporality become orders of existent content: coexistence for sensible points and succession for changing objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the parallel genealogies of extension and duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT2","text":"The claim that the senses present only sensible points and their disposition is asserted phenomenologically rather than independently demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT3","text":"The terminal argument depends on the prior establishment of indivisible component ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT4","text":"Internal coherence is strongest where the copy principle, absence of a distinct impression, and inseparability test converge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT5","text":"The exact-copy principle strains against abstraction: abstract extension omits determinate color while every spatial representative must retain color or tangibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT6","text":"Temporal abstraction separates time from particular perceptions while denying its separation from some perceptions; the type-token mechanism remains compressed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT7","text":"The denial of duration to changeless objects depends on treating genetic origin as a binding semantic limit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT8","text":"The argument establishes conditions of conception more securely than conditions of independent existence; its closing language partially compresses that distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0045","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the chapter’s movement only if spatial disposition and temporal succession remain parallel but nonidentical structures.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIOQ:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 06","title":"Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time","context":"Chapter 06","original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time","original_context":"Chapter 06"},"original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence","order":7,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVIE"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVIE@R001","sha256":"59f7a851dd68eb552883d6142b5a1af280ac54a7b6e886514884b370f7544f6f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS1","text":"Problem: determine the origin and content of the ideas of existence and external existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS2","text":"Argument type: empiricist genealogy conducted through an exhaustive dilemma, elimination, and conceptual boundary test.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS3","text":"The impression-copy principle and the universal conception of perceptions as existent force existence to be either a distinct accompanying perception or identical with the object conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS4","text":"The absence of a distinct impression inseparably accompanying every perception eliminates the first alternative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS5","text":"Existence is identical with the idea of the object and adds nothing to its conceived content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS6","text":"Because only perceptions are present to the mind, no positive idea can represent an existence specifically different from perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AS7","text":"Impression-origin test → exhaustive dilemma → elimination of distinct existence-content → identity thesis → perceptual closure → relative conception of externality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A1","text":"Every idea arises from a similar antecedent impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A2","text":"Every impression or idea available to consciousness or memory is conceived as existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A3","text":"No distinct impression is inseparably conjoined with every perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A4","text":"The idea of existence is identical with the idea of the object conceived as existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A5","text":"A distinction without real difference requires one simple idea to bear different resemblance-relations to other ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A6","text":"Nothing is present to the mind except perceptions: impressions and ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A7","text":"External objects are known only through the perceptions they occasion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A8","text":"An external object supposed specifically different from perceptions can be conceived only relatively, without comprehension of its intrinsic nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:A9","text":"External objects are ordinarily differentiated from perceptions by attributed relations, connections, and durations rather than by a specifically different content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX1","text":"Existence is not derived from a distinct impression attached to every perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX2","text":"Existence is not an additional quality inserted into an object-idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX3","text":"Reflecting on an object simply is not distinct from reflecting on it as existent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX4","text":"Existence cannot be rescued as a distinction without difference, because presented objects supply no differential resemblance with respect to existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX5","text":"Loving, hating, thinking, feeling, and seeing are not modes of mental presence beyond perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX6","text":"No specifically extra-perceptual kind of existence can be positively represented.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX7","text":"Directing attention outward does not move conception beyond the universe of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:NX8","text":"Relative reference to external objects is not comprehension of those objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D1","text":"Conscious awareness supplies the maximal idea and assurance of being available within the argument.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D2","text":"The idea of existence must arise either from a distinct impression universally conjoined with perception or from the perception itself.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A1","CH_THN_SVIE:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D3","text":"The distinct-impression alternative fails, leaving identity between existence and the conceived object.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A1","CH_THN_SVIE:A2","CH_THN_SVIE:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D4","text":"Conjoining existence with an object-idea produces no conceptual enlargement.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D5","text":"No contrast class permits existence to function as a distinction of reason.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A2","CH_THN_SVIE:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D6","text":"Every positive conception remains composed of perceptual content already available within the mind.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A1","CH_THN_SVIE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D7","text":"Knowledge of external objects is necessarily mediated and cannot become direct presence of those objects to the mind.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A6","CH_THN_SVIE:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:D8","text":"Externality is available either as an uncomprehended relative correlate or as a redistribution of relations, connections, and durations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVIE:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SD1","text":"Being functions at the conceptual level as the universal status of presentation rather than as discriminating content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SD2","text":"Conceivability guarantees existence-as-conceived but supplies no additional content establishing independent external actuality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SD3","text":"Positive ontology is bounded by the inventory of possible perceptions; whatever exceeds that inventory cannot be conceived according to its specific nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SD4","text":"External realism remains thinkable only through mediation, relational attribution, or an intrinsically unfilled correlate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX1","text":"An intellectual idea of being independent of impressions is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX2","text":"Existence cannot be both universally attached to perceptions and positively distinguishable from them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX3","text":"Presented objects cannot differ in kind merely through possession or absence of conceived existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX4","text":"Attention and imagination cannot generate access to a domain specifically heterogeneous to perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX5","text":"Merely positing a specifically different external object cannot generate positive content for that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:DNX6","text":"The requirement that relative reference comprehend its relatum is excluded by the permitted conception of external existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","text":"The positive essence of mind-independent being is the governing absence: external existence is retained only where intrinsic comprehension is withdrawn.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SNX2","text":"No bare existential marker separates an imagined object from an independently existing object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SNX3","text":"No representational passage connects the perceptual universe to a positively conceived heterogeneous realm.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SNX4","text":"The external source said to occasion perceptions remains named through relation but unavailable as a comprehended kind of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:SNX5","text":"The intrinsic nature of external objects remains neither positively affirmable nor eliminable within the chapter’s conceptual resources.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS1","text":"Copy principle + universal existential conception → exhaustive origin dilemma.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS2","text":"Candidate universal existence-impression → test for distinctness and inseparability → failure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS3","text":"Elimination of distinct impression → identity of existence with object-conception → zero conceptual addition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS4","text":"Proposed distinction without difference → differential-resemblance test → absence of an existence contrast class → rejection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS5","text":"Perceptions alone present + ideas copied from prior perceptions → closure of positive conception within perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS6","text":"Specifically different external object posited → no qualitative comprehension → relative idea only.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:FGS7","text":"Ordinary external attribution → altered relations, connections, and durations → no specifically different object-content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:AF1","text":"Question of existence-content → impression-origin dilemma → elimination of a distinct existence-impression → identity and non-addition → rejection of distinction by resemblance → perception-only horizon → impossibility of specifically external conception → relative or relational externality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ME1","text":"Existence is reclassified from a detachable predicate or quality into identity with conceived content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ME2","text":"Existential predication loses the power to enlarge an idea or distinguish kinds of conceived objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ME3","text":"The internal/external distinction is displaced from specific qualitative difference to mediation and relational organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ME4","text":"The universe of imagination becomes the boundary of all positive conceptual content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ME5","text":"External-object discourse divides into comprehensible relations and an incomprehensible related term.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ALT1","text":"Surface: the chapter determines what the ideas of existence and external existence contain and from which impressions they derive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ALT2","text":"Structural: the copy principle, separability test, and perception-only presence rule regulate admissible concepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: metaphysical claims are converted into demands for an originating impression, with unsupported positive content eliminated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: being adds no further essence to conceived objects, while external objects are ordinarily differentiated through relations, connections, and durations rather than through a separately conceived ontological kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SVIE:A1 through CH_THN_SVIE:A9 are sufficient to reconstruct both the existence argument and its extension to external existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT2","text":"The legitimacy of a relative idea whose relatum cannot be positively conceived remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent if the copy principle, perceptual presence rule, and separability test are granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT4","text":"The strongest operation is the elimination of a distinct existence-impression through the demand for a universal inseparable source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT5","text":"The argument compresses the difference between existence-as-conceived and independent actuality into an unresolved shift in the use of “existent.”","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT6","text":"The claim that external objects occasion perceptions invokes a causal relation to the very term whose specific nature cannot be conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT7","text":"Because existence adds nothing to an idea, the criterion distinguishing fiction from external actuality is unavailable within this chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIE:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement from conceptual genealogy through eliminative identity to the negative boundary of external conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIE:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 07","title":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence","context":"Chapter 07","original_title":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence","original_context":"Chapter 07"},"original_title":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief","order":8,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVNI"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVNI@R001","sha256":"b62fe1e03a05f25dd82d109c2fca7a5cef6a69805504a3bf535369760e46913c","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS1","text":"Central problem: belief contains an object-idea yet exceeds mere conception; its surplus must be identified without adding representational content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS2","text":"Scope: matter-of-fact assent, whose contrary remains conceivable, rather than demonstrative determination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS3","text":"Elimination: neither existence nor belief contributes a distinct idea or changes the composition of the object-idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS4","text":"Argument type: diagnostic elimination → phenomenological reconstruction → taxonomic revision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS5","text":"Core thesis: belief is an idea conceived with superior force, vivacity, firmness, or steadiness through relation to a present impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AS6","text":"Direction: conception–belief gap → content invariance → modal difference → intensive definition → collapse of the scholastic taxonomy → experiential confirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A1","text":"The idea of an object is necessary but insufficient for belief in that object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A2","text":"Conceiving an object as existent adds nothing to the simple conception of the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A3","text":"Believing an object to exist adds no idea and changes no part of the object-idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A4","text":"Both sides of a question concerning matter of fact remain intelligibly conceivable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A5","text":"Intuition and demonstration determine conception by rendering the contrary unintelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A6","text":"Believer and disbeliever can understand a proposition through the same constituent ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A7","text":"Factual opinion requires a principle that fixes one conceivable alternative as the object of assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A8","text":"Because the fixing principle adds no idea, it can alter only the manner of conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A9","text":"All mental perceptions are impressions or ideas, distinguished only by degrees of force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A10","text":"Ideas copy impressions and represent their constituent parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A11","text":"An idea can vary while representing the same object only through a change in force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A12","text":"Belief is a strong, lively, and steady idea related or associated with a present impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A13","text":"Matter-of-fact inference requires a perception present to sense or memory as its foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A14","text":"The transition from a present perception to a believed idea is determined by custom or association, not reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A15","text":"Superior force gives believed ideas greater presence, weight, persistence, and influence over passion, imagination, and action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A16","text":"Conception, judgment, and reasoning resolve into different ways of conceiving objects rather than compositionally distinct acts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:A17","text":"The distinctive manner of belief is ultimately given as a feeling that philosophical vocabulary can only approximate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX1","text":"Belief is not the mere possession or comprehension of an object-idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX2","text":"Existence is not a separable idea added as a predicate to an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX3","text":"Assent does not add ideas to the proposition assented to.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX4","text":"Matter-of-fact belief is not demonstrative necessity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX5","text":"Disbelief is not an inability to understand or conceive the rejected proposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX6","text":"Judgment does not necessarily consist in uniting or separating two distinct ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX7","text":"Reasoning does not necessarily require a third idea mediating between two others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX8","text":"Imaginative recombination and circumstantial completeness cannot by themselves produce belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:NX9","text":"Belief and fiction are not distinguished by the nature, number, or order of their represented parts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D1","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A2 + CH_THN_SVNI:A3 + CH_THN_SVNI:A6: bare conception, existential conception, and existential belief can preserve identical object-content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D2","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A4 + CH_THN_SVNI:A5 + CH_THN_SVNI:A6: factual assent cannot be identified by contradiction, intelligibility, or semantic content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D3","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A7 + CH_THN_SVNI:A8 + CH_THN_SVNI:A11: the principle fixing opinion must operate through scalar intensification rather than representational modification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D4","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A2 + CH_THN_SVNI:A16: existential judgments can contain only one object-idea, invalidating the universal model of judgment as the union of two ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D5","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A13 + CH_THN_SVNI:A14 + CH_THN_SVNI:A16: causal inference can pass directly from a present perception to a correlative idea without an interposed third idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D6","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A12 + CH_THN_SVNI:A15: belief distinguishes experienced reality from fiction by cognitive weight and practical influence despite representational identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D7","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A9 + CH_THN_SVNI:A11 + CH_THN_SVNI:A12: belief is not a third species of perception but an idea intensified toward the condition of an impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:D8","text":"From CH_THN_SVNI:A12 + CH_THN_SVNI:A17: force, vivacity, solidity, firmness, and steadiness are approximate names for one felt mode rather than separate constituents of belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SD1","text":"The experienced distinction between fact and fiction is relocated from representational content to the felt intensity and stability of conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SD2","text":"Conceptual enrichment cannot convert the possible into the actual because actuality contributes no detachable representational content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SD3","text":"The architecture of understanding becomes operationally monistic: conception is the base act, differentiated by necessity, association, and intensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SD4","text":"Matter-of-fact commitment depends on present experiential anchoring and associative determination rather than insight into necessary connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SD5","text":"The epistemic authority and practical efficacy of belief arise from the same intensification that makes an idea persistent and action-governing.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX1","text":"The system excludes an independent faculty of judgment that stamps truth onto an otherwise complete idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX2","text":"The system excludes any content-internal marker by which an idea announces the actual existence of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX3","text":"The system requires that reason disclose no necessary existential connection between distinct matters of fact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX4","text":"The system excludes the possibility that imaginative detail or coherence alone can generate factual assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX5","text":"The system excludes non-intensive changes in the manner of conception that would preserve the identity of the represented object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX6","text":"The system denies irreducible boundaries between conception, judgment, and reasoning as fundamental mental operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:DNX7","text":"The system requires vivid fiction to remain distinguishable from belief when it lacks the steadiness and experiential relation characteristic of assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SNX1","text":"The foreclosed possibility is a purely conceptual passage from the idea of an object to its actual existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SNX2","text":"No intrinsic representational sign separates the actual from the merely conceivable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SNX3","text":"A self-sufficient reason capable of constructing the factual world through necessity is structurally unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SNX4","text":"Complete discursive capture of belief is unavailable; explanation terminates in a commonly recognizable but indefinable feeling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:SNX5","text":"The absent criterion is an exact threshold separating exceptionally vivid fiction from genuine belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS1","text":"Conception of object O is required for belief in O, but conception of O does not entail belief in O.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS2","text":"Content of simple conception = content of existential conception = content of existential belief; therefore belief cannot be an added idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS3","text":"Conceivable P + conceivable not-P + non-demonstrative selection of P → difference located in mode of conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS4","text":"Same represented object + permissible variation only in force and vivacity → belief as intensified and stabilized conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS5","text":"Present perception → customary or associative transition → intensified related idea → factual assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS6","text":"Intensified idea → greater felt reality → increased influence on passion and imagination → governance of action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:FGS7","text":"Conception as base operation / demonstration as necessary determination / factual judgment as conception plus belief / reasoning as transition among conceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:AF1","text":"Conception insufficient for belief → existence adds no idea → factual contraries remain conceivable → content cannot explain assent → difference relocated to manner → manner reduced to force and vivacity → belief defined through present-impression relation → judgment and reasoning reduced to modes of conception → definition confirmed by felt and practical effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME1","text":"Existence is reclassified from an added predicate into the object as conceived under a mode of assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME2","text":"Belief is reclassified from a content-producing judgment into an intensive modification of an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME3","text":"Belief and disbelief become compatible with identical semantic comprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME4","text":"The boundary between judgment and imagination is redrawn through force, firmness, and influence rather than representational capacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME5","text":"The scholastic division of conception, judgment, and reasoning collapses into variations of one conceiving operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME6","text":"Demonstrative and factual assent are separated by their determining principles: unintelligibility of the contrary versus associative vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ME7","text":"The connection between cognition and action becomes intrinsic to belief’s degree of force rather than dependent on additional content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ALT1","text":"Surface: the immediate object is the difference between merely conceiving a proposition and believing it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ALT2","text":"Structural: the distinction is generated within an impression–idea economy governed by force, vivacity, present perception, and association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: the conventional taxonomy of understanding is displaced by a unified theory of conception differentiated by modal intensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: existence has no separable idea, while belief concerning absent objects depends on their present experiential or mnemonic anchoring.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SVNI:A1–CH_THN_SVNI:A17 are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from representational invariance to the intensive definition of belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT2","text":"The eliminative argument is strongest where identical content is shown to occur under conception, disbelief, and assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT3","text":"The positive reconstruction depends on the exclusivity of the impression–idea taxonomy and the claim that same-object variation is limited to force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT4","text":"The mechanism by which a present impression communicates vivacity through association remains compressed and is deferred beyond the section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT5","text":"No exact degree or threshold distinguishing belief from exceptionally vivid imagination is supplied.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT6","text":"The claim that imagination cannot produce belief is asserted through felt difference more than independently demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT7","text":"The vocabulary of force, vivacity, firmness, solidity, and steadiness marks an acknowledged explanatory compression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT8","text":"The structure remains coherent only if intensification preserves representational identity while experiential relation preserves the distinction between belief and vivid fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVNI:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from the extracted system preserves the section’s real sequence: elimination of added content, relocation to manner, intensive definition, taxonomic revision, and phenomenological confirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVNI:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 08","title":"Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief","context":"Chapter 08","original_title":"Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief","original_context":"Chapter 08"},"original_title":"Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","order":9,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXIB"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXIB@R001","sha256":"3dd7c72030f744b31d0a2b07a97fce3a5229049f073f9550db612e6a9f0a2af7","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS1","text":"Central problem: explain how custom and habit produce belief-effects extending across reasoning, action, passion, and imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS2","text":"Core thesis: belief is a simple idea intensified toward the force and vivacity of an impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS3","text":"Belief mediates between motivationally insufficient present impressions and the intolerable activation of the will by every passing idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS4","text":"Belief, passion, and imagination reciprocally transmit vivacity and thereby reinforce one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS5","text":"Poetry and madness test the mechanism by producing impression-like vivacity without the customary basis of serious conviction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AS6","text":"Argument type and movement: reconstructive naturalistic psychology; scale objection → motivational dilemma → scalar mechanism → reciprocal confirmation → counterfeit cases → rule-governed correction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A1","text":"Pain and pleasure are the chief moving principles of human action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A2","text":"Impressions and ideas differ originally in their degrees of force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A3","text":"Differences in the effects of perceptions arise from differences in their force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A4","text":"Present impressions actuate the mind more strongly than mere ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A5","text":"Belief is almost necessary for an idea to excite the passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A6","text":"Ideas of objects believed to exist or to be forthcoming influence action and passion in the same manner as impressions, but ordinarily in a lesser degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A7","text":"Belief is a more vivid and intense conception of an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A8","text":"Serious conviction arises when customary conjunction conveys vivacity from a present impression to a related idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0041","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A9","text":"An excited passion transfers its vivacity to the idea of its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A10","text":"Forceful and vivid ideas are agreeable to the imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A11","text":"Truth and reality affect imaginative enjoyment by securing easy reception and mental acquiescence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A12","text":"Vivacity can diffuse from one idea to other ideas related to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A13","text":"Repetition and familiarity can facilitate imaginative reception without producing judgmental belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A14","text":"A vigorous imagination can vivify ideas sufficiently to procure assent and authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A15","text":"Extraordinary bodily ferment can raise loose ideas to impression-like vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A16","text":"Poetical vivacity arises from present temperament rather than customary connections among objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:A17","text":"Reflection and general rules regulate assent by discounting vivacity recognized as accidental or disconnected from reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX1","text":"Education is not a philosophically valid ground of assent, despite its power to produce assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX2","text":"The magnitude of an effect does not require an apparently substantial psychological principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX3","text":"The will is not governed exclusively by present impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX4","text":"The will is not activated by every passing idea of good or evil.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX5","text":"Belief is not additional representational content attached to an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX6","text":"Literal truth and absolute assurance are not necessary conditions of imaginative satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX7","text":"Historical anchoring within fiction is not necessarily an attempt to deceive judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX8","text":"Vivacity transmitted through accidental poetic relations does not amount to perfect assurance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX9","text":"Poetical enthusiasm is not serious conviction, even when it produces stronger apparent agitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX10","text":"Apparent mental vehemence is not a reliable measure of genuine cognitive force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:NX11","text":"Every increase in an idea’s occurrent vivacity does not require a corresponding increase in settled belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D1","text":"Selective vivification permits absent and future goods or evils to motivate action without allowing every fiction to disturb the will.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A1","CH_THN_SXIB:A4","CH_THN_SXIB:A5","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D2","text":"Because believed ideas imitate the effects of impressions, they must approach impressions in force and vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A2","CH_THN_SXIB:A3","CH_THN_SXIB:A6","CH_THN_SXIB:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D3","text":"A prevailing passion increases assent to representations suited to that passion by transferring its vivacity to them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A7","CH_THN_SXIB:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D4","text":"Belief pleases the imagination by making represented objects enter the mind forcefully and without reluctance.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A7","CH_THN_SXIB:A10","CH_THN_SXIB:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D5","text":"Familiarity can replace literal truth as a mechanism of imaginative admission.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A11","CH_THN_SXIB:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D6","text":"A believed or familiar narrative component can vivify connected fictional components without converting the whole fiction into assured belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D7","text":"Eloquence can generate assent by transferring imaginative vivacity into judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A7","CH_THN_SXIB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D8","text":"When bodily ferment gives every fiction impression-like force, truth and falsehood become operationally indistinguishable and act equally on the passions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A2","CH_THN_SXIB:A3","CH_THN_SXIB:A7","CH_THN_SXIB:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D9","text":"Poetically induced passions can entertain even when their real counterparts are painful because counterfeit belief excites the spirits without the weight of reality.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A10","CH_THN_SXIB:A16","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D10","text":"Reflection can dissipate poetical illusion by identifying its vivacity as accidental and reality-disconnected.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A16","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:D11","text":"General rules can sustain full conviction despite local reductions in felt force and can resist assent despite local increases in imaginative vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SD1","text":"Belief, causal inference, motivation, passion, aesthetic reception, persuasion, and madness operate within a common economy of distributed mental force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SD2","text":"Cognition and affect form a feedback system: belief excites passion, passion vivifies its object, and the vivified object strengthens belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SD3","text":"Belief is not psychologically binary; inert fiction, poetic counterfeit belief, probable conviction, and impression-like certainty occupy differentiated positions within one mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SD4","text":"The distinction between genuine and counterfeit conviction must be relocated from apparent intensity to causal provenance, felt character, and correction by general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SD5","text":"The mechanism that enables action toward absent realities also exposes judgment to capture by temperament, eloquence, repetition, and pathological vivification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX1","text":"Belief cannot be a sui generis rational act independent of the manner in which an idea is conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX2","text":"Representational content alone cannot determine motivational or affective efficacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX3","text":"Judgment, passion, and imagination cannot be causally sealed faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX4","text":"Truth cannot possess an intrinsic psychological mark that automatically guarantees assent or imaginative pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX5","text":"First-order vividness cannot authenticate its own epistemic legitimacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:DNX6","text":"Fiction and factual conviction cannot be separated by an impermeable boundary at the level of conception alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SNX1","text":"The foreclosed possibility is an infallible immediate criterion by which consciousness always distinguishes genuine belief from its counterfeit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SNX2","text":"The system excludes a pure rational agency insulated from custom, bodily disposition, passion, and rhetoric.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3","text":"No origin-independent warrant is supplied for the general rules that correct vivid appearances; their authority remains the terminal unformalized condition of epistemic discrimination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS1","text":"Impressions alone → no motivation by foreseen objects; every idea efficacious → perpetual disturbance; selectively vivified ideas → viable motivational medium.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS2","text":"Present impression → customary relation → transferred force and vivacity → intensified idea → belief → action and passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0041","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS3","text":"Affecting object → incipient passion → diffusion into imagination → intensified object-idea → assent → reinforced passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS4","text":"Believed or familiar anchor → relational transmission → vivified fictional network → easy reception → imaginative and affective entertainment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS5","text":"Temperament, eloquence, or bodily ferment → non-customary vivification → counterfeit belief; extreme vivification → collapse of truth-falsehood discrimination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:FGS6","text":"Reflection on source and reality-connection → application of general rules → correction of apparent intensity → restoration of graded conviction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:AF1","text":"Educational resistance → scale objection → motivational extremes → belief as selective medium → definition by vivacity → passion-belief reciprocity → truth reduced to easy reception → poetic substitution → imaginative production of assent → madness as limit case → correction by reflection and general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME1","text":"Belief is reclassified from a distinct judgmental content into a mode of conceiving an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME2","text":"Motivation is extended from present sensation to absent and future objects through selective vivification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME3","text":"Truth in imaginative works is reclassified as one mechanism of easy reception rather than an inviolable aesthetic requirement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME4","text":"Judgment, passion, and imagination become reciprocally permeable rather than hierarchically independent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME5","text":"Causal conviction, poetry, eloquence, and madness are placed on one field of vivification while remaining differentiated by source and regulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ME6","text":"Epistemic correction shifts from measuring apparent agitation to applying second-order rules about causal origin and connection with reality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ALT1","text":"Surface: belief modifies the effects of ideas on action, passion, and imaginative enjoyment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ALT2","text":"Structural: mental force is transmitted through custom, affect, familiarity, and relations among ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0041","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: phenomena of passion, fiction, eloquence, and madness are converted into tests of the general theory of belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: absent and future objects acquire present efficacy through graded modes of perception rather than through direct presence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0041","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT1","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s principal definition, reciprocal mechanisms, limiting cases, and corrective closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT2","text":"The magnitude and qualitative dimensions of force and vivacity remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent at the functional level because the same mechanism explains action, passion, imaginative reception, and counterfeit belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT4","text":"The strongest inference is the reciprocal confirmation obtained by showing belief vivifying passion and passion vivifying belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT5","text":"The inference from similarity of effects to similarity in force and vivacity depends on the compressed premise that force and vivacity exhaust the relevant differences among perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT6","text":"The claim that nature selected a motivational medium supplies a functional rationale rather than an independent causal demonstration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT7","text":"The scalar reduction is strained by the claim that poetical imagery may appear more vivid than experience while remaining weaker and qualitatively different in feeling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT8","text":"General rules repair this strain, but the chapter does not fully specify their origin, authority, or mode of operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXIB:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if poetry and madness are retained as mechanism-testing cases rather than discarded as illustrations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXIB:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 09","title":"Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","context":"Chapter 09","original_title":"Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","original_context":"Chapter 09"},"original_title":"Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability","order":10,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXUP"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXUP@R001","sha256":"b314321273c63c891ecc0bc7da77446b5a8448ced2c74901072cd15b2fb19c83","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS1","text":"Problem: philosophically disallowed variations of belief arise from the same principles as sanctioned probability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS2","text":"Thesis: every judgment short of knowledge consists in a degree of force and vivacity communicated to a perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS3","text":"Mechanism: present impression → customary transition → related idea; diminution of either source force or transitional facility weakens belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS4","text":"Diagnostic movement: temporal fading → inferential attenuation → homogeneous-chain qualification → prejudicial generalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS5","text":"Corrective movement: first-order custom mistakes accidental resemblance for efficacy; second-order general rules condemn that operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS6","text":"Structural contradiction: custom and rule-following generate unphilosophical probability and provide its only correction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AS7","text":"Argument type: reconstructive psychology joined to skeptical diagnosis and a unified causal account of probability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A1","text":"Belief in existence is the force and vivacity of a perception in every judgment that does not amount to knowledge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A2","text":"Memory possesses the greatest force and vivacity available to nondemonstrative belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A3","text":"Causal inference borrows vivacity from a present impression and transfers it to a related idea through habit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A4","text":"Evidential force varies with the strength of the originating impression and the facility of the associative transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A5","text":"Repeated conjunction produces a customary transition of the imagination that precedes reflection and cannot be prevented by it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A6","text":"Custom extends from identical cases to resembling cases, losing force by degrees as resemblance diminishes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A7","text":"Temporal remoteness and heterogeneous inferential mediation dissipate vivacity independently of logical validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A8","text":"Resemblance among successive inferential links permits cognitive compression and limits the attenuation caused by numerical length.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A9","text":"In contrary experience, each remembered possibility separately affects the imagination, while net belief follows numerical superiority with a deduction for opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A10","text":"Frequent conjunction enables salient accidental circumstances to evoke the effect associated with efficacious circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A11","text":"Reflective general rules derived from experience of the understanding distinguish efficacious circumstances from accidental conjunctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A12","text":"The more extensive and constant inference is attributed to judgment, while the irregular and uncertain exception is attributed to imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A13","text":"Common, universal, direct, and unitary signs communicate vivacity more effectively than rare, oblique, or dispersed signs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A14","text":"A lively idea and its corresponding passion can reciprocally amplify one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:A15","text":"Philosophical and unphilosophical probabilities differ in normative authorization, not in the psychological principles generating them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX1","text":"Actual conviction is not fixed by philosophy’s recognition of an argument as legitimate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX2","text":"The unchanged content of an argument does not preserve invariant authority as its originating impression recedes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX3","text":"The infallibility of individual links does not guarantee conviction across a heterogeneous inferential chain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX4","text":"The numerical length of historical transmission does not by itself extinguish evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX5","text":"Homogeneous copying is not correctly modeled as an indefinite series of independent probabilistic diminutions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX6","text":"Resemblance to a familiar cause does not establish the presence of its efficacious circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX7","text":"Present observation does not automatically defeat a customary association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX8","text":"General rules are not uniformly philosophical; rash generalization is itself a source of prejudice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX9","text":"Reflection does not prevent the imagination’s initial customary transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX10","text":"Equal assent to the same conclusion does not entail equal imaginative or affective influence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:NX11","text":"The common origin of judgment and imagination in custom does not prevent them from issuing opposed inferences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D1","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A1 + CH_THN_SXUP:A3 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A7, the same remembered fact loses authority as its impression fades despite retaining the same propositional content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D2","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A3 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A5 + CH_THN_SXUP:A7 + CH_THN_SXUP:A14, recent observations receive disproportionate weight because both their impressions and their customary transitions remain intact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D3","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A3 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A7, demonstratively connected reasoning can psychologically degenerate into probability when it requires many distinct transitions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D4","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A8, historical evidence survives long transmission when repeated links are sufficiently homogeneous to be traversed as a compressed general sequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D5","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A7 + CH_THN_SXUP:A8, a chain composed of genuinely distinct probabilistic links would undergo repeated diminution, whereas homogeneous proof-preserving links need not.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D6","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A5 + CH_THN_SXUP:A6 + CH_THN_SXUP:A10, prejudice is ordinary causal habit extended from conspicuous resemblance while material differences are neglected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D7","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A10 + CH_THN_SXUP:A11 + CH_THN_SXUP:A12, philosophical correction occurs when a more general rule reclassifies a first-order customary inference as accidental.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D8","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A1 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A12 + CH_THN_SXUP:A13 + CH_THN_SXUP:A14, convergence between judgment and a common sign produces stronger affect than an equally certain conclusion assembled from oblique signs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D9","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A11 + CH_THN_SXUP:A13 + CH_THN_SXUP:A14, social rules can make an originally stronger offense indirectly less painful by supplying a vivid adverse inference about the offender.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0015","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:D10","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A1 + CH_THN_SXUP:A2 + CH_THN_SXUP:A3 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A9, memory, proof, and probability form a descending continuum of vivacity rather than distinct species of belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A5 + CH_THN_SXUP:A10 + CH_THN_SXUP:A11 + CH_THN_SXUP:A12, philosophical correction redirects custom from first-order resemblance toward more extensive regularity rather than opposing custom with an independent faculty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A1 + CH_THN_SXUP:A4 + CH_THN_SXUP:A7 + CH_THN_SXUP:A13, logical warrant and experienced conviction can vary independently because only the latter tracks the transmission of vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD3","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A7 + CH_THN_SXUP:A8 + CH_THN_SXUP:A13, effective inferential distance is determined by cognitive heterogeneity and labor rather than by the number of links alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD4","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A13 + CH_THN_SXUP:A14, modes of presentation can reorganize passion without altering the conclusion accepted by judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD5","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A1 + CH_THN_SXUP:A2 + CH_THN_SXUP:A3 + CH_THN_SXUP:A9, proof and probability differ by the organization and intensity of one mechanism, not by possessing different psychological foundations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SD6","text":"From CH_THN_SXUP:A12 + CH_THN_SXUP:A15, philosophy remains disposition-sensitive because either the prejudicial rule or its corrective metarule may prevail in a particular mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX1","text":"No independent rational faculty produces belief outside the operations of custom, imagination, and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX2","text":"No proposition carries an intrinsic belief-force invariant under memory, mediation, and presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX3","text":"No causal discontinuity separates sanctioned probability from prejudice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX4","text":"No simple accumulation of inferential links determines evidential loss without regard to their resemblance and cognitive organization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX5","text":"No reflective correction permanently abolishes the first customary propensity it regulates.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:DNX6","text":"No affective response remains insulated from the imaginative mode by which its object is conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1","text":"An extra-customary tribunal of reason is unavailable; philosophical authority can appear only as a more extensive and constant ordering of custom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SNX2","text":"An invariant correspondence between logical validity and felt conviction is structurally foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SNX3","text":"A final reconciliation between judgment and imagination is absent; corrective judgment competes with rather than extinguishes irregular transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:SNX4","text":"A temporally and affectively invariant believing subject is unavailable within the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS1","text":"Repeated conjunction → custom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS2","text":"Present impression + custom → imaginative transition → vivified related idea → belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS3","text":"Source faintness, temporal remoteness, diminished resemblance, contrary instances, or heterogeneous mediation → reduced vivacity → weakened evidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS4","text":"Homogeneous inferential links → compressed traversal → limited attenuation despite numerical length → preservation of historical belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS5","text":"Resemblance + salient accidental conjunction → first-order general rule → expected effect without efficacious condition → prejudice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS6","text":"Reflection on prior operations + counterinstances → more general rule → accidental–efficacious distinction → corrective judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS7","text":"First-order rule ↔ second-order rule → disposition-sensitive conflict between imagination and judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS8","text":"Sign generality and unity → facilitated transition; sign rarity and dispersion → cognitive labor → diminished vivacity and passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS9","text":"Opposed remembered possibilities → separate imaginative forces → aggregation and cancellation → graded probability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:FGS10","text":"All outputs → single custom–imagination–vivacity architecture → normative division between philosophical and unphilosophical probability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:AF1","text":"Sanctioned/unphilosophical disparity → common belief mechanism → recency effects → inferential attenuation → ancient-history objection → homogeneous-chain resolution → prejudice as extended custom → corrective metarules → conflict of general rules → presentational and affective consequences → unified theory of probability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME1","text":"Philosophical probability and prejudice become differently regulated outputs of one psychological mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME2","text":"Evidential validity and actual conviction become separable dimensions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME3","text":"Inferential distance is redrawn as cognitive heterogeneity rather than numerical length.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME4","text":"Prejudice is reclassified from an alien defect of reason into an excessive operation of ordinary causal habit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME5","text":"The opposition between judgment and imagination becomes a conflict between first-order and second-order general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME6","text":"Memory, proof, and probability become positions on a continuum of force rather than psychologically independent kinds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ME7","text":"Social and affective responses become functions of sign structure and transitional facility, not merely of what the judgment accepts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ALT1","text":"Surface: belief varies with recency, inferential distance, generalization, and mode of presentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ALT2","text":"Structural: custom transfers vivacity through imagination, while resemblance, mediation, opposition, and sign form regulate that transfer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: philosophy corrects natural bias only by redirecting the same generalizing mechanism that produced it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: belief is a temporally variable event of force-transmission rather than an invariant possession attached to propositional content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the four unphilosophical probabilities, the historical-evidence objection, the conflict of general rules, and the final unification of belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT2","text":"The quantitative relation between impression-strength, transitional facility, and resulting conviction remains unspecified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT3","text":"The authority of the “more general and authentic” corrective rule remains underdetermined, and the complete regulative rules are deferred beyond the chapter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT4","text":"The system is internally coherent as a psychological explanation of variations in conviction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT5","text":"Its strongest operation is the derivation of multiple evidential distortions from a single model of vivacity transmission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT6","text":"The historical repair depends on treating initial testimony as proof and subsequent transmission as cognitively homogeneous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT7","text":"The central fault line is that custom supplies prejudice, philosophical correction, and the standard by which correction is preferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT8","text":"The argument depends on inherited premises that belief is vivacity and causal reasoning is customary transition rather than establishing them anew.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0021","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXUP:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if historical homogeneity is treated as a qualification of attenuation and the social cases as tests of the same mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXUP:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 10","title":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability","context":"Chapter 10","original_title":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability","original_context":"Chapter 10"},"original_title":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects","order":11,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXRW"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXRW@R001","sha256":"cc6c291eb4e6d152a70c3056733d45b2b55934f5e0fe38ab76cbda6fcb2d516f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS1","text":"Problem: a priori inspection cannot distinguish actual causal relations from unrestricted causal possibilities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS2","text":"Core thesis: actual causation is judged through constant conjunction constrained by contiguity, temporal priority, and causal uniformity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS3","text":"Argument type: reconstructive and methodological, terminating in an anti-scholastic restriction on formal rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS4","text":"Movement: a priori causal indeterminacy → empirical criteria → comparative rules → controlled experimentation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS5","text":"Comparative regularities isolate common causes, differentiating causes, compound effects, and incomplete causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AS6","text":"Formal rule-production yields to recursive experimental application requiring judgment, perseverance, and widened experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A1","text":"Neither causal capacity nor causal incapacity can be determined by mere inspection independent of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A2","text":"No objects are properly contrary except existence and non-existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A3","text":"Causation depends wholly on constant conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A4","text":"Cause and effect are contiguous in space and time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A5","text":"A cause is temporally prior to its effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A6","text":"The same cause always produces the same effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A7","text":"The same effect never arises except from the same cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A8","text":"Every natural phenomenon is compounded and modified by multiple circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A9","text":"Decisive causal inquiry requires experimentally separating superfluous circumstances from essential ones.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A10","text":"Every new experiment remains subject to renewed circumstance-analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A11","text":"The operative circumstances of moral phenomena are more numerous, implicit, and obscure than those of natural phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:A12","text":"Increased security requires enlargement of the experimental sphere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX1","text":"No object is identifiable as a cause or non-cause through mere conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX2","text":"No intrinsic classification of objects restricts in advance what may cause what.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX3","text":"Mere occurrence without constant conjunction does not constitute causation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX4","text":"A non-contiguous or effect-prior relation cannot qualify as causal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX5","text":"A few experiments cannot establish proportional causal decomposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX6","text":"Covariation within an observed range does not license indefinite extrapolation beyond that range.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX7","text":"An object fully present before the effect cannot be the sole complete cause of that effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:NX8","text":"Formal logical precepts neither eliminate experimental judgment nor establish the superiority of scholastic reasoning.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D1","text":"Before experience, anything imaginable remains a possible cause or effect of anything else.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A1","CH_THN_SXRW:A2","CH_THN_SXRW:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D2","text":"A clear causal experiment licenses projection to phenomena of the same kind without awaiting renewed constant repetition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D3","text":"When different objects produce the same effect, causation must be assigned to a quality common to them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D4","text":"When resembling objects produce different effects, the causal difference must be assigned to the circumstance in which they differ.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D5","text":"Adequately repeated proportional covariation indicates a compound effect composed of effects corresponding to parts of the cause.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D6","text":"If a putative cause is fully present while its effect is absent, an auxiliary causal principle is required.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A4","CH_THN_SXRW:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D7","text":"Causal investigation proceeds recursively by varying circumstances, testing essentiality, and submitting each result to renewed variation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D8","text":"Complex experimental application requires perseverance in inquiry and sagacity in selecting discriminating tests.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:D9","text":"Moral inquiry requires broader experimentation because its operative variables are less directly identifiable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A11","CH_THN_SXRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","text":"Causal rules regulate inference among experienced regularities rather than disclose intrinsic productive powers.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A1","CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A4","CH_THN_SXRW:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD2","text":"Within an adequately specified class, apparent irregularity is reassigned to an unisolated difference, compound contribution, or missing co-cause rather than admitted as brute exception.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","text":"Experimental correction is structurally open-ended because every control becomes a new object of control.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","text":"Causal method cannot be reduced to a self-applying algorithm because no rule independently selects the decisive experimental variation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD5","text":"Moral inquiry employs the same causal architecture as natural inquiry under conditions of greater variable opacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A11","CH_THN_SXRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","text":"Causal identity shifts from whole objects to experimentally isolated qualities or circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:DNX1","text":"Objects must not possess causally legible powers available independently of observed conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:DNX2","text":"Kinds, qualities, and relevant similarities must not be wholly unstable, or comparative causal projection becomes impossible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:DNX3","text":"Essential circumstances must not be experimentally inseparable in principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:DNX4","text":"Causal irregularity must not be brute; it must remain attributable to an undiscovered difference, scope condition, or auxiliary cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:DNX5","text":"The obscurity of moral circumstances must not amount to absolute inaccessibility, or enlarged experimentation could provide no increased security.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SNX1","text":"No observable necessary connection beyond regular conjunction is available to ground causal judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SNX2","text":"No a priori ontology assigns fixed causal or receptive roles to kinds of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SNX3","text":"No self-executing logical system can replace experimental discrimination and human judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","text":"No terminal experiment closes causal inquiry against further circumstance-analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS1","text":"Non-contrariety + absence of a priori causal discrimination → unrestricted candidate causal relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS2","text":"Candidate relation + spatial-temporal contiguity + cause-priority + constant conjunction → causal attribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS3","text":"Clear causal instance + cause-effect uniformity → projection to phenomena of the same kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS4","text":"Same effect across differing objects → isolate common circumstance; differing effects across resembling objects → isolate differing circumstance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS5","text":"Repeated proportional part-covariation → compound-effect decomposition; full cause without effect → incomplete-cause diagnosis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS6","text":"Compound phenomenon → remove or vary circumstance → test essentiality → reanalyze resulting experiment → repeat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:FGS7","text":"Increasing circumstantial complexity → increasing dependence on perseverance, sagacity, and expanded experimental range.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:AF1","text":"A priori causal indeterminacy → constant conjunction as empirical ground → spatiotemporal and uniformity rules → comparative isolation → decomposition and completion tests → recursive experimental difficulty → enlargement of experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME1","text":"Causal possibility is detached from the conceptual content of objects and left open pending experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME2","text":"Actual causes are distinguished from merely possible causes by observed relational structure rather than intrinsic intelligibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME3","text":"Resemblance and difference become experimental operators for locating causal circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME4","text":"Disappointed expectation becomes evidence of an omitted distinction or condition rather than an immediate defeat of causal uniformity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME5","text":"Proportional covariation reclassifies effects as compounds while restricting inference to adequately tested ranges.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME6","text":"Possession of causal rules is separated from competence in their application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ME7","text":"Moral phenomena are placed on the same causal map as natural phenomena but assigned a higher degree of circumstantial opacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ALT1","text":"Surface: eight rules specify the immediate criteria and comparative tests for judging causes and effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ALT2","text":"Structural: constant conjunction, controlled resemblance, controlled difference, and recursive experimentation isolate operative circumstances within compound phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: formal logic is subordinated to experimental design, persistence, sagacity, and expansion of the evidential field.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: any non-contradictory object may enter a causal relation, while actual causation takes the form of contiguous, temporally ordered, uniform succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT1","text":"The axiom set is sufficient to reconstruct the transition from unrestricted causal possibility to empirical rules and recursive application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT2","text":"The meanings of same cause, same kind, clear experiment, adequate repetition, full perfection, and essential circumstance remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT3","text":"Internal coherence depends on stable kinds, causal uniformity, and provisional experimental discriminability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest in its paired comparative rules and its account of recursive experimental control.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT5","text":"The claim that causation depends wholly on constant conjunction remains imperfectly reconciled with contiguity and priority as additional necessary conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT6","text":"The same-effect/same-cause rule conflicts superficially with different objects producing the same effect; the common-quality rule resolves this only by shifting the unit of causal identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT7","text":"Projection from one clear experiment remains in tension with the prohibition on inference from a few experiments because clarity and adequate scope are not formally specified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT8","text":"The anti-scholastic claim that natural understanding can supply the rules is asserted by contrast rather than generated from the causal axioms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXRW:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument’s movement but cannot convert its application into a determinate procedure without suppressing the judgment the text declares indispensable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 11","title":"Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects","context":"Chapter 11","original_title":"Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects","original_context":"Chapter 11"},"original_title":"Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy","order":12,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIAP"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIAP@R001","sha256":"30e8a1a64abbc5922c4e31762cca6e6720237656d8a92b2891470692cc29633f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS1","text":"The problem is to explain why collections of distinct and variable qualities are represented as simple, identical substances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS2","text":"Ancient metaphysical categories are fictions generated by natural operations of imagination rather than discoveries of objective structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS3","text":"The argument is genealogical, diagnostic, epistemological, and polemical.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS4","text":"Related qualities → smooth mental transition → imputed identity or simplicity → reflective disclosure of difference → contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS5","text":"Contradiction → fictive substance → homogeneous matter and substantial forms → accidents → occult qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS6","text":"Vulgar custom is first negated by reflection; false philosophy then preserves the demand for objective connection that reflection has made unintelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AS7","text":"Moderate scepticism terminates the impossible search, whereas false philosophy terminates it through meaningless words and anthropomorphic projections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A1","text":"Unreasonable philosophical fictions can be generated by natural propensities of human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A2","text":"The idea of a body is a mind-formed collection of ideas of distinct sensible qualities found in constant union.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A3","text":"Close relations among ideas produce easy transitions of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A4","text":"The imagination substitutes objects when their modes of affecting thought are similar.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A5","text":"Gradual survey of successive qualities suppresses awareness of change.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A6","text":"Comparison of temporally separated conditions makes their differences salient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A7","text":"Close relation among coexistent qualities suppresses awareness of composition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A8","text":"Reflective comparison discloses sensible qualities as distinct, distinguishable, and separable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A9","text":"Contradiction between natural attribution and reflective disclosure prompts imagination to feign a persisting unifier.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A10","text":"Repeated conjunction of qualities with a supposed substance generates an imagined dependence of those qualities upon it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A11","text":"Whatever is distinct and separately conceivable may exist separately.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A12","text":"Customary conjunction makes the vulgar mistake difficulty in separating ideas for impossibility in separating objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A13","text":"Causal inference rests on observed constant conjunction rather than perceived intrinsic connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A14","text":"There is no idea of power or agency as something separate from the mind and inherent in causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A15","text":"False philosophy rejects the vulgar perception of connection while retaining the search for connection in matter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A16","text":"Repetition and verbal resemblance can make unintelligible terms appear secretly meaningful.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:A17","text":"Human imagination tends to transfer inward emotions to external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX1","text":"A body is not given as an intrinsically simple object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX2","text":"Numerical identity is not disclosed by a succession of changing qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX3","text":"Substance is not an intelligible perception, bearer, or explanation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX4","text":"Substantial forms are not discovered sources of species-specific qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX5","text":"Sensible qualities are not accidents that require an unknown subject of inhesion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX6","text":"Constant conjunction is not a perceived necessary connection among objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX7","text":"Faculties and occult qualities are not meaningful explanations of phenomena.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:NX8","text":"Sympathy, antipathy, and horror of a vacuum are not warranted affections of external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D1","text":"Related successive qualities are mistaken for one identical object because their gradual survey resembles the survey of an unchanging object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A4","CH_THN_SIAP:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D2","text":"Related coexistent qualities are mistaken for one simple object because their apprehension resembles a single act of thought.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A4","CH_THN_SIAP:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D3","text":"The same quality-collection appears identical and simple under fluent survey but diverse and composite under reflective comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A6","CH_THN_SIAP:A7","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D4","text":"Substance is invented to preserve identity through variation and unity through composition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D5","text":"Gradual transformations generate the fiction of homogeneous matter, while salient differences generate the fiction of distinct substantial forms.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A6","CH_THN_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D6","text":"The doctrine of accidents converts habitual co-imagination into supposed ontological dependence.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D7","text":"The alleged necessary dependence of qualities on substance collapses once qualities are recognized as separately conceivable.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A8","CH_THN_SIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D8","text":"Occult-quality theory compounds an unintelligible supporting substance with an obscure supported property.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:A10","CH_THN_SIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D9","text":"Vulgar custom produces belief in intrinsic connection; reflection removes that belief; false philosophy continues the external search; true philosophy suspends it.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A12","CH_THN_SIAP:A13","CH_THN_SIAP:A14","CH_THN_SIAP:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D10","text":"Terms such as faculty and occult quality end inquiry by simulating the presence of an absent idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:D11","text":"Sympathy, antipathy, and horror of a vacuum externalize affective structures belonging to the observer.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD1","text":"Ancient ontology is a layered repair-system constructed to conceal conflicts produced by incompatible modes of apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD2","text":"Each metaphysical repair increases ontological complexity while decreasing intelligibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD3","text":"False philosophy occupies an unstable middle position: it has enough reflection to destroy vulgar certainty but insufficient scepticism to relinquish the demand that certainty expressed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD4","text":"Metaphysical terminology functions as cognitive consolation by converting an acknowledged absence of explanation into apparent explanatory closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD5","text":"Substantiation and anthropomorphism are variants of one operation: features of the mind’s activity are transferred to external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SD6","text":"True philosophy and vulgar thought converge in practical indifference while remaining opposed in epistemic basis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX1","text":"Similarity between mental operations and object-apprehensions must not entail similarity in the objects apprehended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX2","text":"Psychological difficulty in separating ideas must not establish metaphysical inseparability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX3","text":"A posit cannot acquire explanatory legitimacy merely by resolving a contradiction in imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX4","text":"Reflection cannot produce a valid positive metaphysics by preserving the object-side agency that it has rendered inconceivable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5","text":"Technical vocabulary has no reserve of meaning independent of an assignable idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:DNX6","text":"External nature cannot be presumed to reproduce the emotions and intentions most available to the human mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SNX1","text":"No positively conceivable invariant bearer exists within the system to underwrite identity, simplicity, or cohesion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SNX2","text":"No object-side reconciliation of sameness with change or unity with plurality is available; the demanded reconciliation exists only as fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SNX3","text":"The supposed inner power of causes remains unavailable as an object of thought, leaving the metaphysical interior of causation empty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:SNX4","text":"No philosophical metalanguage is guaranteed to escape the imaginative mechanisms it diagnoses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS1","text":"Distinct sensible qualities + constant union → closely related quality-collection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS2","text":"Successive relation + gradual survey + similarity of mental act → fictive identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS3","text":"Coexistent relation + imperceptible transition among parts + similarity of mental act → fictive simplicity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS4","text":"Distant or analytic comparison → disclosed variation or composition → contradiction → unknown substrate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS5","text":"Gradual transformation / salient difference → homogeneous matter / substantial form → accident-substrate system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS6","text":"Constant conjunction → vulgar necessary connection → reflective dissolution → sceptical cessation or false-philosophical search.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS7","text":"Retained demand for agency + absent idea → faculty or occult-quality label → semantic illusion → termination of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:FGS8","text":"Internal emotion + projective propensity → affective predicates assigned to nature → anthropomorphic physics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AF1","text":"Quality-collection → smooth transition → identity and simplicity → comparative disruption → substance fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AF2","text":"Substance fiction → homogeneous matter and substantial forms → accidents → occult qualities → total incomprehensibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AF3","text":"Vulgar conjunction → reflective separation → no known connection → false search or moderate scepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:AF4","text":"Meaningless terminology → simulated explanation → inquiry closure → anthropomorphic projection exposed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME1","text":"Identity and simplicity are reclassified from object-properties to effects of a mode of mental survey.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME2","text":"Substance, substantial form, accident, and occult quality are relocated from ontology into a genealogy of imaginative repair.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME3","text":"Philosophical sophistication ceases to form a linear ascent: false philosophy becomes an unstable interval between vulgar custom and true scepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME4","text":"Explanatory terms are reclassified as possible mechanisms for stopping inquiry rather than advancing it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME5","text":"The apparent distance between vulgar thought and true philosophy contracts because both terminate speculation, though for contrary reasons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ME6","text":"Ancient physical principles become legible as projections of affect rather than descriptions of external agency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ALT1","text":"Surface: Substance, matter, substantial form, accident, faculty, occult quality, sympathy, and antipathy are subjected to genealogical dissolution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ALT2","text":"Structural: Relation, smooth transition, comparison, habit, contradiction-repair, verbal resemblance, and affective projection generate the ancient system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Philosophical inquiry is divided between vulgar belief, failed metaphysical repair, and moderate sceptical cessation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Unity and identity vary with temporal and analytic perspective, while intrinsic substance and objective causal power remain unavailable to conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the section’s genealogy of ancient metaphysical categories.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT2","text":"The wider foundations of the separability principle, causal inference, and moderate scepticism remain underdetermined within this section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent insofar as one mechanism—confusion produced by similar effects on thought—generates both identity and simplicity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT4","text":"The argument is strongest where alternative modes of survey produce the contradiction that substance is introduced to conceal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT5","text":"The inference from separate conceivability to possible separate existence is load-bearing but asserted rather than established here.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT6","text":"The genealogy of a belief explains its production but does not by itself establish its falsity; the refutation also depends on the absence of a corresponding idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT7","text":"The ranking of vulgar, false, and true philosophy compresses a normative criterion by treating sceptical cessation as justified and verbal cessation as illusory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIAP:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the movement from cognitive mechanism through metaphysical proliferation to semantic and anthropomorphic exposure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIAP:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 12","title":"Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy","context":"Chapter 12","original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy","original_context":"Chapter 12"},"original_title":"Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul","order":13,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVIS"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVIS@R001","sha256":"8f199b9b4c6cbb525c7d9b36262c1c9dc3b63ca75b74e0cfbb7e81ee586baba3","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS1","text":"The central problem is whether perceptions can intelligibly be said to inhere in a material or immaterial substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS2","text":"The argument is diagnostic, aporetic, empiricist, and polemically retortive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS3","text":"The concepts of substance and inhesion must pass the impression-origin and separability tests before the dispute can proceed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS4","text":"Neither concept passes; the question concerning the soul’s substance is therefore unintelligible rather than merely unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS5","text":"Perceptions divide into extended and nonspatial kinds, defeating every account that assigns all thought to one uniform spatial or nonspatial subject.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS6","text":"The simple-soul hypothesis reproduces the structural difficulties charged against Spinoza’s simple universal substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AS7","text":"The movement terminates by separating substance from causation: constant conjunction licenses matter–thought causation while leaving a priori claims about duration without foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A1","text":"Every idea is derived from a precedent impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A2","text":"No impression can be identified as the impression of a mental substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A3","text":"Whatever is clearly conceived may exist as conceived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A4","text":"Everything different is distinguishable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A5","text":"Everything distinguishable is separable by the imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A6","text":"Perceptions are different from one another and from everything else.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A7","text":"Nothing appears requisite to support the existence of a perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A8","text":"What remains unknown concerning mind must remain undecided rather than be supplied by speculative hypotheses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A9","text":"Perceptions are heterogeneous with respect to extension: some are extended and others exist without spatial place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A10","text":"Whatever has a spatial place must be either extended or an indivisible mathematical point.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A11","text":"A relation of place requires both relata to possess spatial characteristics.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A12","text":"The mind tends to add a new relation to objects already united by other relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A13","text":"Every intelligible quality attributed to an external object must be representable through an impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A14","text":"No necessary connection between cause and effect is perceived in the ideas of objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A15","text":"Constant conjunction constitutes the operative content of the causal relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A16","text":"Any pair of real objects is, a priori, susceptible of constant conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A17","text":"Changes in bodily disposition are constantly conjoined with changes in thought and sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:A18","text":"Every conceivable object can be imagined to become inactive or to be annihilated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX1","text":"Ignorance concerning mind does not authorize contradictory hypotheses concerning its substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX2","text":"The materiality or immateriality of the soul is not an intelligible dispute before substance and inhesion are defined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX3","text":"Independent existence does not distinguish substance from perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX4","text":"Perceptions are not intelligibly defined as accidents requiring an unknown support.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX5","text":"Existence does not universally entail spatial location.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX6","text":"A nonspatial perception is not a mathematical point.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX7","text":"Causal and temporal conjunction is not literal conjunction in place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX8","text":"Thought is not uniformly extended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX9","text":"Thought is not uniformly simple, indivisible, and unextended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX10","text":"A simple immaterial soul is not established by the incompatibility of some perceptions with extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX11","text":"Renaming perceptions “actions” rather than “modifications” does not explain their dependence upon substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0020","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0021","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX12","text":"The absence of an apparent conceptual connection between motion and thought does not prove their causal incompatibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:NX13","text":"Simplicity or immateriality does not yield an a priori proof of immortality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D1","text":"The idea of mental substance lacks legitimate cognitive content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D2","text":"Because perceptions are separably conceivable and possibly separately existent, defining substance by independent existence makes every perception a substance and destroys the intended distinction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A3","CH_THN_SVIS:A4","CH_THN_SVIS:A5","CH_THN_SVIS:A6","CH_THN_SVIS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D3","text":"Inhesion has no intelligible content where no support for perception can be identified as necessary.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D4","text":"The question whether perceptions inhere in material or immaterial substance is composed of terms for which no adequate ideas exist.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A2","CH_THN_SVIS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D5","text":"Nonspatial perceptions must be admitted as existing nowhere, because treating them as points would make aggregates of them extended.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D6","text":"A nonspatial perception cannot be locally conjoined with an extended body.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D7","text":"Where an extended object and a nonspatial perception are causally and temporally associated, the imagination fabricates spatial conjunction to complete their union.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D8","text":"The attribution of place to nonspatial qualities persists through a conflict between associative imagination and spatially discriminating reason.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A10","CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D9","text":"Materialism cannot coherently conjoin all thought with extension because some perceptions exclude place.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D10","text":"The simple-soul doctrine cannot coherently conjoin all perceptions with an indivisible subject because some perceptions are themselves extended and divisible.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D11","text":"Any intelligible repugnance between an extended object and a simple substratum transfers to the relation between an extended impression and a simple soul.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D12","text":"The objections to Spinoza’s simple universal substance apply symmetrically to the theological doctrine of a simple thinking substance.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A6","CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D13","text":"If an action is defined as an inseparable abstract mode of substance, distinct and separable perceptions cannot be explained merely by calling them actions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A4","CH_THN_SVIS:A5","CH_THN_SVIS:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0021","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D14","text":"No a priori comparison of motion and thought can exclude their causal conjunction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A15","CH_THN_SVIS:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D15","text":"Bodily motion and disposition may be regarded as actual causes of thought insofar as causation consists in experienced constant conjunction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A15","CH_THN_SVIS:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D16","text":"Requiring an apparent necessary connection would eliminate every cause; invoking divine agency to repair that absence would make the deity the cause of all volitions and perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:D17","text":"Matter and spirit are equally incapable of supporting an a priori inference to necessary continued existence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A3","CH_THN_SVIS:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","text":"The explanatory burden shifts from identifying a bearer of perceptions to accounting for the relations among perceptions themselves.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0040","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD2","text":"The decisive ontological division is not mind against matter but extended perception against nonspatial perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","text":"Associative relation-completion generates pseudo-ontological placement and allows contradictions to persist under familiar forms of speech.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","text":"Materialism and spiritualism become reciprocal overextensions: each universalizes one class of perception into a subject adequate to all perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD5","text":"Agnosticism concerning mental substance is compatible with an empirical account in which bodily processes cause thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SD6","text":"Religion is deprived of metaphysical support from substance and simplicity while remaining formally open to moral and analogical arguments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX1","text":"No impression-transcendent concept may determine the nature of the mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","text":"Perceptions do not require a numerically identical bearer beneath their differences.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX3","text":"Spatial location is not a universal condition of being.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX4","text":"Theological substance is not exempt from standards applied to materialist or Spinozist substance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX5","text":"Causal efficacy does not require resemblance, conceptual containment, or a perceivable necessary nexus.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:DNX6","text":"Immaterial simplicity does not contain a principle of necessary persistence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SNX1","text":"The unavailable object organizing the dispute is the supposed substratum in which perceptions occur but which can never itself appear as a perception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SNX2","text":"No common ontological medium is available through which extended and nonspatial perceptions can be rendered locally homogeneous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SNX3","text":"No privileged simple subject survives the reciprocal application of mode–substance objections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:SNX4","text":"No a priori bridge remains from the vocabulary of soul to the religious conclusion of immortality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS1","text":"Proposed mental entity → demand originating impression → no impression identifiable → concept excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS2","text":"Difference → distinguishability → imaginative separability → possible separate existence → self-subsistence definition collapses.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS3","text":"Perceptual field → extended/nonspatial partition → spatial relation requires shared spatiality → universal local union fails.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS4","text":"Causal and temporal association → propensity to complete relation → fictive spatial incorporation → concealed contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS5","text":"Nonspatial perceptions defeat universal extension / extended perceptions defeat universal indivisibility → bilateral closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS6","text":"Object-quality mediated by impression → object-level repugnance transfers to impressions → Spinozist and theological hypotheses become structurally equivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS7","text":"Modification renamed action → separability test reapplied → terminological repair fails.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0020","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0021","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS8","text":"No perceived necessary nexus → causal dilemma → constant conjunction selected → matter–thought causation admitted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:FGS9","text":"Conceivable inactivity or annihilation → equal contingency of matter and spirit → metaphysical immortality proof suspended.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AF1","text":"Undefined substance and inhesion → impression test → separability test → definitional collapse → substance question condemned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AF2","text":"Spatial incompatibility argument → heterogeneous perceptions → associative fiction of place → reciprocal defeat of materialism and immaterialism → Spinozist retortion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:AF3","text":"Substance question closed → causal question isolated → necessary connection rejected → constant conjunction installed → matter–thought causation admitted → a priori immortality neutralized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME1","text":"A metaphysical dispute is reclassified as an unintelligible question generated by cognitively empty terms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME2","text":"The definition of substance by independent existence collapses the substance–accident distinction at the level of perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME3","text":"The mind–matter opposition is displaced by a cross-cutting distinction between extended and nonspatial perceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME4","text":"“Existing nowhere” becomes an admissible mode of existence rather than a contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME5","text":"The apparent location of nonspatial qualities within bodies is reclassified as an associative construction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME6","text":"Theological immaterialism and Spinozist monism become mutually testable by the same objections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME7","text":"Causation is detached from substantial ontology and relocated in experienced conjunction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ME8","text":"Immortality is removed from a priori metaphysics without being directly decided by the analysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ALT1","text":"Surface — The soul’s materiality or immateriality is unintelligible, while matter and motion may count as causes of thought.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ALT2","text":"Structural — Impression-origin, separability, spatial heterogeneity, associative relation-completion, and constant conjunction govern the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Reciprocal application of identical standards blocks theological polemic from condemning rival substance systems while exempting its own.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — Some beings exist without place; causal possibility exceeds conceptual resemblance; neither matter nor spirit possesses demonstrable necessary duration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the semantic, spatial, polemical, causal, and temporal movements of the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT2","text":"The positive ontological status of external objects and the precise referent of “matter” remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0015","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT3","text":"The system is internally coherent if the copy principle, conceivability principle, separability principle, and constant-conjunction account are jointly retained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT4","text":"Its strongest operation is the reciprocal transfer of objections between Spinozist substance and the simple theological soul.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT5","text":"Its greatest compression lies in the claims that all real objects are causally combinable a priori and that conceivability establishes possibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT6","text":"A structural fault line remains between condemning substance discourse as unintelligible and continuing to manipulate substance hypotheses for polemical retortion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT7","text":"The claim of actual matter–thought causation depends on treating bodily and mental covariation as sufficient constant conjunction without further causal individuation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT8","text":"The religious apology claims neutrality although the metaphysical arguments for immortality have been withdrawn; neutrality depends on isolating moral and analogical arguments from that loss.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVIS:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from this system preserves the actual movement from conceptual invalidation through reciprocal critique to empirical causal admissibility and temporal suspension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVIS:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 13","title":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul","context":"Chapter 13","original_title":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul","original_context":"Chapter 13"},"original_title":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book","order":14,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVCT"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVCT@R001","sha256":"de0aa867f594757274e736b419c1b42f2ad33f073321678876dc7944752e086e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS1","text":"Central problem: whether philosophical inquiry can continue after reflection deprives reason of any independent criterion for assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS2","text":"Core thesis: belief is generated by experience, habit, imaginative vivacity, and natural propensity rather than rational self-grounding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS3","text":"The imagination is simultaneously indispensable to cognition and productive of error, contradiction, and illusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS4","text":"Neither unrestricted fancy, purified understanding, nor the rejection of refined reasoning supplies a coherent epistemic rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS5","text":"Nature practically dissolves radical skepticism without rationally refuting it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS6","text":"Philosophy resumes as a natural, pleasurable, comparatively safe inclination governed by skeptical restraint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AS7","text":"Argument type and movement: diagnostic → skeptical self-subversion → affective crisis → natural restoration → pragmatic reconstruction → science of human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A1","text":"Assent to reasoning has no further rational ground beyond a felt propensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A2","text":"Experience supplies past conjunctions, and habit projects those conjunctions into the future.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A3","text":"Differential vivacity imparted by imagination is necessary for belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A4","text":"Memory, sensory attribution of existence, and causal understanding depend upon imagination or vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A5","text":"Imagination is an unavoidable but inconstant and fallible cognitive principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A6","text":"Causal inference and belief in continued external existence are equally natural and necessary yet can directly conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A7","text":"The represented connection between cause and effect is a customary determination of mind, not a discoverable external power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A8","text":"Unrestricted assent to imaginative suggestion produces contradiction, obscurity, and error.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A9","text":"Understanding acting solely through its most general principles subverts all evidence, including its own.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A10","text":"A universal prohibition on refined reasoning is itself refined, self-contradictory, and destructive of science.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A11","text":"Embodied sensation, passion, diversion, and social intercourse necessarily restore ordinary belief and action after skeptical crisis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A12","text":"Reason possesses practical authority only when it is lively and conjoined with propensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A13","text":"Curiosity, ambition, and pleasure naturally regenerate philosophical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A14","text":"The human mind generally exceeds the sphere of common affairs and therefore must choose among speculative guides.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A15","text":"Philosophy is generally safer than superstition because philosophical error is behaviorally weak while superstition strongly governs conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A16","text":"A stable philosophy must restrain speculative imagination by common practice, experience, and critical examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A17","text":"Human nature is the only science of man and the privileged domain of attainable assurance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:A18","text":"True skepticism extends diffidence to both philosophical conviction and philosophical doubt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX1","text":"Assent is not grounded in an independently demonstrable rational criterion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX2","text":"Ultimate causal energy is not known as a property residing in external objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX3","text":"Every suggestion of imagination is not entitled to assent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX4","text":"Understanding is not an autonomous faculty separable from imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX5","text":"Refined reasoning cannot be universally prohibited without employing what the prohibition rejects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX6","text":"Total suspension of belief cannot be permanently sustained.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX7","text":"Nature’s restoration of belief is not a rational refutation of skepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX8","text":"No rational obligation compels philosophical labor against inclination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX9","text":"Philosophy is not a universal vocation required of every person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX10","text":"Speciousness, imaginative warmth, and agreeableness do not establish a hypothesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX11","text":"Philosophical inquiry does not guarantee truth or certainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:NX12","text":"Positive expressions of certainty do not necessarily imply dogmatism or infallibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D1","text":"Belief is psychologically generated rather than rationally self-founded.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A1","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D2","text":"The contradiction among natural beliefs cannot be removed by discarding imagination, because imagination is their necessary common source.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVCT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D3","text":"Inquiry into ultimate causal power terminates in an account of customary mental transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D4","text":"The choice between unrestricted fancy and purified reason becomes a choice between false reason and no reason.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A9","CH_THN_SVCT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D5","text":"The practical necessity of ordinary belief supplies continuation without epistemic vindication.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A1","CH_THN_SVCT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D6","text":"Philosophy can continue only through natural inclination rather than purely rational obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D7","text":"Preference for philosophy over superstition is comparative and prudential rather than demonstratively certain.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A14","CH_THN_SVCT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D8","text":"Philosophical legitimacy depends upon methodological restraint and redirection toward human nature.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVCT:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D9","text":"Particular assertions may acquire momentary assurance while remaining globally defeasible under skeptical caveat.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:D10","text":"Skepticism must recur as self-correction rather than stabilize as permanent disbelief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A13","CH_THN_SVCT:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SD1","text":"The system replaces epistemic foundation with regulated alternation between skeptical reflection and unavoidable natural belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SD2","text":"Philosophical activity forms a recursive cycle: inquiry intensifies doubt, nature attenuates doubt, and restored curiosity renews inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SD3","text":"Philosophy is justified by comparative harmlessness, natural pleasure, and critical restraint rather than guaranteed access to truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SD4","text":"The science of human nature is reflexive: fallible faculties investigate the operations and limits of those same faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SD5","text":"Epistemic normativity is naturalized because reason’s title to assent depends upon psychological vivacity and propensity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:DNX1","text":"Human faculties cannot be independently amended or validated by another available cognitive instrument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:DNX2","text":"The will cannot permanently control assent against habit, sensation, passion, and social life.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:DNX3","text":"Pure reason cannot motivate or govern belief independently of affective force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","text":"The natural necessity of a belief cannot by itself establish that belief’s truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:DNX5","text":"Skeptical reasoning cannot coherently exempt its own conclusions from the general fallibility it establishes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SNX1","text":"No Archimedean standpoint is available from which reason, imagination, and natural belief can be jointly validated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SNX2","text":"No final reconciliation is supplied between contradictory natural principles; their conflict is managed rather than resolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SNX3","text":"Ultimate external power and absolute certainty remain unavailable as terminal objects of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:SNX4","text":"Continued philosophy lacks a purely epistemic warrant and survives through inclination, comparative safety, and provisional satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS1","text":"Faculty audit: exact reasoning → demand for its ground → discovery of propensity rather than rational warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS2","text":"Belief production: past conjunction → habit → imaginative vivacity → expectation, memory, and existence-attribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS3","text":"Contradiction production: common imaginative source → equally natural operations → incompatible conclusions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS4","text":"Skeptical dilemma: unrestricted fancy → error; purified understanding → self-subversion; prohibition of refinement → self-contradiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS5","text":"Affective crisis: sustained reflection → loss of comparative probability → melancholy and cognitive paralysis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS6","text":"Natural reset: sensation, passion, diversion, and society → attenuation of reflection → restored common belief and action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS7","text":"Recursive return: restored disposition → curiosity, ambition, and pleasure → renewed philosophical inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:FGS8","text":"Strategic closure: inevitable speculation → philosophy preferred to superstition → hypotheses restrained by practice and experience → inquiry redirected to human nature → local assertion retained under global diffidence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:AF1","text":"Faculty weakness → search for a criterion of assent → imaginative genealogy of belief → conflict among natural principles → self-subversion of purified reason → skeptical delirium → natural restoration → non-foundational preference for philosophy → science of human nature → recursive skeptical caveat.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME1","text":"Reason is reclassified as an operation dependent upon the general and established tendencies of imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME2","text":"Contradiction is relocated from accidental error to the interaction of natural and necessary cognitive operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME3","text":"Nature is reclassified from a guarantor of truth to a mechanism that restores practical viability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME4","text":"Skepticism is reclassified from permanent suspension into diffidence toward both belief and doubt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME5","text":"The philosophy–superstition distinction is redrawn around psychological force, behavioral danger, and comparative moderation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME6","text":"Philosophical certainty is divided into momentary local assurance and prohibited global dogmatism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ME7","text":"The object of philosophy shifts from ultimate external powers to the principles and limits of human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ALT1","text":"Surface: philosophical crisis culminates in the decision whether inquiry should be abandoned or resumed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ALT2","text":"Structural: imagination generates belief, contradictory natural operations, skeptical collapse, and practical restoration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: inquiry is reconstructed as a restrained, inclination-driven, comparatively safe practice directed against superstition and speculative excess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: past, future, external continuity, and remembered identity are accessible only through present vivacity, customary projection, and recurrent changes of disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the skeptical diagnosis, practical restoration, and qualified return to philosophy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT2","text":"The system is coherent as an oscillatory practical structure, not as a deductive resolution of skepticism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT3","text":"Its strongest mechanism is the dependency chain from experience and habit through vivacity to unavoidable belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT4","text":"The criterion distinguishing just philosophy from extravagant hypothesis remains only partially specified by moderation, practice, experience, and critical examination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT5","text":"The transition from reason’s psychological liveliness to its normative title to assent remains under-argued.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0010","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT6","text":"The comparative safety of philosophy is a qualified empirical generalization and admits philosophical counterinstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0012","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT7","text":"The privileging of human nature remains reflexively unstable because the investigating faculties are those already shown to be fallible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0013","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVCT:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if nature’s cure is retained as practical compulsion rather than epistemic solution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVCT:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 14","title":"Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book","context":"Chapter 14","original_title":"Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book","original_context":"Chapter 14"},"original_title":"Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VI Limitations Of This System","order":15,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVLT"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVLT@R001","sha256":"4bf93d17c8bfb4d63c77fb2d73a4d14913327e608b84c7b28f4a03335d7c2826","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS1","text":"The unrestricted system overgenerates pride and humility from every pleasurable or painful object related to self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS2","text":"Pride and humility arise only when affective valence and self-relation satisfy additional relational, comparative, social, and temporal conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS3","text":"The argument is diagnostic, restrictive, and reconstructive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS4","text":"Universal affective law → distinction between joy and self-directed passion → four restrictive thresholds → customary enlargement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS5","text":"Joy and misery require fewer mediations than pride and humility because self is essential only to the latter pair.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AS6","text":"Real happiness and misery can diverge from the distribution of pride and humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A1","text":"Pride and humility take self as their object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A2","text":"Their causes first produce pleasure or pain distinguishable from the resulting self-directed passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A3","text":"Pride and humility require a conjoint association of ideas and impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A4","text":"Relations possess variable degrees of force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A5","text":"Pride requires a closer relation to self than joy requires.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A6","text":"Pride directs attention to both its valenced cause and self, whereas joy properly takes only the valenced cause as its object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A7","text":"A stronger relation of the cause to another person weakens or destroys its relation to self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A8","text":"A cause of pride or humility must be peculiar to oneself or shared with only a few persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A9","text":"Frequent presentation and long familiarity reduce an object's perceived value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A10","text":"Human valuation proceeds more through comparison than through intrinsic merit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A11","text":"A cause of pride or humility must be discernible and obvious both to oneself and to others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A12","text":"A cause must maintain a relatively constant and durable connection with self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A13","text":"Imagination anticipates the loss of an inconstant cause and thereby weakens both satisfaction and self-satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A14","text":"General rules formed through custom and practice stabilize the valuation and proportional production of passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:A15","text":"Custom can carry passions beyond the exact bounds warranted by particular circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX1","text":"Not every pleasurable or painful object related to self produces pride or humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX2","text":"Mere relation is insufficient; the relation must be close.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX3","text":"A common or thoroughly familiar good is not ordinarily a sufficient cause of vanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX4","text":"A private or indiscernible quality is not fully eligible to produce pride or humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX5","text":"A casual or short-lived connection cannot ground a stable inference concerning self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX6","text":"Intrinsic merit alone does not determine affective value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX7","text":"Passions are not calibrated anew from each particular object without customary maxims.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX8","text":"Joy is not pride, and pain or misery is not humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX9","text":"The proudest person is not necessarily the happiest, and the most humble person is not necessarily the most miserable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:NX10","text":"The reality of an evil does not depend on its eligibility to diminish pride.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D1","text":"A weak self-relation can produce joy while failing to produce pride.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D2","text":"Objects too foreign to support vanity can still produce direct pleasure.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D3","text":"Common goods can satisfy without distinguishing their possessor and therefore without producing pride.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A9","CH_THN_SVLT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D4","text":"Commonness weakens pride twice: the cause lacks singularity, and self gains no comparative distinction through possessing it.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D5","text":"Social invisibility blocks one condition by which a valued quality becomes self-apparent.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D6","text":"A transient cause gives little joy and still less pride because anticipated loss weakens satisfaction and its connection with the durable self.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D7","text":"Customary maxims convert initially irregular and delicate affective principles into comparatively regular responses.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D8","text":"Established classifications can govern passion despite individual circumstances that remove the expected enjoyment.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D9","text":"An evil can cause misery without humility whenever it produces pain but fails the relational, peculiar, public, durable, or rule-governed conditions of self-appraisal.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A3","CH_THN_SVLT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:D10","text":"Degrees of pride and humility cannot serve as direct measures of happiness and misery.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD1","text":"The general system is a gated transformation of pleasure and pain into self-directed passions, not an unconditional causal law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD2","text":"Pride is more selective than joy because its dual-object structure multiplies the conditions under which affect must operate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD3","text":"Pride and humility register comparatively legible and temporally stable self-distinction rather than total welfare.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD4","text":"Custom supplies macroscopic regularity to affective mechanisms whose uncultivated operation would remain variable and uncertain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD5","text":"Common, hidden, transient, or externally caused evils can dominate misery while remaining weakly represented in self-evaluative passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SD6","text":"The universal mechanism is preserved by restricting its domain of successful operation rather than by abandoning the mechanism itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX1","text":"Relation cannot be binary; a mere connection to self cannot have the same force as a close and uncontested connection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX2","text":"Affective valuation cannot be a transparent registration of intrinsic goodness or badness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX3","text":"Private consciousness cannot independently determine vanity without comparative and social mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX4","text":"Momentary possession cannot constitute durable evidence of personal excellence or defect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX5","text":"Untutored passion cannot be presumed to assign stable values correctly on first encounter.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX6","text":"Hedonic condition and self-estimation cannot be coextensive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:DNX7","text":"General rules cannot remain merely cognitive; the system requires them to organize passion itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SNX1","text":"There is no unmediated passage from the reality of good or evil to a corresponding judgment of self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SNX2","text":"There is no guaranteed recognition of suffering within the economy of pride and humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SNX3","text":"There is no self-sufficient measure of personal worth insulated from comparison, publicity, and customary classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SNX4","text":"There is no invariant intrinsic scale capable of explaining the passions independently of familiarity and contrast.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:SNX5","text":"There is no assured equilibrium between particular affective reality and general rules, because custom both regularizes and exceeds particular grounds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS1","text":"Valenced object → primary pleasure or pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS2","text":"Primary affect + relation to self → candidate transition toward pride or humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS3","text":"Relational test: closeness sufficient, competing relation subordinate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS4","text":"Comparative test: cause peculiar, unfamiliar enough to retain value, and capable of distinguishing self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS5","text":"Discernibility test: cause apparent to self and others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS6","text":"Temporal test: cause sufficiently constant and proportionate to the duration attributed to self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS7","text":"Customary-rule operation → stabilized valuation, facilitated passion, possible extension beyond particular warrant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:FGS8","text":"Successful tests + association of ideas and impressions → pride or humility; failed tests → pleasure or pain without corresponding self-directed passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AF1","text":"Unrestricted general system → overgeneration problem → necessity of limitations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AF2","text":"Joy/pride distinction → graded relation → peculiarity → public discernibility → temporal constancy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AF3","text":"Variable first-order responses → custom and practice → general maxims → regularized passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:AF4","text":"Qualified causal system → separation of self-estimation from welfare → rejection of any necessary pride–happiness or humility–misery alignment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME1","text":"A universal causal claim is redrawn as a threshold-dependent transformation model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME2","text":"Hedonic affect and self-evaluative passion become distinct classes of response.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME3","text":"Relation is reclassified from simple presence into graded, competitive, and interruptible force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME4","text":"Value is relocated from intrinsic quality alone to familiarity, comparison, rarity, and distinction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME5","text":"Public appearance becomes a causal condition of self-directed passion rather than an incidental accompaniment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME6","text":"Real evil is separated from the mechanisms by which evil becomes a basis of humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ME7","text":"Custom appears simultaneously as an affective regularizer and a source of overextension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT1","text":"Surface — Five qualifications restrict or enlarge the production of pride and humility from agreeable and disagreeable objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT2","text":"Structural — A dual-object passion requires a stronger transition than a direct pleasure or pain and is therefore more vulnerable to failed mediation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT3","text":"Social — Peculiarity, comparison, shared possession, and visibility to others determine whether a quality distinguishes self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT4","text":"Metastrategic — General rules explain how delicate causal principles can operate with sufficient regularity to sustain the larger system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT5","text":"Ontological/Temporal — A relatively durable self functions as the comparator against which transient causes lose self-defining force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:ALT6","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The reality of good and evil remains independent of whether representational conditions convert them into pride or humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the restriction of the general causal system and its concluding separation of welfare from self-estimation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT2","text":"The exact degree of relational closeness required for pride or humility remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT3","text":"The causal basis of public discernibility is deferred rather than demonstrated within the section.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT4","text":"The formation and authority of general rules depend on a prior account of custom that is imported in compressed form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT5","text":"The system is internally coherent where it distinguishes direct pleasure and pain from their conversion into self-directed passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT6","text":"Its strongest operation is the explanation of cases in which pleasure or misery persists while pride or humility fails.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT7","text":"A fault line remains between familiarity as a depreciator of value and custom as a stabilizer of value; no priority rule resolves their interaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT8","text":"General rules can exceed particular warrant, but their capacity to override the other limitations is not formally bounded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT9","text":"The claim that the most solid evils often evade humility is structurally licensed but not demonstrated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVLT:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction from the extracted system preserves the movement from unrestricted law through limitation and enlargement to affective dissociation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVLT:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 15","title":"Sect. VI Limitations Of This System","context":"Chapter 15","original_title":"Sect. VI Limitations Of This System","original_context":"Chapter 15"},"original_title":"Sect. VI Limitations Of This System","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame","order":16,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXLF"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXLF@R001","sha256":"4b63b75d3411137ed732cc527b7684b958f3826519cc14c18324bf9eaf4adcb5","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS1","text":"Problem: explain why reputation governs pride and humility and amplifies their otherwise original causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS2","text":"Thesis: love of fame is secondary; praise and blame affect self through sympathetic communication, while authority governs uptake of the judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS3","text":"Another’s expressed sentiment becomes an idea whose vivacity is increased through its relations to the persistently vivid self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS4","text":"Sufficient vivacity converts the represented sentiment into an impression experienced as one’s own affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS5","text":"Variations in esteem, acquaintance, self-concurrence, proximity, and relational convergence test the mechanism against an undifferentiated instinct for fame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AS6","text":"Explanatory-reconstructive movement: reputational effect → sympathy mechanism → application to self-evaluation → differential tests → assimilation of apparent objections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A1","text":"Ideas and impressions share representational content and differ in force and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A2","text":"Consciousness supplies an invariably intimate and maximally lively impression of self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A3","text":"Relations enable the imagination to transfer vivacity from a present impression to a related idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A4","text":"External signs produce an idea of another person’s affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A5","text":"Causal inference establishes the represented affection as real.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A6","text":"General human resemblance relates another person’s mental structure to one’s own.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A7","text":"Peculiar resemblance, contiguity, kinship, and acquaintance strengthen the relation between self and another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A8","text":"A sufficiently enlivened idea of an affection becomes the corresponding impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A9","text":"Pride and humility require a self-related source of independent pleasure or pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A10","text":"Praise and blame represent another person’s favorable or unfavorable sentiment toward a quality attributed to self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A11","text":"Reasoning treats another person’s judgment as evidence for the attributed quality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A12","text":"Evaluative influence increases with the authority accorded to the evaluator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A13","text":"Praise yields substantial pleasure only when its attribution concurs with, or can alter, the recipient’s own opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A14","text":"Relations amplify sympathy when they converge upon the same evaluator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A15","text":"Sympathetic impressions remain divided when supporting relations are distributed across different evaluators.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A16","text":"Passion gives additional force to related ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:A17","text":"Conscious self-partiality favors confirming evaluations and resists opposing evaluations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX1","text":"Love of fame is not an original instinct responding equally to every favorable or unfavorable opinion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX2","text":"National uniformity of sentiment is not adequately explained by soil or climate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX3","text":"Sympathy is not immediate access to another’s passion; the passion first appears as an idea inferred from signs.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX4","text":"Conversion between idea and impression is not a change in representational content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX5","text":"Approbation is not equally effective regardless of its source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX6","text":"Abstract esteem for a quality is not sufficient to make praise pleasurable when possession of that quality is disbelieved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX7","text":"Relations do not operate merely by existing; they must facilitate associative transition and vivacity transfer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX8","text":"Relations distributed across different persons do not produce the unified force achieved when they converge upon the same persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:NX9","text":"Blame from a despised or unserious source is not affectively equivalent to credible blame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D1","text":"Sympathy occurs when signs and causal inference form an idea of another’s sentiment and self-related relations enliven it into the corresponding impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A2","CH_THN_SXLF:A3","CH_THN_SXLF:A4","CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SXLF:A6","CH_THN_SXLF:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D2","text":"Common humanity makes sympathy generally possible, while specific similarity and proximity regulate its intensity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A3","CH_THN_SXLF:A6","CH_THN_SXLF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D3","text":"Praise generates pride when another’s favorable sentiment is sympathetically reproduced, accepted as evidence, and attached as pleasure to a self-related quality.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A2","CH_THN_SXLF:A3","CH_THN_SXLF:A4","CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SXLF:A6","CH_THN_SXLF:A8","CH_THN_SXLF:A9","CH_THN_SXLF:A10","CH_THN_SXLF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D4","text":"Preference for the approval of competent judges over indiscriminate approval follows from unequal authority and contradicts an undifferentiated fame instinct.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D5","text":"Intimate acquaintance strengthens approbation by increasing both associative proximity and the evidential weight of judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A7","CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D6","text":"Praise incongruent with self-attribution cannot ordinarily establish the pleasure required for pride.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A9","CH_THN_SXLF:A10","CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D7","text":"Separating kinship from contiguity across different evaluators prevents their contempt from forming a unified sympathetic impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A7","CH_THN_SXLF:A14","CH_THN_SXLF:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D8","text":"Concealment of prior rank suppresses unfavorable comparison in observers and prevents private misfortune from being affectively doubled through sympathy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A4","CH_THN_SXLF:A10","CH_THN_SXLF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D9","text":"Numerical accumulation can compensate for low individual esteem by conferring aggregate authority upon popular opinion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D10","text":"Undeserved praise can please when imagination fabricates a provisional self-attribution and sympathetic agreement stabilizes it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A8","CH_THN_SXLF:A10","CH_THN_SXLF:A13","CH_THN_SXLF:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:D11","text":"Contempt can wound without securing assent because the sympathetically received passion conflicts with antecedent pride; the effect disappears when the critic lacks credibility or seriousness.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SXLF:A8","CH_THN_SXLF:A12","CH_THN_SXLF:A16","CH_THN_SXLF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"From","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","text":"Reputation is mediated self-affection: an external evaluation becomes causally effective only by being internally reproduced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","text":"The self is both the source of vivacity and the eventual object of the sentiment that vivacity imports, producing a recursive self–other–self circuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD3","text":"Social evaluation has a relational topology: proximity, authority, convergence, separation, and multiplicity determine affective force more than bare evaluative content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD4","text":"Individual self-esteem and collective uniformity are generated by the same mechanism of communicated sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD5","text":"Social opposition can alter affect without altering belief because sympathetic reception and intellectual assent are separable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SD6","text":"The distinction between original and secondary causes of pride is functionally porous because communicated opinion amplifies the qualities nominally capable of producing pride independently.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX1","text":"Other minds cannot be affectively sealed from one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX2","text":"Ideas cannot be permanently separated from impressions by an absolute difference of kind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX3","text":"Self-evaluation cannot be sovereign or insulated from socially transmitted sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX4","text":"Evaluative influence cannot be reduced to propositional content independently of relational configuration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX5","text":"Relations cannot be inert classifications; they must alter transitions and vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:DNX6","text":"Reason cannot remain neutral when its judgments are connected with passion and self-partiality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SNX1","text":"The system forecloses an autonomous self whose passions originate wholly within its private judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SNX2","text":"Fame has no independent affective substance apart from communicated sentiments and their authority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:SNX3","text":"A neutral tribunal of self-worth is unavailable because both external testimony and internal judgment are modulated by passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS1","text":"Expression → external sign → causal certification → idea of another’s sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS2","text":"Vivid self-impression → human resemblance plus contingent relations → associative transition → transferred vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS3","text":"Enlivened idea → idea–impression threshold crossed → corresponding affection reproduced → sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS4","text":"Evaluative sentiment about self → sympathetic affect plus testimonial evidence → pleasure or pain attached to a self-quality → pride or humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS5","text":"Judge authority plus relational proximity plus self-concurrence plus relational convergence plus numerical accumulation → degree of reputational influence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS6","text":"Distance plus disesteem plus nonconcurrence plus divided relations plus perceived jest → attenuation or interruption of reputational influence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:FGS7","text":"Apparent counterexample → identify compensating authority, imaginative supplementation, or passion-conflict → return variation to the sympathy mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:AF1","text":"Reputational power → rejection of undifferentiated fame instinct → general sympathy principle → vivacity-transfer mechanism → application to praise and blame → differential phenomena → assimilation of objections → communication-of-sentiments thesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME1","text":"Fame is reclassified from an original appetite into a secondary effect of sympathetic communication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME2","text":"Opinions are reclassified as both propositions carrying evidential authority and sentiments capable of affective transmission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME3","text":"Resemblance, contiguity, kinship, and acquaintance become conductors of vivacity rather than merely descriptive relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME4","text":"Reputation becomes a topological phenomenon governed by the arrangement of evaluators around the self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME5","text":"Affective injury is separated from intellectual assent, making resisted contempt psychologically operative.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ME6","text":"Relocation, anonymity, and concealment become mechanisms for altering shame without altering the underlying material condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ALT1","text":"Surface: praise, blame, reputation, and infamy produce differential pride and humiliation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ALT2","text":"Structural: relational networks regulate the conversion of others’ sentiments into self-affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: controlled variations in authority, proximity, concurrence, and relational convergence test sympathy against the rival instinct model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: ideas and impressions occupy a continuum of vivacity, while sympathy unfolds as the compressed sequence sign → idea → enlivening → impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from sympathy’s perceptual mechanism to the secondary production of pride and humility through reputation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT2","text":"The quantitative threshold for idea–impression conversion and the relative weights of authority and relational proximity remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT3","text":"The structure is internally coherent across variations in evaluator status, acquaintance, concurrence, and relational distribution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT4","text":"Its strongest discriminator is the unequal influence of opinions that an original, undifferentiated fame instinct would treat equally.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT5","text":"Reconstruction depends on the prior theories of association and of independent pleasure or pain as causes of pride and humility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT6","text":"The case of consciously undeserved praise requires imaginative fiction to preserve the concurrence condition, introducing an auxiliary mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT7","text":"The response of pride to rejected contempt leaves an unresolved boundary between communication of sentiment and adoption of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT8","text":"The claim of lossless conversion under perfected sympathy coexists with graded ordinary influence without a specified threshold or measure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLF:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the chapter’s real movement only if these qualifications remain visible rather than being absorbed into an unrestricted sympathy principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLF:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 16","title":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame","context":"Chapter 16","original_title":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame","original_context":"Chapter 16"},"original_title":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III Difficulties Solved","order":17,"parents":["RAW_THN_SI_2"],"revision":"CH_THN_SI_2@R001","sha256":"983ad9df8120aa176157e93bedb872b4f6863973277be878b4233652a7f4207a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS1","text":"Problem: reconcile apparent exceptions to the pleasure–pain account of love and hatred and explain their conjunction with further passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS2","text":"Core thesis: affection arises when pleasure or uneasiness is joined to a sufficiently vivid and durable imaginative relation to a person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS3","text":"Argument type: diagnostic, eliminative, and reconstructive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS4","text":"Momentary causation → intention/durability distinction → explanation of transient versus lasting passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS5","text":"Relation, acquaintance, and resemblance → increased vivacity → agreeable agitation → affection, modulated by directional association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS6","text":"Candidate causes of wealth-esteem → elimination of object-pleasure and expected advantage as primary → sympathy → recursive amplification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AS7","text":"Passion-mixture → rejection of identity between love and benevolence → contingent natural conjunction → constructive sympathy as the basis of pity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A1","text":"Love and hatred vary in valence and intensity with the pleasure or uneasiness received from another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A2","text":"Love and hatred require both a relation of ideas to a person and a relation of impressions between pleasure or pain and passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A3","text":"An enduring personal quality connects its pleasure or uneasiness to the person independently of intention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A4","text":"A momentary action requires intention to remain connected to its author after the action disappears.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A5","text":"Intention intensifies injury or service by signifying contempt, hatred, kindness, or esteem toward the recipient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A6","text":"A sufficiently forceful pleasure or pain can activate passion despite only a weak relation of ideas to its personal cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A7","text":"An established passion tends to generate judgments that prevent its diminution.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A8","text":"Ideas and impressions differ principally in force and vivacity, permitting a sufficiently lively idea to become an impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A9","text":"Lively conceptions are agreeable, and conceptions of passions produce especially strong mental agitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A10","text":"Relation, acquaintance, and resemblance increase the force of conception through association, custom, or dispositional correspondence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A11","text":"A personal relation is strongest when imaginative transition between its terms is easy in both directions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A12","text":"Sympathy converts a vivid idea of another’s sentiment into a corresponding impression through resemblance between persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A13","text":"Extension by a general rule requires predominantly uniform experience connecting the relevant condition and effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A14","text":"Possession, power, and utility produce actual or represented satisfaction in their possessor or beneficiary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A15","text":"Power and riches elicit esteem even where the observer can expect no benefit from their possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A16","text":"Sentiments communicated between persons can be reciprocally reflected and amplified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A17","text":"Passions can blend into a compound impression, whereas ideas combine without losing their distinctness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A18","text":"Love and hatred stand in an original but contingent conjunction with benevolence and anger rather than in identity with them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A19","text":"Sympathy can construct the passion customarily associated with a condition even when that passion is absent from the person observed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:A20","text":"Imaginative proximity and contrast intensify sympathetically constructed emotion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0035","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0037","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX1","text":"Mere causal production of pleasure or pain is not sufficient for durable love or hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX2","text":"Intention is not universally necessary for affection toward personal qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX3","text":"Accidental and involuntary harm is not incapable of producing immediate anger.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX4","text":"Acknowledged justice does not necessarily extinguish anger caused by harm.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX5","text":"The opinion of iniquity is not always the antecedent cause of hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX6","text":"Affection arising from relation or acquaintance is not proportioned solely to objective merit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX7","text":"Logical reciprocity between objects does not guarantee equal psychological transition in both directions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX8","text":"Esteem for riches is not principally caused by contemplation of agreeable possessions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0018","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0020","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX9","text":"Esteem for riches is not principally caused by expected personal advantage or a general rule derived from such expectation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX10","text":"Love is not identical with desire for another’s happiness, nor hatred with desire for another’s misery.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX11","text":"The conjunction of love with benevolence and hatred with anger is not conceptually necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX12","text":"Pity does not require antecedent friendship or reflection on one’s own vulnerability to misfortune.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0035","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:NX13","text":"Sympathy does not require the observed person actually to feel the passion communicated to the observer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D1","text":"Enduring qualities produce stable affection without design, whereas transient actions require design to disclose enduring character.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A2","CH_THN_SI_2:A3","CH_THN_SI_2:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D2","text":"Intention strengthens passion twice: by connecting the action to the person and by adding communicated esteem or contempt to its effect.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A4","CH_THN_SI_2:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D3","text":"Accidental harm can produce violent but short-lived anger because the impression remains strong while the personal relation remains weak.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A2","CH_THN_SI_2:A4","CH_THN_SI_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D4","text":"Recognized justice diminishes but need not erase anger because it opposes the painful impression without annihilating it.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A1","CH_THN_SI_2:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D5","text":"Harm can first produce hatred, after which hatred produces an opinion of injury that stabilizes it.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A6","CH_THN_SI_2:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D6","text":"Relation and acquaintance produce affection by making the related or familiar person unusually vivid and therefore agreeable to contemplate.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A9","CH_THN_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D7","text":"Resemblance facilitates affection both by transferring vivacity and by predisposing the observer toward the corresponding emotion.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A10","CH_THN_SI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D8","text":"A competing relation weakens an existing tie when it redirects imagination and obstructs the return transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D9","text":"Agreeable possessions generate esteem chiefly when imagination passes from the possessions to the satisfaction of their possessor.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A10","CH_THN_SI_2:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D10","text":"Expected advantage cannot principally explain wealth-esteem because esteem persists where benefit is impossible and the uniformity required for a general rule is absent.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A13","CH_THN_SI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D11","text":"Sympathy remains the general cause of wealth-esteem because it communicates the proprietor’s satisfaction independently of benefit to the observer.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:A14","CH_THN_SI_2:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D12","text":"The perceived beauty of utility is pleasure communicated from the beneficiary rather than pleasure intrinsic to sensible form.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D13","text":"Wealth produces a feedback circuit in which possession causes satisfaction, communicated satisfaction causes esteem, and perceived esteem increases the possessor’s satisfaction.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A14","CH_THN_SI_2:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D14","text":"Love and hatred acquire practical tendencies through their conjunction with benevolence and anger, not through their own conceptual essence.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A17","CH_THN_SI_2:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D15","text":"Pity for strangers follows from the general human resemblance that permits their sorrow to become a vivid impression in the observer.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:D16","text":"An observed misfortune can produce stronger pity when the sufferer feels less sorrow because general expectation supplies the absent passion and contrast heightens it.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A13","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:A20"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0037","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD1","text":"Personal attribution is temporally organized: durable passion requires either a durable quality or a sign of durable character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD2","text":"Moral accusation can function as an endogenous preservation-mechanism of prior affect rather than as its independent rational cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD3","text":"Kinship, acquaintance, resemblance, and sympathy form one economy of transmitted vivacity whose recurrent output is affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD4","text":"Social attachment depends on the topology of imaginative passage, not merely on the existence of an objective relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD5","text":"Economic rank and utility-based beauty are affective appropriations of satisfaction located initially in another person.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD6","text":"Wealth reproduces its own desirability through recursive exchanges between possessed power, observer esteem, and proprietary vanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD7","text":"The benevolent or destructive direction of love and hatred is naturally installed but not logically or morally entailed by those passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","text":"Sympathy is a constructive simulation capable of exceeding, supplementing, or replacing the actual emotion of its apparent source.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX1","text":"Rational judgment of guilt cannot be the exclusive ground of anger or hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX2","text":"Deliberate intention cannot be the universal gate through which affective attribution passes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX3","text":"Objective merit cannot independently determine affection against the force of familiarity and associative vivacity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX4","text":"The mind cannot be affectively self-sufficient; its animation depends on lively relations to external objects and other minds.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX5","text":"Relations cannot be treated as static, symmetrical properties without regard to the direction and interruption of imaginative movement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX6","text":"Social esteem cannot be reduced to calculated self-interest or probable exchange.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX7","text":"The practical tendencies of passions cannot all be deduced from their conceptual content; some conjunctions remain irreducibly constitutional.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:DNX8","text":"Sympathetic communication cannot be assumed to reproduce another’s inner state with veridical fidelity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SNX1","text":"An affectively neutral standpoint from which persons are judged independently of pleasure, pain, association, and prior passion is unavailable within the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SNX2","text":"Intrinsic social or aesthetic value independent of communicated satisfaction is structurally displaced.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SNX3","text":"No necessary moral order guarantees that love seeks happiness or hatred seeks misery; the alignment rests on contingent nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SNX4","text":"Transparent correspondence between the actual emotion of one mind and the sympathetic emotion of another is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:SNX5","text":"A closed individual mind capable of sustaining pleasure, passion, and value without reflective dependence on other minds is excluded.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS1","text":"Pleasure or uneasiness from another → agreeable or disagreeable impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS2","text":"Enduring quality ∨ intention-as-character-sign → strong person-relation; momentary accident → weak person-relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS3","text":"Impression-relation × person-relation → love or hatred; strong impression × weak relation → acute but unstable passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS4","text":"Intention → communicated esteem or contempt → vanity or mortification → amplified affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS5","text":"Pain-generated passion → threat of diminution by innocence or justice → justificatory injury-judgment → partial stabilization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS6","text":"Self-vivacity → relation, custom, or resemblance → lively idea of another → agreeable agitation → affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS7","text":"Easy outward transition + easy return → strong tie; competing transition + obstructed return → weakened tie.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS8","text":"Possession or power → proprietor satisfaction → sympathetic esteem → perceived esteem → vanity → renewed satisfaction and esteem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS9","text":"Love or hatred + original constitutional coupling → benevolence or anger → blended compound affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:FGS10","text":"Observed condition + human resemblance + customary affect-rule + proximity or contrast → vivid constructed impression → pity despite absent original sorrow.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0037","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AF1","text":"Pleasure–pain rule → intentionality difficulty → quality/action distinction → explanation of passion duration → passion-generated injury judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AF2","text":"Love of relations and acquaintance → search for common cause → force and vivacity → reciprocal-transition model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AF3","text":"Wealth-esteem → competing causal hypotheses → failure of direct object-pleasure and expected advantage → sympathy → recursive status amplification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AF4","text":"Compound passions → identity hypothesis → experiential rejection → original contingent conjunction of love with benevolence and hatred with anger.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:AF5","text":"Pity without friendship → sympathy through resemblance → communication of sorrow → passion without an actual original → constructive imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0035","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME1","text":"Agency is reclassified from immediate causal authorship to durable attribution through character or intention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME2","text":"The judgment of injury becomes potentially an effect of hatred rather than its necessary antecedent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME3","text":"Kinship, familiarity, and resemblance become variants of one vivacity-producing mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME4","text":"Relations become directional pathways of imagination rather than merely reciprocal logical connections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME5","text":"Esteem for wealth is relocated from rational advantage to vicarious participation in proprietary satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME6","text":"Utility-based beauty is relocated from sensible form to sympathetic apprehension of another’s benefit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME7","text":"Love and hatred are separated from the benevolent and destructive desires that ordinarily accompany them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ME8","text":"Sympathy is expanded from emotional transmission to affective construction under association, general rules, and contrast.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0037","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ALT1","text":"Surface: variations in anger, affection, social esteem, benevolence, and pity are treated as test cases for the passion-system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ALT2","text":"Structural: double relations, force and vivacity, directional association, sympathy, and recursive reflection generate the observed passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0027","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: apparent counterexamples are converted into discriminating tests that eliminate rival mechanisms and qualify the governing hypothesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: ideas and impressions differ by degree of force; enduring qualities sustain personal attribution, while transient events produce unstable passions unless mediated by intention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the principal transitions from personal causation through association and sympathy to compound affection and pity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT2","text":"Reconstruction preserves the operative movement: anomaly → distinction → rival elimination → revised mechanism → extension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT3","text":"Internal coherence depends on accepting force and vivacity as the common currency connecting association, pleasure, impression, and passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT4","text":"The structure is strongest where rival explanations generate discriminating cases, especially intention, impossible benefit, and love–desire identity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT5","text":"The relative weights of harm, justice, intention, associative strength, and individual disposition remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT6","text":"Appeal to an original constitutional conjunction preserves the phenomenon of benevolence and anger at the cost of further causal derivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7","text":"Sympathy shifts from reproducing actual emotion to constructing absent emotion without a fully specified criterion governing the shift.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0036","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT8","text":"Malice is classified as a secondary affection but is not derived with the completeness given to pity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SI_2:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SI_2:MRT9","text":"Compression removes illustrative cases without removing the distinctions, eliminations, feedback structures, or terminal tensions required to regenerate the argument.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 17","title":"Sect. III Difficulties Solved","context":"Chapter 17","original_title":"Sect. III Difficulties Solved","original_context":"Chapter 17"},"original_title":"Sect. III Difficulties Solved","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","order":18,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVME"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVME@R001","sha256":"b75f58586d12fa7dea441030fae19fc49850b4079da82f325f4d255301c78022","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS1","text":"Problem: explain unprovoked pleasure in another’s misery and pain in another’s enjoyment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS2","text":"Core thesis: malice and envy arise when comparison converts another’s condition into an inverse estimate of one’s own.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS3","text":"Argument type: reconstructive psychological diagnosis grounded in affective succession and associative transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS4","text":"Comparative judgments vary because comparison alters an idea’s attendant emotion, which custom then attributes to the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS5","text":"Comparison becomes affectively operative only where relations permit easy transition between ideas and their impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AS6","text":"Movement: comparative distortion → altered self-estimate → reverse affect → malice/envy differentiation → relation-conditioned general law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A1","text":"Human sentiments ordinarily assign value through comparison rather than intrinsic assessment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A2","text":"Every sensed object or imagined idea is accompanied by an emotion proportioned to it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A3","text":"A succeeding emotion is magnified or diminished by contrast with the preceding emotion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A4","text":"Habitual general rules carry the mind from a degree of emotion to the object’s customary corresponding degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A5","text":"Satisfaction with one’s condition varies with its apparent fortune.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A6","text":"In comparison, one term alters the felt value of the other in a direction contrary to its own direct affective effect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A7","text":"Associative relation between ideas enables an easy transition of imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A8","text":"Easy transition preserves the first object’s impression during passage to the second.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A9","text":"Preserved impressions can modify one another through comparison or affective contrariety.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A10","text":"Envy is pain produced when another’s present enjoyment comparatively diminishes one’s own apparent condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A11","text":"Malice is the unprovoked desire to produce another’s misery for the pleasure of comparative self-advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:A12","text":"A predominant passion assimilates affective additions to its own direction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX1","text":"Malice is not hatred caused by antecedent offense or injury.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX2","text":"Affective judgment is not governed chiefly by reason or intrinsic value.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX3","text":"Comparative variation is not produced by alteration of the immediate impression, image, or idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX4","text":"The direct survey of another’s condition is not affectively equivalent to comparison with that condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX5","text":"Envy is not proportionate to the absolute magnitude of another’s superiority.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX6","text":"Proximity in merit alone is not sufficient for envy without supporting relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX7","text":"Unrelated ideas cannot substantially affect one another merely through juxtaposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:NX8","text":"Contrary passions do not encounter or neutralize one another when their objects remain associatively separate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D1","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A2 + CH_THN_SVME:A3 + CH_THN_SVME:A4) Comparison can change judgment while leaving representation unchanged because contrast alters the attendant emotion and custom attributes that alteration to the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D2","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A1 + CH_THN_SVME:A5 + CH_THN_SVME:A6) Another’s misery elevates one’s apparent happiness and produces pleasure, while another’s happiness diminishes it and produces pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D3","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A6) The same misery can be painful under direct survey and pleasurable under comparative survey.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D4","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A1 + CH_THN_SVME:A3 + CH_THN_SVME:A5) Past pain becomes agreeable when contrasted with present satisfaction, while past pleasure becomes painful when contrasted with present deficiency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D5","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A1 + CH_THN_SVME:A3 + CH_THN_SVME:A12) Self-inflicted suffering can remove a contrast that would otherwise intensify predominant grief or the imagined punishment of remorse.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D6","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A1 + CH_THN_SVME:A3 + CH_THN_SVME:A5 + CH_THN_SVME:A10) An inferior’s approach converts decreased comparative pleasure into positive pain by comparing the reduced advantage with its former degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D7","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A7 + CH_THN_SVME:A8 + CH_THN_SVME:A9 + CH_THN_SVME:A10) Nearby or resembling rivals provoke more envy than remote superiors because proximity sustains the transition required for comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D8","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A7 + CH_THN_SVME:A8 + CH_THN_SVME:A9) Separating related objects into distinct conceptual units prevents their attendant passions from influencing or contradicting one another.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:D9","text":"(from CH_THN_SVME:A7 + CH_THN_SVME:A8 + CH_THN_SVME:A10) The mind seeks relief from related superiority either by breaking the association or by removing the superiority itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD1","text":"Evaluative magnitude is relationally produced even where the perceived object and its representation remain invariant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD2","text":"Malice converts another’s suffering into an instrument for changing one’s own apparent position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD3","text":"Envy follows associative proximity more reliably than absolute inequality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD4","text":"Comparative passions are recursively temporal: a loss of former comparative pleasure can itself become a new source of pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD5","text":"Associative organization governs whether passions interact across personal, social, political, and aesthetic domains.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SD6","text":"Emotional distance can be produced by weakening relations even when objective superiority remains unchanged.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX1","text":"Affective self-estimation is not insulated from the fortunes of comparable others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX2","text":"Identity of representation does not entail identity of judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX3","text":"Objective inequality cannot by itself generate envy without an associative route of comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX4","text":"Natural contrariety between passions cannot ensure their interaction without continuity between their ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX5","text":"Sensory invariance cannot reliably correct judgments imposed by habitual effect-to-cause association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:DNX6","text":"Evil need not be desired as an independent end for malicious action to arise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SNX1","text":"The system supplies no affectively operative intrinsic baseline capable of stabilizing self-worth against comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SNX2","text":"No autonomous faculty of malevolence is required; comparative self-augmentation occupies its explanatory place.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SNX3","text":"The boundary of operative relation has no independent metric and appears only where transition succeeds or fails.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:SNX4","text":"The system does not fully specify what determines whether another’s condition is surveyed directly or comparatively.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS1","text":"Object O → attendant emotion E(O) proportioned to O.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS2","text":"Relation R(O1,O2) → easy transition O1→O2 → preservation of E(O1).","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS3","text":"Preserved E(O1) + succeeding E(O2) → contrast → altered E′(O2).","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS4","text":"Altered E′(O2) + habitual emotion–magnitude rule → altered judgment of O2 despite invariant representation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS5","text":"Another’s misery compared with self → augmented apparent self-condition → pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS6","text":"Another’s enjoyment compared with self → diminished apparent self-condition → pain → envy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS7","text":"Anticipated misery of another → anticipated comparative elevation of self → desire to produce misery → malice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS8","text":"Strong relation → sustained comparison and impression interaction; broken relation → affective separation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:FGS9","text":"Former comparative advantage A0 → reduced advantage A1 → comparison A1<A0 → lost pleasure converted into pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AF1","text":"Same object/different estimate → attendant-emotion hypothesis → contrast amplification → habitual projection of altered emotion onto the object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:AF2","text":"Comparative self-estimation → inverse response to others’ fortunes → envy/malice distinction → proximity and relation as affective gates → general law of impression transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME1","text":"Malice and envy are reclassified from responses to moral injury as products of comparative self-estimation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME2","text":"Evaluative change is relocated from the object or idea to its attendant impression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME3","text":"Social hierarchy is redrawn through associative proximity rather than absolute rank alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME4","text":"Distance becomes an affective mechanism capable of disabling comparison.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME5","text":"Direct valence and comparative valence become distinct: what is painful in itself may become pleasurable through contrast.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ME6","text":"Personal envy, political rivalry, aesthetic consistency, and passion-conflict become instances of one relation-mediated transition law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ALT1","text":"Surface: malice and envy are differentiated by whether another’s misery is already present or deliberately sought for comparative pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ALT2","text":"Structural: comparison, emotional succession, habitual attribution, and associative relation jointly produce altered judgments and passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: explanation shifts from rational or moral appraisal to the formation, severance, and manipulation of comparative relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: ideas carry attendant impressions whose effects depend on sequence, preserved transition, present comparison, and remembered prior condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SVME:A1–CH_THN_SVME:A12 are sufficient to reconstruct the perceptual mechanism, passion differentiation, and relational boundary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT2","text":"The relative activation of direct survey and comparative survey remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT3","text":"The kinds and degrees of relation required for effective comparison lack a formal threshold.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT4","text":"The structure is internally coherent if proportional attendant emotion and habitual effect-to-cause attribution are granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT5","text":"The strongest explanatory linkage is the relation-conditioned transfer from stable representation to variable affective judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT6","text":"The extension from magnitude and number to virtue, wealth, happiness, and misery depends on analogical generalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT7","text":"The inference from absent relation preventing an effect establishes relation as contributory or necessary, not independently sufficient.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT8","text":"The self-affliction cases require the auxiliary predominance principle rather than following from comparison alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVME:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement from comparative perception through reverse affect to the general associative law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVME:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 18","title":"Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","context":"Chapter 18","original_title":"Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","original_context":"Chapter 18"},"original_title":"Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals","order":19,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXLH"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXLH@R001","sha256":"3a99be11cec27c64fa436cbce9d14add164015776d4a99b54c0bf9499ae41b70","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS1","text":"Problem: whether the preceding causal system of love and hatred extends beyond humanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS2","text":"Thesis: animal affections arise from simple, species-general principles continuous with those governing human passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS3","text":"Argument type: comparative, empirical, and confirmatory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS4","text":"Movement: reduced animal reflection → isolation of sensible causes → qualified relational causes → instinct → sympathy → comparative passion prevalence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS5","text":"Cognitive demand determines which affective mechanisms operate strongly in animals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AS6","text":"The recurrence of the predicted passions under reduced cognition corroborates the foregoing system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A1","text":"Love and hatred are common to the whole sensitive creation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A2","text":"The operative causes of love and hatred require no force of reflection or penetration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A3","text":"The springs governing love and hatred are not peculiar to humanity or any single animal species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A4","text":"Animals are only weakly susceptible to pleasures and pains of imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A5","text":"Animals judge affective objects by the sensible good or evil those objects produce.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A6","text":"Animal love is not restricted to members of the animal’s own species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A7","text":"The affective force of relation is limited by the animal’s capacity to trace relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A8","text":"Cognitively obvious associative relations generate affection in animals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A9","text":"Parental affection toward offspring arises from a peculiar instinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A10","text":"Sympathy communicates passions among animals without knowledge of the initiating cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A11","text":"Animals possess a sense of one another’s pain and pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A12","text":"Sympathetic conjunction intensifies animal passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:A13","text":"Envy and malice require less thought and imagination than pity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX1","text":"Love and hatred are not exclusively human passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX2","text":"Their production does not require reflective penetration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX3","text":"Animal love is not bounded by identity of species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX4","text":"Animal affection is not governed primarily by imagined goods and evils.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX5","text":"Relation is not wholly absent from animal affection, though its influence is cognitively restricted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX6","text":"Parental affection is not reduced to acquired benefit or traced relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX7","text":"Sympathy does not require cognition of the original cause of a passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX8","text":"Restraint toward companions is not explained solely by fear of retaliation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:NX9","text":"Pity is not assumed to be more primitive or prevalent than envy and malice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D1","text":"Benefits generate animal love and injuries generate animal hatred.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D2","text":"Relation is weaker in animals than in humans but remains effective through acquaintance, resemblance, and other obvious associations.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXLH:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D3","text":"Species identity is neither a necessary nor sufficient boundary for an animal’s objects of affection.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A3","CH_THN_SXLH:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D4","text":"Parental attachment constitutes an instinctive branch distinct from ordinary sensible and associative causation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXLH:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D5","text":"Passions can organize animal social conduct without shared knowledge of their originating object.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXLH:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D6","text":"Collective animation exceeds isolated passion when animals act in sympathetic conjunction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXLH:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D7","text":"Envy and malice may be more common among animals than pity because their cognitive threshold is lower.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A4","CH_THN_SXLH:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:D8","text":"The persistence of love, hatred, and sympathy under diminished reflection supports their derivation from simple, species-general springs.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A1","CH_THN_SXLH:A2","CH_THN_SXLH:A3","CH_THN_SXLH:A4","CH_THN_SXLH:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","text":"Animal psychology functions as a cognitive-subtraction test: mechanisms surviving reduced reflection cannot depend essentially on reflective human faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","text":"Affective universality is compatible with unequal cognitive complexity because common passions can be produced through differently available channels.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SD3","text":"The chapter treats lower cognitive cost as a condition of wider animal prevalence among passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SD4","text":"Social intensification of affect precedes and exceeds conscious representation of its causal origin.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SD5","text":"The unity of the system lies in species-general springs rather than in a single uniform route of affective production.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX1","text":"Reflective self-consciousness cannot be a necessary condition of love and hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX2","text":"Species-specific human faculties cannot constitute the ultimate source of the passions shared with animals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX3","text":"Abstract relation-tracing cannot be required for every form of associative attachment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX4","text":"Affective communication cannot depend on propositional knowledge of what caused the communicated passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX5","text":"Animal social restraint cannot be exhausted by retaliation avoidance if it evidences sensitivity to another’s pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX6","text":"A wholly acquired or associative account cannot explain parental affection if peculiar instinct is irreducible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:DNX7","text":"Greater moral valuation cannot be assumed to entail greater natural prevalence; pity may be rarer because it is cognitively costlier.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SNX1","text":"An ontological rupture between human and animal affective life is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SNX2","text":"Direct animal self-testimony is unavailable; behavioral analogy must bear the attribution of internal passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SNX3","text":"Conscious causal representation is unavailable as the universal ground of social affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","text":"A unitary reduction of affection to sensible reward and injury is foreclosed by relation, instinct, and sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS1","text":"Species-general causes + cognitive simplicity → prediction of animal applicability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS2","text":"Reduced reflection and imagination → isolation of sensible affective causation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS3","text":"Sensible good or benefit → love; sensible evil or injury → hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS4","text":"Limited relation-tracing + obvious association → restricted relational affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS5","text":"Parent–offspring configuration → instinctive affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS6","text":"Perceived passion or suffering → sympathetic communication → coordinated or intensified affect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS7","text":"Reduced imaginative demand → greater probable prevalence of envy and malice relative to pity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:FGS8","text":"Convergence of animal mechanisms with prior predictions → confirmation of the foregoing system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:AF1","text":"Foregoing causal system → animal test case → cognitive restriction → sensible causation → qualified association and instinct → sympathetic transmission → cognitive-cost comparison → systemic corroboration.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME1","text":"The human–animal opposition is redrawn as continuity of affective mechanism with variation in cognitive degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME2","text":"Species membership ceases to define the possible object-range of love.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME3","text":"Animal affection is reclassified into sensible, associative, instinctive, and sympathetic causal channels.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME4","text":"Relation becomes cognitively graded rather than simply present or absent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME5","text":"Collective animal animation becomes visible as transmitted and amplified passion rather than mere aggregation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ME6","text":"Comparative prevalence among passions becomes explicable through differing demands on thought and imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ALT1","text":"Surface: animals exhibit love, hatred, sympathy, envy, malice, and sensitivity to one another’s suffering.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ALT2","text":"Structural: sensible effects, obvious associations, instinct, and sympathetic transmission form distinct affective mechanisms.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: animal conduct tests whether the preceding theory depends on specifically human reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ALT4","text":"Ontological: sensitive life forms a continuous affective domain across species.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:ALT5","text":"Epistemic: internal animal passions are inferred from patterned conduct and analogy with human experience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s movement from cross-species continuity to confirmation of the prior system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT2","text":"The precise mechanisms converting sensible good, resemblance, and communicated emotion into affection remain underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent only if patterned animal conduct warrants attribution of passions analogous to human passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT4","text":"The argument is strongest where reduced animal reflection independently tests the claimed simplicity of affective causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT5","text":"The restriction of animal judgment to sensible good and evil is not explicitly reconciled with acquaintance and resemblance as additional causes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT6","text":"Peculiar parental instinct introduces a causal exception without integration into the foregoing system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT7","text":"Cross-species preference and conspecific preference are both asserted, but the conditions governing their relative strength remain unspecified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT8","text":"Human analogy explains animal sympathy while animal sympathy confirms the human system, creating a partially reciprocal evidential structure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXLH:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement only if its universal claims are retained despite their compression from selective observations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXLH:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 19","title":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals","context":"Chapter 19","original_title":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals","original_context":"Chapter 19"},"original_title":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom","order":20,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVEC"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVEC@R001","sha256":"83c276b42ed267646bdb20b1bde3d71a522ad10255ea47e9df46c24f55b433af","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS1","text":"The problem is to explain how custom can intensify or diminish passion and reverse pleasure and pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS2","text":"All effects of custom are reduced to facility and subsequent inclination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS3","text":"The argument is a causal-psychological reduction through variations in mental motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS4","text":"Unfamiliarity produces difficulty, agitation, novelty, and amplified passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS5","text":"Repetition removes novelty and produces tranquillity before generating pleasurable facility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS6","text":"Moderate facility can reconcile the mind to pain, whereas excessive facility destroys interest and can reverse pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AS7","text":"Facility and inclination jointly weaken passive habits while strengthening active habits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A1","text":"Facility and inclination exhaust the original effects of custom.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A2","text":"Repetition first produces facility in action or conception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A3","text":"Repetition subsequently produces inclination toward the repeated action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A4","text":"Unaccustomed action or conception encounters resistance from unpliable faculties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A5","text":"Mental resistance excites the spirits and generates the emotions of novelty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A6","text":"Moderate enlivening of the mind is pleasurable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A7","text":"An emotion attending a passion is readily converted into and augments that passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A8","text":"Repetition extinguishes novelty, agitation, and their amplification of passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A9","text":"Moderate facility produces pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A10","text":"The pleasure of facility consists in orderly motion rather than agitated motion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A11","text":"Excessive facility renders mental action faint, languid, and unable to sustain interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A12","text":"Custom-induced aversion ordinarily requires an object naturally attended by emotion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A13","text":"Extinction of an expected agreeable emotion disposes the mind toward the opposite affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A14","text":"An entirely disagreeable action cannot become an object of inclination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A15","text":"Facility weakens passive habits by rendering the motion of the spirits faint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A16","text":"Active performance supplies independent support to the spirits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:A17","text":"Customary inclination adds force to active performance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX1","text":"Custom does not affect passion monotonically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX2","text":"The pleasure of novelty is not the pleasure of facility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX3","text":"Orderly facility does not augment painful affection as novelty-agitation does.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX4","text":"Frequent repetition does not ordinarily make affectively neutral objects disagreeable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX5","text":"Custom cannot create inclination toward what remains entirely disagreeable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:NX6","text":"Active and passive habits are not strengthened or weakened by the same mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D1","text":"Novel objects produce more pleasure or pain than their intrinsic affective character supplies.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A4","CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A6","CH_THN_SVEC:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D2","text":"Early repetition diminishes passion by removing the agitation that had amplified it.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SVEC:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D3","text":"Moderate facility can convert initial harshness into relish because its pleasure counteracts rather than inflames pain.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D4","text":"Excessive repetition can convert pleasure into pain by extinguishing the emotion required to sustain interest.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SVEC:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D5","text":"Affectively neutral repeated objects normally remain non-aversive because custom has no natural emotion to destroy.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D6","text":"Custom produces inclination only where residual disagreeability does not amount to complete repugnance.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SVEC:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D7","text":"Passive habits decline under repetition, while active habits acquire additional force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A15","CH_THN_SVEC:A16","CH_THN_SVEC:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:D8","text":"The affective trajectory of repetition is non-linear: amplification gives way to tranquillity, pleasure, languor, and possible reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A8","CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","text":"Experienced valence depends on the history and degree of encounter, not solely on the object’s natural affective character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","text":"Custom operates through successive regimes rather than through cumulative reinforcement alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SD3","text":"Difficulty and facility are affectively reversible: either can support or weaken the mind according to degree and mode of operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SD4","text":"Repetition redistributes affective force between passive receptivity and active tendency rather than merely increasing familiarity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:DNX1","text":"Intrinsic pleasure or pain cannot by itself determine the experienced strength or direction of passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:DNX2","text":"Facility cannot be inherently pleasurable, because its excess must be capable of producing languor and aversion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:DNX3","text":"Diminished excitation cannot have a uniform effect across passive reception and active performance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:DNX4","text":"Repetition cannot explain its own effects without distinctions of degree, affective endowment, and activity type.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SNX1","text":"A fixed subject-object relation of affect, insulated from temporal repetition, is unavailable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SNX2","text":"A single continuous law by which increasing custom always increases or always decreases passion is foreclosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:SNX3","text":"Custom is denied unlimited transformative power by the irreducible boundary of complete disagreeability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS1","text":"Custom/repetition → facility + subsequent inclination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS2","text":"Unfamiliarity → resistance → excitation → novelty-pleasure → conversion into the predominant passion → affective amplification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS3","text":"Repetition → novelty-decay → tranquillity → moderate facility → orderly motion → pleasure → possible pain-to-pleasure reversal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS4","text":"Continued repetition → excessive facility → faintness and languor → destruction of sustaining emotion → indifference → possible opposite affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS5","text":"Inclination + complete disagreeability → blocked tendency; inclination + tolerable action → strengthened tendency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:FGS6","text":"Passive mode + facility → weakened habit; active mode + self-supported spirits + inclination → strengthened habit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AF1","text":"Opposed effects of custom → reduction to facility and inclination → novelty-agitation mechanism → repetition and tranquillity → moderate facility and relish → excessive facility and affective reversal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:AF2","text":"Inclination effect → disagreeability boundary → active/passive distinction → active strengthening and passive weakening.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ME1","text":"Repetition is reclassified from a uniform influence into a threshold-dependent process with contrary phases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ME2","text":"Affective force is divided between the object’s natural valence and the mental motion attending its apprehension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ME3","text":"Novelty and familiarity cease to be simple opposites and become distinct regimes of excitation and order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ME4","text":"Habit is divided into passive diminution and active reinforcement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ME5","text":"Pleasure and pain become reversible outcomes of altered mental operation rather than fixed responses to objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ALT1","text":"Surface: Custom increases, diminishes, and reverses passions while differentiating active from passive habits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ALT2","text":"Structural: Resistance, agitation, orderly motion, languor, and tendency mediate custom’s effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Diverse and apparently contradictory phenomena are reduced to two primary operations governed by degree and mode.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Affect is a path-dependent process whose present force is constituted by successive encounters.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s principal causal sequence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT2","text":"The boundary between moderate and excessive facility remains quantitatively undetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT3","text":"The language of the spirits supplies a causal vocabulary without a fully specified mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT4","text":"The transition from acquired facility to inclination is asserted more strongly than it is derived.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT5","text":"The structure is strongest where one graded mechanism explains both affective reconciliation and affective exhaustion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT6","text":"The active/passive divergence depends on the compressed claim that active performance supports its own excitation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT7","text":"The scope boundary between pain convertible by facility and complete disagreeability resistant to inclination remains unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT8","text":"The system is internally coherent if attendant-emotion conversion and degree-sensitive facility are granted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVEC:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement from dual-effect reduction through facility’s phases to the final habit bifurcation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVEC:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 20","title":"Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom","context":"Chapter 20","original_title":"Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom","original_context":"Chapter 20"},"original_title":"Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth","order":21,"parents":["RAW_THN_SXCL"],"revision":"CH_THN_SXCL@R001","sha256":"2554e1b2b7fc3c20f3cf49d5b569e4baf3e9c3a396bd015c61ad98987d0dca3e","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS1","text":"Problem: explain why truth motivates inquiry when truth and assurance alone do not reliably produce pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS2","text":"Thesis: scientific curiosity is generated by qualified acts of discovery, not by truth considered intrinsically.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS3","text":"Argument type: differential psychological genealogy reinforced by cross-domain analogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS4","text":"Active inquiry derives its principal pleasure from exercised understanding, while perceived importance fixes attention and success completes the pursuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS5","text":"Passion originating in pursuit transfers affective concern to the pursued end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS6","text":"Non-investigative curiosity has a distinct source: belief vivifies and stabilizes an engaging idea, relieving the pain of doubt.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AS7","text":"Movement: intrinsic-truth hypothesis → differential test → composite pursuit mechanism → analogical validation → bifurcation of curiosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A1","text":"Equal truth and equal assurance can yield unequal pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A2","text":"Exercised genius and understanding in difficult discovery are the principal source of scientific pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A3","text":"Attention is necessary for cognitive exercise to produce satisfaction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A4","text":"Perceived importance functions as a condition for fixing attention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A5","text":"Perceived importance contributes little direct pleasure independently of its effect on attention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A6","text":"Imagined utility can operate through faint sympathy without genuine concern for its beneficiaries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A7","text":"Some success in attaining the pursued end is required to complete the pleasure of inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A8","text":"Passion originating in action and pursuit naturally produces derivative concern for the related end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A9","text":"The active-inquiry mechanism applies both to abstract relations and to real connections and existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A10","text":"Non-investigative curiosity arises from a principle distinct from exercised inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A11","text":"Belief vivifies an idea in the imagination.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A12","text":"Belief fixes an idea against hesitation and uncertainty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A13","text":"A vivid idea gives pleasure by affecting the imagination like a moderate passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A14","text":"Doubt produces pain by rapidly varying thought among competing ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:A15","text":"Instability generates curiosity when interest, relation, greatness, or novelty makes an idea sufficiently forceful and proximate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX1","text":"Truth is not desired merely as truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX2","text":"Demonstrative superiority or increased assurance is not sufficient to increase epistemic pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX3","text":"Objective difficulty is not valuable when knowledge is acquired without cognitive exertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX4","text":"Exercised understanding alone is not sufficient without attention-fixing importance and some success.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX5","text":"A requirement that inquiry appear publicly useful does not establish public spirit in the inquirer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX6","text":"Utility is not the primary direct passion sustaining scientific inquiry.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX7","text":"Concern for an end need not precede the activity directed toward it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX8","text":"Informational curiosity is not reducible to self-interest or active study.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:NX9","text":"Curiosity attaches neither to every fact nor exclusively to facts of practical interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D1","text":"Variations in epistemic pleasure must be attributed to qualities of discovery rather than to correctness or certainty alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A1","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D2","text":"Scientific enjoyment requires the conjunction of demanding cognitive activity, engaged attention, and partial attainment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D3","text":"A philosopher can require belief in public utility while remaining indifferent to the public good because imagined sympathy supplies attention rather than a terminal motive.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A5","CH_THN_SXCL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D4","text":"Error and disappointment become painful even when the pursued end was initially indifferent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D5","text":"Philosophy, hunting, and gaming instantiate a common architecture in which a represented stake activates attention and difficult pursuit generates concern.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D6","text":"The affective structure of active inquiry remains constant across mathematical, moral, political, and natural-philosophical objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D7","text":"Non-investigative curiosity seeks a vivid and settled conception when an already engaging idea remains unstable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A10","CH_THN_SXCL:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A13","CH_THN_SXCL:A14","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:D8","text":"Familiarity increases curiosity by strengthening relation and ideational force, while fading representation extinguishes it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD1","text":"Conditions can be motivationally indispensable without being original sources of pleasure: importance engages attention, and success consummates an independently generated pursuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD2","text":"Epistemic motivation forms a recursive circuit: represented importance fixes attention → cognitive activity becomes pleasurable → concern transfers to the end → success and error acquire affective force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD3","text":"Apparently disinterested inquiry can arise without benevolence or material interest through the conjunction of imaginative sympathy and exercised understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD4","text":"Truth functions affectively in two distinct ways: as the endpoint of active achievement and as the content of stabilizing belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD5","text":"Operational uncertainty can intensify active pursuit, while doxastic vacillation produces pain and demands closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SD6","text":"Epistemic value within the system depends less on an object considered alone than on its relation to effort, attention, imagination, and temporal stability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX1","text":"Epistemic motivation cannot transparently track truth as an intrinsic good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX2","text":"Cognition cannot be affectively self-sufficient or independent of attention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX3","text":"Objective utility cannot be identical with subjective concern.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX4","text":"Every passion cannot originate in the antecedent value of its end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX5","text":"Belief cannot be treated as merely representational; it modifies pleasure, pain, and ideational stability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:DNX6","text":"Curiosity cannot be determined by informational content alone, independently of relation, novelty, familiarity, and memory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SNX1","text":"The system excludes an autonomous, purely truth-directed intellect.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SNX2","text":"The singular name “love of truth” does not correspond to a single causal passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SNX3","text":"No object-side property of truth guarantees attention, pleasure, or pursuit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SNX4","text":"The account of informational curiosity contains no explicit truth-sensitive discriminator between true belief and a false belief that equally vivifies and fixes an idea.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:SNX5","text":"A normative reason why truth ought to be pursued remains absent; the system supplies an affective genealogy rather than an epistemic justification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS1","text":"Truth and assurance held constant + pleasure varied by mode of attainment → truth-as-such excluded as sufficient cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS2","text":"Perceived utility → faint imaginative sympathy → attention fixed; difficulty → exercised understanding; attention × exercise × success → scientific pleasure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS3","text":"Pleasure in pursuit → relational transfer of passion → acquired concern for end → pain at error or failure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS4","text":"Philosophy / hunting / gaming → represented stake + demanding pursuit → conjoint effect unavailable from either factor alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS5","text":"Abstract relations or real existence → same active-inquiry architecture → general scientific curiosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:FGS6","text":"Interest, relation, greatness, or novelty → forceful unstable idea → doubt and painful alternation → demand for belief → vivification + fixation → pleasure and relief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AF1","text":"Neglected passion → division of truth → intrinsic-truth explanation falsified → mental exercise identified → importance reclassified as attentional support → success added → concern transferred to the end → analogical confirmation → extension across sciences → separate informational curiosity diagnosed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:AF2","text":"Curiosity → active discovery branch: exertion + attention + success → pleasure; informational branch: ideational force + instability → painful doubt → stabilizing belief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME1","text":"Truth is reclassified from an intrinsically desired object into an endpoint mediated by affective and cognitive operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME2","text":"Utility is reclassified from terminal motive to imaginative support for attention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME3","text":"Disinterested inquiry becomes intelligible without attribution of benevolence or material self-interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME4","text":"Philosophy, hunting, and gaming become structurally comparable as stake-mediated pursuits.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME5","text":"Curiosity divides into active challenge-seeking and passive uncertainty-relief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ME6","text":"Belief becomes visible as an affective operation of vivification and stabilization, not merely assent to content.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ALT1","text":"Surface — Variable pleasure in scientific discovery and the desire to know otherwise irrelevant facts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ALT2","text":"Structural — Attention mediates between represented importance and pleasurable cognitive exertion; belief mediates between unstable ideas and affective relief.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — The argument decomposes motivation into principal source, enabling condition, completion condition, and acquired end-concern.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The mind is treated as a succession of ideas whose vivacity, fixation, rapid alteration, and relational transfer generate passions over time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct both the active-inquiry mechanism and the distinct informational-curiosity mechanism.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT2","text":"Reconstruction depends on prior theories of belief, sympathy, attention, and the relational transfer of passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent where it distinguishes original pleasure from attentional conditions and acquired concern for ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT4","text":"Its strongest operation is the controlled separation of truth and assurance from cognitive exertion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT5","text":"The role of faint sympathy is compressed: its capacity to support attention without genuine concern is asserted more than generated.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT6","text":"The informational branch explains desire for settled belief more directly than desire for truth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT7","text":"The opposed valences of pursuit-uncertainty and belief-instability remain differentiated but not fully unified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SXCL:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the argument only if the final bifurcation of curiosity is retained rather than reduced to a single truth-drive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SXCL:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 21","title":"Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth","context":"Chapter 21","original_title":"Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth","original_context":"Chapter 21"},"original_title":"Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI","layer":"CH","title":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice","order":22,"parents":["RAW_THN_PIJI"],"revision":"CH_THN_PIJI@R001","sha256":"a67eca46add21f1a06d2888b20d39c1269a2b52733630b716bad8496ffd0e88a","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS1","text":"The problem is to explain both the establishment of justice and the subsequent attribution of moral virtue to its observance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS2","text":"Justice is an artificial but necessary and non-arbitrary virtue generated by human convention under determinate social conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS3","text":"The argument is eliminative, genealogical, functional, and sentimentalist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS4","text":"Semantic disambiguation of nature leads to an affective criterion of morality, which leads to the priority of motive over action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS5","text":"The circularity of making regard for justice its own first motive forces the elimination of self-love, public benevolence, and private benevolence as original motives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS6","text":"Human weakness, partial affection, transferable scarcity, and social dependence generate a reciprocal convention stabilizing possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AS7","text":"Common interest generates observance; sympathy with public interest generates moral approval; education, reputation, and government reinforce the result.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A1","text":"Virtue is marked by satisfaction and vice by uneasiness produced under general contemplation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A2","text":"Moral merit belongs ultimately to the motive signified by an action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A3","text":"The first virtuous motive for an action must precede and differ from regard for that action’s morality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A4","text":"Standards of duty track the ordinary force and direction of passions in human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A5","text":"Human affection ordinarily prioritizes self, relations, acquaintances, and strangers in descending order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0013","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A6","text":"Society is necessary to compensate human weakness and secure the satisfaction of human wants.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0016","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A7","text":"External goods are scarce relative to desire, transferable, and insecure against appropriation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A8","text":"Uncultivated moral judgment conforms to natural partiality rather than correcting it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0020","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A9","text":"Acquisitive self-interest can be restrained only by its reflective redirection toward the greater advantages of social preservation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A10","text":"Stability of possession arises through mutually known and conditionally reciprocal resolutions grounded in common interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A11","text":"Convention does not depend upon a prior promise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A12","text":"Convention acquires force gradually through repeated experience and increasing confidence in reciprocal conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A13","text":"Justice, injustice, right, and obligation arise only after possession has been stabilized by convention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A14","text":"Property is a moral relation constituted by the laws of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A15","text":"The whole scheme of justice is socially and individually advantageous even when particular just acts are harmful.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A16","text":"Interest in preserving the general scheme supplies the natural obligation to justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A17","text":"Sympathy with public interest supplies the moral approbation of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:A18","text":"The rules of justice are artificial without being arbitrary because their invention is necessary and obvious under human circumstances.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX1","text":"Natural and unnatural do not mark the boundary between virtue and vice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX2","text":"The external performance is not itself the bearer of moral merit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX3","text":"Regard for the justice of an action cannot be that action’s first virtuous motive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX4","text":"Justice is not an innate passion capable of originally producing equitable conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0020","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX5","text":"Unrestrained private interest is not a universal motive to honest action.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX6","text":"An affection for humanity merely as humanity is not an operative universal passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX7","text":"Private benevolence cannot ground invariant property rights or obligations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX8","text":"Direct concern for public interest is not the original ordinary motive of just conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX9","text":"Justice is not founded upon eternal and immutable relations of ideas discovered by reason.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0030","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX10","text":"Convention is not a promise and cannot be founded upon promises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX11","text":"Property, right, and obligation do not exist naturally before justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX12","text":"Justice is not required under universal benevolence or complete abundance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0028","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0029","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX13","text":"The solitary state of nature is not an actual historical condition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX14","text":"Artificiality does not entail arbitrariness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX15","text":"Political inculcation is not the sole cause of moral distinctions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0034","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:NX16","text":"The absence of property before convention does not mean that violating another’s property was permissible; the relevant property relation did not yet exist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0036","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D1","text":"Duty can secondarily motivate an action only because an antecedent natural motive has already made that kind of action morally intelligible.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A2","CH_THN_PIJI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D2","text":"A natural sense of justice cannot be the first cause of just conduct; justice becomes intelligible only after the convention it evaluates.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A3","CH_THN_PIJI:A8","CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0020","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D3","text":"Partial generosity both forms close associations and generates conflict between those associations.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D4","text":"Social instability arises from the conjunction of partial affection with scarce and transferable goods, not from either condition alone.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D5","text":"Immediate self-interest threatens society, while reflected self-interest preserves society by accepting reciprocal restraint.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A6","CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D6","text":"Non-promissory convention avoids regress by grounding coordination in reciprocal expectations rather than antecedent obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A11","CH_THN_PIJI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D7","text":"Justice and property are co-generated; property cannot be invoked to explain the origin of justice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_PIJI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D8","text":"Removing either confined generosity or scarcity removes the functional conditions that make justice necessary.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A7","CH_THN_PIJI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0028","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D9","text":"The justice of a particular act is determined by its place within a beneficial general scheme rather than by its isolated consequences.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_PIJI:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D10","text":"Compliance precedes moralization: interest first establishes observance, and sympathy later converts observance into virtue.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A16","CH_THN_PIJI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D11","text":"As society expands and direct awareness of common interest weakens, sympathy and general rules preserve moral disapproval of injustice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A1","CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_PIJI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D12","text":"Political rhetoric can extend an existing moral sentiment but cannot create moral intelligibility without natural affective materials.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A1","CH_THN_PIJI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0034","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D13","text":"Education and reputation internalize established conventions as durable sentiments of honor.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A4","CH_THN_PIJI:A16","CH_THN_PIJI:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:D14","text":"A pre-conventional condition contains neither justice nor injustice because it contains no moral relation of property.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_PIJI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0036","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD1","text":"Normative order emerges from non-moral coordination: interested restraint produces convention, and convention becomes morally charged through sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD2","text":"The universality of justice is rule-general and reciprocal rather than metaphysical or consequence-sensitive at the level of individual acts.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0030","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD3","text":"Property and right are socially constituted relations sustained by stabilized expectations rather than intrinsic features of persons or objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD4","text":"The same partial interest that generates social conflict also supplies the only passion capable of resolving it when redirected by understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD5","text":"Justice is conditionally necessary: contingent relative to scarcity and limited generosity, yet effectively inseparable from humanity while those conditions persist.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","text":"Artificial rules become second-nature through sympathy, custom, education, public judgment, and reputational interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0034","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD7","text":"Justice converts long-range systemic advantage into stable obligations capable of overriding contrary local advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","text":"Moral and institutional emergence are temporally ordered but mutually reinforcing once established: convention enables approval, and approval stabilizes convention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX1","text":"A moral predicate cannot generate the first passion that makes its own application possible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX2","text":"Natural impartial benevolence cannot be sufficiently strong or general to stabilize possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX3","text":"Property cannot be a pre-institutional relation waiting merely to be recognized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX4","text":"Reason cannot independently create concern, motivation, or moral obligation from relations of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0030","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX5","text":"The consequences of isolated acts cannot determine the justice of those acts independently of general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX6","text":"Neither a sovereign command nor an antecedent promise is necessary for conventions to emerge.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX7","text":"The genealogy of society does not require an actual period of solitary human existence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0026","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:DNX8","text":"Stable restraint cannot persist without confidence that others will regulate their conduct by the same rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX1","text":"Justice occupies the absence of a direct natural route from partial passion to impartial social order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0020","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX2","text":"Property occupies the absence of natural stability in scarce and transferable possessions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX3","text":"Moral approbation occupies the gap between interested compliance and disinterested evaluation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX4","text":"The system excludes self-grounding normativity: every moral obligation depends upon antecedent passions, interests, and sentiments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0030","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX5","text":"Stable property forecloses case-by-case redistribution according to immediate merit, benevolence, need, or public advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:SNX6","text":"The system contains no primordial rights-bearing individual prior to convention; the pre-social subject lacks the relations that would make such rights intelligible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0036","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS1","text":"Naturalness disambiguated → morality detached from nature/unnature classification → pleasure and uneasiness installed as evaluative criteria.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS2","text":"Action treated as sign → motive identified as moral object → regard for morality rejected as first motive.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS3","text":"Candidate natural motives tested → private interest, public benevolence, private benevolence, and universal humanity eliminated → artificial origin required.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS4","text":"Human wants and weakness → sexual and familial association → habit-mediated recognition of social advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS5","text":"Partial affection + scarce transferable goods → unstable possession → threat to socially necessary cooperation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS6","text":"Reflection on long-range interest → redirection of acquisitive passion → reciprocal rule of abstinence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS7","text":"Mutual expression of conditional restraint → non-promissory convention → repeated experience → stable expectation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS8","text":"Stable possession → justice and injustice → property, right, and obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS9","text":"Advantage of the whole scheme → interested observance → sympathy with public effects → moral approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:FGS10","text":"Moral approval → education, political reinforcement, and reputation → internalized probity → increased institutional stability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0034","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AF1","text":"Ambiguity of nature → affective moral criterion → priority of motive → prohibition of motivational circularity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AF2","text":"Failure of natural motives → anthropological and material diagnosis → reflected self-interest → reciprocal convention → artificial justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:AF3","text":"Stability of possession → property and obligation → systemic advantage → sympathetic approval → reinforced moral practice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME1","text":"The natural/artificial distinction replaces the natural/unnatural distinction as the relevant classification of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME2","text":"The primary object of moral judgment shifts from external action to motivating passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME3","text":"Justice is reclassified from an innate virtue into a convention-dependent practice subsequently moralized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME4","text":"Self-interest is divided into an impulsive form destructive of society and a reflective form capable of sustaining it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME5","text":"Property shifts from being the presumed ground of justice to being an effect of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME6","text":"Utility shifts from the isolated act to the coordinated system of general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME7","text":"Convention becomes visible as decentralized reciprocal coordination rather than promise, command, or explicit contract.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME8","text":"The state of nature shifts from historical origin to analytical fiction produced by separating passions from understanding.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0026","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ME9","text":"Moral obligation is separated from natural obligation: sympathy explains approval, while interest explains initial observance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ALT1","text":"Surface: Justice is tested as a natural or artificial virtue, and its rules and moral valuation are assigned distinct origins.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ALT2","text":"Structural: Partial affection, scarcity, transferability, reciprocal expectation, general rules, and social reinforcement constitute the mechanism of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: Explanation must proceed from motive and convention to property and obligation, prohibiting the use of established moral categories to explain their own genesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: Property and right are emergent relations formed gradually through coordinated expectations; their contingency coexists with their practical inseparability from enduring human conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_PIJI:A1–CH_THN_PIJI:A18 are sufficient to reconstruct the argument’s semantic, motivational, genealogical, institutional, and sentimental stages.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0019","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0020","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0032","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT2","text":"The structure is coherent only if the establishment of rules, interested observance, and moral approbation remain distinct explanatory moments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT3","text":"The strongest transition is from motivational circularity and failed natural motives to reciprocal convention grounded in reflected interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0022","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT4","text":"The shift from rejected private interest to accepted common interest depends upon distinguishing immediate unilateral gain from conditional systemic advantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0025","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT5","text":"The co-generation of justice and property risks circularity unless physical possession is kept distinct from morally constituted property.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT6","text":"The account of moral obligation remains underdetermined because the full mechanism of sympathy and the natural virtues is explicitly deferred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT7","text":"General-rule utility explains adherence despite harmful particular acts but leaves the limits, revision, and exception conditions of those rules unresolved.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT8","text":"The artificial-versus-natural tension is contained by assigning different senses to nature, but the resolution depends upon controlled semantic variation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0014","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIJI:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction from the extracted system preserves the movement from conceptual clearing through genealogy to moral internalization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0021","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0023","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIJI:P0035","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 22","title":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice","context":"Chapter 22","original_title":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice","original_context":"Chapter 22"},"original_title":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property","order":23,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIRW"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIRW@R001","sha256":"f34752db164d8282a15b9d3c642160af2d3996b26c5d0caa17146eaabcfdcd41","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS1","text":"Problem: the necessary convention of stable possession does not determine which goods belong to which persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS2","text":"Thesis: particular assignments require general, society-wide rules insulated from case-specific judgments of fitness or utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS3","text":"General utility necessitates determinate property, while imagination principally selects the relations through which titles are assigned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS4","text":"Argument type: genealogical reconstruction joined to psychological and jurisprudential diagnosis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS5","text":"Instability of possession → convention of stability → initial assignment by present possession → restriction of that founding rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS6","text":"Established society differentiates acquisition into occupation, prescription, accession, and succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AS7","text":"Gradual relations and conflicting criteria defeat complete natural determination, requiring municipal law to close residual disputes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A1","text":"Stable possession is necessary to human society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A2","text":"The general rule of stable possession cannot by itself assign particular goods to particular persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A3","text":"Particular fitness or utility is too contestable and partial to serve as a peaceful rule of property.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A4","text":"Property assignments must be governed by general and inflexible rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A5","text":"Property is constant possession secured by social rules, not a real quality inhering in objects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A6","text":"The imagination tends to complete an existing relation between objects by adding further resembling relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A7","text":"General utility requires determinate settlement, while imagination principally fixes the specific forms of property rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A8","text":"At society’s first formation, property is assigned to the present possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A9","text":"The rule favoring present possession is valid only for initial social settlement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A10","text":"After society is established, occupation attaches property to first possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A11","text":"Possession consists in a causal power to use, alter, move, or destroy an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A12","text":"Possessory power varies by degrees of probability and therefore lacks universally precise boundaries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A13","text":"Prescription transfers title toward long and uninterrupted present possession as temporal distance weakens the first possessor’s relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A14","text":"Accession extends ownership from a principal object to an intimately connected and inferior object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A15","text":"Municipal law must determine cases in which natural criteria of relation and comparative importance remain balanced or contradictory.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:A16","text":"Succession directs property toward near relations through presumed consent and public interest, reinforced by associative relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX1","text":"Property is not to be distributed through case-by-case judgments of personal fitness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX2","text":"Justice does not vary title according to the owner’s character, need, or effective use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX3","text":"Property is not an intrinsic or perceptible feature of the owned object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX4","text":"Present possession does not remain a perpetual source of title after society has been established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX5","text":"Occupation is not grounded in a literal incorporation of labor into an object.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX6","text":"Possession is not reducible to immediate physical contact.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX7","text":"Reason and public interest do not supply exact boundaries for every possessory dispute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX8","text":"Mere connection does not produce accession symmetrically; the principal draws the inferior, not the inferior the principal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:NX9","text":"Natural associative principles do not constitute a complete code for deciding compound and borderline cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D1","text":"Justice must disregard whether property is useful to its owner because individualized adjustment would restore the contention that stability was instituted to prevent.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A1","CH_THN_SIRW:A3","CH_THN_SIRW:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D2","text":"Present possession becomes the founding title because custom strengthens attachment and because present possession resembles the constant possession constituted as property.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A5","CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D3","text":"Permanent adherence to present possession would authorize seizure and exclude restitution.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D4","text":"Occupation supplies the default title to previously unassigned goods because rejecting first possession leaves no non-arbitrary basis for preferring a later possession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A4","CH_THN_SIRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D5","text":"The extent of possession varies with imaginative boundedness, salience, and proportionality between possessor and object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A11","CH_THN_SIRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D6","text":"Time produces prescription by changing the influence of relations upon sentiment, not by producing a new real feature in the object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A5","CH_THN_SIRW:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D7","text":"Accession can confer title before actual possession because ownership travels through an established chain of relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D8","text":"The apparent ease of transition from a small object to a great one does not make the great object an accession; the compound whole is imaginatively dominated by its most considerable part.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D9","text":"When inseparable properties are combined, the whole tends toward the proprietor of the most considerable part; when components remain distinguishable, distinct or proportional claims persist.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A14","CH_THN_SIRW:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:D10","text":"Succession can follow the line from which goods originated even when neither presumed consent nor public interest specifies that division.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0016","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD1","text":"Property is generated by a dual mechanism: utility creates the demand for settled rules, while imagination supplies their concrete relational form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD2","text":"The property regime is temporally layered: present possession governs founding, occupation governs first acquisition, prescription revises obscured origins, accession extends existing title, and succession resolves death-created vacancy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","text":"Legal determinacy is practical closure rather than the discovery of an independently precise natural boundary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD4","text":"A title can change without any corresponding real alteration in its object because property depends upon socially stabilized sentiment and relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD5","text":"Juridical attribution follows salience, continuity, proximity, scale, and associative ease rather than distributive optimality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SD6","text":"Municipal rules may be mutually inconsistent at the level of principle while remaining systemically necessary as devices for terminating controversy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX1","text":"The system requires pre-social property rights independent of convention to be false.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX2","text":"The system requires public utility alone to be incapable of deriving every particular title.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX3","text":"The system requires historical facts not to retain unchanged psychological force across temporal distance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX4","text":"The system requires durability, value, extent, visibility, and independence not to form a universally commensurable hierarchy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX5","text":"The usefulness of a natural-seeming property rule does not entail its universal or permanent validity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX6","text":"Social peace and continuously revised allocation by individual suitability cannot operate as coequal governing principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:DNX7","text":"“Natural” property rules cannot mean intrinsic rights antecedent to society; they depend upon natural psychological propensities operating within convention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SNX1","text":"No final adjudicative measure exists beneath convention, imagination, and positive legal stipulation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SNX2","text":"No property bond remains as an objective feature once social recognition, law, and sentiment are removed.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SNX3","text":"Stable justice forecloses continuous correction of ownership according to need, merit, or optimal use.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SNX4","text":"Complete recovery of original title is unavailable as a permanent foundation of present ownership.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:SNX5","text":"The system contains no natural procedure capable of eliminating every borderline dispute without authoritative closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS1","text":"Changeable possession → insecurity and conflict → convention of stable possession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS2","text":"General stability → unresolved allocation problem → demand for modifying rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS3","text":"Case-specific fitness → controversy and partiality → rejection in favor of general rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS4","text":"Antecedent person-object relation + associative completion + social security → property relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS5","text":"Initial social settlement → present possession + custom + resemblance → founding title.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS6","text":"Established society → rejection of perpetual present-possession title → differentiated acquisition rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS7","text":"Unassigned object → first possessory power → occupation → title.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS8","text":"Obscured original title + prolonged current possession → weakened first relation + strengthened present relation → prescription.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS9","text":"Owned principal + intimate inferior connection → imaginative union → accession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS10","text":"Death-created vacancy + kinship association + presumed consent and public interest → succession.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:FGS11","text":"Graded relation or conflicting salience criteria → natural indeterminacy → municipal stipulation → terminated dispute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:AF1","text":"Necessary stability → allocation gap → rejection of particular utility → general-rule requirement → imaginative attachment to antecedent relations → founding title by present possession → restriction of that title → occupation, prescription, accession, and succession → municipal closure of residual indeterminacy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME1","text":"Property is reclassified from an inherent object-quality into a socially secured moral relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME2","text":"General utility and particular title-generation are separated: utility explains why settlement is needed, while imagination explains how goods become connected to persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME3","text":"The natural/artificial opposition is reorganized: artificial conventions acquire their form through natural habits of association.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME4","text":"Spatial proximity, causal power, temporal duration, comparative scale, and kinship become juridically productive relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0013","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME5","text":"Occupation, prescription, accession, and succession become variants of one mechanism: the extension or redistribution of property through antecedent relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ME6","text":"Municipal law is reclassified from discovery of natural right to authoritative completion of naturally underdetermined judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ALT1","text":"Surface: the argument determines the recognized sources by which particular goods become assigned to particular persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ALT2","text":"Structural: conventions transform unstable possession into a rule-governed property regime whose residual ambiguities are closed by municipal law.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: jurisprudential explanation shifts from personal fitness and abstract reason toward the interaction of public utility, imagination, and authoritative settlement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: property has no object-inherent reality, and title changes as time alters the force of relations upon sentiment rather than the object itself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SIRW:A1 through CH_THN_SIRW:A16 are sufficient to reconstruct the allocation problem, the dual explanatory mechanism, and the four established titles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT2","text":"The relative causal weight of public utility and imagination remains explicitly underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT3","text":"The thresholds of possession, temporal prescription, inferiority, and comparative importance remain naturally indeterminate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT4","text":"The structure is coherent once founding rules are distinguished from rules governing an established society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT5","text":"The strongest mechanism is the division between utility’s demand for determinate settlement and imagination’s production of particular relational titles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT6","text":"The argument depends on analogy and comparative psychological plausibility when identifying imagination as the predominant cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT7","text":"Its principal tension is that inflexible legal rules are generated from fluid, context-sensitive, and sometimes contradictory relations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIRW:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the movement from general stability through differentiated titles to the necessity of municipal closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIRW:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 23","title":"Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property","context":"Chapter 23","original_title":"Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property","original_context":"Chapter 23"},"original_title":"Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice","order":24,"parents":["RAW_THN_SVSF"],"revision":"CH_THN_SVSF@R001","sha256":"0d3b432f3c3368878e6bc3631480e3c09917e0003a839422f108dab1858a0ad6","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS1","text":"Problem: whether justice and its juridical relations precede convention or are constituted by it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS2","text":"Thesis: justice is an artificial virtue generated by human convention rather than an original natural principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS3","text":"Argument type: reconstructive genealogy joined to conceptual, modal, and motivational reductio.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS4","text":"Presumed antecedence of property → analysis of property → dependence of property upon justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS5","text":"Gradable natural morality → non-gradable juridical relations → exclusion of justice from natural virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS6","text":"Variable natural motives → socially destructive particular judgment → institution of universal and inflexible rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AS7","text":"Coordinated interest → conventional observance → social utility → subsequent moral approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A1","text":"Human well-being requires society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A2","text":"Social peace and security require strict observance of stability of possession, consensual transfer, and performance of promises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A3","text":"Self-love is the originating passion of the laws of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A4","text":"The self-love of different persons is naturally conflictual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A5","text":"Convention adjusts conflicting interests into a common system of conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A6","text":"Property consists in the influence of object-person relations upon minds and actions as duties.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A7","text":"Duties of justice constitute property; property does not antecedently constitute justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A8","text":"Rules determining property, right, and obligation display contrivance through multiplicity, legal mutability, and orientation toward civil utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A9","text":"Natural moral qualities pass into one another by imperceptible degrees.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A10","text":"Property, right, and obligation are integral rather than gradable within their stipulated scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A11","text":"Property, right, and obligation depend upon justice and injustice for their variations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A12","text":"Natural moral conduct inherits the particularity and variability of its motivating passions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A13","text":"The laws of justice are universal and inflexible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A14","text":"Unregulated particular judgment, under avidity and partiality, produces social disorder.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A15","text":"Convention institutes invariant rules to prevent that disorder.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A16","text":"The first foundation of justice is recognized interest in the possibility and preservation of society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:A17","text":"The second foundation of justice is the moral approval that arises after the social utility of its rules is established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX1","text":"Justice is not a natural virtue grounded in a peculiar original principle.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX2","text":"“Giving every one his due” cannot define justice without presupposing the property and right that justice constitutes.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX3","text":"Property is not independent of moral sentiment and convention.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX4","text":"Property is not a sensible quality of an object, a relation among external objects, or a merely corporeal relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX5","text":"Bare possession, occupation, or transfer does not naturally carry an original sentiment of moral approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX6","text":"Regard for public good is not the originating motive of the rules of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX7","text":"Imperfect property or dominion is not a rationally grounded intermediate juridical state, but a civil fiction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:NX8","text":"Justice is not the flexible application of immediate private or public motives to particular cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D1","text":"Social indispensability does not entail natural origin.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A1","CH_THN_SVSF:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D2","text":"Property changes without sensible or corporeal alteration because its constitutive change occurs in duties governing conduct.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D3","text":"The vulgar definition of justice reverses the order of dependence and becomes circular.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D4","text":"The adjustment of opposed self-interests can produce public advantage without public advantage being intended.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:A4","CH_THN_SVSF:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D5","text":"Even deliberate orientation toward public good would establish artifice rather than naturality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D6","text":"If justice admits degrees, its dependent juridical relations must admit degrees, contradicting their integral form.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D7","text":"If justice does not admit degrees, it lacks the gradual form attributed to natural virtue and vice.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D8","text":"Gradated justificatory pressures underdetermine binary juridical verdicts, forcing adjudication to impose decisive closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A8","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D9","text":"Without convention, circumstance-sensitive natural motives could authorize partial conduct because no antecedent property relation would be violated.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A7","CH_THN_SVSF:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D10","text":"Universal justice is a remedial constraint upon ordinary motivation rather than its immediate expression.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A14","CH_THN_SVSF:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:D11","text":"Moral obligation is posterior to interest-based conventional stabilization.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A16","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD1","text":"Artificiality and natural genesis are compatible: convention is a second-order strategy by which passion secures satisfaction against the destructive effects of its first-order operation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:A4","CH_THN_SVSF:A5","CH_THN_SVSF:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD2","text":"Property has no independent object-ontology; it is a normative effect of institutionally stabilized expectations and duties.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:A7","CH_THN_SVSF:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD3","text":"Juridical impartiality is produced by suppressing the case-sensitive responsiveness characteristic of natural motivation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD4","text":"Legal determinacy exceeds the determinacy of its gradual utilitarian and imaginative grounds, creating recurrent pressure for arbitrary boundary decisions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A8","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD5","text":"Justice has a dual genealogy: interested coordination produces the rule-system, while contemplation of its social effects produces its moral sanction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A5","CH_THN_SVSF:A16","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SD6","text":"Necessity attaches to the social function of justice, not to an innate moral source.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A1","CH_THN_SVSF:A2","CH_THN_SVSF:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX1","text":"What is necessary for human life need not be naturally implanted.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX2","text":"What is artificial need not be arbitrary, dispensable, or opposed to human passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX3","text":"The moral status of justice cannot itself supply the pre-moral motive required to establish just conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX4","text":"No normative fact of mine and thine survives the subtraction of conventionally organized duty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX5","text":"No immediate natural motive possesses the universality and invariability required by juridical order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:DNX6","text":"Collective benefit does not require collective benevolence or a consciously shared public end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SNX1","text":"The system excludes a pre-social normative world in which objects intrinsically belong to persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SNX2","text":"The system forecloses spontaneous social stability through unrestricted case-sensitive equity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SNX3","text":"Nature supplies no impartial legislative motive capable of directly generating the formal structure of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:SNX4","text":"No stable middle term reconciles gradated grounds of entitlement with the binary verdicts required by juridical decision.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS1","text":"Social necessity + opposed self-love → coordination problem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS2","text":"Particular motives + avidity + partiality → variable judgment → social confusion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS3","text":"Recognition of common interest → voluntary convention → universal and inflexible rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4","text":"Inflexible rules → duties of abstention, restoration, transfer, and performance → property, right, and obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS5","text":"Coordinated private interests → unintended public utility → peace and social security.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS6","text":"Perceived social utility → pleasure in observance and uneasiness at violation → moral sanction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:FGS7","text":"Constitutive-priority test + gradation test + motive-rule mismatch test → justice classified as artificial virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AF1","text":"Social necessity of the three laws → question of their origin → artificial-virtue thesis.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AF2","text":"Vulgar definition → presumed antecedent property → relational analysis → priority of justice over property.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AF3","text":"Gradable natural morality → integral juridical relations → dichotomous reductio → exclusion of natural justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AF4","text":"Particular natural motivation → partial judgment → social disorder → conventional inflexibility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:AF5","text":"Interest-based convention → stable social utility → natural moral approval → dual foundation of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME1","text":"Necessity and naturality are separated: an institution may be indispensable without being innate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME2","text":"Property is reclassified from a quality of objects into an effect of norm-governed relations among persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME3","text":"Justice is reclassified from an original sentiment into a conventional rule-system that later receives moral approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME4","text":"Self-interest is reclassified from a mere source of disorder into the generative material of social order.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME5","text":"Public good is reclassified from an originating intention into an emergent effect of coordinated private interests.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME6","text":"Adjudication is reclassified as the forced production of binary closure over grounds that often appear continuous.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0008","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ME7","text":"Moral motivation divides into a pre-moral motive of interest and a post-institutional sentiment of approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ALT1","text":"Surface: the three laws of possession, transfer, and promise are necessary but artificial rules of justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ALT2","text":"Structural: convention redirects opposed self-interests into invariant conduct capable of sustaining society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: the naturality claim is defeated through inversion of conceptual priority, mismatch of gradation, and mismatch of motivational form.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: property exists as a norm-mediated relation, while interest-based institution temporally precedes moral approbation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SVSF:A1–CH_THN_SVSF:A17 are sufficient to reconstruct the chapter’s three proofs and final two-foundation model.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT2","text":"The exact historical mechanism by which voluntary convention first stabilizes is underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT3","text":"The structure is coherent if natural passion is distinguished from the second-order arrangements generated by passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT4","text":"The strongest support lies in the constitutive priority of justice over property and the mismatch between variable motives and invariant rules.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT5","text":"The gradation argument depends on the broad and insufficiently demonstrated premise that all natural moral qualities vary continuously.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT6","text":"The inference from the multiplicity of juridical rules to their non-natural origin is compressed rather than independently established.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT7","text":"The insistence on integral rights remains in tension with the acknowledged graduality of utility, imagination, and ordinary judgments of half-right.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0008","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT8","text":"The claim that justice is both an offspring of passion and contrary to ordinary passionate conduct is resolved only by an implicit distinction between first-order impulse and second-order strategy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SVSF:MRT9","text":"Reconstruction preserves the operative movement: necessity → conceptual inversion → modal reductio → motivational diagnosis → convention → moralization.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SVSF:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 24","title":"Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice","context":"Chapter 24","original_title":"Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice","original_context":"Chapter 24"},"original_title":"Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance","order":25,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIMA"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIMA@R001","sha256":"3cc5d7088dd720bd33ba5ac15637037d713ee7fd9e3967e731e4cf6cad23269b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS1","text":"Problem: determine when allegiance ceases and resistance becomes morally permissible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS2","text":"Contract theory reaches the correct anti-tyrannical conclusion through the false premise of an original promise.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS3","text":"Promise reduces genealogically to convention and interest and therefore cannot be the ultimate source of allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS4","text":"Public interest in security and protection is the original motive and immediate sanction of government and obedience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS5","text":"Intolerable oppression destroys that interest and thereby terminates the natural obligation of allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS6","text":"Moral obligation may outlive its natural cause through general-rule inertia, but recurrent abuses of power establish a general exception permitting resistance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AS7","text":"Reconstructive-genealogical movement: valid conclusion → false contractual ground → interest-ground → moral persistence objection → generalized resistance exception → common-judgment confirmation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A1","text":"A promise is a human convention invented for the sake of interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A2","text":"Government is a human invention ordered to the interest of society.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A3","text":"Political security and protection are unattainable under perfect freedom and independence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A4","text":"Public interest is the immediate natural sanction of allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A5","text":"The natural obligation of allegiance lasts no longer than the public interest that causes it.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A6","text":"The moral obligation of allegiance is founded on the natural obligation of interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A7","text":"Human beings extend general moral rules beyond the reasons that originally established them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A8","text":"An exception to a general moral rule ordinarily requires its own general-rule form, grounded in numerous and common cases.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A9","text":"The frailties and passions of human nature persist in rulers.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A10","text":"Political office gives rulers a more immediate interest in preserving order and executing justice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A11","text":"Rulers can be driven by passion to neglect that immediate interest and pursue cruelty or ambition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A12","text":"Human nature, history, and present experience make severe abuses of ruling power recurrent and foreseeable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A13","text":"Settled moral practice approves resistance to flagrant tyranny and rejects passive obedience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A14","text":"The general opinion of mankind is authoritative and, in morals, infallible.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A15","text":"Imitation and custom cannot originate submission because both presuppose prior instances of submission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:A16","text":"Public interest is the only original motive capable of overcoming natural ambition and producing political submission.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX1","text":"The justified exception to allegiance does not depend on an original contract.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX2","text":"Promise is not a natural, preconventional, or self-grounding source of political obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX3","text":"Government need not be derived through reflective institution followed by a promise of obedience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX4","text":"The cessation of a natural cause does not by itself guarantee the immediate cessation of moral obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX5","text":"A general moral rule is not exceptionless merely because it has become habitual.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX6","text":"Political authority does not transform rulers into beings of superior nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX7","text":"A ruler’s situational interest in justice does not guarantee just conduct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX8","text":"Imitation and custom are not original motives of allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX9","text":"Passive obedience is not a coherent moral absolute.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:NX10","text":"Resistance is not authorized by every partial, private, or isolated disadvantage.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D1","text":"Promise cannot be the ultimate foundation of allegiance because its own authority derives from convention and interest.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_SIMA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D2","text":"When intolerable oppression removes political security, it removes the natural obligation to obey.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A3","CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_SIMA:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D3","text":"Moral allegiance may persist temporarily after its originating interest has ceased because general rules exceed their original causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A6","CH_THN_SIMA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D4","text":"Recurrent rulerly abuse gives the anti-tyrannical exception the generality required to correct the rule of obedience.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:A9","CH_THN_SIMA:A11","CH_THN_SIMA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D5","text":"Once loss of public interest is grave and generalizable, conscience no longer binds submission to violent tyranny.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A5","CH_THN_SIMA:A6","CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D6","text":"Resistance to flagrant tyranny is neither criminal nor unjust within the prevailing moral judgment of mankind.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A13","CH_THN_SIMA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D7","text":"Custom and imitation can transmit allegiance but cannot explain its first production.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A15","CH_THN_SIMA:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:D8","text":"The operative measure of resistance is conjunctive: public interest must fail in a great degree, and the failure must belong to a considerable class of cases.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A5","CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SD1","text":"Allegiance is conditionally binding without being contractually founded: its condition is the continued public function of government.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SD2","text":"The same general-rule mechanism that stabilizes ordinary obedience also generates the exception to obedience when abuses become recurrent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","text":"A ruler who systematically destroys security is reclassified from magistrate into tyrant and public enemy because authority is functionally sanctioned.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SD4","text":"Legitimate resistance is internal to the rule-governed structure of allegiance rather than an arbitrary suspension of political morality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SD5","text":"Common moral practice can embody the causal logic of political obligation implicitly without articulating its philosophical genealogy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX1","text":"Political obligation cannot possess an independent normative foundation severed from public security.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX2","text":"Elevation to office cannot eliminate the passions that make institutional failure foreseeable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX3","text":"General-rule inertia cannot be absolute, or moral allegiance would survive the destruction of its own foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX4","text":"A singular private grievance cannot constitute the public-interest failure required for resistance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX5","text":"Correct moral judgment cannot require explicit theoretical knowledge of its generating principles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:DNX6","text":"Custom cannot be self-originating or explain the first act that later becomes customary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SNX1","text":"No transcendent, natural, or unconditional residue of allegiance remains once public interest and its moral generalization cease.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SNX2","text":"No ruler possesses an indefeasible title to obedience independently of the protective function of government.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SNX3","text":"No exact decision procedure measures intolerable tyranny; the threshold remains confined to qualitative severity and recurrence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:SNX4","text":"A political order from which justified resistance could be permanently excluded is unavailable because rulerly frailty is inseparable from human nature.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS1","text":"Correct anti-tyrannical conclusion → separation from contractual premises.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS2","text":"Promise → human convention → interest → collapse of promise as ultimate foundation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS3","text":"Need for security → invention of government → public-interest sanction → natural allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS4","text":"Intolerable tyranny → destruction of protection → cessation of interest → cessation of natural allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS5","text":"General-rule propensity → persistence of moral allegiance beyond its immediate cause.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS6","text":"Invariant human frailty + rulerly passions + repeated historical abuse → generalizable exception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS7","text":"Generalized exception + common moral judgment → morally permissible resistance to violent supreme power.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:FGS8","text":"Regress of imitation and custom → exclusion of rival origin motives → public-interest foundation secured.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:AF1","text":"Correct resistance conclusion → rejection of original contract → reduction of promise to interest → direct grounding of allegiance in public security → natural obligation canceled by severe tyranny → moral-rule persistence objection → recurrent abuse generalized into an exception → resistance morally authorized → custom and imitation excluded as origins.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME1","text":"Political conditionality is relocated from contractual nonperformance to the functional cessation of public protection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME2","text":"Allegiance is reclassified from an absolute duty to a defeasible effect of public interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME3","text":"Tyranny becomes a reversal of political function capable of converting the governor into a public enemy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME4","text":"General rules acquire a double role: they preserve obedience beyond immediate calculation and authorize resistance through generalized exceptions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME5","text":"History, common practice, and moral sentiment become evidence for the structure and limits of political obligation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ME6","text":"Custom and imitation are demoted from generative causes to mechanisms that direct or reproduce an allegiance generated by interest.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ALT1","text":"Surface: the argument identifies the threshold at which subjects may resist supreme power without crime or injustice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ALT2","text":"Structural: government converts common insecurity into coordinated submission but remains vulnerable to rulers whose passions override institutional incentives.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: the argument preserves a general rule of allegiance while making resistance an internal, generalizable exception rather than a contractual escape.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: invariant human frailty persists across political positions, while moral rules outlive their causes until repeated experience produces a corrective counter-rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the replacement of contractual allegiance by interest-grounded allegiance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT2","text":"The system is strongest where the same public interest explains both the institution of government and the limit of obedience.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT3","text":"The natural-obligation/moral-obligation distinction preserves the actual movement from direct causal cessation to the general-rule objection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT4","text":"The precise threshold between tolerable misgovernment and resistance-authorizing tyranny remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT5","text":"The exclusivity of public interest as original motive is compressed into a regress against imitation and custom rather than established against every possible rival.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT6","text":"The appeal to universal moral approval depends heavily on the asserted infallibility of general opinion in morals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT7","text":"The central tension lies between moral rules exceeding their causes and moral obligation ultimately ceasing with natural obligation; the general exception mediates but does not quantify this transition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0018","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIMA:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the real movement provided qualitative severity, recurrent abuse, and common moral judgment are accepted as sufficient to generalize the exception.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIMA:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 25","title":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance","context":"Chapter 25","original_title":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance","original_context":"Chapter 25"},"original_title":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV","layer":"CH","title":"Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices","order":26,"parents":["RAW_THN_PIOV"],"revision":"CH_THN_PIOV@R001","sha256":"836759be6feff278934bd248b27bd22686d6d52d6efe070c62faca909509b1f9","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS1","text":"The problem is to explain how natural qualities become virtues or vices without reducing morality to either rational judgment or political contrivance.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0008","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS2","text":"Virtue and vice arise from pleasure and uneasiness produced by surveying durable character from a common evaluative standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS3","text":"Sympathy communicates the pleasures and pains attached to a quality’s effects on its possessor or on others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS4","text":"The argument is a sentimental genealogy supported by causal comparison, explanatory parsimony, objection-resolution, and application.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS5","text":"Artificial virtue supplies the controlled case from which the sympathetic basis of approval is extended to natural virtues.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS6","text":"Variability of sympathy and accidental failure of useful dispositions are neutralized by the general point of view and evaluation of natural tendency.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AS7","text":"Pride functions as the decisive test: its moral valence changes with its utility, intrinsic agreeableness, comparative effect, degree, and mode of expression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A1","text":"Moral distinctions are constituted by peculiar sentiments of pleasure and uneasiness elicited by contemplating mental qualities.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A2","text":"A pleasing quality produces virtue, pride, or love; an uneasy quality produces vice, humility, or hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A3","text":"Actions enter morality only as evidence of durable principles of character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A4","text":"Sympathy converts a sufficiently lively idea of another’s inferred affection into a corresponding sentiment in the spectator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A5","text":"An object’s natural fitness for an agreeable end can please the imagination even when external circumstances prevent the end.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A6","text":"Moral pleasure has four sources: usefulness to others, usefulness to the possessor, immediate agreeableness to others, and immediate agreeableness to the possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A7","text":"The usefulness of a quality becomes agreeable to a disinterested spectator through sympathy with its beneficiaries.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A8","text":"Qualities agreeable only to their possessor or associates become agreeable to other spectators through sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A9","text":"Reflection on tendencies determines the principal lines of duty, while immediate taste independently produces lesser moral approvals.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0022","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A10","text":"General moral judgment adopts the interests of the person evaluated and of those connected with that person as the common standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A11","text":"General evaluation corrects variations caused by distance, situation, personal interest, present disposition, and fortune, even when occurrent passions remain unequal.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A12","text":"A disposition’s natural tendency, rather than its accidental actual efficacy, determines its standing as virtue or vice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A13","text":"Imaginative sympathy can determine moral taste without becoming strong enough to motivate sacrifice or restrain private passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A14","text":"Natural and artificial virtues differ in the scale of beneficial operation: natural virtues benefit through particular acts, whereas justice benefits through a general scheme.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A15","text":"A cause demonstrated sufficient for an effect must be extended to analogous effects unless another cause is necessary.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A16","text":"Well-grounded self-esteem derives merit from both its usefulness and its immediate agreeableness to its possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A17","text":"Sympathy and self-comparison respond oppositely to another’s condition: sufficiently vivid and credible representation is shared, while intermediate representation produces a contrary self-feeling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0028","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:A18","text":"Moral love and hatred generate benevolence and anger, thereby producing judgments of good and ill desert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX1","text":"Moral good and evil are not discovered by reason or comparison of ideas.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX2","text":"An isolated action without a constant originating principle is not a proper object of moral praise or blame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX3","text":"Another person’s passion is not perceived immediately; it is inferred from its causes or effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX4","text":"Utility-based beauty and moral approval are not absolute qualities detached from relations to agreeable ends.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX5","text":"Moral distinctions are not inventions imposed wholly by politicians, education, honour, or shame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX6","text":"Public utility does not exhaust the sources of moral approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0008","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX7","text":"The spectator’s private advantage cannot serve as the universal standard of character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0013","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX8","text":"Variation in the intensity of sympathy does not entail variation in general moral esteem.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX9","text":"Actual success is not necessary for a disposition to retain virtue.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX10","text":"Extensive moral sympathy does not entail unlimited generosity or practical self-sacrifice.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:NX11","text":"Pride is not intrinsically vicious, and socially required modesty is not identical with sincere inward self-abasement.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0033","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0035","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D1","text":"Moral inquiry must infer enduring character from conduct rather than assign final value to isolated events.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A1","CH_THN_PIOV:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D2","text":"A spectator can approve qualities useful or agreeable to strangers without possessing any private interest in them.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_PIOV:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0020","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D3","text":"The sympathetic explanation established for artificial justice extends to socially useful natural virtues, whose particular benefits facilitate rather than obstruct sympathy.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D4","text":"Equal esteem for near and remote characters signifies corrected counterfactual judgment, not equal intensity of present affection.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A1","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D5","text":"Accidental impotence or bad fortune can weaken affective vividness without reducing the virtue attributed to a disposition.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A5","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_PIOV:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D6","text":"Limited generosity is compatible with extensive moral concern because taste requires only imaginative participation, whereas action requires stronger passion.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D7","text":"Moral evaluation is irreducibly fourfold, although considerations of useful tendency possess priority in major duties.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0022","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D8","text":"The constancy and universality required for moral discourse exclude each spectator’s shifting private interest from the speculative standard.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D9","text":"Due self-esteem is virtuous because it enables action and supplies immediate satisfaction to its possessor.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0033","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D10","text":"Unsupported self-exaltation becomes vicious when sympathy gives its claim enough vivacity for comparison but not enough credibility for shared admiration.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0032","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D11","text":"Haughtiness toward remote or historical others remains blameworthy through a double rebound: the spectator sympathizes with associates whose uneasiness already arose from sympathetic comparison with the proud person.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0040","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D12","text":"Good-breeding suppresses direct self-applause while permitting concealed, well-grounded self-esteem.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A16","CH_THN_PIOV:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0035","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D13","text":"Heroic pride can remain admirable despite social harm when immediate sympathy with elevation overpowers reflected pain from its public tendency.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:D14","text":"Moral approval and blame terminate in desires to benefit the virtuous and punish the vicious.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD1","text":"Moral universality is produced by intersubjective stabilization of sentiment rather than by escape from sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD2","text":"The authority conventionally attributed to reason is the authority of calm, generalized passion over judgment, not the necessary control of immediate passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","text":"Moral character is a counterfactual-dispositional object: evaluation concerns what a quality is fitted to produce under adequate conditions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD4","text":"Natural and artificial virtues share one mechanism of approval while differing in whether utility belongs to the act or to the coordinated system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD5","text":"The four-source system integrates self-regarding and other-regarding excellence by making sympathetic mobility, rather than direct benefit, the condition of spectator approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0023","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD6","text":"Good-breeding operates as an artificial coordination system for natural pride, preventing reciprocal comparison from making social intercourse intolerable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD7","text":"Heroic admiration exposes moral judgment as a conflict among affective sources rather than as the output of a single utility measure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SD8","text":"Moral desert is not an independent rational relation but the practical extension of pleasure into love and benevolence, or uneasiness into hatred and anger.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX1","text":"Radical affective incommensurability among human beings must be false, because sympathy presupposes similarity of passions and operations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX2","text":"Ideas of another’s condition cannot remain permanently affectless representations, because moral transmission requires their conversion into impressions.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX3","text":"First-person interest cannot possess legitimate universal authority, because its variability would dissolve shared moral language.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX4","text":"Actual outcomes cannot exhaust the value of causes, because fortune may obstruct a disposition without altering its internal fitness.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX5","text":"Moral approval cannot be required to produce corresponding action, because the system distinguishes imaginative taste from motivational force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:DNX6","text":"General judgment cannot be required to coincide with immediate passion, because correction may regulate judgment or language while leaving affection resistant.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SNX1","text":"An affect-free foundation of moral universality is unavailable; universality remains dependent on shared constitution and corrected sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SNX2","text":"Perfect impartiality at the level of lived passion is unavailable; only judgment and evaluative language can be regularly stabilized.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SNX3","text":"A single rule for commensurating utility, immediate agreeableness, self-regarding merit, and other-regarding merit is absent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0022","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SNX4","text":"Unmediated access to character is unavailable; moral evaluation depends on signs, inferred dispositions, general rules, and counterfactual tendencies.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:SNX5","text":"Complete unity between inward virtue and outward presentation is foreclosed by the requirement that legitimate pride conceal itself under conventional modesty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0035","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS1","text":"Conduct or expression → inference to durable character → admission into moral evaluation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS2","text":"Character → useful or immediately agreeable relation → actual or imagined pleasure or pain.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS3","text":"Useful to others / useful to possessor / agreeable to others / agreeable to possessor → four-source moral classification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS4","text":"Causes or effects of another’s condition → lively idea → sympathy → corresponding spectator sentiment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS5","text":"Pleasure in survey → virtue → pride or love; uneasiness in survey → vice → humility or hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS6","text":"Situational sentiment → common standpoint → correction for distance, interest, disposition, and fortune → stable esteem and moral language.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS7","text":"Natural virtue → particular beneficial act → determinate beneficiary → vivid sympathy; artificial virtue → coordinated scheme → general social benefit → diffuse sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS8","text":"Imaginative sympathy below motivational threshold → moral taste without sacrifice; sympathy touching the heart → practical control of passion.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS9","text":"Another’s self-assessment → sympathetic vivacity → credible superiority produces shared elevation and respect / unsupported superiority produces comparison, humiliation, and dislike.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0026","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0029","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:FGS10","text":"Moral love or hatred → benevolence or anger → attribution of good or ill desert.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AF1","text":"Natural-virtue problem → sentimental criterion → character filter → sympathy mechanism → artificial-virtue experiment → extension to natural virtues → variability objection → general-point-of-view correction → inactive-virtue objection → dispositional correction → four-source taxonomy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:AF2","text":"Four-source hypothesis → pride test → sympathy/comparison conflict → condemnation of overweening pride → rehabilitation of due self-esteem → conventional concealment → heroic-source conflict → confirmation with unresolved weighting tension.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME1","text":"The primary moral object shifts from isolated action to durable character and disposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME2","text":"Natural and artificial virtues cease to require different sources of approval while retaining different structures of beneficial operation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME3","text":"Impartiality is reclassified from rational detachment to correction of sentiment through a common standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME4","text":"Virtue expands beyond public utility to include usefulness and immediate agreeableness to the possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0023","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME5","text":"Moral esteem separates from both affective intensity and practical motivation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME6","text":"Moral value becomes counterfactual: natural tendency remains evaluable when fortune suppresses actual effects.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME7","text":"Pride is reclassified from a unitary vice into a variable quality governed by ground, degree, utility, comparative effect, and expression.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0031","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0033","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0038","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME8","text":"Good-breeding becomes visible as a coordination technology managing conflicts among self-evaluations.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0034","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ME9","text":"Heroism becomes a conflict-site where immediate elevation can defeat reflected concern for society without ceasing to be morally ambivalent.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ALT1","text":"Surface — The text classifies natural virtues and vices, then determines the moral limits of pride, humility, modesty, and greatness of mind.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ALT2","text":"Structural — Sympathy, imagination, comparison, general rules, and standpoint correction generate and stabilize moral evaluation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0011","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0028","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic — Artificial virtue functions as an experimental baseline, objections isolate corrective mechanisms, and pride serves as a stress test of the four-source system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal — The person is treated as a durable dispositional character whose tendencies extend across conduct and remain distinct from momentary action, situational feeling, and accidental fortune.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0017","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT1","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the sentimental criterion, sympathetic mechanism, common standpoint, four-source taxonomy, and application to pride.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT2","text":"The structure is internally coherent only if occurrent sentiment, corrected judgment, moral language, and motivating passion remain distinct.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0012","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0014","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0018","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT3","text":"The strongest inferential segment is the movement from artificial virtue as a sufficient-cause case to analogous socially useful natural virtues.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0009","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT4","text":"The weighting of the four moral sources remains underdetermined; predominance of useful tendency is asserted without a general aggregation rule.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0022","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT5","text":"The scope of the relevant connected persons and the procedure for resolving conflicts among their interests remain unspecified.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT6","text":"Heroic admiration exposes an unresolved fault line between social tendency and immediate sympathetic elevation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT7","text":"The inference from actions to durable character and the threshold separating comparison from sympathy depend on compressed probabilistic judgments.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0016","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0029","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT8","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement from causal genealogy through objection-correction to fourfold classification and pride-based verification.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0010","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0015","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0025","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_PIOV:MRT9","text":"The system supplies an explanatory architecture of moral judgment but not a complete decision procedure for conflicting virtues.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0024","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_PIOV:P0039","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 26","title":"Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices","context":"Chapter 26","original_title":"Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices","original_context":"Chapter 26"},"original_title":"Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB","layer":"CH","title":"Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence","order":27,"parents":["RAW_THN_SIGB"],"revision":"CH_THN_SIGB@R001","sha256":"0c06954bc73d358265c3d5d2fb23c83b0fcbfa26c68e1dd509f015c1e2e0764b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS1","text":"Problem: determine the sources and standard of merit attributed to goodness and benevolence under conditions of limited human generosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS2","text":"Thesis: benevolent character receives merit through beneficial tendency and the immediate agreeableness communicated by sympathy.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS3","text":"Argument type: reconstructive moral psychology joined to a diagnostic correction of situated judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS4","text":"Limited generosity → appraisal within the agent’s relational sphere → conflict among private standpoints → socially generalized standard.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS5","text":"Variable sympathy → reflective correction of distance → stable calm judgment without equal emotional concern.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS6","text":"Relational usefulness → benevolence as director of capacities; contagious tenderness → merit independent of utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AS7","text":"Qualified anger → condemnation of cruelty and incommodiousness → role-complete relational desirability as the final test of character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A1","text":"Ordinary human generosity is limited to friends, family, and at most country.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A2","text":"Character must be judged within the ordinary relational sphere in which its passions operate.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A3","text":"A passion is approved or blamed according to its tendency to advantage or harm persons connected with its possessor.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A4","text":"Sympathy communicates the pleasures, pains, and sentiments of affected persons to the spectator.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A5","text":"Judgment from private interest produces contradictions among differently situated spectators.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A6","text":"Social intercourse generates a general and stable standard for judging character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A7","text":"Reflection corrects calm judgment for distance-based variations in sympathetic force.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A8","text":"The general standard can regulate discourse without governing actual love and hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A9","text":"Loving and tender passions are immediately agreeable through sympathy independently of their utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A10","text":"Sympathetically reproduced love readily transfers affection from the displayed sentiment to its bearer.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A11","text":"Courage, ambition, judgment, and capacity acquire social valence from the passions directing them.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A12","text":"Angry passions are constitutionally inherent and can be fitting in limited degree.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A13","text":"Cruelty and inhumanity are vicious through the misery and harm they impose on others.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:A14","text":"Complete virtue requires desirability in every social relation together with adequate regard for oneself.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX1","text":"Goodness does not require impossible or universally extended generosity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX2","text":"Moral judgment is not validly fixed to the spectator’s private interest or position.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX3","text":"Raw sympathetic intensity is not the final measure of character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX4","text":"Benevolence does not derive all its merit from social utility.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX5","text":"Courage, ambition, judgment, and capacity are not intrinsically beneficial.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX6","text":"Angry passions are not categorically vicious merely because they are disagreeable.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX7","text":"The naturalness of anger does not excuse its escalation into cruelty.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:NX8","text":"Adequacy toward others alone does not constitute complete perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"4","name":"Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D1","text":"The object of appraisal remains local to the agent, while the standpoint of appraisal is generalized across spectators.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A1","CH_THN_SIGB:A2","CH_THN_SIGB:A5","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D2","text":"Distance-based variations in sympathy are treated as corrigible appearances rather than differences in character.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D3","text":"Benefits remote from the spectator can ground stable praise despite producing weaker immediate concern.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A3","CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D4","text":"Benevolent direction converts otherwise indifferent capacities into socially useful powers, while hostile direction converts them into instruments of harm.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A3","CH_THN_SIGB:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D5","text":"Sympathy with displayed love generates love toward the benevolent agent through a transfer of the passion’s object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:A9","CH_THN_SIGB:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D6","text":"A tender excess can remain amiable even when its public utility is negligible.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A9","CH_THN_SIGB:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D7","text":"The mere presence of anger cannot determine vice; degree, necessity, and consequence must intervene.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D8","text":"Sympathy with sufferers converts pity into intensified hatred of the cruel agent.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:D9","text":"Any disposition that makes ordinary relations persistently harmful or intolerable constitutes a defect of character.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A3","CH_THN_SIGB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"from","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"5","name":"Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD1","text":"The system universalizes the standpoint of appraisal without universalizing either benevolent motivation or beneficiary scope.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD2","text":"Moral stability is a reflective-social achievement imposed upon variable sympathy, not constancy of spontaneous feeling.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD3","text":"Benevolence possesses two irreducible routes to merit: beneficial tendency and affective self-communication.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD4","text":"Character becomes a relational risk-and-benefit architecture in which capacities receive value from direction and dispositions from their effects across roles.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD5","text":"Shared moral discourse can persist without convergence between calm verdicts and personal affections.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SD6","text":"Moral perfection combines interpersonal harmlessness and usefulness with preservation of the agent’s own good.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX1","text":"Partial sympathy is not epistemically final or incapable of correction.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX2","text":"A shared moral standard does not require equal emotional concern for all persons.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX3","text":"Utility cannot become morally salient to a disinterested spectator without sympathetic access to those affected.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0024","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX4","text":"Virtue cannot be identified with maximal public impact, since minute tenderness can possess exceptional merit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX5","text":"Powerful capacities do not carry self-validating moral worth.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX6","text":"Natural occurrence is not equivalent to moral innocence.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:DNX7","text":"Immediate agreeableness and beneficial consequence are not reducible to a single source of approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Deep Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SNX1","text":"Universal benevolence is unavailable as an ordinary human motive; generalized judgment substitutes for universal affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SNX2","text":"A view from nowhere is unavailable; moral generality is constructed through correction among situated spectators.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SNX3","text":"Complete coincidence between moral verdict and actual love or hatred is absent from the system.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SNX4","text":"No explicit common metric reconciles utility, immediate tenderness, fitting anger, and self-regard when they conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:SNX5","text":"Character conceived apart from relations is excluded from final evaluation; moral identity appears only through effects on others and self.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics"},{"number":"8","name":"Formal Generative Schema","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS1","text":"Experience of human affairs → recognition of limited generosity → restriction of appraisal to feasible relational spheres.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS2","text":"Private interest → contradictory verdicts → conversational pressure → general standard.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS3","text":"Distance-attenuated sympathy → reflective correction → constant calm judgment → possible divergence from actual affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS4","text":"Utility channel: passion → advantage or harm to connected persons → sympathetic uptake → approval or blame.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS5","text":"Immediate channel: displayed tenderness → contagious love → agreeable movement → transfer to bearer → affection and merit.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS6","text":"Regulatory channel: capacity → benevolent direction or hostile direction → usefulness or magnified harm.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS7","text":"Anger boundary: constitutional passion → limited degree → excusability or praise; escalation → cruelty → suffering → intensified hatred.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:FGS8","text":"Closure: test every social relation → no relation refused → test self-relation → complete character.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema"},{"number":"9","name":"Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:AF1","text":"Limited generosity → sphere-bounded appraisal → contradictions among situated judgments → corrected common standpoint → utility-based merit → immediately sympathetic merit → qualified anger → relational test of perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"10","name":"Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME1","text":"Goodness is relocated from universal benevolent reach to beneficial operation within an agent’s feasible relational sphere.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME2","text":"Distance is reclassified from a moral difference into a perspectival distortion correctable by reflection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME3","text":"Moral constancy is separated from emotional impartiality.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME4","text":"Benevolence is reclassified as the directing principle that determines the social valence of power and ability.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME5","text":"Merit is expanded beyond useful consequences to include the immediate sympathetic pleasure of tender affection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME6","text":"Anger is reclassified by degree and relational consequence rather than by a simple agreeable–disagreeable opposition.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ME7","text":"Character perfection is transformed into role-complete relational desirability supplemented by adequate self-relation.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Direct","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Map-Effects"},{"number":"11","name":"Altitude Levels","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ALT1","text":"Surface: explanation of the merit of benevolent qualities and the vice of cruelty and inhumanity.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ALT2","text":"Structural: social intercourse converts conflicting situated sympathies into a stable standard of character judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ALT3","text":"Metastrategic: evaluation proceeds through a generalized relational standpoint and two distinct channels of merit rather than through universal motivation or utility alone.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:ALT4","text":"Ontological/Temporal: human beings possess limited affections, contagious sympathy, and inherent anger, while repeated changes of position and reflection stabilize judgment over time.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Altitude Levels"},{"number":"12","name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT1","text":"CH_THN_SIGB:A1–CH_THN_SIGB:A14 are sufficient to reconstruct the dual-source account of benevolent merit and its relational closure.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT2","text":"The precise mechanism by which conversation produces an inalterable standard remains underdetermined.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT3","text":"No weighting rule determines precedence between beneficial tendency and immediate agreeableness when they conflict.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT4","text":"The structure is coherent insofar as sympathy mediates both consequence-based and immediately affective approval.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Composite","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT5","text":"The strongest generative linkage runs from positional contradiction through reflective correction to a common relational standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT6","text":"The optical-distance analogy establishes the need for correction but does not prove the unique content of the corrected moral judgment.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Negative","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT7","text":"Similarity-based approval reintroduces spectator partiality into the mechanism intended to correct private standpoint.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT8","text":"The merit assigned to excessive tenderness can conflict with the requirement of adequate self-relation unless an unstated threshold limits excess.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT9","text":"The qualified praise of anger prevents immediate agreeableness from functioning as a universal criterion and leaves the system evaluatively plural.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Tension","review":"unreviewed"}]},{"id":"CH_THN_SIGB:MRT10","text":"Reconstruction from the extracted system preserves the movement from limited affection through generalized judgment and dual merit to relational perfection.","refs":[],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0001","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0002","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0003","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0004","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0005","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0006","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"},{"id":"RAW_THN_SIGB:P0007","type":"Structural","review":"unreviewed"}]}],"original_name":"Minimal Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CH","kicker":"Chapter 27","title":"Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence","context":"Chapter 27","original_title":"Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence","original_context":"Chapter 27"},"original_title":"Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_01","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 01–03","order":1,"parents":["CH_THN_INTR","CH_THN_SIOO","CH_THN_SIDS"],"revision":"CL_THN_01@R001","sha256":"4cab4837a91c5ae44fcf685f1482d0d7493e68889f490a353872123e71debd89","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-01","text":"Scientific insecurity is traced beneath disciplinary disputes to inadequate knowledge of the human faculties through which all sciences are constituted and judged.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:AS1","CH_THN_INTR:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-02","text":"Human nature is therefore installed as a metascientific object, but its investigation is denied access to mental essence and restricted to experienced operations and effects.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD1","CH_THN_INTR:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-03","text":"This restriction is operationalized by decomposing mental life into impressions and ideas, then into simple and complex perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D4","CH_THN_SIOO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-04","text":"Simple ideational content is genealogically subordinated to prior impressions, while complex imagination remains free to recombine impression-derived elements.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIOO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-05","text":"The linear copy model mutates when impressions divide into sensation and reflection: ideas remain effects of sensation but become causes of passions and further perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-06","text":"Priority consequently becomes relational rather than absolute, distinguishing original, correspondent, causal, and expository order.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A10","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-07","text":"The inquiry begins from ideas not because ideas are ultimately first, but because they are the operative antecedents of the reflective phenomena selected for moral investigation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A9","CH_THN_SIDS:A10","CH_THN_SIDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BAS-08","text":"The cluster moves by recursive tightening: foundational crisis → empirical restriction → perceptual genealogy → recursive mediation → methodological reversal → bounded closure at unknown sensory origins.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:AS7","CH_THN_SIOO:AF1","CH_THN_SIDS:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] A secure science of human nature is the strategic center of scientific reconstruction because human faculties mediate every scientific judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A5","CH_THN_INTR:A7","CH_THN_INTR:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Mental inquiry must proceed from experienced operations, effects, and relations rather than from speculative access to mental essence.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A9","CH_THN_INTR:A10","CH_THN_INTR:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Empirical investigation of mind requires both a taxonomy of perceptions and a genealogy specifying their dependency and order.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D1","CH_THN_SIOO:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Simple ideas generally derive on first appearance from corresponding prior simple impressions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-05","text":"[Emergent] Restriction at the level of simple content permits recombination and recursive productivity at the level of complex ideas and later perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Temporal priority establishes causal direction only within a specified dependency relation; it cannot assign one absolute order to impressions and ideas as total classes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A10","CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:D2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Ideas are derivative copies relative to sensation but productive mediators relative to impressions of reflection.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:A4","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Mental life forms an asymmetrically grounded recursive sequence: sensation → idea → reflection → idea → possible further perception.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:FGS3","CH_THN_SIDS:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Proper methodological order follows the operative antecedents of the selected object, not necessarily the chronology of absolute origin.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A9","CH_THN_SIDS:A12","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-10","text":"[Terminal] The ultimate causes of sensation and the original qualities of human nature remain unavailable; legitimate explanation terminates at maximally general experienced relations.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_INTR:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:A3","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Scientific warrant at this level belongs to stable empirical generalization rather than exceptionless universality.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A18","CH_THN_SIOO:D8","CH_THN_SIDS:A1","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] The human subject functions as both the medium of inquiry and a relay within the perception-chain being investigated.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD6","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-01","text":"Stable identity: experience remains the admissible ground of inquiry from the foundational program through the perceptual genealogy.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A8","CH_THN_SIOO:A9","CH_THN_SIDS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-02","text":"Functional recurrence: observation of effects becomes classification of perceptions, comparison of correspondences, and reconstruction of temporal dependencies.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A10","CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_SIOO:AS5","CH_THN_SIDS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-03","text":"Mutation: general impression-priority is restricted from a class-wide hierarchy to indexed relations between particular perceptions and genealogical strata.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:D2","CH_THN_SIDS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-04","text":"Reversal: impressions-first genetic order yields an ideas-first exposition once reflective impressions become the focal object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:D1","CH_THN_SIDS:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-05","text":"Mutation: the idea changes from faint copy and terminal effect into an intermediate cause capable of producing desire, aversion, hope, and fear.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A3","CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-06","text":"Escalation: the simple copy relation expands into a recursively extensible production machine without losing its asymmetrical sensory grounding.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:D4","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-07","text":"Displacement: the rejection of speculative metaphysics becomes an operational rejection of innate simple content and self-originating reflective life.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:NX7","CH_THN_SIOO:D9","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-08","text":"Terminal transformation: ignorance changes from a sign of disciplinary failure into the legitimate endpoint of genealogy at unknown sensory causes.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D6","CH_THN_INTR:SD5","CH_THN_SIDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-09","text":"Partial disappearance: ordinary conduct is declared the proper moral archive, but the subsequent machinery relies chiefly on introspective taxonomy, deprivation, and conceptual genealogy.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A17","CH_THN_INTR:A18","CH_THN_SIOO:AS3","CH_THN_SIDS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RMD-10","text":"Contained anomaly: the missing shade and repeated qualifiers convert strict universal precedence into an exception-tolerant empirical rule without being allowed to reorganize the system.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A15","CH_THN_SIOO:A16","CH_THN_SIDS:A1","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-01","text":"[Local] The initial diagnosis of philosophical discredit and rhetorical controversy motivates the reconstruction but does not itself govern the later perceptual machinery.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A1","CH_THN_INTR:A2","CH_THN_INTR:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-02","text":"[Local] Force and liveliness provide the initial impression–idea distinction, but exceptional states prevent them from functioning as the complete current-level criterion.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A2","CH_THN_SIOO:A3","CH_THN_SIOO:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-03","text":"[Local] The missing-shade case remains a concentrated anomaly rather than an alternative generative principle.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A15","CH_THN_SIOO:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-04","text":"[Scales upward] The prohibition on essence-first explanation becomes the common boundary governing both the science of human nature and the origin of perception.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A9","CH_THN_INTR:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] The simple–complex distinction links empirical restriction to imaginative productivity: primitive content is constrained while complex formation remains open.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A5","CH_THN_SIOO:A6","CH_THN_SIOO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-06","text":"[Catalyst] Division of impressions into sensation and reflection converts the linear copy model into an asymmetrical recursive system.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A2","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-07","text":"[Superseded] Undifferentiated impressions-first method is recoded as a default valid only before object, scope, and internal dependency are specified.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:D1","CH_THN_SIDS:D2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:CGD-08","text":"[Scales upward] Disciplinary exclusion of sensory causes becomes a general methodological principle: proximate operations may be investigated securely without resolving absolute origins.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-01","text":"Mental science is not an inquiry from substance or essence to deduced operations.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:NX7","CH_THN_INTR:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-02","text":"Simple ideational content is not generally generated by an autonomous productive intellect.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:DNX1","CH_THN_SIOO:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-03","text":"Complex invention does not establish the production of primitive content independent of prior impressions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D6","CH_THN_SIOO:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-04","text":"Impressions and ideas do not stand in a causally symmetrical or uniformly ordered relation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:DNX3","CH_THN_SIDS:D2","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-05","text":"Reflective impressions are not predominantly original, immediate occurrences independent of antecedent ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A10","CH_THN_SIDS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-06","text":"Chronological firstness does not by itself determine the proper order of inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:DNX3","CH_THN_SIDS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-07","text":"Scientific security does not require access to ultimate causes, essences, or an exceptionless first principle.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD5","CH_THN_SIOO:SNX3","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:GNX-08","text":"Controlled self-intervention is not the exclusive route to empirical warrant in the science of human nature.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A16","CH_THN_INTR:A17","CH_THN_INTR:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any model in which cognition can secure itself from a standpoint outside human faculties and perceptual mediation.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:DNX1","CH_THN_INTR:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-02","text":"It excludes a foundationalism for which only knowledge of first causes can warrant knowledge of derivative regularities.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:DNX6","CH_THN_INTR:SNX4","CH_THN_SIDS:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-03","text":"It disqualifies the mind as a static receptacle: derivative perceptions must be capable of returning as causes within reflective life.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-04","text":"It rejects a single linear chronology as sufficient to organize origin, causation, correspondence, recurrence, and exposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-05","text":"It cannot admit that reflective phenomena are irreducibly singular, because their scientific treatment requires recurrent relations between ideas, circumstances, and passions.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:DNX3","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:DBN-06","text":"It implicitly rejects strict falsification by isolated anomaly, preserving general empirical rules through rarity and qualification.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A15","CH_THN_SIOO:A16","CH_THN_SIDS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSA-01","text":"The system circles an unavailable absolute origin: sensory impressions ground the genealogy, but the cause of their first appearance remains outside the inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A3","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSA-02","text":"No unmediated foundation is available: science depends on human faculties, while those faculties are accessible only through their mediated effects.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A5","CH_THN_INTR:A10","CH_THN_INTR:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSA-03","text":"Purely immediate passion is excluded as the governing model; reflection is structurally dependent on representational return.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A10","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSA-04","text":"Exceptionless closure remains unavailable because the copy principle, impression-priority, and recursive sequence survive only through controlled qualifications.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D8","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX4","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSA-05","text":"The absent possibility organizing the cluster is a nonrecursive foundation capable of grounding cognition without already employing cognition, comparison, and perceptual succession.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:MRT8","CH_THN_SIOO:SD4","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-01","text":"The impression–idea genealogy operationalizes the introductory science of human nature by replacing speculative anthropology with observable dependencies among perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD1","CH_THN_INTR:D4","CH_THN_SIOO:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-02","text":"Foundational security is reconstructed as a bounded genealogy: reliable order among perceptions can be known even when mental essence and sensory origins cannot.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD2","CH_THN_SIOO:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-03","text":"Because ideas are both impression-derived and reflectively productive, human reasoning is neither self-originating nor causally inert.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD6","CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-04","text":"The science intended to provide a foundation begins from an intermediate layer—ideas—because absolute origins are both inaccessible and unnecessary for the selected inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5","CH_THN_SIDS:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-05","text":"Recursive mental productivity does not abolish empiricism: later perceptions may generate one another while the primitive content of the sequence remains asymmetrically grounded in sensation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4","CH_THN_SIDS:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-06","text":"The naturalistic protocol for moral science and the later reliance on introspective perceptual taxonomy remain methodologically misaligned unless introspection is treated as observation of naturally occurring operations.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD3","CH_THN_SIOO:AS3","CH_THN_SIDS:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-07","text":"Empirical certainty must mean robust qualified regularity rather than universality, because the cluster retains both the missing-shade anomaly and rare exceptions to impression-priority.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD5","CH_THN_SIOO:D8","CH_THN_SIDS:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:XD-08","text":"The proposed causal genealogy incurs a methodological debt: it uses conjunction and succession to establish dependence before the promised analysis of causation secures those criteria.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A11","CH_THN_SIOO:SD4","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-01","text":"Foundationalism is converted from discovery of an ultimate ground into disciplined reconstruction of dependency relations within an acknowledged horizon of ignorance.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD2","CH_THN_INTR:SD5","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-02","text":"The science of human nature is necessarily reflexive: faculties investigate the perceptual operations through which those same faculties know and judge.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD1","CH_THN_INTR:SD6","CH_THN_SIOO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-03","text":"The operative subject becomes a relay rather than an origin: it receives impressions, retains copies, returns ideas, and produces reflective effects.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:A4","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-04","text":"Mental temporality is layered: irreversible sensory precedence grounds a downstream field in which derivative perceptions recursively become antecedents.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-05","text":"Ideational creativity is constrained but real: it cannot generally originate simple qualities, yet it can recombine contents and generate new reflective states.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","CH_THN_SIOO:SNX2","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:BSD-06","text":"Methodological relevance supersedes absolute origin: an inquiry may begin from the nearest productive condition of its object while leaving the remote ground unexplained.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:A12","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-01","text":"Foundational recursion: insecure sciences require a science of human nature, but that science must employ the cognition and comparison it is intended to secure.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD1","CH_THN_INTR:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-02","text":"Perceptual production loop: sensory impression → retained idea → reflective impression → reflective idea → possible further impression or idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:FGS3","CH_THN_SIDS:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-03","text":"Restriction–proliferation loop: simple content is empirically restricted, complex ideas recombine it, and returned ideas produce additional reflective states.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-04","text":"Boundary loop: inquiry reaches unknown origin, withdraws to observable effects, compresses them into general principles, and treats the acknowledged limit as closure.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:D5","CH_THN_INTR:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-05","text":"Anomaly-containment loop: universal formulation → counterinstance or qualifier → restriction of scope → retention of the governing maxim.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:FGS7","CH_THN_SIDS:A1","CH_THN_SIDS:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-06","text":"Ideas become the principal conceptual attractor because they connect sensation to reflection, empirical derivation to productivity, and genetic order to expository method.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:RLE-07","text":"Sensation functions as the recessed ground-attractor: every recursive sequence depends on it, yet its causes are deferred beyond the operative system.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A3","CH_THN_SIDS:A8","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B01","text":"Disciplinary insecurity + rhetorical controversy → demand for a common foundation.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B02","text":"Mediation of science by human faculties → human nature installed as the common metascientific object.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B03","text":"Unavailable mental essence → effects, circumstances, and perceptions become the legitimate evidential field.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:FGS4","CH_THN_SIOO:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B04","text":"Perceptual classification → impression/idea distinction + simple/complex distinction → isolable units of genealogy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:FGS1","CH_THN_SIOO:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B05","text":"Simple correspondence + conjunction + first-appearance priority → general impression-to-idea derivation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:FGS4","CH_THN_SIOO:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B06","text":"Division of impressions → sensation grounds ideas → ideas generate reflection → recursive downstream production.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:FGS2","CH_THN_SIDS:FGS3","CH_THN_SIDS:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B07","text":"Selection of reflective phenomena + exclusion of sensory origins → ideas-first exposition within an asymmetrically sensation-grounded system.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:FGS5","CH_THN_SIDS:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FGS-B08","text":"Qualified regularity + terminal ignorance → bounded empirical foundation without essential or absolute closure.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:FGS5","CH_THN_INTR:FGS6","CH_THN_SIOO:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B01","text":"Foundational disorder → human nature identified as the common dependency beneath the sciences.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ME1","CH_THN_INTR:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B02","text":"Human nature as common dependency → experience and observation replace speculation about essence.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A8","CH_THN_INTR:A9","CH_THN_INTR:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B03","text":"Observational restriction → mental life decomposed into impressions, ideas, simple contents, and complex formations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D1","CH_THN_SIOO:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B04","text":"Perceptual decomposition → simple correspondence and temporal succession establish impression-to-idea genealogy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D3","CH_THN_SIOO:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B05","text":"Linear genealogy → division of impressions → ideas become mediators between sensation and reflection.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A2","CH_THN_SIDS:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B06","text":"Ideational mediation → recursive production → absolute priority replaced by indexed priority.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:D4","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:AFM-B07","text":"Indexed priority + disciplinary scope → ideas-first method → bounded closure at unknown sensory origins.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:SD3","CH_THN_SIDS:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-01","text":"Scientific disorder is redrawn as a problem of the human medium through which evidence, judgment, and assent operate.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ME1","CH_THN_INTR:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-02","text":"Foundation is reclassified from access to essence into a qualified causal genealogy of experienced perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ME4","CH_THN_SIOO:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-03","text":"Mind is mapped as a field of ordered perceptions rather than approached through an antecedently known mental substance.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:DNX2","CH_THN_SIOO:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-04","text":"The opposition between receptivity and productivity collapses: ideas are received copies at one stratum and productive causes at another.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIDS:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-05","text":"The opposition between linear and recursive causality is replaced by asymmetrical recursion: one-way grounding supports downstream feedback.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D4","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-06","text":"Priority is redistributed among genetic, correspondent, causal, disciplinary, and expository orders.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:ME3","CH_THN_SIDS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MME-07","text":"Certainty and ignorance cease to be opposites: reliable relations may be established precisely by refusing claims about ultimate origins.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ME7","CH_THN_SIDS:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: insecure sciences, disputed metaphysics, impressions, ideas, sensations, reflections, passions, and the correct order of inquiry constitute the explicit objects.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ALT1","CH_THN_SIOO:ALT1","CH_THN_SIDS:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: perception is organized by classification, copying, conjunction, temporal precedence, return, and recursive mediation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:ALT2","CH_THN_SIDS:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: inquiry is redirected from disciplinary disputes and innate-content vocabulary toward a bounded empirical genealogy of the operative antecedents of reflective life.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ALT3","CH_THN_SIOO:ALT3","CH_THN_SIDS:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological/Temporal level: reality enters mental science as successive effects whose priority is relational, whose sensory grounding is asymmetrical, and whose ultimate origin is unavailable.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:ALT4","CH_THN_SIOO:ALT4","CH_THN_SIDS:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological/Performative level: the argument performs its empiricism by beginning with a general distinction, submitting it to counterinstances and subdivisions, and revising method without abandoning the governing genealogy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:FGS3","CH_THN_SIOO:FGS7","CH_THN_SIDS:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-01","text":"The ordinary-life observational protocol is not fully integrated with the later introspective classification of perceptions and hypothetical deprivation tests.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A17","CH_THN_INTR:A18","CH_THN_SIOO:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-02","text":"Constant conjunction and temporal succession perform causal work before the system has supplied its full account of causation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD4","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-03","text":"The missing shade contradicts the unrestricted copy principle and is contained by rarity rather than incorporated into a revised theory of simple-content production.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIOO:A15","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-04","text":"Sensation is indispensable as the ultimate empirical ground but is simultaneously unexplained and excluded from the present inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A3","CH_THN_SIDS:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-05","text":"The science of human nature remains foundationally recursive because it uses perception, cognition, and comparison to establish the authority of perception, cognition, and comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:SD6","CH_THN_INTR:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:FL-06","text":"The mechanism by which an idea returns and generates a reflective impression is structurally indispensable but causally unexplained.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:A5","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MP-01","text":"Impression mutates from a uniform class opposed to ideas into a divided category containing both original sensations and idea-dependent reflections.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A1","CH_THN_SIDS:A2","CH_THN_SIDS:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MP-02","text":"Idea mutates from derivative effect into causal mediator without becoming the ultimate source of its primitive content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A13","CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MP-03","text":"Priority mutates from class-wide precedence into an indexed relation varying by correspondence, stratum, and selected object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2","CH_THN_SIDS:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MP-04","text":"Methodological order mutates from reproduction of genetic order into selection of the proximate antecedents needed to explain the focal phenomena.","refs":["CH_THN_SIDS:D1","CH_THN_SIDS:D6","CH_THN_SIDS:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is imaged as an asymmetrically grounded cascade whose lower sensory entry is one-way but whose upper perceptual strata form recursive feedback circuits.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:D4","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:HSI-02","text":"The subject is not operationally an originating substance but a switching relay that receives, copies, returns, recombines, and transmits perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:DNX2","CH_THN_SIOO:A14","CH_THN_SIDS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:HSI-03","text":"Time is stratified: first appearance grounds irreversible derivation, while later ideas and reflections repeatedly exchange the positions of cause and effect.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:A10","CH_THN_SIOO:A11","CH_THN_SIDS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:HSI-04","text":"Navigation consists in tracing effects, decomposing complexes, indexing priorities, following operative antecedents, and stopping where experience no longer supplies relations.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:A10","CH_THN_INTR:A12","CH_THN_SIOO:A6","CH_THN_SIDS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B01","text":"The current-level axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the movement from scientific crisis through empirical perceptual genealogy to recursive mediation and ideas-first method.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:MRT1","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT1","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B02","text":"The criteria for simplicity, exact correspondence, force, adequate observation, causal dependence, and the productive return of ideas remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:MRT2","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT2","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent if ultimate origin, genetic order, correspondent order, recursive production, and expository order remain strictly distinguished.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:MRT3","CH_THN_SIOO:MRT3","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B04","text":"Its strongest joint is the conversion of an empirically grounded linear copy relation into recursive reflective production without granting autonomous origin to ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:SD1","CH_THN_SIDS:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on sequencing and recursive return: the methodological reversal is intelligible only after sensory precedence, copying, and reflection have been separately established.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:AF1","CH_THN_SIDS:AF1","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B06","text":"The missing shade, rare exceptions, and unexplained return of ideas resist complete axiomatic reduction and prevent exceptionless closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOO:MRT6","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT3","CH_THN_SIDS:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_01:MRT-B07","text":"The reconstruction preserves the actual movement only by retaining the transition from foundational ambition to bounded genealogy, and from linear derivation to asymmetrical recursion.","refs":["CH_THN_INTR:AS7","CH_THN_SIOO:AS5","CH_THN_SIDS:AS5","CH_THN_SIDS:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 01–03","context":"Introduction · Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas · Sect. II. Division Of The Subject","original_title":"Chapters 01–03","original_context":"Introduction · Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas · Sect. II. Division Of The Subject"},"original_title":"Chapters 01–03","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_02","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 04–06","order":2,"parents":["CH_THN_SIIM","CH_THN_SICA","CH_THN_SIOQ"],"revision":"CL_THN_02@R001","sha256":"45e4013562a51cdd21b30227b6161661d519e35f7a537ee3768cfbc0d98aebb4","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-01","text":"The central problem is to explain how impression-derived ideas can preserve experience, depart from it, form regular complexes, and generate spatial and temporal order without acquiring independent content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:AS1","CH_THN_SICA:AS1","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-02","text":"The governing thesis is that ideational differences arise primarily from force, arrangement, recurrence, and relational operation rather than from heterogeneous elemental origins.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD1","CH_THN_SICA:SD1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-03","text":"The argument becomes an empirical genealogy of mental organization: impressions supply materials, while faculties and relations determine how those materials recur, combine, and appear.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:AS2","CH_THN_SICA:AS3","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-04","text":"Memory conserves force and experienced order; imagination releases copied elements from that order; association then supplies noncompulsory pathways through the liberated field.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-05","text":"Spatiality and temporality complete the architecture by showing that order itself is not an additional impression but the disposition or succession of content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-06","text":"Mental freedom is therefore bounded twice: it cannot originate unprecedented simple contents, and its ordinary transitions remain weighted by natural associative tendencies.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:NX4","CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-07","text":"The movement is recursive rather than linear: copied order is preserved, dissolved, probabilistically regenerated, and finally exposed as constitutive of the ideas of space and time.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BAS-08","text":"Closure occurs through paired limits: conception terminates in content-filled minima, while explanation terminates at experimentally established principles whose ultimate causes remain unknown.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Every legitimate idea must derive its elemental content from antecedent impression-material.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIIM:NX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-02","text":"[Emergent] Elemental provenance and relational organization are distinct explanatory levels: identical copied materials can support preservation, recombination, association, or ordered appearance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME2","CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Order is the common object of the cluster: memory conserves it, imagination alters it, association regenerates it, and space and time consist in its coexistent or successive forms.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Difference licenses distinguishability and separability, but separation remains possible only where a positive component can be conceived apart.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A8","CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A9","CH_THN_SIOQ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Memory is recurrence under retained vivacity and fidelity to the experienced form, order, and position of its objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] Imagination is dependent at the level of simple contents but free to separate, transpose, and recombine those contents at the level of arrangement.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Associative tendency mediates between compulsory connexion and random succession, producing regularity without abolishing imaginative liberty.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:D1","CH_THN_SICA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Resemblance, spatiotemporal contiguity, and cause and effect are the primary operators by which one idea introduces another.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A4","CH_THN_SICA:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SICA:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-09","text":"[Mutating] Relational efficacy is graded: it propagates through intermediaries, weakens with distance, and expands causally from production to capacity and social influence.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Space and time are ordered manners of appearance rather than independent impressions: extension is coexistent disposition, and duration is noncoexistent succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Abstraction produces general representation by selectively regarding a determinate particular, not by generating a contentless universal.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:D2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] An idea’s genealogy limits its legitimate application: recombination may alter conjunctions, but predication cannot properly exceed the kind of content or order supplied by the originating impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:DNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] Empirical explanation may stop at a recurrent principle established through its effects even when the ultimate cause of that principle remains inaccessible.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SICA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GA-14","text":"[Terminal] Conceptual decomposition cannot terminate in nonentity: spatial minima must retain sensible quality, and temporal minima must be filled by successive existence.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A13","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:A15","CH_THN_SIOQ:D12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-01","text":"The copy principle retains stable identity across the cluster, functioning first as a faculty constraint and later as a test of spatial, temporal, and predicative legitimacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIIM:NX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-02","text":"Order mutates from a criterion of mnemonic fidelity into an object of imaginative alteration, an associative product, and finally the constitutive form of extension and duration.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-03","text":"Separability expands from the ground of imaginative liberty to a universal compositional premise, then reverses into a limiting test when no distinct spatial or temporal content can be isolated.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A10","CH_THN_SIOQ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-04","text":"Imagination changes function from a faculty of transposition into a regulated field whose transitions are weighted by resemblance, contiguity, and causation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-05","text":"Contiguity migrates from the experienced order preserved by memory into a customary associative pathway grounded in spatial and temporal arrangements.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-06","text":"Complex ideas divide into three generative regimes: freely rearranged fictions, associatively stabilized objects of thought, and compounds whose order constitutes extension or duration.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SICA:A16","CH_THN_SIOQ:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-07","text":"Causation migrates from an associative relation between ideas into a model of production, counterfactual capacity, authority, and social subordination.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-08","text":"Vivacity remains indispensable for establishing the memory–imagination distinction but disappears from the later explanations of associative order and spatiotemporal composition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A4","CH_THN_SICA:AS4","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-09","text":"Connexion changes valence: memory approximates inseparable conservation, association supplies nonnecessary cohesion, and space and time remain nonseparable from some positive content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A12","CH_THN_SICA:NX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RMD-10","text":"Fiction reverses from a successful exercise of combinatorial liberty into a mark of illegitimate application when duration or spatiality is detached from every possible originating content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME5","CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX7","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-01","text":"[Local] The precise degree of vivacity distinguishing memory from imagination remains a faculty-specific criterion and does not govern association or spatiotemporal conception.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A4","CH_THN_SIIM:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The claim that sight and touch alone provide extension’s source is required for spatial genealogy but does not scale into a general theory of all ideational recurrence.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:D11","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-03","text":"[Local] The asserted exhaustiveness of resemblance, contiguity, and causation remains local to the associative taxonomy; the current level requires only nonnecessary relational regulation.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A4","CH_THN_SICA:SNX3","CH_THN_SICA:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-04","text":"[Scaled] The copy principle scales upward because it constrains faculty content, abstract representation, spatial and temporal conception, and semantic application.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-05","text":"[Scaled] The distinction between content and arrangement becomes the cluster’s primary architecture, subsuming memory, imagination, association, space, and time.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Separability connects imaginative composition to conceptual analysis, but its cross-unit use requires distinguishing separation from an original complex from separation from every content-bearing instance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D6","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A10","CH_THN_SIOQ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-07","text":"[Catalyst] Imaginative freedom generates the problem solved by association: once ideas can combine arbitrarily, recurrent regularity demands a non-random but noncompulsory principle.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:AS1","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-08","text":"[Superseded] The binary opposition between necessary determination and pure chance is replaced by graded tendency, custom, and attenuating relational force.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SICA:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-09","text":"[Recoded] Fiction is divided into lawful relational recombination and genealogically unsupported predication; novelty alone no longer determines illegitimacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:CGD-10","text":"[Transition] The expansion of causal association into social power demonstrates domain migration, but it does not itself govern the spatial and temporal ontology of the cluster.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:D4","CH_THN_SICA:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:MRT7","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-01","text":"No faculty, abstraction, or relational operation can originate an unprecedented simple idea independently of prior impression-material.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:NX1","CH_THN_SIIM:NX4","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-02","text":"Copy-dependence does not entail preservation of original conjunctions, positions, or sequences.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SIIM:DNX1","CH_THN_SICA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-03","text":"Mental regularity is neither necessary determination nor random succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:NX1","CH_THN_SICA:NX3","CH_THN_SICA:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-04","text":"Order, extension, and duration are not additional simple impressions superadded to the contents they organize.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:NX2","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX6","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-05","text":"Generality cannot consist in an indeterminate or contentless universal representation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX3","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-06","text":"Neither empty spatiality nor bare duration can remain positively conceivable after all sensible or successive existence is removed.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:NX4","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX9","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-07","text":"Memory cannot properly vary experienced order as freely as imagination, while imagination cannot escape impression-derived elements.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:NX2","CH_THN_SIIM:NX4","CH_THN_SIIM:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:GNX-08","text":"Empirical adequacy does not require speculative access to the ultimate mechanism producing associative attraction.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:NX6","CH_THN_SICA:NX7","CH_THN_SICA:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-01","text":"The system requires the falsity of any model in which the origin of an idea determines all of its possible arrangements; otherwise imagination and association collapse into mere replay.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:DNX1","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-02","text":"It excludes both the passive-copy model of mind and the sovereign-creation model: the mind transforms relations without supplying its own simple materials.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","CH_THN_SICA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-03","text":"It disqualifies any account that treats relations as independently given substances rather than operations, dispositions, successions, or tendencies among contents.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-04","text":"It rejects an ontology of empty frameworks in which space and time exist for thought independently of sensible points or successive existences.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:D11","CH_THN_SIOQ:D12","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-05","text":"It excludes semantic autonomy from genealogy: common language, objective occurrence, or habitual predication cannot by themselves legitimate an idea’s application.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX5","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-06","text":"It requires that stable mental order be explainable without necessary connexion, because necessity would destroy imaginative freedom while pure chance would destroy recurrence.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:DBN-07","text":"It rejects the demand that every empirically usable principle possess an independently known metaphysical substrate or ultimate cause.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SICA:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSA-01","text":"The deepest unavailable object is pure order without ordered content: empty space, bare time, relation without relata, and memory without a prior arrangement are structurally inadmissible.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSA-02","text":"Sovereign mental spontaneity is absent; all available freedom is post-impressional freedom exercised through separation, recombination, and selective regard.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSA-03","text":"Necessary mental connexion is the missing force around which association is organized: gentle attraction occupies its functional place without acquiring its compulsion.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:NX1","CH_THN_SICA:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSA-04","text":"Ultimate explanatory grounding remains absent; “original qualities of human nature” marks the point where operational regularity survives without disclosed origin.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:SNX2","CH_THN_SICA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSA-05","text":"No independent tribunal verifies original order, absolute succession, or pure spatiality apart from the perceptual materials whose fidelity or arrangement is being judged.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SNX4","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX3","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-01","text":"Mental novelty consists primarily in relational transformation: copied elements remain constrained while their conjunction, sequence, emphasis, and representative function vary.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-02","text":"Association supplies default trajectories to a freely recombinable imagination, explaining stable complex thought without converting tendency into necessity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-03","text":"Memory and associated imagination partially overlap because customary contiguity can lead imagination to reproduce the experienced sequences that memory preserves by fidelity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SICA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-04","text":"Because space and time are arrangements of content, mnemonic fidelity conserves constitutive coexistence and succession rather than merely locating objects inside independent containers.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D3","CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-05","text":"Customary association by contiguity depends on perceived spatial and temporal order: sensory succession supplies the sequence that custom converts into an imaginative transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-06","text":"Separability is conditional rather than absolute: components may be detached from original complexes, but extension cannot be detached from all sensible quality and time cannot be detached from every succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:D8","CH_THN_SIOQ:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-07","text":"Fiction bifurcates into legitimate composition and illegitimate predication: an unexperienced complex can be formed lawfully, whereas duration without change exceeds its genetic basis.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SIIM:ME5","CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-08","text":"Abstract space and time demonstrate how generality can arise without spontaneous simple content: imagination varies instances while retaining a recurrent disposition or succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:D2","CH_THN_SIOQ:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-09","text":"The expansion of causal association into power and government shows that ideational relations can organize social complexes by converting possible action into associative proximity.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11","CH_THN_SICA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Direct","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:XD-10","text":"Impression-audit and effect-oriented inquiry combine into a restricted science of mind that maps sources, operations, and consequences without postulating inaccessible substrates.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SICA:SD4","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-01","text":"The cluster produces a relational ontology in which many apparent mental objects are reclassified as organized configurations of more elementary content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD1","CH_THN_SICA:ME5","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes a differential operator rather than an origin: it retains, weakens, separates, recombines, follows tendencies, and selectively generalizes impression-derived materials.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-03","text":"Temporality is generated through perceptible succession, conserved through memory, routed through customary association, and rearranged through imagination without becoming an empty medium.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-04","text":"Novelty becomes finite and grammatical: new complexes arise through transformations of relation and order rather than through creation of new simple elements.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SICA:A15","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-05","text":"Epistemic legitimacy is jointly genetic and operational: an idea must possess an impression-source and must not be applied beyond the structure that source can represent.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-06","text":"Causal explanation acquires political reach when powers of possible production are treated as sufficient to associate authority, duty, government, and obedience.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11","CH_THN_SICA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-07","text":"Methodologically, the system substitutes source-tracing, compositional analysis, and observable effects for unrestricted speculation about hidden essences.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SICA:ME6","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:BSD-08","text":"Reality is thinkable only as content-bearing order: relations may vary, weaken, or generalize, but no positive spatial, temporal, or ideational structure survives complete removal of its terms.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD4","CH_THN_SIOQ:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-01","text":"Impression → idea → genealogical audit → permissible operation → renewed audit forms the basic recursive loop governing both composition and application.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-02","text":"Impression → memory recurrence → imaginative release → associative rerouting → stable complex converts experienced order into a reproducible but nonnecessary pattern.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-03","text":"Difference → distinction → separation → recombination → recurrent complex → demand for association transforms a logical test into a generative mental machine.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-04","text":"Sensory succession → custom → associative expectation → repeated transition recursively reinforces temporal contiguity as an imaginative pathway.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-05","text":"Order is the principal attractor: it returns as original position, changed conjunction, associative sequence, spatial disposition, temporal succession, and abstract invariant.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-06","text":"Causation follows an escalation path from ideational transition to actual production, possible exertion, interpersonal influence, and institutional hierarchy.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-07","text":"Particular configuration → selective regard → general term → heterogeneous applications → renewed particular representation forms the abstraction loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:D2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:RLE-08","text":"Absence of a distinct impression → non-distinguishability → non-separability → reclassification as manner of appearance is the cluster’s principal eliminative loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A11","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX2","CH_THN_SIOQ:NX6","CH_THN_SIOQ:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B01","text":"Impression-field supplies simple sensible and reflective materials; no subsequent operation adds unprecedented elemental content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIIM:NX4","CH_THN_SIOQ:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B02","text":"Impression-material recurs as ideas differentiated by retained force and fidelity: strong ordered recurrence produces memory, diminished recurrence opens imagination.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A4","CH_THN_SIIM:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B03","text":"Distinguishable components become separable; separable components become recombinable; recombination produces unexperienced complexes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SICA:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B04","text":"Recurrent regularity among free combinations generates the requirement for a bond weaker than necessity and stronger than chance.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B05","text":"Resemblance, contiguity, and causation route ideational transitions through direct and progressively attenuated relational chains.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A4","CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B06","text":"Associative routing stabilizes simple ideas into complex objects of thought while preserving the possibility of divergent recombination.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B07","text":"Analysis of order reveals spatial coexistence and temporal succession as arrangements of content rather than independent receptacles.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B08","text":"Selective recurrence of disposition or succession permits abstract representation without generating a contentless universal.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:D2","CH_THN_SIOQ:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B09","text":"Genealogical testing excludes empty space, bare duration, changeless time, and other applications that outrun possible originating impressions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:D11","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FGS-B10","text":"The machine terminates in content-filled minima and experimentally established original tendencies rather than metaphysically disclosed ultimate causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B01","text":"Impression-given content → copied elemental ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A1","CH_THN_SIIM:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B02","text":"Copied recurrence → differentiation by vivacity and fidelity → memory/imagination bifurcation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A3","CH_THN_SIIM:A4","CH_THN_SIIM:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B03","text":"Imaginative release → separation and recombination → novel but derivative complexes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A7","CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SIIM:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B04","text":"Free combination → recurrent uniformity → rejection of chance → gentle associative regulation.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SICA:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B05","text":"Associative regulation → relational chains → causal expansion → stabilized complex thought and social power.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SICA:A11","CH_THN_SICA:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B06","text":"Analysis of relation and order → coexistence/succession distinction → reconstruction of space and time as content-arrangements.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A7","CH_THN_SIOQ:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:AFM-B07","text":"Impression genealogy → separability test → exclusion of empty forms and unsupported predications → content-filled conceptual closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A11","CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:A14","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-01","text":"Ideas cease to form a homogeneous class of copies and become differentiated by force, fidelity, compositional role, associative position, and representative function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME1","CH_THN_SICA:ME3","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-02","text":"Provenance and operation become separable dimensions: common impression-derived materials can be archived, recombined, associated, or abstractly deployed.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME2","CH_THN_SICA:ME1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-03","text":"Order becomes a productive object rather than a passive background, spanning mnemonic conservation, imaginative transposition, associative transition, and spatiotemporal constitution.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME3","CH_THN_SICA:ME3","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-04","text":"The opposition between freedom and determination collapses into bounded liberty governed by recurrent but defeasible tendencies.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:ME2","CH_THN_SICA:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-05","text":"Space and time are removed from the category of independent containers and relocated within the ordered appearance of positive contents.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:ME1","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-06","text":"Fiction is reclassified by operation: recombined complexes remain conceivable, while contentless forms and genealogically unsupported applications do not.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ME5","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME5","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-07","text":"Causation becomes a cross-domain operator connecting natural production, possible action, mental transition, and institutional authority.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:ME4","CH_THN_SICA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MME-08","text":"Philosophical method is redrawn around impression-audit, operational decomposition, and experimentally observable effects rather than unrestricted metaphysical postulation.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:ME6","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The explicit objects are memory, imagination, ideational association, extension, duration, abstraction, and the formation of complex ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ALT1","CH_THN_SICA:ALT1","CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] The active mechanisms are copying, recurrence, vivacity loss, order preservation, separation, recombination, associative routing, abstraction, and decomposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ALT2","CH_THN_SICA:ALT2","CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Conceptual disputes are reframed as questions of source, operation, relational distance, and permissible application rather than contests among untested metaphysical entities.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ALT3","CH_THN_SICA:ALT3","CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Reality available to thought consists of content-bearing configurations, while time emerges from perceptible noncoexistence and is preserved or rearranged through mental recurrence.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:ALT4","CH_THN_SICA:ALT4","CH_THN_SIOQ:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The system repeatedly performs its own claim by decomposing apparent unities, tracing their sources, removing unsupported additions, and reconstructing them as relational operations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:AS2","CH_THN_SICA:AS3","CH_THN_SIOQ:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-01","text":"The exact-copy principle strains against abstraction, which suppresses determinate sensible peculiarities while requiring a particular sensible representative.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-02","text":"Universal separability of simple ideas conflicts with the insistence that spatiality cannot be separated from all sensible quality and temporality cannot be separated from every succession.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A10","CH_THN_SIOQ:D8","CH_THN_SIOQ:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-03","text":"Memory’s fidelity criterion presupposes access to an original arrangement whose independent recovery the system does not explain.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SIIM:SNX4","CH_THN_SIIM:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-04","text":"Vivacity and relational fidelity can classify the same recurrence differently, yet mixed cases are not formally resolved.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD1","CH_THN_SIIM:SNX3","CH_THN_SIIM:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-05","text":"Imagination is described as freely combinatorial and universally guided by association, but the strength and defeasibility of that guidance remain unquantified.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SICA:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-06","text":"Association is treated as an original quality, while contiguity is explained through acquired custom; the relation between innate principle and acquired pathway remains unresolved.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SICA:A13","CH_THN_SICA:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-07","text":"Causation receives maximal associative force before its nature is analyzed, and its extension into duty and authority compresses normative relations into productive power.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A11","CH_THN_SICA:MRT4","CH_THN_SICA:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-08","text":"The inference from an idea’s perceptual genealogy to the impossibility or illegitimacy of independent existence exceeds what a theory of conception alone securely establishes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A12","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT7","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:FL-09","text":"Experienced duration varies with perceptual rate, but the relation between variable experienced time and objective succession remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:D5","CH_THN_SIOQ:D6","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-01","text":"Order mutates from a standard against which recurrence is judged into a generative operator and then into the constitutive structure of spatial and temporal ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A2","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-02","text":"Separability mutates from a positive authorization of imaginative freedom into a negative criterion for denying independent space and time.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-03","text":"Relation mutates from preserved or altered conjunction into a graded force capable of producing recall, complex thought, and social hierarchy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SICA:A8","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-04","text":"Fiction mutates from lawful recombination into genealogical excess when an idea is predicated where no corresponding perceptual order can occur.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:D5","CH_THN_SIOQ:D9","CH_THN_SIOQ:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-05","text":"Causation mutates from one associative transition among ideas into a generalized schema of actual production, latent capacity, and institutional command.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A7","CH_THN_SICA:A9","CH_THN_SICA:A10","CH_THN_SICA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MP-06","text":"Abstraction mutates copying from literal reproduction into selective representation while retaining dependence on determinate particular content.","refs":["CH_THN_SIOQ:A1","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a field of content-filled points and events whose coexistences, successions, similarities, and productive relations generate the structures ordinarily mistaken for independent forms.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:A4","CH_THN_SIOQ:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:HSI-02","text":"The mind is an archive-workshop-network: memory preserves routes, imagination cuts and recombines components, and association weights the available transitions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD2","CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:HSI-03","text":"The subject is not a sovereign producer but a switching point through which impression-derived materials acquire differential force, order, and relational direction.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SNX1","CH_THN_SICA:A3","CH_THN_SIOQ:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:HSI-04","text":"Time is a pulse of distinguishable succession rather than an empty stream; memory binds its experienced sequence, while imagination and association reroute its components.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:A5","CH_THN_SICA:A6","CH_THN_SIOQ:A5","CH_THN_SIOQ:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:HSI-05","text":"The remaining freedom is navigational: the system permits movement among copied contents and weighted relations but no escape into contentless form or absolute spontaneity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:SD3","CH_THN_SICA:SD1","CH_THN_SIOQ:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if the distinction between elemental provenance and relational organization remains primary.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:MRT4","CH_THN_SICA:MRT1","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B02","text":"Reconstruction is strongest where copy-dependence, separability, nonnecessary association, and content-bearing order jointly explain bounded mental productivity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:MRT5","CH_THN_SICA:MRT6","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B03","text":"The system remains underdetermined at its thresholds and taxonomic closures: mnemonic vivacity, the exhaustiveness of associative relations, and the limits of separation are asserted rather than fully derived.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:MRT2","CH_THN_SIIM:MRT7","CH_THN_SICA:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence depends on recursive sequencing: imaginative freedom must precede associative regulation, and relational analysis must precede the rejection of independent space and time.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:MRT9","CH_THN_SICA:MRT8","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B05","text":"The architecture depends on productive omission at two terminal points: the unknown origin of associative attraction and the unverified phenomenology of sensible or successive minima.","refs":["CH_THN_SICA:SNX2","CH_THN_SICA:MRT3","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT2","CH_THN_SIOQ:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_02:MRT-B06","text":"No indispensable propositional element resists reduction, but the system’s actual movement is preserved only if order is allowed to mutate from remembered relation into associative machine and spatiotemporal form.","refs":["CH_THN_SIIM:AF1","CH_THN_SICA:AF1","CH_THN_SIOQ:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 04–06","context":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination · Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas · Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time","original_title":"Chapters 04–06","original_context":"Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination · Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas · Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time"},"original_title":"Chapters 04–06","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_03","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 07–09","order":3,"parents":["CH_THN_SVIE","CH_THN_SVNI","CH_THN_SXIB"],"revision":"CL_THN_03@R001","sha256":"d872e9c863076b16087dda93ac53003b3f2c1ac192c14c5c7fb28606b6a45add","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-01","text":"The governing problem is how factual assent and practical reality can exceed bare conception when existence contributes no additional idea and positive conception remains bounded by perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:AS5","CH_THN_SVIE:AS6","CH_THN_SVNI:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-02","text":"The core thesis is that actuality is not represented by added content; belief differentiates an otherwise invariant object-idea through its forceful, steady, impression-related manner of conception.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:AS3","CH_THN_SVNI:AS5","CH_THN_SXIB:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-03","text":"A present impression and customary relation generate this manner by transferring vivacity to an absent, future, or otherwise merely conceived object.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-04","text":"The argument begins as an eliminative genealogy of existential content and becomes a reconstructive naturalistic psychology of assent, motivation, and affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:AS2","CH_THN_SVNI:AS4","CH_THN_SXIB:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-05","text":"Its movement is cumulative and recursive: ontological de-addition produces a modal theory of belief, which expands into a distributed economy of mental force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:AS7","CH_THN_SVNI:AS6","CH_THN_SXIB:AS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-06","text":"Poetry, eloquence, passion, and madness then reverse the evidential direction by showing that apparent vivacity can imitate belief without supplying its settled authority.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:AS4","CH_THN_SXIB:AS5","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-07","text":"Closure is operational rather than metaphysical: the system explains how ideas acquire reality-effects while withholding positive comprehension of mind-independent actuality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SD3","CH_THN_SVIE:SD4","CH_THN_SXIB:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ASP-08","text":"The terminal correction appeals to causal provenance, steadiness, reflection, and general rules, but leaves their authority and relation to external reality incompletely grounded.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Positive conceptual content is restricted to impressions, ideas, and their copied or recombined perceptual materials.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A1","CH_THN_SVIE:A6","CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SVNI:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Existence is not an additional quality, predicate, or idea but is identical with the object as conceived.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A4","CH_THN_SVNI:A2","CH_THN_SVNI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-03","text":"[Emergent] Bare conception, existential conception, and factual belief can preserve identical representational content while producing radically different cognitive and practical effects.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:D1","CH_THN_SVNI:A6","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Belief begins as a difference in manner, becomes an intensification of force and vivacity, and terminates as a comparatively steady, impression-related, causally regulated mode.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A8","CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SVNI:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Since contrary matters of fact remain conceivable, factual selection cannot arise from demonstrative necessity and must be determined by custom or association.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A4","CH_THN_SVNI:A5","CH_THN_SVNI:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] A present impression functions both as the experiential anchor of inference and as the reservoir from which vivacity is transmitted to a related idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Selective vivification gives absent and future objects present motivational efficacy without granting equivalent efficacy to every passing fiction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A4","CH_THN_SXIB:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Force and vivacity expand from a distinction between impressions and ideas into a transmissible causal currency linking belief, passion, imagination, and action.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SVNI:A11","CH_THN_SXIB:A3","CH_THN_SXIB:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Judgment, imagination, passion, and volition form a reciprocally permeable system rather than independent faculties.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A16","CH_THN_SXIB:SD1","CH_THN_SXIB:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Factuality converges on stable practical presence: an idea counts as real for the mind by persistently affecting inference, passion, and conduct without acquiring new content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] First-order vividness cannot authenticate itself; settled conviction requires discrimination by source, steadiness, reality-connection, reflection, and general rules.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:DNX5","CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] Reality is retained as a regulative relation organizing assent and correction while its intrinsic extra-perceptual nature remains positively unrepresented.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-01","text":"Stable identity: the non-addition of existence persists unchanged from the analysis of being into the analysis of belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A4","CH_THN_SVNI:A2","CH_THN_SVNI:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-02","text":"Absorption: the original question concerning the idea of existence disappears as an independent inquiry after becoming the premise that forces belief into a modal rather than representational explanation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:D4","CH_THN_SVNI:AS1","CH_THN_SVNI:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-03","text":"Functional mutation: present perception changes from the maximal positively available instance of being into the anchor and force-source of factual inference.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:D1","CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-04","text":"Escalation: force and vivacity move from taxonomic differences between perceptions to the defining mode of belief and then to a trans-domain mechanism of psychological causation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-05","text":"Domain migration: custom moves from selecting one conceivable factual alternative to transmitting vivacity and thereby generating motivation, passion, and action.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-06","text":"Displacement: externality shifts from an intrinsically unfilled relative correlate to the “connection with reality” invoked when reflection regulates assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVIE:SD4","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-07","text":"Reversal: vivacity initially explains why belief exceeds fiction, but counterfeit vivacity later demonstrates that intensity alone cannot establish genuine conviction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A11","CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-08","text":"Terminal transformation: the fact–fiction distinction moves from presumed content difference to scalar force difference and finally to causal pedigree, steadiness, and second-order correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX9","CH_THN_SXIB:SD3","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RMD-09","text":"Disappearance through absorption: conception, judgment, and reasoning cease to function as irreducible faculties once they have been recoded as modes and transitions within one conception-system.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A16","CH_THN_SVNI:ME5","CH_THN_SXIB:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-01","text":"The intrinsic nature of external objects remains a local ontological boundary; at cluster level, its scalable function is to deny any content-internal marker of actuality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","CH_THN_SVNI:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-02","text":"Poetry and madness remain local cases in subject matter but scale upward as indispensable stress tests of the proposed identity between belief and vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:AS5","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT7","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-03","text":"The proposition that existence adds no idea scales directly because it organizes both the boundary of ontology and the reconstruction of factual assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SD1","CH_THN_SVNI:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-04","text":"The present-impression requirement bridges conceptual genealogy and causal psychology by converting perceptual presence into a mechanism for generating belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A2","CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-05","text":"The motivational dilemma catalyzes the major transition from defining belief phenomenologically to demonstrating its systemic necessity for conduct.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:AS3","CH_THN_SXIB:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-06","text":"The simple equation of belief with occurrent intensity is superseded by a distinction between accidental vehemence and settled, source-regulated conviction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:NX10","CH_THN_SXIB:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:CGD-07","text":"The impression–idea taxonomy survives, but its original binary form is recoded into a graded and dynamically transmissible economy of perceptual force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SVNI:D7","CH_THN_SXIB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-01","text":"Neither existence nor belief is an additional predicate, quality, or representational component attached to an object-idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:NX1","CH_THN_SVIE:NX2","CH_THN_SVNI:NX2","CH_THN_SVNI:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-02","text":"No intrinsic semantic mark distinguishes an actual object from the same object merely conceived.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SNX2","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-03","text":"No positive representational passage gives direct comprehension of a specifically extra-perceptual kind of being.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:NX6","CH_THN_SVIE:NX7","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-04","text":"Matter-of-fact assent is not demonstrative necessity, and disbelief is not failure to understand the rejected proposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX4","CH_THN_SVNI:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-05","text":"Judgment, reasoning, passion, and imagination are not causally sealed faculties governed by irreducibly different operations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX6","CH_THN_SVNI:NX7","CH_THN_SXIB:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-06","text":"First-order intensity, imaginative completeness, and rhetorical vehemence do not independently authenticate factual belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX8","CH_THN_SXIB:NX10","CH_THN_SXIB:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-07","text":"Fiction and fact cannot be separated by an impermeable boundary at the level of represented content or raw vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX9","CH_THN_SXIB:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:GNX-08","text":"Viable motivation can arise neither from present impressions alone nor from the indiscriminate efficacy of every passing idea.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:NX3","CH_THN_SXIB:NX4","CH_THN_SXIB:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-01","text":"The system excludes any ontology in which actual existence alters the intrinsic representational constitution of an object.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:DNX2","CH_THN_SVIE:DNX3","CH_THN_SVNI:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-02","text":"It excludes direct representational realism: positive knowledge cannot cross from perceptual content to the intrinsic nature of a heterogeneous external realm.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:DNX4","CH_THN_SVIE:DNX5","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-03","text":"It excludes a rationalist construction of the factual world in which necessary conceptual connection determines existential truth.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:DNX3","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX1","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-04","text":"It excludes faculty psychology as a final explanation: judgment cannot be an independent act that stamps truth onto completed ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:DNX1","CH_THN_SVNI:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-05","text":"It excludes a sovereign rational subject insulated from custom, bodily condition, affective feedback, repetition, and rhetoric.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:SD5","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:DBN-06","text":"It excludes introspective foundationalism because the immediate felt strength of a perception cannot guarantee its own epistemic legitimacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:DNX5","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSA-01","text":"The governing absence is the positively knowable essence of mind-independent actuality: it must remain thinkable as a correlate while unavailable as represented content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX4","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable possibility is an intrinsic sign by which an idea could announce that its object exists independently.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SNX2","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSA-03","text":"No exact threshold separates vivid imagination, counterfeit belief, probable assent, and settled conviction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:SNX5","CH_THN_SXIB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSA-04","text":"The terminal ungrounded condition is the authority of general rules that distinguish legitimate causal vivification from accidental or pathological force.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-01","text":"Since existence adds no idea but belief exceeds bare conception, the surplus of factual assent must be modal and intensive rather than representational.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A4","CH_THN_SVNI:A1","CH_THN_SVNI:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-02","text":"Perceptual closure combined with present-impression anchoring makes factual commitment a relation generated within experience rather than direct cognition of an external object’s intrinsic being.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A6","CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVNI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-03","text":"If identical content produces different degrees of assent and action, force and steadiness must function as causal variables independent of representational composition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A6","CH_THN_SVNI:A11","CH_THN_SXIB:A3","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-04","text":"The same intensification that constitutes factual assent also gives an absent object practical efficacy; epistemic commitment and motivation therefore share one mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A5","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-05","text":"The appeal to “connection with reality” regulates belief by reference to a term whose extra-perceptual nature the system denies it can positively comprehend.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-06","text":"Poetry, eloquence, and madness force belief to mutate from raw intensity into intensity qualified by causal provenance, steadiness, and resistance to reflective correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-07","text":"Customary transfer from impression to idea and passionate transfer back to the object-idea form a self-amplifying loop capable of strengthening assent beyond its initial basis.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-08","text":"Because fictional and factual ideas can share content, a familiar or believed component can lend force to an entire fictional network without converting the network into settled belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:NX9","CH_THN_SXIB:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-09","text":"The reduction of judgment and reasoning to modes of conception, combined with the permeability of passion and imagination, yields a unified dynamics of mental force rather than a hierarchy of faculties.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A16","CH_THN_SXIB:SD1","CH_THN_SXIB:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:XD-10","text":"Custom is accepted as the causal generator of factual assent while education is rejected as a valid ground of assent; the system therefore distinguishes psychological genesis from epistemic warrant without fully formalizing that distinction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:NX1","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-01","text":"The system separates ontological content from actuality-effects: nothing representational is added, yet intensified conception transforms inference, passion, persistence, and conduct.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SD1","CH_THN_SVNI:SD1","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-02","text":"Factual warrant becomes second-order and genealogical: assent must be evaluated by how vivacity was produced and regulated, not merely by how forcefully it is felt.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:DNX5","CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-03","text":"Temporality becomes a force-transfer structure in which a present perception confers present efficacy upon absent and future objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A6","CH_THN_SXIB:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-04","text":"The subject becomes a relay within circulating intensities rather than an autonomous judge: impressions, habits, passions, bodily states, and rhetoric jointly modulate assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-05","text":"Pathological and aesthetic cases are theoretically privileged because they separate apparent intensity from legitimate belief and thereby expose hidden conditions of the ordinary mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:AS5","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-06","text":"The mechanism enabling action toward absent realities simultaneously exposes judgment to capture by repetition, eloquence, temperament, and affective feedback.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:BSD-07","text":"The cluster produces practical realism without positive metaphysical realism: ideas can govern life as realities even though independent actuality supplies no distinct conceptual content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SD4","CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-01","text":"Negative recursion: existence adds no content → belief cannot add content → factual difference relocates to manner → manner requires an intensive mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:D4","CH_THN_SVNI:D1","CH_THN_SVNI:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-02","text":"Generative loop: present impression → customary transition → transferred vivacity → intensified related idea → belief → action and passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-03","text":"Affective feedback loop: belief excites passion → passion vivifies its object → the vivified object increases assent → strengthened assent reinforces passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:SD2","CH_THN_SXIB:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-04","text":"Fictional diffusion loop: familiar anchor → relational spread of vivacity → easier imaginative reception → affective engagement without complete judgmental assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-05","text":"Counterfeit escalation: temperament, eloquence, or bodily ferment raises ideas toward impression-like force, culminating in the possible collapse of operative truth–falsehood discrimination.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-06","text":"Corrective loop: excessive vividness generates error → reflection identifies accidental provenance → general rules discount local force → graded conviction is restored.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:D10","CH_THN_SXIB:D11","CH_THN_SXIB:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-07","text":"Aporetic return: correction invokes reality-connection, but positive comprehension of external reality remains unavailable, returning epistemic regulation to the boundary established by perceptual closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SNX1","CH_THN_SVIE:SNX3","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:RLE-08","text":"The conceptual attractor is regulated vivacity: sufficient force to make ideas cognitively and practically present, sufficient steadiness to resist fluctuation, and sufficient pedigree to distinguish them from counterfeits.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B01","text":"Perceptual admissibility: every positive idea must derive from perceptual materials; no extra-perceptual existential content is available.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A1","CH_THN_SVIE:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B02","text":"Representational invariance: content of object O = content of O-as-existent = content of believed O; therefore factual difference cannot be compositional.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A4","CH_THN_SVNI:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B03","text":"Modal generation: present impression I + customary relation R(I,O) → transfer of vivacity → steady intensified conception of O → belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B04","text":"Operational expansion: belief in O → increased presence and persistence of O → influence on passion, imagination, and action.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A5","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B05","text":"Recursive amplification: passion or related vivid ideas → renewed vivacity in O → strengthened assent and affective efficacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B06","text":"Counterfeit production: non-customary vivification by rhetoric, temperament, or bodily ferment → belief-like force without secure factual pedigree.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FGS-B07","text":"Regulatory closure: source-reflection + general rules + reality-connection → correction of apparent intensity, while external actuality remains an intrinsically unfilled correlate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:AFM-B01","text":"Existential-origin test → elimination of added being-content → perceptual closure → belief-surplus problem → content invariance → relocation to manner → force-and-vivacity definition → customary transfer from present impression → practical and affective expansion → counterfeit vivification → correction by provenance and general rules → unresolved appeal to reality beyond positive representation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:AF1","CH_THN_SVNI:AF1","CH_THN_SXIB:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-01","text":"Being is reclassified from a detachable predicate into the nondiscriminating status of conceived presentation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ME1","CH_THN_SVIE:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-02","text":"The actual–possible distinction is relocated from represented content to the force, steadiness, provenance, and practical effects of conception.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:ME2","CH_THN_SVNI:ME3","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-03","text":"The internal–external distinction is redrawn as a problem of mediation, relational attribution, and causal anchoring rather than qualitative content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ME3","CH_THN_SVIE:ME5","CH_THN_SVNI:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-04","text":"Understanding is reclassified from a hierarchy of faculties into a single conception-system differentiated by necessity, association, intensity, and transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:SD3","CH_THN_SVNI:ME5","CH_THN_SXIB:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-05","text":"Absent and future objects are reclassified as potentially present causes of conduct when belief supplies sufficient force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-06","text":"Fact, fiction, persuasion, and madness become neighboring configurations within one economy rather than categorically separate mental domains.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:ME5","CH_THN_SXIB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MME-07","text":"Epistemic reading must become genealogical and second-order, tracking the source and regulation of vivacity rather than trusting represented content or felt intensity alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:ME6","CH_THN_SXIB:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: the system addresses existence, external objects, belief, factual assent, motivation, passion, fiction, eloquence, and madness.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ALT1","CH_THN_SVNI:ALT1","CH_THN_SXIB:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: impression-copy constraints, present-perception anchoring, customary association, force transmission, affective feedback, and corrective rules compose one distributed mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ALT2","CH_THN_SVNI:ALT2","CH_THN_SXIB:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: metaphysical and epistemic distinctions are converted into tests of perceptual origin, modal difference, causal provenance, and susceptibility to correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ALT3","CH_THN_SVNI:ALT3","CH_THN_SXIB:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological/Temporal level: being adds no essence, while absent and future objects acquire present causal efficacy through impression-related modes of conception.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:ALT4","CH_THN_SVNI:ALT4","CH_THN_SXIB:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological/Performative level: the system first compresses distinctions into a scalar mechanism and then uses poetry and madness to force its own refinement from simple intensity to regulated intensity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:MRT7","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT7","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:FL-01","text":"The system oscillates between existence as the universal status of every presented perception and existence as the actuality affirmed only in belief; the same term covers conceptual presence and factual commitment.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A2","CH_THN_SVIE:SD2","CH_THN_SVNI:A1","CH_THN_SVNI:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FL-02","text":"Perceptual closure denies positive access to extra-perceptual being, yet external objects are said to occasion perceptions and “reality-connection” regulates assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A7","CH_THN_SVIE:MRT6","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FL-03","text":"The scalar reduction of belief is strained by vivid fiction and poetry, which may exhibit greater apparent vehemence while remaining experientially and epistemically weaker.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A11","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX5","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FL-04","text":"General rules repair the insufficiency of first-order force but lack a specified origin, authority, and mechanism capable of avoiding circular dependence on already accepted beliefs.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A17","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:FL-05","text":"Habit is necessary to generate factual assent, while education demonstrates that repeated habit can produce epistemically invalid assent; causal production and warrant remain incompletely separated.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:NX1","CH_THN_SXIB:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MP-01","text":"Existence mutates from the explicit object of conceptual genealogy into a zero-addition constraint governing every subsequent account of belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:AS5","CH_THN_SVNI:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MP-02","text":"Force and vivacity mutate from marks distinguishing impressions and ideas into transferable quantities linking cognition, affect, imagination, and action.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A3","CH_THN_SXIB:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MP-03","text":"Belief mutates from intensified conception into regulated intensification once counterfeit cases show that occurrent vividness is insufficient.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A12","CH_THN_SXIB:A14","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MP-04","text":"Truth and reality mutate from presumed properties of represented objects into causal and regulatory terms governing easy reception, assent, and reflective correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SD4","CH_THN_SXIB:A11","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly modeled as a perceptually closed transmission machine in which impressions and ideas circulate, receive, lose, and redistribute force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:A6","CH_THN_SVNI:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a relay and regulatory threshold within this machine, not its sovereign origin: habit, affect, bodily ferment, and reflection compete to set the force of ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:A8","CH_THN_SXIB:A9","CH_THN_SXIB:A15","CH_THN_SXIB:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:HSI-03","text":"Time is organized around present anchors that draw absent and future objects into current causal efficacy through associative transmission.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:A13","CH_THN_SXIB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:HSI-04","text":"The remaining struggle is navigational: distinguish customary and regulated reality-effects from rhetorical, passionate, poetic, or pathological counterfeits without access to an intrinsic sign of actuality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:SNX2","CH_THN_SXIB:SD4","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B01","text":"The current-level axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the principal engine: existential non-addition → modal belief → customary vivification → practical expansion → counterfeit stress → regulatory correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:MRT1","CH_THN_SVNI:MRT1","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B02","text":"What remains underdetermined is the exact dimensional structure of force and vivacity, the mechanism of their transfer, and the threshold separating belief from vivid imagination.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:MRT4","CH_THN_SVNI:MRT5","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is coherent at the functional level but not metaphysically closed: it explains reality-effects more successfully than independent actuality or the warrant of correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:MRT2","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT3","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B04","text":"Its strongest construction is the transition from representational invariance to a unified mechanism explaining assent, motivation, passion, imagination, and action.","refs":["CH_THN_SVNI:MRT2","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if poetry, eloquence, and madness remain recursive tests that transform the initial scalar account rather than detachable illustrations.","refs":["CH_THN_SXIB:MRT7","CH_THN_SXIB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_03:MRT-B06","text":"The elements resistant to axiomatic reduction are the felt quality of belief, the positive nature of external actuality, and the authority of the general rules that arbitrate genuine and counterfeit force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIE:SNX5","CH_THN_SVNI:A17","CH_THN_SVNI:SNX4","CH_THN_SXIB:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 07–09","context":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence · Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief · Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","original_title":"Chapters 07–09","original_context":"Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence · Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief · Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief"},"original_title":"Chapters 07–09","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_04","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 10–12","order":4,"parents":["CH_THN_SXUP","CH_THN_SXRW","CH_THN_SIAP"],"revision":"CL_THN_04@R001","sha256":"bccfc61a8d5600031f2e8ab700a9cd3b02d1bd57b37d21cc2fa1ed9c9a807366","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B01","text":"The central problem is how the same natural operations can generate causal belief, prejudice, fictive ontology, and the procedures by which philosophy regulates them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:AS1","CH_THN_SXUP:AS6","CH_THN_SXRW:AS1","CH_THN_SIAP:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B02","text":"Repeated conjunction, relation, and facilitated transition form a common engine whose outputs differ less by origin than by the degree to which comparison and experiment control them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXUP:A5","CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B03","text":"At this scale the argument becomes a genealogical-methodological reconstruction: intrinsic substance and power are displaced by collections of qualities, observable relations, customary transitions, and regulated causal projections.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B04","text":"Reflection does not transcend natural belief; it exposes discrepancies within it and forces a bifurcation between fictive metaphysical repair and more extensive general rules.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:AS5","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:AS6","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B05","text":"Resemblance and ease of transition are epistemically neutral: uncontrolled, they produce prejudice and fictive unity; experimentally opposed by difference, they isolate causal circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4","CH_THN_SIAP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B06","text":"Philosophical correction therefore consists in recursive circumstance-analysis, widened experience, and semantic restraint rather than access to necessary connection or an independent rational faculty.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1","CH_THN_SXRW:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:A12","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B07","text":"The movement is spiral and mutation-driven: custom generates an inference, discrepancy reopens it, a higher-order rule reorganizes it, and the corrective result becomes subject to renewed testing.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS7","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS6","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AS-B08","text":"Closure is operational but nonterminal: metaphysical inquiry into hidden substance or power ceases, while empirical causal judgment remains corrigible because neither custom nor circumstance-analysis admits final completion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX3","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Repeated conjunction and close relation acquire cognitive efficacy by facilitating transitions among perceptions rather than by revealing intrinsic bonds among objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Neither substance nor causal power is available through mere inspection; actual causal judgment begins only from experienced relational structure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A1","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX1","CH_THN_SIAP:NX3","CH_THN_SIAP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Ease of mental transition produces belief when it transfers vivacity, but produces fictive identity and simplicity when its own fluency is attributed to objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A4","CH_THN_SIAP:D1","CH_THN_SIAP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Natural customary transition precedes reflection and remains active after reflective correction; philosophy can redirect but cannot abolish it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A5","CH_THN_SXUP:NX9","CH_THN_SIAP:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Resemblance extends inference beyond identical cases, becoming prejudice when material differences are neglected and an experimental operator when resemblance is controlled by contrast.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A6","CH_THN_SXUP:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Reflection works through comparison, counterinstance, and circumstance-variation; it corrects fluent apprehension by making suppressed differences operationally salient.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:A6","CH_THN_SIAP:A8","CH_THN_SXUP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Philosophical normativity is immanent to custom: more extensive and constant regularities regulate narrower associations without invoking an extra-customary tribunal.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1","CH_THN_SXRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Actual causation is operationally constituted by contiguous, temporally ordered, uniform conjunction among experimentally discriminated circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A4","CH_THN_SXRW:A5","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Phenomena are compound, so causal identity must migrate from whole objects to qualities, differences, and auxiliary circumstances isolated through comparative tests.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Contradiction and disappointed expectation do not determine their own resolution: they can provoke fictive substrates, prejudicial persistence, or renewed experimental decomposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SXUP:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Evidential warrant and experienced conviction are separable because conviction varies with vivacity, transition, temporal distance, and presentation, while warrant depends on comparative regularity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","CH_THN_SXUP:SD3","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Corrective inquiry has no self-applying or final form: every metarule competes with prior habit, and every experiment becomes subject to renewed circumstance-analysis.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX3","CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Legitimate explanation converges on two constraints: terms must possess assignable ideational content, and causal distinctions must survive discriminating variation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:D10","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Moderate scepticism suspends claims to hidden substance and power while preserving disciplined empirical inquiry into observable regularities.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:D9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-01","text":"[Stable Identity] Constant conjunction retains its function as the empirical condition of causal inference while remaining explicitly distinct from perceived necessary connection.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A13","CH_THN_SIAP:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-02","text":"[Functional Recurrence] Facilitated transition recurs as belief-production, inferential compression, imputed identity, and imputed simplicity; its function changes with the object onto which fluency is projected.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXUP:A8","CH_THN_SIAP:D1","CH_THN_SIAP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Custom moves from a first-order producer of expectation and prejudice to the material from which second-order philosophical rules are constructed.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A5","CH_THN_SXUP:A10","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Resemblance changes from a source of illicit transfer into a controlled comparative instrument once difference is deliberately introduced.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-05","text":"[Migration] Reflective comparison migrates from exposing variation and composition in bodies to isolating causal circumstances within compound phenomena.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:A6","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-06","text":"[Escalation] The general rule expands from a correction of prejudicial inference into a recursive methodology for causal projection, decomposition, and experimental enlargement.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXUP:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-07","text":"[Displacement] Qualities cease to require a substantial bearer and become separable elements whose causal relevance must be experimentally determined.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A8","CH_THN_SIAP:A11","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-08","text":"[Terminal Transformation] Contradiction ceases to be evidence for an unknown substrate and becomes a demand to identify an omitted difference, scope condition, or auxiliary cause.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-09","text":"[Absorption] The local theory of vivacity is absorbed into the current-level distinction between psychological uptake and methodological warrant.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A1","CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RMD-10","text":"[Disappearance] Substance, substantial form, accident, faculty, and occult quality disappear after supplying negative cases of how unexplained transition is converted into simulated ontology.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:SD2","CH_THN_SIAP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-01","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] Temporal fading, historical transmission, sign generality, and passion-amplification remain local specifications of how belief-force varies.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A7","CH_THN_SXUP:A8","CH_THN_SXUP:A13","CH_THN_SXUP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-02","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] The individual comparative, proportional, and incomplete-cause rules remain local instruments; their recursive subjection to renewed variation scales upward.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D5","CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-03","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] The specific genealogy of matter, substantial forms, accidents, and occult qualities remains a local historical diagnosis.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:D5","CH_THN_SIAP:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-04","text":"[Scaled] The denial of intrinsic causal legibility scales upward into a general restriction against both a priori causal ontology and metaphysical explanation by hidden powers.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A1","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX2","CH_THN_SIAP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] The conflict between first-order and second-order general rules bridges the psychology of prejudice and the methodology of experimental causal judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS7","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Smooth mental transition connects belief-formation to fictive object-construction, revealing a shared mechanism beneath epistemic and ontological error.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-07","text":"[Catalyst] Reflectively disclosed contradiction catalyzes the transition from passive customary association to either metaphysical repair or active experimental differentiation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A8","CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-08","text":"[Recoded] “Same cause” is recoded from identity of whole objects to identity of experimentally isolated operative circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-09","text":"[Superseded] Objective substances, intrinsic faculties, and occult agencies are superseded by relational descriptions that remain answerable to assignable ideas and observable variation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:NX3","CH_THN_SIAP:NX7","CH_THN_SXRW:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:CGD-10","text":"[Current-Level Emergent] Genuine philosophy becomes the conjunction of psychological self-diagnosis, comparative experiment, and refusal of semantically empty closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","CH_THN_SIAP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-01","text":"Causal roles cannot be assigned by conceptual inspection or an a priori taxonomy of objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A1","CH_THN_SXRW:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-02","text":"Identity, simplicity, cohesion, and causal agency cannot be grounded in an intelligible invariant substance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:NX1","CH_THN_SIAP:NX2","CH_THN_SIAP:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-03","text":"No proposition possesses a belief-force invariant under temporal distance, mediation, presentation, and disposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:DNX2","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-04","text":"Philosophical correction cannot appeal to a rational faculty independent of custom, imagination, comparison, and experience.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:DNX1","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-05","text":"Ease, repetition, resemblance, and customary inseparability do not by themselves establish object identity, metaphysical dependence, or causal efficacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:NX6","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX1","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-06","text":"Technical vocabulary cannot acquire explanatory force through repetition, professional authority, or verbal resemblance alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A16","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-07","text":"Neither formal rules nor a decisive experiment can eliminate the need for renewed judgment and circumstance-analysis.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:NX8","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX3","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-08","text":"Reflection does not permanently suppress the customary propensity whose outputs it corrects.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:NX9","CH_THN_SXUP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:GNX-09","text":"Human affects, intentions, and inward agency cannot be projected into nature as explanations of external phenomena.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A17","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-01","text":"The system requires rationalist transparency to be false: causes, substances, and powers must not be intrinsically legible to conception.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:DNX1","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX1","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-02","text":"It also excludes absolute irregularity and total kind-instability; without provisionally stable resemblances, causal uniformity and comparative projection would collapse.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:DNX2","CH_THN_SXRW:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-03","text":"Normative correction must not depend on an external tribunal, yet it must not reduce to whichever custom is psychologically strongest; the system survives only through comparative extension and constancy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXUP:A12","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-04","text":"Object identity and causal identity cannot be primitively given; both must remain decomposable into qualities, relations, and operative circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-05","text":"Explanatory usefulness cannot legitimate an otherwise inconceivable posit; fictive reconciliation and verbal closure must remain distinguishable from explanation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:DNX3","CH_THN_SIAP:D10","CH_THN_SIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-06","text":"A temporally invariant, affectively insulated, and uniformly rational subject cannot be admitted because belief is produced as a variable event of force-transmission and habit.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A4","CH_THN_SXUP:A14","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:DBN-07","text":"Experimental competence cannot be exhausted by possession of explicit rules; decisive variation must remain dependent on judgment that the rules cannot independently generate.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","CH_THN_SXRW:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absent metaphysical interior of causation: repeated conjunction is available, but the power supposedly connecting cause and effect is not.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SNX1","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable possibility of a transcendent criterion gives correction its recursive form: philosophy can judge custom only through a more extensive organization of custom.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX1","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-03","text":"No final reconciliation between natural propensity and reflective discipline is available; their conflict is constitutive rather than temporary.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX3","CH_THN_SIAP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-04","text":"No terminal causal decomposition is admissible because every isolated circumstance can become part of a newly compounded phenomenon requiring further control.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A10","CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-05","text":"The invariant bearer is absent on both sides of cognition: objects dissolve into related qualities, while subjects dissolve into temporally variable operations of habit, comparison, and force.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSA-06","text":"The system circles but cannot fully formalize the exact boundary between warranted generalization and prejudice; that boundary remains dependent on scope, constancy, and discriminating judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT3","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT2","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-01","text":"Because prejudice, fictive ontology, and causal judgment arise from related customary mechanisms, causal method must be understood as a disciplined mutation of association rather than the intervention of a foreign faculty.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","CH_THN_SIAP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-02","text":"Resemblance has no stable epistemic valence: left unopposed, it exports vivacity and object-unity; paired with controlled difference, it isolates operative causal circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D6","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4","CH_THN_SIAP:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-03","text":"Contradiction is a methodological fork: imagination may conceal it through a substrate, preserve it through prejudice, or convert it into a test for omitted differences and auxiliary causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-04","text":"Causal uniformity functions as a regulative rule for organizing experience, not as evidence that necessary connection or productive power has become perceptible.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:A13","CH_THN_SIAP:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-05","text":"A methodologically warranted inference may remain psychologically faint, while an experimentally defective association may remain vivid; epistemic rank and subjective force therefore require separate assessment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","CH_THN_SXUP:A15","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-06","text":"Correction is necessarily provisional because reflective metarules never erase first-order habit and experimental controls never escape renewed circumstance-analysis.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SNX3","CH_THN_SXRW:SD3","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-07","text":"Once bodies are treated as collections of separable qualities, causal inquiry can no longer take whole substances as primitive causes; it must identify operative qualities within relational compounds.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-08","text":"Legitimate theoretical distinctions are constrained simultaneously by semantics and experiment: they must correspond to assignable ideas and support discriminating variations.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","CH_THN_SIAP:D10","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-09","text":"Moral inquiry is especially unstable because obscure variables interact with vivid signs, passions, and anthropomorphic projection, increasing both causal opacity and susceptibility to prejudicial inference.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A13","CH_THN_SXUP:A14","CH_THN_SXRW:A11","CH_THN_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:XD-10","text":"Moderate scepticism does not terminate causal practice; it terminates the conversion of empirically useful rules into claims about hidden ontological powers.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:D9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-01","text":"Normativity is naturalized without being reduced to immediate habit: warranted judgment is the recursive stabilization of custom through broader comparison, counterinstance, and experimental control.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:D7","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-02","text":"The resulting ontology is thin and relational: separable qualities enter recurring spatiotemporal conjunctions, but neither substantial bearers nor intrinsic productive powers are required.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:A11","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-03","text":"The epistemic subject becomes a temporally variable relay among impressions, habits, comparisons, passions, and reflective rules rather than a stable faculty of rational assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A4","CH_THN_SXUP:A14","CH_THN_SXUP:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-04","text":"Anomaly becomes productive only when it is prevented from hardening into fiction: disappointed expectation must trigger decomposition rather than occult supplementation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD2","CH_THN_SIAP:D8","CH_THN_SIAP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-05","text":"Time is constitutive at two levels: causal order requires priority and succession, while belief-strength varies with recency, mediation, and the temporal mode of survey.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-06","text":"Evidence becomes two-dimensional: comparative regularity determines warrant, whereas vivacity and transitional facility determine experienced conviction and affective efficacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","CH_THN_SXUP:SD4","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-07","text":"Method cannot be fully formalized because its rules generate tasks of discrimination whose decisive execution depends on non-algorithmic judgment and expanded experience.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A10","CH_THN_SXRW:A12","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-08","text":"Natural and moral phenomena occupy one causal field, but moral inquiry demands stronger safeguards because its operative circumstances are obscured by affective and imaginative participation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A14","CH_THN_SXRW:A11","CH_THN_SXRW:SD5","CH_THN_SIAP:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:BSD-09","text":"Philosophical success consists not in recovering objective necessity but in keeping inference revisable, terms meaningful, and metaphysical demands from dictating fictive closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3","CH_THN_SIAP:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-01","text":"Repetition → custom → facilitated transition → vivified expectation → reinforced readiness for the same transition forms the basal belief loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXUP:A5","CH_THN_SXUP:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-02","text":"Related qualities → fluent survey → imputed identity or simplicity → reflective contradiction → substance fiction → further metaphysical categories forms the ontological escalation loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:FGS1","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS4","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-03","text":"Resemblance → first-order generalization → counterinstance → more general rule → continued competition with the first propensity forms the prejudice-correction loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS5","CH_THN_SXUP:FGS6","CH_THN_SXUP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-04","text":"Compound phenomenon → controlled variation → provisional circumstance-isolation → new compound description → renewed variation forms the experimental recursion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:FGS5","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS6","CH_THN_SXRW:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-05","text":"Contradiction is the principal bifurcation point: one path proliferates fictive entities, while the other intensifies comparison and experimental differentiation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-06","text":"“General rule” becomes a machine when it ceases merely to state regularity and begins selecting, condemning, and reorganizing lower-order transitions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXUP:A12","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-07","text":"“Circumstance” becomes a machine when causal identity shifts from whole objects to variables that can be compared, removed, decomposed, or supplied as co-causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A8","CH_THN_SXRW:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-08","text":"Moderate scepticism functions as an attractor that halts metaphysical proliferation without halting empirical recursion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:D9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:RLE-09","text":"Apparent repetition marks phase change when customary recurrence is re-entered as an object of reflection: the same mechanism then becomes belief, error, diagnosis, or correction according to its recursive position.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:AS6","CH_THN_SXRW:A10","CH_THN_SIAP:AS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B01","text":"Distinct qualities + repeated conjunction + close relation → facilitated transition among perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B02","text":"Facilitated transition + present force or fluent survey → causal expectation / imputed identity / imputed simplicity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS2","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS2","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B03","text":"Temporal distance, contrary instance, analytic comparison, or changed circumstance → interrupted fluency → weakened conviction or disclosed contradiction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS3","CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B04","text":"Uncontrolled contradiction + retained demand for unity or agency → substance / occult quality / prejudicial persistence → simulated closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS5","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B05","text":"Controlled contradiction + counterinstance + broader comparison → second-order general rule → accidental–efficacious distinction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS6","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B06","text":"Compound phenomenon + controlled resemblance and difference → operative circumstance / common quality / missing co-cause → provisional causal attribution.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:FGS4","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B07","text":"Provisional attribution + renewed circumstance-analysis + enlarged experimental sphere → recursively corrected causal map.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:FGS6","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B08","text":"Absence of intrinsic power + absence of final control → moderate sceptical restriction + continued empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SNX1","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FGS-B09","text":"Variable vivacity and presentation → unequal psychological uptake of equally regulated conclusions → persistent gap between method and assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD2","CH_THN_SXUP:SD4","CH_THN_SXUP:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B01","text":"Repeated conjunction and related qualities → fluent customary transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A5","CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B02","text":"Fluent transition → causal belief, resemblance-extension, and imputed object-unity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A6","CH_THN_SIAP:D1","CH_THN_SIAP:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B03","text":"Temporal, comparative, or experimental disruption → diminished conviction, disclosed composition, or failed expectation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B04","text":"Disruption → bifurcation between fictive metaphysical repair and reflective reclassification.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:D7","CH_THN_SIAP:A9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B05","text":"Reflective reclassification → more general rules + controlled resemblance and difference → causal circumstance-isolation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B06","text":"Circumstance-isolation → provisional projection → renewed experimental control → widened evidential field.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D2","CH_THN_SXRW:D7","CH_THN_SXRW:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:AFM-B07","text":"Recursive correction → rejection of hidden power and empty terminology → moderate sceptical, nonterminal closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:D9","CH_THN_SIAP:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-01","text":"Reason and error cease to belong to independent faculties; both become differently organized outputs of relation, custom, comparison, and rule-governed correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A15","CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-02","text":"Ontology is divided into two registers: fictive intrinsic structure is genealogically dissolved, while observable relational structure remains available for causal practice.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-03","text":"Resemblance becomes a truth-neutral operator whose epistemic status depends on whether difference and counterinstance control its extension.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ME4","CH_THN_SXRW:ME3","CH_THN_SIAP:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-04","text":"Contradiction is reclassified from a demand for an unseen bearer into a diagnostic opportunity for causal decomposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:ME4","CH_THN_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-05","text":"Causation becomes thinkable without intrinsic power: its legitimate content lies in regular succession, controlled comparison, and projection within tested kinds.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:ME2","CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-06","text":"Evidence divides into logical-methodological warrant and psychological-affective force, dissolving the assumption that valid arguments must command proportionate conviction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ME2","CH_THN_SXUP:ME7","CH_THN_SXRW:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-07","text":"Philosophical vocabulary is judged by ideational content and experimental work rather than by systematic position or technical appearance.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","CH_THN_SIAP:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-08","text":"Natural and moral phenomena enter a unified causal terrain, differentiated by variable opacity rather than by distinct causal architectures.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD5","CH_THN_SXRW:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MME-09","text":"The hidden presuppositions of empirical inquiry become visible: stable kinds, discriminable circumstances, and nonbrute irregularity are required even after necessary connection is denied.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:DNX2","CH_THN_SXRW:DNX3","CH_THN_SXRW:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: the system addresses variations in belief, rules for judging causes, and the ancient categories of substance, form, accident, faculty, and occult quality.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ALT1","CH_THN_SXRW:ALT1","CH_THN_SIAP:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: relation and repetition facilitate transitions; comparison and variation interrupt them; higher-order rules reorganize the resulting expectations and contradictions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ALT2","CH_THN_SXRW:ALT2","CH_THN_SIAP:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: philosophy intervenes by redirecting custom, expanding the experimental field, and refusing fictive or verbal closure when objective connection is unavailable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ALT3","CH_THN_SXRW:ALT3","CH_THN_SIAP:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level: reality is composed of separable qualities in temporally ordered conjunctions, while identity, conviction, and causal attribution vary with survey, succession, and experimental scope.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:ALT4","CH_THN_SXRW:ALT4","CH_THN_SIAP:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level: the architecture performs its thesis by repeatedly turning its own rules, categories, and conclusions into objects of further comparison and correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS7","CH_THN_SXRW:A10","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-01","text":"Custom supplies prejudice, causal belief, corrective metarules, and the criterion of greater regularity; the distinction between bad and good custom therefore remains immanent and incompletely secured.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXUP:SD6","CH_THN_SXUP:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-02","text":"The claim that causation depends wholly on constant conjunction remains strained by the additional requirements of contiguity and temporal priority.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A3","CH_THN_SXRW:A4","CH_THN_SXRW:A5","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-03","text":"Strict causal uniformity conflicts with contrary experience and graded probability unless every apparent exception is reassigned to an undiscovered difference or missing co-cause.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A9","CH_THN_SXRW:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-04","text":"A priori causal openness coexists with dependence on stable kinds, clear experiments, and discriminable circumstances whose criteria are not fully specified.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D1","CH_THN_SXRW:DNX2","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-05","text":"Denial of observable necessary connection coexists with strong projection from a clear experiment, leaving the practical authority of uniformity stronger than its ontological basis.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D2","CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-06","text":"A genealogy of substance explains why it is believed but does not alone establish its falsity; the refutation additionally depends on the absence of an assignable idea and the separability principle.","refs":["CH_THN_SIAP:A11","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX3","CH_THN_SIAP:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-07","text":"Experimentally isolated qualities risk becoming replacements for occult qualities unless their identity remains tied to reproducible comparison rather than verbal designation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD6","CH_THN_SIAP:NX7","CH_THN_SIAP:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:FL-08","text":"The architecture distinguishes warrant from conviction but supplies no quantitative rule connecting impression-strength, transitional facility, and resulting assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A4","CH_THN_SXUP:MRT2","CH_THN_SXUP:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MP-01","text":"Resemblance mutates from spontaneous evidence into a controlled experimental variable when paired with discriminating difference.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A6","CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MP-02","text":"The general rule mutates from a habitual extension that produces prejudice into a reflective metarule that regulates lower-order inference.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A10","CH_THN_SXUP:A11","CH_THN_SXUP:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MP-03","text":"Contradiction mutates from a stimulus for ontological repair into an experimental demand for further differentiation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D6","CH_THN_SIAP:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MP-04","text":"Cause mutates from an apparently whole object into a common quality, differentiating circumstance, compound contribution, or auxiliary condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:D3","CH_THN_SXRW:D4","CH_THN_SXRW:D5","CH_THN_SXRW:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MP-05","text":"Sceptical cessation mutates from termination of the search for intrinsic power into the enabling boundary of continued empirical causal inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIAP:D9","CH_THN_SIAP:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a field of separable qualities entering recurrent, contiguous, temporally ordered configurations without an observable inner power binding them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:A4","CH_THN_SXRW:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A2","CH_THN_SIAP:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a relay-system in which impressions, custom, imagination, comparison, passion, and reflective rules transfer, amplify, interrupt, and reorganize force.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A3","CH_THN_SXUP:A14","CH_THN_SIAP:A3","CH_THN_SIAP:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:HSI-03","text":"Time is not a neutral container: causal priority, recency, gradual survey, distant comparison, and inferential mediation actively constitute both objects and beliefs.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:A7","CH_THN_SXRW:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A5","CH_THN_SIAP:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:HSI-04","text":"The remaining struggle is between fluent closure and recursive differentiation; escape is not departure from custom but the controlled reopening of its products through variation, comparison, and sceptical restraint.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:SD1","CH_THN_SXRW:D7","CH_THN_SIAP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if facilitated transition, reflective disruption, and the bifurcation between fictive repair and experimental correction are preserved.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT1","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT1","CH_THN_SIAP:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined at the points where it invokes more general rules, same kinds, clear experiments, adequate repetition, and essential circumstances.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT3","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale as an immanent account of naturalized correction: the mechanisms generating error also supply the materials for disciplined judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT4","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT3","CH_THN_SIAP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B04","text":"Its strongest architecture is the conversion of relation and custom into a branching machine capable of producing belief, prejudice, fictive ontology, and recursive causal method.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT5","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT4","CH_THN_SIAP:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on recursive sequencing: natural transition must precede disruption, and disruption must precede either metaphysical proliferation or methodological control.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:FGS5","CH_THN_SXRW:FGS6","CH_THN_SIAP:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B06","text":"The architecture is weakest where anti-scholastic valuation, causal uniformity, and the superiority of sceptical cessation are asserted more strongly than their internal criteria determine.","refs":["CH_THN_SXRW:MRT8","CH_THN_SIAP:MRT7","CH_THN_SXUP:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B07","text":"Experimental sagacity and the variable force of conviction remain resistant to complete axiomatic reduction because neither decisive variation nor psychological intensity is rendered calculable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:MRT2","CH_THN_SXRW:SD4","CH_THN_SXRW:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_04:MRT-B08","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if correction is not represented as abolishing imagination and scepticism is not represented as abolishing empirical inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SXUP:DNX5","CH_THN_SXRW:SNX4","CH_THN_SIAP:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 10–12","context":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability · Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects · Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy","original_title":"Chapters 10–12","original_context":"Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability · Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects · Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy"},"original_title":"Chapters 10–12","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_05","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 13–15","order":5,"parents":["CH_THN_SVIS","CH_THN_SVCT","CH_THN_SVLT"],"revision":"CL_THN_05@R001","sha256":"668d93c244eed7fd465c965d35e7aecc82504c14c09fd83ae3171f4db122fa84","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B01","text":"The central problem is how explanatory inquiry can remain operative after unknown substances, self-grounding reason, and unrestricted affective laws cease to provide legitimate foundations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:AS1","CH_THN_SVCT:AS1","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B02","text":"The system relocates explanation from hidden bearers and intrinsic powers to observable differences, experienced conjunctions, associative transitions, affective forces, and domain-specific thresholds.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D3","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B03","text":"Its argument becomes an immanent audit: concepts, faculties, and general laws are tested by their experiential origin, internal consequences, reciprocal applicability, and tendency to overgenerate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:AS3","CH_THN_SVIS:AS6","CH_THN_SVCT:AS4","CH_THN_SVLT:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B04","text":"The directional movement is universal claim → contradiction or excess → restrictive analysis → preservation of a limited mechanism rather than restoration of the original foundation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B05","text":"Imagination is the common engine: it completes relations, confers vivacity, sustains belief, mediates passion, and produces error whenever its transitions outrun their conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B06","text":"The self survives the destruction of substantial soul-talk not as an appearing bearer but as an operational object, comparator, and relatively durable pole of affective attribution.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B07","text":"Habit, custom, practice, and nature restore regular operation after metaphysical, epistemic, or causal certainty has been withdrawn, but they do not convert practical stability into truth.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AS-B08","text":"Closure is recursive and pragmatic: inquiry continues through constrained natural propensity, repeatedly correcting both speculative excess and skeptical paralysis without reaching final foundation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:SD2","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6","CH_THN_SVIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Legitimate explanation begins from experienced perceptions, conjunctions, pleasures, pains, and their observable transitions rather than from impression-transcendent entities.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A13","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-02","text":"[Emergent] Once unknown supports are excluded, the primary explanatory object becomes the network of relations and transformations among perceptions and affects.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D3","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Imagination is simultaneously the indispensable medium of cognitive and affective synthesis and the source of fictive completion, contradiction, and excess.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:A4","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Relation is not a uniform binary bond: it changes across separability, spatial compatibility, causal succession, self-reference, comparison, publicity, and temporal durability.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A4","CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A7","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] Repetition becomes operational through habit or custom, which projects conjunctions, stabilizes classifications, and regularizes otherwise delicate transitions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A15","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Cognitive and affective efficacy requires force: vivacity, pleasure, pain, passion, or propensity must animate a relation before it governs assent or self-evaluation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Causal intelligibility does not require perceived necessity; experienced conjunction and the transition it induces provide the operative causal content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A15","CH_THN_SVCT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Heterogeneous effects cannot be explained by indiscriminately extending one medium, faculty, or response-class over the entire field.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Unrestricted principles reveal their inadequacy by overgeneration: universal extension, pure reason, unrestricted fancy, and mere self-relation each produce contradictions or false positives.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A9","CH_THN_SVLT:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Correction is immanent and restrictive: undecidability, skeptical diffidence, empirical practice, and threshold conditions limit mechanisms without abolishing them.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] The self has no established status as a substantial bearer but remains indispensable as an object of passion and a temporally extended standard of comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Natural production and representational success do not guarantee correspondence: belief may lack rational foundation, apparent location may be fictive, and self-estimation may diverge from welfare.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVLT:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] The viable endpoint is a reflexive science of human nature that investigates its own fallible operations under permanent skeptical and empirical restraint.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A17","CH_THN_SVCT:SD4","CH_THN_SVIS:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-01","text":"The demand for origin mutates from a semantic test excluding mental substance into a genealogy of belief and then into a distinction between primary affect and mediated self-directed passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVCT:D1","CH_THN_SVLT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-02","text":"Imagination reverses valence: first exposed as fabricating spatial conjunction, it becomes the necessary source of belief, then the associative medium whose affective operation must be gated rather than removed.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D7","CH_THN_SVCT:A4","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-03","text":"Relation migrates from logical separability and spatial compatibility into causal projection, comparative distinction, public discernibility, and temporal attachment.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A5","CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A10","CH_THN_SVLT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-04","text":"Custom expands from projecting past conjunctions into future expectation to regulating philosophical assent and stabilizing passion, while retaining the capacity to exceed particular warrant.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-05","text":"The self is displaced from the role of hidden support into the role of represented object; substantial identity disappears while functional self-reference persists.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D2","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-06","text":"Contradiction changes function from a reciprocal weapon against material and immaterial systems into an endogenous conflict among natural faculties and finally into a diagnostic of affective overgeneration.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D12","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-07","text":"Causation is absorbed into a broader transition-model: constant conjunction licenses bodily influence on thought, habit converts conjunction into expectation, and affective conversion requires additional relational gates.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D15","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-08","text":"Limitation escalates from suspension of undecidable ontology, through skeptical restraint of cognition, to explicit restriction of a causal law by comparative, social, and temporal thresholds.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RMD-09","text":"Spinozist retortion and immortality cease to be active objects after establishing two higher rules: privileged theories receive no exemption, and no simplicity claim yields necessary persistence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D12","CH_THN_SVIS:D17","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX4","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-01","text":"The extended/nonspatial partition remains local as a spatial analysis, but its higher-order effect is the prohibition against explaining heterogeneous phenomena through one uniform medium.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-02","text":"Peculiarity, visibility, and durability remain local gates of pride and humility; what scales upward is the form of a threshold-dependent transformation rather than those particular thresholds.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-03","text":"Melancholy, diversion, social intercourse, and revived curiosity remain the local phenomenology of skeptical recovery; their scalable function is non-rational restoration of viable cognition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A13","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-04","text":"Separability, contradictory natural beliefs, and the joy–pride distinction scale upward because each differentiates effects that an unrestricted theory falsely homogenizes.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A5","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-05","text":"Associative completion, customary projection, and general affective rules bridge the units by showing how repeated relations acquire force without becoming intrinsic necessities.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-06","text":"Definitional collapse, skeptical self-subversion, and affective overgeneration are transition catalysts: each forces a move from foundational or universal explanation to restricted operation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-07","text":"The simple soul, autonomous understanding, and unconditional passion-law become obsolete as explanatory centers and are recoded as failed cases of unrestricted unification.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:NX4","CH_THN_SVLT:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:CGD-08","text":"Matter–thought causation, the resumption of philosophy, and welfare’s divergence from self-estimation remain distinct local conclusions but jointly establish operation without metaphysical, epistemic, or affective equivalence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD5","CH_THN_SVCT:SD3","CH_THN_SVLT:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-01","text":"No unknown substratum may be installed merely to support perceptions whose relations can be described without it.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:NX4","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-02","text":"No autonomous reason or external cognitive instrument can validate the faculties from outside the natural operations under examination.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:NX4","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-03","text":"Relation is neither an intrinsic, uniform, nor merely binary property; its force depends upon domain, competition, repetition, and mediation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A7","CH_THN_SVCT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-04","text":"Imagination can neither be granted unrestricted authority nor eliminated, because both cognition and error arise through its operations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVCT:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-05","text":"Natural necessity, customary stability, and felt conviction do not establish truth, intrinsic value, or ontological homogeneity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVLT:DNX2","CH_THN_SVIS:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-06","text":"Neither universal extension, universal simplicity, pure understanding, nor mere self-relation can serve as an unrestricted explanatory principle.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:NX8","CH_THN_SVIS:NX9","CH_THN_SVCT:A9","CH_THN_SVLT:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:GNX-07","text":"No necessary alignment joins causal operation to substantial ontology, ordinary belief to certainty, or pride and humility to happiness and misery.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:ME7","CH_THN_SVCT:NX11","CH_THN_SVLT:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of the model in which appearances are accidents that become intelligible only by inhering in an independently knowable substance.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D2","CH_THN_SVIS:D3","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-02","text":"It requires the falsity of any epistemology in which reason can ground assent without habit, imagination, vivacity, passion, or natural propensity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A1","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-03","text":"It excludes the model in which causation is intelligible only through resemblance, conceptual containment, or a perceived necessary nexus.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX5","CH_THN_SVCT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-04","text":"It excludes the model in which affective value transparently registers intrinsic good and evil independently of comparison, publicity, familiarity, and custom.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A10","CH_THN_SVLT:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-05","text":"It disqualifies the assumption that failures at the margins can be ignored while a general law remains genuinely universal; edge cases determine the law’s required restriction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:DBN-06","text":"It cannot admit a self that is simultaneously an inaccessible substance, a transparent foundation of cognition, and an invariant measure of personal worth.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SNX1","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX3","CH_THN_SVCT:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around the absence of an unmediated common medium joining spatial and nonspatial perception, belief and truth, or welfare and self-estimation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SNX2","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVLT:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSA-02","text":"The unavailable possibility is a standpoint from which substance, causation, cognition, and value could be validated together without using the fallible transitions under examination.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SNX1","CH_THN_SVCT:SNX1","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSA-03","text":"No perfect alignment is available between what is naturally produced and what is real: fictive location, unavoidable belief, and socially legible pride can each diverge from their objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:SNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSA-04","text":"Final repose is inadmissible: neither metaphysical certainty, permanent skepticism, nor perfectly calibrated customary judgment can terminate recursive correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:SD2","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSA-05","text":"The deepest absent object is a fully self-present subject that could bear perceptions, authorize reason, measure value, and guarantee its own persistence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SNX3","CH_THN_SVIS:SNX4","CH_THN_SVCT:SNX1","CH_THN_SVLT:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-01","text":"Once the bearer of perception and the intrinsic power of causation are withdrawn, the passion-system reveals the positive replacement: explanation becomes a study of gated transformations among experiential states.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D3","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-02","text":"Associative completion, habitual projection, and customary passion-rules are three scales of one operation by which relations acquire efficacy through repetition and mental transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D7","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-03","text":"Because imagination produces both indispensable cognition and systematic fiction, correction cannot purify thought of imagination; it must discriminate among imaginative operations by their conditions and consequences.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-04","text":"Reciprocal failure of materialism and immaterialism, failure of unrestricted fancy and pure understanding, and overproduction of pride instantiate one rule: explanatory extremes collapse when generalized beyond their proper domain.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-05","text":"The destruction of the substantial soul does not eliminate self-reference; it transforms the subject into a represented and temporally weighted node within causal, imaginative, and affective relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-06","text":"Time has no single function: habit projects future conjunction, anticipated loss weakens self-attribution, and conceivability prevents either matter or spirit from claiming necessary persistence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A13","CH_THN_SVIS:D17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-07","text":"Felt success cannot measure reality: apparent place may be fabricated, natural belief may remain unjustified, and intense self-evaluation may fail to register actual welfare.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:ME5","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVLT:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:XD-08","text":"Once theological privilege is removed and custom is recognized as behaviorally formative, speculative systems must be assessed partly by how their rules organize conduct rather than by claimed access to hidden reality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD6","CH_THN_SVCT:A15","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-01","text":"Reality becomes operationally describable as a heterogeneous field of perceptions and affects linked by contingent, force-bearing transitions rather than unified by a known substratum.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D1","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes an effect and target of organization: it is where memories, beliefs, comparisons, publicity, and durable attachments converge, not the substance from which they emanate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-03","text":"Causal law is transformed into differential conversion: conjunction establishes possible transition, while domain-specific relations determine whether that transition becomes belief, apparent location, joy, pride, pain, or humility.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A15","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-04","text":"Temporality is naturalized as projection, anticipated loss, comparative duration, and conceivability of cessation; it supplies no proof of necessary identity or immortality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A18","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","CH_THN_SVLT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-05","text":"Human agency is non-sovereign: assent and passion arise from propensities, habits, relations, and general rules that the will cannot simply suspend.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-06","text":"Epistemic normativity becomes regulatory rather than foundational: legitimate inquiry is the constrained use of unavoidable faculties, not their validation by an independent criterion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:DNX1","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-07","text":"Error is endogenous to successful machinery: the same operations that locate, project, classify, and stabilize also fabricate, contradict, and overextend.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:BSD-08","text":"The science of human nature must therefore operate experimentally and recursively, treating every generalization as a revisable mechanism whose limits are disclosed by its own excesses.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A17","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6","CH_THN_SVIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-01","text":"Causal or temporal association → imaginative relation-completion → apparent spatial placement → familiar attribution → renewed reinforcement of the fictive relation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A12","CH_THN_SVIS:D7","CH_THN_SVIS:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-02","text":"Repeated conjunction → habit → vivacity and expectation → ordinary action → further experienced conjunction forms the basic belief-production loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-03","text":"Valenced cause → candidate self-relation → comparative and social filtering → pride or humility → customary classification → facilitated future passion forms the affective loop.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-04","text":"Inquiry → skeptical intensification → affective paralysis → natural restoration → curiosity → renewed inquiry is the cluster’s explicit spiral rather than a linear resolution.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:SD2","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS5","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS6","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-05","text":"Universal mechanism → anomalous output → immanent critique → restrictive thresholds → preserved mechanism → renewed exposure to anomalies is the general escalation path.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-06","text":"Human nature is the principal attractor: disputes about soul, causation, certainty, and passion converge on the operations and limits of perception, imagination, habit, and affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:A17","CH_THN_SVLT:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-07","text":"“Relation” becomes a machine when it ceases to name a static connection and begins to regulate conceivability, spatial compatibility, causal expectation, self-reference, comparison, and passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A4","CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:RLE-08","text":"“Custom” marks phase change rather than repetition: projection becomes cognitive stability, cognitive stability becomes methodological restraint, and restraint becomes affective regularization with renewed risk of excess.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B01","text":"Input field: heterogeneous perceptions, conjunctions, pleasures, pains, and candidate relations are given without an independently appearing support.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A6","CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B02","text":"Semantic audit: demand experiential origin, distinguishability, and separability; exclude concepts that add no identifiable content.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A1","CH_THN_SVIS:A4","CH_THN_SVIS:A5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B03","text":"Foundational subtraction: remove unknown substance, external necessary power, autonomous reason, and intrinsic affective scale.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D4","CH_THN_SVCT:NX2","CH_THN_SVCT:NX4","CH_THN_SVLT:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B04","text":"Associative synthesis: conjunction and relation generate transition, while vivacity or valence supplies the force required for belief and passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B05","text":"Productive expansion: imagination completes relations, habit projects expectations, and self-reference converts primary affect into candidate self-evaluation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A12","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B06","text":"Excess detection: fictive placement, contradictory beliefs, and indiscriminate pride expose the failure of unrestricted transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D8","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B07","text":"Restrictive gating: apply spatial compatibility, skeptical diffidence, practice, relational closeness, peculiarity, publicity, and duration.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A8","CH_THN_SVLT:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B08","text":"Natural stabilization: habit, embodied restoration, and general rules produce workable regularity without guaranteeing truth or exact proportionality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FGS-B09","text":"Recursive closure: submit stabilized operations to renewed audit; preserve empirically useful mechanisms while suspending metaphysical completion and global certainty.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A8","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B01","text":"Unknown support and unrestricted law → demand for experiential and operational justification.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:AS3","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B02","text":"Justificatory demand → decomposition into heterogeneous perceptions, conjunctions, pleasures, pains, and relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A6","CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B03","text":"Decomposition → displacement of substance, intrinsic power, and pure reason by associative transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D1","CH_THN_SVCT:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B04","text":"Associative transition → belief, apparent placement, and candidate self-directed passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D7","CH_THN_SVCT:FGS2","CH_THN_SVLT:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B05","text":"Unrestricted transition → spatial fiction, contradictory natural belief, and affective overgeneration.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVLT:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B06","text":"Contradiction and excess → reciprocal critique, skeptical restraint, and relational, comparative, social, and temporal gating.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D12","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:AFM-B07","text":"Gated mechanism → customary and natural stabilization → constrained science of human nature → renewed skeptical audit.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A17","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-01","text":"The substance–accident map is replaced by a field of separable perceptions and describable transitions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D2","CH_THN_SVIS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-02","text":"The mind–matter opposition is subordinated to cross-cutting differences among extended, nonspatial, cognitive, hedonic, and self-directed states.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD2","CH_THN_SVLT:ME2","CH_THN_SVCT:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-03","text":"Reason and imagination cease to be autonomous opposites; reason becomes a regulated operation within the imaginative and affective economy of assent.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:NX4","CH_THN_SVCT:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-04","text":"Causation is detached from substantial ontology and redrawn as conjunction plus a domain-specific transition whose success may require additional gates.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:ME7","CH_THN_SVCT:A7","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-05","text":"The self is reclassified from hidden bearer to operational target of memory, comparison, publicity, and temporally durable attribution.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","CH_THN_SVLT:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-06","text":"Value is divided into experienced good or evil, social legibility, self-estimation, and welfare; these distributions need not coincide.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:SD3","CH_THN_SVLT:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-07","text":"Error is relocated from an alien disturbance to the overextension of otherwise necessary mechanisms such as imagination, habit, and custom.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD3","CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-08","text":"Skepticism is redrawn from terminal suspension into a recurring control function that restrains both philosophical conviction and philosophical doubt.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVCT:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MME-09","text":"General theories become readable as transformation models whose validity depends on specified ranges, thresholds, and failure conditions rather than maximal universality.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level — The explicit objects are the soul’s substance, the grounds of belief and philosophy, and the conditions under which pleasure and pain become pride and humility.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:AS1","CH_THN_SVCT:AS1","CH_THN_SVLT:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level — Heterogeneous inputs are joined by imagination, habit, causal transition, self-relation, comparison, and custom, then restricted where those operations overgenerate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A9","CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A3","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level — Inquiry advances by applying identical standards to privileged and rival systems, forcing universal claims to disclose their exclusions and limiting conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX4","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological/Temporal level — Reality contains spatially heterogeneous states; future and continuity are habitually projected; durable self-relations are stronger than transient ones; necessary persistence remains unavailable.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX3","CH_THN_SVIS:D17","CH_THN_SVCT:ALT4","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological/Performative level — The system performs its thesis by generating speculative excess, exposing collapse, undergoing skeptical interruption, and returning with narrower, provisional machinery.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:AF3","CH_THN_SVCT:AF1","CH_THN_SVLT:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:ALT-B06","text":"Social/Affective level — Public discernibility, comparison, custom, superstition, and philosophy show that representations acquire force through collective circulation and behavioral consequence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A15","CH_THN_SVLT:A10","CH_THN_SVLT:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-01","text":"The substantial self is excluded, yet a relatively durable self remains necessary as the object and comparator of passion; the status of this operational continuity is not fully specified.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-02","text":"No Archimedean criterion validates cognition, yet practice, experience, moderation, and critical examination are assigned regulatory authority without a complete account of their title.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:SNX1","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-03","text":"Constant conjunction licenses actual matter–thought causation, but the available covariation does not fully individuate the cause or exclude competing conjunctions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D15","CH_THN_SVIS:MRT7","CH_THN_SVCT:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-04","text":"Imagination is both the condition of cognition and the source of contradiction; no wholly independent faculty exists to delimit its legitimate operations.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A5","CH_THN_SVCT:A6","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX1","CH_THN_SVIS:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-05","text":"Familiarity depreciates value while custom stabilizes and may amplify passion; the architecture supplies no general priority rule when these effects conflict.","refs":["CH_THN_SVLT:A9","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:FL-06","text":"Nature restores belief and action without vindicating them, leaving practical necessity structurally separated from epistemic legitimacy.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:DNX4","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-01","text":"Substance mutates from a proposed bearer into an excluded surplus, transferring explanatory burden to relations among perceptions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D1","CH_THN_SVIS:D3","CH_THN_SVIS:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-02","text":"Imagination mutates from local fabricator of place into the general medium of belief, memory, causal expectation, and affective association.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:D7","CH_THN_SVCT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-03","text":"Relation mutates from logical and spatial compatibility into a graded force governing social distinction and temporal self-attachment.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A5","CH_THN_SVIS:A11","CH_THN_SVLT:A4","CH_THN_SVLT:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-04","text":"The self mutates from a metaphysical candidate into a functional object that organizes attention, comparison, and passion without appearing as a separate impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:A2","CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVLT:A1","CH_THN_SVLT:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-05","text":"Skepticism mutates from threatened cessation of inquiry into recurrent diffidence that enables rather than terminates constrained philosophy.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A11","CH_THN_SVCT:A18","CH_THN_SVCT:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-06","text":"Universal law mutates into gated mechanism: failures no longer simply refute causal regularity but determine its range and internal conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD4","CH_THN_SVCT:D4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MP-07","text":"Custom mutates from a projector of expectation into a regulator of inquiry and passion, then reverses into a source of renewed overextension.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVCT:A16","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVLT:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a field of unsupported, heterogeneous events linked by associative, habitual, causal, affective, social, and temporal relays.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:D1","CH_THN_SVLT:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a mobile junction within that field: a point at which vivacity becomes belief and valenced relations become self-attribution, not a hidden substance beneath them.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:DNX2","CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVLT:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:HSI-03","text":"Time is a machine of projection and attenuation: repetition opens the future, anticipated loss weakens attachment, and possible annihilation prevents duration from becoming necessity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A13","CH_THN_SVIS:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:HSI-04","text":"No escape outside human faculties is available; viable navigation consists in moving between imaginative overproduction and skeptical paralysis through experience, custom, social practice, and recurrent correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:DNX1","CH_THN_SVCT:SD2","CH_THN_SVLT:A14","CH_THN_SVIS:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B01","text":"The extracted axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if perception-first analysis, associative mediation, heterogeneous effects, threshold restriction, customary stabilization, and skeptical recursion are retained together.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:MRT1","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT1","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined regarding the external status of matter, causal individuation, the normative authority of lively reason, exact affective thresholds, and the scope of general rules.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:MRT2","CH_THN_SVIS:MRT7","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT5","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT2","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale as a recursive operational architecture, not as a deductive foundation or final reconciliation of its natural conflicts.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:MRT3","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT2","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B04","text":"Its strongest architecture is the conversion of failed ontological foundations into a science of constrained transitions whose errors arise from the excess of their own productive mechanisms.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:SD1","CH_THN_SVCT:SD4","CH_THN_SVLT:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if negation is followed by operational return: conceptual invalidation must lead to empirical causation, skepticism to natural restoration, and overgeneration to restriction.","refs":["CH_THN_SVIS:MRT9","CH_THN_SVCT:MRT8","CH_THN_SVLT:MRT10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_05:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable residue resistant to complete axiomatic reduction is differential felt force—vivacity, pleasure, pain, propensity, familiarity, and passion—because these intensities make transitions operative but are not derivable from relation alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SVCT:A3","CH_THN_SVCT:A12","CH_THN_SVLT:A2","CH_THN_SVLT:A9","CH_THN_SVIS:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 13–15","context":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul · Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book · Sect. VI Limitations Of This System","original_title":"Chapters 13–15","original_context":"Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul · Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book · Sect. VI Limitations Of This System"},"original_title":"Chapters 13–15","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_06","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 16–18","order":6,"parents":["CH_THN_SXLF","CH_THN_SI_2","CH_THN_SVME"],"revision":"CL_THN_06@R001","sha256":"093f27ecad33fb692b9e7abefe20ad8e9149a52da0c3f2c8f8f7b2a6191e033f","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-01","text":"The central problem is how socially directed passions vary while their represented objects, qualities, or conditions remain unchanged.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:AS1","CH_THN_SI_2:AS1","CH_THN_SVME:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-02","text":"The governing thesis is that social affect is generated by relation-conditioned movements of vivacity: others affect the self through sympathetic reproduction or construction, and through comparison that alters apparent self-position.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-03","text":"The argument becomes a distributed causal reconstruction in which anomalies eliminate original instincts, intrinsic values, and rational-interest models in favor of associative mechanisms.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:AS5","CH_THN_SI_2:AS3","CH_THN_SI_2:AS6","CH_THN_SVME:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-04","text":"The self functions first as a source of vivacity, then as recipient of communicated sentiment, and finally as the comparative term whose apparent condition is altered by another’s fortune.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS6","CH_THN_SVME:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-05","text":"Sympathy carries or constructs another’s satisfaction, esteem, contempt, or sorrow within the observer, producing reputation, affection, wealth-esteem, utility-pleasure, and pity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D3","CH_THN_SI_2:D9","CH_THN_SI_2:D12","CH_THN_SI_2:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-06","text":"Comparison preserves one affect across transition to another object, reverses its contribution to self-estimation, and generates envy or malice without requiring injury or autonomous malevolence.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:D1","CH_THN_SVME:D2","CH_THN_SVME:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-07","text":"Generated passions recursively modify their own conditions through justificatory judgment, imagined self-attribution, reflected esteem, and attempts to alter or sever comparative relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D10","CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BAS-08","text":"Closure is conditional rather than quantitative: a common relational architecture is established, but the thresholds governing conversion, comparison, and mode selection remain unspecified.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT2","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT5","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7","CH_THN_SVME:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Ideas and impressions differ principally in force and vivacity, permitting associative processes to transform represented affect into operative passion without changing its content.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A1","CH_THN_SXLF:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Relations are effective only as directed pathways that facilitate, preserve, intensify, interrupt, or redirect imaginative transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A3","CH_THN_SI_2:A11","CH_THN_SVME:A7","CH_THN_SVME:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Social passion requires a coupling between affective force and a relation that assigns or compares that force to a person.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A9","CH_THN_SI_2:A2","CH_THN_SVME:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] The self is not a sovereign source of valuation: its initial vivacity enables the very transitions through which external sentiments and comparative conditions alter it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A2","CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SVME:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Sympathy begins as conversion of an inferred sentiment into an impression but expands into constructive simulation capable of supplementing or replacing the source’s actual emotion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Sympathy and comparison are distinct transformations of retained affect: sympathy imports another’s represented condition, whereas comparison converts that condition into an inverse estimate of the self.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:D11","CH_THN_SVME:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Affective valence belongs neither to representation nor object alone; it depends on whether the object is directly surveyed, sympathetically appropriated, or comparatively opposed.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D12","CH_THN_SVME:D1","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Personal attribution is regulated by forms of certification—durability, intention, authority, causal inference, and general expectation—that determine whether an affect is attached to a person and sustained.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SXLF:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Relational proximity is valence-neutral amplification: it can intensify affection, praise, pity, rivalry, envy, or humiliation according to the process routed through it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D6","CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-10","text":"[Attractor] Social affect has a topology in which authority, resemblance, contiguity, acquaintance, convergence, direction, and separation govern force more decisively than bare content or absolute rank.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD4","CH_THN_SVME:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-11","text":"[Attractor] The recurrent generative circuit is self → related other → transformed affect → altered self, with the altered self producing further judgments, signs, desires, and reflected sentiments.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Passions can endogenously produce the beliefs and imagined conditions that preserve or amplify them.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D10","CH_THN_SI_2:A7","CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SVME:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] No intrinsic affective baseline stabilizes self-worth, social value, or hierarchy independently of transmitted sentiment and active comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SNX3","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX1","CH_THN_SVME:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Force and vivacity retain their function as the common currency linking ideas, impressions, associative transitions, and passions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A1","CH_THN_SI_2:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-02","text":"[Functional recurrence] Relation recurs as a conductor of sympathy, a condition of durable affection, a bidirectional pathway of attachment, and a gate permitting comparative impressions to interact.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A7","CH_THN_SI_2:A11","CH_THN_SVME:A7","CH_THN_SVME:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Sympathy shifts from reproducing another’s expressed sentiment to appropriating proprietary satisfaction and finally to constructing sorrow absent from the observed person.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-04","text":"[Domain migration] Resemblance moves from general human access to other minds, through affection and pity, into the wider topology determining which persons become affectively comparable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A6","CH_THN_SI_2:D7","CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-05","text":"[Mutation] The self moves from vivid associative origin, to object of socially communicated judgment, to variable magnitude constituted against related others.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A2","CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SVME:A5","CH_THN_SVME:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-06","text":"[Reversal] Pleasure and pain cease to have invariant direction: another’s satisfaction can become esteem through sympathy or pain through comparison, while another’s misery can become pity or malicious pleasure.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D2","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-07","text":"[Reversal] Proximity changes from a means of sympathetic intimacy into a condition of intensified rivalry without ceasing to perform its basic function of preserving affect across transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D6","CH_THN_SVME:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-08","text":"[Escalation] General rules progress from tests of causal regularity to constructive templates that supply absent passions and project altered emotional magnitude back onto objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A13","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SVME:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-09","text":"[Absorption] Intention and evaluator authority lose the status of isolated causes and become forms of personal certification governing durable attribution and evidential uptake.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-10","text":"[Terminal transformation] Esteem becomes recursive social capital: communicated satisfaction generates esteem, perceived esteem increases satisfaction, and renewed satisfaction generates further esteem.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SI_2:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RMD-11","text":"[Disappearance] Original fame, intrinsic wealth-value, calculated advantage, and autonomous malevolence disappear after their explanatory roles are redistributed across sympathy, comparison, and associative topology.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX1","CH_THN_SI_2:NX8","CH_THN_SI_2:NX9","CH_THN_SVME:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-01","text":"[Lower-layer local] The exact weighting of evaluator authority, self-concurrence, jest, and numerical accumulation remains specific to reputational influence rather than defining every social passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A12","CH_THN_SXLF:A13","CH_THN_SXLF:D9","CH_THN_SXLF:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-02","text":"[Lower-layer local] The distinction between durable qualities and transient intentional actions solves the temporal attribution of love and hatred but scales upward only as a general certification principle.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A3","CH_THN_SI_2:A4","CH_THN_SI_2:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-03","text":"[Lower-layer local] The contingent conjunction of love with benevolence and hatred with anger remains an irreducible constitutional supplement rather than a global relational mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A18","CH_THN_SI_2:SD7","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-04","text":"[Lower-layer local] Habitual projection from altered emotion to altered object-magnitude is the specific perceptual machinery of comparative judgment, not the general form of sympathy.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:A3","CH_THN_SVME:A4","CH_THN_SVME:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-05","text":"[Scales upward] Force, vivacity, associative transition, and retained impressions form the common machinery through which all three units generate social affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT3","CH_THN_SVME:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Fame and wealth-esteem jointly establish that social rank is produced by circulated satisfaction and reflected judgment rather than by isolated possession or intrinsic excellence.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-07","text":"[Bridge] Pity and envy demonstrate that proximity does not determine valence; it intensifies whichever sympathetic or comparative operation controls the transition.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SI_2:D16","CH_THN_SVME:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-08","text":"[Catalyst] Constructed pity, resisted contempt, and direct pain joined to comparative pleasure force the separation of affective reception from belief, source-fidelity, and direct valence.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:CGD-09","text":"[Superseded] Primitive social appetites and static classifications are recoded as outputs of directed transitions, relational weights, and recursive feedback.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME1","CH_THN_SI_2:ME4","CH_THN_SVME:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-01","text":"Social passions are not independent instincts responding uniformly to praise, wealth, misery, enjoyment, or rank.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX1","CH_THN_SI_2:NX8","CH_THN_SVME:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-02","text":"Social value is not fixed by intrinsic merit, objective magnitude, sensible form, or absolute inequality.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:NX6","CH_THN_SI_2:D12","CH_THN_SVME:NX2","CH_THN_SVME:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-03","text":"Relations are not inert, symmetric classifications; their force depends on direction, convergence, continuity, and the transitions they enable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX7","CH_THN_SXLF:NX8","CH_THN_SI_2:NX7","CH_THN_SVME:NX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-04","text":"Invariant representation does not guarantee invariant judgment or affective response.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX4","CH_THN_SVME:NX3","CH_THN_SVME:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-05","text":"Sympathy is neither immediate access to another mind nor necessarily faithful reproduction of another’s actual passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX3","CH_THN_SI_2:NX13","CH_THN_SI_2:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-06","text":"Rational judgment, calculated advantage, and objective justice are not sufficient sovereign causes of social affection.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A17","CH_THN_SI_2:NX4","CH_THN_SI_2:NX9","CH_THN_SI_2:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:GNX-07","text":"A passion is not identical with its ordinary practical tendency: love need not conceptually contain benevolence, hatred need not contain anger, and misery need not remain an object of pain under comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:NX10","CH_THN_SI_2:NX11","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-01","text":"The cluster requires the falsity of an autonomous self whose valuation can remain insulated from communicated sentiments and comparable others.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:DNX3","CH_THN_SI_2:DNX4","CH_THN_SVME:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-02","text":"It excludes a neutral tribunal capable of judging worth independently of prior passion, associative vivacity, and reflected social affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SNX3","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX1","CH_THN_SVME:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-03","text":"It disqualifies any model in which stable representation fixes stable judgment regardless of affective sequence and route.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX4","CH_THN_SVME:D1","CH_THN_SVME:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-04","text":"It cannot admit transparent one-to-one correspondence between another’s interior state and the observer’s sympathetic impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-05","text":"It rules out purely linear causality because passion can generate its own judgments, esteem can amplify the satisfaction it reflects, and comparative pain can motivate alteration of its relational cause.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-06","text":"Objective hierarchy cannot be affectively operative without an associative route that makes persons proximate, authoritative, familiar, or comparable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD4","CH_THN_SVME:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-07","text":"Ideas and impressions cannot be absolutely different kinds, because the entire social-affective architecture requires conversion by altered vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:DNX2","CH_THN_SI_2:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:DBN-08","text":"No necessary moral order may guarantee that relational intensification produces benevolence rather than rivalry, pity rather than malice, or esteem rather than mortification.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SNX3","CH_THN_SVME:D2","CH_THN_SVME:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-01","text":"The deepest absent object is an intrinsic baseline of self-worth against which social communication and comparison could be finally corrected.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SNX3","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX1","CH_THN_SVME:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-02","text":"The system lacks a general criterion determining when another’s condition will be directly surveyed, sympathetically appropriated, or comparatively inverted.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:SNX4","CH_THN_SVME:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-03","text":"No independent metric fixes the threshold at which relation transfers enough vivacity, sustains comparison, or converts an idea into an impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT2","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT5","CH_THN_SVME:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-04","text":"The authentic interior state of the other is unavailable as a final ground because social affect depends on inferred, reflected, supplemented, or fabricated sentiments.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A4","CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-05","text":"A morally guaranteed direction for social relation is inadmissible: the same relational machinery can sustain affection, pity, esteem, envy, humiliation, or malice.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SNX3","CH_THN_SVME:SD5","CH_THN_SXLF:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSA-06","text":"There is no affective position wholly outside the relational system from which its generated values could be judged without participating in their production.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SNX1","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX5","CH_THN_SVME:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-01","text":"Sympathy and comparison form dual operators on socially represented affect: the first reproduces or constructs another’s sentiment in the self, while the second preserves that sentiment as a contrasting force against the self.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-02","text":"Relational topology determines not only affective intensity but whether two personal conditions can interact at all.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD4","CH_THN_SVME:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-03","text":"Proximity is structurally ambivalent: it intensifies affection and pity when transition supports sympathetic appropriation, but intensifies envy when transition sustains comparative opposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D6","CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-04","text":"Authority, intention, acquaintance, and general expectation are heterogeneous certification devices that stabilize otherwise transient affective attributions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-05","text":"Fame and wealth operate through the same recursive status circuit: perceived satisfaction or approval generates esteem, and reflected esteem intensifies the state that elicited it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SXLF:SD6","CH_THN_SI_2:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-06","text":"Once sympathy can construct absent emotion, social effects depend on the observer’s operative model of another’s condition rather than on veridical access to that condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX3","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-07","text":"Another’s misery can simultaneously support direct sympathetic pain and comparative pleasure, so interpersonal affect need not resolve into a single valence.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D3","CH_THN_SVME:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-08","text":"Passion preserves itself through cognitive and imaginative supplementation: hatred generates injury judgments, while praise can generate a provisional self-attribution that makes praise effective.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D10","CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-09","text":"Relational severance is a general affective strategy: concealment weakens shame, competing transitions weaken attachment, and distance disables envy without changing the underlying condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D8","CH_THN_SI_2:D8","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:XD-10","text":"Fame, wealth-esteem, pity, envy, and malice require no autonomous faculty when their functions can be distributed among vivacity transfer, construction, contrast, and recursive self-estimation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX1","CH_THN_SI_2:NX8","CH_THN_SI_2:NX12","CH_THN_SVME:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-01","text":"Social reality becomes a distributed affective computation in which persons continuously transmit, simulate, compare, and reflect one another’s conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD4","CH_THN_SI_2:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes a relay and comparator rather than an enclosed origin: its vivid self-impression enables external affect to return as altered self-affection.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SI_2:DNX4","CH_THN_SVME:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-03","text":"Value is generated in circulation: qualities, possessions, utility, rank, happiness, and misery acquire force through the paths by which their attendant sentiments reach or oppose the self.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME2","CH_THN_SI_2:SD5","CH_THN_SVME:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-04","text":"Social hierarchy is recursively produced rather than merely perceived, because esteem amplifies satisfaction and comparative attention makes relationally proximate differences more affectively real than remote inequalities.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD6","CH_THN_SVME:SD3","CH_THN_SXLF:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-05","text":"Social time is retentive and recursive: durable character sustains attribution, prior impressions remain active during transition, and lost comparative advantage becomes a new present pain.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD1","CH_THN_SVME:A8","CH_THN_SVME:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-06","text":"Social judgment is epistemically endogenous to passion: testimony can alter self-belief, while prior passion can generate the judgment needed to preserve itself.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:D10","CH_THN_SI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-07","text":"Strategic intervention shifts from changing objects to changing relational routes: authority can be discredited, identities concealed, associations severed, and comparative proximity weakened.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D8","CH_THN_SXLF:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:D8","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:BSD-08","text":"No ethical valence inheres in social connectedness itself; intensified relation can produce solidarity or antagonism according to the transformation governing retained affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD7","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-01","text":"Reputation loop: vivid self → inferred external judgment → sympathetic reproduction → pride or humility → increased receptivity to confirming judgments.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SXLF:A17","CH_THN_SXLF:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-02","text":"Wealth loop: possession → proprietary satisfaction → sympathetic esteem → perceived esteem → vanity → renewed satisfaction and esteem.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-03","text":"Injury loop: painful event → hatred → threatened diminution by justice or innocence → generated judgment of injury → stabilized hatred.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A7","CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-04","text":"Envy escalation: comparative advantage → rival approach → loss of former comparative pleasure → comparison with the former state → new positive pain → pressure to sever relation or remove superiority.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:D6","CH_THN_SVME:D9","CH_THN_SVME:FGS9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-05","text":"Constructive-sympathy loop: observed condition → customary expectation of passion → construction of absent sentiment → intensified response through proximity or contrast → confirmation of the general expectation.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:A20","CH_THN_SI_2:D16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-06","text":"Relational intensification spirals rather than advances linearly: increased acquaintance or resemblance strengthens transition, which strengthens passion, which gives further force to related ideas.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A7","CH_THN_SXLF:A16","CH_THN_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-07","text":"The principal attractor is relation-mediated vivacity: heterogeneous passions repeatedly return to the question of which transition preserves, transfers, constructs, or opposes affective force.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D2","CH_THN_SI_2:SD3","CH_THN_SVME:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:RLE-08","text":"The terminal escalation path is sign or condition → lively idea → impression → passion → judgment or desire → altered social condition → new sign or comparison.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS1","CH_THN_SXLF:FGS3","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS5","CH_THN_SVME:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B01","text":"Initialize a persistently vivid self S, represented persons O, attendant affects E(O), and a directed relational network R.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A2","CH_THN_SVME:A2","CH_THN_SI_2:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B02","text":"Weight R by resemblance, contiguity, acquaintance, kinship, authority, convergence, directionality, durability, and separation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A7","CH_THN_SXLF:A12","CH_THN_SI_2:A11","CH_THN_SVME:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B03","text":"Direct attribution operator: pleasure or pain × durable quality or intention-sign × person-relation → stable love or hatred; strong affect × weak attribution → acute but unstable passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:FGS2","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B04","text":"Sympathetic operator Sᵧ: signs or represented conditions → idea of another’s sentiment → relational vivacity transfer → corresponding or constructed impression in the observer.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS1","CH_THN_SXLF:FGS2","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B05","text":"Comparative operator C: R(O₁,O₂) → preservation of E(O₁) during transition → contrast with E(O₂) → altered apparent magnitude or self-condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:FGS2","CH_THN_SVME:FGS3","CH_THN_SVME:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B06","text":"Route Sᵧ through favorable or unfavorable evaluation to generate pride or humility; route it through satisfaction, utility, or misfortune to generate esteem, beauty, or pity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS4","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS8","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B07","text":"Route C through another’s enjoyment to generate diminished self-estimation and envy; route it through anticipated misery to generate comparative elevation and malice.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:FGS6","CH_THN_SVME:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B08","text":"Feed generated passion back into cognition and relation: passion strengthens associated ideas, constructs supporting judgments, reflects esteem, or seeks to alter the comparative field.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A16","CH_THN_SI_2:A7","CH_THN_SI_2:A16","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B09","text":"Attenuate output by disesteem, nonconcurrence, weak return transition, divided relations, distance, or conceptual separation; intensify it by authority, proximity, convergence, custom, and retained impression.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS5","CH_THN_SXLF:FGS6","CH_THN_SI_2:FGS7","CH_THN_SVME:FGS8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FGS-B10","text":"Closure rule: apparent exceptions do not trigger new faculties until variation in routing, certification, retention, construction, or comparison has been exhausted.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS7","CH_THN_SI_2:ALT3","CH_THN_SVME:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B01","text":"Unequal praise, unstable affection, anomalous pity, and unprovoked malevolence expose failures of undifferentiated social-passion models →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:AS1","CH_THN_SI_2:AS1","CH_THN_SVME:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B02","text":"Original instinct, intrinsic value, rational advantage, moral injury, and autonomous malevolence are eliminated as sufficient causes →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX1","CH_THN_SI_2:NX9","CH_THN_SVME:NX1","CH_THN_SVME:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B03","text":"Force and vivacity establish a common currency connecting representation, impression, and passion →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A1","CH_THN_SI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B04","text":"Directed relations establish the pathways, weights, continuities, and interruptions of affective transition →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A14","CH_THN_SI_2:A11","CH_THN_SVME:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B05","text":"Sympathy imports or constructs another’s sentiment, while comparison preserves and inverts its contribution to self-estimation →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B06","text":"The dual operations differentiate reputation, attachment, esteem, pity, envy, and malice without assigning each an independent faculty →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME1","CH_THN_SI_2:ME5","CH_THN_SI_2:ME8","CH_THN_SVME:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B07","text":"Generated passions recursively stabilize themselves through judgments, reflected esteem, imaginative supplementation, and relational intervention →","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D10","CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:AFM-B08","text":"The system closes on a relational social ontology whose governing mechanisms are reconstructible but whose activation thresholds and mode-selection rules remain open.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT8","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7","CH_THN_SVME:MRT2","CH_THN_SVME:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-01","text":"Social passions are reclassified from discrete faculties into outputs of a shared relational economy of vivacity, retention, simulation, and contrast.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME1","CH_THN_SI_2:ME3","CH_THN_SVME:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-02","text":"Relations become causal infrastructure rather than descriptive predicates: their direction and arrangement determine which affects can interact.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME3","CH_THN_SI_2:ME4","CH_THN_SVME:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-03","text":"Other minds are reclassified as inferred and constructible affective models rather than transparent sources or sealed interiors.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:NX3","CH_THN_SI_2:ME8","CH_THN_SI_2:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-04","text":"The opposition between direct and social value collapses: social value is generated either by vicarious participation in another’s satisfaction or by inverse comparison with another’s condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD5","CH_THN_SVME:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-05","text":"Hierarchy is redrawn through affective adjacency: near rivals and credible judges can exert more force than objectively superior but relationally remote persons.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D4","CH_THN_SXLF:D5","CH_THN_SVME:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-06","text":"Agency becomes distributed across author, observer, relation, prior passion, general rule, and reflected response rather than residing solely in intentional action.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:ME1","CH_THN_SI_2:SD2","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-07","text":"Distance, anonymity, concealment, and conceptual separation become active techniques for modifying passion without altering objective conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME6","CH_THN_SI_2:D8","CH_THN_SVME:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MME-08","text":"Interpretation must distinguish content from force, relation from transition, direct valence from comparative valence, and transmitted emotion from constructed emotion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ME2","CH_THN_SI_2:ME8","CH_THN_SVME:ME2","CH_THN_SVME:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: praise, blame, affection, anger, wealth-esteem, pity, envy, and malice present variable responses to persons and their fortunes.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ALT1","CH_THN_SI_2:ALT1","CH_THN_SVME:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: force and vivacity, directed relation, retained impression, sympathetic conversion, constructive simulation, and comparison generate those variations.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ALT2","CH_THN_SI_2:ALT2","CH_THN_SVME:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: intervention targets authority, proximity, concurrence, associative continuity, and comparative framing rather than objective conditions alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ALT3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD4","CH_THN_SVME:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological/Temporal level: reality consists affectively of ideas and impressions differentiated by degree, while social time operates through persistence, succession, retention, recurrence, and reflected amplification.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:ALT4","CH_THN_SI_2:ALT4","CH_THN_SVME:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological/Performative level: the system repeatedly converts anomalies into controlled variations, tests rival causes, and returns apparent exceptions to a progressively enlarged relational mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:FGS7","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT2","CH_THN_SVME:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-01","text":"Sympathy oscillates between reproduction and construction: the system requires causal signs for reputational transmission but permits pity to arise where the source passion is absent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A4","CH_THN_SXLF:A5","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-02","text":"Proximity explains both agreeable affection and painful envy, but no general principle specifies what selects sympathetic identification rather than comparative opposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D6","CH_THN_SI_2:D15","CH_THN_SVME:D7","CH_THN_SVME:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-03","text":"Direct and comparative valence can reverse one another, yet the activation rule deciding between direct survey and comparison remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:D3","CH_THN_SVME:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-04","text":"Relation is repeatedly established as necessary or contributory, but its sufficiency and quantitative threshold are never independently determined.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT2","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT5","CH_THN_SVME:MRT3","CH_THN_SVME:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-05","text":"Reason functions both as evidential certification and as a product distorted or recruited by passion, leaving its independent corrective power unstable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A11","CH_THN_SXLF:DNX6","CH_THN_SI_2:A7","CH_THN_SI_2:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:FL-06","text":"The shared relational machinery does not fully derive practical direction because the conjunction of love with benevolence and hatred with anger remains constitutionally posited.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:A18","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-01","text":"The idea–impression threshold is the first mutation point: unchanged representational content becomes causally operative passion through increased vivacity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A1","CH_THN_SXLF:A8","CH_THN_SI_2:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-02","text":"Sympathy mutates from transmission into simulation when a represented condition and general rule generate an emotion not present in the observed person.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D1","CH_THN_SI_2:A19","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-03","text":"The self mutates from associative source into socially produced magnitude when another’s fortune becomes an inverse measure of its apparent condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SVME:A5","CH_THN_SVME:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-04","text":"Esteem mutates from communicated pleasure into a recursive status mechanism once the possessor perceives and reflects the observer’s response.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:D11","CH_THN_SI_2:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-05","text":"Relation mutates from resemblance or contiguity as static categories into a directional topology of outward passage, return, convergence, obstruction, and severance.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:A14","CH_THN_SI_2:A11","CH_THN_SVME:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MP-06","text":"Direct affect mutates into comparative affect when a preceding impression is preserved and contributes inversely to the succeeding estimate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVME:A3","CH_THN_SVME:A8","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a directed affective network whose nodes are persons and conditions, whose edges conduct vivacity, and whose feedback alters the nodes that initiated transmission.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD3","CH_THN_SI_2:SD6","CH_THN_SVME:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a vivid but permeable relay: it receives signs, simulates other minds, compares positions, and returns the resulting passion as judgment, esteem, injury, or desire.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:SD2","CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:HSI-03","text":"Time is a circuit of retained affect: past impressions persist into present transitions, durable attribution extends passions, and reflected consequences become new causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD1","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:HSI-04","text":"Available struggle consists in routing and rerouting affect—strengthening authority or resemblance, contesting attribution, breaking association, concealing identity, or altering comparative distance.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:D8","CH_THN_SI_2:D8","CH_THN_SVME:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster is reconstructible from force and vivacity, directed relation, personal certification, sympathetic transformation, comparative transformation, and recursive feedback.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT1","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT1","CH_THN_SVME:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B02","text":"These axioms preserve the actual movement only if sympathy and comparison remain distinct operations rather than being flattened into generic social influence.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:SD8","CH_THN_SVME:D2","CH_THN_SVME:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where identical objects or conditions produce divergent effects under controlled changes in authority, intention, proximity, and comparative framing.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT4","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT4","CH_THN_SVME:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B04","text":"Coherence depends on accepting force and vivacity as the common currency and relations as causal pathways rather than static properties.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:DNX5","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT3","CH_THN_SVME:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B05","text":"The principal underdetermination concerns mode selection and measure: the system does not specify when sympathy, direct survey, or comparison dominates, nor how much relation is sufficient.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT2","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7","CH_THN_SVME:MRT2","CH_THN_SVME:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B06","text":"Reconstruction depends on recursive sequencing because passion-generated judgment, reflected esteem, and remembered comparative advantage cannot be reduced to a static inventory of causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SI_2:D5","CH_THN_SI_2:D13","CH_THN_SVME:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_06:MRT-B07","text":"The indispensable residue resistant to full axiomatic reduction is the constitutional direction of compound passions and the unspecified thresholds governing idea–impression conversion and relational activation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLF:MRT8","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT6","CH_THN_SI_2:MRT7","CH_THN_SVME:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 16–18","context":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame · Sect. III Difficulties Solved · Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","original_title":"Chapters 16–18","original_context":"Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame · Sect. III Difficulties Solved · Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy"},"original_title":"Chapters 16–18","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_07","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 19–21","order":7,"parents":["CH_THN_SXLH","CH_THN_SVEC","CH_THN_SXCL"],"revision":"CL_THN_07@R001","sha256":"2c51e4fc172a07dbba2544d90ba8a0230385506e22f361acce293727bde2d317","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-01","text":"The central problem is to explain affective continuity and variation when species, cognitive capacity, encounter history, activity type, and epistemic content vary independently.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:AS1","CH_THN_SVEC:AS1","CH_THN_SXCL:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-02","text":"Affect is generated less by an object’s intrinsic character than by the mode through which a sensitive system encounters, associates, repeats, pursues, transmits, or stabilizes it.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD5","CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-03","text":"The cluster becomes a differential causal argument: cognitive subtraction, temporal iteration, and controlled variation of truth and assurance isolate operative mechanisms.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-04","text":"A species-general affective substrate persists across unequal cognitive powers, while cognition governs which channels, associations, and pleasures become available.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-05","text":"The movement is from object-side explanation toward processual mediation through sensible impact, mental motion, attention, relation, instinct, sympathy, activity, and belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ME3","CH_THN_SVEC:ME2","CH_THN_SXCL:ME1","CH_THN_SXCL:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-06","text":"Affective force is mobile: attendant emotion converts into passion, pleasure in action transfers concern to an end, and perceived passion communicates itself between organisms.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-07","text":"The real movement is recursive and phase-dependent: affective outputs become conditions of repetition, inclination, pursuit, amplification, stabilization, exhaustion, or reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ASP-08","text":"Closure is conditional rather than monistic: common operations unify the field, but instinct, natural salience, cognitive thresholds, and active/passive divergence prevent reduction to one uniform route.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:DNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] Affective value is constituted through an encounter between an object and an operating sensitive system, not fixed by the object alone.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Pleasure and pain function both as direct effects and as mobile forces capable of augmenting, redirecting, transmitting, or reversing passions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A11","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A13","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Cognitive capacity is not the universal source of passion; it regulates the accessibility, complexity, and prevalence of particular affective channels.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A2","CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXLH:A13","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Affective causes become identifiable through controlled differences: remove reflection, vary repetition, or hold truth and assurance constant while altering the mode of attainment.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Difficulty, facility, excitation, and stability have no invariant valence; their effects depend on degree, temporal position, and active or passive mode.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SD3","CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-06","text":"[Emergent] Affect is transferable across functional relations: from attendant emotion into a predominant passion, from action into its end, and from one sensitive being into another.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-07","text":"[Persistent] Present passion is path-dependent: prior encounters, repeated performances, acquired inclinations, and transferred concerns enter into its current constitution.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Relation is a generative operator rather than a static resemblance: it supports animal attachment, strengthens ideational force, transfers concern, and fixes attention.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D2","CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Activity and passivity divide affective dynamics: active exercise can sustain force and produce concern, while passive reception tends toward transmitted affect, fading excitation, or uncertainty-relief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D5","CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Stability is pleasurable when it resolves resistance or vacillation, but destructive when excessive facility removes the excitation required for interest.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Concern for an end need not precede the process directed toward it; repetition and pleasurable activity can generate inclination or acquired end-concern retrospectively.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Affective plasticity is bounded by prior endowment: natural good and evil, complete disagreeability, instinct, and pre-existing salience constrain what process can produce.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A14","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GA-13","text":"[Terminal] The field is unified by species-general and recursively transferable operations, not by a single uniform mechanism of affective production.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD5","CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Pleasure and pain remain the basic affective currency, but their source migrates from sensible benefit and injury to mental motion, exercised understanding, belief, and doubt.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] Cognition shifts from a capacity whose subtraction tests affective simplicity to an activity whose difficult exercise becomes an original source of pleasure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-03","text":"[Expansion] Relation begins as obvious animal association, then expands into attentional support, ideational force, and the transfer of passion from pursuit to end.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Difficulty is painful resistance in unfamiliar passive apprehension but pleasurable exercise when attention and attainable success organize active inquiry.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A4","CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Familiarity extinguishes novelty and may produce languor under custom, yet can intensify informational curiosity by strengthening relation and ideational force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A8","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-06","text":"[Functional recurrence] Stabilization recurs as tranquillity, orderly facility, and fixed belief; it relieves disturbance but ceases to sustain interest once movement becomes excessively faint.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A10","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-07","text":"[Displacement] Sympathy moves from direct communication of animal passion to faint imaginative concern whose function is not benevolence but attentional fixation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXLH:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-08","text":"[Absorption] The active/passive distinction moves from habit formation into a higher division between self-sustaining pursuit and receptive uncertainty-relief.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-09","text":"[Terminal transformation] Truth disappears as an intrinsic motive after performing an exclusionary function, then returns as either an acquired endpoint of pursuit or the content stabilized by belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:NX1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RMD-10","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] Peculiar parental instinct does not generalize into the cluster’s main process model, but remains as the exception that blocks exhaustive associative reduction.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SXLH:D4","CH_THN_SXLH:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Peculiar parental instinct remains specific to animal affection; at cluster scale it functions chiefly as a limit on causal monism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SXLH:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The motion of the spirits is a chapter-level causal vocabulary for custom whose exact physiology does not scale upward.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A15","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-03","text":"[Local] The division between abstract relations and matters of existence, and the special role of imagined public utility, remain machinery of scientific curiosity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:A6","CH_THN_SXCL:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-04","text":"[Scales upward] The survival of passion under reduced reflection becomes the general proposition that cognition selects affective routes without constituting sensitive life itself.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SXLH:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-05","text":"[Scales upward] Custom’s degree-sensitive phases become a general rule that affective operations change valence according to threshold, sequence, and mode.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SVEC:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Conversion, relational transfer, and sympathy jointly produce the current-level principle of mobile affective force.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-07","text":"[Bridge] The active/passive habit distinction connects custom to curiosity’s division between challenge-seeking inquiry and uncertainty-relieving fixation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-08","text":"[Catalyst] Animal comparison, repeated encounter, and equal-truth comparison become three implementations of one differential method for separating content from operation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:AS6","CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:CGD-09","text":"[Recoded] Sensible benefit, natural emotion, utility, and truth retain local efficacy but are subordinated to the histories and operations through which they become affectively forceful.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A5","CH_THN_SXCL:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-01","text":"No intrinsic object-side property is sufficient to determine the strength, direction, or persistence of passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:DNX1","CH_THN_SXCL:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-02","text":"Reflection, propositional understanding, and truth-recognition are not universal prerequisites of affection or social coordination.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:NX2","CH_THN_SXLH:NX7","CH_THN_SXCL:NX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-03","text":"Increasing repetition, facility, certainty, or cognitive complexity does not produce a monotonic increase in pleasure or passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:DNX7","CH_THN_SVEC:SNX2","CH_THN_SXCL:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-04","text":"A single name—love, custom, or curiosity—does not guarantee a single causal mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:NX6","CH_THN_SXCL:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-05","text":"Species identity cannot serve as the ontological or intentional boundary of affect.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:NX1","CH_THN_SXLH:NX3","CH_THN_SXLH:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-06","text":"Antecedent concern for an end is not necessary for inclination, pursuit, or subsequent distress at failure.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:NX7","CH_THN_SXCL:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-07","text":"Affective communication and imagined utility do not require accurate knowledge of causes, beneficiaries, or terminal purposes.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:DNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:A6","CH_THN_SXCL:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:GNX-08","text":"Process cannot transform every object without remainder: complete disagreeability, natural affective endowment, and instinct mark limits to acquired modification.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:NX5","CH_THN_SVEC:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires a synchronic and fixed subject–object relation of affect to be false; present valence must remain penetrable by history, activity, and mediation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SNX1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-02","text":"The sovereign rational subject must be disqualified as the universal origin of passion, because affect precedes reflection, produces its own ends, and operates through non-propositional transmission.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:DNX1","CH_THN_SXLH:DNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-03","text":"Cognitive hierarchy cannot be treated as moral or affective hierarchy: lower-cost passions may be more prevalent, while cognitively demanding passions require special enabling conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD3","CH_THN_SXLH:DNX7","CH_THN_SXCL:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-04","text":"Belief, sympathy, and relation cannot be merely representational links; each must alter force, stability, attention, or transmission.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:DNX5","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-05","text":"Linear reinforcement must be false: recurrence can amplify, reconcile, stabilize, weaken, exhaust, or reverse according to its phase.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SVEC:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-06","text":"Affective individualism must be false wherever another’s passion or imagined beneficiaries can reorganize conduct without explicit shared purpose.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D5","CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SXCL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:DBN-07","text":"The worth of an object cannot be assumed to motivate in proportion to that worth; natural value, public utility, and truth all require operational mediation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:DNX1","CH_THN_SXCL:NX5","CH_THN_SXCL:DNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSA-01","text":"The system circles an unavailable intrinsic ground of affect: it repeatedly subordinates object-value to process while still presupposing natural goods, evils, saliences, and repugnances that it does not derive.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A14","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSA-02","text":"No common metric specifies how much cognition, facility, excitation, relation, or success crosses the threshold between engagement, indifference, and reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:MRT5","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT2","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSA-03","text":"A self-identical subject prior to affective history is unavailable; the subject appears only as the provisional result of habits, transmitted passions, beliefs, and acquired ends.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD4","CH_THN_SVEC:SD4","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSA-04","text":"A truth-sensitive normative discriminator is absent: the genealogy can explain pursuit, belief, and relief without explaining why true fixation should outrank equally vivid false fixation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:SNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSA-05","text":"Final causal unification remains unavailable because instinct, sensible causation, mental motion, sympathy, activity, and belief are functionally coordinated without reduction to one mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT3","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-01","text":"The common method isolates affective causation by varying capacity, encounter history, or mode of attainment while preventing object-content from carrying the entire explanation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-02","text":"Cognition is a variable operator rather than a uniformly beneficial power: passion survives its reduction, inquiry can derive pleasure from its exercise, and excessive ease can extinguish its engagement.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-03","text":"Active pursuit requires managed resistance: sufficient difficulty activates understanding, success keeps the pursuit completable, and excessive facility removes the motion sustaining interest.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SVEC:A16","CH_THN_SXCL:A2","CH_THN_SXCL:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-04","text":"Affect behaves as a transferable magnitude whose destination depends on conjunction: it passes into a dominant passion, a pursued end, or a sympathizing group.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXLH:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-05","text":"Stabilization is pleasurable only relative to a prior disturbance: orderly facility resolves resistance and belief resolves doubt, but prolonged facility can eliminate the contrast required for interest.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-06","text":"Familiarity has opposed effects because it operates through distinct mediators: it removes novelty under custom while strengthening relation and ideational proximity in informational curiosity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-07","text":"Conduct can precede the motive later attributed to it: repeated action generates inclination, pursuit generates concern for its end, and sympathetic response organizes behavior without knowledge of the initiating object.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D5","CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-08","text":"The distribution of passions is governed partly by processing cost: low-reflection mechanisms diffuse broadly, difficult inquiry requires concentrated exercise, and passive repetition weakens when independent activation is absent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD3","CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:XD-09","text":"Affective construction is powerful but not unlimited: every recursive process requires an initially available sensible effect, natural emotion, engaging idea, tolerable action, or instinctive disposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A14","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-01","text":"The subject becomes a path-dependent configuration of sensitivities, habits, beliefs, transmitted passions, and acquired concerns rather than a fixed rational origin.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD4","CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-02","text":"Affective time is nonlinear: recurrence produces thresholds and phase changes rather than mere accumulation, while later outputs revise the conditions of subsequent encounters.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-03","text":"Ends can be temporally posterior to action: inclination, concern, and the pain of failure may be generated by the process they later appear to motivate.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-04","text":"Epistemic motivation is naturalized without being normatively secured: affect explains why inquiry and belief occur but not why truth deserves pursuit.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:SNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:SNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-05","text":"The individual is affectively porous: sympathy, conjunction, and imagined utility allow social force to operate without shared propositions or benevolent intentions.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D5","CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SXCL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-06","text":"Effective agency occupies a moving interval between inert facility and unmastered agitation, using enough resistance, attention, and success to sustain active motion.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SD3","CH_THN_SXCL:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-07","text":"Affective universality is compatible with mechanistic plurality: common passions need not arise through identical cognitive, associative, instinctive, temporal, or epistemic routes.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","CH_THN_SXLH:SD5","CH_THN_SVEC:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:BSD-08","text":"Causal explanation must decompose nominal passions into source, enabling condition, transfer mechanism, temporal phase, and completion or termination condition.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:AS2","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT3","CH_THN_SXLH:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-01","text":"Novelty loop: unfamiliarity → resistance → excitation → amplified passion → repetition → novelty-decay → facility → inclination → further repetition → languor or reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:FGS2","CH_THN_SVEC:FGS3","CH_THN_SVEC:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-02","text":"Inquiry loop: represented importance → fixed attention → difficult exercise → pleasure → transferred concern for the end → heightened stakes of success and error → renewed pursuit.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:SD2","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS2","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-03","text":"Informational-curiosity loop: relation or novelty → forceful but unstable idea → painful vacillation → demand for belief → vivification and fixation → relief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-04","text":"Sympathetic escalation loop: perceived passion → affective communication → conjunction → intensified collective passion → stronger perceptible passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXLH:A12","CH_THN_SXLH:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-05","text":"Active-habit loop: performance supplies excitation → repetition produces facility and inclination → inclination increases performance → performance renews its own support.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A16","CH_THN_SVEC:A17","CH_THN_SVEC:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-06","text":"Transfer attractor: affect generated at one site migrates toward the object, end, passion, or group most strongly related to the generating operation.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-07","text":"Excitatory-band attractor: the system repeatedly seeks sufficient force to secure attention and motion without crossing into overwhelming resistance, painful vacillation, or affect-destroying facility.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A6","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:RLE-08","text":"Comparative-confirmation loop: a prior human passion theory predicts animal behavior, analogy interprets that behavior as passion, and the interpreted behavior then corroborates the theory.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:AS6","CH_THN_SXLH:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B01","text":"Initial endowment: sensible valence, instinct, natural emotion, or ideational salience supplies the minimum difference on which affective processing can operate.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B02","text":"Differential input: vary cognitive capacity, repetition, difficulty, relation, assurance, or activity mode while holding other factors comparatively stable.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B03","text":"Mediation: input encounters resistance, facility, attention, association, sympathy, exercise, vivacity, or fixation rather than producing passion immediately.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ME3","CH_THN_SVEC:ALT2","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B04","text":"Channel selection: cognitive power, temporal phase, and active/passive mode determine which mediator becomes effective.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SVEC:NX6","CH_THN_SXCL:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B05","text":"Affective conversion: the mediator generates pleasure, pain, agitation, relief, or transmitted passion, which then augments or redirects the operative affection.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A13","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B06","text":"Recursive transfer: generated affect becomes inclination, acquired concern, social amplification, or demand for stabilized belief.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SVEC:A3","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B07","text":"Iteration: the output alters the next encounter, producing reinforcement, tranquillity, exhaustion, intensified pursuit, or reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:FGS3","CH_THN_SVEC:FGS4","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FGS-B08","text":"Conditional closure: recurring operations establish a general dynamic architecture, while instinct, irreducible salience, and unspecified thresholds preserve causal plurality.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:DNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:AFM-B01","text":"Fixed object-value challenged → controlled variation introduced → mediating operation isolated → affect generated or transmitted → force transferred to passion, end, or group → recurrence changes operating conditions → inclination, stabilization, amplification, exhaustion, or reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:FGS8","CH_THN_SVEC:AF1","CH_THN_SXCL:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:AFM-B02","text":"Species-general sensitivity → cognition-dependent channel selection → active/passive bifurcation → threshold-sensitive temporal iteration → acquired concern and collective force → conditional processual closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","CH_THN_SVEC:D7","CH_THN_SXCL:ME5","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-01","text":"The human–animal divide is replaced by affective continuity with graded cognitive access to different mechanisms.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ME1","CH_THN_SXLH:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-02","text":"Object-centered affect is replaced by a relational field in which history, mental operation, attention, and transmission determine experienced force.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:ME2","CH_THN_SXCL:ME1","CH_THN_SXLH:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-03","text":"Cognition is reclassified from sovereign source to variable mechanism: it may be unnecessary, limiting, enabling, effortful, or affect-generating.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A2","CH_THN_SXLH:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-04","text":"Repetition becomes a threshold-governed phase process rather than a synonym for reinforcement or familiarity.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:ME1","CH_THN_SVEC:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-05","text":"Motivation is divided into original pleasure, enabling condition, transferred concern, and completion condition, allowing ends to be produced by activity.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:ALT3","CH_THN_SXCL:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-06","text":"Social coordination becomes intelligible without shared causal knowledge, species identity, retaliation calculation, or genuine benevolence.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:NX3","CH_THN_SXLH:NX7","CH_THN_SXLH:NX8","CH_THN_SXCL:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-07","text":"Truth, utility, and natural valence are reclassified as inputs whose motivational force depends on mediation rather than as self-executing causes.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:DNX1","CH_THN_SXCL:ME1","CH_THN_SXCL:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MME-08","text":"Contradictory effects cease to demand separate faculties once degree, sequence, transfer, and active/passive mode become available as explanatory variables.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:ALT3","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT3","CH_THN_SXLH:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: animal affections, the contrary effects of custom, scientific pursuit, and informational curiosity supply heterogeneous cases of variable passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ALT1","CH_THN_SVEC:ALT1","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: sensible effects, association, instinct, sympathy, resistance, facility, attention, cognitive exercise, belief, and doubt form interacting affective mediators.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ALT2","CH_THN_SVEC:ALT2","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: apparent contradictions are decomposed by controlled comparison into source, condition, mode, threshold, transfer, and phase.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ALT3","CH_THN_SVEC:ALT3","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level: sensitive life is continuous across species but internally historical, with present passions produced by successive encounters and recursive modifications.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ALT4","CH_THN_SVEC:ALT4","CH_THN_SXCL:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B05","text":"Epistemic level: animal passion is inferred analogically, while human truth-seeking is explained genealogically; neither procedure yields direct access to an intrinsically truth-governed interior.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:ALT5","CH_THN_SXLH:SNX2","CH_THN_SXCL:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:ALT-B06","text":"Methodological / Performative level: the cluster performs its own theory by repeatedly exposing a fixed explanation to variation until hidden mediators, reversals, and bifurcations appear.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:AS4","CH_THN_SVEC:AS3","CH_THN_SXCL:AS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-01","text":"Species-general springs coexist with peculiar parental instinct, leaving uncertain whether instinct belongs to the common architecture or marks an irreducible exception.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A3","CH_THN_SXLH:A9","CH_THN_SXLH:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-02","text":"The processual account depends on natural goods, emotions, saliences, and repugnances whose origin it does not explain, limiting its apparent constructivism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A5","CH_THN_SVEC:A12","CH_THN_SVEC:A14","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-03","text":"Familiarity weakens passion by removing novelty but can strengthen curiosity by increasing relation; the architecture distinguishes mediators without specifying their relative dominance.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-04","text":"Uncertainty is enabling within active pursuit but painful as doxastic vacillation; the boundary between productive openness and destabilizing alternation remains underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:SD5","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-05","text":"Belief explains vivid fixation and relief without securing truth, separating the motivational closure of curiosity from epistemic correctness.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:SNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-06","text":"Spirits, sympathy, instinct, attention, and belief provide heterogeneous causal vocabularies whose functional coordination exceeds their explicit theoretical integration.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT3","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:FL-07","text":"Animal behavior confirms a theory imported through human analogy, producing an evidential loop whose corroborative force is real but not fully independent.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX2","CH_THN_SXLH:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-01","text":"Difficulty mutates from resistant agitation in unfamiliar apprehension into pleasurable exertion in active discovery.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A4","CH_THN_SVEC:A5","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-02","text":"Cognition mutates from a dispensable condition of elementary passion into a principal source of scientific pleasure.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A2","CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-03","text":"Relation mutates from an obvious association between beings into an operator that fixes attention, vivifies ideas, and transfers concern to ends.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:A4","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SXCL:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-04","text":"Stability mutates from pleasurable tranquillity into affect-destroying languor under excess, while belief-stability remains pleasurable because it terminates vacillation.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:A9","CH_THN_SVEC:A11","CH_THN_SXCL:A12","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-05","text":"The end mutates from an antecedently valued object into a product of action-generated passion and relational transfer.","refs":["CH_THN_SXCL:NX7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8","CH_THN_SVEC:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MP-06","text":"Sympathy mutates from direct interanimal communication into a faint imaginative mechanism that supports attention without producing genuine concern.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:A10","CH_THN_SXCL:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:HSI-01","text":"Affective reality appears as an excitable network in which objects, ideas, actions, and organisms become nodes for the circulation and conversion of pleasure, pain, attention, and passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a permeable and temporally accumulated switching-point rather than a sovereign center: it receives, amplifies, habituates, pursues, transfers, and stabilizes forces.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD4","CH_THN_SVEC:SD4","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:HSI-03","text":"Time is a sequence of phase transitions in which repetition and pursuit alter the mechanism that will govern the next encounter.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:HSI-04","text":"Viable navigation consists in regulating relation, resistance, attention, repetition, and activity so that affect remains forceful without collapsing into repugnance, vacillation, or languor.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SD3","CH_THN_SXCL:D2","CH_THN_SXCL:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:HSI-05","text":"Collective life is an amplification circuit in which passion can cross bodies and organize conduct before its origin is consciously represented.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:D5","CH_THN_SXLH:D6","CH_THN_SXLH:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster if affect is retained as mediated, transferable, path-dependent, degree-sensitive, and cognitively gated rather than intrinsically object-fixed.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD2","CH_THN_SVEC:SD1","CH_THN_SXCL:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B02","text":"The system is coherent at this scale as a plural dynamic architecture, but not as a reduction to one physiological or psychological mechanism.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SNX4","CH_THN_SVEC:DNX4","CH_THN_SXCL:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B03","text":"The architecture is strongest where differential variation separates content from operation and where affective transfer explains the production of amplified passions, inclinations, and acquired ends.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:SD1","CH_THN_SVEC:A7","CH_THN_SXCL:FGS1","CH_THN_SXCL:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B04","text":"It remains underdetermined at thresholds of cognition, facility, salience, success, and relation, and at the origin of instinctive or naturally affective endowments.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:MRT2","CH_THN_SXLH:MRT6","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT2","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if recursive sequence and functional reversal are retained; flattening difficulty, familiarity, stability, or cognition into fixed causes would destroy the system.","refs":["CH_THN_SVEC:SD3","CH_THN_SVEC:D8","CH_THN_SXCL:SD5","CH_THN_SXLH:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_07:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable remainder resistant to axiomatic reduction is the evidential and causal bridge supplied by analogy, spirits, instinct, and compressed appeals to sympathy or attention.","refs":["CH_THN_SXLH:MRT8","CH_THN_SVEC:MRT3","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT5","CH_THN_SXCL:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 19–21","context":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals · Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom · Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth","original_title":"Chapters 19–21","original_context":"Sect. XII Of The Love And Hatred Of Animals · Sect. V Of The Effects Of Custom · Sect. X Of Curiosity, Or The Love Of Truth"},"original_title":"Chapters 19–21","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_08","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 22–24","order":8,"parents":["CH_THN_PIJI","CH_THN_SIRW","CH_THN_SVSF"],"revision":"CL_THN_08@R001","sha256":"1e867b15f0bce30381c4bd24f45a41e613a760f4712b28700e5fca769b475feb","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B01","text":"The governing problem is how partial passions, scarce transferable goods, and social dependence can generate determinate and morally authoritative juridical relations without innate justice or antecedent property rights.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:AS1","CH_THN_PIJI:AS6","CH_THN_SVSF:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B02","text":"Justice is reconstructed as an artificial but necessary rule-system: natural conditions necessitate coordination, but neither natural impulse nor independent reason directly supplies its form.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:AS2","CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SVSF:AS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B03","text":"The argument becomes a combined motivational genealogy, institutional ontology, psychological jurisprudence, and reductive test of rival natural-right models.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:AS3","CH_THN_SIRW:AS4","CH_THN_SVSF:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B04","text":"First-order self-interest threatens cooperation; reflective self-interest redirects the same passion into reciprocal restraint and gradually stabilized expectations.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_PIJI:D6","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B05","text":"Stabilized possession does not recognize pre-existing property but generates property, right, and obligation as duties constituted by justice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D7","CH_THN_PIJI:SD3","CH_THN_SVSF:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B06","text":"The establishment of property creates a second-order allocation problem: general utility requires determinate titles but cannot derive their particular content, so imagination and antecedent relations select juridical forms.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:AS1","CH_THN_SIRW:AS3","CH_THN_SIRW:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B07","text":"Because possessory, temporal, spatial, causal, and associative relations remain gradual or conflicting, municipal law imposes binary closure where natural criteria underdetermine title.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B08","text":"Interest first establishes observance; the perceived utility of the resulting system then produces sympathetic approval, education, reputation, and institutional reinforcement.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:AS7","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SVSF:A16","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AS-B09","text":"The movement is recursive and sedimentary rather than linear: passion generates restraint, restraint generates rights, rights require stipulative determination, and moralization feeds the determinate system back into conduct as second nature.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SVSF:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Society is necessary under conditions in which human weakness, partial affection, conflicting self-love, and scarce transferable goods make unregulated possession unstable.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A6","CH_THN_PIJI:A7","CH_THN_SVSF:A1","CH_THN_SVSF:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] A moral predicate cannot supply the first motive for the conduct whose morality it evaluates; institutional genesis must therefore begin from pre-moral passions and interests.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A2","CH_THN_PIJI:A3","CH_THN_PIJI:DNX1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-03","text":"[Attractor] Justice is artificial without being arbitrary: it is convention-dependent in origin but functionally necessitated by durable human and material conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_PIJI:SD5","CH_THN_SVSF:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Convention arises through mutually known, conditionally reciprocal restraint grounded in common interest and repeated experience, not through an antecedent promise or sovereign command.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A11","CH_THN_PIJI:A12","CH_THN_PIJI:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Self-interest changes function across scales: immediate acquisition destabilizes society, while reflective and coordinated self-interest generates the rules that restrain acquisition.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_SVSF:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-06","text":"[Terminal] Property is not antecedent to justice but is constituted as a socially enforced relation of duties, expectations, rights, and exclusions.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_PIJI:A14","CH_THN_SVSF:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] The convention stabilizing possession does not itself assign goods; institutional existence and juridical individuation are distinct generative problems.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:FGS8","CH_THN_SIRW:A1","CH_THN_SIRW:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Property has a dual determination: general utility necessitates settled allocation, while imagination and antecedent relations principally specify who receives which title.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-09","text":"[Persistent] Stable justice requires society-wide general and inflexible rules because case-sensitive judgments of need, merit, fitness, or immediate utility reproduce partiality and contention.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_SIRW:A3","CH_THN_SIRW:A4","CH_THN_SVSF:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] Title is temporally layered: present possession governs founding settlement, while occupation, prescription, accession, and succession govern differentiated acquisition within established society.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Juridical outputs are integral and binary even though their psychological, utilitarian, temporal, and relational grounds vary by degree.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-12","text":"[Terminal] Municipal law supplies practical closure where natural association and public utility fail to determine a unique title.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] The justice of an act derives from its position within a socially advantageous rule-system rather than from its isolated consequences.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_PIJI:D9","CH_THN_SIRW:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-14","text":"[Persistent] Justice has a temporally divided foundation: common interest generates observance before sympathy with public utility generates moral approval.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A16","CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_PIJI:D10","CH_THN_SVSF:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GA-15","text":"[Attractor] Once established, convention, juridical determination, moral approbation, education, reputation, and government form a reinforcing system that converts artificial rules into durable dispositions.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D13","CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Justice remains an artificial virtue throughout: neither its necessity, its utility, nor its affective approval converts it into an innate moral principle.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SVSF:A17","CH_THN_SVSF:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-02","text":"[Functional recurrence] Stability of possession first solves social conflict, then reappears as an insufficient general rule whose allocation gap generates the detailed property regime.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:A1","CH_THN_SIRW:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-03","text":"[Escalation] Property moves from an effect of stabilized possession, to a differentiated system of relational titles, to evidence that juridical reality is institutionally constituted.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD3","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2","CH_THN_SVSF:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-04","text":"[Mutation] Utility first explains why justice is necessary, then becomes insufficient to specify particular titles, and finally becomes the public effect whose contemplation moralizes the completed system.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Self-love changes from the principal source of possessory conflict into the originating material of reciprocal restraint and unintended public advantage.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_SVSF:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-06","text":"[Displacement] Imagination migrates from a general psychological faculty of associative completion into a juridical mechanism that joins persons to objects, extends titles, and structures inheritance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:D7","CH_THN_SIRW:D10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-07","text":"[Mutation] Generality changes from reciprocal abstention governing possession into differentiated but society-wide rules whose inflexibility suppresses case-specific natural motivation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_SIRW:A4","CH_THN_SVSF:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-08","text":"[Terminal transformation] Present possession produces founding title but disappears as a perpetual criterion once its founding work is complete; occupation and restitution require its restriction.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-09","text":"[Migration] Graduality moves from possessory probability and temporal association into a general challenge to the integral form of property, right, and obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-10","text":"[Absorption] Sympathy begins as the explanation of moral approval and is absorbed into a larger institutional feedback loop involving public judgment, education, reputation, and stabilized compliance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_PIJI:D11","CH_THN_PIJI:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RMD-11","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] Universal benevolence, direct public concern, private benevolence, and an innate sense of justice disappear as originating motives after forcing the transition to convention and reflected interest.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX4","CH_THN_PIJI:NX6","CH_THN_PIJI:NX7","CH_THN_PIJI:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-01","text":"[Local-to-scaling] The semantic disambiguation of “nature” remains preparatory machinery, but it scales upward by preventing artificial justice from being classified as unnatural, arbitrary, or morally defective.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:AS4","CH_THN_PIJI:NX1","CH_THN_PIJI:NX14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The detailed taxonomies of occupation, prescription, accession, and succession remain lower-layer jurisprudential machinery; only their common relational and temporal structure scales upward.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A10","CH_THN_SIRW:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:A14","CH_THN_SIRW:A16"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-03","text":"[Scales upward] The convention of stable possession becomes the cluster’s constitutive threshold between physical control and normative property.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-04","text":"[Bridge] Imagination bridges institutional necessity and determinate title by converting natural relations of proximity, causality, duration, scale, and kinship into socially stabilized juridical attachments.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] The priority of motive over moral judgment bridges the sentimental theory of virtue and the institutional genealogy by requiring pre-moral interest to precede moral obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A2","CH_THN_PIJI:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-06","text":"[Catalyst] The gradation test converts local observations about fluid possession and natural motivation into a general explanation of why juridical decision requires imposed binary closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-07","text":"[Superseded] Present possession is indispensable at the founding threshold but becomes invalid as a universal title once an established regime supports restitution and differentiated acquisition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-08","text":"[Recoded] Education, reputation, and political reinforcement do not originate justice; they recode an established convention as honor, duty, and second nature.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX15","CH_THN_PIJI:D12","CH_THN_PIJI:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-09","text":"[Local] The solitary state of nature remains an analytical fiction used to isolate passions from understanding; the cluster does not require it as an actual historical stage.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX13","CH_THN_PIJI:DNX7","CH_THN_PIJI:ME8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:CGD-10","text":"[Peripheral] Consensual transfer and promise-performance mark the broader perimeter of justice, but the current architecture develops possession and property rather than independently reconstructing those laws.","refs":["CH_THN_SVSF:A2","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4","CH_THN_PIJI:NX10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-01","text":"Justice cannot be explained by property or right antecedent to convention because those juridical relations are effects of justice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX11","CH_THN_PIJI:D7","CH_THN_SVSF:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-02","text":"Justice is not an innate passion, peculiar natural virtue, or direct expression of ordinary impartial motivation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX4","CH_THN_SVSF:NX1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-03","text":"Property is not an intrinsic, sensible, corporeal, or independently real quality of an object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:NX3","CH_THN_SVSF:NX3","CH_THN_SVSF:NX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-04","text":"Neither reason, public utility, personal fitness, nor natural association supplies a complete and exact code of particular titles.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX9","CH_THN_SIRW:A3","CH_THN_SIRW:NX7","CH_THN_SIRW:NX9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-05","text":"Stable justice cannot remain continuously responsive to individual need, merit, character, benevolence, or optimal use.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX5","CH_THN_SIRW:NX1","CH_THN_SIRW:NX2","CH_THN_SVSF:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-06","text":"Convention cannot be founded on a prior promise, and authoritative law cannot be treated as the original source of moral intelligibility.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX10","CH_THN_PIJI:DNX6","CH_THN_PIJI:NX15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-07","text":"Universal humanity, private benevolence, and direct concern for the public good cannot generate the original invariant rules of justice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX6","CH_THN_PIJI:NX7","CH_THN_PIJI:NX8","CH_THN_SVSF:NX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-08","text":"Isolated consequences cannot determine justice independently of the general rule-system to which an act belongs.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:DNX5","CH_THN_PIJI:D9","CH_THN_SIRW:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-09","text":"Social necessity does not prove natural implantation, while artificiality does not entail dispensability or arbitrariness.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX14","CH_THN_SVSF:D1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:GNX-10","text":"Neither present possession nor recoverable original title can remain the permanent and exhaustive foundation of ownership.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:NX4","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX3","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the primordial-rights model to be false: no pre-social subject-object bond can obligate others before convention institutes duties.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX6","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-02","text":"Self-grounding normativity is inadmissible: morality cannot generate its own first motive, and justice cannot presuppose the rights it constitutes.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:DNX1","CH_THN_PIJI:SNX4","CH_THN_SVSF:NX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-03","text":"A rationalist jurisprudence capable of deducing every title from immutable relations must be false because relational grounds remain graded, conflicting, and psychologically mediated.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:DNX4","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX2","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-04","text":"A continuously optimizing distributive model must be excluded because recurrent reassignment by need, merit, or use would dissolve the stability justice exists to produce.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX5","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX6","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-05","text":"Legal determinacy cannot be assumed to mirror an equally determinate natural terrain; binary verdicts exceed their gradual justificatory grounds.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX5","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-06","text":"Social order cannot require benevolent collective design: coordinated private interests may generate public utility without intending it as a shared end.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX8","CH_THN_SVSF:D4","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-07","text":"Historical priority cannot confer perpetual juridical authority because temporal distance changes the force of relations and permits prescription to displace obscured origins.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:D6","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:DBN-08","text":"The inference from indispensability to innateness must remain invalid; otherwise justice’s functional necessity would erase the distinction between natural passion and artificial strategy.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD5","CH_THN_SVSF:D1","CH_THN_SVSF:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-01","text":"Justice occupies the absence of a naturally impartial legislative passion capable of converting partial desire directly into universal conduct.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX1","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-02","text":"Property occupies the absence of a natural and stable “mine and thine” persisting independently of reciprocal recognition, duty, and law.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX2","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX2","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-03","text":"Municipal decision occupies the absence of a final adjudicative measure beneath convention, imagination, utility, and positive stipulation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:SNX1","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX5","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-04","text":"No seamless middle term connects gradual grounds of entitlement to the integral rights demanded by social coordination.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-05","text":"The unavailable possibility organizing the system is a property order that is simultaneously perfectly stable, continuously equitable, naturally exact, and responsive to every particular case.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX5","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX3","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSA-06","text":"The pre-conventional individual is denied the status of a complete rights-bearing subject; juridical individuality emerges only within stabilized relations of reciprocal conduct.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SNX6","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX1","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-01","text":"The first convention generates property only in general form; the allocation rules then individuate that constituted relation by attaching particular persons to particular goods.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D7","CH_THN_SIRW:A2","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2","CH_THN_SVSF:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-02","text":"Utility, imagination, and law perform irreducible functions: utility demands settlement, imagination selects relational titles, and law terminates disputes where those relations conflict.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-03","text":"Artificial order is generated by natural faculties without directly expressing natural impulse: reflection redirects interest, imagination formats title, and sympathy subsequently moralizes the result.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-04","text":"Reciprocal expectation solves the regress of institutional origin, while stabilized rules convert that non-promissory coordination into duties that can later support promises and transfer.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D6","CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A2","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-05","text":"The system can be non-arbitrary at the level of its social function while remaining stipulative at particular boundaries: necessity governs the existence of settlement, not every detail of its form.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SIRW:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-06","text":"Title can change without physical alteration because juridical change occurs in socially organized expectations and duties rather than in the object’s sensible properties.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:SD4","CH_THN_SVSF:D2","CH_THN_SVSF:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-07","text":"Prescription demonstrates that time is constitutive rather than merely chronological: it redistributes title by weakening one relation and strengthening another in sentiment.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:D6","CH_THN_SVSF:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-08","text":"Public utility changes temporal status from an unintended effect of coordinated self-interest into the contemplated object that elicits moral approval.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_SVSF:D4","CH_THN_SVSF:A16","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-09","text":"Moral approval stabilizes a determinate title-system whose particular assignments are not independently derivable from utility; sympathy sanctions the beneficial scheme rather than rationally deducing each title.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D9","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-10","text":"Juridical impartiality is not the expression of an impartial originating motive but the institutional suppression of variable motives through invariant rules.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A8","CH_THN_SVSF:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:XD-11","text":"The grounds of justice divide into genetic interest, systemic utility, imaginative title-formation, authoritative closure, and sentimental sanction; no single ground performs every legitimating function.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A16","CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-01","text":"Normative order has a distributed sequence unavailable to any single mechanism: pre-moral coordination establishes stability, relational rules individuate property, authoritative decisions close residual ambiguity, and sympathy moralizes the completed system.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD1","CH_THN_SIRW:SD1","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SVSF:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-02","text":"Institutions do not merely regulate antecedent objects; they constitute property as an object of moral judgment and then produce sentiments that appear to recognize its authority.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD3","CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-03","text":"Legal universality is manufactured impartiality: it arises from invariant application across persons, not from universal benevolence, metaphysical equality, or naturally impartial motives.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX6","CH_THN_SIRW:A4","CH_THN_SVSF:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-04","text":"Stable justice requires rigidity at the level of ordinary application but mutability at the level of legal form, since positive rules must settle changing and previously underdetermined relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SIRW:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:A8","CH_THN_SVSF:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-05","text":"Local stipulation is contained by global necessity: particular boundary decisions may lack unique natural derivation while the demand for determinate settlement remains non-arbitrary.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-06","text":"Juridical legitimacy is architecturally plural rather than foundationally unitary: origin, function, form, decision, and moral force are assigned to different mechanisms.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A16","CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-07","text":"The property regime is path-dependent: founding possession establishes initial distributions, established titles structure later associative extensions, and prescription allows elapsed time to overwrite recoverable origin.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-08","text":"Agency is neither sovereign moral choice nor passive determination by appetite; it is the capacity of partial agents to reflect, coordinate, internalize general rules, and act through instituted relations.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:D13","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:BSD-09","text":"Justice recursively converts natural instability into normative reality: passions generate convention, convention generates rights, rights reorganize conduct, and reorganized conduct reinforces the convention’s affective authority.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SIRW:SD4","CH_THN_SVSF:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-01","text":"Immediate interest → possessory conflict → reflection on long-range advantage → reciprocal restraint → social stability → enlarged interest in preserving restraint.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS1","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-02","text":"Conditional restraint → repeated reciprocal conduct → confidence in others’ restraint → stronger expectations → convention capable of sustaining further compliance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A12","CH_THN_PIJI:DNX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-03","text":"Stable possession → property relation → differentiated titles → determinate expectations → increased stability of possession.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:FGS8","CH_THN_SIRW:FGS2","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-04","text":"Systemic advantage → sympathetic approval → education and reputation → internalized probity → stronger adherence → more visible systemic advantage.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D11","CH_THN_PIJI:D13","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-05","text":"Possession escalates from causal power over an object, to stabilized social expectation, to constituted property, to integral juridical right.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A11","CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_SVSF:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-06","text":"Graded relations → conflicting title pressures → municipal decision → binary settlement → new relational baselines from which subsequent titles are generated.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-07","text":"Determinate settlement is the principal attractor: utility, imagination, general rules, and positive law converge not on perfect allocation but on terminated controversy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A3","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:RLE-08","text":"Artificiality becomes a generative machine rather than a negative classification when convention converts natural conflict into universal rules and those rules become morally internalized.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B01","text":"Human weakness + social dependence + partial affection + scarce transferable goods → instability of possession and threat to cooperation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A6","CH_THN_PIJI:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B02","text":"Motivational circularity + failure of universal benevolence, direct public concern, and natural justice → requirement for a pre-moral artificial genesis.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A3","CH_THN_PIJI:NX4","CH_THN_PIJI:NX6","CH_THN_PIJI:NX8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B03","text":"Immediate self-interest + reflection on long-range systemic advantage → second-order redirection of acquisitive passion.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B04","text":"Reciprocal expression + conditional restraint + repeated experience → non-promissory convention and stabilized expectations.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A10","CH_THN_PIJI:A11","CH_THN_PIJI:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B05","text":"Stable expectations of abstention and restoration → justice and injustice → property, right, and obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_PIJI:A14","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B06","text":"General property relation → unresolved person-good allocation → rejection of case-specific fitness and immediate utility.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A2","CH_THN_SIRW:A3","CH_THN_SIRW:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B07","text":"Demand for determinate settlement + associative imagination → title through antecedent person-object relations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A6","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B08","text":"Founding present possession → restriction within established society → occupation, prescription, accession, and succession as differentiated title operations.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B09","text":"Graded possessory power + temporal weakening + conflicting salience → natural underdetermination → municipal stipulation → binary juridical closure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SIRW:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FGS-B10","text":"Socially advantageous rule-system → interested observance → sympathetic moral approval → education, reputation, and political reinforcement → recursive institutional stabilization.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:FGS9","CH_THN_PIJI:FGS10","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B01","text":"Partial passion + scarce transferable goods + social dependence → unstable possession and coordination failure.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D4","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B02","text":"Coordination failure + motivational circularity → elimination of innate justice and antecedent moral obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D2","CH_THN_SVSF:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B03","text":"Immediate self-interest → reflective redirection → reciprocal non-promissory convention.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_PIJI:D6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B04","text":"Reciprocal convention → stable possession → constituted property, right, and obligation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:FGS7","CH_THN_PIJI:FGS8","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B05","text":"Constituted property → allocation gap → general-rule requirement.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A2","CH_THN_SIRW:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B06","text":"General utility + associative imagination → temporally differentiated title forms.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B07","text":"Gradual and conflicting relations → municipal closure → integral juridical verdicts.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:AFM-B08","text":"Systemic utility → sympathetic approval → internalized duty → recursive reinforcement of the conventionally constituted order.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D10","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SVSF:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-01","text":"The natural/artificial opposition is redrawn: natural passions and psychological associations generate artificial institutions whose necessity does not make them innate.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ME1","CH_THN_SIRW:ME3","CH_THN_SVSF:ME1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-02","text":"Property shifts from an object-quality or pre-social right into a norm-mediated relation constituted by duties, expectations, and socially secured title.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ME5","CH_THN_SIRW:ME1","CH_THN_SVSF:ME2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-03","text":"Possession and property become distinguishable: causal control supplies raw relational material, while justice converts selected possession into normative ownership.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A13","CH_THN_SIRW:A5","CH_THN_SIRW:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-04","text":"Utility is decomposed into systemic necessity and moral sanction, while particular title-generation is reassigned primarily to imagination and positive settlement.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ME6","CH_THN_SIRW:ME2","CH_THN_SVSF:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-05","text":"Self-interest is split into first-order destabilization and second-order institutional strategy, collapsing the opposition between egoistic passion and social order.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ME4","CH_THN_SVSF:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-06","text":"Impartiality shifts from an assumed motive to an institutional effect of universal and inflexible application.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_SVSF:D10","CH_THN_SVSF:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-07","text":"Municipal law is reclassified from discovery of pre-existing natural right into authoritative completion of an underdetermined relational field.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:ME6","CH_THN_SVSF:ME6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-08","text":"Time becomes juridically productive: founding moments, duration, obscured origins, succession, and institutional sedimentation alter title without altering objects.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:SD2","CH_THN_SIRW:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-09","text":"Moral obligation is separated from natural obligation and legal determination: interest establishes conduct, law determines relations, and sympathy supplies approbation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ME9","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MME-10","text":"Genealogical reading becomes mandatory: established categories such as property, right, duty, and justice cannot be projected backward to explain the processes that constitute them.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ALT3","CH_THN_SVSF:NX2","CH_THN_SVSF:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The explicit objects are justice, stable possession, property, acquisition, title, adjudication, and the moral approval attached to their observance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ALT1","CH_THN_SIRW:ALT1","CH_THN_SVSF:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Partial passions, scarcity, reciprocal expectations, general rules, associative imagination, municipal closure, and sympathetic reinforcement form the operative institutional system.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ALT2","CH_THN_SIRW:ALT2","CH_THN_SVSF:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Explanation proceeds by reversing presumed dependencies: motives precede moral predicates, justice precedes property, systemic utility precedes approval, and legal decision completes rather than discovers natural determinacy.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ALT3","CH_THN_SIRW:ALT3","CH_THN_SVSF:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Reality contains no object-inherent property; juridical relations emerge gradually, change through temporal and institutional mediation, and become integral only through rule-governed recognition.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:ALT4","CH_THN_SIRW:ALT4","CH_THN_SVSF:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The system performs its thesis through successive elimination, inversion, differentiation, and forced closure: its method reproduces the passage from fluid natural materials to determinate artificial relations.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:FGS3","CH_THN_SIRW:FGS11","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-01","text":"Inflexible juridical rules are generated from fluid, context-sensitive, and sometimes contradictory relations, leaving rigidity dependent upon materials that cannot themselves justify rigidity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:MRT7","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-02","text":"Justice is declared non-arbitrary at the systemic level, yet municipal law must stipulate boundaries for which no unique natural or utilitarian solution exists.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SIRW:SD6","CH_THN_SVSF:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-03","text":"Property is constituted by justice, but the genealogy relies on pre-juridical possession; coherence requires strict separation between physical control and morally secured property.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D7","CH_THN_PIJI:MRT5","CH_THN_SIRW:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-04","text":"General utility necessitates determinate title but underdetermines particular title, creating a gap between the justification of having a property system and the justification of its distributions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT2","CH_THN_SIRW:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-05","text":"Justice is both an offspring of passion and contrary to ordinary passionate conduct; the reconciliation depends on an implicit distinction between first-order impulse and second-order strategy.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-06","text":"Integral rights remain in tension with gradual possession, utility, associative force, and ordinary judgments of partial entitlement.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:A10","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:FL-07","text":"The convention is structurally intelligible as reciprocal expectation, but its exact historical emergence, thresholds of confidence, and transition into authoritative law remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A12","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT2","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-01","text":"Self-love mutates from destabilizing appetite into reflective coordination once its object expands from immediate acquisition to preservation of the social system.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D5","CH_THN_SVSF:A3","CH_THN_SVSF:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-02","text":"Possession mutates from causal power over an object into constant socially secured possession and then into an integral property relation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A5","CH_THN_SIRW:A11","CH_THN_SVSF:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-03","text":"Utility mutates from the cause of institutional necessity into an insufficient title criterion and finally into the object of moral contemplation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A15","CH_THN_SIRW:A7","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-04","text":"Present possession changes valence from a peace-preserving founding rule into a seizure-authorizing principle that must be rejected in established society.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A8","CH_THN_SIRW:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-05","text":"Public good changes from an absent originating intention into an emergent effect and then into the object that generates moral approbation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX8","CH_THN_SVSF:D4","CH_THN_SVSF:A17"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MP-06","text":"Artificiality changes from a possible objection to justice into the positive name for passion’s second-order capacity to generate necessary universal order.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:NX14","CH_THN_PIJI:A18","CH_THN_SVSF:SD1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:HSI-01","text":"Reality is figured as a layered conversion machine: unstable passions become reciprocal expectations, expectations become rules, rules become rights, and rights become morally charged habits.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:SD1","CH_THN_PIJI:SD8","CH_THN_SVSF:FGS4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a partial desiring node capable of reflection, association, sympathy, and internalization rather than a pre-social bearer of complete rights or an impartial moral will.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A5","CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_PIJI:A17","CH_THN_PIJI:SNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:HSI-03","text":"Time is sedimentary and transformative: repetition creates confidence, duration alters title, institutional age weakens origins, and reinforcement converts artifice into second nature.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A12","CH_THN_PIJI:SD6","CH_THN_SIRW:A13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:HSI-04","text":"Law is a hard binary shell constructed over a graded field of possessory power, utility, salience, proximity, duration, and sentiment.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:A12","CH_THN_SIRW:A15","CH_THN_SVSF:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:HSI-05","text":"Navigation remains possible through reflective coordination, general-rule revision, and authoritative settlement, but not through escape to natural property, unrestricted equity, or case-by-case passionate judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:A9","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX3","CH_THN_SVSF:A15"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B01","text":"The global axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the cluster’s central machine: social instability, reflected interest, reciprocal convention, constituted property, relational title, legal closure, and subsequent moralization.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:MRT1","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT1","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B02","text":"The system remains underdetermined concerning the exact historical emergence of convention, the relative causal weight of utility and imagination, and the thresholds governing possession, prescription, and comparative importance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:MRT2","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT3","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B03","text":"Coherence is preserved only if physical possession, constituted property, interested observance, legal determination, and moral approbation remain distinct explanatory moments.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:MRT2","CH_THN_PIJI:MRT5","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where it reverses conceptual priority from property to justice and distinguishes utility’s demand for settlement from imagination’s production of particular title.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D7","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT5","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on sequencing and recursive return: convention must precede property, title determination must follow general stabilization, and moral sanction must feed back only after social utility becomes visible.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:D10","CH_THN_SIRW:FGS2","CH_THN_SVSF:D11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B06","text":"The element most resistant to axiomatic reduction is authoritative boundary production: the system demonstrates its necessity but cannot derive every decision from a common natural measure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIRW:SNX1","CH_THN_SIRW:SNX5","CH_THN_SVSF:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_08:MRT-B07","text":"The reconstruction preserves the actual movement from motivational and ontological negation through institutional generation and juridical differentiation to practical closure and recursive moral reinforcement.","refs":["CH_THN_PIJI:MRT9","CH_THN_SIRW:MRT8","CH_THN_SVSF:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 22–24","context":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice · Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property · Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice","original_title":"Chapters 22–24","original_context":"Part II Of Justice And Injustice · Sect. III Of The Rules Which Determine Property · Sect. VI Some Farther Reflections Concerning Justice And Injustice"},"original_title":"Chapters 22–24","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"CL_THN_09","layer":"CL","title":"Chapters 25–27","order":9,"parents":["CH_THN_SIMA","CH_THN_PIOV","CH_THN_SIGB"],"revision":"CL_THN_09@R001","sha256":"e354135cb352e3e724355158a0bb39337b414a443d1e6c97af02e45a77e8dec0","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-01","text":"The central problem is to generate stable moral and political authority from partial passions, variable sympathy, limited benevolence, and fallible rulers without invoking reason, original contract, or unconditional command.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:AS1","CH_THN_PIOV:AS1","CH_THN_SIGB:AS1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-02","text":"The cluster becomes a recursive naturalistic genealogy: interest and affect generate local valuations; social interaction generalizes them; generalized norms then exceed, correct, and sometimes reverse their originating conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:AS3","CH_THN_SIMA:AS6","CH_THN_PIOV:AS2","CH_THN_PIOV:AS6","CH_THN_SIGB:AS4","CH_THN_SIGB:AS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-03","text":"Public security, useful or agreeable character, and benevolent relational effects supply the first-order materials from which allegiance, virtue, and merit arise.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:A3","CH_THN_SIGB:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-04","text":"Because first-order sentiment varies with position, distance, interest, and fortune, moral stability requires general rules, common standpoints, and socially corrected judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A5","CH_THN_SIGB:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-05","text":"Evaluation therefore shifts from isolated acts and immediate outcomes to durable dispositions, natural tendencies, relational roles, and institutional functions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_PIOV:A3","CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-06","text":"Generalization gives normativity temporal reach: allegiance may persist after its immediate interest weakens, and virtue may survive accidental failure or attenuated sympathy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_PIOV:D4","CH_THN_PIOV:D5","CH_THN_SIGB:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-07","text":"Temporal persistence is not absoluteness: recurrent anti-functional conduct establishes a generalized counter-rule that converts anger into blame, cruelty into vice, and destructive government into resistance-authorizing tyranny.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:D7","CH_THN_SIGB:D9","CH_THN_PIOV:A18"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BAS-08","text":"The movement is spiral rather than linear: natural limitation → social stabilization → normative excess → corrective reclassification; closure is conditional, relational, and non-algorithmic.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:AF1","CH_THN_PIOV:AF1","CH_THN_SIGB:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Normative authority is generated within ordinary human nature: interest, pleasure, uneasiness, and sympathetically apprehended relations furnish its operative materials.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_SIMA:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A1","CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Human motivation is partial, variable, and passion-bound; neither rulers nor spectators occupy a naturally impartial or morally transformed position.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A9","CH_THN_SIMA:A11","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_PIOV:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A1","CH_THN_SIGB:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Sympathy makes the effects of qualities on others available to spectators who possess no direct private interest in those effects.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:A10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-04","text":"[Attractor] Shared moral order is produced by generalizing situated responses into stable rules, common standpoints, and publicly usable evaluative language.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A5","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-05","text":"[Persistent] The principal evaluative object is a durable tendency or function, not an isolated act, transient feeling, or accidental outcome.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A12","CH_THN_PIOV:A3","CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:A2","CH_THN_SIGB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Interest and utility change scope across the cluster: they uniquely sanction political allegiance but constitute only part of the plural basis of moral approval.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_SIMA:A16","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A9","CH_THN_SIGB:A9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Artifice does not create ultimate value; it coordinates natural limitations and interests into promises, government, justice, good-breeding, and stable moral discourse.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:SD6","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Generalized judgment acquires relative autonomy from its origin: it can preserve obligation beyond immediate interest and preserve esteem despite weak affection, distance, or bad fortune.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_PIOV:D4","CH_THN_PIOV:D5","CH_THN_SIGB:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] A legitimate exception must itself be generalizable: repeated anti-functional conduct, not private injury or isolated failure, authorizes normative reversal.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:D8","CH_THN_SIGB:D7","CH_THN_SIGB:D9","CH_THN_PIOV:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Corrected judgment, occurrent affection, moral language, and practical motivation remain distinct layers and need not converge.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_PIOV:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-11","text":"[Terminal] Offices, capacities, passions, and dispositions acquire moral valence from their direction and relational effects; systematic reversal of function produces reclassification from virtue or authority into vice or enmity.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A13","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GA-12","text":"[Attractor] The cluster converges on a conditionally ordered relational field: character is judged across its roles and government by its protective function, while no single utility calculus governs every moral source.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD1","CH_THN_PIOV:D7","CH_THN_PIOV:SD7","CH_THN_SIGB:A14","CH_THN_SIGB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Sympathy retains its function as affective transmission: another’s pleasures and pains become spectator sentiments capable of grounding approval, affection, blame, and hatred.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A4","CH_THN_SIGB:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-02","text":"[Mutation] Interest moves from the cause of convention and exclusive sanction of allegiance to one evaluative relation among usefulness, immediate agreeableness, self-regard, and regard for others.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-03","text":"[Functional recurrence] The general point of view, common standard, general moral rule, and general opinion perform homologous work: they convert situated responses into socially stable verdicts.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-04","text":"[Reversal] Generalization first stabilizes allegiance and moral language, then becomes the mechanism by which recurrent abuses establish an exception to obedience.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_SIMA:SD2","CH_THN_PIOV:SD1","CH_THN_SIGB:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-05","text":"[Domain expansion] Natural tendency migrates from the evaluation of personal dispositions under bad fortune to the evaluation of government under repeated historical performance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:D9","CH_THN_SIMA:A12","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-06","text":"[Absorption] The natural/artificial distinction ceases to divide sources of moral authority and instead marks different scales and mechanisms of coordinated benefit.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-07","text":"[Escalation] Neutral capacities become morally charged through direction: benevolence makes power useful, while ambition or cruelty converts capacity and office into magnified harm.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:D4","CH_THN_SIMA:A10","CH_THN_SIMA:A11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-08","text":"[Disappearance after structural work] Promise, custom, and imitation disappear as original grounds once they have exposed the need for a prior interest capable of generating and transmitting allegiance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A15","CH_THN_SIMA:A16","CH_THN_SIMA:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-09","text":"[Absorption after diagnostic use] Pride, tenderness, modesty, and heroic elevation remain local test cases, but their higher-order result is retained as evaluative pluralism and the separation of inward merit from social presentation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:SD6","CH_THN_PIOV:SD7","CH_THN_SIGB:D6","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RMD-10","text":"[Mutation] Negative passion moves from constitutionally fitting anger to moral blame and, under public, recurrent, and severe injury, to resistance against tyrannical power.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:D7","CH_THN_PIOV:A18","CH_THN_SIMA:D8","CH_THN_SIMA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-01","text":"[Local] The regress from promise through convention to interest is specific machinery for dismantling contractual allegiance; its scalable result is the dependence of artificial norms on prior human interests.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D1","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The detailed mechanics of pride, comparison, heroic elevation, and conventional modesty remain a stress test of the four-source moral system rather than a global account of political authority.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:D10","CH_THN_PIOV:D12","CH_THN_PIOV:D13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-03","text":"[Local] Tenderness and qualified anger establish the dual sources and degree-sensitivity of benevolent merit but do not independently determine the limits of allegiance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:D5","CH_THN_SIGB:D6","CH_THN_SIGB:D7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-04","text":"[Scales upward] Correction for distance, private position, current disposition, and fortune becomes the cluster-wide mechanism for separating stable judgment from immediate affect.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:D3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-05","text":"[Scales upward] Evaluation by durable tendency scales from personal character to institutional function and supplies the shared grammar of virtue, vice, legitimacy, and tyranny.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:SD4","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Artificial justice and government are coordinated schemes whose moral standing depends on general benefit rather than the independent value of every isolated act.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:SD4","CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_SIMA:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-07","text":"[Catalyst] The distinction between originating cause and persisting moral rule forces the transition from simple causal genealogy to a temporal account of normative inertia and correction.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A6","CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_PIOV:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:CGD-08","text":"[Superseded] Utility monism, raw sympathetic intensity, isolated outcome evaluation, and absolute allegiance are jointly replaced by plural sources, corrected judgment, dispositional appraisal, and conditional authority.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME2","CH_THN_PIOV:ME3","CH_THN_PIOV:ME6","CH_THN_SIGB:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-01","text":"Moral and political authority is not founded by affect-free reason, a prepolitical promise, or arbitrary political imposition.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:NX1","CH_THN_SIMA:NX2","CH_THN_PIOV:NX1","CH_THN_PIOV:NX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-02","text":"Convention, custom, imitation, and education can transmit or regulate norms but cannot supply their original evaluative force.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:NX8","CH_THN_SIMA:D7","CH_THN_PIOV:NX5","CH_THN_PIOV:SD6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-03","text":"Neither the spectator’s private interest nor raw sympathetic intensity can function as a stable universal standard.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:NX7","CH_THN_PIOV:NX8","CH_THN_SIGB:NX2","CH_THN_SIGB:NX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-04","text":"Isolated action, accidental success, and momentary institutional performance do not exhaust the value of a durable disposition or function.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:NX2","CH_THN_PIOV:NX9","CH_THN_SIMA:NX10","CH_THN_SIGB:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-05","text":"Moral generality does not require universal benevolence, equal emotional concern, or complete practical self-sacrifice.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:NX10","CH_THN_SIGB:NX1","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-06","text":"Public utility does not exhaust virtue, even though public interest remains the specific natural sanction of political allegiance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:NX6","CH_THN_SIGB:NX4","CH_THN_SIMA:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-07","text":"Office, power, capacity, naturalness, and immediate agreeableness do not carry self-validating moral worth.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:NX6","CH_THN_SIMA:NX7","CH_THN_SIGB:NX5","CH_THN_SIGB:NX7","CH_THN_PIOV:NX11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:GNX-08","text":"Passive obedience is not absolute, but resistance is not authorized by every private injury; both obedience and opposition remain answerable to generalized public function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:NX9","CH_THN_SIMA:NX10","CH_THN_SIMA:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-01","text":"The cluster cannot admit a self-grounding norm severed from human affect, interest, social practice, and relational consequence; such a norm would make its genealogies causally idle.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:DNX1","CH_THN_PIOV:SNX1","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-02","text":"Radical affective incommensurability must be false: without transferable passions and sufficiently shared operations, neither sympathy nor common moral language could arise.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:DNX1","CH_THN_PIOV:DNX2","CH_THN_SIGB:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-03","text":"No positional privilege—first-person interest, spectator proximity, or political office—can confer universal evaluative authority.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:DNX3","CH_THN_SIGB:NX2","CH_THN_SIMA:DNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-04","text":"General rules cannot be either identical with their immediate causes or wholly independent of them: identity would prevent stability, while independence would make correction and resistance impossible.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:DNX3","CH_THN_PIOV:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-05","text":"Accidental failure and stable anti-tendency cannot be morally indistinguishable; without their separation, bad fortune would destroy virtue and recurrent tyranny would remain protected as mere mischance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:D5","CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_SIMA:D8","CH_THN_SIGB:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:DBN-06","text":"Shared moral order cannot require convergence among verdict, affection, and action; human partiality would otherwise make stable discourse and generalized political judgment unavailable.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:DNX5","CH_THN_PIOV:DNX6","CH_THN_SIGB:A8","CH_THN_SIGB:SD5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSA-01","text":"The absent center is an external moral tribunal: no view from nowhere, rational relation, natural sovereign title, or transcendent obligation terminates evaluation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SNX1","CH_THN_SIMA:SNX2","CH_THN_PIOV:SNX1","CH_THN_SIGB:SNX2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSA-02","text":"Universal affection is unavailable; generalized judgment substitutes for a motivational impartiality that ordinary human constitution cannot sustain.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:SNX2","CH_THN_SIGB:SNX1","CH_THN_SIGB:SNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSA-03","text":"Unmediated access to moral reality is unavailable: character and authority appear only through signs, inferred tendencies, historical patterns, and relational effects.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:SNX4","CH_THN_SIGB:SNX5","CH_THN_SIMA:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSA-04","text":"The system lacks a common metric capable of calculating conflicts among utility, immediate agreeableness, self-regard, fitting anger, and the threshold of intolerable tyranny.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SNX3","CH_THN_PIOV:SNX3","CH_THN_SIGB:SNX4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSA-05","text":"An authority permanently immune to historical reversal is ontologically inadmissible: human frailty makes recurrent anti-function unavoidable and preserves the possibility of a counter-rule.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SNX4","CH_THN_SIMA:DNX2","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-01","text":"Corrected standpoint converts resistance from a response to private injury into a judgment that public protection has failed across a generalizable class of cases.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D8","CH_THN_PIOV:D8","CH_THN_SIGB:D1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-02","text":"Dispositional evaluation distinguishes accidental failure from systematic anti-function: bad fortune need not destroy virtue, but recurrent oppression reveals a settled institutional tendency and cancels authority.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_PIOV:D5","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-03","text":"Government is evaluable as an artificial virtue operating through a coordinated scheme: its justification belongs to the general security it produces, not to the independent benefit of every governmental act.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_SIMA:A3","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-04","text":"Political power is a morally indeterminate capacity whose valence is fixed by direction: office can coordinate justice, but ambition or cruelty can turn the same capacity into enlarged public harm.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A10","CH_THN_SIMA:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-05","text":"Sympathy supplies the missing moral link between public utility and disinterested judgment, allowing allegiance to be morally intelligible without an original promise.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D1","CH_THN_PIOV:D2","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-06","text":"A calm judgment that allegiance has ceased may precede effective resistance because generalized verdict, habitual obedience, private affection, and motivating passion operate at different intensities.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:D5","CH_THN_PIOV:SD2","CH_THN_SIGB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-07","text":"Fitting anger and legitimate resistance share a proportional structure: negative response becomes justified when harm is severe, attributable to durable agency, and no longer confined to isolated resentment.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD4","CH_THN_PIOV:A18","CH_THN_SIGB:D7","CH_THN_SIGB:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-08","text":"Convention is a secondary coordination technology: custom transmits allegiance, good-breeding regulates pride, and social discourse stabilizes judgment, but none supplies the ultimate end by which it is assessed.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D7","CH_THN_PIOV:SD6","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:XD-09","text":"Political obligation remains more narrowly functional than virtue: immediate tenderness or agreeableness may confer merit on character, but cannot preserve allegiance after government systematically destroys public security.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_SIMA:A5","CH_THN_PIOV:D7","CH_THN_SIGB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-01","text":"The cluster produces a relational-dispositional ontology: persons and institutions are what their durable powers are fitted to produce for themselves and others under socially adequate conditions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-02","text":"Moral objectivity becomes endogenous without becoming private: shared human constitution, sympathetic transmission, and standpoint correction stabilize judgments that no individual interest can dictate.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD5","CH_THN_PIOV:SD1","CH_THN_SIGB:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-03","text":"Normative time is layered: tendencies persist beneath accidents, rules persist beyond immediate causes, and repeated historical failure eventually changes the classification of the object.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_PIOV:D5","CH_THN_SIGB:D2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-04","text":"Resistance becomes an internal operation of moral judgment rather than an external suspension of order: the same generalized functionality that authorizes allegiance also identifies its terminal failure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD1","CH_THN_SIMA:SD2","CH_THN_SIMA:SD4","CH_THN_PIOV:A12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-05","text":"Power has no autonomous moral title; its worth is derivative from the passions directing it and the relational field of benefit or injury it organizes.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A10","CH_THN_SIMA:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:DNX5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-06","text":"The subject divides into partial agent, sympathetic spectator, corrected judge, and potential political resister; these positions communicate but do not collapse into one another.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D5","CH_THN_PIOV:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:BSD-07","text":"The terminal moral order is plural but bounded: utility, immediate agreeableness, self-regard, and regard for others coexist, while role-destroying cruelty and protection-destroying tyranny mark outer limits.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD3","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:SD7","CH_THN_SIGB:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-01","text":"Partial sympathy → contradictory situated judgments → conversational correction → common standpoint → stable moral language → renewed correction of situated judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A5","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-02","text":"Insecurity → invention of government → public protection → allegiance → general-rule inertia → rulerly abuse → historical generalization → resistance exception → reaffirmation of protection as the criterion.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:FGS3","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS4","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS5","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS6","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-03","text":"Conduct or expression → inference to character → sympathetic pleasure or pain → approval or blame → love, anger, and social response → new conduct and expression available for judgment.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:FGS1","CH_THN_PIOV:FGS4","CH_THN_PIOV:FGS10","CH_THN_SIGB:FGS5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-04","text":"Natural limitation → artificial coordination → stabilized conduct → acquired moral force → functional reassessment of the coordinating institution.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A2","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-05","text":"Immediate effect → correction for distance or fortune → evaluation of natural tendency → preservation under accidental failure or reversal under recurrent anti-function.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-06","text":"Uneasiness → anger or blame → attribution to durable harmful character → recurrence and public generalization → phase change from private resentment to legitimate resistance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A18","CH_THN_SIGB:D8","CH_THN_SIGB:D9","CH_THN_SIMA:D8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:RLE-07","text":"The dominant attractor is role-sustaining relational function: dispersed analyses of benevolence, character, justice, government, cruelty, and tyranny converge on whether a disposition makes shared relations supportable.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SD1","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIGB:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B01","text":"Human constraints: limited generosity + variable sympathy + insecurity + persistent rulerly passion.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A3","CH_THN_SIMA:A9","CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B02","text":"Relational encounter: conduct, passion, or institution produces advantage, harm, pleasure, uneasiness, protection, or exposure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B03","text":"Transmission: direct interest and sympathy convert relational effects into allegiance, approval, affection, blame, or anger.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A6","CH_THN_PIOV:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A18","CH_THN_SIGB:A4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B04","text":"Destabilization: distance, private interest, fortune, and divergent position generate variable or contradictory verdicts.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:A5","CH_THN_SIGB:A7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B05","text":"Generalization: common standpoint + general rule + social discourse correct local variation and stabilize evaluative language.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_SIGB:A6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B06","text":"Object construction: isolated event → inferred durable disposition; individual benefit → coordinated scheme; office-holder → protective or destructive institutional function.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A3","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B07","text":"Temporal test: accidental failure preserves standing through natural tendency; recurrent anti-function generates vice, tyranny, and a generalized exception.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:D9","CH_THN_SIMA:D4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FGS-B08","text":"Terminal output: plural virtue + role-complete relational judgment + conditional allegiance + morally internal resistance.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:D7","CH_THN_SIGB:A14","CH_THN_SIMA:SD1","CH_THN_SIMA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:AFM-B01","text":"Partial affection and public insecurity → relational effects → interest and sympathetic uptake → conflict among situated responses → common standpoint and general rules → dispositional-functional evaluation → normative stabilization → recurrent-failure test → conditional moral-political order.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:AF1","CH_THN_PIOV:AF1","CH_THN_SIGB:AF1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:AFM-B02","text":"Immediate sentiment → correction for distance and fortune → calm judgment → stable moral language, while practical passion remains only partially transformed.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:FGS6","CH_THN_PIOV:FGS8","CH_THN_SIGB:FGS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:AFM-B03","text":"Sustained beneficial tendency → virtue or authority; accidental obstruction → preserved standing; recurrent relational harm → vice or tyranny → anger, blame, or resistance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:FGS4","CH_THN_SIMA:FGS6","CH_THN_PIOV:FGS2","CH_THN_SIGB:FGS7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-01","text":"Morality and politics become continuous in generative grammar without becoming identical in criterion: both arise from human effects and generalized judgment, but allegiance remains specifically tied to public protection.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME1","CH_THN_PIOV:ME4","CH_THN_SIGB:ME5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-02","text":"The moral object shifts from event to architecture: durable character, relational disposition, coordinated scheme, and institutional function replace isolated action as the principal unit of evaluation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME3","CH_THN_PIOV:ME1","CH_THN_PIOV:ME6","CH_THN_SIGB:ME7"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-03","text":"Impartiality is reclassified from affective neutrality to socially corrected judgment compatible with unequal concern and limited motivation.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:ME3","CH_THN_PIOV:ME5","CH_THN_SIGB:ME2","CH_THN_SIGB:ME3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-04","text":"The natural/artificial opposition collapses as a division of moral sources and survives only as a distinction between immediate relational benefit and coordinated systemic benefit.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME6","CH_THN_PIOV:ME2","CH_THN_PIOV:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-05","text":"Obedience and resistance cease to be moral opposites: resistance becomes the generalized corrective form taken by allegiance when government reverses its constitutive function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME2","CH_THN_SIMA:ME3","CH_THN_SIMA:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-06","text":"Power and ability are reclassified as directed capacities rather than intrinsic merits; benevolence, ambition, cruelty, and institutional role determine their social valence.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A10","CH_THN_SIMA:A11","CH_THN_SIGB:ME4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-07","text":"Time becomes evaluatively constitutive: tendency corrects fortune, habit extends obligation, and recurrent history supplies evidence for terminal reclassification.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ME5","CH_THN_PIOV:ME6","CH_THN_SIGB:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MME-08","text":"Adequate reading now requires separation of cause, standard, verdict, motive, and presentation; treating them as one level would erase the system’s principal corrections and tensions.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT3","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT2","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: the cluster classifies benevolence, pride, anger, cruelty, political allegiance, tyranny, and resistance.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ALT1","CH_THN_PIOV:ALT1","CH_THN_SIGB:ALT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: sympathy, interest, general rules, corrected standpoints, dispositional inference, and artificial coordination produce stable moral and political judgments.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ALT2","CH_THN_PIOV:ALT2","CH_THN_SIGB:ALT2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: struggle is reframed as classification of tendencies and functions; intervention becomes legitimate when private grievance is converted into a general judgment of recurrent relational failure.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ALT3","CH_THN_PIOV:ALT3","CH_THN_SIGB:ALT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level: reality consists of durable yet fallible affective agents and institutions whose standing persists through accidents but mutates under accumulated evidence of anti-function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:ALT4","CH_THN_PIOV:ALT4","CH_THN_SIGB:ALT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:ALT-B05","text":"Methodological / Performative level: genealogical regress, objection-correction, analogical transfer, and stress cases perform the movement from situated response to generalized architecture.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:AS7","CH_THN_PIOV:AS4","CH_THN_PIOV:AS7","CH_THN_SIGB:AS3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-01","text":"Public interest is presented as the exclusive original sanction of allegiance, while virtue has four sources and benevolence two; the cluster supplies no rule determining how far moral pluralism enters political evaluation.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A16","CH_THN_PIOV:A6","CH_THN_SIGB:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-02","text":"General moral opinion is declared infallible in the political argument, but elsewhere the common standard is a constructed correction whose mechanism and unique content remain underdetermined.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A14","CH_THN_PIOV:SD1","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT2","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-03","text":"Natural tendency protects character from accidental outcomes, whereas allegiance is canceled by destructive performance; no exact criterion separates temporary failure from evidence of settled anti-function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:SNX3","CH_THN_PIOV:A12","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT7","CH_THN_SIGB:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-04","text":"Calm verdict can diverge from actual affection and motivation, yet resistance requires practical mobilization; the passage from moral authorization to effective political action remains unspecified.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D5","CH_THN_PIOV:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-05","text":"Utility, immediate agreeableness, self-regard, heroic elevation, tenderness, and fitting anger lack a common weighting rule, leaving conflicts among approved sources irreducibly qualitative.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:MRT4","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT6","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT3","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-06","text":"Goodness is judged within a limited relational sphere, while allegiance appeals to public interest and general opinion; the transition from connected persons to the relevant political public remains compressed.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A1","CH_THN_SIGB:A2","CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_SIMA:A14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:FL-07","text":"Moral rules must exceed their original causes to secure constancy but remain dependent on those causes to permit correction; the architecture requires this unresolved relative autonomy.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:DNX3","CH_THN_PIOV:DNX6"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MP-01","text":"Interest mutates from causal origin of convention, to sanction of allegiance, to one sympathetically mediated source of moral approval.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A4","CH_THN_PIOV:A7","CH_THN_SIGB:A3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MP-02","text":"General rule mutates from stabilizer of obedience into generator of the exception that terminates obedience.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:A7","CH_THN_SIMA:A8","CH_THN_SIMA:SD2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MP-03","text":"Character mutates from an inferred personal disposition into a model for institutional identity, permitting the magistrate–tyrant distinction to be read as functional reclassification.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A3","CH_THN_PIOV:SD3","CH_THN_SIMA:SD3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MP-04","text":"Anger mutates from an inherent, degree-sensitive passion into blame and finally into a generalized political response to recurrent public harm.","refs":["CH_THN_SIGB:A12","CH_THN_SIGB:D7","CH_THN_PIOV:A18","CH_THN_SIMA:SD4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MP-05","text":"Artifice mutates from a suspected source of arbitrary moral invention into the necessary coordination layer through which natural interests and sentiments acquire stable social form.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:NX5","CH_THN_PIOV:A14","CH_THN_SIMA:A1","CH_THN_SIMA:A2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:HSI-01","text":"Reality is implicitly a network of affective relays and functional arrangements: passions transmit, standpoints correct, conventions stabilize, and recurrent effects reclassify the nodes that produced them.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:FGS6","CH_THN_PIOV:FGS4","CH_THN_SIGB:FGS2"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:HSI-02","text":"The subject is neither sovereign reason nor passive appetite but a divided agent-spectator whose local passions can be generalized in judgment without being extinguished in action.","refs":["CH_THN_PIOV:A10","CH_THN_PIOV:A13","CH_THN_SIGB:A8"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:HSI-03","text":"Time is a layered filter: habit preserves rules, counterfactual tendency survives accidents, and accumulated history converts repeated failure into a phase change of moral status.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D3","CH_THN_SIMA:D4","CH_THN_PIOV:D5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:HSI-04","text":"Struggle remains possible as functional diagnosis and generalized correction: one navigates between private resentment and passive obedience by identifying durable, public, and recurrent anti-function.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:D8","CH_THN_SIMA:SD4","CH_THN_SIGB:D9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B01","text":"The cluster is reconstructible from six indispensable components: affective-interest genealogy, sympathy, corrected standpoint, dispositional tendency, artificial coordination, and generalized exception.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT1","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT1","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT1"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B02","text":"The reconstruction remains underdetermined at three points: the content of the common standard, the weighting of plural moral sources, and the threshold separating misgovernment from resistance-authorizing tyranny.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT4","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT4","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT2","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT3"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent only if cause and moral rule, immediate affection and corrected judgment, accidental outcome and durable tendency, and private injury and public failure remain distinct.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT3","CH_THN_SIMA:MRT7","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT2","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT4"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture is strongest where one generalizing mechanism explains both normative stability and corrective reversal, and where one dispositional grammar evaluates both character and government.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT2","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT3","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT5"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction depends on recursive sequencing rather than source order: local sentiment must generate social correction, correction must produce temporal excess, and excess must encounter recurrent failure before conditional closure appears.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT8","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT8","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"CL_THN_09:MRT-B06","text":"No indispensable structural component resists reduction, but the exact phenomenological weighting of pride, tenderness, anger, common opinion, and intolerable oppression remains irreducibly qualitative.","refs":["CH_THN_SIMA:MRT4","CH_THN_SIMA:MRT6","CH_THN_PIOV:MRT6","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT8","CH_THN_SIGB:MRT9"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"CL","kicker":"Cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Chapters 25–27","context":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance · Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices · Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence","original_title":"Chapters 25–27","original_context":"Sect. IX Of The Measures Of Allegiance · Part III Of The Other Virtues And Vices · Sect. III Of Goodness And Benevolence"},"original_title":"Chapters 25–27","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01","layer":"MC","title":"Metacluster 1","order":1,"parents":["CL_THN_01","CL_THN_02","CL_THN_03"],"revision":"MC_THN_L1_01@R001","sha256":"ce989659be4ac9ad1897ddfe90147fabed53e9ef0d89ac9ae7a1a81cc9654342","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-01","text":"The governing problem is how a science confined to perceptual materials can explain mental productivity, ordered experience, factual assent, and practical reality without appealing to inaccessible essence or independently given forms.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BAS-01","CL_THN_02:BAS-01","CL_THN_03:ASP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-02","text":"The global thesis is anti-additive: simple content derives from impressions, while order, space, time, existence, and belief contribute no autonomous elemental content beyond the perceptions they organize or intensify.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-04","CL_THN_02:GA-10","CL_THN_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-03","text":"The architecture differentiates three irreducible explanatory dimensions: provenance determines available content, relation and order determine configuration, and force determines cognitive and practical efficacy.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-03","CL_THN_02:GA-02","CL_THN_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-04","text":"Sensation asymmetrically grounds ideas, but ideas return as productive relays within reflection, memory, imagination, association, belief, passion, and action.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-07","CL_THN_01:GA-08","CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-05","text":"Mental organization proceeds through preservation, release, recombination, associative weighting, and spatiotemporal arrangement rather than through either passive copying or sovereign creation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BAS-04","CL_THN_02:BAS-05","CL_THN_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-06","text":"Because existence adds no representational content, actuality is reconstructed as regulated vivacity transferred from present impressions through customary relations to absent or future ideas.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GA-02","CL_THN_03:GA-06","CL_THN_03:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-07","text":"The subject consequently becomes a relay and regulator within a distributed perception-machine, while reality becomes operationally available through stable effects rather than intrinsic representation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSD-03","CL_THN_02:HSI-03","CL_THN_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-08","text":"Anomalies and counterfeits do not terminate the machine; they recursively force universal claims to mutate into qualified regularities governed by provenance, steadiness, scope, and correction.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RLE-05","CL_THN_02:FL-05","CL_THN_03:RMD-07","CL_THN_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BAS-09","text":"The movement is spiral: empirical restriction → perceptual decomposition → recursive mediation → relational organization → ontological de-addition → intensive actualization → second-order correction → bounded closure at unknown origins and unrepresented reality.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BAS-08","CL_THN_02:BAS-07","CL_THN_03:ASP-05","CL_THN_03:ASP-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-01","text":"[Attractor] A science of human nature must reconstruct the perceptual medium through which evidence, inference, belief, and every other science operate.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-01","CL_THN_01:GA-12","CL_THN_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Legitimate inquiry proceeds from experienced perceptions, operations, relations, and effects rather than from speculative access to mental or external essence.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-02","CL_THN_02:BSD-07","CL_THN_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Simple ideational content is asymmetrically dependent on antecedent impression-material, even when later ideas become causally productive.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-04","CL_THN_01:GA-07","CL_THN_02:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-04","text":"[Emergent] Provenance, arrangement, and force constitute distinct explanatory coordinates: what an idea contains, how it is ordered, and how efficaciously it is conceived cannot be reduced to one another.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-03","CL_THN_02:GA-02","CL_THN_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] The idea is first a derivative copy, then a recombinable component, an associative node, and finally a force-bearing mediator of belief, passion, and action.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-07","CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:GA-07","CL_THN_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-06","text":"[Attractor] Mental production is asymmetrically recursive: sensory grounding remains one-way, while downstream perceptions repeatedly exchange the roles of effect, antecedent, and regulator.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-08","CL_THN_01:BSD-04","CL_THN_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-07","text":"[Emergent] Novelty is relational and intensive rather than elemental: new complexes, abstractions, expectations, and reality-effects arise without unprecedented simple content.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-04","CL_THN_02:XD-01","CL_THN_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-08","text":"[Mutating] Order changes from a preserved feature of experience into a manipulable arrangement, an associative pathway, and the constitutive form of spatial and temporal appearance.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-03","CL_THN_02:RMD-02","CL_THN_02:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-09","text":"[Emergent] Stable mental regularity is generated by associative tendency and custom, which constrain transitions without imposing demonstrative necessity or abolishing imaginative variation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-07","CL_THN_02:CGD-08","CL_THN_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-10","text":"[Persistent] Space and time are content-bearing orders of coexistence and succession, not independent impressions or empty containers.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-10","CL_THN_02:GA-14","CL_THN_02:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-11","text":"[Emergent] Existence contributes no additional represented quality; factuality must therefore be generated through a difference in the manner and force of conception.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GA-02","CL_THN_03:GA-03","CL_THN_03:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Force and vivacity develop from taxonomic marks into transmissible causal quantities, then into regulated quantities whose epistemic status depends on pedigree and steadiness.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RMD-08","CL_THN_03:GA-04","CL_THN_03:GA-08","CL_THN_03:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] A present impression anchors inference, supplies transferable vivacity, and gives absent or future objects current motivational efficacy.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GA-06","CL_THN_03:GA-07","CL_THN_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-14","text":"[Attractor] Practical reality is stable, regulated presence within the perception-system: an idea becomes real for the mind insofar as it persistently governs inference, passion, expectation, and conduct.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GA-10","CL_THN_03:BSD-01","CL_THN_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-15","text":"[Emergent] Epistemic legitimacy is genealogical and second-order: it requires admissible content, permissible relational application, and a force-producing history capable of surviving correction.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-12","CL_THN_02:BSD-05","CL_THN_03:GA-11","CL_THN_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GA-16","text":"[Terminal] Explanation terminates at maximally general experienced relations while sensory origins, associative attraction, external actuality, and the final authority of corrective rules remain undisclosed.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-10","CL_THN_02:GA-13","CL_THN_03:BSA-01","CL_THN_03:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Experience remains the admissible field from the initial science of human nature through compositional analysis and the genealogy of factual assent.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RMD-01","CL_THN_02:RLE-01","CL_THN_03:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-02","text":"[Functional recurrence] Observation mutates into perceptual classification, source-audit, compositional decomposition, tracking of associative effects, and diagnosis of force-producing pedigrees.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RMD-02","CL_THN_02:BSD-07","CL_THN_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-03","text":"[Escalation] The copy principle moves from a claim about simple ideas to a general constraint on imagination, abstraction, spatiotemporal predication, and existential representation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-04","CL_THN_02:RMD-01","CL_THN_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-04","text":"[Mutation] The idea changes from faint effect into reflective cause, recombinable component, associated transition-node, and practically efficacious object of belief.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RMD-05","CL_THN_02:RMD-04","CL_THN_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-05","text":"[Mutation] Order changes from a standard of mnemonic fidelity into the common machinery of recombination, association, extension, duration, and customary expectation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RMD-02","CL_THN_02:RLE-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-06","text":"[Domain migration] Association moves from a gentle relation among freely recombinable ideas into the customary mechanism that selects factual alternatives and transmits vivacity.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-07","CL_THN_02:RLE-04","CL_THN_03:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-07","text":"[Escalation and reversal] Vivacity progresses from perception-classification and mnemonic discrimination to belief-production, then reverses when poetry, passion, and madness show that intensity cannot warrant itself.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RMD-08","CL_THN_03:RMD-04","CL_THN_03:RMD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-08","text":"[Absorption] Existence disappears as an independent content-question after its non-addition becomes the constraint forcing factual difference into modal and causal explanation.","refs":["CL_THN_03:RMD-01","CL_THN_03:RMD-02","CL_THN_03:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-09","text":"[Reversal] Separability first authorizes imaginative liberty, then becomes a negative test excluding empty space, bare time, and other structures detached from every positive content.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RMD-03","CL_THN_02:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-10","text":"[Escalation] Causation migrates from temporal dependence and ideational association to productive capacity, social authority, force transmission, and the regulation of belief.","refs":["CL_THN_01:XD-08","CL_THN_02:RMD-07","CL_THN_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-11","text":"[Terminal transformation] Ignorance changes from a disciplinary deficiency into a constitutive boundary permitting operational explanation without essential, causal, or metaphysical completion.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RMD-08","CL_THN_02:GA-13","CL_THN_03:ASP-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RMD-12","text":"[Disappearance through absorption] Separate faculty names lose final explanatory status as memory, imagination, judgment, passion, and volition become differentiated operations within one distributed perception-system.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:RMD-09","CL_THN_03:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-01","text":"[Local] The initial diagnosis of philosophical controversy motivates the metascientific project but does not govern the later machinery of order, association, and belief.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-01","CL_THN_01:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-02","text":"[Local] The missing shade remains an exception to strict copy-priority rather than an alternative source of the current-level architecture.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-03","CL_THN_01:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-03","text":"[Local] Exact mnemonic vivacity, the sensory source of extension, and content-filled minima remain local criteria whose broader function is only to constrain unconstrained separation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:CGD-01","CL_THN_02:CGD-02","CL_THN_02:GA-14"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-04","text":"[Local] The exhaustiveness of the three associative relations and the extension of causation into social subordination are domain-specific claims, not universal axioms of the whole meta-cluster.","refs":["CL_THN_02:CGD-03","CL_THN_02:CGD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-05","text":"[Scales upward] The prohibition on essence-first explanation becomes the common boundary governing perceptions, faculties, space, time, existence, belief, and reality.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-04","CL_THN_02:BSD-07","CL_THN_03:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-06","text":"[Scales upward] The copy principle becomes a general genealogy of admissible content, while the distinction between content and operation prevents genealogy from collapsing into passive replay.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-05","CL_THN_02:CGD-04","CL_THN_02:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-07","text":"[Bridge] Ideas connect sensory derivation to reflection, memory, recombination, association, factual belief, and practical action.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-06","CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-08","text":"[Bridge] Association and custom connect relational order to intensive actuality by converting recurrent transitions into channels of transferred vivacity.","refs":["CL_THN_02:XD-02","CL_THN_02:RLE-04","CL_THN_03:GA-05","CL_THN_03:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-09","text":"[Catalyst] Poetry, eloquence, passion, and madness are local cases that scale only as stress tests forcing belief to mutate from raw vividness into regulated intensity.","refs":["CL_THN_03:CGD-02","CL_THN_03:CGD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:CGD-10","text":"[Superseded and recoded] Linear copy chronology, passive receptivity, sealed faculties, empty forms, and intrinsic actuality-markers are replaced by asymmetrical recursion, relational organization, and second-order genealogy.","refs":["CL_THN_01:CGD-07","CL_THN_02:CGD-08","CL_THN_03:CGD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-01","text":"Mental science is not deduction from a known substance, essence, or standpoint external to the faculties under investigation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GNX-01","CL_THN_01:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-02","text":"No operation of memory, imagination, abstraction, association, or belief can generally originate unprecedented simple content.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GNX-02","CL_THN_02:GNX-01","CL_THN_03:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-03","text":"Copy-dependence entails neither passive replay nor preservation of every original conjunction, order, intensity, or practical effect.","refs":["CL_THN_01:DBN-03","CL_THN_02:GNX-02","CL_THN_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-04","text":"Relations, order, space, and time are not independent substances or additional simple impressions superadded to their terms.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GNX-04","CL_THN_02:DBN-03","CL_THN_02:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-05","text":"Existence and belief are not predicates or representational components attached to an otherwise incomplete object-idea.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GNX-01","CL_THN_03:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-06","text":"Mental regularity is neither demonstrative necessity nor random succession; factual assent likewise cannot be generated by conceptual contradiction alone.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GNX-03","CL_THN_03:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-07","text":"No intrinsic semantic feature distinguishes an actual object from the same object merely conceived, and no positive representation discloses extra-perceptual being as such.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GNX-02","CL_THN_03:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-08","text":"Felt intensity, imaginative completeness, repetition, or rhetorical force cannot independently authenticate factual conviction.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GNX-06","CL_THN_03:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-09","text":"The subject is neither a sovereign creator nor an autonomous judge insulated from custom, association, affect, bodily condition, and recursive correction.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSA-02","CL_THN_03:DBN-05","CL_THN_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:GNX-10","text":"Scientific adequacy does not require access to ultimate sensory causes, the metaphysical basis of association, or the intrinsic nature of external actuality.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GNX-07","CL_THN_02:GNX-08","CL_THN_03:ASP-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any model in which cognition can ground itself from outside perceptual mediation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:DBN-01","CL_THN_03:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-02","text":"It excludes first-cause foundationalism: knowledge of stable derivative relations cannot depend on prior disclosure of their ultimate origin.","refs":["CL_THN_01:DBN-02","CL_THN_02:DBN-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-03","text":"It rejects both the passive receptacle and sovereign producer, requiring a mind that transforms inherited materials without becoming their absolute source.","refs":["CL_THN_01:DBN-03","CL_THN_02:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-04","text":"It disqualifies independent forms and empty frameworks: no positive order, space, time, relation, or generality survives the removal of all content-bearing terms.","refs":["CL_THN_02:DBN-03","CL_THN_02:DBN-04","CL_THN_02:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-05","text":"It excludes direct representational realism and any intrinsic actuality-sign capable of transporting thought beyond perceptual content into the essence of external being.","refs":["CL_THN_03:DBN-01","CL_THN_03:DBN-02","CL_THN_03:BSA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-06","text":"It excludes rationalist factuality: conceivability, contradiction, and necessary connection cannot select among contrary matters of fact.","refs":["CL_THN_02:DBN-06","CL_THN_03:DBN-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-07","text":"It cannot identify causal genesis with epistemic warrant, because the same associative and intensive mechanisms can produce ordinary belief, education, persuasion, fiction, and pathology.","refs":["CL_THN_03:XD-06","CL_THN_03:XD-10","CL_THN_03:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:DBN-08","text":"It cannot admit an independent tribunal of correction without violating perceptual closure, yet it cannot abandon correction without collapsing factual belief into successful vivification.","refs":["CL_THN_03:GA-11","CL_THN_03:RLE-07","CL_THN_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-01","text":"The first governing absence is absolute sensory origin: sensation grounds the entire machine while the cause of its first appearance remains outside it.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSA-01","CL_THN_01:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-02","text":"The second governing absence is pure order without ordered content: relation, empty space, bare time, and wholly indeterminate generality are inadmissible.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSA-01","CL_THN_02:DBN-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-03","text":"The third governing absence is a necessary mental bond: association and custom occupy its functional place while remaining defeasible tendencies.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSA-03","CL_THN_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-04","text":"The deepest ontological absence is positively knowable mind-independent actuality; reality persists as correlate, anchor, and corrective term without becoming represented essence.","refs":["CL_THN_03:BSA-01","CL_THN_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-05","text":"The absent subject is a sovereign spontaneous origin capable of creating content, governing transitions, and certifying its own beliefs without inherited impressions or natural tendencies.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSA-05","CL_THN_02:BSA-02","CL_THN_03:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSA-06","text":"The terminal absence is a noncircular authority for general rules: correction must regulate force from within the same customary perception-system whose products it evaluates.","refs":["CL_THN_03:BSA-04","CL_THN_03:FL-04","CL_THN_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-01","text":"Restriction of primitive content produces rather than suppresses mental productivity: impression-derived elements become recursively recombinable, associatively routable, and intensively variable.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-05","CL_THN_02:BSD-04","CL_THN_03:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-02","text":"The full architecture requires a three-axis audit: genealogy tests content, relational analysis tests configuration and application, and force-pedigree tests factual assent.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-03","CL_THN_02:BSD-05","CL_THN_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-03","text":"Space, time, existence, and belief obey one de-additive rule: apparent higher-order structures are reconstructed as arrangements or modes of perception rather than additional ingredients.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-10","CL_THN_02:GNX-04","CL_THN_03:GA-02","CL_THN_03:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-04","text":"Ideas are the machine’s central switching elements because they preserve sensory derivation while serving as causes of reflection, units of recombination, nodes of association, and bearers of practical reality.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-07","CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:GA-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-05","text":"Association becomes the bridge between formal order and factuality: recurrent relations first stabilize imaginative transitions and then channel vivacity from present impressions into belief.","refs":["CL_THN_02:XD-02","CL_THN_02:RLE-04","CL_THN_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-06","text":"Time has a dual generated structure: succession produces duration, while present impressions project force across succession so that remembered, absent, and future objects act now.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-03","CL_THN_02:HSI-04","CL_THN_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-07","text":"Fiction divides along two independent axes: a complex may be compositionally legitimate because its materials are admissible yet doxastically counterfeit because its vivacity lacks regulated factual pedigree.","refs":["CL_THN_02:XD-07","CL_THN_02:CGD-09","CL_THN_03:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-08","text":"Epistemic reading must become recursively genealogical, tracing not only where an idea’s content originated but also how its arrangement and degree of conviction were produced.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RLE-01","CL_THN_03:MME-07","CL_THN_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-09","text":"The system yields practical realism without positive metaphysical realism: perceptually generated ideas can organize conduct as realities despite lacking an intrinsic representation of independent actuality.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-10","CL_THN_02:BSD-08","CL_THN_03:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-10","text":"Power operates through two linked channels: productive capacity associates authority and obedience, while rhetoric, repetition, and affect modulate the vivacity by which such relations become practically compelling.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-06","CL_THN_03:BSD-06","CL_THN_03:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-11","text":"The system’s principal operator is also its principal debt: causation establishes perceptual dependence, associative priority, social power, force transfer, and corrective pedigree before its own nature is secured.","refs":["CL_THN_01:XD-08","CL_THN_02:FL-07","CL_THN_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:XD-12","text":"Because corrective rules arise within the same associative field they regulate, epistemic security can only be reflexive, comparative, and qualified rather than absolutely foundational.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSD-02","CL_THN_02:GA-13","CL_THN_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-01","text":"The meta-cluster produces an anti-additive ontology: apparent mental and formal entities are reconstructed as sourced contents, relational configurations, or intensive modes.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MME-03","CL_THN_02:BSD-01","CL_THN_03:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-02","text":"Mind becomes a distributed transformation machine rather than a substance or faculty hierarchy: it receives, copies, preserves, separates, routes, intensifies, and regulates perceptions.","refs":["CL_THN_01:HSI-02","CL_THN_02:HSI-02","CL_THN_03:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-03","text":"Actuality becomes a system-effect generated when admissible content is placed within a stable associative route and supplied with regulated practical force.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-05","CL_THN_03:GA-10","CL_THN_03:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-04","text":"The subject is decentered twice: it is neither the source of primitive content nor the sovereign author of the order and force by which its ideas govern it.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSD-03","CL_THN_02:BSD-02","CL_THN_03:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-05","text":"Time is neither an empty continuum nor simple chronology; it is generated succession recursively reorganized by memory, expectation, custom, and present force-transfer.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSD-04","CL_THN_02:BSD-03","CL_THN_03:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-06","text":"Agency becomes navigational: freedom consists in recombination, selective attention, rerouting, and reflective correction within constraints imposed by content, association, and affect.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MME-04","CL_THN_02:HSI-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-07","text":"Scientific foundation is converted into recursive self-audit: secure inquiry tracks sources, relations, force-transfers, anomalies, and corrections while refusing claims to absolute origin.","refs":["CL_THN_01:BSD-01","CL_THN_02:BSD-07","CL_THN_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-08","text":"Political and rhetorical vulnerability is structural rather than accidental because the same relational and intensive machinery that enables authority, expectation, and action also permits capture by repetition, eloquence, and passion.","refs":["CL_THN_02:BSD-06","CL_THN_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:BSD-09","text":"The machine generates qualified naturalism: explanatory closure belongs to recurrent operations and effects, while metaphysical closure is systematically withheld.","refs":["CL_THN_01:GA-10","CL_THN_02:GA-13","CL_THN_03:ASP-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-01","text":"Total production loop: sensation → idea → reflection → memory or imagination → associative routing → belief → passion and action → renewed vivification or reflective correction.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RLE-02","CL_THN_02:RLE-02","CL_THN_03:RLE-02","CL_THN_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-02","text":"Restriction–proliferation loop: simple content is constrained → complexes are recombined → relations stabilize transitions → force actualizes selected complexes → new reflective effects return to the system.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RLE-03","CL_THN_02:FGS-B06","CL_THN_03:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-03","text":"Genealogical audit loop: impression-source → permissible operation → application test → force-pedigree → correction → renewed audit of the resulting perception.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RLE-01","CL_THN_03:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-04","text":"Order loop: experienced arrangement → mnemonic conservation → imaginative release → associative regeneration → spatiotemporal constitution → customary expectation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RLE-02","CL_THN_02:RLE-04","CL_THN_02:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-05","text":"Actualization loop: present impression → customary transition → transferred vivacity → factual belief → practical efficacy → passion → amplified object-idea.","refs":["CL_THN_03:RLE-02","CL_THN_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-06","text":"Counterfeit-correction loop: nonstandard vivification → belief-like force → error or capture → source-reflection → general-rule discounting → graded restoration of conviction.","refs":["CL_THN_03:RLE-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-07","text":"Boundary loop: inquiry reaches inaccessible origin or reality → withdraws to observable effects → formulates a qualified regularity → encounters anomaly → restricts scope without abandoning the machine.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RLE-04","CL_THN_01:RLE-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:RLE-08","text":"Attractor convergence: ideas organize recursive production, order organizes relational form, regulated vivacity organizes actuality, and inaccessible reality remains the recessed term around which correction circulates.","refs":["CL_THN_01:RLE-06","CL_THN_02:RLE-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-08","CL_THN_03:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B01","text":"Scientific dependence on human faculties → human nature installed as the metascientific field.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FGS-B01","CL_THN_01:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B02","text":"Unavailable essence and ultimate origin → experienced perceptions, operations, relations, and effects become the admissible evidence.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FGS-B03","CL_THN_01:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B03","text":"Perceptual decomposition → impressions/ideas + simple/complex → isolable units of provenance and operation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FGS-B04","CL_THN_01:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B04","text":"Sensory grounding + ideational return → asymmetrically recursive perception-chain.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FGS-B06","CL_THN_01:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B05","text":"Recurrence differentiated by force and fidelity → memory preserves experienced order while imagination releases components for recombination.","refs":["CL_THN_02:FGS-B02","CL_THN_02:FGS-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B06","text":"Free recombination + recurrent regularity → resemblance, contiguity, and causation weight transitions without necessitating them.","refs":["CL_THN_02:FGS-B04","CL_THN_02:FGS-B05","CL_THN_02:FGS-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B07","text":"Analysis of organized content → coexistence and succession reconstructed as extension and duration rather than empty forms.","refs":["CL_THN_02:FGS-B07","CL_THN_02:FGS-B09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B08","text":"Existential non-addition + content invariance → factual difference relocated from representation to manner of conception.","refs":["CL_THN_03:FGS-B01","CL_THN_03:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B09","text":"Present impression + customary relation → transferred vivacity → steady intensified idea → belief → passion and action.","refs":["CL_THN_03:FGS-B03","CL_THN_03:FGS-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FGS-B10","text":"Affective amplification or counterfeit vivification → source-sensitive reflection and general rules → qualified correction without metaphysical closure.","refs":["CL_THN_03:FGS-B05","CL_THN_03:FGS-B06","CL_THN_03:FGS-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B01","text":"Foundational disorder → human faculties identified as the common medium of scientific judgment.","refs":["CL_THN_01:AFM-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B02","text":"Human mediation → empirical restriction → mental life decomposed into perceptions and genealogical dependencies.","refs":["CL_THN_01:AFM-B02","CL_THN_01:AFM-B03","CL_THN_01:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B03","text":"Linear copy genealogy → division of impressions → ideas become recursively productive mediators.","refs":["CL_THN_01:AFM-B05","CL_THN_01:AFM-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B04","text":"Productive ideas → memory/imagination bifurcation → separation and recombination of inherited contents.","refs":["CL_THN_02:AFM-B02","CL_THN_02:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B05","text":"Free recombination → associative regulation → stable but defeasible relational pathways.","refs":["CL_THN_02:AFM-B04","CL_THN_02:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B06","text":"Relational analysis → space and time reconstructed as content-bearing coexistence and succession.","refs":["CL_THN_02:AFM-B06","CL_THN_02:AFM-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B07","text":"Existential non-addition → belief-surplus problem → customary force-transfer → practical actualization.","refs":["CL_THN_03:AFM-B01","CL_THN_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:AFM-B08","text":"Counterfeit vivacity → second-order genealogy → regulated conviction → unresolved return to unrepresented reality.","refs":["CL_THN_03:RLE-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-06","CL_THN_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-01","text":"Foundation is reclassified from possession of first causes into disciplined reconstruction of perceptual provenance, organization, and force.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MME-02","CL_THN_02:MME-08","CL_THN_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-02","text":"Mind is remapped from substance and faculty hierarchy into a recursive field of perceptions differentiated by source, order, transition, and intensity.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MME-03","CL_THN_02:MME-01","CL_THN_03:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-03","text":"Content, relation, and force become separable analytic dimensions, allowing identical materials to support memory, fiction, abstraction, belief, or practical action.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MME-02","CL_THN_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-04","text":"The opposition between receptivity and productivity collapses into asymmetric recursion, while the opposition between freedom and determination collapses into bounded, tendency-weighted navigation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MME-04","CL_THN_01:MME-05","CL_THN_02:MME-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-05","text":"Space, time, existence, and belief are removed from the category of independent additions and redistributed among arrangement, succession, manner, and force.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MME-05","CL_THN_03:MME-01","CL_THN_03:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-06","text":"Fact and fiction become neighboring configurations differentiated not by elemental content but by composition, associative route, steadiness, provenance, and practical efficacy.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MME-06","CL_THN_03:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-07","text":"Causation becomes a cross-domain operator spanning perceptual genealogy, associative transition, productive capacity, social authority, belief-generation, and affective feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_01:XD-08","CL_THN_02:MME-07","CL_THN_03:GA-06","CL_THN_03:RLE-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MME-08","text":"Reading becomes operational and second-order: apparent entities must be decomposed into sources, configurations, force-transfers, exclusions, and corrective histories.","refs":["CL_THN_02:ALT-B05","CL_THN_03:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:ALT-B01","text":"[Surface] The explicit field comprises human nature, impressions, ideas, memory, imagination, association, space, time, existence, belief, passion, fiction, and factual judgment.","refs":["CL_THN_01:ALT-B01","CL_THN_02:ALT-B01","CL_THN_03:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:ALT-B02","text":"[Structural] Copying, recurrence, separation, recombination, associative routing, ordered coexistence and succession, force-transfer, affective feedback, and correction form one distributed mechanism.","refs":["CL_THN_01:ALT-B02","CL_THN_02:ALT-B02","CL_THN_03:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:ALT-B03","text":"[Metastrategic] Metaphysical disputes are converted into audits of perceptual origin, relational operation, permissible application, causal pedigree, and susceptibility to correction.","refs":["CL_THN_01:ALT-B03","CL_THN_02:ALT-B03","CL_THN_03:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:ALT-B04","text":"[Ontological / Temporal] Reality available to thought consists of content-bearing events and configurations; time is generated succession recursively reorganized by memory, custom, expectation, and present practical force.","refs":["CL_THN_01:ALT-B04","CL_THN_02:ALT-B04","CL_THN_03:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:ALT-B05","text":"[Methodological / Performative] The system performs its claims by decomposing apparent unities, denying unsupported additions, reconstructing operational relations, testing them against anomalies, and replacing universality with qualified recursive regulation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:ALT-B05","CL_THN_02:ALT-B05","CL_THN_03:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-01","text":"Causal succession, associative efficacy, force-transfer, and causal pedigree do indispensable explanatory work before causation itself has received a secured analysis.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FL-02","CL_THN_02:FL-07","CL_THN_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-02","text":"The copy principle strains against the missing shade, abstraction’s selective suppression of detail, and the use of separability to deny forms that cannot be detached from every content-bearing instance.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FL-03","CL_THN_02:FL-01","CL_THN_02:FL-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-03","text":"The declared observational science of ordinary conduct remains incompletely integrated with introspective taxonomy, deprivation tests, and phenomenological judgments of force.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FL-01","CL_THN_02:CGD-01","CL_THN_03:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-04","text":"Association is both an original tendency and a customary acquisition, leaving unresolved how innate relational susceptibility and learned pathways divide explanatory labor.","refs":["CL_THN_02:FL-06","CL_THN_03:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-05","text":"Perceptual closure denies positive access to external actuality, yet sensory causation, reality-connection, and correction continue to invoke it as an operative constraint.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FL-04","CL_THN_03:FL-02","CL_THN_03:XD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-06","text":"Force and vivacity classify impressions, memory, imagination, belief, passion, and counterfeit conviction without a stable metric or exact threshold across these functions.","refs":["CL_THN_02:FL-04","CL_THN_03:FL-03","CL_THN_03:MRT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-07","text":"Custom generates ordinary factual assent, but education, repetition, rhetoric, and pathology can use the same mechanism to generate invalid assent; causal genesis and warrant remain only partially separated.","refs":["CL_THN_03:XD-10","CL_THN_03:FL-05","CL_THN_03:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-08","text":"General rules correct first-order force but lack an independently specified origin and authority, risking circular dependence on the beliefs and customs they are meant to regulate.","refs":["CL_THN_03:BSA-04","CL_THN_03:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:FL-09","text":"The science intended to secure all inquiry remains reflexive because it uses perception, comparison, causal inference, custom, and correction to establish the authority of those same operations.","refs":["CL_THN_01:FL-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-01","text":"Idea mutates from derivative copy into the common relay connecting sensation, reflection, recombination, order, association, belief, and action.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MP-02","CL_THN_02:RMD-04","CL_THN_03:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-02","text":"Order mutates from retained arrangement into a productive relation, spatiotemporal form, customary route, and condition of expectation.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MP-01","CL_THN_02:RLE-04","CL_THN_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-03","text":"Association mutates from a nonnecessary bond among ideas into the causal channel through which present experience selects factual alternatives and transfers vivacity.","refs":["CL_THN_02:GA-07","CL_THN_03:RMD-05","CL_THN_03:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-04","text":"Force mutates from a mark of perception-type into a transmissible psychological currency and then into a regulated quantity whose source matters more than its raw magnitude.","refs":["CL_THN_02:RMD-08","CL_THN_03:MP-02","CL_THN_03:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-05","text":"Existence mutates from an explicit ontological object into a zero-addition rule, while reality mutates into a causal and corrective relation governing belief.","refs":["CL_THN_03:MP-01","CL_THN_03:MP-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MP-06","text":"Fiction mutates from lawful recombination into genealogical or doxastic excess when either its application outruns perceptual order or its vivacity imitates factual authority.","refs":["CL_THN_02:MP-04","CL_THN_03:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:HSI-01","text":"Reality is imaged as an asymmetrically grounded network of content-bearing events whose copied elements circulate through recursive circuits of order, association, and force.","refs":["CL_THN_01:HSI-01","CL_THN_02:HSI-01","CL_THN_03:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:HSI-02","text":"The mind is an archive-workshop-routing system: it preserves sequences, cuts and recombines components, weights pathways, transfers intensity, and applies corrective feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_02:HSI-02","CL_THN_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:HSI-03","text":"The subject is a switching relay and regulatory threshold rather than an originating substance; natural tendencies, habits, passions, bodily states, and reflection compete to direct its ideas.","refs":["CL_THN_01:HSI-02","CL_THN_02:HSI-03","CL_THN_03:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:HSI-04","text":"Time is a pulse of content-bearing succession folded by memory and expectation, with present impressions drawing absent and future objects into current causal efficacy.","refs":["CL_THN_02:HSI-04","CL_THN_03:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:HSI-05","text":"Navigation consists in tracing provenance, decomposing arrangements, following associative routes, measuring force by pedigree, correcting counterfeits, and stopping where perceptual relations no longer support explanation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:HSI-04","CL_THN_02:HSI-05","CL_THN_03:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B01","text":"The current-level axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the engine if provenance, relational order, and intensive force remain distinct but recursively connected dimensions.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B01","CL_THN_02:MRT-B01","CL_THN_03:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B02","text":"The strongest architecture is the conversion of restricted perceptual content into relationally productive and practically efficacious ideas without granting autonomous elemental creation.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B04","CL_THN_02:MRT-B02","CL_THN_03:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at the operational level provided genetic priority, relational configuration, intensive manner, causal genesis, and epistemic warrant are not collapsed into one order.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B03","CL_THN_02:MRT-B01","CL_THN_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B04","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined at the mechanisms of ideational return, associative attraction, vivacity transfer, exact thresholds of belief, and the authority of corrective rules.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B02","CL_THN_02:MRT-B03","CL_THN_03:MRT-B02","CL_THN_03:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only through sequencing and recursive return: sensory grounding must precede recombination, recombination must generate association, and existential non-addition must precede intensive belief.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B05","CL_THN_02:MRT-B04","CL_THN_03:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B06","text":"The indispensable residues resistant to axiomatic reduction are sensory origin, the lived quality of force, mind-independent actuality, causal necessity, and the noncircular basis of correction.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B06","CL_THN_02:MRT-B05","CL_THN_03:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_01:MRT-B07","text":"The minimal reconstruction succeeds as a bounded naturalistic machine, not as metaphysical closure: it explains how perceptions generate order and reality-effects while preserving the absences that make such explanation necessary.","refs":["CL_THN_01:MRT-B07","CL_THN_02:MRT-B06","CL_THN_03:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Metacluster 1","context":"Chapters 01–09 · Introduction · Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas · Sect. II. Division Of The Subject · Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination · Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas · Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time · Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence · Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief · Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief","original_title":"Metacluster 1","original_context":"Chapters 01–09 · Introduction · Sect. I. Of The Origin Of Our Ideas · Sect. II. Division Of The Subject · Sect. III. Of The Ideas Of The Memory And Imagination · Sect. IV. Of The Connexion Or Association Of Ideas · Sect. III. Of The Other Qualities Of Our Idea Of Space And Time · Sect. VI. Of The Idea Of Existence, And Of External Existence · Sect. VII. Of The Nature Of The Idea Or Belief · Sect. X. Of The Influence Of Belief"},"original_title":"Metacluster 1","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02","layer":"MC","title":"Metacluster 2","order":2,"parents":["CL_THN_04","CL_THN_05","CL_THN_06"],"revision":"MC_THN_L1_02@R001","sha256":"aa5da733b687a1022c82028bf6ea022e40c74e9d6d1eb59fa14aeb787af6561b","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B01","text":"The central problem is how the same natural mechanisms can generate warranted causal judgment, metaphysical fiction, operational selfhood, affective valuation, and social hierarchy without access to intrinsic powers or an external rational tribunal.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AS-B01","CL_THN_05:AS-B01","CL_THN_06:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B02","text":"The governing thesis is that relation-conditioned transitions become effective through force, vivacity, repetition, and retention; their outputs depend on how comparison, custom, circumstance, and social topology route them.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AS-B02","CL_THN_05:AS-B05","CL_THN_06:BAS-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B03","text":"At this scale the argument becomes a recursive naturalistic reconstruction: substantial bearers, intrinsic powers, autonomous faculties, and primitive passions are displaced by networks of perceptions, affects, transitions, gates, and feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AS-B03","CL_THN_05:AS-B02","CL_THN_06:BAS-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B04","text":"The directional movement is foundational subtraction → operational transition → productive overextension → anomaly or contradiction → restrictive differentiation → socially recursive application.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AS-B04","CL_THN_05:AS-B04","CL_THN_06:BAS-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B05","text":"Comparison becomes the principal branching operator: controlled difference isolates causal circumstances, while retained contrast converts another’s condition into an altered estimate of the self.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-06","CL_THN_05:GA-04","CL_THN_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B06","text":"The social-affective field is not an external application of the cognitive system; it exposes how vivacity, authority, custom, and relation recursively produce the objects, values, and judgments they appear merely to register.","refs":["CL_THN_05:BSD-07","CL_THN_06:BSD-01","CL_THN_06:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B07","text":"The subject accordingly mutates from a rejected substantial bearer into a temporally variable relay, represented object, comparator, and feedback-node within cognitive and social processes.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-03","CL_THN_05:BSD-02","CL_THN_06:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AS-B08","text":"Closure is operational and recursively skeptical: inquiry preserves tested mechanisms while denying final decomposition, intrinsic value, affective neutrality, and any standpoint outside the operations under examination.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AS-B08","CL_THN_05:AS-B08","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Legitimate explanation begins from perceptions, affects, experienced conjunctions, and observable transformations rather than from unknown substances or intrinsic productive powers.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-02","CL_THN_05:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-02","text":"[Persistent] Relation is causally effective only through the transitions it facilitates, preserves, interrupts, or redirects; it is not an inert predicate or an intrinsically binding nexus.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-01","CL_THN_05:GA-04","CL_THN_06:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-03","text":"[Mutating] Force and vivacity migrate from determinants of conviction into the general currency by which cognitive, affective, and social representations become operative.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-04","text":"[Persistent] Repetition, habit, and custom stabilize transitions without converting psychological regularity, practical necessity, or social circulation into truth.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-04","CL_THN_05:GA-05","CL_THN_05:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-05","text":"[Attractor] Imagination is the indispensable medium of synthesis and the endogenous source of fictive completion, overextension, constructed sentiment, and self-confirming error.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-03","CL_THN_05:GA-03","CL_THN_06:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-06","text":"[Mutating] Comparison changes from controlled experimental differentiation into affective opposition, retaining the common operation of preserving difference while reversing its epistemic function.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-06","CL_THN_05:GA-04","CL_THN_06:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-07","text":"[Mutating] Causation expands from regular conjunction among discriminated circumstances into gated conversion and recursive feedback in which effects alter subsequent causes, judgments, and relations.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-08","CL_THN_05:BSD-03","CL_THN_06:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-08","text":"[Emergent] Anomalies and contradictions reveal unrestricted principles by their excesses and force their conversion into range-bound, threshold-dependent mechanisms.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-10","CL_THN_05:GA-09","CL_THN_06:CGD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-09","text":"[Attractor] Normative correction is immanent: broader comparison, discriminating variation, skeptical restraint, and recursive audit regulate the same natural operations that generate error.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-07","CL_THN_05:GA-10","CL_THN_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-10","text":"[Mutating] The self has no warranted status as a substantial origin but remains operative as a vivid relay, represented object, temporal comparator, and socially altered magnitude.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-05","CL_THN_05:GA-11","CL_THN_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-11","text":"[Persistent] Natural production and felt efficacy do not establish representational truth, epistemic warrant, intrinsic value, or source-fidelity.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:GA-12","CL_THN_06:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-12","text":"[Emergent] Social value is generated in circulation: transmitted satisfaction, reflected judgment, comparative opposition, and recursive uptake constitute rank and self-estimation.","refs":["CL_THN_05:MME-06","CL_THN_06:BSD-03","CL_THN_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-13","text":"[Emergent] Relational proximity and intensity possess no intrinsic valence; they amplify whichever epistemic, sympathetic, or comparative transformation controls the route.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-05","CL_THN_05:GA-04","CL_THN_06:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-14","text":"[Emergent] Time is an active operator of projection, attenuation, retention, durable attribution, and feedback rather than a neutral container for stable objects and subjects.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-05","CL_THN_05:BSD-04","CL_THN_06:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GA-15","text":"[Terminal] The viable endpoint is a reflexive science of human nature that preserves empirically useful causal and affective mechanisms under permanent semantic, experimental, skeptical, and relational correction.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-14","CL_THN_05:GA-13","CL_THN_06:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-01","text":"[Stable Identity] Experienced conjunction remains the minimum causal input, although its output expands from expectation to bodily influence, affective conversion, and social feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-01","CL_THN_05:RMD-07","CL_THN_06:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-02","text":"[Escalation] Vivacity moves from explaining unequal conviction to animating belief and passion, then becomes the transmissible currency of a social-affective network.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-09","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-03","text":"[Mutation] Imagination changes from a producer of causal expectation and fictive object-unity into the general medium of cognition, then into a simulator capable of constructing another’s absent emotion.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-03","CL_THN_05:RMD-02","CL_THN_06:RMD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-04","text":"[Functional Recurrence] Custom recurs as first-order projection, second-order regulation, affective stabilization, and constructive expectation, with renewed overextension at every phase.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-03","CL_THN_05:RMD-04","CL_THN_06:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-05","text":"[Reversal] Comparison changes from a corrective instrument that exposes suppressed differences into a social operator that preserves difference as inverse self-estimation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-05","CL_THN_05:RMD-03","CL_THN_06:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-06","text":"[Migration] Relation migrates from conjunction, separability, and spatial compatibility into cognitive gating, self-reference, social proximity, directed transmission, and comparative interaction.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-01","CL_THN_05:RMD-03","CL_THN_06:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-07","text":"[Terminal Transformation] The self moves from an absent invariant bearer to an operational object and finally to a socially constituted relay-comparator whose states are generated through feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-05","CL_THN_05:RMD-05","CL_THN_06:RMD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-08","text":"[Escalation] Cause shifts from whole-object identity to operative circumstance, then to domain-specific conversion, and finally to nonlinear circuits in which generated passions modify their own conditions.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-08","CL_THN_05:BSD-03","CL_THN_06:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-09","text":"[Mutation] Contradiction changes from a stimulus for metaphysical repair into a diagnostic of unrestricted transition and then into a controlled anomaly for reconstructing social-affective routing.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-08","CL_THN_05:RMD-06","CL_THN_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RMD-10","text":"[Disappearance] Substance, autonomous reason, intrinsic value, primitive social instinct, and sovereign selfhood disappear after their explanatory work is redistributed across relation, force, custom, comparison, and feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-10","CL_THN_05:CGD-07","CL_THN_06:RMD-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-01","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] Particular rules for isolating causal circumstances remain local experimental instruments; their scalable function is recursive differentiation under counterinstance.","refs":["CL_THN_04:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-02","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] Spatial compatibility, soul-substance, immortality, and the exact gates of pride and humility remain local problems rather than current-level explanatory centers.","refs":["CL_THN_05:CGD-01","CL_THN_05:CGD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-03","text":"[Lower-Layer Local] Fame, wealth-esteem, pity, envy, malice, and the constitutional conjunctions of love or hatred remain local outputs of the broader relational machine.","refs":["CL_THN_06:CGD-01","CL_THN_06:CGD-03","CL_THN_06:CGD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-04","text":"[Scaled] Relation-mediated force scales upward as the common architecture connecting belief, causal inference, affective conversion, and social valuation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-01","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-05","text":"[Bridge] Experimental circumstance-analysis and affective threshold-gating jointly establish that causal mechanisms are individuated by controlled differences in routing, not by whole-object classification.","refs":["CL_THN_04:CGD-08","CL_THN_05:MP-06","CL_THN_06:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] The operational self of the skeptical and affective analyses becomes the relay-comparator required by social transmission and reflected valuation.","refs":["CL_THN_05:CGD-08","CL_THN_06:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-07","text":"[Catalyst] Social anomalies demonstrate that unchanged content or objective condition can generate opposed effects, forcing causation to include sequence, topology, retention, and mode selection.","refs":["CL_THN_05:GA-08","CL_THN_06:CGD-08","CL_THN_06:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-08","text":"[Recoded] Causal identity is recoded from an intrinsic power or whole object into a reproducible transformation distributed across input, relation, force, gate, and feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:CGD-08","CL_THN_05:MME-04","CL_THN_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:CGD-09","text":"[Current-Level Emergent] Philosophy becomes a reflexive regulation of cognitive and social-affective machinery rather than a separate faculty judging nature from outside.","refs":["CL_THN_04:CGD-10","CL_THN_05:GA-13","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-01","text":"No substantial bearer, intrinsic causal power, or affective essence is required where relations and transformations among perceptions can perform the explanatory work.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GNX-02","CL_THN_05:GNX-01","CL_THN_06:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-02","text":"No autonomous reason, sovereign self, or neutral tribunal can validate cognition and value independently of imagination, habit, affect, and social relation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GNX-04","CL_THN_05:GNX-02","CL_THN_06:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-03","text":"Relation is not static, symmetric, intrinsically efficacious, or valence-bearing; its force depends on direction, continuity, comparison, competition, and mediation.","refs":["CL_THN_05:GNX-03","CL_THN_06:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-04","text":"Stable content, representation, or objective condition does not guarantee stable belief, judgment, passion, or value.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GNX-03","CL_THN_05:GNX-05","CL_THN_06:GNX-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-05","text":"Imagination and custom can neither be granted unrestricted authority nor eliminated, because both successful operation and systematic error depend on them.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GNX-08","CL_THN_05:GNX-04","CL_THN_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-06","text":"No unrestricted explanatory principle survives: causal uniformity, pure understanding, universal relation, and primitive passion-laws all require domain, threshold, or circumstance restrictions.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GNX-07","CL_THN_05:GNX-06","CL_THN_06:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-07","text":"Neither felt conviction nor sympathetic uptake guarantees warrant, truth, intrinsic worth, or faithful access to another’s interior state.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:GNX-05","CL_THN_06:GNX-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:GNX-08","text":"No final correction abolishes custom, closes causal decomposition, fixes self-worth, or establishes a nonparticipatory position outside the relational system.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-03","CL_THN_05:BSA-04","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-01","text":"The architecture requires the falsity of any model that assigns cognition, passion, and social valuation to autonomous faculties governed by independent laws.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-01","CL_THN_05:MME-03","CL_THN_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-02","text":"It requires an external standpoint to be unavailable: correction must occur through reorganized experience, comparison, custom, and relation rather than through an Archimedean criterion.","refs":["CL_THN_04:DBN-03","CL_THN_05:BSA-02","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-03","text":"It excludes purely linear causality because belief reinforces repetition, passion recruits judgment, esteem amplifies satisfaction, and altered relations become new causes.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-01","CL_THN_05:RLE-02","CL_THN_06:DBN-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-04","text":"It cannot admit an invariant subject whose identity, rationality, and worth remain insulated from temporal force, remembered relation, testimony, and comparison.","refs":["CL_THN_04:DBN-06","CL_THN_05:DBN-06","CL_THN_06:DBN-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-05","text":"It excludes universal commensurability: epistemic warrant, conviction, pleasure, welfare, public esteem, and comparative self-value cannot be reduced to one scale.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-06","CL_THN_05:MME-06","CL_THN_06:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-06","text":"It also excludes absolute irregularity, because recursive correction requires provisionally stable kinds, repeatable transitions, retained impressions, and reproducible relational effects.","refs":["CL_THN_04:DBN-02","CL_THN_05:GA-05","CL_THN_06:RLE-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:DBN-07","text":"It cannot admit an affect-free methodology, because evidential uptake, inquiry, skeptical recovery, authority, and correction remain operations of force within human nature.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:CGD-03","CL_THN_06:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Deep Book Apophatics"},{"number":"7","name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-01","text":"The system is organized around an absent metaphysical interior: neither causal power, substantial self, nor another person’s authentic feeling is directly available as an explanatory ground.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-01","CL_THN_05:BSA-05","CL_THN_06:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-02","text":"The deepest unavailable possibility is a common metric capable of aligning truth, felt conviction, welfare, social esteem, and self-worth.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-06","CL_THN_05:BSA-01","CL_THN_06:BSA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-03","text":"No view from outside human nature can determine the legitimacy of custom, imagination, sympathy, or comparison without using those same operations.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-02","CL_THN_05:BSA-02","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-04","text":"No final alignment joins what nature produces to what is true or good; natural belief, practical stability, social value, and affective intensity can all diverge from warrant and welfare.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:BSA-03","CL_THN_06:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-05","text":"The architecture lacks a general metric for mode selection and activation: it cannot finally specify when custom becomes rule, relation becomes force, or sympathy yields to comparison.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-06","CL_THN_05:MRT-B02","CL_THN_06:BSA-02","CL_THN_06:BSA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSA-06","text":"Final repose is inadmissible because every stabilization can become a new habit, fiction, hierarchy, or compound circumstance requiring renewed differentiation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-04","CL_THN_05:BSA-04","CL_THN_06:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Super-Apophatics of the Whole"},{"number":"8","name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-01","text":"Causal judgment and social passion are two scales of one natural engine: relation organizes transition, force makes it operative, and gating determines whether the result becomes inference, fiction, attachment, or comparison.","refs":["CL_THN_04:XD-01","CL_THN_05:XD-01","CL_THN_06:XD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-02","text":"Comparison bifurcates normatively: controlled difference corrects causal projection, whereas retained contrast can intensify envy or self-elevation without improving truth-tracking.","refs":["CL_THN_04:XD-02","CL_THN_06:XD-03","CL_THN_06:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-03","text":"The distinction between warrant and conviction extends into the social field: authority, praise, familiarity, and reflected esteem can amplify assent or self-belief independently of evidential regularity.","refs":["CL_THN_04:XD-05","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:BSD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-04","text":"Once the substantial self is removed, cognitive and social analyses converge on a subject constituted by incoming force, retained relations, comparative positioning, and recursive self-attribution.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-03","CL_THN_05:XD-05","CL_THN_06:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-05","text":"Constructive sympathy extends the theory of endogenous error: a representation can generate a real affective consequence even when it does not faithfully reproduce its alleged source.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-03","CL_THN_05:XD-03","CL_THN_06:XD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-06","text":"General rules are simultaneously corrective and productive: they can restrain prejudicial inference, stabilize inquiry, or construct expected passions that reinforce the social model from which they arose.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-06","CL_THN_05:RMD-04","CL_THN_06:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-07","text":"Causal explanation becomes irreducibly recursive in the social domain because outputs such as belief, hatred, esteem, and envy alter the judgments and relations governing subsequent transitions.","refs":["CL_THN_04:XD-06","CL_THN_05:BSD-07","CL_THN_06:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-08","text":"Strategic intervention follows the same logic as experiment: instead of positing new faculties, alter authority, proximity, concurrence, associative continuity, or comparative framing and observe the transformed output.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-06","CL_THN_05:FGS-B07","CL_THN_06:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-09","text":"Social topology becomes epistemically relevant because testimony and judgment acquire force through authority and relation; inquiry must therefore distinguish evidential content from its route of affective uptake.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-06","CL_THN_05:ALT-B06","CL_THN_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:XD-10","text":"Moderate skepticism cannot require affective neutrality; it must regulate the unavoidable forces by which custom, curiosity, testimony, and social relations sustain inquiry.","refs":["CL_THN_04:XD-10","CL_THN_05:RLE-04","CL_THN_06:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Cross-Unit Derivative Propositions"},{"number":"9","name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-01","text":"Ontology becomes an operational field of heterogeneous events connected by recurrent, force-bearing, and directionally weighted transitions without known substantial support.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-02","CL_THN_05:BSD-01","CL_THN_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-02","text":"The subject becomes a distributed effect of organization: impressions, habits, memories, testimony, comparisons, and reflected sentiments converge temporarily as self-reference.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-03","CL_THN_05:BSD-02","CL_THN_06:BSD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-03","text":"Epistemic normativity becomes topological and regulatory: warrant depends on how transitions survive counterinstance, variation, competing routes, and recursive feedback rather than on their subjective force alone.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-01","CL_THN_05:BSD-06","CL_THN_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-04","text":"Social reality is causally productive rather than representationally secondary: circulated affect generates rank, self-estimation, attachment, antagonism, and the evidential force of judgments.","refs":["CL_THN_05:ALT-B06","CL_THN_06:BSD-01","CL_THN_06:BSD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-05","text":"Time becomes recursively constitutive: repetition projects futures, distance modulates conviction, retention enables comparison, and reflected consequences become new present causes.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-05","CL_THN_05:BSD-04","CL_THN_06:BSD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-06","text":"Human agency is non-sovereign but strategically variable: subjects cannot suspend the machinery of transition, yet they can alter routes, circumstances, associations, and comparative fields.","refs":["CL_THN_05:BSD-05","CL_THN_06:BSD-07","CL_THN_04:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-07","text":"Error is not external noise but productive excess internal to successful machinery; every mechanism capable of stabilizing cognition or society can also fabricate ontology, sentiment, or hierarchy.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-04","CL_THN_05:BSD-07","CL_THN_06:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-08","text":"The science of human nature becomes experimentally recursive: anomalies are treated as variations revealing hidden routing conditions rather than as immediate grounds for inventing new faculties.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-07","CL_THN_05:BSD-08","CL_THN_06:FGS-B10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:BSD-09","text":"No ethical or epistemic virtue inheres in connectedness itself; intensified relation can support disciplined comparison, prejudice, sympathy, rivalry, or self-confirming passion.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-05","CL_THN_05:GA-03","CL_THN_06:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Super-Derivatives"},{"number":"10","name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-01","text":"Repetition → custom → facilitated transition → vivified belief or action → renewed conjunction forms the basal cognitive loop.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-01","CL_THN_05:RLE-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-02","text":"Fluent transition → projected unity or power → contradiction → fictive repair or controlled differentiation forms the principal epistemic bifurcation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-02","CL_THN_04:RLE-05","CL_THN_05:RLE-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-03","text":"General mechanism → anomalous output → restrictive gating → provisional stabilization → renewed anomaly forms the meta-cluster’s dominant escalation sequence.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-04","CL_THN_05:RLE-05","CL_THN_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-04","text":"Inquiry → skeptical intensification → affective interruption → natural restoration → constrained inquiry forms the recurrent anti-foundational spiral.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-08","CL_THN_05:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-05","text":"Sign or condition → represented affect → vivacity transfer or construction → passion → judgment or desire → altered condition forms the social feedback loop.","refs":["CL_THN_06:RLE-05","CL_THN_06:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-06","text":"Satisfaction → sympathetic esteem → reflected esteem → intensified satisfaction forms a status-producing loop in which social value recursively augments its own cause.","refs":["CL_THN_06:RLE-02","CL_THN_06:RMD-10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-07","text":"Comparison → altered self-position → passion → attention to the comparative relation → intensified or severed comparison forms the recursive rivalry loop.","refs":["CL_THN_06:RLE-04","CL_THN_06:XD-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-08","text":"“Relation” becomes the principal machine when it expands from observed connection into a directed infrastructure governing inference, attribution, simulation, comparison, and intervention.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-07","CL_THN_05:RLE-07","CL_THN_06:RLE-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:RLE-09","text":"Human nature is the hidden attractor: causal method, skeptical regulation, selfhood, passion, and social hierarchy converge on the recursive organization of relation-mediated force.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RLE-09","CL_THN_05:RLE-06","CL_THN_06:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recursive Loops, Escalation Paths, and Conceptual Attractors"},{"number":"11","name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B01","text":"Initialize heterogeneous perceptions, qualities, pleasures, pains, persons, and represented conditions without an independently appearing support.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B01","CL_THN_05:FGS-B01","CL_THN_06:FGS-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B02","text":"Organize inputs through conjunction, resemblance, contiguity, succession, self-reference, authority, proximity, durability, and directed relational pathways.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B01","CL_THN_05:FGS-B04","CL_THN_06:FGS-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B03","text":"Add force through vivacity, pleasure, pain, passion, retained impression, or customary propensity; relation without force remains explanatorily incomplete.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B02","CL_THN_05:FGS-B04","CL_THN_06:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B04","text":"Repetition and general rules stabilize transitions into expectation, attribution, self-reference, and socially anticipated affect.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B05","CL_THN_05:FGS-B05","CL_THN_06:RMD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B05","text":"Route stabilized force through causal projection, imaginative completion, sympathetic appropriation, or comparative opposition to generate belief, object-unity, passion, and social value.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B02","CL_THN_05:FGS-B05","CL_THN_06:FGS-B04","CL_THN_06:FGS-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B06","text":"Detect excess through contrary instance, fictive ontology, contradictory belief, affective overgeneration, reversed valence, or self-confirming judgment.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B03","CL_THN_05:FGS-B06","CL_THN_06:XD-07","CL_THN_06:XD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B07","text":"Bifurcate excess into simulated closure or controlled restriction through semantic audit, circumstance-analysis, skeptical diffidence, threshold-gating, and relational rerouting.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B04","CL_THN_04:FGS-B06","CL_THN_05:FGS-B07","CL_THN_06:FGS-B09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B08","text":"Feed generated beliefs and passions back into custom, judgment, authority, reflected esteem, and comparative positioning, thereby modifying subsequent inputs and routes.","refs":["CL_THN_05:FGS-B08","CL_THN_06:FGS-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B09","text":"Submit every provisional stabilization to renewed variation, including the rules, classifications, and social models produced by earlier correction.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B07","CL_THN_05:FGS-B09","CL_THN_06:FGS-B10"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FGS-B10","text":"Terminate only metaphysical completion: preserve recursive empirical and social-affective inquiry while withholding final necessity, intrinsic baseline, and external validation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FGS-B08","CL_THN_05:FGS-B09","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Formal Generative Schema of the Whole Book"},{"number":"12","name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B01","text":"Hidden substance, intrinsic power, autonomous reason, and primitive passion → demand for experiential and operational justification.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B04","CL_THN_05:AFM-B01","CL_THN_06:AFM-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B02","text":"Justificatory demand → decomposition into perceptions, qualities, affects, conjunctions, and relations.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B03","CL_THN_05:AFM-B02","CL_THN_06:AFM-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B03","text":"Decomposition → relation-conditioned transition animated by force, vivacity, repetition, and custom.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B01","CL_THN_05:AFM-B03","CL_THN_06:AFM-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B04","text":"Animated transition → belief, projected unity, apparent placement, self-attribution, sympathy, and comparison.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B02","CL_THN_05:AFM-B04","CL_THN_06:AFM-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B05","text":"Unrestricted transition → prejudice, metaphysical fiction, contradictory belief, affective overgeneration, and reversed social valence.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B04","CL_THN_05:AFM-B05","CL_THN_06:XD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B06","text":"Excess and anomaly → controlled difference, semantic restraint, skeptical restriction, relational gating, and mode differentiation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B05","CL_THN_05:AFM-B06","CL_THN_06:AFM-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B07","text":"Restricted mechanism → recursive belief, reflected esteem, passion-generated judgment, and strategic alteration of relational routes.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B06","CL_THN_05:AFM-B07","CL_THN_06:AFM-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:AFM-B08","text":"Recursive feedback → nonterminal science of human nature constrained by experiment and skepticism but lacking final thresholds, intrinsic baselines, or external closure.","refs":["CL_THN_04:AFM-B07","CL_THN_05:AFM-B07","CL_THN_06:AFM-B08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Whole-Book Argument Flow Map"},{"number":"13","name":"Macro Map-Effects","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-01","text":"The substance–faculty map is replaced by a field of heterogeneous events, directed relations, force-bearing transitions, and recursive outputs.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-02","CL_THN_05:MME-01","CL_THN_06:MME-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-02","text":"Reason, imagination, passion, and error cease to name autonomous agencies and become differently regulated phases of one natural economy.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-01","CL_THN_05:MME-03","CL_THN_06:MME-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-03","text":"Causation is redrawn as a transformation architecture: conjunction supplies repeatability, relation supplies routing, force supplies efficacy, and gating supplies differential output.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-05","CL_THN_05:MME-04","CL_THN_06:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-04","text":"The self is reclassified from hidden bearer to a temporally extended and socially permeable relay-comparator.","refs":["CL_THN_04:HSI-02","CL_THN_05:MME-05","CL_THN_06:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-05","text":"Evidence becomes multidimensional: comparative regularity determines warrant, vivacity determines uptake, and social topology determines which judgments acquire force.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-06","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-06","text":"Comparison is split into experimental discrimination and affective inversion, dissolving any assumption that heightened contrast is intrinsically rational or irrational.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-03","CL_THN_06:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-07","text":"Social hierarchy is reclassified as recursively generated affective adjacency rather than the passive registration of intrinsic rank.","refs":["CL_THN_05:MME-06","CL_THN_06:MME-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-08","text":"Error becomes a phase of productive machinery whose limits are disclosed through overgeneration rather than a foreign corruption added to otherwise pure faculties.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-04","CL_THN_05:MME-07","CL_THN_06:MME-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MME-09","text":"Intervention and interpretation shift from naming hidden entities to varying circumstances, tracing routes, separating force from content, and testing how feedback transforms the field.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MME-07","CL_THN_05:MME-09","CL_THN_06:MME-07","CL_THN_06:MME-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Macro Map-Effects"},{"number":"14","name":"Altitude Integration","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B01","text":"Surface level: causal judgment, substance and soul, skeptical inquiry, pride and humility, reputation, affection, pity, envy, and malice appear as distinct explicit problems.","refs":["CL_THN_04:ALT-B01","CL_THN_05:ALT-B01","CL_THN_06:ALT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B02","text":"Structural level: relation, repetition, vivacity, imagination, comparison, custom, retention, and feedback generate and differentiate the surface phenomena.","refs":["CL_THN_04:ALT-B02","CL_THN_05:ALT-B02","CL_THN_06:ALT-B02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B03","text":"Metastrategic level: inquiry intervenes by widening comparison, varying circumstances, restricting overgeneralization, and rerouting authority or proximity rather than invoking new faculties.","refs":["CL_THN_04:ALT-B03","CL_THN_05:ALT-B03","CL_THN_06:ALT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B04","text":"Ontological / Temporal level: reality is a field of heterogeneous events in ordered and retained relations; identity, causation, selfhood, and value are provisional products of temporal organization.","refs":["CL_THN_04:ALT-B04","CL_THN_05:ALT-B04","CL_THN_06:ALT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B05","text":"Social / Political level: authority, publicity, custom, reflected esteem, proximity, and comparison distribute the force by which judgments and hierarchies become operative.","refs":["CL_THN_05:ALT-B06","CL_THN_06:BSD-04","CL_THN_06:BSD-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:ALT-B06","text":"Methodological / Performative level: the architecture performs its thesis by producing excess, converting anomaly into controlled variation, and recursively subjecting its own rules and stabilizations to renewed audit.","refs":["CL_THN_04:ALT-B05","CL_THN_05:ALT-B05","CL_THN_06:ALT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Altitude Integration"},{"number":"15","name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-01","text":"The same custom and imagination generate error and correction, leaving the distinction between legitimate and illegitimate transition immanent and never completely secured.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FL-01","CL_THN_05:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-02","text":"Necessary connection is denied, yet causal method depends on strong uniformity, stable kinds, and reproducible transitions whose practical authority exceeds their ontological grounding.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FL-03","CL_THN_04:FL-05","CL_THN_05:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-03","text":"A common transition architecture must explain heterogeneous outputs, but each domain introduces gates and constitutional supplements that resist complete unification.","refs":["CL_THN_05:GA-08","CL_THN_05:FL-05","CL_THN_06:FL-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-04","text":"The substantial self is rejected, yet correction, passion, memory, and comparison require persistent self-reference whose continuity remains operationally necessary but ontologically unspecified.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-05","CL_THN_05:FL-01","CL_THN_06:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-05","text":"Force and relation are indispensable explanatory variables, but no quantitative rule determines sufficient vivacity, adequate repetition, decisive relation, or conversion threshold.","refs":["CL_THN_04:FL-08","CL_THN_05:MRT-B06","CL_THN_06:FL-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-06","text":"Sympathy and comparison can derive opposed affects from the same condition, but the architecture lacks a general selector determining which mode dominates.","refs":["CL_THN_06:FL-02","CL_THN_06:FL-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-07","text":"Nature restores belief, action, and affective order without vindicating them, preserving a structural separation between practical efficacy and epistemic or ethical legitimacy.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-11","CL_THN_05:FL-06","CL_THN_06:GA-13"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:FL-08","text":"Corrective inquiry is itself socially and affectively routed through authority, curiosity, testimony, and vivacity, threatening any claim that experimental regulation is insulated from the mechanisms it diagnoses.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSD-06","CL_THN_05:ALT-B06","CL_THN_06:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-01","text":"Relation mutates from observed conjunction into a directed and weighted infrastructure for causation, self-reference, sympathy, comparison, and intervention.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-05","CL_THN_05:MP-03","CL_THN_06:MP-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-02","text":"Vivacity mutates from a measure of conviction into the common activation threshold for belief, passion, social transmission, and self-alteration.","refs":["CL_THN_04:RMD-09","CL_THN_05:GA-06","CL_THN_06:MP-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-03","text":"Imagination mutates from associative completion into a general productive medium and finally into constructive social simulation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:GA-03","CL_THN_05:MP-02","CL_THN_06:MP-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-04","text":"Comparison mutates from experimental control into a social operation that converts retained affect into inverse self-evaluation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MP-01","CL_THN_06:MP-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-05","text":"The self mutates from excluded substance into operational object and then into a socially generated magnitude within recursive feedback.","refs":["CL_THN_04:BSA-05","CL_THN_05:MP-04","CL_THN_06:MP-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-06","text":"Cause mutates from whole-object identity into discriminated circumstance, gated conversion, and feedback circuit.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MP-04","CL_THN_05:MP-06","CL_THN_06:RLE-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Composite","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MP-07","text":"Skepticism mutates from denial of intrinsic power into recurrent regulation of cognitive and social-affective mechanisms without terminating their operation.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MP-05","CL_THN_05:MP-05","CL_THN_06:BSA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Tension","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Internal Tensions, Mutation Points, and Fault Lines"},{"number":"16","name":"Hidden System Image","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:HSI-01","text":"Reality appears as a recursive laboratory-network: heterogeneous events form nodes, relations form weighted pathways, force animates transmission, and feedback continually alters the field being investigated.","refs":["CL_THN_04:HSI-01","CL_THN_05:HSI-01","CL_THN_06:HSI-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:HSI-02","text":"The subject is a permeable relay-comparator that receives force, projects expectation, simulates others, measures itself through retained differences, and returns the result as judgment or action.","refs":["CL_THN_04:HSI-02","CL_THN_05:HSI-02","CL_THN_06:HSI-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:HSI-03","text":"Time is a nested circuit rather than a line: repetition becomes projection, retained affect becomes comparison, durable attribution becomes identity, and reflected consequence becomes new cause.","refs":["CL_THN_04:HSI-03","CL_THN_05:HSI-03","CL_THN_06:HSI-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:HSI-04","text":"The remaining struggle is over routing rather than transcendence: viable correction alters circumstances, relations, authorities, associations, and comparative distances while remaining inside the machinery of human nature.","refs":["CL_THN_04:HSI-04","CL_THN_05:HSI-04","CL_THN_06:HSI-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Hidden System Image"},{"number":"17","name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B01","text":"The axioms are sufficient to reconstruct the meta-cluster if unsupported heterogeneity, relation-mediated force, customary stabilization, differential gating, recursive feedback, and immanent correction are retained together.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B01","CL_THN_05:MRT-B01","CL_THN_06:MRT-B01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B02","text":"The architecture remains underdetermined concerning stable kinds, adequate repetition, causal individuation, mode selection, relation thresholds, and the continuity of the operational self.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B02","CL_THN_05:MRT-B02","CL_THN_06:MRT-B05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B03","text":"The system is coherent at this scale as a recursive operational naturalism, not as a deductive foundation, unified metric, or final reconciliation of cognition and affect.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B03","CL_THN_05:MRT-B03","CL_THN_06:MRT-B04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B04","text":"Its strongest architecture is the conversion of failed intrinsic explanations into a distributed machine capable of generating belief, error, selfhood, passion, hierarchy, and their correction from related operations.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B04","CL_THN_05:MRT-B04","CL_THN_06:MRT-B03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B05","text":"Reconstruction preserves the actual movement only if foundational subtraction is followed by operational return, and operational return by anomaly, restriction, feedback, and renewed audit.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B05","CL_THN_05:MRT-B05","CL_THN_06:MRT-B06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B06","text":"The architecture is weakest where it assigns regulatory authority to broader custom, experiment, moderation, or social certification without fully deriving their superiority from the machinery they regulate.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B06","CL_THN_05:FL-02","CL_THN_06:FL-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_02:MRT-B07","text":"The indispensable residue resistant to axiomatic reduction is differential force and selection: neither the intensity activating a transition nor the rule choosing among projection, sympathy, direct survey, and comparison is fully calculable.","refs":["CL_THN_04:MRT-B07","CL_THN_05:MRT-B06","CL_THN_06:MRT-B07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Minimal Whole-Book Reconstruction Test"}],"language_code":"en","language_name":"English","translations":[],"identity":{"layer":"MC","kicker":"Meta-cluster · A Treatise of Human Nature","title":"Metacluster 2","context":"Chapters 10–18 · Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability · Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects · Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy · Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul · Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book · Sect. VI Limitations Of This System · Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame · Sect. III Difficulties Solved · Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy","original_title":"Metacluster 2","original_context":"Chapters 10–18 · Sect. XIII. Of Unphilosophical Probability · Sect. XV. Rules By Which To Judge Of Causes And Effects · Sect. III. Of The Antient Philosophy · Sect. V. Of The Immateriality Of The Soul · Sect. VII. Conclusion Of This Book · Sect. VI Limitations Of This System · Sect. XI Of The Love Of Fame · Sect. III Difficulties Solved · Sect. VIII Of Malice And Envy"},"original_title":"Metacluster 2","title_language_code":"en"},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03","layer":"MC","title":"Metacluster 3","order":3,"parents":["CL_THN_07","CL_THN_08","CL_THN_09"],"revision":"MC_THN_L1_03@R001","sha256":"571aacd38736984af332d422ff1398b4721a7d965edbbebe04b43425a4fc4b76","sections":[{"number":"1","name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-01","text":"The central problem is how mediated, partial, historically variable affect can generate stable property, justice, moral judgment, and political authority without intrinsic value, innate right, or an external normative tribunal.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-01","CL_THN_08:AS-B01","CL_THN_09:BAS-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-02","text":"The governing thesis is that normative order is an endogenous construction: affective forces are redirected through repetition, reflection, reciprocal expectation, generalization, and institutional determination until they acquire authority over their own conditions of origin.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-07","CL_THN_08:BSD-09","CL_THN_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-03","text":"At this scale, the argument becomes a recursive naturalistic genealogy joined to a functional test: it reconstructs the genesis of norms and then evaluates them by the relational effects they durably produce.","refs":["CL_THN_07:ASP-03","CL_THN_08:AS-B03","CL_THN_09:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-04","text":"The movement proceeds from object-side value to affective mediation, from mediated passion to convention, from convention to constituted juridical objects, and from institutional utility to generalized moral authority.","refs":["CL_THN_07:ASP-05","CL_THN_08:AS-B05","CL_THN_09:BAS-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-05","text":"Relation becomes the trans-scalar operator: it transfers affect, associates persons with goods, establishes duties, communicates consequences, and supplies the field in which character and authority receive moral classification.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-08","CL_THN_08:CGD-04","CL_THN_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-06","text":"Repetition converts outputs into conditions: encountered force becomes inclination, reciprocal restraint becomes convention, convention becomes second nature, and generalized judgment becomes normative inertia.","refs":["CL_THN_07:FGS-B07","CL_THN_08:AS-B09","CL_THN_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-07","text":"Stability is necessary but nonterminal: rules must resist local passion, yet accumulated anti-function can reactivate correction and reverse property claims, moral standing, or political allegiance.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-10","CL_THN_08:BSD-04","CL_THN_09:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-08","text":"The real movement is spiral and sedimentary: affect generates artifice, artifice reorganizes affect, moralization stabilizes artifice, and historical failure returns the stabilized system to functional reassessment.","refs":["CL_THN_07:ASP-07","CL_THN_08:RLE-04","CL_THN_09:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:ASP-09","text":"Closure is conditional and distributed: shared operations unify the architecture, but instinctive endowment, stipulative boundaries, plural moral sources, and qualitative thresholds prevent reduction to one mechanism or final algorithm.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-13","CL_THN_08:BSD-06","CL_THN_09:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Book-Scale Argumental Spine"},{"number":"2","name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-01","text":"[Persistent] Pleasure, pain, interest, uneasiness, sympathy, and acquired concern are the indispensable materials from which social and normative order must be generated.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-02","CL_THN_08:GA-02","CL_THN_09:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-02","text":"[Attractor] Affective, juridical, and moral value is relationally constituted: objects, goods, persons, dispositions, and offices acquire force or status through mediated relations rather than intrinsic properties alone.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-01","CL_THN_08:GA-06","CL_THN_09:GA-11"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-03","text":"[Persistent] Present force and authority are path-dependent effects of prior encounters, repeated conduct, stabilized expectations, institutional history, and accumulated evaluations.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-07","CL_THN_08:BSD-07","CL_THN_09:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-04","text":"[Mutating] Partiality is both destabilizing material and generative resource: immediate passion produces conflict, while reflected, coordinated, and generalized partiality produces durable order.","refs":["CL_THN_07:BSD-05","CL_THN_08:GA-05","CL_THN_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-05","text":"[Mutating] Reflection is not a sovereign source of value; it changes the scale, temporal horizon, and organization of passions already in operation.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-03","CL_THN_08:GA-05","CL_THN_09:GA-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-06","text":"[Persistent] A generated category cannot explain its own genesis: acquired ends cannot presuppose antecedent concern, justice cannot presuppose property, and allegiance cannot presuppose contractual obligation.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-11","CL_THN_08:GA-02","CL_THN_09:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-07","text":"[Attractor] Artifice is necessary without being arbitrary when durable natural limitations require second-order coordination but do not independently determine the form of that coordination.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GA-03","CL_THN_09:GA-07","CL_THN_07:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-08","text":"[Persistent] Convention begins as mutually legible, conditionally reciprocal conduct whose repetition stabilizes expectation before explicit moral obligation or authoritative command.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-07","CL_THN_08:GA-04","CL_THN_09:RLE-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-09","text":"[Terminal] Institutions constitute normative objects: stabilized possession becomes property, coordinated benefit becomes justice, and protective function becomes political authority only within rule-governed relations.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GA-06","CL_THN_08:BSD-02","CL_THN_09:XD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-10","text":"[Emergent] Integral rights and stable verdicts require imposed closure because their affective, possessory, utilitarian, and relational grounds remain gradual, conflicting, and underdeterminate.","refs":["CL_THN_07:BSA-02","CL_THN_08:GA-11","CL_THN_09:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-11","text":"[Mutating] Utility changes function across the architecture: it motivates coordination, necessitates institutions, sanctions rule-systems, and tests political authority, but does not uniquely determine every title or exhaust moral merit.","refs":["CL_THN_08:RMD-04","CL_THN_09:GA-06","CL_THN_07:MME-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-12","text":"[Mutating] Sympathy expands from affective transmission into institutional sanction and standpoint generalization, while never becoming universal benevolence or complete motivational impartiality.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-07","CL_THN_08:RMD-10","CL_THN_09:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-13","text":"[Attractor] Impartiality is manufactured by general rules, corrected standpoints, and invariant application rather than discovered in an originally impartial subject.","refs":["CL_THN_08:BSD-03","CL_THN_09:GA-04","CL_THN_07:DBN-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-14","text":"[Persistent] Durable tendency, coordinated scheme, and relational function outrank isolated action, immediate consequence, and accidental outcome as objects of normative evaluation.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GA-13","CL_THN_09:GA-05","CL_THN_07:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-15","text":"[Emergent] Generated norms acquire relative autonomy: they can outlast their immediate motives, redirect later passions, and authorize judgments unavailable at the moment of their origin.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GA-15","CL_THN_09:GA-08","CL_THN_07:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-16","text":"[Terminal] Legitimate reversal must itself be generalized: accumulated, public, and recurrent anti-function—not isolated dislike or private injury—can terminate established authority.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-05","CL_THN_08:BSD-04","CL_THN_09:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GA-17","text":"[Terminal] The resulting order is endogenous, relational, plural, and conditionally stable; no single affective, legal, utilitarian, or moral mechanism completes every stage.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-13","CL_THN_08:GA-15","CL_THN_09:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Load-Bearing Axioms"},{"number":"3","name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-01","text":"[Stable identity] Pleasure and pain remain the basic currency, but their function expands from direct affective modulation into support for inclination, institutional observance, moral approval, blame, and resistance.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-01","CL_THN_08:GA-14","CL_THN_09:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-02","text":"[Domain expansion] Relation moves from association and affective transfer to juridical attachment and finally to the evaluative field in which dispositions and institutions acquire moral identity.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-03","CL_THN_08:CGD-04","CL_THN_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-03","text":"[Functional recurrence] Repetition successively produces habit, reciprocal confidence, institutional sedimentation, stable moral language, and historical evidence of durable function or anti-function.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-07","CL_THN_08:RLE-02","CL_THN_09:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-04","text":"[Mutation] Stability changes from affective relief into possessory security, juridical rigidity, normative inertia, and finally a condition vulnerable to reversal when it protects recurrent anti-function.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-06","CL_THN_08:GA-09","CL_THN_09:RMD-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-05","text":"[Escalation] Sympathy begins as inter-organismic communication, becomes approval of public utility, and culminates in corrected moral judgment capable of evaluating strangers, institutions, and political effects.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-07","CL_THN_08:RMD-10","CL_THN_09:RMD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-06","text":"[Mutation] Cognition shifts from an affective channel-selector into reflective interest, genealogical correction, and standpoint adjustment; its function is organizational rather than value-creating.","refs":["CL_THN_07:RMD-02","CL_THN_08:GA-05","CL_THN_09:GA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-07","text":"[Reversal] Self-interest changes from immediate acquisitive disruption into reciprocal restraint and then into allegiance to a system whose generalized rules can oppose immediate interest.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-11","CL_THN_08:RMD-05","CL_THN_09:RMD-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-08","text":"[Mutation] Utility moves from an affectively mediated input to the necessity of settlement, then to institutional sanction, and finally to a functional criterion capable of withdrawing political authority.","refs":["CL_THN_07:MME-07","CL_THN_08:RMD-04","CL_THN_09:GA-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-09","text":"[Reversal] General rules first suppress local variability and preserve authority beyond immediate causes; recurrent public failure then produces a counter-rule that authorizes reclassification and resistance.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GA-09","CL_THN_09:RMD-04","CL_THN_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-10","text":"[Absorption] The natural/artificial distinction is absorbed into a layered model in which natural affects and limitations supply materials while artificial coordination produces objects, rules, standpoints, and second-order dispositions.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-12","CL_THN_08:MME-01","CL_THN_09:RMD-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:RMD-11","text":"[Terminal transformation] Normative objects cease to appear antecedent to their relations: affective value, property, moral character, and political authority become stabilized outcomes of mediation, institution, and functional judgment.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-01","CL_THN_08:RMD-03","CL_THN_09:MME-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Recurrence, Mutation, and Displacement Matrix"},{"number":"4","name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-01","text":"[Local] Species comparison, cognitive thresholds, excitatory bands, and the detailed mechanisms of curiosity remain affective diagnostics; their scalable result is the mediated and phase-sensitive constitution of motivation.","refs":["CL_THN_07:CGD-01","CL_THN_07:CGD-03","CL_THN_07:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-02","text":"[Local] Occupation, prescription, accession, succession, and municipal title rules remain juridical implementations; their scalable result is that gradual relations require socially imposed determinacy.","refs":["CL_THN_08:CGD-02","CL_THN_08:CGD-06","CL_THN_08:GA-12"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-03","text":"[Local] Pride, tenderness, anger, allegiance, and tyranny remain differentiated moral-political cases; their scalable result is evaluation by durable relational tendency and generalizable exception.","refs":["CL_THN_09:CGD-02","CL_THN_09:CGD-03","CL_THN_09:CGD-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-04","text":"[Scales upward] Affective mediation becomes the substrate of institutional genesis: passions can be redirected, transferred, and stabilized because they are not fixed responses to intrinsic object-values.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-01","CL_THN_08:GA-05","CL_THN_09:GA-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-05","text":"[Scales upward] Path-dependence becomes sedimentary social ontology: repeated encounters create inclinations, repeated restraint creates convention, and repeated evaluation creates stable authority or evidence of anti-function.","refs":["CL_THN_07:BSD-02","CL_THN_08:HSI-03","CL_THN_09:BSD-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-06","text":"[Bridge] Affective transfer and sympathy connect individual passion to public normativity by allowing generated force and perceived consequences to cross persons, practices, and institutions.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-06","CL_THN_08:GA-14","CL_THN_09:GA-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-07","text":"[Bridge] Relation and imagination connect affective association to juridical and moral object-construction: the same relational field joins ideas, persons, goods, duties, and evaluative roles.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-08","CL_THN_08:CGD-04","CL_THN_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-08","text":"[Catalyst] The possibility that conduct generates its later motive scales into the claim that observance can precede duty and that institutional authority can be retrospectively moralized.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-11","CL_THN_08:GA-14","CL_THN_09:GA-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-09","text":"[Recoded] Instinct, scarcity, limited benevolence, and shared affective constitution are retained not as complete explanations but as boundary conditions constraining what recursive construction can generate.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GA-12","CL_THN_08:GA-01","CL_THN_09:GA-02"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:CGD-10","text":"[Superseded] Intrinsic value, pre-social property, naturally impartial motivation, and absolute political title are replaced by mediated force, constituted relation, corrected judgment, and conditional function.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GNX-01","CL_THN_08:GNX-01","CL_THN_09:GNX-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Lower-Layer vs Current-Level Distinction"},{"number":"5","name":"Global Apophatic Structure","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-01","text":"No intrinsic object-quality is sufficient to determine affective force, juridical ownership, moral merit, or political authority.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GNX-01","CL_THN_08:GNX-03","CL_THN_09:GNX-07"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-02","text":"Sovereign reason, innate justice, and an affect-free moral standpoint cannot originate the architecture.","refs":["CL_THN_07:DBN-02","CL_THN_08:GNX-02","CL_THN_09:GNX-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-03","text":"Pre-social property, natural right, promise, or contract cannot explain institutions that must first constitute rights, duties, and authoritative expectations.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GNX-01","CL_THN_08:GNX-06","CL_THN_09:CGD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-04","text":"Universal benevolence and motivational impartiality cannot generate or continuously sustain general social rules.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GNX-07","CL_THN_09:GNX-05","CL_THN_07:DBN-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-05","text":"Utility cannot serve as a self-executing and exhaustive measure of affect, title, virtue, or every particular institutional act.","refs":["CL_THN_07:DBN-07","CL_THN_08:GNX-04","CL_THN_09:GNX-06"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-06","text":"Stable order cannot remain continuously responsive to immediate feeling, private interest, isolated need, individual merit, or case-specific optimization.","refs":["CL_THN_07:GNX-03","CL_THN_08:GNX-05","CL_THN_09:GNX-03"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-07","text":"Natural relations do not contain the binary boundaries required by property, legal decision, stable moral verdict, or political classification.","refs":["CL_THN_07:BSA-02","CL_THN_08:DBN-05","CL_THN_09:BSA-04"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-08","text":"Isolated consequences, accidental outcomes, or singular failures cannot determine the status of a rule, disposition, or institution independently of its durable tendency.","refs":["CL_THN_08:GNX-08","CL_THN_09:GNX-04","CL_THN_07:BSD-08"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-09","text":"Neither repetition nor generalization guarantees permanent reinforcement: accumulated stability may terminate affect, conceal injustice, or generate a counter-rule against recurrent anti-function.","refs":["CL_THN_07:DBN-05","CL_THN_08:BSD-04","CL_THN_09:GA-09"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:GNX-10","text":"No office, law, property distribution, moral consensus, or political authority is absolutely immune to historical reclassification.","refs":["CL_THN_08:BSD-04","CL_THN_09:BSA-05","CL_THN_07:GA-05"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]}],"original_name":"Global Apophatic Structure"},{"number":"6","name":"Deep Book Apophatics","items":[{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:DBN-01","text":"A fixed subject–object ontology must be false: both subjects and objects must remain penetrable by affective history, social expectation, and relational classification.","refs":["CL_THN_07:DBN-01","CL_THN_08:BSD-02","CL_THN_09:BSD-01"],"deep_refs":[],"trace_type":"Negative","passages":[]},{"id":"MC_THN_L1_03:DBN-02","text":"The pre-social individual cannot be a complete rational,